10.1162_003355399556151.md,Public good game without punishment (one-stage),Section IV first public good game: simultaneous contributions to a linear public good with no punishment stage.,0.9,Existence of punishment stage: no punishment; linear public good with 1/n < a < 1,"Condition is defined by absence of the punishment stage and parameter a in (1/n,1).",0.9,False,"In this paper the game is analyzed theoretically; specific experimental implementations in Table II are cited from other papers, not run here.",0.95,False,No original data collection; game is part of theoretical model.,0.95,False,No lab sessions described for this specific formulation in this paper.,0.95,False,No computational or agent-based simulations are reported.,0.95,True,Section IV derives propositions 4 about equilibria using algebra and game theory only.,1.0,2,"Model states 'There are n ≥ 2 players'; exact n is a parameter, so use minimal specified value.",0.4,1,Defined as a one-stage simultaneous move game.,0.9,1,"Each player chooses gi ∈ [0, y], a continuous range, not binary all-or-nothing.",1.0,N/A,No human subjects or dynamic default; purely theoretical one-shot choice of gi.,1.0,N/R,Only parameter a (marginal return to public account) is given; MPCR = a but not instantiated to a numeric value.,0.5,N/A,No communication notion in analytical model without humans.,1.0,0,Model is defined by payoff functions; no feedback or histories are part of game specification.,0.8,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1.0,0,One-shot analytical game; no interface or display concept.,0.8,0,This is explicitly the game 'without punishments'.,1.0,N/A,No punishment stage.,1.0,N/A,No punishment stage.,1.0,0,Only negative costly punishment is ever discussed elsewhere; no positive reward here.,0.9,N/A,No reward stage.,1.0,N/A,No reward stage.,1.0,1,Each player has endowment y; they later normalize surplus to 1 in other games; here y is generic. Use 1 as normalized unit.,0.4,N/R,"Paper derives equilibrium structure, not specific contribution rates as numeric outcomes of an experiment.",0.3,N/R,No numeric realized contributions; only conditions for gi=0 vs gi>0.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios reported for this analytical game.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate payoff formula is given but not instantiated numerically.,0.3,N/A,No participants; purely analytical.,1.0,N/A,No participants; purely analytical.,1.0,N/A,No participants; purely analytical.,1.0,N/A,No participants; purely analytical.,1.0,No human,Game is only used for theoretical propositions about inequity aversion.,1.0,"Linear public good game; n ≥ 2 players choose contributions gi ∈ [0,y]; monetary payoff xi = y − gi + a Σj gj with 1/n < a < 1; standard model predicts dominant strategy gi = 0.",Directly summarized from Section IV definition.,1.0
10.1162_003355399556151.md,Public good game with costly punishment (two-stage),Section IV second public good game: same contribution stage followed by punishment stage with parameters c and pij.,0.95,Existence of punishment stage with cost c (0 < c < 1) allowing players to impose pij ≥ 0 on others after observing contributions,Condition defined by addition of stage-2 punishment with cost parameter c.,0.95,False,They reference Fehr and Gächter (1996) experiments but here analyze a generalized version analytically (Proposition 5).,0.95,False,"Within this paper the game is treated theoretically, not via new experiments.",0.95,False,No lab protocol or subject pool for this generalized model.,0.95,False,"Results are closed-form propositions, no numerical simulation.",1.0,True,Derives conditions for equilibria with punishment (Proposition 5) purely algebraically.,1.0,2,Model states 'There are n ≥ 2 players'; n is left symbolic.,0.4,1,Described as a two-stage but single-round game: contributions then punishment.,0.9,1,"First-stage contributions gi ∈ [0,y].",1.0,N/A,Analytical one-shot; no default allocation process with humans.,1.0,N/R,"Marginal per capita return would be a; but a remains a parameter, not fixed numerically.",0.5,N/A,No communication modeled.,1.0,1,"At stage 2 each player is 'informed about the contribution vector (g1,...,gn)', effectively full summary of others' contributions.",0.9,1,"Players choose punishment vectors pij targeting specific players j, implying identities of punished players are known to punishers.",0.8,N/A,No positive rewards; only punishment pij ≥ 0.,1.0,0,Analytical one-shot game with no user interface notion.,0.8,1,Second stage explicitly allows costly punishment pij.,1.0,0.5,General model defines cost as c per unit pij with 0<c<1; later numerical discussion uses example c=0.5 and c=0.2; choose mid example c=0.5 as representative numeric but acknowledge it’s illustrative.,0.3,1,Each unit pij reduces target’s payoff by 1 (term −Σj pji).,0.95,0,No reward mechanism; only punishment costs and damages.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,1,Same as in first public good game: each player has endowment y; treat as normalized to 1.,0.4,N/R,"Proposition 5 shows existence of equilibria with gi=g or gi=y, but no numeric value for g or y is specified.",0.4,N/R,"No specific numeric contributions, only symbolic g or y.",0.4,N/R,They state such equilibria are efficient but do not provide explicit numeric efficiencies.,0.5,N/R,Monetary payoff function is given but not numerically instantiated.,0.3,N/A,No actual participants for this generalized analytical treatment.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,No human,Only theoretical modeling within this paper.,1.0,"Two-stage linear public good game: Stage 1, n ≥ 2 players choose gi ∈ [0,y]; monetary payoff without punishment xi = y − gi + a Σj gj (1/n < a < 1). Stage 2, after observing all gj, each player i chooses punishment vector pi = (pi1,...,pin) with pij ≥ 0; cost to punisher is c Σj pij (0 < c < 1), target loses Σj pji. Final payoff xi = y − gi + a Σj gj − Σj pji − c Σj pij.",Direct summary of the formal game definition before Proposition 5.,1.0
10.1162_003355399556151.md,Ultimatum game (theoretical model),Section III.A defines a standard two-player ultimatum game normalized surplus 1.,0.98,"Division parameter s offered by proposer (responder share) in [0,1]",The only strategic variable is proposer’s offer s; responder’s action is accept/reject.,1.0,False,They compile empirical regularities from many studies but their analysis of this game in the paper is theoretical (Proposition 1).,0.98,False,No new ultimatum experiments are run in this article.,0.98,False,All ultimatum evidence cited is from other papers; here only theory.,0.98,False,Closed-form utility and equilibrium; no simulations.,1.0,True,Proposition 1 derives dominant strategies and optimal offers using inequity-aversion utility.,1.0,2,Ultimatum game defined with one proposer and one responder.,1.0,1,Standard one-shot ultimatum; no repetition in theoretical model.,0.95,1,"Proposer chooses s ∈ [0,1] continuously, not discrete all-or-nothing.",1.0,N/A,No public good or endowment split framing; just division of a fixed surplus.,1.0,N/A,Not a public good; no MPCR concept.,1.0,N/A,No communication modeled.,1.0,0,Responder only sees the offer s; no earnings summaries across others since n=2.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment/reward stage; rejection is inherent to game not modeled as targeted punishment.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,0,Analytical one-shot game; no UI notion.,0.8,0,"Rejection destroys surplus but is intrinsic to accept/reject, not a separate costly punishment technology with parameters.",0.8,N/A,No separate per-unit punishment decision pij.,1.0,N/A,No pij-type mechanism.,1.0,0,No option to pay extra rewards beyond division s.,0.9,N/A,No reward stage.,1.0,N/A,No reward stage.,1.0,1,They normalize the bargaining surplus to one: 'Without loss of generality we normalize the bargaining surplus to one.',1.0,N/A,"Not a contribution game; s is a division of surplus, not a contribution to public good.",1.0,N/A,No contribution decision; just division.,1.0,N/R,They discuss acceptance/rejection and inequity but do not report efficiency ratios in the analytical treatment.,0.4,N/R,"Total surplus is 1 if accepted, 0 if rejected; not summarized as a numeric average outcome within this theory section.",0.4,N/A,Within this theoretical section there are no new subjects; evidence from Table I is cross-study but not an experiment here.,1.0,N/A,No new subjects.,1.0,N/A,No new subjects.,1.0,N/A,No new subjects.,1.0,No human,"In this paper the ultimatum game is used for analytical derivations, not for original experiments.",1.0,"Two-player ultimatum game: proposer offers responder share s ∈ [0,1] of normalized surplus 1; if accepted, payoffs (x1,x2)=(1−s, s); if rejected, (0,0). Responder’s strategy is accept/reject; analysis adds inequity-aversion utilities with parameters α,β.",Direct summary of Section III.A problem setup.,1.0
10.1162_003355399556151.md,"Market game with proposer competition (many sellers, single buyer)","Section III.B describes market game with n−1 proposers and one responder, used in Proposition 2.",0.98,"Market structure: proposer competition with n−1 simultaneous offers si∈[0,1]; buyer accepts highest offer s̄ or rejects","Independent variable is form of competition: many proposers, one responder, taking max offer.",0.95,False,They cite Roth et al. (1991) experiments but here study the game theoretically with inequity aversion.,0.98,False,No new market experiments are run in this paper.,0.98,False,Only theoretical analysis in this article.,0.98,False,Equilibrium characterized analytically; no simulation.,1.0,True,Proposition 2 proves uniqueness of competitive equilibrium outcome using utility (1).,1.0,2,Formal definition uses n−1 proposers and 1 buyer; n is left general (Roth et al. use 9+1 but that’s external).,0.4,1,Analytical game is single-shot.,0.9,1,"Each proposer chooses real-valued si ∈ [0,1].",1.0,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1.0,N/A,Not a public good.,1.0,N/A,No communication modeled.,1.0,0,Players do not observe others’ historical earnings; only offers in this round matter.,0.8,N/A,No punishment technology.,1.0,N/A,No reward technology.,1.0,0,One-shot theoretical model.,0.8,0,No explicit costly punishment; competition is via offer choice.,0.95,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1.0,0,No extra rewards beyond trade payoffs.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,1,Good is indivisible with normalized surplus 1; proposer’s payoff is 1−s̄ if selected.,0.8,N/A,No contribution decision.,1.0,N/A,No contribution decision.,1.0,N/R,Total gains from trade are always fully realized if any offer is accepted; they don’t compute efficiency explicitly.,0.4,N/R,Total surplus is 1 in trade; not summarized numerically.,0.4,N/A,Within this article no specific sample is run.,1.0,N/A,No subjects.,1.0,N/A,No subjects.,1.0,N/A,No subjects.,1.0,No human,Market game is analyzed as a theoretical game with inequity aversion.,1.0,"There are n−1 proposers (sellers) each offering si ∈ [0,1] to a single responder (buyer). Highest offer s̄ = max si is selected; if multiple max, one proposer is randomly chosen. If accepted, buyer gets s̄ and winning proposer gets 1−s̄ while others get 0; if rejected, all get 0. Under inequity aversion, Proposition 2 shows unique subgame perfect equilibrium outcome has at least two proposers offering s=1, so buyer captures entire surplus.",Summarizes Section III.B’s formal description and Proposition 2 conclusions.,1.0
10.1162_003355399556151.md,"Market game with responder competition (one proposer, many responders)",Section III.C describes market game with single proposer and many competing responders.,0.98,"Market structure: responder competition with one proposer offering s∈[0,1]; n−1 responders simultaneously accept/reject; random selection among acceptors","Key condition is many responders, one proposer, with competition in acceptance stage.",0.95,False,"They cite Güth, Marchand, and Rulliere (1997) experiments but their analysis here is theoretical (Proposition 3).",0.98,False,No new responder-competition experiments are run in this article.,0.98,False,Only analytical model.,0.98,False,Equilibrium derived algebraically.,1.0,True,"Proposition 3 characterizes equilibria under inequity aversion with general n and parameters α,β.",1.0,2,"Model: 1 proposer (player 1) and responders 2,...,n; n left symbolic.",0.4,1,The theoretical game is one-shot.,0.9,1,"Proposer chooses s ∈ [0,1] continuously.",1.0,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1.0,N/A,Not a public good.,1.0,N/A,No communication modeled.,1.0,0,Responders observe s but not others’ payoffs in any summary form.,0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1.0,N/A,No reward technology.,1.0,0,One-shot theoretical description.,0.8,0,No costly punishment choices; only accept/reject.,0.95,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1.0,0,No reward stage.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,1,Same indivisible good of surplus 1 as in proposer-competition game; proposer gets 1−s if trade occurs.,0.8,N/A,No contribution decision.,1.0,N/A,No contribution decision.,1.0,N/R,They discuss highest sustainable s and existence of equilibria; no explicit efficiency numbers.,0.4,N/R,Total surplus is 1 in trade; not numerically summarized.,0.4,N/A,"Within this article, purely theoretical.",1.0,N/A,No subjects.,1.0,N/A,No subjects.,1.0,N/A,No subjects.,1.0,No human,Used analytically to derive Proposition 3.,1.0,"One proposer (player 1) offers s ∈ [0,1] to responders 2,...,n. After seeing s, each responder simultaneously chooses accept or reject. If at least one accepts, one accepting responder is chosen uniformly at random; proposer then gets 1−s, chosen responder gets s, others get 0. If all reject, all get 0. Proposition 3 shows that with inequity aversion and β1 < (n−1)/n there is a subgame-perfect equilibrium where proposer offers s=0 and all responders accept; also characterizes highest sustainable s̄ < 1/2.",Summarizes Section III.C description and Proposition 3.,1.0
