ishment point reduced the punished member’s income by 3 MUs.,1,0,Only costly punishment is described; no positive reward option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,20,Each member received an endowment of 20 MUs each period.,1,N/R,"Paper reports various contribution statistics (e.g., proportions contributing full amount, etc.) but not a single overall normalized contribution rate for this condition.",0,N/R,Provides qualitative comparisons and some period-specific frequencies but no single average MU contribution for the entire punishment condition.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff vs. maximum payoff ratio reported for the punishment condition.,0,N/R,"Individual average earnings are given across whole sessions (including both treatments), but not broken out per treatment and per group.",0,Switzerland,"Participants were students from the University of Zürich and ETH Zürich, both in Switzerland.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age range or mean reported.,0,31% female; 69% male,Methods section: 240 undergraduate students (31% females).,1,Undergraduate university students,Explicitly described as undergraduate students from University of Zürich and ETH.,1,On site,Conducted in computer lab with 24 computers in separate booths; subjects present physically.,1,Stranger matching across six periods within treatment; no subject met another more than once in a given treatment. Two treatments (punishment and no-punishment) per session with order counterbalanced across 10 sessions. Subjects did not know a second six-period game would follow the first.,"Described in Methods: random recomposition each period, no reputational information, two 6-period games with unknown continuation after first.",1
