10.1038_415137a.md,Emotion scenarios – Experimental subjects (Scenarios 1–4),"After the second treatment, the 240 experimental subjects answered four hypothetical investment/anger scenarios used to elicit emotional responses.",1,Hypothetical contribution pattern in vignette (Scenarios 1–4 vary own and others’ contributions and role as observer vs. free rider),"Independent variable is scenario description: (1) high own and others’ contributions, large free rider deviation; (2) lower contributions, smaller deviation; (3–4) expected anger when self is free rider under large vs small deviation.",0.9,True,Responses are self-reports from human participants.,1,True,Structured questionnaire with identical hypothetical scenarios given to all participants within the experimental session.,0.9,True,Questionnaire administered in the lab immediately after the experiment.,1,False,No simulations; purely survey-style measurement.,1,False,No analytical derivations; just descriptive stats and nonparametric tests.,1,4,Scenarios explicitly refer to a four-person group (“You” plus three others).,1,1,"Each scenario is a single hypothetical situation, not repeated plays.",0.9,1,"Scenarios specify concrete contribution amounts (e.g., 16, 14, 18, 2 francs), consistent with continuous contribution choice as in the game.",0.9,0,"By design of the underlying game, contributions are chosen from a private endowment; scenarios mirror that structure.",0.8,N/R,Scenarios only mention contribution amounts in francs; returns or multipliers are not described in this section.,0,0,Scenarios describe ‘you accidentally meet this member’ but no pre-decision communication mechanism; chat not relevant.,0.8,0,Participants are simply told the hypothetical contribution amounts; there is no dynamic feedback or summaries as in a game.,0.7,0,No punishment decisions exist in these hypothetical scenarios; only emotions and expectations.,1,0,No reward mechanism is part of the scenarios.,1,0,Scenarios are one-shot descriptions with no mention of repeated interaction or displayed horizon.,0.9,0,Scenarios only ask about feelings and expected feelings; no punishment choices or costs are mentioned.,1,N/A,No punishment option in these hypothetical questions.,1,N/A,No punishment option in these hypothetical questions.,1,0,No rewarding decisions; only emotional ratings.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in these scenarios.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in these scenarios.,1,N/R,"Scenarios only state contribution amounts in francs (e.g., 16, 5, 2) but do not specify the endowment.",0,N/R,"Outcome here is emotional response (anger), not contribution behavior.",1,N/R,Contributions are exogenously specified in the vignettes; no behavioral choice is measured.,1,N/R,No payoffs or efficiency measures are reported for these hypothetical scenarios.,1,N/R,Scenarios do not include payoff computation; only emotional scales.,1,Switzerland,Same 240 experimental subjects from University of Zürich and ETH Zürich answer the scenarios.,1,N/R,Age not reported; only that they are undergraduates.,0,31% female; 69% male,"Same sample composition (240 students, 31% female) as in the main experiment.",1,Undergraduate university students,Identical participant pool as main public-goods experiment.,1,On site,Questionnaire administered in the lab immediately after the experiment sessions.,1,"Four vignette-based scenarios: (1) You contribute 16, others 14 and 18, free rider contributes 2; report anger. (2) You contribute 5, others 3 and 7, free rider 2; report anger. (3) Others contribute 14, 16, 18, you contribute 2; report expected anger of others. (4) Others contribute 3, 5, 7, you contribute 2; report expected anger. Anger measured on 1–7 scale; Wilcoxon tests compare scenarios.",Detailed scenario descriptions and anger results provided in ‘Emotions as a proximate mechanism’ section.,1
