tions are reported.,1,N/R,No game outcomes are realized.,1,N/R,"Only number of subjects (33) is mentioned; affiliation or location is not specified, though likely similar population. Cannot assert country.",0.3,N/R,No demographic information provided for this independent sample.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported for this separate group of 33 subjects.,0,N/R,The text only says '33 subjects' with no mention of being students or other status.,0.2,On site,"Described as being ‘presented to 33 subjects’; given the context of the study at University of Zürich, they were likely tested on site, although not explicitly labeled as lab, but on-site is most plausible.",0.7,"Same four emotion scenarios as for the 240 experimental subjects were administered to 33 additional subjects who had not participated in the games, to check robustness of emotional patterns. The same qualitative anger patterns were observed.",Explicitly stated in ‘Emotions as a proximate mechanism’ that 33 non-participant subjects showed the same emotional patterns.,1
10.1038_nature02043.md,Ultimatum game – 10 repeated rounds with changing responders,Authors: “we ran an experiment with ten proposers who met a different responder in ten successive rounds.”,1,Round number (1–10); previous-round rejection experience for each proposer (rejected vs not rejected),They compare offers in current round conditional on experiencing a rejection in previous round.,0.8,True,Human subjects in a lab bargaining experiment.,1,True,Controlled game with fixed rules and monetary payoffs.,1,True,Described as a laboratory experiment; typical for this literature.,0.9,False,No computer simulation; only human decisions.,1,False,No purely analytical modeling of this specific design.,1,2,Ultimatum game between proposer (A) and responder (B).,1,10,“ten proposers who met a different responder in ten successive rounds.”,1,1,"Standard ultimatum game allows any split of the fixed sum, not only all-or-nothing; no restriction mentioned.",0.8,0,Not a public good; endowment is simply a fixed pie to be divided; no private/public accounts.,1,N/A,Not a public goods or linear VCM setting.,1,0,No communication mentioned; ultimatum games in this literature normally prohibit chat.,0.8,0,No feedback about others’ earnings; only own interaction history discussed.,0.7,0,Interactions are anonymous; punishment via rejection but identities not revealed beyond role.,0.8,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism or identity revelation.,1,1,Subjects in this line of work are typically fully informed about the finite horizon; text: “subjects ... had full knowledge about the structure of the experiment.”,0.8,1,Rejection of offer is a costly punishment for proposer and responder.,1,0,No per-unit punishment choice; rejection cancels the entire pie; per‑unit cost concept not applicable.,0.5,0,"Discrete rejection eliminates payoffs, not a linear technology; per-unit multiplier not defined.",0.5,0,No extra rewarding stage beyond the division proposal.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,