10.1038_nature02043.md,"Third-party punishment game – baseline condition (allocator, recipient, third party)",Section ‘Altruistic punishment’: “we conducted a third-party punishment game involving three subjects…”; only one condition described.,1,Allocator transfer amount (0–100 MUs); third-party punishment decision contingent on transfer (0–50 MUs spent),Allocation is free choice 0–100; third party chooses how many MUs to spend on punishment after observing transfer.,1,True,Game played by human subjects with real money.,1,True,Controlled laboratory game with specified roles and payoffs.,1,True,Part of the authors’ standard lab program; no field setting mentioned.,0.9,False,Not a computer-only study.,1,False,No closed-form modeling of this specific design.,1,3,"Allocator, recipient, third party.",1,1,Described as an experiment to study norm enforcement; no repetition mentioned; typical design is one-shot.,0.7,1,Allocator “is free to give whatever he wants” from 0 to 100; continuous or discrete but not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,No public goods structure; private endowment allocated between self and recipient.,1,N/A,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication described; standard is no chat.,0.8,0,Third party sees only the allocator’s transfer; no earnings summaries reported.,0.8,0,Interactions are anonymous; no identities revealed when punishing.,0.8,0,No reward stage.,1,1,Subjects were fully informed about structure; one-shot nature is common knowledge.,0.8,1,Third party can spend money to reduce allocator’s payoff.,1,1,“Every MU spent on punishment reduces the allocator's income by three MUs.” Thus cost per punishment MU = 1 MU for third party.,1,3,Each MU spent cuts allocator income by 3 MUs (multiplier 3).,1,0,Third party only has punishment option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,150,Allocator endowed with 100 MUs; third party with 50 MUs; total monetary units initially in game = 150.,1,N/R,No normalized transfer or punishment rates reported numerically.,0.2,N/R,"They report that 55% of third parties punish transfers below 50 and punishment increases as transfer decreases, but no average amounts.",0.3,N/R,No payoff/efficiency statistics given.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoff data.,0.1,N/R,No sample location for this particular study specified in text.,0.2,N/R,No age information given.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition given.,0.1,N/R,Education not described; likely university students but not specified.,0.3,On site,Described as laboratory experiments with real money; no indication of online or field setting.,0.9,"Allocator endowed with 100 MUs chooses transfer to a zero-endowment recipient. Third party has 50 MUs and, after seeing the transfer, can spend any portion to punish. Each MU spent by third party reduces allocator’s payoff by 3 MUs. Fair transfer benchmark is 50 MUs. Empirically, 55% of third parties punish transfers below 50, and punishment intensity increases as transfers fall further below 50. Between 70–80% of recipients expect low transfers to be punished.",Directly summarized from description and Fig. 1 discussion.,1
10.1038_nature02043.md,Ultimatum game – Baseline condition (no reputation),In section on reputation and ultimatum game: two conditions described—“reputation condition” and “baseline condition.” This row is baseline.,1,Reputation condition = baseline (no information about responders’ past rejections); round number 1–10,Independent manipulation is whether proposers see responder’s history; in baseline they do not.,1,True,Human laboratory ultimatum game.,1,True,Controlled design with random matching over 10 periods and treatment manipulation.,1,True,Described among laboratory experiments; typical Fehr-Fischbacher lab work.,0.9,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Not purely theoretical.,1,2,Standard ultimatum game between proposer and responder.,1,10,“a series of ten ultimatum games in two conditions”; each subject plays 10 periods per condition.,1,1,No restriction to all-or-nothing stated; typical ultimatum game allows any integer split of 10 MUs.,0.9,0,Not a contribution game; endowment is a bargaining pie.,1,N/A,Not a public good.,1,0,No communication allowed mentioned; standard to forbid chat.,0.9,0,Proposers do not see others’ outcomes; only own responder’s threshold matters.,0.7,0,"Responders’ identities are anonymous; only thresholds are used in reputation treatment, not identities.",0.8,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Subjects informed about experiment structure; 10 periods per condition.,0.9,1,Rejection of offers acts as punishment.,1,0,No continuous punishment choice; rejection is binary; linear cost per “unit” not defined.,0.5,0,Rejection cancels the pie rather than reducing payoffs per spent unit.,0.5,0,No extra reward option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"“In both conditions, 10 MUs have to be divided in every period.” So the pie is 10 MUs.",1,N/R,"Outcome focus is on acceptance thresholds (minimum acceptable offers), not reported as proportion statistics over time.",0.2,3,Average acceptance threshold in baseline is “about 3 MUs”; interpret as the key DV.,0.8,N/R,No payoffs reported to compute efficiency.,0.2,N/R,No mean earnings reported.,0.2,N/R,Location not stated for this specific experiment.,0.2,N/R,No age information reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender information reported.,0.1,N/R,Likely students but not explicitly stated.,0.3,On site,Conducted as lab sessions with face-to-face or cubicle participation and real money.,0.9,"Standard ultimatum bargaining over 10 MUs. In each period, each proposer is matched with a new responder. Responders pre-specify an acceptance threshold (minimum acceptable MU amount). In the baseline condition, proposers receive no information about responders’ past rejection behaviour. Average acceptance thresholds are about 3 MUs when this condition is run first. When switching from the reputation to the baseline condition, average thresholds decrease immediately.",Summary from description and Fig. 3 discussion for the baseline condition.,1
10.1038_nature02043.md,Ultimatum game – Reputation condition (rejection history revealed),Same series of ten ultimatum games with “a reputation condition”; proposers know responder’s past rejections.,1,Reputation condition = proposers informed about current responder’s past rejection behaviour; round number 1–10,"Treatment manipulates information about responder’s history, enabling reputation building.",1,True,Same human laboratory study as baseline condition.,1,True,"Random matching, 10 periods, information treatment.",1,True,Laboratory environment with real stakes.,0.9,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,2,Proposer and responder.,1,10,Same “series of ten ultimatum games”.,1,1,Standard ultimatum splitting of 10 MUs.,0.9,0,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,Not a public good.,1,0,No communication mentioned; info is one-way about past rejections only.,0.9,1,"Proposers are “informed about the current responder's past rejection behaviour,” which is a summary statistic of past actions of that responder.",0.8,0,"Although proposer observes responder’s history, actual personal identities remain anonymous.",0.7,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Structure of 10 periods is known, as in baseline.",0.9,1,"Responders can reject offers, punishing low offers at cost to themselves.",1,0,Binary rejection; no linear cost parameter.,0.5,0,"Rejection nullifies payoffs, not a per-unit multiplier.",0.5,0,No separate rewarding stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,“10 MUs have to be divided in every period.”,1,N/R,Key DV is acceptance threshold; not reported as share-of-pie statistic over time beyond a level jump.,0.2,4,Average acceptance threshold “jump up to more than 4 MUs” in reputation condition; we record 4 as approximate central level.,0.7,N/R,No payoff numbers.,0.2,N/R,No mean earnings reported.,0.2,N/R,Country not specified.,0.2,N/R,No age information.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,N/R,Education level not reported.,0.3,On site,"As with baseline, described as lab experiment with real money.",0.9,"Same ultimatum game as baseline: 10 MUs to be divided, new partner each period, responders set acceptance thresholds. In the reputation condition, proposers are informed about the current responder’s past rejection behaviour, enabling responders to build a reputation for toughness. When entering this condition from baseline, average acceptance thresholds immediately jump from about 3 to more than 4 MUs, forcing proposers to raise offers. 82% of responders increase their average threshold in the reputation condition relative to baseline.",Directly from text and Fig. 3 panels a and b.,1
10.1038_nature02043.md,"Public goods experiment – repeated, no punishment",Section ‘Strong reciprocity and multilateral cooperation’ describes standard repeated public goods games without punishment where cooperation decays.,0.7,Round number (1–10); others’ expected average contribution level,They analyze conditional contributions as a function of expected others’ contributions over 10 rounds.,0.8,True,Described as public goods experiments with human subjects and monetary endowments.,1,True,Controlled repeated-game design with contributions and returns.,1,True,Classical lab public goods experiments (Isaac & Walker; Fehr et al.).,0.9,False,Not a simulation model; empirical behaviour discussed.,1,False,"Although a theoretical relationship is illustrated, the passage refers to actual experiments.",0.8,N/R,They mention larger groups but do not specify a single exact group size for this summarized evidence; referenced studies use multiple sizes.,0.3,10,“rarely stable and deteriorates… if the game is played repeatedly (and anonymously) for ten rounds.”,1,1,Standard VCM public goods allow any integer contribution from 0 to full endowment.,0.9,0,Endowment starts entirely in private accounts; subjects choose how much to contribute.,0.9,N/R,They do not provide the multiplier or group size needed to compute MPCR.,0.2,0,Games are anonymous repeated public goods without communication in standard designs; no chat mentioned.,0.8,1,In typical VCM experiments subjects observe others’ or group’s contributions each round; decay of cooperation implies feedback.,0.7,0,No punishment opportunity in this design; thus identity of punishers not applicable but set to 0.,0.8,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,1,Standard public goods experiments inform subjects about the number of periods; authors: “subjects … had full knowledge about the structure of the experiment.”,0.8,0,This section describes decay of cooperation without punishment opportunities; punishment introduced only later.,0.9,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No post-contribution reward stage described.,0.9,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,They only speak of ‘endowment’ and percentage contributions; no numeric value.,0.2,0.4,"“In public goods experiments that are played only once, subjects typically contribute between 40 and 60% of their endowment”; using 0.4 as conservative lower bound of reported range for a representative condition.",0.6,N/R,"Only percentages of endowment reported, not MU amounts.",0.2,N/R,No payoff aggregate or efficiency numbers provided.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs not reported numerically.,0.1,N/R,Summary across many public goods experiments from various studies; not tied to one country.,0.1,N/R,Demographics for underlying studies not described in the review.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information.,0.1,N/R,Underlying studies predominantly used students but this is not explicitly stated here.,0.3,On site,All referenced public goods experiments are standard lab experiments from 1980s–1990s.,0.9,"Linear public goods (voluntary contributions) game repeatedly played for 10 anonymous rounds. Each subject receives an endowment each round and simultaneously chooses a contribution to the public good. Individual payoffs depend on own remaining endowment plus a share of the multiplied sum of all contributions. Empirically, one-shot contributions are typically 40–60% of endowment, but when repeated anonymously for 10 rounds without punishment opportunities, cooperation decays to low levels over time due to presence of free-riders and conditional cooperators.",Synthesized from narrative on public goods experiments and Fig. 2 description.,0.9
