10.1016_s1090-5138(04)00005-4.md,TP-DG (Third-party punishment dictator game),Authors label this design 'third party punishment in the dictator game (TP-DG)'. Single one-shot condition with third-party punisher.,1,"Dictator transfer level to recipient: 0,10,20,30,40,50 (strategy method over all levels; no between-subject treatment variation).",Only factor varied within strategy method is A's transfer; no separate treatments.,1,True,Human student participants from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with random assignment to roles and specified incentives.,1,True,Conducted in university lab using z-Tree; not field or online panel.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,No formal closed-form model solved as the primary method.,1,3,"Game between dictator A, recipient B, and third party C.",1,1,Each subject played the TP-DG only once.,1,1,"Dictator could choose among multiple discrete transfer amounts, not just all-or-nothing; partial contributions allowed.",0.9,0,Endowment starts entirely with dictator A; no automatic contribution to a public fund.,1,N/R,"Dictator game with transfers, not a linear public good; MPCR not defined.",1,0,Subjects interacted anonymously; no mention of communication or chat.,1,0,No reporting of others’ past earnings or punishment; one-shot strategy method.,1,0,Subjects were never informed of other players' identities; punishment anonymous.,1,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,1,One-shot game; subjects knew game structure and that they play it only once.,0.9,1,Third party C could assign costly deduction points to A.,1,1,Assigning 1 deduction point cost C 1 point.,1,3,Each punishment point reduced A's payoff by 3 points.,1,0,No mechanism to increase others' payoffs described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,100,Player A had an endowment of 100 points; focal contributor.,1,N/R,They report average transfer (24.5) but do not convert to normalized rate in text.,0.6,24.5,Average transfer by A was 24.5 points.,1,N/R,No explicit or easily inferred efficiency ratio for this game reported.,0.4,N/R,Average total group payoff not reported.,0.1,Switzerland,Subjects were students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich in Switzerland.,1,N/R,Only described as students; no age statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,University students,Explicitly stated that subjects were students from two universities.,1,On site,Standard z-Tree lab experiment with university students; not described as online.,0.95,"One-shot dictator game with third-party punishment. A: 100 points; B: 0. C: 50 points and can use up to 50 to punish A (1:3 cost/impact). Strategy method for C. Show-up fee CHF 10; 1 point = CHF 0.3. Average earnings CHF 22.20. Roles A,B,C randomly assigned. B reports expectations about A's transfer and C's punishment but cannot punish.",Summarizes detailed procedural description in Section 2.1.,1
10.1016_s1090-5138(04)00005-4.md,TP-PD (Third-party punishment prisoners’ dilemma),Authors label this design 'TP-PD' and describe a single condition with third-party punisher C observing a PD between A and B.,1,"Action profile in PD (A,B) ∈ {(c,c),(c,d),(d,c),(d,d)}; third party C’s punishment specified for each profile (strategy method).",Within-subject variation over four PD outcomes; no separate treatment arms.,1,True,Uses human subjects in a lab PD with monetary incentives.,1,True,Controlled laboratory design with random roles and specified payoffs.,1,True,Implementing PD and punishment via z-Tree with university students.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,Empirical focus; theory only discussed qualitatively.,1,3,Players A and B interact in PD; Player C is third-party punisher.,1,1,Single PD interaction; no repetition mentioned.,1,0,Each PD player chooses either keep all 10 or transfer all 10; binary all-or-nothing decision.,1,0,Each is endowed with 10 points privately; cooperation requires an active transfer of all 10.,1,1.5,Transferred 10 is tripled to 30 for partner; marginal return to group is 30; group size 2 so MPCR=30/(10*2)=1.5.,0.9,0,Anonymous interaction; no communication reported.,1,0,C only observes actions; no payoff history summaries described.,1,0,"Subjects never informed of identities; punishment target known only by role, not identity.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Game structure explained including that it is a one-shot PD with subsequent punishment stage.,0.9,1,Third party C can assign deduction points to A and/or B.,1,1,Assigning 1 deduction point cost C 1 point.,1,3,Each deduction point reduced sanctioned player's payoff by 3 points.,1,0,Only punishment via deduction points is available.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,"In Stage 1, Players A and B were each endowed with 10 points in the PD.",1,N/R,"They report punishment patterns, not overall cooperation frequencies as normalized rates.",0.4,N/R,No aggregate number of cooperators or average transfer reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures provided.,0.2,N/R,Average group earnings not reported.,0.1,Switzerland,Same subject pool: students from Zurich universities.,1,N/R,No ages provided.,0.2,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,University students,Stated that subjects were students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich.,1,On site,Lab experiment with z-Tree and local students.,0.95,"Two-stage game: Stage 1 PD where each of A,B can keep 10 or transfer all 10; transfer is tripled and goes to partner, so payoffs (c,c):30,30; (c,d):0,40; (d,c):40,0; (d,d):10,10. Stage 2: C endowed with 40 points; A and B each get additional 15 points. C can assign up to 20 deduction points to each of A and B at cost 1 per point, reducing target’s payoff by 3 per point. Strategy method: C specifies punishment for each of four PD outcomes. 72 subjects; experiment ~45 minutes; 1 point=CHF 0.37; average earnings CHF 23.40.",Compiled from Section 3.1 description.,1
10.1016_s1090-5138(04)00005-4.md,"DG – Second-party punishment (SP condition, first in sequence)",Section 4 compares second- and third-party punishment in DG; this row captures the SP condition when used as the first condition (which they use for regression tests).,1,"Punishment regime: Second-party punishment (recipient B punishes own dictator A). Transfer deviation from egalitarian level (D_neg, D_pos) treated as continuous predictors.",Between-condition factor is SP vs TP; regressions use negative and positive deviations from transfer 50 as main covariates.,1,True,Human student participants in lab DG with punishment.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of who can punish whom.,1,True,Same lab environment and software (z-Tree).,1,False,No simulation for this design.,1,False,Empirical design only.,1,2,"Groups of two: dictator A and recipient/punisher B. Third parties only appear via cross-group mapping in TP condition, not within each pair.",0.9,1,Each group plays SP and TP once; each condition is a one-shot DG environment.,1,1,Dictator can transfer any integer amount; article notes that in this DG A could transfer up to more than 50 (broader than TP-DG but still not all-or-nothing).,0.9,0,All 100-point endowment starts with A; transfers are active decisions.,1,N/R,"Dictator game, not a public good; no MPCR defined.",1,0,No communication allowed; anonymity emphasized.,1,0,Subjects not shown over-time summaries; one-shot per condition.,1,0,Identities never revealed.,1,0,No reward option.,1,1,Subjects told game structure and that experiment consists of two conditions only.,0.9,1,Recipient B can assign costly deduction points to dictator A.,1,1,Punishment technology same as elsewhere: 1 point cost to punisher per deduction point.,0.9,3,Stated they kept deduction technology identical across experiments: 1 point reduces target's payoff by 3.,0.9,0,Only deduction (punishment) is available.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Player A endowed with 100 points in Stage 1; both A and B receive additional 50 points in Stage 2 but main transfer decision uses 100.,1,N/R,They report patterns of punishment and expected payoffs but not average transfer amounts specifically for the SP condition.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mean transfer under SP cited.,0.3,N/R,No group efficiency metric reported.,0.2,N/R,Average group earnings under SP not provided.,0.1,Switzerland,Same Zurich student pool.,1,N/R,No ages reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender info.,0.1,University students,Subjects are students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich.,1,On site,Lab setting using z-Tree.,0.95,"Paired groups (A,B) randomly assigned. Stage 1: A (100) transfers any amount to B (0). Stage 2: both receive additional 50. In SP condition, B (now with 50 + received transfer) can punish own dictator A after observing transfer. Punishment via deduction points with 1:3 cost/impact. Strategy method: B specifies punishment for all possible transfer levels. 92 subjects played both SP and TP; sequence counterbalanced. Experiment ~75 minutes; 1 point=CHF 0.13; average earnings CHF 30.",Synthesized from Section 4.1 procedures.,1
