ubjects are students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich.,1,On site,Same lab environment.,0.95,"Paired DGs arranged in triads of groups to avoid reciprocal cross-punishment. In TP condition, B first learns how much own dictator A transferred to B, then chooses punishment for out-group dictator A′ for each possible transfer from A′ to B′ via strategy method. Endowments: Stage 1 A:100, B:0. Stage 2 A,B each get 50. Punishment via deduction points 1:3. SP and TP conditions order counterbalanced but for main analysis only first condition observations used.",From Section 4.1 and regression discussion.,1
10.1016_s1090-5138(04)00005-4.md,"PD – Second-party punishment (SP condition, first in sequence)","Section 5 compares second- and third-party punishment in PD; this row is the SP condition when conducted first, which underlies Tables 5–8.",1,Punishment regime: Second-party punishment (A and B can punish each other). Outcome of own PD (cooperate/defect) and partner’s action serve as situational variables.,Main treatment factor is SP vs TP; conditional analyses split by PD outcomes.,1,True,Human subjects in PD with costly punishment.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of who can punish whom.,1,True,Same lab setup as earlier experiments.,1,False,No simulations run.,1,False,Empirical focus.,1,2,Each PD group has two players A and B who can punish each other in SP condition.,1,1,Each SP and TP condition is a single PD plus punishment stage.,1,0,Binary cooperate/defect: each either keeps all 10 or transfers all 10.,1,0,Players start with 10 each privately; cooperation is to transfer all.,1,1.5,As in TP-PD: transfer 10 → partner gets 30; MPCR to group = 30/(10*2)=1.5.,0.9,0,No communication between players mentioned.,1,0,One-shot; no multi-round histories.,1,0,Identities not revealed.,1,0,No reward option.,1,1,Subjects told about two conditions; each condition one-shot.,0.9,1,Stage 2: A and B can sanction each other.,1,1,Consistent deduction technology: 1 point cost per deduction point.,0.9,3,Each deduction point reduces punished player's income by 3 points.,0.9,0,Only sanctions are deduction points.,1,N/A,No rewards possible.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Stage 1 PD endowment is 10 per player; Stage 2 each receives an additional 20 but base cooperation choice is over 10.,1,N/R,"They analyze punishment, not overall cooperation rate in SP.",0.3,N/R,No average frequency of cooperation or transferred amount provided.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency index reported.,0.2,N/R,Only net profitability of defection vs cooperation is discussed qualitatively.,0.3,Switzerland,Same Zurich student pool.,1,N/R,Not given.,0.2,N/R,Not given.,0.1,University students,Subjects were students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich.,1,On site,Lab experiment using z-Tree.,0.95,"Two-stage PD. Stage 1: same PD as TP-PD (10 each, transfer-all-or-keep, transfer tripled). Stage 2: each player receives additional 20 points. In SP condition, Players A and B can sanction each other (second-party punishment) using deduction points with 1:3 cost/impact. Strategy method: each specifies punishment of partner for each PD outcome. 94 subjects; each played SP and TP, with order counterbalanced. Only first-condition data (SP-first) used for main comparisons. Exchange rate 1 point=CHF 0.3; average earnings CHF 33.40.",From Section 5.1 and subsequent results.,1
10.1016_s1090-5138(04)00005-4.md,"PD – Third-party punishment (TP condition, first in sequence)","Section 5 TP condition where each player can punish an out-group member only, used first in sequence for clean comparison.",1,Punishment regime: Third-party punishment (A punishes B″ from third group; B punishes A′ from another group). Outcome of punished player’s PD (and their partner’s action) are situational variables.,"Between-condition factor is SP vs TP; within TP, punished player’s and their partner’s actions define punishment levels.",1,True,Uses human subjects in PD with third-party sanctions.,1,True,Design systematically changes who the punishment target is relative to punisher’s own PD.,1,True,Conducted in same lab environment.,1,False,No simulation.,1,False,No analytical derivation here.,1,2,Within each TP punishment relation a single punisher sanctions a single out-group target drawn from a different PD pair.,0.8,1,Single PD with single subsequent punishment stage per condition.,1,0,Same PD action space: cooperate (transfer all) or defect (keep all).,1,0,Endowment of 10 is fully private.,1,1.5,Identical PD structure as SP PD condition and TP-PD.,0.9,0,Anonymity; no chat described.,1,0,No repeated interactions; no summaries.,1,0,Identities never revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Subjects informed about two conditions total; each condition is single-shot.,0.9,1,Each player can punish a member of another PD group.,1,1,Same deduction cost as in SP condition.,0.9,3,Each deduction point lowers target’s payoff by 3.,0.9,0,Only punishment (deduction points) supported.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,Each PD player starts with 10 points in Stage 1; 20 additional points provided in Stage 2.,1,N/R,They report punishment of defectors vs cooperators; do not give cooperation rates under TP PD.,0.3,N/R,No numerical cooperation frequency reported.,0.3,N/R,Group efficiency not quantified.,0.2,N/R,Report only comparative profitability of cooperation vs defection.,0.3,Switzerland,Same Zurich-based student subjects.,1,N/R,No age details.,0.2,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0.1,University students,All experiments use local university students.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment with z-Tree.,0.95,"Same two-stage PD as SP PD condition. Stage 2 endowment: 20 points per player. In TP condition, each player sanctions only an out-group member: B punishes A′ from another group, A punishes B″ from a third group, ruling out reciprocal 'you punish mine, I punish yours' strategies. Strategy method: for each possible PD outcome of the punished person and their partner, punisher specifies deduction points. Tables 5–8 report average punishment and proportion punishing for each case (e.g., defector with cooperating partner: 3.09 avg points, 58.7% punish).",From Section 5.1 and tables describing results.,1
