10.2307_1964229.md,"Sanctioning alone – High endowment (25TK, exogenous sanction after round 10, no communication)",Design 'SWORD WITHOUT COVENANTS: SANCTIONING ALONE' describes high-endowment experiments with sanctioning after round 10 and no communication.,1,"Endowment = 25 tokens; Communication = none; Sanctioning = imposed after round 10 with varying fee/fine levels (fee 5–40¢, fine 10–80¢, fee-to-fine ratio 0.25 or 0.5)",Treatment adds sanctioning mechanism only; parameters vary within this family but they are reported jointly.,0.8,True,Uses human participants.,1,True,Lab experiment with controlled sanctioning institution.,1,True,Conducted in a lab on novanet system.,1,False,No simulations here.,1,False,This row covers empirical sanctioning-only experiments.,1,8,Same 8-subject CPR game.,1,N/R,"They state sanctioning introduced after round 10 and experiments include rounds both before and after, but exact total rounds per experiment not specified.",0.3,1,Subjects choose any number of tokens to invest in Market 2.,1,0,All tokens start as private endowment.,1,N/R,MPCR not directly reported.,0,0,Sanctioning-alone design described as having no communication; only private fining forms and no discussion of joint strategies.,1,1,"In these sanctioning designs, 'after each round, subjects received individual data of all decisions' by subject number; this is rich feedback.",1,0,Sanctions are anonymous to the fined person; text: 'any subject who was fined did not know the identity of those who imposed the fine'.,1,0,No reward institution present.,1,0,Subjects not told exact number of decision rounds.,1,1,Sanctioning mechanism explicitly introduced with per-round fining opportunity.,1,N/R,Fees to levy fines vary by experiment (5¢–40¢); a single number not reported as common across all sanctioning-alone sessions.,0.3,N/R,"Fines imposed on target vary 10–80¢, with fee-to-fine ratio 0.25 or 0.5; no single parameter value.",0.3,0,Only negative sanctions (fines) are implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,25,High-endowment environment used in sanctioning-alone design.,1,N/R,"Data summarized in terms of yields and sanctioning frequencies, not contributions.",0,N/R,No averages of Market 2 tokens reported.,0,0.37,Table 3: 'Sanction 25TK' average net yield CPR = 37%.,1,N/R,Payoffs after sanctions not tabulated in raw units.,0,United States,Undergraduates at Indiana University.,1,N/R,Age not specified.,0.3,N/R,No gender information.,0,Undergraduate students,Subjects come from undergraduate population.,1,On site,Lab experiment using university computer system.,1,"High-endowment CPR with individual feedback on all players' investments and an anonymous fining system: each subject can levy at most one fine per round at a cost (fee), causing the target to pay a larger fine. Eight experiments with fee 5–40¢, fine 10–80¢, fee-to-fine ratio 0.25 or 0.5. Average net yield from CPR increased from −38% pre-sanctioning to 37% after introducing sanctioning, but when subtracting fees and fines, net yield is only 9%. 176 sanctioning instances observed; 77% directed at above-average CPR investors in previous round.",Derived from 'SWORD WITHOUT COVENANTS: SANCTIONING ALONE' and Table 3.,1
10.2307_1964229.md,"One-shot communication + imposed sanction – High endowment (25TK, sanction after round 10 plus communication)",Section 'Imposed Communication and Sanctioning' describes three experiments where sanctioning mechanism is imposed and a single communication period is provided after round 10.,1,Endowment = 25 tokens; Communication = single 10-minute discussion after round 10; Sanctioning = imposed after communication with experimentally set fee/fine levels (fee-to-fine ratio 0.5),Combined treatment: exogenous communication and exogenous sanctioning mechanism in high-endowment game.,0.8,True,Human-subject lab experiments.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of institutions.,1,True,Run in physical lab with face-to-face communication.,1,False,No simulations here.,1,False,Empirical treatment.,1,8,Same underlying 8-player CPR game.,1,N/R,"Design description gives sequence X,...,X, C, X-S,...,X-S, X, but total number of repetitions not clearly specified.",0.3,1,Players freely choose integer tokens for Market 2.,1,0,Tokens start as private endowment.,1,N/R,MPCR not reported.,0,1,Subjects have one 10-minute face-to-face discussion period.,1,1,"As in sanctioning-alone, subjects have individual data of all decisions and their own profits.",1,0,Sanctions are anonymous; fined subjects do not know who fined them.,1,0,No positive rewards.,1,0,Exact number of decision rounds not revealed.,1,1,Sanctioning mechanism is imposed and used to fine deviators in some groups.,1,N/R,"Two fee-to-fine ratios reported in joint analysis of endogenous and imposed sanction designs (10/20, 20/40 etc.); exact parameters for these three imposed-sanction experiments not individually specified.",0.3,N/R,Magnitude of fine per unit fee not uniquely reported for these specific experiments.,0.3,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,25,Experiments conducted in high-endowment environment.,1,N/R,Only yields and defection incidences described qualitatively.,0,N/R,"No average tokens reported, though specific example agreements (e.g., 4 tokens) discussed.",0.2,0.85,Table 3: 'One-shot communication sanction 25TK' average net yield CPR = 85%.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoff statistics not given.,0,United States,Indiana University undergraduates.,1,N/R,Age distribution not given.,0.3,N/R,No gender info.,0,Undergraduate students,Subjects are undergraduates.,1,On site,Lab setting with in-person discussions.,1,"High-endowment 25TK CPR with one-shot communication and imposed sanctioning. In one experiment, subjects agreed all would invest 4 tokens in Market 2 and fine any higher investment; they achieved 98% of maximum net yield without using sanctions. In another, they devised a rotation scheme (6 tokens each plus rotating extra 2 tokens) and fined three defections, achieving 86% gross (79% net after fines). In a third, they failed to coordinate a joint strategy, primarily discussed whom to fine, and used fines heavily; achieved 70% gross but only 24% net after fees and fines. Across all three, average net yields were 85% gross and 67% after sanction costs (Table 3). Defection rate from agreements is reported as 1%.",Summarized from 'Imposed Communication and Sanctioning' and Table 3.,1
10.2307_1964229.md,"One-shot communication with endogenous sanction choice – High endowment (25TK, groups that chose no sanction)",Section 'Imposed Communication and Endogenous Sanctioning' describes four experiments where subjects vote to adopt or reject a sanction mechanism; this row covers experiments where groups did NOT adopt sanctioning.,1,"Endowment = 25 tokens; Communication = single 10-minute discussion; Sanctioning = endogenous choice, not adopted (status quo: no sanctioning)",Treatment factor is whether groups endogenously adopt sanctioning; this row is for those that reject it.,1,True,Human-subject experiments.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of institutional choice.,1,True,Lab experiments at Indiana University.,1,False,No simulation-only results.,1,False,Empirical design.,1,8,Same 8-player CPR game.,1,25,Table 2 columns up to '21–25' for this row ('One-shot communication 25TK – No sanction chosen'); suggests at least 25 rounds with communication and choice at round 10.,0.7,1,Subjects can choose any number of tokens to invest.,1,0,Initial endowment fully private.,1,N/R,MPCR not provided.,0,1,One 10-minute face-to-face discussion before they vote on sanctioning.,1,1,"As in other high-endowment communication designs, subjects see own profits and group investment; plus often individual decision data by subject number.",1,0,No punishment mechanism is adopted; hence no IDs to show.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,Exact total rounds not revealed.,1,0,"Groups in this condition vote against implementing sanctioning, so only baseline CPR game remains.",1,N/A,No sanctioning institution.,1,N/A,No sanctioning institution.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,25,High-endowment game in this design.,1,N/R,Only yields and defection percentages are reported.,0,N/R,"Average contributions not tabulated, though an example agreement of 4 tokens in CPR is described.",0.2,0.56,Table 3 row 'One-shot communication 25TK – No sanction chosen' shows average net yield CPR = 56%.,1,N/R,No average payoffs.,0,United States,Undergraduates at Indiana University.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Subjects are undergraduates.,1,On site,Lab experiment with in-person communication.,1,"High-endowment 25TK CPR with one-shot communication and endogenous mechanism choice. In two groups where sanctioning was not chosen, one group initially agreed to invest 4 tokens in CPR (near optimal) but experienced rising defections (defection rate 42%), ending with average net yield 36% and high decay. The other group already had near-maximal yields before communication, refused sanctioning, and experienced gradual yield decline from ~90% to 56% because two subjects gradually increased Market 2 investments. Aggregate average net yield across 'no sanction chosen' groups was 56% (Table 3).",Drawn from 'Imposed Communication and Endogenous Sanctioning' section and Table 2/3.,1
10.2307_1964229.md,"One-shot communication with endogenous sanction choice – High endowment (25TK, groups that chose sanction, communication and choice after round 10)",Same design as previous row but for groups that adopted a sanctioning mechanism after a communication round at period 10.,1,Endowment = 25 tokens; Communication = single 10-minute discussion after round 10; Sanctioning = endogenously adopted after vote (fee-to-fine ratio 10¢/20¢ or 20¢/40¢),Groups vote to establish a sanction mechanism with specified fine/fee levels; this row covers those that chose sanctioning after round 10.,0.9,True,Human-subject lab experiments.,1,True,Experimental choice of institutional mechanism.,1,True,Conducted in a lab setting.,1,False,No simulation results here.,1,False,Empirical treatment.,1,8,Same 8-player CPR game.,1,25,"Table 2 shows yields for this row 'Sanction chosen' across rounds 1–25, with communication and sanction choice after round 10.",0.8,1,Players can invest any number of tokens up to 25 in CPR.,1,0,All tokens begin as private endowment.,1,N/R,MPCR not stated.,0,1,Single communication period before voting on sanction mechanism.,1,1,"Feedback of own profits, total group investment, and often individual decisions by subject number.",1,0,Sanctioning mechanism as earlier: fines are anonymized; fined subjects do not know which player fined them.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,Total number of rounds unknown to subjects.,1,1,Groups in this condition vote to adopt a sanctioning mechanism.,1,0.15,"Two fee-to-fine pairs used: 10¢/20¢ and 20¢/40¢, both with fee-to-fine ratio 0.5. Average fee across these is (0.10+0.20)/2=0.15 dollars; this is an approximation since the paper aggregates results.",0.4,0.3,Corresponding fines 20¢ and 40¢; average is (0.20+0.40)/2 = 0.30 dollars; again an approximate pooled parameter.,0.4,0,No reward institution.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,25,High-endowment CPR game.,1,N/R,Only yields and defection statistics are provided.,0,N/R,No averages of Market 2 tokens reported.,0,0.93,Table 3: 'One-shot communication 25TK – Sanction chosen' average net yield CPR = 93%.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff measures.,0,United States,Indiana University student subjects.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0.3,N/R,Gender not reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Subject pool is undergraduates.,1,On site,In-person lab experiment.,1,"High-endowment 25TK CPR where subjects, after 10 rounds of baseline play, are given a communication period and then vote to adopt a sanctioning mechanism. Two of four groups choose some sanctioning institution (fee-to-fine ratios 10¢/20¢ and 20¢/40¢). These groups develop joint investment strategies and use sanctions sparingly, achieving 91% of maximum net yield from CPR on average; yields fall slightly to 86% when costs of fees and fines are subtracted, and defection rate from agreements is only 4% (Table 3).",From 'Imposed Communication and Endogenous Sanctioning' section and Table 3.,1
10.2307_1964229.md,"One-shot communication with endogenous sanction choice – High endowment (25TK, groups that chose sanction, communication and choice at start)",Two additional experiments reported where communication and sanctioning choice occurred at the outset; these are aggregated in Table 2 row 'Sanction chosen c' and Table 3 under 'Sanction chosen' (combined).,0.9,Endowment = 25 tokens; Communication = single discussion before first round; Sanctioning = endogenously adopted at start (fee-to-fine ratio 0.5; specific levels not separately detailed),Hysteresis check: subjects communicate and vote on sanctioning before any play; in both experiments they quickly agree to a sanctioning mechanism.,0.8,True,Uses human lab participants.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of timing of communication and institutional choice.,1,True,On-site lab environment.,1,False,No simulations here.,1,False,Empirical data.,1,8,Same CPR design with 8 subjects.,1,25,Table 2 'Sanction chosen c' shows yields for rounds 11–26+ combined; experiments appear similar in horizon to others (around 25 rounds). Exact number not specified.,0.5,1,Token allocation is continuous up to endowment.,1,0,Tokens start as private holdings.,1,N/R,No MPCR reported.,0,1,Subjects have an opportunity to communicate and choose a sanctioning mechanism at the outset.,1,1,As in other high-endowment treatments: they receive feedback on investments and earnings.,1,0,Sanctions are anonymous to targets.,1,0,No reward institution.,1,0,Subjects still not informed about game length.,1,1,"In both early-choice experiments, groups choose a sanctioning mechanism.",1,N/R,Paper notes fee-to-fine ratio constraint of 1/2 but does not specify the exact chosen fee and fine levels for these two early-choice experiments.,0.3,N/R,Exact fine magnitude per unit fee not separately given for these two experiments.,0.3,0,No rewards described.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,25,High-endowment 25TK CPR.,1,N/R,Only efficiency measures reported.,0,N/R,No average token allocations reported.,0,0.968,"Table 2 'Sanction chosen c' shows yields in rounds 11–15, 16–20, 21–25, 26+ as 96.8%, 97.0%, 96.7%, 90.4%. Overall average in post-choice phase is approximately between 93% and 95% as summarized in text: 'net yields averaged 95%–94% with fees and fines included'; using mid value 0.968 reflects post-round-10 periods.",0.6,N/R,No raw payoff numbers.,0,United States,Indiana University undergraduates.,1,N/R,Age not provided.,0.3,N/R,No gender info.,0,Undergraduate students,Subjects from undergraduate pool.,1,On site,Laboratory setting.,1,"In these two experiments, subjects could communicate and choose a sanctioning mechanism before any play. In both, they quickly agreed on an investment strategy and a sanctioning mechanism to punish defectors. Across the two experiments, net yields (after accounting for fees and fines) averaged 94–95% of the maximum (text: 'Across the two experiments, net yields averaged 95%-94% with fees and fines included'). These sessions are included within the broader 'sanction chosen' category in Table 3.",From 'Imposed Communication and Endogenous Sanctioning' hysteresis check experiments and Table 2.,1
10.1016_j.physrep.2017.05.004.md,Spatial public goods game – null model on square lattice,Single baseline mathematical model in Section 3.1 describing spatial public goods game on a square lattice with group size G=5 and no punishment or reward.,1,Synergy factor r varied; structured vs well-mixed population (square lattice with G=5 vs random mixing).,Section 3.1 varies r and compares structured (lattice) to well-mixed; key threshold values r=3.74 and r=5.49 reported.,1,False,Model is purely mathematical/simulation-based with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical game model.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; only theoretical/simulation.,1,True,Dynamics studied via Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice as described in Section 4.,1,True,"Analytical thresholds for well-mixed case and benchmarks (r=G, r>3.74, r>5.49) are given.",0.9,25,"Local group size G=5 on square lattice; number of players in a game interaction equals group size, not total lattice size.",0.8,1000000,Section 4.1 states relaxation times up to 10^6 MCS to reach stationary state.,0.7,1,"Cooperators contribute c=1 and defectors 0; binary choice, but independent each round, treated as all-or-nothing discrete action.",0.9,N/A,No initial endowment allocation for human players; purely theoretical tokens.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return not given explicitly as a ratio; only multiplication factor r and group size G are given.,0.3,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,0,Players are simple strategies with no information display; environment is Markovian.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in null model.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism in null model.,1,0,Agents do not know horizon; infinite-time evolutionary process.,0.9,0,Baseline model has only C and D; no punishment terms.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No rewarding strategy in null model.,1,N/A,No rewarding.,1,N/A,No rewarding.,1,N/R,Only unit contributions c=1 are specified; no separate per-round endowment reported.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports survival thresholds and phase composition, not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,Average contributions not tabulated; focus is on fractions of strategies vs r.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios (payoff/maximum payoff) given.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff formula implied but no numerical summary reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation model without empirical data.,1,"Spatial public goods game on L×L square lattice with group size G=5, cooperators contribute c=1, defectors 0, sum multiplied by r and shared equally; strategy updating via pairwise imitation with Fermi rule and noise K.",Summarized from Section 3.1 and 4.1 where lattice structure and update dynamics are specified.,1
10.1016_j.physrep.2017.05.004.md,"Spatial public goods game with peer punishment – (C,D,P) on square lattice","Section 3.2.1 and 5.1 define a public goods game with three strategies: cooperators C, defectors D, and peer punishers P.",1,"Punishment fine β and punishment cost γ varied; synergy factor r fixed in specific analyses (e.g., r=3.8).",Phase diagram in Fig. 12 is plotted on β–γ plane for fixed r=3.8; other r values discussed qualitatively.,1,False,Model implemented via Monte Carlo simulations; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment; theoretical evolutionary game.,1,False,No physical lab or field setting involved.,1,True,Uses Monte Carlo simulation with random sequential strategy updating on a lattice.,1,False,"Critical exponents and universality class discussed, but phase diagram itself obtained via simulation, not closed-form derivation.",0.7,5,Group size in each public goods interaction is G=5 on square lattice as in null model; peer punishment is added but group size unchanged.,0.9,1000000,Section 4.1: relaxation times up to 10^6 MCS used generally; same Monte Carlo procedure applied here.,0.7,1,"C and P contribute c=1, D contributes 0; binary contribution choice each game.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation for human players; theoretical tokens only.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly reported; only r and G given.,0.3,N/A,No communication; non-human strategies.,1,0,Agents have no display of others' payoffs or histories; only local payoffs used.,0.9,0,Identity and observability not modelled; punishment effects only via payoff terms.,0.9,N/A,No rewarding in this model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary process; no round counter available to agents.,0.9,1,Peer punishers P fine defectors as defined in payoff equations.,1,1,Model defines cost parameter γ; punishment cost per defector punished is γ/(G−1). Use γ as unit cost parameter (set to 1 as scale placeholder since absolute units not specified).,0.4,1,"Each P imposes fine β/(G−1) per defector; use β as magnitude parameter, normalized here as 1 placeholder due to unspecified units.",0.4,0,No rewarding cooperators in this model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not separately specified; only contributions (c=1) and payoff functions given.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports fractions of C and P but not explicit normalized contribution rates; both C and P fully contribute so rate could be inferred, but not tabulated.",0.4,N/R,No numerical averages for total contributions per group reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric computed; results focus on phase composition and critical behavior.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff distributions not numerically summarized.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Lattice-based Monte Carlo simulation only.,1,"Spatial public goods game on L×L square lattice, group size G=5. Strategies: C, D, P. C and P contribute c=1, D contributes 0. Total group contributions multiplied by r and divided equally. In each group, each P pays cost γ/(G−1) per defector, each defector pays fine β/(G−1) per punisher. Agents update strategies by imitating neighbors via Fermi rule with noise K.","Summarized from Sections 3.2.1, 4.1 and 5.1.",1
