10.1016_j.physrep.2017.05.004.md,"Spatial public goods game with adaptive punishment – (C,D) with variable punishing activity",Section 3.2.3 and 7.1 define a two-strategy public goods game with cooperators C having adaptive punishing activity μx against defectors D.,1,Synergy factor r and incremental punishment step Δ varied; punishment cost parameter α also varied.,Phase diagrams in Fig. 21 are plotted in r–Δ planes for different α values.,1,False,Adaptive punishment model studied via simulations only.,1,False,No laboratory or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo on lattice with evolving μx values.,1,False,Phase diagrams obtained numerically; no full analytical solution.,0.7,5,Model builds directly on spatial public goods game with group size G=5.,0.9,1000000,Uses same Monte Carlo protocol (up to 10^6 MCS) as in Section 4.1.,0.7,1,"Cooperators contribute c=1, defectors 0.",1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly provided.,0.3,N/A,No communication channels.,1,0,No summary display; only local payoffs used.,0.9,0,Punishment actions not attributed via observable identities; only payoff modifications.,0.9,N/A,Model has no positive rewards.,1,0,Evolutionary infinite time horizon; no knowledge of total rounds.,0.9,1,Cooperators punish defectors adaptively with fines proportional to μx Δ.,1,1,Cost term is μx αΔ/(G−1) per punished defector; α controls relative cost. Use α as cost scale (unit placeholder).,0.4,1,Defectors are fined μy Δ/(G−1) summed over punishing cooperators; Δ determines incremental fine size; use Δ as magnitude with unit placeholder.,0.4,0,"Adaptive model here only for punishment, not rewarding.",1,N/A,No reward terms.,1,N/A,No reward terms.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment size given.,0.3,N/R,All cooperators contribute c=1 while present; no normalized contribution rate reported.,0.4,N/R,No numeric contribution statistics provided.,0.3,N/R,"Paper describes that high cooperation can be achieved with globally negligible punishment costs, but no quantified efficiency ratio.",0.4,N/R,No explicit group payoff numbers reported.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Study is computational.,1,"Spatial public goods game with C and D; each cooperator x has dynamic punishing activity μx (initially 0). When D successfully invades a C, all remaining cooperators in groups containing the defeated C increase μx by 1; if defectors fail to spread, μx decreases by 1 every second round, bounded below by 0. In each group g, a cooperator x pays cost μx αΔ/(G−1) per defector, and each defector is fined sum over neighboring μy Δ/(G−1). Payoffs: ΠC^g = r NC/G −1 − (ND μx αΔ)/(G−1); ΠD^g = r NC/G − (Σy∈g μy Δ)/(G−1). Strategies updated via Fermi imitation.",Summarized from Section 3.2.3 and Section 7.1 (adaptive punishment).,1
10.1016_j.physrep.2017.05.004.md,"Spatial public goods game with adaptive rewarding – (C,D,R) with variable rewarding activity",Section 3.3.3 and 7.3 describe a game where rewarding cooperators R have adaptive rewarding activity μx.,1,"Reward increment Δ, reward cost ratio α, and synergy factor r varied.",Phase diagram in Fig. 25 is Δ–α at fixed r=3.5; other r values discussed.,1,False,No human data; model is simulated.,1,False,"Study is theoretical, not an experiment.",1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice.,1,False,Phase behavior determined numerically; no full analytical solution.,0.7,5,Model extends public goods game with G=5 groups.,0.9,1000000,Uses same MCS scale (10^3–10^6) for stationarity as other models.,0.7,1,"Cooperators (C and R) contribute 1, defectors 0.",1,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly provided.,0.3,N/A,No communication.,1,0,Only local payoff-based imitation.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism; only rewards.,1,0,Rewards not tied to visible identities; purely payoff-based.,0.9,0,No round information to agents.,0.9,0,Only adaptive rewarding implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Rewarding cooperators grant benefits to cooperators with adaptive intensity μx.,1,1,Each rewarding cooperator x pays cost μx αΔ/(G−1) per cooperator rewarded; α is relative cost factor.,0.4,1,Rewards from each rewarding cooperator i are μi Δ/(G−1) per cooperator; Δ controls magnitude.,0.4,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment.,0.3,N/R,All cooperators contribute fully; no separate normalized contribution statistics.,0.4,N/R,Contribution amounts not summarized numerically.,0.3,N/R,Paper notes that adaptive rewarding promotes cooperation but does not report explicit efficiency values.,0.3,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Fully computational exploration of strategy dynamics.,1,"Public goods game on square lattice with strategies C, D, and adaptive rewarding cooperators R. Each R has rewarding activity μx (initially 0). When a defector successfully invades, all remaining rewarding cooperators in affected groups increase μx by 1; every second round μx decreases by 1 if μx>0. In each group, cooperators receive rewards from rewarding cooperators proportional to μi Δ/(G−1), and each R pays costs μi αΔ/(G−1) per rewarded cooperator. Payoffs: ΠC^g = r(NC+NR+1)/G −1 + (Δ/(G−1)) Σi∈g μi; ΠR^g = ΠC^g − (αΔ/(G−1)) μx (NC+NR); ΠD^g = r(NC+NR)/G. Evolution via Fermi imitation.",From Section 3.3.3 equations and discussion in Section 7.3.,1
10.1016_j.physrep.2017.05.004.md,"Spatial public goods game with diverse tolerance levels – 8-strategy model (C,D,L,M0–M3)","Section 3.5 and 9.2 describe an 8-strategy game with cooperators, defectors, loners, and 4 tolerant strategies Mi differing in tolerance threshold i.",1,"Synergy factor r and inspection cost γ varied; tolerance thresholds i=0,…,G−1 are fixed strategy traits.","Phase diagram in Fig. 33 plotted in r–γ plane, showing which combinations of tolerant strategies survive.",1,False,No human data; only simulated populations on lattice.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory environment.,1,True,"Monte Carlo simulations on large lattices (e.g., up to L=6000) with prepared initial conditions.",1,False,Phase transitions and fixation probabilities determined numerically.,0.7,5,Public goods groups of size G (example uses G=5 implicitly as default in the public goods framework).,0.8,1000000,"Simulations use up to 10^6 MCS for relaxation, especially near phase transitions and in stability analysis (Section 4.4).",0.7,1,"Cooperative actions are binary (either contribute c=1 or not, depending on tolerance rule).",1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,"Synergy factor r defined, but MPCR not given as separate value.",0.3,N/A,No communication amongst strategies.,1,0,Information limited to local composition; no high-level summaries.,0.9,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,0,"Infinite-horizon dynamics, no round counter to agents.",0.9,0,No explicit punishment; only opting out (loners) and conditional participation (tolerant players).,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,No explicit rewarding; only baseline public good returns.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Endowment not specified separately from contribution c=1.,0.3,N/R,"Paper focuses on phase composition and average payoffs, not on explicit normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No aggregated contribution data provided.,0.3,N/R,"Text states that certain tolerant phases provide maximal average payoffs, but does not report numeric efficiency ratios.",0.4,N/R,Average group payoff values not tabulated.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Lattice-based simulation without human subjects.,1,"Public goods game with strategies: cooperator C, defector D, loner L, and tolerant players Mi (i=0…G−1). Loners abstain from game and receive fixed payoff σ=1. Tolerant Mi inspects group at cost γ; if number of defectors ND < i, Mi participates as cooperator (contributes 1); otherwise Mi acts as loner and does not contribute but still pays inspection cost γ. Total contributors in group: NTC = NC + Σi δi NMi, where δi=1 if ND<i else 0. Public goods: r*NTC divided among all non-loners (C and Mi who cooperate). Payoffs: ΠD = r NTC/(ND+NTC); ΠC = ΠD −1; ΠL = σ; ΠMi = δi ΠC + (1−δi)σ − γ. Dynamics via Fermi imitation on square lattice; large system sizes used for stability analysis of subsystem solutions.",Equations and description in Section 3.5 and detailed dynamic analysis in Section 9.2.,1
10.1126_science.1153808.md,No-punishment condition (N experiment),Paper states there are two treatment conditions: a no-punishment condition (the N experiment) and a punishment condition (the P experiment); this refers to the no-punishment version of the public goods game.,1,Punishment opportunity: absent (standard public goods game without sanctioning stage).,The only treatment difference described is the existence of a punishment stage; in N it is explicitly absent.,1,True,"Human participants (university undergraduates, n = 1120) played the game.",1,True,"Controlled, randomized public goods game treatments implemented by experimenters.",1,True,"Described as experimental economics study with university undergraduates and computer-mediated interactions, consistent with standard lab experiments; no mention of field setting.",0.94,False,"All results discussed are from human-subject experiments, not agent-based or numerical simulations.",1,False,No formal mathematical model or closed-form derivations used as the primary method; focus is on experimental data.,1,4,"Paper: ""Groups of four members played the following public goods game in both conditions.""",1,10,"Paper: ""we therefore repeated the experiment 10 times under both conditions, keeping the group composition constant.""",1,1,Participants decided how many tokens (0–20) to contribute; implies a continuous (any-amount) choice rather than forced all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment is given as 20 tokens per period in a private account from which they choose how many to contribute; default is fully private.,0.95,0.4,"Paper: ""Each of the four group members earned 0.4 tokens for each token invested in the project."" This is the marginal per capita return.",1,0,"No mention of communication; standard Fehr-Gächter style public goods experiments forbid communication, and interactions were anonymous and computer-mediated.",0.9,1,Participants were informed about other group members' contributions each period; this is a summary of others' behavior.,1,0,"No punishment in this treatment, so there are no punishment identities to show.",1,0,No reward mechanism is described in either condition.,1,1,"Not explicitly stated, but repeated-game public goods experiments virtually always tell subjects the number of periods; design references period 1, 2–10, and final period, implying known horizon.",0.8,0,"Paper: ""the N experiment ended after participants were informed about the other group members' contributions"" and explicitly contrasts it with P where punishment is possible.",1,N/A,No punishment option in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this treatment.,1,0,Only punishment is discussed; no reward/positive sanction mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,"Paper: ""Each member received an endowment of 20 tokens.""",1,N/R,Paper gives ranges of mean contributions in tokens (4.9 to 11.5 out of 20) but not an overall normalized contribution rate across all subject pools; no single aggregate proportion is reported.,0.5,N/R,Only ranges and comparative statements across participant pools and periods are given in the main text; no single aggregate mean contribution for N across all pools is reported.,0.5,N/R,No statement of earnings relative to the social optimum for the N experiment alone; only some comparisons and percentages in P are discussed.,0.2,N/R,Paper mentions per-period earnings differences mainly for the punishment condition and does not report a specific group payoff figure for N.,0.2,Multiple (16 participant pools in different countries),"The study uses ""16 comparable participant pools around the world""; specific countries listed only indirectly via external indices and are not individually enumerated in the main text excerpt.",0.7,"Primarily university undergraduates, typical age late teens to mid-20s (exact ages N/R)","Paper: ""we conducted all experiments with university undergraduates (n = 1120) who were similar in age""; no exact age range is specified.",0.9,N/R,No information on gender composition in the main text.,0.1,University undergraduates (upper/middle class background),"Paper: ""we conducted all experiments with university undergraduates ... shared an (upper) middle class background.""",1,On site,"Computer-mediated, lab-style public goods experiment with student subjects physically recruited; no indication of remote/online participation.",0.9,"Linear public goods game with fixed groups of four, 20-token endowment per period, contribution to group project yields 0.4 tokens per token to each group member (social return 1.6), repeated 10 periods with constant group composition, no punishment or reward stage.",Summarizes structural details explicitly described for the N experiment portion of the public goods design.,1
10.1126_science.1153808.md,Punishment condition (P experiment),"Paper: ""two treatment conditions: a no-punishment condition (the N experiment) and a punishment condition (the P experiment)""; this refers to the punishment version.",1,Punishment opportunity: present (public goods game with costly punishment stage).,P condition differs from N only by adding a punishment stage after contribution information is revealed.,1,True,Human subjects (same participant pools of university undergraduates) played the game.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatment conditions and fixed parameters.,1,True,"Same experimental setting as N: laboratory-style, computer-mediated interactions with student subjects.",0.94,False,No indication that the reported P-condition results are from simulations.,1,False,Focus is on empirical data rather than analytical modeling.,1,4,"""Groups of four members played the following public goods game in both conditions.""",1,10,"Paper: ""we therefore repeated the experiment 10 times under both conditions, keeping the group composition constant.""",1,1,Same contribution decision as in N: any number of tokens from 0 to 20 can be contributed.,1,0,Endowment is initially in the private account; contributions are decided each period.,0.95,0.4,Same public goods production function: 0.4 tokens per contributed token to each group member.,1,0,No communication mentioned; anonymity and standard punishment design (Fehr & Gächter style) normally disallow chat.,0.9,1,Participants are explicitly informed about all other group members' contributions before the punishment stage.,1,0,"Paper: ""Participants were not informed about who punished them,"" implying no identity revelation.",1,0,Only a costly punishment mechanism is implemented; no reward stage.,1,1,"As with N, design refers to period 1, 2–10, and ""final period"" in analysis, indicating subjects knew the finite horizon.",0.8,1,"Paper: ""participants in the P experiment could punish each of the other group members after they were informed about the others' investments.""",1,1,"Paper: ""Each deduction point assigned reduced the punished member's earnings by three tokens and cost the punishing member one token."" Cost per punishment unit is 1 token.",1,3,Same sentence: each deduction point reduces punished member's earnings by three tokens; one token spent yields 3 tokens reduction.,1,0,No positive sanctioning or rewarding described; only negative deduction points.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented in the experiment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented in the experiment.,1,20,"Same as N: ""Each member received an endowment of 20 tokens.""",1,N/R,"Paper gives ranges of average contributions (e.g., most cooperative pool contributes 90% of endowment; least cooperative 29%) but not a single aggregate normalized rate for all participant pools in P.",0.5,N/R,"Only cross-pool ranges (e.g., 29% to 90% of 20 tokens) and comparative results are given; no pooled mean amount for P is reported in the excerpt.",0.5,N/R,"Paper mentions that earnings differ by more than 250 percentage points between best and worst participant pools in P, but does not convert this into an explicit efficiency ratio relative to the social optimum.",0.3,N/R,Only relative earnings differences (percentages) are given; no specific numeric group payoff per period or overall is reported.,0.3,Multiple (16 participant pools in different countries),"Same participant pools as in N: ""16 comparable participant pools around the world.""",0.7,"Primarily university undergraduates, typical age late teens to mid-20s (exact ages N/R)",Same subject pool description applies to both N and P conditions.,0.9,N/R,Gender composition not reported in the main text.,0.1,University undergraduates (upper/middle class background),Same as in N condition description.,1,On site,"Same lab-style setting as N: computer-mediated, anonymous interactions among students.",0.9,"Linear public goods game with fixed groups of four, 20-token endowment per period, contribution to group project yields 0.4 tokens per token to each group member (social return 1.6), repeated 10 periods with constant group composition. After observing all group members' contributions each period, participants can assign 0–10 deduction points to each other member; each deduction point costs the punisher 1 token and reduces the target's payoff by 3 tokens. Punishment decisions are simultaneous and anonymous.",Summarizes all structural details of the P experiment given in the design description.,1
10.1098_rsif.2012.0997.md,Generic spatial public goods game on lattices (reviewed models),The paper is a narrative review summarizing many prior models without fully specifying any single experiment or simulation as a self-contained design; thus a single generic condition is extracted to reflect the canonical lattice public-goods setup described in Section 2.,0.4,"Synergy factor r; group size G determined by lattice type (e.g., G=5 square, G=4 honeycomb, G=5 kagome, G=7 triangular); noise/uncertainty K in Fermi update rule; lattice topology (square, honeycomb, kagome, triangular); sometimes group size varied (e.g., G=5 vs G=301); critical mass M in some variants; presence/absence of additional strategies (punishers, rewarders, loners, conditional cooperators, etc.).","The methods section on lattices defines r, G, K, and topology as primary parameters. Later subsections describe variants that add other independent variables like critical mass M and additional strategies.",0.7,False,"All studies described are theoretical or computational (lattices, networks, agent-based models, differential equations) with no human or animal subjects in experiments; microbes and humans are discussed conceptually only.",1,False,No controlled lab or field experiments are conducted; the work is a review of simulations and analytical models.,1,False,No laboratory setting with human participants is used; only simulations and theory are discussed.,1,True,"Core results are Monte Carlo simulations of public-goods games on lattices and networks; the review repeatedly refers to simulation procedures (Monte Carlo steps, system size L, etc.).",1,True,"The review also discusses analytical approaches such as the replicator equation and mean-field results, though most lattice results are numerical; thus both simulation and analytical components exist.",0.7,0,Only population size scaling N ∝ L^2 and ranges of L (200–1600) are given; exact N is not fixed and varies by study; cannot assign a single numeric value.,0.2,0,"Simulations are run until a stationary state for 10^4–10^6 Monte Carlo steps, but there is no discrete finite number of game rounds as in human experiments; a single numeric count is not well-defined.",0.3,1,"Players contribute a fixed amount a=1 if cooperator and 0 if defector; there is no option of choosing intermediate contribution levels, so contribution is effectively binary rather than continuous, but within the provided coding, this is closer to an all-or-nothing restriction; field definition reverses (1 any amount, 0 all-or-nothing), so value is set to 0 in the next field and 1 here is a placeholder not used.",0,N/A,This parameter pertains to initial allocation of endowment for human participants; here there are no endowments with human players but model tokens; field instructed as N/A for no humans.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is r/G in the basic model, but r and G vary across implementations and no single canonical numeric value is specified.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocols are modeled; agent strategies update via imitation rules without chat.,1,0,Agents only observe payoffs of self and a neighbor in the update rule; there is no explicit display of aggregate summaries as in human interfaces.,0.7,N/A,Identity revelation is a human-interface concept; here punishers and defectors interact via rules without explicit notion of IDs being shown.,1,N/A,Same reasoning as punishment IDs; no human interface.,1,0,Simulated agents are not informed about total or remaining Monte Carlo steps; horizon knowledge is not modeled.,0.8,0,"The baseline spatial public-goods specification in Section 2 describes only cooperators and defectors without punishment; punishment is treated later as an optional strategic extension, but not in the generic base game extracted here.",0.6,N/A,Punishment is not part of the core configuration for this generic condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism included in the base configuration.,1,0,Baseline model has only C and D; rewarding strategies are separately discussed but not included in this canonical condition.,0.7,N/A,Rewards not present in the base configuration.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the base configuration.,1,N/R,"The lattice model specifies contributions a and payoffs directly, not per-round endowments in tokens; no numeric endowment is defined.",0.3,N/R,Outcomes are reported as fraction of cooperators ρC and phase diagrams; no normalized contribution rate beyond ρC is given as a numeric value that can be extracted.,0.3,N/R,"Cooperators always contribute a=1 and defectors 0, but no aggregate or average numeric amount per player is reported for a specific parameter set.",0.4,N/R,The review qualitatively describes promotion of cooperation and efficiency but does not report explicit ratios of realized to maximum payoffs for a specific condition.,0.2,N/R,"Payoff formulas are provided, but no single numeric outcome for group payoff is reported that corresponds to a specific parameter combination.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"All results are from simulations and analytical models; no lab, online, or field environment with human subjects.",1,"Canonical spatial public-goods game on lattices: players on sites of a lattice (e.g., square, honeycomb, kagome, triangular) belong to overlapping groups of size G=k+1, start as cooperators (s_i=1) or defectors (s_i=0) with equal probability, cooperators contribute a=1 (defectors 0) to each group, group contributions are multiplied by synergy factor r, and payoffs are shared equally; net payoff is sum over groups. Strategy updating uses a pairwise comparison (Fermi rule) with noise K: a randomly chosen player i and a neighbor j compute payoffs Pi, Pj, and i adopts j’s strategy with probability 1/(1+exp[(Pi-Pj)/(GK)]). Simulations run on large lattices (L from 200 to 1600) for 10^4–10^6 Monte Carlo steps to stationarity; many variants add different strategies, heterogeneities, benefit functions, and coevolution but share this core structure.","Summarized from Section 2 (Lattices) including the payoff specification, group formation, Fermi update, and simulation protocol.",0.9
10.1073_pnas.0630443100.md,"Base simulation with punishment (p=0.8, k=0.2, c=0.2, e=0.02, m=0.01, μ=0.01, ε=0.015)","Main simulation configuration with altruistic punishment described under Methods as base case parameters where p=0.8, k=0.2, c=0.2, e=0.02, m=0.01, μ=0.01, ε=0.015, 128 groups, one punisher group and 127 defector groups; used for Fig.1b, Fig.2b, Fig.3 (p=0.8 condition), Fig.4 (variable-cost punishment).",0.84,"Presence and structure of punishment: punishment enabled with variable cost proportional to number of defectors (punisher cost k=0.2 per punished group member, punishee cost p=0.8 per punishment), cooperation cost c=0.2, error rate e=0.02, migration rate m=0.01, mutation μ=0.01, conflict rate ε=0.015; group selection via extinction depending on defector frequency.",Independent variables are the punishment mechanism and evolutionary parameters explicitly specified in the Methods as the base case used to explore group size and other sensitivities when punishment is present.,0.86,False,Study is entirely based on computer simulations of evolutionary dynamics; no human participants.,1,False,"Model-based simulation, not a controlled behavioral experiment with subjects.",1,False,No laboratory or field setting with human participants; only simulations.,1,True,Authors explicitly state they used two independent simulation programs implementing the model.,1,False,"While some analytical intuition is given, the results are derived via numerical simulation rather than closed-form analysis.",0.92,100,"Text: ""With parameter values chosen to represent cultural evolution in small-scale societies, cooperation is sustained in groups on the order of 100 individuals."" The base case results with punishment are described for such groups; model earlier denotes group size as n but does not give a specific numeric base other than this mapping to ~100 individuals.",0.5,2000,"Methods: ""Simulations were run for 2,000 time periods."" Interactions per time period are abstracted, but 2,000 periods are the number of modeled rounds.",0.9,1,Agents have a fixed strategy type (contribute or defect) with cooperation occurring probabilistically; there is no continuous choice of contribution level—effectively an all-or-nothing contribution decision each interaction.,0.7,N/A,No explicit endowments or public goods account; payoffs are abstract fitness-like quantities in a simulation with no initial allocation.,1,N/R,Public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is not defined in this model; cooperation reduces extinction risk but is initially assumed to have no direct payoff benefit to others.,1,N/A,No human participants; no communication protocol is modeled.,1,N/A,Simulation agents do not have interfaces or displays; concept not applicable.,1,N/A,No identities or displays for simulated agents; punishment is modeled as payoff changes only.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this model.,1,N/A,No humans or interface; agents do not see a remaining-round counter.,1,1,Punishers pay cost k to reduce defectors’ payoff by p; this is the central mechanism in the base simulation.,1,0.2,"Methods: ""we set the cost of cooperation, c, and punishing, k, ... (c=k=0.2).""",1,0.8,"Methods: ""we set the cost of being punished to four times the cost of punishing (p=0.8)."" Each act of punishment reduces defector payoff by p.",1,0,Model includes only punishment and cooperation/defection; no rewarding mechanism described.,1,N/A,Reward is absent from the model.,1,N/A,Reward is absent from the model.,1,N/R,Model uses abstract payoffs without specifying initial endowments or token budgets.,1,N/R,Results report long-run average frequency of cooperation in populations but do not give precise numeric values for a single parameter set; only qualitative and graphical descriptions.,0.3,N/R,No token amounts per contribution are defined; cooperation is a binary behavior with associated cost c.,1,N/R,Efficiency relative to a maximum is not computed; outcomes are reported as frequencies of cooperation and punishment.,1,N/R,"Group average payoffs are discussed qualitatively (e.g., groups with more cooperators do better) but no numeric payoff levels are reported.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is conducted entirely via computer simulations of theoretical models.,1,"Population partitioned into N=128 groups of constant size n maintained by density-dependent competition; three types of individuals: contributors, defectors, and punishers. Stage 1: contributors and punishers cooperate with probability 1−e (pay cost c and increase group’s competitive ability), defectors always defect. Stage 2: punishers punish each individual who defected in stage 1, paying cost k/n per punished individual and reducing each defector’s payoff by p/n. After the game, individuals interact pairwise with within-group probability 1−m or between-group probability m and may imitate partners with probability Wj/(Wi+Wj). Group selection via intergroup conflict with probability ε per paired groups; group with more defectors more likely to go extinct. Mutation with rate μ causes individuals to switch randomly among the three strategies.",Summary of the game and evolutionary process from the model description under Methods and the introductory model section.,0.96
10.1073_pnas.0630443100.md,"No-punishment simulation baseline (p=0, k=0)","Condition described in Fig.1a and Fig.2a as simulations ""when there is no punishment (p=k=0)"" to show group selection sustaining cooperation only in small groups or low mixing.",0.85,"Punishment disabled: p=0, k=0; contributors incur cooperation cost c=0.2, defectors incur no cost. Other base parameters as in main model: e=0.02, m varies in sensitivity analyses, μ=0.01, ε varies in sensitivity analyses; group selection via extinction depends on defector frequency.",Independent variable is presence/absence of punishment. This row represents the no-punishment control where punishment parameters are set to zero.,0.86,False,Simulation study with no human subjects.,1,False,Numerical simulation rather than a controlled behavioral experiment.,1,False,No laboratory environment; just computational models.,1,True,Condition implemented via computer simulation as part of the same model framework.,1,False,"Results are obtained by simulation, not by closed-form mathematical solution.",0.9,100,Same underlying model as base case; mapping to ~100 individuals per group as discussed in the Discussion section.,0.5,2000,"""Simulations were run for 2,000 time periods"" applies generally, including no-punishment runs.",0.9,1,Individuals either cooperate (pay cost c) or defect; there is no graded contribution level.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments or public fund are modeled.,1,N/R,Model does not define a marginal per capita return for a public good; cooperation affects extinction probabilities instead.,1,N/A,No communication among simulated agents is described.,1,N/A,Not applicable to simulated agents without interfaces.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism is disabled (p=k=0); also no notion of identities displayed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No human players; round information display not applicable.,1,0,Explicitly defined condition with p=k=0 so that punishers have no effect; effectively no punishment.,1,N/A,Punishment is turned off by setting p=k=0; there is no active punishment decision or cost.,1,N/A,Punishment does not operate in this condition.,1,0,Rewards are not part of the model in any condition.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit endowments in the payoff specification.,1,N/R,"Figures show qualitative curves of cooperation as a function of group size and conflict/mixing rates, but do not report numerical values for a specific parameter combination.",0.3,N/R,No token-based contributions defined; only a fixed cost c per cooperative action.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics are not computed or reported.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported in terms of frequencies of cooperation, not payoffs.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is part of the same computational simulation framework.,1,"Same population and group structure as base model (N=128 groups, constant size n). Only two behavioral types effectively matter: contributors (cooperate with probability 1−e, paying cost c and reducing group extinction risk) and defectors (always defect). No punishment stage is active because p=k=0, so second-stage punishment actions do not change payoffs. Imitation, migration (rate m), conflict (rate ε), and mutation (rate μ) operate as in the main model.","Derived from base model description, modified by explicit statement that p=k=0 for no-punishment runs.",0.96
10.1073_pnas.0630443100.md,"Punishment with reduced punishee cost (p=2k, i.e., p=0.4, k=0.2)","Condition described in Results and Fig.3: ""When the cost of being punished is twice the cost of cooperation (p=2k)""; with base c=k=0.2 implies p=0.4.",0.88,"Punishment enabled with lower punishee cost: k=0.2, p=0.4 (p=2k); other base parameters as in main model (c=0.2, e=0.02, m=0.01, μ=0.01, ε=0.015).",Independent variable is the punishment severity p relative to k; this condition halves p compared to base case while keeping other parameters constant.,0.9,False,Simulation with no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical model run.,1,False,No laboratory context.,1,True,Condition is implemented in the same simulation framework with altered p.,1,False,"Outcomes are described from simulations (Fig.3), not closed-form derivations.",0.9,100,Uses same group-size interpretation as base case; model parameters otherwise unchanged.,0.5,2000,"Applies same simulation length of 2,000 time periods.",0.9,1,Cooperation is binary as in base model.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,Public-good multiplier not defined.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No interface for simulated agents.,1,N/A,No identity display; abstract simulation.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,1,Punishment mechanism operates with modified p=0.4.,1,0.2,k is unchanged from base case: k=0.2.,1,0.4,"p is defined as being twice k (and k=0.2), giving p=0.4.",0.95,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments not defined.,1,N/R,Fig.3 is qualitative; it shows lower cooperation than base p=0.8 case but provides no exact frequencies.,0.3,N/R,"Contributions are costs, not token quantities.",1,N/R,Efficiency not reported numerically.,1,N/R,Only cooperation frequencies discussed.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Simulated agents only.,1,"Same structure as base punishment model: contributors, defectors, punishers; two-stage game with cooperation in stage 1 and punishment of defectors in stage 2; imitation, migration, conflict, and mutation as before. Only difference is that each unit of punishment inflicts a lower cost on defectors (p=0.4 instead of 0.8), reducing the deterrence effect and thereby weakening the correlated selection on punishment.",Directly described in Fig.3 narrative as varying p while holding other aspects constant.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.0630443100.md,Punishment with variable cost vs fixed cost equal to c (sensitivity in Fig.4),"Fig.4 compares three cost structures: variable cost punishment (as in base), fixed punishment cost equal to cooperation cost c, and no punishment. This row captures the fixed-cost punishment condition relative to the variable-cost base.",0.79,"Structure of punishment cost: punishers bear a fixed cost equal to the cooperation cost c each period (independent of number of defectors), rather than a cost proportional to number of defectors; p and other base parameters (c=0.2, e=0.02, m=0.01, μ=0.01, ε=0.015) otherwise as in main model.","Independent variable is whether punishment costs are variable (per punished defector) or fixed. Text: ""Punishment does not aid in the evolution of cooperation when the costs born by punishers are fixed, independent of the number of defectors in the group... fixed at a constant cost equal to the cost of cooperating (c).""",0.86,False,Simulation only.,1,False,"Model-based, not empirical.",1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Alternative cost structure implemented in simulation (Fig.4).,1,False,Outcomes illustrated via simulations in Fig.4.,0.9,100,Same mapping of group size to ~100 individuals as base model.,0.5,2000,Uses same simulation protocol length.,0.9,1,Cooperation remains binary.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,Simulation without identity displays.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,Punishers still exist and punish defectors; only the way costs are levied changes.,1,0.2,"Text: ""fixed at a constant cost equal to the cost of cooperating (c)""; c=0.2 in base parameters.",0.95,0.8,"Text implies they compare fixed cost against the same variable cost case (p=0.8, k=0.2); p not stated to change, so punishee cost remains 0.8 per punisher per defector interaction conceptually, while punisher pays fixed cost c.",0.65,0,No rewards modeled in Fig.4 scenario.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments not defined.,1,N/R,"Fig.4 qualitatively shows that cooperation is not sustained under fixed-cost punishment, but no numeric cooperation rates are provided.",0.3,N/R,No contributions in tokens specified.,1,N/R,Efficiency not quantified.,1,N/R,Only cooperation frequencies compared between cost-structure scenarios.,0.3,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,No human,All conditions are computational simulations.,1,"Game and evolutionary dynamics as in base punishment model: two-stage interaction, imitation, migration, conflict, and mutation. Difference: punishers incur a fixed cost equal to c per period regardless of how many defectors are present (monitoring/enforcement cost modeled as fixed), while defectors still suffer reductions in payoff when punished. This altered cost structure reduces the advantage of punishment when defectors are rare and undermines the group selection effect on altruistic punishment.",Summarized from Fig.4 description where monitoring costs are fixed and independent of defector frequency.,0.9
10.1073_pnas.0630443100.md,Modified payoff structure: cooperation yields per capita benefit b/n and extinction depends on payoffs,"Described in Results as a structural variation: ""We modified the payoffs so that each cooperative act produces a per capita benefit of b/n for each other group member, and modified the extinction model so that the probability of group extinction is proportional to the difference between warring groups in average payoffs including the costs of punishment, rather than simply the difference in frequency of cooperators.""",0.85,"Payoff and group-selection structure: each cooperative act provides per capita benefit b/n to every other group member (b tested at 2c, 4c, 8c), and group extinction probability depends on difference in average payoffs (including punishment costs) rather than frequency of defectors. Punishment and other parameters as in base model.","Independent variable here is the structure of payoffs and group selection; they vary b relative to c as 2c, 4c, 8c.",0.88,False,This is another simulation variant.,1,False,"Numerical modeling, not empirical experimentation.",1,False,No lab.,1,True,"Authors state they ""modified"" the model and studied it via simulations.",1,False,"Results are described qualitatively as ""qualitatively similar"" to base simulations.",0.9,100,Same interpretation of n as ~100 individuals; parameters otherwise based on base case.,0.5,2000,"No change to simulation length is mentioned; assume same 2,000 periods protocol.",0.8,1,Cooperation remains a binary act in this variant.,0.8,N/A,No endowment or initial public fund.,1,N/R,"Although each cooperative act yields b/n to others, b/n is not framed as a standard linear public good with an explicitly normalized MPCR; insufficient information to compute a normalized MPCR across parameter sweeps.",0.6,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No identity display.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,1,"Punishers and punishment continue to operate, with costs included in group payoffs for extinction probability.",1,0.2,No change to k is reported; base case k=0.2 likely retained.,0.8,N/R,"p is likely unchanged (0.8) but for this structural variant the text does not explicitly restate p, only discusses varying b; to avoid over-inference we treat it as not reported.",0.4,0,No rewarding mechanism is introduced in this structural modification.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments remain unspecified.,1,N/R,"They say results are ""qualitatively similar"" for b=2c, 4c, 8c but do not publish numeric cooperation frequencies.",0.3,N/R,Contributions not quantified in tokens.,1,N/R,"Efficiency not directly reported, despite payoffs being modeled.",1,N/R,Only qualitative statements about group selection and punishment costs; no explicit payoff numbers.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational model variant.,1,"In this variant, each time an individual cooperates they pay cost c and every other member of their group receives a benefit b/n, turning the interaction into a public-goods–like game where cooperation directly increases others' payoffs. Group selection is altered so that the probability of group extinction in intergroup conflict is proportional to the difference in average payoffs (including costs of punishment) between groups, not just the difference in frequency of defectors. Simulations explore b values of 2c, 4c, and 8c, and the authors report that for these values the qualitative pattern of cooperation and punishment is similar to that of the base model.",Summarized directly from the structural modifications described in the Results section.,0.94
10.1073_pnas.0630443100.md,"Continuous-trait model: cooperation and punishment intensities in [0,1]","Results section: ""We also investigated a model in which cooperation and punishment are characters that vary continuously from zero to one.""",0.9,"Representation of strategies: each individual has continuous cooperation value x∈[0,1] and punishment value y∈[0,1]; behavior is probabilistic according to these values. Other parameters broadly similar to base model (c, k, p, e, m, μ, ε) though not all are restated.",Independent variable is whether cooperation and punishment are discrete types vs continuous intensities.,0.85,False,Again a theoretical simulation extension with no human participants.,1,False,"Numerical experiment, not empirical.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,"Described as a model they ""studied""; context implies simulation like previous models.",0.9,False,Outcomes stated qualitatively (steady-state mean levels similar) rather than via derivations.,0.85,100,They treat this as analogous to base model; group size not changed explicitly.,0.5,2000,Simulation protocol length likely unchanged; not explicitly restated but consistent with other variants.,0.7,1,"Although x and y are continuous probabilities, each interaction is still binary behave-like-cooperator vs defector; the contribution decision remains all-or-nothing per trial.",0.7,N/A,No endowments.,1,N/R,Public goods multiplier not explicitly defined.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,Punishment value y controls propensity to punish; punishment is part of the model.,1,N/R,"They do not restate c, k, p for this variant; presumably similar to base but not explicit.",0.4,N/R,Punishment severity not specified explicitly for this continuous model.,0.4,0,No rewards mentioned.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments not specified.,1,N/R,"They only state that ""steady-state mean levels of cooperation in this model are similar to the base model"" without giving numeric values.",0.3,N/R,No token contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,Only qualitative comparisons in terms of cooperation levels.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model is purely computational.,1,"Each individual has two continuous traits: cooperation level x and punishment level y in [0,1]. In each interaction, with probability x the individual behaves as a cooperator (incurring the cooperation cost and conferring whatever benefits are modeled, as in the discrete-type model), and with probability 1−x as a defector. Similarly, with probability y the individual behaves as a punisher toward defectors, and with probability 1−y as a non-punisher. Mutations in x and y are uniformly distributed over [0,1]. The rest of the evolutionary dynamics (imitation, migration, group conflict, mutation) follow the same general structure as in the base model, and steady-state mean cooperation is reported to be similar to that of the base discrete-type model.",Direct paraphrase of the continuous-trait model description in the Results.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.0630443100.md,"Neighbor-imitation model without extinction (ring structure, no group extinction)","Results: ""Finally, we studied a model without extinction analogous to a recent model of selection among stable equilibria because of biased imitation (23). Populations are arranged in a ring, and individuals imitate only individuals drawn from the neighboring two groups... We could find no reasonable parameter combination that led to significant long run average levels of cooperation in this last model.""",0.9,"Elimination of extinction-based group selection: groups arranged in a ring, individuals imitate only neighbors from two adjacent groups; no explicit group extinction process; cooperative acts produce per capita benefit b/n to others; parameters otherwise analogous to base model.",Independent variable is the presence vs absence of group extinction selection; here they remove extinction and use local biased imitation across groups.,0.88,False,Another theoretical simulation variant.,1,False,"Numerical model, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab.,1,True,"Described as a model they ""studied""; context of previous paragraphs indicates computational simulation.",0.9,False,"Result presented qualitatively (no parameter set produced high cooperation), not via analysis.",0.85,100,They treat this model as analogous to base with same parameter scales; exact group size not specified but inferred similar.,0.4,2000,Simulation length not restated; assume similar timeframe used for exploring long-run averages.,0.6,1,"Cooperation remains a binary act, as in the related payoff-structured variants.",0.7,N/A,No endowments.,1,N/R,"Although each cooperative act produces per capita benefit b/n, MPCR not formalized for this variant.",0.7,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No identity UI.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,1,"They refer to cooperative acts with per capita benefits b/n and discuss punishment in earlier structurally related model; text does not explicitly drop punishment here but focuses on cooperation; ambiguous but likely retains punishment. However, since not explicit, we still code as punishment existing based on continuity.",0.55,N/R,k not specified for this variant.,0.3,N/R,p not specified.,0.3,0,No rewarding mechanism introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments not defined.,1,N/R,Authors state qualitatively that no parameter combination led to significant cooperation but do not give numeric rates.,0.3,N/R,No token-based contributions defined.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics not computed.,1,N/R,Group payoffs only implicitly referenced via imitation of successful neighbors.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational model.,1,"Groups are arranged in a ring topology. Individuals imitate only individuals from the two neighboring groups rather than from the whole population; there is no extinction-based group selection. Cooperative acts create a per capita benefit b/n for other group members, raising average payoffs of more cooperative groups. Because individuals tend to imitate more successful neighbors, cooperation could in principle spread via payoff-biased imitation alone, but the authors report that across reasonable parameter combinations this mechanism alone did not sustain significant long-run cooperation.",Direct from the description of the final model without extinction and arranged in a ring.,0.95
