10.1126_science.1100735.md,Nonintentional and Costly (NC) punishment condition,Paper defines 'nonintentional and costly (NC)' as a separate condition.,1,"Punishment cost structure and intentionality of B's defection: a random device determines B’s action (nonintentional), and punishment is costly for A (1 MU per point) and effective against B (2 MUs per point).",NC is characterized by lack of intentionality in B’s defection and costs of punishment to both players.,1,True,Human-subject experiment as in other conditions.,1,True,NC is one treatment in a controlled experimental design with random order of conditions.,1,True,Conducted in PET/medical research facility.,1,False,No simulation study.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,2,Same 2-player trust interaction.,1,7,A plays seven trials overall; NC is one of the four punishment-eligible trials.,0.9,1,"A can select any number of punishment points 0–20, not just all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Endowments are private; no public-good contribution.,0.9,N/R,No public goods MPCR.,1,0,No mention of communication between players.,0.9,0,No summaries of others' behavior or earnings reported.,0.5,0,Interactions are anonymous; identities not revealed with punishment.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Likely but not specified that subjects knew the number of rounds.,0.4,1,"A can assign punishment points in NC, even though B’s action was randomly determined.",1,1,"In NC, 'Punishment is again costly for both A and B; A loses one MU and B loses two MUs per punishment point assigned to B.'",1,2,Each punishment point reduces B’s payoff by 2 MUs.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,30,Same monetary setup: 10 MUs initial + 20 MUs after B’s decision.,0.7,N/R,No contributions; focus is on punishment behavior.,1,N/R,"Only qualitative: 'almost no punishment is imposed on B in the NC condition' and 'only 3 of 14 subjects reduced B's payoff, and those who did so punished only a little.' No numeric punishment amount reported.",0.2,N/R,Efficiency measures are not provided.,1,N/R,Group payoff values by condition not reported.,1,Switzerland,Same Zurich-based subject pool.,0.4,N/R,No ages reported.,1,Male,All A subjects are male.,1,N/R,No education info.,1,On site,Same PET lab/medical facility as other conditions.,1,"Same trust and punishment structure. In NC, a random device, not B, determines whether B keeps all the money, so B is not perceived as responsible. A can still assign punishment points that cost him 1 MU and reduce B by 2 MUs per point. A generally views B’s action as nearly fair/neutral and shows almost no desire to punish, with only 3 of 14 subjects punishing at all.",Summarized from NC condition description and behavioral results.,1
10.1126_science.1127333.md,Ultimatum Game (UG) – all populations,"Single standardized UG protocol described, run in 15 populations; paper does not distinguish treatment variants within UG.",0.7,Stake size fixed at 1 day’s local wage; Player 1 offer in 10% increments of the stake; Player 2 strategy-method acceptance/rejection for each possible offer.,"UG description specifies stake level, discretized offer space, and strategy method for Player 2.",0.9,True,Games were played by 1762 adult human participants across 15 populations.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized economic games with random matching and specified rules.",1,False,"Described explicitly as ‘field experiments’ in diverse communities, not university laboratory sessions.",0.95,False,No computer/agent-based simulations were used; only human behavioral data.,1,False,No formal analytical model is presented as the experiment itself; only empirical regressions.,1,2,UG is defined with two players (proposer and responder) per interaction.,1,1,Interactions are described as ‘one-shot anonymous’ with no repeat interaction.,1,1,"Player 1 can choose any offer in 10% increments (0–100% of stake), not restricted to all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,"Not a public good game; entire stake is initially under Player 1’s control to divide, so 0 proportion is in a shared/public account by default.",0.6,N/A,"UG is a bargaining/splitting game, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",1,0,Procedures stress anonymity and preventing communication among players about the game; no mention of allowed chat.,0.9,0,No indication that participants saw summaries of others’ outcomes; focus is on own interaction.,0.7,0,Interactions are specified as anonymous; responders’ identities are not revealed to proposers.,0.95,0,No reward mechanism exists in the UG; only potential rejection.,1,1,Game is described as explicitly one-shot; participants necessarily know there is only one round.,0.8,1,"Costly punishment is implemented via Player 2’s ability t