t given.,0,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment or resource base per round not specified.,0,N/R,"Results are about frequencies of strategy types and existence/stability of equilibria, not reported average contribution fractions.",0,N/R,Not reported numerically.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency indices computed; only qualitative statements about how often groups cooperate.,0,N/R,No concrete numeric payoff outcomes given for this three‑strategy system.,0,N/A,The model uses no human subjects.,1,N/A,The model uses no human subjects.,1,N/A,The model uses no human subjects.,1,N/A,The model uses no human subjects.,1,No human,The section relies on analytical derivations and numerical simulations of strategy dynamics only.,1,"Same two-stage n-person prisoner’s dilemma with punishment as base model. Strategies: P always cooperates and punishes defectors; R1 defects until punished once then cooperates forever and never punishes; E always cooperates and never punishes. Errors occur with probability e whenever strategy calls for cooperation. Groups formed randomly (r=0 in Appendix A.4 derivation). Analysis describes parameter conditions for R1 as ESS, for interior equilibria with all three strategies, and possible oscillations.","Summarized from Section 4.2 and Appendix A.4, including definitions and described dynamic outcomes.",1
10.1016_0162-3095(92)90032-y.md,Analytical evolutionary model – M vs R1 vs E (moralistic punishment),"Section 4.3 and Appendix A.5 introduce the moralist strategy (M) which punishes defectors, non‑punishers, and non‑punisher‑punishers, and analyze its evolutionary stability against R1 and E.",1,"Strategy composition: moralists (M) vs reluctant cooperators (R1) vs easy‑going cooperators (E); parameters n, w, b, c, k, p, e; random group formation.",Section 4.3 defines M and compares its fitness to R1 and E under given parameter conditions (inequalities (9) and (10)).,1,False,No empirical data; purely theoretical.,1,False,"Not a controlled experiment, but an analytical investigation.",1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,False,"For moralists, authors derive analytical conditions (9) and (10); they explicitly note they have not presented complete dynamic analysis or simulations for M.",0.9,True,"Appendix A.5 gives symbolic expected fitnesses V(M|n-1), V(R1|n-1), V(E|n-1) and conditions for M to resist invasion.",1,N/R,Group size n remains parametric; no fixed numeric value for this condition.,0,N/R,"As with other sections, infinite expected interactions with continuation probability w.",0,1,"Action in cooperation stage is binary C or D; moralists also choose whether to punish others in the punishment stage, but contributions remain all‑or‑nothing.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment or public/private accounts in an empirical sense.,1,N/R,Same underlying public good structure; MPCR not numerically declared.,0,N/A,No communication between individuals is modeled.,1,N/A,No experimenter display or summary interface.,1,N/A,"Although M punishes based on whether others are in 'good standing', the model does not frame this as an information display design for human subjects.",1,N/A,No rewarding; only punishment.,1,N/A,Infinite horizon parameter w; not a displayed round counter.,1,1,"M punishes defectors, non‑punishers, and non‑punisher‑punishers; punishment costs k to punisher and p to target.",1,N/R,k is symbolic; no numeric instantiation.,0,N/R,p is symbolic; no numeric instantiation.,0,0,No rewards included.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Endowment is not parameterized; payoffs framed directly in terms of b, c, k, p.",0,N/R,"Analysis focuses on whether M is evolutionarily stable, not on average cooperation levels as numeric outputs.",0,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,0,N/R,"Even though M can stabilize arbitrary costly behaviors, the paper does not provide efficiency measures.",0,N/R,Group payoffs under M vs R1 vs E are not reported as specific numeric outcomes.,0,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,No human,Section is theoretical; authors even state that they have not completed a full dynamic analysis.,1,"Same two-stage n-person public goods framework. Moralists (M) always cooperate and punish any individual not in 'good standing', defined as having followed M since last punishment: they punish non‑cooperators, non‑punishers of non‑cooperators, and non‑punishers of non‑punishers. Each M punishes any given target at most once per turn; after being punished, an individual can return to good standing by complying. Conditions (9) and (10) give when M can resist invasion by R1 and E respectively, and show that M can stabilize any costly behavior, not only cooperative ones.",Summarized from Section 4.3 description of M and Appendix A.5 formulas.,1
10.2307_2297925.md,Contagious equilibrium in Prisoner’s Dilemma matching game (Section 4),"Single formal example with specific payoff table (Table 1), random matching, and a specified ‘contagious’ strategy; treated as one theoretical condition.",0.9,Information structure: no information processing (each player only observes own past matches) with uniformly random matching in an infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma; strategy restricted to contagious equilibrium where players choose c if and only if their type is c.,"Only structured variation described is the information regime (no information processing) and the particular strategy profile (contagious equilibrium) under given stage-game payoffs g, l, and discount factor δ.",0.9,False,Pure game-theoretic model; no human subjects or data collection.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only analytical characterizations of equilibria.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; entirely theoretical.,1,False,No computer or numerical simulations reported; results are closed-form/analytical.,1,True,Equilibrium properties proven using algebra and dynamic programming; no numerical approximation or empirical component.,1,2,Stage game is a 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma; community size M=2n but interactions occur in pairs.,0.9,-1,Game is ‘repeated infinitely’ with discount factor δ; encode as -1 for infinite horizon.,1,1,"Action set is {c,d}; not a contribution game with continuous decisions. By definition in schema, 1 corresponds to ability to choose freely rather than a forced all-or-nothing contribution structure; closest mapping is 1.",0.4,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game; no endowment allocation decision.,1,N/R,No public good or linear multiplier described; only Prisoner’s Dilemma payoffs with parameters g and l.,1,0,Text explicitly says ‘Direct communication among the players is assumed to be non-existent.’,1,0,Players only observe their own past action profiles; no summaries of others’ payoffs or actions.,1,0,Players ‘do not observe identity of players’; no way to link punishment or defection to specific identities.,1,0,"No explicit rewarding, and no identities observed.",1,0,Infinite horizon repeated game; no indication that players see the number of rounds or remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Punishment occurs via future defections triggered by observed cheating; this is an endogenous sanctioning mechanism.,0.9,N/R,No explicit per-unit punishment cost parameter; punishments occur through playing d with payoffs defined by parameters g and l.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment ‘points spent’ to loss; punishment is simply playing the stage-game action d.,0.3,0,No mechanism where players incur costs to reward others; only cooperation vs defection.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Stage-game payoffs specified directly; no separate initial endowment in tokens or money.,1,N/R,No contributions or public good; only binary cooperate/defect decisions and theoretical payoffs; no empirical outcome data.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game and no observed quantitative data.,1,N/R,Paper provides only theoretical existence conditions; no realized efficiency metrics calculated or reported as numbers.,1,N/R,"Describes payoff structure symbolically (1, 1+g, -l, 0) but does not report realized or average group payoffs from runs or simulations.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical repeated game model with random matching; no implemented experiment.,1,"Infinite-horizon, uniformly random matching among M=2n players playing a symmetric 2x2 Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs: (c,c) → (1,1); (c,d) → (−l,1+g); (d,c) → (1+g,−l); (d,d) → (0,0). No information processing means each player only observes his own past action profiles and not partner identities or others’ histories; direct communication is absent. ‘Types’ are defined endogenously: type c if never involved in defection; type d otherwise. The contagious equilibrium prescribes that players choose action equal to their type; defection spreads irreversibly and can sustain cooperation for sufficiently large δ and l.",Summarizes key structural details of the theoretical condition from Section 4 and Table 1 that are not captured by other fields.,1
10.1257_aer.99.1.431.md,Control – Two-person sequential games (no identity),Paper describes one control condition where subjects only play the stage‑4 two‑person games without any identity induction.,1,"Group identity induction absent; baseline with no group assignment, no chat, no other-other allocation; standard two-person games only.",Control differs from all five treatments precisely by the absence of any group identity procedures in stages 1–3.,1,True,Human participants in lab sessions making incentivized decisions.,1,True,"Random assignment to roles, controlled payoffs, explicit treatments.",1,True,Sessions conducted in the RCGD lab at the University of Michigan.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported for this condition.,1,False,"Control is behavioral, not a purely analytical model.",1,2,Stage 4 uses two-person sequential games throughout.,1,24,Appendix lists 24 games used to estimate preferences; each is effectively one round of a two-person game.,0.7,1,"Players choose between discrete payoff options but these are not framed as contributing a divisible endowment; public-goods all-or-nothing definition does not apply, treat as free choice over payoff options.",0.5,N/R,"No public-goods contribution structure; only payoff options, so default contribution share not defined.",1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR reported or implied.,1,0,Control sessions contain only stage 4; online chat stage is absent.,1,0,No mention of feedback or summaries during play; payoffs only realized at end.,0.8,0,Interactions are anonymous; identities and group membership are not revealed in the control and no explicit punishment stage exists.,1,0,Same reasoning as for punishment; no explicit reward stage and subjects remain anonymous.,1,1,"Instructions state that two of the games will be randomly selected for payment, implying subjects know the total number of games.",0.8,0,"Although some games involve payoff reductions, there is no separate punishment technology where players spend costly tokens to punish in a repeated public goods sense.",0.7,N/A,No dedicated punishment mechanism as defined in schema.,1,N/A,No dedicated punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward technology; only outcome choices in static games.,0.7,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Games specify monetary outcomes directly; no common per-period endowment is reported.,1,N/R,No contribution-based public goods decisions.,1,N/R,No contributions are made; only choices among payoff allocations.,1,N/R,Authors report SWM choice proportions and earnings but not normalized efficiency relative to maximum.,0.6,N/R,"They present average individual earnings (e.g., 14.40 dollars) but not group-level payoffs per game.",0.7,United States,Experiments run at University of Michigan (USA) with its students and some staff.,0.95,Primarily university students; exact ages N/R,Subjects mostly University of Michigan students; typical ages are young adults but no exact distribution given.,0.6,N/R,No gender composition reported.,1,Mostly university students; some staff,"Paper notes that most subjects were University of Michigan students, with a few staff.",0.9,On site,Conducted in RCGD lab using z-Tree; no indication of online/remotely hosted sessions.,1,One-shot play of 24 two-person dictator and sequential response games from Charness and Rabin (2002); roles A and B randomly assigned; random matching per game; no feedback until end; two games randomly selected for payment; control sessions only include stage 4 with 7–10 games per session.,Design description in Section II.D and footnotes on game sets and payment rules.,1
10.1257_aer.99.1.431.md,Original – Ingroup condition (within-subject),Original treatment uses within-subject design: each subject reports decisions for ingroup and outgroup matches; this row captures ingroup-matching case.,1,Group assignment based on painting preference (Klee vs. Kandinsky); online chat stage present; other-other allocation stage present; decisions conditioned on match being ingroup.,Section II.A–C defines Original treatment; ingroup vs outgroup scenarios are the key independent variation within treatment.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,"Controlled treatments, random matching, incentivized games.",1,True,Run in RCGD lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No purely simulated agents for this condition.,1,False,"Behavioral data, not just analytical derivations.",1,2,Stage 4 consists of two-person games.,1,24,Appendix lists 24 distinct games used to estimate preferences across treatments.,0.7,1,"Choices are between discrete payoff allocations, not divisible contributions; treat as general discrete action space, not restricted all-or-nothing on contributions.",0.5,N/R,No endowment-contribution framework.,1,N/R,Not a linear public-goods game.,1,1,Original includes online chat stage for group problem solving.,1,0,No intermediate feedback or summaries of others’ actions or earnings are described.,0.8,0,Interactions anonymous; no separate punishment stage with identity revelation.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism; only anonymous game outcomes.,1,1,Subjects know there are multiple games and that two will be randomly selected for payment; implies knowledge of game set size within a session.,0.8,0,"Although some game options reduce the other’s payoff, there is no separate costly punishment mechanism across rounds.",0.7,N/A,No generalized punishment technology as defined by schema.,1,N/A,No dedicated punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward technology; only choosing higher-payoff allocations.,0.7,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No