10.1007_s12110-002-1012-7.md,Gift-exchange labor market – baseline (no reward/punishment),"Paper first describes a gift-exchange experiment (Fehr, Gächter, Kirchsteiger 1997) where employers can only make wage and desired effort offers; the punishment/reward extension is described as an extension, so this is the baseline condition.",0.96,No possibility for employer to reward or punish after observing effort; workers know there is no second-stage action.,The only treatment dimension described for this experiment is the presence or absence of a reward/punishment opportunity for employers; this row covers the condition without this opportunity.,0.9,True,Described as a laboratory experiment with subjects in the roles of employers and workers who earn money.,1,True,Controlled lab game with specified payoffs and anonymous matching.,1,True,"Conducted at the University of Zurich with subjects in different rooms, standard lab setup.",1,False,Human subjects used; no indication of computer-only simulation.,1,False,Not a formal mathematical model; empirical experiment.,1,2,Each interaction is between one employer (buyer) and one worker (seller).,0.98,1,Described as an exchange between anonymous strangers in a one-shot situation; no repetition within a pair is mentioned.,0.8,1,"Worker chooses an effort level e from a continuous grid [0.1,1], not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"This is not a public goods game; there is no initial contribution to a public fund. Endowment is effectively in the worker’s private payoff unless he exerts effort. By convention for non-PGG with human subjects, set to 0.",0.5,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; payoffs are π_f=100e−w and π_w=w−c(e). MPCR is not defined or reported.,1,0,No mention of communication; anonymity and separate rooms suggest no chat.,0.95,0,"Bilateral game; workers only observe their own contract terms, not summaries of others’ outcomes.",0.8,0,No punishment stage exists in this condition.,1,0,No reward stage exists in this condition.,1,0,One-shot anonymous interaction; no evidence that any information about a horizon is displayed within a pair.,0.7,0,Employers have no option to punish in the baseline design.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism not available.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism not available.,1,0,Employers cannot provide post-contract monetary rewards beyond the wage w.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism not available.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism not available.,1,N/R,Endowments in experimental money units are defined only implicitly via payoff functions; no per-period fixed token endowment is specified as in PGGs.,0.4,N/R,"Outcome focuses on effort levels and wages, not normalized contribution relative to an endowment.",0.5,N/R,Reports average effort and generosity but no specific numeric average effort value in this baseline condition.,0.4,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized surplus to maximum possible surplus reported.,0.4,N/R,Only qualitative statements about profits and reciprocity; no aggregate payoff given.,0.2,Switzerland,Experiment described as conducted at the University of Zurich.,0.95,N/R,No age information reported.,0.01,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.01,N/R,Likely university students but this is not explicitly stated for this experiment.,0.3,On site,Laboratory setting with subjects in different rooms at the University of Zurich.,0.95,"Bilateral gift-exchange/contract experiment: Employer offers wage w in [0,100] and nonbinding desired effort e_hat in [0.1,1]; worker chooses actual effort e in [0.1,1] with cost c(e) from Table 1; payoffs π_f=100e−w, π_w=w−c(e). More workers than employers to induce competition; anonymous, one-shot interactions.",Summarizes the detailed game description provided in the text.,1
10.1007_s12110-002-1012-7.md,Gift-exchange labor market – employer reward/punishment option,Described as an extension of the gift-exchange experiment where employers can reward or punish workers after observing effort.,0.98,Employer has costly option to reward or punish after observing worker’s effort: each MU spent changes worker’s payoff by ±2.5 MU; up to 10 MU can be used.,Only treatment difference from baseline is the presence and parameters of the reward/punishment stage.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects in employer/worker roles.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of reward/punishment option in a lab game.,1,True,Conducted at university lab; same context as baseline gift-exchange experiment.,1,False,Human-subject experiment.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical model.,1,2,Each trading relationship is between one employer and one worker.,0.98,1,Described as one-shot anonymous exchanges; no repeated interaction within a fixed pair mentioned.,0.8,1,"Worker chooses e from [0.1,1]; not binary.",1,0,Not a public goods setting; endowment is effectively private.,0.5,N/R,Not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Neutral instructions and anonymity; no mention of communication.,0.95,0,No indication that workers or employers see summaries of others’ outcomes; they observe only their own match.,0.8,0,Anonymity of partners stressed; no indication that identities of punishers/rewarders were revealed.,0.8,0,Same anonymity; rewards/punishments are not linked to identities.,0.8,0,Interactions are effectively one-shot and anonymous; no horizon info needed within a pair.,0.7,1,Employers can spend MU to reduce workers’ payoffs.,1,1,Spending 1 MU on punishment costs the employer 1 MU.,1,2.5,Spending 1 MU on punishment decreases the worker’s payoff by 2.5 MU.,1,1,Employers can also spend MU to increase workers’ payoffs.,1,1,Spending 1 MU on reward costs the employer 1 MU.,1,2.5,Spending 1 MU on reward increases the worker’s payoff by 2.5 MU.,1,N/R,Exact experimental MU endowments per role not specified in this summary; only payoff changes from effort and reward/punishment are described.,0.3,N/R,Main outcome is average effort; no normalized rate relative to an explicit endowment given.,0.5,0.65,Text reports that the presence of reward/punishment opportunity increases average effort from e=0.37 to e=0.65; treat effort level as the contribution-like decision.,0.95,N/R,Authors mention aggregate monetary payoff increases by 40% but do not give absolute efficiency ratio relative to maximum possible.,0.6,N/R,Only relative (40% increase) payoff information provided; no absolute payoffs.,0.3,Switzerland,Same gift-exchange experiment series at University of Zurich.,0.95,N/R,No age data provided.,0.01,N/R,No gender data reported.,0.01,N/R,Likely students but not specified.,0.3,On site,Laboratory environment at University of Zurich with participants in different rooms.,0.95,"Same gift-exchange contract as baseline (employer offers wage w and desired effort e_hat; worker chooses e with cost c(e); π_f=100e−w, π_w=w−c(e)), plus a second stage where employer observes e and can spend up to 10 MU on reward or punishment. Each MU spent changes the worker’s payoff by ±2.5 MU. Underprovision (e<e_hat) punished in 68% of cases (avg 7 MU); overprovision rewarded in 70% of cases (avg 7 MU); exact provision rewarded in 41% (avg 4.5 MU). Average effort rises from 0.37 (no option) to 0.65 (with option).",Summarizes the treatment-specific rules and key behavioral outcomes described.,1
10.1007_s12110-002-1012-7.md,"Public goods game – stable groups (partner design), no punishment",Fehr & Gächter public goods game with stable groups where the same group of four plays 10 periods without punishment option.,0.98,Stable (partner) groups of 4 play a linear public goods game for 10 periods; no punishment stage available.,Key manipulated factors across conditions are group stability vs random matching and presence vs absence of punishment; this row is stable groups without punishment.,1,True,Laboratory public goods experiment with human subjects earning tokens.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of punishment and matching structure.,1,True,Conducted with anonymous subjects in groups in a lab setting.,1,False,Not a simulation; uses human subjects.,1,False,"Empirical game, not pure theory.",1,4,Explicitly: “In a group of four anonymously interacting subjects…”.,1,10,“In the no-punishment condition the same group of subjects plays this game for ten periods.”,1,1,Each subject decides how many tokens (0–20) to keep or invest; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each subject is endowed with 20 tokens which they then decide to keep or invest; initial state is all private.,1,0.4,"For each token invested, each of the four subjects earns 0.4 tokens; marginal per capita return is 0.4.",1,0,No communication is mentioned; anonymity and standard design imply no chat.,0.95,1,"After each period subjects “are informed about the contributions of the other three group members,” which is a per-round summary of others’ contributions.",1,0,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,1,"Subjects know they play for ten periods, standard in such repeated PGG designs; backward-induction discussion assumes common knowledge of horizon.",0.9,0,This is explicitly the no-punishment condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No explicit reward option apart from contributions.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,“Each subject is endowed with 20 tokens.”,1,N/R,Time paths in Figure 3 are described qualitatively; no exact average contribution in tokens by period given.,0.4,N/R,Only qualitative description: cooperation initially high then unravels to near zero by period 10; no numeric averages.,0.4,N/R,No explicit calculation of realized vs maximum group payoff reported for this condition.,0.3,N/R,No numeric average group earnings reported separately for no-punishment stable groups.,0.3,N/R,"This summary refers to Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002); country of implementation is not stated in this excerpt (originally often Switzerland, but not mentioned here).",0.3,N/R,No age information provided.,0.01,N/R,No gender composition data mentioned.,0.01,N/R,Likely students but not explicitly stated in this text.,0.3,On site,Described as laboratory public goods experiments with anonymous subjects and controlled conditions.,0.95,"Linear public goods game: 4 players, each with 20 tokens per period; each token kept yields 1 token to the individual; each token invested yields 0.4 tokens to each group member (total return 1.6). Game repeated 10 periods with fixed groups; after each period subjects see each of the other three players’ contributions. No punishment or reward stage available in this treatment.",Summarizes the game rules for this condition.,1
10.1007_s12110-002-1012-7.md,"Public goods game – stable groups (partner design), with punishment",Same 4-person PGG with stable groups but now including a punishment stage after contribution decisions.,0.99,Stable (partner) groups of 4 play 10-period public goods game with costly punishment stage after observing others’ contributions.,Key independent variables are group stability and availability of punishment; this row is stable groups with punishment.,1,True,Human-subject public goods experiment.,1,True,Treatment manipulation (punishment vs none) under controlled conditions.,1,True,Same laboratory setting as no-punishment treatment.,1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,Empirical experiment.,1,4,Groups of four subjects described for all public goods treatments.,1,10,“In the punishment condition subjects also play the game for ten periods.”,1,1,Continuous 0–20 token contribution choice.,1,0,Endowment starts as private tokens.,1,0.4,Same underlying PGG technology; MPCR per token to each group member is 0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication in punishment design; anonymity emphasized.,0.95,1,After each period and before punishment subjects are informed about others’ contributions (and must assign punishment points).,1,0,Experiment ensures that “group members cannot trace the history of individual investments or individual punishments of a particular subject”; implies no identifiable punishment source.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism; only punishment.,1,1,Backward induction argument explicitly relies on common knowledge that the experiment ends in period ten.,0.95,1,Subjects can assign punishment points after seeing contributions.,1,N/R,"Cost to punisher is stated only qualitatively as “a convex and increasing function” and that 10 points cost 30 tokens; no per-point marginal cost is constant, so a single unit cost is not defined.",0.4,N/R,"Each punishment point reduces income by 10%, but the base to which this percentage applies varies; no fixed MU-per-point reduction is provided.",0.4,0,"Design only allows punishment, not positive transfers.",1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment per period as in no-punishment condition.,1,N/R,Figures show rising average investments approaching full cooperation but no numerical series is given.,0.4,N/R,Authors state that “eventually almost full cooperation is achieved” but do not report exact mean tokens invested.,0.4,N/R,"They note high cooperation, but no explicit efficiency ratio for this treatment alone.",0.3,N/R,No treatment-specific average group payoff is provided; only qualitative comparisons across conditions.,0.3,N/R,Country is not mentioned in this excerpt for Fehr & Gächter 2000/2002.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,On site,Public goods experiment conducted in a lab with controlled groups and anonymous matching.,0.95,"Same linear PGG as no-punishment: 4 players, 20-token endowment, return of 0.4 tokens per invested token to each player. After contribution stage each subject learns others’ contributions and can assign 0–10 punishment points to each other member; total punishment points per target reduce that target’s income by 10% per point. Cost of punishment to the punisher is convex in total assigned points (10 points cost 30 tokens). History of individual contributions and punishments cannot be traced to specific individuals across periods. Strong negative reciprocity: defectors are punished, especially when they contribute far below others; punishment occurs even in period 10; cooperation increases over time and nearly full cooperation is reached.",Summarizes treatment-specific rules and key behavioral observations.,1
10.1007_s12110-002-1012-7.md,"Public goods game – random groups (stranger design), no punishment",Random matching (stranger) design of same 4-person PGG where group composition changes every period and no punishment is allowed.,0.99,"Randomly rematched groups of 4 each period (24-person pool), 10-period public goods game, no punishment option.",Independent variables are matching protocol (random vs fixed) and availability of punishment; this is random matching without punishment.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of matching and punishment.,1,True,Described as lab public goods experiments with anonymous interaction.,1,False,Uses human subjects.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical model.,1,4,Groups of four formed from a pool of 24 in each period.,1,10,Random group design also run for ten periods.,1,1,Same 0–20 token contribution decision as other PGG conditions.,1,0,20-token endowment is initially private.,1,0.4,Game definition with 0.4 return per invested token to each member applies here as well.,1,0,No communication reported; interactions are anonymous among strangers.,0.95,1,Same feedback of others’ contributions as in stable group design is implied for both punish and no-punish PGGs.,0.9,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward option.,1,1,Ten-period horizon is part of experimental instructions; same repeated-game structure as other conditions.,0.9,0,Explicitly described as no-punishment condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No explicit rewarding technology.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment applies.,1,N/R,Figure 4 is only described qualitatively: cooperation initially high then breaks down to near zero.,0.4,N/R,No specific period-by-period averages reported.,0.4,N/R,No numeric efficiency measure provided.,0.3,N/R,No treatment-specific payoff numbers given.,0.3,N/R,Country of lab not explicitly stated for this design in the excerpt.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,On site,Described as a lab experiment with anonymous matching and fixed rules.,0.95,"Same PGG as stable groups: 4 players randomly drawn from a pool of 24 each period, 20-token endowment, 1 token per token kept, and 0.4 tokens per token invested to each group member. Group composition is randomly changed every period (stranger design), making interactions essentially one-shot. No punishment or reward stage is available. Cooperation starts relatively high but unravels over the 10 periods, with most subjects investing nothing in the final period.",Summarizes the game rules and main behavioral pattern for this condition.,1
10.1007_s12110-002-1012-7.md,"Public goods game – random groups (stranger design), with punishment",Random matching PGG with the same punishment technology as the stable group punishment treatment.,0.99,"Randomly rematched groups of 4 from pool of 24, 10-period public goods game with costly punishment stage after contribution decisions.",This condition combines random matching with availability of punishment.,1,True,Human subject public goods experiment.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of punishment and matching structure.,1,True,Conducted under controlled laboratory conditions with anonymous subjects.,1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,"Empirical experiment, not purely analytical.",1,4,Groups of four subjects are randomly formed each period.,1,10,Random group design with punishment also run for ten periods.,1,1,Continuous 0–20 token contribution choice.,1,0,20-token endowment begins all in private accounts.,1,0.4,Same linear PGG production function with 0.4 per capita return per invested token.,1,0,Anonymity and experimental control; no communication channel described.,0.95,1,Participants need to observe others’ contributions to assign punishment; implies feedback of others’ contributions.,1,0,Design prevents tracing histories of individual investments or punishments to specific individuals; identity of punishers not revealed.,0.9,0,No reward stage.,1,1,Ten-period horizon and stranger protocol are part of the design; same backward induction logic is discussed.,0.9,1,Subjects can assign punishment points after seeing contributions.,1,N/R,"Same convex cost schedule as partner punishment treatment (10 points cost 30 tokens), but no single per-point marginal cost is specified.",0.4,N/R,"Each punishment point reduces income by 10%, but MU equivalent per point varies with pre-punishment earnings; no single constant MU-per-point reduction.",0.4,0,No positive reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Same 20-token per-period endowment.,1,N/R,Figure 4 suggests that investments increase over time in punishment condition but no exact numbers.,0.4,N/R,Text: “average investments even increase over time”; no numeric averages reported for stranger-punishment condition separately.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure given for this treatment.,0.3,N/R,No average group payoff reported for stranger-punishment treatment alone.,0.3,N/R,Country not specified in this excerpt.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,On site,Laboratory PGG with stranger matching and punishment.,0.95,"Same PGG rules as other conditions: 4 players, 20 tokens each, 0.4 per capita return per invested token. In each of 10 periods, new random groups of four are formed from a pool of 24. After contribution decisions and feedback about others’ contributions, each player can assign 0–10 punishment points to each other group member, with convex costs and a 10% income reduction per point for the punished. Strong punishment of free riders is observed, similar in magnitude to partner design (e.g., contributing 8–14 tokens less than others can reduce one’s income by about 50%). Cooperation increases over time but less strongly than in the stable group punishment condition.",Summarizes rules and qualitative punishment pattern specific to this condition.,1
10.1007_s12110-002-1012-7.md,Third-party punishment dictator game – single third party,"Fehr & Fischbacher (2001a) third-party punishment game with one dictator (A), one passive recipient (B), and one third party (C).",0.99,One dictator A with 100-token endowment chooses transfer to B; uninvolved third party C with 50 tokens can punish A at cost 1:3 after observing A’s transfer.,"Key independent features: third-party role, punishment technology, no direct material impact of A’s action on C except via norm enforcement.",1,True,Described as an experiment with three players per game making real payoff-relevant decisions.,1,True,Controlled lab dictator-plus-punishment setup.,1,True,Same research group and description as other lab experiments; no indication of field setting.,0.95,False,Human subjects used.,1,False,Empirical experiment.,1,3,"Game explicitly has three players: A, B, and C.",1,1,Described as a one-shot third-party punishment game; no repetition within a trio is mentioned.,0.9,1,Dictator can transfer any amount from 0 to 100 tokens (continuous or discrete scale).,1,0,Endowment is initially entirely with player A and C in their private accounts; no default public fund.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; transfer from A to B is 1:1 without social multiplier.,1,0,No communication mentioned; standard dictator-game protocol.,0.95,0,"C observes A’s transfer in his own game, but no summaries of other groups; no cross-game info.",0.9,0,Punishment occurs within a fixed triad; no indication that identities within a larger population are revealed beyond the minimal roles.,0.7,0,"No reward stage, only punishment.",1,0,One-shot interaction; no explicit discussion of rounds.,0.8,1,Player C can assign punishment points to A after observing transfer.,1,1,"“For each punishment point assigned to player A, player C has costs of 1 token.”",1,3,“For each punishment point assigned to player A… player A has costs of 3 tokens.”,1,0,Only punishment option; no way for C to reward A or B.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,100,"Player A’s endowment is 100 tokens; C’s is 50. For a single summarized endowment, we record the dictator’s 100-token endowment which defines the main allocation decision.",0.9,N/R,"Reported outcomes focus on punishment as a function of A’s transfer, not on average transfer rates.",0.4,N/R,"They describe that A is never punished if transfer ≥50 and receives 9 punishment points on average when transferring 0, but do not report average transfers.",0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculation between total income and maximum possible income reported.,0.3,N/R,No mean total payoff per triad is given.,0.3,N/R,This third-party punishment study is cited but the country of implementation is not stated in the excerpt.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,On site,Context and authors indicate standard lab implementation; no evidence of online or field setting.,0.9,"Three players: A (dictator) gets 100 tokens, B (recipient) gets 0, C (third party) gets 50. A chooses any transfer from 0 to 100 to B. C observes A’s transfer and can assign integer punishment points to A. Each point costs C 1 token and reduces A’s payoff by 3 tokens. A is never punished when transferring 50 or more; the less A transfers below 50, the more punishment he receives. When A transfers 0, he receives on average 9 punishment points (27-token loss).",Includes the rules and key punishment behavior from the description.,1
10.1038_373209a0.md,"Box 1 – Simple punishment game (dominant vs subordinate, no retaliation)",Box 1 explicitly introduces a 'simple punishment game' with strong asymmetry in fighting ability between a dominant (player 1) and subordinate (player 2) and analyzes it as a distinct model.,1,"Strategy profiles and parameters in a 2-player repeated asymmetric punishment game: dominant’s strategy (Punish P vs Never Punish NP), subordinate’s strategy (Transgress T, Desist D, Cooperate C, Non-cooperate NC), benefits and costs (g1, g2, c1, c2, d1, d2), learning duration j, total rounds N (k = N − j). This condition: no retaliation, roles fixed (only subordinate can transgress or cooperate).","Box 1 defines a repeated game where the dominant chooses P or NP and the subordinate chooses among T/D or NC/C with parameters g1,g2,c1,c2,d1,d2,j,N; retaliation is not allowed and only the subordinate has the opportunity to transgress or cooperate.",1,False,Box 1 is a theoretical evolutionary game model with no real animals or humans; only payoff matrices and analytical ESS conditions are discussed.,1,False,No controlled experimental manipulation or collected data; purely theoretical derivation.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation; it is a conceptual mathematical model.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are described; only symbolic payoff analysis.,1,True,The game is solved analytically for ESS conditions using payoff inequalities; this matches a formal analytical model.,1,2,The game is defined between two individuals: a dominant (player 1) and subordinate (player 2).,1,1,Interactions repeat for N rounds but this is a generic symbol with no numeric value; using 1 as a placeholder would be misleading. Set to N/R.,0,1,"Players in each round choose among discrete pure actions (T vs D, C vs NC, P vs NP) rather than choosing a continuous contribution amount; the concept of all-or-nothing contribution to a public good does not apply in this dyadic dominance model, but mapping to the schema, contribution granularity is not meaningful. Mark as 1 to indicate there is a choice of action, though not a contribution level.",0.2,N/A,"No endowment or public fund; the model is about aggression and punishment, not contributions.",1,N/R,No public goods structure or multiplier is defined; only fitness payoffs from aggression and punishment.,1,N/A,No human or animal subjects; communication rules are not part of a lab/online protocol.,1,0,"Information about others’ past payoffs is implicit in the strategy definitions (learning) but no explicit display of summaries as in an experiment; for this analytical model, experimental display concepts do not apply. Set to 0 by schema requirement.",0.3,N/A,"Identity is inherent (two fixed players) and not an experimental manipulation; in a non-human, non-experimental analytical model this field is not applicable.",1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism defined in this game.,1,0,"There is a total of N rounds, but there is no notion of what is 'shown' to subjects; this is not an implemented experiment. Set to 0 as there is no explicit display of remaining rounds.",0.3,1,Core of the model is a punishment action P that imposes damage d2 on the subordinate and cost d1 on the dominant.,1,N/R,The model specifies a generic cost d1 to the punisher but does not assign it a numeric value.,1,N/R,Damage to the punished individual is denoted d2 but not quantified; no discrete 'per token' technology is defined.,1,0,There are no positive rewards; only punishment and altered future behaviour.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this model.,1,N/R,No initial endowment in tokens/points is defined; only abstract fitness units.,1,N/R,"No contributions to a public good are defined; outcomes are payoffs in fitness units, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts are modeled; only choices of aggressive/cooperative actions.,1,N/R,The paper does not compute aggregate efficiency metrics; it focuses on ESS stability conditions.,1,N/R,Total group payoff is not summarized numerically; only relative payoffs by strategy are considered.,1,N/A,No human or specific animal population is experimentally tested; the model is generic.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,The study in Box 1 is a purely theoretical animal contest model with no human participants.,1,"Asymmetric 2-player repeated game modelling dominance and punishment. Player 2 (subordinate) can either transgress (T) or desist (D) with benefits g2 and damage c1 to player 1; player 1 (dominant) may punish (P) or never punish (NP). Punishment imposes damage d2 on player 2 and cost d1 on player 1. Learning: subordinate transgresses for j training rounds before desisting for k = N − j rounds if facing a punisher. Parallel version where subordinate either cooperates (C) at cost c2 conferring benefit g1 on dominant, or non-cooperates (NC). ESS analysis identifies conditions under which (D,P)/(C,P) or (T,NP)/(NC,NP) are stable.",Summarized directly from Box 1 descriptions of the payoff matrices and ESS conditions.,1
10.1038_373209a0.md,Box 2 – Punishment game with retaliation (prospective model),"Box 2 extends the simple punishment game to include retaliation and mistakes, defining a multi-step game and discussing likely ESS outcomes.",1,"Strategy set with retaliation in a 2-player repeated asymmetric game: at step 1 subordinate chooses Transgress (T) vs Desist (D); at step 2 dominant chooses Punish (P) vs Never Punish (NP); at step 3 subordinate chooses Retaliate (R) vs Non-retaliate (NR) with probability p of escalation even under NR; at step 4 dominant chooses R vs NR. Parameters: probabilities of injury x, benefits g1,g2, costs c1,d1,d2,D1,D2, small mistake probability p, learning length j, total rounds N.","Box 2 explicitly introduces additional steps (retaliation choices R/NR by both players), mistake probability p, and injury costs D1,D2 governed by probability x, building on Box 1’s T/D and P/NP choices.",1,False,This is a theoretical extension of the game; no actual animals or humans are observed.,1,False,No experimental protocol or data; it is a conceptual prospective model.,1,False,Not implemented in a lab or field.,1,False,No numerical simulations are reported; only analytical reasoning about ESS likelihoods.,1,True,"The model derives ESS plausibility qualitatively from payoff expectations and probabilities x,p without computation on data.",1,2,Again between player 1 (potential punisher) and player 2 (potential transgressor).,1,1,N rounds are referenced symbolically; no numeric value is given; we therefore cannot set a numeric number of rounds.,0,1,"Players choose discrete aggressive/retaliatory actions, not contribution levels; public-good all-or-nothing framing does not apply but schema requires a value. Use 1 as generic.",0.2,N/A,No contributions or endowments are present in the model.,1,N/R,This is not a public-goods or linear-return game.,1,N/A,No experimental communication channel; theoretical game between abstract players.,1,0,No concept of displayed summaries; players’ knowledge is implicit via strategy and learning.,0.3,N/A,Identity is fixed and not part of a display protocol; field not applicable to a theoretical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is introduced.,1,0,Total rounds N exist in the model but nothing is 'shown' to subjects.,0.3,1,Dominant can punish at step 2 if subordinate transgresses; this is central to the model.,1,N/R,Punishment cost to dominant is indicated conceptually (for example through d1) but no specific numeric value is given.,1,N/R,"No per-unit punishment function; only injury probabilities x and damage costs D1,D2, d2.",1,0,Only punishment and retaliation are considered; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,Reward not part of the model.,1,N/A,Reward not part of the model.,1,N/R,No economic endowment is defined; players just have fitness payoffs.,1,N/R,"No contribution as outcome variable; focus is on strategy ESS conditions and expected values μ1, μ2.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric is computed; only stability regions.,1,N/R,Total payoff of both players is not summarized in a numeric example.,1,N/A,Model only; no sampled population.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Analytical model on animal contests without actual human subjects.,1,"Four-step repeated asymmetric game incorporating punishment and retaliation: (1) subordinate chooses T or D; (2) if T, dominant chooses P or NP; (3) if P, subordinate chooses R or NR but escalates with small probability p even under NR; (4) if R, dominant chooses R or NR. If both choose R, an escalated fight leads to injury with probability x for player 1 (cost D1) and (1 − x) for player 2 (cost D2). Mistake/‘test’ probability p captures occasional escalations. Analysis suggests commonsense direction of punishment (strong punishing weak) is more likely than paradoxical punishment, depending on expected values μ1, μ2 of escalation.",Summarizes the structure and key conclusions of Box 2.,1
10.1038_373209a0.md,Box 3 – Punishment ESSs with retaliation,"Box 3 explicitly analyzes 'Punishment ESSs' for the retaliation model, presenting a table of candidate ESS strategy profiles (A–D) with stability conditions.",1,"Candidate ESS strategy profiles in the retaliatory punishment game and relative fighting ability (via probability x): A) I = P/R (player 1), D/NR (player 2); B) P/NR, D/NR; C) P/R, D/R; D) P/NR, D/R. Parameters include payoffs from status changes (−V, +U), training length j, total rounds N, cost and benefit parameters (c1,g2,d1,d2,D1,D2), mistake probability p, and derived expectations μ1, μ2.","Box 3 categorizes strategies by actions at successive steps (P/NR, P/R, D/NR, D/R), introduces V and U, and tabulates for each candidate ESS the inequalities that must hold to prevent invasion, all depending on x and other parameters.",1,False,This section continues the theoretical analysis; no experimental tests.,1,False,No treatment groups or observed outcomes; purely mathematical ESS analysis.,1,False,Not carried out in a lab setting.,1,False,Reasoning is analytic; no numerical simulation outcomes are provided.,1,True,Uses inequalities involving μ1 and μ2 to classify when each ESS is stable under unilateral deviations.,1,2,Same two-player structure as in Boxes 1–2.,1,1,N and corrected active rounds N' are symbolic; numeric value not specified.,0,1,"Choices are discrete strategy actions (P/R, NR etc.), not resource contributions; public-goods framing not applicable but schema requires a binary.",0.2,N/A,No endowment or public account is modeled.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return concept appears.,1,N/A,No subject communication; this is an abstract game.,1,0,No explicit feedback display; outcomes fold into strategy learning but not as experimental summaries.,0.3,N/A,Identity is not an experimental variable; the model has two fixed roles.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in these ESSs.,1,0,"Although N is used in formulas, there is no notion of rounds being displayed to participants.",0.3,1,All candidate ESSs involve a punishing strategy (P) by player 1 paired with subordinate desisting.,1,N/R,"Punishment costs (d1, and potential V, D1 via escalation) are symbolic; no numeric values given.",1,N/R,"The game specifies expected values from escalation, not a discrete punishment technology per unit spent.",1,0,Only punishment and retaliatory aggression are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,N/R,No resource endowment per round in standard game-theoretic sense; only fitness gains/losses.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are ESS existence conditions, not contribution behavior.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,1,N/R,No efficiency (aggregate payoff) ratios are explicitly derived.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are expressed as formulas, not instantiated with numbers to report group totals.",1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical ESS analysis of animal punishment strategies.,1,"Box 3 analyzes four punishment-related ESS candidates in the retaliatory game: (A) P/R (player 1), D/NR (player 2) – commonsense punishment where stronger player 1 punishes and is willing to escalate; (B) P/NR, D/NR – no retaliation; (C) P/R, D/R – both retaliate; (D) P/NR, D/R – weak punisher vs strong retaliator. A table lists, for each candidate, the mutant deviations (J1, J2) and required inequalities involving kc1, jd1, p, V, U, D1, D2, μ1, μ2 and corrected active-round counts Na–Nd. The analysis concludes that ESS A (P/R, D/NR) is by far the most likely when player 1 is stronger, making punishment typically flow from dominant to subordinate.",Condenses the detailed ESS table and narrative conclusions in Box 3.,1
10.1038_373209a0.md,Box 4 – Non-punishment ESSs with retaliation,Box 4 explicitly introduces 'Non-punishment ESSs' in a related transgression-retaliation game focusing on weak vs strong transgression and retaliation by the owner.,1,"Strategies in a 2-player contest over a resource without explicit punishment: player 2 (potential transgressor) chooses Weak Transgression (WT) vs Strong Transgression (ST); player 1 (resource owner) chooses Retaliate (R) vs Non-retaliate (NR). Parameters: N rounds, resource value g2 to player 2 and c1 to player 1, injury probability x (player 1 loses with probability x), injury costs D1, D2.","Box 4 defines WT and ST for player 2 and R/NR for player 1 and evaluates four candidate ESSs (WT,R; WT,NR; ST,R; ST,NR) with conditions based on c1,g2,x,D1,D2.",1,False,This is still part of the theoretical modeling section.,1,False,No implementation as an experiment; only conceptual discussion.,1,False,Not run with live subjects.,1,False,No computational results; arguments are algebraic/qualitative.,1,True,Evaluates ESS conditions by comparing expected payoffs of WT vs ST and R vs NR.,1,2,The game is between player 1 (resource owner) and player 2 (transgressor).,1,1,The text notes there are N rounds but does not specify a numeric value.,0,1,"Action choice is categorical (WT vs ST; R vs NR), not continuous contributions; public-good all-or-nothing notion does not apply but schema forces a value.",0.2,N/A,No contribution structure.,1,N/R,No public good or multiplier.,1,N/A,No implemented communication stage.,1,0,Theoretical model has no experimental interface or feedback summaries.,0.3,N/A,Punishment in the sense of earlier boxes is replaced here by retaliation against transgression of a resource; identity display not relevant.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,"Though N rounds are assumed, nothing is described as being displayed to players.",0.3,1,"While labelled 'non-punishment ESSs', the behavior R (retaliate) functions as costly aggression deterring transgression and is effectively punishment of cheating.",0.6,N/R,"Cost to retaliating player is given abstractly via injury probability x and injury cost D1, but not numerically.",1,N/R,No token-based punishment technology; fights occur with probabilistic injury.,1,0,"The game only involves resource defense and transgression, not prosocial rewards.",1,N/A,Reward is absent.,1,N/A,Reward is absent.,1,N/R,"Each round’s resource is valued at c1 or g2, but these are not described as an endowment per player in the usual game-theoretic sense.",1,N/R,"The outcome of interest is which ESS (WT,R vs ST,NR) is stable depending on strength, not contribution to a common pool.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,The paper does not compute efficiency even for these ESSs; only qualitative likelihoods.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoff per ESS is not numerically derived.,1,N/A,No empirical study.,1,N/A,No empirical study.,1,N/A,No empirical study.,1,N/A,No empirical study.,1,No human,"Non-empirical, analytical game-theoretic model.",1,"Player 2 chooses between WT (weak transgression) and ST (strong transgression) against player 1’s resource; player 1 chooses R (retaliate) or NR. WT gains only if player 1 plays NR and withdraws if player 1 plays R, so WT entails no risk of injury. ST persists even if player 1 retaliates, leading to a hawk–dove style escalated fight where player 1 loses with probability x at cost D1, and player 2 loses with probability 1 − x at cost D2. Four candidate ESSs are: WT,NR (ruled out); ST,R (requires both players to have positive expected values from escalation, unlikely if damage is high or asymmetry large); WT,R (favoured if player 1 is stronger and has positive expectation from fighting); ST,NR (favoured if player 2 is stronger). Authors conclude that common-sense outcomes where the dominant retains resources by force (WT,R) or takes resources from subordinates (ST,NR) are most likely.",Summarizes textual explanation of Box 4’s payoff matrix and ESS conclusions.,1
10.1007_s10683-010-9257-1.md,Fehr and Gächter (2000) – Partners – No Punishment (Rounds 1–10),"Paper describes Fehr and Gächter (2000) design: 20 rounds with partners protocol; first 10 rounds without punishment, second 10 with punishment. This row is the initial no-punishment phase for partners groups.",0.94,Punishment option: absent; Matching protocol: partners; Rounds: 1–10 of 20,"Independent variables varied are availability of costly punishment (on/off) and matching protocol (partners vs strangers in original paper). Here: no punishment, partners, early block.",0.88,True,Laboratory public goods experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to conditions.,1,True,Described as laboratory public goods experiment; no indication of online or field.,0.96,False,"Human-subject experiment, not computer simulation.",1,False,No analytical model-only condition; this is experimental data.,1,4,Paper: “Participants play for 20 rounds in groups of four.”,1,10,This condition is the first 10 rounds without punishment in a 20-round game.,0.98,1,Standard linear continuous public goods: contribution C can range from 0 to full endowment in whole tokens; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,0.96,0,Standard design: endowment starts in private account and subjects decide how much to allocate to public account.,0.9,0.4,Explicitly stated: “In each round a participant has an endowment of 20 tokens and the MPCR is 0.4.”,1,0,No mention of communication; standard no-communication VCM as in Fehr & Gächter (2000).,0.9,1,Participants observe others’ contributions; necessary to compute punishments in later block and standard in this paradigm.,0.78,0,Paper: “having seen the contributions of others (but without learning their identities) can choose to punish”; anonymity implies IDs for punishment not revealed.,0.86,0,No reward mechanism in this study.,1,1,Finite horizon 20 rounds; in this literature subjects are normally informed of total rounds; text implies they know the 20-round structure.,0.75,0,This is the no-punishment phase: “the first 10 rounds without any punishment possibility.”,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,0,Only costly punishment is discussed; no positive rewards.,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Explicitly: “In each round a participant has an endowment of 20 tokens.”,1,0.19,"Across all rounds and treatments, average contribution is 19% without punishment vs 58% with; here we use 19% for no-punishment condition as normalized rate.",0.8,N/R,"Paper reports only percentages, not average tokens contributed.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff/maximum) figures for this specific treatment block.,0.2,N/R,No numerical earnings reported by condition.,0.2,N/R,"Fehr & Gächter (2000) typically used Swiss students, but this survey article does not restate country; cannot be certain from provided text.",0.3,N/R,No age information reported in this survey excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0.1,N/R,Likely undergraduates but not stated in this text.,0.3,On site,Described as laboratory experiment; Internet not mentioned; unlike Egas & Riedl (2008) which is explicitly online.,0.9,Linear public goods game with partners protocol over 20 rounds; this condition is first 10 rounds without punishment. Anonymity regarding identities; subjects see others’ contributions each round.,Summarizes structural details mentioned for this study relevant to game but not covered in specific config fields.,0.9
10.1007_s10683-010-9257-1.md,Fehr and Gächter (2000) – Partners – Punishment (Rounds 11–20),Same experiment; second block of 10 rounds introduces punishment in partners protocol.,0.96,Punishment option: present; Matching protocol: partners; Rounds: 11–20 of 20,"Independent variable here is availability of costly punishment, turned on for second 10 rounds under partners matching.",0.9,True,Lab public goods with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment availability.,1,True,Standard lab environment.,0.96,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Not purely theoretical.,1,4,Groups of four in this study.,1,10,This condition is the 10 punishment rounds out of 20.,0.98,1,Linear continuous contribution choice 0–20.,0.96,0,Endowment initially in private account.,0.9,0.4,Explicit MPCR for all rounds.,1,0,No communication allowed in design description.,0.9,1,Participants “having seen the contributions of others” then punish; implies feedback of others’ contributions each round.,0.9,0,They observe contributions but “without learning their identities”; thus punishment not linked to personal IDs.,0.9,0,No positive reward option in this study.,1,1,Finite horizon (20 rounds) normally announced; not contradicted here.,0.75,1,Second-stage punishment is central to this block: “can choose to punish the other group members.”,1,0.5,"Costs per punishment point in tokens: 0,1,2,4,6,9,12,16,20,25,30 for 0–10 points. Average marginal cost per point (excluding 0) ≈ (1+2+4+6+9+12+16+20+25+30)/10=12.5/?, but these are cumulative schedules. As a single scalar, we approximate linearized marginal cost at midrange ≈5 points where total cost 9 implies ~1.8 per point; however specification asks for per-unit cost. Table is nonlinear, so we set to N/R.",0.2,0.1,"Each punishment point reduces recipient’s payoff by 10% of their earnings; if we normalize 10% of typical round earnings (~full endowment 20+0.4*4*20=52) that’s ~5.2 tokens per point; tech is proportional to payoff not fixed tokens. As spec wants magnitude per coin, best literal statement is 0.1 fraction of payoff; encoded as 0.1.",0.4,0,"Only negative punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment each round.,1,0.58,Survey: “Across all rounds and the two treatments the average contribution to the public good is 19% without punishment and 58% with punishments.” Use 58% as normalized contribution rate for punishment condition.,0.9,N/R,"Only percentages of endowment reported, no token means.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio for this treatment reported here.,0.2,N/R,No numeric payoff figures broken out by treatment.,0.2,N/R,Country not mentioned in this survey excerpt.,0.3,N/R,Age of subjects not provided.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,N/R,Likely students but not specified.,0.3,On site,Laboratory study; not online or field.,0.9,"Two-stage rounds: Stage 1 standard VCM, Stage 2 punishment after seeing others’ contributions. Each punishment point reduces punished participant’s payoff by 10%; punishment costs follow nonlinear schedule (Table 1).",Captures multi-stage structure and nonlinear punishment cost schedule.,0.95
10.1007_s10683-010-9257-1.md,Gürerk et al. (2006) – Sanction-Free Institution (SFI),Survey describes Gürerk et al. (2006) with two institutional choices: sanctioning institution (SI) and sanction-free institution (SFI). This row is SFI condition.,0.97,Institutional choice: Sanction-Free Institution (no punishment/reward stage); Endogenous self-selection into SFI,Independent variable is institutional environment chosen by subjects (sanctioning vs sanction-free).,0.95,True,Laboratory public goods experiment with human subjects choosing institutions.,1,True,Controlled between-subjects design with institutional choice.,1,True,Described as experiment with random rematching; no online/field mention.,0.9,False,Not an agent-based or numeric simulation.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical model.,1,N/R,"Survey text does not state group size explicitly for Gürerk et al. (2006). Original paper uses 4, but that is not in this excerpt.",0.3,30,“The experiment consists of 30 rounds and participants are randomly re-matched at the end of each round.”,1,1,Described as linear public goods game; standard continuous contribution 0–endowment.,0.9,0,Standard linear VCM: endowment initially private; no indication of pre-filled public account.,0.8,N/R,Survey summary does not mention MPCR value.,0.1,0,No communication stage described; focus is on institutional choice and punishment/reward incentives.,0.8,1,Sanctions depend on others’ contributions; feedback on contributions and maybe sanctions is required and implied by description of migration and norm adoption.,0.7,0,"Survey does not specify whether identities are linked to punishment; original paper uses anonymity. Here, leave as 0 (not revealed).",0.6,0,Positive sanctions (rewards) exist only in SI; SFI has no sanctions.,0.95,1,Finite horizon of 30 rounds likely known to subjects as standard; not contradicted.,0.75,0,By definition SFI has no sanctioning stage: “round ends here for participants who choose to be in the sanction-free institution.”,1,N/A,No punishment in SFI.,1,N/A,No punishment in SFI.,1,0,Rewards only within SI via positive sanction points; SFI has no sanctioning at all.,1,N/A,No rewards in SFI.,1,N/A,No rewards in SFI.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not stated in survey text.,0.1,0.14,"“Averaged across all rounds, contributions in the SI are 91%, significantly higher than the 14% in the SFI.” Use 14% as normalized rate.",0.95,N/R,Only percentages of endowment reported (no token amounts).,0.2,N/R,No explicit payoff-vs-maximum figures given for SFI separately.,0.2,N/R,No numerical payoffs reported in survey excerpt.,0.2,N/R,Survey does not specify location; original is German lab but not mentioned here.,0.3,N/R,No age info.,0.1,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0.1,N/R,Likely university students but not explicit in this text.,0.4,On site,Described as standard experimental sessions with random rematching; no indication of online implementation.,0.85,"Multi-stage endogenous institutional choice each round: Stage 1 choose institution (SI or SFI); Stage 2 play linear public goods game. For SFI, round ends after contributions. Random rematching of participants across groups each period.",Summarizes condition-specific game flow where institution has no sanction stage.,0.95
10.1007_s10683-010-9257-1.md,Gürerk et al. (2006) – Sanctioning Institution (SI),Same study; this row is the sanctioning institution environment with both positive and negative sanctions.,0.98,Institutional choice: Sanctioning Institution (punishment and reward available); Endogenous self-selection into SI,Independent variable is participation in the sanctioning institution where sanctions are possible.,0.95,True,Human lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of institutional environment.,1,True,Standard lab protocol.,0.9,False,Human-subject data.,1,False,Not only theory.,1,N/R,Group size not specified in survey paragraph.,0.3,30,30 rounds total per experiment.,1,1,Linear public goods with continuous decisions assumed.,0.9,0,Standard VCM setup; no evidence of pre-allocation to public account.,0.8,N/R,MPCR value not given in this excerpt.,0.1,0,No communication mentioned; focus is on institutional choice and sanctions.,0.8,1,"To award positive/negative sanctions, subjects must observe others’ contributions; text notes adoption of SI norms based on observed behavior.",0.8,0,Survey does not mention identification of punishers; typical design uses anonymity; set to not revealed.,0.6,0,"Similarly, reward identities not stated as revealed.",0.6,1,Finite horizon (30) likely announced.,0.75,1,“Participants who choose to be in the sanctioning institution continue to stage 3 where they can allocate either positive or negative sanction points.” Negative sanctions are punishments.,1,1,“A negative sanction (a punishment) costs the recipient 3 tokens and costs the sender 1 token.” Cost to punisher per point = 1 token.,1,3,Each negative sanction point reduces recipient’s payoff by 3 tokens.,1,1,Positive sanctions: “A positive sanction (actually a reward) awards the recipient 1 token and costs the sender 1 token.”,1,1,Cost to sender of a positive sanction point is 1 token.,1,1,Each reward point increases recipient’s payoff by 1 token.,1,N/R,Per-round endowment not specified in this summary.,0.1,0.91,"“Averaged across all rounds, contributions in the SI are 91%...”. Use as normalized rate.",0.95,N/R,Only contribution percentages reported.,0.2,N/R,Text mentions payoffs qualitatively but not as fraction of maximum or by condition.,0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoff provided.,0.2,N/R,Country or lab not named in the survey summary.,0.3,N/R,No age reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,N/R,Likely students but not explicitly stated.,0.4,On site,"Typical lab sessions, unlike Egas & Riedl which is explicitly online.",0.85,"Each round: Stage 1 choose institution; Stage 2 play linear public goods; Stage 3 (SI only) assign positive or negative sanction points to other SI members. Random rematching across rounds; migration between SI and SFI allowed each round, leading to near-universal adoption of SI over time.","Captures presence of both reward and punishment, multistage structure, and endogenous migration in SI condition.",0.96
10.1007_s10683-010-9257-1.md,Egas and Riedl (2008) – Low Cost–High Impact Punishment,"Survey notes Egas & Riedl (2008) have multiple punishment treatments and that “the only punishment treatment, which succeeds in sustaining cooperation over time is the low cost-high impact treatment where each punishment point costs the punisher one token but reduces the recipient's payoff by three tokens.” This row captures that treatment.",0.9,"Punishment technology: low cost–high impact (cost 1, impact 3); Online participant pool",Independent variable within that study is punishment cost-impact parameters; here we record the stated low-cost high-impact configuration.,0.88,True,“846 participants… experiments were conducted over the Internet.” Human participants.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of punishment technology.,1,False,"Run over the Internet, not in a physical lab.",0.95,False,Not a pure simulation; real human decisions.,1,False,"Experimental, not analytical-only.",1,N/R,Survey summary does not restate group size.,0.2,N/R,Text does not specify number of rounds in this summary.,0.2,1,Linear public goods game context; implies continuous contributions in tokens.,0.85,0,Standard VCM assumptions; no suggestion of pre-funded public account.,0.7,N/R,MPCR not mentioned in this survey passage.,0.1,0,No communication described; focus is on punishment technology and subject pool.,0.7,1,"To punish, subjects must observe others’ contributions; standard in such designs though not spelled out.",0.7,0,Anonymity standard in punishment VCMs; not contradicted in text; identities of punished likely not revealed.,0.6,0,No mention of rewards; only costly punishment technology discussed.,0.95,1,"Typical finite-horizon public goods experiments inform subjects; although not explicit, assume yes.",0.6,1,This is a punishment treatment; focus on cost-impact of punishment.,1,1,Explicit: “each punishment point costs the punisher one token but reduces the recipient's payoff by three tokens.”,1,3,Each punishment point reduces recipient by 3 tokens.,1,0,No mention of rewards; only punishment described.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Per-round endowment not provided in this summary.,0.1,N/R,Survey only notes that cooperation is “sustained” over time; no specific percentage reported.,0.2,N/R,No token-level contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,Described qualitatively as maintaining cooperation; no efficiency ratios.,0.2,N/R,No numeric payoff data in survey text.,0.2,Netherlands,Survey: “any Dutch-speaking person eligible to participate… experiments were conducted over the Internet.” Dutch-speaking suggests Netherlands or Flemish Belgium; original paper is Dutch; choose Netherlands as most likely.,0.6,"Mean 35, range 12–80",Survey: “average age is 35 years with a range of 12–80 years.”,1,N/R,No gender composition provided.,0.1,Heterogeneous (non-student population),"Survey: “non-standard pool… any Dutch-speaking person eligible to participate, making the participants much more representative of the population as a whole.”",0.8,Online,“Participants were recruited via newspaper advertisements and the experiments were conducted over the Internet.”,1,"Large-sample (N=846) online linear public goods experiment with multiple punishment treatments; this condition uses low-cost high-impact punishment (cost 1, effect 3). Study also reports that older males are more likely to punish controlling for contributions.","Summarizes distinctive features (online, large N, demography effects) relevant to this punishment condition.",0.9
10.1006_jtbi.2000.2111.md,Fehr & Gächter (2000) – Public goods with costly punishment – Partner treatment (punishment first),"The paper describes a ten-round public goods game with costly punishment run under three treatments (Partner, Stranger, Perfect Stranger); this row is the Partner (called Personal in one sentence, but later clearly Partner) punishment condition, which is the main focus in Fig. 1 when punishment is played first.",0.84,Group matching structure with punishment enabled; this condition has fixed partners (same 4 subjects interact all 10 periods) and costly punishment available.,Independent variable is the matching protocol (Partner vs Stranger vs Perfect Stranger) while keeping the same public good and punishment parameters; this row is the fixed-partner case with punishment present.,0.86,True,Human subjects participated in a laboratory public goods experiment yielding real money earnings.,1,True,Design involves controlled manipulation of punishment and matching conditions in a lab setting.,1,True,Described as an experimental session with points redeemable for money; this is standard lab terminology and not a field context.,0.96,False,"This treatment uses human subjects, not a computer-only simulation.",1,False,"This specific condition is an empirical lab experiment, not an analytical model.",1,4,Explicitly: “each subject being grouped with three other subjects” implying groups of four.,1,10,Fehr & Gächter protocol and later description: a ten-round public goods game with punishment for this condition when punishment is played first.,0.95,1,"Subjects can contribute “some number of the 20 points” to the common account, i.e., any integer amount from 0 to 20, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is given as 20 points to each subject and they choose how many to move into the common account; initial state is fully private.,0.95,0.4,Total contribution is multiplied such that each subject gets 40% of the sum of contributions into the common account; the marginal per capita return is therefore 0.4.,1,0,"Only contributions, feedback, and punishment are mentioned, under ‘strict anonymity’; no communication or chat is described.",0.88,1,"After each round “the experimenter then tells the subjects how many points were contributed to the common account,” which is a group-level contribution summary.",0.9,0,"Environment is “strict anonymity”; no indication that identities of punishers or targets are revealed, only that punishment can be imposed.",0.7,0,"No reward mechanism is mentioned at all, so no associated identity revelation.",0.95,1,"Explicit: “The experiment continues for exactly ten rounds, and the subjects are informed of this fact at the start of the experiment.”",1,1,Fehr & Gächter condition is explicitly ‘with costly punishment’ and cost structure is described.,1,N/R,"Footnote states that low levels of punishment (1–2 points) were equally costly to punisher and punishee and a 10-point punishment cost the punisher 30 points, implying a nonlinear schedule; no single per-unit cost parameter can be extracted.",0.3,N/R,"Only relative and extreme costs are given (e.g., 10-point punishment costs punisher 30 points); exact fine per unit of punisher’s expenditure is not fully reported.",0.3,0,Only ‘fining’ and punishment are discussed; no reward or positive sanctioning mechanism is mentioned.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,20,Explicitly: “Each subject is then given 20 ‘points’… Each subject then places some number of the 20 points in a ‘common account’.”,1,N/R,Figure 1 is referenced (“Average contributions over time”) but the article does not reproduce the numeric values; only qualitative statements like ‘cooperation increases almost to full cooperation’.,0.2,N/R,No exact mean contribution numbers per round are reported in this summary; only trends are described.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff/maximum payoff ratios or similar efficiency measures are provided.,0.1,N/R,"Only average individual earnings (~$35 per session) are mentioned, with no mapping to points or group totals.",0.2,N/R,"The summary does not specify where Fehr & Gächter ran the experiment (e.g., country or city).",0.1,N/R,No information on age or student status is reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition details are mentioned.,0.1,N/R,No data on education level or student/non-student status are given.,0.1,On site,Described as a laboratory-style ‘experimental session’ with real money; standard reading is an in-person lab experiment rather than online or field.,0.85,"Linear public goods game with 4 players, each endowed with 20 points per round. Players choose how many points to contribute to a common account. Total contributions are transformed such that each player receives 40% of the sum of contributions, yielding b=24 and c=12 if a subject contributes the full 20 points. Ten rounds are played; in this treatment the same four subjects remain together all 10 rounds (partner design) and can impose costly fines on others under strict anonymity.",Summarizes specific structure and parameters of the Fehr & Gächter public goods with punishment partner treatment as described in the text.,0.96
10.1006_jtbi.2000.2111.md,Fehr & Gächter (2000) – Public goods with costly punishment – Stranger treatment (punishment first),"Same public goods with punishment protocol, but here subjects are randomly reassigned to new groups after each round (Stranger treatment) when punishment is played first.",0.86,Group matching structure with punishment enabled; this condition has random rematching of subjects each period (Stranger) and costly punishment available.,Independent variable is matching protocol; this row corresponds to the Stranger treatment with punishment present.,0.9,True,Human subjects in a lab experiment with payoffs in real money.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of matching and punishment in a laboratory setting.,1,True,Described as an experimental session with multiple groups and monetary rewards; typical on-site lab experiment.,0.96,False,"Uses human participants, not only simulated agents.",1,False,Condition belongs to an empirical lab experiment rather than analytical derivation.,1,4,The underlying public goods game is described with one subject grouped with three others (groups of four).,1,10,"Fehr & Gächter used a ten-round public goods game with punishment, unless noted otherwise (only Perfect Stranger needed fewer rounds).",0.9,1,"Subjects choose “some number of the 20 points” to contribute; this implies a continuous (integer) choice, not an all-or-nothing decision.",1,0,"Each subject initially gets all 20 points privately, then decides how many to allocate to the common account.",0.95,0.4,"Each subject’s payoff from the public account equals 40% of the total group contributions, so marginal per capita return is 0.4.",1,0,Only contribution and punishment choices plus feedback under anonymity are described; communication is not mentioned.,0.88,1,"Subjects are informed how many points were contributed to the common account, giving a group-level summary.",0.9,0,Strict anonymity is emphasized and no identification of punishers is described.,0.7,0,There is no mention of any reward mechanism.,0.95,1,Subjects are explicitly told the game lasts for exactly ten rounds.,1,1,This is a costly punishment treatment; footnote details punishment cost structure identical across matching conditions.,1,N/R,"Only qualitative details (low-level punishment equally costly, 10-point punishment costs 30 points) are provided; per-unit cost is nonlinear and not summarized in a single parameter.",0.3,N/R,The fine imposed per unit of expenditure is not precisely parameterized in the text.,0.3,0,Only fining (punishment) is discussed; no positive reward option appears.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism is available in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is available in this treatment.,1,20,Every subject is endowed with 20 points per round in the described protocol.,1,N/R,"Fig. 1 indicates that punishment prevents deterioration of contributions, but no exact average fractions of endowment contributed are numerically reported.",0.25,N/R,"No mean point contributions per round are provided, only qualitative trends.",0.25,N/R,No ratio of achieved to maximum possible payoffs is given.,0.1,N/R,Group-level payoff statistics are not reported; only average individual money earnings are mentioned and not decomposed by treatment.,0.2,N/R,The location of Fehr & Gächter’s lab (country) is not mentioned in this summary.,0.1,N/R,No age or student status information is supplied.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information is reported.,0.1,N/R,No information on educational background is provided.,0.1,On site,Described as a multi-group experimental session with real money; standard interpretation is an in-person lab experiment.,0.85,"Same linear public goods and punishment technology as in the Partner treatment: 4 players, 20-point endowment each, 40% return from the public account to each member, ten periods, costly fines available. In this Stranger treatment, subjects are randomly reassigned to new anonymous groups each round, but punishment is still possible; punishment prevents decay of cooperation but does not raise contributions to near full cooperation as in the Partner treatment.",Summarizes unique matching structure and behavioral pattern for the Stranger punishment condition.,0.94
10.1006_jtbi.2000.2111.md,Fehr & Gächter (2000) – Public goods with costly punishment – Perfect Stranger treatment (punishment first),"This condition is explicitly described as the Perfect Stranger treatment with costly punishment, where subjects are randomly reassigned and never meet the same counterpart twice; punishment condition is played first in the context of Fig. 1.",0.86,Group matching structure with punishment enabled; this condition has random rematching with the constraint that no two subjects meet more than once (Perfect Stranger) and costly punishment available.,Independent variable is matching protocol; this row covers the Perfect Stranger punishment treatment.,0.9,True,Human subjects participated in a lab public goods game with monetary incentives.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of group reassignment and punishment availability in a laboratory.,1,True,Conducted as an experimental session with points and cash redemption; standard on-site laboratory setting.,0.96,False,"The condition uses human participants, not simulated agents.",1,False,"This is an empirical lab condition, not an analytical derivation.",1,4,Underlying public goods protocol uses groups of four (one subject with three others).,1,6,"Specifically: “under the Perfect Stranger treatment the subjects were randomly reassigned and assured that they would never meet another subject more than once (in this case, the number of rounds had to be reduced from ten to six to accommodate the size of the subject pool).”",1,1,Subjects choose any number of the 20 points to contribute; contribution is not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each subject starts with 20 points in a private account and decides how much to move to the public account.,0.95,0.4,Each player receives 40% of the total contributions to the public account; marginal per capita return equals 0.4.,1,0,Only contribution and punishment decisions and feedback are mentioned; the protocol stresses anonymity and does not mention communication.,0.88,1,Subjects learn how many points were contributed to the common account each round.,0.9,0,Strict anonymity is maintained with no suggestion that the identity of punishers or punished is revealed.,0.7,0,No rewards (positive sanctions) are available.,0.95,1,Subjects are informed of the exact number of periods; for Perfect Stranger this is stated to be six rounds.,0.95,1,This is part of the ‘ten-round public goods game with costly punishment’ design; only the number of rounds changes to six in this treatment.,1,N/R,"Punishment is described qualitatively (e.g., 10-point punishment costs 30 points to punisher), but full per-unit cost schedule is not specified.",0.3,N/R,Magnitude of fine per unit of punisher cost is not fully parameterized; multiple levels exist.,0.3,0,Only fining behavior (punishment) is discussed; no reward option is present.,0.95,N/A,There is no reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,20,The same 20-point endowment public goods protocol applies across all three matching treatments.,1,N/R,"Fig. 1 shows time paths of contributions, and text states that punishment prevents deterioration but does not give numerical averages by round for this treatment.",0.25,N/R,No explicit mean contribution values in points are printed.,0.25,N/R,No efficiency statistics (realized vs. maximum group payoff) are reported.,0.1,N/R,No total or average group payoff values are reported for this treatment.,0.2,N/R,Country or city where the experiment was carried out is not noted in this summary.,0.1,N/R,Age of participants is not given.,0.1,N/R,Gender breakdown is not described.,0.1,N/R,No information is given on education or student status.,0.1,On site,Run as part of the same lab experimental sessions as the other treatments with multiple groups simultaneously.,0.85,"Linear public goods game identical in payoff structure to the Partner and Stranger treatments: 4 players, 20-point endowment, 40% return from the public pool to each member, costly fines available. Perfect Stranger matching means subjects are randomly reassigned each round with the additional restriction that no pair of subjects ever meets twice; due to this constraint, only six rounds are played. Punishment prevents the usual decay in contributions despite the absence of repeated encounters.",Summarizes what is specific about the Perfect Stranger matching condition and its behavioral outcome.,0.94
10.1006_jtbi.2000.2111.md,Fehr & Gächter (2000) – Public goods without punishment – Partner/Stranger/Perfect Stranger pooled (no-punishment phase),"The article states that Fehr & Gächter ran ten rounds with punishment and ten rounds without, and that when punishment is not permitted, “the same subjects experience the deterioration of cooperation.” The no-punishment phase is mentioned but not separated by matching condition in this summary.",0.6,"Punishment disabled (no possibility to fine others); same public goods structure and matching protocols as in the punishment phase, but analyzed here as a combined no-punishment condition because the summary does not break down results by matching regime.",The main manipulated factor across phases is availability of costly punishment; this row captures the no-punishment condition as described in aggregate.,0.65,True,Human participants played the same public goods game in a phase where punishment was disabled.,1,True,This condition is part of the same controlled laboratory experiment.,1,True,"Same lab environment as the punishment phase, with real monetary payoffs.",0.96,False,Involves human subjects.,1,False,"Empirical experimental condition, not a theoretical model.",1,4,Same 4-person public goods protocol is implied when punishment is off; only the sanction option changes.,0.95,10,Text: “Fehr & Gächter (2000) performed their experiment for ten rounds with punishment and ten rounds without.” The no-punishment phase thus spans ten rounds.,0.95,1,Contribution choice structure is unchanged: subjects choose some number of the 20 points.,1,0,Endowments start entirely in private accounts as before.,0.95,0.4,The public goods payoff technology is the same (40% of total contributions to each subject).,1,0,No communication is introduced in the description of the no-punishment phase.,0.88,1,"As in the punishment phase, subjects are informed of the total contributed to the common account each round.",0.9,N/A,"Punishment is not available in this condition, so no identities of punishers can be shown.",1,0,No reward option is added in the no-punishment phase; the game is a standard public goods game.,0.95,1,Subjects are informed about the finite number of rounds; the structure mirrors the punishment phase where the horizon is common knowledge.,0.9,0,Explicitly described as ‘ten rounds without [punishment]’.,1,N/A,"Punishment mechanism is disabled, so no cost parameter applies.",1,N/A,Punishment mechanism is disabled.,1,0,No reward mechanism is introduced; this is the standard linear public goods game.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,The no-punishment phase uses the same public goods game with 20-point endowment per round.,0.95,N/R,The summary notes that cooperation deteriorates over time (‘decay of cooperation’) but does not provide exact contribution fractions per round for this phase.,0.25,N/R,No mean point contributions are reported for the no-punishment phase.,0.25,N/R,No explicit efficiency or payoff ratio is presented.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs are not quantified for the no-punishment condition.,0.2,N/R,The country or lab location is not detailed.,0.1,N/R,Participant ages are not mentioned.,0.1,N/R,No gender information is given.,0.1,N/R,No educational background information is reported.,0.1,On site,Same lab experimental setting as the punishment phase.,0.96,"Standard linear public goods game with 4 players per group, 20-point endowments per round, and an MPCR of 0.4. The game is played for 10 rounds without any possibility of costly punishment. Subjects know the finite horizon. Contributions start at moderate levels but decline over time, leading to behavior close to full free-riding in later periods, mirroring results in earlier public goods experiments.",Summarizes key structural aspects of the no-punishment phase as described in the article.,0.9
10.1006_jtbi.2000.2111.md,Gintis – Evolutionary model of strong reciprocity (analytical and simulated trait-group model),"Sections 4 and beyond describe a formal evolutionary model using Price’s equation, followed by computer simulations (Mathematica) to explore equilibrium fractions of strong reciprocators; this is a non-empirical condition.",0.9,"Model parameters including: discount factors in good and bad periods (δ* and δ*_), probability of bad periods p, cooperation cost c, benefit to others b, harm inflicted per strong reciprocator h, retaliation/surveillance cost c_r, group size k, minimum strong reciprocator fraction f_* needed for cooperation, and founder-group composition (binomial sampling with mean fraction \bar{f}). For the simulation illustrated in Fig. 2, example settings include δ* = 0.95, δ_* = 0.10, p = 0.10, group size k = 40 or 8, and f_* = 3/8, with c_r varied from 0.05 to 1.",The model studies how the equilibrium frequency of strong reciprocators depends on these parameters; Fig. 2 varies c_r for two group sizes to obtain equilibrium \bar{f}*.,0.93,False,This part of the paper is a theoretical evolutionary model evaluated with computer simulation; no human subjects are involved.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; it is a theoretical and computational analysis.,1,False,Not a laboratory experiment; it is analytical plus simulation work.,1,True,Author explicitly states: “Simulating this model (I used Mathematica 3.0) allows us to assess the plausibility of the parameters involved and the nature of the equilibrium fraction …”,1,True,Large portion of this section derives conditions using Price’s equation and closed-form expressions before simulation.,1,N/A,"This is not a human-subject game but a population-genetic style model; groups are of size k (e.g., 40 or 8) but these are theoretical group sizes for trait dynamics, not game participants in an experiment.",0.95,N/A,"Time is modeled as evolutionary periods and repeated interaction states, not discrete experimental rounds.",1,N/A,No explicit discrete contribution-choice structure is modeled; agents either cooperate or defect according to strategy types.,1,N/A,No initial monetary endowment or allocation is used for human subjects.,1,N/A,The model is framed in terms of fitness costs and benefits (c and b) rather than points with a defined MPCR for an experimental game.,1,N/A,No human communication; this is a theoretical model and simulation.,1,N/A,No experimental interface where agents observe payoffs; model is specified via fitness parameters.,1,N/A,No explicit identity or information structure for punishment decisions in a human experiment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in a human experimental sense.,1,N/A,No concept of informing human players about number of rounds.,1,1,"The model crucially includes strong reciprocators who ‘inflict a total amount of harm h on non-cooperators, at a personal cost c_r’; this is a punishment mechanism.",1,N/R,"Punishment cost is parameterized as c_r in fitness units, but no single numeric value is fixed across the analysis; instead, results are derived for generic c_r and simulated for ranges (0.05–1 in Fig. 2).",0.4,N/R,The harm per strong reciprocator is h but no specific numeric value for h is set in text when illustrating Fig. 2; the relationship is analyzed symbolically.,0.4,0,Model only considers costly punishment (harm to defectors) and surveillance costs; no positive rewards for cooperators are modeled.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this model.,1,N/A,The model tracks evolutionary fitness rather than monetary endowments per round.,1,N/R,The model does not produce per-round contribution fractions as in a lab game; it characterizes conditions for cooperation vs. defection and the equilibrium fraction of strong reciprocators.,0.9,N/R,No monetary or token contributions are modeled; only fitness costs and benefits are considered.,1,N/R,"While the model compares fitness of cooperative vs. non-cooperative groups, it does not define an explicit normalized efficiency metric between 0 and 1 for payoffs.",0.7,N/R,"Expressions for group fitness π and π_s are provided in terms of parameters, but no single numeric group payoff is reported as a simulation output.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; this is a theoretical and simulated model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is an analytical model explored via computer simulation (Mathematica); there is no human or field environment.,1,"Analytical trait-group model based on Price’s equation with groups that face public-goods-like cooperation problems under good and bad environmental states. Individuals are of two types: self-interested agents, who cooperate only when it is fitness-maximizing given discount factors δ* and δ_*, and strong reciprocators, who always cooperate and punish non-cooperators even in bad states. In bad states, if the fraction f_i of strong reciprocators exceeds a threshold f_* = (c − π δ_*)/(c − π δ_* + h), their punishment induces full cooperation; otherwise, the group fails in bad states, leading to lower fitness π_s. Groups form via a fixed-size founder process of size k, with trait frequencies in new groups drawn from a binomial distribution with mean \bar{f}. Using these assumptions, conditions are derived (e.g., inequality (16)) under which the population frequency of strong reciprocators increases. Simulations (Mathematica 3.0) then compute the equilibrium fraction \bar{f}* of strong reciprocators as a function of punishment cost c_r and group size k (e.g., k = 40 or 8) under sample parameter values δ* = 0.95, δ_* = 0.10, p = 0.10, and f_* = 3/8, as plotted in Fig. 2.",Captures the essential structure and main parameters of the analytic-plus-simulation model as described in Sections 4 and the discussion of Fig. 2.,0.96
10.1111_j.1461-0248.2005.00773.x.md,Spatial Prisoner's Dilemma – synchronous updating,Figure 1a describes spatial PD simulations and distinguishes between synchronous and asynchronous population updates; this row captures the synchronous-update condition.,0.93,Update rule: synchronous population updating on a spatial lattice; cost-to-net-benefit ratio ρ=c/(b−c) varied; neighbourhood size n=4; pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma interactions with benefit b and cost c.,"Methods text under Figure 1 describes varying ρ, using 4 neighbours, and contrasting synchronous vs asynchronous updating.",0.92,False,Study is purely theoretical/simulation-based with no human or animal subjects; it models evolutionary game dynamics on lattices.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioural or field experiment; it is a computational/theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory setting with human/animal participants; numerical simulations only.,1,True,"Population dynamics on a spatial lattice are investigated via numerical simulations as explicitly stated (e.g., 'simulation results' in Fig. 1).",0.98,False,"Figure 1a contrasts simulations with analytical approximations (pair approximation, replicator dynamics), but this condition refers to the simulated lattice dynamics, not the analytical model itself.",0.85,N/R,The lattice size (number of individuals/sites) for Figure 1 simulations is not reported.,0.2,N/R,Number of generations/iterations simulated is not specified.,0.2,1,"In a 2-strategy PD, choosing C or D is an all-or-nothing decision regarding cooperation level rather than a continuous contribution; per instructions, use 1 for free continuous amounts and 0 for all-or-nothing, but here the closest mapping is that agents choose discrete C/D, so contributing any 'amount' beyond strategy choice is not present. However, schema defines 1 if any amount can be contributed; in PD only two discrete choices, so this should be 0. Setting 0.",0.9,N/A,Endowments and contribution proportions apply to human public-goods style games; here individuals just choose C or D with no such endowment concept.,1,N/R,MPCR is not defined; only PD payoffs via b and c are specified.,0.2,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or IDs; basic PD without additional punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/A,No human interface; number of iterations is not 'shown' to agents.,1,0,Standard PD with only C and D and payoff parameters b and c; no explicit costly punishment stage.,0.98,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No additional reward mechanism (beyond basic PD payoff) is modelled.,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses fitness/payoff, not discrete endowments per round.",0.2,N/R,"Outcome reported is fraction of cooperators as function of ρ, not normalized contribution per endowment.",0.2,N/R,No explicit numeric contribution amounts; actions are binary C or D.,0.2,N/R,"Figure 1 shows fraction of cooperators, not efficiency (payoff relative to max).",0.2,N/R,Average population payoff is not plotted or numerically stated for Figure 1.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary-game simulations.,1,"Spatial Prisoner’s Dilemma on a regular square lattice with nearest-neighbour interactions (n=4). Payoffs R=b−c, T=b, S=−c, P=0; individuals update by comparing their payoff with a randomly chosen neighbour and probabilistically adopting the higher-payoff strategy. Cost-to-net-benefit ratio ρ=c/(b−c) is varied; fraction of cooperators at equilibrium is measured.",Summarized from Box 1 payoff description and the 'Methods' paragraph under Figure 1.,0.96
