lity w.,0.8,1,Shunners punish by withholding help from bad-standing individuals; this indirect punishment is central to the model.,1,0,Punishment via withholding aid benefits the punisher rather than costing them; group pays the efficiency cost.,0.7,N/R,No formal per-unit mapping; punishment is simply non-provision of benefit b.,0.5,0,No separate reward institution.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game is specified by costs and benefits but not by initial endowment.,0.6,N/R,"Outcome is assortment threshold r* for Shunner invasion, not contribution levels.",0.5,N/R,No direct quantitative contributions reported.,0.5,N/R,"Panel reports invasion thresholds, not payoffs or efficiency.",0.4,N/R,No explicit payoff values for this condition.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Theoretical model only.,1,"Same two-stage collective action plus mutual aid game as main model, but analysis restricted to two strategies (Shunner, Defector) in a structured population with assortment parameter r analogous to relatedness. Figure 2a plots threshold assortment r* required for rare Shunners to invade Defectors as a function of the expected length of the mutual aid game 1/(1−w) for fixed payoffs b=2, c=1, B=10, group size n=30 and error rate e=0.01.",Summarizes specific modifications for this analysis relative to the base model.,1
