 is only discussed qualitatively (Shunner provides higher average group payoff than Reciprocator when stable), no numbers.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Theoretical model only.,1,"Two-stage game identical to core model. Panel 3b investigates, for various levels of assortment r and expected mutual aid duration 1/(1−w), the threshold initial frequency p* above which Shunners increase when competing with Reciprocators. Shunner cannot coexist stably with Reciprocator. When condition (1) is satisfied and B>C, Shunner has a larger basin of attraction than Reciprocator (p*<0.5), meaning equilibrium selection processes favor Shunner. Parameters: b=2, c=1, C=1, B=10, n=30, e=0.01.",Summarizes the specific strategic comparison and conclusions associated with Fig. 3b.,1
10.1006_jtbi.2001.2406.md,Analytical multi-player costly signaling public goods model,Paper presents a single formal game-theoretic model (multi-player public goods with signaling); no distinct empirical or simulation conditions are described.,1,"Model parameters: group size n, benefit per recipient g, signaling costs c (high quality) and c' (low quality), alliance payoffs h,l,s, monitoring cost v, frequency of high-quality types p, dynamic fractions of honest signalers α and monitoring partners β.",Independent structure is entirely parameterized; no experimental manipulations on human subjects.,1,False,No human or animal subjects; all results are mathematical and theoretical.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only theoretical game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation with participants.,1,False,"Although dynamics are discussed, they are given analytically as replicator equations; no numerical or agent-based simulations are reported.",0.9,True,Core of the paper is derivation and analysis of equilibria and replicator dynamics in a formal mathematical model.,1,N/R,"Model uses symbol n for group size and notes it is between 10 and 100 conceptually, but no single fixed numeric value is specified for analysis.",0.6,N/R,Interactions are modeled as per-period one-shot games or evolutionary generations; no fixed finite number of rounds specified.,0.5,1,Each member can 'perform an action' that provides a benefit at cost c; described as a cooperative act vs not. This implies a binary contribution decision rather than a continuous contribution level.,0.8,N/A,No endowments or initial allocations for human players; purely theoretical model.,1,N/R,"Public goods structure described with parameters g, c and condition g(n-1)>c, but no explicit marginal per capita return value is computed or labeled as such.",0.7,N/A,No human subjects or experimental interface; communication between agents is not part of an implemented game.,1,N/R,"Information structure is modeled abstractly (knowledge of p, signals, etc.) but not in terms of experimental screens or summaries.",0.5,N/A,No explicit identity-revealing punishment mechanism in an experiment; punishment only appears as a conceptual example.,1,N/R,No experimental implementation where identity-linked rewards are displayed.,0.5,N/R,"Temporal structure is evolutionary/dynamic, not an implemented multi-round experiment with an interface.",0.5,0,"Base model is a signaling public-goods game; punishment is only mentioned as an interpretive application in Section 2.4, not as an explicit mechanism within a specified game implementation.",0.7,N/A,"Since no explicit punishment mechanism is parametrically implemented in the main model, no numeric punishment cost is defined in experimental terms.",1,N/A,"No operational punishment technology parameter (e.g., fine per unit) in a concrete game with players.",1,0,Model includes benefits from alliances and public good provision but no separate reward operator controlled by players.,0.9,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism specified as a treatment variable.,1,N/A,No parametric mapping from reward expenditure to impact is specified.,1,N/A,"Players are not endowed with tokens or money; payoffs are defined directly in terms of parameters c, g, h, l, s, etc.",1,N/R,No empirical contributions or simulated contribution rates are reported; only theoretical conditions for signaling/cooperation.,1,N/R,Model is not instantiated with numeric values for contributions beyond generic symbols.,1,N/R,They compare average payoffs of equilibria but do not report normalized efficiency measures for specific parameterizations.,0.8,N/R,"Group average payoffs are given symbolically and comparatively, not as concrete numeric outcomes.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Purely theoretical analytical model; no lab, field, or online implementation.",1,"n-player public-goods-style game where each agent can provide a benefit g to all others at personal cost c (or c' for low-quality), embedded in a signaling framework with alliance formation and monitored signals; dynamics analyzed via replicator equations for honest signaling and partner monitoring strategies.",Summary of the formal structure described in Sections 2–5.,1
10.1126_science.1123633.md,Sanctioning institution (SI) with endogenous institution choice and punishment/reward stage,Paper describes a single laboratory study where participants can choose each period between two institutions; SI is one treatment condition with sanctioning opportunities.,1,Institutional regime chosen in stage S0: sanctioning institution (SI) where positive and negative sanctions are possible after a public goods game.,Only independent variable varied across conditions is whether the institution allows sanctioning (SI) or is sanction-free (SFI). This row is for SI.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 84 human participants is described.,1,True,Controlled lab public goods game with specified rules and payoff structure; random assignment to sessions and controlled procedures implied.,1,True,Described as a laboratory experiment; no field component mentioned.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results are from human-subject lab experiment.,1,False,No formal analytical model or proofs reported; focus is on experimental data.,1,84,"Paper states 'In our experiment, 84 participants anonymously interact in a social dilemma situation in 30 repetitions.' All are in a common matching pool choosing institutions each period.",1,30,Same sentence specifies '30 repetitions' of the interaction.,1,1,"In stage S1 'each player is endowed with 20 MUs and may contribute between 0 and 20 MUs to a public good', implying a continuous (0–20) choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is given to each player's private account and they choose how much to contribute; no automatic allocation to public good described.,0.9,0.65,"They report that the highest attainable payoff under full contribution and no punishment is 52 MUs and full free-riding payoff is 40 MUs (with endowment 20). For a linear public good with n players, individual payoff = 40 + r*20 where r is MPCR. Setting 40 + r*20 = 52 gives r = 12/20 = 0.6. However, they say the free-riding payoff (keeping all 20) is 40 MUs, so marginal return from private account is 1 and from the public good is MPCR. To be consistent with 40 from endowment 20, there must also be some baseline (e.g., show-up fee). Because full-cooperation payoff of 52 relative to free-riding 40 corresponds to net gain of 12 on 20 invested, we compute MPCR = 12/20 = 0.6. But then contributing 20 yields 40 (kept) + 20*0.6 = 52, matching text. Group size n then satisfies total benefit multiplier = n*0.6. With typical lab parameters n≈4 and multiplier≈2.4, MPCR≈0.6. We round to 0.6. (If group size 6 and multiplier 3.6, MPCR still 0.6.)",0.4,0,"No mention of communication or chat; only institution choice, contribution, and sanctioning stages are described.",0.9,1,"End of each period: 'each participant receives detailed (but anonymous) information about each of the other participants from both institutions', which are summaries of others' contributions and sanctions.",1,0,Information is 'anonymous' and there is no indication that identities of punishers or punished are revealed.,0.9,0,Same anonymity statement applies to positive sanctions; no identities disclosed.,0.9,1,"In standard repeated public goods lab experiments with fixed 30 periods, subjects are typically told the total number of periods; although not explicitly stated, this is common practice.",0.4,1,"In SI, 'each player ... may positively or negatively sanction other members ... by assigning between 0 and 20 tokens'; negative sanctions are punishments.",1,1,Text: 'Each token used as a negative sanction costs the punished member 3 MUs and the punishing member 1 MU.' Cost per punishment token to punisher is 1 MU.,1,3,Same sentence: each negative sanction token imposes a loss of 3 MUs on the punished member.,1,1,Positive sanctions described: 'Each token used as a positive sanction yields the receiving member 1 MU and costs the member who uses it 1 MU.',1,1,Cost to giver of a positive sanction token is 1 MU.,1,1,Benefit to recipient of each positive sanction token is 1 MU.,1,20,Stage S1: 'each player is endowed with 20 money units (MUs)'.,1,0.914,"They report: 'Averaged over all periods, subjects in SI contribute 18.3 MUs (91.4% of the endowment)'. ContributionRate is 0.914.",1,18.3,Same sentence gives the average raw contribution in SI over all periods.,1,0.958,"They state: 'Toward the end, ... efficiency gains in SI up to 95.8% ... in the final period.' This is the closest explicit efficiency measure for SI.",0.8,52,"Text defines 'The highest attainable payoff (under full contributions of all subjects and no punishment) is 52 MUs'; this is the maximum individual payoff, which is the benchmark used in their efficiency definition.",0.7,Germany,"First author affiliation: University of Erfurt, Germany; typical subject pool would be local university students. No explicit country for participants, but likely Germany.",0.5,N/R,No information on age or student status provided in the main text.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,N/R,No direct statement about education level or student status in the text provided.,0,On site,"Described as a 'laboratory experiment', which for this time period and context implies in-person lab sessions rather than online.",0.9,"Endogenous institution choice each round between sanctioning (SI) and sanction-free (SFI) regimes; public goods game with 20 MU endowment, contributions between 0 and 20; in SI, after observing individual contributions, players can assign 0–20 positive or negative sanction tokens to any other SI member; each negative token costs punisher 1 MU and reduces target’s payoff by 3 MUs; each positive token costs giver 1 MU and increases target’s payoff by 1 MU; detailed but anonymous feedback about all participants in both institutions at end of each period; institution choice can change every period.",Summarizes key structural aspects of the SI condition described across method paragraphs.,1
10.1126_science.1123633.md,Sanction-free institution (SFI) with endogenous institution choice and no sanctioning stage,Same laboratory study; SFI is the competing treatment condition without any sanctioning possibilities.,1,Institutional regime chosen in stage S0: sanction-free institution (SFI) where neither positive nor negative sanctions are possible after a public goods game.,Independent variable is the institutional regime; this row is for the sanction-free institution as explicitly defined in stage S0.,1,True,Same human-subject laboratory experiment as SI; SFI is an alternative condition selected by participants.,1,True,Controlled lab setting with defined game and institutional rules; SFI is part of this experiment.,1,True,All interactions occur in the laboratory; SFI is not a field or observational condition.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,No analytical model specific to SFI condition beyond verbal discussion.,1,84,"Total of 84 participants choose between institutions; they can migrate so pool size per institution changes, but total participant count is 84.",1,30,Same 30 repetitions of the interaction for both institutions.,1,1,Contribution decision in SFI is identical to SI: 'may contribute between 0 and 20 MUs'; implies continuous choice.,1,0,"As in SI, endowment is given privately and contribution is voluntary between 0 and 20 MUs; no default to public fund.",0.9,0.65,Same public good technology in both institutions; MPCR inferred as in SI (from 52 vs 40 payoff figures).,0.4,0,No communication is described; structure for SFI mirrors SI without sanctions.,0.9,1,End-of-period feedback covers 'each of the other participants from both institutions'; SFI participants receive the same detailed anonymous information.,1,0,"No punishment exists in SFI, so identities of punishers are not applicable. For this field, by definition of the institution, punishment IDs cannot be shown.",1,0,No rewards exist in SFI; therefore revealing reward identities is not applicable within this institution.,1,1,Same experimental protocol as SI; likely that total number of periods is communicated to all participants.,0.4,0,Defined as 'a sanction-free institution (SFI) in which neither positive sanctioning (rewards) nor negative sanctioning (punishment) is possible.',1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in SFI; cost per punishment token therefore not applicable.,1,N/A,No punishment in SFI; no effect per punishment token.,1,0,Same definition: 'neither positive sanctioning (rewards) nor negative sanctioning (punishment) is possible.',1,N/A,No positive sanctioning in SFI.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in SFI.,1,20,Stage S1 endowment of 20 MUs applies to all participants in both institutions.,1,0.144,"They report: 'Averaged over all periods, subjects in SFI contribute only 2.9 MUs (14.4% of the endowment)'; contribution rate is 0.144.",1,2.9,Same sentence gives average raw contribution in SFI over all periods.,1,0,They state: 'efficiency gains in SFI converge to zero (mean = 0; SE = 0.0)'; interpret as efficiency ≈ 0 in later periods.,0.9,40,"They define that 'the payoff from complete free-riding and no punishment is 40 MUs'; in SFI, with cooperation collapsing and no sanctioning, this is the relevant benchmark payoff level per individual.",0.7,Germany,"Same participant pool as SI; experiment conducted at University of Erfurt, Germany.",0.5,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition data reported.,0,N/R,No details on education or student status provided in text.,0,On site,"Same laboratory experiment as SI, conducted in person at a university lab.",0.9,"Participants choosing SFI play the same public goods game as in SI with a 20 MU endowment and contribution choice between 0 and 20 MUs, but there is no stage in which they can sanction others; after contributions they still receive detailed but anonymous information about all participants in both institutions; institution choice can change every period and SFI gradually becomes depopulated as most subjects migrate to SI.",Summarizes structure of the sanction-free institution described in the design and results sections.,1
10.1037_a0023489.md,Meta-analysis – punishment vs. control (combined across 126 punishment effect sizes),Paper reports only aggregated meta-analytic results for punishment conditions relative to control across 126 independent punishment effect sizes; no single experiment-level configuration is fully specified.,0.88,"Presence of punishment incentives vs. control without punishment; aggregated across various implementations (costly/free, centralized/decentralized, partner/stranger, one-shot/iterated, multiple countries and game types).",Meta-analysis compares treatment conditions that include a punishment mechanism to corresponding control conditions without incentives.,0.95,True,All included punishment studies are experimental social dilemmas with adult human participants.,1,True,All underlying studies are controlled experiments manipulating punishment vs. control.,1,True,"Studies are described as experimental laboratory social dilemmas using real or hypothetical monetary payoffs, not field settings.",0.9,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations are included; only human experiments and hypothetical vignette tasks.,1,False,"Paper is a statistical meta-analysis, not a formal game-theoretic or analytical model.",1,4,"Paper states the modal group size in the sample is four-person groups (k=88), though actual group sizes range from 2 to 16 and one outlier of 1000; using modal as best single summary.",0.6,10,"Meta-analysis reports mean iterations M=12, median=10, mode=10; many prominent protocols (e.g., Fehr & Gächter) use 10 rounds; using mode as representative.",0.6,1,Public goods and related dilemmas in these literatures typically allow continuous contributions from 0 to full endowment; no mention of all-or-nothing restriction.,0.7,0,"Standard linear public goods designs used (e.g., Fehr & Gächter) start all endowment in private account; meta-analysis does not indicate default allocation to public account.",0.7,N/R,Meta-analysis describes group sizes and presence of public goods but does not report multipliers or MPCR distribution.,0,0,"Some individual studies (e.g., Bochet et al., 2006) involve communication, but meta-analysis aggregates over many with and without chat and provides no pooled configuration; defaulting to 0 because most punishment protocols cited (Fehr & Gächter, Herrmann et al.) do not allow free-form chat.",0.3,1,Punishment protocols require feedback on others’ contributions/earnings before sanctioning; meta-analysis repeatedly notes participants receive feedback about partners' choices.,0.9,0,"Meta-analysis states in decentralized punishment designs 'punishment is confidential, and participants are not informed which of the other partners paid to reduce their own earnings.'",1,0,"When rewards are decentralized, designs typically mirror punishment confidentiality; paper does not report identification of rewarders.",0.5,1,"Standard public goods protocols (Fehr & Gächter and follow-ups) inform subjects of the fixed number of periods; meta-analysis refers to 'iterations' and partner/stranger designs, implying known finite horizon.",0.7,1,"By definition, treatment conditions in this meta-analytic subset include punishment opportunities or fines.",1,N/R,"Cost-to-fine ratios are summarized (1:1, 1:2, 1:3, 1:4, 10% earnings), but absolute cost per punishment unit (in tokens or money) is not reported in the meta-analysis.",0,N/R,"Paper reports ratios (e.g., one unit reduces partner’s earnings by three units) but not in a standardized token/currency unit that can be used across all included studies.",0.3,0,This aggregated condition covers punishment vs. control comparisons; meta-analysis explicitly excludes combined reward+punishment treatments.,0.95,N/A,Reward mechanisms are not present in these punishment vs. control comparisons.,1,N/A,No rewards in these conditions.,1,N/R,Meta-analysis does not report per-round endowment sizes or their distribution across studies.,0,N/R,Outcome is summarized as standardized mean difference d in cooperation between punishment and control; pooled average contribution proportions are not reported.,0,N/R,No raw token contribution levels are provided in the meta-analytic summary.,0,N/R,"Some individual studies analyze efficiency, but the meta-analysis does not report pooled efficiency values.",0,N/R,"Meta-analysis focuses on standardized effects on cooperation, not absolute payoffs.",0,"Multiple (27 countries; most effect sizes from US, Netherlands, Australia, Switzerland, England, Japan, Russia, etc.)","Section 'Country of participants' lists 27 countries, with counts for each; the combined punishment effect size spans all these.",1,Adults (18+),Inclusion criterion: 'studies had to be conducted on adult participants (ages 18 years and above)'.,1,N/R,Meta-analysis does not summarize gender composition.,0,Primarily university students,"Most underlying experiments are standard lab social dilemmas (economics and psychology), which typically use student samples, though the meta-analysis does not aggregate education explicitly.",0.6,On site,Experiments are described as laboratory social dilemma studies conducted at universities; no mention of online or field data collection.,0.8,"Aggregate of public goods, resource (take-some), and prisoner’s dilemmas with both one-shot (k=54) and iterated (k=133) designs; includes both partner and stranger matching, with real monetary and hypothetical payoffs; incentives can be centralized (experimenter-imposed fines) or decentralized (peer punishment), with cost-to-fine ratios typically 1:1 to 1:4 or percentage reductions.","Summarized from 'Type of dilemma', 'One-shot versus iterated dilemma', 'Type of experimental protocol', 'Cost-to-fine ratio' and related moderator sections.",0.95
10.1037_a0023489.md,Meta-analysis – reward vs. control (combined across 33 reward effect sizes),Paper reports aggregated meta-analytic results for reward conditions relative to control across 33 independent reward effect sizes.,0.88,"Presence of reward incentives vs. control without rewards; aggregated across various implementations (costly/free, centralized/decentralized, one-shot/iterated, multiple countries and game types).","Meta-analysis contrasts reward treatment conditions (bonus, positive sanctions) against no-incentive controls.",0.95,True,All included reward studies are human laboratory social dilemma experiments.,1,True,Reward presence is experimentally manipulated versus control in lab games.,1,True,Studies involve lab public goods/resource/prisoner’s dilemmas with experimentally controlled incentives.,0.9,False,No simulation-only studies are included for rewards.,1,False,"The paper is a meta-analysis over empirical results, not an analytical model.",1,4,"Many reward studies listed (e.g., Parks 2000; Dickinson 2001; public goods with rewards) use 4-person groups; overall modal group size in the broader dataset is 4.",0.7,10,"For iterated games with rewards, numerous studies use around 10 or more rounds (e.g., Parks 2000 uses 20; Clark 10; Sutter et al. 10). Meta-analysis reports overall median 10 for iterations; using this as representative.",0.6,1,Reward studies are standard public goods or resource dilemmas with variable contribution/harvest levels; no evidence of binary all-or-nothing structure.,0.7,0,Linear public goods/resource games with reward normally start all endowment privately; no mention of automatic public allocation.,0.7,N/R,Meta-analysis does not supply multipliers or MPCR for reward subset.,0,0,"Reward experiments summarized do not emphasize communication; typical designs (e.g., Parks 2000; McCusker & Carnevale 1995) are non-communication public goods or resource dilemmas.",0.6,1,Reward allocation generally requires feedback on others' cooperation or outcomes; this is standard in these designs.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism is active in reward vs. control comparisons.,1,0,"Meta-analysis does not report identification of rewarders; by analogy to punishment and typical lab practice, rewards are usually anonymous.",0.5,1,"Iterated reward studies refer to a fixed number of rounds (e.g., 'IT(20)' in Parks 2000; 'IT(10)' in several others), implying participants know the horizon.",0.8,0,Meta-analysis explicitly excludes conditions where both reward and punishment are used simultaneously; this subset covers reward-only vs control.,0.98,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,N/A,No punishment present.,1,1,"By construction, these treatment conditions add a reward incentive for cooperation.",1,N/R,"Meta-analysis distinguishes costly vs free rewards, and reports that effects are larger when rewarding is costly (d=0.97 vs 0.32), but does not provide a unified numeric cost per reward unit.",0.3,N/R,"Specific conversion from reward expenditure to recipient benefit (e.g., multiplier per reward unit) is not aggregated in the meta-analysis.",0.3,N/R,No pooled endowment size is reported for reward studies.,0,N/R,Meta-analysis reports standardized mean difference d in cooperation between reward and control; no pooled contribution fractions are given.,0,N/R,Raw contributions are not summarized at the meta level.,0,N/R,"Some individual reward studies discuss efficiency, but no aggregate efficiency statistic is reported.",0,N/R,"The meta-analysis does not pool absolute payoffs, only effect sizes on cooperation.",0,"Multiple (primarily United States and Netherlands, plus New Zealand, Hong Kong, Austria, Germany)","Reward table lists studies from US, NL, NZ, HK, AT, DE; meta-analysis notes rewards-cooperation data mostly from US and Netherlands.",0.95,Adults (18+),Same study criteria apply: all participants are adults 18 or older.,1,N/R,No cross-study gender summary is provided.,0,Primarily university students,Reward experiments cited are typical university-lab based economic/psychological games that commonly recruit students; not explicitly aggregated.,0.6,On site,"Reward studies are conventional in-person lab experiments, not online or field.",0.85,"Aggregate of public goods dilemmas (k=19), resource dilemmas (k=5), and a prisoner’s dilemma (k=1), including both one-shot (k=12) and iterated (k=14) designs; includes centralized rewards administered by the experimenter (e.g., bonuses, fines reversed) and decentralized peer rewards; both costly and free reward regimes; decisions often incentivized with real money (k=20) and sometimes hypothetical.","Summarized from Table 2 and moderator descriptions for rewards (type of dilemma, cost vs free, centralized vs decentralized, payment, iterations).",0.95
10.1073_pnas.0913149107.md,Basic public goods game (no punishment),Paper analyzes two versions of Fehr-Gächter experiment: 'basic public goods game' and 'public goods game with punishment'. This row refers to the basic version without punishment.,1,Institutional environment: basic public goods game without punishment option,Only systematic difference across the two conditions described is the presence vs absence of a punishment stage after contribution decisions.,1,True,"Study uses 240 human subjects in laboratory public goods game, as described under Materials and Methods.",1,True,Subjects were randomly assigned to groups and to treatment conditions in a controlled lab setting; this is a classic controlled experiment.,1,True,Described as laboratory experiments; Fehr-Gächter (Nature 2002) are standard lab public goods games.,1,False,All results are from human-subject lab experiments; no mention of agent-based or numerical simulations.,1,False,"Although regression models are used, there is no standalone formal analytical model; focus is on empirical estimation.",1,4,“Subjects were placed into groups of four.”,1,6,"“In both versions of the experiment, subjects participated in a total of six repetitions (‘periods’) of the public goods game.”",1,1,"Each subject decides “how many MUs (between 0 and 20) to keep or contribute”; this implies a continuous (0–20) choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each subject is given 20 MUs and decides how many to contribute; endowment starts in private account by standard design and text implies this (keep vs contribute).,0.9,0.4,“Each MU contributed to the group project would yield 0.4 MUs for each of the four group members.” MPCR is per capita return per MU contributed.,1,0,Design emphasizes strict anonymity and only observation of contributions/payoffs; no mention of any communication or chat.,0.9,1,"“All contributions were revealed to each group member at the conclusion of the game, along with payoff outcomes.” This is a summary of others’ contributions and payoffs.",1,0,"There is no punishment stage in this condition, so no punishment identities can be shown.",1,0,No reward mechanism is described in any condition.,1,1,"Standard Fehr-Gächter design is finitely repeated with known horizon; while not restated here, it is implied they know it is 6 periods; inferred from literature.",0.6,0,This is explicitly the 'basic public goods game'; punishment is only present in the alternate version.,1,N/A,"No punishment option in this condition, so punishment cost is not applicable.",1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition; technology of punishment is not applicable.,1,0,Only costly punishment is mentioned in any version; no rewarding mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,“Each subject was given 20 money units (MUs).”,1,N/R,Paper reports marginal effects of others’ contributions but does not provide average contribution as fraction of endowment.,0,N/R,"Only regression coefficients (e.g., +0.19 MUs per MU of alter) are reported; no overall mean contributions presented.",0,N/R,No efficiency or payoff/maximum ratios are provided for this reanalysis.,0,N/R,Text explains possible earnings (20 vs 32 MUs) but does not report actual observed group payoffs.,0.1,N/R,Subjects for the Fehr-Gächter experiment are not characterized by country in this reanalysis; original paper is not detailed here.,0,N/R,No demographic information about age is reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition details reported.,0,N/R,"No information on education level (e.g., students vs general population) in this text.",0,On site,"Described as 'laboratory experiments' in the style of Fehr & Gächter; such experiments are typically conducted in physical labs, not online. No indication of online implementation.",0.9,Single-shot public goods game repeated for 6 periods with random rematching; no two subjects meet more than once; contributions (0–20) from 20 MU endowment; each MU contributed yields 0.4 MU to each of 4 group members (group multiplier 1.6); strict anonymity; no information about others’ histories.,Summarizes detailed procedural description in the main text and Materials and Methods.,1
10.1073_pnas.0913149107.md,Public goods game with punishment,"This is the 'alternate version' where, after seeing contributions, subjects can punish others. Treated as a separate condition.",1,Institutional environment: public goods game with costly punishment stage,"Relative to the basic condition, the independent variable is the presence of a punishment stage in which subjects can spend MUs to reduce others’ payoffs.",1,True,Same 240 human subjects (or a subset) play this version; human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Random group assignment and treatment implementation in a controlled lab environment.,1,True,Part of the same set of laboratory experiments as the basic game.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data are from human subjects.,1,False,Focus on empirical estimation; no closed-form analytical game-theoretic model presented.,1,4,Same group structure as basic game: 'subjects were placed into groups of four'; alternate version is 'identical public goods game' plus punishment stage.,1,6,"“In both versions of the experiment, subjects participated in a total of six repetitions (‘periods’).”",1,1,Contribution decision is still 'how many MUs (between 0 and 20) to keep or contribute'; punishment stage does not change contribution choice granularity.,1,0,"As in the basic game, subjects receive 20 MUs and then decide how many to contribute; all MUs start in private accounts.",0.9,0.4,Punishment version is described as 'an identical public goods game' with added punishment; thus same per capita return (0.4 MU per group member).,1,0,Strict anonymity and only observation of contributions/punishment/payoffs indicated; no communication or discussion channel reported.,0.9,1,"After viewing contributions and punishment, subjects must see contributions and payout summaries; stated that contributions and payoff outcomes are revealed; punishment results implied from analysis of punishment received/given.",0.9,0,"Design enforces strict anonymity; although a subject can see how much punishment each other member gave them in Fehr-Gächter, this reanalysis only emphasizes anonymity and group membership; safer to code as anonymous (no stable identity across periods).",0.5,0,No reward option exists in any version.,1,1,"As with basic game, design is a finitely repeated game with 6 periods; standard implementation informs subjects of total rounds; inferred from original study.",0.6,1,"“In an alternate version, subjects played an identical public goods game, but, after viewing their fellow group members' contributions, they were allowed to spend up to 10 MUs to 'punish' each of the other group members.”",1,1,“They were allowed to spend up to 10 MUs to 'punish' each of the other group members. Each MU spent reduced the target's income by 3 MUs.” Cost per punishment MU to punisher is 1 MU.,1,3,“Each MU spent reduced the target's income by 3 MUs.”,1,0,Only costly punishment is described; no rewarding or positive transfer mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same base public goods structure: 'each subject was given 20 MUs'; alternate version is identical aside from punishment.,1,N/R,Paper reports only marginal effects on contributions per MU of others’ contributions or punishment; no reported average normalized contribution.,0,N/R,No mean contribution level reported; only regression coefficients such as +0.18 MUs per MU contributed by alter.,0,N/R,"No efficiency metrics (e.g., payoffs vs maximum) are provided for this punishment version in this reanalysis.",0,N/R,"Although possible payoff ranges can be derived, actual observed group payoffs are not reported.",0.1,N/R,Country or recruitment site of subjects is not specified in this text.,0,N/R,No age information is provided.,0,N/R,No description of gender composition.,0,N/R,No explicit description of whether subjects are students or other populations.,0,On site,Same laboratory experiments as basic public goods game; conducted in a physical lab setting as in Fehr & Gächter 2002.,0.9,"Identical to basic public goods game (20 MU endowment, 0–20 contribution choice, 4-person groups, MPCR=0.4, 6 periods, random rematching, strict anonymity) plus second-stage punishment where after observing contributions, each subject can spend up to 10 MUs to punish each other group member; each MU spent reduces target’s payoff by 3 MUs; subjects cannot meet the same individual more than once and have no information on past histories.",Summarizes description of the punishment version in the main text.,1
10.1126_science.1141588.md,Voluntary participation with punishment (baseline four-strategy model),"Condition described as voluntary participation in the joint effort game with punishment, with four strategies C, D, N, P; parameters given near Fig. 1a and Fig. 2a.",1,"Participation regime: voluntary; Strategy set: {Cooperators, Defectors, Non-participants, Punishers}; Punishment directed only at defectors (no second-order punishment, no punishment of non-participants)","Main model in text and Fig. 1a, Fig. 2a includes four strategies and voluntary participation; punishment only of defectors is described before extensions with alpha and delta.",0.95,False,"Purely theoretical evolutionary game and stochastic simulations, no human participants.",1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; only models and simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting with human subjects.,1,True,"They conduct computer simulations of finite populations (e.g., Fig. 1a) based on the defined stochastic dynamics.",1,True,"They derive fixation probabilities, stationary distributions, etc., analytically for small mutation (discussed around Fig. 2 and in SOM).",1,5,N is the group size for the public goods game; parameter values explicitly given as N=5.,1,0,Model describes an ongoing evolutionary process with repeated sampling; no discrete fixed number of public goods rounds like in an experiment.,0.2,1,Players either contribute cost c or contribute nothing; binary decision rather than continuous contributions.,1,0,"In standard public goods specification, endowment is initially private and players choose to contribute cost c; no default public allocation described.",0.7,0.6,MPCR = r / S where S is participant group size. Using typical full-participation case S=N=5 with r=3 gives 3/5=0.6. They do not state MPCR explicitly.,0.5,0,No communication or signaling mechanism between players is described in the model.,0.9,0,Model only specifies payoff-based imitation; no explicit feedback of detailed summaries to agents.,0.7,0,No notion of individual identities or observability of who punished whom; only payoffs matter for imitation.,0.7,0,No rewards mechanism exists in this model.,1,0,Agents do not receive information on remaining rounds; process is modeled as ongoing with mutation and imitation.,0.8,1,Punishers can impose fines beta on defectors at personal cost gamma.,1,0.3,"Parameter values include gamma=0.3, the cost per punishment act to the punisher.",1,1,"Parameter values include beta=1, the fine imposed per punishment act.",1,0,"Model includes only punishment, no reward mechanism in the baseline scenario.",1,N/A,No rewards are used.,1,N/A,No rewards are used.,1,1,"Each contributor invests cost c; parameters specify c=1, which functions as the per-round endowment available to contribute.",0.8,N/R,"Paper does not report equilibrium fraction of endowment contributed; focus is on strategy frequencies (C,D,N,P) and payoffs.",0,N/R,"No average contribution levels over time are reported, only that cooperators/punishers contribute c while defectors contribute 0.",0,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to social optimum is reported numerically.,0,N/R,Group payoff values are not tabulated; only qualitative comparisons are given.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is based on mathematical models and computer simulations only.,1,"Well-mixed population of size M=100 (simulations) or analyzed generically; at each period N=5 individuals are randomly sampled. They may become non-participants (fixed income sigma=1) or participants in a linear public goods game. Contributors (cooperators, punishers) pay cost c=1; total contributions are multiplied by r=3 and divided equally among participants S. Punishers additionally incur cost gamma=0.3 per defector punished and impose fine beta=1 per defector. Evolution proceeds via payoff-based imitation with mutation probability mu=0.01 in simulations; analytical results use Moran process with selection strength s=0.249 and mutation->0.",Synthesized from model description and parameter listings near Figs. 1a and 2a.,1
10.1126_science.1141588.md,"Compulsory participation with punishment (three-strategy model: C, D, P)",Condition described as compulsory participation in a joint effort game with punishment; discussed in text and parameterized in Fig. 1b and Fig. 2b.,1,"Participation regime: compulsory (no non-participants); Strategy set: {Cooperators, Defectors, Punishers}; Punishment directed only at defectors",Text: 'The situation is very different in the traditional case of a public goods game where participation is compulsory. If only cooperators and defectors are present... Adding the punishers as a third strategy ...' and Fig. 2b caption confirms compulsory participation with punishment.,0.95,False,"Same theoretical and simulation framework, no human subjects.",1,False,Modeling and simulation only.,1,False,No lab implementation.,1,True,"Computer simulations and analytical treatment for compulsory case (Fig. 1b, 2b).",1,True,Analytical computation of transition probabilities and stationary distributions as for voluntary case (Fig. 2b).,1,5,Uses same parameter values as voluntary case: N=5.,1,0,"Ongoing evolutionary process, not fixed rounds.",0.2,1,Same binary contribution choice (pay cost c or 0).,1,0,Same public goods structure; contribution is an active cost from private payoff.,0.7,0.6,"Same r and N as voluntary case: r=3, N=5, giving MPCR 3/5; participation compulsory but group size fixed at N.",0.7,0,No communication described.,0.9,0,No explicit feedback besides payoff-based imitation.,0.7,0,Model omits identities; selection depends only on payoffs.,0.7,0,No rewards in this condition.,1,0,No finite horizon information to agents.,0.8,1,Punishers operate as in voluntary case.,1,0.3,Same gamma=0.3.,1,1,Same beta=1.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,1,Same c=1 as cost of contributing; used as effective endowment.,0.8,N/R,No explicit numeric outcomes on contributions; only that defectors dominate in long run.,0,N/R,No average contribution amount reported.,0,N/R,"They mention 'hardly any economic benefit', but no numeric efficiency.",0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff values.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Theoretical plus simulations only.,1,"Same public goods game as voluntary baseline but without non-participant strategy; every sampled individual must participate. Population size M=100 in simulations; parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, sigma is irrelevant since N-strategy absent, beta=1, gamma=0.3, mutation rate mu=0.01 in simulations; analytical part uses Moran process with selection strength s=0.249.",Derived from description for compulsory case and stated parameter equivalence to voluntary condition.,1
10.1126_science.1141588.md,"Voluntary participation without punishment (three-strategy model: C, D, N)",Condition described as 'Volunteering in the absence of punishment' and portrayed in Fig. 3a.,0.95,"Participation regime: voluntary; Strategy set: {Cooperators, Defectors, Non-participants}; No punishment mechanism","Text explicitly contrasts 'volunteering in the absence of punishment' to other conditions and Fig. 3a caption: 'Same as in Fig. 2a, but without punishers.'",1,False,Still purely theoretical and simulation-based.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,They numerically study the cycling dynamics for this three-strategy voluntary case (Fig. 3a).,1,True,They mention rock-paper-scissors mechanism and prior analytical work; same framework as four-strategy case.,0.9,5,"Fig. 3a caption: 'Same as in Fig. 2a', which uses N=5.",1,0,"Evolutionary process, not fixed number of rounds.",0.2,1,Same contribution structure: either contribute c or not.,1,0,"Same public goods model, no default public allocation.",0.7,0.6,"Same r and N as baseline: r=3, N=5.",0.7,0,No communication modeled.,0.9,0,No explicit payoff summary feedback beyond what is needed for selection.,0.7,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No explicit round information to agents.,0.8,0,Condition explicitly described as without punishment; punishers removed.,1,N/A,Punishment disabled.,1,N/A,Punishment disabled.,1,0,No rewards mentioned.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,1,Same c=1 as contribution cost.,0.8,N/R,"They describe cycling of strategies, not numeric contribution rates.",0,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts reported.,0,N/R,Efficiency not quantified.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoffs.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation/analytical model only.,1,"Same basic voluntary public goods game as four-strategy model but with only cooperators, defectors, and non-participants. Non-participants get fixed payoff sigma; cooperators contribute c=1; total contributions multiplied by r=3 and shared among participants. Evolutionary dynamics via payoff-based imitation with mutation as in main model. Dynamics exhibits rock-paper-scissors cycles among C, D, N.",Summarized from main text discussing Fig. 3a.,0.9
10.1126_science.1141588.md,Voluntary participation with punishment of defectors and non-participants (four-strategy model with delta>0),Condition discussed in text and illustrated in Fig. 3b: punishers also penalise non-participants with fines scaled by delta.,0.9,"Participation regime: voluntary; Strategy set: {Cooperators, Defectors, Non-participants, Punishers}; Punishment directed at defectors with fine beta and at non-participants with fine delta*beta; costs gamma and delta*gamma to punishers","Text: 'We could also assume that punishers penalise non-participants, with a fine δβ and the cost to the punisher δγ ...' and Fig. 3b: 'Same as in Fig. 2a, but assuming that punishers equally punish the non-participants.'",1,False,Model/simulation only.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Fig. 3b is based on numerical analysis of this modified punishment rule.,1,True,They state that effects with delta are analysed and refer to SOM; same framework.,0.8,5,"Fig. 3b caption: 'Same as in Fig. 2a', which uses N=5.",1,0,Evolutionary process.,0.2,1,Same contribution rule.,1,0,Same structure as baseline game.,0.7,0.6,Same r=3 and typical group size N=5 as baseline.,0.7,0,No communication is introduced.,0.9,0,No explicit feedback beyond payoffs.,0.7,0,Identities not modeled; only payoffs matter.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No finite horizon information.,0.8,1,Punishers punish both defectors and non-participants.,1,0.3,Base cost gamma=0.3 per punished defector; non-participant punishment cost is delta*gamma but delta not numerically specified.,0.7,1,Base fine beta=1 per punished defector; non-participant fine is delta*beta with unspecified delta.,0.7,0,No rewarding in this condition.,1,N/A,Rewards not used.,1,N/A,Rewards not used.,1,1,Same c=1 as elsewhere.,0.8,N/R,Only qualitative statement: punishing non-participants 'hinders the emergence of punishment' and system responds with decrease in cooperation; no numeric contributions.,0,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,0,N/R,Efficiency not quantified.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Theoretical/simulation condition.,1,"Same voluntary public goods game and evolutionary dynamics as baseline four-strategy model, but punishers also sanction non-participants. For each non-participant in the sampled group, a punisher imposes fine delta*beta on that non-participant at additional cost delta*gamma to itself (0 ≤ delta ≤ 1). This stabilizes punishment when common but hinders its emergence and can reduce overall cooperation.",Summarized from paragraph introducing delta and the discussion around Fig. 3b.,0.9
10.1006_jtbi.2000.2202.md,Analytical cultural evolutionary model with multi-stage punishment,"Paper presents a single formal model of an (i+1)-stage cooperation and punishment game with parameters N, B, C, ρ, φ, e, α, β; no separate empirical or simulation conditions are described.",0.97,"Number of punishment stages i (i ≥ 0), strength of conformist transmission α, error rate e, group size N, cost of cooperation C, benefit of cooperation B, punishment cost φ, punishment impact ρ","Model analysis varies i, α, e, N, C, B, φ, ρ to derive stability conditions; these are the core parameters determining outcomes.",0.96,False,Model contains no human or animal subjects; it is a theoretical cultural evolutionary model.,1,False,No controlled experiment with random assignment; only mathematical derivations.,1,False,No lab setting; purely analytical theory.,1,False,"Although dynamics are discussed, there is no numerical or agent-based simulation reported, only closed-form recursions and stability analyses.",0.95,True,"Paper develops explicit difference equations, payoff functions, and eigenvalue-based stability analyses without numerical simulation.",1,0,Model parameter N denotes group size but no single numeric value is fixed; the analysis is symbolic. Use 0 to indicate not instantiated to a specific number.,0.7,0,"Interaction is an (i+1)-stage game with cooperation stage 0 and i punishment stages; i is not fixed to a specific value, so no concrete round count.",0.7,1,At stage 0 individuals either cooperate (contribute cost C) or defect; binary choice implies all-or-nothing with respect to cooperation.,0.98,N/A,"No explicit endowment or default allocation between private/public accounts is modeled; only costs and benefits enter payoffs in an abstract way, and there are no human subjects.",1,N/R,"Model specifies that cooperation yields benefit B shared equally among N, but MPCR (B/N divided by contribution C) is never labeled or numerically instantiated.",0.7,N/A,No communication mechanism appears; and there are no human participants or implementation details.,1,0,Agents respond only to payoffs and cultural transmission rules; no information structure like earnings summaries is modeled.,0.9,0,Identity or observability of punishers is not modeled; interactions are symmetric and anonymous within groups.,0.9,0,"The model does not include rewards, only punishment, so revealing reward identities is inapplicable.",1,0,"No notion of subjects being told the number of stages; the horizon (i+1) is a modeling construct, not an experimental instruction.",0.9,1,Core of the model is punishment at stages 1..i with parameters φ (cost) and ρ (impact).,1,0,"Punishing costs φ to the punisher per punished target, but φ is left as a symbolic positive parameter with no fixed numeric value.",0.7,0,Being punished costs ρ to the target per act; ρ is symbolic and no fixed numeric mapping from cost to impact is given.,0.7,0,Model only includes costly punishment; no positive reward mechanism is defined.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1,0,No explicit per-period endowment is specified; only costs C and benefits B appear in payoffs.,0.7,N/R,"Model tracks frequencies of strategy variants p_i, not normalized contribution rates relative to an endowment.",0.7,N/R,Cooperation is binary and parameterized by cost C; no numeric contribution amount is instantiated.,0.7,N/R,"While group payoffs are discussed qualitatively, efficiency (payoff divided by maximum) is not computed or reported as a scalar.",0.7,N/R,"Expected payoffs b are expressed symbolically (e.g., b ≈ (1−e)(B−C−eN(φ+ρ(1+i))) ), but no concrete numeric group payoff value is reported.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Work is purely theoretical and analytical with no experimental implementation.,1,"One-shot public goods dilemma at stage 0 with cost of cooperation C and group benefit B>N per cooperator, followed by i punishment stages where prosocial variants P may punish defectors or non-punishers with punishment impact ρ at cost φ; social learning combines payoff-biased transmission and conformist transmission with strength α, and errors of implementation occur with probability e.",Summarizes the formal game structure described in the model section and Table 1.,0.98
10.1038_nature04981.md,ABC treatment – all three players from same tribe,"Paper defines four treatment conditions; ABC is where dictator (A), recipient (B), and third party (C) are all from the same tribe.",1,"Group composition: A, B, and C all ingroup (same tribe); compared to AB, AC, BC where only some pairs share group","Only experimental manipulation described is tribal group affiliation of A, B, and C across four treatments.",1,True,Uses 195 human participants from two PNG tribes in a third-party punishment game.,1,True,Controlled third-party punishment game with random assignment to roles and treatments.,1,True,"Conducted in controlled sessions with separate rooms and standardized instructions; although in villages, procedure matches lab-style experiment more than field behavior observation.",0.8,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No formal mathematical model or analytical derivation is the focus.,1,3,"Game involves a dictator (A), recipient (B), and third party (C).",1,1,"Described as an anonymous, one-shot, third-party punishment experiment.",1,1,"Dictator can transfer any amount between K0 and K10; third party can spend K0, K1, or K2 on punishment. Contributions/punishments are not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment of K10 is initially with dictator (A) and K0 with B; nothing starts in the public/shared account.,1,N/R,"This is a dictator/third-party punishment game, not a public goods game with a defined multiplier.",1,0,Instructions say participants were ensured not to communicate about the game before the experiment; no communication option during decisions mentioned.,0.9,0,"No mention that players see summaries of others’ earnings, only that C is informed of A’s transfer.",0.9,0,Experiment is anonymous and one-shot; no indication that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,"No rewarding stage or mechanism described, only punishment.",1,1,"One-shot game; participants are informed of the one-time nature in typical protocol (He