10.1038_nature06723.md,"C1 – Control, b/c = 2, no punishment",Paper describes two control experiments C1 and C2; C1 has cooperation cost 1 and benefit 2 (b/c=2) and no punishment option.,1,Availability of costly punishment (no punishment option); benefit-to-cost ratio of cooperation b/c = 2,"Across conditions, the main manipulated factors are whether punishment is available (control vs treatment) and the cooperation benefit-to-cost ratio (2 vs 3).",1,True,Human subjects played repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma interactions in the lab.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random rematching and pre-specified payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted at Harvard Business School Computer Lab for Experimental Research (CLER).,1,False,No mention of computer/agent-based simulations; all results from human participants.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not formal analytical model as the main method.",1,2,Repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma in pairwise encounters: each interaction involves two players.,1,79,Methods summary: average of 79 total rounds per subject. Number of rounds per interaction varies (1–9) with continuation probability 0.75; using subject-level average as closest numeric summary.,0.6,1,"Subjects choose between discrete actions (C or D), not a continuous contribution level; this does not fit the contribution framing, so mark as 1 (not all-or-nothing contribution game).",0.4,0,Not a public goods / contribution game; no endowment-allocation decision between public/private accounts. Set to 0 as endowment effectively starts in private payoff account.,0.4,N/A,Not a linear public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined in this Prisoner’s Dilemma setup.,1,0,Only interaction is via action choices and payoff feedback; no communication or chat is mentioned.,1,1,"After each round, subjects see other person’s choice and both payoff scores; that is feedback on others’ earnings.",1,0,Interactions are anonymous via computer; identities of punishers/defectors are not revealed beyond being the current anonymous partner.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism in this design.,1,0,Subjects know continuation probability (0.75) but not how long each interaction will last; total remaining rounds are not displayed.,1,0,Control experiments C1 and C2: only cooperation or defection allowed.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,0,Only C and D moves; no explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,Payoffs are defined as units gained/lost per move; no per-round token endowment is specified.,0.2,0.212,"Text: In C1, 21.2% of decisions are cooperation; interpret cooperation as contribution-like action, normalized between 0 and 1.",0.9,N/R,"Only frequencies of cooperation decisions (percent of rounds) are reported, not raw amounts per round.",0.1,N/R,Average payoff per interaction is discussed qualitatively but no numeric efficiency relative to theoretical maximum is reported.,0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff or mean payoff values per pair are provided for C1 specifically.,0.1,United States,"Subjects from Boston-area colleges and universities; lab at Harvard University in Cambridge, MA, USA.",0.9,Mean 22.2 years; adult college students,"Methods summary: 104 subjects, mean age 22.2 years.",1,"45 women, 59 men (across all sessions/conditions)","Methods summary gives total counts; not broken down by condition, so assume similar across conditions.",0.8,College and university students,Participants recruited from Boston-area colleges and universities.,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab (CLER) with subjects at lab computers.,1,"Repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with pairwise anonymous interactions; in each round players choose C (pay 1, other gets 2) or D (gain 1, other loses 1). Continuation probability per round is 0.75; interactions last 1–9 rounds. Scores accumulate over many random-rematching interactions and are converted to money at $0.10 per unit plus show-up and fixed bonus fees.",Summarized from game description and Methods summary.,1
