es and universities, Harvard lab in Massachusetts, USA.",0.9,Mean 22.2 years; adult college students,Same subject pool and summary statistics across all sessions.,1,"45 women, 59 men (across all sessions/conditions)",Overall counts given; condition-specific breakdown not reported.,0.8,College and university students,Recruited from Boston-area colleges and universities.,1,On site,Run in the CLER lab with physical attendance.,1,"Repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with pairwise anonymous interactions; in each round players choose C (pay 1, other gets 3) or D (gain 1, other loses 1). Continuation probability per round is 0.75; interactions last 1–9 rounds; random rematching across many interactions. Scores converted to cash.",Same structure as C1 with higher cooperation benefit (3 instead of 2).,1
10.1038_nature06723.md,"T1 – Treatment, b/c = 2, punishment available",Treatment T1 uses same parameters as C1 (b/c=2) but adds punishment option.,1,Availability of costly punishment (punishment option enabled); benefit-to-cost ratio of cooperation b/c = 2,"Relative to C1, T1 introduces punishment while keeping b/c=2.",1,True,Human participants in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of available actions and payoff matrix.,1,True,Conducted at Harvard’s CLER with subjects at lab computers.,1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,"Empirical, not analytical.",1,2,Pairwise repeated PD interactions.,1,79,Average 79 total rounds per subject; same experimental structure in all sessions.,0.6,1,"Players choose among three discrete actions (C, D, P) each round, not a divisible contribution amount.",0.4,0,Not a contribution game with an explicit endowment allocation.,0.4,N/A,Not a public goods framework.,1,0,Only action choices and payoffs shown; no communication channel.,1,1,After each round players see other’s action and both payoffs.,1,0,Partners are anonymous and rematched; punishers are not identified beyond current opponent.,0.9,0,No reward option.,1,0,Game ends probabilistically with p=0.25 each round; total remaining rounds are unknown to subjects.,1,1,"In T1 and T2, people have three options: cooperate, defect, or punish.",1,1,Punishment means paying 1 unit for the other person to lose 4.,1,4,Punishment technology: 1 unit paid causes 4 units lost by opponent (4:1).,1,0,No separate rewarding action other than cooperation; no costful reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game defined via payoffs per action; no per-round endowment specified.,0.2,0.524,"Text: In T1, 52.4% of all decisions are cooperation.",0.9,N/R,Only proportion of cooperative moves reported.,0.1,N/R,Authors state that punishment does not increase average payoff but provide no numerical efficiency value.,0.2,N/R,Average payoffs are discussed qualitatively without condition-specific numbers.,0.1,United States,Boston-area students in a Harvard lab.,0.9,Mean 22.2 years; adult college students,Methods summary for all 104 subjects.,1,"45 women, 59 men (across all sessions/conditions)",Overall counts given; assumed similar composition in this condition.,0.8,College and university students,Recruitment from Boston-area colleges and universities.,1,On site,Run in physical computer lab CLER.,1,"Repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with three actions: C (pay 1, other gets 2), D (gain 1, other loses 1), P (pay 1, other loses 4). Continuation probability 0.75 yields 1–9 round interactions on average; random rematching between interactions. Punishment can be used repeatedly, including retaliation and counter-punishment.",Summarized from description of treatments and figure notes.,1
10.1038_nature06723.md,"T2 – Treatment, b/c = 3, punishment available",Treatment T2 parallels C2 with b/c=3 while adding punishment option.,1,Availability of costly punishment (punishment option enabled); benefit-to-cost ratio of cooperation b/c = 3,"Relative to C2, T2 adds punishment while keeping higher benefit-to-cost ratio b/c=3.",1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled condition with explicit payoff matrix and random rematching.,1,True,Conducted in CLER lab.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,"Focus on empirical data, not formal derivations.",1,2,Two-player repeated PD interactions.,1,79,Average rounds per subject across all conditions; same structure applies.,0.6,1,Three discrete actions each round; not a divisible contribution game.,0.4,0,No explicit endowment split between private/public; actions directly produce payoffs.,0.4,N/A,Not a public good setting.,1,0,No communication channel mentioned beyond game feedback.,1,1,Players observe opponent’s action and both payoff scores after each round.,1,0,Anonymity and random rematching; punishers not identified beyond being current opponent.,0.9,0,No separate reward option.,1,0,"Subjects know only continuation probability, not total or remaining rounds.",1,1,"T2 includes punishment among the three actions C, D, P.",1,1,Punishment costs 1 unit to the punisher.,1,4,Punishment makes the other person lose 4 units (4:1 punishment technology).,1,0,No explicit rewarding technology.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not specified.,0.2,0.597,"Text: In T2, 59.7% of all decisions are cooperation.",0.9,N/R,Only percentages of cooperative moves are provided.,0.1,N/R,"Authors note that punishment does not increase average payoff, but give no numeric efficiency value for T2.",0.2,N/R,No condition-specific mean payoffs reported.,0.1,United States,Same Boston-area subject pool in Harvard lab.,0.9,Mean 22.2 years; adult college students,"Methods summary applies to all sessions, including T2.",1,"45 women, 59 men (across all sessions/conditions)",Aggregate gender counts reported; not broken down by condition.,0.8,College and university students,Recruited from Boston-area colleges and universities.,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab CLER.,1,"Repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with three actions: C (pay 1, other gets 3), D (gain 1, other loses 1), P (pay 1, other loses 4). Continuation probability 0.75; 1–9 round interactions; random rematching. Punishment often leads to counter-punishment, mutual destruction, or pre-emptive strikes.","Summarized from game description, payoff matrices and example histories in Figure 2.",1
10.1016_j.tpb.2003.07.001.md,Baseline agent-based simulation,Paper describes one core simulation configuration using the baseline parameters in Table 1 and then reports long-run statistics for that configuration.,0.88,"Population composition evolves endogenously among three fixed strategy types (Reciprocators, Cooperators, Selfish) under fixed baseline parameters: b=0.2, c=0.1, c_p=0.1, group size n=20, number of groups=20, emigration rate γ=0.05, immigration rate μ=0.03, pool fitness φ0=-0.1, mutation rate ε=0.01, initial groups of 20 all Selfish with s ~ U[0,1].","Independent structure is parameterized environment and presence of three fixed behavioral types, with initial condition of 100% Selfish; parameters taken from Table 1 and simulation description.",0.96,False,Study is an agent-based evolutionary model with no human or animal participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a computational simulation.,1,False,No lab or field; purely computational.,1,True,They explicitly describe running simulations with parameterized groups and evolving populations.,1,False,"While some analytical derivations are presented, the main results for this condition are from numerical simulations.",0.8,20,Baseline group size is 20 agents per group; this is the size of the interacting unit in the public good environment.,0.95,50000,"Table 2: statistics represent the average of the last 1000 periods of a 50,000 period simulation.",0.9,1,"Agents choose a shirking fraction σ_s in [0,1]; effectively continuous effort choice, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,"No explicit endowment or default allocation between public/private accounts in the simulation; contributions are modeled as effort choices, and there are no human subjects.",0.8,N/R,Public good is modeled via fitness production b and cost c but not in standard MPCR form; MPCR not defined or computable as per typical linear public goods game.,0.6,N/A,No communication mechanism among simulated agents; also no human participants.,1,0,"Agents' decisions depend only on type frequencies and parameters, not on displayed summaries; no such feature in the simulation.",0.9,N/A,"Ostracism is modeled probabilistically; no notion of revealed identity as in human experiments, and no human subjects.",1,N/A,"No reward mechanism is modeled, only punishment via ostracism.",1,0,Simulated agents do not receive information about total or remaining periods; horizon is not part of their decision rules.,0.9,1,"Central mechanism is ostracism of shirkers by Reciprocators, with cost c_p to punishers and cost s to punished agents.",1,0.1,Table 1: cost of punishing (c_p) is 0.1 in baseline.,1,N/R,"They specify cost of punishment c_p and fitness cost s of being ostracized, but not a simple linear 'damage per punishment point' multiplier.",0.6,0,Model includes only punishment (ostracism); no prosocial reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Agents do not receive discrete token endowments; they have production parameter b and cost c in fitness units.,0.7,0.889,Table 2: average shirking rate is 11.1%; contribution rate = 1 - shirking = 0.889.,0.95,N/R,"Model tracks shirking fractions, not absolute token contributions.",0.7,N/R,They present average fitness and shirking rates but do not report or enable direct calculation of total payoff relative to a theoretical maximum across the whole population.,0.5,N/R,"While instantaneous payoffs can be written analytically, the paper does not report numeric average group payoffs for the baseline simulation.",0.5,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Evolutionary, multi-group, public-good-like environment with three fixed strategy types (Reciprocators, Cooperators, Selfish). Within each period, Selfish agents choose shirking level σ_s based on perceived cost of ostracism s and fraction of Reciprocators f_r; group output is n(1-σ)b shared equally. Reciprocators incur punishment cost c_p to ostracize shirkers, who then spend a spell as solitary agents with fitness φ0 before re-entering groups via migration. Reproduction, mutation (ε), migration (γ, μ), and group disbanding below minimum size (6) jointly determine long-run population composition.",Summarizes the strategic environment and dynamics described in Sections 2–3 and the Appendix for the baseline parameter setting.,0.96
