10.1016_j.tpb.2003.07.001.md,Mutation rate and emergence time variation (50 groups),Section 4 and Fig. 6: fourth panel varies mutation rate ε in a population with 50 groups and measures time until cooperation (low shirking) emerges.,0.88,"Mutation rate ε ∈ [0.002, higher values not specified] varied in a population with 50 groups; measure is the number of periods until average shirking over previous 1000 periods first falls below 10%. Other parameters use baseline values unless noted.","Text: “The mutation rate panel shows, for a population of 50 groups, the average over 25 runs of the number of periods until the shirking rate, averaged over previous 1000 periods, first fell below 10%.”",0.95,False,Simulation with no human subjects.,1,False,Parametric computational experiment; not behavioral.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Multiple simulation runs for each ε.,1,False,Outcomes obtained by running simulations.,0.9,20,"They state that simulations otherwise use baseline parameters (Table 1), which include initial group size 20; only the number of groups is explicitly changed to 50.",0.85,N/R,"They report the time until a threshold is hit; upper bound on run length for this panel is not specified, unlike the 30,000-period earlier panels.",0.4,1,Same shirking-fraction decision.,0.9,N/A,No human endowments.,0.9,N/R,Not defined.,0.6,N/A,No humans.,1,0,No such display in simulation.,0.9,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Agents do not use horizon information.,0.9,1,Ostracism remains part of the model.,1,0.1,Baseline c_p=0.1; not said to be changed in this panel.,0.85,N/R,No explicit damage-per-unit specified.,0.6,0,Only punishment modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model is in fitness units with no explicit endowment.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome variable in this panel is time until shirking <10%, not the equilibrium contribution rate itself.",0.4,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,0.5,N/R,They discuss only time to reach low shirking.,0.4,N/R,No fitness/payoff averages presented for this panel.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same multi-group public goods simulation as baseline, but with 50 groups instead of 20. Mutation rate ε is varied, and for each ε they perform 25 runs. For each run they track the number of periods until the population-wide shirking rate, averaged over the previous 1000 periods, first drops below 10%. They report that with ε as low as 0.002, high cooperation is achieved after about 1500 periods in this 50-group setting.",Summarizes the fourth panel and associated text in Section 4.,0.94
10.1016_j.tpb.2003.07.001.md,Exogamy / offspring location variation,Section 4 and Fig. 7: varies degree of exogamy x (fraction of offspring randomly reassigned to other groups) and examines its effect on long-run shirking and fraction of Reciprocators.,0.9,"Degree of exogamy x, the fraction of offspring whose first period of life is in a non-parental group (0 ≤ x ≤ 1). Cases highlighted: x=0 (all offspring stay), x=0.5, x=1 (all offspring randomly reassigned). Other parameters as in baseline (Table 1).","Text explicitly defines x as degree of exogamy and states results for x=0, 0.5, and 1.",0.98,False,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,Computational parameter variation.,1,False,No physical environment.,1,True,Fig. 7 reports simulation averages for different x values.,1,False,"Quantitative results (e.g., 11%, 24% shirking) come from simulations.",0.9,20,"They state: ‘The baseline parameters are as in Table 1.’ for Fig. 7, which includes initial group size 20.",0.95,N/R,Exact run length for Fig. 7 not given; only long-run averages are described.,0.4,1,Same shirking-fraction choice framework.,0.9,N/A,No human endowments.,0.9,N/R,Not defined.,0.6,N/A,No humans.,1,0,Simulation does not include informational displays.,0.9,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanic.,1,0,Agents not modeled as aware of horizon.,0.9,1,Ostracism continues to operate under all x values.,1,0.1,Baseline c_p=0.1; not mentioned as changing in Fig. 7.,0.9,N/R,No explicit punishment multiplier beyond cost and ostracism effect.,0.6,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Simulation uses fitness parameters, not endowments.",0.7,N/R,"They report shirking rates not as overall function, but for specific x cases; this row covers the whole family of x values, so a single contribution rate does not apply.",0.4,N/R,No raw contribution amounts are reported.,0.5,N/R,"They focus on shirking rates and fraction of Reciprocators, not total efficiency.",0.4,N/R,Average payoffs not reported for the exogamy variation.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Same basic model as baseline, with an added parameter x controlling exogamy: with probability 1−x offspring initially stay in the parental group; with probability x they are immediately reassigned randomly across groups (conceptually ‘exogamy reasons’). They find that higher exogamy (higher x) reduces within-group relatedness and thus the long-run fraction of Reciprocators and cooperation: at x=0, average shirking ≈11% and Reciprocators ≈37%; at x=1, average shirking ≈24% and Reciprocators ≈22%; at x=0.5, average shirking ≈18%.",Summarizes results described in text around Fig. 7.,0.97
10.1126_science.1177418.md,Control – standard repeated public goods game,"Paper states: 'In the control experiment, subjects play several rounds of a standard public goods game in groups of four (16 control groups)'.",1,Targeted interaction option: none (standard public goods only),"Control differs from PN, RN, RNP by absence of second-stage targeted interactions.",1,True,Human subjects at Harvard Business School lab; total 192 subjects.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments/groups.,1,True,Conducted at 'Harvard Business School Computer Lab for Experimental Research'.,1,False,All data from human participants; no mention of agent-based or computer-only simulations.,1,False,No formal analytical model; paper reports experimental results only.,1,4,Groups of four in all conditions: 'groups of four'.,1,50,Figures and text refer to 'over 50 rounds of play'. Assumed same for all treatments.,0.9,1,"Standard public goods with 20 MUs endowment; no restriction to binary choice, so continuous/discrete up to 20.",0.9,0,Subjects 'receive 20 MUs and decide how much to contribute'; endowment starts private.,0.9,0.4,Contributions multiplied by 1.6 and split among 4; MPCR = 1.6/4 = 0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication; typical design prohibits chat and only mentions computer interaction.,0.8,1,Standard public goods designs and need to compute targeted actions later imply subjects at least see others' contributions/payoffs; but not explicitly stated.,0.4,0,No punishment stage in control; set to 0 as not applicable within mechanism.,1,0,No reward stage in control; mechanism absent.,1,0,Subjects 'are not told the total number of rounds'.,1,0,Control has only the public goods game; targeted punishment exists only in PN/RNP.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,Control has no targeted reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,20,Eac