R,Education level not explicitly stated.,0,On site,Run in a physical experimental lab.,1,"Each round: stage 1 is standard public goods game (4 players, 20 MUs each, contributions multiplied by 1.6 and shared); stage 2 allows each player to reward or do nothing to each group member; identities fixed across 50 rounds; total number of rounds withheld from subjects; reward interactions can increase total group income.",From description of RN treatment and general setup of 'targeted interactions' as potentially beneficial bilateral moves.,0.8
10.1126_science.1177418.md,"RNP – reward, non-action, or punishment targeted interaction","Described as 'third treatment (""RNP"", 11 groups) subjects can choose between reward, non-action and punishment'.",1,"Targeted interaction option: reward, punishment, or non-action after public goods stage",RNP differs by giving both reward and punishment choices in the second stage.,1,True,Human participants in repeated public goods with bilateral stage.,1,True,Laboratory treatment in controlled design.,1,True,Conducted in a lab environment at Harvard Business School.,1,False,No simulation-only component.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical/analytical study.,1,4,Same 4-person groups as other conditions.,1,50,All figures for RNP show 50 rounds of play.,0.9,1,Uses same public goods contribution choice as other conditions.,0.9,0,Endowment fully private before contribution decision.,0.9,0.4,"Public goods parameters same: multiply by 1.6, divide among 4.",1,0,No mention of communication between subjects.,0.8,1,"To condition reward/punishment on contributions ('probability to reward players who contribute equal to or greater than the group average contribution', 'punish below average contributors'), subjects must observe others' contributions.",0.7,1,"Design stresses persistent identities and 'private pairwise interactions', implying knowledge of who punished whom and scope for retaliation.",0.8,1,Same logic as punishment: repeated bilateral interactions with known partner identities.,0.8,0,Subjects not told the total number of rounds in the experiment.,1,1,RNP allows punishment as one of the targeted options.,1,N/R,Cost per unit of punishment not reported in main text for RNP.,0,N/R,Effect of each punishment unit on recipient's payoff not specified.,0,1,RNP allows reward in the second stage.,1,N/R,Cost of rewarding not given in main text.,0,N/R,Benefit from reward not specified quantitatively.,0,20,Shares same public goods structure as control (20 MUs per round).,1,N/R,Figures and qualitative comparisons only; no explicit averages.,0,N/R,No numeric mean contributions over 50 rounds reported.,0,N/R,"Percent of maximum payoff plotted, but not summarized numerically for entire game.",0.2,N/R,Mean group payoff per round only shown graphically.,0,USA,Experiment run at Harvard Business School in USA.,0.9,N/R,Subject age not described.,0,N/R,No gender data reported.,0,N/R,Likely students but not explicitly stated.,0,On site,Conducted in a physical computer lab at Harvard Business School.,1,"Each round: stage 1 is standard public goods (groups of 4, 20 MUs each, linear MPCR 0.4); stage 2 is a pairwise interaction stage where each player can reward, punish, or do nothing toward each of the three partners; subjects can condition their choices on others' contributions (e.g., rewarding above-average contributors, punishing below-average contributors); identities fixed and known across 50 rounds; total number of rounds not revealed.","Based on explicit description of RNP ('reward, non-action and punishment') and analyses of conditional reward/punishment strategies.",0.9
10.2307_2667052.md,Study 1 – No-sanction condition,"Study 1 is a 2×2 design with sanctions (yes vs. no) and Smith’s decision (cooperate vs. defect). This row captures the no-sanction treatment, averaged over Smith’s decision conditions because game parameters are identical.",0.79,Sanctioning system: none; Smith’s decision feedback (cooperate vs. defect) orthogonal but not part of payoff structure.,"Independent variables described as a 2×2 with sanctions and others’ decisions. For this row, sanction level is ‘no sanctions’.",0.82,True,Human student participants played a repeated prisoner’s dilemma scenario.,1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to sanction vs. no-sanction and Smith’s decision conditions with controlled payoffs.,1,True,Conducted as part of an undergraduate management class in classrooms; this is a lab/onsite classroom experiment rather than field behavior in actual firms.,0.78,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all decisions by human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model tested in this condition; it is a behavioral experiment.,1,2,Described as a two-party prisoner’s dilemma with Jones (participant) and Smith (other manager).,1,4,Participants told there would be four production runs in which they would make inspection decisions.,1,1,"Decision each round is binary: request inspection (cooperate) vs. not (defect). This is an all-or-nothing choice, coded as 0 per instructions, but schema says 1=can contribute any amount, 0=only all-or-nothing. Here only binary, so 0.",0.95,0,"Prisoner’s dilemma framed as inspection decision, not an endowment contribution game; default contribution proportion concept doesn’t apply but schema requires numeric; set to 0 as equivalent to all endowment in private account.",0.2,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined or computable from the payoff matrix.,0,0,No mention of communication between Jones and Smith; only decisions and feedback.,0.9,0,"Participants only see Smith’s specific decisions, not payoff summaries or punishment histories.",0.8,0,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism is described.,1,1,Participants were told in advance there would be four production runs.,0.95,0,Explicitly described as the no-sanction condition; no fines or monitoring.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewards for cooperation described; only payoffs from matrix.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoff matrix directly provides unit profits; no initial token endowment per round is specified.,0,N/R,"Outcomes reported as cooperation proportions per round, but not normalized to an endowment-based contribution rate; binary cooperation rate is conceptually similar but not mapped numerically.",0.3,N/R,Decision is binary inspection vs. no inspection; no numeric contribution amount.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs by condition are not provided in sufficient detail to compute overall efficiency.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoffs reported per condition.,0,N/R,"University locations mentioned (Notre Dame, Northwestern) are authors’ affiliations, not explicit participant nationality.",0.2,N/R,Not reported; participants are undergraduates but no age range is given.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Undergraduate management students,Described as students enrolled in an undergraduate management class.,1,On site,"Conducted in physical classrooms as part of a class discussion, not via online panels.",0.9,"Repeated 2-player prisoner’s dilemma framed as inspection decision; payoff matrix: (You, Smith) in profits: (80,80) if both request inspection, (20,100) if you inspect and Smith does not, (100,20) if you do not inspect and Smith does, (40,40) if neither inspects. Four rounds with feedback on Smith’s choices after round 2.",Summarized from the Study 1 methods and Table 1.,1
10.2307_2667052.md,Study 1 – Weak-sanction condition,"Same repeated prisoner’s dilemma as Study 1 no-sanction but with a weak sanctioning system (low detection probability, small fine).",0.96,Sanctioning system: weak (≤5% random check; $30 fine if inspection should have been requested but was not); Smith’s decision feedback (cooperate vs. defect) orthogonal.,Methods specify participants in the weak-sanction condition were informed of occasional monitoring with ≤5% probability and a $30 fine.,1,True,Same human subject design as Study 1 no-sanction.,1,True,Random assignment to sanction vs. no-sanction conditions with controlled rules.,1,True,Classroom-based lab experiment.,0.78,False,No artificial agents.,1,False,"Empirical study, not purely analytical.",1,2,Two-party prisoner’s dilemma (Jones and Smith).,1,4,Same four production runs as in Study 1 description.,1,0,Binary inspection choice per round (cooperate/defect) indicates all-or-nothing; coded as 0 per schema.,0.95,0,"Not a contribution game; as above, treat as 0 by default.",0.2,N/R,Not applicable to this PD structure.,0,0,No communication between players allowed or mentioned.,0.9,0,Only specific feedback on Smith’s inspection decisions is given.,0.8,0,Punishment is a fine applied by the president; no identity revelation between peers about punishment events is described.,0.8,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,Participants were told at the outset that there would be four runs.,0.95,1,Weak-sanction condition explicitly introduces a monitoring system with fines for not inspecting when one ‘should have’.,1,30,Fine is $30 if caught defecting when an inspection should have been requested; cost per defection under detection.,1,30,"Each detected defection triggers a $30 reduction in payoff; since payoffs are in dollars per unit, treat effect magnitude as 30 per fine event.",0.6,0,"No explicit positive rewards for cooperation, only avoidance of fines.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Unit profits defined directly; no separate endowment specified.,0,N/R,"Reported DV is cooperation proportion across participants per round, but schema’s contributionRate tied to endowments; no direct mapping given.",0.3,N/R,"Choice is binary, not a continuous amount.",0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoff data by condition to compute efficiency.,0,N/R,Group-level earnings are not summarized.,0,N/R,Student nationality not provided.,0.2,N/R,Age of undergraduate participants not reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender breakdown provided.,0,Undergraduate management students,Same sample as Study 1 overall: undergraduates in a management class.,1,On site,Carried out in classrooms during a class discussion.,0.9,"Same 2-player 4-round prisoner’s dilemma inspection game as Study 1 no-sanction. Additional weak monitoring: ≤5% chance of random check each run (simulated with 95% red, 5% yellow jellybeans); if yellow is drawn and you did not request inspection when you ‘should have’, a $30 fine is applied.",Summarized from the Study 1 sanction condition description.,1
10.2307_2667052.md,Study 2 – No-sanction condition,"Study 2 is a one-shot multiparty social dilemma about operating pollution scrubbers, with two between-subject conditions: no sanctions vs. weak sanctions. This row is the no-sanction scenario.",0.97,Sanctioning system: none; one-shot decision on scrubber operating percentage.,Methods say in the no-sanction condition ‘There will be no inspections or enforcement of the agreement.’,1,True,MBA students responded to a hypothetical but incentiveless scenario in class.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to versions with or without sanctions; responses compared across conditions.,0.9,True,"Administered in classroom setting as part of discussion, not in field organizations.",0.85,False,No simulated agents; purely survey/decision task.,1,False,Not an analytical model; behavioral survey experiment.,1,N/R,Scenario is a multiparty industry dilemma; no specific number of firms/players is given.,0.1,1,Each participant makes a single scrubber operation decision; described as a one-trial design.,0.95,1,"Participants choose any operating percentage (0–100 in 20% increments); this is a continuous/discrete amount choice, not binary, so coded as 1.",0.9,0,"There is no explicit endowment; operating scrubbers is akin to a costly contribution, but no default share is specified.",0.2,N/R,Not structured as a linear public good with a known multiplier per capita; costs and legislation effects are non-linear and depend on majority behavior.,0,0,Participants individually fill out scenario responses; no communication between decision makers.,0.9,0,Participants reason about ‘most other manufacturers’ but do not see actual summaries or payoffs.,0.9,0,No sanctioning or fines exists in this condition.,1,0,No reward system present.,1,1,Task clearly framed as a single decision; participants know this.,0.9,0,Explicitly ‘no inspections or enforcement of the agreement’.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewards beyo