ess degree students (MBA),Same Study 3 subject pool.,1,On site,Scenario conducted as part of classroom instruction.,0.9,"Same scrubber-emissions social dilemma as the other Study 3 conditions. Strong enforcement: at least 50% chance of inspection; if inspected and found running scrubbers <80%, the firm pays a $2,000,000 fine, characterized as substantial. Participants also report perceived decision frame and expectations of others’ cooperation. Analyses in paper further split behavior by frame (business vs. ethical).",Taken from the Study 3 strong-sanction condition description and the subsequent results section.,1
10.1038_nature01474.md,Experiment 1 – Trust condition,Paper describes a first experiment with two conditions: a trust condition and an incentive condition; this row is the trust condition from the first experiment.,1,Sanction availability: no fine possible; Game: trust game with tripled investment and no sanction option.,"Independent variable across main conditions is presence/absence of a fine option. Here, investor cannot fine the trustee.",1,True,Human subjects (students) playing with real monetary stakes.,1,True,Controlled behavioral experiment with random assignment to roles and specified payoff rules.,1,True,"Conducted on site in a controlled setting (university lunch room with booths), not a natural field environment.",0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulations; only human play.,1,False,No formal analytical model developed; focus on experimental data.,1,2,Each trial involves an investor and a trustee; described explicitly as two subjects.,1,1,Each pair of subjects played the game just once.,1,1,Investor can send any amount between 0 and 10 MUs; trustee can send any amount between 0 and tripled transfer (continuous/any-amount contribution).,1,0,All 10 MUs start in the investor’s private endowment; nothing is pre-allocated to a joint or public account.,1,N/R,"Not a linear public good; there is a 3x transfer but no standard MPCR definition given and interaction is dyadic trust game, not a group public good.",0,0,"No mention of communication; anonymous, one-shot decisions via decision sheets.",0.9,0,Trustees only see their investor’s transfer and desired back-transfer; no summaries of others’ outcomes mentioned.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism is part of the design.,1,0,"Game is one-shot; no mention that number of rounds is shown, and there is only one interaction.",0.9,0,Explicitly states that in the trust condition no rewards or sanctions are available to the investor.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism 