10.1073_pnas.1108243108.md,Random link condition,"Paper describes four conditions: random link, fixed link, viscous dynamic (k=10%), and fluid dynamic (k=30%). This row is for the random network condition.",1,"Network updating regime: links regenerated randomly each round (well-mixed population); no strategic rewiring (k = N/A, randomization of all links).","Independent variable is how links are updated; here, social network is regenerated randomly after every round, creating a well-mixed population.",1,True,Human subjects recruited via Amazon Mechanical Turk played the game.,1,True,Subjects were randomly assigned to conditions in controlled incentivized economic games.,1,False,"Interaction was over the internet using Mechanical Turk, not a physical lab.",1,False,"All results are from human-subject experiments, not purely computational simulations.",1,False,No formal analytical model developed in the experimental section; focus is on behavioral data.,1,19.6,Average network size reported as 19.6 (SD 6.4) across 40 realizations; use average as player count.,0.8,5,Game length is stochastic with 0.8 continuation probability; expected number of rounds in a geometric distribution is 1/(1-0.8)=5.,0.7,1,"Players choose a binary action (cooperate or defect) toward all neighbors; there is no choice of intermediate contribution amounts, so continuous vs all-or-nothing contribution concept does not apply; coded as 1 because subjects can choose either full cooperation or none, but no constrained discrete contribution levels beyond that.",0.4,0,There is no endowment allocation decision between public/private accounts; cooperation entails paying a cost per neighbor rather than reallocating a pre-given endowment; effectively all resources start in the private account.,0.4,N/R,"This is not a standard linear public goods game with a single group fund; benefits are pairwise (50 cost gives 100 benefit per neighbor), and group size and degree vary. A single marginal per capita return is not defined or reported.",0.2,0,No mention of any communication or chat; interactions are anonymous and limited to choices and payoff/behavioral feedback.,0.9,1,"Subjects see neighbors' previous decisions and are told how many broke/formed links with them, and see own payoff; this constitutes summaries of others’ behavior toward them.",0.8,0,"Interactions are anonymous; subjects are not told identities beyond abstract neighbor labels, and there is no explicit punishment operator with identifiable punisher.",0.8,0,"Similarly, no explicit reward operator with identifiable rewarding individuals; only anonymous neighbors’ actions are shown.",0.8,0,"Subjects are told that after every round the probability of another round is 0.8, implying uncertain horizon; no indication they see total or remaining number of rounds.",0.9,0,No costly punishment mechanism (paying to reduce others’ payoffs); link breaking is only conceptualized as punishment but has no direct cost and is not a separate punishment stage.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism implemented.,1,0,"No separate costly reward stage; cooperation itself generates benefits but is the main game, not a reward mechanism.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Payoff structure describes costs and benefits per neighbor (cooperation costs 50 units per neighbor and gives 100 units to each neighbor), but no fixed per-round endowment is reported.",0.2,N/R,Paper reports fraction of players choosing to cooperate over time but not a single summary rate per condition suitable as a scalar DV; no explicit average over all rounds is reported.,0.3,N/R,"Contributions are binary (cooperate/defect), and the cost in units depends on number of neighbors; no average amount is reported.",0.2,N/R,"Although structure implies an efficiency measure could be computed, no aggregate payoff relative to a maximum cooperative benchmark is reported.",0.2,N/R,Paper does not report total or average group payoffs by condition.,0.1,N/R,Subjects recruited via Mechanical Turk; country not specified.,0.2,N/R,No demographic age information reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0.1,N/R,No education information reported.,0.1,Online,Subjects recruited on Amazon Mechanical Turk and interacted anonymously over the internet.,1,"Repeated pairwise-benefit cooperation game on an initially sparse random graph (20% of possible links formed). Each round, each subject chooses a single action (cooperate or defect) toward all neighbors. Cooperation costs 50 units per neighbor and gives 100 units to each neighbor; defection costs 0 and gives 0. After each round, in the random-link condition, the entire social network is regenerated randomly, resulting in a well-mixed population. Game continues with 0.8 probability after each round.",Summarized from the description of the game and the random link updating condition in the methods and main text.,1
