nism exists.,1,0,No separate rewarding technology beyond cooperation itself.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,Only per-neighbor costs/benefits are given; per-round or initial endowment not specified.,0.2,N/R,"They report cooperation decreasing over time and logistic regression coefficients, but not a single averaged cooperation rate per condition.",0.3,N/R,No aggregate cost paid per player or number of cooperative neighbors reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency metrics relative to a cooperative optimum are reported.,0.2,N/R,Paper does not present numeric group payoffs for this condition.,0.1,N/R,Country of Mechanical Turk participants is not given.,0.2,N/R,Age demographics are not discussed.,0.1,N/R,Gender demographics are not provided.,0.1,N/R,No information on education levels.,0.1,Online,All sessions conducted online via Mechanical Turk.,1,"Same repeated cooperation game as other conditions. After each cooperation decision round, a rewiring round occurs. In each rewiring round, 10% of unordered subject pairs are randomly chosen. For each selected pair, if a link exists, one subject (chosen at random) may break it; if no link exists, one subject may form it. The deciding subject is told the potential partner's previous-round action before deciding.",Summarizes the viscous strategic updating rule (k = 10%).,1
