10.1098_rstb.2009.0134.md,"Figure 1 – m ≥ s (high migration, weak local adaptation)",Condition described in section 3(b) and illustrated in Figure 1(a): case where migration rates are greater than or equal to selection coefficients (m ≥ s).,1,"Relative strength of migration vs selection: m ≥ s (high migration, weak local adaptation).",Text explicitly contrasts cases by the relation between migration rate m and selection coefficient s; this condition is m ≥ s.,1,False,Figure 1 is described as a simple model over a 16×16 array of populations with parameters m and s; no human or animal subjects are involved.,1,False,"No controlled assignment of human participants; this is a theoretical/simulation model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab setting with human subjects; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,True,Described as a model over a grid of subpopulations with migration and selection dynamics; typical of numerical population-genetic simulations.,0.9,False,Outcome shown as spatial plots rather than closed-form derivations; emphasizes equilibrium behavior from iterated dynamics.,0.8,256,Model has 256 subpopulations arranged in a 16×16 array; treat each subpopulation as a 'player' for configuration purposes.,0.9,0,"Model runs to evolutionary equilibrium, not discrete reported rounds like in a repeated game; use 0 to indicate N/A.",0.7,1,Traits are binary (0/1) but each subpopulation can have any frequency from 0 to 1; analogous to continuous contribution rather than all-or-nothing discrete choice.,0.6,N/A,No public-goods contribution structure or human endowments in this population-genetic style model.,1,N/R,No public goods game or payoff multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,0,Model does not include informational feedback between individuals; only migration and selection dynamics are specified.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,"No human interface, rounds, or display.",1,0,Mode