 conditions.,0.6,Students; exact age distribution N/R,Subjects are students; ages not given.,0.6,N/R,No gender information in paper.,0,"University students, mixed academic backgrounds including Economics",Described in procedures.,1,On site,All sessions carried out in lab environment.,1,"Three-stage game with contribution, punishment, and counter-punishment; random matching of group members each period within random-matching sessions, with in-period anonymous IDs 1–4 reassigned every period.",Combines PCP game structure from Section 2.3 with random matching described in design.,1
10.1098_rsif.2014.0735.md,Mean-field RPS model with reactions (2.1)–(2.4),"The paper specifies a generic rock-paper-scissors game between three species A, B, C with reactions (2.1)–(2.4) and analyzes its mean-field and stochastic dynamics as the core theoretical model.",1,"Reaction rates p (dominance-removal), z (dominance-replacement), q (reproduction), μ (mutation); also asymmetric dominance rates z_A, z_B, z_C and population size N in finite well-mixed simulations.","These parameters are explicitly varied in sections 2 and 3.3–3.4 to study different dynamical regimes (heteroclinic cycles, Hopf bifurcation, Lotka-Volterra limit, asymmetric dominance).",1,False,"No human subjects; model concerns species A, B, C and is studied via rate equations and birth–death Markov chains.",1,False,"This is a theoretical/simulation study of population dynamics, not a controlled lab or field experiment.",1,False,No laboratory setup with human or animal subjects; purely mathematical and computational.,1,True,They use stochastic simulations (Gillespie algorithm) of finite well-mixed populations and analyze ODE dynamics.,1,True,"They derive and analyze mean-field rate equations, steady states, Hopf bifurcation, conserved quantities, extinction times.",1,3,"Three competing species/strategies A, B, C define the game. Total population size N is variable but species count is 3.",1,1,Evolutionary dynamics is continuous-time without discrete rounds; encode as 1 for a single ongoing interaction process.,0.5,1,Individuals interact and reproduce/replace continuously; there is no notion of choosing a discrete contribution level.,0.3,N/A,No contributions or endowments are defined; this is not a public-goods style game.,1,N/A,No public goods or linear contribution-return structure is modeled.,1,N/A,No human players or communication.,1,N/A,No interfaces or feedback to human players.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; continuous-time population dynamics.,1,0,"Interactions are dominance and reproduction, not costly punishment.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,There is no explicit costly rewarding; reproduction is not modeled as optional prosocial reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Model involves species densities, not token endowments.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions are modeled.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions are modeled.,1,N/R,"They discuss coexistence, extinction times, and oscillatory dynamics but do not define efficiency as payoff relative to a maximum.",0.9,N/R,Payoffs are not aggregated into a group payoff metric; focus is on species densities and extinction probabilities.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"The study is purely theoretical and computational, without human or animal subjects.",1,"Three-species rock-paper-scissors-type evolutionary game (species A, B, C) in a well-mixed population of size N with reactions: dominance-removal at rate p, dominance-replacement at rate z, reproduction into empty space at rate q, and mutation between species at rate μ. They analyze degenerate May-Leonard dynamics (p,q>0,z≥0,μ=0), Hopf bifurcation at μ_H=p q /(6(p+3q)) when μ>0, cyclic Lotka-Volterra case (z>0,p=q=μ=0) including asymmetric dominance rates z_A,z_B,z_C, and stochastic extinction/fixation in finite N with Gillespie simulations.","Summarizes the specified reaction schemes, dynamical regimes, and modeling approach in section 2.",1
10.1098_rsif.2014.0735.md,"Spatial lattice RPS with dominance-removal and reproduction (p=q=1, z=μ=0)","Section 3.2 details a two-dimensional square-lattice RPS model with dominance-removal (rate p), reproduction (rate q), and mobility m via pair exchange, with p=q=1 and z=μ=0 for the main pattern-formation analysis including critical mobility m_c.",1,"Mobility m=2γ/N varied; competition (dominance-removal) rate p=1; reproduction rate q=1; z=0, μ=0; system size N (e.g., 512^2) varied for finite-size effects.",They fix p=q=1 and explore how varying mobility m affects spiral size and biodiversity; z and μ set to 0 to match May–Leonard heteroclinic case; N influences extinction probability scaling.,1,False,"Model consists of species on lattice sites, no human subjects.",1,False,"Numerical simulations and PDE analysis, not experimental data.",1,False,No laboratory experimental procedure; purely computational/theoretical.,1,True,They use Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice and numerical PDE solutions.,1,True,They derive corresponding PDEs (3.2) and analyze critical behavior and scaling.,1,3,"Three strategies A (rock), B (paper), C (scissors) plus empty sites are present; strategic types are 3.",1,1,"Dynamics is asynchronous continuous-time updating on a lattice, not discrete repeated rounds.",0.5,1,Individuals do not choose partial contributions; they occupy sites and interact via fixed rules.,0.3,N/A,No endowments or contributions exist in this ecological cyclic-dominance model.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No communication; non-human agents.,1,N/A,No user interface or displayed summaries; this is a simulation.,1,N/A,No punishment based on identity.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,No concept of shown rounds; time evolution only in simulation code.,1,0,"Only dominance-removal, reproduction, and mobility; no sanctioning choice with costs.",1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,"No explicit rewarding mechanism; reproduction is automatic, not a chosen reward.",1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,Species do not have token endowments.,1,N/A,There is no contribution decision; key outcomes are coexistence probabilities and pattern scales.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,They evaluate biodiversity (coexistence vs extinction) and pattern size; do not define an efficiency metric.,0.9,N/R,"No aggregate payoff measure is reported; only densities, extinction probability, and pattern characteristics.",0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational experiments on lattices and associated PDEs; not human or field experiments.,1,"Two-dimensional square lattice of size N (e.g., 512^2). Each site is empty or occupied by species A, B, or C. Local interactions: dominance-removal A B -> A ∅, B C -> B ∅, C A -> C ∅ at rate p=1; reproduction X ∅ -> X X at rate q=1; z=0, μ=0. Mobility via pair-exchange: X Y -> Y X and X ∅ -> ∅ X at rate γ, with mobility coefficient m=2γ/N. Starting from random initial conditions, they study emergent spiral/target patterns, critical mobility m_c ≈ 4.5×10^-4 above which spirals outgrow system so two species go extinct, and effect of competition rate p on spiral fragmentation. PDE approximation (3.2) with diffusion coefficient D_m is used to reproduce patterns.","Summarizes key structural and dynamical aspects of the spatial game as described in section 3.2, including interaction rules and main outcomes.",1
10.1073_pnas.161155698.md,"Public Goods with Punishment minigame without reputation (μ=0, ν=0)",Identified the baseline punishment model where μ=ν=0 and reputation is absent; corresponds to Fig. 1 description.,1,"Game type: Public Goods with Punishment minigame; Reputation parameters μ=0, ν=0; Parameters c=1, g=3, β=2, γ=1 (example values used in figure).",Paper specifies these parameters for Fig. 1 and describes this as Public Goods with Punishment without reputation (μ=ν=0).,0.93,False,"Model is a theoretical evolutionary game with replicator dynamics, no human participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; purely theoretical model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; mathematical model only.,1,False,"Dynamics are analyzed analytically; figures are phase portraits from equations, not explicit numeric simulation treatments.",0.8,True,Replicator equations and fixed-point analysis constitute an analytical model.,1,2,Minigame explicitly defined for two players.,1,1,Single-shot minigame interaction; no repetition within a play.,0.95,1,"Players choose binary gift decision, not a continuous amount; contribution is effectively all-or-nothing but encoded as a single discrete option, which we treat as 1 (can contribute ‘any amount’ is not accurate, but schema requires 1/0; using 1 to indicate not forced to contribute full endowment in continuous sense would be misleading, however constraints force a choice; mark with low confidence).",0.3,N/A,No human experimental endowment allocation; purely theoretical minigame.,1,N/R,Standard MPCR for N-player public goods not defined in this 2-player gift framework.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,0,Model does not include feedback information structure of this type.,1,0,Identity is not modeled; only strategy types and payoffs.,1,0,Rewards are not present in this treatment.,1,0,Rounds and their display are not modeled.,1,1,Players can impose a fine β at cost γ to themselves.,1,N/R,"Per-unit ‘cost’ parameter γ is given, but no explicit discrete units of punishment specified; minigame uses a fixed fine, not scalable units.",0.6,N/R,"Model defines a fixed fine β for a punishment act, not a per-unit technology.",0.6,0,Only punishment is available; no reward/payback option.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/R,"Players incur costs and benefits c, g, β, γ, but an explicit initial endowment is not defined.",0.4,N/R,Paper focuses on dynamics over strategy frequencies; does not report normalized contribution rates.,0.2,N/R,"No specific quantitative equilibrium contribution level is given, only qualitative outcomes (e.g., social state G1 vs asocial G3).",0.2,N/R,Efficiency measures (payoff relative to maximum) are not calculated numerically.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff values are reported.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely analytical evolutionary game model.,1,Two-player minigame of gift-giving with punishment; strategies combine cooperate/defect (e1/e2) and punish/not (f1/f2); analyzed under replicator dynamics on simplex S4 with invariant manifolds WK.,Summarized key structural aspects of the modeled game.,1
10.1073_pnas.161155698.md,Public Goods with Punishment minigame with reputation (Public Goods with Second Thoughts),Condition where reputation is introduced via nonzero μ and ν in the punishment minigame; called 'Public Goods with Second Thoughts'.,1,"Game type: Public Goods with Punishment and Reputation ('Second Thoughts'); Reputation parameters μ>0, ν≥0 (e.g., μ=0.1, ν=0.1 in Fig. 3); Punishment parameters c=1, g=3, β=2, γ=1 (example).",Paper defines this modified payoff matrix and analyzes dynamics with nonzero μ and ν; Fig. 3 gives sample parameterization.,0.93,False,Analytical evolutionary game model; no human data.,1,False,Not a controlled subject experiment.,1,False,No physical experimental environment.,1,False,Uses analytical derivations of replicator dynamics; figures illustrate flows but are derived analytically.,0.8,True,"Replicator equations with modified payoffs, analysis of fixed points and bifurcation.",1,2,Same two-player minigame as baseline punishment scenario.,1,1,Single-stage interaction with follow-up punishment opportunity; no explicit repetition.,0.95,1,Binary cooperation choice (send gift or not); treated as discrete contribution decision.,0.3,N/A,Theoretical model without endowment allocation protocol.,1,N/R,Standard MPCR not defined in this gift-punishment minigame.,1,N/A,No communication modeled between agents.,1,0,"Information structure only modeled via reputation parameters μ, ν, not via payoff summaries.",1,0,"Reputation concerns punishing behavior type (f1 vs f2), not individual identities as in an experiment.",0.9,0,Reward not part of this treatment.,1,0,No notion of displayed horizon.,1,1,Players can impose a fine β with cost γ.,1,N/R,"γ defined as cost, but not in discrete per-unit terms required by schema.",0.6,N/R,"β is fine magnitude per punishment act, but not framed as scalable ‘per unit’ technology.",0.6,0,This is a punishment-based minigame; rewards introduced only in later section.,1,N/A,No rewards in this condition.,1,N/A,