10.1073_pnas.161155698.md,"Ultimatum minigame (binary offers, analytical mapping to punishment minigame)",Paper defines a two-option Ultimatum minigame and shows it is a special case of the punishment minigame.,1,"Game type: Ultimatum minigame; Proposer options: high offer h vs low offer l (0<l<h<1); Responder types: f1 accept high only, f2 accept all.",Explicitly described in section ‘Public Goods with Punishment and Ultimatum Minigames’.,1,False,No actual ultimatum experiments run here; only theoretical payoff and dynamics.,1,False,"Modeling, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,Game is analyzed analytically as a special case of the general minigame.,0.9,True,"Derives payoff matrix, R,S,r,s, and conditions such as risk dominance.",1,2,Ultimatum game involves two players: proposer and responder.,1,1,Standard ultimatum game is not repeated.,1,1,"Proposer chooses between exactly two discrete offers (high/low); within schema constraints, coded as 1 because contribution is not forced to be all-or-nothing relative to a continuous endowment, but this is approximate.",0.3,N/A,No public account in ultimatum; it is a bargaining over a fixed sum.,1,N/R,Not a public goods structure.,1,N/A,Communication not modeled.,1,0,No learning across rounds; no summaries defined.,1,0,Rejection is framed as punishment but without explicit identities.,0.9,0,No reward stage; acceptance just follows rules.,1,0,One-shot interaction; no horizon display.,1,1,"Responder can reject low offers, which costs both players – modeled as punishment equivalent.",0.95,N/R,"Responder’s cost of rejecting is the forgone amount l, but not described as scalable per-unit cost.",0.5,N/R,Punishment magnitude is 1−l for the proposer; not parameterized as ‘per coin’.,0.5,0,No explicit reward action beyond fair offers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Sum to be divided is normalized to 1.,1,N/R,Theoretical model does not specify equilibrium offer frequencies numerically.,0.2,N/R,No solved equilibrium offer distributions provided; only analytic conditions like h<1/2.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency measures not numerically analyzed.,0.2,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff outcomes reported.,0.2,N/A,No empirical ultimatum data collected.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical mapping within theoretical framework.,1,"Two-role minigame: proposers choose high (h) or low (l) offers; responders either accept only high (f1) or accept all (f2); mapped to punishment minigame with l=γ, β=1−l, g=c=h−l; dynamics handled via same four-strategy G1–G4 structure.",Summarizes core structural and mapping details relevant to game design.,1
10.1073_pnas.161155698.md,"Public Goods with Reward minigame without reputation (μ=0, ν=0)",Baseline reward model where μ=ν=0; corresponds to description around Fig. 4.,1,"Game type: Public Goods with Reward minigame; Reputation parameters μ=0, ν=0; Parameters e.g., c=1, g=3, β=2, γ=1 (as in figures).",Section 'Reward and Reputation' first defines reward minigame and then discusses case ν=0 (and μ=0 used in Q definition) as no-reputation baseline.,0.93,False,No human experiments; purely theoretical evolutionary model.,1,False,Not a subject experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,False,Dynamics derived analytically.,0.8,True,Replicator dynamics and fixed point analysis for reward minigame.,1,2,Two players simultaneously decide whether to send gift; later can reward.,1,1,Single interaction with follow-up reward opportunity.,0.95,1,Binary gift decision (send or not).,0.3,N/A,No human endowment structure modeled.,1,N/R,Game is not framed as standard N-player public good.,1,N/A,Communication not represented.,1,0,No such feedback components.,1,0,Punishment does not exist in this condition.,1,0,"Reputation about rewarder type exists only when μ,ν>0, but not as identity revelation.",0.9,0,No rounds/horizon modeled.,1,0,Only rewarding (returning part of the gift) is available.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Recipients can return part of gift at cost γ yielding β to donor.,1,N/R,"γ is cost per reward act, but not broken into per-unit ‘tokens’.",0.6,N/R,β defined as benefit per reward act; not scalable per discrete unit.,0.6,N/R,"Endowments are not explicitly defined; just costs and benefits c,g,β,γ.",0.4,N/R,Paper does not report contribution levels numerically.,0.2,N/R,No numeric equilibrium gift amounts beyond binary decision.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency values.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not tabulated.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely analytical model of reward dynamics.,1,"Two-player minigame: each may send gift (cost −c, benefit g to other); recipients of gifts may reward (pay γ to give β to donor); with μ=ν=0, edge G2–G3 is line of fixed points, segment G3–Q are asocial Nash equilibria where no gifts are made but some players would reward.",Captures essential game rules and main dynamic characteristic for this condition.,1
10.1073_pnas.161155698.md,"Public Goods with Reward and Reputation (μ>0, ν>0)",Condition where reward minigame includes nonzero reputation parameters μ and ν; associated with Fig. 5.,1,"Game type: Public Goods with Reward and Reputation; Reputation parameters μ>0, ν>0 (e.g., μ=0.1, ν=0.1 in Fig. 5); Cost/benefit parameters e.g., c=1, g=3, β=2, γ=1.","Section describes adding reputation via μ, ν to reward minigame and analyzes dynamics; Fig. 5 gives example parameterization.",0.93,False,No experimental subjects; model only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab/field context.,1,False,Behavior studied via analytical replicator dynamics and bifurcation analysis.,0.8,True,"Analytical derivation of line L of fixed points, behavior depending on Δ, etc.",1,2,Same two-player reward minigame structure.,1,1,Single interaction with potential reward stage; repeated encounters modeled only at population level.,0.95,1,Binary gift decision (send vs not).,0.3,N/A,No experimental endowment model.,1,N/R,Not a standard public good.,1,N/A,No communication or messaging.,1,0,"Information captured only via reputation parameters μ, ν, not summaries.",1,0,No punishment exists here.,1,0,"Reputation concerns rewarder vs nonrewarder type, not identities.",0.9,0,No explicit round information modeled.,1,0,"Players can only reward, not punish.",1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Players may reward their donor; reward costs γ and yields β to coplayer.,1,N/R,γ is defined but not in ‘per-unit’ terms.,0.6,N/R,"β is benefit per reward act, not described as per discrete unit.",0.6,N/R,No initial per-player endowment specified.,0.4,N/R,Only qualitative statements about frequency of gift-givers and complex dynamics are provided.,0.2,N/R,Gift decision is binary; no numeric frequencies/amounts reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency or average payoffs not given numerically.,0.2,N/R,No explicit group payoff values.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary dynamics.,1,"Two-player gift and reward minigame with reputation: μ is probability cooperators know a nonrewarder and defect; ν is probability defectors know a rewarder and cooperate. Dynamics feature a transversal line L of fixed points; depending on Δ, states spiral toward L or a heteroclinic cycle among corners; yields complex, nonconvergent oscillations between strategies.",Summarizes model structure and notable dynamic behavior for this condition.,1
10.1126_science.1183665.md,"Coordinated punishment evolutionary model (random groups, no assortment)",Paper presents a formal evolutionary model with coordinated punishment; first main condition assumes groups are randomly formed with no genetic assortment.,0.95,"Punisher strategy type (τ-punisher vs nonpunisher), punishment threshold τ, group size n, benefits b, cost of cooperation c, punishment cost parameters (k, a, p), signaling cost q, expected number of periods T, error rate e; random group formation with relatedness r = 0",Model description specifies these parameters and compares punishers vs nonpunishers under random group formation (r = 0).,0.95,False,Model is theoretical; no human or other real-world subjects participate.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral or field experiment; it is an evolutionary game-theoretic model.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; purely analytical/simulation.,1,True,"Population dynamics and equilibria are computed from model equations; figures (e.g., Fig. 1–3) show numerical results typical of simulations, with no human subjects.",0.9,True,Text describes deriving evolutionary outcomes and equilibria analytically (reference to equations in (23)); core results are from analytical derivations.,0.95,18,"Text notes that when τ = 3, a minimum of four out of 18 individuals punish, implying baseline group size n = 18 for benchmark results.",0.8,0,Model uses an indefinite repeated interaction with continuation probability 1 − 1/T; no fixed round count applicable as in a lab game.,0.9,1,"Individuals choose to cooperate or defect (binary decision), not continuous contributions; in this schema, 1 denotes ability to choose any allowed action; here only two discrete options exist but not an enforced 'all must contribute' rule.",0.6,N/A,Model is not framed as a contribution-from-endowment public goods game with an initial allocation; it is a cooperation/defection decision.,1,N/R,"Public good benefit is modeled via parameters b and group size n, but an explicit marginal per capita return (multiplier/group size) is not stated in that terminology.",0.5,N/A,"No human subjects or explicit communication protocol; model includes a signaling stage but that is a strategic variable, not human chat.",1,N/A,No user interface or feedback as in laboratory experiments; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No notion of observable identities; model uses anonymous group interactions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,There is no interface or display of rounds to participants because there are no participants.,1,1,"Punishment is a central mechanism; punishers can inflict cost p on defectors at cost depending on k, a, and number of punishers.",1,N/R,Cost per punisher is modeled as k / n_p^a but the specific numerical value of k is not given in the excerpt.,0.4,N/R,Target cost p is specified symbolically but not numerically; cannot give a numeric tech factor.,0.4,0,Model includes only costly punishment of defectors; no prosocial rewards are described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/R,Model describes costs and benefits per cooperation act but not an explicit per-period endowment in tokens or points.,0.5,N/R,"Outcome variables reported are evolutionary frequencies of punishers and average fitness, not normalized cooperation rates per group member.",0.4,N/R,Model does not report raw contribution amounts per round; only probabilities of cooperation and resulting payoffs.,0.4,N/R,"They discuss that populations with punishers can achieve higher average fitness and up to two-thirds of maximum gains for certain τ, but no explicit normalized efficiency metric (0–1) is numerically reported.",0.5,N/R,Figures and text refer to differences in average fitness but do not provide a specific numeric group payoff for this baseline parameter set in the excerpt.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; theoretical evolutionary agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a theoretical/simulation model with no empirical data collection.,1,"Repeated n-player cooperation game with punishment; group size baseline n = 18, cooperation costs c to cooperator and yields benefit b/n to each group member (b > c > b/n), punishment stage where coordinated punishers impose cost p on each defector at expected cost k / n_p^a to each punisher; first information-gathering period includes signaling with cost q and a threshold τ determining whether punishers cooperate and punish; continuation probability 1 − 1/T determines expected interaction length T.","Summarized key structural aspects of interaction as described in the model section, including stages and payoff parameters.",0.95
10.1126_science.1183665.md,"Coordinated punishment evolutionary model (modest assortment r = 0.07, n = 18)",Same core model as baseline but condition introduces modest positive assortment (relatedness r = 0.07) in group formation with group size n = 18; analyzed in Fig. 4.,0.95,"Punisher strategy type (τ-punisher vs nonpunisher), punishment threshold τ, group size n = 18, benefits b, cost of cooperation c, punishment parameters (k, a, p), signaling cost q, expected number of periods T, error rate e; nonrandom group formation with relatedness r = 0.07; two focal benefit conditions b = 2c and b = 4c","Text for Fig. 4 specifies r = 0.07, n = 18, and compares cases b = 2c and b = 4c across τ.",0.95,False,Condition is an extension of the theoretical evolutionary model; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,Not a behavioral or field experiment; it is a parameter variation within an analytical/simulation model.,1,False,No lab setting; entirely theoretical.,1,True,Equilibrium frequencies under r = 0.07 and different b values are computed and plotted; characteristic of simulations.,0.9,True,Authors derive conditions for invasion and equilibria analytically and then evaluate numerically; still fundamentally analytical.,0.9,18,"Explicitly states that even when τ = 3, meaning that a minimum of four out of 18 individuals punish, groups achieve about two thirds of maximum gains.",0.95,0,Same indefinite repeated structure with continuation probability 1 − 1/T; no finite round count.,0.9,1,Players choose to cooperate or defect in each period (binary action); same reasoning as in baseline condition.,0.6,N/A,Not modeled as redistribution of an initial endowment; binary cooperation decision instead.,1,N/R,Still no explicit MPCR summary; benefits characterized by b and n.,0.5,N/A,No human communication; only model signaling.,1,N/A,No user interface/output to human participants.,1,N/A,No explicit identities modeled.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,No explicit presentation of round counts to agents.,1,1,Same coordinated punishment mechanism as in baseline condition remains in place.,1,N/R,Cost function for punishment (k / n_p^a) is unchanged and still not numerically specified.,0.4,N/R,Punishment cost to targets (p) remains symbolic with no numeric value.,0.4,0,No reward mechanism added in assortment condition.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"As before, model uses costs and benefits per act without explicit numeric endowment.",0.5,N/R,"They state that cooperation is sustained in most groups for certain τ and b values, but do not provide precise numeric cooperation rates.",0.5,N/R,No explicit numeric contributions or number of cooperators reported.,0.4,N/R,Text says groups achieve about two thirds of maximum gains from cooperation for τ = 3 but does not give a precise numeric efficiency measure; approximate fraction is not formalized as DV.,0.6,N/R,They compare average fitness across equilibria but do not provide explicit numeric group payoff levels for these assortment cases in the excerpt.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Condition is a variant of the same theoretical model with modified relatedness parameter.,1,"Same three-stage interaction structure (signaling, cooperation, punishment) with repeated periods and coordination threshold τ for punishment. Here, group formation includes genetic assortment with relatedness r = 0.07 so that similar strategists more often interact; equilibrium analysis considers benefit levels b = 2c (where rare punishers cannot invade for larger τ) and b = 4c (where for 0 < τ ≤ 3 rare punishers invade and the only stable equilibrium is a mixture of punishers and nonpunishers). For larger thresholds, both all-nonpunisher and mixed equilibria can exist, with an unstable interior frequency marking invasion threshold.","Summarizes modifications relative to random-group model, focusing on relatedness r and benefit ratios b/c, as described in text and Fig. 4 discussion.",0.95
10.1126_science.1183665.md,"Coordinated punishment evolutionary model (large groups n = 72, lower relatedness, higher benefit-cost ratio)","Authors briefly describe an additional parameter regime where groups are larger (n = 72) and relatedness is lower, with higher benefit-cost ratio; though details are delegated to (23), it is a distinct modeled condition.",0.7,"Punisher strategy type (τ-punisher vs nonpunisher), punishment threshold τ, group size n = 72, lower relatedness r (exact value not reported), higher benefit-cost ratio b/c (exact numeric ratio not reported), punishment parameters (k, a, p), signaling cost q, expected number of periods T, error rate e",Text states that the result persists when groups are much larger (n = 72) and for lower levels of relatedness if the benefit-cost ratio is somewhat higher.,0.7,False,Still part of the theoretical evolutionary model.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with real participants; only parameter changes in the model.,1,False,No laboratory component.,1,True,"Condition is evaluated via the same analytical/simulation framework for different parameters (n, r, b/c).",0.85,True,Same analytical model underlies this parameter variation.,0.85,72,Explicitly notes that result persists when groups are much larger (n = 72).,0.95,0,Same repeated-game structure with expected length T; no fixed round number.,0.9,1,Binary cooperate/defect choice remains unchanged.,0.6,N/A,Not a standard public-goods-with-endowment formulation.,1,N/R,b and n given but MPCR not explicitly defined; and here b/c is mentioned only qualitatively as 'somewhat higher'.,0.4,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No experimental display.,1,N/A,No identities modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,1,Same coordinated punishment mechanism applies.,1,N/R,No numeric cost parameter values for this regime in excerpt.,0.3,N/R,p remains symbolic; no numeric values provided.,0.3,0,Model continues to use only punishment.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit per-period endowment defined.,0.5,N/R,"Text only states that the result persists (i.e., punishment and cooperation remain stable) but gives no numeric cooperation rate.",0.3,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,0.3,N/R,They claim that the qualitative result on punishment and cooperation holds; efficiency values are not provided.,0.3,N/R,No explicit group payoff numbers for n = 72 condition.,0.3,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,No human,Parameter variation within the same theoretical model.,1,"Same coordinated punishment, signaling, and repeated interaction structure as in baseline and r = 0.07 conditions. Here, group size is increased to n = 72 and relatedness r is reduced (exact value not provided). Authors state that with a somewhat higher benefit-cost ratio b/c, the qualitative result that punishment can proliferate and sustain cooperation under modest assortment continues to hold.",Summarizes the brief description of this robustness check condition appearing in the text.,0.7
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,"Public goods – baseline (no punishment, first 10 periods)","Distinct phase of the Fehr & Gächter (2000) public goods game without punishment, first 10 rounds, described with specific parameters (N=4, Y=20, M=1.6).",0.95,Punishment opportunity: absent; Group composition: fixed partners; Rounds: 10,Condition differs from later phase only by absence of punishment; partners are stable groups; first 10 periods are explicitly separated.,0.9,True,Human subjects in a laboratory public goods experiment are described.,1,True,Controlled laboratory public goods game with random assignment and treatments.,1,True,Described as laboratory experiment; no field setting mentioned.,0.95,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; only human subjects.,1,False,"This condition is behavioral, not a formal model.",1,4,Figure 1 caption: six groups with N=4.,1,10,First ten repetitions (periods) before punishment is introduced.,1,1,"Standard linear public goods game where each subject can invest up to Y; implies continuous choice between 0 and Y, not all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Endowment Y is initially in private account; subjects decide how much to invest in group account.,0.9,0.4,"MPCR = M/N = 1.6/4 = 0.4, using reported M and N.",1,0,No mention of communication; standard Fehr & Gächter public goods game prohibits communication.,0.8,1,Text: 'if the group receives feedback about the individual contributions at the end of each repetition'; implies subjects see others’ contributions.,0.9,0,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,1,Subjects are said to 'know that the experiment ends in round 20'; implies awareness of horizon in earlier rounds too.,0.85,0,This is the baseline without punishment opportunities.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No mention of any rewarding option.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Figure 1 description: Y=20.,1,N/R,"Only qualitative trends and some percentages (e.g., 55% contribute nothing) are given; average normalized contribution over the condition is not reported.",0.2,N/R,No exact average contribution amounts for the 10-round baseline are given.,0.2,N/R,No total or average payoffs reported to compute efficiency.,0.1,N/R,No quantitative payoff data presented.,0.1,N/R,This review summarizes Fehr & Gächter (2000) but does not state the country of the lab in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No demographic details reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,N/R,No education or student status specified in this excerpt.,0.1,On site,Described as laboratory experiments; timeframe (pre-online) implies physical lab setting.,0.9,"Standard linear public goods game with fixed partner matching; six groups of 4; each period: each member endowed with 20, chooses contribution [0,20] to group account; total multiplied by 1.6 and evenly redistributed; feedback on individual contributions each period; 10 periods in this baseline phase.",Summarized from the description surrounding Figure 1.,0.95
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,Public goods – partner punishment condition (periods 11–20),Same groups as baseline but with added punishment opportunity; often termed partner punishment or stable groups.,0.95,Punishment opportunity: present (partners); Group composition: fixed partners; Rounds: 10 following baseline,"After period 10, experimenters 'gave subjects the opportunity to punish each other'; in partner treatment groups remain stable.",0.95,True,Same human-subject laboratory experiment as baseline.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment option within the lab.,1,True,Described as laboratory public goods experiment.,0.95,False,"Behavioral experiment, no agents.",1,False,"Not a formal model, but an experimental treatment.",1,4,Same six groups with N=4 as in baseline; same subjects.,1,10,Second block of ten periods (periods 11–20) with punishment.,1,1,"Contribution choice remains 'up to Y', implying continuous from 0 to 20.",0.9,0,Endowment starts privately; subjects choose contributions.,0.9,0.4,"Public good technology unchanged: M=1.6, N=4; MPCR=M/N.",1,0,No communication reported; standard Fehr & Gächter design forbids chat.,0.8,1,"As before, subjects see individual contributions each period; additional info includes punishment points.",0.9,1,Subjects punish specific group members; must know identities within group to assign punishment points.,0.9,0,Only costly punishment points are mentioned; no rewards.,0.95,1,Text: 'The subjects know that the experiment ends in round 20.',1,1,Explicitly: 'They gave subjects the opportunity to punish each other'.,1,1,Each punishment point assigned reduces punisher’s payoff by 1 money unit.,1,3,"Each punishment point reduces the punished subject's payoff by '10% (i.e., roughly 3 money units)' given typical earnings ~30 MU; use 3 as impact per point.",0.7,0,No rewarding is described; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same endowment Y=20 as baseline.,1,N/R,"They report that average cooperation 'reaches almost 100%' and 83% contribute full endowment in period 20, but not an exact mean across this 10-period condition.",0.3,N/R,No exact numerical average contribution over these rounds reported.,0.2,N/R,No payoff aggregates to compute realized efficiency.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff statistics provided.,0.1,N/R,Country not specified in this summary of Fehr & Gächter (2000).,0.1,N/R,No age data reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,N/R,No explicit mention of students or other education characteristics.,0.1,On site,Laboratory punishment public goods experiment.,0.95,"Same public goods game as baseline, but after observing others’ contributions each period, each subject can assign integer punishment points to each of the other 3 group members. Each punishment point costs the punisher 1 MU and reduces the punished subject’s income from this period by about 3 MU. Groups are fixed (partner treatment). Total of 20 periods; this condition covers periods 11–20.",Summarized from punishment description and Figure 1.,0.95
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,Public goods – stranger punishment condition,"Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002) variant where group composition changes randomly; referred to as 'stranger' in Figure 3.",0.95,Punishment opportunity: present; Matching: random strangers (no pair meets twice); Horizon: multiple periods,Text: group composition changed randomly so that no subject met another more than once; punishment remains available.,0.95,True,Human participants in laboratory public goods experiment.,1,True,Treatment manipulation of matching structure under controlled lab conditions.,1,True,Described as experiments by Fehr & Gächter within lab context.,0.9,False,No non-human simulation.,1,False,"Empirical treatment, not a formal model.",1,4,Assumed same public goods parameters as partner condition (standard Fehr & Gächter design); no contrary information provided.,0.7,N/R,Number of periods in stranger treatment not stated in text excerpt.,0.1,1,Same linear public goods stage game ('contributions up to Y'); no indication of binary choice.,0.8,0,Standard public goods game: endowment starts in private account.,0.8,0.4,Likely identical parameters to partner treatment; Fehr & Gächter use same game structure across partner/stranger; no alternative M or N provided.,0.7,0,No mention of communication; standard design forbids chat.,0.8,1,Punishment must be based on observing others' contributions; same feedback structure as partner treatment.,0.85,1,Subjects assign punishment points to each of the other group members; implies knowledge of who is punished within period.,0.9,0,No reward option described.,0.95,0,"Text emphasizes no future interaction with same partner and compares with earlier work where subjects did not know number of periods; but here, stranger design’s horizon disclosure is not specified; default to no based on uncertainty.",0.3,1,"Same punishment mechanism as partner punishment condition, but with random matching.",1,1,Same costly punishment: 1 MU cost per punishment point.,0.9,3,Same technology: each punishment point reduces target’s payoff by about 3 MU.,0.7,0,No rewards mentioned.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Likely same game (Y=20) as in partner condition; no alternate endowment reported.,0.7,N/R,No contribution averages reported for stranger punishment treatment.,0.1,N/R,No numerical contribution data given.,0.1,N/R,"Only punishment points by deviation are shown, not earnings.",0.1,N/R,No payoff descriptions for this treatment.,0.1,N/R,Country not specified.,0.1,N/R,No demographic info reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,N/R,No education information.,0.1,On site,Laboratory experiment with random matching.,0.9,"Linear public goods game with same contribution and punishment mechanism as partner treatment, but matching is random each period so that no two subjects are ever in the same group twice. Punishment exhibits similar pattern to partner treatment: free riders (those contributing below group average) receive substantial punishment points.",Summarized from description of 'stranger' setting and Figure 3.,0.9
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,Public goods – conditional contribution elicitation,"Fischbacher et al. (2001) design used to measure conditional cooperation, summarized in Figure 2.",0.9,Strategy method for contributions: unconditional vs conditional on others’ average contribution (no punishment),Figure 2: subjects state contributions as a function of others’ expected average contribution; distinguishes conditional cooperators from free riders.,0.9,True,Based on public goods experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled variation using strategy method in a lab game.,1,True,Standard lab public goods experiment referenced.,0.9,False,Human subjects only.,1,False,Empirical; not a purely theoretical model.,1,N/R,This summary does not state group size for Fischbacher et al. (2001).,0.1,N/R,Only refers to baseline public goods incentives; number of repetitions omitted.,0.1,1,Described as same public goods incentives as in Figure 1 where contributions can be any amount up to Y.,0.8,0,Standard linear public good with individual endowment and voluntary contributions.,0.8,N/R,Parameters N and M for this specific experiment are not provided in the text.,0.1,0,No communication mentioned; standard design does not allow chat.,0.7,1,"Conditional contribution choices are based on beliefs about others; usual design provides feedback on others’ contributions, but specifics not stated.",0.6,0,This is a no-punishment public goods setting.,0.95,0,No rewards described.,0.95,N/R,Horizon information not described.,0.1,0,Described explicitly as experiment 'without a direct punishment opportunity'.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward option mentioned.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Text: 'same public goods incentives (without a direct punishment opportunity) as in Figure 1 above'; in Figure 1 Y=20.,0.8,N/R,Figure 2 shows conditional contribution schedule but not a single aggregate rate per condition.,0.2,