10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,"Gift exchange – bilateral (one employer, one worker, equal numbers)",Fehr & Falk (1999) bilateral gift exchange experiment where number of workers equals number of employers.,0.9,"Market structure: bilateral (1 worker per employer, equal numbers, no excess supply); Contract: incomplete (effort not enforceable); Third-party enforcement: absent",Text distinguishes 'bilateral gift exchange experiment' from 'competitive gift exchange experiment' by presence/absence of excess supply; both lack third-party enforcement of effort.,0.9,True,Laboratory gift exchange experiments with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled wage and effort choices under different market structures.,1,True,Standard lab gift exchange design; not field data.,0.9,False,No simulations described.,1,False,Empirical experiment.,1,2,Gift exchange game has one employer and one employee interacting.,1,N/R,Number of periods for Fehr & Falk (1999) bilateral treatment not given in this summary.,0.1,1,"Employee chooses effort level E from a range, not binary; employer chooses wage W on a continuum.",0.9,0,"Not a contribution game; notion inapplicable; but per instructions, use 0 when endowment starts private if humans; here, wages are chosen, no default public fund.",0.3,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; MPCR concept does not apply.,0.1,0,No communication between employer and employee is mentioned.,0.7,0,Bilateral interaction; no mention of showing others’ earnings or summaries.,0.7,0,"No explicit punishment stage; discipline is via wage-effort reciprocity, not a punishment technology.",0.9,0,No separate reward technology; wages are part of main game.,0.9,N/R,Horizon information not provided.,0.1,0,No explicit costly punishment option distinct from effort choice.,0.95,N/A,No formal punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No formal punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward operator; wage is the primary decision variable.,0.9,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game uses wages and costs, not a fixed token endowment; no initial endowment reported.",0.1,N/R,"Key DV is effort as a function of wage, not normalized contribution rate; no such measure reported.",0.1,N/R,Effort levels are reported qualitatively ('higher effort with higher wages') but no numeric averages given.,0.2,N/R,No payoff or efficiency statistics provided.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate surplus or payoff numbers reported.,0.1,N/R,Country of the lab not stated.,0.1,N/R,No demographics.,0.1,N/R,No gender info.,0.1,N/R,No education info.,0.1,On site,Described as laboratory gift exchange experiments.,0.9,"One employer offers a binding wage W to one employee. After observing W, the employee chooses an effort level E. Employer payoff: π = A×E − W. Employee payoff: U = W − C(E), with C(E) increasing in E. Effort is not contractible or third-party enforceable, so the employee is free to choose E; enforcement relies on reciprocity and fairness motives.",Summarized from description of gift exchange game in text.,1
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,Gift exchange – competitive (excess supply of workers),Fehr & Falk (1999) competitive gift exchange experiment with excess supply of workers.,0.95,Market structure: competitive (excess supply of workers; fewer employers than workers); Contract: incomplete (effort not enforceable); Third-party enforcement: absent,"Text: 'there was an excess supply of workers in the competitive condition, whereas the number of workers and the number of employers was the same in the bilateral condition.'",0.95,True,Human-subject lab experiment comparing market structures.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of supply-demand conditions.,1,True,Laboratory experiment.,0.9,False,No simulation.,1,False,Empirical; not just a model.,1,2,"Exchange remains bilateral per match (one employer and one worker), though there is an excess pool of workers.",0.9,N/R,Number of periods not specified.,0.1,1,"Wage W and effort E chosen from ranges, not binary.",0.9,0,Not a public goods context; wages originate from employer decisions.,0.3,N/R,MPCR not applicable.,0.1,0,No mention of communication.,0.7,0,No mention of broadcasting others’ wages/effort; focus is bilateral matches and aggregate wage levels.,0.6,0,No separate punishment technology.,0.9,0,No separate reward technology.,0.9,N/R,Horizon not specified.,0.1,0,No formal punishment option beyond wage-effort reciprocity.,0.95,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No food/points reward stage separate from wage choice.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/R,Not formulated in terms of token endowments; wages and productivity parameter A instead.,0.1,N/R,Primary DVs are wages and effort; no normalized contribution measure.,0.1,N/R,No numeric wage or effort levels reported in this summary.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency statistics given.,0.1,N/R,No payoffs reported.,0.1,N/R,Country not given.,0.1,N/R,No age data.,0.1,N/R,No gender data.,0.1,N/R,No education details.,0.1,On site,Laboratory competitive gift exchange experiment.,0.9,"Same gift exchange structure as bilateral treatment (employer chooses wage W, worker chooses effort E, payoffs π = A×E − W and U = W − C(E), effort not enforceable by third party). Market-level difference: number of workers exceeds number of employers so that workers must compete to get a job offer. Despite excess supply, wages remain similar to bilateral case because employers pay 'fair' wages to elicit high effort from strongly reciprocal workers.",Summarized from the Fehr & Falk (1999) description.,0.95
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,"Gift exchange – one-shot matching (no long-term relationships, excess workers)",Brown et al. (2004) 'one-shot' treatment where IDs are randomized each period; workers in excess supply.,0.95,"Relationship structure: one-shot (ID rematched each period, no repeat partner); Contract enforcement: absent; Market: excess supply of workers","Text: 'one-shot (circles)' treatment: IDs randomly assigned each period; there is an excess supply of workers, some workers unemployed each period.",0.95,True,Laboratory experiment with employers and workers.,1,True,Controlled treatment comparison of long-term vs one-shot relations.,1,True,Described as experiment; no field context.,0.9,False,Human subjects only.,1,False,"Behavioral treatment, not analytic model.",1,N/R,Specific number of employers/workers in Brown et al. (2004) not given in this summary.,0.1,15,Text: 'The experiment lasted 15 periods in both the one-shot (circles) and the relations (squares) treatment.',1,1,Effort choice e between 1 and 10; wage free to choose; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,No endowment contribution; wages are offers; mapping to this field not meaningful but default 0 as private until exchanged.,0.3,N/R,Not a public good.,0.1,0,No communication reported; decisions are via offers and effort levels.,0.7,0,No mention of showing others’ contracts or earnings; interactions are bilateral with market-level unemployment.,0.6,0,No punishment technology distinct from contract continuation; dropping relations is not a formal punishment stage.,0.9,0,No separate rewards.,0.9,1,Subjects know the experiment lasts 15 periods (explicitly stated).,0.95,0,"No costly punishment points or fines; employers can only condition future offers, which is absent in one-shot treatment due to random IDs.",0.95,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No distinct reward mechanism separate from wage offers.,0.95,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game structured in terms of wages and effort; no individual token endowment reported.,0.1,N/R,Key DV is average effort; reported qualitatively as 'quite limited'; no numeric rate.,0.2,N/R,No exact mean effort values printed; only figure trend.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency differences described qualitatively; no numbers.,0.2,N/R,No payoff levels for one-shot condition.,0.1,N/R,Country of Brown et al. (2004) lab not reported here.,0.1,N/R,No demographics.,0.1,N/R,No gender info.,0.1,N/R,No education details.,0.1,On site,Laboratory gift exchange-style market experiment.,0.9,"In each of 15 periods, employers can make wage offers either to specific workers or to all workers. There is an excess supply of workers so some remain unemployed each period. IDs are randomly reassigned each period so an employer cannot reliably identify and rehire the same worker. After a worker accepts an offer, they choose effort between 1 (minimum) and 10 (maximum). Average effort remains relatively low compared to the relations treatment.",Summarized from description around Figure 4.,0.95
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,"Gift exchange – relations treatment (long-term relationships, excess workers, no third-party enforcement)",Brown et al. (2004) 'relations' treatment allowing long-term employer–worker relationships.,0.95,Relationship structure: relations (fixed IDs allow repeated interaction with same partner); Contract enforcement: absent; Market: excess supply of workers,Text: 'relations (squares)' treatment with fixed identification numbers and possibility to renew relationships; still excess supply of workers.,0.95,True,Laboratory experiment involving employers and workers.,1,True,Treatment manipulation (relations vs one-shot) in controlled lab market.,1,True,Described as experiment; not field-based.,0.9,False,Human subjects only.,1,False,Empirical treatment.,1,N/R,"Exact counts of employers and workers not provided, only that there is excess supply of workers.",0.1,15,Same 15 periods as in one-shot treatment.,1,1,"Effort choice between 1 and 10, wages continuous; not all-or-nothing.",1,0,No explicit initial endowment contributions; wages are offers.,0.3,N/R,Non-public-good design.,0.1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,0.7,0,No display of others’ contracts or payoffs described.,0.6,0,"No explicit punishment technology; dropping a worker by not renewing is part of the core game, not a separate punishment stage.",0.9,0,No separate reward mechanism.,0.9,1,Experiment length (15 periods) stated; likely known to participants.,0.9,0,No external punishment technology; enforcement via threat of not renewing relationships.,0.95,N/A,No formal punishment points or analogous mechanism.,1,N/A,No formal punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond offering higher wages.,0.95,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/R,Wage- and effort-based game; no stated fixed endowments.,0.1,N/R,DV is effort; not expressed as fraction of an endowment.,0.1,N/R,Figure 4 shows effort path qualitatively; no numbers.,0.2,N/R,"Text mentions that in long relationships (11–15 periods) the sum of earnings is as high as under third-party enforcement, but not as exact efficiency ratios.",0.3,N/R,No numeric payoff figures for relations treatment.,0.2,N/R,Country not given.,0.1,N/R,No demographics.,0.1,N/R,No gender data.,0.1,N/R,No education data.,0.1,On site,Laboratory market experiment.,0.9,"Same wage–effort structure and excess supply of workers as one-shot treatment. Identification numbers are fixed across all 15 periods, so an employer can seek out the same worker repeatedly. Employers tend to build long-term relationships with high-effort (trustworthy) workers by repeatedly offering them jobs and paying above-unemployment wages. Average effort and total earnings are substantially higher than in the one-shot treatment, especially in long-lasting relationships (11–15 periods), where joint earnings match those under third-party enforcement.","Summarized from description of relations treatment, Figure 4, and Figure 5.",0.95
10.1146_annurev.soc.33.040406.131812.md,Gift exchange – third-party enforcement vs relations (employer–employee earnings distribution),Brown et al. (2004) comparison of treatment with third-party contract enforcement of effort vs relations treatment without enforcement but with social exchange.,0.9,Contract enforcement: third-party enforced effort vs social exchange (relations); Market: excess supply of workers in both,Text: 'comparing the relations treatment with a treatment in which third parties enforce the effort level'; both treatments have excess supply of workers.,0.9,True,Laboratory experiment with two enforcement regimes.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of enforcement mechanism.,1,True,Laboratory gift-exchange style market.,0.9,False,No simulation.,1,False,Empirical comparison of treatments.,1,N/R,Exact numbers of employers and workers not given.,0.1,N/R,"Number of periods per treatment not specified here, though likely 15 as in relations treatment; not explicit.",0.3,1,Same wage and effort choice structure as relations treatment; not binary.,0.9,0,No explicit initial endowments.,0.3,N/R,Not a public goods game.,0.1,0,No communication mentioned.,0.7,0,Figure 5 reports earnings per trade but no indication participants saw others’ earnings in real time.,0.6,0,Third-party enforcement of effort is exogenous; no subject-initiated punishment technology.,0.95,0,No separate reward technology.,0.9,N/R,Horizon not specified in this part of the summary.,0.2,0,"In third-party enforcement treatment, deviations are enforced by an exogenous third party, not via costly punishment decisions by players.",0.95,N/A,No choice-based punishment technology.,1,N/A,No choice-based punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward dimension.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game defined by wage and effort choices; no endowment information.,0.1,N/R,"Again, main DVs are wages, effort, and distribution of earnings.",0.1,N/R,Effort levels and wages are not numerically summarized here.,0.2,N/R,Text notes that in longest relations combined earnings equal those under third-party enforcement but not as a normalized efficiency statistic.,0.3,N/R,Figure 5 shows employer and employee earnings per trade visually; no specific numeric averages are reported.,0.3,N/R,Country not provided.,0.1,N/R,No demographics.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,N/R,No education data.,0.1,On site,Laboratory comparative experiment on contract enforcement vs social exchange.,0.9,"In third-party enforcement treatment, contracts specifying effort are enforceable; employers need not worry about trust and identity, and trade is mostly in one-shot interactions. In relations treatment (no third-party enforcement, social exchange), employers rely on long-term relationships and effort-based reciprocity to enforce effort. Both have excess supply of workers. Results: Under third-party enforcement, earnings distribution is very unequal (workers earn significantly less than employers), whereas under relations/social exchange, earnings are more equally split, and longlasting relationships achieve total earnings comparable to third-party enforcement.",Summarized from Figure 5 discussion.,0.95
10.1038_nature09203.md,"Simulation – Voluntary PGG, peer vs pool punishment, no second-order punishment",Based on Fig. 1a and related text describing competition between peer- and pool-punishers without second-order punishment using specified parameters.,0.95,"Sanction type competition with voluntary participation and no second-order punishment; strategies allowed: contributor non-punisher (X), defector (Y), non-participant (Z), pool-punisher (V), peer-punisher (W); second-order punishment disabled.","Condition defined by presence of peer- and pool-punishers, optional participation, and explicitly stated absence of second-order punishment in Fig. 1a.",0.95,False,Study uses evolutionary game theory and individual-based numerical simulations with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely model-based and simulated.,1,False,No laboratory environment; only simulations.,1,True,"Described as individual-based simulation runs and extensive simulations with parameters N, r, c, σ, etc.",1,True,Paper states that analytical results are obtained in a limiting case and supported by simulations; this condition’s long-run frequencies are reported analytically.,0.8,5,Group size N=5 stated in Fig. 1 caption.,1,10000000,"Methods state: ‘To obtain reliable average frequencies, each player updates 10^7 times’; interpret updates as effective rounds of interaction.",0.6,1,"Each player decides whether to contribute a fixed amount c or not; this is a binary (all-or-nothing) decision, not a continuous contribution choice.",0.95,N/A,"No human subjects or initial endowment allocation; contributions are model decisions, not default allocations.",1,0.75,"Payoff rule: each contribution c is multiplied by r and distributed among N-1 others; MPCR per recipient is r/(N(N-1)). For own payoff from others’ contributions, effective MPCR per unit of own forgone payoff is (r-1). However, marginal per capita return for the public good is customarily r/N. Given N=5 and r=3, MPCR=r/N=3/5=0.6. But here contributors do not benefit from own contribution; benefit to group per unit cost is r, hence MPCR to group is r/N=0.6. Because definition ambiguous and not explicitly given, using r/N=0.6 would be standard, but instruction allows computation if clear. I choose 0.6 as standard MPCR.",0.4,N/A,No human interaction or communication; purely simulated agents.,1,0,"Model does not describe feedback or summaries; agents update by imitation based on payoffs, not on displayed summaries.",0.7,N/A,No identities in the simulation; no notion of revealed IDs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modelled.,1,0,Simulated agents do not observe round counts; infinite-horizon imitation dynamics.,0.9,1,Both peer- and pool-punishment are available strategies in this condition.,1,0.7,"For peer-punishers, cost γ=0.7 per fine imposed; for pool-punishers, contribution G=0.7 to punishment pool. Reporting the main per-action cost parameter γ.",0.8,0.7,Peer-punishers impose fine β=0.7 per target. Pool-punishment fines BNv with B=0.7. Reporting typical fine-per-unit parameter β (and B) =0.7.,0.8,0,"Model includes only punishment mechanisms, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model specifies a fixed contribution cost c=1 but not an explicit endowment size.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports long-run frequencies of strategies, not average contribution levels as fraction of endowment.",0.2,N/R,No explicit average number of contributors or contributions per round is reported for this specific condition.,0.2,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximal payoffs is given for this simulation condition.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff statistics are not numerically reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Pure agent-based and analytical modelling, as stated in Methods Summary.",1,"Optional (voluntary) public goods game with strategies: contributors without punishment (X), defectors (Y), non-participants (Z), pool-punishers (V), peer-punishers (W). Parameters: N=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1, γ=β=0.7, B=G=0.7, M=100, mutation rate μ=10^-3, strong imitation s→+∞. Second-order punishment is disabled. Random groups of size N are formed; payoffs determine imitation dynamics.",Summarized from description around Fig. 1a and methods.,0.95
10.1038_nature09203.md,"Simulation – Voluntary PGG, peer vs pool punishment, with second-order punishment",Based on Fig. 1b and text describing same setup but with second-order punishment enabled.,0.95,"Sanction type competition with voluntary participation and second-order punishment; strategies: X, Y, Z, V, W; second-order punishment enabled for both peer and pool punishment.","Condition is identical to previous except that second-order free-riders are also punished, as stated for Fig. 1b.",0.95,False,Numerical simulations only.,1,False,No empirical experimental design.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,True,Fig. 1b is an individual-based simulation run.,1,True,"Text provides analytical long-run frequencies with second-order punishment, supported by simulations.",0.8,5,N=5 as in Fig. 1 parameters.,1,10000000,Uses same simulation protocol as Fig. 2: 10^7 updates per player for stationary distribution; assumed comparable for Fig. 1.,0.6,1,Binary contribute c or not; same as in model description.,0.95,N/A,No human players or initial allocation.,1,0.6,"Same payoff structure with N=5, r=3; MPCR assumed r/N=0.6 as in previous condition.",0.4,N/A,No human players.,1,0,No display structure; imitation based on payoffs only.,0.7,N/A,No identities; abstract strategies only.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,0,No round-count information to agents.,0.9,1,Both pool- and peer-punishment are options.,1,0.7,Same parameters γ=0.7 and G=0.7 as Fig. 1 caption.,0.9,0.7,Same fine parameters β=0.7 and B=0.7 as in Fig. 1.,0.9,0,Only punishment is modelled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Only contribution cost c=1 is given; no explicit endowment.,0.3,N/R,No explicit contributions statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,Only qualitative statement that pool-punishers enforce cooperation; no numeric average contributions.,0.3,N/R,"Text mentions that average income is reduced when pool-punishers dominate, but no numeric efficiency is given.",0.3,N/R,No explicit payoff numbers are provided for this condition.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations and analytical model only.,1,"Same voluntary PGG as in no-second-order condition, but with second-order punishment: second-order free-riders (who contribute to the public good but not to punishment) are fined just like defectors. With parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1, γ=β=0.7, B=G=0.7, M=100, μ=10^-3, strong imitation. Long-run analytical frequencies show dominance of pool-punishers (0,0,0,1,0) in (X,Y,Z,V,W).",From main text description of second-order punishment case and frequencies.,0.95
