10.1088_1367-2630_12_8_083005.md,PC model – spatial public goods game with punishing cooperators,Paper explicitly defines a minimalist model where punishing cooperators (PC) are included alongside C and D on a lattice; treated as one simulation condition family.,0.95,"Punishment fine f and punishment cost c for punishing cooperators; fixed synergy factor r and noise K in most reported phase diagrams (e.g., r=3.8, K=0.5).",Phase diagrams and results are presented as functions of fine f and cost c with r and K fixed; PC strategy is present instead of PD.,0.96,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice with players as lattice sites, no human subjects.",1,False,No controlled lab/field experiment; purely computational evolutionary game simulations.,1,False,Not a lab experiment; computational simulations only.,1,True,Section 2 describes Monte Carlo simulation procedure on L×L lattice; results are called computational results.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria; focus is on numerical phase diagrams and simulations.,0.9,1600000,"Players occupy sites of an L×L lattice with L between 400 and 3000; to choose a concrete configuration, use midpoint L≈1265, so playerCount≈1265^2≈1.6×10^6, but exact L per run not fixed.",0.2,1000000,They iterate up to 10^6 iterations until fractions stabilize; treat this as number of update sweeps (rounds) in long-run simulations.,0.5,1,"Cooperators contribute fixed asset a=1 and defectors 0; there is no choice of intermediate contribution levels, so contribution decision is effectively all-or-nothing between 0 and 1, but binary strategies are modeled, not choices each round.",0.7,N/A,Not a human experiment; no initial allocation decision interface.,1,0.76,Groups of size G=5; synergy factor r=3.8 in main phase diagrams; MPCR=r/G=3.8/5=0.76.,0.95,N/A,No human subjects; no communication mechanism.,1,N/A,Simulation model does not involve an interface displaying summaries.,1,N/A,No identities or information display; just strategy labels in simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,Simulated agents do not see rounds; time is just iterations.,1,1,PC players punish defectors with fine f at personal cost c; detailed in payoff definition.,1,1,"Cost parameter c is per-unit cost for punishing defectors; each PC pays c·π_d per group; treat cost per standardized punishment action as 1·c, so punishmentCost normalized to 1 and punishmentTech holds scaling via fine f; actual c varies but unit mapping is arbitrary in model currency.",0.3,1,Fine parameter f is per defector with proportionality through π_p; mapping coin-to-fine ratio not separated; we normalize both cost and impact to same base unit since separate scaling is not defined.,0.3,0,"Model includes only costly punishment, no positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes an asset a=1 when cooperating; treat this as the per-round endowment available for contribution.,0.9,N/R,"They report fractions of strategy types (ρ_C, ρ_D, ρ_PC) but not average per-player contribution normalized explicitly; could be inferred as ρ_C+ρ_PC, but they call this cooperation level, not labeled as contribution rate, so left as N/R.",0.4,N/R,No numerical average contributions per player are tabulated; only qualitative behavior and phase compositions reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit measure of total payoff relative to maximum is reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs are not numerically summarized; only strategy fractions and phase descriptions.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational simulations without human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a periodic square lattice; each player belongs to G=5 overlapping groups (self plus 4 nearest neighbors); strategies C, D, and PC; cooperator contribution a=1, defectors contribute 0; total group contribution multiplied by synergy factor r and shared equally; payoff then reduced by punishment fines and costs as functions of local composition; strategy update via pairwise comparison with Fermi rule with noise K; initial strategies uniformly random with equal frequencies.",Summarizes key structural details described in Section 2 for the PC model.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_12_8_083005.md,PD model – spatial public goods game with punishing defectors,Paper defines a second minimalist model where punishing defectors (PD) are included instead of PC alongside C and D; treated as separate simulation condition family.,0.95,"Punishment fine f and punishment cost c for punishing defectors; fixed synergy factor r and noise K in most reported phase diagrams (e.g., r=3.8, K=0.5).",Phase diagram for PD model is given as function of fine f and cost c with r fixed; PD strategy replaces PC.,0.96,False,Model implemented via Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,"This is a computational model study, not a controlled human experiment.",1,False,No physical lab with human subjects; only simulations.,1,True,They repeatedly refer to computer simulations and Monte Carlo procedure on L×L lattice.,1,False,No purely analytical solution of dynamics; results are numerical.,0.9,1600000,Same lattice-based setup as PC model with L between 400 and 3000; use same approximate midpoint-based estimate.,0.2,1000000,Up to 10^6 iterations used until strategy fractions stabilize; treat as simulation length.,0.5,1,Players are either cooperators contributing a=1 or defectors contributing 0; no intermediate choice modeled.,0.7,N/A,No human endowment interface; simulated strategies only.,1,0.76,Same public goods structure as PC model; r=3.8 and G=5 so MPCR=r/G=0.76 in the main diagrams.,0.95,N/A,No communication among agents in the simulation.,1,N/A,No user interface; summaries not displayed.,1,N/A,Identities and information display are not part of model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe number of rounds in the simulation.,1,1,PD players punish defectors (including other PD) with fine f at personal cost c; hypocritical punishment.,1,1,"Cost parameter c is per punisher per punished defector fraction; exact coin-to-cost mapping not separated, so normalize base cost to 1 unit as in PC model.",0.3,1,"Fine parameter f acts as magnitude per punished defector weighted by share of punishers; individual punishment technology not parameterized separately, so normalized to 1.",0.3,0,Only costly punishment is included; no rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Cooperators contribute a=1; interpreted as endowment available for contribution each game.,0.9,N/R,"They show cooperation level (ρ_C) variations with punishment, but no explicit normalized contribution rate measure aside from strategy fractions.",0.4,N/R,No explicit average contribution amounts per group or player are given.,0.2,N/R,They discuss cooperation levels and phases but not efficiency ratios of total payoffs.,0.1,N/R,No numerical payoff statistics are reported.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Simulated spatial public goods game with evolutionary updates only.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a periodic square lattice; each player belongs to G=5 overlapping groups (self plus 4 nearest neighbors); strategies C, D, and PD; C and PD contribute a=1 while D contributes 0; total contribution multiplied by synergy factor r and divided equally among group members; D and PD are punished with fine f proportional to number of punishing players (PD) in group; PD players incur cost c proportional to number of defectors (D and PD) around them; strategy update via pairwise Fermi rule with uncertainty K; initial strategies C, D, PD uniformly random with equal frequencies.",Summarizes key structural details for the PD minimalist model from Sections 2 and 3.,1
