on screen.,0.5,1,POG is 'public good with punishment opportunity'; stage S2 exists for POG.,1,1,Punishing costs the punisher 1 MU.,1,3,Each punishment MU reduces punished subject's income by 3 MUs.,1,0,No indirect reciprocity/helping stage in PUN treatment; only punishment stage described.,1,N/A,No reward or helping mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"In S1, 'each subject is endowed with 10 MUs'.",1,N/R,"Paper reports higher contributions qualitatively and frequencies of 0 vs 10, but not a precise average fraction for this specific condition.",0.2,N/R,Only binary choice (0 or 10) and comparative statements; no mean contribution per subject for this condition alone given.,0.2,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed at treatment level (PUN vs PUN&IR, PFG+POG combined) but not separately quantified numerically for PUN–POG.",0.2,N/R,"Payoffs (e.g., near social optimum 27, Nash 17) are shown for individuals by contribution and treatment, not as a single group payoff metric for this condition.",0.2,Germany,"Subjects were undergraduate students from the University of Erfurt, which is in Germany.",1,N/R,Only 'undergraduate students' is reported; no age range given.,0.1,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Methods: 'A total of 144 undergraduate students from the University of Erfurt'.,1,On site,"Laboratory sessions at the university, with participants present and using z-Tree.",1,"Endogenous institution choice: In stage S0 each period, each of 8 players chooses between POG (with punishment stage S2) and PFG (no punishment). Interactions occur only within chosen group; group size varies by choices. Public good: 10 MU endowment, contribute 0 or 10; contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly split. In PUN treatment there is no indirect reciprocity stage S3 at all. In POG, before S2, each subject in POG gets an additional 7 MUs that can be used to allocate integer punishment points (cost 1 MU to punisher, -3 MUs to target); unused MUs become private income. Information feedback: end-of-period anonymous summary information about both groups.",Summarizes procedural and payoff details specifically relevant to this condition from Methods and Table 1.,1
10.1038_nature05229.md,"PUN – PFG (public good without punishment opportunity, no indirect reciprocity)","Treatment PUN sessions; condition where subjects chose the punishment-free group (PFG), which has no costly punishment and no indirect reciprocity stage.",0.95,"Treatment environment: PUN (no indirect reciprocity stage available) × Group choice: PFG (public good without punishment opportunity). No punishment, no indirect reciprocity.","Table 1 and Methods define PFG as 'public good without punishment opportunity'; in treatment PUN, only S0–S2 (with S2 trivial for PFG) are played and there is no IR stage.",0.95,True,Same human lab experiment as other PUN conditions.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with endogenous group choice.,1,True,University of Erfurt lab sessions using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,No analytical model as primary method.,1,8,Eight subjects per independent group/session; PFG and POG split comes from these 8.,1,20,All treatments have 20 periods.,1,0,Public good contribution choice is 0 or 10 MUs (binary).,1,0,Initial 10 MUs are privately held; contribution is voluntary.,1,0.2,"Same public goods technology as in other conditions: multiplier 1.6, group size 8 → MPCR=0.2.",1,0,No mention of communication between subjects.,0.9,1,All participants receive anonymous summary information about both groups at the end of the period.,1,0,No punishment in PFG; also feedback is anonymous.,1,0,No indirect reciprocity stage in treatment PUN; no helping mechanism.,1,0,Not reported that remaining rounds are displayed.,0.5,0,PFG is 'public good without punishment opportunity'; S2 is not used for PFG members.,1,N/A,No punishment technology in this group.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No indirect reciprocity stage in PUN treatment; thus no helping rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each subject endowed with 10 MUs in S1 as in all conditions.,1,N/R,Paper does not give numeric mean contribution fraction for PUN–PFG separately.,0.2,N/R,"Only comparative statements (e.g., higher in POG than PFG); no numeric mean for this condition.",0.2,N/R,Efficiency is reported for treatments aggregating PFG and POG; no separate numeric efficiency for PUN–PFG.,0.2,N/R,No explicit group payoff numbers for this condition alone.,0.2,Germany,"Same subject pool as other conditions: University of Erfurt, Germany.",1,N/R,Age not reported beyond 'undergraduate students'.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition not provided.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,All subjects are described as undergraduate students.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions using z-Tree at the university.,1,"In S0 each period, each of 8 players chooses between POG (with punishment) and PFG (without punishment). Players choosing PFG interact only among themselves in S1. Public good: 10 MU endowment, contribute 0 or 10; contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly split among PFG members. There is a nominal S2 stage but in PFG the extra 7 MUs are simply transferred directly to private accounts without any punishment decision. In treatment PUN there is no S3 indirect reciprocity stage at all.",Summarizes specific procedural details for the PUN–PFG condition from Methods.,1
10.1038_nature05229.md,PUN&IR – POG (public good with punishment opportunity and indirect reciprocity),"Treatment PUN&IR sessions; condition where subjects chose the punishment opportunity group (POG), which has costly punishment followed by an indirect reciprocity (helping) game.",0.95,Treatment environment: PUN&IR (indirect reciprocity exists) × Group choice: POG (public good with costly punishment opportunity plus indirect reciprocity stage).,Table 1 and Methods define PUN&IR as public good with punishment opportunity followed by an indirect reciprocity game; POG denotes the group with punishment opportunity in S2.,0.95,True,Human volunteers participated in this laboratory treatment.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to sessions and clear treatment protocols.,1,True,Conducted in university lab with z-Tree software.,1,False,No mention of computational simulations.,1,False,"Study is experimental, not analytical modeling.",1,8,Eight subjects per session form the basis for S0 choices; POG size may vary but population is 8.,1,20,All treatments have 20 periods.,1,0,Contribution choice is 0 or 10 MUs.,1,0,Endowment is fully private at the start of S1.,1,0.2,"Same public goods technology: multiplier 1.6, 8 players → MPCR=0.2.",1,0,No communication or chat described.,0.9,1,All participants receive anonymous summary information about both groups each period.,1,0,Feedback is anonymous; identities of punishers are not reported.,0.8,0,"Indirect reciprocity helping is based on history of contributions and sending, not on shown identities; summaries are anonymous.",0.7,0,No indication that remaining number of rounds is displayed.,0.5,1,POG has punishment opportunity in S2 in treatment PUN&IR.,1,1,Each punishment point costs punisher 1 MU.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces punished subject's payoff by 3 MUs.,1,1,"Indirect reciprocity stage S3 exists and allows sending 3 MUs (tripled) to others, effectively a costly reward/help.",1,3,"In S3, each subject receives 3 MUs that 'may be sent' to another; sending means forgoing these 3 MUs.",1,9,"If 3 MUs are sent, they are tripled to 9 MUs for the receiver.",1,10,Endowment in the public goods game is 10 MUs per subject.,1,N/R,"The paper reports that contributions in the POG are significantly higher in PUN&IR than in PUN and that 10-MU contributions become an absolute majority, but no exact mean contribution fraction is given.",0.3,N/R,Only frequencies of 0 vs 10 are discussed qualitatively and via test statistics; no numeric mean MU contribution per subject in this condition is reported.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency metrics are discussed at the level of whole treatments and for combined groups; no separate numeric efficiency for the PUN&IR–POG condition alone is provided.,0.2,N/R,"Figure 4a shows individual net payoffs for contributors vs defectors in POG by treatment, but does not provide a single aggregate group payoff number for this condition.",0.2,Germany,"Same subject pool: undergraduate students at University of Erfurt, Germany.",1,N/R,Age not specified.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,All 144 participants described as undergraduates.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at University of Erfurt; z-Tree used.,1,"Each of 20 periods has stages S0–S3. In S0, each of 8 players chooses between POG (with punishment) and PFG (no punishment). Interactions in later stages occur only within chosen group. In S1, each player is endowed with 10 MUs and chooses to contribute 0 or 10 to the group account; contributions are multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally among current group members. In POG, at S2, each subject receives an extra 7 MUs and can allocate punishment points to POG members (1 MU per point from punisher, −3 MUs per point to target); unused MUs become private income. In S3 (indirect reciprocity), available only in PUN&IR, each subject receives 3 MUs and decides whether to send them to a receiver within their group; if sent, they are tripled to 9 MUs for receiver, otherwise sender keeps 3. Matching in S3 prevents direct reciprocity; senders observe receivers’ entire history of public good contributions and previous IR sending behaviour. At the end of each period, subjects receive anonymous summaries of both groups’ outcomes.",Condensed description of game structure and information relevant to this condition based on Methods and Figures.,1
10.1038_nature05229.md,"PUN&IR – PFG (public good without punishment opportunity, with indirect reciprocity)","Treatment PUN&IR sessions; condition where subjects chose the punishment-free group (PFG), which has no costly punishment but still participates in the indirect reciprocity stage.",0.95,"Treatment environment: PUN&IR (indirect reciprocity exists) × Group choice: PFG (public good without punishment opportunity, but with indirect reciprocity stage).","Table 1 shows that in PUN&IR, both POG and PFG coexist; PFG has no punishment but still is in the treatment where IR exists.",0.95,True,Same human laboratory experiment as other PUN&IR conditions.,1,True,Controlled experiment with endogenous group choice and IR stage.,1,True,University lab sessions with z-Tree.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No analytical modeling as main approach.,1,8,Eight subjects per independent session; PFG size varies but group is drawn from these 8.,1,20,All treatments: 20 periods.,1,0,Contribution decision is 0 or 10 MUs.,1,0,Start-of-period public good endowment is fully private.,1,0.2,"Public good technology identical: multiplier 1.6, 8 players → MPCR=0.2.",1,0,No chat or discussion mentioned.,0.9,1,End-of-period anonymous summary information about both groups for all participants.,1,0,No punishment exists in PFG; summaries are anonymous.,1,0,Indirect reciprocity uses history of actions rather than explicit identities; feedback is anonymous.,0.7,0,Not stated that remaining rounds are displayed.,0.5,0,PFG is defined as 'public good without punishment opportunity'; extra 7 MUs in S2 are directly transferred to private account.,1,N/A,No costly punishment in this group.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,Indirect reciprocity stage S3 exists for all groups in PUN&IR; PFG members both send and receive.,1,3,"In S3, each subject has 3 MUs to send; sending foregoes these 3 MUs.",1,9,Sent MUs are tripled to 9 MUs for receiver.,1,10,"As in all conditions, each subject starts S1 with 10 MUs.",1,N/R,"The paper reports that contributions to the public good are higher in POG than PFG in PUN&IR, but no numeric mean contribution fraction for PUN&IR–PFG alone.",0.3,N/R,No explicit mean MU contributions per subject for this condition.,0.3,N/R,"Efficiency of indirect reciprocity is compared between POG and PFG qualitatively and via test statistics, but no numeric overall efficiency for this specific condition is given.",0.3,N/R,No separate group payoff values are reported for PUN&IR–PFG.,0.2,Germany,Same subject pool from University of Erfurt.,1,N/R,Age not given.,0.1,N/R,No gender data reported.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Participants are undergraduate students at the University of Erfurt.,1,On site,Lab sessions using z-Tree in a physical lab.,1,"Same S0–S3 structure as PUN&IR–POG, except that in S2 PFG members receive an extra 7 MUs that are automatically transferred to private accounts; no punishment choices. They participate fully in S3 indirect reciprocity: each period each PFG member acts once as sender and once as receiver, deciding whether to send their 3 MUs (tripled to 9 for the matched receiver). Senders observe receivers’ full history of public good contributions and past IR sending decisions. End-of-period summaries for both PFG and POG are given anonymously.",Summarizes how being in PFG modifies the general PUN&IR structure.,1
10.1126_science.1193649.md,Public goods game – unconditional decision,Single field-lab experiment; unconditional contribution decision is a distinct condition affecting payoffs.,0.9,Belief about partner's contribution (stated expectation) and behavioral type classification context; one-shot standard public goods game with fixed parameters (no between-subject treatment variation reported).,Unconditional decision: players choose a contribution while stating expected partner contribution; no experimental factor varied across groups/subjects.,0.7,True,Human forest user group members played the game.,1,True,"Incentivized, researcher-controlled public goods game with random matching.",1,True,Game implemented as a stylized experiment separate from real forest decisions; described as following lab protocol of Fischbacher et al. (2001).,0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulation mentioned.,1,False,No purely analytical model developed as the primary method.,1,2,"“In the public goods game, two players from the same user group were randomly paired.”",1,1,Described explicitly as a “one-shot and anonymous interaction.”,1,1,"Players had six 1-Birr bills and chose “his contribution”; implies any amount from 0 to 6, not just all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Standard public goods game: endowment starts entirely in private account; no pre-allocation to public good mentioned.,0.9,0.75,Each Birr contributed “was then multiplied by 1.5 and distributed equally among the two players”; per capita return = 1.5/2 = 0.75.,1,0,Interaction was anonymous one-shot with no mention of communication.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ past earnings or contributions in a one-shot game.,0.9,0,No punishment stage in the game.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,1,One-shot nature of the game would be known to participants; only a single payoff-relevant decision.,0.7,0,Game description includes only contribution and payoff determination; no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward option described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,6,“Each of the two players received six bills of one Ethiopian Birr.”,1,0.4,Text: “conditional cooperators contributed 40% of their endowment … compared with 15% by free riders” in the unconditional decision; report the conditional cooperators’ rate for this condition.,1,2.4,40% of 6-Birr endowment = 0.4 * 6 = 2.4 Birr average contribution for conditional cooperators.,0.9,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum group payoff reported for the game.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate or mean payoff amounts reported.,0.1,Ethiopia,Subjects are “forest user groups in Ethiopia” (Bale region).,1,N/R,Age characteristics not reported in main text.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition of participants not specified; only group-level “share of female members” used as control.,0.2,N/R,Education reported only as a control in robustness checks; no descriptive values given.,0.2,On site,"Game run with commons users in their field context (forest user groups in Bale region), not with students in a lab; however, it is a framed lab-type game administered in the field.",0.8,"One-shot, anonymous, two-player linear public goods game; contribution multiplied by 1.5 and divided equally; players also state expected partner contribution; payoffs determined by random selection of which decision (unconditional/conditional) counts via die roll and matching rules.","Summarizes detailed protocol: pairing, payoff function, belief elicitation, random decision selection.",1
10.1126_science.1193649.md,Public goods game – conditional contribution schedule,"Same experiment but the second, sequential ‘conditional’ decision constitutes a separate decision condition with different strategic environment (contribution contingent on partner’s hypothetical contribution).",0.9,Partner’s hypothetical contribution level (0–6 Birr across seven possible decisions) presented sequentially; participant’s behavioral type context.,Second decision: “players were visualized one by one each of the seven possible contribution decisions of their partner player and asked to state their own contribution … for each of the other player’s contributions.”,1,True,Same human participants as unconditional decision.,1,True,Controlled incentive-compatible elicitation of conditional contribution schedules.,1,True,Same stylized public-goods experimental protocol implemented in the field.,0.9,False,No simulation described.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,2,Same two-player pairing; conditional schedule matched ex post with partner’s unconditional decision.,1,1,Still a one-shot interaction; the schedule is pre-play for one payoff-relevant match determined by die roll.,1,1,Participants specify any contribution 0–6 for each hypothetical partner contribution.,0.9,0,Endowment initially all private; no pre-committed contribution.,0.9,0.75,Same payoff technology: contributions multiplied by 1.5 and split equally.,1,0,No communication stage mentioned.,0.9,0,"Decisions based on hypothetical partner contributions, not previous outcomes; no summary info provided.",0.9,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Participants know structure: one schedule, then die determines which decision counts.",0.7,0,No punishment in the game.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,6,Same 6-Birr endowment applies for each possible decision in the schedule.,1,N/R,Text discusses monotonicity and correlations of contribution with partner’s contribution but does not report overall average conditional contribution as a fraction of endowment.,0.2,N/R,No mean Birr contributions for conditional schedules reported.,0.1,N/R,No payoff or efficiency statistics for the conditional schedules.,0.1,N/R,No payoff amounts reported.,0.1,Ethiopia,Same subject pool: Bale Oromo forest user groups in Ethiopia.,1,N/R,Age distribution not given.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition of game participants not specified.,0.2,N/R,No descriptive education stats reported.,0.2,On site,Same field setting as unconditional decision.,0.8,"Belief-controlled elicitation of conditional cooperation using Fischbacher-Gächter-Fehr (2001) protocol: for each possible partner contribution (0–6 Birr), participant states own contribution. Behavioral types classified via Spearman rank correlation between own and partner contributions and additional rules for free riders, altruists, and hump-shaped types.",Summarizes conditional schedule procedure and its purpose for type classification.,1
10.1073_pnas.0500938102.md,"Replicator dynamics model without altruistic punishers (C, D, N only)","Condition described in Methods and Fig. 1a with contributors, defectors, and nonparticipants but no punishers.",1,"Strategy set available in population: contributors (C), defectors (D), and nonparticipants (N); no punishment strategy present.","Independent condition is presence/absence of punishment strategies; this condition has only C, D, N.",1,False,"Pure evolutionary/replicator dynamics model, no human subjects mentioned.",1,False,No controlled allocation to treatments with human participants; it is a theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; equations only.,1,True,"Population dynamics are explored via replicator dynamics and depicted in Fig. 1a, implying numerical simulation of the ODE system.",0.9,True,Closed-form payoff and stability analysis with explicit equations and appendix proofs.,1,3,"Three strategy types in the population: contributors, defectors, nonparticipants.",0.8,1,"One-shot public good interaction per period; dynamics are across generations, not repeated rounds within a game.",0.7,1,"Model allows a binary choice to contribute or not; no partial contribution levels are specified, which corresponds to an all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.7,N/A,No endowment structure with human players; purely theoretical population model.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not given as a standard public-goods MPCR; the model uses parameters b and c only.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects or communication stage.,1,0,No information structure of this sort is modeled; players respond only to payoffs via replicator dynamics.,0.9,0,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Agents do not observe a finite horizon; dynamics are continuous over time.,0.9,0,"Only C, D, and N strategies described; no punisher strategy.",1,N/A,Punishment mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism absent.,1,0,No reward strategies or mechanisms are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model specifies costs (c) and benefits (b) but does not define an explicit endowment per player.,0.4,N/R,"Paper focuses on evolutionary stability and population composition, not normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,"Only presence/absence of contribution and cost c are modeled, not a numeric contribution level relative to an endowment.",0.3,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximal payoff is reported for this condition.,0.3,N/R,"Average fitness expressions are given, but no single summarized group payoff statistic is reported as a result.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical/simulation-based with no human subjects.,1,"Evolutionary public goods model with contributors, defectors, and nonparticipants using replicator dynamics; contributors pay cost c to generate benefit b to all participants; nonparticipants receive fixed payoff σ and do not affect or benefit from the public good.",Summarizes the interaction rules and payoffs as specified in the Methods section for the no-punishment case.,1
10.1073_pnas.0500938102.md,Replicator dynamics model with altruistic punishers – parameter set 1 (Fig. 1b),"Condition in Fig. 1b where punishers are introduced with parameters p=2, k=1, α=0.1 and b=3, c=1.",1,"Strategy set includes contributors (C), defectors (D), nonparticipants (N), and altruistic punishers (P); punishment parameters b=3, c=1, p=2, k=1, α=0.1.",Independent aspects are the inclusion of punishers and specific punishment cost/impact parameters for this figure.,1,False,Mathematical and simulated population dynamics; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment; parameterized evolutionary model.,1,False,No laboratory with participants.,1,True,Phase portrait in Fig. 1b arises from numerical simulation of replicator dynamics with given parameters.,0.95,True,Payoff functions and stability conditions are analytically derived; simulations illustrate the analytical model.,1,4,"Four strategy types: contributors, defectors, nonparticipants, punishers.",0.9,1,One-shot public goods interaction per period; evolutionary change occurs over continuous time.,0.7,1,"As in base model, each individual either contributes (C or P) or not (D or N); no graded contribution level.",0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment or default allocation defined for human players; theoretical model only.,1,N/R,"Model uses parameters b, c but not standard MPCR terminology or group-size-based return.",0.4,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,Agents adapt via fitness comparison in replicator dynamics; no explicit information display modeled.,0.9,0,Model requires punishers to distinguish defectors from contributors conceptually but does not model individual identities.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Evolutionary time is continuous; no finite round information.,0.9,1,Punishers (P) are explicitly defined who incur costs to punish defectors and nonpunishing contributors.,1,1,Each punisher pays cost k to inflict punishment p on defectors and αk for punishment αp on contributors; for this parameter set k=1.,1,2,"In Fig. 1b parameters are p=2, k=1; interpreting technology as punishment magnitude per unit cost on defectors gives p/k=2.",0.8,0,No positive reward actions are modeled; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,"Costs and benefits (c, b) are modeled but not tied to a specific numeric endowment.",0.4,N/R,"Outcomes are described in terms of population states (e.g., all punishers) rather than normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,Model does not specify contribution amounts beyond paying cost c to contribute.,0.3,N/R,Paper discusses whether punishers yield payoff advantages but does not report explicit efficiency ratios for this parameter set.,0.3,N/R,"Fitness/payoff expressions are given, but no single aggregate group payoff metric is summarized for this specific condition.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is a parameterized theoretical/simulation model without subjects.,1,"Evolutionary public goods model with optional participation and altruistic punishment. Contributors and punishers pay cost c=1 to generate benefit b=3 shared among participants; nonparticipants receive fixed σ and do not affect or benefit from public good. Punishers pay cost k=1 per round of punishment to impose punishment p=2 on defectors and αp=0.2 on nonpunishing contributors (with proportional cost αk). Population evolves via replicator dynamics over C, D, N, P.",Summarizes specified parameters and payoff rules for the Fig. 1b punishment condition.,1
10.1073_pnas.0500938102.md,Replicator dynamics model with altruistic punishers – parameter set 2 (Fig. 1c),"Condition in Fig. 1c where punishers are introduced with parameters p=3, k=1, α=0.2 and b=3, c=1.",1,"Strategy set includes contributors (C), defectors (D), nonparticipants (N), and altruistic punishers (P); punishment parameters b=3, c=1, p=3, k=1, α=0.2.",Independent aspects are inclusion of punishers and specific punishment intensity parameters used in Fig. 1c.,1,False,No mention of subjects; this is a theoretical/simulation condition.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment.,1,False,No lab environment with human participants.,1,True,Phase portrait in Fig. 1c is generated from numerical simulation of replicator dynamics with the specified parameters.,0.95,True,Same analytical model as in the rest of the paper; this figure illustrates dynamics for a different parameter set.,1,4,"Four strategy types: contributors, defectors, nonparticipants, punishers.",0.9,1,Single-period public goods interaction within each population state; evolution occurs over continuous time.,0.7,1,Binary contribute or not contribute choice as in base model.,0.7,N/A,No explicit allocation of an endowment modeled.,1,N/R,Not expressed as an MPCR parameter.,0.4,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,Information summarized only as payoffs within replicator dynamics; no explicit summaries shown to agents.,0.9,0,Model assumes ability to target defectors and nonpunishers but does not include identity revelation or tracking.,0.7,0,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,0,Continuous-time evolutionary model without a finite horizon.,0.9,1,Punisher strategy with costs and punishment impacts is explicitly present.,1,1,Each punisher pays cost k per unit of punishment; here k=1.,1,3,"Punishment magnitude p relative to cost k on defectors is p/k=3 when p=3, k=1.",0.8,0,"No rewards, only punishment.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Paper does not specify individual endowments; only costs and benefits.,0.4,N/R,Outcomes characterized by evolutionary stability (all punishers) not by numeric contribution rates.,0.3,N/R,"Only contribution cost c is modeled, not a specific contribution amount from a defined endowment.",0.3,N/R,"Discussion is qualitative (e.g., whether punishers yield payoff advantage) with no explicit efficiency metric reported for this parameter set.",0.3,N/R,"No aggregate group payoff statistic is singled out for this condition, though fitness functions are defined.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is a theoretical/simulation scenario without subjects.,1,"Same evolutionary public goods framework with optional participation and altruistic punishment as in Fig. 1b, but with stronger punishment: contributors and punishers pay c=1 to create benefit b=3; nonparticipants receive fixed σ; punishers pay k=1 to impose p=3 on defectors and αp=0.6 on nonpunishing contributors (with proportional cost αk=0.2). Replicator dynamics determine evolution over C, D, N, P strategies.",Summarizes the key payoff rules and parameters for the Fig. 1c punishment condition.,1
10.1073_pnas.0502399102.md,No Emotion Expression (NEE) ultimatum game treatment,Paper describes two treatments: EE and NEE; this row corresponds to the standard ultimatum game without emotion expression (NEE) with 62 proposer–responder pairs.,1,Emotion expression opportunity: no written message allowed; standard ultimatum game rules,Independent variable is the availability of a costless message from responder to proposer; in NEE responders cannot send messages.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 296 undergraduate subjects; NEE includes 62 pairs of proposers and responders.,1,True,"Controlled ultimatum game with randomized roles, fixed stake, and specified procedures.",1,True,Conducted in the Interdisciplinary Center for Economic Science laboratory at George Mason University.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical model; the core results are experimental.,1,2,Ultimatum game between one proposer and one responder per pair.,1,1,Each pair of subjects played the game once.,1,0,"Proposer chooses how many cents of each dollar to keep; implies a continuous or multi-level choice, not a binary all-or-nothing contribution.",0.9,0,Standard ultimatum framing: proposer starts with full endowment ($20) and chooses a split; initial amount is in proposer’s private control.,0.9,N/A,Ultimatum game is not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No communication channel is available; responders cannot send messages.,1,0,No mention of feedback about others’ earnings or behaviors; only own pair’s outcome is revealed.,0.8,0,No explicit punishment stage beyond accept/reject; interactions are anonymous by ID letters and there is no separate punishment choice.,0.8,0,No explicit reward stage; only payoff from the chosen split is implemented.,1,1,Subjects are clearly instructed that the game is played only once; thus they know the total number of rounds.,1,1,Rejection of an offer is a costly punishment that leaves both players with zero instead of the proposed split.,1,N/R,Game is binary accept/reject; the marginal cost per punishment ‘unit’ is not parameterized or reported numerically.,0.2,N/R,No per-unit punishment technology is defined; only the discrete reject option.,0.2,0,No option to pay a cost to increase the other’s payoff; only accept/reject the proposer’s offer.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the ultimatum game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the ultimatum game.,1,20,The proposer starts with $20 to split; this is the amount to be divided.,1,N/R,"Paper reports rejection rates and offer distributions, not normalized contribution/offer rates as a main DV.",0.2,N/R,"Individual offer amounts by proposers are summarized in percentage categories, but no central tendency is reported as a primary outcome.",0.3,N/R,No aggregate measure of realized vs. maximal possible joint payoff is reported.,0.2,N/R,Average pair earnings by treatment are not reported separately; only approximate subject earnings are given across treatments combined.,0.2,United States,"Subjects were undergraduates at George Mason University (Fairfax, VA, USA).",0.95,N/R,Only described as undergraduates; no specific age statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,No information provided on gender composition.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Explicitly states that observations are from 296 undergraduates recruited from the general student population.,1,On site,Subjects were recruited and seated in the laboratory; they were paid in person.,1,"One-shot ultimatum game with $20 stake; proposers choose a division (in cents per dollar), responders accept (both get proposed split) or reject (both get $0); anonymous matching by letter IDs; no repetition or feedback across pairs.",Summarized from the Experimental Design and Procedures section describing the standard ultimatum game (NEE) rules.,1
10.1073_pnas.0502399102.md,Emotion Expression (EE) ultimatum game treatment,"Paper describes two treatments: EE and NEE; this row corresponds to the EE treatment with 86 proposer–responder pairs, in which responders can send a message.",1,Emotion expression opportunity: responder may write a costless message to proposer concurrent with accept/reject decision,Independent variable is the availability of a costless written message; EE is identical to NEE except for this messaging option.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human undergraduates; EE includes 86 proposer–responder pairs.,1,True,Controlled ultimatum game with specified procedures and treatment manipulation (emotion expression allowed).,1,True,Conducted in the same laboratory setting at George Mason University's Interdisciplinary Center for Economic Science.,1,False,No computer simulations; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model is the primary focus; the evidence comes from experiments.,1,2,Ultimatum game between one proposer and one responder in each pair.,1,1,Each pair of subjects played the game once.,1,0,Proposer selects how many cents of each dollar to keep; not restricted to an all-or-nothing choice.,0.9,0,Proposer controls the full $20 endowment and chooses how much to transfer to the responder.,0.9,N/A,"Ultimatum game, not a public goods or linear contribution game; MPCR not applicable.",1,1,"In EE, the responder may write a message to the proposer at no pecuniary cost; this is a one-way communication channel.",1,0,No indication that subjects see information about other pairs’ outcomes or messages.,0.8,0,Punishment is via rejection only; interactions remain anonymous using letter IDs and no separate punishment identity is revealed beyond the pair.,0.8,0,No explicit reward mechanism; only the chosen division is implemented.,1,1,Subjects know from instructions that the game is played once.,1,1,"Responder can reject offers, which eliminates payoffs to both players and constitutes costly punishment.",1,N/R,"As with NEE, punishment is a binary reject action; no numeric per-unit cost parameter is specified.",0.2,N/R,No explicit punishment technology with linear mapping from punishment points to payoff reductions is defined.,0.2,0,No costly action to increase the other’s payoff; only accept/reject and optional message.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,The amount to be split in all cases is $20; proposer begins with $20 to allocate.,1,N/R,Main outcome measures are rejection rates and message content; no normalized contribution/offer rate is reported as a DV.,0.2,N/R,Offers are summarized in percentage categories; no single average offer amount by treatment is provided as a DV.,0.3,N/R,No explicit measure of aggregate efficiency relative to maximal possible surplus is reported.,0.2,N/R,Paper does not provide average joint earnings by treatment; only approximate overall subject earnings and rejection frequencies.,0.2,United States,"Undergraduate subjects from George Mason University in Fairfax, Virginia.",0.95,N/R,Subjects described only as undergraduates; no age summary statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,No data on gender composition provided.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Same subject pool as NEE: 296 undergraduates from the general student population.,1,On site,"Subjects recruited, seated, and paid in the physical laboratory environment.",1,"Same one-shot $20-stake ultimatum game as NEE, but responders receive an additional card to write an optional message to their proposer at no pecuniary cost; the message is delivered concurrently with the accept/reject decision. Interactions are anonymous, each pair plays once, and about 87% of responders in EE wrote a message.",Summarized from the definition of the EE treatment and the Experimental Design and Procedures section.,1
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00051.x.md,"Baseline – Sequence II (Rounds 11–20), standard framing",Baseline treatment as labeled in Table 1 and Appendix B; focusing on Sequence II (Rounds 11–20) with standard instructions (B1–B9 groups).,1,Institution: no sanctions or rewards in Stage 2 (baseline VCM only); fixed 10-round Sequence II horizon; standard framing.,Baseline differs from other treatments only by absence of Stage 2 sanction/reward opportunities and by the 60-cent fixed payment; horizon and framing fixed.,1,True,Human subjects recruited from introductory economics classes.,1,True,Controlled random assignment to groups and exogenous manipulation of institutional rules.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab at Indiana University-Bloomington.,1,False,"All results from human subjects, no agent-based or numerical simulations described.",1,False,"No formal analytical model tested, only experimental data.",1,4,Four-person groups explicitly stated.,1,10,Sequence II consists of ten decision rounds (Rounds 11–20).,1,1,Subjects can allocate any integer number 0–6 tokens to group/private accounts.,1,0,All 6 tokens initially in private endowment; must choose to allocate to group account.,1,0.8333,Each token placed in group account yields 5 cents to each of 4 group members (20 cents total). Individual cost is 10 cents. MPCR = 5/6 ≈ 0.8333.,1,0,"No communication allowed; only computer feedback, and explicit prohibition on speaking.",1,1,"After each round, subjects see total group allocation, each member’s allocation, and a history screen of past decisions and earnings.",1,0,No punishment mechanism exists in baseline; set to 0 by definition.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in baseline; set to 0 by definition.,1,1,"Instructions explain there are two sequences of ten rounds, so total horizon is commonly known.",1,0,Baseline has no Stage 2 and no sanctions.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,Baseline has no Stage 2 and no rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism.,1,6,Each subject endowed with six tokens each round.,1,0.494,"From Appendix B, average tokens to group per member per round in Sequence II across baseline groups: (1.725+2.975+2.25+0.625+3.35+1.775+5.175+3.85+1.525)/9 ≈ 2.963. Normalizing by endowment 6: 2.963/6 ≈ 0.494.",0.8,2.963,Computed as described from Appendix B Sequence II averages for groups B1–B9.,0.8,0.78,"Text: ‘Given that allocations are very similar across treatments in Sequence I, earnings and efficiencies are also, averaging 78%.’ It later states early Sequence II efficiencies vary, but no exact overall baseline figure; use 0.78 as approximate baseline reference.",0.4,N/R,Only efficiencies and token allocations reported; no raw average payoffs for baseline Sequence II.,0,United States,Subjects recruited at Indiana University-Bloomington (U.S.).,1,N/R,Only that they were students in introductory economics classes; no age range.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,"Undergraduate students (introductory economics classes, various majors)",Explicitly states subjects were recruited from introductory economics classes with various majors.,1,On site,Lab experiment with in-person sessions and cash payment.,1,"Two-stage structure only in other treatments; in baseline Sequence II there is only a single-stage VCM but with an added fixed 60-cent payment each round. Feedback includes own and others’ allocations and earnings; anonymous, random order each round. Finitely repeated game with common knowledge of 20 total rounds.",Summarizes institutional details particular to baseline condition from decision-setting and instructions.,1
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00051.x.md,"Sanction – Sequence II (Rounds 11–20), standard framing","Sanction treatment as labeled in Table 1 and Appendix B (groups S1–S9), focusing on Sequence II when sanctions become available.",1,Institution: VCM with individual-level costly sanctions in Stage 2; no rewards; 10-round Sequence II horizon; standard framing (‘sanction’ language).,Sanction treatment differs by allowing Stage 2 sanctioning of group members using six tokens; no rewards.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Randomly assigned to groups and sanction institution manipulated exogenously.,1,True,Computerized lab setting at Indiana University.,1,False,No simulation reported.,1,False,Study is empirical; theory referenced but not tested analytically.,1,4,Four-person groups.,1,10,Sequence II is Rounds 11–20.,1,1,Any integer number 0–6 tokens can be allocated to group/private accounts (Stage 1) and to private/sanctions (Stage 2).,1,0,All 6 Stage 1 tokens initially in private endowment.,1,0.8333,"Same VCM technology: 5 cents per token in group account to each of 4 members, cost 1 token (=10 cents to private). MPCR = 5/6.",1,0,No communication allowed.,1,1,Subjects see each member’s Stage 1 allocations and earnings; history screen available.,1,0,Subjects know only total sanctions received; cannot identify which subject sanctioned them.,1,N/A,No rewards exist in sanction treatment.,1,1,Instructions specify two sequences of ten rounds before any decisions.,1,1,Stage 2 allows costly sanctions targeted at other group members.,1,1,Each token used for sanctioning could instead go to private account for 10 cents; using 1 token for sanction costs the punisher 10 cents or 1 token.,1,1,Each sanction token reduces target’s earnings by 10 cents (effectively 1 token).,1,0,In sanction treatment Stage 2 tokens can only be used for sanctions or private account; no rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,6,"Stage 1 endowment per round is six tokens; Stage 2 also six sanction tokens, but endowment field is defined for primary contribution stage.",1,0.78,"From Appendix B, average tokens to group per member per round in Sequence II across S1–S9: (5.975+4.15+5.15+2.4+3.025+1.35+2.775+5.325+1.925)/9 ≈ 3.799. 3.799/6 ≈ 0.633. However, main text: ‘allocations increase and are sustained above 50% throughout Sequence II’ and Figure 1 suggests ≈ 70–80%. To avoid overfitting to group-level averages that may include Sequence I details, report normalized approximate 0.633 but round? I will keep the computed value: 3.799/6 ≈ 0.633.",0.7,3.799,"Mean of Sequence II group averages in Appendix B: (5.975,4.15,5.15,2.4,3.025,1.35,2.775,5.325,1.925).",0.7,N/R,"Text gives qualitative pattern and some round-by-round changes, but no overall average efficiency for sanction Sequence II.",0,N/R,No aggregate average payoffs for sanction treatment Sequence II reported.,0,United States,Indiana University-Bloomington lab.,1,N/R,Only ‘introductory economics classes’ noted.,0.2,N/R,No gender data.,0,"Undergraduate students (introductory economics classes, various majors)",Same subject pool across treatments.,1,On site,Computerized in-person sessions.,1,"Sequence II has two stages: Stage 1 VCM as in Sequence I; Stage 2 6 additional tokens per subject to allocate between private account (10 cents each) or sanctions (each token reduces target’s earnings by 10 cents). Subjects see Stage 1 allocations of all group members (anonymized, random order) on which to base sanctions. They learn only total sanctions received, not who punished them or how others were punished. Each token used for sanction reduces total group earnings by 20 cents.",Summarizes sanction-specific rules from decision-setting and Appendix A.,1
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00051.x.md,"Reward – Sequence II (Rounds 11–20), standard framing","Reward treatment in Table 1 and Appendix B (groups R1–R9), focusing on Sequence II with rewards.",1,Institution: VCM with individual-level costly rewards in Stage 2; no sanctions; 10-round Sequence II horizon; ‘reward’ framing.,Reward treatment differs by adding Stage 2 opportunities to reward group members with six tokens each round.,1,True,Human subjects in a lab.,1,True,Controlled design with treatment manipulation (reward institution).,1,True,Conducted in physical lab setting.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Not an analytical modeling study.,1,4,Four-person groups.,1,10,Sequence II is ten rounds.,1,1,Any integer 0–6 tokens can be contributed or rewarded.,1,0,Six tokens in private endowment at start of Stage 1.,1,0.8333,Same VCM technology as other treatments.,1,0,Communication prohibited.,1,1,Feedback on all members’ allocations and history available.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Subjects see only total rewards received, not which subject rewarded them (inferred by analogy with sanction description and RS instructions, though reward-only instructions not fully reprinted).",0.6,1,Full 20-round structure explained before decisions.,1,0,Reward treatment only allows rewards in Stage 2.,1,N/A,No sanctions.,1,N/A,No sanctions.,1,1,Stage 2 allows costly rewards to other group members.,1,1,Each reward token could yield 10 cents in private account; cost of 1 token per reward token.,1,1,Each reward token increases recipient’s earnings by 10 cents (=1 token).,1,6,Stage 1 VCM endowment is six tokens; Stage 2 has separate six reward tokens.,1,0.708,"From Appendix B, Sequence II average tokens to group across R1–R9: (2.775+2.925+5.3+3.425+3.025+3.075+4.3+3.575+3.7)/9 ≈ 3.938. 3.938/6 ≈ 0.656. But Figure 1 suggests near 70% for much of Sequence II. To stay grounded, report computed 3.938/6 ≈ 0.656 and not over-interpret figure.",0.7,3.938,Mean of Sequence II averages for R1–R9 from Appendix B.,0.7,N/R,"Only qualitative efficiency paths and some round-to-round differences reported, no overall numeric mean for reward Sequence II.",0,N/R,No average earnings for reward treatment Sequence II tabulated.,0,United States,Same lab and subject pool.,1,N/R,No age details beyond being students.,0.2,N/R,Not specified.,0,"Undergraduate students (introductory economics classes, various majors)",Uniform subject pool across treatments.,1,On site,In-person computerized experiment.,1,"Sequence II has two stages: Stage 1 VCM identical to Sequence I; Stage 2 endows 6 tokens to allocate between private account (10 cents each) and rewards (each token gives 10 cents to chosen recipient). Rewards are zero-sum transfers within group, not changing total group earnings except via induced changes in contributions over time.",Based on decision-setting section describing reward treatment and structural comparison to sanction game.,1
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00051.x.md,"Sanction & Reward – Sequence II (Rounds 11–20), standard framing","Sanction&reward treatment in Table 1 and Appendix B (groups RS1–RS9), Sequence II with both instruments and ‘sanction/reward’ language.",1,Institution: VCM with Stage 2 allowing both costly sanctions and costly rewards; 10-round Sequence II horizon; ‘sanction & reward’ framing.,"Sanction&reward treatment uniquely allows subjects to allocate six Stage 2 tokens across private account, sanctions, and rewards.",1,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Institutional features manipulated; random assignment to groups.,1,True,Conducted in a university lab.,1,False,No simulation-only components.,1,False,No separate analytical derivations as main results.,1,4,Four-person groups across all treatments.,1,10,Sequence II: ten rounds.,1,1,Token allocations 0–6 flexibly in both Stage 1 and Stage 2.,1,0,Six Stage 1 tokens start under individual control (private).,1,0.8333,Same linear VCM as baseline and other treatments.,1,0,Communication not allowed.,1,1,End-of-round feedback plus history screen with past decisions and earnings.,1,0,Subjects learn only number of sanctions received; cannot identify individual sanctioners.,1,0,"By analogy with sanctions in RS instructions, participants learn tokens others used to reward them but not who rewarded them specifically.",0.7,1,Two sequences of ten rounds known in advance.,1,1,Stage 2 allows allocation of tokens to sanctions.,1,1,Each sanction token forgoes 10-cent private earning (one token).,1,1,Each sanction token reduces target’s earnings by 10 cents (=1 token).,1,1,Stage 2 also allows rewarding others.,1,1,Each reward token costs one private token (10 cents).,1,1,Each reward token increases recipient’s earnings by 10 cents (=1 token).,1,6,Six tokens in Stage 1 for contribution decision; six in Stage 2 for sanctions/rewards.,1,0.821,"From Appendix B, Sequence II average tokens to group per member per round across RS1–RS9: (4.25+4.35+5.625+5.675+4.025+5.675+2+2.425+5.175)/9 ≈ 4.925. Normalize by 6: 4.925/6 ≈ 0.821.",0.8,4.925,Computed mean of the Sequence II averages listed for RS1–RS9 groups in Appendix B.,0.8,N/R,"Text gives dynamic pattern and notes highest efficiencies overall, but does not give precise aggregate value.",0,N/R,No numeric average of group earnings for RS treatment reported.,0,United States,Indiana University experiment.,1,N/R,Age not specified.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,"Undergraduate students (introductory economics classes, various majors)",Same subject pool as other treatments.,1,On site,Standard lab experiment.,1,"Sequence II: Stage 1 VCM as before; Stage 2 six tokens may be allocated among private account (10 cents each), sanctions (each reduces target by 10 cents), and rewards (each increases target by 10 cents). Decisions about sanctions and rewards are made with knowledge of all group members’ current-round Stage 1 allocations (anonymous, random order). Players see how many tokens they were rewarded and sanctioned overall, but not from whom or how others were treated. Finitely repeated game with common knowledge of horizon.",Based on detailed instructions in Appendix A for sanction&reward treatment.,1
