10.1098_rstb.2008.0275.md,"N-experiment, N-P sequence – Switzerland (St Gallen and Zurich pooled)",One-shot public goods game without punishment (N-experiment) played first in the N-P sequence; Swiss participant pools are statistically indistinguishable and are treated as one condition when reporting country-level behaviour.,0.8,Country = Switzerland; City = St Gallen/Zurich; Sequence = N-P; Punishment option = No; Group size = 3; MPCR (α) = 0.5; One-shot interaction; Anonymous; No communication.,"All features explicitly described in Section 4: α=0.5, groups of three, one-shot, anonymous, two-country design, N-experiment has no punishment.",0.95,True,Laboratory experiment with 603 student participants from Russia and Switzerland.,1,True,Controlled lab design with random assignment to groups and treatments.,1,True,Conducted in computerized laboratories at universities in all four cities.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results from human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model as the primary method of this reported experiment.,1,3,“Groups of three participants played an anonymous one-shot public goods game”.,1,1,Described as a one-shot game; each N-experiment in the sequence is played once.,0.95,1,Standard linear public goods game with a 20-token endowment described earlier allows any integer contribution from 0 to full endowment.,0.9,0,Endowment is initially in the private account and players decide how many tokens to contribute.,0.9,0.5,Section 4: “Groups of three participants played an anonymous one-shot public goods game (with α=0.5).” MPCR is α in their notation.,1,0,No mention of any communication; one-shot anonymous design intended to avoid reputational or strategic considerations.,0.95,1,"Standard public goods protocol implies subjects must see others’ contributions and perhaps payoffs to form beliefs; specifically they report beliefs about others’ contributions, implying such information structure. Use 1 assuming contribution summary is shown after decisions (even though this is the first and only round).",0.6,0,No punishment stage in N-experiment.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present in the design.,1,1,"One-shot design: participants are clearly told there is a single decision in that experiment, so effective horizon is known.",0.8,0,This is explicitly the treatment “with no punishment opportunities (N-experiment)”.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,0,Only public goods contribution and (in other treatments) punishment are discussed; no reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"Section 2 defines the public goods game used: “each of n-group members receives an endowment of, say, 20 tokens,” and subsequent parts of the paper on this experiment use the same parametric structure (α=0.5, 3 players). No alternative endowment is given, so we assume 20 tokens.",0.8,N/R,Country-level average contributions in this specific condition are not given numerically as a fraction of endowment.,0.1,N/R,They state that contributions in Russian N-experiments are significantly lower than in Swiss N-experiments but provide no exact Swiss mean.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoffs or efficiency indices reported for this specific condition.,0.1,N/R,Only comparative statements about earnings in P-experiments are reported; not for N-experiments by country.,0.1,Switzerland,Swiss participant pools in St Gallen and Zurich.,1,N/R,Only that they are students; no age statistics reported for this cross-cultural student sample.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0.1,University students,They explicitly describe the 603 people as students at the universities in the four cities.,0.95,On site,Conducted in computerized university laboratories with participants physically present.,1,"One-shot linear public goods game, groups of three, endowment 20 tokens, α=0.5, anonymous, no punishment, beliefs elicited about others’ contributions.",