ange to endowment reported.,0.8,N/R,"Only that Russian contributions in this P-experiment are significantly lower than Swiss, no exact means.",0.1,N/R,Contribution distributions are shown qualitatively; values for means are missing.,0.1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported for this specific condition.,0.1,N/R,"They report that 80% of Russian participants in P-experiments earned less than 20, but not average group payoffs.",0.3,Russia,Participants are students from Belgorod and Yekaterinburg.,1,N/R,Age range/statistics not given for Russian student sample.,0.1,N/R,No gender data reported.,0.1,University students,Described as students in Russian universities.,0.95,On site,Computerized on-site labs.,1,"One-shot linear public goods game with peer punishment, groups of three, α=0.5, endowment 20 tokens, anonymous. Russian participants exhibit substantial antisocial punishment of high contributors and expect harsher punishment than Swiss even when contributing at or above the group average.",Includes notable behavioural pattern (antisocial punishment) that characterizes this condition and is central to the paper.,0.95
10.1098_rspb.2003.2336.md,Baseline spatial public goods game without punishment or reputation,"Section 2 describes a spatial public goods game with only cooperators and defectors (no punishment, no reputation) and reports equilibrium frequencies as a function of r.",1,"Multiplication factor r for public good (varied; threshold rc≈2.07, full cooperation above r≈2.48)",Figure 1 and text: equilibrium frequencies of cooperators and defectors are depicted as a function of r; c=1.,1,False,"The study uses spatial evolutionary simulations on a hexagonal lattice, not human subjects.",1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational model.,1,False,No laboratory with human participants; only simulations.,1,True,"Describes lattice-based evolutionary dynamics, updating strategies over generations; standard numerical simulation.",1,False,Results presented as simulations and snapshots; no closed-form analytical solution is the primary method for this condition.,0.8,3,Groups of three interacting individuals on hexagonal lattice; each game is played with two neighbours plus focal player.,1,5000,Each run: 3000 generations relaxation + 2000 generations measurement (figure 1). Treat each generation as one round of interaction-imitation.,0.9,0,Binary choice: players either cooperate by contributing fixed amount c or defect by investing nothing.,1,0,Endowment is fully under players' control; they decide whether to invest c or 0; default is private (nothing automatically in public fund).,0.8,0.6666666667,"MPCR = r/n. Text: 1<r<n with n=3, so MPCR=r/3; as a representative value around threshold rc≈2.07 gives ≈0.69. Using general formula r/3; cannot fix single r so approximate with r=2 giving 2/3.",0.3,0,No communication or messaging is described; purely mechanical interactions and updates.,1,0,Agents update based only on neighbours’ scores; no mention of displaying summaries as in human experiments.,1,0,No punishment in this baseline condition; question of identity revelation does not arise but coded 0 as there is no punishment mechanism.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism is modeled or discussed for this game.,1,0,Simulated agents have no interface; rounds/generations are not shown to players.,1,0,Section 2 explicitly describes public goods interactions without punishment; punishment is introduced only in Section 3.,1,N/A,No punishment available in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment available in this condition.,1,0,Only cost of cooperation and public good multiplication are modeled; no explicit rewarding of cooperators beyond the public good return.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Cost of cooperation c is set to 1 (c=1). In binary model, this is effectively the per-round endowment available to invest.",0.7,N/R,"They report equilibrium frequencies of cooperators vs defectors, but not explicit normalized contribution rates as a numeric DV.",0.4,N/R,"While cooperators contribute c=1, the paper does not summarize average contributions per player as a numeric DV across r.",0.4,N/R,Total group payoff relative to maximum is not tabulated or graphed.,0.1,N/R,No explicit average group payoff values reported.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational spatial evolutionary model.,1,Spatial public goods game on a 600x600 hexagonal lattice; groups of 3 (focal plus two neighbours) per game; each player plays 6 overlapping triplet games per generation; strategies update via imitation proportional to payoff differences; initial random distribution of cooperators and defectors with equal frequencies.,Details from Section 2 and Figure 1 caption.,1
10.1098_rspb.2003.2336.md,"Spatial public goods game with punishment (no reputation), four strategies G1–G4","Section 3 introduces punishment options, leading to four strategies G1–G4 and reports equilibrium frequencies as a function of r.",1,"Multiplication factor r for public good (varied; threshold rc≈1.35) with punishment parameters gamma=1, beta=1.5 and initial equal frequencies of G1–G4","Figure 2: frequencies of G1–G4 as functions of r with c=1, gamma=1, beta=1.5; initial equal frequencies on lattice.",1,False,"Described as spatially extended system on a lattice with evolutionary update, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human subjects; numerical simulation.,1,False,No lab environment; computational study.,1,True,Lattice-based evolutionary dynamics with repeated realizations for averaging; typical of numerical simulations.,1,False,"Some theoretical observations, but primary results from simulations (frequency curves, snapshots).",0.8,3,Still groups of three interacting individuals on hexagonal lattice.,1,N/R,"Figure 2 caption mentions that the lattice was allowed to evolve until a homogeneous G3 or frozen G1/G4 state was reached, but does not give a fixed number of generations.",0.4,0,Cooperation remains a binary choice to contribute fixed amount c or nothing.,1,0,Same binary contribution decision structure as baseline; no automatic contribution.,0.8,0.6666666667,"MPCR=r/n with n=3. They vary r; general formula applies; representative value taken as r≈2 giving 2/3, but exact r not fixed.",0.3,0,No communication between players is modeled; actions depend on strategies and observed behaviour only.,1,0,"Agents see neighbours’ scores for imitation, but no explicit summary display concept as for human experiments.",1,0,Punishment is based on observing defection within the triplet; no separate identity-revelation mechanism or anonymity manipulation is modeled.,0.7,0,No reward stage is implemented.,1,0,No notion of displaying total or remaining rounds to agents.,1,1,Section 3 explicitly introduces costly punishment following each public goods interaction.,1,1,Cost to punisher gamma per defecting co-player; given as gamma=1 in Figure 2.,1,1.5,Fine to each defector is beta per punishing co-player; beta=1.5 in Figure 2.,1,0,Only punishment is added; no explicit reward actions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Cost of cooperation set to c=1; this is the per-round investable amount.,0.7,N/R,"Results reported as equilibrium frequencies of strategy types (G1–G4), not explicitly as contribution rates.",0.4,N/R,No numeric average contributions reported beyond per-strategy behaviours (cooperate or defect).,0.4,N/R,No explicit group-efficiency metric is plotted or tabulated.,0.1,N/R,Average payoffs or group payoffs are not given as standalone DVs.,0.1,N/A,"Simulation only, no human subjects.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational spatial evolutionary simulations.,1,"Four strategies: G1 (cooperate and punish defectors), G2 (defect and punish), G3 (defect and do not punish), G4 (cooperate and do not punish). After each triplet public goods game, punishers pay cost gamma per defector, defectors incur fine beta per punisher. Hexagonal lattice of size typically 200x200 (400x400 near rc and discontinuities). Initially equal frequencies of all four strategies; lattice updated synchronously via imitation proportional to payoff differences; simulations repeated and averaged over 100 realizations until convergence to homogeneous G3 or frozen G1/G4 configuration.",Section 3 description and Figure 2 caption.,1
10.1098_rspb.2003.2336.md,Spatial public goods game with punishment and reputation (mu=0.1),"Section 4 introduces reputation on top of punishment, with probability mu=0.1 that cooperators defect if both co-players are non-punishers; Figure 3 reports results.",1,"Multiplication factor r for public good (varied; threshold rc≈1.25) with punishment parameters gamma=1, beta=1.5 and reputation parameter mu=0.1","Figure 3(a): frequencies of G1–G4 as functions of r with c=1, gamma=1, beta=1.5 and mu=0.1.",1,False,Again a lattice-based numerical simulation without human participants.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment; computational model.,1,False,No lab or field with human subjects.,1,True,Frequencies vs r obtained from repeated computational runs with specified parameters.,1,False,"Main results here are numerical (frequency curves, snapshots).",0.8,3,Still using triplets on a hexagonal lattice as in previous sections.,1,N/R,"Simulation parameters are said to be as in Figure 2, which used evolution until convergence, not a fixed generation count.",0.4,0,"Contribution decision remains binary (cooperate by contributing c or defect by contributing 0), with additional conditional defection via reputation.",1,0,No automatic initial contribution; same structure as earlier games.,0.8,0.6666666667,"As before, MPCR=r/3 with n=3; r varied.",0.3,0,Reputation is an informational parameter; there is no modeled chat or direct communication.,1,0,"Agents may learn about others’ punishment behaviour probabilistically via mu, not via displayed summaries.",1,0,Reputation indicates punishing propensity but there is no explicit identity revelation mechanism aside from local knowledge in interactions.,0.6,0,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No indication that agents know or are shown the number of generations.,1,1,"This builds on the punishment model from Section 3, retaining costly punishment.",1,1,"Cost gamma per punished defector remains 1 as in Figure 2; Figure 3 caption: c=1, gamma=1, beta=1.5.",1,1.5,Fine beta per punisher remains 1.5.,1,0,Only punishment and reputation are discussed; no reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Cost of cooperation c remains 1.,0.7,N/R,"They focus on frequencies of strategies, not numeric contribution rates.",0.4,N/R,No explicit average contribution amount is reported.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure presented.,0.1,N/R,Average group payoffs not numerically reported.,0.1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Condition implemented entirely in a computational model.,1,"Same four base strategies G1–G4 as punishment condition, plus reputation-based conditional defection: with probability mu=0.1, a cooperator who learns that both co-players are non-punishers temporarily defects. Reputation is modeled as knowledge (with probability mu) of co-players' punishing behaviour from other interactions. Paradoxical G2 vanishes quickly; for r>rc, G1 dominates and G4 persists only as isolated individuals. Simulation parameters as in Figure 2 (lattice size, updating, averaging over realizations).",Section 4 description and Figure 3 caption.,1
