10.1103_PhysRevE.83.036101.md,"Monte Carlo simulations of spatial public goods game with pool punishment (square lattice, G=5)","Paper describes a single computational study: Monte Carlo simulations of a spatial public goods game with strategies D, C, O on a square lattice with overlapping groups of size 5.",1,"Synergy factor r ∈ {2.0, 3.5, 3.8}; punishment fine β (varied over ranges depending on r, γ); punishment cost γ (varied over ranges depending on r); noise level K fixed at 0.5; system size L varied from 200 to 5000.","Independent variables explicitly mentioned as main parameters: fines β, costs γ, and synergy factor r. K and L are varied but K is kept constant at 0.5 for main results.",1,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of strategies on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or animal subjects; it is a numerical simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,"Authors repeatedly refer to Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice, indicating a numerical/agent-based model.",1,False,"While some theoretical reasoning is provided, core results are from simulations; no closed-form analytic solution presented as main method.",0.8,5,"Each public goods group has G=5 players. Global lattice size L varies, but group interaction size is 5.",1,0,"Iterated in Monte Carlo steps until stationarity; no fixed finite number of game rounds as in human experiments, so coded as 0.",0.7,1,"Each cooperator or punisher contributes a fixed amount 1; defectors contribute 0. No choice over contribution level, so continuous choice does not apply; however, within model structure contributions are effectively all-or-nothing; encode as 1 to indicate fixed binary choice rather than constrained single option per se.",0.6,N/A,"No human endowment or default allocation; contributions are fixed model parameters, not decisions from an endowment.",1,N/R,Only group multiplier r and group size G are given; MPCR would be r/G but r varies across conditions and no single value is specified at study level.,0.5,N/A,No human participants; no communication mechanism.,1,0,Agents update solely based on payoffs of self and a randomly chosen neighbor; no mention of summary feedback.,1,N/A,No identities or revelation mechanisms among simulated agents; punishment is institutional and automatic.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,0,Simulated agents do not observe number of Monte Carlo steps; such information is not modeled.,1,1,Pool punishment is a core mechanism; pool punishers pay cost γ and defectors pay fine β when punishers are present.,1,N/R,"γ is the total cost per punisher per group, not specified per punishment unit; the schema’s unit-based cost cannot be uniquely mapped.",0.4,N/R,Fine β is applied to each defector when at least one punisher is present; no per-unit technology ratio specified that fits schema cleanly.,0.4,0,Only punishment is modeled; no rewards or positive incentives.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model specifies contributions (1 for cooperators and punishers, 0 for defectors) but does not frame them as coming from a numerical endowment per round.",0.5,N/R,"Outcomes tracked as stationary frequencies of strategies (ρ_D, ρ_C, ρ_O); aggregate contribution rate is not explicitly reported, though it could be derived from ρ_C and ρ_O, but no specific numerical values are given.",0.4,N/R,"Individual contribution amounts are fixed (1 for C and O, 0 for D) and not treated as varying dependent variables.",0.8,N/R,Total payoffs and their ratio to maximum possible are not numerically reported; only qualitative phase behavior is discussed.,0.4,N/R,"Payoff formulas are given, but no specific numerical stationary average group payoffs are reported.",0.5,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a computational Monte Carlo simulation with no human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; group size G=5 with overlapping groups; three strategies: defectors (D), cooperators (C), pool punishers (O); cooperators and pool punishers contribute 1 to public good, defectors contribute 0; sum of contributions in each group multiplied by synergy factor r (1<r<5) and divided equally among 5 group members; pool punishers additionally pay cost γ to punishment pool in every group; defectors pay fine β in groups where at least one pool punisher is present; strategy updates via pairwise comparison rule with imitation probability w=1/(1+exp[(P_x−P_y)/K]) and K=0.5; random sequential updates; system sizes L from 200 to 5000; relaxation and sampling times from 10^4 to 10^7 Monte Carlo steps; phase diagrams constructed over (β,γ) for r=2.0, 3.5, 3.8.",Summarizes the core structural details of the simulated game as specified in Methods and Results sections.,1
10.1038_s41562-018-0385-5.md,"Fehr & Schurtenberger (2018) – Norm request, no punishment",The text and Fig. 3 describe a treatment where subjects can form a normative standard via requests in a 15-period public goods game without punishment opportunities.,0.9,Normative request opportunity: present; Punishment opportunity: absent,Condition differs from the punishment treatment only by absence of punishment while both have norm-formation via requests.,0.95,True,Human subjects in a public goods experiment are described.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized public goods game in a lab-style setting.",0.95,True,Standard language for public goods experiments; no field context indicated.,0.8,False,"Involves human participants, not an agent-based or numerical simulation.",1,False,"Section reports experimental results, not purely formal modeling.",1,4,Fig. 3 caption: “fixed groups of four subjects”.,1,15,Fig. 3 caption: “play a public goods game for 15 periods”.,1,1,Standard linear public goods games with Fehr typically allow any integer contribution 0–endowment; no restriction to all-or-nothing mentioned.,0.7,0,Standard design: all endowment starts in private account; no pre-allocation to public good mentioned.,0.7,N/R,No multiplier or MPCR reported in the text.,0.01,0,No mention of communication; standard public goods designs without explicit communication.,0.7,1,Punishment (in the other treatment) and norm requests require observing others’ contributions; implies feedback about others’ cooperation each period.,0.7,0,No punishment stage exists in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,1,Standard finitely repeated public goods experiments announce the fixed horizon; decline-over-time arguments rely on common knowledge of the horizon.,0.8,0,Treatment is explicitly described as ‘without punishment’.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,"No reward or bonus stage is mentioned; only contributions (and in other treatment, punishment).",0.9,N/A,No rew