10.1038_s41562-018-0385-5.md,"Fehr & Schurtenberger (2018) – Norm request, with punishment",Fig. 3b and accompanying text describe a treatment with both norm requests and a peer punishment opportunity in a 15-period public goods game.,0.95,Normative request opportunity: present; Punishment opportunity: present,Condition includes both the norm-formation mechanism and a peer punishment stage following contribution decisions.,0.95,True,Human subjects in a controlled public goods game with punishment.,1,True,Treatment in a controlled laboratory experiment.,1,True,Standard Fehr-style lab public goods with punishment; no field implementation mentioned.,0.9,False,Involves human participants.,1,False,"Reports experimental data, not purely theory.",1,4,Fig. 3: “fixed groups of four subjects”.,1,15,Fig. 3: “for 15 periods”.,1,1,Standard linear public goods with punishment; no indication that contributions are restricted to 0 or full.,0.7,0,Usual design: all tokens start in private account; article does not mention pre-funding of public account.,0.7,N/R,No group multiplier or MPCR parameter specified.,0.01,0,No communication between subjects is mentioned for these treatments.,0.7,1,"Punishment must be targeted at free-riders, so participants must see other group members’ contributions and likely payoffs each period.",0.8,0,"Standard public goods punishment designs keep interactions anonymous; identities of punishers are not typically revealed, and no exception is mentioned.",0.7,0,There is no reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,1,"Game is finitely repeated with known horizon, standard in this literature; decay and stability over periods are discussed assuming that.",0.8,1,Text: “treatments with a punishment opportunity allow for the counter-punishment of those who punish free-riders…”.,1,N/R,The per-point or per-token cost of assigning punishment is not stated.,0.01,N/R,The impact of a punishment point on the punished player’s payoff is not reported.,0.01,0,Only punishment and counter-punishment opportunities are mentioned; no rewards.,0.95,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/R,Token endowment per period is not reported.,0.01,N/R,Fig. 3b qualitatively shows normalized contributions over time but no explicit numbers are given.,0.05,N/R,Only normalized contributions are discussed; raw amounts are not provided.,0.05,N/R,"While text notes that normative standard increases cooperation and reduces punishment, exact efficiency ratios are not reported for this particular condition.",0.05,N/R,No explicit payoff figures are given for this treatment.,0.05,N/R,Location of subject pool is not stated in the review text.,0.2,N/R,No age information reported.,0.01,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0.01,N/R,No information on whether subjects are students or general population.,0.01,On site,Described as a laboratory public goods game with fixed groups and punishment; no reference to online implementation.,0.9,"Linear public goods game with fixed groups of four for 15 periods. In each period, subjects first indicate normative requests (what others should contribute); the average request is communicated as a normative standard. After contribution decisions, a peer punishment stage allows targeted punishment of group members, including counter-punishment of those who previously punished free-riders.",Summarized from the description of the punishment treatment with normative requests in the section “How can we identify social norms?” and Fig. 3.,0.95
