ing a new experimental paradigm.",1
10.1038_nature16981.md,"Study 1 – Punishment-only condition (Signaller as Punisher in TPP game, TG stage present)",Paper explicitly labels a 'punishment-only condition' with n=397 Signaller–Chooser pairs; this row captures that treatment.,1,Available signal type in signalling stage: Punishment only (Signaller plays only as third-party Punisher in TPP game; no helping role available).,Condition differs from others by which signalling opportunities are available (punishment vs helping vs both).,1,True,Amazon Mechanical Turk participants play economic games.,1,True,Authors randomly assign participants to experimental conditions manipulating available signals.,1,False,Run online via Amazon Mechanical Turk rather than in a physical lab.,1,False,"This row pertains to the behavioural experiment, not the agent-based models.",1,False,This condition is not an analytical model.,1,3,"Third-party punishment game involves Helper, Recipient, and third-party Punisher as described in Extended Data Fig. 3.",0.9,1,Standard MTurk one-shot games; text and figure describe single interaction in TPPG and single TG.,0.8,1,"Helper 'decides whether to share money' suggests binary, but TG uses continuous sending; to remain conservative for cooperation measurement we code as allowing any amount (non all-or-nothing).",0.4,N/R,No public-goods style contribution or initial allocation between public/private accounts is described.,1,N/R,"No public goods multiplier is reported; only a trust game with tripling of transfers, which is not a linear public good.",1,0,No mention of communication; MTurk economic games typically disallow chat.,0.9,0,Paper does not state that participants see detailed payoff or punishment summaries from others; only signalling decisions are revealed.,0.6,0,Design focuses on anonymous economic game interactions; no mention of identities being revealed.,0.8,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism is present in the TPP game.,1,0,One-shot game; no indication that any number of rounds is displayed.,0.9,1,Third-party Punisher can pay to punish the Helper.,1,N/R,Cost per unit of punishment is not specified in main text or figures.,0.1,N/R,Magnitude of reduction in Helper payoff per unit of punishment is not reported.,0.1,0,No mechanism for costly rewarding is described; only punishment and helping.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/R,Specific token/dollar amounts for TPP game and TG endowments are not reported in the main text.,0.1,N/R,"No contribution to a public good is measured; outcomes are trust and trustworthiness, not contribution rate.",1,N/R,No public-good contribution amount is reported.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric (group payoff over maximum) is reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff values are not provided; only percentage differences in TG transfers and returns.,0.1,N/R,"MTurk sample location is not explicitly described (e.g., US-only vs international) in the provided text.",0.1,N/R,No age information reported in the main text.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,N/R,No education details reported.,0.1,Online,"Explicitly run 'using Amazon Mechanical Turk', an online platform.",1,"Two-stage design: signalling stage is a third-party punishment game where Signaller acts once as third-party Punisher observing a Helper–Recipient interaction; if the Helper is selfish (does not share), the Punisher decides whether to pay to punish. Partner-choice stage is a trust game between the same Signaller and a Chooser: Chooser selects how much of an endowment to send (any amount sent is tripled), and Signaller chooses how much to return. Choosers can condition sending on observed punishment decision in the TPP game. In this punishment-only condition, Signaller never plays as Helper, so punishment is the only available signal.",Summary based on Extended Data Fig. 3 and the main-text description of the two-stage MTurk experiment.,1
