tion, not as a separate costly rewarding institution with defined cost/tech parameters.",0.8,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism beyond helping.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Token/dollar endowments in TPPG and TG are not stated in the provided text.,0.1,N/R,"Study reports trust and return percentages, not normalized contribution to a public good.",1,N/R,No public-good contribution amount reported.,1,N/R,No calculation of efficiency reported.,1,N/R,Only relative sending/return percentages are provided.,0.1,N/R,Country of MTurk workers is not given.,0.1,N/R,Age is not reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender data mentioned.,0.1,N/R,Education level not specified.,0.1,Online,Conducted using Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Signalling stage consists of two separate TPP games with different others: in one, Signaller is Helper, deciding whether to share money with a Recipient; in the other, Signaller is third-party Punisher, deciding whether to pay to punish a selfish Helper. Both decisions are observed by the Chooser as signals of prosociality. Partner-choice stage is a trust game where the Chooser chooses how much to send (tripled by experimenter) to the Signaller and the Signaller chooses how much to return. In this condition both helping and punishment are available and observable, and Choosers’ trust decisions can condition on both actions.",Derived from Extended Data Fig. 3 and text describing 'punishment-plus-helping condition' where Signaller plays twice as Punisher and Helper.,1
