R,Endowment sizes in helping and TG are not stated.,0.1,N/R,"Study reports probability of helping and TG trust/return percentages, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,Absolute amounts helped or sent/returned are not given.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency statistic reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff values are not reported.,0.1,N/R,Country of MTurk workers not specified.,0.1,N/R,Age data not provided.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,N/R,No education information provided.,0.1,Online,Conducted on Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Signalling stage: Signaller serves solely as Helper in a third-party punishment game, deciding whether to share money with a Recipient while a third-party Punisher (another participant) may punish selfish choices. Chooser observes the Signaller’s helping decision as a signal. Partner-choice stage: same trust game as in other conditions, where the Chooser decides how much of an endowment to send (which is tripled) and the Signaller decides how much to return. In this helping-only condition, punishment is not available to the Signaller as a signal.","Based on Extended Data Fig. 3 description: in 'helping-only condition, the Signaller plays once as the Helper' and then participates in the TG.",1
10.1038_nature16981.md,Study 2 – Trust Game (TG) condition – punishment as signal of trustworthiness,Extended Data Fig. 4 describes 'our additional experiment (study 2)' with a manipulation of whether stage 2 is a trust game (TG) or an ultimatum game (UG); this row is the TG condition.,1,Stage-2 game type: Trust game (TG); Chooser’s second-stage interaction is a trust game rather than ultimatum game.,Study 2 manipulates whether stage 2 is TG vs UG; this row records TG level of that factor.,1,True,Human participants (MTurk) in a behavioural experiment.,1,True,Participants are randomly assigned to TG vs UG second-stage conditions.,1,False,"Conducted online via MTurk, consistent with Study 1.",0.9,False,This is not the agent-based model; it is an additional behavioural experiment.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,3,"Design parallels Study 1: signalling stage is a TPP game with Helper, Recipient, and third-party Punisher.",0.8,1,Description implies a single signalling-stage TPPG and a single TG interaction.,0.8,1,Same game structure as Study 1 with binary Helper choice and continuous trust decisions; conservatively code as non all-or-nothing.,0.4,N/R,No public-good allocation is used.,1,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,0,No communication tools mentioned.,0.9,0,Only punishment decisions are used as signals; no mention of further summaries.,0.6,0,No identity revelation is reported.,0.8,0,No separate reward mechanism included.,1,0,One-shot; no rounds display.,0.9,1,Participants engage in third-party punishment in stage 1 in both TG and UG conditions.,1,N/R,Numerical punishment cost not provided for Study 2 either.,0.1,N/R,Magnitude of punishment effect is not reported.,0.1,0,No costly reward mechanism is mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment size in TG not stated in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No public-good contribution measure; outcome is mean amount sent in TG to punishers vs non-punishers.,1,N/R,"Absolute amounts sent in TG are not reported, only percentage differences.",0.1,N/R,Efficiency is not reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff values reported.,0.1,N/R,MTurk sample location not specified for Study 2.,0.1,N/R,Age not reported.,0.1,N/R,Gender distribution not described.,0.1,N/R,Education not described.,0.1,Online,"Described as 'our additional experiment'; given continuity with Study 1 and use of MTurk in main paper, it is very likely online.",0.8,"Study 2 replicates the signalling-stage third-party punishment game: a Helper chooses whether to share money with a Recipient, and a third-party Punisher decides whether to pay to punish a selfish Helper. Punishment decisions are observed and then used as signals in the second-stage game. In the TG condition, the second stage is a trust game: Choosers decide how much to send to the Signaller (Punisher) and any amount sent is tripled; then Signallers decide how much to return. Extended Data Fig. 4 reports that in TG, Choosers send 16 percentage points more to punishers than to non-punishers, indicating a strong reputational effect of punishment as a signal of trustworthiness.",Summarized from Extended Data Fig. 4 description of Study 2 TG condition and parallel with Study 1 design.,1
10.1038_nature16981.md,Study 2 – Ultimatum Game (UG) condition – punishment as signal of willingness to retaliate,Extended Data Fig. 4 describes Study 2 with a TG vs UG manipulation; this row is the UG condition with n=421 Choosers.,1,Stage-2 game type: Ultimatum game (UG); Chooser’s second-stage interaction is an ultimatum game rather than a trust game.,Independent variable in Study 2 is whether stage 2 is TG vs UG.,1,True,Uses human participants in an economic game.,1,True,Random assignment to TG vs UG is implied by the design.,1,False,"Likely conducted online via MTurk, aligned with Study 1.",0.8,False,Not a simulation but a behavioural experiment.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,3,"As in other conditions, signalling stage is a TPP game with Helper, Recipient, and third-party Punisher; stage 2 is a two-player UG.",0.8,1,One-shot TPP game and one-shot UG per participant pair.,0.8,1,UG offers can vary over a continuous range; Helper decision is likely binary share/no-share; conservatively code as non all-or-nothing.,0.4,N/R,No public-goods style contribution.,1,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,0,No communication options described.,0.9,0,"Participants see punishment decisions, not payoff summaries.",0.6,0,No identity revelation mentioned.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,0,Single-shot design; rounds not displayed.,0.9,1,Stage 1 includes a standard third-party punishment game.,1,N/R,No numerical cost reported.,0.1,N/R,Magnitude per unit of punishment not given.,0.1,0,No staggered reward mechanism in the design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment in UG not reported in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No cooperative contribution measure; outcome is amount sent in UG offers.,1,N/R,Absolute UG offers not provided; only that offers to punishers are 3 percentage points higher.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency not measured.,1,N/R,Group payoff not reported.,0.1,N/R,Country of MTurk workers not specified.,0.1,N/R,Age not given.,0.1,N/R,Gender not given.,0.1,N/R,Education not reported.,0.1,Online,Likely same MTurk-based online environment as other studies in the paper.,0.8,"Same signalling-stage third-party punishment game as in TG condition: a Helper decides whether to share with a Recipient; a third-party Punisher can pay to punish selfish Helpers. Punishment decisions serve as signals. In the UG condition, the second-stage interaction between Chooser and Signaller is an ultimatum game: Chooser proposes a division of money and Signaller can accept or reject. Preferentially sending more to punishers now reflects expectations that punishers are more willing to retaliate against unfair offers. Extended Data Fig. 4 reports that in UG, Choosers send only 3 percentage points more to punishers than to non-punishers, indicating a much weaker reputational effect of punishment when signalling retaliatoriness instead of trustworthiness.","Summarized from Extended Data Fig. 4 text, which interprets UG behaviour as signalling willingness to retaliate.",1
10.1038_nature16981.md,"Theoretical model – Analytical equilibrium analysis (three signalling scenarios, punishment/helping as signals)","Main text describes a game theoretic model with analytical equilibrium analysis for three scenarios (punishment only, helping only, both). This row summarizes that analytical condition set.",1,"Signal availability and informativeness parameters: (1) Punishment-only scenario; (2) Helping-only scenario; (3) Both punishment and helping available, varying informativeness of small and large costs (I_SP, I_LP, I_SH, I_LH) and benefit/cost parameters b, c.","Model varies which signals are available and informativeness/cost-benefit parameters, as shown in Fig. 1a–b.",1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,False,Not an empirical or controlled experiment; it is theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory involved; analytical work only.,1,False,"This particular row describes analytical equilibrium calculations, not the agent-based simulations; simulations are also present but conceptually separate.",1,True,"Authors identify Nash equilibria robust against indirect invasion (RAII) and provide conditions using analytical inequalities on parameters I_SP, I_LP, I_SH, I_LH, b, c.",1,N/A,"Model is at the level of Signaller and Chooser roles, with abstract populations; no fixed group size like in a standard public goods game is specified as a constant parameter.",0.8,N/A,Game-theoretic model considers repeated partnering and evolutionary dynamics abstractly; not a discrete repeated-round experimental game.,1,N/A,"Model actions are binary (signal vs not signal), but this is an analytical abstraction rather than a parameter of an implemented game.",1,N/A,No endowment allocation or contribution stage; only signalling and partner choice.,1,N/A,"Not a public-goods model; uses abstract benefit and cost parameters (b, c).",1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,N/A,No implemented user interface; just mathematical types and strategies.,1,N/A,Identities are not modelled; only types.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution in the mathematical model; only punishment and helping as potential signals.,1,N/A,No explicit round count shown to agents; repeated interactions are modelled abstractly.,1,1,Model includes costly third-party punishment as one possible signal (scenarios 1 and 3).,1,N/R,Model defines punishment costs abstractly as 'small' or 'large' without numeric value.,1,N/R,Model does not specify a granular mapping of punishment to payoff changes beyond general cost/benefit structure.,1,0,"Model includes helping as a separate costly signal to benefit third parties, but not a formal reward technology with cost/tech parameters as defined in the schema.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism; helping is analytically modelled as a separate signal.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,"No token endowment is used in the analytical model; payoffs defined in terms of benefits and costs (b, c and signalling costs).",1,N/A,No measured contributions; only equilibrium strategies regarding signalling and partner acceptance.,1,N/A,No numeric contributions.,1,N/A,"Model focuses on evolutionary stability and expected payoffs, not explicit efficiency ratios.",1,N/A,Group payoff outcomes are discussed qualitatively but not as a single reported DV.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"This row is for the analytical model, which involves no human subjects.",1,"Analytical costly-signalling model with two roles: Signaller and Chooser. Signallers can be 'trustworthy' (cooperate when trusted) or 'exploitative' (defect when trusted). In each potential interaction, a Signaller may face either a small or large cost of sending each available signal (TPP and/or helping). Helping and punishment signals affect payoffs through reputational benefits via partner choice. Choosers do not observe types directly but see which signals are available and which are used, conditioning acceptance or rejection on signals. Three signalling scenarios are analysed: (1) only punishment allowed; (2) only helping allowed; (3) both helping and punishment allowed. Equilibrium analysis focuses on Nash equilibria robust against indirect invasion (RAII), identifying conditions under which 'punishment-signalling', 'helping-signalling', or 'only-helping' profiles emerge, given parameters I_SP, I_LP, I_SH, I_LH (informativeness of small/large costs for punishment/helping), and benefit/cost parameters b and c.","Summarizes sections describing the model, Fig. 1a–b, and supporting description of equilibrium conditions.",1
10.1038_nature16981.md,Agent-based simulations – Scenario set (evolutionary dynamics with punishment and helping signals),"Main text and Extended Data Fig. 2 describe agent-based simulations implementing the theoretical model across three scenarios, with evolving strategies and mutation.",1,"Scenario and signal informativeness parameters: Scenario 1 (only punishment available), Scenario 2 (only helping available), Scenario 3 (both punishment and helping available); informativeness parameters (I_SP, I_LP, I_SH, I_LH) and ratio c/b varied.","Simulations test model for different signal availability and informativeness values, as described around Fig. 1c and Extended Data Fig. 2.",1,False,"Simulations involve computational agents, not humans.",1,False,"Though parameter sweeps are run, they are simulations not empirical experiments with subjects.",1,False,Computation only.,1,True,"Described as 'stochastic evolutionary dynamics where agents interact at random to earn payoffs, strategies with higher payoffs become more common, and mutation maintains variation'.",1,False,"This row references the numerical agent-based simulations, not the closed-form equilibrium analysis.",1,N/A,"Population size and matching structure (random pairings) exist, but a fixed small group size as in lab games is not the independent experimental configuration; not reported numerically.",0.8,N/A,Simulations iterate over many generations; number of interaction rounds per generation is not provided.,1,N/A,"Agents choose binary actions (signal vs not; accept vs reject) but these are model primitives, not public-good contribution decisions.",1,N/A,No concept of endowment allocation or contribution stage in the simulation.,1,N/A,No public goods; only partner-choice payoffs.,1,N/A,No communication modelled.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe payoff summaries; they only respond to signals.,1,N/A,No identities modelled.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Simulated agents are not given explicit information about generation counts or rounds.,1,1,"Simulations explicitly include third-party punishment as a potential signal in Scenarios 1 and 3, with costs that can be small or large.",1,N/R,Costs are parameterized qualitatively as 'small' or 'large' and by informativeness ratios; no single numeric cost per unit is reported.,1,N/R,"Exact mapping from punishment to payoffs is part of simulation code, but not numerically specified in text.",1,0,Helping is present as a signal but is not configured as a separate reward technology with cost-per-unit effect as in the schema.,0.9,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism; only helping costs modelled implicitly.,1,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No token endowments; payoffs defined by abstract benefit b and cost c and signalling costs.,1,N/A,"Simulations track signalling and acceptance strategies, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/A,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,"Text reports evolutionary frequencies of signalling (probability of helping or punishing when costs are low) and acceptance behaviour, not aggregate efficiency metrics.",1,N/R,Main reported outputs are probabilities of signalling and acceptance; group payoffs are not given explicitly.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computer simulations.,1,"Stochastic evolutionary dynamics implement the costly-signalling model numerically. Agents are randomly matched as Signallers and Choosers in Scenarios 1–3. Each agent’s strategy specifies whether to punish or help when signalling costs are small or large, and whether to accept or reject potential partners given observed signals. Payoffs from interactions determine reproductive or imitation success; strategies with higher payoffs become more common over generations, with mutation maintaining variation. Extended Data Fig. 2 reports, for each scenario, equilibrium frequencies of punishment and helping when costs are small, and acceptance rates by Choosers conditional on observed signals, as a function of signal informativeness (I_SP, I_LP, I_SH, I_LH) and c/b. Fig. 1c summarizes how punishment usage declines and helping usage remains high as helping becomes more informative than punishment when both signals are available.",Based on main-text description of evolutionary dynamics and Extended Data Figs. 1–2.,1
10.1146_annurev-psych-081920-042106.md,House et al. (2020) binary dictator game – Fair norm video,Condition where participants saw a community adult say 1/1 is good and 2/0 is bad in the binary dictator game described under House et al. (2020).,0.9,"Normative video prime: fair (1/1 framed as good, 2/0 as bad)",Independent variable is which video participants observed; this row is for the fair condition.,0.9,True,Human adults and children in eight populations played the game.,1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to one of three video conditions before making a decision.,1,False,"Study conducted in diverse field sites (hunter-gatherers, villages, cities), not a centralized behavioral lab.",0.9,False,No computational or agent-based simulation reported for this task.,1,False,Task is empirical; no closed-form analytical model as the primary method.,1,2,Dictator game interaction between allocator and anonymous partner with binary allocations (2/0 vs 1/1).,1,1,Described as a simple dictator game with a single binary choice; no repetition mentioned.,0.9,0,"Two discrete allocation options (2/0 or 1/1), but not an all-or-nothing contribution to a public account; binary transfer, not standard PGG framing.",0.5,0,Standard dictator framing: endowment initially controlled by the dictator; nothing starts in the public/other account.,0.8,N/R,Not a public goods game; no multiplier or marginal per capita return reported.,1,0,Anonymous dictator game with video primes; no mention of communication between players.,0.95,0,No feedback about others’ behavior or payoffs reported.,0.95,0,No punishment stage in this dictator game.,1,0,No reward stage in this dictator game.,1,0,Single decision; no mention of displaying total or remaining rounds.,1,0,Dictator’s decision is unilateral; no punishment option is described.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this game.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this game.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Paper only refers to 'two rewards' vs 'one reward'; specific numeric endowment size not given.,0.3,N/R,Outcomes are frequencies of 1/1 vs 2/0 choices; no normalized rate reported.,0.2,N/R,Only binary allocation choice reported across sites; no average number of rewards shared given.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency calculations provided.,0.1,N/R,No numerical payoff summaries reported.,0.1,"Multiple (Tanzania, Ecuador, Argentina, Vanuatu, India, Germany)","Populations named: Hadza (Tanzania), Shuar (Ecuador), Wichí and La Plata (Argentina), Tanna (Vanuatu), Pune (India), Berlin (Germany).",0.95,"Children (various ages) and adults; children roughly 4–14, adults 18+ (approximate)","Text: children n=833 and adults n=255; figure shows developmental trajectories through middle childhood into adolescence, but exact age bounds not listed.",0.5,N/R,No breakdown by sex/gender reported.,0.1,Mixed; from non-schooled small-scale populations to urban educated participants,Populations range from hunter-gatherers and horticulturalists to urbanites in Berlin and Pune; detailed schooling distributions not given.,0.6,Field experiment,"Game conducted in situ across eight diverse field sites (hunter-gatherer bands, villages, cities), not an online panel or single lab.",0.9,"Binary dictator choice between 2/0 and 1/1 rewards to self/partner; participants randomly assigned to watch a local adult endorse fair, selfish, or neutral normative statements before deciding; some later reported which statement was 'more correct' to measure normative judgments.",Summarized from the dictator game description and the manipulation with three videos in House et al. (2020).,1
