10.1146_annurev-psych-081920-042106.md,Chernyak et al. (2019) reciprocity game – Positive reciprocity condition,Condition where an avatar first gave the child a sticker (participant receives a benefit) and then child could reciprocate.,0.9,Interaction type: positive direct reciprocity (partner previously helped child),Primary manipulation is whether child previously received or lost a sticker; this row is for receiving (positive reciprocity).,0.9,True,Experimental data from children in Boston using a computer-based task.,1,True,Children assigned to conditions with controlled computer-presented interactions.,1,True,Described as a computer-based experimental setup in Boston; suggests a lab or similar controlled environment.,0.9,False,No agent-based or computational simulation used to generate behavioral data.,1,False,"Focus is empirical behavior, not analytical derivations.",1,2,"Focal interaction is between the child and one avatar partner in the reciprocity test (donor–recipient pair), although four avatars are present.",0.7,1,"In the key test, the child has a single opportunity to reciprocate after a prior action by the avatar.",0.8,1,"Child decides whether or not to give a sticker (binary give vs not give), which corresponds to all-or-nothing transfer of that sticker.",0.8,0,Stickers are initially in the child’s possession; none starts in a shared/public account.,0.8,N/R,Not a public goods structure or linear multiplier; simple transfer.,1,0,Interaction is via pre-programmed avatar actions; no communication option for child beyond choosing to give/take.,0.95,0,"Child only sees immediate actions of avatars, not payoff summaries or histories.",0.8,0,No punishment stage in this reciprocity condition.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism beyond giving a sticker.,1,0,Text does not report any display of number of rounds or remaining interactions.,0.7,0,Children can reward partner by reciprocating; no explicit costly punishment option here.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,"Child can give a sticker to the partner avatar after having been helped, which functions as a costly reward.",0.85,N/R,Sticker value as cost per unit not quantified beyond being a sticker; no numeric token value.,0.2,1,Each sticker the child gives increases partner’s payoff by one sticker (1:1 transfer).,0.7,N/R,Number of stickers initially given to the child not stated in the review.,0.3,N/R,"Outcome reported qualitatively as increasing tendency to reciprocate with age, not as a normalized fraction in the review.",0.2,N/R,Review does not give average number of stickers given; only proportions of children who reciprocate.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency calculation reported.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff data reported.,0.1,United States,"Children tested in Boston, which is in the United States.",1,4–9 years (children),Text: 'tested children from ages 4 to 9 in Boston'.,1,N/R,Gender composition not mentioned in the review.,0.1,Pre-school and primary school children,Ages 4–9 implies pre-school and elementary school; no specific schooling details provided.,0.6,On site,Computer-based experiment with children in Boston likely conducted in a lab or school setting rather than online.,0.8,"Children interacted via avatars in a computer task with four avatars. In this condition, one avatar gave the child a sticker (help). Later, the child could choose whether to give a sticker back to that avatar, measuring development of positive direct reciprocity and related normative judgments.",Summarized from the description of the Chernyak et al. (2019) task.,1
10.1146_annurev-psych-081920-042106.md,Chernyak et al. (2019) reciprocity game – Negative reciprocity condition,"Condition where an avatar first took a sticker from the child, and the child could retaliate.",0.9,Interaction type: negative direct reciprocity (partner previously harmed child by taking a sticker),Manipulation is that the partner took rather than gave a sticker; this tests retaliation.,0.9,True,Same experimental task with children in Boston.,1,True,Condition assignment and avatar behaviors are experimentally controlled.,1,True,"Computer-based experiment in Boston, indicating lab or controlled school setting.",0.9,False,No simulations.,1,False,Empirical child behavior study.,1,2,Key reciprocity decision is between child and single avatar partner.,0.7,1,Main negative reciprocity decision occurs once per child in the described setup.,0.8,1,"Child can choose to take or not take a sticker (retaliation) from the partner, a binary all-or-nothing punishment.",0.8,0,"Stickers start with the child, not in a common pool.",0.8,N/R,Not a public goods scenario.,1,0,No inter-participant communication.,0.95,0,Children only observe the partner’s prior action; no summaries of others’ earnings.,0.8,0,"Punishment (if any) is directed at a known avatar; but there is no notion of hidden identity or revelation, so we code as not applicable.",0.5,0,No explicit reward beyond potential reciprocity.,0.8,0,No mention of round indicators.,0.7,1,"Child can respond to having a sticker taken by taking one from the partner, i.e., negative reciprocity functioning as costly punishment.",0.85,N/R,Exact cost in sticker units to the child not reported in the review.,0.2,1,Child’s retaliatory taking removes one sticker from the partner per sticker taken (1:1).,0.7,0,"This condition focuses on retaliation, not rewarding.",0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Number of stickers the child initially has not specified.,0.3,N/R,Outcome is proportion of children who retalia