10.1146_annurev-psych-081920-042106.md,House (2017) repeated binary dictator game – Los Angeles sample,Condition where children in Los Angeles played a repeated binary dictator game choosing between 2/2 and 3/0 options.,0.9,Study site: Los Angeles (US children) in repeated 2/2 vs 3/0 dictator game,Independent variable across conditions is cultural context (Fiji vs Los Angeles); this row is for Los Angeles.,0.9,True,Experimental game data from children.,1,True,Structured repeated dictator game with controlled payoff options.,1,True,"Described as an experiment with children in Los Angeles, likely in a lab or school testing room; no field-site ethnographic context mentioned for LA.",0.8,False,No simulation component reported.,1,False,"Behavioral experiment, not analytical modeling.",1,2,Binary dictator game between an actor child and one partner per trial.,0.8,N/R,"Described as a 'repeated dictator game', but the number of rounds is not specified in the review.",0.2,1,"On each move, the actor chooses either 2/2 (share) or 3/0 (keep more), an all-or-nothing transfer to the partner for that unit of interaction.",0.8,0,By default the payoff is under the actor’s control; no initial allocation in a shared account.,0.7,N/R,Not a standard PGG with multiplier; fixed discrete payoffs.,1,0,No mention of communication; typical child dictator design does not allow chat.,0.9,0,Children respond to partner’s previous choice; no indication of broader payoff summaries.,0.7,0,No punishment mechanism in this repeated dictator setup.,1,0,"Sharing via 2/2 is part of the game’s primary decision, not a separate reward stage.",0.8,0,The review does not indicate that the total number of rounds or remaining rounds was displayed.,0.4,0,No explicit punishment choice in the game; only sharing vs not sharing.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"The 2/2 option is the cooperative choice, not a post-hoc reward tool.",0.7,N/A,No separate reward mechanism beyond primary al