es in this review.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency reported.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff given.,0.1,Multiple (33 communities from 15 societies worldwide),Cross-cultural sample spans 33 communities in 15 small-scale societies; specific countries not enumerated in the review passage.,0.8,Adults (exact age range N/R),Typical UG implementations in these projects use adult participants; review does not give ages.,0.6,N/R,Gender breakdown not mentioned.,0.1,Primarily adults from small-scale subsistence communities with limited formal education,"Described as subsistence-oriented societies (foragers, farmers, pastoralists), where formal schooling is usually limited.",0.6,Field experiment,"Games administered in local communities around the world, not centralized labs.",1,"Ultimatum game used to compute minimal acceptable offers (MAOs) across 33 communities. Small communities showed low frequencies of costly punishment (low MAOs), while large communities showed higher MAOs; used to model relationship between community size and willingness to punish unfair offers.",Synthesized from description of Figure 3 and text around Henrich et al. (2010).,1
