10.1111_j.1468-0262.2005.00644.x.md,High-sanction PD – strategy method,"One-shot three-player PD with punishment, high-sanction parameter f>1, using the strategy (contingent response) method, as described in Section 2 and Section 3.1.",1,"Sanction effectiveness and asymmetry: f=3.33 when punishing cooperators, f=2.5 when punishing defectors; elicitation method = strategy (contingent) responses.",Treatments differ in punishment effectiveness f and elicitation method; this condition has high f with type-contingent values and uses the strategy method.,1,True,Human student subjects from University of Zurich/ETH Zurich participated.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized assignment to groups and incentive-compatible payoffs.,1,True,Conducted in the computer laboratory of the Institute for Empirical Research in Economics.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations described for this treatment.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model is tested here; it is a behavioral experiment.,1,3,Three-player prisoner’s dilemma (three-player PD) explicitly stated multiple times.,1,1,Described as a one-shot PD; each subject plays only one period.,1,1,Contribution decision is binary (cooperate/defect) rather than a continuous amount; interpreted as all-or-nothing contribution choice.,1,0,Cooperation is framed as investing into a project starting from private endowment; standard PD/public good framing implies endowment initially is private.,0.7,N/R,Only payoff table is given; project return structure is embedded in the matrix but MPCR not explicitly stated and not easily isolatable from given numbers.,0.3,0,No communication between subjects mentioned; interactions described as anonymous with no indication of messaging.,0.9,0,Subjects learn others’ individual actions and how much they themselves were sanctioned; no mention of aggregate summaries of others’ payoffs or sanctions.,0.7,0,"All interactions are anonymous and personal identities are not known, so punishers’ identities are not revealed.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists in any treatment.,1,0,Game is one-shot; no explicit statement that a remaining-rounds counter is displayed. In a one-shot setting this is effectively irrelevant.,0.6,1,Second-stage allows assigning costly deduction points to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,1,"For each deduction point pij assigned, punisher’s payoff is reduced by pij, i.e., 1 token per point.",1,2.5,Punishment multiplier f is 2.5 when punishing defectors and 3.33 when punishing cooperators; recorded here as the value relevant for defectors (main target of interest) while noting asymmetry in reason.,0.7,0,Only deduction (punishment) points are mentioned; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/R,"Payoff table lists earnings under different cooperation profiles, but the initial token endowment per player is not explicitly reported.",0.2,0.61,"In high-sanction condition 61% of subjects cooperated (binary cooperate=1, defect=0).",1,0,Binary PD with cooperate/defect only; no numeric contribution amount per se. Use 0 to indicate no meaningful continuous contribution amount.,0.5,N/R,Paper does not report group payoff relative to social optimum as a normalized efficiency measure.,0.1,N/R,"No aggregate group payoff statistics (e.g., mean per group) are given by treatment.",0.1,Switzerland,"Subjects were students from the University of Zurich and ETH Zurich, located in Switzerland.",1,N/R,Only that they are undergraduate or graduate students; no age range or mean reported.,0.3,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,0.1,Undergraduate and graduate university students,Explicitly stated that subjects were undergraduate or graduate students from the universities.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical computer laboratory at the university.,1,"One-shot 3-player PD framed as investments into a project; cooperation stage payoffs given by Table I (e.g., all defect: 20 each; all cooperate: 36 each). After observing others’ choices, each player can assign up to 25 costly deduction points to each of the other two players. Strategy method used: each subject specifies punishment for each of four possible cooperation profiles of the two others before seeing actual choices.",Summary of design details directly described in Section 2.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0262.2005.00644.x.md,Low-sanction PD – strategy method,"One-shot three-player PD with punishment, low-sanction parameter f=1, using the strategy (contingent response) method, as described in Section 2 and 3.1.",1,Sanction effectiveness: f=1 when punishing both cooperators and defectors; elicitation method = strategy (contingent) responses.,This treatment differs from the high-sanction strategy treatment only in the punishment multiplier f=1.,1,True,Human student participants from Zurich universities.,1,True,Controlled lab PD experiment with sanctions.,1,True,Conducted in the same computer laboratory as other treatments.,1,False,No indication of simulations.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,3,Three-player PD is constant across treatments.,1,1,Described as one-shot PD.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect decision as in other PD treatments.,1,0,Same basic PD/project framing as in high-sanction treatment; no indication that initial endowment is in public fund.,0.7,N/R,Project return structure not parameterized as MPCR; only payoff matrix provided.,0.3,0,No communication channel mentioned; anonymity stressed.,0.9,0,"Subjects observe others’ individual actions and own sanctions, not aggregate summaries.",0.7,0,"Personal identities remain anonymous, so punishers’ identities are hidden.",1,0,No reward option in the design.,1,0,One-shot design; no report of displaying number of rounds.,0.6,1,Second-stage deduction point mechanism present as in other treatments.,1,1,Punisher’s payoff reduced by pij per point as in general formula.,1,1,In low-sanction treatment f was set equal to 1 for all targets.,1,0,Only punishment via deduction points.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Initial token endowment not specified.,0.2,0.51,In low-sanction treatment 51% of the 93 subjects cooperated.,1,0,Binary cooperate/defect game; no continuous contribution variable.,0.5,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure reported by treatment.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics per