10.1111_j.1468-0262.2005.00644.x.md,High-sanction PD – specific response method (control),"Control treatment of the high-sanction PD where the specific response method replaced the strategy method, as described in Section 2 and Section 3.3.",1,"Sanction effectiveness and asymmetry: f=3.33 when punishing cooperators, f=2.5 when punishing defectors; elicitation method = specific response to realized cooperation decisions.","Same high-sanction parameters as the main high-sanction treatment, but elicitation method differs (specific response instead of contingent strategy).",1,True,Human student subjects participated (102 subjects).,1,True,Controlled lab experiment analogous to other treatments.,1,True,Conducted in the same university computer lab using z-tree.,1,False,No simulation-only component.,1,False,Not an analytical model; behavioral experiment.,1,3,Uses same three-player PD structure.,1,1,"Described as a one-shot PD in all treatments, including this control.",1,1,Same binary cooperate/defect decision.,1,0,Same PD investment framing; no indication that any tokens start in public account.,0.7,N/R,MPCR not provided; only payoff matrix exists and is unchanged.,0.3,0,No communication channel described; anonymity preserved.,0.9,0,Subjects informed of others’ behaviors and own sanctions; summaries not mentioned.,0.7,0,Interactions anonymous; punishers’ identities not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,One-shot design; no indication of rounds display.,0.6,1,Punishment via deduction points exists as in main high-sanction treatment.,1,1,Punisher’s payoff reduced by pij per assigned point.,1,2.5,Punishment multiplier f=2.5 when punishing defectors and 3.33 when punishing cooperators; record 2.5 with note of asymmetry.,0.7,0,Only punishment is available.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,Initial endowment level not stated for any treatment.,0.2,0.77,"In this control treatment 79 out of 102 subjects cooperated (79 cooperators, 23 defectors), i.e., 79/102 ≈ 0.774.",1,0,Binary cooperate/defect decision; no numeric contribution amount.,0.5,N/R,No efficiency measure reported by treatment.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff means provided.,0.1,Switzerland,Same student pool (University of Zurich and ETH Zurich).,1,N/R,"No age statistics reported, only student status.",0.3,N/R,Gender composition not given.,0.1,Undergraduate and graduate university students,Explicit subject description applies to all experiments in paper.,1,On site,Conducted in the same physical computer laboratory.,1,"Same one-shot 3-player PD with identical cooperation-stage payoffs and high-sanction punishment structure as the main high-sanction treatment. Key difference is elicitation: in each period subjects observe actual cooperate/defect decisions of others and then choose punishment (specific response method), rather than providing contingent strategies for all possible profiles.",Summarizes the specific response control as described in Section 3.3.,1
10.1371_journal.pcbi.1000758.md,Square lattice spatial public goods game with punishment (baseline condition),"Main simulation setup described in Methods: agents on square lattice with k=4 neighbors, four strategies C, D, M, I, with parameters r (synergy), β (fine), γ (cost) varied over ranges.",0.9,"Synergy factor r, punishment fine β, punishment cost γ; spatial interaction on square lattice with k=4 nearest neighbors; stochastic imitation noise K=0.5",These parameters are systematically varied (phase diagrams Fig.1) and define the conditions of interest.,1,False,Study uses agent-based computer simulations with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors repeatedly state they simulate the public goods game on lattices and networks.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution presented; focus is on numerical simulations.,0.9,40000,Methods: fully occupied lattice L×L with L=200…1200 for Fig.1. Baseline parameter scans appear to use L=200 (40000 agents); choose representative baseline size.,0.4,10000000,They describe up to 10^7 iterations (each involving L^2 updates) near phase boundaries; use 10^7 as representative long-run simulation length.,0.4,1,"Each cooperative individual (C, M) contributes exactly 1; defectors contribute 0. This is effectively all-or-nothing rather than continuous choice.",1,N/A,No human endowment or default contribution state; purely simulated strategies.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return not explicitly named; could be r/(k+1) but exact value varies with r and is not fixed for a single condition.,0.2,N/A,No communication or messaging among simulated agents.,1,0,Agents update based on local payoffs only; no global summary is displayed or modeled.,0.9,0,Model has anonymous interactions on a lattice; no identity tracking or revelation in punishment.,0.9,0,There is no reward mechanism at all; only punishment.,1,0,Agents follow fixed imitation dynamics; they are not given information about remaining iterations.,0.9,1,Core of the model is costly punishment of defectors by M and I strategies.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost parameter γ is varied (e.g., 0.005–0.3); no single baseline numeric value for this aggregated condition.",0.2,N/R,"Impact per unit of punishment β/k is determined by β; β is varied, so no single value for this aggregated condition.",0.2,0,Only punishment is modeled; no explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes 1; this is the effective per-round endowment for potential contribution.,0.8,N/R,"They report fractions of strategy types (C,M,D,I) but no explicit aggregate contribution rate across all agents as a number.",0.2,N/R,Individual contributions are fixed by strategy (1 or 0); no aggregate contribution amount over time or per group is reported numerically.,0.2,N/R,They qualitatively discuss system behavior and prevalence of strategies but do not provide normalized efficiency values.,0.1,N/R,Payoff formulas are given but no specific numeric average group payoff is reported.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; agents occupy lattice sites.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computer simulations without human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game with four strategies (C, D, M, I) on square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; group size k+1=5 (focal plus 4 neighbors). Payoffs use synergy factor r on sum of contributions, punishment fine β/k from each punisher to defectors (D,I), and punishment cost γ/k for each punisher (M,I) per defecting neighbor. Strategy updating via pairwise imitation with Fermi rule parameter K=0.5 and random sequential updates.",Summarizes detailed game structure and dynamics from Methods section.,1
10.1371_journal.pcbi.1000758.md,"Square lattice condition: r=4.4, β=0.1, γ=0.1 (elimination of second-order free-riders)",Specific parameter triple repeatedly described and illustrated in Fig.2 and Video S1 as representative case.,1,Synergy factor r=4.4; punishment fine β=0.1; punishment cost γ=0.1; spatial interaction on square lattice (k=4) with noise K=0.5,These are explicitly given values for this simulation condition.,1,False,"Agent-based simulation, no human subjects.",1,False,"Computational model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,Not run in a human lab setting.,1,True,Authors simulate the dynamics on a square lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivation for this condition.,0.9,10000,"Fig.2 states snapshots for lattice size L=100, so 100×100 agents.",1,2000,Fig.2 shows snapshots up to t=2000 for this parameter set; that is the illustrated horizon.,0.9,1,"C and M contribute 1, D and I contribute 0, a binary contribution choice.",1,N/A,No human endowment state; strategies determine contributions.,1,0.88,Group size is k+1=5; MPCR = r/(k+1) = 4.4/5 = 0.88.,0.9,N/A,No communication among agents.,1,0,Agents observe only neighbor payoffs for imitation; no global statistics.,0.9,0,Punishment is based solely on local strategies; identities not modeled.,0.9,0,No rewards in the model.,1,0,Agents do not know or respond to time horizon.,0.9,1,M and I impose punishment fines β/k on D and I.,1,0.1,Punishment cost parameter γ is given as 0.1 for this condition.,1,0.025,"Each punisher inflicts β/k per punished defector; here β=0.1, k=4, so β/k=0.025.",0.9,0,Model includes punishment only.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes 1; effective per-round resource to allocate.,0.9,N/R,They qualitatively show that final state is majority moralists with some defectors; no precise numeric fraction of contributors reported.,0.3,N/R,No aggregate contribution totals per group or per round are provided numerically.,0.2,N/R,No normalized efficiency of total payoffs vs. maximum is given for this parameter set.,0.1,N/R,"Payoff expressions are given, but no numeric averages over time/groups are reported.",0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is an agent-based computational simulation.,1,"Initial condition: each of four strategies C, D, M, I occupies 25% of sites uniformly. Agents on 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundary conditions, Moore neighborhood (k=4). Each step: random focal agent x plays in 5 groups (own and four neighbors), computing total payoff, then a randomly chosen neighbor y imitates x’s strategy with Fermi probability q=1/(1+exp[(P_y−P_x)/K]) with K=0.5.",Directly summarized from Methods and Fig.2 description for this parameter set.,1
10.1371_journal.pcbi.1000758.md,"Square lattice condition: r=3.5, β=0.12, γ=0.005 (moralists–immoralists coexistence)",Specific parameter combination used in Fig.3 and Video S3 to demonstrate coexistence of moralists and immoralists.,1,Synergy factor r=3.5; punishment fine β=0.12; punishment cost γ=0.005; spatial interaction on square lattice (k=4) with noise K=0.5,Explicit numeric values provided in Fig.3 caption.,1,False,Agent-based simulation only.,1,False,"Simulation, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No human lab context.,1,True,Public goods dynamics implemented via computer program.,1,False,No purely analytical treatment for this condition.,0.9,10000,Fig.3 and Video S3 mention grid size L×L=100×100.,1,12000,Fig.3 shows snapshots from t=0 up to t=12000; use last shown time as simulation horizon for this condition.,0.9,1,"Binary contribution: 1 for C/M, 0 for D/I.",1,N/A,No human endowment representation.,1,0.7,MPCR = r/(k+1) = 3.5/5 = 0.7.,0.9,N/A,No communication channel modeled.,1,0,Imitation based only on neighbor payoffs.,0.9,0,No explicit identities or reputation; only strategies matter.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Time horizon not observed by agents.,0.9,1,Both M and I punish defectors.,1,0.005,Punishment cost parameter γ for this condition is 0.005.,1,0.03,"Fine per punisher per defector is β/k; with β=0.12 and k=4, β/k=0.03.",0.9,0,Only punishment is used.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,1,Contribution per cooperator is 1; interpreted as full endowment.,0.9,N/R,"They state final configuration is coexistence of clusters of moralists and immoralists, implying some average contribution rate, but no numeric value is reported.",0.3,N/R,No numeric totals or averages of contributions are given.,0.2,N/R,No explicit performance or efficiency measure beyond qualitative descriptions.,0.1,N/R,No average group payoff reported for this parameter combination.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based model.,1,"Initial state: each of C, D, M, I occupies 25% of the 100×100 lattice. Over time, cooperators die out, defectors prevail transiently, then punishing strategies (M and I) jointly defeat defectors, leading to coexistence of M and I clusters after ~12000 iterations. Dynamics and updating rules are as in the general Methods (Fermi imitation with K=0.5, random sequential update).",Summarizes Fig.3 description for this parameter set.,1
