10.1038_nature16980.md,Local co-religionist game – all sites,Paper describes one random allocation game where each decision is between a local co-religionist and a distant co-religionist; no between-subject treatment differences in the game structure are reported.,0.9,Role of recipient for each cup: local anonymous co-religionist vs geographically distant anonymous co-religionist of same ethnicity and nationality as participant,"In this game, one cup is for a local co-religionist, the other for a distant co-religionist; this is the within-subject factor determining the allocation of each coin.",1,True,Human participants from eight communities completed behavioral allocation games.,1,True,"Controlled behavioral economic game with specified rules, anonymity, and pre-defined payoffs.",1,False,Games were played in field sites (remote communities) rather than in a conventional lab setting.,0.9,False,No computer simulations or agent-based models described.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model is presented as a distinct study.,1,1,"Each participant allocates coins alone; allocations affect payoffs to themselves and recipients, but there is no strategic interaction among simultaneous players.",0.9,30,“Participants play in private with 30 coins… The participant's job is to allocate each coin…”. This constitutes 30 allocation trials (rounds) per game.,1,1,"For each coin, participants choose between two cups; effectively a binary (all-or-nothing per coin) decision rather than contributing a continuous fraction of a coin.",0.8,0,All coins start under participant control; nothing is pre-allocated to any cup.,0.9,N/R,"This is not a linear public goods game; each coin simply transfers 1:1 to a designated recipient with no multiplier, and there is no defined group size sharing a common pool.",0.5,0,Game described as played in private with no mention of communication or discussion among participants.,0.9,0,"No mention of participants seeing others’ allocations, earnings, or punishment histories; anonymity is emphasized.",0.9,0,No punishment mechanism is part of the game.,1,0,Transfers are simple allocations to anonymous recipients; no separate reward mechanism with identity revelation.,0.9,1,Participants are given 30 coins to allocate; thus they know the finite number of allocation opportunities.,0.8,0,The game involves only positive allocations of coins; no option to deduct or punish others.,1,N/A,No punishment option is available in the game.,1,N/A,No punishment option is available in the game.,1,1,Allocating a coin to a cup gives a monetary benefit to the represented person; this is a positive transfer (reward-like) decision.,0.8,1,"Each coin allocated to another person costs the participant that coin, which could otherwise have gone to themselves or their local co-religionist (opportunity cost per coin is 1 coin).",0.7,1,Coins are passed 1:1 to recipients; no multiplier is mentioned.,0.9,30,Each game starts with 30 coins for allocation.,1,N/R,"Paper reports mean number of coins to distant co-religionist (e.g., for subgroups) but not a single aggregate proportion suitable for this row; also behavior is framed as allocations, not contributions to a public pool.",0.5,N/R,Overall mean or distribution of coins allocated to any single target across all participants is not explicitly reported in the main text.,0.4,N/R,No efficiency measure (ratio of realized to maximum payoffs) is reported or directly computable given missing aggregate payoff information.,0.3,N/R,Total payoffs per group/community are not reported.,0.3,"Multiple (Vanuatu, Fiji, Brazil, Mauritius, Russia, Tanzania)","Participants come from eight field sites across six countries: inland and coastal Tanna (Vanuatu), Yasawa and Lovu (Fiji), Pesqueiro (Brazil), Pointe aux Piments (Mauritius), Tyva Republic (Russia), and Hadzaland (Tanzania).",1,Mean ages by site range from approximately 33.5 to 44.6 years,"Table 1 reports mean ages and standard deviations for each site (e.g., 35.02(14.13) for coastal Tanna, 44.56(16.94) for Lovu).",1,Mixed; approximately 310 females out of 591 participants,Abstract notes 591 participants and 310 females; Table 1 shows female counts by site.,1,N/R,Education is mentioned as a control variable in regressions but no descriptive education statistics are given in the main text.,0.4,Field experiment,"Data were collected in eight natural community settings (foragers, horticulturalists, etc.) using standardized game protocols; this fits a field experimental design.",0.9,"Random allocation game with 30 independent coin allocations using a private mental choice and a fair die (3 sides of one colour, 3 of another); if the die matches the mentally chosen colour, the coin goes to the chosen cup, otherwise to the opposite cup. Recipients are an anonymous local co-religionist and an anonymous geographically distant co-religionist of the same ethnicity and nationality. Two such games are played per participant with counterbalanced order.",Direct description in the Methods section and figure caption describing the game mechanics and anonymity.,1
10.1038_nature16980.md,Self game – all sites,"Second random allocation game described, structurally identical except that one cup represents the participant (self) and the other a distant co-religionist.",0.9,Role of recipient for each cup: self vs geographically distant anonymous co-religionist of same ethnicity and nationality as participant,"In this game, one cup is explicitly for the participant themselves, and the other for a distant co-religionist; this defines the within-subject allocation choice.",1,True,Same human participant pool as for the local co-religionist game.,1,True,Controlled random allocation task with defined payoffs and anonymity.,1,False,"Conducted in field communities, not in a conventional laboratory.",0.9,False,No simulation component described.,1,False,No standalone analytical model study.,1,1,Each participant makes allocation decisions individually without direct interaction.,0.9,30,Participants again allocate 30 coins in this second game.,1,1,Each decision is an all-or-nothing assignment of one coin to one of two cups.,0.8,0,"All 30 coins start under participant control, unassigned.",0.9,N/R,Not a public goods game; each coin is a 1:1 transfer to a single specified recipient.,0.5,0,Game is played privately with no mention of communication.,0.9,0,No information on others’ outcomes is provided.,0.9,0,There is no punishment mechanism.,1,0,Recipients are anonymous; no identity revelation is mentioned.,0.9,1,Participants know they have 30 coins for the game.,0.8,0,Only positive transfers are allowed.,1,N/A,No punishment action exists.,1,N/A,No punishment action exists.,1,1,Allocations give coins (benefits) to self or distant co-religionists.,0.8,1,Allocating a coin to the distant co-religionist costs the participant that coin (could have gone to self).,0.7,1,One coin allocated yields one coin to the recipient.,0.9,30,Same per-game endowment of 30 coins.,1,N/R,"Means for allocations to distant co-religionists under specific belief categories are reported (e.g., for those who say god punishes vs those who do not know), but not a single overall normalized rate across all participants.",0.5,N/R,Overall mean number of coins sent to distant co-religionists across all participants is not reported.,0.4,N/R,No reported aggregate payoffs or efficiency ratios.,0.3,N/R,No aggregate or per-group payoff figures given.,0.3,"Multiple (Vanuatu, Fiji, Brazil, Mauritius, Russia, Tanzania)",Same multi-site sample as for the local co-religionist game.,1,Mean ages by site range from approximately 33.5 to 44.6 years,Same field sites and age distributions as reported in Table 1.,1,Mixed; approximately 310 females out of 591 participants,Same participant pool as in the first game.,1,N/R,Education is only referenced as a control in regressions; no descriptive education data are reported.,0.4,Field experiment,Same multi-site field setting and procedure as the other game.,0.9,"Identical random allocation procedure to the local co-religionist game: 30 independent coin allocations using a fair die and a private mental cup choice. In this version, recipients are the participant themselves (self cup) versus an anonymous geographically distant co-religionist of the same ethnicity and nationality. Games are counterbalanced in order across participants.","Directly described as a second, counterbalanced game differing only in the identity of the two recipients.",1
10.1038_nature07601.md,Analytical model of indirect reciprocity with costly punishment,"The paper develops and analyzes a formal model (action rules, social norms, evolutionary stability, efficiency) rather than distinct named experimental conditions with human subjects.",0.95,"Action rule (CD: cooperate with good, defect against bad; CP: cooperate with good, punish bad; DD: unconditional defection), social norm mapping (64 possible norms with second-order assessment), and parameter set (b: benefit of cooperation, c: cost of cooperation, α: cost of punishment, β: effect of punishment, q: social resolution 1−2μ). This row summarizes the full analytical exploration; specific numerical examples include (b=2,c=1,α=0.5,β=2) and variants such as β=2.5.","Independent variables are the elements the authors systematically vary in their analysis: action rules, social norms, and payoff/error parameters (b,c,α,β,q). Numerical examples are explicitly given in text.",0.98,False,The study is purely theoretical with no human participants; it analyzes evolutionary game-theoretic models and simulations.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects or field data; only theoretical analysis and computer simulations of model dynamics.,1,False,No laboratory or field experiment; the work is mathematical and computational.,1,True,"Authors state they performed computer simulations in heterogeneous populations of finite size to test analytical calculations; all agents are modelled, not human.",0.96,True,Core results come from analytical derivations over all 9×64 combinations and stability/efficiency analysis.,1,2,"Model interactions are between a randomly chosen donor and a recipient; i.e., pairwise games.",0.9,1,Each interaction is a single encounter; evolutionary dynamics occur over generations but the game itself is a one-shot donor-recipient interaction.,0.9,1,"Players choose an action (C, D, or P) rather than a continuous amount; contribution is inherently all-or-nothing at the level of the action choice.",0.95,N/A,There is no endowment allocation decision to a public vs private account; only binary actions with specified costs/benefits.,1,N/R,Model is not framed as a linear public goods game; MPCR is never defined or implied.,1,N/A,No human subjects; model agents do not have free-form communication beyond the formalized gossip/reputation process.,1,N/R,No user-interface or feedback structure is specified; payoffs and reputations are implicit in the model.,1,N/A,No human players or identities; reputations are global or private lists without personal identifiers in an experimental sense.,1,N/A,"There is no reward mechanism, only cooperation, defection, and punishment.",1,N/A,No experimental interface; number of evolutionary generations is not presented to agents.,1,1,A core model feature is the option P (punish) with costs α to the donor and β to the recipient.,1,N/R,"Cost of punishment α is treated as a symbolic parameter; specific numerical values (e.g., α=0.5, 0.8, 1.2) are used illustratively but not fixed globally for the model.",0.7,N/R,"Effect of punishment β is a symbolic parameter with several numeric examples (e.g., β=2, 2.5, 3), not one fixed setting.",0.7,0,"Only cooperation (benefit b, cost c) and punishment are modeled; there is no distinct costly reward operator.",0.95,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model specifies costs and benefits per interaction (c, b, α, β) but no initial endowment in tokens or money.",1,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as evolutionary stability conditions and average payoffs, not as normalized contribution fractions.",1,N/R,"The model uses actions (C, D, P) with fixed costs/benefits; no varying contribution amounts are reported as outcome variables.",1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed conceptually as 'most efficient equilibrium' with highest average payoff, but no numeric efficiency ratio is given.",0.9,N/R,"Only qualitative ordering of average payoffs under CD, CP, and DD is provided; no explicit payoff numbers.",0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of analytical modelling and computer simulations without human or field data.,1,"Donor-recipient indirect reciprocity game with binary reputation (G/B). Donor chooses one of three actions: cooperate (cost c, benefit b to recipient), defect (no cost/benefit), or punish (cost α to donor, cost β to recipient). Social norms are second-order: mapping from (donor action, recipient reputation) to new donor reputation. Reputation updating is erroneous with probability μ, giving social resolution q=1−2μ. Action rules (9 total) map recipient reputation to action; focal rules include CD (C to G, D to B), CP (C to G, P to B), and DD (always D). Evolutionary stability and efficiency of these rules are analyzed over all 64 norms and parameter space (b,c,α,β,q). Gossip and individual reputation lists are examined in simulations as mechanisms to restore coherence under private errors.",Summarizes the structure and payoff definitions of the theoretical game and simulations as described in text and methods summary.,0.98
10.1073_pnas.0504902103.md,Climate public goods – Well-informed – Nonanonymous rounds,Condition defined by treatment (well-informed vs little-informed) and climate game anonymity (nonanonymous vs anonymous); this row is well-informed & nonanonymous.,1,"Information: well-informed (received expert climate information); Anonymity: nonanonymous (pseudonyms shown, contributions linked to identity and observable for reputation)",Paper defines two between-group treatments (well-informed vs little-informed) and within-session alternation of anonymous vs nonanonymous climate rounds.,1,True,Human undergraduates in laboratory experiment.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized group assignment and specified treatments.",1,True,Conducted with students at Hamburg University using a computerized lab setup and public screen.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model developed; only empirical analysis.,1,6,Each experimental group consisted of 6 subjects.,1,5,"They played 20 rounds total alternating indirect reciprocity and climate public goods; thus 10 climate rounds, of which 5 are nonanonymous and 5 anonymous.",0.9,0,"In each climate round, players could contribute €0, €1, or €2, i.e., more than two discrete contribution levels and not just full/none.",1,0,Each participant had a starting account of €12; contributions were voluntary deductions from this private account.,1,N/R,"Total contributions were doubled and then transferred to an external climate account, not divided among players; no individual marginal per capita return is defined.",0.2,0,"No communication allowed; subjects separated by partitions, decisions via switches, no mention of chat.",1,1,"In nonanonymous climate rounds, each player's decision and contribution were displayed below their pseudonym for 20 seconds to all players.",1,0,No punishment stage or sanctions mechanism in the climate public goods game; only contributions displayed.,1,0,No explicit positive reward mechanism in climate game; rewards occur only indirectly via the separate indirect reciprocity game.,1,0,Players were told they would play 20 rounds but did not know this number; suggests no display of total or remaining rounds on screen.,0.8,0,No punishment option or costful sanctions within the climate public goods stage.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism implemented.,1,0,No direct monetary reward action within the climate stage; indirect reciprocity game is a separate game.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward option in this stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward option in this stage.,1,12,Each person had a starting account of €12.,1,0.944,"Cooperation is defined as investing either €1 or €2. Average cooperation level in nonanonymous climate rounds for well-informed groups is 94.4%, so normalized rate is 0.944.",0.9,12.8,"Well-informed groups gave €76.8 ± 3.1 in five nonanonymous rounds per group of 6. Average per group per round: 76.8 / 5 = 15.36. Per player per round: 15.36 / 6 ≈ 2.56. As each cooperator can give up to €2, this suggests a mean actual amount around €2.56*? However maximum per player per round is €2, so 76.8 over 5 rounds and 6 players implies 76.8 / (5*6) = 2.56, which exceeds the max, likely reflecting sum over 13 groups. Thus raw average per group across all 5 rounds is 76.8; we record that group-level amount per 5 rounds.",0.3,N/R,"No comparison to a theoretical maximum group payoff reported; funds go to external climate account, not to players.",0.1,N/R,Paper does not report final monetary payoffs to groups; only amounts invested to the climate pool.,0.1,Germany,Students from Hamburg University (Germany) and press ad in a German newspaper.,1,N/R,"Participants are undergraduates, but no age range or mean reported.",0.4,N/R,Paper does not report gender composition.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Described as undergraduates from Hamburg University.,1,On site,Computerized lab-style experiment at the university with subjects physically present in groups.,1,"Public goods game on climate where each of 6 players per group chooses to contribute €0, €1, or €2 from a €12 endowment to a climate pool each climate round. Total contributions are doubled and transferred to an external climate account to fund a press advertisement about climate change. Climate game alternates with an indirect reciprocity game (6 subrounds per round) in which donors can give €1.50 (doubled to €3 for receiver). In nonanonymous climate rounds, pseudonyms and full contribution histories (including previous nonanonymous climate and indirect reciprocity rounds) are visible.",Summarized from Methods sections describing both games and their alternation.,1
10.1073_pnas.0504902103.md,Climate public goods – Well-informed – Anonymous rounds,Condition defined by well-informed information treatment and anonymous climate rounds.,1,"Information: well-informed (received expert climate information); Anonymity: anonymous (no pseudonyms, contributions not linkable to identity or history)","Every second group is well-informed; within game, every second climate round is anonymous with no pseudonyms shown.",1,True,Same human-subjects lab experiment as other conditions.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of information and anonymity.,1,True,Conducted on-site with computers in Hamburg University.,1,False,No purely computational study.,1,False,No formal proofs or models.,1,6,Groups of 6 subjects.,1,5,Five anonymous climate public goods rounds per 20-round session.,0.9,0,"Contribution choices are €0, €1, or €2, not a strict all-or-nothing structure.",1,0,Endowment is in private account; contributions are voluntary.,1,N/R,Public pool's doubled amount is not redistributed; no defined monetary marginal per capita return.,0.2,0,No communication options described.,1,1,"In anonymous rounds, a list of the six contributions (without pseudonyms) is displayed to all players.",1,0,No punishment implemented.,1,0,No explicit reward option in the climate stage.,1,0,Subjects did not know total number of rounds; no indication that remaining rounds were shown.,0.8,0,No punishment opportunity for climate contributions.,1,N/A,No punishment in this stage.,1,N/A,No punishment in this stage.,1,0,No direct rewarding mechanism in the climate game.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward option.,1,N/A,No explicit reward option.,1,12,Each person had a starting account of €12.,1,0.546,Average cooperation level (investing €1 or €2) in anonymous climate rounds for well-informed groups was 54.6%.,0.9,8.04,Well-informed groups gave €40.2 ± 3.7 in five anonymous rounds per group. Average per group per 5 rounds is 40.2; per round is 40.2 / 5 = 8.04.,0.9,N/R,No efficiency metric versus maximum group payoff reported.,0.1,N/R,Final monetary earnings for groups or players are not reported.,0.1,Germany,Students from Hamburg University in Germany.,1,N/R,Only described as undergraduates; no ages given.,0.4,N/R,No gender data reported.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Participants were undergraduates from Hamburg University.,1,On site,Conducted physically at Hamburg University with lab equipment.,1,"Same climate public goods game structure as other conditions: 6 players each climate round choose €0, €1, or €2 to invest; total is doubled and transferred to an external climate account financing a press ad. In anonymous rounds, individual decisions and amounts are displayed without pseudonyms and randomly ordered; decisions from anonymous rounds are never shown elsewhere and cannot affect reputations in the indirect reciprocity game.",Summarized from the Methods description of anonymous rounds.,1
