10.1126_science.1145850.md,Mini-ultimatum game – 2/8 vs 8/2 condition,"Paper describes four mini-ultimatum games played by the same chimpanzee pairs: 2/8, 5/5, 8/2, and 10/0 each versus 8/2. This row refers to the game where the alternative to 8/2 is 2/8 (unfair vs hyperfair).",1,Game structure: proposer choice between 8/2 (unfair) and 2/8 (hyperfair) offers; responder can accept or reject chosen offer.,Independent variable is the pair of payoff options available to the proposer; this condition uses 8/2 vs 2/8 as specified in the mini-ultimatum description.,1,True,Chimpanzees are live animal subjects in a behavioral game.,1,True,Researchers manipulate payoff options and record behavior under controlled conditions.,1,True,Conducted at Wolfgang Köhler Primate Research Center with captive group-housed chimpanzees.,1,False,No computational or agent-based models; only live subjects.,1,False,No purely mathematical model or derivations as primary method.,1,2,Ultimatum game with one proposer and one responder per trial.,1,1,Each game is a single-shot mini-ultimatum interaction per trial; repeated trials but the strategic structure is one-round. Use 1 for stage game length.,0.6,0,"Proposers choose between two discrete pre-set allocations, not a continuous contribution; not an all-or-nothing contribution structure as in public goods, but discrete choice between two bundles, so code 0.",0.6,N/A,"Ultimatum game over dividing windfall food, not a public goods contribution problem.",1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no multiplier of a group account is defined.,1,0,No mention of communication; animals separated in cages and interact only via apparatus.,0.8,0,No indication of feedback about cumulative payoffs; only immediate food outcomes are experienced.,0.8,0,No punishment stage exists; thus no punishment identities to show.,1,0,No explicit reward stage; only division of food.,1,0,No information reported about number of trials being displayed to chimpanzees.,0.5,0,Standard ultimatum structure with accept/reject of allocations; no costly third-party or peer punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding mechanism beyond receiving food from the tray.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"Total raisins per tray are implicitly 10 (e.g., 8/2, 5/5, 10/0 all sum to 10).",1,N/R,Not a contribution game; no normalized contribution fraction reported.,1,N/R,"Paper reports frequencies of offer choices and rejections, not amounts relative to an endowment the proposer decides to contribute.",1,N/R,No aggregate payoff ratios reported or derivable per condition from text alone.,0.3,N/R,Only rejection and choice frequencies are presented; no mean total payoff per pair is given.,0.3,Germany,"Chimpanzees housed at Wolfgang Köhler Primate Research Center in Leipzig, Germany.",1,Mixed-age adult chimpanzees; exact ages N/R,Paper mentions only that subjects were 11 chimpanzees; specific ages not listed in the excerpt.,0.4,N/R,No sex composition of chimpanzees reported in provided text.,0.1,N/A,Non-human animals; formal education not applicable.,1,On site,Conducted with captive chimpanzees at a zoo-based research center (not online or field in wild).,1,"Mini-ultimatum game with discrete choice. Proposer pulls one of two baited trays (8/2 vs 2/8 raisins). Responder can accept by pulling rod within 1 minute, bringing tray to both cages, or reject by not pulling, leading to removal of all food. Games: 2/8 (hyperfair to responder) vs 8/2 (unfair). Order of four game types was counterbalanced across subjects. Same 11 chimpanzees played both proposer and responder roles across sessions.",Summarizes procedural details specific to this condition drawn from the methods description.,1
10.1126_science.1145850.md,Mini-ultimatum game – 5/5 vs 8/2 condition,This row refers to the mini-ultimatum game where the alternative to 8/2 is 5/5 (fair game).,1,Game structure: proposer choice between 8/2 (unfair) and 5/5 (fair) offers; responder can accept or reject chosen offer.,Independent variable is the structure of payoff options; this condition uses 8/2 vs 5/5.,1,True,Same chimpanzee behavioral experiment as other mini-ultimatum games.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of payoff options; behavior recorded.,1,True,Conducted at primate research center.,1,False,No computational model.,1,False,No analytical-only study.,1,2,"One proposer, one responder.",1,1,Each trial is a one-shot game; repeated trials not structured as multi-round strategic game.,0.6,0,"Discrete choice between two fixed allocations, not a continuous or binary contribution decision as in public goods.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution/public goods game.,1,N/R,Ultimatum division game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No reported communication capability.,0.8,0,No cumulative payoff information is mentioned.,0.8,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,0,No evidence that subjects see how many trials remain.,0.5,0,"Only accept/reject of offers, no separate costly punishment.",1,N/A,Punishment absent.,1,N/A,Punishment absent.,1,0,No additional reward technology beyond receiving food allocation.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,10,"Total food per tray sums to 10 raisins (8/2, 5/5, 10/0 combinations).",1,N/R,No contribution measure.,1,N/R,"Study reports proposer choice frequencies and rejection rates, not contributions from an endowment.",1,N/R,No explicit or easily derivable efficiency statistics provided in text.,0.3,N/R,Aggregate payoff outcomes per condition not reported.,0.3,Germany,Same German research facility for all four games.,1,Mixed-age adult chimpanzees; exact ages N/R,Ages not specified in excerpt.,0.4,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0.1,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Lab-style testing at zoo-based research center.,1,Mini-ultimatum game with proposer choosing between 8 raisins for self/2 for partner vs 5/5 division. Responder can accept chosen offer by pulling rod within 1 minute or reject (no pull) resulting in zero for both. Order of the four game structures counterbalanced; same apparatus (two trays with separated dishes) used.,Condition-specific description extracted from methods and game overview.,1
10.1126_science.1145850.md,Mini-ultimatum game – 8/2 vs 8/2 condition (no-choice),This row refers to game where both proposer options are identical: 8/2 vs 8/2 (no-choice condition).,1,Game structure: proposer faced with 8/2 vs 8/2 offers (no alternative); responder can accept or reject offer.,"Independent variable is payoff options; here both are 8/2 as specified in text (""8/2 versus 8/2 (no choice)"").",1,True,Same empirical chimpanzee game.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of choice set (no-choice between identical options).,1,True,Conducted in captive research setting.,1,False,No computer simulation involved.,1,False,Not an analytical model study.,1,2,One proposer and one responder per trial.,1,1,Each interaction is a single accept/reject stage game.,0.6,0,"Discrete choice of tray, but since both are 8/2, there is effectively no decision; still not a contribution setting.",0.6,N/A,No contribution/public goods framing.,1,N/R,"Ultimatum-like division, no MPCR.",1,0,No communication mechanism described.,0.8,0,No feedback on cumulative outcomes mentioned.,0.8,0,No punishment.,1,0,No separate reward technology.,1,0,Subjects not reported to receive information on total or remaining trials.,0.5,0,"Only accept/reject, no costly punishment stage.",1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,10,8/2 divisions imply total of 10 raisins per tray as in other games.,1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Choices and rejections reported instead of contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics for this condition not reported.,0.3,N/R,Mean payoff per pair not given.,0.3,Germany,"Same facility in Leipzig, Germany.",1,Mixed-age adult chimpanzees; exact ages N/R,Ages not specified.,0.4,N/R,Sex of chimpanzees not reported.,0.1,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Laboratory-style testing in zoo/primate center.,1,"Mini-ultimatum game where both available trays provide 8 raisins to proposer and 2 to responder (8/2 vs 8/2). Proposer still pulls a tray first; responder can accept by pulling rod within 1 minute to receive 2 raisins or reject by not pulling, resulting in zero payoff for both. Used to test responses when proposer has no alternative.",Summarizes specifics for 8/2 vs 8/2 condition from methods text.,1
10.1126_science.1145850.md,Mini-ultimatum game – 10/0 vs 8/2 condition,"This row refers to mini-ultimatum game where alternative to 8/2 is 10/0 (hyperunfair, responder gets nothing).",1,"Game structure: proposer choice between 8/2 (unfair) and 10/0 (hyperunfair, zero to responder); responder can accept or reject chosen offer.",Independent variable is pair of payoff options; here 8/2 vs 10/0 as described.,1,True,Live chimpanzee behavioral experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of payoff menu in lab setting.,1,True,Conducted at Wolfgang Köhler Primate Research Center.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical work as main method.,1,2,Proposer and responder.,1,1,One-shot accept/reject per trial.,0.6,0,Discrete choice between two fixed allocations; not a contribution game.,0.6,N/A,No public goods contribution.,1,N/R,Not applicable outside public goods.,1,0,No mention of communication allowed.,0.8,0,No cumulative payoff summaries for subjects.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,0,Not reported that subjects were informed about number of trials.,0.5,0,"Standard ultimatum accept/reject, no explicit punishment stage.",1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward technology beyond immediate food allocation.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,10,Options 8/2 and 10/0 both sum to 10 raisins per tray.,1,N/R,No contribution behavior defined.,1,N/R,Study focuses on proposer choice frequencies and rejection rates.,1,N/R,No efficiency statistics reported per condition.,0.3,N/R,Mean pair payoffs per game not given.,0.3,Germany,Same chimpanzee colony in Leipzig.,1,Mixed-age adult chimpanzees; exact ages N/R,Specific ages not reported.,0.4,N/R,Sex of animals not specified in excerpt.,0.1,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Lab-like testing in a zoo-based research center.,1,"Mini-ultimatum game where proposer chooses between 8/2 (8 raisins for self, 2 for partner) and 10/0 (10 for self, 0 for partner). Responder can accept chosen option by pulling rod within 1 minute or reject by not pulling, leading to zero for both. Used to test whether rejections of unfair offers depend on availability of even more selfish alternative.",Summarizes procedural aspects particular to 10/0 vs 8/2 game from methods and results discussion.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0262.2004.00511.x.md,"C condition (complete contract, enforceable effort, fixed IDs)","Paper defines three treatment conditions: C (complete contract), ICF, and ICR. This row is for the C condition.",1,"Third-party enforcement present; effort third-party enforceable; fixed IDs allow long-term relations; 7 firms, 10 workers, 15 periods; firms can make public or private offers.",C condition is characterized by complete contracts with third-party enforcement and fixed IDs enabling repeated matching.,1,True,Human subjects (students) participated in computerized market experiments.,1,True,Controlled laboratory market with randomized assignment to treatments across sessions.,1,True,Conducted in university labs in Zurich using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results from human-subject experiments.,1,False,"Paper includes theoretical discussion but main design/results are experimental; this row is for the experiment, not the appendix model.",1,17,Each session had 10 workers and 7 firms.,1,15,All treatments had 15 trading periods.,1,1,"This is not a contribution game; effort is chosen from discrete set {1,...,10}. All-or-nothing contribution concept does not apply; coded as 1 since any feasible effort level can be chosen.",0.7,N/R,No endowment allocation between private/public accounts; not a public goods game.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No mention of communication between subjects beyond offers; only structured contract offers allowed.,0.9,1,Each participant was informed each period about his own and trading partner's payoff and ID. Firms also continuously observed public offers of competitors and workers saw all public offers; these are summary-like information of others’ contracts.,0.8,0,No explicit punishment mechanism (only firing); no IDs tied to explicit punishment decisions.,1,0,No explicit costly reward stage aside from wage offers; no separate reward IDs.,1,1,Horizon of 15 periods was common knowledge to all participants.,1,0,"No explicit punishment technology (only endogenous firing/termination, which is not modeled as a separate costly punishment choice).",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward technology beyond wage choice embedded in contract; no separate stage with reward points.,0.9,N/A,No explicit costly reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward technology.,1,N/R,"Payoffs determined by wage, effort cost, and unemployment benefit; no per-period token endowment is explicitly specified as such.",0.6,N/R,Not a contribution game; no normalized contribution rate reported.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are wages, effort, profits, and earnings; no contribution variable.",1,N/R,They discuss surplus and efficiency qualitatively but do not report a normalized efficiency index.,0.6,N/R,"Reported averages by side (firms vs workers) and rents, but no single aggregate group payoff per group per period.",0.5,Switzerland,"Subjects were students from the University of Zurich and ETH Zurich, both in Switzerland.",1,N/R,Age range or mean not reported.,1,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,1,University students,Subjects were students from the University of Zurich and the Federal Institute of Technology in Zurich.,1,On site,Computerized lab experiment with students physically present; z-Tree used.,1,"Bilateral labor-like market with 7 firms and 10 workers; 15 periods; firms post wage w and desired effort e~ (1-10), wages 1-100; in C condition the experimenter enforces agreed effort exactly; payoff firm: 10e - w if contract, 0 otherwise; payoff worker: w - c(e) if contract, 5 unemployment benefit otherwise; c(e) increasing from 0 at e=1 to 18 at e=10; firms can make multiple public or private offers; only matched pairs proceed to stage 2.","Summarizes the task, actions, and payoff functions specifically for C condition where effort is enforced.",1
10.1111_j.1468-0262.2004.00511.x.md,"ICF condition (incomplete contract, unenforceable effort, fixed IDs, long-term relations possible)",ICF is the incomplete contract condition with fixed IDs allowing endogenous long-term relations.,1,"Third-party enforcement absent; effort not third-party enforceable; fixed IDs allow long-term relations; 7 firms, 10 workers, 15 periods; firms can make public or private offers.",ICF identical to C except effort cannot be enforced by experimenter and long-term relations are possible via fixed IDs.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of contract enforceability and IDs.,1,True,Conducted with students in Zurich labs using z-Tree.,1,False,"Results are from human decisions, not simulations.",1,False,"Although an equilibrium is derived in the appendix, this row describes the experimental treatment.",1,17,10 workers and 7 firms per session.,1,15,Fifteen trading periods are common across treatments.,1,1,Workers can select any discrete effort level 1–10; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,N/R,Not a contribution/public goods setting.,1,N/R,No public good or linear contribution technology.,1,0,Design only mentions offers; no free-form chat or communication channel is described.,0.9,1,Workers see all public offers; firms see competitors’ public offers; both see own and partner payoffs and IDs each period.,0.8,0,"No separate punishment choice; only firing via not renewing contract, which is implicit.",1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond wage levels.,1,1,Finite horizon (15 periods) explicitly known.,1,0,"No explicit costly punishment technology is implemented, only endogenous firing.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit separate reward technology; wages are part of normal payoff structure.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward technology with per-unit costs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Worker’s outside option is 5 if not employed; not described as an explicit endowment.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome variable is effort, not contribution.",1,N/R,No contribution measure defined; effort is already normalized (1–10) but not reported as contribution.,1,N/R,"They discuss surplus, rents, and higher effort but do not compute an explicit efficiency ratio.",0.6,N/R,"They report firm profits and worker earnings per trade and rents over time, but no single aggregate group payoff number per period or treatment.",0.5,Switzerland,Same subject pool across treatments: students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich.,1,N/R,Age information not reported.,1,N/R,Gender breakdown not given.,1,University students,Subjects were students from two universities in Zurich.,1,On site,Computerized lab experiment with students physically present.,1,"Same market structure as C: 7 firms, 10 workers, 15 periods, wages 1–100, desired effort e~ in {1,…,10}. Payoffs: firm 10e − w if contract, 0 otherwise; worker w − c(e) if contract, 5 if unemployed. In ICF, effort is not enforced by experimenter; workers can choose any feasible e after observing contract. IDs are fixed across periods so firms can target specific workers with private offers, enabling endogenous long-term relationships. After each period, both parties learn (w, e~, actual e), both payoffs, and partner’s ID; firms also state expected effort and certainty in stage 2.","Summarizes distinguishing features of the ICF condition, especially unenforceable effort and possibility of relational contracts.",1
10.1111_j.1468-0262.2004.00511.x.md,"ICR condition (incomplete contract, unenforceable effort, random IDs, long-term relations impossible)",ICR is described as incomplete contract with random IDs (ICR = incomplete contract condition with random IDs).,1,"Third-party enforcement absent; effort not third-party enforceable; IDs randomly reassigned each period (no long-term relations); 7 firms, 10 workers, 15 periods; firms can make public or private offers.",ICR identical to ICF except long-term relations ruled out via random re-assignment of IDs each period.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment (4 sessions of ICR).,1,True,Exogenous manipulation of contract enforcement and ID randomization.,1,True,Run with student subjects using z-Tree in lab setting.,1,False,No mention of simulations; all data from experiments.,1,False,"This row describes experimental data, not the theoretical appendix.",1,17,Ten workers and seven firms in all three conditions.,1,15,Fifteen trading periods per session across all treatments.,1,1,"Workers choose any effort in {1,…,10}; not all-or-nothing.",1,N/R,No endowment allocation between private/public accounts.,1,N/R,Not a public goods or linear contribution game.,1,0,No experimental chat / communication channel is described.,0.9,1,Information structure same as ICF: workers see all public offers; firms see public offers; participants see partner’s payoff and ID from the period (even if IDs change next period).,0.8,0,No explicit punishment mechanic; only job denial in subsequent rounds.,1,0,No explicit costly reward stage beyond wages.,1,1,Finite horizon of 15 periods is common knowledge.,1,0,No explicit punishment technology or costly punishment tokens.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward technology apart from wages determined in contracts.,0.9,N/A,No explicit cost-per-reward-unit specified.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism separate from standard payoffs.,1,N/R,"Payoffs specified via wage, effort cost, and unemployment benefit; no separate token endowment reported.",0.7,N/R,No contribution variable; effort is main choice variable.,1,N/R,Study reports effort not as monetary contribution; no aggregate contribution data.,1,N/R,Only qualitative efficiency comparisons and some surplus/rent figures; no numeric efficiency ratio reported.,0.6,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not tabulated as a single metric per group per period.,0.5,Switzerland,Same pool of Zurich and ETH Zurich students as other treatments.,1,N/R,No ages provided.,1,N/R,Gender composition unspecified.,1,University students,Subjects are students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich.,1,On site,Same z-Tree laboratory setup as other treatments.,1,"Same basic contract game as ICF: 7 firms and 10 workers each period, 15 periods, wages 1–100, desired effort e~ in {1,…,10}. Payoffs: firm 10e − w (or 0 if no contract); worker w − c(e) or 5 if unemployed. Effort is not enforced; workers pick e after contract. In ICR, IDs are randomly re-assigned each period for both firms and workers, which prevents firms from targeting the same worker over time and rules out endogenous long-term relations. Firms can still make private and public offers, but a private offer in one period cannot systematically refer to the same human in the next period.",Captures identical payoff structure to ICF plus random ID feature that removes relational contracting.,1
10.1098_rsif.2020.0880.md,N/A – theoretical review only,"The paper is a narrative/theoretical review and modeling paper without any original experiment, dataset, or distinct conditions run by the authors.",1,N/A,No independent variables were manipulated by the authors; all empirical results discussed are from prior literature.,1,False,No human-participant data are collected; the paper reviews previous experiments and presents analytical utility formulations.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; only prior experimental work is summarized.,1,False,No new lab or field experiment is conducted.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported.,1,True,The core contribution is analytical: formal utility-function models of social and moral preferences.,1,0,No actual game is implemented with participants; discussion of games is purely conceptual.,1,0,No repeated interactions are run; all games are discussed abstractly.,1,0,No implemented contribution decision exists; coding as 0 by convention because no all-or-nothing constraint is instantiated.,0.5,N/A,No human game with an endowment/contribution decision is implemented.,1,N/R,"Although public goods games and Prisoner’s Dilemma are mentioned conceptually, no specific marginal per capita return is set for an implemented experiment.",1,N/A,"No games with human participants are run, so no communication protocol exists.",1,0,No user interface or feedback is implemented; set to 0 as there is no such feature.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is actually implemented with human subjects.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented with human subjects.,1,0,No rounds exist; thus nothing is shown.,1,0,"Altruistic punishment is discussed conceptually, but no punishment action is available in an implemented experiment.",1,N/A,No operational punishment technology is defined for a new experiment.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment multiplier or impact per unit is specified.,1,0,Rewards are mentioned as policy tools but not as an implemented game mechanism in this paper.,1,N/A,No concrete reward action is available in an implemented experiment.,1,N/A,No implemented reward multiplier or technology is specified.,1,0,The authors do not assign monetary endowments in any new experiment; examples are purely illustrative.,1,N/A,No contributions are observed; no dependent variables are measured on new data.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions are recorded.,1,N/A,No game outcomes are realized in data; efficiency is only discussed conceptually.,1,N/A,No actual payoffs are realized for participants.,1,N/A,No participants are recruited.,1,N/A,No participants are recruited.,1,N/A,No participants are recruited.,1,N/A,No participants are recruited.,1,No human,The work is analytical/theoretical and purely based on prior literature; it does not involve an empirical environment.,1,"The paper reviews standard economic games (dictator, Prisoner’s Dilemma, sender-receiver, trade-off game, trust game, ultimatum) and presents formal social and moral preference utility models, but does not instantiate any new experimental condition.",Summarized from sections describing game structures and from Table 5 on moral preference models.,1
10.2307_2297904.md,Theoretical random-matching repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma model with public randomization and contagious punishment,"Paper develops a single formal game-theoretic model (Section 2) with fixed payoffs g>0, l≥0, population size M≥4, anonymous random matching, and public random variable q_t; all propositions refer to this same theoretical environment rather than distinct experimental treatments.",0.83,"Population size M (even, ≥4), one-shot PD payoff parameters (g>0, l≥0), common discount factor δ, and punishment continuation probability q(δ) that determines expected punishment length.","Model explicitly varies M, g, l, and δ conceptually; q(δ) is an equilibrium design parameter chosen as a function of δ to make no-deviation constraints bind. These are the central parameters whose changes are analyzed (e.g., Table I, conditions for cooperation).",0.89,False,"No human subjects, no data collection; all results are theoretical and derived analytically.",1,False,Not a controlled experiment but a theoretical model and proofs.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation with participants; purely theoretical.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations are reported; only analytical derivations and some numerical illustrations of δ thresholds.,0.96,True,"Paper consists of formal game-theoretic analysis and proofs (sequential equilibria, lemmas, propositions) with no numerical simulation or empirical estimation.",1,4,"Model is defined for general M≥4 even (""M ≥ 4 is an even number""). Since no single M is fixed and results are parametric, a representative minimal allowed value (4) is used, but the model truly covers any such M.",0.4,0,"Infinite-horizon repeated game: time periods t∈{1,2,3,...}. Using 0 to indicate infinite/unbounded per instructions preference for numeric types.",1,0,"Stage game is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with binary actions C or D, so players choose between two discrete actions rather than a continuous contribution level; mapped to 0 (not a contribution-all-or-nothing PGG structure).",1,N/A,"There is no public-goods contribution or endowment structure; actions are C or D in a PD, and there are no human subjects.",1,N/R,Model is not a linear public-goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined or computable from given payoffs.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; purely theoretical.,1,0,Information structure: each player only observes the outcome of his own pairwise PD each period and nothing about others’ games; no summaries or aggregate information are shown.,1,0,Anonymity is central: players cannot recognize identities of past opponents; thus no identity of cheaters/punished players is revealed.,1,0,"There is no separate reward mechanism; only PD payoffs and contagious punishments via play of D, with anonymity.",1,0,Infinite-horizon discounted setting; no mention of participants being told the number of rounds (and no human participants).,1,1,Punishment is implemented endogenously as switching to action D (defection) in a contagious manner after deviations; entire analysis is about these punishments sustaining cooperation.,1,N/R,"There is no explicit cost-per-punishment-token; punishment is simply choosing D in the PD. Per-period payoff consequences depend on the PD payoff matrix parameters 1, g, and l, but no scalar ‘cost’ per punishment action is parameterized in the way requested.",0.35,N/R,Punishment effect is embedded in the PD payoff matrix; there is no separate linear technology mapping punishment expenditure to impact.,0.35,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism or costly positive sanctions are modeled; cooperation is just playing C.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism exists, so cost per reward unit is not applicable.",1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,"Model is a PD with payoffs normalized around 1, g, l; there is no per-period endowment of tokens or money described.",1,N/R,"Outcome variables are equilibrium existence, stability, and expected payoffs; no contribution rate (in public-goods sense) is defined or measured.",1,N/R,"No numeric contributions; actions are discrete C/D, and no average counts are reported beyond theoretical payoffs.",1,N/R,"Paper discusses ‘nearly efficient’ payoffs (e.g., approaching 1 as ε→0, δ→1), but does not provide a specific numeric efficiency ratio value.",0.6,N/R,Per-period payoff to a representative player is discussed (normalized to 1 under full cooperation) but no total group payoff is given in absolute units for any specific parameter set.,0.7,N/A,No participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,"All analysis is mathematical; no lab, online, field, or observational data.",1,"Infinite-horizon anonymous random-matching Prisoner’s Dilemma with M≥4 even players, uniform independent matching each period, PD payoffs defined with cooperative outcome payoff 1, one-shot deviation gain g>0, and loss parameter l≥0 (D,D payoff normalized near 0). Players observe only their own match outcome and a public random variable q_t∼U[0,1]. Cooperation is sustained via contagious punishment strategies with two phases (I: play C, II: play D) and transition probabilities determined by q(δ) (or q′(δ)), leading to breakdown of cooperation after deviations. Noise is later introduced as a constraint that each player must play D with probability at least ε>0 in every period. Extensions consider heterogeneous discount factors and elimination of public randomizations using delayed, deterministic punishments across subsequences of periods.","Summarizes the key structural assumptions and mechanisms directly described in Sections 2–4 and the Appendix, including matching, information, payoff normalization, phases, public randomizations, and noise.",0.96
10.1093_qje_qjz001.md,N/A – No discrete experiments or game-theoretic treatments,The paper is an observational and theoretical study using cross-cultural survey/ethnographic data and an analytical model; it does not report controlled lab/field game experiments or treatment conditions.,1,"Historical kinship tightness (kinship tightness index) and ecological variables (pathogen stress, subsistence mode)","All empirical analyses use kinship tightness and related ecological measures as predictors of moral, institutional, and development outcomes; there are no randomized or experimentally manipulated variables.",1,True,"The paper uses human-subject data from ethnographic records and multiple surveys (WVS, GPS, MFQ, ISEAR, etc.).",1,False,No controlled experimental interventions or randomized treatments are described; the work is observational and correlational.,1,False,There are no lab sessions or controlled lab environments; all data are observational or survey-based.,1,False,The paper develops an overlapping-generations analytical model but does not describe numerical simulations or agent-based simulations.,1,True,"Section III presents a formal overlapping-generations model with analytical comparative statics; however, this is not an experiment/game with parameterized treatments for human or simulated agents in the sense required by the schema.",1,0,No experimental games with players are run; only theoretical PDs and social insurance problems appear in the model without corresponding implemented experiments.,1,0,No repeated-game experiment is implemented with human subjects.,1,0,No contribution game or choice structure is actually implemented; field is not applicable.,1,N/A,There is no public goods game or endowment allocation implemented with participants.,1,N/R,No public goods game with a defined marginal per capita return is conducted.,1,N/A,No interactive multi-player experiment is described where communication could be allowed or disallowed.,1,N/A,No game interface or feedback about others’ actions/earnings is provided in a controlled experiment.,1,N/A,No experimental punishment stage with identity revelation is implemented.,1,N/A,No experimental reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No repeated-game experimental design with a known horizon is used.,1,0,"While punishment attitudes are measured in surveys (GPS), there is no implemented punishment technology in a game.",1,N/A,No numerical punishment cost is specified in an experiment.,1,N/A,No mapping from punishment points to losses is implemented.,1,0,No explicit experimental reward mechanism beyond normal payoffs is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in an experimental game is present.,1,N/A,No technical mapping for rewards is given.,1,0,No per-round endowment in tokens/points is described for an implemented game.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions in a public goods or similar game are measured.,1,N/A,No experimental contribution amounts are reported.,1,N/R,No experimental payoff efficiency measure is defined or reported.,1,N/A,There are no experimental groups with monetary payoffs; outcomes are survey responses and ethnographic codes.,1,Multiple countries and historical ethnic groups (global sample),"Data sources include the Ethnographic Atlas (1,200+ ethnic groups worldwide), WVS (many countries), GPS, MFQ, ISEAR, etc., spanning many countries rather than a single one.",1,Varies by data set; typical adult survey and student samples; exact distributions not reported in main text,The paper mentions university students for ISEAR and adult respondents for WVS/GPS/MFQ but does not give detailed age distributions.,0.5,Varies by data set; proportions not reported,"The surveys and ISEAR have both men and women, but no aggregate gender composition is reported.",0.5,"Mixed: university students (ISEAR) and general population survey samples (WVS, GPS, MFQ); detailed distributions not reported","ISEAR explicitly uses university students; WVS/GPS/MFQ are broader samples but education levels are only mentioned as possible controls, not fully described.",0.6,Observational,"All empirical data are observational/survey or ethnographic; there is no controlled lab, field, or online experiment manipulating payoffs.",1,"No implemented cooperation or punishment game; the paper uses an analytical overlapping-generations model with a prisoner's dilemma and a social insurance problem to derive hypotheses about kinship tightness, moral systems, and development, then tests these hypotheses using observational cross-cultural data (Ethnographic Atlas, WVS, GPS, MFQ, ISEAR, HYDE, etc.).",The only games described (PD and social insurance) exist within the theoretical model; the empirical sections analyze historical and contemporary correlations rather than game outcomes.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_16_8_083016.md,Well-mixed population – probabilistic punishment (replicator dynamics),Single analytical well-mixed condition studied via replicator equation; no separate parameterized treatments are reported as discrete conditions.,0.7,Punishment probability p; punishment fine α; group size n; enhancement factor r,Replicator analysis varies p and α (Figure 1) for given n and r; these are the key parameters determining dynamics.,0.9,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment; mathematical model analyzed via replicator dynamics.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; entirely theoretical.,1,False,"Well-mixed part uses analytical replicator equation, not Monte Carlo or agent-based simulation.",0.9,True,Derives and analyzes closed-form replicator equation (Appendix A.1).,1,5,"Group size n=5 is explicitly stated for well-mixed analysis in text (e.g., Figure 1 description: n=5).",0.9,1,Replicator dynamics models infinitely repeated random group formation; per interaction the game is one-shot. Use 1 as effective rounds per interaction.,0.4,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount c=1; effectively binary decision (cooperate or defect). Thus contribution choice is all-or-nothing, but per rules only these two options exist. Coded as 1 (any amount allowed) would be inaccurate; however schema forces 1 or 0. Use 0 to indicate binary choice.",0.6,0,Endowment starts in private account; cooperators decide to contribute c=1; defectors contribute nothing.,0.9,0.78,"MPCR = r/n. For Figure 1, r=3.9 and n=5, so MPCR=3.9/5=0.78.",0.9,0,No communication mechanism is part of the theoretical model.,1,0,No information feedback about others beyond payoffs is modeled.,0.9,0,Model has no identities or observability of who punishes; only strategy types.,1,0,No rewards in this model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary model; no concept of displayed rounds.,0.9,1,Punishment with fine α and shared cost among punishers is central to the model.,1,N/R,"Cost per unit fine is not parameterized separately as a linear cost; cost to punishers is (n−n_C)α/n_P, not a fixed marginal cost per punishment unit.",0.3,N/R,"Model specifies fine α directly, not a separate technology multiplier per unit cost.",0.3,0,Only punishment (fines) is modeled; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes c=1 and this is effectively the unit endowment in the payoff equations.,0.8,N/R,"Analytical results focus on fraction of cooperators, not on average contribution normalized by endowment.",0.2,N/R,No explicit reported average contribution; only dynamic states (full C or full D).,0.2,N/R,Paper does not report normalized group efficiency relative to maximum possible payoff.,0.1,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff levels are provided; only qualitative outcomes.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game model without human subjects.,1,Public goods game in well-mixed population; group size n=5; cooperators contribute c=1; contributions multiplied by enhancement factor r and shared; a fraction p of cooperators become punishers each round and impose fine α on each defector; punishment costs are exactly equal to total fines and are shared among punishers.,Summarizes formal model specification from main text and Appendix A.1.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_16_8_083016.md,Structured population – square lattice Monte Carlo (baseline probabilistic punishment model),Primary Monte Carlo simulation on square lattice with probabilistic punishment as described in Appendix A.2 and main Results section.,0.95,Punishment probability p; punishment fine α; enhancement factor r; square lattice of size L×L with degree 4 and group size n=5,"Simulation systematically varies p and α for several r (3.6, 3.9, 4.2) to obtain cooperation level (Figure 2).",1,False,"Study uses simulated agents on a lattice, not human subjects.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment; computational simulation of an evolutionary game.,1,False,No physical lab setting; computational model only.,1,True,Uses Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice (Appendix A.2).,1,False,"This condition refers to the simulated structured population, not the analytical well-mixed model.",1,5,Groups of size n=5 (focal plus four neighbors) for public goods interactions on the square lattice.,1,N/R,Evolution runs for 10^4–10^5 Monte Carlo steps until stationary; no fixed number of game rounds per se.,0.4,0,Binary strategy: either contribute full c=1 or contribute nothing; no continuous contribution choice.,0.9,0,Each player starts with full endowment and decides whether to contribute c=1; no pre-allocation to public pool.,0.9,N/R,"r and n are reported but MPCR not explicitly given; multiple r values used, so a single numeric value would be ambiguous.",0.5,0,No communication or signaling between players beyond payoffs and imitation.,1,0,Agents base imitation on individual payoff difference; no explicit reporting of others’ histories or summaries.,0.9,0,"No identities; only strategy types (C, P, D). Punishment is not attributed to specific individuals in information structure.",1,0,No rewards included in simulation.,1,0,Simulated agents do not observe total or remaining rounds; dynamics is ongoing with no horizon.,0.9,1,Model includes punishment via fines α and shared costs whenever at least one punisher is in group.,1,N/R,Punishment cost to each punisher is (n−n_C)α/n_P; not a simple linear cost-per-unit parameter.,0.3,N/R,"Model directly specifies fine α, not a separate technology mapping from cost to fine.",0.3,0,Simulation only implements punishment; no rewarding is modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,1,"As in well-mixed case, cooperators contribute c=1; this is effectively the per-round endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Outcomes reported as fraction of cooperators f in stationary state, not normalized contribution rate per endowment.",0.4,N/R,No explicit average contribution levels (in tokens) are reported; only fraction of C/P vs D.,0.3,N/R,Paper discusses dominance of cooperation vs defection but does not report efficiency as ratio of realized to maximal group payoff.,0.2,N/R,No numerical group payoff values are reported for the simulations.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational Monte Carlo simulation without human subjects.,1,"Public goods game on a square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; L^2 players each connected to 4 neighbors and belonging to 5 overlapping groups of size n=5. Initially each player is cooperator (C) or defector (D) with equal probability. In each group, cooperators contribute c=1 to public pool; total contributions multiplied by r and divided equally among group members. Within each group a fraction p of cooperators is randomly selected as punishers (P) each round. If at least one punisher is present, each defector in group is fined α and punishers share the total cost, each paying (n−n_C)α/n_P. Payoffs are summed over 5 groups. Strategy update: random focal player and neighbor compare payoffs; focal imitates neighbor’s strategy with Fermi rule Γ=1/(1+exp[(Π_x−Π_y)/K]) with K=0.5. Simulations run for 10^4–10^5 Monte Carlo steps for system sizes L=100–400. Primary outcome is stationary fraction of cooperators f.",Compiled from main Results section and Appendix A.2.,1
