pants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,Same lattice public goods setup as baseline structured model but with punishment probability p=0 so no punishers ever arise; cooperation supported only by spatial reciprocity. Snapshots (Figure 3(a)-(d)) show small compact cooperator clusters coexisting with defectors.,Summarized from Figure 3 description for p=0 case.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_16_8_083016.md,Structured population – full punishment (p=1) condition,Condition where all cooperators always punish (p=1) in spatial simulations (Figure 3 panels (i)-(l) and stability analysis for p=1).,0.9,Punishment probability p=1; punishment fine α; enhancement factor r=4 (for Figure 3) and generic r for later analysis; square lattice n=5,"Figure 3 caption states r=4 and α=2 for p in {0,0.5,1}; section 2.3 analyzes p=1 with varying α.",0.9,False,Agent-based simulation condition.,1,False,"Computational simulation, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Uses the same Monte Carlo lattice framework with p=1.,1,False,"Here we code the simulation condition, though the paper also provides an analytical interface-stability calculation for p=1.",0.8,5,Group size n=5 on square lattice.,1,N/R,Number of Monte Carlo steps until observed pattern not numerically given.,0.4,0,Binary contribution decision (cooperate or defect).,0.9,0,Endowment starts privately; cooperators contribute full unit c=1.,0.9,0.8,"For Figure 3, r=4 and n=5 so MPCR=4/5.",0.9,0,No communication.,1,0,No history or summary displays.,0.9,0,No identity or attribution structure modeled.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No horizon displayed to agents.,0.9,1,"With p=1, every cooperator in each group is a punisher; fines α applied when any C present.",1,N/R,"Punishment cost depends on composition of group ((n−n_C)α/n_P), not a constant marginal cost parameter.",0.3,N/R,Fine α specified directly; no separate tech multiplier.,0.3,0,No rewards in model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same public goods rules; cooperators contribute c=1.,0.8,N/R,Only qualitative statement: defectors dominate for large α at p=1; no numeric f reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit contribution statistics.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoff values presented.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,"Same lattice public goods framework as baseline structured model, but punishment probability p=1 so all cooperators always punish. Spatial patterns show smoother interfaces between punishers and defectors; near interfaces punishment costs become prohibitive and defectors eventually dominate, especially for larger α. Additional analytical interface-stability calculation (Figure 5 and related text) derives critical α_c = (24/5)*(3r/2−5) at which direction of invasion reverses.",Summarized from spatial patterns section and stability analysis for p=1.,1
