s are reported.,0.3,N/R,"Although condition leads to complete dominance of cooperators in some parameter ranges, efficiency is not explicitly computed as a metric.",0.3,N/R,No explicit payoff statistics reported for this condition.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is a computational agent-based simulation.,1,"Same lattice public goods setup; for each group, a fraction p=0.5 of cooperators is randomly selected as punishers per round. For r=4, α=2 (Figure 3) or r=3.6, α=1 (Figure 4), probabilistic sanctioning yields compact cooperative domains composed of both punishers (green) and non-punishing cooperators (blue) that successfully eliminate defectors (red). Pie charts of invasion events show that combined C→D and P→D invasions outnumber reverse, with many successful invasions executed by second-order free riders (non-punishing cooperators) benefitting from nearby punishers.",Summarized from description of Figure 3 and Figure 4 probabilistic sharing case.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_16_8_083016.md,Structured population – mixture of permanent cooperators and punishers (no role switching),Simulation variant where C and P are permanent strategies that do not randomly or periodically switch roles; used to contrast with sharing mechanisms (Figure 4(e)-(h)).,0.9,"Strategy set includes permanent cooperators (C), permanent punishers (P), and defectors (D); no probabilistic or periodic sharing between C and P; parameters p=0.5, r=3.6, α=1 for comparison but p only affects imitation, not role switching.","Figure 4 caption lists p=0.5, r=3.6, α=1 for all three cases; middle panels explicitly describe that pure cooperators and punishers are not allowed to randomly switch roles.",0.85,False,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,"Computational, not empirical.",1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Monte Carlo lattice simulation variant.,1,False,No analytical treatment for this condition; only simulation outcomes described.,1,5,Same square lattice public goods game with group size n=5.,1,N/R,Number of Monte Carlo steps until illustrated pattern not specified.,0.4,0,Each cooperator or punisher contributes full c=1; defectors contribute zero.,0.9,0,Initial endowments fully under player control; contribution is full or none.,0.9,0.72,"For Figure 4, r=3.6 and n=5, so MPCR = 3.6/5 = 0.72, same across the three compared variants.",0.9,0,No communication modeled.,1,0,Agents only have access to their own and neighbor payoffs.,0.9,0,Identity not modeled; only local strategy types.,1,0,No rewards modeled.,1,0,No explicit horizon.,0.9,1,Punishers P permanently punish defectors; punishment rules same as in baseline model but only P types bear costs; C types never punish.,1,N/R,Cost per punisher determined endogenously by local composition; no fixed marginal cost parameter.,0.3,N/R,Fine α=1 applied per punished defector; no separate technology parameter reported.,0.3,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"As in other structured conditions, cooperators and punishers contribute c=1.",0.8,N/R,Outcome qualitatively described as failure to outperform defectors; no numeric steady-state f reported.,0.3,N/R,No quantitative contributions reported.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoffs given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Square lattice initially prepared with three homogeneous stripes of C, P, and D strategies (Figure 4(e)); strategies C, P, and D are permanent (no C↔P role switching except via imitation). Punishment rules similar to baseline: P contribute to public good and punish defectors in their groups, bearing full cost; C contribute but never punish; D do not contribute and are punished if P present. Results: simple mixture of permanent C and P cannot defeat defectors; C can invade D only near P, but neutral drift causes spatial segregation of C and P, after which D outperform both and dominate.",Summarized from middle panels and accompanying explanation in Figure 4 discussion.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_16_8_083016.md,Structured population – periodic sharing between cooperators and punishers,Simulation variant where C and P exchange roles periodically each round instead of probabilistically (Figure 4(i)-(l)).,0.9,"Periodic role exchange between C and P every round; parameters p=0.5, r=3.6, α=1; strategy set includes C, P, and D.","Figure 4 caption indicates same parameters (p=0.5, r=3.6, α=1) and text states that in this variant pure cooperators and punishers are exchanged not randomly but periodically after every round.",0.9,False,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,"Computational, not empirical.",1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulation on square lattice.,1,False,No analytical equations for this periodic variant; results shown via snapshots.,1,5,Same group size n=5 as in other structured simulations.,1,N/R,Total number of Monte Carlo steps to reach dominance not specified.,0.4,0,Binary decision to contribute or defect by C/P vs D.,0.9,0,Contributors (C or P) decide to contribute c=1; endowment initially private.,0.9,0.72,"Parameters are r=3.6 and n=5, so MPCR=3.6/5.",0.9,0,No communication mechanism.,1,0,No explicit summaries shown to agents.,0.9,0,No identities modeled.,1,0,No reward in model.,1,0,No finite-horizon information communicated to agents.,0.9,1,"P types punish defectors and roles of C and P are swapped periodically, effectively sharing punishment over time.",1,N/R,"As in baseline, punishment cost depends on composition, not a fixed cost parameter.",0.3,N/R,"Fine α=1 directly specified, no separate tech parameter.",0.3,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Cooperators and punishers contribute unit c=1 as in other structured conditions.,0.8,N/R,Paper reports qualitative outcome: complete dominance of cooperative behaviour; no numeric rate reported.,0.4,N/R,No contribution statistics given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures.,0.2,N/R,No payoff statistics presented.,0.2,N/A,