10.1073_pnas.1105604108.md,Vignette condition – raid Toposa (another ethnic group),"Paper describes a vignette study with two within-subject conditions; this row corresponds to the scenario where two Kwatela warriors raid animals from the Toposa, another ethnic group.",1,"Target of raid in vignette: outgroup (Toposa, another ethnic group)",Only clearly experimental manipulation is which group is raided (Toposa vs. Lukumong).,1,True,Human participants evaluated vignettes and reported attitudes.,1,True,Controlled vignette study with manipulated scenario content and counterbalanced order.,1,False,Conducted among Turkana in the field; not a lab or online setting.,0.9,False,No simulations or artificial agents described for the vignette study.,1,False,No purely analytical/mathematical model for this condition.,1,1,Each respondent evaluates an individual warrior in the vignette; effectively a single decision-maker per response.,0.5,1,Each vignette judgment is a one-shot evaluation; no repeated rounds.,0.9,1,Not a contribution game; participants give scalar attitude responses. Defaulting to flexible response interpreted as non-all-or-nothing.,0.3,N/A,No endowments or contributions in this vignette-based attitude study.,1,N/A,No public goods or payoff multiplier structure in the vignette task.,1,0,Participants provide individual responses to hypothetical scenarios; no communication phase described.,0.9,0,No information about others’ payoffs or behaviors being shown in the vignette task.,0.9,0,No active punishment mechanism in the task; only judgments about whether a warrior should be punished.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identities in the vignette task.,1,0,Not a repeated-round game; number of rounds is irrelevant and not displayed.,1,0,Participants only express attitudes about whether the warrior should be punished; they do not actually impose costly punishment within an incentive system.,0.6,N/A,No implemented costly punishment mechanism with explicit costs.,1,N/A,No parametric punishment technology defined.,1,0,"No structured reward mechanism, only evaluative attitudes.",1,N/A,No explicit costly reward system.,1,N/A,No parametric reward technology.,1,N/A,"No tokens, money, or similar endowment used.",1,N/A,"Study measures attitudes (rightness, willingness to associate, etc.), not contributions to a public good.",1,N/A,No contribution amounts are part of the design.,1,N/A,No payoff-maximizing public good to normalize efficiency against.,1,N/A,No explicit monetary or token payoffs defined in the vignette responses.,1,Kenya,Turkana are described as a pastoral society in East Africa; Turkana region is in Kenya.,0.95,Adult men (exact age range N/R),"Text: “Each condition had 24 male participants”; elsewhere, raiding warriors are adult men, but specific ages for vignette participants not given.",0.6,Male,Text: “Each condition had 24 male participants in a within-subject design.”,1,N/R,No information on schooling or education level reported.,0,Field experiment,Vignette study conducted with Turkana men in their community rather than in a laboratory or online setting.,0.9,"Vignette-based attitude measurement: 24 Kwatela Turkana men each evaluated two scenarios (within-subject, counterbalanced order). This condition described two Kwatela warriors raiding animals from the Toposa (another ethnic group) and bringing them home. Dependent measures included perceived rightness/wrongness of the act, pleasure with the warrior, willingness to criticize or punish, and willingness to interact socially (stand next to in raid, entrust herds, lend a goat, allow daughter to marry).",Summary of the described vignette procedure and measures for this specific condition.,1
