ses 3 units of loss to target (B: 4 vs 12; C: 8 vs 24).,1,0,No positive transfer or reward option is provided.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,0.4,Endowment in the PGG is $0.40.,1,N/R,"Paper reports relationship between opting out and punishment, but not average contribution fraction in compulsory game for entire sample.",0.2,N/R,No numeric mean contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate efficiency measure reported.,0.1,N/R,Average group payoff not reported.,0.1,N/R,Sample recruited via AMT; national composition not specified.,0.3,N/R,No age distribution reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender data reported.,0.1,N/R,No education info reported.,0.1,Online,Experiment 2 also conducted via AMT.,1,"First game: one-shot optional PGG, groups of 4, endowment $0.40, multiplier r=2, contribution choices {0, 10, 20, 30, 40} cents, opt-out for fixed $0.50. Second game: one-shot compulsory PGG with same contribution options, followed by Stage-two pairwise punishment choices using strategy method: action A (no effect), B (punisher loses 4c, target loses 12c), C (punisher loses 8c, target loses 24c). Punishment decisions are conditioned on each partner’s contribution level.",Described in 'Experiment 2 design' section.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2016.06.017.md,"Null model – spatial public goods game on square lattice (Szolnoki, Perc & Szabó 2009 parameters)","Paper describes a baseline two-strategy spatial public goods game on a square lattice, explicitly citing Szolnoki, Perc & Szabó 2009 and reporting key thresholds for this configuration.",0.88,"Multiplication factor r of the public good (group size G=5 fixed, strategies C and D, noise K=0.5). Outcomes described for varying r; key reported thresholds: cooperators survive for r>3.74 and fully dominate for r>5.49.",Section 2 defines the null model and discusses phase transitions and thresholds as r varies while other parameters are fixed.,0.86,False,Model is an evolutionary game on a lattice with Monte Carlo updates and no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational/theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory environment; purely computational simulation.,1,True,Dynamics implemented via Monte Carlo steps on L^2 lattice; typical numerical simulation.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution; results are from numerical simulations and qualitative discussion.,0.8,0,Population size is L^2 but L is not specified numerically in this article.,0.2,0,Monte Carlo steps run until stationary state; specific number of iterations not reported.,0.2,1,Players have binary strategies: cooperate (contribute c=1) or defect (contribute 0). This matches an all-or-nothing contribution decision.,1,N/A,No human subjects and no explicit initial allocation; only strategy states are modeled.,1,0.76,"MPCR is r/G. Benchmark for cooperator survival is r=3.74 with G=5; 3.74/5≈0.748, approximated here as 0.76 to reflect the stated threshold qualitatively.",0.5,N/A,No communication between simulated agents is modeled.,1,0,Agents only use local payoffs; no mention of observing summaries of others’ earnings.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism present in the null model.,1,0,No reward mechanism present in the null model.,1,0,Simulated agents do not see an explicit round counter.,0.9,0,Only strategies C and D; no punishment actions.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewarding strategies or mechanisms in the null model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes c=1; this is the effective per-round endowment used in the payoff equations.,0.8,N/R,"Paper reports only threshold r values for cooperator survival/dominance, not explicit average contribution fractions.",0.1,N/R,"Only qualitative phase outcomes (all D, mixed C+D, all C) and thresholds are provided.",0.1,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) values reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff distributions are not tabulated or quantified.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Lattice-based Monte Carlo simulation only.,1,"Spatial public goods game on square lattice (G=5, overlapping groups, periodic boundary conditions). Strategies C and D; imitation dynamics via Fermi rule with noise K (K=0.5 in discussed results).","Detailed description in Section 2 defines topology, group size, strategies, and update rule.",0.95
10.1016_j.physleta.2016.06.017.md,"Public goods game with punishment – generic (Helbing et al. 2010 parameterization, r=3.8)",Section 3 upgrades the null model to include punishing cooperators and presents a representative phase diagram at r=3.8; model is taken from Helbing et al. 2010.,0.88,"Punishment fine β and punishment cost γ in a three-strategy spatial public goods game (strategies: D, C, P) at fixed r=3.8, G=5, K=0.5.",Fig. 1 is a full β–γ phase diagram and text discusses how varying fine β and cost γ determine phases.,0.93,False,Model uses simulated agents on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,This is not an empirical experiment but a computational evolutionary model.,1,False,"No laboratory environment, only simulations.",1,True,Phase diagram and dynamics obtained via Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice.,1,False,"Results are numerical (phase diagram, transitions) rather than closed-form.",0.8,0,Population size L^2 is not given numerically in this article.,0.2,0,Simulation iterated until stationary state; precise number of MCS not specified.,0.2,1,"Each cooperator (C or P) contributes c=1; defectors contribute 0, implying a binary contribution decision.",1,N/A,"No human endowment allocation, only strategy contributions.",1,0.76,"MPCR=r/G; with r=3.8 and G=5, MPCR=3.8/5=0.76.",0.98,N/A,Model includes no communication between agents.,1,0,Agents only use local payoffs; no global summaries reported.,0.9,0,Punishment acts are modeled as payoff adjustments; no notion of identity revelation.,0.9,0,No rewarding strategy in this punishment-only extension.,1,0,Simulated agents do not observe an explicit time horizon.,0.9,1,Strategy P (punishing cooperator) fines defectors at cost to itself.,1,N/R,"General model specifies cost as γ/(G−1) per punished defector, but numeric γ values vary; no single fixed cost can be reported for the whole β–γ diagram.",0.4,N/R,"General model specifies fine as β/(G−1) per punisher, but β varies; no single fixed value across conditions.",0.4,0,Only punishment is modeled here; no rewarding cooperators.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperative player contributes c=1 to the public good.,0.8,N/R,Only fractions of strategies and phase boundaries are discussed; no explicit normalized contribution rates reported.,0.1,N/R,"Paper shows cooperation levels qualitatively via fractions of strategies, not raw contributions.",0.1,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) values reported for this model.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff statistics are not numerically summarized.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Lattice-based computational simulations only.,1,"Spatial public goods game on square lattice (G=5, periodic boundary conditions) with three strategies: defectors (D), cooperators (C), and punishing cooperators (P). C and P contribute c=1; D contributes 0. Each P fines each D in group by β/(G−1), incurring cost γ/(G−1) per punished D. Payoffs per group g: Π_D^g = r(N_C+N_P)/G − β N_P/(G−1); Π_C^g = r(N_C+N_P+1)/G − 1; Π_P^g = r(N_C+N_P+1)/G − 1 − γ N_D/(G−1). Strategy update via Fermi rule with noise K=0.5.",Explicit payoff functions and interaction structure given in Section 3.,0.97
10.1016_j.physleta.2016.06.017.md,Public goods game with positive and negative reciprocity – β=0.37 cross-section,Section 4 presents cross-sections of the phase diagram at β=0.37 and β=0.55; this entry captures the β=0.37 condition where γ is varied.,0.9,"Cost of incentives γ varied at fixed reward/fine magnitude β=0.37 in four-strategy spatial public goods game (D, P, R, B) with r and G as in base model.",Left panel of Fig. 2 explicitly labeled β=0.37 and shows stationary fractions vs γ.,0.94,False,Model is an evolutionary game simulation with no human participants.,1,False,"Controlled computational study, not an empirical experiment with subjects.",1,False,No physical lab; purely computational.,1,True,Phase diagrams and cross-sections produced via Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice.,1,False,"Cyclic dominance and phase transitions characterized numerically, not by closed-form analysis.",0.8,0,Population size L^2 not numerically specified here.,0.2,0,Simulations run to stationary state; exact number of Monte Carlo steps not given.,0.2,1,"All cooperative strategies (P, R, B) contribute c=1; D contributes 0, implying a binary contribution choice.",1,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation; only strategy-based contributions.,1,N/R,This article’s summary of the model does not state numeric r or explicitly give r/G; only refers to it as the same public goods setup as in Section 3/Helbing et al. 2010 without numbers.,0.3,N/A,Agents do not communicate; only local interactions and payoffs are modeled.,1,0,No mention of global payoff summaries available to agents.,0.9,0,Punishment is modeled via payoff terms; no identity revelation mechanism is described.,0.9,0,Rewards are payoff adjustments without individual identity signaling.,0.9,0,No explicit horizon information for simulated agents.,0.9,1,"Strategies P and B punish defectors with fine β/(G−1), incurring cost γ/(G−1).",1,N/R,"Cost per punishment is γ/(G−1), but γ is varied in this cross-section; no single numeric value.",0.4,0.0925,"Fine per punishing cooperator per defector is β/(G−1). β fixed at 0.37; assuming G=5 as in the base lattice public goods game, β/(G−1)=0.37/4≈0.0925.",0.7,1,"Strategies R and B reward cooperators with β/(G−1), paying cost γ/(G−1).",1,N/R,"Reward cost is γ/(G−1), but γ varies in this cross-section; no single value.",0.4,0.0925,"Reward per rewarding cooperator per recipient is β/(G−1); with β=0.37 and assumed G=5, this is 0.37/4≈0.0925.",0.7,1,Each cooperative strategy contributes c=1 to the public good.,0.8,N/R,"Cross-section in Fig. 2 shows strategy fractions, not explicit fractions of endowment contributed (though contribution is tied to strategy). No numeric averages stated.",0.2,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts over time or at stationarity are reported.,0.1,N/R,"Article focuses on phase composition and oscillations, not on efficiency statistics.",0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff values reported for this β=0.37 condition.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation on a lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game with four strategies on a square lattice: D (defector), P (cooperator that punishes defectors), R (cooperator that rewards cooperators), B (cooperator that both punishes defectors and rewards cooperators). P, R, B contribute c=1; D contributes 0. For each group g, payoffs: Π_D^g = r(N_P+N_R+N_B)/G − β(N_P+N_B)/(G−1); Π_P^g = r(N_P+N_R+N_B+1)/G − γ N_D/(G−1) + β(N_R+N_B)/(G−1); Π_R^g = r(N_P+N_R+N_B+1)/G − γ(N_P+N_R+N_B)/(G−1) + β(N_R+N_B)/(G−1); Π_B^g = r(N_P+N_R+N_B+1)/G − γ + β(N_R+N_B)/(G−1). At β=0.37, varying γ leads to disappearance of P and B as γ increases, yielding continuous transition from D+P+B to D(B) phase.",Model and payoffs are detailed in Section 4; Fig. 2 left panel describes qualitative transition behavior at β=0.37.,0.95
