10.1038_nn.3087.md,Buckholtz et al. 2008 – Responsibility (R) scenarios,The paper describes one fMRI study (Buckholtz et al. 2008) with two main third-party punishment conditions: responsibility (R) and diminished-responsibility (DR) scenarios. This row captures the R condition.,0.9,"Scenario responsibility type: prototypical criminal behavior with clear intent (Responsibility, R). Harm severity varied within condition (petty theft to rape/murder).",Independent variable is whether scenarios depict clear responsibility versus diminished responsibility; this row is the clear-responsibility level. Harm severity is another factor but not separable into distinct game-like conditions here.,0.9,True,Human participants performed punishment judgments during fMRI.,1,True,Controlled laboratory fMRI task manipulating scenario type and measuring punishment responses.,1,True,Participants were scanned with fMRI and read scenarios in a controlled setting; this is a lab experiment.,1,False,No computer-only/agent-based simulation; human subjects were used.,1,False,No purely mathematical or closed-form modeling reported for this task.,1,1,Each participant makes unilateral third-party punishment decisions about a single protagonist; there is no interactive multi-player game.,0.8,1,Not a repeated-round game; each scenario is a one-shot judgment. Treated as a single decision round.,0.6,1,"Participants choose any punishment level from 0 to 9 on a scale, not a binary all-or-nothing choice.",1,N/A,"No endowment contribution or public goods structure; task is legal punishment rating, not a contribution game.",1,N/A,No public goods or linear payoff function is defined in this legal judgment task.,1,0,Participants simply read scenarios; no communication between participants is mentioned.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ earnings or punishments; only individual judgments are collected.,0.9,0,No interaction between participants or revelation of identities; decisions are anonymous ratings.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in this task.,1,0,The paper does not mention displaying total number of scenarios/rounds to participants.,0.5,1,Core task is to assign a punishment level (0–9) to the offender.,1,N/R,Cost to the third-party decision maker of assigning higher punishment is not described (likely hypothetical); cannot be quantified.,0.3,N/R,No explicit conversion from punishment scale (0–9) to any payoff or concrete sanction units is given.,0.3,0,Participants only punish or assign no punishment; there is no option to reward the protagonist.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/R,No monetary endowment or token budget for participants is reported in the description of this fMRI study.,0.2,N/A,"No contribution decision; outcome is punishment rating, not contribution.",1,N/A,No contribution decision is made.,1,N/R,No payoff structure or efficiency measure is described for these legal punishment judgments.,0.2,N/R,No group monetary payoff is defined in this context.,0.2,N/R,The commentary only cites Buckholtz et al. (2008) and does not restate recruitment location or nationality of participants.,0.2,N/R,No age or age range is reported for the fMRI participants in this summary.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition of the sample is not mentioned.,0.1,N/R,"Education level (e.g., students vs. community sample) is not specified in this article.",0.1,On site,"Participants underwent fMRI scanning and read scenarios in a controlled physical lab/clinic setting, not online or in the field.",1,"Third-party legal punishment task: participants read written criminal scenarios depicting norm violations with clear intent (responsibility, R) varying in harm severity (from petty theft to rape/murder) and indicated deserved punishment using a 0–9 scale (0 = no punishment, 9 = extreme punishment). They also rated blameworthiness and subjective arousal; neural activity was measured with fMRI.",Summarized from the ‘Testing a neurocognitive hypothesis of third-party norm enforcement’ section describing the Buckholtz et al. 2008 task and the R condition.,0.95
10.1038_nn.3087.md,Buckholtz et al. 2008 – Diminished-responsibility (DR) scenarios,"Same fMRI study; this row captures the diminished-responsibility (DR) condition, which is contrasted with R scenarios.",0.9,"Scenario responsibility type: diminished responsibility (DR) with mitigating factors (e.g., mental illness or duress). Harm severity matched to R scenarios within pairs.",Independent variable is presence of exculpating/mitigating information lowering blameworthiness; DR is the low-responsibility level.,0.9,True,Human participants made punishment decisions while being scanned.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of scenario responsibility type and measurement of resulting punishment ratings and brain activity.,1,True,Conducted in an fMRI scanner with structured presentation of scenarios.,1,False,Not a computer-only simulation; involves human decision making.,1,False,"The study reports empirical fMRI data, not pure analytical modeling.",1,1,Each subject independently evaluates protagonists as a third-party; no strategic interaction with other players.,0.8,1,A set of one-shot scenario judgments; modeled here as a single-decision task rather than repeated rounds.,0.6,1,"Punishment is chosen on a 0–9 scale with multiple levels available, not all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No contributions or public goods; task is legal sanctioning.,1,N/A,No marginal per capita return is defined in this non-game punishment task.,1,0,Design involves reading scenarios and making ratings; no communication channel is described.,0.9,0,Participants are not told about others’ decisions or payoffs.,0.9,0,No other human recipients observe individual punishment choices; identities are irrelevant.,0.9,0,No reward component exists.,1,0,The text does not mention informing participants of the number of scenarios to be judged.,0.5,1,Participants assign a level of punishment (0–9) based on scenarios.,1,N/R,Task uses hypothetical legal scenarios; no explicit cost function to the third-party for higher punishment is specified.,0.3,N/R,The mapping from scale points (0–9) to concrete sanctions is not described.,0.3,0,Participants can only withhold or impose punishment; no mechanism to reward defendants.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No monetary endowment or payoff information to participants is provided for this study.,0.2,N/A,"Outcome is punishment, not contribution to a public good.",1,N/A,No contribution decision is present.,1,N/R,No explicit payoff structure from which efficiency can be computed.,0.2,N/R,Group earnings or welfare measures are not defined in this judgment task.,0.2,N/R,The commentary does not re-report the sample’s country; original Buckholtz et al. 2008 details are not included.,0.2,N/R,Age information for the fMRI participants is not provided here.,0.1,N/R,No gender breakdown is given.,0.1,N/R,The article does not mention whether participants were students or another group.,0.1,On site,"As an fMRI experiment, it necessarily took place in a physical imaging facility under lab conditions.",1,"Third-party legal punishment task with diminished-responsibility (DR) scenarios: participants read written criminal scenarios in which harm severity matched R scenarios but included mitigating circumstances (e.g., mental illness, duress) that reduce blameworthiness. For each scenario, participants chose a punishment level from 0 (no punishment) to 9 (extreme punishment) and also provided blameworthiness and arousal ratings, while brain activity was recorded via fMRI.","Summarized from the section describing DR scenarios, which are matched in harm to R but include exculpating information and elicit lower punishment, blame, and arousal.",0.95
