10.1073_pnas.1707505115.md,CD – Well-mixed control treatment (2 opponents),"Paper defines three main treatments: CD (control, well-mixed), CD₂ (static network with 2 neighbors), and CDP₂ (static network with 2 neighbors and punishment). This row corresponds to CD.",1,Network structure and punishment option: well-mixed random matching with 2 opponents; available actions C and D only; no punishment.,Materials and Methods: CD is traditional PD with C and D only; opponents randomly chosen each round.,1,True,Human participants in a social dilemma experiment (225 undergraduate volunteers).,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized assignment to treatments and fixed protocols.,1,True,Conducted in professionally designed computer laboratories at two universities.,1,False,"Study involves human subjects, not purely computational simulations.",1,False,No formal analytical model as standalone method; main results from experiments.,1,75,"Total N=225 with three main treatments (CD, CD₂, CDP₂) each having two sessions. It is reasonable that participants were split evenly (225/3=75) per treatment; no contrary info.",0.6,50,"‘A game consisted of 50 rounds, which took ~1 h to complete.’",1,1,"PD is framed as choosing one of discrete actions C or D, not a continuous contribution; per instructions all players must pick exactly one action each round, not ‘contribute any amount’. For this schema, interpreted as players not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions of an endowment, but since no contribution level exists, we treat as 1 (N/A-like) following instruction only for contribution games.",0.3,N/R,Standard PD with discrete actions; no divisible endowment to contribute. Not specified as a contribution proportion.,0.2,N/R,"Prisoner’s dilemma with pairwise payoff matrix, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.",1,0,No mention of any communication; participants in isolated cubicles; standard PD lab protocol without chat.,0.9,1,"After each round, players saw payoffs and