al payoff per round statistics reported.,0.2,China,Same subject pool as CD.,1,Mean age 20.2 years; undergraduates.,Same pool as CD; no differential ages by treatment.,1,"Approximately 50% female, 50% male",Overall ratio ~1:1; no treatment-specific breakdown.,0.8,"Undergraduate university students, multiple majors",Same as CD.,1,On site,Conducted in computer labs with participants present.,1,"Repeated 2-opponent prisoner’s dilemma on a static ring network (degree 2). Each participant plays against two fixed neighbors every round. Same payoff matrix as control (C vs D interactions) with payoffs given in Eq. 1. Two sessions, 50 rounds, 30 s per decision and feedback.",Summarized from description of CD₂ in Experimental Methods and main text.,0.9
10.1073_pnas.1707505115.md,"CDP₂ – Static network, 2 neighbors, punishment available","Defined as ‘static network treatment with punishment (CDP₂)… same game as CD₂, with P being available as an independent action’.",1,"Network structure and punishment option: static ring network with 2 fixed neighbors; available actions C, D, and costly punishment P; punishment fine-to-cost ratio 4:1.",Main text and Materials and Methods: CDP₂ adds independent action P to CD₂; punishment cost 1 to punisher and 4 to punishee.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment with manipulated availability of punishment on static network.,1,True,Run in computer labs as other treatments.,1,False,Not a simulation-only study.,1,False,No separate analytical-only condition.,1,75,Assuming equal split of 225 across three treatments absent contrary detail.,0.6,50,Common 50-round structure for all treatments.,1,1,"Players choose among discrete actions C, D, or P, not continuous contributions.",0.3,N/R,Not a contribution game; no proportion of endowment initially in public fund.,0.2,N/R,Pairwise PD with punishment; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication channels described; same lab protocol.,0.9,1,Interface shows for each opponent their action and payoff consequence each round (as in other treatments).,0.8,0,"Opponents are kept anonymous apart from an ‘opponent number’; while one can infer which neighbor punished based on shown actions, personal identities are not revealed. Schema refers to identity revelation, so coded as 0.",0.7,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,Participants did not know exact number of rounds.,1,1,CDP₂ includes costly punishment P as independent action.,1,1,"‘Because administering punishment requires effort, P is a costly action and a punisher pays one unit.’",1,4,‘A punishee experiences a relatively high loss of four units… ratio of 1:4’. So each 1 unit spent causes 4 units fine.,1,0,"No positive reward action added; only C, D, P.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Same initial 50-point endowment in all games.,1,0.37,"Main text: ‘in the static network with punishment (CDP₂), the level of cooperation was M=37%’.",1,N/R,"Only frequencies of C, D, P actions reported; no numeric contribution per round.",0.2,N/R,Median payoff per round reported (0.58) but no explicit maximum benchmark to compute normalized efficiency.,0.3,N/R,Only individual payoff per round distributions provided.,0.2,China,Same participant recruitment as other treatments.,1,Mean age 20.2 years; undergraduates.,Same pool applies to all treatments.,1,"Approximately 50% female, 50% male",Overall sample has ~1:1 female-to-male ratio; not broken down by treatment.,0.8,"Undergraduate university students, multiple majors",Same as in other treatments.,1,On site,Conducted in on-site computer laboratories.,1,"Repeated prisoner’s dilemma on a static ring network with degree 2 and an added punishment action. Payoff matrix (Eq. 1) specifies payoffs for C, D, and P interactions; punishment costs 1 point to punisher and imposes 4-point fine on each punished opponent. Two sessions, 50 rounds, 30 s per decision and feedback stage; opponents referred to by anonymous numbers.","Compiled from description of payoff matrix, CDP₂ treatment, and Experimental Methods.",0.9
10.1073_pnas.1707505115.md,"CD₄ – Static network, 4 neighbors, no punishment","Neighborhood-size extension mentioned in SI: ‘static network in which neighborhood size equaled four (CD₄)’; same structure as CD₂ but degree 4, without punishment.",0.9,Network structure and punishment option: static network with 4 neighbors; actions C and D only; no punishment.,Main text cites CD₄ and compares its cooperation and defection levels to CD₂; punishment only mentioned for CDP₄.,0.9,True,Described as additional static-network experiment in SI Appendix; same type of human lab experiment.,0.9,True,Controlled manipulation of neighborhood size in lab.,0.9,True,Performed in same computer labs as main treatments (stated that all treatments were implemented there).,0.9,False,"Still human-subject experiment, not simulation.",1,False,Not an analytical-only condition.,1,N/R,"Exact number of participants in CD₄ not given; only overall N=225 and that all treatments (including additional neighborhood-4 ones) consist of two replicates are implied in SI, but split across 5 treatments (CD, CD₂, CDP₂, CD₄, CDP₄) is not provided.",0.2,50,All games in this experimental program consisted of 50 rounds; no exception indicated for CD₄.,0.9,1,Binary PD actions C or D; no continuous contribution.,0.3,N/R,Not a contribution game; no initial split between private and public accounts.,0.2,N/R,"Prisoner’s dilemma, not linear public goods.",1,0,Same software and procedures as main treatments; no communication channels mentioned.,0.8,1,"By design, interface shows consequences of each action vs neighbors each round, as in other treatments.",0.7,0,No punishment action in CD₄.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Participants generally not informed about exact number of rounds; no exception mentioned.,0.9,0,"CD₄ is referred to as counterpart of CD₂ for neighborhood size 4, while punishment appears in CDP₄; so CD₄ has no P action.",0.9,N/A,No punishment available.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,0,No reward mechanism mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Same endowment for all games in this experiment; no exceptions for CD₄.,0.9,0.09,Neighborhood-size results: ‘M=9.0%’ cooperation in CD₄.,1,N/R,"Only frequencies of C and D, not numeric amounts.",0.2,N/R,No payoff per round statistics for CD₄ reported in main text; likely in SI Fig. S7 but not in given excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff values reported for CD₄ in text provided.,0.1,China,Same experiment series and recruitment sites.,0.9,Mean age 20.2 years; undergraduates.,Same pool; no separate description for CD₄ subsample.,0.9,"Approximately 50% female, 50% male",Same overall sample ratio; no specific deviations stated for CD₄.,0.7,"Undergraduate university students, multiple majors",Applies across all treatments.,0.9,On site,Same lab setting as other treatments.,0.9,Repeated prisoner’s dilemma on a static network with neighborhood size 4 (each participant has four fixed neighbors). Same C/D payoff matrix as CD₂ but with larger degree; no punishment. Two replicates mentioned in SI along with analysis of payoff–action correlations.,Summarized from main-text reference to ‘static network in which neighborhood size equaled four (CD₄)’ and the SI description.,0.7
10.1073_pnas.1707505115.md,"CDP₄ – Static network, 4 neighbors, punishment available",Main text: ‘a larger-size neighborhood in which network reciprocity is weaker… punishment (CDP₄)…’; punishment and neighborhood size 4.,0.9,"Network structure and punishment option: static network with 4 neighbors; available actions C, D, and costly punishment P; punishment fine-to-cost ratio 4:1.",CDP₄ is described analogously to CDP₂ but for neighborhood size 4; punishment parameters same as in Eq. 1.,0.9,True,Described as additional human-subject experiment in SI (Fig. S7).,0.9,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment availability in static degree-4 network.,0.9,True,Conducted in same university labs as other treatments.,0.9,False,"Empirical lab data, not just simulation.",1,False,No analytical-only condition.,1,N/R,Total N=225 but specific allocation to CDP₄ not given.,0.2,50,No indication that the 50-round structure was altered for CDP₄.,0.9,1,"Discrete actions C, D, P; no graded contributions.",0.3,N/R,Not a contribution game.,0.2,N/R,Prisoner’s dilemma with punishment; no MPCR concept.,1,0,Same lab software/procedures; no chat or communication reported.,0.8,1,"Interface shows each neighbor’s actions and payoffs, consistent across treatments.",0.7,0,"Opponents are anonymous except for numbers; no identifying info, so identities of punishers are not revealed beyond action choices against each numbered neighbor.",0.7,0,No reward action.,1,0,Same hidden-horizon rule as main treatments.,0.9,1,Text: ‘aiding network reciprocity with punishment (CDP₄)’; punishment usage and frequencies are reported.,1,1,Punishment parameters not stated separately for CDP₄; assumed identical 1:4 cost/fine as CDP₂ per same experiment design.,0.9,4,Same punishment fine magnitude is implied by using ‘same payoff matrix’ with changed neighborhood size only.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Same initial endowment rule applies across experiment.,0.9,0.094,Neighborhood-size comparison: ‘M=9.4%’ cooperation in CDP₄.,1,N/R,Only cooperation/defection/punishment frequencies reported.,0.2,N/R,No payoff per round numbers for CDP₄ in provided text; SI Fig. S7 only qualitatively described.,0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff reported for CDP₄.,0.1,China,Same universities and experiment series.,0.9,Mean age 20.2 years; undergraduates.,Same subject pool used.,0.9,"Approximately 50% female, 50% male",Overall sample has ratio ~1:1; not disaggregated by CDP₄.,0.7,"Undergraduate university students, multiple majors",Same recruitment description applies.,0.9,On site,Conducted in the same university computer laboratories.,0.9,"Repeated prisoner’s dilemma on a static network with neighborhood size 4 and punishment option. Each participant interacts with four fixed neighbors per round, choosing C, D, or P. Punishment costs 1 point and fines each punished neighbor 4 points; used occasionally (median 4.7% of actions; defection median 88%). Two replicates analyzed in SI, focusing on payoff–action correlations that show cooperation becomes maladjusted under punishment in larger neighborhoods.",Summarized from main-text neighborhood-size discussion and references to SI Appendix Figs. S6 and S7.,0.7
