10.1126_sciadv.1601444.md,"T1 – Anonymous repeated PD with C, D, P","Paper describes two main treatments: anonymous (T1) and onymous (T2). This row encodes the anonymous treatment with 3 actions (C, D, P).",1,Opponent identity visibility: anonymous (opponent unknown by name/class identity),"Core manipulation is anonymity vs onymity; here, opponents remain unknown.",1,True,Human undergraduates participated in repeated PD sessions.,1,True,Controlled social dilemma experiment with randomized pairings and treatments.,1,True,Conducted in a computer laboratory with isolated cubicles using z-Tree.,1,False,"This row refers to the human-subject experiment, not the later computer simulations.",1,False,No closed-form mathematical model as the primary method for this condition.,1,80,"Materials and Methods give 154 total participants, and Fig. 4 notes 80 participants in T1 and 74 in T2.",0.95,-1,Number of rounds per interaction is stochastic with continuation probability 0.75; fixed count not reported.,0.2,1,"Actions are discrete (C, D, P), not contribution levels to a public good; the all-or-nothing field for contribution does not apply, but per instructions must be 1 or 0. No contribution choice exists, so coded as 1 by convention that arbitrary continuous/discrete contributions are not constrained here.",0.2,-1,"This is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with fixed payoffs, not a contribution game with endowments.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,"No mention of communication being allowed; participants only see payoffs and, in T2, names.",0.9,0,Participants see result of preceding round and their total payoff; no evidence of summaries of others' earnings or punishments.,0.8,0,Opponents are anonymous; identities of punishers or punished cannot be known.,1,0,No reward action exists in the game.,1,0,"Participants do not know how many rounds an interaction will have, only continuation probability.",1,1,Third action P is costly punishment.,1,1,Definition: punishment means paying 1 unit for opponent to lose 4 units.,1,4,Each unit spent on P causes opponent to lose 4 units.,1,0,"Only C, D, and P are available; no separate reward action.",1,-1,No reward mechanism.,1,-1,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game described directly in payoff units; no per-round endowment explicitly stated.,0.2,N/A,No contribution decision; binary/ternary action PD.,1,N/A,"No contribution decision; actions are C, D, P with fixed payoffs.",1,N/R,They report median payoff per round and correlations but do not normalize payoffs relative to maximum possible.,0.3,N/R,"Only individual median payoff per round (-0.319) is reported, not total pair payoff.",0.3,China,"Participants from universities in Kunming, China.",1,Mean age 21.6; undergraduates,"Materials and Methods: mean age 21.6, undergraduate students.",1,"49.4% women, 50.6% men",Reported for the pooled sample; sessions are balanced with no indication of gender imbalance by treatment.,0.8,"Undergraduate students in mathematics, statistics, and other mostly social sciences and humanities",Materials and Methods specify majors and undergraduate status.,1,On site,Conducted in physical computer lab with isolated cubicles.,1,"Repeated pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with three possible actions: C (pay 1, opponent gets 2), D (gain 1, opponent loses 1), P (pay 1, opponent loses 4). Continuation probability per round 0.75, random matching so each participant plays every other participant once in random order. Median payoff per round in this treatment is -0.319.",Summarized from payoff description and experimental procedure; median payoff value from Results section.,1
10.1126_sciadv.1601444.md,"T2 – Onymous repeated PD with C, D, P",Second main treatment described is onymous (T2) where opponents are made known.,1,Opponent identity visibility: onymous (opponent known by name as classmate),"Core manipulation is anonymity vs onymity; here, participants see each other’s names and are classmates.",1,True,Human undergraduates participated in the onymous condition.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random pairing and specified treatment.,1,True,Same laboratory and z-Tree setup as T1.,1,False,"This row refers to human-subject onymous treatment, not simulations.",1,False,No analytical model reported as primary for this condition.,1,74,Fig. 4 indicates 74 participants in T2 (vs 80 in T1).,0.95,-1,Stochastic continuation with probability 0.75; exact fixed number of rounds is not reported.,0.2,1,Same game structure as T1; contribution framework does not apply but binary field must be set.,0.2,-1,Not a contribution game; PD with fixed payoffs.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No mention of allowed communication beyond knowing opponent’s name.,0.9,0,Participants see result of preceding round and their total payoff; no summaries of others reported.,0.8,1,Opponents see each other’s names and can observe who chose P in the dyad.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in the game.,1,0,"As in T1, participants do not know total or remaining number of rounds; only continuation probability is given.",1,1,Punishment P is available as a third action.,1,1,P action: pay 1 unit for opponent to lose 4.,1,4,Each unit spent on P yields 4 units loss to opponent.,1,0,No separate rewarding action is defined.,1,-1,No reward mechanism.,1,-1,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game is described directly in payoff units per action; no explicit per-round endowment reported.,0.2,N/A,No contribution decision; PD with discrete actions.,1,N/A,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,They report median payoff per round and correlations; no normalized efficiency measure is presented.,0.3,N/R,Only individual median payoff per round (0.395) is stated.,0.3,China,"Same participant pool: universities in Kunming, China.",1,Mean age 21.6; undergraduates,Overall sample characteristics apply to both treatments.,1,"49.4% women, 50.6% men",Overall gender split given; no evidence of treatment-specific differences.,0.8,"Undergraduate students in mathematics, statistics, and other mostly social sciences and humanities",Same recruitment as T1.,1,On site,Conducted in computer laboratory with isolated cubicles.,1,"Same repeated pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with actions C, D, P and continuation probability 0.75. In this treatment, opponents are classmates and their names are shown. Median payoff per round is 0.395. Onymity increases cooperation and decreases defection and punishment relative to T1, and payoff per round correlates positively with cooperation and negatively with defection and punishment.",Summarized from Results and Materials and Methods sections.,1
10.1111_j.1467-9442.2006.00444.x.md,"NoEx – exogenous, no law (no sanction)","Condition label and description from Table 1 and design section: exogenously imposed, no law (no sanction), used as benchmark.",1,"Sanction regime: no law (sanction s = 0), exogenous imposition (no voting stage).",Independent variable is the sanction level and mode of enactment; here sanction is zero and imposed by experimenter.,1,True,Human subjects (undergraduate students) play a public goods game.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with randomized grouping and specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted in sessions in a large lecture hall at the University of St. Gallen.,1,False,All data from human subjects; no agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,"Although theory is discussed, this condition refers to behavioral data, not purely analytical results.",1,3,Groups of size n=3 in all conditions.,1,1,"Subjects played the game only once, known to all subjects.",1,1,"Subjects choose contribution gi from endowment Ei; any amount between 0 and 20 allowed, not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is fully in private account initially and must be allocated to private or public good.,1,0.5,"Marginal return from public good is a=0.5 points per contributed point; group size 3, but MPCR as defined here is multiplier divided by group size; here multiplier per individual is 0.5, which matches their ‘marginal return from public good’.",0.7,0,No mention of communication; anonymity and one-shot decisions emphasized.,1,0,No feedback about others’ behavior or earnings until end; no interim summaries reported.,0.9,0,"Sanctions not applicable in this condition (no law); also interactions are anonymous, so identities are not revealed.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,1,One-shot game and this was common knowledge; therefore number of rounds known to participants.,1,0,This is the no law (no sanction) condition; s=0.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,0,No rewards described; only sanctions for free-riding in law treatments.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Each subject endowed with Ei=20 points in all conditions.,1,0.3,Contribution rate reported as 30 percent in NoEx (average actual contribution as % of full contributions).,1,N/R,"Only normalized contribution rates reported, not average point contributions per subject in this condition.",0.2,0.3,Net efficiency in NoEx reported as 30 percent (average realized income gain from cooperation as % of potential).,1,N/R,Equilibrium prediction table lists theoretical payoffs (20 points) but realized average group payoffs not reported.,0.3,Switzerland,"Experiments conducted at University of St. Gallen, which is in Switzerland.",1,N/R,Described as undergraduate students; no specific age statistics given.,0.3,N/R,No information on gender composition reported.,0.0,"Undergraduate students of business, law and economics",Explicitly stated in procedures.,1,On site,"Two sessions conducted in a large lecture hall at the University of St. Gallen, indicating in-person laboratory setting.",1,Linear public goods game; group size 3; endowment 20 points; marginal return from public good 0.5; no sanctions; one-shot; anonymous; subjects also state expectations of others’ contributions and receive 4 points for correct predictions.,Summarized from design and procedures sections for this specific no-law exogenous condition.,1
10.1111_j.1467-9442.2006.00444.x.md,"MildEx – exogenous, mild law (mild sanction s = 4)",Condition label from Table 1 and design text: exogenously imposed mild law with sanction s^mild=4.,1,Sanction regime: mild law with sanction s = 4 for any gi < 20; exogenous imposition (no voting stage).,Independent variable is sanction severity (here 4 points) and exogenous imposition.,1,True,Human subjects choose contributions under mild sanction regime.,1,True,Controlled treatment in laboratory public goods experiment.,1,True,Same sessions and setting as other conditions at University of St. Gallen.,1,False,No computer simulations used for this condition.,1,False,Outcomes come from experiment; theory only used for predictions.,1,3,Groups of size n=3 in all conditions.,1,1,Game is one-shot and this applies to all exogenous conditions.,1,1,Subjects allocate 0–20 points to public good; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely in private account to be allocated.,1,0.5,"Marginal return from public good a=0.5 per point contributed, same as other conditions.",0.7,0,No communication mentioned; anonymous decisions.,1,0,Outcome information only at end of experiment across all conditions; no interim summaries.,0.9,0,Punishment (sanction) is automatic and anonymous; no identities revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,1,One-shot nature common knowledge.,1,1,Law prescribes full contribution and sanctions free-riding: s_i=4 if gi<20.,1,1,Sanction is a direct deduction in points; each sanction unit costs the punished 1 point per unit of s (here flat 4). Per-unit cost is 1.,0.6,1,Each deducted point reduces the sanctioned player’s payoff one-for-one; per-unit effect on payoff is 1.,0.6,0,Only negative sanctions are present; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Each subject endowed with Ei=20 points.,1,0.38,Contribution rate reported as 38 percent in MildEx.,1,N/R,"Only normalized contribution