D) and NoEnd(SEVERE)) is reported as 19 percent; paper does not separate the two NoEnd variants, so we assign 0.19 with low confidence.",0.4,N/R,Average payoffs by group not reported.,0.2,Switzerland,Study conducted at University of St. Gallen.,1,N/R,No age data provided.,0.3,N/R,No gender information reported.,0.0,"Undergraduate students of business, law and economics",Subject pool same across endogenous conditions.,1,On site,Lab sessions in university lecture hall.,1,"Two-stage game with strategy method. Stage 1 (MILD): subjects vote anonymously on mild law and predict referendum outcome (2-point incentive). Stage 2 (NoEnd(MILD)): given that mild law is rejected, contributions are made under no-law regime with s=0; subjects had pre-specified contributions and expectations for this outcome as part of their strategy method responses. One-shot, anonymous, linear public goods game.",Summarizes voting and contingent decision structure specific to endogenous mild law rejected outcome.,1
10.1111_j.1467-9442.2006.00444.x.md,"SevereEnd – endogenous, severe law accepted by majority vote",Endogenous second-stage condition where severe law is accepted in SEVERE; label defined as SevereEnd.,1,Sanction regime and enactment: severe law (s = 14) endogenously chosen via majority vote; this row covers contribution decisions in groups where severe law is enacted (SevereEnd).,Independent variables are sanction severity (14) and outcome of voting (accept severe law).,1,True,Human subjects vote and then play under severe sanction if accepted.,1,True,Laboratory public goods experiment with endogenous law enactment.,1,True,Sessions held on site at University of St. Gallen.,1,False,No simulation model used.,1,False,Refers to experimental data.,1,3,Groups of size 3 across all treatments.,1,1,Underlying game is one-shot; strategy method used but only one payoff-relevant set of outcomes.,1,1,Subjects can allocate any amount 0–20 to the public good.,1,0,Endowment starts fully in private account.,1,0.5,Same linear public goods parameters; marginal return a=0.5.,0.7,0,No face-to-face communication; only voting and individual decisions.,1,0,Subjects do not receive feedback on votes or contributions before experiment end.,0.9,0,Sanctions are automatic and anonymous; no identification of who is punished.,0.9,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,One-shot structure known to all participants.,1,1,Severe law prescribes full contribution and sanctions free-riding with s=14.,1,1,Sanction is a payoff deduction; each sanction point reduces payoff by 1.,0.6,1,Per-unit sanction has one-for-one effect on payoff.,0.6,0,Only sanctions implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Endowment Ei=20 in all conditions.,1,0.96,Contribution rate in SevereEnd reported as 96 percent.,1,N/R,"No numerical mean contributions given, only proportion fully contributing and overall rate.",0.2,0.96,Net efficiency in SevereEnd reported as 96 percent.,1,N/R,Average realized payoffs not reported; only efficiency and sanction incidence.,0.3,Switzerland,Experiment run at University of St. Gallen.,1,N/R,No detailed age data given.,0.3,N/R,Gender data not provided.,0.0,"Undergraduate students of business, law and economics",Same subject pool.,1,On site,Conducted in a lecture hall at the University of St. Gallen.,1,"Two-stage game with strategy method. Stage 1 (SEVERE): subjects vote anonymously on severe law and predict referendum outcome (2-point incentive). Stage 2 (SevereEnd): given acceptance of severe law, subjects choose contributions under sanction s=14 and provide expectations of others’ contributions for all possible vote configurations; correct predictions earn 4 points. One-shot, anonymous, linear public goods game.","Summarizes voting, sanctions, and belief elicitation for endogenous severe law accepted outcome.",1
