10.1006_jtbi.2003.3104.md,Single-phenotype gene–culture model with internalizable norm C,"Paper’s first formal model: single internalizable norm C with genotypes aa, ab, bb and extraparental transmission; treated as one analytical/simulation condition.",0.95,"Fitness benefit of norm C (t), genetic cost of internalization (u), strength of extraparental transmission (γ), assortative mating rate (ζ); conditions compared: stability of equilibria aaC, aaD, bbD under varying t,u,γ,ζ.","Model varies t, u, γ, and ζ in stability analysis; these parameters determine whether internalization and norm C invade or persist.",0.96,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical population-genetic and cultural transmission model with dynamical systems.,1,False,No controlled assignment or empirical manipulation; only mathematical analysis and simulations.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; entirely theoretical.,1,True,Model analyzed analytically and via computer simulations to check stability (explicitly mentions extensive simulations).,0.98,True,"Closed-form dynamical equations, equilibria, and Jacobian eigenvalues are derived and analyzed.",1,0,No discrete interacting players in a game-theoretic experiment; infinite-population style model.,1,0,"Generational dynamical system, not a finite-round game.",1,0,No contribution decisions; not a public goods or social dilemma game.,1,N/A,No endowment or contribution mechanism with human subjects.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier defined in this analytical gene–culture model.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication stage.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback to human participants.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or identities; theoretical fitness values only.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards in a game setting.,1,N/A,No human subjects; model time is continuous in generations.,1,0,"Model concerns internal norm C that is fitness-enhancing, but no explicit costly punishment mechanism.",0.99,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this model.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this model.,1,0,No explicit interpersonal rewarding technology; only fitness benefits of a norm.,0.99,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No token or monetary endowment for players.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game; no contributions measured.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts defined.,1,N/R,"Model reports fitness and stability, not efficiency relative to a public good benchmark.",1,N/R,Average fitness not normalized or reported as a specific numeric outcome for one condition.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and simulation-based with no subjects.,1,"Diploid gene–culture coevolution model with one internalizable norm C. Fitness: aaC and abC have (1−u)(1+t), aaD and abD have (1−u), bbD has 1. Parental and extraparental cultural transmission; conditions for stability of equilibria aaC and bbD as functions of t, u, γ, and assortative mating ζ.",Summarizes key structural features that are not standard experimental game parameters.,0.98
10.1006_jtbi.2003.3104.md,Two-phenotype gene–culture model with altruistic norm A and fitness-enhancing norm C,Second formal model adding altruistic norm A and fitness-enhancing norm C with norms A/B and C/D and allele a/b; treated as one analytical/simulation condition.,0.95,"Cost of altruistic norm A (s), benefit of norm C (t), internalization cost (u), extraparental transmission rates for A and C (γ, v), plus replicator strength α in extended version; stability of equilibria aaAC, aaAD, aaBC, aaBD, bbBD under these parameters.","Paper explicitly discusses stability of multiple pure equilibria as functions of s, t, u, γ, v and later α.",0.97,False,No data from human or animal subjects; entirely theoretical and simulated.,1,False,No experimental manipulation on real agents; just mathematical comparative statics.,1,False,Not run in a lab; analytical model.,1,True,Author notes many simulations to check for instability and to study dynamics.,0.98,True,Derives equilibria and Jacobian eigenvalues and provides stability conditions.,1,0,"Population-level infinite-population style model, not discrete finite group of players in a lab game.",1,0,"Continuous generational dynamics, not finite repeated rounds.",1,0,No voluntary contribution decision in a game-theoretic sense.,1,N/A,No endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,Model specifies phenotypic fitness multiplicatively but does not define a public-good MPCR.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,"Altruistic behavior is modeled via phenotypes and fitness, not explicit punishment rounds.",1,N/A,No explicit identifiable rewarding technology.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0,"Altruism A is modeled as imposing own-cost s and giving group benefit via q later, but not as explicit targeted punishment technology at individuals.",0.85,N/A,No explicit punishment operator with defined cost per unit.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mapping from cost to target loss.,1,0,No explicit costly rewarding technology.,0.99,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No per-period endowment of tokens.,1,N/A,No contribution decision modeled.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts defined.,1,N/R,"Paper reports stability of altruistic vs. non-altruistic equilibria, not normalized efficiency.",0.8,N/R,Fitness expressions are given but not aggregated into a single numeric group payoff for a specific parameter set.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical and simulation work without human participants.,1,"Diploid gene–culture model with two traits: altruistic norm A vs B (cost s to bearer, group-level effect q later) and fitness-enhancing norm C vs D (individual benefit t). Internalization allele a (cost u) enables cultural inheritance of A/C; bb types default to BD. Extraparental transmission parameters γ (for C) and v (for A). Nine feasible phenogenotypes with multiplicative fitness as in Fig. 5. Analyses of pure equilibria aaAC, aaAD, aaBC, aaBD, bbBD and effects of a replicator dynamic with strength α.",Captures the model structure that plays the role of a game in this theoretical context.,0.98
10.1006_jtbi.2003.3104.md,Replicator-augmented two-phenotype model (copying high-fitness phenotypes),Section 'Copying High-Fitness Phenotypes: The Replicator Dynamic' augments the previous two-phenotype model by adding a phenotypic copying process with parameter α; treated as a distinct analytical condition.,0.96,"Replicator strength α (0 ≤ α ≤ 1) alongside s, t, u, γ, v; examines how varying α affects stability of aaAC and aaBC equilibria.","Text states stability conditions become functions of α: for altruism equilibrium, α < (γ−s)/(1−γ); for non-altruism equilibrium, α > (γ−s)/(1+γ−s).",0.98,False,Still a purely theoretical model without data.,1,False,Analytical stability and simulations rather than experimental manipulation on subjects.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Author mentions using simulations to check stability due to unit root and complexity.,0.95,True,Derives conditions on α for stability of equilibria.,1,0,Population-level replicator dynamic; no explicit finite game group size.,1,0,Continuous or discrete-time dynamic without fixed-round game structure.,1,0,No discrete contribute/shirk choices per round.,1,N/A,No endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment game stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0,"Replicator dynamic changes copying behavior, not a punishment institution.",0.99,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No separate rewarding mechanism.,0.99,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No tokens or monetary assets.,1,N/A,"Behavioral outcome is phenotype distribution, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported; outcomes are stability conditions.,0.8,N/R,Group fitness not summarized as a numeric for a concrete parameter set.,0.7,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Theoretical/simulation analysis only.,1,"Extends two-phenotype model with a replicator dynamic: a-type individuals copy higher-fitness phenotypes among {BC, AC, BD, AD} with probability proportional to α and encounter frequencies. Shows that strong α can destabilize altruistic equilibrium aaAC and stabilize non-altruistic internalization aaBC even when γ > s.",Summarizes key structural change: addition of copying behavior and resulting conditions.,0.97
10.1006_jtbi.2003.3104.md,Multi-group agent-based simulation of altruism with migration and group selection,"Section 'Why is Altruism Predominantly Prosocial?' describes an explicit agent-based simulation with 256 groups, 100 members each, migration, group extinction and replacement.",0.99,"Group-level parameters drawn per group: γ ∈ [0,0.60], α ∈ [0,0.5], s ∈ [0.01,0.10], ζ ∈ [0,0.70], altruism group payoff parameter q ∈ [−1,1]; migration rate (typically 0.05, sometimes >0.20); minimum group size threshold (0 or 10) for extinction.","The simulation explicitly states ranges and values for γ, α, s, ζ, q, migration fraction, and group-size thresholds; these are the key varied inputs across conditions and runs.",1,False,Computer-based agent simulation without real subjects.,1,False,"Not an empirical experiment; parameters are chosen by the modeler, but there is no assignment of human subjects.",1,False,Simulation environment only.,1,True,Explicitly called an agent-based model; run many times over up to 100 rounds.,1,False,"This part reports only simulation results (a–h), not closed-form analysis of the multi-group system.",0.9,25600,"256 groups × 100 members initially per group = 25,600 agents simulated.",0.95,100,Author notes the system always stabilized by 100 periods and describes simulations of 100 rounds.,0.9,0,Agents do not make all-or-nothing contributions; their state is a phenogenotype evolving by rules.,1,N/A,No explicit contributions or endowments.,1,N/R,Public goods marginal return not defined; group payoffs arise via q and phenotype frequencies.,1,N/A,Simulated agents do not exchange messages in a chat sense.,1,N/A,No user interface; purely simulation.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action between identified agents; altruism is encoded in phenotypes and parameter q.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding actions.,1,N/A,No participants to display remaining rounds to.,1,0,"The model addresses altruistic norms broadly, not a specific punishment technology with defined costs and impacts per unit.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment stage with parameters.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism separate from phenotypic fitness effect q.,0.95,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,Agents do not receive monetary endowments; they just have phenotypes affecting fitness.,1,N/A,No contribution decision; key outcomes are phenotype and parameter distributions in equilibrium.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,"Outcomes described qualitatively (e.g., elimination of anti-social norms, high altruist fraction) without explicit efficiency metric.",0.7,N/R,"Group fitness improves with prosocial A (q>0), but numeric average payoffs per group are not reported.",0.6,N/A,"Agents are simulated, not human participants.",1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation run on a computer with no subjects.,1,"256 groups on a 16×16 torus, each with 100 agents initially. Initial composition per group: 10 aaAC, 10 aaBC, 74 bbBD, and one of each other possible type. Common parameters t=0.3, u=0.05. Per group: γ∈[0,0.60], α∈[0,0.5], s∈[0.01,0.10], ζ∈[0,0.70], and altruism group payoff parameter q∈[−1,1]. Group average fitness increased by f·q if fraction f of group exhibits phenotype A. Each round: within-group gene–culture update, migration of ~5% to neighboring groups (torus neighborhood), random drift in s, α, γ, ζ, q, and extinction/replacement of groups when size falls below 0 or 10. Results after ≈100 rounds: near-fixation of altruistic phenotype (>85%), elimination of anti-social norms (q<0), high mean γ (≥0.45), lower mean α (~0.09), dependence of altruistic equilibrium on assortative mating ζ ≥ 0.125.",Summarizes key structural design and principal outcomes of the agent-based model relevant to cooperation and altruism.,0.99
10.1098_rstb.2010.0157.md,"N/A – narrative review, no single focal experiment described","The paper is a conceptual/narrative review that cites many different empirical and theoretical works but does not itself report a specific, clearly described experiment or condition with full design details.",1,N/A,No original experimental manipulation or distinct conditions are reported by the authors; they summarize others’ studies very briefly without sufficient per-condition detail.,1,False,The article contains no new data collection with human or non-human participants; it only reviews prior literature.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run by the authors; referenced experiments belong to other papers and are not described with full protocols.,1,False,No original lab or field experiment is conducted in this paper.,1,False,"No agent-based, numerical, or other computer simulations are presented; only verbal/theoretical discussion.",1,False,"Although the paper discusses theoretical concepts, it does not present new formal mathematical models or derivations; it is a qualitative review.",1,0,"No game with explicitly specified players is implemented by the authors; any numbers (e.g., four players in a standard public goods game) refer to generic paradigms from the literature.",1,0,No repeated game is actually played in this article; discussion of iterated games is conceptual only.,1,0,"No choice structure is implemented; the field is not applicable but requires a numeric value, so set to 0 with explanation.",1,N/A,"No human experiment is run, so no actual endowment allocation exists.",1,N/R,"The public goods paradigm is described verbally (e.g., pool is doubled and divided among four players), but no explicit marginal per capita return is identified as a parameter of a specific implemented experiment in this paper.",0.5,0,No experimental interface is implemented; communication conditions are only discussed conceptually.,1,0,No feedback or summary screens are implemented in this review.,1,0,No punishment interface is implemented; identity revelation is only discussed conceptually.,1,0,No reward interface is implemented.,1,0,"No actual rounds, so no round information is shown.",1,0,Punishment is discussed as a mechanism but no specific punishment option is implemented within an experiment reported in this article.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is implemented in an original experiment by the authors.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment technology.,1,0,"Rewards are conceptually discussed, but no implemented reward mechanism is reported.",1,N/A,No implemented reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No implemented reward technology.,1,0,"Although standard public goods examples implicitly involve an endowment, no concrete endowment for an actual experiment in this paper is specified.",1,N/R,No numerical results for contribution proportions from an original experiment are reported.,1,N/R,No raw contribution data are presented.,1,N/R,No efficiency metrics from original experiments are reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff data are presented.,1,N/A,No human participants are used in original research within this paper; only literature is reviewed.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,The article reports no new empirical study with human subjects; it is a theoretical/narrative review.,1,"Conceptual descriptions of standard game-theoretic paradigms (Prisoner’s Dilemma, public goods game, indirect reciprocity with image scoring/standing) with no original implementation by the authors.","All game structures mentioned (e.g., four-player public goods game, iterated PD, reputation and punishment variants) are described as canonical paradigms or via citations, not as newly run experiments.",1
