10.1016_j.jrp.2009.01.003.md,"Dictator game condition (δ=1, powerless recipient)",Paper explicitly describes a dictator game where δ=1 and the recipient is powerless; treated as one condition within the within-subject design.,1,"Power of recipient in allocation game: δ=1 (dictator game, recipient powerless)",Only experimental manipulation is power of the recipient via δ parameter; this row corresponds to the dictator game (δ=1).,1,True,Human participants (N=134) completed web-based personality scales and economic games.,1,True,Controlled web-based task with specified game rules and a manipulated variable (recipient power) qualifies as an experiment.,1,False,"Study conducted as a web-based survey, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; behavior is from human participants.,1,False,No formal mathematical model or analytical derivations as primary method.,1,2,Both dictator and ultimatum games are standard two-player (allocator and recipient); text references allocator and (hypothetical) other.,0.95,1,Each game played once with a single allocation decision; no mention of repetition.,0.95,1,"Allocator chooses any amount of points out of 100 for self; implies continuous/any amount, not forced all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Not a contribution game with a public account; allocation is a division of a fixed sum between self and other, so the concept does not apply well. Set to 0 to reflect that points are initially under allocator’s control (private).",0.4,N/R,"Dictator/ultimatum division task, not a linear public good; no marginal per capita return reported.",1,0,Web-based study with hypothetical other; no mention of any communication between players.,1,0,Participants simply allocate points; no feedback or summaries of others’ earnings/punishments described.,0.95,0,No punishment mechanism in the game; thus no punishment identities to reveal.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the game; thus no reward identities to reveal.,1,0,Participants perform a one-shot allocation; no mention of displaying total or remaining rounds.,0.9,0,"In dictator game with δ=1, recipient cannot change payoffs; no explicit punishment option for either player.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present in this condition.,1,0,No mechanism for costly rewarding; only allocation of a fixed pie between self and other.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present in this condition.,1,100,Participants asked to divide a total of 100 points between themselves and the other in each game.,1,0.36,Average points allocated to self in dictator game is 64 (SD=18); thus average allocated to other is 36. Normalized contribution to other: 36/100 = 0.36.,0.95,36,"From Table 1, dictator game mean is 64 points to self; with total 100 points, implied mean to other is 36.",0.95,N/R,No report of efficiency defined as group payoff relative to maximum; only allocations to self are reported.,1,N/R,"Total payoff is always 100 points (no discounting in dictator game), but authors do not report it as a dependent variable.",0.7,Germany,Authors are from German institutions and HEXACO-PI used is the German version; sample recruited via German community sources is implied though not named explicitly.,0.6,"17-66 (M=30, SD=9.9)","Reported in Participants section: aged 17-66, M=30, SD=9.9.",1,64 female; remaining 70 male/other (not specified),Participants: N=134 (64 female); no further breakdown reported.,0.9,"About half students (high school or university), about half employed",Participants section: about half were students (high school or university) and the other half were in employment.,1,Online,Described explicitly as a web-based study and internet-based experimenting.,1,"Single-shot dictator game implemented in a web survey. Participants act as allocator, dividing 100 points between self and a hypothetical recipient; no real monetary stakes.","Summary of procedural description in Methods and Discussion (web-survey, hypothetical other, not actually paid).",1
10.1016_j.jrp.2009.01.003.md,"Ultimatum game condition (δ=0, maximally powerful recipient)","Paper explicitly describes an ultimatum game where δ=0 and the recipient can accept or reject, leading to zero for both if rejected; treated as the second condition.",1,"Power of recipient in allocation game: δ=0 (ultimatum game, maximally powerful recipient)",Only manipulated feature is recipient power via δ; this row corresponds to the ultimatum game (δ=0).,1,True,Same human participant web study as dictator condition.,1,True,Within-subject experimental manipulation of recipient power in a controlled web-based game.,1,False,Conducted online rather than in a physical lab.,1,False,"Human behavior, not a computer simulation.",1,False,No analytical model presented as the primary method.,1,2,Standard ultimatum game between allocator and recipient; text refers to two players (allocator and recipient).,0.95,1,Game repeated once after dictator game; each is a one-shot decision with no indication of multiple rounds.,0.95,1,Allocator chooses an amount of points out of 100 for self; implies a continuum of possible splits.,1,0,"Not a contribution-to-public-good structure; one allocator divides an initial 100 points between self and other, effectively treating all as in allocator’s control.",0.4,N/R,"Ultimatum bargaining task, not a linear public good with defined MPCR.",1,0,No communication between allocator and recipient is described; recipients are hypothetical.,1,0,No feedback on others’ payoffs or choices is mentioned.,0.95,0,Rejection is the only sanction and recipient is hypothetical; no identities are revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism beyond accepting an offer; no identities involved.,0.9,0,Task presented as a single decision; no interface information about number of rounds reported.,0.9,1,"Recipient can reject offer, leading to zero payoff for both; this is a punishment opportunity via δ=0.",1,N/R,Rejection is not modeled as a per-unit costly punishment choice for the recipient; no per-point cost or structure is specified.,0.3,N/R,"Rejection causes both to get zero, but authors do not specify a unit punishment technology in terms of points per cost.",0.3,0,No separate costly reward mechanism; acceptance simply implements proposed split.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,100,"As in dictator game, participants divide a total of 100 points between themselves and the other.",1,0.45,Mean self-allocation in ultimatum game is 55 points (Table 1); thus recipient receives 45. Normalized contribution to other: 45/100 = 0.45.,0.95,45,"With total 100 points and mean self-allocation 55, implied mean allocation to other is 45.",0.95,N/R,"Group payoff of 100 occurs if offer accepted; rejections would reduce it, but no efficiency statistic is reported.",1,N/R,"Total payoff depends on acceptance/rejection probabilities, which are not reported as outcomes.",0.4,Germany,Same sample as dictator condition; German-language HEXACO-PI used; authors German-based.,0.6,"17-66 (M=30, SD=9.9)",Same N=134 sample; demographics reported once for the whole study.,1,64 female; remaining 70 male/other (not specified),Same sample as dictator condition.,0.9,"About half students (high school or university), about half employed",Same description applies to both game conditions.,1,Online,Same web-based implementation as dictator condition.,1,"Single-shot ultimatum game implemented online. Allocator divides 100 points; recipient (hypothetical) can accept or reject; if rejected, both receive zero (δ=0). Decisions were hypothetical with no real monetary payments.","Based on Methods description of ultimatum game (with δ=0, maximum power of recipient) and Discussion noting lack of real payoffs.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2005.02.008.md,"Baseline – 10-token CPR, no communication",Section 3 describes baseline experiments with 10 tokens per subject and no communication as the initial design.,1,Endowment size = 10 tokens; Communication = none,The only structural difference within the baseline design is between 10-token and 25-token endowments; both have no communication.,1,True,Conducted with undergraduate students at Indiana University.,1,True,Controlled laboratory CPR game with manipulated endowments and information.,1,True,Described as experiments in an experimental laboratory at Indiana University.,1,False,Human subjects; no computer-only agents in this condition.,1,False,No purely analytical model; focus is on lab data.,1,8,Hackett et al. (1994) and Schmitt et al. (2000) explicitly refer to the same baseline as 8-player; Ostrom et al. (1994) baseline CPR design is 8 subjects.,0.9,25,Paper notes baseline experiments varied between 20 and 30 rounds; later communication designs reference 10 baseline + 15 more (to 25). Choose 25 as typical for original baseline reported in Ostrom et al. (1994).,0.3,1,Subjects allocated any number of tokens out of their endowment between two markets; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,All tokens start as private endowment that subjects choose to invest in Market 1 or CPR (Market 2).,1,N/R,Only total transformation function F(sum xi) is given; per-capita marginal return at equilibrium is not explicitly stated and varies with total investment.,0,0,Baseline experiments explicitly disallowed communication.,1,1,"After each round, subjects learned aggregate investment and could infer aggregate outcomes; implies summary info about group behavior.",0.7,0,No punishment mechanism in baseline.,1,0,No reward mechanism in baseline.,1,1,Subjects were told the number of rounds in each experiment (20–30).,0.8,0,Baseline design has only allocation between markets; no sanctioning option.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism absent.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Baseline 10-token experiments allocated 10 tokens to each subject per round.,1,N/R,"Only average percent of maximum earnings (37%) is reported, not average tokens invested.",0,N/R,Narrative describes overinvestment and pulsing but no numeric mean investment level.,0,0.37,"Text: 'subjects in the 10-token experiments achieved, on average, 37 percent of the maximum earning'.",1,N/R,"Absolute payoffs per group are not given, only proportions and per-person payoffs under predicted/optimal strategies.",0,USA,"Experiments conducted at Indiana University (Bloomington, Indiana, USA).",1,N/R,Described only as 'students from the general undergraduate population'.,0.3,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Explicitly states recruitment from 'general undergraduate population'.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment with students physically attending a session.,1,Common-pool resource game with non-linear transformation function F(Σxi)=23Σxi−25(Σxi)^2; outside option Market 1 with fixed return w=$0.05 per token; CPR outcome units pay $0.01 each; subjects informed of payoff function and given look-up tables; Nash prediction: each invests 8 tokens (total 64); social optimum: total 36 tokens.,Summarizes core payoff structure and predictions described in Sections 3.1–3.2.,1
