10.1016_j.jebo.2005.02.008.md,"Sanctioning – 25-token CPR, no communication, with costly punishment","Section 5.1 describes sanctioning experiments using the 25-token design, adding a costly punishment option after baseline play without prior communication.",1,"Endowment size = 25 tokens; Punishment option enabled; Fee per sanction ∈ [0.05, 0.20]; Fine per sanction ∈ [0.10, 0.80]; Communication = none",Key manipulated variables: availability and cost structure of sanctions; all experiments use 25 tokens and do not allow communication before sanctioning phase in this subsection.,1,True,Human-subject experiments with monetary incentives.,1,True,Controlled non-cooperative game with additional punishment stage.,1,True,Same lab setting as other Indiana University experiments.,1,False,Human players choose sanctions.,1,False,"While a game-theoretic model exists, data come from lab play.",1,8,Sanctioning builds on the 25-token baseline design with 8 subjects.,0.9,20,Text: 'Subjects played ten rounds of the baseline game ... In the subsequent rounds they would have an opportunity to pay a fee'; typical design in Ostrom et al. (1992) is 10 baseline + 10 sanctioning rounds.,0.7,1,Allocation decision between outside option and CPR remains continuous.,1,0,Endowment initially in private control.,1,N/R,Same CPR technology as baseline; MPCR not summarized as a constant.,0,0,No mention of communication opportunities in initial sanctioning experiments; described as baseline then sanctioning stage.,0.9,1,Subjects saw total outcomes and individual contributions were reported in this modified baseline before sanctioning.,0.9,0,Paper does not state that punished players knew who sanctioned them; sanctions are treated as anonymous monetary reductions.,0.4,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Subjects knew there would be 10 baseline and then further sanctioning rounds.,0.7,1,Core of this treatment is the ability to 'pay a fee in order to impose a fine'.,1,N/R,Costs vary across experimental variants between $0.05 and $0.20; no single value for this combined treatment row.,0.3,N/R,Fine per fee varies between $0.10 and $0.80; no single ratio across all variants.,0.3,0,Only fines (negative sanctions) are used.,1,N/A,No reward mechanic.,1,N/A,No reward mechanic.,1,25,All sanctioning experiments used the 25-token design.,1,N/R,Paper reports resulting efficiency but not average investment levels.,0,N/R,No numeric allocations reported.,0,0.39,Text: 'able to increase their returns modestly from the CPR to 39 percent of maximum' (before subtracting sanctioning costs).,1,N/R,Only percent of maximum is given; not absolute payoffs.,0,USA,Same Indiana University lab.,1,N/R,Subjects are undergraduates; no ages.,0.3,N/R,No gender data.,0,Undergraduate students,Same recruitment pool.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment with physical subjects.,1,"After 10 rounds of 25-token baseline game (with individual contributions reported), subsequent rounds add a punishment stage where a subject may pay a fee (between $0.05 and $0.20 depending on treatment) to impose a fine (between $0.10 and $0.80) on another player’s payoff. Sanctions are primarily targeted at high CPR investors. Sanctioning increases CPR returns to 39% of maximum, but when costs of fees and fines are subtracted, overall gains disappear.",Summarizes Section 5.1 sanctioning design without pre-play communication.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2005.02.008.md,Sanctioning + single communication round – 25-token CPR,Section 5.1 describes a variant where subjects had 'a single opportunity to communicate prior to the implementation of sanctioning capabilities'.,1,Endowment size = 25 tokens; Punishment option enabled; Single face-to-face communication opportunity before sanctioning phase,Independent variables differing from previous sanctioning treatment: presence of one communication round prior to sanctioning.,1,True,Human laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled introduction of communication plus sanctions.,1,True,Same Indiana University lab series.,1,False,Not simulation.,1,False,Empirical design.,1,8,Same 25-token CPR baseline groups.,0.9,20,Design parallels other sanctioning experiments (10 baseline + 10 with sanctions after a communication opportunity).,0.6,1,Allocation decision unchanged.,1,0,As in baseline.,1,N/R,Not reported numerically.,0,1,Subjects given 'a single opportunity to communicate' before sanctions are implemented.,1,1,Same feedback as other sanctioning games (individual contributions and total outcomes).,0.8,0,No mention that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.4,0,No reward option.,1,1,Subjects informed about treatment sequence and total duration.,0.6,1,Sanctioning mechanism same as other sanctioning experiments.,1,N/R,Same range of fees/fines as other sanctioning experiments; no single value.,0.3,N/R,Fee-to-fine ratios vary across variants.,0.3,0,Only punishment studied.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,25,All sanctioning experiments use 25 tokens.,1,N/R,Only efficiency outcomes reported.,0,N/R,No allocation amounts presented.,0,0.85,Text: 'they gained an average of 85 percent of the maximum payoffs' before subtracting sanctioning costs.,1,N/R,Absolute payoffs not provided.,0,USA,Same Indiana University experiments.,1,N/R,Undergraduates; ages not given.,0.3,N/R,No information.,0,Undergraduate students,Same subject pool.,1,On site,Lab setting with face-to-face communication.,1,"Design identical to 25-token sanctioning experiments, but subjects first have one face-to-face group discussion to agree on strategies before sanctions are enabled. With this communication plus sanctioning, they achieve 85% of maximum payoffs (69% when fees and fines are subtracted).",Section 5.1 explicitly separates this from no-communication sanctioning treatment.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2005.02.008.md,Endogenous rule-making – groups that adopt covenant,Section 6 describes an experiment where groups choose whether to adopt a covenant specifying investments and fines; four out of six groups adopt it.,1,Endogenous covenant adopted: subjects collectively specify CPR investment levels and fine/fee structure; Endowment = 25 tokens,Independent variable at condition level is whether the group adopts an endogenous covenant.,1,True,Human subjects previously in baseline and sanctioning experiments are recalled.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment where institutional rules are endogenously chosen.,1,True,Same Indiana University laboratory environment.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Empirical behavioral data.,1,8,Groups drawn from earlier 25-token 8-subject sessions.,0.9,N/R,Paper does not report number of rounds in covenant phase.,0,1,Underlying CPR allocation decision unchanged.,1,0,"Endowments are private; covenant sets planned investments, not initial allocations.",0.9,N/R,Same CPR technology; MPCR not given.,0,1,"Subjects 'given an opportunity to choose whether or not they would like to adopt a sanctioning mechanism' and to craft agreements, implying discussion.",0.8,1,"As in previous sanctioning experiments, contributions and outcomes are observable for monitoring.",0.8,0,Not stated that punishers are identified; assume anonymity as in prior sanctioning designs.,0.4,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Number of covenant-phase rounds not described.,0,1,"Groups can adopt their own sanctioning mechanism, and covenant groups do so with specified fines.",1,N/R,"Fines chosen range between $0.10 and $1.00, but associated fee per unit is not specified.",0,N/R,Relationship between sanctioning cost and imposed fine not numerically described.,0,0,Only punishment (fines) discussed.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,25,All such groups 'were endowed with 25 tokens in each round'.,1,N/R,Only efficiency and defection rates reported.,0,N/R,No numeric investments reported.,0,0.93,Groups with covenants 'were able to achieve an average of 93 percent of the maximum in the periods after their agreement'.,1,N/R,Absolute group earnings not given.,0,USA,Same Indiana University lab subjects.,1,N/R,No age data.,0.3,N/R,Not provided.,0,Undergraduate students,Subjects came from same earlier experiments with undergraduates.,1,On site,Laboratory recall sessions.,1,"Subjects previously exposed to baseline and sanctioning games are recalled. Groups are asked whether to adopt a covenant specifying each member’s token investment and the sanctioning system (fines and fees). Four of six groups adopt covenants, choosing fines between $0.10 and $1.00. In periods after agreement, they attain 93% of maximum and only 4% defection rate.",Summarizes Section 6 description for covenant-adopting groups.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2005.02.008.md,Endogenous rule-making – groups without covenant,Section 6 reports outcomes separately for the two groups that did not adopt a covenant.,1,Endogenous covenant not adopted (no agreed joint investment or fine structure); Endowment = 25 tokens,Condition distinguished by absence of adopted covenant while having same opportunity.,1,True,Same human-subject experiment as covenant condition.,1,True,Controlled treatment based on endogenous institutional choice outcome.,1,True,Laboratory sessions at Indiana University.,1,False,Human participants.,1,False,"Empirical, not purely theoretical.",1,8,Same group size as covenant condition.,0.9,N/R,Number of rounds after decision not to covenant not specified.,0,1,Underlying allocation decision remains same.,1,0,Endowments remain private.,0.9,N/R,Same CPR technology; MPCR not given.,0,1,"Groups had opportunity to discuss whether to adopt a sanctioning mechanism, though ultimately failed to agree.",0.7,1,Monitoring and sanctioning framework implies access to others’ contributions and/or outcomes.,0.7,0,No explicit mention of identities of punishers being revealed.,0.4,0,No rewards.,1,N/R,Not described.,0,0,These groups 'lacked agreement' on the covenant including fines; text does not say they implemented any sanctioning mechanism.,0.7,N/A,Assuming no operative punishment scheme without covenant.,0.7,N/A,No punishment.,0.7,0,No reward mechanisms mentioned.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,25,All groups in this endogenous-rule experiment had 25 tokens each round.,1,N/R,Only efficiency and defection rates for these groups are given.,0,N/R,No average allocations.,0,0.56,Text: 'They averaged 56 percent of the maximum available returns'.,1,N/R,No absolute earnings reported.,0,USA,Conducted at Indiana University.,1,N/R,"Undergraduates, ages not given.",0.3,N/R,Not specified.,0,Undergraduate students,Same subject pool as covenant groups.,1,On site,Lab experiment.,1,Groups have opportunity to design their own rules and sanctions but fail to reach agreement. These groups achieve only 56% of maximum returns and exhibit a 42% defection rate relative to any informal understandings.,Section 6 contrasts performance of non-covenant groups to covenant groups.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2005.02.008.md,Exogenous rule – Colombian villagers with imperfectly enforced optimal rule,Section 7 summarizes Cardenas et al. (2000) experiments in Colombia with an externally imposed optimal rule and probabilistic monitoring.,1,External rule imposed: maximum allowed forest-harvesting time per round set at social optimum; Monitoring: 50% chance of inspection each round and 1/8 chance that any given subject is inspected when inspection occurs (overall 1/16 per subject per round); Endowment measured as potential months in forest,Independent variables are presence of exogenous rule plus monitoring probability; harvest decision is months in forest.,1,True,Over 200 villagers participate in field-lab experiments.,1,True,Controlled payoff tables and probabilistic monitoring; treatment contrasts with communication-only conditions.,1,False,Conducted as field laboratories in rural Colombia rather than standard university lab.,1,False,Human villagers as subjects.,1,False,Empirical behavioral evidence.,1,8,"Monitoring rule: experimenter draws from chits numbered 1–8 to decide who is inspected, implying 8 players per group.",1,N/R,Number of decision rounds in these exogenous-rule sessions not reported here.,0,1,Villagers choose any integer number of months to spend in forest each year; not constrained to all-or-nothing.,0.9,0,Participants decide how much time to allocate to the common forest vs. other activities; initial time allocation is private choice.,0.7,N/R,Payoff tables not detailed here; only aggregate efficiency is discussed elsewhere.,0,0,"In this specific exogenous-rule treatment, focus is on rule enforcement by external monitor, not on communication; text compares its outcomes to prior communication treatments.",0.7,0,Monitoring outcome is private: 'No statement was made to others whether the appropriator was complying'.,1,0,Others are not informed who was inspected or penalized; identities of punished players not revealed.,1,0,Only penalties are used; no rewards.,1,1,Standard practice in these experiments; but exact wording not provided. Assume subjects knew experiment length.,0.5,1,Violators inspected above limit incur a penalty subtracted from their payoff.,1,N/R,Penalty magnitude not quantified in this summary.,0,N/R,Mapping between violation size and penalty magnitude is not specified.,0,0,No positive monetary rewards other than normal payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Exact maximum months per year that can be spent in the forest not given here.,0,N/R,Paper states that harvesting levels increased relative to communication-only but does not give normalized values.,0,N/R,No numeric months in forest reported for this treatment.,0,N/R,Only qualitative comparison ('actually increased their harvesting levels' vs. communication) given.,0,N/R,No payoff magnitudes reported.,0,Colombia,Described as 'field laboratories in rural Colombia' with villagers.,1,N/R,No age information.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,Mixed; rural villagers with varied education,Sample consists of rural villagers who are actual forest users; education levels not specified but clearly not exclusively students.,0.4,Field experiment,Cardenas et al. conduct 'field laboratories' with villagers in their communities.,1,"Forest commons game framed in terms of months per year harvesting forest products versus other activities. External authority imposes a rule limiting individual months in forest to the optimal level. Each round, experimenter rolls a die: even roll triggers an inspection; then a random subject among 8 is inspected. Thus each has 1/16 chance per round of inspection. If above the limit, a penalty is deducted from payoff; others are not informed whether the subject complied or was punished. Compared to communication-only treatments, this exogenous rule with modest enforcement leads to higher individual harvesting and lower joint returns, interpreted as crowding out group-regarding behavior.",Summarizes design in Section 7.,1
10.1007_s12110-005-1017-0.md,SCCS cross-cultural correlational study (single observational dataset),The paper analyzes one Standard Cross-Cultural Sample dataset of 186 societies; no distinct experimental conditions or sub-samples with different treatments are defined.,1,"Level of moralizing high gods (SCCS variable 238) coded 1: absent/not reported; 2: present but not active; 3: present and active but not supportive of morality; 4: present, active, and supportive of human morality.","The core predictor throughout is SCCS variable 238 'high gods', treated as an ordinal index of supernatural punishment likelihood.",1,True,Uses ethnographic/archival data about human societies (SCCS).,1,False,No randomized or controlled manipulation; purely observational correlations across societies.,1,False,No lab or field experiment with tasks; secondary analysis of archival cross-cultural data.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,Paper reports statistical analyses of data rather than purely formal/mathematical models.,1,0,There is no game-theoretic experiment with players; data are at the society level.,1,0,No repeated game or rounds; cross-sectional observational study.,1,0,No contribution decision by individual players is modeled or implemented.,1,N/A,No endowment or contribution task with human subjects.,1,N/R,No public goods game with a defined MPCR is conducted.,1,N/A,No experimental interaction between participants.,1,0,Not applicable; no feedback or summaries are shown to decision-makers in an experiment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism in an experimental game.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in an experimental game.,1,0,No rounds exist to be displayed.,1,0,"No implemented punishment option in a game; supernatural punishment is a measured belief, not a game mechanic.",1,N/A,No punishment choice or cost in an experiment.,1,N/A,No punishment technology defined.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism in an experiment.,1,N/A,No experimental rewards for peer sanctioning or similar.,1,N/A,No reward technology specified.,1,0,No monetary or token endowment given to participants.,1,N/R,"No direct behavioral measure of contribution in a public goods game; cooperation is proxied by societal variables like money use, policing, taxation.",1,N/R,No individual-level contribution amounts are recorded.,1,N/R,No payoff structure or efficiency metric of a formal game is defined.,1,N/R,Study does not define or compute group payoff from a game.,1,Multiple world regions (186 societies in SCCS),"Data derived from 186 societies across Africa, Circum-Mediterranean, East Eurasia, Insular Pacific, North America, and South America (SCCS region variable 200, Figure 1).",1,N/R,SCCS codes society-level characteristics; no systematic individual age data.,1,N/R,"Gender composition of coded information is not specified; units are societies, not individuals.",1,N/R,Education levels of individuals are not reported in the SCCS variables used.,1,Observational,The design is a cross-cultural observational analysis of existing ethnographic codes; no intervention or lab/online environment.,1,"No game; main DV proxies are society-level indicators of cooperation and social organization: money and credit systems (17,18), community size and political hierarchy (63,235,237), policing and sanctions (90,776,777,1743), taxation (784), norm compliance and loyalty (775,778,779), food sharing (1718), and internal conflict/warfare (several variables including 1649,1748-1750).","Summarized from Methods, Measures of Cooperation, Table 2, and Appendix descriptions of SCCS variables.",1
10.1103_PhysRevX.7.041027.md,Spatial public goods game with four strategies (Monte Carlo simulation on square lattice),"Paper describes a single modeling setup: a spatial public goods game on a square lattice with four strategies (C, D, PC, PD), varied over r, β, γ. This corresponds to one simulation environment rather than distinct experimental treatments with human subjects.",0.96,"Multiplication factor r (3.0 vs 3.8), punishment fine β, punishment cost γ; strategies available: C, D, PC, PD (all always available).","They systematically vary r (focus on r=3.8 and r=3.0), β (punishment fine), and γ (punishment cost) while keeping other parameters fixed. Strategy set is fixed to C, D, PC, PD.",0.99,False,No human participants; study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on a lattice.,1.0,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or natural subjects; it is a computational model.,1.0,False,No physical laboratory or human subjects are involved.,1.0,True,They explicitly describe Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice.,1.0,False,"While equations define payoffs, core results come from numerical Monte Carlo simulations, not closed-form analytical derivations.",0.9,25.0,"They use an L×L square lattice with L from 400 to 6000. Since only the structure of local groups matters for the public goods game, the effective interacting group size is G=5 (player plus 4 nearest neighbors). PlayerCount here is interpreted as group size G.",0.95,1000000.0,"They run Monte Carlo simulations until stationarity, with relaxation time between 10^4 and 10^6 MCS depending on parameters. Since a single fixed number is required, the maximum reported 10^6 MCS is entered as an upper bound.",0.4,1,"Each cooperator (C or PC) contributes a fixed amount 1; defectors contribute 0. There is no option for partial contributions, so decision is all-or-nothing in terms of cooperating vs defecting. Encoding 1 for all-or-nothing allowed because players choose between two discrete options, not a continuous contribution amount.",0.8,0.0,"Contributions are decided in each game; there is no pre-assigned initial amount in the public pool. Endowment effectively starts fully in the private account until a strategy chooses to contribute, so proportion in public fund at default is 0.",0.7,0.76,"MPCR is multiplier r divided by group size G=5. They use r=3.8 and r=3.0. For r=3.8, MPCR=3.8/5=0.76; for r=3.0, MPCR=0.6. As a single entry is required, we report the MPCR for r=3.8, explicitly noted here.",0.6,0,Model is purely simulation with no communication among agents beyond payoff-based imitation.,1.0,0,Agents only observe neighbors’ strategies and payoffs implicitly through the imitation rule; there is no reporting of summaries or histories.,0.9,0,"Simulation agents respond to payoffs; there is no concept of revealed identities for punishers, and punishment is automatic via payoff functions.",0.9,0,"There is no rewarding in the model, only punishment, so identification of rewarders is irrelevant. Encoded as 0.",0.9,0,Agents do not track or see the number of Monte Carlo steps; strategy updates depend only on current payoffs.,0.9,1,"Two strategies, PC and PD, impose punishment; β and γ define fine and cost.",1.0,1.0,"γ is the maximal cost of punishment a punisher can bear if all others in group are targets. Each punisher pays γ·(number of relevant targets)/(G−1). Cost per unit fine is effectively γ/β, but since schema asks for punishmentCost as coins spent per unit, using γ as basic cost scale set to 1 unit here is not directly possible. Given ambiguity and that units are abstract, we set a normalized value 1 to indicate cost is positive and nonzero; specific γ values vary.",0.2,1.0,"β is the maximal fine imposed if all other players punish a target. Fine per punisher is β/(G−1). As a technology multiplier per cost unit, the effective mapping is β/γ, but this varies with parameters. A generic normalized 1 is entered to indicate presence of punishment technology; exact per-unit mapping not fixed.",0.2,0,"The model includes only punishment (prosocial and antisocial), no rewards.",1.0,N/A,No rewarding mechanism in this model.,1.0,N/A,No rewarding mechanism in this model.,1.0,1.0,Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount equal to 1; this can be interpreted as the per-round endowment.,0.95,N/R,"They report stationary fractions of strategies, not average contributions normalized by endowment. Contribution rate could be inferred from fractions of C and PC (since they always contribute 1), but they do not give numerical stationary fractions in text or tables.",0.3,N/R,"Same as contributionRate: raw contributions can be expressed as number of cooperators per group, but no explicit numerical outcome values (averages) are provided.",0.3,N/R,"They discuss dominance of cooperation, coexistence phases, and pattern formation, but do not provide explicit aggregate payoff ratios relative to maximum.",0.2,N/R,No explicit numerical group payoff outcomes are reported; only qualitative strategy fractions and phase diagrams.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Monte Carlo simulation with artificial agents on a lattice.,1.0,"Spatial public goods game on an L×L square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; overlapping groups of size G=5 (von Neumann neighborhood). Four strategies: C (cooperator, contributes 1), D (defector), PC (cooperator who punishes defectors), PD (defector who punishes cooperators). Contribution sum is multiplied by r and divided equally among group members. Punishment: PC punishes D and PD; PD punishes C and PC. Each punished player incurs fine β·(number of punishers)/(G−1); each punisher pays cost γ·(number of targets)/(G−1). Strategy updating is by pairwise imitation using the Fermi function with noise parameter K=0.5. Initial strategy distribution is random with equal probabilities across the four strategies. System sizes L range from 400 to 6000; relaxation times from 10^4 to 10^6 Monte Carlo steps until a stationary state is reached.","Summarizes the detailed model description, payoff structure, network topology, and update rule provided in the methods section.",1.0
10.1287_mnsc.2015.2168.md,Experiment 1 – Stage A C-Culture,"Stage A repeated PD environment with cooperative parameters in Experiment 1, labeled C-Culture.",1,"Culture treatment in Stage A: C-Culture (δ = 7/8, PD payoffs: (C,C)=4, (D,C)=5, (C,D)=0, (D,D)=1; initial endowment 40 MU).","Paper specifies two Stage A treatments (C-Culture vs D-Culture) differing in continuation probability, PD payoffs, and initial endowment.",1,True,Human subjects in lab.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled game parameters.,1,True,Conducted at Harvard Decision Sciences Laboratory.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; all decisions by humans.,1,False,No purely analytical model as the primary result here; focus is on experiments.,1,2,Prisoner’s dilemma played in pairs.,1,53,Experiment 1 C-Culture: total 53 PD rounds in Stage A.,1,0,PD is binary cooperate/defect; not a contribution game.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game with a divisible endowment; binary PD choices instead.,1,N/A,"Stage A uses a prisoner’s dilemma, not a linear public good.",1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous PD in lab.,0.9,0,"Subjects see partner’s action and payoffs each round, but no aggregated summaries mentioned.",0.7,N/A,No punishment mechanism in Stage A PD.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism beyond PD payoffs.,1,0,Game uses stochastic continuation; subjects not told fixed total rounds.,0.8,0,No costly punishment option described in Stage A PD.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No separate costly reward action in Stage A.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,40,C-Culture subjects begin Stage A with 40 MU in Experiment 1.,1,N/R,Paper reports cooperation fraction over time qualitatively; no single aggregate normalized rate reported.,0.4,N/R,"Outcomes are PD payoffs, not contributions; no aggregate cooperation counts reported in units.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency or payoff-per-maximum metric reported for Stage A.,0.2,N/R,They report mean Stage A earnings including endowment but not separated by treatment and not by pair.,0.3,United States,Harvard Decision Sciences Laboratory with typical US student pool.,0.9,Mean 21.7,Table 1: Experiment 1 mean age 21.7.,1,37% female,Table 1 gives 37% female for Experiment 1 overall; no breakdown by treatment.,0.9,Primarily university students,Run in Harvard Decision Sciences Lab; typical subject pool is university students.,0.9,On site,Physical laboratory at Harvard.,1,"Infinite-horizon stochastic repeated prisoner’s dilemma with δ = 7/8; 10 games using pre-drawn length sequence; payoffs in MU: (C,C)=4 each, (D,C)=5/0, (C,D)=0/5, (D,D)=1 each; same payoff matrix for all pairs.",Design section for Stage A Experiment 1 C-Culture.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2015.2168.md,Experiment 1 – Stage A D-Culture,"Stage A repeated PD environment with defection-favoring parameters in Experiment 1, labeled D-Culture.",1,"Culture treatment in Stage A: D-Culture (δ = 1/8, PD payoffs: (C,C)=4, (D,C)=6, (C,D)=0, (D,D)=1; initial endowment 150 MU).","Stage A treatments differ in δ, payoffs, and endowment; this row is the D-Culture settings.",1,True,Human subjects in lab.,1,True,Random assignment to C- vs D-Culture at session level.,1,True,Harvard Decision Sciences Laboratory.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Primary results are experimental.,1,2,Prisoner’s dilemma in pairs.,1,51,Experiment 1 D-Culture: total of 51 PD rounds in Stage A.,1,0,"Binary C/D decision, not an all-or-nothing contribution game.",1,N/A,Not a divisible contribution game.,1,N/A,"Prisoner’s dilemma, not a linear public good.",1,0,No communication reported among PD partners.,0.9,0,Only partner’s action and payoffs per round described; no summaries.,0.7,N/A,No punishment action.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward option.,1,0,Number of rounds is stochastic and not fixed ex ante.,0.8,0,No costly punishment in Stage A.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No separate reward action besides PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,150,D-Culture subjects in Experiment 1 begin Stage A with 150 MU.,1,N/R,Only qualitative statement of low cooperation; no single aggregate normalized rate given.,0.4,N/R,No aggregate count provided for number of C choices or equivalent.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency ratio reported for Stage A.,0.2,N/R,Stage A earnings by treatment are mixed with starting endowment; no pure per-pair payoff.,0.3,United States,Harvard lab with US-based student sample.,0.9,Mean 21.7,Same Experiment 1 pool; Table 1.,1,37% female,Experiment 1 aggregate; treatment-specific not reported.,0.9,Primarily university students,Subjects recruited at Harvard lab.,0.9,On site,Conducted in physical lab facilities.,1,"Infinite-horizon stochastic repeated prisoner’s dilemma with δ = 1/8; 45 games totalling 51 rounds; payoffs in MU: (C,C)=4 each, (D,C)=6/0, (C,D)=0/6, (D,D)=1 each; chosen so cooperation is not an equilibrium.",Stage A design for D-Culture in Experiment 1.,1
