ntical in payoffs to Experiment 1 C-Culture (δ = 7/8, (C,C)=4, (D,C)=5, (C,D)=0, (D,D)=1), but total of ~40 rounds split into 7 games, with pre-drawn game lengths reused across sessions.",Stage A description for Experiment 2.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2015.2168.md,Experiment 2 – Stage A D-Culture,Stage A repeated PD environment in Experiment 2 for defection-favoring D-Culture treatment.,1,"Culture treatment in Stage A: D-Culture (δ = 1/8, PD payoffs: (C,C)=4, (D,C)=6, (C,D)=0, (D,D)=1; initial endowment 150 MU).",Stage A D-Culture specification same as Experiment 1 except total length; endowment 150 MU in Experiment 2 as well.,0.9,True,Human lab experiment.,1,True,"Randomized treatment, controlled game.",1,True,Run at Harvard Decision Sciences Laboratory.,1,False,Human participants only.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,2,Pairwise PD.,1,40,"Stage A shortened to approximately 40 rounds; exact distribution is in appendix, but ~40 is best available from main text.",0.6,0,C/D binary.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,"PD, no MPCR.",1,0,No mention of communication.,0.9,0,Only round outcomes described.,0.7,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,0,"Random termination, expected length 1.14 rounds per game; total not revealed.",0.8,0,No punishment in Stage A.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,150,"Experiment 2 D-Culture initial endowment is 150 MU (same as Experiment 1, noted for controlling income differences).",0.9,N/R,They report 22% cooperation in D-Culture pooled across Stage A but not detailed per subject or time; here we only know low cooperation overall.,0.7,N/R,Only action frequencies provided.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency for Stage A.,0.2,N/R,Stage A total MU earnings per treatment not broken out from initial endowment in main text for Experiment 2.,0.3,United States,Harvard-based participants.,0.9,Mean 21.8,Experiment 2 mean age.,1,45% female,From Table 1.,1,Primarily university students,Harvard Decision Sciences Lab participants.,0.9,On site,Laboratory experiment.,1,"Stochastic repeated prisoner’s dilemma with δ = 1/8, expected game length 1.14 rounds; 35 games totaling ~40 rounds; PD payoffs: (C,C)=4 each, (D,C)=6/0, (C,D)=0/6, (D,D)=1 each; chosen so no cooperative equilibrium.",Experiment 2 Stage A D-Culture description.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2015.2168.md,Experiment 2 – Stage B C-Culture treatment,Stage B punishment games in Experiment 2 for subjects previously in C-Culture Stage A.,1,Prior Stage A environment: C-Culture (high cooperation) when playing Stage B punishment games.,Independent variable is being from C- vs D-Culture; this row pertains to C-Culture.,1,True,Human subjects in punishment games.,1,True,Random assignment of Stage A treatments with Stage B as outcome.,1,True,Conducted in lab.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,Outcomes are empirical.,1,4,Maximum group size in Stage B Experiment 2 games is 4 (3PPD and PGP).,1,1,All Stage B games are one-shot with strategy method; no repetition.,1,1,"In PGP and 3PDG/3PPD, punishment and contributions can be chosen in discrete increments; PGP contributions from 0 to 100 in steps; not restricted to all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,"In PGP, subjects start with 100 MU and choose how much to contribute; all initially private.",1,0.4,"PGP uses same PGG as Experiment 1: 100 MU endowment, contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly split among 4 players (1.6/4 = 0.4).",1,0,No communication described in Stage B games.,0.9,0,"Strategy method, no feedback between games; no summary outputs mentioned.",0.9,0,No mention that identities are revealed; standard anonymous experimental design implies punishment targets are anonymous within role labels.,0.7,0,3PPD contains reward option but no indication that identities revealed; same anonymity as punishment.,0.7,1,Participants informed they face a battery of specified one-shot games with strategy method.,0.9,1,"All Stage B games in Experiment 2 have punishment components: 3PDG, 3PPD (punish and reward), PGP.",1,1,"In all punishment contexts, each MU spent by sanctioner reduces target payoff by 5 MU; cost per unit of punishment is 1 MU for punisher.",1,5,"Each punishment MU spent reduces target’s payoff by 5 MU in 3PDG, 3PPD, and PGP.",1,1,3PPD includes an option for sanctioners to spend MU to increase PD players’ payoffs (reward).,1,1,Each MU spent on reward costs sanctioner 1 MU.,1,5,"In 3PPD, each MU spent on rewarding increases the player’s payoff by 5 MU.",1,100,Punishers/sanctioners in all Stage B games receive 100 MU endowment; PGP contributors also have 100 MU.,1,N/R,Paper focuses on punishment patterns relative to contributions and cooperation; no aggregate normalized contribution rate in PGP reported by treatment.,0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric averages for PGP contributions or other transfers reported by treatment.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency outcomes reported for Stage B Experiment 2.,0.2,N/R,Group earnings in punishment games not summarized numerically.,0.2,United States,Harvard lab subject pool.,0.9,Mean 21.8,Experiment 2 mean age.,1,45% female,Experiment 2 overall composition.,1,Primarily university students,Harvard Decision Sciences Lab.,0.9,On site,Laboratory environment.,1,"Stage B Experiment 2 battery (strategy method): (1) Third-Party Dictator Game (3PDG): groups of 3 (dictator, recipient, third-party sanctioner). Dictator splits 100 MU (options 0,10,20,30,40,50). Sanctioner has 100 MU, can spend up to 20 MU to reduce dictator’s payoff by 5 MU per MU spent, contingent on dictator’s transfer. (2) Third-Party Prisoner’s Dilemma with Punishment and Reward (3PPD): groups of 4 (two PD players, two sanctioners). PD players choose C or D with payoffs (C,C)=80, (D,C)=120/0, (C,D)=0/120, (D,D)=20 each. Each sanctioner has 100 MU and decides, for each possible PD action of a given player, how many MU (0–20) to spend on punishing (−5 MU per MU to target) and on rewarding (+5 MU per MU to target). (3) Public Goods Game with Punishment (PGP): groups of 4 play same PGG as Experiment 1 (100 MU, contribution 0,25,50,75,100; multiplier 1.6, equal division) then each can spend up to 20 MU punishing each group member conditional on that member’s contribution; each MU spent reduces target’s payoff by 5 MU. All games use strategy method, and subjects also rate social inappropriateness (1–7 Likert) of each potential target behavior.",Section 4.1 details Experiment 2 Stage B design.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2015.2168.md,Experiment 2 – Stage B D-Culture treatment,Stage B punishment games in Experiment 2 for subjects previously in D-Culture Stage A.,1,Prior Stage A environment: D-Culture (low cooperation) when playing Stage B punishment games.,Same Stage B design; independent variable is prior culture; this row is D-Culture.,1,True,Human decision-makers.,1,True,Random assignment of cultures; Stage B observed behavior.,1,True,Harvard Decision Sciences Lab.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,Empirical study.,1,4,Maximum group size across Stage B games.,1,1,Each punishment game is played once via strategy method.,1,1,"Contributions and punishment are selectable quantities, not all-or-nothing decisions.",0.9,0,"In PGP, endowment is initially private.",1,0.4,Same PGG as Experiment 1.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,0,Strategy method with no inter-game feedback.,0.9,0,Identities not mentioned; assume anonymity as in standard lab protocols.,0.7,0,3PPD reward is anonymous; no identities specified.,0.7,1,Participants informed of set of one-shot games in Stage B.,0.9,1,"3PDG, 3PPD, and PGP all allow punishment.",1,1,Each MU spent on punishment costs sanctioner 1 MU.,1,5,Each MU spent reduces target’s payoff by 5 MU.,1,1,3PPD has third-party reward.,1,1,Rewarding in 3PPD costs 1 MU per MU invested.,1,5,Rewarding increases target payoff by 5 MU per MU spent.,1,100,Sanctioners/punishers have 100 MU endowments.,1,N/R,No detailed PGP contribution rate reported separately by treatment.,0.4,N/R,No numeric mean contributions per treatment given.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency of punishment/public-good outcomes not reported.,0.2,N/R,No group payoff averages shown for Stage B Experiment 2.,0.2,United States,Harvard lab sample.,0.9,Mean 21.8,Experiment 2 average.,1,45% female,Table 1.,1,Primarily university students,Harvard Decision Sciences Lab.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical lab.,1,"Identical Stage B structure to C-Culture: 3PDG with 100 MU dictator endowment and 100 MU sanctioner endowment, sanction rate 5:1; 3PPD with PD payoffs (80,80;120,0;0,120;20,20) and sanctioners with 100 MU endowment each who can punish or reward (5:1); PGP with PGG (100 MU, contribution 0/25/50/75/100, multiplier 1.6) followed by punishment decisions (up to 20 MU per other, 5:1 impact). Strategy method and inappropriateness ratings on 1–7 scale in all games.",Experiment 2 Stage B design same across culture treatments.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_14_4_043013.md,Adaptive punishment – spatial public goods game on square lattice,Single adaptive-punishment model described in Section 2; no multiple parameter-specific conditions are given as distinct experiments.,0.7,Synergy factor r; punishment increment Δ; punishment cost parameter α; comparison to steady-punishment model (πx fixed at 1 for punishers). This row refers to the adaptive punishment case.,"Text and Figure 1 describe varying r, Δ, and α and comparing to a steady punishment variant.",1,False,Model is a computer simulation on a square lattice; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a numerical evolutionary-game simulation.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; purely computational.,1,True,They implement the spatial public goods game on a lattice and use Monte Carlo updates; clearly a computer simulation.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations; results are from numerical phase diagrams and spatial snapshots.,1,0,No fixed number of players; simulations use various lattice sizes L=200–7000 so player count N=L^2 varies.,0.3,0,"They report running up to 10^7 Monte Carlo steps depending on parameters, not a single fixed round count.",0.4,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount 1; defectors contribute 0; there is no decision about partial contributions, so in choice terms it is all-or-nothing between C and D. However, contribution level itself is fixed, not chosen.",0.5,N/A,No human endowment allocation; strategies directly set contributions in the simulation.,1,0.2,"Group size G=k+1=5 and the public good is multiplied by r then divided equally, so MPCR=r/5; r varies across simulations so a single numeric value is not fixed. For a normalized structural parameter we report MPCR as r/5; using 0.2 corresponds to r=1 as a baseline.",0.3,0,No communication or messaging between agents is modeled.,1,0,Agents update via payoff comparison; there is no explicit display of summaries analogous to experimental feedback.,0.9,0,No concept of observable identities; interactions are purely payoff-based and local.,1,0,No rewards are modeled; only punishment.,1,0,Agents have no knowledge of total or remaining rounds; infinite-horizon evolutionary updating.,1,1,Punishing cooperators fine defectors with amount πxΔ/k; punishment is central feature.,1,1,"Cost per punisher per punished defector per unit punishing activity is πx αΔ/k; cost-to-fine ratio is α. Normalizing by fine Δ/k, unit cost per fine unit is α; α varies, but the structural cost coefficient is α.",0.4,1,Each unit of punishing activity imposes fine πxΔ/k per defector; fine grows linearly with πx and Δ; we treat the per-unit effectiveness as 1 in normalized units.,0.4,0,"No rewarding mechanism is modeled, only punishment.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes 1 to the public good; defectors contribute 0. This is effectively the per-round endowment in normalized units.,0.8,N/R,"They report stationary fractions of cooperators ρC and defectors ρD, but not normalized contribution rates relative to endowment as a separate statistic.",0.4,N/R,"Outcome reporting focuses on phase diagrams (C, D, C+D) and spatial patterns, not average per-player contributions.",0.4,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (total payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,0.2,N/R,They do not provide numeric group payoff values; focus is on strategy fractions and phase boundaries.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a computational evolutionary-game model on a lattice with no human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a square lattice with degree k=4 (von Neumann neighborhood), group size G=5, cooperators contribute 1, defectors 0; contributions multiplied by synergy factor r then divided equally. Adaptive punishment variable πx increases by 1 when a neighboring cooperator is replaced by a defector and decreases by 1 every second round if defectors fail to spread. Defectors fined πxΔ/k per punishing cooperator; punishing cooperators incur cost πxαΔ/k per punished defector. Strategy and punishment updates follow pairwise comparison with Fermi rule with K=0.5; Monte Carlo simulation with L=200–7000, up to 10^7 MCS; phase diagrams in r–Δ plane for several α.",Summarizes detailed model specification from Section 2 and related descriptions.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_14_4_043013.md,Steady punishment – spatial public goods game on square lattice (comparison model),Section 2 describes a steady punishment variant used for direct comparison; punishers have fixed πx=1 and punishment does not adapt.,0.9,Synergy factor r; punishment increment Δ (interpreted as fixed fine level); punishment cost parameter α; presence of a fixed punishing cooperator strategy versus non-punishing cooperators and defectors.,They state that simulation details are identical but punishing cooperators start with πx=1 and it never changes.,1,False,Again a lattice-based computer simulation without human subjects.,1,False,"Numerical simulation, not a controlled human experiment.",1,False,No physical lab; purely computational.,1,True,Steady punishment model is implemented via Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice.,1,False,No analytical treatment; results shown via spatial snapshots and qualitative comparison.,1,0,"Same lattice-based setup as adaptive model, with L varied; no single fixed player count.",0.3,0,Also run for many Monte Carlo steps until relaxation; not a fixed round design.,0.4,1,Players are either cooperators contributing 1 or defectors contributing 0; no intermediate contribution choice.,0.5,N/A,No human endowment; contributions fixed by strategy.,1,0.2,"Same public goods structure as adaptive model: group size 5, contributions multiplied by r then equally shared; MPCR=r/5 with r varied.",0.3,0,No communication implemented.,1,0,No explicit feedback summaries; only local payoff-based imitation.,0.9,0,No identities or observability beyond local strategy states.,1,0,No rewards are modeled.,1,0,Agents are not informed about horizon length.,1,1,Punishing cooperators always fine defectors with fine Δ/k and bear cost αΔ/k.,1,1,"Cost per punisher per punished defector is αΔ/k, fine is Δ/k, so unit cost per fine unit is α; α varies across simulations but structure is fixed.",0.4,1,Each punishing cooperator imposes a fine Δ/k per punished defector; linear one-to-one mapping per cost unit normalized by α.,0.4,0,No rewards in steady punishment model either.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,"As in adaptive model, cooperators contribute 1 and defectors 0; 1 is effectively the per-round endowment.",0.8,N/R,No explicit average contribution rates reported for this variant; only qualitative comparisons in spatial patterns.,0.3,N/R,They do not provide numeric contribution amounts specific to this steady punishment variant.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency statistics shown for steady punishment model.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff values for this variant are not quantified.,0.2,N/A,Simulation only; no people.,1,N/A,Simulation only; no people.,1,N/A,Simulation only; no people.,1,N/A,Simulation only; no people.,1,No human,Steady punishment model is also a computational simulation.,1,"Same spatial public goods structure as the adaptive model (square lattice, k=4, group size 5, contributions multiplied by r and shared). Difference: a subset of cooperators are designated as punishing cooperators with fixed punishing activity πx=1 that never changes. These punishers fine every defector in their group with amount Δ/k and incur cost αΔ/k per punished defector. When they spread, new punishing cooperators also have πx=1; when overtaken by non-punishing cooperators or defectors, they lose punishing ability. Used as a benchmark to compare to adaptive punishment, especially in spatial-pattern snapshots (figures 2–4).",Summarizes the steady punishment variant as described in the paragraph comparing with [39].,1
10.1016_s1090-5138(01)00093-9.md,Defensive war scenario – punishment and reward attitudes,Single survey condition where participants respond to a defensive (Russian invasion of Alaska) war draft scenario; no random assignment to different incentive or punishment structures.,1,Scenario type fixed as defensive interstate war; measured predictors: SELF-INTEREST IN GROUP GOAL and WILLINGNESS TO PARTICIPATE (both continuous Likert ratings).,"The only manipulated feature is the vignette content (defensive war); within this condition, self-interest and willingness are measured predictors rather than experimentally manipulated treatments.",1,True,Human undergraduate participants completed pencil-and-paper surveys.,1,True,"Although main predictors are measured, participants are exposed to a standardized hypothetical scenario, with controlled question order as a minor manipulation; treated here as an experimental survey condition.",0.7,True,Conducted on a university campus via classroom administration and a recruitment booth; this matches an on-site lab-style setting.,0.9,False,No computer or agent-based simulations were used.,1,False,"Paper reports empirical survey data, not purely analytical or mathematical modeling.",1,1,Each respondent evaluates a hypothetical collective action alone; there is no interactive game with multiple decision-making players in real time.,0.6,1,Single-shot scenario judgment; no repeated interaction or rounds.,0.9,1,"There is no actual contribution decision; when asked about agreeing to serve, it is conceptually a binary serve/refuse choice rather than a continuous contribution level. However, since no real choice is made, coded as allowing any sentiment response.",0.3,N/R,No endowment or contribution structure is defined; only attitudes in a hypothetical draft scenario are measured.,1,N/R,No numeric public goods game or multiplier is specified in the survey scenario.,1,0,Participants complete surveys individually with no opportunity to communicate with others during decision-making.,0.9,0,"Respondents receive no information about others’ answers, earnings, or punishments.",1,0,Punishment is only an attitude rating toward anonymous draft resisters; no identities are revealed or discussed.,1,0,Reward targets are generic drafted citizens; identities are not specified.,1,0,Scenario does not mention rounds or repeated plays.,1,1,"One key item asks whether citizens who resist the draft 'should be punished', explicitly invoking punishment in the scenario evaluation.",1,N/R,No specific cost in money or points per unit of punishment is defined; only attitudes are measured.,1,N/R,Magnitude or technology of punishment is not quantified.,1,1,"One item asks whether citizens who agree to serve 'should be rewarded', explicitly invoking rewards.",1,N/R,No numeric cost per reward unit is described.,1,N/R,Type or magnitude of reward is not specified numerically.,1,N/R,There is no monetary or token endowment; only hypothetical personal risk and benefit.,1,N/R,No actual contributions or normalized contribution rates are made; only willingness-to-participate ratings.,1,N/R,There are no quantified contributions in tokens or money.,1,N/R,No payoff structure or maximum efficiency benchmark is defined.,1,N/R,No monetary or point payoffs are computed for groups.,1,USA,"Participants are described as 'undergraduate US citizens at the University of California, Santa Barbara'.",1,18-25,Method section: 'Subjects were 18-25-year-old undergraduate US citizens'.,1,"53% female, 47% male",Method section states 53% (n=152) female and 47% (n=135) male.,1,Undergraduate university students,Participants are described as undergraduate students at UCSB.,1,On site,"Surveys administered in an anthropology class and at a campus booth, both in-person on campus.",1,"Non-interactive hypothetical collective action (war draft) vignette; key measures are Likert-scale ratings: SELF-INTEREST IN GROUP GOAL, WILLINGNESS TO PARTICIPATE, PUNISH NONPARTICIPANTS, REWARD PARTICIPANTS, and GENERAL PUNITIVENESS. Some surveys reversed the order of punishment and reward items.","Summarizes described survey structure and measured constructs to capture the strategic setting, though no formal game is played.",1
10.1016_s1090-5138(01)00093-9.md,Offensive war scenario – punishment and reward attitudes,Single survey condition where participants respond to an offensive (oil price conflict with Middle Eastern countries) war draft scenario; same participants but analytically distinct condition.,1,Scenario type fixed as offensive interstate war; measured predictors: SELF-INTEREST IN GROUP GOAL and WILLINGNESS TO PARTICIPATE (both continuous Likert ratings).,"The manipulated scenario content is offensive war; within this condition, self-interest and willingness are measured predictors, not experimental treatments.",1,True,Human undergraduate participants completed pencil-and-paper surveys.,1,True,Participants evaluated a standardized hypothetical offensive war scenario with controlled question order; treated as an experimental survey condition.,0.7,True,Conducted in-person on campus via classroom and booth administration.,0.9,False,No non-human simulation was conducted.,1,False,Study reports empirical survey data rather than purely analytical models.,1,1,Each respondent independently evaluates the hypothetical offensive war draft scenario; no real-time multi-player interaction.,0.6,1,Single-shot scenario judgment without repetition.,0.9,1,"No actual contribution is made; conceptually the decision to agree to serve is binary, but only attitudes are measured.",0.3,N/R,No endowment or contribution structure exists in the survey vignette.,1,N/R,No formal public goods payoff function is provided.,1,0,Respondents complete surveys individually; communication is not part of the design.,0.9,0,Participants do not see summaries of others’ answers or payoffs.,1,0,Punishment is an attitude toward anonymous draft resisters; identities are not shown.,1,0,Rewards are attitudes toward generic draftees; identities are not shown.,1,0,No mention of number of rounds or remaining rounds.,1,1,"Item asks if citizens who resist the draft 'should be punished', explicitly involving punishment.",1,N/R,No specific costs per unit punishment described.,1,N/R,Magnitude or implementation of punishment is not quantified.,1,1,Item asks if drafted citizens who agree to serve 'should be rewarded'.,1,N/R,No numeric reward cost is discussed.,1,N/R,Type/magnitude of reward is unspecified.,1,N/R,No monetary or token endowment is part of the design.,1,N/R,There is no actual contribution decision or amount that could be normalized.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions are made.,1,N/R,"No payoff structure, so efficiency cannot be defined.",1,N/R,Group payoffs are not calculated in points or money.,1,USA,All participants were US citizens studying at UCSB.,1,18-25,Participants are specified as 18-25-year-old undergraduates.,1,"53% female, 47% male",Same participant pool as in defensive scenario: 53% female and 47% male.,1,Undergraduate university students,"Described as undergraduate students at the University of California, Santa Barbara.",1,On site,Surveys administered in-person in classes and at a campus booth.,1,"Non-interactive hypothetical collective action (offensive war draft) vignette; key Likert outcomes identical to the defensive scenario: SELF-INTEREST IN GROUP GOAL, WILLINGNESS TO PARTICIPATE, PUNISH NONPARTICIPANTS, REWARD PARTICIPANTS, and GENERAL PUNITIVENESS. Order of punishment and reward items is counterbalanced for a subset of respondents.",Describes the strategic and measurement structure for this scenario-based condition.,1
