10.1073_pnas.0808450106.md,"Simulation – Compulsory public goods, C and D only","Paper and Fig. 2A describe compulsory public goods interactions without punishment, with only cooperators and defectors present, across mutation rates.",0.9,"Game type: compulsory public goods; Strategy set: {C, D}; Mutation rate μ varied","Condition differs by compulsory vs voluntary and by inclusion of punishment/loners; here it is compulsory, no punishment, no loners; μ is systematically varied.",0.9,False,"Study uses evolutionary game theory, simulations, and analytical approximations; no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; entirely theoretical/simulation.,1,True,They report individual-based simulations and numerical solutions of Fokker-Planck equations for evolutionary dynamics.,1,True,They derive and use analytical Markov chain and Fokker-Planck approximations for the same condition.,1,5,"Parameter list for figures: “parameters: M=100, N=5, r=3, c=1; β=1, γ=0.3; σ=1”; N is group size.",1,1,"Evolutionary model with repeated random group formation over many update steps; not organized in explicit rounds, so set to 1 per interaction.",0.3,1,"Standard public goods game described as each cooperator paying cost c; no partial contribution options discussed, so players choose cooperate (pay c) or defect (pay 0). This is effectively all-or-nothing at the strategy level.",0.7,N/A,Endowments and contribution defaults are not modeled at this granularity; only strategy types are modeled in simulations without human UI.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/N. Parameters: r=3, N=5, so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,N/A,No user interface; model-level payoffs only.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No human participants or display.,1,0,Condition is the standard public goods game without punishment; only C and D are present.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism absent.,1,0,"Only punishment, not reward, is discussed elsewhere; this condition has neither.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/R,"Cost c is specified (c=1), but no explicit endowment or maximum contribution is given.",0.2,N/R,"Figures report abundance of strategies C and D, not normalized contribution rate; could be inferred as fraction of C but not explicitly labeled as such.",0.4,N/R,"They do not report average total contributions numerically, only qualitative dominance of D.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure (group payoff relative to maximum) is numerically reported.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs are discussed conceptually but without explicit numeric averages for this condition.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; simulation/analytical study.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results here are from evolutionary game simulations and analytical models.,1,"Finite well-mixed population of size M=100, compulsory N-player public goods game with parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, with imitation dynamics and mutation rate μ varied.",Parameter values and dynamics description are given explicitly around Figs. 1 and 2.,1
