10.1073_pnas.0808450106.md,"Simulation – Voluntary public goods with punishment and loners, C, D, L, and P","Text discusses ‘combine all 4 options’ (C, D, L, P) and the role of loners and punishers for small and large μ; parameters same as figures.",0.9,"Game type: voluntary public goods; Strategy set: {C, D, L, P}; Mutation rate μ varied",Condition includes both voluntary participation through loners and punishment via P; μ is systematically studied.,0.9,False,No new behavioral experiment; discussion is based on the same evolutionary simulation/analytical framework.,1,False,No human experiment conducted for this 4-strategy case.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,"They refer to transition matrices, Markov chain stationary distributions, and qualitative behavior; these imply underlying individual-based simulations analogous to other conditions.",0.8,True,Detailed description of small-μ Markov chain stationary distribution (probabilities p and 1−3p) and large-μ Fokker-Planck behavior for all four strategies.,1,5,They state using a ‘generic choice of parameters’ consistent with N=5 used elsewhere; no alternative N given for this case.,0.8,1,Evolutionary update model; not structured in explicit repeated rounds.,0.3,1,Same public goods structure: discrete strategies of cooperate vs defect vs abstain; no partial contributions.,0.7,N/A,No endowment/UI; strategies determine payments.,1,0.6,"Generic parameter choice satisfies r<N; they later use r=3, N=5; adopt same unless otherwise specified.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,No identity display modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,1,"Punishers P are one of the four strategies, punishing defectors at cost γ with fine β.",1,0.3,Same punishment parameterization: γ=0.3.,0.9,1,Fine magnitude parameter β=1 in other sections; assume same here.,0.7,0,No reward option in this 4-strategy model.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,"As elsewhere, only c, β, γ, σ given; no explicit endowment.",0.2,N/R,"They give stationary probabilities of being in pure states for small μ (probabilities of C, D, L, P pure states) but not an explicit per-period contribution rate.",0.5,N/R,No aggregate contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,Qualitative discussion of success of cooperation and punishment but no normalized efficiency values.,0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoff values shown.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely theoretical/simulation analysis.,1,"Finite population M (large) public goods game with optional participation and punishment; strategies C, D, L, P; loners receive fixed payoff σ with 0<σ<(r−1)c; punishment parameters satisfy β>γ and r<N; dynamics studied for small μ via embedded Markov chain on pure states and for large μ via Fokker-Planck approximation.",All these structural properties and parameter inequalities are stated explicitly in Methods/Results.,1
10.1073_pnas.1105456108.md,Baseline – no monitor,"Paper describes a baseline condition: six rounds of a public goods game without punishment, used as comparison to both monitor treatments.",1,"Sanctioning institution: no monitor (no centralized punishment), monitor selection mechanism not applicable.",Independent variable is the presence and type of centralized sanctioning (none vs random monitor vs elected monitor). This row is the no-monitor baseline.,1,True,"Game played by 1,543 Ugandan farmers randomly sampled from cooperatives.",1,True,Authors randomly assigned participants to three variants of a public goods game and manipulated monitor presence/selection.,1,True,"Lab-in-the-field experiment conducted on-site with controlled game rules, not a naturally occurring field intervention.",0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported; only human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical/mathematical model presented as a separate study; focus is on experimental data.,1,10,Sessions ranged from 8 to 12 players; midpoint 10 used as typical group size because exact per-condition group sizes not specified.,0.3,6,Baseline condition: 'players participated in six rounds of a PGG without punishment.',1,1,"Standard PGG with endowment of 10 coins per round; no indication contributions restricted to all-or-nothing, so any amount from 0–10 is implied.",0.8,0,"Standard PGG: 'what players put in the private account remains theirs, and what is contributed to the public account is doubled'; endowment initially in private account.",1,0.2,"Footnote describes public good technology: contributions to public account are doubled and redistributed evenly among all group members. Assuming group size g, MPCR = 2/g. Using typical group size 10 gives MPCR=0.2.",0.4,0,Subjects 'were not allowed to talk through the entire course of the experiment.',1,0,No mention of feedback beyond what is necessary to play; summaries of others' earnings/punishments not described.,0.3,0,No punishment stage exists in baseline condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism described in any condition.,1,1,"Standard experimental practice and design clarity for 6 fixed rounds; although not explicitly stated, players are typically told the number of rounds.",0.5,0,Baseline: 'players participated in six rounds of a PGG without punishment.',1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No rewarding institution or positive sanctioning described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,10,"Each round: 'Game participants were endowed, in each round, with 10 coins of 100 Ugandan shillings.'",1,N/R,"Paper reports percentage differences across conditions and some marginal effects, but not baseline normalized contribution rate.",0,N/R,Text provides relative increases and marginal effects but not mean MU contributions for baseline.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios (actual/maximum group payoff) reported.,0,N/R,Payoffs for groups or averages not reported; only changes in contributions and punishment effects are given.,0,Uganda,Subjects are 'Ugandan farmers' from cooperatives in nine districts across Uganda.,1,N/R,Age distribution or range of participants not reported in the article text.,0,Mixed; both male and female participants,Gender mix implied because elected monitors are mostly male; exact proportions not given.,0.6,"Low to moderate education; smallholder farmers, some with higher education","Monitors described as 'more educated' than others, suggesting varying but generally limited formal education; no exact levels reported.",0.4,Field experiment,Described as 'lab-in-the-field experiment' conducted with farmer associations in rural Uganda.,1,"Standard linear public goods game with 10-coin endowment per round; contributions to a public account are doubled and evenly redistributed among group members; only one randomly selected round is paid out, plus fixed participation and travel payments; sessions use natural farmer association groups with 8–12 participants; no communication allowed; anonymity of individual contributions is maintained.",Summarizes procedural and payoff structure details scattered across Materials and Methods and explanatory footnotes.,1
10.1073_pnas.1105456108.md,T1 – Random monitor with centralized punishment,Condition labeled 'random monitor condition (T1)' where a monitor is selected via random lottery and endowed with sanctioning power.,1,Sanctioning institution: centralized punishment with randomly selected monitor (T1); monitor selected by lottery after 2 preliminary rounds.,Independent variable is the existence and source of authority for the centralized monitor. This row is the random monitor treatment.,1,True,Human farmers participated in this game variant.,1,True,Assignment to T1 was random and rules were experimentally controlled.,1,True,Lab-in-the-field: structured experimental protocol implemented on site with farmer groups.,0.9,False,No simulation component reported for T1.,1,False,No pure analytical model reported for this treatment.,1,10,"Sessions had between 8 and 12 players; exact group sizes per treatment not specified, so 10 taken as a typical size.",0.3,6,"In treatment conditions, after 2 preliminary rounds without a monitor, the monitor is selected and game continues; total rounds of the main game are 6 (rounds 1–6), with punishment active from round 3 to 6.",0.7,1,"Same PGG structure as baseline: endowment of 10 coins, no restriction mentioned that contributions must be all-or-nothing.",0.8,0,Same as baseline: endowment starts as private and players decide how much to move to public account.,1,0.2,"Same linear PGG technology as baseline: contributions doubled and evenly redistributed; with typical group size ~10, MPCR ≈ 2/10.",0.4,0,Subjects 'were not allowed to talk through the entire course of the experiment.',1,0,No mention of summary feedback on others' payoffs or punishments; only individual-level consequences described.,0.3,1,"Monitors' 'identity was known by the group' and punishment is targeted; punished players must know they were sanctioned and by whom, implying identified punisher.",0.7,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,"As in baseline, likely that number of rounds was known; not explicitly contradicted.",0.5,1,Monitor 'could spend 1 MU to take away three MUs from players whose contribution level they disapproved of.',1,1,Explicitly: 'could spend 1 MU to take away three MUs.',1,3,Each 1 MU spent by monitor removes 3 MUs from punished player's payoff.,1,0,No positive sanction or reward described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same as baseline: 10 coins per round.,1,N/R,Paper reports that in T1 subjects contributed 15.0% more on average than in baseline but does not provide absolute contribution rates.,0.1,N/R,"Only relative changes (e.g., +15%, +16.6% in round 3) and marginal effects (e.g., +0.37 MUs) are reported, not mean contributions.",0.1,N/R,No efficiency ratios for T1 reported.,0,N/R,Group earnings not provided by condition.,0,Uganda,Same Ugandan farmer sample as baseline.,1,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0,Mixed; both male and female participants,Overall sample includes both genders; no indication that T1 differs.,0.6,"Low to moderate education; smallholder farmers, some with higher education",Same population as other conditions; no condition-specific differences in education reported.,0.4,Field experiment,Same lab-in-the-field implementation in farmer associations in Uganda.,1,"Structure mirrors baseline PGG with two preliminary rounds without a monitor, followed by selection of a random monitor via lottery. From round 3 onward the monitor observes contributions and can spend their own MUs to reduce others’ payoffs (1 MU cost to remove 3 MUs from a target). Monitors’ own payoffs depend only on their remaining endowment after sanctioning, not on group contribution levels. One randomly chosen round of the game is paid out.",Drawn from main design description and Materials and Methods.,1
10.1073_pnas.1105456108.md,T2 – Elected monitor with centralized punishment,Condition labeled 'elected monitor condition (T2)' where a monitor is chosen via secret ballot by group members and given sanctioning power.,1,Sanctioning institution: centralized punishment with elected monitor (T2); monitor selected via anonymous vote after 2 preliminary rounds.,Independent variable is the selection process for the centralized monitor (election vs random vs none). This row is the elected monitor treatment.,1,True,Same Ugandan farmer subject pool as other conditions.,1,True,Monitor election conducted within randomized experimental sessions.,1,True,Lab-in-the-field protocol with controlled voting and game rules.,0.9,False,No simulations reported for this condition.,1,False,No standalone analytical model; focus is empirical.,1,10,Sessions had 8–12 players; typical group size approximated as 10.,0.3,6,"Same six-round PGG structure as other conditions, with monitor active from round 3 to 6.",0.7,1,Same public goods contribution rules as baseline; no all-or-nothing restriction mentioned.,0.8,0,Endowment given to each player each round in private account; they decide how much to contribute.,1,0.2,Same linear PGG transformation (doubling and equal redistribution) as in other conditions; MPCR ≈ 2/group size ≈ 0.2 for a typical group of 10.,0.4,0,Participants 'were not allowed to talk through the entire course of the experiment.',1,0,No mention of aggregate feedback on others' payoffs or sanctions.,0.3,1,Monitor’s identity known to group; monitoring and punishment presumably observed by punished players and attributed to the known monitor.,0.7,0,No rewards are part of the design.,1,1,Likely same information about total rounds as in other conditions; not contradicted.,0.5,1,Elected monitor endowed with same sanctioning rule as random monitor: can spend MUs to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,1,Rule identical: 'could spend 1 MU to take away three MUs.',1,3,Each MU spent by monitor removes 3 MUs from targeted player.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same 10-coin endowment per round as in other variants.,1,N/R,"Paper notes that T2 contributions are 25.4% higher than baseline and 9% higher than T1 on average, but not the absolute normalized rate.",0.1,N/R,Only relative percentage differences and marginal MU changes are reported; means are not.,0.1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures (actual vs maximal group payoff) for T2.,0,N/R,Group payoff levels not reported by treatment.,0,Uganda,Same Ugandan farmer subjects as other conditions.,1,N/R,No age data reported.,0,Mixed; both male and female participants,"Elected monitors are 'mostly male', implying both genders among players; no different sampling in T2.",0.7,"Low to moderate education; smallholder farmers, some with higher education","Monitors in T2 tend to be 'more educated' relative to others, but overall population is farmer members; no precise levels.",0.4,Field experiment,Conducted as part of the same lab-in-the-field experiment in farmer associations.,1,"Identical PGG and punishment structure to T1 but the monitor is chosen by an anonymous secret ballot: each participant writes the ID number of their preferred monitor on paper. No communication allowed. Punishment from round 3 to 6 follows the same cost (1 MU) and impact (3 MU taken from target). Elected monitors tend to have a more socially dominant profile (male, wealthier, more educated, locally born), but their sanctioning pattern (number of players punished and maximum contribution sanctioned) is similar to random monitors when facing similar contribution distributions.",Summarizes treatment-specific voting procedure and behavioral findings regarding monitors in T2.,1
10.1016_j.tree.2011.12.004.md,Human n-player public goods games with punishment option (aggregate across cited lab studies),"The review describes a class of standard n-player public goods lab experiments where humans can pay to punish free-riders, citing [2-5,23-26], but does not separate them by specific treatment; aggregated as a generic condition type.",0.4,Presence of a costly punishment option in an n-player public goods game,Text: 'Players can punish free-riders by paying a small fee to impose a larger fine on the cheat.' contrasted with public goods games in general.,0.7,True,Described as 'laboratory experiments on humans' and 'public goods experiments'.,1,True,Controlled payoff-structured lab games with manipulated punishment options.,1,True,Explicitly referred to as 'laboratory experiments' and 'laboratory public goods experiments'.,1,False,"These are human-subject lab studies, not numerical simulations.",1,False,No indication of closed-form mathematical modeling in this description; it summarizes empirical results.,1,0,"Only described as 'n-player public goods games'; specific group size (e.g., 4) not given in this review.",0,0,Review mentions one-shot games and 'long-run benefits' but gives no specific round count for the generic n-player PGGs.,0.1,1,Standard PGGs allow contributing 'some or all' of the endowment; implies continuous/any-amount contributions rather than all-or-nothing.,0.9,0,"Glossary: 'players are endowed with an initial sum of money, some or all of which they may contribute to the communal pot' implies all endowment starts in private accounts.",0.9,N/R,The marginal per capita return is not numerically reported in the review.,0,0,"Standard Fehr & Gächter-style PGGs do not feature communication, and no communication is mentioned here.",0.6,1,"In standard PGG punishment experiments, contributions or actions of others must be known to assign punishment; although not detailed here, some information on others is necessarily shown.",0.6,0,"Canonical studies use anonymous punishment; the review does not discuss identity revelation, so assume anonymity.",0.5,0,These are punishment-focused PGGs; no reward mechanism is mentioned.,0.9,1,"In one-shot and finitely repeated lab PGGs, subjects are normally informed about the horizon; review references 'one-shot games' and 'iterated' structures, implying known horizons.",0.6,1,Explicitly: 'Players can punish free-riders by paying a small fee to impose a larger fine on the cheat.',1,0,Described only qualitatively ('small fee'); no numeric cost per unit reported.,0,0,Only that a 'larger fine' is imposed; no numeric fine-per-unit reported.,0,0,This passage discusses punishment only; no mention of a reward option in these n-player PGGs.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,0,Glossary describes 'an initial sum of money' but gives no numeric value.,0,0,Review states that punishment 'promotes cooperation' but gives no specific average contribution fraction.,0,0,No raw contribution amounts reported.,0,N/R,No explicit group efficiency values reported.,0,0,No numerical payoff data reported.,0,N/R,The review refers to many different lab studies in multiple countries; no single country is specified.,0,N/R,"Typical participants are often students, but no ages are noted in the review.",0,N/R,No gender composition information is given.,0,N/R,Subjects are typically university students but this is not explicitly stated.,0.2,On site,"Standard lab PGGs with human participants, conducted in experimental laboratories, not online as far as this review indicates.",0.7,Standard n-player public goods game with monetary endowments; players decide how much to contribute to a communal pot which is multiplied and redistributed equally. Additional stage where individuals can pay a fee to reduce others’ payoffs (costly punishment).,Summarized from the glossary definition of PGGs and the passage describing punishment option.,0.9
10.1016_j.tree.2011.12.004.md,Iterated two-player prisoner’s dilemma with punishment option (Dreber et al. 2008 type condition),The review summarizes a specific experimental design: 'Dreber et al. [6] used iterated two-player prisoner's dilemma games with and without a punishment option'. This row captures the 'with punishment' condition.,0.9,Presence of a costly punishment option in an iterated two-player prisoner’s dilemma; interactions last between 1 and 9 rounds,Text explicitly contrasts 'with and without a punishment option' and notes interaction length 'between one and nine rounds'.,1,True,Described as an experiment on humans (prisoner’s dilemma games).,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment option in a repeated game.,1,True,Fits typical economic lab experiment; no indication it’s field or online.,0.9,False,Human-subject study.,1,False,No formal analytical model is described in this summary.,1,2,Explicitly 'two-player prisoner's dilemma games'.,1,0,"Interactions last between one and nine rounds, but the exact distribution or expected rounds per pair is not given.",0.4,0,"In a standard PD, choices are binary: cooperate or defect; contribution is all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,"In PD there is no continuous endowment allocation to a public pot; default contribution proportion is not meaningful in this framework, but by convention private payoffs are determined solely by choices.",0.2,N/R,"MPCR concept does not directly apply to two-player PD here, and no numeric payoff matrix is provided.",0,0,No mention of communication; standard PD experiments of this type prohibit communication.,0.7,1,"To punish cheats, players must at least see the partner’s last action or outcome; thus some summary of partner behavior is shown.",0.7,1,"In a two-player game, the partner is always identifiable as the only other player.",0.9,0,No reward option described for this treatment.,1,1,"In typical 'between one and nine rounds' PD designs, subjects are usually informed about the continuation probability or structure; review notes limited time horizon but not hidden.",0.6,1,This is the 'with punishment option' condition.,1,0,Review does not report the numeric cost per unit of punishment.,0,0,No numeric mapping from punishment to fine reported.,0,0,Only punishment is mentioned as an additional option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,0,PD payoffs are per-round; the review does not specify any token endowment.,0,0,Outcome is PD cooperation rate; review says punishment promotes cooperative behaviour but punishers earn lower payoffs. No numeric cooperation rate given.,0,0,There is no continuous contribution; cooperate/defect are binary and no numeric counts are provided.,0,N/R,No group efficiency values reported.,0,0,Only relative payoff ranking is given: punishers < non-punishers; no absolute values.,0,N/R,Country is not specified in the review summary.,0,N/R,Typical PD lab study subjects (students) not described in text.,0,N/R,No information on gender is reported.,0,N/R,No explicit statement about student status or education level.,0,On site,Described as a lab-style experimental game without mention of online implementation.,0.7,"Iterated two-player prisoner’s dilemma with an added costly punishment option. Players repeatedly choose cooperate or defect, and in the punishment treatment they can pay to reduce the partner’s payoff after observing behaviour. Interactions last between 1 and 9 rounds; punishment increases cooperation but punishers earn lower payoffs than non-punishers, while reciprocal defectors earn the most.",Summarized from the paragraph discussing Dreber et al. [6].,1
10.1016_j.tree.2011.12.004.md,Iterated two-player prisoner’s dilemma without punishment option (Dreber et al. 2008 control),Control condition in the described Dreber et al. design: iterated PD 'without a punishment option'.,0.9,Absence of a punishment option in an iterated two-player prisoner’s dilemma; interactions last between 1 and 9 rounds,The independent variable is the availability of punishment; this row is the 'without punishment' level.,1,True,Part of the same human-subject experiment.,1,True,Control arm of a controlled experiment.,1,True,Same lab-context as punishment treatment.,0.9,False,"Human-subjects, not computational.",1,False,No analytical derivations here.,1,2,Two-player game.,1,0,Same 1–9 round structure; exact expected number not given.,0.4,0,Binary cooperate/defect PD choice.,0.9,0,No explicit endowment-based contribution structure; PD framework.,0.2,N/R,Payoff matrix details not provided.,0,0,No communication mentioned.,0.7,1,Players must see partner actions each round to respond strategically.,0.7,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,1,Same reasoning as for punishment treatment (known limited horizon).,0.6,0,Explicitly 'without a punishment option'.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No mention of rewards in either condition.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,0,No numeric endowment described.,0,0,Cooperation rate in no-punishment PD is not given numerically.,0,0,Binary cooperate/defect; no numeric report.,0,N/R,No payoff or efficiency statistics reported.,0,0,"Only relative comparison is that reciprocal defectors (strategy, not condition) yield highest payoffs in the punishment treatment; no baseline numeric payoffs.",0,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,On site,Same lab setting as punishment treatment.,0.7,"Iterated two-player prisoner’s dilemma without any punishment stage. Players repeatedly choose between cooperate and defect over 1–9 rounds with a limited time horizon. In comparison with the punishment treatment, players relying on reciprocal defection achieve high payoffs.",Inferred as the control arm of the described Dreber et al. study.,0.9
