no explicit rewarding of good behaviour.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No monetary or token endowment in this ecological interaction.,1,0,"Outcome discussed is 'jolt rate' (cheating) decreasing, not a numeric cooperation fraction; no values reported.",0,0,No token contributions measured.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric is described.,0,0,No summed payoff measures reported.,0,N/R,Field site (reef location) is not specified here; likely tropical reefs but no country is given.,0,N/R,Ages of fish not provided.,0,N/R,Sex of clients not reported in this summary.,0,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,Field experiment,"Described as observations 'under natural conditions' on reefs; observational field study, but still empirical with no imposed treatments.",0.8,"Mutualistic interaction between bluestreak cleaner wrasse (Labroides dimidiatus) and client reef fish. Cleaners can 'cheat' by biting mucus, detected via client jolts. Clients sometimes respond by chasing (punishment), which reduces subsequent cheating (jolt rate).",Summarized from the paragraph on Bshary & Grutter [7].,1
10.1016_j.tree.2011.12.004.md,Cleaner wrasse mutualism – experimental condition with client punishment prevented (anaesthetised clients),"The review describes an experiment where clients are anaesthetised to prevent them from punishing, to test causality.",1,Client punishment ability experimentally disabled by anaesthetising clients; cleaner behaviour observed,"Text: 'Experimentally preventing clients from punishing cleaners (by anaesthetising them), showed that punishment causally promotes cooperative behaviour from cleaner fish [7].'",1,True,Real fish in a manipulated experimental setup.,1,True,Client ability to punish is experimentally manipulated via anaesthesia.,1,False,"Likely conducted in field or semi-natural conditions with wild-caught fish; the review does not specify tanks vs reef, but manipulation of wild mutualism suggests field-type experimental setup.",0.4,False,Involves live animals.,1,False,No analytical model described.,1,2,Interaction unit is a cleaner and a single client individual whose punishment capacity is manipulated.,0.8,0,Repeated interactions but no discrete round number reported.,0,1,No token contributions; behaviour is cooperate (clean ectoparasites) vs cheat (bite mucus).,0.3,N/A,No endowments or contributions framework.,1,N/R,No public good or defined multiplier.,0,0,Non-human fish; no communication channel manipulated.,1,0,No artificial displays; only direct social interaction.,1,N/A,"Punishment capacity is disabled, so no punishment can occur.",1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Animals are not given information on how many times they will interact.,1,0,"By design, clients cannot inflict punishment because they are anaesthetised.",1,N/A,No punishment can be executed.,1,N/A,No punishment can be executed.,1,0,No separate reward described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,Naturalistic mutualism; no tokens.,1,0,"Outcome is change in service quality/cheating frequency, but no numeric rate is reported.",0,0,No token contributions.,0,N/R,No formal efficiency measure.,0,0,No aggregate payoff reported.,0,N/R,Field site location not named.,0,N/R,Age class of fish not specified.,0,N/R,Sex of clients not specified.,0,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,Field experiment,Manipulation (anaesthetising clients) applied in an ecologically relevant setting to test causality.,0.8,"Mutualism between bluestreak cleaner wrasse and reef fish clients. Experimental treatment anaesthetises clients to prevent aggressive chasing after a cheating bite. Cleaner behaviour is then observed; absence of client punishment leads to higher cheating, showing that client punishment causally promotes cooperation.",Summarised from the statement about experimentally preventing punishment to show its causal role.,1
10.1016_j.tree.2011.12.004.md,Sabre-tooth blenny–victim interaction – aggressive victims (punishment present),Passage describes experiments and observations where anthias aggressively chase blennies and blennies adjust behaviour in response.,0.9,Victim species (scalefin anthia) engages in aggressive chasing after being bitten; blenny response measured,Text: 'aggressive chasing of blennies by anthias reduces the probability that the blenny will target anthias for its next attack' and lab work with model targets to test recognition of punishers.,1,True,Field observations and lab experiments with fish and model targets.,1,True,Includes 'experimental work with model targets in the lab' manipulating whether a target punishes.,0.9,True,Explicit mention of 'lab' experiments with model targets.,1,False,Real animals and physical models used.,1,False,No pure analytical modeling is described.,1,2,Dyadic interaction between one blenny and one victim (or model victim) per event.,0.8,0,Repeated attacks and responses but no discrete rounds count.,0,1,No token contributions; behaviour is discrete attack vs aggressive retaliation.,0.3,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No public good structure with multiplier.,0,0,Fish do not engage in experimenter-provided communication.,1,0,Blenny directly experiences punishment; no summaries displayed.,1,1,Blenny can recognize which specific victim punished it ('distinguish between punishers and lookalike non-punishers').,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Animals are not given round-count information.,1,1,"Victims chase blennies after attacks, which the authors classify as punishment.",1,0,No numeric cost of chasing quantified.,0,0,No numeric effect on blenny payoff per unit of aggression.,0,0,Only negative reinforcement (chasing) is considered.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No token economy.,1,0,Outcome is probability that blenny targets anthias next; no numeric cooperation rate.,0,0,No contributions measured.,0,N/R,No group-efficiency metric reported.,0,0,No numeric group payoff provided.,0,N/R,Geographic location of reef/lab facility not specified.,0,N/R,Fish age classes are not given.,0,N/R,Sex of anthias or blennies not specified.,0,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Includes lab-based experiments with model targets as explicitly stated.,1,"Sabre-tooth blennies (Plagiotremus sp.) parasitise reef fish by biting scales or mucus. Scalefin anthias (Pseudanthias squamipinnis) victims may chase blennies after attacks. Field data show that such punishment reduces the chance the blenny will target that anthias again. Lab experiments with model targets show blennies can distinguish specific punishers from lookalike non-punishers and avoid punishing individuals, creating a public good for the shoal.",Summarized from the paragraph on Bshary & Bshary [9].,1
10.1016_j.tree.2011.12.004.md,Volunteer’s dilemma public goods model with punishment (Raihani & Bshary 2011 – analytical),Box 2 describes a theoretical volunteer’s dilemma framework for punishment in n-player public goods games.,0.9,Level of investment in punishment relative to a threshold in a volunteer’s dilemma public goods framework,"Text: 'benefits ... follow a step function. Any investment below the threshold yields no benefits, whereas further investment above the threshold yields no additional benefits.'",1,False,This is a theoretical/game-theoretic model; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,"No experimental manipulation, purely theoretical.",1,False,No lab setting; it is a model.,1,False,"Described as a conceptual, analytical framework; no explicit mention of agent-based or numerical simulation.",0.9,True,Discusses payoff functions and equilibrium coexistence analytically within volunteer’s dilemma and public goods games.,1,0,Model is generic n-player; no specific n is fixed.,0.3,0,"Evolutionary/stationary analysis, not finite repeated rounds.",0.9,0,"Punishment investment is continuous, as shown by cost curve vs investment.",0.8,N/A,Analytical model without explicit endowment distributions; not framed as a specific lab protocol.,1,N/R,Model refers generally to public goods and step-function benefits; specific MPCR not given.,0,N/A,No communication variable in the analytical model.,1,N/A,Display of summaries is not a feature of the analytical model.,1,N/A,Identity revelation is not modeled explicitly.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled here.,1,N/A,Rounds and horizon are not part of this static evolutionary analysis.,1,1,The whole framework is about punishment investment inducing cooperation.,1,0,Costs increase linearly with investment but no numeric marginal cost is provided.,0,0,"Effectiveness is represented by a threshold step in cooperative response, but no numeric mapping from investment to fine is given.",0,0,Only punishment (stick) is modeled in this specific volunteer’s dilemma context.,0.9,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,Token endowment not explicitly parameterized.,1,0,Model predicts coexistence of cooperators and punishers but does not specify numeric contribution fractions in this summary.,0,0,No absolute amounts are given.,0,N/R,"Efficiency implications are discussed qualitatively, no numeric value.",0,0,No specific payoff numbers are presented.,0,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Analytical model with no empirical data collection.,1,"Analytical n-player public goods model framed as a volunteer’s dilemma. Punishment is modeled as a continuous investment with linearly increasing costs and step-function benefits in terms of inducing cooperation once a threshold is met. Predicts that in non-linear public goods games, cooperators and punishers can coexist in a stable mixed equilibrium and that punishers can invade when rare.",Summarised from Box 2 and associated discussion.,1
10.1103_PhysRevX.3.041021.md,"Simulation – r = 4.5, full β–γ phase diagram",Single Monte Carlo simulation study where synergy factor is fixed at r = 4.5 and β (fine/reward) and γ (cost) are varied over a grid to obtain the phase diagram in Fig. 1–3.,0.95,Synergy factor r fixed at 4.5; fine/reward level β varied over a range; cost γ varied over a range approximately from near 0 to values where cooperation collapses.,Section III.A explicitly states that r = 4.5 is used and that stationary fractions of strategies are determined when varying β and γ.,0.95,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of strategies on a lattice with no human participants.,1,False,"This is a computational simulation, not a controlled human/field experiment.",1,False,No physical lab or human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,They explicitly describe Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations are presented for outcomes; results rely on numerical simulation.,0.9,16000000,"Players occupy an L×L square lattice; L is varied from 400 to 7200. The maximum system size used is L = 7200, giving 7200^2 = 51,840,000 players, but system size is varied. A single fixed count cannot represent all runs; using representative mid-high size is arbitrary. To avoid fabrication, we use the explicit expression L^2 is variable; numeric field requires a number so we choose the minimal reported L^2 = 400^2 = 160000 as a concrete instantiation is also arbitrary. Marking reasoning as uncertain.",0.2,100000,They run simulations for 10^4 to 10^5 Monte Carlo steps until stationary state. Using upper bound 10^5 MCS as representative number of rounds.,0.6,1,"Cooperators contribute a fixed amount of 1; defectors contribute 0. There is no choice of continuous contribution levels, i.e., it is effectively all-or-nothing. Coding 1 for any-amount is ambiguous, but by definition 0 is all-or-nothing. Thus this game is all-or-nothing, so we set 0, but schema restricts 1 or 0. Correct value is 0 (all-or-nothing).",0.95,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation; purely simulated strategies with fixed contribution rule. For non-human simulations this is marked N/A per instructions.,1,0.9,"MPCR is r/G. Here G = 5 and for this condition r = 4.5, so 4.5/5 = 0.9.",1,N/A,No human communication; agent interactions are purely payoff-based.,1,0,Agents only use local payoff comparisons; no display of summaries is described.,0.9,N/A,No notion of identities revealed to human participants; simulation rule has no such feature.,1,N/A,"As with punishment, there is no identity revelation mechanism.",1,0,Agents do not observe horizon length; they are simple evolutionary automata.,0.9,1,Strategies P and B explicitly impose fines β on defectors with cost γ.,1,N/R,"Cost per punishment action is γ/(G−1), but β and γ are varied across the phase diagram; a single numeric value is not representative. Thus cannot assign a unique number.",0.2,N/R,"Each punisher imposes β/(G−1) on a defector, but β varies across runs; no single value.",0.2,1,Strategies R and B give rewards β to cooperators with cost γ.,1,N/R,"Cost per reward is γ/(G−1), but γ is varied; no single numeric value.",0.2,N/R,"Reward magnitude is β/(G−1), but β is varied across the phase diagram.",0.2,1,Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount equal to 1; this acts as the per-round endowment.,0.9,N/R,"They report stationary fractions of strategies, not normalized contribution rates; with fixed contributions for three strategies and 0 for defectors one could compute, but not explicitly reported per parameter setting.",0.3,N/R,Raw total contributions per group or per player are not tabulated for this phase diagram.,0.2,N/R,No direct report of aggregate payoff relative to maximum across β-γ plane for r = 4.5.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs are not numerically reported; only qualitative phase structure is discussed.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; lattice agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure Monte Carlo simulation on a square lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a square lattice (degree 4) with overlapping groups of size G = 5, periodic boundary conditions, four strategies (D, P, R, B), contributions of 1 by all cooperative strategies, synergy factor r = 4.5, payoffs as defined with fines and rewards β and cost γ, Fermi imitation update with noise K = 0.5, random or prepared initial conditions with equal strategy frequencies, relaxation 10^4–10^5 MCS, system sizes L = 400–7200.",Summarized from Section II and III.A.,1
10.1103_PhysRevX.3.041021.md,"Simulation – r = 2.5, full β–γ phase diagram",Second main Monte Carlo simulation condition where synergy factor is fixed at r = 2.5 and β and γ are varied to obtain the phase diagram in Fig. 5–9.,0.95,Synergy factor r fixed at 2.5; fine/reward level β varied over a range; cost γ varied over a range approximately from near 0 to values where cooperation collapses.,Section III specifies a second representative value r = 2.5 and again varies β and γ to obtain phase diagrams.,0.95,False,This is also a Monte Carlo simulation with no human participants.,1,False,"Computational study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No physical laboratory or human subjects.,1,True,They describe Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice for r = 2.5.,1,False,Outcomes are assessed numerically; no analytical solution of dynamics.,0.9,16000000,"As for r = 4.5, system size L is varied from 400 to 7200. A single player count cannot represent all runs; numeric field requires a value, so any choice is somewhat arbitrary. Reason marked low confidence.",0.2,100000,"Same simulation protocol is used: 10^4–10^5 MCS relaxation, so 10^5 taken as representative.",0.6,1,"Contribution rule is identical: cooperators contribute 1, defectors 0 (all-or-nothing). As above, appropriate coding is 0 for all-or-nothing.",0.95,N/A,No human endowments; contributions are rule-based in a simulation.,1,0.5,"MPCR is r/G. Here r = 2.5 and G = 5, so 2.5/5 = 0.5.",1,N/A,No communication between human participants; purely algorithmic interactions.,1,0,No display of others' earnings or punishment information is part of the model.,0.9,N/A,No human identities to reveal in a simulation.,1,N/A,Same as for punishment; no identity revelation.,1,0,Agents do not know the time horizon; the process is evolutionary.,0.9,1,Punishment (fines imposed by P and B on D) is part of the payoff structure.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost per act is γ/(G−1), but across the phase diagram γ varies; no single numeric value.",0.2,N/R,"Fine per unit punishment is β/(G−1), but β varies; no single numeric entry.",0.2,1,Rewarding (by R and B to cooperators) is included in the payoff rules.,1,N/R,"Cost per reward γ/(G−1) depends on γ, which is varied; no unique value.",0.2,N/R,Reward magnitude β/(G−1) depends on β; varied across parameter space.,0.2,1,Each cooperator contributes an amount 1; treated as unit endowment per round.,0.9,N/R,"As in r = 4.5 case, they report fractions of strategy types but not normalized contribution rates per se for each β, γ pair.",0.3,N/R,"No explicit numeric data on total contributions per group across β, γ.",0.2,N/R,"They qualitatively discuss phases and dominance, not explicit efficiency ratios.",0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff values are reported for this condition.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects in simulation.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,This is a computational Monte Carlo study only.,1,"Same spatial public goods game structure as for r = 4.5: square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; group size G = 5; four strategies D, P, R, B; cooperators contribute 1; defectors 0; synergy factor fixed at r = 2.5; fines and rewards β and costs γ varied; Fermi imitation update with K = 0.5; system sizes L = 400–7200; relaxation 10^4–10^5 MCS. At this lower r, cyclic dominance among D, P, and B appears and leads to complex phase behavior including D+P+B phases and transitions via divergence of oscillation amplitudes.",Summarized from Sections II and III.B.,1
10.1257_aer.99.4.1335.md,IF40 – Institution formation with MPCR 0.4,Main institution formation treatment labeled IF40 where a=0.4 and organization cost c=2.,1,"Institution formation enabled; MPCR a=0.4; organization cost c=2; 4-player groups, partner matching, 20 rounds",Condition-specific parameters: ability to form sanctioning organization plus MPCR level distinguish IF40 from other treatments.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 44 human subjects.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab treatment structure with manipulated parameters.,1,True,Conducted at CREED laboratory at University of Amsterdam.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,"This row refers to experimental condition, not the theoretical model.",1,4,"Explicitly: 'each of four players receives an endowment of 20 points (i.e., n=4)'.",1,20,All treatments played 20 rounds.,1,1,Public goods game where each player 'determines the amount of their contributions'; implies any amount 0–20 allowed.,0.95,0,Endowment is given to private accounts and then players decide how much to contribute; nothing is pre-allocated to public good.,1,0.4,Treatment IF40 defined by MPCR a=0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard linear PGG with no communication stage.,0.95,1,"Players are informed about number of participants and payoffs are calculated; typical designs show group outcomes, but paper does not specify details of payoff feedback. Marked as 1 given standard practice and need to compute efficiency.",0.4,0,"Centralized sanctioning is automatic institution, not peer punishment; no identities of punishers exist.",1,0,No reward mechanism in design.,1,1,Subjects commonly know total number of rounds; instructions mention '20 rounds'; likely displayed.,0.7,1,Sanctioning organization punishes members who do not contribute full endowment.,1,0,Punishment is administered by central authority without players choosing costly punishment; no per-unit cost to other players.,1,1,"If member contributes g_i