in periods 11–20 (lab) reported as 16.26; use as average per-person contribution in punishment condition.,1,N/R,"Mean efficiency reported only comparing MTurk vs lab in punishment condition pooled, not decomposed clearly by sample and relative to theoretical max; ambiguous.",0.3,22.64,“Mean efficiency in the experimental condition with punishment was higher on MTurk than in the laboratory (averages 26.91 vs 22.64 points per individual per period).” Use 22.64 as average individual payoff; treat as group payoff proxy for this condition.,0.8,USA,Same Harvard/Yale lab sample as Condition 1.,0.9,Mean 25.2 years (s.d. 7.45),Reported for entire laboratory sample.,1,42.3% female,Reported for entire laboratory sample.,1,University students (Harvard and Yale subject pools),Same recruited lab population across both conditions.,0.9,On site,Conducted in physical laboratories at universities.,1,"Two-stage per period: contribution decision (0–20 from endowment of 20), then punishment stage where each player can assign 0–10 deduction points to each other group member at cost 1 point per deduction point, reducing target by 3 points each. Fixed groups of 4, 10 periods. Losses truncated such that if overall payoff negative, only punishment costs count towards final earnings. Lab exchange rate $0.02 per point plus $10 show-up fee.",Synthesized from Section 2.3 and payment details.,1
10.1007_s10683-017-9527-2.md,MTurk – No punishment (Condition 1),"Online MTurk sample, first condition: repeated four-person public goods game without punishment.",1,Environment: MTurk online; Punishment: No punishment; Matching: Fixed partners; Condition order: First part (no punishment).,"Same Condition 1 design as lab, but run via MTurk with US workers.",1,True,Human participants recruited via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,True,"Randomly formed groups, controlled incentives; manipulated availability of punishment in second condition.",1,False,"Conducted online via MTurk, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Behavioral data from human workers, not simulated agents.",1,False,No analytical model as primary method.,1,4,Same repeated four-person public goods game replicated online. Groups of four (62 groups).,1,10,"Condition 1 consisted of ten periods, identical online and in lab.",1,1,Participants decide how many of 20 points to keep vs contribute (0–20).,1,0,Endowment given each period to private account and then allocated.,1,0.4,Same payoff function applied online: sum contributions *1.6 / 4.,1,0,"No communication tool mentioned; HIT description only explains interactive nature, not chat.",0.8,1,Online instructions and screens identical to lab; participants see average and individual contributions of others.,1,0,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Participants were aware there are two parts of ten periods.,0.8,0,Condition 1 is explicitly without punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment allowed.,1,N/A,No punishment allowed.,1,0,No rewards described for any condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same 20-point endowment per period used online and in lab.,1,N/R,Normalized fraction of endowment not directly reported.,0,12.52,Condition without punishment: “…online sample than in the laboratory (12.52 vs 8.30).” Use 12.52 as mean contribution.,1,N/R,No separate efficiency metric reported for online no-punishment condition.,0,N/R,Earnings by condition for MTurk are not decomposed; only overall average $6.69 reported.,0,USA,MTurk workers restricted to USA for comparability with lab sample.,1,Mean 31.5 years (s.d. 9.06),Reported for MTurk sample.,1,38.4% female,Reported for MTurk sample.,1,Mixed MTurk worker population (not restricted to students),MTurk is a general adult worker pool; no student-only restriction stated.,0.7,Online,Conducted via LIONESS on MTurk; remote participation.,1,"Identical repeated 10-period public goods game as lab: groups of 4 formed ‘on the fly’, fixed across periods; MPCR 0.4; feedback: own and others’ contributions. Recruitment via MTurk within 20-minute window; HIT duration 45 minutes. Online exchange rate $0.01 per point plus $1 show-up fee.",Compiled from Sections 2.1–2.4 and 3.2.1–3.2.3.,1
10.1007_s10683-017-9527-2.md,MTurk – Peer punishment (Condition 2),"Online MTurk sample, second condition: repeated four-person public goods game with peer punishment.",1,Environment: MTurk online; Punishment: Peer punishment available; Punishment cost 1 point per deduction point; Punishment impact 3 points per deduction point; Matching: Fixed partners; Condition order: Second part (with punishment).,Same punishment design as lab Condition 2 but executed online.,1,True,Human MTurk workers interacting in real time.,1,True,Random grouping; controlled manipulation of punishment opportunity.,1,False,Not conducted in a physical lab; entirely online.,1,False,No non-human agents used.,1,False,"Study is empirical, not analytical.",1,4,Groups of four remain constant across both conditions online.,1,10,Condition 2 consists of ten periods as in the lab.,1,1,Contribution decision same as Condition 1 (0–20 of 20).,1,0,Endowment allocated by participants each period.,1,0.4,Payoff technology unchanged; only punishment added.,1,0,No chat or communication tool described.,0.8,1,Online screens identical; participants see other contributions and their own/prior punishment totals.,1,0,No mention that identities of punishers or recipients are disclosed; standard is anonymous punishment.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Participants informed about two ten-period parts.,0.8,1,Condition 2 introduces peer punishment stage online.,1,1,Each deduction point costs 1 point to punisher.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces target’s payoff by 3 points.,1,0,No reward points or positive sanctions allowed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same 20-point endowment each period.,1,N/R,Only raw contribution amounts reported.,0,17.15,“Average group contributions in periods 11–20 vs 1–10: online: 17.15 vs 12.52.” Use 17.15 as mean contribution in punishment condition.,1,N/R,"While mean payoff per individual per period is reported (26.91), efficiency relative to maximum is not explicitly calculated.",0.3,26.91,“Mean efficiency in the experimental condition with punishment was higher on MTurk… (26.91 vs 22.64 points per individual per period).” Use 26.91 as average individual payoff in this condition.,0.8,USA,MTurk workers restricted to USA.,1,Mean 31.5 years (s.d. 9.06),Reported for MTurk sample.,1,38.4% female,Reported for MTurk sample.,1,Mixed MTurk worker population (not restricted to students),Same MTurk sample for both conditions.,0.7,Online,Conducted via LIONESS server with MTurk workers.,1,"Same as lab punishment condition but online: in each of 10 periods, contribution stage (0–20) followed by punishment stage (0–10 deduction points per peer, cost 1 and inflict 3). Groups formed dynamically from lobby, then fixed. Loss handling identical to lab. Online exchange rate $0.01 per point plus $1 show-up fee; average MTurk total earnings reported as $6.69 including show-up.",Synthesized from Sections 2.3–2.4 and 3.2.3–3.2.4.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.92.012819.md,"Simulation – spatial PGG with T=6 punishing cooperator classes and defectors (simultaneous competition, r=3.5)","Single Monte Carlo simulation framework where all six cooperator classes C0–C5 and defectors compete simultaneously on an LxL lattice for multiplication factor r=3.5, as described around Fig. 2(a),(c).",0.9,"Punishment fine α varied; six cooperator classes with punishment probabilities i/(T−1) for i=0,…,5 present simultaneously; enhancement factor r=3.5, group size N=5.","Text for Fig. 2: uses r=3.5, T=6; punishers C_i punish with probability i/(T−1); α is systematically varied and final fractions of strategies measured.",0.9,False,Study is described as Monte Carlo simulations of strategies on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,"It is a computational model, not a controlled experiment with human subjects or field data.",1,False,No lab or field setting with human subjects; entirely simulated.,1,True,Model is studied by means of Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria; focus is on numerical Monte Carlo results.,0.9,14400,"Population is on an L×L square lattice with L varied 120–600. A concrete L=120 is mentioned for some runs (e.g., snapshots use L=100). Player count therefore ranges; choose 120×120=14400 as a representative concrete size used for many simulations, but exact size for Fig. 2 not uniquely specified.",0.3,1000000,"Relaxation time varied from 1e4 to 1e6 Monte Carlo steps (MCS). Use upper bound 1e6 as representative maximum iterations per run, but exact number for this condition not uniquely given.",0.3,1,Cooperators contribute a fixed amount c; defectors contribute 0. There is no choice of partial contribution level; hence contribution decision is effectively all-or-nothing.,0.8,N/A,Agent-based evolutionary game without an explicit initial allocation between private and public accounts; not a human decision interface.,1,0.7,"MPCR = r/N. Here r=3.5 and group size N=5, so MPCR=3.5/5=0.7.",1,N/A,No human subjects and no communication mechanism in the simulation.,1,0,Simulated agents have no observation interface like earnings summaries; only local payoffs used for imitation.,0.9,0,No notion of identities beyond lattice sites and no reporting of who punished whom; only payoffs matter.,0.9,0,There is no reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Simulated evolutionary process; agents are not informed about time horizon.,1,1,Cooperators punish defectors with fines α and share costs; central part of model.,1,N/R,Per-unit cost parameter is embedded in payoff expressions as (n−n_C)α/n_P; no separate fixed cost per punishment unit reported.,0.2,N/R,"Each defector is punished with fine α, but they do not specify how many punishment points per unit cost coin; only fine and cost formula given.",0.3,0,Only punishment of defectors is modeled; no positive rewards mentioned.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/R,"Each cooperator contributes amount c to the public good, but the total endowment per player is not explicitly given nor normalized.",0.2,N/R,"Outcome measures reported are fractions of strategy types (C_i, D), not normalized contribution rates; contribution is fixed for all cooperators.",0.2,N/R,Contribution amount c is a fixed parameter but its numeric value is not reported.,0.2,N/R,"They report fractions of cooperators and strategy compositions, not payoff efficiency ratios relative to maximum.",0.1,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff values provided; only qualitative behavior and fractions of strategies.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a Monte Carlo simulation with no empirical subject pool.,1,"Square lattice of size L×L with periodic boundary conditions; von Neumann neighborhood; each player in 5 overlapping groups (N=5). Cooperators C_i contribute c and punish defectors with probability i/(T−1); defectors contribute 0. Group contribution multiplied by r=3.5 and shared equally. Punishing cooperators share punishment costs equally. Strategy update via Fermi rule with noise κ=0.5; initial condition: half defectors, remaining half equally divided among T=6 cooperator classes.","Summarizes core structural and dynamic features of the spatial public goods game at r=3.5 with all punishing strategies present, as described in Section II and Fig. 2.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.92.012819.md,"Simulation – spatial PGG with single cooperator classes vs defectors (individual competition, r=3.5)","Condition where each punishing cooperator class C_i is paired with defectors alone to test its ability to survive, summarized in Fig. 2(b),(d) at r=3.5.",0.9,"Punishment fine α varied; only one cooperator class C_i present together with defectors (i=0,…,5); enhancement factor r=3.5, group size N=5.","They state that the vitality of punishing classes is estimated by letting them fight defectors individually; r=3.5, T=6; α varied.",0.9,False,Same Monte Carlo simulation framework; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical simulations only.,1,False,No lab with participants; computational model.,1,True,They explicitly use Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice.,1,False,No analytical solution for this condition.,0.9,14400,Same lattice-based model with L between 120 and 600; precise L for Fig. 2(b) not specified; use representative 120×120.,0.3,1000000,Relaxation time 1e4–1e6 MCS; use upper bound as representative.,0.3,1,"Same contribution rule: cooperator contributes c, defector contributes 0.",0.8,N/A,"Simulation, not human allocation task.",1,0.7,Same group size N=5 and r=3.5 as in r=3.5 scenarios; MPCR=3.5/5.,1,N/A,No communication channel; agents update based on payoffs only.,1,0,No explicit feedback beyond local payoffs.,0.9,0,No identity-based observation; only strategy and payoff matter.,0.9,0,No reward implemented.,1,0,No horizon information in simulations.,1,1,"Cooperator class C_i punishes defectors with probability i/(T−1) except C0, which never punishes; punishment mechanism remains part of the model.",1,N/R,Cost per punishment unit not parameterized separately from α and group composition.,0.2,N/R,"Defectors receive fine α when punished, but no explicit 'per unit' tech parameter.",0.3,0,Only punishment modeled.,1,N/A,Reward is absent.,1,N/A,Reward is absent.,1,N/R,Contribution c used but not assigned as explicit numeric endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes are stationary fractions of C_i and D, not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,Numeric value of c not provided.,0.2,N/R,No explicit comparison of total payoffs to maximum.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs not tabulated; only qualitative dominance/extinction of strategies.,0.1,N/A,Simulation without humans.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational simulations.,1,"Same spatial PGG setup as simultaneous competition, but initial conditions include only defectors and one cooperator class C_i. Outcomes are stationary fractions and extinction thresholds of each class vs defectors as α varies at r=3.5.","Summarizes how individual competition experiments are set up and evaluated in Fig. 2(b),(d).",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.92.012819.md,"Simulation – spatial PGG with C5 vs D, C3 vs D, and joint C3+C5 vs D (invasion velocity analysis, r=3.5, α=0.42)","Specific simulations at r=3.5, α=0.42 comparing invasion dynamics for C5 vs D, C3 vs D, and combined C3 and C5 vs D, illustrated in Figs. 3 and 4.",0.9,"Strategy set and initial spatial configuration: (1) few C5 vs many D, (2) many C3 vs few D, (3) half C3 & half C5 vs half D; enhancement factor r=3.5; punishment fine α=0.42 fixed.","Described explicitly around Fig. 3: four initial conditions, but core comparison is C5 vs D, C3 vs D, and C3+C5 vs D at α=0.42,r=3.5.",0.9,False,All are Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,Not empirical but numerical experiments.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo on L×L lattice; time evolution of strategy fractions tracked.,1,False,"These results are numerical, not analytical.",0.9,10000,"Snapshots in Fig. 3 explicitly use L×L=100×100 players, so player count is 10,000.",1,100000,"Relaxation time varies from 1e4 to 1e6 MCS; invasion trajectories in Fig. 4 suggest up to ~1e5 steps for illustrative dynamics. Exact number not specified, choose mid-range 1e5 as representative.",0.3,1,Same binary contribution structure (c or 0).,0.8,N/A,No human decision about default contributions.,1,0.7,"r=3.5, N=5 ⇒ MPCR=0.7.",1,N/A,No communication mechanism.,1,0,Agents only use local payoff information.,0.9,0,No display of identities of punishers.,0.9,0,No rewards present.,1,0,Infinite-horizon style simulation; no horizon info to agents.,1,1,Punishing cooperator strategies C3 and C5 punish defectors; C0 not used here.,1,N/R,Cost formula defined but no single numeric per-unit cost parameter.,0.2,N/R,"Fine per defector is α, but no explicit per coin mapping.",0.3,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,N/R,Numeric value of endowment or contribution c not specified.,0.2,N/R,"All cooperators contribute c; fraction of cooperators over time is reported, not normalized contribution per endowment.",0.2,N/R,c not numerically specified.,0.2,N/R,"Focus is on invasion velocities and survival, not payoff efficiency.",0.1,N/R,Group or global payoff trajectories not given.,0.1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulations.,1,"Same spatial PGG; specific initial spatial configurations: (a) few C5 in sea of D; (b) majority C3 minority D; (c) half lattice C3 and C5 mixed, half D. Punishment fine fixed at α=0.42, r=3.5. Used to analyze invasion velocities and cooperation between punishing strategies.",Condenses description of Fig. 3 and related text for these invasion analyses.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.92.012819.md,"Simulation – spatial PGG with T=6 punishing cooperator classes and defectors (simultaneous competition, r=4.0)","Monte Carlo simulations with all cooperator classes C0–C5 and defectors at r=4.0, varying α, summarized in Fig. 5(a).",0.9,"Punishment fine α varied; six cooperator classes C0–C5 and defectors compete simultaneously; enhancement factor r=4.0, group size N=5.",Text for Fig. 5: 'multiplication factor in both panels is r=4.0'; T=6; α varied.,0.9,False,Same simulated lattice model.,1,False,"Numerical, not empirical.",1,False,No subject lab.,1,True,Monte Carlo on a lattice.,1,False,"Results are numerical phase diagrams, not analytic solutions.",0.9,14400,Same model with L between 120 and 600; use 120×120 as representative.,0.3,1000000,Relaxation time range 1e4–1e6 MCS; use 1e6 as representative maximum.,0.3,1,Cooperators contribute fixed c; defectors 0.,0.8,N/A,No explicit private/public default; simulated contributions.,1,0.8,MPCR=r/N=4.0/5=0.8.,1,N/A,No communication among agents.,1,0,Only local payoff used for imitation.,0.9,0,No explicit identity signaling.,0.9,0,No reward scheme.,1,0,Agents not informed about round count.,1,1,Different punishing strategies C_i present.,1,N/R,"Cost handled via cost-sharing term, not as standalone parameter.",0.2,N/R,Fine α specified; per-unit tech not.,0.3,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,Reward absent.,1,N/A,Reward absent.,1,N/R,Endowment not numerically specified.,0.2,N/R,"Cooperation level measured via fraction of cooperator strategies, not normalized contributions.",0.2,N/R,Numeric c not given.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs or global welfare not tabulated.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational simulations only.,1,Same spatial PGG structure; r=4.0 allows pure cooperators C0 to coexist with D even without punishment. All six punishing cooperator classes