10.1103_PhysRevE.92.012819.md,"Simulation – spatial PGG with single cooperator classes vs defectors (individual competition, r=4.0)","Condition at r=4.0 where each punishing class C_i competes individually against defectors, Fig. 5(b).",0.9,"Punishment fine α varied; only one cooperator class C_i present with defectors (i=0,…,5); enhancement factor r=4.0.",Fig. 5(b) described as stationary fractions when they compete against defectors individually at r=4.0; α varied.,0.9,False,Numerical simulations.,1,False,"No empirical experiment, only computational.",1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Monte Carlo lattice model.,1,False,No analytic solutions reported for these runs.,0.9,14400,Same simulation framework; representative L=120.,0.3,1000000,Relaxation time range 1e4–1e6 MCS; 1e6 as representative max.,0.3,1,"Cooperators contribute c, defectors 0.",0.8,N/A,Not a human contribution interface.,1,0.8,"r=4.0,N=5 ⇒ MPCR=0.8.",1,N/A,No communication channel between agents.,1,0,Only payoffs in local groups matter.,0.9,0,No identity disclosure modeled.,0.9,0,Rewards not modeled.,1,0,Agents not aware of time horizon.,1,1,Punishing strategies C_i (i>0) punish defectors; C0 is pure cooperator without punishment but the punishment mechanism is still part of the model for other classes.,1,N/R,Cost determined by cost-sharing formula; no separate scalar cost parameter given.,0.2,N/R,"Only fine α described, no per-unit mapping.",0.3,0,No explicit reward.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,Endowment or contribution c not numerically specified.,0.2,N/R,"Outcome reported as fractions of strategies at stationarity, not average contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,c not numerically given.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency or total payoff metrics reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs not specified.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational.,1,"Same spatial PGG; focus is on how each single punishing class fares vs defectors at r=4.0 when α varies, including the 'punish but not too hard' effect and survival of mildly punishing strategies.",Summarizes individual-competition results described around Fig. 5(b).,1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2005.01031.x.md,"Baseline – no regrouping, no punishment","Condition described in Section 1.3.1 as the baseline treatment: fixed groups, no regrouping, no punishment.",1,Regrouping: No; Punishment: No,From 2×2 design in Table 1 and treatment description: baseline is cell with no regrouping and no punishment.,1,True,Human subjects (Brown undergraduates) played the game.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments and specified payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab with undergraduates on terminals at Brown University.,1,False,"All results are from human-subject sessions, not agent-based or numerical simulations.",1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not closed-form analytical models.",1,4,"Groups of four in all treatments (Section 1.3, 1.3.1).",1,20,Each session consisted of 20 periods of the finitely repeated VCM.,1,1,Subjects divide a 10-dollar endowment in integer amounts between private and group accounts; any amount 0–10 allowed (not binary).,1,0,Each period subjects are ‘provided with 10 experimental dollars’ and must decide how much to allocate; endowment starts entirely in private account by default.,0.9,0.4,Each group member receives 0.4 times the sum of group contributions (marginal per capita return on public contribution).,1,0,Subjects interacted only via computer and only saw numerical contributions; no mention of communication.,1,1,"After contributions, ‘the computer informed everyone in each group how much each of the other members of the group contributed’ and then showed own earnings.",1,0,"No punishment option in this treatment; identities only as B,C,D for contributions, not for punishment.",1,0,No reward mechanism is present in any treatment.,1,1,"Standard VCM design with finite horizon; instructions describe 20 periods. While not explicitly stated on-screen, typical practice and description imply subjects knew total periods.",0.8,0,Baseline treatment has no punishment stage; only contribution decisions.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,No mechanism to increase others’ earnings; only public good and (in other treatments) punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each subject provided with 10 experimental dollars per period.,1,0.38,Table 2: average contribution 3.8 tokens out of 10 endowment; 3.8/10 = 0.38.,1,3.8,Table 2: ‘Average contribution’ for Baseline is 3.8.,1,0.77,Table 2: Efficiency for Baseline reported as 77%.,1,49.2,"Average earnings per subject 12.3 (Table 2). Group has 4 members, so 12.3×4 = 49.2 per period.",0.9,USA,"Subjects were Brown University undergraduates in Providence, Rhode Island, USA.",0.95,Primarily 18–22; exact distribution N/R,Described as ‘general undergraduate population’; typical US undergraduate age range. No exact ages reported.,0.6,58% female; 42% male (approx.),Footnote 6: 58% of subjects were female; implies about 42% male.,1,Undergraduate students at Brown University,Subjects drawn from the general undergraduate population at Brown University.,1,On site,Lab sessions with physical attendance at Brown using computer terminals.,1,"Standard linear VCM with 4 players, 10-token endowment each period, MPCR 0.4; fixed groups for 20 periods; anonymous IDs B,C,D re-randomized each period.",Synthesized from Section 1.3.1 description of baseline treatment and general design.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2005.01031.x.md,"Regrouping – endogenous group formation, no punishment","Condition described in Section 1.3.2 as the regrouping treatment: endogenous regrouping every 3 periods, no punishment.",1,Regrouping: Yes; Punishment: No,From 2×2 design in Table 1 and treatment description.,1,True,Human subjects in lab.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with specified regrouping mechanism.,1,True,Conducted with Brown undergraduates on computer terminals.,1,False,No computer-only simulation.,1,False,No analytical model used as main method.,1,4,Groups of four (Section 1.3).,1,20,20 periods per session in all treatments.,1,1,Same VCM structure as baseline: integer allocation 0–10 to group account.,1,0,Same as baseline; each period subjects decide how much of 10 to contribute; default is all private.,0.9,0.4,Public good technology unchanged across treatments.,1,0,Only numerical information on contributions and rankings; no communication channel mentioned.,1,1,Subjects see each group member’s contribution and own earnings each period; at ranking times they see average contributions of all 15 others.,1,0,No punishment option in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same finite-horizon design; instructions describe 20 periods.,0.8,0,Only ranking/regrouping is added; no punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism anywhere in design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same 10 experimental dollars per period per subject as baseline.,1,0.7,Text: ‘average contribution to the group account is 70% of endowment’ in regrouping treatment; matches Table 2 value 7.0/10.,1,7.0,Table 2: ‘Average contribution’ for Regrouping is 7.0.,1,0.88,Table 2: Efficiency for Regrouping is 88%.,1,56.4,Average earnings per subject 14.1 (Table 2). Group of 4 ⇒ 14.1×4 = 56.4.,0.9,USA,Same subject pool as baseline: Brown University undergraduates.,0.95,Primarily 18–22; exact distribution N/R,Same undergraduate pool; no additional age info by treatment.,0.6,"58% female; 42% male (approx.), pooled across treatments",Footnote 6 gives overall sample composition; no treatment-specific breakdown.,0.9,Undergraduate students at Brown University,Same as baseline.,1,On site,Conducted in the same lab environment at Brown.,1,"Same 4-player linear VCM as baseline with MPCR 0.4; every 3rd period (3,6,9,12,15,18) subjects may rank all 15 others based on average past contributions; ranking costs 0.25 experimental dollars for first ranking decision and 0.05 for each additional ranking; algorithm forms new groups of four by minimizing sum of mutual ranks; group size constant across regroupings; IDs B,C,D within group re-randomized each period.",From Section 1.3.2 on regrouping treatment including ranking procedure and costs.,1
