-one in experimental dollars.,1,0,No mechanism to increase others’ earnings described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each member receives 10 experimental dollars at start of each period.,1,0.631,Average contribution in 0–120 treatment is 6.31 tokens out of 10; 6.31/10 = 0.631.,1,6.31,Table 4 reports average contribution 6.31 in 0–120 treatment.,1,0.69,Table 4 lists efficiency 69% for 0–120 treatment.,1,12.44,Table 4: 'Average total earnings' in 0–120 treatment is 12.44 per subject per period.,1,United States,"Sessions at University of Rhode Island and Brown University, both in the USA.",1,N/R,Only that they are students; no age statistics reported.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown provided.,0,"University students (mixed majors, mostly without prior economics training)",Subjects recruited from courses and campus; text notes most had no economics training and were students.,0.8,On site,Conducted in physical computer labs/classrooms at universities.,1,"Perfect stranger matching across 5 one-shot VCM-with-punishment rounds; groups of 3; contribution stage with linear public good (MPCR 0.6, group size 3); punishment stage where subjects choose integer punishment units for each of 2 others, subject to budget and target-earnings constraints; punishment prices assigned independently each period from treatment-specific set; no rewards; earnings floored at zero.",Summary of detailed design described in Section 2 and Appendix A.,1
