10.1098_rsbl.2005.0344.md,Learning phase – Client flees (partner switching treatment),"Second experimental group where plate flees after prawn consumption, mimicking client fleeing and partner switching.",1,"Client reaction to cleaner eating preferred food during learning phase: plate flees (rapid removal) after each preferred (prawn) item, with re-presentation until second preferred item consumed.",Methods describe this group as 'client flees'; plate is removed rapidly after prawn is eaten and reintroduced until second prawn eaten.,1,True,Live cleaner fish used as subjects.,1,True,Plate behaviour systematically manipulated as treatment; controlled lab protocol.,1,True,Conducted in aquaria at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,False,No simulation-only results.,1,False,No purely analytical model; main results are experimental.,1,1,Each cleaner interacts alone with one plate per trial.,0.9,6,Each cleaner subjected to six learning trials.,1,1,Cleaner can flexibly choose among 14 items; not constrained to all-or-nothing.,0.8,N/A,Not a contribution or endowment game.,1,N/R,No public good or multiplier.,1,0,No explicit communication channel.,1,0,Fish are not informed about others’ outcomes.,1,0,Only one interacting plate; no identity variation or revelation.,1,0,No rewarding agent with identity.,1,0,No indication that cleaners know number of trials.,0.9,1,"Contingent negative consequence (termination of access to plate) occurs when cleaner eats preferred prawn items, modeled as punishment/partner switching.",0.9,N/R,"No numeric cost per ‘punishment’ unit specified (e.g., time or energy loss not quantified).",0.5,N/R,No quantitative mapping from punisher action to punished payoff specified.,0.5,0,"No extra benefits given for cooperative (flake) choices beyond continued access, which is baseline.",0.8,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism defined.,1,N/R,"14 items provided per trial (2 preferred, 12 non-preferred for first 3 trials; then 7:7), but not described as an endowment under subject control.",0.6,N/R,"Outcomes are preference changes, not contributions to a group outcome.",1,N/R,"Study reports proportions of prawn vs flake eaten, not contribution amounts.",1,N/R,No efficiency metric defined.,1,N/R,No explicit payoff values reported.,1,Australia,"Fish collected around Lizard Island, Great Barrier Reef, Australia.",1,"Adult cleaner fish (6.2–8.9 cm total length), exact ages unknown",Same sample of adult fish across all three treatments.,0.9,Unknown/unspecified,Sex explicitly described as unknown.,1,N/A,Non-human animal subjects.,1,On site,Laboratory-like aquaria at a field research station.,1,"Single cleaner chooses between preferred (prawn) and less preferred (flake) items over six learning trials. In this treatment, eating prawn causes immediate removal of the plate (fleeing), then plate is reoffered after 60 s until the cleaner eats a second prawn, after which the plate is removed until the next trial. First three learning trials use 2 preferred and 12 non-preferred items; next three use 7:7 distribution. Preference shift measured by comparing percentage of prawn among first seven items eaten in initial vs final foraging test.",Summarizes key task structure specific to client-flees condition.,1
10.1098_rsbl.2005.0344.md,Learning phase – Client chases (punishment treatment),"Third experimental group where plate chases the cleaner after prawn consumption, mimicking client punishment.",1,"Client reaction to cleaner eating preferred food during learning phase: plate chases cleaner for 1–2 s after each preferred (prawn) item, then allows foraging until second preferred item triggers chasing and removal.",Methods describe this group as 'client chases'; plate pursues cleaner without touching whenever prawn is eaten.,1,True,Live cleaner fish are experimental subjects.,1,True,Plate behaviour is experimentally manipulated; randomized grouping.,1,True,Experiments conducted in aquaria at a research station.,1,False,No computer simulations reported.,1,False,No analytical modelling presented as a separate experiment.,1,1,Each cleaner interacts individually with a plate.,0.9,6,Six learning trials per cleaner.,1,1,Fish can select items sequentially; not all-or-nothing.,0.8,N/A,No currency or endowment contributed to a public good.,1,N/R,No public-goods payoff structure.,1,0,No communication mechanics present.,1,0,Fish receive no information on other subjects’ outcomes.,1,0,Only one plate per fish; no identity variation.,1,0,No explicit reward agent.,1,0,No evidence cleaners know total number of trials.,0.9,1,Chasing plus termination after eating preferred food is explicitly framed as punishment in the paper.,1,N/R,No numerical cost to punisher (client/plate) specified.,0.5,N/R,No quantitative mapping from punishment to reduction in cleaner payoff specified.,0.5,0,No additional benefits contingent on cooperative behaviour beyond usual access to food.,0.8,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"14 items available per trial with varying composition, but not stated as an endowment.",0.6,N/R,No contributions to a public good recorded.,1,N/R,Outcomes are proportions of item types eaten.,1,N/R,No efficiency or payoff frontier defined.,1,N/R,No numeric payoff values reported.,1,Australia,"Fish sourced from Great Barrier Reef near Lizard Island, Australia.",1,"Adult cleaner fish (6.2–8.9 cm total length), exact ages unknown",All groups use same pool of adult fish.,0.9,Unknown/unspecified,Sex of fish is explicitly unknown.,1,N/A,Non-human animal subjects.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at a field station.,1,"Single cleaner chooses between prawn (preferred) and flake (less preferred) items. During six learning trials, whenever a prawn item is eaten the plate chases the cleaner for about 1–2 s without touching it. Cleaner can resume foraging; after the second prawn in a trial, the plate is chased and then removed until the next trial. First three learning trials use 2 prawn and 12 flake items; subsequent three use 7 prawn and 7 flake items. Preference change assessed via percentage of prawn among first seven items eaten in initial preference test vs final foraging test.",Captures punishment contingency and measurement for this treatment.,1
10.1098_rstb.2010.0135.md,N-condition (no punishment),"Paper states all subjects first played a public good experiment with no punishment opportunities, called the 'N-condition'.",1,Punishment opportunity: absent,Only factor explicitly varied across the two conditions is whether a punishment stage is available (N vs P).,1,True,Experiments run with 1120 human undergraduate participants across 16 locations.,1,True,Controlled public good game with random assignment to groups and fixed experimental rules.,1,True,Described as laboratory experiments with undergraduates in university settings.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported; only human subject data and ANOVA/statistical analysis.,1,False,No formal mathematical model or analytical derivations are presented as a standalone study.,1,4,Both experiments use stable groups of four subjects throughout 10 periods.,1,10,Each experiment lasted for 10 periods.,1,1,"Subjects chose 'how many ECU they wanted to contribute' from their endowment, implying any integer amount, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Subjects receive 20 ECU and then decide how much to contribute; initial endowment is entirely in the private account.,1,0.4,Group contributions are multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally among 4 players: 1.6/4 = 0.4 marginal per capita return.,1,0,No mention of communication; design follows standard public good experiments without communication.,0.8,0,In N-condition subjects only see payoffs via standard feedback; paper does not report any extra summaries or punishment/reward info.,0.6,0,No punishment mechanism exists in the N-condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in either condition.,1,1,"Game is finitely repeated with 10 known periods; standard design reveals horizon. Herrmann et al. (2008a) design (referenced) followed Fehr & Gächter (2000), where number of periods is common knowledge.",0.8,0,Explicitly described as 'no punishment opportunities' in N-condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,Only punishment points are ever mentioned; no positive reward or bonus-assigning mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,20,Subjects received an endowment of 20 ECU in each period in both experiments.,1,N/R,Paper reports only that contributions differ by culture and decline over time; no exact normalized averages by condition are given.,0,N/R,Figure 1 contains averages but not numerically in the text; exact values cannot be extracted.,0,N/R,No aggregate efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is numerically reported.,0,N/R,Total or average group payoff figures are not provided.,0,"Multiple (USA, UK, Australia, Denmark, Germany, Switzerland, Belarus, Ukraine, Russia, Greece, Turkey, Saudi Arabia, Oman, South Korea, China)",Table 1 lists 16 cities across these countries as locations where the experiments were run.,1,Undergraduate students; typical university age (exact ages not reported),Paper specifies 'participants were all undergraduates' but gives no numeric age range.,0.7,Mixed; similar gender composition across most subject pools (exact proportions not reported),States that 'Gender composition was also similar in most subject pools' without detailed breakdown.,0.7,Undergraduate university students,Paper explicitly: 'participants were all undergraduates'.,1,On site,Described as laboratory experiments with undergraduates at universities in various cities; no indication of online implementation.,1,"Linear public good game, stable groups of 4 for 10 periods, endowment 20 ECU each period, contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly shared; individually optimal to contribute zero, socially optimal that all contribute 20.",Design description in Section 2 summarizing Herrmann et al. (2008a).,1
