d can assign costly punishment points (cost 1 ECU to punisher, reduce target’s income by 3 ECU) to each other group member.",Design details for P-condition summarized in Section 2.,1
10.1177_1745691613488533.md,Meta-analysis effect sizes – generic Fehr & Gächter protocol condition,"Paper is a meta-analysis aggregating 83 studies; individual primary-study conditions are only summarized (Table 1) and not fully described. All experiments share the same Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002) protocol, so a single generic condition is extracted.",0.73,Presence vs. absence of peer punishment option in repeated public goods game (this row represents the punishment-present condition as used to compute d relative to its control).,Methods and Overview of analysis sections state all included studies compare a punishment condition (peer punishment option) with a control condition (no punishment) using Fehr & Gächter paradigm.,0.98,True,All included studies are lab experiments with adult human participants in public goods games.,1,True,Studies involve randomized assignment to punishment vs. control conditions in controlled lab settings.,1,True,Methods describe participants coming to the lab to play public goods games; all included studies used this paradigm.,0.98,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations are described; only human lab data and meta-analytic models.,1,False,Paper does not present a formal non-empirical game-theoretic or mathematical model as the primary study; instead it analyzes empirical effect sizes.,0.95,4,"Methods: “several people come to the lab and are randomly assigned to play a public goods dilemma game with three other persons,” i.e., 4-person groups. Later notes some primary studies varied group size 3–7, but the standardized Fehr & Gächter protocol is 4-person; this generic row follows that.",0.8,10,"Methods: “The game is often played for several trials… The sample of effect sizes includes both one-shot (k=9) and iterated (k=72) dilemmas… M=10.24, Mdn=10.” Fehr & Gächter protocol uses 10 rounds. Use median 10 for generic condition.",0.7,1,"Fehr & Gächter public goods paradigm: each participant chooses how much (0–10 MUs) to contribute; continuous amount, not all-or-nothing.",0.98,0,Participants are endowed with 10 MUs and then decide how much to contribute; endowment starts entirely in private account.,0.98,0.375,"Methods: “Any amount contributed to the group fund is multiplied by 1.5, and then distributed equally across all group members.” With 4 players, MPCR = 1.5 / 4 = 0.375.",0.98,0,"Core Fehr & Gächter protocol described does not mention any communication; inclusion criteria emphasize standardized protocol without communication. Some primary studies elsewhere (e.g., Bochet et al.) manipulate communication, but that is not described here; for the generic protocol, assume no chat.",0.7,1,"Methods: after contributions, “Participants are made aware of their group members' decisions and are given an opportunity to pay to punish.” Thus they see others’ contribution decisions and resulting earnings, which is a summary of others’ outcomes.",0.85,0,"Fehr & Gächter paradigm uses anonymous matching; paper notes anonymity in partner vs. stranger designs. Identities of punishers are not revealed, only whether and how much one was punished.",0.85,0,No reward option is included in the Fehr & Gächter peer punishment protocol; hence no reward identities.,0.95,1,"Standard repeated public goods experiments following Fehr & Gächter inform participants about the fixed number of periods; although not explicitly stated, this is standard and implied by design description (participants play for several trials with either partner or stranger matching).",0.7,1,"In the punishment condition, participants “are given an opportunity to pay to punish their group members… pay 1 MU to reduce partner’s earnings by 3 MUs.”",1,1,Methods explicitly: “pay 1 MU to reduce their partner's earnings by 3 MUs.”,1,3,Each 1 MU spent reduces target’s payoff by 3 MUs per protocol description.,1,0,"Inclusion criteria restrict to Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002) peer punishment protocol; no reward mechanism is mentioned.",0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism in this protocol.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this protocol.,1,10,Methods: “Each participant is endowed 10 monetary units (MUs) and then decides … how much … to donate.”,1,N/R,Meta-analysis reports standardized mean differences (d) but not raw contributions or normalized rates; cannot compute average fraction of endowment contributed.,0.1,N/R,Only effect sizes (d) and not the underlying means are presented in this article.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoffs or efficiency ratios are reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff levels are not provided; only effect sizes on cooperation.,0.1,"Multiple (18 countries; primarily US, UK, Switzerland, Russia, Netherlands, Israel, plus Australia, Belarus, China, Denmark, Germany, Greece, Italy, Saudi Arabia, South Africa, South Korea, Turkey, Ukraine)",Methods and Table 2 list the 18 countries from which primary studies were drawn.,1,"Adults (18+), primarily university students around ages 18–25",Inclusion criteria: “studies had to have been conducted on adult participants (age 18 and above).” Later: experimental studies “are primarily young adults (ages 18–25).”,0.95,N/R,No gender composition information is given for the samples.,0.1,Primarily university students in large-scale modern industrialized societies,Limitations section: “experimental studies included in the meta-analysis were conducted with university students.”,0.95,On site,Participants “come to the lab” to play; this indicates in-person laboratory (on-site) experiments.,0.98,"Repeated linear public goods game with 4 players, 10 MU endowment each round, contributions multiplied by 1.5 and shared equally. Two-stage structure each round: contribution stage then peer punishment stage (1 MU spent reduces target payoff by 3 MUs). Both partner-matching and stranger-matching variants and varying numbers of iterations and group sizes (3–7) are present across primary studies; this row captures the canonical Fehr & Gächter setup.","Synthesized from Methods, Coding procedure (partner vs stranger, group size range, iterations), and description of Fehr & Gächter protocol.",0.9
10.1007_s00199-007-0212-0.md,Baseline (one punishment stage),Authors label this treatment 'Baseline'; description: contribution stage then one punishment stage only.,1,"Treatment structure: 1 punishment stage; no second-stage punishment, no counterpunishment, no sanction enforcement. Information after punishment: others’ contributions and total punishment received, but no further sanctioning opportunities.",Baseline is defined as replication of Fehr and Gächter (2000) with only first contribution stage and one sanctioning stage.,1,True,Human undergraduate participants in lab sessions.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab treatments with fixed rules and incentives.,1,True,Conducted at LABEX of University of Rennes I.,1,False,No computer simulations or agent-based models described.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not formal analytical model as main design.",1,4,Groups of size four with fixed membership.,1,20,Each session has 20 periods of interaction.,1,1,"Subjects allocate an endowment between private and public accounts; any amount from 0 to 20 is allowed, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is received privately; subjects must choose any contribution; nothing starts in public account.,1,0.4,"Each ECU contributed yields 0.4 ECU to each of 4 members, so MPCR = 0.4.",1,0,No communication or chat mentioned; standard VCM without communication.,0.9,1,"After each period, screen displays each individual’s contribution, sanctioning information (as allowed), total punishment received, and earnings.",1,1,"In stage two, each member is informed of individual contributions and can assign points to each other; implies identification of who punished whom within the period.",0.9,0,"No rewarding mechanism described, only costly punishment points.",1,1,Instructions describe fixed 20 periods; standard for subjects to know horizon; though not explicitly said that remaining rounds appear on screen.,0.7,1,Stage two consists of assigning punishment points 0–10 to each other member.,1,1,"Cost function identical to Fehr & Gächter (2000): marginal cost c ECU for 2c-2 < p ≤ 2c; but precise per-point cost in ECUs not numerically specified; set to 1 as placeholder is not permitted, however schema requires numeric—actual marginal cost per point isn’t derivable, so use 0 and mark N/R in reason. UPDATED: Set to 0 with explanation.",0,0.1,Each point received reduces earnings by 10% of stage 1 earnings; so 1 point = 0.1 of base payoff.,1,0,No positive rewards; only punishment points.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each participant receives 20 ECUs at beginning of each period.,1,0.7745,Average individual contribution 15.49 out of 20; 15.49/20 ≈ 0.7745.,1,15.49,Table 1 reports average individual contribution 15.49 in Baseline.,1,N/R,"Paper reports earnings qualitatively and some averages, but not in a way that allows clean computation of fraction of maximum payoff per treatment.",0,N/R,Only some average individual earnings given; not full per-treatment averages over all periods.,0,France,"Sessions conducted at University of Rennes I, Rennes, France.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age range given.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,"Undergraduate university students (economics, law, management, medicine)",Described as undergraduate students from a variety of majors; about one-third economics and rest other fields.,1,On site,Lab sessions at LABEX with subjects physically present using z-Tree.,1,"Standard linear public goods game: 4 players, 20 ECU endowment, contribution returns 0.4 ECU to each player. One punishment stage with 0–10 punishment points per target; each point reduces victim’s stage-1 earnings by 10% up to 100%; sanctioner incurs convex cost per point as in Fehr & Gächter (2000). Groups fixed across 20 periods; within-group labels A–D re-randomized each period; identities anonymous.",Summarized from design and payoff equations in Section 2.,1
10.1007_s00199-007-0212-0.md,"Full Information (FI, three stages)","Treatment labeled 'Full Information'; three-stage game: contributions, then two sanctioning stages with full observability of sanctions in stage two.",1,"Treatment structure: 2 punishment stages; in stage 2 and 3 any player may punish any other. Information after stage 2: each player observes complete matrix of sanctions p_k^{j2} for all j,k (full information). Allows both counterpunishment and sanction enforcement.",Section 2.1–2.2 explicitly define Full Information treatment.,1,True,Human undergraduates in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with monetary incentives.,1,True,"Conducted at LABEX, University of Rennes I.",1,False,No simulation study.,1,False,"Design is experimental, not purely analytical.",1,4,Groups of four with fixed membership across 20 periods.,1,20,Each session has 20 periods under identical rules.,1,1,Subjects choose contribution from 0 to 20 ECUs; any amount allowed.,1,0,Endowment is private; contribution decision moves tokens to group account.,1,0.4,Public good technology identical in all treatments: 0.4 ECU per contributed ECU per player.,1,0,No communication phase described.,0.9,1,"End-of-period screen shows contributions, sanctions, total points received per stage, and earnings.",1,1,"In FI treatment, each player observes p_k^{j2} for all j,k; thus identities of sanctioners and recipients are revealed.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Fixed 20 periods; standard that horizon is known, though not graphically specified.",0.7,1,Two sanctioning stages (2 and 3) with punishment points 0–10.,1,0,Convex cost function referenced but exact ECU cost per point not numerically specified; cannot infer a per-unit cost parameter.,0,0.1,Each point received reduces stage-1 earnings by 10%; cumulative across stages up to 100%.,1,0,Only punishment contemplated.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No rewards.,1,20,Same parametric structure as Baseline: 20 ECU per period.,1,0.5295,Average individual contribution 10.59 (Table 1); 10.59/20 ≈ 0.5295.,1,10.59,Table 1 reports 10.59 average individual contribution in Full Information.,1,N/R,"Though maximum earnings noted (32 if full cooperation, 20 if zero contributions, no sanctions), treatment-specific average earnings for FI over all periods not provided numerically.",0,N/R,No numeric group or individual average payoff over sessions reported specifically for FI.,0,France,"All treatments share same subject pool at Rennes, France.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduates.,0.3,N/R,No further demographic breakdown.,0,"Undergraduate university students (economics, law, management, medicine)",Same sample across treatments.,1,On site,Same LABEX environment as other treatments.,1,"Three-stage repeated public goods game. Stage 1: contribute 0–20 ECUs to group account (MPCR=0.4). Stage 2: each player sees all contributions, chooses 0–10 punishment points for each of the three others; each point reduces victim’s stage-1 earnings by 10%, up to 100%; sanctioner pays convex cost per point. Information: after stage 2, each player observes full matrix of sanctions (who punished whom and by how much). Stage 3: same punishment technology and costs; each player may again assign 0–10 points to any other, informed by all earlier sanctions. Final payoff: stage-1 earnings multiplied by factor reflecting all points received across stages 2–3, minus all sanctioning costs.",Summarized from Sections 2.1–2.2 and payoff equations.,1
10.1007_s00199-007-0212-0.md,"Revenge Only (RO, three stages)",Treatment labeled 'Revenge Only'; three stages with information structure allowing only counterpunishment.,1,Treatment structure: 2 punishment stages; in stage 2 and 3 any player may punish any other. Information after stage 2: each player only sees sanctions they personally received in stage 2 (p_k^{i2} for all k). Enables counterpunishment but not sanction enforcement based on third-party punishment.,Section 2.1–2.2 description of 'Revenge Only' treatment.,1,True,Same human-subject lab protocol.,1,True,"Random assignment to treatment, controlled incentives.",1,True,"Done at LABEX, Rennes.",1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Empirical design.,1,4,Groups of four; common across treatments.,1,20,20 periods per session.,1,1,Flexible contributions 0–20 ECUs.,1,0,Endowment initially private.,1,0.4,Same VCM technology.,1,0,No communication mentioned.,0.9,1,"End-of-period summary includes allowed sanction information, total points received, and earnings.",1,1,"In Revenge Only, each player observes p_k^{i2} (who punished them). Identities of their own punishers are thus revealed.",1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Finite horizon of 20 periods known to participants.,0.7,1,Two sanctioning stages with punishment points 0–10 per target.,1,0,Cost function referenced but no numeric per-point cost provided.,0,0.1,Each point reduces stage-1 earnings by 10%; structure same as other treatments.,1,0,No mention of reward points.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Endowment 20 ECUs per period across all treatments.,1,0.3605,Average contribution 7.21 (Table 1); 7.21/20 ≈ 0.3605.,1,7.21,Table 1 lists 7.21 average contributions in Revenge Only.,1,N/R,"Some average earnings reported (19.94 tokens per period) but maximum-without-sanctions vs with sanctions not fully decomposed, making normalized efficiency ambiguous.",0.3,N/R,Only one scalar (19.94) given; not fully clear if this is exactly overall period average; group-payoff per period not detailed.,0.3,France,Same lab and pool.,1,N/R,No ages.,0.3,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0,"Undergraduate university students (economics, law, management, medicine)",Shared recruitment pool.,1,On site,Lab sessions at LABEX.,1,"Three-stage repeated public goods game. Same contribution and punishment technologies as Baseline. Stage 2: players observe others’ contributions and their own provisional earnings; distribute 0–10 punishment points to each other member. Information after stage 2: each player only sees total points received from each other (who punished them and by how much), but no information about sanctions among other players. Stage 3: same punishment technology; players again can punish any others, relying only on information about who punished them in stage 2 (thus counterpunishment possible but sanction enforcement with respect to third parties not directly informed). Final period payoff uses total points received in both punishment stages.",Summarized from treatment description and payoff equations.,1
10.1007_s00199-007-0212-0.md,"No Revenge (NR, three stages)",Treatment labeled 'No Revenge'; information structure disallows identifying personal punishers but allows observing third-party sanctions.,1,"Treatment structure: 2 punishment stages; in stage 2 and 3 any player may punish any other. Information after stage 2: each player observes sanctions each player received from others, except they do not observe who punished themselves (they see p_k^{j2} for all k and j ≠ i, but not for j=i). Enables sanction enforcement on third-party punishment but limits direct counterpunishment toward own punishers.",Section 2.1–2.2 define No Revenge information structure.,1,True,Same lab experiment with students.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of information structure.,1,True,Conducted in university lab.,1,False,No simulation.,1,False,Behavioural experiment rather than pure theory.,1,4,Groups of four in each session.,1,20,20 periods per treatment.,1,1,Continuous 0–20 contribution choice.,1,0,Endowment provided privately.,1,0.4,Same public goods production.,1,0,Instructions mention no communication.,0.9,1,End-of-period screen shows contributions and allowed sanction information plus points received.,1,1,"Player i observes p_k^{j2} for all k and j ≠ i; therefore, identities of sanctioners for others are known; identities of personal punishers are hidden, but punishment identity exists in information set for third parties.",0.9,0,No rewards.,1,1,Fixed 20-period horizon.,0.7,1,Two punishment stages present.,1,0,Exact ECU cost per punishment point not numerically given.,0,0.1,Same 10% earnings reduction per point.,1,0,No reward system.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No rewards.,1,20,20 ECUs each period.,1,0.8085,Average contribution 16.17; 16.17/20 ≈ 0.8085.,1,16.17,Table 1 reports 16.17 average contributions in No Revenge.,1,N/R,No precise average earnings provided for NR to compute efficiency ratio.,0,N/R,Earnings only discussed in comparative/statistical terms.,0,France,Same lab location.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0.3,N/R,Not described.,0,"Undergraduate university students (economics, law, management, medicine)",Same population.,1,On site,Same LABEX lab.,1,"Identical contribution and punishment technologies to FI and RO. Stage 2: players allocate punishment points given knowledge of all contributions and their own provisional earnings. Information after stage 2: player i sees how much each other player j ≠ i was punished by each k (full matrix excluding their own row as recipient), but does not see which players punished i personally. Stage 3: players again may assign punishment informed by history of contributions and third-party sanctions; enables sanction enforcement against low or high third-party punishers while limiting direct counterpunishment against own sanctioners.",Summarized from treatment description.,1
10.1007_s00199-007-0212-0.md,Six-Stage Full Information (6SFI),Treatment labeled 'six-stage full Information' or '6SFI'; extends FI to five punishment stages (stages 2–6).,1,"Treatment structure: 5 punishment stages after contribution (stages 2–6). In each punishment stage any player may punish any other. After each stage s≥2, players observe full history of all sanctions p_j^{k,r} for all j,k and all prior stages r<s (full information over time). Allows complex counterpunishment, sanction enforcement, and higher-order sanctions.",Section 2.1–2.2 define 'six-stage full Information treatment (6SFI)'.,1,True,Two sessions under 6SFI with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled laboratory manipulation of number of sanctioning stages.,1,True,Sessions at LABEX.,1,False,No purely simulated agents.,1,False,Data-based experiment.,1,4,"Groups of four; in 6SFI one session had 8 participants (2 groups), another 16 (4 groups), still 4 per group.",1,20,Same 20 periods apply.,1,1,Contribution decision remains 0–20 ECUs continuous.,1,0,Endowment initially private.,1,0.4,Same VCM parameters.,1,0,No communication between subjects mentioned.,0.9,1,"After each period players see contributions, sanctions, total points received each stage, period earnings, and accumulated earnings.",1,1,"At end of each punishment stage s, subject i observes p_j^{k,r} for all j,k and r<s; thus identities of all sanctioners and recipients in all prior stages are fully revealed.",1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Finite 20 periods; usual to announce number in instructions.,0.7,1,Punishment points exist in five stages per period (2–6).,1,0,Same convex cost function as other treatments; unit cost not numerically specified.,0,0.1,Each point reduces stage-1 earnings by 10%; summed across stages 2–6 up to 100%.,1,0,Only punishments.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No rewards.,1,20,20 ECU endowment per period.,1,0.4965,Average contribution 9.93; 9.93/20 ≈ 0.4965.,1,9.93,Table 1 shows 9.93 average individual contribution in Six Stage Full Information.,1,N/R,Paper notes that average earnings are often below 20 and compares welfare qualitatively; no exact session-wide average payoff reported to compute normalized efficiency.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative pattern and relative Mann-Whitney results included.,0.2,France,Same lab and recruitment.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,N/R,Not specified.,0,"Undergraduate university students (economics, law, management, medicine)",Common subject pool.,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab using z-Tree.,1,"Six-stage repeated public goods game. Stage 1: contribution as in other treatments. Stages 2–6: in each, players observe full history of all prior sanctions (who punished whom, in which stage, and by how many points) and then can assign 0–10 points to each other member. Same punishment technology and convex cost function; victim’s final payoff = stage-1 earnings multiplied by max{0,1 - (1/10)*sum over all stages 2–6 of points received from others} minus sum of all personal punishment costs over all stages. Design enables rich patterns of counterpunishment (e.g., punishing those who punished you earlier), sanction enforcement (punishing those who did not punish free riders or punished high contributors), and higher-order punishment chains. Average points per period per individual = 4.11 (Table 3), 2.71 times next-highest treatment.",Summarized from description of 6SFI treatment and payoff equation.,1
