10.1016_j.plrev.2016.08.015.md,Public Goods game with evolvable punishment – hysteresis experiment (Fig. 13),Fig. 13: hysteresis via adiabatic change in synergy r with evolving punishment.,1,"Synergy factor r slowly increased then decreased (adiabatic sweep); punishment probability evolvable (encoded as gene); k unspecified but likely k=4; N unspecified here but earlier punishment sims use N=1,024; β=0.8, γ=0.2; mutation rate μ=1%; initial population fractions of strategies set to 0.5.","Text: 'r is adiabatically changed', 'Here punishment can evolve, with β=0.8 and γ=0.2, μ=1%. All population fractions are started at 0.5.' N presumed consistent with earlier section.",0.7,False,Simulation only.,1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Agent-based PG with evolvable punishment gene.,1,False,Hysteresis observed numerically; mean-field theory insufficient.,1,1024,"Earlier punishment simulations in same section use N=1,024; assumed same here.",0.6,N/R,Exact number of updates per r-step and total not specified.,0.3,1,Binary cooperate/defect in PG.,0.8,N/R,Not specified.,0.4,N/R,r and group size present but MPCR not enumerated.,0.5,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No UI.,1,N/A,No notion of identity to show; simulation only.,0.9,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,Punishment with effect β and cost γ is central to hysteresis.,1,0.2,γ=0.2 parameter.,1,0.8,β=0.8 parameter.,1,0,Only punishment; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,1,Same PG structure as earlier punishment runs; cooperators pay cost 1.,0.6,N/R,Plot shows 'density of cooperators' vs r for increasing/decreasing sweeps but not specific numeric values apart from approximate crossing near r≈4.7 and 4.2.,0.5,N/R,Average contributions equal cooperator density; only shown graphically.,0.5,N/R,Hysteresis is qualitative; efficiency not computed.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs not quantified.,0.3,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based PG with punishment and evolving punishment gene.,1,"Public Goods game with costly punishment in which both cooperation and punishment propensities evolve. Starting from mixed populations (fractions 0.5), the synergy parameter r is increased slowly from low to high values and then decreased. Simulations reveal meta-stable cooperative and defecting phases and hysteresis: cooperation emerges around r≈4.7 when increasing r, and persists down to r≈4.2 when decreasing r, demonstrating nucleation-like phase transitions driven by punishment dynamics.",Highlights goal of this specific hysteresis protocol and qualitative findings.,1
10.1073_pnas.0507229103.md,Replicator dynamics model with voluntary participation and punishment,"Single theoretical model described, with four strategy types (cooperators, defectors, loners, punishers) and specified payoff functions; no separate parameter-based conditions are treated as distinct experiments.",0.9,"Population state composition (frequencies x: cooperators, y: defectors, z: loners, w: punishers) and parameters r, N, σ, α, β, γ governing payoffs in the public goods game with optional participation and punishment.",Only varying elements are the strategy frequencies and game parameters in the payoff and replicator equations; no human treatment variables.,0.9,False,The paper uses evolutionary game theory and replicator dynamics; no human or animal participants are mentioned.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; it is a theoretical analysis.,1,False,No lab setting; purely theoretical model.,1,True,"Numerical simulations of the replicator dynamics are described to explore basins of attraction and periodic orbits, with figures illustrating trajectories.",0.9,True,"Closed-form payoff expressions and analytical results about fixed points, Nash equilibria, and stability are derived.",1,5,They define a sample of size N and explicitly state parameter values in figures with n=5; interpreted as group size N=5.,0.7,1,Model is a one-shot public goods game repeated across generations via replicator dynamics; intra-game interaction is single-period.,0.7,1,"Strategies are cooperate (contribute cost 1) or defect (contribute 0); no continuous contribution levels specified, so contributions are effectively all-or-nothing.",0.8,N/A,No human subjects or experimental interface; endowment allocation at start is not defined in behavioral terms.,1,N/R,"They specify contribution cost 1, multiplier r, and group size N but do not explicitly provide r/N or label it as MPCR.",0.6,N/A,No human interaction or communication protocol; theoretical population model.,1,0,"No user interface or feedback structure is described; payoffs are computed, not observed by agents as summaries.",0.9,N/A,Punishment is modeled as an abstract payoff reduction; there is no notion of identity revelation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present in the model.,1,0,No experimental interface; number of evolutionary time steps is not shown to agents.,1,1,Punishers incur cost γ (or αγ) to reduce payoffs of defectors by β and of nonpunishing cooperators by αβ.,1,1,Parameter values used in figures are given as γ=1; this is the cost per unit of punishment effect.,0.9,1.2,"For defectors, payoff is reduced by β per punished individual; figures use β=1.2, treated as punishment efficacy per unit cost γ=1.",0.8,0,Model includes only punishment of defectors and cooperators; no positive reward mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,They normalize cost of contributing to 1; interpreted as players having 1 unit they can contribute.,0.7,N/R,They analyze evolutionary frequencies x and w (contributors) but do not report a scalar summary labeled contribution rate.,0.6,N/R,Individual contributions are binary (0 or 1) and not summarized as an explicit dependent variable.,0.6,N/R,"They report average payoffs at attractors (e.g., r−1 vs σ) but do not normalize these by maximum possible payoff as an efficiency measure.",0.6,N/R,"Average payoff per individual at equilibria (e.g., r−1, σ) is discussed, but total group payoff is not explicitly provided.",0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and computational model without human subjects.,1,"Public goods game with optional participation (loners receiving autarkic payoff σ). Group of size N is randomly selected from a large population. Contributors pay cost 1; total contributions are multiplied by r and divided among participants. Strategy types: cooperators (contribute, no punishment), defectors (no contribution), loners (do not participate, receive σ), punishers (contribute and punish defectors by β and cooperators by αβ, at costs γ and αγ). Assumptions: N>r>1+σ and β>1>α>0. Dynamics follow replicator equations on the 4-strategy simplex.",Summarizes the formal game specification given in the Methods section.,1
