or display; theoretical single-interaction model.,1,1,Core of the model is that player 2 may punish defection at cost a to reduce partner fitness by d.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost is represented by parameter a in fitness units, not in experimental tokens; no concrete numeric cost beyond example parameterization (e.g., a=0.1) that would map to tokens.",0.8,N/R,Effectiveness of punishment is parameter d in fitness units; again no mapping to per-token or per-unit effect as in experimental games.,0.8,0,Model includes only punishment of defectors; rewards are discussed conceptually but not included in the formal model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented in the model.,1,N/R,"Model is stated in terms of fitness costs/benefits (c,b,a,d), not endowments or tokens.",1,N/R,"No empirical outcome data; cooperation x is a strategy variable, not a measured dependent variable from participants.",1,N/R,No empirical or simulated token contributions are reported.,1,N/R,No numerical efficiency outcomes are computed relative to a maximum payoff baseline.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not reported as numeric outcome measures; only functional forms of fitness are examined.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists solely of analytical modeling without empirical or field data.,1,"Analytical pairwise interaction model where player 1 chooses cooperation (cost c, benefit b to partner) or defection; player 2 responds to defection with punishment or forgiveness (punishment costs a to punisher, reduces partner fitness by d). Extended example model assumes groups of size N with random matching and facultative cooperation x=f(Y) based on perceived punishment environment.",Summarizes the structure of the core and example models described in the Methods and Example sections.,1
10.1086_425623.md,Facultative cooperation example model with group size N,Paper introduces a more detailed example model where individuals are in groups of size N with facultative cooperation x=f(Y); treated here as a distinct analytical condition from the simpler pairwise model.,0.95,"Group size N, punishment parameters (a, d), cooperation cost and benefit (c, b), behavioral error ε in facultative response function f(Y), absence of relatedness (dY/dy=0)","Evolutionary outcomes (invasion and stability of punishment) are analyzed as functions of N, a, d, c, b, and ε, under zero relatedness.",1,False,No human or animal subjects; theoretical example.,1,False,"No experimental design; only mathematical modeling and some numerical illustration (e.g., Figure 3).",1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,False,"Behavioral error function f(Y) is specified analytically and figures are based on numerical evaluation, but there is no agent-based or stochastic simulation.",0.9,True,Fitness differential Δw and ESS conditions are derived analytically for this facultative model.,1,N/R,"Within groups of size N, individuals interact in random pairs; the number of simultaneous players in the strategic interaction is effectively 2, but N varies and is an analytical parameter. Since N is not fixed to a specific numeric value, a single playerCount cannot be given.",0.5,1,Each encounter is a single interaction where player 1 may cooperate or defect and player 2 may punish or forgive; no repeated rounds with the same partner are modeled.,0.9,0,"Punishment strategy y and cooperation probability are continuous in [0,1]; not constrained to all-or-nothing actions.",1,N/A,No explicit endowments or public account; model is framed in fitness increments.,1,N/R,Not a linear public-goods setting with an MPCR; benefits are parameter b to a single partner.,1,N/A,No experimental communication; model assumes random pairing and no partner recognition.,1,N/A,No feedback or summary information between rounds because there is no explicit round structure.,1,N/A,Identities and information structure are not part of the analytical model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; one-shot analytical interactions.,1,1,"Punishment strategy y is central to the facultative model, affecting partners' cooperation via f(Y).",1,N/R,"Punishment cost is denoted a in fitness terms, but not mapped to experimental units or tokens.",0.8,N/R,Damage from punishment is parameter d; no explicit per-unit technology analogous to lab experiments.,0.8,0,Model includes only punishment; reward mechanisms are not implemented.,1,N/A,No reward in the model.,1,N/A,No reward in the model.,1,N/R,No endowments or tokens; costs and benefits are in fitness units.,1,N/R,"Cooperation probability f(Y) is an evolved strategy, not an empirically measured dependent variable; no numeric outcome reported as a result.",1,N/R,No token amounts; only probabilities and fitness effects.,1,N/R,Efficiency relative to maximum group payoff is not numerically computed; only qualitative ESS conditions are discussed.,1,N/R,Average group fitness expressions are derived but not given as specific numeric outcomes for particular parameter combinations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"The facultative example is entirely analytical with some numerical illustration (e.g., Figures 2 and 3).",1,"Individuals are randomly organized into groups of size N with zero relatedness; in each encounter, a random pair interacts as in the basic model (player 1 cooperation vs defection; player 2 punishment vs forgiveness). Cooperation of player 1 is facultative: x = f(Y), where Y is perceived average punishment in the group, modeled by a logistic response x = 1 / (1 + exp[-(Y d - c)/ε]). Punishment strategy y affects partners' perceived punishment environment and thus their cooperation. Fitness expressions w and w̄ are derived and used to analyze invasion and stability of punishment as a function of N, a, d, c, b, and ε.","Summarizes the structure of the facultative cooperation example section, including group size, random pairing, and the response function f(Y).",1
10.1007_s12110-005-1000-9.md,1974 conversation sample – Ju/'hoansi norm enforcement,Paper reports two main data collections: 1974 conversations in Dobe and /Kae/kae vs. 1996–1997 in Xamsa and !Ao; treat each as an observational condition.,1,"Time period and socioecological context (1974, Dobe and /Kae/kae camps, primarily foragers with emerging changes)",Only clear between-condition difference is year/location/context; no experimental manipulation.,0.9,True,Data are from field notes of 308 naturally occurring conversations among Ju/'hoansi; these involve human participants.,1,False,No random assignment or controlled intervention; descriptive ethnographic/observational study.,1,False,"Conversations recorded in natural village camps, not a lab setting.",1,False,No mention of computer or numerical simulations.,1,False,"Paper is qualitative/quantitative ethnography, not a formal mathematical model.",1,7.5,Table 1: 'Mean number of adults in conversations with criticism*' is 7.5 for 1974.,1,1,Each recorded conversation episode functions as a single interaction; no repeated structured rounds like a game.,0.6,1,"Not a contribution game; when mapping to PGG structure, there is no restricted all-or-nothing choice, so treat as flexible (1).",0.3,N/A,No experimental endowment or formal contribution decision; observational conversations.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or MPCR defined; not an economic game.,1,1,The core behavior studied is face-to-face conversation; communication is inherent.,1,1,"Group conversations involved multiple camp members discussing others' behavior, effectively sharing information about others' actions and sanctions.",0.8,1,"Punishment is via direct verbal criticism, joking, or pantomime with target present or identifiable; identities are known.",1,1,"Praise is also directed toward specific individuals in conversation, so identities are known.",0.9,0,No notion of total number of future conversations/rounds was provided to participants.,1,1,"Central focus is on punishment through criticism, shaming, ridicule, ostracism, and occasional violence.",1,N/A,"No explicit per-unit cost parameter; costs discussed qualitatively (loss of members, conflict).",1,N/A,"No numeric punishment technology (e.g., tokens-to-fines) specified.",1,1,Paper notes 22 conversations (7%) included praise and discusses reward as a norm-enforcement mechanism.,1,N/A,"No numeric cost parameter for rewards (e.g., praise or meat sharing).",1,N/A,No formal mapping from reward action to payoff units.,1,N/A,"No game endowment or tokens; subsistence and social relations, not experimental payoffs.",1,N/R,No normalized contribution rate is defined or calculable from provided data.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions to a public good are reported.,1,N/R,No explicit calculation of total payoffs vs. maximum cooperative payoff.,1,N/R,No monetary or token payoffs; only qualitative welfare and harmony outcomes discussed.,1,Botswana,"1974 sample location is 'Dobe and /Kae/kae, Northwest Botswana' (Table 1).",1,"Unmarried young adults, adults with small kids, adults with mature kids, elderly adults",Tables 3 and 5 define these age categories for initiators and targets; participants span across.,1,"Approximately 51% male, 49% female",Table 4: 'Men made up 51% of the sample and women 49%'.,1,N/R,No formal schooling levels or education information for Ju/'hoansi participants is provided.,1,Field experiment,"Naturalistic field observations in villages; though not an experiment, closest category among options is field setting with humans.",0.8,"Observational ethnographic study of 152 conversations in 4 Ju/'hoansi villages (camp size 20–40) in 1974, focusing on norm enforcement via verbal and social sanctions; 90 cases of punishment in 1974, with 23% pantomime/joking, 41% outright criticism, 31% harsh criticism, 2% criticism plus violent acts. Two-thirds of criticism conversations were within-village, one-third included visiting kin.","Summarizes key structural details (sample size, village count, camp size, forms of punishment) specific to 1974 condition.",1
10.1007_s12110-005-1000-9.md,1996–1997 conversation sample – Ju/'hoansi norm enforcement,Second main data collection: 1996–1997 conversations in Xamsa and !Ao in northeast Namibia; treat as separate observational condition.,1,"Time period and socioecological context (1996–1997, Xamsa and !Ao villages, more sedentary with government rations, wages, and alcohol availability)","Only systematic difference from 1974 is later time, different region, and changed subsistence/settlement context.",0.9,True,Data from 156 naturally occurring conversations among Ju/'hoansi in 1996–1997.,1,False,No manipulation or randomization; purely observational fieldwork.,1,False,"Conducted in actual Ju/'hoansi villages, not a lab.",1,False,No simulations described.,1,False,No purely analytical model; empirical ethnographic analysis.,1,6.8,Table 1: 'Mean number of adults in conversations with criticism*' is 6.8 for 1996–1997.,1,1,Each recorded conversation is treated as a single interaction; no defined repeated-game structure.,0.6,1,Not a formal game; there is no all-or-nothing restriction on behaviors in conversations.,0.3,N/A,No experimental endowment or contribution decision.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier specified.,1,1,Conversations and verbal interactions are the primary data; communication is present.,1,1,"Group discussions convey information about others' behaviors and sanctions (e.g., coalitions rallying against offenders).",0.8,1,Targets of criticism are known to participants and often present or within earshot.,1,1,"Praise in conversations is directed toward identifiable individuals (e.g., for hunting, music, healing, xaro exchange).",0.9,0,No information about total number of future conversational interactions is provided to participants.,1,1,Same norm-enforcement mechanisms as 1974; 81 punishment cases in 1996–1997 with similar distribution of forms (Table 6).,1,N/A,No per-unit punishment cost parameter; only qualitative discussion of social costs.,1,N/A,No numeric fine or loss per unit punishment specified.,1,1,"Praise and positive recognition, plus sharing of meat or resources as reconciliation, described for both periods; no separate frequencies by period but mechanism exists.",0.9,N/A,No quantitative mapping from reward actions to costs.,1,N/A,No defined technology for converting reward actions to benefits.,1,N/A,No experimental tokens or monetary endowments used.,1,N/R,No concept of contribution rate to a public good reported.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions to any pooled resource are given.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure or comparable payoff data.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are in terms of social harmony, ostracism, etc., not quantified payoffs.",1,Namibia,"1996–1997 sample located in 'Xamsa and !Ao, Northeast Namibia' (Table 1).",1,"Unmarried young adults, adults with small kids, adults with mature kids, elderly adults",Same age categories used across combined analyses; 1996–1997 includes adults in all categories.,1,"Approximately 51% male, 49% female",Table 4 describes sex composition of sample; applies to combined dataset spanning both time periods.,1,N/R,No information on schooling or formal education in 1996–1997 sample.,1,Field experiment,"As with 1974, these are field observations in natural village context; among given options, 'Field experiment' best matches non-lab, in-situ human data.",0.8,"Observational ethnographic study of 156 conversations in 2 Ju/'hoansi villages (Xamsa and !Ao, village size 20–40) in 1996–1997, during a period of partial sedentarization and mixed subsistence (about 30% foraging, 70% rations, wages, crafts, pensions). 81 punishment cases coded: 17% pantomime/joking, 41% outright criticism, 38% harsh criticism, 2% criticism plus violent acts. Increased criticism about drunkenness and reclusive behavior, and some complaints about cooperative leaders and land politics.","Summarizes key structural details (sample size, villages, punishment forms, changing issues) specific to 1996–1997 condition.",1
10.1038_srep03055.md,PD – square lattice – no punishment (β = 0),"Baseline Prisoner’s Dilemma without social punishment, inferred from text references to the “standard formulation (i.e., no social punishment)” and insets in Figs. 1 and 3.",0.72,"Game = Prisoner’s Dilemma; Network = square lattice (N = 10^4, ⟨k⟩ = 4); Punishment fine β = 0; Punishment cost γ = N/A (no punishment); Temptation to defect b varied; Initial strategies: C, D, Pu with equal probability but Pu has no effect when β = 0.","Core independent factors are game type, network topology, and presence/absence of punishment (β). Other parameters like N, ⟨k⟩ and b are central to dynamics.",0.7,False,Study uses numerical simulations of evolutionary games on networks with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; purely computational model.,1,False,No physical lab with participants; simulations only.,1,True,Authors repeatedly refer to results from numerical simulations on networks.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations presented; results are from simulations.,1,10000,Methods: all networks have size N = 10^4 nodes.,1,110000,"They simulate up to a transient t0 = 10^5 time-steps, then average over an additional 10^4 time-steps; total 110000 iterations per run.",0.8,1,2-person matrix game with discrete strategies C/D/Pu; no continuous contribution choice; coded as 1 because players choose between full cooperation or defection rather than a fraction of an endowment.,0.6,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game; no endowment allocation.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface; simulations only.,1,N/A,No identities in simulations; and no punishment in this baseline.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents do not observe round counts.,1,0,Baseline PD is described as standard formulation without social punishment; β effectively zero.,0.9,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,Only social punishment is modeled; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"2x2 game with fixed payoffs, not framed as endowment allocation.",1,N/R,"Outcome reported is fraction of cooperative agents ⟨c⟩, not normalized contributions from an endowment.",0.4,N/R,No contributions in token units; payoffs are matrix entries.,1,N/R,They do not report aggregate payoffs normalized by maximum possible.,0.4,N/R,Total or average payoff per group/network not given numerically.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational simulations on complex networks.,1,"2-person Prisoner’s Dilemma on a square lattice (periodic boundary conditions), payoffs R = 1, P = S = 0, T = b > 1; synchronous strategy updating using finite-population analogue of replicator dynamics with imitation probability Π_{i→j} = (P_j − P_i) / (max{k_i, k_j} Δ); initial strategies C, D, Pu assigned with equal probability; three network classes studied but this condition uses the square lattice.",Summarizes key game and dynamic details stated in Methods and Results specific to PD and square lattice.,0.9
10.1038_srep03055.md,PD – square lattice – social punishment (β > 0),"Prisoner’s Dilemma with social punishers on a square lattice; β takes several positive values (0.1, 0.3, 0.5,…), grouped here as the punished PD condition on this topology.",0.78,"Game = Prisoner’s Dilemma; Network = square lattice (N = 10^4, ⟨k⟩ = 4); Punishment fine β ∈ {0.1, 0.3, 0.5,…} with β > γ; Punishment cost γ = 0.1; Temptation to defect b varied; Strategy set = {C, D, Pu}.","Key manipulated parameters are introduction and magnitude of β, with fixed γ and varying b on a given network.",0.85,False,Numerical simulations without human participants.,1,False,No human experimental protocol; this is a computational study.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; simulations only.,1,True,Authors repeatedly mention numerical simulations on networks.,1,False,No analytic solution of dynamics provided.,1,10000,Methods: all networks have N = 10^4 nodes.,1,110000,Transient t0 = 10^5 steps plus 10^4 additional steps for averaging.,0.8,1,Binary strategies in a 2x2 (extended to 3-strategy) matrix game; no continuous contributions.,0.6,N/A,No contribution endowment framework.,1,N/A,Not a public goods setting.,1,N/A,No humans or communication protocol.,1,N/A,Not applicable to simulations without interfaces.,1,N/A,No individual identities or displays; agents interact via rules only.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No displayed round information to agents.,1,1,Punishers (Pu) impose a fine β to defectors at cost γ; this is the central mechanism studied.,1,0.1,Methods: γ represents the cost of punishment; all PD figures use γ = 0.1.,0.95,N/R,"Exact payoff reduction per unit cost is defined by β and γ in the matrix, but an explicit numeric ratio (fine per cost unit) beyond β > γ is not fully specified in text; β itself acts as the per-interaction fine, while γ is a fixed cost per act.",0.4,0,"Model only includes punishment, not positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Matrix payoff game, not an endowment allocation.",1,N/R,"Main outcome is ⟨c⟩, fraction of cooperators and punishers at steady state; not a contribution from an endowment.",0.5,N/R,No explicit contribution amount variable; only strategies and payoffs.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure is reported.,0.4,N/R,"Figures plot strategy fractions, not group payoffs.",0.4,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulations; no humans.,1,N/A,Simulations; no humans.,1,N/A,Simulations; no humans.,1,No human,Numerical simulations of agents on networks.,1,"2-person Prisoner’s Dilemma on a square lattice (periodic boundary conditions). Payoffs: R = 1, P = S = 0, T = b > 1. Strategy set: C, D, Pu. When Pu meets D, Pu pays cost γ and D receives a fine β (β > γ). When Pu meets C or Pu, they behave as cooperators. Synchronous updating with imitation probability Π_{i→j} = (P_j − P_i) / (max{k_i, k_j} Δ). Initial strategies C, D, Pu assigned with equal probability. Results include ⟨c⟩ as function of b for various β, and cluster statistics N_cc, N_c.",Directly summarizes methods and PD plus punishment specifics on square lattice.,0.95
