10.1098_rspb.2008.1082.md,Segment 2 – One-punishment (single designated punisher) condition,Second segment condition where only one randomly selected group member can punish.,1,Punishment availability: only one randomly selected group member per round may punish; punisher selection: single designated,Text states 'one member per group was randomly selected after each investment phase to make deductions' in this condition.,1,True,Human participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to the one-punishment condition and controlled manipulation of punishment structure.,1,True,Executed in a large computer laboratory with networked PCs.,1,False,No simulation-only component.,1,False,Not a closed-form or purely analytical study.,1,4,Game is played in groups of four.,1,6,Six rounds in this second segment.,1,1,Participants can invest any subset of 20 MUs.,1,0,20 MUs are endowed privately; players choose contributions.,0.95,0.5,Contribution technology is identical across conditions.,1,0,No mention of communication; anonymity stressed.,0.95,1,Information on investments of other group members is provided after contributions.,1,0,Only deductions from own account are shown; interactions anonymous; no indication that player identities of punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,No reward option.,1,0,Design does not indicate explicit feedback on remaining rounds.,0.4,1,One individual per group can make deductions in Segment 2.,1,1,Same punishment technology as in all-punishment: a fee in MUs up to 10 MUs per punished member.,0.9,3,Deduction from target is three times the fee.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,20 MUs per participant per round.,1,N/R,"Figure 1 shows similar levels to all-punishment, but no numbers provided.",0.2,N/R,No explicit mean MUs contributed reported for this condition.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency relative to maximum payoff not quantified per condition.,0.1,N/R,"Mean profits for this condition only shown graphically, not numerically.",0.2,UK,Same sample of University of Kent undergraduates.,0.95,N/R,No age data.,0.01,"35% male, 65% female",Overall composition of sample; assignment across conditions unspecified.,0.8,Undergraduate students,Participants were described as undergraduate students.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment in a university computer room.,1,"Same public goods game and stranger protocol as other conditions; after each investment phase, one member per group is randomly selected to have the option to impose costly punishment on others using a fee up to 10 MUs per target with 1:3 fee-to-deduction ratio; only their own received deductions are revealed to players.",Described explicitly under the one-punishment condition.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.06.010.md,z-mixed population model without anti-social punishment (restricted 4-strategy game),"Analytical treatment in Section 3.1 where only DN, DP, CN, CP are allowed (no A or S).",1,"Punishment effectiveness parameters (b, β, α, c) and interaction degree z in the z-mixed population model, with anti-social punishment disabled (strategy set {DN, DP, CN, CP}).","Section 3 analyzes ESS regions in the (b, β) space for fixed c, α and varying z, without allowing anti-social punishment.",1,False,Model is purely theoretical/evolutionary; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment but an analytical evolutionary model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; equations only.,1,False,"This part is treated analytically (ESS conditions), not via numerical simulation.",1,True,Derives ESS conditions and stability inequalities using payoff matrices and viability dynamics.,1,5,"Groups of size z+1, with example stability condition for general z; conceptually equivalent to z+1-player public goods game. A specific numerical example uses z=4 (later mapped to lattice), so here we use z+1=5 as representative.",0.4,1,"Two-stage game (cooperation then punishment) but single evolutionary interaction round per generation, not repeated between same individuals in the game-theoretic sense.",0.6,1,Players choose binary actions C or D; no continuous contribution amounts.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game with movable endowment; binary cooperate/defect with cost c and benefit b.,1,N/R,"MPCR not defined; game framed as pairwise benefit b to each other (or equivalently public goods), but no explicit per-capita multiplier.",0,N/A,No human subjects or communication; evolutionary dynamics only.,1,0,No feedback interface; payoffs enter only into death probabilities.,1,N/A,No identities or information structure described; purely strategic types.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,0,No notion of displayed number of rounds; evolutionary process is ongoing.,1,1,Second stage allows costly punishment of defectors by P-type strategies.,1,1,In examples and figures for parameter regions they set c=1 and α=1; α is cost per punishment act.,0.8,1,"They use β as loss to target; in figures they set α=1, c=1 and vary β, but no fixed numeric β; representative tech ratio β/α not constant. Here punishmentTech is loss per unit cost; with α=1 this equals β which is variable, so a single numeric value cannot be given.",0,0,Only punishment is modeled; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game formulated in terms of costs and benefits (c, b) not explicit endowments.",0,N/R,"Paper reports which strategies are ESS, not quantitative average cooperation levels.",0,N/R,No aggregate amounts reported; only symbolic payoffs.,0,N/R,No normalized efficiency measures reported.,0,N/R,Only individual payoff expressions used analytically; no aggregate numeric outcomes.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical evolutionary game analysis.,1,"Two-stage binary cooperation/punishment game in z-mixed population; strategies DN, DP, CN, CP only. Cost of cooperation c, benefit b to each other group member, punishment cost α to punisher and cost β to target. Score-dependent viability updating with death probability d(f)=γ exp(-θ f); agents interact with z partners (group size z+1). Anti-social punishment strategies A and S excluded.",Summarized from Sections 2 and 3.1 and Figure 1A description (restricted strategy set without antisocial punishment).,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.06.010.md,z-mixed population model with full 8-strategy set (including anti-social punishment),"Analytical treatment in Section 3.2 and surrounding text where all 8 strategies DN, DP, DA, DS, CN, CP, CA, CS are allowed and ESS regions are derived (Figure 1B).",1,"Punishment effectiveness parameters (b, β, α, c) and interaction degree z in the z-mixed population model, with anti-social punishment enabled (strategy set {DN, DP, DA, DS, CN, CP, CA, CS}).","Section 3.2 studies ESS of all eight strategies as a function of b, β, α, c, z; main result is that DN or DS is ESS depending on β relative to zα.",1,False,No human or animal subjects; evolutionary game model.,1,False,Theoretical ESS analysis instead of controlled empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical or lab implementation.,1,False,"Section 3 emphasizes analytical derivations of ESS; simulations are mentioned only later for specific parameter checks, but core z-mixed ESS results are analytical.",0.7,True,Uses payoff matrix Eq.(1) and viability stability condition Eq.(5) with error terms to derive ESS conditions for each strategy.,1,5,"Same z-mixed framework as restricted model; general groups of size z+1, with mapping to public goods game. Representative z=4 example corresponds to 5-player groups.",0.4,1,Single two-stage interaction per generation; not repeated with same partners in game-theoretic sense.,0.6,1,Players choose C or D; no partial contribution allowed.,1,N/A,No explicit endowment-allocation decision; cooperation incurs cost c directly.,1,N/R,"MPCR is not explicitly defined; b is benefit to each partner, but no explicit group-multiplier divided by group size.",0,N/A,No human communication; abstract model.,1,0,No information display or user interface; only payoffs affecting viability.,1,N/A,No notion of identity disclosure; punishment is anonymous in model terms.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,0,No interface or explicit display of horizon.,1,1,Second stage includes options to punish cooperators and/or defectors with cost α and impact β.,1,1,"Examples and parameter plots set c=1 and α=1, with β varied; α is cost paid per punishment act.",0.8,1,"Punishment inflicts loss β per act; in many derivations α=1 so loss per unit cost equals β, but β is variable, so no single numeric punishmentTech can be fixed.",0,0,Only costly punishment is modeled; no reward or positive sanctioning mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoffs expressed via b, c, α, β; no discrete endowment is specified.",0,N/R,"Outcomes reported as which strategies are ESS (DN vs DS), not as average contribution proportions.",0,N/R,No numeric contribution levels reported.,0,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency as ratio of realized to maximal group payoff.,0,N/R,"Analysis focuses on relative fitness and ESS conditions, not aggregate payoffs.",0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical ESS analysis in a stylized evolutionary model.,1,"Two-stage binary cooperation/punishment game in z-mixed population with full 8-strategy set: DN, DP, DA, DS, CN, CP, CA, CS. Cooperation costs c and gives benefit b to each group mate; punishment costs α to punisher and inflicts β on target. Score-dependent viability: each generation a random individual updates with death probability d(f)=γ exp(-θ f) and imitates one of z partners. Errors in executing cooperation and punishment occur with small probability ε. Anti-social punishment is possible: A and S punish cooperators (S punishes both types). Main analytical result: if β<zα, DN is ESS; if β>zα, DS is ESS; CP is never ESS when anti-social punishment is allowed.","Summarized from Sections 2, 3.2 and Appendix A/B plus Figure 1B caption describing ESS regions with full strategy set.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.06.010.md,Lattice model without anti-social punishment – DN-dominated initial condition,"Figure 2A describes lattice simulations with restricted strategy set (DN, DP, CN, CP) and initial frequencies DN=0.79, DP=0.07, CN=0.07, CP=0.07.",1,"Initial strategy composition biased toward defectors (DN=0.79, DP=0.07, CN=0.07, CP=0.07) on a 50×50 lattice; punishment parameter β varied over grid; b varied; c=1, α=1; ε=0.01; viability updating with γ=0.1, θ=0.1; anti-social punishment disabled.",Figure 2A caption lists these initial frequencies and fixed parameters; main independent variables are b and β while holding others constant.,1,False,Agent-based computer simulations; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment; numerical simulation of a model.,1,False,Run in silico rather than in a physical lab with subjects.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent based simulations on a lattice exploring evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,For the lattice case they rely on agent-based simulations rather than closed-form ESS analysis.,1,5,"Each player interacts with the four neighbors in von Neumann neighborhood, so effective group size per public goods interaction is 4 neighbors + focal = 5, matching interpretation as overlapping 5-player public goods games.",0.9,125000000,"Each simulation lasts 125,000,000 generations; treat each generation as one round of interaction-update.",0.7,1,Strategies are unconditional cooperators or defectors; no continuous contribution levels.,1,N/A,No movable endowment; individuals are simply C or D each encounter.,1,N/R,"MPCR not explicitly defined; b is benefit to each neighbor, but MPCR is not reported.",0,N/A,No agents with communication; only evolutionary update based on payoffs.,1,0,No user interface or feedback; only local payoffs matter in the algorithm.,1,N/A,No explicit representation of identity or information structure; just local interactions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Simulations have a fixed length but agents do not observe a horizon.,1,1,Strategies DP and CP punish defectors; second-stage punishment is included.,1,1,Simulation parameters set c=1 and α=1; α is the cost per punishment action.,0.9,N/R,β is varied across parameter grid; no single fixed loss-per-cost value can be specified for this condition.,0,0,No positive rewards modeled; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses abstract payoffs (b, c) and does not specify an endowment per round.",0,N/R,"Results summarized as which strategies prevail (DN vs CP/CN) across parameter regions, not as average cooperation rates.",0,N/R,No explicit aggregate contribution numbers reported.,0,N/R,Efficiency (group payoff relative to maximum) not calculated.,0,N/R,"Paper only reports which strategy wins and coexistence patterns, not total payoffs.",0,N/A,Simulation-only study; no participants.,1,N/A,Simulation-only study.,1,N/A,Simulation-only study.,1,N/A,Simulation-only study.,1,No human,Numerical agent-based simulations of artificial agents on a lattice.,1,"Square lattice of size 50×50 (N=2500) with von Neumann neighborhoods (4 neighbors). Strategies DN, DP, CN, CP only (no anti-social punishment). Parameters: c=1, α=1, ε=0.01, viability updating with γ=0.1 and θ=0.1. At each step, agents play cooperation stage with neighbors, then punishment stage where P-types punish defectors. Score-dependent viability updating: agents with lower payoffs more likely to be replaced by neighbors. Independent variables b and β explored on a grid; for small β, DN dominates; for large β, CP (and sometimes CN) dominate, largely independent of initial conditions.","Summarized specifically from Section 4.1 and Figure 2A caption, which sets parameter values and initial frequencies.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.06.010.md,Lattice model without anti-social punishment – CP-dominated initial condition,"Figure 2B describes the same restricted lattice model but with initial CP frequency 0.79 (DN=0.07, DP=0.07, CN=0.07, CP=0.79).",1,"Initial strategy composition biased toward cooperators (DN=0.07, DP=0.07, CN=0.07, CP=0.79) on a 50×50 lattice; punishment parameter β and benefit b varied; c=1, α=1; ε=0.01; viability updating with γ=0.1, θ=0.1; anti-social punishment disabled.",Figure 2B caption provides these initial frequencies; other parameters as in Figure 2A.,1,False,Computation-only agent-based model.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment with subjects.,1,False,"Simulation environment, not lab.",1,True,Uses agent-based lattice simulations as stated in Section 4.,1,False,"Outcomes obtained from simulations, not closed-form ESS for lattice.",1,5,Each focal agent plus its four neighbors; same as previous lattice condition.,0.9,125000000,"Each simulation run lasts 125,000,000 generations per Figure 2 caption.",0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect actions.,1,N/A,No explicit initial allocation decision over public vs private account.,1,N/R,MPCR not stated; game defined via pairwise cost-benefit.,0,N/A,"No human agents, no communication.",1,0,No display of others’ payoffs; only local payoffs for evolution.,1,N/A,Identities not part of simulation rules.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,Agents do not have knowledge of generational horizon.,1,1,Punishment stage with DP and CP punishing defectors is active.,1,1,"Simulation parameters set α=1 as in Figure 2 caption (c=1, α=1).",0.9,N/R,β varied across grid; no single numeric punishment impact per cost.,0,0,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment amount per agent.,0,N/R,"Reported outcomes indicate parameter regions where CP or DN dominate, but not quantitative cooperation fractions.",0,N/R,No numeric contributions presented.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculations reported.,0,N/R,"Simulation results summarize winner strategies, not group payoffs.",0,N/A,Simulation-only study.,1,N/A,Simulation-only study.,1,N/A,Simulation-only study.,1,N/A,Simulation-only study.,1,No human,Computational modeling without human participants.,1,"Same lattice and updating rules as Figure 2A: 50×50 square lattice, von Neumann neighborhoods, viability updating with γ=0.1, θ=0.1, ε=0.01, c=1, α=1. Restricted strategy set {DN, DP, CN, CP}. Simulations (50 runs per parameter point) track which strategy wins or whether coexistence occurs. With initial CP majority, CP typically wins for large β while DN tends to win for small β, showing robustness of cooperation in absence of anti-social punishment.",Directly from Figure 2B caption and Section 4.1 description.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.06.010.md,Lattice model with full 8-strategy set – DN-dominated initial condition,"Figure 3A: full strategy set on a regular lattice with initial population density DN=0.79, all others 0.03.",1,"Initial composition DN=0.79, DP=0.03, DA=0.03, DS=0.03, CN=0.03, CP=0.03, CA=0.03, CS=0.03 on 50×50 lattice; parameters b and β varied; c=1, α=1; ε=0.01; viability updating with γ=0.1, θ=0.1; anti-social punishment enabled.",Figure 3A caption lists these initial frequencies and parameter settings; b and β are scanned.,1,False,Agent-based simulation without human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical social experiment.,1,False,Runs on computer only.,1,True,Described as agent based simulations of evolutionary dynamics on a lattice.,1,False,"Lattice outcomes for full set are obtained via simulations, not closed-form analysis.",1,5,Each agent interacts with its four von Neumann neighbors; equivalent to overlapping 5-player public goods games as noted in Section 4.,0.9,125000000,"Each simulation run lasts 125,000,000 generations (Figure 3 caption).",0.7,1,Strategies specify unconditional cooperation or defection; no partial levels.,1,N/A,No explicit split of endowment between public and private accounts.,1,N/R,MPCR is not computed; benefits are pairwise between neighbors.,0,N/A,No communication among artificial agents.,1,0,No interface or explicit observation of others’ cumulative payoffs beyond local comparison inherent in updating rule.,1,N/A,Punishment has no identity-revelation component in the model.,1,N/A,No positive rewards present.,1,0,Agents are not modeled as observing temporal horizon.,1,1,"Strategies P, A, S in their second stage punish defectors, cooperators, or both.",1,1,"Parameters set to c=1, α=1; α is cost to punisher.",0.9,N/R,β is varied across grid; no single fixed value of loss per unit punishment cost for this condition.,0,0,"Framework only includes costly punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Agents are characterized by costs/benefits c and b, not by an explicit endowment.",0,N/R,"Outcome summarized as which strategies win over parameter regions (DN, DA, CP or non-convergence), not as cooperation fractions.",0,N/R,No numeric contribution data provided.,0,N/R,Efficiency metrics not reported for lattice simulations.,0,N/R,Group payoffs are not tabulated; only frequencies of winning strategies are shown.,0,N/A,Agent-based simulations only.,1,N/A,Agent-based simulations only.,1,N/A,Agent-based simulations only.,1,N/A,Agent-based simulations only.,1,No human,Computational evolutionary simulations on lattice.,1,"Full 8-strategy set on 50×50 square lattice with von Neumann neighborhoods. Parameters: c=1, α=1, ε=0.01, viability updating with γ=0.1, θ=0.1. Initial majority DN=0.79; all other strategies at 0.03. For each (b, β) point, 50 simulations run up to 125,000,000 generations; outcomes recorded as fractions of runs where CP (or other cooperators), DN, or DA dominate, or no convergence is reached. Results: when β is small, DN typically wins; when β is intermediate, DA often wins; when β is large, CP can win. Anti-social punishment changes parameter regions relative to restricted case.",Summarized from Figure 3A caption and Section 4.2 discussion.,1
