10.1016_j.obhdp.2015.01.003.md,Study 1 – Public Goods Game with punishment under constant threat level τ,Single evolutionary simulation design where the independent factor is the exogenous threat level τ varied across runs; treat each τ value as a condition within the same study.,0.98,"Societal threat level τ applied to base payoff (values 0, 5, 10, … up to catastrophic levels; main analyses focus on 0 ≤ τ ≤ 30)",Methods specify varying degree of societal threat via parameter τ subtracted from base-pay in increments of 5; figures report results by τ.,1,False,Authors describe an evolutionary game theoretic computational model and multi-agent computer simulations without human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; only virtual experimentation via simulations.,1,False,No lab setting; purely computational model.,1,True,They explicitly state they determine results through multi-agent computer simulations under an EGT framework.,1,False,"Although some analytical derivations are provided (Appendix A), main results are from simulations, not closed-form solutions.",0.86,4,"In each interaction, a PGG is played with a group; grid has many agents, but payoff examples and Table 2 show 4-player PGG groups (e.g., OR, ON, ON, CN: four agents).",0.86,5000,They ran simulations for 5000 timesteps and averaged over timesteps to get long-term proportions; treat timesteps as repeated rounds.,0.86,0,PGG implementation allows binary choice: Cooperate by contributing amount c or Defect by contributing nothing. No continuous contribution levels described.,1,N/A,No human subjects; endowments are not framed as an initial allocation between private/public accounts.,1,0.75,"MPCR = r / group size. r = 3 and PGG groups of size 4 (Table 2 examples), so MPCR = 3/4 = 0.75.",0.86,0,Agents do not communicate; they only observe punishment reputations algorithmically.,1,0,"No interface; agents only use local punishment reputation, no payoff or history summaries mentioned.",0.86,1,"They state punishment reputation of neighbors is known and that agents know punishment strategy of co-players, effectively revealing each co-player’s punisher type.",0.9,N/A,"No reward mechanism in the model, only punishment.",1,0,No human interface; agents have no concept of remaining rounds.,1,1,Model includes a punishment phase where agents can pay a cost λ to impose fine ρ.,1,0.5,Table 1 specifies λ = 1/2 as punishment cost to punisher.,1,1.5,Table 1 specifies ρ = 3/2 as punishment fine applied to punished agent per punishment act.,1,0,Only punishment strategies are modeled; no rewarding phase described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,"Contribution cost c = 1; in binary contribute/defect PGG, this is effectively the per-round contribution endowment at risk.",0.7,N/R,They report cooperation rate qualitatively and in figures but do not provide exact numeric values per condition in the text.,0.3,N/R,Contribution choice is binary (0 or 1) but no aggregate amounts reported numerically.,0.6,N/R,They discuss fitness and cooperation but do not report normalized efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) as explicit numbers.,0.2,N/R,Only example payoffs are shown in Table 2; no systematic group payoff outcomes per condition.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary simulations with artificial agents.,1,"Spatial evolutionary Public Goods Game with punishment on a 50×50 lattice, von Neumann neighborhoods; base-pay = 30, threat τ subtracts from base-pay; fitness f(p) = 1 − e^(−0.1·p); each timestep includes birth, base payoff, 4 game interactions with neighbors plus punishment, fitness-based reproduction, mutation (μ = 0.1), and death (d = 0.1). Contribution strategies: always Cooperate (C), always Defect (D), Opportunistic (O) that conditionally cooperates based on numbers of Respons