10.1016_j.obhdp.2015.01.003.md,Study 2 – Coordination game with punishment under constant threat level τ,Second main simulation study; uses a 2-player coordination game with analogous evolutionary dynamics and threat parameter τ varied across runs.,0.98,Societal threat level τ applied to base payoff (values varied in increments of 5; examples given at τ=10 and τ=32),Study 2 repeats approach of Study 1: threat level τ reduces base-pay and is varied; Figure 6 shows results as function of τ and Figure 7 uses τ=10 vs 32.,0.96,False,Coordination norms examined via evolutionary game theoretic computer simulations without human subjects.,1,False,Virtual experiments only; no empirical manipulation with human participants.,1,False,No laboratory environment; simulations only.,1,True,They state they ran simulation experiments for the coordination game and present long-term averages across timesteps and runs.,1,False,Outcomes are obtained numerically; no closed-form equilibrium analysis reported for coordination game.,0.86,2,They specify a two-player coordination game; each interaction involves two agents choosing A or B.,1,5000,"As in Study 1, they ran simulations for 5000 timesteps and averaged over timesteps.",0.86,0,"Players choose between two discrete coordination actions (A or B), not a continuous contribution amount.",1,N/A,"This is a coordination game with discrete actions, not a contribution game with an endowment.",1,N/A,"The concept of marginal per capita return applies to public goods contributions; here payoffs are from a 2×2 coordination payoff matrix, not a linear public good.",1,0,No communication between agents; they only respond to punishment reputations.,1,0,Agents use only partner’s punishment reputation to choose A or B; no payoff summaries mentioned.,0.86,1,They define Opportunistic agents as conditioning on whether partner is a Norm A Enforcer (E-A) or Norm B Enforcer (E-B); thus the partner’s enforcement type (punishment strategy) is known.,0.9,N/A,"No reward mechanism, only punishment.",1,0,Simulated agents do not receive round-count information.,1,1,"After each 2-player game, agents can punish, with strategies E-A, E-B, S, or N.",1,0.5,They use same λ = 1/2 and ρ = 3/2 parameters as in Study 1 (stated under Figure 6).,1,1.5,Punishment fine ρ = 3/2 per punishment act in Study 2 as in Study 1.,1,0,"Only punishment, not reward, is modeled in coordination game.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Coordination game uses fixed payoff matrix (2 for coordinated outcomes, 0 otherwise); no explicit per-round endowment or cost parameter like c reported.",0.3,N/R,"Outcome is coordination rate (% of interactions successfully coordinated), not contributions.",1,N/R,No contributions are made; actions are discrete A or B choices.,1,N/R,They report coordination rate and presence of norm-enforcing punishers; no normalized efficiency metric provided.,0.2,N/R,"They describe example payoffs (e.g., both coordinating on A or B gives 2,2) and expected payoffs in Fig. 7, but no systematic per-condition group payoff statistics.",0.3,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary game theoretic multi-agent simulations only.,1,"Two-player coordination game with payoff matrix: if both choose A → (2,2), both choose B → (2,2), mismatched choices → (0,0). Agents on 50×50 lattice play paired games with four neighbors per timestep; after each game, each agent can punish the partner. Game phase strategies: always A (Norm A Adhering), always B (Norm B Adhering), Opportunistic (O) that chooses A if partner is Norm A Enforcer (E-A), B if partner is Norm B Enforcer (E-B), and randomly otherwise. Punishment strategies: Norm A Enforcer (E-A, punishes B players), Norm B Enforcer (E-B, punishes A players), Spiteful (S, punishes any action), Non-punishing (N). Threat τ reduces base-pay (30), fitness function f(p) = 1 − e^(−0.1·p); other parameters as in Study 1 (λ=1/2, ρ=3/2, μ=0.1, d=0.1). Primary outcomes are long-term percentage of coordination and proportion of norm-enforcing punishers as functions of τ.","Summarized from Study 2 section, Fig. 5, Fig. 6, and parameter descriptions.",1
10.1088_1367-2630_14_9_093016.md,"Adaptive rewarding vs defectors (Δ–r threshold curve, α=0.4)","Condition described in section 3.3 and Figure 7: adaptive rewarding cooperators competing with defectors only; main varied parameters are Δ and r at fixed α=0.4, with outcome whether defectors go extinct.",0.93,Synergy factor r (varied); adaptive reward strength Δ (varied); reward cost ratio α=0.4 fixed; strategies present: rewarding cooperators R and defectors D only.,"Figure 7 explicitly studies minimum Δ required to eliminate defectors as a function of r at α=0.4, with competition between D and R.",0.96,False,Study is a Monte Carlo simulation on a square lattice with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; numerical simulation only.,1,True,"Authors describe Monte Carlo steps on a lattice and strategy update rules, which is a numerical agent-based simulation.",1,False,No closed-form analytical solution for outcomes; results shown via numerical phase diagrams.,0.96,40000,"Players occupy an L×L square lattice with L between 200 and 2000. A typical case is not fixed, but a representative size is L=200 (lower bound), yielding 200×200=40000 players; exact size for Figure 7 not specified.",0.3,1000000,They state that equilibration required up to 10^6 full rounds to avoid finite-size effects. Use this upper bound as representative number of Monte Carlo steps.,0.4,1,Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount 1 (all-or-nothing decision between contributing 1 or 0).,1,N/A,No human players and no notion of initial endowment split between public/private accounts in the simulation; contributions are rule-based.,1,0.2,"Group size G=5, each cooperator contributes 1, sum is multiplied by r and divided equally. MPCR is r/G. A representative mid-range r from Figure 7 is about 1.0 (not explicitly given per point), but the definition MPCR=r/5 holds. Since r is varied, a single numeric MPCR cannot be fixed; we use value at r=1 as illustrative, but this is speculative.",0.1,N/A,No human interaction; no communication channel.,1,N/A,Simulation agents have no interface; no feedback summaries like human experiments.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition and no human identities.,1,N/A,No human subjects and no identities; reward interactions are rule-based and anonymous.,1,N/A,Agents are not informed; time steps are part of simulation code only.,1,0,Only defectors and rewarding cooperators are mentioned; no punishing strategy in this comparison.,1,N/A,Punishment not present in this condition.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism is absent.,1,1,Rewarding cooperators R exist and allocate rewards adaptively.,1,N/R,"Reward cost per unit is parameterized by α and Δ in payoff equations, but a single per-unit cost in tokens is not directly mapped as a constant; it depends on π and group composition.",0.3,N/R,Reward magnitude per unit spent is embedded in Δ and α (ratio of cost to reward) but not given as a single constant token-per-cost value.,0.3,N/R,"Each cooperator contributes 1, but no explicit endowment per round or maximum token budget is stated.",0.4,N/R,"They report stationary fractions of strategies and extinction thresholds, not average contribution fractions normalized by endowment.",0.1,N/R,"While each cooperator contributes 1, the paper does not summarize group-level average contributions as an outcome metric for this condition.",0.3,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency as realized vs. maximum possible group payoff is reported quantitatively.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff distributions are not tabulated; only qualitative outcomes (strategy dominance) and phase boundaries are shown.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice without human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a square lattice (degree k=4, group size G=5), with strategies D and rewarding cooperators R. Cooperators (here R) contribute 1; contributions in each group are multiplied by synergy factor r and divided equally. Rewarding cooperators carry an adaptive activity πx that increases by 1 whenever a neighboring defector successfully spreads and decreases by 1 every second round (πx≥0). Each rewarding cooperator gives reward (πiΔ/k) to each cooperator in the group, bearing a cost (πiαΔ/k) per recipient; self-rewarding is excluded. Strategy updates follow pairwise comparison with Fermi rule at noise K=0.5 and random sequential Monte Carlo updating.",Summarizes the formal game description and update rules in section 2 as they apply to this D vs. R adaptive rewarding condition.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_14_9_093016.md,"Adaptive punishment vs defectors (Δ–r threshold curve, α=0.4)","Condition in section 3.3 and Figure 7: comparison where punishing cooperators replace rewarding cooperators, again competing only with defectors; threshold curve for extinction of defectors vs r at fixed α=0.4.",0.93,Synergy factor r (varied); adaptive punishment strength Δ (varied); punishment cost ratio α=0.4 fixed; strategies present: punishing cooperators and defectors D only.,"Text for Figure 7 states that they repeat the threshold calculation replacing rewarding cooperators with punishing cooperators, using same α and varying r and Δ.",0.96,False,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulation with no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; computational model only.,1,False,No physical lab or participants involved.,1,True,They refer to earlier adaptive punishment model [29] with similar lattice-based Monte Carlo implementation.,1,False,Outcomes derived numerically rather than analytically.,0.96,40000,"Same simulation framework as adaptive rewarding: L between 200 and 2000, so representative size 200×200=40000 agents used as placeholder; exact L for this figure not given.",0.3,1000000,They note up to 10^6 Monte Carlo steps used for equilibration in general; assume similar scale here.,0.4,1,"Public goods structure is unchanged: each cooperator contributes 1, defectors 0.",1,N/A,No human endowments; contributions are rule-based.,1,0.2,MPCR is r/G with G=5; same caveat as in previous condition that r is varied so a single numeric MPCR is not unique; value at r=1 used illustratively.,0.1,N/A,No human communication in the simulation.,1,N/A,No user interface; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No identities as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No rewarding in this condition; only punishment.,1,N/A,Agents are not informed about horizon.,1,1,Punishing cooperators are the cooperative strategy in this comparison.,1,N/R,Punishment cost is parameterized by α and Δ in the punishment model; per-unit cost not explicitly given as a single token value.,0.3,N/R,Magnitude of fine per unit cost is embedded in Δ and α; no single fixed mapping reported.,0.3,0,Rewards are replaced by punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,Rewards are disabled.,1,N/R,Endowment per player per round not explicitly specified.,0.4,N/R,They focus on defector extinction thresholds rather than average contributions.,0.1,N/R,No explicit reporting of aggregate contributions.,0.3,N/R,They do not compute overall efficiency ratios for this condition.,0.1,N/R,No quantitative payoff outcomes presented; only qualitative dominance/extinction.,0.1,N/A,Simulation without humans.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Lattice-based agent simulation; no empirical setting.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a square lattice with defectors and adaptive punishing cooperators (as defined in Perc & Szolnoki 2012, NJP 14 043013). Group size G=5, each cooperator contributes 1, payoffs from public good depend on synergy factor r. Punishing cooperators adapt their punishment activity depending on successful spread of defectors, with a constant drift towards inactivity; cost and fine magnitudes are determined by Δ and α. Strategy updates follow the same Monte Carlo Fermi imitation rule with K=0.5.",Summarizes adaptive punishment model referenced in text as directly comparable to adaptive rewarding in this paper.,0.9
