10.1257_aer.100.1.420.md,"1:1 – Individuals vs Individuals, 20 rounds, no punishment",Paper defines baseline treatment '1:1' as two individual players contesting over 20 rounds without punishment.,1,"Contest party composition and punishment: individual vs individual (1:1), no punishment, 20-round horizon","Treatment varies number of individuals per party and presence of punishment; here both parties are single individuals, no punishment, 20 rounds.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with student participants.,1,True,Controlled lab contest game with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted at University of Nottingham in a lab with computer terminals.,1,False,All data from human subjects; no agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,Paper contains theory but this observation refers to an empirical treatment.,1,2,Treatment 1:1 is a contest between two individual players (2 parties of size 1).,1,20,"Design section: first four treatments, including 1:1, have 20 rounds.",1,1,"Each participant could buy any integer amount of contest tokens between 0 and 1,000, implying a continuous (not binary) contribution choice.",1,0,"Subjects receive an endowment of 1,000 points each round, all initially in private account; they decide how many to spend on tokens.",1,N/R,"This is a Tullock contest, not a linear public good; no marginal per capita return is defined or computable in standard sense.",1,0,Information transmitted only through computer network; any other form of communication was prohibited.,1,1,"After decisions, each participant was informed about total amount invested by rival party and (for teams) each member’s investment; for 1:1, they are reminded of own investment and see rival total.",0.9,0,No punishment exists in this treatment.,1,0,Prize allocation (win/lose) is shown but not linked to personal identities; IDs not revealed across parties.,0.8,1,"Time horizon (20 rounds) was common knowledge; typical lab software shows round number, though explicit display is not detailed.",0.6,0,Design states 1:1 is 'without punishment'.,1,N/A,No punishment in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment in this treatment.,1,1,"Winning party members receive a 1,000 point prize each round.",1,0,Prize is allocated by experimenter; participants do not pay to allocate rewards.,1,1000,"Winning individual receives 1,000 points per round.",1,1000,"At the beginning of each round each participant received an endowment of 1,000 points.",1,N/R,"Paper reports average contest expenditures in points, not normalized by endowment per treatment in a way suitable for a single numeric DV.",0.3,448,"In last five rounds, single players in 1:1 invest on average 448 points.",0.9,N/R,"They report rent dissipation relative to equilibrium, not efficiency as fraction of maximum payoff in a way that can be converted cleanly for this treatment alone.",0.3,N/R,"Only ranges and averages of monetary earnings across treatments are reported, not per-treatment group payoff levels.",0.2,United Kingdom,Experiment conducted at University of Nottingham (UK) with its subject pool.,0.9,University undergraduates; exact ages N/R,Subjects recruited from university-wide undergraduate pool; no age statistics reported.,0.7,N/R,No information on gender composition is provided.,1,Undergraduate students,Subject pool comprised undergraduates.,1,On site,Participants seated at visually separated computer terminals in the lab.,1,"Two-party Tullock contest. Each round: each individual chooses 0–1000 contest tokens at cost 1 point each from 1000-point endowment; lottery probability equals own tokens divided by total; winner gains 1000-point prize; no punishment, fixed matching over 20 rounds.",Summarizes the described round structure and rules for this treatment.,1
