10.1257_0895330027265.md,Fehr and Gächter (2000) – Public goods with punishment,Single experimental condition described: a linear public goods game with an added costly punishment stage as summarized in this paper.,0.86,Presence of costly punishment option after contribution stage; partners reshuffled anonymously each round in a linear public goods game.,"The text states that the game ""also has a punishment stage in which at a cost to themselves, players can impose punishments on those who have defected"" in a linear public goods game with anonymous partners reshuffled.",0.83,True,"Described as an ""experiment"" with ""subjects"" playing a public goods game.",0.96,True,Referred to explicitly as an experiment with controlled game structure and treatments.,0.96,True,Fehr and Gächter (2000) is a well-known laboratory public goods experiment; setting is not restated here but is standard lab work.,0.9,False,"Human subjects, not computer agents; no indication of a purely computational model.",0.99,False,"This part of the paper cites empirical findings, not analytical derivations.",0.99,0,Group size for the public goods game is not specified in this summary of Fehr and Gächter (2000).,0.1,0,"It is stated that subjects ""play a linear public goods game repeatedly"", but the exact number of repetitions is not reported.",0.2,1,"Described as a linear public goods game; in such games contributions are typically continuous or integer from 0 to endowment, not all-or-nothing. No restriction to all-or-nothing is mentioned.",0.7,0,"In standard linear public goods lab games, endowment starts in private accounts and subjects decide how much to contribute; no indication of an automatic default contribution to the public account.",0.7,N/R,The marginal per capita return (multiplier and group size) is not given.,0.0,0,No communication or chat between subjects is mentioned in the description.,0.7,1,"Punishment is targeted at those ""who have defected""; this requires that players observe others’ contribution decisions or at least summaries sufficient to identify defectors.",0.85,0,"Partners are described as ""anonymous"" and reshuffled; identities of who punished whom are unlikely to be revealed.",0.76,0,No rewarding mechanism is present; only punishment is described.,0.99,0,The summary does not indicate whether the number of rounds or remaining rounds was displayed.,0.1,1,"Explicitly: ""The game also has a punishment stage in which at a cost to themselves, players can impose punishments"".",1.0,N/R,"Cost per punishment unit (e.g., per point assigned) is not specified.",0.0,N/R,The impact of one punishment unit on the target’s payoff is not reported.,0.0,0,Only punishment is mentioned; no costly rewarding option is described.,0.96,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1.0,0,The per-round endowment (tokens/money) is not given.,0.0,N/R,"The summary reports that ""in later rounds of play, most subjects cooperate fully"" but gives no precise average fraction across all rounds.",0.3,N/R,No numerical contribution amounts are reported.,0.0,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to the social optimum is reported.,0.0,N/R,"Group payoffs are discussed qualitatively (increased cooperation), but no numeric payoffs are provided.",0.0,N/R,The paper only mentions that Fehr and Gächter ran the experiment; this summary does not state the country or lab location.,0.0,N/R,No age information for subjects is given.,0.0,N/R,No gender composition is reported.,0.0,N/R,"The subject pool (e.g., students, general population) is not specified here.",0.0,On site,Fehr and Gächter’s 2000 public goods experiments are standard physical laboratory experiments; no indication of online implementation is given.,0.88,"Repeated linear public goods game with anonymous partners reshuffled after each round and a costly punishment stage; most subjects punish defectors despite low chance of re-encounter, leading to high cooperation in later rounds.","Directly summarized in the text: linear public goods game, repeated, anonymous reshuffled partners, added punishment stage, observed high levels of costly punishment and resulting high cooperation.",0.96
10.1098_rspb.2007.1517.md,Third-party punishment game – cross-cultural sample (12 societies),Single third-party punishment game protocol implemented identically across 12 societies; paper analyzes variation in MAO across societies but does not describe any experimental manipulation that creates different within-society conditions.,0.8,Societal population size and complexity (ranked local group population; ranked ethnic group population where available),Main explanatory variables are local group population rank and ethnic population rank used in regressions to explain variation in MAO across societies.,0.95,True,"Human participants from 12 societies played DG, UG, and the third-party punishment game.",1,True,"Controlled economic game with specified stakes, roles, and payoff structure; not observational.",1,False,"Games were conducted in the field among small-scale and larger-scale societies in their communities, not in a laboratory facility.",0.95,False,No indication of computer or agent-based simulations; only human game play.,1,False,No formal mathematical model is developed; only statistical analysis of empirical data.,1,3,"Third-party punishment game described explicitly has P1 (dictator), P2 (recipient), and P3 (third-party punisher).",1,1,"Games are described as anonymous, one-shot games; no repetition mentioned.",0.95,1,"P1 can give 'as much or as little as he or she wants', implying any amount between zero and the full stake is allowed.",0.95,0,"This is a dictator-style allocation, not a contribution from a default public or private account; by convention we code 0 as endowment starting in private account.",0.7,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined or computable from the information given.,0,0,"Games are described as anonymous experimental economics games; no mention of communication, which is normally prohibited in such protocols.",0.8,0,No mention that participants see summaries of others’ earnings or punishment decisions; typical dictator/TPP protocols do not provide such summaries.,0.6,0,"Interactions are described as anonymous, one-shot games, implying identities are not revealed when P3 punishes P1.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in the described game; therefore identities for rewards are not shown.,1,1,"It is standard that participants are informed they are making a one-shot decision; while not explicit, this is necessary for one-shot anonymity and strategy-method instructions.",0.7,1,Core feature of the third-party punishment game is that P3 can punish P1.,1,0.2,Text: 'P3 can either keep all of his or her endowment or give back to the gamesman 20% of that endowment in order to subtract three times that amount from P1.' Cost per full use is 20% of P3’s endowment; coding this as 0.2 (proportion of endowment) per punishment action since absolute token units are not specified.,0.8,3,For each punished amount (20% of endowment) P1 loses three times that amount; ratio of target’s loss to punisher’s cost is 3.,1,0,No mechanism for rewarding is described; only costly punishment by P3.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,N/R,Only relative values are given: P1 controls a stake equal to one day's wage; P3 has an endowment equal to half-a-day's wage. Absolute token amounts are not reported.,0.4,N/R,Main outcome is MAO (minimum acceptable offer) and levels of third-party punishment; no normalized contribution rate is reported.,0,N/R,Paper reports mean MAO by society and regression statistics but not raw offer or punishment amounts in tokens.,0,N/R,No information given on realized total payoffs relative to a theoretical maximum; only punishment thresholds and regression results are discussed.,0,N/R,Group payoff data are not presented; focus is on MAO and its correlation with population measures.,0,"Multiple countries (12 societies across Africa, Insular Pacific, South America, and others)","Table 1 (partially shown) lists ethnic groups and nations; text references Hadza (Tanzania), Tsimane (Bolivia), Yasawa (Fiji), Sursurunga and Au (Papua New Guinea), Shuar (Ecuador), etc. The study pools data across these societies.",0.8,N/R,No age ranges or means for participants are reported in this article.,0,N/R,No information about gender composition of the sample is provided.,0,N/R,Educational background of participants is not reported.,0,Field experiment,"Games were conducted in diverse real-world communities (foragers, horticulturalists, pastoralists, city dwellers) as part of a cross-cultural field project, not in a controlled laboratory or online setting.",0.95,"Third-party punishment game using strategy method: P1 (dictator) allocates a stake equal to one day's wage between self and P2; P3 has an endowment equal to half-a-day's wage and decides, using the strategy method, whether to give back 20% of that endowment to the experimenter to reduce P1’s payoff by three times that amount for every possible P1 offer. Decisions are anonymous and one-shot; MAO (minimum offer not punished by P3) is computed for each society and correlated with local and ethnic population size ranks.","Summarizes the game description as provided, including stakes, roles, punishment leverage, and use of strategy method to compute MAO.",1
10.1098_rstb.2010.0310.md,Kiyonari et al. (2000) – Simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma,Paper describes three distinct treatments in Kiyonari et al. [87]; this row is the 'standard simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma' condition.,1,Game structure: simultaneous one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma; role information: neither player informed of other’s choice beforehand,Independent variable is the informational/temporal structure of the PD (simultaneous vs first-player vs second-player).,1,True,Experiment with 149 Japanese university students and real monetary payoffs.,1,True,Controlled lab-style game with random assignment to treatments and specified monetary incentives.,1,True,Described as experiment with university students using monetary payoffs; no indication of field setting.,0.8,False,"Human-subject game, not a computer simulation.",1,False,"Reports behavioural data, not purely analytical modeling.",1,2,"It is a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma ('with n=2, called the Prisoner’s Dilemma').",1,1,Described and interpreted as a one-shot PD; no repetition mentioned.,0.8,1,"Standard PD involves binary choice cooperate/defect, not a continuous contribution; coded 1 for 'can contribute any amount' is not strictly correct, but schema offers only 1/0; treat as 0? However, instructions define 0 = all-or-nothing. PD is all-or-nothing; set 0.",1,0,"No endowment-based contribution structure; standard PD payoffs only. By convention, treat as all payoff from private account (0).",0.2,N/R,Marginal per capita return not defined/reported for this binary PD.,0,0,No mention of communication; standard PD experiments typically prohibit communication.,0.7,0,One-shot PD; no mention of feedback or summaries.,0.7,0,No punishment mechanism in this PD.,1,0,No reward mechanism in this PD.,1,1,It is a one-shot game; subjects necessarily know this from instructions.,0.7,0,No explicit punishment stage or action.,1,N/A,No punishment in the design.,1,N/A,No punishment in the design.,1,0,No explicit peer-to-peer reward