10.1098_rstb.2010.0310.md,"Gneezy (2005) – Game 1 (A:(5,6) vs B:(6,5))","First of three payoff structures in Gneezy’s deception experiment; here A:(5,6), B:(6,5).",1,"Payoff structure: Option A = (5,6), Option B = (6,5); Lie gain to Bob = 1; Cost to Alice from Bob lying = 1",Independent variable across games is payoff structure determining costs/benefits of lying.,1,True,450 undergraduate participants paired off in experimental games.,1,True,Controlled lab game with payoffs and messages.,1,True,"Undergraduate subjects, anonymous pairings, payoff points: standard lab environment.",0.9,False,Human-subject experiment.,1,False,"Reports experimental evidence, not only theory.",1,2,Bob (player 1) and Alice (player 2).,1,1,"Described as one-shot interactions; each pair plays each game, but each game itself is one-shot.",0.8,0,"Not a contribution game; Bob chooses a message, Alice chooses option A or B.",0.9,0,No endowment-to-public-account structure.,0.9,N/R,Not a public-good environment.,0,0,Only fixed-format messages allowed; no free chat.,0.9,0,No information about other pairs’ outcomes provided.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Subjects know there are three games, but each game is essentially one-shot; this row is for one game.",0.7,0,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No separate reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,0,No initial divisible endowment; payoffs are determined by choices. Setting 0 to indicate none.,0.8,N/R,"Not a contribution game; main DV is lying frequency (36% lied), which doesn’t map cleanly to contributionRate.",0,N/R,No contributions defined.,0,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0,N/R,Average joint payoffs not reported.,0,N/R,Described only as undergraduate participants; country not stated here.,0.3,N/R,No age data.,0,N/R,No gender data.,0,University undergraduates,Explicitly '450 undergraduate participants'.,1,On site,Standard lab/classroom experiment.,0.9,"Bob