10.1098_rstb.2010.0310.md,"Gneezy (2005) – Game 2 (A:(5,15) vs B:(6,5))","Second payoff structure: A:(5,15) vs B:(6,5), small gain to Bob, large loss to Alice.",1,"Payoff structure: Option A = (5,15), Option B = (6,5); Lie gain to Bob = 1; Cost to Alice from Bob lying = 10",Differs from Game 1 and 3 in cost to Alice while gain to Bob is 1.,1,True,Same Gneezy deception experiment.,1,True,Controlled variation in payoff matrix.,1,True,Undergraduate lab setting.,0.9,False,Human participants.,1,False,Reports experimental findings.,1,2,Bob and Alice.,1,1,Each game is one-shot.,0.8,0,Not a contribution decision.,0.9,0,No public-good structure.,0.9,N/R,Not a public good.,0,0,Only structured advice messages allowed.,0.9,0,No information about other pairs.,0.9,0,No punishment.,1,0,No reward.,1,1,One-shot game within the three-game sequence.,0.7,0,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,0,No divisible endowment; payoffs fixed by choice.,0.8,N/R,Main DV is lying (17% lied) rather than contributions.,0,N/R,Not a contribution game.,0,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0,N/R,Average joint payoffs not given.,0,N/R,Country not provided.,0.3,N/R,Not provided.,0,N/R,Not provided.,0,University undergraduates,Same 450 undergraduate sample.,1,On site,Lab experiment.,0.9,"Bob sees A:(5,15), B:(6,5); lying (recommending B) yields Bob +1, Alice −10 if believed; only 17% of Bobs lied in this game.",Includes design and key DV (lie frequency).,1
