10.1098_rspb.2010.0065.md,Infinite-population replicator dynamics with punishment only,"Condition where only punishment (P) and no reward (R) is allowed in the incentive stage, analysed via replicator dynamics in infinite populations (figure 1, top row; figure 3, top-left panel).",0.94,"Information level μ with only punishment available (no reward option). μ varied across [0,1]; main thresholds μ > γ/(b+γ), μ > (c+γ)/(c+γ+b), and μ > 1/2; parameters typically b=4, c=1, β=2, γ=2.","Paper explicitly examines how outcomes under punishment-only depend on μ and derives threshold conditions; figures 1 and 3 caption give the parameter set b=4, c=1, β=γ=2.",0.9,False,Pure evolutionary game-theoretic analysis; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment but a theoretical model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; mathematical analysis and simulations only.,1,True,"Uses numerical simulations of replicator equations (e.g., figure 3 left column) in addition to analytical results, with no humans involved.",0.9,True,Derives conditions and classifications via replicator dynamics and payoff matrices analytically (section 2a).,1,2,"Model is explicitly a two-person, two-stage game with donor and recipient per round.",1,1,"Game is modelled as a single two-stage encounter per pairing; repetition is at the population level via evolutionary dynamics, not as finite repeated rounds between the same players.",0.7,1,Players choose between discrete actions C or D; there is no continuous contribution level. This is closer to an all-or-nothing contribution choice.,0.9,N/A,Not a public goods contribution-from-endowment framework but a donor-helping decision with fixed costs and benefits and no explicit endowment.,1,N/R,No group fund or linear multiplier; only pairwise cost-benefit parameters b and c are specified.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modelled.,1,0,Model assumes reputation summarized as a probability μ of knowing co-player’s incentive behaviour; it does not model explicit feedback screens or earnings summaries.,0.7,0,"Model tracks strategy frequencies, not individual identities; punishment is contingent on observed type, not personal identity revelation.",0.7,N/A,Reward is not available in this punishment-only condition.,1,0,No participants and no explicit user interface for rounds.,1,1,Condition explicitly retains P as an option while excluding R.,1,2,In examples they set β=γ=2; γ is the cost to the punishing recipient.,0.9,2,Punishment makes the donor part with amount β; with β=2 this is the per-punishment loss to target.,0.9,0,Described scenario explicitly excludes the R alternative to focus on punishment only (section 2a).,1,N/A,Reward is disabled in this condition.,1,N/A,Reward is disabled in this condition.,1,N/R,Model specifies costs and benefits per interaction but does not define a per-round endowment.,1,N/R,"They discuss cooperation levels qualitatively (e.g., dominance of cooperative strategies) but do not report normalized contribution fractions.",0.7,N/R,"Help is binary (C or D) with cost c, but no aggregate contribution amounts are reported as outcome variables.",0.9,N/R,Paper compares payoffs qualitatively; no explicit normalized efficiency metric is reported.,0.7,N/R,Provides payoff expressions per strategy pair but no single reported aggregate outcome for this condition.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical and simulation-based without human subjects.,1,"Two-person, two-stage donor–recipient game; first stage: donor chooses C (pay cost c, recipient gets benefit b) or D; second stage: recipient chooses N (none) or P (punish C/D according to their strategy; in punishment-only setting, reward R is disallowed). Strategies are combinations of donor types (AllC, OC, OD, AllD) and recipient types (N, P) with incomplete information about incentives captured by μ.",Summarizes the formal game described in section 2 under the constraint that only punishment is available.,0.96
10.1098_rspb.2010.0065.md,Infinite-population replicator dynamics with punishment and reward,"Condition where both punishment (P) and reward (R) options are available in the incentive stage, analysed via replicator dynamics in infinite populations (figure 1, lower panels; figure 3, bottom-left).",0.95,"Information level μ when both punishment and reward are available. μ varied across [0,1]; key thresholds μ > γ/(2b), μ > γ/(b+γ), μ > γ/b, and μ > 1/2; baseline parameters b=4, c=1, β=2, γ=2.",Section 2a and figures 1–3 discuss how access to R alters thresholds in μ; figure 3 caption states μ threshold shifts to μ>γ/2b when rewards are available.,0.9,False,No human subjects; theoretical evolutionary model.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment but a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No physical lab setting; only mathematical/simulation work.,1,True,Includes numerical simulations of replicator dynamics with multiple strategies (as in figure 3 bottom-left).,0.9,True,Provides analytical classification of dynamics and thresholds in μ using replicator equations and payoff matrix.,1,2,Same two-person donor–recipient game structure.,1,1,Single encounter per pair; repetition is at population level.,0.7,1,Binary choice C vs D for donor; no continuous contribution.,0.9,N/A,No endowment-based contribution model.,1,N/R,No linear public-goods MPCR specified.,1,N/A,No humans; no communication channel.,1,0,"Information is abstracted as parameter μ, not as interface feedback.",0.7,0,"Strategies react to known incentive behaviour, not to personal identifiers.",0.7,0,Model specifies that players may know whether a co-player provides incentives; does not model identity revelation per se.,0.7,0,No participant interface with rounds.,1,1,P is one of the available recipient moves; focus is on combined reward and punishment.,1,2,"As in the base model, γ is punishment cost and γ=2 in the parameter example.",0.9,2,Punishment imposes a loss β on the donor; β=2 in main parameterization.,0.9,1,R and I (both reward and punish) are explicitly modeled as recipient actions.,1,2,Rewarding costs γ to the recipient; γ=2 in main parameter set.,0.9,2,Reward gives donor benefit β; β=2 in examples.,0.9,N/R,No fixed monetary endowment per round defined.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are described via dominance of cooperative strategies (e.g., [OC,P]) and qualitative cooperation, not explicit normalized rates.",0.7,N/R,Help is binary; no aggregate contribution statistics reported.,0.9,N/R,"They discuss that [OC,R] populations can have higher payoff than [OC,P] etc., but no explicit 0–1 efficiency measure is reported.",0.7,N/R,Provide payoff formulas for strategies but not a single reported group payoff summary for this condition.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Model is purely theoretical/simulation with no participants.,1,"Two-person, two-stage game. Donor chooses C or D with cost c and benefit b. Recipient may choose N (do nothing), P (punish defection), R (reward cooperation), or I (both punish D and reward C). Opportunistic donor types OC and OD condition on incentive availability given imperfect reputation information (probability μ of knowing co-player’s incentive behavior). Evolutionary dynamics via replicator equation over 16 strategies in infinite populations.",Summarizes the full incentive-augmented game with both P and R used in this condition.,0.97
10.1098_rspb.2010.0065.md,Finite-population Moran process with punishment only,"Finite-population evolutionary condition using Moran-like death-birth process with exploration (mutation), restricted to punishment and no reward, corresponding to figure 3, top-right and text in section 2b for punishment-only structure.",0.9,Information level μ in a finite population (size M=100) with only punishment and no reward. μ varied; key that high μ needed for punishment-enforced cooperation to dominate.,Figure 3 top-right shows stationary distribution vs μ for punishment-only; text discusses dependence on μ.,0.85,False,No human subjects; stochastic evolutionary simulation.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment; it is a computational Markov process analysis.,1,False,No physical lab or field environment.,1,True,Implements a Moran-like death-birth process with mutation on a finite population (M=100) numerically; figure 3 right column and figure 4 are simulation outputs.,0.98,True,"Section 2b derives results for the adiabatic limit and fixation probabilities analytically, though stationary distributions are also simulated.",0.9,2,"Underlying interaction game is the same two-person donor–recipient game; population size M=100 refers to number of individuals, interacting pairwise.",0.95,1,Each update involves random pairwise interaction; no fixed finite number of repeated rounds between specific players is specified.,0.6,1,Binary C/D choice for donors.,0.9,N/A,No endowment contribution structure with proportions.,1,N/R,MPCR concept not applicable to this pairwise helping model.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication.,1,0,Modelled as Markov process without UI; no earnings summaries.,0.8,0,"Punishment contingent on strategies and reputation, not on individualized identities.",0.7,N/A,Reward option not allowed in punishment-only finite-population scenario reflected in top-right of figure 3.,0.95,0,No explicit finite horizon shown to agents; infinite-time Markov chain analysis.,0.9,1,Strategies include P in this scenario; focus is on punishment-enforced cooperation.,1,2,"Simulation parameter values given: b=4, c=1, β=γ=2; γ is cost of punishing.",0.9,2,Each punishment event makes donor part with β=2.,0.9,0,This finite-population condition corresponds to punishment only row in figure 3; rewards are not available.,1,N/A,Reward not available.,1,N/A,Reward not available.,1,N/R,No per-round endowment defined.,1,N/R,"Figure 3 right column shows stationary probabilities of strategies, not explicit normalized contributions.",0.7,N/R,Binary help; no numeric contribution totals reported.,0.9,N/R,"They compare dominance of strategies and invasions, not explicit efficiency metrics.",0.7,N/R,"No average payoffs of stationary distribution are numerically reported, only qualitative dominance.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Finite-population evolutionary simulation only.,1,"Finite population of size M=100, strategies updated via Moran-like death-birth process with small exploration rate (mutation). Underlying interaction is same two-person, two-stage donor–recipient game; recipient actions restricted to N or P (no R). Fitness is (1−s)B + sP with selection strength s=1/10.",Summarizes structure of finite-population punishment-only simulations as described in section 2b and figure 3 top-right.,0.95
10.1098_rspb.2010.0065.md,Finite-population Moran process with punishment and reward,"Finite-population evolutionary condition using Moran-like process where both punishment and reward are available, corresponding to figure 3 bottom-right and figure 4.",0.95,"Information level μ in a finite population (size M=100) with both punishment and reward available. μ varied; key effect that rewards lower the μ threshold at which punishment-enforced cooperation ([OC,P]) dominates and reduce waiting time to leave asocial states.",Figure 3 bottom-right and discussion in section 2b describe how stationary distributions and invasion times depend on μ when rewards are allowed.,0.9,False,"This is a stochastic evolutionary simulation, not a human-participant study.",1,False,No behavioral experiment; algorithmic simulations of a Markov chain.,1,False,Not conducted in lab or field.,1,True,Authors simulate a Moran process with mutation to obtain stationary distributions (figure 3 bottom-right) and waiting times (figure 4).,1,True,They also analyze fixation probabilities and adiabatic limit analytically for small mutation rates.,0.9,2,Interactions remain pairwise donor–recipient games; population size M=100 overall.,0.95,1,Game is not finite-horizon repeated between fixed pairs; updates involve new random pairings each time.,0.6,1,Donor move is C or D only.,0.9,N/A,No contribution-from-endowment structure.,1,N/R,Public-good MPCR not defined in this pairwise model.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,0,Simulation does not include explicit feedback summaries; only strategy frequencies tracked.,0.8,0,"Punishment decisions based on strategies and reputational information, not personal identifiers.",0.7,0,"Reward decisions similarly depend on strategy and information parameter μ, not on explicit identity visibility.",0.7,0,No explicit round count interface; Markov chain runs indefinitely.,0.9,1,"Recipient strategies include P (and I, which includes punishment).",1,2,"Same parameters used as elsewhere in simulations: b=4, c=1, β=γ=2; γ is punisher’s cost.",0.9,2,A punishment event imposes loss β=2 on donor.,0.9,1,"Rewarding R is available and plays a catalytic role in emergence of [OC,P].",1,2,Rewarding costs γ to recipient; γ=2 under given parameterization.,0.9,2,Reward delivers benefit β=2 to cooperative donor.,0.9,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment specified.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as stationary probabilities of strategies like [OC,P], [OD,N], etc., and as waiting times, not explicit contribution fractions.",0.7,N/R,Binary help; no numeric amounts are summarized.,0.9,N/R,"Paper highlights that [OC,P] dominates in stationary distribution and that rewards reduce time to leave asocial state, but does not provide explicit efficiency ratios.",0.7,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes for this finite-population condition are listed; focus is on frequencies and invasion times.,0.7,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,No human,Computational evolutionary simulations only.,1,"Finite population of size M=100; strategies updated by Moran-like death-birth rule with small exploration rate. Fitness is (1−s)B + sP with selection strength s=1/10. Underlying interaction game is the two-person, two-stage helping game with donor strategies (AllC, OC, OD, AllD) and recipient strategies (N, P, R, I), with incomplete information μ about whether co-player uses incentives. Condition allows both reward and punishment, so R-players and P-players can emerge and interact; rewards act as a catalyst enabling transition from asocial [OD,N] to prosocial [OC,P].","Synthesizes the finite-population setup with both incentive types as explained in section 2b, figure 3 bottom-right, and figure 4.",0.97
