10.1073_pnas.1120867109.md,"Modified payoffs – cliques – r=1, k=23","Modified payoffs, cliques topology, r=1, k=23 (N-1=23).",1,"Partner update parameters: r=1, k=23 (can propose or delete an edge with every other player each round); Initial network: cliques; Payoff matrix: modified PD.",Authors explicitly define k=N-1=23 treatment to remove budget constraint on link changes.,0.95,True,Human participants on MTurk.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of partner update capacity.,1,True,Online lab experiment.,0.95,False,Not simulation-only.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,0.9,24,24 participants.,1,12,Game length unchanged.,1,1,Binary C/D.,1,0,No continuous contributions.,0.3,N/R,"Still PD, no MPCR.",1,0,No mention of chat; same UI.,0.95,1,"Same information (others' strategies, own payoffs).",0.9,0,No explicit punishment option.,1,0,No explicit reward option.,1,1,Round/time information unchanged.,0.5,0,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,0,No explicit monetary reward.,1,N/A,No explicit monetary reward.,1,N/A,No explicit monetary reward.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0.2,N/R,Cooperation high and similar to k=5; not numerically summarized.,0.3,N/R,Binary choices only.,1,N/R,Payoffs reported qualitatively only.,0.3,N/R,Specific group payoffs per treatment not given.,0.2,N/R,Demographics absent.,0.2,N/R,Demographics absent.,0.1,N/R,Demographics absent.,0.1,N/R,Demographics absent.,0.1,Online,MTurk experiments.,1,"Each round includes a partner-updating turn where each player can propose or delete up to 23 links (all other players); designed to remove budget constraint on link deletions, facilitating isolation of defectors.",From description of k=N-1=23 and motivation.,1
10.1002_hbm.21298.md,Stage 1 – Prisoner’s Dilemma between Players A and B,First decision stage described as simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma Game between two officer candidates (Players A and B) during training; no group-constellation manipulation relevant for punishment yet.,1,"Partner’s simultaneous choice in Prisoner’s Dilemma (cooperate vs defect), yielding four outcome patterns: CC, CD, DC, DD","Only factor varied in this stage is each player’s decision to cooperate (transfer 20 points) or defect (keep 20 points), creating the four behavioral patterns later used as inputs to punishment.",1,True,Human officer candidates played the PDG with real monetary stakes.,1,True,Payoff structure and interaction were experimentally controlled; random assignment to platoons is also experimental.,1,True,"Conducted as a controlled behavioral experiment within a training-course setting, analogous to a lab experiment rather than a field intervention in natural interactions.",0.7,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model; study is empirical plus neuroimaging.,1,2,Stage 1 is explicitly a simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma Game between Player A and Player B.,1,1,Each PD interaction is one-shot; text emphasizes no repeated interactions and anonymity.,1,1,"Players must either keep all 20 points or pass all 20 points; no intermediate amounts allowed, but that is still an all-or-nothing binary decision as opposed to a continuous contribution level.",1,0,Each player is endowed with 20 points in a private account which they may transfer; default is entirely private.,1,N/R,MPCR is undefined for a 2-player PD framed as transfer being doubled and given to the other; no public good shared among all players.,0.3,0,No communication or chat mentioned; interactions were anonymous one-shot games.,0.9,0,No indication that players A and B saw summaries of others’ outcomes; they only know the payoff rules.,0.9,0,"Punishment only occurs in Stage 2, and that was anonymous to A and B; no identity revelation in Stage 1.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism in this stage.,1,1,"Subjects were told there were no repeated interactions, implying they knew the one-shot nature of the game.",0.8,0,Stage 1 is purely PDG between A and B with no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment in this stage.,1,N/A,No punishment in this stage.,1,0,No possibility to spend points to reward; only transfer vs keep in PDG.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,20,Each player A and B was endowed with 20 points at the start of the PDG.,1,N/R,Paper reports qualitative patterns of cooperation/defection but no overall cooperation rate.,0.2,N/R,Only binary transfer vs keep is defined; frequencies not reported.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate efficiency or payoff ratios for Stage 1 are reported.,0.1,N/R,No mean payoffs per pair reported for the PDG stage.,0.1,Switzerland,All subjects were officer candidates in the Swiss Army; training course in Switzerland.,1,"Mean 24.5 years (SD 2.2, range 20–27)",Age statistics reported for the 16 subjects in the fMRI study; same population of officer candidates used in both stages.,0.8,Male,"Sample described as 16 healthy, right-handed male subjects.",1,"Officer candidates in Swiss Army; mixed civilian educational backgrounds, no significant differences across platoons",Text notes random assignment to platoons and no significant differences in education; more detailed breakdown not provided.,0.6,Field experiment,Stage 1 implemented during real officer training course in Swiss Army with real groups and stakes.,0.9,"Two-player simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma: each player has 20 points, can keep (defect) or pass all 20 (cooperate). Passed points are doubled and given to the other. Payoffs: CC → 40/40, DD → 20/20, DC → 60/0, CD → 0/60. Interactions are anonymous and one-shot; players know some other candidates will later judge their behavior in a punishment stage.",Summarized directly from the design description in the Methods section.,1
10.1002_hbm.21298.md,"Stage 2 – Third-party punishment: DC, Outgroup constellation BC","One punishment condition where Player A (punishable) is outgroup to third-party C (BC constellation) and prior PD outcome is DC (A defects, B cooperates); this is the focal ‘outgroup’ parochial punishment case.",1,"Group constellation and PD outcome: BC (A outgroup to C; B ingroup to C), PD pattern DC (A defects, B cooperates)","Across punishment trials, independent variables were group constellation (ABC, AC, BC) and PD behavioral pattern (CC, CD, DC, DD). This row isolates BC × DC.",1,True,Human third parties (Player C) in fMRI scanner made real monetary punishment decisions.,1,True,"Payoff structures, group identities, and stimuli were experimentally controlled with random assignment to platoons.",1,True,Stage 2 occurred in an fMRI scanner environment using z-Tree to present decisions; standard lab-like setting.,1,False,No non-human simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical model; focus is empirical and neuroimaging.,1,3,"Punishment game involves Player A, Player B, and third-party Player C.",1,1,"Each punishment trial presents a new pair (A,B) to C; each interaction is one-shot and C cannot engage in repeated interaction with same targets.",0.9,1,"Player C chooses one of four discrete punishment options (0,3,6,9 deduction points); not continuous, but they are discrete amounts from a fixed menu, which fits the ‘any (discrete) amount’ interpretation as opposed to a strict contribute-all-or-0 binary.",0.7,0,C’s 10-point endowment starts entirely as C’s private payoff; any used for punishment are voluntarily spent.,1,N/R,"This is a punishment game, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",0.2,0,No communication between players during punishment stage; only information displayed is group affiliation and prior behavior.,1,0,C sees only A and B’s group affiliations and their prior PD actions in each trial; no summaries of others’ payoffs or past punishments.,0.9,0,Interactions are anonymous; no identities or specific IDs of punishers are revealed to punished players.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism; only costly punishment points.,1,1,Subjects were instructed about the total number of punishment trials (30) in the scanner; implies knowledge of finite horizon.,0.8,1,Core feature: Player C can assign punishment points to Player A at a cost.,1,1,Assigning one punishment point costs C one point.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces A’s income by three points.,1,0,No option to spend points to reward; only to punish or keep.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,10,"At the beginning of each punishment trial, Player C received an endowment of 10 points.",1,N/R,Authors report average punishment points (not fully enumerated) per condition but do not convert to proportion of endowment explicitly; exact means for this specific cell are only described qualitatively (‘punished much more severely’).,0.2,N/R,"Mean punishment points in BC-DC condition are not numerically reported, only relative comparisons and significance tests.",0.2,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) for each constellation is reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs (A+B+C) by condition are not provided.,0.1,Switzerland,Same 16 officer candidates in Swiss Army; Stage 2 is a subset invited to the scanner.,1,"Mean 24.5 years (SD 2.2, range 20–27)",Age statistics for the 16 fMRI subjects reported explicitly.,1,Male,"Sample described as 16 healthy, right-handed male subjects.",1,"Officer candidates in Swiss Army; no major differences across platoons in branch, education, or age",Design section notes lack of significant differences in platoon composition regarding education.,0.7,On site,Conducted in a 3T MRI scanner at a research facility with computerized task presentation; not an online nor natural field context.,1,"Third-party punishment of prior PD decisions. In each of 30 trials, Player C observes A and B’s PD choices and their group affiliations (your group vs other group, plus color cue). In BC constellation, A is from another platoon (outgroup to C) and B is from C’s platoon (ingroup). Outcome DC means A defected (kept 20) and B cooperated (transferred 20). C receives 10 points and chooses one of 0,3,6,9 punishment points for A; each point costs C 1 and reduces A’s payoff by 3. Only A can be punished each trial.",Summarized directly from the Stage 2 design and group-constellation descriptions.,1
10.1002_hbm.21298.md,"Stage 2 – Third-party punishment: DC, Ingroup constellation AC","Condition where Player A (punishable) and C are from same platoon (AC), while B is outgroup; PD outcome DC (A defects, B cooperates). This is one ingroup case with lenient punishment.",1,"Group constellation and PD outcome: AC (A and C ingroup; B outgroup), PD pattern DC (A defects, B cooperates)",This is one of the two ingroup constellations; the analysis contrasts punishment between ingroup (AC+ABC) and outgroup (BC) for DC.,1,True,"Same human fMRI punishment experiment, focusing on a different group-constellation cell.",1,True,Experimental manipulation of group constellation and PD history remains identical to BC condition.,1,True,"Conducted inside MRI scanner, same as other Stage 2 conditions.",1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,No purely analytical treatment.,1,3,"Same triadic setup (A, B, C) as other Stage 2 conditions.",1,1,Each AC-DC instance is a one-shot punishment decision within the 30 trials.,0.9,1,"Same discrete punishment menu (0,3,6,9) for C as in other Stage 2 conditions.",0.7,0,C’s 10 points start as private money; only spent if punishing.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,0.2,0,"No communication allowed, as per overall Stage 2 description.",1,0,C only sees current trial’s group affiliations and PD actions.,0.9,0,Punishment decisions remain anonymous; no IDs shown.,0.9,0,No reward options exist.,1,1,Subjects knew the experimental structure and number of trials.,0.8,1,C can assign punishment points to A.,1,1,One point of punishment costs C one point.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces A’s payoff by three.,1,0,No explicit reward scheme.,1,N/A,Rewards not offered.,1,N/A,Rewards not offered.,1,10,C’s endowment per trial remains 10 points.,1,N/R,"Paper states that ingroup members are punished less severely than outgroup members (paired t-tests reported), but does not give the mean punishment for AC-DC.",0.2,N/R,Exact mean punishment points in AC-DC are not numerically reported.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency measures per constellation.,0.1,N/R,Group-level payoffs are not tabulated by condition.,0.1,Switzerland,Same 16 Swiss Army officer candidates as in other Stage 2 conditions.,1,"Mean 24.5 years (SD 2.2, range 20–27)",Age reported for the entire fMRI sample.,1,Male,All 16 subjects were male.,1,Officer candidates in Swiss Army; no systematic education differences between platoons,Same participant pool; education comparability discussed in Design section.,0.7,On site,Same fMRI lab environment as BC-DC condition.,1,"Same third-party punishment task as BC-DC but with AC group constellation: A and C belong to the same platoon (ingroup); B is from another platoon (outgroup). In DC, A defects against B who cooperates.",Directly follows the definition of AC constellation and DC outcome.,1
10.1002_hbm.21298.md,"Stage 2 – Third-party punishment: DC, Ingroup constellation ABC","Condition where all three players (A, B, C) are from same platoon (ABC) and prior PD outcome is DC; second ingroup case with similar lenient punishment as AC.",1,"Group constellation and PD outcome: ABC (A, B, and C all ingroup), PD pattern DC (A defects, B cooperates)",ABC is the other ingroup constellation used in behavioral and fMRI analyses of parochial punishment.,1,True,Same third-party punishment paradigm with human subjects.,1,True,Condition is part of the controlled experimental manipulation of group constellations.,1,True,Conducted in scanner with computerized task.,1,False,No simulations involved.,1,False,No pure analytical model.,1,3,"Triadic interaction (A,B,C).",1,1,Each ABC-DC instance is one-shot within the 30 trials.,0.9,1,"Same discrete punishment options (0,3,6,9).",0.7,0,C’s 10 points start fully private.,1,N/R,Not a public-goods contribution game.,0.2,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,Only current trial information is displayed.,0.9,0,Anonymity maintained; no IDs shown.,0.9,0,No reward option.,1,1,Subjects knew general structure and that interactions were one-shot.,0.8,1,C can punish A.,1,1,Cost per punishment point is 1 for C.,1,3,Each point assigned reduces A’s income by 3.,1,0,No rewarding option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,C’s endowment is 10 in every trial.,1,N/R,Paper states reduced punishment of ingroup members and reports significant differences vs BC but does not give ABC-DC mean punishment.,0.2,N/R,Average punishment points for ABC-DC not numeric in text.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency data provided by constellation.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff statistics by constellation.,0.1,Switzerland,Same subject pool.,1,"Mean 24.5 years (SD 2.2, range 20–27)",Same 16 fMRI subjects.,1,Male,All subjects male.,1,Officer candidates in Swiss Army; similar education across platoons,Same as other Stage 2 conditions.,0.7,On site,Same fMRI lab setting.,1,"Same third-party punishment setting; now all three are from the same platoon. In DC, A defects against cooperating ingroup B; C is also ingroup. C chooses 0,3,6,9 punishment points for A.",Derived from definition of ABC constellation and DC behavior pattern.,1
10.1038_nclimate1927.md,No institution (baseline CRD),Black lines/triangles in Fig. 1 represent dynamics without any sanctioning institution.,0.9,Presence of sanctioning institution = none; perception of risk r and mutation rate μ varied across simulation runs,"Condition defined by absence of sanctions while varying r and μ as shown in Fig. 1a,b.",0.9,False,Study is an evolutionary game-theoretic/simulation model with no human subjects described.,1,False,No controlled human experiment or field intervention; only theoretical/simulation analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting with human participants.,1,True,"Markov process over configurations, stationary distribution, and gradient of selection are computed numerically.",1,True,"Methods section specifies payoff functions, Markov process, and analytical definitions; combined analytical–computational approach.",0.9,100,Population size Z=100 reported in Fig. 1 caption.,1,0,Model is evolutionary and continuous-time in terms of birth-death events; no fixed number of game rounds.,0.6,1,"Individuals choose a strategy type (C, P, or D) rather than a continuous contribution level; contribution is a fixed fraction c of endowment for Cs and Ps, none for Ds.",0.8,0,"Endowment b starts as private benefit; only Cs and Ps contribute cost c, so nothing is automatically in the public fund.",0.9,N/R,Threshold collective-risk dilemma with probabilistic loss of endowment; no linear MPCR or multiplier is specified.,0.3,N/A,No human participants or communication mechanisms in the simulation.,1,0,Model does not include information feedback about others’ earnings; only payoffs enter evolutionary dynamics.,0.8,N/A,No punishment institution exists in this baseline condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modelled.,1,0,Agents do not observe a finite horizon; evolutionary process is indefinite.,0.8,0,This condition is explicitly ‘absence of any institution’.,1,N/A,No punishment is available.,1,N/A,No punishment is available.,1,0,"Model only includes punishment institutions, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Payoffs are expressed as −c + b etc. with cost ratio c/b=0.1 in Fig. 1; set b=1 as a normalization without loss of generality to match c/b ratio.,0.6,N/R,"Only aggregate group achievement η_G and institution prevalence η_I are reported, not average contribution fractions.",0.2,N/R,No raw contributions per player or per group are reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,0.2,N/R,Only game structure and qualitative results for η_G and η_I are given; no numeric group payoff.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,"Collective-risk dilemma with group size N=4, population size Z=100, coordination threshold n_pg=0.75*N, perception of risk r∈[0,1], cost-to-benefit ratio c/b=0.1, mutation rate μ varied (including μ=1/Z), no punishment institution. If total Cs+Ps in a group are below n_pg, everyone loses remaining endowment b with probability r; otherwise they keep it.",Parameters drawn from Fig. 1 caption and Methods description of CRD without Ps.,0.95
10.1038_nclimate1927.md,Global punishment institution,Red lines/squares in Fig. 1 and global-institution dynamics in Figs. 2–3 correspond to one global sanctioning institution.,0.95,Type of sanctioning institution = global; perception of risk r and mutation rate μ varied across simulation runs,Condition defined by presence of a single global punishment institution; r and μ are swept in Fig. 1.,0.95,False,"Model with strategies C, P, D and institutional punishment implemented in a stochastic evolutionary simulation.",1,False,No direct human or field experiment; numerical exploration of a theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,True,Stationary distributions and gradients of selection computed via Markov process simulation/numerical solution.,1,True,Analytical payoff functions and transition probabilities combined with numerical eigenvector solution.,0.9,100,Population size Z=100 in Fig. 1 caption.,1,0,Evolutionary Markov process without a predetermined number of rounds.,0.6,1,"Players adopt discrete strategies: Cs and Ps contribute fixed cost c, Ds contribute 0.",0.9,0,Endowment b is private; contributions c are made only if agent is C or P.,0.9,N/R,"Game is threshold-based with risky loss, not a linear MPCR public goods game.",0.3,N/A,No humans or communication protocol.,1,0,Agents only respond through payoffs and evolutionary update; no explicit display of others’ outcomes.,0.8,0,Institutional punishment is impersonal (pool punishment); no identities mentioned.,0.8,N/A,No rewards modelled.,1,0,No finite-round horizon in evolutionary dynamics.,0.8,1,Global institution taxes Ps and fines Ds when Ps exceed global threshold n_p.,1,0.03,Punishment tax π_t=0.03 given in Fig. 1 caption; each P pays this to support the institution.,1,0.3,"Punishment fine π_f=0.3 in Fig. 1 caption; each D pays π_f when institution active; we take tech as fine per unit tax, π_f/π_t≈10, but the model does not define a per-unit mapping, so we record fine magnitude 0.3 as effective tech.",0.5,0,Only punishment institutions described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Normalize b=1 to be consistent with c/b=0.1; only relative values matter.,0.6,N/R,Outcome metrics are η_G and η_I; average contribution rates are not reported numerically.,0.2,N/R,No explicit numeric contributions per player are reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency as payoff ratio to maximum is not quantified.,0.2,N/R,"Group achievement and institution prevalence are reported, but not payoffs.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is a theoretical/simulation scenario.,1,"Collective-risk dilemma with N=4, Z=100. Cs and Ps contribute cost c with c/b=0.1; Ds contribute 0. If j_C + j_P < n_pg, all players in group lose remaining endowment b with probability r; otherwise keep it. Global pool-punishment institution: Ps pay tax π_t=0.03; if total Ps in population i_P ≥ n_p (25% of population: n_p=0.25*Z), a fine π_f=0.3 is imposed on every D in the population. Threshold n_pg is 75% of group: n_pg=0.75*N. Risk r and mutation rate μ are varied; β=5 controls intensity of selection; mutations with probability μ allow random strategy exploration.","Parameter values and institutional rules taken from main text, Methods, and Fig. 1 caption including ‘local (global) institutions are created whenever 25% of the group (population) contributes to its establishment’ and ‘coordination threshold n_pg is set to 75% of the group size’.",0.95
10.1038_nclimate1927.md,Local punishment institutions,Blue lines/circles in Fig. 1 and local-institution dynamics in Figs. 2–3 correspond to group-level sanctioning institutions.,0.95,Type of sanctioning institution = local (group-level); perception of risk r and mutation rate μ varied across simulation runs,"Condition defined by local (group-wide) institutions with same parameters as global case, but institution acts at group level.",0.95,False,Evolutionary simulation without human subjects.,1,False,"The study is theoretical/computational, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No physical lab or field site.,1,True,Stationary distributions over configurations computed numerically.,1,True,Uses analytical payoff functions and Markov chain specification with numerical solution.,0.9,100,Population size Z=100 given.,1,0,Continuous evolutionary process; no fixed round count.,0.6,1,"Strategies C, P, D correspond to fixed contribution decisions, not continuous levels.",0.9,0,Endowment is initially private; contributions require being C or P.,0.9,N/R,Risk-based threshold game does not specify a linear MPCR.,0.3,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No explicit observation of others’ payoffs beyond fitness used in evolutionary rule.,0.8,0,Punishment is institutional at group level; no mention of individual identity revelation.,0.8,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Model omits any finite-horizon or round-display mechanic.,0.8,1,Local institutions collect taxes from Ps and fine Ds when local threshold reached.,1,0.03,Punishment tax π_t=0.03 in Fig. 1 caption; paid by Ps to support local institution.,1,0.3,Local punishment fine π_f=0.3 per D when group has at least n_p Ps; we treat π_f as effective punishment magnitude.,0.5,0,Model does not include rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Endowment b normalized to 1 for consistency with c/b=0.1.,0.6,N/R,Contribution rates per player are not numerically summarized; only group achievement η_G.,0.2,N/R,No raw contribution levels reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency measure is not reported.,0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoff data given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational/simulation condition.,1,"Same collective-risk dilemma as other conditions: N=4, Z=100, c/b=0.1, coordination threshold n_pg=0.75*N contributors (Cs or Ps) needed; if not reached, all group members lose remaining endowment b with probability r; otherwise they keep it. Local pool-punishment institutions: each P pays tax π_t=0.03 to a group-level institution; if number of Ps in group j_P ≥ n_p (25% of group: n_p=0.25*N), each D in that group is punished with fine π_f=0.3 (Δ_local=π_f Θ(j_P − n_p)). Mutation probability μ (including μ=1/Z) introduces random strategy changes; selection intensity β=5.","Extracted from Methods and figure captions, including explicit statement that ‘local institutions are created whenever 25% of the group contributes’ and formula for Δ_local.",0.95
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2009.00268.x.md,VCM rounds 1–5 (no disapproval) – Fishermen (poolers and non-poolers),First five rounds are standard repeated VCM without social disapproval; fishermen (both pooling and non-pooling) participate.,0.9,Subject type: fishermen (poolers and non-poolers); Stage: baseline VCM without social disapproval (rounds 1–5).,"Across the paper, behavior is compared by subject type (poolers, non-poolers, students) and by before vs after introduction of disapproval; this row aggregates all fishermen in baseline VCM.",0.8,True,Human participants (fishermen) make contribution decisions.,1.0,True,Controlled experimental protocol with random group assignment and specified payoffs.,1.0,True,"Conducted in meeting room at fishing cooperative using hand-run lab-style protocol, not in natural work setting.",0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulations; all decisions by humans.,1.0,False,No formal purely analytical model tested as an experiment.,1.0,4,“participants were divided into four person groups”.,1.0,5,First five rounds are described as standard VCM before rule change.,1.0,1,Participants could contribute “as many coins as (s)he wanted” from 0 to 10; not all-or-nothing.,1.0,0,Endowment starts entirely in private envelope; participants move chosen amount to public-good envelope.,1.0,0.5,Group total is doubled then divided by 4; marginal per capita return = 2/4 = 0.5; also stated explicitly.,1.0,0,No mention of communication being allowed; instructions stress confidentiality and no speaking.,1.0,1,"After each round, slips show “individual contributions in random order, the group total contribution, and the individual payout”.",1.0,0,"No punishment/disapproval stage in rounds 1–5; only contributions are shown, and in random order with anonymity.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in any round.,1.0,1,Instructions clearly state the exercise “will be repeated ten times”; implies subjects know total rounds.,0.9,0,Social disapproval only starts in last five rounds; baseline VCM has no punishment mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No punishment or disapproval option in these rounds.,1.0,N/A,No punishment technology defined before round 6.,1.0,0,No mechanism to spend resources to reward others is described.,1.0,N/A,Reward not available in design.,1.0,N/A,Reward not available in design.,1.0,10,“each group member was endowed with ten 50 yen coins” per round.,1.0,0.473,"Overall fishermen contribution mean 5.14 coins (Table 1); normalized by 10-coin endowment ⇒ 5.14/10 ≈ 0.514. But Table 1 separates pre- and post-disapproval: pre-disapproval contribution for fishermen overall is not directly reported; only pooler, non-pooler, student. For all fishermen: weighted mean = (14*5.21 + 13*4.38)/(27*10) ≈ 4.82/10=0.482. Using fishermen-only, pre-disapproval; ignoring students and later rounds.",0.6,4.82,Computed weighted average pre-disapproval contribution for fishermen from Table 1: (14*5.21 +13*4.38)/27 ≈ 4.82 coins.,0.7,N/R,No explicit or inferable report of total earnings vs maximum for this subset.,0.2,N/R,"Only regression coefficients on group totals are shown, no raw average group payoff.",0.1,Japan,"Fishermen are in Toyama Prefecture, Japan.",1.0,N/R,Age distributions or means are not reported.,0.0,N/R,"Gender composition is not reported, though fishermen likely mostly male; cannot assert.",0.2,N/R,No schooling information reported for fishermen.,0.0,On site,"Sessions with fishermen took place in a large meeting room at the fishing cooperative, not their natural work setting or online.",1.0,"Standard linear public goods game (VCM) with four-person fixed groups, repeated 5 rounds, endowment 10 coins per round, per-coin MPCR 0.5, anonymous decisions, feedback on individual contributions in random order and group total each round.",Summarized from Section III and Appendix A descriptions.,1.0
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2009.00268.x.md,VCM rounds 6–10 with social disapproval – Fishermen (poolers and non-poolers),Last five rounds introduce social disapproval option; this row aggregates all fishermen under that treatment.,0.9,Subject type: fishermen (poolers and non-poolers); Stage: VCM with costly social disapproval (rounds 6–10).,Independent manipulation is availability of disapproval compared to baseline; also analyzed by subject type.,0.9,True,Human fishermen participate in repeated game with punishment-like mechanism.,1.0,True,Controlled change in rules after round 5; causal experimental design.,1.0,True,Conducted in controlled meeting room with experimental protocol.,0.9,False,No simulation-only results.,1.0,False,No closed-form theoretical experiment only.,1.0,4,Same four-person fixed groups used throughout.,1.0,5,Social disapproval institution applies only to last five of ten rounds.,1.0,1,Contribution decision remains continuous 0–10 coins.,1.0,0,Same endowment and procedure: all coins start as private and can be moved to project.,1.0,0.5,Payoff technology unchanged: total doubled and split four ways.,1.0,0,No communication allowed; only anonymous signals via unhappy faces.,1.0,1,"Participants still receive individual contributions (random order), group total, and their payoff each round.",1.0,0,"Signals are aggregate, not individualized; envelopes show total number of unhappy faces, and sending is anonymous.",1.0,0,No reward option is available at any time.,1.0,1,Instructions at start mention 10 total rounds; rule change is explained at round 6 but horizon remains known.,0.9,1,Costly social disapproval (unhappy face) is a non-monetary punishment technology.,1.0,10,Ordering an unhappy face “will cost you 10 yen”.,1.0,0,Signals impose no monetary loss on others; only psychological/shame effect; thus marginal monetary impact per coin spent is 0.,1.0,0,No mechanism to positively reward others exists.,1.0,N/A,No reward stage.,1.0,N/A,No reward stage.,1.0,10,S