10.3389_fphy.2018.00107.md,"N/A – conceptual/methodological review, no specific condition","The paper is a perspective/review outlining potential games and models; it does not report any concrete experiment, simulation run, or defined treatment/control condition with parameters.",1,N/A,No independent variables are manipulated in an actual study; only general classes of games and mechanisms are discussed.,1,False,No new human-subject data are collected; only prior literature is summarized and proposed future directions are discussed.,1,False,The article does not describe an implemented controlled experiment; it is a conceptual review.,1,False,No lab or field experiment is conducted; only descriptions of possible paradigms and references to others' work.,1,False,"Although simulations are mentioned (e.g., spatial public goods games), no specific new simulation setup or results are presented in this paper.",1,False,The paper does not develop new formal analytical models; it surveys existing approaches.,1,0,No concrete game implementation is reported; only example paradigms are named without fixed parameters.,1,0,No repeated-game implementation is actually run in this paper.,1,0,Not applicable because no actual choice structure is implemented.,1,N/A,No human experiment or specific public goods game parametrization is run.,1,N/R,"No numerical parameters (e.g., marginal per capita return) are specified for any actually executed game.",1,N/A,No interaction protocol with human participants is implemented.,1,0,Not applicable; retained default 0 as there is no experiment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism with human subjects is actually implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism with human subjects is actually implemented.,1,0,No rounds are run; field is effectively inapplicable.,1,0,"Punishment is discussed conceptually and via cited work, but not instantiated in a new study here.",1,N/A,No implemented punishment treatment; cost parameters are not defined for this paper’s own study.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Reward is discussed via prior literature, but there is no concrete reward treatment here.",1,N/A,No implemented reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No implemented reward mechanism.,1,0,No specific experimental endowment is assigned because no experiment is conducted.,1,N/R,No outcome data are reported; only conceptual discussion and prior results are mentioned.,1,N/R,No new behavioral data.,1,N/R,No empirical or simulated efficiency outcomes are calculated in this paper.,1,N/R,No payoff data are reported.,1,N/A,No human participants are recruited.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The article presents theoretical and review content only; it does not run human or field studies.,1,"Conceptual overview of social dilemma games and moral-behavior paradigms (prisoner’s dilemma, stag hunt, public goods, trust, ultimatum, dictator, sender-receiver, distribution experiments) and of statistical-physics-based models of cooperation and punishment; no single parametrized game or condition is executed in this paper.",Summarizes the article’s scope: it proposes games and references prior simulations but adds no new implemented design.,1
10.1073_pnas.1703857114.md,Us-vs.-nature agent-based simulations (collective action with punishment),"Paper describes a class of agent-based simulations labeled 'us-vs.-nature games' with collective action, punishment, and evolving norm internalization; treated as one modeling condition because parameters are varied systematically within this game type rather than as discrete treatments with human subjects.",0.78,"Game type = us-vs.-nature; group size n varied (e.g., 8, 16, 24); benefit of collective good b=4; half-success parameter X0=n/2 (or X0=8 in Fig.1 example); number of competing groups K (e.g., K=3 or 4); normative values v_x and v_y varied (including cases v_x=0,v_y=0; v_x>0,v_y=0; v_x=0,v_y>0; v_x>0,v_y>0); punishment and monitoring parameters δ, κ, c_mon; optimization and internalization costs c_opt, c_int; mutation rate and optimization error e.","Extracted from descriptions of us-vs.-nature games, parameter sets in Fig.1 and Fig.3 captions, and Methods; independent variables are game-type and parameter values manipulated across simulations.",0.74,False,The study uses agent-based simulations and mathematical specification of payoffs and fitness; there are no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human or animal subjects; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No physical lab or field setting; simulations only.,1,True,Explicitly described as 'agent-based simulations' in abstract and Models and Results.,1,False,"Although equations are specified, results are obtained via simulations rather than closed-form analytical solutions.",0.86,16,"Example us-vs.-nature run in Fig.1 specifies n=16; other values (8,24) are also used, but 16 is a concrete reported group size for this game type.",0.68,N/R,Number of collective action rounds per generation is denoted Q in Methods but no numerical value is given.,0.3,0,"Action variables x and y are binary (0 or 1), so contributions/punishment are all-or-nothing in amount, not continuous.",1,N/A,No human endowments or initial allocations; agents choose binary effort x each round in a modeled payoff function.,1,N/A,"This is not a standard linear public goods game with a marginal per capita return; payoff is defined via nonlinear P(X) and b,c parameters in a biological fitness context.",1,N/A,No human communication; simulation of decision rules only.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human participants; only modeled payoff calculations.,1,N/A,No identities or anonymity concepts; agents are algorithmic and do not observe identities as in lab games.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism to identify; only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No interface; thus nothing is 'shown' to subjects.,1,1,"Model explicitly includes a punishment stage, with variable y and costs δ and κ for punishing and being punished.",1,N/R,Punishment cost parameter δ is defined but no specific numerical value is reported in the main text for us-vs.-nature examples.,0.3,N/R,Magnitude of impact per unit punishment (analog of 'technology') is embedded in κ and δ but no simple per-unit mapping is specified numerically.,0.3,0,Only punishment of free riders is modeled; no peer rewards or positive transfers are included.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,"Agent payoffs accrue from modeled costs and benefits, not from experimental endowments to human players.",1,N/R,"Results are reported qualitatively (e.g., 'individuals make no effort', 'dramatic increase in production') and via plots of mean x, but no explicit normalized contribution rates are numerically stated.",0.35,N/R,Mean effort x is plotted but no numerical values are given in the text for particular parameter sets.,0.35,N/R,"Efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is not defined or numerically reported, though average fitness is mentioned qualitatively.",0.2,N/R,"Average fitness and material payoffs are discussed and graphed, but no explicit numeric group payoff values are stated.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations only; no experimental environment with human subjects.,1,"Volunteer-dilemma-type collective action game labeled 'us-vs.-nature'. Groups of size n (e.g., 8,16,24) repeatedly face a collective action problem where individuals choose effort x∈{0,1}. Group success probability P=X/(X+X0), with X total group effort and X0 a half-success parameter. Benefit from successful collective action is bP, individual cost of effort is c x. After a punishment stage, payoffs are π(x,y)=π_CA−y[(1−p̃)δ+c_mon]−(1−x)κ q̃. Norm internalization trait η∈[0,1] affects utility u_η(x,y)=(1−η)π(x,y)+η(v1 x+v2 y). Biological fitness is w=1+π̄−c_opt(1−η)−c_int η. Group and individual selection implemented via a two-level Fisher-Wright reproduction scheme with group survival proportional to average success in collective actions and within-group reproduction proportional to fitness.","Summarizes the us-vs.-nature model structure from Models, Results, and Methods sections.",0.92
10.1073_pnas.1703857114.md,Us-vs.-them agent-based simulations (intergroup contest with punishment),"Paper describes a second class of agent-based simulations labeled 'us-vs.-them games' involving intergroup contests, cooperation, punishment, and norm internalization; treated as a distinct modeling condition because game form differs systematically from us-vs.-nature.",0.86,"Game type = us-vs.-them; group size n varied (e.g., 8, 16, 24); benefit parameter b=1; number of competing groups K (e.g., K=2 or 3); normative values v_x and v_y varied over combinations v_x=0,v_y=0; v_x>0,v_y=0; v_x=0,v_y>0; v_x>0,v_y>0; punishment and monitoring parameters δ, κ, c_mon; optimization and internalization costs c_opt, c_int; migration/dispersal rate; optimization error e.","Extracted from description of us-vs.-them games, parameter sets in Fig.2 and Fig.4 captions, and Methods; independent variables are game-type and associated parameter values varied across runs.",0.8,False,These are computational simulations with no human participants.,1,False,No empirical experimental manipulation with subjects; only simulation studies.,1,False,Not conducted in a laboratory or field setting; simulations only.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent-based simulations; dynamics illustrated in Figs.2 and 4.,1,False,"Although a payoff and fitness structure is given, the core results are numerical simulation outcomes.",0.86,8,"Example us-vs.-them run in Fig.2 specifies n=8; other sizes (16,24) are used, but 8 is a concrete reported value for this game type.",0.7,N/R,Number of rounds per generation Q is defined but not numerically specified in text for us-vs.-them simulations.,0.3,0,Effort x and punishment choice y are binary 0/1 so actions are all-or-nothing at the individual level.,1,N/A,No notion of initial endowment or default allocation for human participants; only modeled effort choices.,1,N/A,Payoff is determined by a Tullock contest success function P=X/ X̄ rather than a linear public good multiplier; MPCR is not defined.,1,N/A,No communication among human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,"No subject interface; only modeled information (e.g., assumed individuals know group efforts) used in optimization.",1,N/A,Identities and their visibility are not part of this simulation framework.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,"No human players, so nothing is displayed.",1,1,"Punishment variables and costs (y, δ, κ, c_mon) are explicitly included, and results are discussed in terms of punishment levels.",1,N/R,δ is defined as the cost of punishing free riders but no concrete value is given in main text for us-vs.-them examples.,0.3,N/R,Technology of punishment (effect on victims per unit cost) is implicit in κ and δ but no explicit numeric mapping is given.,0.3,0,Only punishment and disapproval of free riders are modeled; no peer reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Payoffs arise from intergroup contests and punishment costs, not from initial token endowments to experimental subjects.",1,N/R,Results are shown by plots of average effort x but no explicit numeric normalized contribution rates are reported.,0.35,N/R,"Only qualitative statements and figures (e.g., 'small increase in production') are given; no specific per-round effort numbers.",0.35,N/R,"Although average fitness is discussed (often decreasing in us-vs.-them), efficiency relative to a maximum is not numerically defined.",0.25,N/R,"Average fitness and material payoffs are treated conceptually and via graphs, with no specific numeric values provided.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based modeling only.,1,"Intergroup contest game labeled 'us-vs.-them'. Groups of size n (e.g., 8,16,24) choose individual efforts x∈{0,1} contributing to a group total X, which competes with other groups' efforts via a Tullock contest success function P=X/ X̄, where X̄ is the average effort across all groups. Material payoff from the collective action is π_CA=b P−c x with benefit parameter b (e.g., b=1) and cost c. Punishment is modeled as in the us-vs.-nature case: individuals choose y∈{0,1} to punish defectors at cost δ and monitoring cost c_mon, while defectors incur punishment cost proportional to κ and the frequency of punishers. Utility incorporates norm internalization via u_η(x,y)=(1−η)π(x,y)+η(v1 x+v2 y). Biological fitness is w=1+π̄−c_opt(1−η)−c_int η. Group selection is implemented by group survival and reproduction probabilities proportional to average contest success P̄ across rounds; within surviving groups, individuals reproduce with probabilities proportional to fitness, followed by dispersal.","Summarizes the structural features of the us-vs.-them model from Models, Results, and Methods sections.",0.92
