10.1111_j.1468-0297.2009.02288.x.md,Baseline treatment – no punishment – Part 1,Baseline treatment without punishment opportunities; behaviour in the first play of the game is analysed separately as 'part 1'.,1,Punishment institution absent; game play part = 1,Treatment varies presence of punishment (baseline vs punishment) and parts 1 vs 2 are played as separate one-shot interactions.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with student participants making decisions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in CREED laboratory at the University of Amsterdam.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations described.,1,False,"Study reports experimental data, not purely analytical models.",1,2,Game is explicitly described as a two-person social dilemma game with a first mover and a second mover.,1,1,Within each part the game is explicitly one-shot; part 1 is one such play.,1,1,"First mover chooses between sending 50 or 0; this send is a binary all-or-nothing decision, but the requested variable concerns whether players can contribute any amount. Here, only fixed discrete options are available, so code as all-or-nothing=1 (restricted choice).",0.6,0,All endowments start in private accounts; any transfer is an active decision by players.,1,N/R,"Not a standard linear public good; transfers are discrete and asymmetric, so MPCR is not defined as in VCM.",1,0,No communication between participants is mentioned; typical z-Tree lab protocol without chat.,0.9,0,Players see only own and counterpart’s decisions/payoffs; no mention of aggregate or summary information.,0.9,0,"No punishment in baseline, so identities associated with punishment are not applicable; however field requires 0/1, so set to 0 and note N/A logically.",0.5,0,No reward mechanism present or mentioned.,1,1,Instructions state that each subject plays the game twice and that each part is a one-shot game; participants are informed about the two independent parts.,1,0,Baseline described explicitly as 'without punishment opportunities'.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in baseline treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in baseline treatment.,1,0,No reward or positive sanctioning mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,150,"At the start of the game, first mover receives 150 points (100 base earnings + 50 decision points); for a single decision-maker-endowment we report the deciding player’s total initial points.",0.7,0.846,"In baseline part 1, 84.6% of first movers cooperate (send 50). Normalized contribution rate equals cooperation frequency because choice is binary 0 or full 50.",0.9,42.3,Table B2 'Mean points sent (cooperation)' in part 1 is 42.3; this is the average amount sent by first movers (including defectors as 0 in the mean over all first movers).,0.7,N/R,"Paper reports only earnings in points, not normalized efficiencies relative to maximum possible surplus in this treatment.",0.3,230.8,"Section 2.1: 'In part 1, the average earnings of all participants are actually higher in the baseline treatment (230.8 vs. 189.0 points)'; this is average total per participant, which equals group average payoff per capita.",0.8,Netherlands,"Experiment conducted in CREED laboratory at the University of Amsterdam, implying subjects were in the Netherlands.",0.9,Mean 22 years; students,Section 2: 'The average age was 22 years'.,1,58% male,Section 2: '58% of the participants were male.',1,"University students, ~54% economics majors, remainder various fields",Section 2: '162 students... Approximately 54% were students of economics and the rest came from a variety of fields.',1,On site,Conducted in the CREED laboratory with subjects seated at desks.,1,"Sequential two-player social dilemma (trust-like game): First mover (A) has 50 decision points on top of 100 base; may transfer 50 (multiplied by 6 to 300 for B) or 0. Second mover (B) then chooses to return 150, 50, or 0. No punishment stage in this treatment; game played once (part 1) with perfect strangers matching.",Summarizes the structure described in Section 1.1 and the procedures for part 1 in baseline.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2009.02288.x.md,Baseline treatment – no punishment – Part 2,"Same baseline treatment without punishment, but behaviour in the second independent play is analysed as 'part 2'.",1,Punishment institution absent; game play part = 2,Independent variable values: punishment absent (baseline) and it is the second play with a new partner.,1,True,Same lab experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled experimental conditions maintained across parts.,1,True,Conducted in CREED laboratory.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Empirical data reported.,1,2,Two-person game (first and second mover).,1,1,Each part is a one-shot interaction; part 2 is one such play.,1,1,Same decision structure as part 1: binary 0 vs 50 transfer.,0.6,0,Endowments start privately.,1,N/R,Non-VCM sequential trust structure; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No mention of communication allowed.,0.9,0,Subjects only see their own interaction outcomes.,0.9,0,"No punishment mechanism; identity question is moot, coded as 0.",0.5,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Subjects are informed there are exactly two parts and that each is played once.,1,0,Baseline treatment has no punishment opportunities.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward stage implemented.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,150,Same game structure as part 1: first mover has 100 base + 50 decision points; total 150.,0.7,0.462,Table B2: 'Frequency of cooperation' in part 2 is 46.2%; normalized contribution rate equals binary cooperation frequency.,0.9,23.1,Table B2: 'Mean points sent (cooperation)' in part 2 is 23.1; this is the average points sent by first movers across the population.,0.7,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios reported for this part.,0.3,182.7,"Section 2.1: 'in part 2, average earnings are higher in the punishment treatment (187.3 vs. 182.7 points)' indicating baseline mean per participant in part 2 is 182.7.",0.8,Netherlands,Same subject pool and location as part 1.,0.9,Mean 22 years; students,Same sample characteristics across both parts.,1,58% male,Same pool across parts.,1,"University students, ~54% economics majors, remainder various fields",Same as reported for total sample.,1,On site,Same lab setting.,1,"Identical sequential two-player social dilemma as part 1 baseline: First mover with 50 decision points can transfer 50 or 0; if 50 is transferred, it becomes 300 for B; B then returns 150, 50, or 0. No punishment; partners are rematched using perfect strangers protocol.","Design explicitly states part 2 uses the same three steps and instructions as part 1 in baseline, with a new partner.",1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2009.02288.x.md,Punishment treatment – with punishment – Part 1,Main treatment where both players can punish/retaliate; behaviour in the first play (part 1) treated as a separate condition.,1,Punishment institution present; game play part = 1,Independent variable is presence of punishment opportunities vs baseline; this row covers punishment present and first interaction.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with students.,1,True,Controlled design with clearly defined treatments.,1,True,Conducted in CREED lab at University of Amsterdam.,1,False,No simulation methods described.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not pure theory.",1,2,Two-person social dilemma game with first and second mover.,1,1,Each part is a one-shot game; part 1 is one play.,1,1,"First mover's decision is discrete: transfer 50 or 0, not a continuous amount.",0.6,0,All points start in private accounts.,1,3,"First mover's 50 points, if sent, generate 300 for second mover (net +250 group surplus on 50 sacrificed). The effective group multiplier on the 50 is (first mover final + second mover final) relative to keeping them. Without sending: total 250; with sending and no return: total 400; incremental surplus 150 on 50 => group multiplier of 4. However standard MPCR per capita considers return to each: marginal per capita return to B is 300/50 = 6, to A is -1; but requested MPCR is multiplier divided by group size, approximated as 6/2 = 3.",0.4,0,Instructions describe only computerised decision screens and questionnaires; no communication.,0.9,0,Subjects only learn their counterpart’s choices and resulting payoffs; no group summaries reported.,0.9,1,"Punishment is strictly bilateral and each player knows whether their current counterpart punished them (e.g., B is told 'how many penalty points you assigned him'); thus identity of punisher within pair is known.",0.9,0,"Design only includes penalty points, no rewarding.",1,1,"Subjects are informed there are exactly two parts; within a part, the punishment process continues in turns until someone sets punishment to 0 or earnings are non-positive, so total potential rounds are endogenous but the two-part horizon is common knowledge.",0.8,1,Section 1.1 describes punishment stage where both players can assign penalty points.,1,1,Each penalty point assigned costs the assigner 1 point.,1,4,Each penalty point reduces the other player's payoff by 4 points.,1,0,No mechanism for positive transfers beyond the main game’s return stage; these are not framed as rewards.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,150,"In the game description, first mover has 150 points at start (150 vs 100 for second mover in the no-punishment description). Punishment treatment uses the same underlying social dilemma but with added penalty rounds; for the main contribution decision we take first mover's total starting points.",0.7,0.864,"Table B1 'Frequency of cooperation' in part 1 of punishment treatment is 86.4%, equal to normalized contribution rate.",0.9,43.4,"Table B1: 'Mean points sent (cooperation)' for part 1 punishment is 43.4, the average sent by first movers.",0.7,N/R,Paper discusses average earnings but does not normalize by theoretical maximum surplus in this treatment.,0.3,189,"Section 2.1: 'In part 1, the average earnings of all participants are actually higher in the baseline treatment (230.8 vs. 189.0 points)'; 189.0 is punishment-treatment mean per participant in part 1.",0.9,Netherlands,Conducted in Amsterdam lab.,0.9,Mean 22 years; students,Applies to full sample including punishment treatment.,1,58% male,Overall sample composition given; no indication of imbalance across treatments.,0.9,"University students, ~54% economics majors, remainder various fields",Same pool across treatments.,1,On site,Lab experiment in CREED.,1,"Sequential trust-like social dilemma: initial endowments 150 (A) and 100 (B). Step 1: A either keeps 50 decision points or transfers 50, which are multiplied by 6 and added to B's earnings (B gets 300). Step 2: B chooses to return 150, 50, or 0 to A. Step 3: If B returned less than 150 or any amount, A may assign penalty points to B at cost 1:4 (1 cost, 4 harm). If A assigns >0, B may retaliate with same cost/impact; if B retaliates >0, A can assign additional punishment, and so on in alternating rounds until someone assigns 0 or a player’s earnings are non-positive. Negative earnings are allowed via punishment, but players cannot be further punished once their earnings are zero or less.",Summarizes Section 1.1 and detailed instructions for part 1 of punishment treatment.,1
