s majors, remainder various fields",Same pool.,1,On site,Same CREED lab environment.,1,"Identical to part 1 punishment treatment: two-player sequential social dilemma with multiple potential rounds of punishment and retaliation at 1:4 cost/impact. Perfect strangers matching: in part 2 each subject is matched with a different partner than in part 1, and instructions emphasise independence of payoffs across parts.",Procedures section states that part 2 consists of the same three steps and punishment structure as part 1 with a new partner.,1
10.1016_j.cogsys.2005.11.010.md,"N/A – theoretical review paper, no distinct experimental or simulation conditions","The provided text is a conceptual and theoretical review; it cites many other works but does not itself report any new experiment, simulation, or observational condition with its own design or parameters.",1,N/A,"No original experimental or simulation study is conducted in this paper, so no independent variables are manipulated.",1,False,The article is a theoretical and review paper integrating prior literature; it does not collect new data from human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run by the authors; experimental results mentioned are secondary citations.,1,False,There is no original lab or field experiment reported.,1,False,"Although many mathematical and simulation models are discussed, no specific new simulation study with defined parameters is presented as an original result of this paper.",0.9,True,"The paper presents and synthesizes analytical/mathematical theory (e.g., Hamilton’s rule, reciprocity conditions, cultural group selection) without new data collection.",1,0,No game with actual players is run; only abstract game-theoretic discussion of n-person dilemmas and dyads.,1,0,No implemented repeated game; only theoretical discussion of iterated interactions.,1,0,Not applicable because no actual game is implemented.,1,N/A,"No human-subject game, so no default contribution structure.",1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not specified for any concrete experimental public goods game in this paper.,1,0,No experiment with communication among participants is run.,1,0,"No interface or feedback is implemented, as there is no experiment.",1,0,No punishment stage with revealed identities is operationalized in this paper.,1,0,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,0,No actual rounds are played.,1,0,"Punishment is discussed conceptually, but no experimental or simulated punishment mechanism is implemented as a treatment condition.",1,N/A,No implemented punishment technology in this paper’s own methods.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment technology.,1,0,Rewards are not implemented as a mechanism in any original experiment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,0,No actual endowed tokens or money are given to participants in this paper.,1,N/A,"No contributions are measured, as no game is run.",1,N/A,No behavioral data on contributions are collected.,1,N/R,No efficiency outcome is calculated from experimental or simulated data.,1,N/A,No groups of participants earning payoffs are studied directly.,1,N/A,No human participants are recruited in this paper’s own work.,1,N/A,No participant sample.,1,N/A,No participant sample.,1,N/A,No participant sample.,1,No human,The work is theoretical/analytical with no human-subject data collection.,1,"Conceptual discussion of Prisoner’s Dilemma, Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma, and public goods problems, but no new experimental game implementation.","Several standard games are described to explain theory (e.g., PD payoff matrix, n-person dilemmas, indirect reciprocity), but parameters are illustrative and not tied to an original experiment.",1
10.1073_pnas.0407370102.md,Analytical continuous-entry model with constant information probability q,The section 'A Continuous-Entry Model for Indirect Reciprocity' first analyzes the case where the probability q of knowing the coplayer's score is constant; treated as one analytical condition.,0.9,Constant information probability q relative to threshold c/(b r): case q < c/(b r) and case q > c/(b r) within same analytical framework,Model behavior and stability depend on q versus c/(b r); both regimes discussed under same constant-q condition.,0.9,False,Purely theoretical evolutionary game model without human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only mathematical analysis.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; analytical theory.,1,False,"Although dynamics are illustrated, they are derived analytically, not via numerical/agent-based simulation.",0.9,True,"Closed-form derivation of payoffs and replicator dynamics, fixed points, and stability.",1,2,"Model assumes dyadic interactions: 'two individuals meet randomly, one as donor, the other as recipient.'",1,-1,Number of rounds per individual is variable and tied to age; no fixed finite number; using -1 to indicate indefinite/variable rounds in non-empirical model.,0.4,1,Each donor chooses either to help or not; no partial contributions described.,1,N/A,"Not a contribution-to-public-good game; helping is a binary act with cost c and benefit b, no initial endowment allocation.",1,N/R,Model is not a linear public goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; analytical model only.,1,0,Information is abstracted as probability q of knowing a score; no notion of numeric payoff summaries shown to agents.,0.8,0,No explicit punishment stage or identities; only reputational scores known with probability q.,0.9,0,"No explicit reward mechanism separate from helping; identities in reputation sense are abstract, not revealed in a stage.",0.9,0,"Agents do not condition on knowing the total number of rounds; model treats rounds via probabilities w_n, not as displayed information.",0.9,1,Costly punishment is implicit: discriminators refusing to help Bad recipients receive a Bad score and future loss of help; modeled as costly punishment via reputation.,0.9,N/R,"Punishment is indirect through reputational loss, not a token-based punishment action with specified per-unit cost.",0.7,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment tokens to reductions in others' payoffs; only probabilistic loss of future help.,0.7,0,Helping yields benefit b and Good scores but there is no separate costly rewarding stage distinct from the helping decision.,0.9,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology with per-token effect.,1,N/R,Model specifies fixed cost c to help and benefit b to recipient; no finite endowment per round or per player is defined.,0.8,N/R,"No empirical data; model analyzes evolutionary payoffs and frequencies, not measured contribution rates.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts beyond binary help decision with cost c.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency as realized payoff divided by maximum possible; focus is on stability and invasion.,0.9,N/R,"Only type-specific and average payoffs (P_x, P_y, P_z, ␀P␀) are analyzed symbolically; no numeric group payoff reported.",0.9,N/A,No human participants in the analytical model.,1,N/A,"Age is a theoretical variable representing rounds, not demographic data of real participants.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical without human or field data.,1,"Indirect reciprocity helping game with three strategy types: undiscriminating altruists (always intend to help), defectors (never help), and discriminators (help Good or unknown-score recipients). Each help costs c to donor and gives benefit b > c to recipient. Errors: intended help fails with probability 1 - r; defection implementation is assumed error-free. Reputation is a binary score (Good/Bad) updated via first-order assessment: donors who help get Good, those who refuse get Bad. Players interact as both donor and recipient each round with randomly chosen coplayers, and evolutionary dynamics follow replicator equations on frequencies x, y, z.","Summarized from description of strategies, payoffs P_x, P_y, P_z, error parameter r, and replicator dynamics on simplex S_3.",1
10.1073_pnas.0407370102.md,Analytical continuous-entry model with age-dependent information q_n (trustful discriminators),Second part of 'A Continuous-Entry Model for Indirect Reciprocity' introduces age-dependent probabilities q_n with trustful discriminators and derives a cooperative attractor.,0.95,Age-dependent information probabilities q_n with q_n > q_{n-1} and trustful discriminators; condition s > c/(b r) and small w_1,"Key change from first model is that individual information q_n increases with round n, affecting stability; trustful assumption maintained.",0.95,False,Still a mathematical evolutionary model without human data.,1,False,No controlled experiment; theoretical analysis of differential equations.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab or field setting.,1,False,Dynamics are analyzed symbolically; figures illustrate analytical phase portraits rather than simulation results.,0.9,True,Condition for coexistence (z_cr = c/(b r s)) and bistability are derived analytically.,1,2,Same dyadic helping structure as in constant-q model: donor and recipient per interaction.,1,-1,Rounds correspond to age; w_n is probability to be in round n. Number of rounds is variable and potentially unbounded; using -1 to indicate indefinite/variable rounds.,0.4,1,Decision per encounter is to help or not; no gradation of help level.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game with initial endowments; only binary helping with cost c.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier for a group; benefits are dyadic.,1,N/A,"No human subjects or explicit communication protocol, only abstract information probability q_n.",1,0,"Information is modeled as knowledge of binary scores, not summaries of payoffs or actions.",0.8,0,Identities are not explicitly modeled beyond reputation scores; no identifiable punishment mechanism.,0.9,0,No separate reward stage; helping itself is the cooperative act.,0.9,0,Agents do not condition behavior on known horizon length; number of rounds is embedded in distribution w_n.,0.9,1,"As in constant-q case, refusing to help a Bad recipient leads to a Bad score and reduced future support, constituting costly punishment in reputational terms.",0.9,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost of punishment beyond opportunity costs via future reputation; not quantified as tokens.,0.7,N/R,No explicit mapping between punishment units and payoff reduction is provided.,0.7,0,"Helping is not modeled as an additional, optional reward beyond the main action; no separate rewarding stage.",0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No reward technology with specified effectiveness.,1,N/R,Only costs c and benefits b per interaction are defined; no starting endowment per round.,0.8,N/R,"Outcome of interest is evolutionary stability and coexistence, not empirical contribution rates.",1,N/R,Model is binary (help vs no help) with symbolic parameters; no numeric contribution amounts.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute normalized efficiency; focuses on stability and invasion dynamics.,0.9,N/R,Only type-specific payoffs and average payoff are symbolically given; no numeric group payoff.,0.9,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,Age is a model index for rounds; not demographic.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Entire study is analytical theory.,1,"Same indirect reciprocity game with three strategy types as in constant-q model, but the probability that a discriminator knows a coplayer's score depends on the round n via q_n, increasing with age (q_n > q_{n-1}). Average information level is q = Σ w_n q_n and s = Σ w_n q_{n-1}, with condition s > c/(b r) and small w_1 ensuring a cooperative attractor. Trustful discriminators help recipients with Good or unknown scores. The model shows bistability: one attractor of all defectors and another attractor F_xz = (1 - z_cr, 0, z_cr) with z_cr = c/(b r s), a stable mixture of discriminating and undiscriminating altruists that repels defectors.","Summarizes the q_n-dependent extension, definitions of s, z_cr, and resulting bistable replicator dynamics.",1
10.1073_pnas.0407370102.md,Analytical continuous-entry model with age-dependent information q_n (suspicious discriminators),"Final part of modeling section considers the case where discriminators are suspicious and q_n is decreasing, leading to analogous bistable dynamics.",0.95,Age-dependent information probabilities q_n with q_n < q_{n-1} and suspicious discriminators,Key independent features are suspicion (refuse to help when information is absent) and decreasing q_n with age.,0.95,False,This is a further analytical variant of the theoretical model.,1,False,No empirical or simulated experiment; assumptions analyzed mathematically.,1,False,"Pure theory, no lab setting.",1,False,Conclusions reached via algebraic derivations; no numerical simulations reported.,0.9,True,"Payoffs P_x(n), P_y(n), P_z(n) for suspicious discriminators are written and used to argue for bistability analytically.",1,2,Still based on dyadic donor-recipient interactions.,1,-1,Rounds again indexed by n with distributions; total number is variable/indefinite.,0.4,1,Only choice per interaction is to give or not.,1,N/A,No endowed public good contributions.,1,N/R,Not framed as a public goods game.,1,N/A,No actual players or communication channel.,1,0,Only binary scores are potentially known; no payoff summaries.,0.8,0,No explicit identification of punishers to others beyond reputation scores.,0.9,0,No additional reward mechanism.,0.9,0,Horizon knowledge is not part of agent information in the model.,0.9,1,"As in other variants, refusal to help Bad or unknown players can result in reputational changes and future loss, interpreted as costly punishment.",0.9,N/R,No explicit token cost for punishment outside lost future benefits.,0.7,N/R,No quantified technology of punishment.,0.7,0,No distinct rewarding stage or costly reward action beyond help.,0.9,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,Reward technology is not specified.,1,N/R,Costs and benefits are per interaction; no stated endowment.,0.8,N/R,No empirical fraction of help is measured; model yields conditions for coexistence and cooperation theoretically.,1,N/R,Interactions are described symbolically with cost c; no numeric contribution amount.,1,N/R,Paper does not provide normalized efficiency values.,0.9,N/R,Group payoff is not numerically instantiated.,0.9,N/A,No human participants involved.,1,N/A,Age only appears as model index via rounds n.,1,N/A,No sample of human subjects.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,No human,Condition is purely mathematical.,1,"Variant of the indirect reciprocity game where discriminators are 'suspicious': they refuse to help when they lack information about the recipient's score. Information probability q_n is now decreasing with age (q_n < q_{n-1}). The per-round payoffs become P_x(n) = -c r + b r x + b q r^2 z, P_y(n) = b r x, and P_z(n) = -c r q_n g + b r x + b r^2 z q g q_{n-1}. An argument analogous to the trustful, increasing-q_n case shows that such a system also yields bistable dynamics between an all-defector equilibrium and a cooperative mixture of discriminating and undiscriminating altruists, illustrating that shrinking social networks with suspicion can still support stable cooperation.",Summarizes suspicious-discriminator equations and the statement that 'the same bistable dynamics holds if q_n is decreasing.',1
10.1006_obhd.1995.1015.md,Experiment 1 – Give dilemma – No sanction – Cooperative others,"Condition defined by 2 (dilemma) × 3 (sanction) × 2 (others’ behavior) design; this row is give frame, no sanction, cooperative others.",1,Dilemma frame: Give; Sanction system: None; Others’ behavior: Cooperative,Directly from the 2×3×2 factorial description and manipulation sections.,1,True,Human undergraduate participants played the resource dilemma via computers.,1,True,"Random assignment to conditions and controlled manipulations of dilemma, sanctions, and others’ behavior.",1,True,Conducted in a laboratory with IBM PS/2 computers at the university.,1,False,"Although others were simulated, focal subjects were humans; primary method is lab experiment.",1,False,No purely analytical or closed-form model is presented as the experiment itself.,1,8,"Subjects were told they interacted with 7 others, i.e., 8-person groups.",1,12,"Experiment always had 12 trials, though subjects were told 10–20.",1,1,On each trial subjects chose any integer from 0 to 25 to give/take; not binary.,1,0,All 25 tickets each trial started in the personal fund; nothing was pre-placed in the public fund.,1,N/R,"A replenishment rate (1–10%) and doubling of remaining fund are described, but per-unit marginal per capita return is not specified in a way that yields a single MPCR.",0.3,0,No mention of communication; interaction only via programmed feedback.,0.9,1,"Feedback included total contributed/taken by 7 others, size of public fund, and others’ aggregate personal funds.",1,0,No sanctions in this condition; thus no punished individual and no identities; treated as 0 because mechanism absent.,0.9,0,No sanctions in this condition; thus no rewarded individual and no identities; treated as 0 because mechanism absent.,0.9,0,Subjects were told there would be between 10 and 20 trials; exact remaining number was concealed.,0.9,0,This is the no-sanction control; no penalty fund.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this