10.1103_PhysRevE.84.046106.md,"Hard peer punishment limit (m=1), spatial PGG on lattice","Single simulation framework for hard peer punishment (m=1) across r, β, γ; no separate experimental treatments with humans.",1,"Synergy factor r ∈ {2.0, 3.5, 3.8}; punishment cost γ; punishment fine β; peer punishment cost multiplier m=1 (hard limit).",These parameters are systematically varied in Monte Carlo simulations to generate phase diagrams.,1,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice with strategies, not human participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment with human or field data; it is a computational model study.,1,False,No lab or field environment; purely simulated agents on a lattice.,1,True,They explicitly describe Monte Carlo simulations of strategy evolution on a square lattice.,1,False,"While some analytical reasoning is used, core results come from numerical Monte Carlo simulations, not closed-form analysis.",0.8,160000,"Players are on an L×L square lattice; L varies from 400 to 7200. A typical explicit size mentioned is L=400 in figures, giving 400×400=160000 players. Use this as representative size.",0.4,1000000,"They state t_r = t_s > 10^6 MCS in crucial cases; each MCS gives each player one update chance, analogous to 10^6 rounds.",0.6,1,Cooperators/punishers always contribute a fixed amount 1; defectors contribute 0. There is no choice of partial contributions; contribution is effectively all-or-nothing.,1,N/A,No human decision on starting allocation; contribution is fixed by strategy in the simulation.,1,0.4,"MPCR = r/G; group size G=5, and they use r values 2, 3.5, 3.8. For the base model MPCR depends on r; taking a representative example r=2 gives 2/5=0.4.",0.3,0,No communication or signaling between players is modeled; only imitation dynamics.,1,0,Agents update strategies based on direct payoff comparison with one neighbor; no summaries or aggregate info is mentioned.,1,0,No notion of identity or information about who punished whom; only payoffs matter.,1,0,"No reward mechanism is present, only punishment.",1,0,Simulated agents do not have information about total or remaining rounds; infinite-horizon imitation process.,1,1,"Both peer and pool punishers levy fines on defectors via parameters β, γ.",1,1,"Base punishment cost parameter is γ; in hard limit m=1, peer punishment cost per defector is γ, pool cost is γ flat. Use γ=1 as a representative example from text (e.g., Fig. 2 uses γ=1).",0.3,1,"Fine per defector from peer punishment is β m N_E^g; with m=1, marginal fine per punishing neighbor per defector is β. Use 1 as a normalized representative magnitude.",0.2,0,Model includes only punishments (peer and pool); no reward or positive sanction strategies.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,"For C, O, E strategies, contribution to public good is a fixed amount equal to 1; this serves as the effective per-game endowment.",0.9,N/R,"They report only stationary frequencies of strategies, not average contribution per player normalized by endowment.",0,N/R,"Only strategy frequencies and phase diagrams are reported, not aggregate contribution amounts.",0,N/R,No explicit reporting of efficiency as group payoff divided by maximum possible payoff.,0,N/R,Group payoff is defined via payoff equations but no aggregate or average values are tabulated.,0,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Agents are positions on a square lattice and updated by Monte Carlo rules, with no empirical setting.",1,"Square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; group size G=5 via overlapping von Neumann neighborhoods; four strategies (D, C, E=peer punisher, O=pool punisher); synergy factor r (1<r<5); peer punishment cost γ m N_D^g, pool cost γ; fines on defectors β m N_E^g (peer) and β if at least one O in group; update via pairwise comparison with Fermi rule with noise K=0.5; random sequential Monte Carlo dynamics.",Summarizes specified structural details of the spatial public goods game and evolutionary update rule.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.84.046106.md,"Weak peer punishment limit (m=1/(G−1)), spatial PGG on lattice","Single simulation framework for weak peer punishment (m=1/(G−1)) across r, β, γ; conditions differ only parametrically inside same simulation model.",1,"Synergy factor r ∈ {2.0, 3.5, 3.8}; punishment cost γ; punishment fine β; peer punishment cost and fine multiplier m=1/(G−1)=1/4 (weak limit).",These parameters are varied to generate phase diagrams in the weak peer punishment section.,1,False,"Numerical Monte Carlo simulations, not human subjects.",1,False,No empirical experimental protocol; purely computational.,1,False,Not conducted in any physical or online lab setting with people.,1,True,They explicitly run Monte Carlo simulations for different parameter regimes under weak peer punishment.,1,False,Results are shown via numerical phase diagrams not analytic solutions.,0.8,160000,"Same lattice-based setup as hard limit. Example size shown in snapshots is 400×400, so use 160000 as a representative player count.",0.4,1000000,Again they mention t_r = t_s > 10^6 MCS for crucial cases; use 10^6 as representative number of Monte Carlo steps.,0.6,1,"Each cooperator or punisher contributes a fixed 1, defectors 0; no gradation of contributions.",1,N/A,No explicit initial allocation choice by agents; contribution is fixed by strategy.,1,0.4,"As in the hard limit, MPCR = r/5; use r=2 as a representative example giving 0.4.",0.3,0,Model has no channel for communication; only local imitation.,1,0,Agents observe only neighbor’s payoff in pairwise comparison; no summaries displayed.,1,0,Identities are irrelevant; payoffs depend only on local configuration.,1,0,No rewards are modeled.,1,0,There is no notion that agents know the number of rounds; infinite horizon process.,1,1,Both peer and pool punishment mechanisms operate (with scaled m).,1,0.005,"In weak limit examples they discuss γ as low as 0.005 (e.g., Fig. 7). Use this explicit value as representative cost level.",0.8,0.25,"In weak limit m=1/(G−1)=1/4, so peer-punishment fine and cost per defector or per punisher are scaled by 0.25 relative to β, γ. Use 0.25 as representative per-unit magnitude.",0.9,0,Only punishment strategies are included.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Contribution amount is fixed to 1 for C, O, E types as in the general model.",0.9,N/R,They report only frequencies of strategies and qualitative phase descriptions.,0,N/R,No aggregate contribution amounts are provided.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency index is computed or graphed.,0,N/R,They do not tabulate average payoffs per group or per capita; only discuss phase structure.,0,N/A,No human participants involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Lattice-based agent simulations only.,1,"Same spatial public goods game structure as hard limit: square lattice with periodic boundary conditions, group size G=5, four strategies (D, C, E=peer punisher, O=pool punisher), synergy factor r, but peer punishment costs and fines are scaled by m=1/(G−1)=1/4. Payoffs use P_C^g = r(N_C^g+N_O^g+N_E^g+1)/G − 1, P_O^g = P_C^g − γ, P_E^g = P_C^g − γ m N_D^g, P_D^g = r(N_C^g+N_O^g+N_E^g)/G − β m N_E^g − β f(N_O^g). Strategy update uses Fermi rule w=1/(1+exp[(P_x−P_y)/K]) with K=0.5.",Summarizes the weak peer-punishment version of the same spatial PGG model.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2007.05.001.md,Intention treatment – threat,Condition where investor can choose to impose a conditional $4 sanction (punishment threat present) in the intention (investor-choice) treatment.,1,Sanction mechanism: investor-chosen conditional $4 payoff cut; Threat status: Threat; Back-transfer request fairness: both fair and unfair requests pooled in design (analyses often separate but mechanism identical).,Independent variable is the presence of an investor-chosen punishment option vs none; this row represents cases where the investor chose to threaten. Request size/fairness is varied but the core treatment distinction is the threat decision.,0.9,True,Laboratory experiment with 532 human subjects total; this treatment includes 149 investor–trustee pairs.,1,True,Random assignment of roles and conditions; controlled incentives; investigator-manipulated punishment option.,1,True,Conducted at the Interdisciplinary Center for Economic Science lab at George Mason University; on-site lab sessions.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all decisions by human participants.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model tested separately from experiment.,1,2,One investor (Actor 1) and one trustee (Actor 2) paired anonymously.,1,1,Design is explicitly one-shot; subjects participate only once in the decision problem.,1,1,Investors and trustees can choose any integer amount within ranges (0–10 for transfer; 0–3T for back-transfer); not all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment starts entirely in each participant’s private account; transfers are voluntary from private to other; no default contribution to a public/investment account.,1,N/A,"This is a trust/investment game, not a standard linear public goods game with MPCR; tripling of transfer is a bilateral trade, not a shared public fund.",1,0,Instructions forbid talking; no communication channel besides decisions.,1,0,Participants only see their own pair’s decisions and payoffs via decision sheets; no summaries of others’ outcomes are mentioned.,0.9,0,"Interactions are anonymous between rooms; only role and decisions are known, not personal identity.",0.9,0,There is no explicit reward mechanism; only punishment; thus no reward identity to show.,1,1,Instructions state clearly that subjects participate only once; thus they know the total number of rounds.,1,1,Investor may impose a conditional payoff cut of 4 E$ if back-transfer < requested; this is a punishment.,1,0,The 4 E$ fine does not accrue to the investor but to the experimenter’s research budget; investor does not pay any tokens to punish.,1,4,Trustee’s earnings reduced by $4 if condition triggered; 4 E$ fine per punishment act.,1,0,No explicit positive reward or bonus mechanism; only transfers and conditional fines.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Both actors receive an initial endowment of 10 E$.,1,N/R,"Paper reports mean investments and back-transfers in E$ and as percentages of tripled investment, but not normalized contribution rate relative to endowment as a single DV for this condition; values differ across subcases.",0.4,N/R,"For Intention treatment – threat, paper reports average investment and back-transfer separately for fair vs unfair requests (e.g., Table 1, Table 3) but not a single pooled mean for all threat cases.",0.4,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency or ratio of actual to maximal group payoff is provided.,0.1,N/R,"Earnings formulas are given, but average total payoffs per pair for this specific condition are not reported and cannot be cleanly inferred without joint distributions of transfers and back-transfers.",0.3,USA,"Subjects were recruited from George Mason University in Fairfax, VA, USA.",1,N/R,Only described as students from the general student population; no age statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0.1,University students,Subjects recruited from George Mason University's general student population.,0.9,On site,Subjects were physically present in lab rooms A and B at the ICES lab; not online.,1,"One-shot trust/investment game (gift-exchange variant): both players start with 10 E$, investor transfers T (0–10), experimenter triples T giving trustee 3T, investor requests desired back-transfer D (0–3T) and chooses whether to impose a conditional fine of 4 E$ on trustee if back-transfer < D. Trustee observes T, D, and whether fine is in effect, then chooses back-transfer BT (0–3T). Payoffs: Investor = 10 − T + BT; Trustee = 10 + 3T − BT − 4 (if fine chosen and BT < D, else 0). Threat is intentionally chosen by investor.",Summarized from Sections 3.1–3.2 and Appendix A instructions.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2007.05.001.md,Intention treatment – no threat,Condition in the intention treatment where investor chooses not to impose the conditional $4 sanction (no punishment threat).,1,Sanction mechanism: investor-chosen conditional $4 payoff cut; Threat status: No threat; Back-transfer request fairness: both fair and unfair requests pooled in design (analyses often separate but mechanism identical).,Independent variable is presence vs absence of investor-chosen punishment; this row represents the case where the investor selects no conditional payoff cut.,0.9,True,Same sessions and subject pool as intention–threat; subset where investor chose no sanction.,1,True,"Within the intention treatment design, the availability of punishment is experimental; investor’s actual choice defines the condition.",1,True,Conducted in George Mason University experimental lab with on-site participants.,1,False,All decisions made by human subjects.,1,False,No separate analytical-only component as a DV source.,1,2,One investor and one trustee per pair.,1,1,One-shot design; no repetition.,1,1,"Players may choose any integer amount in allowed ranges, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each participant’s endowment starts fully in their private account; transfers are voluntary.,1,N/A,Not a linear public good; transfer is tripled but not shared across a group.,1,0,No communication allowed per instructions.,1,0,No summary information about other pairs’ outcomes is provided.,0.9,0,"Anonymity across rooms; only decisions and roles known, not identity.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism at all.,1,1,Instructions explain explicitly that the decision problem occurs only once.,1,1,Sanction option exists in the environment even if the investor chooses not to apply it; presence of punishment option is part of the treatment.,1,0,"When used, the 4 E$ fine does not cost the investor; however, in this condition the investor has chosen not to use it; per definition we record the technical parameters of the mechanism.",1,4,Mechanism is a 4 E$ reduction in trustee’s earnings if applied and condition met; in this condition it is not activated but the tech remains 4.,1,0,No explicit rewards beyond voluntary back-transfers.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,10,Both actors start with 10 E$ endowments.,1,N/R,"Means for investments and back-transfers reported separately by fairness and threat in Tables 1 and 3, but not aggregated to a single contribution rate for all no-threat cases.",0.4,N/R,"Paper provides means for subsets (fair/unfair; sometimes by request size), not a single overall DV per condition.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,0.1,N/R,Average total payoff per pair under intention–no-threat not provided and cannot be derived without joint distributions.,0.3,USA,"Subjects from George Mason University in Virginia, USA.",1,N/R,Age distribution not reported.,0.2,N/R,Gender distribution not given.,0.1,University students,Subjects described as from the general student population of GMU.,0.9,On site,Run in physical lab rooms A and B at ICES.,1,"Same one-shot trust/investment game as in Intention treatment – threat, but here the investor has the option to impose a 4 E$ conditional fine yet chooses the 'No' option. Trustee observes T, D, and that no conditional payoff cut will be imposed regardless of back-transfer.","Derived from main design description and Appendix A, where investor can choose to impose or not impose the conditional payoff cut.",1
10.1016_j.geb.2007.05.001.md,Random treatment – payoff-cut (threat assigned),Condition in the random treatment where the trustee is randomly assigned to face the conditional 4 E$ payoff cut if back-transfer < requested amount.,1,Sanction mechanism: randomly assigned conditional $4 payoff cut; Threat status: Payoff-Cut (Yes sticker); Back-transfer request fairness: both fair and unfair requests pooled in design.,Key manipulated variable is whether the trustee is randomly assigned to the Payoff-Cut vs No Payoff-Cut; this row corresponds to those assigned to Payoff-Cut.,0.9,True,Human subjects (trustees and investors) in a lab experiment with random assignment to payoff-cut vs no payoff-cut.,1,True,"Experimenters randomly assign sanction condition independent of investor actions, allowing causal inference.",1,True,"Sessions run at ICES lab, George Mason University.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations used for DV.,1,False,Analytical predictions discussed but not used as a standalone analytical study entry.,1,2,Each match is an investor–trustee pair.,1,1,Explicitly one-shot; no repetition.,1,1,Any integer transfer/back-transfer within allowed ranges; not binary.,1,0,Participants start with entire endowment in private account; transfers are voluntary.,1,N/A,Not a linear public good; tripling applies to bilateral transfer only.,1,0,No communication allowed beyond decisions on sheets.,1,0,Only own pair’s decisions are revealed; no cross-pair summaries.,0.9,0,Anonymity maintained between rooms; sanction status known but not identity of sanctioning agent (since nature assigns it).,0.9,0,No reward option.,1,1,Participants told the game is played only once.,1,1,Half of trustees are randomly assigned to a conditional 4 E$ payoff-cut if they return less than requested.,1,0,Fine is deducted from trustee’s payoff only; no cost to investor or any other subject.,1,4,Conditional payoff cut equals 4 E$ when back-transfer < desired amount.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism beyond voluntary back-transfers.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism.,1,10,Investors and trustees each have an initial 10 E$ endowment.,1,N/R,"Mean investments and back-transfers with and without payoff-cut reported (e.g., Table 1, Table 3) but not condensed into a single normalized contribution rate across all payoff-cut cases.",0.4,N/R,Specific means given separately for fair vs unfair requests; overall contribution/back-transfer level for all payoff-cut cases not directly reported.,0.4,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (actual over maximum group payoff) provided.,0.1,N/R,"Although payoff formulas are given, no aggregated group payoffs for this condition are published; distributions of choices needed to compute them.",0.3,USA,Subjects recruited from George Mason University in the United States.,1,N/R,No age details provided.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,University students,Described as drawn from GMU’s general student population.,0.9,On site,Participants attended lab sessions in two rooms (A and B).,1,"One-shot trust/investment game where investors choose transfer T (0–10) and desired back-transfer D (0–3T). Trustees are randomly assigned to either Payoff-Cut or No Payoff-Cut before seeing T and D. In this condition, a Payoff-Cut sticker indicates that if BT < D, trustee’s payoff is reduced by 4 E$. Crucially, investors do not know that any trustee may face a payoff cut when they choose T and D. Trustees know that the assignment is random and that investors are unaware of sanctions at decision time; investors learn about the payoff-cut mechanism only after all decisions.",Summarized from Section 3.2 and Appendix B instructions describing the random treatment.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2007.05.001.md,Random treatment – no payoff-cut,Condition in the random treatment where the trustee is randomly assigned to the No Payoff-Cut status (no sanction regardless of back-transfer).,1,Sanction mechanism: randomly assigned conditional $4 payoff cut; Threat status: No Payoff-Cut (No sticker); Back-transfer request fairness: both fair and unfair requests present in design.,Independent variable is assignment to Payoff-Cut vs No Payoff-Cut; this row captures the latter condition.,0.9,True,Human-subject experiment with trustees randomly allocated to no-sanction status.,1,True,Randomization used to assign sanction condition; controlled lab setting.,1,True,Experiment carried out in-person at ICES lab.,1,False,No non-human simulation used.,1,False,No stand-alone analytical model run as experiment.,1,2,Investor–trustee pairs.,1,1,Subjects informed it is a one-shot decision.,1,1,"Action spaces allow any integer amounts within ranges, not just 0 or all.",1,0,Endowments start entirely private; contributions are voluntary transfers.,1,N/A,"Investment game, not a linear public good with MPCR parameter.",1,0,Instructions explicitly forbid talking.,1,0,No feedback about others’ outcomes is described.,0.9,0,"Even though there is a potential punishment mechanism in the other condition, here no punishment is possible and roles/identities are anonymous.",0.9,0,No reward option exists.,1,1,Subjects know it is a single-round decision problem.,1,1,"At the treatment level, the random sanction mechanism exists; this row captures the No Payoff-Cut realization, but the environment includes possible sanctions for other trustees.",1,0,"When in effect (other condition), trustee loses 4 E$; in this specific No Payoff-Cut realization no fine is ever applied, but technology is same overall.",1,4,Sanction magnitude is 4 E$ in the treatment; in this realization it is disabled by assignment.,1,0,No formal reward scheme.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each participant receives 10 E$ at start.,1,N/R,"Reported means (e.g., investment 6.2, BT 5.7 for fair requests; Table 1) apply to specific subsets and fairness classifications; no overall normalized contribution rate for No Payoff-Cut is summarized.",0.4,N/R,Paper provides subset means but not a single aggregate DV for all No Payoff-Cut cases.,0.4,N/R,No efficiency index is directly computed.,0.1,N/R,Average group payoffs are not reported separately for this condition and cannot be reconstructed without full joint distributions.,0.3,USA,All sessions at George Mason University (USA).,1,N/R,No age-related statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not specified.,0.1,University students,Drawn from general student population.,0.9,On site,Lab-based experiment with two physical rooms.,1,"Same trust/investment structure as Random treatment – payoff-cut, but the decision sheet for the trustee carries a 'No Payoff-Cut' sticker: regardless of whether BT < D, there is no fine. Investors remain unaware of the existence of any payoff-cut mechanism until after decisions are made, and trustees know this.",Summarized from Appendix B: trustees randomly assigned to 'No Payoff-Cut' receive a sticker specifying that the payoff-cut does not apply.,1
10.1038_srep00344.md,Simulation – spatial public goods game with pool punishment (baseline configuration),"Single agent-based spatial public goods model described in Methods; all reported phase diagrams and time series use this same structural setup (square lattice, k=4, contributions=1, beta=1, K=0.5). Treated as one simulation condition because variations are only in continuous parameters r, γ, δ within the same model.",0.9,"Synergy factor r, punishment fine γ, second-order punishment scaling δ; all varied over ranges (e.g., r on r–γ plane, γ on fixed-r slices, δ on δ–γ plane) with β=1.0 and K=0.5 fixed.",Paper explicitly varies r and γ in Fig. 1 for fixed δ and varies δ and γ in Fig. 3 for fixed r; β and K are stated as constants. No discrete experimental treatments beyond continuous parameter sweeps.,1,False,Model uses players on a lattice updated by Monte Carlo simulation; no human or animal subjects involved.,1,False,"This is a computational simulation study, not a controlled experiment with subjects.",1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Uses Monte Carlo simulations on L×L square lattice to obtain stationary fractions and phase diagrams.,1,True,"Includes analytical derivation of discontinuous transition line γ = β/δ by equating P_P and P_C, used in Fig. 3.",1,400,"System size L varies from 200 to 1600; number of players is L×L. This varies, so a single value cannot represent all runs.",0,1000000,Equilibration required up to 10^6 full MC steps depending on proximity to phase transitions; number of steps (rounds) varies by run.,0.3,1,"Cooperators and punishers contribute fixed amount 1 and defectors 0; no choice of continuous contribution level, but contribution decision is discrete between full (1) or nothing (0). Mapped as all-or-nothing allowed.",0.6,N/A,"No human subjects or default endowment allocation; contributions are model parameters, not choices from an endowment.",1,0.2,"MPCR = r/(group size). Group size is k+1=5; r varies, so MPCR is r/5. A single numeric value cannot represent all parameter sweeps.",0,N/A,No human interaction or communication in simulation.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human subjects; model agents do not observe explicit payoff summaries beyond payoffs used in update rule.,1,N/A,No identities or information presentation to human subjects in simulations.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism and no human subjects.,1,N/A,Agents are not informed about total number of MC steps; this is a simulation control parameter only.,1,1,Core of the model is pool-punishment with punishers contributing β and free-riders fined γ or δγ.,1,1,Each punisher contributes amount β to punishment pool; β is set to 1.0 throughout reported results.,1,1,"Each defector is punished with fine γ; each cooperator with fine δγ. One unit of cost β from a punisher conceptually supports fines, but mapping cost-to-damage per token is not one-to-one. Using γ as magnitude per punished player is also parameterized. No single tech ratio per token is specified.",0,0,Only punishment (pool-punishment) is modeled; no rewarding mechanism is included.,1,N/A,Rewarding not present in this model.,1,N/A,Rewarding not present in this model.,1,N/R,Standard spatial public goods game parametrization: players contribute 1 if cooperative; no explicit per-round endowment size (in tokens) is reported beyond that unit contribution.,0.4,N/R,"Study reports stationary fractions of strategy types (ρ_P, ρ_C, ρ_D) and punishment pool balance Σ, but not aggregate contribution rate normalized by endowment.",0.2,N/R,No explicit per-player or per-group contribution totals are tabulated; only qualitative outcomes and phase regions are shown.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is not explicitly computed or reported.,0.1,N/R,Group or population payoffs are not given numerically; only strategy frequencies and sustainability of punishment pool are analyzed.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; purely computational model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results generated by Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice with no empirical data.,1,"Spatial public goods game on L×L square lattice with periodic boundary conditions and group size 5 (k=4 neighbors plus self). Strategies: punishers (P), cooperators (C), defectors (D). P and C contribute 1 to public good; D contributes 0. Sum of contributions in each group is multiplied by synergy factor r and divided equally among group members. Punishers pay cost β to a global punishment pool. Defectors are fined γ; cooperators (second-order free-riders) are fined δγ with 0≤δ≤1. Punishment is institutional and non-local: fines and costs are applied population-wide, independent of local group composition. Evolutionary update uses pairwise comparison with Fermi rule with noise parameter K=0.5, under random sequential Monte Carlo updating.",Directly summarized from Methods and model description sections.,1
