10.1086_510602.md,Cooperation and punishment coevolution model – baseline condition,"Named to reflect the specific multilocus coevolutionary model of cooperation and punishment described in section 'Example: Coevolution of Cooperation and Punishment'; there is only one parameterization discussed, not multiple contrasting treatments.",0.78,"Genetic relatedness between partners R; costs and benefits of cooperation (c, b); cost and damage of punishment (a, d); intrinsic cost of being a punisher e; recombination rate between loci r","These parameters are explicitly varied in the conceptual analysis of when cooperation and punishment can invade or be stable (R, b, c, a, d, e, r), and they are the main determinants of outcomes across considered parameter ranges.",0.86,False,Model is purely theoretical with no human or other empirical subjects; only mathematical analysis of multilocus genetics.,1,False,No controlled experiment; it is an analytical population-genetic model.,1,False,No laboratory component; analysis is entirely theoretical.,1,False,Authors emphasize analytical multilocus recursions and invasion analysis; no simulations are described for this model.,0.9,True,Section explicitly derives recursions and invasion conditions with multilocus analytical methodology.,1,2,"Model specifies 'haploid individuals interacting in pairs in one-shot games'; one is player 1, the other player 2.",1,1,"Each pair 'play a one-shot, symmetrical game in each generation'; that is a single interaction per generation.",1,0,"Players choose discrete pure strategies (C vs D; P vs F), not continuous contributions; treat as non-all-or-nothing contribution game.",0.7,N/A,This is not a public goods contribution game with endowments; it is a binary cooperation/punishment game in a theoretical model with no human participants.,1,N/R,"No public-good multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; payoffs are additive cost/benefit parameters b, c, a, d, e instead.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No information feedback to human players; model uses genetically encoded strategies.,1,N/A,No identities or information structure are modeled; no human or agent-level observation stage.,1,N/A,"Model includes only punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,Agents are genes/strategies in a theoretical model; they do not view number of rounds.,1,1,Second-stage action for player 2 is punishing (P) vs forgiving (F) after defection by player 1; costly punishment is central to the model.,1,N/R,"Punisher pays cost a when punishing a defector, plus intrinsic cost e when in punisher role; but there is no single 'per-unit' punishment cost in token units as defined in schema.",0.6,N/R,"Damage to target is parameter d (cost to defector), but no discrete per-unit technology mapping from coins to loss is defined.",0.6,0,"Model features only punishment (cost a to punisher, damage d to defector), no positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,No monetary endowment or token budget per player is defined; only fitness payoffs around baseline 1.,1,N/R,"Outcome of interest is allele frequency change and invasion/stability of cooperation and punishment, not contribution rates in an experiment.",1,N/R,Model does not define a numeric contribution quantity; cooperation is a binary act with cost c.,1,N/R,"They derive conditions for evolutionary stability, not explicit efficiency ratios of payoffs to social optimum.",1,N/R,Payoff to a pair is implicit in fitness equation but no single summarized group payoff value is reported as a DV.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical analytical model without human or animal subjects.,1,"Two-player one-shot cooperation–punishment game in a haploid multilocus genetic model: player 1 chooses cooperate (C) or defect (D); cooperation costs c to self and gives benefit b to partner. If player 1 defects, player 2 chooses punish (P) or forgive (F); punishment costs a to punisher and d to defector, and punishers also bear intrinsic cost e in role 2. Social partners are identical by genotype with probability R (kin) and random otherwise; cooperation and punishment are controlled by separate biallelic loci i and j with possible recombination rate r between them. Dynamics analyzed via multilocus recursions and invasion conditions.",Direct summary of the structure and payoff parameters of the coevolutionary model as described in 'Example: Coevolution of Cooperation and Punishment'.,1
10.1086_510602.md,Synergistic cooperation model – additive plus synergy,Refers to the extension of the simple two-player cooperation model where a synergy term d is added when both players cooperate.,0.82,Presence and magnitude of synergistic payoff d in addition to cost c and benefit b; relatedness R between partners; cooperation allele frequency p,Section 'Extending Hamilton's Rule' introduces parameter d for synergy and examines conditions as a function of R and p.,0.84,False,Purely theoretical population-genetic model.,1,False,No experimental manipulation or data; only analytical derivations.,1,False,"No lab setting, only theory.",1,False,"They present algebraic results, not numerical simulations, for this model.",0.9,True,Hamilton's rule is extended analytically with synergy term d.,1,2,Same two-player interaction as in earlier simple cooperation model.,1,1,They again analyze a single one-shot game per pair.,1,0,"Binary cooperate vs defect strategies, not continuous contributions.",0.7,N/A,No explicit endowments or default contributions; theoretical model.,1,N/R,"No public goods MPCR; payoffs specified by b, c, d.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication stage.,1,N/A,No feedback or displays; evolutionary model.,1,N/A,"No punishment stage in this synergy extension, only cooperation.",1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,Not an implemented experiment.,1,0,"Model only modifies payoffs for CC via d, without adding any punishment action.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Synergy is an automatic payoff bonus, not a discretionary reward choice.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward choice or cost.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No endowment is defined; only fitness outcomes.,1,N/R,"Outcome is selection condition on cooperation allele, not measured contribution rate.",1,N/R,"Binary cooperation, no numeric contributed amount.",1,N/R,They do not compute efficiency ratios; focus is on Hamilton's rule form.,1,N/R,"Payoffs can be inferred (e.g., CC group payoff 2*(1 - c + b + d/2) conceptually), but no explicit summarized group payoff DV is reported.",0.6,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Theoretical model only.,1,"Two-player one-shot cooperation game where each individual chooses cooperate (C) or defect (D). Baseline additive payoffs: cooperation costs c to self and yields benefit b to partner. Extended with a synergistic payoff d: when both players cooperate, each receives an additional benefit d (modelled via term d * X1 * X2 in fitness). Relatedness between partners R and current cooperation frequency p enter the condition for increase, producing an extended Hamilton's rule R(b + d p) - c + d p + (1 - 2p) R d > 0, which can be rewritten in standard form RB - C > 0 with redefined B and C.",Summarizes the structure and payoffs of the synergy-extended model as described in 'Extending Hamilton's Rule'.,1
10.1086_510602.md,Interspecific mutualism model – evolution of relatedness between species,Labels the 'Example: The Evolution of Relatedness' model where two species form mutualistic pairs and relatedness between species evolves.,0.9,Group-level synergy parameter ε; group-level cooperation benefit s; within-group conflict parameter f; migration/dissociation probability m; cooperation allele frequency p (assumed equal in both species),"Model explicitly varies ε, s, f, and m in deriving conditions for cooperation to increase and equilibrium relatedness; p is treated in analysis of rarity.",0.9,False,Model concerns two interacting species in a purely theoretical multilocus framework.,1,False,No experimental manipulation; authors analyze recursions using levels-of-selection and multilocus transmission.,1,False,No laboratory or field experiment described.,1,False,"They derive analytical conditions and expressions for R, not simulations.",0.9,True,Closed-form conditions like f < s and (1 - m)[(1 + s)^2 + ε] > 1 and an expression for R are derived analytically.,1,2,"Social groups 'consist of pairs of individuals, with one of each pair being drawn from a species 1 and the other from a species 2'.",1,1,"Within each generation, a group-level interaction (reproduction and dispersal) happens once per pair.",0.9,0,Individuals are either cooperators (allele 1) or defectors (allele 0); no graded contribution level.,0.8,N/A,No endowments or contribution proportions for agents; this is a genetic cooperation/defection model across species.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; group reproduction depends nonlinearly on genotype via parameters s and ε.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,N/A,No interface or informational feedback; entities are genes.,1,N/A,No punishment is modeled; only cooperative vs defective types.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No human or agent decisions based on displayed horizons.,1,0,"There is no action that imposes a personal cost to harm partner beyond baseline payoff structure; within-group conflict f is automatic, not a chosen punishment.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment decision.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment decision.,1,0,"Cooperative benefits are automatic fitness consequences of genotype composition, not chosen rewards.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No notion of per-agent endowment; reproductive output is modeled via group and individual fitness.,1,N/R,Dependent variable is whether cooperation allele increases from rarity; not a measured contribution rate.,1,N/R,"Binary types, no continuous contribution amount.",1,N/R,They do not compute efficiency as payoff relative to optimum; they focus on conditions for evolution and the evolved relatedness R.,1,N/R,"Group reproductive output is specified for each genotype combination (DD:1; CD/DC:1+s; CC:(1+s)^2+ε), but the paper does not present a single aggregate DV summarizing group payoff across the population.",0.8,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Model involves species and genes only.,1,"Two-species mutualism model where each social group consists of one individual from species 1 and one from species 2. Each individual is either a cooperator (allele 1 at locus i) or defector (allele 0). Group-level reproductive output: DD groups produce 1 daughter group; CD or DC groups produce 1 + s daughter groups; CC groups produce (1 + s)^2 + ε daughter groups, where ε captures synergistic deviations from multiplicativity. Within groups, cooperators paired with defectors have fitness 1 + s - f, defectors paired with cooperators have fitness 1 + s + f, defectors paired with defectors have fitness 1, and cooperators paired with cooperators have fitness (1 + s)^2 + ε. Excess defectors from mixed groups replace missing cooperators to form DD groups. After reproduction, with probability m, groups dissociate and individuals re-pair at random; otherwise they remain associated, generating between-species genetic associations. Using multilocus and levels-of-selection analysis, authors derive that when f < s, cooperation is trivially selfish; for f > s, cooperation is favored when (1 - m)[(1 + s)^2 + ε] > 1, which can be written as a Hamilton's rule 1 + R B - C > 1 with an evolved between-species relatedness R = 1 - m[1 + (1 + s - f)/(s + s^2 + ε + f)].","Summarizes the interspecific mutualism model structure, genotype-dependent group and individual fitness, migration, and main analytical results as given in 'Example: The Evolution of Relatedness'.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2013.02.008.md,"Spatial public goods game with four strategies (C, D, Pc, Pu) on a square lattice","Paper describes a single modeling framework: a spatial public goods game with four strategies on a square lattice, varied only by parameter values r, β, and γ.",0.98,"Synergy factor r; punishment fine β; punishment cost γ; four available strategies: cooperators (C), defectors (D), conditional punishers (Pc), unconditional punishers (Pu). This row describes the general configuration across all explored (r, β, γ).","The only manipulated elements are r, β, γ and the presence of the four strategies; no distinct treatment labels beyond parameter sweeps.",0.98,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of strategies on a lattice; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with participants; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No lab or field setting; simulation only.,1,True,Explicitly described as Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice.,1,False,"Although payoffs are written analytically, results rely on numerical Monte Carlo simulations rather than closed-form analysis.",0.9,160000,"Players arranged on L×L square lattice; L varied from 400 to 3200. A concrete count is not fixed, but 400×400=160000 is the smallest explicitly used. Using this as a representative value since a single scalar is required.",0.3,1000000,"They use 10^5 to 10^7 Monte Carlo steps (MCS) depending on condition. Each MCS lets everyone update once on average. A single fixed number is not reported, but 10^6 MCS is mentioned as typical for slow invasions; used as an approximate representative.",0.2,1,"Each non-defector contributes a fixed amount 1; defectors contribute 0. Players are not choosing continuous contribution levels; contributions are fixed by strategy type, which corresponds to an all-or-nothing cooperative decision structure.",0.85,0,"Endowment allocation between private and public is not modeled; each round, contributors add 1 unit to the public good directly and defectors add 0. By convention, treat as all endowment initially private (0 in public) before the contribution decision.",0.3,0.76,"Public good payoff per contributing unit is r/G; they fix r values and G=5. Main phase diagrams use r=3.8 and r=3.5, giving MPCRs 0.76 and 0.7. Because a single scalar is required and r=3.8 is used first and extensively, report MPCR=r/G=3.8/5=0.76.",0.6,0,No communication or signaling between agents is modeled; only imitation dynamics based on payoffs.,1,0,"Agents have no explicit information display; strategy adoption probability depends only on own and neighbor payoff, not on group-level summaries.",0.95,0,Simulation does not model identities; only strategies and payoffs matter. No concept of revealing who punished whom.,1,0,"No rewarding mechanism is implemented, only punishment; thus no reward identities exist.",1,0,Agents are not modeled as knowing the number of Monte Carlo steps; horizon length is not an input to decision rules.,0.95,1,Both conditional and unconditional punishers impose fines on defectors with associated costs.,1,0.4,"Punishment cost parameter is γ, varied in phase diagrams. One specific value discussed in detail is γ=0.4 (e.g., Fig. 4 left panel, Fig. 6). A single scalar is required; use γ=0.4 as a representative setting.",0.5,1,"By parametrization, in a homogeneous group of unconditional punishers a single defector receives total fine β. Each punisher imposes β/(G−1); thus each ""unit"" of punishing action directly reduces the defector payoff by the fine applied. The paper does not define a discrete coin mechanism; set to 1 as a neutral scaling, since cost and fine are parameterized continuously.",0.2,0,Only punishment and its variants are modeled; no rewarding or positive incentives are implemented in this game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present; cost of reward is not applicable.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present; reward technology is not applicable.,1,1,"Each cooperator or punisher contributes a fixed amount equal to 1, described as 'without loss of generality'; effectively the per-round endowment for contribution decisions.",0.9,N/R,"They report stationary densities of strategies, not normalized contribution rates. While Pc, Pu, and C always contribute 1 and D contributes 0, the paper does not aggregate this as a normalized contribution rate in [0,1].",0.4,N/R,"Per-player contributions are structurally 1 for cooperative-type strategies and 0 for defectors, but no averaged raw contribution amount per group or per player is numerically reported as a dependent variable.",0.4,N/R,"They discuss 'efficiency of punishment' defined as cooperation level divided by average punishment cost (Fig. 6), but do not report payoff efficiency (group payoff relative to maximum) as defined in the schema.",0.3,N/R,"Group payoffs can be computed from formulas, but the paper presents only phase diagrams and strategy densities, not explicit group payoff numbers.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; spatial agents on a lattice only.,1,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,No human,Model uses agent-based Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice without human subjects.,1,"Square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; overlapping groups of size G=5 (each player in 5 groups). Strategies: C contributes 1, no punishment; D contributes 0, no punishment; unconditional punishers Pu contribute 1 and impose fine β/(G−1) per defector at cost γ/(G−1); conditional punishers Pc contribute 1 and impose fines and bear costs proportional to the total number of punishers (Pc and Pu) in the group, keeping fine-to-cost ratio equal to that of Pu. Total contributions in each group are multiplied by synergy factor r and divided equally among group members. Evolutionary dynamics: random asynchronous update, imitation with Fermi rule q = 1 / (1 + exp[(π_y − π_x)/K]) with K=0.5. System size L varied from 400 to 3200; relaxation 10^5–10^7 MCS. Main parameter sweeps in (β, γ) at r=3.8 and r=3.5; outcome measured as stationary densities of strategies and existence of mixed or absorbing phases.",Summarizes game definition and update rules as described in Methods and Results.,1
