10.1093_ei_cbi045.md,MP – Monetary Punishment treatment,"Paper defines three treatments: MP (monetary punishment), NP (nonmonetary punishment), BP (both). This row corresponds to MP.",1,"Sanctioning system: monetary punishment only (costly to punisher and punished, 0–10 points, nonlinear cost schedule, up to 100% income reduction); nonmonetary punishment unavailable",MP differs from NP and BP by allowing only monetary punishment with specified cost and impact on earnings.,1,True,Human subjects in lab sessions at University of Canterbury.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and fixed rules.,1,True,Conducted in a computer laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,Game-theoretic discussion but primary results are experimental.,1,4,Groups of size 4 are stated explicitly.,1,15,Each session consisted of 15 plays (periods) of the game; common knowledge.,1,1,Subjects chose any integer contribution between 0 and 20 ECU; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each subject endowed with 20 ECU and then decides how many to contribute; all endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,For every ECU contributed each of four members receives 0.4 ECU; marginal per capita return is 0.4.,1,0,No mention of free-form or structured communication; only feedback screens and rating/punishment interface.,0.9,1,"End-of-period summary shows own and total group contribution, own points received, cost of points allocated, and earnings.",1,0,Contributions and points displayed without subject identifiers and in changing order; cannot track individuals.,1,0,No reward mechanism in this treatment; thus identities for rewards do not apply.,1,1,Total number of periods (15) and identical rules each period were common knowledge.,1,1,Monetary punishment mechanism explicitly described for MP.,1,1,"Cost per punishment point is nonlinear (0,1,2,4,6,9,12,16,20,25,30 for 0–10 points). No single scalar; encode typical marginal cost at 1 point