10.1093_ei_cbi045.md,NP – Nonmonetary Punishment treatment,Paper labels treatment where punishment points do not affect earnings as NP (nonmonetary punishment).,1,"Sanctioning system: nonmonetary punishment only (disapproval points 0–10, costless to punisher and punished, purely expressive); monetary punishment unavailable",NP is identical to MP except points have no monetary cost or payoff effect and represent disapproval.,1,True,Human subjects in nine lab sessions; NP applied in three sessions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with fixed rules and randomized assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in university lab with computer terminals and z-Tree.,1,False,No computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,Game theory used only for interpretation; not a standalone analytical study.,1,4,Groups of four in all treatments.,1,15,Each session has 15 periods; NP sessions follow same structure.,1,1,Contribution decision is an integer between 0 and 20 ECUs.,1,0,Endowment starts fully private; then subjects choose contribution.,1,0.4,Same VCM production technology in all treatments: 0.4 ECU per contributed ECU to each group member.,1,0,"No communication beyond disapproval point allocation, which is structured and not free-form chat.",0.9,1,End-of-period summary screen in all treatments.,1,0,Information shown without subject identifiers; order changes each period.,1,0,No reward mechanism in NP.,1,1,Subjects knew there would be exactly 15 periods.,1,1,Nonmonetary disapproval points can be allocated to others.,1,0,Nonmonetary points do not affect earnings of punisher or punished; thus cost per point is zero.,1,0,Nonmonetary points are purely symbolic; they have no direct monetary effect on payoffs.,1,0,No ability to increase others’ earnings.,1,N/A,No rewards are present.,1,N/A,No rewards are present.,1,20,Same 20 ECU endowment per period as in MP.,1,0.44,Discussion states average contributions of 44% of endowment in NP.,0.9,8.8,44% of 20 ECU endowment = 0.44*20 = 8.8 ECU.,0.85,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported per treatment.,0,N/R,Figure 2 and Table 2 compare earnings across treatments but do not provide numeric averages by treatment.,0,New Zealand,"Same subject pool as other treatments: University of Canterbury in Christchurch, New Zealand.",0.95,N/R,Only specified as first-year undergraduates; no ages.,0.4,N/R,Gender not mentioned.,0,First-year undergraduate university students (economics and mathematics courses),Same recruitment description applies to all treatments.,1,On site,Lab sessions at the university.,1,"Partner matching over 15 periods; linear VCM; subjects observe anonymous group contributions and assign costless disapproval points that do not affect earnings; feedback each period on own contribution, group total, disapproval points, and earnings.",NP described as identical to MP except that points are nonmonetary and costless.,1
10.1093_ei_cbi045.md,BP – Both Punishments treatment,BP treatment is defined as having both monetary and nonmonetary sanctions available.,1,"Sanctioning system: both monetary punishment (costly to punisher and punished, 0–10 points, nonlinear cost, up to 100% income reduction) and nonmonetary punishment (costless disapproval points 0–10) simultaneously available",BP combines the mechanisms from MP and NP; both types of points can be allocated in second stage.,1,True,Human participants interact in lab setting under BP rules.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with treatment manipulation.,1,True,Game run in a computer lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No standalone numerical simulation study reported.,1,False,"Although equilibria are described, the main evidence is experimental.",1,4,Same group size of 4 in all treatments.,1,15,15 periods per session; BP sessions are included.,1,1,Contribution choice is any integer 0–20 ECU.,1,0,Endowment begins fully in private account before contribution choice.,1,0.4,Public good technology identical across treatments: 0.4 ECU per ECU contributed to each group member.,1,0,No explicit communication beyond structured punishment/nonmonetary rating interface.,0.9,1,Same end-of-period summary screen used in all treatments.,1,0,Contributions and punishments shown anonymously without identifiers; order randomized each period.,1,0,No reward option in BP; only two types of punishment.,1,1,Subjects knew total number of periods was 15.,1,1,Both monetary and nonmonetary punishment mechanisms are present.,1,1,"Monetary punishment has same nonlinear cost schedule as MP; nonmonetary is costless. Encode representative cost as 1 ECU per first point, acknowledging nonlinearity.",0.3,0.1,"Each monetary punishment point reduces first-stage earnings by 10%, up to 100%; nonmonetary points have no direct payoff effect.",1,0,"Design includes only punishment (monetary and nonmonetary), not rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in BP.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in BP.,1,20,Same 20 ECU endowment at start of each period.,1,0.78,Discussion states average contributions of 78% of endowment in BP.,0.9,15.6,78% of 20 ECU = 0.78*20 = 15.6 ECU.,0.85,N/R,Text compares average earnings but does not provide explicit efficiency ratios for BP.,0,N/R,Numerical group payoffs per period or on average are not tabulated by treatment.,0,New Zealand,Same recruitment location and subject pool across all treatments.,0.95,N/R,Only that participants were first-year undergraduates; no age statistics.,0.4,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0,First-year undergraduate university students (economics and mathematics courses),Same description of subject recruitment applies.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at University of Canterbury using computer terminals.,1,"Partner matching in fixed groups of 4 over 15 periods; linear VCM with MPCR 0.4; in stage 2 subjects can assign both monetary and nonmonetary punishment points simultaneously to each other group member; anonymity of identities; feedback each period on own contribution, group total, points received of each type, cost of monetary punishment allocated, and earnings.",BP defined as combining MP and NP rules; text details simultaneous allocation of both point types and same feedback structure.,1
10.1037_xge0000365.md,Experiment 1–4 & 6–7: Common altruistic vs. common selfish blocks (moral and punishment judgments),"Primary lab (Exp 1) and MTurk (Exp 2–4,6,7) task with identical PGG observer design; commonness manipulated in blocks (20 trials common altruism, 20 trials common selfishness).",0.9,Block-level commonness of observed behavior (altruistic common 80% / selfish rare 20% vs. selfish common 80% / altruistic rare 20%); trial-level degree of consensus (proportion of group choosing rated behavior).,"Design description: 2 (common behavior: altruistic/selfish) × 2 (rated behavior: altruistic/selfish); within each 20-trial block one behavior occurs with 80% frequency, plus probabilistic variation in degree of consensus.",0.98,True,"Human participants in lab (Exp 1) and MTurk (Exp 2–4,6,7) making ratings.",1,True,Commonness is exogenously manipulated in a controlled task.,1,False,Only Exp 1 is on-site lab; pooled analysis dominated by online MTurk experiments; at condition level better coded as not strictly lab.,0.6,False,"Human-subject experiments, not purely computational.",1,False,No closed-form analytical model as standalone condition; SIT is used only as fitted model.,1,4,Experiments 1–4 use groups of four PGG players; Experiments 6–7 use eight. This record captures the 4-player implementation explicitly described as primary design; 8-player version is similar but distinguished in a separate record.,0.8,40,Participants observed 40 one-shot PGG interactions as third-party observers.,1,1,"Observed players choose to keep everything (selfish) or invest all of a fixed sum (altruistic); no partial contributions allowed, i.e., all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,Endowment is initially in private control and can be invested into the common pool; standard one-shot PGG framing with no default in the public fund.,0.7,N/R,Payoff multiplier or marginal per capita return for the PGG is not numerically specified.,0,0,No mention of communication between PGG players; they are anonymous one-shot interactions.,0.95,0,Observers see choices but no explicit summaries of others’ earnings or past punishments.,0.8,0,Participants rate punishment for a focal player identified only by initials; no persistent identity or mapping across trials.,0.9,N/R,,0,0,No report that total number of observer trials or remaining trials is displayed to participants.,0.6,0,Participants only make hypothetical punishment ratings on a 1–9 scale; no actual payoff changes for observed players.,1,N/A,"No real punishment mechanism implemented, only ratings.",1,N/A,No realized deduction points in these experiments.,1,0,No rewarding or bonus to third parties for rewarding others is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,Text only mentions a 'fixed sum of money' for PGG players; no specific token or dollar amount reported.,0,N/R,Outcome variables are moral and punishment judgments; no contributions by participants or observed players are analyzed as DVs.,0,N/R,No numeric contribution levels reported beyond binary altruistic/selfish behavior.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency metric (group payoff/maximum) reported.,0,N/R,No numeric payoffs for groups are reported.,0,Mixed (Sweden lab; MTurk international and U.S.),"Exp 1: campus of Karolinska Institutet, Sweden. Exp 2,3,6: MTurk 'international'; remaining MTurk: U.S. citizens only.",0.8,Adults (approx. 27–42 years; experiment-specific means reported),"Each experiment lists mean ages (e.g., Exp 1: M=27.16, SD=6.0; Exp 2–7: means in 30s–40s).",0.9,Mixed (both women and men),"Gender breakdown given for each experiment (e.g., Exp 1: 23 women of 32; others similar).",1,N/R,No educational attainment reported beyond recruitment sources.,0,On site,"Exp 1 conducted in lab at Karolinska (on campus); while pooled with MTurk, the underlying design itself is a controlled experimental setting rather than a natural field or purely observational study.",0.6,"Third-party observers watch sequences of one-shot PGGs with 4 players choosing between 'Everyone' (altruistic invest) vs. 'Myself' (selfish keep). Observers rate morality (1=morally wrong, 9=morally right) and appropriate punishment (1=no reduction, 9=total reduction) of a randomly selected focal player each trial. Two 20-trial blocks: in one, altruistic acts are common (~80%) and selfish rare; in the other, selfish acts common and altruistic rare. Degree of consensus varies probabilistically within 80/20 constraints. Player identities are two-letter initials with no repetition; focal player location randomized across screen corners.",Summarized from the Experimental Design and Figure 1 description.,1
10.1037_xge0000365.md,"Experiment 6–7: 8-player PGG version (common altruistic vs. common selfish, moral and punishment judgments)",Experiments 6 and 7 replicate the main design but with 8 PGG players; same CIM manipulation with different group size.,0.9,Block-level commonness of observed behavior (altruistic common 80% / selfish rare 20% vs. selfish common 80% / altruistic rare 20%); trial-level degree of consensus in 8-player groups.,Text states Experiments 6 and 7 doubled player number from 4 to 8 while keeping degree of consensus identical to earlier experiments.,0.98,True,Human MTurk participants provided ratings.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of commonness within an experimental paradigm.,1,False,Experiments 6 and 7 were MTurk online studies.,1,False,"Human-subject online experiment, not purely simulated agents.",1,False,No closed-form analytical condition; SIT is only a fitted model.,1,8,Experiments 6 and 7 doubled number of PGG players from 4 to 8.,1,40,Design remains 40 one-shot PGG interactions as in the primary task.,0.9,1,Same binary choice structure (invest vs. keep) as earlier experiments.,0.95,0,Same one-shot PGG framing; endowment initially private until invested.,0.7,N/R,No per-capita return or multiplier values given.,0,0,No communication channel reported.,0.95,0,Observers see choices and frequencies but not earnings summaries.,0.8,0,"Initials are unique per trial and punishment is hypothetical, not linked across rounds.",0.9,N/R,,0,0,No statement that total or remaining trials are explicitly displayed.,0.6,0,Only hypothetical punishment ratings on a 1–9 scale; no payoff changes.,1,N/A,No actual deduction mechanism.,1,N/A,No realized punishment.,1,0,No rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment reported for these PGG players.,0,N/R,Participants do not contribute; outcome variables are moral and punishment ratings.,0,N/R,Altruistic vs selfish are categorical behaviors; no amounts reported.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff data.,0,International MTurk sample,"Because of an experimenter error, Experiments 2, 3, and 6 used MTurk's international option; Experiment 7 used U.S. citizens only. Condition-level country therefore mixed but predominantly MTurk.",0.7,"Adults (Exp 6 M=38.6, SD=10.85; Exp 7 M=42.31, SD=13.75)",Age statistics reported explicitly for Experiments 6 and 7.,1,Mixed (both women and men),Exp 6: 24 women of 37; Exp 7:16 women of 41 (1 unknown).,1,N/R,No educational attainment reported.,0,Online,Experiments 6 and 7 were conducted on Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Same third-party observer PGG paradigm as Experiments 1–4 but with 8 players per PGG. In each of two 20-trial blocks, one behavior ('Everyone' altruistic invest vs. 'Myself' selfish keep) is common at 80% and the other rare at 20%, with probabilistic variation in consensus. Observers rate moral status (1–9) and appropriate punishment (1–9) of a randomly selected focal player each trial.",Explicitly stated that Experiments 6 and 7 'doubled (from 4 to 8) the number of players in the PGG' while keeping consensus structure.,1
