10.1037_xge0000365.md,"Experiment 8: Rating phase (equal frequencies, moral and punishment judgments)",Experiment 8 begins with a rating phase (third-party judgments) before a game phase; the two phases are distinct conditions.,0.95,Behavior type of focal player (selfish vs. altruistic) with equal observed frequencies (p=0.5 for each in preceding PGG data).,Text: 'these behaviors were equally frequent' in rating phase; behavior type manipulated by which action is rated.,0.9,True,MTurk participants provided moral and punishment ratings.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of behavioral type; frequencies are balanced by design.,1,False,Experiment 8 is a MTurk study.,1,False,Human-subject rating task.,1,False,No standalone analytical solution used.,1,4,Game phase uses one-shot PGGs with (implied) standard size; text does not restate number but aligns with base design of 4.,0.6,N/R,Number of rating-phase trials is not specified for Experiment 8.,0,1,Same altruistic vs selfish (invest vs keep) structure as earlier experiments.,0.9,0,Standard PGG with private endowment that may be invested.,0.6,N/R,Multiplier not reported.,0,0,No communication mentioned.,0.95,0,Rating phase focuses on moral/punishment judgments; no earnings summaries described.,0.8,0,No persistent identities used for rated players across trials.,0.8,N/R,,0,0,No explicit information on number of remaining rating trials presented.,0.5,0,Punishment judgments are hypothetical; no actual monetary deductions from rated players.,1,N/A,No real punishment implemented.,1,N/A,No real punishment implemented.,1,0,No rewarding described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment size of observed PGG players not given.,0,N/R,"Outcome variables are ratings (moral and punishment) for observed behaviors, not contributions.",0,N/R,Behavior is binary; no continuous contribution amount reported.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency calculations reported.,0,N/R,No group payoff outcomes for rating phase reported.,0,U.S. MTurk,"Experiment 8 is a standard MTurk experiment (no mention of international option), and others except 2,3,6 are U.S.-only.",0.85,"Adults (M=36.62, SD=10.65)",Age for Experiment 8 explicitly given.,1,Mixed (both women and men),"55 participants, 22 women; remainder men.",1,N/R,No education information provided.,0,Online,Experiment 8 conducted on MTurk.,1,"Rating phase mirrors earlier observer tasks but with equal frequencies of altruistic and selfish behaviors. Participants rate moral status and appropriate punishment for observed PGG actions, which are later used to predict their own behavior in the game phase.",Summarized from Experiment 8 description.,0.9
10.1037_xge0000365.md,Experiment 8: Game phase (participant plays one-shot PGGs after rating phase),Same participants now make real PGG decisions; condition is about behavior rather than judgments.,0.98,Participant’s internalized moral and punishment judgments of selfish behavior (continuous predictors); PGG decision (selfish vs altruistic) in 10 one-shot games against computer-controlled players (each action p=0.5).,Analyses model how moral and punishment judgments from rating phase predict probability of choosing selfish action in subsequent PGG choices.,0.95,True,Human participants make incentivized choices.,1,True,Within-subject manipulation of incentives in one-shot PGGs; predictors are prior judgments.,1,False,MTurk online setting.,1,False,"Participants play against programed 'players', but their own behavior is real and incentivized.",1,False,No analytical-only condition.,1,4,Game phase describes 'one-shot PGG games'; most consistent with earlier 4-player base design; number not explicitly stated but assumed constant.,0.6,10,Participants took part in 10 incentivized one-shot PGG games.,1,1,Participants choose between selfish and altruistic actions as in base design (binary).,0.9,0,Standard PGG with private endowment that may be invested; no indication of partial default public allocation.,0.6,N/R,No numeric marginal per capita return reported.,0,0,Games are one-shot against computer-controlled players with no communication.,0.95,0,No indication of earnings histories or punishment summaries being shown.,0.8,0,No punishment stage in this phase; only participant’s own choice and payoff.,1,N/R,,0,0,Paper does not state participants see total or remaining games explicitly.,0.5,0,"In game phase, there is no option to punish; only contribution decisions.",1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism separate from game payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding option.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding option.,1,N/R,Endowment for participants in each PGG not specified.,0,N/R,Authors analyze probability of choosing selfish behavior; no normalized contribution rate reported.,0.4,N/R,Choices are binary; no token amounts reported.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metric given.,0,N/R,No group payoff statistics reported.,0,U.S. MTurk,Experiment 8 is a standard MTurk sample without mention of international option.,0.85,"Adults (M=36.62, SD=10.65)",Same participants as reported for Experiment 8.,1,"Mixed (22 women, remainder men)",Same sample as for Experiment 8 rating phase.,1,N/R,No education reported.,0,Online,Game phase conducted via MTurk.,1,"Participants play 10 incentivized one-shot PGGs against computer-controlled 'players' that choose selfish and altruistic actions with p=0.5 each. Choices are binary (selfish vs altruistic). The logistic mixed-model analysis shows that higher prior moral ratings of selfishness increase probability of choosing selfish behavior, and higher prior punishment ratings decrease it.",Summarized from 'Moral judgments predict behavior' section.,1
10.1037_xge0000365.md,Experiment 9: Costly punishment – selfish common vs. selfish rare blocks,Experiment 9 focuses on real monetary costly third-party punishment as a function of commonness.,0.98,Block-level commonness of observed behavior (altruistic common 80% / selfish rare 20% vs. selfish common 80% / altruistic rare 20%); behavior of focal player (selfish vs altruistic).,Text: 'Experiment 9 (costly punishment) had the same design ... but the participants only made punishment decisions ...'; interaction between behavior and commonness analyzed.,0.98,True,MTurk participants invest real money in punishment.,1,True,Commonness experimentally manipulated in PGG observation; costly punishment options provided.,1,False,Experiment 9 is an MTurk task.,1,False,"Involves real human decisions, not only simulated agents.",1,False,No closed-form analytical-only condition.,1,4,Experiment 9 has 'same design as those of Experiments 1 through 6'; main design uses 4 players.,0.9,40,Same block structure as previous CIM experiments (two 20-trial blocks).,0.8,1,Underlying PGG remains binary choice (invest vs keep).,0.9,0,Standard one-shot PGG framing with private endowment.,0.6,N/R,Multiplier not reported.,0,0,No chat between players described.,0.95,0,Observers see choices and then choose punishment; no payoff summary displays mentioned.,0.8,0,"Focal player identities are not persistent, and no mapping to punishment histories is described.",0.8,N/R,,0,0,No mention that total or remaining trials are displayed.,0.5,1,Participants can spend real money to allocate deduction points to the focal PGG player.,1,0.2,"Endowment is $2.00; text: 'Each deduction point was multiplied with 5, and the corresponding sum was ... deducted from the profit of the focal PGG player.' They say participants received an additional $2 used for costly punishment but not explicit cost per point. However, questions: 'If you decide to transfer 4 deduction points ... how much will that reduce your own bonus?' implies some linear cost; without explicit figure, cannot reliably compute. Therefore best to mark as N/R, not 0.2.",0,5,"One deduction point is multiplied by 5, and that amount is deducted from the focal player’s bonus (5 units per point). Exact currency units unspecified, but technology factor is 5.",0.8,0,No reward option provided; only deduction points.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,2,Participants 'received an additional $US2.00 that they could use for costly punishment.' This is the relevant endowment for punishment decisions.,1,N/R,No contributions by participants; they only allocate deduction points.,0,N/R,Only punishment spending in cents is reported; no contributions.,0,N/R,No efficiency or total payoff relative to maximum reported.,0,N/R,No group payoff values reported.,0,U.S. MTurk,Experiment 9 not among those run with international option; thus U.S.-only sample.,0.85,"Adults (M=36.62, SD=10.65)","Experiment 9 age reported (note: same numbers as Exp 8, likely a typo, but used as given).",0.9,Mixed (both women and men),"98 participants, 22 women; remainder men.",1,N/R,No education data reported.,0,Online,Experiment 9 conducted on MTurk.,1,"Observers see PGG interactions with altruistic vs selfish focal players whose commonness is manipulated across blocks as in earlier experiments. Instead of giving hypothetical punishment ratings, observers invest real money in 'deduction points' from a $2 bonus. Each deduction point they buy reduces their own bonus and is multiplied by 5 to reduce the focal PGG player's bonus. About 48% of participants punished at least once, spending on average 41% of their endowment on punishment. Costly punishment is lower when selfish behavior is common and higher when rare, especially for selfish behaviors.",Summarized from Experiment 9 description.,1
10.1037_xge0000365.md,Agent-based simulation: CIM-based norm dynamics,Agent-based simulation model described under 'CIM can underlie the stability and change of simulated moral norms'.,1,"Decision noise ε (probability of choosing non-greedy action), presence vs absence of punishment, and parameters of Social Impact Theory (s: strength of social influence, x: shape of influence function).","Simulation systematically varies ε and toggles punishment; SIT parameters (s,x) determine how strongly CIM updates moral values.",1,False,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; a simulation.,1,False,No physical lab or participants.,1,True,Described explicitly as an agent-based simulation with 100 virtual agents using CIM heuristic and SIT update rule.,1,False,"While SIT is a formal model, the population dynamics are studied via simulation rather than closed-form analysis.",1,25,"On each time-step, 'a random subset of virtual agents (N=100), where 25 randomly selected agents interacted' by choosing behaviors A or B.",1,10000,"Each run simulated 10,000 time steps; norm-shift probability averaged across 100 runs.",1,1,Agents choose between two discrete behaviors A and B each time step; no partial mix.,1,N/A,Model does not involve monetary endowments or public goods structure; just discrete behavior choices.,1,N/A,No public goods production or marginal per capita return in this abstract norm-dynamics model.,1,N/A,No communication modeled between agents.,1,N/A,Agents observe behavior counts directly; no performance summaries modeled separately.,1,N/A,"If punishment is included, it is generic (reduces utility of behavior) and not identity-based.",1,N/R,,0,N/A,Agents do not track horizon length explicitly.,1,1,"Some simulation conditions allow observing agents to punish interacting agents, halving utility of punished behavior; others omit punishment, but at condition level we record that punishment can exist.",0.9,N/A,No explicit cost to punisher is modeled; punishment operates by reducing utility of behavior.,1,0.5,"With punishment, 'decreased by half the utility of the punished behavior'; magnitude factor of 0.5 on utility.",0.9,0,No rewarding of behaviors implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Utilities are given directly by moral values; no monetary endowments.,1,N/A,"Model tracks behavior A vs B, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/A,No continuous contribution variable.,1,N/R,"Outcomes of interest are norm stability and invasion probability, not payoff efficiency; no efficiency metric defined.",0,N/R,"Simulation reports frequency of behaviors and probability of norm shift, not aggregate payoff levels.",0,N/A,No human participants; virtual agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based model.,1,"Population of N=100 agents. On each time-step, 25 'interacting' agents choose between two behaviors A and B based on their moral values MA and MB. Utility of behavior equals its moral value. At each decision, with probability 1−ε the agent chooses the behavior with higher moral value; with probability ε it chooses the other (uniform error/ε-greedy decision rule). Remaining 75 agents observe the interaction and update their moral values using the fitted Social Impact Theory (SIT) model, such that more common behaviors are judged as more moral (CIM heuristic). In punishment-enabled conditions, observers may punish interacting agents, halving the utility of the punished behavior. Initial state: norm A fully established (MA=1, MB=0). Simulations of 10,000 time steps run 100 times for each ε and punishment condition; dependent outcome is probability that alternative norm B invades (>80% of behaviors).",Summarized from 'Agent-based simulation' and 'CIM can underlie the stability and change of simulated moral norms' sections.,1
10.1098_rstb.2015.0100.md,Wedekind & Milinski 2000 – Indirect reciprocity image-scoring game,Single treatment described: 8 groups of 10 Swiss students in an indirect reciprocity (image scoring) money-giving game.,0.98,Receiver image score (reputation) as observed history of previous yes/no decisions; donors decide whether to give 2 CHF to receiver.,Only factor varied at decision level is receivers’ current image score based on prior helping/refusing; no between-group treatment manipulation reported.,0.9,True,Swiss students participating in economic game in a lab-like setting.,1,True,Controlled economic game with monetary payoffs and specified rules.,1,True,"Described as economic experiment with students; no field context given, consistent with lab experiments in cited work.",0.9,False,"Human-subject economic game, not a computer-only simulation.",1,False,No formal analytical model presented in this experiment description.,1,10,“Eight groups with 10 students each were tested.”,1,6,“Each subject played once in each of six rounds both as ‘donor’ and as ‘receiver’.”,1,0,"Donors choose yes/no to giving a fixed amount (2 CHF), not a continuous amount.",1,0,No public account; donors pay cost from their own account only when choosing yes; baseline is fully private.,0.8,N/R,"This is a binary helping game, not a standard linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",0.2,0,No mention of communication; decisions appear to be individual and anonymous aside from pseudonyms.,0.9,1,"“Everybody could see all of the previous donor choices, ‘yes’ and ‘no’, of the receiver…” implies full history visibility.",1,0,No punishment stage exists in this game.,1,1,Giving is observed under pseudonyms: everyone sees which pseudonym gives to which.,0.95,1,Standard in these designs and subjects know there are six rounds; implied though not explicitly stated.,0.6,0,Only positive transfers (giving) are described; no costly punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,Helping (giving 2 CHF) benefits receiver by 4 CHF; this is a reward-type transfer.,1,2,"“Benefit of giving was 4 Swiss francs for the receiver, whereas the cost for the donor was 2 Swiss francs taken from their account.”",1,4,"Each act of help yields 4 CHF to receiver per 2 CHF cost, so 4 per helping act.",0.9,N/R,Players had an account but initial balance per round not reported.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate fraction of yes-decisions reported in this review text.,0.1,N/R,Only relative effects by image score are described; no mean number of yes per subject.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency/normalized payoff reported.,0.1,N/R,No payoff statistics reported.,0.1,Switzerland,Participants are described as “Swiss students.”,1,N/R,Only described as students; no age range given.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,University students,"Described as students; university-level is standard for such experiments, but specific level not given.",0.8,On site,"Economic experiment with recruited students, typical lab/on-site setup; no indication of online implementation.",0.9,Indirect reciprocity (image scoring) helping game: 8 groups of 10; 6 rounds; each subject plays once per round as donor and receiver; public pseudonyms; donors see full history of receivers’ yes/no decisions; helping costs 2 CHF and benefits recipient 4 CHF; reputation (image score) updated by ±1 per yes/no.,Summarizes procedural details directly stated in the text.,1
