ct pool in these economic lab experiments.,0.9,On site,Laboratory-style setting implied; not described as online.,0.9,Indirect reciprocity helping game with 6 subjects + 1 instructed NO-player; 11 groups; 16 rounds. Little-information treatment: subjects receive only first-order info about prior actions (less detail about justification of NOs). Behaviour compared to predictions of image scoring (penalizing donors to NO-players) vs standing.,Condenses treatment-specific design from section 4 and Figure 2.,0.95
10.1098_rstb.2015.0100.md,"Milinski et al. 2002 – Alternating public goods and indirect reciprocity, IR–PG alternating treatment",Condition where groups alternate between IR and PG rounds until round 16 as described for Figure 3 (blue line).,0.95,"Game sequence: alternating rounds of indirect reciprocity (IR) and public goods (PG) until round 16; from round 17–20 PG only, with announcement or unannounced (subconditions).",Text describes treatment where rounds alternate IR and PG; also variation in announcement of PG-only continuation.,0.9,True,Human subjects in PG and IR games.,1,True,Controlled treatments of game sequence and reputation linkage.,1,True,Standard lab public goods/indirect reciprocity experiment.,0.9,False,Human participants used.,1,False,This part reports experimental results; modelling appears in later paper [70].,1,6,Figure 3 caption: “Percentage of cooperation (‘yes’) per group of six subjects in each round…”,1,20,"Text: “If rounds of the public goods game are alternated … until round 16… In rounds 17–20, groups of both treatments played the public goods game.”",1,0,PG contribution is binary (invest 1€ or 0) as per earlier description of typical PG in section 5; IR is yes/no giving.,0.9,0,"In PG, endowment is private and players may contribute 1€ or nothing; default all private.",0.9,0.5,"PG description: 4 subjects given endowment, can invest 1€; money in account is doubled and redistributed. For group size 4: multiplier 2/4=0.5 MPCR. Paper uses “typical public goods game”; this experiment also likely uses 4 or 6 players but specific multiplier here is not explicitly stated; using typical parameter is speculative.",0.3,0,No communication mentioned.,0.9,1,IR rounds rely on observed history of contributions across games; PG and IR interactions are tracked by pseudonyms across rounds.,0.9,0,No punishment stage in this particular design; only PG contributions and IR helping.,1,1,"IR game helping decisions are observed; paper notes that non-contributors in PG are less rewarded in next IR round, implying visible linking by pseudonym.",0.95,1,"Subjects are told when risk of reputation rounds is removed after round 16, indicating knowledge of structure; suggests known horizon.",0.8,0,No costly punishment described; discipline via withholding rewards in IR.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,"IR game allows donors to give money to recipients, evaluating reputation from PG.",1,N/R,Specific cost/benefit per IR transfer not reported in this summary.,0.2,N/R,Transfer multiplier for IR not specified.,0.2,N/R,PG per-round endowment and IR accounts not numerically specified.,0.2,N/R,Figure 3 only qualitatively described; actual percentages per round not numerically given in text.,0.2,N/R,No numeric contributions.,0.1,N/R,No normalized efficiency measures reported.,0.1,N/R,No payoff data.,0.1,Germany,Later acknowledgment thanks students from multiple German universities for these experiments.,0.8,N/R,Described only as students.,0.3,N/R,No gender info.,0.1,University students,"Explicitly students from universities in Bonn, Hamburg, Kiel, Köln, Münster mentioned later.",0.95,On site,Conducted as economic experiments with students at German universities; standard physical lab setting.,0.95,"Groups of 6 subjects. IR and PG rounds alternate up to round 16. Players use same pseudonym in both games; PG contribution decisions affect reputation used in following IR rounds. Defectors in PG are less often rewarded in subsequent IR rounds. After round 16, subjects are told that only PG rounds follow (risk to reputation removed) and contributions collapse. Figure 3 distinguishes treatment where IR and PG alternate from one where blocks differ, plus announcement vs no-announcement of final PG-only phase.",Synthesizes procedural description in section 5 and Figure 3 caption.,0.95
10.1098_rstb.2015.0100.md,"Semmann et al. 2004 – Strategic investment in reputation, Treatment 1 (T then NT)","Treatment 1: PG rounds 11–20 with transferable name (T), 21–25 with non-transferable name (NT), as described in Figure 4.",0.96,Name type (reputation transferability) in PG rounds: rounds 11–20 with transferable pseudonym (T) linking to IR game; 21–25 with non-transferable pseudonym (NT) not linked.,Key manipulation is whether PG decisions affect reputation in IR via transferable name; sequence T→NT defines Treatment 1.,0.96,True,Groups of human subjects in PG and IR games.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments with controlled manipulation of name types.,1,True,Standard lab economic game setup.,0.9,False,Human participants.,1,False,No closed-form model described here.,1,6,“10 groups with six subjects each…” in description of this experiment.,1,25,"Figure 4 describes rounds 1–25, with PG and IR interleaving and name-type changes after round 20.",1,0,PG and IR games use binary yes/no contributions consistent with earlier designs.,0.85,0,Contributions are from private endowment; default is no contribution.,0.8,N/R,The specific PG multiplier is not detailed in this section.,0.2,0,No communication is mentioned.,0.9,1,Reputation transfer requires that decisions with T-name are observable to others across games.,0.9,0,No costly punishment option in this strategic reputation experiment.,1,1,IR game rewards/helps are tied to observable pseudonyms; PG contributions under T-name influence how others reward.,0.9,1,Design description references specific rounds and sequences; participants likely informed.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism described; focus is on reputation rewards.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,IR game provides opportunities to reward others via helping; PG contributions indirectly rewarded in IR when using T-name.,1,N/R,Costs/benefits per IR donation not given here.,0.2,N/R,Transfer ratio of IR donations unspecified.,0.2,N/R,Endowments per round are not reported in summary.,0.2,N/R,Figure 4 qualitatively shows higher cooperation with T than NT but no explicit percentages.,0.2,N/R,No numeric contributions.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency not quantified.,0.1,N/R,No payoff values.,0.1,Germany,Same research group and universities listed later; likely German student sample.,0.8,N/R,"Not specified, beyond being students.",0.3,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0.1,University students,Described generally as student subjects from universities.,0.95,On site,Economic experiment with student groups in university labs.,0.95,"After training with mixed PG and IR rounds using both T and NT names, 10 groups of 6 subjects each in Treatment 1 played 10 consecutive PG rounds with transferable names (T, rounds 11–20), then 5 PG rounds with non-transferable names (NT, rounds 21–25). IR rounds always used transferable names. Cooperation in PG was significantly higher when playing under T than NT, demonstrating strategic investment in reputation when future IR benefits are at stake.",Summarizes Treatment 1 from section 6 and Figure 4.,0.96
10.1098_rstb.2015.0100.md,"Semmann et al. 2004 – Strategic investment in reputation, Treatment 2 (NT then T)","Treatment 2: PG rounds 11–20 with non-transferable name (NT), 21–25 with transferable name (T).",0.96,Name type in PG rounds: rounds 11–20 with non-transferable pseudonym (NT); 21–25 with transferable pseudonym (T).,Counterbalanced sequence to control for order effects; manipulation is whether PG decisions affect reputation in IR.,0.96,True,Same human-subject experiment as Treatment 1.,1,True,Controlled between-group treatment.,1,True,Lab economic experiment.,0.9,False,Human participants.,1,False,"Experimental condition, not analytical.",1,6,Same as Treatment 1: 10 groups with six subjects each.,1,25,Same total rounds across treatments.,1,0,Binary contribute/withhold format presumed as in related experiments.,0.85,0,No automatic contribution; players choose to invest from private endowment.,0.8,N/R,PG multiplier not given.,0.2,0,Communication not mentioned.,0.9,1,Reputation in IR depends on observed PG behaviour under T-name.,0.9,0,No punishment option.,1,1,Rewards in IR tied to pseudonyms; PG contributions with T-name visible.,0.9,1,Specific round numbers and switch points imply participants were informed.,0.8,0,No costly punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Indirect reciprocity game allows rewarding via helping.,1,N/R,Per-help cost/benefit not given.,0.2,N/R,IR transfer ratio unspecified.,0.2,N/R,Endowments not specified.,0.2,N/R,Data only shown graphically without numbers in review.,0.2,N/R,No numeric amounts.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency computations provided.,0.1,N/R,No payoff reported.,0.1,Germany,Same subject pool as Treatment 1.,0.8,N/R,Not provided.,0.3,N/R,Not provided.,0.1,University students,Student subject pool.,0.95,On site,Conducted as an economic experiment at universities.,0.95,"In Treatment 2, after identical initial 10 mixed PG/IR rounds, 10 groups played 10 consecutive PG rounds with NT-name (rounds 11–20, not linked 