10.1098_rstb.2015.0100.md,Semmann et al. 2005 – Reputation within vs outside own PG group,Experiment in §8 and Figure 5 testing value of reputation across groups.,0.9,Receiver’s group origin in prior PG game: same PG group as donor vs other PG group; both have observable PG histories.,Design combines two IR groups and two PG groups; key comparison is treatment of own vs other PG group members in subsequent IR.,0.9,True,Groups of students playing specified games.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of group composition across game types.,1,True,Lab-based economic experiment.,0.9,False,Human subjects involved.,1,False,Reports experimental data only.,1,12,“Semmann et al. [40] had groups of 12 students each playing simultaneously. They were informed that six of them would play together in one group for the pair (IR) game and the other six in another group…”,1,12,“There were six rounds of each type of game.” (6 IR + 6 PG).,1,0,IR: yes/no helping; PG: likely standard binary contribution of fixed amount.,0.85,0,PG is a standard public goods game with voluntary contributions from private endowments.,0.8,N/R,Multiplier for PG round is not given in this summary.,0.2,0,No communication described.,0.9,1,“Everybody could observe every interaction on the screen and the sequence of ‘yes’ and ‘no’ decisions of each potential receiver was displayed in the indirect reciprocity rounds.”,1,0,No costly punishment stage.,1,1,"IR donors see and act on receivers’ histories and group labels, implying visible identities (pseudonyms).",0.95,1,Tests ensured that subjects understood the design with six rounds of each type; implies known horizon.,0.8,0,No punishment described.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,IR helping confers benefits to receivers based on PG contributions and IR history.,1,N/R,Exact transfer amounts not reported here.,0.2,N/R,Multiplier for IR giving not specified.,0.2,N/R,Endowment size per PG or IR round not specified.,0.2,N/R,"Review notes that subjects treated own and foreign group members similarly, but no numeric rates given.",0.2,N/R,No numeric contributions.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency outcomes reported.,0.1,N/R,No payoff data provided.,0.1,Germany,Same research group as other German-lab experiments; not explicitly stated but inferred.,0.7,N/R,Described as students; no ages.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition.,0.1,University students,Identified as students at universities.,0.9,On site,Lab experiment with computerized display of all interactions.,0.95,"Each session: 12 students. For IR rounds, two fixed groups of 6 (group 1 and group 2) play pairwise helping game with observable histories. For PG rounds, two mixed groups (A and B) each consisting of 3 players from each IR group; this PG group composition is fixed across PG rounds. There are 6 IR and 6 PG rounds, alternating. Donors in IR rounds can observe whether the receiver’s ‘yes’ or ‘no’ in the previous PG round occurred in their own vs the other PG group. Result: subjects do not discriminate between own and foreign PG group members; reputation is valued regardless of group origin.",Compiled from §8 and Figure 5 description.,0.95
10.1098_rstb.2015.0100.md,"Milinski et al. 2002 – Indirect reciprocity with charitable donations, public condition",Condition where UNICEF donations are observable to group under pseudonyms.,0.95,Visibility of donations to UNICEF: donations made in public (observed by group with pseudonym).,Key manipulation is whether charitable giving is announced to group; this condition is the public one.,0.95,True,Human subjects playing IR game with real donations.,1,True,Controlled between-condition manipulation of donation visibility.,1,True,Lab economic experiment with students and real money donations.,0.9,False,Involves human participants.,1,False,No analytical model used as primary method.,1,6,“Groups of six subjects each played several rounds of the indirect reciprocity game.”,1,N/R,Only ‘several rounds’ mentioned; exact number not given in review.,0.2,0,"At each IR decision, donors choose yes/no to fixed transfer of DM 2.50; also yes/no to UNICEF donation of DM 2.50.",1,0,Baseline is not giving; all contributions require active yes choice.,0.9,N/R,This is not a standard PG game; per-donation multiplier to recipients and UNICEF is 4/2.5 but MPCR concept not used.,0.3,0,No mention of communication.,0.9,1,Group members can see others’ UNICEF donations and IR decisions under pseudonyms when public.,0.95,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,IR helping decisions and UNICEF donations are labeled with donor pseudonyms and visible.,0.95,0,Whether number of rounds displayed to subjects not discussed; cannot infer.,0.2,0,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,IR game allows donors to reward recipients; UNICEF donations influence reputation and thus likelihood of being rewarded.,1,2.5,"IR donations: “do you want to give DM 2.50 to this person?” UNICEF donations: same cost. However, specific cost/benefit for IR giving (to other individuals) is 2.5 cost; multiplier 4 to recipient; we use per-coin cost 2.5 DM.",0.8,4,"“If yes, the receiver obtained DM 4” for both IR and UNICEF donations, so reward magnitude is 4 DM.",0.9,N/R,"Subjects have accounts from which DM 2.50 is deducted per donation, but initial amounts not reported.",0.3,N/R,Text states that giving to UNICEF was rewarded in IR and that cooperation was higher in public than private condition but no numeric probabilities.,0.2,N/R,No numeric total donations provided.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff stats not provided.,0.1,Germany,Milinski is at a German institute; experiments later acknowledged as conducted with German university students.,0.8,N/R,Only described as subjects; ages not given.,0.3,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,University students,Standard subject pool in these experiments; implied by later acknowledgements.,0.9,On site,Economic experiment with real-world donation via lab context.,0.9,"Groups of 6 subjects play several rounds of an IR game. After each IR helping decision, players are asked whether to donate DM 2.50 to UNICEF (if yes, UNICEF gets DM 4). Donations to UNICEF are made in public: all group members see each donation with donor pseudonym, and total is actually forwarded to UNICEF with receipt shown later. Result: giving to UNICEF increases donor’s chance of being helped in subsequent IR rounds; cooperation (charitable giving) is high when donations are public.",Derived from §9 description.,0.95
