tion structure as public treatment except that information about individuals’ donations to UNICEF is not disclosed to other group members. As a result, giving to UNICEF is not rewarded in IR, and substantially less money is donated compared to public condition.",Summarizes control condition in §9.,0.95
10.1098_rstb.2015.0100.md,"Milinski et al. 2006 – Climate public goods game, anonymous treatment, little-informed",Condition with anonymous investments and no additional expert information.,0.9,Anonymity of climate contributions (anonymous) and information level about climate (little-informed: no additional expert info).,Two factors: anonymity vs pseudonymous visibility and presence/absence of expert climate information; this row is anonymous + little-informed.,0.9,True,Groups of six students invest in climate public goods and IR game.,1,True,Treatment structure with random assignment and monetary stakes.,1,True,Lab experiment at German institutes.,0.9,False,Human participants.,1,False,Not a model-based study.,1,6,“Groups of six students each to invest in a ‘climate public goods’ pool…”,1,N/R,Figure 6 shows multiple rounds but number not stated in review; original paper uses 10 rounds but not given here.,0.3,0,"Climate contributions likely chosen from discrete set (e.g., multiple units); at minimum, they choose how much to invest, more than simple yes/no; but granularity not specified; conservatively treat as any-amount.",0.4,0,Players have private accounts and voluntarily invest into climate pool.,0.8,N/R,"Money in climate pool is doubled and spent on newspaper advertisement, not redistributed to players; standard MPCR not applicable.",0.4,0,No communication channels discussed.,0.9,0,"In anonymous treatment, pseudonyms are not used for climate investments; others cannot link contributions to individuals; no summaries tied to IDs.",0.8,0,No punishment mechanism; discipline via IR rewards in non-anonymous condition only.,1,0,"In anonymous climate condition, others do not know who contributed how much to the climate pool; thus no IDs linked to climate rewards.",0.8,0,Not reported whether number of rounds was displayed.,0.2,0,No punishment stage in this climate PG setup.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,"In combined IR-climate design, contributions to climate can be rewarded in IR when observable; in anonymous condition, this channel is effectively disabled but IR game still has help rewards.",0.7,N/R,Transfers in IR game not detailed here.,0.2,N/R,Multiplier for IR transfers unspecified.,0.2,N/R,Per-round endowment for climate contributions not stated.,0.3,N/R,"Figure 6 shows money invested per group per round, but values not numerically provided in text.",0.2,N/R,No total contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metric computed; climate pool goes to external advertisement.,0.2,N/R,Group monetary payoffs not central and not reported.,0.2,Germany,Milinski et al. climate experiment done with German students (Max Planck Institute and German universities).,0.8,N/R,Students but no ages.,0.3,N/R,Not given.,0.1,University students,Stated as students; conducted at universities.,0.95,On site,Lab experiment combining IR and climate PG.,0.95,"Groups of 6 students decide how much money to invest into a climate public goods pool. Money in the pool is doubled and spent on a newspaper ad about climate and CO2 reduction, not redistributed. In anonymous treatment, climate investments cannot be linked to individual pseudonyms, so contributions cannot directly affect reputation in IR game. In little-informed condition, no extra expert climate information is provided. Figure 6 shows lowest investments in anonymous + little-informed combination.",Synthesized from §10 and Figure 6 description.,0.9
