10.1037_a0019861.md,Tryptophan depletion session – Ultimatum Game responder,One within-subject condition where participants completed the Ultimatum Game after receiving the tryptophan-depleting amino acid drink.,1,"Serotonin manipulation: acute tryptophan depletion (within-subject, compared to placebo); offer fairness (fair: 40–50%, medium: 27–33%, unfair: 18–22% of stake). This row refers to the depletion session.",Paper describes a crossover design with two treatments (tryptophan depletion vs placebo) and varying offer fairness levels. This row encodes the depletion treatment with the fairness structure.,1,True,Study uses 22 healthy human volunteers who play the UG and complete tasks.,1,True,"Participants were randomly assigned to treatment order in a double-blind, counterbalanced experimental manipulation of serotonin.",1,True,Tasks were administered via computer in a controlled clinical/research facility (Addenbrooke's Hospital / university lab).,0.9,False,No computational or agent-based simulations; only human behavioral tasks.,1,False,"Paper reports empirical/anova/correlation analyses, not a formal analytical model.",1,2,Ultimatum Game involves a proposer and a responder; participants always played as responder against (ostensible) human proposers.,1,48,"“On each session, participants played 48 games, each with a different proposer.”",1,1,"UG responders choose accept or reject; there is no continuous contribution choice. According to the coding rule, 1 indicates players can contribute any amount; here that construct does not apply but game is not an all-or-nothing contribution mechanism in a public goods sense. Set to 1 as not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions in a contribution framework.",0.3,N/R,Ultimatum Game does not involve a contribution endowment into a public account; this concept is not defined here.,0.1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined in a simple UG.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat between players; interaction is limited to offers and responses.,0.95,0,Participants see only single offers per trial with stake and proposer photo; no summaries of others’ earnings/punishments reported.,0.9,1,Responder sees a photograph of each proposer before each offer; thus the identity of the potential target of punishment (rejection) is revealed.,1,0,There is no explicit rewarding mechanism distinct from accepting standard UG offers; no identities associated with rewards are discussed.,0.9,0,The text does not state that the total number of 48 rounds or remaining rounds was displayed on screen.,0.5,1,Rejection of unfair offers is explicitly treated as 'costly' or 'altruistic' punishment of the proposer.,1,N/R,"UG rejection cost equals forgone payoff, but specific token values per punishment action are not detailed.",0.5,N/R,"There is no parameterized punishment technology (e.g., 1-to-3 fee-to-fine); rejection simply sets both players’ payoffs to zero.",1,0,No costly peer reward mechanism is available; only accept/reject.,1,N/A,No reward stage exists.,1,N/A,No reward stage exists.,1,N/R,In UG the responder has no fixed personal endowment; they receive stakes offered by proposers. Specific stake sizes are not listed numerically.,0.4,N/R,"Primary behavioral DV is proportion of offers rejected by fairness/size, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts are defined; only binary accept/reject responses.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric (payoff as fraction of max) is reported.,1,N/R,Actual monetary earnings or group totals from UG are not reported.,0.5,N/R,"Participants were tested in Cambridge and other institutions are mentioned, but subjects’ nationality is not specified.",0.3,Mean age 25.7 years; individual range not reported,"“Twenty-two healthy volunteers (7 men, mean age = 25.7) participated.”",1,"7 men, 15 women",Paper reports only number of men; infer women as 22-7=15.,0.95,N/R,"Educational background (e.g., students vs general population) is not described.",0.2,On site,Sessions conducted at the Wellcome Trust Clinical Research Facility / university lab with blood draws and in-person procedures.,1,"One-shot Ultimatum Games with 48 distinct ostensible proposers per session; offers categorized as fair (40–50% of stake), medium (27–33%), unfair (18–22%). Stakes varied so that identical monetary amounts could be fair or unfair depending on total stake. Participants were told payoffs from 2 randomly selected trials would be paid; decisions were accept/reject while viewing the offer for 3000 ms after seeing proposer photograph and stake.",Summarized from the detailed UG task description.,1
