 women",Same sample as depletion condition.,0.95,N/R,No education details.,0.2,On site,Same clinical/lab facility procedures as depletion session.,1,Same one-shot UG structure as depletion: 48 trials with varying stake sizes and fairness categories; subjects told they would be paid based on 2 randomly selected trials; proposer identities represented by photos were randomly matched to offers; offer structure identical across placebo and depletion sessions with randomized order.,Summarized from Methods; identical game except serotonin manipulation.,1
10.1098_rspb.2012.2498.md,Analytical model – Type A costly punishment,Paper defines a Type A model introducing a punisher strategy with costly punishment in a public goods game; treated as one analytical condition because parameters are symbolic and the analysis is general.,0.9,"Presence of costly punisher strategy P in addition to cooperator C and defector D; punishment fine per punisher β and cost per defector γ, with group size n, multiplication factor r, and contribution cost c as parameters.","Model varies presence and frequency of punishers versus defectors and cooperators and studies outcomes as functions of β, γ, n, r, c.",0.9,False,Model is purely theoretical and game-theoretical; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; only mathematical analysis.,1,False,No lab setting; analytical theory only.,1,False,Section here is analytical; simulations are mentioned only in supplement and not detailed in the main text provided.,0.7,True,Uses replicator equations and closed-form payoff expressions to analyze dynamics.,1,5,"They explicitly use group size n=5 in the illustrative parameter set for figures (e.g., figure 1: 'Parameters: group size n=5').",0.9,1,One-shot public goods interaction per generation in replicator dynamics; no repeated rounds are modeled.,0.9,1,Each player has option to contribute cost c or nothing; no indication that contributions can be fractional between 0 and c.,0.8,N/A,No human subjects or user interface; concept of default allocation not applicable.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/n. For the parameter example, r=3 and n=5 (figure 1: 'n=5, r=3, c=1'), so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.9,N/A,No human players or communication stage in analytical model.,1,0,No informational feedback structure is modeled; only payoffs enter replicator dynamics.,0.9,N/A,No identities or observability assumptions about who punished whom are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,0,"Infinite-horizon evolutionary dynamics, not discrete known rounds; no such information.",0.9,1,Type A adds a punisher who imposes fines β on defectors at cost γ.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost per defector is parameter γ, kept symbolic; no specific numeric value is fixed in main text besides illustrative inequalities.",0.7,N/R,"Fine on a defector per punisher is β; β is symbolic, no single numeric value specified in the formal model (though example β=0.5 appears in a figure caption not fully parameterized for punishment technology definition).",0.7,0,"Only punishment (costly punisher) is modeled, no reward or positive incentives.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Model only specifies a contribution cost c; does not specify total endowment or maximum feasible contribution beyond c.,0.6,N/R,Outcomes are strategy frequencies and payoffs; average contributions can be inferred conceptually but not numerically specified.,0.6,N/R,No numeric contribution levels beyond symbolic c and frequencies are given.,0.6,N/R,Paper discusses cooperative versus defecting equilibria but does not provide normalized efficiency values.,0.6,N/R,Payoff expressions per strategy are given symbolically; no single aggregate numeric group payoff is reported.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical evolutionary game model without human subjects.,1,"Standard linear public goods game with group size n≥2 (example n=5), contribution cost c>0, multiplication factor r>1 with r<n; strategies are cooperators (contribute c), defectors (contribute 0), and punishers (contribute c and impose costly punishment β,γ). Dynamics governed by replicator equation in an infinite well-mixed population.",Summarized from Section 2 description of the baseline public goods game and Type A costly punishment extension.,1
10.1098_rspb.2012.2498.md,"Analytical model – Type B social exclusion (perfect exclusion, general parameters)","Paper defines a Type B model where a third strategy is an excluder E with social exclusion, initially analyzed under perfect exclusion β̄=1, with γ̄ symbolic.",0.9,"Presence of excluder strategy E (social exclusion) in addition to cooperator C and defector D; exclusion success probability β̄=1 and exclusion cost per defector γ̄, with group size n, multiplication factor r, and contribution cost c as parameters.",Model varies frequency of excluders and cost γ̄ under perfect exclusion to study evolutionary dynamics.,0.9,False,Game-theoretical replicator analysis without human data.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with human subjects; purely analytical.,1,False,Not a laboratory study.,1,False,Main text section is analytical; simulations mentioned in supplement not specified as separate conditions here.,0.7,True,Uses explicit payoff functions and replicator dynamics to derive conditions and equilibria.,1,5,"Example parameter set reuses n=5, r=3, c=1 as in figure 2 caption referencing 'parameters are as in figure 1a'.",0.9,1,Single public goods interaction per generation in the evolutionary model.,0.9,1,"As in baseline game, players contribute c or 0; no partial contributions are described.",0.8,N/A,No explicit endowments or default allocation for human players.,1,0.6,MPCR = r/n with r=3 and n=5 in the parameter example.,0.9,N/A,No human communication component.,1,0,No UI or feedback mechanism beyond payoffs is modeled.,0.9,N/A,No explicit identity revelation of excluders; model assumes only payoffs and exclusion effects.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,0,"Infinite evolutionary time, not finite known rounds.",0.9,1,Social exclusion is a form of punishment where excluders penalize defectors by denying them benefits.,1,N/R,Cost per defector is parameter γ̄; symbolic with no specific numeric value in the main text within this general analysis.,0.7,N/R,"Exclusion probability β̄=1 is stated, but the model does not specify a linear 'units per coin' technology as in standard punishment cost/impact terms; β̄ is a success probability parameter.",0.6,0,"Only exclusion of defectors, no positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Only cost of contributing c is specified; total possible endowment per player is not given.,0.6,N/R,"Results are in terms of strategy frequencies and stability, not numeric contribution rates.",0.6,N/R,No numeric contributions beyond symbolic c and strategic composition.,0.6,N/R,"While they discuss achieving group optimum c(r−1), no normalized efficiency metric is reported.",0.6,N/R,Group payoffs are described symbolically; no single numeric group payoff is presented.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game theory only.,1,"Public goods game with cooperators (contribute c), defectors (contribute 0), and excluders E (contribute c and attempt to exclude defectors). Under perfect exclusion β̄=1, excluded defectors receive zero benefit, remaining contributors share multiplied contributions r*c among non-excluded players. Payoffs P_C, P_D, P_E given explicitly in equations (2.4a–c); dynamics analyze conditions for excluder dominance or bi-stability.",Summarized from Section 2(b) description of Type B social exclusion with β̄=1.,1
