es (0.03, 0.13, 0.18, 0.28) are used across panels, but there is no single punishment cost per 'condition' asked at this row level; keeping as not reported numerically for a single setting.",0.7,N/R,"β̄=0.5 is the success probability of exclusion; not framed as a linear 'effect per token' technology, and no further tech parameter is given.",0.7,0,No positive incentive or reward mechanism included.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Total endowment per agent is not specified; only contribution cost c=1 is given.,0.6,N/R,Paper examines stability of cooperative regimes but gives no numeric contribution fractions under these β̄ and γ̄ values.,0.6,N/R,No explicit numeric average contribution amounts reported.,0.6,N/R,"Although it discusses convergence to full cooperation and group optimum, does not quantify efficiency values for β̄=0.5 cases.",0.6,N/R,No aggregate payoffs under these parameters are numerically reported.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Parameter exploration within an analytical evolutionary game model.,1,"Same public goods structure as other Type B models (n=5, r=3, c=1) with excluders, cooperators, and defectors. Here the exclusion probability is intermediate (β̄=0.5). Figure 3 analyzes how varying γ̄ (0.03, 0.13, 0.18, 0.28) affects the phase portrait, existence and stability of equilibria R, Q, and segments EK0 and K1K0.",Summarized from the caption and discussion surrounding figure 3 on intermediate social exclusion.,0.95
