10.1523_jneurosci.2761-12.2013.md,Third-party punishment game – Placebo,Third-party punishment task completed outside the scanner under placebo treatment.,1,Serotonin level: Placebo; role: Third-party observer; punishment option: costly reduction of proposer payoff at 1:3 rate,Within-subject treatment factor ATD vs placebo in third-party game; this row captures placebo session behavior.,1,True,Human volunteers performed third-party punishment decisions.,1,True,Designed incentive-compatible game with controlled offers and randomized payment of one round.,1,True,Task performed at the lab after fMRI scanning; not online/field.,1,False,No computer-only simulation.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model as a separate study.,1,3,"Each round involves proposer, receiver, and the subject as third-party punisher.",1,16,“Participants completed 16 rounds of the third-party punishment game.”,1,1,"Participants can pay any amount up to 50p to punish, but schema’s allOrNothing is defined for contribution discreteness. Here action is continuous up to cap, so they can choose any amount; that corresponds to 1 (not all-or-nothing).",0.7,0,Subjects are endowed with £5 each round; endowment starts fully in their private account until they choose to spend up to 50p on punishment.,0.9,N/R,There is a punishment multiplier (1:3) but not a marginal per capita return from a public investment.,0.9,0,No communication between players; only observation of offers.,1,0,"Subjects see photographs and current proposer’s offer, not accumulated summaries.",1,1,Photographs of proposer and receiver shown; punisher sees the identity of the punished proposer.,1,0,No option to increase others’ payoffs; only punishment.,1,0,No description that total or remaining number of rounds is displayed.,0.8,1,“Participants were endowed with £5 and could pay up to 50p to reduce the proposer’s payoff at a 1:3 ratio.”,1,1,"Each unit of currency the subject spends (e.g., 1p) is lost to them; cost per unit of punishment spending is 1.",0.9,3,"Punishment technology: 1:3 ratio, so each unit spent reduces proposer’s payoff by 3 units.",1,0,No opportunity to pay to increase proposer or receiver payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,5,"“On each trial, participants were endowed with £5 and could pay up to 50p…”",1,N/R,"Key DV is amount spent on punishment, not a contribution to a public project; normalized rates not reported.",1,N/R,They report that the dependent measure is ‘the amount of money paid to reduce the proposer’s payoff’ but do not give numeric means per fairness level or treatment.,0.4,N/R,They do not report overall efficiency or payoff ratios.,1,N/R,"No aggregate payoffs for proposer, receiver, and punisher reported.",1,United Kingdom,Participants are the same Cambridge-based sample.,0.8,"Mean 25.1 years (SD 3.2), adults",Same sample.,1,"17 female, 13 male (initial sample); 28 analyzed",Underlying sample same.,0.9,N/R,No education details provided.,1,On site,"Task performed at the research facility after scanning, not online.",1,"16 one-shot third-party punishment games per session. Each round: subject sees photos of proposer and receiver (2000 ms), then sees proposer’s offer (10%, 20%, 30%, or 50% of stake; 4 rounds of each) to the receiver and chooses how much (0–50p) of a £5 endowment to spend to reduce proposer’s payoff at 1:3. One round randomly selected for payment to subject, proposer, and receiver. Under placebo, participants pay more to punish as offers become more unfair.",From ‘Third-party punishment task’ and ‘Behavior: ATD and third-party punishment’ sections.,1
10.1523_jneurosci.2761-12.2013.md,Third-party punishment game – ATD,Third-party punishment task under acute tryptophan depletion.,1,Serotonin level: ATD; role: Third-party observer; punishment option: costly reduction of proposer payoff at 1:3 rate,Same third-party game as placebo but after ATD drink.,1,True,Human volunteers decide how much to punish under ATD.,1,True,Within-subject pharmacological manipulation and controlled task.,1,True,"Performed at research facility, outside scanner, after ATD session.",1,False,No simulation-only study.,1,False,Data-driven; not purely analytical.,1,3,"Same triadic structure: proposer, receiver, and third-party punisher.",1,16,“Participants completed 16 rounds of the third-party punishment game”; same under ATD.,1,1,Subjects can choose any amount up to 50p to punish (continuous within cap); coded as 1 for non-all-or-nothing according to schema.,0.7,0,Entire £5 endowment starts in subject’s private account before punishment decision.,0.9,N/R,No MPCR in the sense of public good; punishment multiplier only.,0.9,0,No communication channel.,1,0,Only current round’s proposer and receiver photos and offer are displayed.,1,1,"Punisher sees proposer’s photograph, thus identity known.",1,0,No rewarding action is available.,1,0,No mention of displaying total or remaining rounds.,0.8,1,Subjects may spend part of endowment to reduce proposer payoff (1:3).,1,1,Cost per unit of punishing spend is 1 unit of own payoff.,0.9,3,Each unit spent reduces proposer payoff by 3 units (1:3 ratio).,1,0,No mechanism to pay to increase others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,Reward absent.,1,N/A,Reward absent.,1,5,Each round: “participants were endowed with £5 and could pay up to 50p…”; same under ATD.,1,N/R,DV is amount spent to punish; normalized rates not reported.,1,N/R,"Authors provide qualitative trends (e.g., punishment decreases with fairness; ATD tends to decrease punishment of unfair offers) but no numeric means.",0.4,N/R,No efficiency statistics reported.,1,N/R,Joint payoff outcomes across three players not summarized numerically.,1,United Kingdom,Same UK sample.,0.8,"Mean 25.1 years (SD 3.2), adults",Same participants.,1,"17 female, 13 male (initial sample); 28 analyzed",Underlying sample same.,0.9,N/R,Education not provided.,1,On site,Same lab setting at University of Cambridge.,1,"Same 16-round third-party punishment game as under placebo, with identical offer distribution (10%, 20%, 30%, 50% of stake). Under ATD, participants tended to spend less to punish unfair, but not fair, behavior (fairness-by-treatment interaction F(1,27)=2.709, p=0.050), with no RT effects.",From ‘Behavior: ATD and third-party punishment’ section.,1
10.1073_pnas.1115219109.md,Compulsory public good game with institutional rewarding or punishing (analytical and numerical model),"Paper presents a single prototypical compulsory public good game analyzed with two institutional incentive types (rewarding vs punishing) as part of one formal model, not split into distinct parameterized experimental treatments with human subjects.",0.82,Type and size of institutional incentive in a compulsory public good game: rewarding vs punishing; per capita incentive I varied with main thresholds I_- = c/n and I_+ = c.,"Model explicitly varies institutional incentive regime (reward vs punishment) and per capita incentive I, analyzing outcomes across ranges (I < I_-, I_- < I < I_+, I > I_+).",0.9,False,No human subjects; study is evolutionary game-theoretic with replicator dynamics and analytical derivations.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it is a theoretical analysis plus numerical exploration.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,True,"Authors mention extensive numerical investigations and replicator dynamics simulations for robustness (e.g., SI Text, S3–S5).",0.86,True,Methods section states a fully analytical investigation of a prototypical case using evolutionary game theory and replicator dynamics.,1,5,Parameter n is group size; Fig. 2 caption gives n = 5 for the prototypical parameter set.,0.8,1,"Model is a one-shot public good game repeatedly faced by randomly sampled groups; each interaction is single-round, though embedded in evolutionary time.",0.7,0,"Players contribute a fixed amount c or nothing (binary decision C vs D), not a continuous share.",0.95,N/A,No human endowment account structure; purely theoretical model without initial allocation between private and public accounts.,1,N/R,Payoff: contribution c is multiplied by r and distributed to others (m−1 recipients); marginal per capita return to a contributor is not reported as a single scalar for standard MPCR; not straightforwardly defined under their payoffs without extra assumptions.,0.4,N/A,"No communication; also, no human subjects.",1,N/A,No user interface or feedback; purely analytical/simulation model.,1,N/A,Institutional sanctions not tied to individual observability within an experiment; no human subjects.,1,N/A,Same as for punishment; no experimental interface.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; evolutionary dynamics over continuous time.,1,1,One treatment class uses institutional penalties applied to defectors; explicitly described as punishing.,1,N/R,Institution has a per capita incentive I but the cost per unit of punishment to the punisher is not normalized to a separate per-coin cost in this framework.,0.4,N/R,"Punishment reduces defectors’ payoffs by mI/m_D each, but not framed as a tech multiplier per coin spent; coin-based mapping not given.",0.5,1,Alternative treatment uses rewards distributed to cooperators; explicitly described.,1,N/R,Analytical model includes total incentive mI but does not specify separate cost per reward unit in a coin-based way.,0.4,N/R,Rewards are given as mI/m_C per cooperator; no per-coin technology defined.,0.5,N/R,"Players may decide whether to contribute a fixed amount c; c is a cost, not clearly defined as an initial endowment of tokens.",0.5,N/R,"Outcomes are described in terms of evolutionary equilibria (full cooperation, full defection, coexistence), but no numeric steady-state fraction of contributions is tabulated in normalized form for this specific parameter set.",0.4,N/R,"Each cooperator contributes fixed amount c, but average contribution per player across the population or at equilibrium is not given numerically.",0.4,N/R,"They discuss group welfare qualitatively and in figures (e.g., Fig. 3), but no explicit numeric efficiency (payoff / maximum payoff) is provided for the compulsory baseline.",0.4,N/R,"They mention payoffs such as (r−1)c−g and −g, but do not provide a single aggregated group payoff metric over the evolutionary process for this condition.",0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"The work is purely theoretical/analytical and numerical; no lab, online, or field experiment with humans.",1,"Compulsory public good game with n players; participation is obligatory (z = 0). Each potential participant pays a fixed participation cost g > 0. If at least 2 players are present, each chooses to contribute a fixed amount c > 0 or not. Total contributions are multiplied by r (1 < r < n) and equally distributed among the other m−1 members. Social dilemma: if all contribute, each gets (r−1)c − g > 0; if none contribute, each gets −g < 0. Institutional incentive represented as total ml, where I is per capita incentive and m is number of participants; in rewarding regime, ml is shared among cooperators (each gets ml/m_C); in punishing regime, defectors lose ml/m_D from payoff. Evolutionary dynamics via replicator equation for strategies C and D (and N fixed at 0 in compulsory case). Thresholds in I: below I_- = c/n incentives have no effect and defection dominates; above I_+ = c, cooperation dominates; intermediate I lead to coexistence under rewards and bistability under punishment.",Summarized directly from Methods and Results sections defining the compulsory public good game and institutional incentives.,0.96
10.1073_pnas.1115219109.md,"Optional public good game with institutional rewarding or punishing (analytical and numerical model, baseline parameter set n=5, r=3, c=1, g=0.5)","Paper’s core new condition introduces optional participation (strategy N) with same incentive mechanisms; figures (Fig. 2) explicitly use parameter set n=5, r=3, c=1, g=0.5 and various I.",0.9,"Type and size of institutional incentive in an optional-participation public good game: rewarding vs punishing; per capita incentive I varied around thresholds I_- = c/n, J_-, J_+, K_-, K_+, and I_+ = c.","Optional participation variant is analyzed for both rewards and punishments under varying I; additional thresholds J_-, J_+, K_-, K_+ define qualitative regime changes.",0.92,False,Still part of same theoretical evolutionary game model; no human data.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment but a theoretical/simulation analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,They show phase portraits and trajectories (Fig. 2) and mention extensive numerical investigations for robustness.,0.9,True,Methods: fully analytical investigation of prototypical case; Results derive equilibria and bifurcations in optional participation case.,1,5,Fig. 2 caption specifies n = 5 as group size for the illustrated optional public good game.,0.95,1,Each group interaction is a single public good game; repetition occurs only via evolutionary updating.,0.8,0,"As in compulsory game, each participant chooses to contribute fixed amount c or nothing.",0.95,N/A,No explicit human endowment; model defines only costs and payoffs.,1,N/R,"Same payoff structure as in compulsory case; MPCR is not given as a scalar and would require a specific definition regarding self-returns, which is not fully standard here.",0.4,N/A,No communication modeled and no human subjects.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback beyond payoffs within equations.,1,N/A,Institution acts impersonally; no notion of revealed identities in a human experiment.,1,N/A,Same as punishment; no experimental identities.,1,N/A,No explicit rounds shown; only replicator dynamics over continuous time.,1,1,One institutional regime uses penalties for defectors in the optional participation setup.,1,N/R,"Cost of operating punishment institution is discussed qualitatively (e.g., law enforcement apparatus) but no per-unit cost parameter separate from I is specified in base model.",0.4,N/R,Punished defectors lose mI/m_D; model doesn’t express this as a technology per coin.,0.5,1,Alternative institutional regime uses rewards distributed to cooperators in optional participation.,1,N/R,No explicit cost per reward unit beyond the general incentive pool parameter I.,0.4,N/R,"Rewards equal mI/m_C per cooperator, but not normalized to a tech multiplier per coin.",0.5,N/R,"Model specifies contribution cost c and entrance cost g, but not an explicit initial token endowment.",0.6,N/R,"They describe qualitative regimes (full cooperation, coexistence, rock-paper-scissors cycles) but do not provide numeric steady-state contribution rates for the chosen parameter set.",0.4,N/R,"Each cooperator invests c = 1 in the illustrated parameter set, but average contributions across population or time are not reported numerically.",0.5,N/R,"They discuss group welfare and show average payoffs (e.g., in Fig. 3) but do not translate into an explicit efficiency ratio against a maximum payoff baseline for this base optional condition.",0.4,N/R,Long-term average payoffs are plotted (Fig. 3) but concrete numeric values for the illustrated parameter set and specific I values are not given in the text.,0.45,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model is theoretical with simulations of replicator dynamics; no experimental environment.,1,"Optional public good game with three strategies: C (cooperate), D (defect), N (nonparticipation). Large well-mixed population; random samples of n = 5 players occasionally face opportunity to play. Each potential participant pays entrance cost g > 0 (g = 0.5 in illustrated case). Number of willing participants is m (0 ≤ m ≤ n). Game requires m ≥ 2; otherwise, no public good interaction happens and nonparticipants obtain payoff 0. Participants then choose to contribute c > 0 (c = 1) or not. Contributions are multiplied by r (r = 3) and distributed equally among other m−1 participants; full cooperation yields individual payoff (r−1)c − g; mutual defection yields −g. Institutional incentives: total incentive is mI, where I is per capita incentive. In reward regime, mI is shared among cooperators (each gets mI/m_C, where m_C is number of cooperators); in punishment regime, defectors lose mI/m_D from their payoff (m_D defectors). Strategies C, D, and N update via replicator dynamics with occasional random perturbations. Without incentives and with 0 < g < (r−1)c, C, D, and N form rock–paper–scissors cycles. With incentives: rewards vs punishments plus optional participation lead to different phase portraits (Fig. 2a–h). Thresholds in I: I_- = c/n defines when incentives begin to matter; I_+ = c when they enforce full cooperation. Intermediate thresholds J_-, J_+, K_-, K_+ mark creation and annihilation of interior equilibria for rewards and punishments, respectively. For punishment with I just above I_- and optional participation, long-run dynamics converge to full cooperation from any initial condition, at substantially lower incentive levels than required under compulsory participation.","Compiled from Methods and Results describing the optional participation version, its parameters (n, r, c, g, I), and the replicator dynamics over C, D, N.",0.98
10.1073_pnas.1115219109.md,"User-pays variant with rewarding or punishing (optional or compulsory participation, analytical/numerical extension)","Section on 'User-pays variant' (Fig. 3) introduces an additional parameter a and modified entrance fee g + aI, with both rewards and punishments; treated as a robustness variant of the main model.",0.85,Institutional regime (reward vs punish) and per capita incentive I under a user-pays funding rule where entrance fee equals g + aI (with a = 1).,Variant specifically varies I and considers both rewarding and punishing under the modified cost structure (user pays for sanctioning system).,0.88,False,Same theoretical framework; no human data.,1,False,"Analytical plus numerical model variant, not experimental.",1,False,No lab or field environment.,1,True,Fig. 3 shows numerical results (cooperator frequency and long-term average payoffs) as functions of I in this variant.,0.9,True,Described as a model variant analyzed alongside the main analytical results; long-term average payoffs are derived or computed.,0.9,5,"Fig. 3 caption: parameters n = 5, r = 3, c = 1, a = 1, g = 0.5.",0.95,1,Same single-shot interaction structure per game as base models.,0.8,0,Players still choose to contribute fixed amount c or nothing (binary contribution decision).,0.95,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation described for human subjects.,1,N/R,Same public good technology with r and n as before; MPCR not reported as such.,0.4,N/A,No human experiment.,1,N/A,Purely theoretical/simulation setting.,1,N/A,No experimental identities.,1,N/A,No experimental identities.,1,N/A,No rounds displayed in an interface.,1,1,"Variant includes punishing case, analyzed in panels c and d of Fig. 3.",1,N/R,"Entrance fee g + aI helps fund the institution, but per-unit cost of punishment to the institution is not separately parameterized beyond I.",0.45,N/R,Punishment effect per defector remains based on mI/m_D; not mapped to a coin-based tech parameter.,0.5,1,Rewarding version depicted in Fig. 3a and 3b.,1,N/R,"In this user-pays setup, entrance fees fund rewards, but there is no explicit per-unit reward cost separate from I and a.",0.45,N/R,Rewards still based on mI/m_C; coin technology not specified.,0.5,N/R,Players pay entrance fee g + aI; no explicit initial token endowment is defined.,0.6,N/R,"Fig. 3a and 3c display cooperator frequencies qualitatively as a function of I, but precise numeric values are not reported in text.",0.45,N/R,"Each cooperator contributes c = 1, but average contribution levels at each I are not specified numerically.",0.5,N/R,Fig. 3b and 3d show long-term average payoffs; efficiency as a normalized ratio is not explicitly provided.,0.4,N/R,Long-term average payoffs depicted graphically; exact numbers not given in text.,0.45,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Variant is still purely theoretical/numerical.,1,"User-pays variant modifies the base (primarily optional) public good game by having players pay an entrance fee g + aI when they choose to participate, where g is a base cost and aI is the part funding the sanctioning institution, with a > 0 and a = 1 in Fig. 3. Public good technology is unchanged: n = 5, r = 3, c = 1, and contributions multiplied by r and shared among other participants. Institutional incentive is still characterized by per capita incentive I and total incentive mI. For rewards (panels a, b), the system largely redistributes payoffs and marginally increases group welfare only in a narrow I range; cooperator frequency can be reduced to zero in that range when participation is optional. For punishments (panels c, d), the highest group welfare occurs when I is just above I_- = c/n, the minimum incentive to sustain full cooperation; despite potentially long transient oscillations between N, C, and D, the C norm eventually prevails. Additional robustness checks in SI Text consider cases where unused fees are returned and alternative punishment logistics (e.g., handling time, capped penalties), but qualitative outcome that small negative incentives with optional participation can stabilize cooperation remains.",Summarizes the specific modifications and qualitative results described in the 'User-pays variant' section and Fig. 3 caption.,0.9
10.1016_j.amc.2017.08.049.md,Monte Carlo simulation on multilayer lattice – baseline parameters,"Single core simulation framework described in Section 2 with specified parameter set (e.g., alpha=0.8, gamma=0.1, epsilon=0.02, b=1.06, omega=0.1, beta=0.1, rho variable). No separate human experiments.",0.78,"Punishment cost ρ, punishment severity β, reputation factor ω, temptation to defect b, Q-learning parameters (α, γ, ε)","Section 3 varies ρ, β, ω, b and Q-learning parameters to assess their impact on cooperation frequency f_c.",0.94,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on a lattice; no human or animal subjects are involved.,1,False,"It is a computational simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human participants.",1,False,No physical or online lab with human subjects; purely in silico.,1,True,They explicitly describe Monte Carlo simulations on L×L lattices with programmed agents and Q-learning.,1,False,"While equations define dynamics, results rely on numerical Monte Carlo simulation rather than closed-form analysis.",0.87,40000,"They simulate on an L×L regular square lattice with L=200, giving N=L×L=40000 agents.",0.98,100000,Algorithm 1 states simulation step T=1×10^5; results are averaged over last 5000 steps.,0.98,1,"Agents choose pure strategies C or D each interaction; there is no continuous contribution level, so binary (all cooperate vs all defect) actions.",0.96,N/A,"This is an iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma on a lattice, not a public goods contribution game with endowments.",1,N/R,"No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; only pairwise PDG payoffs R,S,T,P and modifications via β.",1,N/A,No human participants; communication among artificial agents is not modeled as chat.,1,N/A,"Information exchange is through payoff-based imitation and Q-learning, not via explicit summaries shown to human players.",1,N/A,No identities are shown to human players; agents only observe neighbors’ strategies and payoffs algorithmically.,1,N/A,There is no explicit reward targeting identities; reputation ω affects payoffs but not shown to human subjects.,1,N/A,Agents do not have an interface with a displayed round count; time is an internal simulation index.,1,1,"Management-layer punishers P incur cost ρ to punish defectors in classic layer, reducing their payoff by β.",1,N/R,"ρ is the total cost parameter in payoff matrix M1, but the model does not specify a per-token mapping as in discrete punishment points.",0.4,N/R,β is defined as punishment severity (payoff reduction for defectors) but not in terms of per coin spent; mapping to a per-unit technology is absent.,0.45,1,"Punishers receive a positive reputation payoff ω in M1, functioning as a reward for engaging in punishment.",0.86,N/R,"The model does not define a separate cost to grant the reputation ω beyond ρ, nor a per-unit reward cost.",0.35,N/R,"Reputation factor ω is an additive payoff term, not modeled as a technology with tokens spent per reward unit.",0.4,N/R,Agents do not have explicit endowments; they receive payoffs directly from PDG matrices M2 and M3.,1,N/R,"Outcome variable is frequency of cooperators f_c, not normalized contribution from an endowment.",0.9,N/R,No token-based contributions are modeled; agents choose C or D.,1,N/R,They report cooperation frequency and Q-values but not efficiency defined as payoff relative to maximum possible.,0.85,N/R,Simulation results focus on frequencies and Q-values; total or average group payoff is not reported numerically.,0.85,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents in simulation.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,No human,"All results are from Monte Carlo simulations of artificial agents, not from human experiments or field data.",1,"Two-layer lattice model with one-to-one node mapping: management layer (strategies P punishers, I inactive) and classic layer (strategies C cooperators, D defectors). Classic layer plays a weak Prisoner’s Dilemma with R=1, S=P=0, T=b when paired with an inactive manager, and a modified game with T=b−β when the corresponding manager is a punisher. Management-layer payoffs follow matrix M1 with punishment cost ρ and reputation factor ω and include a feedback term δ(π_i^C)=tanh(x)×σ from classic-layer payoffs. Agents update actions using self-adapting Q-learning (Q-table 2×2 over states=strategies and actions) with learning rate α, discount factor γ, and ε-greedy exploration, and update strategies via Fermi imitation based on payoffs. Simulations run on a regular square lattice L×L with L=200 using random-walk updating over T=10^5 Monte Carlo steps, recording cooperation frequency f_c in the classic layer and strategy frequencies in management layer.","Summarized from Section 2 (Model), Algorithm 1, and parameter descriptions in figure captions.",0.98
10.1177_1948550614527115.md,Study 1 – Ultimatum game with racial group proposers (within-subjects offers),Study 1 is a single within-subjects ultimatum game where responder decisions are measured for multiple offer sizes from White vs. Black proposers; treated as one experimental condition because no between-subject manipulation of game structure.,1,"Proposer racial group (White vs. Black) and Offer size (US$10, 8, 7, 6, 4 out of 20), both within-subjects","Method section: 'task comprised a 2 (Group: White vs. Black) × 5 (Offer Size: US$10, US$8, US$7, US$6, or US$4) within-subjects design.'",1,True,Human undergraduates played the ultimatum game as responders.,1,True,Researchers manipulated proposer race and offer size in a controlled lab-like task.,1,True,Participants were NYU undergraduates in private computer cubicles on campus.,0.95,False,All decisions made by human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,No purely mathematical model; empirical behavioral data only.,1,2,Ultimatum game defined as interaction between one proposer and one responder.,1,10,Participants 'considered a series of 10 critical offers from White and Black proposers'; each is a one-shot interaction.,0.95,1,"Responders choose accept vs. reject; they cannot contribute partial amounts, but in this coding scheme 'allOrNothing' refers to contribution flexibility in PGGs; for ultimatum responders there is no contribution decision. Treated as able to choose any binary action, so marked 1 as general, though construct does not fit well.",0.3,N/A,Ultimatum game with no contribution endowment decision; concept not applicable.,1,N/R,"No public good or multiplier is defined; standard ultimatum division, so MPCR not reported.",1,0,Only offers and avatars shown; no mention of communication between players.,0.95,0,Participants only see each incoming offer; no summaries of others’ earnings or past behavior.,0.95,0,"Rejections are anonymous, and proposers are not described as learning responder identity or specific punishment.",0.7,0,There is no separate reward mechanism; only acceptance which follows from the offer.,1,1,"Participants knew they would interact with each player only once and saw a sequence of trials; implies awareness of multiple offers and that each is one-shot, though exact display is not specified.",0.6,1,Rejecting an offer yields zero for both players: described as 'costly punishment.',1,1,"In ultimatum game, rejecting any positive offer sacrifices the responder’s entire potential payoff. Normalizing per unit of 'punishment' is not formalized, but effectively each rejection costs the responder their proposed amount while removing the proposer’s larger share. Coded as 1 token of cost per 1 unit of self-loss for approximate mapping.",0.3,1,"Rejection removes the entire proposed payoff from the proposer. Given coding constraints, marked as 1 unit of loss to proposer per unit of self-loss, though actual mapping varies by offer size.",0.3,0,No costly reward option beyond simply accepting the offer.,1,N/A,No distinct costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct costly reward mechanism.,1,20,Offers are divisions of US$20; total stake per dyad is $20.,1,N/R,"Study reports acceptance rates, not contributions as fraction of endowment.",1,N/R,Outcome variable is accept/reject; no participant contribution amounts.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric (group payoff relative to maximum) is reported or derivable across all conditions from provided text.,0.4,N/R,Only acceptance rates reported; not average earnings.,1,USA,Described as 'White American New York University undergraduates.',1,N/R,Age not provided.,1,"Mixed, same-sex avatar pairings per trial; exact proportions N/R","Participants viewed same-sex avatars, implying both men and women, but no distribution.",0.5,Undergraduate college students,NYU undergraduates participating for course credit.,1,On site,"Participants were placed into private computer cubicles, indicating in-person lab setting.",1,One-shot ultimatum game as responder only; hypothetical money; 10 critical offers (5 amounts × 2 proposer races) plus 6 fillers; responders interact once with each proposer avatar; no repeated interaction or learning; anonymity via avatars.,Summarized from Study 1 method description.,1
10.1177_1948550614527115.md,Study 2 – Ultimatum game with college group proposers (within-subjects offers),Study 2 is another single within-subjects ultimatum game with proposer college group and offer size varied; no separate between-subject game-structure conditions.,1,"Proposer college group (in-group vs. out-group rival college) and Offer size (US$10, 8, 7, 6, 4 out of 20), both within-subjects; in-group identification measured as moderator","Method: 'task comprising a 2 (Group: in-group vs. outgroup) × 5 (Offer Size: US$10, US$8, US$7, US$6, or US$4) within-subjects design'; identity strength measured, not manipulated.",1,True,Human Amherst College undergraduates played the ultimatum game as responders.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of proposer group and offer size in a lab task.,1,True,Participants were Amherst College undergraduates likely run in campus lab rooms; procedure parallels Study 1’s cubicle setup.,0.9,False,Human data only.,1,False,No purely analytical model.,1,2,Standard ultimatum game dyads.,1,10,"They 'considered critical proposals' in a 2×5 within-subjects design, implying one trial per cell similar to Study 1; 10 critical offers.",0.8,1,Responder has accept vs. reject; contribution notion not applicable but coded as general binary choice as in Study 1.,0.3,N/A,No contribution decision by responders.,1,N/R,Ultimatum division with no public good multiplier.,1,0,No communication described between participants and proposers.,0.95,0,Participants only see individual offers; no mention of aggregate summaries.,0.95,0,Anonymity via generic avatars and three-digit ID; proposers are not described as seeing who punished them.,0.7,0,No separate costly reward option; acceptance simply implements the split.,1,1,Participants know they are in a task with multiple proposals and that they interact with each player only once; implies awareness of finite interactions.,0.6,1,"Rejection of an offer leads to zero earnings for both, explicitly interpreted as costly p