10.1016_j.jtbi.2005.10.011.md,"Completely mixed population – Viability model – ODE analysis and Monte Carlo simulations over (b, p)","Paper describes a score-dependent viability model in a completely mixed population with four strategies (AP, AN, SP, SN), studied via differential equations (Appendix A) and Monte Carlo simulation (Section 3.1, Fig. 1). This constitutes one simulation condition family.",0.9,"Benefit of cooperation b and punishment fine p systematically varied; costs fixed at c = 1, q = 1; four strategy types AP, AN, SP, SN present with varying initial densities.",Section 3.1 and Fig. 1 specify that analyses are on the p–b plane with c = q = 1 and four strategies; initial densities are given for some runs.,0.9,False,No human or animal subjects; purely mathematical and Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,Not an empirical controlled experiment but a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; simulations only.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations and ODEs on strategy frequencies in populations without human subjects.,1,True,Appendix A presents analytical differential equation model; Section 3.1 discusses mathematical analysis and thresholds Y1–Y4.,1,2500,"For Monte Carlo viability simulations in mixed population, they sample opponents randomly from a population of size N. They state simulations used 50×50 and 100×100 lattices for lattice cases, but for completely mixed they do not specify N; could be arbitrary large. Using 2500 would be speculative, so not appropriate.",0,200000,"They state numerical calculation for each (b, p) is done for 200000 time steps in Fig. 1B; treat time steps as rounds.",0.7,1,"This is not a contribution game but a two-player interaction with fixed payoffs; no contribution choice variable, so the all-or-nothing notion does not apply.",0,N/A,No endowment or contribution decision; only fixed strategy interactions.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human players; no communication.,1,N/A,Not an experiment with feedback to human subjects.,1,N/A,No identities or information structure; purely strategic types.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No human subjects; time steps are not displayed to agents.,1,1,Strategies AP and SP punish selfish players with fine p and cost q.,1,1,"Cost of imposing punishment is q and they set c = q = 1 in the analyses (e.g., Fig. 1A caption).",1,N/R,Magnitude of reduction per unit cost is not parameterized as a ratio; p and q are independent parameters.,0.5,0,No positive rewards; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model is in terms of fitness payoffs, not token endowments.",1,N/A,No contribution decision; only cooperative vs selfish strategic types.,1,N/A,No per-round contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They discuss which strategy dominates, not normalized efficiency.",1,N/R,They give payoffs of pairwise interactions but not total group payoff time series.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation population dynamics.,1,"Two-person symmetric game with four fixed strategies (AP, AN, SP, SN); payoffs defined by parameters b (benefit to partner when altruistic), c (cost of altruism), p (fine to selfish player when punished), q (cost of punishing). Each time step: each individual plays z = 4 games with randomly chosen opponents, score is sum of payoffs. In viability model, higher score reduces death probability, and replacement of dead individual is by one of its recent opponents, proportional to equal chance among them. Dynamics studied via differential equations and Monte Carlo.",Summarized from Section 2 and 3.1 and Appendix A.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2005.10.011.md,"Completely mixed population – Fertility model – ODE analysis and Monte Carlo simulations over (b, p)","Section 3.2 and Appendix B analyze a score-dependent fertility model in a completely mixed population with four strategies, plus numerical simulations (Fig. 2).",0.9,"Benefit of cooperation b and punishment fine p varied; costs fixed at c = 1, q = 1; four strategies AP, AN, SP, SN with specified initial densities in simulations.","Fig. 2 caption: horizontal axis p, vertical b, c = q = 1; initial densities given; same four strategies.",0.9,False,No actual subjects; model and simulations only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab; numerical analysis only.,1,True,Monte Carlo and differential equations in a virtual population.,1,True,Appendix B gives differential equations and analytical invasion conditions.,1,N/R,Population size N is not specified for completely mixed fertility simulations.,0.1,N/R,"Number of time steps for each (b, p) run in Fig. 2 is not reported.",0.1,1,There is no contribution choice; notion does not apply.,0,N/A,No endowment or contribution mechanics.,1,N/R,Not structured as a public goods game.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No human feedback or displays.,1,N/A,No identities in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface or awareness of time steps.,1,1,Punishers AP and SP reduce fitness of selfish opponents with fine p at cost q.,1,1,They set c = q = 1 in Fig. 2 and text.,1,N/R,No explicit per-unit technology parameter; p and q independent.,0.5,0,Only punishment included.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Fitness payoffs only; no explicit endowment.,1,N/A,No per-round contribution variable.,1,N/A,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,"Outcomes described in terms of which strategy dominates, not efficiency.",1,N/R,No aggregated payoff statistics reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Theoretical/simulation fertility model.,1,"Same two-person game and payoff matrix as in viability model, with four strategies AP, AN, SP, SN. Each individual interacts with z opponents (z = 4 for completely mixed case). In the score-dependent fertility model, death is random (independent of score), and vacant sites are colonized by neighbors with probability proportional to their scores (expected payoffs E1–E4). Analytical conditions show that when c < p, dynamics converge either to SN or to AP+AN mixture; when p < c, SN dominates. SP never beats SN in this model.",Summarized from Section 3.2 and Appendix B.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2005.10.011.md,"Lattice-structured population – Viability model – 50×50 lattice – Monte Carlo over (b, p) with varying AN/AP and SN/SP ratios",Section 4.1 and corrected Fig. 4A (corrigendum) describe Monte Carlo simulations of the viability model on a 50×50 lattice over b and p for different initial composition ratios AN/AP and SN/SP.,0.9,"Benefit of cooperation b and punishment fine p varied; costs c = 1, q = 1; initial total altruist density (AP+AN) = 0.5 and selfish density (SP+SN) = 0.5 with different AN/AP and SN/SP ratios.","Fig. 4 text: vertical axis p, horizontal axis b, c = q = 1; initial densities of altruist and selfish populations are both 0.5, and AN/AP and SN/SP ratios are changed.",0.9,False,Lattice simulations of abstract agents only.,1,False,"It is a simulation study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,Not run with human subjects in a lab.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on a 50×50 lattice with periodic boundary conditions.,1,False,"While some analytical pair-edge approximation is discussed (Appendix C), this particular condition refers to 2D Monte Carlo results (corrected Fig. 4A).",0.7,2500,"They state using a 50×50 lattice, so N = 50×50 = 2500.",1,50000,They say each simulation was run for 50000 units of time for Fig. 4A.,1,1,No contribution choice exists; classification not applicable.,0,N/A,No explicit endowment or contribution.,1,N/R,Not a public goods framework.,1,N/A,Non-human agents; no communication protocol.,1,N/A,Agents do not receive explicit summaries; only local interactions.,1,N/A,No identities beyond local type; no display mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Agents do not know time horizon.,1,1,"Same punishment structure (fine p, cost q) as in mixed population.",1,1,They fix c = q = 1 for these simulations (Fig. 4 caption).,1,N/R,No per-unit tech parameter; p and q defined independently.,0.5,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Game is defined at fitness payoff level; no endowments.,1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/A,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as which strategy wins (fractions of runs), not payoff efficiency.",1,N/R,They do not report aggregate payoffs across the lattice.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation on a lattice.,1,"Square lattice (50×50) with Neumann neighborhood (4 nearest neighbors), periodic boundary conditions. Each time unit: each site engages in 2-person games with its four neighbors, accumulating a score from Table 1 payoffs. In the viability model, death probability at each site decreases with score; when a site becomes empty, one of its four neighbors colonizes it randomly. Initial densities: total altruists (AP+AN) = 0.5 and total selfish (SP+SN) = 0.5. Ratios of AN/AP and SN/SP are systematically varied to study their effects on regions of parameter space (p, b) where AP, AN, SP, or SN dominate.","Summarized from Section 2, 4.1, and corrected Fig. 4A description.",1
