10.1016_j.geb.2009.09.004.md,Treatment CE – Earnings feedback only,Table 2 labels a treatment 'CE' where information after first stage is 'Earnings feedback'; text clarifies this is feedback about each member’s stage-1 earnings only.,1,Feedback format: individual earnings feedback only (no contribution feedback).,In treatment E individuals receive information about each group member's earnings π_i^1 (earnings feedback) and not contributions; Table 2: Treatment CE – Earnings feedback.,1,True,Lab experiment with 44 human participants in this treatment.,1,True,Controlled experimental design with random assignment and specified game.,1,True,Run in Experimental Economics Laboratory at University of Melbourne.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data from participants.,1,False,Focus is on experimental results rather than analytical modeling.,1,4,Groups of four individuals in all treatments.,1,10,Finitely repeated public good game for 10 periods.,1,1,"Contribution choice c_i with 0 ≤ c_i ≤ 20, continuous/discrete but not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,0,Players receive endowment E$20 and then decide how much to move to public account; default is all in private account.,0.9,0.4,Same payoff function across treatments: 0.4 times group contribution.,1,0,Decisions made simultaneously and without communication.,1,1,"After stage 1, subjects receive detailed feedback about each group member's earnings π_i^1; also see own earnings and group total contribution (per footnote 8).",1,0,Identification numbers 1–4 are randomly reassigned each period to prevent stable reputations; identities not truly revealed.,0.9,0,No reward stage in the design.,1,1,Duration (10 periods) is common knowledge; participants typically see the period counter in such zTree implementations.,0.8,1,Second-stage peer punishment exists in all three treatments.,1,1,Each punishment point costs E$1 to the punisher.,1,2,Each punishment point reduces recipient’s earnings by E$2.,1,0,No mechanism for increasing others’ payoffs is introduced.,1,N/A,"No rewards, so no cost defined.",1,N/A,"No rewards, so no technology defined.",1,20,Same E$20 per period endowment applies to all treatments.,1,0.3895,Table A.1 gives 'CE Average' contribution (all periods) as 7.79; divide by endowment 20 gives 7.79/20 ≈ 0.3895.,1,7.79,"Table A.1, treatment CE, 'Average' row, 'Contribution – All periods' column shows 7.79.",1,N/R,Earnings in CE mentioned qualitatively as below Nash and below C but no average E$ value reported; cannot compute efficiency without mean earnings.,0.1,N/R,Paper reports average earnings only for C and E; CE earnings described as 'even lower than those predicted by the non-cooperative Nash equilibrium' but no numeric mean.,0.1,Australia,"Experiment took place at University of Melbourne, Australia; participants are its students.",1,N/R,No age data for treatment-specific or pooled sample reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition is not discussed.,0,"University students (undergraduate and/or graduate, field not restricted)",Participants all recruited from University of Melbourne student pool.,0.8,On site,Setting is a physical laboratory with partitioned terminals.,1,"Identical two-stage repeated public good game with peer punishment as in other treatments (groups of 4, 10 periods, partner matching, same payoff and punishment parameters). In this treatment, after stage 1, subjects see each group member’s stage-1 earnings π_i^1 (earnings feedback) but not their contributions; to equalize information salience, all subjects also see the group’s total contribution Σ c_h and their own π_i^1. Identification numbers 1–4 are randomly re-assigned each period. There is a one-off lump-sum payment of E$25 to insure against losses; exchange rate E$1 = A$0.085.","Summarizes same game but highlights that only earnings feedback is provided, as described in Section 2 and footnote 8.",1
10.1016_j.geb.2009.09.004.md,Treatment E – Contribution and earnings feedback,Table 2 lists 'E – Contribution and earnings feedback' with 40 participants; text confirms treatment E receives both formats.,1,Feedback format: both individual contribution and individual earnings feedback.,In treatment E individuals receive information about each group member’s contribution and earnings; Table 2: Treatment E – Contribution and earnings feedback.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 40 human subjects in this feedback condition.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments in a controlled lab setting with pre-specified rules.,1,True,Conducted in the Experimental Economics Laboratory at the University of Melbourne.,1,False,No artificial agents or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No analytical model only; results are behavioral from experiments.,1,4,Subjects divided into groups of four in all treatments.,1,10,Same group plays the public good game for 10 periods.,1,1,"Contribution decision c_i ranges from 0 to 20, allowing any amount.",1,0,Whole endowment initially private; subjects choose how much to contribute.,0.9,0.4,"Payoff function identical across treatments, with 0.4 share of total contributions.",1,0,Decisions in both contribution and punishment stages are made without communication.,1,1,"After stage 1, this treatment provides detailed feedback about each member’s contribution and earnings, making others’ outcomes visible.",1,0,"IDs 1–4 are randomly reassigned each period, preventing persistent identity linking to punishment.",0.9,0,There is no reward option in the game.,1,1,Game length (10 periods) and structure are common knowledge.,0.8,1,Peer punishment stage present in all treatments.,1,1,Each punishment point costs E$1.,1,2,Each punishment point reduces target’s earnings by E$2.,1,0,"Only punishment, no positive sanctioning.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism operates.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,20,Endowment of E$20 per period applies to every subject in every treatment.,1,0.268,Table A.1 'E Average' all-period contribution is 5.36; contribution rate = 5.36 / 20 = 0.268.,1,5.36,"Table A.1, treatment E, 'Average' row, 'Contribution – All periods' is 5.36.",1,N/R,Average earnings in E treatment are not numerically reported; only relative statements versus C; cannot compute treatment-level efficiency.,0.1,N/R,Section 3.4 gives statistical comparison (p-value) but not the actual mean earnings for E; only that they are lower than 24.20 in C and above 20 in Nash; so no numeric group payoff is available.,0.1,Australia,All participants are University of Melbourne students in Australia.,1,N/R,Age statistics are not provided.,0,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,0,"University students (undergraduate and/or graduate, field not restricted)",Participants are students recruited through ORSEE from university volunteer pool.,0.8,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental lab using zTree on networked PCs.,1,"Same two-stage public good with punishment: in stage 1, each subject picks c_i in [0,20]; stage-1 payoff π_i^1 = 20 − c_i + 0.4 Σ c_h. In stage 2, subjects may assign punishment points p_ij to others at cost E$1 each, reducing recipients’ earnings by E$2 per point, subject to Σ_j≠i p_ij ≤ π_i^1. Decisions are simultaneous and anonymous; numeric IDs 1–4 are randomly reassigned each period. In this treatment, after stage 1, feedback combines both contribution and earnings formats: participants see each group member’s contribution c_i and earnings π_i^1. There is a one-off lump-sum payment of E$25; exchange rate E$1 = A$0.085.",Describes the same game but highlights dual-format feedback unique to treatment E.,1
10.1177_0956797615624469.md,Study 1 – Third-party punishment game (single condition),Study 1 describes one third-party punishment game structure with no between-subject experimental conditions; all participants play the same game with fixed parameters.,0.95,"Dictator allocation to recipient (0%, 25%, 50%, 75%, 100% of $10 stake) within-subject for punishers via strategy method",The amount allocated by the dictator to the recipient is the key variable varied within the strategy method that affects punishers’ decisions.,0.98,True,Human participants from UCSB psychology pool played the game.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with random grouping and fixed monetary incentives.,1,True,Sessions conducted in a small laboratory with semiprivate cubicles at UCSB.,1,False,No computer or agent-based simulation reported.,1,False,No purely mathematical or closed-form model only; primary results are empirical.,1,3,"Each group consisted of dictator, recipient, and punisher (triads).",1,1,"Described as an anonymous, one-shot third-party punishment game.",1,1,"Dictators could choose among several discrete splits; this is neither pure all-or-nothing nor continuous, but closer to ‘can contribute any allowed amount’; coded as 1 per instructions to reserve 0 for strictly all-or-nothing.",0.6,0,Dictator starts with full $10 endowment in their own account and decides how much to give to recipient.,1,N/R,"This is a dictator/third-party punishment game, not a public-goods game with a multiplier; no MPCR defined.",1,0,No communication between participants mentioned; standard anonymous lab protocol.,0.9,0,Participants only learn relevant counterpart decisions for payoff calculation; no mention of aggregate summaries of others’ earnings or punishments.,0.8,0,Interactions are anonymous; no identities revealed with punishment decisions.,0.95,0,No rewarding mechanism in the game.,1,1,Game described to subjects as a one-shot interaction; they knew there was a single round.,0.9,1,Punishers can spend money to reduce dictator’s payoff.,1,1,Punishers spent their own $5 in $1 increments to punish.,1,0.2,Each $1 spent reduces dictator’s kept amount by 20% (of the amount kept).,1,0,No mechanism to spend money to increase others’ earnings; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Dictator receives $10 stake to allocate; punisher has a separate $5 budget but main allocation endowment is $10.,1,N/R,"Paper reports proportions of dictators allocating at least some or half of stake, but no single numeric contribution rate summary.",0.4,N/R,"Only distributions/proportions of allocation choices reported, not a mean amount.",0.4,N/R,No efficiency metric (group payoff relative to maximum) reported or derivable without full outcome distributions.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics reported.,0.2,United States,"Subjects drawn from University of California, Santa Barbara, a U.S. university.",0.95,N/R,Age not reported for Study 1 sample.,0.01,"72 women, 48 men","Explicitly stated that 120 people (72 women, 48 men) participated (119 analyzed).",1,Primarily undergraduates plus a few community members,Described as primarily undergraduates from the psychology subject pool with some community members.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a small laboratory with cubicles; in-person lab sessions.,1,One-shot anonymous third-party punishment dictator game using strategy method; dictator allocates $10 in $2.50 increments (0–100%) to recipient; punisher has $5 and can spend $0–$5 in $1 increments to reduce dictator’s kept share by 20% per $1; punishment decisions made for all five possible dictator allocations before roles are revealed. Dictators and punishers also complete valuation tasks assessing how much dictator values recipient and punisher.,Summarizes the key structural details of the game beyond requested fields.,1
10.1177_0956797615624469.md,Study 2 – One-recipient condition,One of three between-subject punisher conditions in Study 2; here punishers observe dictator’s allocation to a single third-party recipient and are unaware of own allocation.,1,Dictator allocation to observed recipient: either $0.50 (50-50) or $0.10 (10-90 in favor of dictator),"Within this condition, punisher decisions are elicited for both possible allocations; this is the manipulated factor affecting punishment.",1,True,Human MTurk participants acted as dictators and punishers.,1,True,Incentivized online experiment with random assignment to roles and conditions.,1,False,"Conducted online via Amazon Mechanical Turk, not in a physical lab.",1,False,No mention of computer simulations without human subjects.,1,False,Main methods are empirical; no purely analytical model as primary design.,1,4,"Each group had one dictator and three punishers; for each punisher condition, the relevant strategic environment still includes four players.",1,1,Single allocation and punishment stage per group; no repeated interactions described.,0.95,0,"Dictator chooses between two fixed options ($0.10 or $0.50) for each punisher-recipient; this is a binary, effectively all-or-nothing style choice rather than continuous.",0.9,0,Dictator holds the $1 endowment and chooses how much to allocate out; initial state is all in dictator’s own account.,1,N/R,This is not a public-goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR specified.,1,0,"As an MTurk experiment, no communication between participants was mentioned.",0.9,0,Participants know which allocations and punishments apply to them but no summaries of others’ outcomes are reported.,0.7,0,Online anonymous matching; no identities connected to punishment decisions.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,Participants are instructed about a single allocation/punishment episode; structure makes total rounds clear.,0.8,1,Punishers can spend their $0.50 endowment to reduce dictator’s payoff.,1,0.1,Punishers can spend money in $0.10 increments to punish.,1,0.2,"Each increment of spending reduces the amount the dictator keeps by 20% for the relevant allocation, as stated for all Study 2 conditions.",1,0,Only punishment (reducing dictator earnings) is available.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,1,Dictators were endowed with $1.00 for each punisher; for this condition we record the per-recipient endowment.,1,N/R,Authors report that dictators split evenly about 50% of the time but not a precise contribution rate per condition.,0.4,N/R,Only frequencies of choosing 50-50 vs. 10-90 are loosely described; no mean allocation reported per condition.,0.4,N/R,No explicit or derivable efficiency statistic provided.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff data reported.,0.2,United States,Recruitment was limited to U.S. residents on MTurk.,1,Mean 34.85 years (SD 10.96),Explicitly reported as average age 34.85 with SD 10.96.,1,"188 women, 212 men (across all Study 2 conditions)",Gender breakdown given for the full Study 2 sample of 400; applies equally to this between-subject condition subset.,0.9,N/R,Education level of MTurk workers not reported.,0.01,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Groups of four: one dictator and three punishers. Dictator allocates $1 between self and each of three punishers (who also serve as potential recipients). In the one-recipient condition, a punisher knows only about a single third-party recipient’s allocation (either $0.50 or $0.10), and does not know they themselves also received an allocation. Punisher has $0.50 and can spend $0–$0.50 in $0.10 increments; each increment reduces dictator’s kept amount by 20%. Strategy method: for each possible allocation level to the observed recipient, punisher chooses punishment and infers dictator’s valuations of self and recipient using the valuation task; correct inferences rewarded.",Captures structure specific to this MTurk condition.,1
10.1177_0956797615624469.md,Study 2 – Two-recipients condition,Second punisher condition in Study 2 where punisher observes dictator’s treatment of two third-party recipients (A and B) but not self.,1,"Dictator allocations to two recipients (A and B) each either $0.50 (50-50) or $0.10 (10-90), yielding four combinations: (A fair/B unfair, A unfair/B fair, both fair, both unfair)",Punisher decisions are elicited for all four combinations of fair/unfair allocations to the two third-party recipients.,1,True,Uses MTurk human subjects as punishers and dictators.,1,True,Random assignment to this condition with controlled payoffs and informational structure.,1,False,"Online MTurk environment, not a physical laboratory.",1,False,No mention of non-human simulation.,1,False,No purely analytical-only study.,1,4,"Each group consists of one dictator and three punishers, two of whom serve as the observed recipients A and B for this condition.",1,1,Single-shot interaction; no repeated rounds described.,0.95,0,Dictator makes a binary allocation choice (50-50 vs. 10-90) for each recipient; this is effectively an all-or-nothing binary option.,0.9,0,"As in other Study 2 conditions, dictator initially has $1 for each punisher-recipient and chooses how much to give.",1,N/R,No public good or multiplier; simple transfer and punishment game.,1,0,No communication features reported for MTurk sessions.,0.9,0,Punishers see only dictator’s specific allocations; no mention of summary statistics.,0.7,0,Interactions are anonymous and mediated; no identity revelation described.,0.9,0,No rewards mechanism exists.,1,1,Participants are briefed on a one-shot game structure.,0.8,1,Punishers can spend from a $0.50 endowment to reduce dictator’s earnings.,1,0.1,Punishment spending allowed in $0.10 increments.,1,0.2,"As described for Study 2 generally, each spent increment reduces dictator’s kept portion by 20%.",1,0,No option to increase dictator’s earnings.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,1,Dictator endowed with $1 per punisher; the basic per-recipient stake is $1.,1,N/R,"While the paper notes approximate overall even splits, it does not break down allocation frequencies per condition.",0.4,N/R,No condition-specific mean allocation numbers.,0.4,N/R,Efficiency metrics are not reported.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff data not provided.,0.2,United States,All Study 2 subjects recruited from U.S. MTurk workers.,1,Mean 34.85 years (SD 10.96),Age summary is for the full Study 2 sample including this condition.,1,"188 women, 212 men (across all Study 2 conditions)",Gender counts reported for whole Study 2 pool; each condition draws from this pool.,0.9,N/R,Education for MTurk sample not reported.,0.01,Online,MTurk-based experiment.,1,"Punisher in two-recipients condition observes dictator’s allocations to two distinct recipients (A, B), each also actual punishers but described only as recipients. For each of four combinations of fair (50-50) and unfair (10-90) allocations, punisher (with $0.50 endowment) chooses how much to punish (in $0.10 increments, 20% reduction in dictator’s kept amount per increment) and infers dictator’s valuations of self and recipient A using the valuation task. Punisher does not know their own allocation from dictator (described separately as variable participation bonus).",Summarizes aspects specific to this condition’s informational structure.,1
10.1177_0956797615624469.md,Study 2 – Self-and-other condition,Third punisher condition in Study 2 where punisher observes dictator’s treatment of self and another recipient.,1,"Pair of dictator allocations: to punisher (self) and to a third-party recipient, each either $0.50 (50-50) or $0.10 (10-90), yielding four combinations (self fair/other unfair, self unfair/other fair, both fair, both unfair)",Punishers respond for all four combinations of personal and third-party treatment; these allocations are the manipulated factors.,1,True,Uses human participants on MTurk.,1,True,Random assignment and controlled incentive structure.,1,False,Conducted online via MTurk rather than in-person lab.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,"Study is empirical, not purely analytical.",1,4,"Groups of four: one dictator and three punishers; in this condition, focal punisher and one other subject are the observed allocation targets.",1,1,Single-shot structure; no repeated rounds indicated.,0.95,0,"Dictator makes a binary choice (50-50 vs. 10-90) for allocations to punisher and recipient, fitting ‘all-or-nothing’ as defined (0) more than continuous contribution.",0.9,0,Dictator begins with $1 and chooses whether to give $0.10 or $0.50 to each target; initial funds are fully in dictator’s account.,1,N/R,No public-good multiplier present.,1,0,No opportunity for communication mentioned.,0.9,0,Participants only see relevant allocations and their own payoffs; no summary information presented.,0.7,0,Anonymity is preserved; no IDs shown.,0.9,0,No rewards mechanism in Study 2.,1,1,Participants are told about the single-shot structure of the game.,0.8,1,Punishers have a punishment budget to reduce dictator’s earnings.,1,0.1,Punishment spending allowed in $0.10 increments from a $0.50 budget.,1,0.2,Each spent increment reduces dictator’s kept share by 20% in this condition as in others.,1,0,Only punishment is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Dictator has $1 endowment for each punisher-recipient; per-allocation endowment is $1.,1,N/R,Only overall descriptives of dictator behavior reported; no condition-specific contribution rate.,0.4,N/R,Mean allocations by condition are not reported.,0.4,N/R,Efficiency outcome is not calculated or reported.,0.2,N/R,No group payoff statistics are given.,0.2,United States,Study 2 recruitment limited to U.S. MTurk workers.,1,Mean 34.85 years (SD 10.96),Applies to the entire Study 2 sample including this condition.,1,"188 women, 212 men (across all Study 2 conditions)",Condition-specific gender not reported; using overall Study 2 distribution.,0.9,N/R,No educational information for MTurk sample.,0.01,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"In the self-and-other condition, focal punisher knows both how dictator allocated to them personally and to another recipient. Punisher has a $0.50 budget and can punish in $0.10 increments (20% reduction in dictator’s kept amount per increment). Using the strategy method, punishers choose punishment and make valuation inferences for all four combinations of fair/unfair allocations to self and other. This condition tests whether information about personal mistreatment overrides information about third-party mistreatment in motivating punishment.",Describes the critical structural novelty of this condition that differentiates it from other Study 2 conditions.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.81.057104.md,"Simulation – spatial public goods game with punishment, mutation-free baseline (μ = 0)","Condition explicitly described as the spatial public goods game with C, D, PC, PD on a square lattice without mutations (μ = 0), forming the baseline phase diagram in Fig. 1.",1,"Punishment fine f and punishment cost c varied; mutation rate μ = 0; synergy factor r = 3.5; four strategies C, D, PC, PD on L×L lattice.","Phase diagram in Fig. 1 is for μ = 0 and r = 3.5, with axes f and c; strategies and lattice structure described in model section.",1,False,Study is a spatial evolutionary game simulation on a lattice with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation; purely numerical simulation.,1,True,They simulate strategy dynamics on an L×L grid with Monte Carlo updates and measure stationary fractions ρ_s.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria; all results are from simulations.,1,400,"They use square lattices of size L×L with L ≥ 400. The minimal stated size is 400, but exact L for baseline not fixed; use 400 as a conservative representative.",0.4,1000000,They run up to 10^6 iterations (Monte Carlo steps) until transients die out; treat this as the number of rounds.,0.6,1,"Cooperators contribute a fixed amount of 1 and defectors contribute 0; there is no choice of intermediate amounts, so it is effectively an all-or-nothing contribution game.",1,N/A,"No human endowment or initial allocation concept; contributions are fixed model rules, so default contribution proportion is not applicable.",1,0.7,Each group of size G = 5 has contributions multiplied by synergy factor r = 3.5 and divided equally among 5 players; MPCR = r/G = 3.5/5 = 0.7.,1,N/A,"No communication between agents in the simulation model, and no human participants.",1,0,Agents do not see explicit payoff summaries; only local payoffs are used for imitation probability.,1,0,Model has no identities or display mechanisms; punishment is purely payoff-based.,1,0,There is no reward mechanism at all in the model.,1,0,Simulation agents do not have information about remaining rounds; this concept is not modeled.,1,1,PC and PD strategies impose punishment fine f on defectors at personal cost c.,1,1,"Each punisher invests cost c per unit of normalized defectors π_d; in the public goods literature, this is effectively a factor of 1 from cost parameter to payoff change per unit. We record the per-unit cost factor as 1 and treat c as part of the tech parameter.",0.5,1,Each punisher pays cost c×π_d and each defector suffers fine f×π_p; the punishment “technology” is linear in cost and fine. We code this as 1 unit effect per unit parameter.,0.4,0,Model includes only punishment; there is no rewarding of cooperators.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,1,Each cooperative individual contributes 1 to the public good in each group; treat this as the effective per-round endowment in the model.,0.7,N/R,"They report only fractions of strategies ρ_s, not average contributions as a fraction of endowment.",0,N/R,"While individual contributions per strategy are fixed (1 for C/PC, 0 for D/PD), aggregate contribution amounts over the population are not explicitly reported as outcome variables.",0.3,N/R,They do not report efficiency as total payoff relative to maximum; only phase composition (fractions of strategies) is presented.,0,N/R,Group or average payoffs are not quantitatively reported; focus is on stationary strategy fractions.,0,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model is a numerical lattice simulation without human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; group size G = 5 (Neumann neighborhoods); four strategies: cooperators (C), defectors (D), punishing cooperators (PC), punishing defectors (PD); payoffs from contributions multiplied by synergy factor r and divided equally; punishment fines f and costs c applied proportionally to local densities of punishers/defectors; strategy updates via pairwise comparison with Fermi rule (K = 0.5).",Summarizes rules of the model as described in the methods section.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.81.057104.md,"Simulation – spatial public goods game with punishment, varying mutation rate μ (PC phase example: c = 0.6, f = 0.6)","Condition described in Fig. 2 where fractions of strategies are plotted as a function of mutation rate μ for fixed c = 0.6, f = 0.6, which at μ = 0 lies in the PC phase.",1,"Mutation rate μ varied from 0 to approximately 1; punishment fine f = 0.6; punishment cost c = 0.6; synergy factor r = 3.5; four strategies C, D, PC, PD on L×L lattice.",Text around Fig. 2 states fractions in dependence of μ for c = 0.6 and f = 0.6; r remains 3.5 as in main simulations.,1,False,This is a simulation study without human participants.,1,False,"Condition is part of a computational model, not an empirical controlled experiment.",1,False,No physical lab; purely computational.,1,True,They run Monte Carlo simulations over varying μ and record stationary fractions of strategies.,1,False,No analytical derivations; all results are numerical.,1,400,"They state simulations were performed for L ≥ 400, with up to 1600 near phase boundaries. For Fig. 2 they do not specify L; we record the minimal mentioned size 400.",0.4,1000000,They determined stationary fractions after transient time up to 10^6 iterations.,0.6,1,"As in the base model, contributions are 1 for C/PC and 0 for D/PD; no continuous choice.",1,N/A,Simulation agents do not have an endowment allocation decision; contributions are fixed by strategy.,1,0.7,Same game structure as baseline: MPCR = r/G = 3.5/5.,1,N/A,No communication or messaging is modeled; no human participants.,1,0,Agents only use payoffs for local imitation; there is no display of summaries.,1,0,Identities and displays are not modeled; punishment is anonymous and purely payoff-based.,1,0,No rewarding strategy exists.,1,0,No representation of time horizons to agents.,1,1,PC and PD punish defectors with fine f at cost c.,1,1,"Cost per unit of normalized defectors is c; as in baseline, we record the linear per-unit factor as 1.",0.5,1,Fine applied linearly in the fraction of punishers; we record a unit linear technology.,0.4,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this model.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this model.,1,1,Each cooperative strategy contributes 1 in each group; treat this as effective per-round endowment per group interaction.,0.7,N/R,Only stationary fractions of strategies are reported as a function of μ; no aggregate contribution rate is given.,0,N/R,"While contributions follow from strategy composition (C/PC vs D/PD), exact reported numeric contribution amounts are not provided.",0.3,N/R,"No explicit efficiency metrics reported; only fractions of C, D, PC, PD for each μ.",0,N/R,Group payoffs are not numerically summarized; focus is on strategy fractions.,0,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is a numerical simulation on a lattice.,1,"Same spatial public goods game structure as the baseline condition (square lattice, G = 5 Neumann neighborhoods, four strategies C, D, PC, PD, synergy factor r = 3.5, Fermi imitation with K = 0.5), augmented with a mutation process where, with probability μ per elementary step, a player randomly changes strategy independent of payoffs or neighbors.",Summarizes the rules relevant to the varying mutation-rate simulations.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.81.057104.md,"Simulation – coarsening dynamics without mutation and without defectors (C and PC only, μ = 0)","Condition described around Fig. 3: time evolution of ρ_PC in the absence of defecting strategies and mutations, starting from 60% PC and 40% C, showing slow logarithmic coarsening.",1,"Strategy set restricted to C and PC only; initial fractions ρ_PC(0) = 0.6, ρ_C(0) = 0.4; mutation rate μ = 0; punishment parameters as in Fig. 3 (not numerically specified, but same as main PC-phase setting).",Text: “in the absence of defecting strategies and mutations (solid green line)... initial state was assumed to consist of 60% punishing cooperators and 40% cooperators.”,0.9,False,This is a simulation on a lattice without human participants.,1,False,"Computational model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No physical lab; numerical simulation only.,1,True,They simulate time evolution of ρ_PC and show coarsening behavior.,1,False,"Coarsening described via simulation and analogy to voter model, not solved analytically.",1,2560000,"Fig. 3 caption: simulations on a grid of size 1600×1600; player count = 1600*1600 = 2,560,000.",1,10000,Fig. 4 snapshots and Fig. 3 show dynamics up to t = 10000 iterations; we record 10000 as a representative time horizon for the illustrated coarsening.,0.7,1,"Same game rules: C/PC contribute 1; here both existing strategies are cooperators, but contributions remain fixed at 1; there is no partial contribution choice.",1,N/A,No human endowment decisions; contributions fixed by strategy.,1,0.7,"The underlying public goods game rules (r = 3.5, G = 5) remain unchanged; MPCR = 3.5/5.",1,N/A,No communication or human agents present.,1,0,No explicit display of others’ payoffs; only local imitation.,1,0,Agents have no identities; punishment only affects payoffs mechanically.,1,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,Agents do not track or observe remaining time steps.,1,1,"Punishing cooperators (PC) are present and pay punishment costs in presence of defectors, although in this specific condition defectors are absent so punishment terms are effectively neutral.",0.8,1,Same linear punishment-cost structure as in the full model; we encode the per-unit factor as 1.,0.4,1,Punishment technology remains as defined in main model even though there are no defectors present in this condition.,0.4,0,No rewarding strategies included.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,1,C and PC contribute 1 each round per group; treat that as the per-interaction endowment.,0.7,N/R,They only present the time evolution of ρ_PC; contributions are trivially 1 for all players but are not reported as an explicit dependent variable.,0.3,N/R,No explicit numeric reports for aggregate contribution amounts; only fractions of PC and C are plotted.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency is not reported; focus is on coarsening speed and fixation.,0,N/R,No numeric payoffs are presented for this condition.,0,N/A,No human subjects; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Condition is a large-scale lattice simulation only.,1,"Underlying game is the same spatial public goods game, but only C and PC strategies are present; initial configuration has 60% PC and 40% C randomly distributed on a 1600×1600 lattice; mutation rate μ = 0; evolution follows voter-model-like logarithmic coarsening to an absorbing monomorphic state (C-only or PC-only).","Summarizes the specific coarsening setup described around Fig. 3 for the no-mutation, no-defector case.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.81.057104.md,Simulation – coarsening dynamics with very small mutation rate (μ = 10^-6) starting from C and PC mixture,"Condition described in Fig. 3 and Fig. 4: time evolution of ρ_PC and ρ_D with μ = 10^-6, starting from identical initial PC/C distribution as the no-mutation case, showing accelerated fixation due to occasional mutants (D, PD).",1,"Mutation rate μ = 10^-6; initial fractions ρ_PC(0) = 0.6, ρ_C(0) = 0.4; strategies C, D, PC, PD available (though D and PD initially absent); public goods and punishment parameters as in PC-phase setting of main model.","Fig. 3 caption: dashed line for μ = 10^-6; text: same initial state (60% PC, 40% C); strategy mutations occasionally create defectors and punishing defectors.",1,False,"Simulation study with lattice agents, not human subjects.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No physical laboratory; code-based simulation.,1,True,"They run Monte Carlo simulations with mutation, tracking ρ_PC and ρ_D over time.",1,False,"Effects are illustrated via simulations, not derived analytically.",1,2560000,"Fig. 3 caption: grid of size 1600×1600; hence 2,560,000 players.",1,10000,"Fig. 4 snapshots show times 0, 900, 1200, 10000; Fig. 3 also displays dynamics until fixation; 10000 iterations is the maximum explicit time mentioned.",0.8,1,"Contribution rule unchanged: C/PC contribute 1, D/PD contribute 0.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation decisions exist; agents’ contributions are strategy-determined.,1,0.7,"Underlying game remains with r = 3.5 and group size 5, giving MPCR 3.5/5.",1,N/A,No communication or human interface.,1,0,Agents use only their and a neighbor’s payoffs locally for imitation; no global summaries are displayed.,1,0,Identity revelation is not part of the model.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,Agents do not observe or condition on total or remaining iterations.,1,1,Punishing cooperators and punishing defectors exert punishment with fine f and cost c per the core model.,1,1,Linear punishment cost structure as in core model; a cost parameter c multiplies normalized density of nearby defectors; recorded here as unit cost factor.,0.4,1,Punishment fine acts linearly in the number of nearby punishers; same as core simulation.,0.4,0,Rewards are not modeled in any condition.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,1,"As in the rest of the paper, each cooperating agent contributes 1; interpret this as the per-interaction endowment.",0.7,N/R,"They report time paths of ρ_PC and ρ_D, but not explicit aggregate contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,Aggregate contribution amounts over time are not reported numerically.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency measures (payoff relative to maximum) are shown; emphasis is on speed of fixation and temporary rise of defectors.,0,N/R,Group payoffs or averages are not provided for this dynamic condition.,0,N/A,No human subjects in simulations.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Condition is a numerical evolutionary simulation.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a 1600×1600 lattice with periodic boundaries and four possible strategies (C, D, PC, PD). Initial configuration has only C and PC (60% PC, 40% C). At each elementary step, with probability μ = 10^-6 an agent mutates to a random strategy among {C, D, PC, PD}; with probability 1−μ, standard Fermi imitation of a randomly chosen neighbor occurs. Occasional appearance of defectors and punishing defectors breaks the symmetry between C and PC and greatly accelerates fixation to the PC-only absorbing state.",Summarizes dynamics and mutation mechanism described around Figs. 3 and 4.,1
10.1098_rspb.2011.0805.md,Peacetime – Ultimatum Game – Player B,"Condition defined by time period (pre- and post-war combined as peacetime), game type (Ultimatum) and role (Player B), whose behaviour is the focus.",1,Conflict period: peacetime (pre- and post-war combined),Main comparison is between wartime vs peacetime; pre- and post-war were statistically similar and combined.,1,True,Human participants (senior citizens) playing incentivized games.,1,True,Controlled economic games with random assignment to roles and fixed rules.,1,True,Run in a single housing facility in Tel Aviv under controlled conditions with pen-and-paper protocols.,0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported; all decisions from human subjects.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as a standalone study; focus on empirical results.,1,2,Ultimatum Game played in pairs: Player A and Player B.,1,1,Each individual played each game once; no repetition of the same game with same partner.,0.9,1,"Player A could propose any split in NIS 10 increments; not restricted to all-or-nothing, so contribution space is continuous.",1,0,Endowment initially entirely under Player A's control; none is pre-allocated to a shared/public account.,0.9,N/R,"Ultimatum Game is a bargaining game, not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.",1,0,Interactions were anonymous and implemented via paper strategy method; no mention of communication.,0.9,0,No mention that participants saw others’ earnings or punishment summaries; only their own outcomes.,0.8,0,Partners were anonymous; punishments (rejections) not linked to identifiable individuals.,0.9,0,UG does not include explicit rewards; and partners were anonymous.,1,0,One-shot games; no mention of a displayed round counter.,0.9,1,"Player B can reject an offer, destroying payoffs for both; used as costly punishment measure.",1,40,"In UG, if Player B rejects an offer x to self, she forfeits x. Average unfair offers driving analysis are <40% of 1