onditions in the Dutch university lab.,0.8,Mean age 20.19 years; undergraduates,Same participant pool as entire main study.,1,69% female,Sample-level gender distribution applies to all conditions.,1,Undergraduate students,All main study participants are undergraduates.,1,On site,Conducted in university lab cubicles via computer.,1,"One-shot incentivized trust game: investor interacts with a partner described as previously having punished an unfair allocator by spending €1.50 out of €10, reducing allocator’s payoff by €3. Partner’s behavior in the trust game is preprogrammed to return 50% of the tripled transfer.",Summarizes the weak-punisher signal context and trust game structure for this condition.,1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2012.05.014.md,"No Feud (NF) – Heterogeneous returns, no punishment","Uses the paper’s treatment label 'No Feud (NF)' and adds concise description of its defining features (heterogeneous returns, no punishment).",1,"Punishment/feuding possibility: none (no punishment stage); Returns from public account: heterogeneous (two players mi=0.5, two players mi=0.3); Normative conflict: present.","Treatment differs from others by (i) absence of any punishment or feuding, while maintaining (ii) heterogeneity of marginal return mi and thus normative conflict.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with student subjects making contribution decisions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments and fixed groups.,1,True,Conducted in the Experimental Economics Laboratory at the University of Melbourne.,1,False,No computer or agent-based simulations described.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not purely analytical derivations.",1,4,“Subjects are assigned to groups of four players.” applies here as well.,1,10,Duration of the experiment (10 periods) stated for this treatment.,1,1,"Players choose ci ∈ {0,…,20}, i.e., any integer amount from 0 to full endowment, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each period begins with private endowment E$20 and players choose how much to move into public account; initial allocation is entirely private.,1,0.4,"Each E$1 contributed yields 0.3 to each of two low-return + 0.5 to each of two high-return players: total return=1.6. Group size=4, so MPCR=1.6/4=0.4.",1,0,Decisions made 'without communication'; no chat or discussion allowed.,1,1,"End of each period, participants see total group contribution, each individual’s contribution, type (low/high return), and earnings – a summary of others’ actions/outcomes.",1,0,"No punishment in this treatment, so identities of punishers cannot be shown.",1,0,No rewarding mechanism exists.,1,1,Duration of experiment (10 periods) is common knowledge; implies subjects know total number of rounds.,1,0,Described explicitly as 'No Feud' treatment with no punishment or feuding; only contribution stage exists.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,0,No positive sanctioning or reward mechanism mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"“At the beginning of each period, each participant is given an endowment of E$20.”",1,0.1765,"Average contribution reported as 3.53 in this treatment. Max per period is 20, so mean fraction=3.53/20≈0.1765.",0.9,3.53,Result 2: 'average contribution ... 3.53 in No Feud.',1,0.6913,Average earnings in No Feud: E$22.12. Max possible average is E$32 (all contribute 20). Efficiency ≈22.12/32≈0.6913.,0.9,N/R,Only average per-person earnings and contributions reported; no explicit average group-total earnings.,0.3,Australia,Experiment run at University of Melbourne with students from that university; country is Australia.,1,N/R,Participants described only as university students; no age statistics reported.,0.3,"Approximately 53% male, 47% female (overall pool; not treatment-specific)","Overall sample: '53.1% men and 46.9% women.' No breakdown by treatment, so assume similar composition.",0.5,University students,Participants were 'students from the University of Melbourne.',1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory at the University of Melbourne with subjects at partitioned computer terminals.,1,"Linear public good; group size 4; heterogeneous marginal returns from public account (two mi=0.5, two mi=0.3) determined by prior 10-minute Encryption Task tournament; fixed groups; E$1=AU$0.15; 10 periods; feedback each period on all individuals’ contributions, types, and earnings; no punishment or reward stages; normative conflict between equal-contributions and equal-earnings rules.",Summarizes core game-structure and procedural details specific to this treatment.,1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2012.05.014.md,Asymmetric (ASYM) – Heterogeneous returns with punishment/feuding,Uses treatment label 'Asymmetric (ASYM)' and notes heterogeneous returns plus punishment/feuding opportunities.,1,"Punishment/feuding possibility: multiple punishment stages with feuds allowed; Returns from public account: heterogeneous (two players mi=0.5, two players mi=0.3); Normative conflict: present.",ASYM is main treatment with both (i) normative conflict via heterogeneous mi and (ii) possibility of repeated punishment stages enabling feuds.,1,True,Lab experiment with human subjects making contribution and punishment decisions.,1,True,Controlled design with treatments and random assignment of subjects to groups and treatments.,1,True,Conducted in Experimental Economics Laboratory at the University of Melbourne.,1,False,No mention of simulation models.,1,False,Focus is on experimental data analysis rather than purely analytical modeling.,1,4,"Groups of four used in all treatments, including Asymmetric.",1,10,“The duration of the experiment (10 periods) ... are common knowledge” for Asymmetric.,1,1,"Stage 1 identical to No Feud: ci ∈ {0,…,20}; any integer contribution allowed.",1,0,Endowment starts as E$20 in private account; subjects choose how much to contribute.,1,0.4,"Contribution stage payoff identical to No Feud: two mi=0.5, two mi=0.3, so MPCR=1.6/4.",1,0,Decisions described as simultaneous and without communication.,1,1,"After each contribution stage and each punishment stage, subjects observe others’ contributions, earnings, and punishment behavior.",1,1,"Interface lets a player connect who punished whom: subjects see points each member assigned to them and to others, linked via fixed IDs (1–4).",1,0,No positive reward mechanism described; only punishment.,1,1,"The 10-period horizon is common knowledge, so participants know total number of periods.",1,1,Multiple punishment stages where subjects pay a fee and assign punishment points.,1,1,“An individual must pay E$1 in order to assign points. This punishment fee allows an individual to assign as many points ... for the remainder of the current period.” So marginal fee per period to unlock punishment is E$1.,1,1,“Each punishment point reduces the earnings of its recipient by E$1.”,1,0,No rewarding or positive points; only reductions in earnings.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each period begins with endowment of E$20 in the contribution stage.,1,0.522,Average contribution in Asymmetric is 10.44. Normalized by endowment 20 gives ≈10.44/20=0.522.,0.95,10.44,Result 2: 'The average contribution in the Asymmetric treatment is 10.44.',1,0.6747,Average earnings in Asymmetric: E$21.59; maximum possible E$32. Efficiency≈21.59/32≈0.6747.,0.9,N/R,"Only per-person average earnings reported, not explicit average group total.",0.3,Australia,Subjects recruited from University of Melbourne in Australia.,1,N/R,No age range or mean age reported.,0.3,"Approximately 53% male, 47% female (overall pool; not treatment-specific)",Overall experiment gender composition given; no per-treatment breakdown.,0.5,University students,Subjects are students from the University of Melbourne.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory with partitioned computer terminals.,1,"Two-part experiment: 10-minute Encryption Task determines which two group members are high-return (mi=0.5) and which two are low-return (mi=0.3). Part 2: in each of 10 periods, players receive E$20, choose ci∈{0,…,20} for public account; payoff after contributions: 20−ci+mi*sum(ch). Multiple punishment stages may follow: each player can pay E$1 (once per period) to assign any number of punishment points to any others; each point reduces recipient’s earnings by E$1. Total punishment per recipient per period capped at stage-1 earnings. Period continues to next punishment stage if at least one point was assigned and some players still have positive earnings; otherwise, next period starts. Feuds defined as sequences of mutual retaliatory punishment across at least three consecutive punishment stages within a period. Fixed groups of four; IDs 1–4; feedback each stage on contributions and detailed punishment matrix.","Summarizes the Asymmetric treatment’s key mechanics, including punishment/feud structure and heterogeneity source.",1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2012.05.014.md,Symmetric (SYM) – Homogeneous returns with punishment/feuding,Uses the paper’s label 'Symmetric (SYM)' and highlights that returns are homogeneous but punishment/feuding is allowed.,1,Punishment/feuding possibility: multiple punishment stages with feuds allowed; Returns from public account: homogeneous (all players mi=0.4); Normative conflict: absent.,SYM mirrors Asymmetric’s punishment structure but sets mi=0.4 for all players to remove heterogeneity and thus normative conflict.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants making contribution and punishment decisions.,1,True,Controlled lab setting with defined treatment and protocol.,1,True,Conducted in the Experimental Economics Laboratory at University of Melbourne.,1,False,No simulations; only lab data.,1,False,Paper presents empirical analysis rather than purely analytical model.,1,4,“Subjects are assigned to groups of four players.” applies to this treatment as well.,1,10,Each session consisted of 10 periods under a single treatment.,1,1,"Contribution stage structurally same as Asymmetric: ci ∈ {0,…,20}.",1,0,Endowment starts as E$20 private; subjects choose contribution to public account.,1,0.4,mi=0.4 for all four players; each contributed unit yields 4*0.4=1.6 total. MPCR=1.6/4=0.4.,1,0,No communication mentioned; same basic structure as Asymmetric except return homogeneity and extra real-effort condition.,0.9,1,"By analogy with Asymmetric, subjects see others’ contributions, earnings, and punishment in each stage; no indication this differs.",0.9,1,"Same punishment interface as Asymmetric: fixed IDs, matrix of points sent/received by each ID.",0.95,0,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,1,10-period horizon common knowledge in all treatments.,1,1,"Symmetric treatment is 'similar to the Asymmetric treatment' with feuding opportunities, i.e., same punishment structure.",1,1,Cost structure identical to Asymmetric: E$1 punishment fee per period unlocks assignment of any number of points.,0.95,1,Punishment technology described for the general feuding treatments; no indication it differs in Symmetric: one point reduces recipient’s earnings by E$1.,0.95,0,No rewarding option mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is available.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Contribution stage same as other treatments: E$20 per period.,1,N/R,Paper states that average contribution is about 3 ECU (≈15% of endowment) higher than Asymmetric but does not report exact mean contribution for Symmetric.,0.2,N/R,"Only relative statement ('about 3 ECU greater than Asymmetric') is provided, not a precise mean.",0.2,0.8016,Average earnings in Symmetric treatment: E$25.65. Maximum possible average: E$32. Efficiency≈25.65/32≈0.8016.,0.9,N/R,Only per-person earnings averages are provided.,0.3,Australia,Experiment done with University of Melbourne students.,1,N/R,No age info reported.,0.3,"Approximately 53% male, 47% female (overall pool; not treatment-specific)",Only overall gender proportions given for the full sample.,0.5,University students,Subjects described as students from University of Melbourne.,1,On site,Performed in a physical laboratory environment.,1,"Structure mirrors Asymmetric treatment: 10-minute Encryption Task first, but here used as a participation threshold (“encode 30 words” to proceed). In the public good game, all four players have identical marginal per capita return mi=0.4 from the public account, chosen so that maximum group earnings under full contribution equal the other treatments. In each of 10 periods, subjects receive E$20, choose ci∈{0,…,20} to contribute; payoff after contributions: 20−ci+0.4*sum(ch). Multiple punishment stages are possible, with same E$1 fee per period to enable punishment and 1:1 effectiveness of points as in Asymmetric. Feuds defined identically, and group size, fixed IDs, and feedback about contributions and punishment are the same. Normative conflict is minimized because all players have equal returns from the public good.","Summarizes elements unique to Symmetric: homogeneous mi=0.4 and different stated role of the Encryption Task, while sharing punishment/feud mechanics with Asymmetric.",1
