10.1073_pnas.0905918106.md,"C1 – Control, low cooperation payoff (Beijing)",Paper describes control experiment C1 as repeated two-player PD without punishment where cooperation yields 1 to partner at cost 1 to self and partner’s cooperation yields 2 to self.,1,Costly punishment option absent; cooperation benefit to partner = 2 units; only two actions: Cooperate (C) or Defect (D).,Control C1 differs from T1 by absence of punishment option and from C2/T2 by lower cooperation benefit (2 vs 3).,1,True,Human university students in Beijing participated in the game.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized matching and specified payoff structure.,1,True,"Conducted in the computer lab at School of Life Sciences, Beijing Normal University.",1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulation mentioned; all results from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper reports empirical game outcomes, not formal analytical model as main method for this condition.",1,2,Described explicitly as a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma repeated game.,1,79,Subjects played on average 79 total rounds per subject; horizon is stochastic but average reported as 79.,0.4,1,"Game is a discrete-strategy PD (C or D), not a continuous contribution; treat as ‘can choose any of the discrete actions’, coded as 1.",0.4,0,"Not a public-goods endowment framework; no default contribution to a public account, so set to 0 by convention.",0.2,N/R,MPCR is not defined for this binary-action PD; no group multiplier reported.,0,0,Interactions were anonymous via computer with no mention of communication or chat.,0.9,0,After each round only the other person’s choice and both payoff scores are shown; no aggregate summaries mentioned.,0.8,0,"No punishment option in this control; IDs are anonymous, and punishment does not exist.",1,0,No reward mechanism described in any condition.,1,0,Subjects did not know how many rounds; only the continuation probability (0.75) was given.,1,0,Control experiments are standard PD with only C and D