10.1371_journal.pcbi.1006347.md,Analytical model – general parameterization,Single coupled replicator–resource ODE system analyzed across parameter regions; no separate named experimental conditions beyond parameter regimes.,0.76,"Intrinsic growth rate r of the renewable resource (slow, moderate, rapid relative to ec and ed); inspection probability p; punishment fine β; defection severity α; maximal individual share bm; population size N; resource carrying capacity Rm.",These parameters are systematically varied in the analytical treatment to derive equilibria; they determine inspection strength (pβ) and resource dynamics (r).,0.9,False,No human or biological subjects; purely theoretical dynamical system.,1.0,False,No controlled experiment; only mathematical analysis of differential equations.,1.0,False,No lab or field setting; purely analytical work.,1.0,False,"This entry refers to the analytical ODE model, not the Monte Carlo simulations.",1.0,True,They derive and analyze fixed points and Jacobians of a coupled replicator–logistic resource system.,1.0,1000,"N is the population size; they often set N=1000 in illustrative examples (e.g., Fig 1: N=1000).",0.6,0,Continuous-time evolutionary model; no discrete rounds.,1.0,1,"Players do not choose a continuous contribution; they select one of two discrete usage levels (bl or bl(1+α)), which is effectively a discrete strategy choice rather than partial contribution. This fits better as non all-or-nothing contributions, but per instructions 1=any amount and 0=all-or-nothing. Here strategies are binary, so treat as all-or-nothing regarding cooperation vs defection.",0.4,N/A,"No public-goods style contribution; resource is withdrawn from a common pool, not contributed.",1.0,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no multiplier or MPCR reported.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/R,Not applicable to an analytical model of strategy fractions; no UI or feedback description of others’ payoffs.,1.0,N/A,No identities or information channels; punishment is centralized and impersonal.,1.0,N/A,There is no rewarding mechanism in the model.,1.0,N/R,Continuous-time model; no notion of displayed remaining rounds.,1.0,1,Defectors are fined β with probability p upon inspection.,1.0,N/R,They define a fine β deducted from defectors’ payoff; no explicit separate cost to punishers or enforcers.,0.3,N/R,"Fine β is a direct payoff reduction, but no per-unit punishment technology like 'damage per coin' is specified.",0.4,0,Model includes only inspection and punishment; no rewards.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/R,Players do not receive a fixed endowment; their payoff equals allocated resource share minus fines.,1.0,N/R,Main outcomes are fractions of cooperators x and resource abundance y; no contribution rate to a public good.,1.0,N/R,They track resource usage (withdrawals) not contributions to a common pool.,1.0,N/R,They discuss sustainability and depletion qualitatively; no normalized efficiency metric is computed.,1.0,N/R,Focus is on strategy fractions and resource level; aggregate payoffs are not reported.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Purely mathematical analysis.,1.0,"Coupled replicator dynamics for share of cooperators x and logistic growth for common resource y with usage rules: cooperators withdraw bl = bm * y/Rm; defectors withdraw bv = bl(1+α). Inspection detects defectors with probability p each time unit, imposing fine β. Resource dynamics: ẏ = r y (1 - y/Rm) - N[bl x + (1-x) bv]. They analyze fixed points and stability for different intrinsic growth rates r relative to ec = bm N/Rm and ed = bm N(1+α)/Rm, and different inspection-punishment strengths pβ.",Summarizes the formal model that defines payoffs and dynamics.,1.0
10.1371_journal.pcbi.1006347.md,"Monte Carlo simulation – slowly growing resource (r = 0.3, p = 0.5, β = 0.5, α = 0.5, bm = 0.5, N = 1000, Rm = 1000)",Distinct simulation regime described under 'Individual-based simulation for slowly growing resource' with specified parameter set.,0.98,"Intrinsic growth rate r = 0.3 (slow, r < ec < ed), inspection probability p = 0.5, punishment fine β = 0.5, defection severity α = 0.5, maximal legal share bm = 0.5, population size N = 1000, carrying capacity Rm = 1000.",All these parameters are explicitly listed for Fig 5 and characterize this simulation condition.,1.0,False,Simulation with abstract agents; no human subjects.,1.0,False,"Numerical Monte Carlo study, not an empirical experiment.",1.0,False,No physical lab or field deployment.,1.0,True,They describe 'Monte Carlo simulations' with agents and update rules.,1.0,False,"This entry refers to the stochastic individual-based model, not the ODE analysis.",1.0,1000,They state N=1000 for this simulation.,1.0,N/R,They iterate time steps t but do not specify the total number of Monte Carlo steps.,0.4,0,"Each agent chooses either to follow the rule (cooperator) or take a larger amount (defector); no intermediate contribution levels are allowed, fitting an all-or-nothing cooperation decision.",0.8,N/A,There is no contribution of endowment; agents withdraw from a common resource.,1.0,N/R,Not a public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1.0,N/A,No humans or communication.,1.0,N/R,Agents have no observation interface; only payoffs determine imitation.,1.0,N/A,No identities or information structure; punishment is centralized.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/R,No explicit representation of number of remaining steps to agents.,1.0,1,Defectors are inspected with probability p and fined β if detected.,1.0,N/R,Fine β is a loss to defectors; no explicit resource cost to an enforcing agent is modeled.,0.3,N/R,β is applied as a direct fine; no linear 'damage per coin spent' structure is introduced.,0.4,0,"Simulation includes only punishment, no rewards.",1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/R,Agents do not receive a fixed per-period endowment; they only withdraw from the resource.,1.0,N/R,Outcome variables are fraction of cooperators x and resource level y(t); no contribution fraction to a public good is reported.,1.0,N/R,"They show phase portraits and qualitative convergence, not average withdrawals by type.",1.0,N/R,"They focus on whether the resource becomes depleted or sustainable, not on a normalized efficiency metric.",1.0,N/R,Group payoffs are neither calculated nor plotted.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Agent-based computational simulation.,1.0,"Population of N=1000 agents on a well-mixed structure. Strategies: cooperator (sl=1) withdraws yl = y(t) * bm / Rm; defector (sv=0) withdraws yv = y(t) * bm * (1+α) / Rm. Whole population is inspected; each defector is detected with probability p and, if detected, pays fine β so payoff Pi = (1+α) y(t) bm / Rm − β with probability p, otherwise Pi = (1+α) y(t) bm / Rm. Cooperators receive Pi = y(t) bm / Rm. Resource updates: y(t+1) = y(t) + r y(t)(1 − y(t)/Rm) − Σ_i [si y(t) bm / Rm + (1−si) y(t) bm (1+α)/Rm]. Strategy update: each agent i may imitate a randomly chosen j with probability q = (Pj − Pi)/M if Pj > Pi, where M is the maximal possible payoff difference. In this slow-growth regime (r < ec < ed), simulations converge to x=1 (all cooperators) and y → 0 (depleted resource), regardless of initial conditions.",Summarized from the Monte Carlo simulation description and Fig 5 narrative.,1.0
