10.1287_mnsc.1110.1466.md,"Experiment 2 – Competitive Group Environment – Punishment – A1 in-group, A2 in-group (from B1’s perspective)","Third-party punishment Experiment 2 in CG, treatment where all three players B1, A1, A2 share same platoon.",0.9,Economic environment: Competitive (CG) with platoon bonus; Group composition: B1 in-group with A1 and A2 (all same platoon).,"One of four compositions, now under CG with 20-point platoon bonus determined by A-player payoffs before punishment.",0.9,True,Based on B-player behavior.,1,True,Randomly assigned group composition and economic environment.,1,True,"Controlled, on-site experiment.",0.9,False,No simulations.,1,False,Empirical condition.,1,4,"A1, A2, B1, B2.",1,1,One-shot PD and one-shot punishment choices.,1,0,PD decision is binary cooperate/defect with full endowment.,1,0,A-players start with private endowments.,1,N/R,PD not linear public good.,1,0,No communication option described.,1,0,Only local PD outcome relevant; no stated summaries.,0.9,0,Anonymity preserved through mailed payoffs and no revelation of identities.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,1,Game known to be one-shot.,0.9,1,B1 has punishment option as in NG.,1,1,Same cost structure: 1 point per deduction point for B1.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces A1’s payoff by 3.,1,0,No voluntary rewarding.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,70,B-player endowment.,1,N/R,Cooperation rates of A-players in Experiment 2 by composition not reported.,1,N/R,Not given.,1,N/R,No combined efficiency calculations with punishment.,1,N/R,No payoff stats for this condition.,1,Switzerland,Same overall sample.,1,19–26,Officer candidate cohort.,0.8,Predominantly male,Swiss Army officer candidates are male.,0.9,"41% apprenticeship, 59% university track",Common for all subjects.,1,On site,Same as other conditions.,1,"In CG, a 20-point platoon bonus is awarded based on A-players’ average PD payoff before deducting punishment points. Thus B1’s punishment choice does not affect probability of winning the bonus; punishment is purely expressive of preferences. Regression (Table 3, CG) shows that in CG, punishment of A1 is not significantly sensitive to A2 being in-group, but rather to whether A1 is out-group; however in this particular composition A1 is in-group so B1’s punishment of A1 is relatively low and weakly differentiated by A1’s behavior (norm-enforcement diminished relative to NG).",Derived from CG punishment regression and discussion; specific composition implies low antisocial punishment.,0.8
10.1287_mnsc.1110.1466.md,"Experiment 2 – Competitive Group Environment – Punishment – A1 in-group, A2 out-group (from B1’s perspective)","Third-party punishment Experiment 2 in CG, treatment where A1 shares platoon with B1 and A2 is from another platoon.",0.9,Economic environment: Competitive (CG); Group composition: B1 and A1 in-group; A2 out-group.,CG condition with only A2 out-group relative to B1.,0.9,True,Based on B1’s punishment decisions.,1,True,Randomized group composition and bonus condition.,1,True,On-site experiment.,0.9,False,No simulation.,1,False,Empirical data.,1,4,Two A-players and two B-players.,1,1,One-shot PD and one-shot punishment.,1,0,Binary cooperate/defect.,1,0,Private endowment.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,No aggregate summaries mentioned.,0.9,0,Identities not revealed.,1,0,No rewards.,1,1,One-shot known.,0.9,1,B1 chooses deduction points.,1,1,1 point per deduction point.,1,3,3-point loss per deduction point to A1.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,70,B-player endowment.,1,N/R,No A-players’ cooperation reported for this composition.,1,N/R,Not given.,1,N/R,No efficiency measured.,1,N/R,No payoff stats.,1,Switzerland,Same sample.,1,19–26,Officer-candidate cohort.,0.8,Predominantly male,Swiss Army officer candidates are male.,0.9,"41% apprenticeship, 59% university track",Same across experiments.,1,On site,Same auditorium experimental setting.,1,"In CG, punishment no longer reflects norm-enforcement conditional on A2’s identity; instead, Table 3 CG columns show A1 out-group status predicts substantially more punishment when A1 defects (3.742***) and when A1 cooperates (2.898***), while A2 in-group status is insignificant. In this composition A1 is in-group so antisocial punishment is not directed at A1; however overall, in CG defection is only weakly more punished than cooperation (Table 4), indicating that punishment ceases to be a strong norm-enforcement device even when victim A2 is out-group.",Derived from CG regressions and discussion on weakened norm enforcement.,0.8
10.1287_mnsc.1110.1466.md,"Experiment 2 – Competitive Group Environment – Punishment – A1 out-group, A2 in-group (from B1’s perspective)","Third-party punishment Experiment 2 in CG, treatment where A1 is out-group and A2 is in-group to B1.",0.9,Economic environment: Competitive (CG); Group composition: B1 and A2 in-group; A1 out-group.,"CG condition with only A1 out-group, A2 in-group.",0.9,True,Punishment data from human subjects.,1,True,Controlled experimental design.,1,True,On-site controlled environment.,0.9,False,No simulations.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,4,Two A and two B players.,1,1,Single round of PD and punishment.,1,0,A-players binary cooperate/defect.,1,0,Endowment starts private.,1,N/R,Not a MPCR public-goods game.,1,0,No communication described.,1,0,Only local PD outcome used.,0.9,0,Identities hidden.,1,0,No reward process.,1,1,Participants know it is one-shot.,0.9,1,B1 can punish A1.,1,1,Cost per deduction point is 1.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces A1’s payoff by 3.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,70,B-player endowment.,1,N/R,A-players’ cooperation in this composition not separately reported.,1,N/R,Not given.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric.,1,N/R,No group payoff statistic issued.,1,Switzerland,Same sample.,1,19–26,Same cohort.,0.8,Predominantly male,Officer candidates are male.,0.9,"41% apprenticeship, 59% university track",Same as entire sample.,1,On site,In-person sessions at army training center.,1,"Key antisocial-punishment composition: A1 is out-group, A2 in-group. In CG, regression (Table 3 columns (2) and (4)) shows A1 out-group coefficient 3.742*** when A1 defects and 2.898*** when A1 cooperates, while A2 in-group is insignificant. Figure 3(b) shows higher punishment for out-group A1 irrespective of cooperation/defection; B1 punishes out-group A1 heavily even when A1 cooperates, demonstrating antisocial punishment triggered by intergroup competition.",Directly summarizes regression and figure findings for CG with A1 out-group.,1
10.1287_mnsc.1110.1466.md,"Experiment 2 – Competitive Group Environment – Punishment – A1 out-group, A2 out-group (from B1’s perspective)","Third-party punishment Experiment 2 in CG, treatment where both A1 and A2 are out-group relative to B1.",0.9,Economic environment: Competitive (CG); Group composition: B1 in-group with neither A1 nor A2 (both out-group).,Fourth composition with both A1 and A2 from different platoons than B1.,0.9,True,Punishment decisions from human subjects.,1,True,Controlled experiment with random compositions.,1,True,On-site experimental setup.,0.9,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,4,Two A and two B players.,1,1,One-shot interaction.,1,0,PD choices are all-or-nothing contributions.,1,0,Private endowment.,1,N/R,PD game.,1,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,Only PD actions matter.,0.9,0,Players are anonymous.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Known one-shot design.,0.9,1,B1 can assign deduction points.,1,1,Each deduction point costs B1 one point.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces A1’s payoff by 3.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,70,B-player endowment specified as 70 points.,1,N/R,No cooperation rate reported for this composition.,1,N/R,Not provided.,1,N/R,No efficiency measure.,1,N/R,No group payoff stats.,1,Switzerland,Same participant pool.,1,19–26,Same cohort.,0.8,Predominantly male,Swiss male officer candidates.,0.9,"41% apprenticeship, 59% university track",Applies to sample.,1,On site,Conducted in army auditorium.,1,"In CG, antisocial punishment is generally directed toward out-group A1 regardless of A2’s status. For both A1 defects and A1 cooperates, regression shows significant positive A1 out-group coefficients, indicating heavier punishment of out-group A1 even when both A1 and A2 are out-group. Norm-enforcement (stronger punishment of defection vs cooperation) is weaker in CG than in NG (Table 4) and almost disappears, while antisocial punishment of out-group A1 remains.",Summarizes CG 