10.1126_science.1178883.md,Symbolic punishment (SP) treatment,Paper describes two treatments: harmful punishment (HP) and symbolic punishment (SP); this row represents the SP control condition.,1,Punishment impact on recipient: symbolic (no monetary loss or gain for recipient when hurt); cost to donor for punishment fixed at 50 francs; cost/benefit of help fixed at -200/+250 francs.,Only experimental difference across treatments is whether punishment is harmful (250-franc loss) or symbolic (0) to recipient; other parameters identical.,1,True,Study uses 140 human participants in laboratory decision-making experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized matching and exogenously varied treatment conditions.,1,True,Described as laboratory experiment with cohorts; typical CREED / university lab setup; no field context mentioned.,0.9,False,"This treatment involves human subjects, not purely computational agents.",1,False,No formal closed-form analytical model is the focus of this treatment; it is an experiment.,1,10,Each independent cohort consists of 10 participants; SP treatment has 6 such cohorts.,1,100,"Participants are 'repeatedly (100 rounds), anonymously, and randomly matched into donor-recipient pairs.'",1,0,"Donor has three discrete actions (help, pass, hurt), not a continuous contribution level or binary all-or-nothing contribution to a public good.",0.9,0,Game is not a standard contribution game with an endowment allocation between private/public accounts; no default contribution proportion is defined.,0.2,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined or inferable.,0,0,Interactions are described as anonymous with no mention of communication or chat between participants.,0.9,1,Donor always observes recipient’s three most recent donor actions and may also buy second-order information; this is a summary of recipient behavior/earnings-relevant actions.,1,1,Recipient observes the donor’s action each round; donor actions are tied to an identifiable counterpart within the pair for that round.,0.8,1,Same visibility applies to helping choices as to hurting; recipient observes donor’s action.,0.8,1,"Endgame effects are reported, which implies that subjects know or can infer the finite horizon (100 rounds).",0.7,1,Donor can choose to 'hurt' recipient; this is the punishment option.,1,50,In both treatments 'the donor loses 50 francs for hurting.',1,0,"In SP, 'a hurt recipient loses or earns no francs'; impact per punishment on recipient is 0 francs.",1,1,"Donor can 'help' recipient, which is a costly action that benefits the recipient.",1,200,In both treatments 'the donor loses 50 francs for hurting or 200 francs for helping.',1,250,Recipient earns 250 francs when receiving help in both treatments.,1,N/R,Paper reports per-action payoffs but not any per-round or initial endowment of francs.,0,0.607,"Aggregate frequency of helping is 60.7%; treating 'help' as a cooperative act, normalized fraction is 0.607.",1,N/R,Help is a discrete action; no token amount beyond payoff changes is defined as a 'contribution amount.',0,N/R,"Total or maximum possible group payoffs are not reported, so relative efficiency cannot be computed.",0,N/R,Only average individual payoffs by strategy are given; no aggregate payoff per cohort or treatment is reported.,0.2,"Netherlands / USA (mixed, exact shares N/R)","Authors are based in Netherlands and USA; experiment location is not explicitly stated; likely mainly Dutch lab subjects, but this cannot be confirmed from text.",0.2,N/R,No age range or mean age is described in the text.,0,N/R,No information on gender composition is provided.,0,N/R,"Typical lab experiments use university students, but this is not explicitly reported here.",0.2,On site,Described as a lab-style experiment with cohorts in an experimental economics setting; no indication of online participation.,0.9,"Indirect helping game with punishment: 6 independent cohorts of 10 participants, random anonymous matching into donor-recipient pairs each round for 100 rounds. Only donors choose actions (help, pass, hurt). Help: donor -200, recipient +250; pass: 0,0; hurt: donor -50, recipient 0. Donors always see recipient’s three most recent donor actions (first-order info) and can pay a small price (amount N/R) to see what first-order information recipients previously observed (second-order info). Recipients observe donors’ actions. Analysis focuses on rounds 1–90 due to endgame effects.",Summarized from the procedural description of the indirect helping game and treatment SP parameters.,1
10.1126_science.1178883.md,Harmful punishment (HP) treatment,Paper describes two treatments: harmful punishment (HP) and symbolic punishment (SP); this row represents the HP main condition.,1,Punishment impact on recipient: harmful (recipient loses 250 francs when hurt); cost to donor for punishment fixed at 50 francs; cost/benefit of help fixed at -200/+250 francs.,Only experimental difference across treatments is whether punishment causes a 250-franc loss (HP) versus zero impact (SP).,1,True,Human participants in controlled laboratory experiment.,1,True,"Treatment is part of a randomized, parameter-manipulated experimental design.",1,True,Conducted with cohorts in an experimental economics environment; no field/online context indicated.,0.9,False,"Human-subject experiment, not purely computational.",1,False,No closed-form analytical model as primary method for this treatment.,1,10,Each independent cohort consists of 10 participants; HP treatment has 8 such cohorts.,1,100,Participants are repeatedly matched for 100 rounds.,1,0,"Donor chooses among three discrete actions, not contribute-all vs contribute-nothing to a public fund.",0.9,0,No initial allocation between private/public accounts is defined; game is not a contribution game in this sense.,0.2,N/R,Game is not a linear public good; marginal per capita return is undefined.,0,0,Interactions are anonymous and no communication is mentioned.,0.9,1,Donors see recipient’s three most recent donor actions and can buy additional second-order information.,1,1,"Recipients observe donors’ actions (including hurt); thus, identity within the pair is revealed for punishment events.",0.8,1,Recipients similarly observe helping actions by their donors.,0.8,1,Endgame behavior indicates knowledge of finite 100-round horizon.,0.7,1,"Donor has option to 'hurt' recipient, which in HP has material consequences.",1,50,Donor loses 50 francs for hurting in both treatments.,1,250,"In HP, 'a hurt recipient loses 250 units of our experimental money, francs.'",1,1,Donor can help recipient at a cost to self and benefit to recipient.,1,200,Donor loses 200 francs when helping in both treatments.,1,250,Recipient gains 250 francs from help.,1,N/R,No per-round or initial endowment is specified.,0,0.612,Donors choose help with 61.2% frequency on average across eight HP cohorts; this is the fraction of cooperative acts.,1,N/R,Help is a discrete choice; no token quantity of contribution is defined.,0,N/R,Insufficient information to compute realized vs maximum possible group payoffs.,0,N/R,Only strategy-specific and not treatment-wide average payoffs are reported; overall group payoff per round is not given.,0.2,"Netherlands / USA (mixed, exact shares N/R)",Author affiliations include Dutch and US universities; subject pool location is not specified.,0.2,N/R,No direct information.,0,N/R,No information provided.,0,N/R,Likely university students but not stated.,0.2,On site,Described as a lab experiment with cohorts; no indication of online recruitment or remote participation.,0.9,"Indirect helping game with harmful punishment: 8 independent cohorts of 10 participants, random anonymous donor-recipient matching each round for 100 rounds. Donors choose one of three actions: help (donor -200, recipient +250), pass (0,0), hurt (donor -50, recipient -250). Recipients observe donor’s action each round. Donors always see the recipient’s three most recent donor actions (first-order info) and can pay a small price (amount N/R) to view the information their recipients observed when acting in those past rounds (second-order info). Analysis focuses on rounds 1–90 to avoid strong endgame effects. Observed overall helping frequency is 61.2% and punishment frequency is 3.4%, mostly targeted at defectors and cautious defectors.",Compiled from treatment description and behavioral result summaries.,1
