10.1038_nature07184.md,Aquarium experiment – male singleton trials,"In the lab experiment, each sex was tested alone and in pair; this condition is males feeding alone.",1,Social context: male cleaner feeds alone from plate (singleton); food preference structure: preferred prawn vs less-preferred flakes; plate removed upon prawn bite.,Methods describe singleton trials where each cleaner is offered the plate alone; results compare male singleton to male pair behavior.,1,True,Live cleaner fish in aquaria are observed under controlled conditions.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of social context (alone vs pair) and food distribution; order balanced.,1,True,Conducted at Lizard Island Research Station aquaria.,1,False,No computational simulation for this condition.,1,False,"This part is experimental, not analytical.",1,1,Singleton trials involve one cleaner fish with the plate.,1,8,"Methods: each individual experienced 16 test trials; for the sequence, half started with four pair, eight singleton, four pair, implying 8 singleton trials per fish.",0.9,1,Fish can choose which items (prawn vs flake) to eat; not limited to all-or-nothing contributions.,0.9,0,All food is initially available on the plate; there is no pre-allocation to a 'public' vs 'private' account; effectively equivalent to an endowment entirely in private control.,0.6,N/R,Not a PG game; no multiplier.,1,N/A,Non-human animal subjects; no communication channel like chat.,1,N/A,No on-screen summaries; fish just see food on plate.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism in this lab task.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards other than food.,1,N/A,Fish are not informed of the number of remaining trials.,1,0,"Task structure has only removal of plate upon prawn bite; this is a consequence of choice, not a punishment decision by another agent.",0.8,N/A,No deliberate punishment action.,1,N/A,No deliberate punishment action.,1,0,No peer-controlled reward mechanism; prawn and flake are simply payoffs.,0.8,N/A,No explicit rewarding.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding.,1,N/R,Number of items per plate (prawn and flake) not fully quantified in text; only positions are described.,0.6,N/R,"Outcome is preference-based feeding (ratio of prawn to flake eaten), not mapped to contribution proportion.",1,N/R,"They report ratios and frequencies (e.g., how often prawn eaten) rather than total counts as contributions.",1,N/R,No explicit comparison to maximal possible payoff across both fish; just ratios and aggression.,1,N/R,Singleton condition involves one fish only; no group payoff reported.,1,Australia,"Aquarium experiments conducted at Lizard Island Research Station, Great Barrier Reef, Australia.",1,N/R,Sizes of fish are given but not ages.,1,Male cleaner fish,Condition explicitly concerns male members of the male-female pairs.,1,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Controlled lab-like setting in aquaria at a research station.,1,Outcome: ratio of prawn items eaten per flake items eaten for males in singleton trials; higher ratios indicate more 'cheating' (eating preferred items leading to plate removal) relative to cooperative feeding against preference.,Summarizes behavioral measure and interpretation for this condition.,1
