e=4.,1,0,No reward action; only help and punishment defined.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Payoffs are accumulated points from interactions; no per-round token endowment.,1,0.98,"They report ""mean rate of cooperation = 0.98"" for generations 18000–20000 across 20 replicates; treating cooperation as contribution.",1,N/R,"No raw count of helps per individual or tokens reported, only proportion (rate).",0.8,N/R,No explicit ratio of achieved vs maximal group payoff reported.,0.5,N/R,Group’s total points or average payoff per generation is not numerically reported.,0.5,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computer simulation with artificial agents.,1,"Individual-based simulation of a helping game with roles donor/receiver. In each interaction, donor decides to help or not; helping costs c=1 to donor, gives benefit b=2 to receiver; if donor does not help, receiver may punish, paying cost s=1 and reducing donor’s payoff by e=4. Each agent has m=30 interactions per generation, in either role. Fitness equals total points from interactions; reproduction is proportional to fitness with mutation rate μ=0.02 on helping and punishment strategy components. There are 10 helping strategies and 4 punishment strategies (40 combinations) using punishment scores k1, k2, k3 and image score Is that range from −5 to +5. Initial population consists of non‑punishing defectors.","Summarized from section 2(b), Table 2, Table 3, and Figure 1 caption.",1
10.1098_rspb.2010.1275.md,"Simulation – parameter sweep, benefit b=2 (Figure 2a–d)","Figure 2 panels a–d show simulation results with benefit of cooperation b=2, varying s and e and fixed c=1, across 20 replicates for generations 18000–20000.",0.95,"Cost/benefit grid with c=1, b=2; costs of punishment s and of being punished e varied over multiple values; n=500, m=30, mutation rate μ=0.02, no perception error (ε=0).","Text for Figure 2: ""Evolution of punishment at cost of helping c=1 and benefit b=2 (a–d)... for different costs s to the punisher and costs e of being punished""; earlier they state n=500, m=30, μ=0.02, no mention of errors here so ε=0.",0.9,False,Agent-based computer simulations.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No human lab setting.,1,True,Described as simulations with finite population n.,1,False,"These are numeric results, distinct from analytical model.",1,500,They used n=500 in all simulations.,1,30,Each player has on average m=30 interactions per generation; used in all simulations.,0.9,1,Binary help and punishment decisions.,1,N/A,No endowment/public good.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No communication among agents.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,"Reputation exists as scores, but no identities in an interface.",1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,Simulated agents; no display.,1,1,Receivers can punish in all simulations.,1,N/R,s is varied across a range of values; specific numeric grid not fully listed.,0.4,N/R,"e is varied; different s,e combinations across panels and parameter grid; no single value.",0.4,0,No reward behavior described in these simulations.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Payoffs accumulated from interactions; no fixed per-round endowment.,1,0.98,They report for a typical parameter set with b=2 (Figure 1 described in same section) a mean cooperation rate of 0.98 when punishment emerges; but Figure 2 shows many parameter combinations without a single overall average. Using 0.98 as indicative but not specific to each grid point.,0.3,N/R,No raw number of cooperative acts per round reported for b=2 grid.,0.4,N/R,No numerical efficiency values reported across the parameter sweep.,0.3,N/R,"Only frequencies of strategies are plotted, not payoffs.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computer-based simulations.,1,"Same helping/punishment game and 40 strategies as baseline simulation. Cost of helping c=1; benefit of help b=2. Punishment cost s and damage e are varied systematically; average frequencies of key strategies (x_N, y_P, z_P, a_P, others) are reported over generations 18000–20000 and 20 replicates for each (s,e) combination. Results show that punishing discriminators (z_P) predominate for a large set of parameters when e>c and s<b.",Summarized from figure 2 caption and main text describing parameter sweeps.,0.9
