10.1007_s10683-010-9266-0.md,"Give-P (Give frame, with punishment)",Authors define Give-P as Give frame for contributions with punishment opportunities in second stage.,1,Frame of contribution decision: Give (contribute to group project); Punishment opportunity: Yes (peer-to-peer costly punishment stage),Treatment varies framing (Give vs Take) and punishment opportunities (Yes vs No). This row: Give frame with punishment.,1,True,Uses human subjects in a controlled game.,1,True,Randomized lab treatment with exogenous manipulation of frame and punishment availability.,1,True,Clearly described as conducted in a laboratory (CeDEx).,1,False,No simulation-only study included.,1,False,Theoretical discussion is background; core results are experimental.,1,3,Groups of three in all treatments.,1,1,One-shot public goods game followed by a single punishment stage.,1,1,Contribution decision: any amount 0–20.,1,0,"As in Give frame: 20 tokens endowed privately, then subjects choose how many to contribute.",1,0.5,Same payoff function as Give-N for contribution stage.,1,0,No communication/cheap talk mentioned during the game.,0.9,1,Players must see each other’s contributions in order to assign punishment and report emotions.,0.95,0,Standard Fehr-Gächter style punishment stage; paper does not mention revealing identities; groups are anonymous within lab.,0.7,0,No reward option exists.,1,1,Game is explicitly one-shot; subjects know there is only one interaction.,1,1,Second stage: each subject can assign up to five punishment points to each other member.,1,1,Each punishment point assigned costs the punishing player one Guilder.,1,2,Each punishment point reduces punished player’s payoff by two Guilders.,1,0,"Only punishment points, no positive rewards, are available.",1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,20,Give frame instructions: endowment of 20 tokens.,1,0.3605,Average contribution 7.21 tokens out of 20; 7.21/20 = 0.3605.,1,7.21,Table 1: Average contribution leve