10.1007_s10683-010-9266-0.md,"Take-P (Take frame, with punishment)",Authors define Take-P as Take frame for contributions with punishment opportunities.,1,Frame of contribution decision: Take (withdraw from group project); Punishment opportunity: Yes (peer-to-peer costly punishment stage),Treatment manipulates both frame and punishment; this is Take frame with punishment.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment in a laboratory environment.,1,True,Conducted in CeDEx lab at University of Nottingham.,1,False,No separate simulation study.,1,False,Focus is empirical analysis; theory is background only.,1,3,Groups of three players for all treatments.,1,1,One-shot contribution stage followed by one punishment stage; no repetition.,1,1,"In Take frame, players can choose any withdrawal amount 0–20.",1,1,"Initially 60 tokens in group project; each subject controls 20 and must decide how many to withdraw, so all tokens start in the public project.",1,0.5,"Same contribution-stage payoff function as Take-N, with MPCR of 0.5.",1,0,No mention of discussion or chat among participants.,0.9,1,Punishers must observe each partner’s withdrawal/remaining tokens to assign punishment and to evaluate emotions.,0.95,0,Paper does not state that punishment identities or sources are revealed; standard implementation keeps them anonymous within group feedback.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Subjects know game is one-shot.,1,1,Same punishment stage as Give-P: subjects can assign punishment points.,1,1,Each punishment point costs the punisher one Guilder.,1,2,Each punishment point reduces punished player’s payoff by two Guilders.,1,0,"Only punishment is feasible, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,20,Each group member controls 20 tokens in project; operational decision endowment.,1,0.3205,Average contribution 6.41 tokens out of 20; 6.41/20 = 0.3205 (interpreting contribution as tokens left in project).,1,6.41,Table 1: Average contribution level 6.41 tokens in Take-P.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed or reported at treatment level.,0,N/R,Group payoff statistics not provided.,0,United Kingdom,Same UK lab and subject pool as other treatments.,0.95,N/R,No detailed age data.,0,N/R,No information on gender composition for this treatment.,0,Undergraduate students from various fields,Identical subject pool description across treatments.,0.95,On site,Laboratory experiment at CeDEx using z-Tree software.,1,"Two-stage one-shot game. Stage 1: Take-frame linear public goods/common-pool equivalent with 60 tokens initially in project, each player controls 20 and chooses how many to withdraw; own payoff equals withdrawn tokens plus 0.5 times tokens left in project. Non-incentivised beliefs about others’ behavior elicited after contribution decision. Stage 2: peer punishment identical to Give-P (0–5 punishment points to each other, 1 Guilder cost to punisher, 2 Guilder loss to target). After punishment, subjects report emotions towards each other player on 7-point scale for 13 emotions (including anger and guilt).",Summarized from Take frame and punishment-stage description for Take-P.,1
