10.1038_srep07843.md,Treatment m=0 (no memory),"Paper describes four treatments distinguished by memory m=0,1,3,5; this row is the m=0 treatment.",1,Memory length of past actions shown (m=0; no information about others' past actions or identities),"Independent variable manipulated across treatments is memory m (0,1,3,5).",1,True,Human participants in lab sessions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized assignment and predefined protocol.,1,True,Conducted in Experimental Economics Lab at Universidad Carlos III de Madrid.,1,False,No mention of agent-based or numerical simulations; all data from human sessions.,1,False,"Paper reports empirical results, not a standalone analytical model as a condition.",1,22.6,"Groups of 17,23,24 and two of 25 participants for the m=0,1 treatments. Exact split per m not given; report approximate average across five groups (113/5≈22.6).",0.3,25,Game consisted of 25 rounds.,1,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D, not a continuous contribution amount; coded as all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Prisoner’s Dilemma with actions C/D, not an endowment-allocation public goods; by convention set to 0 (no automatic contribution).",0.5,N/R,"Payoffs: cooperator receives 7*n_C, defector 10*n_C; no per-capita return from a common project is defined as in a linear PGG.",0,0,Participants played anonymously and could not interact during the experiment; no communication channel mentioned.,1,0,"Only information described is n_C and, depending on treatment, list of past actions; no aggregate payoff or punishment summaries reported.",0.7,0,Punishment mechanism is breaking links; link-breaking inherently identifies the punished neighbour but there is no explicit ID revelation mechanism beyond existing links. Coded as 0 as there is no separate punishment stage with identity display.,0.3,0,"No explicit rewards or associated identities beyond forming links, which are not framed as rewards.",1,0,Number of rounds (25) was unknown to the participants.,1,1,Players can punish by breaking ties with defectors; described as punishing mechanism implicit in network plasticity.,1,0,Linking with defectors is costless and no explicit point cost is mentioned for breaking a link; coded as 0 cost per punishment action.,0.4,0,"No direct token deduction from targets; effect is opportunity cost via future foregone interactions, not a parametrized per-token punishment technology.",0.3,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism such as costly positive transfers is implemented; forming links is not framed as material reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs per round are calculated from actions and neighbours; no fixed per-round or initial endowment reported.,0,0.239,"Table 1, fraction of cooperative actions for m=0 is 0.239.",1,N/R,Binary C/D choice; no token-valued contribution amount specified.,0,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff reported.,0,N/R,No aggregate or per-group payoff data in text or table.,0,Spain,Participants recruited from Universidad Carlos III de Madrid (Spain).,1,N/R,Age range or mean not provided; only that volunteer pool is open to students at any degree/level.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,University students (undergraduate and postgraduate),"Volunteer pool open to students enrolled at the University at any degree and level, including postgraduate.",0.9,On site,Sessions conducted in Experimental Economics Lab; subjects present on specific dates.,1,"Iterated multi-player Prisoner's Dilemma on a dynamic network. Initial network: ring with nearest and next-nearest neighbours (degree 4). Each round has two phases: (1) simultaneous C/D choice with all current neighbours; payoffs per neighbour: cooperator gets 7, defector gets 10; (2) rewiring phase where players can break any existing links and propose up to five new links to non-neighbours. In m=0, participants know only the total number of cooperating neighbours (n_C) but not which neighbours did what, and they receive no identity or history information about any participant.","Summarized from description in Experiments and Methods sections, specialized for m=0 information structure.",1
10.1038_srep07843.md,Treatment m=1 (memory of 1 action),Paper defines treatment by memory length m; this row for m=1.,1,Memory length of past actions shown (m=1; last action of every participant visible),Independent variable is number of past actions shown; here m=1.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled random-assignment experiment with predefined treatments.,1,True,Conducted at Experimental Economics Lab at Universidad Carlos III de Madrid.,1,False,No computational-only conditions.,1,False,No separate analytical condition.,1,22.6,"Same pool of groups as m=0,1 combined; exact split not specified, so approximate as average of five groups.",0.3,25,All treatments used 25 rounds.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect choice.,1,0,No explicit endowment allocation; binary PD action. By convention set to 0.,0.5,N/R,"Game is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with pairwise payoffs, not a linear PGG.",0,0,Participants could not interact during the experiment.,1,0,"Information described is individual past actions, not payoff or punishment summaries.",0.7,0,Punishment only via link-breaking; no explicit punishment stage with identity revelation beyond network adjacency.,0.3,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Total number of rounds unknown to participants.,1,1,Players can punish by cutting ties with defectors; explicitly discussed as punishment.,1,0,No direct monetary cost is mentioned for breaking a tie; only forgone future interactions.,0.4,0,No parametric per-token punishment intensity; impact is structural (loss of link).,0.3,0,No explicit costly rewarding mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments per round are not specified; payoffs only via PD interactions.,0,0.492,"Table 1, fraction of cooperative actions for m=1 is 0.492.",1,N/R,"Only binary action, no token-valued contribution amount.",0,N/R,Efficiency measure not computed in paper.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff values reported per treatment.,0,Spain,All sessions at Universidad Carlos III de Madrid.,1,N/R,Age data not given.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0,University students (undergraduate and postgraduate),Same volunteer pool description applies to all treatments.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental economics lab.,1,"Same iterated multi-player Prisoner's Dilemma on a dynamic network as other treatments. Initial network: ring with degree 4. Each round: (1) simultaneous C/D choice with all neighbours; payoffs per cooperating neighbour: 7 to a cooperator, 10 to a defector; (2) rewiring phase where players may sever any links and propose up to five new links to non-neighbours; proposals must be accepted by the target to form a new link. For m=1, subjects see the last action (cooperate or defect) of every participant in the experiment when deciding on linking.",General game description plus specifying information structure for m=1.,1
