10.1103_PhysRevE.95.052316.md,"Spatial public goods game with C, D, P, and E strategies on a square lattice","The paper presents a single spatial evolutionary game model (square lattice PGG with four strategies: cooperators C, defectors D, punishers P, excluders E). All reported parameter sweeps and subsets (e.g., only D and E present) are variations within this same simulation framework, not separate experimental designs with humans.",0.95,Synergy factor r; punishment fine β; punishment cost γ; exclusion cost ε; initial strategy composition; system size L; interaction topology (square lattice vs random regular graph),"These parameters are systematically varied in the simulations (e.g., high-r–high-γ region, high-r–low-γ region, low-r region, ε sweeps, two-strategy D–E model, different L and topology). They constitute the main independent variables of the computational experiments.",0.95,False,No human participants; the study uses evolutionary Monte Carlo simulations of strategies on graphs.,1.0,False,There is no controlled experiment with human subjects; it is a computational modeling study.,1.0,False,No laboratory or field setting with human participants; only simulations.,1.0,True,"The methods section describes evolutionary dynamics implemented via Monte Carlo steps on lattices and random graphs, which are numerical simulations.",1.0,False,"While payoff functions are written analytically, the results rely on numerical simulations rather than closed-form analytical solutions.",0.95,25,"In a PGG on the described square lattice, interactions occur in overlapping groups of size G=5 (one focal + 4 neighbors). For this schema, playerCount is interpreted as the group size G rather than total population L², which varies (e.g., L=100, 1200, 1600, 6000).",0.8,0,"The model is an evolutionary process with many Monte Carlo steps until stationarity; there is no discrete, predetermined number of game rounds as in human experiments. Using 0 to indicate not applicable in this context.",0.7,1,"Each cooperator, punisher, and excluder contributes a fixed amount c=1; defectors contribute 0. There is no decision about partial vs all-or-nothing contributions by agents; strategy fully determines contribution. This corresponds best to an all-or-nothing structure, so coded as 1.",0.7,N/A,No human endowment allocation decision; strategies directly encode whether a player contributes c=1 or 0. Concept of initial allocation between public/private is not applicable to this simulation.,1.0,N/R,"MPCR (per-capita return) is not explicitly computed. The synergy factor r multiplies total contributions, which are then divided by group size G=5 and further modified by exclusion/punishment; an overall constant MPCR is not reported.",0.6,N/A,No communication between agents is modeled.,1.0,N/A,Agents are simple strategies without informational interfaces; no feedback displays are described.,1.0,N/A,"There are no identities in the human sense; punishment/exclusion are encoded in payoff functions, not as visible actions between identified players.",1.0,N/A,No rewarding mechanism in the model; only punishment and exclusion.,1.0,N/A,Simulated agents do not receive information about number of remaining rounds.,1.0,1,"Strategy P imposes fines β on defectors at personal cost γ, defined in the payoff equations.",1.0,N/R,"The per-unit cost-to-impact ratio is not framed as in human experiments. γ is defined as the maximum cost to a punisher in a group of 4 defectors, while β is the maximum fine to a defector in a group of 4 punishers. A single scalar 'punishmentCost' per unit cannot be cleanly extracted.",0.4,N/R,"Similarly, the exact mapping from each unit of punishing expenditure to fine on defectors is not specified as a simple multiplier.",0.4,0,No rewarding or positive incentives apart from the public goods benefit are modeled.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,1,"Each contributor (C, P, E) pays c=1 into the public good, which acts effectively as their per-round endowment for contribution decisions.",0.9,N/R,"Outcomes are reported in terms of frequencies of strategies (fractions of C, D, P, E) and extinction probabilities, not normalized contributions. While one could infer average contributions from fractions of contributing strategies, specific numeric results are not given in the text.",0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric contribution levels per group or per player over time are reported beyond the generic c=1 rule.,0.4,N/R,"Group payoffs or overall efficiency (actual vs maximum possible) are not numerically reported; only qualitative outcomes (e.g., defector-free vs coexistence) are described.",0.3,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff values are reported; focus is on strategy frequencies and extinction probabilities.,0.3,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,All results are from agent-based simulations on lattices and random regular graphs.,1.0,"Evolutionary spatial public goods game on a square lattice (and random regular graph) with overlapping groups of size G=5, synergy factor r, punishment (fine β, cost γ) and social exclusion (cost ε). Strategies: D (defector, contributes 0), C (cooperator, contributes 1), P (punisher, contributes 1 and punishes D), E (excluder, contributes 1, monitors and excludes D at additional cost). Selection via pairwise imitation with Fermi update (K=0.5). System sizes L vary (e.g., 60–6000; also N=10^6 for random regular graphs). Initial conditions include random and patchlike clusters. Outcomes are stationary fractions of strategies and extinction probabilities as functions of parameters.","Summarizes detailed model description from Methods and Results sections (group size, topology, strategies, payoff structure, update rule, parameter ranges).",0.95
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2015.12.012.md,Positive emotions – Without announcement,One of four 2×2 between-subject treatments: positive mood induction crossed with no announcement of punishment-stage content.,1,Induced emotion: positive (happiness) via Mr. Bean clip; Punishment stage content: not announced ex ante (subjects only told there are two stages).,Design section describes factors: emotions (positive vs negative) and announcement (without vs with announcement).,1,True,Human subjects in a laboratory public goods experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments with controlled manipulation of emotions and information.,1,True,Conducted at University of Birmingham lab using z-Tree and ORSEE.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model developed or solved.,1,3,"Design: ""individuals randomly form groups of 3 members"".",1,1,Game is explicitly one-shot.,1,1,"Subjects choose gi with 0 ≤ gi ≤ 20, any integer amount allowed, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each subject endowed with 20 tokens they decide how many to contribute; all start private.,1,0.5,Each contributed token increases every member’s payoff by 0.5 ECUs; group size is 3 so marginal per capita return is 0.5.,1,0,No communication or chat channel mentioned; standard anonymous VCM.,0.9,1,"Second stage: subjects ""can see the profile of contributions of the other two group members""; also observe punishment effects via earnings, treated as summary info.",0.9,0,No indication that identities are revealed; typical protocol is anonymous punishment.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism in design.,1,1,"Subjects are instructed they will play a two-stage, one-shot game; they know there is only one contribution decision.",0.9,1,Design is VCM with punishment; second stage allows assigning punishment points.,1,1,Each punishment point costs punisher 1 ECU.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces recipient’s payoff by 3 ECUs.,1,0,Only costly punishment is described; no positive sanctions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each subject is endowed with 20 tokens.,1,6.19,"Average contribution is 6.19 tokens out of 20; normalized rate = 6.19/20 = 0.3095. However, user asks for actual values; table reports 6.19, so we record contributionAmount separately and keep rate consistent with raw/20. Here I enter 6.19/20=0.3095.",0.95,6.19,Table 3: average contribution levels; this cell is 6.19.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency (payoff/maximum payoff) reported or directly computable per treatment from available aggregates.,0.2,20.52,"Table 7: ‘Total earnings’ for this treatment is 20.52 ECUs; interpreted as average individual payoff; group payoff would be 3×20.52, but since DV is ambiguous, we record individual average.",0.7,UK,Subjects recruited at University of Birmingham in the UK.,0.95,N/R,No age information reported.,0.0,Mixed; proportion not reported,Gender dummy used in regressions; exact breakdown not provided.,0.7,University students,Recruitment from University of Birmingham subject pool implies mainly students.,0.9,On site,Computerized lab experiment using z-Tree at the university.,1,"One-shot 3-player VCM with punishment; each token kept yields 1 ECU; each contributed token yields 0.5 ECU to each player; punishment points range 0–5 per target; mood induced via film clip; this cell pools data from one-stage and undisclosed-two-stage versions where content of second stage is not announced at start, since authors find no contribution differences and pool them.",Summarized from Design and footnote 9 describing pooling of one-stage and undisclosed two-stage games into 'without announcement'.,0.95
