10.1016_j.jebo.2009.03.011.md,"Small team, low productivity (n=4, MPCR=0.3)","Condition with four-person teams and q/n=0.3, as described in the design (small n, low q/n).",1,Team size n=4; MPCR=q/n=0.3; punishment allowed with cost 1 and impact 2; strangers matching over 10 rounds,Independent variables explicitly varied across treatments are group size (4 vs 8) and q/n (0.3 vs 0.7); punishment parameters and strangers protocol held constant across treatments.,1,True,Human subjects recruited from University of Massachusetts participated in public goods experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab-style randomized public goods game with specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab setting on site with student subjects and controlled instructions.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; all data from human experiments.,1,False,"Paper contains a theoretical model, but this row refers to the empirical experiment, not the model.",1,4,This treatment uses four-person teams ('four-person teams').,1,10,Each session lasted ten periods.,1,1,"Subjects could contribute 'any fraction of the endowment' to the public account, implying continuous choice 0–25, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Each period subjects are 'given an endowment of w=25 EMUs, and allowed to contribute ... any fraction ... to a public account, the remainder going to the subject's private account'; endowment starts fully in private account.",0.95,0.3,"Design states 'two values of q/n, 0.3 and 0.7'; this condition is the low productivity case, so MPCR=q/n=0.3.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard anonymous computer-based VCM with no communication.,0.9,1,"After contributions, 'the total group contribution, the subject's gross earnings, and the contributions of other team members (presented in random order) were revealed'; this is summary info about others.",1,0,"Groups are anonymous and subjects assign sanctions to 'others'; identities beyond within-round subject IDs not reported, and punishment is individual but not linked to real identity; assume anonymized IDs with no persistent identification.",0.6,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,Standard lab protocols and text 'each session lasted ten periods' strongly imply subjects were informed about the fixed horizon.,0.9,1,Players can assign costly sanctions to others after seeing contributions.,1,1,Cost of inflicting a penalty of two EMUs is one EMU for the punisher; s_ij is punisher expenditure.,1,2,Payoff formula: '-∑ s_ij - 2 ∑ s_ji'; each unit spent on punishing another costs target 2 EMUs.,1,0,Only sanctions/reductions are described; no positive reward option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,25,Each player receives an endowment of w=25 EMUs at the beginning of each round.,1,0.54,"Pooled across treatments, average contributions 'end at 54% in period ten'; no treatment-specific means reported, so using pooled terminal contribution rate as approximate outcome.",0.4,N/R,Only pooled means and some regression coefficients reported; no treatment-specific average contribution amounts given.,0,N/R,No explicit or computable measure of realized group payoff as fraction of first-best is reported by treatment.,0,N/R,Paper does not report average total or per-capita payoffs by treatment.,0,United States,"Subjects recruited from the general student population at the University of Massachusetts, USA.",1,Primarily university students; exact age not reported,Described as 'general student population'; no age summary statistics given.,0.6,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,0,University students (undergraduate/graduate; level not further specified),Recruited from general student population at a university; no breakdown by level.,0.8,On site,"Sessions conducted with subjects physically present, seated at 'partially isolated computer terminal' in the lab.",1,Strangers design with random reassignment each round; 10 periods; punishment stage after contribution stage; contributions and individual others’ contributions revealed in random order; payoff: π_i = w[σ_i + q(1-σ̄)] - Σ_j s_ij - 2 Σ_j s_ji; q chosen so that q/n=0.3; endowment 25 EMUs; punishment technology: 1 EMU spent reduces target’s payoff by 2 EMUs; no rewards.,"Summarizes detailed structural rules described in the experimental design section for this treatment (n=4, low q/n).",1
