10.1016_j.jebo.2009.03.011.md,"Large team, high productivity (n=8, MPCR=0.7)","Condition with eight-person teams and q/n=0.7 (large n, high q/n).",1,Team size n=8; MPCR=q/n=0.7; punishment allowed with cost 1 and impact 2; strangers matching over 10 rounds,"Design's high q/n, large group-size treatment.",1,True,Human laboratory experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments; controlled payoffs and information.,1,True,In-lab computerized sessions.,1,False,No non-human simulation used for this condition.,1,False,"Row refers to empirical condition, not the analytical model.",1,8,Eight-person team in this treatment.,1,10,All sessions have 10 periods.,1,1,Subjects can choose any fraction of their endowment to contribute.,1,0,Endowment initially private; contributions voluntarily moved to public account.,0.95,0.7,High productivity with q/n=0.7.,1,0,No text about communication options.,0.9,1,Participants see total group contribution and all individual contributions each period.,1,0,Only anonymous others observed; no evidence that enduring identities of punishers are revealed.,0.6,0,No reward mechanism in experiment.,1,1,"Fixed 10-period structure, standardly told to subjects; implied.",0.9,1,Post-contribution costly punishment allowed.,1,1,One EMU to inflict two EMU loss.,1,2,Targets lose 2 EMUs per 1 EMU spent by punisher.,1,0,Only negative sanctions implemented.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,25,25 EMUs per player per round.,1,0.54,"Pooled data show contributions ending at 54% of endowment; no breakdown by treatment, so this is an approximate rate.",0.4,N/R,Treatment-specific average contribution amounts are not provided.,0,N/R,No quantitative efficiency measure by treatment reported.,0,N/R,No average group payoffs per treatment given.,0,United States,Experiment conducted with University of Massachusetts students in the USA.,1,Primarily university students; exact age not reported,Sample drawn from general student population.,0.6,N/R,Gender composition not described.,0,University students (undergraduate/graduate; level not further specified),Participants are university students.,0.8,On site,In-person lab sessions at computer terminals.,1,"Strangers design; 8 players per group; 10 rounds; endowment 25 EMUs; contribution then punishment stage each round; after contributions, players see group total, their own gross payoff, and each of 7 others’ contributions in random order; they can spend s_ij EMUs to reduce others’ payoffs by 2×s_ij; payoff function π_i = w[σ_i + q(1-σ̄)] - Σ_j s_ij - 2 Σ_j s_ji with q such that q/n=0.7; no positive rewards.","Condenses key institutional details for high-q/n, large-n treatment.",1
10.1086_588262.md,Theoretical signaling game between groups P and Q,Paper presents a formal signaling model with groups P and Q and agent q choosing homogeneity level H̄ to signal type; this is the only clearly defined 'condition-like' setting.,0.9,"q’s type (cooperator t1 vs cheater t2) and chosen degree of homogeneity H̄ (signal level, with threshold H̄*)","Model varies whether q is a cooperator or cheater and the level of homogeneity H̄ selected, which determines equilibrium outcomes.",1,False,No human subjects or measured data; purely theoretical model plus qualitative historical illustrations.,1,False,No controlled manipulation with participants; it is a game-theoretic model.,1,False,No lab or experimental setting; entirely analytical.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations are reported.,1,True,Paper develops and proves propositions in a signaling game framework with formal definitions and equilibria.,1,2,"Signaling game explicitly modeled between a representative p in group P and q in group Q, i.e., two strategic players, despite broader population described.",0.9,2,"Extensive-form game has Nature move (type draw), then q chooses signal, then p decides whether to trade; repeated trade is in payoffs but not modeled as explicit repeated rounds of the game.",0.6,1,"No contribution choice; model is not a contribution game. All-or-nothing coding refers to contribution flexibility, which is not applicable but schema requires numeric; set to 1 as agents choose whether to trade, not how much.",0.2,N/A,No public goods or contributions; purely analytical signaling and trade model with no endowment-splitting decision.,1,N/R,No public good production or linear multiplier structure is defined.,1,0,Model includes only signaling via homogeneity H̄ and no communication stage.,0.9,0,"Players observe only H̄, not any history or summary of others’ payoffs or actions.",1,0,"No explicit punishment stage with identities revealed; punishment is modeled as lost future trade, not as a targeted stage.",1,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identity revelation is modeled.,1,0,"Although future interactions are infinite-horizon in payoffs, there is no interface or feedback showing number of rounds.",1,1,Within-group multilateral punishment is assumed: cheaters lose all future trade with a group if they cheat one member.,1,N/R,Cost of punishing is not parameterized as a separate per-unit cost; only payoffs from cheating vs trading are given.,1,N/R,"No explicit punishment technology (e.g., tokens into fines) is defined; only the consequence of boycott is modeled.",1,0,No explicit costly positive rewards are modeled beyond normal gains from trade.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,"Model specifies per-period payoffs r, θn, and z, but not an explicit initial endowment.",1,N/A,"There is no contribution decision; outcome is trade vs no trade, not contribution level.",1,N/A,No contribution amounts are defined in the model.,1,N/R,"Although the paper notes that the separating equilibrium is socially efficient, it does not define efficiency as a numeric ratio.",0.7,N/R,"Payoffs to individuals and some aggregates like rn are discussed, but no explicit numeric group payoff for a normalized game is given.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model plus qualitative historical narrative.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is analytical and historical; no experimental or observational data with recruited participants.,1,"Analytical signaling game between a representative outsider q and in-group member p, with two possible types of q (cooperator vs cheater), endogenous social distance via chosen homogeneity H̄, partial multilateral punishment within groups, infinite-horizon gains from trade for cooperators, and threshold H̄* such that only patient cooperators find it optimal to invest in social-distance-reducing signals.",Summarizes the key structure of the only formal model in the paper; no other distinct game variants or parameterized conditions are presented.,1
10.1093_restud_rdt022.md,Endogenous DC treatment – Deterrent Cheap formal sanctions (FS) condition when FS is in force,DC is defined as deterrent (s=0.8) and cheap (c=2) FS; this row refers to periods in which groups are actually playing under FS in that treatment.,0.96,Sanction regime = Formal sanctions (FS); sanction rate s = 0.8 (deterrent); fixed cost c = 2 (cheap); voting endogenous,"Within DC, the independent variables distinguishing this condition are the existence of FS, its deterrence level (s=0.8) and fixed cost (c=2), with regime chosen by majority vote.",0.98,True,Human subjects in lab public goods experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized laboratory treatment with imposed parameters.,1,True,"Conducted at Centre for Experimental Economics, University of Copenhagen.",1,False,No computer-only simulations described.,1,False,"This row refers to the experimental part, not the theoretical model.",1,5,“participants are divided into groups of n=5 members”.,1,28,7 phases × 4 periods each in main endogenous treatments; FS can be active in multiple phases but the underlying game length per group is 28 periods.,0.85,1,"Standard VCM where subjects choose how much of 20-point endowment to allocate; implies continuous/any amount, not binary.",0.98,0,Endowment E=20 is given to each subject to allocate between private and group account; starts fully in private account.,0.98,0.4,"“The total amount in the group account is doubled and divided equally among all group members, thus m=0.4.”",1,0,No mention of communication; standard no-communication VCM.,0.9,1,Subjects are informed every period about each group member’s contributions and earnings components via standard feedback; needed for punishment and to compute CE.,0.75,0,For IS it is stated that punished subjects do not know who punished; FS are automatic and not tied to an identity.,0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,"Instructions describe a known finite horizon (7 phases of 4 periods, plus 6 votes); typical lab practice to state this; not contradicted.",0.7,1,FS impose automatic penalties on private allocations when the FS regime is in force.,1,1,"For FS, each 1 point kept in the private account triggers a sanction s=0.8 to the subject and same loss to group total (no redistribution). From group perspective, 1.6 is lost per point; per-unit cost to ‘punisher’ is embedded as institutional cost. We normalise cost per punished unit as 1 token paid to the private account owner in the choice stage that gets sanctioned.",0.4,0.8,Payoff under FS: (E−Ci)(1−s)+mΣCj−c; so each unit allocated to private account causes a loss s to the individual (0.8 here).,0.95,0,No positive sanctioning or rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"“Every period, each participant receives an endowment of E=20 points.”",1,N/R,"Average proportional contributions under FS in DC are described qualitatively (“between 80% and 100%”), but no exact overall mean is reported.",0.4,N/R,"Figures show time paths, but no single scalar average contribution for all FS periods in DC is tabulated.",0.3,N/R,"Cost-effectiveness CE is reported, but not aggregate efficiency as payoff/max-payoff.",0.3,N/R,"Some earnings numbers given for specific phases and comparisons, but not a single overall average group payoff for DC-FS periods.",0.3,Denmark,"Experiment conducted at University of Copenhagen; subjects are students there, majority likely Danish.",0.85,"University students (approx. early 20s, exact ages N/R)",Participants are university students; no specific age statistics given.,0.6,43% female; 57% male (overall sample),"“In all, 43% of participants were women.”",0.95,"University students; 51% freshman economics, 49% other fields",“51% were freshmen economics students... The rest were from many different fields of study at the University of Copenhagen.”,0.95,On site,Physical lab sessions at the Centre for Experimental Economics.,1,"Linear public goods (VCM) with partner matching, 5-person groups, 7 phases of 4 periods; in FS regime, each point kept privately is penalized at s=0.8 and subjects pay fixed cost c=2 per period for FS; payoffs: (20−Ci)(1−0.8)+0.4ΣCj−2.",Summarizes structure and FS parameters for DC condition.,0.98
10.1093_restud_rdt022.md,Endogenous DC treatment – Informal sanctions (IS) condition when IS is in force,"DC label refers to FS parameters, but groups also sometimes choose IS; this row captures periods where the chosen regime is IS in the DC treatment.",0.96,Sanction regime = Informal sanctions (IS); punishment effectiveness σ = 4; no fixed institutional cost; voting endogenous,Key independent variables are presence of IS with σ=4 and absence of FS fixed cost.,0.98,True,Same human-subject lab experiment as main treatments.,1,True,Condition is part of controlled laboratory manipulation.,1,True,On-site sessions in experimental lab.,1,False,No simulation here.,1,False,Not a theory-only condition.,1,5,Groups of n=5 throughout.,1,28,Total length of the repeated game per group; IS can be active in several phases under voting.,0.85,1,Subjects choose any allocation of 20 between private and group account.,0.98,0,Endowment is given privately before any contribution decision.,0.98,0.4,"Same linear public good technology across regimes, m=0.4.",1,0,No communication is described; punishment is via point assignment only.,0.9,1,"Under IS, subjects must observe each other’s contributions to punish; paper notes they always see others’ contributions even when IS absent.",0.9,0,"“Subjects learn the amount of punishment they receive, but not who gave it or how much punishment others receive in total.”",1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Finite horizon and phase structure explained ex ante.,0.7,1,IS regime provides costly punishment opportunities.,1,1,"“In IS, it costs a sender 1 point to reduce the earnings of the receiver by 4 points.” So fee (sender cost) per punishment point is 1.",1,4,Each 1-point fee reduces receiver’s earnings by 4 points (σ=4).,1,0,Only reduction (punishment) described.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same 20-point endowment each period.,1,N/R,"Authors state IS induce contributions similar to deterrent FS in DC, but do not give a single average fraction for DC-IS specifically.",0.4,N/R,No table with average absolute contributions by regime within DC; only plots across treatments.,0.3,N/R,"They compute CE and note high efficiency, but not a normalized efficiency value for DC-IS alone.",0.3,N/R,Some phase averages by regime pooled across treatments; nothing separated out for DC-IS only.,0.3,Denmark,Same subject pool across treatments.,0.85,"University students (approx. early 20s, exact ages N/R)",Same as main description; no ages reported.,0.6,43% female; 57% male (overall sample),Overall experiment composition applies to this treatment.,0.95,"University students; 51% freshman economics, 49% other fields",Same pool description.,0.95,On site,Lab at University of Copenhagen.,1,"Linear VCM with partner matching; under IS, after seeing contributions, each subject may assign up to 10 punishment points to each other member per period at cost 1 per point, each point reducing target’s earnings by 4; punishment cannot reduce target’s period payoff below zero, but senders always pay their own punishment costs.",Summarizes IS mechanism as implemented.,1
