10.1016_0303-2647(95)01548-5.md,"Study 1 – Punish (non-costly punishment, Dictator Game with Taking Option)",Study 1 has three between-subjects response conditions; this row is the Punish condition where Alex takes $5 from Casey in round 3.,1,Type of response to defection: Punish (Alex took $5 from Casey in round 3) in a non-costly punishment DG with Taking Option,"Independent variable is Alex’s response to Casey’s defection (punish, withdraw, no sanction); this row is punish.",1,True,Human participants observed interactions and rated Alex.,1,True,Between-subjects assignment to scripted conditions with controlled vignettes and ratings.,1,False,"Data collected via Prolific as an online study, not in a physical lab.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only human ratings.,1,False,No formal mathematical model is analyzed as a standalone experiment.,1,2,Alex and Casey repeatedly interact in a dyadic Dictator Game with Taking Option.,1,3,Participants observed three rounds of DGwT between Alex and Casey.,1,1,"Giver can give or take in $1 increments from 0 to 5, not all-or-nothing choices.",1,0,This is not a public goods contribution game; endowments are individual. By convention we set 0.,0.3,N/A,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication between Alex and Casey is described; observers just view scripted choices.,0.9,0,Observers see Alex and Casey’s final payoffs only for their condition; no summaries of others’ behavior mentioned.,0.7,1,Observers know which named actor (Alex) punished Casey; identity of sanctioner and target are explicit.,1,1,"When Alex gives money, observers know identities of giver and receiver.",1,0,Participants were told Alex and Casey knew they would interact repeatedly but not for how many rounds; no explicit display of total or remaining rounds.,1,1,Punish condition involves Alex taking $5 from Casey as a negative sanction.,1,0,"In Study 1, punishment is non-costly: Alex reclaims $5 by taking it from Casey, e