10.1016_j.ejor.2008.01.021.md,"Symmetric discrete-strategy e-collaboration game with social punishment (Table 4, generic parameters)","Paper presents a theoretical 2-player symmetric e-collaboration game with discrete cooperate/defect strategies and social punishment δ, summarized in Table 4 and Proposition 1, as a single generic condition.",0.93,"Benefit b, cost of cooperation c, and social punishment δ determining payoff matrix entries (b−c/2, b−c, b−δ) and resulting equilibrium types.","The only things varied conceptually in this game are b, c, and δ, which change whether the game is SD-like or not and which equilibrium (cooperate/cooperate, defect/defect, etc.) is stable.",0.94,False,No human participants; all results are analytical game-theoretic derivations.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it is a theoretical game model with symbolic parameters.,1,False,No lab setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical/agent-based simulations are described for this discrete game; only algebraic analysis.,0.95,True,"The game is solved by closed-form derivations (best responses, Nash equilibria, propositions).",1,2,Explicitly described as a two-player symmetric e-collaboration game.,1,1,Game is presented as a one-shot 2x2 matrix game; evolutionary stability is discussed conceptually but not modeled as repeated discrete rounds here.,0.8,1,"Players can choose cooperate or defect, and may use mixed strategies interpreted as effort; in terms of strategic options the underlying contribution choice is binary, so this is treated as all-or-nothing.",0.8,N/A,No explicit endowment or allocation mechanism; theoretical payoff parameters only and no human subjects.,1,N/R,No linear public good or MPCR concept is defined; payoffs are not derived from a standard PGG multiplier.,1,N/A,No communication protocol or human experiment; purely analytical.,1,0,No notion of feedback or displayed summaries is modeled in the stage game.,0.9,N/A,"Punishment is an abstract social cost δ embedded