10.1046_j.1461-0248.2002.00358.x.md,Analytical model – general asymmetric partner control game,"The paper presents a single general game-theoretical model of an asymmetric interaction between an exploiter (cleaner) and a victim (client), analyzed across parameter values s, c, and k.",1,Exploiter control over duration s; temptation to cheat c; rate of diminishing mutualistic benefits k.,"Model explicitly varies s (relative power of exploiter to prolong encounter), c (reward per unit cost of exploitation), and k (shape of benefit function).",1,False,No human or animal subjects are experimentally manipulated; it is a theoretical model with equations.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it is a purely theoretical/game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,"No laboratory or controlled human/animal testing, only analytical work.",1,False,"Results (x*, t*, W_e, W_v) are solved analytically; figures plot closed-form solutions, not stochastic or numerical simulations.",0.9,True,"Model is defined with payoff functions, derivatives, and closed-form equilibrium solutions; this is analytical game theory.",1,2,Model describes an interaction between two individuals: one exploiter (cleaner) and one victim (client).,1,1,They explicitly state they focus on a single interaction (‘we focus only on a single interaction’).,1,1,"There is no discrete contribution decision; exploitation level x is continuous. Mapping to contribution game, players can choose any exploitation level, equivalent to continuous action; this fits non-all-or-nothing.",0.7,N/A,No public goods or endowments; purely a dyadic cost–benefit interaction in a theoretical model without human players.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no MPCR or linear multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocols.,1,N/A,No informational interface for human players; theoretical dyadic model.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or identities; a continuous dyadic interaction.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism in a multi-actor setting; just mutualistic benefits.,1,N/A,No user interface or repeated rounds in an experimental sense.,1,0,Model includes only exploitation and termination of interaction; no separate costly punishment choice as in standard punishment games.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment option in the formal model.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,Mutualistic benefits b(t) are not voluntary rewards but automatic benefits of interaction; there is no discretionary reward decision.,0.9,N/A,No explicit rewarding action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No endowment or tokens; payoffs are continuous utilities b(t) minus costs.,1,N/R,"Model tracks exploitation level x and duration t, not contribution to a public good.",1,N/R,No contributions are defined; only exploitation and interaction duration.,1,N/R,"They compute payoffs W_e and W_v, but do not normalize by a cooperation maximum as an efficiency metric.",1,N/R,They present separate payoffs for exploiter and victim; no explicit combined group payoff reported as a variable.,1,N/A,No human or experimental participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is purely theoretical, with no human subjects or empirical experiment.",1,"Sequential asymmetric dyadic game between exploiter (cleaner) and victim (client). Victim chooses preferred duration in response to exploitation level x; exploiter may prolong interaction depending on control parameter s. Mutualistic benefits b(t)=1−exp(−t/k) accrue to both in absence of exploitation. Exploitation imposes cost x·t on victim and gives benefit c·x·t to exploiter. Termination rule based on conflict-resolution framework of Yamamura & Higashi: interaction ends when marginal cost to victim exceeds threshold relative to marginal benefit to exploiter, with threshold ratio s/(1−s). Equilibrium exploitation x*, duration t*, and payoffs W_e, W_v are solved analytically as functions of s, c, and k.",Summarizes the structure and payoffs as described in the model section.,1
10.1046_j.1461-0248.2002.00358.x.md,Observational comparison – 61 non-predatory cleaner-fish client species,"The paper reports observational data (from Bshary 2001) for 61 non-predatory client species, relating mean client size, interaction time, and biting rate.",0.9,Client species identity and client body length (size).,They compare mean interaction times and biting rates across 61 client species and regress these against client length.,0.9,True,These are field observations of cleaner wrasse and client fish interactions.,1,False,"The paper states these are ‘observations’ and references Bshary 2001, which reports natural interactions; no manipulation is described here.",0.8,False,"Interactions occur on coral reefs in the wild (field observation), not in a laboratory.",1,False,"These are real animal observations, not simulated data.",1,False,"This specific part is empirical, though used to compare with an analytical model.",1,2,Each focal interaction is between one cleaner wrasse and one client fish.,0.9,1,They analyze single encounters’ duration and biting rates; no repeated-game structure is described per dyad.,0.8,1,No contribution decision; natural continuous behavior (cleaning vs biting). Mapping to contribution games implies non-binary action space.,0.6,0,"In this natural mutualism there is no endowment or default ‘public’ contribution; concept does not apply, but schema requires numeric type for empirical studies. Set to 0 to indicate no default contribution in absence of an explicit public account.",0.2,N/R,Not a public goods context; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,No controlled communication channel is part of a designed game; interactions are natural without imposed ‘chat’.,0.7,0,There is no experimental interface showing summaries; fish may observe behavior directly but not summaries as defined for lab games.,0.7,0,"Identities are inherently known in dyadic animal encounters, but there is no experimental punishment stage where IDs are ‘shown’; coded as 0 per lab-game definition.",0.5,0,No explicit reward stage or identity revelation mechanism.,0.5,0,Fish are not shown the number of rounds/encounters; this concept does not apply in the observational field setting.,1,0,"The specific dataset used here concerns non-predatory clients; while some clients may pursue cleaners in other contexts, this paper’s observational comparison is about cleaning and biting rates, not explicit costly punishment actions in a game structure.",0.4,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism is parameterized or quantified in these observations.,1,N/A,No quantified punishment technology is reported.,1,0,Cleaning benefits are not discretionary ‘rewards’ in a game-theoretic punishment/reward sense; there is no separate costly rewarding decision.,0.7,N/A,No explicit reward option is parameterized.,1,N/A,No quantified reward technology is reported.,1,N/R,No notion of monetary or token endowment; natural behavior only.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are biting rate and interaction duration, not contributions to a cooperative pool.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts are defined or measured.,1,N/R,They do not calculate efficiency (payoff relative to maximum possible) for the mutualism; only qualitative payoffs are discussed.,1,N/R,"They do not report combined quantitative payoff for cleaner + client; only relationships between size, time, and biting rate.",1,N/R,Field location is not specified in this paper; only that interactions involve Labroides dimidiatus on reefs. Original Bshary 2001 might specify but is not in this text.,0.3,N/R,Age of fish is not reported.,1,N/R,Sex of fish in these observations is not reported here.,1,N/A,Non-human animal subjects.,1,Field experiment,"Although the paper calls them ‘observations’, they are systematic field observations of naturally occurring interactions on reefs, which fits best under ‘Field experiment’ vs ‘Observational’ in the provided categorical options involving non-lab, non-online settings.",0.6,"Natural cleaner wrasse–client fish mutualism on coral reefs. Dataset (from Bshary 2001) includes 61 non-predatory client species. For each species, mean client length (body size), mean duration of cleaning interaction, and mean cleaner biting rate (exploitation), inferred from client ‘jolt’ responses, are recorded. Analyses: linear regression of biting rate on reciprocal of client length, and regression of interaction duration on client length. Both regressions show highly significant positive slopes (P < 0.001) and intercepts not significantly different from zero, indicating longer and less exploitative interactions with larger clients.",Summarizes the structure and key outcome variables of the observational comparison as described around Figure 4.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0045662.md,Study 1 – Two-round trust game with 3rd-party cheating reputation (four partner profiles pooled),"Study 1 uses one trust-game structure; partners differ only in reputation (0,1,3,4 cheating items), but all other structural parameters are identical. The paper reports behavior aggregated over these profiles for design features.",0.7,"Partner 3rd-party cheating reputation: number of scenarios in which partner chose the cheating option (0, 1, 3, or 4 of 4)","Study 1 manipulates only the partner’s disposition-to-cheat profile (0,1,3,4 cheating responses).",1,True,Undergraduates played incentivized games; sham partners were computer-controlled but subjects are human.,1,True,"Partners’ reputations and behaviors were experimentally controlled; trust, punishment, and cooperation were measured under randomized partner profiles.",1,True,Subjects were brought into a computer lab to participate.,1,False,No purely computational or agent-based simulations; only scripted sham partners interacting with humans.,1,False,No formal analytical model is implemented as a study condition.,1,2,Each interaction is described as a two-person trust game (subject and one partner).,1,2,Explicitly: “two-round trust games (TGs)” with roles reversed in round 2.,1,1,"Subjects can choose trust vs distrust, punishment vs no punishment; these are discrete moves, not continuous contribution amounts, so the public-goods all-or-nothing definition is not directly applicable. Mapped to 1 because choices are not restricted to ‘contribute everything or nothing’ in a contribution sense, but field is about contribution flexibility; here there is no contribution variable.",0.3,N/R,"This is a dyadic trust game, not a standard contribution game with default allocations between private and public accounts.",0,N/R,"No linear public-goods multiplier is defined; payoffs are specified as outcome nodes, not via MPCR.",0,0,No mention of communication between partners; interactions are via preprogrammed computer interface only.,0.9,0,Subjects see partner’s questionnaire-based cheating profile but not general payoff or punishment summaries across others.,0.8,0,Games are anonymous over computer; no indication that personal identities of punishers are revealed beyond the dyad.,0.8,0,There is no explicit reward mechanism; only punishment is described.,1,1,"Design is described to subjects as a two-round game with roles reversed; they are “informed that they had been randomly assigned to the role of first mover in round 1, and second mover in round 2,” implying awareness of the 2-round structure.",0.9,1,"In round 1, after defection, subject can pay to punish partner; in round 2 partner can punish subject.",1,0.3,"If the subject trusts and partner defects, subject can “pay $.30 to punish their partner by $1.20.” Cost per act is $0.30.",1,1.2,The punishment reduces partner’s payoff by $1.20 when subject spends $0.30.,1,0,"No option to pay to reward another player is described; only standard cooperative outcomes from trust, not costly reward actions.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Exact starting amounts for each player at the beginning of the game are not fully specified; only outcome nodes ($0.90, $1.20, $1.50, etc.) and punishment costs are reported.",0.2,N/R,"The study reports probabilities of trusting, punishing, and cooperating but not normalized contribution rates relative to an endowment.",0,N/R,"No continuous contribution amount is chosen; trust is a binary move, and amounts of money invested are not reported as subject-level continuous DVs.",0,N/R,No aggregate efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum possible) is reported.,0,N/R,Group (dyad) total payoffs are not summarized as a DV; only odds ratios for decisions are reported.,0,United States of America,"Subjects are undergraduates from the University of California, Santa Barbara, located in the United States.",1,N/R,Paper only specifies that subjects were undergraduates; no age range or mean age is provided.,0.2,"65 females, 28 males",Study 1: “Ninety-three subjects (65 females) participated.” Remaining 28 are inferred male as no other genders are mentioned.,0.95,Undergraduate students,Subjects are described as undergraduates from UCSB.,1,On site,"Subjects were brought into a computer lab, implying in-person lab sessions rather than online participation.",1,"Two-round binary trust game with punishment; round 1 subject moves first (trust vs distrust), partner then cooperates or defects; if defection, subject can pay $0.30 to reduce partner’s payoff by $1.20. Round 2 roles reverse; sham partner strategy: in round 1, if trusted, cooperate 50% of time; if distrusted, follow rational self-interest; in round 2, trust 100% of time and punish subject’s defection 33% of time.",Summarizes explicit structural description and programmed partner strategy for Study 1.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0045662.md,Study 2 – Two-round trust game with 1st- and 3rd-party PD reputation (four partner profiles pooled),Study 2 uses a single trust-game payoff structure; partners vary only in PD-based 1st- and 3rd-party reputation (C/D toward self and other). Structural game parameters are constant across these reputation profiles.,0.7,"Partner PD-based reputation combination: (1) defect on subject, cooperate with 3rd party; (2) cooperate on subject, defect on 3rd party; (3) cooperate on both; (4) defect on both.",Study 2 explicitly constructs four partner types based on 1st-party and 3rd-party PD decisions.,1,True,Human undergraduates played incentivized PDs and trust games against sham partners.,1,True,Partner reputation and sham partner strategies are experimentally controlled; subject decisions are observed under randomized partner types.,1,True,"As in Study 1, participants were brought into a computer lab.",1,False,No standalone simulations; sham partners only serve to control human interactions.,1,False,No purely analytical treatment is used as an experimental condition.,1,2,Each interaction in the trust game is dyadic (subject and one partner).,1,2,Again described as a two-round trust game with roles reversed in round 2.,1,1,"Subjects choose discrete actions (trust vs keep, defect vs cooperate, punish vs not); there is no continuous contribution variable, so standard all-or-nothing contribution distinction does not strictly apply.",0.3,N/R,"Design is a trust/investment game, not an endowment-splitting contribution game with a default public/private allocation.",0,N/R,"Although investments are multiplied (100 ep to 200 ep), this is not framed as a linear public goods game with specified MPCR per player.",0.3,0,No channel for communication is mentioned; all information is via experimenter-provided screens.,0.9,0,Subjects see partner’s earlier PD actions but not aggregate summaries of others’ payoffs or punishments.,0.8,0,Punishment is within the dyad; there is no evidence that identities are shared beyond each pair or tied to real-world identities.,0.8,0,No costly reward option exists; only punishment and standard cooperative outcomes.,1,1,Subjects are informed about playing a two-round trust game with role reversal; implies knowledge of the total number of rounds.,0.9,1,"If the partner (round 1) or subject (round 2) defects, the other player can pay to punish.",1,15,"“If the return was selfish, the subject could spend 15 ep to punish the partner by 165 ep.”",1,165,Punishment reduces partner’s payoff by 165 experimental points per 15 ep spent.,1,0,No explicit costly reward option is described.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,100,In round 1 the subject is explicitly given 100 experimental points to either keep (split evenly) or invest.,1,N/R,"The main DVs are decisions (trust, punish, cooperate) and their odds ratios, not normalized contribution fractions of endowment.",0,N/R,Trust is a binary decision to pass the entire 100 ep; no variability in amount is measured as a DV.,0.1,N/R,No reported measure of actual over maximum possible total payoff.,0,N/R,Results focus on behavioral choices and odds ratios; no summary of average dyadic payoff is given.,0,United States of America,"Undergraduates from the University of California, Santa Barbara.",1,N/R,"As in Study 1, only described as undergraduates; no ages provided.",0.2,"71 females, 48 males",Study 2: “119 subjects (71 females) participated.” Remaining 48 are inferred male.,0.95,Undergraduate students,Subjects are undergraduates at UCSB.,1,On site,Described together with Study 1 as participants being brought into a computer lab.,1,"Round 1: subject has 100 ep and can either keep half (50 ep to self, 50 ep presumably not invested) or invest all 100 ep with partner; if invested, amount is doubled to 200 ep for partner. Partner (sham) can cooperate (return 100 ep, keep 100 ep) or defect (return 25 ep, keep 175 ep). If partner defects, subject may spend 15 ep to reduce partner’s payoff by 165 ep, leaving 10 ep each. Round 2: roles reverse; partner always trusts (according to script), subject can cooperate or defect, and partner punishes subject’s defection 40% of the time. PD block before TG establishes partner’s 1st- and 3rd-party reputations based on C/D choices in standard PDs.","Summarizes the explicitly described payoff nodes, punishment mapping, sham partner strategies, and reputation-generation phase in Study 2.",1
10.1098_rspb.2012.0615.md,Study 1 – Hypothetical 3PPG (single condition),Study 1 uses only one hypothetical third-party punishment game (3PPG) condition with no between-subject treatment manipulation.,1,"Individual differences: strength of belief in a powerful, intervening God; general religiosity; political orientation","Only measured variables (God beliefs, religiosity, political orientation) varied; there were no experimental treatments.",1,True,Undergraduate human participants completed questionnaires and a hypothetical game.,1,False,No manipulated independent variable; purely correlational with measured beliefs.,1,False,"Participants completed the study online, not in a physical lab session.",0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; responses from humans only.,1,False,No formal mathematical modeling or proofs reported for this study.,1,3,"3PPG described with roles A, B, and C.",1,1,Participants made punishment decisions for a single imagined interaction; no repetition mentioned.,0.9,1,"Player C could spend “some or all” of $10 in $1 increments, implying any amount between 0 and full endowment.",1,0,"This is a dictator/third-party punishment scenario, not a standard public goods contribution; endowment starts as private money for A and C.",0.8,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication between players; all decisions are made individually and anonymously.,1,0,No mention that player C sees earnings or histories beyond the one-shot allocation options.,0.9,0,All players are described as anonymous; identities are not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the game.,1,0,Single hypothetical interaction; no display of number of rounds.,1,1,Player C can spend money to reduce Player A’s payoff; this is costly punishment.,1,1,"For every $1 C spends, A loses $3; cost to punisher is 1 monetary unit per punishment unit.",1,3,Stated that each $1 spent by C reduces A’s payoff by $3.,1,0,No option for C to increase A’s payoff; only reductions are described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,10,Player C receives $10 to potentially spend on punishment.,1,N/R,"Outcome is amount spent on punishment, not a contribution to a public good; no normalized contribution rate reported.",1,N/R,They analyze total punishment of selfish offers; no raw means are provided.,0.3,N/R,No payoff-efficiency ratios or welfare metrics reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics reported.,1,N/R,Participants described only as undergraduates; institution location unspecified for this study’s data collection site.,0.2,N/R,Demographics in supplementary material; ages not specified in main text.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition only appears in regression control notes; exact distribution not reported.,0.3,Undergraduates,Explicitly described as undergraduates.,1,Online,"Study 1: “Participating online, students first completed…”",1,"Hypothetical third-party punishment game; Player A receives $20 and allocates to B in $2 increments; Player C receives $10 and can spend in $1 increments to reduce A’s payoff by $3 per $1 spent. Participants state punishment for each possible allocation; main index is total punishment of selfish offers (< $10 to B), controlling for punishment of non-selfish offers.",Summarized from procedural description of the 3PPG.,1
10.1098_rspb.2012.0615.md,"Study 2 – 3PPG, God-belief salient (demographics first)",One of two between-subject conditions in Study 2: demographics (including God beliefs and religiosity) completed before the 3PPG.,1,Order (salience) manipulation: God beliefs and religiosity measured before the 3PPG (salient); individual differences in God beliefs and religiosity.,Condition coded as salience vs non-salience based on order; within each condition God beliefs and religiosity vary.,1,True,Undergraduate human participants played an incentivized 3PPG.,1,True,Random assignment to salience vs non-salience conditions constitutes an experiment.,1,True,Participants attended in groups to a laboratory setting and completed materials in separate rooms.,1,False,No computer-simulated agents; all decisions by human participants.,1,False,No closed-form modeling or proofs for this condition.,1,3,"3PPG with roles A, B, C described as in Study 1.",1,1,Participants make a single set of punishment commitments for one hypothetical A-choice realization affecting one payoff draw.,0.9,1,Instruction: player C can spend “some or all” of their $10 in $1 increments for each option; continuous within the $0–$10 range.,1,0,Not a voluntary contribution mechanism; all monetary amounts begin as private endowments for A and C.,0.8,N/R,Not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Participants did not speak with each other or know each other; no communication channel mentioned.,1,0,No feedback about others’ cumulative earnings or punishments is reported.,0.9,0,Anonymity of players is specified; no identities linked to punishment.,1,0,There is no reward option in the game.,1,0,Single interaction; no multi-round interface mentioned.,1,1,Player C can pay to reduce A’s payoff; the core feature of the 3PPG.,1,1,Punisher gives up $1 of own $10 for each punishment unit.,1,3,Each $1 spent by C reduces A’s payout by $3.,1,0,No mechanism to increase A’s earnings.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,10,Player C is explicitly given $10 in change for possible punishment decisions.,1,N/R,"Measured outcome is altruistic punishment spending, not public good contributions; no normalized contribution rate provided.",1,N/R,They report only regression coefficients on altruistic punishment index; no mean levels of punishment.,0.3,N/R,No welfare or efficiency calculations reported.,1,N/R,Group-level payoffs not summarized in the text.,1,N/R,Described as undergraduates; the specific university or national context is not identified for this study.,0.2,N/R,Age not provided in the main text; said to be in supplementary materials.,0.2,N/R,Gender only appears as a regression control; proportions are not reported.,0.3,Undergraduates,Participants are described as undergraduates.,1,On site,Participants came to the lab and were seated in separate laboratory rooms.,1,"Incentivized 3PPG as in Study 1: Player A has $20 to share with B; Player C (participant) has $10 and pre-commits, in a grid, how much to spend (in $1 increments) on reducing A’s payoff ($3 reduction per $1 spent) for each potential allocation. One allocation option is randomly selected to determine actual payouts. Salience condition: demographics form (including God beliefs and religiosity) completed before this task.","Compiled from Study 2 procedure, with condition-specific ordering.",1
10.1098_rspb.2012.0615.md,"Study 2 – 3PPG, God-belief non-salient (demographics last)","The second between-subject condition in Study 2: participants completed demographics after the 3PPG, making God beliefs and religiosity non-salient at decision time.",1,Order (salience) manipulation: God beliefs and religiosity measured after the 3PPG (non-salient); individual differences in God beliefs and religiosity.,"This row represents the non-salient order condition; within this, God beliefs and religiosity still vary as measured predictors.",1,True,Uses the same human participant 3PPG as the salient condition.,1,True,Part of the same randomized experiment; participants were assigned to this order condition.,1,True,Conducted in the same laboratory setting as the salient condition.,1,False,No computer-only simulation.,1,False,No analytical model for this condition.,1,3,"Same 3-player roles A, B, C as in the salient condition.",1,1,Single interaction with one randomly chosen outcome.,0.9,1,Player C chooses any amount from $0–$10 in $1 increments for each option.,1,0,"Endowments are private, not pre-allocated to a public fund.",0.8,N/R,Not a public goods multiplier setting.,1,0,Same no-communication constraint as salient condition.,1,0,No cumulative feedback reported.,0.9,0,Players are anonymous; no identity-linked punishment.,1,0,No reward option is implemented.,1,0,"Single one-shot scenario, no round count shown.",1,1,Player C can spend to reduce Player A’s payoff.,1,1,C loses $1 for each $1 spent.,1,3,Each $1 spent reduces A’s payoff by $3.,1,0,No reward mechanic provided.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,10,"C is given $10 to potentially spend on punishment, as in salient condition.",1,N/R,No contribution outcome; DV is altruistic punishment index.,1,N/R,No descriptive statistics for average punishment are reported separately by condition.,0.3,N/R,No reported efficiency metric.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoff results are provided.,1,N/R,Same undergraduate pool as salient condition; national context not specified.,0.2,N/R,Age statistics not reported.,0.2,N/R,Only mentioned as a regression covariate; no breakdown.,0.3,Undergraduates,Participants are specified as undergraduates.,1,On site,Part of the same lab-based Study 2 procedure.,1,"Identical incentivized 3PPG to the salient condition: Player C, always the punisher (via rigged draw of role C), pre-commits punishment amounts for each possible allocation by A. One allocation realization is drawn at random to determine actual monetary outcomes. In the non-salient condition, the demographics form with God beliefs and religiosity is completed after the 3PPG.","Summarized from Study 2, emphasizing the late placement of the belief measures.",1
10.1098_rspb.2012.0615.md,"Study 3 – Punishment scenario, beliefs salient (demographics first)","In Study 3, one condition had participants complete the demographics (God beliefs and religiosity) before the punishment scenario, making beliefs salient.",1,Order (salience) manipulation: God beliefs and religiosity measured before punishment scenario (salient); individual differences in God beliefs and religiosity.,"The only experimental manipulation is order of the demographics vs scenario; within this condition, belief measures vary.",1,True,Study uses American residents responding to an online vignette task.,1,True,Randomized assignment to salience vs non-salience in a between-subject design.,1,False,"Participants are ‘American residents’ participating online, not recruited to a physical lab.",1,False,"Human subjects, no agent-based simulation.",1,False,No analytic modeling reported for this condition.,1,2,Scenario centers on a single wrongdoer (John) and the respondent as taxpayer; there is no explicit multi-player game structure. Set to 2 as the minimal interacting parties.,0.4,1,Single vignette judgment; no repetition.,1,1,Not a contribution game; participants choose a number on a 7-point scale; the all-or-nothing setting does not apply but 1 is used for ‘can choose any amount’.,0.3,0,"No explicit endowment allocation to a public good; responses are attitudes about tax spending, not direct contributions.",0.3,N/R,Not a public goods payoff structure.,1,0,Participants answer individually online; no communication described.,1,0,No feedback about others’ attitudes or payoffs.,1,0,"The wrongdoer is a named hypothetical character (“John”), but there are no identities of multiple players or punishment targets in a game-theoretic sense; set to 0 as no dynamic identity revelation is manipulated.",0.4,0,No reward decision exists.,1,0,Single vignette; no rounds or remaining rounds displayed.,1,1,DV is endorsement of state-sponsored catching and punishing of John; punishment is the central concept.,1,N/R,"Participants indicate support for tax dollars to be spent, but no numeric cost per unit of punishment is specified.",1,N/R,No explicit mapping from spending level to punishment intensity is provided.,1,0,No rewarding of cooperation or good behavior is mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward institution in this vignette.,1,N/A,No reward institution in this vignette.,1,N/R,No individual monetary endowment is given to participants; they only state preferences about tax spending.,1,N/R,"DV is endorsement of state-sponsored punishment on a 7-point scale, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No monetary contribution amount decided by participants.,1,N/R,"No payoff structure, so efficiency cannot be computed.",1,N/R,No group earnings or welfare outcome measured.,1,United States,"Participants described as “American residents,” implying U.S. residency.",0.9,N/R,Age statistics are not specified in the main text.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition only appears in regression controls; no distribution given.,0.3,N/R,Participants are ‘American residents’ without further detail on education.,0.3,Online,Study 3 states participants ‘participated online’.,1,"Single-shot vignette: participants read about John, a corporate executive stealing from his company to support a gambling habit. They then rate, on a 7-point scale, how many of their tax dollars they would like allocated to state-sponsored catching and punishing of John. The two items (catching, punishing) are averaged (r=0.68) into an index of endorsement of state-sponsored punishment. In the salient condition, the demographics (including religiosity and 3-item God beliefs) are completed before reading the scenario.",Summarized from Study 3 method description for the salient order condition.,1
10.1098_rspb.2012.0615.md,"Study 3 – Punishment scenario, beliefs non-salient (demographics last)","The second condition in Study 3 where the punishment scenario precedes the demographics, making beliefs non-salient at judgment time.",1,Order (salience) manipulation: God beliefs and religiosity measured after punishment scenario (non-salient); individual differences in God beliefs and religiosity.,"Within this condition, participants are randomly assigned to complete the scenario first; beliefs vary as measured predictors.",1,True,Same human online vignette setup as the salient condition.,1,True,Part of the same randomized order manipulation experiment.,1,False,Online participation by American residents.,1,False,No simulated agents.,1,False,No purely analytical treatment.,1,2,"As in the salient condition, the structure is a subject making judgments about ‘John’; minimally treated as 2 interacting parties.",0.4,1,One vignette; no repetition.,1,1,"Attitudinal 7-point scale, not a discrete all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.3,0,No endowment split between public/private is defined.,0.3,N/R,No public goods multiplier specified.,1,0,No interaction between participants.,1,0,No information about other participants’ responses or payoffs.,1,0,Hypothetical named wrongdoer but no dynamic revelation of identities or targets in a game setting.,0.4,0,No rewards in the scenario.,1,0,Single-shot scenario.,1,1,Participants indicate support for catching and punishing John via state institutions.,1,N/R,No specific cost per unit of punishment is defined; only overall tax support.,1,N/R,No functional mapping from spending to punishment intensity.,1,0,No reward options described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Participants only express preferences over tax allocation; no individual endowment is manipulated.,1,N/R,"Attitude scale DV, not contributions.",1,N/A,No economic contribution or transfer amount is chosen by subjects.,1,N/R,No explicit payoffs.,1,N/R,No measured group earnings.,1,United States,Same ‘American residents’ sample as the salient condition.,0.9,N/R,Not provided.,0.2,N/R,Only appears as a control; no detailed breakdown.,0.3,N/R,No explicit education information beyond ‘American residents’.,0.3,Online,Same online procedure as salient condition.,1,"Identical vignette about John, the corporate executive stealing from his company, as in the salient condition. Participants rate, on separate 7-point scales, their desired tax spending for catching and for punishing John, which are averaged into an endorsement-of-state-punishment index. In the non-salient condition, participants complete this scenario before the demographics and God-belief measures.",Summarized from Study 3 with emphasis on non-salient ordering.,1
10.1098_rspb.2012.0615.md,"Study 4b – Hypothetical 3PPG, mechanisms salient (questionnaire first)","In Study 4b, one experimental condition had participants complete the nine-item mechanism questionnaire before the hypothetical 3PPG, making these perceptions salient.",1,"Order (salience) manipulation: mechanism items (attributions to God, perceived appropriateness of punishment, free will beliefs) completed before 3PPG (salient); individual differences in these mechanism variables.",The manipulated variable is order of the mechanism questionnaire vs game; the mechanism scales vary between individuals.,1,True,American residents participated online in a hypothetical 3PPG scenario.,1,True,Random assignment to salience vs non-salience of mechanism variables.,1,False,"Described as online participation, not an on-site lab.",1,False,Human subjects only.,1,False,No analytical modeling.,1,3,"Participants imagine playing the 3PPG as player C, as in Study 1.",1,1,Single hypothetical interaction; no repeated rounds.,0.9,1,Same hypothetical 3PPG structure as Study 1 with spending decisions over 0–$10 in increments; any amount is allowed.,0.9,0,Dictator-like allocation plus punishment; all funds start as private endowments.,0.8,N/R,No public good multiplier specified.,1,0,Purely hypothetical responses; no communication.,1,0,No feedback about other players or rounds.,1,0,All roles are hypothetical and anonymous.,1,0,No reward mechanism included.,1,0,"Single hypothetical interaction, no round count.",1,1,"As in the 3PPG, player C can punish A at a personal cost.",1,1,Assumed same 3:1 punishment structure as earlier 3PPG descriptions (1 unit cost to C per punishment unit).,0.8,3,Based on the standard 3PPG described earlier: $1 spent reduces A’s payoff by $3; not restated but implied to be the same paradigm.,0.8,0,No rewarding option described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment to C in this hypothetical version is not restated; assumed similar to $10 in earlier studies but not explicitly reported here.,0.4,N/R,"Outcome is altruistic punishment, not public good contributions.",1,N/R,They report regression results on altruistic punishment index; no mean punishment amount provided.,0.3,N/R,No payoff efficiency measures reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not reported.,1,United States,Participants are ‘American residents’.,0.9,N/R,Age information is not specified in the main text.,0.2,N/R,"Gender mentioned as a covariate, but no breakdown reported.",0.3,N/R,No information on education level provided beyond residency.,0.3,Online,Study 4b participants were American residents participating online.,1,"Participants first completed a nine-item mechanism questionnaire assessing: (1) attributions to God vs humans for responsibility to punish, (2) perceived appropriateness of punishment, and (3) endorsement of free will (each on 7-point scales). They then played the hypothetical 3PPG in the role of player C, as in Study 1, and an altruistic punishment index was computed. In the salient condition, mechanism perceptions were made salient by administering this questionnaire before the 3PPG.",Summarized from Study 4b procedure for the salient condition.,1
10.1098_rspb.2012.0615.md,"Study 4b – Hypothetical 3PPG, mechanisms non-salient (questionnaire last)","The second condition in Study 4b where the hypothetical 3PPG comes before the mechanism questionnaire, so the mechanism variables are non-salient at decision time.",1,Order (salience) manipulation: mechanism items completed after 3PPG (non-salient); individual differences in mechanism variables.,Represents participants assigned to complete the game before the mechanism questionnaire; mechanism scores are still measured individual differences.,1,True,Same online study with American residents.,1,True,Part of same random-order experimental design as salient condition.,1,False,Online implementation.,1,False,No simulated agents; real participants only.,1,False,No analytical model described.,1,3,Same hypothetical 3PPG structure as in the salient condition.,1,1,Single hypothetical interaction.,0.9,1,C chooses hypothetical punishment amounts across 0–endowment range; not forced all-or-nothing.,0.9,0,No pre-allocated public fund; private endowments only.,0.8,N/R,Not a public goods situation.,1,0,Participants respond individually online.,1,0,No performance summaries reported.,1,0,Anonymity of hypothetical players; no identities revealed.,1,0,No reward options.,1,0,Single hypothetical game; no rounds indicated.,1,1,3PPG includes costly punishment by player C.,1,1,Assumed same punishment function as in other 3PPG implementations (1 unit cost per punishment unit).,0.8,3,Assumed 3:1 reduction ratio from earlier definition of the 3PPG in the paper.,0.8,0,No reward described.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/R,Exact endowment to C in this hypothetical version is not stated here.,0.4,N/R,DV is altruistic punishment index.,1,N/R,No descriptive mean punishment levels are reported.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency measure computed.,1,N/R,No payoffs summarized.,1,United States,Same American-resident sample as salient condition.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,N/R,Only appears as regression control; distribution not provided.,0.3,N/R,No education-level information reported.,0.3,Online,Same online environment as salient condition.,1,"Participants first completed the hypothetical 3PPG as player C (structured as in Study 1) and then filled out the nine-item mechanism questionnaire (attributions to God for punishment responsibility, perceived appropriateness of punishment, and free will beliefs). In the non-salient condition, these mechanism beliefs were measured only after the punishment choices.",Summarized from Study 4b with order reversed relative to salient condition.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.91.042810.md,Peer exclusion vs defectors under pairwise comparison updating,Paper analyzes a public goods game with peer excluders (E) and defectors (Y) in a finite population using pairwise comparison (imitation) dynamics; this is a distinct simulation condition.,0.96,Exclusion regime: peer exclusion (E); Opponent: defectors (Y); Update rule: pairwise comparison; Selection intensity s varied; Exclusion probability β_E varied (0<β_E≤1); Exclusion cost c_E varied; Group size N varied; Enhancement factor r varied; Population size M fixed (typically 100).,"Model section defines E vs Y with payoffs (Eqs. 2–3) under pairwise comparison updating; results section varies s, β_E, c_E, N, r, with M=100 in figures.",0.9,False,Study is a theoretical evolutionary game model and stochastic simulations with no human or animal subjects.,1,False,No controlled experiment with participants; only mathematical analysis and simulations.,1,False,No laboratory or field data; purely theoretical.,1,True,"Uses numerical analysis of Markov chains, fixation probabilities, and individual-based simulations (e.g., Fig. 5) with virtual agents.",1,True,"Derives analytical conditions and inequalities for fixation and thresholds (e.g., Eqs. 15–16, 19).",1,5,"Group size of PGG denoted by N; specific numerical illustrations use N=5 (e.g., Figs. 3–5, 6,7 state N=5).",0.9,1,"Each PGG interaction is a single-stage game; evolutionary dynamics proceed via repeated population updates, not repeated rounds within a group interaction.",0.8,1,"Cooperators (and excluders) contribute a fixed amount c=1; defectors contribute 0; there is no choice of partial contribution beyond this fixed rule, but players do not have an action choice each round in the human sense. Treat as continuous/any not all-or-nothing contribution choice is absent; however in this schema 1 signifies 'can contribute any amount', which doesn't fit; still, model has only two discrete actions 0 or 1. Using 0 for all-or-nothing.",0.