on union' (cooperators+excluders), not explicitly reported as normalized contribution rate.",0.4,N/R,"Figure reports fractions of cooperative individuals, not point contributions.",0.4,N/R,Efficiency not computed explicitly in Fig. 7.,0.3,N/R,Only fractions of cooperative union are shown.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"CPGG setting with mandatory participation for X, Y, excluders (E or F). Parameters fixed: s=0.01 (weak imitation), r=3, M=100, high exclusion costs c_E=c_F=0.7 and exclusion probabilities β_E=β_F=0.8. Group size N is varied; Fig. 7 reports stationary fraction of 'cooperation union' (cooperators+excluders) for each N under peer vs pool exclusion. Result: pool exclusion maintains significantly higher cooperative union fractions as N increases, highlighting advantage of cost-sharing institutions under unfavorable conditions.",Summarizes design and DV for this comparison.,0.96
10.1038_s41467-020-17286-2.md,Daily assessment phase – naturalistic norm violations,"Single longitudinal daily diary design; all analyses draw on the same set of reported norm‐violation events (k≈1468 daily, k=1507 including follow-up). No discrete experimental conditions or manipulations are described, so the whole daily assessment phase is treated as one observational condition.",0.93,"Relational, situational, and emotional predictors of punishment (e.g., valuation of offender, self vs. other victim, moral wrongness, relative power, anger, disgust) all measured, not experimentally manipulated.","Paper explicitly states it is a longitudinal observational study using daily assessments; predictors such as WTR, self-relevance, moral wrongness, power, emotions are measured via questionnaires rather than randomly assigned.",0.97,True,Human participants (N=257 Dutch adults) reported real-life experiences and responses in daily and follow-up surveys.,1,False,No randomized assignment or controlled manipulation; purely observational self-reports of naturally occurring norm violations.,1,False,"Although intake took place in the lab, the focal punishment data come from daily life outside the lab via mobile surveys (field/observational).",0.96,False,No agent-based or computational simulations are conducted in this paper.,1,False,No formal game-theoretic or closed-form analytical model is developed; the work is empirical.,1,2,Each focal event involves at minimum a participant (punisher/observer) and an offender. Third-party victims or bystanders may be present but the core interaction is dyadic (participant–offender).,0.5,1,Each reported violation is a single episode in daily life; there is no repeated strategic game structure per event.,0.8,1,"There is no formal contribution choice; contribution structure from public good games does not apply. Following instructions, treat as free-form (use 1 to indicate not restricted to all-or-nothing).",0.3,N/A,No endowments or contribution