nt.,1,10,A and B are each endowed with 10 points; C is endowed with 40 points but the primary endowment per active investor/responder is 10.,0.9,N/R,"Only mean transfer amounts are reported, not normalized by endowment.",0.6,6.02,Table 1: Mean transfer by A in Treatment 2 is 6.02 points.,1,N/R,No direct or implied efficiency statistics reported for this treatment.,0.4,N/R,Paper does not provide average total payoffs per group per treatment.,0.3,United States,"Sessions conducted at UCSB, a US university.",0.95,N/R,Only described as university students; no age summary.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,0.1,University students,Subjects recruited from university-wide student email list.,0.95,On site,Sessions took place in a large classroom with physical attendance.,1,"Three-player extension of investment game. A and B each start with 10 points; A sends any amount, tripled for B. B returns any amount (unmultiplied). Elected third-party C starts with 40 points, observes A and B’s choices, and can spend points to reduce B’s payoff at a 3:1 rate. Three rounds without feedback, random rematching across rounds; one round randomly selected for payment. Conversion: A and B 2 points per dollar, C 3 points per dollar.",Synthesis of design details for Treatment 2 in Section 3.,1
