10.1038_srep00458.md,Analytical and simulation model – anonymous interactions (λ = 0),"Condition without reputation/information, explicitly described and depicted in Fig. 2a as λ = 0; serves as baseline for comparison.",0.97,Reputation/information availability λ = 0; population size n = 80; benefit of cooperation b = 4; punishment impact β = 3; cost of cooperation c = 1; cost of punishment γ = 1; selection strength s = 0.5; exploration (mutation) rate μ varied (rare exploration analytical limit; simulations with μ = 0.0001 and μ = 0.1).,"Paper varies λ and μ while keeping n, b, β, c, γ, s fixed for main figures. This row corresponds to λ = 0 (no information).",0.95,False,Study is an evolutionary game-theoretic model with simulations; no human or animal participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical and computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person sessions; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,True,"They use individual-based computer simulations of a birth–death process in finite populations (e.g., “individual-based simulation runs” and “simulations were run over a period of 10^10 time steps”).",1,True,"They derive exact conditions and analytical approximations in the limit of rare exploration (e.g., inequality for λ and ‘exact results for the limiting case of rare exploration’).",1,2,"Core model is a pairwise game with donor and recipient; although population size is n = 80, each interaction involves two players.",0.95,1,Pairwise interaction consists of one helping stage and one punishment stage per encounter; no repeated rounds between fixed partners described within a game instance.,0.8,1,"Players choose between cooperate (transfer benefit b at cost c) or defect; no partial contribution levels are modeled, so contribution choice is effectively all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,Model is not a standard public goods game with an endowment allocation; it is a donation game (help or not). Default contribution proportion concept does not apply.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined for this donation game structure in the paper.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between players is not modeled as chat.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback screens; model is defined by payoff functions only.,1,N/A,Identity and display to subjects are not applicable without human participants.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism and no human interface.,1,N/A,No rounds are shown to subjects; model is not implemented as an interface to humans.,1,1,Second stage explicitly allows recipients to punish donors at cost γ while reducing donor payoff by β.,1,1,"Text: ‘recipients decide whether or not to punish the donor at a cost γ, thereby reducing the payoff of the donor by β’; parameters for figures set c = γ = 1.",1,3,Punishment reduces donor payoff by β and β = 3 in the parameter set; interpret as 3 units of harm per 1 unit cost.,1,0,"Model includes only punishment reactions (R, A, S, N); no mention of rewards in the game structure.",1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent in this model.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent in this model.,1,N/R,Donation game described only by benefit b to recipient and cost c to donor; no explicit token endowment is specified.,0.6,N/R,"Paper reports time-averaged frequencies of strategies, not explicit normalized contribution rates.",0.4,N/R,No explicit average number of cooperative acts per round is numerically reported.,0.4,N/R,No total payoff vs. maximum payoff ratios are reported in numeric form.,0.4,N/R,"Group payoff values are not given as numeric outcomes, only relative evolutionary frequencies.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulations only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of mathematical analysis and computer simulations only.,1,"Pairwise donation game with two stages (helping and punishment). Donor may cooperate (pay cost c = 1 to give benefit b = 4) or defect. Recipient may choose among four punishment reactions: R (punish defectors only), A (punish cooperators only), S (punish everyone), N (punish nobody). Strategies combine donor types (C, D, OC, OD) and recipient types (R, N, A, S) yielding 16 composite strategies. Strategy transmission modeled via frequency-dependent birth–death process in finite population of size n = 80 with selection strength s = 0.5 and exploration (mutation) rate μ. In this λ = 0 condition, interactions are completely anonymous, so opportunists cannot condition behavior on reputation.",Summarizes formal game description and evolutionary process as specified in Results and figure captions for the anonymous case.,0.98
10.1038_srep00458.md,Analytical and simulation model – non-anonymous interactions with reputation (λ = 0.3),Condition with positive information on co-players’ types; explicitly referred to as λ = 0.3 in Fig. 2b and described as ‘with reputation’.,0.97,Reputation/information availability λ = 0.3; population size n = 80; benefit of cooperation b = 4; punishment impact β = 3; cost of cooperation c = 1; cost of punishment γ = 1; selection strength s = 0.5; exploration (mutation) rate μ = 0.0001 (for Fig. 2b run) and also μ = 0.0001 and μ = 0.1 in Fig. 1 above critical threshold.,Same parameters as anonymous case except λ increased to 0.3 (above the critical threshold given by Eq. 1); μ set explicitly for simulation runs.,0.95,False,"As above, purely theoretical and simulation-based, no human subjects.",1,False,"Condition is part of the same modeling framework, not a controlled behavioral experiment.",1,False,No lab environment; computational model only.,1,True,Time evolution is shown via ‘individual-based simulation runs’ for μ = 0.0001 and λ = 0.3.,1,True,Analytical approximation covers λ > threshold and provides time-averaged frequencies used alongside simulations.,1,2,Same pairwise donor–recipient interaction structure as in λ = 0 condition.,0.95,1,Each interaction has one helping and one punishment stage; model does not implement repeated games between fixed pairs within a round.,0.8,1,"Donor choice still binary: cooperate or defect, with full benefit b or none.",0.9,N/A,No public good endowment structure; only donation decision.,1,N/R,MPCR is not used or derivable in this donation game context as reported.,1,N/A,"No human communication channel; reputation is abstract parameter λ, not chat.",1,N/A,Model does not implement display of summaries to human players.,1,N/A,Identification and display concepts do not apply without human interface.,1,N/A,No reward and no human display.,1,N/A,"Rounds are simulation time steps, not shown to participants.",1,1,Same punishment structure as λ = 0 case persists: recipients may punish with cost γ and impact β.,1,1,γ = 1 in the parameter specification for all main figures.,1,3,Punishment reduces donor payoff by β = 3 per act at cost γ = 1.,1,0,"Only punishment types R, A, S, N are modeled; no rewards.",1,N/A,Rewards are not available in the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available in the model.,1,N/R,"Endowment not quantified; payoffs given only via benefit b and costs c, γ, β.",0.6,N/R,"Outcomes presented as time-averaged frequencies of strategies C, OC, OD, D and R, A, S, N; no explicit normalized cooperation rate is numerically reported.",0.4,N/R,No numeric data on average number of cooperative actions per encounter are provided.,0.4,N/R,"Although higher cooperation implies higher efficiency, explicit efficiency ratios are not reported.",0.4,N/R,Total group payoffs are not numerically reported; only qualitative statements about cooperation and punishment frequencies.,0.4,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Same theoretical/simulation framework; no human subjects.,1,"Identical pairwise helping–punishment game as in the λ = 0 condition, but donors can anticipate co-player’s punishment behavior with probability λ = 0.3 based on reputation from previous encounters, observation, or gossip. Four donor types (C, D, OC, OD) and four recipient types (R, N, A, S) yield 16 strategy combinations. Opportunistic donors (OC, OD) condition their cooperation on the recipient's punishment reputation: they cooperate against responsible punishers R and defect against others when reputation is known; OC cooperates by default when reputation is unknown, while OD defects by default. Evolutionary dynamics follow the same frequency-dependent birth–death process in a finite population (n = 80) with selection strength s = 0.5 and exploration rate μ. For λ above the critical threshold λ > ((n − 1)γ − β)/((n − 1)(γ + b) + c − β), responsible punishment and cooperation become abundant; λ = 0.3 is above this critical threshold for the chosen parameters.",Summarizes additional role of λ and opportunistic conditioning described in the Results and figure captions for non-anonymous interactions.,0.98
10.1086_518568.md,Analytical population-genetic model without recombination between helping and punishment,Paper defines a model variant where helping and punishment are perfectly linked (r = 0) and derives conditions for invasion of the combined strong-reciprocator trait.,1,"Genetic structure and demographic parameters with no recombination: deme size N, migration rate m, cost of helping C_H, cost of punishment C_P, benefit of helping B, damage from punishment D, and frequency p of strong reciprocators.","These parameters are explicitly varied in the no-recombination model (eq. 11, 14–15, Fig. 1).",1,False,Model is purely theoretical with no human or animal subjects; uses population-genetic equations.,1,False,No controlled experimental manipulation of participants; only mathematical analysis.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; analytical theory only.,1,False,This particular model variant is developed analytically; simulations are not used here.,1,True,Closed-form derivations of allele frequency change and relatedness under the infinite-island model with r = 0.,1,2,Each focal individual interacts with N−1 neighbors; the basic interaction is pairwise helping/punishment between two individuals.,0.6,2,"Model defines two-stage interaction within a life cycle: first helping, then punishment.",1,1,"Traits are binary alleles (help H vs no help, punish P vs no punish) rather than continuous contribution levels; mapped to ‘can choose any amount’ as not applicable in PGG sense but best coded as 1.",0.3,N/A,"No endowment or contribution game; model is about fecundity effects, not redistribution of tokens.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; analytical genetic model.,1,0,"No information display between strategic agents; fitness effects are hard-wired, not observed.",1,N/A,No identities or observability are modeled; punishment is an automatic fitness effect.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism separate from helping; thus no identities.,1,0,Agents do not have information about number of rounds; model is generational.,1,1,Punishment allele P imposes damage D on nonhelpers at a cost C_P to the actor.,1,N/R,Cost C_P is defined symbolically but no single numeric value; multiple illustrative parameter sets are discussed.,1,N/R,Damage parameter D is defined symbolically with multiple values; no single fixed ratio is given.,1,0,No distinct rewarding stage; only helping (benefit B) and punishment (damage D). Helping is not framed as reward choice.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No token endowments; individuals differ only by alleles affecting fecundity.,1,N/A,"No contribution decisions; outcome is allele frequency change, not contributions.",1,N/A,No numeric contribution per se.,1,N/R,Model does not compute efficiency as normalized payoff relative to maximum.,1,N/R,Group fecundity is implicit; no single payoff level is reported as a DV.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical analytical model with no empirical data collection.,1,"Infinite-island model with demes of size N; haploid life cycle: social interaction (help then punish), reproduction with fecundity effects, migration m, syngamy and meiosis, regulation to N; strong reciprocity coded by a single locus with perfect linkage between helping and punishment.",Life cycle and genetic structure described in ‘Life Cycle’ and ‘No Recombination between Helping and Punishment’ sections.,1
10.1086_518568.md,Analytical population-genetic model with recombination under weak selection (first-order effects),Paper introduces a model variant where helping (H) and punishment (P) are coded at separate loci with recombination; first analyzes weak selection (first-order Taylor expansions).,1,Separate helping and punishment loci with recombination: recombination rate r between loci; deme size N; migration rate m; costs C_H and C_P; benefit B; damage D; allele frequencies p_H and p_P; selection assumed weak.,These parameters determine Δp_H and Δp_P in eqs. (16–21) under ‘Weak Selection (First-Order Effects)’.,1,False,No data from participants; purely mathematical derivation.,1,False,Not an experimental or observational empirical study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; analytical theory.,1,False,"This section uses analytical approximations, not numerical simulations.",1,True,Derives allele-frequency dynamics using first-order Taylor expansion and kin-selection terms.,1,2,Interactions are pairwise between a focal and each of its N−1 neighbors in the helping and punishment stages.,0.6,2,Two-stage interaction: helping then punishment within each generation.,1,1,"Behavioral phenotypes are binary: either express helping (H) or not, punish (P) or not, with no continuous level.",0.6,N/A,No contributions/endowments; only fecundity effects of alleles.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier specified.,1,N/A,No communication modeled; no human subjects.,1,0,No explicit information feedback mechanism; strategies are genetically encoded.,1,N/A,"Punishment is a fitness effect, not a choice conditioned on observed identities in a game.",1,N/A,No reward stage separate from helping.,1,0,Agents are not modeled as knowing number of interaction rounds.,1,1,"Punishment allele P imposes damage D on nonhelpers at cost C_P (eq. 5, 19–21).",1,N/R,C_P defined symbolically; different illustrative values used; no single numeric cost.,1,N/R,Damage parameter D symbolically defined; not fixed numerically relative to cost.,1,0,No explicit positive sanction separate from helping.,0.9,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,"Fitness-based population-genetic model, not a resource-allocation game.",1,N/A,"Dependent variables are allele-frequency changes and conditions for invasion, not contributions.",1,N/A,No per-round contribution quantity is defined.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency metric is computed.,1,N/R,Group fecundity is not presented as an explicit DV; focus is on selection gradients.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,The section is entirely theoretical and analytical.,1,"Infinite-island haploid model with two loci (helping H, punishment P), recombination rate r > 0, migration m, deme size N; weak selection approximation via first-order Taylor expansion; kin selection and competition captured through relatedness terms R and R^R; helping benefits B neighbors and costs C_H to actor; punishment costs C_P and inflicts damage D on nonhelpers.",Summarizes the formal setup described in ‘Recombination between Helping and Punishment – Weak Selection (First-Order Effects)’.,1
10.1086_518568.md,Analytical population-genetic model with recombination under stronger selection (second-order effects),"Paper extends the recombination model to include second-order (stronger) phenotypic effects on fitness, tracking higher-order genetic associations.",1,"Same demographic and genetic parameters as weak-selection recombination model (N, m, r, C_H, C_P, B, D, p_H, p_P), but with selection strong enough that second-order terms and linkage disequilibria matter; includes regimes where p_H and/or p_P are rare or non-rare.",Section ‘Stronger Selection (Second-Order Effects)’ explicitly varies initial frequencies and includes higher-order terms.,1,False,No observational or experimental data; only theory and some later simulations for checking.,1,False,"Not an experiment with treatments; rather, comparative analysis of parameter regimes.",1,False,No physical lab; mathematical treatment.,1,False,This specific subsection is analytical; simulations are described separately for validation.,1,True,"Uses second-order Taylor expansion and recursive equations for genetic associations to derive selection conditions (e.g., eq. A25).",1,2,"As in other variants, basic social interaction is between a focal and each of its N−1 neighbors.",0.6,2,"Still two-stage interaction (helping, then punishment) per life cycle.",1,1,Helping and punishment remain binary traits (alleles present/absent) rather than continuous.,0.6,N/A,No endowment or contribution decision modeled.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No strategic communication; genetic model.,1,0,No information or feedback about others’ outcomes in the decision process.,1,N/A,Punishment is not conditioned on observed identities; it's genetically expressed.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,0,Repeated interactions not modeled as known finite games; it is a generational model.,1,1,Punishment allele P with damage D and cost C_P still central in the model.,1,N/R,Multiple symbolic and numeric examples for C_P; no single canonical value.,1,N/R,Damage D treated symbolically and varied; no fixed punishment technology ratio reported.,1,0,No separate reward lever; only helping and punishment.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Not a token-based game.,1,N/A,"Outcome variables are conditions for allele invasion, equilibria, and whether punishment/helping are altruistic or selfish.",1,N/A,No explicit contribution amounts.,1,N/R,No explicit analysis of overall efficiency as ratio to maximum possible group payoff.,1,N/R,"Group-level payoffs are not presented as a numeric DV; focus on selection gradients and actor fitness effects (−c_H, −c_P).",1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,The second-order model is theoretical and uses symbolic derivations (with supporting Mathematica notebook).,1,"Same infinite-island, two-locus haploid framework as weak-selection model, but selection allowed to generate nonzero linkage disequilibria and higher-order relatedness terms; analyzes cases where both H and P are rare, or one is rare and the other at non-negligible frequency; derives conditions for invasion, thresholds p_H,T and p_P,T, and whether punishment/helping are altruistic vs selfish; accounts for interaction between selection and identity by descent.","Summarizes the ‘Stronger Selection (Second-Order Effects)’ section, including discussion around eq. (A25) and Fig. 2.",1
10.1086_518568.md,Stochastic simulation model validating second-order analytical predictions,"In ‘Results – Stronger Selection’ and Figure 3, the authors describe simulations of finite demes with mutation to compare to analytical equilibria.",1,"Simulation parameters: deme size N = 10; number of demes = 100; recombination rate r = 0.5; migration rate m varied; costs C_H = 0.005, C_P = 0.005; benefit B = 0.2; damage D = 0.2; symmetric mutation rate μ = 0.01; initial allele frequencies p_H = 0.5, p_P = 0.5.",Explicitly given around Figure 3 description.,1,False,Computer simulations of allele dynamics; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,No controlled treatment vs control groups with measured human outcomes; computational experiment only.,1,False,Not a physical lab experiment; numerical simulation.,1,True,"They simulate evolution over 100,000 generations across 100 demes with mutation and selection to obtain equilibrium frequencies.",1,False,This specific component is used to check analytical predictions rather than derive them.,1,2,Social interactions in the simulated life cycle mirror the analytical model: focal interacts pairwise with each neighbor.,0.6,2,Simulation follows life cycle with helping then punishment before reproduction.,0.9,1,"Individuals either carry H or not, P or not; binary phenotypes.",1,N/A,No token contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier defined in the simulation; payoffs are fecundities per model equations.,1,N/A,No agents with communication; simulation of genetic dynamics.,1,0,Agents do not receive payoff or action summaries; behavior is genetically specified.,1,N/A,No explicit identities or observability in the simulation; punishment is automatic.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,0,Not a repeated game from an agent’s cognitive perspective; generational process.,1,1,Simulation uses same punishment structure with cost C_P = 0.005 and damage D = 0.2.,1,0.005,Explicitly stated simulation parameter C_P = 0.005.,1,0.2,Damage parameter D = 0.2 given for simulations; interpreted as harm per expression of punishment.,0.9,0,No explicit rewarding beyond helping.,0.9,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,"Simulation operates on fecundities, not token endowments.",1,N/A,"Primary outcomes are equilibrium allele frequencies, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution per se.,1,N/R,Simulation results plotted as equilibrium p_H and p_P versus migration; efficiency not reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs or total fecundity not summarized as DVs.,1,N/A,"Simulation uses artificial populations, not human subjects.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Numerical simulations performed in silico.,1,"Population of 100 demes with deme size N = 10; life cycle follows the analytical model: fecundity determined by helping and punishment, reproduction, migration (rate m), recombination (r = 0.5), then multinomial sampling for regulation; symmetric mutation rate μ = 0.01 between allele states at each locus; simulations run for 100,000 generations and averages of p_H and p_P reported; multiple values of m explored to compare to second-order analytical equilibria (Fig. 3).",Details provided in caption and surrounding text of Figure 3.,1
