10.1257_aer.20120700.md,Third-Party Punishment Game (leaders observing 2-player public goods game),"Single experimental setup where each leader, as third party, observes contributions of two group members in a 2-player linear public goods game and can assign punishment points using the strategy method.",1,"Leaders’ punishment strategies over contribution profiles of two players (0,2,4,6 Birr each); classified ex post as non-punishing (L_NP), equality-driven (L_EQ), equality+efficiency-driven (L_EQEF), or antisocial (L_AS).","Paper varies contribution profiles of two players (C1,C2) in the strategy method; leader type is a measured characteristic, not experimentally assigned, but is the key independent variable used later.",0.83,True,Game played by real forest group leaders (and members) in Ethiopia.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized behavioral game with specified payoffs and anonymous, one-shot interaction.",1,False,"Described as a natural field setting with lab-in-the-field style experiments conducted in communities, not in a physical laboratory.",0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical/mathematical model tested without data; experiments plus regressions only.,1,2,"Stage 1 public goods game: ‘randomly assigned to groups of two.’ Leader is a third-party observer, not a contributing player.",1,1,Game explicitly described as ‘one-shot’ public goods game with third-party punishment; strategy method over hypothetical profiles but payoffs realized once.,1,1,"Members choose any of four discrete contribution levels: 0,2,4,6 Birr from an endowment of 6; not restricted to only 0 or full contribution.",1,0,"Endowment of six 1-Birr bills given to each member; they decide how many to ‘put in his pocket’ vs. the project, implying all starts private.",1,0.75,Text: ‘Any amount in the project was multiplied by 1.5 and then distributed equally between the two players’ so MPCR = 1.5/2 = 0.75.,1,0,"No mention of communication; design stresses anonymous, one-shot decisions with no indication of pre-play talk.",0.85,0,Members only know multiplier and that leader may reduce income; no indication they see others’ earnings or a punishment/earning summary screen.,0.8,0,Design aims to prevent reputational concerns; members know leader might punish ‘based on their contribution decision’ but it is anonymous: leader cannot link identities to contributions and members do not see actual punishment decisions.,0.95,0,No reward mechanism in the game; only costly punishment by the leader.,1,1,"Game is announced as one-shot; participants are informed of the one-shot nature, so total number of rounds known.",0.9,1,"Stage 2: leader can assign ‘deduction points’ at a cost, explicitly a punishment option.",1,1,‘Assigning one deduction point cost the leader 1 Birr and reduced the payoff of the punished member by 3 Birr.’,1,3,Each punishment point reduces member’s payoff by 3 Birr.,1,0,No positive transfers or reward points described; only punishment through deduction.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,6,Each member: ‘endowment of six bills of 1 Birr’ in the Stage 1 public goods game.,1,N/R,"Paper reports contribution distributions and medians descriptively (e.g., for conditional cooperators later) but not as an aggregate normalized rate for this TPPG across all subjects.",0.3,N/R,"While individual decision options (0,2,4,6) and some subgroup histograms are shown, no single aggregate mean contribution for this specific TPPG is reported.",0.3,N/R,No reported ratio of realized group payoffs to maximum possible payoffs in the TPPG.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff or average earnings reported for the TPPG.,0.1,Ethiopia,"Field setting: forest user groups in Adaba and Dodola districts, Bale Zone, Oromia State, Ethiopia.",1,Leaders: mean age 41.18 years (SD 7.72); members: mean age 36.68 years (SD 13.81).,Table 2 reports ages for leaders and members participating in the broader study including the experiments.,0.95,Groups’ female share mean 0.20 (SD 0.11); leaders are male tribal leaders per descriptive context.,"Table 1 gives female share in groups; leadership described within traditional male-dominated Oromo system, implying leaders are male though not explicitly quantified.",0.7,Leaders: mean 4.67 years (SD 2.75); members: mean 3.23 years (SD 3.10).,Table 2 reports years of education for leaders and members.,0.95,Field experiment,"Described as ‘natural field setting’ with real-world leaders and group members in their communities, not a lab or online setting.",1,"Stage 1: 2-player linear public goods game, each with 6 Birr, contribution options {0,2,4,6}, MPCR=0.75, one-shot, anonymous. Stage 2: leader (or vice-leader) with 10 Birr endowment can assign integer punishment points to each player for each (C1,C2) profile using the strategy method over 10 ordered profiles with C1≤C2; each point costs 1 Birr and reduces target’s payoff by 3 Birr. Players informed that leader can reduce income but identities remain anonymous; leader knows contributions but not identities; members cannot infer punishment from final payoff because payments from multiple games are pooled.",Synthesized from the detailed methodological description of the TPPG and its implementation.,1
