d punishment, not public-good contributions.",1,N/R,"No single numeric effort or punishment summary reported; only qualitative changes (e.g., underfulfilled contracts dropped).",0.3,N/R,"Text notes '40% increase in net payoffs', but not the normalized efficiency relative to theoretical maximum.",0.6,N/R,"Only relative increase (40%) reported, no absolute group payoff.",0.5,N/R,Source paper summarized without country/institution identifier.,0.2,Primarily college-age adults,Subjects described as college students.,0.6,N/R,No gender breakdown given.,0.1,College students,Explicit statement that participants are college students.,1,On site,Standard in-person lab experiment with students; online implementation not mentioned.,0.86,"Same gift-exchange labor market as baseline: employer offers wage w and desired effort e*; employee chooses actual effort e∈[0.1,1]. Added stage where employer, after observing effort, can at cost 1 increase or decrease employee payoff by 2.5. Interactions remain one-shot and anonymous; majority of employers both punish low effort and reward high effort.",Directly summarized from the extended game description in Section 3.1.,1
