10.1038_srep46634.md,"Simulation 1 – Optional PGG, pool exclusion vs pool punishment, no second-order sanctioning","Condition described in Figure 1(a): optional public goods game with five strategies (C, D, L, pool excluder, pool punisher) and only first-order sanctions.",1,"Sanction type (pool exclusion vs pool punishment), game type (optional PGG), second-order sanctioning = off",This scenario varies the presence of pool exclusion and pool punishment with optional participation and without second-order sanctions.,1,False,Study uses evolutionary game-theoretic modeling and numerical analysis of stationary distributions in finite well-mixed populations; no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human participants; entirely theoretical/simulation-based.,1,False,No lab setting; purely computational/theoretical.,1,True,Uses pairwise comparison rule with mutation-selection in a finite population and computes stationary distributions; this is a numerical simulation.,1,True,"Provides closed-form stationary frequencies in the strong imitation limit (e.g., fractions for [X,Y,Z,F,V]); indicates analytical derivations in SI.",1,5,Group size N=5 is explicitly stated in the caption of Figure 1.,1,1,Model is a repeated population process where groups play a one-shot PGG each time step; contribution decision per group is single-stage (one-shot) public goods interaction.,0.7,1,"Standard linear PGG: each cooperator contributes cost c, defector contributes 0; effectively binary all-or-nothing contribution choice.",1,N/A,No human subjects and no default endowment allocation; only model parameters for contribution decisions.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/N with r=3 and N=5 (from parameters), giving 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,"No human interaction, purely simulated strategies; no communication mechanism.",1,0,"Model does not include information displays; payoffs are computed algorithmically, not shown to agents.",1,N/A,No explicit identities in the simulation; only strategy types matter.,1,N/A