10.1038_s41467-021-21602-9.md,Cross-national vignette survey – all scenarios and responses,The paper describes a single preregistered cross-national survey in which all participants completed the same 10 scenarios and rated 4 responses plus the behavior itself. There are no separate experimental conditions or manipulations; analyses are correlational and comparative across countries and responses.,1,"Perceived appropriateness of norm-violating behaviors and type of response considered (verbal confrontation, physical confrontation, social ostracism, gossip, non-action), plus country-level cultural/societal variables (e.g., individualism, tightness, median income).",The study varies scenarios (types of norm violations) and asks for ratings of different responses; it also examines how country-level predictors relate to metanorms. There is no randomized treatment; independent variables are naturally varying perceptions and country characteristics.,1,True,Human participants rated scenarios and responses in 57 countries.,1,False,The design is a survey with hypothetical scenarios and correlational analyses; there is no random assignment to treatment conditions or behavioral interaction.,1,False,"Data were collected mainly online via Qualtrics, with a few paper-and-pencil sessions; this is not a controlled laboratory game setting.",1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations are reported.,1,False,The paper does not present a formal mathematical model; only statistical analyses of survey data.,1,1,"Each respondent evaluates hypothetical scenarios individually; there is no interactive multi-player game. For the purposes of this schema, this is coded as a single 'player'.",0.9,1,Participants give one set of ratings over a fixed battery of scenarios; there are no repeated strategic rounds as in a repeated game.,1,1,"There is no contribution decision with a constrained action space; responses are scalar appropriateness ratings on a scale. This does not correspond to an all-or-nothing contribution game, so we code as allowing any 'amount'.",0.7,N/A,No endowments or contributions are involved; this is not a public goods or contribution game.,1,N/A,There is no public goods or common-pool contribution mechanism with a defined marginal per capita return; only vignette ratings.,1,0,Participants do not communicate with others; they complete an individual survey.,1,0,"Respondents are not shown others’ payoffs, sanctions, or behaviors; only scenario descriptions and their own items.",1,0,"There is no interactive punishment mechanism; scenarios sometimes describe perpetrators and responders, but this is not an experimental identification feature shown to participants in a game.",1,0,No reward mechanism or identification is present.,1,0,The survey does not involve rounds; participants are not informed of any repeated interaction length.,1,0,"Although scenarios involve sanctions (e.g., confrontation, ostracism, gossip), participants do not themselves choose punishments in an incentive-compatible mechanism; thus there is no implemented punishment technology as in an economic game.",0.9,N/A,No costs in tokens/points are implemented; only hypothetical judgments.,1,N/A,No mapping from spent punishment points to target loss is defined.,1,0,"The study concerns sanctions like confrontation, gossip, and ostracism; there is no positive reward option.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism with costs is implemented.,1,N/A,No formal reward technology exists in the design.,1,N/A,Participants do not receive experimental endowments of money or tokens.,1,N/A,There are no contribution decisions; primary outcomes are appropriateness ratings and derived metanorm indices.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts are elicited.,1,N/A,No payoff structure or efficiency measure as in cooperation games is defined.,1,N/A,"The survey elicits attitudes, not monetary group payoffs.",1,"Multiple (57 countries across Africa, Americas, Asia, Europe, and Australia)",Participants were recruited in 57 specified countries; analyses are primarily at the country level.,1,18+ (exact age distribution N/R),They excluded respondents under 18; no further age summary is given in the main text.,0.8,N/R,The main text does not report gender composition.,0,Mixed students and non-students (convenience samples around universities; additional non-student samples in 31 countries),They targeted approximately 200 college students per country and also collected non-student samples; exact proportions per education level are not provided in the excerpt.,0.9,Online,"The survey was conducted anonymously online using Qualtrics, with a few in-person paper-and-pencil exceptions; overall it is best characterized as an online survey environment rather than a lab or field interaction game.",1,"Not a game-theoretic cooperation/punishment experiment; a large cross-national vignette survey using 10 scenarios (1 common-resource cooperation violation, 4 out-of-place behaviors, 5 meta-violation scenarios) where participants rate appropriateness (0–5 scale, later standardized) of the behavior and of four responses (verbal confrontation, gossip, social ostracism, non-action). Country-level 'metanorm' measures are estimated via multilevel models adjusting for perceived inappropriateness of the underlying behavior. Additional items measure cultural values (individual autonomy, emancipative moral judgments, tightness, perceived threat, pro-violence attitudes) and self-reported use of sanctions.",Summarizes the design details relevant to cooperation/norm enforcement while noting that it is not an incentivized game.,1
