10.1016_j.jebo.2011.01.017.md,"Baseline (B) – fixed groups, 1 punishment stage, random IDs each period","Table 1 and Section 2.1 describe the Baseline treatment: 4-player public good with a single punishment stage, random IDs each period, 16 groups.",1,Punishment stage structure and identification persistence: 1 punishment stage; IDs randomly reassigned each period (no counter-punishment or feuds across periods).,"Independent variable across treatments is the structure/number of punishment stages and whether IDs are random or fixed, affecting possibility of feuds.",1,True,Human subjects in a lab public good experiment are explicitly described.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab treatment with fixed groups and pre-specified rules.,1,True,Sessions run at universities using z-Tree in laboratory settings.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations mentioned.,1,False,"Paper reports empirical lab experiment, not pure analytical model.",1,4,Section 2.1: 'Four players simultaneously decide...'.,1,10,Section 2.1: 'The game is repeated 10 times and group composition remains unchanged.',1,1,"Players choose 'how much' of endowment to contribute; implies any integer amount between 0 and 20, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each period they receive an endowment of 20 ECU and decide how much to contribute; endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,Contributions multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally among 4 players; MPCR = 1.6/4 = 0.4 (explicitly stated).,1,0,No communication or chat mentioned; standard VCM with punishment only.,0.9,1,Punishment stages: players see each member’s contribution and later number of punishment points each assigned to each other; thus summaries of others’ actions/earnings components are shown.,1,1,"They know which group member assigned how many punishment points to whom, using IDs.",1,0,No reward mechanism in design.,1,1,Finite repeated game with 10 periods is common knowledge; subjects know total number of periods from instructions.,0.9,1,Public good wi