10.1016_j.jebo.2011.01.017.md,"Short feuds (SF) – endogenous ≥1 punishment stages, random IDs each period","Table 1 and Section 2.1 define SF: endogenous number of punishment stages within period, IDs random each period, 13 groups.",1,"Punishment stage structure and identification persistence: ≥1 punishment stages, number of punishment stages endogenous within each period; IDs randomly reassigned each period (feuds only within periods, not across).",Key treatment dimension is possibility of multi-stage within-period feuds but no inter-period feuds.,1,True,Human participants in lab public-good game.,1,True,Controlled experimental treatment with specified rules.,1,True,Conducted in university labs using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation-only component described.,1,False,Not a pure analytical model.,1,4,Same base game: 'Four players' per group.,1,10,Game repeated 10 times; SF differs only in punishment stages per period.,1,1,Contribution decision identical to Baseline: any amount 0–20 ECU.,1,0,Endowment of 20 ECU given to private account before contribution choice.,1,0.4,Contribution technology same: multiply by 1.6 and split among 4; MPCR=0.4.,1,0,No communication stage described.,0.9,1,"Same feedback structure: contributions of each member and, in further stages, punishment matrices.",1,1,"In each punishment stage players learn number of punishment points each player assigned to each other player, linked to IDs.",1,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,"Total of 10 periods is known in instructions, as in Baseline.",0.9,1,Punishment stages present and central to design.,1,1,Cost per punishment point identical: 1 ECU to punisher.,1,2,Each point reduces target’s payoff by 2 ECU.,1,0,No positive transfers or rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Same endowment of 20 ECU per period.,1,N/R,Only raw contribution amounts given.,0,11,Section 4.2: 'average contribution in SF (11.00)'.,0.95,N/R,No efficiency ratio explicitly computed; punishment costs and variation prevent easy reconstruction.,0,21.3,Section 4.3: 'earnings in SF (21.30 ECU)' mean per-person earnings.,0.9,United Kingdom; Australia,Same pool as full experiment: sessions at Royal Holloway and Melbourne; SF is one of the treatments.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students (various disciplines),Subjects described uniformly as students from various disciplines.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at two universities using z-Tree.,1,"Fixed groups of 4 across 10 periods; within each period: one contribution stage, then an endogenous sequence of punishment stages (at least one); a period ends if no punishment is assigned in a stage, or if further punishment is infeasible (all payoffs ≤0 or at least three players cannot be further punished); maximum theoretical 44 punishment stages per period (not revealed to subjects); IDs fixed within a period but randomly reassigned between periods; subjects can engage in altruistic punishment, counter-punishment, sanction enforcement, delayed punishment, and limited within-period feuds.",Summarized from Section 2.1 and discussion of SF in results.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2011.01.017.md,"Long feuds (LF) – endogenous ≥1 punishment stages, fixed IDs across periods",Table 1 and Section 2.1: LF has endogenous number of punishment stages and fixed IDs so feuds can span periods; 13 groups.,1,"Punishment stage structure and identification persistence: ≥1 punishment stages per period, number endogenous; IDs fixed across all 10 periods (allowing within- and inter-period feuds).","LF differs from other treatments by allowing long, inter-period feuds via fixed IDs and endogenous punishment stages.",1,True,Human-subject lab treatment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment-feud structure.,1,True,Sessions at universities using z-Tree.,1,False,No non-human simulations in this treatment.,1,False,"Empirical experimental design, not purely analytical.",1,4,Same 4-player group public good game.,1,10,Game repeated 10 times; in LF only punishment stages per period are endogenous.,1,1,Continuous (integer) contribution choice from 0–20 ECU.,1,0,20 ECU endowment given then contribution decision.,1,0.4,Contribution technology identical: 1.6 multiplier / 4 players.,1,0,No chat or communication reported.,0.9,1,Players see individual contributions and detailed punishment information each stage.,1,1,"They are informed 'about the number of points assigned by each individual player to each of the players' in previous stage, using fixed IDs.",1,0,No reward described.,1,1,Finite 10 periods known at outset; same repeated-game framing.,0.9,1,Multiple punishment stages explicitly defined.,1,1,Punishment point costs 1 ECU.,1,2,Each point reduces punished player's payoff by 2 ECU.,1,0,No rewards or positive bonuses under player control.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,20,Same 20 ECU per period endowment.,1,N/R,Only average ECU contributions reported.,0,9.64,Section 4.2: 'average contribution in LF (9.64)'.,0.95,N/R,Efficiency ratio not provided and cannot be safely derived without detailed punishment data.,0,23.43,Section 4.3: 'earnings in LF (on average 23.43 ECU)'.,0.9,United Kingdom; Australia,LF sessions are part of the same experiment run at Royal Holloway and Melbourne.,1,N/R,No age information given.,0,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,0,University students (various disciplines),Same subject pool description.,1,On site,Lab sessions at universities using z-Tree.,1,"Fixed groups of 4 across 10 periods; in each period a contribution stage is followed by an endogenous series of punishment stages; period ends if no punishment is assigned in a stage or further punishment is impossible (all payoffs ≤0 or at least three players cannot be further punished); punishment restricted to integers and max total points assignable per player per stage is limited by current payoff; IDs are fixed across all periods, allowing subjects to recognize and retaliate against specific others over time and enabling inter-period feuds; one-off starting capital (125 ECU) provided to cover possible losses from feuding.","Compiled from Sections 2.1 and 2.2, and discussion of LF.",1
10.1073_pnas.0908855106.md,Trust game – sanction condition (investor threatens sanction),Condition where the investor chooses to impose a sanction threat; used in sanction vs no-sanction contrasts.,0.9,Presence of sanction threat: sanction,Main treatment variable is whether investor threatens a fixed 4-MU sanction or not; this row corresponds to the sanction-present case.,1,True,Human subjects playing a two-party investment (trust) game with fMRI.,1,True,Controlled lab-like economic game with random role assignment and fixed rules.,1,True,Conducted in an fMRI scanner environment at a medical school; participants recruited via ads.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations described.,1,False,No purely formal or closed-form analytical model reported; focus is on empirical and neuroimaging analyses.,1,2,Game is explicitly described as involving 2 mutually anonymous participants (investor and trustee).,1,10,Text: “paired together for 10 trials… A total of 10 rounds are played”.,1,1,"Players choose ‘amount of money’ to send/return; implies any integer/continuous amount up to 10 MUs, not just all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Standard trust game: each has 10 MUs in private account; investor must actively send to create the shared surplus. Thus endowment starts fully private.,0.9,N/R,"MPCR is defined for public goods; here investment is tripled and returned in a bilateral trust game, not a linear public good. MPCR not reported.",1,0,"No communication between anonymous partners is mentioned; standard scanner-based experiment, and no chat feature described.",0.8,1,"After each round, “each player's earnings are revealed to both players,” which is a summary of outcomes.",1,0,Partners are “mutually anonymous”; sanctions are known but not tied to real identities.,0.9,0,"No rewards exist, only sanctions; identities are anonymous.",1,1,"They explicitly state there are 10 rounds; in such economic lab tasks subjects are normally informed of total number of rounds, and analysis invokes finite-horizon strategies.",0.7,1,Design includes an investor option to threaten a ‘monetary sanction’.,1,4,Sanction is a fixed 4-MU deduction from trustee’s final earnings if request not met. This is the effective punishment magnitude borne by the trustee per threat realized.,1,4,"For 1 sanction ‘unit’ chosen (binary decision), trustee loses 4 MUs; so 1 unit causes 4-MU loss. Treated as 4 MUs damage per sanction action.",0.8,0,No mention of any reward or bonus mechanism beyond regular payoffs from the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,10,Both players are given 10 MUs at the beginning of each trial.,1,0.47,"For fair requests, with sanctions, trustees return “47% of the tripled investment amount.” This normalized return is the cooperation rate relevant to trustee behavior under sanction.",0.9,6.05,Average repayment in sanction cases is reported as 6.05 MUs.,1,N/R,No efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum) reported or derivable without full distribution of investments/returns and punishments.,0.7,N/R,Paper gives averages of repayments and sanction use but not average final joint payoffs per round.,0.5,USA,"Study conducted at Baylor College of Medicine (Houston, USA); recruitment via local newspapers and school flyers suggests US-based participants.",0.85,18–58 (overall); trustees 18–35 (mean 25.4 ± 0.4); investors 20–58 (mean 31.1 ± 1.2),Methods: overall range 18–58; role-specific ranges and means for trustees and investors reported.,1,Overall: 61 females out of 104; investors: 36 females out of 52; trustees: 25 females out of 52,Methods section reports these counts separately.,1,N/R,"No information on education level (e.g., student/non-student, degree) is reported.",1,On site,In-person fMRI scanning at a medical school with physical button boxes and projector.,1,"Iterated (10-round) two-player trust game. Each round: investor and trustee each start with 10 MUs; investor chooses amount to send (tripled en route to trustee), amount to request back, and whether to threaten a sanction. If investor’s request is not met when threatened, trustee loses a fixed 4 MUs. Roles and pairs are fixed across 10 rounds; both players see all decisions and earnings at end of each round. Analyses focus on trustee reciprocity under sanction vs no-sanction threats.",Summary synthesized from the task description and figures.,1
10.1073_pnas.0908855106.md,Trust game – no-sanction condition (investor does not threaten sanction),Condition where the investor chooses not to impose a sanction threat; contrasted with sanction condition.,0.9,Presence of sanction threat: no-sanction,"Same game, but the independent variable level is absence of sanction threat.",1,True,Human subjects in the same fMRI trust game without sanction threat in that trial.,1,True,Within the same controlled lab experiment; this is the no-sanction condition.,1,True,Same scanner-based laboratory context as sanction condition.,1,False,No simulations described.,1,False,No separate analytical-only study.,1,2,Same two-player trust game structure.,1,10,Same 10 rounds for each pair.,1,1,Same choice structure (continuous/any amount) for investments and repayments.,0.9,0,Endowments start entirely in private accounts for both players each round.,0.9,N/R,MPCR is not defined or reported for this bilateral trust game.,1,0,No communication channel described; anonymity and scanner setting imply no chat.,0.8,1,"As in sanction condition, round earnings are revealed to both players.",1,0,No sanction is used in this condition; identities remain anonymous.,0.9,0,"No rewards, only base game payoffs; anonymity maintained.",1,1,Game is explicitly 10 rounds; subjects likely informed of horizon as in typical repeated trust game designs.,0.7,0,"In this condition, investor chooses not to threaten a sanction, so no punishment mechanism is active for the round.",0.9,N/A,No sanction is available/used in the no-sanction condition.,1,N/A,No sanction mechanism is active.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism exists in this condition either.,1,N/A,No rewards are possible.,1,N/A,No rewards are possible.,1,10,Both players start each round with 10 MUs in all conditions.,1,0.59,"For unfair requests, returns are 59% of the tripled investment; for fair requests 53%. Both are higher than under sanctions. To characterize no-sanction cooperation, use the higher reported 59