10.1257_aer.98.2.494.md,Sequential one-shot trust game with third-party punishment – HHH (all high caste),Paper describes a single exchange game with four caste-composition treatment conditions; this row is the HHH condition where all three players are high caste.,0.98,"Caste composition of the three players: HHH (A high caste, B high caste, C high caste)","Treatments differ only in the caste status of players A, B, and C; here all are from high castes (HHH).",1,True,"Game played by 621 adult male participants in villages in Uttar Pradesh, India.",1,True,Design involves random assignment to roles and controlled variation in caste composition.,1,False,"Conducted in villages in Uttar Pradesh, not a conventional university or centralized lab; best classified as field/artefactual field experiment.",0.7,False,All decisions made by human participants; no agent-based or numerical-only model.,1,False,Game is implemented experimentally rather than solved only analytically.,1,3,"Game explicitly involves three players A, B, and C.",1,1,"Game described as sequential, one-shot exchange game.",1,0,"Player A has a binary choice (keep or send all 50); player C can choose any number of 2-rupee coins to spend on punishment, so contributions/punishment are not all-or-nothing. The variable refers to contribution flexibility; here the key costly action (punishment by C) is continuous.",0.6,0,"Exchange/trust game: initial endowments are in private accounts of A, B, and C; nothing starts in a public account.",1,N/R,Not a public goods or linear VCM structure; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No mention of communication among players; players drawn from different villages and names not revealed; implies no communication.,0.8,0,C only observes B’s action (keep/share). No evidence that earnings summaries or histories are shown.,0.7,0,Players come from different villages whose names are never revealed; implies identities are not revealed in punishment.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in this game; thus no identity revelation in rewards.,1,1,Described as one-shot and rules fully explained; participants necessarily know there is only one round.,0.8,1,Player C can spend 2-rupee coins to reduce B’s payoff by 10 rupees (third-party punishment).,1,2,"For every two-rupee coin C spends, B loses a 10-rupee note; cost to punisher is 2.",1,10,Each 2 rupees spent by C reduces B’s payoff by 10 rupees; the technology factor is 10 per punishment decision unit (2-rupee coin).,1,0,Only punishment from C to B is described; no positive reward option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,50,Players A and B have an endowment of 50 rupees; C has 100. For comparability we record the trustor’s basic endowment (50); heterogeneity noted here.,0.7,N/R,Primary outcomes are punishment decisions; A’s send/keep rate and B’s share/keep decisions are not normalized as contribution rates in the paper.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports proportions of third parties who punish cooperators/defectors, not rupee amounts spent on punishment or sent by A.",0.3,N/R,No explicit or computable group efficiency measure over all C decisions is reported.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate payoffs by treatment are not reported.,0.1,India,"Experiments conducted in Uttar Pradesh, one of the least developed regions in India.",1,Adult males,"Text: “In total, 621 adult male players participated.” No further age statistics.",0.9,Male,All 621 participants are described as adult male players.,1,N/R,Education is mentioned as a control variable in analysis but no distribution is provided here.,0.2,Field experiment,Game conducted with villagers in Uttar Pradesh; roles assigned and payoffs in rupees; this matches artefactual field experiment.,0.9,"Sequential one-shot trust/exchange game with third-party punishment. A (50 rupees) decides to keep 50 or send 50 to B; if sent, amount is tripled to 150, giving B 200 total. B either keeps 200 or shares equally (100,100) with A. C (100 rupees) then chooses punishment: for each 2 rupees paid by C, B loses 10 rupees. C chooses punishment levels for both possible B actions before learning B’s actual choice.",Summarizes detailed description of game structure and timing from Section II.,1
10.1257_aer.98.2.494.md,Sequential one-shot trust game with third-party punishment – HLH (only B low caste),Same game as above with different caste composition: HLH where only trustee B is low caste.,0.98,"Caste composition of the three players: HLH (A high caste, B low caste, C high caste)",Paper lists HLH as treatment where only B is from a low caste.,1,True,Same participant pool and procedures as other treatments of this trust game.,1,True,Controlled variation in caste composition between otherwise identical game structures.,1,False,"Conducted in villages, not in a standard lab.",0.7,False,Human subjects only.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical model.,1,3,"Three players A, B, and C.",1,1,Game is one-shot.,1,0,"As in HHH, C’s punishment choice is continuous in 2-rupee coins.",0.6,0,All endowments start in private accounts.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication is mentioned; anonymity across villages stressed.,0.8,0,Only B’s action is observed; no summaries stated.,0.7,0,Names not revealed; implies anonymous punishment.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Game is explicitly one-shot and explained as such.,0.8,1,C can punish B at a cost.,1,2,Each 2 rupees spent reduces B’s payoff by 10.,1,10,Punishment multiplier for B’s loss per 2-rupee coin spent by C.,1,0,No positive sanction option.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,50,"Same monetary endowments as in HHH: A and B have 50 rupees, C has 100.",0.9,N/R,"Paper gives only proportions of Cs who punish cooperators/defectors by treatment, without contribution rate normalization.",0.3,N/R,Does not report rupee amounts punished/spent by treatment.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency measure per treatment reported.,0.2,N/R,No treatment-level payoff aggregates in the text.,0.1,India,Same Uttar Pradesh setting.,1,Adult males,All 621 participants described as adult male players.,0.9,Male,Sample specified as adult male players.,1,N/R,Education not broken out by treatment.,0.2,Field experiment,Same field setting in rural India.,0.9,"Identical sequential one-shot exchange/trust game with third-party punishment as in HHH; only caste composition differs: trustee B is low caste, A and C high caste.",Game rules do not depend on caste composition by design.,1
