10.1257_aer.98.2.494.md,Stag hunt coordination game – LL treatment (both players low caste),Section III describes a stag hunt game with three treatments differing only in caste composition; this row corresponds to LL pairs.,0.98,Caste composition of the two players in the pair: LL (both low caste),Authors define an ‘LL treatment (both players from a low caste)’.,1,True,"Game played by human subjects in India, with payoffs in rupees.",1,True,Controlled assignment to caste-composition treatments and repeated-game structure.,1,False,Conducted in rural India similar to other Hoff & Pandey experiments; best treated as field/artefactual field experiment.,0.7,False,No computer simulation; all decisions by human participants.,1,False,"Game is implemented experimentally, not purely analytically solved.",1,2,Stag hunt is played in pairs (two players).,1,10,"Subjects played the game ten times: five periods with one partner, five with another.",1,1,Action space is binary: cooperate or defect; no intermediate contribution levels.,1,0,This is a normal-form coordination game with no endowment allocation between private/public accounts; set to 0 by convention.,0.6,N/R,Not a linear public good; simple 2x2 payoff matrix without MPCR concept.,1,0,No mention of communication; repeated anonymous play with feedback only about partner’s action.,0.8,0,"After each period subjects receive feedback about partner’s choice, not aggregate summaries.",0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in this game.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Ten-period structure (two blocks of five) is part of the instructions, so participants know number of rounds.",0.9,0,No possibility to punish partner; only cooperate/defect decisions.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit rewarding choices beyond game payoffs.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoffs specified directly in tokens (3,7,10), not as endowment plus contributions.",0.5,N/R,"Outcome is coordination on the (Cooperate,Cooperate) equilibrium rather than continuous contribution.",0.4,N/R,Only binary actions with reported frequencies; no token amounts chosen.,0.4,N/R,"Authors report proportion of pairs in ‘good equilibrium’, not normalized efficiency index, though (10,10) is Pareto-dominant.",0.5,N/R,Aggregate average group payoff over rounds for LL not provided.,0.3,India,Hoff and Pandey experiments cited are from rural India; consistent with prior description.,0.9,N/R,Age range for stag hunt game participants not specified in the excerpt.,0.2,N/R,"For this experiment, gender composition not explicitly stated; prior trust game used adult males but cannot be assumed identical.",0.3,N/R,No education breakdown given here.,0.2,Field experiment,"Described as conducted by Hoff and Pandey in India, similar context to their other field experiments.",0.7,"2x2 stag hunt with payoffs: (Cooperate,Cooperate) gives (10,10), (Cooperate,Defect) gives (3,7), (Defect,Cooperate) gives (7,3), (Defect,Defect) gives (7,7). Tokens converted 1:1 to rupees. Ten periods: first five with same partner (feedback on partner’s action each period), periods 6–10 with a new partner. LL treatment pairs both players from low caste. In round 5, LL pairs reach (Cooperate,Cooperate) in 10/15 cases; in the second half they reach it in 12/15 cases.",Directly summarizing the payoff matrix and procedural details in Section III.,1
