rately given for LH in the excerpt.",Based on descriptive statistics provided for mixed pairs in Section III.,0.9
10.1257_aer.98.2.494.md,Binary choice dictator game – Game 1,Table 1 lists seven distinct binary choice dictator games; this row corresponds to Game 1.,1,"Dictator game payoff options: B chooses between (other 90, self 90) and (other 70, self 100); within-game comparisons by dictator caste (high vs low) but game structure identical across them.",Game 1 defined as choice between these two allocations; only caste of B varies across subjects.,1,True,240 subjects make incentivized allocation decisions; human participants.,1,True,Binary choices in controlled dictator games with randomized matching and double-blind protocol.,1,False,Sessions held in nearby locations in villages; resembles artefactual field experiment rather than standard lab.,0.7,False,No simulation or artificial agents.,1,False,Behavior measured experimentally.,1,2,Dictator (B) and recipient (A).,1,1,Each subject makes one choice in one dictator game as B.,1,1,Decision is a binary choice between two fixed allocations; no continuous contribution.,1,0,No endowment split or public good; dictator simply selects an allocation.,0.7,N/R,Not a public goods or VCM structure.,1,0,Double-blind protocol and separate locations intended to prevent interaction; no communication mentioned.,0.9,0,Subjects only know partner’s caste and village; no payoff summaries.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,Allocations are anonymous and double-blind; recipient identity not revealed beyond caste and village.,0.9,1,Instructions clarify that each subject makes a single decision as B in that game.,0.8,0,No punishment stage; only allocation choice.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,Allocations themselves are the game outcome; no separate rewarding decision.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game defined directly by payoff allocations; no initial endowment is specified.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes reported as fraction choosing the ‘left option’ per caste, not contribution rates.",0.8,N/R,Binary choice between allocations; no variable amount chosen.,0.9,N/R,Paper reports choice frequencies and uses them to infer preferences; no explicit efficiency measure.,0.8,N/R,Average payoffs for game 1 decisions are not reported.,0.4,India,Hoff and Pandey’s dictator games conducted in rural India as part of same research program.,0.9,N/R,Age distribution not specified for dictator game participants.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not specified; cannot assume all male.,0.3,N/R,No education information provided.,0.2,Field experiment,"Sessions run in ‘nearby locations’ with villagers, double-blind, typical of artefactual field experiments.",0.7,"Binary dictator decision: B chooses between egalitarian allocation (90 to other, 90 to self) and selfish allocation (70 to other, 100 to self). N=60 subjects (30 high caste, 30 low caste) for Game 1; 40% of high-caste and 47% of low-caste dictators choose the left (more egalitarian, less selfish) option.",Directly from Table 1 notes and text.,1
10.1257_aer.98.2.494.md,Binary choice dictator game – Game 2,Second dictator game listed in Table 1.,1,"Dictator game payoff options: B chooses between (other 70, self 90) and (other 90, self 90); comparison across dictator caste.","Game 2 defined as B chooses (70,90) vs (90,90).",1,True,Same experimental dictator design with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled binary choices with random matching.,1,False,Field/artefactual-field setting in villages.,0.7,False,No simulation.,1,False,Empirical.,1,2,Dictator and recipient.,1,1,Each subject makes one allocation as B in one game.,1,1,Binary choice between two fixed payoff vectors.,1,0,No endowment splitting; pure allocation choice.,0.7,N/R,Not a public good.,1,0,Double-blind protocol precludes discussion.,0.9,0,Subjects only know partner’s caste and village; no feedback summaries.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,Allocations are anonymous; no identity revealed.,0.9,1,Single decision per subject as B.,0.8,0,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No separate rewarding decisions.,1,N/A,No separate rewarding.,1,N/A,No separate rewarding.,1,N/R,Outcomes fully specified by payoff options; no endowment described.,0.8,N/R,Outcome data: fraction choosing left option; not a contribution rate.,0.9,N/R,No continuous contribution.,0.9,N/R,Authors focus on prevalence of spiteful choices; no efficiency index.,0.9,N/R,Mean group payoffs not reported.,0.4,India,Same sample frame as other Hoff & Pandey dictator games.,0.9,N/R,Not specified.,0.2,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,N/R,Not provided.,0.2,Field experiment,"Nearby locations, villagers, double-blind, not standard lab.",0.7,"B chooses between (70 to other, 90 to self) and (90 to other, 90 to self). Left option reduces partner’s payoff without changing own, lowering total surplus and violating equality norm. N=76 (38 high caste, 38 low caste). 42% of high-caste and 21% of low-caste subjects choose the left (spiteful) option; difference significant.",From Table 1 and accompanying discussion highlighting Game 2 as spiteful.,1
10.1257_aer.98.2.494.md,Binary choice dictator game – Game 3,Third dictator game in Table 1.,1,"Dictator game payoff options: B chooses between (other 100, self 100) and (other 180, self 110); comparison across dictator caste.","Game 3: B chooses (100,100) vs (180,110).",1,True,Same binary-choice dictator experiment.,1,True,Incentivized choices in controlled setting.,1,False,Field/artefactual-field nature as above.,0.7,False,Human decision makers.,1,False,Empirical.,1,2,Dictator and recipient.,1,1,Single choice.,1,1,Binary allocation choice.,1,0,No endowment; pure allocation choice.,0.7,N/R,Not public-goods.,1,0,Double-blind and spatial separation.,0.9,0,No feedback during game.,0.9,0,No punishment.,1,0,Anonymity maintained; no explicit identity revelation with rewards.,0.9,1,One-shot decision.,0.8,0,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,Allocations are the only outcome.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoff vectors given directly.,0.8,N/R,Binary choices; reported as fractions choosing left option.,0.9,N/R,No flexible amount chosen.,0.9,N/R,"Analysis focuses on inequity aversion and spite, not efficiency.",0.9,N/R,Group payoff stats not provided.,0.4,India,Same rural India context.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,Field experiment,Same setting as other dictator games.,0.7,"B chooses between (100 to other, 100 to self) and (180 to other, 110 to self). This allows B to escape disadvantageous inequality by reducing A’s payoff. N=90 (45 high, 45 low). 44% of high-caste vs 29% of low-caste dictators choose the left (less disadvantageous inequality) option; difference weakly significant.",Summarizes Game 3 description and stats from text.,1
