10.1257_mic.6.2.247.md,N treatment – Rounds 11–20 (No Communication),Control condition in Section II.C labeled 'No Communication (N Treatment)' where rounds 11–20 have no communication.,1,Communication availability in rounds 11–20: none (no messages or chat).,Treatments differ only by communication structure; here no communication is allowed in any period.,1,True,Human undergraduates participated in lab sessions.,1,True,"Random matching, controlled payoff matrices, and treatments manipulated by the experimenters.",1,True,Conducted in a computerized laboratory at Case Western Reserve University using z-Tree.,1,False,All results are from human-subject lab sessions; no agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,"Paper contains theoretical discussion but the reported results are from experiments, not formal analytical derivations alone.",1,2,Two-player (row and column) TPBG; subjects matched with a single opponent each round.,1,20,Subjects play 20 rounds of the TPBG in all treatments.,1,0,"In each period players choose among three discrete price actions (Low, Medium, High), not a continuous contribution nor an all-or-nothing decision.",1,0,No endowment/contribution framing; effectively all payoff comes from own choices; default private account interpretation.,0.2,N/R,"Game is a 2x3 Bertrand/coordination game with fixed payoffs, not a linear public good; MPCR not defined.",1,0,No communication available in any period for N treatment rounds 11–20.,1,1,Interface shows a summary of prices and payoffs for both players for all previous rounds and per-period feedback.,1,0,"No explicit punishment mechanism; only anonymous opponent with a random chat id in chat treatments, but here no chat.",0.9,0,No explicit reward mechanism and no identities revealed.,1,1,Subjects knew they would be playing a total of 20 rounds of the TPBG.,1,0,No separate costly punishment stage; only choice of stage-game actions.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Subjects directly choose actions with associated payoffs; no per-round endowment specified.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes measured as prices (Low/Medium/High), not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No contribution variable; only price choices and earnings.,1,N/R,"Paper reports earnings and frequencies of actions, but no normalized efficiency measure.",0.2,N/R,"Only some average individual payoffs reported (e.g., round 1 averages across treatments); treatment-specific group payoffs not tabulated.",0.2,United States,Sessions conducted at Case Western Reserve University in the U.S.,0.9,N/R,Only that subjects are undergraduates; no age statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Subjects recruited via emails sent to all undergraduates.,1,On site,Sessions run in a computerized laboratory on campus; not online or field.,1,"Two-Period Bertrand Game (TPBG): 2 players, 2 periods per round. Period 1 payoff matrix: choices Low, Medium, High with listed ECUs (e.g., (L,L)=15, (M,M)=45, (H,H)=60 for row). Period 2 is a coordination game derived from an infinitely repeated Bertrand game with three Pareto-ranked equilibria (L,L), (M,M), (H,H). Random rematching across 20 rounds; fixed pairing within round; abstract labels A,B,C and D,E,F used instead of prices.",Summarizes the game structure and payoff environment stated in Section I and II.,1
10.1257_mic.6.2.247.md,P1 treatment – Rounds 11–20 (Period 1 Limited Communication),Condition (ii) in Section II.C labeled 'Period 1 Limited Communication (P1 Treatment)'.,1,"Communication availability in rounds 11–20: one-shot, limited, simultaneous pre-Period-1 intent messages about own and other's Period 1 actions; no messages about Period 2.",P1 treatment description specifies this limited pre-Period-1 message structure only.,1,True,Human undergraduate subjects in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Between-subjects communication treatments with random matching and controlled conditions.,1,True,Run in computerized laboratory at Case Western Reserve University using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation results presented.,1,False,Results are experimental rather than purely analytical.,1,2,Same TPBG two-player game as in other treatments.,1,20,All treatments use 20 rounds of the TPBG.,1,0,"Discrete choice among Low/Medium/High prices, not all-or-nothing contributions.",1,0,No public-good endowment framework; payoffs determined solely by chosen actions.,0.2,N/R,"Price game, not linear public-goods; MPCR concept not applicable.",1,0,Only structured pre-Period-1 intent messages; no free-form chat.,1,1,Interface shows history of prices and payoffs for both players for all previous rounds and feedback after each period.,1,0,No identity revealed; no explicit punishment stage.,0.9,0,No explicit reward mechanism or identity disclosure.,1,1,Subjects know total of 20 rounds.,1,0,No separate costly punishment action; only standard stage-game choices.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Payoffs determined from matrices; no token endowment described.,0.2,N/R,"Dependent variables in paper are frequencies of High/Medium/Low, cheating, and payoffs, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts defined.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure reported by treatment.,0.2,N/R,Treatment-level average payoffs per pair are not tabulated.,0.2,United States,Sessions at Case Western Reserve University.,0.9,N/R,Only recruitment of undergraduates reported.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition statistics given.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Subjects were undergraduates recruited via campus-wide emails.,1,On site,Computerized lab sessions using z-Tree.,1,"Same TPBG as other treatments with random rematching. In rounds 11–20, before Period 1, each subject sends a structured message: 'I think we should choose the following in Period 1' specifying Low/Medium/High/No Response for 'My Choice' and 'Your Choice'. Messages from both players are shown simultaneously before Period 1 decision and reiterated in Period 1 feedback. No messages about Period 2.",Summarizes communication protocol particular to P1 on top of the shared game structure.,1
10.1257_mic.6.2.247.md,P1C treatment – Rounds 11–20 (Period 1 Limited Communication with Contingencies),Condition (iii) in Section II.C labeled 'Period 1 Limited Communication with Contingencies (P1C Treatment)'.,1,"Communication availability in rounds 11–20: one-shot, limited, simultaneous pre-Period-1 messages specifying intended Period 1 actions (own and other's) plus contingent recommendations for Period 2 actions depending on whether Period 1 agreement is realized.",P1C treatment adds contingent messages about Period 2 to the P1 message structure.,1,True,Human-subject experimental treatment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of available messages with random matching.,1,True,Run in computerized lab at Case Western Reserve using z-Tree.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Reports experimental outcomes.,1,2,Two players per TPBG round.,1,20,All treatments use 20 rounds.,1,0,Three discrete price choices each period.,1,0,No contribution/endowment framing.,0.2,N/R,Not a public-goods environment.,1,0,Only pre-Period-1 and contingent message forms; no free-form chat window.,1,1,Subjects see summary of outcomes for all previous rounds and feedback after each period.,1,0,No explicit punishment stage or identity revelation.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism with IDs.,1,1,Total number of rounds (20) known in advance.,1,0,No separate costly punishment choice; only continuation-play discipline via normal actions.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No costly rewarding moves.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,No token endowment reported.,0.2,N/R,Study focuses on price choices and cheating rather than contributions.,1,N/R,No contribution measure used.,1,N/R,Efficiency not explicitly computed per treatment.,0.2,N/R,No detailed treatment-wise group payoff table.,0.2,United States,Same lab and recruitment as other treatments.,0.9,N/R,Only that they are undergraduates.,0.3,N/R,Gender shares not reported.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Explicitly recruited among undergraduates.,1,On site,Computer laboratory experiment on campus.,1,"Same TPBG as in other treatments. In rounds 11–20, pre-Period-1 messages include: (1) proposal for Period 1 actions ('I think we should choose the following in Period 1' for both players) and (2) two contingent statements for Period 2: if the proposed Period 1 actions occur, and if they do NOT occur. Limited message space: only Low/Medium/High/No Response choices. Both players see the complete set of messages before choosing Period 1 actions; messages are displayed in feedback.",Summarizes distinctive contingent-message structure used to support potential punishment schemes.,1
10.1257_mic.6.2.247.md,"PChat treatment – Rounds 11–20 (Preplay Chat, pre-Period-1 only)",Condition (iv) in Section II.C labeled 'Preplay Chat (PChat Treatment)' with chat prior to Period 1 only.,1,"Communication availability in rounds 11–20: unrestricted, synchronous free-form chat between matched pair before Period 1; no chat between periods.",PChat treatment allows z-Tree chat prior to Period 1 decisions only.,1,True,Based on human-subject experimental data.,1,True,Within-lab randomized pairing and exogenous communication treatment.,1,True,Sessions conducted in a computer lab using z-Tree chat.,1,False,No non-human simulations run.,1,False,"Results are empirical, not purely analytical.",1,2,Two-player TPBG per round.,1,20,Game repeated for 20 rounds.,1,0,Three discrete price options each period.,1,0,No contribution framing.,0.2,N/R,Price coordination game.,1,1,Pre-Period-1 chat via z-Tree is allowed in rounds 11–20.,1,1,Summary of previous rounds’ prices and payoffs visible.,1,1,Chat messages labeled with a randomly generated 10-digit 'chat id' so players can distinguish own vs opponent's messages; this is an identifier relevant for verbal punishment.,0.9,1,Same chat id labeling applies to any positive or reward-type messages; opponent identifiable within the round.,0.9,1,Subjects informed they will play 20 rounds.,1,0,No formal costly punishment stage; punishment only via choice of actions or verbal admonitions.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology with token costs.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Only stated that payoffs are denominated in ECUs; no explicit endowment per round.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes tracked as proportion choosing High/Medium/Low, cheating rates, and payoffs, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics not explicitly computed per treatment.,0.2,N/R,No table of treatment-level group payoffs.,0.2,United States,Experiment run at U.S. university lab.,0.9,N/R,Population is undergraduates but no age numbers.,0.3,N/R,Gender not discussed.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Participants are undergraduates.,1,On site,Computer lab with in-person participation and chat via z-Tree.,1,"Same TPBG with random rematching across 20 rounds. In rounds 11–20, at the start of each round, matched players have a free-form chat window (like IM) before selecting Period 1 actions. Continuous back-and-forth messages allowed until one player submits a decision for the period. Instructions stress that chat is cheap talk, not affecting payoffs directly. No chat is available between Period 1 and Period 2 decisions.",Captures the rich communication environment and timing in the PChat treatment.,1
10.1257_mic.6.2.247.md,"RChat treatment – Rounds 11–20 (Renegotiation Chat, pre- and mid-game)",Condition (v) in Section II.C labeled 'Renegotiation (RChat Treatment)'.,1,"Communication availability in rounds 11–20: unrestricted, synchronous free-form chat before Period 1 and again between Period 1 and Period 2 (renegotiation stage).",RChat treatment is PChat plus additional chat between periods to allow renegotiation.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of renegotiation chat availability.,1,True,Conducted in a computerized laboratory using z-Tree chat.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,Reports empirical data.,1,2,Two-player TPBG game per round.,1,20,"Intended 20 rounds; one RChat session ended at 16 rounds and another at 18 due to time, but design is 20.",0.9,0,Discrete price actions Low/Medium/High.,1,0,No contribution mechanism.,0.2,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,1,Free-form chat permitted both before Period 1 and between Period 1 and 2.,1,1,Outcome summaries of prior rounds and current round payoffs provided on screen.,1,1,"Chat messages tagged with random 'chat id' per player, enabling directed verbal punishment within a round.",0.9,1,Same id labeling for any positive messages; opponent distinguishable within round.,0.9,1,Subjects informed they will play 20 rounds of TPBG.,1,0,No monetary punishment stage; only strategy choices and verbal admonitions.,1,N/A,No separate punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No costly rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment given.,0.2,N/R,No contribution decisions; focus on collusive pricing behavior.,1,N/R,No contribution variable recorded.,1,N/R,"While collusion yields higher payoffs, no explicit efficiency ratio is reported.",0.2,N/R,Average payoffs by treatment not tabulated in the text.,0.2,United States,Conducted at Case Western lab.,0.9,N/R,Participants are undergraduates; no age distribution.,0.3,N/R,Gender not specified.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate population.,1,On site,In-person computerized experiment with chat.,1,"Same TPBG and random rematching as other treatments. In rounds 11–20, subjects have a chat window before Period 1 identical to PChat, and an additional chat window between observing Period 1 actions and choosing Period 2 actions. This enables explicit renegotiation of punishments and continuation play and extensive verbal punishment (admonitions) after cheating. Some sessions auto-terminated at 16 or 18 rounds due to a 2-hour time cap.",Highlights the renegotiation chat stage and its behavioral role as nonpecuniary punishment.,1
10.1016_s0167-2681(02)00032-x.md,Whole paper – theoretical and simulation survey (no single focal condition),"The article is a survey of evolutionary game theoretic models; it does not report a specific, well-defined experiment, simulation run with numeric parameters, or observational dataset that could serve as a distinct condition.",1,N/R,"No concrete experimental, simulation, or observational conditions with varying independent variables are implemented by the authors; they only summarize others’ models qualitatively.",1,False,"The paper is purely theoretical and survey-based, with no original data collection from human participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment is designed or run by the authors; they only discuss others’ theoretical and experimental work.,1,False,There is no lab or other direct experimentation performed in this paper.,1,False,"Although the paper mentions simulations (e.g., Axelrod’s tournaments), it does not present new simulations or specify simulation conditions implemented by the authors.",1,True,The paper develops and summarizes analytical evolutionary game-theoretic arguments and models; all results are derived analytically.,1,0,"No single concrete game instance is implemented as an experiment or simulation; numerous theoretical games are discussed with varying player counts (often 2, sometimes k>2).",1,0,"The paper covers both one-shot and repeated games, but does not define a specific experimental protocol with a fixed number of rounds.",1,0,Not applicable because no actual decision interface or contribution choice set is implemented with human or simulated agents by the authors.,1,N/A,No human-subject or concrete public-good contribution game is actually run; only theoretical payoff matrices are discussed.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return values are not specified for a concrete experimental public goods design; the paper mostly analyzes general prisoners’ dilemma and other generic games.,1,N/A,There is no experimental interface; communication opportunities are not implemented by the authors.,1,0,Not applicable to an actual experiment; no interface is implemented.,1,N/A,Punishment is discussed only theoretically; no experimental feedback protocol is implemented.,1,N/A,Rewards are only part of theoretical models; no implemented mechanism with identity revelation exists.,1,0,No experimental rounds are run with subjects; therefore nothing is displayed.,1,0,"Although costly sanctions are analyzed in theory, there is no implemented punishment mechanism in a concrete experiment or simulation run by the authors.",1,N/A,"No specific numeric cost of punishment is implemented in an experiment or simulation; only generic symbols (e.g., γ, δ) appear in theoretical payoff matrices.",1,N/A,Damage inflicted per punishment unit is only symbolically represented (δ) and varies across discussed models; no fixed implemented value exists.,1,0,The paper focuses mainly on cooperation and punishment; no specific reward mechanism is implemented in an experiment or simulation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology is operationalized by the authors.,1,N/A,No implemented reward multiplier or technology is specified.,1,0,"There is no actual experiment with endowments controlled by the authors; only abstract payoffs (α, β, etc.) are defined.",1,N/R,No empirical or simulated contribution rates are reported by the authors for a specific game implementation.,1,N/R,The paper presents no numeric contribution data.,1,N/R,"Although efficiency in payoff terms is discussed conceptually, no numerical efficiency outcomes are reported for a concrete implemented condition.",1,N/R,No realized or simulated group payoff data are tabulated; only symbolic payoff expressions are used.,1,N/A,No human participants are used in this theoretical survey.,1,N/A,No human participant sample exists.,1,N/A,No human participant sample exists.,1,N/A,No human participant sample exists.,1,No human,"The work is purely theoretical and does not involve human-subject data collection in lab, online, field, or observational settings by the authors.",1,"Survey of evolutionary game-theoretic models of reciprocity, including infinitely repeated prisoners’ dilemma, public goods games, costly punishment, assortative matching, signaling, and preference evolution. All games are presented analytically with symbolic payoffs (e.g., α, β, γ, δ) and without a single implemented experimental condition.","Summarizes the overall content of the paper, which is exclusively theoretical and synthetic across many models rather than reporting one specific experiment.",1
10.1257_aer.100.1.448.md,Analytical model – Incomplete information game Γ_AI,Single formal game-theoretic model analyzed under asymmetric information; no separate empirical treatments are described.,1,"Information structure (player 2 informed about productive actions; player 1 uninformed with improper uniform prior); action productivity set N and benefits b(a); discount factor δ; probabilities p and q; costs k, c, and benefit π; number of productive actions N.","Model parameters and information structure are the only elements varied conceptually across propositions (e.g., Assumptions 1 and 2).",0.9,False,The paper develops and analyzes a theoretical repeated-game model; there are no human participants or data collection.,1,False,No controlled laboratory or field experiment is run; the work is purely theoretical.,1,False,No lab or experimental setting is used; only analytical modeling.,1,False,The paper does not report numerical or agent-based simulations; results are derived analytically.,1,True,"All results (definitions, propositions, lemmas) are obtained via formal analytical derivations in a repeated-game framework.",1,2,"The model explicitly considers two players i ∈ {1,2}.",1,-1,"The game is played in infinite horizon, discrete time t ∈ ℕ; use -1 to denote infinite repetition.",1,1,"Player 2 chooses an action a from a set; this is not a divisible contribution but rather discrete action choice; mapping to contribution is N/A, but by instructions must choose 0/1. Use 1 to indicate not restricted to a binary contribute/keep decision.",0.3,N/A,There is no endowment allocation between public/private accounts in this theoretical repeated game; concept does not apply.,1,N/R,"No public-goods marginal per capita return is defined; payoffs are given via benefits b(a), probabilities q, and costs k, c, π.",1,N/A,No human participants; communication protocols among experimental subjects are not applicable.,1,0,"The model specifies public observations of states, actions, and realized benefits each period, but not summary statistics; those are not part of experimental feedback design.",0.6,N/A,"There is no explicit punishment technology as in PGG-with-punishment; punishment occurs via exit decisions, not via targeted sanctioning with identities.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism analogous to peer rewards with identities.,1,0,Infinite-horizon repeated game; no notion of showing remaining rounds to participants.,1,1,"Inefficient punishment is modeled via player 1’s exit decision, which imposes future payoff losses; this plays the role of a punishment mechanism.",0.9,N/R,"Punishment is not parametrized as tokens-per-unit; its cost is endogenous in continuation payoffs from exit and lost surplus, not a simple scalar parameter.",0.5,N/R,There is no linear mapping from punishment tokens to reductions in others’ payoffs; punishment is via exit altering continuation values.,0.5,0,No separate costly rewarding institution is modeled beyond standard equilibrium payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,The model does not define per-period endowments in tokens as in standard public-good games; players receive flow utilities from stay/exit and productive actions.,1,N/R,"No empirical contributions are measured; the model analyzes equilibrium strategies (e.g., probabilities of taking productive actions) but does not report normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,"There are no token contributions as outcome variables; only theoretical payoffs and value functions V1, V2.",1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (inefficient punishment, Pareto-efficient equilibria) but no scalar efficiency index is reported.",1,N/R,Equilibrium values V1 and V2 are characterized symbolically; no single numeric group payoff is reported.,1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographics not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographics not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographics not applicable.,1,No human,The work is purely analytical with no empirical implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon discounted repeated game with two players. Each period, player 1 decides to stay or exit; staying yields cost k to player 1 and benefit π to player 2. Then a random subset A_t of actions is available to player 2; a finite subset N of actions is productive (cost c, benefit b(a) to player 1 with success probability q) while other actions are costless and yield no benefit. Asymmetric information: player 2 knows which actions are productive; player 1 does not but observes A_t, chosen action a_t, and realized benefit. The paper studies Pareto-efficient perfect Bayesian equilibria, patterns of exit as punishment, learning which actions are productive, and the emergence of routines where only confirmed productive actions are used.",Summary of the formal game specification in Sections I–III.,1
