ct as informal punishment, there is no dedicated punishment stage.",1,N/A,No punishment stage or points-to-punishment conversion.,1,N/A,No punishment technology defined.,1,0,No mechanism for costly rewarding beyond ordinary contribution choice.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,15,"Each period, each subject decides how to divide 15 tokens.",1,N/R,Paper gives averages in tokens but not normalized as explicit fraction of endowment by treatment.,0.3,4.42,"Result 4: 'average contribution levels for each treatment are 4.42, 4.84, and 4.33 for periods 6–20' with 4.42 for Free Entry/Exit.",1,N/R,"Earnings by treatment and group size are described (e.g., regression, figures) but no explicit efficiency ratio relative to maximum.",0.2,N/R,"Some per-person earnings by group size (e.g., 56 vs 45 ECUs) are given, but no single aggregate payoff for this treatment is reported.",0.2,USA,"Subjects were students at Florida State University (in Florida, USA).",0.95,N/R,Only that subjects were mostly recruited from economics courses; no age statistics reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition statistics reported.,0.1,Mostly university students in economics courses,Subjects recruited mostly from economics courses at various levels at Florida State University.,0.9,On site,"Conducted in a physical computer lab using zTree, not online.",1,"Public-goods (VCM) variant with interior dominant strategy; 12 potential groups (A–L); endogenous group sizes 1–12; payoff π_i = 0.5(15−x_i) + 1.5(x_i + Σ x_j) − (1/27)x_i^3; preliminary 3 rounds with n=1 followed by 20 main periods with endogenous group formation; in this treatment, any subject may freely choose any group each period without voting; information screen shows for each group: average contribution over past 5 periods, group size, and number who chose to remain.",Summary of the game structure and this treatment’s specific group formation rule drawn from Section 2.1 and descriptions of information screens.,1
10.1111_j.1467-9779.2008.00357.x.md,"Treatment 2 – Restricted Entry, Free Exit","Paper’s Table 2 lists Treatment 2 as 'Restricted Entry, Free Exit'.",1,Group formation rule: restricted entry (majority vote required to join a group) and free exit.,"Section 2.1: in the Restricted Entry treatment, exit is unrestricted, but entry into a new group requires approval by a majority of members who chose not to move.",1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment; this is a treatment subset (48 subjects).,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatment manipulation of group formation rules.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab at FSU using zTree.,1,False,No computer-only simulations.,1,False,"Study reports experimental results, not purely analytical derivations as main method.",1,12,Each session has 12 subjects; Table 2 shows 4 sessions for this treatment for 48 subjects total.,1,20,Same game structure across treatments: 3 preliminary solo rounds then 20 main periods with endogenous groups.,1,1,Choice of how many of 15 tokens to contribute; any integer 0–15 feasible.,1,0,All 15 tokens initially at subject’s disposal in private account; contributions are active decisions.,1,1.5,Payoff function identical across treatments; each token in group account yields 1.5 ECUs to every member.,1,0,No communication or chat reported; only numerical information about groups and applicants is shown.,0.9,1,"Before moving, subjects see for each group the average contribution levels over previous 5 periods, group sizes, and number who stayed.",1,0,"No explicit punishment; only voting on entry; identities of individual contributors per period are not reported, only averages and applicant’s contribution history.",1,0,No explicit reward mechanism to identify; contributions and admission decisions are not framed as rewards.,1,1,Game is described as 'known finite repetitions'; consistent with subjects being informed of total number of periods.,0.8,0,"Although the paper interprets denied entry as a form of punishment, there is no dedicated costly punishment stage; by schema definition this is not a punishment technology.",0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment stage with token-to-punishment conversion.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No formal rewarding technology beyond normal payoffs from contributions.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,15,Same 15 token endowment per period for every subject as described in design section.,1,N/R,No normalized contribution/ endowment ratio by treatment is directly reported.,0.3,4.84,"Result 4 reports average contributions of 4.42, 4.84, and 4.33 for FEE, RE, and RX, respectively; thus 4.84 for Restricted Entry.",1,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (earnings vs group size) but not as an explicit fraction of maximum for this treatment.,0.2,N/R,"Per-person earnings by group size (e.g., 56 ECUs in size-7 RE groups) are provided, but not a single mean payoff aggregated for this entire treatment.",0.3,USA,"Same subject pool: Florida State University students in Tallahassee, Florida, USA.",0.95,N/R,No direct age data; only that they were mostly students.,0.1,N/R,No gender data reported.,0.1,Mostly university students in economics courses,Recruitment description applies uniformly across treatments.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical lab with zTree at FSU.,1,"Same nonlinear VCM game as other treatments with dominant-strategy contribution of 3 tokens and group-optimal contributions depending on N (Table 1). Endogenous group sizes from 1 to 12. In this treatment, movement into groups requires majority approval among incumbent members who chose not to move; exit is free. Voters see the applicant’s contribution history over the prior 5 rounds and the group’s contribution history. Votes require more than 50% 'yes' for success. Applications to join an empty group do not require a vote.","Summarizes the common stage game and the specific restricted-entry, free-exit institution described in Section 2.1 and later results.",1
10.1111_j.1467-9779.2008.00357.x.md,"Treatment 3 – Free Entry, Restricted Exit","Table 2 defines Treatment 3 as 'Free Entry, Restricted Exit'.",1,Group formation rule: free entry (no vote required to join a group) and restricted exit (majority vote required to leave a group).,"Section 2.1: in the 'Restricted Exit' treatment, entry is unrestricted but exit requires majority approval by members of the group from which the subject wants to depart.",1,True,Human-subject lab experiment; Treatment 3 is one of three between-subject treatments.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment manipulating the exit rule.,1,True,Sessions run in a computer lab at FSU with zTree.,1,False,No purely computational simulations are reported.,1,False,Study does not present formal analytic model as primary method; focuses on experiment.,1,12,All sessions use 12 subjects; Table 2: 4 sessions * 12 = 48 in Treatment 3.,1,20,Same number of main periods across treatments; 20 periods with endogenous groups after 3 preliminary periods.,1,1,Subjects choose integer token amount from 0 to 15 to invest in group account.,1,0,All 15 tokens start with subject; no automatic public allocation.,1,1.5,Payoff structure identical across all three treatments.,1,0,No communication channel beyond numeric history displays is mentioned.,0.9,1,"Same group-history screens with average contributions, sizes, and numbers staying are used in all treatments.",1,0,"No explicit punishment technology; only votes on exit and general contribution histories are visible, not labeled as punishment identities.",1,0,No rewarding technology exists in the design.,1,1,Repeated game described as known finite; consistent with participants being told number of rounds.,0.8,0,"Although denial of exit can be interpreted as punishment, there is no explicit point-burning punishment mechanism.",0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment stage.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward technology described.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,15,Same 15-token endowment per round as in other treatments.,1,N/R,Only raw contribution averages in tokens by treatment are reported.,0.3,4.33,"Result 4: average contributions for periods 6–20 are 4.42 (FEE), 4.84 (RE), 4.33 (RX).",1,N/R,Efficiency not given explicitly as payoff relative to maximum for this treatment.,0.2,N/R,"Graphical and regression results mention average earnings over time and by group size, but no single treatment-average payoff is given numerically.",0.3,USA,"Same recruitment site: Florida State University, U.S.A.",0.95,N/R,No age distribution reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender distribution reported.,0.1,Mostly university students in economics courses,"Subjects recruited mostly from economics courses at various levels, same across treatments.",0.9,On site,Sessions took place in a physical computer lab.,1,"Same nonlinear VCM with interior dominant contribution of 3 tokens. Endogenous group sizes 1–12. In this treatment, subjects can freely enter any group (no vote) but attempting to leave a group requires majority 'yes' votes from members (who chose not to move) of the current group; more than 50% 'yes' is required. Subjects can always move back to their previous-period group without a vote; exiting a singleton group requires no vote. Restricted exit creates stickiness in group membership and leads to declining contributions over time.","Summarizes common game plus the free-entry, restricted-exit rule from Section 2.1 and Result 6–7 discussion.",1
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00028.x.md,Individual contribution baseline – Stage 2 (Periods 11–20),Stage 2 of the individual-contribution-baseline treatment is the benchmark condition without voting or punishment.,1,Institutional rule in Stage 2: individual contribution game without voting or punishment,Treatment varies only in institutional rules in Stage 2; here the rule is to continue the standard public good game (operational game) with no voting or punishment.,1,True,Human subjects (students) played the game in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with randomized assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in a university laboratory using computers.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results come from human play.,1,False,"Payoff functions are described but main results are experimental, not closed-form analytical derivations.",1,5,“number of group members n=5” and each group consisted of five subjects.,1,20,Each treatment consists of two 10-period stages; Stage 1 and Stage 2 both played by the same subjects (partner design).,1,1,"Each individual chooses xi with 0 ≤ xi ≤ E, implying any amount between 0 and full endowment is allowed.",1,0,Endowment E=10 tokens is initially held privately and then allocated between private and public; nothing pre-assigned to public account.,1,0.4,Marginal return a=0.4 and group size n=5; MPCR is defined as a in the linear public good payoff πi^0=E−xi+aX.,1,0,No communication opportunities are mentioned; only contribution decisions are made.,0.9,1,"Subjects see earnings and, in punishment treatments, punishment; standard public good interfaces show group outcomes. Text: examples of losses from punishment and average group payoffs reported, implying feedback.",0.7,0,Punishment stage not available in this treatment; identities therefore not shown.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,0,Subjects are unaware that there will be an additional ten periods after Period 10; horizon length is not disp