10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00028.x.md,Binding vote – Stage 2 (Periods 11–20),Stage 2 of the binding-vote treatment where a majority proposal is implemented and replaces individual choice in most periods.,1,Institutional rule in Stage 2: binding majority vote over contribution proposals; operational game only if no majority proposal,"In Stage 2, subjects propose and then vote; any proposal with ≥3 votes is imposed on all; if no majority, they play the standard operational game.",1,True,Uses human participants in laboratory sessions.,1,True,Controlled variation of voting institution relative to baseline.,1,True,Computerized experiment at a university lab.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,"Main results are experimental, though some theory is discussed.",1,5,Groups of five subjects with n=5.,1,20,Two stages of 10 periods each with same subjects (partner design).,1,1,"When operational game is played, xi can range from 0 to E; when binding proposal is implemented, contribution is set by vote but underlying action space is continuous.",0.9,0,Endowment initially private; contributions assigned either individually or by imposed rule but no automatic allocation to public account.,1,0.4,"Same linear public-good parameters: a=0.4, n=5.",1,0,Subjects interact only via proposals and anonymous voting screen; no free-form chat or face-to-face communication.,0.9,1,Voting screen lists proposals and results; contribution/earnings feedback implied as in other treatments.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No rewards used.,1,0,Subjects did not know there would be periods 11–20 when starting; horizon not shown.,0.9,0,Binding-vote treatment has no punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,Rewards never introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,E=10 tokens per period.,1,0.95,Text: groups initially contributed about 90% and reached 100% in final three periods; Stage-2 average thus around 95% of endowment.,0.6,N/R,No single numeric average contribution (in tokens) for Stage 2 in this treatment is reported.,0,N/R,Figures show relative efficiency curves but not numeric efficiency by treatment.,0,N/R,Average payoffs for binding vote are depicted graphically only.,0,United States,Same subject pool as other treatments: St. Lawrence University students.,1,N/R,No ages reported.,0,N/R,No gender information provided.,0,"University students (undergraduate, unspecified field)",Described as students; no further detail.,0.8,On site,Laboratory sessions at the university using computers.,1,"Stage 1: standard linear public good. Stage 2: each period begins with proposal stage, then anonymous vote. If any proposal gets ≥3 votes, that contribution level is imposed; otherwise they play the standard individual contribution game. Groups fixed (partner design).",From binding-vote treatment description.,1
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00028.x.md,Nonbinding vote – Stage 2 (Periods 11–20),Stage 2 of the nonbinding-vote treatment where majority proposals are announced but not enforced.,1,Institutional rule in Stage 2: nonbinding majority vote over contribution proposals; operational game played after vote; no punishment,Same voting framework as binding vote but majority plan is not imposed; subjects observe result then choose contributions individually; no punishment stage.,1,True,Uses human participants in a computerized lab setting.,1,True,Random assignment to institutional treatments in a controlled experiment.,1,True,Run in a university computer lab.,1,False,"Results from human play, not simulated agents.",1,False,Main analysis is empirical.,1,5,Each group consisted of five subjects (n=5).,1,20,Two 10-period stages per treatment.,1,1,"In the operational game after the vote, participants choose xi with 0 ≤ xi ≤ E.",1,0,"Endowment starts fully in private account, then subjects decide how much to contribute.",1,0.4,Same linear public good parameters across all treatments.,1,0,Interaction is restricted to proposals/votes and standard feedback; no general communication channel.,0.9,1,"Subjects observe voting results and, given standard design, see contribution/payoff feedback; Table 4 uses averages conditioned on majority proposals, implying such information was available.",0.8,0,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,Subjects unaware of Stage 2 at start; horizon not announced.,0.9,0,Explicitly described as nonbinding vote treatment without punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,"Paper mentions only punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,E=10 tokens.,1,0.37,"Stage 2: After initial increase, contributions fell to 35% within three periods and to 23% by the end (period 20). Simple midpoint approximation between early (≈0.5–0.45) and late (0.23) contributions gives ~0.37.",0.4,N/R,