10.1111_j.1465-7295.2007.00028.x.md,Nonbinding vote with punishment – Stage 2 (Periods 11–20),Stage 2 of the nonbinding-vote-with-punishment treatment where subjects vote nonbindingly and can punish cheaters.,1,Institutional rule in Stage 2: nonbinding majority vote over contribution proposals plus costly peer punishment of members who do not adhere to a majority proposal,"Stage 2: proposals and majority vote as in nonbinding vote; if a majority proposal exists, any member deviating from it can be punished by others; if no majority, no punishment is possible.",1,True,Human subjects participated under this treatment.,1,True,Institutional features experimentally manipulated.,1,True,Conducted with university students in a computerized lab.,1,False,No purely computational agents.,1,False,"Results come from lab behavior, not just analytical derivation.",1,5,Groups of five subjects; n=5.,1,20,Two 10-period stages; same subjects throughout.,1,1,"In operational game after vote, each subject chooses contribution xi with 0 ≤ xi ≤ E.",1,0,"Endowment starts privately, then contributions chosen.",1,0.4,"Public good parameters unchanged: a=0.4, n=5.",1,0,"Interaction limited to proposals, votes, and punishment choices; no free-form chat channel.",0.9,1,"Subjects observe majority proposal, actual contributions (since they must identify cheaters), and payoffs; punishment levels and associated costs must be displayed for implementation.",0.9,0,Paper does not state that identities are revealed; only “group member i punishes j” in payoff notation; standard Fehr-Gächter style is anonymous within group regarding identities in reported interfaces.,0.5,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,0,Subjects were unaware of Stage 2 at the start of Stage 1; no indication horizons were displayed later.,0.9,1,“This treatment introduces a punishment opportunity–group members can now punish others in the group who do not adhere to a majority voting outcome.”,1,0.5,"From Table 1: costs f(c) for punishment levels 0–10 are {0,1,2,4,6,9,12,16,20,25,30}. On average this is ~3×c; marginal cost per punishment point ≈3 tokens; but tech definition asks: cost per coin for punisher relative to impact on punished. Here 1 punishment point reduces cheater earnings by 10% (≈1 token when full contribution) at cost f(1)=1; mapping to model: 1 ‘coin’ spent ≈1 token, and 1 point of punishment costs between 1 and 3 tokens depending on level; no single linear rate, so approximate average marginal cost across 1–10 is 3 tokens. However schema expects a single number; we approximate cost per ‘unit’ as 3.",0.3,0.1,Each punishment point reduces punished subject’s income by 10% for c_i ≤ 10; thus 1 unit of punishment causes a 0.1 (10%) reduction in income.,0.9,0,No mention of positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,E=10 tokens per subject per period.,1,0.8,Text: “Contributions in the nonbinding-vote-with-punishment treatment initially reached about 80% of group endowments and remained at or above this level throughout Stage 2.” So average contribution rate in Stage 2 ≈0.8.,0.9,N/R,"Although Table 4 gives average aggregate contributions conditional on majority proposals (48.6 proposed vs 44.4 actual), there is no single unconditional average individual contribution amount for Stage 2 reported.",0.2,N/R,Efficiency curves shown in Figures 2–4; statements like “efficiency above 80%” but no exact numeric average for this treatment alone.,0.2,N/R,"Average group payoff values are only shown graphically, not numerically per treatment.",0,United States,Same subject pool: students from St. Lawrence University in the USA.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0,"University students (undergraduate, unspecified field)",Described simply as students; likely undergraduates.,0.8,On site,Laboratory experiment using computers and z-Tree-style software.,1,"Stage 2 sequence: (1) proposal stage; (2) majority vote; (3) operational public good game with individual contributions; (4) punishment stage P if and only if there was a majority proposal. Punishment: each punisher i chooses integer c_{i,j}∈[0,10] for each cheater j; each point costs f(c_{i,j}) to the punisher (see Table 1) and reduces cheater’s income by 10% per point up to 100%. Groups fixed. Average majority proposal ≈48.6; average actual contribution ≈41.2; average number of cheaters in Stage 2 ≈0.54; average number of punishers ≈2.84 when cheating occurred.","From nonbinding-vote-with-punishment description, theoretical section, and Table 1 & 4.",1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2007.04.001.md,Third round – In-group cheater condition,Design section defines two between-subjects conditions: in-group cheater vs. out-group cheater. This row covers the in-group cheater condition in the third (observer) round.,1,"Cheater group membership = In-group cheater condition; within-subject factor = six trader targets with contributions 50, 180, 200 (in-group) and 120, 160, 200 (out-group)","Design: 2×6 with cheater group (in-group vs out-group) as between-subjects factor and six trader targets as within-subjects factor. Here, cheater (50 yen) is in the in-group.",1,True,Human undergraduates participated in a computerized laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of cheater group membership with random assignment and specified payoffs.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory complex with individual computer rooms at Hokkaido University.,1,False,All decisions made by human participants; other players were simulated but the study itself is not a non-human simulation study.,1,False,No formal mathematical or analytical model; purely experimental.,1,8,Game is played among eight actors divided into two groups of four.,1,3,"Description of first, second, and third rounds; participants are told third is last round.",1,1,"Traders ""decide how much"" of 200 yen to give, implying any amount from 0 to 200 is allowed (not only all-or-nothing).",0.9,0,Each trader is individually endowed with 200 yen that they may give; all endowment initially in private account.,1,2,Gift is doubled by the experimenter (multiplier 2) and benefits only one recipient; effectively a 2× return to the recipient relative to giver’s cost.,0.7,0,No mention of any communication or chat; participants isolated in separate rooms.,0.95,0,Observers see each trader’s contribution amounts but no mention of aggregate summaries of others’ earnings or punishments.,0.8,0,Traders are not informed during the game whether they were punished or by whom; no indication that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,0,Participants are told there will be several rounds but not exactly how many until informed that the third is last; total number not displayed upfront.,0.9,1,Observers can spend money to reduce others’ payoffs in the third round.,1,1,"Observers ""spend"" yen from their own income; three times as much as they pay is subtracted from target’s earnings, implying cost per punishment unit is 1.",1,3,Three times as much money as the participant pays is subtracted from the punished trader’s earnings.,1,0,No mechanism for increasing others’ payoffs beyond the gift-giving itself; only punishment described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,200,Traders are endowed with 200 yen each in each round of gift-giving game.,1,0.735,Average second-round contribution was 147.02 yen out of 200 (73.5%); used as normalized fraction of endowment in focal cooperative classification round.,0.9,147.02,Reported mean contribution in the second round.,1,N/R,No explicit or computable report of realized total group payoffs vs. maximum possible efficiency across rounds.,0,N/R,"Paper reports individual averages and punishment costs, not total group payoff figures.",0,Japan,Participants were undergraduate students at Hokkaido University in Japan.,1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age statistics provided.,0,"52 males, 38 females (3 males excluded from analyses)",Participants section reports gender composition and exclusions.,1,Undergraduate university students,Participants recruited from undergraduate subject pool at Hokkaido University.,1,On site,Conducted in physical laboratory rooms with individual computers on campus.,1,"One-shot third-party punishment decision occurs in round 3; observers know individual contributions of six traders (three in-group, three out-group). Only one real participant per putative 8-person session; other players are simulated. Third-party punishment cost–benefit ratio is 1:3. In this condition, the least cooperative trader (50 yen) is an in-group member. Observers could spend up to 200 yen to punish each trader; traders are unaware of punishment until experiment end, so punishment cannot change future behavior or reputation.",Summarizes structural details of the third-round third-party punishment game specific to in-group cheater condition from Procedure and Design sections.,1
