10.1086_682418.md,Dictator Game – Variant 1 (Stranger & Producer-Organization Member),Paper describes two DG variants; this variant has recipients as a stranger and a member of the producer organization for each participant.,1,Recipient identity: stranger vs member of producer organization (within-subject); assignment to this DG variant vs village variant (between-subject).,"Design randomly assigns half of participants to this variant, where each decides once for a stranger and once for a comember.",1,True,Lab-in-the-field game with Ugandan farmers.,1,True,Random assignment to DG variants; controlled payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted as behavioral games in controlled sessions on site (“lab-in-the-field”).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described for DG.,1,False,No formal analytical DG model solved; focus is empirical measurement.,1,2,Dictator game involves one decider and one receiver.,1,2,"Each participant allocates two separate 10-MU endowments, one to each recipient type.",0.9,1,Standard DG allows any split of the endowment; no restriction to all-or-nothing mentioned.,0.9,0,Endowment is fully under dictator’s control at start; nothing pre-allocated to recipient.,1,N/R,DG is not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,DG conducted under anonymity; no mention of communication between players.,1,0,No feedback about others’ allocations or earnings described.,1,0,No punishment stage in DG.,1,0,Only dictator’s transfer; no separate reward identity revelation.,1,1,Script is structured; participants told about two allocation decisions; typical in lab-in-the-field protocols though not verbatim stated.,0.7,0,DG has no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism; only main transfer decision.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology beyond giving itself.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,10,Each endowment was 10 coins of 100 Ugandan shillings (10 MUs).,1,N/R,"Paper reports average MU amounts given (e.g., ~30% of 10 MUs) but not the exact mean per condition as a normalized fraction.",0.5,N/R,"Figure 3 and text give differences (e.g., 1.12 MUs more to comembers vs strangers) but not the absolute mean amounts by condition.",0.4,N/R,No total surplus or maximum surplus reported for DG allocations.,1,N/R,Aggregate or mean payoffs for dictator+recipient are not provided.,1,Uganda,Farmers are members of Ugandan producer organizations; study is in rural Uganda.,1,N/R,Age used as covariate (units of 10) but no summary ages reported.,0.5,Mixed (male and female); exact proportions N/R,Models control for male indicator; sample composition not given.,0.7,Varied; measured and standardized; distribution N/R,Education (std.) appears as covariate; no level distribution described.,0.7,Field experiment,Explicitly described as lab-in-the-field with farmers in their natural setting.,1,Within-subject DG: each council member decides on two 10-MU endowments; one recipient is a stranger (measure of generalized altruism) and one is a member of their producer organization (measure of group solidarity). Recipients’ specific identities remain unknown. Order of decisions (stranger first vs comember first) is randomized.,Direct description in section on DG variants and footnotes.,1
10.1086_682418.md,Dictator Game – Variant 2 (Stranger & Covillager),Second DG variant where the in-group recipient is a villager rather than a producer-organization member.,1,Recipient identity: stranger vs covillager (within-subject); assignment to this DG variant vs producer-organization variant (between-subject).,Design randomly assigns half of participants to variant where second recipient is someone from their village.,1,True,Same lab-in-the-field DG with human farmers.,1,True,Random assignment to DG variants and controlled payoffs.,1,True,Behavioral game sessions run in the field but under lab-like protocol.,1,False,No computational model.,1,False,No closed-form DG analysis; focus is empirical.,1,2,Dictator and recipient.,1,2,Two allocation decisions per participant: stranger and covillager.,0.9,1,No restriction to all-or-nothing; standard DG.,0.9,0,Entire 10-MU endowment initially with dictator.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,Played under anonymity; no communication mentioned.,1,0,No outcome feedback about others.,1,0,No punishment option.,1,0,No separate reward identity stage.,1,1,Participants informed they will allocate two endowments; implied in scripted protocol.,0.7,0,No punishment possibility.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No separate costly reward stage besides DG allocation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,10,Each endowment was 10 MUs of 100 Ugandan shillings.,1,N/R,Paper gives average differences (0.48 MU more to covillager vs stranger) but not exact normalized means by condition.,0.5,N/R,Only marginal differences reported; no absolute mean per condition.,0.4,N/R,No efficiency ratios computed.,1,N/R,No payoff totals reported.,1,Uganda,Same farmer council population.,1,N/R,Age appears as regressor but descriptive stats missing.,0.5,Mixed (male and female); exact proportions N/R,Male dummy in models; composition unspecified.,0.7,Varied; measured and standardized; distribution N/R,Education used as standardized predictor.,0.7,Field experiment,Lab-in-the-field DGs with farmer group members.,1,"Within-subject DG: each council member allocates two 10-MU endowments, one to a stranger and one to a covillager. This variant is used to separate effects of social proximity (village membership) from group attachment to the producer organization. Order of decisions is randomized across participants.",Summarized from DG section and notes.,1
10.1086_682418.md,"Public Goods Game – Baseline (No Communication, No Sanctioning)",One of three PGG variants; baseline has repeated PGG with no communication or punishment.,1,Institutional environment: baseline PGG without communication or sanctioning; comparison to communication and sanctioning variants (between-subject).,"Participants are randomly assigned at DC-level sessions to baseline, communication, or monitor/sanctioning condition.",1,True,Human farmers played the PGG in field sessions.,1,True,"Random assignment to variants, controlled payoffs, repeated rounds.",1,True,Described as lab-in-the-field behavioral experiment.,1,False,No PGG simulations reported.,1,False,No analytical solution; results entirely empirical.,1,N/R,PGG group size per session is not explicitly stated; only that all farmer group members participated and subjects assigned to one of three variants.,0.3,6,“All farmer group members participated in six rounds of a PGG.”,1,1,Participants decide how much (0–10 MUs) to keep vs contribute; standard continuous contribution PGG.,1,0,Each round they receive 10 MUs to allocate; no default in public account.,1,0.5,"Contributions to public account are doubled (multiplier 2) and redistributed evenly among all group members. MPCR = 2 / group size. Group size of 4 is typical but not reported; however, because MPCR depends on true group size and this is not given, this numeric value is uncertain.",0.2,0,"In baseline, no interaction or communication allowed; only decisions each round.",1,0,Protocol description does not mention feedback on others’ contributions beyond standard payoff realization; summaries not described.,0.5,0,No punishment stage in baseline.,1,0,No peer reward mechanism; only public good returns.,1,1,Standard repeated-game protocols and reference to “six rounds” suggest total rounds known; explicit mention absent.,0.7,0,No sanctioning option described for baseline PGG.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,No costly reward technology implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"In each round, they were endowed with 10 MUs.",1,N/R,Text: baseline contributions decline by almost 10%; no exact mean contributions per round reported to compute normalized rate.,0.4,N/R,Figure 4 shows trends; paper states initial contributions 45–50% of endowment but not precise averages in MUs for baseline across rounds 3–6.,0.4,N/R,Total and maximum possible earnings per group not reported.,1,N/R,No payoff totals given by condition.,1,Uganda,Same DC council sample in Ugandan producer organizations.,1,N/R,Age used as covariate; no descriptive statistics.,0.5,Mixed (male and female); exact proportions N/R,Male indicator used; composition unspecified.,0.7,Varied; measured and standardized; distribution N/R,Education is included as standardized predictor.,0.7,Field experiment,PGG run as lab-in-the-field game with farmers.,1,"Baseline treatment consists of six rounds of a linear PGG. In each round, each participant receives 10 MUs, chooses how much to keep vs contribute to a common pot. The pot is doubled and evenly redistributed among group members regardless of their contributions. There is no communication or punishment at any point in this baseline condition.",Summarized from PGG design description.,1
10.1086_682418.md,Public Goods Game – Communication Variant,PGG variant where players can communicate for 3 minutes after round 2 and after subsequent rounds.,1,Institutional environment: PGG with periodic public communication (face-to-face discussion) after round 2.,"Described as second PGG condition where, after two preliminary rounds, players are allowed to publicly discuss their strategy for three minutes at end of every successive round.",1,True,Human subjects in lab-in-the-field setting.,1,True,"Random assignment to communication vs baseline and sanctioning, controlled environment.",1,True,Run as structured behavioral game in natural field setting.,1,False,No simulation for this variant.,1,False,No analytical solution presented.,1,N/R,Size of each PGG group not numerically reported.,0.3,6,"Same six rounds as baseline, with first two as preliminary rounds before communication is introduced.",1,1,Continuous contributions 0–10 MUs each round.,1,0,Endowment starts entirely private.,1,0.5,Same payoff structure as baseline (contributions doubled and shared equally). True MPCR depends on unreported group size; numeric value thus uncertain.,0.2,1,Players are allowed to publicly discuss their strategy for 3 minutes after round 2 and after each subsequent round.,1,0,"Description mentions communication, not quantitative feedback displays like summaries.",0.5,0,No punishment mechanism in communication variant.,1,0,No costly rewards.,1,1,Six rounds stated; typical to inform subjects; not explicitly stated but implied.,0.7,0,Only communication is added; no sanctioning option.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,Same PGG structure: 10 MUs each round.,1,N/R,Only average treatment effect reported: contributions 1.91 MUs higher than baseline; no full means for normalization.,0.4,N/R,Mean MU contributions per round or across rounds not tabulated.,0.4,N/R,No group-payoff relative to maximum reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate earnings reported.,1,Uganda,Same Ugandan producer-organization councils.,1,N/R,Age included in regression; summaries missing.,0.5,Mixed (male and female); exact proportions N/R,Male dummy appears; composition unreported.,0.7,Varied; measured and standardized; distribution N/R,Education standardized in models.,0.7,Field experiment,Lab-in-the-field PGG with added communication stage.,1,"Same six-round PGG as baseline. After two preliminary rounds without communication, players can publicly discuss their strategies for 3 minutes at the end of each subsequent round (rounds 3–6). Communication is face-to-face, non-binding, and group-level. This treatment is used to study reciprocity based on verbal commitment.",Directly summarized from communication variant description.,1
10.1086_682418.md,Public Goods Game – Centralized Monitor Sanctioning Variant,Third PGG variant with an elected monitor who can sanction by spending MUs to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,"Institutional environment: PGG with centralized monitor endowed with costly sanctioning power, introduced after two preliminary rounds.",Described as condition where one participant is elected monitor after round 2 and can spend MUs to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,True,Human farmer participants in lab-in-the-field game.,1,True,Random assignment to sanctioning vs baseline/communication conditions; controlled variation of punishment institution.,1,True,Run as structured behavioral experiment in field setting.,1,False,No simulation model here.,1,False,No analytical derivations.,1,N/R,Exact group size not quantitatively reported.,0.3,6,"All PGG variants have six rounds, with first two as preliminary.",1,1,Continuous choice of 0–10 MUs each round.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely private.,1,0.5,Same public-good technology (doubling and equal redistribution) as baseline; true MPCR depends on unreported group size.,0.2,0,No communication opportunity is mentioned in the monitor condition; only monitoring and punishment added.,1,0,Description focuses on monitor’s sanctioning; no mention of aggregate summaries to players.,0.5,1,"Monitor is an elected participant; when the monitor punishes, targets must know who punished them for the centralized sanctioning mechanism to operate and be meaningful. Paper describes a centralized monitor rather than anonymous peer punishment, implying known identity.",0.6,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Six rounds indicated in protocol; typical for participants to be informed of total rounds.,0.7,1,Monitor can punish low contributors by spending MUs.,1,1,Monitor can spend 1 MU to take away 3 MUs from subjects whose contributions they disapprove.,1,3,Each 1 MU spent by monitor removes 3 MUs from target.,1,0,No positive sanction or reward described; monitor only subtracts payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same as other PGG variants: 10 MUs per round. Monitor also starts with 10 MUs but cannot contribute or receive public good payoff.,1,N/R,Paper reports that sanctioning condition contributions are on average 1.55 MUs higher than baseline; exact mean contributions not provided.,0.4,N/R,No table with mean contributions by condition and round.,0.4,N/R,No payoffs vs maximum values reported.,1,N/R,Group earnings not tabulated.,1,Uganda,Same Ugandan farmers.,1,N/R,Age as covariate; summary not given.,0.5,Mixed (male and female); exact proportions N/R,Both genders included (male dummy).,0.7,Varied; measured and standardized; distribution N/R,Education (std.) in models.,0.7,Field experiment,Lab-in-the-field sanctioning PGG with elected monitor.,1,"Same six-round PGG as baseline for regular players. After two preliminary rounds, one participant in the group is elected as a monitor. The monitor receives the same 10-MU endowment per round but cannot contribute to or benefit from the public good; instead, the monitor can spend 1 MU to reduce a target participant’s payoff by 3 MUs in that round. This centralized sanctioning institution is used to assess the effect of punishment threat on cooperation. An interviewer effect led to slightly higher initial contributions in this condition.",Summarized from detailed sanctioning variant description and footnote regarding interviewer effect.,1
10.1142_s0218202519500428.md,Numerical example – stable interior equilibrium (Fig. 1),"Condition defined by parameter set in Fig. 1 caption, where interior fixed point is stable and all three strategies coexist.",1,"Expected loss vs expected bribe with parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.1, b=0.8, q=1, p=0.8; regime (1-pq)B + pqh - pqb < 0",Independent variables are game and corruption parameters; this row uses the specific parameterization given for Fig. 1.,1,False,"Study uses replicator equations and numerical integration in infinite well-mixed populations, not human subjects.",1,False,No controlled behavioral or field experiment; only theoretical and numerical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or online participants.,1,True,Numerical examples integrate the deterministic replicator ODE system for given parameters.,1,True,Paper develops and analyzes closed-form replicator dynamics and equilibria.,1,5,Group size N=5 specified in Fig. 1 parameters.,1,1,Replicator dynamics assumes repeated random matching but the underlying PGG is a one-shot interaction per encounter; set as 1.,0.6,1,Model describes fixed contribution cost c for cooperators and punishers; defectors contribute nothing. This is effectively an all-or-nothing choice between c and 0.,0.9,N/A,No explicit per-round decision process for human players; infinite-population evolutionary model.,1,0.6,MPCR = r/N with r=3 and N=5 from parameters.,1,N/A,No human communication in replicator model.,1,N/A,No feedback interface to human players; only payoffs in equations.,1,N/A,No individual identities in infinite well-mixed replicator framework.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,Not a bounded-round human experiment.,1,1,Pool punishers pay G into punishment pool and defectors are fined B (possibly avoided via bribes).,1,0.5,Punishers contribute G=0.5 to the punishment pool each game; this is the cost parameter.,1,N/R,Model specifies a fine B per punisher but does not define a separate multiplier of fine per unit cost G.,0.3,0,Only punishment and bribery are modeled; no rewarding of cooperators.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Players incur fixed costs c, G, h, but no explicit initial endowment per round is given.",0,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as strategy frequencies over time, not contributions as fraction of endowment.",0,N/R,Only fixed contribution cost c is specified; no aggregate group contributions reported numerically for this condition.,0,N/R,Paper does not report total payoffs relative to maximum possible for this parameter set.,0,N/R,No explicit average or total group payoff values given in numerical example.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire study uses mathematical and numerical models only.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population public goods game with three strategies: cooperators contribute c=1, defectors contribute 0 and may bribe, pool punishers pay G=0.5 to a punishment pool and also contribute c=1; defectors bribing probability q=1, punishers accepting bribes with probability p=0.8, fine per punisher B=0.5, bribe amount b=0.8, bribe cost to defectors h=0.1.",Summarizes the defined game parameters and corruption mechanism for this numerical condition.,1
10.1142_s0218202519500428.md,Numerical example – Hamiltonian system with limit cycle (Fig. 2),Condition defined by Fig. 2 caption where expected loss equals expected bribe and dynamics show closed orbits.,1,"Expected loss equals expected bribe with parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.4, b=0.4, q=1, p=1; regime (1-pq)B + pqh - pqb = 0",Independent parameters specified for Fig. 2; equality of expected loss and bribe is core manipulated regime.,1,False,No human subjects; replicator ODEs numerically integrated.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,Purely theoretical and numerical.,1,True,Trajectories in the simplex are obtained by numerical solution of replicator equations.,1,True,Hamiltonian and constant of motion derived analytically.,1,5,N=5 in Fig. 2 parameters.,1,1,Underlying game is a one-shot PGG within an evolutionary repeated matching framework.,0.6,1,Binary choice: contribute fixed c or 0.,0.9,N/A,No explicit initial allocation decision by human players.,1,0.6,MPCR = r/N = 3/5.,1,N/A,No human communication modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,No agents with identities in a human sense.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,"Continuous-time replicator dynamics, no round display.",1,1,Pool punishment with cost G and fine B present.,1,0.5,G=0.5 in Fig. 2.,1,N/R,No ratio of fine to cost explicitly defined beyond parameters G and B.,0.3,0,No rewarding institution modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Initial per-round endowment not specified.,0,N/R,"Outcomes shown as frequencies, not normalized contribution rates.",0,N/R,No explicit aggregate contribution amounts reported.,0,N/R,No payoff efficiency measures calculated for this condition.,0,N/R,No numerical group payoff given.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Deterministic evolutionary model only.,1,"Infinite well-mixed PGG with strategies C, D, P; parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, b=0.4, h=0.4, p=1, q=1. All defectors bribe and all punishers accept bribes, leading to a Hamiltonian system with a neutrally stable interior fixed point surrounded by closed orbits.",Summarizes structure and corruption mechanism for this parameter regime.,1
10.1142_s0218202519500428.md,Numerical example – stable heteroclinic cycle (Fig. 3),Condition defined by Fig. 3 caption where interior equilibrium is a repeller and trajectories converge to boundary heteroclinic cycle.,1,"Expected loss larger than expected bribe with parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.4, b=0.4, q=0.8, p=1; regime (1-pq)B + pqh - pqb > 0 and additional inequalities for heteroclinic cycle",Specific parameterization and inequality regime are what define this dynamical condition.,1,False,"Numerical solution of replicator ODEs, no humans.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment or field study.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Heteroclinic behavior illustrated via numerical integration.,1,True,Existence and stability of heteroclinic cycle derived in Theorem 3.4.,1,5,N=5 from Fig. 3 parameters.,1,1,Underlying one-shot PGG interaction per encounter.,0.6,1,"As in model definition, strategies either contribute fixed c or 0.",0.9,N/A,No explicit human decision about default allocation.,1,0.6,MPCR = r/N = 3/5.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No feedback interface.,1,N/A,No identities in simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Continuous-time dynamics, no rounds UI.",1,1,Pool punishment as in base model.,1,0.5,G=0.5.,1,N/R,No explicit fine-per-cost multiplier specified.,0.3,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment not defined.,0,N/R,Only strategy frequencies and their oscillations reported.,0,N/R,No aggregate contribution numbers provided.,0,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically summarized for this regime.,0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Replicator dynamics only.,1,"Same three-strategy PGG with pool punishment and probabilistic corruption as base model. Parameters: N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.4, b=0.4, p=1, q=0.8. Expected loss to defectors exceeds expected bribe income to punishers, generating 