10.1142_s0218202519500428.md,Numerical example – global convergence to all defectors (Fig. 4),Condition defined by Fig. 4 where D is a global attractor.,1,"Expected loss larger than expected bribe with parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.4, b=0.2, q=1, p=1; regime (1-pq)B + pqh - pqb > 0 and rc/N - c - G + pq(N-1)b < 0","This parameter set shifts stability to all-D, as discussed in Sec. 4.4.",1,False,Deterministic evolutionary simulation only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No participants.,1,True,Phase portrait and time series generated by numerical integration.,1,True,Theorem 3.4 and related stability analysis characterize when D is globally stable.,1,5,N=5 in Fig. 4 parameters.,1,1,Single-shot PGG per encounter within evolutionary model.,0.6,1,Contribution choice is binary: pay c or not.,0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation step by human subjects.,1,0.6,r/N = 3/5.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,No player identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Not a round-based experiment.,1,1,Pool punishment present with cost G and fine B.,1,0.5,G=0.5.,1,N/R,No explicit mapping from cost G to fine magnitude beyond B.,0.3,0,No rewarding institution.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment per player not provided.,0,N/R,Dynamics reported are strategy frequencies; contributions follow directly from strategy but not summarized numerically.,0.1,N/R,No explicit aggregate contributions reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency statistics given.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not summarized numerically.,0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Mathematical/numerical study only.,1,"Three-strategy PGG with corruption as previously, parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.4, b=0.2, p=1, q=1. Here the condition rc/N - c - G + pq(N-1)b < 0 makes the all-defector state globally attracting; pool punishment and cooperation die out.",Captures the structural and parameter features leading to defection dominance.,1
10.1142_s0218202519500428.md,Numerical example – stable boundary equilibrium of defectors and punishers (Fig. 5),Condition defined by Fig. 5 where a stable fixed point on the D–P edge exists and C disappears.,1,"Interior equilibrium absent and boundary equilibrium stable with parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.1, b=0.8, q=1, p=1; regime with p q b > max{G/[(N-1)(1-α)], (1-pq)B + pq h} and no interior fixed point",Fig. 5 uses given parameter set and theoretical conditions for existence and stability of boundary equilibrium on D–P edge.,0.9,False,No human participants.,1,False,"This is a numerical illustration of the analytical model, not an experiment with subjects.",1,False,No lab or online environment.,1,True,Phase portrait and time series from numerical solution of replicator equations.,1,True,Sec. 3.2.2 derives conditions for stable boundary equilibria; Fig. 5 confirms numerically.,1,5,N=5 given in Fig. 5 parameters.,1,1,Each group interaction is a one-shot PGG.,0.6,1,Strategies choose between fixed contribution c and 0.,0.9,N/A,Not a human experiment with initial endowment allocation.,1,0.6,MPCR = r/N = 3/5.,1,N/A,No human communication modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No identities in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Not round-based for subjects.,1,1,Pool punishment present.,1,0.5,G=0.5.,1,N/R,No explicit mapping of G to fines besides B parameter.,0.3,0,No rewards in model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment not specified.,0,N/R,Only strategy frequencies are depicted; contribution rate could be inferred from z but not quantified in text.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate contribution values reported.,0,N/R,No payoff efficiency values given.,0,N/R,Group payoff not reported numerically.,0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/numerical work.,1,"Three-strategy PGG with probabilistic bribery and corruption as in the main model. Parameters: N=5, r=3, c=1, G=0.5, B=0.5, h=0.1, b=0.8, p=1, q=1. Here, cooperators die out and a stable mixture on the D–P edge emerges, with approximately 0.8 punishers and 0.2 defectors in the long run.",Summarizes structure and outcome composition for this boundary-equilibrium regime.,1
10.1257_aer.102.7.3317.md,No noise – No punishment,One of six treatments in the 3×2 design: no noise in monitoring and no punishment option.,1,Monitoring: no noise (perfect); Punishment: none,Treatment defined by crossing noise (no) with punishment regime (no punishment).,1,True,Human subjects participated in lab public good experiments.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments.,1,True,Conducted in the UNSW experimental laboratory with computers.,1,False,All results are from human-subject sessions; no agent-based simulations.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not formal analytical models.",1,3,Subjects matched into fixed groups of 3 for all 50 rounds.,1,50,They played a 50-round repeated public good game.,1,0,Subjects could choose to contribute all or none of their 20-token endowment; no intermediate amounts.,1,0,Endowment starts with each participant; they choose whether to move all 20 tokens to group account.,1,0.5,"If 20 tokens contributed, each of 3 gets 10 points: multiplier 1.5, so MPCR = 1.5/3 = 0.5.",1,0,No mention of communication; standard public good design without chat.,0.8,1,"After each round, participants were informed about choices and thus associated earnings; this implies summary of others’ outcomes.",0.7,0,No punishment option exists in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present in any treatment.,1,1,"Finite 50-round horizon explained in instructions; subjects typically see remaining rounds in such designs, though not explicitly stated.",0.6,0,Explicitly termed a no punishment treatment; no second-stage punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,0,Only punishment technologies are described; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each participant endowed with 20 tokens per round.,1,5.59,"Average raw contribution 5.59 tokens (Table 1) with max 20; normalized rate = 5.59/20 = 0.2795. But authors label column as 'Average contribution' without units; based on binary 0/20 choice, this value must already equal the normalized rate times 20. To avoid over-interpretation, we report the normalized fraction as 5.59/20 ≈ 0.2795 is not directly stated, so leave as N/R and keep 5.59 as amount. However schema requires rate numeric; set equal to 5.59/20 ≈ 0.2795.",0.6,5.59,Table 1: Average contribution = 5.59 tokens.,1,N/R,No direct or easily inferable ratio of actual to maximal possible payoffs reported by treatment.,0,22.8,"Table 1: 'Average net profits' 22.80 are per participant per round; group payoff would be 3×22.80, but not explicitly reported; we therefore store the per-person value.",0.7,Australia,"Subjects recruited from University of New South Wales (Sydney, Australia).",1,N/R,Only described as university student population; no age statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,Demographic survey mentioned but no gender breakdown reported.,0,University students,Recruited from the university student population.,1,On site,Sessions at physical experimental lab with participants at computer desks.,1,Linear public good; group of 3; binary contribution (0 or 20) each round; contributions multiplied by 1.5 and evenly shared; perfect monitoring of contribution choices; fixed groups; 50 rounds; earnings converted at AU$0.02 per point plus AU$5 show-up.,Summarizes detailed design description in Experimental Design section.,1
10.1257_aer.102.7.3317.md,No noise – Regular punishment,"Treatment with perfect monitoring and regular punishment technology (3:1, cap).",1,"Monitoring: no noise (perfect); Punishment: regular (3:1 damage, capped, max 5 points assigned to each of 2 others)",Defined by crossing no noise with regular punishment in 3×2 design.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment-based design.,1,True,Conducted in university experimental lab.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical model presented as a treatment.,1,3,Groups of 3 fixed over 50 rounds.,1,50,Repeated game lasted 50 rounds.,1,0,Binary choice: contribute all or none of 20 tokens.,1,0,Endowment initially private; subjects choose to contribute or not.,1,0.5,Same payoff mapping across all treatments.,1,0,No communication option mentioned.,0.8,1,Participants informed about others’ contribution decisions and thus resulting payoffs.,0.7,1,"Decisions/public records of the other two members displayed anonymously but in random order; punishment choices elicited on that same ordering so punishment can be targeted, implying the punisher knows which anonymous ID they punish.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Finite 50-round horizon known to participants.,0.6,1,Second-stage punishment available; can assign deduction points.,1,1,Each assigned deduction point costs punisher 1 point.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces punished member’s income by 3 points; capped at that round’s earnings.,1,0,Only punishment discussed.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment per round.,1,12.4,"Table 1: average contribution = 12.40 tokens. Normalized fraction would be 12.40/20 = 0.62; as above, unit labeling is ambiguous. We therefore compute 0.62 as the rate.",0.6,12.4,Average contribution 12.40 from Table 1.,1,N/R,No explicit treatment-level efficiency ratios reported.,0,23.66,Table 1 'Average net profits' per participant = 23.66; group total would be 3×23.66 but only per-person clearly reported.,0.7,Australia,UNSW student pool.,1,N/R,No age stats.,0.3,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0,University students,Recruited from university student population.,1,On site,Conducted in physical computer lab.,1,"Same binary linear public good; perfect monitoring of true contributions; after contribution stage, second-stage punishment where each subject can assign up to 5 deduction points to each of the other two members; each point costs 1 and reduces target’s income by 3, with punishment capped at that round’s earnings; negative round incomes not allowed under regular punishment since capped.",Summarizes regular punishment and monitoring details for this treatment.,1
10.1257_aer.102.7.3317.md,No noise – Strong punishment,"Treatment with perfect monitoring and strong punishment technology (6:1, uncapped).",1,"Monitoring: no noise (perfect); Punishment: strong (6:1 damage, uncapped, max 5 points assignable to each of 2 others)",Cross of no noise and strong punishment in 3×2 design.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Run in physical laboratory.,1,False,No mention of computer-only simulations.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,3,Groups of 3 fixed over all rounds.,1,50,50-round repeated game.,1,0,Binary 0 or 20 contribution choice.,1,0,Endowment initially private.,1,0.5,Same public good technology.,1,0,No communication channel described.,0.8,1,Participants informed about others’ choices and therefore earnings.,0.7,1,Anonymous but distinct IDs for the two others; punishment assigned per ID.,0.9,0,No reward stage.,1,1,Finite known 50-round horizon.,0.6,1,Strong punishment treatment includes second-stage punishment.,1,1,Each assigned reduction point decreases punisher’s payoff by one point.,1,6,Each deduction point reduces punished subject’s income by 6; negative round incomes allowed; no cap.,1,0,Only punishment technologies implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,20 tokens per participant per round.,1,17.61,Table 1: average contribution = 17.61 tokens. Normalized fraction would be 17.61/20 ≈ 0.8805; we compute this as the rate.,0.6,17.61,Average contribution 17.61 tokens (Table 1).,1,N/R,Net profits reported but no explicit efficiency ratio.,0,25.45,Table 1: average net profits per participant = 25.45; group total not explicitly given.,0.7,Australia,"UNSW lab, Sydney.",1,N/R,Only student status mentioned.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,University students,Sample from university student population.,1,On site,Physical lab sessions.,1,"Same public good with perfect monitoring; after contributions, subjects can assign up to 5 deduction points to each of 2 group members; each point costs 1 and reduces target by 6; no cap, so negative round incomes allowed, though total experiment income capped at zero.",Summarizes strong punishment and monitoring in this condition.,1
10.1257_aer.102.7.3317.md,Noise – No punishment,Treatment with noisy public monitoring and no punishment option.,1,Monitoring: noise (10% chance a contribution appears as no contribution); Punishment: none,"Noise dimension set to noise, punishment dimension to no punishment.",1,True,Human subjects in lab game.,1,True,Controlled treatment assignment.,1,True,Conducted at UNSW lab.,1,False,All data from experiments with human subjects.,1,False,Experimental rather than analytical.,1,3,Groups of 3 fixed.,1,50,Same 50-round design.,1,0,Contribute all or none of 20 tokens.,1,0,Endowment is private unless contributed.,1,0.5,Same mapping between contributions and payoffs in all treatments.,1,0,No communication allowed per description.,0.8,1,"Participants observe public records of each group member’s contribution decisions, from which payoffs follow deterministically.",0.7,0,No punishment in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite 50-round horizon announced.,0.6,0,No punishment treatment: round ends after information stage.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No rewards used.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,20 tokens each round.,1,4.04,Table 1: average contribution = 4.04 tokens. Normalized fraction would be 4.04/20 ≈ 0.202; we compute this as the rate.,0.6,4.04,"Average contribution 4.04 tokens (Table 1, Noise row for '—' then value 4.04).",1,N/R,Efficiency not reported explicitly.,0,22.02,"Table 1: under Noise heading, first row (no punishment) 'Average net profits' 22.02; interpreted as per participant per round.",0.7,Australia,Same subject pool.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,N/R,Not specified.,0,University students,Recruited from UNSW student population.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions.,1,"Same binary public good; imperfect public monitoring of contributions: if a group member contributes, public record shows 'no contribution' with 10% probability; if they do not contribute, the record always shows 'no contribution'; noise affects only information,