10.1257_aer.102.7.3317.md,Noise – Strong punishment,"Treatment with noisy public monitoring and strong (6:1, uncapped) punishment.",1,"Monitoring: noise (10% misrecording of contributions as no contribution); Punishment: strong (6:1 damage, uncapped, up to 5 points to each other)",Noise added to monitoring; strongest punishment severity.,1,True,Lab experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Part of controlled factorial design.,1,True,Conducted in physical lab.,1,False,No simulations in this treatment.,1,False,Not a theoretical model.,1,3,Three-person fixed groups.,1,50,Same repeated structure.,1,0,Binary contribution decision (0 or 20).,1,0,Endowment initially private.,1,0.5,Contribution technology unchanged.,1,0,No communication.,0.8,1,Subjects see public records of each member’s contributions.,0.7,1,Anonymous IDs visible across contribution and punishment stages.,0.9,0,No rewards implemented.,1,1,Finite 50-round horizon known.,0.6,1,Strong punishment option available.,1,1,Each reduction point costs punisher 1 point.,1,6,Each deduction point reduces target’s income by 6; uncapped; negative round incomes allowed.,1,0,No reward stage in any treatment.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,20-token endowment per round.,1,16.04,Table 1: average contribution = 16.04 tokens. Normalized fraction 16.04/20 ≈ 0.802; we compute this as rate.,0.6,16.04,Average contribution 16.04 tokens (Noise – Strong punishment row).,1,N/R,Net earnings reported but not scaled relative to maximum possible.,0,23.48,Table 1: average net profits per participant = 23.48 for Noise – Strong punishment.,0.7,Australia,Same UNSW population.,1,N/R,Not given.,0.3,N/R,Not given.,0,University students,Recruited from student pool.,1,On site,Sessions held at UNSW lab.,1,"Same binary public good with imperfect public monitoring (10% chance contributors’ public record shows 'no contribution'; noncontributors always show 'no contribution'); punishment stage with strong technology: up to 5 deduction points assignable to each other; each costs 1 and reduces target by 6; uncapped so negative round incomes allowed, though total experimental income capped at zero.",Combines noise and strong punishment details.,1
10.1073_pnas.1315273111.md,Block II – Institution without second-order punishment,Block II contains rounds with and without second-order punishment; this row captures the condition without second-order punishment pooled over treatments A and B as analyzed in Fig. 1 and related text.,0.9,"Institution type: central punishment without second-order punishment (defectors punished, tax evaders not punished)","Condition defined by whether the institution punishes only defectors or also tax evaders; here, only defectors are punished.",1,True,Human subjects played a public goods game in the lab.,1,True,Controlled randomized economic game with imposed institutional rules.,1,True,Conducted at University of Kiel laboratory setting.,1,False,"This row describes the human-subject experiment, not the mathematical model.",1,False,Analytical model exists but is separate; this entry is for the experimental condition.,1,5,Twenty-five groups of five subjects.,1,10,"Block II consists of 10 rounds (rounds 11–20). Within that block, each institution (with vs without 2OP) is implemented for 5 rounds but analyses refer to the pooled 10-round block; we record the block length.",0.7,1,Contributions are 0 or €0.50 to the pool; binary cooperate/defect decision treated as all-or-nothing contribution possibility.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely in private account; subjects decide whether to contribute €0.50.,0.9,0.62,Total contributions multiplied by 3.1 and redistributed among 5 participants: MPCR = 3.1 / 5 = 0.62.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard PGG lab design without chat.,0.8,0,Paper does not report feedback of others’ earnings or punishment details beyond necessary info; assume not shown.,0.4,0,Central authority punishes via fines; no indication that individual identities of punished players are revealed.,0.5,0,No rewards in design.,1,0,Not specified if total or remaining rounds are displayed.,0.3,1,"If at least one subject paid taxes, a central punishment institution was established that fined defectors.",1,0.05,Subjects fund a central pool rather than per-target punishment; per-unit cost of punishment is not defined; use base tax component as minimal per-round cost to enable punishment.,0.2,1,Fine for each defector (and in 2OP case tax evader) is €1.00 per offense; treating tech as 1 unit fine per offense per round.,0.7,0,"Design only includes punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0.9,"Players can choose to be loners with fixed payoff €0.40 or participate: if they contribute €0.50 and pay up to about €0.50 tax, their gross amount per round is roughly €0.90 at stake (0.40 alternative plus 0.50 contribution less tax). However, explicit per-round monetary endowment is not clearly specified; 0.90 is an approximate stake based on described amounts.",0.2,0.614,Fraction of cooperators (contributors) without second-order punishment in block II is 61.4%. Binary contribution decision so contribution rate equals that fraction.,1,N/R,"Average raw contribution amount in euros not reported, only fraction of cooperators.",0.1,N/R,"Average payoff per round is €0.63, but maximum possible payoff under full cooperation without taxes is not clearly given due to varying taxes and loner option, so efficiency ratio cannot be computed reliably.",0.2,3.15,Average individual payoff €0.63 per round without 2OP; group of 5 so average group payoff 0.63×5 = €3.15.,0.9,Germany,"Experiments conducted at University of Kiel, Germany, with local first-year biology students.",1,N/R,Only that they are first-year biology students; no age statistics given.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,First-year university biology students,Subjects recruited from a first-year course in biology.,1,On site,Standard lab experiment at a university; no indication of online implementation.,0.9,"Voluntary participation with loner option paying €0.40; if they participate, subjects decide whether to pay taxes to a central authority and whether to contribute €0.50 to a public good; total contributions multiplied by 3.1 and redistributed to all participants; if at least one tax payer, authority established and fines defectors €1.00 under this institution.",Summary of the public goods and punishment institution rules in this condition.,1
