10.1073_pnas.1315273111.md,Block III – Foot voting treatment – Institution without second-order punishment,"In treatment A (foot voting), during block III subjects can choose each round between communities with or without second-order punishment; this row captures observations for those playing under the institution without second-order punishment across all 15 rounds.",0.8,Voting mechanism: foot voting; chosen institution: central punishment without second-order punishment,Independent variables are voting rule (foot voting vs majority voting) and institution type (with vs without 2OP); here: foot voting and no 2OP.,1,True,Human subjects played a public goods game in the lab.,1,True,Controlled randomized economic game with endogenous institutional choice via foot voting.,1,True,Conducted at University of Kiel laboratory setting.,1,False,"This row describes the human-subject experiment, not the mathematical model.",1,False,Analytical model exists but is separate; this entry is for the experimental condition.,1,5,"Groups of five subjects; in foot voting, effective interacting group at a given institution can be fewer than five, but nominal group size is five.",0.8,15,Block III consists of 15 rounds (rounds 21–35).,1,1,Contributions are 0 or €0.50 to the pool; binary cooperate/defect decision.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely in private account; subjects decide whether to contribute €0.50.,0.9,0.62,Total contributions multiplied by 3.1 and redistributed among participants: MPCR = 3.1 / 5.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard PGG lab design without chat.,0.8,0,Paper does not report feedback of others’ earnings or punishment details beyond necessary info; assume not shown.,0.4,0,Central authority punishes via fines; no indication that individual identities of punished players are revealed.,0.5,0,No rewards in design.,1,0,Not specified if total or remaining rounds are displayed.,0.3,1,"If at least one subject in that community paid taxes, a central punishment institution was established that fined defectors only.",1,0.05,Subjects fund a central pool rather than per-target punishment; per-unit cost of punishment is not defined; use base tax component as minimal per-round cost to enable punishment.,0.2,1,Fine for each defector is €1.00 per offense; treating tech as 1 unit fine per offense per round.,0.7,0,"Design only includes punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0.9,As in earlier blocks: design does not specify a distinct per-round endowment; approximate based on loner payoff and contribution size.,0.2,N/R,"For block III foot-voting no-2OP condition, only percentages of cooperators/defectors/loners aggregated over the entire treatment are graphed without numerical values separate by institution choice.",0.1,N/R,Average raw contribution amount per subject not separately reported for this condition.,0.1,N/R,"Average payoffs by institution choice within foot voting are not numerically reported; only overall treatment average (€0.64) is given, which mixes both institutions.",0.1,N/R,"Only overall average payoff for the foot-voting treatment is given, not separated by chosen institution.",0.1,Germany,"Experiments conducted at University of Kiel, Germany, with local first-year biology students.",1,N/R,Only that they are first-year biology students; no age statistics given.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,First-year university biology students,Subjects recruited from a first-year course in biology.,1,On site,Standard lab experiment at a university; no indication of online implementation.,0.9,"Foot-voting treatment: in each round of block III, subjects choose between two communities defined by institutional rules (with vs without 2OP). They interact only with those who chose the same institution. Within the chosen no-2OP community, the stage game is as in earlier blocks: loner option, tax decision, and contribution decision, with punishment of defectors only if at least one tax payer.",Summary of institution choice and game structure specific to foot voting without second-order punishment.,1
10.1073_pnas.1315273111.md,Block III – Foot voting treatment – Institution with second-order punishment,"In treatment A (foot voting), during block III subjects can choose each round between communities with or without second-order punishment; this row captures observations for those playing under the institution with second-order punishment across all 15 rounds.",0.8,Voting mechanism: foot voting; chosen institution: central punishment with second-order punishment,Independent variables are voting rule (foot voting vs majority voting) and institution type (with vs without 2OP); here: foot voting and with 2OP.,1,True,Human subjects played a public goods game in the lab.,1,True,Controlled randomized economic game with endogenous institutional choice via foot voting.,1,True,Conducted at University of Kiel laboratory setting.,1,False,"This row describes the human-subject experiment, not the mathematical model.",1,False,Analytical model exists but is separate; this entry is for the experimental condition.,1,5,"Groups of five subjects; in foot voting, effective interacting group at a given institution can be fewer than five, but nominal group size is five.",0.8,15,Block III consists of 15 rounds (rounds 21–35).,1,1,Contributions are 0 or €0.50 to the pool; binary cooperate/defect decision.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely in private account; subjects decide whether to contribute €0.50.,0.9,0.62,Total contributions multiplied by 3.1 and redistributed among participants: MPCR = 3.1 / 5.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard PGG lab design without chat.,0.8,0,Paper does not report feedback of others’ earnings or punishment details beyond necessary info; assume not shown.,0.4,0,Central authority punishes via fines; no indication that individual identities of punished players are revealed.,0.5,0,No rewards in design.,1,0,Not specified if total or remaining rounds are displayed.,0.3,1,"If at least one subject in that community paid taxes, a central punishment institution wa