ame structure specific to foot voting with second-order punishment.,1
10.1073_pnas.1315273111.md,Block III – Majority voting treatment – Institution without second-order punishment,"In treatment B (majority voting), at the beginning of block III the group votes; the institution that gets the majority is imposed on all 15 rounds. This row captures groups whose majority chose institution without second-order punishment.",0.8,Voting mechanism: majority voting; implemented institution: central punishment without second-order punishment,Independent variables are voting rule (majority vs foot voting) and institution type (with vs without 2OP); here: majority voting and no 2OP.,1,True,Human subjects played a public goods game in the lab.,1,True,Controlled randomized economic game with institutions chosen by majority vote.,1,True,Conducted at University of Kiel laboratory setting.,1,False,"This row describes the human-subject experiment, not the mathematical model.",1,False,Analytical model exists but is separate; this entry is for the experimental condition.,1,5,Groups of five subjects.,1,15,Block III consists of 15 rounds (rounds 21–35). The elected institution applies for all 15 rounds.,1,1,Contributions are 0 or €0.50 to the pool; binary cooperate/defect decision.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely in private account; subjects decide whether to contribute €0.50.,0.9,0.62,Total contributions multiplied by 3.1 and redistributed among participants: MPCR = 3.1 / 5.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard PGG lab design without chat.,0.8,0,Paper does not report feedback of others’ earnings or punishment details beyond necessary info; assume not shown.,0.4,0,Central authority punishes via fines; no indication that individual identities of punished players are revealed.,0.5,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,"Because block lengths are fixed and voting occurs at its start, it is standard to inform subjects of the total number of rounds the decision applies to; however, not explicitly stated.",0.5,1,"If at least one subject paid taxes, a central punishment institution was established that fined defectors only.",1,0.05,Subjects fund a central pool rather than per-target punishment; per-unit cost of punishment is not defined; use base tax component as minimal per-round cost to enable punishment.,0.2,1,Fine for each defector is €1.00 per offense; treating tech as 1 unit fine per offense per round.,0.7,0,"Design only includes punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0.9,As in earlier blocks: design does not specify a distinct per-round endowment; approximate based on loner payoff and contribution size.,0.2,N/R,"For block III majority-voting no-2OP condition, contributions are not numerically reported separately; only that one group managed to choose no 2OP and fully cooperate without paying taxes.",0.2,N/R,Average raw contribution amount per subject not separately reported for this condition.,0.1,N/R,Average payoffs in majority-voting treatment (€0.86) mix groups with no-2OP and with-2OP; not separated by implemented institution.,0.1,N/R,"Only overall average payoff for the majority-voting treatment is given, not separated by chosen institution.",0.1,Germany,"Experiments conducted at University of Kiel, Germany, with local first-year biology students.",1,N/R,Only that they are first-year biology students; no age statistics given.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,First-year university biology students,Subjects recruited from a first-year course in biology.,1,On site,Standard lab experiment at a university; no indication of online implementation.,0.9,"Majority-voting treatment: at the beginning of block III, each five-person group votes for preferred institution (with vs without 2OP). The institution with majority support is implemented for all 15 rounds and applies to all members. Within the chosen no-2OP regime, the stage game is as before: loner option, tax decision, contribution decision, with punishment of defectors only.",Summary of institution choice and game structure specific to majority voting without second-order punishment.,1
10.1073_pnas.1315273111.md,Block III – Majority voting treatment – Institution with second-order punishment,"In treatment B (majority voting), at the beginning of block III the group votes; 12 of 15 groups chose the institution with second-order punishment. This row captures those groups over all 15 rounds.",0.9,Voting mechanism: majority voting; implemented institution: central punishment with second-order punishment,Independent variables are voting rule (majority vs foot voting) and institution type (with vs without 2OP); here: majority voting and with 2OP.,1,True,Human subjects played a public goods game in the lab.,1,True,Controlled randomized economic game with institutions chosen by majority vote.,1,True,Conducted at University of Kiel laboratory setting.,1,False,"This row describes the human-subject experiment, not the mathematical model.",1,False,Analytical model exists but is separate; this entry is for the experimental condition.,1,5,Groups of five subjects.,1,15,Block III consists of 15 rounds (rounds 21–35). The elected institution applies for all 15 rounds.,1,1,Contributions are 0 or €0.50 to the pool; binary cooperate/defect decision.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely in private account; subjects decide whether to contribute €0.50.,0.9,0.62,Total contributions multiplied by 3.1 and redistributed among participants: MPCR = 3.1 / 5.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard PGG lab design without chat.,0.8,0,Paper does not report feedback of others’ earnings or punishment details beyond necessary info; assume not shown.,0.4,0,Central authority punishes via fines; no indication that individual identities of punished players are revealed.,0.5,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,"Because block lengths are fixed and voting occurs at its start, it is standard to inform subjects of the total number of rounds the decision applies to; however, not explicitly stated.",0.5,1,"If at least one subject paid taxes, a central punishment institution was established that fined defectors and tax evaders.",1,0.05,Subjects fund a central pool rather than per-target punishment; per-unit cost of punishment is not defined; use base tax component as minimal per-round cost to enable punishment.,0.2,1,Fine for each defector and tax evader is €1.00 per offense; treating tech as 1 unit fine per offense per round.,0.7,0,"Design only includes punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0.9,As in earlier blocks: design does not specify a distinct per-round endowment; approximate based on loner payoff and contribution size.,0.2,N/R,"For block III majority-voting 2OP condition, contributions are not numerically reported separately; text only says behaviors were stable and majority of groups chose 2OP.",0.2,N/R,Average raw contribution amount per subject not separately reported for this condition.,0.1,N/R,Average payoffs in majority-voting treatment (€0.86) mix groups with no-2OP and with-2OP; not separated by implemented institution.,0.1,N/R,"Only overall average payoff for the majority-voting treatment is given, not separated by chosen institution.",0.1,Germany,"Experiments conducted at University of Kiel, Germany, with local first-year biology students.",1,N/R,Only that they are first-year biology students; no age statistics given.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,First-year university biology students,Subjects recruited from a first-year course in biology.,1,On site,Standard lab experiment at a university; no indication of online implementation.,0.9,"Majority-voting treatment: at the beginning of block III, each five-person group votes for preferred institution (with vs without 2OP). For 12 of 15 groups, 2OP wins and is implemented for all 15 rounds. Within the 2OP regime, stage game is as before but the institution punishes both defectors and tax evaders €1.00 if at least one subject pays taxes.",Summary of institution choice and game structure specific to majority voting with second-order punishment.,1
10.1257_aer.99.3.979.md,Private monitoring,One of the four main treatments listed in Table 1 and described under 'Treatments' as the benchmark case with private monitoring and no personal punishment.,1,"Monitoring and punishment institution: private monitoring, no personal punishment, anonymous identities, only current opponent’s action/outcome observed",This treatment differs from others only in info structure and punishment options (Table 1 and 'Treatments' section).,1,True,Uses human subjects in lab sessions at Purdue University.,1,True,Controlled randomized laboratory design with exogenous treatments.,1,True,Conducted in the Purdue University laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,"Paper contains theory but this row describes the experimental condition with human play, not the analytical model alone.",1,4,Each 'economy' comprised four agents ('In our experiment we simplify ... by designing economies of four agents.').,1,71,Table 1 'Number of periods' lists 71 for the private monitoring treatment; design uses indefinite continuation but this is realized length.,1,1,"Stage game is a 2x2 PD with discrete actions Y (cooperate) or Z (defect); players choose between two pure actions, not contribution levels. Coded as 1 since they can freely choose either action (not an all-or-nothing endowment allocation).",0.7,0,Not a contribution game with an endowment split; no default allocation to a public account. Set to 0 as entire payoff is determined by chosen action in private account.,0.5,N/R,"Game is a prisoner's dilemma with fixed payoffs, not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.",1,0,Subjects could not communicate ('cannot directly communicate').,1,0,"Under private monitoring, subjects only see actions and outcomes in their pair; no aggregate histories shown.",1,0,No personal punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,0,Indefinite horizon with probabilistic continuation; paper does not state that remaining rounds or maximum length were displayed.,0.6,0,Table 1 'Ways to punish: Only by defecting' indicates no explicit costly punishment technology beyond choosing Z in the PD.,1,N/A,No costly punishment mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No costly punishment mechanism defined.,1,0,No option to pay to increase others’ payoffs described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Points payoffs per outcome are given but not framed as starting endowment plus gains/losses.,0.5,0.595,"Result 1: 'the rate of (coordination on) cooperation was ... 59.5 percent' for private monitoring, averaged across all periods and economies. Treat cooperation as contribution.",1,N/R,"Only binary cooperate/defect actions reported, no numeric 'amount' of contribution.",1,0.595,"Realized efficiency e_k is defined and its average for private monitoring is 0.595 ('Average realized efficiencies e_k ... were 0.595, 0.586, 0.652, and 0.815').",1,N/R,"Average efficiency provided, but not translated into absolute group payoff totals.",0.5,United States,"Subjects recruited at Purdue University (Indiana, USA).",0.95,N/R,Paper states only that subjects were recruited from undergraduate classes; no age statistics provided.,0.4,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Recruited through announcements in undergraduate classes at Purdue University.,1,On site,"Laboratory sessions at Purdue University with no eye contact, using z-Tree.",1,"2x2 prisoner’s dilemma stage game with payoffs: (Y,Y)=(25,25); (Y,Z)=(10,30); (Z,Y)=(50,10); (Z,Z)=(15,15). Infinite horizon implemented via continuation probability δ=0.95. Random matching of 4 players into 2 pairs each period; 5 cycles per session; 20 subjects per session.",Parameterization of the stage game is in Table 2B; continuation probability and matching protocol are in 'The Supergame' and 'The Experimental Session' sections.,1
10.1257_aer.99.3.979.md,Private monitoring with punishment,"Second treatment in Table 1, private monitoring plus a personal punishment stage.",1,"Monitoring and punishment institution: private monitoring with costly personal punishment (pay 5 to reduce opponent’s payoff by 10), anonymous identities, only current opponent’s action/outcome and punishment observable",Described as 'private monitoring with punishment' in Treatments; Table 1 notes punishment technology and information.,1,True,Human-subject lab treatment.,1,True,Controlled lab manipulation of punishment institution.,1,True,Conducted in Purdue’s lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation in this treatment.,1,False,"This row covers the experimental implementation, not the theory only.",1,4,Same economy size across all four main treatments.,1,139,Table 1 lists 139 periods for private monitoring with punishment.,1,1,Stage 1 is still a binary-action prisoners’ dilemma.,0.7,0,No public good with default allocations; payoffs come directly from PD and punishment choices.,0.5,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication allowed; same as other treatments.,1,0,Information remains private to the pair; no aggregate or others’ histories displayed.,1,0,"No subject IDs, and 'No one could observe any of the actions outside their pair, including the personal punishment.'",1,0,No reward technology.,1,0,Same indefinite horizon with δ=0.95; no evidence of rounds remaining being displayed.,0.6,1,Second stage allows subjects to pay 5 points to reduce opponent’s payoff by 10.,1,5,Explicitly: 'pay five points to reduce the opponent's earnings by ten points.',1,10,Each 5 points spent reduces opponent's payoff by 10 points; report 10 as effect per unit of cost token.,1,0,No option to pay to increase others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Points outcomes define payoffs; no explicit starting endowment per period specified.,0.5,0.742,Result 3: adding personal punishment raises average cooperation from 59.5 to 74.2 percent.,1,N/R,"Only binary cooperate/defect, no numeric amount.",1,0.652,"Average realized efficiency for the four treatments given in order: 0.595, 0.586, 0.652, 0.815; third corresponds to private monitoring with punishment.",1,N/R,Aggregate efficiency but not absolute group payoff reported.,0.5,United States,Same subject pool at Purdue University.,0.95,N/R,Only 'undergraduate classes' noted.,0.4,N/R,Gender not reported.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate classes.,1,On site,Lab sessions at Purdue with subjects physically present.,1,"Same PD stage game and δ=0.95 random continuation as other main treatments; two-stage period: Stage 1 PD, Stage 2 optional costly personal punishment within the pair; random matching among 4 players.",Treatments differ only in information and punishment options; base game and matching unchanged.,1
10.1257_aer.99.3.979.md,Anonymous public monitoring,"Third treatment in Table 1, labelled 'Anonymous public monitoring'.",1,"Monitoring and punishment institution: anonymous public monitoring, no personal punishment, histories of all actions in the economy shown without IDs",Described under 'Treatments' as public monitoring without identities; Table 1 specifies info as 'History of all actions taken in the economy without IDs'.,1,True,Human-subject lab treatment.,1,True,Controlled lab manipulation of monitoring institution.,1,True,Run in the Purdue University lab.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Row refers to empirical treatment condition.,1,4,All economies consist of four subjects.,1,129,Table 1 lists 129 periods for anonymous public monitoring.,1,1,Same binary-action PD as other treatments.,0.7,0,No explicit endowment-sharing game; binary actions determine payoffs.,0.5,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication allowed in any treatment.,1,1,"Public monitoring: 'every subject could observe the current actions taken in every pair'; in anonymous version, histories of whole economy displayed in random order without identifiers.",1,0,No punishment technology in this treatment.,1,0,No reward technology.,1,0,Indefinite horizon; no statement about displaying remaining periods.,0.6,0,Table 1: 'Ways to punish: Only by defecting'; no personal punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit costly punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit costly punishment.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,No per-round endowment specified.,0.5,0.586,"Figure 2 and text: 'Average cooperation for all periods is around 59 percent in both treatments'; later explicitly states average realized efficiency 0.586 for this treatment, and earlier 58.6% cooperation.",0.95,N/R,Cooperation measured as binary actions; no numeric amount.,1,0.586,"Second number in the list of average realized efficiencies 0.595, 0.586, 0.652, 0.815 corresponds to anonymous public monitoring given ordering in text.",1,N/R,Only normalized efficiency provided.,0.5,United States,Same lab and subject pool.,0.95,N/R,Age not reported.,0.4,N/R,Gender not reported.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate classes.,1,On site,Same Purdue lab setting.,1,"Same PD stage game and δ=0.95 continuation; random matching of 4 players; actions of all pairs in the economy revealed each period in random order without IDs (aggregate, anonymous public history).",Monitoring dimension changes but base game and matching remain constant across treatments.,1
10.1257_aer.99.3.979.md,Public monitoring (nonanonymous),"Fourth main treatment, labelled 'Public monitoring (nonanonymous)' in Table 1.",1,"Monitoring and punishment institution: nonanonymous public monitoring, no personal punishment, individual histories of everyone in the economy with subject IDs",Described as 'nonanonymous public monitoring' where 'Subject IDs are public; Individual histories of everyone in the economy'.,1,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab manipulation of whether histories are linked to IDs.,1,True,Same Purdue lab.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Row concerns the experimental treatment.,1,4,Economies of four agents in all main treatments.,1,86,"Table 1 shows 86 and 128 periods under this column; they correspond to two sessions of the same treatment. To avoid inventing an aggregate, we record the shorter 86 as a concrete reported length.",0.6,1,2x2 PD with Y/Z choices.,0.7,0,No endowment split; binary action game.,0.5,N/R,Not a public good game.,1,0,No communication or cheap talk allowed.,1,1,Public monitoring: everyone observes current actions in every pair and can inspect individual histories of all subjects.,1,0,No personal punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Indefinite horizon; paper does not report showing remaining number of periods.,0.6,0,Table 1: 'Ways to punish: Only by defecting'. No separate costly punishment stage.,1,N/A,No explicit costly punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit costly punishment.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,Endowment not framed explicitly.,0.5,0.815,"Result 4 and Figure 2: 'In the nonanonymous public monitoring treatment, average cooperation across economies was 81.5 percent.'",1,N/R,Only binary cooperation indicator used.,1,0.815,"Fourth number in average realized efficiencies list (0.595, 0.586, 0.652, 0.815) corresponds to nonanonymous public monitoring.",1,N/R,Only normalized efficiency reported.,0.5,United States,Same Purdue undergraduate pool.,0.95,N/R,Age distribution not given.,0.4,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Participants recruited from undergraduate classes.,1,On site,Lab sessions with 20 subjects each at Purdue.,1,"Same PD stage game and δ=0.95 continuation; random matching among 4 players; complete individual histories with subject IDs are public to all participants each period, enabling targeted strategies based on past behavior.",Monitoring is public and nonanonymous per Treatments section.,1
