10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2017.07.003.md,Third-party punishment game – Ingroup dictator / Ingroup recipient / Ingroup punisher,One of four experimentally manipulated group-composition conditions; all three players share ideological ingroup (liberal or conservative).,0.95,"Group composition: dictator, recipient, and punisher all from same ideological ingroup (all liberal or all conservative). Dictator transfer level to recipient (0%, 25%, 50%) varied within punisher via strategy method.",Paper states four group-composition conditions and that punishers made punishment decisions for each of three possible dictator transfers.,0.95,True,Human participants recruited from Amazon Mechanical Turk playing incentivized games.,1,True,Group composition randomly manipulated; economic game with controlled incentives.,1,False,Game run over the Internet with MTurk workers rather than in a physical lab.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; all data from humans.,1,False,"Paper presents empirical experiment and statistical analyses, not a formal analytical model.",1,3,"Triads composed of a dictator, recipient, and punisher.",1,1,Game is described as one-shot and anonymous.,1,0,"Dictator can choose among 0%, 25%, 50% transfers; intermediate choices allowed, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,Dictator is given a fixed $1 stake to divide; all money starts with dictator (private account).,0.9,N/R,"This is a dictator/third-party punishment game, not a linear public goods game; no multiplier or MPCR defined.",1,0,Gameplay over Internet described as anonymous; no mention of communication between players.,0.95,0,Punishers only see dictator’s division options and complete valuation tasks; no mention of summary feedback on others’ earnings or past punishment.,0.9,0,Interactions anonymous; no identities are revealed to link punishment to specific individuals beyond role labels.,0.95,0,No reward option in the game; only punishment is available.,1,1,Game explicitly framed as one-shot; participants necessarily know number of rounds.,0.9,1,Punishers have a $0.50 stake they can spend to reduce dictator’s earnings.,1,0.1,Each $0.10 punishers spent reduced the dictator’s stake by 20%. Cost per punishment unit is $0.10.,1,0.2,"Each $0.10 reduces dictator’s earnings by 20% of their stake, so 0.2 stake reduction per $0.10 spent.",1,0,Design only includes costly punishment; no mechanism to increase dictator’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward option in this game.,1,N/A,No reward option in this game.,1,0.5,Punishers have a $0.50 stake to spend on punishment; this is their endowment in the punishment stage.,1,N/R,No contribution decision to a public good; only a division by dictator and punishment by third party are measured.,1,N/R,"Outcome of interest is punishment spending, not contributions; raw punishment amounts reported but not as ‘contributions’.",1,N/R,No efficiency metric (total payoff relative to maximum) reported or derivable from text provided.,0.7,N/R,No aggregate payoff for triads by condition is reported.,0.7,United States,Participants came from MTurk’s US worker pool.,1,N/R,No age information reported in the excerpt.,0.1,39% women among punishers; 44% women among dictators,Participant section reports gender composition separately for punishers and dictators.,1,N/R,No information on educational attainment provided.,0.1,Online,Game was played over the Internet with MTurk workers.,1,"Third-party punishment dictator game using ideological (liberal/conservative) group labels as ingroup/outgroup manipulation. Dictator receives $1 stake with discrete division options (0/1, 0.75/0.25, 0.5/0.5). Punisher uses strategy method to specify punishment ($0–0.50 in $0.10 increments) for each possible dictator transfer. Each $0.10 reduces dictator’s earnings by 20%. Dictators and punishers also complete incentivized valuation tasks (0–0.75) for recipient and punisher.",Summarizes key structural aspects of the game beyond required fields.,1
