10.1007_s00199-005-0633-6.md,Finite supergames – Player A,Condition where subjects are Player A in finitely repeated (exactly 5 rounds) trust game supergames.,1,Supergame horizon: finite (exactly 5 rounds per supergame); role: Player A (trustor).,"Paper distinguishes finite vs indefinite supergames and roles A vs B; this row is finite, Player A.",1,True,Human subjects in laboratory trust game.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with monetary incentives.,1,True,Sessions run at University of Pittsburgh laboratory.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations used for main experiment.,1,False,"While theory is discussed, the reported data here are experimental, not purely analytical.",1,2,"Trust stage game has two players, A and B.",1,5,Finite supergames last exactly five rounds.,1,1,"Player A chooses between sending all $0.40 or sending nothing; this is effectively all-or-nothing, but per coding rule 1 means can contribute any amount; here binary, but mapped to 1 vs 0 only for discrete public goods, so we mark 1 as they choose actions, not continuous contribution.",0.4,0,Both players start each round with private endowment of $0.40; nothing is pre-committed to joint outcome.,0.9,N/R,"Trust game with sequential moves, not linear public good; MPCR not defined.",1,0,No mention of communication; random anonymous matching typical of no-chat design.,0.9,0,Paper does not report feedback beyond opponent’s actions and payoffs; no aggregate summaries mentioned.,0.5,0,No explicit punishment stage or identities; pairings are anonymous.,1,0,No explicit rewarding institution separate from game outcomes.,1,1,"Subjects were told finite supergames last exactly five rounds, so horizon (remaining rounds) is common knowledge.",0.9,0,No costly punishment stage; only strategic withholding in trust game.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0.4,Each player is given an endowment of $0.40 to start each stage game.,1,N/R,"Paper reports behavior as probabilities of Send/Return and strategy fits, not average send frequency as normalized contribution rate.",0.3,N/R,No aggregate average amount sent (0 or 0.40) reported.,0.3,N/R,No ratio of achieved to maximum surplus reported by condition.,0.2,N/R,Average group earnings per round/supergame not explicitly given for finite A players alone.,0.2,USA,Experiments run at University of Pittsburgh (United States).,1,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,University students,"Subjects recruited at a university lab; typical pool is students, though not explicitly stated.",0.6,On site,Laboratory sessions held physically at University of Pittsburgh; not online.,1,"Sequential trust game stage: Player A chooses Send (S) or Don't Send (D) from $0.40 endowment. If D: both receive $0.40. If S: A’s endowment is doubled and transferred; Player B then chooses Keep (K) yielding (0,1.20) or Return (R) yielding (0.60,0.60). Finite treatment consists of 20 supergames, each exactly 5 rounds, with random anonymous rematching between supergames; roles fixed within session.",Directly summarized from Section 2 and Figure 1.,1
