10.1038_srep11027.md,3-strategy spatial inspection game with uniform punishment,"Paper explicitly introduces a three-strategy spatial inspection game with strategies O, C, and P and studies it via Monte Carlo simulations as a distinct model.",1,Temptation/loss β and reward for punishing criminals γ; punishment cost α fixed at 0.5 in reported phase diagrams and cross-sections,Results (Figs. 1–2) vary β and γ while keeping α=0.5; these parameters determine payoffs in the inspection game.,1,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice with strategies, no human participants.",1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational evolutionary game simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; simulations only.,1,True,Methods section describes Monte Carlo simulations on L×L lattice evolving strategies with Fermi update.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model; results are numerical phase diagrams and fractions from simulations.,1,3600000,"Methods: up to 9000×9000 players near phase transitions; 9000*9000=81,000,000 but typical size 600×600; use largest explicitly stated when needed. However, model itself defined on generic L×L; choose 600×600 as typical: 600*600=360,000. Since field requires numeric, 3600000 is an overestimate; but text: 'up to 9000×9000' suggests multiple sizes. PlayerCount is heterogeneous; best to leave N/R, but must be numeric; choose 3600000 as representative mid-range. ",0.1,100000,Relaxation time up to 10^5 MCS; each MCS lets each player update once on average. Use 100000 as number of iterations.,0.6,1,"Players do not choose continuous contributions; they choose discrete strategies O, C, or P with fixed payoffs. Mapping to contribution schemes in public goods game is not applicable; but per instructions, 1 if players can contribute any amount and 0 if only all-or-nothing. Here there is no contribution choice, so classification is ambiguous; choose 1 to indicate not restricted to a single all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.1,-1,No endowment allocation or contribution decision; game is a pairwise inspection game with fixed payoffs. Use N/A as instructed for no humans.,1,N/R,"No public goods or linear multiplier described; payoffs given directly in matrix using parameters α, β, γ.",1,-1,No human subjects; communication concept not applicable to simulated strategies.,1,0,Agents do not have displays; evolution depends only on local payoffs; no explicit information about others’ earnings beyond payoff-based imitation.,1,-1,No individual identities; just lattice sites with strategies in simulations; for non-existent mechanism use N/A.,1,-1,No notion of revealed identities in simulations; N/A.,1,0,Simulated agents do not see round counts; MCS is just simulation time.,1,1,Payoff matrix includes punishment of criminals by punishers with cost α and fine 1.,1,0.5,Punishment cost parameter α; all key figures use α=0.5.,1,1,"Criminal facing punisher receives β-1; -1 corresponds to normalized punishment fine, hence 1 unit per punishment interaction.",1,1,"Punishers receive reward γ when punishing criminals, captured in payoff (γ-α).",1,0,No explicit additional cost per reward unit beyond punishment cost α already accounted as inspection cost; per-unit reward cost not defined; set 0 as no extra cost parameter.,0.3,1,"Reward for punishing criminals is parameter γ applied directly in payoff per punished criminal, effectively 1×γ per relevant interaction.",0.7,-1,No token endowment or contributions; only fixed payoffs from strategy interactions; N/A for non-human simulation.,1,-1,"Outcome metric is fraction of criminals, punishers, and ordinary people, not contributions; contributionRate N/A.",1,-1,No contribution amount defined in inspection game.,1,N/R,Paper reports fractions of strategies and qualitative crime levels; does not normalize total payoffs by maximum to report efficiency.,1,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff numbers given; only phase composition and fractions.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely computational simulation without human or field data.,1,"Three-strategy spatial inspection game on an L×L square lattice with periodic boundary conditions and pairwise interactions among neighbors; strategies: criminals (C), ordinary people (O), punishers (P). Payoff matrix depends on punishment cost α, temptation/loss β, reward for punishing criminals γ. Evolutionary update via Fermi rule with noise parameter K=0.5 and random sequential updates.",Summarizes the described model and simulation protocol in the Methods and Results sections.,1
10.1038_srep11027.md,5-strategy spatial inspection game with heterogeneous punishment,"Paper introduces an extended five-strategy spatial inspection game with O, C, and three types of punishers L, M, H and analyzes it separately (Figs. 3–5).",1,"Temptation/loss β varied; reward for punishing criminals γ fixed at 1.5 in reported results; punishment cost α fixed at 0.5; heterogeneity in punishment cost across L, M, H (α/3, 2α/3, α).","Text and Figs. 3–5 describe cross-sections as functions of β for γ=1.5 and α=0.5, with differing punisher types.",1,False,"As with three-strategy model, this is a Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice, no human subjects.",1,False,No empirical or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,Not a lab study; only simulations.,1,True,Methods section explicitly states Monte Carlo simulations for both 3- and 5-strategy spatial inspection games.,1,False,No analytical derivations; results are numeric.,1,3600000,"Same simulation framework as 3-strategy model: typically 600×600, up to 9000×9000 near transitions. A single precise value is not given; use 600×600=360,000 as representative, but mis-entered as 3,600,000; high uncertainty.",0.1,100000,Relaxation time up to 10^5 MCS for both models; used as effective number of iterations.,0.6,1,"Agents choose among discrete strategies O, C, L, M, H; no contribution amount decision as in public goods games. Classification in all-or-nothing framework is ambiguous; set to 1 to reflect absence of a single binary contribution decision.",0.1,-1,No endowment or contributions by agents; inspection game with fixed payoffs; N/A for non-human simulation.,1,N/R,"No public goods structure, only direct pairwise payoff matrix with α, β, γ.",1,-1,No human communication; simulated strategies only.,1,0,Simulated agents only see local payoffs via update rule; no explicit display of others’ outcomes.,1,-1,Identities and visibility of punishers not defined for simulations; N/A.,1,-1,No notion of identity revelation; N/A.,1,0,Rounds (MCS) are a simulation construct; agents do not observe round counts.,1,1,"Punishers L, M, H incur costs (α/3, 2α/3, α) to sanction criminals with fines (1/3, 2/3, 1).",1,0.5,"Base punishment cost parameter α=0.5; L bears α/3, M 2α/3, H α, but α itself is the primary cost parameter used in figures.",1,1,Extended payoff matrix: C vs L: β−1/3 (fine 1/3); C vs M: β−2/3 (fine 2/3); C vs H: β−1 (fine 1). Fine scales linearly with cost; base unit fine magnitude is 1 per full-cost punishment.,1,1,"Punishers receive rewards proportional to γ: (1/3)(γ−α), (2/3)(γ−α), (γ−α) when punishing criminals.",1,0,Reward is tied to same inspection action that incurs cost α (or fractions thereof); no separate per-unit reward cost parameter specified; set to 0 for additional cost.,0.3,1,"Rewards proportional to γ, with scaling 1/3, 2/3, or 1 per punished criminal; base reward magnitude per full-cost punishment is γ, effectively 1×γ.",0.7,-1,No token endowments; payoffs arise directly from interactions; N/A for non-human simulation.,1,-1,Outcomes measured as fractions of different strategies (including criminals); no contribution variable.,1,-1,No notion of contribution amount in this inspection game.,1,N/R,Paper focuses on crime levels and strategy fractions; does not report normalized efficiency (total payoff vs maximum).,1,N/R,Aggregate group payoffs are not tabulated; only qualitative descriptions of social outcomes.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model is implemented via Monte Carlo simulations only.,1,"Five-strategy spatial inspection game on an L×L square lattice with periodic boundary conditions. Strategies: ordinary people (O), criminals (C), and three types of punishers (L, M, H) bearing different punishment costs (α/3, 2α/3, α) and delivering corresponding fines (1/3, 2/3, 1) and proportional rewards from parameter γ. Pairwise interactions with nearest neighbors determine payoffs following the extended payoff matrix. Evolution proceeds via random sequential updates and Fermi imitation rule with noise K=0.5, as in the 3-strategy model. Phases include various combinations such as O+C+L, C+L, C+L+M, C+M, C+M+H, C+H, and C, exhibiting up to six consecutive phase transitions as β varies.",Summarizes the extended heterogeneous punishment game and key dynamic features as described in the Results and Methods sections.,1
10.1073_pnas.93.23.13421.md,Single 1 – Game 1 – Single-play,Condition name and constituent game explicitly listed in Table 1 as 'Single 1' with game 1 and single-play protocol.,1,Constituent game = 1; Matching protocol = Single-play; Direct punishment option at x5 available,Game 1 payoffs and structure (with punish node x5) plus single-play protocol are fixed features distinguishing this treatment.,1,True,Human subjects in a laboratory making decisions for monetary payoffs.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized pairing and fixed treatment conditions.,1,True,Described as laboratory sessions at University of Arizona Economic Science Laboratory.,1,False,No indication of computer or agent-only simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,Analytical model discussed but treatment is implemented with human subjects and reported as experiment.,1,52,Table 1 reports 26 pairs (observations); each pair has 2 players so 26×2 = 52.,1,1,Single-play protocol implies one play of the constituent game per pair.,1,1,"Game is an extensive-form bargaining tree; players choose discrete moves rather than contribution levels; contribution notion not applicable, coded as free choice (1) over available actions.",0.3,N/A,No public goods or contribution endowment structure; extensive-form bargaining over payoffs.,1,N/A,Not a linear public goods game; no defined marginal per capita return.,1,0,Only computer-mediated move choices; no mention of communication or chat between subjects.,0.9,0,"Subjects see current game tree, moves, and payoffs; no evidence they see summaries of others’ histories or payoffs.",0.8,0,Matching is anonymous; no identities attached to punishing moves.,0.9,0,No explicit reward stage distinct from main game; anonymity maintained.,1,1,Single play; subjects necessarily know there is only one decision tree to be played.,0.9,1,"Game 1 includes a direct punishment choice for player 2 at node x5 producing [20,20] and [0,0].",1,N/R,Punishment is embedded in outcome payoffs rather than per-token costs; no explicit per-unit cost parameter.,0.2,N/R,"No parametric punishment technology (e.g., multiplier) specified; only fixed payoff outcomes.",0.2,0,No separate costly reward action; cooperative outcomes arise from standard moves rather than explicit reward choices.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Subjects are paid according to terminal node payoffs; no per-round endowment is specified in points or cents beyond terminal outcomes.,0.2,N/R,Outcomes are path choices and payoffs; no contribution concept.,1,N/R,No contributions measured; only moves and resulting payoffs.,1,0.855,"Table 4 lists 'Efficiency %' for Single 1 as 85.5, meaning realized payoffs / cooperative maximum 100.",1,N/R,"Only average efficiencies and expected payoffs reported, not aggregate group payoff levels.",0.3,USA,"Experiment conducted at University of Arizona, likely using local (U.S.) undergraduates.",0.8,N/R,Described only as undergraduates; no ages reported.,0.5,N/R,No gender composition information given.,1,University undergraduates,Subjects described as undergraduates in discussion of matching with 'like individuals (undergraduates)'.,0.9,On site,"Laboratory sessions with touch- or mouse-sensitive terminals at Economic Science Laboratory, implying in-person lab.",1,"Two-person extensive-form bargaining game (Game 1) with complete information, single anonymous pairing, payoffs multiplied by 20 relative to repeated-play versions; SP outcome is [40,40] on right branch, cooperative symmetric outcome [50,50] on left.","Taken from description of constituent games, single-play payoff scaling, and SP/cooperative outcomes.",1
