10.1073_pnas.93.23.13421.md,Random 1 – Game 1 – Repeat random pairs and roles,Table 1: 'Random 1' uses game 1 with repeat random pairs and roles.,1,Constituent game = 1; Matching protocol = Random matching each period with random roles; Direct punishment option at x5 available,Random 1 is Game 1 under random matching; roles and counterparts re-drawn each repetition.,1,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled repeated-play design.,1,True,Performed in lab with computers.,1,False,Not a computational simulation.,1,False,Empirical data are central.,1,48,Table 1: 24 pairs; 24×2 = 48 players.,1,20,Table 1: 24 pairs (480 observations) => 20 repetitions; Tables 8 etc. show blocks up to 20.,1,1,Extensive-form decisions.,0.3,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No public good.,1,0,No communication reported; subjects remain anonymous.,0.9,0,No mention of being shown global stats or others’ histories.,0.9,0,Anonymity implies no identity revelation of punishing parties.,0.9,0,No explicit reward choice.,1,0,Length of experiment not told to participants.,1,1,Game 1 includes direct punishment node.,1,N/R,No per-unit punishment cost parameter reported.,0.2,N/R,No punishment multiplier specified.,0.2,0,No explicit reward action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Only outcome payoffs shown.,0.2,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,0.826,Table 4: efficiency for Random 1 is 82.6%.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not provided.,0.3,USA,Same lab and student pool.,0.8,N/R,Not reported.,0.5,N/R,Not reported.,1,University undergraduates,Undergraduate pool referred to.,0.9,On site,Lab sessions with terminals.,1,"Game 1 repeated 20 times with random rematching and random assignment of player 1 or 2 roles each period; no information on counterpart histories provided, making repeated-game strategies more diffuse.",From matching protocol description and Random 1 details.,1
10.1073_pnas.93.23.13421.md,Random 2 – Game 2 – Repeat random pairs and roles,Table 1 lists 'Random 2' as constituent game 2 with repeat random pairs and roles.,1,Constituent game = 2 (no direct punishment); Matching protocol = Random matching per repetition with random roles,Random 2 uses Game 2 under random matching.,1,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled experimental condition.,1,True,Conducted in a physical lab.,1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,"Data from human behavior, not formal-only analysis.",1,48,Table 1: 24 pairs; 2×24 = 48 players.,1,20,Table 1: 24 pairs (480 observations) -> 20 repetitions per pair group; repetition blocks 1–20 shown in Table 9.,1,1,Extensive-form moves rather than contributions.,0.3,N/A,No contribution framework.,1,N/A,No linear public good.,1,0,No indications of chat/communication between participants.,0.9,0,Histories of others or global summaries not provided.,0.9,0,Anonymous matching; no identity revelation.,0.9,0,No explicit reward action.,1,0,Subjects are not told how many rounds will be played.,1,0,Game 2 lacks direct punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No distinct reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Only terminal payoffs are specified.,0.2,N/R,No contribution dimension.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,0.847,Table 4: efficiency for Random 2 is 84.7%.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not given explicitly.,0.3,USA,Same lab and subject pool at University of Arizona.,0.8,N/R,Not reported.,0.5,N/R,Not reported.,1,University undergraduates,Described as undergraduates.,0.9,On site,Laboratory sessions with touch/mouse interfaces.,1,Game 2 repeated 20 times under random matching of partners and roles; investigates trust/cooperation without direct punishment when interactions are random rather than with fixed counterparts.,From Random 2 description and game 2 structure.,1
10.5751_es-05598-180302.md,Baseline (Rounds 1–10) – High-performance unions,"Uses the baseline/open-access regime in rounds 1–10, restricted to subjects from high-performance unions as defined by the performance index.",1,"Community type = High-performance unions; Enforcement regime = Baseline (no inspections, de facto open access); Round = 1–10","Independent variables that differ across conditions are community type and enforcement regime (pre-change vs post-change); here, baseline with high-performance unions in rounds 1–10.",1,True,Human artisanal fishers participated in the CPR game.,1,True,Controlled common-pool resource game with randomized components and fixed rules.,1,True,"Computerized experiment conducted on portable computers in a controlled room setting, not in actual fishing trips.",0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation without humans was used for this condition.,1,False,"Although payoff functions are presented analytically, the results reported here are from a human-subject experiment.",1,5,Each experimental group consisted of five subjects.,1,10,Baseline treatment is defined as the first 10 rounds (1–10) of the 20-round game.,1,1,"Players choose any integer overharvest between 0 and 50, so contribution/overharvest is not all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Endowment of 100 loco is always granted as individual quota in private account; overharvest is additional extraction, not initial public allocation.",0.9,0.5,"Each extra unit overharvested imposes 0.5 unit loss on each of 4 others: group loss = 2 units per 1 unit extracted; marginal group return = 1/(1+2)=1/3, but MPCR as defined is multiplier/group size. Here public ‘multiplier’ is −2/5 per unit, so MPCR of externality magnitude is 0.5 per other; using per capita marginal effect: each unit gives −0.5 to each other, so MPCR in standard PGG sense is not directly applicable. Entered 0.5 as the per capita marginal externality magnitude.",0.3,0,Instructions explicitly state that no communication among group members was allowed.,1,1,"After every round, subjects were informed about the average harvest of their group and their losses from the externality.",1,0,No punishment by peers; only external negative externality and later computer inspections. No identities revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present.,1,1,"Instructions explain that the game is divided into 20 rounds, so total number of rounds is known to subjects.",0.9,0,Baseline open-access regime explicitly states that quota is not enforced and no sanctions occur.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in baseline.,1,0,No positive rewards beyond standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,Each subject endowed with 100 units of loco per round as individual quota.,1,N/R,"Paper reports overharvest, not normalized contribution (i.e., quota compliance) rates by condition.",0,N/R,Only mean group overharvests are reported; individual overharvest or compliant contribution amounts per condition are not provided.,0,N/R,Payoffs by round and condition are not provided; only overall earnings across the entire game for pooled high-performance union members are reported.,0,N/R,No round-by-round or condition-specific group payoff totals are reported.,0,Chile,Subjects were artisanal fishers from Chilean coastal communities.,1,N/R,No age information provided.,0,N/R,Gender composition is not mentioned.,0,N/R,No education details given.,0,On site,"Computerized sessions using portable computers conducted in fishing communities (field-lab setting), not online or in natural fishing activity.",0.8,"Common-pool resource game with 5-person groups; each round, each subject automatically harvests an individual quota of 100 loco and chooses extra overharvest 0–50 units. Each extra unit gives the individual +1 loco and imposes −0.5 loco on each of the four others. Payoff per loco is CLP$10. Baseline regime has no inspections or quota enforcement; subjects see their own harvest, group average harvest, externality losses, and monetary payoff each round; no information on individual others’ choices.",Summarizes the CPR design as described in the methods and instructions.,1
10.5751_es-05598-180302.md,Regulated regime (Rounds 11–20) – High-performance unions,Same groups of high-performance union members but after the unexpected change in rules introducing random inspections and confiscation in rounds 11–20.,1,Community type = High-performance unions; Enforcement regime = Regulated (random inspections with confiscation if quota violated); Round = 11–20,Captures the post-enforcement treatment for high-performance unions.,1,True,Same human-subject experiment as baseline.,1,True,Controlled CPR experiment with change in institutional regime.,1,True,"Same portable-computer, controlled setting in the field.",0.9,False,Human-subject data only.,1,False,"Condition refers to human experiment, not pure theory.",1,5,Groups remain of size five for all rounds.,1,10,Regulated treatment covers the last 10 rounds (11–20) of the 20-round game.,1,1,Subjects can still overharvest any amount 0–50.,1,0,Structure of endowment and decision remains unchanged: 100-unit individual quota plus optional overharvest.,0.9,0.5,Externality technology unchanged from baseline; see previous condition.,0.3,0,Communication remained forbidden throughout the experiment.,1,1,"Subjects continue to see group average harvest, losses, and payoffs after each round.",1,0,Inspections and sanctions are conducted by computer; players are not told who was inspected or sanctioned.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Total number of rounds (20) is known from the beginning.,0.9,1,"From round 11 onward, two randomly selected subjects are inspected; if overharvesting (x_i>0) is detected, entire round’s harvest is confiscated, a probabilistic external sanction.",1,0,Punishment is automatic via the computer (regulator); subjects do not pay to punish others.,1,1,"If sanctioned, the punished subject loses 100% of their harvest from that round; i.e., one unit of sanction removes the full payoff from that round. Coded as full confiscation (1× of that round’s payoff).",0.5,0,No extra rewards beyond baseline payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,Each round begins with 100-unit quota as in baseline.,1,N/R,No normalized contribution (quota adherence) reported by condition.,0,N/R,"Only overharvest amounts are reported, not compliant harvest or contribution in original resource units by condition.",0,N/R,"Cannot compute efficiency for this subset because only mean group overharvests, not full payoff sequences, are reported.",0,N/R,"Earnings are reported only aggregated over all 20 rounds, not separately for regulated regime.",0,Chile,Same subject pool: Chilean artisanal fishers.,1,N/R,Not given.,0,N/R,Not given.,0,N/R,Not given.,0,On site,"Same portable-computer, in-person sessions in fishing communities.",0.8,"Same CPR game and payoff function as baseline; new rule from round 11: after each round, two of five subjects are randomly inspected; if a inspected subject overharvested (x_i>0), their entire harvest that round (100 + x_i minus externality) is confiscated, yielding zero monetary payoff for that round. Subjects are informed of the inspection regime but not of which others are inspected or sanctioned.",Summarizes enforcement change relevant to this condition.,1
