ts including outcome characterization relevant to this condition.,1
10.1093_scan_nst023.md,Right TPJ rTMS – Player C third-party punishment stage,Condition defined by low-frequency rTMS applied to right TPJ before the third‑party punishment paradigm.,1,"Brain stimulation site: right TPJ (1 Hz rTMS, 20 min, 1200 pulses, 110% resting motor threshold)",Independent variable manipulated between subjects is stimulation site (right TPJ vs left TPJ vs sham); this row is the right TPJ condition.,1,True,Human subjects (soccer fans) made punishment decisions with real monetary stakes.,1,True,Random assignment to stimulation conditions with controlled task and payoffs.,1,True,Third-party punishment stage conducted in TMS laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model as standalone study.,1,3,"Third-party punishment paradigm involves Players A, B, and C.",1,30,Player C was confronted with 30 decision situations (30 punishment trials).,1,1,Player C had 10 points and could use any number of points to assign punishment; no all-or-nothing restriction mentioned.,0.9,0,No contribution to a public good; all 10 points are Player C’s private endowment for punishment/keeping.,1,N/A,"Design is a third-party punishment over PDG outcomes, not a linear public goods game.",1,0,Interactions were anonymous with no mention of communication.,0.95,1,Player C saw Players A and B’s PDG choices and resulting payoffs implicitly needed to know incomes; described as being informed about decisions.,0.75,0,Interactions conducted in complete anonymity; no identities revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the paradigm.,1,1,"Standard z-Tree implementations typically show remaining rounds, but not explicitly stated; uncertain.",0.3,1,Core of paradigm is third-party punishment via costly punishment points.,1,1,Assigning one punishment point costs Player C one point.,1,3,One punishment point reduces sanctioned player’s income by three points.,1,0,No option described for Player C to reward; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Player C received an endowment of 10 points at the beginning of each punishment trial.,1,N/R,No contribution decision to a public good by Player C; only punishment expenditure.,1,N/R,"Study reports punishment points invested, not contributions to a group account.",1,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,1,N/R,Paper does not report aggregate payoffs for the triads.,1,Switzerland,"Subjects were strong fans of two top Swiss soccer teams and the study conducted at University of Basel, Switzerland.",0.9,"Mean 24.3 years (SD 4.2), adult men","Reported as 36 healthy men, mean age ± s.d. = 24.3 ± 4.2 years; groups randomly assigned.",1,Male only,Described sample: 36 healthy men.,1,N/R,No details on student status or education level.,1,On site,Third-party punishment and TMS performed in the TMS laboratory with subjects physically present.,1,"Third-party punishment over pre-recorded PDG outcomes; Player C punishes only one player per trial (recoded as A), stake 10 points (10 pts = 1 CHF). A and B previously played a simultaneous PDG (endowment 20; keep vs pass all; passed points doubled). Group constellations: IN/IN, IN/OUT, OUT/IN. PDG decisions of A and B were preselected so all Cs faced same 30 situations in random order.",Summarizes task structure and payoff details directly described in methods.,1
