group.,0.9,"Mean 24.3 years (SD 4.2), adult men","Same overall sample (36 healthy men, mean age 24.3 ± 4.2) randomized into groups.",1,Male only,All participants described as men.,1,N/R,No level of education reported.,1,On site,TMS and task run in laboratory.,1,"Identical third-party punishment design as right TPJ condition: Player C punishes pre-recorded PDG outcomes (A and B; 20-point endowment, keep vs pass all, passed points doubled). C has 10 points per trial, 10 points = 1 CHF, can punish only one player (recoded as A), punishment costs 1 and reduces target’s income by 3, 30 preselected and randomized decision situations with group constellations IN/IN, IN/OUT, OUT/IN.",Same task and payoffs across stimulation conditions; only stimulation site differs.,1
10.1093_scan_nst023.md,Sham rTMS (right or left TPJ) – Player C third-party punishment stage,Control condition with sham stimulation over right or left TPJ before third‑party punishment paradigm.,1,"Brain stimulation: sham rTMS (coil positioned for right or left TPJ but no effective stimulation, 20 min)",Sham stimulation condition contrasted with right and left TPJ rTMS in analyses.,1,True,Human subjects participated under sham stimulation.,1,True,Random assignment to sham vs active stimulation in a controlled task.,1,True,Sham applied in the same TMS laboratory context.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,"Experimental behavioral data, not analytical modeling.",1,3,"Same triadic structure as other conditions (A, B, C).",1,30,Each Player C faced 30 punishment decision trials.,1,1,C can invest any number of the 10 points in punishment; no binding to all-or-nothing.,0.9,0,No public good; endowment entirely private.,1,N/A,Not a public goods contribution game.,1,0,Interactions explicitly anonymous; no communication mentioned.,0.95,1,C is informed about PDG decisions and likely associated payoffs to judge norm violations.,0.75,0,Complete anonymity implies no identity information with punishment.,1,0,No reward option in design.,1,1,Likely displayed by standard z-Tree interface but not specified; low confidence.,0.3,1,Third-party punishment is the main decision in this condition too.,1,1,One punishment point costs the third-party one point.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces punished player’s income by three points.,1,0,No mention of any reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards not allowed.,1,N/A,Rewards not allowed.,1,10,C has 10 points at the beginning of each punishment trial.,1,N/R,No contribution variable; only punishment points recorded.,1,N/R,Study does not analyze contributions; only punishment behavior.,1,N/R,No reported efficiency measure.,1,N/R,No explicit group payoff statistics given.,1,Switzerland,Sham participants drawn from same Swiss soccer fan pool at Basel.,0.9,"Mean 24.3 years (SD 4.2), adult men","All 36 men (mean age 24.3 ± 4.2) randomized, including sham group.",1,Male only,Sample described as 36 healthy men.,1,N/R,Education level not reported.,1,On site,Sham administered in TMS lab with same computerized task.,1,"Identical third-party punishment task as active TMS groups: Player C (soccer fan) punishes preselected PDG outcomes between A and B (20-point endowment each; pass vs keep; passed points doubled). C has 10 points per trial, can use them to punish only one player (coded as A), cost 1 to reduce target by 3, 30 randomized decision situations spanning IN/IN, IN/OUT, OUT/IN group constellations. No actual brain disruption due to sham.",Sham group serves as control with same behavioral paradigm but without cortical disruption.,1
10.1093_scan_nst023.md,Online PDG – baseline cooperation (all treatment groups pooled),First-stage simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma Game conducted online weeks before TMS; used to assess baseline parochial cooperation.,1,Interaction partner’s group membership: ingroup vs outgroup opponent in simultaneous PDG,Key condition within PDG is whether opponent is ingroup or outgroup; all later TMS groups participated but no between-group manipulation at this stage.,1,True,Human soccer fans played PDG via Internet questionnaire tool.,1,True,Controlled payoff structure and randomized pairing in an incentivized PDG.,0.95,False,"PDG conducted online using Unipark, not in physical lab setting.",1,False,"Real human decisions, not simulated agents.",1,False,No closed-form analytical model as standalone study.,1,2,Simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma Game between Player A and Player B.,1,N/R,Number of PDG interactions per subject not stated; only that they played with ingroup and outgroup members.,0.2,0,Decision is binary: keep 20 or pass all 20; no intermediate amounts.,1,0,All 20 points start as private endowment; cooperation means passing all.,1,2,"Each passed point is doubled for the other player; endowment 20, if one passes all 20, other gets 40. MPCR = 40/20 = 2 for the recipient per unit given.",0.9,0,Internet questionnaire tool with simultaneous one-shot decisions; no communication mentioned and anonymity stressed.,0.95,0,Standard PDG feedback likely limited to own outcome; no evidence of summary statistics being shown.,0.3,0,No punishment stage in PDG; thus no punishment identities.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism; PDG is simple cooperation/defection.,1,0,"Design suggests independent anonymous interactions with no repeated interaction; total number of interactions not reported to subjects (inferred, low confidence).",0.2,0,PDG stage has only cooperate vs defect; no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,"No costly reward option; only passing vs keeping, which is the main strategic choice.",1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism; cooperation is part of base game.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,20,Each player was endowed with 20 points.,1,0.73,"Paper reports cooperation rate advantage of ingroup over outgroup (left TMS: 75%, right TMS: 70%, sham: 73%). Using sham group 73% as an example cooperation rate toward ingroup; overall exact pooled rate not given, but sham ingroup rate is the only explicit percentage. Recorded here as normalized cooperation rate (0–1) with low confidence.",0.3,N/R,Raw average number of points passed (0 or 20) not reported by group; only cooperation percentages by group membership.,0.2,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure reported.,1,N/R,No summary of dyadic payoffs reported.,1,Switzerland,Same subject pool: strong fans of two top Swiss soccer teams recruited around University of Basel.,0.9,"Mean 24.3 years (SD 4.2), adult men","PDG involved same individuals later serving as Players C (36 healthy men, mean age 24.3 ± 4.2).",1,Male only,"Sample described as 36 healthy men, who also played the PDG.",1,N/R,No education information given.,1,Online,First-stage PDG conducted as an Internet experiment using Unipark.,1,"Simultaneous Prisoner’s Dilemma between A and B, both soccer fans. Each has 20 points and chooses to keep (defect, D) or pass all 20 (cooperate, C). Passed points are doubled so the recipient gets 40 while giver gets 0. Four possible outcome patterns: CC, CD, DC, DD. Pairings with ingroup and outgroup opponents; decisions used later as stimuli for third-party punishment. Subjects were informed that there were no repeated interactions and full anonymity to exclude reputation effects.",Direct summary of PDG design and its use as first stage feeding into the TMS punishment experiment.,1
10.1038_s41559-019-0907-1.md,Supplementary Model 1 – Early replicators in protocell (no enforcement allowed),Named from text: 'Supplementary Model 1' describing early replicators in a protocell; this condition is the baseline case 'without the possibility of enforcement'.,0.9,Enforcement trait a not allowed to evolve (a fixed at 0); parameters such as benefit of cooperation and cost of selfishness varied in theory but not specified numerically in main text.,The independent manipulation is the absence of enforcement; other parameters are swept but only generically mentioned.,0.8,False,Described as a mathematical model of replicators in protocells; no human or other empirical subjects.,1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; purely theoretical.,1,False,Not a lab study; analytical/theoretical only.,1,False,Main text calls them 'models' with equations; simulations may exist in Supplementary Information but not clearly distinguished from analytical work here.,0.4,True,"Described as 'mathematical models' with explicit equations (e.g., equation (S4)); no numerical parameters given in text.",0.9,0,"No discrete 'players'; replicators in protocells in a theoretical model, not a game with a stated group size.",0.2,0,No repeated rounds; evolutionary model in continuous or generational time.,0.2,1,"Contributions/selfishness are continuous traits (z and a) in the model, not binary all-or-nothing choices.",0.8,N/A,Not a public goods game with endowments; no human subjects.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return for a linear public goods game is not defined in the theoretical replicator model in the main text.,0,N/A,No human or agent communication in this abstract evolutionary model.,1,0,No information feedback structure like a game; not applicable.,1,N/A,No punishment stage or identities; and no humans.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in a game-theoretic sense.,1,0,Not a finite-round game; evolutionary dynamics instead.,1,0,Enforcement trait is disabled; hence no punishment-like enforcement.,0.9,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment technology defined.,1,0,"Model focuses on selfishness versus cooperative investment, not explicit rewarding.",0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward technology defined.,1,0,No individual endowments as in an economic game.,1,N/R,Model tracks trait z (selfishness) and cooperation implicitly; no normalized contribution rate is reported.,0,N/R,No token-based contributions are specified.,0,N/R,They discuss group fitness qualitatively; no normalized efficiency measure is given.,0,N/R,Group fitness is conceptual; no numeric payoff reported.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical evolutionary model.,1,"Mathematical model of early replicators in protocells without enforcement; trait z denotes selfishness, cooperation lowers replication rate but benefits group.",Summarized from description of Fig. 2a and reference to Supplementary Model 1 and equation (S4).,0.9
