yer 3 VUs.,1,0,"Only negative punishment points, no positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,At the start of the game participants were endowed with 7×10 VUs; effectively 10 VUs per round.,1,N/R,They report only that 9 participants refused to transfer; no mean transfer rate among included 38 participants is given.,0.2,N/R,Only binary transfer decision described; no aggregate transfer statistics provided.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff measures vs maximum reported for this condition.,0.2,N/R,Mean group earnings per round or overall are not given.,0.2,Netherlands,Participants were Dutch undergraduates from VU University Amsterdam.,1,N/R,No age information reported.,0.1,N/R,Gender not specified.,0.1,University undergraduates,Sample described as Dutch undergraduates.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment with participants seated in individual cubicles with computers.,1,"After anger induction, participants (Player 1) played 7-round STG. Endowment: 7×10 VUs (1 VU = €0.05). Participants made one decision for all seven rounds whether to transfer 10 VUs per round to Player 2 or not. Player 2 decisions came from a pre-existing pool: across rounds participants encountered 3 cooperators (who returned 25 VUs leading to 25–25 payoffs) and 4 non-cooperators (keeping all 50 VUs, yielding 50–0). After outcome each round, participants received 20 extra VUs that they could keep or spend as punishment points (0–20) each costing 1 VU and reducing Player 2’s payoff by 3 VUs.",Summary from Study 3 procedure section for anger induction group; game is identical to control except for prior emotion induction.,1
