 reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,University undergraduates,Described as Dutch undergraduates.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment; same environment as anger condition.,1,"Identical 7-round STG as in anger condition, but with neutral writing task instead of anger induction. Participants decide once whether to transfer 10 VUs across seven rounds or not. For those who transfer, they interact with 3 cooperative and 4 non-cooperative pre-recorded Player 2 decisions per round. After each outcome, participants can assign 0–20 punishment points, each costing 1 VU and reducing Player 2’s payoff by 3 VUs.",Derived from Study 3 description; only difference relative to anger condition is absence of anger induction.,1
10.1098_rspb.2013.0247.md,"Symmetric – No penalty (rounds 1–15, pooled over high/low-penalty sessions)","Symmetric cost structure with Ki=30 for all, and no penalty for person X in part 1; both symmetric sessions share identical parameters in rounds 1–15 so they are pooled.",0.9,"Punishment-cost symmetry (symmetric: U_i=70, K_i=30 for all) and penalty for X (no penalty)","Independent variables that differ across conditions are symmetry of punishment costs and penalty size; this row is the symmetric, no-penalty case.",0.95,True,Laboratory experiment with 120 human student subjects.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions and controlled payoff structure in a lab game.,1,True,Conducted in an experimental laboratory at ETH Zurich using z-Tree.,1,False,No mention of agent-based or computational simulation; only human subjects.,1,False,"Paper reports theoretical background elsewhere but this condition is from a lab experiment, not a standalone analytical model.",0.9,4,"Groups of four: persons 1, 2, 3, and X in each round.",1,15,Part 1 is defined as rounds 1–15 with no penalty.,1,0,"Reclaim (""up"") decision is binary but this is not a contribution public-goods game; field defined for contribution games only. Set to 0 as there is no continuous contribution choice.",0.6,N/A,This is not a standard public goods contribution setting; there is no default allocation between private and public accounts.,1,N/R,No linear public-good multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; only step-level reclaim.,1,0,No communication between participants is mentioned; standard z-Tree lab setup without chat.,0.8,1,"Subjects know when money is stolen, reclaimed, and the resulting payoffs; decision screens and feedback shots are mentioned in instructions. Assume outcome feedback at least for the group.",0.7,0,Decisions are described as anonymous; identities of who chose 'up' are not stated as revealed.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,1,Parts are defined by round numbers and subjects typically see round counters in z-Tree; likely informed about total of 30 rounds.,0.7,1,Reclaiming stolen money via choosing 'up' is a costly sanctioning action against X.,1,30,"In symmetric condition, each non-X person choosing 'up' is charged 30 MU.",1,210,"If at least one 'up' is chosen, 210 MU are removed from X (the stolen 3×70) and returned to others. Each ""unit"" of punishment (one person choosing up) can trigger full reversal; effective max impact per up is 210.",0.6,0,No option to spend resources to increase others' payoff is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,140,Each subject is endowed with 140 MU in every round.,1,0.71,Use group-level reclaim rate without penalty in symmetric condition: 71% of groups have at least one reclaim; treat this as rate of costly second-order cooperation.,0.9,N/R,Only binary reclaim decisions and group reclaim rates are reported; no per-individual frequency reported separately for this pooled symmetric condition.,0.4,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to maximum is reported or derivable by condition from text alone.,0.3,N/R,Average group earnings by condition are not reported.,0.3,Switzerland,"Subjects were students from the University of Zurich and ETH Zurich, both in Switzerland.",1,Mean 23.1 years (s.d. 3.13),Reported average age and standard deviation for the full sample.,1,52.5% female,Gender composition reported for the full sample.,1,University students,Participants were students from two universities.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental laboratory (DeSciL at ETH Zurich).,1,"Volunteer’s dilemma structure: groups of four (3 potential punishers, 1 potential thief). Person X can steal 70 MU from each of the other three (total 210 MU). If at least one of the three chooses 'up', stealing is undone and all return to 140 MU; each 'up' costs 30 MU to that player. No penalty is imposed on X in this part beyond losing the stolen amount.",Summarizes the described payoff structure specific to this condition’s part.,1
