10.1016_j.tree.2005.07.012.md,Fehr and Gächter public goods game – Punishment condition,Paper explicitly contrasts a “punishment condition” with the baseline public goods game.,0.99,"Punishment availability: After contribution stage, subjects can assign costly punishment to others.",Independent variable is presence of a punishment stage; this condition includes it.,0.99,True,Human participants (same student pool) played the modified public goods game with punishment.,1,True,Controlled intervention adding punishment opportunity in a repeated public goods game.,1,True,"As with the baseline condition, this was part of the same laboratory experiment.",0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; results arise from human play.,1,False,"This section summarizes experimental outcomes, not analytical derivations.",1,4,Punishment condition is described as a version of the same 4-person public goods game.,1,6,Punishment condition is another “version of the game” in the six-period design; Fehr and Gächter 2002 uses six periods for both treatments.,0.9,1,"Contribution stage is identical to baseline: “contribute real money, in an amount of their choosing”.",0.98,0,Same contribution structure as baseline; endowment starts in private account and is voluntarily contributed.,0.8,N/R,The multiplier and implied MPCR are not stated in this article.,0.2,0,No communication allowed is mentioned; standard Fehr & Gächter punishment design disallows chat.,0.85,1,"As in baseline, participants are informed of others’ investments prior to punishment decisions.",0.98,0,"Paper notes punishment targets but not that identities were revealed; standard design keeps players anonymous, so punished subjects see reduction but not identity.",0.75,0,Only costly punishment stage is described; no rewards or identity-linked rewards.,0.98,1,"Same six-period structure; subjects are almost certainly informed, as in usual protocol, though not stated explicitly.",0.7,1,Text: “they can each impose a cost on individuals of their choice at a lesser cost to themselves. All punishment decisions are made simultaneously.”,1,N/R,"Cost per unit of punishment is not specified in this article (original Fehr & Gächter 2002 gives it, but it is not repeated here).",0.2,N/R,Magnitude of loss per punishment unit to the punished player is not reported here.,0.2,0,No positive reward mechanism in this particular game; only punishment is discussed.,0.95,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,Endowment per subject per round is not given in this summary article.,0.1,N/R,"Text: contributions “start out even higher and then increase from round to round” in the punishment condition, but no exact fractions of the endowment are reported.",0.2,N/R,No absolute contribution amounts given for the punishment condition.,0.1,N/R,"Although punishment raises contributions, no explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is quantified here.",0.1,N/R,No numerical payoff data for groups under punishment condition are provided.,0.1,N/R,Country or city of the lab is not specified in this article.,0.1,N/R,Participants are described as “students”; no age data reported.,0.1,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0.1,University students,Text: “24 students”; implies university-level but further detail not provided.,0.9,On site,Same lab-based experimental setting as the baseline condition.,0.9,"Same 4-player linear public goods game as baseline, but after learning others’ contributions each subject can impose costly punishment on any other group member. Over 80% of subjects punish at least once and about one third punish more than five times over six periods. Roughly three quarters of punishment acts are by above-average contributors targeting below-average contributors. Contributions begin high and increase over the six rounds.","Directly follows description: punishment frequency, direction (high contributors punishing low contributors), and dynamic effect on contributions.",1
10.1016_j.tree.2005.07.012.md,Milinski et al. – Indirect reciprocity helping game with reputation (image scoring/standing),Box 4 summarizes experiments by Milinski et al. [75] on cooperation through indirect reciprocity; this row represents the human subject helping game they implemented.,0.85,Recipient reputation (good vs bad) based on prior helping decisions; subjects decide whether to help given recipient’s reputation while knowing that observers update their own reputation.,Key manipulated information is whether the potential recipient has a good or bad reputation and how helping/refusing affects the actor’s reputation.,0.8,True,Box 4 explicitly refers to “experiments by Milinski and colleagues” with “subjects’ reputations”.,1,True,Structured game with controlled reputation update rules; not observational field data.,0.95,True,Standard indirect reciprocity experiments with human subjects are run in an experimental lab setting; no indication of field context.,0.8,False,Box 4 distinguishes simulations of strategies from “experiments by Milinski and colleagues”; this part is empirical.,1,False,"This row refers only to the behavioral experiment, not to the theoretical image scoring models.",1,N/R,"Game described involves “groups of moderate size” in Nowak & Sigmund’s model, but actual group size in Milinski et al.’s experiment is not stated here.",0.2,N/R,No explicit number of interaction rounds reported for Milinski et al.’s experiment.,0.1,0,Helping game is binary: either “aid” or “fail to aid” individuals in need; contributions are all-or-nothing help decisions.,0.9,0,Structure is help/no-help rather than continuous contributions; there is no personal endowment being automatically allocated to a public account.,0.6,N/R,Payoff technology (cost and benefit of helping) is only qualitatively described as “costly to the donor and beneficial to the recipient”; no numeric parameters.,0.1,0,No mention of communication among participants; only reputation information is exchanged via design.,0.7,1,"Reputations (image scores/standing) must be visible to others; Box 4 notes that “image scores are common knowledge” in the model, and the experiment measures effects of reputation.",0.8,0,There is no explicit punishment operator; reputational consequences occur but not identity-linked costly punishment decisions like in a public goods punishment game.,0.7,0,"While helping improves reputation, there is no separate reward action; identities of ‘rewarders’ are not specifically discussed.",0.6,N/R,The number of periods or whether subjects knew the horizon is not mentioned.,0.1,1,"Box 4: “subjects' reputations suffered even when they only refused aid to those with bad reputations…they paid a price for punishing non-cooperators”; refusing aid functions as costly, reputational punishment.",0.8,N/R,Magnitude of reputational or material cost of ‘punishing’ (refusing aid) is not numerically specified.,0.1,N/R,Exact mapping from refusal to reputation decrease (and to payoffs) is not reported.,0.1,1,"Helping others yields benefits to recipients and improves the helper's reputation, thus acting as a form of social reward.",0.7,N/R,Cost of helping per act is qualitatively described as “costly to the donor” but no numeric value is given.,0.1,N/R,Magnitude of benefit to recipient and reputational gain for helper are not quantified.,0.1,N/R,No per-round monetary or token endowment reported for this helping game.,0.1,N/R,Box 4 only states qualitative findings about reputations suffering when refusing to aid those with bad reputations; no numeric helping frequencies.,0.1,N/R,There is no continuous contribution amount; decisions are binary and their frequencies are not numerically reported.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency measures are mentioned.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff numbers reported.,0.1,N/R,Milinski et al. 2001 is only cited; location not summarized here.,0.1,N/R,Age of subjects not described in this Box.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition given.,0.1,N/R,The article does not specify whether subjects were students or from general population.,0.1,On site,"Described as controlled experiments by Milinski et al.; typical setting is a behavioral lab rather than online or field, though not explicitly stated.",0.7,"Indirect reciprocity helping game where subjects decide whether to aid individuals in need. Reputations track past helping: refusing aid to individuals with bad reputations still lowers one’s own reputation. Thus, participants incur a reputational cost when they punish non-cooperators by withholding help, with no compensating benefit, matching the predictions of Nowak and Sigmund’s image-scoring framework rather than Sugden’s standing strategy.","Summarized from Box 4 description of Milinski et al. [75]: reputational effects of helping and refusing aid, and their interpretation as altruistic punishment in indirect reciprocity.",0.95
10.1016_j.tree.2005.07.012.md,Leimar and Hammerstein – Indirect reciprocity model with image scoring (Nowak & Sigmund strategies),Box 4 describes simulations of indirect reciprocity strategies by Leimar and Hammerstein [73] implementing Nowak and Sigmund’s image scoring models; this row captures that simulation condition.,0.9,Strategy set includes image scoring strategy: help those with image score above a critical threshold and not those below; large population with multiple groups and migration.,Key independent factor in the simulations is the strategy (image scoring and related strategies) under specific population structure assumptions.,0.95,False,Described explicitly as “simulations of a large population”; no human subjects.,1,False,"This is a theoretical computer simulation, not a controlled human experiment or field trial.",1,False,No lab subjects or physical lab setting; purely computational.,1,True,Box 4: “In simulations of a large population with multiple groups and some migration...”,1,False,The described work is simulation-based rather than closed-form analytical derivation (though underlying theory exists).,0.9,N/R,Population is “large” with “multiple groups”; specific group size not reported here.,0.1,N/R,Simulations run over evolutionary time; the article does not specify discrete game rounds in this summary.,0.1,0,"Modeled decision is binary: aid vs not aid an individual in need, as per Nowak & Sigmund’s framework.",0.95,N/A,"No human players or monetary endowments; this is an evolutionary helping game, not a contribution game with endowments.",1,N/R,"Helping is described as costly to donor and beneficial to recipient, but numeric benefit/cost ratios are not given in this summary.",0.1,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,N/R,"In the model, image scores are common knowledge, but this field is specifically about what human participants see; not applicable to simulations.",0.2,N/A,No human identities; model implementation detail not captured by this variable.,1,N/A,No human subjects; identity display is not meaningful.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or human subjects to show number of rounds to.,1,1,Leimar and Hammerstein highlight that refusing aid to those with bad reputations entails a decrease in one’s own reputation: a form of altruistic punishment embedded in the strategy.,0.85,N/R,Specific cost parameters of the reputational penalty or payoff reduction are not provided in the summary.,0.1,N/R,Exact mapping from refusal to aid to changes in reputation and fitness is not numerically described.,0.1,1,Helping increases an individual’s image score (good reputation) and provides benefits to recipients; these are modeled as beneficial (reward) consequences.,0.8,N/R,"Although help is costly to the donor, actual numeric cost values are omitted.",0.1,N/R,Magnitude of benefit to the recipient and reputation increase is not specified.,0.1,N/A,There are no monetary endowments in this evolutionary simulation.,1,N/R,Simulation outcomes are summarized qualitatively (“all of the strategies discussed by Nowak and Sigmund fared poorly”) without numeric cooperation rates.,0.1,N/R,Binary helping actions; frequencies or counts not numerically reported.,0.1,N/R,Summary does not provide efficiency or average payoff values for populations or strategies.,0.1,N/R,No explicit group payoff values reported.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly a computer simulation of strategies in a large population.,1,"Evolutionary simulation of indirect reciprocity strategies in a large population distributed across multiple groups with some migration. Individuals intermittently face opportunities to aid others at a cost, with benefits to recipients. Image scoring strategy: help those with image score above a threshold (good reputation), do not help those below (bad reputation). Refusing to aid those with bad reputations still reduces one’s own image score, making such punishment altruistic. Under this population structure, all of the strategies discussed by Nowak and Sigmund perform poorly.",Summarized from Box 4 discussion of Leimar and Hammerstein’s simulations and Nowak & Sigmund’s image scoring dynamics.,0.95
10.1016_j.tree.2005.07.012.md,Leimar and Hammerstein – Indirect reciprocity model with Sugden-style standing strategy,Box 4 contrasts Nowak & Sigmund’s image scoring with Sugden’s suggested standing strategy and notes that this latter strategy does well and is evolutionarily stable in Leimar and Hammerstein’s scenario.,0.9,Strategy: standing strategy where helping those with good reputation is rewarded and refusing aid to those with bad reputation does not decrease one’s own reputation.,"The crucial manipulation in the model is the reputational rule: under standing strategy, refusing help to bad-reputation individuals is not reputationally costly.",0.97,False,Described in Box 4 as a strategy evaluated within the same simulation framework; no human subjects.,1,False,"This is an evolutionary simulation, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field subjects; purely theoretical-computational.,1,True,"Box 4: “In Leimar and Hammerstein's population scenario, this strategy does well and is evolutionarily stable.” This refers to simulation within the same modeled population.",1,False,Reported result is from simulation in a “population scenario”; not presented here as an analytical proof.,0.9,N/R,Uses the same “large population with multiple groups and some migration” as the prior simulation; exact group size is not given.,0.1,N/R,Evolutionary time rather than explicit rounds; not specified.,0.1,0,"As with the image scoring simulation, decision is binary: aid vs not aid.",0.95,N/A,No human endowments; evolutionary helping interactions.,1,N/R,Qualitative cost-benefit structure only; no numeric benefit/cost ratio provided.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/R,"Reputations are part of the model state, but not ‘shown’ to human subjects; this variable is not conceptually applicable.",0.2,N/A,No human punishers or punished; identity display is not meaningful.,1,N/A,No human reward identities.,1,N/A,No experimental interface to subjects.,1,1,"Refusing help to those with bad reputations denies them benefits and may be seen as punishment, though crucially it is not reputationally costly to the punisher under this strategy.",0.7,N/R,The cost (if any) of refusing help in terms of fitness is not numerically specified in this summary; reputational cost is explicitly zero for this action.,0.2,N/R,Magnitude of penalty to recipients when refused aid is not quantified.,0.1,1,Helping those with good reputations improves one’s own standing and benefits recipients; these mechanisms function as rewards.,0.85,N/R,Cost of helping acts is qualitatively described but not parametrized.,0.1,N/R,Benefit to recipients and reputational gain to helpers under standing strategy are not given numerically.,0.1,N/A,No monetary payoffs or endowments; evolutionary fitness framework.,1,N/R,Summary reports qualitative result (“does well and is evolutionarily stable”) but not explicit cooperation frequencies.,0.1,N/R,Binary help decisions; numeric levels not reported.,0.1,N/R,No explicit efficiency or average payoff reported in the text.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff statistics are not summarized.,0.1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulation of strategies in a modeled population.,1,"Within the same large-population, multi-group, migration framework used to test image scoring, Sugden’s standing strategy is introduced: individuals help those with good reputations, and refusing aid to individuals with bad reputations does not reduce the actor’s own reputation. Under this rule, cooperation via indirect reciprocity is stable; the standing strategy does well and is evolutionarily stable in Leimar and Hammerstein’s simulations.","Summarized from Box 4 statement: Sugden’s strategy where failing to aid bad-reputation individuals does not hurt one’s own reputation, and its performance in Leimar and Hammerstein’s population scenario.",0.95
10.1016_j.jtbi.2005.01.006.md,One-parameter model – baseline fluctuating dynamics,"Main agent-based model in Section 2 and 3 with single acceptance threshold ti, empathy (offer = own threshold), finite s, random matching and death-birth after s games.",0.9,"Selection interval s (games between death–birth events), population size N, stake per game M, mutation rate on acceptance threshold ti","Paper systematically varies s (1, 10000), N (10–10000, with N=1000 as main example), M (always 100 in reported figures), mutation rate (1/3, 1/30, 1/300, 1/3000) while keeping the basic game and evolutionary rule fixed.",0.9,False,Model is explicitly described as an agent-based simulation with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; purely computational evolution of artificial agents.,1,False,No lab or field setting; simulation only.,1,True,"They state 'we introduce an agent-based model' and report outcomes from simulations with varying N, s, mutation rates.",1,False,"Some analytical discussion, but main results are from numerical simulations of the agent-based model, not closed-form derivations.",0.9,1000,Representative parameter choice in figures: 'Population size is N=1000' for the main dynamics (Figs. 1–3). Other N are explored but 1000 is the detailed case.,0.8,10000000,"Fig. 1: distributions shown 'after 2, 7.5 and 10 million games'; treat 10 million games as the simulated horizon for the main run.",0.7,1,"Ultimatum proposer can offer any amount between 1 and M; continuous (discrete integer) action space, not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,"This is an Ultimatum Game offer, not a public goods contribution; field not applicable to this non-contribution game and non-human simulation.",1,N/R,MPCR concept applies to linear public goods; not defined or computable for Ultimatum bargaining in this paper.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between agents is not modelled.,1,N/A,Interface/feedback concepts do not apply to artificial agents without UI.,1,N/A,"No explicit punishment stage or identity revelation; rejection is endogenous to the game, and model has no identities shown to humans.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or human interface.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are not informed of total games.,1,0,"No separate costly punishment stage; 'altruistic punishment' corresponds to rejecting low offers within the Ultimatum structure, not a distinct punishment technology.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism beyond standard Ultimatum rejection; no per-unit punishment cost parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment multiplier defined.,1,0,Agents do not have a separate costly rewarding option; they just make offers and accept/reject.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,100,Stake per game is M=100: 'the capital to be shared per game is M=100'. In Ultimatum Game this is the amount the proposer allocates.,1,N/R,"Outcome reported is distribution and mean of acceptance thresholds and offers, not normalized contributions in a public goods sense.",1,N/R,"They report distributions of ti and offers oi, but not average offer amounts as a single numeric DV suitable for this field.",0.6,N/R,No aggregate efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum) is numerically reported.,0.7,N/R,They discuss fitness qualitatively but do not report numeric mean group payoffs.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants; age not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; gender not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; education not applicable.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based model with no empirical data collection.,1,"Ultimatum Game with stake M=100 each game. Population of N agents; in each game a random ordered pair (proposer, responder) is drawn. Each agent i has an integer acceptance threshold ti (1 ≤ ti ≤ M). In one-parameter empathy version, proposer offers the minimum amount she would accept, i.e., offer = ti. Responder accepts offer x if x ≥ ti, otherwise rejects; if accepted, payoffs are x to responder and M−x to proposer; if rejected, both get 0. Fitness is accumulated capital across games. After s games, the agent(s) with minimum fitness is removed and replaced by a copy of the maximum-fitness agent with same fitness and threshold, subject to mutation: new agent’s ti can change by ±1 with probability 1/3 each, or stay the same with probability 1/3. No memory, no repeated-game strategies, no reputation, no explicit group structure.",Summarized directly from Section 2 'One-parameter model' description of the game and evolutionary dynamics.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2005.01.006.md,Two-parameter model – independent offer and acceptance thresholds,"Extension in Section 5 introducing independent strategical variable oi (offer) in addition to acceptance threshold ti, with same evolutionary dynamics.",0.9,Presence of independent offer threshold oi in addition to acceptance threshold ti; selection interval s=1000; population size N=1000; stake per game M=100; mutation rate on both ti and oi,"Section 5: 'assign agents a new strategical variable, oi... subject to the same mutation rules as the acceptance threshold, ti.' Figure 3 specifies N=1000, M=100, s=1000, initial ti=oi=1.",0.9,False,Again an agent-based model only.,1,False,Computational simulation; no empirical experimental control with humans.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely simulated agents.,1,True,They explicitly run simulations for this two-parameter agent model as shown in Fig. 3.,1,False,No closed-form solution of the two-parameter dynamics; only simulation results.,0.9,1000,Fig. 3 caption: 'Population size is N=1000'.,1,100000000,Fig. 3 shows distributions 'after 50 million games and 100 million games'; take 100 million as the simulation length discussed.,0.8,1,Proposer’s offer oi is an integer from 1 to M; not restricted to full or zero.,1,N/A,Ultimatum bargaining in a simulation; contribution-proportion notion not applicable.,1,N/R,No linear public goods structure; MPCR not defined.,1,N/A,No humans; no communication interface.,1,N/A,No user-facing summaries; artificial agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants; no identity display.,1,N/A,No identity display of rewards.,1,N/A,Agents are not given information about game horizon.,1,0,"As in the one-parameter case, no separate costly punishment technology beyond the inherent rejection option of the Ultimatum game.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology with tunable costs.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism; only offers and accept/reject decisions.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,100,Fig. 3 caption: 'the capital to be shared per game is M=100'.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported are distributions and time series of acceptance thresholds and offers, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,They plot distributions of offers oi but do not provide a single numeric mean offer suitable here.,0.6,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio between realized and maximum possible payoffs is reported.,0.7,N/R,Group payoffs are not tabulated; focus is on distributions of strategies.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same Ultimatum Game structure as in the one-parameter model with M=100 per game and random ordered pairs. Each agent i now has two integer strategy parameters: acceptance threshold ti and offer amount oi. When chosen as proposer, agent i offers oi (not constrained to equal ti). When responder, agent accepts an offer x if x ≥ ti, otherwise rejects; accepted offers yield payoffs x to responder and M−x to proposer, rejected offers yield 0 to both. Evolutionary dynamics is unchanged: after every s=1000 games, the least fit agent is removed and replaced by a copy of the fittest agent, and both ti and oi of the offspring can mutate by ±1 with probability 1/3 each (or remain unchanged with probability 1/3). Simulations start with fully rational agents (ti=oi=1). Results show that offers typically exceed acceptance thresholds and that the joint distribution of ti and oi does not converge to full rationality within simulated time.",Summarized from Section 5 description and Fig. 3 caption.,1
10.1016_j.paid.2010.05.002.md,"Replicator dynamics – parameter sweep over r, u, β, N","Paper analyzes deterministic evolutionary (replicator) dynamics of N-person snowdrift game with disguise and punishment for varying parameter sets (r, u, β, N, etc.), not separated into discrete experimental treatments with human subjects; treated as one analytical/simulation study.",0.88,"Cost-to-benefit ratio u; disguise-cost-to-benefit ratio r; punishment cost α; punishment fine β (and multiplicative factor β/α); punishment and disguise probabilities p and q; group size N; initial frequencies (x, y, z).","Model outcomes are explored as functions of u, r, β (or β/α), N, and fixed α, p, q, with different initial conditions; these are the parameters varied across figures.",0.93,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical evolutionary game analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment; all results are from mathematical analysis and numerical iteration of differential and Markov processes.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation with participants; only theory and computation.,1,True,Uses numerical iteration of replicator dynamics and Markov mutation-selection dynamics (agent counts) without human subjects.,0.98,True,Derives closed-form payoff functions and replicator equations; analyzes equilibria and stability analytically.,0.98,4,"Competing group size N is varied; explicit examples include N=4 and N=5 (e.g., Fig. 1(a),(b) N=4, Fig. 1(c) N=5). A single canonical value cannot represent all runs; 4 is chosen as a representative but heterogeneity exists.",0.4,1,"Snowdrift interaction is a one-shot N-person game; evolutionary change occurs across generations, not repeated rounds within a game.",0.7,1,"Players choose pure strategies C, D, or P; there is no continuous contribution level; the binary cooperate/defect/punish choice corresponds to an all-or-nothing style contribution decision.",0.75,N/A,Not a public-goods contribution model with an endowment allocation; purely payoff-based snowdrift formulation; also no human subjects.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return for a PGG is not defined; model uses benefit b and total cost c with cost-to-benefit ratio u=c/b; no MPCR reported.,1,N/A,No communication or interface; theoretical model without human interaction.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback; population-level payoffs computed analytically.,1,N/A,No notion of player identity visibility; punishment is anonymous in the model and there are no human subjects.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented in this model.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or rounds shown to participants; purely theoretical dynamics.,1,1,Punishers (P) pay cost α and impose fine β on defectors; peer punishment is central to the model.,1,0.01,"Punisher cost per punishment α is given as α=0.01 in multiple figures (e.g., Figs. 1–3 and 5).",0.95,5,"Multiplicative factor is β/α; for β=0.05 and α=0.01, β/α=5. This is the common baseline combination used (e.g., Figs. 1(a),(c), 3(a), 5).",0.9,0,Only punishment and disguise are modeled; no reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model framed in terms of benefit b and cost c; no discrete per-player endowment specified as in experimental economics.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes reported as frequencies of strategies (x, y, z) and total cooperation f_C^Total=x+z; no normalized contribution rate relative to an endowment is defined.",0.7,N/R,"Model does not specify token contributions; cooperation means sharing labor cost c, but no numeric per-player contribution amount is tabulated as a dependent variable.",0.6,N/R,"They discuss cooperation density and strategy frequencies, not explicit efficiency defined as payoff relative to maximum.",0.9,N/R,"Although individual payoffs P_C, P_D, P_P are defined, aggregate group payoff statistics are not reported as a main outcome.",0.9,N/A,No participants; theoretical and simulation study only.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Work consists of mathematical analysis and computer simulations with no human subjects.,1,"N-person snowdrift game in a well-mixed population with three pure strategies: cooperation (C), defection with possible disguise (D), and punishment (P). Payoffs depend on cost-to-benefit ratio u=c/b, disguise-cost-to-benefit ratio r=r'/b, punishment cost α=α'/b, punishment fine β=β'/b, disguise probability q, punishment probability p, group size N, and composition (n_c, n_d, n_p). Evolutionary dynamics studied via replicator equations in infinite populations and a mutation-selection Markov process in finite populations of size M (e.g., M=1200).","Summarizes the formal game structure and dynamics as described in Sections 2, 3, and 5.",0.97
