10.1038_srep01735.md,Illustrative experiment – Self-monitoring (control),"Paper describes three experimental conditions in the MIT 'Friends and Family' study: self-monitoring (control), peer-view, and peer-reward. This row captures the self-monitoring condition.",1,Incentive mechanism: participants rewarded for their own increased physical activity; no buddy info shown.,"Condition is defined by the type of reward/information structure; here only self-reward, no peer visibility.",1,True,Experiment with human participants in the Friends and Family study.,1,True,Different incentive mechanisms were randomly assigned and compared prospectively.,0.9,False,"Study conducted in participants’ real-world environment via mobile phones, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"This condition involves human subjects, not only simulated agents.",1,False,Not a purely mathematical/analytical model; it’s an implemented field experiment.,1,2,"Each individual was assigned two peers/buddies in the overall design; even though buddies are not used strategically here, the dyadic structure is still 1 focal + 2 peers.",0.4,20,Post-intervention behavior was monitored for 20 days; treated as 20 repeated periods.,0.6,1,"Physical activity is continuous; participants can vary activity level, not binary choices.",1,N/A,This is not a contribution game with an endowment/public good structure.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined in the description.,1,0,No mention that participants could communicate within the app as part of the mechanism.,0.7,0,"In self-monitoring, only own activity and own rewards are mentioned; no peer or group summaries.",0.8,0,No punishment mechanism was used.,1,1,Participants knew they personally were rewarded for their own activity; identity is trivially their own.,0.7,0,No mention that subjects were informed of the total or remaining days used for analysis.,0.5,0,Only rewards for increased activity are described; no penalties.,1,N/A,No punishment 