10.1038_srep01735.md,Illustrative example – Pigouvian mechanism (analytical model),Analytical illustration of Pigouvian reward mechanism in a 100-agent homogeneous model; not an empirical experiment.,1,Mechanism type: Pigouvian direct reward for reduced electricity consumption.,Scenario distinguishes Pigouvian vs social mechanism analytically; this row is the Pigouvian case.,1,False,This is a fully specified analytical/numerical example with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled assignment to human subjects; purely theoretical.,1,False,No lab or field context; just a model.,1,False,"Described with closed-form functions, not as Monte Carlo or agent-based simulation; primarily analytical.",0.8,True,Utility and externality functions specified and equilibria computed analytically (with some numeric evaluation).,1,100,"Illustrative example: 'Consider a homogeneous set of agents N={1,...,100}'.",1,1,Static consumption choice model; no repeated periods.,1,1,Agents choose continuous consumption level x_i ≥ 0.,1,N/A,No initial endowment nor contribution decision; continuous consumption choice.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human agents; communication not applicable.,1,0,Analytical model does not include feedback about others’ earnings/consumption beyond externalities encoded in v_i.,1,N/A,No punishment or identity in non-human analytical setting.,1,N/A,No identities; mechanism is modeled abstractly.,1,0,One-shot model; no rounds to show.,1,0,Only rewards/subsidies; no peer or institutional punishment in this example.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Pigouvian mechanism directly rewards agents for reduced consumption.,1,N/R,Total reward budget (3995.40) is given but not normalized per-unit; marginal reward per unit not explicitly stated in this illustrative numerical example.,0.4,N/R,No explicit mapping of units of reward to changes in payoffs beyond qualitative description.,0.3,N/R,No standard endowment; agents choose a consumption level with price 4 per unit.,1,N/R,"Focus is on consumption levels vs social optimum, not contribution fractions.",1,31.19,"Under Pigouvian mechanism, consumption is reduced to the socially optimal level x_i^o=31.19 for all i.",1,N/R,Social surplus values versus maximum are discussed qualitatively; no numeric efficiency ratio reported.,0.6,399.54,Total redistribution loss under Pigouvian is 399.54; used here as an aggregate cost/negative payoff indicator.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical model of 100 agents.,1,"100 homogeneous agents, each with 10 peers. Raw utility u_i(x_i)=12 x_i^0.8 − 4 x_i; externality v_i(y)=0.0001 y^1.5; price 4 per unit; marginal cost of peer pressure c=1; redistribution loss δ=0.1. Without peer pressure equilibrium consumption x_i*=79.61; socially optimal x_i^o=31.19. Pigouvian mechanism reduces consumption to 31.19 with reward budget 3995.40 and redistribution loss 399.54; no peer pressure in equilibrium.",Directly summarized from the illustrative example section.,1
10.1038_srep01735.md,Illustrative example – Social mechanism (analytical model),Analytical illustrative example with the same 100-agent setting under the social (peer-reward) mechanism.,1,Mechanism type: social mechanism rewarding peers for others’ reduced electricity consumption.,Contrasted with Pigouvian mechanism in same analytical environment.,1,False,"Analytical/numerical model only, no human subjects.",1,False,Not a controlled human experiment.,1,False,Pure theoretical illustration.,1,False,"Model is closed-form; calculations appear deterministic, not simulation-based.",0.8,True,Uses explicit utility and externality functions to derive budgets and losses analytically.,1,100,Same as in the illustrative example: 100 agents.,1,1,Single-shot consumption decision.,1,1,Continuous consumption choice.,1,N/A,No endowment-sharing structure.,1,N/R,Not a PGG; externalities captured via v_i.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,0,Model describes rewards and peer pressure but not explicit information feedback beyond utility structure.,1,N/A,No punishment and no identities.,1,N/A,Abstract agents; no identities to show.,1,0,Static model.,1,0,"Mechanism uses rewards to peers, not punishments.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,Peers are rewarded based on others’ consumption levels.,1,N/R,"Total reward budget is given (1095.26), but not marginal per-unit reward; only shape of reward function (affine) is defined in theory.",0.4,N/R,Reward function r_{ji}(x_j) is specified symbolically; no scalar tech factor derived.,0.4,N/R,No standard endowment; agents choose consumption.,1,N/R,No contribution to a public good is modeled.,1,31.19,Social mechanism is designed so equilibrium action equals the socially optimal consumption x_i^o=31.19.,1,N/R,Qualitative statements that social mechanism reaches optimum at lower budget; no explicit numeric efficiency given.,0.6,192.03,Combined total social cost and redistribution cost under the social mechanism is 192.03; used here as the mechanism’s total loss measure.,0.5,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Analytical multi-agent model only.,1,"Same 100-agent homogeneous setting. With peer pressure allowed and an appropriately designed social reward r_{ji}(x_j)=(α_j+β_i)(x_j−x_j^*), consumption can be reduced to the socially optimal level with reward budget 1095.26. Social cost of exerting peer pressure is 82.50; combined redistribution plus social cost is 192.03, about half the redistribution loss under Pigouvian mechanism.",Summary of the social mechanism part of the illustrative numerical example.,1
10.1073_pnas.1215443110.md,Study 1 – Similarity focus,Study 1 manipulates cognitive focus (similarities vs. differences); this condition is the similarity-focus treatment.,1,Cognitive focus induced before the game: instructed to list similarities between two pictures (similarity focus).,"Paper describes that about half the participants listed similarities, inducing a similarity focus prior to the cooperation game.",1,True,Human participants in university lectures played the game for real monetary stakes.,1,True,Random assignment to similarity vs. dissimilarity focus with controlled instructions in a cooperation game.,1,True,Conducted in university lecture halls with controlled procedures and anonymous matching; standard lab-style environment.,0.95,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all decisions by human participants.,1,False,No formal closed-form or purely theoretical model as the primary method.,1,2,Described as a two-person cooperation game with each participant paired with one anonymous coplayer.,1,1,Single-shot interaction; each participant engaged in a single-shot cooperation game with one other.,1,1,"Participants could transfer “any portion” of their €6 endowment (0–6), not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is initially fully in the private account and participants choose how much to transfer; nothing starts in public/shared form by default.,1,1,"Each euro sent is doubled for the recipient only, not shared; marginal return to receiver is 2, to sender is -1; for total group payoff, each € transferred changes total by +1 per euro, so MPCR on group output relative to sender’s cost is effectively 1. This is not a standard public good, but closest consistent value is 1.",0.4,0,Instructions emphasized no communication; no mention of any chat or communication channel.,1,0,Participants only know transfers and induced payoffs within their pair; no mention of aggregate summaries of others’ earnings or punishment.,0.8,0,Matching was anonymous and in large lecture halls; no identities linked to punishment decisions.,0.95,0,"No explicit rewarding stage; only punishment, so there was no identity revelation about rewards.",1,1,Described as a single-shot interaction; subjects necessarily know there is only one round.,0.9,1,Phase 2 is a punishment phase where participants can pay to reduce the opponent’s payoff.,1,0.5,“Every reduction of €1 in the coplayer’s payoff reduced the participant’s own payoff by €0.50.” Thus cost per 1 unit reduction is 0.5.,1,1,Each €1 of reduction corresponds to 1 unit decrease in opponent’s payoff; hence marginal impact (tech) is 1 per punishment unit.,0.9,0,No stage where participants can pay to increase others’ payoff; only cooperation transfer and punishment reduction.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in this game.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in this game.,1,6,In phase 1 participants receive an initial endowment of €6.,1,N/R,Only average cooperation levels in euros are reported; no explicit normalized rate (0–1) is given or can be uniquely tied to this condition.,0.2,3.74,Text reports average cooperation level as 3.74 for similarity-focus vs. 3.04 for dissimilarity-focus in Study 1.,0.95,N/R,No measure of total payoffs relative to maximum is reported.,0,N/R,No raw group payoff figures are reported.,0,Germany,"Participants recruited at University of Cologne; paper notes locations in Cologne, Germany.",1,N/R,No age information is provided.,0,N/R,No gender composition is reported.,0,University students in introductory economics lecture,Study 1 recruited 106 participants in an introductory economics lecture at the University of Cologne.,1,On site,"Conducted physically in lecture halls with paper materials and envelopes, not online.",1,"Two-phase, two-player cooperation and punishment game. Phase 1: each receives €6 and can transfer any amount (0–6) to coplayer; any euro sent is doubled for the recipient. Phase 2: each receives additional €3 and can spend it to reduce opponent’s payoff by €0–6 at a cost of €0.50 per €1 reduction. Conditional punishment schedule elicited for all possible cooperation levels (0–6); actual punishment applied based on partner’s actual cooperation.",Direct description from materials and methods section common to both studies.,1
