an universities (Cologne and Düsseldorf).,1,N/R,Age statistics absent.,0,N/R,No details on gender composition.,0,University students in introductory business and economics lectures,Study 2 sample drawn from introductory business and economics lectures at two universities.,1,On site,Study run in lecture halls with paper-based materials.,1,"Identical to in-group condition: two-phase, two-player cooperation and punishment game with €6 initial endowment, doubling of transfers, and €3 punishment endowment with cost 0.5 per 1 euro reduction.",Study 2 uses same payoff equation and game structure across conditions.,1
10.1086_683024.md,"Mpimbwe helping network (Mtakula subvillage, 2010)",Single observational study of helping relationships among 136 adult household heads in Mtakula subvillage; no experimental or treatment conditions are distinguished.,1,"Dyadic attributes: genealogical relatedness category (1–4), contingency/reciprocity measures, geographic distance between households, affinal relationship (dummy), giver/receiver age, giver/receiver wealth index, household neediness (consumer–producer ratio), need difference, gender (giver/receiver), and others as listed in Table 1.",Paper explicitly lists these as explanatory variables for helping scope and contingency (Table 1 and methods).,1,True,Data collected via interviews with 136 adult household heads in 2010.,1,False,No random assignment or experimental manipulation; purely observational social-network study.,1,False,"Study conducted in a rural Tanzanian village, not in a laboratory or controlled lab-like setting.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; all analyses use observed network data.,1,False,"While using statistical models, the paper does not present a formal closed-form analytical model as a standalone method.",1,136,136 adult household heads interviewed and included in the 136×136 helping-scope matrix.,1,1,Single cross-sectional survey over a defined recent-recall window; no repeated game rounds.,0.9,1,"Helping is reported as whether help occurred in each of several domains, not constrained to an all-or-nothing choice in a formal game.",0.8,N/A,No endowment or contribution game; observational helping behavior only.,1,N/R,No public-goods or payoff-multiplier structure is defined.,1,1,Helping occurs in a natural village context with unrestricted communication; no imposed communication restrictions.,0.7,1,"Villagers have informal knowledge of others’ helping, wealth, and behavior via everyday interaction and gossip, though no formal summaries are presented by researchers.",0.6,1,Punishment through witchcraft accusations is socially known and attributed to specific kin/villagers.,0.7,1,Acts of help are dyadic and personally known; helpers and recipients know each other’s identities.,0.9,0,There is no fixed number of interaction rounds communicated; social interaction is ongoing.,1,1,Paper repeatedly notes punishment of uncooperative kin through witchcraft accusations.,1,N/R,"No quantitative cost per unit of punishment (e.g., tokens, time) specified.",1,N/R,No numeric mapping from punisher’s investment to punished loss specified; only qualitative witchcraft sanctions discussed.,1,1,Helping itself is a form of social reward/benefit to recipients.,0.9,N/R,"No standardized unit cost of helping (e.g., in tokens) is quantified, only qualitative costs like time and resources.",1,N/R,No formal payoff technology mapping help to payoffs is specified.,1,N/R,No experimental endowment of tokens or money; only real-life wealth and resources measured.,1,N/R,No normalized contribution rate is defined; main DV is helping scope (count of help domains in a dyad).,1,N/R,"Raw magnitudes of help (amount of food, money, hours of labor) are not reported as a primary DV; only whether help occurred in given domains.",1,N/R,No measure of group efficiency relative to a theoretical maximum payoff is defined.,1,N/R,"No aggregate monetary or token payoff per group is reported; outcomes are described qualitatively (e.g., food security).",1,Tanzania,"Study site described as an African village in Mpimbwe, Rukwa Valley, western Tanzania.",1,Mean age 36 years (range 18–73) for interviewed adults.,Methods: sample consisted of adults with mean age 36 and range 18–73.,1,"85 women, 51 men (adult household heads).",Methods: sample consisted of 85 women and 51 men.,1,Majority had attended but not completed primary education.,Methods: 'the majority of individuals had followed but not completed primary education.',1,Field experiment,"Although non-experimental, it is an empirical field study of naturally occurring behavior in a real village; closest category is 'Field experiment' rather than lab or observational with no structured instrument.",0.7,"Observational social-network study of helping: 136×136 asymmetric helping-scope matrix constructed from 10 domains of help (food sharing, illness assistance, agricultural labor, child care, interest-free loans, advice, etc.), combining reports of giving and receiving. Contingency (reciprocity) measured as correlation between helping-scope matrix and its transpose. Additional matrices built for kinship categories, geographic distance between households (meters), wealth index (PCA of assets, productivity, and income), household neediness (consumer–producer ratio), affinal ties, age, gender. Analyses use bivariate QAP correlations, Poisson MRQAP (with and without hurdle models), and logistic MRQAP to predict helping scope and contingency.","Summary of core design, variables, and analytical approach from Methods and Results sections.",1
