 reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Participants are college students.,0.8,On site,Lab-based economic experiment.,0.9,"Same 4-person VCM with mutual monitoring and costly punishment as the increasing-price condition, but punishment price per 1 EMU destroyed follows a decreasing sequence {4, 2, 1, 0.5, 0.25} across five 3-period blocks.",Restates manipulation with reversed price order.,1
10.1007_s12110-003-1003-3.md,Study One – Shuar village status and altruism correlations,Single observational correlational study in Study One; no separate treatment conditions are defined.,0.97,"Individual differences in engagement in pro-community activities: years and rank-weighted time in public office (Public Office), attendance record at community meetings and work sessions (Attendance), and perceived work effort in mingas (Work Effort).",These three variables are explicitly described as predictor variables used to explain perceived altruism and social status.,1,True,"Data collected from 25 adult male human residents through interviews, pile sorts, and archival attendance records.",1,False,No random assignment or experimental manipulation; purely correlational/observational field study.,1,False,"Study conducted in a natural village setting, not in a controlled laboratory.",1,False,No computer or numerical simulations are described.,1,False,No formal mathematical or closed-form modeling; only statistical analysis of field data.,1,25,Study population consisted of 25 adult male village residents.,1,1,Single cross-sectional measurement of variables; no repeated strategic rounds like in a game.,0.7,1,"There is no discrete contribution game; contributions (attendance, effort, office holding) vary continuously across individuals, which is more analogous to a continuous contribution setting than all-or-nothing.",0.5,N/R,No endowment or initial allocation structure is defined; this is not a token-based contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods payoff function or multiplier is specified; only naturalistic community work and offices.,1,1,Villagers interact and communicate freely in meetings and daily life; no experimental restriction on communication.,0.7,1,"Community members monitor others’ attendance, office holding, and work effort; perceived altruism and status ratings rely on such social information.",0.9,1,"Respect and status allocations, as well as formal fines for 