ose who are perceived to respect the most altruistic President (sanctioners) are themselves higher in social status; no explicit public goods payoff structure or token-based game.,Summary of design details given under Study Two Method and Result.,1
10.1126_science.1202599.md,"Mostar public goods game – Segregated schools, homogeneous groups, no sanctions","One of the eight cells of the 2×2×2 factorial: segregated vs integrated schools, homogeneous vs mixed groups, sanctions vs no sanctions; this condition is segregated, homogeneous, no sanctions.",0.9,Institutional context: segregated schools; Group composition: mono-ethnic (homogeneous); Sanctioning: not available,"Paper states three between-session manipulations (institution type, diversity, sanctions) forming 2×2×2 design and that each session falls into one of eight possible conditions.",1,True,Human high-school students in Mostar played the game.,1,True,Authors describe it as a field experiment with randomization of treatment conditions.,1,False,"Conducted in real-world school/field setting in Bosnia, not a conventional laboratory.",0.8,False,No computer or agent-based simulation is described.,1,False,No formal purely analytical model; focus is on empirical data.,1,4,"Explicitly: “all players (four in each round, in this case)”.",1,20,Five practice rounds followed by 20 rounds during the session; the 20 are the paid/analyzed part.,1,1,"Contribution gi ∈ [0, yi] indicates a continuous choice from zero up to the endowment, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Standard public goods game: endowment is in private account at start; no mention of any automatic allocation to public good.,0.9,0.3,Contributions are summed and increased by 20% (multiplied by 1.2) then divided equally among 4; MPCR = 1.2 / 4 = 0.3.,1,0,"No mention of communication; typical design with anonymous, simultaneous choices implies no chat allowed.",0.8,1,"For sanctions to be meaningful, players must receive information on others’ contributions; text: “players receive information on each others' contribution and then decide to impose sanctions”. Assumed same visibility even when sanctions disabled, to isolate effect of sanctions’ availability.",0.7,0,"Groups are anonymous with “identities of the group members withheld, except for information about ethnicity”, so individual identities of punishers/sanctioned are not revealed.",0.8,0,"No reward mechanism exists in the design, only sanctions.",1,1,"In repeated PGG experiments with fixed 20 rounds, participants are normally told number of rounds; not stated explicitly but is standard and needed for consent and payment expectations.",0.6,0,This condition is explicitly described as “were permitted to use sanctions or were not permitted to use sanctions”; here it is the no-sanctions cell.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,0,Only costly punishment/sanctions are discussed; no rewarding or positive sanctions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,N/R,Endowment yi is mentioned but its numeric value in KM is not given.,0.1,N/R,Only differences in KM contributions are reported; no normalized rates by endowment.,0,N/R,"Results state marginal effects (e.g., 1.46 KM differences) but not absolute mean contributions by condition.",0.1,N/R,No efficiency or ratio of realized to maximal payoffs is reported.,0,N/R,Only individual contribution levels and sanction amounts are analyzed; payoffs are not tabulated.,0,Bosnia-Herzegovina,"Experiments conducted in Mostar, Bosnia-Herzegovina.",1,High-school students; exact ages N/R,Participants are students from three participating high schools; typical ages ~15–19 but not specified.,0.5,N/R,No information on gender composition is provided.,0,Secondary school students (high school level),Explicitly: “Two of the four segregated high schools” and “students of the three participating high schools”.,1,Field experiment,Described as bringing a controlled experiment to a relevant field setting and explicitly as a field experiment.,1,"Standard linear public goods game with 4 players, continuous contributions from 0 to endowment; sum of contributions multiplied by 1.2 and evenly redistributed. G