utional context with mixed-ethnicity groups and sanctions. Main results emphasize that in integrated groups, availability of sanctions significantly increases contributions (e.g., 1.78 KM per round) and that sanctioning responds to under-contribution under integration.",From discussion of sanctions having a large substantive effect in presence of institutions of integration and table S5 description.,1
10.1073_pnas.1320451111.md,Control treatment (CTR) – Stage 1 PD without third party,Paper describes a control treatment (CTR) where 554 children play a one-shot PD without any third party; this is stage 1 of CTR and serves as baseline.,1,"Third-party punishment availability: absent; Role: PD player; Stage: 1 (baseline, no prior third-party experience)",Key manipulation is presence vs. absence of a third party with punishment power; this row is the no-third-party baseline.,1,True,Human child participants played incentivized games.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled manipulation of punishment institution.,1,True,"Conducted in school rooms with one-on-one instructions, pen-and-paper; standard lab-in-the-field style but under experimental control; classified as on-site lab.",0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulations; all decisions by children.,1,False,No purely theoretical or closed-form model presented as a separate study.,1,2,"Prisoner’s dilemma is explicitly two-player: “we let our subjects play a one-shot, simultaneous prisoner's dilemma (PD) game… see Fig. 1.”",1,1,"One-shot PD: “one-shot, simultaneous prisoner's dilemma (PD) game… played the game once.”",1,0,Action set is binary cooperate (pass both tokens) or defect (keep both); no continuous contribution choice; coded 0 for all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each child is endowed with two tokens that they can keep or pass; endowment starts fully in private account.,1,N/R,"This is a 2x2 PD with discrete payoffs, not a linear public good; marginal per capita return is not defined or reported.",0.2,0,No mention of any communication between matched children; they are from different schools and matched anonymously.,0.9,0,Children are not informed about partner’s decisions until months later; no earnings or punishment summary displays are described.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in CTR; also all matching is anonymous so identities are not revealed in any case.,1,0,"No direct peer reward mechanism; only tokens-to-gifts exchange with experimenters, no identities revealed.",1,1,Children are clearly told the game is played once (one-shot); so the total number of rounds is known.,0.9,0,Control PD is ‘without any third party’; no punishment option for partner or third party.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No institution to reward partners or third parties; only own tokens exchangeable for gifts.,1,N/A,No explicit peer reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit peer reward mechanism.,1,2,"“One child and his or her partner were endowed with two tokens each, which could be either kept or passed on to the other player.”",1,0.246,"Contribution is cooperating (passing both tokens). Overall cooperation rate reported as 24.6% ≈ 0.246 in CTR; each player can only contribute either 0 or all, so rate equals cooperation probability.",1,N/R,Raw mean tokens passed per player are not directly reported; only fraction cooperating is given.,0.2,N/R,"They report expected payoff (2.49 tokens), but not normalized by theoretical maximum of 4 tokens; efficiency ratio is not given.",0.3,N/R,"Only individual expected payoff (2.49) is reported, not group payoff; group payoff could be 4.98 but that is not explicitly stated, so we do not impute.",0.3,Italy,"Experiment conducted in all 14 elementary schools in Merano, South Tyrol, Italy.",1,7–11 years (grades 2–5 primary school),“We worked with children in grades 2–5… aged 7 to 11 y.”,1,Mixed; approximately equal share of boys and girls (exact proportions not reported),They mention controlling for gender in regressions but do not report distribution.,0.4,"Primary school students (grades 2–5) in Merano, Italy",Experiment in all 14 elementary schools; grades 2–5 only.,1,On site,Conducted in school buildings in separate rooms with experimenters and children physically present.,1,"Two-player one-shot PD implemented as a binary cooperation (send both tokens) vs defection (keep both); passed tokens to partner are doubled. Payoff matrix: (C,C) -> (4,4); (D,C) -> (6,0); (C,D) -> (0,6); (D,D) -> (2,2). Decisions incentivized with tokens convertible to gifts after ~3 months. Beliefs about partner cooperation elicited and rewarded with 1 token for correct guess.","Summarized from the description of the PD, payoff table (Fig. 1), and methods section.",1
10.1073_pnas.1320451111.md,Control treatment (CTR) – Stage 2 third-party observer,"In stage 2 of CTR, the same children from CTR act as third-party observers with a punishment option for players in TPP.",1,"Role: third-party observer; Punishment availability: yes (binary, altruistic only); Punishment decision via strategy method",Independent manipulation here is the role and the presence of a punishment token for observers; they decide conditionally on PD player’s defection.,1,True,Human children making incentivized punishment decisions.,1,True,Same randomized experimental setup; this is stage 2 of CTR within the controlled design.,1,True,Same on-site school setting as other stages.,0.9,False,No computational agents.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical analysis.,1,2,Each third-party observer is paired with one PD player in TPP whose outcome they can affect; interaction is dyadic observer–observed for the punishment decision.,0.9,1,Observer makes a one-time conditional decision via strategy method for a single PD outcome.,0.9,1,"Observer can either invest 1 token or 0; binary choice, no partial fraction.",1,0,Observer is endowed with one token in a private account that can be spent to punish or kept.,1,N/R,Not a standard contribution game; it is a sanction decision; MPCR not defined.,0.2,0,No communication between observer and PD player is mentioned; matching is anonymous across schools.,0.9,0,"Observers are not shown detailed histories or summaries, only the hypothetical defection scenario.",0.9,0,"Matching is anonymous, and no identities are revealed when punishment occurs.",1,0,Observers’ income (token kept) is private vis-à-vis PD players; no identity information.,1,1,Stage and one-time nature of the punishment decision are explained; they know they decide once.,0.8,1,"Observer can “invest a token to punish… As a consequence of punishment, the child in the TPP lost all gains from the PD experiment if it had defected.”",1,1,Observer pays their single token if they choose to punish.,1,1,Investing 1 token destroys all of the observed child’s PD payoff (which is either 2 or 6 when defecting). We code punishmentTech as 1 token invested per full destruction event as the unit specified.,0.5,0,Observers have no option to increase others’ payoffs; they only can punish or not.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,1,“The observer… was endowed with one token. This token could either be kept… or could be spent to destroy the whole payoff…”,1,N/R,This role does not involve a cooperation/defection decision in a PD; only punishment is chosen. No contribution rate defined.,1,N/R,No cooperation tokens to contribute; decision is invest-to-punish vs keep.,1,N/R,Efficiency measure reported in paper aggregates over PD players and third parties; not reported separately for observers.,0.2,N/R,No separate payoff statistics for observer–observed pairs are provided.,0.2,Italy,Same sample of Merano primary school children.,1,7–11 years (grades 2–5 primary school),"Same children as in CTR stage 1, just in observer role.",1,Mixed; approximately equal share of boys and girls (exact proportions not reported),Same subject pool; overall gender distribution not specified.,0.4,"Primary school students (grades 2–5) in Merano, Italy",Same sample description as main experiment.,1,On site,"Same school-based, in-person experimental setup.",1,Observer (third party) uses the strategy method: decides whether to invest 1 token to destroy the PD payoff of their assigned TPP child in case that child defects. No punishment allowed against cooperators (no spiteful punishment). Observers are CTR children who previously played the PD in stage 1.,Summarized from ‘The third party (the child in the CTR) had to decide whether to invest a token to punish the assigned child… decision implemented only in case of defection… we restricted… to altruistic punishment.’,1
10.1073_pnas.1320451111.md,Third-party punishment treatment (TPP) – Stage 1 PD with punishment threat,566 children assigned to TPP play the PD once under threat of third-party punishment in stage 1; this is the key treatment.,1,Third-party punishment availability: present (binary punishment by age-matched peer observer); Role: PD player; Stage: 1,Main manipulation relative to CTR is presence of third-party observers with a punishment token; this row captures PD players under that institution.,1,True,Children make incentivized PD and belief decisions.,1,True,Random assignment to TPP vs CTR; controlled design.,1,True,Same controlled in-school environment.,0.9,False,No simulations involved.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical model.,1,2,"Each TPP child plays a 2-player PD (with a partner also in TPP); each is additionally linked to an external third party, but the PD itself has 2 decision-makers.",1,1,“Played the PD once” in stage 1; one-shot game.,1,0,Same binary cooperate/defect decision as in CTR.,1,0,Each PD player endowed with two tokens in private account; can decide to pass or keep.,1,N/R,Nonlinear PD payoff matrix; MPCR not defined or reported.,0.2,0,"No communication allowed; partners from different schools, matched anonymously.",0.9,0,Players are not shown histories or earnings summaries; only know the structure and that a third party may punish.,0.9,0,Punishment by third parties is anonymous; children only know a same-age observer exists.,0.9,0,No peer rewards nor identity revelation described.,1,1,Game is one-shot and explained as such; children know it is only played once in that stage.,0.9,1,Each PD player is assigned an exclusive third-party observer who can pay 1 token to destroy their payoff if they defect.,1,1,"From PD player’s perspective, punishment occurs only if third party invests 1 of their own token; cost to punisher is 1.",1,1,One token paid by third party eliminates the entire PD payoff of the defector. We code magnitude as 1 punishment event per token; exact payoff ratio (destroyed/paid) is 2 or 6 depending on outcome and not single-valued.,0.5,0,No positive sanctioning mechanism; belief rewards are personal and not directed to others.,1,N/A,No peer reward institution.,1,N/A,No peer reward institution.,1,2,Same PD structure: “one child and his or her partner were endowed with two tokens each…”; TPP extends PD by adding observer but keeps same endowment.,1,0.58,"They report: “58% of players cooperated in the one-shot PD in the TPP”; cooperation implies contributing both tokens, so normalized rate is 0.58.",1,N/R,"Binary choice; mean tokens sent not explicitly calculated in paper, only cooperation fraction.",0.3,N/R,"They provide an expected net surplus of 2.98 tokens vs 2.49 in CTR, but not normalized relative to max 4 tokens; efficiency ratio not reported.",0.4,N/R,"They give expected individual payoff (3.01 tokens before subtracting third-party costs), not total per group; 6.02 could be derived but not explicitly stated, so not imputed.",0.3,Italy,"All TPP participants are primary school children in Merano, Italy.",1,7–11 years (grades 2–5 primary school),Same age cohorts: children aged 7 to 11 in grades 2–5.,1,Mixed; approximately equal share of boys and girls (exact proportions not reported),No separate TPP gender composition given; same overall pool where gender is controlled in regressions.,0.4,"Primary school students (grades 2–5) in Merano, Italy",Same as in CTR.,1,On site,"Same school-based, in-person experimental conditions.",1,"Two-player one-shot PD with same payoff matrix as CTR. Additionally, each PD player is assigned a same-age third-party observer (from CTR) who can invest 1 token to destroy the player’s entire PD payoff if the player defects. No punishment is possible against cooperators. Players know ex ante that (i) they have such an observer, (ii) their partner also has an observer, and (iii) observers’ decisions are not known to them before choosing. After choosing, children report beliefs about (a) partner cooperation and (b) likelihood of third-party punishment; each correct belief earns 1 token.",Summarized from the description of TPP treatment and belief elicitation in Results and Methods.,1
10.1098_rsbl.2012.0470.md,Treatment A – P1 punishment decision,Authors define three treatments (A–C) differing by initial endowments; P1’s punishment decision is the main behavioral condition per treatment.,1,"Treatment A: Initial endowments P1=$0.70, P2=$0.10; P2 cheating decision (cheat vs not cheat)",Independent variables are the treatment-specific initial endowments and whether P2 cheated; both affect P1’s punishment decision.,1,True,Human subjects recruited via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,True,Controlled assignment to roles and treatments with specified payoffs and actions; authors describe it as an experiment.,1,False,"Conducted online via AMT, not in a physical lab.",1,False,No computer simulation or agent-based model; all decisions made by human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model derived; focus is on empirical data.,1,2,Game is a two-player moonlighting-style game between P1 and P2.,1,1,One-shot interaction: subjects assigned one role and one treatment and paired ex post; no repeated rounds described.,0.9,1,"No contribution decision; only discrete cheating and punishment actions. Public-goods contribution concept not applicable, but per instructions use 1 when not restricted to all-or-nothing contribution, here interpreted as N/A-type; set to 1 as there is no all-or-nothing contribution structure.",0.3,N/A,Game is not a contribution/public-goods game; there is no default contribution allocation.,1,N/R,No group contribution or multiplier reported; moonlighting game with transfers and punishment only.,1,0,Subjects interacted anonymously via IDs and no communication or chat is mentioned.,0.9,0,No mention that participants saw summaries of others’ earnings or punishment decisions; only their own payoff is implied.,0.7,0,Game is anonymous via worker IDs; authors state worker IDs would not be revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the game; therefore no reward identities are shown.,1,0,One-shot task; no indication that a number of rounds was shown.,0.8,1,Stage 2 explicitly allows P1 to pay $0.10 to reduce P2’s income by $0.30.,1,0.1,"P1 can ""pay $0.10 to reduce P2's income by $0.30"".",1,0.3,Each $0.10 spent reduces P2’s income by $0.30; thus 0.30 per 0.10 spent.,1,0,No option to pay to increase partner’s income is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0.8,Total initial money in the pair: P1=$0.70 + P2=$0.10 = $0.80.,1,N/R,No contribution decision; only cheating and punishment measured. Contribution rate not defined.,1,N/R,No contribution game; only transfers via cheating and punishment.,1,N/R,"Paper reports proportions punishing, not efficiency or payoff ratios.",1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics by treatment are reported.,1,N/R,Recruitment via AMT is described but no country breakdown reported.,0,"16–69, mean 29","""Subjects varied in age from 16 to 69 (mean =29 ± 0.4) years old.""",1,"361 males, 195 females, 4 unspecified (across all treatments and roles)","Reported overall: ""361 males, 195 females, four unspecified"" out of 560.",1,N/R,No education information given.,0,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk with external survey website.,1,"Simplified moonlighting game with two stages: P2 may take $0.20 from P1; then P1 may pay $0.10 to reduce P2’s payoff by $0.30. Treatment A endowments: P1=$0.70, P2=$0.10. If P2 cheats: P1=$0.50, P2=$0.30 (P1 still advantaged).",Summarizes structure and payoffs for treatment A as described in methods section.,1
