10.1098_rspa.2021.0397.md,Deterministic eco-evolutionary multigame model with punishment and free space (system 2.10),"The paper presents a single mathematical/agent-free eco-evolutionary model defined by system (2.10); all results are analyses or simulations of this same model, not distinct experimental conditions with human subjects.",1,"Model parameters: temptation β, punishment fine δ, mutual defection payoff η, PD game probability p, free-space benefits σ1, σ2, σ3, death rate ξ; initial fractions of cooperators x0, punishers y0, defectors z0 (with w0 = 1 − x0 − y0 − z0).",All variation in the paper comes from changing these parameters and initial conditions in the differential equation system; there is no experimental treatment in the sense of human or agent-based experiments.,1,False,"The study is purely theoretical, based on differential equations and numerical integration; no human or animal participants are involved.",1,False,There is no controlled empirical experiment; only mathematical analysis and numerical simulations of ODEs.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; the work is theoretical and computational.,1,True,They integrate the eco-evolutionary model (system 2.10) numerically with Runge–Kutta methods and explore parameter spaces; this is a computer simulation without agents or humans.,1,True,"They perform analytical stability and bifurcation analysis, derive equilibria and eigenvalues, and sometimes closed-form solutions; this is formal mathematical work.",1,2,The underlying interactions are defined as symmetric two-person games (PD and SD: “two different traditional symmetric two-person games”).,1,0,"The model is continuous-time eco-evolutionary dynamics without discrete rounds; ‘0’ used to indicate not applicable in this continuous-time, infinite-horizon setting.",0.8,1,"Strategies are C, D, P in 2×2 games; players choose pure actions, but contribution “amounts” are not modeled as continuous choices. For the requested coding, using 1 to indicate not an all-or-nothing contribution game in the public-goods sense would be misleading, but 0 is defined as all-or-nothing contribution choices; here contribution is binary C vs D, so it is effectively all-or-nothing. However, because this is not a PGG at all, this variable is somewhat ill-posed; I code as 0 to reflect binary choice.",0.5,N/A,There is no endowment or marginal contribution structure; only payoffs in pairwise matrix games are specified.,1,N/R,"The model is based on PD and SD pairwise payoffs, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR is defined.",1,N/A,No human players or communication; variable not applicable.,1,N/A,"No human subjects, no user interface or feedback design.",1,N/A,"Punishment is a strategy in the payoff matrix, not an implemented institution with identities.",1,N/A,There is no explicit reward mechanism; only punishment and free-space benefits.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds or human interface.,1,1,A punisher strategy P imposes a fine δ on defectors and pays the same cost; punishment is central to the model.,1,N/R,"They specify a fine δ that reduces both the defector’s and punisher’s payoffs by δ, but do not frame it as ‘cost per punishment point’ in a token sense; no numeric endowment or unit scale is defined.",0.5,N/R,The mapping from punisher investment to fined amount is not parameterized as separate ‘technology’; payoffs simply include δ. No explicit ratio cost:impact beyond 1:1 is defined in a public-goods style.,0.5,0,There is no explicit rewarding strategy or institution; only free-space benefits and punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/R,Model does not use endowment or contribution levels; payoffs are dimensionless utilities.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are frequencies of strategies x, y, z and free space w in continuous time; there is no notion of contribution rate as share of endowment.",1,N/R,No explicit contribution quantities are modeled.,1,N/R,"They analyze coexistence, stability, cycles, and chaos; they do not report aggregate efficiency relative to a maximum payoff benchmark.",1,N/R,"Average payoffs f_C, f_P, f_D are defined, but no aggregate group payoff statistic is reported as a main outcome.",1,N/A,There are no participants; this is a purely theoretical/simulation study.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study consists of mathematical analysis and numerical ODE simulations; no lab, online, or field data.",1,"Well-mixed eco-evolutionary multigame combining Prisoner’s Dilemma and Snowdrift games: each pairwise encounter uses PD with probability p and SD with probability 1−p; strategies are Cooperators (C), Defectors (D), and Punishers (P), plus an ecological ‘free space’ state (F) that grants benefits σ1, σ2, σ3 to C, P, D respectively but receives 0; payoffs for PD and SD are parameterized with R=1, S=0, T=β>1, P_PD=η∈(0,1), P_SD=−η, and punishment fine δ>0 applied symmetrically to P and D when they interact. Population dynamics follow eco-evolutionary ODEs where growth rates equal payoff minus constant death rate ξ, and free space fraction w=1−x−y−z.","Summarizes the specified payoff matrices (A, B, E) and dynamic equations to capture the game structure.",1
