10.1098_rspb.2011.0252.md,"PUN group in PUN-NOPUN (asymmetric competition, punishment-enabled group)","In asymmetric competition treatment PUN-NOPUN, one group has punishment opportunity; this row is that group-level condition.",1,Punishment opportunity: yes; Group competition: asymmetric competition vs non-punishing rival group,Independent variables: presence of punishment within group and presence/type of intergroup competition.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Randomly assigned treatment with controlled payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted at Max Planck Institute laboratory.,1,False,No computational simulation for this treatment.,1,False,No analytical derivation only.,1,8,"Table 1: asymmetric competition PUN-NOPUN has 8+8, meaning each group contains 8 participants.",1,30,All treatments consisted of 30 identical periods.,1,1,Same public goods contribution mechanism: allocation of 0–20 MUs.,0.95,0,Endowment initially private; contributions voluntary to group account.,0.95,0.25,Within-group returns identical: total contributions doubled then divided by 8.,1,0,Communication excluded across all treatments.,1,1,Participants informed about individual contributions and earnings in own group and total contribution of competing group after contribution stage.,1,0,Text: 'Participants were not informed about the individual punishment decisions of other participants.',1,0,No reward stage exists.,1,1,Likely informed of 30 periods in instructions; standard in z-Tree experiments.,0.6,1,"In PUN-NOPUN, 'participants of one group had the possibility to engage in costly punishment'.",1,1,Each deduction point cost punisher 1 MU.,1,3,Each deduction point reduced receiver’s earnings by 3 MUs.,1,0,Only punishment mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward option in any treatment.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Same 20 MU per period as all treatments.,1,0.965,Results: 'groups with punishment 19.3 MUs' average in PUN-NOPUN; 19.3/20 = 0.965.,0.95,19.3,Results: 'groups with punishment 19.3 MUs' mean contributions.,1,N/R,"No explicit efficiency ratio reported, and exact maximum payoff including group-competition component is nontrivial to compute from given information.",0,1485,Results: 'groups with punishment 1485 MUs' mean total net pay-offs in asymmetric competition.,0.8,Germany,"Experiment run in Jena, Germany.",0.9,Primarily undergraduate students; exact ages not reported,Described as 'vast majority ... undergraduate students'.,0.6,"169 female, 119 male across all treatments",Only aggregate gender statistics reported.,0.8,Mostly undergraduate university students from various disciplines,Same subject pool across treatments.,0.9,On site,Lab experiment at Max Planck Institute using z-Tree.,1,"Linear public goods game with peer punishment and asymmetric intergroup competition; each period, two groups of 8 compete: the group with higher total contributions to group account wins twice the difference in total investments, which is divided equally; the losing group loses the same amount per member. After seeing own group contributions/earnings and rival’s total contribution, punishment group members can assign costly deduction points to in-group members only. Non-punishing rival group is not informed about the other group’s punishment opportunity.","Summarizes features specific to punishment-enabled groups in asymmetric competition: competition rule, information, and punishment asymmetry.",1
