,586,Results: 'groups without punishment 586 MUs' mean total net pay-offs in asymmetric competition.,0.8,Germany,Same subject pool/location as other treatments.,0.9,Primarily undergraduate students; exact ages not reported,Overall sample description applies here.,0.6,"169 female, 119 male across all treatments",Gender breakdown not given by treatment.,0.8,Mostly undergraduate university students from various disciplines,Same pool across treatments.,0.9,On site,Lab-based experiment with z-Tree.,1,"Linear public goods game without punishment but with asymmetric intergroup competition against a punishment-enabled group; two groups of 8 compete each period based on total contributions, with the higher-contributing group gaining and lower-contributing group losing twice the contribution difference, split equally; rival group has a hidden punishment opportunity that this group is not informed about.","Summarizes how this no-punishment group differs within the asymmetric competition treatment, including information asymmetry about punishment.",1
