info reported.,0.8,Mostly undergraduate university students from various disciplines,Same subject pool.,0.9,On site,Laboratory setting in Jena using z-Tree.,1,"Linear public goods game with peer punishment and symmetric between-group competition: two groups of 8 with punishment capability compete each period on total contributions; payoff externalities from competition are as in other group-competition treatments (winner gains, loser loses twice the difference), and then group members can punish in-group free riders using costly deduction points; severity of punishment responds to intensity of group conflict (larger defeats trigger harsher punishment).","Summary of features where both groups can punish, including findings about punishment intensity under conflict.",1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2011.02476.x.md,"Main experiment – HHH (single status, all high caste; A and C same specific caste, B different specific caste)",Paper defines four main treatments by caste-status composition of triple; HHH is one of the single status treatments with all three players high caste and B from a different specific caste than A and C.,1,"Caste-status composition of the triple: A=high caste, B=high caste, C=high caste; A and C from same specific high caste; B from a different specific high caste.","Treatments vary caste status of A, B, C while holding game structure constant. For HHH all three are high caste with B from different specific caste (Fig. 2 and Section 1.2).",1,True,Male adult human subjects from villages in Uttar Pradesh played the game.,1,True,Controlled assignment to game roles and treatment conditions with monetary incentives.,1,False,"Conducted in villages and inside a car in the field, not in a conventional laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data from human play.,1,False,No formal analytical model as primary method for this treatment’s results.,1,3,"Game explicitly between three individuals A, B, and C.",1,1,One-shot sequential exchange; no repetition mentioned.,0.9,0,"A chooses between sending 0 or full 50 (all-or-nothing), but B chooses between 0 or 100 out of 200 (binary, not full endowment), and C chooses any number of punishment coins up to his endowment; overall contribution/punishment choices are not restricted to all-or-nothing across all decision makers.",0.6,0,Endowments start as private money for each player; nothing is pre-committed to any joint account.,1,N/R,"This is a sequential exchange/third-party punishment game, not a linear public goods game with a standard MPCR; no MPCR defined.",1,0,Decisions made privately in the car; no communication between players reported.,1,0,"C only learns A and B’s realized actions, not summaries of others’ earnings or history.",1,0,Interactions are anonymous across villages; no identities or punishers revealed to targets.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the game; thus no identities to reveal.,1,0,One-shot game; no mention of displaying number of rounds.,1,1,Player C can impose losses on B at a cost to himself.,1,2,"For each two-rupee coin C spends, B loses a ten-rupee note; cost to punisher per unit is 2 rupees.",1,10,Each two-rupee punishment coin leads to a 10-rupee loss for B.,1,0,No possibility to increase B’s payoff beyond not punishing; only losses can be imposed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,50,"Players A and B each have endowment of 50 rupees; C has 100. Endowment field taken as primary exchange players’ endowment, 50.",0.8,0.85,"For HHH, 85% of high caste players B cooperated (sent 100 back); cooperation is the effective ‘contribution’ relative to max 100.",1,100,"When B cooperates, he sends 100 rupees back to A; binary amount for cooperative choice.",1,N/R,No aggregate payoff ratios or efficiency measures reported by treatment.,1,N/R,No mean total rupee payoffs per triple reported by treatment.,1,India,"Subjects recruited from rural villages in Uttar Pradesh, India.",1,Mean age ≈ 35 years for high caste players; range 24–50 years,Section 1.3 reports mean and range for high caste subjects.,1,Male only,They recruited male subjects for each role.,1,"Varied; some completed at least 10 years of schooling, others less.",Regressions include dummy for completion of secondary school; distribution described but not summarized by treatment.,0.8,Field experiment,Game run in rural villages; decisions taken individually in a car on site.,1,"Sequential exchange (trust) game with third-party punishment: A chooses send 50 or opt-out; if send, experimenter triples to 150 and B has 200 total, then B chooses keep all or send 100 back; C with 100 rupees decides, via strategy method, how many 2-rupee coins to spend to reduce B’s payoff by 10 rupees per coin in each of the two contingencies (cooperation vs defection).",Summarized from Section 1.1 and figure description.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2011.02476.x.md,"Main experiment – HLH (mixed status; A high caste, B low caste, C high caste; A and C same specific caste, B different specific caste)","HLH is one of the four main treatments; mixed status with low caste B and high caste A,C.",1,"Caste-status composition of the triple: A=high caste, B=low caste, C=high caste; A and C from same specific high caste; B from a specific low caste.",Defined in Section 1.2 as mixed status treatment HLH.,1,True,Same field experiment with human participants.,1,True,Same controlled game with random assignment and monetary incentives.,1,False,"Conducted in villages, not in lab.",1,False,No simulations involved.,1,False,"Results based on observed behavior, not on an analytical derivation.",1,3,"Triple of A, B, C.",1,1,Design identical across treatments; one-shot game.,0.9,0,Same game structure as HHH with non-all-or-nothing punishment choices.,0.6,0,Endowments assigned privately.,1,N/R,No linear public good; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,"Only immediate actions revealed, no summary statistics.",1,0,Anonymity across villages prevents identity revelation of punishers.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,0,One-shot; no info about remaining rounds.,1,1,C can impose losses on B.,1,2,Two-rupee cost per punishment coin.,1,10,Each coin reduces B’s payoff by 10 rupees.,1,0,No payoff-increasing action by C.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,50,"Same endowments for A (50), B (50), C (100) as in base game.",1,0.87,"For HLH, 87% of low caste players B cooperated (reported as 87% in HLH; text: 87% but rounding from 87? Actually 87% stated for low caste B in HLH).",1,100,Cooperation means sending 100 rupees back.,1,N/R,No efficiency calculations by treatment reported.,1,N/R,No average total payoff by treatment given.,1,India,"Villages in Uttar Pradesh, India.",1,Mean age ≈ 35 years for high caste players; range 24–50 years,"Same subject pool as other treatments; reported age stats are by caste, not by treatment.",1,Male only,All roles recruited among males.,1,"Varied; some with ≥10 years schooling, others with less.",Education distribution is overall for sample; no treatment-specific differences mentioned.,0.8,Field experiment,Same procedures as in other treatments in villages using a car.,1,"Same sequential exchange game with third-party punishment as in HHH; only caste composition of A, B, C differs.",Design states that only caste-status composition varies across treatments.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2011.02476.x.md,"Main experiment – LLL (single status, all low caste; A and C same specific caste, B different specific caste)",LLL is one of the four main treatments; single status with all low caste players and B from a different specific low caste.,1,"Caste-status composition of the triple: A=low caste, B=low caste, C=low caste; A and C from same specific low caste; B from a different specific low caste.",Defined in Section 1.2 as single status low caste treatment.,1,True,Same field experimental setup with human participants.,1,True,Controlled incentives and random matching.,1,False,Conducted in villages (field) rather than a university lab.,1,False,No simulation methods used.,1,False,"Empirical behavioral data, no pure analytical treatment at condition level.",1,3,"Triples of A, B, C.",1,1,One-shot game with no repetition reported.,0.9,0,Same structure as other main treatments; punishment choice continuous in coins.,0.6,0,Endowments initially private.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No mention of inter-player communication.,1,0,"Players only see specific partners’ actions, not broader summaries.",1,0,Player identities anonymized across villages.,1,0,No reward option implemented.,1,0,Single round only; no rounds info displayed.,1,1,C can spend coins to reduce B’s payoff.,1,2,Two-rupee cost per punishment coin.,1,10,Each coin reduces B’s payoff by 10 rupees.,1,0,No positive-transfer action for C.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Same 50/50/100 endowment structure applies.,1,0.77,"For LLL, 77% of low caste players B cooperated (text, Section 2.1).",1,100,Cooperation is sending 100 rupees to A.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,No mean group payoff given by treatment.,1,India,Rural Uttar Pradesh villages.,1,Mean age ≈ 34 years for low caste players; range 24–50 years,Section 1.3 reports average age for low caste subjects.,1,Male only,Male subjects recruited for each role.,1,"Varied; some subjects completed at least 10 years of schooling, many did not.",Education variation described for full sample; no treatment-specific breakdown.,0.8,Field experiment,On-site implementation in villages using car sessions.,1,Identical sequential exchange and third-party punishment game as other main treatments; only caste composition differs.,"Section 1.2 states game same across treatments, only caste status varies.",1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2011.02476.x.md,"Main experiment – LHL (mixed status; A low caste, B high caste, C low caste; A and C same specific caste, B different specific caste)","LHL is the fourth main treatment; mixed status with high caste B and low caste A,C.",1,"Caste-status composition of the triple: A=low caste, B=high caste, C=low caste; A and C from same specific low caste; B from a specific high caste.",Described as mixed status treatment LHL in Section 1.2.,1,True,Same field experiment with human subjects.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized game.",1,False,Conducted in rural field setting.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,"Behavioral data analysis, not purely analytical.",1,3,"Triples of A, B, C.",1,1,Same one-shot game as other main treatments.,0.9,0,Same action space as in other treatments.,0.6,0,Endowments fully private initially.,1,N/R,No linear public good; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,Only partner’s actions and direct payoffs used.,1,0,Subjects anonymous across villages.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,One-shot; no round counter.,1,1,C can punish B at a cost.,1,2,Two-rupee coin cost per punishment.,1,10,Punishment reduces B’s payoff by 10 rupees per coin.,1,0,No condoned way to increase B’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,50,Same endowment parameters as other treatments.,1,0.88,"For LHL, 88% of high caste players B cooperated (Section 2.1).",1,100,Amount sent back when cooperating.,1,N/R,No treatment-level efficiency statistic.,1,N/R,Group payoff by treatment not reported.,1,India,"All subjects from Uttar Pradesh villages, India.",1,Mean age ≈ 34 years for low caste players; range 24–50 years,"Same subject pool, same age stats for low castes.",1,Male only,Recruitment restricted to male subjects.,1,"Mixed education levels; some completed secondary school (10 years), others did not.","Education data reported for overall sample, not by treatment.",0.8,Field experiment,Identical field