10.1111_j.1468-0297.2011.02476.x.md,"Follow-up BC-condition – HHH_BC (single status high caste; B and C same specific caste, A different specific high caste)",Second experiment introduces BC-condition; HHH_BC is high caste single-status triple where B and C share specific caste and A is another high caste.,1,"Caste/status and in-group structure: All players high caste; B and C from same specific high caste, A from a different specific high caste (BC-condition).","Section 3 and Fig. 6 describe HHH_BC: B and C same specific caste, A different, all high in status.",1,True,Same type of field experiment with human adults.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of in-group affiliation in the same game.,1,False,Conducted in villages using same car setup.,1,False,No simulation components.,1,False,"Results empirically estimated, not analytically derived.",1,3,"Still a triple A, B, C.",1,1,Same underlying one-shot game used; follow-up only changes caste matching.,0.9,0,Same action spaces as in main experiment.,0.6,0,Endowments are private at start.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined for this trust/punishment game.,1,0,Procedures unchanged; no communication among players.,1,0,Only individual actions in the triple revealed.,1,0,Anonymity maintained as before.,1,0,No reward option.,1,0,One-shot game; no visual round counter mentioned.,1,1,C can still punish B in same manner.,1,2,Cost structure unchanged: 2 rupees per punishment coin.,1,10,B loses 10 rupees per punishment coin.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism in BC-condition either.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Game structure (50/50/100 endowments) not altered in BC-condition.,1,N/R,Cooperation rates for B in HHH_BC are not separately reported.,0.2,100,"Cooperative action, when it occurs, still consists of B sending 100 rupees back.",1,N/R,No aggregate payoff efficiency reported for BC-condition.,1,N/R,No mean group payoff given by treatment.,1,India,Same sampling frame: rural Uttar Pradesh.,1,Mean age mid-30s; range 24–50 years (high caste subjects),Same high caste subject pool statistics apply; no different ages mentioned for follow-up.,0.9,Male only,All experimental subjects were male.,1,"Varied, with some completing 10 years of schooling and others not.",Education distribution unchanged; no new recruitment rule stated.,0.8,Field experiment,Same village-based car sessions; only matching rule changed.,1,"Same sequential exchange and third-party punishment game; difference is in-group relation: B and C same specific caste (high caste), A from different specific high caste, which tests in-group favouritism of punishers toward victims and violators.",Section 3 and Fig. 6 describe this alteration to in-group configuration.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0297.2011.02476.x.md,"Follow-up BC-condition – LLL_BC (single status low caste; B and C same specific caste, A different specific low caste)",Second experiment BC-condition for low castes; LLL_BC with B and C from same specific low caste and A different low caste.,1,"Caste/status and in-group structure: All players low caste; B and C from same specific low caste, A from a different specific low caste (BC-condition).",Described in Section 3 and Fig. 6 example (Pasi-Chamar-Chamar) as LLL_BC.,1,True,Same kind of field experiment with human participants.,1,True,Manipulation of in-group affiliation in a controlled game.,1,False,Run on site in villages via individual car sessions.,1,False,No simulated agents used.,1,False,"Based on observed choices, not analytic model.",1,3,"Same triple structure of A, B, C.",1,1,Same one-shot exchange game as other conditions.,0.9,0,Game action spaces unchanged from main experiment.,0.6,0,Initial endowments are private.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication between players allowed.,1,0,No summaries of others’ behavior or payoffs are mentioned.,1,0,Identities remain anonymous due to recruitment from different villages.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present.,1,0,One-shot game; no indication of rounds display.,1,1,C can incur costs to reduce B’s payoff.,1,2,Cost per punishment coin is 2 rupees.,1,10,Each coin causes a 10-rupee loss to B.,1,0,No possibility of increasing B’s payoff via pro-social action by C.,1,N/A,Rewards not part of the design.,1,N/A,Rewards not part of the design.,1,50,Endowments kept same as main experiment: 50/50/100.,1,N/R,Cooperation rate for B in LLL_BC not separately reported.,0.2,100,"In cooperative outcome, B still sends 100 rupees back.",1,N/R,No efficiency ratios given for BC-condition treatments.,1,N/R,Group payoff figures not reported by condition.,1,India,Same rural Uttar Pradesh setting.,1,Mean age ≈ 34 years for low caste players; range 24–50 years,Same low caste sample characteristics; no separate reporting for BC-condition.,0.9,Male only,Experiment recruited only male subjects.,1,Varied educational attainment; some completed secondary school.,No new inclusion/exclusion criteria stated for follow-up.,0.8,Field experiment,Follow-up carried out in same field environment as main experiment.,1,"Same sequential exchange/trust game with third-party punishment; only in-group relationship changed so that B and C share the same specific low caste while A belongs to a different specific low caste, used to test whether low castes exhibit in-group favouritism in punishment.","Section 3 explains that BC-condition varies only caste configuration, not game structure.",1
10.1038_srep17050.md,Infinite well-mixed population – baseline public goods with implicated punishment and within-group enforcement,"The paper defines a single analytical model for infinite well-mixed populations with parameters N, r, c, p, d, q, α, β; this row captures that general condition.",1,"Monitoring probability p, implicated punishment fine d, within-group enforcement probability q, group size N, enhancement factor r, contribution cost c, peer punishment cost α, peer punishment fine β; all varied parametrically within 1 < r < N, 0 < p < 1, d > 0, 0 < q < 1, 0 < α < β.",Model description and Results/Methods sections state that these parameters are varied to study effects on cooperation.,1,False,Pure evolutionary game-theoretic analysis with no human or animal subjects.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory component; analytical treatment only.,1,True,They use numerical analysis of replicator dynamics and compute gradients and equilibria; this is a mathematical/numerical simulation without human subjects.,0.9,True,Closed-form derivations of payoffs and replicator dynamics in Methods for infinite populations.,1,5,Illustrative parameter choice in Fig. 1 and several panels: 'Parameter values are: N=5 ...'. N is group size in the public goods game.,0.8,1,"Public goods interaction is a single-shot game in each group in the evolutionary model; repeated play is modeled via population dynamics, not discrete rounds for agents.",0.8,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed cost c, defectors contribute 0; there is no continuous decision over contribution level, so contribution choice is effectively binary (all-or-nothing). Coding 1 since contributors give the full contribution c when cooperating.",0.8,N/A,No human participants or explicit endowment allocation; contributions are modeled as costs c.,1,N/R,"They specify total contribution multiplied by r and split among N, implying MPCR = r/N, but an explicit numeric MPCR is not fixed because r and N are varied symbolically.",0.7,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human participants.,1,N/A,No identities; model uses anonymous well-mixed populations.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or display of rounds.,1,1,Model includes implicated punishment (fine d when monitoring detects a defector) and peer punishment (fine β with cost α).,1,1,"Implicated punishment: cost to each individual is fine d when applied; peer punishment: cost α per punished defector to a punisher. No single unified 'per unit' cost is defined; we list d generically, but its numeric value varies (often 1.0 in examples).",0.4,1,Peer punishment imposes fine β per defector at cost α (0<α<β); implicated punishment imposes fine d per individual. The 'multiplier' per cost unit is β/α for peer punishment but is not fixed numerically; we enter a placeholder since schema requires a number.,0.3,0,Only punishment mechanisms (implicated and peer) are modeled; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Players incur cost c when cooperating; no explicit initial endowment in tokens/points is specified.,0.6,N/R,"Dependent variable is the fraction of cooperators x in the population, not normalized per-round contribution relative to an explicit endowment.",0.6,N/R,"They analyze evolutionary frequency of cooperators, not per-player token amounts.",0.8,N/R,They discuss basins of attraction and cooperation levels but do not report efficiency as total payoff relative to maximum.,0.7,N/R,Group payoff expressions are derivable from payoffs but no single reported numerical outcome per condition is given.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and numerical analysis without human subjects.,1,"Two-strategy public goods game (cooperate by paying cost c, or defect) in well-mixed infinite population with group size N; total contributions multiplied by r and evenly shared; probabilistic monitoring with probability p triggers implicated punishment fine d on all group members if any defector is present; cooperator may become a peer punisher with probability q, imposing fine β on each defector at cost α.",Summarized from model description in Results and Methods for infinite populations.,1
10.1038_srep17050.md,Finite well-mixed population – baseline public goods with implicated punishment and within-group enforcement,"Methods for finite populations defines a single model of size Z with same public goods and punishment structure, analyzed via pairwise comparison, gradient G(k), and Markov chain.",1,"Monitoring probability p, implicated punishment fine d, within-group enforcement probability q, group size N, population size Z, enhancement factor r, contribution cost c, peer punishment cost α, peer punishment fine β, selection intensity s, mutation rate μ; varied parametrically.","Finite population section and figures (3–5) vary these parameters to study roots of G(k), stationary distribution, and average cooperation.",1,False,Model uses evolutionary dynamics in finite populations without human subjects.,1,False,No behavioral experiment; stochastic evolutionary model only.,1,False,No laboratory setting with participants.,1,True,"They numerically simulate Markov chains and compute stationary distributions for given Z, p, d, q, etc.",1,True,"They derive analytical expressions for payoffs, transition probabilities, G(k), and stationary distributions.",1,5,"Group size in the public goods game is N; example finite-population parameter sets use N=5 (e.g., Fig. 3, 4(a–c), 5(a–c)).",0.8,1,Each interaction is a one-shot public goods game; evolutionary process handles repetition.,0.8,1,"Strategy set is cooperate (pay cost c) or defect (pay 0), so contributions are binary.",0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment structure for human subjects.,1,N/R,"Effective MPCR is r/N in the stage game, but r and N are symbolic; no fixed numeric MPCR is reported.",0.7,N/A,No communication or interface; model is algorithmic.,1,N/A,No human participants or feedback display.,1,N/A,No identities in the model.,1,N/A,No reward in the model.,1,N/A,No notion of discrete experimental rounds displayed to subjects.,1,1,"Finite population model retains implicated punishment (fine d under monitoring) and peer punishment (fine β, cost α).",1,1,"As in infinite case, implicated punishment fine d and peer punishment cost α are defined but not as a single cost-per-unit; we enter a generic numeric placeholder per schema requirement.",0.3,1,Peer punishment imposes fine β at cost α; implicated punishment fine is d per individual. No fixed numeric multiplier; we provide a placeholder as a numeric field.,0.3,0,Only punishment mechanisms are modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Finite population payoffs are defined via costs c and benefits rc/N; no explicit initial endowment is specified.,0.6,N/R,"Key dependent variables are k/Z (fraction of cooperators), internal roots of G(k), stationary distribution over k, and average cooperation level; they do not separately report normalized per-round contribution rate relative to an endowment.",0.7,N/R,"They track number of cooperators, not token contributions.",0.9,N/R,They compute stationary distributions and average cooperation levels but do not report payoff efficiency ratios.,0.7,N/R,Group payoff formulas are provided but no explicit numeric outcome per parameter setting is reported.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Finite-population analysis is theoretical and computational without subjects.,1,"Two-strategy public goods game in finite, well-mixed population of size Z with group size N; individuals are cooperators or defectors. Cooperators pay cost c, benefits multiplied by r and shared equally. Monitoring with probability p triggers implicated punishment fine d on all when a defector is present. Cooperators may be peer punishers with probability q, paying cost α per defector to impose fine β. Evolution follows pairwise comparison with intensity s, allowing mutation with rate μ, leading to Markov-chain stationary distributions over number of cooperators.","Summarized from Methods for finite populations and Results describing G(k), stationary distribution, and average cooperation level.",1
10.1038_srep06025.md,Study 1 – City sample Ultimatum + Dictator Games,Single survey-experiment (Study 1) on a city's adult population; UG and DG decisions are part of one condition with random order.,1,"Game type (Ultimatum Game responder vs Dictator Game allocator); within-subject random order of UG and DG decisions; DG transfer level (0%, between 0% and 50%, 50%) used only for analysis, not as exogenous treatment.",The design varies roles/game frames (UG vs DG) within subjects and randomizes decision order. No between-subject treatment manipulation is described.,0.8,True,Human participants in a survey-experiment.,1,True,"Incentivized, randomized experimental tasks (UG and DG) with controlled payoffs.",1,False,"Conducted at participants' households as a survey-experiment, not in a laboratory.",1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model as a standalone method.,1,2,Both the UG and DG are bilateral games between two participants.,1,1,Each participant makes a single set of UG (strategy method) and DG decisions; no repetition or multiple rounds mentioned.,0.9,1,"In UG, responders choose accept/reject for a discrete set of proposer offers, not an all-or-nothing contribution structure; in DG, dictators can give any of several amounts (in €2 increments).",0.9,0,"These are bargaining/allocation games, not public goods; endowment is held by the active player who decides how much to transfer. By convention, treated as 0 (all initially private).",0.6,N/A,"MPCR is specific to public goods; here games are UG and DG, not contribution games.",1,0,Interactions are anonymous and there is no mention of communication between participants.,0.9,0,No mention that subjects see summaries of others' earnings or decisions; only their own payoffs are implied.,0.8,0,Punishment is implicit via rejection in a bilateral anonymous UG; no identities revealed.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identity revelation is described.,1,0,One-shot decisions; no information on remaining rounds because there are none.,1,1,"In the UG, responders can reject offers, which is modeled and interpreted as costly punishment.",1,20,"If a responder rejects any positive offer x, she forgoes x euros and the proposer forgoes 20−x. Using standard convention, cost to punisher is her forgone payoff; here the maximum possible forgone payoff is €10 at the equal split but cost varies with offer. Since a single scalar is required, and no specific value is stated, this is not well-defined; set to 20 would overstate it. Therefore mark as N/R.",0,N/R,"Rejection sets both players' payoffs to zero, so the effect on the proposer depends on the proposed split; no single multiplier per unit of cost is defined.",0.2,0,"No costly reward mechanism besides standard acceptance of offers, which is not modeled as a separate reward technology.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,20,“The pie to be split was €20 in each game.”,1,N/R,Paper reports percentages rejecting unequal offers and DG transfers but does not provide normalized contribution rates suitable for this field.,0.2,N/R,"Only categorized DG offers (0%, between 0 and 50%, 50%) and aggregate percentages are reported; no mean transfer amount is given.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff data or efficiency ratios are reported.,0,N/R,Group payoffs are not summarized quantitatively.,0,Spain,"Ethics approval and hosting institution are at the University of Granada (Spain), and Study 1 is described as a representative sample of a city's adult population; city is not named but implied to be in Spain.",0.7,Adult population (representative city sample),Described as “a representative sample of a city's adult population”; no exact age range provided.,0.9,N/R,No information on gender composition reported.,0,Mixed; literacy not required,"Text notes that literacy was not required to participate in Study 1, implying inclusion of varying education levels; no further breakdown.",0.7,Field experiment,"Study 1 is a survey-experiment carried out at participants' households, i.e., in the field with random matching and incentives.",1,"Ultimatum Game responder decisions elicited via strategy method over discrete offers: (20,0), (18,2), (16,4), (14,6), (12,8), (10,10). Dictator Game: dictator chooses transfer from €0 to €20 in €2 increments to an anonymous recipient. One of every ten participants is randomly selected for real payment after random matching. UG proposer decisions also collected but not analyzed here.",Directly from Methods description of UG and DG procedures and payment protocol.,1
10.1038_srep06025.md,Study 2 – Lab student sample Ultimatum + Dictator Games,Single laboratory experiment (Study 2) with university freshmen; UG and DG decisions are part of one condition with random order.,1,"Game type (Ultimatum Game responder vs Dictator Game allocator); within-subject random order of UG and DG decisions; DG transfer level (0%, between 0% and 50%, 50%) used only for analysis, not as exogenous treatment.",Design matches Study 1 but in a lab with student subjects; no between-subject treatments are reported.,0.8,True,Human university student participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized lab experiment with random matching.",1,True,Explicitly described as “a replication of Study 1 in the laboratory employing university students (freshmen) as subjects.”,1,False,No non-human simulations reported.,1,False,No standalone analytical model.,1,2,Bilateral UG and DG between two players.,1,1,Same one-shot structure as Study 1; no repeated rounds mentioned.,0.9,1,UG responders evaluate multiple discrete offers; DG dictators select among several transfer amounts in €2 increments; not an all-or-nothing contribution constraint.,0.9,0,"Bilateral bargaining/allocation games; the active player holds the entire €20 endowment and decides on a transfer; by convention, starting share in public/other's account is 0.",0.6,N/A,Not a public goods game; MPCR not defined.,1,0,Laboratory experiment with anonymous matching; no mention of communication.,0.9,0,Subjects are paid based on their own matched interaction; no summaries of others’ outcomes mentioned.,0.8,0,UG interactions are anonymous; identities of punishers (rejectors) are not revealed.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identity revelation is present.,1,0,One-shot interactions; no remaining rounds to display.,1,1,"As in Study 1, rejection of offers in UG functions as costly punishment.",1,N/R,Cost of punishment (rejection) to the responder depends on the foregone payoff x and is not summarized as a single constant; no specific value reported.,0,N/R,"Rejection sets both payoffs to zero regardless of x, making the effect on the proposer variable relative to responder’s loss; no single multiplier per unit cost is specified.",0.2,0,No costly reward technology beyond standard acceptance.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,20,“The pie to be split was €20 in each game.” applies to both studies.,1,N/R,Aggregate normalized contribution rates are not reported; only categorical DG offer groups and rejection rates in UG are given.,0.2,N/R,No mean or median DG transfer levels are reported.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency or payoff ratio statistics reported.,0,N/R,No quantitative group payoff outcomes are provided.,0,Spain,Lab hosted by the University of Granada in Spain.,1,University freshmen,"Participants are described as university students (freshmen); typical age range late teens to early twenties, but exact ages not provided.",0.9,N/R,Gender composition is not reported.,0,University freshmen,Explicitly stated that subjects are university students (freshmen).,1,On site,"Described as a laboratory experiment with university students; standard lab, on-site setting.",1,"Game structure matches Study 1: UG responder decisions via strategy method over discrete offers (20,0), (18,2), (16,4), (14,6), (12,8), (10,10); DG transfers from €0 to €20 in €2 increments to an anonymous participant. Order of UG and DG decisions randomized per subject. After decisions, participants randomly matched and one of every ten selected for payment.","Methods state that Study 2 is a replication of Study 1 in the laboratory, using the same UG and DG procedures and payment rule.",1
10.1177_1069397108320422.md,"N/A – Theoretical/ethnographic argument, no discrete experiments or game conditions","The paper is a theoretical and ethnographic synthesis about social selection and altruism; it does not report any controlled experiment, game, or clearly separated treatment/control conditions.",1,N/A,No manipulated independent variables or experimental conditions are described; the work is conceptual and ethnographic.,1,True,"The article draws on ethnographic fieldwork (e.g., with Montenegrin Serbs and multiple forager societies) involving human participants, even though it is not structured as an experiment.",0.7,False,"No randomized assignment, controlled treatments, or experimental protocols (e.g., economic games) are reported.",1,False,All observations are field/ethnographic; there is no laboratory setting.,1,False,"No agent-based, numerical, or computer simulations are presented.",1,True,"The paper develops formal conceptual arguments and discusses evolutionary models (e.g., runaway selection), but without presenting new mathematical derivations; still, it is best classified as analytical/theoretical.",0.7,0,No game or strategic interaction experiment with defined players is conducted.,1,0,No repeated-round game or experiment is described.,1,0,There is no contribution game; field left at default non-game value.,1,N/A,No endowment or contribution structure with human subjects is specified.,1,N/R,No public goods game or multiplier is reported.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or chat functionality; study is not a controlled game.,1,0,No experimental feedback or summary information is described.,1,0,No designed punishment stage in a game; only naturalistic social sanctions are discussed.,1,0,No formal reward mechanism in an experiment.,1,0,No round-based structure.,1,0,"While naturalistic punishment in societies is central to the paper, there is no implemented punishment mechanism within an experimental or game design.",1,N/A,No experimental punishment action with a defined cost.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology is specified.,1,0,No explicit experimental reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in an experiment.,1,N/A,No formal reward technology is given.,1,0,No monetary/token endowments for participants are described.,1,N/A,No contribution game or quantitative cooperation measure is reported.,1,N/A,No experimental contributions are measured.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric is defined or calculated.,1,N/A,No experimental payoffs are calculated.,1,N/R,"Multiple ethnographic populations from various regions (e.g., Montenegro, Australia, Africa, Arctic, Americas) are referenced; no unified sample for an experiment.",0.3,N/R,Ages of ethnographic subjects are not systematically reported.,1,N/R,The paper refers to men and women in various societies but provides no aggregated sample composition.,0.5,N/R,Educational levels of subjects are not described.,1,Field experiment,"Closest category is real-world/field, as the data are ethnographic observations in natural settings; although not an experiment, 'Field experiment' is the nearest of the allowed labels compared to 'Online', 'On site', 'Observational', or 'No human'.",0.4,"The paper presents cross-cultural ethnographic data on moral deviance and social sanctions among 10 Pleistocene-appropriate forager societies (e.g., Table 1), plus a qualitative case study of Montenegrin marriage patterns, but does not use any formal economic game or structured punishment/reward protocol.","Summarizes the closest thing to structured design elements (coding categories and frequencies), clarifying that these are observational codings, not game parameters.",1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2009.10.007.md,Observed punishment treatment (all periods pooled),Paper defines an Observed treatment (standard VCM with punishment revealed each period); analyses pool all observed sessions and periods.,0.98,Punishment observability: punishment totals revealed at end of each period (Observed),Treatment differs only in whether punishment is revealed each period vs. only after 10 periods.,0.99,True,Uses human subjects in lab public-good experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized matching with pre-specified payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted at Harvard Business School CLER lab.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model as the main study design.,1,4,"“In each period, subjects played a public good contribution game in groups of 4”.",1,10,"They focus on ‘sets of 10 periods’; Observed treatment has 3 such sets, but comparison is by set of 10 periods.",0.95,1,"Contribution choice is any integer from 0 to 20, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment of 20 tokens is initially private; subjects decide how much to contribute.,1,0.4,Each participant receives 0.4 times sum of total group contribution; this is marginal per capita return.,1,0,Explicitly prohibits communication; only on-screen decisions and feedback.,1,1,Stage 1 income screen shows total group contribution and own income; stage 2 information about others’ contributions and own punishment totals.,0.9,0,"Interactions are anonymous; only total punishment received is shown, not identity of punishers.",1,0,No reward mechanism is present.,1,1,"Screens display the period number; instructions refer to ‘10 periods’, so subjects know horizon.",0.9,1,Second-stage deduction points that reduce others’ income are available.,1,1,“Each deduction point cost the participant 1 unit”.,1,3,Each deduction point causes the recipient to lose 3 units.,1,0,Only ‘deduction points’ are described; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"“At the beginning of each period, each participant receives 20 tokens.”",1,0.45,Fig.1 and text: Observed contributions relatively stable between 35% and 45% of endowment; use mid-point 0.45 as approximate typical level.,0.5,8.69,Table 2 averages for Observed sets: (10.07 + 8.45 + 5.55) / 3 ≈ 8.69 tokens.,0.95,N/R,No direct or computable measure of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,0.1,N/R,Only per-participant incomes reported; no total group payoff given.,0.1,United States,Subjects recruited from Boston-area universities; experiment at Harvard Business School in the U.S.,0.95,N/R,Only that they are from Boston-area universities; no age stats.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.01,University students,Participants from Boston area universities implies current university students.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical lab (Harvard Business School CLER lab) with z-Tree terminals.,1,"VCM with punishment, random rematching every period, 20-token endowment, contributions 0–20, return 0.4×sum of group contributions, then costly deduction points (cost 1, reduce target income by 3). Punishment feedback and total punishment received displayed at end of each period.",Summarizes the described stage game and matching protocol for Observed treatment.,1
