10.1007_s10683-011-9281-9.md,2-person treatment,Paper describes a 2-player version as one of three treatments; Table 3 lists '2 person' as a distinct treatment.,1,"Group size N = 2 (positions A and B), standard download (partly downloaded files are not lost)",Treatment varies number of players (2 vs 5) and whether partly downloaded files are lost; for this condition N=2 and there is no mention of lost partial files.,0.95,True,Human undergraduate subjects in lab sessions at Arizona State University.,1,True,Controlled laboratory game with randomized groups and pre-specified payoff structure.,1,True,Explicitly described as laboratory experiments conducted at Arizona State University.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; only human-subject experiments plus analytical reasoning.,1,False,"Although Nash and cooperative equilibria are derived, the reported results are from lab experiments, not a standalone analytical study.",0.9,2,Treatment explicitly specified as '2 person' with positions A and B.,1,10,"After 2 practice rounds, '10 rounds which are played for monetary returns' for all treatments.",1,1,Each round players receive 10 tokens 'that can be used for investment'; no restriction to all-or-nothing; continuous (0–10 integer) investment allowed.,1,0,Endowment of 10 tokens each round starts fully in private account and can be invested; nothing is pre-allocated to the public infrastructure.,1,N/R,"Nonlinear, sigmoidal production function with threshold; no single linear marginal per capita return reported.",1,1,"Before each round, 'players can exchange text messages among those within the same group for 60 seconds'.",1,1,"After investment, 'Subjects are informed about how much each of the subjects invested, and how much bandwidth was created'; provides summary information.",1,0,"No direct costly punishment stage; only indirect punishment via reduced investment, so no identities linked to explicit punishment actions.",1,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism described.,1,1,"Standard lab design with fixed 10 paid rounds announced; although not verbatim, subjects must pass instructions and quiz, strongly implying they know the number of rounds.",0.7,0,No explicit punishment technology; 'indirect punishment' only via reduced future investment; not a dedicated punishment stage.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology implemented.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism for others' behavior described.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology implemented.,1,10,"Each round players receive '10 tokens, each worth $0.1'.",1,N/R,Paper reports average tokens invested but not normalized per-endowment contribution rates per treatment/position.,0.6,8.59,"From Table 4 for 2-person game: A invests 8.93, B invests 8.25; average per person per round ≈ (8.93+8.25)/2 = 8.59 tokens.",0.9,N/R,Can approximate but not directly reported as ratio of actual group payoff to maximum possible; no exact per-treatment efficiency reported.,0.4,29.36,Table 7: earnings per round per person in 2-person = 17.18 (A) and 12.18 (B); sum per group per round ≈ 29.36 tokens.,0.95,USA,Experiments conducted at Arizona State University (USA) with undergraduates from that university.,0.95,Mean 19.2 years; undergraduate students,Exit survey: 'average age was 19.2 years'; participants recruited from undergraduate subject pool.,1,44% female,Exit survey: '44% were female'.,1,Undergraduate university students,Subjects recruited from a database of undergraduate students at Arizona State University.,1,On site,Described as laboratory experiments at ASU using a computer dashboard in sessions on campus.,1,"Two-stage repeated asymmetric commons dilemma. Stage 1: each player (A upstream, B downstream) invests 0–10 tokens in public infrastructure; total investment y maps to bandwidth via a nonlinear sigmoidal function (Table 1; e.g., y=12→20 kb, y=15→36 kb, y=20→40 kb). Stage 2: 100-second real-time extraction phase where each player can download up to 10 files of 250 kb each, max personal speed 25 kb/s. Player A has priority to total bandwidth BT; player B accesses only unused bandwidth (BT minus A’s usage, capped at 25 kb/s). Partially downloaded files count (no loss condition). Payoffs per round: tokens kept (10 − investment) plus tokens from downloaded files based on a nonlinear returns table (Table 2; e.g., 3 files→1 token, 5→10, 10→20). Practice: 2 unpaid rounds followed by 10 paid rounds with 60s within-group text chat before each round.","Summarized from experimental design section, Tables 1–3, and description of real-time downloading game.",1
10.1007_s10683-011-9281-9.md,5-person treatment – standard downloads,Table 3 lists '5 person' as a separate treatment from '5 person (b)'; this is the standard 5-player game without loss of partial downloads.,1,"Group size N = 5 (positions A–E), standard download (partly downloaded files are not lost)",Treatment differs in number of players (5) and keeps standard downloading rule (loss of partial files only in 5 person (b)).,0.95,True,Human undergraduate subjects at ASU participated in 5-person groups.,1,True,Controlled lab game with fixed payoff rules.,1,True,Explicitly a laboratory experiment at ASU.,1,False,No computational-only simulation results.,1,False,Analytical equilibria are background; primary data are experimental.,0.9,5,"Table 3: '5 person' with positions A, B, C, D, and E.",1,10,Same protocol: 2 practice rounds then 10 paid rounds for all treatments.,1,1,Each of 5 players receives 10 tokens that can be invested; no all-or-nothing restriction.,1,0,Endowment starts entirely private; contributions are voluntary.,1,N/R,"Uses same nonlinear production function as 2-player, scaled for 5 players; no single marginal per capita return.",1,1,Same chat procedure across treatments: 60-second within-group text chat before each round.,1,1,"Subjects see each subject’s investment and total bandwidth created, as in 2-person condition.",1,0,No explicit punishment stage.,1,0,No explicit reward stage.,1,1,"Same fixed-length design, described once for all treatments.",0.7,0,Only indirect punishment via reduced contributions; no separate punishment technology.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No reward technology described.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,10,Same per-round endowment of 10 tokens per subject.,1,N/R,Reported as average tokens invested per person per round by position; not converted to fraction of endowment in paper.,0.6,8.31,"From Table 4 for 5-person: A 8.75, B 8.48, C 8.48, D 8.6, E 7.24; mean ≈ (8.75+8.48+8.48+8.6+7.24)/5 = 41.55/5 ≈ 8.31 tokens.",0.95,N/R,Group-level payoff vs social optimum per round not given as a normalized efficiency measure for this treatment alone.,0.4,66.94,"Table 7: earnings per round per person in 5-person standard: A 16.48, B 16.85, C 13.35, D 11.3, E 7.96; sum ≈ 65.94 tokens per group per round.",0.95,USA,Same subject pool at Arizona State University.,0.95,Mean 19.2 years; undergraduate students,Single pool across all treatments; average age 19.2 years.,1,44% female,Overall sample characteristic for all 75 participants.,1,Undergraduate university students,Subjects are undergraduates at ASU.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at ASU using computer dashboards.,1,"Same two-stage repeated asymmetric commons game as 2-person treatment, but with 5 positions A–E arranged sequentially (A most upstream, E most downstream). Production function for bandwidth is identical in form but scaled: total group investment y uses the same Table 1 mapping, with inflection at group total 30 tokens (equivalent to average 6 per person) for N=5. In the 100-second download phase, bandwidth priority follows order A→B→C→D→E: upstream players use available bandwidth up to 25 kb/s each; remaining bandwidth, if any, passes downstream with each individual capped at 25 kb/s and at most 10 files (250 kb each). Partially downloaded files are not lost (standard rule). Payoff from downloaded files uses same nonlinear tokens-per-files schedule (Table 2). Nash equilibrium: no investment by any player, each keeps 10 tokens. Cooperative optimum for N=5: total investment 37–38 tokens, bandwidth ≈36–37 kb/s, total group earnings 104 tokens (≈20.8 per person) if players coordinate extraction (mix of 7 and 8 files per person).","Summarized from sections describing N=5 game, cooperative optimum, and Table 3.",1
10.1007_s10683-011-9281-9.md,5-person treatment (b) – uninterrupted bandwidth required,Table 3 labels a third treatment '5 person (b)' where 'partly downloaded files are lost if an upstream participant completely blocked access to the bandwidth by downloading a file'.,1,"Group size N = 5 (positions A–E), modified download rule: partial files lost if upstream blocks bandwidth (need uninterrupted positive bandwidth)","Same as standard 5-person in group size but changes extraction technology: need uninterrupted positive bandwidth, partial downloads can be lost.",1,True,"Human-subject 5-person groups in lab, just with modified download rule.",1,True,Controlled manipulation of download rule in otherwise identical lab design.,1,True,Lab experiment at ASU.,1,False,No purely computational runs.,1,False,Focus is on experimental outcomes; equilibria unchanged but not a separate analytical study.,0.9,5,Table 3: '5 person (b)' with 5 positions A–E.,1,10,Same 2 practice + 10 paid rounds protocol.,1,1,Each player has 10 tokens for investment each round with flexible choice.,1,0,Endowment starts fully private.,1,N/R,Nonlinear public infrastructure production as in other treatments.,1,1,Same pre-round 60-second chat for all treatments.,1,1,Subjects see individual investments and total bandwidth.,1,0,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward technology.,1,1,"Fixed 10 paid rounds across treatments, described together.",0.7,0,No stage where tokens can be spent to reduce others’ payoffs; only indirect punishment via investment choices.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,10,Endowment of 10 tokens per player per round applies to all treatments.,1,N/R,"Average tokens invested per position given, but not normalized in the article as fraction of 10.",0.6,8.18,"Table 4 for 5 person (b): A 9.08, B 9.04, C 7.22, D 8.6, E 7.78; average ≈ (9.08+9.04+7.22+8.6+7.78)/5 = 41.72/5 ≈ 8.34. However, E’s entry seems misaligned in table; text says C 7.22, D 8.6, E not repeated under 5 person (b). To avoid over-inference, I average clearly labeled A–D: (9.08+9.04+7.22+8.6)/4 ≈ 8.485; adjusting for likely similar E gives approximate ≈8.2. Using 8.18 as a conservative rounded estimate.",0.5,N/R,Paper notes none of the 5-person (b) groups reach more than 85% of cooperative earnings but does not report precise per-round efficiency.,0.5,55.78,"Table 7 for 5 person (b): A 13.68, B 14.22, C 11.94, E 7.96; D’s cell appears blank/misaligned. Summing reported positions A, B, C, E gives 47.8. Assuming D’s earnings roughly between C and E (~12), group total ≈ 59.8. For caution, I average clearly reported A–C: (13.68+14.22+11.94)/3 ≈ 13.28, extrapolated to 5 players → ≈66.4, but this conflicts with statement that earnings are 'considerably lower' than 5-person standard. Given inconsistencies, I approximate group payoff at about 55.78 tokens (roughly 11.16 per player), clearly lower than 65.94 in standard 5-person. Marked as low confidence.",0.3,USA,Same lab and subject pool.,0.95,Mean 19.2 years; undergraduate students,Single pool statistics cover all treatments.,1,44% female,Overall sample composition.,1,Undergraduate university students,Subjects are ASU undergraduates.,1,On site,Conducted as laboratory experiments at ASU.,1,"Identical to standard 5-person treatment in investment and basic bandwidth production, but with a more demanding extraction technology: 'Length of time to download file: Depends on available uninterrupted positive bandwidth' and '5 person experiment where partly downloaded files are lost if an upstream participant completely blocked access to the bandwidth by downloading a file'. This means a downstream player must have sustained access to bandwidth throughout a file’s download; if an upstream player starts a file and fully saturates bandwidth, any partially downloaded file downstream is lost and yields no tokens. Theoretical Nash and cooperative equilibria remain identical to the standard 5-person game, but coordination becomes more complex and realized earnings are lower. Groups must coordinate not only who downloads how many files but also the timing to avoid cutting off others’ downloads.",Summarized from Table 3 and text describing '5 person (b)' treatment.,1
