of Valencia, Spain.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students.,0.4,N/R,No mention of gender composition.,0,Undergraduate university students,Recruited from pool of undergraduate students.,1,On site,Conducted physically at LINEEX lab.,1,"Same linear public goods game with fixed groups of 4 for 30 rounds. In rounds 11–20 in punishment treatment, after observing contributions, subjects may assign 0–10 punishment units to each other group member; each unit costs 1 ECU to punisher and 3 ECUs to target. Punished subjects see IDs of all punishers and number of punishment points received. No normative messages can be sent. 40 ECUs = €1; 200 ECU show-up payment.",Summarized features of punishment treatment from design and instructions.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0064941.md,Human experiment – Sanction treatment,Row corresponds to sanction treatment where both punishment and normative message are available in rounds 11–20.,1,Availability of punishment and/or normative message during rounds 11–20: combined sanction (material punishment plus normative message).,Sanction treatment allows both assigning punishment points and sending normative message.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled treatment with defined rules and random assignment.,1,True,Conducted at LINEEX lab using z-Tree; subjects in cubicles.,1,False,This is human experiment condition.,1,False,No analytical model here.,1,4,Twelve groups of four subjects per treatment.,1,30,Thirty rounds in all treatments.,1,1,Contributions are integers from 0 to 20.,1,0,Subjects receive 20 ECUs and choose how much to invest; initial allocation is private.,1,0.4,Identical payoff function across treatments.,1,0,No free-form communication; only structured normative messages provided by interface.,1,1,"After each contribution phase, group members see each member’s contribution and own payoff.",1,1,Sanction treatment uses same punishment system as punishment treatment where IDs of punishers are revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Horizon length specified but round counter display not mentioned.,0.3,1,Sanction treatment includes material punishment opportunity after each round.,1,1,Same punishment units as punishment treatment: each costs punisher 1 ECU.,1,3,Each punishment unit reduces target’s payoff by 3 ECUs.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,20 ECUs endowment each round.,1,N/R,"Block-specific average contributions reported (8.23, 14.46, 9.08) but not normalized overall rate.",0.4,N/R,No single overall average contribution amount for sanction treatment; only block averages.,0.4,N/R,Only relative payoff improvements vs other treatments and blocks reported.,0.2,N/R,Exact payoff numbers per group not given.,0.2,Spain,Same subject pool and location as other treatments.,1,N/R,Undergraduates but no age detail.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,0,Undergraduate university students,Shared recruitment procedure across treatments.,1,On site,Conducted in LINEEX lab cubicles.,1,"Identical public goods game with 4-person fixed groups for 30 rounds. In rounds 11–20, after contributions and feedback, participants in sanction treatment can both assign 0–10 punishment points to each other member (cost 1 ECU per unit to punisher, 3 ECU per unit to target, with IDs of punishers revealed) and send a normative message: ‘One should contribute X, because (a) we are all better off; (b) it is what one should do; (c) if not it will have consequences for you.’ Messages cost nothing and have no direct payoff effect. 40 ECUs = €1; 200 ECU show-up payment.",Summarizes rules unique to sanction treatment from instructions and text.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0064941.md,Simulation experiment – Message treatment,Agent-based model condition corresponding to message-only environment.,1,Simulation treatment corresponding to human message treatment: agents can send normative messages but cannot punish; cooperation binary (C or D).,"Simulation reproduces three experimental conditions from lab: punishment, message, sanction.",1,False,No human subjects; agent-based model.,1,False,Not an experiment with human or field subjects; it is a computational simulation.,1,False,"Run as simulation, not in laboratory with subjects.",1,True,Described as agent-based model; parameters like Individual Weight and Normative Weight specified.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations; results presented from simulations.,1,N/R,Text states simulations reproduce public-good game but does not specify group size; likely 4 but not explicitly stated for simulations.,0.3,N/R,"They discuss rounds 11–30 and 21–30, implying multiple rounds, but exact total simulation horizon not explicitly stated.",0.3,0,Cooperation choices are binary: each agent chooses whether to cooperate (C) by contributing a fixed amount or to defect (D) by contributing nothing.,1,N/A,"No human endowment; simulation agents follow modelled utilities, not endowed tokens in same sense.",1,N/R,Simulation reproduces public good game but exact multiplier and group size in formal payoff equation for agents not given in main text.,0.3,N/A,No human communication; only modelled message decisions by agents.,1,N/A,Information is modelled internally (agents update drives) rather than shown to participants.,1,N/A,No human identities; identity revelation is not meaningful.,1,N/A,No rewards and no human IDs.,1,N/A,No human interface in simulation.,1,0,"In message simulation treatment, agents can send messages but cannot punish, mirroring human message treatment.",1,N/A,No punishment in this simulation condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this simulation condition.,1,0,"Model includes only punishment and messages, not rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents do not receive an explicit endowment in ECUs; only payoffs used for updating drives.,1,N/R,"Figure 2A shows cooperation levels over time, but no single numerical value summarized per treatment in text.",0.3,N/R,Binary decisions (C or D) with unspecified contribution magnitude; no numeric token amounts given.,0.2,N/R,They discuss average payoffs qualitatively and refer to Figure S7; no numeric efficiency index.,0.2,N/R,Average payoffs shown only in Supporting Information figure; values not given in main text.,0.2,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model only.,1,"Agent-based model with agents choosing binary cooperation (C contributes fixed amount, D contributes zero) based on a cooperation probability updated each round as a function of an individual drive (instrumental payoff-based, winner-stay-losers-change) and a normative drive (depends on norm salience). In message treatment, agents can send normative messages indicating that the norm prescribes C but cannot punish. Norm salience updated based on observed behaviour and communication; probabilities of sending messages are a function of perceived norm salience. Parameters at initialization for baseline runs shown in Figure 2: Individual Weight = 0.5, Normative Weight = 0.5, Initial Punishment Probability = 0.5, Forgetting Probability = 0.3 (though punishment not used in this treatment).",Summarized from simulation experiment description and figure caption for Figure 2.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0064941.md,Simulation experiment – Punishment treatment,Agent-based model condition corresponding to punishment-only environment.,1,Simulation treatment corresponding to human punishment treatment: agents can punish but cannot send normative messages; cooperation binary (C or D).,Simulation reproduces punishment condition where only punishment is available.,1,False,No human subjects.,1,False,"Simulation, not human or field experiment.",1,False,Not a lab study with humans.,1,True,Described as agent-based simulation.,1,False,No explicit analytical solution.,1,N/R,Group size in simulation not explicitly provided.,0.3,N/R,Rounds discussed but exact horizon for simulations not explicitly stated.,0.3,0,Cooperation decisions are binary C or D.,1,N/A,No human endowment; pure model.,1,N/R,Simulation payoff structure not fully specified numerically in main text.,0.3,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,"Information is internal state updates, not displayed.",1,N/A,No human identities to reveal.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,"In punishment simulation treatment, agents can punish but cannot send messages.",1,N/R,"Text indicates punishment is costly and intensity is binary (high/low) and cost of being punished always greater than net cost of cooperating, but no exact numeric cost per punishment unit.",0.2,N/R,Magnitude of payoff reduction per unit of punishment not numerically specified.,0.2,0,Model includes only punishment and messages.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,N/A,No explicit token endowment like human experiment.,1,N/R,"Panel A of Figure 2 shows cooperation levels, but no numeric summary reported in text.",0.3,N/R,Contribution amount per cooperating agent not numerically specified.,0.2,N/R,Only qualitative statements about payoffs; numeric efficiency not provided.,0.2,N/R,Simulation payoffs summarized graphically in Supporting Information; no numeric values in main text.,0.2,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Pure computational agent-based simulation.,1,"Agent-based public goods environment with binary cooperation. Agents’ cooperation probability updated via individual drive (instrumental, winner-stay-losers-change; defection preferred when it yields higher payoff) and normative drive (depends on norm salience). In punishment treatment, agents can impose costly punishment with binary intensity (high/low); punishment is costly and cost of being punished is always greater than net cost of cooperating. Probability of punishing is negatively affected by number of defectors. No messages are sent in this treatment. Parameter settings for baseline simulation in Figure 2: Individual Weight = 0.5, Normative Weight = 0.5, Initial Punishment Probability = 0.5, Forgetting Probability = 0.3.",From simulation experiment description and Figure 2 caption.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0064941.md,Simulation experiment – Sanction treatment,Agent-based model condition corresponding to combined punishment + message environment.,1,Simulation treatment corresponding to human sanction treatment: agents can both punish and send normative messages; cooperation binary (C or D).,Simulation reproduces sanction condition where both mechanisms are available.,1,False,No human participants.,1,False,"Computational, not human experiment.",1,False,No physical lab with humans.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent-based model; results in Figure 2.,1,False,No mathematical equilibrium analysis reported.,1,N/R,Group size for agents not stated explicitly.,0.3,N/R,"Refers to rounds up to at least 30, but exact total simulation horizon only implied, not clearly specified.",0.3,0,Binary cooperation decision (C vs D).,1,N/A,No explicit monetary endowment per round.,1,N/R,Numerical multiplier not given in simulation description.,0.3,N/A,No human communication; messages are modelled actions.,1,N/A,No user interface; information is processed internally by agents.,1,N/A,No human IDs.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,Sanction simulation treatment allows punishment and messages.,1,N/R,"Punishment costly with high/low intensity, but cost per unit not numerically specified.",0.2,N/R,Payoff reduction from punishment qualitatively described (higher than net cost of cooperating) but no numeric mapping.,0.2,0,Only sanctions and messages; no explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Model does not specify ECUs per round as in human experiment.,1,N/R,Figure 2A shows time series; no single numeric summary reported.,0.3,N/R,Amount contributed under C not specified.,0.2,N/R,Average payoffs are mentioned qualitatively but no efficiency ratio given.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs reported only in Supporting Information figure; no numerical values in main text.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational experiments only.,1,"Agent-based public goods simulation including both material punishment and normative messages. Cooperation decision C vs D updated by interaction of individual drive (payoff-based, updated via winner-stay-losers-change) and normative drive (depends on norm salience, which is updated based on observed behaviour and communication). In sanction simulation treatment, agents can punish defectors with binary punishment intensity (high/low), incurring a cost, and can send normative messages indicating that norm prescribes C. Probability of punishing is negatively affected by number of defectors; probability of sending messages is a direct function of perceived norm salience. Cost of being punished is always greater than net cost of cooperating, so punishment is deterrent. Baseline parameterization for Figure 2: Individual Weight = 0.5, Normative Weight = 0.5, Initial Punishment Probability = 0.5, Forgetting Probability = 0.3. Sanction treatment yields higher and more stable cooperation and lower punishment frequency after round 10 than punishment-only treatment.",Summarized from sections 3 and 4 of paper and Figure 2 caption.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2010.08.010.md,Theoretical model of networks of relations with alternative information structures and punishment strategies,"The paper is purely theoretical, analyzing a repeated-game model under different information structures (I1–I3) and strategy profiles (MGs, MRs) without reporting any empirical, simulation, or field experiments.",1,"Information structure (I1 Public Information, I2 Private Information without Communication, I3 Word-of-Mouth Communication) and punishment strategy (Multilateral Grim Trigger vs Multilateral Repentance)","Across the analysis, the authors vary the information regime and the type of multilateral punishment strategy to study sustainability of cooperation in networks; these are the core comparative statics.",1,False,No human participants or data collection are described; the work is entirely game-theoretic.,1,False,There is no controlled empirical experiment; all results are analytical propositions about equilibria.,1,False,No lab or physical setting is used; only theoretical analysis is presented.,1,False,"The paper does not describe numerical or agent-based simulations, only analytic derivations and proofs.",1,True,"The study develops and analyzes an infinitely repeated-game model, states propositions, and provides formal proofs.",1,0,"No fixed number of players in an experiment; instead, a theoretical set N={1,...,n} of agents is considered. Use 0 for not applicable to an actual game implementation.",0.9,0,"The model assumes infinitely repeated interactions, not a finite number of rounds in an experiment.",1,1,Players choose between two discrete actions (Cooperate C or Defect D) in each bilateral Prisoner’s Dilemma; this corresponds to an all-or-nothing contribution/cooperation decision.,0.9,N/A,There is no endowment allocation or contribution game with a default allocation; the framework is a PD with actions C and D and no explicit endowment.,1,N/R,"The payoffs are given as generic PD payoffs (c, d, l, w) rather than a linear public goods structure with a marginal per capita return.",1,N/A,"There are no human subjects or experimental interface; Word-of-Mouth is a modeled information structure, not actual chat between participants.",1,N/A,No experimental UI or feedback summaries are specified; only theoretical observation structures are defined.,1,N/A,No experimental punishment stage with identity revelation is implemented; sanctions are modeled as strategy-contingent actions in the game.,1,N/A,There is no distinct reward mechanism with identity display in an experiment.,1,N/A,The horizon is infinite and theoretical; no interface informing subjects about rounds exists.,1,0,"There is no separate costly punishment technology; punishment is modeled as future defection or reduced cooperation, not as a dedicated stage where players spend points to punish.",0.9,N/A,No explicit per-unit punishment cost is defined; opportunity costs of lost cooperation are embedded in the PD payoffs.,1,N/A,There is no numeric mapping from punishment points to target loss; punishments occur via equilibrium strategies.,1,0,There is no separate reward mechanism; only cooperation/defection actions with given payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit costly rewarding technology is modeled.,1,N/A,No per-unit reward technology is defined.,1,0,"The stage game is defined via outcome payoffs (c, d, l, w) and not via an endowment and contributions; set to 0 as there is no endowment parameter.",1,N/R,No empirical outcomes are measured; only theoretical conditions for cooperation are derived.,1,N/R,There are no numeric contribution data; actions are symbolic (C or D).,1,N/R,The paper discusses welfare and sustainability conceptually but does not report numerical efficiency measures from experiments.,1,N/R,No realized group payoffs from data are reported; only theoretical payoff expressions are provided.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved; hence no participant country information.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,"The study is a theoretical game-theoretic analysis with no human participants, field data, or lab implementation.",1,"Infinitely repeated asymmetric bilateral Prisoner’s Dilemma interactions on a fixed network of agents; agents discount future payoffs with factor δ<1; three information structures considered (I1 public, I2 private without communication, I3 Word-of-Mouth with possible lying and speed v of message transmission); cooperation is enforced via multilateral trigger strategies (Multilateral Grim Trigger and Multilateral Repentance) and network closure and density conditions are characterized.",Summarizes the core theoretical game and network assumptions described in Sections 2–4.,1
