10.1086_595622.md,Dictator game – Ju/'hoansi,"Paper explicitly describes a dictator game with Ju/'hoansi; treated as one condition because no experimental variation (e.g., stakes, anonymity) across subgroups is reported.",1,Game type = Dictator game; Stake size effectively 9 Namibian $ due to self-imposed minimum gift of 1; Players are anonymous; One-shot play,Condition is defined by being the dictator game with anonymity and $10 laid out but effective stake of 9 because minimum gift was 1.,0.84,True,Human Ju/'hoansi participants played the game for real money.,1,True,Controlled economic game with specified rules and payoffs implemented by researcher.,1,True,"Game conducted in villages at the researcher's hearth under controlled instructions, not embedded in naturally occurring market or social interactions; best classified as lab-style.",0.7,False,No computer or agent-based simulation; only human players.,1,False,No formal closed-form model developed; only empirical games.,1,2,Dictator game involves one proposer and one anonymous recipient.,1,1,Each subject plays the dictator game once; described as one-shot and then a short break before the ultimatum game.,0.95,1,Participants chose any integer number of coins (subject to minimum 1); no binary all-or-nothing restriction.,1,0,All 10 coins start as the proposer’s endowment to divide; initially all are in the proposer’s control (private) before deciding how much to give.,0.8,N/R,No public goods multiplier; this is a bilateral transfer game.,1,0,Instructions given; decisions made privately one by one; no indication that proposer and recipient could communicate.,0.9,0,Only own decision known to experimenter; no feedback about others’ earnings or choices reported.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in dictator game.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism; only sharing decision.,1,1,Participants were told they would play two games; number of games (rounds across game types) was common knowledge.,0.8,0,Dictator game has no strategic punishment option; recipient cannot refuse.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Game is unilateral splitting of an endowment, not costly rewarding by spending on others.",1,N/A,No explicit costly reward stage.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward stage.,1,10,Script: 'You will be given $10 and asked to decide how you will divide the money.' Ten Namibian $1 coins laid out.,1,N/R,Paper reports distribution of offers (1–5) and means (not visible) but not normalized mean fraction in a way that can be reconstructed exactly from this excerpt.,0.3,N/R,"Table 1 with mean offer values not fully reproduced in text; only frequency table by amount is shown, from which mean could be computed if stake basis were clear, but note explains stake considered 9 not 10, creating ambiguity on how the author computed means.",0.4,N/R,No total payoffs relative to a defined efficiency benchmark are reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate or per-pair payoff statistics are provided.,1,Namibia,Ju/'hoansi of the Nyae Nyae area in northeast Namibia; Tsumkwe mentioned as nearby town.,1,Adults and some older individuals; exact age range N/R,Mentions 'five old people' needing more explanation but no age distribution or range reported.,0.4,Mixed male and female; exact proportions N/R,"Text: 'Most of the men, but none of the women, had engaged in wage labor...' and 'All men and women could manage cash'; suggests both genders participated but no counts given.",0.7,Mostly <4 years of schooling; none above 7 years,"For the residents of the three villages: 'All but six of the residents of the three villages had less than 4 years of education, and the rest had less than 7 years.' Participants drawn from these villages.",0.8,Field experiment,"Games implemented in participants’ home villages (Makuri, !Obaha, Xamsa) around the anthropologist’s hearth, not in a formal lab or online.",0.95,"Bilateral dictator game in three Ju/'hoansi villages with real stakes (10 Namibian $1 coins; daily wage) 