10.1086_595622.md,"Ultimatum game – Ju/'hoansi (anonymous, one-shot)","Paper explicitly describes an ultimatum game with Ju/'hoansi; treated as one condition because no systematic manipulation (e.g., varying stakes or anonymity) is reported.",1,Game type = Ultimatum game; Stake size effectively 9 Namibian $ due to self-imposed minimum accepted gift of 1; Players are anonymous; One-shot play; Responder can accept or reject offer.,Condition is defined by being the ultimatum game with same stake and anonymity as dictator game and with rejection possibility.,0.9,True,Human Ju/'hoansi subjects played the ultimatum game for real money.,1,True,Controlled game with specified strategic structure and payoffs implemented by researcher.,1,True,"Conducted under controlled conditions at the researcher’s hearth in villages, not as part of routine economic life.",0.7,False,No non-human simulation used.,1,False,No purely analytical model presented.,1,2,One proposer and one responder in each ultimatum interaction.,1,1,"Each dyad plays once; same participants played one dictator and one ultimatum game, but ultimatum itself is one-shot.",0.95,1,Proposers choose any integer number of coins to offer; responders can accept or reject; no binary-only contribution constraint.,1,0,Entire $10 stake is initially under proposer’s control (private) to divide.,0.8,N/R,Not a public goods game; no group multiplier defined.,1,0,No mention of communication between anonymous proposer and responder.,0.9,0,No information on others’ outcomes provided; only private offer and accept/reject decision.,0.9,0,Anonymity is emphasized; responders do not know proposer’s identity even when rejecting (which is the punishment).,1,0,Anonymity applies; acceptance of offers (a form of allowing payoff) does not reveal identities.,1,1,Subjects were informed that they would play two games in total.,0.8,1,"Responder can reject low offers, leading to zero payoff for both parties; widely interpreted as costly punishment.",1,N