10.1016_j.jpubeco.2017.03.002.md,PUNISH-NOINEQUALITY – 10 periods (randomly paid period),Additional treatment eliminating inequality while keeping growth and punishment; with random single-period payment.,1,Inequality: exogenously eliminated; Growth: endogenous; Punishment: present; Payment rule: random single period; Horizon length: 10 periods,Manipulates inequality and payment scheme while preserving punishment and dynamic growth.,1,True,Described as experimental condition with 56 participants.,1,True,Part of planned set of lab treatments.,1,True,Same in-person lab.,1,False,Human participants involved.,1,False,Empirical data row.,1,4,Group size unchanged.,1,10,Described as 10-period game.,1,1,Same continuous token and punishment choices as main PUNISH.,1,0,Endowments equalized but individually held before contribution.,1,0.375,Group account technology identical to other treatments.,1,0,No mention of communication.,0.9,1,Feedback structure likely mirrors PUNISH; not stated to change.,0.8,0,Same random relabeling procedure likely used; identities obscured.,0.7,0,No rewards implemented.,1,1,"10-period horizon known, but final payment based on random period.",0.8,1,PUNISH-NOINEQUALITY treatment includes punishment stage.,1,1,Punishment cost structure same as PUNISH: cost p to cause 3p loss.,1,3,Punishment multiplier unchanged.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Initial endowment is 20; subsequent endowments equalized via redistribution formula.,1,N/R,Paper only reports that eliminating inequality increases average contributions by 42 tokens in the punishment treatments; no normalized rate reported.,0.3,N/R,"Only relative differences (e.g., NOINEQUALITY vs. baseline) are quantified.",0.3,N/R,No group wealth or maximum benchmark given for this treatment.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not reported for NOINEQUALITY treatments.,0,Netherlands,Same lab location.,0.95,N/R,No age data provided.,0,N/R,No gender data provided.,0,University students,Likely same student subject pool.,0.6,On site,In-person lab sessions as per other conditions.,1,"Dynamic public good with equalized endowments each period and costly punishment. After each period’s returns and punishment, total group wealth is divided equally among four players. One period is selected at random for actual monetary payment.",Combines equal-redistribution and punishment features described in Section 5.2.,1
10.1098_rspb.2012.2043.md,One-shot public good game with punishment (PGP),Only one cooperation/punishment game is described: a one-shot public good game with punishment played by all participants under the same rules.,1,No between-subject treatment manipulation; analysis focuses on continuous individual characteristics (delay discounting parameter k and contribution level) within a single game condition.,"Methods and results describe one homogeneous game structure; variation is in individual delay discounting and contributions, not experimental conditions.",1,True,160 human participants played the PGP and completed the discounting task.,1,True,"The PGP and discounting task were incentivized, controlled behavioral tasks with randomized group composition.",1,False,"Described explicitly as non-laboratory, field experiments conducted in community locations in small towns.",1,False,No computer or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model or closed-form derivations presented; analysis is empirical (regressions).,1,4,"Methods: ""In the PGP, four anonymous players...""",1,1,"Described as a ""one-shot public good game with punishment""; only a single contribution and punishment opportunity.",1,1,"Players decide ""how much money to contribute"" from a €10 endowment, implying a continuous/any-amount choice rather than binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Standard PGG framing: each player gets €10 endowment in a private account and decides how much to move to the public pot; initially all funds are private.,0.9,0.5,"Text: ""The sum of contributions in the pot was multiplied by 2 and evenly shared among the four group members."" MPCR = 2/4 = 0.5; also explicitly α = 2/4.",1,0,No mention of any communication; standard anonymous one-shot PGG with punishment.,0.9,1,"After contribution stage ""The results of the contribution stage were then made public""; thus each participant sees others' contribution outcomes and implied earnings.",0.9,0,"Groups are described as ""four anonymous players""; anonymity implies no identity revelation of who punished whom.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in the game; therefore no reward identities to show.,1,1,"Game is explicitly one-shot; participants are informed of the one-shot nature in instructions (standard for such designs), so they know the total number of rounds.",0.9,1,"Core feature: ""participants were allowed to reduce other group members' earnings at their own cost (punishment stage).""",1,1,"""Participants were allowed to spend up to €3 to reduce other group members' earnings, with each euro spent reducing the target player's earnings by €3."" Thus cost to punisher per punishment unit is €1.",1,3,"Same sentence implies each €1 spent reduces target's earnings by €3, so punishment technology multiplier = 3.",1,0,Only reduction of others' payoffs is available; no mechanism to increase others' earnings is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,10,"Methods: ""four anonymous players endowed with €10"" and ""from their endowment (€10)"".",1,N/R,No aggregate contribution fraction (0–1) is reported; only qualitative statements about contributions and deviations.,0,N/R,Paper does not report mean or distribution of contribution amounts; only individual ranges (0–10) and deviations from group mean.,0,N/R,No measure of total payoff relative to maximum is reported or can be computed without mean contributions.,0,N/R,"They report ""€2585 earned by cooperation"" and ""€496"" reduced via punishment for the whole sample, but not average group payoffs per group or per condition.",0.3,Spain,"Participants were inhabitants of small populations in northern Granada, Andalusia, Spain.",1,"16-82 (mean 46.8, SD 18.5)","Methods state ages between 16 and 82, mean 46.8 ± 18.5 sd.",1,64% female (103 of 160 participants),"Results: ""mean age 46.8 years; 64% females"" and methods: 103 of 160 were female.",1,N/R,"Paper notes participants were ""from all walks of life"" but does not report formal education levels.",0.1,Field experiment,"Described as ""field experiments"" and ""non-laboratory experiments"" conducted in community locations in semi-rural towns.",1,"One-shot public good game with punishment; 4 anonymous players each receive €10 and decide simultaneously how much (0–10) to contribute to a common pot. Total contributions are doubled and evenly divided among all 4. After seeing all contributions, each player can spend up to €3 on punishment, where each €1 spent reduces a chosen partner's payoff by €3; total punishment-induced reduction is capped at €9 per target to avoid negative payoffs. Participants also state expectations about punishment they will receive. Delay discounting (inter-temporal preference) is measured in a separate incentivized task using 20 binary choices between €150 in 1 month vs larger amounts (up to €225) in 7 months, used to estimate individual hyperbolic discount parameter k (0.02–1.211).",Summary assembled from the Methods section description of the PGP and delay discounting task.,1
10.3389_fnbeh.2014.00248.md,Simulation – Institutional rewarding with absolute payoffs,The Results section explicitly analyzes the condition 'INSTITUTIONAL REWARDING WITH ABSOLUTE PAYOFFS' as one of four main simulation regimes.,1,"Type of institutional incentive: rewarding; Payoff type: absolute; Enhancement factor r (e.g., 1, 2); Average available incentives δ; Distribution strength α","Condition differs from others by incentive type (reward), payoff definition (absolute), and explores variations in r, δ, and α.",1,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on Barabási–Albert networks with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; simulations only.,1,True,Methods: 'We perform Monte Carlo simulations of the public goods game described in the Methods section.',1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria; results are numerical.,1,1000,"Results: 'on networks comprising N=1000 to 10,000 nodes.' A single canonical size is not fixed; choose lower bound as representative. Heterogeneity not further specified.",0.3,0,Number of generations/Monte Carlo steps not reported; they only state that they 'determine the stationary fractions' after evolution.,0,1,Standard spatial public goods game with cost c=1 per cooperator; no restriction to binary all-or-nothing; strategies are C or D; contributions are fixed for cooperators.,0.6,0,"Public goods game: cooperators contribute c=1 from their own endowment to the public good; funds are initially in private possession, not pre-allocated to the group.",0.5,N/R,"They report enhancement factor r and group size k_y+1, but do not explicitly give MPCR=r/(k_y+1); group sizes differ by degree, so no single MPCR reported.",0,0,No communication between nodes; only strategy update via imitation.,1,0,No notion of feedback displays; only payoffs used internally for imitation.,1,0,No identities or observability of who punished whom; punishment is institutional and automatic.,1,0,Institutional reward is automatic; no identity information modeled.,1,0,Agents do not observe horizon or number of rounds; infinite/indefinite evolutionary dynamics.,1,0,This condition uses institutional rewarding only.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,1,Institutional incentives I_x are used 'for rewarding cooperators'.,1,1,"Budget I_x=(k_x+1)δ is entirely exogenous institutional resource; cost per reward unit to the institution not decomposed. Effective marginal impact per incentive unit on payoff is 1, since it is added directly.",0.2,1,Each unit from I_x contributes linearly to payoffs; no multiplier for rewards beyond the pool division rule.,0.3,1,Each cooperator pays cost c=1 per game; this is the effective endowment available for contribution in each group interaction.,0.6,N/R,"They focus on stationary fractions of cooperators, not normalized contribution rates beyond strategy frequency.",0,N/R,Individual or average contribution amounts per game not separately reported from cooperation fraction.,0,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) reported.,0,N/R,Group total payoffs not numerically reported; only qualitative payoff differences and cooperation fractions.,0,N/A,No human participants; agent-based Monte Carlo simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational Monte Carlo simulations on networks.,1,"Public goods game on Barabási–Albert scale-free network; each node x (degree k_x) plays in k_x+1 groups (own and neighbors’); cooperators contribute c=1, defectors 0; payoffs in group G_y: (r c n_c)/(k_y+1) minus cost, plus institutional reward term proportional to k_y^α from group budget I_x=(k_x+1)δ; strategy update by Fermi rule with absolute payoffs and noise K=0.1; initial conditions random; stationary fraction of cooperators measured over N=1000–10,000 nodes and averaged over 100 realizations.",Summarizes Methods description specific to this simulation condition: reward payoffs (equations given) and absolute payoff-based imitation.,1
10.3389_fnbeh.2014.00248.md,Simulation – Institutional punishment with absolute payoffs,Results section has a distinct subsection 'INSTITUTIONAL PUNISHMENT WITH ABSOLUTE PAYOFFS'.,1,"Type of institutional incentive: punishment; Payoff type: absolute; Enhancement factor r (e.g., 1, 2); Average available incentives δ; Distribution strength α","Condition differs by using punishment incentive and absolute payoff for imitation; parameters r, δ, α are varied.",1,False,No human subjects; Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,"Computational, not experimental with human participants.",1,False,No lab or field.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations of public goods game on networks.,1,False,No formal analytical solution presented.,1,1000,"Simulations on networks with N=1000 to 10,000 nodes; use lower bound as representative.",0.3,0,Number of Monte Carlo steps to reach stationarity not reported.,0,1,"Same public goods structure: cooperators contribute fixed c=1, defectors 0.",0.6,0,Contributions come from private cost c=1 per cooperator; no initial allocation to public fund.,0.5,N/R,MPCR varies with degree via group size k_y+1; not summarized as single value.,0,0,No communication modeled among agents.,1,0,Agents do not receive explicit summaries; they only use payoffs for imitation.,1,0,Institutional punishment is an automatic fine; no identities specified.,1,0,No reward in this condition.,1,0,No explicit horizon information in the model.,1,1,Incentives I_x are used 'for punishing defectors'.,1,1,Fines are drawn from institutional pool I_x and subtracted linearly from defectors' payoffs; each unit of I_x corresponds to one unit of fine.,0.3,1,One unit of punishment budget results in one unit punishment effect on payoff; no multiplier specified.,0.3,0,This regime uses punishment instead of reward.,1,N/A,No rewards present in this condition.,1,N/A,No rewards present in this condition.,1,1,Cooperators pay cost c=1 per group they participate in; effective endowment per game.,0.6,N/R,"They report fractions of cooperators, not normalized contributions per se.",0,N/R,No explicit token or payoff contribution totals reported.,0,N/R,Group efficiency not quantified numerically.,0,N/R,Only qualitative payoff comparisons between C and D across degrees.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same Barabási–Albert scale-free network and public goods structure as reward condition; institutional incentives I_x=(k_x+1)δ are now used to fine defectors. In group G_x, a cooperator’s payoff is (r c n_c)/(k_x+1)−c, a defector’s payoff is (r c n_c)/(k_x+1) minus its share of I_x proportional to k_y^α over all defectors. Distribution strength α>0 means high-degree defectors receive larger fines; α<0 reverses this. Strategy updating uses the Fermi rule with absolute total payoffs (Equation 1) and noise K=0.1.",Summarizes Methods equations for punishment case with absolute payoffs.,1
10.3389_fnbeh.2014.00248.md,Simulation – Institutional rewarding with degree-normalized payoffs,Results subsection 'INSTITUTIONAL REWARDING WITH DEGREE-NORMALIZED PAYOFFS' describes this distinct payoff regime.,1,"Type of institutional incentive: rewarding; Payoff type: degree-normalized; Enhancement factor r (e.g., 1, 2, 3); Average available incentives δ; Distribution strength α","Condition differs by using reward incentives and degree-normalized payoff in the Fermi update; r, δ, α varied.",1,False,No human subjects; simulations only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on scale-free networks.,1,False,No closed-form analytical results.,1,1000,"Networks comprising N=1000 to 10,000 nodes; select 1000 as representative.",0.3,0,Number of simulation steps until stationarity not specified.,0,1,Same C/D public goods contributions as other regimes.,0.6,0,Cooperators contribute from their own unit cost; no default public allocation.,0.5,N/R,MPCR depends on degree-specific group size; no single value given.,0,0,No communication among agents.,1,0,No feedback display; only payoff-based strategy imitation.,1,0,Punishment absent; identities not modeled.,1,0,Reward allocation is institutional and automatic; no identity information.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary process; no round info to agents.,1,0,"This regime uses institutional rewarding, not punishment.",1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,1,I_x incentives are used to reward cooperators.,1,1,Institutional incentives I_x are linearly allocated as rewards to cooperators; one unit of I_x increases payoff by one.,0.3,1,No amplification of rewards beyond direct addition of I_x shares.,0.3,1,Per-game cooperative cost c=1.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes reported as stationary cooperator fractions, not normalized contribution rates.",0,N/R,No explicit total contributions reported.,0,N/R,No direct measure of efficiency reported.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically summarized.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Same network and payoff structure as in absolute-reward case, but strategy updating uses degree-normalized payoffs: imitation probability f=1/[1+exp((P_x/(k_x+1)−P_y/(k_y+1))/K)]. Rewards are still computed from I_x=(k_x+1)δ distributed among cooperators in G_x proportional to k_y^α. Enhancement factors r=1,2,3 examined; δ and α varied, and stationary cooperator fractions measured.",Combines Methods equation for degree-normalized payoffs with reward payoff definition.,1
10.3389_fnbeh.2014.00248.md,Simulation – Institutional punishment with degree-normalized payoffs,Results subsection 'INSTITUTIONAL PUNISHMENT WITH DEGREE-NORMALIZED PAYOFFS' covers this regime.,1,"Type of institutional incentive: punishment; Payoff type: degree-normalized; Enhancement factor r (e.g., 1, 2, 3); Average available incentives δ; Distribution strength α","Condition defined by using punishment incentives and degree-normalized payoffs with parameters r, δ, α varied.",1,False,No human data; simulated agents on networks.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations described in Methods and Results.,1,False,No analytical solution reported.,1,1000,"Simulations performed on networks with N=1000–10,000 nodes; 1000 used as representative size.",0.3,0,Number of evolutionary steps not specified.,0,1,Same public goods game with binary strategies and fixed cooperative cost.,0.6,0,Contributions are from private cost c=1; no initial public allocation.,0.5,N/R,Enhancement factor r and group size vary by degree; no single MPCR summarized.,0,0,Communication is absent in the model.,1,0,No explicit display of others’ outcomes; only local payoffs affect imitation.,1,0,Institutional punishment is non-personal; no identities associated.,1,0,No rewards in this regime.,1,0,Agents do not know or use total round count.,1,1,I_x incentives are used to fine defectors as institutional punishment.,1,1,Punishment budget I_x is translated linearly into fines for defectors; one budget unit implies one payoff unit subtracted.,0.3,1,No multiplicative amplification specified for fines beyond allocation rule.,0.3,0,Only punishment incentives considered.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Cooperators incur cost c=1 per game.,0.6,N/R,"Results reported via stationary cooperation levels, not normalized contributions.",0,N/R,No explicit token contribution statistics given.,0,N/R,Efficiency not measured or reported numerically.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not directly reported.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Same public goods and network framework as other regimes, but strategy updating uses degree-normalized payoffs: f=1/[1+exp((P_x/(k_x+1)−P_y/(k_y+1))/K)]. Institutional punishment assigns I_x=(k_x+1)δ across defectors in G_x proportional to k_y^α, with α>0 focusing punishment on high-degree defectors and α<0 on low-degree defectors. Enhancement factor r values 1,2,3 explored; δ and α varied, and stationary cooperator fractions measured along with degree-stratified payoffs.",Summarizes the specific punishment with degree-normalized payoff dynamics described in Methods and Results.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.06.041.md,Compulsory PGG – No punishment,Paper defines this as one of three compulsory game conditions: 'No punishment' with strategies C-NNN and D-NNN.,1,"Compulsory public goods game; punishment availability condition = No punishment (strategies C-NNN, D-NNN only); mutation rate u varied; selection strength w varied in later analysis.",Condition differs by which punishment options/strategies are allowed; mutation rate and selection strength are systematically varied within this strategy set.,0.9,False,Model is evolutionary game theory with agent-based simulations and analytical calculations; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,True,Authors use 'agent based simulations' of a Moran process and provide analytical results.,1,True,They derive closed-form expressions for ΔC in the high mutation limit.,1,100,Compulsory game simulations use N=100.,1,10000000,They simulate 10^7 generations and average over the second half.,0.9,0,Players 'decide whether or not to contribute a fixed amount c' – binary cooperate/defect choice.,1,N/A,No human subjects; default endowment allocation not applicable.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/n; for compulsory game simulations r=3 and n=5, so 3/5=0.6.",1,N/A,"No human participants, so no communication interface.",1,N/A,No user interface; simulation only.,1,N/A,No identities; strategies interact anonymously in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No subjects to whom the number of rounds would be shown.,1,0,"In 'No punishment' condition, punishment is not an option; only C-NNN and D-NNN are allowed.",1,N/A,Punishment not available in this condition.,1,N/A,Punishment not available in this condition.,1,0,No mention of reward; only costly punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,1,Contribution amount c is fixed and set to 1 in simulations (ω=β=c=1). Endowment effectively equals c per round.,0.9,N/R,"They report frequencies of cooperative strategies, not normalized contribution rates per se.",0.3,N/R,No explicit average contributions reported; only frequencies of cooperators/defectors.,0.3,N/R,Total group payoff relative to maximum is not reported.,0.1,N/R,"They do not provide aggregate payoffs from simulations, only frequencies.",0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study uses simulations and analytical models only.,1,"Compulsory public goods game with group size n=5, population size N=100, multiplication factor r=3, cost of contributing c=1; no punishment strategies allowed; evolutionary dynamics via frequency-dependent Moran process with mutation rate u varied and selection strength w varied in some analyses.",Collected from model description and parameter listings for compulsory PGG simulations.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.06.041.md,Compulsory PGG – Pro-social punishment,"Paper defines this compulsory condition as 'Pro-social punishment' with strategies C-NNN, C-NPN, and D-NNN.",1,"Compulsory public goods game; punishment availability condition = Pro-social punishment (only cooperators may punish defectors; strategies C-NNN, C-NPN, D-NNN); mutation rate u varied; selection strength w varied.",Condition differs by strategy set allowing only pro-social punishment; mutation and selection intensity varied.,0.9,False,Purely theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No empirical experiment with subjects.,1,False,No lab or field experiment.,1,True,Agent-based simulations of a Moran process plus analytical calculations.,1,True,High-mutation-limit expressions for ΔC are derived for this strategy set.,1,100,Simulations use N=100.,1,10000000,They simulate 10^7 generations and average over the second half.,0.9,0,Binary decision to contribute fixed amount c or not.,1,N/A,No human endowment interface.,1,0.6,MPCR = r/n = 3/5 in compulsory game simulations.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No UI in simulations.,1,N/A,No identities for agents.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,Condition explicitly allows punishment of defectors by cooperators.,1,0.3,γ is the cost to punisher per target; simulations use γ=0.3.,1,1,"β is the cost per punishment received; simulations specify ω=β=c=1, so β=1.",1,0,"Only punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,1,Contribution cost c set to 1 (ω=β=c=1); per-round endowment effectively equals c.,0.9,N/R,"They present cooperator frequency, not normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No explicit mean contribution amount over runs reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculation reported.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not tabulated.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation and analytical model only.,1,"Compulsory public goods game, N=100, n=5, r=3, c=1; strategy set limited to C-NNN (non-punishing cooperator), C-NPN (cooperator punishing defectors), D-NNN (non-punishing defector). Punishment cost γ=0.3 to punisher, damage β=1 to target. Evolutionary dynamics via Moran process; mutation rate u and selection intensity w varied.",Summarizes the model parameters and strategy set for this condition from Sections 2–4 and figure captions.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.06.041.md,Compulsory PGG – Full punishment,Paper defines 'Full punishment' condition for compulsory game where all 8 strategies are available.,1,"Compulsory public goods game; punishment availability condition = Full punishment (all 8 W-XYN strategies with W∈{C,D}, X,Y∈{P,N}); mutation rate u varied; selection strength w varied.",Condition differs by allowing all punishment types (including antisocial); mutation and selection intensity varied.,0.9,False,Purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Not a lab or field study.,1,True,Uses agent-based simulations plus analytical results.,1,True,High-mutation-limit payoffs and ΔC computed for full strategy set.,1,100,Compulsory game simulations use population size N=100.,1,10000000,Simulations run for 10^7 generations.,0.9,0,Binary cooperate/defect choice regarding contributing fixed amount c.,1,N/A,No human endowment management.,1,0.6,MPCR = r/n = 3/5.,1,N/A,No communication in a human sense.,1,N/A,No GUI; simulation only.,1,N/A,No identities represented.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,1,"All punishment options, including anti-social, are enabled in this strategy set.",1,0.3,Punisher cost γ=0.3 per victim.,1,1,Target cost β=1 per punishment (β=c=1).,1,0,Rewards do not appear in the model.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,1,Contribution amount c is fixed to 1; that is the effective per-round stake.,0.9,N/R,"They report frequencies of cooperators vs defectors, not normalized contributions.",0.3,N/R,No explicit average contributions given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit total-payoff-over-maximum measure reported.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not tabulated.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based and analytical model only.,1,"Compulsory PGG with N=100, n=5, r=3, c=1. Full punishment strategy set with 8 strategies (W-XYN, W∈{C,D}, X,Y∈{P,N}). Punishment costs: γ=0.3 to punisher, β=1 to punished. Evolutionary dynamics via Moran process with mutation rate u and selection strength w varied; high-mutation-limit analytics show ΔC = −Fc/2 as in no-punishment case.",Summarizes parameters and payoff structure from Sections 2–4 and Appendix A.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.06.041.md,Voluntary PGG – No punishment,"Paper defines this as one of four voluntary game conditions: 'No punishment' with strategies C-NNN, D-NNN, L-NNN.",1,"Voluntary public goods game; punishment availability condition = No punishment (strategies C-NNN, D-NNN, L-NNN only); mutation rate u varied; selection strength w varied.",Condition differs by strategy set and presence of voluntary participation; mutation/selection varied.,0.9,False,Study is theoretical with simulations; no humans.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Agent-based simulations of an evolutionary Moran process.,1,True,High-mutation-limit ΔC derived for this voluntary no-punishment strategy set.,1,100,Voluntary game simulations use N=100.,1,10000000,They simulate 10^7 generations.,0.9,0,Cooperators contribute fixed c; defectors contribute 0; loners abstain.,1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,0.6,"Same PGG parameters used: r=3, n=5 => MPCR=3/5.",1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No UI.,1,N/A,No punishment enabled.,1,N/A,Rewards not considered.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,0,"By definition, no punishment is available.",1,N/A,Punishment disabled.,1,N/A,Punishment disabled.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism mentioned.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,1,Contribution cost c set to 1; that is the per-game contribution amount.,0.9,N/R,"They present frequencies of C, D, L, not normalized contributions.",0.3,N/R,No explicit contribution averages.,0.3,N/R,No report of efficiency metric.,0.1,N/R,No group payoffs from simulations shown.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Analytical and simulation study.,1,"Voluntary PGG with strategies C (cooperate), D (defect), L (loner). Groups of size n=5 drawn from N=100 each round. Cooperators contribute c=1; defectors contribute 0; loners receive loner payoff σ=1 and do not participate when group has <2 non-loners. No punishment allowed. Parameters: r=3, γ not used, β not used, σ=1. Moran process dynamics with mutation rate u and selection intensity w varied.","Compiled from Sections 2, 5, Appendix A, and figure captions describing voluntary no-punishment condition.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.06.041.md,Voluntary PGG – Pro-social punishment,"Voluntary condition where only punishment of defectors by cooperators is allowed; strategies C-NNN, C-NPN, D-NNN, L-NNN.",1,"Voluntary public goods game; punishment availability condition = Pro-social punishment (only cooperators can punish defectors; strategies C-NNN, C-NPN, D-NNN, L-NNN); mutation rate u varied; selection strength w varied.",Condition defined by restricted punishment strategy set combined with voluntary participation; parameters u and w varied.,0.9,False,No human or field data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,"Agent-based simulations for this condition (Fig. 3b, Fig. 4a, Fig. 6b).",1,True,High-mutation-limit ΔC formula derived for this strategy set.,1,100,Voluntary simulations use N=100.,1,10000000,Each simulation run lasts 10^7 generations.,0.9,0,Binary choice to contribute fixed c or not; loners abstain.,1,N/A,No human participants or initial allocations.,1,0.6,"PGG parameters are r=3, n=5 so MPCR=3/5.",1,N/A,No humans to communicate.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No player identities.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,Cooperators can pay to punish defectors in this condition.,1,0.3,Punisher cost γ=0.3 per punished individual.,1,1,Punished player incurs cost β=1 (ω=β=σ=c=1).,1,0,No rewards described.,1,N/A,Rewards not used in the model.,1,N/A,Rewards not used in the model.,1,1,Contribution amount c is set to 1; effectively the per-game endowment.,0.9,N/R,"Results reported as frequencies of strategy types (C, D, L) rather than normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No mean token contribution reported.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency measure not given.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff values not shown.,0.1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,All results from simulations/analytics.,1,"Voluntary PGG with N=100, n=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1. Strategy set: C-NNN (non-punishing cooperator), C-NPN (cooperator punishing defectors), D-NNN (non-punishing defector), L-NNN (loner). Punishment cost γ=0.3, damage β=1. Mutations introduce new strategies uniformly at rate u; selection intensity w varied (including w=1 baseline and range w∈[0.1,10]). High-mutation analytics show ΔC includes positive term in (β−γ) and punishment promotes cooperation compared to no-punishment case.","Summarizes model and parameterization from Sections 2, 5, 6 and Appendix A.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.06.041.md,Voluntary PGG – Full punishment,"Voluntary condition where all 24 W-XYZ strategies are allowed, including antisocial punishment.",1,"Voluntary public goods game; punishment availability condition = Full punishment (all 24 W-XYZ strategies with W∈{C,D,L}, X,Y,Z∈{P,N}); mutation rate u varied; selection strength w varied.",Condition varies by inclusion of all punishment types; u and w also varied.,0.9,False,Study is theoretical/simulation-based.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab environment.,1,True,"Agent-based simulations used for this condition (Fig. 3c, Fig. 4b, Fig. 6c).",1,True,"High-mutation-limit analytical ΔC derived, showing equivalence to no-punishment case when antisocial punishment allowed.",1,100,Population size N=100 in voluntary simulations.,1,10000000,Simulations run for 10^7 generations.,0.9,0,Fixed c contribution vs no contribution; loners abstain.,1,N/A,No human economic interface.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5 so MPCR=3/5.",1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Agents have no identities.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,1,"All forms of punishment (including antisocial) available to C, D, L strategies.",1,0.3,Punisher cost γ=0.3 per punished player.,1,1,Target cost β=1 per punishment; β=σ=c=1.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,1,Contribution amount c is fixed at 1.,0.9,N/R,"They report frequencies of C, D, and L strategies, not normalized group contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,Average contribution not numerically reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency numbers.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff figures presented.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulations and analytical calculations only.,1,"Voluntary PGG with N=100, n=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1. Full punishment strategy set of 24 strategies: W∈{C,D,L}, X,Y,Z∈{P,N}. Punishment can target cooperators, defectors, and loners. Punishment cost γ=0.3, damage β=1. Evolutionary dynamics via Moran process, mutation rate u varied and selection strength w varied. High-mutation analytics show ΔC equals the no-punishment case, meaning punishment does not promote cooperation when antisocial punishment is possible.","Summarizes voluntary full-punishment condition from Sections 2, 5, 6, and Appendix A.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.06.041.md,Voluntary PGG – Loners alone,"Voluntary condition labeled 'Loners alone', where loners cannot punish or be punished; only certain C and D punishment strategies plus L-NNN allowed.",1,"Voluntary public goods game; punishment availability condition = Loners alone (cooperators and defectors may punish, but loners cannot punish or be punished; 9 strategies: 4 C-types, 4 D-types, 1 L-NNN); mutation rate u varied; selection strength w varied.",Condition is defined by restricting punishment interactions involving loners; u and w varied in simulations.,0.9,False,No empirical data; model + simulations.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,"Agent-based simulations run for this condition (Fig. 3d, Fig. 4c, Fig. 6d).",1,True,High-mutation-limit ΔC derived showing punishment inhibits cooperation in this strategy set.,1,100,Simulations use N=100.,1,10000000,10^7 generations simulated.,0.9,0,Fixed c contribution vs no contribution; loners abstain.,1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5 so MPCR=3/5.",1,N/A,No human communication interface.,1,N/A,No UI in simulations.,1,N/A,No identities of agents.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,1,"Punishment among cooperators and defectors is allowed, but loners cannot punish or be punished.",1,0.3,Punisher cost γ=0.3 per target.,1,1,Target cost β=1 as in other voluntary simulations (ω=β=σ=c=1).,1,0,No reward mechanisms discussed.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,1,Contribution amount c=1.,0.9,N/R,"They report frequencies of C, D, L and of punishing strategies, not normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No explicit numerical contributions given.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency metric not presented.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff figures are not provided.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Condition analyzed via agent-based simulations and analytics only.,1,"Voluntary PGG with N=100, n=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1. Strategy set restricted to 9 strategies: four cooperator strategies (C-NNN, C-NPN, C-PNN, C-PPN), four defector strategies (D-NNN, D-NPN, D-PNN, D-PPN), and one loner strategy (L-NNN). Loners cannot punish or be punished. Punishment parameters γ=0.3, β=1. High-mutation-limit analytics yield ΔC that decreases with both β and γ, implying punishment inhibits cooperation under this 'loners alone' rule; simulations confirm that defectors dominate cooperators when mutations are sufficiently common.","Summarizes model specification from Section 2 (voluntary 'loners alone' condition), Section 5, and Appendix A.",1
