10.1177_0037549711406912.md,REPAGE market simulation – L1 Image-only condition,Paper describes a first artificial market study with two conditions: L1 (only image transmitted) and L2 (image + reputation). This row is for L1.,0.96,Type of social information available: only image transmission (no reputation); varying percentage of informational cheaters and distributions of good/bad products.,L1 is defined as the condition where only image is transmitted; cheating rate and product distributions are parameters varied across runs.,0.9,False,Study is clearly an agent-based simulation with artificial agents in a market-like environment using REPAGE architecture; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational model exploration.,1,False,No physical lab or human subjects; simulation only.,1,True,Described explicitly as agent-based simulations (ABSs) using REPAGE in a market scenario.,1,False,No closed-form mathematical analysis; results come from simulations.,1,N/R,Only percentages of Buyers and Sellers are mentioned; exact number of agents is not specified.,0,N/R,Number of simulation ticks/rounds for REPAGE market scenario is not reported.,0,1,"Agents choose partners and interact; there is no mention of binary all-or-nothing contribution constraints as in public goods games. They act with flexible decisions, so mapped as non-all-or-nothing (coded 1 per instructions).",0.6,N/A,No human endowments or contribution decisions; not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods structure or MPCR is defined in this market scenario.,1,N/A,"Communication is modeled as exchange of evaluations between artificial agents, not human chat.",1,N/R,No equivalent construct described for this simulation.,0,N/A,No explicit punishment action with identity revelation; retaliation is modeled via sending false information but not via an interface.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,Not applicable to artificial agents in this context; not reported.,0,1,"Informational cheaters are punished with false information, described as a sanction; this counts as a punishment mechanism.",0.8,N/R,Costs of punishing in terms of payoffs or resources are not numerically specified.,0,N/R,Magnitude of punishment effect per unit effort is not parameterized numerically in text.,0,0,No mechanism where agents can actively reward others is mentioned.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Not a contribution/endowment game; market agents have stocks of products but no per-period endowment is specified in comparable units.,1,N/A,"Outcome of interest is quality of products and discovery of good sellers, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They report system performance and quality of products, but do not normalize by theoretical maximum to provide an efficiency index.",0.3,N/R,Payoffs are discussed qualitatively (system performance) without explicit numeric group payoff.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly an agent-based simulation with artificial agents only.,1,Agent-based market scenario with Buyers and Sellers using the REPAGE cognitive architecture; two conditions tested regarding type of social evaluations transmitted. This L1 condition allows exchange and use of only image (personal evaluations) with informational cheating present. Performance measured via average product quality across 28 market scenarios with varying cheating rates and product quality distributions.,Summarizes the key structural aspects of the simulation as described in the REPAGE section.,0.94
10.1177_0037549711406912.md,REPAGE market simulation – L2 Image + Reputation condition,Same REPAGE market study; L2 is defined as the condition where both image and reputation circulate.,0.96,Type of social information available: both image and reputation transmission; varying percentage of informational cheaters and distributions of good/bad products.,"L2 allows agents to circulate and receive both image and reputation; other parameters (cheating, product distributions) varied across runs.",0.9,False,Agent-based simulation with artificial agents; no human data.,1,False,Computational study rather than empirical experiment.,1,False,No human or physical lab.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent-based simulations using REPAGE.,1,False,"Results come from simulations, not analytical derivations.",1,N/R,Number of Buyers and Sellers not specified.,0,N/R,Simulation ticks/rounds not reported.,0,1,Agents flexibly choose partners and actions; no binary contribution constraint described.,0.6,N/A,No endowment or contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,N/A,"Communication is modeled algorithmically among artificial agents, not human chat.",1,N/R,No such parameter is specified.,0,N/A,No explicit identity-based punishment interface; retaliation is internal to simulation logic.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,Not described for artificial agents.,0,1,Informational cheaters are punished with false information; as in L1.,0.8,N/R,No numeric cost parameter detailed.,0,N/R,Magnitude of punishment not numerically parameterized in text.,0,0,No active rewarding of partners or informers is implemented.,0.95,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No notion of per-round endowment as in public goods games.,1,N/A,No contributions defined.,1,N/A,No contributions defined.,1,N/R,They emphasize quality of products and identification of liars; no normalized efficiency metric is provided.,0.3,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are discussed qualitatively as performance; no numeric payoff reported.,0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based simulations.,1,"Same market-like environment as L1 with Buyers and Sellers. Agents use the REPAGE architecture to form image and reputation of potential partners based on direct experience and others’ reports. In L2, agents exchange both image (personal evaluations) and reputation (meta-evaluations). Main dependent measure is quality of products obtained under varying informational cheating rates; results show reputation helps identify liars and maintains higher product quality up to about 60% cheaters before system performance collapses.",Condenses structural details given for L2 condition and its reported performance differences vs. L1.,0.95
10.1177_0037549711406912.md,SOCRATE industrial district simulation – Image-only condition,"In the SOCRATE model, two settings are tested: image setting and reputation setting. This row refers to the image-only setting.",0.97,"Type of social information: image-only (agents transmit only their personal evaluations, exposing themselves to retaliation); percentage of informational cheaters varied.","Text contrasts an image setting, where only personal evaluations (image) are transmitted and cheaters can be retaliated against, with a reputation setting.",0.95,False,Described as a simulation model (SOCRATE) of an artificial industrial district; no humans involved.,1,False,No empirical experimental protocol; parameter sweeps in a simulation.,1,False,No lab or field environment with human subjects.,1,True,Explicitly an agent-based model of firms in an industrial district (SOCRATE).,1,False,No analytical solutions; outcome measures from simulations.,1,300,They state: '300 agents on three layers interacting for 300 simulation ticks' when describing this model.,1,300,Same sentence specifies 300 simulation ticks; treated as number of rounds.,1,1,Firms choose among suppliers and informers; no binary all-or-nothing choice structure.,0.7,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods structure; outcome is product quality and profits.,1,N/A,Communication is automated information exchange among simulated firms.,1,N/R,No mention of displaying summaries; not applicable to human interface.,0,N/A,Punishment is modeled via sending false information in response to having received false image; no user interface or identity display.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,Artificial agents do not have a GUI with remaining rounds; not reported.,0,1,"When agents receive a false image, they 'retaliate by providing cheaters with false information'—a punishment mechanism.",0.95,N/R,No numeric cost of retaliation is specified.,0,N/R,"Magnitude of punishment per act (e.g., reduction in payoff) is not quantified.",0,0,No formal reward of other agents is described; only punishment via false information.,0.95,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit per-round endowment; firms produce and trade products instead.,1,N/A,"Dependent variables concern quality of production and profits, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution mechanic.,1,N/R,"They discuss economic performance and profits qualitatively, but do not report normalized efficiency.",0.3,N/R,Cluster profits are mentioned as higher or lower but not given numerically.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Clearly described as an agent-based model of an industrial district.,1,"SOCRATE agent-based model simulates an ideal-typical industrial district with 300 firm-agents organized into three layers. Agents engage in informational exchange (asking informers about suppliers) and material exchange (choosing suppliers) to maximize product quality. In the image setting, agents transmit only their own evaluations (image); when they receive a false image they retaliate against the identified cheater by sending false information (recommending the worst supplier from their experience list). Simulations run for 300 ticks and are repeated 10 times per condition. Performance is measured via average quality of production and cluster profits for different percentages of informational cheaters.",Summarizes the structural and procedural details explicitly provided for the image-only condition.,0.96
10.1177_0037549711406912.md,SOCRATE industrial district simulation – Reputation condition,"This is the second setting of the SOCRATE model, where reputation is exchanged instead of image-only information.",0.97,"Type of social information: reputation condition (agents exchange reputational information without reporting the source, preventing retaliation); percentage of informational cheaters varied.",Text explicitly contrasts 'reputation setting' to 'image setting'; key manipulation is whether evaluations are source-indefinite and thus non-retaliable.,0.95,False,Agent-based computational simulation; no human data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment but a simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field; purely simulated.,1,True,Described as a model implemented and tested via simulations.,1,False,No analytical solution; uses simulation runs.,1,300,Same SOCRATE setup: '300 agents on three layers interacting for 300 simulation ticks'.,1,300,Same as image setting: 300 simulation ticks per run.,1,1,Firms flexibly choose partners and informers; no binary contribution restriction.,0.7,N/A,No contribution or endowment game.,1,N/R,No public goods structure or MPCR discussed.,1,N/A,"Communication is simulated exchange of messages among agents, not human chat.",1,N/R,No equivalent feature described.,0,N/A,"In reputation condition, retaliation is de facto impossible because sources are unknown; no explicit punishment identity display.",1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanisms described.,1,N/R,No GUI or round display described for agents.,0,0,"In this setting, 'reactions against bad informers were de facto impossible' since the source of a false reputation is unknown; thus there is effectively no punishment mechanism.",0.9,N/A,No punishment is possible in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No description of rewarding behavior among firms.,0.95,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Not a contribution framework.,1,N/A,"Dependent variables are product quality and economic performance, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,They mention higher quality and profits but no normalized efficiency index.,0.3,N/R,"Cluster’s total profits are described qualitatively (higher in reputation networks), not numerically.",0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation of firms’ interactions; no human subjects.,1,"Same SOCRATE industrial district structure as the image condition: 300 firms on three layers, engaging in informational and material exchange to choose suppliers and maximize product quality, with varying percentages of informational cheaters and also alternative distributions of firms across layers. In the reputation condition, agents exchange reputational information, where evaluations are communicated without disclosing their source, making it impossible to retaliate against bad informers. The system records results of past interactions with suppliers and stores informers’ suggestions separately. Performance measures include average quality of production and profits of the cluster. Results show that with reputational information, clusters sustain higher average product quality and profits than image-based clusters, especially when the percentage of cheaters is high (up to about 75%).","Captures the structure and differential behavioral rule (no retaliation, source-indefinite evaluations) that define the reputation condition.",0.96
10.1177_0956797617752642.md,Control condition – PGG with punishment,"Paper describes a control condition that is the standard public-goods game with punishment, without an observer or subsequent trust-game partner choice.",1,"Partner-choice competition: absent (no observer, no subsequent trust-game partner choice).",Only factor differing between conditions is presence vs. absence of an observer who later chooses a partner for a trust game.,1,True,Human participants recruited from University of Guelph psychology participant pool.,1,True,Controlled lab economic game with random assignment by arrival pattern and specified conditions.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory using computers and z-Tree software.,1,False,No indication of agent-based or numerical simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No formal mathematical model developed; focus is on empirical results.,1,4,Participants played in groups of four people in the PGG.,1,5,Participants played five rounds of a PGG with punishment.,1,1,"Each group member could contribute any number of L$10 to the public good, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Each round, each group member received L$10 as a private endowment and decided how much to contribute; endowment initially in private account.",1,0.5,Experimenter doubled all contributions and redistributed evenly among 4 members: each L$1 contributed yields L$2/4 = L$0.50 to each player.,1,0,No mention of communication; participants sat at computers with visual dividers and only made decisions via software.,0.9,1,"After contribution, participants found out what everyone contributed in that round, which is a summary of others’ behavior.",1,0,"Participants were not told who punished them, only the total that they had been punished.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,Participants were not told the number of rounds.,1,1,Participants had the option to punish others by reducing their earnings.,1,1,Every L$1 spent on punishment reduced the target’s earnings by L$3.,1,3,Punishment technology: 1 unit spent reduces target payoff by 3 units (L$3 per L$1 spent).,1,0,No option to positively reward others is described; only punishment (reducing others’ earnings).,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,10,"In each round, each group member received L$10.",1,N/R,Average normalized contribution (0–1) is not explicitly reported; only raw contributions in L$ are provided.,0.2,6.25,First-round mean contribution in control condition reported as M = L$6.25.,1,N/R,No aggregate efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) reported or derivable from given summary statistics.,0.1,N/R,Group-level total payoff amounts are not reported.,0.1,Canada,"Participants were students at the University of Guelph in Ontario, Canada.",1,"Mean age 19.6 years (SD = 2.0), combined across conditions.",Reported overall for the full sample; not broken down by condition.,0.9,"75 females, 42 males total across both conditions; condition-specific breakdown not reported.","Paper reports only overall gender counts, not by condition.",0.6,"University students (psychology participant pool, earning course credit).",Recruited from University of Guelph psychology participant pool.,1,On site,"Participants came to a lab, sat at computers with dividers; not an online or field setting.",1,Standard linear public-goods game with punishment; group size 4; contributions doubled and evenly redistributed (MPCR 0.5); 5 rounds with stranger groups fixed across rounds; punishment anonymous; participants not told total number of rounds.,"Summarizes core structural aspects of the PGG with punishment from the procedure description, specific to the control condition (no observer, no trust game).",1
10.1177_0956797617752642.md,Markets condition – PGG with punishment plus observer and trust-game partner choice,Paper describes a markets (biological-markets) condition where an observer later chooses a PGG participant for a trust game based on their contributions.,1,Partner-choice competition: present (observer sees average contributions and chooses a partner for a subsequent trust game).,Only difference relative to control condition is the presence of the observer and subsequent trust game linking PGG contributions to being chosen.,1,True,Human participants recruited from University of Guelph psychology participant pool.,1,True,Controlled lab economic game with specified treatment manipulation (presence of observer/partner choice).,1,True,Conducted on-site in a lab using computers and z-Tree software.,1,False,All data come from human participants; no computational simulations described.,1,False,Study does not develop or analyze a formal mathematical model.,1,4,"PGG groups consisted of four active players; markets sessions additionally had a fifth participant acting as observer, but not as a PGG player.",1,5,Participants played five rounds of a PGG with punishment.,1,1,Each group member could contribute any number of their L$10 to the public good.,1,0,"Endowment given as L$10 privately each round, from which participants decide how much to contribute.",1,0.5,Experimenter doubled all contributions and redistributed equally among the 4 group members (L$2/4 per L$1 contribution).,1,0,No communication between participants mentioned; sessions described with physical dividers and computer decisions only.,0.9,1,"After contribution stage, participants found out what everyone contributed in that round.",1,0,"Participants were not told who punished them, only the total that they had been punished.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists for players.,1,0,Participants were not told the number of rounds.,1,1,"Option to reduce others' earnings present, identically to control condition.",1,1,Every L$1 spent on punishment reduced the target’s earnings by L$3.,1,3,Punishment technology multiplies expenditure by 3 in terms of payoff reduction to target.,1,0,"No rewarding mechanism is available, only punishment.",1,N/A,Players cannot reward others.,1,N/A,Players cannot reward others.,1,10,"Each round, each group member received L$10.",1,N/R,Normalized contribution fraction (0–1) not reported; only L$ amounts given.,0.2,6.08,First-round mean contribution in markets condition reported as M = L$6.08.,1,N/R,No explicit or easily derivable efficiency metric reported.,0.1,N/R,Total group payoffs are not provided.,0.1,Canada,"Participants were from University of Guelph (Ontario, Canada).",1,"Mean age 19.6 years (SD = 2.0), combined across conditions.","Only overall sample age reported, not broken down by condition.",0.9,"75 females, 42 males total across both conditions; condition-specific gender split not reported.",Paper reports aggregate gender only.,0.6,"University students (psychology participant pool, earning course credit).",Same participant pool as in control condition.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical lab setting on computers.,1,"Same PGG with punishment structure as control: 4-player groups, 5 rounds, L$10 endowment, contributions doubled and equally redistributed, anonymous punishment with 1:3 cost-to-impact ratio, participants not told number of rounds. Additional market feature: a 5th participant (observer) watches after the PGG, sees each player’s average contributions (not punishment), receives L$50 to entrust to one chosen PGG participant in a subsequent trust game where entrusted amount is tripled and trustee decides how much to return. Partner choice based solely on contribution history.","Summarizes all structural details of the markets condition, including the observer and trust-game partner choice which implement the partner-choice competition manipulation.",1
10.1073_pnas.0712173105.md,Analytical model – baseline public goods game without punishment,"Identified from the section 'First-Order Altruism and Selfishness' describing the public goods game with only two strategies: altruist (invest all) and selfish (invest none), and no punishment.",1,Presence of punishment mechanism: no punishment; Strategy set: altruists (invest all) vs selfish (invest none),Condition defined by absence of punishment and restriction to two strategies that differ in first-order altruism.,1,False,"Model is purely theoretical/analytical with no human or animal subjects; payoffs are symbolic (b, m, N).",1,False,No controlled assignment of human participants or observational data; it is a theoretical game-theoretic model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; fully analytical.,1,False,Authors explicitly separate this from prior computer simulations; equations are closed-form analytical.,1,True,Section presents formulae for W_A and W_S and compares fitnesses analytically.,1,N/R,Group size is denoted symbolically as N; no single numeric value is specified.,0.7,1,Text: 'The model considers the two pure strategies ... after one round of interactions.',1,0,Model: 'two pure strategies of investing all (altruist) or none (selfish)'; contributions are all-or-nothing.,1,N/A,No human subjects or actual interface; default allocation not meaningful in an analytical model.,1,N/R,"Public good return per contributor is bm, but MPCR (m/N) is not numerically specified; parameters left symbolic.",0.6,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,0,Analytical payoffs do not include any informational feedback structure.,0.6,N/A,Punishment does not exist in this baseline model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,Number of rounds is fixed by theory; no display to agents is modeled.,0.7,0,Section explicitly analyzes case 'in the absence of punishment'.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism is described; only public good sharing.,1,N/A,No explicit reward action distinct from contribution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,b,"Model: 'each member of a group is provided an endowment, b'; remains symbolic.",1,N/R,"Outcomes are analytical fitness expressions, not reported as average contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,Only strategy types (all or none) and symbolic b are given; no aggregate outcome values.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff possibilities are described qualitatively; no explicit efficiency ratio is reported.,0.3,N/R,Total group payoff is implied but not summarized as a separate variable for results.,0.4,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model; no experimental environment.,1,"Linear public goods game with two pure strategies: altruist invests all endowment b, selfish invests none; group return from public good is multiplied by m and shared equally among N group members; fitness increment W_A = p b m, W_S = p b m + b, with p proportion of altruists.",Summarizes the specified payoff structure and strategy set from 'The Model' section.,1
10.1073_pnas.0712173105.md,Analytical model – altruistic punishers vs selfish nonpunishers,"From 'Punishment' and 'Altruistic Punishers vs. Selfish Nonpunishers' sections where only altruistic punishers and selfish nonpunishers are considered, forming an unstable equilibrium.",1,Strategy set and second-order behavior: altruistic punishers (contribute and punish selfish) vs selfish nonpunishers (do not contribute and do not punish); punishment of selfishness enabled.,Condition defined by which of the four possible strategies are allowed and the presence of punishment.,1,False,All entities are strategy types in an analytical model; no empirical subjects.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with observed data; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,Not a lab or field study.,1,False,"Results come from analytical fitness equations, not numerical simulation trajectories (simulations are referenced as prior work).",1,True,Fitness expressions W_ap and W_sn are derived and plotted to identify equilibria.,1,N/R,Group size is represented symbolically as N; no specific numeric group size given.,0.7,1,Authors note 'one round of interactions' for the model.,1,0,Carries over from base model: individuals either invest all (altruist) or none (selfish).,1,N/A,Theoretical types choose strategies; no interface default for human subjects.,1,N/R,Return specified symbolically via m and N but not instantiated numerically.,0.6,N/A,No human agents; no communication modeled.,1,0,No information feedback variables are part of the analytical description.,0.6,0,Punishment affects payoff probability based on frequency of punishers; identity revelation is not modeled.,0.6,N/A,No reward mechanism considered.,1,0,Round count is fixed in the model and not part of any information structure.,0.7,1,Model includes costly punishment c that can expel selfish individuals with probability equal to punisher frequency.,1,c,Text: 'punish selfish group members at a personal cost c'; cost per punished selfish individual.,1,N/R,Qualitatively: punishment causes selfish individuals to lose acquired energy; no numeric 'units lost per cost unit' beyond this is specified.,0.4,0,No separate rewarding or positive sanction mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,b,Endowment per player remains parameter b as specified for the game.,1,N/R,"Analyses focus on fitness and equilibrium frequencies, not reported average contributions.",0.3,N/R,Only strategy definitions (all vs none) are given; no aggregate realized values.,0.3,N/R,Relative fitness and ESS characterization are reported; no explicit efficiency metric.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff is implicitly determined by contributions and punishment but not summarized numerically.,0.4,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Model is analytical only.,1,"Four possible strategy types exist conceptually, but this condition restricts to altruistic punishers (AP) who contribute their entire endowment and punish all selfish group members at cost c per selfish target, and selfish nonpunishers (SN) who do not contribute and do not punish. Fitness functions: W_ap = (p_an + p_ap) b m − c (p_sn + p_sp), W_sn = [(p_an + p_ap) b m + b] (1 − (p_ap + p_sp)), with p_ap and p_sn as frequencies in the group; punishment causes selfish individuals to risk losing b + p b m with probability equal to the frequency of punishers.","Summarizes modeled strategies, punishment rule, and central payoff equations for this restricted strategy set.",1
10.1073_pnas.0712173105.md,"Analytical model – altruistic punishers, altruistic nonpunishers, selfish nonpunishers","From the continuation of 'Punishment' section and Figs. 2–3 where altruistic nonpunishers are added to the AP vs SN system, yielding three-strategy dynamics.",1,"Strategy set: altruistic punishers (AP), altruistic nonpunishers (AN), selfish nonpunishers (SN); punishment only by altruists.","Condition is defined by allowing three specific strategies, with punishment executed solely by altruistic punishers.",1,False,Still an analytical extension of the same model with symbolic parameters.,1,False,No actual experiment; theoretical comparison of strategies.,1,False,No lab or real-world setting.,1,False,Analysis uses equations and phase diagrams; simulations are referenced only for separate prior work.,1,True,Triangle plot and fitness functions are derived analytically for three-strategy interactions.,1,N/R,Group size N retained as symbolic parameter only.,0.7,1,Model is explicitly constrained to 'one round of interactions' as stated later in Discussion as well.,1,0,First-order strategies remain 'invest all or none of one's endowment'.,1,N/A,No explicit dynamic of default allocations with human participants.,1,N/R,m and N left symbolic; no numeric MPCR.,0.6,N/A,Analytical model with no explicit communication.,1,0,No information-reporting component modeled.,0.6,0,"Punishment probability depends on punisher frequency, not on individual identification.",0.6,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,0,Number of rounds not modeled as information for agents.,0.7,1,"Altruistic punishers punish selfish group members at cost c, as previously defined.",1,c,Same punishment cost parameter c applies.,1,N/R,Qualitative rule unchanged: selfish individuals risk losing b + p b m; no numeric technology.,0.4,0,No separate rewarding institution.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,b,Endowment parameter b is common across these conditions.,1,N/R,"Results are presented as fitness comparisons and trajectories, not as normalized contribution data.",0.3,N/R,No explicit realized contributions per group are reported.,0.3,N/R,Model reports stability/instability but not explicit efficiency values.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs are derivable but not reported as a summarized dependent variable.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical model only.,1,"Strategy types: altruistic nonpunishers (AN) contribute full endowment b and do not punish; altruistic punishers (AP) also contribute b and punish all selfish group members at cost c per target; selfish nonpunishers (SN) contribute nothing and do not punish. Fitnesses: W_an = (p_an + p_ap) b m; W_ap = (p_an + p_ap) b m − c (p_sn + p_sp); W_sn = [(p_an + p_ap) b m + b] (1 − (p_ap + p_sp)), with p_an, p_ap, p_sn, p_sp denoting frequencies. Dynamics show AP can be undermined by invasion of AN due to avoiding cost c, leading to selfishness (SN) as an ESS under many conditions.","Summarizes described strategies, payoff equations, and qualitative dynamic outcome for this three-strategy system.",1
10.1073_pnas.0712173105.md,Analytical model – selfish punishers vs altruistic nonpunishers,From 'Selfish Punishment' and Fig. 4 where a two-strategy model is considered with selfish punishers and altruistic nonpunishers forming a stable equilibrium.,1,"Strategy set and first-order behavior: selfish punishers (do not contribute, punish selfish) vs altruistic nonpunishers (contribute, do not punish).",Condition is defined as the two-strategy subset (SP and AN) of the full four-strategy model.,1,False,This is still within the analytical modeling framework with symbolic parameters.,1,False,No empirical implementation; purely theoretical.,1,False,Not run in a lab or field.,1,False,Equilibrium conditions derived analytically; simulations are mentioned separately elsewhere.,1,True,Authors derive condition (b + p_an b m)/N > c(1 − p_an − 1/N) and analyze stability.,1,N/R,Group size appears as parameter N; no specific value given.,0.7,1,Paper notes the model is 'constrained to one round of interactions'.,1,0,First-order altruism/selfishness still modeled as investing all b or none.,1,N/A,No explicit human-subject representation of default contributions.,1,N/R,Public good return multiple parameter m is present but not numerically instantiated.,0.6,N/A,No communication or human participants.,1,0,Information about others' payoffs or actions is not modeled explicitly.,0.6,0,"Punishment depends on frequencies and group membership, not on revealed identities.",0.6,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Round number not part of any agent information interface.,0.7,1,Selfish punishers punish all selfish individuals (including other selfish punishers).,1,c,"Each punisher pays personal cost c per selfish group member punished, minus themselves.",1,N/R,Specified only as causing punished selfish individuals to lose their acquired energy with some probability; magnitude per c not numerically parameterized.,0.4,0,No reward for cooperation modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,b,Endowment parameter b applies to this condition as well.,1,N/R,"Analytical focus is on equilibrium frequencies and inequality conditions, not explicit contribution statistics.",0.3,N/R,No aggregated contribution data are presented.,0.3,N/R,They describe that this equilibrium maintains altruism but do not quantify efficiency.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff is not given as a single numeric or symbolic outcome for this condition.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Analytical, no empirical implementation.",1,"Two strategies: altruistic nonpunishers (AN) contribute full endowment b to the public good, do not punish; selfish punishers (SP) withhold b (selfish in first-order interaction) yet punish all other selfish individuals (including other SP) at cost c per target, thereby creating negative frequency dependence and a stable polymorphism. Stability condition against invasion by selfish nonpunishers (SN) is (b + p_an b m)/N > c (1 − p_an − 1/N), where p_an is the frequency of altruists in the group, m is the public good multiplier, and N is group size.",Summarizes strategy definitions and the central stability inequality described in the 'Selfish Punishment' section.,1
10.1073_pnas.0712173105.md,"Analytical model – selfish punishers, altruistic nonpunishers, selfish nonpunishers","From Figs. 5–6 and surrounding text where the model includes selfish punishers, altruistic nonpunishers, and selfish nonpunishers, and examines conditions under which the SP–AN equilibrium resists invasion by SN.",1,"Strategy set: selfish punishers (SP), altruistic nonpunishers (AN), selfish nonpunishers (SN); punishment by selfish punishers.",Condition defined by permitting three strategies and having only selfish types engage in punishment.,1,False,Still part of the same theoretical model.,1,False,No real experiment performed.,1,False,No laboratory execution.,1,False,Described via fitness functions and phase diagrams; simulations only cited from other work.,1,True,Trajectories and equilibria determined by analytical conditions on parameters and frequencies.,1,N/R,Group size N used symbolically; not assigned a specific number.,0.7,1,Analytical model remains single-round as stated.,1,0,First-order behaviors still 'invest all b' vs 'invest none'.,1,N/A,No human-subject interface or default allocations.,1,N/R,MPCR parameter m/N is symbolic only.,0.6,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,No explicit modeling of information feedback.,0.6,0,"Punishment mechanics depend on strategy frequencies and group membership, not revelation of specific identities.",0.6,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Round number not part of an information structure in the model.,0.7,1,"Selfish punishers punish selfish group members, including other selfish punishers, at cost c.",1,c,Same cost parameter as in earlier punishment sections.,1,N/R,Only qualitatively described loss of acquired energy; no explicit magnitudes specified.,0.4,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,b,Endowment parameter b remains unchanged.,1,N/R,"Results expressed as trajectory diagrams and ESS conditions, not average contribution measures.",0.3,N/R,No explicit aggregation of contributions reported as outcomes.,0.3,N/R,Discussion refers to maintaining altruism and within-group stability but does not give explicit efficiency ratios.,0.3,N/R,"Group payoff is implicit in fitness expressions, not separately reported.",0.4,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Analytical model exclusively.,1,"Three strategies: altruistic nonpunishers (AN) who contribute endowment b and do not punish; selfish punishers (SP) who withhold b and punish all other selfish group members at cost c each; selfish nonpunishers (SN) who withhold b and do not punish. Groups with only SP and AN converge to a stable equilibrium mixture; invasion by SN is resisted if inequality (b + p_an b m)/N > c (1 − p_an − 1/N) holds. Triangle plot (Fig. 6) shows attraction to SP–AN equilibrium when SN is initially sufficiently scarce; otherwise, population is attracted to all-SN corner.",Summarizes set of strategies and central qualitative findings on stability and invasion as described around Figs. 5–6.,1
10.1007_s00406-013-0392-5.md,Ultimatum Game – MDD patients (responders),Distinct group of currently depressed inpatients with recurrent MDD compared to healthy controls; same task structure but different participant group.,1,"Participant diagnosis: Major depressive disorder (recurrent), inpatient, medicated; Role: responder; Offer size varied within-subject (0–5.5 € of 10 €).","Between-subject factor is group (MDD vs HC); within-subject factors include offer size and treatment session, but diagnosis is the key independent variable differentiating conditions.",0.96,True,Human inpatients with MDD played the ultimatum game.,1,True,Controlled economic game with randomized offers and standardized instructions.,1,True,Conducted at University of Bonn psychiatry department with computers in a lab-like clinical setting.,0.92,False,No computational or agent-based simulations described.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical modeling; focus on behavioral data.,1,2,Ultimatum game is explicitly described as a two-person exchange game (proposer and responder).,1,54,They state that in each test session there were 54 trials; game is one-shot per counterpart but 54 one-shot trials per subject.,1,1,Responder can only accept or reject a given offer; contribution concept (all-or-nothing vs continuous) is not directly applicable but mapped as standard binary choice.,0.5,0,"In UG, entire 10 € endowment is initially with proposer; responder has no default share until acceptance; analogous to 0 in public account for responder.",0.6,N/A,Ultimatum game is not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No communication between players is mentioned; cover story stresses one-shot anonymous interactions.,0.95,0,Participants only see current proposer photo and current offer; no feedback on others’ earnings or history.,0.95,0,"Responder sees a photo of the proposer, but the punishment decision (rejection) is not linked back to an identified partner in future rounds; no e