10.1038_srep10639.md,"Simulation – Commitments without apology (four scenarios PP, NP, PN, NN jointly)","Paper reports a single evolutionary game-theoretic simulation of IPD with commitments but without apology, analysing all four outside-commitment scenarios together (Fig. 1b, Fig. 2).",0.9,"Presence of commitment strategies and outside-commitment scenarios (PP, NP, PN, NN) in an evolutionary IPD with noise α, continuation probability ω=0.9, benefit-cost ratio b/c=2, commitment cost ε=0.25, compensation for breaking commitment δ=4.","Model section and captions for Figs. 1–2 specify parameters and that individuals may choose among PP, NP, PN, NN scenarios when commitment is possible; baseline is IPD without commitment.",0.9,False,No human participants; all results are analytical and numerical simulations of evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational and analytical.,1,True,They simulate an evolutionary IPD in a finite population using Markov chains and Fermi imitation dynamics.,1,True,"Methods and Results sections derive closed-form expressions (e.g., fixation probabilities, payoff formulas, risk-dominance conditions).",1,100,"Model description: “We consider a finite population of N individuals, with N=100 in our analysis.”",1,10,"They use continuation probability ω=0.9, giving average number of rounds RT=(1−ω)−1=10.",0.9,1,"In each round of IPD players choose between C and D; binary choice so contribution concept doesn’t apply, treated as general contribution freedom.",0.5,N/A,No endowment allocation or public-good contribution structure; it is a Prisoner’s Dilemma.,1,N/R,"Study uses Prisoner’s Dilemma donation game payoffs, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.",1,N/A,No human subjects; no communication protocol implemented beyond strategy space.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback design; it is an abstract simulation model.,1,N/A,No player identities or information structure for punishment display in a human interface.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identity revelation in a user interface.,1,N/A,No human interface; rounds are determined by ω in the simulation.,1,1,Breaking a commitment incurs a compensation δ from defector to partner; functionally a punishment mechanism.,0.9,4,Compensation δ=4 is paid by the defector when breaking a commitment; treated as cost per punishment event.,0.8,4,"Every unit of ‘punishment’ reduces defector’s payoff by δ and increases partner’s by δ (as a transfer); here we approximate tech as 4 payoff units per event, matching δ.",0.5,0,No separate rewarding mechanism; only compensation for breach and later apology transfers.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward stage or cost.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Donation game payoffs b and c are defined, but not as endowments; no explicit per-round endowment is given.",0.6,N/R,"They report frequencies of cooperative strategies and levels of cooperation qualitatively, but no explicit normalized contribution statistic.",0.4,N/R,"No numeric average number of C moves per round reported, only qualitative plots.",0.4,N/R,They discuss cooperation and defection levels but do not provide total payoff relative to maximum cooperation as a numeric measure.,0.4,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff numbers for groups or population reported.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational and analytical evolutionary game-theoretic model.,1,"Finite population evolutionary Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma (Donation game) with benefit b, cost c (b/c=2, c=1), continuation probability ω=0.9, implementation error rate α, commitment cost ε=0.25, compensation for breaking commitment δ=4. Strategies defined by commitment role Sc∈{P,A,NC}, in-commitment behavior Sin∈{C,D}, outside-commitment reactive strategy Sout∈{AllC,AllD,TFT,ATFT}, and scenario (PP,NP,PN,NN) determining whether play continues when commitments fail.",Summarized from model and parameter descriptions and captions for Figs. 1–2.,1
10.1038_srep10639.md,"Simulation – Commitments with costly apology and forgiveness (PP scenario, α varied, external γ)",Fig. 3 and Fig. 4 analyze apology and forgiveness for the PP scenario under specified parameter values; this is a distinct simulation condition set with apology cost γ as main variable.,0.9,Apology cost γ (compensation upon apology) varied with costly apology (γ>0) in PP scenario; noise levels α=0.01 and α=0.1; continuation probability ω=0.9; benefit-cost ratio b/c=2 with c=1; commitment cost ε=0.25; compensation for breaking commitment δ=4. Presence vs absence of apology-forgiveness mechanism with q_apo=1 or 0.,"Text under ‘Forgiveness requires a sincere apology…’ and Fig. 3–4 captions specify γ as varying parameter, PP scenario, α, ω, b/c, c, ε, δ, and that apology-forgiveness mechanism is introduced.",0.95,False,All results are from simulations of strategy frequencies; no human subjects.,1,False,"Not a behavioral or field experiment, but a model-based numerical study.",1,False,No physical lab; simulations only.,1,True,They numerically compute stationary distributions under Fermi imitation dynamics with apology strategies.,1,True,"They derive analytical conditions for dominance, e.g., cooperative proposer dominant when γ>c and γ>c+3ε/4.",1,100,Same evolutionary model with N=100 is used throughout the analysis.,1,10,Continuation probability ω=0.9 is kept; average rounds RT=(1−ω)−1=10.,0.9,1,"Binary C/D choices per IPD round, consistent with previous part; no alternative contribution granularity.",0.5,N/A,"Prisoner’s Dilemma structure, no endowment contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,"Model uses donation-game PD payoffs, not a PGG; MPCR not defined.",1,N/A,"No human communication; apology is a modeled transfer decision parameter q_apo, not chat.",1,N/A,No user interface; strategy payoffs are computed directly.,1,N/A,No identities or display decisions in a human interface.,1,N/A,"No explicit rewarding mechanism besides apology and compensation transfers, and no identity display.",1,N/A,No human players; number of rounds is probabilistic via ω.,1,1,"Breaking commitments triggers compensation δ; also, if commitment is broken without forgiveness, δ-related transfers occur.",0.9,4,Compensation for breaking commitments is δ=4 throughout these simulations.,0.9,4,"We approximate punishment efficiency as 4 payoff units per breaking event, matching δ; model treats it as a fixed compensation transfer.",0.5,1,Apology mechanism includes a positive transfer γ from wrongdoer to victim upon apologizing; can be viewed as a form of costly compensatory reward for the victim.,0.7,1,"Base cost of cooperation c is set to 1; apology cost γ is varied but not a fixed unit cost, so we record the unit cost scale as 1 (c).",0.3,N/R,Exact mapping between a unit of ‘reward’ and payoff change is not parameterized; γ itself is the full transfer and is varied.,0.2,N/R,Model defines payoffs via donation parameters b and c (with c=1) but no explicit per-round monetary or token endowment.,0.6,N/R,Figures show ‘levels of cooperation’ and defection qualitatively as functions of γ but without numerical values or explicit normalization.,0.4,N/R,No tables or numbers for average C per round are provided.,0.4,N/R,"They discuss that apology and forgiveness ‘increase the level of cooperation’ and that cooperation becomes more beneficial, but do not present efficiency ratios.",0.4,N/R,Group or population average payoffs under apology are not numerically reported.,0.4,N/A,Simulation study; no participants.,1,N/A,Simulation study; no participants.,1,N/A,Simulation study; no participants.,1,N/A,Simulation study; no participants.,1,No human,All analyses with apology-forgiveness are computational simulations of strategies.,1,"Finite-population evolutionary Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with commitments and explicit apology-forgiveness. Strategies extended to S_i=(Sc,Sin,Sout,q_apo), where Sc∈{P,A,NC}, Sin∈{C,D}, Sout∈{AllC,AllD,TFT,ATFT}, and q_apo∈{0,1} indicates whether agents apologise after defecting. In PP scenario, players continue to play reactive strategies when commitments fail. An apology entails paying γ to the partner to maintain commitment; forgiveness is granted if apology meets sincerity thresholds (γ larger than cost of cooperation c, but less than commitment-breaking compensation δ). Parameters: N=100, ω=0.9, b/c=2 with c=1, ε=0.25, δ=4; noise α typically 0.01 or 0.1 in the analyzed figures. Evolutionary dynamics follow Fermi imitation with β=0.1 under small mutation.","Compiled from ‘Forgiveness requires a sincere apology…’, ‘Evolution selects sincerity…’ and Methods sections plus figure captions.",1
10.1038_srep10639.md,Simulation – Individual apology cost γ and forgiveness threshold τ_γ evolution (PP scenario),Section ‘Evolution selects sincerity in apology and forgiveness’ and Fig. 5 analyze an evolutionary model where individuals have personal apology costs γ and thresholds τ_γ for forgiveness in PP scenario.,0.95,Personal apology cost γ and personal forgiveness threshold τ_γ co-evolving among commitment proposers in PP scenario; noise α varied; continuation probability ω=0.9; commitment cost ε=0.25; compensation for breaking commitment δ=4; benefit-cost ratio unspecified but same as before (b/c=2).,"Text: ‘individuals can apologise at different costs γ and can forgive defection conditional on a personal threshold τ_γ… Figure 5 reveals which thresholds (τ_γ) are preferred and which apologies (γ) are required for the PP scenario… We assumed ω=0.9, ε=0.25 and δ=4’; earlier sections keep b/c=2 constant.",0.9,False,Still within the same simulation framework; no humans.,1,False,The study remains theoretical and simulation-based.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,"They examine stationary distributions over (γ, τ_γ) strategy pairs via numerical evolutionary dynamics.",1,True,"While most detail is from simulations, the broader paper includes analytical structure; however, for this figure insight is mostly numerical.",0.7,100,Same N=100 finite population assumption applies; no change indicated.,0.95,10,"Continuation probability ω=0.9 used here as well, yielding RT=(1−ω)−1≈10.",0.9,1,Iterated PD with binary C/D actions; same structure as other simulations.,0.5,N/A,Not a contribution game with an endowment.,1,N/R,MPCR concept not used; model is PD-based.,1,N/A,No communication; apology and forgiveness are modeled internal parameters.,1,N/A,No human-visible summaries; only computed payoffs.,1,N/A,No interface; punishment is implicit via payoffs.,1,N/A,Apology transfers are not presented via an interface.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe remaining rounds; geometric stopping governed by ω.,1,1,Commitment-breaking compensation δ remains part of the model.,0.9,4,"Compensation δ=4 is held fixed in Fig. 5 (caption: ‘We assumed ω=0.9, ε=0.25 and δ=4’).",0.95,4,"As before, treat δ as punishment intensity per event.",0.5,1,Apology is implemented as a positive transfer γ to the co-player when forgiving is considered.,0.8,1,"Base cooperation cost c is 1; actual apology cost γ varies, so 1 represents the unit scale.",0.3,N/R,No separate unit-level conversion; γ itself is the transfer magnitude.,0.2,N/R,"As before, payoffs defined by b and c but not by explicit token endowments.",0.6,N/R,"Fig. 5 shows stationary distribution of cooperative proposers over (γ, τ_γ) but not explicit cooperation rates.",0.4,N/R,No numerical contribution amounts are listed.,0.4,N/R,They state that apology and forgiveness help maintain cooperative commitments but do not compute explicit efficiency ratios.,0.4,N/R,No average group payoff values are provided in the figure or text for this analysis.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects in this simulation.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,"This is a numerical evolutionary dynamics study of strategy parameters (γ, τ_γ).",1,"Evolutionary IPD with commitments and apology-forgiveness in which only strategies that defect outside commitments and propose commitments are considered, as these dominate in previous analyses. Each such strategy carries two continuous/discrete parameters: apology cost γ and forgiveness threshold τ_γ, representing the minimum apology the player requires to forgive. Individuals interact under PP scenario, with N=100, ω=0.9, ε=0.25, δ=4, and b/c maintained as in earlier sections (2:1). They examine the stationary distribution over (γ, τ_γ) pairs across different noise α values, finding that strategies with τ_γ approximately matching their own γ and with γ between c and δ are favored, while τ_γ>γ is disfavored.",Summarizes description under ‘Evolution selects sincerity in apology and forgiveness’ and Fig. 5 caption.,0.95
