10.1038_srep08917.md,"Baseline analytical model with reward and punishment (n=5, r1=3, r2=2, r3=1.6, c1=1, c2=1, c3=0.1)",Single core parameterization explicitly given in Methods/Figure 1 caption; used throughout numerical analyses.,0.9,"Analytical evolutionary game parameters: group size n=5, PGG cost c1=1, reward cost c2=1, punishment cost c3=0.1, PGG multiplier r1=3, reward multiplier r2=2, punishment multiplier r3=1.6, with second-order punishment enabled (k_PC=1, k_RP=2, k_RR=1, k_PR=0) and optional or compulsory participation as specified in analyses.",Paper defines a single parameter set for illustrations and states other variants do not qualitatively change results.,0.9,False,No human participants; model is purely theoretical with replicator dynamics.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a mathematical/evolutionary analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,True,Uses numerical simulations of replicator dynamics for strategy frequencies.,1,True,Derives replicator equations and analytically studies stability and dynamics.,1,5,Group size n=5 given in Figure 1 caption and used in examples.,1,1,One-shot PGG interactions per generation in evolutionary model; no repeated-game rounds.,0.8,1,"Players choose whether to contribute fixed costs c1, c2, c3; contributions are binary decisions, not continuous amounts; coded as able to “contribute” or “not” rather than all-or-nothing endowment contribution levels.",0.6,N/A,No human endowments; only strategy-dependent costs in analytical model.,1,0.6,Marginal per capita return r1/n = 3/5 = 0.6.,1,N/A,No human interaction or communication.,1,N/A,Not applicable to non-human analytical model.,1,N/A,No identities in population-level replicator model.,1,N/A,No identities in population-level replicator model.,1,N/A,No user interface or rounds displayed to participants; model-based.,1,1,Pool punishment fund with cost c3 and fines multiplied by r3 is central to the model.,1,0.1,Punishers contribute c3=0.1 to the punishment fund (Figure 1 caption).,1,1.6,Punishment fines are proportional to r3 c3; r3=1.6 is the fine multiplier; taken as technology per unit contribution.,0.8,1,Pool reward fund with cost c2 and multiplier r2 is explicitly modeled.,1,1,Rewarders contribute c2=1 to the reward fund (Figure 1 caption).,1,2,Reward benefits are multiplied by r2=2; used as the reward technology factor.,1,N/A,No explicit individual endowment; only fixed contribution costs and payoffs per interaction.,1,N/R,"Paper analyzes evolutionary frequencies of strategies, not normalized per-player contribution proportions.",0.2,N/R,Model specifies fixed costs but does not report aggregate contribution amounts as outcome variables.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio (actual vs. maximum group payoff) is reported.,0.2,N/R,Only relative payoffs and stability conditions are discussed; no numeric group payoff outcomes summarized as DVs.,0.2,N/A,No human or animal participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely theoretical/simulation-based with replicator dynamics and no empirical data.,1,"Infinity large, well-mixed population using replicator dynamics with five strategies: cooperators (C) contribute only to PGG; defectors (D) contribute to none; punishers (P) contribute to PGG and punishment fund; rewarders (R) contribute to PGG and reward fund; non-participants (N) take outside option g>0. Order of decisions: participation, reward, punishment, then PGG. Pool reward and pool punishment are institutional funds; second-order punishment targets those not contributing to punishment fund. Optional vs compulsory participation is varied analytically; figures typically use r1 in (2,n), r2 in (1,n), and conditions for rock-paper-scissors cycles among subsets of strategies.","Summarizes core structural features of the modeled public good, reward, and punishment institutions as described in Methods and Results.",0.95
