10.1098_rstb.2009.0116.md,Jensen et al. 2007b – Collapsible-table experiment: social vs non-social condition,Second Jensen study under spite: rope-pulling to collapse a food table; social vs non-social contexts.,0.9,"Condition: social (another chimpanzee with food) vs non-social (food on table, adjacent room empty)",Independent manipulation is presence of conspecific benefitting from food vs empty room.,0.9,True,Chimpanzees as subjects.,1,True,Controlled experimental contrast of social vs non-social setups.,1,True,Conducted with captive chimpanzees in research facility.,0.9,False,Not simulated.,1,False,Empirical.,1,2,"Active actor chimpanzee and potential recipient chimpanzee in social condition; in non-social, second slot is empty but game design still two-role.",0.6,N/R,Number of trials not specified.,0.2,1,Actor decides to pull rope (collapse table) or not each trial.,0.7,0,No endowment or public good; behavior is potential spite (destroying other's food).,0.4,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,0,No communication component described.,0.8,0,"Outcomes directly observed, no statistics.",0.8,0,No explicit punishment institution; rope-pulling is the main decision.,0.8,0,No separate rewarding mechanism.,0.8,0,No information about number of remaining trials.,0.6,0,"Rope-pulling could reduce another’s payoff but is intrinsic to game, not an additional punitive stage.",0.7,N/A,No distinct punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Units of food on table not quantified in excerpt.,0.2,N/R,"DV is frequency of rope pulls, not contributions to a public good.",0.2,N/R,No contributions measured.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency data.,0.2,N/R,No numeric payoffs.,0.2,Germany,"Same authors and period as Jensen et al. 2007a, likely at Max Planck Institute, Leipzig.",0.7,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,N/A,Non-human primates.,1,On site,Captive lab environment.,0.9,"Actor chimp