10.1016_j.amc.2021.126797.md,Monte Carlo simulation – structured population with tax T = 0,Paper studies one spatial evolutionary game; a main treatment dimension is tax level. This row captures the T = 0 (no tax) condition discussed in Section 3.1 and Fig. 1–4.,1,Tax level T = 0; punishment cost G_P varied; fine β varied; synergy factor r = 3; group size N = 5; square lattice interaction structure,"Model description specifies r = 3 fixed, N = 5, and structured population; Section 2 and 3.1 state that G_P and β are systematically varied under T = 0.",1,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on a lattice, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No physical laboratory or human subjects; computational model only.,1,True,They explicitly run Monte Carlo simulations on L×L lattices with varying L and long relaxation times.,1,False,They reference prior analytical/replicator work but this paper’s main results are numerical simulations.,1,40000,"Population size is L×L; they use different L from 200 to 800. A typical snapshot in Fig. 4 uses L = 400, giving 400×400 = 160000, but cross-size comparisons (Fig. 3) include 200. Since no single canonical L is fixed, using a representative mid-size is arbitrary. Set to 40000 (L=200^2) as the minimum size they mention systematically; exact N varies.",0.2,1000000,They state relaxation time between 10^5 and 10^6 Monte Carlo steps; we record upper bound 10^6 as typical duration until stationarity.,0.3,1,"This is a public goods game where cooperators contribute fixed c = 1 and D, P contribute 0; there is no binary choice of ‘all endowment or nothing’ because endowment is not modeled as a variable pot; contributions are fixed by strategy rather than chosen continuously. Map to 1 (not an all-or-nothing decision) per instruction that 0 is only if players can only choose all or nothing from an endowment.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects and no explicit endowment allocation between private and public accounts; model directly defines payoffs and contributions.,1,0.6,"Marginal per capita return MPCR = r/N with r = 3 and N = 5, so 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,Agent-based Monte Carlo model with no communication mechanism.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents update based on payoffs only.,1,N/A,No identities in the simulation beyond strategies; visibility not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents are not informed about number of rounds.,1,1,Punishers P impose fine β on defectors and pay cost G_P; central to model.,1,1,"In payoff for P, a fixed G_P cost is subtracted independent of number of defectors; no per-unit or per-target cost is defined. Framework’s numeric field expects a cost per unit; true mapping is not applicable. Use 1 as a neutral placeholder magnitude but note that actual cost parameter G_P is varied externally, not a per-punishment-unit cost.",0.1,1,"Defectors’ payoff decreases by β per punisher in group. As with cost, the effect per punisher is parameter β; not a discrete per-coin conversion. Use 1 as neutral unit, noting that actual fine parameter β is varied independently.",0.1,0,No strategy that rewards cooperators; only negative sanctioning is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model specifies a contribution c = 1 for cooperators but does not define a starting endowment per round.,0.2,N/R,"They report stationary fractions of strategies (C, D, P) but not normalized contribution rate as a DV; contributions are implicit via fraction of C players.",0.2,N/R,No explicit average contribution amount per player is tabulated; only qualitative discussion and phase diagrams.,0.2,N/R,"Although total payoffs are discussed qualitatively and average payoff curves plotted vs G_P, no normalized efficiency measure (relative to maximum) is reported numerically.",0.3,N/R,"Average payoff of the whole population is plotted as a function of G_P (Fig. 2), but no specific numeric values are given in the text or tables.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; countries not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game on L×L square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; group size N = 5 (von Neumann neighborhood plus focal); strategies: C contributes c = 1, D contributes 0, P contributes 0 and punishes D. Payoffs per group: Π_C = (r c (N_C + 1))/N − c − T; Π_D = (r c N_C)/N − β N_P − T; Π_P = (r c N_C)/N − G_P + (N T)/(N_P + 1) − T. Update rule: pairwise imitation with Fermi function Γ = 1 / (1 + exp[(Π_y − Π_x)/K]) with noise K = 0.5. Parameters varied: punishment cost G_P, fine β; tax T fixed at 0; synergy r = 3; L between 200 and 800; relaxation 10^5–10^6 MC steps.","Summarizes formal model and dynamics as stated in Section 2 and 3.1, including payoff equations and simulation protocol.",1
10.1016_j.amc.2021.126797.md,Monte Carlo simulation – structured population with tax T = 0.2,"Second main treatment is substantial tax T = 0.2, analyzed in Section 3.2 and Fig. 5–7.",1,Tax level T = 0.2; punishment cost G_P varied; fine β varied; synergy factor r = 3; group size N = 5; square lattice interaction structure,"Section 2 notes that T = 0.2 is considered as significant tax; then they vary β and G_P under this tax level (phase diagram Fig. 5, cross-sections Fig. 6).",1,False,"As above, no human participants.",1,False,"Computational, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice.,1,False,Results obtained numerically.,1,160000,"They state L ranges 200–800; Fig. 7 uses L = 400 explicitly, so N = 400×400 = 160000. Use this as representative for the stability analysis and tax T = 0.2 regime.",0.4,1000000,Same simulation protocol as T = 0: relaxation time between 10^5 and 10^6 MC steps; we record upper range as typical.,0.3,1,"Same game structure as T = 0; contribution rule determined by strategy, not a binary endowment choice.",0.4,N/A,No human endowment account structure.,1,0.6,"Same r = 3 and N = 5, so MPCR = 3/5.",1,N/A,No communication in simulations.,1,N/A,No interface for agents.,1,N/A,No identities modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,1,Same punisher P with fine β and cost G_P; now additionally benefits from tax pool.,1,1,"As in T = 0 case, cost is fixed G_P not per-use; field here cannot represent that exactly; 1 used as neutral magnitude.",0.1,1,Fine reduces D’s payoff by β per punisher in group; recorded generically as 1 unit with β treated as external parameter.,0.1,0,No rewards are introduced under T = 0.2 either.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Contribution c = 1 is specified but not a formal endowment.,0.2,N/R,"They again focus on stationary fractions of C, D, P and phase transitions, not explicit normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,No numeric average contribution values provided.,0.2,N/R,"They mention average payoff being negative for chosen G_P region, and plot average payoff vs G_P (Fig. 6), but do not present normalized efficiency values.",0.3,N/R,Average payoff curves shown qualitatively without tabulated numbers.,0.3,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulations only.,1,"Same spatial public goods game on L×L lattice with periodic boundaries, N = 5 groups, strategies C, D, P, and Fermi imitation dynamics with K = 0.5. Payoff functions identical to T = 0 case but with T = 0.2: Π_C = (r c (N_C + 1))/N − c − T; Π_D = (r c N_C)/N − β N_P − T; Π_P = (r c N_C)/N − G_P + (N T)/(N_P + 1) − T. They explore phase diagram in (G_P, β) plane under T = 0.2, identify phases full P, full D, P+C, and P+D, and analyze stability and finite-size effects (e.g., L = 400, G_P ≈ 0.87–0.88, β = 0.2).",Summarizes modifications and phenomena specific to the tax T = 0.2 condition based on Section 3.2 and figures.,1
