10.1103_PhysRevE.91.022121.md,"Structured population – square lattice simulation with mutual punishment (parameters b, α, ρ0, K=0.1)","Section IV describes a single class of structured-population simulations on a square lattice where cooperation depends on b, α, and initial ρ0.",0.9,"Temptation to defect b, punishment fine α, initial cooperator density ρ0 on a 100×100 square lattice with degree 4 and noise parameter K=0.1.","They systematically vary b, α, and ρ0 while holding lattice size and K fixed.",1,False,Agent-based simulations with no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; computational simulation instead.,1,False,No laboratory environment with participants.,1,True,They run Monte Carlo simulations of strategy updating on a lattice for many realizations.,1,False,"Although they later use pair-approximation theory, this row focuses on the simulation condition; analytical approximation is separate.",0.8,10000,They state using a 100×100 square lattice; 100×100=10000 players.,1,30000,They run simulations for 30000 time steps to reach steady state.,1,1,"Each player uses one of two pure strategies, cooperation or defection; contribution decision is binary.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment or continuous contribution to a public good; only binary cooperate/defect.,1,N/R,"Model is based on PDG payoffs, not a linear public goods mechanism.",1,N/A,There are no human players or communication channels in the simulation.,1,0,Simulation agents update strategies based on local payoffs only; no summaries are displayed.,1,N/A,No explicit identity or display mechanism; interactions are anonymous lattice neighbors.,1,N/A,No reward implemented in the model.,1,0,Simulated agents are not given information about total or remaining time steps.,1,1,"Payoff function includes −α(1−sx^T sy) for each neighbor with a different strategy, representing mutual punishment.",1,N/R,They explicitly say the cost is absorbed into the punishment fine; separate cost parameter is not specified.,0.4,N/R,"Model specifies only a punishment fine α applied symmetrically, without a separate multiplier between cost and imposed fine.",0.4,0,Only punishment is modeled; no mention of rewards or positive incentives.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No monetary or token endowment is defined; agents simply receive PDG payoffs.,1,N/R,"Primary dependent variable is fraction of cooperators ρc, not contributions from endowments.",1,N/R,No explicit contribution amounts; only strategy type is tracked.,1,N/R,They do not compute payoff efficiency relative to a maximum-cooperation benchmark as defined in the instructions.,1,N/R,Results are reported in terms of ρc and time evolution; group payoff totals are not tabulated.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computer simulations without human subjects.,1,"Players occupy sites on a 2D square lattice (100×100, periodic boundaries) and interact with four nearest neighbors via a rescaled PDG (T=b>1, R=1, P=S=0) plus symmetric punishment of magnitude α whenever neighboring strategies differ. Strategies update asynchronously via the Fermi rule with noise K=0.1 over 30000 time steps, measuring the fraction of cooperators ρc in steady state, averaged over 200 realizations; initial cooperator density ρ0 varied (typically 0.2, 0.5, or 0.8).",Summarizes detailed simulation protocol from Section IV.,1
