10.1098_rspb.2008.1623.md,Non-rival goods model (theoretical condition with optional participation),"Paper presents a formal evolutionary game-theoretic model for non-rival public goods with optional participation, defined by payoff functions V_C, V_D, V_L and parameters (b, n, h_c, h_d, h_l, w_0). This is one distinct analytical condition.",0.86,"Type frequencies of strategies (cooperators, defectors, loners) in a large population under non-rival public good; key parameters: benefit parameter b, group size n, individual payoff parameters h_c, h_d, h_l, baseline fitness w_0.",The independent variables in this analytical model are the strategic composition of the population and payoff parameters; there is no experimental manipulation on human subjects.,0.9,False,No human or animal participants; purely mathematical evolutionary model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; equations and numerical iteration only.,1,False,No lab or field setting; theoretical work only.,1,True,"Authors numerically iterate the replicator equation to study dynamics, which constitutes simulation without human subjects.",0.9,True,Main results are derived from analytical payoff functions and stability analysis.,1,10,Non-rival model defines groups of size n; representative parameterization in Figure 1a and 3a uses n=10. Use this as the concrete configuration example.,0.7,1,"Model is explicitly a one-shot public goods game; dynamics occur across generations, not repeated rounds within a game.",0.95,1,"Contribution is a binary strategy (cooperate vs defect), not a continuous contribution level; players either contribute or not.",0.8,N/A,"No explicit endowment or default allocation between private/public accounts is modeled; payoffs specified directly, and there are no human subjects.",1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not expressed in standard linear PGG form; benefits defined via parameter b but not convertible directly to MPCR without an endowment structure.,0.3,N/A,No human interaction or communication stage; theoretical population model.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback; purely evolutionary dynamics.,1,N/A,Punishment is only discussed conceptually; not implemented in this model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in the formal model.,1,N/A,No user interface; infinite-horizon evolutionary dynamics.,1,0,"Model tracks only three strategies: cooperators, defectors, loners. Punishers are only mentioned in discussion, not included in payoff equations.",0.98,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the formalized model.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the formalized model.,1,0,No explicit rewarding; only baseline payoffs and public good benefits.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/R,"Model does not describe individual monetary or token endowments; only costs/benefits via h_c, h_d, h_l, and b.",0.2,N/R,Outcomes are frequencies of strategy types at equilibria or cycles; no explicit normalized contribution rate is reported.,0.3,N/R,No token-based contributions; binary strategy choice with cost embedded in payoff parameters.,0.2,N/R,"Paper discusses relative payoffs and stability, not normalized efficiency against a maximum benchmark.",0.2,N/R,Group payoffs are not summarized numerically; focus is on individual payoff functions and evolutionary trajectories.,0.2,N/A,No participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical model.,1,No human,Study is fully analytical/simulation with no human subjects.,1,"Three-strategy evolutionary public goods model with optional participation for non-rival goods. Strategies: cooperators contribute; defectors do not; loners opt out and obtain fixed payoff h_l. Payoffs when i cooperators and j defectors among other n-1 individuals: V_C = h_c + ((i+1)/n) * b, V_D = h_d + (i/n) * b, V_L = h_l, with lone cooperator or defector among loners assigned payoff h_l. Groups of size n drawn randomly; evolutionary dynamics follow discrete-time replicator equation with baseline fitness w_0.",Summarizes the non-rival model specification provided in section 2(a) and evolutionary dynamics description.,0.96
10.1098_rspb.2008.1623.md,Rival goods model – general case (including economies/diseconomies of scale) with optional participation,Paper defines a separate payoff structure for rival public goods (section 2(b)) with parameter s capturing returns to scale; this is a distinct analytical condition from the non-rival case.,0.9,"Type frequencies of strategies (cooperators, defectors, loners) in a large population under rival public good; key parameters: b (baseline production), s (returns to scale), group size n, individual payoff parameters h_c, h_d, h_l, baseline fitness w_0.","Independent variables are again strategy frequencies and payoff parameters, with s newly controlling economies/diseconomies of scale for rival goods.",0.9,False,No empirical data or participants; purely theoretical.,1,False,Not an experiment but a mathematical and computational analysis.,1,False,No laboratory setting involved.,1,True,"Authors numerically iterate replicator dynamics for rival-good parameterizations (e.g., Figures 1b, 2b, 3b–3d).",0.95,True,They derive expected payoffs and stability conditions analytically for rival goods as well.,1,10,"Rival goods model uses generic group size n; figures use several values (e.g., n=15 in Fig.1b, n=10 in Fig.3b–3d). Use n=10 as a representative configuration that is repeatedly parameterized in the results.",0.7,1,"As with non-rival, the game is one-shot; dynamics are across generations.",0.95,1,Cooperation vs defection remains a binary strategy with fixed individual payoff differences; no continuous contribution choice.,0.8,N/A,No endowment/account structure; no human players.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return not expressed in linear PGG form; benefits given via nonlinear function of (i/n), b, and s.",0.3,N/A,No communication stage in the theoretical model.,1,N/A,No display or feedback to agents; evolutionary process only.,1,N/A,Punishment not implemented in this model.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit game interface; infinite-generation replicator dynamics.,1,0,"As with non-rival case, only cooperators, defectors, and loners are modeled.",0.98,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit rewarding.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/R,No monetary or token endowment is specified.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes focus on frequencies of strategy types and existence of oscillations or stable polymorphisms, not normalized contribution levels.",0.3,N/R,No token contributions; strategies encode effort and costs via payoff parameters.,0.2,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximal group payoff is reported.,0.2,N/R,Group-level payoff statistics are not tabulated; only individual payoffs and dynamic outcomes described.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Entire analysis is theoretical plus simulations.,1,"Three-strategy evolutionary public goods model with optional participation for rival goods. Payoffs when i cooperators and j defectors among other n-1 individuals and i+j>0: V_C = h_c + ( ((i+1)/n)*b + ((i+1)/n)^2 * s ) / (i + j + 1); V_D = h_d + ( (i/n)*b + (i/n)^2 * s ) / (i + j + 1); V_L = h_l. Lone cooperator or defector among loners receives h_l. Parameter s > 0 yields economies of scale, s = 0 constant returns, s < 0 diseconomies. Groups of size n drawn randomly from large population with frequencies x, y, z for C, D, L; expected payoffs computed using multinomial distribution; dynamics given by discrete-time replicator equation with baseline fitness w_0. Different parameter settings (e.g., s>0, s=0, s<0) generate oscillations or stable polymorphisms as described in Figure 3.",Summarizes the rival-goods payoff and dynamic structure from section 2(b) and 3(c).,0.97
10.1209_0295-5075_93_58001.md,"Two-level MPGG with cooperation, defection, and punishment (analytical model)","Paper presents a single theoretical two-level multilevel public goods game (MPGG) with three strategies (C, D, P) and analyzes fixation probabilities and stationary distributions as functions of imitation strengths.",0.9,"Imitation strengths within communities (w1) and between communities (w2); community sizes m1 and m2; punishment parameters (beta1, gamma1, beta2, gamma2); contribution cost c; within-community allocation fraction k1; enhancement factor r1.","These parameters are explicitly varied or discussed as drivers of differences in long-run abundance of C, D, and P (e.g., figs. 3–4 vary w1 and w2; text discusses large m1 and m2 and conditions on punishment parameters).",0.9,False,No human participants; work is a mathematical evolutionary game-theoretic model with analytical expressions for fixation probabilities and stationary distributions.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a theoretical analysis of a stochastic evolutionary process.,1,False,No lab setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,"Although figures may be numerically evaluated, the core method is analytical derivation; no explicit agent-based or Monte Carlo simulation is described as the primary method.",0.7,True,"Paper derives closed-form and symbolic expressions for fixation probabilities, transition matrices, and stationary distributions of strategies.",1,400,"For figures with explicit parameters they set m1 = 20 and m2 = 20, and total population size is m1 * m2 in the two-level case.",0.9,1,Evolutionary process is continuous with repeated imitation; there is no discrete finite-horizon round structure like in standard experimental PGGs. Represented as effectively one ongoing game.,0.4,1,Each cooperator (and punisher) contributes a fixed amount c; defectors contribute 0. There is no option for partial contributions beyond this fixed choice.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects and no default endowment allocation choice; strategies directly determine contributions.,1,N/R,"They specify an enhancement factor r1 for the G1 PGG and allocation fraction k1, but do not define MPCR explicitly as multiplier divided by group size.",0.3,N/A,No human interaction or communication protocol; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback design; model assumes payoff-based imitation only.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as payoff adjustments; there are no identities or displays.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface; infinite-horizon evolutionary process.,1,1,They explicitly model punishers at both levels who fine defectors/defecting communities.,1,N/R,"Two separate cost parameters: gamma1 at individual level and gamma2 at community level; schema offers a single scalar, so keep as not reported in that aggregate form.",0.4,N/R,Two separate fine parameters: beta1 and beta2; no single unified punishment intensity is defined.,0.4,0,Model only includes costly punishment; no rewarding of cooperators or punishers is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,They specify a contribution cost c per cooperator but do not define an explicit endowment amount per player.,0.3,N/R,"They report stationary distribution of strategy types (XC, XD, XP) rather than per-round contribution fractions.",0.2,N/R,"No explicit equilibrium or time-averaged contribution levels are reported, only strategy frequencies and fixation probabilities.",0.2,N/R,They discuss abundance of strategies but do not quantify payoff efficiency relative to the cooperative optimum.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs are implicit in payoff expressions but no particular numeric group payoff outcome is reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a theoretical evolutionary model without empirical data.,1,Two-level (n=2) multilevel public goods game on a community-structured population. Each G1 community has m1 individuals; each G2 community has m2 G1 communities. Cooperators and punishers contribute c in G1. Fraction k1 of G1 pool is multiplied by r1 and shared locally; fraction (1-k1) feeds higher-level pool. Punishers impose fines beta1 on defectors at cost gamma1 in G1; homogeneous punisher communities fine defecting communities at G2 with fine beta2 and cost gamma2. Strategy update via pairwise imitation with Fermi rule with strengths w1 (within G1) and w2 (between G1s within same G2); rare mutations with rate mu -> 0 allow Markov-chain approximation over homogeneous states.,Summarizes the formal MPGG and punishment structure explicitly described in the model and two-level punishment sections.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2009.01.006.md,Trust (implicit contracting baseline),Baseline treatment described as 'Trust' and 'implicit contracting game' without third-stage incentives.,1,"Incentive environment: implicit contract only (no reward, no punishment), lambda = N/A",This condition varies the presence/absence and type of incentives relative to other treatments; here there is no incentive stage.,1,True,Uses undergraduate human participants in lab sessions.,1,True,Random assignment to controlled incentive treatments in a lab.,1,True,Conducted at Harvard Business School's Computational Laboratory for Experimental Research.,1,False,All decisions made by human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical model; focuses on experimental results.,1,2,Investment/contracting game with Principal (P1) and Agent (P2).,1,1,Subjects 'played the game once and only once'.,1,1,"P1 can send 'some, all, or none' of the investable portion; contributions are continuous 0–10.",1,0,All endowment starts as private wealth; P1 must actively invest X to move into the 'shared' growing account.,1,1,"Amount sent X is tripled (3X) and split between two players; total return from 1 unit sent is 3 to the pair, i.e., MPCR = 3/2 = 1.5 to group, but definition asks per player. Since each unit moved creates 3 units for group of 2, marginal per capita return = 3/2 = 1.5. However, players individually only get what is returned/kept; paper does not present MPCR explicitly. To avoid over-interpretation, mark as N/R.",0,1,"P1 sends a cheap-talk message proposing desired division (Z, 3X−Z).",1,0,No mention of feedback beyond own interaction outcomes.,0.6,0,No punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward stage in this treatment.,1,1,It was common information that subjects played the game once and only once.,1,0,Implicit contracting game explicitly has no punishment or reward options.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward option in baseline.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,P1 endowment M1 = $20; P2 has M2 = $10 but main decision maker for contribution is P1.,1,0.545,Average transfer by P1 is $5.45 out of investable $10 (Table 1): 5.45/10 = 0.545.,1,5.45,"Table 1, 'Transfer ($)' for Trust.",1,0.8,Text: 'High Punish boosts efficiency to 89.5% compared to 80% in Trust'.,1,N/R,Average total payoffs by condition not reported; only average individual earnings pooled across treatments.,0,United States,Sessions run at Harvard Business School with undergraduates from 'universities in the Boston area' (US).,0.9,N/R,Only that they are undergraduates; no age statistics.,0,Mixed; proportion N/R,"Gender collected (codes placed in 'male' or 'female' boxes), but no overall proportions reported.",0.8,Undergraduate students,Explicitly states 'undergraduate students from universities in the Boston area'.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical computer lab at Harvard Business School.,1,"Two-player investment/implicit contract game; P1: M1=$20, investable M1*=$10; P2: M2=$10; P1 chooses transfer X∈[0,10] and desired return Z; P2 accepts/rejects, then chooses Y∈[0,3X] to return; amounts sent multiplied by 3.",Summarizes structure and parameters from Sections 2.1 and 3.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2009.01.006.md,"Low Reward (λ = +1, reward only)",Treatment named 'Low Reward' with λ=+1 and only reward option available.,1,"Incentive environment: Reward-only, low level; reward if Y ≥ Z with λ = +1 (P2 gain 1 per 1 spent by P1).",This treatment introduces low-cost reward mechanism as the main manipulation.,1,True,Lab experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Subjects randomly allocated to this treatment; controlled rules.,1,True,Conducted in Harvard Business School laboratory.,1,False,"Human decision makers, not simulated agents.",1,False,No purely analytical game solution beyond equilibrium discussion.,1,2,"Principal-agent pair (P1,P2).",1,1,One-shot game as in all treatments.,1,1,P1 can choose any X between 0 and 10.,1,0,"Endowment starts in private accounts, no automatic investment.",1,1,Same underlying investment technology as baseline (tripling of X). MPCR not explicitly reported; leave as N/R.,0,1,Same implicit contract extension with cheap-talk Z message.,1,0,No mention of aggregate histories or others' outcomes.,0.6,N/A,No punishment mechanism in reward-only treatment.,1,0,Identities kept double-blind; no indication that reward decisions reveal identity.,0.9,1,One-shot nature common information.,1,0,Reward-only environment; no punish option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,P1 can reward if Y ≥ Z.,1,1,Low Reward: 'P1 can spend 1 to increase P2's earnings by 1 (ρ = 1c)'. So cost per reward unit is 1.,1,1,Impact scalar λ = +1: P2 gains 1 per 1 spent by P1.,1,20,Same M1 = $20 for P1; P2 has $10.,1,0.668,Average transfer $6.68 (Table 1) divided by investable 10 gives 0.668.,1,6.68,"Table 1, 'Transfer ($)' for Low Reward.",1,N/R,Efficiency percentages reported only for Trust and High Punish.,0,N/R,No treatment-specific average total payoffs reported.,0,United States,Same subject pool across all treatments.,0.9,N/R,Only undergraduate status is given.,0,Mixed; proportion N/R,Gender tracked but not summarized by treatment.,0.8,Undergraduate students,Common design across treatments.,1,On site,Lab-based computerized sessions.,1,"Same 2-stage implicit contracting (X,Z; accept/reject; Y) plus third-stage reward: if Y ≥ Z, P1 can choose c∈[0,10] from non-investible $10 to increase P2's payoff by ρ = +1·c; double-blind protocol.",Summarizes Low Reward variant description.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2009.01.006.md,"High Reward (λ = +3, reward only)",Treatment named 'High Reward' with λ=+3 reward-only environment.,1,"Incentive environment: Reward-only, high level; reward if Y ≥ Z with λ = +3 (P2 gain 3 per 1 spent by P1).",Key manipulation is higher reward leverage compared to Low Reward and no-incentive baseline.,1,True,Same human-subject experiment design.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with random assignment.,1,True,Run in computational laboratory.,1,False,No artificial agents.,1,False,"Focus on behavioral results, not pure theory.",1,2,Principal-agent dyad.,1,1,Single play only.,1,1,Continuous transfer X within 0–10.,1,0,No automatic contribution; P1 must invest.,1,1,Tripling of X as in other treatments; MPCR not explicitly provided.,0,1,Implicit contract with cheap-talk Z is retained.,1,0,No evidence of cross-pair feedback.,0.6,N/A,No punishment mechanism here.,1,0,Double-blind protocol hides counterpart identity; reward choice not linked to identity.,0.9,1,One-shot nature common knowledge.,1,0,Reward-only treatment.,1,N/A,No punish option.,1,N/A,No punish option.,1,1,Principal can reward compliant agents.,1,1,"Cost c to P1 comes from M1−M1*=$10, with ρ=3c; cost per unit of c is 1.",1,3,High Reward: 'P1 can spend 1 to increase P2's earnings by 3 (ρ = 3c)'.,1,20,"Same M1=20, M1*=10; P2 gets 10.",1,0.653,Average transfer $6.53 (Table 1) / 10.,1,6.53,"Table 1, 'Transfer ($)' for High Reward.",1,N/R,Paper does not report overall efficiency for High Reward; only notes increases in returns.,0,N/R,No condition-specific total payoff given.,0,United States,Same subject pool as other treatments.,0.9,N/R,Age not provided.,0,Mixed; proportion N/R,Both males and females included; proportions by treatment not given.,0.8,Undergraduate students,Design description applies to all treatments.,1,On site,Computerized sessions in a lab.,1,"Same contracting game plus high-leverage reward stage: if Y ≥ Z, P1 can spend c∈[0,10] from non-investible endowment to increase P2’s payoff by ρ = +3c; about 44–46% of P1s implement positive rewards when available, with mean c≈2.22 (ρ≈$6.5).",Summarizes High Reward incentive structure and key third-stage usage statistics from Table 3.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2009.01.006.md,"Low Punish (λ = −1, punishment only)",Treatment named 'Low Punish' with λ=-1 punishment-only environment.,1,"Incentive environment: Punish-only, low level; punishment if Y < Z with λ = −1 (P2 loses 1 per 1 spent by P1).",Main manipulation is low-intensity punishment capability.,1,True,Human participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled conditions with random allocation.,1,True,Conducted at a computer laboratory.,1,False,All data from humans.,1,False,No standalone analytical model.,1,2,Game is between one principal and one agent.,1,1,Participants play the game once.,1,1,"P1’s transfer decision X∈[0,10].",1,0,No pre-allocated public fund; investment is active.,1,1,Same investment technology (3X); MPCR not stated explicitly.,0,1,P1 proposes division Z as cheap-talk contract.,1,0,No indication that other pairs’ results are shown.,0.6,0,Double-blind procedures hide identity; punishments do not reveal identities.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism in punish-only treatment.,1,1,One-shot nature disclosed to subjects.,1,1,P1 can punish if Y < Z.,1,1,Low Punish: 'P1 can spend 1 to reduce P2's earnings by 1 (ρ = −1c)'.,1,1,Impact scalar λ = −1 in absolute terms means P2 loses 1 per 1 spent.,1,0,"Punish-only environment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,P1 has M1=$20; P2 has $10.,1,0.641,Average transfer $6.41 (Table 1) / 10.,1,6.41,"Table 1, 'Transfer ($)' for Low Punish.",1,N/R,No explicit efficiency figure reported for Low Punish.,0,N/R,Total group payoffs by condition not provided.,0,United States,Same Boston-area undergraduate pool.,0.9,N/R,Age distribution not given.,0,Mixed; proportion N/R,Both genders included; treatment-level shares not reported.,0.8,Undergraduate students,Shared design.,1,On site,Sessions in HBS lab.,1,"Same contracting game plus low-intensity punishment stage: if Y < Z, P1 can choose c∈[0,10] from non-investible $10 to reduce P2's payoff by ρ = −1c; very low actual punishment use (a