10.1073_pnas.1108996108.md,SPG (Simple Public Goods Game),Paper states 'The experiment consists of three games... In the simple public goods (SPG) game...'. This is one of three treatments.,1,Game type: Simple public goods game without punishment or giving; Observation/hiding options present; Group size fixed at 4 players + 1 observer; Two blocks of 15 rounds each.,SPG differs from PG&PUN and PG&IR by absence of punishment or giving stage while keeping other structural elements.,0.9,True,Laboratory experiment with undergraduate human participants.,1,True,Controlled allocation to treatments and game structure; randomized observer; incentives controlled.,1,True,Conducted in experimental sessions with university students; no field setting mentioned.,0.95,False,All results come from human-subject sessions; no agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,No formal mathematical model presented as a separate analytical study.,1,4,‘four players play a public goods game in each round’; plus one observer who is not a player.,1,30,‘groups of five participants play two blocks of 15 rounds each’ so 30 rounds total per subject in a treatment.,1,0,"Players can contribute 0, 5, or 10 from 10, i.e., multiple discrete levels, not an all-or-nothing choice.",1,0,Endowment is initially in private account and then players choose how much to contribute.,1,0.5,Total contribution is doubled and divided evenly among 4 players: each gets (2*C)/4 = 0.5*C per unit contributed.,1,0,No communication or chat options described; standard anonymous computer experiment.,0.9,0,Players learn own payoff; no mention that they see others’ payoffs or punishment summaries.,0.6,0,No punishment stage in SPG; thus no punishment identities to show.,1,0,No giving/reward stage in SPG.,1,1,"Standard in such finite-horizon lab PGGs; they explicitly describe blocks of 15 rounds, implying subjects know the block length; SI likely confirms.",0.7,0,Simple public goods game has only contribution stage; punishment only in PG&PUN.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in SPG.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in SPG.,1,0,No give/indirect reciprocity phase; only in PG&IR.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"‘From his initial endowment of 10 points, each player can contribute 0, 5, or 10 points’.",1,N/R,"They report average contribution amounts (e.g., 5.74 points) but not normalized as 0–1 proportions by treatment.",0.4,5.74,‘Contributions are significantly higher in PG&PUN (8.54 ± 0.41 points) than in SPG (5.74 ± 0.77 points)’; use 5.74 as treatment mean.,0.9,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximal possible payoff reported by treatment.,0,N/R,Paper does not provide group-level aggregate payoff numbers by treatment.,0,Germany,"Participants are undergraduates from the University of Erfurt, Germany.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0,Undergraduate university students (mixed disciplines),Methods: ‘One hundred twenty undergraduate students from the University of Erfurt... recruit students from many different disciplines’.,1,On site,Physical lab sessions with students; not an online or field study.,0.95,"Two blocks of 15 rounds. In each block one subject is observer (non-player) and four are players. At each contribution phase, both players and observer choose observation/hiding options. Players can close their window at cost 0.5 points so the observer cannot learn their decision; observer can choose open or concealed observation, with concealed observation costing 0.5 points and not informing the player. Observer may observe up to two players per phase. After block 1, observer may pay 0.5 points to actively choose three of the four players as coplayers for block 2 (the remaining player becomes observer) or opt for random assignment at zero cost.",Summarized from description of observation/hiding mechanisms and block/role structure.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.1108996108.md,PG&PUN (Public Goods with Punishment),Described as ‘public goods game with punishment possibility (PG&PUN)’; one of three treatments.,1,Game type: Public goods game with punishment stage; Punishment technology fixed at cost 1 to punisher and -3 to target per punishment point; Observation/hiding options present; Group size 4 players + 1 observer; Two blocks of 15 rounds each.,PG&PUN differs from SPG and PG&IR by the presence and structure of the punishment phase.,1,True,Human-subject lab treatment with undergraduates.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized laboratory treatment.",1,True,Same lab setting as SPG.,0.95,False,No computer-only simulations described.,1,False,No standalone analytical model.,1,4,Same base PGG structure: ‘four players play a public goods game in each round’; plus one observer.,1,30,Same two blocks of 15 rounds per group.,1,0,"Contribution levels 0, 5, or 10 from 10.",1,0,Endowment initially in private account.,1,0.5,Same public goods technology: total contribution doubled and evenly redistributed among four players.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,0,Players see individual round payoff; no indication they see others’ payoffs globally.,0.6,1,"Punishment is assigned to each of the other three identifiable players; observer learns ‘the punishment decision of each player’ and players learn if their activity is openly observed, implying decisions are linked to player IDs.",0.8,0,"No giving/reward stage in PG&PUN, only punishment.",1,1,"Same finite 15-round block structure, likely known to subjects.",0.7,1,‘public goods provision is followed by a punishment stage in which each player may assign a punishment point’.,1,1,‘A punishment point deducts one point from the punisher's account and three points from the account of the punished player’.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces punished player's account by 3 points.,1,0,No give/indirect reciprocity phase in PG&PUN.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same 10-point endowment at start of each round for each player in the PGG phase.,1,N/R,"Only raw contributions reported, not normalized as 0–1; 8.54 from 10 could imply 0.854 but they never present proportions.",0.4,8.54,‘Contributions are significantly higher in PG&PUN (8.54 ± 0.41 points) than in SPG (5.74 ± 0.77 points)’; use 8.54 as treatment mean.,0.9,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio.,0,N/R,No treatment-level group payoff reported.,0,Germany,"Same subject pool as other treatments: University of Erfurt, Germany.",1,N/R,Age distribution not reported.,0.3,N/R,No gender information.,0,Undergraduate university students (mixed disciplines),Same pool across treatments.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions with physical attendance.,0.95,"Same two-block structure and observation/hiding options as SPG. After the contribution stage, a punishment phase occurs: each player can assign punishment points to each of the other three players. Each punishment point costs the punisher 1 point and reduces the punished player’s payoff by 3 points. Before punishment decisions, players decide whether to keep their punishment window open (no cost) or closed (cost 0.5 points), which hides their punishment decisions from the observer. The observer can observe up to two players’ punishment decisions per round, choosing open (free, player informed) or concealed (cost 0.5 points, player not informed) observation.",Summarized from description of PG&PUN and Figure 1.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.1108996108.md,PG&IR (Public Goods with Indirect Reciprocity/Giving),Described as ‘public goods game followed by an indirect reciprocity game (PG&IR)’; one of three treatments.,1,Game type: Public goods game followed by a giving (indirect reciprocity) phase; Points given are tripled for the receiver; Observation/hiding options present in both contribution and giving phases; Group size 4 players + 1 observer; Two blocks of 15 rounds each.,PG&IR differs by adding a 'give' phase with tripling of given points instead of punishment.,1,True,Human-subject lab treatment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory manipulation of game structure across treatments.,1,True,"Same session-based, in-person lab as other treatments.",0.95,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,No stand-alone analytical derivation as primary method.,1,4,Same PGG base: four players in each round; one observer.,1,30,Two blocks of 15 rounds.,1,0,"Contribution options 0, 5, or 10; not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment starts in private account.,1,0.5,Same PGG technology as other treatments: total contribution doubled and evenly divided among 4.,1,0,No chat or communication options mentioned.,0.9,0,No report that players see group-level summaries of payoffs or giving; only individual payoffs and observer’s information flows discussed.,0.6,0,No punishment stage in PG&IR.,1,1,"In ‘give’ phase, each player is donor to a specific receiver and also receiver for another; giving decisions are about identifiable partners, and the observer can observe particular players’ giving decisions by their ID. This implies identity-linked giving.",0.8,1,Finite 15-round blocks; subjects likely informed of round numbers.,0.7,0,No punishment phase; instead a give phase.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,‘each round of the public goods game is followed by a “give” phase... Points given are tripled for the receiver’.,1,1,"When a player gives 1 point, his own account is reduced by 1 and the receiver gains triple that amount; thus per unit given, the cost is 1 to the giver.",0.9,3,‘Points given are tripled for the receiver’ so 1 given yields +3 to receiver.,1,10,Same 10-point endowment for the PGG each round; giving presumably uses remaining points but base endowment is 10.,0.9,N/R,No normalized 0–1 contribution rate reported for PG&IR.,0,N/R,Mean contribution amount for PG&IR is not reported; only SPG vs PG&PUN comparison is given.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoffs for PG&IR reported.,0,Germany,"Same subject pool: University of Erfurt, Germany.",1,N/R,No age data given.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Undergraduate university students (mixed disciplines),Same recruitment description applies.,1,On site,Same lab environment.,0.95,"Same two-block structure and observer/player role rotation as other treatments. After the contribution phase, a ‘give’ phase occurs each round: each player is assigned as donor to one specific coplayer (receiver) and can decide how many points to give; each point given is tripled for the receiver. Giving decisions are made after players are informed about the last contribution and former give decisions of the receiver. Before giving, players can choose to keep their ‘give’ window open (free) or closed (cost 0.5 points) to hide their giving decision from the observer. The observer can observe up to two players’ giving decisions, choosing open (free, player informed) or concealed (cost 0.5 points, player not informed) observation.",Summarized from PG&IR description and general observation/hiding mechanism.,0.95
10.1007_s10683-013-9367-7.md,First-order treatment – all rounds (Pool1 available),Paper describes two treatments distinguished by Pool punishment variant. First-order treatment pools 9 groups where Pool is first-order (no punishment of second-order free riders). Condition is the whole 50-round history in that treatment.,0.86,"Pool punishment variant available: first-order pool (Pool1), where only non-contributors to Mutual Aid are fined; second-order non-payers to punishment pool are not punished. Players can choose between NoPun, Peer, Pool1, or No each round.","Treatment definition explicitly states that in groups 1–9 Pool was first-order, with second-order free riders not sanctioned. Choice set {NoPun, Peer, Pool, No} is common but Pool’s rule differs.",0.96,True,"Experiment with 238 first-year students in Vienna, divided into groups, playing 50 rounds.",1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomly allocated subjects and imposed incentive structure.,1,True,Conducted with university students in sessions using z-Tree; no field context described.,0.96,False,All results reported are from human-subject sessions; theory cited separately.,1,False,Analytical model described in Sect. 2 but experiment reports empirical data; this record is for the empirical condition.,1,13,"Groups have 12–14 players. Exact per-group sizes not given; use mid-point as representative, acknowledging variance.",0.4,50,“for the duration of 50 rounds”; players not told number but design fixed.,1,1,Contributions are binary: either contribute 1 MU or 0 MU. This matches discrete (all-or-nothing) contribution choice.,0.98,0,Each round players receive 3 MU endowment; they must actively decide to contribute 1 MU. Endowment starts in private account.,0.95,3,Each 1 MU contributed is multiplied by 3 and divided among others. This is an other-only game; marginal per capita return to others is 3.,0.9,0,Players were anonymous and “prevented from communicating.”,1,1,"After each round players learned how many chose each game, how many in each game chose which strategy, and which payoff they obtained. This is summary info about others’ choices/payoffs.",0.96,0,"Anonymity stressed; no information on who punished whom, only counts. So identities not revealed.",0.98,0,No rewarding mechanism in design.,1,0,"Players “were not told that the number of rounds was fixed beforehand at 50,” implying total/remaining rounds were hidden.",0.9,1,Players can choose Peer (peer punishment) or Pool (institutional punishment).,1,0.5,"In Peer, each punisher pays 0.5 MU per defector. Pool uses a separate fee F=0.5 MU to punishment pool, but cost per fine not fixed; base individual cost is 0.5.",0.9,1,"In Peer, each defector is fined 1 MU per peer punisher (β=1 MU). In Pool, fine per punisher B=1 MU.",0.95,0,Design only includes punishment; no positive rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in this experiment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in this experiment.,1,3,"“In each round, players were given 3 MU.”",1,0.489,"“All in all, 48.9 per cent of all decisions were in favor of contributing to the Mutual Aid” in first-order treatment.",0.98,N/R,Paper gives frequencies but not average MU contributed per player per round across all games.,0.2,N/R,No direct measure of efficiency (actual group payoff / maximum possible) reported or derivable from aggregate data.,0.1,N/R,Only average individual payoffs per game/treatment reported; not aggregate per group per round.,0.2,Austria,Subjects are first-year students from universities in Vienna; country inferred as Austria.,0.96,First-year university students; exact age distribution N/R,Described only as first-year students; typical ages not explicitly given.,0.4,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,0.1,First-year university students,Explicitly: “238 first-year students from universities in Vienna.”,1,On site,Lab-style experiment with university students using z-Tree in sessions; implies in-person at lab or computer room.,0.96,"Mutual Aid public good variant with ‘others-only’ benefit. In this treatment, the Pool game is first-order: Pool punishers pay F=0.5 MU into punishment pool and 1 MU to Mutual Aid; only non-contributors to Mutual Aid (defectors) are fined 1 MU per pool punisher, while contributors to Mutual Aid who do not pay into pool (second-order free riders) are not punished. Players choose each round among: NoPun MA, Peer MA with optional peer punishment, Pool1 MA, or non-participation (No) that yields 0.5 MU baseline without social interaction.",Summarizes the specific rule variant defining this treatment including Mutual Aid structure and Pool1 sanction rule.,0.95
10.1007_s10683-013-9367-7.md,Second-order treatment – all rounds (Pool2 available),Second treatment uses a different rule for Pool punishment (second-order). Groups 10–18 play this variant; condition is the full 50-round history under this rule.,0.86,"Pool punishment variant available: second-order pool (Pool2), where all players who do not contribute to the punishment pool (including Mutual Aid contributors who do not pay into pool and pure defectors) are fined. Players can choose between NoPun, Peer, Pool2, or No each round.",Explicitly described as second-order variant where everyone who has not contributed to both Mutual Aid and punishment pool is fined B per pool punisher.,0.97,True,"Same human-subject experimental sessions, but with different Pool rule for 9 groups (118 subjects).",1,True,Controlled design with randomized grouping and induced payoffs.,1,True,"Laboratory-style sessions with students, described similarly to first-order treatment.",0.96,False,Results are from human participants rather than simulated agents.,1,False,Analytical model separated; this record is for empirical treatment with second-order Pool.,1,13,Each group has 12–14 players; mid-point used as representative since per-group size not detailed.,0.4,50,Identical 50-round structure in both treatments.,1,1,Contribution decision is binary: contribute 1 MU or 0.,0.98,0,Endowment is 3 MU at start of each round; no automatic contribution to public fund.,0.95,3,"Same Mutual Aid game parameters: c=1 MU, r=3, contribution benefits only others.",0.9,0,Communication prohibited; identical across treatments.,1,1,"After each round, players see counts of strategy choices and payoffs for strategies in their group.",0.96,0,Anonymity preserved; no mapping from punishments to individual IDs.,0.98,0,Experiment does not implement rewards.,1,0,Subjects not informed about the 50-round horizon.,0.9,1,Peer and Pool punishment games are available as options.,1,0.5,Peer fee γ=0.5 MU per defector; Pool requires payment F=0.5 MU into punishment pool.,0.9,1,"Each non-payer to pool is fined B=1 MU per pool punisher; in Peer, each defector is fined 1 MU per peer punisher.",0.95,0,No reward scheme; only punishment mechanisms.,1,N/A,Rewarding is not part of the design.,1,N/A,Rewarding is not part of the design.,1,3,Each round players receive 3 MU; same as first-order treatment.,1,0.882,“the frequency of contributions was significantly higher in the second-order treatment … 88.2 per cent.”,0.98,N/R,"No aggregate average MU contribution per player per round is reported, only frequency of contributing.",0.2,N/R,Paper notes average payoffs but not normalized efficiency relative to full-cooperation benchmark for each group.,0.1,N/R,"Only mean individual payoffs per game type are mentioned, not total group payoff.",0.2,Austria,Same subject pool: first-year students from Vienna universities.,0.96,First-year university students; exact age distribution N/R,As in first-order treatment; only educational status given.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not described.,0.1,First-year university students,Same recruitment description applies: first-year students.,1,On site,"Same lab-style setup as first-order treatment, using z-Tree in sessions.",0.96,"Mutual Aid game with ‘others-only’ benefit. In this treatment, the Pool game is second-order: Pool punishers pay 1 MU to Mutual Aid and 0.5 MU to punishment pool; all players who do not contribute to both Mutual Aid and punishment pool (i.e., pure defectors and Mutual Aid-only contributors) are fined 1 MU per pool punisher. Players each round choose between NoPun MA, Peer MA with peer punishment, Pool2 MA, or non-participation (No) yielding 0.5 MU. This treatment led to high overall cooperation and frequent adoption of pool punishment institutions.",Summarizes distinctive feature of second-order Pool variant and same overall game structure.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.1604989113.md,"Treaty participation public goods coalition experiment (Dannenberg, Lange & Sturm 2014)",Only concrete empirical study with human subjects described: 'In an experiment testing this theory... (19).',0.9,"Presence of self-enforcing treaty mechanism for coalition formation in a public goods game (treaty vs. no-treaty baseline across rounds; within the treaty treatment, coalition size is endogenous, targeting k* = 3 when N = 10 under quadratic cost).",Theory section describes a self-enforcing treaty game; experiment (19) is said to 'test this theory' and compares behavior with and without the treaty; coalition size is the key treatment variable within the treaty mechanism.,0.6,True,Described explicitly as an 'experiment' with 'subjects' whose payoff depends on randomly selected rounds.,1,True,"Controlled experimental public goods game in a lab-style setting, varying institutional rules.",0.95,True,Framed as an economics experiment with rounds and monetary payoffs; reference Economica 2014 lab-style study; no indication of field setting.,0.8,False,"Human subjects with monetary payoffs, not a computer-only agent-based model.",1,False,"This row refers specifically to the cited experiment, not the surrounding analytical models.",0.95,10,"Text: 'coalition size settles out to a level of 3.2 on average, although n = 10.' Here n denotes the number of players in the group.",0.9,10,Text: 'choices being made over 10 rounds (but with each subject's actual payoff depending only on how the game is played in just 1 of these rounds chosen at random)'.,1,1,Public goods game with contribution choice; theory portion specifies continuous (or at least multi-level) contributions; no indication of binary all-or-nothing in the experiment.,0.6,0,Standard voluntary contribution mechanism: endowment starts in private account and players decide how much to contribute.,0.7,N/R,Marginal per capita return b is discussed theoretically but no numerical value for b is given for the actual experiment.,0.2,0,No mention of communication; standard public goods coalition experiments typically disallow free-form chat unless stated otherwise.,0.5,1,Public goods experiments with coalition formation normally must show others' contributions/payoffs to allow learning across 10 rounds; inferred though not explicitly stated.,0.5,0,No punishment mechanism is mentioned for this experiment.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism is mentioned for this experiment.,0.9,1,Participants usually know total number of rounds (10); common in designs where only one random round is payoff-relevant; not explicitly reported but likely.,0.6,0,No punishment or sanction stage described for this specific experiment; focus is on coalition participation and contributions.,0.9,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No mention of any reward or bonus mechanism beyond standard payoffs from the public good.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Endowment per round is not numerically specified in this paper.,0.1,N/R,Paper reports only qualitative comparison (contributions/payoffs 'no higher in the early rounds but improve slightly in later rounds'); no numerical fraction of endowment.,0.1,N/R,No specific token/point contribution levels reported.,0.1,N/R,Only relative statements about payoffs improving slightly; no numeric efficiency measure reported.,0.1,N/R,"Average payoffs are described qualitatively ('improve slightly'), but no numbers.",0.1,N/R,Recruitment location or nationality of subjects in (19) is not specified in this summary; original Economica article would contain this.,0.1,N/R,No age information is provided in this paper.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information is provided.,0.1,N/R,No education/background information about participants is given.,0.1,On site,"Lab-style public goods experiment with human subjects and monetary payoffs; in 2014 Economica context, this would almost certainly be conducted in a physical laboratory rather than fully online, though not explicitly stated here.",0.6,Voluntary coalition formation public goods game with quadratic contribution costs; 10-player groups; 10 rounds; one randomly selected round for payment; theory implies self-enforcing treaty with predicted equilibrium coalition size k* = 3; observed average coalition size approximately 3.2; contributions and payoffs slightly higher in later rounds when coalition size approximates k*; no significant difference from baseline without treaty in early rounds.,"Direct description: 'The experiment assumes that the cost of contributing to the public good is quadratic rather than linear, which implies k* = 3 for any N. In this experiment, over time, the coalition size settles out to a level of 3.2 on average, although n = 10.' Plus statements about contributions/payoffs relative to the no-treaty case.",0.95
10.1016_j.jtbi.2021.110606.md,Numerical integration of eco-evolutionary PD model – baseline parameter set (Fig. 1),"Single ODE model with fixed parameters σ1=0.52, σ2=0.72, σ3=0.41, ξ=0.5, δ=1.39, β=2.6; multiple initial conditions are explored but same parameter set, summarized together in text around Fig. 1.",0.88,"Initial fractions of strategies (x0,y0,z0) under fixed parameters σ1=0.52, σ2=0.72, σ3=0.41, ξ=0.5, δ=1.39, β=2.6; examples: (0.7,0,0.2), (0.3,0.3,0.3), (0,0.2,0.7), (0.4,0.2,0.3).",Model parameters held fixed while qualitative outcomes are compared across differing initial conditions; these are the manipulated aspects in the numerical study.,0.9,False,Purely mathematical eco-evolutionary ODE model with no human or animal subjects; only numerical analysis.,1.0,False,No controlled laboratory or field experiment; only simulations of differential equations.,1.0,False,Not a lab or field setting; simulations only.,1.0,True,They perform numerical integration of system (7) with Runge–Kutta–Fehlberg and explore parameter and initial-condition space.,1.0,True,"Substantial analytical work: derivation of equilibria, Jacobian, eigenvalues, Routh–Hurwitz conditions.",1.0,0,"No discrete players; infinite, well-mixed population modeled by continuous densities x, y, z, w.",1.0,0,"Continuous-time replicator-like dynamics, not a repeated game with discrete rounds.",1.0,0,"Not a contribution game; strategies are pure types (C,P,D,F) with continuous population shares, not discrete contribution choices.",0.8,N/A,No endowments or contribution decisions; eco-evolutionary PD model with fixed payoff matrix.,1.0,N/A,"Prisoner’s dilemma pairwise payoff matrix, not a public goods game with MPCR.",1.0,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely theoretical model.,1.0,N/A,No interface or feedback to agents; continuous dynamics without explicit information display.,1.0,N/A,"Punishment is a payoff parameter δ applied in the matrix, not an individual-level decision with identities.",1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1.0,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1.0,1,Model includes punishers P imposing a fine δ on defectors at cost δ to themselves.,1.0,N/R,"The cost is δ in payoff units, but no endowment/currency is defined; cannot express as ‘coins per unit’ in experimental sense.",0.4,N/R,"Magnitude per coin spent is undefined since no discrete punishment action or coin is modeled, only a symmetric cost δ.",0.3,0,No separate rewarding strategy or reward parameter beyond standard PD payoffs.,1.0,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1.0,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1.0,N/A,No notion of individual endowments; payoffs are per interaction.,1.0,N/A,"Not a contribution game; outcomes are population fractions and dynamical attractors, not contributions.",1.0,N/A,No contributions defined.,1.0,N/R,"They describe coexistence, extinction, oscillations, etc., but do not define efficiency as payoff relative to a maximum.",0.4,N/R,"Average population payoff 0f is defined, but no specific numerical outcome is reported as a main dependent variable for this baseline set.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Model is a set of deterministic ODEs without subjects.,1.0,"Weak Prisoner’s Dilemma with three strategic types (C, D, P) and an altruistic free-space type F. Payoffs: R=1, S=0, T=β>1, P=0, punishers and defectors interacting incur symmetric cost δ>0. Free space provides positive payoffs σ1, σ2, σ3 to C, P, D respectively, and has zero own payoff. Population is infinite and well mixed; eco-evolutionary dynamics given by modified replicator-like ODEs with mortality rate ξ and free-space feedback.",Summarized from payoff matrix and model description in Section 2.,1.0
10.1088_1367-2630_abf205.md,"Fixed contribution feedback-evolving game (delay τ, theoretical model)",Paper defines a case where cooperators invest a fixed amount g; analyzed via ODE/DDE system (Appendix A) for various τ.,1,Time delay τ in applying cooperative reinvestment to environmental dynamics; monitoring probability p; punishment fine β; intrinsic growth rate r; fixed cooperative contribution g; population size N; maximum individual harvest b_m; carrying capacity R_m.,These parameters are systematically varied in the fixed-contribution model equations and stability analysis.,1,False,No human participants; purely mathematical and numerical analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it's a theoretical dynamical systems study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only equations and simulations.,1,True,Uses delay differential equations and numerical calculations; no human subjects.,1,True,"Substantial analytical derivations of fixed points, eigenvalues, and stability conditions.",1,1000,"Representative simulations use N=1000 (e.g., Fig. A1: N=1000).",0.9,0,Continuous-time evolutionary model; no discrete rounds.,1,1,"Continuous replicator dynamics with continuous endowment/payoffs, not binary contributions.",0.7,N/A,No explicit individual endowment allocation decision; contributions are modeled as parameters in equations without human agents.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or MPCR terminology; model is a resource-harvesting framework.,1,N/A,No human interaction or communication in theoretical model.,1,0,No informational interface; only population-level dynamics.,1,N/A,No identities; only fractions of cooperators and defectors.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism; only institutional punishment and cooperative reinvestment.,1,0,No discrete rounds; continuous time.,1,1,Defectors are punished with fine β with probability p by centralized management.,1,N/R,"Model specifies fine β to defectors, not the cost to punishers or institution.",1,N/R,"Only overall fine β is given, without a per-token structure.",1,0,No positive rewards for cooperation beyond resource feedback; only punishment is institutional.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Endowment per individual is y(t)/R_m * b_m, but no fixed numeric baseline endowment is singled out.",0.6,N/R,No normalized contribution variable reported; only contributions via parameter g.,1,N/R,"Contribution is a model parameter g, not a measured outcome.",1,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximal group payoff reported.,1,N/R,Only individual payoffs and resource levels are described analytically; no aggregate payoff statistic is given.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical analysis (analytical + numerical) of dynamical system.,1,Feedback-evolving common-pool resource model with logistic growth; population of size N well-mixed; strategies are cooperation (fixed reinvestment g) and defection; defectors monitored with probability p and fined β; cooperator fraction x(t) evolves by replicator equation; resource y(t) follows logistic growth minus harvesting plus delayed cooperative reinvestment g N x(t-τ).,Summarizes model specification in section 2 and Appendix A for fixed contribution case.,1
10.1088_1367-2630_abf205.md,"Proportional contribution feedback-evolving game (delay τ, theoretical model)",Main model in sections 2–3 and Appendix B where cooperators reinvest proportion α of endowment; time delay τ affects dynamics and can induce Hopf bifurcation.,1,Time delay τ in applying cooperative reinvestment; reinvestment proportion α of cooperator endowment; monitoring probability p; punishment fine β; intrinsic growth rate r; population size N; maximum individual harvest b_m; carrying capacity R_m.,"These parameters define the proportional-contribution feedback-evolving game and are varied in stability and bifurcation analysis (e.g., Figs. 2–5).",1,False,Study is fully theoretical and simulation-based without human data.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with subjects; only parameter sweeps in equations.,1,False,Not conducted in a laboratory with participants.,1,True,Uses delay differential equations simulated numerically plus analytical work.,1,True,"Derives fixed points, stability conditions, and Hopf bifurcation analytically.",1,1000,Representative examples for proportional case all use N=1000 (Figs. 2–5).,0.9,0,Continuous-time dynamics; no discrete rounds.,1,1,"Strategy is a type (C or D); resource use and reinvestment are continuous functions, not binary contributions.",0.7,N/A,No human choices of contribution; reinvestment is mechanically α times endowment for cooperators.,1,N/R,No standard public goods MPCR defined; payoffs depend on resource level and punishment.,1,N/A,No human communication in the model.,1,0,Model does not include any informational displays to agents.,1,N/A,No individual identities or observation structure; only anonymous fractions.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,0,No rounds shown; continuous time.,1,1,Defectors monitored with probability p and punished with fine β.,1,N/R,Cost to punisher/institution is not modeled separately from fine β.,1,N/R,Only overall fine parameter β; no per-unit mapping given.,1,0,No explicit positive incentives; only environmental feedback and punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Individuals receive time-varying endowment y(t)/R_m * b_m; no fixed numeric baseline is specified.,0.6,N/R,No normalized contribution fraction is reported as an outcome; reinvestment is fixed by α.,1,N/R,"Actual reinvestment amount α * (y/R_m * b_m) is part of the model structure, not reported as a measured DV.",1,N/R,"Study reports cooperation levels and resource levels, not explicit efficiency ratios.",1,N/R,Group payoff is not aggregated or numerically reported; focus is on dynamical behavior of x(t) and y(t).,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model-only study (analytical + simulation).,1,"Well-mixed population of size N with strategies C and D; resource y(t) follows logistic growth with carrying capacity R_m and intrinsic rate r; each individual harvest per unit time is (y(t)/R_m) * b_m; cooperators reinvest fraction α of their endowment into the resource with a time delay τ, so reinvestment term in resource equation is (α N b_m x(t-τ) y(t-τ))/R_m; defectors do not reinvest and are punished with fine β with probability p, payoffs P_C(t)=b_m y(t)(1-α)/R_m and P_D(t)=b_m y(t)/R_m - pβ; cooperator fraction x(t) evolves via replicator equation with environmental feedback. Analysis explores parameter regions of resource growth (slow, moderate, rapid) and institutional strength, showing that for proportional reinvestment time delay can induce Hopf bifurcations and stable oscillations in x(t) and y(t).","Condenses model description from section 2, equation (2), and Appendix B.",1
10.1016_j.chaos.2015.06.002.md,"Simulation – spatial PGG with mutual punishment (generic condition over r and α, K=0.1, L=100)",Single simulation model on 100×100 lattice with noise K=0.1; r and α varied parametrically in Figs. 1–3. Treated as one baseline condition because structure and update rule are identical across those parameter sweeps.,0.8,"Multiplication factor r and punishment fine α varied across runs (r range as in Fig. 1/2, α range as in Fig. 1/2) with noise K=0.1 and lattice size L=100",Model section defines r and α; Results section describes ρc as function of r for different α and as function of α for different r with fixed K=0.1 and L=100.,0.9,False,Study is an evolutionary game simulation on a lattice with no human or animal subjects mentioned.,1.0,False,"No controlled experiment with participants, only numerical simulations of a defined model.",1.0,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational.,1.0,True,"Authors describe lattice-based evolutionary dynamics with time steps and realizations, typical of numerical simulations.",1.0,False,There is a simple payoff difference expression (Eq. 4) but main results rely on numerical simulations on lattices.,0.9,10000,Players occupy a 100×100 lattice in main simulations (Section 3). Player count = 100×100.,1.0,30000,"They run simulations for 30,000 time steps and assess steady state using last 2,000 time steps.",0.95,1,Each cooperator contributes a fixed total unit cost if choosing C and zero if D; binary strategy choice C or D implies all-or-nothing at strategy level; there is no continuous contribution choice.,0.8,0,In evolutionary simulations there is no default allocation between private vs public; cooperators actively contribute unit cost each round while defectors contribute 0. This is most consistent with endowment starting fully private.,0.6,0.2,"Each cooperator contributes 1, group size is 5, total contributions multiplied by r and divided equally among 5. The marginal per capita return per contributed unit is r/5. For standard PGG definition, MPCR = r/N = r/5; since r is varied, the structural MPCR factor per r is 1/5=0.2.",0.7,0,No communication or messaging among lattice players is described; purely strategy updating by imitation.,1.0,0,No interfaces or feedback summaries beyond payoff-based updating; simulation agents do not view summaries.,1.0,0,Identities and visibility of punishment are not modeled; punishment is implicit in payoff calculation.,1.0,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1.0,0,Agents are simple automata with no interface; number of rounds is not displayed.,1.0,1,Model introduces mutual punishment with fine α whenever neighboring strategies differ.,1.0,1,"Payoff term includes −α|si−sj|; for differing strategies this equals −α, interpreted as the punishment fine or cost per punished neighbor. Thus per punishment event the cost/fine magnitude is α; normalized here as 1·α, so cost coefficient is 1.",0.6,1,There is no separate technology parameter; each punishment interaction directly imposes a fine α on payoff. Mapping to cost×multiplier form implies a 1:1 mapping (multiplier 1 on the fine parameter α).,0.6,0,No reward or positive sanction mechanism described; only punishment fines.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1.0,1,Each cooperator contributes a total unit cost per time step across its five groups; this unit is effectively the per-round endowment used in PGG.,0.8,N/R,Paper reports only the fraction of cooperators ρc (strategy-level) and not contribution as proportion of endowment,0.4,N/R,Only strategy frequencies and payoffs are discussed; no explicit average contribution per player beyond binary C/D.,0.4,N/R,No explicit measure of group efficiency (total payoff vs maximum) reported or derivable from aggregate statistics given.,0.3,N/R,Paper focuses on fractions of cooperators and qualitative payoff comparisons; no numeric group payoff values given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; purely computational model.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Lattice-based numerical simulation with no empirical data.,1.0,Spatial public goods game on a 2D square lattice (von Neumann neighborhood) with group size 5; each cooperator contributes 1 unit divided over its 5 groups; total contributions in each group multiplied by r and shared equally; mutual punishment fine α applied symmetrically between neighboring players holding different strategies; strategies updated synchronously by imitation with Fermi rule and noise K.,Summarized from the Model and Results sections including payoff equations and update rule.,1.0
