hird 0.37, Low 0.20).",Summarizes punishment statistics unique to this condition.,1
10.1177_0190272512442397.md,"Study 1 – Conflict (IPD, live groups)",Study 1 has two between-subjects conditions: conflict (intergroup prisoner’s dilemma) vs no conflict (separate PDs). This row describes the conflict condition.,1,Structural conflict present: intergroup prisoner’s dilemma (IPD); outgroup competition endogenous (observed outgroup contributions 0–3 each round).,"In this condition, payoffs depend on both ingroup and outgroup contributions (IPD). Outgroup contribution varies naturally and is used as a moderator.",1,True,Human undergraduates directly made decisions in the lab.,1,True,Random assignment of sessions to conflict vs no conflict with controlled payoffs.,1,True,Conducted in a university computer lab with experimental software.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulation; all decisions from human subjects.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as a standalone method.,1,6,"Each session had two teams of three (Red and Green), so 6 players per interacting set.",1,30,Participants completed 30 rounds after a practice round.,1,0,Binary decision each round: contribute full 10-point endowment or keep it; no continuous choice over amount.,1,0,Endowment e=10 is given to each participant each round and they decide whether to contribute it; starts in private account.,1,N/R,Intergroup PD payoff: h_i=5(m_a−m_b)+15+e(1−σ_i). This is not a standard linear public-goods MPCR; no per-capita return parameter is labeled as such.,0.6,0,All interaction took place via experimental software; no mention of communication or chat allowed.,0.9,1,"After each round participants saw number of contributors and punishers in that round and in total, plus earnings.",1,0,No indication that identities of punishers were revealed; feedback only aggregated numbers and payoffs.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism was implemented.,1,1,"They were instructed about a 30-round task plus practice, implying known horizon typical of such designs, though not explicitly stated as displayed each round.",0.6,1,Participants could invest a ‘punishment account’ into a group punishment fund to punish noncontributors.,1,1,Each punishment point contributed is a direct cost of 1 point to the punisher (foregone earnings).,1,N/R,Noncontributors lose the total fund divided by number of noncontributors. Per-unit impact of 1 contributed point depends on number of noncontributors (1/k); no single fixed ‘tech’ parameter is reported.,0.4,0,Only punishment of noncontributors is described; no rewarding institution.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,20,"Each round: 10-point contribution endowment plus separate 10-point punishment account, both described as ‘endowments’.",1,0.64,Reported mean contribution frequency in conflict condition: M=.64.,1,6.4,"All-or-nothing contribution of 10 points with probability 0.64, so expected contribution = 0.64*10=6.4.",0.9,N/R,No normalized efficiency (actual vs maximum possible payoff) is reported or derivable from summary stats.,0.3,N/R,Only means and SDs of behaviors are given; no total or average group payoffs by condition.,0.2,USA,Participants were undergraduates at a large private university in the Northeast United States.,0.9,N/R,Described as undergraduates but no age statistics reported.,0.6,"83 women, 37 men",Participant section for Study 1 reports 83 women and 37 men (n=120).,1,Undergraduate students,Both described as undergraduate volunteers.,1,On site,"Participants came to a lab, were led to cubicles, and interacted over a local computer network.",1,"Intergroup prisoner’s dilemma with three-person teams; payoff: h_i = 5(m_a − m_b) + 15 + e(1 − σ_i), e=10. Second 10-point punishment account each round funds a group punishment pool that is equally divided as losses among noncontributors. Leadership preference measured once post-game by vote to create a leader with power to set required investments and punish violators.","Summarizes key payoff structure, punishment rule, and leadership decision relevant to cooperation and punishment.",1
10.1177_0190272512442397.md,"Study 1 – No conflict (separate PDs, live groups)",Same Study 1 but control condition: teams play independent 3-person prisoner’s dilemmas without affecting the other team.,1,Structural conflict absent: two separate 3-person prisoner’s dilemmas (PDs); outgroup contributions observable but payoff-irrelevant (m_b fixed constant in payoff).,"No conflict condition constructed by setting m_b to a constant so that outgroup behavior cannot affect payoffs, though participants can still see the other team.",1,True,Same participants and procedures as conflict condition.,1,True,Random assignment of sessions to conflict vs no conflict with controlled payoff transformation.,1,True,Conducted in the on-site laboratory environment.,1,False,Live human interaction only.,1,False,No purely analytical model.,1,6,Same sessions: two teams of three per session.,1,30,Same number of rounds in both conditions.,1,0,Contribute entire 10 or keep; no partial contributions.,1,0,Endowment is initially fully in private account.,1,N/R,Transformation sets m_b to constant; no explicit marginal per capita return parameter provided.,0.4,0,Identical communication restrictions as conflict condition; no chat mentioned.,0.9,1,Participants in both conditions saw numbers of contributors and punishers in both teams and their earnings each round.,1,0,No IDs for punishers mentioned; only counts shown.,0.9,0,No reward institution.,1,1,"Game length (30 rounds) explained ex ante, implying subjects know horizon.",0.6,1,Same Yamagishi-style punishment fund available.,1,1,As in conflict condition: each punishment point contributed costs the punisher 1 point.,1,N/R,Impact per unit varies with number of noncontributors; no fixed coefficient.,0.4,0,No reward institution implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same structure: 10-point contribution endowment plus 10-point punishment account per round.,1,0.55,Reported mean contribution in no conflict condition: M=.55.,1,5.5,Expected contribution = 0.55*10=5.5 under all-or-nothing 10-point endowment.,0.9,N/R,No normalized efficiency values reported.,0.3,N/R,No average payoffs by condition reported.,0.2,USA,Same subject pool: private university in the Northeast United States.,0.9,N/R,Undergraduate sample; no age statistics.,0.6,"83 women, 37 men","Overall Study 1 gender composition; participants randomly assigned across conditions, no separate breakdown provided.",0.8,Undergraduate students,Participants described as undergraduate volunteers.,1,On site,Same lab setting as conflict condition.,1,Three-person prisoner’s dilemmas within each team; other team’s behavior is visible but payoff-irrelevant (m_b set to constant). Punishment mechanics and leadership vote identical to conflict condition.,Summarizes control setting’s main structural differences while keeping sanction and leadership mechanisms.,1
10.1177_0190272512442397.md,"Study 2 – Conflict, Continuous payoff (IPD), competitive outgroup",Study 2 uses a 2 (conflict) × 2 (continuous/binary) × 2 (competitive/noncompetitive outgroup) design and simulated partners. This row is the conflict × continuous (IPD) × competitive-outgroup condition.,1,"Conflict present (IPD payoff table), continuous production function; outgroup competitiveness high (competitive block: 2–3 outgroup contributors each round with equal probability).",Conflict vs no conflict and continuous vs binary are between-subjects; outgroup competitiveness is within-subjects (15-round block each). This row represents the subset of rounds in which subjects in the conflict/continuous treatment face the competitive outgroup.,1,True,Human undergraduates made decisions in z-Tree sessions.,1,True,Fully randomized factorial design with controlled simulated partners.,1,True,Participants came to the lab; experiment run with z-Tree.,1,False,Participants are humans; only their partners are simulated.,1,False,No separate analytical model.,1,6,Six-person intergroup setting: two 3-person groups (as stated for Study 2).,1,15,Effectively this condition corresponds to one 15-round block (competitive outgroup) within the 30-round session.,1,0,"Participants decide whether to invest (binary choice); payoff tables shown only for invest vs not invest, no partial investment allowed.",1,0,Endowment is initially under participant control; they choose to invest or keep.,1,N/R,"Continuous IPD payoffs in appendix show fixed point returns based on relative numbers of contributors, not a simple linear MPCR per contributed unit.",0.5,0,No communication or chat is described; only screen feedback.,0.9,1,"As in Study 1, feedback includes outcome information; though Study 2 description is briefer, design is closely modeled on Study 1 and uses payoff tables plus feedback.",0.7,0,Punishment present but no indication that identities are revealed; only behavior and payoff info.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism in Study 2.,1,1,"Participants told they will interact for 30 rounds divided in two 15-round blocks; standard z-Tree practice also shows remaining rounds, though not explicitly stated.",0.6,1,Study 2 used the same norm-enforcement mechanism as Study 1: invest in a punishment fund to sanction noncontributors.,1,1,Structure is unchanged: punishment account consists of points that can be invested and then are not kept by the punisher.,0.9,N/R,"Study 2 refers back to Study 1 mechanism but does not specify exact per-unit loss function again; by analogy, losses are some function of total fund, not a fixed coefficient.",0.4,0,Study 2 mentions only punishment as norm enforcement.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Appendix gives point earnings per round contingent on invest/not invest but does not clearly state a numerical endowment per round like Study 1’s 10+10.,0.3,0.75,"Across all rounds in conflict vs no conflict, mean contribution rates were 75% vs 67%. Within the conflict condition, contribution did not vary significantly by outgroup competitiveness or production function; use 0.75 as approximate rate.",0.7,N/R,Per-round endowment for contribution is not numerically specified; cannot convert rate into token amount confidently.,0.2,N/R,No normalized efficiency reported.,0.2,N/R,Only behavioral frequencies given; no payoffs by condition.,0.2,USA,Participants were undergraduates at a large state university in the Midwest United States.,0.9,N/R,"Described as undergraduate volunteers, but no age statistics.",0.6,"91 women, 53 men (before exclusions)",Study 2 participant section reports 91 women and 53 men among 144 original volunteers.,0.9,Undergraduate students,Explicitly stated that participants were undergraduate volunteers.,1,On site,Run in a physical lab with z-Tree; confederates used if enrollment was low.,1,"Continuous intergroup prisoner’s dilemma using payoff table (top panel of appendix). Each round players choose invest vs not invest. Competitive outgroup: 2 or 3 outgroup contributors per round with equal probability. One simulated teammate contributes and enforces 70% of the time; the other contributes 30% and never enforces. Punishment decision and contribution decision each round; 30 rounds in total, divided into two 15-round blocks with different outgroup behavior.","Captures the specific payoff structure (IPD), outgroup behavior, and fixed teammate strategies that define incentives.",1
10.1177_0190272512442397.md,"Study 2 – Conflict, Continuous payoff (IPD), noncompetitive outgroup",Same treatment as above (conflict × continuous) but with low outgroup competitiveness block (0–1 outgroup contributors).,1,"Conflict present (IPD), continuous production function; outgroup competitiveness low (noncompetitive block: 0–1 outgroup contributors each round with equal probability).",Within-subjects manipulation of outgroup competitiveness; this row isolates the noncompetitive outgroup block under the conflict/continuous environment.,1,True,Same participants and lab environment as other Study 2 conditions.,1,True,Outgroup competitiveness and block order experimentally randomized.,1,True,On-site lab using z-Tree.,1,False,Human subjects with simulated partners.,1,False,No analytical-only component.,1,6,"Same 6-player, 2-group structure.",1,15,Represents the 15-round noncompetitive block.,1,0,Binary invest vs keep decision.,1,0,Endowment starts privately; invest decision moves it to group.,1,N/R,Same continuous IPD payoff structure; no MPCR parameter provided.,0.5,0,No communication tool mentioned.,0.9,1,Feedback on payoffs and group behavior each round implied by the payoff-based design mirroring Study 1.,0.7,0,No indication that individual punisher identities are shown.,0.8,0,No rewarding institution.,1,1,Participants informed of 30 total rounds and mid-way team reassignment.,0.6,1,Same norm-enforcement mechanism as competitive block.,1,1,Punishment fund contributions cost the punisher points they do not keep.,0.9,N/R,Exact mapping from contributed punishment points to losses not numerically specified for Study 2.,0.4,0,Only punishment available.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Study 2 does not explicitly state numerical endowment per round, only outcome tables.",0.3,0.75,Contribution did not significantly depend on outgroup competitiveness; authors report 75% in conflict condition overall.,0.7,N/R,No explicit endowment amount; cannot convert rate to tokens.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency statistic reported.,0.2,N/R,No payoff summaries by condition.,0.2,USA,Same Midwest U.S. undergraduate pool.,0.9,N/R,"Not given, only ‘undergraduate volunteers’.",0.6,"91 women, 53 men (before exclusions)",Same subject pool as other Study 2 conditions.,0.9,Undergraduate students,Undergraduates at a state university.,1,On site,Same lab implementation.,1,"Continuous IPD payoff table; here, noncompetitive outgroup has 0 or 1 contributor per round (each with 50% probability). Simulated teammates: one high contributor/punisher (70%), one low contributor/non-punisher (30%). Norm enforcement and contribution measured each round.",Captures differences in outgroup behavior relative to competitive condition while holding other structures fixed.,1
10.1177_0190272512442397.md,"Study 2 – Conflict, Binary payoff (IPG), competitive outgroup","Conflict present plus binary ‘intergroup public goods game’ (all-or-nothing step-level), with competitive outgroup block.",1,"Conflict present (intergroup public goods game), binary step-level production function; outgroup competitiveness high (2–3 outgroup investors each round).",IPG condition is the ‘binary’ production function; conflict/no conflict is group-level payoff dependence; competitive outgroup has high contributions.,1,True,Same lab experiment with undergraduates.,1,True,Part of randomized 2×2×2 design.,1,True,Conducted using z-Tree in a physical lab.,1,False,"Only partners are simulated, not the decision-makers.",1,False,No closed-form theoretical analysis only.,1,6,Two teams of three.,1,15,Refers to 15-round block with competitive outgroup.,1,0,"Decision each round is to invest or not invest; although payoffs are step-level, contribution is still binary, not continuous.",1,0,Tokens are under private control until invested.,1,N/R,Intergroup public goods game is a threshold (step-level) setting; no marginal per capita return parameter specified.,0.4,0,No mention of communication.,0.9,1,Payoff tables and outcome info are displayed; behavior across teams must be visible for threshold comparison.,0.7,0,Enforcement present but no ID revelation described.,0.8,0,No reward institution in any Study 2 condition.,1,1,30-round total with defined mid-point; likely known to participants.,0.6,1,Same punishing norm-enforcement choice on each round.,1,1,"Based on Study 1 mechanism, contributing to punishment pool forfeits those points as earnings.",0.8,N/R,Exact loss magnitude per invested punishment point is not specified for Study 2 IPG condition.,0.3,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards available.,1,N/A,No rewards available.,1,N/R,Payoff table shows payoffs for invest/not invest but does not specify a numeric initial endowment.,0.3,0.75,Authors report no significant effect of production function on contribution; overall contribution in conflict = 75%.,0.7,N/R,Endowment per round not explicitly stated for Study 2.,0.2,N/R,No report of realized vs potential maximum payoff ratios.,0.2,N/R,No payoff aggregates by condition provided.,0.2,USA,Same Midwest U.S. undergraduate pool.,0.9,N/R,Age not reported.,0.6,"91 women, 53 men (before exclusions)",Overall Study 2 composition; similar across conditions.,0.9,Undergraduate students,All participants were undergraduates.,1,On site,Same z-Tree lab sessions.,1,"Intergroup public goods game (binary, step-level). Each round, total ingroup contributions compared to outgroup’s contributions: if ingroup exceeds outgroup, each member earns 30 points; if tied, 15; if lower, 0. Competitive outgroup contributes 2 or 3 each round. Punishment and contribution decisions each round; simulated teammates have fixed contribution/punishment propensities.",Summarizes key structural features distinguishing binary conflict and high outgroup competitiveness.,1
