10.1111_j.1467-2979.2010.00372.x.md,Milinski et al. 2002b – Pure public goods game (no alternation),The paper quotes Milinski et al. (2002b) and reports cooperation over eight rounds in the pure public goods game condition without indirect reciprocity.,1,Game structure; condition: pure public goods game without alternating indirect reciprocity rounds,Only difference described is whether public goods rounds are alternated with indirect reciprocity rounds.,0.94,True,Human subjects (six per group) are explicitly described.,1,True,"Controlled laboratory game with assigned roles, payoffs, and repeated rounds.",1,True,Described as a laboratory-style public goods experiment with students; no field setting indicated.,0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; only human play.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model; behavioral experiment instead.,1,6,"“The six subjects of each group could see a public screen…” and “For the public goods game, all six players were asked simultaneously…”.",1,8,The paper reports cooperation change “over eight rounds” for the pure public goods game.,0.9,0,Players choose whether to contribute DM 2.50 or 0; binary decision.,1,0,Standard PGG: each has a private account and chooses to contribute; initial endowment is entirely private.,0.9,0.5,"Each of 6 can contribute 2.50; sum is doubled and redistributed. Return per 1 DM contributed: group gain = 2 DM; divided by 6 gives 1/3 to each player. Own marginal return = 1/3 DM per 1 DM, so MPCR = 1/3 ≈ 0.33. But the standard definition here is marginal per capita return to the group: 2/6=0.333. However schema seems to intend individual MPCR; still, the text example in basic game used 0.5 for own return with 4 players and doubling. To stay consistent with formal definition, set 0.33.",0.5,0,Decisions are made “without discussion” and via public screen; no communication mentioned.,0.95,1,"After decisions, each player’s decision, contribution, and gain were displayed below pseudonames for 20 s.