en choosing to help.,1,4,Each DM 2.50 spent yields DM 4 to receiver; per 1 DM cost gives 4/2.5=1.6 DM to receiver. Using total reward amount (4) as ‘tech’ per action as per description.,0.7,20,Same starting account of DM 20 per subject is used for both games in the session.,1,N/R,Paper gives probabilities of cooperation (around/above 84%) qualitatively but no exact average fraction or per-round values.,0.3,2.5,Contribution amount per cooperative choice in PGG remains DM 2.50.,1,N/R,Only relative payoff (1.45 times higher than pure PGG) is reported; no absolute efficiency ratio.,0.2,N/R,Average individual payoff is reported only relatively; total group payoff is not specified.,0.2,N/R,Country of lab not stated in this summary.,0.2,N/R,Subjects are not characterized demographically in this text.,0.3,N/R,No gender information provided.,0.01,N/R,"Referred to generically as student volunteers elsewhere, but not explicitly for this specific experiment in this paper.",0.3,On site,Physical co-location with a public screen in a lab-like environment.,0.9,"Within each round of this condition, players first play a public goods game (6 players, contribute DM 2.50 or 0; total contributions doubled and evenly redistributed), and in alternating rounds they also play an indirect reciprocity game where designated donors decide whether to pay DM 2.50 to grant DM 4 to a receiver, with all such decisions publicly recorded under pseudonyms; reputation from both games is observable and influences subsequent decisions.",Directly summarizes the alternation protocol and payoff rules described.,1
