10.1111_j.1467-2979.2010.00372.x.md,Rockenbach & Milinski 2006 – Punishment + indirect reciprocity environment,"Condition where subjects could choose an environment with costly punishment plus reputation-building via indirect reciprocity, which most preferred and which produced high cooperation and payoff.",0.9,Institutional environment; condition: public goods game with costly punishment option plus subsequent indirect reciprocity game (reputation building),The key manipulation is availability of costly punishment in addition to reputation from indirect reciprocity; contrasted with a punishment-only control.,0.95,True,Human subjects in a lab game.,0.95,True,Experimental manipulation of institutional environment (punishment with/without reputation).,0.95,True,"Described as an experimental design with 20 rounds and controlled payoffs, typical of lab public goods studies.",0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,"Behavioral experiment, not a purely mathematical model.",1,N/R,Group size in Rockenbach & Milinski 2006 is not given in this text.,0.1,20,"“In the experiment, 20 rounds were played that each consisted of a public goods game, followed by the option of costly punishment … followed by an indirect reciprocity game.”",1,0,Public good experiments by these authors typically allow continuous contributions; no binary restriction mentioned.,0.4,0,Standard PGG design: subjects decide contributions from private endowment.,0.5,N/R,Multiplier and group size for this study not restated here.,0.1,0,No communication or discussion between rounds is mentioned.,0.7,1,Reputation building and targeted punishment require that contributions and perhaps punishments are observable to group members.,0.8,1,Participants effectively punish specific free-riders; the text notes free-riders were punished heavily; implies that who punished whom is known within group.,0.7,1,"Indirect reciprocity game follows; donation decisions there contribute to reputation, so id