10.1093_restud_rds016.md,Analytical model of repeated public good provision with network monitoring,The paper presents only a formal theoretical/model-based analysis of repeated games with network monitoring; no separate empirical experiments or simulations are described.,1,"Network monitoring structure (random matching, fixed network, equal monitoring vs unequal; group size n; type of public good: pure vs divisible; discount factor δ; benefit functions f and weights α_{i,j})","Across the analysis, the main comparative statics vary network structure, group size, type of public good, and discount factor to study effects on maximum sustainable cooperation.",0.94,False,"No human subjects, data collection, or experiments are reported; the work is purely theoretical.",1,False,There is no controlled experiment; all results come from mathematical analysis of game-theoretic models.,1,False,No laboratory setting or in-person/online experimental sessions with participants are conducted.,1,False,The paper does not describe numerical or agent-based simulations; all results are analytical proofs.,0.98,True,"The study is entirely analytical, deriving equilibrium conditions and comparative statics in repeated games with network monitoring.",1,0,"No concrete numeric player count is fixed; the model uses a generic set N={1,…,n} and studies comparative statics in n.",1,0,"The game is infinitely repeated over periods t∈{0,1,…}; no finite number of rounds is specified.",1,1,"Actions x_i are any nonnegative real number (x_i∈ℝ_+), so contributions are continuous, not all-or-nothing binary.",1,N/A,"There is no endowment-allocation or default contribution mechanism; players directly choose costly contribution levels in a utility function, and there are no human subjects.",1,N/R,"A standard linear public goods MPCR is never defined; benefits are given by general concave functions f_{i,j}(x_j) rather than a linear multiplier.",1,N/A,The model has no communication between human subjects; players are theoretical agents in a game-theoretic model.,1,0,"Players observe certain neighbors’ actions (through the monitoring network) but there is no notion of displayed summaries of others’ earnings or punishments, which is a lab-interface concept.",0.9,0,There is no explicit punishment stage or identity revelation of punishers; punishments are implicit via grim-trigger strategy prescriptions.,1,0,The model contains no explicit rewarding mechanism or identity disclosure of rewarders.,1,1,The horizon and discount factor δ are common knowledge in the model; players know that the game is infinitely repeated with discounting.,0.9,1,"Punishment is implemented via grim trigger strategies, where players stop cooperating after observing deviations; there is no separate punishment action but a punishment mechanism exists in equilibrium strategies.",0.95,N/A,There is no separate costly punishment decision variable; punishment is encoded as future non-cooperation rather than a token-based cost per punishment unit.,1,N/A,The model does not define a per-unit punishment impact technology like ‘each token reduces target’s payoff by k’; punishments occur via strategy-induced cooperation breakdown.,1,0,The author explicitly emphasizes that robust cooperation is maximized through punishments and not rewards; no reward technology is modelled.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards exist in the model.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards exist in the model.,1,N/R,"Players do not receive a per-period fixed endowment in tokens; instead they choose effort x_i with cost x_i directly in utility, so no separate endowment is reported.",1,N/R,The paper derives theoretical equilibrium cooperation levels x_i* but does not report normalized contribution rates relative to a finite endowment.,0.95,N/R,Cooperation levels x_i* are solved symbolically as functions of parameters; no specific numeric contribution amounts are reported as empirical outcomes.,0.9,N/R,"Although welfare comparisons to the first-best are discussed, no single numeric efficiency index (ratio of achieved to maximal payoff) is reported as a dependent variable.",0.9,N/R,Group payoffs are characterized analytically but not presented as numeric outcome measures from data or simulation runs.,0.9,N/A,There are no human participants; it is a theoretical model.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical with no empirical or experimental implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated n-player contribution game with continuous action space x_i≥0, discount factor δ∈(0,1), and payoffs u_i(x)=Σ_{j≠i} f_{i,j}(x_j)−x_i. Monitoring is via a stochastic or fixed network L_t where players observe neighbors’ actions perfectly and others not at all; higher-order information about who observed whom may be perfect or imperfect. Key equilibrium concept is weak PBE, and main result is characterization of robust maximum cooperation x_i* sustained by grim-trigger strategies under various monitoring structures (equal monitoring, random matching, fixed networks with centrality).",Summarized core structural and informational assumptions of the theoretical game described throughout Sections 2–5.,1
