10.1111_j.1468-0351.2005.00228.x.md,Relative incentives scheme (baseline cooperative environment),Panel field study contrasts worker behavior under relative incentives vs piece rates; treat each incentive regime as a separate condition.,0.95,Incentive scheme = Relative incentives; group size; group nationality composition; heterogeneity in ability (cost of effort); share of interns (stakes).,These are the main variables whose variation is analyzed to infer cooperation; here the regime is the relative incentive scheme condition.,0.9,True,Uses personnel records of 142 human workers on a UK farm.,1,False,Naturalistic change in pay scheme and naturally varying groups; no randomized controlled manipulation by researchers.,0.9,False,"Study occurs in a real workplace, not a controlled laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations are reported.,1,False,"While a theoretical compensation formula is given, the main results are empirical, not closed-form analytical modeling.",0.9,41.1,Table 1 reports average group size 41.1 (SD 28.8) under relative incentives; use mean as representative player count.,0.8,108,Data cover 108 days; in this field context each day-field is analogous to one round.,0.7,1,"Workers choose continuous effort/productivity levels, not binary all-or-nothing actions.",0.9,0,"Not a standard contribution game; there is no endowment-to-public-account structure, so private output fully accrues to the worker absent externality; set 0 by analogy.",0.3,N/R,No public good multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; payoff is linear in own output via β.,0,1,Workers live and work together and can talk freely; no restrictions on communication are mentioned.,0.9,1,Workers can infer others’ productivity via field work and know that β depends on average productivity; however explicit numerical summaries are not mentioned. Assume de facto observable co-worker performance.,0.4,1,"Punishments/transfers are informal and social; any sanction must be personally directed, implying knowledge of identities.",0.7,1,"As with punishments, any rewards/transfers are interpersonal and thus identity-linked.",0.7,1,"Harvest season has known approximate duration (mid-May to end of August); workers plausibly know season length, though not explicitly stated.",0.4,1,Text notes many mechanisms to make transfers or hand out punishments to others among co-workers.,0.9,N/R,Punishments are informal/social; no per-unit cost in tokens or money is specified.,0,N/R,No quantified mapping from punishment action to payoff reduction is provided.,0,1,Workers can make transfers to others; described as one of the mechanisms within social ties.,0.7,N/R,Transfers/rewards are informal; no cost technology per unit is given.,0,N/R,No explicit payoff mapping from reward actions to others’ earnings.,0,N/R,Workers choose effort; no fixed token or monetary endowment per round is defined.,0,N/R,Outcome is productivity (kg/hr). No normalized contribution proportion relative to an endowment is reported.,0,5.01,Use worker productivity (kg/hr) as the key action; Table 1: 5.01 kg/hr average under relative incentives.,0.95,N/R,No direct comparison to maximum possible group payoff without externalities is computed.,0,N/R,Total group earnings not reported; only individual productivity levels and wage schedule form are given.,0,Multiple Eastern and Central European countries working in the UK,"Workers are university students from eight Eastern/Central European countries (Poland, Bulgaria, Russia, Belarus, Ukraine, Slovakia, Baltic Republics, Romania, China) employed on a UK farm.",0.95,19–25,Eligibility requirement: full-time university students aged between 19 and 25.,1,N/R,Gender composition of workers is not reported.,0,Full-time university students (at least one year before graduation),Eligibility requires being full-time university students returning to same university with >=1 year before graduation.,1,Field experiment,"Real workplace setting with an exogenous change in incentive scheme mid-season; typically classified as a field experiment per Harrison & List (2004), which they cite.",0.9,"Relative incentives: Pay = β * kilograms picked; β = (w̄)/(average productivity on field-day). Individual effort increases average productivity, reducing β and co-workers’ pay, creating a negative externality and a collective action problem where the social optimum is minimum effort.",Formulas (1) and (2) and accompanying explanation specify the payoff structure and externality under the relative scheme.,1
10.1111_j.1468-0351.2005.00228.x.md,Piece rates scheme (no cooperative incentives),Same workers later paid under exogenous piece rates; used as comparison condition to identify cooperative effects of group characteristics.,0.95,Incentive scheme = Piece rates; group size; group nationality composition; heterogeneity in ability (cost of effort); share of interns (stakes).,Main contrast is incentive scheme; other group characteristics still vary but should not affect behavior via cooperation under piece rates.,0.9,True,Same human worker panel data from the UK farm under a different pay regime.,1,False,"Change to piece rates is implemented by management, not as a randomized experiment by researchers.",0.9,False,Conducted in the field workplace.,1,False,No computer simulations are used.,1,False,Empirical estimation using observed productivity; formulas specify pay but not a full analytical model.,0.9,38.1,Table 1 reports average group size 38.1 (SD 18.2) under piece rates.,0.8,108,Same harvest period; the change in incentive scheme occurs mid-season but overall observation window is 108 days.,0.7,1,Workers still choose continuous effort/productivity; no binary contribution constraint.,0.9,0,"As in the relative scheme, there is no defined public endowment; effort fully benefits self in monetary terms, absent externality.",0.3,N/R,No public good multiplier is relevant under this pay scheme either.,0,1,Workplace and living arrangements unchanged; communication unrestricted.,0.9,1,"As before, workers can observe coworkers’ behavior; no explicit numerical summaries reported.",0.4,1,Same informal social punishment possibilities in the workplace.,0.7,1,Same informal reward/transfer mechanisms as under relative incentives.,0.7,1,Workers face same seasonal work horizon; knowledge of approximate season length unchanged.,0.4,1,"Same social environment; workers can still punish or reward, though there is no formal incentive to cooperate.",0.9,N/R,No quantitative cost structure is provided for social punishments.,0,N/R,No explicit mapping from social punishments to payoffs.,0,1,Transfers/rewards remain possible in same living/working setting.,0.7,N/R,No quantitative technology for rewards is provided.,0,N/R,No explicit payoff mapping for rewards.,0,N/R,No per-round token endowment is defined; workers exert effort with continuous output.,0,N/R,No normalized contribution fraction is computed or reported.,0,7.98,Use worker productivity (kg/hr) as behavioral measure; Table 1: 7.98 kg/hr average under piece rates.,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric vs maximum possible payoff is reported.,0,N/R,"Group earnings are not directly reported, only individual productivity and wage formula.",0,Multiple Eastern and Central European countries working in the UK,Same worker population as under relative incentives.,0.95,19–25,Same eligibility conditions as for the relative incentive phase.,1,N/R,Gender composition is never reported.,0,Full-time university students (at least one year before graduation),Same population: university students with specified requirements.,1,Field experiment,Same field context with exogenous institutional change mid-season; commonly treated as field experiment.,0.9,"Piece rates: Pay = β * kilograms picked; β is set exogenously at start of each day based on supervisor’s expected productivity and is not revised. There is no dependence of β on realized average productivity, so individual output does not impose a payoff externality on co-workers.","Section 4.1 describes replacement of relative incentives with standard piece rates, where β no longer depends on group average productivity.",1
