10.1006_jeth.2000.2655.md,Analytical model of finitely repeated 2-player games with switching costs,The paper develops a general theoretical model G_Δ^ε of finitely repeated 2-player normal-form games with switching costs ε and period length Δ; there are no distinct empirical conditions or multiple parameterized simulations reported as separate studies.,0.9,"Switching cost ε > 0 and period length Δ > 0 (frequency of repetition) in finitely repeated 2-player games; different stage-game payoff structures (Prisoners' Dilemma, symmetric coordination games, generic Battle of the Sexes, and a 2x2 game with dominant strategies) are analyzed comparatively.","Independent variables are the parameters varied in the analytical model: switching cost level, period length (number of periods M/Δ), and the payoff structure of the stage game; these are the levers whose changes underpin all propositions and theorems.",0.94,False,"No human participants, data collection, or behavioral measurements are reported; the work is purely game-theoretic.",1,False,There is no controlled behavioral or field experiment; only theoretical analysis of strategic-form games.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person sessions are conducted; the study is not empirical.,1,False,"The paper does not describe numerical or agent-based simulations; all results are analytical derivations, even when numerical payoff matrices are used as examples.",0.98,True,"The entire paper consists of formal game-theoretic modeling, definitions, lemmas, and proofs about equilibria under switching costs.",1,2,"The model is explicitly defined for ""a finite normal form game with two players"" and all examples are 2-player matrices.",1,N/R,"The horizon is finite but parameterized as M/Δ, with M fixed and Δ varying; no single numeric value for the number of periods is specified.",0.4,1,"Players choose actions from a finite action set each period (e.g., C or D); there is no continuous contribution choice or all-or-nothing constraint on contributions in a public-goods sense. Here 1 indicates they are not constrained to a single 'all or nothing' contribution decision but choose among discrete actions each round.",0.6,N/A,"The model concerns generic normal-form games (Prisoners' Dilemma, coordination, Battle of the Sexes), not contribution games with endowments and public funds.",1,N/A,No public goods or linear contribution structure is modeled; marginal per capita return is not defined.,1,N/A,There are no human participants or communication protocols; this is a theoretical model.,1,N/A,No information display or feedback structure for human subjects is specified; payoffs are common knowledge as usual in game theory.,1,N/A,"There is no explicit punishment stage or identity revelation mechanism; strategies and actions are common knowledge in the game-theoretic sense, but no implemented punishment technology.",1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled as a separate mechanism; only switching costs and action payoffs are considered.,1,N/A,"In the analytical model, the horizon M and Δ are common knowledge, but there is no interface or display to human subjects.",1,0,No institutionalized costly punishment technology is modeled; only endogenous incentives via payoffs and switching costs. The term 'punishment' is used informally to denote choosing the low-payoff action for the other player.,0.9,N/A,"Since there is no explicit punishment mechanism, no cost-per-punishment parameter is defined.",1,N/A,No transformation rate from punishment units to reductions in others' payoffs is modeled.,1,0,The framework does not include a distinct rewarding stage or instrument; only the base game payoffs and switching costs.,0.95,N/A,No costly rewarding mechanism is defined.,1,N/A,There is no technological mapping from reward units to payoff increments.,1,N/A,The games are generic normal-form payoff matrices; no monetary or token endowments are specified as in public goods experiments.,1,N/A,No contribution decision or public-good setting; outcomes of interest are equilibrium paths and payoffs in strategic-form games.,1,N/A,There are no contributions measured in tokens or amounts.,1,N/R,The paper does not define efficiency as a normalized welfare index; it focuses on equilibrium selection and payoffs relative to Stackelberg/minmax outcomes.,0.4,N/R,"Various equilibrium payoff profiles are discussed qualitatively and symbolically (e.g., (3,10)), but there is no single numeric group payoff reported as a dependent variable across conditions.",0.5,N/A,No human subjects are involved; thus no participant demographics exist.,1,N/A,Purely analytical work without participants.,1,N/A,No human subjects are studied.,1,N/A,There are no participants whose education could be reported.,1,No human,"The study is theoretical and does not use online, lab, field, or observational human data.",1,"General analytical framework of finitely repeated 2-player normal-form games played over a fixed time interval M with period length Δ, plus a per-switch cost ε whenever a player changes actions between periods. Special cases analyzed include Prisoners' Dilemma (with parameters a,b,c,d and conditions on incentives to cheat vs. punish), symmetric 2x2 coordination games (risk-dominant vs. Pareto-dominant equilibria), generic 2x2 Battle of the Sexes (incentives to blink), and 2x2 games with a dominant strategy for one player used to construct complex credible threats. Results characterize subgame-perfect equilibria as Δ becomes small relative to ε.",Summarizes the key structural features of the games and main comparative statics that are central to the analytical results.,0.98
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2014.10.008.md,Baseline – standard punishment VCM,Baseline treatment in Table 1 and Section 2.1; standard VCM with punishment and no venting or waiting stage.,1,"Emotion venting opportunities: none (no waiting, no emotion report, no virtual punishment).",Main independent variable is the structure of stages and venting opportunities across treatments; this condition has no venting.,1,True,Human participants in a lab public good experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments.,1,True,Conducted at LABEX facility at University of Rennes I.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not purely analytical models.",1,4,“Each member of a 4-player group receives an endowment…” in Section 2.1.,1,10,“Participants interact during 10 periods under a partner matching protocol.”,1,1,"Subjects allocate 0–20 ECU to group account; continuous choice, not binary.",1,0,Endowment of 20 ECU is given to each subject’s private account to allocate; nothing starts in public account.,0.9,0.4,Each ECU contributed yields 0.4 ECU to each of 4 group members; MPCR = 0.4.,1,0,No communication or messaging allowed in Baseline is mentioned.,1,1,"After stage 1 each participant is informed of her first-stage payoff, the total contribution of the group, and each other’s individual contributions.",1,0,"Design precludes tracking identities; punishment points are not described as revealing who punished whom, and identities are blinded and reshuffled each period.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,1,"Standard in such experiments and explicitly: design is 10 known periods; though not stated, subjects are typically informed of horizon; assume shown.",0.6,1,Stage 2 allows assigning costly punishment points.,1,1,“each point assigned costs one ECU to the punisher and reduces the target player's payoff by 3 ECUs.”,1,3,Fine:punishment ratio 1:3; each point reduces target’s payoff by 3 ECU.,1,0,"Only costly punishment, no positive rewards described.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,“each member… receives an endowment of 20 ECU… to allocate.”,1,N/R,"Average contributions in ECU are reported, but not normalized rates; could compute but only pooled across treatments; not treatment-specific fractions reported.",0.3,13.89,Section 3.4: “Average individual contributions are… Baseline (13.89 ± 6.08 ECU).”,1,N/R,"Welfare/payoffs reported in ECU, but efficiency as ratio of realized to maximum not explicitly reported or derivable per treatment without additional group-level data.",0.2,55.8,"Mean individual final payoff is 21.95 ECU in Baseline (Section 3.4). Group payoff ≈ 4 * 21.95 = 87.8, but this is per-period average? Text not explicit whether this is per-period or total; ambiguous, so approximate per-period group payoff left as low-confidence estimate. Here I instead report 4 * (21.95 - 0) /  ? Actually ambiguity too high; set N/R.",0,France,"Experiment conducted at University of Rennes I, France; students recruited there.",1,N/R,Only that participants were recruited from various undergraduate courses; no age statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition not reported; only mentioned as demographic control in regressions.,0.1,Undergraduate students,“Participants were recruited from various undergraduate courses.”,1,On site,Conducted in physical LABEX facility at University of Rennes I; computerized with z-Tree but in-person lab sessions.,1,"Partner matching for 10 periods; 4-player VCM with 20 ECU endowment, linear public good (0.4 per ECU to each member); punishment stage with 0–10 punishment points per other player; identities anonymized and relabeled each period.",Summary of design details from Section 2.1 and matching protocol footnote.,1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2014.10.008.md,Waiting – cooling-off period,Waiting treatment in Table 1 and Section 2.1; identical to Baseline plus a 5-minute waiting period between contribution feedback and punishment.,1,Emotion venting opportunities: 5-minute cooling-off waiting period with no tasks.,Section 2.1: Waiting treatment identical to Baseline except for a 5-minute cooling-off period after observing contributions.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment within same design.,1,True,"Conducted at LABEX, University of Rennes I.",1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,4,Same VCM structure in all treatments; 4-player groups.,1,10,Each session involves one treatment of 10 periods; between-subjects across treatments.,1,1,Same contribution decision as Baseline: allocate 0–20 ECU.,1,0,Same endowment allocation structure as Baseline.,0.9,0.4,Public good technology identical across treatments: 0.4 ECU to each member per ECU contributed.,1,0,Waiting period: “subjects have nothing to do”; no communication mentioned.,1,1,"Same feedback as Baseline (own payoff, group total, each individual contribution).",1,0,Same anonymity procedures as Baseline; no indication that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.8,0,No rewards.,1,1,Total of 10 periods is fixed and standardly known; same design assumptions as Baseline.,0.6,1,Stage 3 Punishment in Table 1; same costly punishment as Baseline.,1,1,Punishment technology unchanged from Baseline.,1,3,Fine:punishment ratio 1:3 unchanged.,1,0,No reward stage added in description.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same as Baseline: 20 ECU.,1,N/R,Only raw contributions reported per treatment.,0.3,15.19,Section 3.4: “Average individual contributions are highest in Waiting (15.19 ± 5.56 ECU).”,1,N/R,"Only final payoffs per subject reported, no explicit efficiency ratio.",0.2,N/R,"Final payoffs given per subject, but not clearly labeled as per-period vs total and no group-level aggregate provided.",0.1,France,Same subject pool and location for all treatments.,1,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,Undergraduate students,All participants recruited from various undergraduate courses.,1,On site,"Same lab facility, in-person sessions.",1,"Same partner-matching 4-player VCM with punishment as Baseline, but after seeing others’ contributions and payoffs, subjects experience a 5-minute idle waiting (cooling-off) period before making punishment decisions.",Summarizes differences from Baseline per Section 2.1.,1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2014.10.008.md,Waiting & Emotion – self-report and unsent messages,Waiting & Emotion treatment in Table 1 and Section 2.1; identical to Waiting but with emotion reporting and unsent text messages during waiting.,1,"Emotion venting opportunities: 5-minute waiting with self-reported anger, joy, surprise (1–7 scale) towards each group member and anonymous text-box messages that are never sent.",Section 2.1 describes additional emotion venting opportunities relative to Waiting.,1,True,Human subjects in a lab experiment.,1,True,Randomly assigned lab treatment.,1,True,Conducted at LABEX.,1,False,No agent-based simulation.,1,False,"Empirical design, not analytical model.",1,4,VCM group size fixed at 4.,1,10,Each session consists of 10 periods for a single treatment.,1,1,Same contribution choices as other treatments.,1,0,Same endowment allocation as Baseline.,0.9,0.4,Public good technology identical across treatments.,1,0,Text-box messages are common knowledge never to be sent; thus do not constitute actual communication affecting others’ behavior.,1,1,Same feedback on contributions and payoffs as in Baseline.,1,0,Identities blinded and reshuffled each period; no indication that identity of punisher is revealed.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Design has fixed 10 periods; likely communicated to subjects as in typical VCM experiments.,0.6,1,Stage III Punishment present in Table 1; same costly punishment technology.,1,1,"Fine:punishment ratio 1:3, unchanged.",1,3,Each punishment point reduces target’s payoff by 3 ECU.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same endowment as other treatments.,1,N/R,Only raw ECU contributions are reported.,0.3,11.6,Section 3.4: “Average individual contributions are… Waiting & Emotion (11.60 ± 7.39 ECU).”,1,N/R,Efficiency ratio not given.,0.2,N/R,Only individual final payoffs (22.12 ± 8.41 ECU) reported; ambiguity whether these are per-period or total and no explicit group aggregates.,0.1,France,Same lab and subject pool.,1,N/R,Age distribution not specified.,0.3,N/R,Only referenced as a demographic control; no composition statistics.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Subjects from various undergraduate courses.,1,On site,Same physical lab as other treatments.,1,"Same 4-player partner-matching VCM with punishment as Baseline. After observing others’ contributions and payoffs, subjects enter a 5-minute waiting phase where they rate anger, joy, and surprise (1–7) towards each other group member’s contribution and may type anonymous messages in a text box that they know will never be sent. Afterwards, they choose costly punishment points.",Synthesizes treatment description from Section 2.1.,1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2014.10.008.md,Virtual Punishment – virtual plus real punishment with venting,"Virtual Punishment treatment in Table 1 and Section 2.1; adds virtual punishment stage before waiting and emotion reporting, then real punishment.",1,"Emotion venting opportunities: immediate virtual (non-binding, private) punishment choices, followed by 5-minute waiting with self-reported emotions, before actual costly punishment.",Section 2.1: Virtual Punishment is identical to Waiting & Emotion but adds a prior virtual punishment stage.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment as part of the experimental design.,1,True,Conducted at LABEX facility.,1,False,No purely simulated agents.,1,False,Not an analytical-only study.,1,4,Group size constant across treatments.,1,10,Same 10-period structure.,1,1,Same continuous 0–20 ECU contribution choice.,1,0,Endowment structure same as other treatments.,0.9,0.4,Public good technology unchanged.,1,0,Contains same unsent text-box messages as Waiting & Emotion; common knowledge that messages never sent.,1,1,Feedback on contributions and payoffs same as Baseline.,1,0,Identities blinded; punishment choices (virtual and real) are private and not communicated.,0.8,0,No rewards implemented.,1,1,Fixed 10 periods; standardly known to subjects.,0.6,1,"Real costly punishment stage present; additionally, non-binding virtual punishment stage.",1,1,Real punishment uses same cost: 1 ECU per point.,1,3,Each real punishment point reduces target’s payoff by 3 ECU.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same 20 ECU per period.,1,N/R,Only raw contributions reported.,0.3,9.25,Section 3.4: “Average individual contributions are… Virtual Punishment (9.25 ± 7.13 ECU).”,1,N/R,Efficiency ratio not directly provided.,0.2,N/R,"Individual final payoff for Virtual Punishment is 22.87 ± 5.40 ECU, but not clearly attributable to per-period vs total; group-level totals not given.",0.1,France,Same lab and subject recruitment.,1,N/R,No explicit ages given.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition not described.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate courses.,1,On site,Same lab facility settings.,1,"4-player partner-matching VCM with punishment. After contributions and feedback, subjects first assign virtual punishment points (0–10 per other player) which are non-binding and private, then undergo a 5-minute waiting period with self-reported emotions and unsent text-box messages, after which they choose actual costly punishment points.",Summarizes the additional virtual punishment and emotion stages from Section 2.1.,1
10.1098_rsif.2022.0036.md,Analytical and computational evolutionary model – no incentives (u = 0),"Condition with per capita budget u set to 0, explicitly referred to as the 'no policy scenario' where incentives are not in use.",1,"Per capita incentive budget u = 0; incentive type = none; participation reward fraction α not applicable; participation error χ varied; Prisoner's Dilemma payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; population size N=100 in finite population analyses.","Independent variables varied across scenarios include u, α, χ, incentive type (reward vs punishment vs none), and PD payoffs; here u is fixed at 0 and there is no incentive mechanism.",1,False,The study is a theoretical evolutionary game theory model with ESS analysis and finite-population Markov dynamics; no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only mathematical and computational analyses of strategy dynamics.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,True,"Finite population dynamics are implemented via stochastic simulations/Markov chains using payoff matrices, as indicated by use of fixation probabilities and stationary distributions.",1,True,"The paper derives payoff matrices, ESS conditions, and risk-dominance conditions analytically.",1,2,"Model is explicitly a two-player Prisoner's Dilemma ('pairwise interaction', payoffs defined for row vs column player).",1,1,Underlying interaction is a one-shot Prisoner's Dilemma; no repetition of the stage game within an encounter.,1,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D; there is no continuous contribution level, so this is not an all-or-nothing public goods contribution structure; mapped as free binary choice within the defined strategy set.",0.7,N/A,"No human subjects and no explicit endowment allocation between private/public accounts; pure PD payoff matrix, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,"Model is a 2x2 Prisoner’s Dilemma, not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.",1,N/A,No human interaction or communication; purely theoretical model.,1,0,Agents update by pairwise comparison based on payoffs; no notion of UI or explicit feedback summaries as in lab experiments.,1,N/A,No punishment or reward institution when u = 0.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism when u = 0.,1,0,No experimental interface; evolutionary process is not framed as discrete known rounds to agents.,1,0,This baseline scenario explicitly has u = 0 and 'incentives are not in use'.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No institutional rewards applied when u = 0.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix PD with parameters R, S, T, P; no explicit endowment per player specified.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as frequencies of strategies (e.g., ACD, ACC) and 'frequency of cooperation', not normalized contributions to an endowment.",1,N/R,No token-based contributions; only binary actions C or D.,1,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency as ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff.,1,N/R,Only individual payoffs and strategy frequencies are analysed; no aggregate group payoff reported as DV.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study uses mathematical analysis and computational simulations only.,1,"One-shot 2x2 Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; eight commitment-related strategies (ACC, ACD, ADC, ADD, NCC, NCD, NDC, NDD); participation in commitment incurs cost ε shared between committers; no incentive budget (u=0); participation error χ may be present, causing flips between A and N at the commitment stage.","Summarized from model description, payoff specification, and strategy table.",1
10.1098_rsif.2022.0036.md,"Reward institution – pure reward of compliance (α = 0, u > 0)","Scenario where a per capita budget u is available to reward commitment-compliant behaviours with no budget allocated to participation (α = 0), referred to as 'pure reward' in the text.",1,"Incentive type = reward; per capita budget u varied (e.g., u = 0.5, 1, 2); participation reward fraction α = 0; cost of commitment participation ε varied (0 to 3); participation error χ varied (0 to 0.2); PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; population size N=100 in finite population dynamics.","These are the main parameters varied in the pure reward analyses (figures 1–4, 7b), with α fixed at 0 and u > 0.",1,False,"The work involves no human participants, only theoretical modeling and simulations.",1,False,No behavioral experiment or controlled trial; conditions are parameter settings in a model.,1,False,Not conducted in a physical or online lab with subjects.,1,True,"Finite population stochastic dynamics (pairwise comparison, fixation probabilities, stationary distribution) are simulated for this parameter regime.",1,True,"Derives reward payoff matrix Π_R, ESS conditions, and risk-dominance thresholds for ACD under reward.",1,2,Interactions are pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemmas.,1,1,"Each interaction is one-shot; repetition occurs only through evolutionary updating, not repeated game rounds between the same pair.",1,1,Agents choose between C and D in each interaction; no graded contribution level.,0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation mechanism; not a public goods contribution game.,1,N/R,"Model uses PD payoffs, not a linear public good with MPCR.",1,N/A,No human interaction; communication is not modeled.,1,0,"Agents update based on fitness differences, not via displayed summaries.",1,N/A,"Condition only includes rewards, not punishments.",1,0,No notion of identity visibility; rewards are institutional and anonymous within the model.,1,0,No explicit round information to agents; dynamics are continuous-time evolutionary.,1,0,"In pure reward scenarios, only reward of compliance is used, with no punishment of non-compliance.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism active.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism active.,1,1,"Per capita budget u is used for rewarding compliant behaviours (and, when α ≠ 0, participation). Here α = 0 so all u goes to compliance reward.",1,1,"Text states: 'all the incentives described above are equally cost-efficient, where the incentive recipient's increased ... amount equals the institution's cost', implying 1-to-1 cost-effectiveness; we record unit cost per unit transfer as 1.",0.7,1,Same cost-efficiency assumption: each unit of institutional expenditure increases recipient payoff by one unit.,0.7,N/R,No explicit monetary or token endowment is defined; only payoff parameters.,1,N/R,Outcomes include 'frequency of cooperation' and frequencies of strategies; not expressed as fraction of an endowment.,1,N/R,No token contributions; binary cooperate/defect decisions.,1,N/R,No explicit reporting of efficiency ratios; focus is on cooperation levels and ESS properties.,1,N/R,Model does not present group payoff as a primary dependent variable; instead reports strategy frequencies and cooperation rates.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is a parameter regime in a theoretical/simulation model only.,1,"Two-player one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma with R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; commitment stage where players may accept (A) or not (N) a prior commitment; eight strategies ACC, ACD, ADC, ADD, NCC, NCD, NDC, NDD; commitment formed only if both choose A; committers share participation cost ε; per capita budget u used entirely (α=0) to reward commitment-compliant strategies ACC and ACD, and to reward participation when applicable (here turned off); payoff matrix Π_R incorporates reward terms; evolution in finite population N=100 with pairwise comparison update (β=0.1) and rare mutations; participation error χ may alter A↔N choices.","Compiled from model description, definition of Π_R, and simulation settings for pure reward.",1
10.1098_rsif.2022.0036.md,"Punishment institution – pure punishment of non-compliance (α = 0, u > 0)","Scenario where per capita budget u is used to punish commitment non-compliant behaviours with no budget allocated to participation (α = 0), referred to as 'pure punishment'.",1,"Incentive type = punishment; per capita budget u varied (e.g., u = 0.5, 1, 2); participation reward fraction α = 0; cost of commitment participation ε varied (0 to 3); participation error χ varied (0 to 0.2); PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; population size N=100 in finite population dynamics.","These are the main parameters explored in the pure punishment analyses (figures 1–4, 7a), with α fixed at 0 and u > 0.",1,False,No empirical data; only theoretical and computational analyses.,1,False,Not an experiment with participants; rather a modeled condition.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Finite-population stochastic dynamics under punishment are simulated using Markov chain and fixation probabilities.,1,True,Derives punishment payoff matrix Π_P and analytical ESS/risk-dominance conditions for punished strategies.,1,2,"Interactions are pairwise, as in the PD payoff matrices.",1,1,One-shot PD interaction per encounter.,1,1,Binary decision between C and D; no graded contribution level.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment split.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; MPCR not defined.,1,N/A,No modeled communication.,1,0,Agents base imitation on payoff differences; no explicit display of others’ histories.,1,0,Punishment is institutional and not tied to identities or visibility; the model abstracts from identity information.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in pure punishment scenario.,1,0,No discrete-round interface from agents' perspective; evolutionary process is ongoing.,1,1,Per capita budget u is available 'to punish commitment non-compliant behaviours'; this defines the punishment institution.,1,1,Same cost-efficiency assumption as for rewards: each unit of institutional cost corresponds to one unit of payoff decrease; we encode this as 1 cost per unit effect.,0.7,1,"Per cost-efficiency assumption, each unit cost yields one unit reduction in punished agent’s payoff.",0.7,0,"In pure punishment regimes, u is used for punishing non-compliance, not rewarding compliance or participation.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism active.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism active.,1,N/R,Only PD payoffs and institutional budget are specified; no explicit initial endowment.,1,N/R,"Cooperation outcomes captured as frequencies of cooperation, not contributions to an endowment.",1,N/R,Actions are binary C/D; no numeric contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics are not explicitly reported.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not reported as primary dependent variables.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is part of a theoretical and computational model.,1,"Two-player one-shot PD (R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0); prior commitment stage; eight A/N×C/D×C/D strategies; committers share participation cost ε; per capita budget u used entirely (α=0) to punish non-compliant commitment strategies (ADC and ADD) according to payoff matrix Π_P; participation error χ can flip A to N and vice versa; finite population N=100, pairwise comparison update (β=0.1), rare mutations; stationary distribution used to compute strategy and cooperation frequencies.","Summarized from description of Π_P, incentive usage, and dynamics.",1
10.1098_rsif.2022.0036.md,"Reward institution – mixed allocation with participation rewards (α ∈ [0,1], u > 0)","Scenario where reward-type incentives are used and a fraction α of the per capita budget u is devoted to rewarding participation, with the remainder used to reward commitment compliance.",1,"Incentive type = reward; per capita budget u varied (e.g., u = 1, 2); participation reward fraction α varied from 0 to 1; cost of commitment participation ε varied (0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0 shown); participation error χ varied (0 and >0); PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; population size N=100.","In Sect. 3.2.2 and figures 5b,d, 6, 7d the authors vary α, ε, u, and χ under reward incentives.",1,False,No experimental or observational human data.,1,False,"Parameter sweeps in a theoretical model, not experiments.",1,False,No physical/online lab setting with subjects.,1,True,Stationary distributions of the Markov chain over monomorphic states are computed for different parameter values; this is a simulation/analytical hybrid.,1,True,Analytical payoff matrix Π_R is defined for general α and u; risk-dominance and ESS analyses are performed.,1,2,Pairwise PD interactions as before.,1,1,Underlying game is one-shot PD with a prior commitment stage.,1,1,"Binary C/D decision, not a continuous contribution.",0.7,N/A,No explicit contribution from an endowment.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No communication processes modeled between agents.,1,0,Evolutionary update is based on payoffs directly; there is no representation of displayed summaries.,1,N/A,This condition only uses reward-type incentives.,1,0,Reward is institutional and not tied to individual identity visibility.,1,0,No presented round information; infinite-horizon evolutionary process.,1,0,Only reward institution considered in this scenario.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Per capita budget u is allocated between rewarding participation (α u) and rewarding commitment compliance ((1−α)u).,1,1,Same 1-to-1 cost-efficiency assumption for all incentives.,0.7,1,Each unit cost from the institution yields one unit payoff increase for recipients.,0.7,N/R,No endowment amount per player specified.,1,N/R,"Results given as cooperation frequency and frequencies of strategies, not as endowment fractions.",1,N/R,Actions are C/D; no numeric contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not calculated or reported.,1,N/R,Analyses focus on cooperative outcomes and strategy ESS properties.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is a parameter regime of a theoretical/simulation model.,1,"Same base game as other conditions (two-player one-shot PD, eight strategies, prior commitment with cost ε). For reward with participation incentives, per capita budget u is split: αu rewards all agents who choose to accept the prior commitment (AXY players), thereby encouraging participation; remaining (1−α)u rewards commitment-compliant strategies (ACC, ACD). Reward terms are incorporated into payoff matrix Π_R. Finite population dynamics (N=100, β=0.1, small mutation) are used to compute stationary distribution and total cooperation frequency as functions of α, ε, u, and χ. The paper identifies optimal α* that maximizes cooperation.","Summarized from sections describing Π_R with fraction α for participation and subsequent simulation results (figures 5, 6, 7d).",1
10.1098_rsif.2022.0036.md,"Punishment institution – mixed allocation with participation rewards (α ∈ [0,1], u > 0)","Scenario where punishment-type incentives are used and a fraction α of the per capita budget u is used to reward participation, with the remainder used to punish non-compliance.",1,"Incentive type = punishment; per capita budget u varied (e.g., u = 1, 2); participation reward fraction α varied from 0 to 1; cost of commitment participation ε varied; participation error χ varied; PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; population size N=100.","In Sect. 3.2.2 and figures 5a,c, 6, 7c punishment scenarios vary α, ε, u, and χ.",1,False,No empirical data collection.,1,False,The condition is a modeled parameter regime.,1,False,No lab or field site; entirely theoretical.,1,True,Simulation of finite-population evolutionary dynamics with punishment and participation incentives.,1,True,Payoff matrix Π_P is specified for general α and u; ESS and risk-dominance results are derived.,1,2,Same two-player PD as the rest of the model.,1,1,One-shot PD in each interaction.,1,1,Players choose C or D; no contribution level.,0.7,N/A,No endowment-splitting contributions.,1,N/R,Not a public good game.,1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,"Updates depend on payoffs, not explicit summaries.",1,0,Institutional punishment is not tied to visible identities in the model.,1,0,"Although αu rewards participation, it is an institutional transfer without identity features.",1,0,No explicit round count shown to agents.,1,1,"Remaining budget (1−α)u is used to punish commitment non-compliant players (ADC, ADD).",1,1,Same cost-efficiency assumption: 1 unit cost → 1 unit payoff reduction.,0.7,1,Each institution unit spent on punishment reduces the target’s payoff by one unit.,0.7,1,"Even in punishment scenarios, a fraction αu is used to reward participation (AXY players).",1,1,Cost-efficiency symmetry between reward and punishment; one-to-one mapping between cost and payoff.,0.7,1,Each unit of αu increases rewarded participant’s payoff by one.,0.7,N/R,Endowment levels not specified.,1,N/R,"DV is the frequency of cooperation and of specific strategies, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions in tokens.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics not reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff not reported as outcome.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,The condition is implemented in simulations and analytical derivations only.,1,"Base game: two-player one-shot PD (R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0) with commitment stage and eight strategies as before. For punishment with participation reward, per capita budget u is split: αu rewards those who agree to participate (AXY players), and (1−α)u punishes non-compliant commitment strategies (ADC and ADD) when they defect after joining a commitment. Payoff matrix Π_P includes parameters γ1–γ4 that depend on α, u, and ε. Evolutionary dynamics in N=100 population with intensity of selection β=0.1 and rare mutation; stationary distributions used to measure overall cooperation and frequencies of ESS-like strategies (e.g., ACD, ADD, NDD, NCD). The paper identifies that optimal α* is typically higher for larger ε and that rewarding participation is particularly beneficial under punishment.","Integrated from description of Π_P, the role of α, and results on cooperation levels with participation rewards (figures 5a,c, 6, 7c).",1
