10.1016_j.joep.2009.04.004.md,POS (reward scheme active in a phase),"Condition where the leader has chosen the positive incentive scheme (allocation of positive tokens, POS) for the current 10-round phase; contributions and payoffs are reported separately for POS vs. NEG across phases.",1,Incentive scheme chosen by leader in this phase: POS (positive tokens / rewards),The only treatment variable that differs across conditions within the same structural game is whether the leader’s scheme is POS or NEG for a block of 10 rounds.,1,True,Human subjects (120 students) in laboratory teams making contribution and incentive decisions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to groups and exogenous rules.,1,True,Conducted in Laboratory for Experimental Economics at University of Erfurt.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations described; all data from human subjects.,1,False,Paper contains verbal/theoretical discussion but no stand-alone analytical model generating data.,1,6,Teams of N=6 players (one leader and five teammates).,1,30,Three phases (blocks) with T=10 periods each; total 30 rounds per group.,1,1,Each member chooses an integer contribution between 0 and 20 tokens; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,"Each round, participants receive an endowment of 20 tokens in their private account and then choose how many to contribute; all endowment starts private.",1,0.2666667,"MPCR a = R/N with R=1.6 and N=6, so 1.6/6 ≈ 0.2667.",1,0,Instructions explicitly forbid communication during the experiment.,1,1,"End of each round: detailed overview of contributions, payoffs, and received/assigned tokens for all group members; plus history summaries.",1,0,"Type B participants are shown as an anonymized list with random order each round; cannot link behavior to a fixed identity, so punishments/rewards are anonymous.",0.9,0,Same anonymity mechanism applies to positive tokens as to negative; no stable identity mapping.,0.9,1,Subjects informed that the experiment has 30 rounds in three blocks of 10; leaders see this before choosing schemes; thus total number of rounds is known.,1,0,In POS blocks the active mechanism is allocation of positive tokens (rewards); negative tokens (punishment) are not available.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is active in POS phases.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is active in POS phases.,1,1,In POS blocks the leader can assign positive tokens that increase teammates’ payoffs.,1,1,Leader receives 20 extra tokens and any token allocated to a teammate is forgone income for the leader; cost per token =1.,1,3,Each positive token assigned increases the teammate’s payoff by 3 tokens (leverage 1:3).,1,20,"In stage 1 of each round, each group member is given an endowment of 20 tokens.",1,N/R,"Average contributions are reported mostly as raw token amounts (e.g., 9.0, 11.1) or percentages of change, but no explicit normalized fraction of endowment over all POS observations is given.",0.3,N/R,"They provide several conditional averages (e.g., under POS vs. NEG, before and after switches, path-dependent), but not a single aggregate average contribution for all POS phases that can be isolated from NEG.",0.3,0.76,"They state: “Averaged over all three phases, ... in POS teams manage to obtain 76.0% of the maximum possible payoff.”",1,N/R,"They give payoffs per player (e.g., leaders vs teammates, paths) and an aggregated per-member payoff of 32.7 tokens in path POS&POS&POS, but not a single average group payoff across all POS observations.",0.3,Germany,"Participants are students from the University of Erfurt, located in Germany.",1,N/R,Described only as ‘students’; no age range or mean reported.,0,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,0,"University students (undergraduate/graduate level, unspecified)",Participants are 120 students from the University of Erfurt; no further breakdown.,0.8,On site,"Standard physical lab sessions are implied (90-minute sessions, neutral-framed instructions, payment in cash in the lab) with no indication of online implementation.",1,"Linear public good / team production game with N=6, endowment 20 tokens per round, marginal productivity R=1.6 shared equally, leader has additional 20-token budget per round for incentives; 3 phases of 10 rounds; leader is a team member and cannot be sanctioned; feedback each round includes individual contributions, earnings and incentive tokens for all members.",Summarizes structural features not captured by other fields but directly described in design and instructions.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2009.04.004.md,NEG (punishment scheme active in a phase),"Condition where the leader has chosen the negative incentive scheme (allocation of negative tokens, NEG) for the current 10-round phase; compared directly to POS on contributions, payoffs, and efficiency.",1,Incentive scheme chosen by leader in this phase: NEG (negative tokens / punishment),The only structural treatment variable is again the incentive scheme; this row covers periods when NEG is active.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment; NEG is another condition within it.,1,True,NEG blocks follow the same controlled experimental protocol.,1,True,Conducted in the same physical laboratory setting.,1,False,No purely simulated data.,1,False,No purely analytical dataset.,1,6,Teams of six in all phases and conditions.,1,30,Total interaction length is unchanged; NEG can occur in any of the 3 blocks of 10 rounds within these 30.,1,1,Contribution choice 0–20 tokens continuous in NEG as well.,1,0,Same endowment procedure; all tokens start privately each round.,1,0.2666667,R and N do not vary with scheme; MPCR is identical to POS.,1,0,Communication prohibited throughout the experiment.,1,1,Same feedback and history displays apply under NEG.,1,0,Type B positions are randomly permuted each round; cannot map punishments to persistent identities.,0.9,0,"Positive tokens are inactive in NEG, but in general recipients are anonymized; however, here the reward mechanism does not operate.",0.9,1,Total number of rounds and structure common knowledge in all schemes.,1,1,In NEG the leader can assign negative tokens that reduce teammates’ payoffs.,1,1,Leader’s additional 20 tokens are the same discretionary budget; any token used to punish is lost to the leader (cost 1 per token).,1,3,Each negative token assigned reduces the teammate’s payoff by 3 tokens (1:3 leverage).,1,0,"In NEG blocks, the active mechanism is negative tokens; positive tokens are not an option.",1,N/A,Reward mechanism is disabled in NEG.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is disabled in NEG.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment for contributions in all rounds.,1,N/R,"Authors report that contributions are higher in NEG than POS and give some phase averages (e.g., from 9.0 to 11.1 tokens when switching to NEG), but do not provide a single normalized rate over all NEG observations.",0.3,N/R,"Multiple conditional averages (e.g., 11.1 tokens after switch, 14.8 in POS&NEG&NEG phase 3) are given, but not a unified across-all-NEG average.",0.3,0.727,"They state: “Averaged over all three phases, in NEG, teams obtain 72.7% of the maximum possible payoff.”",1,N/R,"They decompose payoffs by type and by path, but do not state a single mean group payoff across all NEG phases.",0.3,Germany,Same participants and setting as in POS.,1,N/R,Age not specified.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,"University students (undergraduate/graduate level, unspecified)",Same subject pool description applies.,0.8,On site,Same lab setup for NEG as for POS.,1,"Same linear public good / team production structure as POS (N=6, endowment 20, R=1.6, equal sharing). The leader is unsanctionable, has a 20-token incentive budget per round, and can reduce teammates’ payoffs via negative tokens with a leverage of 1:3; negative tokens can make a teammate’s period income negative. Full feedback and history summaries are provided each round.",Restates NEG-specific mechanics and shared structure that are not captured by parameter fields alone.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0125193.md,Partner Control condition – Responder,Paper explicitly labels a 'Partner Control condition' where Responders can add/subtract money from the Roller; this row captures that treatment.,1,Response mechanism: Partner Control (Responder can costlessly add or subtract up to $2 from Roller’s payoff with 1/12 probability of implementation),Main experimental manipulation is whether Responders have partner control (punish/reward) vs partner choice (stay/switch). This row is the partner control mechanism.,1,True,Human participants recruited through an online study pool and tested in person.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to conditions; controlled payoff structure and instructions.,1,True,Participants seated at the same table in the same room with barriers; indicates on-site lab-like setting.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations described; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model as a standalone study.,1,2,Game is played in Roller–Responder pairs; text: 'matched with each other in pairs of one Roller and one Responder'.,1,1,"Explicitly: 'Partner Choice condition required the use of 2 rounds, whereas only one was needed in the Partner Control condition.'",1,1,"Responder’s 'contribution' (punish/reward) is any integer amount from -2 to +2 dollars, not all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,"This is not a standard contribution game; all $4 are initially under Roller’s control, so no initial public fund.",0.7,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR reported.,1,0,No communication allowed; barriers prevented seeing others’ choices and no mention of chat.,1,0,Responders only see their own Roller’s die choice and outcome; no earnings summaries reported.,1,0,Interactions anonymous; 'participants had no way of knowing who they were actually playing with.',1,0,Same anonymity for positive and negative adjustments; identities never revealed.,1,1,"Instructions specify number of rounds (one) for this condition, so participants know game length.",0.8,1,Responders can 'subtract up to $2' from the Roller’s payoff.,1,0,Text: 'Adding or subtracting was costless for the Responder.',1,1,Each $1 of subtraction reduces Roller payoff by $1; no multiplier indicated.,0.9,1,Responders can 'add up to $2' to the Roller’s payoff.,1,0,Same mechanism as punishment; explicitly costless for Responder.,1,1,Each $1 of addition increases Roller payoff by $1; no multiplier specified.,0.9,4,Roller allocates '$4 per round between themselves and the Responder.',1,N/R,No normalized 'contribution' proportion reported; outcomes are allocations and punish/reward amounts.,1,N/R,Only means by condition are described qualitatively or in a figure; no numeric table provided in text.,0.2,N/R,No report of efficiency or ratio of realized to maximum payoffs.,1,N/R,Final payoffs per pair or group totals are not numerically reported.,1,United States,Participants recruited through an online pool at Brown University (USA) and tested in the U.S.,0.9,Mean 19.2 years (range 18–23),"Participants: 'mean age = 19.2 years, range 18–23 years.'",1,27.8% male,"Reported aggregate: 'N=117, 27.8% male'; no further breakdown by condition.",0.9,University students (partial course credit),"Recruited from an online study pool and received partial course credit, implying college students.",0.9,On site,Participants 'seated at the same table in the same room' with physical barriers.,1,"Two-player trembling-hand allocation game: Roller chooses between a selfish die (2/3 chance $4–$0, 1/3 $2–$2) and a fair die (2/3 $2–$2, 1/3 $4–$0). Responder, using the strategy method, specifies for all four combinations of die choice (selfish vs fair) and outcome (selfish vs fair) how much to add or subtract (−$2 to +$2) from Roller’s payoff; adjustment is implemented with probability 1/12.","Summarizes structure needed to understand cooperation/punishment environment, based directly on Materials and Methods.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0125193.md,Partner Choice condition – Responder,Paper explicitly labels a 'Partner Choice condition' where Responders choose to stay with or switch partners; this row captures that treatment.,1,Response mechanism: Partner Choice (Responder chooses to stay with current Roller or switch to a new Roller for round 2; decision is deterministic),Main manipulation is partner choice vs partner control; here Responders engage in partner choice via stay/switch decisions.,1,True,Same human participant sample; condition assignment differs only in response mechanism.,1,True,Controlled game with random assignment to conditions and specified payoffs.,1,True,"Conducted in-person with participants in the same room with barriers, like in the Partner Control condition.",1,False,No simulations run; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Study reports empirical data and regressions, not purely analytical models.",1,2,"As in Partner Control, game played in Roller–Responder pairs.",1,2,Explicitly: 'Partner Choice condition required the use of 2 rounds...'; second round structurally identical but with hypothetical stay/switch.,1,1,Roller’s allocation and Responder’s stay/switch are not contribution amounts; no contribution discretization constraint applies.,0.6,0,All $4 under Roller’s control each round; no initial amount in any shared account.,0.7,N/R,Not described as a public goods game; no MPCR reported.,1,0,"As in Partner Control, no communication between participants is described; anonymity maintained.",1,0,Responders know only their own Roller’s actions; no summary info of others’ outcomes or switches.,1,0,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition; interactions anonymous.,1,0,No direct monetary reward mechanism for partners; anonymity maintained.,1,1,Instructions state that 'two rounds of the game would be played'; thus total rounds are known.,1,0,Responders cannot deduct money from Roller; only stay or switch partners.,1,N/A,No punishment option exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism implemented.,1,0,Responders cannot directly add to Roller’s payoff; only partner choice actions.,1,N/A,No direct monetary reward in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism to parameterize.,1,4,Roller allocates '$4 per round between themselves and the Responder' in all conditions.,1,N/R,Key DV is probability of switching vs staying; no normalized contribution measure reported.,1,N/R,No numeric allocation averages by condition are given in main text.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency of payoffs not computed or reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff statistics reported by condition.,1,United States,Same overall sample (Brown University in the U.S.) split across conditions.,0.9,Mean 19.2 years (range 18–23),Condition-specific ages not provided; overall sample statistics used.,1,27.8% male,Only aggregate gender for N=117 is reported; assumed similar across conditions.,0.9,University students (partial course credit),Same recruitment and compensation as Partner Control condition.,0.9,On site,Same physical setting with participants seated in the same room as in Partner Control.,1,"Same trembling-hand allocation as Partner Control: Roller chooses selfish vs fair die (selfish die: 2/3 $4–$0, 1/3 $2–$2; fair die: 2/3 $2–$2, 1/3 $4–$0). Using the strategy method, Responders specify for all four combinations of die choice (selfish/fair) and outcome (selfish/fair) whether they would stay with the same Roller or switch to a new Roller for the next round. In round 1, stay/switch decisions are implemented deterministically; round 2 is played similarly but stay/switch choices are hypothetical.",Summarizes the partner choice implementation and its relation to the underlying die-allocation game.,1
10.2307_2786703.md,"Experiment 1 – p(P|N)=0.1, high average punishment power, balanced punishment power","Condition defined by punishment strategy (p(P|N)=.1), high average punishment power (.7), and balanced punishment power, as described in design and Table 2/Table 3.",0.96,Punishment strategy p(P|N)=0.1; average punishment power high (0.7); punishment power balanced,These three manipulated factors form the 3×2×2 factorial design cells; this row corresponds to one such combination.,0.96,True,Undergraduate human subjects earned money by exchanging points with computer-simulated partners.,1,True,Random assignment to cells of a 3×2×2 factorial design in a controlled lab setting.,1,True,Described explicitly as a laboratory experiment with subjects in isolated rooms interacting via computers.,1,False,"Although partners were simulated, human subjects’ behavior is primary DV; not a pure simulation study.",0.98,False,No formal closed-form/mathematical model presented as the main method.,1,2,Focal exchange relation is dyad S–POM; analysis and design focus on this dyad even though a third (POC) exists in the network.,0.85,200,“Subjects engaged in nonnegotiated exchange for 200 opportunities.”,1,0,"On each opportunity, actors choose one of three discrete actions (reward, punish, or not act); no continuous contribution choice.",0.9,0,No endowment to allocate between private/public accounts; actors only add/subtract to others. By convention set to 0 for absence of contribution mechanism.,0.7,N/A,Not a public goods or linear contribution game; no group fund or multiplier.,1,0,Interaction only through preset computer choices; no mention of communication.,0.98,1,"After each opportunity, subjects were told each partner’s action and their own point totals, which summarize others’ effects on earnings.",0.9,1,Punishment actions are partner-specific within a small network; feedback indicates which partner (POM vs POC) punished or rewarded.,0.9,1,"Analogous to punishment, identities of rewarding partners are implicit in partner-specific feedback.",0.9,0,Text does not state that remaining or total opportunities were displayed on the screen.,0.5,1,Actors could subtract points from partners’ earnings; this is the central manipulation.,1,0,“Subtracting from the other's points did not increase the subject's own earnings”; only opportunity cost; per-unit cost to punisher is zero.,0.95,1,One action subtracts a fixed number of points; each unit of ‘punishment’ maps 1:1 to partner’s loss.,0.9,1,Actors could add points to partners’ earnings (reward-based power).,1,0,“Adding to the other's points did not reduce the subject's own earnings”; per-unit cost to rewarder is zero.,0.95,1,One reward action adds a fixed number of points; 1:1 mapping to partner’s gain.,0.9,200,Subjects began with $3.00 (200 points) as initial account.,1,0.26,Use S’s rewarding frequency toward POM as analogue of contribution. Table 2: S’s rewarding=.26 in this cell; already normalized over 200 opportunities.,0.9,52,0.26 of 200 opportunities ≈ 52 reward actions to POM.,0.85,N/R,No report of total payoffs relative to maximum possible across conditions.,0.2,N/R,Paper does not provide raw group earnings by condition.,0.2,USA,Study conducted at University of Arizona; typical subject pool is U.S. undergraduates.,0.8,N/R,Participants described only as undergraduates; no age statistics reported.,0.4,Approximately equal male and female within each condition,Design: “10 subjects (five males and five females) in each condition.”,1,Undergraduate students,Described as undergraduate subjects.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment with subjects in isolated rooms using a computer network.,1,"Dyadic reciprocal exchange game in a 3-actor network. On each of 200 opportunities, subject chooses toward one partner (reward, punish, or no action). Subject is reward-advantaged over POM (reward power .8 vs .4) and equal with POC (.6/.6). In this condition, average punishment power in S–POM dyad is .7, balanced; punishment magnitude per opportunity adjusted via partners’ alternative links. POM uses modified contingent punishment strategy with p(P|N)=0.1, p(R|R)=1.0, p(P|R)=0, p(P|P)=0.9; both POM and POC reciprocate S’s past actions with specified probabilities (Table 1). S’s compliance and retaliation measured as proportion of opportunities rewarding or punishing POM.","Summarized from design description, power structure section, and Table 1.",0.95
