10.1038_srep02695.md,"Commitment model without explicit cost sharing (COMP vs C, D, FAKE, FREE)","Paper analyzes an evolutionary game with five strategies (COMP, C, D, FAKE, FREE) as one model condition without explicit cost sharing beyond implicit 50% sharing between COMP players.",0.95,"Commitment cost ε and compensation δ varied in a one-shot symmetric pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with five strategies (COMP, C, D, FAKE, FREE). Example parameters: T=2, R=1, P=0, S=-1; ε ranged around boundary 2(b−c)/3; δ varied, e.g., δ=4.","Model explicitly varies ε and δ and studies frequencies/fixation; PD payoffs given, with Donation game mapping b,c discussed.",0.9,False,No human participants; purely theoretical and numerical evolutionary game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a mathematical/simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; model only.,1,True,"Uses numerical simulations of evolutionary dynamics in finite populations (Markov chains, fixation probabilities, stationary distributions).",1,True,Derives risk-dominance inequalities and conditions on ε and δ analytically.,1,2,"Model is explicitly a symmetric, pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma; populations of size N interact in pairs.",1,1,"Described as non-repeated, one-shot PD game.",1,1,Strategies are discrete C/D choices; no continuous contribution concept; treated as general contribution freedom so coded as any amount allowed.,0.3,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game; PD with fixed payoffs and no explicit endowment.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return not defined for this PD model.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; abstract strategies only.,1,0,Model payoffs depend only on pairwise interaction; no information feedback structure specified.,0.8,N/A,No identities or observation structure; punishment modeled via payoff parameters only.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards with identity.,1,0,One-shot game in theoretical model; no interface showing rounds.,1,1,Compensation δ acts as a punishment cost imposed on FAKE when they defect after accepting commitment.,0.9,N/R,"Cost structure of compensation to punished player encoded via δ and ε in payoff matrix, but not in per-token units typical of punishment technologies.",0.4,N/R,No separate technology parameter; punishment effect is lumped into δ.,0.4,0,"No separate positive reward mechanism; R is standard cooperation payoff, not an added reward technology.",0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses payoffs R,S,T,P, not explicit endowments.",1,N/R,"Outcome variables are strategy frequencies, fixation probabilities, and stationary distributions, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No token contributions defined.,1,N/R,Paper discusses higher cooperation levels qualitatively but does not provide normalized efficiency values.,0.6,N/R,Does not report aggregate payoffs; focuses on risk-dominance and frequencies.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and computational evolutionary model.,1,"One-shot symmetric pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma (PD) with strategies C, D, COMP (commitment proposer), FAKE (accepts commitment then defects), FREE (accepts commitment and cooperates but never proposes). Payoffs: standard PD with parameters T,R,P,S (e.g., T=2,R=1,P=0,S=-1). Donation game specialization uses T=b, R=b−c, P=0, S=−c. Commitment cost ε paid by COMP to set up deal; compensation δ paid by FAKE to COMP if they defect after accepting.",Summarizes rules and payoff definitions as described in Results and Methods.,1
10.1038_srep02695.md,"Commitment model with explicit cost sharing (COMS vs D, FAKE, FAKS, FREE, FRES, plus C and COMP)","Second major model condition explicitly introduces COMS (commitment with explicit cost sharing) and additional sharing strategies (CS, FAKS, FRES).",0.95,"Commitment cost ε and compensation δ varied in a one-shot symmetric pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with strategies including COMS, COMP, C, D, FAKE, FA KS, FREE, FRES. Risk-dominance conditions for COMS: ε < 2(b−c), δ > c + ε/4; example payoffs T=2, R=1, P=0, S=−1; δ=4.","Text defines COMS, cost-sharing strategies, and new analytical conditions, and Figure 2c–d explores ε and δ.",0.9,False,No human subjects; extension of theoretical/simulation framework.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment; theoretical and numerical analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setup.,1,True,"Uses same evolutionary simulation framework (finite populations, Markov chain stationary distributions) now including COMS and sharing strategies.",1,True,Derives new inequalities for COMS risk-dominance.,1,2,Still pairwise PD interactions.,1,1,Still a one-shot PD game.,1,1,Binary C/D choices as in base model; no continuous contributions.,0.3,N/A,No endowment-contribution structure; PD model.,1,N/R,MPCR concept not used.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol.,1,0,Model does not provide feedback summaries; dynamics based solely on payoffs and imitation.,0.8,N/A,No identity or observation structure defined.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Theoretical one-shot setup; no user interface.,1,1,"As in COMP model, compensation δ serves as punishment for defaulting on accepted commitments.",0.9,N/R,"Punishment/compensation encoded via δ and ε, but no per-unit cost parameter formulated.",0.4,N/R,No distinct punishment technology variable; effect is embedded in δ.,0.4,0,No separate reward beyond standard cooperative payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment; payoffs defined by PD matrix.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as frequencies of strategies (COMP, COMS, etc.), not contribution measures.",1,N/R,No token contributions specified.,1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (e.g., wider parameter range supports cooperation) but not quantified as payoff ratio.",0.6,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff values provided; focus is on dominance and frequencies.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical and computational model only.,1,"Extension of one-shot pairwise PD commitment model introducing explicit cost-sharing commitment proposers (COMS). When two COMS players interact, they share arrangement cost ε; when COMS plays with cost-sharing acceptors (CS, FAKS, FRES), co-player shares cost upon accepting. Existing non-sharing strategies (C, FAKE, FREE) can accept commitment but refuse to share, or opt out of playing. Payoff matrix including these additional strategies is detailed in Supplementary Information. Risk-dominance conditions for COMS against all defecting/free-riding strategies (D, FAKE, FAKS, FREE, FRES) simplify in Donation game parametrization (T=b, R=b−c, P=0, S=−c) to ε < 2(b−c), δ > c + ε/4.",Summarizes COMS rules and differences from COMP based on Results text.,1
10.1111_evo.12108.md,Rounds 1–8: Baseline punishment with reputation (all treatments pooled),"Rounds 1–8 were identical across all later treatments (PUN/REP, PUN/NOREP, NOPUN/REP): punishment available and punishment reputation displayed to observers.",0.95,"Stage of game: Part 1 (rounds 1–8), punishment available and punishment reputation displayed",Independent factor is the game stage; during rounds 1–8 punishment and its reputation are implemented identically for all players.,0.95,True,Human students played a helping game with punishment in the lab.,1,True,Game rules were controlled by experimenters with random assignment into groups and roles.,1,True,Conducted with students in isolated cubicles at universities in Switzerland.,1,False,No mention of computer/agent-based simulations; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model developed; only statistical analysis of experimental data.,1,8,Groups were of 7–9 players. Exact per-group size not specified; use midpoint 8 as approximate typical group size.,0.3,8,Part 1 consists of the first eight rounds; total game has 16 rounds but this condition covers rounds 1–8 only.,1,0,"Donor chooses between two discrete actions (donate or refuse), not a continuous or all-or-nothing contribution to a public good.",0.7,0,Each player receives 20 CHF endowment that remains in private account unless used in bilateral helping or punishment; no automatic contribution to a public fund.,0.9,N/R,"This is a bilateral helping/punishment game, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.",0,0,"Players in isolated cubicles, no communication described or allowed.",0.95,1,"Donor’s decision (cooperate/defect) and each Receiver’s ID and punishment score were projected to all players each round, summarizing others’ cooperative and punitive behavior.",0.95,1,"Receivers’ IDs and their punishment scores were displayed to all; thus, punishers can be identified via Receiver IDs and associated scores.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in t