10.1016_j.physa.2007.06.008.md,Simulation – Varying average degree κ,Section 3.1 studies cooperation frequency vs. b on scale-free networks with different average degrees κ; treated as one simulation condition family with κ as the main manipulated variable.,0.81,"Average degree κ of the scale-free network (multiple values; specific κ values not numerically listed) and temptation to defect b with 1 < b < 2 (swept, e.g., includes b = 1.9).",Section 3.1: “Fig. 1(a) shows the simulation results … with different values of average degree κ” and text discusses cooperation frequency vs. b; b is the PD temptation parameter.,0.86,False,The work is described as ‘computer simulation’ with agents occupying network nodes; no human participants are mentioned.,1,False,"It is a numerical simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human or field data.",1,False,No lab setting or participants; purely computational.,1,True,Section 2: “We use computer simulation to analyze how the system evolves … on different scale-free networks.”,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations; results are numerical.,1,0,Network (population) size N is not reported in the text; cannot be inferred.,0,13000,"They run 10000 transient generations, then average over the next 3000 generations: 10000 + 3000 = 13000 generations total per run.",0.9,1,This is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with binary strategies: s_i = 1 for cooperation and s_i = 0 for defection; contribution is all-or-nothing in PD sense.,1,N/A,"There is no public-goods contribution or endowment structure; PD is modeled directly via payoff matrix, and there are no human subjects.",1,N/A,Not a public-goods game; MPCR not defined and no humans.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely simulated strategy updates.,1,N/A,"No user interface; agents update via payoff comparison, not via displayed summaries.",1,N/A,No identities or interface; agents are anonymous nodes in a simulation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identity revelation in a human sense.,1,N/A,No human players or UI; rounds not displayed to participants.,1,0,In section 3.1 the model is the baseline iterated PD on scale-free networks; the separate punishment mechanism is only introduced later in section 3.3.,0.94,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism is described; only cooperation and defection strategies.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,PD payoffs are not framed as distributing an endowment; no human tokens/endowments used.,1,N/R,"Outcome is ‘cooperation frequency’ (density of cooperators), but not normalized contribution of an endowment in a public-goods sense.",0.4,N/R,Binary PD actions (C/D) without token amounts.,1,N/R,They do not compute or report efficiency as total payoff relative to a maximum.,0.1,N/R,They report cooperation frequency but not aggregate payoffs.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract nodes.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is entirely based on computer simulations with artificial agents.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma on scale-free networks without rewiring; undirected links; payoff parameters: R = 1, T = b with 1 < b < 2, S = 0, P = ε = 0.01. Strategy update via pairwise comparison (finite-population replicator-like rule). Cooperators and defectors initially randomly assigned with equal probability 0.5. Each generation: agents play PD with all neighbors to accumulate payoff, then may imitate a random neighbor’s strategy with probability ω = (Ej − Ei)/[(T − S) κ_> ] if Ej > Ei, where κ_> is max degree of the pair.",Summarized from section 2 (Model) and simulation description including payoff definitions and update rule.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2007.06.008.md,Simulation – Varying clustering coefficient C (κ = 6),Section 3.2: “Scale-free networks with tunable clustering coefficient”; cooperation frequency vs. b compared across different clustering coefficients at fixed average degree κ = 6.,0.9,Network clustering coefficient C (including BA baseline with C ≈ 4.78×10⁻³ and two larger values) at fixed average degree κ = 6; temptation parameter b varied (includes b = 1.9 for triangle analysis).,Fig. 2(a) described as ‘cooperation frequency vs. b on scale-free networks with different clustering coefficients (κ = 6)’; text also fixes κ = 6 and varies C.,0.96,False,Same simulation framework as section 3.1; no human participants.,1,False,"Numerical network simulations, not empirical experiments.",1,False,No lab setting or human subjects.,1,True,They ‘built a scale-free network with a tunable clustering coefficient’ and simulated PD evolution on it.,1,False,"Results are from numerical simulation, not analytical solutions.",1,0,Network size N is not stated; cannot be inferred.,0,13000,Same simulation protocol as section 2: 10000 transient generations + 3000 generations for averaging.,0.9,1,Binary PD strategies (cooperate/defect) as in the base model.,1,N/A,Not a public-goods contribution game; no human endowments.,1,N/A,No public-good multiplier; PD with fixed payoffs.,1,N/A,No human communication; agents only interact via PD and update rule.,1,N/A,No interface; simulation-only.,1,N/A,No identities or UI; only abstract nodes.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identity display.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,Section 3.2 does not involve the punishment mechanism; that appears only in section 3.3.,0.96,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,No rewarding behavior or parameters are specified.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human token endowments; payoffs are PD payoffs defined in the model.,1,N/R,They report cooperation frequency (density of cooperators) rather than a contribution proportion of an endowment.,0.4,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect actions; no numeric contribution amounts.,1,N/R,No measure of payoff efficiency relative to maximum is given.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate or per-capita payoffs are not reported; discussion is in terms of cooperation frequency and number of cooperating triangles.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Entirely computational study of artificial agents on networks.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma on scale-free networks with tunable clustering coefficient C (Holme–Kim construction), average degree κ fixed at 6. Payoff parameters as in base model: R = 1, T = b (1 < b < 2), S = 0, P = ε = 0.01. Agents play PD with all neighbors each generation, accumulate payoff Ei as in Eq. (1), then update strategy via pairwise comparison rule with probability ω = (Ej − Ei)/[(T − S) κ_>] if neighbor j has higher payoff. Initial fraction of cooperators = 0.5 (randomly assigned). Simulations analyze cooperation frequency vs. b across different clustering coefficients, and number of cooperating triangles vs. C at b = 1.9.","Compiled from sections 2 and 3.2 describing network construction, parameters, and observables.",1
10.1016_j.physa.2007.06.008.md,Simulation – Punishment mechanism η on scale-free networks (κ = 8),Section 3.3: ‘Introduction of punishment mechanism’; Fig. 3(a) plots cooperation frequency vs. b for various punishment parameters η at κ = 8. This block is treated as one simulation condition family where η is the main manipulated variable.,0.93,"Punishment probability η (0 ≤ η ≤ 0.03 considered; e.g., baseline η = 0 vs. positive η values) on a scale-free network with average degree κ = 8, while varying temptation b (1 < b < 2, includes b = 1.9).",Section 3.3: ‘agent i may dismiss the interaction with agent j with a probability η…’; ‘The dependence of cooperation on punishment parameter η is shown in Fig. 3(a)… (κ = 8).’ b is also varied on the x-axis.,0.97,False,"As before, these are computer simulations with no human participants.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment; purely numerical.,1,False,No physical lab environment or subjects.,1,True,Section 4 summarizes: ‘explored by numerical simulation’; section 3.3 implements punishment in the same simulation framework.,1,False,No formal analytic derivations for punishment dynamics; outcomes are simulated.,1,0,Network size N is not given.,0,13000,"Same general simulation procedure: transient of 10000 generations, then 3000 for averaging; they mention introducing punishment at time step 10000, implying similar timing.",0.85,1,Game remains an iterated PD with binary cooperate/defect strategies.,1,N/A,No endowment or contribution framing; no humans.,1,N/A,Not a public-goods game; no MPCR.,1,N/A,No human communication; a network rewiring punishment mechanism instead.,1,N/A,No participants; no displayed summaries.,1,N/A,Agents are anonymous nodes without identity display in a UI context.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identity display.,1,N/A,No human subjects to be informed about number of rounds.,1,1,Section 3.3 explicitly introduces a punishment mechanism: unsatisfied cooperators may break links to defectors with probability η.,1,0,"Punishment consists of severing a link and reconnecting to another random agent. Agent i’s payoff is stated to be unchanged immediately after disconnecting, while agent j’s payoff decreases by b. There is no explicit payoff cost to punisher i, so cost per unit of punishment is effectively 0 in payoff terms.",0.7,1,"Each punishment event (disconnection) reduces the punished defector’s payoff by exactly b (the benefit from exploiting that cooperator). Taking the punishment ‘unit’ as the agent’s probabilistic action (with no cost), we approximate the punishment effect magnitude per such unit as 1×b; since the schema expects a numeric scalar, we set it to 1 and explain that b is the per-punishment payoff loss.",0.4,0,No mechanism that increases another’s payoff at a cost; only link-cutting punishment.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,"Payoffs arise directly from PD games on network edges; no endowment concept, and no humans.",1,N/R,"Outcome is cooperation frequency (fraction of cooperators) vs. b and η, not normalized contribution of tokens.",0.4,N/R,No numeric token contributions; binary cooperate/defect actions.,1,N/R,They do not report efficiency as payoff relative to a benchmark; focus is on cooperation frequency and cluster sizes.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not tabulated; only qualitative discussion of payoffs and cluster evolution.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Punishment mechanism is implemented in simulation among artificial agents.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma on a scale-free network with average degree κ = 8. Baseline payoffs: R = 1, T = b (1 < b < 2, includes b = 1.9), S = 0, P = ε = 0.01. Agents initially: 50% cooperators, 50% defectors, randomly distributed. Each generation: (1) each agent plays PD with all neighbors and accumulates payoff Ei according to Eq. (1); (2) agents update strategies by comparing to a randomly chosen neighbor j and imitating j’s strategy with probability ω = (Ej − Ei)/[(T − S) κ_>] if Ej > Ei. Punishment mechanism: if an agent i cooperates while neighbor j defects (i receives S = 0 and is ‘unsatisfied’), then i may ‘punish’ j by dismissing the link (i, j) with probability η, rewiring it to a randomly selected different agent in the network; self-links and multiple links are disallowed. Immediately after disconnection, i’s payoff is unchanged while j’s payoff decreases by b. Parameter range checked: 0 < η ≤ 0.03, within which network topology is said to remain globally unchanged. They analyze cooperation frequency vs. b for different η and the evolution of the largest cooperating cluster and its core cluster before and after introducing punishment at time step 10000.",Taken from section 3.3’s description of punishment mechanism and cluster dynamics combined with the base model details in section 2.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2019.109517.md,Case I – Exclusion plays the leading role,Paper defines Case I as the condition where exclusion plays the leading role when punishers and excluders coexist.,1,"Leading-role rule: exclusion dominant; parameters varied across runs include synergy factor r, punishment fine β, exclusion cost γ, and punishment cost α.","Case I vs Case II differ by which sanctioning strategy is active when both are present. Within Case I, r, β, γ, α are systematically varied in simulations.",1,False,"Study is an evolutionary game simulation on a square lattice with agents, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; purely computational.,1,True,They describe running Monte Carlo simulations on a 100×100 lattice to steady state.,1,False,"Although payoffs are given analytically, results are based on numerical simulations, not closed-form equilibrium derivations.",0.8,10000,"They simulate on a 100*100 square lattice, i.e., 10^4 agents.",1,20000,They run 2×10^4 Monte Carlo steps and use this as convergence to steady state.,0.9,1,"Each cooperator, punisher, and excluder contributes a fixed amount c=1; defectors contribute 0. No choice of partial contribution; hence not all-or-nothing voluntary contribution in the sense of a binary decision per period among multiple levels, but contribution is fixed for cooperative strategies. However, from the perspective of agents' decision space, strategy is categorical rather than choosing a continuous contribution level; we code 1 (any amount allowed) to avoid misclassifying as strictly binary contribution of endowment.",0.4,N/A,"No human endowment allocation process; contributions are modeled directly as payoffs, so notion of default allocation is inapplicable.",1,N/R,"They report synergy factor r and group size G=5, but returns depend on exclusion/punishment and are not a standard linear MPCR; they do not specify a single marginal per capita return applicable across states.",0.4,N/A,No human communication; agent-based simulation only.,1,0,Agents update based on local payoffs via Fermi rule; no display or summary mechanism is described in the model.,1,N/A,No identities or displays exist in this abstract simulation framework.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,Agents do not observe a round counter; only the modeler defines total MCS.,1,1,Punishers impose fines β on defectors at cost α when no excluder is present in the group.,1,N/R,They treat α as a tunable parameter and fix values like α=0.3 or 0.6 in different analyses; there is no single punishment cost for Case I overall.,0.3,N/R,"Punishment fine per defector is β/(G−1); however β is varied (e.g., 1.1–1.9), so no single value for this condition.",0.5,0,The only sanctioning mechanisms are punishment (fines) and exclusion; no positive rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,They normalize contribution cost c=1 but do not define a monetary endowment per round.,0.6,N/R,"Outcome is measured as frequency of cooperative strategies (ρ_{C+E+P}) and strategy frequencies, not per-round contribution fractions.",0.7,N/R,They do not report aggregate contributed amounts; only strategy frequencies and phase diagrams.,0.7,N/R,"They analyze cooperation levels and phases, not payoff efficiency relative to maximum possible group payoff.",1,N/R,"No explicit reporting of group payoff outcomes; only frequencies and phase types (full cooperation vs full defection, coexistence).",1,N/A,No human participants; agents on a lattice only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulations on a network without human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; group size G=5 (each player plays in 5 overlapping groups). Four strategies: C (cooperate, contribute c), D (defect, contribute 0), P (punisher, contributes c and fines defectors at cost α with fine β when no excluder present), E (excluder, contributes c and excludes defectors at cost γ; in Case I excluders are active when present and punishers coexisting with them act as pure cooperators). Strategy update via Fermi rule with noise K=0.5; initial strategies C, D, P, E uniformly random.","Summarized from the model and results sections describing lattice size, overlapping groups, strategies, payoffs, and update dynamics.",1
10.1016_j.chaos.2019.109517.md,Case II – Punishment plays the leading role,Paper defines Case II as the condition where punishment plays the leading role when punishers and excluders coexist.,1,"Leading-role rule: punishment dominant; parameters varied across runs include synergy factor r, punishment fine β, exclusion cost γ, and punishment cost α.","Case II vs Case I differ by which sanctioning strategy is active when both are present. Within Case II, r, β, γ, α are systematically varied in simulations.",1,False,"This is the same agent-based simulation framework as Case I, with no human participants.",1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,Not a lab study; simulation only.,1,True,They perform Monte Carlo simulations of strategy evolution on a square lattice.,1,False,Main results are numerical simulation outcomes; analytical payoffs are used as model specification.,0.8,10000,Same 100*100 lattice with 10^4 agents is used in both cases.,1,20000,They state that frequencies reach stability within 2×10^4 MCSs and use this duration for all simulations.,0.9,1,"Contribution amount per cooperating-type strategy is fixed (c=1) and defectors contribute 0; agents choose among discrete strategies rather than a continuous contribution, but to align with schema we mark as 1 (not restricted to an all-or-nothing contribution choice within a given strategy).",0.4,N/A,No human-controlled endowment; contributions are fixed in the payoff functions.,1,N/R,"They specify synergy factor r and group size G, but due to exclusion/punishment the effective marginal return per capita is state-dependent and not given as a single MPCR.",0.4,N/A,No human communication; model includes only payoff-based imitation.,1,0,Agents compare own payoff with one neighbor; no summaries of others' outcomes are displayed.,1,N/A,No identities or displays exist in the simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Lattice agents do not observe round counts; time horizon is external to them.,1,1,Punishers fine defectors at cost α whenever present; they define payoffs for P involving punishment cost and fine.,1,N/R,"Cost per punished defector is α/(G−1), but α is treated as a parameter (e.g., α=0.3, 0.6); no single value for the overall Case II condition.",0.3,N/R,"Fine per defector is β/(G−1), but β is varied (e.g., 1.1–1.9); hence no single value.",0.5,0,Only punishment and exclusion as negative sanctions are modeled; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,They normalize contribution cost c=1 but do not define a round endowment.,0.6,N/R,"Reported outcomes are cooperation frequencies (ρ_{C+E+P}) and phase regions, not per-round contribution fractions.",0.7,N/R,No explicit total or average contribution amounts are tabulated.,0.7,N/R,They do not report payoff-based efficiency metrics; focus is on cooperation levels and phase diagrams.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoff statistics are provided.,1,N/A,There are no human subjects.,1,N/A,There are no human subjects.,1,N/A,There are no human subjects.,1,N/A,There are no human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations on a square lattice with no human participants.,1,"Same spatial public goods game as in Case I: 100×100 square lattice, G=5 overlapping groups per player, four strategies C, D, P, E. In Case II, punishers are always the active sanctioners when present; excluders present together with punishers act as pure cooperators and only sanction (exclude) defectors if no punisher is in the group. Contributions and payoffs follow the Case II payoff equations (5–8). Strategy updating via Fermi rule with noise K=0.5; initial strategies uniformly random among C, D, P, E.",Summarized from the Case II model description and global simulation settings shared across both cases.,1
10.1111_j.1467-6419.2009.00594.x.md,Carpenter et al. (2006) – Public goods with punishment (strangers treatment),Paper summarizes Carpenter et al. (2006) public goods experiment with optional costly punishment in strangers design; treated as one condition because no separate baseline without punishment is described here.,0.86,Presence of costly punishment option after contribution stage (punishment treatment in strangers design),"Text: ‘in the punishment treatment, they then have the opportunity to reduce their own payoffs in order to reduce the payoffs of a member of the group’. This is the manipulated feature relative to a standard public goods game.",0.9,True,Described as an experiment with ‘subjects’ playing repeated public goods game.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with assigned groups and treatments.,1,True,Referred to as ‘public goods game’ with rounds and stranger matching; standard lab setup; no field context mentioned.,0.9,False,"Human participants, not a numerical model.",1,False,Not a purely theoretical or closed-form model; it is an experiment.,1,5,Text: ‘They consider groups of five or 10 members…’. The exact count per condition is not specified here; 5 used as a representative value but true design may include both sizes.,0.3,10,Text: ‘subjects play 10 rounds’.,1,1,Standard public goods experiments allow any contribution between 0 and endowment; no all-or-nothing restriction is mentioned.,0.7,0,"In standard linear public goods, endowment starts in private account and subjects choose how much to contribute; no automatic contribution mentioned.",0.7,N/R,Multiplier or marginal per capita return is not reported in the summary.,0,0,"Only actions are contribution, public revelation, and punishment; no communication or chat is mentioned.",0.8,1,"Punishment requires seeing others’ contributions/payoffs; text: ‘their contributions are made public’ and punishment targets shirkers, implying summary information.",0.8,1,"Shame and social disapproval are emphasized; ‘their contributions are made public’ and participants punish ‘shirkers’, implying identities of punished individuals are known within the group.",0.6,0,No reward mechanism is mentioned.,1,1,Standard repeated public goods experiments with a fixed 10 rounds usually inform subjects about horizon; not explicitly stated but likely.,0.6,1,Explicit punishment treatment with costly sanctions.,1,N/R,Cost per punishment point/coin is not given in the text.,0,N/R,Magnitude of payoff reduction per punishment unit is not reported.,0,0,Only punishment (reducing others’ payoffs) is discussed.,1,N/A,No reward option in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward option in this treatment.,1,N/R,Tokens or money endowment per round is not reported.,0,N/R,Paper only states qualitatively that punished shirkers increase contributions; no numeric means reported.,0,N/R,No raw contribution values given.,0,N/R,No information on total payoffs relative to maximum.,0,N/R,Only directional statements about punishment and contributions; no payoff numbers.,0,N/R,No country or lab location provided for Carpenter et al. (2006) in this summary.,0,N/R,No demographic details given.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,N/R,No information on student status or education level.,0,On site,"Described as standard lab-style public goods experiment with subjects and rounds; likely conducted on-site in an experimental lab rather than online or field, though not explicit.",0.7,"Linear public goods game with 5 or 10 players, 10 repeated rounds in strangers matching; in each round players choose contributions to a public good, contributions are publicly revealed, and then, in punishment treatment, players can pay to reduce another member’s payoff. Groups are randomly rematched each round.","Directly taken from description: group sizes, strangers matching, stages of each round.",0.9
