10.1038_srep20767.md,Endogenous power transfer condition,Paper labels this as the 'endogenous power transfer condition' where participants can voluntarily transfer punishment power.,1,Punishment power determination: endogenous voluntary power transfer with mirroring of transfers as default each round,Independent variable is how punishment power is determined; this condition features voluntary power transfer by group members.,1,True,Human participants (undergraduate students in BEElab) played the game.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to conditions and pre-specified rules.,1,True,Conducted in the Behavioural and Experimental Economics Lab (BEElab) at Maastricht University with subjects in cubicles.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; data from human subjects only.,1,False,No formal analytical or purely theoretical model is developed as a standalone study.,1,5,Groups of five participants played the game.,1,20,Experiment consisted of 20 rounds in fixed groups of five.,1,1,"Contribution decisions are continuous from 0 to 20 MUs (any amount up to endowment), not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is given to private account; participants decide how much to transfer to group project.,1,0.3,Total contributions multiplied by 1.5 and split equally among 5 members; MPCR = 1.5/5 = 0.3.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat; participants are seated in separate cubicles and only see on-screen info.,0.9,1,"After contribution stage, participants are informed how many MUs each member contributed and kept; after punishment, they see how many punishment points each member assigned and to whom.",1,1,"Participants see how many punishment points each group member assigned and to whom, with unique symbols allowing tracking of individuals.",1,0,No rewarding mechanism is described; only punishment exists.,1,1,"Standard BEElab design typically informs subjects of total rounds; although not explicitly stated, timeline and instructions imply known 20 rounds.",0.6,1,There is a distinct punishment stage where members assign punishment points.,1,1,"For each assigned punishment point, the punisher pays 1 MU.",1,1,"Each punishment point reduces punished player's earnings by (punisher's power × 1 MU). Baseline power is 1, so 1 MU per point at power 1. We record the per-point effect at unit power as 1.",0.9,0,No rewarding or positive points mechanism mentioned; only costly punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"In contribution stage, participants receive an endowment of 20 MUs.",1,N/R,Exact average proportion of endowment contributed is not numerically reported; only trends are given.,0,N/R,"Mean MU contributions per round/condition are not given in numbers, only described qualitatively and via figures.",0,N/R,"Figure 2a includes pie charts of earnings as % of social optimum, but exact percentages are not stated in text.",0.5,N/R,"Average earnings per group/condition over rounds are not numerically reported, only relative differences and regression coefficients.",0,Netherlands,Participants were students at Maastricht University in the Netherlands.,0.95,"Mean age 21.1 years (SD 2.6), adults",Methods: 'A total of 350 undergraduate students (mean age = 21.1 +/- 2.6)'.,1,"204 female, remaining male (146), overall sample",Methods report 204 female out of 350 participants; no allocation by condition. Assume similar composition across conditions due to random assignment.,0.7,Undergraduate university students,Methods: 'A total of 350 undergraduate students...',1,On site,Conducted in BEElab with participants in physical cubicles at the university.,1,"Power transfer game with 3 stages from round 3 onward: (1) power transfer (voluntary in this condition, in units of 0.1, sum of powers fixed at 5), (2) public goods contribution with endowment 20 MUs and multiplier 1.5, (3) peer punishment with 0–10 punishment points per target; power status and past transfers shown as default each round; fixed groups of 5; round 1: PGG only; round 2: PGG with punishment; rounds 3–20: full power transfer game.",Summarizes specific structural and timing details of the game described for the endogenous condition.,1
10.1038_srep20767.md,Exogenous power transfer condition,Paper labels this as the 'exogenous condition' where power transfers are imposed exogenously by mirroring an endogenous group.,1,"Punishment power determination: exogenous, power changes mirrored from paired endogenous group; no voluntary power transfer decisions",Independent variable is how punishment power is determined; here it follows a pre-specified mirrored history instead of choices.,1,True,Human participants (undergraduate students) played in this condition.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment and specific treatment rules.,1,True,Same BEElab setting as other conditions.,1,False,"Outcomes come from human decisions, not a simulation.",1,False,No purely analytical model as separate study.,1,5,Groups of five participants in all conditions.,1,20,Experiment lasted 20 rounds in fixed groups of five.,1,1,Contribution choices in PGG are continuous from 0 to 20 MUs.,1,0,Endowment initially held privately; contributions chosen voluntarily.,1,0.3,"Same PGG structure: multiplier 1.5, 5 players → 1.5/5.",1,0,No mention of communication; same lab protocol as other conditions.,0.9,1,Same feedback: contributions and punishment assignments by each member are displayed.,1,1,Unique symbols and display of who punished whom apply to all conditions.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,1,Same 20-round structure and instruction procedure as other conditions; likely common knowledge.,0.6,1,"Includes punishment stage from round 2 onward, just like other conditions.",1,1,Same punishment rules: 1 MU per punishment point.,1,1,Effect per punishment point is punisher's power in MUs; unit-power effect is 1 MU per point.,0.9,0,No rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same PGG with 20 MU endowment.,1,N/R,Exact proportion of endowment contributed not numerically reported by condition.,0,N/R,No explicit mean contributions in MUs for this condition are given.,0,N/R,Pie chart in Fig. 2a shows % of social optimum but no numbers in text for each condition.,0.5,N/R,Average earnings per group/condition not numerically specified.,0,Netherlands,Subjects are Maastricht University students in the Netherlands.,0.95,"Mean age 21.1 years (SD 2.6), adults",Age summary given for overall sample; conditions randomly assigned.,1,"204 female, remaining male (146), overall sample",Gender composition reported only at aggregate level.,0.7,Undergraduate university students,All participants were undergraduate students.,1,On site,Same lab setting (BEElab) as other conditions.,1,"Same 3-stage structure as endogenous treatment from round 3 onward, but power transfers are not chosen by participants. For each exogenous group, the history of power transfers from one endogenous 'twin group' is mirrored at the individual level: each participant follows the same change in punishment power across rounds as her twin in the endogenous condition. Participants cannot affect their own power allocation.",Summarizes the distinctive exogenous mirroring mechanism and shared game structure.,1
