10.1086_681231.md,Study 1A – Unenforced condition,Condition label and description: unenforced one-shot transactions with random partners (baseline in Study 1A).,1,"Enforcement type: unenforced (no contractual, reputational, or relational enforcement) in both phases.",Study 1A manipulated enforcement type across markets; this condition has no enforcement.,1,True,Uses student participants making decisions for monetary payoffs.,1,True,Random assignment to enforcement conditions with controlled payoff structure.,1,False,"Participants played from any computer with Internet access; procedure explicitly describes a web-based study, not in-lab terminals.",1,False,"Human subjects, not only artificial agents.",1,False,No purely analytical model; behavioral experiment.,1,2,Investment game with one client and one agent per transaction.,1,11,Participants completed 8 rounds in phase 1 plus 3 additional transactions in phase 2 (rounds 9–11).,1,1,Clients choose any integer s with 0 ≤ s ≤ 10; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment of 10 points is initially entirely under client control; nothing is pre-committed to ‘public’/other party.,1,N/R,"This is a bilateral investment/trust game, not a linear public good; MPCR not defined or reported.",1,0,No mention of communication; only asynchronous decisions via web interface.,1,1,"Result screen showed agent’s quality choice, client’s earnings for the transaction, and cumulative earnings; this is a summary of partner outcomes.",0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in unenforced condition.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism apart from payoffs.,1,0,Participants were told experiment would last ‘up to 15 rounds’ but not that there would be exactly 11; remaining rounds not displayed.,0.8,0,"No sanctions, contracts, or feedback in this condition.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism beyond standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"In each transaction, the client was given an endowment of 10 points.",1,N/R,"Paper reports hours of service purchased, but not as normalized fraction of endowment.",0.6,N/R,Figure 1 shows mean hours per round but no exact numeric table.,0.4,N/R,No reported efficiency or ratio of actual to maximum group payoff.,1,N/R,Only individual client earnings and regressions are described; no aggregate payoff numbers.,1,USA,Participants are university students recruited via campus mailing lists and flyers; journal and context indicate U.S. setting.,0.8,Mean 20.5 years (students),"Study 1A reports 101 students, 20.5 years old.",1,58 male; remaining mostly female (exact female count not explicitly stated),"Study 1A: 101 students (58 male, 20.5 years old); rest inferred as non-male.",0.9,University students (undergraduate/college level),Participants described as students recruited on campus.,1,Online,Described as a web-based experiment; participants played from any computer with Internet access.,1,"Client role only; agents are simulated and always provide high-quality service. Phase 1 rounds 1–8 in unenforced market; phase 2 rounds 9–11 also unenforced with new random partners. Payoffs: client pays s points, agent chooses high vs low quality; client’s and agent’s payoffs depend on quality as specified.",Summarized from Study 1A design and payoff description.,1
10.1086_681231.md,Study 1A – Contract condition,"Condition label: ‘Contracts’ in Table 1; random partners under contractual enforcement in phase 1, then unenforced.",1,"Enforcement type: contractual enforcement in phase 1 (automatic contract with 90% chance of 10-point penalty on agent for low quality, costless to client); no enforcement in phase 2.",Study 1A contract condition description.,1,True,Same participant pool and procedure as other Study 1A conditions.,1,True,Random assignment across four enforcement ‘markets’.,1,False,"Web-based, completed from any Internet-connected computer.",1,False,Human clients; agents are simulated but study overall is empirical.,1,False,No closed-form model only.,1,2,Each transaction is between one client and one agent.,1,11,8 rounds with contracts (phase 1) then 3 unenforced rounds (phase 2).,1,1,Client chooses any s in 0–10 hours.,1,0,All 10 points start with the client.,1,N/R,Bilateral trust game; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No communication channel described.,1,1,Clients see transaction results and cumulative earnings each round.,0.9,0,Contracts specify monetary penalty but there is no identity revelation associated with punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward or identity revelation.,1,0,Participants told up to 15 rounds; no evidence of exact remaining-round display.,0.8,1,Contract condition imposes a 10-point penalty on agent with 90% probability for low-quality service.,1,0,Penalty is ‘at no cost to the client’; agents are simulated and never incur actual penalties because they always fully cooperate.,0.8,10,Contract specifies penalty of 10 points on the agent for low-quality service.,1,0,No explicit extra reward mechanism beyond standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same investment game structure as other Study 1A conditions.,1,N/R,Mean hours per round are graphed but not given numerically as proportion of endowment.,0.5,N/R,Figure 1 only; no numeric table.,0.4,N/R,Efficiency not computed or reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff reported per condition.,1,USA,Same student sample as other Study 1A conditions at a U.S. university.,0.8,Mean 20.5 years (students),Study 1A reports overall sample age; conditions randomly assigned.,1,58 male; remaining mostly female (across all conditions),Study 1A provides aggregate gender counts only.,0.9,University students (undergraduate/college level),Described as students.,1,Online,Same web-based setup as other Study 1A conditions.,1,"Phase 1 rounds 1–8: random partner each round under an automatic contract; if agent chose low quality, they would incur a 10-point penalty with 90% probability (clients informed, agents simulated to always choose high quality). Phase 2 rounds 9–11: clients transferred to unenforced market with new random partners, no contracts.",Summarized from contract condition description and Table 1.,1
10.1086_681231.md,Study 1A – Fixed-partner condition,Condition label: ‘Fixed partner’ in Table 1; relational enforcement via repeated interaction with same agent in phase 1.,1,Enforcement type: relational enforcement via repeated interaction with a fixed partner in phase 1; no enforcement with random partners in phase 2.,Study 1A fixed-partner condition description.,1,True,Same Study 1A participants and procedures.,1,True,Random assignment to fixed-partner vs other enforcement conditions.,1,False,Conducted online rather than in a physical lab.,1,False,Human clients; agent decisions simulated but not a pure simulation study.,1,False,No closed-form analysis only.,1,2,One client and one agent per transaction.,1,11,8 fixed-partner rounds plus 3 unenforced rounds.,1,1,Client chooses any integer s between 0 and 10.,1,0,Client controls full 10-point endowment at start of each round.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication described between fixed partners.,1,1,Results screen shows quality delivered and earnings.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit rewards.,1,0,Same information structure as other Study 1A conditions.,0.8,0,"Relational enforcement via possibility of future payoffs, but no formal punishment mechanism or fees.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond exchange payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same game structure as other Study 1A markets.,1,N/R,Only plotted hours; no normalized values.,0.5,N/R,Exact average hours not tabulated.,0.4,N/R,Efficiency measures not reported.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoff per condition reported.,1,USA,Same Study 1A student pool.,0.8,Mean 20.5 years (students),Study 1A overall sample; assignment random.,1,58 male; remainder non-male across all Study 1A conditions,Only aggregate gender counts are provided.,0.9,University students (undergraduate/college level),Described as students.,1,Online,Same web-based procedure as other Study 1A conditions.,1,Phase 1 rounds 1–8: client is matched with the same agent each round (agent ID did not change). Agent is simulated and always provides high-quality service. Phase 2 rounds 9–11: client transferred to unenforced market with random partners and no enforcement.,From Study 1A description and Table 1 and 2 note.,1
10.1086_681231.md,Study 1A – Reputation condition,"Condition label: ‘Reputation’ in Table 1; random partners under reputational enforcement in phase 1, then unenforced.",1,Enforcement type: reputational enforcement in phase 1 (agents have reputation scores; clients can leave +/− feedback); no enforcement in phase 2.,Study 1A reputation condition description and Table 2.,1,True,Same Study 1A human subjects and design.,1,True,Enforcement type randomly assigned.,1,False,Participants play via custom website from any computer.,1,False,Human clients; agents simulated but not an agent-only simulation study.,1,False,No purely analytical treatment.,1,2,One client and one agent per transaction.,1,11,8 reputation rounds then 3 unenforced rounds.,1,1,Client chooses integer s between 0 and 10.,1,0,Client holds entire 10-point endowment initially.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,Only rating interface; no communication channel mentioned.,1,1,"Clients see transaction outcome, earnings, and agent’s reputation info (transactions completed and % positive feedback) in phase 1.",1,0,Feedback is about partner but no indication that real identities are revealed beyond anonymized agent IDs.,0.9,0,Same as punishment; + feedback not tied to real-world identities.,0.9,0,Participants told experiment lasts up to 15 rounds; not told exactly 11.,0.8,1,"Negative feedback can reduce future opportunities via reputation, functioning as reputational punishment.",1,0,Providing feedback is described as free: voluntary and at no real cost.,1,1,Each negative rating changes feedback score by −1 point (binary +1 or −1 feedback).,0.9,1,Positive feedback (+1) improves agent’s reputation and future prospects.,1,0,Feedback has no direct material cost to rater.,1,1,Each positive feedback adds +1 point to reputation score.,0.9,10,Same client endowment as other Study 1A conditions.,1,N/R,Only hours purchased are graphed.,0.5,N/R,No numeric average provided.,0.4,N/R,No efficiency statistics.,1,N/R,No aggregated payoffs by condition.,1,USA,Same Study 1A student sample at a U.S. campus.,0.8,Mean 20.5 years (students),Study 1A sample statistic.,1,58 male; remainder non-male across full sample,Only overall gender distribution given.,0.9,University students (undergraduate/college level),Described as students.,1,Online,Web-based experimental platform.,1,Phase 1 rounds 1–8: each agent has a reputation score shown as transactions completed and % positive feedback (values in Table 2). Clients can submit binary +/− feedback after each transaction. Agents preprogrammed to always provide high-quality service; two agents (rounds 4 and 6) had one negative point to test attention. Phase 2 rounds 9–11: clients moved to unenforced market without reputation information.,Synthesized from Study 1A text and Tables 1–2.,1
10.1086_681231.md,Study 1B – Unenforced condition,Study 1B includes an unenforced condition (N=21) for agents measuring trustworthiness.,1,"Enforcement type: unenforced (no contracts, no reputation, no relational enforcement) in all rounds.",Study 1B design describes this as the unenforced baseline.,1,True,Human participants in agent role making payoff-relevant decisions.,1,True,Random assignment to four enforcement conditions.,1,False,Study 1B executed entirely online over up to 10 days.,1,False,Agents are human subjects in this study (playing agent role).,1,False,Not a formal analytical model.,1,2,Investment game between one client and one agent.,1,23,Instructions: up to 30 transactions; each agent completed 20 transactions (phase 1) plus 3 unenforced phase-2 transactions (rounds 21–23).,1,1,Agents choose any integer h with 1 ≤ h ≤ 10 hours of high-quality service (low-quality is residual 10−h).,1,0,Endowment is on client side; agent chooses costly high-quality hours. No initial allocation of agent resources to client.,0.6,N/R,"Bilateral trust game, not a public goods game.",1,0,No communication described; only decisions via web interface.,1,1,Agents see their net earnings from the transaction immediately after choosing h.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit rewards beyond payoffs.,1,0,"Agents told experiment would last up to 30 transactions, not exactly 23; no indication remaining rounds shown.",0.8,0,Unenforced condition has no contracts or reputation.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,10,Each client chooses whether to hire agent for 10 hours of service; effectively 10-hour ‘endowment’ of potential service.,1,N/R,Trustworthiness measured as hours of high-quality service; no normalized fraction reported.,0.6,N/R,Figure 2 gives trajectories but not numeric averages.,0.4,N/R,No efficiency statistic reported.,1,N/R,Group-level earnings not tabulated.,1,USA,Same general campus setting as Study 1A; specific university not named but consistent with U.S. context.,0.8,Mean 20.04 years (students),"Study 1B: 83 participants, 39 men, 20.04 years old.",1,39 male; remaining mostly female (total N=83),Study 1B sample description.,1,University students (undergraduate/college level),Recruited as students similarly to Study 1A.,1,Online,All transactions took place online; server notified via e-mail.,1,"Agents play 20 rounds in phase 1 and 3 rounds in phase 2. In unenforced condition, clients are simulated and always hire the agent for 10 hours (for phase 2 they are explicitly hired every round). Agent chooses h hours of high-quality service; payoffs: agent earns h points for high-quality hours and 3(10−h) for low-quality hours; client earns 2h from high-quality and h from low-quality.",Summarized from Study 1B design description.,1
10.1086_681231.md,Study 1B – Contract condition,Described as ‘contracts condition (N=18)’ in Study 1B.,1,Enforcement type: contractual enforcement in phase 1 (90% enforcement; agent pays 4-point penalty per hour of low-quality service if enforced); unenforced in phase 2.,"Study 1B contracts condition: ‘high-quality service was enforced with 90% probability, which cost the agent 4 points for each hour of low-quality service delivered.’",1,True,Same Study 1B human subjects.,1,True,Random allocation to contractual vs other enforcement types.,1,False,"Web-based, multi-day online experiment.",1,False,Participants play agent role; clients simulated but the overall study is empirical.,1,False,No closed-form model only.,1,2,"One client, one agent per transaction.",1,23,20 rounds under contracts plus 3 unenforced rounds.,1,1,Agent can select any integer 1–10 of high-quality hours.,1,0,No pre-committed high-quality hours.,0.6,N/R,Not a public good.,1,0,No communication beyond system messages.,1,1,Agents see their own earnings per transaction and know enforcement status via messages (penalties).,0.8,0,Contractual sanctions apply but identities are anonymous.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identity revelation.,1,0,They were told the experiment would last up to 30 transactions; remaining rounds not specified.,0.8,1,Contract condition imposes monetary penalties for low-quality service.,1,4,Agent pays 4 points per hour of low-quality service when enforcement occurs.,1,4,Penalty magnitude per hour of low-quality service is 4 points; per ‘unit’ of shirking hour.,0.9,0,No additional reward mechanism beyond higher payoffs from low-quality service.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,10,Clients hire for 10 hours; trust game structure same across conditions.,1,N/R,Trustworthiness measured in hours; no normalized rate reported.,0.6,N/R,No numeric averages presented.,0.4,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff values.,1,USA,Same Study 1B student pool.,0.8,Mean 20.04 years (students),Study 1B description.,1,39 male; remainder non-male across Study 1B sample,Study 1B provides aggregate gender only.,0.9,University students (undergraduate/college level),Described as students.,1,Online,All Study 1B transactions conducted via web over email prompts.,1,"Phase 1: contracts enforce high-quality service with 90% probability; if low quality is delivered, agent loses 4 points per hour of low-quality service when enforced. Clients are simulated and hire for 10 hours when matched. Phase 2: all agents play 3 unenforced rounds where they are always hired, with no sanctions.",Synthesized from Study 1B contract condition description.,1
10.1086_681231.md,Study 1B – Fixed-partner condition,Study 1B includes a fixed-partner condition (N=23) for relational enforcement on the agent side.,1,Enforcement type: relational enforcement via a fixed client partner in phase 1 with probabilistic future rejection based on prior trustworthiness; unenforced in phase 2.,Study 1B: fixed-partner condition description with probability of client withholding cooperation next round inversely related to h.,1,True,Human agents; clients simulated but experiment overall is empirical.,1,True,Random assignment to fixed-partner vs other conditions.,1,False,Online over up to 10 days.,1,False,Human subjects.,1,False,Behavioral experiment.,1,2,Agent interacts with a single client partner (simulated) over time.,1,23,"20 rounds in phase 1, 3 rounds in phase 2.",1,1,Agent chooses any h between 1 and 10.,1,0,Agent’s high-quality effort is fully discretionary.,0.6,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication interface described between fixed partners.,1,1,Agent sees own earnings and presumably whether the client hired them again.,0.7,0,Rejection is from same partner but identities are anonymous; no explicit identity revelation.,0.8,0,Continuing to be hired is a relational reward but no explicit identity revealed.,0.8,0,Only ‘up to 30 transactions’ stated.,0.8,1,Client can withhold cooperation in next round (probabilistic rejection) based on lower h; this functions as punishment.,1,0,"Client is simulated; from agent perspective, punishment costs future earning opportunity but no explicit point expenditure by client.",0.7,10,"If agent is rejected in a round, they effectively lose entire 10-hour earning opportunity that round; operationally functions as loss of up to 10h earnings. Using 10 as approximate magnitude per ‘rejection event’.",0.3,1,"Providing more high-quality hours increases chance of continued hiring, rewarding trustworthiness.",1,0,No point cost to client for rewarding via continued hiring.,0.7,10,Being rehired grants up to 10 hours of earning opportunity; magnitude approximated per hiring decision.,0.3,10,Clients hire for 10 hours when they hire at all.,1,N/R,Trustworthiness measured as hours of high-quality service.,0.6,N/R,No table of mean high-quality hours.,0.4,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff summary.,1,USA,Same Study 1B student context.,0.8,Mean 20.04 years (students),Study 1B overall sample.,1,39 male; remainder non-male across Study 1B,Only aggregated gender given.,0.9,University students (undergraduate/college level),Described as students.,1,Online,Same as other Study 1B conditions.,1,"Phase 1: agents interact repeatedly with same simulated client. After receiving h hours of high-quality service, client may hire zero hours next round with probability 1−(h/10); e.g., after h=7, probability of 0 next round is 0.3. Thus lower trustworthiness leads to higher chance of losing next-round trade. Phase 2: agents are always hired in each of 3 unenforced rounds with new partners; no rejection mechanism.",From Study 1B fixed-partner condition description.,1
10.1086_681231.md,Study 1B – Reputation condition,Study 1B describes a reputation condition (N=21) with reputation scores and probabilistic client rejection.,1,Enforcement type: reputational enforcement in phase 1 (reputation score aggregating positive and negative feedback; clients probabilistically reject based on most recent feedback); unenforced in phase 2.,Study 1B reputation condition description.,1,True,Human agents choose high-quality hours; reputation and client responses simulated.,1,True,Randomized enforcement conditions.,1,False,Conducted online via custom website and email notifications.,1,False,Human participants are core decision-makers.,1,False,"Reports behavioral data, not only analytical derivations.",1,2,Agent matched with one client per round.,1,23,20 reputation rounds plus 3 unenforced rounds.,1,1,Agent selects any integer h between 1 and 10.,1,0,Agent decides how many of 10 hours to make high-quality.,0.6,N/R,"Bilateral trustworthiness task, not a PGG.",1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,1,1,Agents see their reputation score and are notified of rejections via email.,0.9,0,Feedback and rejection relate to anonymous partners; no identity revelation.,0.9,0,Positive feedback and acceptance are anonymous.,0.9,0,Agents were told ‘up to 30’ transactions; remaining rounds not displayed.,0.8,1,Negative feedback reduces reputation; clients more likely to reject after negative rating (65% rejection vs 5% after positive).,1,0,Feedback is generated by system with probabilities; no point cost to rater/client.,1,1,Each negative rating yields −1 feedback point (since each trial gives either a positive or a negative point).,0.9,1,Positive feedback and lower rejection probability reward high trustworthiness.,1,0,Positive feedback has no direct cost to rater.,1,1,Each hour of high-quality service gives a positive point with 10% probability (per hour); net effect is additional positive feedback points.,0.7,10,Client chooses whether to hire for 10 hours of service.,1,N/R,Trustworthiness is number of high-quality hours; fraction relative to 10 not numerically summarized.,0.6,N/R,No numeric mean high-quality hours.,0.4,N/R,Not computed.,1,N/R,Group payoff per condition not reported.,1,USA,Same Study 1B student context.,0.8,Mean 20.04 years (students),Study 1B sample statistic.,1,39 male; remainder non-male across full Study 1B,Aggregate only.,0.9,University students (undergraduate/college level),Participants are students.,1,Online,Same as other Study 1B conditions.,1,"Agents start with a null reputation. For each hour of high-quality service rendered, they receive a positive feedback point with 10% probability; otherwise a negative point. Client rejection is based on most recent feedback: after negative rating, agent is rejected with 65% probability; after positive rating, with 5% probability. After three rejections, agents forfeit the entire exchange round; fewer than 2% of rounds were forfeited. Phase 2: all agents are hired each round; no reputation or rejection effects.",From Study 1B reputation condition description.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0076063.md,Spatial inspection game – square lattice (baseline Monte Carlo simulation),"Only one main simulation setup is described: agents on an L×L square lattice with k=4 neighbors, varying parameters α, β, γ and sometimes λ; treated as a single simulation family.",0.75,"Relative inspection cost α, relative temptation β, relative inspection incentive γ, and rewiring probability λ (network topology). This row represents the general parameterized simulation, not a specific parameter point.","The model systematically varies α, β, γ (phase diagrams in Fig. 2 and cross-sections in Fig. 3) and λ (Fig. 5). No distinct experimental conditions beyond these continuous parameters are defined.",0.95,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on networks; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with participants; it is a computational simulation study.,1,False,No physical lab or human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Methods section explicitly describes Monte Carlo simulations of the spatial inspection game.,1,False,"While some limiting cases are discussed qualitatively, there is no formal analytical model with closed-form derivations as the primary method.",0.7,40000,"Methods: simulations on square lattices with L between 200 and 1600. The smallest explicitly mentioned concrete size for figures using periodic grid with rewiring is 400×400; 400×400 = 160000. However, no single canonical L is fixed; choosing 400×400 would be arbitrary. To still provide a numeric, take an illustrative mid-range: 200×200=40000, acknowledging this is approximate.",0.2,1000000,Methods: ‘Equilibration in a statistical sense takes up to 10^6 full rounds of the game.’ Use 10^6 as the characteristic number of iterations.,0.8,1,"Agents do not choose contribution levels to a public good; they choose discrete strategies (C, O, P). The all-or-nothing vs. continuous contribution distinction does not apply; encoded as 1 because there is no constrained contribution grid.",0.3,N/A,No human subjects and no endowment/contribution structure; field is not applicable.,1,N/R,This is not a public goods game; no multiplier or MPCR is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; simulation only.,1,N/A,No interface to human subjects; information presentation is irrelevant.,1,N/A,"No humans, no identities to reveal.",1,N/A,"No humans, no identities to reveal.",1,N/A,No user interface; agents do not observe total rounds.,1,1,Inspectors impose a fine f on criminals; this is a punishment mechanism.,1,N/R,Inspectors pay inspection cost c; punishment cost per unit of fine is not parameterized separately as in standard public goods punishment; no clear mapping to ‘per punishment unit’.,0.3,N/R,"Model specifies fines f and dimensionless parameters α=c/f, β=g/f, γ=r/f but not a punishment technology in terms of payoff change per cost unit as used in public goods punishment frameworks.",0.3,1,Inspectors receive a reward r from catching criminals; described as ‘reward r ≥ 0’.,1,N/R,Rewards r come from fines or subsidies; there is no explicit cost parameter for giving rewards per unit in the sense used in public goods reward games.,0.3,N/R,Technology of mapping inspector action to others’ payoffs is model-specific but not given as a simple per-unit reward multiple suitable for this field.,0.3,N/A,No endowed tokens or budgets for agents; they only receive payoffs from interactions.,1,N/R,No contributions to a public good; main outcomes are stationary fractions (densities) of strategies and ‘crime rate’ (density of criminals).,1,N/R,No contribution amounts are defined in the model.,1,N/R,Paper reports phase diagrams and densities but does not compute efficiency as payoff relative to maximum.,0.7,N/R,Aggregate payoff statistics are not reported; focus is on strategy densities and phase transitions.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely computational (Monte Carlo simulations) with no human or field data.,1,"Three-strategy spatial inspection game on L×L lattice (or regular small-world/random network with rewiring probability λ). Strategies: criminals (C), punishing inspectors (P), ordinary individuals (O). Payoffs per player depend on numbers of neighbor types: π_O = −β N_C, π_P = γ N_C − α, π_C = β N_O + (β − 1) N_P, where α=c/f (relative inspection cost), β=g/f (relative temptation), γ=r/f (relative inspection incentive). Agents update strategies by Fermi imitation rule with noise parameter K=0.5, interacting with k=4 nearest neighbors and periodic boundary conditions. Initial conditions: uniform random distribution of C, O, P at 1/3 each.","Summarized from ‘Spatial Inspection Game’ and ‘Methods’ sections: lattice, neighborhood, payoffs, parameters, update rule, initial fractions.",1
