10.1038_srep21104.md,"Simulation – Square lattice, p=0, δ=0, r/c=3, γ_max=α_max=β_max=1","Distinct parameter combination described in Fig. 3(a): square lattice without punishment (p=0), δ=0, r/c=3, and default upper limits γ_max=α_max=β_max=1.",0.9,Punishment probability p=0; system bias δ=0; resource-to-cost ratio r/c=3; topology=square lattice; γ_max=1; α_max=1; β_max=1,These parameters are explicitly stated for Fig. 3(a) in the Results and Methods sections.,0.95,False,"Study is an evolutionary game simulation with agents on graphs, no human participants.",1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational simulations.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; simulations only.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on networks as described in Methods.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model; results are numerical.,1,10000,Methods: typical system size contains N=10^4–10^5 nodes; choose lower bound 10^4 as representative since no specific N per condition is given.,0.5,10000,Methods: stationary frequencies determined by averaging over 10^4 MC generations in the stationary state; treat as effective number of generations.,0.6,1,"Agents do not choose a contribution amount; they decide to claim or not, which is a binary choice, but the schema defines 1 as 'can contribute any amount' vs 0 'all-or-nothing contribution'; this is not a contribution game, so use 1 to avoid mislabeling strict all-or-nothing contributions.",0.3,N/A,No public-goods contribution or endowment allocation; resource competition game with claiming decisions only.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No communication between agents is described in the simulation rules.,1,0,Agents only observe neighbors’ payoffs indirectly via imitation; no explicit summaries shown.,0.8,0,"Punishment is implemented as parameter changes, not as an identifiable source; no identities revealed.",0.9,0,"No explicit rewards exist in the model, only resources from contests.",1,0,Agents are not described as knowing the horizon or MC steps.,0.8,0,Condition explicitly described as 'without punishment (p=0)'; punishment step is inactive.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism active at p=0.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism active at p=0.,1,0,"Model includes only punishment of deception, not explicit prosocial rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/R,Agents do not have a fixed endowment of tokens; they gain r or pay cost c per conflict.,1,N/R,"No contribution decisions; outcomes are overconfidence and bluffing levels, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decisions; resource claiming is binary without quantities.,1,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is not reported or computed.,0.9,N/R,Only individual payoffs and deception levels are discussed; no aggregate payoff reported numerically.,0.9,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,No human,Study is a Monte Carlo simulation on networks with artificial agents.,1,"Resource competition game on a square lattice with periodic boundary conditions. Each agent i has fixed real capability γ_i∈[0,1], evolving overconfidence α_i∈[0,1], and bluffing β_i∈[0,1]. Agents decide whether to claim a resource r based on self-perceived capability k_i=γ_i+α_i and perceived opponent displaying capability m_j=γ_j+β_j plus system bias δ. If both claim, each pays cost c and higher γ wins r−c while loser gets −c. Evolution via imitation with Fermi rule (K=0.1); no punishment step used in this configuration (p=0).",Summarized from Methods and figure caption for Fig. 3(a).,1
10.1038_srep21104.md,"Simulation – Square lattice, p=1, δ=1, r/c=3, γ_max=α_max=β_max=1","Distinct condition described in Fig. 3(b): square lattice with punishment (p=1), δ=1, r/c=3, γ_max=α_max=β_max=1.",0.9,Punishment probability p=1; system bias δ=1; resource-to-cost ratio r/c=3; topology=square lattice; γ_max=1; α_max=1; β_max=1,Parameters explicitly given in Fig. 3(b) caption and Methods.,0.95,False,No human subjects; computational agents only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; simulations of an evolutionary game.,1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on networks.,1,False,No formal analytic solution presented.,1,10000,Methods specify N=10^4–10^5; use 10^4 as a representative size.,0.5,10000,Stationary values averaged over 10^4 MC generations.,0.6,1,Model does not involve graded contributions; classification as contribution type is not directly applicable. Keep consistent with other conditions as 1.,0.3,N/A,No endowment allocation between public and private accounts.,1,N/R,Not a public goods setup; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication channel between agents discussed.,1,0,Agents use only local payoff comparisons; no explicit summaries shown.,0.8,0,Punishment is an automatic rule; no agent identities or punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,No explicit rewards; only resource payoffs and punishment of deception.,1,0,No indication agents know time horizon.,0.8,1,Condition uses punishment with probability p=1; punishment mechanism is active.,1,0,Punishment reduces α_i and β_i to minimum values but does not reduce material payoff or consume tokens; treat cost per punishment action as 0 in payoff terms.,0.7,1,"Punishment fully resets deception to minimum (α_i=ε_α, β_i=ε_β) when applied; code as unit intensity effect.",0.6,0,"Model contains no explicit reward mechanism, only resource acquisition and punishment of deception.",1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/R,Agents do not have an endowment; they accrue payoffs through resource contests and costs c.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are overconfidence and bluffing levels, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,"Paper reports qualitative changes in overconfidence and bluffing, not efficiency metrics.",0.9,N/R,No numeric group payoff results are given.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational simulation.,1,"Same resource competition game as in the p=0 condition, but with punishment: when two agents claim and the lower-γ agent loses, that loser is punished with probability p=1, resetting its overconfidence α_i and bluffing β_i to the current population minima (ε_α, ε_β). Otherwise, agents may imitate neighbors’ deception profiles with probability given by a Fermi rule W=(1+exp[(P_i−P_j)/K])^{-1}, K=0.1. Topology is a square lattice with periodic boundary conditions.",Summarized from Methods and Fig. 3(b) description.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2012.10.011.md,"Baseline – unrestricted punishment, full information",Paper defines a Baseline treatment with unrestricted punishment and full information; treated as one condition because all groups share identical rules.,1,Punishment nature: unrestricted; Feedback: full (all individual contributions shown).,Section 3.2 describes treatments varying punishment restrictions (unrestricted vs restricted) and feedback (full vs partial). This row captures the Baseline combination.,1,True,"Laboratory experiment with 168 human subjects total, with 56 in Baseline.",1,True,Controlled lab manipulation of punishment rules and feedback across randomized groups.,1,True,Conducted at CEEL Lab of the University of Trento.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical model as a separate study; lab data are primary.,1,4,Groups of size N = 4 in all treatments.,1,20,Participants played T = 20 periods in all treatments.,1,1,"Subjects choose any g_i ≤ 20, implying continuous/any-amount contributions rather than binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each participant receives endowment and decides how much to invest in public project; endowment initially in private account.,1,0.4,Payoff formula π_i = (20 - g_i) + 0.4 Σ g_j; MPCR is 0.4.,1,0,No communication or chat mentioned; standard VCM without communication.,0.9,1,"After stage 1, subjects in Baseline observe all other individual contributions and their own earnings; also see punishment points assigned/received implicitly as part of feedback.",0.8,0,Punishment is anonymous; subjects do not know identity of punisher.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Game explicitly described as finitely repeated with T = 20; standard design reveals horizon; no indication that number of rounds was hidden.,0.8,1,Stage 2 allows costly assignment of punishment points.,1,1,Costs given in Table 2; cost for 1 punishment point is 1 token. Reporting marginal cost of first point as representative unit.,1,0.1,Each point received reduces earnings at stage 1 by 10%.,1,0,"Only costly punishment, no positive points or rewards mentioned.",1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Each participant receives fixed amount e = 20 tokens each period.,1,N/R,"Paper reports contributions in tokens (0–20) and means, not normalized rates (0–1). Rate could be computed but only group means table is given, not overall exact mean by treatment.",0.3,8.42,Table 3 gives mean contributions by group; Baseline column mean is 8.42 tokens.,1,N/R,Average earnings by treatment shown only graphically (Fig. 4) without exact numbers; cannot compute ratio to maximum 32 tokens without precise values.,0.2,N/R,Only average individual earnings per treatment are discussed qualitatively; no exact group payoff levels reported.,0.2,"Italy (majority), mixed student sample",80.3% Italians; remaining are other nationalities; experiment run in Italy.,0.8,Undergraduate students; exact age distribution N/R,Subjects described as undergraduate students; no ages reported.,0.7,49.5% female (overall sample),Overall sample is 49.5% female; not broken down by treatment.,0.8,Undergraduate university students (64.3% economics majors overall),All subjects are undergraduates; 64.3% from economics; no treatment splits.,0.8,On site,Conducted in CEEL Lab with subjects seated at terminals; standard physical lab setting.,1,"Partner protocol with fixed groups of 4 for 20 periods; anonymous and changing numeric labels each period; payoff function π_i = (20 - g_i) + 0.4 Σ g_j; after contributions in stage 1, subjects see all individual contributions of group members; in stage 2, each subject may assign 0–10 punishment points to each other group member at convex costs (Table 2), each point reducing the recipient’s stage-1 earnings by 10% up to 100%; punishment is anonymous; unrestricted: any subject may punish any other subject regardless of relative contribution levels.",Compiled from detailed design description in sections 3.1 and 3.2.1.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2012.10.011.md,"Full R. – restricted (legitimate) punishment, full information",Full R. treatment has restricted punishment with full information; treated as single condition.,1,Punishment nature: restricted (legitimate; only higher contributors can punish lower contributors); Feedback: full (all individual contributions shown).,Section 3.2.2 defines Full R. as restricted punishment with same feedback as Baseline (whole contribution vector).,1,True,Human subjects in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory treatment varying punishment rules and feedback.,1,True,"Run at CEEL Lab, University of Trento.",1,False,No non-human simulation study.,1,False,No separate analytical-only model used as experiment.,1,4,Groups of size N = 4.,1,20,All treatments use T = 20 periods.,1,1,Same contribution decision as Baseline: choose any g_i ≤ 20.,1,0,Endowment is private initially; contributions are voluntary.,1,0.4,Stage-1 payoff formula identical to Baseline: π_i = (20 - g_i) + 0.4 Σ g_j.,1,0,No communication opportunity described.,0.9,1,Full R. is stated to have the same feedback as Baseline: vector of all other members’ individual contributions plus one’s own earnings.,0.9,0,"Punishment mechanism described as anonymous, as in Baseline.",1,0,No rewards available.,1,1,"Same finitely repeated design with T = 20, presumably common knowledge.",0.8,1,"Stage 2 with punishment options is present, with restrictions.",1,1,Cost function (Table 2) identical across treatments; cost of first punishment point = 1 token.,1,0.1,Each punishment point received reduces stage-1 earnings by 10%.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,20,Stage 1 in legitimacy-based treatments is exactly the same as Baseline (e = 20 tokens).,1,N/R,Only token contributions reported; normalized rates not directly given.,0.3,14.53,Table 3 shows overall mean contribution in Full R. column as 14.53 tokens.,1,N/R,"Text states Full R. has significantly higher earnings, but no numerical average efficiency ratios reported.",0.3,N/R,"Only relative comparisons of average earnings are provided, no exact payoffs by treatment.",0.2,"Italy (majority), mixed student sample","Same subject pool as Baseline: 80.3% Italians, experiments at Italian university.",0.8,Undergraduate students; exact age distribution N/R,Subjects are undergraduates; no ages given by treatment.,0.7,49.5% female (overall sample),Global sample gender split given; not split by treatment.,0.8,Undergraduate university students (64.3% economics majors overall),Same pool description applies across treatments.,0.8,On site,Conducted in physical lab with terminals.,1,"Partner protocol with fixed groups of 4 across 20 periods; anonymous changing IDs each period. Stage 1 identical to Baseline (choose g_i ∈ [0,20]; payoff π_i = (20 − g_i) + 0.4 Σ g_j). Stage 2: legitimacy-based restriction, player i can punish player j only if g_i > g_j in that period; antisocial punishment (low punishing high or equal contributors) is not allowed. Costs of punishment points and payoff reduction from received points as in Baseline. Feedback after stage 1: vector of all individual contributions of other group members (full information). Punishment remains anonymous.",Summarized from legitimacy-based treatment description in section 3.2.2.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2012.10.011.md,"Partial R. – restricted (legitimate) punishment, partial information",Partial R. treatment has restricted punishment and partial information feedback; treated as one condition.,1,Punishment nature: restricted (legitimate; only higher contributors can punish lower contributors); Feedback: partial (only average group contribution and contributions of less virtuous peers shown).,Section 3.2.2 defines Partial R. as restricted punishment with partial information about others’ contributions.,1,True,Human-subjects lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment and feedback in a lab.,1,True,Conducted at CEEL Lab.,1,False,No non-human simulation described.,1,False,No separate analytical experiment.,1,4,Groups of size N = 4.,1,20,All treatments use 20 periods.,1,1,Contribution decision identical: choose any g_i ≤ 20.,1,0,Endowment is private at start; voluntary contributions.,1,0.4,Stage-1 payoff function same in all treatments.,1,0,No communication mentioned.,0.9,1,"After stage 1, subjects see average contribution of group plus individual contributions of peers who contributed strictly less than them, which constitutes feedback about others’ behavior.",0.9,0,Punishment anonymity stated generally; not relaxed in this treatment.,1,0,No reward scheme present.,1,1,Same finitely repeated design (T = 20) as other treatments; no suggestion that horizon is hidden.,0.8,1,Stage 2 punishment options exist under legitimacy constraints.,1,1,Uses same convex cost schedule for points (Table 2); cost of one point is 1 token.,1,0.1,Each point received reduces stage-1 earnings by 10%.,1,0,No rewards allowed.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Stage 1 identical to Baseline: e = 20 tokens.,1,N/R,Only token contributions reported; normalized rates not provided.,0.3,8.33,Table 3 shows overall mean contribution in Partial R. column as 8.33 tokens.,1,N/R,Fig. 4 shows average earnings but without exact numeric values; efficiency ratio not explicitly reported.,0.3,N/R,"No exact group payoff statistics reported, only relative comparisons.",0.2,"Italy (majority), mixed student sample",Same subject pool for all three treatments.,0.8,Undergraduate students; exact age distribution N/R,Subjects are all undergraduates; ages not given.,0.7,49.5% female (overall sample),Only overall gender split reported.,0.8,Undergraduate university students (64.3% economics majors overall),No treatment-specific education breakdown; same pool.,0.8,On site,In-lab experiment at CEEL Lab.,1,"Partner protocol with fixed groups of 4 across 20 periods; anonymous changing numeric labels between rounds. Stage 1 identical to Baseline: each subject receives 20 tokens and chooses g_i ∈ [0,20]; payoff π_i = (20 − g_i) + 0.4 Σ g_j. Stage 2: same legitimacy-based restriction as Full R., player i can punish j only if g_i > g_j in that period. Punishment uses convex cost schedule (Table 2), and each point received reduces the recipient’s stage-1 earnings by 10% up to 100%. Feedback after stage 1 is partial: each subject is informed only about the average contribution level of the group and the specific contribution levels of co-players who contributed strictly less than them; no information about contributions of more virtuous peers.",Summarized from description of Partial R. in section 3.2.2.,1
10.1023_a_1023952115422.md,One-Time treatment – all punishment scenarios (including no-punishment baseline),Paper describes a One-Time treatment where each subject makes 20 allocation decisions under 19 probability×severity punishment combinations plus one baseline with zero probability; all share the same institutional environment and only punishment parameters vary.,0.95,"Punishment probability (0, 0.1–0.9) and punishment severity (0,1–4) for a given scenario; One-Time (one-shot style, no feedback) regulatory regime.","Design systematically varies probability and severity across 20 scenarios; One-Time vs Repeated is the key treatment-level variable, with this row capturing the One-Time regime.",0.96,True,Human undergraduate subjects recruited for lab sessions.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with imposed payoff structure and treatments.,1,True,Conducted in experimental lab/cubicles at College of William and Mary.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,No formal analytical model as a standalone study; focus is on experimental data.,1,10,Each group consists of ten subjects sharing the group account.,1,20,Each subject makes decisions for 20 scenarios (19 punishment combinations + 1 baseline).,1,1,Subjects can split the $5 endowment between group and private account in any penny increment.,1,1,Design frames as controlling $5 already in group account; default location is public until moved to private.,0.9,0.2,"Money left in group account doubled and divided among 10, so each dollar yields $2/10 = 0.20 to each player (w/v since v=1).",1,0,Subjects seated in cubicles and explicitly instructed not to communicate.,1,0,"During One-Time decisions, no information about others’ decisions is provided; only one scenario is realized for payment at the end.",0.9,1,Punished subjects selected by drawing lettered tiles (A–J) and letters announced to group; identities in sense of experimental ID are public.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in this experiment.,1,1,Instructions clearly state there are 20 rounds and show a 20-row record sheet.,1,1,Financial penalty (earnings adjustment) applied in 19 of 20 scenarios; baseline has zero probability but mechanism is part of treatment.,1,1,Penalty equals severity s times amount moved to private; effectively 1 token of private income sacrificed per 1 unit of severity per token punished.,0.9,1,Each dollar shifted to private reduces expected private earnings by p*s per dollar; punishment directly subtracts s dollars per dollar when audited. Interpreted as 1 unit of punishment per 1 unit of private income docked (linear).,0.7,0,No positive sanction or reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,5,Each subject controls $5 from the $50 group account each round; the $15 private balance is not investable in the game.,1,0.764,"Overall actual earnings $22.91 vs socially optimal $25 implies average contribution of $3.64 to group account (25−22.91=2.09 group payoff shortfall; since group payoff = 10+avg_contrib, solve 10+avg_contrib=22.91−15=7.91? This simple back-out is muddied by punishment; therefore we instead use Table 2 statement: when punishment factor >0.8, average contribution ≈90% of maximum. Given heterogeneity across factors and One-Time vs Repeated, any single normalized rate is approximate. Here we approximate 0.764 using Back-of-envelope from Table B-1 averages divided by $5; treat as indicative, not precise.",0.2,N/R,"Paper reports various averages by punishment factor and severity, but not a single pooled mean contribution for all One-Time scenarios.",0.1,N/R,Overall efficiency (realized total payoff / first-best without punishment) not directly reported and cannot be reliably computed due to stochastic punishments and $15 buffers.,0.1,N/R,Only individual average earnings reported ($22.91 across all treatments); not decomposed by One-Time vs Repeated or by condition.,0.3,United States,"Subjects recruited from College of William and Mary, a U.S. institution.",0.95,N/R,Described only as undergraduates; no age statistics given.,0.5,Mixed; exact proportions N/R,Gender used as a regression covariate (Female dummy) but sample composition not reported.,0.7,Undergraduate students,Subjects recruited from undergraduate courses at the College of William and Mary.,1,On site,Conducted with subjects physically present in lab cubicles; no indication of online or field implementation.,1,"Voluntary Contribution Mechanism with negatively framed contribution (moving funds to private account is framed as generating a negative externality). Group account initial $50, each of 10 players controls $5. Group account is doubled then evenly split. Private account starts with $15 buffer not investable. Punishment: with probability p a subset of players is audited; if selected, earnings adjustment subtracts severity s times amount moved to private. Only one of the 20 scenarios is randomly selected for payment at the end, so decisions are strategically independent across scenarios.",Summarized from Sections 3–4 and Appendix A description of the institution.,1
10.1023_a_1023952115422.md,Repeated treatment – all punishment scenarios (including no-punishment baseline),Paper describes a Repeated treatment where the same 20 scenarios are implemented one per period with feedback; this row captures that regulatory regime as distinct from One-Time.,0.95,"Punishment probability (0, 0.1–0.9) and punishment severity (0,1–4) for a given scenario; Repeated (feedback, round-by-round) regulatory regime.","Design varies probability and severity across the same 20 scenarios, but now with repeated play, audit and contribution feedback each round.",0.96,True,Human undergraduate subjects took part in lab sessions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomization of punishment selection and fixed treatment protocols.,1,True,Conducted in person at the College of William and Mary with cubicles and record sheets.,1,False,No non-human simulation reported.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model used as standalone evidence.,1,10,Same 10-person group VCM structure as in One-Time treatment.,1,20,Subjects make one allocation decision in each of 20 rounds; a 20-sided die picks the paying round.,1,1,Subjects choose any split of the $5 in one-cent increments between group and private accounts.,1,1,"As in One-Time, subjects control $5 of a $50 group account, which is conceptually in the group account unless moved to private.",0.9,0.2,"Institutional returns identical to One-Time: group dollars doubled then divided by 10, private dollars 1-for-1.",1,0,Instructions forbid discussion of decisions with any other participant.,1,1,"Between rounds, experimenters announce the group account amount (after doubling) each period and subjects record it.",1,1,"Each round the number of people to have earnings adjusted is known, letters of selected players are drawn from Scrabble tiles in front of subjects; selected IDs are common knowledge.",0.95,0,No rewards are implemented.,1,1,Instructions explicitly mention exactly 20 rounds corresponding to the 20-sided die; record sheet lists all 20.,1,1,Earnings adjustments (punishments) occur with positive probability in 19 of 20 rounds; no punishment only in baseline round with 0 adjustees.,1,1,"If audited, subject subtracts severity s times dollars moved to private; so each unit of s costs 1 private dollar per dollar allocated when selected.",0.9,1,Linear proportional penalty to extent of free riding; one unit of punishment directly reduces private payoff by $1 per $1 allocated (scaled by s).,0.7,0,Only negative monetary adjustments are present.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,5,"Each round, each player controls $5 from the group account; $15 in private account is a buffer and cannot be invested.",1,N/R,"Paper reports averages by punishment factor and some regression results; no single normalized contribution rate over all scenarios for Repeated treatment is provided, and combining heterogeneous factors would be misleading.",0.2,N/R,"Average contributions for Repeated treatment are only reported conditional on ranges of punishment factors or on specific probability×severity cells, not as a single pooled mean.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency or welfare ratio is reported for Repeated treatment; stochastic punishment and $15 buffers make it nontrivial to reconstruct.,0.1,N/R,Earnings reported as overall average $22.91 across all subjects and treatments; not separated for Repeated sessions.,0.3,United States,Subjects are undergraduates at a U.S. university (College of William and Mary).,0.95,N/R,Only educational status (undergraduate) given; no age distribution statistics.,0.5,Mixed; exact proportions N/R,Female dummy used in regressions but underlying counts not specified.,0.7,Undergraduate students,Subjects recruited from undergraduate courses.,1,On site,Repeated sessions held in the same physical lab with scrabble tile draws and public announcements.,1,"Repeated VCM with 20 periods and feedback. After each period, experimenters announce the total group account amount (after doubling) and publicly draw Scrabble tiles to determine which players have earnings adjusted. Variables such as Selected Last Period, Punished Last Period, Ever Selected, Ever Punished, and Group Account Last Period are tracked and used in random-effects tobit regressions, indicating that subjects know their audit and punishment history and see the group contribution history.","Summarized from Sections 3–5 and Appendix A, plus regression variable descriptions in Tables 3 and 4.",1
10.1037_pspi0000389.md,Global meta-analysis of Prisoner's Dilemma and Public Goods Game studies,"The paper is a meta-analysis aggregating 1,506 prior studies rather than reporting a new primary experiment; all cooperation estimates are pooled into one meta-analytic dataset.",1,"Study-level structural characteristics (e.g., K index, group size, communication, repetitions, number of choice options, decision protocol, sanctions, symmetry) and society-level indicators (formal institutions, kin-based institutions, religion, values, trust, ecologies, subsistence style).",The meta-regressions treat these as predictors of cooperation across the collected studies and societies.,1,True,The meta-analysis summarizes empirical studies with human participants in PD and PGG paradigms.,1,True,All included primary studies are controlled experiments using economic games; the meta-analysis itself analyzes experimental results.,1,True,"The majority of included studies are laboratory experiments; some field/lab-in-field/online are also included, but the overall corpus is predominantly lab-based.",0.6,False,No agent-based or purely computational simulations are analyzed; only human experiments.,1,False,"Paper reports statistical meta-analysis, not formal analytical game-theoretic derivations as a primary contribution.",1,3.5,Median group size reported: 'ranged from 2 to 324 participants interacting together... (Mdn = 3.5)'.,0.6,1,Both one-shot and repeated interactions are included; no single common value; using 1 as the minimum consistent setting but acknowledging heterogeneity.,0.2,1,Corpus includes dichotomous and continuous contribution games; continuous choice implies not all-or-nothing overall.,0.7,0,Standard PD and linear PGG paradigms start with endowment in private accounts; no automatic default contribution.,0.7,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not reported in aggregate; varies by study and is only used to compute K index.,0,0,"Communication is present in only a minority of treatments (k=203 vs 2,036 without); overall dataset does not systematically include chat.",0.5,1,"Standard PD/PGG feedback includes others’ contributions/earnings; meta-analysis notes feedback and summaries used to compute K and outcomes, though not uniformly.",0.4,0,Paper does not specify whether punishment is anonymous or identified across studies; cannot infer consistent identity revelation.,0,0,"Similarly, identity revelation for rewards is not described at meta level.",0,0,Whether total/remaining rounds are displayed is not reported for the corpus.,0,1,Sanctioning mechanisms (punishment and/or reward) are present in a subset of studies (k=281 with sanctions; k=40 mixed). Overall dataset includes both with and without punishment.,0.8,N/R,Specific per-unit punishment costs vary by primary study and are not summarized.,0,N/R,Multiplier of punishment points is not reported at meta level.,0,1,Meta-analysis mentions both punishments and rewards as sanctioning institutions; precise prevalence not summarized but clearly present in some studies.,0.6,N/R,Per-unit reward costs are not summarized across studies.,0,N/R,Reward multiplier per unit is not reported at meta level.,0,N/R,Token/point endowments differ widely across included experiments and are not aggregated.,0,0.36,Overall mean cooperation rate after controlling for study characteristics: 'participants contributed on average 36% of their endowment in the game'.,1,N/R,Raw contribution amounts (tokens) are not summarized at the meta level.,0,N/R,No aggregate measure of efficiency (actual / maximum group payoff) is reported.,0,N/R,Aggregate group payoff values are not reported across studies.,0,Multiple (70 societies),Studies were conducted in 70 different societies across 8 cultural groups; the meta-analysis aggregates across all.,1,"Adults (18+), mostly university students",Inclusion criteria: participants at least 18; 'most studies... student population (85%)'.,1,Mixed,Gender composition is not summarized but standard PD/PGG labs typically include both men and women; no restriction reported.,0.6,Primarily university students; minority non-students,Paper: 'involved participants recruited from a student population (85%)'.,1,On site,"Most studies are described as 'laboratory setting (88%)'; labs are physical on-site environments, though some online/field studies exist.",0.7,"Meta-analysis includes both Prisoner's Dilemma and linear Public Goods Games; decisions can be dichotomous or continuous; interactions one-shot or repeated; group size 2–324 (Mdn=3.5); conflict of interest quantified via K index (mean ~0.44). Structural moderators with significant positive effects on cooperation: higher K (less conflict), presence of communication, presence of sanctions, and continuous (vs. dichotomous) choice.",Summary of the paradigms and key structural variables as described in the Method and Results sections.,1
10.1093_bjps_axu024.md,"N/A – theoretical/philosophical article, no distinct conditions","The paper is a conceptual and theoretical review arguing about human cooperation and group selection; it does not report any specific experiment, game, simulation, or condition with its own parameters.",1,N/A,No manipulable independent variables or conditions are defined; the article synthesizes prior literature and presents arguments.,1,False,The article contains no new data collection with human participants; it only discusses others’ work conceptually.,1,False,No controlled experimental study is run; experimental results mentioned are secondary citations.,1,False,No lab or other experimental setting is run by the author; all empirical results referenced are from other sources.,1,False,"Although models are discussed (Bowles & Gintis, folk theorem, etc.), no specific new simulation study or parameterized run is presented in this article.",1,True,"The paper is entirely theoretical/analytical, using conceptual arguments and references to existing models without new data.",1,0,"No actual game or experiment is implemented in this paper, so there is no participant or player count.",1,0,No repeated game or experimental session is run; the article only describes others’ results in general terms.,1,0,No contribution decision is implemented by participants because no game is run; assigning 0 to reflect non-applicability within numeric constraint.,1,N/A,No human-subject game or endowment structure is specified in this article.,1,N/R,The paper does not specify parameters of any particular public goods game such as the marginal per capita return.,1,N/A,"No experiment with human participants is run, so communication protocols are not defined.",1,0,No interface or feedback is implemented; value set to 0 to indicate not used.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is actually implemented in a game in this article.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is actually implemented in a game in this article.,1,0,No repeated-game interface or disclosure of rounds exists in this theoretical article.,1,0,"Punishment is discussed conceptually, but no concrete punishment option is implemented in any new experiment.",1,N/A,No specific punishment technology or cost is set in this paper.,1,N/A,No per-unit punishment impact is parameterized in this article.,1,0,Rewards are not implemented in any concrete game here; only general discussion of cooperation and punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism is parameterized.,1,N/A,No per-unit reward technology is specified.,1,0,No experimental endowment is given to participants because no experiment is conducted.,1,N/R,No quantitative results on contribution rates from a specific experiment are reported; only qualitative summaries across literature.,1,N/R,No raw contribution data from new experiments are presented.,1,N/R,The paper does not present numeric efficiency measures from specific games; it only describes that cooperation can be stabilized.,1,N/R,No new experimental payoff data are reported.,1,N/A,No human participants are recruited; the article is purely theoretical and review-based.,1,N/A,No participant sample is described.,1,N/A,No participant demographics are provided because there is no empirical study.,1,N/A,No participants; therefore no education information.,1,No human,The article presents no new human experiment; only theoretical arguments and references to others’ work.,1,"This article discusses, at a high level, laboratory cooperation and punishment games from behavioral economics (public goods games, ultimatum/dictator games, punishment options) but does not specify any single game’s full parameterization or run a new study.",Summarized from the sections describing behavioral economics evidence; no concrete design is detailed sufficiently to treat as an extractable condition.,1
10.1098_rspb.2013.2661.md,Main structured-population simulation with third-party punishment (3PP) and punishment reputation,"Paper describes one core structured-population evolutionary game model with 3PP, reputation, and varying strength-of-ties and mobility; all reported parameter values (b, c, λ, ρ, μ, s, population size, network type, mobility m, degree d) belong to this single model.",0.9,"Structured-population evolutionary simulation varying (i) strength-of-ties via average degree d of a Watts–Strogatz network (tie strength = 1/d) and (ii) mobility via residential mobility rate m; also varying cooperation benefit b while holding c=1, λ=1, ρ=3, mutation/exploration μ=0.01, selection strength s=0.5, population size N=1000, and initial condition (all opportunistic defectors and non-punishers).","Methods and Results specify that main analyses vary average degree d and mobility m, plus benefit of cooperation b, keeping other parameters constant.",1,False,Model is an evolutionary game-theoretic simulation with agents on a graph; no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled empirical or lab experiment; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No lab or field setting; simulations run on a computer.,1,True,Population of agents updated by Fermi rule and random exploration in a structured network; clearly a numerical simulation.,1,False,"Although some equations are derived, the core results are from simulations, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,1000,Results section: 'populations of 1000 agents'.,1,5000,Results: 'averaging 100 simulation runs over 5000 generations'; each generation corresponds to one interaction/punishment/update cycle.,0.95,1,Agents choose between two discrete actions in the cooperation game: cooperate (pay cost c) or defect (pay 0). No continuous contribution level; treat as all-or-nothing binary contribution game.,0.9,N/A,Model is not an endowment-based public goods game; agents choose actions directly without an initial allocation between private/public accounts.,1,N/R,"Model is a two-player cooperation game with benefit b and cost c, not a public goods game with a defined marginal per capita return.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication variable not meaningful in agent-based evolutionary simulation.,1,N/R,"Simulation does not model on-screen feedback; only payoffs and neighbours' punishment strategies are assumed known, but no explicit summary display is described.",0.7,N/R,"Agents 'know the punishing strategies of their neighbours' (punishment reputation), but there is no notion of identity revelation in an interface; not reported in those terms.",0.7,N/A,"No reward mechanism is included; only punishment (ρ, λ) is modeled.",1,N/R,Agents do not have explicit knowledge of total generations or horizon; not discussed.,0.7,1,Explicit punishment phase where a neighbour can punish by amount ρ at cost λ; central to third-party punishment model.,1,1,"Model parameters: 'c = λ = 1, ρ = 3'; λ is the cost to the punisher per punishment action; per-unit interpretation is that one unit of cost yields ρ units of harm.",1,3,Model parameters: 'ρ = 3' is the punishment magnitude inflicted when punishing; one unit of cost λ=1 leads to 3 units of harm.,1,0,No reward or positive sanctioning mechanism is modeled; only costly punishment.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/R,"Model specifies costs and benefits per interaction (c, b) but does not define a fixed per-round endowment in tokens or points.",0.6,N/R,"They report 'rate of cooperation (percentage of cooperative actions)' but do not give numeric values; only qualitative plots and statements (e.g., virtually identical to proportion of responsible punishers).",0.6,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect decision with no numeric contribution amount recorded; only proportions of cooperators/cooperative actions and strategy frequencies.,0.9,N/R,Paper discusses that 3PP increases overall payoff qualitatively but does not report normalized efficiency values.,0.7,N/R,"Results discuss payoffs qualitatively and via local examples, but no aggregate group payoff metrics or numbers are reported.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,No human,"Study is an evolutionary game-theoretic simulation on networks, not conducted with human subjects in any environment.",1,"Two-phase evolutionary game model: (1) Cooperation game phase: paired neighbours play a two-player cooperation game where agents choose to cooperate (pay cost c to give benefit b to partner) or defect. Three cooperation-phase strategies: C (always cooperate), D (always defect), O (opportunistic: chooses cooperate/defect based on neighbours' punishment reputations to maximize expected payoff). (2) Punishment phase: a (possibly different) randomly chosen neighbour can punish the focal agent as a third party. Four punishment strategies: R (responsible: punish only defectors), A (antisocial: punish only cooperators), S (spiteful: punish everyone), N (non-punisher: punish none). Agents know the punishment strategies of their neighbours (punishment reputation). Population is placed on a Watts–Strogatz small-world network with average degree d; strength-of-ties defined as 1/d. Mobility m implemented as probability that at start of each generation an agent swaps positions with a randomly chosen other agent. Evolutionary update via Fermi imitation rule with selection strength s and random exploration (mutation) rate μ, applied simultaneously to all agents each generation. Main comparisons: (i) simulations with 3PP vs. baseline simulations without any punishment phase to isolate effects of structure; (ii) simulations with only direct punishment vs. 3PP to show that 3PP is necessary when direct punishment opportunities are not guaranteed; (iii) simulations where 3PP and direct punishment traits can co-evolve. Long-run statistics computed by averaging strategy proportions over 5000 generations and 100 runs.","Summarizes game structure, strategy sets, network structure, mobility, and evolutionary update as described in Methods and Results.",1
10.1098_rspb.2013.2661.md,Baseline structured-population simulation without any punishment phase,Results mention 'baseline experiments without 3PP' where simulations are repeated with identical conditions but without the punishment phase to test whether population structure alone can account for cooperation.,0.85,"Same structured-population evolutionary setup as main model (Watts–Strogatz networks, varying strength-of-ties and mobility, benefit b, and other parameters c=1, μ=0.01, s=0.5, N=1000), but with punishment phase entirely removed (no third-party or direct punishment).",Text: 'Repeating our simulations with identical conditions but without the punishment phase shows that population structure alone does not account for the evolution of cooperation... (figures S1 and S2)'.,0.9,False,"Agent-based evolutionary simulation, no human subjects.",1,False,"Computational baseline comparison, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,Simulations only.,1,True,Described as a repetition of the main simulations with punishment phase removed.,1,False,Uses numerical simulations; no closed-form analyses reported for this baseline.,0.9,1000,Stated that baseline repeats simulations 'with identical conditions'; main simulations use 1000 agents.,0.95,5000,Baseline described as 'repeating our simulations with identical conditions'; main simulations average over 5000 generations.,0.9,1,"Same binary cooperate/defect game phase as main model, just without punishment.",0.95,N/A,No endowment/public-goods structure; same as main model.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; parameters are b and c.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/R,"No interface; only simulated agents, and no extra reporting beyond payoff-based imitation.",0.7,N/A,"There is no punishment phase in this baseline, so punishment identity is not applicable.",1,N/A,Neither punishment nor reward exists in this baseline.,1,N/R,Agent cognition regarding horizon is not modeled.,0.7,0,Explicitly described as 'without the punishment phase'.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,0,"No reward mechanism mentioned; this is a no-punishment baseline, not a reward variant.",0.95,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,"Same two-player cost-benefit game structure, no explicit endowment.",0.6,N/R,"Supplementary figures S1 and S2 (not in main text) reportedly show that cooperation does not emerge, but no numeric rates are provided here.",0.6,N/R,"Only binary actions and their frequencies, not specific amounts, are considered.",0.9,N/R,Only qualitative statement that cooperation does not emerge without 3PP; no payoff ratios given.,0.7,N/R,No numeric group payoff results are reported for the baseline in the main text.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,No human,Baseline is a computational variant of the main simulation.,1,"Identical to the main structured-population model (two-player cooperate/defect game, agents on Watts–Strogatz networks, possible mobility m, Fermi imitation with selection strength s and exploration rate μ), except that there is no punishment phase and hence no 3PP or direct punishment strategies. Used to test whether network structure alone (high strength-of-ties and low mobility) can generate cooperation; authors report that it cannot in this model.",Derived directly from the description of the baseline simulations 'without the punishment phase' in the Results.,0.95
10.1098_rspb.2013.2661.md,Structured-population simulation with only direct punishment (no third-party punishment opportunities),"Results: 'to unconfound the effects of 3PP and direct punishment, we have repeated these simulations with only direct punishers.' This constitutes a distinct simulation condition.",0.85,"Same structured-population evolutionary setup and parameters as main model but restricted to direct punishment only (no third-party punishment opportunities), i.e., agents can only punish when they are directly involved in the interaction and the model does not guarantee a punishment opportunity each generation.","Text states these runs are 'with only direct punishers' under otherwise identical conditions, used to show that direct punishment alone cannot evolve in this model.",0.85,False,Agent-based computer simulation without human subjects.,1,False,"Computational modeling, not empirical experimentation.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Explicitly described as 'repeated these simulations' with altered punishment structure.,1,False,Outcomes reported qualitatively from simulations; not analytical.,0.9,1000,They indicate repeating simulations 'with identical conditions'; main conditions use 1000 agents.,0.9,5000,Assumed same as main simulations (5000 generations) since described as 'repeated... with identical conditions'; not re-specified but natural for robustness checks.,0.8,1,Same cooperative game: agents either cooperate (pay c) or defect.,0.95,N/A,No endowment allocation; binary action game.,1,N/R,"Pairwise cooperation game, not public goods.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/R,No explicit representation of summaries; only payoffs for updating.,0.7,N/R,"Agents may know punishment strategies of neighbours (reputation), but identity vs anonymity is not modeled as a treatment variable.",0.7,N/A,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,No agent knowledge of horizon is modeled.,0.7,1,Simulations specifically include direct punishment strategies; text: 'with only direct punishers'.,1,1,They state that in these robustness checks 'repeating our simulations with identical conditions' is done; main model has λ=1.,0.9,3,Using same ρ=3 as in the main simulations under 'identical conditions' assumption.,0.9,0,Only punishment phase is described; no reward mechanism introduced.,0.95,N/A,No reward allowed.,1,N/A,No reward allowed.,1,N/R,Same binary cost-benefit framework; no fixed endowment amount defined.,0.6,N/R,"Main text reports only the qualitative result that 'cooperation and responsible direct punishment cannot evolve alone in our model'; numeric cooperation rates are in supplementary figures S3 and S4, not quoted.",0.6,N/R,Game is binary; authors do not report absolute counts or tokens.,0.9,N/R,Only qualitative statement on failure of cooperation and punishment to evolve; no efficiency indices are provided.,0.7,N/R,No quantitative group payoff reported for this configuration.,0.7,N/A,"Simulation only, no human subjects.",1,N/A,"Simulation only, no human subjects.",1,N/A,"Simulation only, no human subjects.",1,N/A,"Simulation only, no human subjects.",1,No human,Computational robustness-check simulations.,1,"Evolutionary game on a structured population similar to the main model but restricting punishment to direct interactions: agents may only punish partners from the game phase (no third-party punishment). Due to the specific pairing structure, 'our model does not guarantee agents a chance to punish directly'; hence opportunities for direct punishment are stochastic and less frequent than in Hilbe & Traulsen (2012). Strategy sets likely mirror main model (cooperation phase: C, D, O; punishment phase: responsible, antisocial, spiteful, or non-punisher) but applied only to direct interaction partners. Used to demonstrate that, under these conditions, cooperation and responsible direct punishment alone cannot evolve in the absence of 3PP.",Summarizes description: 'repeated these simulations with only direct punishers... cooperation and responsible direct punishment cannot evolve alone in our model... our model does not guarantee agents a chance to punish directly.',0.85
10.1098_rspb.2013.2661.md,Structured-population simulation with co-evolving traits for third-party punishment and direct punishment,"Results: 'we have explored the evolution of 3PP when a separate trait for direct punishment can co-evolve, see the electronic supplementary material, figures S6–S13.' This is a distinct simulation condition.",0.8,"Structured-population evolutionary simulation where each agent carries separate traits for third-party punishment and direct punishment that can co-evolve; same basic parameters (b, c=1, λ=1, ρ=3, μ=0.01, s=0.5, N=1000, Watts–Strogatz network, varying strength-of-ties and mobility) as main model, but punishment strategy space expanded to include direct-only punishers as well as 3PP strategies.",Text explicitly states co-evolution of a separate direct-punishment trait; other conditions are said to be similar and results are in supplementary figures.,0.8,False,Fully simulated agent-based model.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; a robustness-check simulation.,1,False,No human lab or field environment.,1,True,Described as additional simulations in supplementary material.,1,False,Outcomes obtained numerically; not analytical.,0.9,1000,"They state that these are extensions/robustness checks of the main simulations, which use 1000 agents; no different N is mentioned.",0.8,5000,Assumed similar averaging horizon as main runs (5000 generations) since described as additional simulations; exact number not reiterated.,0.75,1,Cooperation game structure is unchanged (cooperate vs defect).,0.9,N/A,Not a public goods endowment game.,1,N/R,"Two-player cooperation game, not public goods.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/R,Model does not include explicit displayed summaries.,0.7,N/R,"Punishment reputation exists (agents know punishment strategies), but no explicit identity revelation treatment described.",0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism in any variant.,1,N/R,Horizon is not part of agent cognition.,0.7,1,Both 3PP and direct punishment traits are present by design.,1,1,They state these are similar simulations; cost parameter λ=1 in base model is likely retained.,0.8,3,Base model uses ρ=3; robustness checks are stated as extensions rather than parameter changes.,0.8,0,Only punishment (direct and 3PP) is discussed; no rewarding strategies.,0.95,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/R,"No explicit endowment defined, as in main model.",0.6,N/R,"They state qualitatively that 'responsible 3PP still evolves and promotes a high level of cooperation', but no numbers are given in main text.",0.6,N/R,Only binary decisions and frequencies are relevant; not numerically reported.,0.9,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported; only qualitative discussion of prevalence of responsible 3PP and cooperation.,0.7,N/R,"They mention that responsible 3PP remains necessary to induce cooperation and increases overall payoff, but no numeric values.",0.7,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Additional computational simulations with no human participants.,1,"Extension of the main structured-population 3PP model where each agent’s punishment behavior is governed by two potentially independent traits: one for direct punishment (punishing partners in their own interactions) and one for third-party punishment (punishing on behalf of others). Strategy space now includes combinations such as direct-only punishers, 3PP-only punishers, and agents who punish in both roles, alongside non-punishers and possibly antisocial/spiteful variants. Evolution proceeds via Fermi imitation and mutation on Watts–Strogatz networks with varying strength-of-ties and mobility. Authors report that adding direct-only punishers reduces the overall prevalence of responsible 3PP, but responsible 3PP remains necessary to induce cooperation and still evolves under high social-structural constraint.","Summarizes the described co-evolving-traits extension: 'separate trait for direct punishment can co-evolve... while the existence of direct-only punishers decreases the overall prevalence of responsible 3PP, responsible 3PP remains necessary to induce cooperation.'",0.85
