10.1093_sf_sov110.md,Prosocial leader condition – punishment phase,Condition where a single group member with the most prosocial SVO score is assigned as the only punisher; analysis and figures clearly separate this from proself leader and peer-punishment conditions.,1,Sanctioning institution and leader social value orientation: centralized punishment with an internal prosocial leader as sole punisher (vs other institutional forms).,"Paper varies (a) whether punishment is centralized in one leader vs peer-based, and (b) whether that leader is prosocial or proself; this row is the prosocial-leader centralized-punishment condition.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with 312 human participants (288 analyzed).,1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to conditions and rules of the public good game were manipulated.,1,True,Sessions conducted in a lab at a large public university using z-Tree; participants came to the lab.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model as a standalone study; theory is verbal and supported by lab data.,1,4,“Each session was conducted in a group of four.”,1,6,Punishment phase consisted of six rounds: “This process was repeated for six rounds.” referring to the punishment phase.,1,1,"Participants chose “a whole number from zero to 20” to contribute, i.e., any amount, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each round participants receive a 20-point endowment in a private account and choose how much to move to the public fund; initial allocation is fully private.,1,0.5,"Contributions to the group fund are doubled (multiplier 2) and split equally among 4 members, so MPCR = 2/4 = 0.5.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat; anonymity and private rooms emphasized.,0.9,1,"After each round, everyone could see each group member’s contribution and earnings; that is a summary of others’ outcomes.",1,0,"In leader conditions, punished parties see deductions but not stated that the punisher’s ID is revealed; explicit ID revelation is only mentioned for the peer-punishment condition.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,0,“Participants were not told in advance how many rounds they would complete” for both phases.,1,1,This is a sanctioning (punishment) phase where leaders can deduct points.,1,1,“It cost 1 point to deduct 3 points from another group member.”,1,3,Each punishment point spent removes 3 points from the target’s earnings.,1,0,Only punishment (deductions) are available; no reward option described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,20,“20-point endowments” each round in both phases.,1,N/R,"They report contributions in points and changes (e.g., +4 points) but no normalized (0–1) averages by condition are given.",0,N/R,"Figures and models describe patterns and differences, not condition-level mean contributions in points across the punishment phase.",0,N/R,"Earnings and punishment costs are discussed qualitatively and via regression, but no normalized efficiency metric (0–1) is reported.",0,N/R,"They mention earnings ranges ($10–$15) and compare conditions, but no raw per-group payoff values are provided.",0,United States,Participants were from a large public university in the southeastern United States.,0.95,N/R,No age or age range reported.,0,"182 female, 130 male (approximate, 312 total participants; gender distribution not broken down by condition)",Overall sample composition reported as 312 participants (182 female); not specified by condition but applies to experiment as a whole.,0.7,Undergraduate students at a large public university,“Participants were recruited from introductory classes at a large public university.”,1,On site,Lab-based sessions with participants escorted to private rooms at the university.,1,"Two-phase repeated linear public good game. Phase 1: 6 rounds without punishment. Phase 2 (this condition): 6 rounds where a single prosocial leader, selected as the most prosocial by SVO, can punish any of the three non-leaders; leader cannot be punished. After each contribution stage, all see contributions and earnings; then only the leader chooses costly deductions. Punishment: 1 point spent removes 3 from target; contributions from 20-point endowment, MPCR 0.5; group size 4; stranger vs partner design not explicitly discussed but groups remain fixed across rounds.",Summary of procedural details specific to the prosocial leader punishment phase drawn from Methods and Procedure sections.,1
10.1093_sf_sov110.md,Proself leader condition – punishment phase,Condition where a single group member with the fewest prosocial (most proself) SVO answers is assigned as the only punisher.,1,Sanctioning institution and leader social value orientation: centralized punishment with an internal proself leader as sole punisher (vs other institutional forms).,Same institutional setup as prosocial-leader condition but leader is selected as most proself on SVO.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to sanctioning conditions; controlled manipulation of leader type.,1,True,Conducted in university lab rooms with z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation study reported.,1,False,"No separate analytical model as a method, only verbal theory.",1,4,Groups of four in all conditions.,1,6,Punishment phase repeated for six rounds.,1,1,Same contribution interface: choose 0–20 points.,1,0,Endowment starts as 20 points in private account each round.,1,0.5,Group fund is doubled and equally split among 4 members (2/4).,1,0,No communication described in any condition.,0.9,1,Participants view each member’s contributions and earnings after contributions stage.,1,0,Only peer-punishment condition is explicitly described as revealing IDs of who punished whom; no such statement for leader conditions.,0.9,0,No rewards available.,1,0,Participants not told number of rounds in advance.,1,1,Leader can deduct points from earnings of each group member after each round.,1,1,Same punishment technology for all conditions: 1 point cost to deduct 3 points.,1,3,Each point used for punishment reduces target’s payoff by 3.,1,0,Only deduction (punishment) options are mentioned.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,20,Each round endowment is 20 points.,1,N/R,Condition-level normalized contribution rate not reported.,0,N/R,"Only changes and regression effects (e.g., proself leaders contributed 10 points less than baseline) are given, not mean contributions by condition.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio presented.,0,N/R,Group earnings compared across conditions but not as raw average payoffs per group.,0,United States,Same participant pool as other conditions (US university).,0.95,N/R,Age not reported.,0,"182 female, 130 male (overall sample; not broken down by condition)",Gender breakdown given for full N; assumed similar across conditions due to random assignment but not explicitly reported by condition.,0.7,Undergraduate students at a large public university,Identical sample description for all conditions.,1,On site,Lab experiment with participants physically present.,1,"Two-phase repeated linear public good game with centralized punishment. After 6 baseline no-punishment rounds, participants in this condition play 6 rounds where only a proself leader (fewest prosocial SVO choices) can punish others. Leader cannot be punished. After contributions (20-point endowment, MPCR 0.5, group size 4), all see contributions and earnings; then only leader chooses costly deductions at 1:3 cost-to-impact ratio. Evidence indicates proself leaders reduced their contributions by about 10 points relative to baseline and often punished hypocritically (targets contributed as much or more than leader 73% of the time).",Compiled key institutional and behavioral details for the proself-leader punishment phase.,1
10.1093_sf_sov110.md,Peer-punishment condition – punishment phase,"Condition where all four group members can punish all others; described as the peer-punishment condition, the standard in the literature.",1,Sanctioning institution: decentralized peer punishment where all group members can punish all others (no designated leader).,This condition differs by distributing punishment ability equally to all group members instead of centralizing it.,1,True,"Same human-subject lab experiment, different institutional rule.",1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to this sanctioning rule and compared to leader conditions.,1,True,Conducted in the same university laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No computational-only model.,1,False,No separate analytical experiment.,1,4,Groups of four across all conditions.,1,6,Punishment rounds repeated six times in this condition as well.,1,1,Same contribution choice of 0–20 points.,1,0,All 20 points start private; subjects decide how much to invest in the group fund.,1,0.5,Group fund doubled and divided among 4 members.,1,0,No communication channel or discussion mentioned.,0.9,1,"Participants see each member’s contributions and earnings after each round, plus post-punishment outcomes.",1,1,"“In the peer-punishment condition, participants could see the ID numbers of who punished whom.”",1,0,No rewards implemented.,1,0,Number of rounds was not disclosed beforehand.,1,1,All members can punish all others using deduction points.,1,1,Same costs: 1 point to deduct 3 points from another.,1,3,Each punishment point causes 3-point loss for target.,1,0,No rewarding institution described.,1,N/A,There is no reward technology.,1,N/A,There is no reward technology.,1,20,Each round endowment is 20 points.,1,N/R,Only relative comparisons to leader conditions over time are provided; no normalized contribution fraction for this condition alone.,0,N/R,Graphs qualitatively show that peer-punishment contributions fall between prosocial-led and proself-led groups but no explicit mean amounts are given.,0.2,N/R,They compare earnings (after punishment) across conditions but no explicit efficiency ratio is calculated.,0,N/R,Model 6 presents regression coefficients on earnings but not a simple aggregated payoff for this condition.,0.2,United States,Same participant pool for all conditions.,0.95,N/R,Age is not provided.,0,"182 female, 130 male (overall sample; not broken down by condition)",Only overall gender reported; distribution by condition not specified.,0.7,Undergraduate students at a large public university,Common description for the experimental subject pool.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at the university.,1,"Two-phase repeated public good game. After 6 baseline rounds without punishment, participants in this condition enter 6 rounds with standard peer punishment. All four group members can punish each of the other three after observing contributions and earnings. Punishment technology: each participant independently chooses deductions; 1 point spent removes 3 points from the target. IDs of punishers are displayed to recipients. Contributions from 20-point endowment, MPCR 0.5, fixed groups of 4. Peer-punishment groups exhibit high punishment levels (members punished significantly more and could be punished up to 3× as often as in leader groups) and ultimately lower earnings than prosocial- and even proself-led groups despite comparable or higher contribution levels at some points.",Summary of the decentralized punishment institution and key comparative findings for this condition.,1
10.1257_aer.20130768.md,Analytical model – bilateral enforcement benchmark,The paper defines and analyzes a bilateral enforcement equilibrium as a distinct benchmark environment in Section I and III.,0.96,"Enforcement regime: bilateral enforcement only; no ostracism, no forgiveness, no third-party punishment.","This condition restricts punishment to bilateral partnerships only, with no community/third-party sanctions.",0.98,False,The study is purely theoretical with no human participants; it analyzes equilibria in an infinite-horizon repeated game.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; only mathematical analysis of strategic models.,1,False,There is no laboratory setting; the work is a theoretical model.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; results are analytical.,0.98,True,"Equilibria are derived with inequalities and closed-form expressions (e.g., constraints for T(φ) and φ̄).",1,3,"Section I example describes a society of three players Ann, Bob, and Carol used for the benchmarks.",0.9,0,"The interaction is an infinite-horizon continuous-time repeated game; a finite round count is not defined, so coded as 0.",0.8,1,"Effort level a ≥ 0 or stakes φ are continuous choices, not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.",1,N/A,This is not a contribution game with an initial endowment; it is a repeated prisoner’s dilemma/effort model without such a fund.,1,N/R,"No public good or MPCR is defined; payoffs are given via T(φ), V(φ), etc.",1,1,A communication stage exists where partners can send evidentiary messages before effort decisions.,1,1,"Through evidentiary communication, players can share detailed histories of others’ past interactions, effectively summarizing others’ behaviors.",0.8,1,"Players are named and identified in communications (Ann, Bob, Carol, indices i,j); identity of deviators is known to recipients.",0.9,1,"Any future cooperative treatment after deviation is relationship-specific with named partners, so the identity of partners receiving favorable treatment is known.",0.7,0,Continuous-time infinite horizon with Poisson meetings; no finite total number or countdown is displayed in the model.,0.9,1,Punishment is implemented via future shirking (grim trigger) after deviations in bilateral links.,1,N/R,No parametric per-unit punishment technology is specified; punishment is an equilibrium reduction in future cooperation.,1,N/R,There is no explicit mapping of punishment tokens to payoff reductions; only strategy-contingent payoffs.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding institution is modeled; only standard cooperation/shirking.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,There is no discrete endowment of tokens; effort/stakes are continuous decisions with cost and benefit functions.,1,N/R,The paper does not report a normalized contribution rate; it characterizes maximal enforceable stakes φ̄ but no numerical value.,0.6,N/R,"Only symbolic effort/stakes levels (a, φ̄) are discussed, no specific numeric outcome level is reported.",0.6,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio relative to first-best is computed.,0.7,N/R,Expected payoffs are given symbolically but not as a single reported number suitable for extraction.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"The model is an analytical, infinite-horizon game with no experimental implementation.",1,Continuous-time networked repeated prisoner’s dilemma/variable-stakes partnership model under bilateral enforcement only; links meet via independent Poisson processes with intensity λ; efforts a ≥ 0 (or stakes φ) determine costs and partner benefits.,Summarizes main structural features directly taken from Sections I–III where bilateral enforcement is defined.,0.98
10.1257_aer.20130768.md,Analytical model – permanent ostracism with mechanical (forced) communication,Section I describes a benchmark where communication is mechanical and players are permanently ostracized after deviation.,0.96,"Enforcement regime: community enforcement with permanent ostracism and mechanical, full disclosure communication.","This condition adds community-level punishment and exogenous, truthful, full information revelation compared to bilateral enforcement.",0.98,False,This is part of the same theoretical model; no human data.,1,False,No experimental manipulation in a lab; only analytical comparison of equilibria.,1,False,The scenario is theoretical; not implemented in a physical lab.,1,False,Outcomes are solved symbolically; no simulation results are reported.,0.98,True,Effort level supported (2ā) is derived from incentive constraints.,1,3,"Same three-player example (Ann, Bob, Carol) is used to illustrate permanent ostracism with mechanical communication.",0.9,0,Infinite horizon continuous time; no fixed number of rounds.,0.9,1,Effort a is a continuous variable; not constrained to all-or-nothing.,1,N/A,No endowment contribution structure; decisions are effort/stakes choices.,1,N/R,"No MPCR defined, as the interaction is not a linear public goods game.",1,1,There is a communication stage before effort choices; here it is assumed mechanical and fully revealing.,1,1,"Mechanical communication forces revelation of the full details of all prior interactions, including others’ behaviors.",0.95,1,"Mechanical, full-truth messages include names and deviations, making the identity of punished deviators known.",0.95,1,"Partners and their innocence/guilt are fully identified in communications, so any favorable cooperative treatment is tied to identities.",0.8,0,No finite horizon; the model does not specify display of any remaining-length information.,0.9,1,Permanent ostracism is implemented as punishment: deviators are permanently excluded by all partners.,1,N/R,The model does not define a numerical ‘cost per unit’ for ostracism; costs are via forgone cooperation.,1,N/R,No explicit token-to-payoff punishment technology; punishment is an equilibrium strategy.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism is added; only cooperation vs ostracism.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/R,There is no discrete endowment; agents choose effort/stakes.,1,N/R,Equilibrium path effort 2ā is characterized relative to ā but not normalized as a fraction of a defined maximum or endowment.,0.6,N/R,Effort level 2ā is given symbolically; no numeric amount is provided.,0.7,N/R,The paper compares effort levels but does not give an explicit efficiency ratio for this regime.,0.7,N/R,Expected payoffs are described via integrals but not summarized as explicit numeric outcomes.,0.7,N/A,Theoretical model; no human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical scenario only.,1,"Same continuous-time three-player networked partnership model; with mechanical communication, all past interactions are fully and truthfully disclosed, allowing permanent ostracism of any deviator and support of higher equilibrium path effort level 2ā compared to ā under bilateral enforcement.",Summarizes key structural difference and main equilibrium implication.,0.98
10.1257_aer.20130768.md,Analytical model – permanent ostracism with strategic evidentiary communication,Section I and Theorem 1 analyze permanent ostracism when communication is evidentiary and strategic (players choose which interactions to disclose).,0.97,Enforcement regime: permanent ostracism with strategic evidentiary communication (players can selectively conceal truthful interactions).,"The only change from bilateral enforcement is the availability of community ostracism and evidentiary communication, but reporting is voluntary, not mechanical.",0.98,False,Still a purely theoretical model.,1,False,There is no empirical experiment; only analysis of equilibrium constraints.,1,False,Not run in a lab setting.,1,False,No simulations are presented.,1,True,"Theorems and lemmas (e.g., Theorem 1) give analytical bounds on achievable stakes/payoffs.",1,3,"The illustrative example of three players (Ann, Bob, Carol) is explicitly used for the strategic communication analysis in Section I.",0.9,0,Infinite-horizon continuous-time setting; no finite rounds.,0.9,1,Effort/stakes remain continuous choices.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game with initial endowments.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return is defined.,1,1,Partners communicate in a designated communication stage; here messages are evidentiary and voluntary.,1,1,"Players may disclose subsets of their history, including interactions involving third parties, thus sharing summaries of others’ past behavior.",0.85,1,"When communication occurs, messages specify which player shirked in which interaction, revealing deviator identity; however, players may conceal such messages.",0.9,1,Cooperation and continued work are link-specific and identities are explicit; any de facto ‘reward’ (continued cooperation) is partner-identified.,0.8,0,Infinite horizon; no explicit display of remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Permanent ostracism is the prescribed punishment for deviators.,1,N/R,No numerical punishment cost parameter is specified; punishment is via exclusion.,1,N/R,No token-based or parametric punishment technology is given.,1,0,No explicit costly rewards; any benefits are via cooperative play.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/R,"Model defined over effort/stakes and cost/benefit functions, not endowments.",1,N/R,"They derive that maximal supportable effort is ((r+4λ)/(r+3λ))ā in a hypothetical equilibrium, but Theorem 1 then shows in general permanent ostracism cannot exceed bilateral enforcement; no normalized contribution metric is reported.",0.6,N/R,No numeric effort levels are provided; only symbolic bounds relative to ā.,0.7,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (no better than bilateral enforcement) but not quantified as a ratio.,0.7,N/R,Group payoffs are bounded by bilateral enforcement payoffs but not turned into explicit numerical values.,0.7,N/A,Theoretical work; no real participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical equilibrium analysis only.,1,"Permanent ostracism strategies require innocent players to truthfully reveal histories and permanently punish guilty players; communication is evidentiary (truthful but selectively concealable). Theorem 1 shows that in any such equilibrium, expected payoffs and stakes cannot exceed bilateral enforcement stakes φ̄ on any link.",Summarizes structure and main result for this enforcement regime.,0.98
10.1257_aer.20130768.md,Analytical model – temporary ostracism with forgiveness and strategic evidentiary communication,Section I (Temporary Ostracism with Strategic Communication) and Theorem 2 define and analyze a regime with temporary ostracism via Poisson clocks (forgiveness).,0.98,"Enforcement regime: temporary ostracism with forgiveness using individual Poisson clocks (rate μ), evidentiary but selective communication, and fixed stakes φ > φ̄ when partners are innocent.","Key independent features: introduction of forgiveness technology (public Poisson clocks) and a temporary rather than permanent ostracism rule, holding other aspects of the networked repeated game constant.",0.98,False,"Still an analytical, game-theoretic model without human subjects.",1,False,No controlled empirical experiment is conducted.,1,False,Not a laboratory implementation.,1,False,Equilibrium is constructed analytically; no simulation results.,1,True,Theorem 2 and Lemma 1 analytically derive existence conditions for temporary ostracism equilibria.,1,N/R,"The general construction applies to n ≥ 3, and some results (e.g., theorem condition r < 2λ(n−3)) hold for arbitrary n. No single fixed n is chosen for the core result.",0.6,0,Infinite-horizon continuous-time model with repeated Poisson meetings and Poisson forgiveness; number of rounds is not finite.,0.9,1,Partners propose continuous stakes φ; not restricted to binary cooperation choices.,1,N/A,The framework does not use endowments or initial contributions.,1,N/R,No linear public good MPCR is defined; payoffs depend on T(φ) and V(φ).,1,1,Model includes both pre-interaction and post-interaction communication stages with evidentiary messages.,1,1,"Messages can reveal past interactions, including those with third parties, summarizing others’ histories.",0.85,1,Ostracism and forgiveness rules are link- and player-specific; communications about guilt specify which player deviated and when.,0.9,1,"Forgiveness events are public via player-specific Poisson clocks, and innocence/guilt is tracked per player; thus the identity of forgiven (and hence ‘rewarded’) players is clear.",0.9,0,Players do not observe a terminal time or remaining number of interactions; only Poisson process realizations.,0.9,1,Guilty players are ostracized (partners set zero stakes or shirk) until their Poisson clock rings.,1,N/R,No per-unit punishment cost is parameterized; punishment is via loss of cooperation payoffs.,1,N/R,No explicit punishment technology with cost and impact per token is modelled.,1,1,"Forgiveness (re-admission to cooperative society with stakes φ) functions as a positive change in treatment for previously guilty players, akin to a reward.",0.75,N/R,No explicit cost parameter for forgiveness; it changes continuation payoffs but not via a token-like cost.,0.7,N/R,No per-unit mapping from reward tokens to payoff increases is specified.,0.7,N/R,"As elsewhere, the game is defined in terms of stakes and cost/benefit functions, not discrete endowments.",1,N/R,"They show existence of φ > φ̄ supporting mutual effort under temporary ostracism when r < 2λ(n−3), but do not give a normalized contribution fraction.",0.6,N/R,Stakes φ and bilateral stakes φ̄ are symbolic; no explicit numeric equilibrium stakes are reported.,0.7,N/R,"Theorem 2 states payoffs under temporary ostracism are strictly higher than under permanent ostracism, but no numeric efficiency ratios are provided.",0.7,N/R,Group payoffs are characterized qualitatively (‘strictly higher’) but not given as explicit numbers.,0.7,N/A,No actual human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical equilibrium construction.,1,"General n-player (n ≥ 3) continuous-time network with each pair interacting via Poisson(λ) meetings. Each player also has an independent public Poisson clock with rate μ that triggers forgiveness (transition from guilty to innocent). Stage game has pre-interaction evidentiary communication, stake selection (both propose, minimum is used), prisoner's dilemma effort choice at stakes φ, and post-interaction communication. Temporary ostracism equilibrium prescribes cooperation at fixed stakes φ > φ̄ among innocent pairs, ostracism of guilty players until their clock rings, and a communication rule where only the first victim of a guilty player spreads information. Theorem 2: if r < 2λ(n−3), there exists such an equilibrium yielding payoffs strictly higher than any permanent ostracism equilibrium.",Summarizes the richer structure and main comparative result of temporary ostracism with forgiveness.,0.99
10.1073_pnas.1718309115.md,"Field experiment – Native observer, native confederate","Condition explicitly listed in Table 1 as 'Native observer, native confederate'.",1,Ethnicity of observer: native; Ethnicity of confederate: native,One of four experimental encounter types varying observer and confederate ethnicity.,1,True,Natural field experiment with passersby reacting to an actor's littering.,1,True,Authors describe it as a natural field experiment with randomized staged interventions.,1,False,"Conducted at metro station entrances, not in a laboratory.",1,False,No computer or agent-based simulations are reported for this condition.,1,False,"Condition is based on observed human behavior, not purely analytical modeling.",1,2,Each trial involves one confederate and one observer (passerby).,1,1,Each passerby experiences a single one-shot encounter with the confederate.,1,0,"Observers can choose among multiple reactions (verbal reprimand, dirty looks, etc.), not a binary all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.5,N/A,No contribution game or endowment is used; this is a norm-enforcement field setting.,1,N/A,No public goods or payoff multiplier is involved.,1,0,No pre-play communication; only potential reaction to the littering act itself.,0.9,0,Observers see no summaries of others' behavior or payoffs.,1,1,Any verbal reprimand is face-to-face; identities (appearance) are naturally visible.,1,0,No reward mechanism is described.,1,0,There is no multi-round structure or information about number of rounds.,1,1,"Main outcome is whether the observer issues a verbal reprimand, which functions as social punishment.",1,N/R,No explicit cost per punishment action is quantified in tokens or money.,0,N/R,No formal mapping from punishment action to payoff reduction is provided.,0,0,Only sanctions against littering are studied; no rewards are mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No monetary endowments or tokens are used.,1,N/A,"No contribution decision; dependent variable is sanctioning, not contribution.",1,N/A,No contribution amounts are defined.,1,N/A,No payoff structure or efficiency metric is defined.,1,N/A,No explicit group payoffs are measured.,1,Germany,Study conducted in German neighborhoods (Bonn and Cologne); passersby are residents there.,0.9,"Adults, predominantly between 18 and older (exact distribution N/R)","Passersby at metro stations; age of passersby was recorded in categories, but specific age stats not reported here.",0.5,Mixed; both men and women passersby,Results report sanctioning differences between male and female observers.,1,N/R,No educational attainment information reported for field experiment passersby.,0,Field experiment,Described as a natural field experiment at metro station entrances.,1,Naturalistic one-shot interaction: confederate litters a coffee cup near a trash can as an unaccompanied passerby approaches; research assistant records whether the observer issues a verbal reprimand or other negative reactions.,Summarized from the 'Experimental Protocol' section.,1
