10.1073_pnas.1718309115.md,"Field experiment – Minority observer, minority confederate","Condition explicitly listed in Table 1 as 'Minority observer, minority confederate'.",1,Ethnicity of observer: minority; Ethnicity of confederate: minority,One of four encounter types systematically varied.,1,True,Based on human passersby observing an actor.,1,True,Natural field experiment with controlled staged norm violation.,1,False,Implemented in real-world metro settings.,1,False,No simulation agents.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,2,One confederate and one passerby in each trial.,1,1,Each passerby is exposed once.,1,0,Response options are not framed as all-or-nothing contributions.,0.5,N/A,No contribution game.,1,N/A,No public good payoffs.,1,0,No structured communication beyond possible reprimand.,0.9,0,No summaries or feedback.,1,1,Punishment targets are directly observable individuals.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No rounds information.,1,1,Observers may verbally reprimand minority confederates for littering.,1,N/R,No numeric cost specified.,0,N/R,No quantitative punishment technology defined.,0,0,Rewards are not part of the design.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No monetary endowment.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions; DV is sanctioning.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No efficiency concept defined.,1,N/A,No monetary group payoffs.,1,Germany,Same German urban neighborhoods as other conditions.,0.9,"Adults, predominantly between 18 and older (exact distribution N/R)",Same passerby pool; detailed age distribution not reported.,0.5,Mixed; both men and women passersby,Gender composition same as in other encounter types.,1,N/R,Education data for passersby not reported.,0,Field experiment,Part of the metro-station natural field experiment.,1,Same staged littering protocol; this cell covers interactions where both observer and confederate are visually classified as having migration background.,Encounter type defined in 'Experimental Conditions'.,1
10.1006_jtbi.1996.0286.md,Iterated Prisoner's Dilemma – General Theoretical Analysis,Paper presents a general analytical treatment of the Iterated Prisoner's Dilemma (IPD) using state space strategies; no distinct empirical conditions with human subjects are defined.,1,"Payoff parameters (R,S,T,P) and continuation probability ω; structure of state-space strategies (e.g., Pavlov, Grim, CTFT, TFT-like) varied analytically.","Text explicitly analyzes ranges of ω and payoff inequalities for which different strategies are limit ESS, and compares different state-space structures.",1,False,The work is purely theoretical and analytical; no human or animal subjects are run in experiments.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment is conducted; only game-theoretic derivations and enumeration of strategies.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; the study is mathematical.,1,False,"Although strategies are enumerated by computer, the core results are analytical; no stochastic or agent-based simulations of populations are reported.",0.7,True,The paper derives theorems and conditions for limit ESS using dynamic programming and Selten’s framework.,1,2,IPD is explicitly defined as a two-player repeated game.,1,-1,Game is infinitely repeated with continuation probability ω (0<ω<1); no fixed finite number of rounds.,1,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D each round; within this binary choice, there is no intermediate contribution level, which corresponds to all-or-nothing in contribution terms.",0.7,N/A,"No endowment or public-good contribution framework; abstract PD payoffs only, and no human players.",1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined because this is not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,No communication or human interaction is modeled; purely theoretical.,1,0,"Standard PD representation; no interface or feedback summaries described, and no empirical subjects.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage with identities; only abstract payoffs between two fixed players in theory.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with identity revelation is modeled.,1,0,Infinitely repeated with continuation probability ω; no user interface or display of remaining rounds.,1,0,"In the standard IPD formulation used here, payoffs are fixed (R,S,T,P) with no separate costly punishment choice; defection is not modeled as a distinct punishment technology.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment action or cost parameter is defined for the IPD section.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism defined.,1,0,"No separate rewarding stage or technology; cooperation is part of the base game, not additional reward.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism beyond the baseline PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,No explicit endowments or tokens are assigned to players in the analytical model.,1,N/R,"Theoretical results concern ESS conditions and value functions, not average empirical contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts are reported; only abstract actions C/D.,1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (e.g., Grim vs Pavlov) but no numerical efficiency indices are reported.",0.8,N/R,No empirical or simulated average group payoff values; only symbolic expressions.,1,N/A,No human or animal participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All work is analytical and theoretical.,1,"Two-player infinitely repeated Prisoner's Dilemma with simultaneous moves; actions C or D each round; continuation probability ω (0<ω<1); payoffs R,S,T,P satisfying T>R>P>S and typically 2R>T+S. Analyzes many pure state-space strategies (Pavlov, Grim, CTFT, TFT-like, etc.) and their ranges of evolutionary stability as limit ESS under Selten’s framework.",Summarizes the formal IPD setup and strategic focus as described in Section 2.,1
10.1006_jtbi.1996.0286.md,Dominance Game – General Theoretical Analysis,"Section 3 presents an analytical dominance game with roles dominant and subdominant, examining punishment-based control; not an empirical experiment.",1,"Roles (dominant vs subdominant); punishment parameters c (cost), d (damage), resource value v, and continuation probability ω; state-space strategy structures (e.g., Quarrel, Tyranny, Flip, Flip-flop).","The analysis varies c, v, d, ω and strategy structures to determine when dominance or other outcomes are limit ESSs.",1,False,No actual dominant/subdominant animals or humans are observed; purely model-based.,1,False,No experimental manipulation; only mathematical analysis of payoff matrices and strategies.,1,False,No lab setting; theoretical model only.,1,False,"Counts of strategies are computed, but no dynamic simulations of play are described.",0.8,True,Uses Selten’s limit ESS framework and optimality equations to derive conditions for dominance and punishment.,1,2,"Dominance game is defined for two individuals: one dominant, one subdominant.",1,-1,"Game is infinitely repeated with continuation probability ω, not a fixed round count.",1,1,"Action sets are discrete {L,P} for dominant and {R,S} for subdominant; no graded continuous decisions.",0.9,N/A,"Model concerns appropriation and punishment of a resource, not contributions to a public good; and no human players.",1,N/R,Not a public goods or linear contribution game; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No communication modeled; and no human participants.,1,0,No interface; theoretical dyadic game.,1,N/A,Players’ identities/roles (dominant/subdominant) are fixed and known; no additional punishment identity revelation is modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage in the model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon model without an explicit displayed round counter.,1,1,Dominant has an explicit P (punish) action with cost c and damage d to subdominant.,1,N/R,"c is introduced as punishment cost, but no numeric value except example c=0.3; we do not choose a single value.",0.5,N/R,Damage d to the subdominant is specified qualitatively (0<c<v<d and example d=3) but no normalized ‘per unit’ technology parameter is defined.,0.6,0,No explicit rewarding action; only respect vs steal and leave vs punish.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game is specified in terms of resource value v and punishment parameters, not explicit per-round endowments.",1,N/R,"Outcome measures concern dominance and control via punishment, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No notion of contribution amount; only qualitative actions and payoffs.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not quantified; results focus on whether dominants can enforce respect vs steal and on stability sets.,0.9,N/R,No numerical group payoff statistics; only symbolic and conditional payoffs per strategy.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Dominance game is entirely theoretical.,1,"Two-player repeated dominance game with roles dominant and subdominant. In each round subdominant chooses Respect (R) or Steal (S) a resource of value v; dominant chooses Leave (L) or Punish (P). Punishment costs dominant c and imposes damage d on subdominant, with parameters satisfying 0<c<v<d, continuity probability ω (0<ω<1). Analyses pure state-space strategies (e.g., Quarrel, Tyranny, Flip, Flip-flop) as limit ESS under Selten’s framework, including conditions such as d>2v or high ω.",Summarizes the formal dominance-game setup given around Fig. 1 and Table 3.,1
10.1006_jtbi.1996.0286.md,Alternating Prisoner’s Dilemma – Reciprocity Strategy Analysis,"Section 4 analyzes the alternating Prisoner’s Dilemma and in particular the state-space strategy Reciprocity as a limit ESS; it is a theoretical condition, not an empirical treatment.",1,"Cost of helping c, benefit b (0<c<b), continuation probability ω, and structure of alternating move roles (leader vs follower) with state-space strategy Reciprocity.","The condition ω>c/b is derived as the range where Reciprocity is a limit ESS, with c and b as main parameters.",1,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical alternating PD model.,1,False,No controlled experimental manipulation; only analytical derivations.,1,False,No lab setting; all results are analytical.,1,False,"Although strategies are characterized and compared, there is no numerical simulation of population dynamics reported.",0.8,True,Derives optimality equations and ESS conditions (ω>c/b) for the Reciprocity strategy and others.,1,2,Alternating PD is defined for two players with roles leader and follower.,1,-1,Infinitely repeated alternating moves with continuation probability ω.,1,1,Each move is a binary choice C (help) or D (defect); no intermediate contribution levels.,0.9,N/A,"Model is not framed as an endowment-contribution game, and no human participants.",1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no MPCR is defined.,1,N/A,No communication mode; analytics only.,1,0,Theoretical; no interface or feedback summaries discussed.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage in the alternating PD; only help vs no help.,1,N/A,"Cooperation is part of base game, not a separate reward mechanism.",1,0,Infinite-horizon model; no display of remaining rounds.,1,0,"The alternating PD only has C and D actions, with costs/benefits (c,b); no distinct costly punishment action.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism separate from defection.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward stage; help is simply C with cost c and benefit b.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/R,Payoffs are framed as cost c and benefit b per act of help; no explicit endowments.,1,N/R,No empirical contribution data; only theoretical characterization of strategies.,1,N/R,No numeric average C-per-round is reported; analysis is symbolic.,1,N/R,"While mutual cooperation yields high payoffs, no explicit efficiency ratios are computed.",0.9,N/R,No numeric group payoffs over time are provided.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,All results are theoretical.,1,Two-player infinitely repeated alternating Prisoner’s Dilemma with roles leader (moves in odd rounds) and follower (even rounds). Each helpful move C costs c to the actor and benefits the partner b (0<c<b); D gives/pay zero. Continuation probability ω per round. Defines and analyzes the pure state-space strategy Reciprocity (leader and follower each with 2 states) and shows Reciprocity is a limit ESS iff ω>c/b; also shows randomised ‘generous’ strategies with finite memory cannot be limit ESS due to role asymmetry.,Summarizes the formal alternating PD structure and main result for Reciprocity as described in Section 4 and Tables 4–5.,1
10.1086_381411.md,Deterministic one-shot game – 4-strategy minimal strategy space,"Paper’s Section 5.1: simulations of the one-shot trust game using the 4 unconditional, role-contingent strategies TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR with deterministic replicator dynamics.",0.99,"Game type: one-shot trust game (r=1); Strategy set: 4 unconditional strategies {TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR}; Dynamics: deterministic replicator dynamic; Initial population proportions varied across 27 conditions.","Section 5.1 and Table 1 specify that these simulations use the 4x4 payoff matrix of Figure 4, r=1, deterministic replicator dynamic, and many different initial proportion vectors.",0.99,False,All results are from numerical simulations of strategy proportions; no human subjects are mentioned.,1,False,This is not a controlled behavioral experiment with participants but a numerical simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe these as simulations and provide Mathematica code.,1,False,"While analytical concepts are discussed, the reported results here are from numerical simulation, not closed-form derivations.",0.95,4,"Population ‘players’ correspond to 4 pure strategies in the minimal strategy space: TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR.",0.9,1,"One-shot game: Section 5.1 states that in the one-shot game payoffs are realized after a single round, and Figure 4 uses r=1.",0.99,1,"Trust decision is binary (T or nT), but no continuous contribution choice exists; mapping to public goods ‘allOrNothing’ is approximate; here interpreted as 1 meaning a discrete binary choice rather than continuous contributions.",0.5,N/A,"Trust game with send/endow and invest decision, not a standard contribution game with default allocations.",1,N/R,"MPCR (marginal per capita return) is not defined; payoffs are described via trust game multipliers (x to 3x, splits) not in MPCR terms.",1,N/A,No human participants or interface; communication not applicable in a purely computational model.,1,N/A,No human subjects or on-screen feedback; concept not applicable.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism between agents in this minimal one-shot model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism; only trust/reciprocate choices.,1,N/A,Simulated agents do not have interfaces showing rounds; they follow fixed strategies.,1,0,In the one-shot minimal game there is no formal punishment action; only T/nT and R/nR.,0.95,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit costly rewarding; only the built-in payoff structure of the trust game.,0.95,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,1,Section 5.1: ‘we set x = 1’ for the simulations; x is the sender’s endowment.,1,N/A,No notion of contribution fraction; binary trust decision instead.,1,N/A,"Model tracks strategy proportions and payoffs, not ‘contribution’ amounts in public-good units.",1,N/R,"Paper reports strategy frequencies and qualitative dominance, not normalized efficiency measures.",0.8,N/R,"Payoff matrices are described, but no aggregate group payoff statistics across the population are reported.",0.7,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from computer simulations (Mathematica code) of strategy dynamics.,1,"Two-player trust game where Nature assigns sender/receiver roles with p=1/2; sender has endowment x=1 and chooses T or nT; if T, amount is tripled to 3x and receiver chooses R (split 3x/2,3x/2) or nR (keep 3x). Strategies are role-contingent pure strategies TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR. Population updated via deterministic replicator dynamics over 1000 iterations, varying initial proportions (27 conditions).","Summarized from Sections 2, 4.1, 5.1 and Table 1.",0.99
10.1086_381411.md,"Deterministic repeated game – 16-strategy conditional/unconditional set, equal initial proportions","Section 5.2–6.2 describe repeated game simulations with 16 strategies (4 sender x 4 receiver), using deterministic replicator dynamics; Figure 7 reports results for equal initial proportions.",0.99,"Game type: repeated trust game; Strategy set: 16 strategies combining sender {T, nT, G, H} and receiver {R, nR, G, S}; Dynamics: deterministic replicator dynamic; Initial condition: equal population proportions of all 16 strategies; Number of rounds per game r varied from 1 to 30 (δ from 1 to 0.0333).",Section 5.2 introduces repeated games with r rounds; Section 5.2 and 6.1–6.2 define the 16 strategies and state that for the main deterministic results they start from equal initial proportions and vary r.,0.99,False,No human participants; simulations of strategy frequencies.,1,False,"Study is computational modeling, not an empirical controlled experiment.",1,False,No laboratory or field setting.,1,True,Explicitly described as numerical simulations with payoff matrices and replicator dynamics.,1,False,"Main reported findings based on numerical runs, not closed-form solutions.",0.9,16,"Population ‘player types’ correspond to 16 pure strategies formed by 4 sender x 4 receiver strategies (TR, TnR, TG, TS, nTR, …, HS).",0.95,30,"They vary r from 1 to 30; to characterize the configuration we take the maximum r used, where long-run cooperative behavior is emphasized.",0.9,1,Players make binary trust decisions (T or nT) each round; no continuous contribution; mapped as a discrete all-or-nothing style decision.,0.6,N/A,Trust game with discrete invest vs. not; no default contribution proportion.,1,N/R,Model defined via trust-game payoff transformations (x to 3x) not framed as MPCR.,1,N/A,Simulated agents follow fixed strategies without communication.,1,N/A,No user interface; concept not applicable.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment operator; conditional strategies incorporate retaliation but not identity revelation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward operator separate from the game’s payoffs.,1,N/A,Agents are boundedly rational rule-followers; no display of remaining rounds.,1,1,"Conditional strategies (Grim, Hopeful, Selfish) implement punishment-like behavior via future non-trust or non-reciprocation in response to defection.",0.8,N/R,No separate cost parameter is defined; costs of retaliation are implicit in foregone cooperative payoffs.,0.5,N/R,No explicit technology mapping from punishment tokens to losses is specified.,0.5,0,"There are no explicit, costly reward actions distinct from cooperation itself.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Same trust-game parameters as one-shot; x is set to 1 in simulations (Section 5.1) and repeated-game payoff matrices are built on that base game.,0.9,N/A,"Outcomes are described in terms of strategy frequencies and trust/reciprocation, not contribution fractions.",1,N/A,No explicit contribution variable is recorded; trust is a binary move each round.,1,N/R,"Paper narrates dominance of cooperative strategies as r increases, but does not provide normalized efficiency statistics.",0.7,N/R,"Cumulative payoffs per strategy pair are given in matrices, but no aggregate group payoff across the population is reported.",0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Study is entirely computational.,1,"Repeated trust game built on the base trust game (sender endowment x=1, tripled to 3x when trusted). Each repeated game consists of r rounds (r from 1 to 30, where r=1/δ and δ is the continuation probability). Strategies are role- and history-contingent: sender strategies T (always trust), nT (never trust), G (Grim: trust until not reciprocated, then never trust), H (Hopeful: trust, retaliate once after non-reciprocation, then give another chance; if repeatedly not reciprocated, stop trusting); receiver strategies R (always reciprocate), nR (never reciprocate), G (Grim on being trusted), S (Selfish: initially do not reciprocate when trusted, but switch to reciprocating if not trusted in a round). These combine into 16 pure strategies (TR, TnR, TG, TS, nTR, nTnR, nTG, nTS, GR, GnR, GG, GS, HR, HnR, HG, HS). Payoff matrices (Figures 6a, 6b) give cumulative payoffs after r rounds for every sender–receiver pair, then combined into a 16×16 symmetric matrix. Population shares of strategies evolve via deterministic replicator dynamics over 1000 games for each r. With equal initial proportions, for r=1 non-trusting strategies (nTnR and nTS) dominate; for r≈3 there is a tipping point, and for larger r, cooperative conditional strategies (HR, HG, GR, GG) make up most of the population, with some TR/TG supported by conditional strategies.","Condensed from Sections 2, 5.2, 6.1–6.2, including description of strategies, payoff construction, and dynamic behavior as r varies.",0.99
10.1086_381411.md,"Deterministic repeated game – 16-strategy set, initial population dominated by non-trusting nTnR",Section 6.2 describes an exception where nTnR initially dominates the population; Figure 10 shows results for that initial condition.,0.95,"Game type: repeated trust game; Strategy set: same 16 strategies; Dynamics: deterministic replicator dynamic; Initial condition: population initially dominated by nTnR (non-trusting, non-reciprocating) strategy; Number of rounds per game r varied from 1 to 30.","Section 6.2: ‘When nTnR is initially the most frequent strategy, we still observe a shift to the mix of strategies outcome described earlier, but not until r=10 (see Figure 10)... nTnR remains strong for low values of r, but once r equals or exceeds 10, this strategy becomes extinct.’",0.95,False,Computer simulation only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment with human participants.,1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,True,Authors explicitly call these simulations and use replicator dynamics.,1,False,Condition-level results (extinction threshold at r≈10) come from simulations.,0.9,16,Same 16-strategy polymorphic population as in the main deterministic repeated-game simulations.,0.95,30,"They vary r up to 30; the key reported effect is when r≥10, where outcomes shift away from nTnR dominance.",0.9,1,Same binary trust decision as other repeated-game simulations.,0.6,N/A,"Trust game, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,No MPCR reported.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,Not applicable to simulations.,1,N/A,No explicit ID-linked punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Boundedly rational programmed strategies; no displayed round count.,1,1,"Same conditional retaliatory strategies as in the other repeated-game simulations (Grim, Hopeful, Selfish).",0.8,N/R,No explicit punishment parameterization.,0.5,N/R,No explicit punishment technology variable.,0.5,0,No distinct costly reward action.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Same underlying trust game as elsewhere with x set to 1.,0.9,N/A,"Outcomes tracked via strategy frequencies, not contribution levels.",1,N/A,No explicit contribution measure.,1,N/R,"Only qualitative shift away from nTnR dominance reported, not efficiency.",0.7,N/R,Aggregate payoffs by condition are not provided.,0.7,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Purely computational model.,1,"Same repeated trust game and 16-strategy polymorphic population as in the equal-proportions condition, but with an initial population heavily skewed toward nTnR (never trust, never reciprocate). Deterministic replicator dynamic run for 1000 games at each r. For small r, nTnR remains highly prevalent and non-trusting behavior dominates. As r increases, there is a delayed tipping point: not until r≈10 do conditional trust/reciprocation strategies become strong enough that nTnR eventually becomes extinct in the long run, leading to a cooperative polymorphic equilibrium similar to the equal-initial-proportion case.",Summarized from Section 6.2 and Figure 10.,0.95
10.1086_381411.md,Stochastic model – 4 unconditional strategies only (B4) with mutation,"Section 6.3–6.4 examine the stochastic replicator dynamic using only the four unconditional strategies TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR; Figures 11a and 11b show outcomes.",0.98,"Strategy set: 4 unconditional strategies B4={TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR}; Dynamics: stochastic replicator dynamic with noise term s=0.01; Game type: trust game with payoffs as in one-shot case; Number of rounds r irrelevant (unconditional strategies perform the same in one-shot or repeated). Initial population proportions varied.","Section 6.4: ‘We begin by examining the behavior of the original four unconditional strategies… For all the simulations below, we are using a tuning parameter s=.01… Regardless of the initial conditions, we see a convergence…’",0.98,False,Model-only simulations; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment.,1,False,Pure computation.,1,True,Uses stochastic replicator dynamics with random shocks ε_i(t).,1,False,"Reported stable mix (TR=.01, etc.) comes from simulations, not analytic solution.",0.95,4,"Population consists only of four unconditional strategies TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR.",0.99,1,"Section 6.4 notes that for these unconditional strategies, r does not matter: ‘none of these strategies will perform any differently in a one-shot game as compared to an iterated game’; effectively equivalent to one-shot payoff matrix.",0.8,1,Binary trust decision per play; no continuous contribution.,0.6,N/A,No public-goods contribution.,1,N/R,No MPCR reported.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,Not relevant to simulations.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment operator; and under B4 there are no history-contingent retaliations.,1,N/A,No explicit reward operator.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe rounds via an interface.,1,0,"With only unconditional strategies, there is no conditional punishment or retaliation behavior.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward action separate from cooperation.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Base trust-game parameter x=1 is used throughout simulations; applies equally here.,0.9,N/A,No contribution variable; primary outcome is stationary strategy mix.,1,N/A,No explicit contributions tracked.,1,N/R,"Results reported only as mean stationary proportions: TR=.01, TnR=.01, nTR=.12, nTnR=.86.",0.7,N/R,"Group payoffs are not aggregated in the report, only relative frequencies.",0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational stochastic dynamics.,1,"Population contains only the four unconditional, role-contingent strategies TR (always trust & reciprocate), TnR (trust as sender, never reciprocate), nTR (never trust, always reciprocate), nTnR (never trust or reciprocate). Strategy fitness and proportions evolve according to a stochastic replicator dynamic: p_i(t+1) = [p_i(t) A_i p(t) / (p(t)^T A p(t))] + s|ε_i(t+1)|, with ε_i standard normal and s=0.01; proportions are renormalized each period. Over 1000 games, regardless of initial proportions, the system converges to a stochastically stable mix with mean proportions approximately TR=0.01, TnR=0.01, nTR=0.12, nTnR=0.86, with small fluctuations; extinction is prevented by mutation.",Summarized from Section 6.3–6.4 and Figures 11a–11b.,0.98
10.1086_381411.md,Stochastic model – full 16-strategy set with mutation,Section 6.5 describes simulations where all 16 strategies are included under the stochastic replicator dynamic; Figure 13a shows the final mix starting from all 16 strategies; Figure 13b shows robustness when HG dominates initially.,0.98,"Strategy set: all 16 strategies (TR, TnR, nTR, nTnR, TG, TS, nTG, nTS, GR, GnR, GG, GS, HR, HnR, HG, HS); Dynamics: stochastic replicator dynamic with tuning parameter s=0.01; Game type: repeated trust game with r sufficiently large (focus on large-r behavior); Initial population proportions varied, including equal mix and HG-dominated mix.","Section 6.5: ‘Finally, we introduced all 16 strategies together in the stochastic model. The result, illustrated in Figure 13a… In Figure 13b, we observe that this same distribution continues to hold if… HG dominates the initial population.’",0.98,False,No human subjects.,1,False,"Simulation study, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly uses stochastic replicator dynamics with random noise.,1,False,"Stationary proportions are computed numerically, not analytically solved.",0.95,16,Full polymorphic population over 16 strategies.,1,30,Section 5.2 mentions r from 1 to 30; Section 6.5 says ‘for large r’ conditional strategies dominate; we take r at the high end to represent the long-run cooperative regime.,0.85,1,Binary trust decisions per round as in other repeated-game simulations.,0.6,N/A,Not a public goods contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR provided.,1,N/A,No communication between simulated agents.,1,N/A,Not applicable to simulations.,1,N/A,"No explicit punishment tokens or identity revelation, only strategic retaliation.",1,N/A,No explicit rewards beyond cooperative play.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe round counters; they follow fixed conditional rules.,1,1,"Conditional strategies (G, H, S) implement punishment-like reductions in future cooperation conditional on past play.",0.85,N/R,Punishment is endogenous via foregone gains; no explicit per-unit cost is parameterized.,0.5,N/R,No distinct punishment technology is defined.,0.5,0,No explicit costly rewards aside from ordinary reciprocity.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Underlying round payoffs derive from the same base trust game with x=1.,0.9,N/A,"Outcome measured as stationary strategy distribution, not contributions.",1,N/A,No explicit contribution decisions.,1,N/R,"Authors focus on presence of conditional cooperation and frequencies of strategy types, not normalized efficiency.",0.7,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not numerically summarized; only strategic composition is.,0.7,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,All results from computational experiments.,1,"Same repeated trust game and 16-strategy set as in deterministic repeated simulations, but with stochastic replicator dynamics incorporating mutation: p_i(t+1) = [p_i(t) A_i p(t) / (p(t)^T A p(t))] + s|ε_i(t+1)|, s=0.01, followed by renormalization. For large r, and regardless of initial conditions (equal proportions or initial dominance of HG), the population converges to a stochastically stable polymorphic mix where conditional cooperative strategies dominate. In the reported equilibrium, HR and HG each occupy roughly 20% of the population; GR and GG about 10% each; GS and HS about 6% each; most other strategies around 4–5%, with non-reciprocating types around 2% and nTnR around 1%, the latter surviving only due to the mutation term. The equilibrium mix is robust to initial conditions and variability in s (for positive s) and represents a stochastically stable state supporting widespread trust and reciprocity.",Condensed from Section 6.5 and Figures 13a–13b.,0.98
