10.1111_j.1439-0310.2011.01882.x.md,"N/A – theoretical review, no distinct experimental or simulation conditions","The paper is an invited review and conceptual framework; it does not report any new, well-defined experiments, simulations, or observational datasets with their own design parameters. It only cites others’ work illustratively without giving full experimental details.",1,N/A,No experimental or observational conditions are actually implemented in this paper; it only discusses conceptual ‘game structures’ and previously published studies at a descriptive level.,1,False,No new data with human or non-human participants are collected; the paper is a conceptual/theoretical review.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run by the authors; only literature and theory are discussed.,1,False,"There is no experiment at all, so it cannot be a lab experiment.",1,False,The paper does not present numerical or agent-based simulations; it is purely verbal/theoretical.,1,True,"The article develops a general scheme and decision tree linking game parameters to partner control mechanisms; this is a conceptual/analytical (verbal game-theoretic) framework without explicit new mathematical derivations but still fits ‘analytical, no human’.",0.8,2,The framework explicitly restricts itself to two-player interactions in game-theoretic terms (“We restrict ourselves to an analysis of interactions between two players”).,1,0,No single implemented game is run; the framework discusses both one-off and repeated interactions abstractly. A concrete number of rounds is never instantiated.,1,0,"Contribution decisions are not instantiated as a choice variable like in a public goods game. The paper talks about ‘cooperate’ vs ‘defect’ as binary strategic options, not contributions along a continuum, but there is no actual implemented contribution choice. Marked as 0 only because a specific contribution mechanism does not exist here.",0.3,N/A,No endowment or contribution mechanism with human players is implemented.,1,N/R,No linear public-good or similar payoff function with marginal per capita return is specified.,1,N/A,No human or animal experiment with controllable communication is implemented; communication networks are discussed conceptually only.,1,0,No experimental interface or feedback design exists in this review.,1,N/A,No punishment implementation with identifiable actors is run; punishment is only discussed conceptually across species.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented experimentally.,1,0,"There is no interface showing the number of rounds, since there is no experiment.",1,0,Punishment is discussed as a partner-control mechanism in nature but is not actually implemented in a game or experiment in this paper.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology is instantiated.,1,N/A,No explicit mapping from punishment units to payoff reductions is defined.,1,0,Reward is not implemented; only conceptual discussion of positive reciprocity and pseudoreciprocity.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology is instantiated.,1,N/A,No mapping from reward units to payoff gains is defined.,1,N/R,The paper does not specify any token/point endowments because it does not run a game.,1,N/R,No experimental data on contributions are collected.,1,N/R,No experimental data on contribution amounts are collected.,1,N/R,No payoffs are empirically measured; efficiency is not computed.,1,N/R,No empirical payoffs are reported.,1,N/A,No human participants are used.,1,N/A,No human participants are used.,1,N/A,No human participants are used.,1,N/A,No human participants are used.,1,No human,The work is a theoretical review/game-structure framework without human or non-human subjects run by the authors.,1,"Conceptual decision tree for two-player cooperation games linking five key parameters (investment, relative payoff of cooperate vs defect, payoff symmetry, number of interactions, and partner choice) plus an extension with communication networks to nine main partner-control mechanisms (by-product mutualism, direct/indirect reciprocity and pseudoreciprocity, punishment, sanctions, partner switching, power, parcelling, indirect reciprocity, social prestige).",Summary of the theoretical framework as described in the text and Fig. 1.,1
