10.1038_s41598-017-16140-8.md,"Simulation – stochastic evolutionary voluntary public goods game with punishment (parameter sweeps over α, γ, r, σ)","The paper presents one unified stochastic evolutionary model analyzed via numerical simulations while varying punishment probability α, punishment cost γ, public goods return r, and non-participation income coefficient σ. All figures and discussions refer to this single simulation framework.",0.98,"Punishment probability α, punishment cost γ, public goods return coefficient r, and non-participation fixed income coefficient σ (swept over ranges: α ∈ {0.1, 0.5, 1}, γ ∈ {0.3, 0.8}, r ∈ [1.1, N), σ ∈ (0, r−1))","Model description and Results section state that parameters M=20, N=4, c=1, κ=1, β=1 are fixed and the effects of α, γ, r, σ on stochastic stable equilibria are investigated by numerical simulation.",0.97,False,No human or animal participants; purely theoretical and computational model.,1,False,"Work consists of analytical derivations and numerical simulations, not a controlled behavioral or field experiment.",1,False,No laboratory or experimental setting with human subjects.,1,True,They numerically compute limit distributions of a Markov/quasi-birth-and-death process using Gauss-Seidel iterations and refer to these as numerical experiments.,1,True,"Paper develops formal payoff expressions, transition rates, and Markov process structure analytically before running simulations.",1,4,N is defined as the number of individuals randomly selected to play the public goods game each time; later fixed as N=4 for simulations.,1,0,Infinite-horizon stochastic process; not structured as discrete finite rounds in an experiment. Set to 0 to indicate not applicable in this simulation context.,0.7,1,"Contribution cost c is a fixed amount for cooperators/punishers while defectors contribute 0; players do not choose arbitrary continuous contribution levels, so the contribution decision is effectively all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,"Endowment allocation between private and public accounts is not modeled for individual decision; the model assumes fixed cost c for contributors, and there are no human participants.",1,N/R,"Multiplier r and group size N are given so MPCR would be r/N; with N=4 and variable r, MPCR would vary, but authors never explicitly define or use MPCR.",0.4,N/A,"No communication modeled; additionally, there are no human participants.",1,0,Model has no information-display structure like feedback screens; interactions are encoded only via expected payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No notion of identities or observability of who punishes whom; abstract population model with anonymous mixing.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Time is continuous and infinite; participants are not modeled as observing round counts.,0.9,1,Punishing strategy P is explicitly included; defection types incur penalties when matched with punishers.,1,1,"Cost per punishment act to punisher is γ; in payoff expressions punishment cost term is (expected # punished defectors) × α × γ, so each punishment event costs γ to punisher.",0.95,1,"Fine per punishment act on defectors is β; payoff deduction term is (expected # punishers) × α × β, so each punishment event yields a fine β to target.",0.95,0,Only costly punishment and optional participation are modeled; no rewarding strategy or positive sanction is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Cost of cooperation (and participation) is denoted c; for simulations they set c=1. This effectively functions as per-round endowment invested by cooperators/punishers.,0.8,N/R,"Outputs are probabilities of different equilibrium states (All L, All D, C+P coexistence). They do not report average contribution fractions or normalize contributions relative to endowment.",0.3,N/R,No numeric time-averaged contributions are reported; only qualitative and graphical description of equilibrium type probabilities.,0.2,N/R,"They discuss evolution of cooperation and stable states, not aggregate payoffs relative to maximum possible payoffs.",0.2,N/R,"Expected payoffs per type are defined, but final reported results are probabilities of equilibrium states, not group payoff levels.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical and computational study with no empirical data.,1,"Finite well-mixed population of size M=20; four strategies: cooperation (C), defection (D), punishment (P), and non-participation (L). Each period N=4 individuals are randomly sampled. Cooperators and punishers each pay cost c=1 to contribute; defectors pay 0. Public good return is r (1<r<N); total contributions r·c·(n_C+n_P) are shared equally among S participating individuals (C, D, P). Non-participants and solitary participants (S=1) obtain fixed income σ·c. Defectors interacting with punishers are punished with probability α, suffering fine β per punishment event; punishers incur cost γ per punished defector. Strategy update follows a continuous-time Markov process with mutation/noise ε>0 and response speed κ=1, with transition rates between strategies based on positive payoff differences.","Summarized from model description in Results and Methods: voluntary public goods game with punishment and optional participation, finite population M=20, N=4, c=1, κ=1, β=1.",0.98
10.1371_journal.pone.0085531.md,Monte Carlo simulation – baseline evolutionary crime game,Paper describes one Monte Carlo–simulated evolutionary game with varying parameter values but no distinct named experimental conditions beyond parameter sweeps; treat the main simulation framework as a single simulation condition.,0.7,"Model parameters of the evolutionary crime game: rehabilitation intensity h (0–1), assistance duration τ (≤3), punishment severity θ (0–1), intrinsic crime propensity parameter p0 (various values), under fixed apprehension probability α=0.25 and maximum punished crimes R=3. Some analyses impose a resource constraint h·τ + θ = c (with c ∈ {0.4, 0.6, 0.8}).","The paper varies h, τ, θ, p0, and sometimes c, exploring their effects on outcomes such as P/U ratio, crime and recidivism rates.",0.95,False,All results are from Monte Carlo simulations and ODEs; no human participants are used.,1,False,"Work is purely theoretical/simulation-based, not a controlled experiment with empirical subjects.",1,False,No laboratory or in-person environment; only computational models.,1,True,They implement the criminal game as Monte Carlo simulations tracking individuals over rounds.,1,True,They also derive and analyze a system of coupled ODEs approximating the simulation dynamics.,1,400,They state: “we investigate how all of the above quantities vary … for a set of N=400 individuals.”,1,100,"Figure 1 description notes curves are truncated at r ≃ 100, when P+U=N and the game ends; this implies the game typically finishes by about 100 rounds.",0.7,1,"Players face an opportunity to commit a crime (binary: commit vs not) rather than choosing a continuous contribution level, but this mapping to public-goods-style contribution is not used; mark as 1 (not an all-or-nothing contribution structure in the sense of fraction of endowment) is inappropriate, yet schema forces 0/1. Given actions are binary, we interpret as all-or-nothing and set 0.",0.3,N/A,"Model is about criminal offenses and rehabilitation, not contributions to a public good; no endowment or initial contribution proportion.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined in the model.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely computational agents.,1,0,"Agents respond to aggregate societal state (fractions like ΣNk/N) but this is part of the model’s probability rules, not information shown to human subjects; no explicit notion of displayed summaries.",0.8,N/A,No identities or display of punishment to human players; purely simulated population fractions.,1,N/A,"No explicit individual rewards are displayed; only abstract assistance parameters h, τ in the model.",1,0,"No human subjects, thus nothing is displayed about remaining rounds.",1,1,Punishment severity θ is central; criminals are punished with amount θ upon arrest.,1,N/R,"Model uses θ as severity of punishment, not an explicit per-unit cost paid by a player; no numeric cost structure per punishment unit is specified.",0.4,N/R,No separate mapping from punishment coins to payoff reduction is given; θ directly modifies crime propensity.,0.4,1,Rehabilitation/assistance (the “carrot”) with intensity h and duration τ acts as a positive intervention after punishment.,1,N/R,They define an aggregate resource cost h·τ but not a per-unit cost per token or action.,0.6,N/R,"No explicit linear technology mapping assistance units to payoff; h and τ alter probabilities, not monetary payoffs.",0.4,N/R,"No monetary or token endowment per player is defined; model tracks crime and state transitions, not payoffs.",1,N/R,"No cooperative contribution variable exists; main outcomes are P/U ratio, crimes per player, punishments per player, recidivism rates.",1,N/R,"As above, no contribution amount metric is defined.",1,N/R,They do not define or report efficiency as payoff relative to maximum payoff.,1,N/R,No explicit payoff totals are provided; only crime-related statistics and P/U ratios.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,No human,Research consists solely of Monte Carlo simulations and analytical ODEs with no human or field data.,1,"Evolutionary crime and rehabilitation model with N=400 agents, subpopulations Nk (history of k crimes), paladins P, unreformables U (after R=3 punished crimes). Agents face crime opportunities each round with probability p_crime depending on personal history (punished/unpunished offenses via θ, k_p, k_u, p0), societal crime level (Σ_{k≠0}Nk/N), and attenuation from post-punishment assistance (h, τ). Apprehension probability α=1/4; upon arrest, agents receive punishment θ and assistance of intensity h decaying over τ. After each opportunity, agents may reform and become paladins with probability p_reform depending on P/N, α, θ, k_p, k_u, p0. The game ends when all agents are in P or U, with outcomes summarized by final P/U ratio, total crimes per player, punishments per player, and recidivism rates. Parameter sweeps explore effects of h, θ, τ, p0 and resource constraint h·τ+θ=c.","Summarizes the formal game structure and key mechanisms as described in Analysis, Methods, and Results sections.",0.98
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2015.02.002.md,P1 – Treatment A – P2 stole – Effective punishment,"Condition defined by P1 role, treatment A endowments, P2 stealing, and effective punishment fee-to-fine 1:3.",1,"Initial endowments: P1 $1.10, P2 $0.60 (Treatment A); P2 action: stole; Punishment condition: effective (fee-to-fine 1:3).",These are the manipulated factors that distinguish this condition.,1,True,Uses 4912 human AMT subjects.,1,True,"Random assignment to roles, treatments, and punishment conditions in a controlled game.",1,False,"Run on Amazon Mechanical Turk, which is an online environment rather than a physical lab.",0.9,False,No computer-only simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical model; this is a behavioral experiment.,1,2,Game explicitly involves player one (P1) and player two (P2).,1,1,Described as an anonymous one-shot game with two stages but a single interaction.,1,1,"P2 can choose to steal or not (binary), but P1 can assign 0–4 punishment points, i.e., multiple discrete contribution levels.",0.8,0,No public good; all money starts as private endowments.,1,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game.,1,0,No mention of communication between players.,0.9,0,P1 only sees P2’s steal/not decision; no earnings summaries reported.,0.8,0,Game conducted anonymously on AMT; no identities revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,Described as one-shot; participants know there is only a single interaction.,0.8,1,Stage 2 allows P1 to assign punishment points to P2.,1,0.05,Each punishment point cost P1 $0.05 in both punishment conditions.,1,0.15,Effective punishment: each point reduces P2’s earnings by $0.15 (1:3 fee-to-fine).,1,0,"Only punishment points, no reward or positive tokens available.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1.1,"In treatment A, P1’s stage 1 payoff is $1.10, which is their initial endowment.",1,N/R,No report of normalized punishment or stealing rates framed as contribution rate.,0,N/R,"Paper reports proportions of P1s choosing non-zero punishment, but not mean punishment points by treatment.",0,N/R,No aggregate efficiency or payoff ratio statistics reported.,0,N/R,No mean or total payoffs by treatment reported.,0,USA,“Subjects were all based in the USA.”,1,N/R,No age information given.,0,"Mixed (2856 males, 1967 females, 89 unreported) at study level",Overall gender breakdown reported but not by condition.,0.7,N/R,No education information provided.,0,Online,Experiment conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Two-player one-shot stealing and punishment game. Stage 1: P2 can steal $0.20 from P1. Stage 2: P1 learns P2’s decision and can assign 0–4 punishment points. Condition: Treatment A endowments (P1 $1.10, P2 $0.60), P2 stole, effective punishment (1:3). P1 loss from stealing is $0.20, resulting in advantageous inequality ($0.90:$0.80) for P1 before punishment.",Summarizes structural details for this specific condition from Table 1 and methods.,1
