tial token endowment is specified.,0.3,0.21,"In PD, treat cooperation as contribution. Reported mean individual proportion of cooperation in No-Info is M = .21.",1,N/R,Only proportions of cooperation reported; no count of cooperative moves or token amounts.,0.3,N/R,They report pair point totals but not normalized efficiency relative to theoretical maximum.,0.4,-232,Performance measured as sum of both players' points over 200 rounds; No-Info mean is M = -232.,1,USA,"Study conducted at Carnegie Mellon University (Pittsburgh, PA, USA); participants presumably in USA.",0.9,"M = 24.1, SD = 5.51 (pooled across all conditions)",Overall sample age given; no breakdown by condition.,0.9,"30% women, 70% men in this condition",Text gives gender proportion by condition: 30% women in No-Info.,1,N/R,No education information provided.,1,On site,Participants recruited to a computer laboratory at Carnegie Mellon University and seated in different rooms.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with 200 unnumbered rounds; payoffs per round: (A,A) = (−1, −1), (A,B) = (10, −10), (B,A) = (−10, 10), (B,B) = (1,1). Choices simultaneous, anonymous. In No-Info, subjects not told they have an opponent and only see own action and payoff each round; they know payoffs depend on their action and unspecified ‘other factors’. Payoffs converted at 1 cent per point plus $10 base.","Summarized from method, Table 1, and condition instructions.",1
10.1002_bdm.1810.md,Min-Info condition,Second information treatment where interdependence is acknowledged but counterpart’s specific actions/outcomes are hidden.,1,"Interdependence information level: Min-Info (participants know they interact with another player and that both payoffs depend on both actions, but they only see their own action and payoff; not the other’s actions or outcomes).",Described under Information conditions as Minimal information level.,1,True,Human laboratory participants.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of information condition.,1,True,Same lab setting and procedure as other conditions.,1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,No separate analytical model as experiment.,1,2,Pairs of participants.,1,200,Same 200 unnumbered rounds for all conditions.,1,1,Binary A/B choice each round; see reasoning in No-Info condition.,0.2,0,No endowment allocation; same reasoning as No-Info.,0.2,N/R,Not a public goods game; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,1,0,Only individual round feedback; no summaries mentioned.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Players not told number of rounds; unnumbered.,1,0,"Only PD choices, no costly punishment stage.",1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit costly reward action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No token endowment specified.,0.3,0.21,Reported mean individual proportion of cooperation in Min-Info is M = .21.,1,N/R,No raw count of cooperative moves reported.,0.3,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure reported.,0.4,-220,Pair performance (sum of points) in Min-Info is M = -220.,1,USA,Same sample/location as No-Info condition.,0.9,"M = 24.1, SD = 5.51 (pooled across all conditions)",Overall sample age; not broken down by condition.,0.9,"50% women, 50% men in this condition",Gender proportion given: 50% women in Min-Info.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,On site,Same lab environment for all four conditions.,1,"Same 2-player 200-round IPD as other conditions with payoff matrix: (A,A) = (−1, −1), (A,B) = (10, −10), (B,A) = (−10, 10), (B,B) = (1,1). In Min-Info, each player is told they have an anonymous opponent whose action jointly determines payoffs, but they only observe their own action and payoff each round; they do not see the other’s actions or payoffs or the full payoff matrix. Payoff conversion 1 cent per point plus $10 base.",Summarized from Methods and Min-Info instructions.,1
10.1002_bdm.1810.md,Mid-Info condition,Third information treatment where players see each other’s actions and outcomes over time.,1,Interdependence information level: Mid-Info (participants know they interact with another player; both players’ actions and payoffs revealed each round over time; no explicit payoff matrix).,Described as Experiential information level with observation of counterpart’s actions and outcomes as they occur.,1,True,Human-subject IPD in the lab.,1,True,Part of same randomized information-condition manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in computer lab with participants in separate rooms.,1,False,Not a computer-only model.,1,False,No separate analytical-only study.,1,2,Pairs of participants.,1,200,Same across all conditions.,1,1,Binary A/B choice structure; same reasoning as other conditions.,0.2,0,No initial endowment allocation to a public account.,0.2,N/R,"IPD, not public goods.",1,0,No chat or communication channel mentioned.,1,0,"Players see round-by-round actions and payoffs, but no mention of aggregate summaries.",0.8,0,No punishment institution; only simultaneous moves with anonymous opponent.,1,0,No explicit reward institution.,1,0,Total number of rounds not disclosed; rounds unnumbered.,1,0,No costly punishment stage in the game.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,No explicit costly reward action.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/R,No per-round or initial token endowment specified.,0.3,0.31,Reported mean individual proportion of cooperation in Mid-Info is M = .31.,1,N/R,"Only proportions, not counts, of cooperation reported.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency ratio reported.,0.4,-149,Pair performance (sum of points) in Mid-Info is M = -149.,1,USA,Same sample from CMU in USA.,0.9,"M = 24.1, SD = 5.51 (pooled across all conditions)",No condition-specific ages.,0.9,"43% women, 57% men in this condition",Gender proportion reported: 43% women in Mid-Info.,1,N/R,Not discussed.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at CMU.,1,"Same 2-player 200-round IPD with payoff matrix (A,A) = (−1, −1), (A,B) = (10, −10), (B,A) = (−10, 10), (B,B) = (1,1). In Mid-Info, each round both players choose A or B simultaneously; afterwards, both players see both actions and both resulting payoffs but are not shown a complete payoff matrix ex ante. Payoffs converted to cash at 1 cent per point plus $10 base.",Summarized from Mid-Info condition instructions and overall method.,1
10.1002_bdm.1810.md,Max-Info condition,Fourth information treatment with full payoff matrix plus experiential information.,1,Interdependence information level: Max-Info (participants see both players’ actions and payoffs each round and have continuous access to the full payoff matrix from the outset).,Described as Descriptive information level with payoff matrix shown throughout.,1,True,Human IPD experiment.,1,True,Random assignment of pairs to information conditions; controlled procedures.,1,True,All conditions run in a computer laboratory.,1,False,No separate simulation-only condition.,1,False,No formal analytical study treated as an experiment.,1,2,Two-player IPD.,1,200,Same repeated game length as others.,1,1,Binary A/B action each period; see earlier reasoning.,0.2,0,No initial allocation to public account.,0.2,N/R,IPD setup; MPCR not specified.,1,0,No communication allowed beyond implicit signaling via actions.,1,0,"Players see matrix and period-by-period feedback, but no mention of summary statistics.",0.8,0,No punishment stage; identities remain anonymous.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,0,Number of rounds unknown to participants.,1,0,No separate costly punishment decision.,1,N/A,Punishment not implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism; only PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/R,No initial token endowment described.,0.3,0.43,Reported mean individual proportion of cooperation in Max-Info is M = .43.,1,N/R,Only proportions of cooperation given.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency (fraction of max possible payoffs) not reported.,0.4,-53.7,Pair performance (sum of both players' points) in Max-Info is M = -53.7.,1,USA,Same CMU lab sample.,0.9,"M = 24.1, SD = 5.51 (pooled across all conditions)",Age data only given for full sample.,0.9,"35% women, 65% men in this condition",Condition-level gender reported: 35% women in Max-Info.,1,N/R,Education not discussed.,1,On site,Same laboratory environment for all conditions.,1,"Same 2-player, 200-round simultaneous-move IPD with payoff matrix: if both choose A, both get −1; if you choose A and opponent B, you get 10, opponent −10; if you choose B and opponent A, you get −10, opponent 10; if both choose B, both get 1. In Max-Info, this payoff matrix is shown from the outset and throughout the interaction, and after each round both players see both actions and resulting payoffs. Payoffs converted to money at 1 cent per point plus a $10 base payment.",From Table 1 and Max-Info instructions.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2012.03.027.md,"Fixed tolerance model – h = 0.1 (local mutation, r = 4.0)","Condition with uniform tolerance h = 0.1; main fixed-h treatment used in Fig. 1(a), Fig. 2, Fig. 3, Fig. 4 with r = 4.0 and local mutation.",0.93,Uniform tolerance level h fixed at 0.1; punishment cost α and fine β varied; enhancement factor r = 4.0; local (small) mutation in strategy only,"Model section and captions: h = 0.1, r = 4.0 in Fig. 1(a); Fig. 2 and Fig. 3 use h fixed (implicit 0.1 or as specified), with α and β varied. Mutation described as small local perturbation of s.",0.9,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice with N = 10,000 artificial individuals; no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational evolutionary model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; simulation only.,1,True,Explicitly described as Monte Carlo simulations of an evolutionary game on a lattice.,1,False,"Although some analytical background is cited, the results reported here are numerical simulations, not closed-form derivations.",0.9,10000,"Results section: population of N = 10,000 individuals on a square lattice.",1,11000,"They run 10,000 generations as transient and average over 1,000 generations; total simulated update steps ≈ 11,000.",0.7,1,"Continuous public goods game with investment s_i in [0,1]; players can choose any amount in this interval.",1,N/A,No human participants or endowment allocation; agents directly choose continuous investment s_i.,1,0.8,"Payoff from one group is r * sum_j s_j / 5 - s_i. Group size = 5, enhancement factor r = 4.0, so marginal per capita return = r / group size = 4 / 5 = 0.8.",0.95,N/A,No human subjects; communication is not modeled.,1,0,Simulation agents only observe payoffs of themselves and a role model neighbor for imitation; no mention of summary information.,0.8,0,Model has no explicit identities or observability of who punished whom beyond payoff effects; nothing suggests revealed identities.,0.7,0,There is no rewarding mechanism in the model.,1,0,Agents do not have explicit information about total number of generations; evolution proceeds indefinitely in simulation.,0.7,1,Core of the model is costly punishment based on tolerance; payoff P'_i subtracts punishment costs and fines.,1,N/R,"α is varied over a range in Fig. 1 and Fig. 2; this condition is a family of (α, β) values, not one fixed α.",0.2,N/R,Fine β per punisher is varied; no single fixed ratio is specified for this aggregated condition.,0.2,0,"Only punishment (cost α, fine β) is modeled; no positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses investments s_i ∈ [0,1]; no explicit per-round endowment in discrete units is defined.",0.4,N/R,"They report average investment, but not a normalized rate explicitly labeled or numerically specified; plots are qualitative.",0.3,N/R,Average investment values are shown graphically but not given as explicit numeric values in text or tables.,0.3,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,0.1,N/R,"They focus on average investment distributions, not on payoff statistics.",0.1,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is based on computer simulations without human subjects.,1,"Continuous public goods game on a 100x100 periodic square lattice; each individual participates in 5 overlapping groups (self plus 4 neighbors). Investment s_i ∈ [0,1] contributed to each group; group contribution multiplied by r = 4 and equally shared. Strategies updated by pairwise imitation with Fermi rule (κ = 0.1) and local mutation δ ∈ (−0.005, 0.005). Uniform tolerance h = 0.1; players punish neighbors j with s_j < s_i − h; punisher pays cost α per punished neighbor and target pays fine β per punisher.",Summarizes rules from the Model section specific to fixed uniform h treatments and the lattice structure.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2012.03.027.md,"Fixed tolerance model – h = 0.3 (local mutation, r = 4.0)",Condition with uniform tolerance h = 0.3; directly contrasted with h = 0.1 in Fig. 1(b); same basic model parameters otherwise.,0.95,Uniform tolerance level h fixed at 0.3; punishment cost α and fine β varied; enhancement factor r = 4.0; local (small) mutation in strategy only,"Caption of Fig. 1(b): h = 0.3, r = 4.0. α and β varied over grid; mutation rule identical to base model.",0.95,False,Same Monte Carlo simulation framework; no human participants.,1,False,"Computational model, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab/human setting.,1,True,Results generated via simulations on square lattice.,1,False,Outcomes shown via simulation plots; no closed-form solution.,0.9,10000,"Same N = 10,000 individuals on 100x100 lattice.",1,11000,"Same transient of 10,000 generations plus 1,000 for averaging.",0.7,1,"Continuous strategies s_i ∈ [0,1].",1,N/A,Simulation without explicit initial endowment.,1,0.8,"Same payoff function and r = 4.0, group size 5 → MPCR = 4/5.",0.95,N/A,No human communication modeled.,1,0,No description of summary feedback; only local payoffs used for imitation.,0.8,0,Identity is not a modeled dimension; punishment only affects payoffs.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents not described as knowing horizon; runs are for analysis only.,0.7,1,Core of this treatment is costly punishment with tolerance threshold.,1,N/R,α is varied systematically; not a single fixed value in this condition.,0.2,N/R,Fine β per punisher varied; no single value applicable.,0.2,0,Only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No explicit discrete endowment per round defined.,0.4,N/R,Average investment outcomes shown only via contour plots; no numeric values reported.,0.3,N/R,Investment distributions are described qualitatively; no exact numbers given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric is calculated.,0.1,N/R,They do not tabulate total or average payoffs.,0.1,N/A,Simulation agents only.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computational simulations with artificial agents.,1,"Same continuous PGG on a 100x100 periodic square lattice with group size 5 and enhancement factor r = 4.0. Uniform tolerance h = 0.3; punishment rule: agent i punishes j if s_j < s_i − h, paying cost α per punished neighbor and inflicting fine β per punisher. Strategies updated with Fermi rule (κ = 0.1) and local mutation of s_i by δ ∈ (−0.005, 0.005).",As in base model but with higher uniform tolerance parameter.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2012.03.027.md,"Co-evolution model – variable tolerance and investment (local mutation, r = 4.0)","Condition where each individual's strategy includes both investment s and tolerance h, both subject to imitation and mutation; results shown in Fig. 5 and Fig. 6.",0.96,"Coevolving investment s_i and tolerance h_i; initial s_i and h_i random in (0,1); punishment cost α and fine β varied; enhancement factor r = 4.0; local mutation in both s and h","Results section: 'We combine an individual's investment and tolerance as his strategy ... Initially, both individuals' tolerances and investments obey a statistically random distribution in the interval (0,1).' Fig. 5 uses r = 4.0 and varies α, β.",0.96,False,Still purely simulation-based with artificial agents.,1,False,No empirical or field data; just computational experiments.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on spatial lattice as before.,1,False,Dynamics explored via simulation; no analytical solution.,0.9,10000,"Same lattice size N = 10,000 given for all simulations.",1,11000,"Same protocol: 10,000 generations transient plus 1,000 for averaging per data point.",0.7,1,"Continuous investment s_i ∈ [0,1].",1,N/A,No human endowments; continuous investment variable.,1,0.8,Same PGG structure with r = 4.0 and group size 5.,0.95,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,Only local payoffs used for strategy updating; no aggregate feedback.,0.8,0,Punishment is implicit via payoffs; no explicit identity or observability beyond local interactions.,0.7,0,No reward in the game.,1,0,Agents not modeled as knowing total generations.,0.7,1,Punishment based on tolerance remains central: i punishes j if s_j < s_i − h_i.,1,N/R,α is varied across a range; no single α value for this aggregated coevolution condition.,0.2,N/R,β is varied; no single fixed fine level.,0.2,0,No positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game defined directly in terms of investments; endowment not explicitly specified.,0.4,N/R,They report average investment levels in Fig. 5(a) and Fig. 6 qualitatively; no numerical values.,0.3,N/R,Investment levels shown via contour/point plots; no exact numbers in text.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency measure reported.,0.1,N/R,Payoffs are internal to update rule; not summarized.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Evolutionary game analyzed via computer simulation only.,1,"Continuous PGG on a 100x100 periodic square lattice (N = 10,000). Each agent has two continuous traits: investment s_i ∈ [0,1] and tolerance h_i ∈ (0,1). Initially, s_i and h_i are random in (0,1). Agents play in 5 groups (self plus 4 neighbors); group contributions multiplied by r = 4.0 and shared equally. Punishment rule: in each group, i punishes j if s_j < s_i − h_i; each punishment costs α to punisher and imposes fine β on punished. Strategies (both s and h) are updated by imitating a randomly chosen neighbor j with Fermi rule (κ = 0.1); if imitation occurs, s_i(t+1) = s_j(t) + δ_s and h_i(t+1) = h_j(t) + δ_h with small random mutations δ in (−0.005, 0.005).",Summarizes the coevolutionary extension of the base model as described in Results.,1
