10.1007_s10683-005-0869-9.md,Fehr & Gächter 2000 – Stranger treatment,Paper explicitly re-analyzes Fehr and Gächter (2000) and distinguishes Stranger and Partner treatments with same game but different matching protocol.,0.95,"Matching protocol: random re-matching each round (Stranger); Punishment technology: FG variable fine-to-fee ratio with cost schedule in Table 2 and public good parameters a=0.4, N=4.","Text: 'In one treatment, group composition was changed after each round by randomly matching the 24 participants (""Stranger"") while in another treatment groups were stable across the ten rounds (""Partner"").' Both use same FG punishment technology described by expression (1) and Table 2.",0.96,True,"Study re-analyzes data from Fehr and Gächter (2000), a lab experiment with human subjects in public good game with punishment.",1,True,Fehr and Gächter (2000) is a controlled laboratory experiment manipulating punishment opportunities and matching.,1,True,"Fehr and Gächter (2000) is a standard lab public goods experiment (non-field, controlled environment).",0.98,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only human-subject data and analytical decomposition.,1,False,"Paper contains analytical discussion of technologies but this condition refers to empirical Fehr & Gächter data, not a pure analytical model.",0.9,4,Text: 'first-stage game ... in groups of four persons.',1,10,"Text: 'groups were stable across the ten rounds (""Partner"").' Same number of rounds applies to Stranger treatment in Fehr and Gächter (2000).",0.9,1,"Contribution choice: 'individual contribution to the public good is g_i ∈ [0,20]' implying continuous/any amount, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,Standard VCM: each subject receives private endowment and decides how much to contribute; endowment starts in private account (no automatic contribution).,0.8,0.4,"Payoff function: 'a=0.4, N=4' and each token contributed yields a to everyone; marginal per capita return equals a=0.4.",0.95,0,No mention of communication or chat; Fehr & Gächter (2000) design is known as no-communication baseline.,0.8,1,Punishment requires knowing others' contributions and earnings; standard design shows others' contribution decisions and likely earnings summary before punishment. Otherwise participants cannot target free riders. Inferred from description: 'each agent learns about the action taken by the other and then has the opportunity to punish.',0.75,1,Peer punishment within fixed groups implies that individual identities (within group) must be visible to assign p_i^j; punishment points are directed to specific group members.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism in the FG design; only punishment points are mentioned.,1,1,Standard repeated public good experiments with fixed horizon inform subjects about the number of periods; not directly stated but typical for Fehr & Gächter (2000).,0.7,1,Central topic is 'peer punishment' with explicit punishment stage and cost schedule c(p_i^j).,1,1,"Cost per first punishment point is 1 (Table 2: for p_i^j=1, cost=1). This is the basic marginal cost at the lowest level.",1,0.1,Fine per punishment point equals 10% of first-stage earnings: 'earnings ... are lowered by 10% for each punishment point received.' Fine as fraction of target’s earnings is 0.1 per point.,0.95,0,No mention of any reward or positive sanction; only costly punishment points.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"Contribution choice g_i ∈ [0,20] and payoff term is (20 - g_i + a Σ g_j); 20 is per-period endowment.",1,N/R,"Paper re-analyzes only punishment, not average contribution levels or rates.",0,N/R,No numerical results on average contributions provided here.,0,N/R,No aggregate efficiency metrics reported.,0,N/R,No numeric payoff outcomes given.,0,N/R,Country of Fehr & Gächter (2000) subject pool not stated in this note.,0,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,No gender information reported.,0,N/R,Likely university students but not stated here.,0,On site,Fehr & Gächter (2000) is a standard physical laboratory experiment (pre-online era) though this note doesn't repeat full procedural details.,0.85,"Linear public goods game with parameters: 4 players, per-period endowment 20, contribution choice g_i ∈ [0,20], return function 20 - g_i + a Σ g_j with a=0.4, punishment stage where each subject can assign 0–10 punishment points p_i^j to each other group member. Cost schedule c(p_i^j): 0,1,2,4,6,9,12,16,20,25,30. Each punishment point reduces target’s first-stage earnings by 10%, with payoff truncated at zero if punishment exceeds earnings. Matching protocol: stranger (random rematching each of 10 rounds among 24 participants).","Directly synthesized from expressions (1), Table 2, and text describing Stranger treatment and group size.",0.98
10.1007_s10683-005-0869-9.md,Fehr & Gächter 2000 – Partner treatment,Same experimental game as Stranger treatment but with stable group composition; identified in text as separate treatment.,0.95,"Matching protocol: fixed partners for all 10 rounds (Partner); Punishment technology: FG variable fine-to-fee ratio with cost schedule in Table 2 and public good parameters a=0.4, N=4.","Text: 'in another treatment groups were stable across the ten rounds (""Partner"").' FG punishment technology identical across matching treatments.",0.96,True,Human-subject lab experiment data from Fehr & Gächter (2000).,1,True,Controlled laboratory public goods experiment with punishment.,1,True,Fehr & Gächter (2000) conducted in physical lab.,0.98,False,No computer-only simulation for this condition.,1,False,"Although the note contains analytical decomposition, this row refers to empirical Partner treatment.",0.9,4,Same game as Stranger: 'groups of four persons.',1,10,"Text: 'groups were stable across the ten rounds (""Partner"").'",1,1,"Contribution choice g_i ∈ [0,20] implies any amount allowed.",1,0,Same VCM structure as Stranger; endowment starts private.,0.8,0.4,"Same parameters: a=0.4, N=4.",0.95,0,No mention of communication.,0.8,1,"As in Stranger, punishment stage requires knowledge of others’ contribution/earnings.",0.75,1,Punishment points are assigned to specific group members in fixed groups; IDs must be visible.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Fixed and stated number of periods is standard; not explicitly given but inferred.,0.7,1,Same FG punishment technology.,1,1,Cost of the first punishment point c(1)=1.,1,0.1,Each punishment point removes 10% of target’s earnings.,0.95,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same as Stranger; endowment 20 implied by payoff function.,1,N/R,No contribution statistics for Partner treatment reported here.,0,N/R,Not provided in this note.,0,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0,N/R,No payoff outcomes given.,0,N/R,Country not mentioned in this note.,0,N/R,No age information.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,N/R,Likely students but not stated.,0,On site,Fehr & Gächter (2000) conducted in a physical laboratory environment.,0.85,"Same linear public goods game and FG punishment technology as Stranger treatment: 4-person groups; 10 periods; per-period endowment 20; contribution choice g_i ∈ [0,20]; payoff 20 - g_i + a Σ g_j with a=0.4; after seeing others’ contributions, each subject can assign 0–10 punishment points p_i^j to each other group member with cost schedule c(p_i^j) = {0,1,2,4,6,9,12,16,20,25,30}; each point reduces target’s first-stage earnings by 10% with truncation at zero. Matching protocol: fixed groups (partners) over all 10 periods.","Directly parallels Stranger treatment as described, differing only in matching protocol.",0.98
10.1007_s11071-020-05965-0.md,Simulation – square lattice – threshold T=0,"Paper studies a spatial PGG on an L×L square lattice, varying punishment threshold T∈{0,1,2,3,4,5}. T=0 is explicitly interpreted as one limiting punishment-type condition.",0.9,Punishment threshold T=0 (all punishers use pool punishment); network type: square lattice; multiplication factor r varied,"Independent variables across conditions are threshold T, network structure, and r. This row isolates T=0 on the square lattice; r is scanned but not fixed to a single value.",0.86,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of evolutionary game dynamics on networks; no human participants.,1,False,"It is a computational model, not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects.",1,False,No physical lab or human subjects are involved; purely numerical.,1,True,They repeatedly state that results come from Monte Carlo simulations on networks.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations; only numerical phase diagrams and snapshots.,1,90000,Model is on an L×L lattice; L varies 300–1500. A concrete value is not fixed per condition. Use representative L=300 (lower bound) so 300^2=90000 as a conservative specific size.,0.2,10000,"They state simulations are averaged up to 10^4 generations; interpret this as 10,000 Monte Carlo steps.",0.6,1,"Players are pure strategists (C, D, P). There is no continuous contribution choice; cooperation is contributing c=1 vs 0, which is effectively all-or-nothing in contribution level.",0.7,N/A,No explicit individual endowments or initial allocations; public goods defined only via contribution c=1. Also no human subjects.,1,N/R,They specify group size G=5 and enhancement factor r (1<r<G) but do not normalize to a marginal per capita return r/G or name it as MPCR. Not directly reported.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,0,Agents update based on payoffs of themselves and one neighbor via imitation rule; no mention of aggregate summaries being shown.,0.8,N/A,No identities or information structures for agents; anonymity is implicit in spatial neighborhood interactions.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism at all.,1,0,Agents follow an imitation rule with no mention of awareness of total or remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Punishers P are explicitly modeled; punishment payoffs are given.,1,0.05,"They state γ=0.05 is the contribution/cost parameter; in peer punishment each punisher pays γ/(G−1) per punished defector, and in pool punishment γ/(G−1) regardless of neighbors. Use base γ as unit cost parameter.",0.8,0.5,They set β=0.5 as the fine parameter. In both punishment types each defector loses β/(G−1) either per punisher (peer) or if any pool punisher exists. Use base β as fine magnitude parameter.,0.8,0,Only punishment is included; no reward mechanism discussed.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/R,Standard PGG formulation with c=1 but no explicit per-round endowment is provided.,0.3,N/R,"Outcomes reported as frequencies ρP, ρC, ρD over time and phase regions; no explicit normalized contribution rate across all players.",0.4,N/R,They do not report aggregate or average contribution levels numerically; only strategy frequencies.,0.4,N/R,No total payoffs or efficiency ratios relative to maximum are reported.,0.4,N/R,"They never provide explicit payoff numbers or averages, only qualitative dynamics and frequencies.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,No human,Entire study is computational Monte Carlo simulation without human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a 2D square lattice with overlapping groups of size G=5 and strategies C, D, P (punisher). Multiplication factor r satisfies 1<r<G and is varied (e.g., 3–5). Strategy update via Fermi imitation rule with noise K=0.5, each Monte Carlo step consists of L^2 update attempts.",Summarizes the key structural aspects of the game and update rule described in the Model and Simulation sections.,1
