10.1093_restud_rdv040.md,"BT (Baseline Treatment, n=8)","Baseline weakest-link game with fixed group of 8, no neighbourhood choice; labeled BT in design section.",1,"Neighbourhood choice disabled; group size 8; payoff parameters a=20, b=10, c=60; full information history; standard payoff function π_i = a*min_j(e_j) - b*e_i + c.",BT differs from NT only by absence of neighbourhood choice; other parameters explicitly stated.,0.96,True,Laboratory experiment with student subjects at Maastricht BEElab.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with random assignment and controlled incentives.,1,True,"Conducted at BEElab, on-site with cubicles.",1,False,All results are from human-subject experiments; no agent-based or numerical simulations.,1,False,Paper contains no separate analytical-only condition; theory only for interpretation.,1,8,BT uses groups of size 8.,1,30,Subjects played the game repeatedly for thirty rounds in fixed matching groups.,1,1,"Players choose integer effort 1–7; any level allowed, not binary contribute/all-or-nothing.",1,0,Weakest-link effort game with linear cost; no endowment pre-allocated to public account; standard assumption is all in private account.,0.6,N/R,"Minimum-effort game, not linear public good; no marginal per capita return defined.",1,0,Communication was not allowed; only on-screen decisions and history.,1,1,Subjects had access to complete history of others’ effort choices and interaction links each round.,0.95,0,"No punishment mechanism exists in BT; only anonymous IDs A, B, etc. for efforts.",1,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Standard z-Tree implementations and mentioning of ‘round 30’ and endgame effects imply subjects knew total rounds.,0.85,0,No explicit costly punishment or sanction choice in BT.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward choice.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs given directly as function of effort and group minimum; no discrete endowment per round specified.,0.5,N/R,"Paper reports effort levels and welfare, not normalized contribution rates relative to an endowment.",1,4.0,Mean group average effort reported as 4.00 in BT (Table 2).,1,N/R,They report welfare in points versus optimal but not normalized 0–1 efficiency for BT separately.,0.6,629.5,Average welfare (sum of earnings of all group members) across all rounds for BT is 629.5.,1,Netherlands,Sessions at Maastricht University (Netherlands); subjects are students there.,0.98,N/R,Only that participants are university students; no age range given.,0.4,N/R,No gender composition reported.,1,Primarily university students in business and economics; remainder other programs.,"Section 4 states 53% business, 25% economics, 22% other programs.",1,On site,Conducted in BEElab with sight-shielded cubicles and on-site payment.,1,Weakest-link (minimum effort) game in fixed group of 8. Effort levels 1–7. Payoff: π_i = 20*min_j(e_j) - 10*e_i + 60. Everyone interacts with all 7 others each round.,Directly from Sections 2.1 and 3 and payoff Table 1.,1
10.1093_restud_rdv040.md,"NT (Neighbourhood Treatment, n=8)",Neighbourhood Treatment implementing neighbourhood game NG with group size 8.,1,"Neighbourhood choice enabled with mutual consent; group size 8; payoff parameters a=20, b=10, c=60; full history information; payoff scaled by relative neighbourhood size: π_i = (n_i/(n−1)) [20*min_{j in N_i∪{i}} e_j − 10*e_i + 60].",Section 2.2 and 3 define NG and NT as BG plus endogenous interaction neighbourhood and payoff scaling.,0.98,True,Human subjects in lab setting.,1,True,Randomly assigned lab treatment with controlled manipulations.,1,True,Conducted in BEElab.,1,False,Human-subject only.,1,False,No standalone analytical treatment.,1,8,NT groups size 8.,1,30,Repeated for thirty rounds.,1,1,Players choose integer effort 1–7 freely.,1,0,Same underlying weakest-link effort-cost structure; no pre-existing contribution in public account.,0.6,N/R,"Weakest-link game, not linear public good; no MPCR defined.",1,0,No communication beyond interface; no chat mentioned.,1,1,"Subjects had access to complete history of own and all others’ actions, including links and efforts.",1,0,"No explicit punishment action; only identity labels A,B,... for effort and link history.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Design refers to endgame effect and fixed thirty rounds; typical to display remaining rounds.,0.85,0,No explicit costly punishment action; exclusion emerges via link choice but not modeled as punishment option.,0.95,N/A,No explicit punishment variable; exclusion embedded in networking and payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward actions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Same payoff structure as BG but scaled by neighbourhood size; no initial token endowment specified.,0.5,N/R,Reports effort levels not relative contributions.,1,6.85,Mean of group average effort in NT is 6.85 (Table 2).,1,N/R,They compare welfare to optimal but no explicit normalized efficiency number for NT alone.,0.7,913.1,Average welfare in NT across all rounds is 913.1.,1,Netherlands,Same subject pool at Maastricht University.,0.98,N/R,No age stats given.,0.4,N/R,No gender composition reported.,1,Primarily university students in business and economics; remainder other programs.,Same pool as BT.,1,On site,Same BEElab setup.,1,"Neighbourhood (minimum-effort) game in group of 8 with endogenous undirected links via mutual consent. Each period: choose effort 1–7 and propose links to others. Payoff: π_i = (n_i/7)[20*min_{j in N_i∪{i}} e_j − 10*e_i + 60]. If no neighbours, payoff 0.",From Section 2.2 and footnote on isolation; n−1=7.,1
10.1093_restud_rdv040.md,"NT-IL (Neighbourhood Treatment with Information Loss, n=8)",Robustness treatment NT-IL where information on others is limited to past interaction partners.,1,"Neighbourhood choice with mutual consent; group size 8; restricted information: see only effort history of those you interacted with in that round, and no info on others’ neighbours; same payoff function as NT with scaling by n_i/(n−1).",Section 5.5.1 describes NT-IL modifications relative to NT.,0.98,True,Conducted as additional lab sessions with 72 subjects.,1,True,Controlled treatment with exogenous info-structure manipulation.,1,True,Same BEElab setting.,1,False,Human subjects only.,1,False,No standalone analytical treatment.,1,8,Nine groups of size 8 in NT-IL.,1,30,Same repeated-game length as base treatments.,1,1,Effort choice 1–7.,1,0,"Same game structure as NT, only information varies.",0.6,N/R,Not a linear public-good setting.,1,0,No mention of communication; same no-chat rule.,1,0,Subjects only see effort history for rounds and players with whom they interacted; no global summaries across non-neighbours.,0.95,0,No explicit punishment action.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same overall design length; typical to inform subjects.,0.85,0,No dedicated punishment instrument; only link choices.,0.95,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Same payoff structure as NT; no token endowment stated.,0.5,N/R,"Reports efforts and welfare, not normalized rates.",1,6.87,Mean group average effort in NT-IL is 6.87 (Table 4).,1,N/R,Only welfare vs optimal is described qualitatively; no explicit efficiency ratio.,0.7,894.9,Average welfare in NT-IL across all rounds is 894.9.,1,Netherlands,Same lab and recruitment location.,0.98,N/R,No age detail provided.,0.4,N/R,No gender stats.,1,"University students (majority business and economics, rest other programs).",Same recruitment pool as baseline treatments.,1,On site,Ran in BEElab with same procedures.,1,"Same neighbourhood minimum-effort game as NT (n=8) but information restricted: in round t, subject sees effort of j in earlier rounds only if j was in her neighbourhood in those rounds; no information on j’s other links.",Section 5.5.1 and Figure 7.,1
