10.1073_pnas.1608990113.md,Simulation – Public goods game with 2 vs 11 investment choices,"Paper describes evolutionary simulations comparing populations constrained to two investment choices (C1=0, C2=1) versus populations with 11 discrete investment choices between 0 and 1 (increments of 0.1), as summarized in Fig. 3.",0.9,"Number of available investment choices in the iterated public goods game: d=2 (choices {0,1}) versus d=11 (choices {0,0.1,...,1}); public benefit to individual cost ratio r varied.","Fig. 3 text: 'We compared populations with only two investment choices available, C1=0 and C2=1, versus populations in which players could choose among 11 levels of investment, between 0 and 1 in increments of 0.1. … We compared the mean payoffs … as a function of the ratio of public benefit to individual cost, r.'",0.95,False,Study uses evolutionary simulations of strategies in iterated public goods games; no mention of human or animal subjects.,1,False,"This is a numerical simulation study, not a controlled behavioral experiment with subjects.",1,False,No laboratory subjects or setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors repeatedly state they 'ran evolutionary simulations' of populations under weak mutation.,1,False,"This specific condition refers to the numerical simulations (Fig. 3), not to the analytical derivations elsewhere in the paper.",0.9,2,"They simulate 'pairwise iterated public goods games'; thus each game involves 2 players, even though the evolving population size is N=100.",0.95,1000,"Fig. 3 caption: 'each game played for 1,000 rounds.'",1,1,"Players can invest any allowed discrete amount between 0 and 1 (2 or 11 options), not a forced all-or-nothing binary in the d=11 case; this matches 'can contribute any amount' interpretation.",0.8,N/A,"Model is an abstract iterated public goods game with investment levels, not framed as an endowment initially placed in private vs public accounts.",1,N/R,"They define benefit function B = r C in the two-player public goods game; marginal per capita return in a 2-player linear PGG would be r/2, but this exact MPCR is not explicitly labeled or clearly tied to the simulation parameterization for all r values shown in Fig. 3.",0.4,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely simulated strategies.,1,N/A,Not a participant-interface design; no such display described in simulations.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or identities; behavior is strategy-based in a simulation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,No human subjects or interface elements.,1,0,"Game structure is standard linear two-player public goods with investment choices and no separate costly punishment or sanctioning stage; 'punishment' is only via reduced investment, not an explicit institution.",0.7,N/A,No explicit costly punishment mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment operation that maps punishment points to losses.,1,0,No explicit costly reward stage described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"They normalize investment choices between 0 and 1 (or C1=0, C2=1), but do not describe an explicit per-round 'endowment' in tokens.",0.5,N/R,They report 'mean payoffs' and qualitatively describe 'higher levels of investment' but do not give a numeric normalized contribution fraction outcome across r values.,0.3,N/R,"Fig. 3 shows ensemble mean fitness as a function of r, not actual contribution magnitudes; no specific numeric contribution outcomes are provided.",0.3,N/R,They discuss 'mean fitness' and 'roughly 10% payoff reduction' but do not provide explicit ratios of realized payoff to maximum possible for specific r.,0.3,N/R,Reported outcomes are ensemble mean fitness (per individual) over many replicates; no raw group payoff numbers or units.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; simulations only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"All results here are from computational evolutionary simulations and analytical models, not human-subject environments.",1,"Well-mixed population of N=100 haploid, asexual individuals, reproducing via copying process with selection strength σ=10 under weak mutation; each individual's fitness determined by payoffs from pairwise iterated public goods games played for 1,000 rounds. Mutants drawn uniformly from full set of d-choice memory-1 strategies; d=2 with choices {0,1} vs d=11 with choices {0,0.1,...,1}. Benefit function in the public goods game is linear: B = r C, and r (ratio of public benefit to individual cost) is varied.","Directly summarized from the Fig. 3 caption and Methods text describing evolution in populations of d-choice memory-1 strategies with parameters N=100, σ=10, 1000 rounds, weak mutation, and linear benefit B=rC.",0.95
10.1073_pnas.1608990113.md,Simulation – Iterated rock-paper-scissors with symmetric costs,"Paper analyzes and simulates an iterated rock-paper-scissors game where C1=C2=C3=C, focusing on memory-1 strategies that use all three actions and can resist invasion by single-action strategies; quantified in Fig. 4 (symmetric case).",0.9,"Rock-paper-scissors payoff structure with three behavioral choices (1,2,3) and symmetric costs C1=C2=C3=C; fixed benefit B; strategies are arbitrary memory-1 over three actions.","Section 'Maintaining Behavioral Diversity in a Game of Rock-Paper-Scissors' states: 'We first consider the case of a completely symmetric game of rock-paper-scissors, with C1=C2=C3=C. … We will assess the potential for maintaining behavioral diversity … looking for strategies that can resist invasion by players who use a single behavioral choice (1,2, or 3).' Fig. 4 notes symmetric case as maximum robustness.",0.95,False,Rock-paper-scissors interactions are modeled with abstract payoffs and memory-1 strategies; no subjects are recruited.,1,False,"These are theoretical/analytical results and numerical characterization of strategy space, not an experimental manipulation with observed subjects.",1,False,No lab environment or physical subjects.,1,True,"They 'calculate the probability that a randomly drawn memory-1 strategy satisfies' the robustness inequality, which requires numerical sampling/simulation of strategies and payoff calculations.",0.9,True,"Derive payoff relationships, robustness conditions (inequality 5), and characterize symmetry analytically before numerical sampling.",0.9,2,Described as 'two players' in a rock-paper-scissors game; payoffs R_jk refer to two-player outcomes.,1,N/R,"They analyze the long-term expected payoff in infinitely repeated (discounted) or indefinitely repeated games via stationary distributions, not a fixed finite number of rounds for this rock-paper-scissors analysis.",0.4,1,"At each round, a player selects one of three discrete actions (rock, paper, scissors); this setting does not fit 'all-or-nothing' contribution restriction of a PGG. Under the schema, 1 corresponds to being able to choose among multiple discrete actions rather than a forced binary all-or-nothing contribution.",0.6,N/A,Game is not an endowment-contribution public goods game; it is rock-paper-scissors with fixed benefit B and costs C_i.,1,N/A,Rock-paper-scissors is not a public goods game with an MPCR definition.,1,N/A,No agents with communication; purely modeled strategies.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; only payoff computations.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identity display.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,0,"Payoff structure is defined by nontransitive wins/losses (win B−C, lose −C, draw B/2−C); no separate costly punishment choice or stage.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mapping.,1,0,"Benefit B is part of baseline game outcome, not a voluntary costly reward action.",0.9,N/A,No costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game with per-round endowments.,1,N/A,"Rock-paper-scissors game has no 'contribution' variable; dependent outcome is robustness to invasion and payoffs, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contributions are made; moves are categorical actions.,1,N/R,"They note 'payoff is conserved' so S_xy+S_yx=B−2C, but do not present efficiency as realized payoff relative to maximum possible in normalized form.",0.5,N/R,They give general expressions for sums of payoffs but no specific numeric group payoff outcomes for sampled strategies.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Game is analyzed analytically and via computational sampling of memory-1 strategies; no empirical environment.,1,"Two-player iterated rock-paper-scissors with three actions (1=rock, 2=paper, 3=scissors). Symmetric costs: C1=C2=C3=C. In any interaction, fixed benefit B is at stake. Payoffs: when player i beats j, R_ij = B − C_i; when i loses, R_ij = −C_i; when tie, R_ii = B/2 − C_i. In symmetric case, total payoff S_xy + S_yx = B − 2C (payoff-conserving). Strategies are symmetric memory-1 over outcomes win (+), lose (−), draw (o), defined by probabilities such as p_-^+ (probability to play move that would have won, given loss). Robustness condition against single-action invaders derived as inequality (5). Numerical sampling of random memory-1 strategies shows about 50% are robust to loss of behavioral diversity in the symmetric case.",Summarized from 'Nontransitive Payoff Structures' and 'Maintaining Behavioral Diversity in a Game of Rock-Paper-Scissors' sections.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.1608990113.md,Simulation – Iterated rock-paper-scissors with asymmetric costs,"Fig. 4A describes numerical calculations of robustness of behavioral diversity as a function of varying C1 and C2 relative to fixed B and C3, beyond the symmetric case.",0.9,"Rock-paper-scissors payoff structure with three behavioral choices (1,2,3), fixed benefit B=2 and fixed cost C3=1, while costs C1 and C2 are varied (reported in terms of ratios C1/C3 and C2/C3).","Fig. 4 caption: 'With fixed benefit B=2 and cost C3=1 we systematically varied costs C1 and C2, and we calculated the percentage of memory-1 strategies that could successfully maintain behavioral diversity.'",1,False,Study uses model-based numerical calculations over strategy space; no human subjects.,1,False,This is not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is computational analysis.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,They 'numerically calculate the overall robustness of randomly drawn strategies as a function of the costs C1/C3 and C2/C3' (described around Fig. 4).,0.95,True,Payoff structure and conditions for invasion are derived analytically; simulation is used to evaluate robustness frequency across parameter space.,0.8,2,Game remains a two-player rock-paper-scissors interaction as defined earlier: payoffs R_jk for each pair of actions.,1,N/R,Analysis concerns long-term payoffs and robustness conditions for iterated games; no specific finite number of rounds is provided for these numerical robustness calculations.,0.4,1,"Players choose among three discrete behavioral types (actions 1,2,3); schema’s 'allOrNothing' field pertains to contribution flexibility in PGGs and is not directly applicable here. Set to 1 to indicate multiple discrete action choices.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments; rock-paper-scissors interaction.,1,N/A,Rock-paper-scissors is not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No communication between simulated players.,1,N/A,No interface or displays; only internal simulation.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,0,"Payoff asymmetries arise from costs C1,C2,C3 and benefit B in the core game; there is no separate willing punishment choice.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment action.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Benefit B is inherent to game outcomes, not a separate reward decision.",0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Not an endowment-based contribution game.,1,N/A,No contributions; rock-paper-scissors actions.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,"They report 'percentage of memory-1 strategies that could successfully maintain behavioral diversity' across cost parameters, not normalized efficiency of payoffs.",0.4,N/R,No specific numeric group payoffs; focus is on proportion of robust strategies.,0.3,N/A,Purely computational model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Numerical exploration of strategy space; no empirical setting.,1,"Iterated two-player rock-paper-scissors-type game with three behavioral options having possibly different costs C1, C2, C3. Benefit B is fixed at 2 and C3 fixed at 1. Payoffs defined as: if i beats j, R_ij = B − C_i; if i loses, R_ij = −C_i; if tie, R_ii = B/2 − C_i. For this condition, C1 and C2 are systematically varied, reported as ratios C1/C3 and C2/C3. For each (C1,C2) parameter pair, a large number of memory-1 strategies that employ all three behaviors are randomly drawn, and the percentage that can resist invasion by each single-action strategy (1-only, 2-only, 3-only) is computed, yielding a heatmap of 'percentage of robust strategies' as a function of costs. Behavioral diversity is found to be maintainable for a wide range of cost combinations, including some with C1 > B and C2 > B.",Summarized from Fig. 4 caption and surrounding text describing cost variation and calculation of robustness percentages.,0.95
10.1371_journal.pone.0012530.md,Numerical simulations of multi-population games with incompatible preferences (all reported parameter regimes),"The paper presents only analytical derivations and numerical simulations of replicator equations; no distinct experimental treatment labels are defined, so all simulated parameter regimes are grouped as one modeling study.",0.7,"Game type (multi-population Prisoner’s Dilemma, Harmony Game, Stag Hunt, Snowdrift), payoff parameters B and C (signs and magnitudes), relative power f of population 1, presence/absence of costly punishment, and presence/absence of group pressure; independent variables varied across simulations to study qualitative dynamics.","Text states they solved Eqs. (1) and (2) by numerical simulation for different parameter values B, C and f, and later vary costly punishment parameters β, γ and group pressure δ to transform games.",0.9,False,Study uses game-dynamical replicator equations and numerical simulations; no human or animal participants are involved.,1,False,There is no controlled empirical or field experiment; only theoretical and computational analysis of differential equations.,1,False,No lab or online experiment with participants is conducted.,1,True,Authors explicitly state they solved the equations by numerical simulation and show phase diagrams and flow fields from these simulations.,1,True,"They derive and analyze coupled replicator equations and discuss fixed points and stability, referencing a complementary analytical paper; this is formal mathematical modeling.",1,2,"Model is defined for symmetric 2×2 games involving pairwise interactions between two individuals at a time, even though populations are large.",0.9,0,Replicator dynamics are continuous-time and represent an infinite-horizon limit; there is no notion of discrete rounds as in a finite repeated game.,0.9,1,"Individuals choose between two discrete behaviors (1 or 2), not a contribution amount on a continuum; but within that binary choice there is no partial contribution concept, so contribution-amount framing is inapplicable; coded as 1 because players are not restricted to an all-or-nothing contribution in a PGG sense but rather choose a strategy.",0.5,N/A,There is no public-goods contribution or endowment structure; only strategy choice in abstract games.,1,N/R,No linear public goods game or contribution multiplier is modeled; MPCR is never defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely theoretical model.,1,0,"Information structure is not framed in terms of round summaries; payoffs depend only on current interaction partner’s behavior, as typical in replicator models.",0.8,N/A,"Punishment, when modeled, is a payoff parameter in the equations; there are no identities or information about who punished whom.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with identifiable agents; only payoffs within the game.,1,0,There are no discrete rounds or displays; continuous-time replicator dynamics instead.,1,1,"Baseline games have no punishment, but a section explicitly introduces costly punishment into the payoff matrices (β, γ), so punishment is part of at least some modeled conditions; at the study level, punishment is considered.",0.9,N/R,"Punishment costs β1, β2 are specified symbolically and one numerical example uses β=2.5, but there is no single unified cost across all simulations; aggregating to one numeric value would be misleading.",0.6,N/R,"Punishment fine γa is specified symbolically with an example γ=5β, but no single general magnitude is fixed for all analyses.",0.6,0,"They only model costly punishment and group pressure, not a separate costly reward mechanism.",0.9,N/A,No costly reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,No costly reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/R,"Games are specified directly in terms of payoffs T, R, P, S and derived parameters B, C; there is no notion of a monetary endowment.",1,N/A,"Model outcomes concern fractions of individuals using each behavior and stability of equilibria, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/A,No contribution amounts are modeled; only strategy choices in 2×2 games.,1,N/R,"Although payoffs exist, the paper does not report aggregate efficiency measures normalized by social optimum; focus is on equilibrium types and fractions of behaviors.",0.8,N/R,"No explicit numeric group payoff outcomes are tabulated; results are qualitative (who cooperates, coexistence, norms, revolutions).",0.9,N/A,No human subjects are involved; only populations in a mathematical sense.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of theoretical analysis and computer simulations of differential equations without participants.,1,"Study analyzes continuous-time replicator dynamics for two interacting populations with incompatible preferences over two behaviors, across four main 2×2 game types (Prisoner’s Dilemma, Harmony, Stag Hunt, Snowdrift), varying payoff parameters B and C and relative power f. They examine how these factors yield outcomes such as universal defection, coexistence of subcultures, emergence of shared norms, polarization, and revolutionary transitions. Extensions include costly punishment and group pressure that transform effective game types.","Summary compiled from Results, Methods, and figures describing multi-population PD, HG, MSH, MSD, punishment, and group-pressure transformations.",1
10.1016_j.jrp.2016.08.004.md,Experiment 1 – Uncostly Retaliation Game (player 2 decisions after fair move by player 1),"Experiment 1 URG with player 2 behavior specifically conditional on fair distribution by player 1, as analyzed separately in Table 1 and text.",1,"Player 1 behavior: fair distribution (5€,5€) vs. baseline of unfair behavior not included in this row",Player 1's move (fair vs. unfair) is the experimental manipulation; this row restricts to cases where player 1 acted fairly.,1,True,Human participants in a lab experiment making incentivized decisions.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized role assignment and incentivized game in the lab.",1,True,Participants invited to the laboratory to play the URG using computers.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulation described.,1,False,No formal closed-form analytical model as standalone study.,1,2,URG is a two-player (player 1 and player 2) sequential game.,1,1,Game is explicitly one-shot for each role; no repeated interaction reported.,0.9,1,"Player 1 chooses between two discrete allocations; player 2 chooses among discrete reduction amounts, not a continuous slider. This is closer to multiple discrete options than binary all-or-nothing; coded as 1 because players can choose several intermediate amounts including zero.",0.5,0,Not a public goods game; endowment is privately assigned to player 1 to allocate. Default public contribution proportion concept does not apply but spec requires numeric; set to 0 (all starts private).,0.4,N/R,"No public goods multiplier; it is a bargaining/retaliation game, not a linear public good.",1,0,No mention of communication; anonymity emphasized.,0.9,0,Participants only see their own and partner's outcomes for their match; no summaries of others' earnings/punishments mentioned.,0.9,0,Partners are anonymous; identities of punishers/targets are not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the URG; hence no reward identities shown.,1,1,"Game is clearly framed as a single decision situation; given the one-shot structure, participants effectively know the number of rounds.",0.6,1,Player 2 can reduce player 1’s payoff (retaliation/‘punishment’) at no cost.,1,0,Text repeatedly emphasizes that retaliation has no consequences/costs for player 2.,1,1,"Player 2’s reduction choice is 1:1 in MU on player 1’s payoff (reduce by 2.5, 5, or 7.5 MU).",1,0,No way for player 2 to increase player 1’s payoff beyond initial allocation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,10,Player 1 received an endowment of 10€ to distribute.,1,N/R,No contribution to a public good; only allocation and punishment/forgiveness decisions.,1,N/R,"DV is extent of retaliation (reduction in player 1’s payoff), not a contribution.",1,N/R,No report of efficiency (group payoff relative to maximum) for this condition.,1,N/R,Only distributions and frequencies are reported; no mean group payoff reported by condition.,0.4,Germany,"Participants recruited from University of Koblenz-Landau, Germany.",1,"18-46 (M=21.89, SD=4.43) for full Experiment 1 sample; age not broken down by player 2 and condition.",Reported overall for Experiment 1.,0.9,62.2% female in Experiment 1; gender not broken down by role or condition.,Reported overall sample composition.,0.9,Likely university students; exact education level not reported.,Recruited from local university participant pool; no explicit education stats.,0.6,On site,Participants were invited to the lab and seated at computers.,1,"Two-stage sequential URG with asymmetric retaliation options in this experiment: Player 1 chooses fair (5,5) vs unfair (7.5,2.5) allocation. Given a fair move, player 2 can reduce player 1 by 0 or 2.5 MU; given an unfair move, player 2 can reduce by 0, 2.5, 5, or 7.5 MU. Player 1 is unaware of the second stage.",Summarized from the URG description and Experiment 1 section.,1
10.1016_j.jrp.2016.08.004.md,Experiment 1 – Uncostly Retaliation Game (player 2 decisions after unfair move by player 1),"Same experimental setup as Experiment 1, but this row isolates player 2 behavior conditional on unfair distribution by player 1, as separately analyzed.",1,"Player 1 behavior: unfair distribution (7.5€,2.5€)",Player 1’s unfair move is the manipulation; this row restricts to those cases.,1,True,Human laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled economic game with random role assignment.,1,True,Run in a physical lab with zTree.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,No separate analytical model as experiment.,1,2,URG is a 2-player game.,1,1,One-shot interaction per role.,0.9,1,Discrete options including several intermediate retaliation levels; coded as 1 per instructions (not strictly binary all-or-nothing).,0.5,0,No public account; all 10€ starts with player 1 as private endowment.,0.4,N/R,Not a public goods framework.,1,0,Anonymity and no communication mentioned.,0.9,0,Only final payoffs of own interaction are given; no summary of others.,0.9,0,Decisions are anonymous toward ‘another unknown participant’.,1,0,No reward option.,1,1,Participants engage in a clearly single interaction.,0.6,1,Player 2 can reduce player 1’s payoff costlessly.,1,0,Reductions have no impact on player 2’s payoff.,1,1,1 MU spent (conceptually) reduces 1 MU from player 1; direct mapping of options.,1,0,No increase option for player 1’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,10,Player 1 receives 10€ to allocate.,1,N/R,No contribution variable in this task.,1,N/R,Only allocation choice (fair/unfair) and retaliation extent are measured.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure reported; payoff distributions differ but no normalized efficiency.,0.5,N/R,Mean group payoff by condition not reported.,0.4,Germany,Same sample as Experiment 1 overall from German university.,1,"18-46 (M=21.89, SD=4.43) for full Experiment 1 sample; age not broken down by role or condition.",Only overall sample age reported.,0.9,62.2% female in Experiment 1; not broken down by role or condition.,Reported once for Experiment 1 sample.,0.9,Likely university students; exact education level not reported.,Participant pool from university; no further education data.,0.6,On site,Conducted in the lab using zTree.,1,"Same URG as other Experiment 1 condition: Player 1 chooses fair (5,5) vs unfair (7.5,2.5) allocation. Given an unfair move, player 2 has four options to reduce player 1’s payoff by 0, 2.5, 5, or 7.5 MU, with own payoff fixed at 2.5 MU.",Summarized from URG description and Fig. 1.,1
