10.1111_ele.12178.md,Single well-mixed interaction group (single patch) simulation,Paper explicitly describes results for a population where social interactions are well-mixed (single patch) before introducing spatial structure.,1,"Population structure: well-mixed (single patch); Parameters: K_a=20, B=0.9, I=0.1, γ=0.0075; others at baseline (C=0.1, r_a=2, α_sa=0.05, α_as=0.05, β=300, μ=0.01, σ=0.1, N_p=1 implied).",Condition defined by having a single interaction group (well-mixed) with listed parameter values; other parameters at baseline from Table 1.,0.86,False,Model is a theoretical individual-based simulation with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled assignment of human subjects; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,False,No lab or field setting; simulation only.,1,True,Authors explicitly state they analyse the stochastic process by means of individual-based simulations.,1,True,Paper presents explicit fitness equations and model structure in addition to simulations.,0.8,20,Well-mixed case uses carrying capacity parameter K_a=20 and states K_a can be regarded as the intrinsic carrying capacity of a patch in the absence of social interactions; single patch thus supports about 20 individuals.,0.6,5000,Figure 1 panels described over time; explicit run length not given but panel axes suggest thousands of generations; use 5000 as described for structured case Fig. 2a–c and assume similar for Fig. 1.,0.3,1,"Model is not framed as discrete contribution choices; individuals are cooperators, defectors, or asocial; no continuous contribution amount choice.",0.4,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation; purely theoretical types and parameters.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or MPCR is defined; instead public good parameter B and demographic effects are used.,1,N/A,No human players or communication protocol.,1,0,No user interface or information display in a simulation without human subjects.,1,N/A,No identities or visibility to human players; punishment is modelled as sanctioning costs in fitness.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism separate from sanctions in a human interface sense.,1,0,No human interface to display number of rounds.,1,1,Model includes sanctioning of defectors via fraction (1-h) of public good devoted to monitoring and sanctioning.,1,N/A,"Cost of sanctioning is modelled demographically, not as a per-token cost in a contribution game.",1,N/A,No per-unit punishment technology defined; sanctioning enters as fitness reduction term.,1,0,No explicit positive rewards are modelled beyond general benefits of cooperation and technology.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate mechanism.,1,N/A,No per-period token or monetary endowments for human players.,1,N/R,"Simulation tracks frequencies of cooperators, defectors, asocials and h-preferences, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,No explicit per-individual contribution amount outcome is tabulated; contributions enter implicitly via type C and B parameters.,1,N/R,No explicit measure of payoff efficiency vs social optimum is reported.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are expressed in terms of carrying capacities and frequencies, not group payoff sums.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical and computational with no human subjects.,1,"Individual-based simulation of a population subdivided into social types (cooperator, defector, asocial) on a single patch with Beverton–Holt demographic dynamics, institution parameter h determining fraction of public good for technology vs sanctioning.",Summarizes key structural features of the modeled interaction under the well-mixed condition.,0.9
10.1111_ele.12178.md,"Structured population baseline (K_a=20, B=0.9, I=0.1, β=300, γ=0.0075) simulation",Section 'Evolution in a spatially structured population' defines a structured population with these ecological and demographic values and presents Fig. 2.,1,"Population structure: 50 patches (spatially structured), migration rate m=0.1 (from sensitivity fig. 4 captions as base); Parameters: K_a=20, B=0.9, I=0.1, β=300, γ=0.0075, C=0.1, r_a=2, α_sa=0.05, α_as=0.05, μ=0.01, σ=0.1, N_p=50.",Condition defined by spatially structured case with base parameters from Table 1 and text; m inferred from sensitivity section as baseline.,0.8,False,Simulation of cultural and demographic evolution; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; model-based simulations.,1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,Authors explicitly use individual-based simulations over patches.,1,True,Includes explicit mathematical specification of fitness and carrying capacity functions.,0.8,20,"Initial patch intrinsic carrying capacity K_a is 20 individuals; simulations start from asocials only at this patch size, with social carrying capacity increasing later.",0.7,100000,Fig. 2 panels show dynamics over 5000 and 10^5 generations; taking the longer horizon as the main run length for long-run stability.,0.7,1,Individuals are type-based (cooperator/defector/asocial) rather than choosing arbitrary contribution amounts.,0.5,N/A,No human endowments or default allocations.,1,N/R,"Public good is not parameterized via a linear multiplier and MPCR, but via B and demographic functions.",1,N/A,No human interaction or communication interface.,1,0,No user-facing display in a simulation without human subjects.,1,N/A,No identities of human punishers or punished; only types in the model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward identity mechanism.,1,0,No human participants to whom rounds are displayed.,1,1,Model includes sanctioning of defectors using fraction (1-h) of public good; central to institutional dynamics.,1,N/A,"Sanctioning costs and benefits are encoded in fitness functions, not as discrete cost per punishment unit.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment effectiveness per unit expenditure as in lab games.,1,0,No separate prosocial reward mechanism besides demographic benefits from cooperation.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,No individual endowments of tokens or money.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as frequencies of cooperators, defectors, asocials and mean h, not normalized contribution rate.",1,N/R,No explicit per-individual contribution amounts are tabulated.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure relative to social optimum is provided.,1,N/R,Focus is on carrying capacities and type frequencies; no aggregate monetary payoff.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure simulation study.,1,"Individual-based simulation on 50 patches with migration. Individuals carry social type (asocial, cooperator, defector) and institutional preference h. Socials form an institution that allocates fraction 1-h of public good to sanction defectors and h to technology that increases carrying capacity K_s via a concave function. Fitness follows a two-niche Beverton–Holt model with competition between asocial and social niches.",Summarizes interaction structure under baseline structured condition.,0.95
10.1111_ele.12178.md,"Structured population with large potential carrying capacity (β=3000, γ=0.00075) simulation",Section 'The co-evolution of institutions and demography can produce a transition to very large-scale cooperation' and Fig. 3 describe this parameter change.,1,"Population structure: 50 patches, migration rate m=0.1 assumed as baseline; Parameters: K_a=20, B=0.9, I=0.1, β=3000, γ=0.00075, C=0.1, r_a=2, α_sa=0.05, α_as=0.05, μ=0.01, σ=0.1, N_p=50.",Condition differs from baseline structured case only in β and γ; other parameters unchanged from Table 1 and previous section.,0.85,False,Simulation of model without human data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No human lab environment.,1,True,Authors simulate dynamics under altered β and γ.,1,True,Same analytical model with different parameter values.,0.8,20,Initial patch carrying capacity K_a remains 20; technology allows growth to ~1000 but starting size is 20.,0.7,100000,Fig. 3 shows long-run dynamics; precise generation count not given but likely comparable (10^5) to Fig. 2 long-run plots.,0.6,1,Contribution determined by type; no continuous choice described.,0.5,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,No MPCR parameter.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,No UI for human subjects.,1,N/A,No human players and no identity display.,1,N/A,No reward identity mechanism.,1,0,No human participants.,1,1,Same sanctioning institution as baseline structured case.,1,N/A,Punishment not framed as per-unit cost.,1,N/A,No discrete punishment effectiveness parameter.,1,0,No explicit reward system.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,"Results show frequencies of cooperators and mean h, not explicit contribution rates.",1,N/R,No explicit per-player contributions tabulated.,1,N/R,Efficiency metric not reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff not explicitly quantified; focus on patch sizes and frequencies.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure simulation condition.,1,"Same institutional public goods and sanctioning game as baseline structured case, but with β increased tenfold (3000) and γ decreased tenfold (0.00075), allowing carrying capacity of social individuals to grow from 20 to about 1000 under high cooperation and high h (0.8–0.9).",Summarizes how this condition differs from the baseline structured scenario.,0.95
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2016.09.008.md,Treatment ONE – DecPun (decentralized punishment institution),Condition with perfect information (λ=1) where citizens choose the decentralized punishment institution.,1,"Information accuracy λ = 1.0; Institution type = DecPun (decentralized punishment allowed, no central authority)","Main manipulation is λ ∈ {1, 0.9, 0.5}; within each λ, institution choice (DecPun, CenPun, NoPun) creates distinct conditions.",1,True,Human subjects in a lab public goods experiment.,1,True,Payoffs and information structures are experimentally controlled and randomized.,1,True,"Conducted at EconLab, University of Bonn.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,Paper has a theoretical section but this row refers to the human-subject experimental condition.,1,10,Each matching group has 10 citizens plus 1 authority; institutions are subsets of the 10 citizens. PlayerCount refers to potential group size in the institution.,0.9,32,Game is repeated for 32 periods.,1,1,"Citizens choose g_i ∈ {0,2,…,20}, i.e., a range of contribution levels, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment of 20 ECUs is allocated to private account; players decide how much to contribute.,1,0.16,Each ECU contributed is multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally. MPCR = 1.6 / group size. For a typical size 10 this is 0.16; true MPCR varies inversely with actual institution size.,0.4,0,No communication stage or chat mentioned.,1,1,"At beginning of each phase, citizens see number of citizens, average contribution, and average profit in all institutions for all previous periods.",1,0,"Citizens learn only total punishment received, not number or identifier of punishers; identities not revealed.",1,0,No rewards in the design.,1,1,Participants know they play for a finite number of 32 periods.,1,1,In DecPun citizens can assign punishment points to others.,1,1,Each punishment point costs the punisher 1 ECU.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces the punished citizen’s payoff by 3 ECUs.,1,0,Only costly punishment is available; no positive sanctions described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each citizen receives an endowment of 20 ECUs each period before contribution; extra 3 ECUs are specifically for punishment.,1,N/R,"Paper reports raw contributions (ECUs), not normalized rates; could be computed but not provided directly by condition and institution size.",0.3,18.4,"Table 2: in ONE, DecPun contribution = 18.4 ECUs.",1,N/R,Profits reported but not normalized to maximum feasible payoff net of punishment for full contribution; cannot be cleanly inferred given endogenous group sizes and punishment.,0.2,31.6,"Table 2: in ONE, DecPun profit = 31.6 ECUs (average individual payoff per period in this institution).",1,Germany,"Experiment conducted at EconLab, University of Bonn.",1,"18–64, median 22",Results section: age ranged between 18 and 64 (median 22).,1,56% female,Reported in results section.,1,Mostly undergraduate students; 6% non-students,Stated composition of subject pool.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at university experiment lab; subjects physically present.,1,"Public goods game with 10 citizens and 1 authority in matching groups; three institutions (DecPun, CenPun, NoPun). Contribution choice g_i ∈ {0,2,…,20}. Per-period structure: contribution stage, signal/punishment stage, feedback. Citizens choose institution every 4 periods (8 phases). Signals about others’ contributions are perfectly accurate in this treatment (λ=1). Extra 3 ECUs per citizen are available for punishment.",Summary of structural game details for this specific condition.,1
