10.1017_mor.2017.42.md,Study 1 – Retributive punishment – Individual punishment animation,Study 1 uses two retributive-punishment animations as within-subject conditions: individual punishment vs group punishment. This row is the individual punishment animation condition.,1,Punishment implementer: individual peer punisher (Blue triangle) versus non-punishers (Green and Pink triangles) in an individual-punishment animation; Target of rating: Punisher vs Deviant vs Others.,Design varies animation type (individual vs group punishment) within subjects and target triangle whose behavior is evaluated; here focuses on the individual-punishment animation and ratings for different targets.,0.94,True,Human student participants in eight countries rated animations.,1,True,"Controlled design with scripted animations, counterbalanced order, and manipulation check; participants randomly exposed to order, but both conditions viewed within subjects.",0.98,True,"Study run either online or in more controlled conditions at universities; both are experimental, not field or observational. Treated as lab/onsite experiment.",0.8,False,"Animations evaluated by humans, not agent-based or numerical simulations.",1,False,No formal mathematical model; empirical ratings only.,1,4,"Animations show four triangles (Blue, Green, Pink, Purple).",1,1,Single vignette-like interaction per animation; no repeated rounds described.,0.7,1,Not an economic contribution game; triangles harvest circles but no discrete choice to contribute all or nothing. Coded as allowing any behavior rather than binary.,0.3,N/R,No endowments or contribution decisions; only harvesting from a common resource is depicted.,1,N/R,No payoff structure or marginal per capita return specified; only visual common-resource context.,1,0,No communication between triangles or participants is possible or described.,1,0,Participants only view animations; no summaries of others’ payoffs or actions provided.,1,1,Identity of punisher (Blue triangle) is visually explicit in the animation.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism exists in the study.,1,0,Participants not told about number of rounds; only a single animation per condition.,1,1,Core of the vignette is a peer punishment act against the over-harvester.,1,N/R,No numeric costs to punisher or target are defined; only qualitative aggression is shown.,1,N/R,No numeric mapping from punishment action to payoff reduction is specified.,1,0,No positive incentives or rewards are described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No individual endowments or payoffs are specified for triangles or participants.,1,N/R,"Outcome is approval ratings toward agents, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions measured.,1,N/R,No payoffs or efficiencies computed; dependent variables are ratings.,1,N/R,No group payoff amounts reported.,1,"USA, Netherlands, Sweden, Japan, Russia, UAE, China, Pakistan",Participants recruited from student populations in eight named countries in Study 1.,1,Mean age 21.1 years (SD = 4.50; range 16–63),Table 1 reports age summary for Study 1 aggregated across countries.,1,37% male,Table 1 shows 37% male for Study 1 total sample.,1,University students,Participants recruited from student populations at universities in each country.,1,On site,Study conducted either online or ‘in more controlled conditions at their universities’. Mixed-mode; classified as experimental on-site/online rather than field.,0.7,"Vignette-style common-pool resource dilemma depicted via geometric animations; Purple triangle over-harvests circles, then is punished retributively by Blue alone while others (Green, Pink) do not punish. Participants rate appropriateness and social desirability of each triangle’s behavior on 7-point Likert scales; Punisher, Deviant, and Others composite ratings constructed.",Summarizes Study 1 individual-punishment animation and measured outcomes as described in the Method and Results sections.,1
