on: after Purple over-harvests, Green convenes Pink and Blue; all three enter Purple’s corner and jointly remove all circles there and return them to the center, fully restoring the common pool. Participants rate the behavior of each triangle using items framed as if they were in their own group; Punisher, Deviant, and Others composites computed.",Summarizes Study 2 group-restorative-punishment animation and measurement.,1
10.1098_rspb.2010.2342.md,Dictator Game (cross-society sample),Paper reports mean DG offers across a set of small and large societies drawn from Henrich et al. and Barr et al.; all DG play shares the same structure.,0.9,"Society-level variables (local group population rank, ethnic population rank, continent); within-game condition is a standard one-shot anonymous dictator game with 0–100% choice.",DG structure is constant; analyses vary societies by population size and continent rather than experimental parameters.,0.9,True,Human subjects played DG with real money in multiple societies.,1,True,"Incentivized, researcher-controlled economic game with random anonymous matching.",1,False,"Games were run in field settings across small-scale and larger societies, not in a conventional lab.",0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,No formal game-theoretic or closed-form model as the primary method.,1,2,Dictator game involves a dictator (P1) and a recipient (P2).,1,1,"Described as anonymous, one-shot games.",1,1,"P1 decides how much (0–100%) of the stake to give; implies a continuous/proportional choice, not a binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Entire stake is initially allocated to P1 to divide; equivalent to all endowment starting in private account.,0.9,N/R,No public-goods multiplier or per-capita return described; DG is a pure transfer game.,1,0,Games are anonymous one-shot with no mention of communication.,0.9,0,Standard one-shot DG; no feedback on others’ earnings is mentioned.,0.9,0,No punishment stage exists in DG.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism exists in DG.,1,1,Described explicitly as one-shot; subjects necessarily know there is only one round.,0.9,0,DG has no rejection or punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism; transfers are the main decision.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage distinct from allocation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/R,Stake size in money or tokens is not specified; only proportions (0–100%) are discussed.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports mean DG offers qualitatively and in regressions, but not the numeric values in this text.",0.2,N/R,Only percentages are mentioned; no raw amounts.,0.2,N/R,DG does not involve a social multiplier; no efficiency metric is reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoffs are reported.,0.2,Multiple countries/societies,"Cross-cultural project includes many societies (hunter-gatherers, horticulturalists, pastoralists, farmers, city dwellers) across continents; individual countries not listed in this excerpt.",0.4,N/R,No information about age ranges or means.,0.1,N/R,No information about gender composition.,0.1,N/R,No education data reported.,0.1,Field experiment,"Games conducted in multiple small-scale and larger societies in situ as part of a cross-cultural project, not in a single lab or online.",0.9,Standard one-shot anonymous dictator game where P1 decides how much (0–100%) of an experimenter-provided stake to give to P2; mean DG offer is used as a measure of generosity and is analyzed in relation to local group and ethnic population sizes.,Summarized directly from the Methods and introductory description of the DG.,1
10.1098_rspb.2010.2342.md,"Ultimatum Game (cross-society sample, strategy method)",Paper treats all UG data from the cross-cultural project as one experimental structure; UG MAO is the key measure of second-party punishment.,0.9,"Society-level variables (local group population rank, ethnic population rank, continent); within-game condition is a standard one-shot anonymous ultimatum game with 0–100% proposer choice elicited via strategy method.",UG structure is constant; analyses vary societies by population size and continent.,0.9,True,Human participants played UG with real money.,1,True,Controlled economic game with experimentally imposed rules and payoffs.,1,False,"Conducted across field sites in many societies, not in a conventional lab.",0.9,False,No simulation mentioned.,1,False,No closed-form or purely theoretical model presented for UG.,1,2,UG features a proposer (P1) and responder (P2).,1,1,"Described as an anonymous, one-shot game.",1,1,P1 decides how to divide the stake from 0 to 100%; implies a continuous allocation choice.,1,0,Stake is initially under P1’s control to divide; equivalent to full private endowment.,0.9,N/R,"UG is a bargaining game, not a public goods game with a multiplier.",1,0,Game is anonymous and one-shot; no communication described.,0.9,0,No indication that players see summaries of others’ outcomes or punishment.,0.9,0,Interactions are anonymous; identity of rejecters/punishers is not revealed.,0.9,0,No explicit reward stage; partners are anonymous.,0.9,1,Clearly framed as a one-shot game; participants know there is only one interaction.,0.9,1,P2 can reject the offer; rejection sets both payoffs to zero and is interpreted as second-party punishment.,1,N/R,"Cost is offer-dependent (foregone income if rejecting), but no single unit cost is reported or inferable from this excerpt.",0.3,N/R,Effect of punishment depends on the offer and stake; no constant per-unit punishment technology is described.,0.3,0,UG has no separate costly reward mechanism aside from accepting offers.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,"Stake size not specified; only fractions (e.g., 30% offers) are mentioned.",0.3,N/R,Main reported outcome is UG MAO and offers; numeric offer proportions by society are not given in this excerpt.,0.2,N/R,"Only proportions / MAOs are discussed, not raw amounts.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum) reported.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not reported.,0.2,Multiple countries/societies,Cross-cultural sample spanning various small-scale and large societies; specific countries not listed here.,0.4,N/R,No age information reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0.1,N/R,No education data reported.,0.1,Field experiment,Games conducted in community settings across diverse societies as part of the cross-cultural project.,0.9,"Standard one-shot anonymous ultimatum game. P1 chooses how to divide a stake with P2. If P2 accepts, both receive according to the division; if P2 rejects, both get nothing. Strategy method used, allowing calculation of each responder’s minimum acceptable offer (MAO), which is the measure of second-party punishment. UG MAOs are analyzed versus local group and ethnic population ranks.",Summarized from the description of UG and strategy method in Methods.,1
10.1098_rspb.2010.2342.md,"Third-Party Punishment Game (3PPG, cross-society sample, strategy method)",Paper refers to 3PPG data from the same cross-cultural project; MAO in 3PPG is the measure of third-party punishment.,0.9,"Society-level variables (local group population rank, ethnic population rank, continent); within-game condition is a one-shot anonymous third-party punishment game with fixed punishment technology (1:3) and strategy method for punishment thresholds (MAO).",3PPG design is constant; analyses focus on population size differences across societies.,0.9,True,Human subjects play a three-player game with real money.,1,True,Experimenter-controlled rules and payoffs in a structured economic game.,1,False,Run in multiple societies in situ rather than a single central laboratory.,0.9,False,No numerical simulations described.,1,False,"Empirical game, not an analytical model.",1,3,"Game includes P1 (allocator), P2 (recipient), and P3 (third-party punisher).",1,1,"Described as an anonymous, one-shot game.",1,1,"P1 can give “whatever amount” of the stake to P2, suggesting a continuous 0–100% choice.",1,0,"Stake for the P1–P2 allocation starts with P1, analogous to full private endowment for P1.",0.8,N/R,Not a contribution-to-public-good setting; no MPCR specified.,1,0,"Interactions are anonymous, one-shot; no communication described.",0.9,0,No mention of feedback or summary statistics from others’ games.,0.9,0,Game is anonymous; P1 does not learn the identity of P3 and vice versa beyond their roles.,0.8,0,No explicit reward institution; only punishment by P3.,1,1,"Clearly a one-shot design, which participants are informed of as part of the standard cross-cultural protocol.",0.9,1,"P3 can pay to reduce P1’s payoff, defined as third-party punishment.",1,0.2,Text: “P3 can return 20 per cent of her allotment (half as much as the stake) to the researcher in order to take away three times that amount from P1.” Interpreting unit cost as proportion of P3’s own allotment per unit of punishment money given back; each 0.2 of her allotment finances 0.6 of deduction from P1.,0.7,3,"For each unit P3 returns, P1 loses three times that amount (1:3 punishment leverage).",1,0,No mention of P3 being able to increase P1’s or P2’s payoff; mechanism is purely punitive.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Absolute monetary size of P1’s stake and P3’s allotment are not given; only relative sizes and percentages.,0.4,N/R,"Main reported outcomes are P1’s offers and P3’s MAO-based punishment, not normalized contribution rates; numeric offers by society not detailed here.",0.2,N/R,Only qualitative description and percentage relationships; no raw amounts per participant reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff values presented.,0.2,Multiple countries/societies,Same cross-cultural participant pool as DG and UG; specific countries not detailed here.,0.4,N/R,Age distributions not reported.,0.1,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0.1,N/R,No education information reported.,0.1,Field experiment,"Conducted in diverse societies in the field as part of the cross-cultural project, not in a lab.",0.9,"In the 3PPG, P1 chooses how much of a stake to give to P2 (as in a dictator game). P2 has no action. A third player, P3, receives an allotment equal to half of P1’s stake and can spend up to 20% of her allotment to punish P1: each unit she gives up to the experimenter removes three units from P1’s payoff. Strategy method used to elicit P3’s punishment as a function of P1’s offer, producing a minimum acceptable offer (MAO) that serves as the measure of third-party punishment. 3PPG MAOs are analyzed across societies and compared to UG MAOs.",Summarized from the detailed description of 3PPG in the Methods and introduction.,1
