10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.10.037.md,Model with asymmetric enforcer exploration favoring corruption,"Condition in Section 4.2 and Fig. 2c,d where exploration rates between enforcer types are asymmetric (v1 ≠ v2), leading to a defective outcome.",0.95,"Asymmetric exploration among enforcers: μ = 0.002, v2 = 0.002, v1 = 0.012 (corrupt→honest vs honest→corrupt asymmetry), giving exploration-induced equilibrium y1* = v2/(v1+v2) ≈ 1/7; payoff parameters b=1, c=0.5, A=2, s=0.2, B=0.2; no information on corrupt enforcers.","Exact values μ, v1, v2 stated under Fig. 2c,d; exploration-induced equilibrium y1* derived in text; other payoff parameters as in Fig. 1.",0.95,False,The study is exclusively modeling and simulation.,1,False,No experimental subjects or randomized assignment.,1,False,No lab or field environment.,1,True,"100 simulated trajectories for this parameter set shown in Fig. 2c,d.",1,True,Analytical condition comparing exploration-induced equilibrium y1* to corruption threshold ỹ1 is discussed in Section 4.2.,0.9,2,Underlying game structure unchanged: pairs of harvesters plus an enforcer.,1,1,One-shot interactions; time evolution via differential equations.,0.8,1,Discrete strategy types; no graded contributions.,0.8,N/A,No human subjects/endowment structure.,1,N/R,Not formulated as linear public good with MPCR.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,No explicit summary feedback mechanism.,0.8,0,Identity information about enforcers is not modeled here (information on corrupt enforcers is added only in Section 5).,0.8,0,No rewards present.,1,0,No explicit round or horizon; continuous dynamics.,0.9,1,Same enforcement structure with penalty A remains.,1,N/R,Cost not parameterized as in standard punishment cost-per-unit form.,0.7,N/R,Penalty A exists but not cast as units per paid cost.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,No endowment parameter is specified.,1,N/R,"Outcomes expressed as (x1,x3,x5,y1) equilibrium: (0.06, 0.08, 0.86, 0.13); no normalized contribution DV reported.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions; only cooperation vs defection strategies.,1,N/R,The paper does not translate these equilibrium compositions to explicit efficiency ratios.,1,N/R,Numerical group payoffs are not provided.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Mathematical model and simulations only.,1,"Same harvester-enforcer interaction as in baseline but with exploration among harvesters (μ=0.002) and asymmetric exploration among enforcers (v1=0.012, v2=0.002). This yields an exploration-induced equilibrium honesty level y1*≈0.14 that lies below the corruption threshold ỹ1, so the unique globally stable equilibrium is close to the DLSE with (x1,x3,x5) ≈ (0.06,0.08,0.86) and y1≈0.13, i.e., dominated by non-committing defectors and corrupt enforcers.",Summarizes Section 4.2 and the specific numerical equilibrium values given.,0.95
10.1016_j.jtbi.2014.10.037.md,"Model with information on corrupt enforcers (opportunistic strategies, p = 0.34)","Condition in Section 5 and Fig. 3a with information about corrupt enforcers and opportunistic versions of conditional cooperators and committing defectors, at probability p=0.34 of recognizing corruption.",0.95,"Information on corrupt enforcers: honest enforcers are always correctly identified; corrupt enforcers are recognized as corrupt with probability p = 0.34; opportunistic conditional cooperators and opportunistic committing defectors adjust behavior based on perceived enforcer type; exploration and learning as in basic model (no μ, v1, v2 specified for this figure). Payoff parameters b=1, c=0.5, A=2, s=0.2, B=0.2.","p=0.34 and opportunistic behavior described in Section 5 and Appendix B; payoffs same baseline parameters as stated below Fig. 3; exploration parameters are not mentioned for this condition, so interpreted as absent beyond replicator dynamics.",0.85,False,Again an evolutionary game model with simulated trajectories; no participants.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with subjects.,1,False,No lab/field environment.,1,True,Fig. 3a shows trajectories from 100 randomly chosen initial conditions under this informational regime.,1,True,Appendix B and C analytically derive payoffs and classify dynamics depending on p.,0.95,2,Interactions still between pairs of harvesters and an enforcer.,1,1,Same one-shot game per interaction; evolution over time via replicator equations.,0.8,1,Agents choose among discrete opportunistic/non-committing strategies; no continuous contribution decision.,0.8,N/A,No human participants or endowment structure.,1,N/R,The game is not framed with a marginal per capita return.,1,N/A,"Information about enforcers is modeled probabilistically, not as communication among human subjects.",1,0,Only information about individual enforcer honesty/corruption is modeled; no payoffs or summary displays.,0.8,1,"By assumption, harvesters know whether the specific enforcer they face is honest (always) and may recognize corrupt enforcers with probability p; this effectively reveals the enforcer’s type.",0.85,0,No rewards in this model.,1,0,No explicit horizon or round information in the model.,0.9,1,Honest enforcers still impose fines A; corruption modifies whether fines or bribes occur.,1,N/R,"As before, monitoring costs are assumed negligible and rolled into fee s, without a per-unit punishment cost parameter.",0.7,N/R,Penalty A and bribe B specify payoffs but not punishment tech in units per cost.,0.7,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/R,No endowment variable given.,1,N/R,Paper reports the fraction of trajectories ending in cooperative harvesting (95/100) rather than numeric contribution rates.,1,N/R,No continuous contribution amount is modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculation reported for this condition.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not reported numerically.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is implemented only in simulations of replicator dynamics.,1,"Extension of the harvester-enforcer game adding information about enforcer honesty: honest enforcers are always correctly known, corrupt enforcers are identified with probability p. Harvesters become opportunistic conditional cooperators or opportunistic committing defectors who adjust their commitment and cooperation decisions based on perceived enforcer type. With p=0.34 and baseline payoff parameters, most trajectories (95 out of 100) converge to regimes with cooperative harvesting and predominantly honest enforcers, enlarging the domain of attraction of cooperative outcomes compared to the basic model.","Summarizes Section 5 and description of Fig. 3a,b.",0.95
10.1088_1674-1056_19_11_110201.md,Approximate best response dynamics model with punishment (baseline parameter set),"Paper presents a single theoretical/simulation model of an optional public goods game with punishment; figures and text repeatedly refer to this one model using a baseline parameter set N=5, r=3, α=0.1, β=1.2, γ=1.0, K=0.4.",0.9,"Magnitude of rationality K (bounded rationality; K=0.4 for main simulations); optional public goods participation with four strategies (cooperator, defector, punisher, loner) and punishment parameters α=0.1, β=1.2, γ=1.0","Model varies K conceptually (perfect, bounded, completely irrational), but main numerical results and figures are for K=0.4; also includes punishment parameters and group size as fixed settings.",0.9,False,No human participants; paper develops a theoretical evolutionary game model and analyzes it numerically.,1.0,False,No controlled human or field experiment is conducted; only mathematical model and numerical simulations.,1.0,False,No lab or field setting with human subjects; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1.0,True,"Section 3 explicitly discusses ""numerical results"" and ""simulations"" of the approximate best response dynamics and individual-based simulations without human subjects.",1.0,True,"Derives payoff functions, dynamical system, and analyzes fixed points/equilibria using approximate best response dynamics; includes closed-form expressions and qualitative analysis.",0.95,5,"Text and figure captions specify N=5 as the size of the group repeatedly (e.g., Fig. 1 and Fig. 2 captions).",1.0,1,"Evolutionary model over continuous time frequencies; the underlying public goods interaction is a single-stage game repeatedly played by the population, not a finite repeated-game with discrete rounds; set to 1 as there is no finite round structure.",0.7,1,"Each participant chooses a strategy type rather than a continuous contribution level; cooperators and punishers invest a fixed amount 1, defectors and loners invest 0, so there is no choice of intermediate contribution; coded as all-or-nothing=1 as per schema meaning 'players can contribute any amount', but in this model they effectively have only 0 or 1 via strategy choice; however, schema requires binary and closest fit is 0 (all-or-nothing only).",0.3,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment allocation process; model assumes cooperators and punishers invest 1 with no default endowment structure.,1.0,0.6,"In a standard linear PGG, each unit contributed is multiplied by r and divided among N participants; with N=5 and r=3 (given), MPCR = r/N = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.9,0,No communication between agents is modeled; only strategy frequencies and payoffs enter the dynamics.,1.0,0,"Model has no informational interface; agents respond to payoffs, not explicit summaries of others’ earnings or punishments.",1.0,0,Punishment is modeled as a cost and payoff reduction aggregated at the population level; identities or pairings are not represented.,1.0,0,"No reward mechanism is included in the model, only punishment.",1.0,0,There is no concept of displayed rounds in this continuous-time evolutionary model.,1.0,1,Punishers pay cost γ to inflict punishment β on defectors and αγ to inflict punishment αβ on cooperators; punishment is central to the model.,1.0,1.0,"Model defines γ as the cost to punish defectors; in parameter set captions γ=1.0, so cost per punishment unit is 1 for defectors. Cooperators are punished at cost αγ with α=0.1 so 0.1, but baseline γ is 1.0.",0.8,1.2,"Punishment imposes a fine β on defectors with cost γ, and β is specified as 1.2 in parameter set; thus each unit cost γ=1 corresponds to fine β=1.2 on defectors.",0.8,0,"Only punishment terms (β, γ, α) appear; no reward or positive sanctioning mechanism is discussed.",1.0,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent in the model.,1.0,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent in the model.,1.0,1,"They set each cooperator’s investment equals to 1, effectively the per-round contribution endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Paper reports equilibria in terms of strategy frequencies (x_c, x_d, x_p, x_l), but does not report normalized contribution rates explicitly or as averages; no single numeric outcome is provided.",0.3,N/R,"Contributions per cooperator/punisher are fixed at 1, but the paper does not report aggregate or equilibrium average contributions as a single number (depends on equilibrium mix).",0.4,N/R,"No explicit ratio of realized to maximum group payoff is reported; discussion focuses on stability and existence of equilibria, not efficiency metrics.",0.2,N/R,"Average payoffs per strategy are given symbolically, but no single numerical group payoff value is reported for the equilibria.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation model.,1.0,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation model.,1.0,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation model.,1.0,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation model.,1.0,No human,Study is a mathematical and numerical analysis of an evolutionary game with no human participants.,1.0,Optional public goods game with four strategies: cooperators and punishers contribute 1 to a common pool; defectors contribute 0; loners do not participate and receive fixed payoff σ. Total contributions are multiplied by r (with (1+σ) < r < N) and shared equally among participants. Punishers incur cost γ to impose fine β on defectors and cost αγ to impose fine αβ on cooperators. Evolution of strategy frequencies follows approximate best response dynamics with rationality parameter K.,"Summarized directly from the model description in Section 2 and parameter conditions around Eqs. for P_c, P_d, P_p, P_l and K.",1.0
10.1073_pnas.1900078116.md,"Individual-based simulation – sustained oscillations (Fig. 1A,B)","Condition described as individual-based simulations with specific parameter set producing sustained oscillations in Fig. 1A,B.",1,"Simulation parameters: benefit b=1, cost c=0.5, fee f=0.2, bribe B=0.2, information cost h=0.1, fine A=2, player population size M=50, umpire population size N=10, player exploration rate μ=0.001, umpire exploration rate ν=0.005, selection strength s=1e10 (hard imitation limit).","These are the explicit parameter values given for Fig. 1A,B producing sustained oscillations.",1,False,Study uses evolutionary game-theoretic simulations and analytical equations without human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only simulations and analytical models.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Condition is an individual-based Monte Carlo style simulation of strategy dynamics.,1,False,"This specific condition refers to numerical simulation, not closed-form analysis.",1,2,Model is a two-player donation game: 'each instance of this game involves only two players'.,1,1,Game is a one-shot donation game repeatedly sampled in simulations; each interaction is one round.,0.8,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D, not a continuous contribution level; treated as 'can contribute any amount' in binary sense.",0.5,N/A,No endowment allocation between private/public accounts; binary donate decision.,1,N/R,"Donation game defined by benefit b and cost c, not by a public good multiplier/group size formulation.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication mechanism; purely simulated agents.,1,0,Model description does not include feedback on others' earnings beyond payoff-based imitation; no explicit summaries shown.,0.7,N/A,No explicit identity or visibility structure; agents are anonymous in random matching.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in model.,1,0,No notion of displayed remaining rounds in the simulation description.,0.7,1,Honest umpires impose a fine A on defectors; this is institutional punishment.,1,N/R,Cost to umpires of imposing fines is not specified; only players’ fines and fees/bribes are defined.,0.3,N/R,"Model provides fines as fixed amounts, not as a per-token transformation per punishment unit.",0.3,0,"No positive reward mechanism; only fees, bribes, and fines.",1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/R,Players' initial wealth/endowment per game is not specified; only payoffs relative to 0 are given.,0.2,N/R,"Paper reports frequencies of strategy types and corruption levels, not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate or average number of cooperative acts per player reported numerically.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum) is reported for this simulation.,0.2,N/R,Total or average payoffs are not numerically summarized; only qualitative dynamics are depicted.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is a computer simulation with no human subjects.,1,"Two-population evolutionary donation game with four player strategies (optimistic cooperator, prudent cooperator, optimistic defector, prudent defector) and two umpire types (honest, corrupt). Players may pay information cost h to learn umpire type; prudent types may drop out if umpire type is unfavorable. If both proceed, each pays fee f; corrupt umpires also take bribe B from defectors; honest umpires impose fine A on defectors. Social learning via Fermi imitation process with strong selection (s≈1e10) and low exploration (μ, ν).",Summarizes the rules and dynamic process described for the simulated condition.,1
