,N/R,Only frequencies of strategies and corruption levels qualitatively described; no normalized rate reported.,0.2,N/R,No numerical average contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not numerically summarized.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Condition is a computational simulation of agents.,1,"Same two-population donation game as in sustained oscillation condition, but with much larger player and umpire populations and higher exploration rates plus weaker selection, leading to damped oscillations and convergence to a mixed-strategy equilibrium with persistent corruption.",Summarizes how this condition differs dynamically from the sustained-oscillation simulation.,1
10.1073_pnas.1900078116.md,"Replicator-mutator dynamics – small exploration, large populations (limit cycle, Fig. 3A,C,D, orange orbit)","Condition described as large-population replicator-mutator dynamics with small exploration rates (orange orbit in Fig. 3A,C,D).",0.9,"Analytical/ODE parameters: benefit b=1, cost c=0.5, fee f=0.2, bribe B=0.2, information cost h=0.1, fine A=2, exploration rates μ=ν=0.0001 (players and umpires), replicator-mutator dynamics in infinite populations.","Fig. 3A,C,D caption gives A=2 and exploration rates μ=ν=0.0001 for the orange limit cycle; other payoff parameters are as in Fig. 1A,B.",0.9,False,No human data; system of differential equations describes infinite populations.,1,False,"Deterministic evolutionary model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field environment.,1,True,Trajectories of replicator-mutator ODEs are computed numerically for Fig. 3.,0.9,True,Replicator-mutator equations and their fixed points/limit cycles are analyzed analytically in the text and SI Appendix.,1,2,Underlying game remains the two-player donation game.,1,1,"Interactions are single-shot; dynamics over time is in strategy frequencies, not repeated rounds within a dyad.",0.8,1,Actions are binary (cooperate or defect).,0.5,N/A,No divisibility of endowment; binary donation game.,1,N/R,Model not specified as linear public good with MPCR.,1,N/A,No communication; model specifies only strategy imitation driven by payoffs.,1,0,No explicit representation of payoff summaries; only average payoffs enter dynamics.,0.7,N/A,No identity or observability structure in analytical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No concept of displayed round count in the ODE description.,0.7,1,Honest umpires impose fines A>c on defectors in the payoff matrices Q_H and Q_C.,1,N/R,Cost to umpires for administering A is not modeled.,0.3,N/R,Fine A is specified as a lump sum; no per-unit transformation definition.,0.3,0,Only punishments (fines) and bribes are included; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model does not define initial token endowments.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as frequencies of strategy types (OC, PC, OD, PD) and honest/corrupt umpires, not normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No explicit average number of cooperative acts per interaction reported numerically for this limit cycle.,0.2,N/R,"No explicit efficiency index reported for the limit cycle in Fig. 3A,C,D.",0.2,N/R,Average payoffs are used internally but not summarized as a reported outcome measure.,0.3,N/A,Analytical and numerical model without human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Infinite-population ODE model and its numerical integration have no human environment.,1,"Replicator-mutator dynamics in the state space S=Δ4×[0,1] for four player strategies (OC, PC, OD, PD) and two umpire types (honest, corrupt). Average payoffs derive from payoff matrices Q_H and Q_C and umpire payoffs P_H(x), P_C(x). Small but positive symmetric exploration rates μ=ν induce a limit cycle near the boundary, with strategy frequencies and corruption levels oscillating over time as shown by the orange orbit.",Summarizes how the analytical ODE model is configured for this small-exploration condition.,1
10.1073_pnas.1900078116.md,"Replicator-mutator dynamics – high exploration, large populations (damped oscillations, Fig. 3A,B, violet and blue orbits)","Condition described as large-population replicator-mutator dynamics with higher exploration rates (violet and blue orbits in Fig. 3A,B) leading to damped oscillations and stable fixed points.",0.9,"Analytical/ODE parameters: benefit b=1, cost c=0.5, fee f=0.2, bribe B=0.2, information cost h=0.1, fines A=2 (panel A) or A=0.7 (panel B), exploration rates μ=ν=0.02 (violet orbit) and μ=ν=0.07 (blue orbit).","Fig. 3A,B caption states exploration rates for violet (0.02) and blue (0.07) orbits and notes A=2 in A and A=0.7 in B; other parameters as in Fig. 1A,B.",0.9,False,No human subjects; deterministic evolutionary model.,1,False,"Model is analytical/numerical, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Trajectories of replicator-mutator equations are simulated numerically for given parameters.,0.9,True,Same analytical replicator-mutator framework as in limit-cycle case; here showing parameter regime with stable interior fixed point.,1,2,Underlying game is two-player donation game.,1,1,Each interaction is one-shot; time is continuous in ODE.,0.8,1,Binary cooperate/defect decision.,0.5,N/A,No divisible endowment into public/private accounts.,1,N/R,Model is not stated as linear public good.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,No explicit summary information beyond payoff-driven imitation.,0.7,N/A,No identity or monitoring of who punishes whom.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Continuous-time dynamics; no round count displayed.,0.7,1,Honest umpires impose fines A on defectors.,1,N/R,Punisher costs not specified.,0.3,N/R,"Fine magnitude A given as payoff adjustment, not per-unit technology.",0.3,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Results are described in terms of strategy frequencies and corruption levels, not as normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No numeric contribution levels given.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not reported as a metric.,0.2,N/R,Group-level payoffs not summarized.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Deterministic evolutionary model without human subjects.,1,"Same donation game and strategy sets as other replicator-mutator conditions. With higher exploration rates (μ=ν=0.02,0.07), the limit cycle shrinks and the dynamics converges to a stable interior equilibrium with all types present; frequencies of honest and corrupt umpires and of OC, PC, OD, PD undergo damped oscillations before settling.","Summarizes dynamics described for violet and blue orbits in Fig. 3A,B.",1
10.1073_pnas.1900078116.md,"Rare-exploration Markov chain over absorbing states (hard imitation, small μ,ν)","Condition described as rare-exploration limit with hard imitation, leading to Markov chain on 10 absorbing states with stationary distribution and H:C ratio.",0.9,"Analytical limit parameters: very small exploration probabilities in player and umpire populations (μ for players, ν=λμ for umpires), hard imitation (selection strength s→∞), positive information cost h>0, number of umpires N finite, players M finite but arbitrary; payoff parameters satisfy inequalities f+h<b−c, c+h<A, B+h<c, h<√(f(f+B)).","Methods section 'Rare-Exploration Limit' specifies hard imitation, very small exploration, parameters constraints, and derives transition matrix T and stationary H:C ratio.",0.9,False,No empirical data; purely analytical Markov chain over strategy states.,1,False,"Analytical derivation, not experiment.",1,False,No human lab setting.,1,False,"Core result (stationary vector x and H:C ratio) is presented analytically, though simulations could illustrate it.",0.8,True,Transition matrix T and stationary distribution x are derived symbolically.,1,2,Underlying interaction still the two-player donation game.,1,1,Each basic game is one-shot; rare exploration focuses on long-run transitions between absorbing states.,0.8,1,Binary cooperate/defect actions.,0.5,N/A,No divisible endowment; just cooperate or defect.,1,N/R,Model is not given as MPCR-based public good.,1,N/A,No communication component.,1,0,"Agents update via imitation based on payoffs, but no summary feedback mechanism is modeled.",0.7,N/A,No identity-based structure in analytical Markov chain.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Not applicable in continuous-time rare-exploration limit.,0.7,1,Same payoff matrices Q_H and Q_C with fines A apply.,1,N/R,Cost to punisher not specified.,0.3,N/R,"Fine A specified, but no per-unit tech.",0.3,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment not defined.,0.2,N/R,"Outcome of interest is stationary ratio of honest to corrupt umpires and occupancy of absorbing states, not contributions per se.",0.3,N/R,No numeric contributions reported in rare-exploration analysis.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure computed from stationary distribution.,0.2,N/R,"Stationary distribution reported, but not converted into group payoff statistics.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical limiting case of evolutionary model.,1,"Ten absorbing states (five with all umpires honest, five with all corrupt) over combinations of player strategy mixtures (all OC, all PC, all OD, all PD, or specific OD/PD or OD/PC mixes). Rare exploratory mutations move the system between these states via transition matrix T, with entries depending on μ, ν, and N. Stationary distribution x yields long-run fraction of time in each state and the overall ratio of honest to corrupt umpires; for λ=ν/μ=1 the H:C ratio is (19N+87):109, implying a majority of honest umpires even under persistent corruption episodes.",Summarizes the Markov-chain-based rare-exploration analysis.,1
