2: Contribution Sanction = 19.17 (SD 2.96).,1,N/R,"No average realized efficiency vs maximum reported, though higher contributions imply higher efficiency.",0.2,N/R,Average earnings per group not directly reported.,0,France,"Same subject pool as Baseline; all sessions at GATE in Lyon, France.",1,N/R,Only specified as undergraduates; no age numbers.,0.3,41.67% male,Overall sample proportion; allocation to treatments not broken down but likely similar.,0.9,Undergraduate students in local business and engineering schools,Recruitment details identical across treatments.,1,On site,Lab sessions with physical presence and psychophysiological recording equipment.,1,"Two-stage linear public goods game with partner matching in fixed groups of 4 for 20 periods. Stage 1: each subject gets 20 ECU and chooses contribution 0–20 to group account; total group contribution multiplied by 1.6 and split equally. Stage 2: each subject observes the three others’ contributions and may assign 0–10 punishment points to each; punishment points reduce target’s first-stage payoff by 10% per point (capped at 100%) and cost the punisher according to a convex cost function (e.g., 1,2,4,6,9,... ECU for 1–10 points). Identities of punishers are hidden via random scrambling of IDs each period. Emotions measured with SCR and 10-point valence scale at contribution decision, feedback on others’ contributions, punishment decision, and feedback on received punishment.","Summarized from Experimental Design, Table 1, instructions, and SCR acquisition sections for Sanction treatment.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0044747.md,DG-P – Sham rTMS,"One within-subject stimulation condition in the Dictator Game with Punishment option: sham rTMS, contrasted with right and left DLPFC stimulation.",1,"Stimulation condition: sham rTMS; Offers observed: 5:5, 7:3, 8:2, 9:1 splits of 10 MU.","Independent variable manipulated was rTMS condition (sham, right DLPFC, left DLPFC); all three used the same DG-P structure with four offer types.",1,True,Study used 20 healthy human subjects in a DG-P task.,1,True,rTMS conditions were randomly ordered within subjects and task behavior measured under controlled manipulations.,1,True,Participants tested at Ruhr-University Bochum; seated in front of a monitor; lab-like controlled setting.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model developed; only statistical analyses of behavioral data.,1,3,"DG-P explicitly described as involving proposer, responder, and third-party punisher (the participant).",1,44,"44 trials per DG-P session: 11 trials for each of four split-conditions (5:5, 7:3, 8:2, 9:1).",1,1,"Participants 'had the option to choose not to punish at all, or to invest as many MUs as they wanted to spend' implying a continuous range.",1,0,Endowment of 10 MU per trial was for the third party to invest in punishment; no pre-existing contribution to any public fund; all starts in private account.,0.9,N/A,This is not a standard public goods game; there is no linear public good return defined by MPCR.,1,0,No mention of any communication between participants or with proposer/recipient in the task description.,0.95,1,"Outcome screens showed participant investment, proposer reduction, and recipient gain simultaneously on slide bars each trial, i.e., per-round feedback.",0.95,0,Participants saw generic photos of allocators/recipients from a database; no identifying information suggesting real identities revealed.,0.9,0,"Same anonymity as punishment; no real identities, only standardized facial stimuli.",0.9,0,Text does not state that participants were told or shown how many trials remained or total number of trials.,0.7,1,DG-P explicitly includes 'option to punish unfair proposals' by reducing proposer payoff.,1,0.5,"For every 0.5 MU invested in punishment, the proposer's payoff was reduced by 1 MU; cost per punishment unit is 0.5 MU.",1,2,"Each 0.5 MU cost reduces proposer by 1 MU, so 1 MU cost corresponds to 2 MU reduction: tech = 2 MU penalty per MU spent.",1,1,Same operation increased the recipient's payoff by the same amount; this is effectively a reward/compensation for the victim.,0.95,0.5,The same 0.5 MU that reduces proposer by 1 MU also increases recipient by 1 MU; cost per 1 MU reward is 0.5 MU.,1,2,1 MU of cost produces 2 MU reward for recipient (since 0.5 cost -> 1 recipient MU).,1,10,Participants were endowed with 10 MU per trial to invest in punishing unfair proposals.,1,N/R,No normalized proportion of endowment invested reported; only mean MU investments and totals are given.,0.4,1.14,"Across rTMS conditions, mean punishment for all unfair offers under sham is 1.14 MU (Table 1, 'All unfair offers' sham mean).",1,N/R,No data on total group payoff versus maximum possible payoff across all players and offers; cannot compute normalized efficiency.,0.2,N/R,"Paper does not report overall group earnings or payoffs in MU or Euros, only investments and punishment amounts.",0.2,Germany,"Participants recruited from Ruhr-University Bochum, Germany; German-language measures used.",1,Mean 25.55 years (SD 4.22); adults only.,"Participants section reports M_age = 25.55 years, SD_age = 4.22.",1,"13 women, 7 men","Participants section: 'Twenty healthy subjects (13 women, seven men)'.",1,University students/affiliates; recruited by advertisement from Ruhr-University Bochum.,"Recruited from a university population; likely mostly university students or similar, but exact level not detailed.",0.6,On site,Participants attended sessions with TMS equipment and lab computer; not online or field.,1,"Third-party Dictator Game with punishment and compensation: 10 MU to split (5:5, 7:3, 8:2, 9:1) between proposer and responder; third-party punisher endowed with 10 MU per trial; can spend any amount (0–10) to reduce proposer and increase recipient at 1:2 leverage (0.5 MU cost → -1 MU proposer, +1 MU recipient). 11 trials per split type (44 per session). No punishment for fair 5:5 offers observed; punishment increased with unfairness.",Summarizes the DG-P structure and parameters as described in the Tasks section.,1
