10.1007_s00191-009-0162-8.md,Infinite population – others-only (OO) voluntary joint effort game with punishment,"Single analytical replicator-dynamics model for infinite, well-mixed population in OO case with four strategies (non-participants, cooperators, defectors, punishers).",1,"Participation regime: voluntary; Game type: others-only (OO) strong altruism; Strategies available: non-participant, cooperator, defector, punisher; Population size: infinite.","Model explicitly specifies voluntary participation, others-only benefit structure, four strategies, and infinite population.",1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical evolutionary game model.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only analytical treatment of replicator dynamics.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; theoretical model only.,1,False,"Section 3 focuses on closed-form payoffs and phase portraits, derived analytically, not on discrete-time computational simulation.",0.8,True,Replicator equations and fixed points analyzed symbolically for infinite population.,1,5,"Figure 1, illustrating OO replicator dynamics, uses N=5; these are the only concrete parameter values specified for this infinite-population game.",0.8,1,"Each interaction is a one-shot joint effort game with two stages (public good, punishment) but no repeated rounds between the same players.",0.9,0,"Players decide whether to contribute a fixed amount c or not; contribution choice is binary, not a continuous contribution amount.",1,N/A,No endowment allocation between private and public accounts is modeled; only a cost c if contributing.,1,N/R,Return structure is described by factor r and group composition; marginal per capita return is not given as a single constant.,0.3,N/A,No human players or communication stage.,1,0,Model only specifies payoffs based on strategies and frequencies; no feedback or summary information to agents.,0.9,N/A,Punishment is modeled as anonymous payoff adjustments; no notion of identity or information structure.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,0,Agents do not observe or reason about a finite horizon; evolution is continuous-time.,1,1,"Second stage allows punishers to fine defectors at cost γ, fine β.",1,1,"Punishers pay total cost γ N_y; with γ=1 in Fig. 1 parameter set, cost per unit of imposed fine is γ/β = 1/1.2 ≈ 0.83, but per-unit mapping isn’t defined; we record γ as the primitive cost parameter.",0.5,1.2,"Each defector pays fine β N_w; β=1.2 in Fig. 1, representing the punishment impact parameter.",0.8,0,Only costly punishment of defectors is modeled; no rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/R,"Players incur cost c if contributing, but an explicit endowment size is not specified.",0.2,N/R,"No single scalar outcome such as average contribution fraction is reported; only qualitative dynamics (e.g., dominance of punishers vs. non-participants).",0.2,N/R,"Contribution is fixed at c for contributors, but average contribution amount is not summarized as a result.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) values are given.,0.1,N/R,"Expected payoffs are given symbolically, but no numeric aggregate group payoff outcome is highlighted.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary model without human data.,1,"Others-only (strong altruism) public goods structure: contribution c yields benefit rc shared only among co-participants other than contributor; voluntary participation; if only one participant in sample, game does not take place and participant receives autarkic income σ.","Direct description in Sections 2 and 3, including OO definition and autarkic payoff σ.",1
10.1007_s00191-009-0162-8.md,"Finite population – voluntary OO game with four strategies (non-participant, cooperator, defector, punisher)",Finite-population Moran-like process with four strategies and voluntary participation analyzed in Section 4 and Fig. 2a/3b.,1,"Participation regime: voluntary; Game type: others-only (OO) strong altruism; Strategies available: non-participant, cooperator, defector, punisher; Population size M=100; Mutation rate μ≈0 (analytical) and μ=0.001 (simulations); Selection strength s varied (0–1).","Section 4 specifies M, μ, s, and that all four strategies are allowed in voluntary case.",1,False,Population consists of strategy types updated by imitation rule; no human subjects.,1,False,"This is a theoretical evolutionary model plus computer simulations, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No physical lab or field environment.,1,True,"Finite-population dynamics are implemented as a Markov chain and explored via simulations (e.g., Fig. 2, Fig. 3 dots).",1,True,Transition probabilities and stationary distribution are derived analytically in the small-mutation limit.,1,5,Parameter values for simulations and stationary distributions: N=5 (group size in joint effort game).,1,1,Each sample of N plays one joint effort interaction (public good plus punishment) per update event.,0.9,0,Contribution choice is binary: either contribute fixed amount c or not.,1,N/A,No explicit individual endowment division; only a cost c to contributors.,1,N/R,"Payoff depends on r, c, and group composition; marginal per capita return is not summarized as a single constant across all group sizes.",0.3,N/A,No communication or messaging stage in the simulation.,1,0,"Agents update based on fitness (payoff) but the model does not include explicit feedback of summaries; imitation probability acts on fitness, not displayed summaries.",0.8,N/A,Punishment is modeled as an aggregate cost/ fine term; no identity or observability structure.,1,N/A,No rewards are included.,1,0,No notion of a finite horizon in the agents’ decision rules; process is ongoing.,1,1,Second stage allows punishers to fine defectors with parameters β and γ.,1,0.3,"For finite-population simulations in Fig. 3, parameters include γ=0.3; this is the per-defector cost coefficient for punishers.",0.9,1,"Fig. 3 parameter list for finite-population case: β=1, the per-punisher fine coefficient on defectors.",0.9,0,"Model includes only punishment, not rewards.",1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/R,"Agents incur cost c=1 when contributing, but initial endowment is not specified.",0.3,N/R,"Results are presented as stationary frequencies of strategy types and time series, not as normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,"Contribution amount per cooperator/punisher is fixed at c=1, but aggregate contributions are not summarized numerically.",0.5,N/R,"Paper reports qualitative dominance of punishers and cooperative regimes, but not numeric efficiency ratios.",0.2,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff figures or averages over time are provided; only relative frequencies of strategies.,0.2,N/A,Simulation model; no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation model; no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation model; no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation model; no human participants.,1,No human,"Finite-population Moran process with computer simulations, not human-subject data.",1,Others-only joint effort game with voluntary participation; population size M=100; random samples of size N=5 offered game; non-participants obtain autarkic income σ=1; contributors pay cost c=1 to benefit others with factor r=3; punishers additionally pay γ per defector and impose fine β on defectors; baseline fitness B=1 and selection strength s varied.,Parameter set explicitly stated under Fig. 3 and described in Section 4.,1
