wards.,1,N/R,"Contribution cost c=1 is specified, but endowment not.",0.3,N/R,Outcomes are reported as strategy frequencies with defectors dominating; not converted into contribution rates.,0.3,N/R,No numeric summary of contribution amounts is provided.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency is not quantified; only qualitative statement that defectors take over.,0.2,N/R,No explicit group payoff numbers reported for compulsory case.,0.2,N/A,Simulation model; no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation model; no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation model; no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation model; no human subjects.,1,No human,"Condition is implemented computationally, not with human participants.",1,"Others-only joint effort game with compulsory participation; strategies: cooperators, defectors, punishers; non-participants excluded; population size M=100; random samples of N=5 always participate; contribution cost c=1, multiplier r=3; punishers pay γ=0.3 per defector and impose fine β=1; baseline fitness B=1, selection strength s varied; mutation rate μ≈0 in analytical results and μ=0.001 in simulations; defectors emerge as clear winners in stationary distribution.",Details drawn from discussion around Fig. 2b and Fig. 3c and parameter list for strong altruism compulsory case.,1
10.1007_s12110-009-9072-6.md,2002 Dictator Game – Hadza,The 2002 DG is described as one of three experimental economics games played with the Hadza; treated as its own condition.,1,"Game type (Dictator Game); role = P1 as allocator; stakes = 2,000 TZS (10 coins of 200 TZS); anonymous partner; one-shot; strategy method not relevant for P1 decisions.",DG distinct from UG and TPPG in available actions and absence of punishment or rejection.,1,True,Human Hadza participants played for real monetary stakes.,1,True,"Controlled, researcher-designed economic game with random matching and fixed rules.",1,True,"Games played individually and privately in the researcher's Land Rover, not in participants’ natural economic environment.",0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all results from human play.,1,False,Paper reports empirical game outcomes rather than formal mathematical models only.,1,2,DG defined as involving two players (P1 and P2).,1,1,Standard protocol and text indicate one-shot games; no repetition mentioned.,0.9,1,"P1 can give P2 anything from 0% to 100% in increments of 10%, i.e., any discrete share, not forced all-or-nothing.",1,0,"All 10 coins (2,000 TZS) are initially given to P1 as his/her endowment to divide; none starts in a public pot.",0.9,N/R,This is not a public goods game with a multiplier; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Players made decisions privately in the Land Rover; no communication between matched partners described.,0.9,0,No mention of feedback about others’ choices or earnings.,0.9,0,No punishment stage exists in DG.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism exists.,1,0,One-shot game; no explicit statement that remaining rounds or total rounds were displayed.,0.8,0,"P2 cannot reject, and there is no punishment stage.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in DG.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in DG.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No costly rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No costly rewarding mechanism.,1,2000,"Stakes in all three games were 2,000 TZS, consisting of 10 coins of 200 TZS; P1 gets this to allocate.",1,0.26,Mean offer by P1 in DG in 2002 was 26%; normalized fraction is 0.26.,1,520,"26% of 2,000 TZS = 0.26 * 2000 = 520 TZS.",1,N/R,No explicit efficiency or ratio of actual to maximum group payoff reported for DG.,0.2,N/R,Total group earnings in DG not reported.,0.2,Tanzania,Hadza are hunter-gatherers in Tanzania.,1,Mean age men 36.1 (SD 14.6); mean age women 36.3 (SD 16.0); overall age distribution not further broken down by game.,Aggregate ages given for 111 people across all 2002 games; DG participants are a subset.,0.6,"64 men, 47 women in full 2002 sample; gender breakdown by game not reported.","Only overall sample gender counts provided, not per game.",0.5,Largely non-literate hunter-gatherers; no formal schooling levels reported.,Text notes no written language and limited number words; no formal education statistics.,0.7,Field experiment,Games conducted with Hadza camps in their natural setting using a mobile lab (Land Rover).,0.9,"One-shot anonymous DG with 2,000 TZS stake; P1 chooses 0–100% in 10% increments, P2 passive; 31 pairs in 2002 played DG and UG (roles fixed across these two games). Strategy method not applied to DG choices.",Summarized game description from methods and results sections.,1
10.1007_s12110-009-9072-6.md,2002 Ultimatum Game – Hadza,The 2002 UG is described as a separate game condition measuring second-party punishment.,1,"Game type (Ultimatum Game); role = P1 offerer and P2 responder; stakes = 2,000 TZS; anonymous partner; one-shot; strategy method for P2 (minimum acceptable offers).",UG differs from DG and TPPG by allowing P2 to accept/reject offers; used to study second-party punishment.,1,True,Human Hadza participants making real-money decisions.,1,True,Controlled ultimatum bargaining game implemented by researcher.,1,True,Conducted individually in the Land Rover with instructions and private decisions.,0.9,False,No computer simulation component.,1,False,Empirical data collection rather than purely analytical model.,1,2,UG described as involving P1 and P2 only.,1,1,Described and interpreted as one-shot UG; no repetitions mentioned.,0.9,1,P1 can offer any amount 0–100% in 10% increments; not restricted to all-or-nothing choices.,1,0,"P1 initially controls the full 2,000 TZS and chooses the amount to transfer; no initial public fund.",0.9,N/R,Bilateral bargaining game; no public goods multiplier.,1,0,No communication between matched players reported; play was private and isolated.,0.9,0,No feedback on others’ strategies or earnings mentioned.,0.9,0,"Punishment in UG is via rejection, but identities were anonymous and no IDs of punishers were revealed.",0.9,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism exists.,1,0,Game is one-shot; no statement that round information was displayed.,0.8,1,"Second-party punishment exists via P2’s ability to reject offers, destroying both players’ payoffs.",1,N/R,Cost per unit of rejection to P2 not parameterized like a punishment technology; rejecting sacrifices P2’s whole share.,0.4,N/R,No linear punishment technology specified; rejection simply sets both earnings to zero.,0.4,0,No separate costly reward mechanism besides acceptance of offers.,1,N/A,No costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No costly reward mechanism.,1,2000,"Stakes in all three games were 2,000 TZS (10 coins).",1,0.26,Mean UG offer by P1 in 2002 was 26%; normalized contribution = 0.26.,1,520,"26% of 2,000 TZS equals 520 TZS.",1,N/R,Only qualitative statement that group earnings in the UG were low; no numeric efficiency ratio reported.,0.3,N/R,Total realized payoffs not tabulated; only rejection rates and offers reported.,0.3,Tanzania,Hadza live in northern Tanzania.,1,Mean age men 36.1 (SD 14.6); mean age women 36.3 (SD 16.0); game-specific ages not given.,Only overall 2002 sample ages provided.,0.6,"64 men, 47 women in full 2002 sample; distribution across UG not specified.",Gender counts reported only for total participants.,0.5,Non-literate hunter-gatherers; no formal education data.,Paper notes lack of written language and difficulty with numbers; no schooling levels.,0.7,Field experiment,"Games run in Hadza camps but under controlled, experimental conditions in a Land Rover.",0.9,"One-shot anonymous UG with stake 2,000 TZS. P1 offers 0–100% in 10% increments. P2, using the strategy method, states minimum acceptable offers (MAO) before knowing the actual offer. Mean P1 offer = 26%; modal offer = 10%. Mean MAO = 25%. 42% of P2s rejected 20% offers and 29% rejected 40% offers; 65% of actual offers rejected overall.",Compiled from description of UG rules and results.,1
10.1007_s12110-009-9072-6.md,2002 Third-Party Punishment Game – Hadza,The 2002 TPPG is a distinct experimental condition designed to measure third-party punishment.,1,"Game type (Third-Party Punishment Game); roles = P1 proposer, P2 recipient, P3 third-party potential punisher; stakes: P1+P2 share 2,000 TZS, P3 endowment = 1,000 TZS (5 coins); P3 can pay 200 TZS (one coin = 20% of P3 endowment) to reduce P1’s payoff by 600 TZS (three coins). Strategy method for P3 (punishment decisions for each possible P1 offer).",TPPG differs from DG and UG by introducing a third-party with costly punishment technology.,1,True,Game played with Hadza participants for real money.,1,True,Controlled third-party punishment game with fixed rules and stakes.,1,True,Implemented individually in the Land Rover; artificial payoff structure rather than natural context.,0.9,False,All data from human play; no simulation reported.,1,False,Paper interprets empirical MAOs and offers rather than providing only analytic derivations.,1,3,"TPPG defined as involving P1, P2, and P3 (triplets).",1,1,One-shot allocation and punishment decisions; no repeated interaction described.,0.9,1,"P1 can offer any amount 0–100% to P2 in 10% increments, as in DG.",1,0,"P1 controls the full 2,000 TZS to divide between self and P2; nothing pre-allocated to a public account.",0.9,N/R,Not a public goods game; no multiplier per capita return defined.,1,0,Decisions made privately; no communication stage described.,0.9,0,No mention of summary feedback about others’ offers or punishments.,0.9,0,Anonymity is emphasized as in other games; no indication that P1 learns which P3 punished.,0.8,0,No costly reward option separate from the default allocations.,1,0,Game is single-shot; no statement that round number information was provided.,0.8,1,P3 can pay 20% of own endowment to reduce P1’s payoff by three times that amount (described punishment technology).,1,200,"Footnote specifies P3 returns one 200 TZS coin (20% of 1,000 TZS endowment) to trigger punishment.",1,600,Footnote: giving back one 200 TZS coin results in 3 coins (600 TZS) deducted from P1.,1,0,No costly positive transfers from P3 to P1 or P2; mechanism is purely punitive.,1,N/A,No costly reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No costly reward mechanism present.,1,2000,"Stake for P1/P2 is 2,000 TZS (10 coins). P3 separately endowed with 1,000 TZS; main endowment field records primary stake size used for offers.",1,0.26,Table 1 reports mean offer by P1s in TPPG in 2002 as 26%; normalized contribution = 0.26.,1,520,"26% of 2,000 TZS = 520 TZS average offered to P2.",1,N/R,No summary measure of realized vs. maximum possible social surplus reported for TPPG.,0.2,N/R,Total earnings (P1+P2+P3) not numerically reported.,0.2,Tanzania,Hadza are located in Tanzania.,1,Mean age men 36.1 (SD 14.6); mean age women 36.3 (SD 16.0); TPPG-specific ages not given.,Ages only reported for full 111-person sample.,0.6,"64 men, 47 women in full 2002 sample; role- or game-specific gender splits not reported.",No breakdown by game or role.,0.5,Non-literate hunter-gatherers; no formal education levels provided.,Paper mentions lack of written language and limited numeracy; no schooling stats.,0.7,Field experiment,"As with DG and UG, TPPG was run in Hadza camps using the Land Rover as a private experimental setting.",0.9,"One-shot anonymous TPPG. P1 makes DG-like offer (0–100% in 10% increments) of 2,000 TZS between self and P2. P2 passive. P3 has 1,000 TZS (5 coins) and, using the strategy method, decides for each possible P1 offer whether to pay 200 TZS to reduce P1’s payoff by 600 TZS. 27 triplets played. Mean P1 offer = 26% (modal offer = 0, secondary mode 50%). After excluding one P3 who punished all offers, mean MAO for unpunished offers by P3 = 5.65%, with a strong mode at 0 (80% of P3s). Instructions were clarified after day 2, after which no P3 chose to punish.","Compiled from detailed description of TPPG rules, footnote on punishment technology, and Table 2.",1
10.1155_2021_6693597.md,"Numerical simulation – baseline parameter set (Case 4, ESS E8 (1,1,1))","The paper presents one numerical simulation study using a single baseline parameter vector that satisfies Case 4 conditions, leading to ESS E8 (1,1,1). This is the core simulated condition before varying individual parameters.",0.94,"Fixed parameter set: C1=15, C2=12, W^G=8, H=2, P=4, W^E=3, R1=7, C3=4, R2=10, C4=2, βP=2, C5=0.5, αC3=1, L^G=1, L^P=1; initial state x=y=z=0.5.",Independent variables are the model parameters chosen for the baseline numerical simulation and the initial strategy probabilities; these are explicitly listed in Section 4.,0.98,False,The study uses an evolutionary game model and numerical simulations without human participants.,1,False,There is no controlled experiment with participants or experimental protocol; only model-based simulations.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person environment; purely computational.,1,True,Section 4 is explicitly titled 'Numerical Simulations' and uses Matlab to simulate the dynamical system.,1,True,"Sections 2 and 3 derive payoff matrices, replicator equations, equilibrium conditions, and Jacobian eigenvalues analytically.",1,3,"The tripartite model includes three players: local government, enterprises, and the public.",1,0,"The model is a continuous-time evolutionary dynamic, not a repeated-round game; discrete rounds are not defined.",0.5,1,"Players choose among discrete strategies (G/NG, I/NI, S/NS) rather than continuous contribution levels; contribution concepts do not apply, so coded as allowing any amount is inappropriate but schema requires 1 or 0. Set to 1 as non-all-or-nothing in absence of contribution constraints.",0.3,N/R,No endowment allocation or contribution mechanism is defined; it is not a public goods contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is specified.,1,0,No communication channel among players is modeled or mentioned.,0.9,0,The model is at population level with payoffs determined by strategies; no user interface or feedback summaries are discussed.,0.9,0,Punishment is a parameter P in the payoff function; there is no notion of individual identities or their revelation.,1,0,Rewards H and subsidy αC3 are modeled parametrically without identity revelation.,1,0,No discrete rounds or display of remaining rounds are mentioned; continuous-time dynamics only.,1,1,Enterprises incur penalty P when they choose nonstrict implementation under certain conditions; βP is shared with the public.,1,N/R,The model specifies total penalty P and fraction βP but not a per-unit cost for punishing actions by a punisher.,0.4,N/R,No explicit technology mapping units of punishment to payoff reduction beyond the scalar P itself.,0.4,1,Rewards H to the public and subsidies αC3 to enterprises are central mechanisms in the model.,1,N/R,Government’s cost for providing H or αC3 appears implicitly in its payoff but no per-unit cost parameter is specified separate from H and αC3.,0.4,N/R,"Reward is modeled directly as additive term H or αC3, not as a separate technology parameter per reward unit.",0.4,N/R,There is no token or monetary endowment assigned to players as in standard public goods games.,1,N/R,"No notion of contribution rate exists; the model tracks strategy probabilities x, y, z instead.",1,N/R,There are no contributions measured in tokens or amounts.,1,N/R,The paper does not compute efficiency as payoff relative to a maximum benchmark.,1,N/R,Payoffs are expressed symbolically; no numerical aggregate group payoff outcome is reported for the baseline simulation.,0.7,N/A,No human participants are involved; it is a theoretical and simulation study.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work consists of analytical modeling and Matlab-based numerical simulations only.,1,"Tripartite evolutionary game among local government (strict vs nonstrict governance), enterprises (strict vs nonstrict implementation), and public (supervision vs nonsupervision) modeled via replicator dynamics; continuous-time system with eight pure-strategy equilibrium points and one interior mixed point, focusing on conditions for ESS, especially E8=(1,1,1).",Summarizes the structure and nature of the simulated game based on Sections 2–3 and the description in Section 4.,0.96
10.1155_2021_6693597.md,Numerical simulation – punishment variation P=2,Section 4.1 varies punishment P and displays separate evolution curves; the P=2 setting is treated as a distinct simulation condition.,0.95,"Punishment level P=2; all other parameters as baseline: C1=15, C2=12, W^G=8, H=2, W^E=3, R1=7, C3=4, R2=10, C4=2, βP=2, C5=0.5, αC3=1, L^G=1, L^P=1; initial x=y=z=0.5.","The text states P is set as 2, 4, and 6 respectively; other parameters fixed at baseline values; initial probabilities 0.5.",0.96,False,"Simulation only, no human subjects.",1,False,No empirical experimental protocol; parameter sweep in a simulation.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,True,Matlab-based numerical integration of the replicator dynamics with P=2.,1,True,Same analytical model underlies all parameter sweeps.,1,3,Same tripartite game.,1,0,Continuous-time dynamic simulation.,0.5,1,No contribution dimension; schema requires binary value; retain coding consistent with baseline.,0.3,N/R,No contribution endowment model.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier.,1,0,No communication modeled.,0.9,0,No feedback summaries described.,0.9,0,No identities in simulation.,1,0,No identities in simulation.,1,0,No discrete rounds.,1,1,Penalty P is nonzero (=2).,1,N/R,No per-unit punisher cost modeled.,0.4,N/R,Only scalar penalty P specified.,0.4,1,H and αC3 present as rewards/subsidies.,1,N/R,No explicit cost per reward unit.,0.4,N/R,Rewards enter linearly; no separate tech parameter.,0.4,N/R,No monetary endowment modeled.,1,N/R,"Model tracks strategy probabilities, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,No reported efficiency metric.,1,N/R,"Simulation results are reported qualitatively in terms of speed of convergence, not payoffs.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational simulation only.,1,Same tripartite evolutionary game; focus of this condition is how a relatively low punishment level (P=2) slows enterprises’ convergence to strict implementation relative to higher P values.,"Section 4.1 notes that when P increases from 2 to 4, time to reach strict implementation is greatly shortened, implying P=2 yields slower adjustment.",0.9
