10.1155_2021_6693597.md,Numerical simulation – punishment variation P=6,P=6 appears as the high-punishment condition in the punishment sweep.,0.95,"Punishment level P=6; other parameters as baseline: C1=15, C2=12, W^G=8, H=2, W^E=3, R1=7, C3=4, R2=10, C4=2, βP=2, C5=0.5, αC3=1, L^G=1, L^P=1; initial x=y=z=0.5.","Section 4.1 explicitly sets P to 2, 4 and 6, with others fixed at baseline.",0.96,False,Simulation only.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Not a lab setting.,1,True,Matlab simulations with higher P.,1,True,Same analytical model.,1,3,Same players.,1,0,Continuous dynamic.,0.5,1,No contributions modeled.,0.3,N/R,No contribution framework.,1,N/R,No public goods.,1,0,No communication dimension.,0.9,0,No summaries or displays.,0.9,0,No identities.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,Continuous-time model.,1,1,Penalty parameter P (=6) is present.,1,N/R,No cost per unit of punishment modeled.,0.4,N/R,Only scalar magnitude P.,0.4,1,Rewards remain.,1,N/R,No explicit cost term.,0.4,N/R,No separate tech parameter.,0.4,N/R,No endowment given.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,"Narrative focuses on convergence speed, not payoffs.",0.7,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"High-punishment condition demonstrating that while increasing P from 4 to 6 further speeds convergence of enterprises to strict implementation, the marginal effect is weaker than going from P=2 to P=4.",Directly described in Section 4.1: influence of punishment is gradually weakened as P increases.,0.9
10.1155_2021_6693597.md,Numerical simulation – reward variation H=1,"Section 4.2 examines H values 1, 2, and 3; H=1 is the low-reward scenario.",0.95,"Reward H=1; other parameters held at baseline (except H): C1=15, C2=12, W^G=8, P=4, W^E=3, R1=7, C3=4, R2=10, C4=2, βP=2, C5=0.5, αC3=1, L^G=1, L^P=1; initial x=y=z=0.5.","Text states H is set as 1, 2, and 3; other parameters as in the baseline numerical experiment.",0.96,False,No human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Simulates dynamics under different H values.,1,True,Same analytical game.,1,3,Same three-agent game.,1,0,Continuous-time simulation.,0.5,1,No contributions dimension.,0.3,N/R,No contribution process.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication modeled.,0.9,0,No feedback mechanisms.,0.9,0,No identities.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,Not a round-based game.,1,1,Penalty P and βP included.,1,N/R,Punisher cost not parameterized.,0.4,N/R,Penalty magnitude only.,0.4,1,Reward H (=1) and subsidy αC3 are present.,1,N/R,Cost per reward intuited but not parameterized separately.,0.4,N/R,No explicit tech mapping; H is direct.,0.4,N/R,No endowment.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,No numerical payoffs reported for this condition.,0.7,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Low-reward scenario used to show that smaller H yields weaker incentives for public supervision and slower or lower convergence of z to 1, while making strict governance less fiscally burdensome.","Inferred from comparative description: with increase in H, public’s incentive increases but growth rate weakens, and higher H slows government’s evolution to strict governance.",0.85
10.1155_2021_6693597.md,Numerical simulation – reward variation H=2 (baseline H),"H=2 serves both as baseline and as the middle level in the H=1,2,3 sweep.",0.95,"Reward H=2; other parameters at baseline: C1=15, C2=12, W^G=8, P=4, W^E=3, R1=7, C3=4, R2=10, C4=2, βP=2, C5=0.5, αC3=1, L^G=1, L^P=1; initial x=y=z=0.5.",Explicit in Section 4 and 4.2.,0.97,False,Simulation-based.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Numerical simulation for H=2.,1,True,Same analytical model.,1,3,Unchanged.,1,0,Continuous-time.,0.5,1,No contribution decisions.,0.3,N/R,Not applicable.,1,N/R,Not a public good.,1,0,Communication not modeled.,0.9,0,No interface.,0.9,0,No identities.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,No rounds.,1,1,Penalty present.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0.4,N/R,Not specified.,0.4,1,Reward H and subsidy exist.,1,N/R,Not parameterized.,0.4,N/R,No tech parameter.,0.4,N/R,None defined.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,Not computed.,1,N/R,Not reported numerically.,0.7,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,Middle-reward scenario; described as providing strong enough incentives for supervision while not overly burdening government finances compared to higher H.,Section 4.2 emphasizes trade-off between incentivizing public and fiscal burden with varying H.,0.84
10.1155_2021_6693597.md,Numerical simulation – reward variation H=3,H=3 is the high-reward condition in the reward sweep.,0.95,"Reward H=3; other parameters baseline: C1=15, C2=12, W^G=8, P=4, W^E=3, R1=7, C3=4, R2=10, C4=2, βP=2, C5=0.5, αC3=1, L^G=1, L^P=1; initial x=y=z=0.5.",Explicitly listed in Section 4.2.,0.96,False,Simulation-based.,1,False,No human experiment.,1,False,Not lab-based.,1,True,Dynamics simulated under high H.,1,True,Underlying analytical model unchanged.,1,3,Unchanged.,1,0,Continuous-time.,0.5,1,No contribution dimension.,0.3,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/R,No public goods.,1,0,No communication.,0.9,0,No UI.,0.9,0,No identities.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,Continuous dynamic.,1,1,Penalty mechanisms present.,1,N/R,Not parametrized.,0.4,N/R,Not parametrized.,0.4,1,Higher H is a reward.,1,N/R,Government cost not separately parameterized.,0.4,N/R,No explicit tech parameter.,0.4,N/R,None specified.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not numerically reported.,0.7,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"High-reward scenario showing stronger supervision incentives (z moves to 1 faster), but also slower evolution of government toward strict governance due to higher fiscal burden, with diminishing marginal benefits to supervision speed.","Section 4.2: with increase in H, supervision incentive increases but growth rate weakens, and higher rewards slow government evolution.",0.88
10.1155_2021_6693597.md,Numerical simulation – subsidy coefficient variation α=1/8,"Section 4.3 sets α to 1/8, 3/8, and 5/8; α=1/8 is the low-subsidy condition.",0.95,"Subsidy coefficient α such that αC3=0.5 (α=1/8 with C3=4); other parameters baseline: C1=15, C2=12, W^G=8, H=2, P=4, W^E=3, R1=7, C3=4, R2=10, C4=2, βP=2, C5=0.5, L^G=1, L^P=1; initial x=y=z=0.5.","Text: α set to 1/8, 3/8, 5/8 with other parameters fixed; computed αC3 by multiplying C3=4.",0.96,False,Simulation only.,1,False,No human experiment.,1,False,Not lab-based.,1,True,Numerical dynamics for differing α.,1,True,Analytical model unchanged.,1,3,Same three players.,1,0,Continuous dynamics.,0.5,1,No contribution decisions.,0.3,N/R,No endowment splitting.,1,N/R,No public good.,1,0,No communication modeled.,0.9,0,No summaries.,0.9,0,No identities.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,Continuous-time model.,1,1,Penalty P and βP present.,1,N/R,Not given.,0.4,N/R,Not given.,0.4,1,Subsidy is a reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Subsidy cost implicit in payoff but not parameterized.,0.4,N/R,Subsidy enters linearly; no tech parameter.,0.4,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Payoffs not numerically summarized.,0.7,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Low-subsidy scenario illustrating weaker incentives for enterprises to adopt strict implementation but less fiscal burden on government, leading to higher government willingness for strict governance compared to higher α.","Section 4.3: subsidy promotes enterprises’ strict implementation, but high subsidy burdens the government, reducing its willingness to govern strictly.",0.86
