10.1007_s13235-010-0001-4.md,"Others-only optional public goods game without punishment (finite population, social learning simulation)","Paper describes an optional others-only public goods game with strategies X (contributors), Y (defectors), Z (non-participants) analyzed via individual-based simulations and analytical limits; this is the baseline condition without punishment.",0.9,"Availability of participation option (optional vs compulsory); no punishment; finite population with social learning (pairwise comparison, exploration rate μ, imitation strength s). This condition: optional participation with strategies X,Y,Z; no punishment.",Independent factors distinguishing this condition are the existence of the non-participant strategy Z (optional participation) and absence of any punishment mechanism.,0.9,False,Study uses mathematical analysis and computer simulations of evolutionary dynamics; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment with subjects; only theoretical and simulation work.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,True,Explicitly describes individual-based computer simulations and Markov/replicator dynamics of strategies in finite and infinite populations.,1,True,Provides closed-form payoff expressions and analytical treatment in strong imitation and infinite population limits.,1,8,"Group size denoted N; simulations and figures use N=8 (e.g., Fig.1 caption: 'Parameters: M=100, N=8...').",0.9,1,"Model is a repeated evolutionary process but each public goods interaction is a single-shot game; no fixed number of rounds per match is specified, so treated as 1-shot stage game.",0.6,1,Players contribute a fixed amount c>0 or not; no option for partial contribution between 0 and c; treated as binary (all-or-nothing) contribution decision.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects; endowments and allocations are not implemented as default balances.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not reported explicitly as MPCR; only multiplier r and group size N are given.,0,N/A,No human interaction; communication not modeled.,1,0,Model only specifies payoffs from contributions and abstention; no observation stage of others’ payoffs or histories is described.,0.7,0,No punishment in this condition; identity revelation not applicable but coded as 0 because no punishing stage exists.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,Agents update via evolutionary dynamics and do not receive explicit round-count information.,0.8,0,This baseline game is explicitly 'without punishment.',1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewarding mechanisms are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Contribution cost c is specified but initial endowment per player (budget constraint) is not reported.,0,N/R,"Outcomes are described qualitatively (cyclic dominance, frequencies of strategies) but no explicit average contribution rates are reported.",0,N/R,No numerical average contribution amounts are given; only parameter c as contribution level.,0,N/R,Paper discusses existence of social dilemma but does not report normalized efficiency statistics.,0,N/R,Expected payoffs per type are derived symbolically but no aggregate group payoff values are reported as results.,0,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results stem from simulations and analytical models.,1,"Others-only optional public goods game; group size N=8, multiplier r=3, outside option payoff σ=0.5, cost of cooperation c (not numerically fixed in simulations section, but appears as parameter); population size M=100; strategies: X (cooperate), Y (defect), Z (opt out). Imitation strength s large or varied; exploration rate μ small.","Summarized from model description and figure captions (e.g., Fig.1 and Fig.2).",0.9
10.1007_s13235-010-0001-4.md,"Others-only optional public goods game with peer punishment, no second-order punishment (finite population, social learning simulation)",Section 'Peer Punishment' plus Fig.1(b) describe simulations where peer-punishers (W) are added to the optional PGG without second-order punishment.,0.95,"Presence of peer punishment strategy W; punishment targets only first-order defectors Y; optional participation (strategies X,Y,Z,W); imitation strength s and mutation μ as given.","Main manipulation relative to baseline is introduction of peer-punishers with fines β and cost γ, without punishing non-punishers.",0.95,False,"Simulations and analytic results, no human data.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment in the behavioral sense.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Individual-based simulations of M=100 agents with imitation dynamics; transition matrices derived.,1,True,"Provides payoff formulas and transition matrices, analyzes strong imitation and infinite population limits.",1,8,"Caption of Fig.1(b): 'Parameters: M=100, N=8, r=3, σ=0.5, γ=β=G=B=0.2, μ=10^{-4} and s=100000'.",1,1,One-shot PGG per interaction; evolutionary time modeled separately.,0.6,1,Players either contribute fixed c or 0; no continuous contribution levels.,0.9,N/A,No human endowments or default allocations.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly defined; only multiplier r and others-only structure.,0,N/A,No communication modeled between agents.,1,0,Payoff-based imitation uses own and comparison partner’s payoffs but no explicit global summaries are revealed.,0.7,0,Punishment is modeled as payoff externalities; no representation of identities or who punished whom.,0.8,0,No reward stage.,1,0,Agents are not described as observing remaining rounds; infinite-horizon evolutionary process.,0.8,1,Peer punishment is explicitly implemented with parameters β (fine) and γ (cost).,1,0.2,Parameter γ represents cost per punished defector; caption indicates γ=0.2.,0.95,0.2,Parameter β is fine per punisher per defector; caption indicates β=0.2.,0.95,0,"Only punishment, no reward mechanism.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment not specified; only contribution cost c and punishment parameters reported.,0,N/R,Results described qualitatively (peer-punishers dominate long intervals; cycles); no numerical contribution rates.,0,N/R,No numeric average contributions reported.,0,N/R,No normalized efficiency measures for this condition.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics reported; only strategy frequencies and stability.,0,N/A,Simulation agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is a simulation model with no subject pool.,1,"Others-only optional PGG with N=8, r=3, σ=0.5, cost c; strategies: X (cooperator), Y (defector), Z (non-participant), W (peer-punisher who cooperates and punishes Y). Population size M=100, imitation strength s=100000, exploration rate μ=10^{-4}. Peer-punishers fine each defector β per punisher at personal cost γ per defector; simulation uses β=γ=0.2.",Taken from peer punishment section and Fig.1(b) caption.,0.95
