10.1007_s13235-010-0001-4.md,"Optional public goods game with both peer and pool punishment, no second-order punishment (finite population, social learning simulation)","Section 'The Competition of Pool- and Peer-Punishers' and Fig.4(c) consider strategies X,Y,Z,V,W with no second-order punishment available.",0.95,Competition between peer-punishers W and pool-punishers V; punishment only first-order (defectors Y); optional participation including Z; parameters β=γ=G=B=0.2.,Simultaneous availability of both peer and pool punishment without second-order punishment is the distinct condition.,0.95,False,All results from simulations and analytical transition matrices.,1,False,No human subjects.,1,False,No lab/field context.,1,True,Finite-population stochastic dynamics with M=100 shown in Fig.4(c).,1,True,Transition matrices for strong imitation derived and stationary distributions computed.,1,8,Shared parameter set in Fig.4: N=8.,1,1,Stage game is one-shot PGG.,0.6,1,Contribution is c or 0.,0.9,N/A,No human endowment state.,1,N/R,Not defined.,0,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No explicit information on others’ histories beyond imitation payoff comparison.,0.7,0,Identity of punishers not modeled.,0.8,0,No rewards.,1,0,No explicit horizon.,0.8,1,Both peer and pool punishment mechanisms exist.,1,0.2,Peer punishers pay γ=0.2 per punished defector; pool-punishers pay G=0.2 into pool. We record the cost parameter magnitude as 0.2.,0.9,0.2,Defectors fined via peer punishment with β=0.2 and via pool punishment with B=0.2.,0.9,0,No rewards described.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment per agent not provided.,0,N/R,"Figure shows frequency of strategies as a function of imitation strength s, not direct contribution metrics.",0,N/R,No contribution amounts reported numerically.,0,N/R,No numeric efficiency data.,0,N/R,Group payoff not reported.,0,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results derived from simulations/analysis.,1,"Others-only optional PGG, N=8, r=3, σ=0.5, M=100, μ=10^{-6}, β=γ=G=B=0.2. Strategies: X, Y, Z, V (pool-punisher), W (peer-punisher). No second-order punishment; peer-punishers punish only Y; pool-punishers punish only Y. For strong imitation, stationary distribution shows majority peer-punishers.",From competition section and Fig.4(c) caption plus transition matrix discussion.,0.9
10.1007_s13235-010-0001-4.md,"Optional public goods game with both peer and pool punishment, with second-order punishment (finite population, social learning simulation)",Same competition setting as previous but section states 'with second order punishment' and Fig.4(d) plus corresponding transition matrix show pool-punishers prevail.,0.95,"Competition between peer-punishers W and pool-punishers V when second-order punishment enabled: pool-punishers punish W and X (non-contributors to pool), while peer-punishers do not punish V; optional participation with X,Y,Z,V,W.","Key manipulation vs prior condition is activation of second-order punishment, especially by pool-punishers against non-pool contributors.",0.95,False,Simulation/analytical only.,1,False,No behavioral experiment.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Finite-population simulation results shown in Fig.4(d).,1,True,"Transition matrices for strong imitation and stationary distribution (0,0,0,1,0) computed.",1,8,Fig.4 parameters: N=8.,1,1,"Per interaction, the game is one-shot.",0.6,1,Contribution choice is binary at c.,0.9,N/A,No human endowment structure.,1,N/R,MPCR not calculated.,0,N/A,No model of communication.,1,0,No explicit summaries stage.,0.7,0,Identity resolution not modeled.,0.8,0,No rewards present.,1,0,No finite-horizon feedback.,0.8,1,Both peer and pool punishment implemented with second-order punishment.,1,0.2,"γ and G both 0.2 as costs of peer and pool punishment, respectively.",0.9,0.2,Fine parameters β and B both 0.2.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment not mentioned.,0,N/R,Results reported as stationary frequencies of strategies not contribution rates.,0,N/R,No explicit contribution amounts.,0,N/R,Efficiency not numerically reported.,0,N/R,Group payoff stats absent.,0,N/A,Simulation agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The environment is computational.,1,"Others-only optional PGG with strategies X,Y,Z,V,W. Parameters: M=100, N=8, r=3, σ=0.5, β=γ=G=B=0.2, μ=10^{-6}. Second-order punishment: pool-punishers punish X and W (since they do not pay into pool) as well as Y, whereas peer-punishers do not punish V. Strong imitation limit yields stationary distribution (0,0,0,1,0), meaning pool-punishers dominate.","From competition section description, assumptions about second-order punishment, and given transition matrices.",0.9
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,Baseline OPGG without punishment or reputation,Paper describes the traditional optional public goods game (OPGG) without punishment or reputation as the benchmark reproduced in figure 1 and text in section 3.,1,"Institutional environment: no punishment, no reputation (baseline OPGG). Social norm: none.",Condition defined by absence of both punishment stage and reputational system; serves as baseline in results section.,1,False,Model is an agent-based / evolutionary simulation with agents updating strategies; no human participants mentioned in methods.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational evolutionary simulation.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely simulated agents.,1,True,"Section 2 describes a computational model with N agents, strategy updating, parameters, and extensive simulations.",1,False,"While some theoretical discussion exists, results rely on numerical simulations rather than closed-form analysis.",0.9,5,Group size n=5 reported in figure 1 caption and section 2: 'groups of n ≥ 4'; figure 1 specifies n=5 for simulations.,1,1,"Each time-step consists of an OPGG round followed (in some treatments) by punishment; repeated interactions are modeled by continuation probability Ω, not fixed discrete rounds; per interaction, there is a single OPGG decision round, so we record 1.",0.7,0,"Players choose among discrete actions C, D, or L; cooperators contribute fixed cost c, defectors contribute 0; no continuous contribution level.",1,N/A,"No human subjects; endowment and contributions are model primitives, not initial allocations in an interface.",1,0.6,"MPCR = r / n. Figure 1: r=3, n=5, so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No human communication; agent interaction governed by model rules only.,1,N/A,"Simulation agents have full payoff information needed for updating, but no UI; human-oriented display concept not applicable.",1,N/A,No human identities; punishment decisions are based on observed actions in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,Agents are programmed with continuation probability Ω; no display of round numbers to human subjects.,1,0,Baseline OPGG is described 'in the absence of both punishment and any reputational element'.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,"Paper only models costly punishment, no rewarding stage.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/R,Utility function uses w_i as initial endowment but its numerical value is not specified.,0.3,N/R,"They report proportions of actions (e.g., 20% of time cooperate) but not as normalized fraction of endowment because contribution if cooperating is fixed cost c; no explicit rate relative to endowment.",0.4,N/R,"Contribution cost c is not numerically specified, only symbolically.",0.3,N/R,No explicit ratio of group payoff to maximum payoff reported for this baseline.,0.1,N/R,Average payoffs are discussed qualitatively; numerical group payoff not provided.,0.1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents; no human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational / in silico evolutionary simulations.,1,"Optional public goods game with three pure actions (C, D, L); group size n=5; contribution cost c if cooperating; loner payoff σ=1; contribution sum multiplied by r=3 and shared among participants; if only one participant, game cancelled and all receive σ; groups persist with continuation probability Ω=10/11 across interactions; population size N=1000 with imitation dynamics, mutation rate ε=0.1, and mixing parameter m=0.95.",Summarized from model description in section 2 and parameter settings from figure 1 caption.,1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with punishment only (no reputation),"Section 3 describes introducing the full set of punishing strategies 'as in Rand & Nowak [25]' without reputation, benchmarked against the baseline OPGG.",1,"Institutional environment: punishment available, no reputation. Social norm: none. Punishment parameters γ=1, β=2.","Figure 1 caption: 'Those including punishment additionally have γ=1, β=2'; this condition lacks any reputational system.",1,False,Condition implemented in the same agent-based simulation framework; no humans.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; purely simulated.,1,False,No physical lab or human interface.,1,True,Described as adding punishment strategies in the computational model.,1,False,"Outcomes obtained numerically, not analytically.",0.9,5,Same simulation parameters as in figure 1: n=5.,1,1,"Each interaction consists of one OPGG decision followed by a punishment substage; repetition via Ω, not fixed rounds.",0.7,0,"Actions are discrete C, D, L with fixed cost c for C.",1,N/A,No human endowment display; simulation only.,1,0.6,"Same r=3, n=5 as baseline, so MPCR = 3/5.",1,N/A,No communication among human subjects.,1,N/A,No human UI; agents compute payoffs internally.,1,N/A,No human identities; punishment is modeled algorithmically.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No display to humans; only Ω governs repetition.,1,1,Players can 'punish any or all of the other n−1 members' after each round; this condition has punishment but no reputation.,1,1,Figures with punishment specify γ=1 as the cost to punisher per target.,1,2,Figures with punishment specify β=2 as penalty per punishment received; thus each punishment reduces target payoff by 2.,1,0,No rewarding modeled; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Initial endowment w_i used symbolically; value not given.,0.3,N/R,"They discuss proportions of actions (C, D, L) qualitatively, but not normalized contributions relative to endowment.",0.4,N/R,Contribution cost c not specified numerically.,0.3,N/R,"Population fitness is compared qualitatively (e.g., lower fitness), but no explicit efficiency ratio reported.",0.2,N/R,Average payoffs per group not numerically reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Completed entirely via computational simulations.,1,"Same OPGG as baseline with N=1000, n=5, r=3, σ=1, m=0.95, ε=0.1, Ω=10/11, T=2×10^5. After each round, players may punish any others; each punishment costs γ=1 to punisher and imposes β=2 penalty on target. Full spectrum of 24 strategies (3 pure actions × 8 punishment patterns) when only punishment is available.",Summarized from section 2 description of punishment and figure 1 caption specifying parameters.,1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with reputation only – AD (Anti-Defector) norm,Section 3(a) examines 'Reputation without punishment' and highlights the AD social norm as the one sustaining the highest cooperation.,1,"Institutional environment: reputation system enabled, no punishment. Social norm: AD (Anti-Defector) – reputations: C→+1, D→−1, L→0.","AD norm defined in section 2; section 3(a) explicitly studies OPGG + reputation, no punishment, for each norm separately.",1,False,Agent-based simulation; no experimental subjects.,1,False,"Configuration is a simulation treatment, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Uses the same computational framework with additional reputational dynamics.,1,False,"Results derived numerically, not via closed-form analysis.",0.9,5,Figure 1 parameters (n=5) apply to all simulations including reputation-only variants.,1,1,Each interaction has one OPGG action stage; repetition via Ω as before.,0.7,0,Discrete C/D/L with fixed contribution c for C.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,0.6,"Same r=3, n=5 as in figure 1.",1,N/A,No communication among simulated agents beyond modeled signals.,1,N/A,Display concept doesn’t apply without human participants.,1,N/A,No punishment allowed in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents are coded with Ω; no display.,1,0,This subsection explicitly analyzes reputation 'without punishment'.,1,N/A,Punishment disabled.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,"Only reputational updates, no material rewards.",1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,w_i present symbolically; no numeric value.,0.3,0.4,"Section 3(a): 'about 40% of the OPGG actions are cooperative' under AD norm with reputation only, interpreted as normalized cooperation frequency.",0.9,N/R,Numerical value of c not given.,0.3,N/R,They state population fitness is highest under AD but do not give explicit efficiency ratio.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mean payoffs reported for this condition.,0.2,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,In silico simulations with N=1000 agents.,1,"OPGG with reputation dynamics: reputations ∈ {−1,0,+1} assigned after each round according to AD norm (C→+1, D→−1, L→0). Strategies include unconditional C, D, L and eight conditional strategies X^{k_min,Y} (e.g., C^{0,L}, D^{0,L}), actions conditioned on average reputation of others exceeding threshold k_min. Groups reshuffled with mixing parameter m=0.95; continuation probability Ω=10/11; mutation rate ε=0.1; N=1000, n=5, r=3, σ=1.",From section 2 strategy description and figure 1 parameters.,1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with reputation only – AN (Anti-Neither) norm,"Section 2 defines AN norm; section 3(a) analyzes 'other social norms' in reputation-only setting and notes they fail to raise cooperation above baseline, including AN.",1,"Institutional environment: reputation system enabled, no punishment. Social norm: AN (Anti-Neither) – reputations: C→+1, D→0, L→0.","AN norm assigns 0 to both defectors and loners, detailed in model description.",1,False,Simulation condition.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab/human setting.,1,True,Implemented in the agent-based model.,1,False,Outcomes via simulations.,0.9,5,Same as other reputation-only simulations (n=5).,1,1,Single OPGG decision per interaction; repetition via Ω.,0.7,0,Discrete C/D/L choices.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5.",1,N/A,No communication channel.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,Punishment disabled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,Reputation-only treatment; no punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,w_i unspecified numerically.,0.3,N/R,Text: 'all other social norms are unable to sustain levels of cooperation higher than the baseline' but does not state explicit cooperation percentages for AN.,0.3,N/R,c not given.,0.3,N/R,Population fitness relative to baseline discussed qualitatively but not quantified for AN.,0.2,N/R,No numeric payoff data for AN.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulated agents only.,1,"Same OPGG and evolutionary setup as AD reputation-only case, but with AN norm: cooperators receive reputation +1, defectors and loners receive 0. Conditional strategies use average group reputation as a cue; however, because defectors and loners are indistinguishable reputationally, conditional cooperators cannot discriminate between them, resulting in low cooperation comparable to baseline.",Summarized from model description of AN norm and discussion in 3(a).,1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with reputation only – AL (Anti-Loner) norm,AL norm defined in section 2; its performance is discussed in section 3(a) as failing to sustain cooperation.,1,"Institutional environment: reputation system enabled, no punishment. Social norm: AL (Anti-Loner) – reputations: C→+1, L→−1, D→0.",Model description: 'AL norm assigns loners a reputation of −1 and defectors a 0'.,1,False,Simulated environment.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,AL is one of several social norms implemented in simulations.,1,False,Results numeric.,0.9,5,Same n=5 as other reputation-only treatments.,1,1,Single OPGG action stage per interaction.,0.7,0,"Three discrete actions C, D, L.",1,N/A,No human UI.,1,0.6,"Same r=3, n=5.",1,N/A,No chat in simulation.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,Reputation-only scenario.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,w_i not specified numerically.,0.3,N/R,AL is grouped with other norms that 'are unable to sustain levels of cooperation higher than the baseline'; no explicit percentage given.,0.3,N/R,c not given.,0.3,N/R,They note lowest payoffs for AL in some contexts but not quantified for reputation-only.,0.2,N/R,No specific numeric payoff for AL reputation-only case.,0.2,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations.,1,"Same OPGG, parameters, and strategy space as other reputation-only treatments, but AL norm makes loners strictly least reputable (C→+1, D→0, L→−1). Text notes that assigning loners too low a reputation 'hampers the role of loners in providing a backup option' and AL fails to raise cooperation above baseline, leading to populations largely dominated by loners in the longer run.",Based on norm definition and discussion in 3(a).,1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with reputation only – AB (Anti-Both) norm,AB norm defined in section 2; its performance included in comparison of social norms in section 3(a).,1,"Institutional environment: reputation system enabled, no punishment. Social norm: AB (Anti-Both) – reputations: C→+1, D→−1, L→−1.",Model description: 'AB social norm ... assigns −1 to both [loner and defector] actions'.,1,False,Simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No lab/humans.,1,True,One of several norms tested in simulations.,1,False,Results numeric.,0.9,5,Same n=5 for all figure 1 reputation-only simulations.,1,1,Single OPGG decision per interaction.,0.7,0,C/D/L discrete actions.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5.",1,N/A,No communication.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,Reputation-only treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,w_i unspecified numerically.,0.3,N/R,Grouped with other norms that do not raise cooperation above baseline; no explicit percentage for AB.,0.3,N/R,c not specified.,0.3,N/R,Population fitness under AB in reputation-only setting not quantified numerically.,0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoffs.,0.2,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations.,1,"Same OPGG and reputation framework as other reputation-only conditions, but AB norm gives both defectors and loners the worst reputation (−1). Text notes that 'attributing loners too low a reputation (in AL and AB) hampers the role of loners in providing a backup option against invading defectors', leading to low cooperation and dominance of loners/defectors.",Based on norm definition and discussion in 3(a).,1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with punishment and reputation – AD (Anti-Defector) norm,Section 3(b) analyzes 'Reputation with punishment' and highlights AD as producing the highest cooperation when both mechanisms are present.,1,"Institutional environment: punishment and reputation jointly enabled. Social norm: AD (Anti-Defector) – C→+1, D→−1, L→0. Punishment parameters: γ=1, β=2.",AD norm definition plus punishment parameters from figure 1; section 3(b) explicitly studies their combination.,1,False,Simulated evolutionary population.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Core treatment in computational model.,1,False,"Results presented via simulations and transition matrices, not analytic solution.",0.9,5,n=5 stated in figure 1; applies to all shown simulations with punishment and reputation.,1,1,Per interaction: OPGG round followed by punishment and reputation update; further repetitions determined by Ω.,0.7,0,Binary cooperate/defect contribution choice plus loner option.,1,N/A,Simulation only; no initial allocation interface.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5 as in figure 1; MPCR=0.6.",1,N/A,No communication channel among human players.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,Identities are not a concept for simulated anonymous agents.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No display to participants; Ω governs repetition.,1,1,Players can punish others after each round; now coexists with reputation.,1,1,γ=1 as per figure 1 and section 2.,1,2,β=2 penalty per received punishment.,1,0,Only punishment and reputation considered; no rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"w_i appears in utility, numeric value not given.",0.3,N/R,"They state cooperation becomes 'the most played strategy' and 'high levels of cooperation', but no explicit fraction for AD with punishment and reputation is reported.",0.3,N/R,Contribution amount c not numerically defined.,0.3,N/R,Population fitness is compared qualitatively (highest among treatments) but no numeric efficiency.,0.3,N/R,No explicit payoff numbers for this condition.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computer simulation.,1,"OPGG with both punishment and AD-based reputation. Strategy space extends to 88 strategies (3 pure actions, 8 conditional action strategies, each with 8 punishment patterns toward C, D, L). After each OPGG round, agents may punish others (cost γ=1, inflict β=2) and reputations are updated per AD norm. Evolution via pairwise imitation with mutation ε=0.1, mixing m=0.95, continuation Ω=10/11, N=1000, n=5, r=3, σ=1. Results: cooperation becomes dominant; punishment mostly disappears except for small fraction of prosocial punishing variants; anti-social punishment almost vanishes.","Combined from section 2 strategy description, figure 1 parameters, and discussion in sections 3(b) and 4.",1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with punishment and reputation – AN (Anti-Neither) norm,"Section 3(b) discusses the impact of adding AN norm with punishment: 'other social norms, excluding AL, are also able to increase and stabilize cooperation albeit to a lesser extent'.",1,"Institutional environment: punishment and reputation jointly enabled. Social norm: AN (Anti-Neither) – C→+1, D→0, L→0. Punishment parameters: γ=1, β=2.",Norm definition from section 2 and mention in 3(b) combined with global parameter settings.,1,False,Simulation treatment.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Agent-based model.,1,False,Numeric results.,0.9,5,Same n=5 as for other conditions.,1,1,One OPGG action per interaction; repeated via Ω.,0.7,0,Discrete choice C/D/L.,1,N/A,No human setup.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5.",1,N/A,No communication.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,Identities not relevant.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Sim-only.,1,1,Punishment is available in 'reputation with punishment' models.,1,1,γ=1 as per figure 1.,1,2,β=2 penalty per punishment.,1,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,w_i unspecified numerically.,0.3,N/R,"Text notes AN with punishment 'able to increase and stabilize cooperation albeit to a lesser extent' than AD, but no explicit proportions.",0.3,N/R,c not numeric.,0.3,N/R,Population fitness is compared qualitatively; no numeric efficiency given.,0.3,N/R,No numeric payoffs for AN+punishment.,0.2,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computational simulations.,1,"Same OPGG and punishment-reputation setup as AD combined condition, but AN norm does not distinguish reputationally between defectors and loners (both reputation 0). Discussion indicates cooperation increases and stabilizes compared to baseline and punishment-only models, but to a lesser extent than AD; higher reputability of defectors leads to higher levels of prosocial punishment to compensate.","Derived from description in 3(b), esp. comparisons of AD, AB, AN.",1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with punishment and reputation – AL (Anti-Loner) norm,AL norm performance under punishment plus reputation is described in 3(b): 'AL fails to stabilize cooperation altogether'.,1,"Institutional environment: punishment and reputation jointly enabled. Social norm: AL (Anti-Loner) – C→+1, L→−1, D→0. Punishment parameters: γ=1, β=2.","Norm definition, plus generic punishment parameters, plus explicit discussion of AL in 3(b).",1,False,Simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Agent-based model.,1,False,Results numeric.,0.9,5,Same n=5.,1,1,One decision round per interaction.,0.7,0,C/D/L discrete.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5.",1,N/A,No communication.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Simulation-only.,1,1,Punishment enabled.,1,1,γ=1.,1,2,β=2.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,w_i unspecified numerically.,0.3,N/R,"Text notes 'AL fails to stabilize cooperation altogether, resulting in large fluctuations of cooperation levels, high proportions of the loner strategy' but no exact cooperation frequency.",0.3,N/R,c not numeric.,0.3,N/R,"They state AL yields 'the lowest payoffs among the social norms both with and without punishment', but not as explicit efficiency value.",0.4,N/R,No numeric payoff for AL with punishment.,0.3,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computational simulations.,1,"Same OPGG and punishment-reputation setup as other combined treatments, but AL norm makes loners strictly least reputable. Section 3(b) reports that AL leads to unstable cooperation, high loner proportions, and lowest population fitness, even when punishment is allowed.",Condensed from description of AL in 3(b).,1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0293.md,OPGG with punishment and reputation – AB (Anti-Both) norm,"AB norm's behavior under punishment+reputation is discussed alongside AD and AN in 3(b), especially regarding cooperation and hostile environments.",1,"Institutional environment: punishment and reputation jointly enabled. Social norm: AB (Anti-Both) – C→+1, D→−1, L→−1. Punishment parameters: γ=1, β=2.",Norm definition and general punishment parameters from model and figure 1.,1,False,Simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Agent-based model treatment.,1,False,Numeric results.,0.9,5,n=5 as for all treatments.,1,1,One action stage per interaction.,0.7,0,Discrete C/D/L.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0.6,"r=3, n=5.",1,N/A,No chat.,1,N/A,No UI.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Simulation-only.,1,1,Punishment allowed.,1,1,γ=1 per punished target.,1,2,β=2 penalty per punishment received.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,w_i not numerically specified.,0.3,N/R,They note high cooperation for AB+punishment (though less than AD) but do not give explicit percentages.,0.3,N/R,c not numeric.,0.3,N/R,Population fitness compared qualitatively; no explicit numbers.,0.3,N/R,No numeric group payoff data.,0.2,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computational.,1,"Same OPGG plus punishment and reputation as AD combined condition, but AB norm gives both defectors and loners the worst reputation (−1). Figure 3 description notes that under AB both defecting and exiting are least reputable, creating 'the most hostile environments and the least reputable OPGG groups', causing conditional cooperators to be cautious and frequently opt out. Nonetheless, with punishment, AB still supports relatively high cooperation compared to baseline and punishment-only models, though less than AD.",Summarized from 3(b) and figure 3 caption.,1
10.1111_brv.12260.md,Synthetic evolutionary model and illustrative functions (no human subjects),"Paper develops a general synthetic evolutionary model with example production functions and response/relatedness functions, but no distinct experiments or empirical game treatments are implemented.",0.78,"Group size N; form of production function (linear vs concave vs sigmoidal); presence/absence and magnitude of responsiveness ρ_N, relatedness κ_N, punishment cost P_N.","Across the theory, selection terms are functions of group size and functional forms for benefits, responsiveness, relatedness, and punishment, varied conceptually to illustrate effects.",0.8,False,No human or animal subjects are studied; the work is a theoretical review with analytical models and schematic examples.,1,False,No controlled experiment with manipulated conditions is conducted; only theoretical analysis and literature review.,1,False,No laboratory setting is used; the work is purely theoretical.,1,False,"Although figures refer to functional forms (e.g., Equations S14–S17, S22, S24), the text does not describe running explicit numerical/agent-based simulations; they are analytical functions plotted for illustration.",0.7,True,"Core contribution is analytical population-genetic and game-theoretic modelling (derivations in Supporting Information, synthetic model, coalescent-based relatedness, etc.).",1,0,No explicit game with a fixed number of human players is implemented; N is a symbolic group size variable in analytical models.,1,0,No actual repeated game experiment with rounds is run; repeated interactions are treated analytically.,1,0,Not applicable as there are no concrete player decisions; contribution structures are generic continuous investments in helping.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects or implemented game; default allocation between private/public accounts is undefined.,1,N/R,"A generic public goods framework is used, but no single marginal per capita return value is specified as a parameter of an implemented game.",0.6,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,Design elements like feedback screens do not exist; purely analytical.,1,N/A,No identities or UI; punishment is a parameter P_N in the model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with identities; only conceptual benefits.,1,0,No interface or rounds; repeated interactions are mathematical constructs.,1,0,Punishment is discussed as a theoretical mechanism (P_N) but no concrete mechanism is implemented in an experiment or simulation.,0.7,N/A,No operational punishment technology in an implemented game; only symbolic costs.,1,N/A,Magnitude per punishment unit is not instantiated as a treatment parameter in a concrete design.,1,0,Rewards are only discussed conceptually as benefits; no explicit reward stage is modeled as a configurable mechanism.,0.8,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with a defined cost per unit.,1,N/A,"No implemented reward technology; only abstract benefits B_N, D_N.",1,N/A,There are no endowed tokens or money for participants; only fitness/payoff functions.,1,N/A,No observed contributions; all results are theoretical inequalities about selection gradients.,1,N/A,No empirical or simulated token-level contributions are reported.,1,N/R,"Although group productivity functions are analyzed, no explicit normalized efficiency statistic (actual/maximum payoff) is reported as a primary outcome for a specific game setting.",0.6,N/R,"Analytical expressions for productivity exist, but no single numeric group payoff is given as a design-level dependent variable.",0.6,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is theoretical/analytical, with no empirical data collection.",1,"The paper provides a general synthetic evolutionary model where selection on helping depends on marginal direct benefits D_N, marginal benefits to others B_N, responsiveness ρ_N, and scaled relatedness κ_N as functions of group size N. It illustrates cases of linear, concave, and sigmoidal public-good production, punishment via a cost P_N, and functions for responsiveness and relatedness that typically decline on the order of 1/N.",Summarizes the structure of the theoretical model since no concrete experimental game configuration exists.,0.9
10.1016_j.amc.2022.127308.md,Optimal rewarding strategy u_R*(t) with institutional reward,Paper analytically defines and evaluates the optimal rewarding strategy u_R*(t) as a distinct control protocol for institutional reward.,1,"Type of institutional incentive (reward only), with per capita incentive u(t) treated as a continuous control variable; optimal protocol u_R*(t) = [2(n−r)c/n] * x / [1 − (1−x)^n]",The only factor varied in this condition is the incentive type (reward) and the specific control protocol u_R*(t).,1,False,"Model is based on replicator dynamics in an infinitely large, well-mixed population; no human participants.",1,False,No controlled lab/field experiment; the work is theoretical and numerical.,1,False,No lab setting; purely mathematical and numerical analysis.,1,True,"Time evolution of x(t) under u_R*(t) is computed numerically (e.g., Fig. 1); this is simulation of the differential equations without human subjects.",1,True,Closed-form derivations using replicator equation and Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman optimal control (Appendix A).,1,5,Numerical examples for rewards use n = 5 (caption of Fig. 1).,1,0,Model is in continuous time (replicator dynamics from t=0 to t_f); no discrete rounds; use 0 as not applicable to repeated-round count.,0.8,1,Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount c and defectors contribute 0; there is no decision about partial contribution levels; treated as an all-or-nothing contribution structure.,0.9,N/A,No human players or explicit endowment allocation; only strategy fractions and payoffs are modeled.,1,0.6,"In standard PGG, MPCR = r/n. Numerical examples use r = 3 and n = 5, so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No communication between players; model is purely mathematical without agents who can chat.,1,N/A,No information interface for agents; continuous population model.,1,N/A,No individual identities; infinitely large well-mixed population and institutional reward only.,1,N/A,Rewards are institutional and anonymous in the population model; no identities.,1,N/A,No user interface or human awareness of horizon; only mathematical time interval.,1,0,This condition considers institutional reward in isolation; punishment is not applied.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,1,Incentives are used as rewards to cooperators (institutional reward scenario).,1,N/R,"Model defines total institutional incentive to a group as n*u and uses cost functional J = ∫[(n u)^2/2] dt, but does not map this to a per-token marginal cost in game tokens as required by the schema.",0.5,N/R,"Payoff formulas show each cooperator obtains a reward a n u /(n_C+1) with a=1, but the mapping to a ‘per coin spent’ technology parameter is not specified in the paper’s units.",0.5,N/R,"Each cooperator contributes fixed c>0, and numerical examples set c=1, but the initial endowment per player is not explicitly defined as a resource stock.",0.6,N/R,"Model tracks fraction of cooperators x, not normalized contribution per player over an explicit endowment.",0.7,N/R,No aggregate contribution amount is reported as an outcome; only x(t) and cumulative costs are discussed.,0.7,N/R,Paper does not report total or relative efficiency (group payoff relative to maximum) for this condition.,0.7,N/R,Group payoff is not summarized or reported as a main numerical outcome; focus is on cooperative fraction and cost J.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of analytical derivations and numerical simulations without human subjects.,1,"Infinitely large well-mixed population; public goods game with group size n=5, synergy factor r=3, contribution cost c=1 in numerical examples; institutional reward with leverage a=1; per capita incentive u(t) is dynamic and chosen optimally to minimize cumulative cost J = ∫[(n u)^2/2] dt such that x(t_f)=1−δ with δ=0.01 from initial x0=0.5.",Summary of the specified parameter values and structural assumptions used in the reward simulations (Fig. 1 and Section 3.1).,1
