utions.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency measure reported by treatment.,0,N/R,No per-treatment group payoff levels reported.,0,Germany,Same subject pool and location across all treatments.,1,"Median 24; university students, mostly non-economics.",Demographics reported for full sample; treatments are between-subjects but no indication of systematic differences.,1,"36% male, 64% female (pooled across treatments).",Same pool; no per-treatment breakdown.,0.9,"University students (3rd year on average), excluding economics and related fields.",Same restrictions applied to all sessions.,1,On site,Same lab setting.,1,"One-shot linear public goods game with groups of 4, strategy-method elicitation of unconditional and conditional contribution schedules as in Base. One randomly selected group member becomes CDM; others are UDMs. In CPun, CDM also fills a conditional punishment table specifying payoff reductions (0–10 points) for each possible contribution of another player. Punishment is costless (no impact on punisher’s payoff) and reduces the selected UDM’s payoff 1:1, up to 10 points. Only punishment by the CDM is implemented and applied to one randomly selected UDM.","Design description in Section 2, Table 1, and instructions for CPun.",1
