10.1038_srep10321.md,Symmetric MHD – pooled parts (no-penalty and penalty phases),"One of four between-subject game structures in Table 1: symmetric missing hero dilemma (MHD), aggregating its within-subject penalty manipulation as authors do for main punishment analyses.",0.95,Second-order game type: Missing Hero Dilemma (MHD); Punishment cost structure: symmetric (K_i = 55 MU for all punishers); Within-subject phase: pooled over P = 0 MU (rounds 1–15) and P = 60 MU (rounds 16–30).,"Game type (MHD vs VOD), symmetry (symmetric vs asymmetric), and penalty P (0 vs 60) are the designed treatment dimensions; here we describe the specific level for this condition, with parts pooled per Results section.",0.98,True,Human subjects in a computerized lab experiment (216 students).,1,True,Random assignment of subjects to conditions in controlled lab sessions.,1,True,Conducted in ETH Zurich Decision Science Laboratory (DeSciL).,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,"Paper contains a theoretical model but this row refers to the lab experiment, not the analytic results.",0.98,4,Groups of four in all rounds (“groups of four are disbanded and randomly formed anew”).,1,30,Experiment comprises 30 rounds; groups rematched each round.,1,1,Decision is binary: steal vs not steal; reclaim vs not reclaim; no continuous contribution choice.,0.9,0,No continuous contribution to a public account; endowment is individually held unless stolen; treat as 0.,0.7,N/R,Not a standard linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,"No communication mentioned; z-Tree implementation with individual, anonymous decisions.",0.95,1,After each round subjects “received full information feedback about the outcome of their interaction and learned how every group member had decided.”,1,1,Full feedback on how every group member decided implies identities within group are observable on screen.,0.9,0,"No rewarding mechanism in the design, only punishment via reclaim decisions.",1,1,"Standard z-Tree repeated-game interface; experiment split into two clearly labeled parts at round 16; subjects must be informed of phase change and total/remaining rounds, though exact wording not quoted.",0.7,1,Victims can ‘reclaim’ stolen endowment at a personal cost K_i; authors label this ‘punishment’.,1,55,"Symmetric MHD in Table 1: U_i = 50 MU, K_i = 55 MU.",1,50,"One punisher restores U_i = 50 MU to all three victims and, in part 2, additionally triggers a fixed penalty P on the thief irrespective of number of punishers. Interpreting ‘tech’ as MU of endowment restored to each punisher’s own stake; exact per-unit mapping not reported, so use U_i as effective impact size.",0.3,0,No positive rewards; only reclaim/punish options.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,"Not directly given, but thief can ‘steal half the endowment of the other three’ which equals U_i = 50 MU taken from each victim. Thus half-endowment = 50 MU ⇒ initial endowment per person = 100 MU.",0.95,N/R,"Outcomes reported are punishment and defection (stealing) rates, not normalized cooperative contributions.",1,N/R,No per-round average reclamation or non-stealing amounts in MU reported numerically.,1,N/R,Paper discusses efficiency qualitatively (efficient punishment when one person punishes) but does not provide numeric efficiency ratios.,1,N/R,Average earnings given across whole experiment (CHF 38) but not broken down by condition or mapped back to MU per group.,0.4,Switzerland,"Subjects were students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich; lab located in Zurich, Switzerland.",0.95,"Mean 23.1 years (sd = 5.57), university students",Reported in Experimental procedure section.,1,57.9% female,Reported in Experimental procedure section.,1,University students (University of Zurich and ETH Zurich),Subjects recruited from these universities’ student subject pool.,1,On site,Computerized lab sessions at DeSciL (physical lab).,1,"Step-level second-order public good; 4 players. In each round, one randomly chosen potential thief may steal half of each of the other three players’ endowment (50 MU from each). If at least one non-thief chooses to ‘reclaim’ (punish), everyone’s endowments are restored; in rounds 16–30, thief additionally pays penalty P = 60 MU. Punishers each pay cost K_i = 55 MU (symmetric across the 3 non-thieves). Groups rematched every round; each subject randomly assigned thief/non-thief role each round.",Summarized from design description and Table 1 for symmetric MHD.,1
