itive rewards.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Endowment structure identical across conditions (half-endowment stolen = 50 MU).,0.95,N/R,"Outcomes are stealing and punishment frequencies, not public good contributions.",1,N/R,No reported average MU punishment or cooperation per round by condition.,1,N/R,"Efficiency discussed qualitatively (e.g., more often exactly one punisher) but not quantified as payoff ratio.",1,N/R,Condition-specific payoffs not given.,0.4,Switzerland,Same Swiss university lab sample.,0.95,"Mean 23.1 years (sd = 5.57), university students",Single sample across all conditions.,1,57.9% female,Reported aggregate gender composition.,1,University students (University of Zurich and ETH Zurich),Recruitment from university subject pool.,1,On site,Lab experiment at DeSciL.,1,"Same basic 4-player structure but with heterogeneous punishment costs: strong member (type 2) pays K_2 = 25 MU to reclaim; two weak members (types 1 and 3) pay K_1,3 = 35 MU. Benefits of successful punishment U_i = 50 MU per victim; in rounds 16–30, thief additionally pays penalty P = 60 MU if at least one victim punishes. Groups are rematched each round and thief role is randomly assigned, so each subject sometimes plays as thief and sometimes as potential punisher.",Summarized from asymmetric VOD description in Table 1 and text.,1
