sons are given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency statistic is provided.,0.1,N/R,"Average period earnings by role are provided only under aggregate limited-information description (citizens $0.62, authorities $0.70) and not broken out by this specific treatment in tokens as group sums.",0.3,United States,Same NYU-based subject pool.,0.9,Undergraduate students; exact ages N/R,Recruited from undergraduate pool.,0.8,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.3,Primarily university undergraduates,Same recruitment mechanism described.,0.9,On site,Conducted in the NYU lab with networked computers.,1,"Same basic extensive-form structure as in appropriation/full information: 4 citizens decide binary contribute/keep from 20 tokens; authority observes contributions privately and may target one citizen at cost 2; baseline enforcement success probability 0.5, adjusted by ±1/6 per helper/hinderer; successful enforcement penalizes target 30 tokens and pays authority 30 tokens. In this limited information version, untargeted citizens do not see individual contributions or who was targeted; they only know that someone was targeted and later the total group contribution.",Summarizes rules while emphasizing appropriative incentives and limited transparency specific to this condition.,1
10.1111_jssr.12045.md,"Treatment 1 – Free Entry, Free Exit (main phase)","Paper’s Table 2 labels this as Treatment 1: Free Entry, Free Exit; main phase is the 20 repeated periods with endogenous groups after preliminary rounds.",1,Group formation rule: Free Entry and Free Exit (no voting required for either entry or exit).,Design section specifies three institutional treatments; this row corresponds to the treatment with free entry and free exit.,1,True,Human subjects recruited from economics courses at Florida State University.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments and pre-specified payoff function.,1,True,Sessions conducted in a computer lab using zTree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results from human subjects.,1,False,Paper is primarily experimental; payoff function is analytical but no separate analytical model study.,1,12,Each session has 12 subjects playing simultaneously.,1,20,Main phase consists of 20 periods with endogenous group formation; preliminary 3 single-player rounds are excluded by convention.,0.95,1,"Subjects choose how to divide 15 tokens; implies any integer amount 0–15 is feasible, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Tokens start under subject’s control and are allocated between private and group account; default is fully private.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear payoff with cubic cost; no constant marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat between subjects; only numerical histories shown.,0.95,1,"At beginning of each period, subjects see average contribution levels and group sizes for all groups over previous 5 periods and number of subjects per group.",1,0,No individual-to-individual punishment stage; only group membership choices and contribution histories are displayed.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond normal payoffs.,1,1,Finite repeated game with known length is stated; standard practice and no indication it was hidden.,0.7,0,"No costly punishment technology; only behaviorally-interpreted ‘punishment’ via group exclusion or low contributions, which is not an explicit punishment stage.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No costly reward technology beyond public good returns.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,15,Each subject divides 15 tokens between private and group accounts each period.,1,N/R,"Paper reports average tokens contributed (e.g., 4.42) but not normalized as 0–1 rates.",0.3,4.42,Result 4: average contribution in periods 6–20 for Free Entry/Exit is 4.42 tokens.,0.95,N/R,Earnings by treatment and group size are discussed but no explicit efficiency ratio reported or derivable at treatment level from provided numbers.,0.3,N/R,Figure 4 shows earnings per person by group size; no single aggregate group payoff number is reported for the treatment as a whole.,0.3,USA,Sessions conducted at Florida State University (U.S.).,1,N/R,Only states subjects were mostly from economics courses; no age data.,1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,1,Mostly university students in economics courses,Subjects recruited mostly from economics courses at various levels at Florida State University.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical computer lab with zTree.,1,"Variant of voluntary contribution mechanism with endogenous group formation; 12 labeled groups (A–L), group size N∈[1,12]; payoff: π_i = 0.5(15−x_i)+1.5(x_i+∑_{j∈G_i}x_j)−(1/27)x_i^3; dominant strategy contribution 3 tokens independent of N; group-optimal contribution increases with N; subjects can freely choose each period whether to switch groups and which group to join, with no voting or cost.",Summarizes stage game and institutional rules specific to free entry/exit treatment.,1
10.1111_jssr.12045.md,"Treatment 2 – Restricted Entry, Free Exit (main phase)","Paper’s Table 2 labels this as Treatment 2: Restricted Entry, Free Exit; this row captures that institution in the main repeated phase.",1,Group formation rule: Restricted Entry (majority approval needed to enter a group) and Free Exit (no approval needed to leave).,"Design section defines this treatment as restricted entry with free exit, implemented via majority voting in destination group.",1,True,Human subjects in laboratory sessions.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled payoff environment.,1,True,All sessions in computer lab with zTree.,1,False,No purely computational experiments reported.,1,False,No separate formal model analyzed apart from describing payoff function.,1,12,Each experimental session involves 12 subjects.,1,20,Main group-formation phase lasts 20 periods; preliminary 3 periods with N=1 are separate.,0.95,1,Subjects choose integer investment 0–15 across accounts; not restricted to contribute all or nothing.,1,0,Tokens start in subject control and must be actively allocated to group account to become public.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear return and cost; no constant MPCR given.,1,0,No mention of communication channels; only numeric histories and votes.,0.95,1,Subjects see for all 12 groups the average contribution levels and group sizes for previous 5 periods and number of subjects.,1,0,Voting is on anonymous applicants with shown contribution histories (not explicitly identified in payoff-relevant sense); no targeted punishment stage.,0.9,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,1,"Finite, known-horizon repeated game; no indication that horizon was hidden.",0.7,0,"Although authors interpret denial of entry as a form of punishment, there is no explicit punishment stage with cost-per-punish parameters.",0.9,N/A,No formal punishment technology defined.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology defined.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism beyond baseline payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,15,Same VCM stage game across all treatments: 15 tokens per period.,1,N/R,Only raw average contributions per period are reported (4.84 for this treatment).,0.3,4.84,Result 4 reports average contribution of 4.84 tokens in periods 6–20 for Restricted Entry treatment.,0.95,N/R,"Paper gives earnings by group size and treatment qualitatively and via figures, but not a single normalized efficiency measure.",0.3,N/R,"Average earnings by group size (e.g., 56 ECUs per person in groups of 7) are given, but no aggregate group payoff for treatment overall.",0.3,USA,Conducted at Florida State University in the U.S.,1,N/R,No age statistics reported.,1,N/R,No gender composition mentioned.,1,Mostly university students in economics courses,Subjects recruited mostly from economics courses at Florida State University.,0.9,On site,Sessions run in an on-site computer lab.,1,"Same VCM as other treatments with payoff π_i = 0.5(15−x_i)+1.5(x_i+∑_{j∈G_i}x_j)−(1/27)x_i^3; dominant strategy contribution is 3 tokens. Endogenous group formation with 12 possible labeled groups (A–L). Each period, subjects may request to switch groups; entry into a group with at least one non-moving member requires majority ‘yes’ votes from those members who chose not to move. Exit from one’s current group is always free (no vote). Voters see applicant’s contribution history over prior 5 periods and their own group’s recent contributions. Groups of intermediate size (around 5–7) frequently form and have relatively high contribution levels; subjects often sacrifice potential earnings by staying in smaller, more cooperative groups instead of forming grand coalitions.",Summarizes institutional features and key behavioural patterns for the Restricted Entry treatment.,1
10.1111_jssr.12045.md,"Treatment 3 – Free Entry, Restricted Exit (main phase)","Table 2 defines Treatment 3 as Free Entry, Restricted Exit; this entry refers to that treatment’s main 20-period phase.",1,Group formation rule: Free Entry (no vote to join a group) and Restricted Exit (majority approval needed to leave one’s current group).,Design describes this treatment with unrestricted entry but exit subject to majority vote of staying group members.,1,True,Involves human participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with explicit treatment conditions.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab using zTree.,1,False,No simulation-only results reported.,1,False,No stand-alone analytical model study.,1,12,Each session includes 12 subjects.,1,20,"After preliminary 3 periods with n=1, main endogenous group phase runs for 20 periods.",0.95,1,Investment into group account is an integer 0–15; not a binary decision.,1,0,"Tokens are initially under the subject’s control, who can then choose any number to invest in group account.",0.9,N/R,Nonlinear payoff; marginal per capita return not constant or reported.,1,0,No communication or messaging described between participants.,0.95,1,Subjects observe for all groups the 5-period average contributions and group sizes and previous period’s group sizes.,1,0,No explicit punishment stage; group change votes in this treatment apply only to exit and are not framed as punishment.,0.9,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite repetition with known number of periods; standard information in such experiments.,0.7,0,"No formal monetary punishment action; although low contributions can be interpreted as punishment, there is no dedicated punishment technology with parametric costs.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit costly reward stage or technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,15,All treatments use the same stage game with 15 tokens per period.,1,N/R,Paper gives average contributions (4.33) but not normalized by endowment.,0.3,4.33,Result 4 reports average contribution 4.33 tokens for Restricted Exit treatment in periods 6–20.,0.95,N/R,"While figures show earnings patterns and regression tables relate earnings to group size and contributions, no explicit efficiency ratio is given.",0.3,N/R,"Figure 4 shows earnings by group size, but aggregate group payoff for the treatment is not directly reported.",0.3,USA,All sessions run at Florida State University (USA).,1,N/R,No ages given.,1,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,1,Mostly university students in economics courses,Subjects mostly from economics courses; implies typical university student population.,0.9,On site,Computer-lab-based experiment with zTree.,1,"Same VCM payoff as other treatments: π_i = 0.5(15−x_i)+1.5(x_i+∑_{j∈G_i}x_j)−(1/27)x_i^3; dominant strategy 3 tokens, group-optimal contribution higher and increasing with group size. Group formation is fully endogenous with 12 potential labeled groups. Entry into any group is free: no vote required. However, exit from a group requires majority approval from members who indicated they wish to stay (answered ‘no’ to switching). Applicants’ last 5 contributions are shown to voters. High contributors in low-contributing groups are often blocked from leaving, and contributions in this treatment exhibit a statistically significant downward trend over time; restricted exit tends to ‘teach’ high contributors to lower their contributions.",Summarizes stage game and the specific free-entry/restricted-exit institutional rules and key behavioral outcome (decay in contributions).,1
10.1086_690066.md,Analytical network model of interethnic cooperation,"The paper presents a formal, game-theoretic model of repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with two ethnic groups and network-mediated information spread, with no empirical or simulation component.",1,"Network structure (reach/integration, density, presence of peripheral/controlling positions), information transmission rate r, punishment length T, probability p of interethnic match, discount factor δ, payoff parameters α and β, group sizes n and network topologies g^A and g^B",These parameters are varied conceptually and in propositions/lemmas to compare conditions under which full intra- and interethnic cooperation can be sustained.,1,False,"No human subjects, data collection, or observations are reported; the article is purely theoretical.",1,False,There is no controlled manipulation with human or field subjects; only theoretical analysis of a formal game.,1,False,No laboratory setting or human participants.,1,False,"Although networks are illustrated with figures, there is no numerical/agent-based simulation; results are analytical propositions and lemmas.",1,True,"The article develops and analyzes a formal, infinitely repeated game with networks, deriving lemmas and propositions analytically.",1,2,"Stage game is a pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma; interactions occur in dyads, even though there are 2n total players in two groups.",0.9,-1,The game is explicitly defined as an infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma.,1,1,"Action set in the Prisoner’s Dilemma is binary (C or D), but contributions are not framed as continuous vs. all-or-nothing public-good investments; by the given coding rule, players can choose either action, so not restricted to a single forced option.",0.7,N/A,No human experiment and no public-goods contribution endowment is modeled; only binary PD actions.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined; the model is a Prisoner’s Dilemma payoff matrix, not a linear public goods game.",1,N/A,No human subjects or experimental communication protocol; only abstract gossip transmission via fixed network links.,1,0,Information structure is modeled as local observation and gossip; there is no notion of experimental feedback screens or payoff summaries as in lab games.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology with identities revealed is implemented as in lab experiments; sanctions are endogenous PD actions within the model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon theoretical game; no user interface or disclosure of remaining rounds.,1,1,Peer punishment via future defection (D) against offenders is central to the model; finite punishment length T is specified.,1,N/R,"Punishment is implemented as action profiles in the PD (punisher gets α, punished gets −β); no separate per-unit cost parameter for an explicit punishment technology is given.",0.6,N/R,"Magnitude of punishment is embedded in α and β payoffs, not as a separate ‘per coin’ technology parameter.",0.6,0,"The enforcement mechanism relies on sanctions (punishment), not on positive rewards for cooperation.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,The model is stated directly in terms of payoffs α and −β in the PD; no initial endowment or token budget is defined.,1,N/R,Outcomes are characterized as conditions for full cooperation vs. defection; no quantitative contribution rates (0–1) are reported.,1,N/R,"There is no continuous contribution decision; only binary PD actions, and no empirical averages are reported.",1,N/R,"Theoretical results talk about ease of sustaining full cooperation and bounds on cooperative interethnic interactions, but no numeric efficiency ratio is reported.",1,N/R,The paper does not report numeric group payoff levels; it gives symbolic payoffs and comparative statics.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,"The study is a purely theoretical, analytical model without human or field data.",1,"Infinitely repeated two-group Prisoner’s Dilemma with random matching; two ethnic groups A and B (each of size n) interact with probability p across groups and 1−p within group. Information about defections spreads deterministically through a within-group communication network at rate r (degrees per round). Punishment is implemented via a finite-length T period phase in which informed coethnics play D against offenders according to a network in-group policing strategy. Key network concepts include k-neighborhoods, reach, and integrated networks; the model derives conditions under which full intra- and interethnic cooperation can be sustained and an upper bound p^max on the proportion of cooperative cross-group interactions.",Summarizes core structural aspects of the theoretical game central to the paper’s results.,1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2014.09.001.md,Generosity scoring with punishment stage,"One of the two main punishment-stage reputation mechanisms explicitly contrasted in the core simulations: reputation based on cooperative/non-cooperative actions in the PGG, with punishment stage following.",0.93,"Reputation mechanism: generosity scoring (reputation updated only from contribution vs defection in PGG stage); punishment stage present (punish defectors). Other parameters: N=500, m=5, c=1, r=0.9·n in Fig.1 and r=0.66·n in Fig.2, α=0.1, β=0.3 or 0.5, ε varied or fixed at 0.",Paper states they first investigate two reputation mechanisms in the same PGG with punishment and only differ in what information is used (generosity vs punishment scoring). Parameter values are given near Figs.1 and 2 and in Model section.,0.86,False,Study describes computer simulations of strategy dynamics in a population; no human participants.,1,False,"This is a computational model, not a controlled lab or field experiment with subjects.",1,False,No laboratory setting; purely simulated population.,1,True,"Authors repeatedly refer to ""computer simulations"" and detail an agent-based style generational update process.",1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations for these conditions; results obtained numerically.,0.96,500,"Population size N=500 stated in Model as default used ""in all our simulations"" unless otherwise noted.",0.9,5,Each individual plays on average m=5 interactions per generation; treat m as number of interaction rounds per individual.,0.8,1,"In PGG stage players can ""contribute or not"" by investing amount c; decision is binary contribute/defect, so not all-or-nothing between 0 and c? Here 'allOrNothing' defined as can contribute any amount vs only all-or-nothing; contributions are binary 0 or c so it's all-or-nothing => code 0, but schema: 1 if can contribute any amount; 0 if only all-or-nothing. They only choose 0 or c, so set 0.",0.78,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation; simulated agents simply choose to invest cost c or not.,1,N/R,"MPCR would be r/n; r specified as function of n (e.g., 0.9n or 0.66n) but n itself not fixed in main text; could vary, so a single numeric MPCR not reported.",0.4,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents with fixed strategies.,1,0,Model only specifies that everyone observes current interaction and knows reputations; no mention of aggregate payoff or punishment summaries between rounds.,0.6,1,Each interaction is public and everybody knows who punished defectors in their last interaction; effectively identities of punishers are observable.,0.9,N/A,"This condition uses punishment stage, not rewards.",1,0,Agents do not condition on generation or remaining interactions; no indication that information about total number of interactions is represented.,0.7,1,Second stage allows individuals to impose fines on each defector by paying cost α to inflict β.,1,0.1,Parameter α=0.1 given for Figs.1 and A-figures.,0.95,0.3,Parameter β=0.3 for Fig.1 stated; this is loss per punished defector.,0.9,0,"Stage 2 is punishment, not rewarding, in generosity vs punishment scoring comparison.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/R,"Agents pay cost c=1 to contribute, but no explicit total endowment per interaction/round is specified.",0.4,N/R,"They report ""cooperation frequency"" qualitatively and in figures, but no exact numeric values are provided in the text.",0.3,N/R,Only frequency of contribution is summarized; no raw total contributions reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit group efficiency ratios or normalized payoffs reported.,0.2,N/R,Payoffs determine evolutionary success but are not summarized as outcome statistics.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents.,1,No human,Explicitly a computer simulation; no empirical data collection environment.,1,"Public goods game with binary contribution c=1 per interaction, group size n (varied in analyses), multiplication factor r function of n (e.g., r=0.9n; r=0.66n), population size N=500, discrete generations with payoff-proportional reproduction and mutation rate μ=0.002; reputation state in {−1,0,+1} based solely on contribution behaviour; punishment decision binary with cost α per punished defector and fine β.",Synthesized from Model section and figure captions describing dynamics and parameterization.,0.9
