10.1016_j.chaos.2021.111395.md,Analytical feedback-evolving game model,The core model is a system of differential equations (replicator dynamics + modified logistic resource dynamics) analyzed theoretically; this is the primary 'condition' studied.,1,Monitoring probability p; punishment intensity β; violation rate α; maximum individual allocation b_m; natural growth rate parameter k; group size N; maximum resource capacity R_m; initial cooperator proportion x(0); initial resource level y(0).,"The model equations explicitly include p, β, α, b_m, k, N, R_m and the analysis discusses behavior for different initial conditions x(0), y(0).",1,False,No human or animal participants; purely theoretical and numerical analysis of equations.,1,False,Not a controlled lab or field experiment; it is a mathematical modeling study.,1,False,No laboratory setting or human subjects are involved.,1,False,"This row refers to the analytical ODE model, not the Monte Carlo simulations; behavior studied via fixed point and stability analysis.",1,True,"The paper develops and analyzes closed-form differential equations, proving theorems about fixed points and stability.",1,1000,Parameter N is the population size; numerical examples and stability diagrams use N=1000 and the model text references population of N individuals.,0.9,0,Continuous-time or discrete-time evolutionary dynamics without a fixed number of interaction rounds; use 0 to represent N/A for analytical continuous-time model.,0.8,1,Individuals take a continuous amount of resource b_l or b_v from a resource pool; not an all-or-nothing contribution decision.,0.9,N/A,There is no contribution decision to a public good account; agents just extract from a common resource according to rules or violate them.,1,N/R,No public goods linear payoff structure or MPCR is defined; only direct resource extraction.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No interface or information display; not a human experiment.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as a parameter pβ; no identities or visibility assumed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented in the model; only punishment.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or displayed horizon; continuous dynamics.,1,1,Defectors are penalized with expected punishment pβ; monitoring probability p and penalty strength β are central.,1,N/R,The model specifies a penalty pβ for defectors but does not model a separate cost for applying punishment to others.,0.6,N/R,Only the size of the fine to defectors is specified; there is no mapping between a 'coin spent' and impact.,0.6,0,Authors explicitly discuss punishment and inspection; rewards are mentioned only as possible future extensions and are not included.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,There is no individual endowment concept; individuals directly obtain resource amounts from the resource pool.,1,N/R,"Outcomes studied are proportions of cooperators x and resource level y, not contributions relative to an endowment.",1,N/R,No contribution decision variable; only extraction amounts b_l and b_v.,1,N/R,They discuss sustainable vs depleted resource states but do not compute efficiency as defined in the schema.,0.7,N/R,Payoffs P_C and P_D are defined per individual; no explicit group payoff aggregate is reported.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The analytical model contains no human-subject data or real-world observations.,1,"Coupled social-ecological system with two strategies (cooperate obeying allocation rule with payoff P_C = b_m y/R_m, defect taking (1+α)b_m y/R_m but suffering expected fine pβ). Resource dynamics follow a modified logistic growth with growth rate r(y) = -4k y^2 / R_m^2 + 4k y / R_m and harvesting term proportional to current resource and strategies.",Summarized from the formal model description in Sections 2–3.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2021.111395.md,Monte Carlo simulation – Scenario A (high violation rate),Fig. 6 panel A is explicitly described as one of three different scenarios in the Monte Carlo simulations; it uses α=0.7 and other specified parameters.,1,Violation rate α=0.7; monitoring probability p=0.5; punishment intensity β=0.2; b_m=0.5; k=2; N=1000; R_m=1000; initial conditions for x and y chosen to illustrate low initial resource level.,"Parameters are given in the caption of Fig. 6 for panel A; text mentions low initial resource level for the case converging to (1,0).",0.95,False,Monte Carlo simulations over artificial agents; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical simulation of the theoretical model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Section 5 explicitly states they conduct Monte Carlo numerical simulations with agents updating strategies probabilistically.,1,False,"This row refers to the stochastic agent-based Monte Carlo implementation, not the differential equation analysis.",1,1000,Caption of Fig. 6 panel A specifies N=1000.,1,N/R,They show evolution over time t but do not specify the total number of time steps used in the simulations.,0.4,1,Agents have a binary strategy (cooperator or defector) but resource extraction amount is determined by rule and is not an all-or-nothing contribution decision; closest mapping is flexible rather than all-or-nothing contribution.,0.7,0,"Agents do not have a pre-allocated contribution in a public account; they extract from a common resource; by convention, the 'endowment' is effectively in private hands before extraction, so 0.",0.6,N/R,No linear public good multiplier structure is defined in the Monte Carlo setup.,1,0,No communication or signaling between agents is described in the simulation rules.,0.9,0,Imitation uses pairwise payoff comparison (F_i and F_j) without mention of global summaries; no evidence that summaries are shown.,0.7,0,Punishment is implemented as a reduction in payoffs pβ; there is no notion of identity revelation.,0.9,0,No rewards are modeled.,1,0,Simulation description does not specify any information display; agents update based on payoffs only.,0.8,1,"Defectors' payoff includes a term '-p β', described explicitly in the Monte Carlo rules.",1,N/R,There is no explicit cost to punishers; only a fine on defectors.,0.7,N/R,No per-unit technology mapping from cost to fine; punishment is a fixed expected penalty pβ.,0.7,0,"Monte Carlo payoff rules include only punishment, no reward to cooperators beyond standard payoff.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,Agents receive payoffs from resource extraction; no fixed per-period token endowment is defined.,0.9,N/R,Outcome variables tracked are proportion of cooperators x and resource level y; no normalized contribution rate is defined.,1,N/R,Agents do not contribute to a common pool; they extract from it.,1,N/R,They compare sustainability vs depletion but do not calculate efficiency as total payoff over maximum possible.,0.7,N/R,Simulation results show x(t) and y(t); aggregate payoff is not reported numerically.,0.8,N/A,"Agents are simulated, not human participants.",1,N/A,"Agents are simulated, not human participants.",1,N/A,"Agents are simulated, not human participants.",1,N/A,"Agents are simulated, not human participants.",1,No human,This is a Monte Carlo simulation with artificial agents only.,1,"At each time t, each agent i chooses s_i=1 (cooperator) or s_i=0 (defector). Payoffs: F_i = (b_m y(t))/R_m if cooperator; F_i = (1+α)(b_m y(t))/R_m - pβ if defector. Resource update: y(t+1) = y(t) + [-4k y(t)^2/R_m^2 + 4k y(t)/R_m] y(t) (1 - y(t)/R_m) - Σ_i [ s_i y(t) b_m/R_m + (1-s_i) y(t) b_m(1+α)/R_m ]. Strategy update: each individual i randomly samples j; if F_i < F_j, i adopts j's strategy with probability q = 1/(1 + exp((F_i - F_j)/M)) with M=0.1.",Directly transcribed from Section 5 (Monte Carlo simulations) including the given equations and parameters.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2021.111395.md,Monte Carlo simulation – Scenario B (low violation rate),"Fig. 6 panel B is the second scenario in the Monte Carlo simulations, differing primarily in violation rate α=0.1.",1,Violation rate α=0.1; monitoring probability p=0.5; punishment intensity β=0.2; b_m=0.5; k=2; N=1000; R_m=1000; initial conditions chosen such that the system approaches a high-cooperation sustainable-resource fixed point.,"Caption of Fig. 6 panel B lists parameters; text notes that as α decreases, the proportion of cooperators at steady state increases.",0.95,False,Uses simulated agents only.,1,False,"This is a computational scenario, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab environment is involved.,1,True,Scenario B is one of the Monte Carlo simulation runs with specified parameters.,1,False,Refers to stochastic simulation rather than the closed-form analysis.,1,1000,Caption of Fig. 6 panel B specifies N=1000.,1,N/R,Simulation horizon (number of time steps) is not stated.,0.4,1,"Same underlying game structure as Scenario A: continuous/resource-based extraction, not discrete all-or-nothing contributions.",0.7,0,No initial allocation to a public account; resources are in the common pool; by the schema’s convention we treat as 0 for public fund.,0.6,N/R,No MPCR is defined in this resource extraction setting.,1,0,No communication mechanisms are part of the model.,0.9,0,"Strategy update depends only on payoffs from a sampled neighbor, not on global summaries.",0.7,0,No explicit representation of identities in punishment; only payoff reduction.,0.9,0,No reward is modeled.,1,0,No mention of agents seeing the total number of time steps.,0.8,1,Same payoff structure as Scenario A with penalty pβ to defectors.,1,N/R,No explicit punisher cost is introduced; only a fine to defectors.,0.7,N/R,"Punishment is parameterized as fixed pβ, not as a transform from a costly input.",0.7,0,No rewards are present in the payoff definitions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No per-agent endowment variable is defined; only resource pool y(t).,0.9,N/R,"Outcomes tracked are x(t) and y(t), not contribution rates.",1,N/R,"Agents extract from, not contribute to, the common pool.",1,N/R,The paper does not compute efficiency metrics as defined.,0.7,N/R,Group payoff is not reported; focus is on resource and cooperation trajectories.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Scenario B is a Monte Carlo simulation with artificial agents only.,1,"Same Monte Carlo rules as Scenario A: binary strategies s_i ∈ {0,1}, payoffs F_i = (b_m y(t))/R_m if cooperator, F_i = (1+α)(b_m y(t))/R_m - pβ if defector; resource dynamics and imitation dynamics identical, with α=0.1. This lower α makes defection less profitable, leading to higher cooperation at steady state.",Derived from Section 5 and the caption for Fig. 6 panel B.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2021.111395.md,Monte Carlo simulation – Scenario C (intermediate violation rate),"Fig. 6 panel C is the third explicitly defined simulation scenario, with α=0.3.",1,"Violation rate α=0.3; monitoring probability p=0.5; punishment intensity β=0.2; b_m=0.5; k=2; N=1000; R_m=1000; initial conditions chosen to approach the interior coexistence equilibrium (x*, y*).",Caption of Fig. 6 panel C provides parameter values; text relates this scenario to coexistence of cooperators and defectors.,0.95,False,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical exploration of the model.,1,False,"Purely computational, no lab.",1,True,One of the Monte Carlo simulation scenarios defined in Section 5 and Fig. 6.,1,False,Refers to stochastic agent-based simulation rather than analytical derivations.,1,1000,Caption of Fig. 6 panel C specifies N=1000.,1,N/R,Number of simulation time steps is not given.,0.4,1,Same resource-extraction game structure as the other Monte Carlo scenarios.,0.7,0,No default contribution to a public fund; resources are extracted from a common pool.,0.6,N/R,No marginal per capita return parameter is used.,1,0,The simulation rules contain no communication component.,0.9,0,Agents update by comparing to a randomly sampled neighbor; they do not observe aggregate summaries.,0.7,0,Identity of who is punished is not modeled; only payoff reduction for defectors.,0.9,0,No rewards implemented.,1,0,No display of time horizon is described.,0.8,1,Same penalty term pβ for defectors as in the other scenarios.,1,N/R,No explicit cost for administering punishment.,0.7,N/R,Punishment levels are not decomposed into cost and impact.,0.7,0,No reward term included in payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model defines only a shared resource pool y(t), not individual endowments.",0.9,N/R,No contribution decision; state variables are x and y.,1,N/R,Agents do not contribute to a public good.,1,N/R,Efficiency as defined is not computed.,0.7,N/R,They do not report aggregate payoffs; emphasis is on coexistence dynamics and resource level.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Scenario C is part of the Monte Carlo simulations over artificial agents.,1,"Same Monte Carlo rules as Scenarios A and B with α=0.3. This intermediate violation rate leads to an interior stable fixed point (x*, y*), representing coexistence of cooperators and defectors with sustainable resources, consistent with the analytical conditions 0 < x* < 1 and pβ < α b_m < 3 pβ.",Summarizes how this parameter regime relates to the analytically derived coexistence equilibrium.,1
10.1007_s10640-006-9046-7.md,Numerical simulations without resource dynamics – sanction level δ = 280,"Section 4 describes a large batch of numerical simulation runs with fixed parameters, varying initial strategy shares and spatial distributions, at sanction level δ = 280 without resource dynamics; this is a distinct simulation condition.",1,"Sanction level δ = 280; no resource dynamics; effort levels x_L = 100, x_H = 120; learning rule: imitate strategy with highest average payoff in neighborhood; initial strategy shares and spatial locations randomized over specified grid.","These parameters are fixed for this set of simulations (δ, no resource dynamics, x_L, x_H, imitation rule), while initial shares and spatial positions are varied randomly.",1,False,No human participants; model is an evolutionary CPR game studied via numerical simulations.,1,False,Not a controlled human experiment; it is a computational study of a formal model.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors explicitly refer to numerical simulations of the theoretical model.,1,False,"This condition refers specifically to the numerical simulations, not the analytical derivations.",1,100,"They state: ""consider a population of n = 100 agents"" for these simulations.",1,200,"For the randomized initial shares and locations, they note: ""For every z0, we perform 100 so-called runs of 200 time-steps""; convergence occurred within 200 steps.",0.95,1,Agents choose fixed continuous effort levels x_L and x_H; no indication of binary all-or-nothing contributions.,0.8,N/A,"Model is a CPR harvesting game with effort levels, not an endowment-contribution setup; and no humans.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or MPCR is defined; payoffs come from harvest function F(X).,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,Not a human interface; information structure is coded in neighborhood payoffs only.,1,N/A,"No identities; sanctioning is embedded in payoff functions between strategy types, not human players.",1,N/A,"No reward mechanism modeled, only punishment.",1,N/A,No user interface or display; simulated time steps only.,1,1,"Enforcers punish defectors at cost γ, defectors pay sanction δ per punishing enforcer.",1,300,In the stability simulations they specify γ = 300; this applies to these δ = 280 runs as well (same parameter block).,0.95,280,Sanction δ = 280 per enforcer detection; cost to defector per unit of punishment.,0.95,0,Model only includes punishment; no rewards are described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Agents choose effort levels; no discrete endowment is specified.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as frequencies of equilibrium types and time spent in configurations, not as contribution/effort rates per agent.",1,N/R,"They do not report average effort levels chosen; effort is fixed by type, not a measured DV.",1,N/R,Paper discusses profits qualitatively; no normalized efficiency metric is reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics are provided for this simulation condition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire study is theoretical and computational with artificial agents.,1,"Spatial evolutionary common-pool resource game on a circle with 100 agents; three fixed strategy types: cooperators (x_L = 100), defectors (x_H = 120), enforcers (x_L and punish); harvest function F(N, X) = N^{1/2} X^{1/2} with N0 = 10^6 but no resource dynamics in this condition (stock effectively fixed); agents imitate the strategy in their neighborhood with highest average payoff; initial shares of C, D, E drawn from grid in 0.05 steps (excluding CD and CE only), spatial locations randomized; 19,000 runs (190 share vectors × 100 spatial draws) each of 200 time steps; main outcome measures are frequencies of convergence to D, DE, CE, CDE equilibria and cycling/blinkers.",Summarizes the model and simulation setup for δ = 280 without resource dynamics as described in Section 4.,0.95
10.1007_s10640-006-9046-7.md,"Numerical simulations with resource dynamics – sanction level δ = 300, proportional efforts","Section 5 describes simulations ""with resource dynamics"" using proportional effort rules x_H = a_H N, x_L = a_L N and compares equilibrium frequencies to a no-dynamics case in Table II; this forms a distinct simulation condition.",1,"Resource dynamics enabled with logistic growth G(N) = ρ N (1 − N/K); effort proportional to stock: x_L = a_L N, x_H = a_H N; sanction level δ = 300; various high-effort coefficients a_H (implied x_H = 200, 300, 350, 400 at N = 10^6); other parameters fixed.","Resource dynamics, proportional effort scaling, δ, and high-effort parameter a_H (hence x_H) are the main varied/defining independent variables across these simulations.",0.95,False,These are computer simulations of a theoretical model without human subjects.,1,False,"Controlled computational experiments, but not empirical human experiments.",1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,"They explicitly ""run simulations"" with the resource dynamics model.",1,False,"This entry refers to numerical results, not general analytical derivations.",1,100,"Same population size n = 100 agents is used as in previous simulations; they say ""consider a population of n = 100 agents"" and reuse parameters.",0.95,N/R,"For these resource-dynamics runs underlying Table II they do not specify the number of time-steps; earlier work used 200, but this is not clearly restated here.",0.4,1,"Agents follow continuous effort rules x_L, x_H proportional to N; contributions are not binary.",0.8,N/A,Model is a harvesting effort game without endowments or accounts; and no humans.,1,N/R,"No MPCR is defined; payoffs derive from harvest F(X, N) and costs.",1,N/A,No human communication; artificial agents only.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents only observe local payoffs by model assumption.,1,N/A,No human identities; strategies are types with defined interactions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,Pure simulation; no displayed round counter to subjects.,1,1,"Enforcers punish defectors, who incur sanction δ per punishing enforcer; enforcers bear cost γ per punishment.",1,N/R,"In this section, sanctions δ = 300 are specified but γ is not explicitly restated; earlier γ = 300, but the text does not confirm reuse here, so treat as not reported for this condition.",0.4,300,"They set ""we fix a_L = 0.0001 and take δ = 300""; δ is the per-enforcer sanction borne by defectors.",0.95,0,Only punishment via sanctions is discussed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Agents choose effort as a function of stock N; no discrete endowment specified.,1,N/R,"Results are reported as frequencies of convergence to equilibrium types (D, DE, CDE) in Table II, not per-agent effort fractions.",1,N/R,"Effort levels are determined by type and resource stock, but no average realized values are tabulated as outcomes.",1,N/R,"They discuss that resource dynamics affects profits and equilibrium occurrence, but no explicit efficiency metric is reported.",1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not numerically summarized for these runs.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,These are computational simulations of a theoretical CPR model.,1,"Spatial evolutionary common-pool resource game on a circle with approximately 100 agents and three strategy types: cooperators (low effort x_L), defectors (high effort x_H), and enforcers (low effort plus punishment of neighboring defectors). Resource dynamics: logistic growth G(N) = ρ N (1 − N/K) with carrying capacity K = 2·10^6 and intrinsic growth rate ρ = 0.2; harvest function F(X, N) = X^β N^{1−β} (β between 0 and 1). Individual efforts are proportional to resource stock: x_L = a_L N with a_L = 0.0001, and x_H = a_H N with various a_H chosen so that at reference stock N = 10^6, x_H ∈ {200, 300, 350, 400}. Sanction level fixed at δ = 300. Agents imitate the most successful strategy in their neighborhood (two nearest neighbors) based on payoffs that include harvest returns, effort costs, and punishment or sanction costs. For each x_H level and with/without resource dynamics, many simulation runs are used to estimate the probability of convergence to D-, DE-, or CDE-equilibria; Table II reports these frequencies, showing that with resource dynamics CDE-equilibria occur more often than in the corresponding no-dynamics case at the same implied x_H.",Summarizes the dynamic CPR game with resource feedback and the design of the comparative simulations in Table II.,0.95
10.1177_1043463116634035.md,"Bern – No litter (clean setting, near trashcan)",Broken-windows treatment in Bern with no litter; experiments in Bern were run near a trashcan as baseline.,0.9,City=Bern; Disorder=no litter (clean); Distance to trashcan=near,"Condition defined by location, litter manipulation, and proximity to trashcan.",0.95,True,Natural field experiment with unaware passersby.,1,True,Design involves randomized or scheduled treatment conditions (litter vs no litter) and controlled procedure.,0.95,False,"Conducted at tram stop in public space, not in a lab.",1,False,No computational or agent-based simulation described.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as main method.,1,1,Each ‘trial’ centers on a single target individual as potential enforcer even though bystanders can also react.,0.6,1,Each target faces a single one-shot norm violation.,1,1,Not a contribution game; no continuous contribution decision defined.,0.5,N/A,No monetary endowment or contribution mechanism.,1,N/R,No public goods payoff structure with MPCR specified.,1,0,Participants are unaware of experiment; no structured communication channel set up.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ payoffs or sanctions is provided.,1,1,Any direct or subtle sanction is face-to-face and non-anonymous.,0.95,0,No rewarding mechanism implemented; only sanctions and picking up litter.,1,0,Bystanders are not informed about number of trials/rounds.,1,1,Direct verbal confrontation and subtle sanctions are negative sanctions (punishment).,1,N/R,Costs are in time/effort and not monetized.,1,N/R,No parametric mapping from punishment cost to reduction in violator payoff is defined.,1,0,No positive sanctions or rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No monetary endowment or tokens.,1,N/R,"Study records sanctioning/maintenance behaviors, not contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution decisions.,1,N/R,No payoff structure to compute efficiency.,1,N/R,No monetary group payoff defined.,1,Switzerland,"Experiment conducted at Bern tram stop; majority of passersby are local Swiss, and in-group defined as Swiss in Switzerland.",0.9,18–70 (estimated visually),Targets defined as first individual between ages 18–70 based on appearance.,1,Both male and female; approx. 54% male among targets overall,Table 1 reports 54.10% male for all targets; both genders present.,0.9,N/R,No information on participants’ education.,1,Field experiment,Described as a natural field experiment in public transit settings.,1,"Naturalistic norm-enforcement situation: experimenter drops a plastic bottle near a trashcan in front of a target; observers record whether any bystander directly sanctions, subtly sanctions, or picks up the bottle. 78 no-litter and 81 litter trials in Bern overall; this condition refers to the 78 no-litter trials.","Summarizes procedural description under ‘Subjects, places, and procedures’.",0.95
