 punishment condition.,1,200,Observers (person C) receive 200 coins to potentially assign as sanctions.,1,N/R,No measure of A/B’s contribution behavior as a dependent variable; only sanction choices are DVs.,1,8.8,Mean number of punishment coins assigned in public good condition is 8.80 (Table 2); treat as sanction amount.,1,N/R,No aggregate efficiency metric reported or derivable from provided statistics.,0.2,N/R,No raw group payoff numbers reported.,0.1,Netherlands,Participants are students at Leiden University in the Netherlands.,1,"M=19.67, SD=3.48",Reported for Experiment 1 sample.,1,"92 women, 30 men (before exclusion)",Reported for Experiment 1 sample; breakdown not by condition.,0.9,University students,Participants described as students at Leiden University.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory with cubicles.,1,Third-party sanction game. Persons A and B play a one-shot public good dilemma with 100-coin endowments; contributions to a common pool are multiplied by 1.5 and equally shared. They choose 80 and 20 coins respectively (high vs low cooperators). Observer C has 200 coins to assign as decrement coins to one target; each assigned coin costs 1 and reduces target’s payoff by 3. Lottery selects some participants to be paid based on task outcomes.,Summarizes the task specifics for this condition.,1
