10.1016_j.obhdp.2014.09.005.md,Experiment 2 – Public Good,Experiment 2 has a one-factor design on Social Dilemma Type; this row is the Public Good condition where sanction type is chosen endogenously.,1,Social dilemma type = Public Good; Sanction type = participant’s choice (reward vs punishment vs none),"Only Social Dilemma Type manipulated between participants; within this condition, individuals choose whether to reward or punish or abstain.",1,True,Human university students participated.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to public good vs common resource framing.,1,True,Procedure described as an experiment in the laboratory.,1,False,No non-human simulations are used.,1,False,No stand-alone analytical model.,1,3,"Same third-party setup: A, B, and observer C.",1,1,Design is again one-shot.,1,1,Observers choosing to sanction can assign an amount between 0 and 200 coins of the chosen type.,1,0,"In the public good framing, A and B start with private endowments and may contribute to a pool.",0.8,0.75,Same public good payoff structure as Experiment 1 (×1.5 then /2).,1,0,No communication or chat channels are reported.,0.9,0,Observers see individual decisions (80 vs 20 contributions) but not extensive payoff summaries.,0.7,0,Targets labeled A and B only; identities remain unknown if punished.,1,0,Same anonymity if rewarded; only role labels are visible.,1,0,Single-round interaction; no display of round number is mentioned.,0.9,1,Participants can choose to assign decrement coins as punishment.,1,1,Each decrement coin costs the observer 1 coin from their 200-coin endowment.,1,3,Each punishment coin reduces target’s payoff by 3.,1,1,Participants can alternatively choose increment coins as rewards.,1,1,Each reward coin is subtracted from observer’s endowment.,1,3,Each reward coin adds 3 to the target’s outcome.,1,200,Observers get 200 coins to allocate as either reward or punishment if they choose to sanction.,1,N/R,No normalized 0–1 sanction or contribution variable reported.,1,45.53,"Among those who chose to reward in the public good condition, mean rewards are 45.53 coins (Table 4). Mean punishments among punishers are 13.30. Overall coded mean (rewards positive, punishments negative, no sanction zero) is 32.59, but a single raw sanction amount is not uniquely reported; we record the larger of the two reported sanction magnitudes used for cooperation promotion (reward).",0.5,N/R,No efficiency index across the group is provided.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff measures were not reported.,0.1,Netherlands,"Experiment 2 also used students at Leiden University, The Netherlands.",1,"M=18.99, SD=1.73",Reported for Experiment 2 sample.,1,"94 women, 20 men",Given for the Experiment 2 sample as a whole.,1,University students,Experiment 2 participants described as students at Leiden University.,1,On site,Same lab setup as Experiment 1.,1,"Same third-party public good task as Experiment 1: A and B each start with 100 coins, contributing 80 and 20 to a common pool (multiplied by 1.5 and shared). Observer C has 200 coins and first chooses whether to sanction and whether to use decrement (punishment) or increment (reward) coins, then if sanctioning, chooses a target (typically the high cooperator when rewarding, low cooperator when punishing) and a number of coins (0–200). In this public good condition, 53 of 56 participants sanctioned; 10 punished and 43 rewarded (Table 3).",Summarizes the combined reward/punishment choice structure under the public good framing.,1
10.1016_j.obhdp.2014.09.005.md,Experiment 2 – Common Resource,Common Resource condition of Experiment 2 in which sanction type is chosen endogenously.,1,Social dilemma type = Common Resource; Sanction type = participant’s choice (reward vs punishment vs none),Only Social Dilemma Type varied experimentally; sanction type is a choice variable for participants.,1,True,Involves human participants from Leiden University.,1,True,Random assignment to social dilemma framing.,1,True,Laboratory-based computer task.,1,False,No simulation model mentioned.,1,False,"Study is empirical, not purely analytical.",1,3,"Triad of A, B, and C as observers and actors.",1,1,Described as a one-shot social dilemma.,1,1,Sanctioners can select any number from 0 to 200 coins after choosing sanction type.,1,1,"In common resource framing, 200 coins are in the common pool and can be harvested by A and B; none starts as private endowment.",0.8,0.75,Same common resource payoff structure as Experiment 1: remaining pool ×1.5 shared by 2.,1,0,No chat or communication channels.,0.9,0,Observers see only A and B’s harvest amounts (20 vs 80).,0.7,0,Punishment targets are labeled A or B without identities.,1,0,Same anonymity for reward targets.,1,0,Single trial; no mention of displaying number of rounds.,0.9,1,Participants can choose decrement coins.,1,1,One decrement coin costs observer 1 coin.,1,3,Each punishment coin reduces target’s payoff by 3.,1,1,Reward option via increment coins is available.,1,1,Each reward coin subtracts 1 coin from observer.,1,3,Each reward coin adds 3 to receiver’s payoff.,1,200,Observer C has 200 coins to allocate as sanctions.,1,N/R,No rate-type dependent variable is provided.,1,31.7,"Among participants who rewarded in the common resource condition, mean reward is 31.70 coins (Table 4). Mean punishment among punishers is 17.50. Overall coded mean sanction (positive for reward, negative for punishment, zero for none) is 17.64, but a single raw sanction measure for all is not clearly provided; we record the mean reward magnitude as the main positive cooperation-promoting action.",0.5,N/R,No efficiency data reported.,0.2,N/R,No group payoff data reported.,0.1,Netherlands,"Experiment 2 is also run at Leiden University, NL.",1,"M=18.99, SD=1.73",Stats given for Experiment 2 sample.,1,"94 women, 20 men",Reported at the experiment level.,1,University students,Participants were students at Leiden University.,1,On site,Same lab context as other conditions.,1,"Common resource version of Experiment 2: A and B can each harvest up to 100 coins from a 200-coin pool; in the fixed feedback, A harvests 20 and B 80. Remaining coins are multiplied by 1.5 and shared. Observer C (200 coins) chooses whether to sanction, then to use decrement (punishment) or increment (reward) coins, then selects a target and number of coins (0–200). In this condition, 54 of 58 participants sanctioned; 14 punished and 40 rewarded (Table 3).",Summarizes the endogenous sanction choice for the common resource framing.,1
