10.1371_journal.pcbi.1004232.md,Simple Meta-Incentive Game (S-MIG) – Generic configuration,"Paper presents a family of theoretical game configurations (24 MIG types and 3 IG types) analyzed analytically via replicator dynamics, rather than a single empirical experiment. Use a generic label for the core S-MIG model.",0.8,"Incentive configuration (24 MIG types P, R, P+R, PP, PR, PB, RP, RR, RB, P+RP, P+RR, P+RB, PP+R, PR+R, PB+R, PP+RP, PP+RR, PP+RB, PR+RP, PR+RR, PR+RB, PB+RP, PB+RR, PB+RB) and incentive game (3 IG types P, R, P+R), parameterized by cost-effect ratio μ>1 and discount factor δ with 0<δ<1.",Independent variables are the presence/absence and type of first- and second-order rewards/punishments (Table 1) plus continuous parameters μ and δ (definitions in Results/Methods).,1,False,"Model is an evolutionary game with infinitely large, well-mixed population and replicator dynamics; no human or animal participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment or data collection; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; purely theoretical.,1,False,Authors state they ‘solve the replicator dynamics’ analytically and provide phase portraits and conditions; no numerical/agent-based simulation procedures are described.,0.8,True,"Paper derives and analyzes replicator equations, equilibria, and stability conditions analytically (e.g., explicit f(x), g(x), eigenvalues).",1,4,"Fig 1: ‘Four individuals are randomly drawn from the population and randomly assigned to one of four roles, recipient, donor, first-order player, and second-order player.’",1,1,"Each interaction consists of a single sequence of three stages (DG, FIG, SIG). Evolution occurs via continuous-time replicator dynamics across many such one-shot interactions; per-game round count is 1.",0.8,0,"Donation game framed as donor deciding ‘whether to help the recipient’ (binary cooperate/defect), but incentive decisions are also binary; contribution is not a continuous amount choice in a PGG sense. Set to 0 to reflect non-continuous contribution choice.",0.6,N/A,No endowment allocation or default contribution state defined for human players; purely analytical model.,1,N/R,"This is a donation game, not a linear public goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR is defined.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocols; abstract evolutionary model.,1,N/R,"Information structure is ‘perfect information’ about actions, but no notion of payoff summaries being shown to players as in an experiment.",0.6,N/R,"Players are roles in an anonymous, infinite population model; identity revelation is not modeled.",0.7,N/R,"As above, identity of rewarders is not a modeled variable; only strategy frequencies matter.",0.7,N/R,"No user interface; continuous-time evolutionary process, not finite known horizon for agents.",1,1,"The meta-incentive game (MIG) generally allows for first- and second-order punishments (negative incentives) in many of its 24 types (e.g., P, PP, PB, RB+PB etc.), and the main theory analyzes punishment configurations explicitly.",1,N/R,"Underlying parameters P1, PP, PR, etc. are defined, and δ = P1/c etc., but no single numeric value is fixed; analysis is in terms of symbols μ and δ.",1,N/R,"Effectiveness of punishment is embedded in fine parameters F1, FP, FR etc. and summarized via μ, but no single numeric punishment-per-cost factor beyond the symbolic μ is instantiated.",1,1,"Positive incentives (rewards A1, AP, AR, etc.) are central to many MIG types and to the main conclusions (e.g., necessity of second-order incentives for first-order rewards).",1,N/R,"Reward costs R1, RP, RR are defined symbolically and linked via δ, but no fixed numeric value is chosen.",1,N/R,"Reward magnitude per unit cost is captured symbolically via μ (e.g., A1/R1 = μ), but no numerical instantiation is given.",1,N/R,Donation game modeled via benefit b and cost c; there is no explicit per-player endowment in tokens/points.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are described in terms of equilibrium cooperation (x>0) and stability of cooperative regimes, not in normalized contribution proportions.",1,N/R,Model uses symbolic benefit b and cost c; no numeric contribution amounts or averages are reported.,1,N/R,"Paper discusses efficiency qualitatively (e.g., costs of punishment funds vs. ex-post incentives) but does not compute normalized efficiency values.",0.7,N/R,"Expected payoffs U_CI, U_CN, U_NN are given symbolically; no numeric group payoff statistics are reported.",1,N/A,No human participants; infinite well-mixed theoretical population.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Study is purely analytical evolutionary game theory with no experimental or field data.,1,"Meta-incentive game (MIG) consists of three sequential stages with four roles: a donor may help a recipient in a donation game with cost c and benefit b; a first-order player may apply incentives (rewards A1 at cost R1 or punishments F1 at cost P1) to the donor; a second-order player may apply incentives (A_P, R_P, F_P, P_P, A_R, R_R, F_R, P_R) to the first-order player for (not) incentivizing. Strategies are CI (cooperative incentive-provider), CN (cooperative incentive-non-provider), and NN (non-cooperative incentive-non-provider). Population is infinitely large, well-mixed, and evolves via replicator dynamics parameterized by μ (cost-effect ratio of incentives) and δ (discount factor on incentive costs). A simplified incentive game (IG) with C-FO linkage is also analyzed by removing second-order incentives.","Summarizes formal description of stages, roles, strategies, parameters, and dynamics as specified in Results and Methods.",1
