10.1103_PhysRevE.80.066101.md,BA scale-free network – varying selfishness degree,Simulation on Barabási-Albert (BA) scale-free network with 10^4 vertices and 4×10^4 edges; selfishness degree p̄ varied to observe phase transition.,1,"Selfishness degree of population (p̄), varied over [0,1] on a fixed BA scale-free network (n=10000, E=40000, γ=3)",They systematically vary p̄ and measure fraction of pure punishers and ρ on a fixed BA scale-free topology.,1,False,Study uses numerical simulations of evolutionary dynamics on networks; no human participants.,1,False,This is not a controlled human or field experiment but a computational simulation.,1,False,No lab setting; purely computational.,1,True,They explicitly describe numerical simulations of the model on BA networks.,1,False,"Paper includes analytical derivations, but this specific condition refers to the numerical BA simulation.",0.7,10000,BA network generated with 10^4 vertices.,1,0,"Continuous-time dynamics discretized with Δ=10^-2 until Lyapunov function ρ stabilizes; not described as discrete rounds of a finite repeated game, so use 0.",0.6,1,Players do not contribute discrete amounts; their choice is a probability r_i of accepting; not an all-or-nothing contribution structure.,0.8,N/A,"Ultimatum game on networks, not a public goods game with initial endowments.",1,N/R,"Ultimatum bargaining, not a public goods game with a linear multiplier.",1,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,0,Agents update based on local payoff ratios via equations; no explicit summary display concept.,0.8,N/A,Punishment is implicit via rejection; no identity revelation modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,No human subjects or user interface; number of time steps is not shown to agents.,1,1,Rejection in the ultimatum game is modeled as costly punishment.,1,N/R,Model captures rejection as costly punishment but does not parameterize per-unit punishment cost.,0.3,N/R,No explicit punishment technology scalar; payoffs follow the ultimatum payoff matrix.,0.3,0,"No rewarding mechanism, only acceptance/rejection.",1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,1,Sum of resources to split in every proposal is assumed to be 1.,1,N/R,"Outcome measures are fraction of pure punishers, Lyapunov function ρ, and payoff statistics; no contribution rate defined.",1,N/R,Ultimatum offers are fixed at 1−p̄; they do not report contributions as a DV.,1,N/R,They do not report total payoff relative to maximum in normalized efficiency terms.,0.7,N/R,"They report average payoffs by type (punishers vs others) qualitatively, but not a single group payoff statistic.",0.6,N/A,No human participants; agents on a network.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational network simulations; no empirical environment.,1,"Symmetric ultimatum game on a BA scale-free network (n=10000, E=40000, degree distribution P(k)~k^-3). Each player i has accepting probability r_i ∈ [0,1], initially random in (0,1]. Fixed claim p̄ for all players; each proposes to keep p̄ and offer 1−p̄ to neighbors. Payoff matrix M=[[1, 1−p̄],[p̄, 0]]. Accumulative payoff u_i = Σ_{j∈N_i} s_i^T M s_j. Payoff ratio ũ_i = (1/k_i) Σ_{j∈N_i} s_i^T M s_j. Strategy updating via consensus-like dynamics discretized with Δ=10^-2 until Lyapunov function ρ = 0.5 ũ^T L̃ ũ stabilizes. Independent variable p̄ acts as a temperature-like parameter; fraction of pure punishers (r_i=0) and ρ exhibit a phase transition at p_c ≈ 0.61, close to analytical prediction 1/λ̃_n^BA ≈ 0.608 with λ̃_n^BA ≈ 1.645.",Summarizes rules and network parameters for the BA simulation from the main text and figure description.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.80.066101.md,WS small-world network – varying selfishness degree,"Simulation on Watts-Strogatz (WS) small-world networks with same size as BA case, rewiring probability 0.25; selfishness degree p̄ varied.",1,"Selfishness degree of population (p̄), varied over [0,1] on a fixed WS small-world network (n=10000, same edge count as BA, rewiring probability 0.25)",They repeat the evolutionary ultimatum dynamics while varying p̄ on WS networks with specified rewiring probability.,0.9,False,Numerical simulations on WS networks; no humans.,1,False,"Computational model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Explicitly described as simulation on WS small-world networks.,1,False,"This condition refers to numerical WS results, not the analytical derivation.",0.7,10000,WS networks of the same size as the BA case: 10^4 vertices.,0.9,0,Continuous-time dynamics discretized until ρ stabilizes; not a finite number of game rounds.,0.6,1,Players choose acceptance probabilities r_i; no discrete all-or-nothing contribution restriction.,0.8,N/A,"Ultimatum game, not a contribution game with initial endowment allocation.",1,N/R,No marginal per capita return defined in ultimatum bargaining.,1,N/A,No humans; no communication modeled.,1,0,"Agent updates depend on neighbors’ payoff ratios via equations, not via displayed summaries.",0.8,N/A,Punishment is rejection; identity revelation is not modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No human interface; rounds not displayed.,1,1,Costly punishment is implemented as rejection in the ultimatum game.,1,N/R,No scalar cost parameter for punishment is separately specified.,0.3,N/R,"Punishment impact determined implicitly by payoff matrix, not as a separate technology parameter.",0.3,0,No rewarding mechanism defined.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,1,Sum of resources per proposal is 1; same game parametrization as BA case.,1,N/R,They only report fraction of pure punishers and ρ as functions of p̄.,1,N/R,No contribution variable is tracked as a DV.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed; only qualitative payoffs and order parameters reported.,0.7,N/R,No aggregate group payoff is reported for WS simulations.,0.6,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Simulation study on WS networks.,1,"Symmetric ultimatum game on a WS small-world network with n=10000 vertices and same edge count as the BA network; rewiring probability 0.25. Same payoff matrix and updating rule as BA case: agents start with random r_i∈(0,1], interact with neighbors to get payoffs, compute payoff ratios ũ_i, and update r_i via discretized consensus dynamics with Δ=10^-2 until Lyapunov function ρ stabilizes. Independent variable p̄ is varied; fraction of pure punishers and ρ exhibit a phase transition at p_c ≈ 0.63, close to 1/λ̃_n^WS ≈ 0.628.",Aggregates WS-specific network parameters and outcome description from figures and text.,1
