/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computational generation of Holme-Kim networks and eigenvalue calculations.,1,"Holme-Kim scale-free networks with n=2000, scale-free exponent γ=3, and average degree ⟨k⟩=10 are generated for various triad formation probabilities (TFP). For each TFP, the clustering coefficient and average path length are measured, and the largest normalized Laplacian eigenvalue λ̃_n is computed to obtain selfishness tolerance 1/λ̃_n. Results show that selfishness tolerance increases with TFP, positively correlated with clustering coefficient and average path length.",Summarizes Fig. 5 description.,1
10.1007_s11427-010-4016-2.md,Asymmetric Hawk-Dove game analytical model,Paper develops a single analytical Hawk-Dove game with asymmetric payoffs parameterized by k (or h) and m; no distinct experimental conditions with humans are reported.,1,Degree of asymmetry h = k/(1-k); benefit-to-cost ratio of conflict m = v/c,Model explicitly studies how equilibrium cooperation frequency x0 and cost-to-benefit ratio r' vary as functions of h and m.,1,False,No human or animal subjects; paper presents a theoretical game-theoretic model.,1,False,No controlled experiment or data collection is described; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,Results are derived analytically; no numerical/agent-based simulations are reported.,1,True,"Derives closed-form expressions for payoffs, equilibrium frequencies x0, and r' using algebra and game theory.",1,2,Hawk-Dove game is defined between two players A and B throughout the model.,1,1,Static 2x2 game with mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium; no repetition structure specified.,0.9,1,"Players choose between two pure strategies H or D; contribution is binary rather than a continuous amount, which corresponds to an all-or-nothing choice.",0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment or contribution process; theoretical Hawk-Dove game payoffs are defined directly.,1,N/R,Model is not a standard public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,Players have full strategic payoff information but no notion of displayed summaries as in experiments.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action choice or identity revelation; punishment is implicit via payoff structure.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism analogous to experimental designs.,1,0,One-shot analytical game; no rounds shown.,1,0,"While some outcomes are labeled 'punishment' in classical Hawk-Dove payoff interpretation, there is no strategic punishment decision; payoffs arise automatically from H/D choices.",0.8,N/A,No separate punishment action with an associated cost parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology is modeled.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism where players can pay to reward others.,1,N/A,No explicit reward action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,Model payoffs are in terms of v and c; no initial token or monetary endowment is specified.,1,N/R,"Outcome variable is cooperation frequency x0 in equilibrium as a function of parameters, not measured contributions by subjects.",1,N/R,Binary action choice H/D with no numeric contribution amount.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute payoff efficiency ratios relative to a maximum; focus is on cooperation frequency and cost-benefit ratios.,1,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff for the pair is reported as a focal dependent variable.,1,N/A,No human or animal participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical game-theoretic model with no empirical implementation.,1,"Two-player asymmetric Hawk-Dove game with resource value v, conflict cost c (assumed c > v), asymmetry parameter k (0 < k < 1) giving strength ratio h = k/(1-k); payoffs RA = kv, RB = (1-k)v when both cooperate, PA = (v-c)/(4k), PB = (v-c)/(4(1-k)) when both defect, and classical Hawk-Dove payoffs when actions differ. Mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium cooperation frequency x0 = ((1-m)(1+h)^2) / ((1+h)^2 - m(h-1)^2) where m = v/c.","Summarizes the core formal structure described in Section 1 and the Results, including payoff definitions and equilibrium expression.",1
10.1177_1474704917742720.md,Study 1 – Defense condition,Study 1 presents a within-subject manipulation where each participant responds once to a defensive scenario and once to an offensive scenario; this row captures the defensive scenario condition.,1,"Conflict domain = Defense (vs. Offense); scenario framed as repelling invading raiders to protect ancestral territory (public-good, defensive war).",The only experimental manipulation in Study 1 is whether the vignette describes repelling invaders (defense) or seizing a city (offense). Here the vignette is the defensive one.,1,True,Human undergraduates completed survey-experiment vignettes and Likert-scale responses.,1,True,The paper explicitly calls it an experiment-based survey with randomized (counterbalanced) order of conditions.,1,True,"Data were collected from university undergraduates in 2007; no indication of online administration, so this is best categorized as an on-site/lab-style survey experiment.",0.8,False,No computer or agent-based simulations were used in Study 1.,1,False,"The study uses statistical analysis of survey responses, not formal analytical modeling.",1,1,Each respondent makes an individual decision in response to a hypothetical scenario; there is no interactive game with multiple contemporaneous players.,1,1,Each scenario is answered once per participant; there is no repeated interaction or multiple rounds.,1,1,"Willingness to participate is reported on a 7‑point Likert scale, implying a continuous/graded decision rather than an all-or-nothing binary contribution.",0.9,0,There is no explicit endowment or default allocation between public and private accounts in a contribution game sense; set to 0 by convention as all resources are initially private in the narrative.,0.3,N/R,No public-goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; this is not a numerical public-goods game.,1,0,Respondents cannot communicate with others during the survey; no communication treatment is described.,1,0,Participants do not observe other participants’ choices or payoffs; only hypothetical coalition behavior is described in the vignette.,1,0,Punishment is only asked as an attitude (“should be punished”); no actual identity-revealing punishment mechanism is implemented.,1,0,Reward is similarly only an attitudinal measure; there is no implemented reward mechanism with identifiable targets.,1,0,No repeated rounds; no information about number of rounds is presented to subjects.,1,0,"Although punitive sentiment is measured via survey items, no actual costly punishment option is available in an incentivized game sense.",1,N/A,No real punishment mechanism exists; only self-reported attitudes.,1,N/A,"No implemented punishment technology (e.g., point reduction per punishment unit).",1,0,Reward sentiment is elicited but there is no real rewarding option or transfer.,1,N/A,No implemented reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No implemented reward technology.,1,N/R,"No numeric endowment (tokens, points, money) is provided; the scenario is verbal and hypothetical.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution relative to a defined endowment is reported; only Likert measures of willingness and sentiments.,1,N/R,No token/point contributions are elicited.,1,N/R,No payoff structure or maximum efficiency benchmark is defined.,1,N/R,Group outcomes are not monetized or quantified.,1,USA,"Subjects were undergraduates at the University of California, Santa Barbara, a U.S. institution; while nationality is not directly reported, the country of data collection is the USA.",0.8,"18–39, mostly traditional undergraduates",Study 1 reports: “mostly political science majors between the ages of 18 and 39.”,1,"83 female, 112 male (total N = 195)",Study 1: “195 undergraduate students (83 female)”; the remainder are male.,1,Undergraduate university students (mostly political science majors),"Explicitly described as undergraduates, mostly political science majors at UCSB.",1,On site,"Data described as a 2007 experiment-based s