10.1177_1474704917742720.md,Study 1 – Offense condition,"Study 1 also includes an offensive scenario; the same participants respond separately to an offense vignette, constituting a distinct condition.",1,"Conflict domain = Offense (vs. Defense); scenario framed as initiating an unprovoked raid to seize a neighboring city (privatized, offensive war).","Offense vignette describes forming a war party to seize a neighboring city, explicitly as initiating aggression without prior attack.",1,True,Same human-subject survey experiment as the defense condition.,1,True,Within-subject experimental manipulation of offense/defense domains.,1,True,Same UCSB on-site survey setting as defense condition.,0.8,False,No simulations are used.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,1,Individual respondents answer the offensive scenario independently; no interactive multi-player structure.,1,1,Each participant responds once to this vignette; no repeated decisions.,1,1,"Willingness and other responses are on 7-point Likert scales, not discrete all-or-nothing choices.",0.9,0,"No explicit game endowment; as in defense, by convention we treat initial resources as private.",0.3,N/R,No numeric public-goods structure is provided.,1,0,No communication among participants during the survey.,1,0,Participants do not receive feedback about others’ responses.,1,0,No real punishment mechanism with identities; only attitudes toward hypothetical nonparticipants.,1,0,"Similarly, reward is attitudinal only.",1,0,Single-shot vignette without round information.,1,0,Punitive sentiment questions are not implemented as an actual costly choice.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment option.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology.,1,0,No real rewarding mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No implemented reward.,1,N/A,No formal reward technology.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment is specified.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution is defined.,1,N/R,There is no contribution amount variable.,1,N/R,No explicit payoff-maximization or efficiency concept is operationalized.,1,N/R,No group payoff measure.,1,USA,Same UCSB undergraduate sample as defense condition.,0.8,"18–39, mostly traditional undergraduates",Same Study 1 sample description applies.,1,"83 female, 112 male (total N = 195)",All Study 1 participants answered both offense and defense scenarios.,1,Undergraduate university students (mostly political science majors),Same as defense condition.,1,On site,Same on-campus survey administration as defense condition.,0.8,"Survey-experiment with hypothetical nomadic warfare vignette (offensive raid on a city). Participants answered 7-point Likert items on willingness to participate, expected personal and group benefit, punitive and reward sentiments. Offense/defense order was counterbalanced.",Summarizes the offensive-condition survey structure relevant to cooperation and punishment constructs.,1
10.1177_1474704917742720.md,"Study 2 – Defense, labor sufficient",Study 2 adds a between-subject manipulation of labor sufficiency (sufficient vs. insufficient) crossed with the within-subject offense/defense manipulation. This row captures responses to the defensive vignette under the labor-sufficient condition.,1,Conflict domain = Defense; Labor condition = Sufficient (current volunteers enough to repel invaders even if reluctant people do not join).,"Study 2 design: within subjects on conflict type (offense/defense), between subjects on labor condition (sufficient vs. insufficient), with specific added sentences describing each labor condition to the end of the defense vignette.",1,True,Human undergraduates completed this second survey experiment.,1,True,Includes randomized between-subject labor condition and within-subject offense/defense vignettes.,1,True,Again run on undergraduate subjects at UCSB (or similar campus setting) with no indication of online administration; treated as on-site/lab.,0.8,False,No simulations.,1,False,Empirical survey analysis only.,1,1,Individual responses to hypothetical scenarios; not an interactive multi-player task.,1,1,Each participant answers each of the two vignettes (offense and defense) once; each decision is one-shot.,1,1,"Willingness to participate and punitive sentiment are measured on Likert scales, not as binary all-or-nothing contributions.",0.9,0,No explicit endowment specification; we again treat resources as initially private in the narrative sense.,0.3,N/R,No public-goods payoff function specified.,1,0,Subjects do not interact or communicate during the survey.,1,0,No feedback on others’ responses or hypothetical decisions.,1,0,Punitive sentiment toward nonparticipants is hypothetical and anonymous.,1,0,Reward sentiments are similarly hypothetical.,1,0,Single evaluation per scenario with no round-count information.,1,0,"As in Study 1, only attitudes about punishment are elicited; no real costly punishment option is provided.",1,N/A,No implemented punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,"No actual rewarding mechanism, only survey items.",1,N/A,No reward implementation.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment present.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution behavior.,1,N/R,No token or point allocations.,1,N/R,No payoff or efficiency metric defined.,1,N/R,Group outcomes not quantified.,1,USA,"Study 2 describes 188 undergraduate subjects; given continuity with Study 1 and author’s affiliation, we infer U.S. university undergraduates.",0.7,N/R,"Study 2 reports only counts by sex (74 male, 114 female); no age range is provided for this sample.",0.2,"74 male, 114 female (total N = 188)",Explicitly reported: “74 were male and 114 were female.”,1,Undergraduate university students,Study 2: “One hundred and eighty-eight undergraduate subjects participated in this study.”,1,On site,"As with Study 1, this appears to be an in-person undergraduate survey experiment; no mention of online administration.",0.8,"Survey-experiment with hypothetical nomadic warfare vignette (defense). Between-subject manipulation: labor condition (sufficient vs. insufficient) via added sentences about current war-party size. Within-subject manipulation: offense vs. defense scenarios. 7-point Likert items on willingness to participate, punitive sentiment, expected benefits, and expected success.",Summarizes the Study 2 defensive condition including the added labor-sufficient manipulation relevant to labor recruitment and punitive sentiment.,1
10.1177_1474704917742720.md,"Study 2 – Defense, labor insufficient",Same as prior Study 2 defense condition but under the alternative between-subject labor manipulation where current war-party size is insufficient.,1,Conflict domain = Defense; Labor condition = Insufficient (current war party unlikely to repel invaders if reluctant people do not join).,"Defense vignette with added sentences describing that without additional joiners, the war party may be insufficient and defeat is likely.",1,True,Human-subject survey experiment.,1,True,Between-subject manipulation of labor condition plus within-subject manipulation of offense/defense.,1,True,Same setting as other Study 2 conditions.,0.8,False,No simulations.,1,False,Empirical only.,1,1,Individual hypothetical responses.,1,1,Single response per scenario.,1,1,Likert-scale rather than discrete all-or-nothing decisions.,0.9,0,No explicit endowment; same reasoning as other conditions.,0.3,N/R,Not a numerical public-goods game.,1,0,No communication.,1,0,No observation of others’ responses.,1,0,Punitive attitudes are not connected to identified real participants.,1,0,Reward attitudes only.,1,0,One-shot vignette.,1,0,No implemented costly punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,0,No implemented reward.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution.,1,N/R,No tokens/points.,1,N/R,No payoff structure.,1,N/R,No group payoff.,1,USA,Same Study 2 undergraduate sample in a U.S. university.,0.7,N/R,Age is not reported for Study 2.,0.2,"74 male, 114 female (total N = 188)",Same subject pool; random assignment splits these but aggregate composition is this.,1,Undergraduate university students,Explicitly undergraduates.,1,On site,Same environment as other Study 2 conditions.,0.8,"Study 2 defense vignette with added labor-insufficient cue: text states that unless reluctant people join, the current war party will likely be too small and the tribe may be de