anism in the design.,1,1,Described to subjects as a one-shot interaction; number of rounds known.,0.9,1,Punishers can assign costly deduction points to the Target.,1,1,Assigning deduction points incurs a cost of 1 MU to the Punisher.,1,3,Each punishment costs Punisher 1 MU and reduces Target’s payoff by 3 MU.,1,0,No option to reward others is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoffs defined directly by actions (defect: 5 MU self, 0 others; cooperate: 2 MU each); no explicit per-round endowment stated.",0.3,0.48,Cooperation rate in PGG stage is reported as 48%; binary cooperate/defect so rate equals fraction cooperating.,1,1,"In binary PGG, cooperators choose the cooperative option; treat this as full contribution (1 unit) vs 0 for defect; average amount not reported but unitary contribution per cooperator is implicit.",0.6,N/R,No aggregate efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) reported.,0,N/R,Only some conditional payoffs and MU conversions reported; no average group payoff provided.,0,USA,Participants were all US citizens recruited on MTurk.,1,Mean 32.7 years,Methods: mean age of 32.7 years reported for primary experiment sample.,1,"55% male, 45% female",Methods: 55% male; remaining assumed female as no other categories reported.,0.9,N/R,No educational attainment information reported.,0,Online,Conducted as an online experiment with MTurk participants using LIONESS.,1,"Two-stage one-shot binary PGG with 3 players, followed by punishment stage where two Punishers decide (unconditionally and conditionally via strategy method) whether to assign costly deduction points to a Target based on Target’s PGG choice. One Punisher’s unconditional decision is implemented and paired with the corresponding conditional decision of the other Punisher.",Summary of key structural features described around Fig. 1 and in Methods.,1
