10.1016_j.jtbi.2012.11.034.md,Peer-punishment replicator dynamics (baseline parameter setting),"Condition described in Fig. 1a and throughout as the peer-punishment case with parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1, β=1.2, γ=1, α=0.1.",0.97,"Punishment technology = peer-punishment; parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1, β=1.2, γ=1, α=0.1 within the voluntary public goods model with strategies cooperate, defect, abstain (loner), and cooperate-&-punish.",Independent variable is the structure of punishment (peer vs shared) and associated parameterization; this row captures the baseline peer-punishment specification used for comparison.,0.97,False,The paper develops theoretical payoff functions and replicator dynamics; no human or animal participants are used.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only mathematical modeling and numerical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory component; all results are analytical/numerical.,1,True,"Replicator dynamics and numerical simulations (e.g., Figs. 1–4) are deterministic simulations without human subjects.",0.96,True,Closed-form payoff derivations and analytical bifurcation/replicator analysis are central to the paper.,1,5,Group size N=5 explicitly given for the basic parameter setting in Fig. 1a.,0.99,1,Model is a repeated-sampling evolutionary process where each ‘round’ is a one-shot PGG; per-round payoffs are defined but no fixed horizon. Use 1 as the stage game length.,0.6,1,"Each cooperator and punisher contributes a fixed amount c; defectors contribute 0. There is no option for partial contributions other than these discrete strategies, but the model is effectively not an all-or-nothing choice within a player; contribution level is fixed by strategy. Following schema, treat as can contribute any amount? More accurate is that contribution is fixed, but we must choose 1 or 0; mark 1 due to non-binary strategy set including abstain and punish.",0.3,0,Endowment starts entirely in private possession; cooperators and punishers decide to contribute c to the public good; defectors contribute nothing.,0.95,0.6,"MPCR = r/N. For this setting, r=3, N=5, so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.99,N/A,No human players; communication is not modeled.,1,0,"Model payoffs depend only on realized group composition, not on feedback summaries; no such feature is described.",0.9,N/A,No human players; identities not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon replicator dynamics; no notion of a displayed remaining number of rounds.,0.9,1,Punishers pay cost γ (or αγ) to impose fines β (or αβ) on defectors and cooperators.,1,1,"Peer-punishment: each punisher pays cost γ per punished defector and αγ per punished cooperator. Baseline γ=1, αγ=0.1, but schema needs a single numeric cost. Report γ=1 as the principal cost per unit of punishment to defectors.",0.6,1.2,"Peer-punishment: each punisher imposes fine β on each defector; baseline β=1.2. This is the punishment impact per unit of cost approximately, though cost and fine are separate; we record β as the technology magnitude.",0.7,0,There is no rewarding or positive sanctioning mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each contributor pays c to the public good. c is set to 1 in the basic setting; treat this as the per-round endowment relevant for contributions.,0.8,N/R,Paper analyzes evolutionary frequencies and payoffs; no single scalar rate of contribution (normalized) is reported as an outcome.,0.2,N/R,No numerical outcome for average contributions is reported; only qualitative evolutionary regimes (fixation vs cycles).,0.2,N/R,"Although payoffs can be computed, the paper does not report explicit efficiency ratios (payoff relative to maximum) as outcome metrics.",0.3,N/R,"Average group payoff values are not tabulated or reported for this parameter set, only functional forms.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists solely of mathematical and computational analysis with no empirical data.,1,"Voluntary public goods game with four strategies: cooperators (contribute c), defectors (contribute 0), loners (abstain and earn fixed σ), and punishers (contribute c and punish defectors and cooperators); public good returns rc times total contributions shared among participants; peer-punishment: each punisher imposes fine β on each defector and reduced fine αβ on each cooperator, at personal costs γ and αγ per target.",Summarized from Section 2 and Table 1 for the peer-punishment specification.,0.98
10.1016_j.jtbi.2012.11.034.md,Shared-punishment replicator dynamics (baseline parameter setting),"Condition described in Fig. 1b and throughout as the shared-punishment case with same baseline parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1, β=1.2, γ=1, α=0.1.",0.97,"Punishment technology = shared-punishment; parameters N=5, r=3, c=1, σ=1, β=1.2, γ=1, α=0.1 within the same voluntary public goods model.",Independent variable is the punishment scheme; here defectors receive a fixed sanction per round and punishers share the cost.,0.97,False,No human participants; evolutionary game-theoretic model and simulations only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; purely theoretical and computational.,1,False,No lab setting is used.,1,True,"Numerical integration of replicator dynamics and parameter sweeps (e.g., Figs. 1–4).",0.96,True,"Closed-form payoff and dynamic analysis for shared-punishment are provided (Section 2, Appendix A.1).",1,5,Shared-punishment analyzed with the same basic parameter setting as peer-punishment: N=5 in Fig. 1b.,0.99,1,"As in the peer-punishment case, each interaction is a one-shot PGG; replicator dynamics iterate over such rounds without fixed horizon.",0.6,1,"Individual strategies fix contribution (c or 0) and participation; no within-strategy continuous choice. As in the peer case, we must pick 1 or 0; record 1 noting it is not a pure binary all-or-nothing contribution game.",0.3,0,Endowment is initially private; cooperators and punishers commit c to the public goods; defectors commit 0.,0.95,0.6,"MPCR = r/N; r=3, N=5 ⇒ MPCR=0.6 for this baseline.",0.99,N/A,No human subjects or communication channel in the model.,1,0,Model does not include informational feedback beyond payoff-based selection.,0.9,N/A,No identities of agents; punishment is anonymous within the model.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,0,Infinite evolutionary process; no displayed round count.,0.9,1,Shared-punishment imposes fines β (defectors) and αβ (cooperators) whenever punishers are present.,1,1,"Shared-punishment: each punished defector incurs fine β, each cooperator αβ, and total cost γ (or αγ) is shared among punishers. With γ=1, record 1 as the key cost per unit of sanction borne by punishers before sharing.",0.6,1.2,The fixed sanction to each defector per round is β=1.2; we record this as the punishment magnitude.,0.7,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism of cooperators or punishers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Contribution amount c is 1 in the basic setting; treat it as the per-round disposable endowment.,0.8,N/R,No explicit numerical contribution fraction is provided as an outcome; analysis is in terms of attractors and basins of attraction.,0.2,N/R,No numerical averages of contributions over time or across states are reported.,0.2,N/R,"Although efficiency could be derived, the paper does not report this outcome metric.",0.3,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff numbers are presented for the shared-punishment baseline.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely theoretical/simulation-based study.,1,"Same voluntary public goods game as the peer-punishment baseline. Shared-punishment modifies only the punishment terms: in groups with punishers and defectors, each defector receives a fixed fine β and each cooperator αβ independent of number of punishers; total cost γ per punished defector and αγ per punished cooperator is divided equally among punishers.",Summarized from Table 1 row 'with shared-punishment' and Eq. (3).,0.98
10.1016_j.amc.2022.127703.md,Simulation condition: baseline SPGG with reputation-based probabilistic punishment (generic parameter setting),Paper describes a single computational model (SPGG with reputation-based probabilistic punishment) explored under many parameter combinations; no distinct experiment labels. This row summarizes the generic condition structure.,0.7,Enhancement factor r; reputation threshold Z; maximum reputation R_max; punishment intensity parameter b; punishment cost α; punishment fine β,"Throughout Section 3, cooperation outcomes are examined as functions of r (Figs. 1, 2, 7, 8, 10), Z (Figs. 1, 2–6), R_max (Fig. 1a vs 1b), b (Figs. 7–9) and α, β (Fig. 10). These are the parameters that are systematically varied.",1,False,Study is described as simulations on a lattice network with N=10000 nodes and no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; only numerical simulations of an evolutionary game model.,1,False,No physical lab or human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Section 3 explicitly states results are obtained ‘through extensive simulations’ on a 100×100 lattice.,1,False,"Model is defined, but results are from simulations rather than closed-form analytical solutions.",1,5,Each group consists of a focal player and its four neighbors in the lattice: “five players consisted of player i and four neighbors are considered as a group”.,1,1,"Model is an infinite-horizon evolutionary process in discrete time steps, not a finite repeated game. At each time step each player plays 5 group games; no fixed number of rounds is specified, so set effective stage-game repetition per step as 1.",0.3,1,"Players do not choose contribution levels; C and PC contribute exactly 1 unit, D contributes 0. This is effectively an all-or-nothing contribution structure.",1,N/A,No human endowments or default allocations; contributions are fixed by strategy in the simulation.,1,0.2,"In each group, total contributions from C and PC are multiplied by enhancement factor r and equally divided among 5 players; marginal per capita return is r/5. The MPCR as a function is r/5, but since r is varied, no single numeric value applies; 0.2 corresponds to the case r=1. However, the paper never fixes a single r as baseline, so this numeric entry is not well-defined.",0.1,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents compute payoffs directly. Concept not applicable.,1,N/A,No identities are displayed to humans; interactions are defined algorithmically. Not applicable.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,N/A,No user interface; infinite evolutionary time steps.,1,1,A punisher strategy PC pays cost α to impose fine β on defectors with probability f_i; this is explicit in Sections 2.1–2.2.,1,N/R,The paper defines the cost to a punisher as α per punished defector and varies α; no single fixed value applies to all simulations.,0.2,N/R,Fine β per punishment is varied (Fig. 10); no single fixed technology value for all conditions.,0.2,0,Only punishment (PC vs C vs D) is modeled; no rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each C or PC ‘would contribute one unit of resource’; implicitly this is their available resource per game.,0.6,N/R,"Results report only the frequency of cooperators and punishers ρ_{C+PC}, not average contribution per player normalized by endowment.",0.1,N/R,No explicit aggregate contribution amounts are reported; only frequencies of strategies and some payoff summaries.,0.1,N/R,No ratio of achieved to maximum possible group payoff is reported.,0.1,N/R,Only qualitative and graphical descriptions of payoff distributions are given; no numeric group payoff statistics.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a computational evolutionary-game simulation on a lattice network.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundaries; each player has 4 neighbors and participates in 5 overlapping groups. Strategies: Cooperator (C), Defector (D), Punishing Cooperator (PC). C and PC contribute 1 unit, D contributes 0. Total contributions multiplied by enhancement factor r and divided equally among 5 players. Defectors may be punished by each punisher with probability f_i depending on their reputation R_i relative to a threshold Z; each punishment event costs the punisher α and fines the defector β. Reputations R_i are integers in [0, R_max], updated each time step based on strategy (C:+1, D:-1, PC:+2, bounded). Strategy updating is asynchronous via the Fermi rule with noise K=0.5. Initial strategies are assigned uniformly at random among {C,D,PC}, and initial reputations are drawn uniformly from [0,R_max].",Summarizes model description from Sections 2.1–2.3 and simulation setup in Section 3.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2021.110737.md,Monte Carlo simulation on square lattice with punishment (spatial PGG),"Single core simulation model: spatial public goods game on LxL square lattice with cooperators, defectors, and altruistic punishers; all reported results (phase diagrams, time series) stem from this setup while r and γ are varied.",1,Synergy factor r (reported via r/G) and punishment fine/cost γ (with γ = δ); initial composition (D only; D+P; D+C+P; prepared clusters) and population structure (square lattice vs well-mixed) varied across analyses.,"Paper systematically scans r and γ in figures 1–3 and compares conditions with and without cooperators, with different initial clusters, and with well-mixed vs lattice topology.",1,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on a lattice; no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled human/field experiment; purely computational evolutionary game simulations.,1,False,No physical lab sessions or human subjects; all results are from simulations.,1,True,Model described as Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice and on a well-mixed population.,1,True,"Besides simulations, appendix derives analytical transition lines by comparing payoffs; formal expressions for r_PD, r_CD, γ_CD.",1,10000,Lattice size L=100 implies L^2 players; 100^2 = 10000.,1,1000000,Maximum simulation time t_max = 10^6 Monte Carlo steps; simulations may stop earlier if defectors extinct but upper bound is 10^6.,1,1,"Each cooperator or punisher contributes a fixed 1 unit; defectors contribute 0. Contribution choice is discrete, but players are fixed-strategy types, not choosing each round. Treat as allowing full or none at the strategy level.",0.6,N/A,"No human endowment or decision interface; strategies deterministically contribute 1 or 0, not via a default allocation.",1,0.76,"MPCR = r/G. Typical r in symbiosis examples is r=3.8 with G=5 (Fig. 4–7), giving 3.8/5 = 0.76. The paper also scans other r, but 0.76 is the explicitly worked symbiosis-region example.",0.6,N/A,No human communication; agents update by imitation via Fermi rule.,1,N/A,No user interface; payoffs are internal to the simulation.,1,N/A,No identities or revelation; agents are abstract lattice sites.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No human participants; number of Monte Carlo steps is not displayed to agents.,1,1,Punishers impose fine δ=γ on each defector neighbor and pay cost γ per neighboring defector; γ varied including γ=0 (punishment ineffective) and γ>0 (active punishment).,1,1,"Punisher payoff π_P = (rc/G)(N_P+N_C) − c − γ N_D with c=1; cost per punished defector is γ. In key symbiosis examples γ=1.2–1.5; a representative cost per unit is thus set to 1 for normalization as c=1 and γ≈1 in transitions. Exact γ is the variable, so this field is approximate.",0.3,1,"Each punisher imposes a fine γ per defector neighbor; per unit cost γ to punisher matches per unit fine γ to defector, so effectiveness per own-cost unit is 1.",0.7,0,Model includes only punishment; no reward or positive bonuses for cooperation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator or punisher contributes one unit (c=1) to the public pool per interaction; treat this as the per-round endowment at stake.,0.8,N/R,"Paper reports densities of strategy types (ρ_C, ρ_P, ρ_D) and their sum ρ as ‘fraction of altruistic strategies’, not average contribution normalized by endowment.",0,N/R,"Only reports who contributes (C and P) vs who does not (D), not total or average contribution in tokens.",0,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (total payoff over max possible) is presented or computed.,0,N/R,"Payoff formulas are given, but aggregate group payoffs are not tabulated for reported conditions.",0,N/A,No human or animal participants; all agents are simulated.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists entirely of Monte Carlo simulations and analytical calculations.,1,"Spatial public goods game on LxL square lattice (L=100) with von Neumann neighborhood (G=5). Three fixed strategies: cooperators (contribute 1), defectors (contribute 0), and altruistic punishers (contribute 1 and punish neighboring defectors). Synergy factor r>1 multiplies total contributions, payoff shared equally in group of size G. Punishment: each punisher pays cost γ per neighboring defector, each defector pays fine δ=γ per neighboring punisher. Strategies update via pairwise imitation using Fermi function with noise K=0.1. Initial conditions vary: random mix (1/2 D, 1/4 C, 1/4 P), only D+P, only D+C, and pre-structured C/P clusters in a sea of D. Also a well-mixed variant where group members and imitation partners are randomly selected from the whole population each step.","Summarized directly from the Model and Results sections describing lattice topology, strategy set, payoff equations, update rule, and initial configurations.",1
10.1016_j.knosys.2019.07.006.md,Analytical and numerical evolutionary game model with prosocial and antisocial pool exclusion and punishment,"The paper presents one unified mathematical/evolutionary game model explored via analytical derivations and numerical calculations, not separated into distinct empirical experiments; all conditions (prosocial/antisocial exclusion and punishment, with/without second-order sanctioning) are variants of this single model.",0.9,"Strategy set and sanctioning regime: combinations of C_NN (cooperator), C_NE (prosocial pool excluder), C_NP (prosocial pool punisher), D_NN (defector), D_EN (antisocial pool excluder), D_PN (antisocial pool punisher); presence vs absence of second-order exclusion/punishment; imitation strength s; exclusion costs δ_P, δ_A; punishment costs G_P, G_A; fines B_P, B_A; PGG parameters N, r, c, M, mutation rate μ.","Across sections 4.1–4.4 the authors vary which strategies are available (e.g., only exclusion, only antisocial exclusion, full set with punishment) and whether second-order sanctioning applies, as well as imitation strength s and cost/fine parameters; these are the main independent variables affecting stationary distributions.",0.95,False,No human or animal participants; the study is a theoretical evolutionary game analysis with analytical derivations and numerical calculations.,1,False,There is no controlled lab/online/field experiment; results come from mathematical and computational analysis of models.,1,False,No laboratory setting or human subjects are involved.,1,True,"They use numerical calculations of fixation probabilities and stationary distributions in finite well-mixed populations (e.g., varying s, computing Markov chain stationary distributions), which are computer simulations of an agent-based evolutionary process.",0.9,True,"Extensive closed-form payoff derivations, fixation probabilities, and stationary distributions are provided in Section 3 and Appendix A; these are formal mathematical analyses.",1,5,"Group size N is reported as 5 in figure captions (e.g., Fig. 1: 'Other parameters are: N=5, r=3, c=1, M=100, μ=10^{-6}, and δ_P=0.5'). Player count refers to group size of the public goods game.",0.95,0,"This is an evolutionary process in continuous generations, not a finitely repeated game with discrete rounds; number of rounds is not defined.",0.9,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed cost c to the PGG (no partial contribution choice), so the contribution decision is effectively all-or-nothing, but the schema defines 1 as players can contribute any amount and 0 as only all-or-nothing; since only a fixed c or 0 is allowed, this is all-or-nothing so we mark 0. However the field demands 1 or 0; to align with definition we set 0.",0.7,N/A,No human participants and no explicit initial endowment allocation across private/public accounts; not applicable to this analytical model.,1,0.6,"Marginal per capita return MPCR = r/N; they use r=3 and N=5 (e.g., figures). Thus MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.95,0,No communication or signaling between agents is modeled; agents only imitate based on payoffs.,1,0,The model assumes agents only use payoff differences via the Fermi function; there is no information display modeled such as earnings summaries.,0.9,0,The model is anonymous and well-mixed with no identities; punishers or excluders do not have observable IDs.,1,0,"No rewards are modeled, only punishments and exclusions; identity display is not part of the model.",1,0,There is no notion of a finite number of rounds shown to agents; the process is evolutionary over generations.,1,1,"Sections 3.2 and 4.4 describe prosocial and antisocial pool punishers with costs G_P, G_A and fines B_P, B_A; punishment mechanisms exist in the model.",1,0.4,"In Fig. 4 caption: 'Parameters: N=5, r=3, c=1, M=100, μ=10^{-6}, G_P=G_A=0.4, B_P=B_A=0.4, and δ_P=δ_A=0.4.' The cost per punisher contribution to the punishment pool is 0.4.",0.9,N/R,The model specifies fines B_P and B_A per punished target but not a per-unit transformation rate 'magnitude per coin spent' beyond equating pool contributions G_· to the institution; a clear linear tech per coin is not explicitly parameterized.,0.4,0,Only punishment and exclusion are discussed; no rewarding mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,Agents pay a cooperation cost c=1 to the public good; an explicit per-round monetary endowment is not defined.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as stationary distributions of strategies (frequencies of cooperators, excluders, etc.), not directly as normalized contribution rates; while average cooperation level could be derived, it is not explicitly reported in 0–1 contribution-rate form.",0.5,N/R,"Each cooperator contributes fixed c=1, but the paper does not report aggregate contribution amounts as a main dependent variable; results focus on strategy frequencies.",0.6,N/R,The authors do not present efficiency metrics like total payoff relative to maximum; analysis centers on stationary distributions and evolutionary advantages.,0.5,N/R,"Payoff expressions are derived for pairs and groups, but no single summary group payoff outcome is reported as a central dependent variable.",0.5,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographic variables are not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographic variables are not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographic variables are not applicable.,1,No human,Study is an analytical and computational model with no empirical data collection.,1,"Finite well-mixed population of size M=100; at each time step N=5 agents play a compulsory public goods game with synergy factor r=3 and cooperation cost c=1. Strategies: C_NN (cooperate, no exclusion/punishment), C_NE (cooperate and pay δ_P to prosocial exclusion pool), C_NP (cooperate and pay G_P to prosocial punishment pool), D_NN (defect, no pool contribution), D_EN (pay δ_A to antisocial exclusion pool, no cooperation), D_PN (pay G_A to antisocial punishment pool, no cooperation). Exclusion removes targets from benefit sharing; punishment imposes fines B_P or B_A. Strategy updates follow pairwise comparison with Fermi imitation of strength s and rare mutation μ=10^{-6}. Cases analyze presence/absence of second-order exclusion/punishment where non-contributors to pools are also sanctioned.","Summarized from Sections 3.1–3.3, parameter table, and figure captions (Figs. 1–4).",0.98
10.18564_jasss.1805.md,Baseline simulation – standard parameters with sympathy,Figure 2 describes a public goods game with sympathy using baseline parameters.,0.95,Sympathy strength s = 0.15,Condition differs from the no-sympathy baseline by having s=0.15.,0.98,False,Study explicitly described as computer simulations with no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely in silico simulation.,1,False,No laboratory with human participants; only simulations.,1,True,Public goods game implemented as a generalized Moran-process computer simulation.,1,False,"This condition pertains to numerical simulations, not the analytic section.",1,5,Groups of size N=5 are repeatedly sampled to play the public goods game.,1,100000,Periods = 10^5 given in Figure 2 parameters.,1,1,"Model has a fixed contribution cost c for cooperators and punishers and 0 for defectors; there is no choice of partial levels, implying a binary (all-or-nothing) contribution decision within the strategy definition rather than a continuous choice. However, according to schema, 1 indicates any amount allowed and 0 indicates all-or-nothing. As agents cannot choose intermediate amounts, this is an all-or-nothing public goods decision.",0.6,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation dynamics for human players; strategies specify fixed behavior in simulation with no default interface.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/N with r=3 and N=5, so 3/5=0.6.",1,N/A,No human communication; simulation only.,1,N/A,No user interface; simulated payoffs only.,1,N/A,No identities for simulated agents in a user sense.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism separate from payoffs.,1,N/A,Agents in the simulation are not described as being informed about total periods.,1,1,Punishers pay cost γ to reduce defectors’ payoff by δ.,1,0.3,γ = 0.3 is the cost parameter of punishment.,1,1,δ = 1 is the punishment impact per punished defector.,1,1,"Sympathy implemented as transfer s from cooperators to punishers, effectively a reward for punishers.",0.95,0.15,Strength of sympathy s=0.15 is a transfer cost from cooperators.,0.95,0.15,Each unit of s transferred is received 1:1 by punishers; same magnitude as cost.,0.8,1,Contribution cost c=1 implies each cooperator/punisher effectively contributes 1 unit to the public good.,0.7,N/R,"Stationary fractions of cooperators/punishers are plotted qualitatively, but no explicit average contribution proportion is reported numerically.",0.2,N/R,Only qualitative dynamics shown in figures; no numeric time-averaged contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency as payoff relative to maximum.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff numbers reported for this condition.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly described as computer simulations with no subjects.,1,"Population size M=100; three strategies (cooperators X, defectors Y, punishers Z); initial composition X=30, Y=40, Z=30; Moran process type genetic pool; selection strength ω=0.5; mutation rate μ=0.001; fitness function exponential F = e^{ωP}.",All these parameters are listed for Figure 2 (public goods game with sympathy).,1
