10.1037_pspa0000375.md,N/A – theoretical/meta-analytic review only,"The paper is a narrative review and meta-analysis summarizing many other studies; it does not report a new, concrete experimental or simulation condition run by the author.",1,N/A,No original experimental manipulation is implemented in this article; it only synthesizes prior work.,1,False,The article reports no new data collection with human participants; all results are secondary analyses of previously published studies.,1,False,No new controlled experiment is conducted; only meta-analytic and theoretical work are presented.,1,False,No new lab or online experiment is run by the author.,1,False,The paper does not present an agent-based model or numerical simulation; it aggregates empirical findings.,1,True,"The contribution is analytical/theoretical and meta-analytic, integrating and modeling prior findings without new primary data.",1,0,No specific game is run; all experimental configurations belong to external studies.,1,0,No repeated-game design is implemented by the author.,1,0,Not applicable without an original game; set to 0 by convention.,1,N/A,No human-subject game is implemented; field is not applicable.,1,N/R,No single public-goods configuration is defined; many different MPCRs are only mentioned for prior literature.,1,N/A,No interaction among participants is designed in this article.,1,0,No experimental interface is used; thus no feedback or summaries are shown.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is implemented by the author.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism is implemented by the author.,1,0,No rounds exist in this purely analytical/meta-analytic work.,1,0,No experimental punishment option is provided by this article itself.,1,N/A,No punishment technology is specified because no game is run.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this work.,1,0,No reward stage or reward option is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward technology is specified because no game is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this work.,1,0,No tokens or points are allocated by this article in an experiment.,1,N/A,No public-goods or contribution behavior is directly measured; only literature is reviewed.,1,N/A,No new experimental contributions are recorded.,1,N/R,No payoff data are generated; hence efficiency is not computed.,1,N/A,No groups receive payoffs in this analytical work.,1,N/A,No participants are recruited by this study.,1,N/A,No sample is collected.,1,N/A,No participants are involved.,1,N/A,No participants are involved.,1,No human,The article is a review/meta-analysis and theoretical framework; there is no data collection environment.,1,"This article synthesizes and meta-analyzes many prior cooperation, altruism, lying, reciprocity, and moral-dilemma experiments, but does not introduce any new game or experimental condition itself.","Based on the abstract, methods, and meta-analytic sections, all empirical content refers to previously published studies.",1
10.1016_j.techsoc.2021.101843.md,"Peer punishment model – with bilateral commitments (PP, 5-strategy)","Condition where self-enforcing bilateral commitments and peer punishment are available; population has five strategies (AS-in, AS-out, AU-in, AU-out, PS).",1,"Presence of voluntary bilateral commitments (enabled); sanctioning by peers via speed reductions s_alpha and s_beta; parameters e.g. s_alpha ∈ {0.3,1}, s_beta=1, p_r varied across regions I–III.",Independent features distinguishing this condition are availability of commitments plus peer punishment; key sanction parameters and risk p_r are explicitly varied in figures.,0.86,False,No human subjects; purely evolutionary game-theoretic population model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a theoretical/simulation model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; only mathematical/simulation study.,1,True,Uses numerical computation of fixation probabilities and stationary distributions in finite populations; figures report frequencies from such simulations/Markov chains.,1,True,"Derives payoff matrices and analytical conditions (e.g., risk-dominance, fixation probabilities, Markov chains) alongside numerical results.",1,2,DSAIR model is defined as a two-player game; they focus on two-participant race in this paper.,1,100,They set W=100 development steps/rounds in parameter lists for main figures.,0.95,1,Players choose between discrete actions SAFE vs UNSAFE; there is no partial or continuous contribution choice.,1,N/A,Model is not a public goods contribution game; no endowment allocation concept.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,"No human participants; communication is conceptual only, not an experimental interface feature.",1,0,Model specifies payoffs and strategies but no information display about others’ earnings is discussed.,0.9,0,Punishment is modeled as speed reduction; no notion of identity revelation in an interface.,0.9,0,No reward or identity revelation mechanisms are modeled.,1,0,"Number of rounds W is a parameter of the model, not information shown to agents.",0.9,1,"Peer punishment is central: punisher’s speed reduced by s_alpha, punished player’s speed by s_beta.",1,N/R,"Punishment cost is modeled as development-speed reduction s_alpha, but no token/coin cost metric is defined.",0.4,N/R,"Effectiveness defined via speed reduction s_beta, not in terms comparable to ‘units per coin’.",0.4,0,Only punishment mechanisms (peer and institutional) are considered; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/R,"Model has benefits per round (b) and final prize B, but no explicit per-round endowment variable.",0.5,N/R,No notion of contributions relative to endowment; outcomes are frequencies of SAFE vs UNSAFE and social welfare.,1,N/R,No contribution quantity is defined in the model.,1,N/R,They discuss ‘social welfare’ qualitatively but do not report normalized efficiency values.,0.6,N/R,Do not list explicit group payoff magnitudes; focus is on dominance and frequencies.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical/simulation-based with no empirical data collection.,1,"Two-player repeated AI development race (W≈100 rounds). At each round players choose SAFE (cost c>0, speed 1) or UNSAFE (no cost, speed s>1). Intermediate benefit b is split proportionally to speeds; final prize B is obtained when W steps are reached. Disaster probability p_r increases with unsafe actions; winners lose B with probability p_r. Strategies (AS-in, AS-out, AU-in, AU-out, PS) differ in commitment, action under/without commitment, and whether they punish dishonest partners. Punishment reduces speeds: punisher’s by s_alpha, target’s by s_beta; commitment incurs per-round cost ε.",Summary of the formal model description in Sections 2.1–2.3 and 3.1.,0.96
10.1016_j.techsoc.2021.101843.md,"Peer punishment model – without commitments (PP, 2-strategy baseline)",Comparison scenario where commitments are disabled; interpreted as system where ‘commitment is not voluntary’ and everyone is implicitly assumed committed and punishable.,0.96,Absence of voluntary bilateral commitments (disabled); unconditional peer punishment of UNSAFE; effectively two strategies AS and AU under sanctioning.,Distinguished from commitment condition by removal of commitment stage; paper treats this as baseline ‘no commitments’ scenario for PP.,0.9,False,Same evolutionary simulation framework; no humans.,1,False,"Theoretical/simulation study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab implementation.,1,True,Uses the same numerical Markov-chain/fixation analysis to compute stationary distributions of AS vs AU.,1,True,Relies on analytical EGT expressions for fixation probabilities and risk-dominance.,1,2,Underlying DSAIR interaction remains two-player.,1,100,Same W=100 parameter as in commitment scenarios.,0.95,1,Binary SAFE/UNSAFE decision per round.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,No explicit information-feedback mechanism modeled.,0.9,0,Identities and their display are not modeled.,0.9,0,No rewards present.,1,0,"W is a parameter, not an on-screen indicator.",0.9,1,Unsafe behavior is unconditionally penalized by peer punishment in the model without commitments.,1,N/R,Cost dimension only appears as speed reduction s_alpha; not convertible to coins.,0.4,N/R,"Impact is a reduction of speed by s_beta, not expressed per unit cost.",0.4,0,No positive incentives are introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment is not parameterized; only benefits b and B.,0.5,N/R,"Model reports frequencies of UNSAFE, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No concept of contribution amount.,1,N/R,They discuss over-regulation and social welfare qualitatively; no numeric efficiency ratio reported.,0.6,N/R,No explicit numeric group payoff outcomes are tabulated.,0.5,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation/theoretical study only.,1,"Same DSAIR two-player race as with commitments, but the pre-play commitment stage is removed. Practically all players are treated as implicitly under regulation, with UNSAFE actions always subject to peer punishment. Strategy set effectively reduces to AS (always SAFE) vs AU (always UNSAFE) under sanctioning; evolutionary dynamics computed via fixation probabilities and stationary distribution.",Summarizes the ‘commitment absent’ benchmark used in Fig. 2 and Fig. 4 discussions.,0.9
10.1016_j.techsoc.2021.101843.md,"Institutional punishment model – with bilateral commitments (IP, 4-strategy)","Condition with institutional punishment and voluntary bilateral commitments; strategy set {AS-in, AU-in, AS-out, AU-out}, no PS.",0.96,"Presence of voluntary bilateral commitments (enabled); institutional punishment of AU-in when both players committed; s_beta varied (e.g. 1 and 0.5), p_r varied across regions.","Distinguished by institutional (third-party) enforcement, existence of commitment option, and value of punishment effectiveness parameter s_beta.",0.86,False,No human or field data; pure model.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Uses simulation/Markov-chain computations to get frequencies under IP with commitments (Fig. 5).,1,True,Relies on same analytical framework for payoffs and fixation probabilities.,1,2,Still a two-player DSAIR game.,1,100,W=100 in parameter list for Fig. 5.,0.95,1,SAFE vs UNSAFE discrete choice.,1,N/A,No contribution endowments.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,0,Information display is not modeled.,0.9,0,Institution acts abstractly; no interface-level identity display.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,0,Number of rounds is a parameter only.,0.9,1,Institutional punishment reduces AU-in’s speed by s_beta when a commitment is violated.,1,N/R,No explicit resource cost to institution is modelled in tokens.,0.4,N/R,"Punishment tech is encoded as a speed reduction s_beta, not per-unit mapping.",0.4,0,Only punitive sanctions are considered.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"As elsewhere, only b and B are defined.",0.5,N/R,"Outcome metric is frequency of UNSAFE, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,They compare ‘safety outcomes’ and ‘over-regulation’ qualitatively; no numeric efficiency data.,0.6,N/R,Group payoff statistics are not tabulated.,0.5,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation/analytical model.,1,"Two-player DSAIR race with voluntary commitments to SAFE and an external institution that punishes commitment violations. Strategies: AS-in (commits, SAFE under commitment, UNSAFE otherwise, no punishment), AS-out (no commitment, always SAFE), AU-in (commits but plays UNSAFE, hence gets institutionally punished when partner also committed), AU-out (no commitment, always UNSAFE, never punished). Punishment reduces AU-in’s speed from s to s−s_beta in committed interactions; institution itself bears no modeled cost.",Based on Section 3.2 and Fig. 5 description of IP with commitments.,0.94
10.1016_j.techsoc.2021.101843.md,"Institutional punishment model – without commitments (IP, 2-strategy baseline)",Baseline institutional punishment scenario without the option of prior bilateral commitments; only AS and AU exist.,0.94,"Absence of voluntary bilateral commitments (disabled); institutional punishment directly targets UNSAFE (AU), reducing its speed to s−s_beta; s_beta varied.",Key difference is that commitment stage and associated strategic types are removed; institution sanctions any UNSAFE behavior.,0.9,False,No empirical data collection.,1,False,Theoretical/simulation scenario.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Frequencies of UNSAFE under IP without commitments are computed numerically (Fig. 5 right panels).,1,True,Same analytical EGT foundation is applied.,1,2,Underlying game is still two-player.,1,100,Uses W=100 as in other IP scenarios.,0.95,1,Binary SAFE vs UNSAFE actions.,1,N/A,No public goods contributions.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,0,No explicit output displays to agents.,0.9,0,Identities are not part of the model.,0.9,0,No reward.,1,0,"W is a fixed parameter, not displayed.",0.9,1,Institutional punishment reduces the speed of UNSAFE strategy AU by s_beta.,1,N/R,No explicit punishment cost metric besides speed reduction for punished agents; institutional costs not modeled.,0.4,N/R,"Punishment characterized by speed reduction s_beta, not per-unit payoff deductions.",0.4,0,Only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward in this condition.,1,N/R,Endowment not parametrized.,0.5,N/R,No contributions tracked.,1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Outcomes compared via unsafe frequencies and qualitative social welfare; no numeric efficiency.,0.6,N/R,No explicit reporting of group payoff sizes.,0.5,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Model-only condition.,1,"Two-strategy population (AS: always SAFE; AU: always UNSAFE) under institutional enforcement. Institution reduces AU’s development speed from s to s−s_beta in all interactions (no commitment prerequisite). Evolutionary dynamics computed in finite population of size Z using fixation probabilities and stationary distribution, varying p_r and s_beta to assess over-regulation and safety levels across regions I–III.",Summarizes the institutional punishment scenario without agreements as described in Section 3.2 and Fig. 5.,0.9
10.1016_j.ocecoaman.2022.106447.md,Numerical simulation – baseline scenario (no strict policies),Section 4.2 describes a specific parameter combination called the baseline case used for numerical simulation of the evolutionary game.,1,"Policy parameters set at baseline: performance weights δ1=0.6, δ2=0.2; no punishment for LGs (F=0); no ecological damage compensation (φ=0); fine on SEs D=2; collusion benefit R=3; transfer S=4; implementation strengths λ1=λ2=λ3=0; costs C1=8, C2=6, C3=12; pollution Q1=5, Q2=2; LG loss/benefit L=E=15; γ=0.5.",Simulation condition defined under “Baseline scenario” with full parameter list.,1,False,Authors explicitly state “using numerical simulation technology” and “No data was used for the research described in the article.”,1,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; only model-based simulations.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; purely computational.,1,True,They construct an evolutionary game model and conduct numerical simulations over parameter values.,1,True,"Sections 2–3 derive payoff matrices, replicator dynamics, equilibrium points, and Jacobian analytically.",1,3,"Three strategic agents: central government (CG), local governments (LGs), sea-using enterprises (SEs).",1,0,"Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics, not discrete repeated rounds as in standard public goods games.",0.2,1,"Players choose among discrete strategies (full vs partial), not contribution levels; all-or-nothing contribution concept not applicable but coded as free choice rather than binary contribution amount.",0.2,N/A,No human subjects or endowments; not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods payoff structure with marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication stage.,1,N/A,No user interface; model-level payoffs only.,1,N/A,No identities; no human agents.,1,N/A,No identities; no human agents.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or finite rounds.,1,1,"Model includes punishments F (CG→LGs) and D (LGs→SEs)