e maximum possible contribution, but no numeric values or explicit formula reported for extraction.",0.6,N/R,No explicit numeric contributions given; contributions depend on rich/poor cooperator frequencies.,0.6,N/R,No explicit ratio of actual to maximum payoff reported.,0.6,N/R,Group payoffs not summarized in numeric form.,0.7,N/A,No human participants in the model; experimental references are external papers.,1,N/A,No human participants in the model.,1,N/A,No human participants in the model.,1,N/A,No human participants in the model.,1,No human,The section provides a mathematical model calibrated on external experiments but itself contains no empirical data collection.,1,"Population split into Z_R=40 rich and Z_P=160 poor (Z=200). Group size N=6, target M=3 c \bar{b} (with \bar{b}=1). Rich and poor have different endowments b_R=1.7 and b_P=0.3 and costs c_R=c_P=c=0.1. Risk r as in base model (specific value not numerically given in Fig. 3 description). Success probability P=Θ(k_R c_R + k_P c_P − M \bar{c}), where \bar{c} is average cost, and rich/poor payoffs are Π_D^X=b_X P + (1−r)b_X(1−P), Π_C^X=Π_D^X−c_X. Homophily parameter h biases imitation toward same wealth class. Mutation rates μ_X=1/Z_X, selection intensity β=5.",From Sec. 4 and Fig. 3 caption.,0.95
10.1142_s0218202515400163.md,Sanctioning institutions – local punishment,Sec. 5 describes two punishment scales; this row captures the configuration with local institutions (left panels of Fig. 4).,0.95,Presence and scale of sanctioning institution: local punishment institution active when number of punishers in a group k_P ≥ M_I; risk level r (low r=0.2 vs high r=0.5) also varied.,Sec. 5 defines local vs global punishment via Π_scale and Fig. 4 top/bottom panels contrast low vs high risk.,0.9,False,"Model with strategies C, D, P and institutional punishment; no human data.",1,False,"Theoretical/simulation analysis, not an experiment.",1,False,No lab implementation.,1,True,"Stationary distributions and gradient fields computed numerically over (i_C, i_P) state space.",0.95,True,"Analytical payoff functions, fitnesses, and transition probabilities defined.",0.95,200,Fig. 4 caption: Z=200.,1,1,Single interaction per group per update; evolutionary repetition treated separately.,0.7,0,"C and P contribute fixed amount c, D contributes 0; no continuous choice.",0.9,N/A,No explicit initial allocation interface; theoretical model.,1,N/R,No linear public-good multiplier defined; benefits modeled via risk of losing endowment.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/R,Agents observe others only in abstract payoff-comparison sense; no reporting of summaries.,0.5,N/A,No individual identities; punishment carried out by institution in model.,1,N/A,No rewarding institution or identity revelation.,1,N/R,"Infinite-horizon evolutionary model, not framed in displayed rounds.",0.5,1,Punishers P pay a tax to an institution which fines defectors when threshold M_I is reached.,1,0.03,Punishment tax π_t=0.03 given in Fig. 4 caption; each P pays this when contributing to institution.,1,0.3,Punishment fine π_f=0.3 applied to each defector when institution is active; treated as punishment magnitude per punishment episode.,0.9,0,Only punishment institutions are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,1,Fig. 4 caption: b=1.,1,N/R,Figures show regions of state space but no explicit aggregate contribution rates reported numerically.,0.6,N/R,No numeric reported contributions; only qualitative description of cooperative states.,0.6,N/R,Paper compares qualitative efficiency (robustness of cooperation) but no explicit payoff/maximum ratio.,0.6,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff values given for local punishment condition.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Institutional punishment studied through theoretical/simulation model only.,1,"Population Z=200, group size N=8, threshold M=6 cooperators (C or P) needed to avoid climate disaster, risk r=0.2 (low risk) or r=0.5 (high risk), c=0.1, b=1, selection intensity β=5, mutation rate μ=0.05. Strategies: C (cooperate), D (defect), P (cooperate and pay punishment tax). Punishers pay tax π_t=0.03 to local institution; if number of punishers in the group k_P ≥ M_I (M_I=25% of population, as per caption), each D in that group is fined π_f=0.3, making punishment a second-order public good at group scale. Dynamics analyzed via gradient of selection and stationary distribution.","Compiled from Sec. 5 and Fig. 4 caption; M_I described as 25%, π_t and π_f specified.",0.9
10.1142_s0218202515400163.md,Sanctioning institutions – global punishment,Sec. 5 global-institution configuration where institution activation depends on total number of punishers in the whole population; right panels of Fig. 4.,0.95,Presence and scale of sanctioning institution: global punishment institution active when total number of punishers in population i_P ≥ M_I; risk level r (0.2 vs 0.5) also varied.,Sec. 5 defines Π_global using i_P and same risk manipulation as for local case.,0.9,False,No human participants; same modeling framework as local institution.,1,False,"Modeling, not experimental.",1,False,No physical lab environment.,1,True,Gradient fields and stationary distributions computed numerically.,0.95,True,Analytical structure of payoffs and transition probabilities provided.,0.95,200,Fig. 4 caption specifies Z=200.,1,1,As with local case: stage game is single-shot.,0.7,0,Players choose among fixed strategies with fixed contributions (C and P contribute c; D contributes 0).,0.9,N/A,No human-controlled initial contributions.,1,N/R,No linear public good multiplier is used.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/R,Model does not describe any presentation of summarized outcomes to human subjects.,0.5,N/A,Institutional punishment is anonymous and modeled; no identities.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/R,"Infinite-horizon process, not implemented as rounds for participants.",0.5,1,Punishment institution collects taxes from Ps and fines Ds when global threshold reached.,1,0.03,Punishment tax π_t=0.03 per punisher as in Fig. 4 caption; same as local case.,1,0.3,Punishment fine π_f=0.3 per defector when institution active; same as local case.,0.9,0,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,No reward mechanisms.,1,1,Endowment b=1 specified in Fig. 4 caption.,1,N/R,"No numeric reporting of normalized contribution rates; results are qualitative trajectories over (i_C, i_P).",0.6,N/R,No explicit average contribution amounts given.,0.6,N/R,Paper states local institutions are more efficient than global but without giving numeric efficiency values.,0.6,N/R,No aggregate payoff numbers presented for global-institution condition.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Same simulation framework as local-institution model.,1,"Same population and game as local institution: Z=200, N=8, M=6, c=0.1, b=1, β=5, μ=0.05, M_I=25%, π_t=0.03, π_f=0.3, with risk r=0.2 (low) and r=0.5 (high). Difference is in punishment scale: for a group with k_C cooperators, k_P punishers and N−k_C−k_P defectors, payoffs are Π_D=b P + (1−r)b(1−P) − Π_global, Π_C=b P + (1−r)b(1−P) − c, Π_P=Π_C−π_t, where P=Θ(k_C + k_P − M) and Π_global=π_f Θ(i_P − M_I) depends on total number of punishers in the population i_P instead of group-level k_P. Dynamics show coexistence of full defection attractor with cooperative states depending on risk.",Summarized from Sec. 5 description of global institution and Fig. 4 caption.,0.9
10.1257_mic.5.2.213.md,Analytical repeated trust game with private information,"The paper presents a single formal model: a two-player repeated trust game with private information, analyzed analytically across several equilibrium constructions (HSSGL, SSE, hybrid), but these are theoretical conditions within one analytical framework rather than separate empirical or simulated experiments.",0.86,"Model parameters of the repeated trust game: probability each player is investor p, success probability of investment q, investment return factor k, common discount factor β; structural assumptions about availability of immediate reciprocity, public randomization, and equilibrium class (HSSGL, SSE, hybrid).","Throughout Sections I–IV the authors vary p, q, k, and β and consider different institutional assumptions (immediate reciprocity allowed or not, presence/absence of public-randomization device, and whether equilibria are HSSGL, SSE, or hybrid). These are the theoretical independent variables; there are no treatment arms with human subjects.",0.93,False,The article develops a game-theoretic model and analytical equilibrium characterizations only; no human-subject data or experiments are conducted.,1,False,"No controlled lab, online, or field experiment with participants is run; all results are theoretical.",1,False,There is no laboratory setting or physical experimental environment; only analytical derivations.,1,False,The paper does not report numerical or agent-based computer simulations; it uses closed-form analytical methods.,0.99,True,"The core of the paper is analytical derivation of PPE, self-generating sets, HSSGLs, SSE, and hybrid equilibria in a repeated game with private information, using tools from Abreu-Pearce-Stacchetti and related theory.",1,2,"Section I defines a stylized model with two players, a and b, interacting in the repeated trust game.",1,-1,The game is an infinitely repeated (discounted) interaction; no finite number of rounds is specified. Using -1 to denote indefinite/infinite horizon.,0.95,1,"Stage game: an investor may send any x ∈ [0,1] of income, so contributions are continuous, not all-or-nothing binary.",1,N/A,This is not a public-good or contribution game with default allocations between private/public accounts; it is a bilateral trust game with income potentially invested.,1,N/R,MPCR is not defined as such. The investment produces kx with probability q and is lost otherwise; no per-capita public good multiplier is reported or directly applicable.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects; communication protocols among experimental subjects are not applicable. The model assumes public signals and private information but not chat.,1,N/R,"In a theoretical model there is common knowledge of the structure and observed public histories (T_t, R_t). The paper does not frame this as showing numerical summaries in an interface; that concept is not applicable in the empirical sense.",0.6,N/R,"Punishment in the sense of negative reciprocity or symmetric punishment phases is modeled as continuation payoff changes, not as identifiable punishment actions between anonymous IDs in a lab interface.",0.6,N/R,"Immediate reciprocity is modeled as transfer r or s in the trust game, but not in a context where identities of punishers/rewarders are experimentally hidden or revealed.",0.6,N/R,Infinite-horizon discounted games typically assume common knowledge of the discount factor but not a known finite number of remaining rounds; the paper does not discuss displaying remaining periods to participants.,0.7,1,"Theoretical mechanisms include symmetric punishment phases and lower continuation payoffs after certain histories, interpreted as punishment or negative reciprocity.",0.95,N/R,"Punishment is implemented via continuation value changes, not an explicit costly punishment action with a specified per-unit cost in tokens.",0.7,N/R,"No explicit punishment technology (e.g., each punishment point subtracts X) is parametrized; punishment is endogenous via equilibrium continuation payoffs.",0.7,1,The model includes immediate reciprocity (sending back r or s when investment succeeds) and dynamic reciprocity via higher continuation values; both are forms of rewarding.,0.95,N/R,"When a trustee reciprocates by sending r or s out of kx, the cost is implicit but not parameterized as a separate unit cost per reward point.",0.7,N/R,"No explicit reward technology like 'one token given costs one and gives other three' is specified apart from the kx return structure; reciprocity is flexible within [0,kx].",0.7,1,"In the stage game, the selected player receives income of $1 when they are the investor.",1,N/R,"The paper does not report empirical average contribution rates; x and y are strategic choices in equilibrium expressions, not measured data.",0.7,N/R,"Values of x and y are solved symbolically (e.g., x=1, y=(β−β*)/(β+β*)), but no empirical realized amounts or averages are reported as outcomes.",0.7,N/R,They compare theoretical total payoffs to first-best and Nash benchmarks but do not provide a single numeric efficiency measure (0–1) as an experimental outcome.,0.7,N/R,"Expressions for total utility T are derived symbolically (e.g., T=p[2+(x+y)(qk−1)]/(1−β)), but no concrete numerical payoffs from data are reported.",0.7,N/A,There are no human participants; thus no participant nationality.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved; age is not applicable.,1,N/A,No empirical participants; gender composition is not applicable.,1,N/A,No participant pool; education variables do not apply.,1,No human,The work is purely theoretical with no empirical or field setting.,1,"Infinite-horizon two-player repeated trust game with private information. Each period, with probability p player a gets income 1, with probability p player b gets income 1, and with probability 1−2p neither gets income. An informed investor may invest any x∈[0,1]; investment succeeds with probability q and yields kx to the trustee, or fails and yields 0. Trustee privately observes success and can reciprocate r∈[0,kx]. Players observe public histories of transfers and reciprocal payments and have private information about income and investment success; they share common discount factor β. The paper analyzes perfect public equilibria via self-generating sets, characterizing Highest Symmetric Self-Generating Lines (HSSGL), Strongly Symmetric Equilibria (SSE), and hybrid equilibria (honeymoon plus favor-exchange or symmetric punishment), including cases with and without immediate reciprocity and with or without public randomization devices.",Summary based on the formal model description in Section I and the subsequent equilibrium constructions in Sections II–IV.,0.98
10.1098_rsif.2019.0127.md,"Simulation – Non-observable institutions (9 unconditional strategies, default parameters)",Paper’s section 3.1 describes evolutionary simulations with nine unconditional strategies where institutions are not observable; Figure 2 reports results for a specific default parameter set.,1,"Institution observability: non-observable; Strategy set: 9 unconditional strategies including pro- and anti-social institutions and loner; Game optionality: optional public goods game; Parameters: n=5, c=1, r=3, γ=0.7, β=1.5, σ=1, N=50, μ=0.001, ω=10, k=1","Condition is defined by lack of observability (actions cannot depend on institutions), inclusion of anti-social institution, and the listed numerical parameters (given in text and Figure 2 caption).",1,False,"Study uses an evolutionary Moran process and computer simulations with strategies, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational evolutionary model.,1,False,No lab or online participants; only simulations.,1,True,Section 2.2 explicitly describes numerical simulations of a Moran process over strategies.,1,True,"Section 3.1 includes analytical payoff expressions and stability arguments, though the focal results here are simulation-based.",0.8,5,Default parameters state n=5 in Figure 2 caption.,1,1,"The public goods interaction per generation is a single-stage PGG within the three-stage institutional game; repeated over evolutionary time, but game itself is one-shot per group formation.",0.6,1,"Players choose to contribute c or not; there is no continuous contribution choice; treated as binary but within this coding they can choose any of the two discrete options, which is closer to ‘any amount’ than forced all-or-nothing of the whole endowment.",0.5,N/A,No human endowments or default allocations; only strategy-defined contributions in a theoretical model.,1,0.75,"MPCR = r/(n-1) because contributions are multiplied by r and split among the other n-1 players (self-excluded). With r=3 and n=5, MPCR=3/4=0.75.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modelled.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human players; theoretical payoffs only.,1,N/A,No individual identities; punishment is institutional and symmetric in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards are modelled.,1,N/A,No human subjects or interface; evolutionary time has no displayed round count.,1,1,Model explicitly includes institutional punishment (pro- and anti-social).,1,0.7,Cost γ is paid by supporters when punishment takes place; γ=0.7 in default parameters.,1,1.5,Punishment fine β multiplied by number of supporters; β=1.5 given in default parameters.,1,0,Only punishment institutions are modelled; no rewards discussed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Players either contribute c or not; with c=1 (default parameters) treat c as the effective per-round endowment for contribution decisions.,0.7,N/R,"Figure 2 reports stationary distributions of strategies, not explicit normalized contribution rates; a precise average fraction cannot be extracted numerically from text.",0,N/R,"Individual actions are binary (0 or c), but mean contribution amount across population not reported.",0,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (total payoff relative to maximum) is given.,0,N/R,Payoff formulas are provided but no single aggregate numeric group payoff outcome is reported for this condition.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary simulations with no subjects.,1,"Optional public goods game with institutional punishment and loner option; institutions not observable so actions are unconditional; parameters n=5, c=1, r=3, γ=0.7, β=1.5, σ=1, mutation μ=0.001, selection intensity ω=10, population N=50, threshold k=1 for institution implementation.",Summarized directly from model description in sections 2.1–2.2 and Figure 2 caption.,1
10.1098_rsif.2019.0127.md,"Simulation – Observable institutions (20 consistent conditional strategies, k=1, default parameters)",Section 3.2 analyzes 20 consistent conditional strategies with observable institutions; Figure 3 uses default parameter set with k=1.,1,"Institution observability: observable; Strategy set: 20 consistent conditional strategies including pro- and anti-social institutions and optional loner; Game optionality: both non-optional subset and optional game are discussed; Parameters: n=5, c=1, r=3, γ=0.7, β=1.5, σ=1, N=50, μ=0.001, ω=10, k=1","Condition defined by observability (actions can condition on institution presence), restriction to consistent conditional strategies, and default parameters carried over from earlier sections.",1,False,Model uses strategies in an evolutionary Moran process; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; theoretical + simulation.,1,False,No lab environment; purely computational.,1,True,They implement a computational evolutionary model with finite population N=50.,1,True,Section 3.2 derives analytical expressions for stable coexistence (x* and institution implementation probability).,1,5,Default group size n=5; also used for analytical expressions.,1,1,Single public goods interaction per group per evolutionary step; not repeated strategic rounds.,0.6,1,Players choose to contribute fixed amount c or defect; binary action.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments with default allocations.,1,0.75,"Same public goods production technology as in model definition: MPCR = r/(n-1) = 3/4 for r=3, n=5.",1,N/A,No players or communication channel; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No UI or feedback to humans.,1,N/A,No notion of personal identity; institutions are abstract.,1,N/A,No rewards modelled.,1,N/A,No display of rounds to players.,1,1,Model includes pro- and anti-social institutional punishment with visibility.,1,0.7,Institutional supporters pay γ when punishment takes place; γ=0.7 default.,1,1.5,Each target is fined β per supporter; β=1.5.,1,0,Only punishment institutions in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,"As before, treat contribution cost c=1 as effective per-period endowment.",0.7,N/R,"They report that a small fraction of institutional supporters can induce high levels of cooperation and give probabilities of pool implementation, but not explicit average contribution fraction normalized to endowment.",0,N/R,No explicit average contribution amount reported numerically.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio (payoff relative to maximum) provided.,0,N/R,"Analytical payoffs for strategies are described, but no aggregate numeric group payoff outcome is given.",0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Finite-population evolutionary simulations only.,1,"Optional public goods game with institutional punishment; institutions observable so strategies can condition on whether (and which) pools are implemented. Strategy space restricted to 20 consistent conditional strategies (including loner L). Analytical coexistence between a minority of pro-social supporters (W◦C◦C) and opportunists (NDC◦◦); for default parameters and k=1, equilibrium fraction of supporters x*=(γ/(rc−c))^(1/(n−1)) and pool implementation probability 1−(x*)^n.","Summarized from section 3.2, including analytical expressions and description of stable coexistence.",1
10.1098_rsif.2019.0127.md,"Simulation – Observable institutions, full 65-strategy space, k=1, default parameters",Section 3.3 describes simulations with the full strategy set of 65 strategies; Figure 4 shows a time series for k=1 and default parameters.,1,"Institution observability: observable; Strategy set: full 65 strategies (all conditional, consistent and inconsistent, plus loner); Game optionality: optional public goods game; Parameters: n=5, c=1, r=3, γ=0.7, β=1.5, σ=1, N=50, μ=0.001, ω=10, k=1",Condition differs from previous by allowing the entire 65-strategy space; observability and parameters same as earlier sections.,1,False,Again a purely computational evolutionary model.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,They implement the same computational model on the full 65-strategy space; Figure 4 shows simulation results.,1,True,Analytical reasoning from 3.2 still applies; 3.3 uses simulations to verify robustness rather than new derivations.,0.7,5,Same default group size n=5 as in other simulations (explicitly noted for the parameter set in Figure 4).,1,1,Single PGG per group per evolutionary step.,0.6,1,Binary contribute-or-defect actions as in the base game.,0.8,N/A,No human endowment setting.,1,0.75,"Same production technology; r=3, n=5 implies MPCR=3/4.",1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No feedback display.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rounds shown to participants.,1,1,Model includes both pro- and anti-social pool punishment institutions.,1,0.7,γ=0.7 as before.,1,1.5,β=1.5 as before.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Use c=1 as effective per-period endowment for contribution choice.,0.7,N/R,"They state that 23% of institutional supporters induce cooperation in 73% of all games, but do not give the normalized average contribution across all players.",0.2,N/R,"Binary contributions and implementation frequency reported, but no explicit average contribution amount.",0,N/R,No efficiency index is presented.,0,N/R,No explicit average group payoff is given for this condition.,0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulations only.,1,"Full 65-strategy space including all possible institutional support and conditional contribution patterns plus loner. Institutions observable; threshold k=1. For the parameter set shown, about 23% of institutional supporters induce cooperation in 73% of all games over a window of 2×10^5 time steps (Figure 4). System spends most time in stable coexistences analogous to those found in the restricted 20-strategy set.",Directly from section 3.3 and Figure 4 caption and description.,1
10.1098_rsif.2019.0127.md,Simulation – Observable institutions with higher threshold k=2 (conditional strategies),Section 3.4 discusses the case where at least two supporters (k=2) are required for implementing punishment institutions.,0.9,"Institution observability: observable; Strategy set: conditional strategies (including those yielding coexistences like WCCDD and NDCDD); Game optionality: optional public goods structure as before; Parameters: n=5, c=1, r=3, γ=0.7, β=1.5, σ=1, N=50, μ=0.001, ω=10; Threshold k=2 for implementing institutions",Condition differs primarily by the implementation threshold k=2 instead of k=1 while keeping other game parameters as in default; text gives specific equilibrium fractions for k=1 and k=2.,0.8,False,This is still part of the computational evolutionary model without subjects.,1,False,"The analysis and simulations are theoretical, not experimental with humans.",1,False,No lab experiment.,1,True,Section 3.4 references simulations and pairwise analysis for k=2.,1,True,They relate this to threshold public goods games and analytically discuss equilibrium fractions and stability properties.,0.9,5,"They compare equilibrium fractions for k=1 and k=2 using the same default parameter set, which had n=5.",0.9,1,Same base game as in prior conditions: a one-shot PGG per group per evolutionary step.,0.6,1,Contribution decisions remain binary (cooperate with c or defect).,0.8,N/A,No human endowments.,1,0.75,No indication that r or n change relative to default; assume MPCR as 3/4.,0.8,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No interface to participants.,1,N/A,Punishment is institutional and anonymous.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,1,Institutional punishment remains part of game; only threshold k changes.,1,0.7,Uses same default γ in comparison between k=1 and k=2 (explicit in text: default set of parameters).,0.9,1.5,No change reported for β relative to default; assume β=1.5.,0.9,0,Rewards are never introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Contribution cost remains c=1 as per default parameters; treat as effective endowment.,0.7,N/R,"They state that the probability that the pool is implemented decreases slightly (approx. 72% vs 73%), but not the explicit contribution fraction conditional on pool implementation.",0.1,N/R,No numeric average amount of contributions reported.,0,N/R,Efficiency not quantified.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff measures given.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Still a simulation study.,1,"Observable institutional punishment with higher threshold k=2 for implementation. This increases equilibrium fraction of pro-social supporters from about 23% (k=1) to about 43% (k=2), while probability that the punishment pool is implemented decreases slightly from about 73% to about 72%. Additional coexistences (e.g., WCCDD with NDCDD) become possible but are unstable to invasion by a third strategy; only the coexistences analogous to k=1 remain stable.",Directly summarized from section 3.4 where these specific percentages and stability properties are discussed.,1
10.1016_j.leaqua.2019.04.002.md,Rustagi et al. 2010 – Ethiopian forest users public goods game (lab-in-field),Single experimental design described: two members from same forest user group play one-shot linear public goods game with 0–6 Birr contribution choice.,0.95,Partner’s contribution level in conditional contribution schedule (0–6 Birr) determining own planned contribution; role assignment (conditional vs unconditional) randomly determined ex post.,Only experimentally varied dimension within game is other’s hypothetical contribution in conditional decision; roles assigned randomly and not a treatment variable.,0.8,True,Experiment conducted with >600 human forest user group members in Ethiopia.,1,True,Controlled behavioral economic experiment with specified payoff function and random role implementation.,1,True,Described as a ‘lab experiment’ conducted with forest users; no indication of naturally occurring decisions without experimental control.,0.8,False,All data from human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulation mentioned.,1,False,"Section reports empirical results, not purely theoretical derivations for this game.",1,2,“Two members from the same group play a one-shot linear public goods game.”,1,1,Described explicitly as a one-shot public goods game.,1,1,"Players can contribute between 0 and 6 Birr, i.e., any integer amount, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each player has endowment of 6 Birr and can decide how much to contribute; endowment starts in private account.,0.9,0.75,Payoff: Π_i = 6 − C_i + 0.75(C_1 + C_2); marginal per capita return from 1 more unit contributed is 0.75 for each player.,1,0,Game is anonymous and no communication is mentioned.,0.8,0,No indication that players see summaries of others’ past behavior or earnings in this one-shot game.,0.8,0,No punishment stage exists in this game.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,1,One-shot game; number of rounds is inherently known to participants.,0.8,0,Design description contains only contribution decision and payoff function; no post-contribution punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No mechanism where players can spend resources to reward others.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,6,"Each player can contribute between 0 and 6 Birr and payoff formula uses 6 − C_i, implying initial endowment of 6 Birr.",1,N/R,"Paper reports proportions of CC and FR types, not average contribution levels.",0.2,N/R,"Only type shares (45% CC, 11% FR) are given; no mean contribution reported.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency statistics presented for this experiment.,0.1,N/R,No numerical group payoff outcomes reported.,0.1,Ethiopia,"Participants are members of forest user groups in Bale Mountains, Ethiopia.",1,N/R,No age information provided.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,N/R,No education information reported.,0.1,On site,Experiment conducted with community members in Ethiopian villages as a lab-in-the-field; not online and not purely observational.,0.8,One-shot linear public goods game with two members of same forest user group. Each has 6 Birr and chooses contribution 0–6. Payoff Π_i = 6 − C_i + 0.75(C_1 + C_2). Each participant makes both a conditional contribution schedule (as if second mover) and an unconditional contribution (as if first mover). One player’s unconditional and the other’s conditional decision (evaluated at that unconditional amount) are implemented with random role assignment.,Summarizes detailed design and payoff information provided in the text.,1
10.1016_j.leaqua.2019.04.002.md,Falk & Kosfeld 2006 – C10 treatment: Principal chooses trust vs minimum requirement,The text explicitly refers to ‘the C10 treatment’ where the minimum requirement is 10 units. That is a distinct experimental condition.,0.95,Principal decision: impose binding minimum investment of 10 (control) vs not impose minimum (trust). This row describes the overall C10 treatment aggregating over principal choices.,Only exogenous treatment parameter mentioned is minimum requirement set to 10; principal’s trust vs control choice is endogenous within treatment.,0.8,True,Game is played by human participants in a laboratory principal–agent experiment.,1,True,"Controlled lab experiment with specified roles, endowments, and strategy options.",1,True,Classic lab experiment; described as ‘economic experiments’ with experimental units converted to money.,1,False,No computational simulations described for this treatment.,1,False,"Section discusses empirical results from Falk & Kosfeld (2006), not purely analytical derivations.",1,2,One principal and one agent interact in each instance of the game.,1,1,Described as a one-shot encounter between principal and agent.,1,1,"Agent can invest any amount x between 0 and 120; continuous/interval choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Agent starts with 120 experimental units in private resources and chooses how much to invest in project.,1,2,"Each unit invested costs the agent 1 and yields 2 to principal; for the beneficiary (principal) the marginal return per unit invested is 2. Group size is 2, so MPCR per capita from the contribution to the public ‘project’ for the principal-beneficiary is 2. Using the beneficiary’s marginal return because only principal benefits monetarily.",0.6,0,No opportunity for communication between principal and agent is described.,0.8,0,No mention that participants see summaries of others’ earnings; they only know payoff functions.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism exists in this game.,1,0,No reward mechanism where players actively reward others.,1,1,"Game is explicitly one-shot, hence horizon is known.",0.9,0,Principals cannot spend resources to reduce agents’ payoffs; only constraint is minimum investment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No stage where principals can spend own payoff to reward agents beyond contractual structure.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,120,Agent has an endowment of 120 experimental units; principal’s endowment is zero.,1,N/R,"Paper text here reports distributions of motivated vs non-motivated agents and average principal payoffs, not normalized investment rates.",0.2,N/R,Only proportions investing >10 or <10 and a payoff-equalizing x=40 are mentioned; no mean x reported.,0.2,N/R,No summary efficiency measure (total surplus vs maximum) reported for C10 treatment.,0.2,N/R,"Text gives average principal payoff (e.g., 35 vs 46) but not full group (principal+agent) payoff; cannot reconstruct without agent average x and behavior by condition.",0.3,N/R,Country of lab is not specified in this excerpt; Falk & Kosfeld (2006) is known to be European but not stated here.,0.2,N/R,No age information reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,N/R,No information on education or student status in this summary.,0.1,On site,Standard economic lab experiment with experimental units converted to money; not online.,0.9,"Principal–agent game: Agent has 120 units, chooses investment x in [0,120] into project. Agent payoff Π_a = 120 − x. Principal payoff Π_p = 2x. Principal initially has endowment 0 and can decide to impose a binding minimum investment x̲ = 10 (C10 treatment) resulting in agent choice set [10,120], or leave agent unconstrained with [0,120]. Agents differ in motivation: ‘motivated’ agents would voluntarily choose x_m > 10 (many choose x=40, equalizing payoffs) absent control; ‘non-motivated’ choose x < 10 if allowed. Hidden costs of control are measured by comparing agent investments and principal payoffs between trust vs control decisions within C10 treatment.",Directly summarizes payoff functions and decision structure specified in the text for the C10 treatment.,1
10.1016_j.leaqua.2019.04.002.md,Kosfeld & Rustagi 2015 – Third-party punishment game with forest leaders,Single experimental game where leaders decide on punishment for all possible contribution profiles of two group members in a linear public goods game.,0.95,"First-stage contribution profile of two group members (each chooses 0, 2, 4, or 6 Birr) determining leader’s punishment allocation; this row aggregates over all such profiles to describe the punishment institution.","Within this game, only variation affecting leader’s decision is the pair of member contributions; no separate between-subject treatment arms are described here.",0.8,True,Game played by real leaders of Ethiopian forest user groups and two members from each group.,1,True,Structured third-party punishment game implemented with real monetary stakes.,1,True,A lab-style experiment conducted on site with real leaders; payoff-relevant choices in a controlled game.,0.8,False,No artificial agents or simulations; entirely human-subject experiment.,1,False,"Section reports experimental design and empirical correlations with field outcomes, not analytical modeling only.",1,3,"Two group members play the public goods game; a third participant, the leader, observes and can punish them.",1,1,Third-party punishment game is described as a one-shot decision over all hypothetical contribution outcomes; no repetition mentioned.,0.9,1,"In first stage, each group member can contribute up to six Birr, and in this elicitation variant the choice set is {0,2,4,6}, i.e., more than just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each member receives an endowment (implicitly 6 Birr as in earlier game) and chooses how much to contribute; starts in private account.,0.9,0.75,First-stage linear public goods game is explicitly stated to have same structure as earlier: Π_i = 6 − C_i + 0.75(C_1 + C_2).,1,0,No communication between group members or with leader is mentioned.,0.8,0,No feedback about others’ past earnings or punishments; only know payoff functions.,0.8,0,Group members do not learn actual punishment decisions of their leader; identities of punished not revealed to others.,0.9,0,No reward option in the game.,1,1,Game is one-shot and participants know structure; no repeated rounds described.,0.9,1,Leader can allocate deduction points that reduce members’ payoffs.,1,1,Each deduction point costs the leader 1 Birr.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces a member’s payoff by 3 Birr.,1,0,Leader can only deduct payoffs; no mechanism to increase a member’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,6,"First-stage game is ‘of the same type as introduced above’, which had endowment 6 Birr per member; leader has separate endowment of 10 Birr for punishment.",0.9,N/R,Text focuses on punishment patterns and leader types; does not report mean contributions of members in this specific game.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate contribution statistics provided; only mentions that CC types contribute less when leader is antisocial.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure for this game is reported.,0.1,N/R,No numerical data on combined payoffs of members and leader in the experiment.,0.1,Ethiopia,Leaders are from the same Ethiopian forest user groups in Bale Mountains discussed throughout.,1,N/R,No age information for leaders or members provided.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition of leaders or group members not reported.,0.1,N/R,No education information for leaders given.,0.1,On site,Described as experimental field evidence with leaders and group members in Ethiopian forest user groups; implemented in the field but with lab-style control.,0.9,"Two-stage third-party punishment game. Stage 1: Two members of a forest user group simultaneously choose contributions to a linear public good, with choice set restricted to {0,2,4,6} Birr. Payoffs: Π_i = 6 − C_i + 0.75(C_1 + C_2). Stage 2: Group leader, endowed with 10 Birr, can assign deduction points to each member for every possible contribution pair. Each deduction point costs the leader 1 Birr and reduces the targeted member’s payoff by 3 Birr. Leaders specify punishment for all hypothetical first-stage outcomes before learning actual contributions; actual payoffs are then realized using the contribution profile and corresponding pre-specified punishments. Leader punishment profiles are used to classify leader types (money-maximizing, equality, equality+efficiency, antisocial) and relate them to forest management performance.",Summarizes all structural rules and payoffs of the third-party punishment game as described.,1
10.1016_j.leaqua.2019.04.002.md,"Bauer, Kosfeld & von Siemens 2017 – Treatment T1 (identical teams A and B)",One of three explicitly named treatments (T1) in the self-selection experiment; teams A and B are identical.,0.95,Treatment T1: Team A vs Team B identical – marginal value of uninvested units is 5 in both teams.,"Across treatments, main independent variable is relative material attractiveness of teams via opportunity cost of investing; in T1 the two teams are identical.",0.9,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants choosing teams and investments.,1,True,Clear randomized treatment structure (T1–T3) and controlled game.,1,True,Described explicitly as a lab experiment.,1,False,No simulation; only human decisions.,1,False,Here only empirical design and results are presented.,1,N/R,Number of participants per team/session not specified in excerpt.,0.1,20,“Participants play in total 20 rounds in the experiment.”,1,1,Each round participants can invest any amount from their 10-unit resource; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Participants start each round with private resource of 10 units; decide how much to invest in generating donations.,1,N/R,Donation payoff is d_i = x_i * average x_j; mapping from donation to participant monetary payoff is via uninvested units only; no per-capita monetary return from contribution specified.,0.2,0,No communication or discussion between participants mentioned.,0.8,1,"After each round, participants receive feedback about number of participants and average investment in both teams in the previous round; this is a summary of others’ investments.",1,0,No punishment in the game.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Participants know they play 20 rounds total (stated in instructions).,0.9,0,No option to deduct or sanction others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No active rewarding choices; only charitable donation production and private payoff from uninvested units.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"“In each round, participants receive a private resource of 10 experimental units.”",1,N/R,"Only approximate average investment levels are reported (“a bit more than two units”), not normalized by endowment.",0.3,2,"Text: “In treatment T1 participants invest, on average, a bit more than two units in both teams.” Approximate mean contribution per round chosen as 2 to keep numeric; exact figure not given.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency measure or total donations vs maximum provided.,0.1,N/R,Monetary payoff per group depends on uninvested units; no aggregate reported.,0.1,N/R,Charity is German but participant location is not explicitly stated in this excerpt.,0.2,N/R,No age information provided.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,N/R,No education or student status information provided.,0.1,On site,"Described as a lab experiment, implying on-site laboratory setting.",0.9,"Repeated team-choice and investment game over 20 rounds. Each round, each participant chooses between team A and team B, then chooses an investment x_i ∈ [0,10] from their 10-unit private resource. Effective charitable donation to Deutsche Krebshilfe: d_i = x_i * (average x_j of others in same team). Private monetary payoff: 5(10 − x_i). In T1, teams A and B are identical: uninvested units earn 5 regardless of team. After each round, participants observe, for each team, the number of participants and the average investment in the previous round. At the end, one round is randomly selected for payment to participants and for actual donation.","Summarizes all structural aspects described for T1, including payoff and feedback.",1
10.1016_j.leaqua.2019.04.002.md,"Bauer, Kosfeld & von Siemens 2017 – Treatment T2 (self-selection, team B more attractive)",Second named treatment: T2 where team B has higher marginal value (7) for uninvested units; allows self-selection.,0.95,Treatment T2: Self-selection between team A and team B; marginal value of uninvested units is 5 in team A and 7 in team B.,Key manipulated factor is opportunity cost of investing across teams; self-selection present in T2.,0.95,True,Same experiment as T1 with human subjects.,1,True,Part of same controlled laboratory experiment with treatments.,1,True,Lab experiment setting remains unchanged across treatments.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,"Empirical treatment condition, not purely theoretical.",1,N/R,Number of participants per session/team not specified.,0.1,20,All treatments share same 20-round structure.,1,1,Investment decision is continuous up to 10 units.,1,0,Resources start as private and can be invested.,1,N/R,"As in T1, donations depend on product of own and others’ investments; no monetary MPCR reported.",0.2,0,No communication option described.,0.8,1,Same feedback as T1: number of participants and average investment by team.,1,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,1,Known 20-round horizon.,0.9,0,No sanctioning choices.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism beyond game payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same 10-unit private resource each round as T1.,1,N/R,"Only level in units (‘about five units in team A’, ‘two units in team B’) is given, not normalized.",0.3,N/R,"Two different teams with different averages (‘about five’ vs ‘two’), and no single overall average contribution is reported.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency statistics are provided.,0.1,N/R,Payoff depends on uninvested units; no aggregates reported.,0.1,N/R,Country not specified in excerpt.,0.2,N/R,No ages reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,N/R,No education details reported.,0.1,On site,Lab experiment; physical lab assumed.,0.9,"Same 20-round team-choice and investment game as T1. In T2, marginal value of each uninvested unit is 5 in team A and 7 in team B: monetary payoff is 5(10 − x_i) if in team A and 7(10 − x_i) if in team B. Donations to Deutsche Krebshilfe follow d_i = x_i * (average x_j in chosen team). Self-selection: Participants freely choose team A or B each round. Empirically, about 20% of participants choose team A and 80% choose team B over rounds. Average investment in team A rises to about five units, while remaining around two units in team B.",Summarizes specific modifications for T2 as described.,1
10.1016_j.leaqua.2019.04.002.md,"Bauer, Kosfeld & von Siemens 2017 – Treatment T3 (random team assignment, differential payoffs)",Third named treatment: T3 with same marginal values as T2 but without self-selection; teams assigned randomly.,0.95,Treatment T3: Random assignment to team A or B with marginal value of uninvested units 5 in team A and 7 in team B; no self-selection.,Independent variable is assignment regime (random vs self-selection) while keeping payoff asymmetry between teams.,0.95,True,Same lab experiment framework with human subjects.,1,True,Random assignment constitutes experimental manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in lab as part of the same study.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,This is an empirical treatment.,1,N/R,Number of subjects per session not provided.,0.1,20,Uses same 20-round structure as other treatments.,1,1,Participants choose any investment from 0 to 10 units.,1,0,Endowment is private each round.,1,N/R,MPCR not reported; donations determined by multiplicative function as in other treatments.,0.2,0,No communication described.,0.8,1,Participants again receive feedback on number of participants and average investment in each team each round (implied by design similarity).,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,20-round horizon known.,0.9,0,No sanctioning.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,Same 10-unit resource each round as other treatments.,1,N/R,Only statement is that average investments are ‘a bit more than two units’ in both teams; no normalized rate reported.,0.3,2,Described as ‘a bit more than two units’ in both teams; recorded approximately as 2 units.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff data reported.,0.1,N/R,Location of lab not specified.,0.2,N/R,Age not given.,0.1,N/R,Gender not reported.,0.1,N/R,No education data described.,0.1,On site,Same lab environment as T1 and T2.,0.9,"Same as T2 regarding payoffs: Each round, participants receive 10 units. If assigned to team A, uninvested units pay 5 each; if assigned to team B, uninvested units pay 7 each. Donations: d_i = x_i * (average x_j in assigned team). Unlike T2, participants cannot choose teams; instead, they are randomly assigned to team A or B in each round. With random assignment, average investments remain around a bit more than two units in both teams, indicating that the strong increase in team A’s investment seen in T2 is due to self-selection rather than incentive differences alone.",Summarizes modifications specific to T3 from the text.,1
10.1016_j.ecocom.2018.06.001.md,Baseline homogeneous agents – default parameters,"Represents the core ABM configuration with homogeneous agents (no skill or land heterogeneity) and default parameter values from Table 1, used throughout Sections 3.1–3.3 as the benchmark.",0.83,"Mean inflow Q̄ = 700000 m³, inflow variability σ_Q = 200000 m³, punishment strength ω/μ (varied in sensitivity analyses), initial proportion of cooperators C0 (varied), social capital λ (varied), relative price Pd/Pr (varied); agents homogeneous in skill (σ_ρ = 0) and land (σ_a = 0).",Model description (Sections 2.1–2.4) and Table 1 give default values; Sections 3.1–3.2 vary social and economic parameters around this baseline while keeping agents homogeneous.,0.74,False,Study is an agent-based model with no human participants; all results are simulations.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person environment; purely in silico.,1,True,Explicitly described as an agent-based model implemented and solved in MATLAB; all parameter sweeps are simulation runs.,1,True,"Water allocation optimization problems for cooperators and defectors are said to be solvable analytically with Kuhn-Tucker conditions, though main results rely on simulation.",0.67,100,Table 1: N (Community size) = 100.,1,2,"Model describes two harvesting seasons per year (rainy and dry); social dynamics repeat each year. At the core social dilemma level, agents allocate water across these 2 seasons.",0.7,1,"Water withdrawals w_i,r and w_i,d are continuous decision variables bounded by constraints; no indication of binary all-or-nothing choices.",1,N/A,"No human contribution game; water extraction decisions are continuous resource-use choices, not framed as contributing from an endowment.",1,N/R,Model is not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined or derivable in standard PG terms.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication mechanisms between real participants do not apply.,1,N/A,Agents are programmed entities; there is no UI or feedback design as in human experiments.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as utility losses μ and ω in encounters; there are no human identities to reveal.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled; only punishment.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents do not see a number of rounds as participants would.,1,1,Central mechanism: cooperators punish defectors incurring cost μ and imposing cost ω (Section 2.4).,1,400,Table 1: μ (unit cost of punishing) = 400 $.,1,600,Table 1: ω (unit cost of being punished) = 600 $; interpreted as the punishment impact per enforcement act.,0.9,0,Only sanctions of defectors are modeled; no positive rewards are described.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent; no associated cost parameter.,1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,"Agents have land a_i and a shared water inflow Q_r,t; there is no fixed token or monetary endowment as in PG games.",1,N/R,Outcomes reported are proportions of runs reaching full cooperation/defection and utilities/profits; no normalized contribution rates as in PG experiments.,1,N/R,Individual water withdrawals W or w_i are not summarized as an explicit dependent variable; focus is on equilibrium type (full-C vs full-D).,1,N/R,No explicit ratio of achieved total payoff to maximum feasible payoff is reported.,1,N/R,Only qualitative statements and plots of utilities; no numeric group payoff values are tabulated.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All work is based on an agent-based model solved in MATLAB; there is no empirical setting.,1,"Agent-based common-pool resource model with 100 farmers extracting stochastic river inflows Q_r,t across two seasons (rainy, dry). Cooperators use socially optimal water allocations and punish defectors; defectors maximize myopic seasonal profits. Social dynamics follow evolutionary imitation based on utilities including punishment costs. Key parameters: mean inflow Q̄, variability σ_Q, punishment strength ω/μ, initial cooperators C0, social capital λ (encounter rate), prices P_r, P_d, and costs c.",Summarizes the core structure and payoffs of the simulated CPR game as described in Sections 2 and 3.,0.9
10.1016_j.ecocom.2018.06.001.md,Skill heterogeneity – varying σ_ρ,Section 3.4.1 describes simulations where the standard deviation of the skill parameter σ_ρ is varied to introduce heterogeneous agent skills; this constitutes a distinct simulation condition relative to the homogeneous baseline.,0.82,"Standard deviation of skill parameter σ_ρ varied (higher σ_ρ = higher skill heterogeneity), combined with environmental parameters: mean inflow Q̄ (varied) in Fig. 7(a) or inflow variability σ_Q (varied) in Fig. 7(b); other parameters at or around defaults.",Section 2.2 defines ρ_i with mean ρ̄ and SD σ_ρ; Section 3.4.1 and Fig. 7 specify that the x-axis is σ_ρ and y-axis is either mean inflow or water variability.,0.78,False,Agent-based simulation without human subjects.,1,False,No empirical experimental protocol; in silico parameter sweeps.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab with participants.,1,True,Skill heterogeneity is implemented in the same agent-based model by sampling ρ_i from a distribution with SD σ_ρ.,1,True,Same underlying model in which water allocation problems can be solved analytically; results here rely on simulation.,0.67,100,Community size N = 100 is maintained for all simulations (Table 1; no alternative N mentioned).,0.98,2,Same two-season allocation structure (rainy and dry) as baseline.,0.7,1,Water decisions remain continuous; heterogeneity only affects productivity parameters.,1,N/A,No human contribution setup.,1,N/R,Not defined for this CPR ABM.,1,N/A,No human communication stage.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No human identities involved.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human time-horizon display.,1,1,Same punishment mechanism as in the baseline; only skills differ.,1,400,μ unchanged (Table 1); not stated otherwise for heterogeneity runs.,0.98,600,ω unchanged (Table 1).,0.98,0,No rewards introduced in heterogeneity analysis.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,"Agents possess land and skill, not a game-theoretic endowment.",1,N/R,Outcomes captured as percentage of runs reaching full cooperation vs full defection as in Fig. 7.,1,N/R,No explicit reporting of individual or average water withdrawals as DV.,1,N/R,"They discuss profitability and collapse of cooperation, but not payoff efficiency ratios.",1,N/R,Only qualitative profit and cooperation outcomes are shown (heatmaps).,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Skill heterogeneity simulations are agent-based and computational.,1,"Same CPR ABM as baseline, but each agent i has skill ρ_i drawn from a distribution with mean ρ̄ and varying standard deviation σ_ρ. Higher ρ_i increases yield for given water use. Simulations explore how increasing σ_ρ, in combination with changes in mean inflow Q̄ or variability σ_Q, affects the probability that the system reaches full cooperation vs full defection.",Synthesizes model extensions in Section 2.2 and their use in Fig. 7.,0.9
10.1016_j.ecocom.2018.06.001.md,Land inequality – land heterogeneity and share of large landholders,"Section 3.4.2 and Fig. 8 describe simulations varying land heterogeneity (SD of land) and the proportion of large landholders, interacting with environmental changes; these form a distinct set of conditions on top of the baseline.",0.83,"Land heterogeneity (standard deviation of land endowment σ_a varied) and proportion of large landholders (mixing proportion of two land-size Gaussians varied), combined with environmental parameters: mean inflow Q̄ (varied) and variability σ_Q (varied).","Section 2.2: land a_i is modeled with mean ā and SD σ_a and as a bimodal mixture for small (ā_S) and large (ā_L) landholders. Section 3.4.2 and Fig. 8: x-axis is land heterogeneity (σ_a or proportion large landholders), y-axis is mean inflow or variability.",0.78,False,All results derived from simulated agents.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting with participants.,1,True,Land inequality is introduced by specifying different land distributions in the ABM and running multiple simulations.,1,True,"Underlying optimization problems remain analytically solvable; however, inequality results are based on simulations.",0.67,100,Community size N remains 100 across scenarios (Table 1; no alternate N given).,0.98,2,"Two-season allocation per year (rainy, dry) is unchanged.",0.7,1,Water withdrawal decisions remain continuous for each land unit.,1,N/A,No human contribution framework.,1,N/R,Not framed as a linear public good.,1,N/A,No human communication stage.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No human IDs; punishment is an internal model process.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No human-visible round structure.,1,1,Same peer punishment mechanism is retained.,1,400,μ unchanged (Table 1).,0.98,600,ω unchanged (Table 1).,0.98,0,No reward mechanisms added in inequality analysis.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,"Land a_i serves as productive capacity, not a PG-token endowment.",1,N/R,Primary reported metric is the percentage of runs yielding full cooperation across combinations of land inequality and environmental parameters (Fig. 8).,1,N/R,No explicit reporting of water extraction volumes as dependent variables.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure is calculated.,1,N/R,"The analysis focuses on whether cooperation collapses under inequality, not on numeric group payoff values.",1,N/A,No human participants involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All inequality scenarios are implemented with simulated agents only.,1,"Same CPR ABM as baseline, with land endowments a_i drawn from either a unimodal distribution with varying SD σ_a or a bimodal mixture of small and large landholders characterized by means ā_S, ā_L and a varying proportion of large landholders. Profit is proportional to land, so inequality in a_i amplifies payoff differences. Simulations evaluate how increasing land heterogeneity and the share of large landholders, combined with changes in mean inflow Q̄ and variability σ_Q, affect the probability of sustaining full cooperation versus collapse to full defection.","Summarizes the land inequality extension (Section 2.2) and its role in the cooperation outcomes (Section 3.4.2, Fig. 8).",0.9
10.1007_s00182-014-0419-9.md,Analytical model of infinitely repeated Prisoner's Dilemma (baseline symmetric case),"The paper is purely theoretical; the main analysis concerns a generic symmetric infinitely repeated discounted Prisoner’s Dilemma with parameters (a,b,c,d,δ) and grim-trigger versus other cooperation equilibria.",1,"Discount factor δ and stage-game payoffs (a, b, c, d) varied parametrically to study when cooperation equilibria are risky or non-risky.","All results characterize regions in the parameter space S = {a,b,c,d,δ} and derive thresholds like δ̲ and δ* as functions of these parameters.",1,False,No human or animal participants; only game-theoretic modeling and proofs.,1,False,There is no controlled experiment; the work is theoretical.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely analytical.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,True,"The paper develops formal definitions, propositions, and theorems for repeated PD; all results are derived analytically.",1,2,Standard 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma throughout the model.,1,-1,Game is infinitely repeated with discounting; finite number of rounds is not defined.,1,1,"In PD each player chooses between two discrete actions (c or d), but there is no continuous contribution decision; the allOrNothing flag is defined for contribution games, so this mapping is not meaningful.",0.2,N/A,Model is not a contribution game with public vs private accounts; concept does not apply.,1,N/R,"No linear public goods structure or multiplier; only PD payoffs (a,b,c,d) are specified.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; analytical model only.,1,0,Information structure is standard perfect monitoring of actions and payoffs in repeated PD; there is no notion of experimental summaries shown to participants.,0.3,N/A,"No players or UI; punishments are strategy-contingent outcomes in the model, not choices between identifiable individuals.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,Infinite-horizon analytical game; no interface showing remaining rounds.,1,1,"Cooperative equilibria are supported by trigger strategies and other punishment paths (grim-trigger, defection wars, repentance, etc.), which function as endogenous punishment strategies in the repeated game.",1,N/R,"Punishment is via deviation to d with implied continuation payoffs, not via a priced punishment technology (no per-unit cost parameter is defined).",1,N/R,"No explicit mapping from punishment points to losses is parameterized; only outcome payoffs (a,b,c,d) and continuation values.",1,0,"No separate costly reward mechanism is modelled; cooperation is part of equilibrium strategies, not a distinct reward stage.",1,N/A,No explicit reward technology exists in the model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology exists in the model.,1,N/R,"The PD is specified directly in terms of payoffs (a,b,c,d), not initial endowments of tokens or money.",1,N/R,No empirical data or contribution decisions; only theoretical cooperation vs defection actions.,1,N/R,No experimental contributions are measured.,1,N/R,Theoretical analysis compares payoffs and riskiness; no empirical efficiency statistic is reported.,1,N/R,No realized payoffs from experiments; only symbolic payoffs and parametric expressions.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is purely theoretical; no lab, field, or online environment.",1,"Infinitely repeated discounted 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with symmetric payoffs (a,b,c,d) satisfying b>c>d>a and 2c>b+a; focus on cooperative equilibria (e.g., grim trigger, tit-for-tat, defection wars, repentance) and their riskiness relative to all-defect equilibrium, deriving critical discount factor δ* = (b - a - (c - d)) / (b - a) and concept of risk perfection.",Summarizes the core analytical game structure and concepts introduced in Sections 2–5 for the symmetric case.,1
10.1007_s00182-014-0419-9.md,Analytical model of infinitely repeated Prisoner's Dilemma (asymmetric payoffs and discount factors),"Section 6 extends the baseline model to an asymmetric PD with player-specific payoffs (a1,b1,c1,d1) and (a2,b2,c2,d2) and discount factors δ1, δ2.",1,"Player-specific discount factors δ1, δ2 and payoff parameters (a1, b1, c1, d1, a2, b2, c2, d2) varied to characterize region S^ω where all cooperation equilibria are risky.","The section defines S over these parameters and characterizes S^ω, including a projection in Figure 1 and formulas for δ̲i and δ* when δ1 = δ2.",1,False,This extension is also purely theoretical.,1,False,No data collection; only mathematical characterization.,1,False,No lab or field component.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations; only analytical expressions and one illustrative figure based on formulas.,1,True,"Section 6 derives conditions and closed-form expressions (e.g., δ*, X,Y,Z,W) analytically.",1,2,"Still a 2-player PD, but with asymmetric parameters.",1,-1,Infinitely repeated game with player-specific discounting.,1,1,"Binary action set {c,d}; no graded contribution; mapping to contribution framework is not substantive.",0.2,N/A,Not a contribution game with an endowment split.,1,N/R,No public goods or linear multiplier is defined; only PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No communication or human subjects.,1,0,"Information structure is part of the theoretical game (monitoring of actions), not an experimental interface.",0.3,N/A,No experimental identities or UI are involved.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism as a separate stage.,1,N/A,Infinite horizon analytic model; no display of rounds.,1,1,"Cooperative equilibria require punishment paths (e.g., grim-trigger) in the asymmetric setting as well; riskiness is computed relative to all-defect.",1,N/R,"No explicit cost-per-punishment-unit parameter; punishments are encoded in continuation payoffs driven by (a_i,b_i,c_i,d_i,δ_i).",1,N/R,No separate technological mapping from punishment decisions to losses is parameterized.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding technology in the asymmetric model.,1,N/A,No reward stage or cost is defined.,1,N/A,No reward stage or technology is defined.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are directly specified by (a_i,b_i,c_i,d_i); no underlying endowment is described.",1,N/R,No empirical contributions measured; theoretical cooperation rates are not quantified as a DV.,1,N/R,No data on amounts; only theoretical actions c or d.,1,N/R,"Although efficiency is discussed conceptually, no numeric efficiency outcome is reported as an empirical DV.",1,N/R,No realized group payoffs from participants; only symbolic outcomes.,1,N/A,There are no participants.,1,N/A,There are no participants.,1,N/A,There are no participants.,1,N/A,There are no participants.,1,No human,Section 6 is a theoretical extension of the analytical model; no experimental environment.,1,"Asymmetric 2-player infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs (a1,b1,c1,d1) and (a2,b2,c2,d2), with conditions b_i>c_i>d_i>a_i and c1 + c2 > max[b1 + a2, a1 + b2]. Each player has own discount factor δi. The analysis defines S (admissible parameters) and characterizes S^ω where all cooperation equilibria are risky using the lower bound on riskiness ρ̲(s) = (d1 − a1)(d2 − a2) − [(c1/(1−δ1) − b1 − δ1 d1/(1−δ1)) (c2/(1−δ2) − b2 − δ2 d2/(1−δ2))] > 0, and shows substitution between players’ patience.",Summarizes the asymmetric game’s structure and main theoretical characterization.,1
10.1007_s00182-014-0419-9.md,Analytical reduced-form repeated Cournot duopoly (collusion vs Cournot competition),"Section 7 applies the riskiness framework to a repeated Cournot duopoly with linear demand, reduced to a 2×2 PD-like stage game with actions q^c (collude) and q^d (Cournot).",1,"Discount factor δ in the repeated Cournot game, with fixed reduced-form PD payoffs (a,b,c,d) = (54X, 81X, 72X, 64X) after normalization X=1.",The section computes δ̲ and δ* for these specific payoffs and studies when collusive equilibria are risky vs non-risky.,1,False,This is a theoretical application to a Cournot model; no data are collected.,1,False,No experimental implementation; only analytical mapping and thresholds.,1,False,Purely theoretical Cournot game.,1,False,No numerical simulation of agents or trajectories; only analytic formulas.,1,True,Derives PD payoffs from linear demand Cournot model and calculates δ̲ and δ* analytically.,1,2,Duopoly with two firms.,1,-1,The Cournot interaction is considered as an infinitely repeated supergame with discount factor δ.,1,1,Stage game is reduced to two discrete quantity choices (q^c or q^d); no continuous or fractional contributions.,0.7,N/A,Firms choose quantities; there is no notion of contributing an endowment to a public good.,1,N/R,This is not a voluntary contribution mechanism; MPCR is not defined.,1,N/A,No experimental communication protocol; this is a theoretical oligopoly model.,1,0,"Theoretical firms observe actions/payoffs via standard Cournot assumptions, not via an experimental UI.",0.3,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or identities beyond two firms; model is analytical.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Infinite-horizon theoretical game; no interface with round information.,1,1,Collusion in the repeated Cournot PD is supported by trigger-like strategies where deviation is punished by reversion to Cournot quantities; these are endogenous punishments in the model.,1,N/R,"Punishment is implemented via choosing competitive quantities, not through a separate costly punishment technology with explicit per-unit cost.",1,N/R,No explicit technological parameter maps punishment choices to counterpart losses; losses result from standard Cournot payoffs.,1,0,"There is no separate costly reward stage; higher profits from mutual collusion are equilibrium outcomes, not chosen rewards.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism defined.,1,N/R,Firms have profits from demand; no fixed token or money endowment per round is specified.,1,N/R,Outcome of interest is whether collusive equilibria are risky for given δ; no empirical or simulated contribution rate is reported.,1,N/R,"Quantities (q^c, q^d) are defined but not observed as a data variable in an experiment.",1,N/R,Efficiency is discussed in terms of collusion vs Cournot but not quantified as a numeric DV with empirical data.,1,N/R,Joint profits from collusion vs Cournot can be computed from model but no empirical group payoff is measured or reported as data.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Section 7 is an analytical application to Cournot duopoly, not an experiment.",1,"Reduced-form 2×2 stage game derived from linear-demand Cournot duopoly with actions q^c (joint monopoly quantity) and q^d (Cournot quantity). After normalization X=1, payoffs are λ = (a,b,c,d) = (54, 81, 72, 64). The repeated game is infinitely repeated with discount factor δ. Using the general formulae, the paper states that (δ̲, δ*) = (9/17, 19/27) ≈ (0.53, 0.7). For δ in (δ̲, δ*), all collusive equilibria are risky; for δ ≥ δ*, some collusive equilibria are non-risky and some remain risky.",Summarizes how the Cournot model is reduced to a PD and the key threshold results on riskiness.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.91.062804.md,Numerical simulation – resource inflow hysteresis on complete graph (N=50),"Condition described in Fig. 2: hysteresis driven by variation of resource inflow c, with well-mixed population represented by complete graph, N=50.",1,Resource inflow c varied from 0 to 60 in steps of 0.1; network is complete graph (well-mixed) with N=50,"Text for Fig. 2 specifies 0 ≤ c ≤ 60, increment 0.1, N=50, complete graph.",1,False,Study is a numerical and analytical model of socio-ecological dynamics with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; only simulations of equations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Results are from numerical simulations of the coupled social-ecological model on networks.,1,True,Paper includes analytical derivations and fixed-point analysis; this condition is primarily numerical but within an analytical framework.,0.8,50,Fig. 2 caption: population of size N=50.,1,10000,Each point is run for a period of 10^4 time steps for the system to reach equilibrium (Fig. 2). Use 10^4 as number of update steps per c value.,0.8,1,Agents do not make discrete contribution choices; they have fixed effort levels ec and ed; contribution choice all-or-nothing vs continuous is not defined; marked as 1 (any amount) by default for non-game simulations.,0.2,N/A,"No endowments or public goods contributions; model is effort-dependent production, not a PGG.",1,N/R,No public goods linear multiplier or MPCR is defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication.,1,0,Agents update via local comparison of utilities; no interface or summaries exist.,1,N/A,"No explicit individual punishment decisions; ostracism is a modeled payoff term, not an action with IDs.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,No participants or interface; time horizon is not displayed to agents.,1,1,Social ostracism term O(n_c) reduces defectors’ utility; functions as punishment.,1,N/A,Ostracism is not modeled as costly actions chosen by cooperators; no explicit per-unit cost.,1,N/A,No discrete per-unit punishment technology; ostracism directly modifies payoffs.,1,0,Only sanctions/ostracism are modeled; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Model uses effort and resource states, not initial monetary endowments.",1,N/R,"Outcome variables reported are fraction of cooperators f_c and resource level R*, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,No individual contribution amounts are reported; agents have fixed effort types.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,1,N/R,Paper discusses payoffs conceptually but does not report aggregate payoff values.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely numerical/analytical model with no subjects.,1,"Coupled social-ecological model with N=50 agents on a complete graph; cooperators exert effort e_c = e_eff, defectors e_d = e_nash; production F = γ E^α R^β with diminishing returns; asynchronous pairwise comparison updating with mutation every N^2 selection events; resource inflow c varied quasi-statically 0–60 to generate hysteresis in fraction of cooperators and resource level.",Summary of model description and Fig. 2 condition.,1
