10.1007_s10683-011-9292-6.md,"One-to-one punishment treatment (public good with one active punisher per target, periods with one-to-one structure)",Ten periods used a one-to-one punishment structure after contribution stage; same across sessions though order with sequential varied.,1,Punishment technology = one-to-one; each subject can effectively punish exactly one other member; both requested and carried-out punishment recorded.,"Design: in one-to-one treatment each subject submits two punishment requests, but a random matching selects exactly one active punisher per target.",1,True,Human subjects play contribution then punishment stages.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment technology and sequence; random rematching and random assignment of targets.,1,True,Lab sessions at University of Siena using z-Tree.,1,False,"This is an actual human-subject treatment, separate from the later simulation based on its data.",1,False,Not purely theoretical; payoff functions are used but tested experimentally.,1,3,Groups of n=3 individuals in all periods.,1,10,Ten periods for the one-to-one punishment structure within each 24-period session.,1,1,Contribution choice 0 ≤ g_i ≤ 20; flexible continuous integer contributions.,1,0,Endowment starts fully in private account; contributing is voluntary.,1,0.6,Same linear public good technology with a = 0.6.,1,0,No communication allowed among subjects.,1,1,After contribution stage subjects saw others’ contributions and earnings; punishment feedback showed aggregate punishment on each member.,1,0,Identities were not revealed; feedback at end of period excluded information that would reveal punishers.,1,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,1,"Instructions reference three parts and total periods, implying subjects know or see period numbers.",0.7,1,After contribution stage there is an opportunity to buy deduction points against group members.,1,0.25,Each punishment point costs c = 1/4 token to punisher.,1,1,Each punishment point requested 