10.1007_s10683-011-9292-6.md,"Sequential punishment treatment (public good with two-step sequential punishment, periods with sequential structure)",Ten periods used a sequential punishment design where each subject moves in two steps and can punish two different partners.,1,Punishment technology = sequential two-step; subjects observe earlier-step punishment on a target before later-step decisions.,"Design: in sequential treatment, punishment decisions occur in two ordered steps with information about prior punishment.",1,True,Conducted with human participants in lab.,1,True,Punishment sequence is experimentally manipulated and compared to one-to-one punishment.,1,True,On-site lab experiment at University of Siena using z-Tree.,1,False,"This treatment uses human subjects, distinct from simulation analysis.",1,False,Predictions are analytical but the treatment itself is experimental.,1,3,Groups of n=3 in all periods.,1,10,Ten periods are allocated to the sequential punishment structure.,1,1,Contributions are between 0 and 20 tokens inclusive.,1,0,Endowment starts fully private each period.,1,0.6,Same linear public good technology applies with a=0.6.,1,0,No communication allowed in any treatment.,1,1,After each period subjects observe others’ contributions and earnings and aggregate punishment.,1,0,Feedback on punishment is aggregate and anonymous; identities not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,1,Subjects know experiment has 3 parts and 24 periods; typical z-Tree interface shows period count.,0.7,1,"After contribution stage, subjects can buy deduction points against others in sequential steps.",1,0.25,Each punishment point costs c=1/4 token to the punisher in all punishment conditions.,1,1,Each point of punishment reduces the target’s payoff by 1 token; multiple punishers’ points cumulate.,1,0,Design only includes deduction (punishment) points.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Endowment y=20 tokens per subject per period.,1,N/R,"Contribution distributions compared graphically (Fig. 1) and via non-parametric tests, but no numeric mean rate provided for sequential only.",0,N/R,Average contribution levels by treatment not numerically reported.,0,N/R,No treatment-specific efficiency measure is reported.,0,N/R,No explicit group payoff figures per treatment.,0,Italy,Subjects are University of Siena undergraduates.,1,N/R,Age distribution not given.,0.3,N/R,Gender not reported.,0,Undergraduate university students,Recruitment description for subject pool.,1,On site,Computerized lab experiment using z-Tree in person.,1,"After contribution, punishment occurs in two steps. In step 1, a subject punishes one partner and predicts how many additional points that partner will receive in step 2 from the third member. In step 2, the subject punishes the other partner after observing how many points that partner already received in step 1. Order in the sequence is randomized each period. Subjects can choose p_ik in {0,…,10} for each of two opponents; both punishment choices are always carried out and cumulate on the target. At period end, subjects see aggregate punishment they received and aggregate punishment on others, but not individual sources.",Summarizes the sequential punishment protocol including belief elicitation and information structure.,1
10.1007_s10683-011-9292-6.md,Instrumental punishment simulation based on one-to-one treatment data,Authors construct a simulation using one-to-one punishment data to predict instrumental punishment in sequential design.,1,Punishment motivation = instrumental (γ_i = 1); simulated sequential punishment decisions derived from one-to-one standalone punishment data.,Section 5 describes a simulation assuming instrumental utility u_i = π_i + α_i ln(p_ik + E[p_-ik]).,1,False,No new human decisions; uses previously collected data as inputs for a numerical simulation.,1,False,Not a separate experimental condition; it is an ex post simulated benchmark.,1,False,No new lab interaction; computation only.,1,True,Explicitly labeled as a simulation on one-to-one treatment data to generate predicted sequential punishment.,1,False,Uses a specified functional form and regression-based expectations; not closed-form derivations only.,1,3,Simulation mimics the experimental environment with groups of size n=3.,1,10,Simulation maps one-to-one periods into a hypothetical 10-period sequential punishment environment.,0.7,1,Simulated contributions and punishments are based on original design where contributions are 0–20 tokens.,1,N/A,No new endowment allocation; simulation only manipulates punishment mapping based on observed data.,1,0.6,Simulation uses same payoff structure as empirical game (a=0.6).,1,N/A,No live communication or interface; numerical simulation only.,1,N/A,No participants receive feedback in the simulation.,1,N/A,No human subjects and no feedback; identities irrelevant.,1,N/A,No rewards or identities in simulation.,1,N/A,No user interface in simulation; round information not displayed to any subject.,1,1,Simulation models punishment choices and outcomes under sequential punishment rules.,1,0.25,Uses same cost parameter c=1/4 as in experiment.,1,1,Simulated punishment reduces target payoff one-for-one as in design.,1,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in model.,1,N/A,Rewards not part of simulated technology.,1,20,Simulation takes contribution and payoff environment as in the real experiment (y=20).,1,N/R,"Simulation focuses on punishment levels; contributions are inputs from one-to-one data, not separately summarized.",0,N/R,No new contributions generated; underlying contributions from one-to-one treatment not restated here.,0,N/R,Only aggregate punishment predictions reported; no efficiency metric calculated.,0,N/R,Group payoffs under simulated instrumental behavior are not provided.,0,N/A,No human participants in this simulation condition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational simulation using regression estimates and utility assumptions.,1,"Simulation assumes u_i = π_i + α_i ln(p_ik + E[p_-ik]). Expectations E[p_-ik] estimated via OLS individual random effects regression on one-to-one treatment data using others’ average contributions, own deviation from group average, session and period dummies, plus a dummy for contributions above 15. Aggregated prediction: simulated sequential punishment yields average 0.56 points in step 1 and 1.56 in step 2, total 2.12 per contribution decision.",Summarizes modeling assumptions and reported output in Table 1 and related text.,1
10.2307_3005886.md,Discount 0.7 – Grim Required,Condition with discount parameter .7 and mandated grim trigger strategy in the 2x2 design.,1,"Discount parameter = 0.7; Punishment phase requirement = Grim Required (once Z chosen, cannot choose Y again)",From description of 2x2 factorial: Discount (.7 or .8) crossed with Strategy (No Required Length or Grim Required).,1,True,Human subjects recruited from university classes.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of discount and trigger strategy in a lab game.,1,True,Subjects seated at computer terminals at the university; standard lab setting.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulation described.,1,False,Paper contains theory but the reported results are from lab experiments with humans.,1,4,"Each group had two men and two women, implying 4 players.",1,5,"Expected number of trials for discount .7 is 1/(1-0.7)=3.33, but text’s example mapping probability .3 to ~3 trials suggests actual expected length 1/p; probabilities used were .3, .2, and .05. For .7 discount condition, probability of ending per trial is .3 so expected trials ≈ 3.33; however, they explicitly say for p=.20 expect 5 trials and for p=.30 expect about 3–3⅓. Using that mapping, expected trials for .7 ~3.33; choose nearest integer 5 would be wrong, but any exact fixed round number is not set; they use random stopping. Therefore CONFIG_numRounds refers to fixed known rounds, which here do not exist.",0,1,"Actions are binary: Y (cooperate) or Z (defect). Players choose one of two options, an all-or-nothing contribution decision.",1,0,Standard binary public good with no pre-assigned contributions; endowment is fully private until choosing Y. Treat as 0 (all in private) by definition.,0.7,N/R,Only payoff matrix is given in tokens; no explicit linear public good multiplier or marginal per capita return is reported or derivable as a constant.,0,0,No communication between subjects is described; only individual computer decisions.,0.9,1,"After each trial, computer reported decisions of other group members, earnings per trial, and cumulative earnings; this is feedback about others.",1,0,Punishment is via group-level defection; no individual punishment identity display is mentioned.,0.9,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond standard payoffs; no identities revealed.,1,0,"Number of trials is random via chip draw; subjects know probabilities, not exact or remaining number of rounds.",1,0,No separate costly punishment stage or action; only strategic punishment via defection; per schema this does not count as a punishment mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment option.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment option.,1,0,No costly reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No explicit reward option.,1,N/A,No explicit reward option.,1,N/R,"Game is described only via payoff matrix, not via initial tokens/endowment under subject control.",0,0.63,"Table 7: mean proportion of cooperative responses for Grim Required, discount .7 is .63; this is the share of Y choices over all trials.",1,N/R,Only proportions of Y vs Z choices reported; no token amounts per Y decision.,0,N/R,No total payoff relative to maximum reported by condition.,0,N/R,Only expected payoffs under theoretical assumptions and individual-level expectations given; no realized group payoffs by condition.,0,USA,Large public university in the southwest; Texas A&M University is in the United States.,0.9,N/R,Described as students from introductory classes; no specific age range reported.,0.4,"Mixed (2 men, 2 women per group)","Text: each group had two men and two women, except one all-male group.",1,Undergraduate university students,Subjects recruited from introductory social science and humanities classes.,0.95,On site,Subjects reported to the study and were seated at a computer terminal in a lab-like setting.,1,"4-player repeated binary public good game using payoff matrix (Table 1). Discount parameter represented via random stopping with end probability 0.3 each round (7 red, 3 white chips). T