10.1371_journal.pone.0155773.md,Weak player – symmetric game,Paper states each of 120 subjects played two games: one symmetric (partner of same type) and one asymmetric (partner of different type). This row isolates behavior of weak players in symmetric games.,1,Player type = weak; Game type = symmetric; Punishment technology = 1:1 fee-to-fine,Weak vs strong types and symmetric vs asymmetric games are the main between-condition variables; weak players always have 1:1 punishment ratio.,1,True,Human participants (120 students) played the game in the lab.,1,True,"Game was pre-programmed and conditions (weak/strong, symmetric/asymmetric) were randomly assigned and counterbalanced.",1,True,Conducted in the experimental laboratory at UCL Department of Economics.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model tested; focus is on behavioral data.,1,2,Described as a two-player modified iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma where players interacted in pair-wise encounters.,1,50,Methods: 'The modified IPD game lasted 50 rounds.',1,1,"Players could choose any cooperation time between 0 and 5 seconds; thus variable, not binary, investment (not an all-or-nothing restriction).",1,0,Each round starts with full endowment available in private account and players choose how long to ‘cooperate’; there is no pre-allocation to a public fund.,0.7,N/R,Game is a 2-player PD with a termination bonus; no linear public good or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,Interactions were via computer with no mention of communication channels.,0.95,1,"After each round, players saw each player's payoffs for step 1 and step 2 plus cumulative payoffs for both players.",1,1,"At end of step 2 players were shown their own and their partner's choice and payoff from step 2, so they know who punished whom.",1,0,No reward/positive sanction mechanism is present in the design.,1,0,Players were told game would last 20–100 rounds to avoid end effects; no indication that exact remaining rounds were displayed.,0.8,1,Step 2 explicitly gives an option to punish the partner.,1,1,Weak players: 'if they chose to punish their partner it would cost them one unit'.,1,1,Weak players punish with a 1:1 fee to fine ratio: 1-unit cost causes 1-unit loss to partner.,1,0,No reward or positive transfer option is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the game.,1,100,All players began each game with 100 units to play with.,1,N/R,Analyses report cooperation levels but not normalized as fraction of maximum (0–5) in a way that yields a single reported rate per condition.,0.2,2.51,"Table 2 lists 'Cooperation level' for weak symmetric as 2.51 (median, with IQR). This is the raw cooperation amount (seconds).",0.9,N/R,Authors explicitly focus on behavioral rather than payoff consequences and do not report efficiency ratios.,1,N/R,No summary of average total group payoff per round or per game by condition is provided.,1,United Kingdom,Experiment conducted at University College London with UCL student subject pool; most likely UK-based participants though nationality not explicitly listed.,0.6,Mean 20.89 years (SE 0.20); student sample,Methods: 'mean age ± se = 20.89 ± 0.20 years'; participants recruited from student population.,1,"71 women, 49 men (overall sample; not broken down by condition)",Reported for full sample of 120 participants; no per-condition breakdown.,1,University students,Participants recruited from the student population at UCL.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental laboratory with partitioned computers.,1,"Two-step iterated PD with variable cooperation (0–5 s). Step 1: both choose cooperation duration; minimum choice sets interaction length, both earn 1 unit per second of mutual cooperation; player choosing minimum gets 6-unit termination bonus (split 3–3 if equal). Step 2: optional punishment; this condition uses weak-type 1:1 punishment (1-unit cost, 1-unit fine). Opt-out option in step 1 skips round with no payoffs.","Summarizes payoff matrix and sequence from Methods and Table 1, restricted to aspects that define the game structure.",1
