pQ.,0.6,0,This section only adds punishment; incentives (V) are handled separately in Section 4.2.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment; only general profit and cost terms.,0.8,N/R,"Main outcome is that only (C,C) remains as evolutionary equilibrium when pQ is sufficiently large; no numeric cooperation rate is reported.",1,N/R,No contribution-level variable defined.,1,N/R,They discuss 'win-win' and cooperation but do not compute normalized efficiency ratios.,0.9,N/R,No numeric total payoff; only symbolic conditions.,0.9,N/A,"Theoretical model, no participants.",1,N/A,"Theoretical model, no participants.",1,N/A,"Theoretical model, no participants.",1,N/A,"Theoretical model, no participants.",1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game with punishment; no empirical environment.,1,"Two-player evolutionary game with same strategy set (C,D) but modified payoffs including punishment: if one cooperates and the other defects, the defector pays expected penalty pQ and the cooperator gains pQ. Replicator dynamics updated accordingly. Stability analysis shows four equilibria (0,0), (1,0), (0,1), (1,1); when pQ > max{c1+f1, c2+f2}, only (1,1) (cooperation, cooperation) remains as evolutionary stable strategy.",Synthesizes description around Table 4 and associated text in Section 4.1.,1
10.1155_2017_1676045.md,Evolutionary game with incentive mechanism,"Section 4.2 describes 'Evolutionary Game Based on Incentive Mechanism' introducing rewards V, with payoff matrix in Table 5.",1,"Presence of external mechanism: incentive mechanism with monetary reward V for cooperation. Condition V > max{c1+f1, c2+f2}.",This condition differs from baseline and punishment case by adding incentive V to cooperative payoffs and analyzing its size relative to costs.,1,False,No application to human subjects; still theoretical.,1,False,Analytical modification of the model; no empirical experiment.,1,False,No experimental lab context.,1,False,No computational simulations reported; only analytical stability results.,0.96,True,Replicator dynamics are re-derived with V and equilibria are analyzed.,1,2,Still two prefabricated producers.,1,0,Infinite/continuous evolutionary time horizon as before.,0.8,1,Strategy set remains binary cooperation vs competition.,0.9,N/A,No contribution endowments and no human subjects.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods framework.,1,N/A,No human interaction protocol.,1,0,Not specified as part of the theoretical model.,0.9,N/A,"Although pQ appears in Table 5, this section's focus is incentive V; in any case there are no identifiable human agents.",1,N/A,"Reward V is a generic monetary reward from government/partners, not modeled as identity-targeted in an experiment with participants.",1,0,"Continuous-time replicator dynamics, no discrete round count displayed.",0.9,1,"Table 5 for incentive mechanism still includes terms with pQ, implying both punishment and incentive operate in this model.",0.9,N/R,"As in punishment model, p and Q are parameters; no per-unit punishment cost specified.",0.6,N/R,No unit punishment effectiveness defined; only expected penalty pQ.,0.6,1,Section 4.2 introduces government monetary reward V for cooperation and includes V in cooperative payoffs.,1,N/R,Model specifies size of reward V but not the per-unit cost of delivering it; no finer structure.,0.6,N/R,"Reward is a lump-sum addition V to payoff, not defined per coin or unit.",0.7,N/R,No explicit endowment variable; payoffs use profit and cost parameters.,0.8,N/R,"Outcome statement: when V > max{c1+f1, c2+f2}, (1,1) (cooperation, cooperation) is the only equilibrium; they do not report numeric cooperation levels.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They discuss enhanced economic benefits and 'win-win', but no normalized efficiency metric is calculated.",0.9,N/R,No numeric group payoff is reported; only qualitative improvements.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects in this condition.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Theoretical evolutionary game with incentive and punishment; no empirical environment.,1,"Two-player evolutionary game where cooperative players receive additional reward V from government or partners. Payoff matrix adds V to cooperative outcomes and retains punishment terms pQ when unilateral defection occurs. Replicator dynamics re-derived with V, and stability analysis shows that when V exceeds max{c1+f1, c2+f2}, only full cooperation (1,1) is an evolutionary stable strategy, analogous in phase portrait to the punishment-only case.",Summarizes model structure from Table 5 and discussion in Section 4.2.,1
