y earnings are given; group payoffs not reported.,0.2,United States,Experiment conducted at University of Massachusetts Amherst.,1,N/R,Age of subjects not mentioned.,0,Proportion male: 0.5,"Table 2: No punishment, Heterogeneous: Male = 0.5.",1,University students (specific level N/R),Student sample; no more detail.,0.8,On site,Lab experiment with physically present students.,0.9,"Same interior-solution non-linear public good as homogeneous treatments (π_i = w(e_i - x_i) + (1/n)[a Σx_j - b(Σx_j)^2], w=1, a=6, b=0.025, n=4). Endowment distribution: two group members have 60 EDs (high types), two have 40 EDs (low types), fixed across all periods. Subjects know the composition but not who is high/low in feedback stage. Demonstrated efficient and equal-earnings contributions: high 35 ED, low 15 ED, giving each 112.5 EDs per period.",Taken from design description and Table 1.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2015.12.002.md,"HetPun (Heterogeneous endowment, with punishment)",Condition label and description: heterogeneous endowment with punishment; named as HetPun.,1,"Endowment distribution: heterogeneous (two 60 ED, two 40 ED); Punishment: present (1 ED costs target 3 EDs)",Heterogeneous endowments as in HetNoPun plus addition of punishment stage.,1,True,Human-subject public good experiment.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of both endowment distribution and punishment institution.,1,True,Conducted with students in controlled lab environment.,1,False,No separate simulation study.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical paper; includes experiments.,1,4,Groups of four subjects.,1,15,15 paid periods after a practice period.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer contribution between 0 and their endowment.,1,0,Endowment initially in private account; group contribution is chosen.,0.9,N/R,Non-linear public good; no MPCR reported.,1,0,No opportunities for communication are described.,0.9,1,"After each period subjects saw own and others’ contributions (anonymous IDs), total group contribution, and earnings before and after punishment.",1,0,Punishment directed at other players by their anonymous IDs; no real identity revealed.,0.7,0,No rewards in the design.,1,0,Not mentioned whether round count was displayed.,0.3,1,Subjects could pay to impose sanctions reducing others’ earnings.,1,1,Cost to punisher is 1 ED per punishment unit.,1,3,Each unit of punishment received reduces target’s payoff by c=3 EDs.,1,0,No beneficial transfer or reward stage.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the design.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the design.,1,50,Mean per-person endowment is 200/4=50; heterogeneity between 40 and 60 ED is detailed in independent variable and other_game_info.,0.6,0.338,Average contribution in HetPun is 16.9 EDs (Table 3); divide by mean 50 ED endowment: 16.9 / 50 = 0.338.,0.8,16.9,"Table 3: Heterogeneous, Pun., All periods = 16.9 EDs (pooled).",1,N/R,They compare average monetary earnings across conditions but do not provide normalized efficiency relative to maximal possible earnings.,0.1,N/R,Average individual payoffs are given; aggregate group payoffs are not explicitly reported.,0.2,United States,Conducted at University of Massachusetts Amherst.,1,N/R,No explicit age information.,0,Proportion male: 0.63,"Table 2: With punishment, Heterogeneous: Male = 0.63.",1,University students (specific level N/R),All participants are students; study does not separate undergraduates and graduates.,0.8,On site,Lab environment at a university.,0.9,"Non-linear public good with punishment: π_i = w(e_i - x_i) + (1/n)[a Σx_j - b(Σx_j)^2] - Σ p_ij - c Σ p_ji, w=1, a=6, b=0.025, n=4, c=3. Two players per group are high-endowment (60 ED), two are low-endowment (40 ED). Types are fixed and common knowledge in aggregate but individual types are never revealed in feedback or punishment stage. Demonstrated efficient equal-earnings contributions: high 35 ED, low 15 ED. In punishment stage, subjects may pay 1 ED per unit to reduce any other member’s payoff by 3 EDs, constrained by their current-period earnings; multiple sanctions per target allowed, negative period earnings possible.","Synthesized from payoff, heterogeneity, and punishment descriptions.",1
10.1098_rstb.2020.0047.md,Agent-based simulation with endogenously evolving punishment in a public goods game,Only explicit micro-level experimental/simulation condition described is a single agent-based model where agents can introduce and adjust punishment in a PG game.,0.98,Proposed punishment fine level for free-riders (continuous parameter varied via random proposals each generation); initial proportion of cooperators fixed at 0.05; punishment cost-to-fine ratio fixed at 1:2; contribution rate fixed at 0.1 of budget.,"Simulation varies punishment level over time via proposals; other parameters (initial cooperator share, contribution rate, punishment ratio) are fixed model settings, not varied across conditions.",0.9,False,Described explicitly as an agent-based simulation with artificial agents and no human participants.,1,False,"No controlled study with human or field subjects; it is a computational model, not an experiment in the empirical sense.",1,False,No laboratory setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors state they use an agent-based model and present simulation outputs (100 iterations over 1000 generations).,1,False,No closed-form mathematical analysis of equilibria; results are from numerical simulation.,1,10,"Each agent has a budget of 10 units and contributes 10%; text does not specify group size. However, punishment is described as selecting an equal number of punishers as free-riders. The precise number of agents is not reported, so 10 is not supported by the text.",0,1000,They report “1000 generations of 100 simulation iterations”; interpret generations as repeated rounds of the game.,0.7,1,Economic game: agents “contributing a set amount (10%) of their budget (10 units)”; agents have fixed strategies of cooperator (always contribute 10%) or free-rider (contribute 0). No choice of intermediate contribution; thus effectively all-or-nothing strategies.,0.85,N/A,No human subjects; endowment allocation framing does not apply.,1,0.2,"Each cooperator contributes 10% of budget (1 unit). Contributions are “doubled and redistributed to all agents.” MPCR = (multiplier / group size). Multiplier is 2; group size not reported, so exact MPCR cannot be computed. 0.2 is an arbitrary placeholder and not supported by the text.",0,N/A,No human players; communication not defined for artificial agents in this sense.,1,0,"Agents base votes on payoff assessments but there is no notion of interface or displayed summaries; this variable applies to human UI, not simulations.",0.6,N/A,No human identities; peer punishment is modelled mechanically without identity information.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Agents do not have an interface displaying total or remaining generations; no such feature is discussed.,0.7,1,Core of the model is an institutional punishment fine that can be introduced and adjusted; starts at zero and becomes positive.,1,1,"Text: “free-riders pay a cost that is twice this amount” where ‘this amount’ is the cost to punishers. If fine is F and punisher cost is C, they state free-rider cost = 2C. But they never give numeric C; 1 is an arbitrary placeholder, not supported by explicit values.",0,2,"Per description, free-riders pay twice the cost borne by punishers, i.e. the punishment technology has a 1:2 cost-to-fine ratio.",0.85,0,Only punishment of free-riders is modelled; no reward or positive sanction mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,10,Economic game: “agents contributing a set amount (10%) of their budget (10 units)”; budget treated as endowment per round.,1,N/R,"They report qualitative dynamics of proportions of cooperators, not average contribution rates normalized by endowment across time.",0.2,N/R,"Mechanically, cooperators contribute 1 unit and free-riders 0, but no aggregate contribution statistics are reported as results.",0.3,N/R,They do not report numerical measures of total group payoff vs. maximum possible; only qualitative payoff trends.,0.1,N/R,Only relative payoff changes of cooperators vs. free-riders and qualitative increases are discussed; no numeric group payoff reported.,0.1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents only; no human participants or countries.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model only.,1,"Public goods game with fixed strategies: cooperators contribute 10% of a 10-unit budget, free-riders contribute 0; contributions doubled and evenly redistributed. Population starts with 5% cooperators. At each generation a proposed punishment fine (drawn from a normal distribution) may replace the current fine based on voting over predicted long-term payoffs. If fine > 0, in each round all free-riders are punished by an equal number of randomly selected obligate punishers who pay a cost; free-riders pay twice that cost. Payoffs determine reproductive success; group size fixed; simulation run for 1000 generations and replicated 100 times.",Summarizes explicit structural details of the simulated game and political mechanism as described in Section 3.,0.98
10.1016_j.jeem.2007.08.001.md,Section 5 numerical illustration – baseline parameterization,"Paper presents a single numerical example in Section 5 defined by specific parameter values (c(s)=1/s, g(s)=s+rs(1-s/K), r<1, K=100, p=0.05) and then varies N and δ in figures; treat the baseline numerical setup as one simulation condition.",0.7,"Number of players N and discount factor δ (varied across simulations to test Assumptions 2 and 3) with fixed biological and economic parameters (c(s)=1/s, g(s)=s+rs(1-s/K), K=100, p=0.05, r in {0.5, 0.8}).","Section 5 explicitly varies N and δ and, for different figures, r, while keeping the functional forms and K,p fixed to illustrate when cooperation is sustainable.",0.9,False,No human participants; Section 5 is a numerical example of the theoretical model.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is parameterized analysis of a model.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting with participants.,1,True,"Section 5 describes a numerical illustration and simulations over parameter values N, δ, r using the specified functional forms.",1,True,Core of paper is analytical derivations of equilibria and conditions; numerical illustration supplements this.,1,4,"Text for Fig. 1 states example with N=4 (r=0.5, K=100, δ=0.9, p=0.05, N=4).",0.9,0,"Infinite-horizon discrete-time game (t=0,1,...) so finite number of rounds not applicable; encode as 0.",0.9,1,"Harvest choice h_it is any nonnegative level, continuous; not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human players or public-goods endowment structure; simulation of harvesting decisions.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return concept for public goods not defined; model uses profit integrals and growth, not a linear MPCR.",1,N/A,No human interaction or communication; theoretical/simulation setting.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; only model variables.,1,N/A,No identities of human subjects; punishment is a strategy profile in a symmetric theoretical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in an experimental sense and no human identities.,1,N/A,"Not an implemented experiment; infinite-horizon model, no display of rounds.",1,1,Model includes a two-part punishment scheme (Phase II with harsh over-harvest followed by recovery).,1,N/R,"Punishment operates via harvest decisions affecting profits and stock, not via a separate costly punishment action with a per-unit cost parameter.",0.5,N/R,No separate punishment technology parameter like impact per punishment unit; punishment is embedded in harvest/payoff structure.,0.5,0,Strategies involve only cooperative harvesting and punishments; no explicit rewarding mechanism beyond normal payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Model does not specify a per-period endowment; agents choose harvest subject to stock dynamics.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are expressed as value functions and profits, not as contribution rates relative to an endowment.",1,N/R,Paper does not report specific numerical harvest paths or cooperative harvest amounts for the numerical illustration beyond qualitative characteristics.,0.7,N/R,"No explicit efficiency ratio reported; analysis focuses on whether cooperative (first-best) outcome can be supported, not on computed efficiency levels.",0.8,N/R,"Figures (e.g., Fig. 1) depict functions v(s) and π(s), but no specific numeric group payoff values are tabulated for the baseline case.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation study.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a theoretical model plus numerical simulations only.,1,"Dynamic common-property renewable resource game with N>=3 symmetric players, discrete time, stock dynamics s_{t+1}=g(s_t-h_t) with logistic growth g(s)=s+rs(1-s/K), stock-dependent unit cost c(s)=1/s, common price p, imperfect monitoring of individual harvests (only total harvest observable via stock levels), equal sharing of total returns, discount factor δ, cooperative strategy with optimal escapement z*, and a two-part symmetric punishment strategy (Phase I cooperation, Phase II over-harvesting to induce worst perfect equilibrium payoffs followed by recovery). Numerical illustration uses K=100, p=0.05, r in {0.5, 0.8}, δ around 0.9, and varies N (at least 3 and 4) and δ to map parameter regions where Assumptions 2 and 3, and thus cooperation as subgame-perfect equilibrium, hold.",Summarizes the specified model and simulation setup described throughout Sections 2–5 and figure captions.,1
