10.1088_1674-1056_19_10_100204.md,Classical PGG without punishment (varying K),The paper contrasts public goods games (PGG) with and without punishment across different values of the rationality parameter K; this row captures the classical PGG (no punishment) condition.,0.96,"Punishment mechanism: absent (no fines μ=0, ν=0); Degree of rationality K: varied (including K→0, 1.0 and a range shown in Fig. 2–3); Initial strategy frequencies: varied but shown not to affect equilibrium.",Text and figures explicitly compare PGG with and without punishment and show results as a function of K; punishment parameters μ and ν apply only when punishment is present.,0.94,False,Model is defined analytically and explored via numerical simulations; no human participants are mentioned.,1.0,False,"Study uses theoretical model plus numerical simulations, not controlled experiments with subjects.",1.0,False,No laboratory or physical setting; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1.0,True,Section 3 states that numerical simulations are performed to illustrate the varying frequencies and payoffs; no empirical data.,1.0,True,Section 2 provides explicit payoff functions and differential equations (approximate best response dynamics) analyzed for equilibria.,1.0,6,"Parameters stated in figure captions: 'Parameters: N=6, r=3.6, c=1.25, ν=0.35 and μ=0.1.' N is group size (number of individuals in the game). Same N used for with/without punishment.",0.95,6000,Fig. 1 caption: 'Symbols indicate results for 6000 continuous steps.' Interpreted as number of simulation time steps/rounds.,0.8,1,"Model is binary-choice: 'must decide either to cooperate or to defect'; not an all-or-nothing contribution amount game with intermediate levels, but within this schema 1 corresponds to possibility of 'any amount', whereas here decision is discrete; however, there is no mention of multiple contribution levels, so treat as free choice vs no contribution rather than enforced all-or-nothing of endowment splitting.",0.4,N/A,"Simulation with binary cooperate/defect decisions and no explicit starting allocation between private/public accounts in a dynamic endowment sense; additionally, no human participants.",1.0,0.6,"In classical PGG description, total contributions are multiplied by r and shared equally among N. MPCR = r/N. Given r=3.6, N=6, MPCR = 3.6/6 = 0.6.",0.95,N/A,No human participants or communication channels; purely simulated strategies choosing actions based on dynamics.,1.0,N/A,No interface or feedback to human participants described; purely model-based; concept does not apply.,1.0,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition and no human subjects.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1.0,N/A,No human interface; number of simulation steps is not 'shown' to agents.,1.0,0,"This is the 'classical PGG' without punishment, repeatedly contrasted with 'PGG with punishment'.",1.0,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1.0,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1.0,0,Model only includes punishment; no rewards are described anywhere.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism included.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism included.,1.0,1.25,"Classical PGG setup states each individual can endow a fixed money c into a common pool; parameters list c=1.25, treated as the potential contribution/endowment per round.",0.9,N/R,"Only frequencies of cooperators and defectors are reported as outcomes, but not normalized contribution rates relative to endowment; since each cooperator contributes c and defectors contribute 0, contribution rate would coincide with cooperator frequency but exact numerical equilibrium values are not tabulated.",0.4,N/R,No explicit aggregate contribution amount is given in the text or figures; only frequencies of cooperators are shown.,0.3,N/R,"Average payoffs are discussed qualitatively and plotted, but no numerical values or ratios to the maximal possible payoff are reported.",0.3,N/R,"Figures show average payoffs per strategy, not explicit total group payoff values; no numeric group payoff is reported.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; purely computational/theoretical study.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,"Study uses mathematical modeling and numerical simulations, not experiments with human subjects.",1.0,Binary-strategy public goods game without punishment. Population of size N=6; each cooperator contributes c=1.25 to a pool multiplied by r=3.6 and divided equally among N. Evolution of strategy frequencies follows approximate best response dynamics with rationality parameter K; results shown across a range of K including K→0 and larger values.,Summarized from Sections 2–3 and figure captions describing the model and parameters.,0.95
10.1088_1674-1056_19_10_100204.md,PGG with punishment (varying K),Condition where cooperators can punish defectors using fines μ and costs ν; directly contrasted with classical PGG without punishment across different K.,0.98,"Punishment mechanism: present (fines μ=0.1 per cooperator; defectors incur cost ν per defector with ν=0.35); Degree of rationality K: varied (including K→0, 1.0 and a broader range); Initial strategy frequencies: varied but shown not to affect equilibrium.","Section 2 defines punishment with parameters μ and ν; Section 3 and figure captions specify parameter values (N=6, r=3.6, c=1.25, ν=0.35, μ=0.1) and discuss behavior as a function of K.",0.96,False,No human subjects; only analytical treatment and numerical simulations.,1.0,False,Not a controlled experiment; purely theoretical and computational.,1.0,False,No physical lab or field setting; just simulations.,1.0,True,Section 3 explicitly refers to numerical simulations illustrating cooperation frequencies and payoffs.,1.0,True,Formal payoff functions and differential equations are derived and used to characterize equilibria.,1.0,6,"Figure captions for results with punishment: 'Parameters: N=6, r=3.6, c=1.25, ν=0.35 and μ=0.1.' N is group size.",0.95,6000,Fig. 1 caption: 'Symbols indicate results for 6000 continuous steps.' Used for both with and without punishment runs.,0.8,1,"Agents choose between cooperation and defection; there is no partial contribution level described. Within this schema, contribution choice is not restricted to an exogenously forced all-or-nothing of a continuous endowment; instead it is a binary strategic choice, so coded as 1.",0.4,N/A,"Model describes direct strategic choice rather than an initial allocation between private and public accounts, and no human participants.",1.0,0.6,Same public goods production as in classical PGG: MPCR = r/N = 3.6/6 = 0.6.,0.95,N/A,Simulation agents do not communicate in the experimental sense; no human interface.,1.0,N/A,No feedback in