10.1038_s41598-017-18727-7.md,"Infinite well-mixed population – replicator dynamics with conditional punishment, low threshold (H=1)","Condition corresponds to infinite well-mixed replicator model including conditional punishers with low threshold H=1 as explicitly discussed in Figure 1(a,c) and related text.",1,"Presence of conditional punishers with observation cost (γ) and punishment parameters (α, β), threshold H=1 in an infinite well-mixed public goods game with four strategies (C, D, P, M).",Independent variables are the strategic composition and conditional punishment rule; H=1 is the key varied parameter for this condition.,1,False,Study uses theoretical replicator equations; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,False,No lab setting; mathematical analysis only.,1,True,Uses numerical integration of replicator dynamics (computational simulation) in an infinite well-mixed population.,1,True,Derives and analyzes closed-form replicator equations and equilibria.,1,5,Group size G=5 is explicitly stated for comparison with structured populations.,1,1,One-shot public goods interactions per generation are assumed in replicator framework; no repeated rounds within the model.,0.7,1,"Each cooperator/punisher contributes fixed amount c=1, defectors contribute 0; contribution decision is effectively all-or-nothing rather than continuous choice.",1,N/A,No human players; default endowment allocation concept not applicable in this analytical model.,1,0.6,"Marginal per capita return is r/G; for the parameter set in Fig.1 a,b they use r=3, G=5, so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6. This game structure is the same across H conditions.",1,N/A,No communication in theoretical replicator dynamics; also no human subjects.,1,N/A,Information display to human participants not applicable; no human experiment.,1,N/A,No identities in analytical model; not a human-subject game.,1,N/A,No reward stage or human identities.,1,N/A,No interface/round display to participants; theoretical model only.,1,1,Unconditional and conditional punishers impose fines α on defectors; described as costly punishment.,1,0.7,"Parameter β is the cost per punished defector to the community of punishers; for Fig.1(a,c) they specify β=0.7, α=1.0, γ=0.05, used to illustrate H effects.",1,1,Each punisher imposes fine α on each defector; in the main parameter set α=1.0 so each unit of punishment cost β (shared) corresponds to fine magnitude α=1 per punisher; we record α as the fine per punished defector per punisher.,0.9,0,"Model includes only costly punishment, no rewarding mechanisms.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,1,Each cooperator/punisher contributes fixed amount c=1; interpret this as full endowment per round.,0.9,N/R,"They analyze basins of attraction and frequencies of strategies, not explicit normalized contribution rates.",0,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as frequencies of strategies, not average contributions per player.",0,N/R,No explicit total payoff normalized by social optimum is reported.,0,N/R,"Analyses focus on equilibria and basins, not explicit payoff averages.",0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Infinite population replicator dynamics are purely theoretical.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population; four strategies C, D, P, M; group size G=5; contribution c=1; public good multiplied by r (1<r<G) and shared equally; punishment activated when at least one defector and one punisher present; conditional punishers pay observation cost γ and punish only if number of unconditional punishers ≥ H=1; punishment costs β shared equally among punishers.",Summarizes key structural aspects from Model and Results sections for this H=1 condition.,1
