Simulated agents.,1,No human,Structured population simulations only.,1,"Public goods game on L×L square lattice (L=100) with periodic boundaries, von Neumann neighborhood; overlapping groups of size G=5; each player in 5 groups; parameters r=2.0, c=1, α=1.0, β=0.8, γ=0.05 (γ irrelevant here without M), selection intensity s=2. Strategies C, D, P available; initial strategies assigned uniformly. Simulations show rapid dominance of D (D-only phase) with equilibrium average frequencies approx. f_C=0, f_P≈0.33, f_D≈0.67 (Fig.6(a)).",Summarizes structured-baseline configuration and main outcomes.,1
10.1038_s41598-017-18727-7.md,"Structured population – spatial public goods game with conditional punishment, low threshold (H=1)",Structured (lattice) population case with conditional punishers and H=1 in Fig.5 middle row and Fig.6(b).,1,"Presence of conditional punishers with observation cost γ and low threshold H=1 in a structured population (L×L lattice) alongside C, D, P strategies.",Independent manipulation vs baseline is the addition of M strategy with H=1.,1,False,Simulation-only study.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab/human environment.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on square lattice with local imitation.,1,False,Results for structured case shown numerically; no closed-form analysis.,0.7,5,G=5-player overlapping groups as specified.,1,N/R,They simulate many Monte Carlo steps; a fixed round count is not reported.,0.2,1,"C, P, and M contribute c=1; D contribute 0.",1,N/A,Model does not use human endowments.,1,0.4,"Same structured parameters: r=2.0, G=5, so MPCR=2/5.",1,N/A,No communication layer.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No identifiable human players.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,Rounds not displayed.,1,1,Both unconditional (P) and conditional (M) punishers can punish defectors.,1,0.8,"Structured simulation parameters: α=1.0, β=0.8, γ=0.05.",1,1,Each active punisher imposes fine α=1.0 on each defector; costs β shared.,0.9,0,No reward incentive.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Contribution c=1 per cooperating/punishing player.,0.9,N/R,"They report frequencies of C, D, P, M, not explicit contribution rates.",0,N/R,Contribution amounts not aggregated as a DV.,0,N/R,No payoff efficiency metrics given.,0,N/R,Group payoff levels are not provided numerically.,0,N/A,Simulation agents only.,1,N/A,Simulation agents.,1,N/A,Simulation agents.,1,N/A,Simulation agents.,1,No human,Structured population simulation without humans.,1,"L×L=100×100 square lattice, periodic boundaries, von Neumann neighborhood; overlapping groups of size G=5; parameters r=2.0, c=1, α=1.0, β=0.8, γ=0.05, s=2.0. Conditional punishers pay observation cost γ and punish if number of unconditional punishers in group ≥ H=1. Snapshots (Fig.5 middle row) and averaged frequencies over 30 runs (Fig.6(b)) show defectors initially expand but are later confined and eliminated, leading to coexistence of C and P in equilibrium, with M and D frequencies ≈0.",Summarizes structured low-H condition and its qualitative effects on cooperation.,1
10.1038_s41598-017-18727-7.md,"Structured population – spatial public goods game with conditional punishment, high threshold (H=3)","Structured (lattice) population case with conditional punishers and H=3, shown in Fig.5 bottom row and Fig.6(c).",1,"Presence of conditional punishers with observation cost γ and high threshold H=3 in a structured population with strategies C, D, P, M on a square lattice.",Independent manipulation is using high threshold H=3 for conditional punishment.,1,False,Agent-based lattice simulations; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field data.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on structured network.,1,False,No closed-form structured analysis presented; relies on simulations.,0.7,5,Each group has G=5 (focal + 4 neighbors).,1,N/R,They iterate Monte Carlo updates; do not specify a fixed round count.,0.2,1,Same binary contribution scheme c=1 vs 0.,1,N/A,No human endowment structure.,1,0.4,"Uses same r=2.0, G=5 parameters as other structured cases.",1,N/A,No communication.,1,N/A,No participant reports.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No displayed rounds; simulation only.,1,1,"Punishment mechanism still operates via P and, when activated, M.",1,0.8,"Structured simulation parameters same: α=1.0, β=0.8, γ=0.05.",1,1,Fine α=1.0 per punished defector per active punisher; costs β shared.,0.9,0,No rewards included.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same c=1 contribution per cooperator/punisher.,0.9,N/R,Reported outcomes are strategy frequencies; contributions are implied but not separately measured.,0,N/R,Contribution amounts not tabulated.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculations.,0,N/R,Group payoff metrics not reported.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Structured population Monte Carlo simulation.,1,"L=100 square lattice, von Neumann neighborhood, groups of size G=5; parameters r=2.0, c=1, α=1.0, β=0.8, γ=0.05, s=2. Strategies C, D, P, M initially distributed uniformly. Conditional punishers pay observation cost γ and punish only when number of unconditional punishers in group ≥ H=3, so they rarely engage. Spatial patterns (Fig.5 bottom row) and averaged dynamics (Fig.6(c)) show that system tends towards D-only phase; equilibrium frequencies similar to baseline but with lower P frequency (~0.15 vs ~0.33) indicating inhibition of cooperation.",Summarizes structure and main qualitative findings for high-H structured condition.,1
