10.1371_journal.pone.0068423.md,Simulation – coevolutionary quantum PD on evolving random network (baseline parameters),"Single main computational experiment: N=2500 agents on weighted, directed regular random network, coevolution of strategies and links; baseline parameters explicitly given (k_out=10, pn varied, w_min varied, b varied).",0.95,"Temptation to defect b (1 < b ≤ 2), probability of structural update event p_n ∈ {0, 0.2, 0.5, 1}, initial outdegree k_out ∈ {10, 20, 30}, punishment weight minimum w_min ∈ {0.1, 0.3, 0.5}","Paper systematically varies b, p_n, k_out, and w_min and reports resulting fractions of strategies and network statistics.",0.98,False,Study uses agent-based simulations with artificial agents on networks; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or field data; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No laboratory setting or human subjects.,1,True,Described as simulations of strategy evolution on networks with iterations up to 5×10^4 time steps and averaging over many realizations.,1,False,Model equations are defined but results come from numerical simulations rather than closed-form analysis.,0.9,2500,"Results section: 'coevolution starts from ... network G0(V,E) with N=2500 nodes'.",1,50000,Methods: 'iterated for a maximum number of t = 5×10^4 time steps'.,1,1,"Agents choose among discrete pure strategies C, D, Q1, Q2 without a continuous contribution amount; mapped as unrestricted choice among available strategies rather than binary all-or-nothing contribution, so treat as 1 (not restricted to single action).",0.4,N/A,No public goods or endowment-contribution structure; only pairwise PD payoffs.,1,N/R,"MPCR applies to public goods games; this is a pairwise Prisoner's Dilemma with parameters R, T, S, P, and no MPCR defined.",1,N/A,No human agents or communication; simulation of strategy update rules only.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents algorithmically observe neighbors' payoffs but not via displayed summaries.,1,N/A,Punishment is implemented as reduction of link weights between artificial agents; no identities shown to human participants.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism beyond link rewiring and weight adjustments within simulation.,1,N/A,No human interface; time horizon known only in code.,1,1,"Network evolution rules: agents 'punish' neighbors by setting weights on incoming links to w_min, reducing neighbors' payoffs.",1,N/R,"Model defines punishment as lowering link weights, but does not specify an explicit cost per punishment action to the punisher in payoff units.",0.2,N/R,No linear mapping like “1 coin removes X tokens”; only qualitative weight reduction to w_min.,0.2,1,"Rewiring to 'best friends' and upgrading weights on bidirectional links to 1 increases mutual payoffs, functioning as endogenous reward.",0.6,N/R,No explicit per-unit reward cost specified; rewiring has structural but not direct payoff cost parameter.,0.2,N/R,Reward implemented via setting weights to 1; no explicit token-per-cost mapping.,0.2,N/A,No token endowments; payoffs arise directly from pairwise PD quantum game interactions.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions; only discrete strategy choices in a PD.,1,N/A,No contribution quantities defined.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute total payoff relative to maximal cooperative benchmark; focuses on fractions of strategies and network statistics.,0.3,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are discussed qualitatively but no explicit group payoff values reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely computational with artificial agents and networks.,1,"Two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs R=1, T=b (1<b≤2), P=0, S=0 played as a maximally entangled 2×2 quantum game between connected agents on a weighted, directed, evolving random network. Strategies are C, D (classical) and two quantum strategies Q1, Q2 (parameterized unitary operators). 