: male-female breeding pairs in year 1; same-sex (male-male) pairs in year 2.,Text specifies both partner and competitor pairings.,0.95,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Same controlled aquarium setting as other inequity-aversion treatments.,0.8,"Control for sensitivity to the presence vs absence of own reward: actor performs tactile stimulation but receives no food while recipient gets flake. Same apparatus with empty plate unlocking a food plate, partition preventing physical interaction/punishment. Used to test whether behaviour changes when actor is unrewarded but partner is rewarded.",Summarizes description of the 'reward inequity' treatment introduced to distinguish value vs presence/absence effects.,0.96
10.1016_j.jebo.2015.12.006.md,Theoretical public good contribution model with peer discipline,"The paper presents only analytical/theoretical models; the key application is the linear public good contribution game analyzed in Section 4, not split into separate empirical conditions.",0.9,"Group size N; per-capita benefit of the public good s; feasibility/parameters of peer discipline (P, θ1, θ, π0, π, πp, σ0, σ); meeting cost parameters in extensions (κ0, κ(aR)); punishment spillover ψ; punishment size choices P1, P in Section 5.",Throughout Sections 2–5 the authors vary these parameters in comparative statics to characterize when full contribution aR=1 can be sustained; there are no human-subject treatment arms.,0.9,False,"No human participants, data collection, or experiments are reported; the paper develops formal models and comparative statics only.",1,False,No controlled experimental protocol with subjects; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are described; results are derived analytically.,1,True,"The work consists of formal analytical game-theoretic models and proofs (lemmas, theorems, comparative statics).",1,3,"Model assumes N>2 identical members; a specific numeric N is not fixed. Using the minimal feasible N (3) to keep numeric type, as an illustrative lower bound consistent with N>2.",0.3,0,The primitive game is one-shot at t=0; the subsequent auditing game has potentially unlimited rounds (infinite horizon). No finite numeric round count can be assigned.,0.2,1,"In the public good application each member chooses a∈{0,1}; while this is technically binary, within that model this is the full action space, so partial contributions beyond 0 or 1 are not allowed. Coding as 1 to reflect that not an all-or-nothing over a richer continuous endowment structure; but this is ambiguous.",0.2,0,"There is no explicit endowment allocation dynamic; players simply choose 0 or 1 cost to contribute. By convention, with no pre-allocation to public good, we treat it as 0.",0.2,N/R,"The model specifies a binary cost of contribution and group benefit s divided among N, but does not frame this as a standard MPCR parameter; no explicit MPCR is given.",0.1,0,"The model includes no communication stage among players beyond the abstract ‘meeting’ used for mechanism design; no endogenous, strategic chat during play is modeled.",0.6,0,Information structure is via abstract signals z_t and audit outcomes; no payoff or contribution summaries are mentioned.,0.8,0,All interactions are within a fixed group and network but anonymity or identity revelation is not modeled; punishments are not described as tagged to observable identities in a way matching this field.,0.3,0,"No reward mechanism is modeled, only punishment.",1,0,Players face stochastic continuation (δt); there is no suggestion that they observe a known finite horizon or remaining rounds count.,0.7,1,Core of the model is peer discipline via costly punishment with cost P to punished and θtP to auditor.,1,1,Punishment imposes cost P on the auditee. P is treated as a positive scalar parameter; using 1 as a canonical normalized unit since no specific numeric value is provided.,0.2,1,Magnitude of punishment effect per punishment unit is not parameterized separately from P; we treat 1 as a normalized mapping from 1 unit punishment to 1 unit cost.,0.2,0,Only punishment technologies are modeled; rewards are mentioned only in passing in the literature review.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,1,In the public good application each agent’s decision is a binary cost 0 or 1 to contribute; treating this ‘1’ as an effective endowment/cost unit for normalization.,0.3,N/R,Theoretical results characterize whether the equilibrium is full contribution aR=1 or zero contribution but do not report empirical contribution rates across rounds.,0.3,N/R,No empirical payoffs or contributions are reported; only symbolic utilities u(·) and conditions for full vs. zero contribution.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (utility expressions) but no normalized efficiency metric over 0–1 is explicitly computed or reported as a DV.,0.2,N/R,"Analytical expressions for individual utility U and some payoff conditions are given, but no single numeric DV for group payoff is instantiated.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical models.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical models.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical models.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical models.,1,No human,All results are derived from analytical game-theoretic models with no empirical implementation.,1,"Analytical model of a collusive group with N>2 identical members that plays a one-shot primitive game (e.g., linear public good with binary contribution a∈{0,1} and group benefit s shared among N), followed by an open-ended peer auditing and punishment game with noisy private signals z_t, punishment cost P for deviators, audit costs θ1P and θP, and endogenous continuation probabilities δ_t chosen by the group to sustain target actions aR via peer discipline. Extensions cover renegotiation costs of meetings, generalized matching networks for auditing, punishment spillovers ψ, and endogenous choice of punishment severity P under a spillover cost function.",Summarizes key structural features of the theoretical public good/peer discipline game as described in Sections 2–5.,0.95
10.1007_s10539-004-5155-2.md,Boyd & Richerson (1992) punishment-based n-player game – cooperate & punish strategy,Paper describes using the Boyd and Richerson (1992) punishment-based evolutionary game where the focal ESS strategy is to cooperate and punish non-cooperators (and punish non-punishers).,0.86,"Strategy type in evolutionary game; here: players follow strategy that (1) cooperates in the cooperation phase, (2) punishes non-cooperators, and (3) punishes non-punishers; number of players n is large (sizable group).",Condition is defined by the specific strategy analyzed as an ESS in the described punishment-based n-player game.,0.82,False,"Boyd & Richerson (1992) is explicitly described as an evolutionary model/simulation with populations of strategies, not human subjects.",0.97,False,No controlled assignment of human or observed units; it is a theoretical evolutionary game model.,0.97,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical/simulation work.,0.99,True,Described as an evolutionary model of strategies in an n‑player punishment game; this fits computer/numerical simulation.,0.9,True,The paper states they ‘analyzed strategies’ and showed the strategy is an ESS; this implies formal analytical derivations in addition to any simulation.,0.78,0,"Described as an n‑player game with n arbitrarily large, but no specific numeric n is reported here.",0.4,0,Game is ‘indefinitely repeated’ but no exact number of rounds is given.,0.4,1,"Players choose only actions Cooperate/Defect (and Punish/Not Punish), so there is no continuous contribution level; this corresponds to a binary/all-or-nothing cooperation decision.",0.7,N/A,No human endowment; abstract PD payoffs.,1,N/R,"Model is described via a generic Prisoner’s Dilemma payoff matrix (3,0,5,1), not as a linear public goods game with MPCR.",0.7,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocols; theoretical model.,1,0,Information structure is not discussed in terms of payoff summaries; model only specifies that players can condition punishment on previous actions.,0.5,0,No notion of identity revelation is specified; punishment is strategy-contingent at the model level.,0.5,0,No rewards are mentioned in the described Boyd & Richerson (1992) game.,0.9,0,Game is defined as indefinitely repeated; no mention that agents know or see the number of rounds.,0.6,1,Second phase is explicitly a punishment phase where each player chooses Punish or Not Punish.,1,0,"Punishment costs k to the punisher; k is a symbolic parameter, not numerically specified in this paper.",0.9,0,"Punishment inflicts harm h on the punished; h is symbolic, no numeric ratio per cost unit given here.",0.9,0,Model includes only punishment for non-cooperation and non-punishment; no rewards are described.,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Game is described only via PD-style payoffs; no explicit endowment per round is given.,0.4,N/R,Paper only states that the cooperate-and-punish strategy is an ESS; no explicit equilibrium cooperation frequencies are reported.,0.3,N/R,"Model is in terms of abstract Cooperate/Defect actions and payoffs, not concrete contribution magnitudes.",0.3,N/R,No explicit comparison of realized vs. maximum possible group payoff is reported.,0.3,N/R,No numerical aggregate payoff is given for the ESS outcome in the description here.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/evolutionary game model with no human participants.,1,"Indefinitely repeated n-player game with two phases per round: (1) cooperation phase with Prisoner’s Dilemma payoffs (3,3;0,5;5,0;1,1); (2) punishment phase where each player can choose to punish or not punish each other player based on prior actions; punishment costs k to punisher and reduces punished’s payoff by h; analyzed for large n, showing that a strategy that cooperates and punishes non-cooperators and non-punishers can be an ESS.",Summarizes the structure of the Boyd & Richerson (1992) punishment-based game as presented in the text.,0.94
10.1007_s10539-004-5155-2.md,Generalized punishment-based game for arbitrary social rules – comply & punish strategy,"Paper introduces a generalized punishment-based model (Figure 3 text) where the first phase is a Comply/Violate game for an arbitrary rule, followed by a punishment phase; the focal condition is that everyone complies and punishes violators.",0.84,Nature of the enforced social rule (arbitrary payoff structure in Comply/Violate phase) under a strategy profile where agents comply with the rule and punish rule violators (and may punish non-punishers).,Independent variable is the abstract rule/payoff structure; this row captures the described equilibrium where the rule is sustained by punishment.,0.78,False,"Described as a modification/extension of the theoretical punishment-based model, not as an empirical study.",0.98,False,No assignment or observation of human subjects; purely analytical modelling.,0.98,False,No lab or field setup; model only.,0.99,True,"Generalized as an evolutionary punishment-based game; follows the same modelling tradition as Boyd & Richerson (1992), which includes simulation-style population dynamics.",0.75,True,"Author notes that, following Boyd & Richerson (1992) and folk-theorem style reasoning, one can show equilibria where everyone complies and punishes; indicates formal analytical arguments.",0.86,0,Again described as an n‑player game with n potentially very large; no concrete n given.,0.4,0,Game is described as indefinitely repeated; exact round count not specified.,0.4,1,"In the rule-compliance phase, players choose discrete actions Comply or Violate, not continuous contribution levels.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment or contribution pool defined.,1,N/R,Generalized model is not expressed as a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is provided.,0.7,N/A,No communication among human subjects; abstract model only.,1,0,Information structure only mentions that punishment is conditional on others’ actions; no explicit display of payoff summaries.,0.5,0,No explicit modelling of identity revelation; punishment is defined at the strategy level.,0.5,0,No rewards or positive sanctions are mentioned in this generalized game.,0.95,0,"Indefinitely repeated interaction, with no statement that agents know the horizon.",0.6,1,Second phase remains a punishment phase where players may punish based on compliance/violation.,1,0,Carries over the punishment cost parameter k from the earlier punishment-based game; still symbolic.,0.9,0,Carries over harm parameter h per punished individual; still symbolic.,0.9,0,No reward mechanisms are introduced in the generalized model; only punishment.,0.98,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,0,Game is in terms of payoffs from compliance/violation and punishment; no per-round numeric endowment described.,0.4,N/R,Outcome is described qualitatively as ‘everyone follows the rule’; no normalized compliance rates are numerically reported.,0.4,N/R,"Model concerns binary compliance vs. violation for arbitrary rules, not numeric contributions.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum feasible) is provided.,0.3,N/R,"Author only states that for sufficiently severe punishment, there is an equilibrium with universal compliance; no numeric group payoff is given.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a generalized analytical model of an n‑player punishment game for arbitrary norms.,1,"Indefinitely repeated n-player game for an arbitrary social rule. Phase 1: each player chooses Comply or Violate; payoffs can represent any rule’s incentive structure. Phase 2: punishment phase where each player can punish or not punish others conditional on their prior compliance/violation; punishment costs k to punisher and inflicts harm h on target. Author argues that, for sufficiently severe punishment and a mechanism stabilizing punishment (e.g., higher-order punishment), there exists an equilibrium in which everyone complies with the rule and punishes violators, independent of the underlying cooperative structure of the rule.",Summarizes the generalized punishment-based model as explained under ‘Punishment as a solution to the problem of moral compliance’.,0.94
