gy is specified in an original game.,1,0,No endowments are assigned because no game is run.,1,N/A,No contributions are measured by the authors.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts are recorded in this article.,1,N/A,No efficiency metric is computed for an original experiment.,1,N/A,No group payoffs are reported from an original study.,1,N/A,No human participants are recruited in this article.,1,N/A,No sample of participants is described.,1,N/A,No sample of participants is described.,1,N/A,No sample of participants is described.,1,No human,The paper does not include new human-subject or field data; it is a conceptual and literature review.,1,"This article is a theoretical and literature review on punishment, prestige, and dominance, summarizing prior empirical and formal work but not reporting new games or experiments.","Derived from the structure and content of the paper, which only cites others’ experiments and models.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2018.04.018.md,No corruption control (baseline replicator model),Paper defines three main interaction scenarios; this is the benchmark case 'without corruption control' analyzed in Section 3.1 and Appendix A.,1,Corruption control regime: none (altruistic punishers cannot punish corrupt punishers). Corrupt punishers choose spiteful corruption with probability p and bribery with probability 1-p.,"This condition differs from the others only by the corruption control regime; p is varied in analysis but is part of the model, not a between-condition manipulation relative to other regimes.",1,False,Model is an infinite well-mixed population analyzed with replicator dynamics; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; purely theoretical model.,1,False,No lab setting; analytical theory.,1,False,Main results are closed-form replicator equations and analytical phase diagrams; numerical plotting does not constitute a stand-alone simulation study for our coding purposes.,0.9,True,Replicator dynamics are derived and fixed points analyzed mathematically (Appendix A).,1,5,"Group size n is used in all numerical illustrations (e.g., caption Fig. 2: 'n=5').",0.95,1,Each interaction is a single public goods game before reproduction; no repeated-round structure is modeled.,0.9,1,"Each contributing type contributes a fixed amount c; defectors contribute 0. Strategy set is binary (cooperate/defect), equivalent to all-or-nothing rather than continuous choice.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects or user interface; endowments are theoretical parameters.,1,0.6,"MPCR is r/n; numerical examples use r=3.0 and n=5, giving 3/5=0.6.",0.95,N/A,No human interaction or communication modeled.,1,0,Model assumes payoffs affect reproduction via replicator dynamics; no explicit feedback of detailed summaries to agents.,0.9,N/A,No explicit identities or information structure; punishment is aggregate and anonymous in replicator model terms.,0.9,N/A,"No rewards are modeled, only punishment and bribes.",1,0,Agents do not observe a horizon; evolution is continuous time.,0.9,1,Cops (altruistic or corrupt punishers) can impose fines on defectors; central mechanism of the model.,1,0.4,"Numeric examples specify β=0.4 (e.g., Fig. 2 caption) as cost to punisher per punished defector.",0.95,0.7,Fine per punished defector is α; numerical examples use α=0.7.,0.95,0,No positive reward mechanism; only public good returns and punishment/bribes.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,1,"Each cooperator contributes fixed amount c; numerical examples set c=1.0, consistent with a unit endowment fully contributed.",0.95,N/R,They report qualitative 'cooperation level' and proportions of cooperators/punishers but no explicit normalized contribution rate beyond binary strategies.,0.4,N/R,No explicit equilibrium average contribution in tokens is tabulated; only proportions of cooperators (who contribute c).,0.4,N/R,Population mean payoff is discussed (Fig. 8) under other regimes; not quantified for this baseline in normalized efficiency terms.,0.3,N/R,No raw group payoff per game is reported; only qualitative outcomes and some mean payoff plots later (under control regimes).,0.3,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game in infinite population.,1,"Role-asymmetric public goods game in infinite well-mixed population with two roles: civilians and cops. Civilians: cooperators (C) contribute c or defectors (D) contribute 0. Cops: altruistic punishers (AP) cooperate and always punish civilian defectors at cost β imposing fine α; corrupt punishers (CP) choose with probability p to defect and punish like AP (spiteful corruption) or with probability 1-p to cooperate and accept bribes γ from defectors instead of punishing them. Group size n, synergy factor r with 1<r<n; proportions of cops and civilians are ρ and 1-ρ. Strategies evolve via replicator dynamics within each role; no role switching.",Summarized from Section 2 and Appendix A definitions.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2018.04.018.md,Symmetrical punishment as corruption control,Section 3.2 and Appendix B analyze the case 'where symmetrical punishment is adopted as the instrument of corruption control'.,1,"Corruption control regime: symmetrical punishment (altruistic punishers impose same fine α on both civilian defectors and corrupt punishers, cost β per target). Corrupt punishers still mix between spiteful corruption (probability p) and bribery (1-p).",Condition differs from baseline and asymmetrical regimes only by how altruistic punishers treat corrupt punishers.,1,False,Same analytical framework; no human data.,1,False,Not an experimental study.,1,False,No lab implementation.,1,False,Derivations and phase diagrams come from solving replicator dynamics analytically; numerical plotting is secondary.,0.9,True,Appendix B derives closed-form payoff gaps and fixed-point stability; full analytical characterization of dynamic scenarios.,1,5,"All example parameterizations (e.g., Fig. 5, 6, 7, 8) set n=5.",0.95,1,Single-stage group interaction before evolutionary update.,0.9,1,Binary cooperate/defect contribution decision with fixed c or 0.,0.9,N/A,No user interface or time-zero allocation for human subjects.,1,0.6,"As above, MPCR=r/n=3/5 in all numerical illustrations.",0.95,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,Agents react only to payoffs via selection; no explicit feedback of detailed summaries.,0.9,N/A,No explicit identities; only aggregate payoffs matter.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No horizon concept in replicator dynamics.,0.9,1,Altruistic punishers sanction both civilian defectors and corrupt punishers.,1,0.4,β=0.4 in numerical examples; cost per target for altruistic punishers.,0.95,0.7,Fine α=0.7 imposed on each punished target in examples.,0.95,0,No explicit rewarding; only sanctioning and bribes.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,"Contribution amount parameter c is set to 1.0 in all numerical examples (e.g., Figs. 5–8).",0.95,N/R,They characterize equilibria in terms of fractions of cooperators and altruistic punishers but do not report a scalar normalized contribution rate separately.,0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric equilibrium contribution (tokens) is given; only strategy frequencies.,0.4,N/R,"Population mean payoff is discussed (e.g., Fig. 8) but not normalized against maximum possible payoff to yield an efficiency ratio.",0.4,N/R,They compute an average population payoff W (Appendix D) but do not provide closed-form or tabulated group payoff values per parameter set.,0.4,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Theoretical evolutionary game; no data collection environment.,1,"Same role-asymmetric public goods game as baseline, but altruistic punishers, when present, punish both civilian defectors and corrupt punishers with the same fine α at cost β per punished individual. Expected payoff gap f(x,y) and civilian payoff gap g(x,y) are derived in Appendix B, leading to quadratic replicator dynamics in x and linear in y. Key composite parameters: e=(n−1)ρ(pα+γ−pγ)−σ and c0=(1−p)(n−1)(1−ρ)γ+(n−1)ρβ+pσ determine five dynamic regions with different stable equilibria (CP+D, CP+C, AP+C, AP+D combinations). Bribe amount γ and probability p of spiteful corruption remain as in baseline.",Summarized from Section 3.2 and Appendix B equations.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2018.04.018.md,Asymmetrical punishment (social exclusion) as corruption control,"Section 3.3 and Appendix C analyze the case 'asymmetrical punishment is adopted as an instrument of corruption control', where AP exclude CP from benefits.",1,Corruption control regime: asymmetrical punishment via social exclusion (altruistic punishers impose fine α on civilian defectors at cost β and exclude corrupt punishers from sharing public good benefits at cost η per corrupt punisher). Corrupt punishers still mix between spiteful corruption (probability p) and bribery (1-p).,Distinct condition defined by different treatment of corrupt punishers (social exclusion instead of symmetric fines).,1,False,Entirely theoretical replicator-dynamics model.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment with subjects.,1,False,No lab context; analyses are mathematical.,1,False,"Although some numerical phase diagrams are shown, core results are from analytical derivations in Appendix C.",0.9,True,Appendix C derives expected payoffs and nonlinear replicator equations including (1−ρx)^{n−1} terms and analyzes fixed points.,1,5,"Example configurations for this regime (Fig. 6b, 7b, 8) use n=5.",0.95,1,Single public goods interaction per evolutionary update; no repetition.,0.9,1,"As in other regimes, contribution decision is binary: contribute c or 0.",0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation procedure for human subjects.,1,0.6,"Again r=3, n=5 in all comparative figures, so MPCR=r/n=0.6.",0.95,N/A,Communication mechanisms are not part of the model.,1,0,Agents only experience payoff-driven selection; they do not observe detailed outcome summaries.,0.9,N/A,No identification structure modeled.,0.9,N/A,No reward actions included.,1,0,No notion of discrete rounds available to agents.,0.9,1,Altruistic punishers fine civilian defectors and exert exclusion punishment on CP.,1,0.4,"β=0.4 and η=β are used in numerical examples (e.g., Fig. 6b, 7b, 8); β is cost per punished civilian defector, η per excluded CP.",0.95,0.7,"Fine α=0.7 on civilian defectors; corrupt punishers are punished by exclusion from benefits, not by a fine, but α characterizes fine intensity where used.",0.9,0,No positive incentives are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Contribution amount c is set to 1.0 in numerical illustrations under this regime as well (shared parameters).,0.95,N/R,They compare average cooperation levels (fractions of AP and C) across regimes but do not provide a numeric contribution rate metric normalized to endowment.,0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric average contribution in tokens is reported for asymmetrical regime.,0.4,N/R,They compute and plot population mean payoff (Fig. 8) but do not express it as efficiency relative to a maximum cooperative benchmark.,0.5,N/R,Population mean payoff W is discussed; no per-group payoff value is tabulated.,0.4,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game model only.,1,"Same basic role-asymmetric public goods game, but when altruistic punishers are present they: (i) contribute c; (ii) fine each civilian defector α at cost β; and (iii) exclude each corrupt punisher from sharing the multiplied public good at cost η per corrupt punisher (with η often set equal to β). Corrupt punishers obtain benefits from the public good only when no altruistic punishers are in the group; otherwise their share is removed. Payoff functions include factors (1−ρx)^{n−1}, the probability that a focal CP faces no AP among the other n−1 group members. This asymmetry in punishment intensity leads, for sufficiently large ρ and p, to unique stable equilibrium AP+C (full cooperation and altruistic punishment), with higher average cooperation and population mean payoff than either no-control or symmetric-punishment regimes.","Summarized from Section 3.3 and Appendix C equations for f_AP, f_CP, f_C, f_D and subsequent discussion, plus Fig. 6–8.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0045093.md,Public Goods Game (PG) – single condition with punishment & potential vendettas,"Paper describes one PG treatment: 4-person public goods game with 1 contribution stage followed by 5 punishment stages per period, repeated for 3 periods with random rematching.",1,"Game type = Public Goods; Group size = 4; Contribution choice = binary (contribute €1 or €0); Punishment enabled (0–10 units, cost 1:3); 5 punishment rounds; 3 periods; anonymous fixed alias within session","Summarizes fixed structural features that define this experimental condition, drawn from Methods section describing the PG setup.",1,True,Human participants (first-semester biology students) played the PG in the lab.,1,True,"Controlled laboratory game with random assignment, monetary incentives, and specified rules.",1,True,Conducted in computer lab sessions at universities with partitions and individual computers.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported for the PG.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as a standalone experiment; focus is on behavioral data.,1,4,Participants were arranged into subgroups of four individuals for the PG.,1,18,3 periods; each period: 1 PG contribution round + 5 punishment rounds = 6. Total 3×6 = 18 decision rounds.,0.95,0,Contribution decision was binary: contribute €1.00 or not; cannot choose any intermediate amount.,1,0,"Each participant had a starting amount of €20; they chose whether to contribute €1 to the public good, so all endowment starts in private account.",1,0.4,Sum of contributions multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally among 4 members. MPCR = 1.6 / 4 = 0.4.,1,0,Instructions emphasized that participants should not talk to one another; no communication channel described.,1,1,"After PG round, participants were informed about all subgroup members’ PG investments and could then assign punishment; implies visibility of others’ contributions and thus payoff-relevant summaries.",0.9,1,In successive punishment rounds participants knew exactly who punished whom with how much money for each subgroup member; identities via aliases were stable within session.,1,0,No reward mechanism was implemented; only punishment is described.,1,0,Participants were not informed about the number of PG or punishment rounds; they could only guess after first period.,1,1,"Five punishment rounds followed each PG round, where participants could assign punishment points.",1,0.1,Example: investing €0.30 (=3 units) to punish implies cost €0.10 per unit. 0.30 / 3 = 0.10.,1,0.3,"Each point of punishment assigned resulted in a threefold fine to the punished member: one unit (cost €0.10) causes €0.30 fine, so 0.30 per unit.",1,0,No positive reward or bonus-allocating mechanism described; only punishment points.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in this condition.,1,20,Starting amount was set to €20 for each participant.,1,0.619,"Overall cooperation level in PG rounds increased: period 1 = 47.9%, period 2 = 65.6%, period 3 = 71.9%. Mean across 3 periods ≈ (0.479+0.656+0.719)/3 ≈ 0.618; rounded to 0.619.",0.9,0.619,"Each player can contribute either €1 or €0; contribution amount per player equals contribution rate in euros, since max is €1; use overall mean rate ≈ 0.619.",0.7,N/R,Paper reports cooperation levels and earnings but not total group payoff relative to theoretical maximum; efficiency not derivable without full payoff accounting including punishment flows.,0.2,N/R,Only individual average earnings in euros are reported (mean €13.41±3.01); average per-group or per-period group payoffs not provided.,0.3,Germany and Austria,"Participants were students from Universities of Kiel, Hamburg, Münster (Germany) and Vienna (Austria).",1,First-semester university students (exact ages N/R),Described as first-semester biology students; typical age range can be inferred but exact ages are not reported.,0.6,N/R,"Paper explicitly states that no demographic data (e.g. gender, age) were collected during the games.",1,First-semester biology students at university,Described as first-semester biology students from several universities.,1,On site,Sessions took place at universities with participants seated at individual computers with partitions; standard physical lab environment.,1,Binary public goods game with 4-player groups; 3 periods; random rematching between periods avoiding repeat partners; aliases used for within-session identification; PG: each player chooses to contribute €1 or €0; total contributions multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally; 5 subsequent punishment rounds per period where each player can assign 0–10 punishment units to each of 3 others; punishment cost €0.10 per unit and imposes a €0.30 fine per unit; negative earnings allowed but truncated to €0 at payment; participants unaware of total number of PG and punishment rounds.,Condensed description of key structural rules and timing from Methods section for the PG condition.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0045093.md,Prisoner’s Dilemma (PD) – single condition with punishment & potential vendettas,"Paper describes one PD treatment: 2-person prisoner’s dilemma with 1 contribution (cooperate/defect) stage followed by 5 punishment stages per period, repeated for 5 periods with random rematching.",1,"Game type = Prisoner’s Dilemma; Group size = 2; Contribution choice = binary (contribute €1 or €0); Punishment enabled (binary rounds, fixed €0.50 cost, 1:3 fine); 5 punishment rounds; 5 periods; anonymous fixed alias within session",Summarizes fixed structural features that define this experimental condition based on PD setup in Methods.,1,True,Human participants played the PD game with punishment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with monetary incentives and random assignment into dyads.,1,True,Conducted in sessions at universities on individual computers.,1,False,No simulation-only PD study reported.,1,False,No pure analytical PD model as standalone experiment.,1,2,Six participants were randomly assigned into subgroups of two (dyads) in each PD session.,1,30,5 periods; each consists of 1 PD decision round + 5 punishment rounds = 6 per period; total 5×6 = 30 rounds.,0.95,0,Players decide whether or not to contribute €1.00; binary cooperation/defection; not continuous contributions.,1,0,Starting amount €20 is fully private; players choose whether to invest €1 into the joint multiplier.,1,0.8,Sum of contributions multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally between 2 players. MPCR = 1.6 / 2 = 0.8.,1,0,Participants were instructed not to talk; no communication mechanism mentioned.,1,1,"After PD outcome, participants were informed about the PD outcome and then could assign punishment; implies they see partner’s cooperation decision and earnings outcome.",0.9,1,"In each punishment round, ‘punishment amounts were announced to both players and the next round of punishment followed’; with fixed dyads and aliases, each knows whether and how much their partner punished.",1,0,No reward option is described in PD setup.,1,0,Participants were not informed about the number of PD rounds nor the number of punishment rounds.,1,1,Each PD round is followed by five successive punishment rounds where participants can punish their partner.,1,0.5,Punishment incurred a cost of €0.50 to the punisher per punishment act.,1,1.5,The punishment imposed a threefold fine of €1.50 to the target per €0.50 spent; 3×0.50 = 1.50.,1,0,No reward or positive transfer mechanism in PD condition.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available in this condition.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available in this condition.,1,20,"Participants received a starting amount of €20 each, as in the PG setup.",1,0.804,"Cooperation (contribution) rates by period: 0.784, 0.786, 0.8071, 0.857, 0.786. Mean ≈ (0.784+0.786+0.8071+0.857+0.786)/5 ≈ 0.804.",0.95,0.804,Each player can contribute at most €1; average contribution per player equals cooperation rate in euros; use overall mean rate ≈ 0.804.,0.7,N/R,Total dyad earnings relative to maximum (assuming full cooperation and no punishment) are not reported and cannot be reconstructed due to missing full punishment and payoff distributions.,0.2,N/R,Only individual average earnings reported (mean €15.40±6.43); average per-dyad payoff not explicitly provided and would require detailed per-round payoff data.,0.3,Germany and Austria,"Same participant pool as PG: students from universities in Kiel, Hamburg, Münster (Germany) and Vienna (Austria).",1,First-semester university students (exact ages N/R),Described as first-semester biology students; exact age distribution not recorded.,0.6,N/R,Authors state no demographic data like gender or age were collected.,1,First-semester biology students at university,Participants characterized as first-semester biology students.,1,On site,"Same lab setting as PG: in-person sessions at universities, computers with partitions.",1,"Binary prisoner’s dilemma implemented as a contribution game: each player chooses to contribute €1 or not; total contributions multiplied by 1.6 and split equally between 2 players, yielding mutual cooperation payoff €0.60 each, mutual defection €0.00, cooperator in CD gets −€0.20 and defector gets €0.80 (relative to their €1 decision). Each period: 1 PD stage followed by 5 punishment stages. In each punishment stage, a player may choose to punish partner at cost €0.50, imposing a fine of €1.50 on partner. Punishment amounts are announced to both players each round. Five periods total, with random rematching between periods and no repeat partners, blocking reputation and direct reciprocity across periods. Negative earnings allowed but truncated at €0 for payment.","Condenses PD rules, payoff structure, timing, and rematching details given in Methods.",1
10.1038_srep38349.md,Support-present condition (leader support system),Paper explicitly compares a 'support-present condition' with a 'no-support condition'; this row captures the support-present treatment.,1,Support condition: support-present (followers decide whether to transfer 20 tokens each to leader as punishment capital); punishment technology identical to no-support.,Only experimental manipulation between main conditions is presence vs absence/source of leader punishment capital via follower support.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 162 student participants in this condition.,1,True,Researchers randomly assigned participants to conditions and controlled payoffs and information.,1,True,Described as a 'group experiment conducted in a laboratory'; ran sessions with 18 participants per session.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as a standalone method; focus is on experimental data.,1,6,Each group consists of one leader and five followers: 'six-person groups... five followers... one leader'.,1,15,Three-stage sequence is 'repeated 15 times'; participants informed periods would be repeated 15 times.,1,0,"Followers choose whether to 'contribute all 100 tokens ... or not at all', i.e., binary all-or-nothing contribution.",1,0,"At start of PGG stage, each member is 'given 100 tokens'; nothing is pre-assigned to group pool so all endowment is initially private.",1,0.4,Each contributing follower’s 100 tokens are doubled (to 200) and distributed equally among 5 followers → each follower gets 40 per contributor; MPCR = 40/100 = 0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous z-Tree implementation with no chat channel reported.,0.8,1,"After each stage all members are informed who contributed, who supported, and who was punished and by how much; this is a group-level summary of others’ actions and sanctions.",1,1,Followers 'were informed who had been punished and by how much'; implies identities of punished followers are revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design; only punishment.,1,1,"Participants were told before beginning that periods 'would be repeated 15 times', so they know total number of rounds.",1,1,Dedicated 'punishment stage' where leader can reduce followers’ tokens.,1,20,"Leader determines punishment 'in increments of 20 tokens'; if leader uses 20 tokens to punish, follower loses 40; thus cost per step is 20 tokens.",1,2,Punishment rate is described as 'double'; 20 tokens spent causes 40-token loss; magnitude = 2 tokens lost per 1 token spent.,1,0,No mechanism for leaders or followers to grant additional positive tokens; only losses are implemented.,1,N/A,No reward stage or technology in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this experiment.,1,100,At start of each PGG stage all six members receive 100 tokens; this is the PGG endowment per period.,1,N/R,"Paper reports average total group contribution and thresholds (e.g., >80%) but not a single numeric mean fraction for this condition separately.",0.3,N/R,"Only qualitative and threshold statistics (e.g., number of groups above 80%) are given; no explicit average token amounts.",0.3,N/R,No direct or indirectly computable measure of total payoff vs maximum payoff per group by condition is reported.,0.2,N/R,Profits of leaders and followers are compared across leader types within this condition but not reported numerically for the entire support-present condition.,0.2,Japan,Experiment run at Waseda University and other Japanese institutions; recruitment via a university portal website in Japan.,0.9,N/R,Described only as 'university students'; no age range or mean reported.,0.2,N/R,No information about participant sex or gender composition.,0.0,University students,Participants described explicitly as 'university students'.,1,On site,Sessions held in a physical laboratory using z-Tree with groups assembled on site; not an online experiment.,1,"Six-person groups with one fixed leader and five fixed followers for all 15 periods. PGG: each follower has 100 tokens and makes a binary choice to contribute all 100 or 0; each contribution is doubled and shared equally among the 5 followers (each receives 40 per contributing follower); the leader’s 100 tokens are unaffected by PGG outcome. Support stage (support-present): each follower and leader receive 20 tokens; each follower decides to transfer (support) their 20 tokens to the leader or keep them. Punishment capital for leader in each period is 20 + 20 × (number of supporting followers), min 20, max 120. Punishment stage: leader allocates capital in 20-token increments to reduce any follower’s tokens at a 2:1 rate (20 spent → follower loses 40); unused capital is added to leader’s profit. Full information after each period: all players learn which followers contributed, which supported, and who was punished and by how much; roles and group composition fixed; tokens converted to yen at 1 token = 0.7 yen plus a 500-yen show-up fee.",Summarizes procedural and payoff structure details directly described in the Methods and design sections for the support-present condition.,1
10.1038_srep38349.md,No-support condition (external punishment budget),"Second main treatment condition described as 'no-support condition', contrasted with support-present condition.",1,Support condition: no-support (leader receives fixed 120-token punishment capital exogenously; followers have no support decision).,Only difference from support-present is removal of support decision and provision of fixed 120 tokens to leader as punishment capital.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory treatment with 108 students (18 groups).,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of support vs no-support within the same game structure.,1,True,Same laboratory setting and procedures as support-present condition.,1,False,No simulations described for this condition.,1,False,Condition is purely empirical; no separate analytical model.,1,6,"Identical six-person group structure (one leader, five followers) as in support-present condition.",1,15,Same 15 repetition structure across both conditions.,1,0,PGG decision structure (contribute all 100 tokens or none) is unchanged.,1,0,Followers start each PGG stage with 100 private tokens; no initial group allocation.,1,0.4,Public good production and sharing rule is identical: each 100-token contribution yields 40 tokens to each of the 5 followers.,1,0,No communication channel is described for any condition.,0.8,1,"After each period all six members know who contributed, who supported (trivial here, as no support choice), and who was punished and by how much.",1,1,Paper states all members are informed who has been punished and by how much after leader’s decision.,1,0,No rewarding institution implemented.,1,1,"Participants are told the number of repetitions (15) beforehand, same as in other condition.",1,1,Leader has punishment power with fixed capital in this condition.,1,20,"Leader still chooses punishment in units of 20 tokens, with a 2:1 loss rate for followers.",1,2,Punishment rate remains double: 20 spent → 40 lost by follower.,1,0,No reward option exists in the no-support condition either.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,100,"Each period, each of the six members receives 100 tokens at start of PGG stage, identical to support-present condition.",1,N/R,"Only relative statements (e.g., fewer groups achieve >80% contribution; U-test p-values) are given; no numeric mean contribution fraction for this condition.",0.3,N/R,Paper does not provide average token contributions for no-support condition separately.,0.3,N/R,No condition-level payoff vs maximum payoff statistics reported that would allow computing efficiency.,0.2,N/R,Group or condition-specific payoffs are not numerically reported for no-support condition.,0.2,Japan,Same participant pool (Japanese university students) as support-present condition.,0.9,N/R,"Only described as university students, no age stats.",0.2,N/R,No gender information provided.,0.0,University students,Participants were university students recruited via a university portal website.,1,On site,Conducted in a lab with on-site sessions; no evidence of remote or field implementation.,1,"Group composition and PGG stage identical to support-present condition: one fixed leader and five fixed followers per group over 15 periods; each follower gets 100 tokens per period and chooses to contribute 100 or 0 to a group pool; each contribution is doubled and shared equally among the 5 followers (40 each); leader’s 100 tokens are unaffected. Support stage (no-support): leader receives 120 tokens; each follower receives 20 tokens; neither leader nor followers make any support-related decision (no transfer choice exists). The 120 tokens correspond to the maximum capital a leader could get in the support-present condition if all 5 followers supported. Punishment stage: leader uses the fixed 120 tokens as punishment capital, in increments of 20 tokens per target, with a 2:1 punishment rate (20 spent → 40 lost by follower); unused capital is added to leader’s profit. After each period all players are informed who contributed to the PGG, and who has been punished and by how much; there is no variation in punishment capital across periods within this condition.",Summarizes condition-specific procedural differences and maintains shared game elements from Methods section for the no-support condition.,1
10.1007_s11127-006-9075-3.md,"BL (Baseline) – rounds 1–14, no CSM",Baseline treatment described as 14 rounds of the game without centralized sanctioning mechanism (CSM).,1,"Treatment: Baseline (no CSM), 14 rounds non-CSM game",Baseline differs from AR and VR by absence of CSM in all 14 rounds.,1,True,Human subjects (students) played the game.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to groups and pre-specified rules.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab at Universitat Pompeu Fabra using z-Tree.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,Paper contains theoretical analysis but main results are from lab experiment with humans.,1,5,Groups of five in all treatments.,1,14,Baseline played 14 repetitions of the step-level public good game.,1,1,Players choose an integer contribution between 0 and 10; not constrained to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment given to each player who then chooses how much to contribute; all starts in private account.,1,0.24,"If threshold reached, group gets 5*12=60 from fund for 31+ tokens: 60/31≈1.935 to group; per capita marginal return is 12/31≈0.387. However, standard MPCR in step-level games is often defined as individual marginal return over contribution at threshold; here payoff when threshold reached does not depend on own contribution (within 0–10) near step, so MPCR<1 and effectively approx 0.387. To conform to definition multiplier/group size: group benefit at margin 12/31 per person; group size 5 gives MPCR≈(12/31)/5≈0.077, but that is nonstandard. Given ambiguity, set to 0.24 as approximate average (60/31)/5.",0.1,0,Instructions explicitly forbid any kind of communication.,1,1,Result screens show own contribution and group sum of contributions; this is a summary of others' behavior.,1,0,No individual-to-individual punishment and no identities revealed; only a centralized rule.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,"Instructions inform subjects that the experiment has 14 rounds and about rule changes in other treatments; typical z-Tree setups show current round, so assume they know number of rounds.",0.8,0,Baseline game defined by (1) has no fines or CSM costs.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in baseline.,1,0,No rewarding institution is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each player in every round has an endowment worth 10 units.,1,N/R,"Average contributions in tokens and step-level success percentages reported, but not normalized contribution rates.",0.3,4.0,Table A.1 “Average” column for BL reports overall average group contribution 4.0? Actually table average row gives per-round group sum averages; 4.0 is group-level; per-person average would be 4.0/5=0.8 but table labels are ambiguous. Use reported ‘Average’ value 4.0 as group contribution proxy.,0.3,N/R,Paper reports success rates and average contributions but not normalized efficiency relative to maximum theoretical payoff.,0.1,N/R,"Only contributions and success indicators are provided, not payoffs by treatment.",0.1,Spain,"Sample consisted of students of Universitat Pompeu Fabra in Barcelona, Spain.",1,N/R,Paper only states they are students; no ages given.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,"University students (mainly Economics, Business, Law)","Sample description specifies students from different faculties, mostly Economics, Business and Law.",1,On site,Participants came to the laboratory and used lab computers.,1,"Step-level public goods game with threshold 31 (group of 5, each endowed with 10). If sum of contributions ≥31, each earns 12 from the fund plus what they keep; otherwise contributions are lost.",Directly from experimental design description and payoff function (1).,1
10.1007_s11127-006-9075-3.md,AR – CSM active (rounds 1–7),Automatic Removal (AR) treatment specified as 7 rounds with CSM followed by 7 rounds without CSM; this row covers the CSM phase.,1,"Treatment: AR, centralized sanctioning mechanism present (Rule Set A), rounds 1–7",Independent variable is presence of CSM; in AR rounds 1–7 CSM is active and removal is automatic after round 7.,1,True,Human subjects in lab sessions.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of rule set (CSM vs no CSM).,1,True,Same lab setting as baseline using z-Tree.,1,False,No purely simulated experiments.,1,False,"Condition is part of empirical experiment, not purely analytical.",1,5,Groups of five in all treatments.,1,7,This condition refers to the first 7 rounds of AR under CSM.,1,1,Contribution choice is integer 0–10.,1,0,Endowment allocated to private account; subjects decide how much to deposit.,1,0.24,Same underlying step-level public good as baseline; CSM adds fines and fixed cost but does not change threshold payoff structure. Use same approximate MPCR value.,0.1,0,Communication forbidden in all treatments.,1,1,Results screen shows own contribution and group sum.,1,0,Sanction is centralized and automatic; no identities of punishers shown.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Instructions state that experiment has 14 rounds and that CSM will be removed after round 7; subjects thus know horizon and change point.,0.8,1,Rule Set A/CSM subtracts 6 units if contribution<6 and 3 units from everyone each round.,1,1,Operationalized as 1 ECU of CSM leads to 1 ECU reduction in payoff (no separate resource spent by a punisher; cost and impact are 1:1). Use 1 as cost per unit of fine levied.,0.2,1,Each unit of fine directly reduces punished player’s payoff 1:1; no amplification.,0.8,0,Only negative sanctions (fines) and fixed cost; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each player has endowment 10 each round.,1,N/R,"Paper gives average group contributions by round and success rates (e.g., AR 1–7 success 89%) but not normalized contribution fractions.",0.3,32.4,"Table A.2 “Avg. 1–7” row averages to roughly 33–34 across groups; overall average of column 'Average' for 1–7 is about 33.1. However, Abstract/Table 1 indicate threshold reached in 89% of rounds, implying average group contributions slightly above 31. Use 32.4 as approximate from first-line ‘Average 32.4’ for round 1 and 33.7 for round 2, etc.; 31.7–34.4 range; 32.4 is mid-range estimate.",0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios per treatment-phase reported.,0.1,N/R,Payoffs by treatment not tabulated; only earnings overall (euros) and success rates.,0.1,Spain,Same subject pool for all treatments.,1,N/R,Not provided.,0,N/R,Not provided.,0,"University students (mainly Economics, Business, Law)",Same as overall sample description.,1,On site,Lab experiment at university using computers.,1,"Step-level public good with threshold 31; Rule Set A (CSM): each player pays 3 units per round; if sum of contributions ≥31, each gets 12; if own contribution <6, an additional 6-unit fine is subtracted.",From section 2 and Appendix 1 definitions of Rule Set A and payoff function (2).,1
