10.3390_su10124585.md,"Scenario 1 – Situation 1 (low incentives and punishments, government non-intervention ESS)","Named according to Section 4, Scenario 1, Situation 1, where parameter values are given in Table 4 and ESS is (0,0,0) and (1,1,0).",0.98,"Government incentive and punishment levels: f_A = 3.5, f_B = 2.5, P_A = 2, P_B = 1; profit condition ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0 < ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0 < 0 (government cannot profit from intervention).","Scenario 1 is defined by inequality on ΔD, f_A, f_B, P_A, P_B, C0; Situation 1 uses specific parameter values from Table 4.",0.97,False,Study uses a three-population evolutionary game model with numerical simulations in MATLAB; no human participants.,1,False,"Numerical simulations of differential equations, not a controlled lab/field experiment with subjects.",1,False,No lab setting; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,True,Section 4 explicitly states that numerical studies in MATLAB are used to simulate the evolutionary dynamics.,1,True,"Sections 2–3 derive analytical evolutionary game model and stability conditions (replicator dynamics, Jacobian, eigenvalues).",1,3,"Three populations: enterprises, URIs, government; model is population-level, not discrete players, but three strategic types interact.",0.9,50,Simulations set t from 0 to 50 when solving the dynamic system.,0.8,1,"Enterprises and URIs choose between two discrete strategies (positive vs negative cooperation), not a continuous contribution level.",0.9,N/A,No public goods contribution endowment structure; model uses abstract payoffs and costs.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or MPCR defined in the model.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,N/R,No interface or feedback to human subjects; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,"Punishment is a parameter in payoff functions, not a decision observed by human players.",1,N/A,"Incentives are parameters, not observed rewards between identified individuals.",1,N/R,No subjects; notion of displaying remaining rounds does not apply.,1,1,Punishment parameters P_A and P_B are part of the payoff matrix and nonzero in this situation.,1,N/R,"Model specifies fixed punishment payoffs P_A, P_B but not a per-unit cost of assigning punishment as a decision variable.",0.4,N/R,No mapping from punishment expenditures to harm (no per-unit punishment technology) is specified.,0.4,1,Incentive parameters f_A and f_B exist and are used in payoffs for positive cooperation.,1,N/R,Cost to government of each unit of incentive is not modeled separately; only total intervention cost C0 appears.,0.4,N/R,No per-unit reward technology (benefit per incentive unit) is separately defined.,0.4,N/R,"No initial token endowment for agents is defined; only abstract payoffs E, N_A, etc.",1,N/R,No contribution variable; outcomes are cooperation strategy frequencies and stability.,1,N/R,"Model tracks positive vs negative cooperation, not contribution magnitudes.",1,N/R,No efficiency ratio (actual vs maximum payoff) is computed or reported.,1,N/R,Paper focuses on stability of strategies; does not report aggregate payoff numbers for simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulation and analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is theoretical and simulation-based without human subjects.,1,"Three-population evolutionary game between government, enterprises, and URIs. Two strategies each: government (financial intervention vs non-intervention), enterprises/URIs (positive vs negative cooperation). Scenario 1: parameter values Q_A=6, Q_B=4, f_A=3.5, f_B=2.5, P_A=2, P_B=1, ΔD=10, C0=35, R_A=3, R_B=2 lead to ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0 < ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0 < 0, with ESS at (0,0,0) and (1,1,0).",Summarizes the modeled game and specific parameters for Situation 1 based on Table 4 and text in Scenario 1.,0.98
10.3390_su10124585.md,"Scenario 1 – Situation 1(a) (higher incentives and punishments, government non-intervention ESS)","Named according to Scenario 1, Situation 1(a) with increased f_A, f_B, P_A, P_B in Table 4.",0.98,"Government incentive and punishment levels: f_A = 10, f_B = 8, P_A = 11, P_B = 9; profit condition still ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0 < ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0 < 0 (government cannot profit from intervention).",Situation 1(a) in Table 4 sets larger incentives/punishments while keeping ΔD and C0 the same; used to compare dynamics vs Situation 1.,0.97,False,Numerical simulation with no human subjects.,1,False,"The condition is part of a computational study, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,True,MATLAB-based numerical integration of replicator dynamics for this parameter set.,1,True,Shares analytical model with other scenarios; only parameters change.,1,3,Same three-population structure as in Scenario 1.,0.9,50,t ranges from 0 to 50 in all simulation situations.,0.8,1,Binary cooperation strategies only.,0.9,N/A,No endowment structure with contributions.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No subjects or communication.,1,N/R,No user interface or feedback is modeled.,1,N/A,"Punishment is parametric, not a chosen action observed by players.",1,N/A,"Incentives are parameters, not observed assignments to identities.",1,N/R,Not relevant in non-human simulation.,1,1,P_A and P_B are nonzero and influence payoffs.,1,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost of punishing action defined.,0.4,N/R,No discrete punishment decision with technology specified.,0.4,1,f_A and f_B represent government incentives for cooperation.,1,N/R,Total intervention cost C0 not decomposed per incentive unit.,0.4,N/R,No reward production function specified.,0.4,N/R,No token endowment defined.,1,N/R,"Outcome metrics are strategy frequencies, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable exists.,1,N/R,Efficiency not calculated in simulations.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational/theoretical condition.,1,"Uses same three-population evolutionary game as Situation 1 but with larger incentives and punishments: Q_A=6, Q_B=4, f_A=10, f_B=8, P_A=11, P_B=9, ΔD=10, C0=35, R_A=3, R_B=2. Scenario 1 conditions hold (government loses from intervention), and ESS remain (0,0,0) and (1,1,0). Increased incentives raise transient cooperation frequencies but long-run outcome is still government non-intervention and potentially negative cooperation.",Synthesized from parameter table and narrative describing evolution paths in Figures 3–5 for Situation 1(a).,0.96
