ncial intervention, but magnitudes are too small to offset additional cooperation costs. Simulation (Figure 6) shows ESS at (0,0,1): enterprises and URIs converge to negative cooperation while government adopts financial intervention.","Summarized from Scenario 2 description, Table 5 values, and ESS identification in text.",0.96
10.3390_su10124585.md,"Scenario 2 – Situation 2(a) (high incentives and punishments under profitable intervention, TAC exceeded)","Scenario 2, Situation 2(a) in Table 5 where f_A, f_B, P_A, P_B are increased substantially.",0.98,"Government incentive and punishment levels: f_A = 10, f_B = 8, P_A = 11, P_B = 9; profit condition 0 < ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0 < ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0; total incentives plus punishments exceed total additional cost (TAC) for enterprises and URIs.","Values from Table 5 (2(a)) and narrative that when total incentives+punishments>TAC, enterprises and URIs choose positive cooperation.",0.96,False,Simulation-only condition.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Simulated via MATLAB as per Section 4.,1,True,Same analytical model applied.,1,3,Three interacting strategic populations.,0.9,50,Time horizon t=0..50 used broadly.,0.8,1,Binary cooperative strategies.,0.9,N/A,No contribution setting.,1,N/R,No public-goods MPCR.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/R,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/R,No display to subjects.,1,1,Nonzero P_A and P_B in this condition.,1,N/R,No cost-per-unit modeled.,0.4,N/R,No explicit punishment technology.,0.4,1,"Incentive parameters f_A, f_B > 0.",1,N/R,C0 is total intervention cost; unit costs unspecified.,0.4,N/R,No mapping of incentive units to benefit specified.,0.4,N/R,No endowment concept.,1,N/R,"Cooperation frequency, not contributions, is key outcome.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Efficiency indicators not reported.,1,N/R,No quantitative payoffs reported for this situation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition implemented via simulation only.,1,"Same three-population game with Q_A=6, Q_B=4, f_A=10, f_B=8, P_A=11, P_B=9, ΔD=50, C0=20, R_A=-3, R_B=-2. Scenario 2 ensures government profit under intervention. Text states that when total incentives+punishments exceed TAC (|R_j|+Q_j), both enterprises and URIs eventually adopt positive cooperation and the ESS becomes (1,1,1), as illustrated in Figure 7.","Based on Scenario 2 discussion, TAC definition, and explicit ESS identification in Situation 2(a).",0.96
10.3390_su10124585.md,"Scenario 3 – Situation 3 (moderate punishments, unstable dynamics)","Scenario 3, Situation 3, with parameters in Table 6 where ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0 < 0 < ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0 and given punishment levels.",0.98,"Government incentive and punishment levels: f_A = 3.5, f_B = 2.5, P_A = 5.5, P_B = 4; profit condition ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0 < 0 < ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0, requiring punishments for government profitability.",Taken from Table 6 for Situation 3 and Scenario 3 defining inequality.,0.97,False,No human subjects.,1,False,Simulation of differential equations only.,1,False,Not a lab setting.,1,True,Dynamic evolution simulated numerically in MATLAB for this parameterization.,1,True,Stability and inequalities derived analytically.,1,3,Same three-population game.,0.9,50,"Simulations on t∈[0,50].",0.8,1,Binary cooperation strategies modeled.,0.9,N/A,No contribution structure.,1,N/R,No public-goods MPCR.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,N/R,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No identities in simulation.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/R,No experimental interface.,1,1,Nonzero P_A and P_B appear centrally in Scenario 3.,1,N/R,Unit costs not broken out.,0.4,N/R,No punishment production function specified.,0.4,1,Incentive parameters f_A and f_B > 0.,1,N/R,Total intervention cost is C0=2; no per-unit reward cost given.,0.4,N/R,No explicit reward technology.,0.4,N/R,No endowment.,1,N/R,"Focus is on cyclical evolution of cooperation probabilities, not contributions.",1,N/R,Contribution amounts not modeled.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically presented.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,The condition is fully simulated.,1,"Three-population game with Q_A=6, Q_B=4, f_A=3.5, f_B=2.5, P_A=5.5, P_B=4, ΔD=5, C0=2, R_A=3, R_B=-2. Scenario 3: government must rely on punishments to profit (ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0<0<ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0). Simulation (Figure 8) shows no ESS; dynamics are unstable and cyclical, particularly for y (URIs) and z (government intervention), indicating that moderate punishments focused where negative cooperation is more beneficial cannot stabilize cooperative outcomes.",Compiled from Scenario 3 text and Table 6 parameter values; ESS absence noted explicitly.,0.95
10.3390_su10124585.md,"Scenario 3 – Situation 3(a) (higher punishments on enterprises, still unstable)","Scenario 3, Situation 3(a) with higher P_A and P_B values in Table 6.",0.98,"Government incentive and punishment levels: f_A = 3.5, f_B = 2.5, P_A = 8, P_B = 7; profit condition ΔD − f_A − f_B − C0 < 0 < ΔD + P_A + P_B − C0; increased punishment especially on enterprises.","Uses Table 6 (3(a)) values and Scenario 3 inequalities; text notes increased P_A and P_B, with particular discussion of raising P_A.",0.96,False,Simulation without subjects.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with humans.,1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,MATLAB simulations assess evolutionary trajectories under higher P_A and P_B.,1,True,Analytical model unchanged; only parameter values differ.,1,3,Three-population structure.,0.9,50,Same t-range used for all simulated situations.,0.8,1,Binary cooperation choice for enterprises and URIs.,0.9,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR.,1,N/A,Simulation-only.,1,N/R,No displays.,1,N/A,"No agents, no identities.",1,N/A,Same.,1,N/R,Not relevant.,1,1,Nonzero P_A=8 and P_B=7.,1,N/R,Per-unit costs not expressed.,0.4,N/R,No punishment production mapping defined.,0.4,1,Incentive parameters remain positive.,1,N/R,Unit incentive costs not modeled.,0.4,N/R,No explicit reward technology parameter.,0.4,N/R,No endowments defined.,1,N/R,"Analysis centers on evolution of x, y, z, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution measure.,1,N/R,No reported efficiency statistics.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoffs presented.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Entirely computationally simulated.,1,"Parameters: Q_A=6, Q_B=4, f_A=3.5, f_B=2.5, P_A=8, P_B=7, ΔD=5, C0=2, R_A=3, R_B=-2. Scenario 3 conditions still apply. The text notes that increasing P_A makes enterprises’ cooperation probability x more stable, whereas raising P_B does not yield comparable stabilization for URIs (y) or government intervention (z). Figures 9–11 indicate cyclical evolutionary trajectories remain and no ESS emerges; greater punishments alone, when incentives are limited, cannot stabilize cooperation.",Based on Scenario 3 discussion and interpretation of Figures 9–11 for Situation 3 vs 3(a).,0.95
10.1093_ajae_aar154.md,"Analytical repeated-game model with collective reputation and peer monitoring (baseline specification, z=1, n firms)","Paper presents a single stylized infinite-horizon repeated-game model; the main varying element is group size n and neighborhood size z. The core, repeatedly analyzed configuration is local peer monitoring with z=1 and arbitrary n.",0.84,"Group size n (number of firms sharing the collective brand) with local peer monitoring neighborhood size z = 1; other parameters (δ, τ, Δ/σ, σ) varied only in comparative statics, not as discrete treatment arms.",Model outcomes are analyzed as functions of n under a fixed local peer-monitoring structure z=1. These are the core comparative statics for ‘optimal group size’.,0.86,False,"No human participants, no observed data; purely theoretical game-theoretic model.",1,False,No controlled experiment; only analytical derivations.,1,False,No lab or field implementation; fully theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are described; only analytical results and some illustrative numerical examples without specifying computational experiments.,0.86,True,Infinite-horizon repeated-game model solved with equilibrium inequalities and propositions; all results are closed-form or inequality-based derivations.,1,0,"No explicit experimental players; in the model there are n firms and many customers, but this is not an implemented game with participants. Use 0 for no-human analytical model.",0.83,0,"Time is discrete and infinite (t=1,2,...). Not a finite-round game.",1,1,"Firms choose between two discrete efforts e_h and e_l rather than a continuous contribution; this maps to an all-or-nothing style choice, so coded as 1 per definition that 0 is only all-or-nothing and 1 is general. However this mapping is imperfect since it’s effort, not contribution amount.",0.4,N/A,"No human subjects or contribution game; model is about firms’ effort levels in quality provision, not a public goods allocation.",1,N/R,"No public-good multiplier or MPCR defined; payoffs come from price and cost parameters, not linear public good returns.",1,N/A,"No human participants, no communication protocol.",1,0,"Customers and firms observe a public signal y_t and firms observe neighbors’ efforts depending on z. There is no mention of summary feedback screens as in experiments; but since this is not an implemented experiment, set 0 rather than N/A as it is conceptually absent.",0.55,N/A,No explicit punishment actions by identified agents in an experiment; punishments are endogenous strategy outcomes in the model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism distinct from continuation of cooperation.,1,0,Infinite horizon game; no concept of participants being shown remaining rounds.,1,1,"Model centers on public punishment (loss of customers’ trust, ‘public punishment phase’) and peer punishment (neighbor firms switch to low effort) as equilibrium punishment devices.",1,N/A,"No explicit per-unit punishment cost parameter; punishment arises from foregone future surplus, not a token-based mechanism.",1,N/A,"No parametric punishment technology (e.g., coins to reduce others’ payoffs); punishment is endogenous via continuation values.",1,0,"No separate costly rewarding mechanism; cooperation is baseline, not a reward choice.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model specifies per-period price p and effort costs c_h, c_l, but no endowment in the sense of tokens available to contribute.",1,N/R,"Outcomes are equilibrium effort choices and payoffs, not contribution fractions in an experiment.",1,N/R,No contributions are measured; only effort levels and profits are theoretically characterized.,1,N/R,"Paper discusses welfare qualitatively and gives formulae like W(k_N), but does not normalize them relative to a maximal payoff to define an efficiency index in [0,1].",0.78,N/R,"Expressions for per-firm payoff π_n,0 and welfare W(k_N) are given symbolically, but no single numeric outcome is reported for a specific parameterization as a main result.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model with no empirical implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated game with n identical firms and many customers. Each period, firms choose high or low effort (e_h, e_l) with costs (c_h, c_l). Customers observe a noisy public signal y_t of average effort plus idiosyncratic noise and decide whether to buy. Firms may observe neighbors’ past efforts in a network (parameter z). Equilibria are grim-trigger type: customers stop buying if the public signal falls below cutoff k~, and firms switch permanently to low effort if public signal is low or if they or neighbors have shirked. Main comparative statics: optimal group size n* as function of discount factor δ, relative deviation gain τ = (c_h - c_l)/(p - c_h), and signal precision Δ/σ under different intragroup monitoring regimes (z=0, local z=1, global z≥n−1).","Summary of the formal game as described in the Model and subsequent sections, focusing on strategic structure and monitoring/punishment relevant to cooperation and free riding.",0.97
10.1093_ajae_aar154.md,"Analytical model variant with no peer monitoring (z=0, collective reputation only via public signal)",Section 'No Peer Monitoring' explicitly analyzes the special case z=0 as a distinct theoretical condition with its own proposition (Proposition 4).,0.93,"Intragroup information structure: no private peer monitoring (z = 0), group size n ≥ 1; other parameters δ, τ, Δ/σ enter analytically but not as discrete treatments.","The only structural change relative to the baseline is turning off peer monitoring by setting z=0, while varying n conceptually.",0.96,False,The z=0 case is a theoretical subcase of the same analytical model; no data or subjects.,1,False,No experiment is run; this is comparative statics within the analytical model.,1,False,No lab or field.,1,False,No computational simulations for this variant.,1,True,Constraints are re-derived for z=0 and Proposition 4 is proved analytically.,1,0,"As before, this is a theoretical model, not an implemented game.",1,0,Infinite horizon repeated game.,1,1,Effort choice remains binary (high vs low).,0.4,N/A,No contributions or endowments.,1,N/R,No public good payoff function.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Only public signal y_t exists conceptually; not an experimental feedback screen.,0.6,N/A,No discrete punishment action in an experiment.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,0,"Infinite horizon, no displayed rounds.",1,1,Public punishment (triggered by low public signal) still operates; peer punishment is absent because z=0.,1,N/A,"Punishment is via equilibrium continuation payoffs, not explicit costly actions.",1,N/A,No per-unit punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward device.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowments are not modeled.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are equilibrium payoffs and existence of cooperation, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"No numeric efficiency index reported for z=0 subcase, only comparative welfare statement that n* ≤ 1 and collective reputation is inferior without peer monitoring.",0.7,N/R,Analytical comparison: proposition states n*≤1 and that collective branding cannot improve on individual branding; no specific numeric payoff instance provided.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical subcase of a theoretical model.,1,"Same repeated-game structure as baseline (n firms, infinite horizon, effort choices high/low, noisy public signal y_t). In this variant, z=0 so firms have no private information about other firms’ choices; strategies depend only on public history. Peer monitoring/sanction is impossible; only public trigger strategies can be used. Proposition 4 shows optimal group size n* ≤ 1, implying firms prefer individual reputation when no peer monitoring is available because free-riding on collective reputation weakens incentives.",Summarizes the specific structural change (z=0) and main comparative result for this condition.,0.96
10.1093_ajae_aar154.md,Analytical model variant with perfect global peer monitoring (z ≥ n−1),Section 'Perfect Global Peer Monitoring' treats z≥n−1 as another polar case with its own main result (Proposition 5).,0.94,"Intragroup information structure: perfect global peer monitoring (each firm observes all others’ efforts, z ≥ n−1), with group size n varying conceptually; parameters δ, τ, Δ/σ as in baseline.","The structural change is global rather than local monitoring, which fundamentally changes punishment speed and the n* result.",0.96,False,"Still within the same theoretical framework, no data.",1,False,No actual experiment conducted.,1,False,Theoretical analysis only.,1,False,"All results are analytical derivations (e.g., condition τ < δ^2/(1−δ^2)).",1,True,Proposition 5 is proved using inequalities and limits as n→∞ with z≥n−1.,1,0,No human players.,1,0,Infinite horizon.,1,1,Effort choice remains binary high/low.,0.4,N/A,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,No public-good MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Only conceptual monitoring via signals; no screens or summaries as in lab designs.,0.6,N/A,No discrete punishment choices in an experiment.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,0,Infinite horizon game.,1,1,Both public punishment (loss of trade) and peer punishment (all firms switch to low effort after one-period delay) are central to this variant; peer punishment becomes immediate and global.,1,N/A,"Punishment is via equilibrium continuation values, not direct costly actions.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology parameter.,1,0,No separate reward device introduced.,1,N/A,No reward actions.,1,N/A,No reward actions.,1,N/R,Endowments not modeled.,1,N/R,"Cooperation here is measured as sustaining high effort, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"Paper notes that as n→∞ under condition τ<δ^2/(1−δ^2), per-firm payoff approaches v_h−c_h, the efficient benchmark, but does not explicitly report a normalized efficiency number.",0.78,N/R,Provides limiting expression (v_h−c_h) and condition τ<δ^2/(1−δ^2) but not a specific numeric payoff under a fixed parameter choice.,0.78,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical theoretical variant.,1,"Same repeated-game setting as baseline, but with perfect global peer monitoring: every firm observes all others’ efforts (z≥n−1). After any deviation, all firms know of shirking in the next period and trigger collective shirking, which, in large n, almost surely destroys customers’ trust due to the highly informative public signal. Proposition 5: if τ < δ^2/(1−δ^2), then as n→∞ the optimal group size n* also tends to infinity and firms can attain the first-best average payoff v_h − c_h, effectively replicating perfect public monitoring through the combination of public and peer sanctions.",Captures the key structural change (global monitoring) and resulting ‘bigger is better’ conclusion under a specific parameter condition.,0.97
10.1103_PhysRevE.84.066115.md,"Simulation – No punishment (v=0.2, b=1.1, ps=0, pa=0)","Condition explicitly described in Fig. 1(a) and text: v=0.2, b=1.1, and punishment not allowed.",1,"Punishment regime: no punishment (ps=0, pa=0) at mobility v=0.2 and temptation b=1.1",Condition differs from others only by punishment parameters ps and pa; v and b are fixed here.,1,False,Study uses agent-based computer simulations with N=1000 artificial agents; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; only simulations.,1,True,Authors repeatedly refer to simulations of moving agents in a 2D plane.,1,False,"While some analytical expressions are given, the focal results are from numerical simulations.",0.9,1000,Section II: 'we study a population of N=1000 individuals'.,1,5693,"For this parameter set, the system reaches all-D earlier than the all-C cases; text only gives an explicit convergence time for one soft nonsocial run (5693 rounds). Number of simulated rounds for this specific condition is not clearly specified; use the reported 5693 as an upper bound proxy.",0.2,1,"Agents choose between two discrete strategies C or D for all neighbors; this is not a continuous contribution game, but within this schema it corresponds to all-or-nothing cooperation choice; coded as 1 to reflect binary full/zero cooperation choice.",0.6,N/A,"This is a Prisoner’s Dilemma, not a public goods contribution game; no endowment allocation.",1,N/R,MPCR is not defined for this pairwise PD payoff matrix.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; artificial agents follow fixed rules.,1,N/A,"No user interface; agents do not 'see' summaries, only payoffs used in update rule.",1,N/A,No identity display to human players; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents are not informed about horizon.,1,0,Condition described as 'punishment is not allowed at all'.,1,