10.1098_rsif.2013.1044.md,"Simulation – Baseline without corruptors, low institutional punishment (B = 0.7)",Condition described in Fig. 1a and the general model: no corruptor strategy; institutional punishment severity parameter B set to 0.7.,0.9,"Presence of corruptor strategy: absent; Institutional punishment severity B = 0.7; Strategy set = {cooperator, defector, loner, peer-punisher, pool-punisher}",Based on text around Fig. 1 describing runs without corruptors and varying B.,0.9,False,Study is an evolutionary game-theoretic model with numerical simulations; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors explicitly refer to numerical simulations of the evolutionary model.,1,True,"Paper states they also derive equilibria analytically in the limit of strong imitation, though this condition focuses on the simulation implementation of that model.",0.8,5,Group size N = 5 reported in Fig. 1 caption; players are randomly sampled groups of size N.,1,100000,Number of simulation steps S = 100000 given in Fig. 1 caption; interpreted as rounds/updates.,0.9,1,"Each individual chooses whether or not to contribute a fixed amount c>0; binary contribution decision rather than continuous, but still a standard public good game choice; coded as allowing a contribution decision (all-or-nothing = 1 since they either give c or 0).",0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment account structure with a default allocation; also no human participants.,1,0.6,"Payoff from public good is r*c*(Nc/N) with r = 3 and group size N = 5, so MPCR = r/N = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface or display; purely simulated payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit identities; interactions are anonymous in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No human participants or display of remaining rounds.,1,1,Both peer and pool punishment strategies ar