stributions.,1,N/R,No contribution amounts are modeled.,1,N/R,"Although they discuss societal welfare, no numeric efficiency metrics are reported.",0.6,N/R,Group payoffs remain symbolic and parameterized; no single numeric payoffs are summarized.,0.6,N/A,Model without human subjects.,1,N/A,Model without human subjects.,1,N/A,Model without human subjects.,1,N/A,Model without human subjects.,1,No human,Pure modeling/simulation work.,1,"Adds strategy RS, which always plays SAFE and, after observing SAFE by its co-player in the previous round, rewards by increasing the co-player’s speed by s_β while reducing its own speed by s_α. The payoff matrix among AS, AU, RS is given explicitly, assuming reward is sufficiently cost-efficient (1 + s_β > s_α). Evolutionary dynamics in finite populations (typically Z=100, β between 0.01 and 0.1) are used to compute stationary frequencies of AU and other strategies over regions (I)-(III) and over (s_α, s_β).",Summary of reward mechanism and evolutionary analysis as described in the Results section.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0244592.md,"Combined punishment and reward model (AS, AU, PS, RS)",Supplementary condition where both PS and RS strategies coexist with AS and AU in the population.,1,"Simultaneous availability of punishment (PS) and reward (RS) strategies, with parameters s_α and s_β, along with risk p_r and unsafe speed s.","SI analyzes a four-strategy population (AS, AU, PS, RS) and studies AU frequencies for varying s_α, s_β and regions (I)-(III).",1,False,No empirical data; only evolutionary modeling.,1,False,Not an experiment with participants; it is theoretical and computational.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Computes transition graphs and stationary distributions for four-strategy Markov chains (S2–S3 Figs).,1,True,Uses analytical payoff definitions of PS and RS interactions derived earlier to parameterize the simulations.,0.8,2,Base game remains two-player; population comprises four possible strategies.,1,100,"Uses same W and parameterization as main text for comparisons (e.g., W=100).",0.7,1,Actions are SAFE/UNSAFE with additional PS/RS rules; no graded contributions.,0.3,N/A,No initial endowment or contribution stage.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR.,1,N/A,No human agents or communication modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,Identities not modeled.,1,N/A,Identities not modeled.,1,N/A,No display of rounds; W is a model parameter only.,1,1,PS strategy is present and sanctions AU (and possibly others that play UNSAFE) by reducing their speed.,1,1,Punishment cost is encoded as speed reduction s_α to PS; we record an abstract unit cost.,0.2,1,Punishment reduces opponent’s speed by s_β; abstracted as 1 unit effect per unit cost.,0.2,1,RS strategy is also present and rewards SAFE co-players by boosting their speed.,1,1,"Reward cost is speed reduction s_α for RS, recorded as 1 abstract unit cost.",0.2,1,Reward increases co-player speed by s_β; abstracted as 1 unit effect per unit cost.,0.2,N/R,No fixed starting endowment is part of the model.,1,N/R,No contribution variable; principal DV is AU frequency in stationary distribution.,1,N/R,No contribution amount modeled.,1,N/R,"Outcomes described via AU frequencies and qualitative welfare, not normalized efficiency.",0.6,N/R,No aggregate numeric group payoff reported.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model-based simulations only.,1,"Four-strategy evolutionary model including AS (always SAFE), AU (always UNSAFE), PS (SAFE plus punishment of UNSAFE) and RS (SAFE plus reward of SAFE). Interactions follow the same repeated DSAIR race, with PS and RS modifying speeds via s_α and s_β. Markov-chain stationary distributions over the four monomorphic states are computed (small mutation limit) to obtain AU frequencies for different regions (I)-(III) and for grids of (s_α, s_β). Results show that PS tends to dominate over RS, and AU frequencies resemble the punishment-only case.",Summary of the combined incentives scenario from Supporting Information (S2–S3 Figs).,1
10.1098_rsif.2024.0547.md,Peer incentives – peer punishment condition,"Section 3.1 defines a model with strategies C, D, and SP (social punisher) and Figure 1 reports outcomes for this peer punishment case.",1,"Type of peer incentive = punishment; impact-to-cost ratio δ/ε varied from 0 to 5; selection intensity β varied (e.g., 1.0, 0.1, 0.01, 0.001); mutation rate μ fixed at 0.01; population size N=50; PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; cost of peer incentive ε=1.","Peer punishment versus reward is the main treatment difference; within this condition, figures vary δ/ε and β while holding other parameters fixed as listed in the Figure 1 caption and main text.",0.9,False,Authors use evolutionary game theory and Markov chains with no human participants; ethics section confirms no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled lab/online experiment; only theoretical and numerical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,True,"They numerically compute mutation-selection stationary distributions for stochastic evolutionary dynamics; code is provided, indicating computational simulations.",1,True,"They derive closed forms for long-term expected social welfare and describe analytical Markov chain structure, alongside simulations.",0.9,50,"Figure 1 caption: 'population size, N=50'.",1,1,"They model a one-shot Prisoner's Dilemma repeatedly played in evolutionary time; each strategic interaction is one-shot (single round per encounter), so game length per interaction is 1.",0.8,1,"In a PD with C vs D, each player chooses a binary action, not a continuous contribution amount; therefore not an all-or-nothing contribution game in public-goods sense. For this schema, set 1 to indicate free choice of either action.",0.6,0,Prisoner’s Dilemma does not use an endowment/public pool framing; by convention for non-contribution games set to 0.,0.5,N/R,"MPCR is defined for public goods games; here the stage game is a 2×2 PD with payoffs R,S,T,P, and no multiplier or group size parameter for a public good.",1,0,No communication mechanism appears in the formal model.,1,0,Players are evolutionary agents with payoffs determined by matrix; no informational feedback about others is modelled.,1,0,Model does not include identities or observability of who punishes; incentives are purely payoff-based.,1,0,This condition has only punishment as peer incentive; no rewards are implemented.,1,0,Agents do not observe a horizon; evolutionary process is infinite-horizon Markov chain without explicit round display.,1,1,Section 3.1: SP pays cost ε to reduce defector’s payoff by δ.,1,1,Figure 1 parameters: 'cost of peer incentive ε=1'; punishment is the peer incentive here.,1,1,"Impact-to-cost ratio is δ/ε; with ε=1, the effect per unit cost equals δ. One unit of cost ε=1 yields δ units impact, so per-cost impact ratio is δ; however, at the unit scale (per ε) this is δ. Since schema asks per-coin magnitude, use δ/ε; at ε=1, this simplifies to 1×δ. Because δ is varied, a single numeric value cannot be fixed; we set it to 1 as the base scaling per ε and note variation in indep_var.",0.3,0,Peer reward is analysed in a separate model with SR; this condition includes only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/R,"Prisoner’s Dilemma is defined directly via payoff matrix R,S,T,P without an explicit monetary endowment per round.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported are frequency of cooperation f_C and social welfare SW over the evolutionary process, not normalized contributions of an endowment.",1,N/R,"No token-based contribution levels are defined, only binary actions C and D.",1,N/R,"They discuss social welfare as total payoff, not normalized efficiency relative to a maximum benchmark.",1,N/R,"Figures show qualitative and relative levels of SW, but no explicit numeric group payoff per game/round is given.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is based on evolutionary game theory models and simulations without human data.,1,"Well-mixed finite population N=50 uses one-shot 2×2 Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0. Three strategies: unconditional cooperator (C), unconditional defector (D), and social punisher (SP) that cooperates and then pays cost ε to reduce defectors’ payoffs by δ. Dynamics follow a mutation–selection process with mutation rate μ=0.01 and Fermi imitation with selection intensity β. Stationary distribution of the Markov chain is used to compute the long-term frequency of cooperation f_C and social welfare SW.",Directly summarized from Sections 2.1–2.2 and 3.1 plus Figure 1 caption.,1
10.1098_rsif.2024.0547.md,Peer incentives – peer reward condition,"Section 3.1 also defines a model with strategies C, D, and SR (social rewarder); Figure 1 compares this to the punishment case.",1,"Type of peer incentive = reward; impact-to-cost ratio δ/ε varied from 0 to 5; selection intensity β varied (e.g., 1.0, 0.1, 0.01, 0.001); mutation rate μ fixed at 0.01; population size N=50; PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0; cost of peer incentive ε=1.",The reward case mirrors the punishment setup but replaces SP with SR; Figure 1 directly compares both while varying δ/ε and β under the same background parameters.,0.9,False,Same modelling framework as the punishment case; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a behavioural experiment; theoretical and simulation-based.,1,False,No physical or online lab environment.,1,True,Mutation-selection Markov chain numerically evaluated for this peer reward model.,1,True,"Same analytical derivations apply (closed forms for welfare, transition probabilities) as stated in methods.",0.9,50,"Figure 1 caption: 'population size, N=50'.",1,1,Underlying game is a one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma; each pairwise interaction is one round.,0.8,1,Binary action (C or D) without constrained all-or-nothing contribution mechanic; consistent treatment with punishment condition.,0.6,0,No public-goods style endowment; PD payoff matrix used directly.,0.5,N/R,No public good or multiplier defined; only PD payoffs.,1,0,No explicit communication between agents.,1,0,Model omits informational feedback beyond payoffs and imitation probabilities.,1,0,This condition uses only reward incentives; no punishment.,1,0,No notion of individual identities or visibility of who rewards whom in the model.,1,0,"Evolutionary process has no finite, known horizon for agents.",1,0,Peer punishment is not included in the SR model; only reward is applied.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,1,Section 3.1: SR cooperates and then pays cost ε to increase payoffs of cooperative co-players by δ.,1,1,Figure 1 parameters: 'cost of peer incentive ε=1'; here the incentive is reward.,1,1,"Impact-to-cost ratio is δ/ε; with ε=1, a unit incentive cost yields δ payoff increase for recipients. δ is varied, so a single fixed magnitude cannot be specified; we record 1 as the base per-unit scaling and describe δ variation in indep_var.",0.3,N/R,"No explicit token endowment is specified; game defined directly via R,S,T,P.",1,N/R,"Reported outcome is the long-term frequency of cooperation f_C, not contribution fraction of a monetary endowment.",1,N/R,No continuous contribution variable defined.,1,N/R,They do not normalize social welfare by a theoretical maximum; only raw SW comparisons are shown.,1,N/R,Figures show qualitative SW as a function of parameters without explicit numeric values for a single game or state.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and computational study.,1,"Same evolutionary PD framework as the peer punishment model, but strategies are unconditional cooperator (C), unconditional defector (D), and social rewarder (SR). SR cooperates in the PD and then pays cost ε to increase a cooperative co-player’s payoff by δ. Population is well-mixed, N=50, with mutation rate μ=0.01 and Fermi imitation with selection intensity β. Long-run cooperation level f_C and social welfare SW are computed from the stationary distribution.",Summarized from Section 3.1 and Figure 1 caption.,1
10.1098_rsif.2024.0547.md,Institutional incentives – institutional reward condition,"Section 3.2 distinguishes institutional reward from punishment; Figure 2 panels (a,c) concern reward.",1,"Type of institutional incentive = reward; institutional efficiency parameter a varied (e.g., a>1, a=1, a=0.7); incentive impact δ varied; population size N=50; mutation rate μ=0.001; selection intensity β=0.1; PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0.","Institutional reward vs punishment is the main manipulation; within reward, Figure 2 varies efficiency a and impact δ while holding other parameters fixed (caption and text).",0.9,False,"Institutional incentives are analysed via evolutionary models and simulations, not human experiments.",1,False,No experimental data collection; purely modelling.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,They compute social welfare and cooperation numerically from the institutional incentive Markov chain (population size N=50).,1,True,Closed-form expressions for social welfare with external intervention are derived in Section 2.3.,0.9,50,"Figure 2 caption: 'population size, N=50'.",1,1,Underlying interaction is a one-shot PD; institutional incentives modify payoffs per interaction.,0.8,1,Game is a PD with binary choices; not an all-or-nothing contribution structure; consistent coding as 1 as in prior conditions.,0.6,0,No explicit contribution pool or endowment used in this model.,0.5,N/R,No public goods multiplier provided; PD payoff matrix is used.,1,0,No communication modeled between agents; only institutional incentives and payoffs matter.,1,0,No visibility of others’ earnings or sanctions is specified; dynamics driven by payoffs and imitation.,1,0,Institution applies incentives impersonally; no mention of identities or observability of punished individuals.,1,0,"Similarly, no individual-level identity for rewarded players is modelled.",1,0,Agents do not observe how many evolutionary steps remain; process is ongoing.,1,0,This condition focuses on institutional reward only; punishment is analysed separately.,1,N/A,No institutional punishment applied in this condition.,1,N/A,Punishment is not present.,1,1,"Section 3.2: to reward a cooperator, institution pays δ/a so the cooperator’s payoff increases by δ.",1,N/R,"Per-reward cost is δ/a, but δ and a are varied across parameter space; no single numeric value is specified for this condition.",0.3,N/R,"Per-unit cost-effectiveness is represented by efficiency parameter a; because cost is δ/a for impact δ, the per-unit cost impact ratio is a, which itself is varied; no single fixed numeric value is reported.",0.4,N/R,The institutional incentive model uses PD payoffs directly without defining an endowment.,1,N/R,"Results focus on 'level of cooperation' (frequency of cooperators) and social welfare, not normalized contributions of tokens.",1,N/R,No token contribution variable.,1,N/R,They analyse social welfare SW(Θ)-E(Θ) but do not normalize it to define an efficiency ratio between 0 and 1.,1,N/R,Figure 2 shows regions of positive/negative social welfare but not explicit group payoff values.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulations and analytical calculations only.,1,"Well-mixed finite population of N=50 individuals plays one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma (R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0) with two strategies: C and D. An external institution rewards cooperators by increasing their payoff by δ at a per-capita cost δ/a, where a>0 denotes the efficiency of reward. The incentive budget depends on the state (composition of C and D) and affects transition probabilities via modified payoffs. Mutation rate μ=0.001 and Fermi imitation with selection intensity β=0.1 define the mutation–selection dynamics. Social welfare is defined as total population payoff minus expected institutional cost, evaluated in the stationary distribution.",Summarized from Sections 2.2–2.3 and 3.2 plus Figure 2 caption.,1
10.1098_rsif.2024.0547.md,Institutional incentives – institutional punishment condition,"Section 3.2 analyses institutional punishment as the counterpart to reward; Figure 2 panels (b,d) describe punishment effects.",1,"Type of institutional incentive = punishment; institutional efficiency parameter b varied (e.g., b>2, b=3); incentive impact δ varied; population size N=50; mutation rate μ=0.001; selection intensity β=0.1; PD payoffs R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0.",Punishment vs reward is the main mechanism difference; Figure 2 discusses how varying b and δ affects cooperation and social welfare while holding other parameters fixed.,0.9,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical and computational.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioural experiment.,1,False,No physical lab or field site is involved.,1,True,Institutional punishment outcomes are obtained from numerical evaluation of the Markov chain.,1,True,"Same analytical setup (transition probabilities, welfare with intervention) is used for punishment.",0.9,50,Figure 2 caption specifies N=50.,1,1,Interactions are one-shot PD games; punishment modifies per-interaction payoffs.,0.8,1,Binary cooperation/defection choice; consistent coding with other PD conditions.,0.6,0,No endowment/public good structure.,0.5,N/R,Game is a PD rather than a linear public goods game with a multiplier.,1,0,No communication is modelled.,1,0,Agents do not receive explicit summaries of others’ outcomes.,1,0,Institutional punishment is impersonal; no identity visibility is described.,1,0,No rewards exist in this condition.,1,0,Evolution is ongoing; no explicit horizon in the agents’ information set.,1,1,"Section 3.2: to punish a defector, the institution pays δ/b so the defector’s payoff decreases by δ.",1,N/R,"Cost per punished defector is δ/b, but δ and b are varied; no single numeric value given.",0.3,N/R,"Effectiveness per unit cost is b, but b is itself a varied parameter; no fixed numeric value for this condition.",0.4,0,Institutional punishment only; reward is analysed separately.,1,N/A,No institutional rewards are used here.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment; PD payoffs given directly.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are cooperation level and social welfare as functions of (b, δ), not contribution fractions.",1,N/R,No continuous contribution choice.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency metric between 0 and 1 is given; only sign and relative magnitude of social welfare.,1,N/R,"They focus on whether social welfare is positive/negative and where it is maximized, without explicit payoff numbers.",0.7,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,The condition is a theoretical/simulation model with no subjects.,1,"Same two-strategy (C and D) one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma (R=1, S=-1, T=2, P=0) in a well-mixed population of size N=50. An external institution punishes defectors by decreasing their payoff by δ at cost δ/b to the institution, where b>0 is a punishment efficiency parameter. Incentive policies determine state-dependent budgets and modify payoffs, affecting the mutation–selection Markov chain with μ=0.001 and Fermi imitation at β=0.1. Social welfare is total population payoff minus institutional punishment costs in the stationary distribution, with emphasis on how it changes over b and δ.",Derived from Sections 2.3 and 3.2 and Figure 2 discussion.,1
