essions.,1,On site,Physical laboratory sessions with z-Tree.,1,"Same linear VCM as baseline. Added Stage 2: after seeing all contributions, each subject can assign 0–6 approval points to each of 3 others, up to 18 total; anonymous, costless, no payoff effect; subjects see aggregate approval received by each contribution level.",Captures approval-only social communication mechanism.,1
10.1111_j.1465-7295.2011.00422.x.md,Approval-Disapproval (A-D),"Fourth treatment, labeled 'approval-disapproval (A-D)'.",1,"Presence and type of non-monetary rating: second stage where subjects may assign either approval or disapproval points (0–6) to each other member, but not both to the same member.","Design section: in A-D, a subject can assign either approval or disapproval to each member; both options available institutionally.",1,True,Same human-subject lab framework.,1,True,Controlled treatment manipulating combined availability of positive and negative non-monetary feedback.,1,True,Sessions conducted in University of Calgary lab.,1,False,No simulation.,1,False,Not an analytical-only study.,1,4,Groups of 4 in all treatments.,1,15,15 periods per session.,1,1,Contributions are chosen as a quantity of tokens 0–20; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Same as other treatments: endowment in private account by default.,1,0.4,Public good technology unchanged.,1,0,No free-form communication; only structured point assignments.,1,1,End-of-period info includes each contribution and two aggregate scores (approval and disapproval) per member.,1,0,Points (both types) are assigned anonymously; IDs not shown.,1,0,Same anonymity; approval receivers not identified by ID.,1,1,Same 15-period finitely repeated structure likely announced.,0.7,1,Subjects can assign disapproval points (non-pecuniary punishment).,1,0,Points are costless and payoff-neutral.,1,0,Disapproval points have no payoff effect.,1,1,Approval points can also be assigned in this treatment.,1,0,Approval points are costless and have no payoff effect.,1,0,Approval points are payoff-neutral.,1,20,Same per-period endowment.,1,0.5945,Average individual contribution 11.89 out of 20 (Table 1). 11.89/20 ≈ 0.5945.,1,11.89,Table 1: 'Average (SD)' for Approval-Disapproval treatment.,1,N/R,Earnings series illustrated in Figure 2 but no numeric efficiency ratio provided.,0.2,N/R,No numeric average group payoff for A-D separately.,0.2,Canada,Same lab and population.,1,N/R,Age data not reported.,0.3,N/R,No gender info in text.,0,Undergraduate university students,All subjects described as undergraduates.,1,On site,Behavioral lab setting with physical attendance.,1,"Same linear VCM. Stage 2: each subject can assign either 0–6 approval or 0–6 disapproval points (integers) to each other member, but not both types to the same person; up to 18 points across the three others. Points are anonymous, costless, and payoff-neutral. At period end, subjects see each member's contribution and two aggregate scores (approval and disapproval) received.",Summarizes combined non-monetary reward and punishment institution.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2018.03.054.md,Simulation – baseline multigame with social punishment,"Paper describes a single simulation framework (square lattice multigame with punishment) in which key parameters (b, δ, v) are varied; there are no separately named experiments, so the whole parameterized simulation is treated as one condition family.",0.7,"Temptation to defect b, sucker’s payoff magnitude δ (sign depends on type: −δ for type A, +δ for type B), and proportion of type A players v (type A play PD, type B play SD). Social punishment parameters fixed at β=0.3, γ=0.1.","Methods and Results specify that b, δ, and v are systematically varied (e.g., Fig. 1 varies b for v=0.5 and different δ; Fig. 2 varies δ and v for fixed b=1.44); β and γ are constants.",0.95,False,Study is a numerical simulation on a lattice; no human or animal subjects are mentioned.,1,False,This is not a controlled behavioral or field experiment but a computational evolutionary game simulation.,1,False,No laboratory with human participants; purely computational model run.,1,True,Methods section clearly describes Monte Carlo steps on a square lattice with N=4×10^4 players as a numerical simulation.,1,False,No closed-form analytical treatment or proofs; results are all numerical simulations.,1,40000,Explicitly stated: 'All simulation results are obtained on the square lattice with N = 4 × 10^4 players.',1,300000,They run until stationary state with 3×10^5 MCS; treat each Monte Carlo step as a round of evolutionary updating.,0.9,1,"Players choose among three discrete strategies (C, D, Pu); there is no continuous contribution level, so the 'contribution amount' choice is effectively all-or-nothing in terms of cooperating versus defecting.",0.8,N/A,This is not a public goods or contribution game with an explicit endowment and contribution fraction; only pairwise PD/SD payoffs are modeled.,1,N/R,No public goods production function or marginal per capita return is defined; only pairwise payoff matrices for PD and SD are given.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely computational agents with no chat or messaging.,1,N/A,Agents do not receive explicit payoff or punishment summaries as experimental interface elements; they just observe neighbors’ payoffs via the update rule in the simulation.,1,N/A,No notion of identity visibility exists in the simulation; there is only local interaction and payoff-based updating.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism modeled; only punishment as a game payoff modification.,1,N/A,There is no experimental interface for participants; Monte Carlo steps are controlled by the simulation code only.,1,1,The third strategy 'social punishment (Pu)' is explicitly modeled and used in all simulations.,1,0.1,Methods: 'the values can be set as β = 0.3 and γ = 0.1'; γ is the cost to punishers.,1,0.3,β represents the fine imposed on defectors per punishment; set to 0.3.,1,0,No reward or positive incentive strategy is introduced; only punishment is added.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/R,"Game is formulated directly in terms of payoffs R, P, T, S; there is no explicit per-round endowment reported.",0.9,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as fractions of strategy types (ρ⟨c⟩, ρc, ρd, ρp) and payoffs, not as normalized contribution rates from an explicit endowment.",0.8,N/R,"No token-level contribution decisions are modeled; players simply choose strategies C, D, or Pu.",0.9,N/R,"They report average payoff ⟨P⟩ and fractions of cooperators/punishers, but do not normalize payoffs to a theoretical maximum group payoff.",0.9,N/R,Average payoff over the population ⟨P⟩ is discussed qualitatively (Fig. 4b) but no numerical group payoff values are provided.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire study is a numerical evolutionary game simulation without human or field data.,1,"Structured population on a square lattice with periodic boundary conditions and degree 4; two player types: type A play PD with payoffs (R=1, P=0, T=b, S=−δ), type B play SD with (R=1, P=0, T=b, S=+δ); three strategies C, D, Pu with payoff matrix including punishment cost γ and fine β; strategy updating via pairwise comparison with Fermi rule W(sx←sy)=1/[1+exp((Ux−Uy)/K)], K=0.1. Initial strategies C, D, Pu are assigned at random with equal probability. Fractions of cooperators and punishers (ρ⟨c⟩, ρc, ρd, ρp) are measured in the stationary state over last 3×10^4 MCS after total 3×10^5 MCS.","Summarizes key structural and dynamical details from the Methods section such as lattice, payoff rescaling, strategies, and update rule.",1
10.1007_s11269-017-1661-2.md,A1 – Local government vs MWR (analytical model and numerical simulations),"Single evolutionary game system A1 between local government i and MWR, analytically derived and then numerically simulated with different parameter sets.",1,"Payoff and supervision parameters determining inequalities: case 1 (σc_i−(ε+ηπ_i)>P and ωP>mM), case 2 (ηπ_i<σc_i and ωP<mM), case 3 (ηπ_i>σc_i). This row refers to the general analytical model over these parameter regimes.","The model varies parameters (η, σ, π_i, c_i, ω, m, P, M, ε) and studies resulting ESS; three parameter regimes are discussed. This entry aggregates the analytical treatment since structure is otherwise identical.",0.78,False,No human participants; purely theoretical and simulated evolutionary game.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical model plus numerical simulation.,1,False,No lab setting or human subjects.,1,True,Replicator dynamics numerically simulated with Matlab for different parameter values.,1,True,"Replicator equations, Jacobian, and stability conditions derived analytically.",1,2,System A1 is a two-player game between a representative local government i and MWR.,1,0,"Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics over t, not discrete repeated rounds; use 0 to indicate N/A for standard round count.",0.6,1,"Strategies are binary (strong vs weak), but there is no contribution choice as in a PGG; all-or-nothing contribution notion does not apply, coded as general/any (1) by default.",0.3,N/A,No contribution or endowment structure; purely strategic government willingness and supervision.,1,N/R,No public goods structure or explicit MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication stage.,1,0,No information feedback of others’ earnings/punishments described; model only specifies payoffs and evolution.,0.7,N/A,No identities or human players; penalties are parameters in payoff functions.,1,N/A,No individual rewarding among humans.,1,0,Agents do not observe a number of rounds; continuous evolutionary process.,0.9,1,Penalty P is imposed by MWR when local government shows weak willingness under strong supervision.,1,N/R,Cost of imposing punishment P to MWR is not defined as a separate per-unit or variable cost; only supervision cost M and mM are specified.,0.4,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment spending to damage; P enters directly as payoff loss.,0.4,1,Reward ε (increase in performance evaluation score and political achievement) is given when local government has strong willingness under strong supervision.,1,N/R,No explicit cost to MWR for granting ε is stated; only supervision costs.,0.4,N/R,"No per-unit reward technology specification, only direct payoff term ε.",0.4,N/R,No token or monetary endowments are defined; only costs and benefits in symbolic form.,1,N/R,No contributions to a public good; main outcomes are strategy frequencies and ESS.,1,N/R,Not applicable; no contribution variable.,1,N/R,No explicit calculation of total payoffs relative to maximum; only qualitative efficiency implications.,0.5,N/R,Model lists symbolic payoffs but does not report aggregate numeric group payoffs as outcome statistics.,0.7,N/A,No human participants in the analytical/simulation model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is purely theoretical and computational with no subjects.,1,"Two-player asymmetric evolutionary game between MWR and a representative local government i; strategies: local government chooses strong vs weak implementation willingness; MWR chooses strong vs weak supervision. Payoff parameters include local emergency cost c_i, additional cost σc_i for strong willingness, profit π_i and increased profit ηπ_i, system profit π_g and increase gπ_g, supervision cost M and additional cost mM for strong supervision, penalty P for weak implementation detected, reputational benefit ωP, and reward ε for strong implementation under strong supervision. Replicator dynamics defined for probabilities x_t (strong implementation) and y_t (strong supervision); stability analyzed via Jacobian and inequalities. Numerical simulations in Matlab illustrate convergence under different parameter regimes and initial conditions.",Summarizes the game structure and core parameters as described in Sections 3 and 4.,1
