10.1007_s11269-017-1661-2.md,A2 – Local–local cooperation (Henan vs Hebei) analytical model and numerical simulations,System A2 evolutionary game between Henan and Hebei with analytical derivation and simulations under varying parameters.,1,"Inter-local cooperation parameters: basic profits R1,R2; basic cost C; additional cost factors θ,β; political loss F and adjustment λ; cooperation profit multiplier α. Analytical results consider regimes λF< (θ−β)C and λF> (θ−β)C.",These parameters define the payoff matrix in Table 2 and the conditions for different equilibria.,1,False,No human players; theoretical and simulated.,1,False,"Not an empirical experiment, but analytical modeling.",1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,True,Matlab simulations shown in Figs. 6 and 7.,1,True,"Derivation of replicator equations, equilibrium points, and Jacobian-based stability for A2.",1,2,Game played between Henan (B1) and Hebei (B2).,1,0,Continuous-time replicator dynamics in period t.,0.9,1,Each local government chooses strong vs weak cooperative willingness; no graded contributions.,0.4,N/A,No public good contribution.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,Model does not involve communication among human subjects.,1,0,No mechanism of showing payoff summaries; model only defines payoffs.,0.7,N/A,No human identities; penalties are abstract political losses.,1,N/A,No individual reward attribution to identities.,1,0,No finite number of rounds shown.,0.9,1,Political loss F and λF act as punishment for weak cooperation.,1,N/R,No explicit cost of imposing political penalties given.,0.4,N/R,Punishment represented by payoff reduction F and λF; no per-cost multiplier.,0.5,0,No explicit positive reward mechanism separate from baseline payoffs; cooperation yields higher profits but not modeled as a discrete reward action.,0.7,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment defined; only costs and profits.,1,N/R,"Key outcomes are cooperation probabilities p_t and q_t, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Paper qualitatively discusses cooperative order but does not compute efficiency metrics.,0.5,N/R,Symbolic payoffs given; no reported aggregate numeric payoffs.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Theoretical and simulation study only.,1,"Two-player symmetric-style evolutionary game between Henan (B1) and Hebei (B2). Strategies: strong vs weak cooperative willingness. Base profits R1,R2 when weak cooperation; if both strongly cooperate, each obtains αR_j−C; if one strong, one weak, the strong pays higher cost θC while the weak pays C and suffers political loss F; if both weak, each pays βC and suffers political loss λF. Replicator dynamics defined for p_t (Henan strong) and q_t (Hebei strong); equilibria at (0,0), (1,1), (0,1), (1,0), and interior point (p_t*, q_t*). Stability depends on λF relative to (θ−β)C: if λF<(θ−β)C, both (0,0) and (1,1) can be ESS; if λF>(θ−β)C, (1,1) is ESS. Numerical simulations in Figs. 6 and 7 illustrate dynamics under specific parameterizations.","Synthesizes description in Sections 3.2.2, 3.3, and 4.",1
10.1007_s11269-017-1661-2.md,"A2 – Numerical case I (λF<(θ−β)C) – bistability of (0,0) and (1,1)","Section 4, numerical analysis point (4) with specific parameters for A2 and showing two possible attractors.",1,"Parameter set: R1=R2=18, C=9, θ=2, α=1.1, β=1.1, F=3, λ=1.1 satisfying λF<(θ−β)C; four different initial states (not numerically listed) for (p_t,q_t).",These exact values and inequality are given for Fig. 6 simulations.,1,False,Simulation of theoretical model; no human data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory context.,1,True,Dynamic evolution simulated with Matlab for this parameter set.,1,False,Row focuses on numerical illustration of already-derived A2.,0.8,2,Henan and Hebei as players.,1,0,Continuous dynamics; no discrete rounds.,0.9,1,Binary cooperation willingness.,0.4,N/A,No contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR concept.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication.,1,0,No feedback mechanism.,0.7,N/A,No human IDs.,1,N/A,No human IDs.,1,0,No finite rounds.,0.9,1,Political loss F=3 and adjusted λF operate as punishment.,1,N/R,No explicit resource cost for imposing F.,0.4,N/R,"Modeled as fixed payoff loss, not a function of expenditure.",0.5,0,No separate reward mechanism beyond higher cooperative payoffs.,0.7,N/A,No explicit reward action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward action.,1,N/R,No endowment reported.,1,N/R,"Key outcome is which ESS (0,0) or (1,1) is reached.",1,N/R,No contribution measure.,1,N/R,"No numeric efficiency computed, though text notes instability and risk of weak cooperation.",0.5,N/R,No aggregate payoff values after convergence reported.,0.6,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation of model A2.,1,"Using A2 game with R1=R2=18, C=9, θ=2, α=1.1, β=1.1, F=3, λ=1.1, which implies λF<(θ−β)C. Simulations from four different initial (p_t,q_t) states show that trajectories may converge to either (0,0) (both weak cooperation) or (1,1) (both strong cooperation). Thus, there are two stable equilibria and the long-run outcome depends on initial conditions, making overall cooperative order unstable.",Summarizes narrative for Fig. 6.,1
