10.1016_j.jebo.2018.04.002.md,"Additional Experiment 2 – Voting (Group size 12, democratic punishment rule)",Separate one-shot experiment labeled 'Additional Experiment 2 (Voting)' with group size 12 and median voting on punishment levels.,1,Collective median-vote punishment rule; group size N=12; number of third party punishers=10,Key manipulation is that third parties vote over punishment levels rather than choose unilaterally; group size fixed at 12.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 48 students.,1,True,Controlled treatment with binding voting scheme.,1,True,Same lab and software as other treatments.,1,False,"Involves human decision-making, not just simulation.",1,False,Not a purely theoretical model.,1,12,"Group size 12 (2 PD players, 10 potential third parties).",1,1,Described as a one-shot experiment with group size 12.,0.9,0,Each third party votes on a non-negative integer punishment level per scenario; not restricted to only 0 or max.,0.9,0,Same PD payoff structure; endowment is private.,0.9,N/R,"PD game, no MPCR.",1,0,No communication among third parties reported; decisions made individually through voting interface.,0.8,0,No mention of interim feedback; only PD players informed of sending decisions at end.,0.8,0,Voting is anonymous over levels; individual third-party identities behind punishment not revealed to PD players.,0.7,0,No rewarding option.,1,1,One-shot; subjects know this is the game for payment.,0.9,1,Group-level punishment is implemented according to voted rule.,1,1,"Implicitly same punishment technology as original: each third party spends c points, incurring cost c.",0.9,3,Same as other experiments: each reduction point reduces PD payoff by 3.,0.9,0,No provision for rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,25,Same underlying PD; no change to stage-1 endowment described.,0.8,0.646,Text: 'The percentage of those who sent 10 points was 64.6%.',1,10,Cooperation defined as sending 10 points.,1,N/R,Only expected payoffs are reported; no explicit efficiency ratio.,0.2,N/R,No average group payoff statistic provided.,0.2,United Kingdom,Same lab and student pool.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender details.,0.1,University students,48 students at the University of York participated.,1,On site,Same experimental laboratory and protocol.,1,"Game: same 2-player PD with send 10 or not. Roles: 2 PD players, 10 third-party players per group. Procedure: PD decisions made first. Then 10 third parties vote, via strategy method, on how many punishment points each third party should impose in each of four scenarios (a–d). The median vote in each scenario determines binding punishment level c; total punishment applied to a PD player in that scenario is 10×c. Group-level vote outcomes in four groups: scenario b medians 2,1,2,5; scenario d medians 0,0,2,3; overall median across all third parties: (a)0,(b)2,(c)0,(d)1. Result: no cooperators received punishment; mutual cooperation is unique NE; expected payoff from cooperation 34.38, from defection −5.00.","Summarized from Section 6 and Fig. 5, including panel (C) vote outcomes.",1
10.1016_j.econlet.2014.05.017.md,Single treatment – VCM with conditional and unconditional punishment,Paper describes one experimental design; no separate treatment or control conditions are reported.,0.95,Not varied across conditions; single treatment with voluntary contribution mechanism and peer punishment (conditional and unconditional stages).,Design description shows a single structure; there is no mention of alternative institutions or parameter changes across groups/sessions.,0.95,True,52 undergraduate students participated in laboratory experiments.,1,True,Controlled decision-making task with random assignment to groups and specified payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted at University of Michigan-Ann Arbor; standard lab experiment setup implied.,0.98,False,No mention of computer/agent-based simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,Paper reports experimental results rather than formal analytical model.,1,4,“Each subject is randomly assigned to a group of four.”,1,1,Design describes a one-shot VCM followed by conditional and unconditional punishment decisions; no repetition over multiple periods reported.,0.7,0,Allocation decision is binary: allocate all 10 points to private or public account; no intermediate contributions allowed.,1,0,Subjects are given an endowment of 10 points and then decide where to allocate it; default is entirely in private account.,0.9,0.5,"Explicitly stated: “In other words, the MPCR (Marginal Per-Capita Return) is 0.5.”",1,0,No mention of any communication or chat; standard neutrally framed VCM with punishment.,0.9,1,"In unconditional punishment stage, subjects are informed of allocation decisions of the three other members; they must infer payoffs from known structure. This counts as seeing others’ outcome-related information.",0.75,0,No statement that identities of punishers are revealed; standard design in similar studies keeps punishment anonymous.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism in the design; thus identities of rewarders cannot be shown.,1,1,One-shot design; participants necessarily know the number of rounds from instructions describing the task.,0.8,1,Both conditional and unconditional punishment stages where subjects assign punishment points.,1,1,"“For each punishment point he assigns to a member, he loses one point from his payoff.”",1,3,“…and the punished loses three points from her payoff.”,1,0,Design only discusses punishment points and fines; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,“Each subject…is given an endowment of 10 points.”,1,N/R,"Paper reports classifications of types and punishment behavior, but not aggregate cooperation rates or proportions contributing to public account.",0,N/R,No numerical summary of how many subjects allocated endowment to public vs private account.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency statistics reported.,0,N/R,Average or total group payoffs are not reported.,0,United States,Experiments conducted at University of Michigan-Ann Arbor in the United States.,0.95,N/R,Subjects described as undergraduate students; no specific age statistics given.,0.6,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,0,Undergraduate students,“52 undergraduate students participated in the experiments.”,1,On site,Conducted at a university lab with physical attendance implied; not described as online.,0.95,"Public goods game (voluntary contribution mechanism) with group size 4, binary contribution (all 10 points to public or private). Conditional punishment stage uses a strategy method with 18 decisions per subject: for each target type (cooperator or non-cooperator) and each possible average punishment by the other two group members (0.0 to 4.0 in steps of 0.5), subject chooses an integer number of punishment points (0–4). Subsequently, an unconditional punishment stage where subjects see others’ allocation decisions and choose punishment points for each other member. One conditional punishment schedule per group is randomly selected to determine payoffs together with unconditional punishment decisions.",Summarizes detailed design elements explicitly described in the experimental design section.,1
10.1038_s41598-019-44725-y.md,Well-mixed population – synchronous exclusion,"Paper analyzes a finite-size, well-mixed population under two exclusion mechanisms; this row isolates the synchronous exclusion case.",1,"Exclusion mechanism = synchronous; parameters varied in analysis: investment amplification factor r, loner payoff σ, exclusion success probability β","Synchronous vs asynchronous exclusion is the primary mechanism compared; within this mechanism they scan r, σ, β.",1,False,No human participants; all results are theoretical and numerical.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a theoretical/numerical study.,1,False,No laboratory or human setting.,1,True,They run numerical experiments of a Markov process model in a finite well-mixed population.,1,True,They derive analytical payoff expressions and Markov-process-based dynamics and analyze stochastic stable equilibria.,1,20,They fix population size M=20 for main well-mixed results.,1,1,Stage game is repeated through evolutionary time but payoffs defined per sampled interaction; modeled as continuous-time Markov process rather than discrete repeated rounds; record as 1 for the underlying PGG interaction.,0.4,1,Model assumes a fixed investment cost of 1 for cooperators; no choice of intermediate contribution levels; thus contribution decision is effectively all-or-nothing.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects; endowment framing not applicable.,1,0.2,"Standard PGG payoff: total contributions multiplied by r and divided among N players; MPCR = r/N. They fix N=5 and consider r between 1 and 4.5, so MPCR ranges from 0.2 to 0.9; no single fixed value, but base N gives MPCR functional form r/5, so using nominal N-based MPCR 1/5=0.2.",0.3,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,N/A,No user interface; purely modeled payoffs.,1,N/A,No identities; exclusion is a modeled mechanism without agents observing IDs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in model.,1,N/A,No human subjects or interface.,1,1,Exclusion is modeled as a costly sanction preventing defectors from sharing benefits; treated as a form of punishment.,1,0,"Excluders pay a unit exclusion cost c_E per targeted defector. They set c_E = 0.2 * 10^β; cost proportional to number of targeted defectors. Per-unit punishment cost parameter is c_E; no single numeric value since β varies, so record baseline coefficient 0.2 and note dependence.",0.3,N/R,They specify a probability β of successful exclusion and that excluded defectors get no benefit; they do not define a linear 'fine per cost unit' parameter.,0.3,0,"Model includes only cooperation, defection, loner, and exclusion; no prosocial reward mechanism.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Investment cost of cooperation is normalized to 1; effectively each cooperator contributes an endowment of 1.,0.9,N/R,"They report probabilities of stochastic stable states (e.g., All C, All D, All L, C+E), not normalized contribution rates.",0,N/R,No explicit average contribution amounts are reported; strategies are binary.,0,N/R,"They compare probabilities of cooperative states, not payoff efficiency ratios.",0,N/R,Group payoffs are used symbolically in payoff functions but no aggregate numeric results are reported.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study uses mathematical modeling and numerical simulations only.,1,"Optional public goods game with four strategies (C, D, L, E) in a finite well-mixed population; group size N=5 sampled from M=20; r between 1 and ~4.5; loner payoff σ; exclusion success probability β; excluders bear cost c_E=0.2*10^β per targeted defector; asynchronous vs synchronous exclusion differ only in exclusion cost structure and success probability aggregation.",Summarizes key structural details explicitly described for the well-mixed model.,1
10.1038_s41598-019-44725-y.md,Well-mixed population – asynchronous exclusion,Same finite well-mixed model but with asynchronous (sequential) exclusion mechanism.,1,"Exclusion mechanism = asynchronous; parameters varied in analysis: investment amplification factor r, loner payoff σ, exclusion success probability β","Asynchronous vs synchronous exclusion is central comparison; here the mechanism is asynchronous while r, σ, β vary.",1,False,No human subjects; theoretical and numerical.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,They perform numerical experiments for the Markov process model under asynchronous exclusion.,1,True,Analytical payoff and transition structure for asynchronous exclusion are derived.,1,20,M=20 for main well-mixed results.,1,1,Single-stage PGG interaction per sampling; evolutionary process is continuous rather than fixed repeated rounds.,0.4,1,Cooperators invest fixed cost 1; defector invest 0; no continuous choice.,0.9,N/A,No human players or initial allocation decisions.,1,0.2,"Same PGG structure as synchronous case: MPCR = r/N with N=5, base MPCR 1/5=0.2 though r varies.",0.3,N/A,No communication among modeled agents.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,No identities; exclusion is probabilistic without observability of who punishes whom.,1,N/A,No reward strategy.,1,N/A,No discrete experimental rounds displayed.,1,1,Asynchronous exclusion is modeled as a costly sanction that excludes defectors.,1,0,"Excluders bear expected unit exclusion cost c_R per defector, with c_R = [(1-(1-β)^{l+1})/((l+1)β)] * c_E; base c_E=0.2*10^β. No single fixed numeric value; record baseline coefficient 0.2.",0.3,N/R,"Model defines probabilistic exclusion, not a linear fine-per-cost parameter.",0.3,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,Investment cost normalized to 1 per cooperator.,0.9,N/R,"Outcomes are expressed as probabilities of stable states, not contribution fractions.",0,N/R,No explicit aggregate contributions reported.,0,N/R,They do not report total payoff relative to maximum.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes reported.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Mathematical and numerical study only.,1,"Same optional PGG with strategies C, D, L, E in a finite well-mixed population (M=20, N=5). Asynchronous exclusion: excluders act sequentially; once a defector is expelled by one excluder, remaining excluders no longer pay cost for that defector. Expected unit cost c_R = [(1-(1-β)^{l+1})/((l+1)β)] * c_E with l excluders; this reduces average exclusion cost relative to synchronous exclusion and increases cooperative state probability, especially when r is small and β large.",Key distinctive features of asynchronous mechanism summarized from Methods and Results.,1
10.1038_s41598-019-44725-y.md,Structured population – synchronous exclusion,Square lattice simulations are run under two exclusion mechanisms; this row describes the synchronous exclusion case.,1,"Exclusion mechanism = synchronous; parameters varied: investment amplification factor r (1.5–4.5), loner payoff σ=0.1, exclusion success probability β (0.1 and 0.8 highlighted)",In structured population simulations they compare synchronous vs asynchronous exclusion for different r and β at fixed σ.,1,False,Agent-based computational simulations; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical controlled experiment.,1,False,No lab or field setting with humans.,1,True,They simulate strategy evolution on a 100×100 lattice using Monte Carlo steps.,1,False,"For structured population they use numerical simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.8,10000,"They use a 100×100 square lattice, i.e., 10,000 individuals.",1,10000,"They state the system iterates 10,000 to 50,000 rounds (each round = 10,000 update attempts); using the lower bound 10,000 as a conservative number of rounds.",0.4,1,Strategies are discrete: cooperate by investing cost 1 or defect by investing 0; no continuous contribution choice.,0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation decision; purely modeled strategies.,1,0.2,"Same PGG structure with group size 5 (focal plus four neighbors) and multiplication factor r (1<r<5); MPCR=r/5, base MPCR 1/5=0.2 though r varies.",0.3,N/A,Simulated agents do not communicate.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback presentation; only local payoffs computed.,1,N/A,No notion of observable identity; interactions are purely structural.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,1,Synchronous exclusion is a costly sanctioning mechanism that prevents defectors from receiving benefits.,1,0,Each excluder E excludes adjacent defectors with probability β and pays a β-related cost c_E per targeted defector; c_E functional form as in well-mixed model (c_E=0.2*10^β). Report base coefficient 0.2.,0.3,N/R,They do not specify a simple fine-per-cost ratio; punishment is modeled as exclusion from benefits.,0.3,0,No reward strategies exist in lattice simulations.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Cooperators invest cost 1 in each PGG interaction; treated as unit endowment.,0.9,N/R,"Reported outcomes are frequencies of C, D, L, E in the stationary state; they do not directly provide normalized contribution rates, though one could compute from frequencies; not explicitly given.",0.2,N/R,No explicit aggregate token contributions reported.,0,N/R,"They discuss frequencies, not payoff efficiency.",0,N/R,Group payoffs per configuration are not numerically reported.,0,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations on networks.,1,"100×100 square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; each individual participates in five PGGs with self and four neighbors; strategies C, D, L, E initially random. Update rule: pairwise comparison with Fermi imitation, p(s_i→s_j)=1/(1+exp[(π_sj-π_si)τ]), τ=0.1. Exclusion individuals E synchronously attempt to exclude each adjacent defector with probability β, paying cost c_E per targeted defector; excluded defectors receive no benefit in that round. Simulations run 10,000–50,000 rounds (10,000 update attempts per round); averages taken over last 1000 rounds and 20 independent runs.",Captured main structural details for synchronous exclusion on lattice from Methods and Results.,1
