s.",0.2,N/R,No explicit aggregate contributions reported.,0,N/R,"They discuss frequencies and relative advantages, not efficiency measures.",0,N/R,No numeric group payoff results are provided.,0,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,No human,Computational simulations only.,1,"100×100 lattice with periodic boundaries; four strategies (C, D, L, E) initially populated at random. Each individual plays in 5 overlapping groups (self and four neighbors). Update rule: Fermi imitation with τ=0.1. Asynchronous exclusion: excluders act sequentially on each adjacent defector; once a defector is successfully excluded by one excluder, subsequent excluders need not pay cost for that defector in that round, reducing expected cost per excluder and enhancing cooperation for certain r and β (especially when β is large and r in a middle range). Frequencies of strategies after 10,000–50,000 rounds are averaged over final 1000 rounds and 20 runs.",Summarizes distinctive features of asynchronous exclusion in structured population.,1
10.1007_s10683-013-9360-1.md,Game without punishment – all conditions (strategy method cases),Single public good game without punishment; variation is within-subject over 10 hypothetical contribution profiles of others rather than between experimental treatments.,0.97,"Hypothetical contributions of the two other players: ordered pairs (0,0),(0,2),(0,4),(0,6),(2,2),(2,4),(2,6),(4,4),(4,6),(6,6); each subject reports unconditional and conditional contributions for each case.",Design section states the 10 ordered cases used in the contribution table and that subjects also make an unconditional contribution.,0.99,True,Uses human subjects in a lab experiment at an Australian research university.,1,True,Controlled public good game with randomized matching and specified parameters.,1,True,Conducted in a university laboratory using z-Tree; not a field setting.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,Paper discusses theoretical predictions but the reported study is experimental with data from subjects.,1,3,Design specifies a linear public good with n=3 players.,1,1,Each subject completed the game as a one-shot game; contribution determined once via unconditional/conditional procedure.,0.95,1,"Subjects can choose among four contribution levels {0,2,4,6}, i.e., more than a binary all-or-nothing choice.",1,0,"Each player has an endowment of 6 points; they choose how much to contribute, so all starts in private account.",0.95,0.5,Design states marginal per capita return a=0.5.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous z-Tree public good with no chat described.,0.95,0,Subjects only see hypothetical contributions of the other two players for each case; no feedback about realized past earnings or punishment since game is one-shot and feedback is delayed.,0.9,0,No punishment exists in this game.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present.,1,1,Instructions explicitly describe each as a one-shot game; subjects know there is a single decision (plus table