10.1007_s10683-013-9360-1.md,"Game with punishment – NP order (No-punishment first, then punishment)",Subset of sessions (three NP sessions) where subjects played the game without punishment first and then the game with punishment.,0.95,"Game order: NP (no-punishment game first, then game with punishment). Within this order, each subject makes a single contribution choice (0,2,4,6) and punishment decisions for 10 hypothetical cases.",Procedures describe three NP sessions (N=60) with order: game without punishment followed by game with punishment. This order is experimentally varied vs PN.,0.99,True,Human subjects in controlled lab sessions.,1,True,Random matching and controlled payoff structure in a public good with punishment.,1,True,Lab experiment at a university using z-Tree.,1,False,No purely computational study here.,1,False,"This part corresponds to the empirical punishment game, not the theoretical FS predictions.",1,3,Same 3-player public good setting as in Design section.,1,1,"Each subject completes the punishment game as a one-shot game; contributions chosen once, punishment table applied to the realized case.",0.95,1,"Contribution choices are {0,2,4,6}, more than all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each subject starts with 6 points and chooses how much to contribute.,0.95,0.5,Same linear public good with marginal per capita return a=0.5.,1,0,No communication allowed is mentioned; standard anonymous lab setting.,0.95,0,"In punishment game, subjects see own and others' contributions and pre-punishment earnings for each hypothetical case, but no dynamic summaries over rounds or aggregated histories.",0.8,0,No indication that identities of punishers are revealed; standard anonymous punishment implementation.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Subjects are informed that the game is one-shot and that they will be rematched; they know there is a single realization.,0.9,1,This is the game with punishment; subjects assign deduction points to others.,1,1,Design states each unit of punishment costs one point to the punisher.,1,3,Each unit of punishment reduces the target’s earnings by e=3.,1,0,Only costly punishment (deduction points) available; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,6,Same endowment y=6 points in punishment game.,1,N/R,Only raw contributions (in points) are reported; no normalized rate.,0.3,2.133,"Section 6 reports, for NP order, mean contribution in the game with punishment is 2.133 points.",0.98,N/R,No efficiency metric is computed separately by order.,0.1,N/R,Paper does not report average group earnings by order.,0.1,Australia,Same subject pool as overall study; sessions at an Australian research university.,0.95,N/R,No age data provided.,0.1,"Approximately 61 females, remainder males (pooled across NP and PN); NP-only breakdown not reported.","Footnote gives aggregate gender count for all 123 subjects, not by order.",0.6,"University students; 14 Economics majors overall, others from various disciplines",Footnote for whole sample; not broken down by NP vs PN.,0.8,On site,Lab experiment at University of Sydney.,0.98,"Linear public good with punishment. Each subject first chooses a contribution (0,2,4,6) with no contribution table. Then, for each of ten ordered cases of others’ contributions and given their own actual contribution, subjects assign punishment p∈{0,1,2,3} to each of the two other players. One case is randomly matched to the realized contributions to determine actual punishments. Payoffs: π_i^P = max{(y−c_i^P)+a·Σc_j^P − e·Σ_{j≠i}p_{ji}, 0} − Σ_{j≠i}p_{ij}, with y=6,a=0.5,e=3. Beliefs about punishment received are elicited with a quadratic scoring rule. No feedback until both games finished.","Summarized from sections 3.2, 3.3, and 6.",0.99
10.1007_s10683-013-9360-1.md,"Game with punishment – PN order (Punishment first, then no-punishment)",Subset of sessions (three PN sessions) where subjects played the game with punishment first and then the game without punishment.,0.95,"Game order: PN (punishment game first, then no-punishment game). Within this order, each subject makes one contribution choice (0,2,4,6) and punishment decisions for 10 hypothetical cases.",Procedures describe three PN sessions (N=63) where the order is reversed relative to NP.,0.99,True,Same human-subject lab experiment as NP but different order.,1,True,Controlled design with random matching and monetary incentives.,1,True,"Lab-based, implemented in z-Tree at a university.",1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,This entry refers to the empirical PN-order punishment game.,1,3,Same 3-player public good structure for all sessions.,1,1,Game is explicitly one-shot.,0.95,1,"Contribution choice set {0,2,4,6} allows intermediate contributions.",1,0,Subjects allocate how much of their 6-point endowment to contribute.,0.95,0.5,Same marginal per capita return as stated in design.,1,0,No communication is described.,0.95,0,Subjects view hypothetical case screens showing contributions and pre-punishment earnings but not session-wide summaries.,0.8,0,No mention of revealing who punished whom; typical design keeps this anonymous.,0.8,0,There is no reward institution.,1,1,Subjects are informed that this is a one-shot game and then rematched to new groups for the second game.,0.9,1,Same punishment mechanism as in NP-order punishment game.,1,1,Each unit of punishment costs one point to punisher.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces target’s earnings by 3.,1,0,Only costly punishment is available.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,6,Endowment y=6 as per game design.,1,N/R,Only mean contributions in points reported.,0.3,3.111,"Section 6 reports, for PN order, mean contribution in the game with punishment is 3.111 points.",0.98,N/R,Efficiency metrics not reported separately by order.,0.1,N/R,No group-earnings aggregates by order are given.,0.1,Australia,Same lab and subject pool as NP sessions.,0.95,N/R,Age information not provided.,0.1,"Approximately 61 females, remainder males (pooled across NP and PN); PN-only breakdown not reported.",Gender composition known only for full sample.,0.6,"University students; 14 Economics majors overall, remaining subjects from other majors",Education composition only described for full sample.,0.8,On site,Same lab environment as NP sessions.,0.98,"Identical punishment-game structure as in NP order: linear public good with a=0.5 and endowment 6; contribution choice {0,2,4,6}; punishment choice p∈{0,1,2,3} toward each of two others for ten ordered cases of others’ contributions. Strategy-method elicitation of full punishment profile; only the case consistent with realized contributions is payoff-relevant. Beliefs about incurred punishment elicited with a scoring rule. In PN, this game is played before the game without punishment, with rematching and no feedback between games.",Replicates design of game with punishment; difference is only order relative to no-punishment game.,0.99
10.1016_j.jebo.2012.06.001.md,Homogeneous population analytical public goods model,"Single main analytical model in Section 3 describing a homogeneous population with parameters (a, λ) and punishment.",0.9,"Unconditional altruism level a (continuous), reciprocity strength λ (continuous), marginal per capita return φ (continuous), group size n (parameter). This row represents arbitrary a within the analyzed range  -λ < a < λ.",Model analyzes how varying altruism a and reciprocity λ affect equilibrium contributions and punishment; φ and n are environmental parameters.,0.9,False,No human participants; purely theoretical/analytical model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment but a theoretical game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting; analytical model only.,1,False,No numerical/agent-based simulations are reported; analysis is closed-form.,1,True,"Paper derives utility functions, first-order conditions, and Nash equilibria symbolically, including formal propositions and proofs.",1,N/R,Group size denoted n but no specific numeric value fixed; treated as a parameter.,0.4,N/A,One-shot public goods game; but with no human data this is purely conceptual; coded N/A for non-empirical.,0.8,1,"Effort e_i is continuous in [0,1], so contributions are not all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment structure with private vs public accounts; continuous effort choice only and no human implementation.,0.8,N/R,"They define φ = q/n as marginal private benefit of contribution; standard MPCR would be φ, but no numerical value is provided, only bounds 1/n < φ < 1.",0.6,N/A,Analytical model of a simultaneous public goods game; no communication channel is specified and there are no human subjects.,1,N/A,Information structure is abstract (everyone observes efforts); not an implemented interface for human subjects.,1,N/A,"Players are indexed i and j in theory, but this is not an identity revelation mechanism in an experiment.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,Single-stage game in analytical model; no human interface.,1,1,Model explicitly includes costly punishment s_ji with cost c_ij(s_ij).,1,N/R,"Cost function is c_ij(s_ij) = 0.5 * s_ij^2, but the schema’s punishmentCost is per-unit cost; marginal cost at s>0 is s_ij, so no single constant can be given.",0.5,N/R,They define s_ij as the monetary-equivalent cost imposed on target j; it is not parameterized as a multiplier per token spent.,0.5,0,"Only punishment (reducing payoffs) is modeled; rewards are mentioned only in a brief extension discussion, not in the core model.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Players choose effort e_i ∈ [0,1] with quadratic cost; no explicit monetary endowment is specified.",0.6,N/R,Equilibrium efforts e* are derived symbolically as functions of parameters; no normalized empirical rate is reported.,0.6,N/R,Only symbolic expressions for e* are provided; no specific numerical outcomes.,0.6,N/R,They discuss net benefits qualitatively; no explicit efficiency ratio (0–1) is computed.,0.4,N/R,"Group surplus is discussed conceptually via π_i and sums, but no single numeric payoff is reported.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model with no experimental implementation.,1,"Two-stage public goods game with continuous effort e_i∈[0,1]; material payoff π_i = φ Σ_k e_k − 0.5 e_i^2, with 1/n < φ < 1. After observing effort, players can impose costly punishment s_ij on others with cost c_ij(s_ij)=0.5 s_ij^2. Standing b_j=2e_j−1 determines reciprocity-based hostility; utility u_i combines own payoff, punishment costs, and weighted others’ payoffs with parameters a (altruism/spite) and λ (reciprocity). Analysis characterizes punishment thresholds e_0(a), Nash equilibrium efforts with and without punishment, and how increasing altruism a can reduce equilibrium contributions over an intermediate range.","Summarizes the formal game structure described in Sections 2–3, including payoff and utility functions and timing.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2012.06.001.md,Heterogeneous population analytical public goods model,Second analytical model in Section 4 introducing two types: altruistic reciprocators and selfish individuals.,0.9,"Type composition α (fraction of altruistic reciprocators), altruism level a for reciprocators, reciprocity strength λ for reciprocators, marginal per capita return φ, group size n. This row represents generic values in the parameter ranges specified (1/4 < α, φ < 1/2; 1−2φ < λ < 3/4).","Model studies how varying α, a, λ and φ affects equilibrium contributions for each type; n appears in thresholds and stability conditions.",0.9,False,No empirical data; model is purely theoretical.,1,False,Not an implemented experiment but a theoretical extension.,1,False,No lab or field execution; only equations and proofs.,1,False,"Equilibria derived analytically, not via simulation.",1,True,Defines utility and best responses and solves for equilibrium efforts symbolically with multiple punishment regimes.,1,N/R,Group size n appears symbolically with some conditions for 'sufficiently large n'; no concrete number given.,0.4,N/A,One-shot game staged in two decision phases but not repeated across rounds with human subjects.,0.8,1,"Effort levels e_i are continuous in [0,1], not discrete all-or-nothing choices.",1,N/A,No explicit monetary endowment or initial allocation between private/public accounts; theoretical effort choice only.,0.8,N/R,"As in homogeneous model, φ = q/n is defined but no specific numeric MPCR is given beyond bounds 1/n < φ < 1/2.",0.6,N/A,No communication mechanism; analytical only.,1,N/A,"Information about others’ efforts and payoffs is assumed, but not as an implemented experimental interface.",1,N/A,"Players are indexed, but there is no notion of interface identity revelation in an empirical setup.",1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism is built into the formal heterogeneous model.,1,N/A,Single interaction analytically modeled.,1,1,Punishment variables s_ji and cost function c(s) explicitly included; only altruistic reciprocators punish.,1,N/R,Cost function again c(s)=0.5 s^2; no constant per-unit cost parameter.,0.5,N/R,s_ji directly denotes cost inflicted on target; not parameterized as output per unit of some purchased punishment.,0.5,0,Heterogeneous model retains only punishment; rewards mentioned only in speculative extension section.,1,N/A,No reward option in the formal heterogeneous model.,1,N/A,No reward option in the formal heterogeneous model.,1,N/R,Individuals choose effort subject to quadratic cost; initial monetary endowment is not specified.,0.6,N/R,"Equilibrium efforts for each type (e_R, e_S) are functions of parameters and not instantiated with numeric values.",0.6,N/R,Only symbolic expressions and qualitative comparative statics are provided.,0.6,N/R,"They discuss how total contributions and net benefits vary with a and α, but do not compute a normalized efficiency measure.",0.4,N/R,"Total contribution levels and some payoff relations are given algebraically, but no single numeric group payoff is reported.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; analytical heterogeneous-type model only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Model is purely theoretical with defined types and payoffs, not an empirical study.",1,"Same two-stage public goods game structure as homogeneous model, but population has two types: altruistic reciprocators with (a_i, λ_i)=(a, λ) and selfish individuals with (0,0). Fraction of reciprocators is α, giving αn reciprocators and (1−α)n selfish players. Only reciprocators punish, with punishment function s_ji*(e_i) positive when e_i below threshold 0.5−a/(2λ). Effort choices for each type (e_R, e_S) derived under several punishment regimes: no type punished (NP), only selfish punished (PS), only reciprocators punished (NSPR, ruled out), and both punished (PA). Proposition 2 shows that for parameter ranges 1/4<α, φ<1/2 and 1−2φ<λ<3/4, there exists an interval of altruism levels where increases in a for reciprocators reduce their equilibrium contributions, and that this interval widens as α increases.","Summarizes the heterogeneous-type structure, punishment behavior, and main comparative-static result from Section 4 and Appendix A.2.",1
10.1017_ehs.2020.58.md,Passive disapproval model – lognormal v distribution baseline,Condition where only passive disapproval is modelled and v has a lognormal distribution; used for Figure 1 parameter sweeps.,0.86,"Mean normative value v̄, standard deviation σ of lognormal v, and passive disapproval cost κ; material benefit b fixed at 1","Text for Figure 1: v is lognormal with mean v̄ and SD σ; different columns are different v̄, rows different κ; b is set to 1.",0.92,False,Purely mathematical model with analytical and numerical analysis; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment but a theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; model only.,1,True,Uses numerical bifurcation analysis and iteration of recurrence equations without human data.,0.94,True,"Derives recurrence equations and equilibria analytically (e.g., p' = 1 − F(b − κp)).",1,0,"Model is population-level with continuous frequency p, not discrete players.",1,0,"Discrete-time recursion, not a finite number of game rounds.",1,1,"Individuals choose between two discrete actions (comply vs not); continuous contribution concepts do not apply, but coded as free choice rather than all-or-nothing contribution to a public good.",0.4,N/A,No contribution game or endowment.,1,N/R,No public goods game or linear payoff from group contribution specified.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,0,"Model actors base decisions on p (population frequency) conceptually, but there is no explicit interface or summaries as in experiments.",0.6,N/A,No explicit punishment stage with identities; only psychic cost from disapproval.,1,N/A,No rewards are modelled.,1,0,"No experimental interface, only iterative dynamics.",1,1,Violators experience passive disapproval modelled as a normative cost κp interpreted as a form of punishment.,0.85,N/A,"Punishment is encoded as a normative cost in the utility function, not as a choice with per-unit cost.",0.9,N/A,No per-unit punishment technology defined.,1,0,No explicit rewards in this baseline condition; only costs of disapproval and internal normative value.,0.95,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No tokens or monetary endowment.,1,N/R,"Outcome variable is norm compliance frequency p, not a contribution rate to a public good.",1,N/R,No contributions quantified.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum) reported or defined.,1,N/R,Utilities defined per individual; no aggregate payoff reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation study.,1,"One-population deterministic model of norm compliance with passive disapproval only; state variable is frequency p, v is lognormally distributed, dynamics p' = 1 − F(b − κp) with b fixed to 1; analyses include bifurcation diagrams in σ, effects of distributional shape, population mixing, decision errors and persuasive interventions.","Summary of model description in sections 'Passive disapproval of norm violators', 'Uniform distribution of v', 'Log-normal distribution of v', and related supplementary references.",0.93
10.1017_ehs.2020.58.md,Passive disapproval model – population mixing (immigration of non-normative types),Extension where fraction m of population has v = κ = 0 (materially motivated only); used for Figure 2.,0.9,"Immigration / mixing fraction m of individuals with v = κ = 0 (examples shown for m = 0, 0.1, 0.2); b fixed at 1, remaining population has distribution F(v)","In 'Effects of population mixing' section: m is proportion with v = κ = 0; Figure 2 shows equilibria for m = 0, 0.1, 0.2 with b = 1.",0.96,False,Mathematical extension of baseline model; no subjects.,1,False,"Theoretical/simulation condition, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Iterates modified recurrence p' = 1 − F(b − κ(1 − m)p) numerically to obtain equilibria.,0.95,True,Recurrence with m explicitly derived; qualitative properties analysed.,0.95,0,Population-level frequency model.,1,0,No fixed number of rounds; iterative dynamics.,1,1,Binary choice (comply vs not) as in baseline; no graded contributions.,0.4,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No linear public goods production.,1,N/A,No actors or communication channels.,1,0,"Again, individuals conceptually estimate p but no experimental feedback mechanism.",0.6,N/A,"Only passive disapproval cost term, no explicit punisher identity.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No reported display of time horizon.,1,1,Passive disapproval captured by κ term remains for normative subpopulation.,0.85,N/A,No explicit per-unit cost choice for punishers.,0.9,N/A,No discrete punishment technology defined.,1,0,No rewards introduced in this extension.,0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No endowments or payoffs in tokens.,1,N/R,"Outcome remains norm compliance frequency p, shown in Figure 2; not cast as a contribution rate.",1,N/R,No numerical contributions defined.,1,N/R,No total payoff vs maximum efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Group payoff not computed; focus is on equilibrium p under mixing.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,