10.1017_ehs.2020.58.md,Passive disapproval model – additional normative motives (approval and conformity),Condition where utilities include passive approval va and conformity vc parameters in addition to passive disapproval κ.,0.82,Presence and magnitude of passive approval value va and conformity strength vc; modifies compliance threshold v* and thus dynamics of p,"In 'Other costs and benefits' the model adds va and vc terms, yielding modified thresholds v* = b − (κ + va)p and v* = b + vc − (κ + 2vc)p.",0.9,False,Analytical extension of utility structure; no data.,1,False,"Theoretical scenario, lacks empirical manipulation.",1,False,No lab context.,1,True,"Though not graphed in main text, these modified equations would be analysed numerically similarly to earlier sections.",0.75,True,New thresholds and qualitative effects derived symbolically.,1,0,Population-level frequency model.,1,0,"Time is discrete but unbounded, not fixed rounds.",1,1,Behaviour is still binary (follow vs violate).,0.4,N/A,No contributions to a public good.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita returns specified.,1,N/A,No agents.,1,0,Model assumes individuals know or estimate p; no explicit summary display mechanism.,0.6,N/A,Only passive (dis)approval; no explicit punishing agents with identities.,1,N/A,"Approval is internal/normative (parameter va), not an explicit reward assigned with identity.",1,0,No experimental interface.,1,1,Passive disapproval κ still operates; no active punishment cost term c in this section.,0.85,N/A,No active punisher decision variable or per-unit cost is introduced here.,0.95,N/A,No punishment technology defined.,1,1,"Passive approval by others is modelled via va and vc parameters, representing normative benefits akin to rewards.",0.75,N/A,Approval benefits are not costly acts; no resource cost per unit reward.,0.9,N/A,Approval incorporated directly into utility; no per-unit transformation from cost to reward.,0.9,N/A,No endowment structure.,1,N/R,Outcome still norm compliance p; not framed as contribution rate.,1,N/R,No quantified contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency variable computed.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not reported; discussion is qualitative.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,No human,Analytical model only.,1,"Extends passive disapproval utility by including passive approval (parameter va) so that u1 = v + va p and conformity (parameter vc) so that u0 and u1 get ± vc terms. These modify the compliance threshold to v* = b − (κ + va)p and v* = b + vc − (κ + 2vc)p, respectively. Also mentions frequency-dependent material benefits (e.g., cf terms) in the same framework.",Summarizes 'Other costs and benefits' section formulas and interpretations.,0.94
10.1017_ehs.2020.58.md,Passive disapproval model – errors in utility evaluation (quantal response),Condition incorporating decision errors via a quantal response equilibrium approach.,0.84,Decision precision parameter in quantal response (error level); affects stochastic choice around utilities u1 and u0,Section 'Errors in utility evaluation' states using quantal response equilibrium and discusses decreasing precision; SI Figure S11 shows its effect.,0.9,False,Errors are modelled theoretically; no behavioural data.,1,False,"Simulated strategy choice with noise, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Quantal response model is evaluated numerically to obtain equilibria (SI Figure S11).,0.9,True,"Adopts quantal response formalism conceptually, analytically characterizing qualitative changes (e.g., equilibria disappearance).",0.85,0,Population state remains continuous in p.,1,0,"Iterative dynamics with stochastic best response, not finite repeated game.",1,1,Choice set still binary; only choice probabilities become noisy.,0.4,N/A,No endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,No public goods payoffs.,1,N/A,No agents in an experiment.,1,0,Individuals conceptually estimate p; no interface.,0.6,N/A,Only passive disapproval; no punitive agent identity.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No experimental timeline.,1,1,Passive disapproval κp still in utility.,0.85,N/A,No active punisher choosing to pay costs.,0.95,N/A,Passive cost is parameterized directly; no per-unit tech.,1,0,No reward mechanisms introduced.,0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No endowments.,1,N/R,Outcome variable is equilibrium p under noisy decision-making; not expressed as contribution rate.,1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,No efficiency measure reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff metrics are not given for this scenario.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,"Quantal response is implemented in a theoretical model, not in an experiment.",1,Quantal response equilibrium is applied so that choice probabilities depend smoothly on utility differences between compliance and violation. Decreasing precision (increasing noise) causes disappearance of equilibria with small attraction basins and shifts remaining equilibria towards p ≈ 0.5 as decisions become more random (as in SI Figure S11).,Summarizes description in 'Errors in utility evaluation' section.,0.9
10.1017_ehs.2020.58.md,Passive disapproval model – heterogeneity in disapproval sensitivity κ,Condition where individuals differ in sensitivity to disapproval κ instead of normative value v.,0.86,Distribution F(κ) of sensitivity to disapproval; normative value v is common across individuals,In 'Variation in other characteristics': κ has cdf F(z) and compliance threshold is κ* = (b − v)/p with p' = 1 − F((b − v)/p).,0.96,False,Analytical 'what-if' variation; no data.,1,False,No experimental manipulation; model structure only.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Bifurcation diagrams (SI Figure S14) are generated numerically for this κ-heterogeneity model.,0.92,True,Recurrence in terms of F((b − v)/p) is derived.,0.95,0,Continuous population.,1,0,Iterative dynamics with no fixed horizon.,1,1,Each individual still chooses between compliance and violation.,0.4,N/A,No contributions to a fund.,1,N/R,No public goods game.,1,N/A,No agents.,1,0,Agents infer p implicitly; no summary output.,0.6,N/A,Disapproval cost is parameter κ; no punisher identity.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,0,No experimental interface.,1,1,Passive disapproval cost with heterogeneous κ remains.,0.9,N/A,No explicit active punishment act.,0.95,N/A,No discrete punishment technology.,1,0,No rewards described for this variant.,0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No token endowment.,1,N/R,Outcome remains norm compliance p.,1,N/R,No amounts contributed.,1,N/R,No efficiency variable computed.,1,N/R,Payoff aggregates not reported.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,No human,Purely theoretical heterogeneity scenario.,1,"Instead of heterogeneity in normative value v, individuals differ in κ (sensitivity to disapproval), with cdf F(z). Compliance threshold becomes κ* = (b − v)/p, and population dynamics follow p' = 1 − F((b − v)/p). Bifurcation diagrams (SI Figure S14) illustrate resulting equilibria structures.",Summarizes 'Variation in other characteristics' section.,0.94
10.1017_ehs.2020.58.md,Passive and active disapproval model – lognormal v distribution with punishment,"Two-dimensional model where individuals can both comply (x) and actively disapprove/punish (y), v is lognormal; equilibria for p and q shown in Figures 3 and S15.",0.94,"Maximum cost of being actively disapproved/punished c and cost of punishing others δ; standard deviation σ of lognormal v used as bifurcation parameter; mean v̄ and κ fixed (e.g., v̄ = 0.8, κ = 0.2, b = 1)",In 'Both passive and active disapproval' and Figure 3: parameters c and δ varied across panels; σ is bifurcation parameter; v is lognormal with mean v̄ = 0.8 and SD σ; κ = 0.2; b previously set to 1.,0.98,False,Two-dimensional theoretical model; no subjects.,1,False,"Controlled simulation/analysis, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab context.,1,True,"Stable equilibria in (p, q) for varying parameters are computed numerically and presented in Figures 3–4 and SI.",0.97,True,"Utility functions and recurrence equations for p and q are explicitly derived, including thresholds v* and v**.",0.98,0,Model tracks continuous frequencies p (compliers) and q (punishers).,1,0,Recurrence equations iterate until equilibrium; no set number of rounds.,1,1,Individuals have binary decisions for x (comply vs violate) and y (punish vs not); no continuous action intensity.,0.7,N/A,No endowment or contribution stage.,1,N/R,Not a PGG; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No explicit communication between agents.,1,0,"As before, agents conceptually know p and q; no interface or summary screen.",0.6,N/A,Punishment/disapproval is modelled via aggregate frequency q and costs; punisher identities are not represented.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards; only normative/punitive terms.,1,0,Not an implemented experiment.,1,1,Model introduces active disapproval/punishment decision y with cost δ to punisher and cost c to punished individuals (social punishment).,1,N/A,Parameter δ is a total cost of punishing scaled by fraction of violators (1 − p'); the paper does not specify a per-token cost as in PGG punishment tech; thus no numeric per-unit punishment cost can be extracted.,0.7,N/A,"Parameter c is maximum cost of being punished, not a discrete technology mapping coins to fines.",0.7,0,No explicit reward or positive transfer mechanism in the active disapproval section.,0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No token endowment or monetary payoffs structure given.,1,N/R,Key outcome is equilibrium norm compliance p*; not characterized as contribution rate.,1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed for this model.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not explicitly derived or reported; focus is on frequencies p* and q*.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Entirely theoretical and simulation-based.,1,"Individuals first choose whether to comply with the norm (x = 1) or violate (x = 0). Utilities: u1 = v+ for complying; u0 = b − v− − κp − cq for violating, where p is compliance frequency and q is punishment frequency. Compliance threshold: v* = b − κp − cq. Among compliers (x = 1), individuals decide whether to actively disapprove/punish (y = 1). Utilities for punishing: v − (1 − p')δ; for not punishing: κ q. Punishment threshold: v** = (1 − p')δ − κ q. v has a lognormal distribution with mean v̄ and SD σ. Dynamics: p' = 1 − F(v*); q' = 1 − F(max(v*, v**)). Analysis explores how varying c, δ, and σ affects stable equilibria p* and q*, showing that punishment can create additional cooperative equilibria and complex multistability.","Compiled from 'Both passive and active disapproval of norm violators' section, including equations and discussion of Figures 3, S15.",0.98
10.1017_ehs.2020.58.md,Passive and active disapproval model – bimodal v distribution with punishment,"Variant of the passive+active model where v has a bimodal distribution (mixture of two lognormals), used for Figure 4.",0.9,"Bimodal distribution of normative values v (sum of two lognormals with means at v̄ and 1 − v̄, common σ); punishment parameters c and δ varied; σ varied","Figure 4 caption: underlying distribution of v is sum of two lognormal distributions with mean values at v̄ and 1 − v̄ and same σ; v̄ = 0.2, κ = 0.8; c, δ, σ varied.",0.98,False,"No empirical measurement; purely simulated dynamics for (p, q).",1,False,Theoretical scenario.,1,False,No lab study.,1,True,"Stable equilibria in (p, q) and their basins of attraction are computed numerically and displayed in Figure 4.",0.97,True,Model equations are as in passive+active case; only F(v) is bimodal; analytical reasoning used plus simulations.,0.9,0,Continuous population frequencies p and q.,1,0,"Phase-plane analysis focuses on fixed points and basins, not round counts.",1,1,Action set remains binary in x and y.,0.7,N/A,No endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,No PGG.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Model-level knowledge of p and q; no experimental display.,0.6,N/A,Punishment is anonymous and aggregated; identities absent.,1,N/A,Rewards not modelled.,1,0,Not an experiment.,1,1,"Same active disapproval mechanics (parameters c and δ, choice y) as in previous passive+active model.",1,N/A,Cost δ is parameterized but not as a per-token cost; schema requires per-unit; not specified.,0.7,N/A,Parameter c is maximum total cost to violators; no per-unit mapping.,0.7,0,No reward stage.,0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No endowment concept.,1,N/R,Outcomes are equilibrium p* and q* and number of coexisting equilibria; not expressed as contribution rates.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric computed.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not reported.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Simulation of dynamical system.,1,"Same utility and dynamic structure as the passive+active disapproval model, but v is drawn from a bimodal distribution: a sum of two lognormals with means at v̄ and 1 − v̄ and common σ. With v̄ = 0.2 and κ = 0.8, phase-plane analysis (Figure 4) shows 1–4 simultaneously stable equilibria in (p, q) with differing levels of compliance and punishment and distinct basins of attraction, demonstrating strong path dependence and increased norm diversity.",Directly from Figure 4 description and corresponding text about bimodal v in models with punishment.,0.96
