receive an initial allocation of £10.,1,N/R,"Outcome for Player 1 is theft amount (0–10), not a public good contribution; no normalized contribution reported.",1,N/R,Only theft amount is reported (median 0 in A); no cooperative transfer is defined as a contribution.,0.9,N/R,"Mean payoffs are reported by neighborhood, but maximum possible efficiency baseline is not formally defined for this theft/fine structure.",0.5,N/R,"Mean individual payoff (e.g., £9.26 in Neighborhood A) is reported, but not mean or distribution of triad-level group payoff.",0.5,United Kingdom,"Residents of two neighborhoods in Newcastle Upon Tyne, England.",1,N/R,Age was recorded but no age statistics given in text.,0.5,Mixed (both male and female; 16 male out of 40 Player 1s in Neighborhood A),Counts by sex are given for each role and neighborhood.,1,N/R,No educational attainment information reported.,1,Field experiment,"Game and questionnaire delivered to households in their neighborhoods, not an online or lab setting.",1,One-shot three-player theft and third-party punishment game; Player 1 chooses integer 0–10 pounds to steal from Player 2; Player 3 uses strategy method over potential theft amounts to decide when to pay £2 to impose a £6 fine on Player 1; Player 2 makes no payoff-relevant choice.,Summarized from Study 1 game description.,1
