10.7717_peerj.450.md,Study 2 – Neighborhood A – Norms treatment – Player 2 expectation of 3PP,Same as previous but for Neighborhood A participants who received manipulated descriptive norms information (Norms treatment).,1,Neighborhood = A; Treatment = Norms manipulation presenting more negative descriptive norms (higher perceived cheating) than actual,"Within Neighborhood A, Norms treatment shows scales with high perceived cheating values to participants.",1,True,Human participants receive textual manipulation and answer expectation question.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of information on local descriptive norms.,0.95,False,Field setting via mail.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,3,Same game as Study 1.,1,1,One-shot game remains unchanged.,0.9,1,Structure of game decisions unchanged from Study 1.,0.9,0,Same £10 private endowment; no change under treatment.,1,N/R,Not a public goods setting.,1,0,No indication of allowed communication.,0.9,1,Norms treatment explicitly shows averages of prior neighbors’ perceived cheating responses (summary information on others’ answers).,1,0,No identities for punishers are given; manipulation concerns norms only.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Participants are informed about the one-shot nature of the game as before.,0.8,1,Game structure unchanged.,1,2,Punishment costs stay the same.,1,6,Punishment magnitude unchanged.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Game parameters not changed by information treatment.,1,N/R,DV is expectation of 3PP.,1,N/R,Player 2 does not contribute.,1,N/R,No efficiency measure reported.,0.5,N/R,Group payoffs not broken out by treatment.,0.5,United Kingdom,Same neighborhoods in Newcastle Upon Tyne.,1,N/R,Age breakdown not presented.,0.5,"Mixed (both male and female; total Neighborhood A Study 2 sample 21 male of 41, though not split by treatment)",Only overall neighborhood-level counts reported.,0.6,N/R,No education data provided.,1,Field experiment,Same field setting with experimental informational manipulation.,1,"Norms treatment in Neighborhood A displays manipulated descriptive norms suggesting higher local cheating (e.g., values 5.7, 5.5, 6.7 on 1–10 scale); 41.46% of these participants expect 3PP (vs 36.36% Baseline), an effect not statistically robust; some participants also spontaneously opted to forgo payment at higher rates (14.63%).","From Study 2 description of manipulation, presented values, and results for Neighborhood A.",1
