ment,Same field context with between-subjects information manipulation.,1,"Norms treatment in Neighborhood B presents low perceived cheating values (2.2 avoid fare, 2.3 cheat benefits, 1.7 cheat taxes on 1–10 scale). 58.97% of these participants expect 3PP versus 30.23% in Baseline; odds ratio 3.32 (95% CI [1.33–8.25]). Participants in B were also more likely to judge the manipulated norms as ‘about right’.",From Study 2 description and statistical results for norms manipulation in Neighborhood B.,1
10.1016_j.cognition.2019.104040.md,Experiment 1 – 2PP condition (victim as first-order punisher),Authors describe Experiment 1 with between-subjects second-party (victim) vs third-party (observer) conditions for the first-order punisher; this row is the second-party condition.,1,"Role of first-order punisher: second party (victim); First-order punishment decision: punish vs. not punish (strategy method, within-subject from HOP perspective).",Main manipulated factor for HOP subjects is whether the first-order punisher is victim vs observer (between-subjects) and whether they punished or not (within-subjects via strategy method). This row fixes second-party role.,0.93,True,Human participants from Amazon Mechanical Turk played an incentivized economic game.,1,True,Random assignment to second- vs third-party conditions and controlled payoff structure indicate a laboratory-style economic experiment.,1,False,"Data collected online via Amazon Mechanical Turk, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model; only empirical mixed-effects regressions on behavioral data.,1,4,Stage 1: Perpetrator and Victim; Stage 2: First-Order Punisher; Stage 3: Higher-Order Punisher. Total unique roles/participants in one full game path are four.,0.85,1,Single multi-stage interaction with no repetition between same partners; described as a one-shot game.,0.98,1,Higher-order punishers could pay 'between 0 and 4 cents' continuously; contributions to punishment are not all-or-nothing.,1,0,Standard punishment game with individual endowments; no mention of any portion starting in a public account.,0.8,N/R,This is not a public goods game; no group multiplier or marginal per capita return reported.,1,0,No mention of any communication or chat between participants in the online game.,0.98,0,Higher-order punishers learned about the first-order punisher’s behavior but there is no indication they saw payoff summaries beyond what is needed to make decisions.,0.7,0,MTurk experiment with anonymized roles; no indication identities of punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism is implemented in the game.,1,0,Game is effectively one-shot; no mention of informing participants about number of rounds.,0.9,1,Higher-order punishers could spend money to reduce first-order punishers’ payoffs.,1,0.01,Stage 3: 'paying between 0 and 4 cents to reduce the First-Order Punisher's payoff by $0.05 for each cent paid'; cost is 1 cent per punishment unit.,1,0.05,Each cent spent removes $0.05 from the first-order punisher’s payoff.,1,0,Only costly punishment options are described; no positive transfers or rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,0.25,Higher-order punishers were 'given $0.25' to make punishment decisions.,1,N/R,No contribution to a public good; only punishment decisions are measured.,1,N/R,"The main dependent variable is amount spent on higher-order punishment, not contributions.",1,N/R,No analysis of overall efficiency or welfare ratio is reported for the game outcomes.,1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff statistics are reported.,1,N/R,Participants recruited on MTurk; country not stated (likely US but not specified).,0.3,N/R,Demographics were collected but specific ages not reported in the text.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.2,N/R,Education not reported.,0.2,Online,"Participants were 'recruited through Amazon Mechanical Turk', an online platform.",1,"Three-stage higher-order punishment game based on a Dictator Game with theft. Stage 1: Perpetrator can steal Victim's $0.15 bonus for extra $0.05. Stage 2: First-order punisher (here, the Victim as second party) has $0.25 and can pay $0.05 to remove $0.20 from perpetrator. Stage 3: Higher-order punisher has $0.25 and can pay 0–$0.04 (1-cent increments) to reduce first-order punisher's payoff by $0.05 per cent (max $0.20). Strategy method used: HOP subjects state punishment for both cases (FOP punish vs FOP not punish). Analyses focus only on histories where the perpetrator actually stole.",Summarized from detailed procedure in Exp. 1 methods.,1
10.1016_j.cognition.2019.104040.md,Experiment 1 – 3PP condition (observer as first-order punisher),"Same Experiment 1 game, but this condition sets the first-order punisher as a third-party observer instead of the victim.",1,"Role of first-order punisher: third party (impartial observer); First-order punishment decision: punish vs. not punish (strategy method, within-subject from HOP perspective).",Between-subjects factor is second- vs third-party role of first-order punisher; this row fixes third-party condition while the within-subject variation is whether FOP occurred.,0.93,True,Same human-subject MTurk experiment as other Exp. 1 condition.,1,True,Controlled payoff structure with random assignment to third-party vs second-party conditions.,1,False,"Online MTurk setting, not in-person lab.",1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical modeling.,1,4,"As in other Exp. 1 condition: Perpetrator, Victim, First-Order Punisher (observer), Higher-Order Punisher.",0.85,1,Single-shot multi-stage game; no repeated interactions.,0.98,1,Higher-order punishment choice is continuous from 0 to 4 cents.,1,0,Endowments are private; no public pot.,0.8,N/R,Not a public goods setting; no marginal per capita return.,1,0,No communication described between participants.,0.98,0,HOP subjects are told about the first-order punisher’s action but not general summaries of others' payoffs.,0.7,0,"Identities are not revealed; roles are described generically (Perpetrator, Victim, Observer).",0.9,0,No reward stage exists.,1,0,One-shot game; no mentioning of multiple rounds.,0.9,1,Higher-order punishment option is available as in the 2PP condition.,1,0.01,Same stage-3 punishment technology: 1 cent paid per $0.05 reduction.,1,0.05,Each cent spent decreases first-order punisher's bonus by $0.05.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism is part of the design.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this game.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this game.,1,0.25,Higher-order punishers are endowed with $0.25 in all conditions.,1,N/R,No public good contributions are part of the design.,1,N/R,"Only punishment decisions, not contributions, are measured.",1,N/R,No efficiency metric is reported.,1,N/R,Paper does not report aggregate group payoff in this experiment.,1,N/R,Recruitment via MTurk; country of residence not specified.,0.3,N/R,Ages not reported in main text.,0.2,N/R,Gender breakdown not provided.,0.2,N/R,Education levels not reported.,0.2,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Same three-stage higher-order punishment game as in the 2PP condition. Difference: in Stage 2, the First-Order Punisher is an impartial observer who is 'told all details from stage 1' rather than the victim. Observer has $0.25 and a binary choice to pay $0.05 to reduce the perpetrator's bonus by $0.20. Stage 3 higher-order punisher sanctions this observer using the same 1-to-5 punishment leverage.",Derived from Experiment 1 methods (3PP condition description).,1
10.2753_mis0742-1222310208.md,PP – Parent with punishment advice treatment,Condition where advice source is parent who is financially punished for the teen’s pirating; labeled PP in Table 1 and throughout (Parent with punishment).,1,Source of advice: Parent with social tie and stake (parent punished α_p = 0.6 per pirated song).,"Treatments vary by source of advice along two dimensions (social tie, stake). This row corresponds to PP: social tie present, stake present via parental punishment.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with human teen and parent participants and student record labels.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled lab game with 20 rounds.,1,True,Conducted in Vernon Smith Experimental Economics Laboratory at Purdue University.,1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations; only human subjects using z-Tree.,1,False,"Paper contains theory but this row refers to empirical lab treatment, not the analytical model.",1,4,Each group has 4 music consumers n=4; parents and record label are not decision-making consumers.,1,20,“Each session involved 20 rounds of the Piracy Game being played.”,1,1,Within 25 seconds per round consumers can repeatedly choose buy/download/do nothing; no all-or-nothing restriction.,1,0,Endowment is 8 tokens in private account; nothing is pre-allocated to a public account.,0.8,N/A,This is not a standard linear public goods/VCM with a defined per-capita return; it is a volunteer’s dilemma style piracy game.,1,0,Instructions say no communication between subjects; only one-way advice from adviser.,1,1,"Advice always contained information about record label’s earnings and average piracy in the group, which participants saw when advice was delivered.",0.9,0,Teens know only their own parent is punished but not other identities; group members’ identities concealed to minimize reputation effects.,0.7,0,"No explicit rewards mechanism, only earnings from own decisions.",1,1,Subjects are told there will be 20 rounds; standard z-Tree setup typically displays current round number.,0.8,1,Parents in PP incur loss α_p=0.6 tokens per song pirated by their child.,1,N/R,"Framework defines punishment as peer-imposed costly action; here punishment is exogenous loss to parent, no cost choice by others.",0.3,N/R,No decision-based punishment technology (no choice of how many punishment points to buy).,0.3,0,No mechanism for costly rewarding of others’ behavior is described.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism.,1,8,Each music consumer is endowed with e=8 tokens each round.,1,N/R,"Paper reports songs purchased and pirated, not normalized contribution fractions.",0,4.29,"From Table 6, PP treatment rounds 9–20: average number of songs purchased per consumer is 4.29.",0.95,N/R,No efficiency measure (actual vs max possible group payoff) is explicitly reported or derivable from aggregate stats.,0.2,N/R,"Table 3 reports average record label profit per group, but not full group payoff (consumers+parent+label).",0.2,United States,"Experiment at Purdue University (Indiana, USA) with university students and their parents.",0.9,Primarily late teens for consumers; parents are adults,Subjects are new university students (teens/young adults) and their parents; exact ages not reported.,0.7,N/R,No gender composition info provided.,0,Consumers: incoming university students; Parents: mixed adult education; Record labels: senior university students,"Roles explicitly tied to “new students”, “senior University student”, and “parents”.",0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical economics laboratory on campus with participants at terminals.,1,"Volunteer’s dilemma style piracy game with 4 consumers, 1 record label, and parents; 25-second continuous-decision rounds; each round, consumers choose repeatedly to buy for 1 token (earning 1.1), download for free (earning 0.5) or do nothing; each has 2 initial songs and 8 tokens per round; record label has 32 songs and earns 1 per sale plus 0.1 per unsold song; parents in PP lose 0.6 tokens per pirated song by their child; advice provided twice (after rounds 8 and 14) including info on label earnings and average piracy and later moral statements.",Summarizes the core strategic environment and payoff structure that are not captured by standard public-good fields.,1
10.2753_mis0742-1222310208.md,PNP – Parent no punishment advice treatment,Condition where advice source is parent without being punished; labeled PNP in Table 1 (Parent no punishment).,1,Source of advice: Parent with social tie and no stake (parent not punished; fixed payoff E = 12).,This treatment varies the stake dimension while keeping social tie (parent) constant; in PNP parents incur no punishment for child’s actions.,1,True,Same lab experiment with human participants; only advice source differs.,1,True,Controlled randomized treatment in lab.,1,True,Run in Vernon Smith Experimental Economics Laboratory.,1,False,No non-human simulations used.,1,False,"Row describes empirical treatment, not theoretical model.",1,4,Same game structure: 4 music consumers per group.,1,20,All treatments use 20 rounds.,1,1,Same flexible repeated choice within 25 seconds per round.,1,0,"Endowment is 8 tokens, all private.",0.8,N/A,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication between subjects allowed; only one-way advice.,1,1,Advice message includes record label’s earnings and average piracy.,0.9,0,There is no punishment applied in this treatment.,1,0,No rewards mechanism exists.,1,1,Total of 20 rounds known ex ante to subjects.,0.8,0,Parents in PNP do not incur penalties; no other punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No costly rewarding.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,8,Consumers in all treatments get 8 tokens each round.,1,N/R,No normalized contribution rates reported.,0,2.79,"Table 6 shows for PNP, rounds 9–20, average number of songs purchased per consumer is 2.79.",0.95,N/R,No overall efficiency numbers provided.,0.2,N/R,Full group payoff not reported.,0.2,United States,Same lab and recruitment setting at Purdue University.,0.9,Primarily late teens for consumers; parents are adults,New students and their parents as before.,0.7,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0,Consumers: incoming university students; Parents: mixed adult education; Record labels: senior university students,Roles specified identically across parent treatments.,0.9,On site,Same physical lab setting.,1,"Same Piracy Game as PP: 4 consumers, 1 record label, parents; 25-second rounds allowing multiple decisions; endowments and payoffs identical; parents here have fixed 12-token endowment and are not punished; advice is handwritten by parents and delivered twice (after rounds 8 and 14) including group piracy and label earnings plus later moral assessments.",Only difference from PP lies in presence/absence of parental punishment; rest of configuration is shared.,1
