10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.02.015.md,Rockenbach & Milinski 2006 – Treatment with option of punishment (SR only),Paper explicitly describes Rockenbach and Milinski (2006) and that one treatment has two stages: a PGG where subjects chose beforehand whether punishment is possible in second stage; this corresponds to an SR-only enforcement condition.,0.86,"Availability of costly punishment after the public goods game: participants can pre-choose to be in a group where costly punishment in a second stage is possible (SR condition, no IR stage).",The treatment varies whether subjects choose a group with or without punishment in a linked second stage; this row corresponds to the subgroup using punishment only.,0.78,True,Described as an economic experiment with subjects contributing in public goods games and choosing punishment options.,0.96,True,Laboratory-style controlled economic game with assigned stages and treatments.,0.96,True,Framed as an economic experiment of the type usually run in labs; no indication of field implementation.,0.78,False,"Human-subject economic experiment, not a computer-only model.",0.99,False,No purely analytical derivations for this treatment; it is an experiment.,0.99,N/R,The summary mentions groups and public goods games but not exact group size.,0.0,N/R,The passage does not state how many periods the PGG or punishment phases last.,0.0,1,"Text says: 'Subjects can either contribute all or contribute nothing', indicating a binary all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.98,0,Standard PGG protocols and no indication of an automatic public allocation; thus endowment starts private.,0.7,N/R,No multiplier or marginal per capita return is reported in the summary.,0.0,0,No mention of communication; standard punishment PGGs typically forbid chat to preserve anonymity.,0.7,1,Punishment requires knowledge of others’ contributions/behavior; subjects must see at least summary info about others to punish.,0.8,N/R,The description does not state whether punishers o