nforcement relies solely on SR.",0.86
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.02.015.md,Rockenbach & Milinski 2006 – Treatment with IR only (no punishment),"Within the same study, some groups chose not to have punishment in the second stage, and later participated in the third-stage IR game. This yields a condition where enforcement is via IR only.",0.82,Availability of enforcement method: subjects in groups that chose 'no punishment' in stage two rely solely on a third-stage indirect reciprocity game to discipline PGG defectors.,The treatment structure yields a subgroup where costly punishment is absent and only IR (reputational withholding of benefits) is available.,0.8,True,Same human-subject experiment as above.,0.99,True,Controlled allocation into treatments with and without punishment and an IR stage.,0.99,True,Standard lab-type economic experiment; no indication of field.,0.78,False,"Uses real participants, not only simulated agents.",0.99,False,Not a formal analytical model.,0.99,N/R,The text does not specify group size in the Rockenbach & Milinski experiment.,0.0,N/R,Number of repetitions of PGG and IR interactions not reported here.,0.0,1,Same PGG structure as other treatment: 'Subjects can either contribute all or contribute nothing'.,0.98,0,"As for the SR-only treatment, endowments standardly begin in private accounts.",0.7,N/R,No multiplier reported.,0.0,0,No mention of communication; typical PGGs forbid it.,0.7,1,IR requires observation of others’ past cooperation/defection; thus some summary of others' behavior must be available.,0.85,0,There is no punishment stage in this subgroup; hence no punishment identities.,0.95,0,Only reputational withholding and selection of partners in IR; no explicit reward transfers are described.,0.75,N/R,Not specified whether the horizon is known.,0.0,0,This condition is defined by the absence of a punishment option.,0.99,N/A,No costly punishment mechanism in this condition.,1.0,N/A,No costly punishment mechanism in this condition.,1.0,0,"No explicit positive reward mechanism i