10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.02.015.md,Milinski et al. 2001 – IR game testing use of SCORING vs STANDING,"The paper discusses Milinski et al. (2001) as an experiment where subjects played an IR game with randomly changing partners and continuous interactions, testing whether they use SCORING or STANDING.",0.9,"Information structure about reputations: participants play an indirect reciprocity game with only first-order action histories easily accessible, while second-order information (recipient reputation) is technically available but cognitively difficult to use.","The study manipulates the complexity of information presentation, effectively testing whether subjects apply SCORING despite second-order data being available.",0.82,True,Described as an experiment where seven players interact in pairs for 16 rounds.,0.99,True,"Controlled game with fixed structure, repeated random pairings, and recorded behavior.",0.99,True,Standard IR experiments of this type are run in a physical lab; nothing suggests otherwise.,0.8,False,Involves human participants.,0.99,False,Not a purely mathematical treatment.,0.99,7,The text says: 'seven players interacted randomly and continuously in pairs for 16 rounds'.,0.99,16,Explicitly stated: 'for 16 rounds'.,0.99,N/R,The nature of the giving decision (binary vs continuous) is not described.,0.0,0,"In IR donation games, endowment usually starts private and players decide whether to give; no mention of any automatic public allocation.",0.75,N/R,No payoff function or MPCR is provided in this summary.,0.0,0,No communication or negotiation described; typical IR experiments prevent chat to isolate reputation effects.,0.75,1,Participants are given histories of others’ cooperative or defective actions in the network; this constitutes summaries of others’ behavior.,0.95,0,There is no costly punishment stage; only helping/withholding decisions.,0.98,1,"Players can see who helped whom in the history network; thus, identities of those 'rewarding' (helping) others are effectively visible.",0.8,1,"Repeated IR experiments of this kind typically announce the fixed number of rounds (16); given explicit mention of 'for 16 rounds', it is likely common knowledge.",0.7,0,The IR game involves only helping vs not helping; no costly punishment stage is described.,0.98,N/A,No costly punishment mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No costly punishment mechanism.,1.0,1,The basic action is to 'help' (give) or not; helping is effectively a costly reward to the recipient.,0.9,N/R,The cost per act of helping is not quantified in the summary.,0.0,N/R,The benefit to the recipient per unit given is not specified.,0.0,N/R,Per-round endowment is not given.,0.0,N/R,The article only reports that subjects used SCORING rather than STANDING; aggregate rates of helping are not presented.,0.0,N/R,No numeric giving amounts are reported.,0.0,N/R,Efficiency is not discussed quantitatively.,0.0,N/R,No payoff statistics provided.,0.0,N/R,Country/lab location not mentioned.,0.0,N/R,No age data given.,0.0,N/R,Gender composition is not described.,0.0,N/R,No information on educational status.,0.0,On site,Standard IR lab experiment with small groups interacting in person.,0.8,"Seven participants interact in randomly paired dyads over 16 rounds in an indirect reciprocity helping game. After each interaction, information about who helped or refused to help whom is added to a network-like history. The design provides technically sufficient data to implement second-order norms like SIMPLE STANDING but in a cognitively demanding format, leading subjects to actually behave in line with first-order SCORING (cooperating based only on past actions, not on whether previous recipients were good or bad).","Drawn from the description that histories of cooperative or defective actions are presented in a network over 16 rounds, and that subjects relied on SCORING despite availability of second-order information.",0.92
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.02.015.md,Fehr & Gächter 2000/2002 – PGG with costly punishment (altruistic punishment),"The article refers to Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002) as lab experiments with PGG plus second-stage punishment showing 'altruistic' punishment.",0.88,Presence of a second-stage costly punishment option after contributions in a repeated public goods game; interactions are one-shot and anonymous per period to exclude future-benefit motives.,Fehr & Gächter’s key manipulation is enabling costly punishment in an otherwise one-shot PGG context.,0.86,True,They are canonical human-subject public goods experiments.,0.99,True,Participants are assigned to PGGs with controlled punishment options.,0.99,True,Standard economic lab experiments.,0.9,False,Not a simulation-only study.,0.99,False,"Empirical, not purely analytical.",0.99,N/R,Group size (often 4 in original studies) is not repeated in this article.,0.0,N/R,Number of periods is not stated in the summary.,0.0,0,"Classic Fehr & Gächter PGGs allow any amount from endowment, not only all-or-nothing; the summary does not contradict this.",0.7,0,Endowment starts fully private in standard PGG; no mention of default public allocation.,0.9,N/R,MPCR not reported in this text.,0.0,0,"Design emphasizes anonymous one-shot interactions, which preclude communication.",0.95,1,Punishers must see others’ contributions to assign punishment.,0.95,N/R,The summary does not specify whether identities of punishing players are revealed to others.,0.0,0,Only punishment is mentioned; no reward option.,0.95,1,"Interactions are explicitly one-shot and anonymous per period; subjects know they will not meet the same partner again, implying awareness of the effective horizon.",0.8,1,Central feature is a second-stage costly punishment opportunity.,0.99,N/R,Cost per punishment point not specified in this article.,0.0,N/R,Effect of each punishment point on target not detailed here.,0.0,0,No mention of a reward mechanism in these treatments.,0.99,N/A,No reward implemented.,1.0,N/A,No reward implemented.,1.0,N/R,The passage does not state the per-round endowment.,0.0,N/R,Article only notes that high levels of cooperation are achieved; no numeric rate is given.,0.0,N/R,No numeric contribution data in this summary.,0.0,N/R,"While cooperation increases, efficiency numbers are not provided.",0.0,N/R,Payoff levels not specified.,0.0,N/R,"Country (e.g., Switzerland in original papers) not restated here.",0.0,N/R,No age demographics provided.,0.0,N/R,No gender information.,0.0,N/R,No description of educational background.,0.0,On site,Canonical lab-based public goods experiments.,0.9,"An n-person public goods game repeated in an anonymous, effectively one-shot manner each period. After observing others’ contributions, each participant can spend costly 'punishment points' to reduce others' payoffs, with no future interaction benefits because partners are reshuffled and identities remain anonymous. Despite the lack of direct future benefits, many subjects punish defectors, raising contribution levels in the group.",Paraphrased from description emphasizing anonymous one-shot interactions and costly punishment increasing cooperation.,0.9
10.1016_j.jtbi.2010.02.015.md,Fehr & Fischbacher 2004a – Third-party punishment in one-shot PD,The article discusses 'third-party punishment experiments' from Fehr & Fischbacher (2004a) with third parties punishing defectors in observed one-shot two-person PD.,0.9,Presence of a third-party with costly punishment option observing a one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma between two players.,The key manipulation is the ability of a non-involved observer to punish defectors after a one-shot PD interaction.,0.9,True,Described as an experiment with human third parties punishing others.,0.99,True,Controlled PD game with an added third-party punishment stage.,0.99,True,Standard social preference experiment conducted in a lab.,0.9,False,Involves human subjects.,0.99,False,"Empirical, not analytical only.",0.99,3,Two PD players plus one third-party punisher.,0.9,1,Interactions are described as 'one-shot two-person PD' observed by a third party.,0.95,1,Standard PD has binary cooperate/defect; no mention of continuous contributions.,0.95,0,In PD each player controls their entire decision; nothing is pre-allocated.,0.85,N/R,Specific payoff numbers not given here.,0.0,0,Experiments emphasize anonymous one-shot nature; chat would undermine this.,0.9,1,Third party must observe the PD outcome (who cooperated/defected) to punish defectors.,0.95,N/R,The summary does not indicate whether first-order players learn the identity of the third-party punisher.,0.0,0,Only punishment is discussed; no third-party rewards.,0.95,1,One-shot structure implies known single round.,0.95,1,Third party has a costly punishment option against defectors.,0.99,N/R,The passage does not specify cost per unit of punishment.,0.0,N/R,Impact per punishment unit on target payoff not reported here.,0.0,0,No third-party reward mechanism mentioned.,0.99,N/A,No reward option.,1.0,N/A,No reward option.,1.0,N/R,Exact endowment/payoff parameters not specified.,0.0,N/R,"The summary focuses on presence of third-party punishment, not