favor report branch, briber’s payoff is 100-40-10=50? But instructions for framed/unframed symmetric example use 50. However, for asymmetric treatment, only p_B changes to 10. So punishment cost to briber when she reports is 10 taler (not 50). Here we code cost per punished briber as 10.",0.7,1,Each punished player loses p_B or p_R taler per punishment event; one-to-one mapping of fine units to loss.,0.8,0,No positive sanctioning or reward described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,80,"Average of role-specific endowments: briber 100 taler, recipient 60 taler. To avoid picking one role only, we record the mean.",0.6,N/R,No normalized contribution rate reported; design is not a contribution game.,1,N/R,"Binary/fixed bribe amount; paper reports frequencies of offers, not average taler transferred.",1,N/R,No aggregate efficiency measure (payoff vs maximum) is reported.,1,N/R,Mean joint payoffs per pair are not tabulated.,1,Germany,Sessions 3 and 4 are run at BonnEconLab in Germany; this is session 3 (Asymm Germany).,1,23 (mean),Overall German sample mean age reported as 23; no country-by-treatment differences noted.,0.7,50% female,German sample: 50% female; treatments not differentiated.,0.7,"Mainly undergraduate students from various disciplines (law, economics, political science, mathematics)",Described for the combined sample; applies here.,0.8,On site,Lab experiments conducted in physical experimental labs in Bonn.,1,"Two-player bribery game; briber endowment 100 taler, recipient 60 taler; fixed bribe 40 taler; favor yields +120 taler to briber; if favor granted, detection probability 0.25 with fines p_B=10, p_R=50 and confiscation of bribe; if favor not granted after accepting bribe, briber can report leading to fines p_B=10, p_R=50 and confiscation.",Summarized from Section 3 parameterization for asymmetric punishment.,1
