10.1007_s00265-019-2667-y.md,Analytical NIR model – baseline condition,Paper presents a single analytical evolutionary game-theoretic model of negative indirect reciprocity (NIR); no separate empirical or simulation conditions are described. Treat the core model as one analytical condition.,0.9,"Model parameters: frequency of contribution game (ρ), assortment (r), cost and benefit of contribution (c, b), takings and damage in theft game (t, d), error probabilities in contribution and theft games (ε, η), and reputational response to non-contribution (ζ). This row represents the general analytical treatment over the full parameter space, including special cases such as ρ→0, ζ→0, ζ→1.",Theoretical analysis varies these parameters symbolically and via inequalities/limits; no fixed experimental treatments with human subjects are defined.,0.95,False,The work is a formal theoretical model of reputation and cooperation with no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment or manipulation with subjects; purely theoretical derivations.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; analytical modeling only.,1,False,"Although the paper mentions simulations in supplementary materials to illustrate analytical results, the main text provided here contains only analytical expressions; no explicit simulation design details are given to extract as a separate condition.",0.6,True,"Core contribution is closed-form analytical derivations of stability conditions and equilibria for strategies D, R, S, M under NIR.",1,2,"Interactions are modeled as pairwise encounters between a focal individual and a randomly drawn peer (e.g., theft game victim); group-level population is large but strategic interaction is dyadic.",0.8,1000000,"The model considers long-run stationary distributions and evolutionary stability, effectively an infinite-horizon repeated-interaction framework; represent as very large number of rounds.",0.4,1,"In each game individuals choose between discrete actions (exploit vs not; contribute vs not). There is no continuous contribution level but also no all-or-nothing endowment division as in standard public goods games. Use 1 to indicate that contribution is not restricted to an all-or-nothing contribution of a divisible endowment, but note that action is binary.",0.4,N/A,No human endowment structure or preallocated contribution is modeled; only binary contribute/not decisions.,1,N/R,"Model refers to benefit b from contribution and assortment r, not to a standard linear public goods MPCR; no explicit multiplier divided by group size is defined.",0.7,N/A,No human subjects or communication process are modeled.,1,N/A,Information structure is abstract (reputation states) in an analytical model; no user interface or summaries as in experiments.,1,N/A,No explicit identity revelation mechanism to human subjects; reputational effects are modeled abstractly.,1,N/A,No explicit reward identity mechanism in a human-subject context.,1,N/A,Analytical infinite-horizon model; no interface displaying round numbers.,1,1,Negative indirect reciprocity functions as a punishment mechanism via tolerated exploitation of bad-reputation individuals.,1,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as exploitation delivering takings t to the exploiter, not as a costly sanction in the public goods sense; no per-unit cost of punishment is parameterized.",0.7,N/A,"No explicit linear punishment technology mapping punisher cost to victim loss beyond t and d, which are general payoffs of theft, not a calibrated punishment scheme.",0.7,1,"Individuals can pay cost c in the contribution game to gain reputational benefits and produce benefit b to others; this is effectively a reward-like pro-social act, though framed as contribution not targeted reward.",0.6,N/A,"Cost c is the cost of contribution, not a cost per unit of reward directed to a specific partner as in reward-punishment games; no separate reward technology is defined.",0.6,N/A,No explicit mapping of reward units to recipient payoff; only a generic benefit b from contribution.,0.7,N/A,"No human players with monetary endowments; only costs and benefits (c, b, t, d) in an abstract payoff space.",1,N/R,Model derives conditions for stability of contributing strategies but does not report a single numeric steady-state contribution rate as a fraction 0–1.,0.6,N/R,Contributions are binary acts with abstract cost c; no concrete token or point amounts are given.,0.7,N/R,Paper discusses welfare qualitatively but does not present explicit normalized efficiency values relative to a maximum payoff benchmark.,0.6,N/R,"Expected fitness expressions are given for individuals of each strategy (w_R, w_D, etc.), not aggregated group payoff numbers.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a theoretical analysis without empirical data collection.,1,"Analytical evolutionary model of negative indirect reciprocity with two games: a theft game (exploit vs do nothing with payoffs t and d) and a contribution game (pay cost c to produce benefit b and potentially improve reputation). Population has binary reputations (good/bad). Strategies: D (exploit all, never contribute), R (never exploit well-reputed, always contribute), S (never exploit well-reputed, never contribute), M (exploit all, always contribute). Reputation transitions depend on exploitation of good-reputation victims, failure to contribute when community cares (ζ), and perception errors in both games (ε, η). Stability conditions derived for R against D, S, and M, exploring parameter regimes and stages of socio-cognitive complexity (ρ and ζ varying across stages 1–3).",Summarizes the structure and strategic elements of the analytical model as described in the Model and Results sections.,1
10.1017_bpp.2019.4.md,Experiment 1 – Hidden condition,"Condition where participants only saw donation amounts, not donor income ranges.",1,Income visibility: incomes hidden; Task: choose one of five donors to receive $1 bonus,Only manipulation described is whether donors’ income ranges are shown or not.,1,True,Used Amazon Mechanical Turk participants making real bonus allocation decisions.,1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to hidden vs revealed conditions.,1,False,"Conducted on Amazon Mechanical Turk, not a physical laboratory.",1,False,No computer-only agents or simulations described.,1,False,No formal closed-form modeling; purely behavioral data.,1,1,Each participant makes an individual choice about five donors; not an interactive multi-player game.,0.9,1,Single decision about which donor to reward.,1,0,Participants could only choose a single donor for a fixed $1; no continuous contribution choice.,0.9,0,No endowment or contribution decision by participants; default 0 per instructions when no public goods contribution exists.,0.5,N/A,No public goods game or multiplier; just allocation of a fixed bonus.,1,0,No communication between MTurk participants mentioned.,1,0,"Participants only saw donor incomes/donations, not behavior of other choosers.",1,0,No punishment stage or identities of punished parties.,1,0,"Donors were described, but not as co-players; no identity revelation across participants.",0.8,0,One-shot decision; number of rounds not relevant or displayed.,0.9,0,Participants only assign a positive $1 bonus; no option to reduce payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,Participants assign a $1 bonus to a chosen donor.,1,0,Participants did not sacrifice their own payoff; experimenter-funded $1 bonus.,0.9,1,Each chosen donor receives a fixed $1; effectively 1 unit of reward yields 1 bonus unit.,0.8,0,Participants allocate an experimenter-provided bonus; they have no token endowment to divide.,0.8,N/R,No participant contribution decision to a public good.,1,N/R,"Outcome is which donor is rewarded, not a contribution amount.",1,N/R,No public goods structure to compute efficiency.,1,N/R,No group payoff is defined; only a donor bonus allocation.,1,USA,Explicitly states participants were recruited on MTurk from the USA.,1,N/R,No age information reported.,1,N/R,No gender distribution reported.,1,N/R,Education level of participants not described.,1,Online,"Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk, an online platform.",1,"One-shot allocation task: participants choose one of five real donors, whose past annual donations mirror US donation distribution by income quintile, to receive a $1 bonus. Hidden condition shows only donation amounts, not income ranges.",Summarized from Experiment 1 methods description.,1
