when making punishment decisions, allowing sanctions based on contribution as a percentage of income.",Derived from Experiment 2 methods and the described manipulation between hidden and revealed income.,1
10.1017_bpp.2019.4.md,"Experiment 2 – Revealed income, Reward only",Condition where incomes are revealed and only reward is allowed.,1,Income visibility: revealed; Sanction type: reward only,Fourth cell of Experiment 2’s 2×2 design.,1,True,Involves human participants performing incentivized tasks.,1,True,Randomized between-participants design with controlled conditions.,1,False,Online MTurk context.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation without humans.,1,False,"Results are empirical, not analytical derivations.",1,5,Groups of five.,1,10,Ten repeated rounds.,1,1,Any amount of income can be contributed to the pool.,1,0,Endowments begin as private tokens.,1,0.4,Standard PGG structure with doubled contributions split among 5.,1,0,No facility for participant communication reported.,1,1,Participants receive a summary of their payoff and others’ sanctions each round.,0.9,N/A,Punishment not available in this reward-only condition.,1,0,Anonymity is maintained via changing random letter IDs; no stable IDs linked to rewards.,1,0,No explicit mention of displaying number of rounds.,0.5,0,Condition only permits reward.,1,N/A,No punishment in this cell.,1,N/A,No punishment in this cell.,1,1,Participants can spend to increase others’ payoffs.,1,1,Reward mechanism: 1 unit spent yields +3 to target.,1,3,Reward multiplier is 3.,1,100,Total endowment for group per round is 100 units.,0.7,N/R,No separate normalized contribution rate by this exact cell reported.,0.5,N/R,"Main text aggregates by hidden vs revealed, not by sanction mode.",0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculation by condition reported.,0.5,N/R,"Payoffs discussed primarily by quintile and visibility, not sanction type.",0.5,USA,Experiment 2 uses US income distribution and MTurk US-like participants.,0.9,N/R,Age not specified.,1,N/R,No gender data.,1,N/R,Education not provided.,1,Online,Runs on Amazon Mechanical Turk platform.,1,"Heterogeneous-income 10-round PGG. Stage 2: participants can spend up to 2 units per other player on reward (1:3 cost-to-impact). Incomes of all players are revealed when making reward decisions, enabling conditioning on contribution as a percentage of income.",From Experiment 2 description and revealed condition details.,1
10.1017_bpp.2019.4.md,"Experiment 3 – Hidden income, Punishment and reward available",Experiment 3 condition with incomes hidden where both reward and punishment options are available.,1,Income visibility: hidden; Income origin: earned via effort; Sanction options: both punishment and reward,"Experiment 3 manipulates income visibility; incomes are merit-based, and both sanction types are allowed simultaneously.",1,True,MTurk participants performed an effort task and then played a public goods game.,1,True,Random assignment to hidden vs revealed visibility; controlled game structure.,1,False,Conducted on MTurk (online).,1,False,Empirical human-subject experiment.,1,False,No analytical modeling as the primary method.,1,5,"Same public goods paradigm as Experiment 2 with groups of five; though not re-stated, implied.",0.8,10,"Described as the same repeated two-stage economic game as Experiment 2, which had 10 rounds.",0.8,1,Same basic PGG structure as Experiment 2 where any amount can be contributed.,0.8,0,Endowments are incomes allocated to private accounts; participants choose contributions.,0.9,N/R,They state the setup is similar to Experiment 2 but do not explicitly restate the multiplier; main text for Experiment 3 omits multiplier details.,0.4,0,"As in Experiment 2, no communication between players mentioned.",0.9,1,"Design is said to be similar to Experiment 2, where payoff summaries and sanctions received are shown each round.",0.7,0,No indication that stable identities are provided; anonymity likely as in Experiment 2.,0.6,0,Same anonymity argument as for punishment IDs.,0.6,0,No information on round display in Experiment 3 methods.,0.4,1,Text: “both reward and punishment options were available simultaneously.”,1,N/R,They do not restate specific cost/impact parameters for sanctions in Experiment 3.,0.4,N/R,Magnitude of punishment per unit not reported in Experiment 3 description.,0.4,1,"Reward options are available, in parallel to punishment.",1,N/R,Reward cost per unit not explicitly described for Experiment 3.,0.4,N/R,Reward technology (effect per unit spent) not reported in main text.,0.4,N/R,"While incomes are based on US quintiles, specific token endowments are not provided in the main text.",0.4,N/R,Only qualitative patterns are reported (richer contribute more in absolute terms but less in percentage).,0.6,N/R,Absolute amounts per condition not fully reported in the main text.,0.6,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric given.,0.5,N/R,"Only Gini indices and per-quintile round payoffs are described, not total group payoffs per condition.",0.5,USA,Explicitly states Experiment 3 participants were recruited exclusively from the USA.,1,N/R,Age not reported for Experiment 3.,1,N/R,No gender information.,1,N/R,Education not reported.,1,Online,Participants recruited on Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Two-phase experiment. Phase 1: individual effort task determines rank in an income distribution modeled on US quintiles; best performer gets highest income, etc. Phase 2: repeated two-stage PGG similar to Experiment 2 with heterogeneous earned incomes. Both reward and punishment are possible in Stage 2, and participants cannot spend more on sanctions than earned in Stage 1 of each round. Hidden condition: participants see only their own income and the sum of all incomes; they are informed that the income distribution is derived from the US income distribution but do not see specific incomes of others.",Summarized from Experiment 3 methods and subsequent description of hidden condition.,1
10.1017_bpp.2019.4.md,"Experiment 3 – Revealed income, Punishment and reward available",Experiment 3 condition with incomes revealed and both punishment and reward options available.,1,Income visibility: revealed; Income origin: earned via effort; Sanction options: both punishment and reward,Visibility of income is the experimental manipulation; merit-based incomes and sanction options are constant.,1,True,Behavioral experiment with MTurk participants.,1,True,Random assignment to revealed vs hidden income conditions.,1,False,Online via MTurk.,1,False,"Study uses human participants, not simulations.",1,False,No closed-form modelling as primary method.,1,5,"Design parallels Experiment 2, which uses groups of five.",0.8,10,Described as the same repeated two-stage game as Experiment 2 (10 rounds).,0.8,1,Standard PGG with free choice of contribution amount.,0.8,0,Endowment is private income from which contributions are made.,0.9,N/R,Multiplier not restated in Experiment 3 description.,0.4,0,No communication among participants is mentioned.,0.9,1,"Likely same feedback structure as Experiment 2, given 'same repeated two-stage economic game' wording.",0.7,0,No indication of stable identities linked to sanctions.,0.6,0,Same anonymity assumptions as punishment IDs.,0.6,0,Number of rounds displayed is not specified.,0.4,1,Both reward and punishment options were available.,1,N/R,No explicit parameters given for punishment in Experiment 3.,0.4,N/R,Effect per punishment unit not detailed here.,0.4,1,Reward is also available in Stage 2.,1,N/R,Reward cost parameters not restated.,0.4,N/R,Reward technology not given in the Experiment 3 main text.,0.4,N/R,"Exact token incomes by quintile not provided, only that distribution derived from US quintiles.",0.4,N/R,No aggregate rate per condition reported; only regression coefficients by income level.,0.6,N/R,Contributions are described qualitatively but not summarized numerically per condition.,0.6,N/R,Efficiency metrics not provided.,0.5,N/R,Only Gini comparisons between hidden and revealed are given.,0.5,USA,States that participants were recruited exclusively from the USA.,1,N/R,No age data.,1,N/R,Gender not described.,1,N/R,Education not mentioned.,1,Online,Conducted using Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Same two-phase structure as in the hidden condition: Phase 1 effort task determining income ranks; Phase 2 heterogeneous-income repeated PGG with both punishment and reward options. In the revealed condition, participants see their own income, incomes of all other group members, and the sum of incomes when deciding on sanctions, and they are informed that the income distribution is based on the US income distribution.",Based on Experiment 3 methods statement that revealed participants see all incomes.,1
10.1017_bpp.2019.4.md,Experiment 4 – Hidden income (school district taxes),Condition where participants see PTA contributions but not district incomes when assigning additional taxes.,1,Income visibility: hidden; Task: choose which of five school districts’ parents should pay an additional $2000 per-school tax,Only difference between conditions is whether median incomes are shown alongside PTA donations.,1,True,MTurk participants making incentivized (or at least consequential in analysis) policy preference choices.,1,True,Random assignment to hidden vs revealed conditions.,1,False,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,False,"Human-subject data, no simulation.",1,False,No analytical formalism described as the study.,1,1,Each participant independently chooses which of five districts should be taxed; not an interactive group game.,0.9,1,Single decision per participant.,1,0,Not a contribution game; participants pick a district; no continuous contribution choice.,0.8,0,Participants are not given an endowment to contribute; default 0 for human non-PGG designs.,0.5,N/A,No public goods contribution decision driven by marginal return; government tax scenario with fixed target.,1,0,No communication reported.,1,0,Participants do not see others’ choices or outcomes.,1,0,Districts are labeled with random two-letter strings; participants do not see others’ identities in a social game.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism; only selection of a district for extra tax.,1,0,One-shot vignette; no round information.,1,1,Choosing which district pays additional tax is interpreted as punishment.,1,0,Participants do not pay to punish; tax is hypothetical and borne by selected district.,0.9,N/R,Magnitude per unit not structured like token-based punishment; total tax is fixed at $2000 per school; no per-unit decision.,0.5,0,There is no option to increase payoffs for districts; only choosing who is taxed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Participants are not endowed with tokens or money to allocate.,0.9,N/R,"Outcome is district chosen for tax, not contribution level.",1,N/R,No contribution decision amount.,1,N/R,No efficiency concept for this one-shot tax assignment choice.,1,N/R,Group payoff is not computed; only distribution of chosen districts.,1,USA,"Experiment 4 participants recruited on MTurk, and school data are from New York; consistent with earlier experiments using US participants.",0.8,N/R,"Age distribution not given; only an exploratory age analysis is mentioned, without numbers.",0.9,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,1,N/R,Education not described.,1,Online,Participants recruited on Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Participants see five anonymized schools, each showing only the average PTA donation (e.g., $353, $1227, $4249, $9759, $1486), modeled from real New York City district data, but no income information. They are told the city wants to raise an additional $2000 per school through taxation and asked which school’s parents should pay this additional tax. Choice of district is interpreted as targeting poorer vs richer districts for punishment.","Summarized from Experiment 4 methods, focusing on hidden-income condition description.",1
10.1017_bpp.2019.4.md,Experiment 4 – Revealed income (school district taxes),Condition where participants see both PTA contributions and median incomes when assigning additional taxes.,1,Income visibility: revealed; Task: choose which of five school districts’ parents should pay an additional $2000 per-school tax,Same task as hidden condition plus median income information for each district.,1,True,Human participants making choices about hypothetical tax policy.,1,True,"Randomized assignment to revealed vs hidden, controlled vignette.",1,False,Online MTurk sample.,1,False,No simulation-only methods.,1,False,"Data-based study, not a theoretical derivation.",1,1,Each decision-maker acts independently in a vignette.,0.9,1,Single-choice scenario.,1,0,No contribution to a PGG; participants select one district.,0.8,0,No personal endowment contribution decision.,0.5,N/A,Not a PGG; no multiplier.,1,0,No communication among participants.,1,0,No feedback on others’ choices.,1,0,Districts are anonymized; no social identity of punished agents is displayed.,0.9,0,No reward options.,1,0,"One-shot choice, no rounds.",1,1,Selecting which district to bear additional tax is a form of punishment.,1,0,Participants incur no personal cost to assign tax burden.,0.9,N/R,Total tax is fixed at $2000 per school; not parameterized in per-unit sanction terms.,0.5,0,No ability to increase any district’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Participants are not endowed with tokens to allocate.,0.9,N/R,"Decision is which district to tax, not contribution.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts are chosen.,1,N/R,No efficiency-based payoffs.,1,N/R,No group payoff metric calculated.,1,USA,MTurk participants; education funding scenario based on New York districts; consistent with US sample.,0.8,N/R,Age breakdown not given.,0.9,N/R,No gender reporting.,1,N/R,Education not detailed.,1,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Participants view five anonymized schools, each with its average PTA donation and median household income based on actual New York City district data (e.g., $353 donation / $35,176 income; $9759 donation / $71,622 income). They are told the city wants to raise an additional $2000 per school and asked which school’s parents should pay this tax. Revealing incomes allows them to consider both generosity (donations) and ability to pay (incomes) when choosing a district to ‘punish’ with the tax.",Summarized from Experiment 4 methods for the revealed-income condition.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0085691.md,Public goods game with costly punishment – single condition,"Only one experimental condition is described: a standard public goods game with punishment, no variations or treatments are mentioned.",1,"Basal endogenous cortisol level (continuous, individual differences)",Study examines correlation between pre-game basal cortisol and costly punishment; cortisol is not manipulated but treated as a predictor.,1,True,Human male volunteers participated in a public goods game.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with specified rules and payoffs conducted in a lab setting.,1,True,"Participants from a German university played in computer-mediated sessions; typical lab experiment description, not field.",1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations; all behavior from human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical or formal modeling; primary method is behavioral experiment plus correlational analysis.,1,4,"Paper: ""four players constituted one group"" in the public goods game.",1,6,"Paper: ""Six periods of the public goods game were played"".",1,1,"Each player could choose how many MUs to keep or contribute, implying any amount from 0 to 20, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Endowment is given to each player, and they decide how many MUs to keep or contribute; all initially in private account.",1,0.4,"Each MU contributed is multiplied by 1.6, and each of 4 players gets one fourth: MPCR = 1.6 / 4 = 0.4.",1,0,"No mention of any communication; described as anonymous, computer-mediated with simultaneous decisions.",0.9,1,"After contributions, ""each player was given accurate information on the contributions of the other three players""; they also observe payoff effects implicitly via punishment stage outcomes.",0.9,0,Game is anonymous; no indication that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,Standard z-Tree public goods game experiments inform subjects about the number of periods; not explicitly stated but typically reported and necessary for instructions.,0.6,1,"Explicit ""option to punish"" with MUs reducing others' payoffs.",1,1,"Participants ""investing own MUs""; one MU invested is a cost of 1 MU to punisher.",1,3,"Investment of x MUs reduces another's payoff by 3x (e.g., ""investment of 2 MUs decreased the payoff of another by 6 MUs"").",1,0,Only punishment option described; no possibility to spend MUs to increase others' payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Each player was endowed with 20 money units.,1,0.6540833333333333,"Mean contribution across six periods is 78.49 MUs out of max 120 (6×20), so rate = 78.49/120 ≈ 0.6541.",1,78.49,Reported directly as mean number of MUs invested in contribution across six periods.,1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff or efficiency measure versus theoretical maximum is reported.,0,N/R,"Only average individual earnings are given (€5.88), not group-level MUs or payoffs per group.",0,Germany,"Participants are volunteers from a German university, and the study is conducted at University of Ulm in Germany.",1,Mean 22.0 years (SD = 1.55); adults,"Paper: ""M_age = 22.0, SD = 1.55"" for participants.",1,All male,"Paper: ""one hundred and eighty-two healthy non-smoking male volunteers"".",1,University students,Described as volunteers from a German university; typical subject pool of university students.,0.9,On site,"Lab-style, computer-mediated experiment with z-Tree; participants attended sessions physically and were paid privately at end.",1,"Standard repeated public goods game with punishment: 4 players, 6 anonymous periods, 20 MU endowment each round, each MU contributed multiplied by 1.6, equal share of public good, stranger matching (group composition changes each period), punishment stage allows 0–10 MU punishment per other player with 1:3 cost-to-damage ratio. Costly punishment variable sums MUs spent across six periods on players who contributed less than punisher.",Summarizes structural features explicitly described in the method section.,1
10.1093_jleo_ewp039.md,Analytical signaling model of sanctions in a public good game,"The paper presents only a formal, sequential game-theoretic model of government sanctions and citizen contributions; no distinct experimental treatments with human or simulated agents are implemented.",1,"Government sanction level g and proportion of conditional cooperators ω; information structure (symmetric vs asymmetric) considered analytically, not as empirical treatments.","The model varies g and ω, and compares symmetric vs asymmetric information cases, but these are theoretical parameters, not experimental manipulations on subjects.",1,False,"No human participants, no data collection; the paper develops a theoretical signaling model with propositions and proofs.",1,False,There is no controlled experiment or randomized treatment; only comparative statics in a formal model.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person sessions are conducted; analysis is purely theoretical.,1,False,The paper does not report any numerical or agent-based simulations; all results are analytical.,1,True,"The study consists of a closed-form game-theoretic signaling model with propositions and proofs, fitting the definition of an analytical study.",1,1,"The model assumes a countably infinite population of citizens of measure 1; since this is a continuum and not a discrete player count, no concrete numeric group size like 2, 4, etc. is specified. Using 1 as a placeholder would be misleading, but schema requires a number, so 1 is entered with explanation that it represents a unit mass rather than an actual player count.",0.2,1,"The timing specifies a single-shot game: nature selects ω, government chooses g, then citizens choose c∈{0,1} once.",1,0,"Citizens choose contribution level c∈{0,1}, so they face an all-or-nothing contribution choice rather than a continuous amount.",1,N/A,No human subjects or implemented endowment structure; this construct does not apply to a purely analytical model.,1,N/R,"The public good return is modeled as h(m), an increasing function of the proportion of contributors; no explicit linear MPCR is reported.",1,N/A,No experimental communication protocol exists because there are no human participants.,1,0,"In the theoretical model, citizens know only their type, prior over ω, and g; they do not observe others' earnings or contribution summaries.",1,0,Sanctions are imposed by an anonymous government authority; no individual punisher identities are modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards/subsidies are discussed only conceptually as an extension; no explicit reward technology is modeled in detail.,1,0,The model is one-shot; there is no notion of displaying the number of rounds.,1,1,A central element is a government-imposed sanction g≥0 on defection; defection incurs g while cooperation does not.,1,N/R,"The model includes a government cost parameter α in W(m,g)=m−αg, but this is not a per-unit cost borne by punishers in a game-theoretic sense; individual players do not pay to punish.",0.3,N/R,"The sanction g directly subtracts from a defector’s payoff; no explicit per-token punishment technology (e.g., 1 coin causes 3 units of loss) is specified.",0.5,0,"Rewards are mentioned as a possible extension, but no concrete reward mechanism is implemented in the formal model.",1,N/A,"No explicit reward mechanism is modeled, so reward cost is not defined.",1,N/A,No explicit reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,"Citizens choose c∈{0,1} and incur a cost of contributing, but no explicit initial endowment in tokens or monetary units is specified.",0.5,N/R,There are no observed data; the model analyzes equilibrium fractions of contributors m analytically but does not report numeric outcome statistics.,1,N/R,No empirical or simulated contribution amounts are reported; contributions are abstract costs (0 or 1).,1,N/R,"While welfare W(m,g) is analyzed conceptually, no normalized efficiency measure (total payoff over maximum possible) is numerically reported.",1,N/R,The paper does not report specific numerical group payoffs; only functional forms and comparative statics are given.,1,N/A,There are no human participants; thus no participant country.,1,N/A,No human sample is used.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No participant pool is described because the study is theoretical.,1,No human,The study is purely analytical with no human or field implementation.,1,"Sequential public good game with a continuum of agents of measure 1, two types (egoists and conditional cooperators), government sets a sanction g on defection after observing the proportion of high types ω, and citizens then choose contribution c∈{0,1}; payoff from public good is h(m), increasing in the fraction of contributors m, and government objective is W(m,g)=m−αg.",Summarizes the core game structure as described in Sections 3–4 of the paper.,1
10.1093_esr_jcw014.md,Study 1 – Male confederate – Non-silent IC cars,Study 1 is explicitly described as 31 interventions with a male confederate in intercity (IC) trains that comprise non-silent-area cars only.,1,"Train line and car type with male confederate: IC Zurich–Bern, non-silent-area cars only","Across conditions they vary train line, car type (IC vs ICE; silent vs non-silent-area), and confederate gender; here the level is IC, non-silent-area, male.",1,True,Field study with actual train passengers reacting to a confederate’s behavior.,1,True,"Authors call it a quasi-experiment with controlled interventions (standardized norm violation, measured responses).",0.95,False,"Conducted in trains in natural setting, not a laboratory.",1,False,No computer or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,Paper contains theoretical modeling but this condition describes empirical field interventions.,1,12.39,"Mean number of passengers in car is 12.39 across all 90 interventions; study-1-only mean not separated, so overall mean used as best available.",0.4,1,Each intervention is a one-shot situation in a car; no repeated rounds with same group.,0.9,1,"Passengers can either sanction or not; no constrained contribution grid like in standard PGG, treated as binary contribution choice (sanction).",0.7,0,"No endowment or contribution mechanism; by convention for such non-PGG field settings we set 0, indicating no initial contribution to public fund.",0.2,N/R,"No tokens, multipliers, or payoff functions are specified in numerical form.",0,1,"Passengers can freely talk; no restriction on communication is mentioned, so communication is possible.",0.8,0,Passengers observe behavior directly but are not shown summary statistics of others’ earnings or sanctions.,1,1,Any sanction necessarily reveals who is speaking to whom in a face-to-face setting.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism is implemented or discussed.,1,0,"Passengers are not informed that they are in an experiment, nor about number of interventions; the remaining time to destination is given by the timetable but not framed as ‘rounds’.",0.8,1,Negative peer-sanctioning (reprimanding the norm breaker) is the focal behavior.,1,N/R,"Costs are discussed qualitatively (effort, risk of aggression) but not quantified in tokens or money.",0,N/R,No numeric mapping from sanction units to payoffs or noise reduction is specified.,0,0,Only negative sanctions are measured; no positive rewards are provided.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No experimental monetary or token endowment is provided to passengers.,0,0.29,"Study 1 sanctioning rate reported as 29%; in this VOD framing, ‘contribution’ is sanctioning, normalized between 0 and 1.",0.95,0,"No numeric contribution units (tokens, money) are specified; sanctions are binary events so we code amount as 0 tokens.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff nor maximum payoff benchmarks reported in numeric form.,0,N/R,No earnings or payoff amounts are reported.,0,Switzerland,"Paper: ‘Most people, at least in Switzerland, where the two studies were conducted…’; trains are Swiss intercity lines.",1,Estimated 5–75 years; mean around 40 years across both studies,"Overall passenger descriptive statistics: estimated age 5–75, mean 39.55; study-specific ages not separated.",0.7,Mixed male and female passengers; proportion female about 0.54 across both studies,Overall variable ‘Is female’ has mean 0.54; not broken out by study.,0.7,N/R,No education information reported.,0,Field experiment,Quasi-experimental interventions in naturally occurring train environments.,1,Volunteer’s dilemma (step-level public good) framed as silence norm enforcement by passengers in a single open-plan IC train car; one confederate plays loud music for up to 4:48 minutes; the first passenger who directly asks to stop counts as sanctioner; outcome is whether a sanction occurs and time to sanction.,Summarizes the strategic structure and key procedural details explicitly described in Methods.,1
