10.1038_s41598-017-02952-1.md,Dictator game,Single dictator game described where each participant allocates 10 points between self and a recipient.,1,No between-subject treatment; dictator chooses allocation from 10:0 to 0:10.,"Only one dictator-game condition; variation is each participant’s chosen split, not an experimentally assigned factor.",1,True,Human MTurk participants played incentivised dictator game.,1,True,Controlled incentivised decision task with specified rules and payoffs.,1,False,"Study conducted via MTurk online, not in a physical lab.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all decisions from humans.,1,False,No purely analytical/mathematical model described as a separate condition.,1,2,Dictator and one recipient are involved in each game instance.,1,1,Each participant makes a single allocation decision; no repeated rounds reported.,1,1,Dictators can choose any split in one-point increments from 10:0 to 0:10 (continuous within range).,1,0,All 10 points are initially endowed to the dictator’s private account; nothing starts in a public fund.,1,N/R,No public good or linear contribution technology; simple transfer game.,1,0,Only anonymous one-shot decisions via survey; no mention of communication.,1,0,Dictators see only the choice menu; no feedback on others’ earnings is described.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism exists in the dictator game.,1,0,No separate rewarding mechanism; only transfer choice.,1,1,Structure clearly one-shot; participants informed that one game is payoff-relevant.,0.7,0,No option to spend points to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this game.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this game.,1,0,Transfers are the main allocation; no separate costly reward stage.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,10,Dictator is endowed with 10 points to allocate.,1,0.34,Average allocation to partner was 3.41 out of 10 points; 3.41/10 ≈ 0.34.,1,3.41,Reported in Table 1 as mean dictator allocation in points.,1,N/R,No efficiency ratio (total payoff vs. maximum) reported or derivable without explicit utility baseline.,0.3,N/R,Only allocations reported; average combined payoffs not given.,0.2,United States,Sample described as 340 United States residents recruited via MTurk.,1,"18-74 (M = 35.41, SD = 11.39)","Age range and mean/SD reported for the full sample, which includes dictator players.",1,44% female,Overall sample composition reported as 44% female.,1,N/R,No education information reported.,1,Online,Data collected using Qualtrics via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,One-shot dictator game with 10-point endowment; 11 possible splits (10:0 to 0:10) with 0.10 USD per point; no feedback or responses from others.,"Described in Methods: Economic Games, Dictator game section and payment conversion.",1
10.1038_s41598-017-02952-1.md,Third-party punishment game (3PP),Condition where participants act as third party with costly punishment after observing dictator allocations.,1,"Game type: third-party punishment; fairness level of dictator transfer (10:0, 9:1, 8:2, 7:3, 6:4, 5:5) varied within-subject via strategy method.",Between-subject factor is assignment to 3PP vs 3PR; within-subject factor is dictator transfer level for which punishment choices are elicited.,1,True,Human MTurk participants made incentivised punishment decisions.,1,True,Controlled economic game with random assignment to punishment vs recompensation variant.,1,False,"Study run online via MTurk, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"All data from human subjects, no simulation-only condition.",1,False,No separate analytical model condition.,1,3,"Each game involves dictator (A), recipient (B), and third party (C).",1,1,"Single interaction; strategy method over hypothetical dictator transfers, but no repeated dynamic rounds.",1,1,Third party can spend any portion of 5-point endowment (0–5) on punishment in one-point increments.,1,0,Endowment of 5 points is initially held by third party; nothing starts in a public pool.,1,N/R,Not a public goods or linear contribution setting; punishment technology instead.,1,0,No communication options mentioned; anonymous one-shot decisions.,1,0,Third party sees dictator’s choice but no aggregate histories or others’ payoffs are summarized.,0.8,0,Partners are anonymous strangers; no identifying information tied to punishment.,1,0,No reward stage in this condition.,1,1,Participants are told that one of the games is payoff-relevant; structure indicates single-shot; no explicit multi-round info.,0.7,1,Third parties can spend points to deduct dictator’s payoff.,1,1,Each punishment point is spent from third party’s 5-point endowment (1-point cost per unit spent).,1,3,Text: each punishment point spent reduces dictator’s payoff by three points.,1,0,No option to increase another player’s payoff in this variant; only deductions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,5,Third party is endowed with 5 points for punishment decisions.,1,0.662,Total punishment mean = 3.31 points out of 5; 3.31/5 ≈ 0.662.,1,3.31,Table 1 reports total punishment points (mean 3.31).,1,N/R,Overall efficiency vs. maximum not reported; would require full joint payoff structure including dictator choices’ distribution.,0.2,N/R,"Average combined payoffs for A, B, C not provided.",0.2,United States,All participants are US residents on MTurk; 3PP subsample drawn from this pool.,1,"18-74 (M = 35.41, SD = 11.39)",Age distribution reported for the overall sample; no differing ages stated for 3PP subset.,0.9,44% female,Gender proportion given for overall sample; no subset figures.,0.9,N/R,No education data are reported.,1,Online,Games implemented via Qualtrics and MTurk.,1,"Three-player third-party punishment game. Stage 1: dictator with 10 points chooses transfer to recipient in 1-point increments from 10:0 to 5:5. Stage 2: third party with 5 points chooses, via strategy method, how many points (0–5) to spend to reduce dictator’s payoff by 3 per point, for each of the six possible dictator transfers. Participants informed that one game is payoff-relevant, with 1 point = 0.10 USD.","Summarized from Methods: Economic Games, description of 3PP and incentive scheme.",1
10.1038_s41598-017-02952-1.md,Third-party recompensation game (3PR),Condition where participants act as third party with costly recompensation to the victim after observing dictator allocations.,1,"Game type: third-party recompensation; fairness level of dictator transfer (10:0, 9:1, 8:2, 7:3, 6:4, 5:5) varied within-subject via strategy method.",Between-subject assignment to recompensation vs punishment; within-subject manipulation of dictator transfer levels.,1,True,Human participants on MTurk made recompensation decisions.,1,True,Controlled random assignment to game variant with specified payoff functions.,1,False,Conducted online via MTurk.,1,False,No non-human simulation conditions.,1,False,No standalone analytical model as condition.,1,3,"Dictator (A), recipient (B), and third party (C) as in 3PP.",1,1,Single interaction structure with strategy method over hypothetical transfers.,1,1,Third party can spend any portion of their 5 points (0–5) on recompensation.,1,0,Third party’s 5-point endowment starts fully in their own account.,1,N/R,Not a public goods setting; only recompensation technology.,1,0,No communication between players described.,1,0,"Third party only observes specific dictator transfer, not histories or aggregated statistics.",0.8,0,No punishment mechanism in recompensation variant.,1,0,"Recompensation targets a recipient, but players are all anonymous; no identities revealed.",1,1,Design is one-shot with strategy method; no repeated-round display mentioned.,0.7,0,Third party cannot reduce any player’s payoff; only increase recipient’s payoff.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,1,Third party can spend points to increase the recipient’s payoff (a reward/helping mechanism).,1,1,Each point spent on recompensation is deducted from the third party’s 5-point endowment.,1,3,Each recompensation point increases recipient’s total payoff by three points.,1,5,Third party endowed with 5 points for recompensation decisions.,1,1.296,"Total 