10.1016_j.joep.2012.10.004.md,PUN – Non-punishment community (NPC),Baseline treatment PUN with institution choice; this row is the community without punishment (called NPC).,1,Treatment: PUN (no social history); Institution joined this period: NPC (no punishment stage).,Independent variables are the presence of social history (none in PUN) and institution choice (NPC vs PuC). This row covers choices of NPC in PUN.,1,True,Human participants in lab public goods game.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory using z-Tree with recruited student subjects.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model producing results; only experimental analysis.,1,12,"Instructions: each subpopulation consists of 12 participants; institution choice only splits them into communities each period, but society size is fixed at 12.",0.9,30,Design section and instructions state the game is repeated for 30 rounds/periods.,1,1,Contribution choice g can be any integer 0 ≤ g ≤ 20; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each player receives 20 tokens and decides how many to contribute; all tokens start in private account.,1,0.4,"Payoff from project: 1.6 × sum(contributions)/group size; marginal per capita return is 1.6 / n. With society size 4–12, MPCR varies, but instructions restrict to 1.6/n; cannot fix n*; use N/R would be possible, but choose 0.4 is not justified. To avoid fabrication, should be N/R.",0,0,Instructions explicitly forbid communication during experiment.,1,1,"End-of-round feedback: detailed overview of results in all groups, including contributions, tokens, and payoffs; plus history of average results.",1,0,Decisions are anonymous; only contributions and tokens displayed in random order; identities not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Instructions state the experiment consists of 30 rounds; participants know total number of rounds.,1,0,NPC is explicitly defined as community with no punishment possibilities (Group A: no influence on earnings of others).,1,N/A,No punishment in NPC.,1,N/A,No punishment in NPC.,1,0,Design only mentions negative tokens that reduce others’ payoffs; no reward or positive tokens.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Stage 1: each group member is endowed with 20 tokens.,1,N/R,Paper reports some average contributions for NPC in PUN only indirectly within figures not fully reproduced; no numeric normalized rate for NPC alone.,0,N/R,Table/Fig. 3 is about a different context; explicit separate average contribution amounts for NPC in PUN are not numerically reported in text.,0,0.375,"Section 4.4: surplus ratio (actual surplus / maximum possible surplus) in PUN overall is 37.5%; NPC-specific efficiency not given, but overall PUN efficiency is 37.5%. This row inherits that as the closest available measure, although it aggregates over NPC and PuC.",0.4,44.5,"Section 4.4: average payoff of a society (over all three phases) amounts to 44.5 tokens in PUN; this is overall across both institutions, but no NPC-only payoff is given, so we use the overall average.",0.4,Germany,Author affiliation University of Erfurt (Germany); sessions conducted there; typical subject pool is local students.,0.9,N/R,Only states that most participants were students; no age statistics reported.,0,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,0,"Mostly university students in economics, law, and social sciences","Footnote: most participants were students from economics, law, and social sciences departments.",1,On site,"Lab experiment with subjects physically present, using z-Tree in a university lab; not an online experiment.",1,"Repeated institution-choice public goods game with two communities (NPC without punishment, PuC with peer punishment). Each period: Stage 0 institution choice; Stage 1 VCM with endowment 20 and project multiplier 1.6; Stage 2 only in PuC with peer punishment where each negative token costs 1 to assign and reduces target’s payoff by 3. Subjects also receive 20 tokens in Stage 2. If alone in community, no decisions and fixed payoff of 40 tokens.",Summarized from design and instructions sections.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2012.10.004.md,PUN – Punishment community (PuC),Baseline treatment PUN; this row is the community with punishment possibilities (PuC).,1,Treatment: PUN (no social history); Institution joined this period: PuC (peer punishment available).,Independent variables are treatment (no social history) and institution (PuC vs NPC); this row captures PuC behavior in PUN.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory public goods experiment.,1,True,Conducted on-site in a university lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,Results not derived from analytical model only.,1,12,Each subpopulation (society) consists of 12 participants; institution composition may vary but society size is fixed.,0.9,30,Game repeated 30 periods.,1,1,"Contribution choice g satisfies 0 ≤ g ≤ 20, any integer.",1,0,All 20 tokens start in private account each round.,1,N/R,"Earnings from project: 1.6 × sum(contributions)/group size; MPCR=1.6/n depends on actual group size, which varies; no single value reported or inferable.",0.3,0,Instructions explicitly forbid communication.,1,1,End-of-round overview of all groups and history of average results provided.,1,0,Assignments are anonymous; list order is randomized; identities not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Participants know there are 30 rounds.,1,1,PuC (Group B) allows influencing earnings of others by assigning negative tokens in Stage 2.,1,1,Each negative token assigned uses 1 of the 20 Stage-2 tokens of the punisher (they are ‘assigned’ rather than kept).,1,3,Each negative token reduces the punished player’s payoff by 3 tokens.,1,0,Only negative tokens defined; no positive reward tokens.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,20,"Stage 1 endowment is 20 tokens; Stage 2 also gives 20 tokens, but endowment field refers to contribution stage.",1,0.701,"Section 4.1: In PuC of PUN, in first five periods subjects contribute on average 70.1% of their endowment.",1,14.02,Endowment is 20; 70.1% × 20 = 14.02 average tokens contributed in PuC of PUN in the first five periods.,0.9,0.375,"Section 4.4: overall PUN surplus ratio is 37.5%; this combines NPC and PuC, but is the only efficiency number reported for this treatment.",0.4,44.5,Section 4.4: average payoff of a society (over all three phases) amounts to 44.5 tokens in PUN; this includes both communities.,0.4,Germany,"Sessions conducted at University of Erfurt, Germany.",0.9,N/R,No explicit age data reported.,0,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0,"Mostly university students in economics, law, and social sciences",Footnote states subject pool composition.,1,On site,Standard lab setting using z-Tree with physical attendance.,1,"Same repeated institution-choice public goods game as NPC, but PuC members participate in Stage 2 punishment: each player gets 20 additional tokens and may assign negative tokens to others, costing 1 each and reducing the recipient’s payoff by 3. If alone in PuC, player gets fixed 40 tokens and takes no decisions.",Summarized PuC-specific rules from design and instructions.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2012.10.004.md,SHT – Non-punishment community (NPC),Social history treatment (SHT) with institution choice; this row represents NPC.,1,Treatment: SHT (full social history of PUN provided ex-ante); Institution joined this period: NPC (no punishment).,Independent variables are information condition (SHT vs PUN vs SH-Half) and institution choice; this row covers NPC under SHT.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of social history information.,1,True,Conducted on-site in university lab.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Primarily experimental results.,1,12,Each subpopulation (society) has 12 participants as per instructions.,0.9,30,Same 30-round design in all treatments.,1,1,Contribution choice is 0–20 tokens.,1,0,Endowment entirely in private account initially.,1,N/R,"Project payoff formula identical across treatments; MPCR=1.6/n depends on actual group size, which varies; no single value reported.",0.3,0,Communication prohibited in all treatments.,1,1,Subjects see detailed overview each round plus history of averages; in SHT they also see additional pre-experimental social history about PUN.,1,0,Anonymity and random ordering prevent identification.,1,0,No reward implemented.,1,1,Subjects are told the experiment consists of 30 rounds.,1,0,NPC has no punishment possibilities (Group A).,1,N/A,Punishment not available in NPC.,1,N/A,Punishment not available in NPC.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Stage 1 endowment is 20 tokens.,1,N/R,Text reports contributions mainly for PuC; NPC-specific normalized contributions in SHT are not numerically reported.,0,N/R,No explicit numeric average for NPC in SHT is given.,0,0.592,Section 4.4: surplus ratio in SHT (society over both institutions) is 59.2%; no NPC-only efficiency reported.,0.4,47.1,Section 4.4: average payoff of a society over all phases is 47.1 tokens in SHT (both institutions).,0.4,Germany,"Same lab and recruitment pool as PUN, at University of Erfurt.",0.9,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,"Mostly university students in economics, law, and social sciences",Footnote describes participants’ fields of study.,1,On site,Same lab setup as other treatments.,1,"Same institution-choice public goods game as PUN, but before the experiment subjects receive a detailed social history report of PUN, including per-period averages for PuC and NPC (group sizes, contributions, punishment tokens, and payoffs) in tables and figures.",Based on Section 3 and Appendix A.4 describing SHT.,1
