10.1016_j.jtbi.2019.110103.md,Traditional punishment and reward (ρ=1),"Paper contrasts a 'traditional punishment and reward' PGG, where punishment and reward are fully executed, against probabilistic execution cases. This corresponds to execution probability ρ=1.",0.93,"Execution regime: traditional (ρ=1, full execution); fine f and bonus r; cost h; synergy factor R","Traditional model differs from probabilistic model by execution probability (implicit ρ=1) and is compared across f=r and h,R in figures.",0.86,False,Study uses an agent-based model on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human experiment but a numerical simulation.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Described as 'numerical simulations' on a square lattice with agents.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solutions; only simulation-based results.,1,10000,Simulations conducted on a square lattice of size N=10000 (100x100).,1,10000,"System evolves 10,000 time steps after initialization.",1,1,Each cooperator contributes ai=1 and defectors ai=0; continuous PGG here effectively binary contribute-or-not.,0.7,N/A,"Agent-based model with no explicit endowment allocation; contributions defined directly, and there are no human subjects.",1,N/R,"Synergy factor R=2.6 and group size 5 are given, but payoff formula subtracts contribution; standard MPCR not directly defined.",0.3,N/A,No communication between (human) participants; purely simulated agents.,1,0,Agents have local information about neighbors’ strategies but no payoff or punishment/reward summary displays described.,0.7,0,Agents respond to local punishers’ presence/credibility but there is no identity concept analogous to human experiments.,0.7,0,Same reasoning as punishment identity; no explicit identity displays.,0.7,0,Time steps are part of simulation; no agents are 'shown' remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Punishing cooperators (PC) and defectors (PD) impose fine f at cost h.,1,0.5,"Costs of punishment and reward are equal, denoted h; in most simulations h=0.5.",0.9,1,"One unit of cost h is associated with one fine f; baseline figures use f=1 and h=0.5, so 1 unit of fine per unit of cost is a reasonable punishment technology representation.",0.6,1,Rewarding cooperators (RC) and defectors (RD) bestow bonus r at cost h.,1,0.5,Same h applies to reward; h=0.5 in main simulations.,0.9,1,"One unit of cost h provides one unit bonus r (baseline r=1, h=0.5); using 1 as normalized technology factor.",0.6,N/R,Endowment per agent per round is not defined; only a fixed contribution level (1) for cooperators.,0.3,0,"Under traditional punishment/reward with baseline parameters, system reaches global defection (cooperation rate 0).",0.95,0,"With all defectors (DN), total contribution per agent is zero.",0.95,N/R,Mean payoff (MP) is discussed qualitatively but not normalized against theoretical maximum for this specific traditional baseline.,0.3,N/R,Only mean payoff over population is mentioned; no raw group payoff values reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,No human,All results generated via computational simulations; no empirical environment.,1,"Spatial public goods game on 100x100 square lattice (degree 4, group size 5) with six strategies (CN, DN, PC, PD, RC, RD), full execution of punishment and reward (ρ=1), Fermi imitation dynamics with noise Φ=0.1, synergy factor R typically 2.6, fines and bonuses equal (f=r), and cost h shared for punishment and reward.",Summarizes the described game structure and dynamics for the traditional full-execution benchmark.,0.96
10.1016_j.jtbi.2019.110103.md,Probabilistic punishment and reward (ρ=0.2),Fig. 2 explicitly analyzes PGG with probabilistic execution at ρ=0.2 and compares it with other ρ values and traditional execution.,0.97,Execution probability ρ=0.2; fine f=1; bonus r=1; cost h=0.5; synergy factor R=2.6,"Parametrization for Fig. 2 given: Φ=0.1, R=2.6, f=r=1, h=0.5 and varying ρ including 0.2.",0.98,False,"Agent-based simulation, no human subjects.",1,False,"Numerical simulation, not an experiment with participants.",1,False,No physical lab; simulation only.,1,True,Described as numerical simulations on a lattice.,1,False,No analytical closed-form solution; results from simulations.,1,10000,Simulations on square lattice of size N=10000.,1,10000,"System evolves 10,000 time steps per initialization.",1,1,"Cooperators contribute ai=1, defectors 0; no continuous contribution choice.",0.7,N/A,No human endowments; contributions are model parameters.,1,N/R,"Synergy factor R given, but no marginal per capita return defined.",0.3,N/A,No communication among human participants; only local information among agents.,1,0,Agents only observe local strategies and credibility; no explicit summary displays.,0.7,0,No identity display; just neighbor-specific credibility scores in the simulation.,0.7,0,As above for punishment.,0.7,0,Time horizon not displayed to agents in a meaningful sense.,0.9,1,Punishing strategies PC and PD punish defectors when they truly implement.,1,0.5,Baseline simulations specify h=0.5 for cost.,0.95,1,Each punished neighbor imposes cost h on punisher per punished defector; we encode 1 unit fine per unit of cost as normalized technology.,0.6,1,Reward strategies RC and RD reward cooperators when truly implementing.,1,0.5,Same h applies to reward; h=0.5.,0.95,1,One unit of cost h produces one unit of reward r in normalization.,0.6,N/R,No per-round endowment variable is described.,0.3,0,"For ρ=0.2, evolution leads to 'global defection' per Fig. 2 description.",0.95,0,All defectors imply zero average contributions.,0.95,N/R,No normalized efficiency metric reported for this parameter set.,0.2,N/R,Mean payoff MP is not explicitly tabulated for this exact ρ; only qualitative statements.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based numerical simulation.,1,"Same spatial PGG structure as traditional case but with probabilistic execution: each punisher/rewarder implements with probability ρ=0.2, generating true (PCt, PDt, RCt, RDt) and false (PCf, PDf, RCf, RDf) implementers; decision-making includes trust-based rule using credibility scores c_ik and tolerance C_T.",Summarizes game mechanics specific to probabilistic model.,0.96
