10.1016_j.jtbi.2011.08.034.md,"Spatial lattice – high punishment & low cost vs low punishment & high cost (β1=0.35, γ1=0.2; β2=0.05, γ2=3.8)","Section 3.2, Fig. B.2f: class 1 has β1>β2 and γ1<γ2, i.e. efficient punishers; numerical values mirror Fig. B.2e but swapped.",0.9,"Population structure: spatial 50×50 lattice; Classes: M=2, f1=f2=0.5; Class 1: γ1=0.2, β1=0.35; Class 2: γ2=3.8, β2=0.05; Other parameters: σ=0.75, c=1.0, r=1.9, N=5, α=0.1.","Text: ‘When one group is strictly better at punishing (β1>β2 and γ1<γ2)…’ and they hold average β̄=0.2, γ̄=2.0, so symmetric swap of values from Fig. B.2e.",0.85,False,Simulation study.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,"Simulation, no lab.",1,True,Agent-based simulations visualized in Fig. B.2f.,1,False,Explored numerically rather than closed-form.,0.7,5,N=5 games.,1,500,500 rounds per run.,1,1,Altruists invest full c; others invest zero.,1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,0.38,"r=1.9, N=5.",1,N/A,No communication functionality.,1,0,No user interface.,1,N/A,No identity modeling.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,0,No human subjects.,1,1,"Altruistic punishers present with heterogeneous β, γ.",1,2,γ̄=(0.2+3.8)/2=2.0 as in other spatial conditions.,1,0.2,β̄=(0.35+0.05)/2=0.2.,1,0,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,c=1.0.,0.8,N/R,Only altruist proportions shown graphically.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,No human,Computational lattice model.,1,"Efficient punishers (class 1 with high β, low γ) almost exclusively carry out punishment across initial conditions and strongly promote altruistic behavior beyond other spatial cases.",Described in Section 3.2 for Fig. B.2f.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2020.3762.md,Theoretical model – specialized team assignment,Paper develops a formal model comparing specialized vs diverse team compositions; this row captures the specialized-team condition.,1,Team composition set to specialized teams (homogeneous types A-A and B-B); technology with productive substitutability f_s(·) and higher success probability at full effort than diverse teams (f_s(2) > f_d(2)).,Model explicitly defines specialized assignment as pairing same-type agents with production function f_s exhibiting productive substitutability and higher productivity at total effort 2.,1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical model with analytical results.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; it's a theoretical analysis.,1,False,No lab or in-person data collection; only a model.,1,False,They do not describe agent-based or numerical simulations as a method; only use numerical parameter examples inside an analytical model.,0.9,True,"Paper consists of formal analytical derivations, lemmas, and propositions for the model.",1,2,Each team (task) consists of two agents in the model; this condition is at the team level.,1,0,"Infinite-horizon repeated interaction with discount factor δ; no fixed finite number of rounds is specified, so coded as 0.",0.8,1,"Effort choice is binary e∈{0,1} so agents effectively choose all-or-nothing work vs shirk; but there is no continuous contribution budget like in public-good games, so coded as allowing any within this binary set.",0.7,N/A,"Model is about effort in team production, not contributions from an endowment.",1,N/R,No linear public good structure or MPCR is defined; production is via success probability functions f_s.,1,N/A,"No human experiment; communication protocols are not implemented as in a lab game. Theoretical blocking of communication to principal is an assumption, not an experimental chat feature.",1,N/A,No interface or feedback design for human subjects; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,There is no explicit punishment operator chosen by players; punishments are equilibrium play in the repeated game.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism among agents beyond wages from principal.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; infinite-horizon repeated model.,1,0,There is no separate costly punishment technology; punishments are implicit reversion to stage-game equilibria.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action or cost parameter is defined.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,"Aside from wages paid by the principal, players do not choose reward actions toward each other.",1,N/A,No peer reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,"Model uses cost of effort c and wages w_k, not an endowment of tokens to allocate.",1,N/R,"Outcomes of interest are wages, probabilities of success, and principal’s payoff; no contribution rate is defined.",1,N/R,"Agents choose effort (0/1), not a divisible contribution amount in a public-good framework.",1,N/R,They discuss payoff comparisons and optimality but do not report a normalized efficiency statistic.,1,N/R,"Principal and agent payoffs are given symbolically (V_s etc.), not as numeric experimental outcomes.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a theoretical model with analytical derivations only.,1,"Principal-agent model with four agents (two of type A, two of type B) assigned either to specialized teams (A,A and B,B) for two tasks or to diverse teams; each period, each agent chooses binary effort e∈{0,1} at cost ce. Task success yields S>0 with probability f_s(∑e_i) under specialized assignment, with productive substitutability (diminishing marginal returns) and f_s(2)>f_d(2). Agents observe each other’s efforts, the principal does not, and wages are short-term and contingent on success/failure. Repeated interaction and discount factor δ enable implicit incentives via grim-trigger strategies, mutual monitoring, and potential collusion (alternating work/shirk).",Summarized from Sections 2–3 where the specialized-team technology and assignment structure are defined.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2020.3762.md,Theoretical model – diverse team assignment,"Same analytical model but with agents assigned to heterogeneous (A,B) teams; this row captures the diverse-team condition.",1,Team composition set to diverse teams (heterogeneous types A-B on each task); technology with productive complementarity f_d(·) and lower success probability at full effort than specialized teams (f_d(2) < f_s(2)).,Model defines diverse assignment as pairing different-type agents with production function f_d exhibiting productive complementarity and typically lower f_d(2) than f_s(2).,1,False,No empirical data collection; purely theoretical.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; an analytical comparison of team types.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation.,1,False,No separate simulation study; only algebraic and numerical examples within theory.,0.9,True,"Uses closed-form inequalities, lemmas, and propositions to characterize diverse-team incentives.",1,2,"Each diverse team (task) consists of two agents (one A, one B).",1,0,Infinite-horizon repeated interactions modeled with discount factor δ; no finite R is specified.,0.8,1,"Each agent chooses binary effort e∈{0,1}; this is an all-or-nothing work decision in the model’s terms.",0.7,N/A,There is no starting endowment or default contribution; only effort decisions.,1,N/R,Team output arises from a probability-of-success function f_d; no linear public-good multiplier or MPCR is given.,1,N/A,"Theoretical blocking of communication to the principal is an assumption, not an implemented chat feature in an experiment.",1,N/A,No UI or feedback for human subjects.,1,N/A,No explicit costly punishment instrument exists; punishments are equilibrium reversion strategies.,1,N/A,No peer-to-peer reward choices.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; infinite-horizon discounting only.,1,0,No separate punishment technology; only implicit punishments via equilibrium strategies.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action with a per-unit cost.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mapping from cost to damage.,1,0,There is no agent-chosen reward technology; only wages from principal.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism specified.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,"Model has effort cost c and wages, not agent endowments.",1,N/R,"Theoretical focus is on equilibrium effort, wages, and payoffs, not contribution rates as in a public-good game.",1,N/R,Effort is binary and not reported as a continuous contribution amount.,1,N/R,They compare principal’s expected payoffs V_d vs V_s symbolically; no normalized 0–1 efficiency metric is reported.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are given in symbolic form (e.g., V_d=2 f_d(2)S − 4E[w_d*]); no numeric outcome reported as in data.",1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Entire study is a theoretical agent-principal model without human-subject implementation.,1,"Same principal-agent setting as specialized condition: four agents (two A, two B) are assigned to two tasks, but each task is performed by a diverse pair (A,B). Each agent chooses e∈{0,1} at cost ce. Task success S>0 occurs with probability f_d(e_i,e_j), increasing in both efforts and exhibiting productive complementarity: marginal productivity is higher when the teammate works, and f_d satisfies inequalities such as f_d(2)−f_d(1) > f_d(1)−f_d(0). Agents perfectly observe each other’s efforts, the principal cannot observe efforts but sees outcomes and pays wages w_d on success and v_d on failure. Repeated interaction with discount factor δ allows grim-trigger strategies where deviation triggers the (shirk, shirk) stage-game equilibrium as punishment. Collusion (alternating shirk/work) is shown not to arise as a binding constraint for diverse teams; the mutual-monitoring wage is already collusion-proof.","Summarized from Sections 2–3 (assumptions, mutual monitoring, collusion) specifically for the diverse-team technology f_d and incentive properties.",1
