10.1016_j.ejpoleco.2012.03.001.md,Analytical model of norm compliance with punishment and reputation,The paper presents a single formal theoretical model; no separate empirical conditions or simulations are described.,1,Probability of punishment p; benefit-cost ratio of punishment b = B/T; fraction of compliers μ; individual social concern type g; compliance cost D; punishment cost C; punishment cost to punisher T; benefit from others' compliance B; adjustment rate α; upper bound of type distribution G.,"These are the parameters explicitly varied or analyzed in comparative statics (e.g., higher p, lower b, different μ).",1,False,The study is entirely theoretical with no human participants or data collection.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; it is a formal model.,1,False,There is no laboratory or field experiment; the work is theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; only analytical derivations.,1,True,The paper develops and analyzes a system of equations and equilibrium conditions without numerical simulation or empirical data.,1,2,The model is based on pairwise random matching between one agent from group i and one from group j.,1,0,The model uses continuous time and an infinite-horizon adjustment process rather than discrete experimental rounds; encoded as 0 because a finite round count is not defined.,0.6,0,"Agents of group i choose x∈{0,1} (comply or not); this is a binary contribution/choice setting, not free continuous contributions.",1,N/A,There is no public goods endowment or initial allocation; it is not a contribution game with monetary endowments.,1,N/R,"The game is not framed as a linear public goods game, and no marginal per capita return is defined.",1,N/A,No human-subject interaction protocol exists; communication is not modeled as a decision variable.,1,N/A,There is no experimental interface or feedback structure with human participants.,1,N/A,Identities are not modeled as observable experimental features; it is an abstract matching model without such implementation details.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism and no experimental identification are specified.,1,N/A,No discrete-round experimental interface is described.,1,1,"Group j agents can punish violators with probability p, imposing cost C on group i and incurring cost T themselves.",1,N/R,The model specifies C as a fixed penalty cost to the violator but does not give it a numeric value; our schema requires a numeric per-unit cost which cannot be instantiated from symbols alone.,0.5,N/R,The mapping from a unit of punisher effort to violator loss is not parameterized as a multiplier; punishment is modeled only as a fixed cost C when punishment occurs with probability p.,0.5,0,No explicit material or costly reward mechanism is modeled; only reputation benefits and punishment are considered.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"The model does not define monetary endowments or tokens; utilities are specified directly via parameters D, C, B, T.",1,N/R,"Theoretical analysis focuses on probability of compliance Pr(x=1) and fraction μ, but no normalized contribution rate relative to an endowment is defined in experimental terms.",0.7,N/R,There are no token contributions; decisions are binary compliance choices without amounts.,1,N/R,The paper does not compute payoffs relative to a maximum feasible group payoff in the sense required by the schema.,1,N/R,The model provides individual utility functions but not explicit numeric group payoff aggregates.,1,N/A,There are no human participants; the model is purely theoretical.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,No human,"The study is an analytical model without human subjects, field data, or lab implementation.",1,"Analytical model of a norm-compliance game in a continuum population split into two equally sized groups (i and j). Group i agents choose binary norm compliance x∈{0,1}, with utility U = x(gμ − D) − (1 − x)pC, where g is social concern type uniformly distributed on [0,G], μ is fraction of compliers in group i, D is compliance cost, and C is penalty cost when punished. Group j agents choose punishment probability p∈[0,1], with utility V = Pr(x=1)B − {1 − Pr(x=1)}pT, where B is psychological benefit from facing a complier and T is psychological cost of punishing; b = B/T is the benefit-cost ratio. Short-run compliance probability in group i is Pr(x=1) = 1 − (D − pC)/(μG). Optimal punishment probability in group j is p = min{(D − bC)/(2C), 1}. The long-run dynamics of μ follow dμ/dt = α[Pr(x=1) − 1/2], yielding critical μ* = min{2(D − pC)/G, 1/2}. Two scenarios are analyzed: (a) high punishment (higher p, lower b) sustaining norms even for small μ, and (b) strong reputation effects (higher μ) sustaining norms despite low p.","Summarizes the structure, key equations, and the two main scenarios (punishment-based and reputation-based norm compliance) described in Sections 2 and 3.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2016.09.015.md,Complete network – standard punishment public goods game,One of three network treatments; groups of 4 linked in a complete monitoring/punishment network for 10-period matches.,1,Network structure = Complete (each player monitors and can punish all 3 others),Treatment variable is the underlying monitoring and punishment network; this condition uses the Complete network in Fig. 1.,1,True,Human subjects recruited from FSU participated in laboratory sessions.,1,True,Network structure is exogenously assigned and varied in a controlled lab setting.,1,True,Conducted in the XS/FS laboratory at FSU.,1,False,"All results are from human-subject experiments, not computer simulations.",1,False,No formal analytical model is used as a main method for this treatment.,1,4,Subjects randomly assigned into groups of 4 for each match.,1,10,Each match consisted of 10 periods of the repeated two-stage game.,1,1,Standard linear VCM with 10-token endowment; subjects can choose any integer (and typical VCM allows 0–10) rather than just all-or-nothing.,0.8,0,"Each period, players receive an endowment of 10 tokens they allocate between individual and group exchanges; all starts in private account.",1,0.4,Each token invested in the group exchange yields 0.4 tokens to every player in the group.,1,0,No mention of communication; only information is via on-screen feedback on contributions and punishment.,0.95,1,"After Stage 2, players observed the number of deduction points they assigned and received from each neighbor, and after Stage 1 they observed total group contribution.",0.9,1,"Players observed the number of deduction points they received from each of their neighbors, implying identity of each punisher among neighbors.",1,0,No rewards are available in the design.,1,1,Instructions and interface describe a 10-period match; standard z-Tree implementation reveals current period and total number of periods.,0.8,1,Second stage allows players to assign costly deduction