10.1016_j.jebo.2016.09.015.md,Asymmetric network – Player A position,"Main treatment is the Asymmetric network; behavior is heterogeneous by position A, B, C, D. This entry is for player location A.",1,Network structure = Asymmetric; player location = A (degree-2 node sharing monitoring of B with C and monitored by C and B),Asymmetric network positions A–D differ in exposure and responsibility; this row isolates player A.,0.9,True,Same laboratory human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of network and fixed positions over 10 periods.,1,True,Conducted in XS/FS lab at FSU.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations for this treatment.,1,False,Empirical design only.,1,4,Groups of 4 players.,1,10,Each Asymmetric-network match has 10 periods.,1,1,Same linear VCM allocation decision for all positions.,0.8,0,Same 10-token endowment fully under player control.,1,0.4,Public good technology is common across treatments.,1,0,No communication stage.,0.95,1,"Players see neighbors’ contributions and punishment flows with themselves, plus total group contribution.",0.9,1,They observe deduction points received from each neighbor separately.,1,0,No rewards.,1,1,10-period design is common knowledge and typically visible in the UI.,0.8,1,Stage-2 punishment with deduction points is present.,1,1,Each deduction point costs 1 token.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces target’s earnings by 3 tokens.,1,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,10,Same 10-token endowment for all players each period.,1,N/R,Paper reports overall Asymmetric-network average (7.1586/10) and highlights that only player D is significantly lower; average for player A not numerically reported.,0.2,N/R,No table of average contributions by location in Asymmetric network; only qualitative description and regression results.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency by position within Asymmetric network is only shown graphically for earnings; no numeric normalized efficiency.,0.2,N/R,No numeric earnings by position reported; only relative patterns.,0.2,United States,Same subject pool as other treatments.,1,"Undergraduate students, typical range approx. 18–24; exact ages not reported",Same undergraduate pool with no additional age breakdown.,0.6,N/R,Gender composition not given.,0,Undergraduate students,All subjects from FSU undergraduate pool.,1,On site,Same laboratory implementation.,1,"Asymmetric undirected monitoring/punishment network with positions A–D fixed within each 10-period match. In this network, some players have more neighbors than others: notably, one player is under-monitored (D) and one player (C) has greater punishment responsibility. Player A has two neighbors and shares responsibility for monitoring/punishing some targets (e.g., A and B can both punish each other and C). Game structure (two stages, payoffs, punishment budget of 6 tokens, max 2 deduction points per neighbor) is identical to other treatments.","Summarizes Asymmetric topology as it pertains to player A, based on narrative surrounding Fig. 1 and discussion of positions.",0.8
