ints to each using a 6-token Stage-2 budget. C is solely responsible for punishing D but shares responsibility for sanctioning A and B with other players. This creates potential incentives for discriminatory punishment (hypothesized but not supported) and exposure to retaliation from under-monitored D.","Summarizes C’s central role, punishment responsibilities, and the experimental hypotheses about discriminatory punishment and retaliation.",0.95
10.1016_j.jebo.2016.09.015.md,Asymmetric network – Player D (under-monitored) position,Asymmetric network’s key under-monitored player who faces only one potential punisher (C) and is central to main results.,1,"Network structure = Asymmetric; player location = D (under-monitored node with only one neighbor/potential punisher C, but can punish C)","Text states player D faces only one potential punisher (C) and is under-monitored, with asymmetric exposure to punishment.",1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to D position within controlled Asymmetric network.,1,True,Run in XS/FS laboratory at FSU.,1,False,No simulation-only analysis.,1,False,No purely analytical model for this condition.,1,4,Groups of 4 players.,1,10,10-period matches.,1,1,Player D has same VCM choice set as others.,0.8,0,10 tokens all under D's initial control each period.,1,0.4,Same group exchange technology.,1,0,No communication option.,0.95,1,"D sees neighbor C’s contribution, and through total group contribution can infer others; sees punishment flows involving D.",0.9,1,D observes deduction points received from neighbor C specifically.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same 10-period structure.,0.8,1,Stage-2 punishment applies and D can punish C.,1,1,Cost of 1 token per deduction point.,1,3,Each deduction point costs target 3 tokens.,1,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,10,D receives the same 10-token endowment each period.,1,N/R,"Results state D contributes significantly less than others (Result 2 and Table 3), but no numeric mean is reported.",0.2,N/R,"No table with D’s average contribution amount; only regression effects (e.g., Asymmetric × l_D).",0.2,N/R,Position-specific efficiency for D is only described qualitatively (higher average earnings in Fig. 8).,0.2,N/R,Exact average payoff for D is not printed.,0.2,United States,Same FSU pool.,1,"Undergraduate students, typical range approx. 18–24; exact ages not reported",Same population.,0.6,N/R,No gender data.,0,Undergraduate students,FSU undergraduate pool.,1,On site,Same lab experiment.,1,"Player D has only one neighbor C in the Asymmetric undirected network, so D faces only one potential punisher but can also punish C. Under-monitoring allows D to contribute less and exploit position through anti-social punishment and retaliation. Data show D is significantly less likely than others to increase contributions after being punished and uses targeted revenge (higher incidence of counter-punishment against C when prior punishment and contribution conditions are met).","Summarizes D's distinct monitoring exposure and documented behavioral patterns (lower contributions, retaliation) from Results 2, 4, and 5.",1
10.1037_a0033495.md,"Study 4 – Public good game (contributors, punishment of non-contributors)",Study 4 describes a lab public good game; punishment behavior is analyzed separately for contributors vs non-contributors. This row covers contributors in the public good condition.,1,"Good type in within-subjects design; this row: public good game, contributor subgroup (participants who contributed their 10 tokens to group account).",Within-subject factor is public vs club good. Authors analyze punishment conditional on whether the participant contributed in the public good game; this is effectively a condition.,0.86,True,Undergraduates made real token decisions in lab games.,1,True,"Rules, payoffs, and order of games were experimentally controlled and partially randomized.",1,True,Conducted in university lab sessions with 7–10 participants at private stations.,1,False,No agent-based or computational simulation; all decisions by humans.,1,False,No formal mathematical derivations as the primary method.,1,4,Each game was played in anonymous four-person groups.,1,1,Each game had a single contribution round followed by a single punishment round; only one contribution round fits the public good stage definition.,0.9,0,Participants chose between contributing all 10 tokens or keeping all 10; no intermediate amounts.,1,0,All 10 tokens were initially in private possession; contribution required active transfer to group account.,1,0.7,Each 10-token contribution became 28 tokens split among 4 (7 each). MPCR = 7/10 = 0.7.,1,0,"Participants could not communicate with each other, only with the experimenter for rule clarification.",1,0,Participants only learned others’ choices after the entire session; no real-time summaries or earnings tables described.,0.7,0,Punishers and targets were anonymous; participants did not learn target identity.,1,0,No reward mechanism existed in the game.,1,1,Instructions clearly stated there was a single contribution round and a single punishment round; thus horizon was known.,0.9,1,Second stage allowed spending punishment tokens to reduce a target’s payoff.,1,1,Every 1 token spent on punishment subtracted 2 tokens from the target.,1,2,One punishment token reduced the target’s payoff by 2 tokens.,1,0,No mechanism for increasing others’ payoffs via costly reward was described.,1,N/A,No reward stage/mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward stage/mechanism.,1,10,"In contribution round, participants received 10 tokens.",1,1,This condition is defined as contributors in the public good game; they contributed all 10 tokens (100%).,1,10,Contributors transferred their full 10-token endowment.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to maximum efficiency is reported.,0,N/R,Average or total group tokens by condition not reported.,0,United States,"Participants were undergraduates at the University of California, Santa Barbara (U.S. institution).",0.98,N/R,Age statistics for Study 4 sample not reported.,0,"49 women, 16 men",Study 4 reports 65 undergraduates (49 women); remaining 16 inferred male/other.,0.9,Undergraduate students,Described as undergraduates participating for course credit and/or money.,1,On site,"Participants attended sessions with 7–10 people and were orally instructed by an experimenter, indicating in-person lab.",1,"One-shot public good with binary contribute-or-keep choice; 4-person groups; 10-token endowment; contribution multiplied to 28 and equally divided; punishment via strategy method where each participant, assuming the target did not contribute, chose 0–5 punishment tokens, each costing 1 and reducing target’s payoff by 2.",Summary of Study 4 public good game rules and punishment implementation.,1
10.1037_a0033495.md,"Study 4 – Club good game (contributors, punishment of non-contributors)",Study 4 describes a lab club good game structurally parallel to the public good game; punishment analyzed for contributors vs non-contributors. This row covers contributors in the club good condition.,1,"Good type in within-subjects design; this row: club good game, contributor subgroup (participants who contributed their 10 tokens to group account).","Within-subject factor is public vs club good. Authors analyze punishment conditional on contribution decisions in the public game, but punishment decisions were elicited for both games; here we isolate club good, contributors.",0.78,True,Same human-subject lab protocol as public good game.,1,True,Rules and order of public vs club games experimentally controlled.,1,True,Conducted in person with experimenter instructions and anonymous groups.,1,False,No computer-only simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical model as primary method.,1,4,Each club good game used anonymous four-person groups.,1,1,Single contribution round plus one punishment round for the club good game.,0.9,0,"As in public good game, participants chose to contribute all 10 tokens or none.",1,0,All 10 tokens initially private; contribution was an active transfer.,1,0.7,"Ten tokens contributed yielded 7 tokens back to each contributor per contributor: payoff per contributor is 7n for n contributors, so marginal per capita return relative to own contribution is 7/10 = 0.7.",0.9,0,No communication allowed among participants.,1,0,"As in public good game, no running summaries or feedback per round; only final outcome revealed.",0.7,0,Punishers and targets remained anonymous.,1,0,No explicit rewarding stage/mechanism.,1,1,Instructions specified a single contribution and a single punishment round.,0.9,1,Identical punishment stage followed club good contribution round.,1,1,One punishment token cost to the punisher per 2-token reduction for the target.,1,2,Each punishment token deducted 2 tokens from target.,1,0,No costly reward option described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Participants again received 10 tokens in the club good contribution round.,1,N/R,"Authors state that all but two participants contributed in club good game, but this is across whole sample, not separately for this contributor-defined subgroup; normalized rate for this row is intrinsically 1 but that is by definition and may be misleading, so left N/R.",0.4,10,"Within this contributor subgroup, they chose the contribute option, which transfers all 10 tokens.",0.9,N/R,No measure of realized or relative efficiency is provided.,0,N/R,No aggregate token outcomes by condition reported.,0,United States,Same undergraduate sample at UCSB as in public good condition.,0.98,N/R,No age stats for Study 4 sample reported.,0,"49 women, 16 men",Same 65-undergraduate sample as public good game.,0.9,Undergraduate students,Described as undergraduates.,1,On site,Same lab sessions as public good game.,1,One-shot club good with binary contribute-or-keep choice in 4-person groups; 10-token endowment; only contributors received returns from the group account and each received 7 tokens per contributor (7n) while non-contributors always kept their initial 10; identical strategy-method punishment stage where each participant chose how many of 5 tokens to spend to reduce an assigned non-contributor’s payoff by 2 per token.,Summary of Study 4 club good game and punishment implementation.,1
10.1007_978-1-4419-6896-8_17.md,Masclet et al. 2003 – Monetary punishment (formal sanctions),Paper explicitly describes Masclet et al. (2003) public goods experiment with a monetary punishment / formal sanctions condition.,1,Sanction type available: monetary punishment (formal sanctions),Condition differs from others in Masclet et al. (2003) by allowing costly monetary punishment only.,1,True,Described as a laboratory public goods experiment with participants.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with manipulated sanctioning institutions across conditions.,1,True,Referred to as an experiment in economics with standard public goods lab design; not field or online in the description.,0.8,False,Human participants are involved; no indication of computer-only agents.,1,False,Not a formal model; it is an experiment.,1,0,Group size for the public goods game in Masclet et al. (2003) is not reported in this review.,0,0,Number of rounds not specified in the review text.,0,1,"Described as a standard voluntary contributions public goods game; these allow continuous contribution amounts, not all-or-nothing, though exact wording not provided.",0.6,0,"In standard VCM, endowment starts in private account and players decide how much to contribute; review does not mention any pre-assigned contributions.",0.7,N/R,Multiplier or MPCR is not reported in the review.,0,0,No mention of communication; standard VCM punishment designs typically prohibit chat.,0.6,1,Participants must see others' contributions/earnings to target punishment; implies some feedback on others' behavior.,0.8,0,Review does not say identities are revealed; standard monetary punishment VCMs usually keep identities anonymous while showing contribution and punishment amounts.,0.6,N/A,No rewarding mechanism in this condition.,1,0,Whether total or remaining rounds were displayed is not mentioned.,0,1,Defined as the monetary punishment/formal sanctions condition where participants can reduce others' earnings at a cost.,1,N/R,Cost per unit of punishment is not given in the review.,0,N/R,How much the target loses per unit spent is not specified.,0,0,Only punishment (reducing others' earnings) is mentioned; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,0,Exact endowment size per round is not reported.,0,N/R,Review only states that formal sanctions increase cooperation and compares earnings; no numerical rates reported.,0,N/R,No specific token amounts reported.,0,N/R,"Review mentions that overall earnings were similar across social vs formal sanctions, but does not give normalized efficiency values.",0.2,N/R,Only relative statement (similar earnings) is provided; no numeric payoffs.,0,N/R,Recruitment location/nationality not mentioned.,0,N/R,Age information not provided.,0,N/R,Gender composition not mentioned.,0,N/R,Not described; likely students but not stated.,0,On site,Described as a lab public goods game in economics; typical design is in-person lab; no indication of field or online implementation.,0.7,Voluntary contributions public goods game with a stage allowing costly monetary punishment that reduces others’ earnings; compared to social-sanction and no-punishment conditions to study cooperation and earnings.,Summarized from review’s description of Masclet et al. (2003).,0.9
10.1007_978-1-4419-6896-8_17.md,Masclet et al. 2003 – Nonmonetary punishment (social sanctions),Paper describes a nonmonetary punishment (social sanctions) condition in Masclet et al. (2003).,1,Sanction type available: nonmonetary punishment via costless disapproval points (social sanctions),This condition differs by allowing social sanctions only.,1,True,Laboratory public goods experiment with human participants.,1,True,Sanctioning opportunities are experimentally manipulated between conditions.,1,True,Standard economics lab public goods design; no indication of field setting.,0.8,False,Human participants are used.,1,False,Not a mathematical analysis.,1,0,Group size not reported here.,0,0,Number of periods not specified.,0,1,Standard VCM public goods game assumed; such games allow continuous contributions.,0.6,0,In VCMs endowment starts in private account; no evidence of pre-funded public account.,0.7,N/R,Not mentioned in review.,0,0,"No mention of free-form communication; social sanctions are structured as disapproval points, not chat.",0.7,1,Subjects must see others’ contributions to send disapproval.,0.8,0,"The review does not state that identities are revealed; social sanctions are via points, likely anonymous.",0.5,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No information on whether total/remaining rounds are displayed.,0,1,Nonmonetary social punishment via disapproval points is explicitly available.,1,0,Disapproval points are described as costless to both sender and receiver.,1,0,"Points are nonmonetary, so they do not change earnings directly.",1,0,"Only disapproval, not approval/rewards, is mentioned.",0.9,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Endowment per subject per round not reported.,0,N/R,"Review only states that social sanctions significantly increase cooperation relative to no punishment, but provides no numeric contribution shares.",0,N/R,No specific token amounts.,0,N/R,"Overall earnings are said to be similar to formal sanctions condition, but not quantified.",0.2,N/R,Only relative comparison (similar earnings) is given.,0,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not reported in review.,0,N/R,No information.,0,N/R,Not specified; likely students but not stated.,0,On site,Described as a lab-type public goods experiment; no signs of field/online.,0.7,"Voluntary contributions public goods game with an additional stage where participants can allocate non-costly disapproval points to others; these points do not alter payoffs but act as social sanctions. Cooperation is higher than in the no-punishment control, and earnings similar to the monetary-punishment condition.",Summarized from the review’s description of the nonmonetary punishment condition.,0.9
