10.1007_978-1-4419-6896-8_17.md,Noussair and Tucker 2005 – Social sanctions only,Review describes a condition with only social sanctions (nonmonetary) in Noussair and Tucker (2005).,0.9,Sanction type available: social sanctions only (nonmonetary disapproval/approval),"Independent variable is sanction type; here, only social sanctions are available.",0.9,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Sanctioning possibilities manipulated across conditions.,1,True,Standard laboratory public goods framework.,0.8,False,Involves human participants.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,0,Not reported.,0,0,Not specified.,0,1,Standard VCM with variable contributions assumed.,0.6,0,Typical for VCM: entire endowment starts privately held.,0.7,N/R,Not mentioned.,0,0,"No mention of generalized communication; sanctions are structured signals, not free chat.",0.7,1,Participants must see others’ behavior to direct social sanctions.,0.8,0,No details on identity revelation; standard assumption is anonymous feedback.,0.5,0,"Social sanctions can in principle be approval or disapproval, but the review emphasizes only sanctioning of free-riding, not identity revelation of sender or target.",0.4,0,Horizon information not discussed.,0,1,Social sanctions are used to punish free riders in this treatment.,1,0,Social sanctions described as nonmonetary; cost to earnings is zero though there may be psychological costs.,0.8,0,Sanctions affect utility but not payoffs directly.,0.8,0,Focus is on sanctions for free-riders; no explicit reward mechanism is described.,0.7,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Endowment amount not specified.,0,N/R,Review only notes that combined system is more effective at increasing contributions than social-only; no absolute rate.,0,N/R,No amounts reported.,0,N/R,Review states payoffs are lower than in combined system but does not quantify.,0.2,N/R,"Only relative statements about payoffs, no numeric values.",0,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,On site,Lab-style public goods game.,0.7,"Public goods game with only social sanctions (e.g., expressions of approval/disapproval) available. Contributions and payoffs are lower than in the combined sanctions treatment but may differ from the formal-only case.",Summarized from Noussair and Tucker (2005) description in the review.,0.8
10.1007_978-1-4419-6896-8_17.md,Noussair and Tucker 2005 – Combined monetary and social sanctions,Review explicitly notes a combined system where both formal and social sanctions can be used.,1,Sanction type available: both formal monetary and social sanctions,This treatment combines both sanction options.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Sanction regime experimentally manipulated.,1,True,Standard economic lab public goods study.,0.8,False,Involves human participants.,1,False,Not theoretical modeling.,1,0,Not given.,0,0,Not provided.,0,1,Assumed standard VCM with graduated contributions.,0.6,0,No indication of default contributions; usual is full endowment privately held.,0.7,N/R,Not reported.,0,0,Sanctions are structured; no mention of free-form communication.,0.7,1,"To impose sanctions, subjects must know others’ contributions or earnings.",0.8,0,Identity revelation not mentioned; typical designs keep it anonymous.,0.5,0,Approval/disapproval signals may be anonymous; no explicit identity feedback reported.,0.4,0,No information provided.,0,1,Formal monetary sanctions are available in this combined condition.,1,N/R,Monetary cost per unit not given.,0,N/R,Impact on target's earnings per punishment unit not stated.,0,1,"Social sanctions can possibly include expressions of approval (a form of reward), and combined system allows both types; however, the review focuses on sanctions, so this is inferred with low confidence.",0.3,N/R,Any cost of positive social signals is unspecified.,0,N/R,No information on how social approval affects payoffs.,0,0,Endowment amount not given.,0,N/R,Review states that contributions are higher than in single-sanction systems but provides no figures.,0,N/R,No token-level data.,0,N/R,"Review says combined system is cheaper than formal-only and more effective than social-only, leading to higher payoffs, but does not quantify efficiency.",0.3,N/R,Only relative description (payoffs higher in combined than in either single type) is given.,0,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not specified.,0,On site,Economic laboratory study.,0.7,"Public goods game where participants have access to both monetary punishment and nonmonetary social sanctions. Contributions and group payoffs are higher than in treatments with only one type of sanction, and the system is cheaper than using formal sanctions alone.",Summarized from the review’s description that combined formal and social sanctions yield higher contributions and payoffs.,0.9
10.1016_j.paid.2010.05.013.md,"Simulation – weak dilemma (r = 0.05), parameter sweep over q and α","Main Monte Carlo simulation on L=100 lattice for weak dilemma strength r=0.05 with varying information level q and cost α, reported in Fig. 1–3 and related text.",0.92,"Dilemma strength r = 0.05 (weak); information level q ∈ [0,1]; cost of reward/punishment to super cooperators α ∈ [0,1]; strategies available: C, SC, SD.","Text: “we mainly focused on the representative scenarios of weak (r=0.05) …”; Fig. 1 shows α–q phase diagram; α and q are systematically varied; strategies are C, SC, SD.",0.96,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on a lattice, not human participants.",1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational evolutionary game model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only simulations.,1,True,Explicitly described as Monte Carlo simulations on a 2D lattice.,1,False,No closed-form mathematical analysis; results are numerical phase diagrams and time series.,0.96,10000,"Population on 2D lattice with L=100; player count = L×L = 10,000.",0.99,30000,They “performed an iteration of 3×10^4 Monte Carlo steps (MCS)” as the main run length.,0.98,1,"Not a contribution game; actions are discrete strategies C, SC, SD rather than continuous contributions. Players choose one of three pure strategies, so ‘can contribute any amount’ is not applicable; coded as 1 as they are not restricted to all-or-nothing contribution of a continuous endowment.",0.4,N/A,Not a public goods contribution game and no human endowments; only pairwise PD payoffs in simulations.,1,N/R,"MPCR pertains to public goods games; this is a pairwise prisoner’s dilemma with specified payoff matrix, no MPCR defined.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication; agent strategies update via Fermi imitation.,1,N/A,No user interface; only simulated payoffs and strategies.,1,N/A,No identities; anonymous lattice agents in simulation.,1,N/A,As above; no human identity or display.,1,N/A,No humans or interface; number of Monte Carlo steps is not shown to agents.,1,1,Super cooperators punish defectors per payoff matrix and description.,1,0,"They state: “For simplicity, we assumed that the cost for egotistic punishers equals 0, and that the cost-to-fine (reward) ratio equals 1.”",0.98,1,"Cost-to-fine ratio = 1 and cost = 0 conceptually, but SD loses amount α when punished while SC gains α; per interaction magnitude of imposed loss is α, which equals cost-to-fine ratio 1. To map to tech parameter (fine per cost unit), we use 1 as normalized effect.",0.4,1,Super cooperators reward cooperators; payoff matrix includes +α rewards.,1,0,Same egotistic mechanism as punishment; reward is costless to SC per text.,0.98,1,"Reward magnitude equals α per rewarded cooperator, with effectively zero cost; as with punishment, encode normalized magnitude per nominal cost unit as 1.",0.4,N/A,No notion of an endowment or contribution budget; agents receive payoffs from pairwise PD encounters only.,1,N/A,"Outcome variables are fractions of strategies and average social payoff, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions; only strategy selection and resulting payoffs.,1,N/R,They report “average social payoff” but do not normalize by the theoretical maximum payoff in 0–1 terms required for efficiency.,0.5,N/R,"They present average social payoff in figures but do not give explicit numeric values in the text for specific α, q pairs.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; only agents in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"All results are from computer simulations on a lattice; no lab, field, or online subjects.",1,"Two-player extended prisoner’s dilemma with three strategies (C, SC, SD) played on an L×L=100×100 regular lattice with 8 neighbors (Moore neighborhood) and periodic boundary conditions. Payoff matrix parameterized by dilemma strength r, cost/reward parameter α, and information level q. Strategy updating via pairwise Fermi imitation with κ=0.1; 3×10^4 Monte Carlo steps per run, last 2000 used for averages, results averaged over 50 independent runs. Weak dilemma condition fixes r=0.05.",Summarizes explicit method details and parameter values in the Methods and Results sections relevant for this simulation condition.,0.99
10.1016_j.paid.2010.05.013.md,"Simulation – strong dilemma (r = 0.2), parameter sweep over q and α","Second main Monte Carlo simulation set on L=100 lattice for strong dilemma strength r=0.2 with varying information level q and cost α, reported in Fig. 4–7 and related text.",0.92,"Dilemma strength r = 0.2 (strong); information level q ∈ [0,1]; cost of reward/punishment to super cooperators α ∈ [0,1]; strategies available: C, SC, SD.",Text: “we mainly focused on the representative scenarios of weak (r=0.05) and strong (r=0.2) dilemma strengths”; Fig. 4 and 5 discuss the strong dilemma case with α–q phase diagrams; α and q are varied.,0.96,False,"Same computational model, no human subjects.",1,False,Numerical simulations rather than controlled lab/field experiments.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,"Monte Carlo simulations on lattice, identical framework as weak dilemma case.",1,False,No purely analytical treatment provided for this condition.,0.96,10000,Same L=100 lattice used throughout simulations unless otherwise noted.,0.99,30000,Same Monte Carlo length (3×10^4 MCS) described in Methods applies here.,0.98,1,"Same game structure as weak dilemma case: discrete strategies C, SC, SD, not continuous contributions. Use same coding.",0.4,N/A,No endowment-based contribution game or human subjects.,1,N/R,"Pairwise PD matrix, not a public goods game with MPCR.",1,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No interface; only simulation outputs.,1,N/A,No identities in simulation.,1,N/A,No identities in simulation.,1,N/A,Agents are not informed about simulation length.,1,1,Same SC punishment mechanism is used; only r differs.,1,0,"Punishment is egotistic with zero cost to punishers, as defined earlier, unchanged by r.",0.98,1,Same normalized magnitude per cost unit assumption as in weak dilemma condition.,0.4,1,SC reward mechanism is unchanged; they reward cooperators.,1,0,"Reward is also egotistic with zero cost, same as punishment.",0.98,1,Same reward magnitude normalization as weak dilemma case.,0.4,N/A,No endowment notion in this simulation.,1,N/A,No contributions; PD strategies only.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,"Only average social payoffs reported, not normalized efficiency values.",0.5,N/R,Average social payoff diagrams do not provide explicit numeric group payoff values for specific parameter points in text.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"As with weak dilemma case, this is a computer simulation.",1,"Same extended prisoner’s dilemma on 2D L×L=100×100 lattice with 8 neighbors and periodic boundaries as in the weak dilemma condition. Dilemma strength set to r=0.2 representing a strong social dilemma. Strategy updating uses pairwise Fermi imitation with κ=0.1 over 3×10^4 Monte Carlo steps, averaging last 2000 steps, with averages taken over 50 runs. Phase diagrams and time-evolution snapshots explore how information level q and cost parameter α affect coexistence of C, SC, and SD and average social payoff under strong dilemma.",Summary of methods and the specific change (r=0.2) relative to the weak dilemma condition from the Methods and strong-dilemma Results sections.,0.99
10.1016_j.jecp.2022.105376.md,Study 1 – Children – Neutral emotion condition (UG second-party punishment),Study 1 manipulates emotion (anger vs neutral) between subjects and includes three age groups; this row is for 8-year-old children in the neutral condition.,1,Emotion induction condition: Neutral autobiographical recall (dinner description); Age group: 8-year-olds; Role: Person B (UG responder).,Independent variables explicitly described as emotion condition (anger vs neutral) and age group; all were responders B in ultimatum game.,1,True,"Human participants (children, adolescents, adults) engaged in economic games.",1,True,Random assignment to emotion conditions with controlled tasks.,1,True,"Adults in university lab; minors in school rooms but still controlled laboratory-style setting, so classified as on-site lab experiment.",0.9,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No formal mathematical modeling as primary method.,1,2,Ultimatum game between Person A (proposer) and Person B (responder).,1,1,One UG outcome was randomly selected for payoff; strategically a one-shot interaction.,1,1,"Responder chooses accept or reject for each offer; no contribution continuum as in PGG, but here binary choice; coded as can effectively choose either full proposed amount or zero; mapped to 1 as not an all-or-nothing contribution structure in our cooperation sense.",0.4,0,"Not a contribution game; endowment is initially with proposer, responder has 0. In our schema this corresponds to 0 proportion in public fund.",0.5,N/R,Ultimatum game does not involve a linear public good with MPCR.,1,0,No communication between participants mentioned.,1,0,"Responders only see current allocation, no summaries of prior behavior or earnings.",1,0,Interactions are anonymous; identities not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism and roles are anonymous.,1,1,"Instructions state there are seven distributions and that one will be randomly selected, so participants know total number of decisions.",0.9,1,Rejection of a positive offer is interpreted as costly punishment (both players get 0).,1,1,"If responder rejects, they forgo the offered points. Marginal cost per unit of reduced proposer payoff is effectively 1: rejecting an offer of x vs 0 means losing x to reduce proposer’s payoff by 10−x (ratio varies), but standard UG punishment cost is treated as 1 unit of own payoff for 1 unit not accepted; approximated as 1.",0.3,1,"By rejecting, responder reduces proposer’s payoff from (10−x) to 0 while also reducing own payoff; mapping to a simple 1:1 for schema though exact leverage varies with x.",0.3,0,"There is no costly rewarding option, only acceptance or rejection.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Proposer allocates 10 points between A and B.,1,N/R,"No contribution to a public good; outcomes are acceptance/rejection rates, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,"Paper reports rejection frequencies by offer, not mean accepted amounts by condition.",1,N/R,No aggregate efficiency (payoff/maximum) reported per condition.,1,N/R,No mean joint payoffs reported per condition.,1,United Kingdom,Participants recruited from schools and university in southern England and Plymouth University.,1,"8-year-olds (M = 8.82, SD = 0.76)",Age group and mean age for children reported.,1,"39 girls, 42 boys",Gender breakdown for 71 eight-year-olds reported.,1,Primary school children from working- and middle-class communities,Described as primary school children; schools serve working- and middle-class communities.,0.9,On site,Children tested in quiet rooms at their schools on laptops.,1,Ultimatum game with strategy method: responders (Person B) make accept/reject decisions for seven possible offers (0–6 of 10 points). One decision is randomly matched with a real proposer decision for payoff. Points converted to raffle tickets for vouchers or USB sticks.,Summarized from methods description of UG implementation and incentives.,1
