10.1016_j.joep.2018.03.003.md,Individual – Ex-post (third-party punishment condition set),"Condition where the third party is an individual and punishment decisions are revealed after the PD players choose; corresponds to 'Individual Ex-post' in Table 2. Each individual C provides a full punishment strategy for all 7 scenarios, which is the relevant condition set.",0.96,Third-party type: Individual; Timing of revelation: Ex-post (punishment strategy shown after PD decisions).,Two-by-two design described in Section 2.1 and Table 2 varying third-party type (Individual vs Group) and timing (Ex-ante vs Ex-post).,0.99,True,Uses human participants in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with predefined treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the Pittsburgh Experimental Economics Laboratory.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; all results from human subjects.,1,False,"Contains theoretical discussion but core results are experimental, not purely analytical.",0.95,3,"Each game has Person A, Person B (PD players) and Person C (third party).",1,1,Design is a one-shot prisoner's dilemma game.,1,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions (cooperate/defect) and cannot choose an intermediate contribution; coded as 1 since 'can contribute any amount' vs all-or-nothing refers to contribution amount decisions, here only binary choice is available so they cannot choose any amount. However schema forces 1 or 0; we treat it as 0 meaning all-or-nothing.",0.4,0,"Standard PD game with no endowment pre-assigned to public account; all payoffs determined by actions, so default proportion in public fund is effectively 0.",0.3,N/R,"This is a prisoner's dilemma with fixed payoff matrix, not a linear public good; no marginal per capita return reported.",1,0,Only group third parties are allowed to chat; individuals make decisions alone.,1,0,No mention that subjects see aggregates or histories; one-shot game with only current scenario info.,0.9,0,Persons A a