ost normalized to c=1 when cooperating.,0.75,N/R,"They discuss abundances of various strategies (e.g., C-NPN, L-PNN, D-NNP) but do not translate this to overall contribution rates.",0.55,N/R,No explicit average contributions reported.,0.55,N/R,They do not compute efficiency measures; main outcome is which strategies are prevalent.,0.5,N/R,Group payoff distributions are not reported numerically.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is based on computational simulations and theory without empirical subjects.,1,"Larger optional public goods game with 24 strategies from Rand & Nowak (2011). Strategy is a 4-tuple [a1,a2,a3,a4] where a1∈{D,C,L} specifies contribution/participation, and a2,a3,a4∈{P,N} specify whether to punish cooperators, defectors, or loners, respectively. Parameters: N≈100, n=5, r=3, c=1, β=1, γ=0.3, σ=1. Uniform mutation allows transitions between any pair of strategies. Analysis highlights prevalence of altruistic punishers (C-NPN), loners that punish defectors (L-PNN), and defectors that punish loners (D-NNP).",Summarizes structure and parameterization of the 24-strategy optional public goods game under uniform mutations.,0.9
10.1371_journal.pone.0035287.md,"Optional public goods with punishment – 24-strategy model, bitwise-like mutation kernel",Second condition in 24-strategy optional public goods game uses a bitwise-like mutation kernel restricting changes to one position in the 4-tuple (Figure 6 Panel B).,0.93,"Mutation kernel: bitwise-like, allowing mutations only to strategies differing in one of the four positions [a1,a2,a3,a4]; each strategy has 5 neighbors with mutation probability μ/5, self-mutation 1-μ.",They describe 'bitwise-like kernel' where 'mutations can only take you to a strategy that differs in one position' and give example for D-NNN having 5 neighbors with probability μ/5.,0.97,False,Same modeling framework as uniform 24-strategy condition; no empirical participants.,1,False,"Model comparison, not an experiment with human subjects.",1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,They compute stationary distributions numerically and depict them in Figure 6 for this kernel.,1,True,They formulate the mutation structure and analyze its impact on stationary distributions; assumes same Moran rare-mutation analytical framework.,0.85,100,Parameters inherited from previous optional public goods analysis (N=100 used in prior figures).,0.85,1,Single-stage public goods game per group-formation event.,0.75,1,Individuals either contribute full cost c or do not; no fractional contributions described.,0.9,N/A,No explicit initial allocation into a group account.,0.8,0.6,"Same PGG parameters: r=3, group size n=5.",0.9,N/A,No human communication or chat.,1,N/A,"No interfaces, purely types and payoffs.",1,N/A,"Punishment operates on types, not identities.",1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No human players or round displays.,1,1,Strategies include punishment decisions for each target type; fines and costs as before.,1,0.3,Same γ parameter as other optional public goods variants.,0.85,1,Fine β remains 1 per punished opponent.,0.85,0,Model only describes punishment behaviors.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,1,Contribution cost c is normalized to 1 when cooperating.,0.75,N/R,"They emphasize which strategies are most abundant (altruistic punishers, loners who punish cooperators, pure abstainers) but do not present aggregate contribution rates.",0.5,N/R,No explicit contribution amounts per group reported.,0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency or welfare comparisons are quantified.,0.45,N/R,Group payoff outcomes discussed qualitatively ('no play being a very successful alternative') but not numerically.,0.45,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,This is a computational/theoretical condition with no participants.,1,"Same 24-strategy optional public goods game as the uniform 24-strategy condition: strategies [a1,a2,a3,a4] with a1∈{D,C,L} and a2–a4∈{P,N} indicating whether each type is punished. Public goods parameters: N≈100, n=5, r=3, c=1, β=1, γ=0.3, σ=1. Bitwise-like mutation restricts changes to one of the four positions per mutation, giving each strategy 5 neighbors, e.g., D-NNN can mutate to D-NNP, D-NPN, D-PNN, C-NNN, L-NNN, each with probability μ/5. This kernel increases prevalence of abstaining strategies and loners who punish cooperators compared to the uniform kernel.",Captures the detailed definition of the bitwise-like kernel and how it operates over the 24-strategy space.,0.9
10.1209_0295-5075_121_48005.md,Monte Carlo simulation condition: general PDG with punishment and opportunistic partner switching,"Single theoretical Monte Carlo simulation model with varying parameters (b, w, α, γ, ⟨k⟩, etc.). No distinct experimental 'treatments' with humans; all conditions are parameter settings of the same simulation framework.",0.9,"Temptation to defect b, punishment fine α, punishment cost γ, partner switching frequency w, average degree ⟨k⟩, mutation rate μ, selection intensity β, population size N. Specific parameter combinations varied across figures (e.g., b ∈ (1,2), α ∈ [0,0.88], γ ∈ {0,0.05,0.3,0.5}, w ∈ [0,0.8], ⟨k⟩ ∈ {8,32}, N=50000, β=0.1, μ=0.005).",Paper describes a single simulation model whose behavior is examined under multiple parameter combinations; these parameters are the independent variables driving differences between reported outcomes.,0.95,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on networks; no human or animal participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a computational simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe 'Monte Carlo simulation' of evolutionary PDG on networks.,1,False,No closed-form analytical results or proofs; all results are numerical simulations.,0.95,50000,Model section states 'We consider a set of N agents' and results specify N=50000 for the main simulations.,0.95,300000,"Simulation continued for 10000×N periods, then averaged over following 20000×N periods. With N=50000 this is (10000+20000)×N=30000×50000=1.5e9 update events; interpreting each N events as one 'round', they report payoffs every N-period, implying 30000 such aggregate steps. To give a single scalar, using 30000 aggregate rounds is most consistent.",0.4,1,"PDG strategies are pure C, D, or P; there is no continuous contribution choice. However, the 'all-or-nothing' field is defined for contribution magnitudes in public goods; PDG has only discrete cooperate/defect choices. Best mapping is that contribution is binary rather than continuous, so coded as allowing 'any amount' is inappropriate; still, schema forces 0/1, and 1 is used to reflect non-all-or-nothing contribution levels in other games. Here this field is conceptually N/A but must be numeric.",0.2,N/A,This is a prisoner's dilemma with binary strategies rather than a contribution game with an initial endowment allocated between private and public accounts; not applicable.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined for this PDG with payoff matrix; no public goods multiplier is reported.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication between agents is not modelled as chat.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents have payoff and strategy information only as defined in update rules.,1,N/A,No identities in a human sense; punishment is automatic in pairwise interactions without identification concerns.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is included in the model.,1,N/A,Agents are not human subjects and are not informed about horizon or number of periods.,1,1,Model is explicitly 'PDG with punishment'; strategy P inflicts punishment on defectors.,1,0.05,"Punishment cost γ is given as γ=0.05 in main parameter sets (e.g., Fig.1,2,3). This is the cost to a punisher per punished interaction.",0.9,0.17,"Fine α inflicted on defectors by punishers is varied; a commonly analyzed 'weak punishment' case uses α=0.17 (e.g., Fig.2b). Since one scalar is required, 0.17 is chosen as representative; other conditions use α up to ≈0.88.",0.5,0,No rewarding mechanism or positive sanction strategy is modelled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix is normalized (entries like 1, 0, b, b−α, −γ) without specifying a per-period endowment or resource constraint.",0.2,N/R,"Outcome variables reported are frequencies of strategies (ρC, ρD, ρP) and payoffs, not normalized contribution rates from an endowment.",1,N/R,"Binary PDG actions (C, D, P) without a continuous or token-valued contribution; no token amounts reported.",1,N/R,Paper discusses average payoffs qualitatively but does not report total group payoff divided by maximum possible in a normalized 0–1 form.,0.3,N/R,"Only mean payoffs per game for each strategy (πC, πD, πP) and their differences are discussed qualitatively; no aggregate group payoff figures provided.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract nodes in a simulated network.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists solely of Monte Carlo simulations of agents on networks.,1,"Evolutionary 2-player prisoner's dilemma on a dynamic network with three pure strategies: cooperation (C), defection (D), and punishment (P). Rescaled payoff matrix: C vs C:1, C vs D:0, C vs P:1; D vs C:b, D vs D:0, D vs P:b−α; P vs C:1, P vs D:−γ, P vs P:1, with 1<b<2. Network: initially homogeneous random network with average degree ⟨k⟩, N agents, degree heterogeneity emerges via rewiring. Dynamics: in each elementary step, either strategy update (probability 1−w) or partner switching (probability w). Strategy update: a link is selected at random; one endpoint is focal, the other role; both accumulate payoffs from PD with all neighbors; focal imitates role's strategy with Fermi rule P(s_f→s_r)=[1+exp(−β(Π_r−Π_f))]^{-1}. Mutation: with probability μ in a strategy update event, a randomly selected agent adopts a random strategy (C,D,P) uniformly. Partner switching (opportunistic rewiring): a focal agent considers a randomly selected neighbor and a randomly selected non-neighbor; if rewiring that link strictly improves focal's payoff (based on strategy combinations and b−α sign per Table 1), it severs the neighbor link and creates a link to the non-neighbor, with the constraint that nodes of degree 1 cannot lose their last link. Key parameters explored: b (temptation), α (fine), γ (cost), w (link adaptation frequency), ⟨k⟩ (average degree), β (selection intensity, typically 0.1), μ (mutation, typically 0.005). Outcomes tracked: stationary frequencies ρC, ρD, ρP, cooperative share ρC+ρP, time series of mean payoffs per game πC, πD, πP and their differences, transition counts between strategies (e.g., n_{D→C}, n_{D→P}) and interaction counts (e.g., n_{D−C}, n_{D−P}).","Summarizes the game structure and dynamic rules from the Model and Results sections, including the payoff matrix and update processes.",0.98
10.1103_PhysRevE.101.062419.md,Analytical model – consensual peer punishment (public goods game),"Single analytical evolutionary game model is developed for a public goods game with consensual peer punishment characterized by parameters N, r, c, alpha, beta, T, q.",1,"Consensus threshold T (0–1) and cooperators' willingness to punish q (0–1), with other parameters such as N, r, c, α, β varied in robustness analyses.","The paper systematically studies how varying T and q (and in some figures N, r, α, β) affects evolutionary outcomes; these are the core independent variables.",1,False,Model is purely theoretical with no human participants; uses replicator dynamics and analytical derivations.,1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; only mathematical modeling and analysis.,1,False,"No laboratory or on-site experimental setup, only analytical theory.",1,False,Main results are given as analytical formulas and bifurcation/gradient plots derived from them; no explicit agent-based or numerical Monte Carlo simulation with stochastic agents is described.,0.8,True,Payoffs and dynamics are derived in closed form using replicator equations and combinatorial sums; appendices detail analytical derivations.,1,5,"Public goods group size is denoted N; most numerical illustrations fix N=5 (e.g., Fig. 2 caption: 'N=5').",0.9,1,"Standard infinite-population evolutionary PGG: each game instance is a single-shot interaction; repetition is modeled by continuous-time replicator dynamics, not repeated rounds for the same players.",0.7,1,"Cooperators 'each contribute c (c>0)' and defectors contribute 0; no option for partial contributions is described, implying binary contribute-or-not.",0.8,N/A,No human subjects or interface with default allocations; purely analytical model.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/N. In main parameter set, r=3 and N=5 (Fig. 2 caption), so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No human players or communication; model only specifies strategy types and punishment voting.,1,N/A,No user interface; informational feedback is not defined at participant level.,1,N/A,No identities or anonymity concepts as there are no actual players.,1,N/A,"There is no reward mechanism in this model, only punishment.",1,N/A,No experimental interface; infinite-time evolutionary dynamics.,1,1,Core mechanism is peer punishment imposed on defectors once consensus is reached.,1,0.3,"Cost per cooperator per defector is α; main parameter choice uses α=0.3 (e.g., Fig. 2 caption: 'α=0.3').",0.9,1,Fine per defector per cooperator is β; main parameters set β=1 (Fig. 2 caption: 'β=1').,0.9,0,Only punishment (peer or shared) is modeled; no positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,1,"Each cooperator contributes c; main parameter set uses c=1 (e.g., Fig. 2 caption: 'c=1'). In standard PGG interpretation this is their whole endowment each round.",0.9,N/R,"Outcome measures are equilibrium fractions of cooperators x and their basins of attraction, not normalized individual contribution rates (which are fixed at 0 or c).",0.6,N/R,Model does not report average contribution per capita; it focuses on frequency x of full contributors and stability of equilibria.,0.6,N/R,"No explicit ratio of total payoff to maximum possible payoff is reported, only qualitative statements about facilitation of cooperation.",0.6,N/R,Group payoff expressions are embedded in π_C and π_D but no specific numerical group payoff outcome is highlighted as a dependent variable.,0.6,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical population.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is an analytical evolutionary game model without human or field data.,1,"Infinite, well-mixed population. Groups of size N randomly formed to play a linear public goods game with binary strategies: cooperation (contribute c) or defection (contribute 0). Total contributions are multiplied by enforcement factor r (1<r<N) and equally shared. Cooperators independently vote to punish defectors with probability q (average WTPD). Peer punishment is implemented by all cooperators if the fraction of pro-punishment cooperators reaches threshold T (n_P/n_C ≥ T). When implemented, each cooperator pays α per defector and each defector is fined β per cooperator. Evolution follows replicator dynamics on the fraction of cooperators x. Comparative statics also consider autonomous punishment (no voting, punishment depends only on q) and an alternative shared punishment implementation in an appendix.","Summarizes formal description in Materials and Methods, Fig. 1, and appendices A–C.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.101.062419.md,Analytical model – autonomous peer punishment (baseline without democratic procedure),"Section III.B derives payoffs and dynamics for a second model where punishment is autonomous (no voting), contrasted to consensual punishment.",1,"Cooperators' willingness to punish q (0–1), with parameters N, r, c, α, β varied; no consensus threshold T (effectively T=0 decision rule).","In autonomous punishment, only q is varied to study cooperation; T is not used, and the comparison with consensual punishment highlights q as the key driver.",1,False,This is another analytical scenario of the same theoretical model with no empirical data.,1,False,No controlled human experiment; purely mathematical analysis.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab environment.,1,False,Dynamics analyzed via analytical expressions g(x); no explicit agent-based or numerical-only simulation described.,0.8,True,Average payoffs π_C' and π_D' and payoff difference g(x) are derived analytically; equilibria discussed using these formulas.,1,5,"Same public goods setting as main model; comparative statements use N=5 in examples (e.g., 'for N=5, r=3, c=1, β=1').",0.9,1,"As with the consensual model, interactions are one-shot PGGs embedded in continuous-time evolutionary dynamics.",0.7,1,Same binary cooperation/defection contribution rule (contribute c or 0) is used.,0.8,N/A,No human interface or default allocation.,1,0.6,"Still a linear PGG with multiplier r and group size N; main comparative example uses N=5, r=3 so MPCR = r/N = 0.6.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,Not applicable to analytical model.,1,N/A,No identities of punishers/targets in the analytical model.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,1,Autonomous model also includes peer punishment: cooperators punish defectors at cost α and fine β dependent on q.,1,0.3,Same α as in main parameter set; comparisons explicitly use α=0.3.,0.9,1,Same β as consensual model; examples use β=1.,0.9,0,Only punishment considered.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Same c=1 assumption for worked examples; cooperators contribute c.,0.9,N/R,"Dependent variable is the equilibrium/cooperative basin in x, not per-capita contribution rate.",0.6,N/R,"With binary contributions, average contribution correlates with x but is not separately reported or analyzed.",0.6,N/R,The paper does not quantify efficiency numerically for the autonomous case.,0.6,N/R,Group payoffs are implicit in π_C' and π_D' but not extracted as a main outcome.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical comparison scenario without empirical data.,1,"Same infinite well-mixed population and linear public goods game as the consensual model. Cooperators contribute c, defectors 0; contributions multiplied by r and split evenly. Under autonomous peer punishment, punishment is not subject to a voting threshold: any cooperator who intends to punish does so independently. The average degree of willingness to punish q determines expected punishment. Cooperators incur punishment cost q·α(N−1)(1−x); defectors are fined β·q·x(N−1). Payoff difference g(x) = π_C' − π_D' is analyzed to find equilibria and basin of attraction of cooperation, and outcomes are compared to consensual punishment variants.","Summarizes Section III.B explicit formulas for π_C', π_D' and discussion about conditions on q under autonomous punishment.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.101.062419.md,Analytical model – consensual shared punishment (alternative cost-sharing mechanism),"Appendix C introduces a variant with 'shared punishment' under the same consensual voting rule, with different cost allocation.",1,"Consensus threshold T and cooperators' willingness to punish q under a shared punishment cost structure; same N, r, c, α, β as base model.","Appendix C derives payoffs π_Cs and π_Ds as functions of x, T, q introducing shared punishment; these are varied conceptually as in the main model.",0.9,False,This is another analytical variant; no experiments are run.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment.,1,False,Purely theoretical.,1,False,Model is specified analytically; no dedicated simulation section.,0.8,True,Appendix C gives explicit analytical expressions for payoffs under shared punishment.,1,5,"Variant is defined by modifying punishment terms in the main model; illustrative parameters (e.g., N=5) remain applicable.",0.8,1,"As in other analytical variants, it is a one-shot PGG within replicator dynamics.",0.7,1,Still a binary contribution decision (C vs D).,0.8,N/A,No human interface.,1,0.6,"Same linear PGG structure with r and N; examples keep r=3, N=5.",0.9,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No player identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,1,Shared punishment is a punishment institution triggered by consensus.,1,0.3,"Cost parameter α still governs total punishment cost; Appendix C modifies how this cost is shared (factor (N−k−1)/(k+1)), not its unit value.",0.9,1,Fine parameter β remains the per-defector fine; only allocation of costs differs.,0.9,0,Only shared punishment; no rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Same assumed contribution c=1 for cooperators in examples.,0.8,N/R,Appendix C notes that qualitative results are similar but does not present explicit outcome measures beyond equilibrium structure.,0.5,N/R,Average contributions are not numerically reported.,0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure is computed for shared punishment variant.,0.5,N/R,Payoffs are expressed analytically but no numerical group payoff is highlighted.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical variant only.,1,"Same infinite, well-mixed PGG with cooperation and defection, consensus threshold T, and willingness to punish q. Under consensual shared punishment, if consensus is reached, defectors each suffer a fixed fine β (independent of number of cooperators), while the total cost of punishment is equally shared among all cooperators: each cooperator’s cost is proportional to (N−k−1)/(k+1), where k is the number of other cooperators. Appendix C provides payoff formulas π_Cs and π_Ds and states that qualitative evolutionary dynamics under shared punishment are similar to those under peer punishment.",Directly summarizes Appendix C description of shared punishment variant.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.101.062419.md,"Analytical model – autonomous shared punishment (no voting, cost-sharing)","Appendix C further defines payoffs π_Cs' and π_Ds' for autonomous shared punishment, a distinct analytical condition.",1,"Cooperators' willingness to punish q in a shared punishment regime without consensus voting; same game parameters N, r, c, α, β as other variants.",Appendix C gives π_Cs' and π_Ds' as functions of x and q; T is not used under autonomous implementation.,0.9,False,Pure theoretical construct with no empirical data.,1,False,Not an experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,Analytical formulas are used; no simulation section.,0.8,True,Payoffs of autonomous shared punishment are derived in closed form in Appendix C.,1,5,"Same base PGG structure, with N used as general group size; numerical statements elsewhere typically use N=5.",0.8,1,One-shot PGG per interaction within replicator dynamics.,0.7,1,"Cooperators contribute c, defectors 0; no partial contributions are introduced.",0.8,N/A,No human interface.,1,0.6,"Same PGG as other conditions; r/N with common example r=3, N=5.",0.9,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No subject identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,1,Autonomous shared punishment is a punishment institution.,1,0.3,α continues to represent punishment cost parameter; in π_Cs' it scales the shared cost term.,0.9,1,β is the per-defector fine; π_Ds' includes a term with β.,0.9,0,No positive incentives modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Same c=1 example; cooperators contribute c.,0.8,N/R,Appendix C notes qualitative similarity but does not present explicit outcome metrics for this variant.,0.5,N/R,Not separately quantified.,0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency outcomes provided.,0.5,N/R,Group payoffs are not quantitatively summarized.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical construct only.,1,"Autonomous shared punishment retains the same PGG structure and parameterization as other conditions. Punishment is 'shared': when any cooperator chooses to punish (with probability depending on q), defectors suffer a fixed fine β each, and the total punishment cost is shared among cooperators according to terms involving (N−k−1)/(m+1) where m is the number of punishers. Appendix C provides formulas for π_Cs' and π_Ds' and indicates that qualitative evolutionary outcomes resemble those of peer punishment.",Summarizes text and equations in Appendix C defining autonomous shared punishment.,1
10.1038_s41562-022-01341-7.md,Observational study of Mentawai tulou cases,Paper reports a single observational ethnographic dataset of 302 reliable transgression cases; no distinct experimental conditions are defined.,1,"Naturally varying case attributes (e.g., within-clan vs between-clan, severity of transgression, mediation vs face-to-face resolution, presence/absence of tulou payment).",Authors analyze how outcomes vary with these naturally occurring factors rather than manipulating variables experimentally.,1,True,Ethnographic interviews and observations with 95 human participants about 444 (302 analyzed) real-world cases.,1,False,No randomized assignment or controlled interventions; purely observational/retrospective reports plus participant observation.,1,False,"All work was field ethnography in communities, not laboratory-based.",1,False,"Although they ran statistical simulations to compare mediator selection to chance, the primary data are human observational; no standalone simulation study of strategic agents.",0.9,False,Paper includes statistical modeling but no formal game-theoretic or closed-form analytical model.,1,2,Each tulou case fundamentally involves at least two disputing sides (offender and victim plus their clans); closest analogue to a dyadic interaction.,0.5,1,Each recorded case is a single conflict episode with a single outcome; no repeated strategic rounds like in experimental games.,0.8,1,Resource transfers (tulou) can be negotiated at many magnitudes and compositions; not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.,0.8,N/R,No public-goods contribution game or default allocation structure is described.,1,N/R,No linear public goods game with defined multiplier or group size; only compensation/punishment-like payments.,1,1,Conflicts are resolved via discussions and negotiations (often lengthy meetings); free verbal communication is central.,1,1,"Negotiations are often public with many observers; parties hear each other’s claims and responses, functioning as full outcome transparency.",0.7,1,Identities of transgressors and victims are always known in these real-world cases; punishments/payments are directed at named individuals/clans in public.,1,0,No reward or prosocial bonus mechanism is described; only penalties/compensation.,1,0,There is no fixed number of rounds in a strategic game sense; participants are not shown a remaining-rounds counter.,1,1,Tulou explicitly includes a punitive component (tulounia) and is understood as a fine/penalty; some cases involve seizure of resources.,1,N/R,Payments are complex bundles of heterogeneous goods; no per-unit punishment cost parameter is defined as in lab games.,1,N/R,No standardized mapping from one unit of cost to one unit of loss/penalty is specified; magnitudes are fully negotiated.,1,0,There is no described system where third parties provide costly positive rewards for good behavior; focus is on penalties/compensation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No standardized endowment of tokens or money per player; cases involve real-world property and livestock owned prior to conflict.,1,N/R,Study does not measure voluntary contributions to a public good as a fraction of endowment.,1,N/R,No public-goods contribution amounts are reported; only tulou payment values are analyzed.,1,N/R,No notion of efficiency defined as payoff relative to a maximum in a structured game.,1,N/R,"No aggregate numerical payoffs by group are reported; focus is on whether tulou is paid, its size, and mediation patterns.",1,Indonesia,"Mentawai live on Siberut Island in the Mentawai Archipelago, Indonesia.",1,"Adults; 95 interview participants (65 males, 30 females), adult men for mediator analyses","They describe adult men, women; mediator analysis restricted to adult men and age classes (young adults, junior elders, senior elders). Exact age ranges not given.",0.7,Both male and female participants; 65 male and 30 female interviewees; only men served as mediators.,Methods explicitly report 65 males and 30 females; also state that no women or children served as mediators.,1,Mixed; largely non-literate horticulturalists; no formal education distribution reported.,They note many participants lack functional literacy and live in small-scale horticultural communities; specific schooling levels not quantified.,0.6,Field experiment,"Although not an experiment, among given options this is best approximated as a field-based observational study of real-world behavior in natural communities.",0.7,"Observational ethnographic dataset of 302 reliable real-world transgressions and tulou outcomes among Mentawai, analyzed with Bayesian regressions and simulations; no structured economic game or controlled manipulation.",Summarizes that data stem from retrospective interviews and observed cases; statistical models relate naturally varying predictors to justice outcomes.,1
