ithout human subjects.,1,N/A,No identities in the human sense; model agents punish based on traits only.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled; also no human identities.,1,N/A,No user interface; infinite-horizon evolutionary simulation.,1,1,Second stage explicitly modeled as punishment stage with continuous punishment cost and damage functions.,1,N/R,"They specify punishment cost as f_i * exp(-(x_j/u_i)^a_i), but no per-unit linear cost/tech parameter analogous to ‘1 coin gives X damage’ is defined.",0.3,10,"Efficiency of punishment β>1, and simulations in Fig. 3 and related text use β=10.",0.95,0,Only punishment is modeled; no positive rewards to others are described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,Payoff is modeled in abstract score units with contribution x_i but no explicit numeric endowment per round.,0.2,N/R,"They report evolved average cooperation level x qualitatively and graphically, but not as explicit numeric summary values in text or tables.",0.3,N/R,Contributions equal x_i but no specific final or average numeric values are tabulated.,0.3,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff is reported.,0.1,N/R,They focus on trait evolution and do not report aggregate payoffs numerically.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary simulation with no subjects.,1,"Population size N×N with N=50 (total 2500 individuals). Groups of 4 from Moore neighborhood; each individual belongs to 4 groups. Traits x (cooperation), f (punishment severity), u (threshold), and a (strictness) are continuous (x,f,u in [0,1], a≥0). In this condition, a is fixed exogenously at given values and does not evolve; x,f,u evolve with mutation rate m=0.01, step size σ=0.01. Viability model: death probability c*exp(-d*s_i) with c,d>0; after death, one of 8 neighbors fills site. Parameter