10.1371_journal.pone.0059894.md,Analytical model – Random-matching condition (adaptive dynamics of continuous traits with fixed strictness a),"In “The Mathematical Model of the Random-matching Condition” they derive invasion fitness and pairwise invasibility plots for x given fixed a, f, u.",0.9,"Analytical parameters: public goods multiplier r, punishment efficiency β, group size z+1 with z opponents, strictness a fixed at various values (0, 0.01, 0.07, 1, 2, 5, 20, 100, 1000), punishment severity f and threshold u set to values near simulated equilibria; focal trait x considered as evolving in adaptive dynamics framework.",They analyze invasion gradient of x for different fixed a values and parameter settings to generate PIPs in Fig. 7.,0.9,False,This is a theoretical analytical model with no simulations or human data in this section.,1,False,No experimental manipulation; purely mathematical.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,False,"Here they derive equations (fitness gradient, invasion fitness) and plot PIPs analytically, not via population simulations.",0.9,True,"They use adaptive dynamics and derive closed-form invasion gradient equations (Appendix S1, Eq. A2a–c).",1,9,"They state: ‘a focal individual and z opponents are randomly chosen… Then z+1 members play a public goods game.’ For Fig. 7 they use z=8, so group size is 9.",0.95,1,Analytical model examines a single game per death event; no repeated rounds specified.,0.8,1,"Cooperation level x is continuous trait in [0,1].",1,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation in this analytical framework; variables are abstract continuous strategies.,0.7,N/R,"Equivalent MPCR would be r/(z+1), but r is a symbolic parameter and not fixed numerically for the general analytical model.",0.5,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,"No identities, no humans.",1,N/A,No rewards in analytical model.,1,N/A,Not meaningful for analytical model.,1,1,Punishment cost and damage terms included in payoff; they consider both pro-social and potential anti-social punishment in analysis.,1,N/R,Cost is modeled as continuous function f*exp(-(x_j/u)^a); no single scalar per-unit cost is extracted.,0.3,N/R,"Analytical treatment uses generic β with various values (e.g., β>z for coevolution); not a single fixed value.",0.5,0,No reward term added in analytical payoff function.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Analytical model directly uses contributions x_i; no explicit finite endowment parameter.,0.3,N/R,"They characterize equilibria qualitatively (e.g., x→1 or x→0) but don't report numeric rates as data.",0.4,N/R,X is a continuous trait; analytic results identify ESS values rather than numeric contributions for a particular experiment.,0.4,N/R,They do not compute overall efficiency metrics in the analytical section.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff appears symbolically; no numeric outcomes reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely mathematical analysis.,1,"They simplify the random-matching simulation by assuming that in each event a focal and z opponents are randomly drawn from a large population (z=8 in Fig. 7) to form a group of size z+1. Payoff (score) s_i is analogous to that in the simulation but without explicit spatial structure. Death rate uses same viability function c*exp(-d*s_i); they derive invasion fitness I for mutants in a resident population and compute fitness gradients (Eqs. A2a–c). Using adaptive dynamics, they identify conditions under which x converges to 0 or 1 for different strictness values a, confirming that with small a (graduated punishment) high cooperation can evolve in random-matching environments.",Summarized from ‘The Mathematical Model of the Random-matching Condition’ and Appendix S1 description.,0.96
10.1016_j.jtbi.2012.10.022.md,Analytical and numerical models of two-species N-player public goods games,Paper describes only mathematical/analytical models and numerical analyses (no distinct experimental conditions with human or animal subjects); treat the entire modeling framework as a single analytical/simulation study.,0.9,"Shape of benefit function (linear vs sigmoid/logistic vs step/threshold), group sizes N1 and N2, thresholds k1 and k2, benefit-to-cost ratios b1/c1 and b2/c2, and presence vs absence of punishment cost d_l for defectors.","Model explicitly varies N1, N2, k1, k2, steepness s_m (controlling linear vs sigmoid vs step), benefit parameters b_m, costs c_m, and whether a punishment cost d_l is imposed on defectors.",1,False,No human or animal participants; paper is purely theoretical with analytic derivations and numerical phase-plane analyses.,1,False,No controlled behavioral or biological experiment was conducted; only models are analyzed.,1,False,No laboratory or field experiment; only theory and simulations.,1,True,"Phase diagrams, vector fields, and equilibria are obtained numerically from the model (e.g., Figs. 2–4), which constitutes computer simulation of the dynamical system.",1,True,"Paper provides explicit analytical derivations of equilibria, stability conditions, and dynamics (e.g., Appendices A–D).",1,2,"Model concerns two interacting species; within each species individuals form groups of size N1 and N2, but at the top level there are 2 strategic populations.",0.8,1,Model is a continuous-time replicator dynamics with no discrete repeated rounds; mapped here as effectively a single indefinitely repeated interaction.,0.5,1,"Individuals are either cooperators or defectors (binary contribution choice), but the public good production is based on counts of cooperators; contribution is not an arbitrary continuous amount but a 0/1 decision; encoded here as 1 since standard PGG contribution decision exists, though discrete.",0.6,N/A,"No human subjects or monetary endowments; contributions are costs c_m to biological fitness, not allocations from an endowment.",1,N/R,"Model uses benefit parameter b_m and enhancement factor r_m=b_m/c_m, but no explicit marginal per capita return (multiplier divided by group size) is defined.",0.5,N/A,No human or agent communication protocol is modeled.,1,0,Model assumes well-mixed infinite populations and does not incorporate any information display or feedback about others’ actions beyond their effect on fitness.,1,N/A,"Punishment, when present, is modeled as a cost d_l applied to defectors without any notion of identity or observability of specific individuals.",1,N/A,No individual-level rewards are modeled; only public goods and optional sanctions on defectors.,1,0,Continuous-time replicator dynamics with no round structure or round information.,1,1,"Core model is without punishment, but Appendix D explicitly introduces sanctions (cost d_l on defectors); since the condition set spans cases with and without punishment, overall model includes punishment as a possible mechanism.",1,N/R,Punishment cost to defectors is parameter d_l but no specific numerical value is given; only inequalities such as d_l>c_l are discussed.,0.7,N/R,Model does not define a conversion rate between punisher effort and reduction in target payoff; punishment is implemented as a direct cost d_l to defectors.,0.7,0,No explicit costly rewarding mechanism is modeled; only non-discriminatory public goods and optional sanctions on defectors.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,There is no notion of an experimental monetary endowment; individuals have baseline fitness W0 and may pay cost c_m to cooperate.,1,N/R,"Model tracks frequencies of cooperators x1 and x2 as state variables, but does not report specific equilibrium numeric values except in examples; no single canonical contribution rate is given.",0.6,N/A,No discrete token contributions; only binary strategy choice with cost c_m.,1,N/R,"Although total fitness outcomes can be derived, the paper does not report efficiency as total payoff divided by maximum possible payoff.",0.7,N/R,Paper discusses fitness functions and equilibria qualitatively and via phase diagrams but does not report a specific numeric group payoff outcome.,0.7,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical work.,1,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical work.,1,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical work.,1,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical work.,1,No human,Study consists solely of mathematical analysis and simulations without human subjects.,1,"Two-species N-player public goods game with cooperators and defectors in each species. Public goods are traded between species as nonlinear benefit functions of the number or frequency of cooperators (logistic/sigmoid or step/threshold). Benefits are multiplicative across species via H_l(x_l). Costs of cooperation are c1 and c2; maximal net benefits are b1 and b2 (enhancement factors r_m = b_m / c_m). Group sizes N1 and N2 and thresholds k1 and k2 shape the nonlinearity. Dynamics are modeled by coupled replicator equations for x1 and x2 in infinite well-mixed populations. Punishment is optionally included as an additional cost d_l to defectors, which turns the game into one of no conflict.",Summarizes the formal game structure and key parameters as defined in sections 2.1–2.4 and Appendices A–D.,1
10.1017_s1355770x08004592.md,"Fehr & Gächter (2000) – VCM with monetary punishment, partner design","Paper explicitly describes Fehr and Gächter (2000) introducing monetary sanctions into the VCM, with a partner (fixed matching) treatment.",0.9,Availability of monetary peer punishment (fines) in a VCM; fixed partner matching across rounds,Text: 'Fehr and Gächter (2000) introduced sanctioning into the VCM... Group members are allowed to use monetary fines... They considered two experimental treatments: people are either randomly paired... or are paired with a continuous partner from round to round.' This row is the continuous partner (partner) treatment with punishment.,0.85,True,Human subjects in a laboratory public goods game.,1,True,Controlled VCM with specified treatments and incentives.,1,True,Fehr and Gächter (2000) is a standard laboratory public goods experiment with student subjects.,0.95,False,No indication of computer-only agents; human participants used.,1,False,"Study reports experimental results, not pure theory.",1,4,"Typical Fehr & Gächter (2000) VCM design used groups of 4; although not stated in this text, it is standard in that paper.",0.6,10,Canonical Fehr & Gächter 2000 design uses 10 periods; not stated here but known from the cited study.,0.6,1,Standard VCM allows any contribution from 0 to full endowment; all-or-nothing binary not mentioned.,0.8,0,"In VCMs, endowment starts in private account; subjects choose how much to contribute.",0.9,N/R,No marginal per capita return reported in the summary of Fehr & Gächter (2000).,0,0,Communication not mentioned for Fehr & Gächter (2000); their design did not include pre-play chat.,0.7,1,Punishment targeted at low contributors relative to group average; implies information on others’ contributions was provided.,0.8,0,Described as group members sanctioning each other but identities generally anonymous in this line of work; no text indicating identity revelation.,0.6,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition (punishment only).,1,1,Standard repeated public goods experiments inform participants about the number of periods; assumed from canonical design though not stated.,0.6,1,Text: 'Group members are allowed to use monetary fines to sanction or punish...'.,1,N/R,"Cost per punishment point (e.g., 1 to 3) not specified in this summary.",0,N/R,Reduction in target’s payoff per punishment unit not given here.,0,0,"Only fines/punishment mentioned, no rewards.",0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Per-period endowment (e.g., 20 tokens) not specified in the text.",0,N/R,Only qualitative statement that 'Group behavior evolves toward Pareto optimum... cooperation is the most frequent action'; no numerical rates.,0,N/R,No token amounts reported.,0,N/R,Higher cooperation mentioned but not quantified as share of maximum surplus.,0,N/R,No absolute payoffs given.,0,Switzerland,Fehr & Gächter (2000) was run with Swiss university students in Zurich; not in text but known from the cited paper.,0.6,N/R,Age range not stated; typically university students but no details here.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students,Fehr & Gächter (2000) standard subject pool is university students; not mentioned here but well-known.,0.6,On site,"Laboratory experiment with student subjects in a physical lab, not online.",0.9,Standard linear VCM with post-contribution punishment stage; fixed partner matching; punishment focused on negative deviation from group average contribution; evolution toward high cooperation (near Pareto optimum) in partner design.,"Summarized directly from description: VCM with monetary fines, continuous partners, and behavioral outcome.",0.9
