10.1038_s41598-017-12823-4.md,"Simulation – P vs D, baseline PGG with punishment and mobility","Whole paper describes a single spatial PGG simulation framework with parameters varied (r, θ, γ, β, f_IP, ρ); this row captures the generic baseline configuration common to all conditions.",0.7,Enhancement factor r; punishment cost γ; punishment intensity β; migration threshold θ; initial fraction of punishers f_IP; population density ρ; presence/absence of cooperators C,"These parameters are systematically varied across figures (r in Figs.1,3–7; γ and β in Figs.2 and 5; θ in Figs.1–3,5–7; f_IP in Figs.3,6; ρ fixed at 0.5; presence of C differs between P+D and C+D+P scenarios).",0.95,False,Model is a spatial evolutionary game on an L×L lattice studied via Monte Carlo simulations; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; purely computational simulations of an evolutionary model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only in silico simulations.,1,True,Authors state they use Monte Carlo simulations on square lattices to study evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,Paper does not present closed-form analytical solutions; focuses on numerical simulations and contour/phase plots.,0.9,N/R,Population is on an L×L lattice with L varied from 100 to 400; exact N depends on L and density ρ; no single fixed player count for all simulations.,0.3,N/R,"They report relaxation time from 10^4 to 10^6 Monte Carlo steps and averaging over runs, but not a fixed, discrete number of ‘rounds’ per game in the sense of experimental periods.",0.3,1,"Each cooperator or punisher contributes a fixed cost c=1; defectors contribute 0; there is no decision about contribution level beyond type, so there is no continuous contribution choice – but within this coding scheme this corresponds to no partial choice. However, schema defines 1 as players can contribute any amount; here they cannot, so use 0.",0.2,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation; strategies intrinsically determine contribution c=1 or 0 in the model.,1,N/R,Public good returns are described via enhancement factor r applied to total contributions in groups with variable size (due to empty sites); MPCR per capita is not explicitly given and depends on local group composition.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No user interface; not a human experiment.,1,N/A,No identities displayed; simulated agents update based on payoffs only.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism for agents’ UI; purely payoff-based simulation.,1,N/A,No participants to whom the number of rounds could be shown.,1,1,Punishers reduce defectors’ payoff by β at cost γ whenever number of defectors ≤ θ.,1,N/R,"γ is varied across conditions (e.g., 0.3, 0.7, 0.9) but no single fixed value characterizes all simulations; this row is generic.",0.4,N/R,β is varied (intensity parameter); magnitude per unit cost is not fixed globally in this summary row.,0.4,0,Model includes only costly punishment; no reward or positive sanction mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,"Agents do not have a per-round endowment choice; they either contribute a fixed cost c=1 or 0 depending on strategy, without explicit endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as fractions of strategy types (P, C, D) in the stationary state, not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,They do not report average contributions per group; only strategy fractions and phase boundaries.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency index (total payoff divided by max possible) is reported or derived.,0.2,N/R,"Payoff structure is defined, but aggregate group payoffs are not numerically reported.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a computational Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game on L×L square lattice with periodic boundary conditions and population density ρ; groups defined by Moore neighborhood (k=8) plus focal player; cooperators and punishers contribute c=1, defectors contribute 0; total contributions multiplied by r and shared equally; punishers punish defectors with intensity β at cost γ if number of defectors ≤ θ, otherwise migrate to neighboring empty site; synchronous strategy updating by Fermi imitation with selection intensity κ=0.1; background random migration with probability ε=0.01.","Summarizes key model details from Methods section including lattice, group size, payoff, punishment rule, migration, and update dynamics.",1
