10.1017_epi.2013.34.md,"Merit-Demerit game – opportunistic punishment (b>0, p>0) baseline example","Condition described in Fig. 1(a): dynamics of the Merit-Demerit game with parameter set μ=10, θ=1, p=2, b=3, c=0.5 where punishment can be opportunistic.",0.95,"Payoff parameters in Merit-Demerit game; this condition: μ=10, θ=1, p=2, b=3, c=0.5 with b>0, p>0 (potentially opportunistic punishment).","Only parameters varied across modeled conditions are payoff parameters μ, θ, p, b, c; this row records the specific combination used in Fig. 1(a).",0.98,False,The paper presents a theoretical evolutionary game-theoretic model with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only mathematical and dynamical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely theoretical model.,1,True,"Figures (e.g., Fig. 1(a)) depict numerical trajectories of replicator dynamics given parameter values, which is a computer simulation of the evolutionary dynamics.",0.86,True,"Derives mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium, replicator dynamics, Jacobian, and eigenvalues analytically in the appendix.",1,2,The Merit-Demerit game is given as a 2-player normal-form payoff matrix (Table I).,1,1,Game is defined as a single-period interaction; repeated play is modeled via evolutionary dynamics rather than explicit multi-round play.,0.85,1,"There is no contribution decision; players choose a discrete strategy (H, P, C), so the all-or-nothing contribution concept does not apply; coded as 1 per instruction for general contribution freedom.",0.4,N/A,Model is not a public goods or contribution game; no endowment allocation between private and public accounts.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined in the model.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between players is not modeled.,1,0,"Information structure is not about observing payoffs or histories, only strategy payoffs in an anonymous game; no summaries are mentioned.",0.7,N/A,"No explicit identities i