10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2021.04.002.md,CP experiment – Punishment-conditional punishment,The second main experiment is the CP experiment where punishment is conditioned on descriptive norms of punishment in a reference group.,1,Descriptive norm of punishment in reference group: average punishment intensity across situations spanning 0–10 deduction points,"Section 2.3 states that in the CP experiment the descriptive norm is the average intensity of punishment in the reference group, and participants state punishment for each possible situation.",1,True,Human MTurk workers participate in the same two-stage game structure as in the CC experiment.,1,True,Controlled experimental design with random matching and specified payoff rules.,1,False,"Conducted online via MTurk and LIONESS Lab, not in a physical lab.",1,False,This condition involves real human decisions.,1,False,"Analytical modeling is presented separately in the dynamic model section, not as part of this empirical condition.",1,2,Participants were randomly matched in pairs to play the game.,1,1,Described as a one-shot two-stage prisoner's dilemma with punishment.,1,0,Binary cooperate/defect choice plus a graded choice of 0–10 deduction points implies more than all-or-nothing actions.,0.8,0,Prisoner’s dilemma structure with fixed payoffs and no divisible public good; by convention treated as 0 proportion in a public fund.,0.4,N/A,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,The procedure does not mention any communication or chat functionality between players.,0.9,0,Participants only see descriptive norms from the reference group; there is no indication of feedback about others’ payoffs or punishment in their actual dyad.,0.7,0,No identities or identifiers of punishers are reported as being revealed.,0.8,0,No reward option exists in the game.,1,1,Participants are informed they play a one-shot interaction; thus they know the number of rounds.,0.8,1,Stage 2 allows assignment of up to 10 deduction points to a defecting partner.,1,1,Each deduction point costs the punisher 1 point.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces the partner’s payoff by 3 points.,1,0,Only costly punishment is implemented; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this experiment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this experiment.,1,25,"Participants receive an initial endowment of 25 points as in the general design description, which applies to both experiments.",1,0.655,"Cooperation rate in CP experiment is reported as 65.5%, used as the normalized cooperation rate.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amount is defined beyond binary cooperate/defect.,1,N/R,The paper does not report efficiency measures relative to the social optimum.,1,N/R,Group-level payoff statistics are not reported.,0.2,United States,Sample restricted to US-based MTurk workers.,1,"Mean 35.5 (SD 10.3, range 18–71) for full sample; not broken down by experiment",Age distribution provided only for the combined N=999 sample.,0.7,43% male in full sample; not broken down by experiment,Gender information is reported at the aggregate level over both experiments.,0.7,N/R,No education information is given.,1,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk and LIONESS Lab.,1,"Same two-stage one-shot prisoner's dilemma with punishment as CC: Stage 1 payoffs CC:18/18, CD:9 vs 25, DD:16/16; punishment allowed only against defectors with costs 1:3 to punisher:target. Strategy method elicits punishment for situations varying in the average punishment intensity in a reference group of 50 sampled from a pre-recorded pool (average pool punishment 2.7, cooperation 69%).","Summarizes structural aspects of the CP experiment, emphasizing that the conditioning variable is the reference group’s punishment behavior rather than cooperation.",1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2021.04.002.md,Dynamic model – Analytical component,The paper presents a formal dynamic model with analytical results on stationary distributions and thresholds for cooperation.,1,"Relative frequencies of punishment strategies in the population (Q_IP, Q_NE, Q_CP); threshold parameters θ_C, θ_NE, θ_CP; mistake probability ε; observation sample size m; population size n",Section 3.2 defines the analytical model in terms of these parameters and derives results varying these frequencies and thresholds.,1,False,This part of the study is a theoretical model with no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; it is purely analytical.,1,False,No laboratory or experimental setting; purely mathematical analysis.,1,False,"This row captures the analytical results, not the computational simulations.",1,True,Authors derive analytical results about stationary distributions and equilibria of the stochastic dynamic model.,1,N/A,The model considers a population of size n with random matching into pairs each period; no fixed experimental group size in the usual sense.,1,N/A,The analytical results consider the limit as T→∞; not a finite predefined number of rounds like in an experiment.,1,N/A,No human contributions; cooperation and punishment are modeled as binary decisions within the theory.,1,N/A,No human endowment allocation is modeled in terms of a default contribution proportion.,1,N/A,"The model uses a prisoner's dilemma with punishment, not a linear public good.",1,N/A,No communication features exist because there are no human participants.,1,N/A,Information structure is abstracted as beliefs b_c and b_p; no user interface.,1,N/A,Identities are not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards and identities are not part of the analytical model.,1,N/A,The analytical model studies long-run behavior rather than participant knowledge of round numbers.,1,N/A,"Punishment is part of the game form, but CONFIG fields are defined for empirical designs with human interface; here it is a theoretical construct.",1,N/A,Costs are embedded in theoretical payoffs; not specified as an experimental parameter for participants.,1,N/A,"Punishment impact is part of payoff functions, not an experimental configuration.",1,N/A,Only punishment is modeled; CONFIG applies to experimental protocols.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the analytical model relevant to CONFIG.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the analytical model relevant to CONFIG.,1,N/A,Endowments are not parameterized as in a lab experiment; payoffs follow a generic PD-with-punishment structure.,1,N/A,"Analytical results concern conditions for cooperation equilibria and stationary distributions, not a single numeric DV.",1,N/A,No single contribution amount is measured; behavior is characterized theoretically.,1,N/A,"Efficiency is discussed qualitatively via cooperation equilibria, not as a numeric dependent variable.",1,N/A,"Group payoffs are embedded in the payoff structure, not reported as empirical DVs.",1,N/A,No human participants are involved in the analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants in this component.,1,N/A,No human participants in this component.,1,N/A,No human participants in this component.,1,No human,Purely analytical model without empirical data collection.,1,"Stochastic dynamic model with n agents repeatedly matched into pairs each period. Agents choose binary cooperate/defect and binary punish/not punish. Agents sample m others to form beliefs b_c and b_p about cooperation and punishment rates. Cooperation occurs if b_p > θ_C. Four punishment strategies: independent, norm enforcement (punish if b_c > θ_NE), conformist (punish if b_p > θ_CP), and never punish. Mistakes occur with probability ε; stationary distribution analyzed as ε→0 and m=n. Key condition: cooperation uniquely emerges in the stationary distribution if Q_IP + 0.5(Q_NE+Q_CP) > 0.5.",Summarizes the analytically studied game and main theoretical result described in Section 3.2.,1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2021.04.002.md,Dynamic model – Agent-based simulations (baseline without conditional punishment),"Section 3.2 reports agent-based simulations for different compositions of punishment strategies; one baseline condition includes independent punishers only and no conditional strategies, in which cooperation does not emerge.",0.86,"Population composition: Q_IP=0.30, Q_NE=0, Q_CP=0; initial beliefs high (b_c=b_p=0.75) or low (b_c=b_p=0.25); n=100, m=10, ε=0.05; belief update probability u=0.5",Figure 4 panels a and b are described as cases with only independent punishers at 30% and no norm enforcers or conformist punishers; simulation parameters are specified in the caption.,0.86,False,These are numerical simulations of artificial agents using the theoretical model.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; code-based simulations.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe using simulations and provide code in a repository.,1,False,This row focuses on the computational simulation runs rather than closed-form analysis.,1,100,Simulation settings in Fig. 4 caption: n=100 agents.,1,100000,"Fig. 5 caption states each simulation runs for 10^5 periods; Figure 4 uses similar long trajectories, so we approximate with 100,000 periods.",0.7,1,"Within the simulated model, cooperation and punishment are binary decisions (cooperate/defect; punish/not), i.e., effectively all-or-nothing choices.",0.9,N/A,No monetary endowment is modeled in simulation CONFIG terms; only binary actions.,1,N/A,"The simulated game is a PD-with-punishment, not a linear public good.",1,N/A,No communication is represented between agents.,1,N/A,Agents hold beliefs b_c and b_p based on sampling; there is no user interface.,1,N/A,No identities in the agent-based model.,1,N/A,No rewards or identities are modeled.,1,N/A,Agents do not have explicit knowledge of horizon; model focuses on repeated interaction over many periods.,1,N/A,"Punishment is part of the theoretical game, but CONFIG parameters for human interface are not applicable.",1,N/A,Specific numeric cost is not detailed in the simulation description here.,0.4,N/A,Specific punishment impact is not parameterized in the excerpt.,0.4,N/A,No positive rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the simulated model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the simulated model.,1,N/A,No explicit per-period monetary endowment for agents is specified in simulation description.,1,N/R,"Figures show time paths of cooperation but no single summary statistic (e.g., long-run mean) is numerically reported for this baseline condition.",0.3,N/A,Actions are binary; no numeric contribution amount analogous to tokens.,1,N/R,"Simulation results are reported in terms of cooperation levels and transition times, not efficiency ratios.",0.2,N/A,Group payoff trajectories are not reported for the simulations.,1,N/A,Agents are artificial; no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulations run in silico.,1,"Population of n=100 agents repeatedly matched into pairs. Only independent punishers are present at frequency Q_IP=0.30; remaining agents are non-punishers. Cooperation and punishment decisions follow the stochastic rules of the dynamic model with mistake probability ε=0.05, observation sample size m=10, belief update probability u=0.5, and initial beliefs either high (b_c=b_p=0.75) or low (b_c=b_p=0.25). Results indicate that when independent punishers alone are too rare and no conditional punishment exists, cooperation never emerges.","Synthesizes simulation setup and qualitative outcome described for Fig. 4a,b.",0.86
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2021.04.002.md,Dynamic model – Agent-based simulations with norm enforcement,"Figure 4c,d describe simulations where independent punishment is complemented with norm enforcers (Q_NE>0, Q_CP=0) to study stability of cooperation.",0.8,"Population composition: Q_IP=0.30, Q_NE>0 (exact value not reported), Q_CP=0; initial beliefs high or low (b_c=b_p=0.75 or 0.25); n=100, m=10, ε=0.05; u=0.5","Text notes that they examine cases where independent punishment is complemented with norm enforcement (no conformist punishment) at fixed Q_IP=30%; figure caption gives n, m, ε, u and initial beliefs.",0.7,False,These are numerical simulations of artificial agents.,1,False,Not a human experiment.,1,False,Computation only.,1,True,Described as simulations with specific parameter values.,1,False,Outcome obtained from simulation rather than derivation.,1,100,Simulation settings specify n=100 agents.,1,100000,Simulations used long trajectories up to 10^5 periods as noted for Fig. 5; we apply the same duration.,0.7,1,Cooperation and punishment are binary decisions.,0.9,N/A,No explicit monetary endowment or continuous contribution modeled.,1,N/A,"Simulated game is a PD-with-punishment, not a linear public goods game.",1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,N/A,"Agents’ information is modelled via beliefs b_c and b_p, not UI elements.",1,N/A,No identities in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,Agents do not have explicit knowledge of horizon.,1,N/A,"Punishment is part of game form, not a configurable experimental interface.",1,N/A,Specific numeric cost parameters are not restated for simulations.,0.4,N/A,Not quantified in the simulation description.,0.4,N/A,No rewards are used in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Endowments are not parameterized like in an experiment.,1,N/R,Figures show cooperation over time qualitatively; no single summary value reported for this condition.,0.3,N/A,"Actions are binary, not amounts.",1,N/R,"Outcomes are discussed in terms of cooperation persistence and emergence, not efficiency.",0.2,N/A,No group payoff statistics for simulations are given.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,No human,Simulations are computational.,1,"Same dynamic model as described earlier with n=100, m=10, ε=0.05, u=0.5. Population includes independent punishers at 30% and some share of norm enforcers, with no conformist punishers. Results show norm enforcement has a strong stabilizing effect when cooperation is already high, but cooperation may take a long time to emerge when starting from low initial beliefs.","Summarizes the condition and qualitative findings associated with Fig. 4c,d.",0.8
