ist punishers, but no norm enforcers. Results show that in this configuration cooperation readily emerges due to positive feedback with conformist punishment, but is unstable and the system alternates between low and high cooperation with sudden shifts.","Summarizes the simulation condition and findings for Fig. 4e,f.",0.8
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2021.04.002.md,Dynamic model – Agent-based simulations with norm enforcement and conformist punishment,"Figure 4g,h and Fig. 5 illustrate simulations where both norm enforcers and conformist punishers are present along with independent punishers.",0.8,"Population composition: Q_IP=0.30, Q_NE>0, Q_CP>0 (such that overall conditional punishers’ frequency held constant across comparisons); initial beliefs high or low (b_c=b_p=0.75 or 0.25); n=100, m=10, ε=0.05; u=0.5","Text states that with both conformist punishers and norm enforcers present, while keeping overall frequency of conditional punishers the same, cooperation rapidly emerges and remains stable.",0.7,False,These are computational simulations.,1,False,Not a human experiment.,1,False,Simulations only.,1,True,Condition is part of the simulation suite in Section 3.2.,1,False,"This row captures simulated, not derived, results.",1,100,Simulation uses n=100 agents.,1,100000,Simulation durations up to 10^5 periods in Fig. 5; applied to this condition.,0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect and punish/not punish.,0.9,N/A,No explicit divisible endowment.,1,N/A,"Game is a PD-with-punishment, not linear public good.",1,N/A,No communication between agents.,1,N/A,Information structure is purely in terms of sampled beliefs.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit horizon knowledge.,1,N/A,Punishment is inherent in the model game but not as a UI setting.,1,N/A,Costs not numerically specified in this simulation description.,0.4,N/A,Magnitude not specified.,0.4,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Endowment not parameterized.,1,N/R,Described qualitatively as rapid emergence and stable high cooperation; no explicit numeric rate given.,0.3,N/A,Binary actions; no amount.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not numerically reported.,0.2,N/A,Group payoff is not reported.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model.,1,"Dynamic model with n=100, m=10, ε=0.05, u=0.5. Population has 30% independent punishers plus a mix of norm enforcers and conformist punishers, with overall fraction of conditional punishers held constant across comparisons. Simulations show that conformist punishers help cooperation emerge from low levels, while norm enforcers stabilize high cooperation, yielding rapid emergence and long-term stability of cooperation.","Summarizes simulation configuration and main qualitative outcome for Fig. 4g,h and associated discussion.",0.8
10.1016_j.jebo.2017.06.006.md,Zero Monitoring treatment,One of three treatments where insiders cannot observe or sanction any outsiders; serves as control.,1,Outsider observability/monitoring level: insiders observe 0 of 3 outsiders and cannot sanction outsiders,Treatment variable is the ability of insiders to observe/sanction outsiders; in Zero Monitoring they observe none.,1,True,"Laboratory experiment with 96 student subjects, 8 per session.",1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted in a lab with z-Tree and university student pool.,1,False,All decisions made by human subjects; no agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,Paper includes analytical benchmarks but main results are from lab experiment.,1,8,Each experiment involved a group of eight subjects.,1,15,Subjects participated in fifteen periods of the experiment.,1,1,Subjects invest any whole number of tokens from 0 to 12; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment is 12 tokens privately held; subjects decide how much to invest in the common account.,1,N/R,Nonlinear common pool production F(G)=aG-bG^2; no constant marginal per capita return.,1,1,Insiders (Group 1) can communicate via online chat in every odd-numbered period.,1,1,"After harvest, all see total harvests of insiders and outsiders and aggregate payoffs.",1,1,In sanctions stage insiders see others’ group and ID numbers with harvests when assigning deductions.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists; only costly deductions.,1,1,Instructions state there are 15 periods; typical z-Tree implementation and explicit statement of 15 periods implies subjects know horizon.,0.8,1,Insiders can assign deduction points to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,1,Each deduction point assigned costs the punisher 1 experimental dollar.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces target’s payoff by 3 experimental dollars (1:3 scheme).,1,0,Design only allows negative deductions; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mec