tution described for R-game.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same linear public-good stage as P-game; payoff function retains 20−g_i term each period.,1,N/R,Paper gives time paths and regression coefficients but not a single overall normalized contribution rate for all R_p groups.,0.2,N/R,"Average contributions are reported by type-composition and period (e.g., 17.2 tokens in some groups), but no single overall mean for this condition.",0.3,N/R,Group payoff relative to maximum is not summarized as a single efficiency statistic for R_p.,0.1,N/R,Only qualitative or comparative results are given; no single average group payoff for partner protocol.,0.1,Germany,Same sessions and subject pool as other games at BonnEconLab.,0.9,N/R,No descriptive age statistics reported.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition overall not reported.,0.1,University students,Subjects are from the BonnEconLab ORSEE pool; typically university students.,0.8,On site,Laboratory at University of Bonn.,1,"Ten-period repeated VCM with peer punishment. Groups of four remain fixed over all 10 periods (partner design), but partners are 'not identifiable between periods'. In each period, contribute part of 20-token endowment to a public good (MPCR 0.4), then may assign costly punishment points (cost 1, effect 3) to each other group member. After each period, subjects see results and payoffs from that period.",Sections 2.3–2.4 and later results describe the R_p structure.,1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2018.06.010.md,R-game – stranger protocol (R_s),Authors distinguish R-game with stranger protocol (R_s): subjects are rematched within matching-groups each period.,1,Repeated public-goods game with peer punishment under stranger matching: random rematching within fixed matching-groups of eight each period.,Independent variation relative to R_p is matching protocol: strangers vs partners.,1,True,Human subjects in repeated PG with punishment under stranger design.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with specified matching and payoffs.,1,True,Same BonnEconLab setup.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,4,"Although matching groups have 8 subjects, each period subjects are divided into groups of four for the public-goods interaction (consistent with payoff function and standard design).",0.9,10,R-game is played for 10 periods under both matching protocols.,1,1,Same contribution decision as other games: 0–20 tokens continuous integer choice.,1,0,Endowment provided privately each period; contribution is an active choice.,1,0.4,Payoff function identical to P-game: same marginal per capita return.,1,0,"No mention of communication; multiple matching-groups are used to limit reputational effects, not to allow chat.",0.8,1,"As in partner protocol, 'Results and payoffs from the R-game were revealed after each period.'",0.8,0,No statement that specific punishers are identified; standard stranger PG punishment maintains anonymity.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite horizon of 10 periods is part of instructions.,0.9,1,R-game payoff function equals P-game with peer punishment.,1,1,Same punishment parameters as P-game: 1 token cost per point.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces target income by 3 tokens.,1,0,No rewarding.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same per-period endowment as other VCM stages (20 tokens) implied by unchanged payoff function.,1,N/R,Figures show contribution dynamics and regressions by type composition but not a single overall normalized rate for R_s.,0.2,N/R,"Paper reports contributions by type prevalence (e.g., lower than in partner design) but not a single grand mean.",0.3,N/R,No global efficiency number reported for stranger protocol.,0.1,N/R,Only relative comparisons; no single mean payoff for R_s.,0.1,Germany,Same multi-part experiment at BonnEconLab.,0.9,N/R,No ages reported.,0.1,N/R,Overall gender composition not given.,0.1,University students,Same student subject pool.,0.8,On site,Lab sessions at BonnEconLab.,1,"Ten-period repeated VCM with peer punishment under stranger matching: at start of R-game, subjects assigned to matching-groups of eight. Each period, they are randomly rematched within their matching-group into groups (of four) to play the VCM with peer punishment (same 1:3 technology). After each period, outcomes and payoffs are revealed. Subjects know groups will be randomly re-shuffled each period but not the number of matching groups.",Section 2.3 explains stranger protocol and matching-group structure.,1
