10.1016_j.chaos.2018.03.036.md,"Baseline spatial PGG without punishment (α = 0, no tolerance effect)","Condition described as the traditional/original PGG obtained by setting punishment fine α=0, which also nullifies the tolerance mechanism.",0.9,Punishment fine α = 0; tolerance threshold M irrelevant (no punishment); synergy factor r varied; selection intensity K varied,"This condition is used as the traditional case with α=0; M has no effect; r and K are scanned in figures (e.g., r in Fig. 2, K in Fig. 5).",0.9,False,"Study is a Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice with agents, not human subjects.",1,False,No controlled experiment with participants; purely computational model.,1,False,No physical lab or human subjects; only simulations.,1,True,They run Monte Carlo simulations on L×L lattices with agents.,1,False,No explicit analytical or closed-form derivations; results are numerical.,0.9,40000,"Population size ranges from 200×200 to 400×400; figures with snapshots and phase diagrams use 400×400 lattice, so 400×400 = 160000; however, baseline general simulations mention 200×200 to 400×400. Using typical main-case lattice 400×400 would give 160000; but no single fixed size for all, so value is N/R.",0,100000,"They run 1×10^5 Monte Carlo steps and average over the last 10,000 for equilibrium.",0.9,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed cost c=1; defectors contribute 0; agents choose between C and D, not a continuous amount, so contribution choice is all-or-nothing between 0 and 1.",1,0,"In each round, cooperators pay cost c=1 from their own resource to public pool; endowment is effectively fully private until contributed, so default proportion in public fund is 0.",0.8,N/R,"Group size is k_i+1; on regular lattice with degree 4, group size G=5 and total contribution is multiplied by r and split equally; MPCR would be r/G, but specific numeric r varies and MPCR not explicitly given as a fixed parameter per condition.",0.6,0,No communication among agents is modeled.,1,0,Agents update strategies based on payoffs of a randomly chosen neighbor only; no mention of global or summary feedback.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition (α=0).,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,0,Agents are simple adaptive automata; no interface conveying number of rounds.,1,0,"They state the model recovers the traditional version as α=0, where punishment and tolerance lose corresponding effects.",1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,"Only punishment is modeled, no reward mechanism described.",1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes fixed cost c=1; this is effectively the per-round endowment available to contribute.,0.8,N/R,"They report fraction of cooperators ρ_c, not normalized contribution rate per player beyond strategy fraction.",0.8,N/R,"Only strategy fractions are reported, no explicit per-player contribution totals per round.",0.8,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to maximum is reported.,1,N/R,"Payoff formulas are given, but no numeric group payoff values are reported as outcomes.",0.8,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,No human,Study is entirely based on Monte Carlo simulations with no human subjects.,1,"Spatial public goods game on regular L×L lattice (degree 4, G=5 overlapping groups), binary strategies (C,D), contribution cost c=1, synergy factor r (1<r<G), synchronous Monte Carlo updating with Fermi rule H = 1/(1+exp[(P_i−P_j)/K]).","Summarized from Methods section describing lattice, group size, payoff function, and update rule.",1
