10.1038_s41598-021-85814-1.md,Egoistic peer punishment (EP) – Fermi updating,"Distinct simulation condition where only egoistic peer punishers PB are available, analyzed mainly in Figures 1c,d, 2a–d, 3 (EP panels), 4 (EP row), 5 (PB series), using Fermi updating as baseline.",0.9,"Punishment mode = peer; Punisher motivation = egoistic (PB only); Fine β ∈ (0,1]; Cost α ∈ [0,1]; Synergy factor r ∈ {2.0, 3.5, 3.8, 4.0}; Fermi updating rule with K = 0.5",Methods and Results describe EP separately with these parameter ranges and updating rule.,0.96,False,Study is a Monte Carlo simulation on a spatial lattice with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational simulations.,1,False,No physical lab or subjects; only simulations.,1,True,They explicitly use Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice-based public goods game model.,1,False,"Although payoff equations are given, main results are from numerical Monte Carlo simulations, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,250000,"L is chosen from 400 to 4000; typical detailed snapshots use L = 1000. A 4-neighbor lattice with L = 1000 has 1000^2 = 1,000,000 players, but for general results they mention L from 400 to 4000. Using a single fixed count would be arbitrary; however, the only concrete L repeatedly specified for detailed EP dynamics is L = 1000, so playerCount = 1000^2.",0.4,1000000,They use a relaxation time of 10^6 Monte Carlo iterations before averaging; this is the effective number of simulated steps.,0.8,1,"Each cooperator (C or P) contributes a fixed amount c; defectors contribute 0. There is no choice of partial contribution; thus contributions are all-or-nothing, but the variable as defined asks whether players can contribute any amount; here they cannot, so coding 1 (any amount) would be wrong. However, schema defines 1 as 'can contribute any amount'; in this model they cannot, so value should be 0. I therefore set 0.",0.9,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation; purely abstract tokens in simulation.,1,N/R,"They specify group size g = 5 and multiply total contributions by r, but do not define a marginal per capita return relative to individual contributions; MPCR could be r/5 but this mapping is never stated as MPCR.",0.5,N/A,No human interaction; communication is not modeled.,1,N/R,Simulation does not include user interfaces or feedback descriptions in these terms.,1,N/A,Agents have no identity visibility concept; punishment is determined mechanically.,1,N/A,No explicit reward identity; only payoff updates.,1,N/R,No user-facing display; iterations are internal to the simulation.,1,1,EP explicitly models peer punishment by egoistic punishers PB.,1,0,"Cost parameter is α, varied from 0 to 1. Since this row summarizes the condition family rather than a single α, a single numeric value cannot represent all cases; but the schema disallows N/R for numeric. Using 0 as a placeholder tied to α’s lower bound, with explanation.",0.2,0,"Fine per defector per punisher is β * r / 5, but β and r vary; a single constant cannot capture this. Set 0 as placeholder with explanation.",0.2,1,"Egoistic punishers retrieve β * r / g from defectors as compensation, functioning as a material reward.",1,0,Reward is not bought separately; it is transferred from defector fines. No per-unit reward cost is defined; cost is α (punishment).,0.3,0,"Egoistic punisher gain per defector is β * r / 5, but β and r vary; no single scalar. 0 as placeholder.",0.2,N/R,"Contribution c is used symbolically but its numeric value (e.g., c=1) is not specified explicitly.",0.4,N/R,"They report frequencies of strategies (ρC, ρD, ρPB), not normalized contribution rates; all cooperators and punishers contribute fully, defectors 0.",0.5,N/R,"Contribution per cooperator is c, but c’s numeric value is not given.",0.4,N/R,They discuss cooperation level and payoffs qualitatively but do not report efficiency as total payoff divided by maximum.,0.5,N/R,Payoff formulas are given but no specific numeric equilibrium group payoff is reported.,0.4,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All work is computational on artificial agents.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a four-neighbor lattice with overlapping groups of size g = 5; strategies: C, D, PB; PB contributes c, pays cost α * ND to punish defectors in group and receives β * r / 5 per defector, while defectors lose β * r / 5 per PB.",Summarized from Methods equations for EP and lattice description.,1
