10.1038_s41598-020-73855-x.md,Evolutionary simulation – baseline reactive memory-one mutants only,Paper describes an evolutionary simulation without CAPRI and TFT-ATFT as a baseline where mutants are reactive memory-one strategies (Fig. 3a).,1,"Mutant strategy space restricted to discretized reactive memory-one strategies (p_c, p_d) ∈ {0,0.1,…,1}×{0,0.1,…,1}; environmental parameters b (benefit-to-cost ratio), N (population size), and e (error rate) varied one at a time with defaults b=3, N=50, e=1e-3.","Simulation section states that mutants are reactive memory-one strategies with probabilities discretized in steps of 0.1 and that b, N, and e are varied individually with given default values.",1,False,No human participants; purely computational evolutionary simulations and analytical calculations.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a numerical simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; simulations run on supercomputers.,1,True,Authors describe exhaustive enumeration and evolutionary simulations implemented computationally.,1,True,"Paper includes closed-form derivations (e.g., fixation probability formula, inequalities) in addition to simulations.",1,2,Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma game between two players Alice and Bob throughout the paper.,1,0,Infinite-horizon repeated game with long-term average payoffs; number of rounds not fixed (stationary distribution as e→0). Use 0 to indicate not fixed.,0.7,1,"In IPD each move is binary cooperate/defect; there is no continuous contribution level, so all-or-nothing public good framing is inapplicable; coded as can choose either pure action each round.",0.6,N/A,Prisoner’s Dilemma is not framed as allocating an endowment to a public good; no default contribution proportion defined.,1,N/R,"No marginal per capita return is defined because the setting is a 2-player PD, not a linear public goods game.",1,0,No communication or messaging between agents; strategies are fully specified response rules.,1,0,Simulation agents do not observe summarized earnings; they only use prescribed strategies.,1,0,No punishment stage or identity revelation; only actions c/d.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond game payoffs.,1,0,Agents are programmed strategies not informed about total or remaining rounds; infinite-horizon limit.,0.8,0,"No separate costly punishment action; defection is part of the base PD payoff matrix, not a distinct punishment technology.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism implemented.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism beyond standard cooperation payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Game is defined via payoff matrix (R,T,S,P); no per-round endowment specified.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported in terms of strategy abundance and payoffs, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,Binary actions (c/d) in PD; no numeric contribution amounts reported.,1,N/R,They do not compute efficiency as realized payoff divided by cooperative maximum; only report payoff distributions and abundances.,1,N/R,"Figures show distributions/abundances, not explicit aggregate group payoffs.",1,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from simulations and analytical work; no experimental session.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma without discounting; simplified parametrization with benefit b and cost 1 so that R=b−1, T=b, S=−1, P=0; agents have implementation error with probability e; population evolves via Moran imitation process with selection strength σ, mutation rate μ low, and mutants drawn from discretized reactive memory-one strategy space.",Summarized directly from the evolutionary simulation description in the Results section.,1
