10.1038_s41598-020-73855-x.md,Payoff distribution simulation – Alice uses TFT-ATFT against random probabilistic memory-three co-players,Figure 2b right panel shows payoff distribution when Alice’s strategy is TFT-ATFT and Bob is sampled from probabilistic memory-three strategies.,1,"Alice fixed to deterministic memory-two strategy TFT-ATFT; Bob’s strategy drawn uniformly at random from the 64-dimensional unit hypercube of probabilistic memory-three strategies; elementary payoffs (R,T,S,P)=(3,4,0,1); error probability e>0 small.","Results: 'Figure 2b shows the distribution of payoffs when Alice's strategy is CAPRI (left) or TFT-ATFT (right), whereas Bob adopts one of probabilistic memory-three strategies uniformly at random. The elementary payoffs are (R,T,S,P)=(3,4,0,1)'.",1,False,Strategy-payoff mapping computed numerically; no human subjects.,1,False,Simulation rather than experimental study.,1,False,No laboratory participants.,1,True,Payoff distributions obtained by simulating IPD for many randomly drawn co-player strategies.,1,False,"This condition is reported as numerical payoff sampling, not closed-form analysis.",0.7,2,IPD between Alice and Bob.,1,0,Long-run average payoffs; rounds effectively infinite.,0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect actions per round.,0.9,N/A,Not framed as splitting an endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR used.,1,0,No communication.,1,0,"Agents rely on last actions, not feedback summaries.",1,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,0,Stationary average payoffs; no finite horizon displayed.,0.8,0,No explicit costly punishment mechanism aside from defection in PD.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game specified by payoff matrix, not endowment.",1,N/R,"Results reported as payoff distributions, not contribution shares.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts reported.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric is computed; only payoff scatter.,1,N/R,Group payoff statistics across pairs are not reported numerically.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computer simulation only.,1,"Iterated PD with payoffs (R,T,S,P)=(3,4,0,1); Alice follows TFT-ATFT, a deterministic memory-two friendly rival based on Tit-for-Tat and Anti-Tit-for-Tat; Bob’s strategy is a probabilistic memory-three strategy with cooperation probabilities drawn uniformly from [0,1]^64; implementation error e>0 (small). Payoffs π_A and π_B are computed for each sampled Bob, yielding a payoff distribution concentrated near the diagonal, reflecting TFT-ATFT’s tendency to equalize payoffs.",Summarized from description of Fig. 2b and TFT-ATFT properties.,1
10.1007_s10955-018-2012-2.md,Basic egalitarian model without punishment or foresight (agent-based simulation),Single homogeneous-condition simulation model in Sect. 2.1 covering egalitarian groups with parameters as in Table 1 and Fig. 1.,0.8,"Group size N ∈ {4, 8, 16}; game frequencies ratio E ∈ {1:0, 3:1, 1:1, 1:3, 0:1}; cultural group selection probability E3 ∈ {0.0, 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2}; number of candidate strategies K ∈ {1,2,4,8,16}; precision parameter λ ∈ {10,20,40,80,∞}. No punishment (y_i not active) and no foresight (ω=0).",Sect. 2.1 and Table 1 list the parameter ranges; text explicitly states no punishment and no foresight.,0.9,False,Pure agent-based simulations; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory setting; computational model only.,1,True,Described repeatedly as agent-based simulations with parameters in Table 1.,1,False,"While some analytical expressions are quoted, main results here are numerical simulations.",0.9,8,"Representative group size; simulations use N ∈ {4,8,16} but no single fixed N, so 8 chosen as mid; exact N varies across runs.",0.4,0,"Continuous-time, event-driven model without discrete rounds; effective number of events not specified.",0.2,1,"Effort x_i is a nonnegative continuous variable; agents can choose any effort ≥0, not just all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment or public- vs private-account structure; only continuous effort cost.,1,N/R,"Model uses public-goods contest formulation with parameters B, b, c, X0 but no marginal per capita return in standard PG game sense.",0.1,N/A,No human participants or communication mechanism.,1,0,Agents are algorithmic; paper does not define interfaces or summaries analogous to experimental feedback.,0.2,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the basic egalitarian model.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism is modeled.,1,0,No human interface; continuous-time simulation so this notion does not apply.,1,0,Section 2.1 explicitly describes a model without punishment.,1,N/A,Punishment is not enabled in this model.,1,N/A,Punishment is not enabled in this model.,1,0,Only public good benefits via P_j; no separate reward action.,1,N/A,No targeted rewarding modeled.,1,N/A,No targeted rewarding modeled.,1,N/R,Model specifies payoff as 1 + benefit - cost * x_i but not a per-round token endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Results report total effort X, not normalized per-agent contribution fraction relative to an explicit endowment.",0.2,N/R,Figures give qualitative or averaged X but not explicit numeric values suitable to record as a single DV.,0.2,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximum possible payoff is reported.,0.1,N/R,"Paper focuses on efforts X and relative payoffs π, not absolute group payoff numbers.",0.1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model only.,1,"Continuous-effort collective action contest: us-vs-nature games with payoff fraction P_j = X_j/(X_j+X0) and us-vs-them games with P_j = X_j/(X_j+X_k); individuals update strategies via myopic optimization over K candidate efforts with Quantal Response (parameter λ); events occur with probabilities E1, E2, E3 (E1+E2+E3=1).","Summarized from Sect. 2.1, including payoff and update rules.",0.9
10.1007_s10955-018-2012-2.md,Basic hierarchical model without punishment or foresight (agent-based simulation),"Sect. 2.1 ""Results with within-group heterogeneity"" and Figs. 2–3 describe hierarchical groups where individuals differ in strength and reward shares; still no punishment or foresight.",0.9,"Hierarchy steepness β ∈ {1,2,4}; group size N ∈ {4,8,16}; game frequencies ratio E ∈ {1:0,3:1,1:1,1:3,0:1}; benefit b ∈ {0.0:0.25:2}; cost c ∈ {0.5,1.0}; precision λ ∈ {10,20,40,80,∞}; number of candidate strategies K (often 2). No punishment (y_i not active) and no foresight (ω=0).",Parameter sets from Table 1; hierarchical structure and β described in text and Fig. 2–3 captions; punishment and foresight added only later.,0.9,False,Purely simulated heterogeneous agents.,1,False,No empirical manipulation; computational simulations instead.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab with human subjects.,1,True,Described as agent-based simulations; results in Figs. 2–3.,1,False,Uses numerical simulation rather than closed-form analysis for hierarchical case.,0.9,8,"Figures 2–3 use N=4,8,16 and specifically N=8 in Fig. 3; 8 is a representative size though simulations vary N.",0.5,0,"Continuous-time, event-based dynamics; no fixed number of rounds specified.",0.2,1,Effort x_i is continuous ≥0; not all-or-nothing.,1,N/A,No token endowment; only continuous effort with cost.,1,N/R,"Public good returns determined by B, b, c, X0 and contest functions but no per-capita marginal return reported.",0.1,N/A,No human subjects or communication.,1,0,No interface; internal state only in simulation.,0.2,N/A,No punishment in basic hierarchical model.,1,N/A,No explicit interpersonal reward mechanism.,1,0,No discrete rounds in a user interface.,1,0,Punishment is introduced later in Sect. 2.2; this subsection explicitly lacks punishment.,1,N/A,Punishment inactive.,1,N/A,Punishment inactive.,1,0,Only automatic benefit sharing via v_i; no strategic rewarding.,1,N/A,No reward action exists.,1,N/A,No reward action exists.,1,N/R,Payoff baseline 1 plus benefit minus cost c x_i; no fixed token endowment per event.,0.2,N/R,"Reported outcomes are efforts per rank and total X, not normalized by an explicit endowment.",0.3,N/R,Figures are qualitative/graphical without specific numerical X or x_i listed.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0.1,N/R,They discuss relative payoffs π_i but not explicit group payoff levels.,0.1,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model with heterogeneous strengths and reward shares.,1,"Same continuous-effort us-vs-nature and us-vs-them contests as egalitarian model, but individuals have fixed strengths s_i ~ U[0,1] and receive shares v_i = s_i^β / Σ s_k^β; dominant individuals tend to contribute most of X, sometimes showing 'altruistic bully' effect (dominant contributes much yet can receive lower payoff). Strategy updating via Quantal Response with λ and K candidate efforts.",Summarized from Sect. 2.1 'Results with within-group heterogeneity' and model definition around v_i.,0.9
10.1007_s10955-018-2012-2.md,"Hierarchical model with punishment but without foresight (ω = 0, punishment enabled)","Sect. 2.2 introduces punishment in hierarchical groups, still using myopic optimization (ω=0). They note punishment occurs only by error for low λ.",0.9,"Punishment parameters: punishment threshold trait y_i active; incurred cost κ_ij = e (y_i − x_j) with e=1; punisher cost δ_ij = κ_ij S_ij, S_ij = S0 exp[φ (s_j − s_i)], S0=1, φ=5; punishment condition s_i > s_j + ξ with ξ ~ N(0, ε), ε=0.1. Hierarchy steepness β ∈ {0,1,2,4}; group size N ∈ {4,8,16}; other parameters as in Table 1; foresight parameter ω=0.","Punishment structure and parameters from Table 1 and Sect. 2.2; ω not yet introduced until Sect. 3, so equals 0 here.",0.9,False,Model is simulated with artificial agents.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational simulation.,1,False,Not in a physical lab environment.,1,True,Agent-based model run numerically; described as 'our numerical results for this model'.,1,False,"Behavior studied via simulations, not purely analytical.",0.9,8,"Representative N within {4,8,16}; specific value varies across parameter sweeps.",0.4,0,Continuous-time simulation; no set number of discrete rounds.,0.2,1,Effort x_i continuous ≥0; contributions not all-or-nothing.,1,N/A,Model lacks discrete endowments; only continuous effort inputs.,1,N/R,Public good production defined via nonlinear P(X); no marginal per capita return specified.,0.1,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,"Simulation does not emulate on-screen feedback; agents compute using full state, but not via 'summary displays'.",0.2,N/A,No concept of identity feedback to human participants; simulation agents inherently know who they punish but this variable is defined for human experiments.,0.2,N/A,No explicit rewarding stage or identity signaling as in lab games.,0.2,0,No explicit information display; continuous simulation.,1,1,"Sect. 2.2 explicitly 'adds the possibility of punishment' using y_i, κ_ij, δ_ij.",1,1,"Each unit κ_ij of harm costs δ_ij = κ_ij S_ij to punisher; base scaling S0=1; e=1. There is no single linear 'cost per point', but the core harm scaling is 1.",0.4,1,"Harm to punished j is κ_ij = e (y_i − x_j); with e=1, harm per unit of threshold-minus-effort is 1.",0.6,0,"Model includes only punishment, not positive rewards.",1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,Individual payoff formula Π_i = 1 + b P(X) v_i − c x_i − punishment terms; no initial token endowment specified.,0.2,N/R,No normalized contribution fraction relative to a maximum or endowment defined.,0.2,N/R,Analysis describes qualitative presence of punishment and low contributions by non-dominant individuals; no fixed numeric outcome reported.,0.2,N/R,"They note punishment only occurs 'by error' and does not establish cooperation, but do not give efficiency numbers.",0.1,N/R,Only qualitative statements about payoffs; no explicit averages reported.,0.1,N/A,No human participants involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation of hierarchical groups with punishment.,1,"Hierarchical groups with strengths s_i and shares v_i = s_i^β / Σ s_k^β. Each agent has production effort x_i ≥0 and punishment threshold y_i ≥0. Agent i punishes j if x_j < y_i and s_i > s_j + ξ (ξ~N(0,ε)); punishment inflicted reduces j's payoff by κ_ij = e (y_i − x_j), and costs i an amount δ_ij = κ_ij S_ij with S_ij = S0 exp[φ (s_j − s_i)]. Strategy updates over K candidate (x,y) pairs via Quantal Response with precision λ, but with ω=0 only immediate payoffs are considered; punishment occurs only due to low-precision errors and does not robustly solve second-order free-riding.","Summarized from Sect. 2.2 including functional forms for κ_ij, δ_ij, and punishment condition.",0.95
10.1007_s10955-018-2012-2.md,"Hierarchical model with punishment and foresight (full model, ω > 0)","Sect. 3 introduces foresight in strategy revision. Figs. 4–6 analyze behavior for various ω, λ, K, E, E3, N, b, β with punishment enabled.",1,"Foresight parameter ω ∈ {0, 0.25, 0.5}; precision λ ∈ {40, ∞}; number of candidate strategies K ∈ {1,2,4}; game frequencies ratio E ∈ {1:0,1:1,0:1}; cultural group selection probability E3 ∈ {0.00,0.05,0.10,0.15,0.20}; group size N ∈ {4,8,16} (e.g., N=8 in Figures 4–6); benefit b ∈ {0.25,...,2.0} (e.g., b=1.0 in core figures); hierarchy steepness β ∈ {1,2,4}; punishment parameters as in Table 1 (e=1, φ=5, S0=1, ε=0.1).",Ranges given in Table 1 and in captions of Figs. 4–6; ω is the new key variable controlling weight of future payoff.,1,False,"Study uses simulated agents, not human participants.",1,False,No real-world experimental control; only computational parameter sweeps.,1,False,No physical laboratory study.,1,True,Described as agent-based model with simulations summarised in multiple figures.,1,False,Key results obtained numerically; some heuristic/analytical comments but not a purely analytical model.,0.9,8,Core figures (4–6) use N=8 unless varied; N also varied to 4 and 16 in some analyses.,0.7,0,"Continuous-time, event-driven simulations; no discrete round count.",0.2,1,Production effort x_i is continuous; agents can choose any nonnegative amount.,1,N/A,No discrete endowments or initial allocation between accounts.,1,N/R,"Public good benefit via b, B, P(X) but no direct marginal per capita return analogous to PG experiments.",0.1,N/A,No human agents or communication channel.,1,0,Simulation agents access payoff functions directly; there is no experimental UI with summaries.,0.2,N/A,Identity revelation is not defined as an experimental interface feature; agents internally target individuals by index.,0.2,N/A,No separate reward action or identity signaling as in lab settings.,0.2,0,No round display to subjects; simulation runs for a number of time steps.,1,1,"Full model includes punishment via y_i, κ_ij, δ_ij; Figures show 'punishment inflicted' and 'punishment incurred'.",1,1,Harm κ_ij costs δ_ij = κ_ij S_ij to punisher; baseline S0=1 gives unit baseline cost per harm scaled by exp[φ(s_j−s_i)].,0.5,1,Payoff reduction to punished j is κ_ij = e (y_i − x_j) with e=1; one unit increase in (y_i−x_j) causes one unit more damage.,0.7,0,No rewarding action beyond baseline benefit allocation v_i.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Per-event payoff uses baseline 1 plus benefit minus costs and punishment; no explicit resource endowment.,0.2,N/R,Outcomes are reported as total group effort X and individual efforts by rank; no normalization to a maximum contribution is specified.,0.3,N/R,Paper gives qualitative and graphical results for X and x_i; no single numeric summary suitable for DV is reported.,0.2,N/R,"They discuss that foresight can reduce absolute payoffs due to punishment costs, but do not report efficiency ratios.",0.1,N/R,Results use relative payoffs π_i and qualitative trends; no explicit group payoff averages.,0.1,N/A,No human participants in the simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results generated by an agent-based simulation model.,1,"Same hierarchical collective action game with strengths s_i and shares v_i = s_i^β / Σ s_k^β, continuous efforts x_i, and punishment thresholds y_i. Strategy revision uses foresight: for a candidate strategy (x_i', y_i'), agent i evaluates expected payoff at next event Π_i^(1) and at the subsequent event Π_i^(2) assuming group-mates best-respond, then maximizes utility U_i = (1−ω) Π_i^(1) + ω Π_i^(2) via Quantal Response with precision λ over K candidate strategies. Punishment: if x_j < y_i and s_i > s_j + ξ, j loses κ_ij = e (y_i − x_j) and i pays δ_ij = κ_ij S_ij where S_ij = S0 exp[φ (s_j − s_i)]. Events include 'us vs nature' and 'us vs them' contests with probabilities E1 and E2, and cultural group selection events with probability E3. Foresight (ω>0) leads to stronger individuals specializing in punishment, weaker individuals increasing contributions, and emergent division of labor.","Summarized from Sects. 3.1–3.2 describing foresight, punishment, and event structure.",1
