esent.,1,0,Only costly punishment is modeled; no reward mechanism mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,Simulation of PD payoffs without explicit individual endowments.,1,N/R,"They report counts of cooperators and defectors, not normalized contribution rates.",0,N/R,Binary strategies C/D; no token contributions measured.,0,N/R,They report average payoff but not normalized efficiency relative to a theoretical maximum.,0,N/R,Only time series plots of average payoff are given; no single numerical summary at final generation is reported.,0,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results come from computational simulations of agents on networks.,1,"Spatial prisoner's dilemma on a regular 1D lattice with periodic boundary conditions, average degree ⟨k⟩=4, N=1000, payoff matrix A=[[1,0],[b,0]] with b=1.5; initial mix ~50% cooperators/defectors; strategies updated by copying the neighbor (or self) with maximum payoff each generation; P(i) not normalized by degree.",Summarizes the model description in the Model and Methods sections for this condition.,1
10.1038_srep25413.md,"Regular topology, ⟨k⟩=4, with probabilistic peer-punishment",Condition described in Results for regular topology with average degree 4 and proposed punishment (Fig. 2b).,1,Topology=regular 1D lattice with ⟨k⟩=4; Punishment=present (proposed probabilistic peer-punishment based on payoff difference); b=1.5; r=0.15,Independent factors relative to baseline are topology (regular) and presence of probabilistic punishment with coefficient r=0.15; b fixed at 1.5.,1,False,Simulated agents on a lattice; no humans.,1,False,Computational simulation study.,1,False,No lab-based human experiment.,1,True,Authors refer to simulation runs and generations over N=1000 players.,1,False,"Results are numeric simulations, not purely analytical derivations.",1,1000,Main simulations use N=1000 for all presented topologies except where explicitly varied in Supplementary Information.,1,300,Time series and averages reported up to 300 generations for these figures.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect strategies; no partial contribution levels.,0.7,N/A,No endowment or contribution decision in a public-goods sense.,1,N/A,"Pairwise PD, not a public goods game.",1,N/A,No human agents or communication.,1,N/A,No interface; only internal simulation state.,1,N/A,No notion of displayed identities to humans.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or human interface.,1,N/A,Simulation generations are not shown to participants.,1,1,Condition includes the proposed probabilistic peer-punishment.,1,0.15,Coefficient of punishment r=0.15 multiplies P(i) as the punisher's payoff loss per punished neighbor; treated as per-unit cost.,0.8,0.15,Punished player j loses r·P(i); same coefficient r=0.15 determines impact per unit of punisher payoff sacrificed.,0.8,0,"Only punishment is modelled, no reward.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit endowment; payoffs accumulate from PD interactions.,1,N/R,"Results focus on number of cooperators and defectors, not normalized contributions.",0,N/R,No token contribution amounts defined; only strategy choice.,0,N/R,Only average payoff time series reported; no normalized efficiency statistic.,0,N/R,Average payoff is plotted but not given as a specific numeric value at a particular generation.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely computational simulations.,1,"Spatial prisoner's dilemma on regular 1D lattice (⟨k⟩=4, N=1000, periodic boundary). Payoff matrix A=[[1,0],[b,0]] with b=1.5. Initially ~50% cooperators/defectors randomly distributed. After PD interactions, players may probabilistically punish higher-payoff defectors among neighbors: if P(i)<P(j)≤2P(i), player i punishes j with probability q_i(j)=(P(j)−P(i))/P(i), otherwise q_i(j)=1 if P(j)>2P(i). When punishment occurs, P(i)'=P(i)−rP(i), P(j)'=P(j)−rP(i), with r=0.15 and P' floored at zero. Then each player copies the strategy of the highest-payoff individual in its neighborhood (including self); synchronous updating.",Summarizes the full model including probabilistic punishment rules specific to this condition.,1
