10.1111_j.1749-6632.2009.04503.x.md,Study 2 – One-shot three-person public good game with punishment (baseline condition),Only one condition is described for Study 2: participants play a one-shot three-person public good game 10 times with a punishment option; no variants are distinguished.,0.7,Others' contribution level to the public good (lower vs higher contributions within the same punishment-enabled public good game),"The text states that ""the lower the contribution of others, the more angry participants felt and the harsher they punished others""; the only systematically varied factor within this paradigm is the contribution level of others.",0.7,True,Human participants played a public good game and reported anger; clearly a human-subject study.,1,True,"Contributions of others and punishment behavior were measured in a controlled game repeatedly played under researcher-designed rules, consistent with an experiment rather than observation.",0.9,True,University-based psychology research with controlled game interactions is typically conducted in the lab; no indication of field or online setting is given.,0.6,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulation; human participants played the game.,1,False,No formal mathematical or purely analytical model is described for this study; it reports empirical relationships.,1,3,"Explicitly described as a ""three-person, public good game"".",1,10,"Text: ""participants played a one-shot, three-person, public good game 10 times""; interpreted as 10 independent one-shot rounds.",1,1,Public good games typically allow any contribution from 0 up to the endowment; no mention of an all-or-nothing restriction.,0.6,0,"In standard public good games, endowments are initially in private accounts and participants decide how much to contribute; the paper follows this tradition and gives no indication of an alternative default.",0.6,N/R,No information about the multiplier or marginal per capita return is provided.,0,0,No mention of communication or discussion; default assumption is that standard public good experiments do not allow chat unless specified.,0.6,1,"Participants must know others' contributions to be able to punish them based on ""the lower the contribution of others""; this implies feedback about others' behavior.",0.7,0,The article does not state that punishees learn who punished them; standard altruistic punishment public good designs are often anonymous with respect to punisher identity.,0.5,0,No rewarding mechanism is mentioned in Study 2.,1,1,"Participants are told they ""played ... 10 times"", suggesting they knew the total number of rounds. Most repeated public good experiments inform subjects of the fixed round length.",0.6,1,"The study explicitly measures how harshly participants ""punished others"" in the public good game.",1,N/R,"No details are provided about the cost structure of punishment (e.g., tokens spent per unit of fine).",0,N/R,The magnitude of punishment per unit of cost is not described.,0,0,Only punishment of others is mentioned; no positive reward or bonus option is described.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this study.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this study.,1,N/R,The per-round endowment or number of tokens given to subjects is not provided.,0,N/R,The paper reports only that lower contributions by others increase anger and punishment; no numerical average contribution rates are provided.,0,N/R,No specific token amounts or averages are reported.,0,N/R,No data on payoffs relative to the cooperative optimum are given.,0,N/R,Group payoff values or averages are not reported.,0,Netherlands,"Authors are affiliated with Dutch universities (University of Amsterdam, VU University Amsterdam). It is most likely that participants were recruited locally in the Netherlands, though not explicitly stated.",0.6,N/R,No age range or mean age is described.,0,N/R,No information on participant gender composition is provided.,0,N/R,"The text does not specify whether participants were students or another population, though university student samples are likely.",0.4,On site,"Given the time and context (psychology department studies on economic games) and no indication of online administration, an in-lab, on-site environment is the most plausible.",0.6,"One-shot, three-person public good game repeated 10 independent rounds with a punishment stage in which participants can sanction others based on their contributions; anger is measured and mediates the effect of others' low contributions on punishment.","Summarizes the described structure: ""one-shot, three-person, public good game 10 times"" with punishment, focusing on the mediating role of anger.",0.9
10.1111_j.1749-6632.2009.04503.x.md,"Study 3 – Sequential trust game with punishment, anger-primed condition",Study 3 includes two conditions distinguished by an anger prime; this entry covers the condition where participants were primed with anger before playing the sequential trust game with punishment.,0.9,Emotion prime: anger (autobiographical recall of an anger episode),The key manipulation was that half of participants recalled an anger episode whereas the other half described a normal day.,1,True,Human participants recalled emotions and then played a trust game with punishment.,1,True,The study manipulated an independent variable (anger prime) randomly and measured its effect on punishment behavior in a controlled game.,1,True,Emotion priming plus sequential trust game with punishment is standard lab methodology; no indications of field or online setting.,0.7,False,"Involves human participants, not simulated agents.",1,False,No purely analytical model; it's an empirical priming experiment.,1,2,"A sequential trust game typically involves two players (trustor and trustee). The text references ""a sequential trust game with the possibility of punishment"" and distinguishes encountering a defector vs cooperator, consistent with dyadic interaction.",0.8,6,"Explicitly: ""they played six times a sequential trust game with the possibility of punishment.""",1,1,Standard trust games allow continuous or at least multiple discrete transfer amounts rather than all-or-nothing; no restriction to binary choices is mentioned.,0.6,0,In trust games the endowment starts entirely with the first mover; there is no default transfer to the other player.,0.8,N/R,The multiplication factor on invested amounts (return from trustee) is not reported.,0,0,No mention of communication; economic trust game experiments usually prohibit chat unless explicitly allowed.,0.7,1,"Participants must observe whether the counterpart defected or cooperated in each game to decide on punishment, implying feedback about partner behavior.",0.9,0,The article does not state that the target learns who punished them; anonymity of punisher identity is common in such paradigms.,0.5,0,Only punishment is mentioned; no reward option.,1,1,"Participants are informed they will play six games; in such designs, the fixed number of repetitions is normally communicated.",0.7,1,"Described as a ""sequential trust game with the possibility of punishment""; outcome is punishment intensity.",1,N/R,No numerical description of the cost to the punisher per unit punishment is provided.,0,N/R,The fine imposed per unit of punishment is not reported.,0,0,Only punishment is discussed and measured; no mention of a rewarding mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/R,The endowment given to the first mover in each trust game is not specified.,0,N/R,The article focuses on punishment frequency/intensity after defection and does not report transfer rates as normalized contributions.,0,N/R,No numerical information on amounts sent or returned is provided.,0,N/R,No payoff or efficiency metrics are reported.,0,N/R,Neither individual nor group average payoffs are given.,0,Netherlands,"Same research team and institutional context as Studies 1 and 2 (University of Amsterdam, VU University Amsterdam), implying Dutch subjects, though not explicitly stated.",0.6,N/R,No age information is reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition details are provided.,0,N/R,"The paper does not specify participants' educational background, though likely university students.",0.4,On site,"Emotion priming and sequential trust game with punishment suggest a laboratory, on-site setting at the university.",0.7,"Participants first completed an emotion-priming task (recalling and describing an anger episode), then played six rounds of a sequential trust game with a punishment stage, encountering a defector in half the games and a cooperator in the other half; anger-primed participants punished more overall.",Summarizes the procedural details explicitly given for Study 3.,0.95
