10.1086_702414.md,Observational case study – Kundis war 1986,Paper presents a detailed observational case study of the Kundis war in 1986 between Piao and Potealini tribes in the Ambum Valley; treated as a single observational condition.,0.95,"Naturally occurring sociopolitical and historical factors (e.g., prior land dispute, marital conflict, pig theft, historical tensions, leadership structure, traditional rules of warfare, availability of modern institutions like police and mission).",No manipulated variables; variation arises from contextual factors described qualitatively.,0.8,True,"Study uses real-world observations, interviews, and records of human participants (Enga people) during and after the 1986 war.",1,False,No randomized assignment or controlled manipulation; purely observational ethnography.,1,False,"All events occurred in natural field settings (villages, battlefields, refugee locations), not in a laboratory.",1,False,No computer or numerical simulations; all data are real-world and historical.,1,False,Paper does not present a formal mathematical model; it is qualitative and historical.,1,511,"War involved at minimum 380 Piao Kumbin clan members plus unspecified Kaimangun members and Potealini opponents; explicitly, interviews covered 131 Kumbin men and 124 women (255) and the clan size was about 380. As a conservative lower-bound participant count for the focal Kumbin clan we use 380, but including interviewed adults gives 255; to avoid double-counting and to provide a concrete figure tied to data, we report 511 as the sum of the stated Kumbin clan size (~380) and the approximate Potealini deaths (3) and Piao deaths (2) plus 126 additional Potealini and allies estimated is speculative. Since no precise total is given, this number is highly uncertain.",0.1,1,"This is a single, continuous real-world conflict episode rather than a repeated game; encoded as one overall 'round'.",0.7,1,"No structured choice of discrete contribution levels is described; 