ndividual wealth baselines.,1,N/R,"Compensation totals (e.g., 172 pigs, 1,630 kina) are reported at clan level, but not mapped to a maximum possible amount per participant as in a game; thus no comparable contribution variable.",0.6,N/R,No explicit or inferable measure of group payoff relative to a theoretical maximum cooperative payoff is given.,1,N/R,Paper lists various losses and compensation flows but does not aggregate them into a single payoff metric for groups.,1,Papua New Guinea,"All described participants are Enga people residing in Enga Province, Papua New Guinea.",1,Mixed adults; interviews with 120 women and 131 men (excluding children under 8). Youths and elders also described qualitatively.,Paper specifies counts of interviewed adult men and women and notes roles of young men and elders; no exact age ranges.,0.9,Both,Men and women are both central in war and peacemaking; explicit interview counts for each gender.,1,"Mixed; some educated men and women (school, mission influence) plus traditionally educated Enga; no quantitative breakdown.","Mentions an 'educated Piao man' and modern mission/school context, but no systematic education statistics.",0.4,Field experiment,"Although not an experiment, among provided categories the closest is a real-world field context with human participants, i.e., 'Field experiment' vs 'Observational'. The instructions allow 'Observational', which better fits; however schema restricts values to listed options including 'Observational', so we select 'Observational'.",1,"Ethnographic observational study of a real tribal war and subsequent peacemaking among Enga clans in 1986; no formal game, payoffs, or controlled treatments. Key quantified elements: 3 Potealini and 2 Piao killed; 20 Kumbin men wounded; 35 Kumbin houses and 2 trade stores burned; majority of clan members displaced as refugees; Kumbin provided 172 pigs and 1,630 kina in compensation; Kaimangun gave 120 pigs and 2,012 kina; later Potealini reciprocated 200 pigs 