10.1371_journal.pcbi.1002868.md,"Two-strategy model without punishment (p=0, q=0)",Baseline condition described explicitly as the scenario without punishment (p=q=0) used to generate Figure 1 and compute Ω=0.49.,1,"Punishment parameters p=0, q=0; game payoffs T in (0,2), S in (-1,0); scale-free network structure fixed; two strategies C (punishing cooperators) and D (defectors) effectively without punishment because p=q=0; selection intensity β=0.25.",Condition is defined by absence of punishment costs/effects (p=q=0) while varying T and S on a fixed scale-free network with β=0.25.,1,False,Model is an evolutionary game-theoretic simulation on scale-free networks with no human or biological subjects.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human/animal subjects; purely computational simulations.,1,False,Not a lab study; numerical simulations only.,1,True,They generate 10^2 Barabási–Albert networks and run 2.5×10^4 simulations of evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,"Although some analytical reasoning is provided, main results here are from numerical simulations, not closed-form solutions.",0.8,1000,They state networks with 10^3 nodes; each node is a player.,1,25000,They average final fraction of cooperators over 2.5×10^4 simulations each starting from random initial conditions and running to fixation; treat each simulation as an independent evolutionary run (here encoded as number of runs since discrete 'rounds' per game are not specified).,0.3,1,"This is a 2×2 game where players choose a pure strategy C or D, not a contribution amount; in this coding, we mark 1 to indicate no continuous contribution choice constraints apply.",0.2,N/A,Prisoner’s Dilemma-payoff model with no endowment allocation or contribution stage and no human subjects.,1,N/R,"They use pairwise PD/SH payoffs (R,T,S,P) and not a linear public goods game; MPCR is not defined.",1,N/A,No human participants; communication not applicable.,1,0,Agents update strategies based only on payoff comparison with neighbors