stic punishment with q/p=0.4). C vs D yields S−0.04 to C and T−0.1 to D. They find that the effects of punishment are ambiguous: for some (T,S) punishment enhances cooperation (red areas), for others it hinders cooperation (blue areas between the vertical dashed lines in Figure 3 lower panel). Other aspects: scale-free networks (1000 nodes, average degree 4), β=0.25, initial 50-50 C and D distributed randomly, dynamics to fixation, outcome summarized via regions in T-S plane where cooperation dominates.","Condenses text referencing 'Lower panel (q=0.04, p=0.1): the effects of punishment are ambiguous ...'.",1
10.1371_journal.pcbi.1002868.md,"Two-strategy model with punishment: full p-q parameter sweep (Ω vs p,q)",Figure 4 shows Ω for a wide range of p and q values and describes the separation curve q/p≈0.54; this constitutes a broader simulation condition where p and q are systematically varied.,0.9,"Punishment parameters p and q varied over a grid (with focus on p>q for altruistic punishment); game payoffs T in (0,2), S in (-1,0); scale-free network structure fixed; two strategies C (punishing cooperators) and D (defectors); selection intensity β=0.25.",They state 'Figure 4 shows Ω for a wide range of p and q values ...' and discuss separation curve q/p=0.54 between enhanced and diminished cooperation.,0.9,False,Still numerical simulations with agents on networks.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Purely computational study.,1,True,They compute Ω numerically across the p-q parameter space.,1,False,They fit/approximate the separation curve; main quantitative results come from simulations.,0.7,1000,Same network size used throughout: 10^3 nodes.,1,25000,"They use 2.5×10^4 simulations for averaging x_ff in the baseline; likely the same here, but not separately specified.",0.4,1,Agents choose between C and D only.,0.2,N/A,No human endowment/contribution phase.,1,N/R,MPCR not defined for pairwise PD/SH.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No feedback other than local payoffs.,0.9,0,Identities not modeled.,0.9,N/A,Reward not modeled.,1,0,No horizon variable.,0.9,1,"Punishment included via p,q>0 in general; includes both altruistic (p>q) and non-altruistic regions.",1,N/R,p and q vary across a range; no single numeric value applies to this aggregated condition.,1,N/R,"As above, p varies over multiple values.",1,0,No reward mechanism introduced.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/R,No endowment parameter.,1,N/R,"They summarize outcomes as area-wide cooperation index Ω for each (p,q) pair, not per-round contributions.",1,N/R,Binary strategies only.,1,N/R,They focus on Ω rather than payoff efficiency.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoff results reported.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulations on networks.,1,"Model identical in structure to other two-strategy conditions: agents on Barabási-Albert scale-free networks (100 networks, 1000 nodes, average degree 4) play pairwise PD/SH games with R=1, P=0, and T,S varying over 0<T<2, −1<S<0. Punishing cooperators (C) pay cost q to inflict punishment p on defectors (D), altering payoffs to (S−q, T−p) in C-D interactions. Strategy updating via pairwise comparison with β=0.25, initial equal proportions of C and D, runs to fixation. For each (p,q) pair they compute area-wide cooperation index Ω as the fraction of T-S plane where final fraction of cooperators x_ff>0.5. Figure 4 shows that regions of enhanced vs diminished cooperation in (p,q) parameter space are separated by an approximately straight line with slope q/p≈0.54. Below this line (lower q/p) punishment enhances cooperation (Ω>0.49), above it punishment diminishes cooperation (Ω<0.49). They also note that in the weak selection limit (β→0) the separation line becomes exactly q/p=1.",Summarizes the broad parameter sweep description in the Results/Discussion section associated with Figure 4.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.81.036115.md,"Simulation – Asynchronous update, N=40 or 100, b=2",Paper reports simulations in well-mixed populations with asynchronous update for N=40 and 100 and defection tendency b=2; these are run under one conceptual condition: asynchronous updating with the implicit punishment rule.,0.86,"Update rule = Asynchronous; Population size N ∈ {40, 100}; Defection tendency b = 2; ε = 0.001; Initial cooperation probability per interaction = 0.5","Asynchronous update explicitly contrasted with synchronous; simulations reported for N=40 and 100, b=2 and ε=0.001, with random initial configuration consisting of 50% cooperation (i.e., probability 0.5 per interaction).",0.9,False,Study is purely computational and analytical with no human participants; described as computer simulations and mean-field analysis.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human or field subjects; only simulations of evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only numerical simulations.,1,True,Repeatedly described as simulations in well-mixed populations accompanied by mean-field equations.,1,True,Sections III.A and III.B derive mean-field differential equations and analytical approximations without additional data.,1,100,Simulations performed for N=40 and 100; Figure 3 (asynchronous) is explicitly N=100; choose 100 as representative size for this asynchronous condition.,0.72,0,Number of simulation time steps not specified; only qualitative statements about short-time and long-time regimes; thus treat as not reported.,0,1,"Each interaction’s strategy is binary (C or D), not a continuous contribution; this is a prisoner’s dilemma, not a public goods game; mapped to all-or-nothing contribution setting.",0.8,N/A,No human players and no explicit endowment or contribution structure as in public goods games; not applicable.,1,N/A,"Prisoner’s dilemma payoff structure, not a linear public good; marginal per capita return concept does not apply.",1,N/A,No human participants; communication not defined.,1,N/A,No user interface or human feedback; only simulated payoffs internal to model.,1,N/A,"No explicit punishment stage with identities; implicit punishment is an endogenous dynamical effect in the model, not a player action; no human subjects.",1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism or identities; and no human interface.,1,N/A,No human subjects and no displayed round information.,1,0,Paper describes an 'implicit punishment' effect via strategy replacement but no explicit costly punishment action or stage; mapped as no punishment mechanism in the sense of dedicated punishment decisions.,0.8,N/A,No explicit costly punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology or impact per unit cost.,1,0,Model includes only C and D actions and imitation; no separate reward stage.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,"Payoffs defined directly by PD parameters (b, ε, etc.), not by an endowment an agent chooses from.",1,N/R,"Outcome variable reported is fraction of cooperation f_c over interactions, not normalized by endowment since there is no endowment; no direct mapping defined in schema; treat as not reported.",0.3,N/R,Only binary actions (C or D) per interaction; no numeric contribution amounts as in public goods games.,0.3,N/R,"They do not report total payoffs normalized by maximum possible payoff, only fractions of cooperation.",0.4,N/R,No table or figure reports aggregate group payoff values; only cooperation fractions and some analytical equilibrium conditions.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; simulations only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from computational simulations and analytical equations; no empirical environment.,1,"Well-mixed population prisoner’s dilemma with pairwise payoffs P(D,C)=b>1 (here b=2 in simulations), P(D,D)=ε=0.001, P(C,C)=1, P(C,D)=0. Each player i has a strategy vector over N−1 opponents, S_j ∈ {C,D}. Payoffs from all pairwise interactions are summed as cumulative payoff. Update rule (asynchronous): at each elementary step, a random focal player selects a random opponent, compares cumulative payoffs, imitates opponent’s strategy used against it with probability p = |ΔP_cum|/[(N−1)b] if opponent’s payoff is larger, and replaces the strategy used in the focal interaction that gives the worst payoff, ordered (C,D) < (D,D) < (C,C) < (D,C). Time step defined as N such elementary updates.",Summarized directly from the Model and Evolutionary Analysis sections describing payoffs and asynchronous update dynamics.,0.96
10.1103_PhysRevE.81.036115.md,"Simulation – Synchronous update, N=40 or 100, b=2","Paper reports a distinct condition using synchronous update in well-mixed populations with same payoff structure, explicitly contrasted with asynchronous update (e.g., Figs. 4 and 5).",0.9,"Update rule = Synchronous; Population size N ∈ {40, 100}; Defection tendency b = 2; ε = 0.001; Initial cooperation probability per interaction = 0.5","Synchronous update explicitly analyzed; simulations for N=40 (Fig. 4 short- and long-time regime) and also mention N=100 in general; parameters b=2, ε=0.001, initial 50% cooperation used as in asynchronous case.",0.9,False,Condition is based on numerical simulations with no human participants.,1,False,No human or field experiment; model explored via simulations and mean-field equations.,1,False,Not a lab-based human experiment.,1,True,Authors repeatedly refer to simulation results for synchronous update in well-mixed populations.,1,True,Section III.B develops mean-field approximation for synchronous update without new data.,1,40,"For synchronous update, Figure 4 and 5 explicitly use N=40; finite-size analysis in Figure 6 varies N but 40 is the primary detailed case.",0.82,0,Number of time steps to termination not specified; only qualitative long- vs short-time regimes and finite-size scaling of time T.,0,1,Binary C/D actions in prisoner’s dilemma setting; equivalent to all-or-nothing contributions.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments or contribution share defined; simulation of strategies only.,1,N/A,"Prisoner’s dilemma, not linear public goods game; MPCR not applicable.",1,N/A,No human interaction or communication channel described.,1,N/A,"No user interface, only internal simulation variables.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment action or identity revelation; implicit punishment is a structural effect.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding or identities.,1,N/A,No human players to whom number of rounds would be shown.,1,0,"Although authors talk about 'implicit punishment', 