entual dominance of cooperation.",Directly summarized from the Model section and the part of the Evolutionary Analysis and mean-field approximation specific to synchronous update.,0.97
10.1109_tcsii.2019.2910893.md,"Analytical model – four-strategy public goods game with punishment, speculation, and loners","Single theoretical evolutionary game model with four strategies (C, D, S, L) defined in Section II and analyzed throughout; no separate empirical conditions.",1,"Model parameters and initial strategy shares: group size N, multiplication factor r, punishment fine α, insurance cost λ, loner payoff σ, initial fractions x_c, x_d, x_s, x_l","The dynamics depend on these parameters and initial conditions (replicator equation with payoffs parameterized by N, r, α, λ, σ, and state variables x_i).",1,False,No human or animal participants; purely theoretical evolutionary game analysis.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; only mathematical modeling and analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; entirely analytical.,1,False,Paper presents closed-form payoffs and qualitative phase portraits; no explicit numerical or agent-based simulation procedure is described as a study.,0.7,True,"Derives payoffs, expected values, and replicator dynamics analytically; proves theorems and propositions about equilibria and attractors.",1,5,"Text specifies public goods game with group size N and uses N=5 in illustrative parameter choices for figures (e.g., Fig. 1–4: “Parameters: N=5 …”).",0.7,1,"Standard evolutionary game/replicator framework with random matching; each encounter is a single-shot PGG and evolution occurs in continuous time, not discrete repeated rounds.",0.7,1,"They ‘set the cost c of cooperation equal to 1’ and treat C vs non-C as discrete strategies; players either contribute the fixed amount c or nothing, implying binary choice rather than continuous amount.",0.8,N/A,No human subjects; endowment allocation interface not applicable.,1,N/R,They define total contribution multiplied by r and redistributed among N−N_l participants; marginal per capita return r/(N−N_l) varies with N_l and no single MPCR value is specified.,0.5,N/A,No human players or communication protocol.,1,0,Analytical model does not include any information feedback about others’ earnings or punishments; payoffs depend only on current composition.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as an external fine α with no identity structure.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,No user interface or round information; continuous-time replicator dynamics.,1,1,Defectors ‘may be caught and fined by α’; punishment is central to the model.,1,0,"Punishment is imposed externally as a fine α on defectors; no cost to any punishing player is modeled, so cost to a punisher is effectively 0.",0.8,N/R,"The mapping from any expenditure to fine is not parameterized; only the fine α itself is modeled, without a per-token technology ratio.",0.5,0,"No positive reward or bonus to others is included; only contributions, fines, insurance, and loner income.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"They state: ‘For simplicity and without loss of generality, we set the cost c of cooperation equal to 1.’ The endowment each player receives is c, so normalized to 1.",0.9,N/R,Outcome variables are fractions of strategy types and stability of equilibria; no explicit numeric contributions are reported as results.,0.5,N/R,No empirical data; contributions are fixed at c=1 for cooperators by definition and not reported as an outcome statistic.,0.6,N/R,They discuss payoffs and global attractors but do not compute efficiency as ratio to maximal payoff.,0.5,N/R,Group payoffs are not reported as aggregate numerical outcomes; only functional expressions for individual payoffs are given.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model analyzed with replicator dynamics; no experimental environment.,1,Well-mixed infinite population; four pure strategies (C: contribute c=1; D: defect and risk external fine α; S: defect but pay insurance cost λ and avoid fine; L: abstain and receive fixed payoff σ). Groups of size N are sampled randomly. Public goods returns: total contributions by cooperators multiplied by r (1<r<N) and shared equally among non-loners in the group. Evolution follows continuous-time replicator dynamics over strategy frequencies.,"Summarized from Section II (Problem Formulation) and formal payoff definitions P_c, P_d, P_s, P_l and replicator equation.",1
