10.1016_j.jtbi.2004.12.015.md,Strategic analysis – Model fitting to untrained–untrained altruistic food-exchange sessions,"Section 3 fits multiple behavioral strategy models (e.g., Punish 2, TFT) via maximum-likelihood to data from untrained tamarins in the altruistic exchange game.",1,"Behavioral strategy model being fit (e.g., Punish 2, Punish 3, Tit for Tat, Fictitious play, Stimulus-response). This row aggregates across all models as one analytical condition.","This is an analytical/simulation-like analysis varying hypothesized decision rules, not an experimental treatment on subjects.",0.8,False,"This entry summarizes analytical model-fitting, not a separate human/animal data collection; underlying data are from earlier experiments.",0.9,False,No new manipulation; re-analysis of existing datasets.,0.9,False,No new lab session; only analytical/statistical modeling.,1,False,"Although models generate predictions, there is no stand-alone agent-based simulation described; focus is on likelihood of observed data under each rule.",0.7,True,Described as maximum-likelihood estimation and Bayesian posterior odds across a candidate set of finite-state strategies.,1,2,Analysis is restricted to sessions from the repeated food-exchange game with two untrained actors.,1,N/R,They aggregate 'all sessions from the repeated food-exchange game' but do not specify the total number of rounds used in the ML analysis.,0,1,Underlying game remains binary pull/no-pull.,1,N/A,No new human or animal participants; analytical condition.,1,N/R,Not framed as public-goods multiplier; analysis uses binary actions and payoffs implicit in earlier experiments.,0,N/A,No new experimental interface; this is model-fitting.,1,N/A,"Analytical condition, no subjects receiving summaries.",1,N/A,No punishment stage; analysis concerns inferred strategies.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No subjects in this analytical 'condition'.,1,0,Underlying game has no explicit punishment move; some strategies are 'punishing' but only via withholding cooperation.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward stage in underlying game.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No new experimental tokens; purely analytical.,1,N/R,"The analysis infers strategies and reports error rates and posterior likelihoods, not average contribution rates.",0,N/R,"No new behavioral data, only model fits to existing action strings.",0,N/R,"Outcome efficiency is not computed; focus is on model fit statistics (posterior likelihood, chi-square, error rate).",0,N/R,Not reported in this analytical section.,0,N/A,No additional participants; re-analysis of existing datasets.,1,N/A,No new subjects.,1,N/A,No new subjects.,1,N/A,No human participants in this analytical step.,1,No human,Purely analytical/statistical modeling based on previously collected animal data.,1,"Maximum-likelihood comparison of finite-state and learning strategies (including Punish 2, Punish 3, TFT, fictitious play, stimulus-response, mimic rules) applied to binary pull/no-pull sequences from untrained–untrained altruistic exchange sessions; Table 2 reports posterior likelihoods and error rates.",Condenses description from Strategic analyses and Table 2.,1
10.1088_1478-3975_12_4_046005.md,Agent-based evolutionary simulation with adaptive punishment (baseline configuration),"Paper describes a single core agent-based simulation setup (well-mixed, k=4, N=1024, evolving pC and pP, with punishment enabled and parameters varied). This row summarizes that baseline configuration rather than each r, β, γ grid point separately because no additional structural changes are described.",0.7,"Synergy factor r (varied across runs; examples: 3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, 5+), punishment effectiveness β (0–1), punishment cost γ (0–1), and in some runs fixed punishment probability pP (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0) instead of evolving pP.","Section 4 varies r while holding β=0.8, γ=0.2 (figure 6). Figure 5 varies β and γ for fixed r. Figure 8 holds pP fixed at different values and varies r to find critical points.",0.95,False,All results are from agent-based computer simulations with no human subjects.,1,False,"This is a computational study, not a controlled experiment with human or field data.",1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; purely in silico evolution.,1,True,"Section 3 explicitly describes ""agent-based simulations"" and genetic algorithms with populations of 1024 simulated individuals.",1,True,"Section 2 develops mean-field theory and replicator/fitness equations for critical points, a formal analytical model alongside simulations.",1,5,"Groups consist of k+1 players; they set k=4, so each game has 5 players.",1,500000,They run 500000 population updates per simulation before reconstructing the line of descent; each update every individual plays k+1 games. This is the primary reported temporal length.,0.8,1,"Each individual contributes 1 unit if cooperating and 0 if defecting; there is no partial contribution level described, so the choice is effectively all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human subjects; default contribution framing does not apply to simulated genetic strategies.,1,0.8,"Marginal per capita return is r/(k+1). Baseline example uses r=4 and k=4, so MPCR=4/5=0.8. Other r values change MPCR but 0.8 is the central illustrative case.",0.7,N/A,Simulated agents do not communicate; no chat or messaging is modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents only use payoffs for selection. Display of summaries is meaningless here.,1,N/A,No identities or observation structure for human participants; not applicable in a simulation without UI.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism distinct from cooperation; and no human-visible IDs.,1,N/A,Simulated agents are not informed of the total number of updates.,1,1,"Core model includes costly punishment: defectors suffer fine β/k per punisher, punishers pay cost γ/k.",1,0.05,"Per game, a punisher pays γ/k. In baseline figures γ=0.2 and k=4, so cost per punished defector is 0.2/4=0.05. Other γ values are explored but 0.05 reflects the main parameter set (β=0.8, γ=0.2, k=4).",0.8,0.2,"Each punisher imposes a fine β/k on each defector. With β=0.8 and k=4 in the main parameter set, per-punisher impact is 0.8/4=0.2.",0.8,0,Only costly punishment is modeled; no separate reward mechanism is implemented in the focal simulations.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Each individual may contribute 1 unit to the public good when cooperating, so per-round endowment is normalized to 1.",1,N/R,They report probabilities to cooperate pC at evolutionary fixed points but do not translate these into normalized contribution rates over the endowment in a way directly labeled as such; no single numeric outcome is given.,0.4,N/R,"Although each cooperator contributes 1 unit, the paper does not report explicit average total contributions per game as numeric outcome variables.",0.3,N/R,They discuss payoffs and phase transitions but do not report efficiency as total payoff relative to maximum in numeric form.,0.3,N/R,"Payoff formulas are given, but no aggregated group payoff data are tabulated or plotted numerically.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; all agents are simulated.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely computational with agent-based simulations and analytical derivations.,1,"Well-mixed population of 1024 agents; each update, every agent plays a public goods game in a group of size 5 (k=4 partners) and accumulates payoff. Strategies are encoded as two loci giving probabilities to cooperate (pC) and punish (pP). A Moran-like replacement process replaces 2% of the population per update, with mutation probability μ=0.02 per locus (some tests at μ=0.01). Synergy factor r, punishment effectiveness β, and punishment cost γ are varied; some runs fix pP externally to study critical r.","Summarizes model details from sections 3 and 4: N=1024, k=4, Moran-like 2% replacement, mutation rate, genes for pC and pP, and parameter sweeps.",1
10.1111_j.1558-5646.2010.01194.x.md,Corruption Game – Full analytical and simulation model,Paper presents a single theoretical game (Corruption Game) analyzed analytically and with computer simulations over parameter ranges; no separate empirical or human-participant conditions are described.,1,"Game parameters varied across analyses: punishment of civilians p, punishment of policers q, cost of punishing civilians c, cost of punishing policers d, and derived social cost of punishing policers (q+d). Some figures fix r=2, s=1, t=4, p=10 and vary c and q+d; simulations vary p, q+d, and c over specified ranges.","Independent variables are the payoff parameters explicitly manipulated in the analytical derivations and numerical simulations (e.g., ranges for p, q+d, c described in Results and Appendix).",1,False,Study is purely theoretical/mathematical with numerical simulations; no human or animal subjects participate.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; only game-theoretic analysis and simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; analysis is mathematical and computational.,1,True,Appendix explicitly states use of numerical simulations of the continuous-time replicator dynamics for various parameter values.,1,True,Core results derive from closed-form analysis of equilibria and stability for the replicator dynamics of the Corruption Game.,1,2,Corruption Game is defined as an extension of the two-player Prisoner's Dilemma with a 4x4 payoff matrix between two individuals each choosing a strategy type.,1,1,Authors state they consider single interactions (one-shot game) rather than repeated interactions.,1,1,"Players choose among discrete pure strategies (C, D, H, K) with no partial contribution levels; interpreted as all-or-nothing cooperation/defection choice, but schema requires 1 when any amount allowed, so set to 1 is conservative. Exact mapping is not explicit.",0.3,N/A,Public good framing with an endowment is not used; model is a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma-type payoff matrix without explicit endowments and without human subjects.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined; payoffs are given as (r, -s, t, etc.) in a Prisoner’s Dilemma matrix, not a standard linear public good.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; purely theoretical model.,1,0,Information structure of a matrix game does not involve feedback about others’ past earnings or punishments; nothing of this kind is modeled.,0.9,N/A,Model does not involve identities of specific punishers or information revelation; also no human subjects.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identity revelation modeled.,1,0,One-shot theoretical game; there is no concept of a displayed number of rounds.,1,1,"Core of the model is punishment of defectors by policers, with explicit punishment parameters p (civilians) and q (policers).",1,N/R,"Schema expects a single numeric cost per punishment unit, but model distinguishes c (cost to punish civilians) and d (cost to punish policers); no single combined numeric value is designated.",0.2,N/R,"Punishment effectiveness is represented by p and q in payoff matrix, but not as a linear ‘tech’ multiplier per cost unit in standard public good punishment sense.",0.3,0,Only punishment (costs inflicted on defectors) is modeled; no positive rewards are described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,Game payoffs are specified directly as outcomes of action profiles; no endowment or token budget per player is defined.,1,N/R,"Outcomes assessed are frequencies of strategy types, equilibrium structure, population mean payoff, and basins of attraction rather than normalized contribution rates in a public good sense.",1,N/R,No explicit contributions in tokens or amounts; only cooperative vs defective strategies and payoffs.,1,N/R,They discuss population mean payoff and efficiency qualitatively but do not normalize it as total payoff divided by a clearly defined maximum group payoff under full cooperation in a token-based framework.,0.6,N/R,"They compute population mean payoff expressions (e.g., W̄_x) but not as a single numeric outcome across a specific parameter condition designated as main result; multiple parameter sweeps are shown.",0.7,N/A,No human or animal participants; purely theoretical and simulation work.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is based on analytical derivations and numerical simulations of evolutionary dynamics with no human subjects.,1,"Two-player extension of Prisoner’s Dilemma with four strategy types: cooperating civilians (C), defecting civilians (D), honest policers (H), and corrupt policers (K). Payoff matrix A is 4x4 with entries parameterized by r, s, t (Prisoner’s Dilemma payoffs) and punishment parameters p (punishment on defecting civilians), q (punishment on defecting policers), c (cost of punishing civilians), d (cost of punishing policers). Dynamics studied via continuous-time replicator equations; equilibria include pure populations (C, D, H, K), mixed equilibrium of C and K (x), and their stability depending on inequalities involving q+d, s, and p (e.g., 0<q+d<s favors pure K; s<q+d<p allows stable coexistence of C and K). Simulations explore basins of attraction from initial conditions near corner C for parameter grids (e.g., r=2, s=1, t=4, p from 1 to 10, q+d from 1 to 10, c from 0.2 to 2).","Summarizes game structure and dynamic analysis as described in Methods, Results, and Appendix, including parameterization and main equilibrium conditions.",1
10.1177_0022002714564426.md,SPD baseline (no punishment),"Baseline stochastic prisoners' dilemma game without punishment, labeled 'SPD' in Table 1 and design.",1,Game type: Stochastic PD; Punishment mechanism: none,"Condition varies along two main independent variables across treatments: game type (SPD vs DPD) and punishment mechanism (none, BothPun, InvPun). This row is SPD with no punishment.",1,True,Human subjects in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the Behavioral Lab at the Wharton School.,1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations described.,1,False,No purely analytical model; focus is on experimental data.,1,2,Design states 'two subjects are paired' and 'two-person SPD game'.,1,20,"Subjects play with same partner for ten periods then with a different partner for another ten periods, total 20.",1,0,Binary decision each period: Invest vs Not Invest; no continuous contribution amount.,1,0,"Not a public goods contribution game; each subject simply receives an endowment and chooses an action. Default proportion in 'public' account not applicable, but mapped to 0 as endowment starts as private talers.",0.6,N/R,"This is a 2x2 loss/insurance style PD, not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.",1,0,No mention of communication; anonymous computerized play implies no chat.,0.9,1,"At end of each period players are informed of counterpart's decision, whether negative event occurred, and total losses to each player, which constitutes outcome summaries.",1,0,"No punishment in baseline, so identity revelation of punisher is not applicable; set to 0 because there is no punishment stage.",1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,"Subjects are told they will play for ten periods with one partner, then another ten with a new partner; implies knowledge of horizon length.",0.9,0,Defined as baseline without punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward or positive sanction mechanisms described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,48,"At the beginning of each period, each subject is given 48 talers.",1,0.3688,Investment percentage in SPD baseline reported in Table 1 as 36.88%. Interpreted as cooperation rate.,1,N/R,"Only binary invest/not-invest choices; no taler amount of 'contribution' reported beyond the fixed cost of 12, and average number of invests per subject is not given directly.",0.3,N/R,"Average earnings in talers are reported, but maximum possible benchmark for efficiency (without losses, without any investment or with both investing) not explicitly defined in a way that allows a clear 0–1 normalization across SPD states.",0.3,66.6,"Average earnings per player per period are 33.30 talers (Table 1). For a 2-person group, group payoff ≈ 66.6 talers.",0.9,United States,"Subjects recruited from University of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia, USA.",1,N/R,Only described as 'general student population'; no age statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition details provided.,0.01,University students,Recruited from general student population at University of Pennsylvania.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical Behavioral Lab with subjects at computer terminals.,1,"Two-person stochastic prisoner's dilemma framed as investing in protection against a random negative event (loss 24 talers). If both invest, no event and each pays 12. If one invests, 40% chance event: investor loses 36, non-investor 24; 60% no event: investor loses 12, non-investor 0. If none invest, 64% chance each loses 24; otherwise 0.",Payoff contingencies and probabilities detailed in the design section.,1
10.1177_0022002714564426.md,DPD baseline (no punishment),"Deterministic prisoners' dilemma game without punishment, labeled 'DPD' in Table 1 and design.",1,Game type: Deterministic PD; Punishment mechanism: none,Variation across treatments is game type and punishment mechanism; this is deterministic PD without punishment.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment.,1,True,Conducted in Wharton Behavioral Lab.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,No closed-form modeling only.,1,2,Two-person deterministic PD game.,1,20,Ten periods with one partner then ten with another.,1,0,Binary choice: Invest vs Not Invest.,1,0,"Insurance-style binary decision, not a continuous contribution; endowment is private.",0.6,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,0,No communication channel described.,0.9,1,"At end of each period, players are informed of counterpart's decision, whether event occurred, and total losses to each player.",1,0,No punishment in this baseline treatment.,1,0,No rewards implemented.,1,1,Number of periods and rematching described ex ante.,0.9,0,Baseline without punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward stage described.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,48,"DPD game uses the same per-period endowment of 48 talers (by design symmetry with SPD); explicitly stated for SPD, and DPD matches expected losses but endowment level implied constant across treatments.",0.8,0.3396,Investment percentage in DPD baseline reported in Table 1 as 33.96%.,1,N/R,"Binary decision, no mean number of invests directly reported as counts.",0.3,N/R,"Average earnings reported, but maximum benchmark payoff (e.g., both invest vs both not invest) not normalized in paper for efficiency.",0.3,66.94,Average earnings per player per period are 33.47 talers; group payoff ≈ 66.94.,0.9,United States,University of Pennsylvania student sample.,1,N/R,No age statistics.,0.2,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0.01,University students,Recruited from general student population.,1,On site,Lab at Wharton with computer terminals.,1,"Two-person deterministic prisoners' dilemma mirroring expected losses from SPD. If both invest: each pays 12 talers, no loss. If one invests: both suffer deterministic loss of 10 talers plus investor pays 12 (total 22) and non-investor pays 10. If none invest: each suffers deterministic loss of 15 talers.",DPD payoffs described as expected losses of SPD scenario.,1
10.1177_0022002714564426.md,SPD_BothPun,"Stochastic PD with unrestricted punishment, labeled 'SPD_BothPun' in design and Table 1.",1,Game type: Stochastic PD; Punishment mechanism: BothPun (both players may punish counterpart regardless of own action).,Treatment definition: SPD_BothPun differs from SPD by adding unrestricted punishment stage.,1,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to treatment; controlled payoffs.,1,True,Conducted in Wharton Behavioral Lab.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,2,Two-person SPD game.,1,20,Ten periods with one partner and ten with another.,1,0,Binary invest vs not-invest choice.,1,0,Same SPD structure as baseline; contribution modeled as investment decision from private endowment.,0.6,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication stage mentioned.,0.9,1,"Players see counterpart's decision, event realization, total losses, and whether counterpart punished and by how much.",1,1,"Two-player game where any punishment must come from the sole counterpart; subjects are told whether counterpart inflicted punishment and its severity, so punisher identity is effectively revealed.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Horizon (10+10 periods) described at outset.,0.9,1,BothPun treatment adds a punishment stage after each period.,1,1,Every 3 talers deducted from counterpart costs punisher 1 taler.,1,3,Punisher can deduct 3 talers of counterpart's payoff per 1 taler spent.,1,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,48,Same SPD payoffs as baseline; 48 talers per period.,1,0.4823,Investment percentage 48.23% (Table 1).,1,N/R,Binary decision; mean number of invests not provided.,0.3,N/R,"Average earnings reported (30.89 talers), but normalized efficiency not calculated in paper.",0.3,61.78,Average earnings per player per period 30.89 talers; group payoff ≈ 61.78.,0.9,United States,UPenn student subject pool.,1,N/R,No age details.,0.2,N/R,No gender details.,0.01,University students,General student population sample.,1,On site,Lab experiment at Wharton.,1,"Same stochastic PD as SPD baseline, plus punishment stage: after seeing counterpart's decision and whether the negative event occurred, each agent may choose a punishment amount (up to 24 talers deducted) before learning counterpart's punishment decision. One taler spent deducts 3 talers from counterpart. Negative earnings in a period are truncated to zero.",Design section for SPD_BothPun and punishment rules.,1
