10.1016_j.pursup.2017.11.006.md,"Experiment 1 – Condition 1 (Base-case payoff, no sanctions)",Experiment 1 has two payoff conditions; this row is the initial base-case payoff structure without sanctions.,1,Incentive structure: base-case payoff matrix (temptation payoff = 4 when other cooperates); Sanctions: absent,Condition 1 differs from Condition 2 by payoff matrix (temptation to defect) while sanctions are not available in Experiment 1.,1,True,Behavioral experiments with 14 practicing managers.,1,True,Controlled behavioral lab-style experiment with randomized roles and repeated rounds.,1,True,Conducted in a meeting room with PCs and z-Tree at the company site; this matches a lab/onsite experimental setting.,1,False,No agent-based or purely computational simulation; only human participants.,1,False,No formal analytical model tested as an experiment; only regression analysis of behavioral data.,1,3,Each participant “plays the Prisoner's Dilemma game within this network context” in triads of three decision-makers.,1,20,“We collect decisions over the course of 20 rounds” and this applies to each condition in the experiment.,1,1,"Participants can “distribute the 5 EMUs towards group or private project in any proportion… fractional allocations not allowed” but 0–5 integral EMUs allowed, so not all-or-nothing.",1,0,All 5 EMUs per round are initially under the participant’s control as private budget; nothing is pre-committed to group.,0.9,N/R,"Public-good style component exists (shared group allocation), but no explicit linear multiplier or group-size-based marginal per capita return is reported or derivable from the complex payoff rule.",0.3,0,“Participants were told not to communicate with anyone during the experiment.”,1,1,"After each round, the highest earning and associated strategy are revealed; Appendix A shows a table of all members’ group/private allocations and payoffs after each round in this experiment.",0.9,0,No punishment option exists in Experiment 1.,1,0,