10.1098_rspb.2016.0488.md,"Simulation – Well-mixed population, baseline costs, varying rounds T","Uses the baseline well-mixed case d=1, n=10000, Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, C1=1, μ=0.01, z=T, while varying the independent variable T (number of rounds) as described for figure 2a and in Results section 3(a).",1,"Number of rounds T in repeated game; T varied from 1 to 30 (and higher conceptually, e.g., threshold statements for T≥70) while holding Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, C1=1, μ=0.01, d=1, n=10000, z=T constant.",Section 3(a) specifies baseline parameters and that T is varied between 1 and 30 in the reported simulations for the well-mixed case (figure 2a).,1,False,Study is an evolutionary model analyzed with individual-based (agent-based) simulations; no human or animal subjects are experimentally tested.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment with participants; results are from computational simulations.,1,False,Not a laboratory experiment; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,True,Section 2(f) states they used individual-based simulations to track strategy frequencies over generations.,1,False,"While a formal model is described, main results here are from numerical simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.8,10000,"Well-mixed case uses a single group with d=1 and n=10000, so total population size N=d×n=10000.",1,30,They vary T between 1 and 30; taking the maximum T reported for this condition as representative since simulations are run separately for each T in this range.,0.7,1,"Actions are binary cooperate or defect in a Prisoner’s Dilemma; no graded contribution amount is modeled, so not a continuous contribution game. Per instructions, 1 indicates players can choose any amount; here that does not literally apply but binary choice is closer to flexible than to an enforced all-or-nothing contribution of a fixed endowment, so set to 1.",0.3,N/A,No public goods contribution structure or initial endowment allocation is modeled for human players; model uses abstract PD payoffs without explicit endowments.,1,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; MPCR is not defined or reported.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication channels; purely simulated strategies.,1,0,Players (agents) do not observe payoff summaries; only own and partner’s actions enter strategies.,0.9,0,"Identity is irrelevant in the model; strategies respond to partner behavior only, not to IDs or reputation. No mention of identity revelation upon punishment.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in the model; only punishment and partner switching are modeled.,1,0,Agents have fixed strategy vectors and do not condition on remaining or total rounds; no modeling of information about T available to players.,0.8,1,Move 3 is an explicit punishment decision (P or N) with costs Cp and effect Dp.,1,1,Baseline parameter Cp=1 (table 1 and Results 3(a)).,1,2,"Punishment reduces partner’s payoff by Dp=2 per act (table 1, move 3 description).",1,0,"No positive rewarding mechanism is included; only cooperation costs/benefits, punishment, and partner switching.",1,N/A,No reward option in the model.,1,N/A,No reward option in the model.,1,N/R,"Repeated PD is parameterized by cost Ch and benefit Bh, not by an explicit per-round token endowment.",1,N/R,"Outcome measures are frequencies of cooperation and strategy classes, not normalized contribution rates of a divisible endowment.",1,N/R,No explicit token contributions; actions are cooperate or defect with fixed cost/benefit parameters.,1,N/R,"They report cooperation frequencies and class frequencies, not efficiency (payoff relative to maximum possible).",1,N/R,Total or mean payoffs are used internally for reproduction but no numerical payoff outcomes are reported.,1,N/A,No human participants; evolutionary simulation of abstract agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is purely theoretical and uses individual-based simulations with no human subjects.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma implemented as a 5-move extensive-form game per round: move 0 random pairing; move 1 simultaneous cooperate (C) or defect (D) with cost Ch=1 and benefit Bh=2 to partner; move 2 partner switching decision leave (L) or stay (S) incurring switching cost C1=1 to both if pair breaks; move 3 punishment decision P or N with cost Cp=1 and damage Dp=2 to partner; move 4 response to being punished: ignore (I), alter behaviour for z=T future rounds (A), or leave (L). Strategies are pure, memory-1 for move 1, contingent on previous partner action, and simulations track six pooled classes: positive reciprocity (Rc), switching (Sc), punishment (Pc), always cooperate (Cc), always defect (Dc), and rest, over 10^6 generations with mutation rate μ=0.01.","Summarizes the described extensive-form game, strategy structure, and simulation protocol from sections 2(b)–2(f) and table 1.",1
10.1098_rspb.2016.0488.md,"Simulation – Well-mixed population, high switching cost (C1=5), varying rounds T",Described in Results 3(a) and figure 2b as a variant of the well-mixed scenario where C1 is increased to 5 while varying T and keeping other parameters as in the baseline.,1,"Cost of switching partner C1=5 and number of rounds T; T varied (threshold result that Sc dominates only for T≥70) with Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, μ=0.01, d=1, n=10000, z=T.",Figure 2b is specified as C1=5 and text notes how the T threshold for Sc dominance shifts when C1=5.,1,False,Parameter-sweep in a computational evolutionary simulation; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical controlled experiment but a simulation study.,1,False,No lab environment; simulation only.,1,True,Same individual-based simulation framework as the baseline well-mixed model.,1,False,"Condition-level results here are from simulations with C1=5, not closed-form analysis.",0.8,10000,Still the well-mixed case with a single group of size n=10000 (d=1).,1,70,"Text states that when C1=5, the number of rounds needed for Sc to dominate increases to T≥70; use this threshold T as a representative configuration within this condition.",0.7,1,Same binary cooperate/defect action structure as baseline; no divisible endowment contributions.,0.3,N/A,No explicit endowment model for human players.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; MPCR is not defined.,1,N/A,No human communication; simulation only.,1,0,"Agents only condition on partner’s last action and punishment, not on payoff summaries.",0.9,0,Identities are not modeled; only actions matter.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,0,Strategies do not condition on knowledge of remaining or total rounds.,0.8,1,Punishment decision in move 3 remains part of the game.,1,1,Cp remains 1 in this variant.,1,2,Dp remains 2 in this variant.,1,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,"Model uses abstract costs/benefits, not an explicit numeric endowment.",1,N/R,"They report cooperation frequency and class frequencies, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No token-level contribution amounts are reported.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff vs. maximum) reported.,1,N/R,Only relative frequencies are presented; payoff aggregates are not given numerically.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulation with artificial agents.,1,"Same 5-move repeated PD with partner switching and punishment as in the baseline well-mixed model, but with a higher fixed cost of switching partners C1=5. Simulations explore how increasing T interacts with higher switching cost to determine dominance of partner switching (Sc) versus other classes.",Summarizes the only change relative to the baseline: increased C1 and its implications for Sc dominance threshold (T≥70).,1
10.1098_rspb.2016.0488.md,"Simulation – Well-mixed population, endogenous switching cost C1(i), varying rounds T","Described in section 2(b) and referenced in Results 3(a) and figure 2c as a variant where the cost of switching partner depends on the number of unpaired individuals via C1(i)=a/(1+i^k) with a=100, k=0.9, under d=1 and varying T.",1,"Number of rounds T in repeated game and endogenous cost of switching C1(i)=a/(1+i^k) with a=100, k=0.9; T varied with Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, μ=0.01, d=1, n=10000, z=T.","Figure 2c is specified as using equation (2.1) with a=100, k=0.9, under d=1, n=10000 and varying T.",1,False,No real-world participants; model evaluated via simulations.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Same individual-based simulation framework with altered switching cost function.,1,False,Condition-level results for this cost function are given via simulation (figure 2c).,0.8,10000,"Well-mixed case with d=1, n=10000 (N=10000).",1,30,"As for figure 2c, T is varied; take the upper bound (T up to 30) as representative for this condition.",0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect PD; no divisibility of contributions is modeled.,0.3,N/A,No endowment allocation structure for human players.,1,N/R,Not applicable to this PD setting.,1,N/A,No human communication component.,1,0,Agents do not receive payoff summaries; they only track partner actions.,0.9,0,Identity not modeled; only behavioral contingencies.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,0,Strategies do not condition on number of rounds played or remaining.,0.8,1,Move 3 punishment decision remains part of game.,1,1,"Cp=1 as in baseline, unchanged in this variant.",1,2,Dp=2 as in baseline.,1,0,No reward modeled.,1,N/A,Reward option not present.,1,N/A,Reward option not present.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment per round; payoffs determined via Bh and Ch.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are cooperation frequency and class frequencies, not contributions.",1,N/R,No explicit contribution amount beyond binary action C or D.,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not reported numerically.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulation with an endogenous cost-of-switching function.,1,"Same repeated PD with partner switching and punishment as in the baseline, except switching cost is no longer fixed. The cost C1(i)=a/(1+i^k) decreases with the number i of currently unpaired individuals; with a=100 and k=0.9 this makes switching cheaper when more individuals are searching for partners. Simulations show that the tendency for partner switching (Sc) to dominate with large T is robust even under this state-dependent cost.",Draws from eq. (2.1) and the description of figure 2c and SM-II.2.,1
10.1098_rspb.2016.0488.md,"Simulation – Group-structured population, T=30, z=T, C1=1, varying group size n","Described in Results 3(b) and figure 3a with group structure introduced: d=250, varying n, T=30, z=T, and baseline parameters Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, C1=1, μ=0.01.",1,"Group size n (number of individuals per group); n varied while holding d=250, T=30, z=T, Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, C1=1, μ=0.01 constant.",Text explicitly states they keep d=250 and vary n in figure 3a to study effects of group size on which strategy class dominates.,1,False,Simulation of evolutionary dynamics in a group-structured population.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment with subjects.,1,False,No lab setting; fully computational.,1,True,Same individual-based simulation protocol applied to a structured population (d>1).,1,False,Dominance patterns over n are presented from simulations (figure 3a).,0.8,7500,"Total population size N=d×n. Group size n is varied; as a representative concrete configuration we use an intermediate case from the described ranges (e.g., n=30 yields N=250×30=7500) where punishment dominates for n<300.",0.4,30,They explicitly set T=30 in this suite of simulations.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect PD; no divisible contributions.,0.3,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined in this PD setup.,1,N/A,No human communication; simulation only.,1,0,"Strategies depend on partner actions within dyads, not on population summaries.",0.9,0,Identity is irrelevant; decisions depend only on last move actions and punishment receipt.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,0,No strategic conditioning on remaining T.,0.8,1,Move 3 punishment remains available.,1,1,Cp=1 in this condition.,1,2,"Dp=2, as baseline.",1,0,No rewards included.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are given by Bh and Ch, not an explicit numeric endowment.",1,N/R,Reported outcomes are cooperation and strategy-class frequencies as functions of n.,1,N/R,No explicit numeric contributions beyond action C/D.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure reported.,1,N/R,Numerical payoff outputs by group are not provided.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Group-structured evolutionary simulation.,1,"Same 5-move repeated PD as in well-mixed case, but with population subdivided into d=250 groups of equal size n. Pairing occurs randomly within groups each round (no genetic structure; dispersal random among groups between generations). Punishment alters behaviour for z=T rounds in this condition. Simulations over 10^6 generations show that for n<300, the punishment class (Pc) generally dominates in frequency, often coexisting with an always-defect-and-punish strategy, while for n≥300, the switching class (Sc) dominates.","Summarizes group structure, within-group pairing, and main dominance results from figure 3a.",1
10.1098_rspb.2016.0488.md,"Simulation – Group-structured population, T=30, z=5, C1=1, varying group size n","Described in Results 3(b) and figure 3b as a variant in group-structured populations with reduced duration of punishment effect (z=5) and C1=1, T=30, d=250, varying n.",1,"Group size n with fixed d=250, T=30, reduced punishment duration z=5, Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, C1=1, μ=0.01.","Figure 3b specifically varies n and sets z=5, with other parameters as in baseline group-structured scenario.",1,False,Evolutionary simulation.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Same individual-based simulation framework as other conditions.,1,False,Dominance over n under z=5 is reported from simulations (figure 3b).,0.8,12500,"Representative configuration using an intermediate group size in range where Rc dominates (e.g., n≈50), giving N=250×50=12500; exact n values are not numerically listed, so this is illustrative.",0.3,30,They fix T=30 in this set of simulations.,1,1,Binary C/D PD game as elsewhere.,0.3,N/A,No endowment-based contribution game.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Agents do not see payoff summaries.,0.9,0,No identity information modeled.,0.9,0,No rewards present.,1,0,No conditioning on T.,0.8,1,Punishment decision present in move 3.,1,1,Cp=1 retained.,1,2,Dp=2 retained.,1,0,No rewards included.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,Same payoff parameterization as other conditions.,1,N/R,Primary outcomes are frequencies of cooperation and control-mechanism classes as n varies.,1,N/R,No quantitative per-round contributions reported.,1,N/R,Efficiency measure not provided.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation context.,1,"Same group-structured repeated PD as in figure 3a, but the duration z of behavioural change after punishment is reduced to z=5 rounds instead of lasting for the whole game (z=T). Simulations show that with z=5, Pc remains dominant only up to group size n≈52; for intermediate group sizes positive reciprocity (Rc) dominates, and for n≥76 the switching class (Sc) becomes dominant.",Summarizes changed parameter z=5 and dominance patterns by n from figure 3b.,1
10.1098_rspb.2016.0488.md,"Simulation – Group-structured population, T=30, z=5, C1=0, varying group size n","Described in Results 3(b) and figure 3c as a further group-structured variant with zero cost of switching (C1=0), z=5, T=30, d=250, varying n.",1,"Group size n with fixed d=250, T=30, z=5, Bh=2, Ch=1, Dp=2, Cp=1, C1=0, μ=0.01.",Figure 3c varies n and specifies C1=0 and z=5.,1,False,Simulation of evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab; simulation only.,1,True,Individual-based simulations as in other conditions.,1,False,"Figure 3c’s patterns arise from simulations, not analytic solution.",0.8,7000,"Representative mid-range group size for which Pc dominates in small groups (e.g., n=28 gives N=250×28=7000) as noted that Pc dominates for n≤28 even when C1=0.",0.3,30,T=30 in this condition.,1,1,Binary C/D actions; no divisible endowment.,0.3,N/A,No contribution endowment model.,1,N/R,MPCR not applicable.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No payoff summaries are modeled.,0.9,0,Identities not modeled.,0.9,0,No rewards in model.,1,0,No conditioning on total or remaining rounds.,0.8,1,Punishment remains as move 3 choice.,1,1,Cp remains 1.,1,2,Dp remains 2.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Reward not modeled.,1,N/A,Reward not modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment.,1,N/R,Outcome variables are cooperation and class frequencies.,1,N/R,No continuous contribution measure.,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,1,N/R,Group-level payoffs are not provided numerically.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation context.,1,"Group-structured repeated PD with d=250, zero cost of partner switching (C1=0), and short punishment-induced behavioural change (z=5). Despite zero switching cost, simulations show that in small groups (n≤28), punishment (Pc) still outcompetes partner switching (Sc), likely because switchers are frequently rematched with the same defectors due to small group size. As group size increases beyond this range, Rc and/or Sc gain relative importance (details beyond small-n threshold are summarized qualitatively).","Summarizes the parameter changes (C1=0, z=5) and main dominance claim that Pc dominates in small groups (n≤28) from figure 3c.",1
10.1007_978-3-642-02624-9_8.md,Wedekind and Milinski 2000 – Positive indirect reciprocity (image scoring in humans),Paper cites Wedekind and Milinski 2000 as demonstrating that humans help individuals they observed helping others in an indirect reciprocity game.,0.9,Partner’s past behavior (helped vs. did not help others) as publicly observed image score; decision to help in subsequent interaction with that partner.,"Indirect reciprocity paradigm: key manipulation is whether the current partner previously helped others, affecting their image score.",0.9,True,Study involves human subjects in economic game experiments.,1,True,Controlled laboratory-style economic game with assigned roles and payoffs.,1,True,Standard economic game; not described as field or online; default is on-site lab experiment.,0.7,False,"Involves human subjects, not purely computational agents.",1,False,Empirical experiment rather than purely mathematical modeling.,1,2,Indirect reciprocity games are played in pairwise donor–recipient interactions even though bystanders observe.,0.7,1,Described as one-shot help decisions towards partners with a known image score; repeated structure across different partners but single decision per pairing.,0.6,1,Economic helping games typically allow any amount to be transferred; chapter does not specify discrete all-or-nothing choices.,0.5,0,Standard helping/transfer games start with endowment in private account; no mention of pre-allocation to public fund.,0.6,N/R,Chapter does not report multiplier or marginal per capita return for this study.,0,0,No mention of communication; standard design prohibits communication in such experiments.,0.7,1,"Participants observe others’ past helping behavior (image scores), which is a summary of previous actions.",0.9,0,No punishment stage is mentioned for this study.,0.9,1,Helping decisions are directed towards identified partners whose past behavior is known to observers.,0.8,0,No mention that subjects are informed about total rounds or remaining rounds.,0.5,0,"Study is about helping (reward) based on image scoring, not about punishment.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this design.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this design.,1,1,Helping (paying to help) functions as a costly reward to others.,0.9,N/R,Exact cost per unit transferred is not reported in the chapter.,0,N/R,Multiplier or effect of each unit of help on recipient not given.,0,N/R,No stake size or initial endowment is reported.,0,N/R,Chapter only states qualitative result that humans help observed helpers; no quantitative rates given.,0,N/R,No token/point amounts reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency measure reported.,0,N/R,Only individual-level helping outcomes mentioned.,0,N/R,Chapter does not report country of subject pool.,0,N/R,No age information provided.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,N/R,No education/student status reported.,0,On site,Standard economic experiments at the time were conducted in physical laboratories; no indication of online or field setting.,0.7,Indirect reciprocity helping game where participants decide whether to pay to help partners with known image scores based on previous helping of others.,Summarizes conceptual description in the chapter.,0.9
