10.1016_j.chaos.2019.04.015.md,"Simulation – baseline parameters (r=4, N=1000, p=0.5, m=3, β varied, γ varied)","Single agent-based evolutionary public goods game on an activity-driven network; paper reports results for various β and γ with fixed r=4, N=1000, p=0.5 or 0.1, and m varied. This row summarizes the core simulation framework rather than a distinct treatment.",0.7,Maximal fine of punishment β and maximal cost of punishment γ varied across runs; network parameter m (links per activation) and initial cooperator proportion p also varied in separate analyses.,"Section 3 describes simulations where β, γ, m, and p are systematically varied while observing resulting densities of C, D, Cp, Dp.",1,False,Model is a computer simulation on an activity-driven network with 1000 nodes; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only simulations.,1,True,They use numerical/agent-based simulations on a time-varying network (activity-driven model) to obtain results.,1,False,They explicitly state theoretical analysis is difficult and rely on computer simulations instead.,1,1000,"Section 3: ""assuming there are 1000 nodes in total"".",1,N/R,They simulate until the system is stable but do not state the exact number of rounds.,0.2,1,"Public goods game contributions are fixed at 1 for cooperators and 0 for defectors, but players choose strategy type (C, D, Cp, Dp); no separate continuous contribution decision. However, from the perspective of contribution choice, it is effectively all-or-nothing per strategy, so coded as 1 to indicate binary contribution possibility.",0.4,N/A,No explicit endowment or default allocation between private/public accounts; only payoff functions defined.,0.1,N/R,"They specify basic average benefit Π_PGG = r * (N_C+N_Cp)/N with r=4, but do not define individual endowment or per-capita marginal return relative to contribution, so MPCR cannot be computed unambiguously.",0.3,N/A,No human subjects or communication; agent strategies update via Fermi rule only.,1,N/A,"No user interface; agents only use local payoff comparison, no mention of summaries.",1,N/A,No identities or information design; purely algorithmic punishment within payoff equations.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No human interface; round count not displayed to agents.,1,1,Model explicitly includes prosocial (Cp) and antisocial (Dp) punishment in payoff equations.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost parameter γ is defined as ""maximal cost of punishment"", but mapping to 'coins per unit punishment' is not specified within an endowment framework, so no numeric cost per punishment unit in token terms can be extracted.",0.3,N/R,"Punishment fine parameter β is defined as ""maximal fine of punishment"", but no explicit per-cost transformation ratio linking γ and β in token units is provided.",0.3,0,Model only includes punishment (prosocial and antisocial); no reward or positive sanctioning mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"They use contributions of 1 for cooperators but do not specify a per-round endowment (e.g., 1, 10 tokens).",0.3,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as densities of strategy types (ρ_C, ρ_D, ρ_Cp, ρ_Dp); aggregate contribution rate could be derived as ρ_C+ρ_Cp, but authors do not explicitly define or report it as a dependent variable.",0.4,N/R,No total or per-capita contribution values reported; only strategy densities.,0.2,N/R,No measure of efficiency (total payoff relative to maximum) is reported or can be reliably derived without individual payoff distributions and endowments.,0.2,N/R,Group or average payoffs are not reported numerically; only qualitative dynamics of densities are shown.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Research uses computational simulations of evolutionary dynamics on networks.,1,"Evolutionary public goods game on an activity-driven, time-varying network with four strategy types (cooperators C, defectors D, punishing cooperators Cp, punishing defectors Dp). Activity rates a are drawn from F(a) ∼ a^(-φ) with φ=2.1, each activated node creates m random links per round, and all links are reset between rounds. Payoffs per group: Π_PGG = r * (N_C + N_Cp)/N with r=4. Individual payoffs include punishment fines β and costs γ as given in Eqs. (1)-(2). Strategy updating follows a pairwise Fermi rule with noise parameter κ=0.1. Simulations run with N=1000, varying β, γ, p (initial cooperator proportion), and m (links per activation), averaging over 100 independent realizations and evaluated at stationarity.","Summarizes the model details given in Sections 2 and 3 including network, payoff structure, strategies, and update rule.",1
10.1016_j.amc.2024.128534.md,Simulation – baseline lattice PGG with probabilistic reputational punishment,"Single computational model described, applied across parameter sweeps; treated as one simulation condition family.",0.7,"Synergy factor r ∈ {2,3,4}; retaliation emotion factor γ; reputation memory factor λ; co-beneficiary capacity M (or ratio M/N); initial proportion of cooperators.","Section 3 varies r, γ, λ, M (or M/N) and initial cooperation rate to study effects on evolution.",0.98,False,No human participants; numerical simulations of a discrete dynamical system on networks.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human subjects; purely computational simulations.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; only simulations.,1,True,Section 3 explicitly states use of computer simulations on lattice networks.,1,False,"Although equations are defined, results are obtained via numerical simulation, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.95,10000,Section 3.1: 'The experiment uses 100 × 100 closed square lattice network' so N = 100*100.,1,100000,Section 3.1: 'independent simulations ... for 10 cycles for a total of 10^5 rounds.',0.95,1,Cooperators contribute fixed cost c=1 to each group they are in; not a binary all-or-nothing choice on total endowment but fixed mandatory cost per game; mapped as flexible rather than discrete 0/1 endowment decision.,0.6,N/A,Model does not use an explicit endowment-allocation decision between private and public accounts; contributions are automatic for cooperators.,1,N/R,"Standard MPCR (multiplier/group size) not explicitly reported; groups differ in size because of varying degree k_i, and payoff uses r and local group size non-trivially; no single MPCR value.",0.4,0,No mention of communication or messaging between agents.,1,1,Strategy update uses neighbors' payoffs (π_j^t) so agents effectively observe payoff summaries of neighbors.,0.9,0,Punishment is implemented via payoff redistribution; model does not specify identity revelation of punishers to others.,0.7,0,Rewards occur implicitly via selection as co-beneficiaries; no indication that identities of rewarders are revealed.,0.7,0,Agents follow fixed update rules without information about total horizon; no statement that they know remaining rounds.,0.9,1,PC action alters benefit distribution to penalize defectors by excluding them from larger benefit shares.,1,1,Punishers are cooperators and pay cost c=1 per group. No extra explicit cost for switching from NC to PC; thus marginal punishment cost per se is effectively embedded in the contribution cost and redistribution. Set to 1 as the per-group contribution cost representing punishment-related expenditure.,0.4,1,"Each cooperator's contribution c is multiplied by r and then shared over more people when punishing; no discrete linear 'fine per cost' is defined, but punishment works via rescaling payoffs. Set to 1 as a neutral placeholder since no explicit tech parameter exists.",0.2,1,High-reputation individuals are selected as co-beneficiaries and gain additional payoff when others punish.,1,0,Rewards are funded by redistributing the same common pool proceeds; no extra cost beyond contribution c=1 is incurred by rewarders.,0.7,1,No explicit 'reward per unit cost' ratio; rewards occur via sharing the multiplied pool equally with co-beneficiaries. Set to 1 as a neutral placeholder because a specific tech factor is not defined.,0.2,N/R,"Model specifies cooperation cost c=1 per team, but does not define a finite per-round endowment or budget from which this is drawn.",0.5,N/R,"Outcomes reported as proportions of cooperators C, PC, and D; no normalized contribution fraction relative to an endowment is given.",0.4,N/R,"Although each cooperator pays c=1 per group, aggregate or per-agent contribution statistics are not reported as a main result.",0.4,N/R,Paper does not report total realized payoff relative to a first-best benchmark; focus is on strategy frequencies.,0.3,N/R,"Figures and text discuss proportions of strategies and actions, not total or average payoffs.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,No human,Study is a numerical simulation with no human subjects.,1,"Structured public goods game on a 100×100 closed square lattice; actions Ω = {NC (ordinary cooperator), PC (punishing cooperator), D (defector)}. Cooperators pay cost c=1 into each neighborhood common pool they belong to; total contributions in each pool are multiplied by gain factor r (r=2,3,4 in simulations). PC players select M high-reputation co-beneficiaries from the whole population (excluding themselves) to share their pool equally with local team members; NC and D share only among local team. Reputation f_i^t updated each round with discount factor λ, increasing for NC and PC and decreasing for D. Strategy update via pairwise comparison imitation (Fermi rule) with update frequency p=0.01; conditional choice between PC and NC among cooperators governed by retaliation emotion factor γ and local number of D neighbors. Simulations run for 10^5 rounds; some visualizations use 200×200 lattice. Parameters swept: γ, λ, M (or M/N), r, and initial fraction of cooperators.",Summarized from Sections 2 and 3 describing model rules and simulation setup.,1
