10.1016_j.ins.2016.07.041.md,"Simulation 2a – CPRT, CMPT and GT (NR) with complete information","Second group of experiments, first run: 'add the GT, TFT and LNR strategies and simulate the coevolution ... separately'; this row is for the GT (grim trigger) NR strategy case.",1,"Available strategies in populations: CPRT, CMPT, and NR of type GT (grim trigger) under complete information; initial population majority GT (≈80%), with invading CMPT (10%) and CPRT (20%).","Section 5.2.2 describes sequential runs adding GT, TFT, LNR and starting with majority NR plus 10% CMPT and 20% CPRT.",0.9,False,Simulated advertisers; no human subjects.,1,False,Computational simulation rather than empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab with humans; simulation only.,1,True,Described as 'coevolutionary algorithm' and 'coevolutionary simulation'.,1,False,This part validates analytical predictions numerically.,1,10,Same auction parameters (Table 3) used for all complete-information simulations.,1,50,Table 3: Auction Periods 50.,1,1,Bids are continuous reals; no discrete all-or-nothing constraint.,0.8,N/A,No contribution game; and no humans.,1,N/A,Not a public goods or linear contribution game.,1,N/A,Simulated agents; communication not modeled in this condition.,1,N/A,No human interface; summaries not relevant.,1,N/A,"Punishment is via bidding rules between anonymous simulated advertisers, not identity-revealing sanctions.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents are programmed over 50 periods; no concept of displayed rounds to humans.,1,1,"GT is a 'nice and retaliatory' NR strategy, punishing deviations with competitive bids in all remaining periods.",1,N/A,Punishment is endogenous via bidding higher (competitive bids); no separate cost parameter per punishment unit is specified.,0.3,N/A,No explicit mapping from punishment decision to payoff apart from the auction payoff function.,0.3,0,No explicit rewarding action where agents pay to increase others' payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward implemented.,1,N/R,Advertisers have valuations but no fixed spendable endowment is provided per round.,0.3,N/A,"Outcomes are revenues, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They note that with NR, advertisers can 'obtain the socially optimal VCG revenue', but do not provide a normalized efficiency metric.",0.5,N/R,They discuss individual and comparative revenues (UBNE vs GEAE) but not total group payoff figures.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Entirely agent-based coevolutionary simulation.,1,"Same repeated GSP auction environment as Simulation 1 (10 advertisers, 5 slots, 50 periods, discount factor 0.98, specified value and CTR vectors). Strategy set includes unconditional CPRT (always GEAE bids), unconditional CMPT (always UBNE bids), and contingent NR of type GT, which starts cooperatively (GEAE) and retaliates with competitive (UBNE) bids forever after any deviation. Initial polymorphic population: majority GT, with 10% CMPT and 20% CPRT invaders across advertisers. Evolutionary parameters: population size 20, 500 generations, MaxTest 20, mutation rate 0.02. Results: CMPT quickly becomes extinct; CPRT and GT co-exist, bids converge to GEAE and advertisers achieve socially optimal VCG-equivalent revenues.",Synthesized from Table 3 and Section 5.2.2.,1
10.1016_j.ins.2016.07.041.md,"Simulation 2b – CPRT, CMPT and TFT (NR) with complete information","Second group of experiments, second run: coevolution of CPRT and CMPT with NR strategy TFT (tit-for-tat).",0.95,"Available strategies in populations: CPRT, CMPT, and NR of type TFT under complete information; initial population majority TFT (≈80%), with CMPT (10%) and CPRT (20%) as invaders.","Section 5.2.2: 'sequentially add the GT, TFT and LNR strategies ... and create a polymorphic population with a majority of each kind of these NR strategies in addition to a small number of the invading CMPT and CPRT strategies with proportions of 10% and 20%, respectively.'",0.9,False,Agent-based simulation only.,1,False,Not an empirical or field experiment.,1,False,No lab with human participants.,1,True,Coevolutionary simulation with evolutionary algorithm.,1,False,This is numerical validation.,1,10,Shared auction parameters (Table 3).,1,50,Auction Periods = 50.,1,1,Continuous bid choices; not binary.,0.8,N/A,No contribution game or humans.,1,N/A,Not a public goods context.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No displayed info to human subjects.,1,N/A,"Punishment embedded in contingent bidding, no identities.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,"TFT is a nice and retaliatory NR strategy, responding to deviations with competitive bids matching opponents' previous actions.",1,N/A,No explicit per-punishment cost parameter.,0.3,N/A,"Retaliation realized via auction payoff function, not via a separate punishment technology parameter.",0.3,0,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Endowment size per round is not specified.,0.3,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not quantified; they discuss stability and revenues qualitatively.,0.4,N/R,Group payoff not explicitly reported.,0.4,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Same repeated complete-information GSP auction as other complete-info simulations (10 advertisers, 5 slots, 50 periods, given value and CTR vectors, discount factor 0.98). Strategy set includes CPRT, CMPT, and NR of type TFT, which starts with cooperative bids and thereafter mirrors opponents' last-period cooperative/competitive actions. Initial population mostly TFT with minority CMPT and CPRT. Evolutionary algorithm parameters from Table 3. Results mirror the GT case qualitatively: CMPT dies out, CPRT and TFT co-exist, cooperative bidding emerges and bids converge near GEAE, giving higher revenues than UBNE.",Derived from Section 5.2.2 and general simulation setup; exact time paths for TFT shown only qualitatively.,0.8
10.1016_j.ins.2016.07.041.md,"Simulation 2c – CPRT, CMPT and LNR (NR) with complete information","Second group of experiments, third run: coevolution of CPRT, CMPT, and LNR (lightest NR strategy).",0.95,"Available strategies in populations: CPRT, CMPT, and NR of type LNR (lightest retaliation) under complete information; initial population majority LNR (≈80%), with CMPT (10%) and CPRT (20%) invaders.","Section 5.2.2 states they add GT, TFT, and LNR separately and for each create a polymorphic population with majority NR and 10% CMPT, 20% CPRT.",0.9,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab participants.,1,True,Coevolutionary simulation of strategies.,1,False,Numerical coevolutionary runs.,1,10,From Table 3.,1,50,Auction Periods = 50.,1,1,Continuous bids.,0.8,N/A,No contributions or humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No chat; simulated agents.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,No human IDs.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,1,LNR is an NR strategy that retaliates with a single competitive bid after deviation.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment cost parameter.,0.3,N/A,"Punishment realized through auction payoff details, not a separate tech parameter.",0.3,0,No reward behavior.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Endowment per period not specified.,0.3,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not numerically reported.,0.4,N/R,Group payoff not given.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations.,1,"Same baseline GSP repeated auction as other complete-information runs. Strategy set includes CPRT, CMPT, and LNR, an NR strategy that retaliates once with a competitive bid in a specified future period after observing deviation, then returns to cooperative bidding. Initial majority LNR with 10% CMPT and 20% CPRT. Coevolutionary algorithm as in Section 5.1. Results: CMPT strategies die out early; CPRT and LNR weakly invade each other, with cooperative bidding sustained and bids converging to GEAE, yielding higher advertiser revenues than the UBNE outcome.",Summarized from Section 5.2.2 plus general algorithm description.,0.8
10.1016_j.ins.2016.07.041.md,"Simulation 3 – CPRT, CMPT and GT (NR-I) with incomplete information","Third group of experiments: 'SSA auctions with incomplete information' using incremental NR-I strategies; figure 9 is 'Coevolution of the CPRT, CMPT and GT strategies with incomplete information.'",1,"Available strategies in populations under incomplete information: CPRT (always decreasing bids by small amount), CMPT (always increasing bids by small amount), and NR-I of type GT (incremental nice and retaliatory: gradually decrease bids, then retaliate by increasing for all remaining periods upon detecting deviation). Initial population majority NR-I GT (80%), plus minority CPRT and CMPT.","Section 5.2.4 defines NR-I and Fig. 9 caption specifies CPRT, CMPT, GT under incomplete information with majority GT.",0.9,False,No human advertisers; strategies encoded algorithmically.,1,False,"Simulation, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab with participants.,1,True,Coevolutionary simulation extended to incomplete information.,1,False,This section reports simulation results complementing theory.,1,10,"They change information structure and encoding but keep the same SSA scenario unless otherwise stated; # of advertisers not redefined, so assume 10 as in Table 3.",0.8,50,Number of simulated periods P is not redefined; consistent with earlier 50-period design.,0.8,1,Bids adjust by small increments up or down; not all-or-nothing.,0.9,N/A,No contributions or humans.,1,N/A,Not a public good.,1,N/A,No modeled communication; only monitoring of rivals' bids algorithmically.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,"Punishment is coded in strategy behavior, not in a social identity context.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents are programmed for P periods; no displayed rounds for humans.,1,1,"NR-I GT prescribes retaliatory increases in bids upon deviation detection, functioning as punishment.",1,N/A,No explicit per-punishment cost parameter is given.,0.3,N/A,"Punishment acts through auction payoff, not via an exogenous multiplier.",0.3,0,No explicit prosocial reward.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/R,Endowment per period not discussed in incomplete information extension.,0.3,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,They validate neutral evolutionary stability; efficiency metrics are not reported.,0.4,N/R,Group payoff values not provided.,0.4,N/A,No human advertisers.,1,N/A,No human advertisers.,1,N/A,No human advertisers.,1,N/A,No human advertisers.,1,No human,All agents are programmed strategies in a simulation.,1,"SSA auctions with incomplete information about other advertisers' values and slot CTRs; advertisers cannot compute GEAE/UBNE exactly. Strategy encoding (Fig. 8): each row of the bid matrix is a sequence of bids over P periods. CPRT: starting from an arbitrary SNE bid b_u1, repeatedly decreases bids by small increments ε_ui over periods. CMPT: starting from b_v1, repeatedly increases bids by ε_vi. NR-I GT: starts from b_w1, initially decreases bids by ε_wi up to some period k, then upon deviation detection increases by ε_wj for all remaining periods. Constraints: ε_ui<ε_uj, ε_vi<ε_vj, ε_wi<ε_wj, and initial bids plus cumulative ε remain in [0,1]. Coevolutionary simulation similar to complete-information case; initial population 80% NR-I GT, 10% CMPT, 10% CPRT (or similar small minorities). Results (Fig. 9): once GT strategies are adopted by a majority, no other strategy can strongly invade; GT co-exists with CPRT, confirming that NR-I has neutral evolutionary stability under incomplete information.","Summarized from Section 5.2.4, Fig. 8 and Fig. 9.",0.9
