en and reduced payoff is parameterized.,0.3,0,Only punishment via rejection is discussed; no positive reward mechanism beyond standard payoffs.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate sanctioning technology.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate sanctioning technology.,1,100,"Section 6.1.3: 'For every experiment, we start with an amount of 100 being equally divided over the n agents.'",1,N/R,"They report convergence to offer strategies (e.g., 50 out of 100) but do not summarize as normalized contribution rates.",0.4,50,"For this parameter set, analysis predicts and simulations find proposer offering 50 (i.e., 50-50 split).",0.9,N/R,They do not report total payoffs relative to maximum possible as a numeric efficiency statistic.,0.2,N/R,"Qualitative outcomes (e.g., allocations) are described but not aggregated as average group payoff.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,No human,Purely computational model with artificial agents and learning automata.,1,"Two-player continuous Ultimatum Game over resource 100; both agents use Homo Egualis utility with α=0.6, β=0.7; learning via CALA over 2000 repeated games; rejection if utility < 0 functions as punishment.","Summarized from Sections 6.1.1–6.1.3 and 7.1.1, first experiment.",1
