10.1007_s10458-010-9122-9.md,"PGG social networks – static network, mixed FS/DSr/DSh, varying population size","Section 7.2.1: conditions with static population structure (no rewiring) for populations of 50, 100, 200, 500 agents with 33% FS, 33% DSr, 33% DSh.",0.95,"Network structure: static scale-free network (no rewiring); population composition 33% FS, 33% DSr, 33% DSh; population size varied (50, 100, 200, 500)",Key manipulated variables described are population size and whether rewiring is allowed; here the 'static' (no rewiring) condition across sizes.,0.95,False,Public Goods Games are run between artificial agents in simulation.,1,False,Not an empirical human or field experiment.,1,False,No physical laboratory setting.,1,True,Model described in Section 6.2 as computational model on social networks.,1,False,"Results are from stochastic simulations, not only analytical derivations.",1,100,"Section 7.2.1: experiments for 50, 100, 200, 500 agents; 100 is a central case; but condition conceptually covers all these sizes. A single numeric value required, so 100 chosen as representative.",0.4,600000,"Section 6.2.4: 'we run 6,000 n games, with n the number of agents.' For n=100, this is 600,000 pairwise games.",0.7,1,PGG contribution is continuous from 0 to C; agents choose continuous strategies.,1,0,Each agent has a private amount C and chooses how much to invest; endowment initially in private account.,1,0.75,"PGG payoff: pool multiplied by r and divided equally among n participants; marginal per capita return is r/n. For pairwise PGG (n=2) with r=1.5 (Section 6.2.2), MPCR = 1.5/2 = 0.75.",0.8,N/A,Agents are simulated; no explicit communication channel beyond game and punishment moves.,1,N/A,No experimental GUI or information display for human participants.,1,N/A,Identity is implicit in pairwise network links; no separate treatment of identity visibility.,0.7,N/A,No reward phase separate from game payoffs.,1,N/A,No humans or UI notion of shown rounds.,1,1,Section 6.2.2: agents may punish with probability p_i^p based on utility; punishment reduces opponent’s reward by e=3.,1,1,"From Section 3.2.2 and 6.2.2: altruistic punishment lets players 'give up a small amount c to decrease another player’s gain by a larger amount e'; with r=1.5, C=10, e=3 and p_i^p=-u_i/C; implies natural per-unit cost c=1 and effect e=3 (1:3 ratio).",0.6,3,Text states effect of punishment on reward is e=3 (Section 6.2.2 and footnote 11).,0.9,0,No mention of positive reward mechanism; only punishment and rewiring.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"Section 6.2.2: 'We use C=10, r=1.5, and e=3' for the PGG.",1,N/R,They report converged strategies (around 9) but not normalized as a fraction of C in a summary statistic.,0.5,9,"Section 7.2.1: 'we observe a good result in a static population structure (i.e., around 9, where 10 would be desired)' as average learned contribution.",0.9,N/R,"Performance is reported as percentage of neighbor pairs with similar strategies, not payoff efficiency.",0.3,N/R,No aggregated group payoff or average earnings reported; focus on strategies and convergence.,0.2,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,No human,Simulated PGG on scale-free networks with artificial agents.,1,"Pairwise Public Goods Game on a static scale-free network; population composed of 33% fixed-strategy cooperators (FS, always contribute C=10 and punish), 33% DSr (start at 0 contribution), 33% DSh (initial contributions ~ N(7,1)); each iteration selects a random agent and a random neighbor to play a two-player PGG with r=1.5, C=10; altruistic punishment with cost approximately 1 and effect 3 applied probabilistically with p_i^p=-u_i/C; no rewiring allowed; results show convergence to contributions around 9 and performance (agreement on strategies among neighbors) around 75%.",Summarized from Sections 6.2.1–6.2.4 and 7.2.1 for static-structure condition.,0.