10.1007_s10458-010-9122-9.md,"PGG social networks – rewiring allowed, mixed FS/DSr/DSh, varying population size","Section 7.2.1: conditions with dynamic structure (rewiring) for populations of 50, 100, 200, 500 agents with 33% FS, 33% DSr, 33% DSh.",0.95,"Network structure: dynamic scale-free network with rewiring based on utility; population composition 33% FS, 33% DSr, 33% DSh; population size varied (50, 100, 200, 500)",Main manipulation relative to static condition is allowing rewiring according to p_i^r=-u_i/C.,0.95,False,No humans involved; simulation.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; computational study.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Described as simulation of agents on networks with learning and rewiring.,1,False,"Results are simulated, not purely analytical.",1,100,"As in static condition, multiple n; 100 used as representative numeric value.",0.4,600000,"Same learning protocol: 6,000 n games; for 100 agents this is 600,000.",0.7,1,"Continuous contribution space [0,C].",1,0,Endowment C is privately held before contribution decision.,1,0.75,Two-player PGG with r=1.5 implies MPCR=r/n=1.5/2.,0.8,N/A,No human communication channel modelled.,1,N/A,No user-facing summaries; simulation only.,1,N/A,Punishment is directed to a unique neighbor in the link; no separate identity-visibility parameter.,0.7,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human-experiment interface.,1,1,Same altruistic punishment rule as in static case is used.,1,1,"Same punishment parameters (c≈1, e=3) as in static condition.",0.6,3,Same e=3 punishment effect parameter.,0.9,0,No separate rewarding mechanism discussed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same C=10 as in other PGG simulations.,1,N/R,They report improved convergence to near full cooperation but not as normalized contribution rate.,0.5,10,Section 7.2.1: 'even better result in a dynamic structure'; performance nearly 100% and described as achieving 'full cooperation' when rewiring used; in this PGG full cooperation corresponds to contributing C=10.,0.8,N/R,No explicit payoff-relative-to-maximum is reported.,0.3,N/R,"Outcomes are reported in terms of strategies and network structure, not payoffs.",0.2,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,No human,Fully computational model on networks.,1,"Same pairwise PGG as static condition (r=1.5, C=10, MPCR=0.75) on an initial scale-free network, with three agent types (FS, DSr, DSh); agents may both punish and rewire: rewiring probability p_i^r=-u_i/C if unwiring will not isolate an agent; new link formed to random neighbor of the punished neighbor; simulations show roughly 10% fewer games to convergence, higher average contributions approaching C=10, almost 100% performance (neighbors with matching strategies), and emergence of strong hubs (cooperators) while defectors become weakly connected.",Summarized from Sections 6.2.2–6.2.4 and 7.2.1 dynamic (rewiring) condition.,0.95
10.1007_s10458-010-9122-9.md,"PGG social networks – static network, varying FS proportion, n=100",Section 7.3: static population structure results for 100 agents with varying proportion of FS agents.,0.95,Network structure: static scale-free network (no rewiring); population size n=100; varying proportion of FS agents (0%–100%) with remaining agents split equally between DSr and DSh,Key manipulation here is percentage of FS agents in static network with 100 agents.,0.95,False,All PGG social-network studies are simulations with artificial agents.,1,False,Not an empirical study with humans.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Same computational model as in Section 6.2 used.,1,False,Section 7.3 reports simulation results (box plots).,1,100,Section 7.3 explicitly reports results for population of 100 agents.,1,600000,"Same rule of 6,000 n games; n=100 implies 600,000 games.",0.8,1,Continuous contributions to PGG.,1,0,Initial endowment C is private; players choose contributed portion.,1,0.75,"Pairwise PGG with r=1.5, n=2 gives MPCR 0.75.",0.8,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,Identity visibility not parametrically manipulated.,0.7,N/A,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,1,Same probabilistic punishment as other PGG network experiments.,1,1,"Assume same punishment cost as base PGG network model (per-unit cost 1, effect 3).",0.6,3,Same punishment impact (e=3).,0.9,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,10,Same C=10 as in other PGG simulations.,1,N/R,"Results discussed as average converged strategies, not normalized rates.",0.4,N/R,"For this multi-level manipulation (FS proportion) they describe qualitative patterns (e.g., convergence to 7 with 0% FS, higher with more FS) but do not give a single numeric value per condition in the text.",0.3,N/R,Efficiency is not computed; they report 'performance' as percentage of neighbors with similar strategies.,0.2,N/R,No payoff summary reported per FS-proportion condition.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Static scale-free network with 100 agents; proportion of FS agents varied from 0% to 100%, with remaining agents split equally between DSr (initially 0) and DSh (initially ~N(7,1)); pairwise PGG (r=1.5, C=10) with probabilistic punishment p_i^p=-u_i/C but no rewiring; simulations repeated 50 times per setting; results: with 0% FS agents, DS agents converge to the most cooperative initial strategy (≈7); adding FS agents allows DS agents to learn more cooperative strategies, but performance (fraction of neighbors with common strategy) initially decreases as FS share increases until DS strategies adjust toward 10.",Summarized from Section 7.3 static-population description.,0.95
10.1007_s10458-010-9122-9.md,"PGG social networks – rewiring allowed, varying FS proportion, n=100",Section 7.3: dynamic population structure (rewiring allowed) results for 100 agents with varying FS proportion.,0.95,Network structure: dynamic scale-free network with rewiring; population size n=100; varying proportion of FS agents (0%–100%) with remaining agents split equally between DSr and DSh,Same FS proportion manipulation as previous condition but now with rewiring permitted.,0.95,False,Simulation study with artificial agents.,1,False,Not an experiment with human or field data.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Same agent-based PGG network model with rewiring.,1,False,Section 7.3 provides simulation-based findings.,1,100,Section 7.3 focuses on populations of 100 agents for FS proportion analysis.,1,600000,"Same protocol (6,000 n games) gives 600,000 games for 100 agents.",0.8,1,PGG is continuous-action.,1,0,Agents start with full endowment in private accounts.,1,0.75,Two-player PGG with r=1.5; MPCR=r/2.,0.8,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No experimental UI.,1,N/A,Identity not treated as manipulated information.,0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No humans to show rounds to.,1,1,Same probabilistic punishment model as in other PGG network simulations.,1,1,Same inferred punishment cost (1) per unit as base PGG model.,0.6,3,Same punishment effect parameter e=3.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,10,Same C=10 as elsewhere.,1,N/R,"They discuss qualitative movement toward full cooperation, but not normalized contributions per FS level.",0.4,N/R,Text notes that 'a low percentage of FS agents is already sufficient to achieve full cooperation' but does not give numeric contributions per FS percentage; full cooperation implies 10 but timing and exact averages per condition are not numerically specified.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures.,0.2,N/R,No payoff figures reported.,0.2,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,No human,Simulation with artificial agents on adaptive networks.,1,"Dynamic scale-free network with 100 agents; FS proportion varied from 0% to 100%, rest DSr and DSh; pairwise PGG with r=1.5, C=10, probabilistic punishment p_i^p=-u_i/C; rewiring allowed with probability p_i^r=-u_i/C provided no node loses last link; when rewiring, agent connects to random neighbor of former neighbor; results: introducing even a low percentage of FS agents and allowing rewiring yields nearly full cooperation (contributions close to 10) and very high performance (near 100% neighbors with same strategy); strong cooperative hubs emerge, especially when FS agents are few (e.g., one node with degree 63 in 10% FS case).",Based on Section 7.3 dynamic-network discussion and Figure 5 description.,0.95
10.1628_0932456042776140.md,Game H – No communication,"Condition with game H payoffs (PunEff=7, MaxCost=8) and no preplay communication.",1,"Game payoff structure H (PunEff=7, MaxCost=8); preplay communication = none","Paper describes 3x3-1 design varying game (H,M,L) and communication (none, e-mail, video).",1,True,Human student subjects played one-shot bargaining games.,1,True,Random assignment to roles and controlled treatment conditions in a lab.,1,True,Conducted in university classrooms/cabins with experimental control.,1,False,No computer/agent simulations; only human decisions.,1,False,No purely theoretical or closed-form model tested without data.,1,2,Two-person sequential bargaining game: first mover and second mover.,1,1,Games were played one-shot.,1,1,"Players choose between discrete actions (A/B,1/2), not contributions; mapped as free choice rather than all-or-nothing contribution mechanism.",0.4,N/A,No public goods or contributions; sequential bargaining over a fixed pie.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game with marginal per capita return.,1,0,Explicitly described as no-communication treatment.,1,0,No feedback about others beyond own pair’s outcome mentioned.,0.8,0,Partners remain anonymous; identities never revealed.,1,0,Same anonymity; no identity revelation for rewarding either.,1,1,Subjects know they make a single decision in a one-shot game.,0.9,1,Second mover can choose off-equilibrium B1 that reduces first mover’s payoff at own cost; interpreted as punishment option.,1,2,"From decision formula using same payoff structure for all games: SM gets 7 at B2 and 5 at B1, so cost = 7-5=2.",0.9,14,"PunEff = (FM loss)/(SM cost) = 7 in game H. With SM cost 2, FM loss =14, so punishmentTech=14 tokens removed per 2-token cost, i.e. 7 per 1; we record absolute difference 24-10=14 (FM B2 24 vs B1 10).",0.8,1,"If FM deviates to A and SM chooses 2, outcome A2 is described as ‘efficient equal-split reward’.",1,8,"From decision formula: at A1 SM=20, at A2 SM=12, so SM loses 8 to reward FM.",0.9,8,"At A1 FM=4, at A2 FM=12, so FM gains 8 when SM rewards; recorded as reward impact per decision.",0.9,N/R,"Subjects receive a show-up fee plus outcome-based DM, but no fixed per-round endowment is specified as a decision budget.",0.3,N/A,No contribution decisions; only discrete bargaining actions.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts defined in this bargaining game.,1,N/R,Paper reports proportions of A and punishment/reward choices but not efficiency metrics relative to maximum total payoff per pair.,0.4,N/R,"Outcomes at each node reported in DM, but average group payoff per treatment not summarized.",0.4,Germany,"Subjects were students at Otto-von-Guericke University Magdeburg, Germany.",1,N/R,Only described as students; no age statistics provided.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.0,University students,Described as students at Otto-von-Guericke University Magdeburg.,1,On site,Conducted in classrooms and soundproof cabins at the university.,1,"Two-stage sequential bargaining: FM chooses A or B; SM, informed of FM’s choice, chooses 1 or 2. Unique SPE outcome is B2 (FM-favoring). A2 is efficient equal-split; A1 and B1 are off-equilibrium punishment/reward outcomes. One-shot interaction with random anonymous matching.",Summarized from sections 2–3 and Appendix describing the game tree and procedure.,1
