10.1007_s13235-019-00339-7.md,Infinite population – no second-order punishment,"Condition described in Sect. 2.1.1 and Appendix A: infinite well-mixed population with cooperators, defectors, punishers, individual disguise, and no second-order punishment.",1,Presence of second-order punishment: absent; Disguise probability q (0–1); Disguise cost γ; Punishment fine α; Punishment cost β; Synergy factor r; Group size N.,"Model varies these parameters in the infinite-population, no-second-order-punishment case.",1,False,"Study uses theoretical evolutionary game models (replicator dynamics), not human participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; purely theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory setting; mathematical model only.,1,True,"Uses numerical phase portraits and dynamics based on replicator equations for infinite populations (e.g., Fig. 2).",1,True,Derives and analyzes replicator equations and equilibria analytically in Appendices A and main text.,1,5,"Representative numerical examples use N=5 (e.g., Fig. 2 caption: ‘N=5’).",1,1,"Public goods interaction is a single-stage game repeated by evolutionary replication, not discrete repeated rounds for the same players.",0.7,0,Each cooperator or punisher ‘contributes a fixed amount c’; defector contributes nothing; no choice of partial contribution level.,1,N/A,No human subjects; endowment allocation concept not applicable.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return r/N is implied but no single numeric MPCR is fixed across all analyses.,0.5,N/A,No communication among agents is modeled; also no human interface.,1,0,Payoffs are determined mathematically; no feedback or display mechanism is modeled.,1,0,Model has anonymous strategies in a well-mixed population; no identities.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,No concept of displayed rounds in this theoretical model.,1,1,Peer punishment with cost β and fine α is core to the model.,1,1,"Representative parameter choice in figures: β=0.5 and fine α=0.5; cost per unit fine effectively β/α=1 for those examples. Since per-unit definition is not explicit, set to 1 as normalized.",0.3,1,"Per above, α and β often set equal (0.5), implying 1 unit fine per 1 unit cost in example parameterization.",0.3,0,Only punishment is modeled; no reward or exclusion in this paper’s core model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Each cooperator and punisher contributes a fixed amount c; representative examples use c=1 (e.g., Fig. 2 caption). Treat c as the per-round endowment.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as equilibrium compositions of strategies, not normalized contribution rates; with all-or-nothing, contribution rate equals fraction of C and P but no single DV value is given.",0.4,N/R,No explicit aggregate contribution amounts per group reported; only qualitative dynamics.,0.2,N/R,"Paper discusses regions of cooperation/defection, not normalized efficiency metrics.",0.2,N/R,Average payoffs are formulated symbolically but no specific numeric group payoff outcomes reported as main DVs.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Infinite-population replicator dynamics with no empirical implementation.,1,"Public goods game in infinite well-mixed population with 3 strategies (C,D,P). Cooperators and punishers each contribute c to a common pool, multiplied by r and shared among N. Defectors may pay disguise cost γ with probability q to avoid being punished with probability p; punishers pay cost β to impose fine α on each non-disguised defector. No second-order punishment: cooperators are not punished.","Summarized from Sect. 2.1, 2.1.1 and payoff equations (1).",1
10.1007_s13235-019-00339-7.md,Infinite population – with second-order punishment,Condition described in Sect. 2.1.2 and Appendix B: infinite well-mixed population with second-order punishment added.,1,Presence of second-order punishment: present; Disguise probability q (0–1); Disguise cost γ; Punishment fine α; Punishment cost β; Synergy factor r; Group size N.,This condition adds second-order punishment as the key independent manipulation relative to the no-second-order case; other parameters varied as in the model.,1,False,Analytical and numerical evolutionary dynamics; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting; theoretical model only.,1,True,"Phase portraits and dynamics under replicator equations with second-order punishment (e.g., Fig. 3).",1,True,"Derives full replicator dynamics, equilibria, and stability in Appendix B.",1,5,Numerical illustrations use N=5 (Fig. 3 caption).,1,1,"One-shot public goods interaction per grouping, with evolutionary updating rather than repeated rounds with same players.",0.7,0,"As before, each contributor gives fixed amount c; defectors give nothing.",1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,MPCR equals r/N but r and N are not fixed to a single value across all analyses; no explicit MPCR DV.,0.5,N/A,No communication in model; no subjects.,1,0,No informational feedback mechanism is modeled.,1,0,Population is anonymous and well-mixed; no identities.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No round counter or display in the theoretical setting.,1,1,Peer punishment applied to both defectors and cooperators (second-order punishment).,1,1,"As in the first infinite-population condition, examples use α=β=0.5 (Fig. 3); cost per unit fine normalized to 1.",0.3,1,"With α=β numerically, one unit cost yields one unit fine in the representative parameterization.",0.3,0,No prosocial reward; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"As before, c=1 used in numerical examples, treated as per-round endowment.",1,N/R,"Paper focuses on stable states (all C, all D, all P, coexistence) rather than explicit contribution rates.",0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric group contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure presented; only qualitative outcomes.,0.2,N/R,Average payoffs are in symbolic form; not summarized numerically as a DV.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely mathematical and numerical analysis.,1,"Same public goods game as in the no-second-order case, but punishers also sanction cooperators (second-order free-riders). In payoff equations (2), cooperators are fined α by each punisher and punishers incur additional cost β for punishing cooperators, on top of punishing defectors. Individual disguise for defectors (probability q, cost γ, success probability p) continues to reduce likelihood of being punished.",Summarized from Sect. 2.1.2 and payoff equations (2).,1
10.1007_s13235-019-00339-7.md,Finite population – no second-order punishment,"Condition in Sect. 2.2.1 and Fig. 4: finite population with cooperation, defection, punishment, and individual disguise, without second-order punishment.",1,"Presence of second-order punishment: absent; Disguise cost γ (e.g., 0.5 vs 1.5); Other parameters: population size Z, group size N, synergy factor r, punishment fine α, punishment cost β, disguise probability q, success probability p, mutation rate μ, selection strength η.",These quantities are specified for finite-population analysis without second-order punishment; γ is varied to compare low vs high disguise costs.,1,False,Finite-population dynamics are simulated and analyzed theoretically; no human data.,1,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Uses Markov process and numerical computation of stationary distribution and gradient of selection (Figs. 4).,1,True,Derives average payoffs and transition probabilities analytically (Sect. 2.2.1–2.2.3).,1,5,Public goods group size N=5 in finite-population figures (Fig. 4 caption).,1,1,Each update step involves a single public-goods interaction; evolutionary process is many steps but not framed as rounds within a fixed game for the same agents.,0.7,0,"Contribution rule identical to infinite-population model: cooperators and punishers contribute fixed c, defectors contribute 0.",1,N/A,No explicit initial endowment or allocation for human participants.,1,N/R,"MPCR = r/N implied (r=3, N=5 used) but not presented as a central reported value; they focus on strategy distributions.",0.6,N/A,No communication; no humans.,1,0,No interface or feedback beyond payoffs in the mathematical process.,1,0,No identities exist in the Markov model; strategies are anonymous.,1,0,No reward.,1,0,No explicit notion of rounds shown to agents.,1,1,Peer punishment is part of the payoff functions f_P and f_D.,1,1,"Representative finite-population parameters: α=0.5, β=0.5 (Fig. 4), suggesting 1 unit fine per 1 unit cost; normalized per-unit cost set to 1.",0.3,1,"With α=β numerically, punishment technology yields equal fine per cost.",0.3,0,Model in this paper includes only punishment and no reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,1,"As before, each cooperator and punisher contributes c=1 in example; use that as endowment.",1,N/R,"Finite-population results focus on stationary distribution over (i_C,i_D,i_P), not explicit normalized contribution rates.",0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric contribution amounts are reported from simulations.,0.2,N/R,"Results are described in terms of time spent in cooperation/defection/punishment regions, not efficiency ratios.",0.2,N/R,No numerical group payoff values reported as main outcomes.,0.3,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Population consists of abstract agents in a Markov process.,1,"Finite population of size Z (e.g., Z=100) where individuals adopt C, D, or P. Groups of size N are formed randomly to play the same public goods game as in infinite case (cooperators and punishers contribute c, contributions multiplied by r, shared among N, defectors may disguise with probability q at cost γ to avoid punishment with success probability p). Without second-order punishment, punishers sanction only defectors, paying cost β to fine them α. Evolution follows a mutation-selection process using pairwise comparison with mutation rate μ and selection strength η; stationary distribution and gradient of selection describe stochastic dynamics.",Summarized from Sects. 2.2.1–2.2.3 and Fig. 4 description.,1
10.1007_s13235-019-00339-7.md,Finite population – with second-order punishment,Condition in Sect. 2.2.2 and Fig. 5: finite population model with second-order punishment enabled.,1,"Presence of second-order punishment: present; Disguise cost γ (e.g., 0.5, 1.5, 2.5); Other parameters: population size Z, group size N, synergy factor r, punishment fine α, punishment cost β, disguise probability q, success probability p, mutation rate μ, selection strength η.",This condition adds second-order punishment and varies disguise cost γ while keeping other parameters as specified in Fig. 5.,1,False,Finite-population simulations with no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Computes stationary distributions and selection gradients numerically for the finite-population Markov process (Fig. 5).,1,True,Analytical payoff expressions and transition rules given in Sect. 2.2.2–2.2.3.,1,5,Group size N=5 used in Fig. 5.,1,1,Single public-goods interaction per update; evolutionary Markov chain over many updates.,0.7,0,Same fixed-cost contribution structure as other conditions.,1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,MPCR r/N implied but not directly reported as a key scalar DV.,0.6,N/A,No communication or interface.,1,0,No display of others’ outcomes; purely internal Markov process.,1,0,Agents defined only by strategies; no identities.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No explicit rounds visible to agents.,1,1,Peer punishment targets both defectors and cooperators (second-order punishment) in this finite-population case.,1,1,"Numerical parameters identical to other finite cases: α=0.5, β=0.5; assume 1:1 fine-to-cost ratio.",0.3,1,"Given α=β, one cost unit generates one fine unit in examples.",0.3,0,No reward in this model.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,1,Each contributor invests c=1 as in other sections; used in finite-population parameter sets.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are described as time spent in regions of cooperation, defection, punishment; no explicit normalized contribution rate is provided.",0.4,N/R,No numeric total contributions per group reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is not computed explicitly; only qualitative regime descriptions.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs not summarized as a central DV in results.,0.3,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,"Finite population of model agents; data come from computation, not human behavior.",1,"Finite population size Z=100 with three strategies (C, D, P). Game structure as in other conditions, but punishers additionally sanction cooperators, paying cost β and inflicting fine α on them (second-order punishment). Defectors still may disguise (probability q, cost γ, success probability p) to avoid punishment. Strategy update follows pairwise comparison with mutation rate μ=1/Z and selection strength η=2. Stationary distribution over states and gradient of selection are analyzed for different disguise costs γ.",Summarized from Sects. 2.2.2–2.2.3 and Fig. 5 caption.,1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2004.03.002.md,Analytical model of negative reciprocity in a Prisoner's Dilemma,Paper presents only a formal analytical model (no distinct named experimental conditions); treated the single modeled game with punishment as one condition.,0.98,"Negative reciprocity parameter v (cost of punishing), punishment technology cost c, environmental hostility parameter b, group norm level v^n, enforcement rigor a","Model systematically varies v, c, b, v^n, and a to study effects on cooperation and fitness.",0.96,False,No human participants; purely theoretical and analytical modeling.,1,False,No controlled experiment is conducted; only mathematical analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported; only analytical derivations.,0.99,True,Paper develops closed-form game-theoretic and evolutionary analysis without simulation or data.,1,2,Underlying game explicitly described as a symmetric two-player Prisoner's Dilemma.,1,1,Model is a one-shot PD with added punishment; repeated interactions are discussed only conceptually.,0.95,1,Players choose discrete actions C or D; no continuous contribution choice so mapped as standard non-all-or-nothing public good not applicable; default to 1 as they are not constrained to a single fixed contribution level but a strategy choice.,0.6,N/A,Not a voluntary contribution game with endowments; and there are no human subjects.,1,N/R,Not framed as a linear public goods game with a reported marginal per capita return.,1,0,No communication or signaling stage is modeled between players.,0.95,0,Model specifies only payoffs and types; no feedback or summaries displayed.,0.95,0,Punishment is modeled as direct harm from victim to culprit; no notion of identity revelation stage.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,Single-shot interaction; number of rounds not a decision-relevant parameter.,0.95,1,Model explicitly adds a punishment technology where a victim can inflict harm h at cost c*h.,1,1,"Punishment cost is modeled as c*h; marginal cost parameter c lies between 0 and 1, so per unit harm cost is c. Set punishmentCost equal to c’s role as marginal cost; normalized here as 1 unit of c per unit h.",0.6,1,Technology is linear: harm h per expenditure c*h; thus 1 unit of harm per (1/c) units cost; encoded generically as 1 to indicate linear harm per unit h decision variable.,0.5,0,Only negative reciprocity (punishment) is modeled; no rewarding action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,Players’ payoffs are defined directly as utilities/fitness from outcomes; no explicit endowment specified.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome variables are cooperation probability, fitness, and equilibrium v; no normalized contribution rate defined.",1,N/R,Game is not structured as allocating a numeric contribution amount from an endowment.,1,N/R,"While efficient all-C outcome is discussed conceptually, no explicit efficiency ratio values are reported.",0.8,N/R,Group fitness functions are expressed symbolically; no single numeric group payoff is reported as a result.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical with no empirical component.,1,"Symmetric two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with material payoffs: (C,C) = (1,1), (D,D) = (0,0), (C,D) = (-1,2), (D,C) = (2,-1). Negative reciprocity modeled via parameter v: after receiving the sucker payoff, a victim can impose harm h on the defector at personal cost c*h (0 < c < 1) and gains utility v ln h. Optimal harm h* = v/c; fitness cost of punishing is v; modified payoffs for sucker/temptation are (-1 - v, 2 - v/c). Evolutionary dynamics for v, group norms v^n, enforcement a, and encounter frequency f( v̄ ) = exp( - v̄ / b ) are analyzed.","Summarized structure and key parameters of the analytical game as described in Sections 2, 5, and 6.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0061458.md,"Session 1 – All strategies allowed, Profile A, init 90% Villains–10% Informants, q=0.3","Directly described under “All Strategies Available, Parameter Profile A” as Session 1 with Profile A, initialization 90% Villains–10% Informants, normal inertia (q=0.3).",1,"Strategy set: Paladin, Apathetic, Informant, Villain all available; Parameter profile: A (ε=10); Initialization: 90% Villains–10% Informants for periods 1–5; Behavioral inertia q=0.3; Endogenous conviction probability r determined by fraction of Paladins+Informants.","Treatment dimensions explicitly stated: strategies allowed, parameter profile, initialization mix, and inertia level.",1,True,Human subjects in a lab making decisions over multiple periods.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized pairing and controlled treatment variables.,1,True,Conducted at the Experimental Social Science Laboratory at UC Irvine.,1,False,"Session 1 involves human participants, not only computer agents.",1,False,No formal closed-form analytical model estimated in this session; it is behavioral data.,1,2,Subjects are randomly assigned into groups of two each period (criminal and injured party).,1,30,Each session lasts a minimum of 25 periods with 75% continuation up to a maximum of 35. Exact realized number for Session 1 is not specified; approximate mid-range of 30 used.,0.2,1,"Game is a binary decision to steal a fixed amount δ or not, and to report or not; there is no continuous contribution choice, so players effectively choose discrete actions rather than contributing a fraction. Coded as 1 since there is no contribution scale in the public-goods sense.",0.3,0,No public-good account; each player starts each period with full endowment y in private account.,1,N/A,This is not a public goods game; there is no group multiplier or marginal per capita return.,1,0,No communication or chat between participants is mentioned; anonymity and separation by dividers are emphasized.,0.9,1,"Results