10.1016_j.amc.2024.128844.md,Simulation – Heterogeneity PEPG public goods game (general parameter setting),"Single core simulation model on L×L lattice with C, D, and PEPG strategies; all reported results (Figs. 1–9) vary only parameters r, a, γ within this one model rather than define separate experimental treatments with qualitatively different rules.",0.7,"Enhancement factor r (1<r<5), distribution parameter a (0–4), bonus-penalty value γ (e.g., 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8), and fixed PEPG cost β=0.1; strategies available: C, D, PEPG.","Model description specifies r, a, γ, β as parameters; results section explicitly varies r, a, and γ while keeping β=0.1 and the game structure fixed.",0.9,False,Study uses computational simulations on a square lattice; no human participants.,1,False,"It is a numerical simulation study of an evolutionary game, not a controlled lab or field experiment with subjects.",1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; purely computational.,1,True,"They describe Monte Carlo simulations on a 200×200 lattice, iterating strategy updates over time.",1,False,"Although the model is described mathematically, core results come from simulation experiments, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.9,40000,Population size is a 200×200 square lattice: 200*200 = 40000.,1,10000,Each trial has 1×10^4 iterations; cooperation level is based on iterations 9001–10000.,1,1,"Model description uses a unit investment c for contributors and zero for defectors, implying a binary choice to contribute c or not, not a continuous contribution level.",0.7,N/A,No human endowment distribution; evolutionary agents do not start with a private vs public allocation in the usual sense.,1,N/R,They state contributions are multiplied by enhancement factor r and divided heterogeneously by H_i(k); explicit marginal per capita return is not given and is nonstandard due to reputation-weighted allocation.,0.3,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely algorithmic strategy updates.,1,0,Agents observe only neighbor’s payoff for imitation via Fermi rule; no mention of explicit aggregate summaries being displayed.,0.6,N/A,No human subjects or identity concept; punishment/reward is automatic via PEPG mechanism.,1,N/A,No human or identity-revealing mechanism; rewards are algorithmic.,1,0,Simulation agents have no explicit knowledge of total iterations; paper does not state horizon information shown.,0.7,1,PEPG players impose penalties γ on defectors; also PEPG agents themselves pay cost β per group.,1,0.1,"Unit cost β for PEPG is set to 0.1, and they pay 4β per group; unit cost per punishment action is β=0.1.",0.9,0.2,Baseline simulations use γ=0.2 as unit bonus-penalty; each unit of punishment reduces defector payoff by γ.,0.8,1,PEPG players reward cooperators by γ per PEPG player in the group.,1,0.1,PEPG cost β=0.1 funds both reward and punishment actions; unit cost per reward-punish action is β.,0.7,0.2,Cooperators receive γ per PEPG player; default γ in many simulations is 0.2.,0.8,1,"Unit investment c is set to 1; contributors invest c in each group, functioning as the per-game endowment.",0.8,N/R,They track cooperation frequency f_c(k) and f_c (proportion of C and PEPG players). Exact numerical steady-state values are in figures only; no specific numeric values are reported in text.,0.3,N/R,Only proportions of cooperators/defectors are reported; not the average number of contribution units per player beyond binary contribution choice.,0.2,N/R,They compute and discuss average payoffs of cooperators and defectors qualitatively but do not report normalized efficiency (actual over maximum).,0.2,N/R,They discuss average cumulative payoffs per strategy type; no explicit numeric group payoff values are given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract nodes on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,All results are from Monte Carlo simulations of an evolutionary game with artificial agents.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a 2D L×L (e.g., 200×200) square lattice with periodic boundaries and von Neumann neighborhoods; group size 5 (focal + 4 neighbors), each player participates in 5 overlapping groups. Strategies: C (contribute c=1), D (contribute 0), PEPG (contribute c=1 and pay cost 4β per group to reward cooperators and punish defectors). Payoff in each group: contributions from C and PEPG summed and multiplied by enhancement factor r (1<r<5), then shared heterogeneously according to reputation-weighted factor H_i(k)=R_i(k)^a / Σ_j∈G R_j(k)^a. Reputation R_i(k) initialized uniformly in [1,100]; updated each round by +1 for C, −1 for D, +2 for PEPG, bounded to [1,100]. Strategy updating via Fermi rule with noise κ=0.1 using payoff comparison with a random neighbor. 50 independent trials per parameter set, 10,000 iterations each; cooperation level f_c computed from last 1000 iterations, averaging over trials. Robustness checks also run for lattice sizes 60×60 and 300×300 and different initial strategy mixes.","Summarizes the game structure, network, group formation, payoff, reputation, and update rules as described in Sections 2–3 and the robustness footnote.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0108423.md,Baseline model without peer selection,The paper explicitly describes a baseline model where the peer selection rule is not implemented and all group members participate in the PGG.,1,No peer selection; all candidates automatically become group members and participate in the PGG,Baseline condition is defined by the absence of participation/exclusion thresholds and peer selection rules.,1,False,Authors state they used an evolutionary game model analyzed by computer simulation; no human participants.,1,False,"This is a computational model, not a controlled human or field experiment.",1,False,No lab or in‑person environment; purely simulated agents.,1,True,"They state: 'We analyzed this model through 10,000 generations by computer simulation.'",1,False,"Results are obtained via simulations, not closed‑form analytical derivations.",1,100,"Baseline parameters given as N=100, m=5, h=10, x=1, μ=0.005.",1,10,h=10 is the number of units of time (rounds of grouping+PGG) per generation.,0.9,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount x and defectors contribute 0; there is no choice of partial contributions, so contribution decision is binary but not an ‘all‑or‑nothing’ endowment choice in the human‑experiment sense. However, since agents have only two discrete actions, we code as 1 to indicate free binary choice rather than enforced full contribution.",0.3,N/A,No human endowment allocation; only strategy types C or D and fixed cost x for C.,1,N/R,"Payoff is π^C(n_c)=b x n_c/n − x and π^D(n_c)=b x n_c/n; the per‑capita return on one cooperator’s contribution is b/n, but n varies after selection and is not fixed as a design parameter; they do not report a single MPCR value.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects or communication; simulated agents follow fixed rules.,1,N/A,Not applicable to simulations without interfaces.,1,N/A,"No explicit punishment/reward actions by agents; ostracism is modeled via selection rules, and there are no identities shown to human participants.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No human interface to display number of rounds.,1,0,Baseline has no ostracism or exclusion; only C vs D in PGG.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model specifies a contribution cost x=1 but not an explicit endowment or maximum resource per player.,0.3,N/R,They report qualitative outcome: 'cooperation never evolves'; numerical stationary contribution fraction is not tabulated.,0.4,N/R,No specific numeric contribution per player or per group outcome is provided.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,0.1,N/R,"Payoff functions are defined, but realized average group payoff values are not tabulated.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from computer simulations of an evolutionary game; no lab/field/online environment.,1,"Voluntary public goods game with two strategies (cooperator C, defector D); group size before any selection is m=5; population size N=100; benefit factor b between 1 and 2; contribution cost x=1; groups re‑formed randomly each unit of time; image score s ∈ [−5,5] tracks reputation; strategies updated via proportional imitation with mutation μ=0.005 over 10,000 generations.",Summarizes core structural parameters and rules explicitly stated for the baseline model.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0108423.md,Participation selection – Average group reputation Sg,One of the 16 peer selection rules: participation selection with group reputation defined as Average Sg.,1,Peer selection rule: participation selection only; group reputation Sg = average of candidate image scores; candidate participates if Average Sg ≥ k_pa,Model section defines participation selection using one of four Sg definitions; this row is for Average Sg.,1,False,Evolutionary game model via simulation; no human data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting; purely computational.,1,True,"Outcomes come from computer simulations over 10,000 generations.",1,False,No closed‑form analysis presented for outcomes.,1,100,"Baseline parameters N=100 are used unless otherwise varied (N=1,000 only in later robustness checks).",0.9,10,"h=10 units of time per generation for main simulations, including Fig. 1.",0.9,1,Agents choose between contributing x or 0; binary choice modeled as full contribution vs no contribution.,0.5,N/A,No explicit endowment or default allocation; simulated strategies C or D only.,1,N/R,Per‑capita return b/n depends on net group size n after selection; no single value reported.,0.4,N/A,No communication among simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects or interface.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment actions or identities displayed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Simulation without human awareness of rounds.,1,0,No exclusion from membership in pure participation selection; only self‑selection into groups.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewarding cooperators modeled.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Only contribution cost x=1 is specified.,0.3,N/R,"They describe that in the participation selection 'cooperation never evolves regardless of criterion type', but no precise asymptotic fraction is tabulated.",0.5,N/R,No numeric averages per player are given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency statistic reported.,0.1,N/R,"Payoff functions are defined, but no condition‑specific payoffs are listed.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulated evolutionary game.,1,"Peer selection: only participation condition is active. Each candidate observes Average Sg (mean of candidate image scores in its temporary group). If Average Sg ≥ k_pa for that candidate, they join and become a member; otherwise, they nonparticipate. No exclusion rule; nonparticipants receive payoff 0; reputations of nonparticipants do not change.",Summarizes the specific selection mechanism and its effect on participation.,1
