10.1098_rstb.2020.0300.md,Analytical signalling models across four two-person games,"The paper presents a unified formal/analytical model of gossip honesty decisions instantiated in four types of two-person games (stag-hunt, snowdrift, helping, punishment); these are analytical conditions within one modelling framework rather than separate empirical experiments.",0.9,"Game type and parameterization (stag-hunt with cooperating/defecting target: payoffs R,T,S,P; snowdrift with cooperating/defecting target: payoffs R,T,S,P; helping game with cooperating/defecting target: cost c and benefit b; punishment game with cooperating/defecting target: cost c and fine γ) and fitness interdependence values between gossiper and receiver (V_gr) and between gossiper and target (V_gt), plus presence/absence and magnitude of noise in parameter estimation (μ, σ).","Across the paper the authors vary the type of two-person game (four games), whether the target is cooperating or defecting, the payoff parameters (e.g., T, S, c, b, γ), and the fitness interdependence parameters V_gr and V_gt; in section on noise they add stochasticity with normal-distributed noise (μ, σ). These are the independent variables determining model outcomes.",0.93,False,"The study explicitly presents ""formal models"" and ""theoretical models"" with no human participants or data collection.",1,False,There is no controlled manipulation with human or field subjects; it is purely theoretical modelling.,1,False,No laboratory setting is used; the work is analytical.,1,True,"They use formal models and also explicitly ""add noise"" drawing from a normal distribution to examine probabilities of mistakes, which constitutes numerical/simulation analysis without human subjects.",0.86,True,"Core results are derived as inequalities and equilibrium conditions (e.g., inequalities (3.3)-(3.10)) in signalling games and fitness interdependence; this is formal analytical modelling.",1,3,"The gossip triad consists of a gossiper, a receiver, and a target, although only receiver and target play the two-person game.",0.98,1,"The paper states: ""We assume that the receiver and the target play a one-shot two-person game.""",1,1,"In the modelled games (stag-hunt, snowdrift, helping, punishment) players choose discrete strategies like cooperate/defect or help/not or punish/not; there is no continuous contribution level, so the public-goods style 'all-or-nothing' contribution restriction does not apply. Per instructions, 1 indicates players can choose among available actions rather than an all-or-nothing contribution in a contribution game.",0.4,N/A,"There is no public-goods contribution structure or endowment allocation; the games are generic 2x2 or help/punish games, and there are no human subjects.",1,N/R,"No linear public goods game or marginal per capita return is defined; only generic payoffs R,T,S,P,c,b,γ are specified.",1,0,Communication other than the gossip signal itself is not modelled; there is no explicit chat or negotiation channel.,0.9,0,The receiver only observes gossip and then interacts with the target; there is no mention of summaries of others’ earnings or actions.,0.95,0,"The punishment game is represented via payoffs (cost c, fine γ) but does not include identity revelation of punishers; identities are not modeled.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism is modeled; only helping (benefit b) and punishment (fine γ). Identity of any rewarding agent is not a concept in the model.,0.9,0,The game is explicitly one-shot and there is no notion of participants being shown a remaining number of rounds.,0.95,1,"One of the four games is a ""punishment game"" where the receiver may punish a defecting target at cost c imposing fine γ.",1,1,"In the punishment game examples (figure 2d,h; figure 3d,h) the cost of punishing is set to c=1.",0.95,1,In the punishment game examples the fine on the target is γ=1; per schema this is the punishment technology (fine per unit cost).,0.95,0,There is no explicit rewarding mechanism that increases another's payoff at a personal cost beyond the one-shot helping game (which is more akin to unilateral cooperation than institutionalized reward).,0.9,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/R,The models specify payoffs and costs/benefits but no fixed endowment of tokens or points.,1,N/R,"No contribution behavior or normalized contribution rate is defined; outcomes concern honesty vs dishonesty of gossip, not public good contributions.",1,N/R,There is no contribution amount variable in the model.,1,N/R,The paper does not compute efficiency as total payoff relative to a maximum; it focuses on conditions for honest vs dishonest equilibria.,1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff (over the triad) is reported as an outcome variable; only individual payoff structures and fitness inequalities.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved in these analytical/simulation models.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work consists of formal mathematical models and simulations without human or field data.,1,"Signalling-style model of gossip in a triad (gossiper, receiver, target). Receiver and target play a one-shot two-person game (stag-hunt, snowdrift, helping, punishment) with payoff parameters (R,T,S,P;c,b;γ). Gossiper knows target type (cooperator/defector) and chooses honest vs dishonest gossip; receiver fully trusts gossip. Gossiper’s fitness is a linear combination of receiver and target payoffs weighted by fitness interdependence parameters V_gr and V_gt. Analytical conditions (inequalities) characterize regions of V_gr, V_gt, and game parameters where honest vs dishonest gossip is evolutionarily optimal. Additional analysis introduces normally distributed noise in estimation of game parameters and/or fitness interdependence to study error probabilities in gossip decisions.",Summary of the described model structure and game-theoretic setup across sections 3(a) and related figures/tables.,0.97
