10.1177_0951629806065012.md,Theoretical compliance model with continuous action space and imperfect public monitoring,Paper develops a general repeated-game model with continuous compliance choice and imperfect public signal; no separate empirical conditions are defined.,1,"Not applicable – purely analytical model, no experimental treatment conditions with human subjects.","Model varies abstract parameters (discount factor, utility curvature, punishment length T, monitoring precision) but not in an experimental sense.",1,False,The work is entirely theoretical; no human participants or data are used.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; the paper presents game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or other empirical setting is described.,1,False,"Although dynamic games are analyzed, there is no numerical or agent-based simulation; results are closed-form/analytical.",1,True,The article constructs and solves a general class of compliance models analytically using game theory and dynamic programming.,1,N/R,Model allows a generic number of agents n≥2 and does not fix a numeric group size.,0.7,N/R,"The interaction is modeled as an infinitely repeated game, not a fixed number of rounds.",1,1,"Agents choose a compliance level ci in [0,1], a continuous range rather than an all-or-nothing choice.",1,N/A,No endowment or initial allocation is modeled in a public-goods experimental sense; pure utility over compliance and public good.,1,N/R,Paper does not instantiate a specific linear public-goods technology with a marginal per capita return.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,"Information structure is summarized via a public signal F(c,ω); not in terms of experimental feedback screens.",1,N/A,"Punishment is through reversion to a punishment regime, not targeted between identified individuals.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,Game is infinitely repeated; no experimental interface or display to participants.,1,1,Core of the model is a trigger mechanism leading to a finite or grim punishment regime when the public signal falls below threshold τ.,1,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as a phase of non-compliance with payoff X, not as per-unit costly punishment actions by individuals.",1,N/A,No per-token punishment technology is defined; punishment is a state with lower utility X.,1,0,Only punishment regimes (reversion to low-payoff state) are modeled; no explicit rewarding institution.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses abstract compliance levels and utilities, not experimental endowments in tokens or money.",1,N/R,"Paper characterizes equilibria in compliance level a∈[0,1] but does not report numeric outcome values as data.",0.6,N/R,No numerical contributions or tokens are reported.,1,N/R,No quantitative efficiency ratios are computed; results are qualitative about existence/non-existence of full-compliance equilibria.,1,N/R,"Group payoffs are expressed symbolically via utility functions U and X, not as numeric outcomes.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a theoretical analysis with no empirical or field component.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated compliance/public-good game with n symmetric agents choosing continuous compliance levels in [0,1], imperfect public monitoring via common signal F(c,ω), trigger strategies with finite or grim punishment regimes, discount factor δ, and analysis of conditions under which full vs partial compliance equilibria exist.",Summary of the formal game structure as specified in the model section.,1
