10.1016_j.physa.2015.01.029.md,Replicator dynamics with punishment (general parameterization),"Paper analyzes an N-person evolutionary snowdrift game with three strategies (C, D, P) using replicator dynamics in a well-mixed population; this is the core analytical condition.",1,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r (0<r<1), group size N, punishment cost α, punishment fine β (with β ≥ α); initial frequencies x, y, z of C, D, P.","Eqs. (5)-(8) and Secs. 2–4 specify r, N, α, β and initial conditions as parameters whose variation determines steady-state frequencies and phases.",1,False,Model is purely evolutionary game theory with replicator equations; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only theoretical analysis and numerical simulations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only analytical/model work.,1,False,"This condition is the analytical replicator dynamics, not the Monte Carlo simulation algorithm.",1,True,"Derives and studies closed-form dynamical equations (replicator dynamics Eqs. (3),(5)-(8)) without sampling noise.",1,5,"Representative value N=5 used extensively (e.g., Fig. 1 and flow diagram Fig. 4) to illustrate dynamics with punishment.",0.8,1,Evolutionary game consists of repeated one-shot N-person interactions; each interaction is a single round game before strategy updating.,0.7,1,"Players choose among discrete strategies C, D, or P; there is no continuous contribution choice, so they are not restricted to 'all-or-nothing' contributions of an endowment.",0.7,N/A,"No endowment or public-good contribution model; payoffs are from task cost-sharing, not contributions.",1,N/R,Public goods marginal per capita return is not defined or discussed; model instead uses benefit b and total cost c of a task.,1,0,No communication or signaling among agents is modeled.,1,0,Agents update strategies based on payoffs and replicator dynamics; no information displays are modeled.,1,0,Well-mixed anonymous population; no identities or recognizability are modeled.,1,0,No rewards are present in the model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary dynamics without any notion of displayed round count.,1,1,Model explicitly adds a punishing strategy P that pays cost α and imposes fine β (Sec. 2).,1,0.01,"Most figures with punishment fix α=0.01 (e.g., Fig. 1, Fig. 2, Fig. 3); use this as the focal analytical condition.",0.8,0.05,"Punishment fine parameter β set to 0.05 in several analyses (e.g., Fig. 1, Fig. 3, Fig. 4).",0.8,0,No reward or positive sanctioning mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent from the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent from the model.,1,N/R,"Model defines benefit b and cost c, but not an endowment from which agents contribute.",1,N/R,"Outputs are frequencies of strategies C, D, P, not normalized contribution rates of an endowment.",1,N/R,No individual contribution amount is defined; only participation in cooperative or punishing actions.,1,N/R,"Paper reports steady-state frequencies of strategies, not efficiency ratios of realized vs. maximal group payoffs.",1,N/R,"Analyses focus on payoffs of strategies and their frequencies, but no explicit aggregate group payoff levels are tabulated.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and numerical study without human subjects.,1,"N-person evolutionary snowdrift game in a well-mixed infinite population with three pure strategies: cooperators (C), defectors (D), punishers (P). Payoff parameters: benefit b, total cost c with ratio r=c/b; punishment cost α, punishment fine β≥α. Dynamics follow replicator equations for frequencies x, y, z.",Summarizes the core analytical model structure as described in Sections 2 and 3.,1
