10.1098_rstb.2015.0011.md,No punishment public goods game,"Paper describes two versions of a public goods game: a 'no punishment game' played first for 10 rounds, followed by a 'punishment game'. This row captures the no-punishment condition.",1,Institution type: no punishment (baseline public goods game without sanctioning stage),"Independent variable manipulated is the presence vs absence of a punishment institution. In this condition, only the standard contribution stage exists, with no option to assign negative tokens.",1,True,Human subjects (184 participants) played the games in computer clusters.,1,True,Controlled lab public goods game with random assignment into groups and imposed game rules.,1,True,Sessions conducted in computer clusters on Newcastle University campus; standard lab setting.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported; only human play.,1,False,No purely analytical or formal game without data; analysis is statistical on experimental data.,1,4,"They follow Herrmann et al. (2008) exactly and use the same payoff formula: each player gets 20 tokens, public good is multiplied by 1.6 and each player gets 0.4 times the group contribution. 1.6 / 0.4 = 4, so group size must be 4.",0.9,10,No punishment game is explicitly stated to be played for 10 rounds before introducing the punishment game.,1,1,"“Each player received 20 tokens per round and privately decided how many tokens to contribute to the group fund” indicating any amount from 0 to 20 is allowed, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Standard PGG structure: participants receive 20 tokens privately and then decide how many to contribute. All endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,"Public good (sum of contributions) is multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally among 4 players, so each player receives 1.6/4 = 0.4 per token contributed to the public fund.",0.95,0,Participants were instructed not to communicate with each other in any way; no chat or communication channel described.,1,1,"After each round “each player was shown the contribution and income of all players in her group”, which is a round summary of others’ contributions and incomes.",1,0,"No punishment is available in this condition, so no punishment identities; set to 0 by design.",1,0,No reward mechanism is mentioned at all in either version of the game.,1,1,"Participants were told they would play the current game for 10 rounds before a different version, implying known finite horizon. Exact on-screen display is not specified but public goods experiments of this design typically display round numbers; inferred.",0.5,0,This is explicitly the ‘no punishment game’; there is no stage where players can assign negative tokens.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,0,Only negative tokens (punishment) are mentioned in the other condition; no positive reward stage exists here.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,20,"“In the no punishment game, each player received 20 tokens per round …”",1,N/R,"Paper reports regression coefficients and qualitative patterns, but not average contributions expressed as a fraction of endowment for each condition.",0,N/R,No table or text gives mean contribution levels in tokens by condition; only model-based predicted curves shown in figures not numerically specified.,0.1,N/R,No explicit or computable measure of group payoff relative to maximum reported for this condition alone.,0,N/R,Total or average earnings per group/condition are not reported numerically.,0,United Kingdom,"Participants were tested at Newcastle University (UK). While nationality is not explicitly listed, typical subject pool is local; we code by country of study location.",0.7,Mean 20.8 years; detailed distribution N/R,Paper states “mean age 20.8 years”; no further age distribution details.,1,77 males; remaining 107 participants of other/unspecified genders,"“One hundred and eighty-four subjects (77 males …)” implies 184-77=107 non-male, but specific female/other breakdown not given.",0.9,Primarily university students,Subjects described as “primarily students”; no further detail on degree level.,0.8,On site,"Data collected in person in computer clusters on campus, not online or in natural field settings.",1,"Standard linear public goods game following Herrmann et al. (2008). Each round, each of 4 players receives 20 tokens, chooses any contribution from 0 to 20 to the public fund; sum of contributions is multiplied by 1.6 and each player receives 0.4 times this sum, in addition to retained tokens. Groups are fixed over 10 rounds; contribution and income of all players are revealed after each round, but player identities cannot be tracked over rounds.",Summarizes structural details explicitly described in the methods section.,1
10.1098_rstb.2015.0011.md,Punishment public goods game,"Second condition described as the 'punishment game', played after the no-punishment game for 10 rounds.",1,Institution type: punishment public goods game (public goods game with decentralized costly punishment stage),Independent variable manipulated is presence of decentralized punishment. This condition adds a punishment stage after contributions are revealed.,1,True,Same human participants continue into the punishment game.,1,True,Controlled lab manipulation of institution (punishment vs no punishment) within subjects.,1,True,"Conducted in university computer clusters, identical environment to the no-punishment game.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation is used.,1,False,"Focus is on experimental data and regression models, not on closed-form analytical game theory.",1,4,"Same grouping as in the preceding no punishment game; payoff structure (multiplied by 1.6, 0.4 per token to each) implies 4 players.",0.9,10,"“Participants were then introduced to the punishment game, which was also played for 10 rounds.”",1,1,"Same contribution structure as no-punishment game: each player chooses how many of 20 tokens to contribute, implying continuous/discrete range, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each player again receives 20 tokens privately at start of each round and chooses contribution amount; tokens initially in private account.,1,0.4,"Contribution production technology unchanged: sum multiplied by 1.6 and evenly split among 4 players, giving 0.4 marginal per capita return.",0.95,0,Participants instructed not to communicate; no chat functionality described.,1,1,"After contributions, each player sees contribution and income of all players, plus, after punishment, a summary including number and cost of negative tokens given/received and adjusted income.",1,0,"Instructions indicate that players see only totals: “summary screen that included the number and cost of negative tokens given and received,” not the identity of specific punishers or recipients beyond role labels; player identity could not be tracked from round to round.",0.8,0,No rewards are implemented in any condition.,1,1,Participants are told they will play the punishment game for 10 rounds; standard to display current round/remaining rounds; inferred.,0.5,1,Punishment game “differed … in that after players' contributions and incomes … each player could assign up to 10 negative tokens to each other player.”,1,1,“Each negative token cost the giver one token …” so punishment cost per unit is 1 token.,1,3,“… and the recipient three tokens.” So each negative token reduces recipient’s payoff by 3 tokens.,1,0,Only negative (punishment) tokens exist; no mechanism to positively reward others is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Punishment game is a 'different version of the game'; contribution and payoff functions are unchanged except for punishment stage. Endowment remains 20 tokens per round as in the no-punishment game; no alternative endowment is stated.,0.95,N/R,Paper does not provide average normalized contributions by condition; only qualitative statement that punishment led to increased contributions and model-based plots.,0.1,N/R,Mean contributions in tokens under punishment vs no-punishment are not tabulated.,0.1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric (actual earnings vs maximum feasible) reported separately for this condition.,0,N/R,Aggregate or average group payoffs under punishment are not numerically stated.,0,United Kingdom,"Same participant pool and setting as the no-punishment game (Newcastle University, UK).",0.7,Mean 20.8 years; detailed distribution N/R,Same sample; no different age statistics by condition.,1,77 males; remaining 107 participants of other/unspecified genders,Same 184-participant pool is used in both games.,0.9,Primarily university students,Same participants as in first game; description unchanged.,0.8,On site,Still in on-campus computer clusters; environment is identical across both game variants.,1,"Same linear public goods structure as in the no-punishment game (4 players, 20-token endowment, contributions summed and multiplied by 1.6, each player receives 0.4 times group contribution plus retained tokens). Groups remain fixed across the 10 punishment rounds. After seeing all players’ contributions and incomes, each participant may assign 0–10 negative tokens to each of the other 3 group members; each negative token costs the punisher 1 token and reduces the target’s payoff by 3 tokens. A summary screen then shows each player the number and cost of negative tokens given and received and their income adjusted for punishment.",Restates the structural details given in the punishment game description.,1
