10.1086_696880.md,Analytical model – case 1 (consumers observe quality and litigation outcomes),Paper develops a formal repeated-game model; case 1 is the information structure where consumers observe both past quality realizations and litigation outcomes.,1,"Information structure: consumers observe both past quality realizations q_t and litigation outcomes (l_t, o_t); sanction mix chosen by firm via damage level d and implied boycott length T(d).",Case 1 explicitly contrasts what consumers observe with case 2 and drives how legal vs reputational sanctions operate.,1,False,Model is purely theoretical with no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled experiment; it is an analytical game-theoretic model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulation; only analytical derivations and a small numerical illustration embedded in theory.,1,True,The paper sets up and solves a repeated-game model with closed-form inequalities and propositions.,1,2,Model has one long-lived firm and a representative consumer each period; effectively a firm vs. a sequence of single-period consumers; base game is two-player.,0.8,0,"Infinite-horizon repeated interaction t in {0,1,2,...}; use 0 to represent infinite/indefinite as no finite number is given.",1,1,"Consumer either purchases or not (a_t∈{0,1}), but effort and sanctions are not modeled as all-or-nothing contributions to a public good; there is no contribution choice in the public-goods sense. Mark as 1 (not restricted to all-or-nothing contribution game).",0.3,N/A,No public-goods contribution or endowment allocation; purely a price/effort/warranty model.,1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier or marginal per capita return discussed.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; analytical model only.,1,0,Information structure only specifies observation of quality realizations and litigation outcomes; no mention of payoff or punishment summary displays.,1,0,No notion of individual identities