fixed per-period endowment defined as in public-goods games.,1,N/R,"Outcomes of interest are deterrence, firm profit, and welfare, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decision modeled.,1,N/R,"Although welfare comparisons are made, no normalized efficiency metric is provided.",1,N/R,Total surplus is expressed symbolically; no numeric payoff summarized.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical theory only.,1,"Same underlying infinite-horizon firm–consumer game as case 1, but future consumers cannot observe realized quality; they observe only whether suits were filed and whether the court found the firm liable. Reputational sanctions (boycotts) are conditioned on these litigation outcomes, creating nuisance reputational sanctions when courts err.",Summarizes the distinct strategic environment for case 2.,1
