participants.,1,No human,Analytical extension only.,1,"Firm promises to pay damages d immediately upon delivery of low quality; if it reneges, consumers observe nonpayment and trigger reputational sanctions plus litigation. In equilibrium, firm pays d when quality is low and only nuisance suits occur when quality is high.",Key change in strategic structure is observability of payment history and resulting equilibrium with only nuisance suits.,1
10.1086_696880.md,Analytical extension – relational contract with long-lived buyer,"Section 7 further extends to a long-lived buyer and seller, changing equilibrium possibilities (first-best vs nuisance suits).",1,Counterparty type and contract form: long-lived buyer vs sequence of short-lived consumers; presence or absence of relational contract terms where buyer promises not to sue on high quality and imposes relational punishment on seller who fails to pay on low quality.,"Extension varies the nature of the buyer (long-lived) and allows relational punishments, affecting whether nuisance suits are allowed.",1,False,Still a theoretical model; no subjects.,1,False,No experimental component.,1,False,Not run in a lab.,1,False,Results are derived analytically.,1,True,Corollary 3 gives analytical conditions under which relational contracts implement first best or the seller prefers nuisance suits.,1,2,One long-lived seller and one long-lived buyer in a relational contract.,1,0,Long-term relationship analyzed in repeated-game framework; effectively infinite horizon.,1,1,"No contribution stage; decisions are effort, paying damages, and suing.",0.3,N/A,No endowment-contribution decision.,1,N/R,Not a public-goods or linear-return game.,1,N/A,No human subjects or chat.,1,0,Players are assumed to know the history; no explicit summary displays.,1,0,"Only two players, identities implicit and fixed.",1,0,No rewards modeled.,1,0,Indefinite relational contract; no notion of displayed remaining rounds.,1,1,Relational sanctions (future trade suspension or worse terms) and legal sanctions (lawsuits) are part of the analysis.,1,N/R,Costs are modeled in terms of foregone surplus and litigation costs; no single numeric cost parameter provided.,1,N/R,"Effectiveness parameters are embedded in inequalities, not a single scalar.",1,0,Actions focus on sanctions; no separate rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No fixed endowment specified.,1,N/R,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,Corollary 3 discusses first-best vs. second-best profits qualitatively; no 0–1 efficiency value.,1,N/R,No explicit numeric group payoff reported.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Relational-contract extension is theoretical.,1,"Long-lived buyer observes full history including realized quality and whether seller pays promised damages. Buyer and seller can agree on a relational contract where seller pays d when quality is low and buyer does not sue on high quality, using relational punishment if either deviates. Corollary 3 gives conditions under which first-best is implementable and when seller may prefer allowing nuisance suits.",Captures key structural differences from baseline consumer-sequence model.,1
10.1086_696880.md,Numerical illustration – comparison of legal vs reputational sanctions (Table 1 example),"Section 3 provides a specific numerical example with concrete dollar values for damages, litigation costs, and boycott periods, summarized in Table 1.",1,"Sanction mechanism and level: (a) legal sanctions with damages D ∈ {20,30,40,50}; (b) reputational sanctions with boycott period T ∈ {1,3,6,10}; litigation costs k drawn uniformly from {20,30,40,50}.","Example varies damages and boycott length to hold deterrence at 5,15,30,50 and compare expected costs by mechanism.",1,False,"Illustrative calculation, not data from human subjects.",1,False,No experimental protocol; just worked numerical example.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab.,1,False,"Expected values are computed analytically from a simple distribution, not via simulation runs.",1,True,Deterrence and expected cost are computed exactly from specified probabilities and payoffs.,1,2,"One firm and one representative consumer in each period, as stated in the example.",1,0,Example is embedded in an infinite-horizon interaction; deterrence is explained in per-period terms.,0.7,1,No contribution choice; binary purchase interaction.,0.5,N/A,No public-goods contribution.,1,N/R,Not a public-goods setting.,1,N/A,No experimental communication.,1,0,Analytical illustration; no experimental interface.,1,0,Single firm; no IDs.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Rounds not explicitly counted; interaction considered long-term.,1,1,Both lawsuit-based legal sanctions and boycott-based reputational sanctions are present in the example.,1,N/R,"Example uses several litigation cost realizations (20,30,40,50) and varying lost-profit amounts; no single cost per unit punishment.",1,N/R,"Effectiveness of sanctions is described via expected penalties, not a per-unit tech parameter.",1,0,Only sanctions considered.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Firm’s per-period profit when investing in quality is $5, but that is not an agent endowment in the experimental sense.",0.7,N/R,"Deterrence is measured as expected sanctions in dollars, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,"Example reports deterrence and expected cost, but not an efficiency ratio.",1,N/R,Payoffs are discussed qualitatively; no explicit group payoff for each mechanism/level is tabulated.,1,N/A,No actual participants.,1,N/A,No actual participants.,1,N/A,No actual participants.,1,N/A,No actual participants.,1,No human,Worked out example within the analytical model.,1,"Single firm, representative consumer per period. Legal regime: consumer’s litigation cost k takes values {20,30,40,50} with equal probability; damages D chosen by firm. Reputational regime: firm’s per-period profit from high quality is $5; boycott of length T destroys $5 per period. Table shows deterrence levels 5,15,30,50 under alternative D and T with corresponding expected costs.",Summarizes structure and parameterization of the numerical example.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2012.07.078.md,"Simulation – asynchronous updates, square lattice SG with punishment (α=0.01, β=0.05, varying r)","Fig. 1 and Fig. 2 describe numerical simulations on a 90×90 lattice with asynchronous updating for α=0.01, β=0.05 and r varied.",1,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r varied in (0,1); punishment parameters fixed at α=0.01, β=0.05; asynchronous updating on square lattice; initial strategies C,D,P uniformly random.",Text around Fig. 1: “parameters are α=0.01 and β=0.05… as a function of r… asynchronous updating scheme… uniform initial distribution… 90×90 lattice.”,1,False,Study uses numerical simulations of evolutionary game dynamics on a lattice; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled human/field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; simulations only.,1,True,Described repeatedly as “numerical simulations” on a lattice-based evolutionary model.,1,False,"This condition refers to direct simulation results, not the pair-approximation equations.",1,8100,Square lattice 90×90 for these simulations (explicitly stated). Player at each node ⇒ N = 90×90 = 8100.,1,5000000,For phase-diagram runs they use 5×10^6 time steps; Fig. 1 steady states described after long time. Use 5×10^6 MCS as representative horizon.,0.5,1,"Standard SG formulation and payoff matrix imply continuous/any-amount-like contribution encoded in strategies, not an explicit all-or-nothing contribution choice; but here strategies are discrete (C,D,P) so contribution amount choice is N/A. However per instructions this variable refers to contribution flexibility; model does not define discrete investment levels, so treat as any-amount (1).",0.2,N/A,No explicit endowments or contribution decisions; payoffs determined directly by strategies and payoff matrix.,1,N/R,"This is a 2-player SG payoff matrix, not a linear public good with defined MPCR.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication; simulation of abstract strategies only.,1,0,Agents update based only on own and one neighbor’s payoff difference; no aggregate summary is mentioned.,0.9,N/A,No identities or revelation; agents are nodes on a lattice without labels in the human sense.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Agents follow fixed update rule with no awareness of horizon length; no variable for displaying remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Punisher strategy P pays cost α and inflicts damage β on D; core of the model.,1,0.01,α is defined as the cost to punisher; here α=0.01 in the dimensionless payoff matrix used.,1,0.05,β is defined as damage to D when punished; β=0.05 in this condition.,1,0,Model only includes costly punishment; no reward or positive sanction strategy.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No individual endowment is defined; players receive payoffs per interaction based on matrix entries.,1,N/R,"Outputs reported are frequencies of strategies f_c, f_d, f_p as function of r, not normalized contribution measures.",1,N/R,No explicit contribution quantities; only discrete strategies.,1,N/R,They do not report aggregate payoff or efficiency ratios; focus is on phase structure and strategy frequencies.,1,N/R,No explicit group payoff statistics are given.,1,N/A,No human participants; lattice agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary game simulations.,1,"Evolutionary snowdrift game with three strategies (C,D,P) on 2D square lattice (von Neumann neighborhood k̄=4); payoff matrix normalized with R=1, P=0, S=1−r, T=1+r; punisher behaves as cooperator vs C,P but pays cost α to reduce D’s payoff by β; asynchronous imitation updating with probability ω=(V̄_j−V̄_i)/(1+r) when neighbor j has higher average payoff.",Summarizes model definition in Section 2 and the asynchronous updating description used for these simulations.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2012.07.078.md,"Simulation – synchronous updates, square lattice SG with punishment (α=0.1, β∈(0.1,1.0), varying r)",Fig. 3(b) and discussion describe phase diagram in r–β space for synchronous updating with α=0.1; phases and isolated dots discussed.,1,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r varied in (0,1); punishment magnitude β varied with 0.1<β<1.0; α fixed at 0.1; synchronous updating on 30×30 (and checked 60×60) lattice; initial strategies C,D,P uniformly random.",Text: “detailed simulations over the range 0.1=α<β<1.0 and 0<r<1… Fig. 3(b) synchronous updating… results obtained in square lattices of size 30×30… checked 60×60… uniform initial distribution mentioned earlier applies here too.”,1,False,Numerical simulations with no human agents.,1,False,"Computational study, not a human/field experiment.",1,False,Not conducted in a physical lab with human participants.,1,True,Explicitly referred to as simulations on a lattice.,1,False,"This row refers to simulation-based phase diagram, not analytical pair approximation.",1,900,Phase diagram simulations: “results are obtained in square lattices of size 30×30” ⇒ N=900; 60×60 checked only for robustness.,1,5000000,For Fig. 3(a) asynchronous they used 5×10^6 time steps; text: “results in Fig. 3(a) were obtained after an extremely long time of 5×10^6 time steps.” Synchronous runs for diagram likely use similar horizon; assume same order.,0.6,1,"As above: game uses discrete strategies (C,D,P) instead of explicit contribution levels; classify as not constrained all-or-nothing contribution choice; per schema mark 1.",0.2,N/A,No explicit endowment or contribution decision; payoffs directly from matrix.,1,N/R,Two-person SG payoff matrix without MPCR parameter.,1,N/A,No communication; artificial agents in simulation.,1,0,Update rule uses only payoffs of a player and one neighbor; no global or summary feedback.,0.9,N/A,No notion of identity visibility; agents just occupy lattice nodes.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No horizon info used by agents in synchronous update rule.,0.9,1,Same three-strategy SG with punishers is used: punishing P strategy with cost α and damage β.,1,0.1,Here α is set to 0.1: “for the case of α=0.1” in the phase diagram description.,1,0.55,"β is systematically varied between 0.1 and 1.0; no single value characterizes all conditions. Use midpoint 0.55 only as a neutral placeholder is not allowed by instructions, so mark N/R instead.",0,0,Only costly punishment is modelled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs defined per interaction; no endowment concept.,1,N/R,"Reported outcomes are phase types (C+P, C+D, D+P, AllD, AllP, C+D+P) and tiny f_d, etc., not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No explicit contribution quantities.,1,N/R,They do not compute efficiency; only classify steady-state strategy compositions.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics reported.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Purely numerical simulations on lattice.,1,"Evolutionary snowdrift game with strategies C,D,P on 2D square lattice (k̄=4). Payoff matrix with R=1, P=0, S=1−r, T=1+r, and interactions involving P use parameters α=0.1 and β∈(0.1,1.0). Punishers pay cost α and reduce D’s payoff by β. Synchronous updating: all players simultaneously attempt strategy updates each time step using same imitation rule based on payoff comparisons.",Summarizes core model plus specifics for synchronous update phase-diagram simulations.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2012.07.078.md,Analytical pair-approximation – three-strategy SG with punishment on square lattice (k̄=4),"Section 4 and Appendix A describe analytical pair-approximation equations for the three-strategy lattice game, compared to simulations in Fig. 4.",1,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r; punishment parameters α and β; same three-strategy SG with P on square lattice; dynamics approximated via link-density ODEs for f_cc, f_cd, f_cp, f_dd, f_dp.","Pair approximation equations (Eq. 8 and Appendix) include r, α, β as parameters; compare predictions vs simulation for cases like α=0.01, β=0.05 and α=0.1, β=0.5.",1,False,No human data; this is an analytical approximation of the simulation model.,1,False,Not an experiment; a theoretical analysis.,1,False,No lab setting; pure theory.,1,False,"This row captures analytical pair approximation, not Monte Carlo simulations.",1,True,Closed-form evolution equations for link densities derived via pair approximation; solved or iterated deterministically.,1,N/A,Pair approximation is in infinite-population/mean-field limit; no fixed finite N.,1,N/A,Time evolution is continuous/iterative ODE-like; no discrete finite round count is specified.,1,1,"Same underlying SG with strategies C,D,P and no explicit contribution levels; per earlier logic classify as non-all-or-nothing contribution choice (1) though concept is not central here.",0.2,N/A,No endowment or contribution structure in analytical model; only strategy payoffs.,1,N/R,Not a public-good MPCR formulation.,1,N/A,No agents with communication; analytical population model.,1,0,Agents’ behavior represented via payoff-based switching probabilities; no explicit summary information.,0.9,N/A,No identities in analytical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No round or horizon information states present.,0.9,1,"Pair approximation extends the three-strategy SG with punisher P; same α,β parameters.",1,N/R,"Equations are general in α; no single numeric α characterizes this analytical condition, as multiple parameter sets are considered. Hence cannot assign one value.",1,N/R,"Similarly, β is treated as a symbolic parameter in the analytical formulation; no single fixed numeric β.",1,0,Model only includes costly punishment among strategies.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in analytical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Analytical model is defined via payoffs per interaction without endowments.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are frequencies f_c, f_d, f_p derived from link densities; no contribution measures.",1,N/R,No explicit contributions.,1,N/R,"They do not report efficiency metrics from analytical solution; focus is on strategy frequencies and phases like AllD, AllP.",1,N/R,No group payoff aggregates reported from analytical model.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Analytical treatment of abstract population dynamics.,1,"Pair-approximation on a square lattice (degree k̄=4) for three-strategy SG (C,D,P). Link densities f_xy for x,y∈{c,d,p} evolve according to derived equations (Eq. 8, Appendix). Switching probability from x to y along an x–y link is ω_xy = (V̄_y − V̄_x)/(1+r), with V̄ computed using SG-with-punishment payoff matrix and local neighborhood composition approximated from link densities. Model captures only up to pair correlations and treats higher-order structure approximately.",Summarizes analytical model structure as described in Section 4 and Appendix A.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2021.04.029.md,PCP – BASE (Bonn),"Main PCP game baseline condition in Bonn, listed in Table 2 as BASE and described in Sections 3.1–3.2.",1,Pre-play information about other groups' cooperativeness: none (no additional information sheet),"Section 3.2 defines BASE as identical PCP game without any pre-play information, unlike FAV and UNFAV.",1,True,Human subjects in Bonn EconLab; Table 2 reports subjects and groups.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments; controlled lab game with induced monetary incentives.,1,True,Conducted at Bonn EconLab (laboratory for experimental economics).,1,False,All results come from human-subject lab sessions; no agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,Paper does not present formal analytical model as a standalone method; focus is experimental.,1,4,PCP game features n=4 players per group (Section 3.1).,1,10,Participants played the game repeatedly for ten announced periods (Section 3.5).,1,1,"Players choose contributions from 0 to 20 tokens (continuous within that range), not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment of 20 tokens is initially in private account and must be actively contributed to public project.,1,0.4,Each token contributed increases each player’s payoff by 0.4 tokens; MPCR α=0.4 (Section 3.1).,1,0,No communication between participants allowed; instructions explicitly forbid communication; no chat tool.,1,1,"Feedback includes own contribution, sum of contributions, individual others’ contributions, and detailed punishment/counter-punishment info (Section 3.3).",1,1,Stage 3: players observe who punished them by how much in stage 2 (Section 3.1).,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in PCP design; only punishment and counter-punishment.,1,1,Number of periods (10) is announced; game is finitely repeated with known horizon (Section 3.1 and 3.5).,1,1,Stage 2 offers costly punishment points that reduce others’ income (Section 3.1).,1,1,Marginal cost of first punishment point given is 1 token per point; cost schedule is non