ment point costs punisher 1 and decreases punished peer’s payoff by 3; unused punishment budget kept.,Summarizes P-PUN structure from design section.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2016.03.002.md,Mini public goods game with punishment – Quantal response equilibrium analysis,Section 6.1–6.2 analyzes a specific 2-player mini public goods game with punishment using QRE; this is the only concrete game with cooperation/punishment structure in the paper.,0.98,"Punishment parameters (fine β, cost of punishment α) and cooperation cost a, benefit b; analysis varies β relative to a and α (e.g., β>4α, β>2a, β>α) determining whether the limiting QRE selects cooperative equilibrium (1,1/2) or asocial equilibrium (0,0).","Section 6.2 states explicitly that a cooperative equilibrium is selected if punishment is strong enough and derives condition 2β>4a+α+√(8aα+α²); later special cases β>4α, β>2a, β>α are discussed.",0.96,False,The paper contains only theoretical and numerical analysis of game-theoretic models; no human subjects or experimental data are collected.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only analytical characterization of QRE and equilibrium selection.,1,False,There is no lab or field setting; the study is purely theoretical.,1,False,"Although figures show numerically computed QRE correspondences, core results are analytical; there is no standalone agent-based or computational simulation study defined as a separate method.",0.8,True,"The game is analyzed via closed-form derivations of QRE limit sets and inequalities on parameters (a, b, α, β).",1,2,"Section 6.1: ""we consider a two players PGG"".",1,1,The mini PGG is a one-shot 2-stage game (contribution then punishment) without repetition.,0.95,0,"Players choose between C (cooperate) and D (defect) and between P and N; these are discrete strategy choices, not a continuous contribution amount.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects and no dynamic endowment allocation; only strategy profiles are modeled.,1,N/R,"The game is parameterized by benefit b and cost a, but MPCR is never defined or computed.",1,N/A,No human experiment; communication is not modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or feedback to human players is described.,1,N/A,Identity revelation is not a concept in this analytical 2-player normal-form game.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism and no human subjects.,1,N/A,Not an implemented multi-round experiment; number of rounds is not shown to subjects.,1,1,Section 6.1: players can punish their coplayer; strategies P vs N refer to punishing defectors.,1,N/R,"Cost of punishing is denoted α, but no numerical value is fixed as the primary analysis is symbolic and inequalities over α and β are given; no single canonical value can be extracted.",0.7,N/R,"Fine imposed per unit of punishment is β, but again only symbolic conditions (e.g., β>4α) are given with different illustrative choices; no single numerical mapping per cost unit is standardized.",0.7,0,"Only punishment (fine β, cost α) is modeled; no separate reward opportunity is described.",1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this game.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this game.,1,N/R,"The mini PGG is described in terms of sending a benefit b at cost a, but initial token endowments are not specified.",0.6,N/R,"The analysis focuses on equilibrium selection (which Nash/QRE equilibrium is limiting) and frequencies of cooperation and punishment, but no normalized contribution rate over an explicit endowment is computed.",0.7,N/R,Cooperation is binary (send or not) and represented as a strategy; no explicit numeric contribution amount is treated as an outcome variable beyond parameters a and b.,0.7,N/R,Group efficiency ratios are not calculated; results are phrased in terms of which equilibrium is selected and whether cooperation dominates.,0.9,N/R,"While payoffs are parameterized in the matrix, the study does not report aggregate group payoff levels as outcome statistics.",0.9,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical game analysis.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The paper conducts mathematical analysis and QRE computation only; there is no implemented experiment.,1,"Two-player mini public goods game with punishment, equivalent to a mini ultimatum game or Prisoner’s Dilemma with punishment. Players choose between cooperating (C) or defecting (D) in the first stage and between punishing (P) or not punishing (N) defectors in the second stage. Parameters: benefit to coplayer b from cooperation at cost a to cooperator; punishment imposes fine β on target at cost α to punisher. Game has one strict Nash equilibrium (defect and do not punish, (0,0)) and a continuum of cooperative Nash equilibria (1,ŷ) with a/β ≤ ŷ ≤ 1, all weakly dominated by second-order free riding (1,0). QRE analysis shows that the limiting QRE can be the cooperative equilibrium (1,1/2) if punishment is sufficiently strong (e.g., 2β>4a+α+√(8aα+α²); special case a=α gives β>4α). For intermediate punishment levels, cooperation can temporarily dominate for moderate λ even when the limiting QRE is (0,0).","Summarized from Sections 6.1–6.2 including the payoff description, equilibrium characterization, and the derived inequalities for QRE-based selection.",0.98
10.1007_s11211-008-0064-1.md,Foundations 15-society project – Ultimatum game (generic condition),"The review describes a single standardized ultimatum game protocol implemented across 15 small-scale societies; no society-specific procedural differences are given, so this is treated as one generic condition.",0.6,Game type = Ultimatum game; cross-cultural setting (15 small-scale societies) but specific society-level conditions not detailed.,Only the game type and the fact that it was run in 15 cultures are specified; procedural manipulations within the ultimatum game are not differentiated.,0.7,True,Human participants from 15 small-scale societies played ultimatum games.,1,True,Described as experimental economic games with controlled rules and payoffs.,1,False,"Games were conducted in small-scale field societies, not standard university labs; likely field settings.",0.6,False,No mention of computer-only or agent-based simulation; these are human behavioral experiments.,1,False,"This condition refers to behavioral experiments, not formal models.",1,2,Ultimatum game involves a proposer and a responder.,1,1,Explicitly described as one-shot: each interaction with a particular co-player occurs only once.,0.9,1,"Standard ultimatum games allow any split of the endowment, not only all-or-nothing; no restriction mentioned.",0.7,0,Endowment starts under control of proposer who decides how much to transfer; nothing is pre-placed in a common/public account.,0.8,N/A,MPCR applies to linear public goods; ultimatum game is a bargaining game without contribution multiplication.,1,0,Economic games are described as anonymous with no indication of pre-play communication.,0.8,0,No mention that participants saw payoff or behavior summaries beyond their own interaction.,0.6,0,No punishment stage is part of the ultimatum game in this description.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond the standard offer; no separate reward stage mentioned.,1,1,Participants are told it is one-shot; thus they effectively know the number of rounds for a given partner interaction.,0.7,0,"Ultimatum game here is standard accept/reject; rejection is endogenous to the game, not a costly punishment stage funded by tokens.",0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism in the form of costly sanctions funded by players.,1,N/A,No separate punishment technology is present.,1,0,No mention of any explicit costly reward mechanism beyond the basic offer.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology present.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology present.,1,N/R,The review does not specify the amount of money or tokens used as endowment in the ultimatum games.,0.1,N/R,The review discusses mean offer percentages (25%–57%) but not normalized contribution/offer rate in a way that can be assigned to this single generic condition.,0.3,N/R,Absolute offer amounts in tokens or money are not reported.,0.1,N/R,No information on total payoffs relative to maximum possible group payoff is provided.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate group earnings are not reported.,0.1,Multiple non-Western small-scale societies (15 cultures on several continents),"Described as 15 small-scale, non-Western, non-industrialized societies across several continents; specific countries not listed.",0.7,N/R,No age ranges or averages are discussed.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information is given.,0.1,"Low formal education; small-scale, non-industrialized societies","Participants are from non-industrial small-scale societies, implying generally low exposure to formal schooling, but specifics are not reported.",0.4,Field experiment,"Games were run within small-scale societies in their own environments, not in standard university labs.",0.7,"Anonymous, one-shot ultimatum game; mean offers varied by culture (e.g., 25% among Quichua of Ecuador, 57% among Lamalera of Indonesia); cross-cultural variation partly explained by market integration and payoffs to cooperation (47% of variance).",These details are explicitly summarized in the review for the ultimatum games in the 15-society project.,1
10.1007_s11211-008-0064-1.md,Foundations 15-society project – Public good game (generic condition),"The review notes that public good games were run in six of the 15 cultures with a common general structure; specific between-culture procedural differences are not detailed, so treated as one generic condition.",0.6,Game type = Linear public good game; cross-cultural setting (6 small-scale societies) but specific society-level conditions not detailed.,Only the fact that a public good game with standard structure was administered in six cultures is described.,0.7,True,Human participants in six small-scale societies played public good games.,1,True,These are experimental economic games with predefined rules and monetary incentives.,1,False,Conducted in small-scale field societies rather than conventional university laboratories.,0.6,False,Not a computer-only simulation; human subjects are involved.,1,False,"This condition concerns behavioral experiments, not purely analytical models.",1,N/R,Described only as involving a 'small group' of co-players; exact group size not specified.,0.2,N/R,"It is mentioned that initial rounds show 40–60% contributions, implying multiple rounds, but the exact number of rounds is not given.",0.3,1,Standard public good games allow any contribution from 0 up to the endowment; no restriction to all-or-nothing is described.,0.7,0,Participants are endowed and choose what portion to donate to the public good; the entire endowment starts in their private account.,0.9,N/R,"The game is described as a standard linear public good where donations are multiplied by a factor >1 and < group size, but exact parameters or group size are not provided, so MPCR cannot be calculated.",0.2,0,No suggestion that participants were allowed to communicate; economic experiments of this type typically prohibit communication and the review emphasizes anonymity and lack of coordination opportunities.,0.7,0,Review does not report any feedback of others’ detailed earnings or punishment; only the structural payoff rule is described.,0.5,0,No punishment stage is included in the described public good game.,1,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism is described.,1,N/R,"Although initial rounds are mentioned, it is not specified whether participants knew the total number of rounds.",0.2,0,"The public good game described here does not include a punishment phase; punishment experiments are referenced separately (e.g., Fehr & Gächter, 2000) but not as part of this cross-cultural PGG.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit costly reward or bonus stage is described.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment magnitude (tokens or money) is not specified.,0.1,N/R,"The review reports that participants contribute 40–60% of their endowments in initial rounds in public good games in general, but does not break this out by culture or this specific 6-society subset; cannot assign a single precise rate.",0.3,N/R,No absolute contribution amounts are reported.,0.1,N/R,No report of realized versus maximum possible group payoffs.,0.1,N/R,Group-level earnings are not provided.,0.1,Multiple non-Western small-scale societies (6 of the 15 cultures),"Described as six small-scale, non-Western, non-industrialized societies; specific locations not named here.",0.6,N/R,No age information described.,0.1,N/R,No gender information provided.,0.1,"Low formal education; small-scale, non-industrialized societies","Participants are from small-scale societies with limited integration into Western-style education systems, but explicit schooling data are not given.",0.4,Field experiment,Games conducted in local environments of small-scale societies rather than in standard labs.,0.7,Standard linear public good game: participants receive an endowment each round and decide what portion to contribute to a group account that is multiplied by a factor greater than 1 but less than the number of group members and then divided equally among all group members. Initial-round contributions in such games are generally reported as 40–60% of endowment.,These structural details and typical contribution levels are explicitly described in the review.,1
10.1007_s11211-008-0064-1.md,Foundations 15-society project – Dictator game (generic condition),"The review notes that dictator games were conducted in three of the 15 cultures but provides only a generic description of the game structure, so this is treated as one generic condition.",0.6,Game type = Dictator game; cross-cultural setting (3 small-scale societies) but specific society-level conditions not detailed.,Only the existence and general structure of dictator games across three cultures are discussed.,0.7,True,Human participants from three small-scale societies played dictator games.,1,True,This is a controlled economic game with specified payoff rules.,1,False,"Like the other games in Foundations, these were run in field settings among small-scale societies.",0.6,False,"Involves real human participants, not purely simulated agents.",1,False,"Describes behavioral experiments, not analytical modeling.",1,2,Dictator game involves one allocator (dictator) and one recipient.,1,N/R,"The review does not specify whether the dictator game in these societies was strictly one-shot per partner, though dictator games are typically structured that way.",0.3,1,Standard dictator games allow any allocation from 0 to full endowment; no binary restriction mentioned.,0.7,0,The dictator is endowed and chooses an amount to give; nothing is pre-assigned to the recipient.,0.9,N/A,There is no group multiplication of contributions in the dictator game.,1,0,No mention of communication being allowed; dictator games in this context are anonymous and typically prohibit discussion.,0.7,0,No indication that dictators or recipients see others’ behavior or earnings summaries.,0.5,0,No punishment stage exists in the dictator game.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding mechanism beyond the dictator’s allocation decision.,1,N/R,The text does not state whether subjects were informed about the number of rounds for dictator decisions.,0.2,0,There is no explicit punishment mechanism in the dictator game as described.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"The dictator’s transfer is a direct allocation, not a separate costly reward technology of the type defined in the schema.",0.8,N/A,No distinct reward mechanism defined beyond the allocation decision.,1,N/A,No distinct reward technology defined.,1,N/R,Endowment size in dictator games is not provided.,0.1,N/R,The review mentions typical sharing rates (40–50% in ultimatum games) but does not report dictator game-specific average giving for these three cultures.,0.2,N/R,Absolute amounts given are not provided.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency metrics are reported for dictator games.,0.1,N/R,Group or dyad payoff totals are not reported.,0.1,Multiple non-Western small-scale societies (3 of the 15 cultures),"The review states that dictator games were run in three of the 15 small-scale societies, without naming them.",0.6,N/R,No age information given.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition is mentioned.,0.1,"Low formal education; small-scale, non-industrialized societies","As with the other games in these societies, participants likely have limited formal schooling, but specific data are absent.",0.4,Field experiment,Conducted in three small-scale societies in their local context.,0.7,Standard dictator game: one participant receives an endowment and unilaterally decides how much to transfer to a single recipient; the remainder is kept by the dictator.,The review outlines the classic dictator game structure in general terms.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2023.113562.md,"MPPAP multi-heterogeneity model – parameter setting (r, a, ω) on 100×100 lattice","Single agent-based evolutionary public goods game with punishment and aspiration updating; all results stem from the same simulation framework on a 100×100 lattice with varying r, a, ω. Treated as one simulation condition family rather than separate lab experiments.",0.8,"Enhancement factor r (1<r<5, e.g., r=4 in figures), aspiration factor a (e.g., a∈{0.1,0.2,0.3,0.4,1.7}), and total punishment amount ω (varied; examples: ω=0.2, 2.5, 3.1, 3.74). Network size L×L with L∈{80,100,200} and initial cooperator share varied in robustness checks.","Paper explicitly varies r, a, and ω and reports their effects on cooperation (Figs. 1,2,7). It also states using different lattice sizes and initial strategy distributions to test robustness.",0.9,False,Study is a numerical Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,It is not a controlled experiment with human subjects but a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Described as simulation experiments using Monte Carlo method on a regular lattice evolutionary game.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations; results come from numerical simulations.,1,10000,"Population scale is 100×100 on the main simulations, i.e., 10,000 players.",1,10000,Each independent experiment consists of 10000 rounds and is referred to as a realization.,1,1,"Cooperator investment C_ij(t)=N_cj(t)/5 is a continuous/adaptive amount determined by group composition, not an all-or-nothing 0/1 choice. Thus contributions are not restricted to all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,Agent-based evolutionary game does not specify individual endowments or an initial allocation between private and public accounts; only adaptive contributions defined by C_ij(t). No human endowments.,0.3,N/R,"Total cooperative investment in a group is multiplied by enhancement factor r and shared among 5 players; MPCR would be r/5 but the paper only bounds r (1<r<5) and uses r=4 in examples. Since no single value is fixed across simulations, an exact MPCR is not globally reported.",0.4,N/A,"No human subjects, hence no communication between participants.",1,0,Agents update only based on their own total and aspiration payoff; no mention of displaying others’ summaries.,0.9,0,Model does not include identity revelation; interactions are purely local and anonymous on the lattice.,0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism exists in the model; only punishment is modeled.,1,0,Agents do not have information about total or remaining rounds; they just apply a Markovian aspiration-driven rule.,0.8,1,Core of the model is a modified pool punishment mechanism with cost γ_ij(t) for cooperators and fine β_ij(t) for defectors; ω is total punishment amount.,1,N/R,"Per-unit cost per unit of fine is not constant; γ_ij(t)=0 or ω/(N_cj(t)+1), and β_ij(t)=0 or ω/N_dj(t). Cost is heterogeneous and state-dependent; no single scalar parameter reported.",0.3,N/R,"There is no constant technology like ‘each coin causes X fine’; costs and fines are defined directly from ω and group composition, so a fixed multiplier per unit is not specified.",0.3,0,Only punitive mechanisms are discussed; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/R,"Model defines payoffs via C_ij(t), γ_ij(t), β_ij(t) and r; it does not specify per-round endowment of tokens for players.",0.2,N/R,"Reported main outcome is cooperation fraction f_c (frequency of cooperators and punishers), not average contribution as fraction of an endowment.",0.4,N/R,"While C_ij(t)=N_cj(t)/5 is defined, average or total contribution amounts over time are not summarized numerically.",0.4,N/R,"Paper reports average total payoffs of cooperators and defectors and describes ‘lose-lose’ scenarios, but does not compute payoff as fraction of the theoretical maximum.",0.4,N/R,Average group or population payoff trajectories are discussed qualitatively but not reported as specific numerical aggregates.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is a Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice; environment is computational, not human-subject.",1,"Spatial public goods game on a regular L×L lattice with periodic boundaries and 5-person groups (focal plus 4 Von Neumann neighbors). Strategies: cooperation (C) and defection (D); cooperators can be punishers (P) depending on group composition. Payoff per group: if cooperator, p_ij(t) = r*C_ij(t)*N_cj(t)/5 − C_ij(t) − γ_ij(t); if defector, p_ij(t) = r*C_ij(t)*N_cj(t)/5 − β_ij(t). Investment C_ij(t)=N_cj(t)/5; cooperative cost γ_ij(t)=0 if all 5 are cooperators, else ω/(N_cj(t)+1); defector fine β_ij(t)=0 if all 5 are defectors, else ω/N_dj(t). Total payoff p_i(t) is sum over 5 groups. Strategy updating uses aspiration-driven Fermi rule: aspiration payoff p_ai(t)=a*C_i(t)+γ_i(t) for cooperators, a*β_i(t) for defectors; switch probability W=1/(1+exp((p_i(t)−p_ai(t))/κ)), κ=0.1. Cooperation level f_c measured as average fraction of cooperators and punishers over last 1000 of 10000 rounds across 30 realizations.",All elements taken directly from the model description (Sections 2.1–2.3) and measurement definitions in Section 3.,1
10.1063_5.0099697.md,Replicator dynamics model of public goods game with global exclusion,Single theoretical model analyzed throughout the paper; no separate empirical experiments or human-subject conditions are defined.,1,Cost of global exclusion c_GE; group size N; enhancement factor r; construction probability function p(x_GE) including its parameterizations (Class i: constant p; Class ii: discounted/synergistic dependence via logistic with h=0 or h=1; Class iii: sigmoidal logistic with 0<h<1; limiting step-function case s→∞; various phase conditions i–xxi).,"Across the analysis, dynamical phases and behaviors are classified by values of c_GE, N, r and by functional forms and parameter ranges of p(x_GE) (p_min, p_max, h, s).",1,False,The study uses analytical derivations of replicator equations and numerical phase-portrait simulations with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects or real-world observational data; purely theoretical and computational.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only mathematical analysis and simulations.,1,True,Replicator dynamics are explored by numerical simulations (phase diagrams and portraits) over parameter space without human agents.,1,True,"Core results obtained via closed-form derivations, fixed-point analysis, stability and center manifold analysis.",1,5,"Numerical examples and phase portraits explicitly use N=5 (e.g., Fig. 2, Fig. 3 captions).",0.9,0,"Replicator dynamics in infinite populations describe continuous-time evolutionary change, not discrete repeated rounds; use 0 to indicate not applicable in this modeling context.",0.8,1,"Each player either contributes a fixed amount 1 or 0; not a continuous contribution game, but within this schema 1 indicates players can contribute any allowed discrete amount, and here the only allowed positive amount is the full unit.",0.7,0,"In standard PGG formulation used, endowment is initially in private possession and players decide to contribute or not.",0.8,0.4,"MPCR = r/N. In numerical examples, r=2 and N=5 (e.g., Fig. 2 caption), giving 2/5 = 0.4.",0.9,0,No communication or signaling between agents is modeled; interactions are anonymous and well-mixed.,1,0,Model agents respond only to payoffs and population frequencies; no explicit feedback of summaries of others’ outcomes.,1,0,Global exclusion is centralized and anonymous in the model; no individual identities are tracked or revealed.,1,0,"No reward mechanism exists in this model, only exclusion.",1,0,Continuous-time replicator dynamics have no discrete round structure to display.,1,1,Global exclusion is modeled as an extreme form of punishment implemented by a centralized institution that excludes defectors from payoffs.,1,0.2,"In main numerical examples, c_GE is varied; specific portraits use c_GE=0, 0.1, 0.2 etc. (e.g., Fig. 2: 0, 0.1, 0.2; Fig. 4 and others use 0.2). Choosing c_GE=0.2 as a representative value.",0.6,1,"Each global excluder pays cost c_GE regardless of number of defectors; defectors in a group with active institution receive zero from the public good (full exclusion, effectively a large per-unit impact). For schema, set as 1 token of payoff reduction per unit cost as an approximate mapping.",0.3,0,The model includes only exclusion/punishment; no reward strategy or institution is introduced.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,1,Contribution cost is normalized to 1; standard PGG assumption implies unit endowment available to contribute.,0.9,N/R,"Paper analyzes frequencies of strategies (D, C, GE) and phase behavior, not explicit normalized contribution rates as an outcome variable.",0.3,N/R,"Although each cooperator and global excluder contributes 1 by definition, no outcome statistics (e.g., average contribution over time or across simulations) are reported.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure (group payoff relative to maximum) is computed or summarized across conditions; only qualitative phase behavior is discussed.,0.2,N/R,"Analytical payoffs for each strategy are derived, but aggregate group payoff statistics for parameter settings are not reported as a primary dependent variable.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical and simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely analytical and computational with no empirical environment.,1,"Infinite, well-mixed population playing a 3-strategy public goods game (Defector D, Cooperator C, Global Excluder GE). Group size N (typically N=5), enhancement factor r (typically r=2). Cooperators and global excluders each contribute 1; defectors contribute 0. Sum of contributions is multiplied by r and evenly shared among participants, subject to global exclusion: a centralized institution, built with probability p(x_GE), may exclude defectors in groups containing at least one global excluder from sharing the public good. Global excluders pay a fixed cost c_GE for supporting the institution. Dynamics follow standard replicator equations with strategy frequencies x_D, x_C, x_GE and detailed phase classification (21+ variants) over parameter space of c_GE and p(x_GE).","Summarizes game structure and dynamics as described in Sections II and III, including strategies, payoffs, and exclusion mechanism.",1
