10.1016_j.jesp.2020.104096.md,Symmetric – Central punishment,Condition defined by symmetric endowments (30 MUs) and the central punishment rule.,1,"Asymmetry: symmetric (all players 30 MUs); Punishment system: central punishment (rule-based fine 0.6 MU per MU not contributed, executed with 75% probability, costs shared by others)",This row corresponds to symmetric + central punishment.,1,True,Human laboratory experiment.,1,True,Manipulated punishment system and asymmetry.,1,True,Conducted in lab cubicles with computers.,1,False,No non-human agents.,1,False,Not just an analytical model.,1,4,Groups of four.,1,6,Six periods under each punishment system.,1,1,Players choose any amount of endowment to contribute.,1,0,Endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,Same PGG parameters as other conditions.,1,0,No communication channel described.,0.9,1,Participants saw each member's contributions and incomes every period.,1,0,Punishment is mechanical and anonymous; labels reshuffled each period.,0.95,0,No rewards.,1,1,Design disclosed number of periods.,0.9,1,Central punishment is a punishment system.,1,0.3,"When a target loses 0.6 MUs in punishment, this cost is shared equally across the three other players; each pays 0.2 MUs. Expected cost per 1 MU of potential central punishment considering 75% implementation probability: 0.2 * 0.75 = 0.15. However, per unit of actually executed punishment, each non-target pays 0.2; since punishment is triggered per MU not contributed (0.6 fine per unit), cost per 0.6 MU of punishment is 0.2, implying cost-to-fine ratio 1:2 overall (as they explicitly state); we approximate per unit fine of 1 as cost 0.3 (since 0.2/0.6 ≈ 0.333). Given text emphasizes total cost-to-fine 1:2 at group level, we set a representative cost parameter of 0.3.",0.3,0.6,Rule: participants are punished with 0.6 MUs for every MU of endowment not contributed.,1,0,Only punishment is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,30,Symmetric condition.,1,N/R