10.1016_j.obhdp.2018.07.001.md,Study 1 – Iterated PGG with second-party punishment (Round 1),Study 1 uses a 2-round public goods game with second-party punishment; analyses focus explicitly on the first round to avoid learning effects.,1,"Endogenous variation in contribution levels and above-/below-average contributions within fixed groups; decision time for punishment (measured, not manipulated); individual differences in SVO and negative affect.","No between-subject manipulation; predictors are measured variables such as decision time, SVO, affect, and relative contribution within the group.",1,True,Human participants in a lab public goods game with punishment.,1,True,"Incentivized interactive lab game with controlled rules and payoffs, even though key predictors (e.g., decision time) are measured rather than manipulated.",1,True,Data collected at the Decision Lab of the Max Planck Institute for Research on Collective Goods in Bonn.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation is described; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model; analyses are statistical regressions on experimental data.,1,4,Groups of four players are repeatedly mentioned for the PGG.,1,2,"Iterative two-round PGG; however, analyses focus on round 1. Field requires total number of rounds in the game.",1,1,"Participants could contribute any amount from 0 to 4€ in 2-cent units, i.e., continuous choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Standard PGG where endowment starts in private account and participants decide how much to move to the public project.,1,0.5,Text: 'The contributions to the public project are doubled and distributed equally between the four group members (Marginal per capita return = 0.50 Euro)'.,1,0,No mention of any communication or chat; standard anonymous lab PGG without communication.,0.9,1,"After contribution, participants are 'informed about the contributions of all other members of their group'; this is feedback on others.",1,0,Interactions ar