10.1257_mic.20180336.md,Part II – Endogenous Institution Treatment – PGG No Rule,Condition is Part II endogenous institution treatment; this row captures the unregulated PGG No Rule.,1,Institution presence and type: Endogenous minimum contribution institution applies only in parallel PGG Rule; this game remains unregulated.,Endogenous voting determines MCR for PGG Rule only; PGG No Rule is not subject to institution.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Treatment manipulation of institution origin (endogenous vs none).,1,True,Conducted in on-site labs.,1,False,No computer simulation of agents.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,4,Groups of four.,1,20,20 periods in Part II.,1,1,Subjects free to contribute any integer 0–20.,1,0,Full endowment initially private.,1,0.5,Same linear PGG technology in both games.,1,0,Design does not allow communication.,1,1,Subjects see contribution vectors and payoffs every period.,1,0,No institution operates on this game.,1,0,No reward scheme.,1,1,Participants told there are 20 periods.,0.6,0,"Enforcement institution applies only to PGG Rule, not to PGG No Rule.",1,N/A,No punishment technology in this game.,1,N/A,No punishment technology in this game.,1,0,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,20,Same 20-point endowment for both PGGs.,1,N/R,"Only raw average contributions reported, not normalized share.",0.4,12.26,"Text: “Average contributions to PGG No Rule start at 11.60 … remain between 11.60 and 13.20 until period 20, when they decrease to 9.27”; Table 3 spillover effect over all periods is 3.12, implying average ≈ baseline+3.12 ≈ 9.15+3.12=12.27.",0.8,N/R,No efficiency ratio for this specific game reported.,0,N/R,"Only overall welfare gains are given, not separate group payoffs by game.",0.2,"Netherlands, Switzerland",Same pool and labs.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,University students,Recruited from local universities.,1,On site,In-lab sessions.,1,"Played simultaneously with PGG Rule under an endogenously determined MCR. Before contributions, subjects vote on MCR for PGG Rule only, observe implemented MCR and vote distribution, and then state beliefs and contributions for both games. PGG No Rule payoffs determined purely by standard PGG without sanctions; contributions and payoffs revealed each period.","From description of endogenous institution treatment, institution formation stage only affecting PGG Rule.",1
10.1257_mic.20180336.md,Part II – Endogenous Institution Treatment – PGG Rule,Condition is Part II endogenous institution treatment; this row is the regulated PGG Rule with democratically chosen MCR each period.,1,Institution presence and type: Endogenously chosen minimum contribution requirement (MCR between 0 and 20) via unanimity-like voting; forfeiture of income if contribution below implemented MCR.,MCR is determined each period by group vote; lowest proposed threshold becomes binding MCR for PGG Rule.,1,True,Data from human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Treatment randomization and institutional manipulation.,1,True,Experiments at BEElab Maastricht and UZH lab.,1,False,No agent-based simulation.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,4,Four-person groups.,1,20,Part II repeated for 20 periods.,1,1,Any integer 0–20 allowed; sanction only if g_i<MCR.,1,0,Endowment 20 in private account initially.,1,0.5,Payoff when not punished identical functional form π_i=20-g_i+0.5∑g_j.,1,0,No opportunity for free-form communication; only anonymous voting and information.,1,1,"End-of-period feedback includes contributions and payoffs, plus realized MCR and distribution of votes (anonymized).",1,0,Punishment (forfeiture) is automatic; screen shows contributions but not identities; votes displayed anonymously in descending order.,0.7,0,No explicit reward institution.,1,1,Participants know there are 20 periods; typical interface displays current period.,0.6,1,Central authority punishes contributions below endogenously set MCR by forfeiting income from this game.,1,0,Punishment is institutional; no cost to peers for imposing it.,0.8,1,"If punished, player loses 100% of their payoff from PGG Rule that period (full forfeiture).",0.9,0,No explicit reward scheme beyond standard returns.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each subject has 20 points per game.,1,N/R,Average raw contributions to PGG Rule under endogenous institution are described qualitatively (increasing with MCR) but not given as a single aggregate number for all periods.,0.3,N/R,"They report that MCR is ‘virtually never violated’ (2/1,840) and that MCR averages rise to 17.75 in final periods, but no single average contribution is reported over all periods.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency metric at the game level reported.,0,N/R,Overall welfare gains are given but not disaggregated by game.,0.2,"Netherlands, Switzerland",Same lab locations.,1,N/R,Age summary not included.,0,N/R,Gender composition not described.,0,University students,Subjects drawn from local universities.,1,On site,In-lab sessions at universities.,1,"Each period begins with institution formation stage: each player votes integer MCR∈[0,20]; implemented MCR is minimum vote (unanimity requirement). Subjects see realized MCR and anonymous distribution of votes. Then beliefs about others’ average contributions in both games are elicited, followed by contribution decisions. Payoff from PGG Rule equals standard PGG payoff if g_i≥MCR, else 0; punishments do not affect others’ earnings from the punished player’s contributions.",Summarized from endogenous institution treatment description.,1
