10.1111_jels.12042.md,Higher probability compensatory – forfeiture option,"Table 1 treatment (7) 'Compensation of active player' 1/4, 'Retain or forfeit damages'; text: compensatory forfeiture option treatment.",1,"Damages regime: compensatory to active player, with option to forfeit (destroy) damages; an active player exists every period (probability 1); each individual has probability 1/4 to be active; active can either retain or forfeit claimed amount.",Section III.D describes forfeiture variation for higher probability compensatory treatment.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Randomized treatment in controlled experiment.,1,True,Conducted on site in Bonn lab.,1,False,No simulations run.,1,False,Empirical treatment condition.,1,4,Same groups of four.,1,30,Design constant across treatments.,1,1,"Contributions in [0,20].",1,0,Endowment fully private initially.,1,0.4,Same MPCR by design.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,1,Feedback on contributions and incomes maintained.,0.9,0,IDs randomized each period; only see transfers affecting oneself.,1,0,"No explicit reward option, only damages or forfeiture.",1,1,Finite number of 30 periods told to subjects.,1,1,Active can reduce others’ payoffs via retaining or forfeiting their income.,1,0,"If active retains damages, net cost is -1 per ECU (gain); if she forfeits (destroys) ECUs, there is no change to her own payoff for those ECUs. Because both options exist and forfeiture was used ~1% of time, we approximate effective marginal cost as 0.",0.5,1,"Every ECU removed from target reduces target by 1; if retained, active gains 1; if forfeited, ECU is destroyed but same loss to target.",0.9,0,No institution for increasing others’ incomes.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Unchanged endowment.,1,0.525,Mean contribution 10.5/20 from Table 2.,1,10.5,Table 2 'Compensation of active player' 1/4 'Retain or forfeit damages' row.,1,N/R,No per-treatment efficiency fraction.,0.2,N/R,Only mean individual profit (26