10.1016_j.eswa.2014.02.050.md,"Simulation – Scenario 1: 0% selfish (all cooperative), various Tit for Tat strategies","Paper describes simulations with 100 users where percentages of selfish users are varied (0%, 20%, 40%, 60%, 80%) and several TFT strategies are applied. This row captures the 0% selfish case across those simulations.",0.8,"Proportion of selfish users = 0%; Strategy type varies: Classical TFT, Generous TFT, Reputation-based TFT, Group reputation-based TFT; δ and k parameters varied as specified per strategy","Independent variables manipulated in simulations are the fraction of selfish users and the TFT strategy (including parameters like δ, k, and number of required offers). This row corresponds to 0% selfish case.",0.8,False,The study uses computer simulations of agents in a game-theoretic model; no human participants.,1,False,"Model-based simulation study, not a controlled behavioral experiment with human subjects.",1,False,No physical lab or in-person setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors explicitly state they simulate participants’ behaviours using different Tit for Tat strategies.,1,True,"Paper develops formal analytical equations (e.g., Eqs. (1)–(5), (9)–(11)) alongside simulations.",1,100,Multiple sections explicitly consider scenarios with 100 participants/users.,1,10000,"They state: ""Our game will run for 10,000 iterations.""",1,1,Agents either respond (cooperate) or ignore (defect) a request; no continuous contribution amounts like tokens.,0.9,N/A,No endowments or contribution accounts; binary cooperate/defect actions only in a PD framework.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents follow programmed strategies without communication.,1,N/A,No user interface elements; information is encoded as histories/reputation in the model.,1,N/A,There is no explicit punishment action; only cooperation/defection decisions.,1,N/A,No explicit reward action beyond cooperation itself; no identities shown.,1,N/A,Agents are not described as observing the total number of iterations.,1,0,No costly punishment mechanism; defection simply withholds cooperation.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action defined.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action defined.,1,0,"No separate costly reward; cooperation is the basic game move, not an add-on reward mechanism.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses payoffs per interaction (a, b, c, d) but no initial token endowment per round.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as gains/payoffs, not as normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,No notion of contribution amount; binary cooperation only.,1,N/R,They discuss gains and survivability but do not normalize by theoretical maximum group payoff.,0.7,35370,"For scenario with 100% cooperative participants they report an upper bound of total cooperative gain ≈ 35,370 (Eq. (4), Fig. 1). This is taken as group payoff.",0.9,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results come from computational simulations of a theoretical model.,1,"Asynchronous repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with 10,000 iterations, 100 participants, cost/reward parameters a = -1, b = 10, c = 0, d = -10, per-iteration request probability Pr = 1/10, individual answer probability Pa ∈ (0, 1/10) normally distributed; all participants cooperative (Ns = 0, Nc = 100). Various Tit for Tat strategy rules applied to decide cooperation on each request.",Summarizes key model parameters and rules for the 0% selfish scenario.,1
