10.1016_j.eswa.2014.02.050.md,"Analytical Scenario – Impact of free riding: 0% vs 20% selfish users, upper bound gains",Subsection 2.3 derives approximate upper bounds 35370 and 27800 for total cooperative gains in 0% vs 20% selfish scenarios.,0.95,"Analytical comparison of two scenarios over 10,000 iterations: (1) 100% cooperative users (Ns=0, Nc=100); (2) 80% cooperative users and 20% selfish (Ns=20, Nc=80). Other parameters: Pr=1/10, payoff parameters a=-1, b=10, c=0, d=-10; Pa distribution as in base model.",Section 2.3 defines these two scenarios and gives Eqs. (4)-(5) with approximate numerical results.,1,False,Analytical calculation of upper bounds.,1,False,No experiment.,1,False,Theoretical analysis.,1,False,"These specific numbers (≈35370 and ≈27800) are given as analytical approximations from equations, not simulation outputs.",0.9,True,Based on Eqs. (4)-(5).,1,100,Both scenarios explicitly assume 100 participants.,1,10000,"Eqs. (4)-(5) have factor 10,000 × 1/Pr, corresponding to 10,000 iterations.",1,1,Same binary game.,1,N/A,No endowment.,1,N/R,Not PG game.,1,N/A,No agents' UI.,1,N/A,Analytical only.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,0,No costly punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/R,"Focus on gains, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution tokens.,1,N/R,"They interpret difference as loss due to selfish users, not normalized efficiency.",0.7,27800,"For second scenario (80% cooperative, 20% selfish) Eq. (5) yields approximation ≈ 27,800 as upper bound of total cooperative gain. This scenario, not the all-cooperative one, is the canonical 'with selfish' case in this analytical comparison.",0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical derivation.,1,"Using Eqs. (4)-(5), they compute approximate upper bounds for total cooperative gains over 10,000 iterations: ≈35,370 with 0% selfish (all cooperative) vs ≈27,800 with 20% selfish (80% cooperative). The gap represents loss inflicted on cooperators by selfish users and potential gain if selfish users were forced to cooperate.",Summarizes analytical comparison of free riding impact.,1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Lab experiment – 0 Punishers treatment (Rounds 1–40),Condition described as '0 punishers treatment' where each human is grouped with three nonpunisher confederate virtual players over 40 rounds.,1,Number of punisher confederate players in group = 0,"Treatment differs only in number of punisher confederate virtual players (0,1,2,3); this row is 0.",1,True,Human subjects played the game with virtual confederates.,1,True,Controlled laboratory public goods game with randomized assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in the Experimental Economics Lab of the Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona.,1,False,"Human-subject lab experiment, not only computational agents.",1,False,No formal analytical model only; main results are experimental/simulation.,1,4,Groups of four subjects: one human and three virtual players.,1,40,Game consists of 40 rounds; subjects knew experiment would last 40 rounds.,1,0,Binary choice each round: contribute or not contribute entire endowment.,1,0,Endowment is private and subjects decide whether to invest it in group fund.,1,1.25,"Table 1 payoff matrix implies a linear public good: each contribution increases all players’ payoffs by 5. One contribution costs the contributor 5 but yields +5 to each of 4 players (total 20), so MPCR = 20/4/10 = 0.5 relative to 10? However, direct per capita marginal return from one contribution to others is 5/10=0.5 and to self is also 5/10=0.5; the marginal per capita return on 10 tokens is 0.5. However, using given structure: each contributor’s action adds 5 to each of 4 players so total 20 per 10 invested = 2; MPCR = 2/4 = 0.5. So MPCR=0.5.",0.4,0,Participants could not communicate during a session.,1,1,"After stage 1, subjects are informed about decisions and earnings of other participants in their group.",1,0,Participants could not identify one another; anonymity prevents learning identities of punishers.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism is present in the design.,1,1,"Humans were told experiment would last 40 rounds, so they knew remaining horizon.",1,1,"In all four treatments, a punishment stage is available; in this treatment, confederates are nonpunishers but the mechanism exists.",1,0,Cost to punishers is mentioned but no numeric cost per punishment is reported.,0,0,"Material damage to punished subject is stated to reduce payoff to zero for 1–3 punisher treatments; in 0-punisher treatment, only human could punish but confederates never do; unit relationship per punishment point not reported.",0.2,0,Only costly punishment is described; no positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"Table 1 implies payoffs from 0–3 contributions go 5,10,15,20 for C and 10,15,20,25 for D. This is consistent with standard Fehr & Gächter (2002) binary PGG with 10-token endowment and per-contribution return of 5.",0.6,N/R,Figure 1 shows average contribution rates but no exact values per treatment are tabulated.,0,N/R,"Only rates (proportions) over rounds are graphed, no raw amounts.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure reported.,0,N/R,Group average earnings not tabulated.,0,Spain,Sessions conducted at Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona (Spain).,0.9,N/R,Only described as 'students from a wide range of fields'; no age statistics given.,0,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0,"University students, mixed fields",Participants described as 'students from a wide range of fields of study'.,0.9,On site,"Lab sessions in a physical experimental economics lab, not online.",1,"Binary public goods game with punishment; one human and three virtual confederate players per group; first 9 rounds identical across treatments, rounds 10–40 differ in confederate punishment behavior. In this treatment, confederates never punish from round 10 onward.",Summary of structural features described in Experimental Design and Procedures sections.,1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Lab experiment – 1 Punisher treatment (Rounds 1–40),Condition described as '1 Punisher treatment' where each human is grouped with one punisher and two nonpunisher confederate virtual players.,1,Number of punisher confederate players in group = 1,Treatment manipulation is number of punisher confederate virtual players; here it is one.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with punishment and treatment variation.,1,True,Conducted in on-site Experimental Economics Lab.,1,False,"This entry is for the human lab treatment, not for simulations.",1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations as primary method.,1,4,Groups of four subjects.,1,40,Game lasts 40 rounds.,1,0,"Decision is contribute or not, not continuous contributions.",1,0,Subjects start with private endowment and then choose whether to invest.,1,0.5,Same payoff structure (Table 1) and group size as other treatments; marginal per capita return computed as in 0-punisher treatment.,0.4,0,No communication allowed.,1,1,Participants informed about own and others’ decisions and earnings.,1,0,Participants cannot identify one another; anonymity preserved.,0.8,0,No reward stage.,1,1,Participants know experiment lasts 40 rounds.,1,1,Punishment stage present in all treatments.,1,0,Design notes that punishment 'inflicts a cost to the punisher' but does not specify its magnitude.,0,0,"Material damage to punished subject reduces payoff to zero in treatments with 1,2,3 punishers, but the exact per-unit mapping of punishment cost to fine is not given.",0.3,0,No positive reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Same game and payoffs as 0-punisher treatment; inferred from Table 1 and standard design.,0.6,N/R,"Contribution dynamics over rounds shown in Figure 1, but no numeric averages per treatment are provided.",0,N/R,Only rates/relative levels graphed.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,0,N/R,No payoff summary by treatment.,0,Spain,"Lab located in Barcelona, Spain.",0.9,N/R,No age information provided.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown provided.,0,"University students, mixed fields",Participants described as students from various fields.,0.9,On site,Sessions conducted in a physical experimental lab.,1,"Binary public goods game with punishment and one punisher confederate. First 9 rounds: all confederates contribute with probability 0.5 and punish with probability 0.25 irrespective of actions. From round 10: all confederates contribute with probability 0.9; the single punisher confederate punishes defectors with probability 0.9, nonpunishers never punish. Material damage from punishment reduces punished subject’s payoff to zero.",Directly from Experimental Design section and Tables 2 and 3.,1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Lab experiment – 2 Punishers treatment (Rounds 1–40),Condition described as '2 Punishers treatment' with two punisher and one nonpunisher confederate virtual players.,1,Number of punisher confederate players in group = 2,Treatment varies only number of punisher confederate players; here it is two.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory treatment.,1,True,Manipulated number of punishers in controlled PGG.,1,True,Conducted in experimental economics lab.,1,False,"This entry covers the human experiment, not the agent-based model.",1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,4,Groups of four subjects.,1,40,Experiment length is 40 rounds.,1,0,Subjects choose whether or not to contribute; no partial contributions reported.,1,0,Endowment starts in private account.,1,0.5,Same payoff matrix as other treatments.,0.4,0,Explicitly no communication allowed.,1,1,Subjects see others’ decisions and earnings.,1,0,Identities of participants not revealed; anonymity stressed.,0.8,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,1,Subjects were told game length (40 rounds).,1,1,Punishment stage available in all treatments.,1,0,"Punishment is costly to punishers, but numerical cost is not specified.",0,0,"Punishment reduces punished subject’s payoff to zero, but fine per punishment unit is not given.",0.3,0,Only punishment is implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Same public goods structure as in Table 1; inferred as 10-token binary decision.,0.6,N/R,"Figure 1 shows higher contributions than 0- and 1-punisher treatments in some intervals, but no numeric averages.",0,N/R,Raw amounts not presented.,0,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0,N/R,No tabulated group payoffs.,0,Spain,Experiment conducted at Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona.,0.9,N/R,No ages given.,0,N/R,No gender distribution reported.,0,"University students, mixed fields",Described as students from a wide range of fields.,0.9,On site,Lab sessions in a physical lab.,1,"Same binary public goods game as other treatments. First 9 rounds: all confederates have 0.5 probability to contribute, 0.25 probability to punish any group member. From round 10: all confederates contribute with probability 0.9; the two punisher confederates punish defectors with probability 0.9 each, the single nonpunisher never punishes. Material damage to punished defectors is identical across 1-, 2-, and 3-punisher treatments and reduces payoff to zero; probability of being punished for a defector in this treatment is approximately 99%.","From design description, Tables 2–3, and discussion (probabilities 90%, 99%, 99.9%).",1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Lab experiment – 3 Punishers treatment (Rounds 1–40),Condition described as '3 Punishers treatment' where the human is grouped with three punisher confederate virtual players (whole group punishes).,1,Number of punisher confederate players in group = 3,Treatment manipulation is the number of punisher confederate players; here all three are punishers.,1,True,Involves human subjects interacting with virtual players in the lab.,1,True,Treatment within a controlled lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted in Experimental Economics Lab.,1,False,"This is the human-subject condition, not its agent-based replication.",1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,4,Groups of four subjects.,1,40,40-round game.,1,0,Contribute vs not contribute binary decision.,1,0,Initial endowment is private.,1,0.5,Same payoff matrix and group size as other treatments.,0.4,0,Communication among participants was not allowed.,1,1,Participants learn decisions and earnings of all group members after stage 1 and stage 2.,1,0,Anonymity of participants; identities not revealed.,0.8,0,No reward option exists.,1,1,Subjects knew the game length (40 rounds).,1,1,Punishment stage is integral to the game and is used heavily in this treatment.,1,0,Text notes cost to punisher but no value given.,0,0,States only that punishment reduces punished subjects' payoff to zero; per-unit mapping unspecified.,0.3,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,"Same public good structure as in Table 1, inferred 10-token binary contribution.",0.6,N/R,Figure 1 shows highest contribution rates among treatments for this condition but no numeric averages.,0,N/R,Only contribution rates graphed.,0,N/R,Efficiency not computed in paper.,0,N/R,Group earnings not tabulated.,0,Spain,Experimental lab is at Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,"University students, mixed fields",Participants were students from various fields.,0.9,On site,Physical lab sessions.,1,"Binary public goods game with punishment where the entire group of three confederates are punishers. First 9 rounds: confederate behavior identical across treatments (Pr(C)=0.5, Pr(punish any member)=0.25). From round 10: all confederates contribute with probability 0.9; each punisher confederate punishes defectors with probability 0.9. Material damage from punishment is identical to 1- and 2-punisher treatments and reduces the defector’s payoff to zero. The probability that a defector is punished in this treatment is approximately 99.9%.",From Experimental Design description and subsequent explanation of punishment probabilities.,1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Simulation – EMIL-I-A agents – 0 Punishers treatment,Agent-based simulation condition reproducing the 0-punisher treatment with one EMIL-I-A agent and three confederate agents.,1,Agent type = EMIL-I-A; Number of punisher confederate players in group = 0; Initial norm salience = 0,"Simulation reproduces lab treatments varying number of punisher confederates (0,1,2,3) using EMIL-I-A architecture with initial norm salience set to 0.",1,False,"Simulation uses artificial agents only, no human participants.",1,False,"This is a computational simulation, not an empirical experiment with participants.",1,False,Not conducted with humans in a physical lab.,1,True,Described explicitly as an agent-based simulation reproducing the public goods game.,1,False,No closed-form mathematical solution; numerical simulation instead.,1,4,"Each simulated group consists of one EMIL-I-A agent and three confederate agents, matching lab group size.",1,40,Simulations reproduce the 40-round public goods game used in the laboratory experiment.,1,0,Simulated game reproduces same binary contribute/defect structure.,0.9,N/A,No human endowment; artificial agents follow programmed decision rules.,1,N/R,"Simulations reproduce lab payoff matrix, but the paper does not re-state or parameterize MPCR explicitly for the simulation code.",0.2,N/A,"No human communication; agent communication is part of internal model, not a configurable chat option.",1,N/A,Question refers to information shown to human participants; here only artificial agents and internal state updates.,1,N/A,No human identities; agents use internal architecture to interpret punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism and no human identities.,1,N/A,"Applies to what humans see; here, time horizon is a simulation parameter.",1,1,Simulation implements punishment and sanction events that affect agents’ payoffs and norm salience.,1,N/R,Simulation description does not specify numerical punishment cost parameters.,0,N/R,Paper does not define punishment fine per unit within the simulation.,0,0,"Only punishment/sanction modeled, no positive reward.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Simulation agents are not endowed with a human monetary endowment; payoffs are abstract utilities.,1,N/R,Figure 3 shows contribution rates but only graphically; no numeric averages per treatment reported.,0,N/R,"Only contribution probabilities/rates plotted, no raw amounts.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure for simulations presented.,0,N/R,"Simulation results focus on contributions, not payoffs.",0,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure agent-based computational model.,1,"Agent-based model with one EMIL-I-A agent and three confederate agents per group reproducing the lab public goods game under 0-punisher conditions (confederates never punish from round 10 onward). EMIL-I-A agents update norm salience based on social cues (compliance, punishment, sanctions, etc.) and balance individual drive (instrumental payoff-based) and normative drive. Results averaged over 5,000 simulation replications.","Summarized from 'Group Punishment: A Simulation Experiment', 'Salience Mechanism', and 'Simulation Design' sections.",1
