ontributions, closely resembling human data in the 3-punisher lab treatment, and demonstrating the interaction of individual and normative drives.",From discussion of Figure 3 and description of EMIL-I-A architecture.,1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Simulation – Q-Learning agents – 0 Punishers treatment (α = 0.10),"Simulation condition with Q-Learning (reinforcement learning) agents under 0-punisher configuration, highlighted as representative (α=10%) in Figure 4.",0.8,Agent type = Q-Learning (RL); Learning rate α = 0.10; Number of punisher confederate players in group = 0; Exploration rate = 0.05,Paper specifies Q-Learning agents with exploration rate 5% and discusses α=10% as case with highest distinction among treatments; this row captures that configuration for 0-punisher treatment.,0.8,False,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,False,"Numerical simulation, not empirical data.",1,False,Not an on-site human lab experiment.,1,True,Explicitly described as simulations using Q-Learning agents.,1,False,"Behavior analyzed through simulation, not via closed-form results.",1,4,"Simulation uses groups of four agents, mirroring lab game.",1,40,RL simulations run the same interaction length as the lab experiment.,0.9,0,Agents choose between contribute and defect actions as in humans’ binary PGG.,0.9,N/A,No human initial allocation; RL agents have abstract state-action values.,1,N/R,Public goods payoff details in RL simulations not specified.,0,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,Display to human subjects not applicable.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or human participants.,1,N/A,Number of rounds is a simulation control variable.,1,1,"RL agents experience punishment events and associated payoff changes in treatments with punishment; in 0-punisher treatment, only the focal RL agent could punish but confederate behavior includes no punishment after round 10.",0.8,N/R,Exact punishment costs in RL scenario not reported.,0,N/R,Fine magnitude per punishment not specified.,0,0,No reward mechanism is described for RL simulations.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,"RL agents operate on abstract payoff signals, not human monetary endowments.",1,N/R,Figure 4 shows RL contribution rates for different α but does not provide numeric values per treatment.,0,N/R,Raw amounts not reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency outcomes for RL simulations reported.,0,N/R,"RL results summarized via contribution, not payoffs.",0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational RL simulations.,1,"Q-Learning agents with exploration rate 5% and learning rate α=0.10 choose C or D based on Q-values updated from payoff rewards in a public goods game environment matching human experiment structure. Treatments vary number of punishing confederate agents (0–3). Results show that varying punishment probability across treatments does not significantly affect RL agents' contribution rates, even at α=10%.","Based on 'Simulation Design', 'Results' for RL agents, and Figure 4 description.",1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Simulation – Q-Learning agents – 1 Punisher treatment (α = 0.10),"RL simulation condition for 1-punisher case, highlighted alongside 0 and 3 punishers in Figure 4.",0.8,Agent type = Q-Learning (RL); Learning rate α = 0.10; Number of punisher confederate players in group = 1; Exploration rate = 0.05,"Configuration described in RL section with α=10% and exploration 5%, under 1-punisher treatment.",0.8,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Numerical simulation.,1,False,No lab with humans.,1,True,RL agent-based simulations.,1,False,Outcomes from simulation.,1,4,Groups of four simulated agents.,1,40,Simulates same game length.,0.9,0,Binary contribute/defect actions.,0.9,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,RL payoff mapping not numerically described.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Applies to human subjects only.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No human rewards or identities.,1,N/A,Rounds are simulation parameter.,1,1,"Punishment probability varies with number of punishing confederate agents, but the impact on RL contribution is negligible.",0.9,N/R,Not numerically specified.,0,N/R,Exact fine structure not given.,0,0,No reward system described.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,RL agents do not have token endowments like humans.,1,N/R,Contribution trajectories only plotted in Figure 4.,0,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts provided.,0,N/R,Efficiency not discussed for RL simulations.,0,N/R,RL results not reported in payoff terms.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Fully computational RL simulation.,1,"Q-Learning agents with α=0.10 and exploration rate 5% interact in a public goods game with one punishing confederate agent whose punishment behavior mirrors that in the human experiment. The study finds that despite varying the number of punishers across treatments, RL contribution rates do not differ significantly, supporting the claim that norm-signaling, rather than material punishment probability alone, explains human behavior.",Summarized from RL results and discussion around Mann–Whitney tests showing no significant differences among RL treatments.,1
10.1177_0894439313511396.md,Simulation – Q-Learning agents – 3 Punishers treatment (α = 0.10),"RL simulation condition for 3-punisher case with α=10%, used in Figure 5 comparison.",0.8,Agent type = Q-Learning (RL); Learning rate α = 0.10; Number of punisher confederate players in group = 3; Exploration rate = 0.05,"In Figure 5, 3-punisher RL results with α=100% are plotted, but α=10% is discussed as a typical RL configuration with distinctiveness; this row specifies α=0.10 as described earlier.",0.6,False,Simulation-only data.,1,False,Not a human experiment.,1,False,No on-site lab.,1,True,Numerical RL simulation.,1,False,Not analytically solved.,1,4,Groups of four RL agents/confederates.,1,40,Simulated for same number of rounds as human experiment.,0.9,0,Binary contribution decision.,0.9,N/A,Not applicable without human endowment.,1,N/R,RL payoff function not specified.,0,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,Applies only to human information displays.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No rewards/humans.,1,N/A,Rounds are simulation parameter.,1,1,Treatment with three punishing confederate agents in RL simulations.,0.9,N/R,Specific values not given.,0,N/R,Fine structure not detailed.,0,0,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,RL agents do not have explicit token endowments.,1,N/R,Only graphs show relative contribution; no numeric values.,0,N/R,No tables of contributions.,0,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0,N/R,RL outcomes not summarized in payoff terms.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based RL simulation.,1,"Q-Learning agents with α=0.10, exploration rate 5% and three punishing confederate agents in a public goods game setting. Despite the higher punishment probability in this 3-punisher treatment, RL simulations show no statistically significant differences in contribution rates compared to 1- and 2-punisher RL treatments, contrasting with both human and EMIL-I-A patterns and underscoring the importance of norm-signaling mechanisms.",Derived from RL results (Mann–Whitney tests show p>0.8 among RL treatments) and discussion comparing RL vs EMIL-I-A.,1
