are allowed; no reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,"Same 20-token per-period endowment as in Fig. 1, shared by all main treatments.",0.9,N/R,"Text reports that contributions are significantly higher than Baseline and similar to Punish 1, but no numeric averages.",0,N/R,Figure 1 shows a time path but exact numbers are not given in text.,0.2,N/R,"Earnings relative to maximum are not calculated, only that earnings are higher than Baseline and not significantly different from other punishment treatments.",0,N/R,Fig. 2 describes earnings comparisons but no numeric values are stated in the excerpt.,0,China,Same Tianjin university student subject pool mentioned for the experiment.,1,N/R,No age statistics provided.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0,University students,"Experiment conducted with students at universities in Tianjin, China.",1,On site,Described as a laboratory experiment with physical sessions of 20 participants.,0.9,"4-player linear public goods game, 20-token endowment per period, 15 periods, fixed groups. Stage 1: contribution decision. Stage 2: first-order peer punishment after seeing contributions. Stage 3: each subject who was punished learns amount and identity of punisher(s) and can spend money to counter-punish those who punished them. No information about punishment between others; no third-party higher-order punishment.",Summarizes the described multi-stage structure and information restrictions for Punish 2.,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2017.01.016.md,Punish 2′ (full-information higher-order punishment),Paper labels this treatment 'Punish 2′' (Punish 2 prime).,1,"Punishment structure and information: two punishment stages with full information on all punishments between any pair, fixed identifiers, and ability to punish any member in the second punishment stage.","Described as 'a treatment in which we show subjects information about all punishments, then allow them to punish in the period's second punishment stage without restriction, keeping fixed subject identifiers for all periods'.",1,True,Same human-subject experimental framework; Punish 2′ is an additional treatment.,1,True,Controlled variation of information and punishment opportunities.,1,True,Conducted as part of the lab experiment with Tianjin students.,1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,Empirical design.,1,4,Same 4-person group structure across all main treatments.,1,15,Included with other main treatments in '15 periods in fixed groups'.,1,1,Uses the same standard voluntary contribution game as the other treatments.,0.9,0,Same initial allocation design as the other treatments.,0.7,N/R,No parameter values reported.,0,0,No mention of communication being allowed.,0.8,1,"Text: 'Subjects are also shown a reminder of the previous period's contributions and punishments and of average contribution and punishments of each group member and dyad in periods before that', which constitutes summary information.",1,1,"Subjects see 'information about all punishments' with fixed subject identifiers, so identities associated with punishments are known.",1,0,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,0,Number of periods remaining not mentioned as being displayed.,0.3,1,Has 'a second punishment stage without restriction' plus first-order punishment stage (by analogy with Punish 2).,1,N/R,Specific cost parameters are not reported.,0,N/R,Per-unit punishment effect is not given.,0,0,Only punishment and counter-punishment options are discussed.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Uses the same 20-token per-period endowment as represented in Fig. 1 for all treatments.,0.9,N/R,"Text notes contributions significantly higher than Baseline and similar to other punishment treatments, but no numeric rate.",0,N/R,No explicit mean contribution figures provided.,0,N/R,"Efficiency measures are not directly calculated, though it is said that earnings are higher than Baseline and not significantly different from other punishment treatments.",0,N/R,Fig. 2 shows earnings visually; numbers not reported in text.,0,China,Same Tianjin subject pool as other treatments.,1,N/R,No information in the excerpt.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,University students,Subjects described as students at universities in Tianjin.,1,On site,Part of the same laboratory experiment conducted with students in a physical lab.,0.9,"4-player linear public goods game with 20 tokens per period, 15 periods, fixed groups. After contribution and first-order punishment, subjects are shown detailed information about all punishments between all pairs and can then punish in a second punishment stage without restriction. Subject identifiers are fixed across periods, and subjects receive reminders of past contributions, punishments, and average contribution and punishment statistics for each group member and dyad.",Restates Punish 2′ design and information structure as described.,1
