10.1016_j.joep.2016.02.008.md,Stage C – In-group treatment (identity-homogeneous matching),Stage C is the one-shot PGG with punishment; this condition uses in-group matching after induced social identity.,1,Group composition / social identity: identity-homogeneous in-group (all three players share same induced color-identity); one-shot PGG with punishment.,Paper defines an in-group treatment where Stage C groups comprise members from the same color group created in Stage B.,1,True,Human participants in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and specified payoff functions.,1,True,Conducted in the Göttingen Laboratory of Behavioral Economics (GLOBE).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model tested without subjects.,1,3,Groups of three in all stages: “every subject was a member of a group of three.”,1,1,Stage C is explicitly described as a one-shot PGG with punishment.,1,1,"Subjects choose any integer contribution from 0 to 20 points, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Endowment is 20 points per subject; they actively choose how much to contribute; remaining points go to private account, so initial allocation is entirely private.",1,0.4,"Payoff function Ei = 20 - ci + 0.4 * sum ck; each point invested returns 0.4 to each member, so MPCR=0.4.",1,0,Chat tool is used only in Stage B identity-induction task; no communication is mentioned in Stage C decision phase.,0.9,0,Participants see each member’s contribution and total punishment points received; paper does not mention showing summaries of others’ earnings or past punishments.,0.6,0,"Others are labeled as player x and y, “anonymously”; no identities linked to who punished whom.",0.9,0,There is no reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,Game is clearly framed as one-shot; subjects know there is only this single decision in Stage C.,0.7,1,Stage C introduces peer punishment via disapproval points P_ik.,1,0,"Costs K(P_ik) are nonlinear (Table 2: 0,1,2,4,6,9,12,16,20,25,30). No single per-point constant can be extracted.",0,0.1,"Each point received reduces victim’s income from stage by 10%; 10 or more points => 100% reduction, consistent with 0.1 per point capped at 1.",1,0,No option to increase others’ payoffs; only punishment points that reduce income.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,80,"Stage C: basic endowment 20 plus lump-sum payment of 60 points, so 80 usable points in that stage.",1,N/R,Only average contribution in points is reported; no normalized fraction of endowment is explicitly given.,0,9.3,Average contribution in Stage C in in-group treatment is reported as 9.3 points (SD 5.8).,1,N/R,No ratio of actual to maximum possible group payoff is reported or can be reconstructed from aggregates by treatment.,0,N/R,Average group earnings by treatment are not reported; only overall mean earnings (16.4 Euro) across treatments.,0,Germany,Subjects recruited at University of Göttingen in Germany and experiment run in Göttingen lab.,1,Mean 23.4 years,Reported average age of subjects is 23.4 years.,1,50.2% female,Paper states 50.2% of subjects were female.,1,University students (various faculties including economics),Subjects recruited via university ORSEE database and campus cafeterias; variables 'Economics' vs others used.,0.8,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory (GLOBE) with standard lab procedures.,1,"One-shot public goods game with peer punishment, strategy method for contributions (unconditional and conditional), random device selects which two players’ unconditional contributions count; punishment via disapproval points 0–10 per other member, nonlinear punisher cost K(P_ik), each received point reduces income by 10%; emotions (including anger, contempt, irritation) reported on 7-point Likert scale toward each other group member after seeing contributions; identity induced earlier via group color task, in-group treatment keeps same-color members together.",Summarizes key institutional features and measurement details from design section specific to Stage C in-group condition.,1
