10.1016_j.jebo.2012.12.024.md,"NP – Students (no personal punishment, student pool)",One of four treatments: NP vs PP crossed with students vs workers. This row is NP (no personal punishment) with student participants.,1,Personal punishment availability: No (NP); Subject pool: Students,Treatment variables are availability of personal punishment (NP or PP) and subject pool (students or workers).,1,True,Human participants (undergraduate students) played repeated prisoner’s dilemma games.,1,True,Controlled randomized laboratory experiment with exogenously varied treatment conditions.,1,True,Sessions run in the Vernon Smith Experimental Economics Lab at Purdue University.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations; only human subjects.,1,False,"Paper discusses theory but the reported results here are from experiments, not purely analytical derivations.",1,4,Each participant played within a group of four persons who interacted privately and anonymously.,1,84,Indefinite horizon with continuation probability 0.95; average session length reported as 84 periods. Use 84 as typical number.,0.5,1,"Stage game is a 2-action PD: cooperate or defect, so decision is binary, not a continuous contribution level; coded as 1 since players freely choose between the two actions rather than being forced all-or-nothing on a contribution amount scale.",0.6,0,Not a standard endowment-contribution public good; payoffs directly determined by action pair. By convention set 0 (no pre-assigned contribution to public fund).,0.3,N/R,"Prisoner’s dilemma with fixed payoffs, not a linear public good; no MPCR defined or reported.",1,0,Subjects could neither communicate with each other nor observe others’ histories.,1,0,Only own pair’s outcomes observed; no mention of aggregate summaries of others’ payoffs or sanctions.,0.9,0,No personal punishment option exists in NP; identities never observable.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,0,Indefinite horizon with random continuation and no indication that remaining periods or total length were shown.,0.9,0,NP is explicitly the treatment without personal punishment.,1,N/A,No personal punishment option in NP treatment.,1,N/A,No personal punishment option in NP treatment.,1,0,"Design only introduces costly punishment, no rewarding institution is described.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoff table lists outcome payoffs (25, 30, 10, 5) but does not describe initial token endowment per period.",0.2,0.558,Cooperation rate for students in NP treatment reported as 55.8%; treat ‘cooperate’ as full contribution so rate = 0.558.,0.95,N/R,Only binary cooperate/defect choices and cooperation rates reported; no token amounts.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric (actual vs maximal payoff ratio) is reported.,1,N/R,Average earnings reported only at session level (USD 18) and not by treatment × pool; no PD-point payoffs by treatment.,0.2,United States,"Subjects were undergraduates at Purdue University, a large U.S. university.",0.9,Mean ≈ 21.2 years,Table 2 reports average age for students as 21.2.,1,53.8% male,Table 2 lists 53.8% male among students.,1,Primarily undergraduate students; 10.0% with college or higher completed,Recruitment from undergraduate population; Table 2 gives 10% with college or higher.,0.9,On site,Laboratory sessions conducted physically in Vernon Smith Experimental Economics Lab.,1,"Indefinitely repeated 2×2 prisoner’s dilemma with payoffs: (C,C) = 25,25; (C,D) = 5,30; (D,C) = 30,5; (D,D) = 10,10. Groups of 4 with random matching into pairs each period; continuation probability δ=0.95; 5 cycles per session; private and anonymous interaction; no communication; perfect stranger matching across cycles.",Direct description in design section and Table 1.,1
