10.1016_j.chaos.2022.112413.md,Baseline – binary trust game,"Baseline treatment described as the standard binary trust game without punishment (Section 4, Fig. 3).",1,Institution: Baseline trust game with no collective punishment mechanism,Only dimension varied across the three main treatments is the presence/form of the collective punishment institution.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 96 human subjects (Section 4).,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment and specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted at Bologna Laboratory for Experiments in Social Sciences (BLESS).,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulation reported.,1,False,Theoretical model exists but this treatment is empirical; we code only the experiment here.,1,2,Trust game played in pairs within groups of six (Section 4).,1,1,Each treatment is a one-shot game with one payoff-relevant interaction per subject (within-subject across treatments).,1,0,Binary trust game: first mover chooses trust/not; second mover chooses cheat/not; no continuous contribution.,1,0,Endowment initially held by each player; trusting involves actively transferring it.,0.8,N/A,"This is a trust/investment game, not a linear public goods game with MPCR.",1,0,No communication allowed during the experiment (Section 4).,1,0,No feedback about others' earnings or detailed actions is given before the end; only own payoff at end.,0.8,N/A,No punishment institution exists in Baseline.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,"Subjects are told the experiment has three parts; each part is a single game, so horizon is known.",0.8,0,Baseline game has no collective punishment (Section 4).,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit rewarding institution described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment denoted x in model; numerical value for x not provided in the excerpted text.,0.2,0.281,"In this binary trust game, contributionRate is fraction of first movers choosing trust; reported as 28.1% in Baseline (Section 5.1).",1,0,Trust decision is binary (all x or 0); average amount not reported in tokens/euros.,0.2,N/R,No overall efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum) reported.,0,N/R,Average or total payoffs per pair/group not reported.,0,Italy,"Experiment conducted at University of Bologna, Italy.",1,Mean 25.1; mostly undergraduate students,Average by vote group (25.6 vs 24.1) suggests overall mid-20s; subjects mostly undergraduate students.,0.7,53% male,Section 4: about 53 percent of subjects were male.,1,Mostly undergraduate university students; some with higher education (bachelor or more),Section 4 notes subjects mostly undergraduates; Table 3 shows 57.3% have higher education overall.,0.9,On site,Lab experiment at BLESS with subjects in cubicles and paid in cash.,1,Binary trust game using strategy method: each subject chooses as first mover (trust/not) and as second mover (cheat/not). Roles randomly assigned; one-shot; matching within fixed groups of six; no feedback until end.,Design description in Section 4.,1
