10.1016_j.physleta.2023.128837.md,Replicator dynamics – hierarchical N-player trust game with punishing investors (baseline model),Single theoretical model analyzed; all parameter cases (different α and λ regions) are variations within the same analytical/simulation framework rather than separate experimental treatments with measured behavioral data.,0.82,"Punishment intensity λ (e.g., 0.01, 0.05, 0.14, 0.2, 0.5), fraction of investors α (0.1, 0.2, varied), temptation ratio r, trustee return multiplier R_T, group size N, investment t_v; strategies available: punishing investor P, normal investor M, trustworthy trustee T, untrustworthy trustee U.","Paper systematically varies λ and α in the analysis and figures, with r, R_T, N, t_v also specified as parameters of the model; strategies P, M, T, U define the strategic environment.",0.96,False,Authors explicitly state they use a replicator equation approach and theoretical/numerical calculations; they also note that no data was used.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human or animal subjects; purely theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person experimental sessions; only equations and simulations.,1,True,They integrate/iterate replicator equations and show phase flows and time evolution numerically; this is a computer simulation of evolutionary dynamics without human subjects.,0.97,True,"They derive payoff functions, replicator equations, fixed points, and stability conditions analytically in the main text and appendices.",1,10,Model defines groups of size N>2. For concrete numerical examples most figures use N=10 (stated in multiple figure captions); one caption uses N=20 for a robustness check. A single representative value (10) is recorded here but multiple N values are explored.,0.7,1,"Each group interaction is a single-stage trust game; repetition is at the population level via replicator dynamics, not repeated rounds with the same players.",0.8,1,"Each investor pays a fixed amount t_v when they participate; there is no binary choice between all or nothing endowment, but contribution is fixed and not a continuous decision variable, so players do not choose any amount. However, per instructions, 1 denotes ability to contribute any amount and 0 denotes all-or-nothing; here the choice is invest vs not invest? In this model all investors always pay t_v, so contribution is not a strategic choice; we mark 1 to reflect that the game is not an all-or-nothing PG structure but note the ambiguity.",0.3,N/A,No human endowments or contribution interface; theoretical payoffs only.,1,N/R,"Model is an N-player trust game, not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined. Returns depend on R_T, R_U and group composition, not a constant MPCR.",1,N/A,No human subjects; no communication protocol.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; only mathematical payoffs.,1,N/A,No identities; punishment is modeled as payoff reductions in a population-level model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled; only punishment.,1,N/A,No user-facing rounds; continuous-time replicator dynamics.,1,1,Core of model is punishing investors spending budget λ t_v to punish untrustworthy trustees and normal investors.,1,1,Each punishing investor spends a budget λ t_v per target category (U and M). Cost per unit punishment on each punished opponent is λ t_v divided by k_U (or k_M). Thus cost-to-impact ratio is 1: punishment inflicted equals total budget λ t_v distributed across targets; we record punishment cost per unit as 1 in normalized units of inflicted loss per own-cost.,0.4,1,"Total punishment inflicted on each target is λ t_v/k_U (or /k_M) while total cost is λ t_v, so total harm equals total cost; thus a unit cost causes a unit total harm, giving an effective punishment technology of 1.",0.4,0,Model only includes punishment of U and M by P; no positive rewards are implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,"Investors pay a fixed investment t_v to trustees, but an overall endowment per player (including retained amount) is not specified beyond this parameter.",0.5,N/R,"Outcome variables are fractions of strategies (x_i, y_i, x_t, y_t), trust and trustworthiness levels, and attraction domains; no normalized contribution rates are defined.",1,N/R,"Investment amount t_v is a fixed parameter, not a measured or varying decision outcome.",1,N/R,"Although payoffs are given, the paper reports phase portraits and attraction domains, not aggregate efficiency as ratio to maximum possible payoff.",0.7,N/R,Explicit group payoff values are not reported as main results; only strategy payoffs and qualitative dynamics are analyzed.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study uses mathematical modeling and numerical simulations only.,1,"Infinite well-mixed hierarchical population split into investors (fraction α) and trustees (fraction 1−α); N players sampled each round; investors always invest t_v which is pooled and equally divided among trustees; trustees choose to be trustworthy (T, return and keep R_T times their share) or untrustworthy (U, keep R_U times, return nothing, with 1 < R_T < R_U < 2 R_T and temptation r = (R_U − R_T)/R_T ∈ (0,1)); investors can be normal (M) or punishing (P); P pays budgets λ t_v to punish U and λ t_v to punish M, distributing each budget equally among all U or M in the group; dynamics follow coupled replicator equations for strategy fractions within each role, with investors and trustees learning only within their own subpopulations; analysis focuses on boundary equilibria (e.g., M+U, P+T) and parameter regions in (λ, α) space that yield coexistence of punishing investors and trustworthy trustees.",Summarizes the core formal game structure and evolutionary rules described in Section 2 and the appendices.,1
10.1523_jneurosci.0595-16.2016.md,fMRI – Computer_Waive,One of the four Decider × Threat within-subject conditions in the fMRI dictator game: Computer (unintentional decider) and Waive (no punishment threat).,1,Decider = Computer (unintentional); Threat = Waive (no punishment right retained); same stimulation state (none).,Condition defined by the 2 (decider: Computer vs Partner) × 2 (threat: Waive vs Retain) design; this row refers to the Computer_Waive cell.,1,True,Human participants allocated money in a modified dictator game during fMRI.,1,True,Within-participant factorial manipulation of Decider and Threat with controlled instructions and payoffs.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory/fMRI scanner environment at Peking University.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only human behavioral and neural data.,1,False,No standalone mathematical model; only standard statistical analyses.,1,2,Modified dictator game between allocator (participant) and one responder partner/confederate.,1,32,Two blocks of 32 trials total; each of the four conditions had 16 trials. Each participant experienced 16 Computer_Waive trials.,1,1,"Participant could adjust allocation in steps of 2 yuan between 0 and 20 yuan, i.e., a continuous range, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Endowment of 20 yuan was to be allocated; initial amount on participant’s side was either 0 or 20 yuan, implying endowment starts under participant control, not pre-allocated to partner.",0.8,N/R,This is a dictator game with no multiplier or public-good return; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No mention of communication between allocator and partner; interaction was via pre-programmed screens only.,1,0,No trial-by-trial payoff feedback was provided to avoid learning; no summaries of others’ earnings or punishments described.,1,0,Punishment is automatic (4 yuan deduction) when conditions met; no indication that the identity of a punisher is revealed (and here decider is a computer).,0.8,0,No reward mechanism described in the game.,1,0,"Although they told subjects that one trial would be randomly selected, there is no statement that the total number of trials or remaining rounds was displayed on-screen.",0.7,1,"Game includes a punishment threat of 4 yuan when allocation is below partner’s minimum and threat retained; in this condition, threat is waived so not applied, but the mechanism exists in the overall design.",1,4,"If the punishment is executed, 4 yuan is subtracted from participant’s payoff for the current trial.",1,4,Punishment is a fixed 4-yuan deduction; interpreted as each ‘unit’ of punishment costs the participant 4 yuan with a 1:1 effect on their payoff (no multiplier reported).,0.6,0,No additional positive payoff or reward described beyond standard allocation; only punishment is manipulated.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,20,Participant allocated 20 yuan between self and partner each round.,1,N/R,"Paper reports only that allocations differ by condition (e.g., more in Partner_Waive vs Computer_Waive) and approximate ranges (30–40%) but no exact mean allocation per condition.",0.2,N/R,"No table or numerical means for allocation amounts by condition are provided, only ANOVA statistics and qualitative patterns.",0.2,N/R,No group payoff or efficiency ratios are reported for this dictator-game setting.,0.2,N/R,Group (dyad) payoffs per condition are not shown.,0.2,China,"Participants recruited from Peking University in Beijing, China.",1,18-27 (mean 21.2),"Reported for the fMRI sample: age range 18–27 years, mean 21.2.",1,"14 female, 11 male","For the final fMRI sample of 25 participants, 14 female; remainder assumed male.",0.95,Undergraduate and graduate students,Participants are described as graduate and undergraduate students.,1,On site,Task performed in-lab during fMRI scanning at a university; not an online or field setting.,1,"Repeated one-shot dictator game; participant allocates 20 yuan with 2-yuan increments; partner’s minimum acceptable amount is privately set and unknown to allocator; punishment (4 yuan deduction) occurs if allocation below this minimum and threat retained; no trial-by-trial payoff feedback; roles fixed (participant allocator, confederates responders).",Summarized from the Design and procedures section describing task structure and timing.,1
10.1523_jneurosci.0595-16.2016.md,fMRI – Computer_Retain,Within-subject condition where the decider is the Computer and it chooses to retain punishment threat (unintentional punishment threat).,1,Decider = Computer (unintentional); Threat = Retain (punishment right kept by computer); no brain stimulation.,One cell of the 2 × 2 Decider × Threat design in the fMRI experiment.,1,True,Same human fMRI experiment as for the other Decider × Threat conditions.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of decider and threat within participants.,1,True,Performed in an fMRI scanner at a laboratory site.,1,False,No computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,"Analyses are statistical, not analytical modeling.",1,2,Allocator plus one responder partner per round.,1,32,Each of the four conditions (including Computer_Retain) has 16 trials out of total 32 trials.,1,1,Allocations adjustable from 0 to 20 yuan in 2-yuan steps.,1,0,"Endowment is initially under allocator’s control; initial display shows either all 20 on self side or all on partner side but this is a UI starting point, not an automatic transfer.",0.7,N/R,No public good or multiplier structure.,1,0,No communication between players is described.,1,0,No feedback about accumulated earnings or others’ behavior was given.,1,0,Punishment is automatic and decider is a computer; no identities shown.,0.9,0,No rewards are present in the design.,1,0,Paper does not say that remaining or total rounds were displayed.,0.7,1,Threat retained in this condition means punishment can occur when allocation < partner’s unseen minimum.,1,4,4 yuan deduction from participant’s payoff if punished.,1,4,Punishment described only as a 4-yuan loss; interpreted as fixed unit effect.,0.6,0,No rewarding mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,20 yuan to allocate each round.,1,N/R,"Only ANOVA stats and qualitative direction (e.g., no strong facilitatory effect) are given; no numerical mean contribution rate.",0.2,N/R,No condition-wise means reported.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0.2,N/R,No raw payoffs reported by condition.,0.2,China,"All participants recruited at Peking University, Beijing.",1,18-27 (mean 21.2),Same fMRI sample characteristics apply to all four Decider × Threat conditions.,1,"14 female, 11 male",Gender distribution reported for the final fMRI sample of 25.,0.95,Undergraduate and graduate students,Described as graduate and undergraduate students.,1,On site,Laboratory fMRI study.,1,"Same repeated one-shot dictator game as other fMRI conditions; here the computer decides to retain punishment right before allocation, making punishment possible when allocation below partner’s undisclosed minimum.",Condition-specific manipulation concerns only the decider and threat state; game structure otherwise identical.,1
10.1523_jneurosci.0595-16.2016.md,fMRI – Partner_Waive,Condition in fMRI experiment where the human partner (intentional decider) waives the punishment threat.,1,Decider = Partner (intentional); Threat = Waive (partner voluntarily waives punishment right); no brain stimulation.,One of four Decider × Threat conditions; this is the intentional-no-threat cell.,1,True,Same human fMRI dictator game experiment.,1,True,Decider and Threat experimentally manipulated within-subjects.,1,True,In-lab fMRI scanning at Peking University.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,No separate analytical model.,1,2,One allocator and one responder per trial.,1,32,16 Partner_Waive trials out of 32 total trials per participant.,1,1,Allocation adjustable in 2-yuan increments; not an all-or-nothing choice.,1,0,Endowment begins under allocator control; starting display toggles between 0 or 20 on participant side but that is just an initial cursor position.,0.7,N/R,Dictator game without multiplier.,1,0,No communication channel between allocator and partner described.,1,0,No feedback about payoffs or others’ actions.,1,0,"Although partner holds/waives punishment right, no explicit feedback about who punished whom or identities is given.",0.8,0,No rewards.,1,0,Not reported that total/remaining rounds were displayed.,0.7,1,"Punishment threat is part of the overall game, decided by partner here, even though in this condition it is waived.",1,4,"Punishment, when applicable in other threat-retain conditions, is a 4-yuan loss.",1,4,Fixed 4-yuan deduction per punishment event.,0.6,0,No separate positive incentives are described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,20-yuan endowment per trial.,1,N/R,"They report that allocations are highest in Partner_Waive and about 30–40% on average overall, but do not give a precise mean for this condition.",0.3,N/R,No condition-specific mean allocations are tabulated.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency or payoff ratios.,0.2,N/R,No group payoffs per 