10.1007_s10683-015-9452-1.md,Envy game – within-subject ethnicity conditions,"Single envy allocation game where each subject chooses amounts for recipients of different ethnicities (own group, other in-group, Chinese, Khmer, etc.) using strategy method; all ethnicity-contingent decisions belong to one game configuration.",0.9,"Recipient ethnicity (own group vs out-group members: Vietnamese, Khmer, Chinese; decisions elicited for each ethnicity via strategy method)",Paper varies only the ethnicity of the matched opponent via colored ID tags and asks for choices contingent on these groups.,1,True,Human subjects in Vietnamese villages played incentivized games.,1,True,"Researchers control game structure, partner information, and incentives.",1,True,"Conducted in controlled on-site sessions in village halls/classrooms, not in naturally occurring market interactions; standard lab-style protocol albeit in the field.",0.7,False,No computational/agent-based simulations described.,1,False,No purely theoretical or closed-form models; focus on empirical data.,1,2,Envy game is described with a sender (player 1) and a receiver (player 2).,1,1,Each subject plays one decision per contingency (ethnicity condition) without feedback; structurally a one-shot allocation per condition.,0.7,1,"Player 1 can choose among several discrete positive amounts to send (0,5,10,12,15,30,60 thousand dong), not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"This is an allocation game, not a public good; no endowment is pre-allocated to a group account, standard dictator-style frame (all under sender’s control).",0.6,N/A,"There is no public good production; transfers do not change sender’s payoff, and total surplus depends only on amount given to receiver.",1,0,No communication or discussion between players is mentioned; subjects make individual decisions assisted by experimenters.,0.9,0,No mention of feedback or summaries of others’ allocations or earnings.,0.9,0,No punishment in this game.,1,0,No explicit reward or reputation mechanism; only monetary transfer decision.,1,0,"Multi-game session, but no statement that total number of rounds/games is displayed on screen or otherwise emphasized.",0.6,0,Envy game only involves sender choosing allocation; no punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism; allocation itself is the main decision.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage distinct from allocation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,12,"Sender (player 1) receives 12k dong to allocate; “player 1 receives 12k dong, and decides how much player 2 should receive”.",1,N/R,"Paper reports mean amounts sent, not normalized as a 0–1 fraction.",0.4,N/R,Figure 2 shows mean amounts graphically but exact numeric values are not provided in text.,0.4,N/R,No explicit ratio of total payoff to maximum possible payoff is reported.,0.2,N/R,Payoffs by group or dyad are not tabulated for this specific game.,0.2,Vietnam,"Experiments were conducted in twelve villages in the Mekong Delta, Vietnam.",1,Mean age per village ranges roughly 37–56; overall mean not explicitly given,Table 1 provides mean age by village; overall pooled mean is not reported.,0.7,Mixed; village-level male share ranges from 0.33 to 0.60,Table 1 lists proportion male per village.,0.9,Mean years of schooling per village between 2.5 and 8.3,Table 1 reports village mean education; individual distribution not given.,0.7,On site,Games played in person in rural villages with experimenters present; not an online platform or purely naturalistic field interaction.,0.9,"Sender gets 12k dong; can give 0,5,10,12,15,30,60 (thousand dong) to receiver without affecting own payoff. Within-subject strategy method for recipient ethnicity via colored ID tags indicating ethnic group (and income in some villages).",Directly summarized from section 3.1 and footnote about tag colors and strategy method.,1
