lages; Khmer: 6620 dong, Vietnamese: 8839, Chinese: 12609. Reporting Khmer figure as one example; others also implied.",0.9,Vietnam,Same Mekong Delta villages.,1,Mean age per village ranges roughly 37–56; overall mean not explicitly given,Same participants.,0.7,Mixed; village-level male share ranges from 0.33 to 0.60,Same participants.,0.9,Mean years of schooling per village between 2.5 and 8.3,Same participants.,0.7,On site,Same overall experimental sessions.,0.9,"Three players simultaneously choose one coalition partner. If two players choose each other, each earns 15k dong; if no mutual choice, everyone earns 0. Analyses focus on frequency with which participants of each ethnicity choose partners from own vs other ethnic groups; in VKC villages (V, K, C present), V choose C over K 61% of time, C choose V 56% of time, leading to expected earnings ranking C > V > K (12609, 8839, 6620 dong respectively).",Summarized from section 3.3 and coalition results paragraph.,1
10.1038_s41598-017-15188-w.md,Third-Party Punishment Game (TPPG) – Observer role,Punishment behavior measured when observers could spend own money to reduce dictator’s earnings in the third-party punishment game.,1,Observer punishment decision toward unfair dictators (continuous amount from 0 JPY upward),"Main manipulation is whether and how much the observer spends to punish an unfair dictator; all observers face the same unfairness threshold (≤500 of 1,500 JPY).",0.8,True,Game played by human participants in the lab.,1,True,"Incentivized, controlled economic game with pre-specified rules and payoffs.",1,True,Conducted in a laboratory with individual computer booths at Tamagawa University.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data from humans.,1,False,No purely analytical model; focus is on behavioral and MRI data.,1,3,"Explicitly: TPPG was played by three players: dictator, recipient, observer.",1,1,Described as a one-shot game; each participant engaged in each game one time.,1,1,"Observer decided how much of their own money to spend; implies a continuous or multi-unit choice, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.8,0,Not a standard public goods contribution setting; no pre-assigned contribution to a public fund. Endowments are fully in private accounts.,0.8,N/R,Game is not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,Participants were visually isolated in private compartments; no mention of communication.,0.9,0,"Observers only see a single dictator’s allocation to a recipient, not summaries or histories.",0.9,0,Interactions were anonymous; no indication that identities were revealed with punishment.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism exists in this game.,1,1,Games are one-shot and described as such to subjects; effectively total rounds are known.,0.8,1,Observers could spend own money to reduce dictator’s earnings.,1,1,"Earnings of target dictator were reduced by four times the amount the observer spent, so 1 JPY spent costs observer 1 JPY.",1,4,Text: dictator’s earnings reduced by four times the amount the observer spent.,1,0,Only costly reduction of dictator’s payoff is described; no rewarding option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Amount of money the observer could spend is not reported in the excerpt.,0,N/R,"Outcome of interest is amount of punishment spent, not a contribution to a public good; no normalized rate reported.",1,102.276,"Among observers who punished, mean amount spent per potential target was JPY 102.276.",1,N/R,No efficiency or welfare ratio reported for this game.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff for all three players reported.,0,Japan,"Participants were non-student residents from Machida, a suburban city of Tokyo, Japan.",1,20–59,Sample drawn from five 10-year age blocks: 20–29 to 50–59.,1,Both; approximately equal men and women across 20–59 age blocks,Sampling frame used 75 men and 75 women per age block; overall 564 participants were 290 female and 273 male.,0.9,Non-student general population; detailed education not reported,Described as non-student residents; no further education data in provided text.,0.6,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory with 10 private compartments and on-site sessions.,1,"Observer sees a dictator’s one-shot dictator game allocation of 1,500 JPY to a recipient; observers can spend their own money (endowment not reported) to reduce unfair dictators’ earnings by a factor of four; punishment decision elicited using the strategy method; unfair dictators defined as allocating one-third (500 JPY) or less to the recipient; measure of punishment is amount spent toward unfair dictators.",Summarizes reported procedural and payoff details of the TPPG.,1
10.1038_s41598-017-15188-w.md,Ultimatum Game (UG) – Responder role,Punitive behavior operationalized as rejection of unfair offers when participants act as responders in the ultimatum game.,1,"Responder rejection decision for unfair offers (≤500 JPY from 1,500 JPY total)",Primary behavioral variable is whether and how often responders reject unfair offers within the set of five potential proposals.,0.9,True,Game played by human participants recruited from Tokyo suburbs.,1,True,Standard incentivized UG with controlled payoffs and random matching.,1,True,Same behavioral sessions and lab setup as other games in the study.,1,False,No computer-simulated players; all decisions from humans.,1,False,No purely theoretical solution concepts presented as main result.,1,2,Two players: proposer and responder.,1,1,Each participant played each game once and UG is described as a one-shot game.,1,1,"Proposers make a split of 1,500 JPY; this is a divisible amount, so offers can vary continuously or in multiple discrete steps, not binary.",0.8,0,"This is a bargaining game, not a public goods contribution; initial endowment is entirely controlled by proposer.",1,N/R,Not a linear public goods setting; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Participants were isolated and no communication is mentioned.,0.9,0,Responders only observe their own offer; no aggregate payoff or history shown.,0.9,0,Interactions are anonymous; no identification of partners is reported.,0.8,0,No separate reward mechanism is present in the UG.,1,1,Described as one-shot; subjects would know the game is played once.,0.8,1,Rejection of an offer destroys payoffs and is treated as costly punishment of an unfair proposer.,1,1,"If responder rejects, they forgo the entire offered amount; effectively, to reduce proposer’s payoff by 1 JPY of their proposed share, responder loses 1 JPY as well. This is captured as a 1:1 cost ratio.",0.6,1,Each 1 JPY of responder payoff given up via rejection removes 1 JPY from proposer’s payoff relative to acceptance (both fall to zero).,0.6,0,No option to spend resources to increase others’ payoffs; acceptance just implements proposer’s split.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1500,"Proposer splits 1,500 JPY between self and responder; total pie equals endowment.",1,N/R,"Outcome of interest is rejection rate of unfair offers, not contributions; normalized contribution rate not defined.",1,N/R,No mean offer amount or responder monetary sacrifice is reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency index is reported for UG outcomes.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not reported; only frequencies of rejection are given.,0,Japan,"Same participant pool from Machida, Tokyo suburbs.",1,20–59,Same sampling frame as for the overall study.,1,Both; approximately equal men and women across 20–59 age blocks,Same sample composition as main behavioral study.,0.9,Non-student general population; detailed education not reported,Same as for TPPG; described only as non-student residents.,0.6,On site,UG administered in same laboratory sessions with private booths.,1,"Two-player one-shot ultimatum game with a 1,500 JPY pie. Proposer chooses a split; responder chooses accept or reject using the strategy method over five possible proposals. If accepted, both earn proposed amounts; if rejected, both earn zero. Unfair offers are defined as proposer offering one-third (500 JPY) or less to the responder. Key measure is the proportion of rejections of unfair proposals; more than half (58.1%) rejected at least one unfair proposal; conditional on rejecting at least one, the mean number of unfair proposals rejected out of five was 3.837.",Summarizes the described structure and outcome measures of the UG responder task.,1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,"Single-kingdom simulation – n=10 subjects, fixed punishment strength s, varying subject intelligence β","Section 3.2 describes simulations with a single kingdom, population size n=10, fixed king punishment strength s (no king learning), and varying subject intelligence β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}. These constitute one family of simulation conditions; here we capture the generic configuration since exact s and β-specific outcomes are only shown graphically and not parameterized numerically in text.",0.7,"Punishment strength s (exogenous, fixed for each run) and subject intelligence β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}; subject mutation rate μ_s=0.05; population size n=10; single kingdom (N=1).","Section 3.2 explicitly states that subject populations of n=10 and n=100 are used, with different fixed strengths of institutional punishment s and different subject intelligence levels β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}, and that in each simulation μ_s=0.05 with no king learning (s fixed). Here we record the n=10 case as a representative condition.",0.9,False,The study is a theoretical evolutionary game model implemented via simulations; no human participants are involved.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment is run; these are computational experiments/simulations.,1,False,There is no physical laboratory or in-person data collection; only simulations are used.,1,True,Authors describe ‘agent-based simulations’ and iterative evolutionary updates in a model with no human subjects.,1,False,"While the paper includes analytical Nash equilibrium analysis, this condition refers specifically to the agent-based simulation results in Section 3.2.",0.9,11,Single kingdom (implicitly N=1) with n=10 subjects and 1 king (Section 3.2). Total strategic agents in the game: 10+1.,0.9,1000000,They ‘simulate for 10^6 strategy updates’ in each run (Section 3.2). Interpreted as discrete update periods/rounds.,0.9,1,"Each subject’s strategy p∈[0,1] is the fraction of income paid in tribute, so contributions are continuous, not all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,This is not a standard public-goods/contribution experiment with initial default allocations; it is a tribute model in a simulation without human endowment defaults.,1,N/R,No public good return or marginal per capita return is defined; only tribute transfer and punishment between subject and king.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication channel exist in the simulation.,1,N/A,Simulated agents update based on payoffs via specified rules; there is no interface or feedback design as in human experiments.,1,N/A,Identity and information display are not concepts in this agent-based model.,1,N/A,No rewards or identity display mechanisms are modeled.,1,N/A,There is no participant interface; the number of updates is fixed algorithmically.,1,1,Core of the model is kings punishing subjects; punishment cost s(1−p_ij) paid by king and inflicted on subject.,1,1,"For each subject j, king pays cost s(1−p_ij) and subject incurs cost s(1−p_ij). One unit of king cost produces one unit of subject cost, so punishment cost per unit imposed is 1.",0.9,1,"Punishment function is linear: king pays s(1−p) to cause subject cost s(1−p); 1:1 mapping of king cost to subject cost, so technology factor is 1.",0.9,0,Only costly punishment is modeled; there is no positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,1,Each subject produces 1 unit of income per interaction period (Section 2). This is effectively the per-round endowment.,1,N/R,Outcomes for tribute payment p are reported qualitatively and via figures but not as explicit numerical summary statistics in text.,0.3,N/R,Average p values over the second half of the simulation are described but only visually in Fig. 1; no explicit numeric amounts in text.,0.3,N/R,"Authors discuss relative efficiency (e.g., coercive vs. noncoercive equilibria) but do not provide explicit normalized efficiency values for these simulations.",0.3,N/R,"They discuss subject and king payoffs qualitatively (π, Π) and via plots, but no concrete numeric group payoff values are listed.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Entire study is a theoretical model plus agent-based simulations, with no human-subject data collection.",1,"Asymmetric dyadic interactions within each kingdom: each subject chooses tribute fraction p∈[0,1] from income 1, keeps 1−p; king punishes shortfalls from p=1 with linear punishment s(1−p), incurring equal costs for king and subject. Subject payoffs: π_ij=(1−s_i)(1−p_ij); king payoff: Π_i=Σ_j (p_ij−s_i(1−p_ij)). Evolutionary updating via pairwise comparison with noise (β for subjects), mutation μ_s=0.05, and no king learning (s fixed). Simulations initialized with p drawn uniformly from [0,1]; 10^6 strategy updates with averages taken over second half.",Summarized directly from model description in Section 2 and the specific simulation protocol in Section 3.2.,1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,"Single-kingdom simulation – n=100 subjects, fixed punishment strength s, varying subject intelligence β",Section 3.2 repeats the same analysis for subject populations of n=100 as a robustness check; this is a distinct simulation condition due to different population size.,0.9,"Punishment strength s (exogenous, fixed for each run) and subject intelligence β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}; subject mutation rate μ_s=0.05; population size n=100; single kingdom (N=1).","Section 3.2 explicitly states they use subject populations of n=10 and n=100, varying fixed punishment strength s and β values, with μ_s=0.05; protocol is otherwise identical.",0.9,False,No human subjects; purely computational model.,1,False,"Simulations, not controlled human or field experiments.",1,False,No lab environment; numerical simulations only.,1,True,Agent-based simulations of evolutionary dynamics (Section 3.2).,1,False,"This condition refers to simulation results, not the analytical Nash equilibrium analysis.",0.9,101,One kingdom with n=100 subjects plus 1 king (Section 3.2).,0.9,1000000,"They ‘simulate for 10^6 strategy updates’ per run, same as for n=10 (Section 3.2).",0.9,1,"Subjects choose tribute fraction p∈[0,1], allowing continuous contributions.",1,N/A,No human endowment allocation or default contribution; just simulated tribute choices.,1,N/R,No public-good multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,"Simulation, no human communication.",1,N/A,No interface or explicit feedback structure as in human experiments.,1,N/A,Agent identities are not part of a human interface.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Number of updates is algorithmic; not shown to human subjects.,1,1,Punishment by the king is central to the model.,1,1,"King pays cost s(1−p), which equals the subject’s punishment cost; hence 1 unit king cost per 1 unit punished.",0.9,1,Linear 1:1 mapping of king cost to subject cost in the punishment function.,0.9,0,"Only punishment modeled, no positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each subject produces 1 unit income per interaction period.,1,N/R,Tribute rates p are described only graphically in Fig. 1; no numeric summary values.,0.3,N/R,No explicit numerical tribute amounts reported in text.,0.3,N/R,"Although equilibria efficiencies are discussed qualitatively, no normalized efficiency statistics are provided for this simulation set.",0.3,N/R,Subject and king payoffs are plotted but not tabulated numerically.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a numerical simulation study.,1,"Same asymmetric king–subject tribute and punishment game as in the n=10 condition, but with 100 subjects and 1 king in a single kingdom. Subjects evolve p via pairwise comparison with intensity β and mutation μ_s=0.05; king’s punishment strength s is held fixed (no king learning). Simulations run for 10^6 strategy updates with averages over the last half.",Directly from Section 3.2 where n=100 is used as a robustness check with otherwise identical setup.,1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,"Multi-kingdom coevolution – probabilistic king learning, s∈[0,3]","Section 3.3 describes coevolutionary simulations with N=10 kingdoms, n=10 subjects per kingdom, with kings learning probabilistically (β_K=0.1) and king punishment strength s initialized in [0,3]. This is distinct from deterministic king learning and from the s∈[0,10] robustness runs.",0.9,"Subject intelligence β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}; king update probability θ∈[0.001,1); subject update probability 1−θ; probabilistic king learning with β_K=0.1; mutation rates μ_s=0.05 (subjects), μ_k=0.05 (kings); initial king punishment strength s drawn uniformly from [0,3]; N=10 kingdoms with n=10 subjects each.","Section 3.3 specifies β values, θ range, μ_s, μ_k, β_K=0.1, initial s∈[0,3], and structure N=10, n=10 for probabilistic king learning.",0.9,False,No humans; purely agent-based computational model.,1,False,Co-evolutionary simulations rather than empirical experiments.,1,False,Not run in a physical lab.,1,True,Described as simulations of coevolutionary dynamics across multiple kingdoms.,1,False,"This condition references the numerical simulations shown in Fig. 2, not the Nash equilibrium analysis.",0.9,110,"There are N=10 kingdoms, each with n=10 subjects and 1 king, giving 10×(10+1)=110 agents.",1,10000000,‘We then simulate for 10^7 strategy updates’ (Section 3.3).,0.9,1,"Subjects choose continuous tribute fraction p∈[0,1].",1,N/A,No human endowment default contribution state is defined.,1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication.,1,N/A,No human-facing summaries; payoffs used internally for imitation.,1,N/A,No identities shown to humans.,1,N/A,No reward and no human interface.,1,N/A,"Update count is a simulation parameter, not something shown.",1,1,Kings punish subjects using parameter s as in the base model.,1,1,"Punishment cost to king per unit subject loss is 1, as in the underlying model.",0.9,1,Linear 1:1 punishment mapping remains unchanged in this extension.,0.9,0,No reward or positive sanctioning mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,1,"Each subject produces 1 unit per interaction period, same as in base model.",1,N/R,Average tribute p across kingdoms and time is shown in Fig. 2 but without numeric tabulation.,0.3,N/R,No explicit numerical tribute amounts are provided.,0.3,N/R,They compare payoffs qualitatively but do not report efficiency metrics as normalized numbers for these coevolution runs.,0.3,N/R,King and subject payoffs are plotted and discussed but not numerically summarized.,0.3,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Co-evolutionary simulations with artificial agents.,1,"Ten kingdoms, each with 10 subjects and 1 king. In each interaction round, subjects in each kingdom simultaneously choose tribute fractions p_ij∈[0,1]; their payoffs and the king’s payoffs are as in the base model. After each interaction, with probability θ a king learning round occurs (each king may update s via pairwise comparison with kings of other kingdoms, using intensity β_K=0.1 and mutation μ_k=0.05); with probability 1−θ a subject learning round occurs (one subject per kingdom updates p via pairwise comparison with another subject in the same kingdom, with intensity β and mutation μ_s=0.05). Initial subject p strategies are drawn uniformly from [0,1]; kings’ s from [0,3]. Simulations run for 10^7 updates with averages over the second half to estimate long-run average punishment strength, tribute, and payoffs.",Summarizes the coevolution protocol in Section 3.3 and the caption of Fig. 2 for the probabilistic king learning case.,1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,"Multi-kingdom coevolution – deterministic king learning, s∈[0,3]","Section 3.3 also analyzes coevolution with deterministic king learning (β_K→∞) using the same structural parameters; results are shown in Fig. 2, Column 2.",0.9,"Subject intelligence β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}; king update probability θ∈[0.001,1); subject update probability 1−θ; deterministic king learning with β_K→∞; mutation rates μ_s=0.05, μ_k=0.05; initial king punishment strength s drawn uniformly from [0,3]; N=10 kingdoms, n=10 subjects per kingdom.",Section 3.3: ‘We repeat this same analysis for both probabilistic king learning (β_K=0.1) and deterministic king learning (β_K→∞).’ Other parameters are stated as identical.,0.9,False,No human data collection; purely computational.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; agent-based simulations.,1,False,No physical lab environment.,1,True,Deterministic version of king learning implemented in simulations.,1,False,"These results stem from simulations, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,110,"Same structure: N=10 kingdoms, each with 10 subjects and 1 king.",1,10000000,Simulations described as 10^7 strategy updates for this coevolution analysis.,0.9,1,"Continuous tribute choice p∈[0,1].",1,N/A,Not a human public-goods contribution task.,1,N/R,No public-good return parameter.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,No interface for human participants.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Rounds are a simulation parameter only.,1,1,Kings use punishment parameter s as in base model.,1,1,Punishment remains linear with 1:1 king-cost to subject-loss ratio.,0.9,1,Same punishment technology as in all other conditions.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Each subject produces 1 unit income per period.,1,N/R,Subject tribute payment p is plotted in Fig. 2 but not numerically reported.,0.3,N/R,No numeric table of p values in text.,0.3,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed conceptually, but not computed as explicit metrics in these runs.",0.3,N/R,Group payoffs only appear graphically; no explicit numbers.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Numerical coevolutionary simulations only.,1,"Same 10-kingdom, 10-subject-per-kingdom structure as in the probabilistic case. Subjects update p with intensity β and mutation μ_s=0.05, only learning from subjects in the same kingdom. Kings update s deterministically (β_K→∞) via pairwise comparison with kings from other kingdoms, with mutation μ_k=0.05. King updates occur with probability θ per iteration; subject updates with probability 1−θ. Initial p and s values are as in the probabilistic case (p uniform in [0,1], s in [0,3]).","Directly parallels the probabilistic king learning condition, changing only the king learning intensity to deterministic.",1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,"Multi-kingdom coevolution – probabilistic king learning, s∈[0,10] robustness","Section 3.3 states that they replicate the analyses using a different upper bound on s, specifically s∈[0,10], and show robustness in Fig. 3. One of these sets corresponds to probabilistic king learning.",0.8,"Subject intelligence β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}; king update probability θ∈[0.001,1); probabilistic king learning (β_K as in the probabilistic case, value not re-specified but implied); mutation rates μ_s=0.05, μ_k=0.05; initial king punishment strength s drawn uniformly from [0,10]; N=10 kingdoms, n=10 subjects per kingdom.","Section 3.3: ‘We now replicate the same simulations using s∈[0,10] and get very similar results (see Fig. 3).’ ‘Same simulations’ implies same β, θ, μ_s, μ_k, β_K values as before.",0.7,False,These are still computer simulations.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Not in a lab; numerical only.,1,True,Robustness simulations with different s upper bound.,1,False,No additional analytical derivation; just simulation.,0.9,110,Same 10 kingdoms × (10 subjects + 1 king).,1,10000000,They ‘replicate the same simulations’ which had 10^7 updates.,0.9,1,"Continuous p∈[0,1] tribute choice.",1,N/A,Simulation without human default endowment choices.,1,N/R,No MPCR in the model.,1,N/A,No human chat.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Update count is internal only.,1,1,Same coercive punishment structure; only s range changes.,1,1,Linear punishment function unchanged; cost ratio 1.,0.9,1,Same linear 1:1 technology.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism added.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Subjects continue to produce 1 unit per period.,1,N/R,Fig. 3 displays tribute p qualitatively; numeric values not provided.,0.3,N/R,No numeric tribute amounts.,0.3,N/R,"Robustness figure focuses on p, s, π, Π; no explicit efficiency measures.",0.3,N/R,Group payoffs shown graphically only.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation robustness check.,1,"Same multi-kingdom coevolutionary game as before, but kings’ punishment strengths s are mutated and initialized in a wider range [0,10] instead of [0,3]. All other learning and update rules preserved; purpose is to test robustness of the emergence of strong punishment and tribute.","Section 3.3 notes the same simulations are replicated with s∈[0,10] to show robustness.",1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,"Multi-kingdom coevolution – deterministic king learning, s∈[0,10] robustness",Fig. 3 is said to replicate results for both probabilistic and deterministic king learning with maximum s=10; this is the deterministic version.,0.8,"Subject intelligence β∈{0.2,0.8,2,∞}; king update probability θ∈[0.001,1); deterministic king learning (β_K→∞); mutation rates μ_s=0.05, μ_k=0.05; initial s drawn uniformly from [0,10]; N=10 kingdoms, n=10 subjects per kingdom.","They state they ‘replicate the same simulations’ with s∈[0,10], which for deterministic king learning means same β_K→∞, β, θ, μ_s, μ_k.",0.7,False,Pure simulations.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Deterministic king-learning robustness simulations.,1,False,"Numerical, not analytical.",0.9,110,10 kingdoms with 10 subjects and 1 king each.,1,10000000,Same as previous coevolution runs (10^7 updates).,0.9,1,Continuous p choice.,1,N/A,No human default contribution state.,1,N/R,No MPCR parameter.,1,N/A,No human chat.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Rounds internal only.,1,1,Same punishment mechanism.,1,1,Punishment cost ratio unchanged.,0.9,1,Same linear technology.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Subjects produce 1 unit per period.,1,N/R,No numerical p values reported.,0.3,N/R,No numeric tribute amounts.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency metrics given.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs shown only graphically.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations only.,1,"Identical to the deterministic coevolution setup with s∈[0,3], except that the maximum and initial range for s is expanded to [0,10]. Used to show that kings still evolve to strong punishment levels and subjects to high tribute under favorable β and θ conditions, even with larger possible s.","Based on description in text that Fig. 3 replicates Fig. 2’s results with s∈[0,10].",1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,Multi-kingdom coevolution with inter-kingdom subject learning – probabilistic updating,"Section 3.3’s last part and Fig. 4 examine inter-kingdom (global) learning among subjects, with probabilistic updating parameters (diamonds in Fig. 4).",0.8,"Global learning probability φ∈[0,0.7]; subject intelligence β=2; king intelligence β_K=1; king update probability θ=0.01; N=10 kingdoms; n=10 subjects per kingdom; probabilistic updating (finite β, β_K).","They ‘choose two sets of parameters from Fig. 2 which were favorable for coercion (n=N=10, θ=0.01, μ_s=μ_k=0.05, with either probabilistic learning β=2, β_K=1 or deterministic learning β→∞, β_K→∞) and simulate for φ∈[0,0.7].’ Diamonds in Fig. 4 denote the probabilistic set.",0.9,False,Still a simulation with no human participants.,1,False,Agent-based simulation of learning dynamics.,1,False,Not a physical lab experiment.,1,True,Inter-kingdom learning is implemented in the agent-based model.,1,False,"Results derived numerically, not analytically.",0.9,110,10 kingdoms × (10 subjects + 1 king).,1,N/R,"For the φ simulations, the exact number of strategy updates is not explicitly stated; likely similar to 10^7 but not confirmed.",0.3,1,"Continuous tribute choice p∈[0,1].",1,N/A,No human default contribution.,1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier parameter.,1,N/A,No human chat.,1,N/A,No user interface; only internal payoff observations.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Not a human game.,1,1,King punishment remains as in the main model.,1,1,Linear punishment with 1:1 cost ratio unchanged.,0.9,1,Same technology.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Each subject produces 1 unit per interaction.,1,N/R,"Plots in Fig. 4 show how p changes with φ, but numeric p values are not provided.",0.3,N/R,No explicit numerical tribute amounts.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency effects of φ are discussed qualitatively; no normalized metrics.,0.3,N/R,King and subject payoffs vs φ are graphed but not tabulated.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results from simulations.,1,"Same 10-kingdom coevolutionary setup (β=2 for subjects, β_K=1 for kings, θ=0.01, μ_s=μ_k=0.05), but subject learning is modified: with probability φ, a subject compares her payoff to a randomly chosen subject from the entire population (any kingdom); with probability 1−φ, she compares to a subject from within her own kingdom. As φ increases, inter-kingdom learning undermines coercion—beyond a critical φ, punishment collapses and subjects stop paying tribute, as seen in Fig. 4.",Directly from the description of ‘global awareness’ φ and its effects in Section 3.3 and Fig. 4.,1
10.1007_s13235-011-0020-9.md,Multi-kingdom coevolution with inter-kingdom subject learning – deterministic updating,"The second parameter set for the φ study (squares in Fig. 4) uses deterministic updating (β→∞, β_K→∞).",0.8,"Global learning probability φ∈[0,0.7]; subject intelligence β→∞; king intelligence β_K→∞; king update probability θ=0.01; N=10 kingdoms; n=10 subjects per kingdom; deterministic updating.","Section 3.3: second parameter set uses ‘deterministic learning, β→∞, β_K→∞’ with n=N=10, θ=0.01, μ_s=μ_k=0.05, and φ varied.",0.9,False,Simulations only.,1,False,Not an empirical study.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Agent-based simulation with deterministic imitation rules.,1,False,Results come from simulations.,0.9,110,"10 kingdoms, each with 10 subjects and 1 king.",1,N/R,Specific number of updates for φ runs not explicitly stated.,0.3,1,"Subjects choose continuous tribute p∈[0,1].",1,N/A,No human contribution default.,1,N/R,No MPCR parameter.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Internal simulation parameter only.,1,1,Punishment mechanism remains in place; subject and king strategies coevolve.,1,1,Same linear punishment cost structure (1:1 king cost to subject loss).,0.9,1,Same punishment technology.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Subjects produce 1 income unit per period.,1,N/R,Tribute p vs φ for deterministic case is depicted in Fig. 4 but not numerically reported.,0.3,N/R,No numerical tribute data provided.,0.3,N/R,"Only qualitative discussion of payoffs, not explicit efficiency measures.",0.3,N/R,Group payoffs drawn but not tabulated.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,These are computational simulations.,1,"Uses same structure as the probabilistic φ condition but with deterministic imitation: subjects and kings always imitate better-performing strategies (β, β_K→∞). Global awareness φ governs whether a subject compares her payoff to someone inside her kingdom (probability 1−φ) or from the entire population (probability φ). As φ rises, coercive punishment and high tribute can be maintained up to higher φ thresholds with deterministic learning than with probabilistic, but eventually punishment still collapses beyond a critical φ.",Summarizes deterministic parameter set and qualitative results described for squares in Fig. 4.,1
10.1007_s12110-020-09371-8.md,Society-level observational condition – Standard Cross-Cultural Sample (96-case subset),"The paper reports one observational, cross-cultural dataset (96 SCCS societies with reliable natural hazard coding) used across all analyses; no separate treatment arms or experimental manipulations are defined.",1,"Societal resource stress (chronic scarcity, famine threat, natural hazards, and derived climate/resource factors) predicting supernatural weather beliefs, moralizing high gods, and beyond-household seasonal food and labor sharing","Resource stress indicators (chronic scarcity, famine, natural hazards, and factor-derived climate stability/resource abundance) are the primary predictors; sharing and religious belief variables are outcomes in regressions and mediation models.",1,True,Data are coded from ethnographic reports about human societies (SCCS); no artificial agents are used.,1,False,There is no manipulation or controlled assignment; the study is observational and correlational using archival ethnographic data.,1,False,No laboratory or controlled environment with participants; data come from field ethnographies and secondary databases.,1,False,"They use Monte Carlo simulations for statistical mediation, but the core data are empirical about human societies, not agent-based or purely simulated populations.",1,False,The paper uses statistical modeling but not purely analytical or closed-form mathematical models of behavior.,1,0,No game or interactive task with defined players is used; analysis is at the society level using ethnographic codes.,1,0,No repeated game or rounds are described; cooperation is measured as customary sharing practices.,1,0,"No contribution game is implemented, so this setting does not apply; set to 0 by convention.",0.5,N/A,"There is no endowment or contribution game; beyond-household sharing is an ethnographic practice, not a lab allocation task.",1,N/A,No public goods or similar game with a multiplier is used; cooperation is operationalized via sharing codes.,1,0,No interactive experiment or real-time communication between participants is described.,1,0,No feedback or summaries of others’ behavior/earnings are part of the design; this is an archival observational study.,1,0,No experimental punishment mechanism is implemented.,1,0,No experimental reward mechanism is implemented.,1,0,There are no game rounds or horizon information.,1,0,"Although supernatural punishment beliefs are coded conceptually, there is no implemented punishment technology within an economic game.",1,N/A,No formal punishment mechanism with defined costs is used.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology or impact per punishment unit is defined.,1,0,No formal reward mechanism within an experimental game is implemented.,1,N/A,No formal costly reward is modeled.,1,N/A,No defined mapping from reward expenditure to recipient payoff.,1,0,"There is no per-player experimental endowment; sharing is coded as a cultural practice, not as allocations from standardized endowments.",1,N/R,"No normalized contribution rate is reported; outcomes are binary/ordinal codes for seasonal sharing practices, not amounts relative to an endowment.",1,N/R,The study does not quantify individual contributions in tokens or currency; sharing is coded as presence/absence of practices.,1,N/R,No payoff structure or efficiency metric is defined as in public goods games.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoff is calculated; results are regression coefficients and presence/absence of sharing.,1,Multiple countries worldwide (Standard Cross-Cultural Sample societies),The sample is a worldwide SCCS sample of 96 nonindustrial societies; specific nation-states vary and are not exhaustively listed in the main text.,0.8,N/R,The unit of analysis is the society; ages of individuals in ethnographies are not reported as part of the design.,1,N/R,Gender composition of the ethnographic informants or wider population is not reported for the purposes of this study.,1,N/R,Educational attainment of individuals is not described; focus is on societies and cultural practices.,1,Observational,"The work is based on archival ethnographic and environmental data, analyzed with regressions and multilevel models; no field intervention, lab, or online interaction is used.",1,"No economic game is implemented; cooperation is operationalized as customary seasonal beyond-household food sharing and labor sharing coded from ethnographies, with predictors including multiple resource stress indices, climate-based factors, and religious belief variables (supernatural weather involvement and moralizing high gods).","Summarized from the Methods (Beyond-Household Sharing, Resource Stress, Moralizing High Gods) and Analysis sections, which describe variables and models rather than any structured game.",1
10.1111_ecoj.12088.md,Random matching – No communication,"Baseline one-shot-style treatment with random matching and no communication, as in Table 1 and text; treated as separate condition.",1,Matching protocol: random matching; Communication: absent; Repetition structure: 50 independent one-shot interactions (no fixed partners); Horizon: 50 periods.,Condition differs from others by random regrouping every period and no communication; horizon fixed at 50 periods.,1,True,Human subjects in a laboratory experiment are explicitly described.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized assignment to treatments and explicit payoff structures.,1,True,Conducted in university laboratories at Monash and Purdue.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations are reported.,1,False,Paper contains theoretical discussion but this condition refers to empirical data only.,1,3,Each group consists of one leader (Person 1) and two responders (Persons 2 and 3).,1,50,Random matching treatments were conducted for 50 periods.,1,1,"Responders choose binary actions X or Y; leader chooses among four squares; no continuous contribution scale, so contribution concept not applicable; coded as 1 because there is free choice among discrete actions rather than constrained proportional contributions.",0.5,N/A,This is not a contribution/public-goods game; no endowment allocation between private/public accounts.,1,N/R,"No linear public good or multiplier is defined; payoffs given in normal-form matrix, not as MPCR.",1,0,Random matching baseline here is explicitly the no communication treatment.,1,1,At end of each period subjects learned all actions and monetary pay-offs for the three individuals in their group and recorded them.,1,0,There is no punishment technology; leaders choose transgression type but do not assign costly punishment actions to identified individuals.,1,0,No explicit reward technology is implemented.,1,1,"Horizon is fixed and common knowledge at 50 periods, as in standard lab designs; although not literally stated they see period counters in z-Tree; coded as known.",0.7,0,Game only includes leader transgression and responder challenge; no separate costly punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the design.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the design.,1,0,No mechanism for responders or leader to bestow costly rewards; only payoffs from game structure.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,Payoffs are directly in francs from the normal-form game; no per-period token endowment for allocation is specified.,1,N/A,"There is no contribution decision; outcomes are transgression and resistance, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contributions in tokens or points are made.,1,N/R,"Paper reports transgression and resistance rates, not normalized efficiency measures.",1,N/R,Average or total group payoffs per condition are not reported numerically.,1,Australia and United States,Sessions held at Monash University (Australia) and Purdue University (USA).,1,N/R,Only described as general student population; no age range given.,1,N/R,No information on gender composition.,1,"University students (undergraduate/graduate, exact level N/R)",Subjects recruited from general student population at two large universities.,0.8,On site,Physical lab sessions with z-Tree at universities; not online.,1,"Divide-and-conquer Coordinated Resistance (CR) game. One leader (Person 1) chooses among four payoff squares (A,B,C,D) corresponding to transgression choices (no transgression, DAC against A or B, or transgression against both). Responders (Persons 2 and 3) simultaneously choose X or Y to challenge or acquiesce; payoffs are given in a 2x2 matrix for each leader choice. Successful joint challenge (both X or Y depending on mapping) can overturn DAC/transgression at a cost.","Summarized from description of CR game and instructions; figure with exact payoffs is referenced but values only partially stated in text (e.g., leader gains 3, responder loses 6 on successful transgression).",0.8
10.1111_ecoj.12088.md,Random matching – Communication,"Random matching baseline with communication, as described in Table 1 and text; communication added to one-shot-style environment.",1,Matching protocol: random matching; Communication: present (binary intention messages X/Y between responders each period); Repetition structure: 50 independent one-shot interactions; Horizon: 50 periods.,Same random matching as no-communication baseline but with added private cheap-talk communication between responders.,1,True,Human subjects in lab.,1,True,Controlled experimental treatment.,1,True,Run in Monash and Purdue labs.,1,False,No purely computational agents.,1,False,Empirical data only for this condition.,1,3,Groups of one leader and two responders.,1,50,Random matching treatments ran for 50 periods.,1,1,"Game involves discrete strategic choices, no partial contributions.",0.5,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No linear public-good structure.,1,1,Responders send binary non-binding intended choices (X or Y) to each other before acting; this is private communication.,1,1,Post-period screen shows all players’ actions and payoffs.,1,0,No punishment technology.,1,0,No reward technology.,1,1,50-period horizon is fixed and standard z-Tree interfaces show period counters; not explicitly stated but implied.,0.7,0,No explicit costly punishment action.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs directly generated from game matrix.,1,N/A,No contribution decision.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,"Study reports transgression and resistance rates, not efficiency.",1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics reported by treatment.,1,Australia and United States,Sessions at Monash and Purdue.,1,N/R,Only described as students.,1,N/R,No gender data.,1,University students (exact level N/R),Recruited from general student population.,0.8,On site,Conducted in physical labs with z-Tree.,1,"Same CR game as other treatments: leader chooses among four payoff squares (A–D); responders choose X or Y. Here, after observing leader choice, responders first send simultaneous private intention messages (intended X or Y) to each other, then choose actual actions. Leader does not observe messages.",Summarized from Section 2 and Appendix A instructions for communication treatments.,1
