y using an 8-sided die; new random 3-person groups formed when die shows 8. Average of six repeated games (3–10 range) per session.,From Section 2 description of indefinite repetition.,1
10.1111_ecoj.12088.md,Indefinite repetition (7/8 continuation) – Communication,Indefinite-horizon repeated game with communication.,1,Matching protocol: partner matching within each indefinitely repeated game; Communication: present (binary intention messages); Repetition structure: repeated games with 7/8 continuation probability and random regrouping upon termination; many super-games per session.,Same as indefinite no-communication but with private intention messages between responders.,1,True,Human subjects.,1,True,Experimental manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in labs.,1,False,Not simulation.,1,False,Empirical.,1,3,Three-person groups.,1,48,Sessions lasted at least 48 decision periods; indefinite groupings until constraints; approximate session length similar to other repeated treatments.,0.6,1,Discrete actions; no contributions.,0.5,N/A,No contribution stage.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,1,Responders send binary intended choices to each other each period after seeing leader choice.,1,1,Outcome info fully revealed.,1,0,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No reward technology.,1,0,Termination random via die; only continuation probability known.,1,0,No formal punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/R,Only payoff matrices.,1,N/A,No contribution decision.,1,N/A,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Group earnings not provided.,1,Australia and United States,Sessions run at Monash and Purdue.,1,N/R,No age info.,1,N/R,No gender info.,1,University students (exact level N/R),Students recruited.,0.8,On site,Physical lab sessions.,1,Indefinite repetition with communication allows both history and cheap talk to coordinate resistance; findings show communication is more informative than past resistance in guiding current resistance.,"Based on Findings 2, 5, 6 and Table 7.",1
10.1093_restud_rdy048.md,Analytical model – infinite-memory benchmark with temporary exclusion,Paper defines an equilibrium benchmark with infinite public memory where defaulters are excluded for K periods; used as main reference outcome.,1,Length of exclusion K under infinite memory; borrower discount factor δ; involuntary default probability λ; gain from default g.,These parameters determine incentives and equilibrium outcomes in the infinite-memory benchmark.,1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical repeated-game analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; analytical model only.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; theoretical work.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported for this benchmark.,1,True,Closed-form expressions and equilibrium derivations for payoffs and constraints under infinite memory.,1,2,Stage game Γ is a bilateral trust game with player 1 (lender) and player 2 (borrower); large continuum population but interactions are pairwise.,1,-1,"Infinite-horizon repeated game Γ^∞; horizon is infinite, coded as -1.",1,1,"Players choose discrete actions (trust/not, repay/default); there is no continuous contribution choice; coded as 1 since the binary choice is not an all-or-nothing contribution level in a public good sense.",0.5,N/A,"Trust game, not a contribution game with default endowment allocation.",1,N/A,Not a public goods or linear contribution game; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication structure among model agents not instantiated as chat.,1,N/A,"Information is formal signal structure over borrower histories, not experiment feedback to humans.",1,N/A,No identities or punishment stages among human subjects.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with identities.,1,N/A,Infinite horizon theoretical model; no display to subjects.,1,1,Punishment is modeled as exclusion from credit for K periods after default.,1,N/R,"No explicit per-unit cost of punishing to any player; exclusion is imposed by equilibrium/community, not by costly individual action.",0.5,N/R,Severity is K-period exclusion but not parameterized as tokens-per-coin style technology.,0.5,0,No separate costly positive reward mechanism; only standard loan payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses payoff parameters (1, g, ℓ, etc.) but does not define a standard endowment as in contribution games.",0.5,N/R,"Outcome of interest is lending and repayment, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No numerical contribution amounts reported.,1,N/R,"They define efficient benchmark payoffs (V*, W*, V, W) but no scalar efficiency ratio is computed.",0.7,N/R,Payoffs V and W are per-role; no aggregate group payoff is tabulated.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Purely theoretical analysis, no empirical environment.",1,"Infinite-horizon random-matching trust game with one-sided moral hazard; lenders observe full infinite history; defaulters (wilful or involuntary) are excluded from credit for K periods; borrower involuntary default probability λ; borrower discount factor δ; equilibrium payoffs V, W for minimal integer K and fully efficient V*, W* achievable with public randomization over punishment length.",Summarizes structure of the infinite-memory benchmark defined in Section 3.1.,1
10.1093_restud_rdy048.md,Analytical model – perfect bounded memory with fine information,Section 3.2 studies the case where lenders observe the finest partition of the last K outcomes; main negative result (no lending) under bounded memory.,1,"Memory length K (finite) with perfect information on last K outcomes; payoff shocks ε for purification; borrower parameters δ, λ, g and lender loss ℓ.",Result holds for arbitrary K under perfect monitoring and small payoff shocks.,1,False,The result is purely theoretical.,1,False,Not an implemented experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,No numerical simulations reported for this condition.,1,True,Proposition 1 is proven analytically.,1,2,Each match is a bilateral lender-borrower trust game.,1,-1,Infinite-horizon repeated matching.,1,1,"Actions are discrete (trust/not, repay/default), not continuous contributions.",0.5,N/A,"Trust game, not a public good contribution game.",1,N/A,No public goods production.,1,N/A,No communication between human subjects.,1,N/A,"Information structure is formal histories, not experiment displays.",1,N/A,No identities or explicit punishment choices.,1,N/A,No identity-linked rewards.,1,N/A,"Infinite horizon, no display.",1,1,Intended equilibrium structure uses temporary exclusion for K periods after default; Proposition 1 shows such punishments unravel.,1,N/R,No explicit per-punishment cost parameter.,0.5,N/R,"Punishment is K-period exclusion, not expressed as per-unit tech.",0.5,0,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model defined via payoffs, not via explicit endowment and contribution.",0.5,0,"Proposition 1: in any purifiable equilibrium with perfect bounded memory, lender never lends and borrower never repays; interpreted as zero cooperative action.",0.9,0,"No lending occurs in equilibrium, so effective 'trust' amount is zero.",0.9,0,"Outcome is repeated play of backward-induction profile (no lending, default); Pareto-dominated by cooperative (Y,R); relative efficiency effectively zero.",0.7,N/R,No single numeric payoff given; just qualitative result that no lending occurs.,0.5,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,"Analytical proposition, no data collection.",1,"Trust game with one-sided moral hazard; bounded memory K for lenders over borrower outcomes (N,R,D); perfect fine-grained information on last K outcomes; with sequential strictness or purifiability, any attempt to condition on borrower history unravels so only backward-induction outcome (no trust, default) is sustainable.",Summarizes the condition studied in Proposition 1.,1
10.1093_restud_rdy048.md,"Analytical model – simple coarse information structure, pure-strategy equilibrium with lending only after good signal",Section 4.1 and Proposition 2 analyze equilibrium under the 'simple information structure' where lenders only see whether there was any default in last K periods and lend only after good history.,1,"Memory length K (finite, sufficiently large, K ≥ max{\bar{K},2}); simple partitional information structure with binary signals G (no default in last K periods) and B (at least one default in last K periods); payoffs δ, λ, g, ℓ.",These parameters define this analytical condition and determine existence of the pure equilibrium.,1,False,Analytical characterization only.,1,False,Not implemented as an experiment with subjects.,1,False,Theoretical.,1,False,No numerical simulations explicitly described for Proposition 2.,1,True,Equilibrium conditions and bounds on m*(K) derived in closed form.,1,2,Bilateral lender-borrower trust game per match.,1,-1,Infinite-horizon repeated matching.,1,1,"Discrete actions (Y/N, R/D) rather than divisible contributions.",0.5,N/A,No public account endowment structure.,1,N/A,"Trust game, not linear public good.",1,N/A,No human interaction setting.,1,N/A,Signals summarize borrower’s own history; no experimental feedback.,1,N/A,No individual punishers; punishment is exclusion via rating.,1,N/A,No explicit peer rewards.,1,N/A,Infinite horizon theoretical model.,1,1,Defaulters are excluded from credit (no loans after B) for up to K periods until their history clears.,1,N/R,No explicit cost per unit of exclusion.,0.5,N/R,"Punishment severity captured by remaining exclusion length m, not by per-token mappings.",0.5,0,"Lending is part of baseline game, not a separate costly reward stage.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model specifies payoffs directly; no standard endowment token level.,0.5,N/R,"Outcomes described in terms of lending/repayment probabilities and stationary distributions, not normalized contribution rates.",0.7,N/R,Loan sizes normalized but no specific amount stated.,0.7,N/R,They compare to efficient benchmarks but do not give a numerical efficiency ratio here; they show lending can be sustained.,0.7,N/R,No explicit numeric steady-state payoffs given for general K in Proposition 2.,0.5,N/A,No empirical implementation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical model.,1,"Lenders observe only a coarse binary signal: G if the borrower has no default (D) in last K periods, B otherwise. Borrower’s private state m is number of remaining periods until regaining G. Candidate equilibrium: lender lends after G only; borrowers with G (m=0) repay, some with B near rehabilitation (small m) also repay, those with large m default. Because all B-types are pooled, lender’s expected repayment probability at B can be made low enough (for large K) that not lending after B is optimal, sustaining punishment without unraveling.",Summarizes structure and equilibrium behavior under simple information structure in Proposition 2.,1
10.1093_restud_rdy048.md,"Analytical model – simple information structure with minimal punishment length \bar{K}, pure-strategy equilibrium when loans not too profitable",Section 5.1 and Proposition 3 examine the case K = \bar{K} with simple information structure and a pure-strategy equilibrium when ℓ is large (loans not very profitable).,1,"Memory length K fixed at minimal exclusion length \bar{K} ≥ 2; simple (or random) information structure; lender loss parameter ℓ satisfying ℓ > 1/(\bar{K}−1); other parameters δ, λ, g as before.",This combination determines existence of pure equilibria with minimal punishment and potentially randomization at boundary histories.,1,False,Theoretical characterization only.,1,False,No human-subject treatment.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,False,General Proposition 3 is analytic; example later uses numbers but not described as simulation algorithm.,0.8,True,"Equilibria and payoff bounds (\bar{V}, \bar{W}, V*, W*) derived analytically.",1,2,Pairwise lender-borrower interactions.,1,-1,Infinite repetition.,1,1,Discrete choices in trust game.,0.5,N/A,No contribution endowment structure.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No empirical communication.,1,N/A,"Signals are theoretical constructs, not on-screen feedback.",1,N/A,No individual punishers identified.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/A,Infinite horizon and no human subjects.,1,1,Defaulters are excluded for \bar{K} periods (minimal length that sustains repayment under infinite memory).,1,N/R,No explicit per-unit punishment cost.,0.5,N/R,"Punishment specified as exclusion length, not with a numeric tech factor.",0.5,0,No separate costly rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Game specified via payoffs 1, g, ℓ etc., not via explicit endowments.",0.5,N/R,"They focus on borrower and lender payoffs (\bar{V}, \bar{W}, V*, W*), not normalized cooperation rates.",0.7,N/R,Loan size normalized; not given as numeric amount.,0.7,N/R,"They label \bar{V}, \bar{W} as constrained-efficient and V*, W* as fully efficient but do not compute ratios.",0.8,N/R,Do not report joint payoff as a single scalar; instead provide separate roles’ payoffs.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical study.,1,"Under simple information with K = \bar{K} ≥ 2, in pure-strategy equilibrium when ℓ > 1/(\bar{K}−1), lender lends only after G, never after B. Among B histories, only m=1 type repays; thus probability of repayment at B is 1/\bar{K}, making lending at B unprofitable when ℓ is large enough. This equilibrium gives borrower constrained-efficient payoff \bar{V} and lender \bar{W}. With a random refinement of the signal at the boundary history (single default exactly \bar{K} periods ago), using probability x of sending B, one can approximate fully efficient payoffs V* and W*.",Summarizes Section 5.1 and Proposition 3.,1
10.1093_restud_rdy048.md,"Analytical model – simple information structure with minimal punishment length \bar{K}, mixed-strategy equilibrium when loans very profitable",Section 5.2 and Proposition 4 analyze mixed equilibria when ℓ is small and lenders lend with positive probability at B.,1,"Memory length K = \bar{K} ≥ 2; simple information structure; lender loss ℓ in (0, 1/(\bar{K}−1)); mixing probabilities p (lender lends at B) and q (borrower repays when m∈{0,1}); other parameters δ, λ, g.","Equilibrium exists for ℓ in this range, with endogenous p and q chosen so lender is indifferent at B.",1,False,Theoretical characterization with a worked numeric example; no subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,They compute stationary distributions and example numbers but do not describe algorithmic simulation.,0.8,True,"Equilibrium mixing conditions and stationary distributions derived analytically, with example parameterization.",1,2,Each interaction is a 2-player trust game.,1,-1,Infinite horizon.,1,1,Discrete trust/default choices.,0.5,N/A,Not a public good game.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,"Theoretical, no chat variable.",1,N/A,Information structure is coarse record (G/B) only.,1,N/A,No identified punishers.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,"Infinite horizon, no display.",1,1,"Defaulters face exclusion, but now effectively random because lenders sometimes lend at B.",1,N/R,No explicit per-punishment cost.,0.5,N/R,"Punishment tech summarized by exclusion length and mixing, not numeric penalty per unit.",0.5,0,No separate reward instrument.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment level; payoffs normalized.,0.5,N/R,"Described via stationary share of good histories μ0 and lender profit level, not contribution fractions.",0.7,N/R,Loan unit normalized to 1 but not labeled as contribution.,0.7,N/R,"They show borrower payoff exceeds \bar{V} and can reach V*, while lender payoff falls with ℓ, but no scalar efficiency ratio.",0.7,N/R,"Present separate role payoffs in examples, not combined.",0.7,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,No human,"Analytical model with example parameterization (δ=0.9, λ=0.1, g=2, various ℓ).",1,"With K = \bar{K}, when loans are very profitable (0 < ℓ < 1/(\bar{K}−1)), pure equilibrium with no lending at B fails. Equilibrium involves lender lending after G with prob.1 and after B with prob.p>0, while borrowers with m>1 always default and those with m∈{0,1} repay with prob.q (or 1 when ℓ above a threshold ℓ* within this range). Stationary distribution over m shifts toward larger m among B-borrowers, increasing default risk at B and disciplining lenders. Borrower’s effective punishment length is ≤\bar{K}, giving payoff strictly above \bar{V}; when ℓ small enough that borrower mixes, her payoff reaches the fully efficient V*. Lender’s steady-state profit equals share of G-borrowers times net profit per G-loan and falls as ℓ decreases.","Summarizes mechanism and outcomes highlighted in Proposition 4 and the numerical example (e.g., \bar{K}=4 case with specific p and q).",1
10.1093_restud_rdy048.md,Analytical model – modified trust game with borrower-initiated interaction and simple information structure,"Section 5.3 and Proposition 5 analyze a variant where borrower must apply for a loan first, under simple information, and show full efficiency can be approximated.",1,"Stage game modified to include borrower application decision with outside option b>0; memory length K=\bar{K}≥2; random version of simple information structure with parameter x for boundary histories; lender profit parameter ℓ>0 arbitrary; δ, λ, g as before.",These define the environment in which Proposition 5’s equilibrium \sigma* is constructed.,1,False,Theoretical study; no human experiments.,1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab implementation.,1,False,Equilibrium described analytically.,1,True,Proposition 5 provides analytical equilibrium characterization with signaling and D1 refinement.,1,2,Borrower (player 2) and lender (player 1) in each interaction.,1,-1,Repeated indefinitely.,1,1,"Discrete actions (apply/not, trust/not, repay/default).",0.5,N/A,No endowment allocation to public/private accounts.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No empirical communication channel.,1,N/A,Lenders observe only G/B signal and application; no summary statistics.,1,N/A,No individualized punishment beyond equilibrium exclusion.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,Infinite-horizon analytical model.,1,1,"Borrowers with bad histories (B) optimally do not get loans; attempted applications by B-types are rejected in equilibrium, constituting exclusion.",1,N/R,No explicit cost per exclusion action specified.,0.5,N/R,Punishment operates as denial of future loans until history clears.,0.5,0,No separate costly rewarding distinct from normal payoffs.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Trust game does not define a standard token endowment.,0.5,N/R,"Performance characterized via equilibrium payoffs approaching V* and W*, not a numeric cooperation rate.",0.7,N/R,Loan size normalized but not expressed as contribution amount.,0.7,N/R,"They state that equilibrium payoffs approximate fully efficient V*, W*, implying high efficiency, but no numeric ratio.",0.8,N/R,"Payoffs V*, W* given separately for borrower and lender; no combined scalar.",0.7,N/A,No empirical study.,1,N/A,No empirical study.,1,N/A,No empirical study.,1,N/A,No empirical study.,1,No human,Analytical signaling model.,1,"Stage game: borrower first chooses whether to apply for a loan (paying a small cost) or take outside option b>0. If she applies, the standard trust game is played. Under the random version of simple information with K=\bar{K}, equilibrium σ*: G-borrowers apply, receive loan, and repay; B-borrowers do not apply. Off-path, if B-borrowers applied, lenders would reject, with beliefs (satisfying D1) putting probability 1 on default. This yields approximate fully efficient payoffs V* and W* regardless of ℓ, as trust can be sustained with minimal exclusion.",Summarizes modified trust game and equilibrium characterization in Proposition 5.,1
10.1023_a_1011612522206.md,"Simulation 1 – Baseline temptation T=5 without dominance asymmetry (generic K1, K2, L grid)","Represents the main family of simulations with T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0, w=0.99 varying K1, K2, L over ranges shown in Figure 3 without dominance asymmetry.",0.8,"Costs of punishment and apology: K1 (cost of giving P), K2 (cost of receiving P), L (cost of giving AS), under payoff matrix T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0; no dominance asymmetry.","Text: 'When T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0, and w=0.99… We simulated which strategies dominated populations when the values of K1, K2, and L satisfied these conditions.' plus Figure 3 description.",0.9,False,Numerical simulations of strategies in IPD; no human or animal participants.,1,False,"The work is theoretical simulation, not a controlled behavioral or field experiment with subjects.",1,False,No lab setting; purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly described as 'numerical simulations' with strategy frequencies updated by game dynamics.,1,False,"Although analytical conditions for ESS are derived elsewhere, this row refers to the simulation component.",0.9,2,IPD is defined as a two-player game: 'Two players in the prisoner's dilemma game…'.,1,0,Repeated game with continuation probability w=0.99; effectively infinite horizon. Number of rounds per encounter not fixed or reported.,0.6,1,"Players choose between C and D each round; not a contribution game with continuous amounts. In this coding, 'can contribute any amount' vs 'all-or-nothing' does not apply; treat as all-or-nothing=1 to reflect binary choice.",0.5,N/A,No endowments or public-good structure; pure payoff matrix game with no human players.,1,N/A,"Prisoner's dilemma payoff matrix, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modeled.,1,0,"Model is defined only by payoff matrix, strategies, and signals; no concept of feedback screens or summaries.",1,N/A,No identities; agents are anonymous strategies in a population.,1,N/A,No rewards distinct from cooperation; no identities.,1,0,Simulation uses continuation probability w; agents do not observe horizon length.,0.9,1,Punishment signal P is explicitly modeled with costs K1 (giver) and K2 (receiver).,1,0,Cost of giving P is denoted K1 and is varied as the independent variable; a single numeric value is not attached to this generic condition.,0.3,0,"Model specifies costs (K1, K2) but not a simple linear 'damage per unit' technology as in public good punishment; cannot be mapped to a numeric magnitude per coin.",0.2,0,No modeled reward mechanism; only punishment (P) and apology signal (AS).,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,"Matrix game with payoffs (T,R,P,S); no token endowment per round.",1,N/A,Not a contribution game and no human decisions; main outcomes are population frequencies and average payoffs of strategies.,1,N/A,No continuous contributions; only choices C/D within IPD.,1,N/R,"Paper reports average payoffs for populations of AllC, TFT, GTFT, Pavlov, and pPAS (e.g., 2.94, 2.96) but does not normalize them explicitly as efficiency (0–1) relative to the maximum (R=3).",0.4,N/R,"Average payoff per player is reported (e.g., 2.94 in pPAS population) but not total group payoff per round or over the game.",0.5,N/A,No human or animal participants; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational simulations with strategy dynamics; no empirical environment.,1,"Iterated prisoner’s dilemma with error probability e=0.01 in moves and e′=0.01 in signals; continuation probability w=0.99; payoffs T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0. Strategy set: six basic strategies (AllD, AllC, AltDC, TFT, GTFT, Pavlov), four signal-based strategies (P, AS, PAS, pPAS), and six deceptive strategies (AllD-p, AllD-as, AllD-pas, AltDC-p, AltDC-as, AltDC-pas). Evolutionary dynamics: discrete generations up to 10,000, initial frequencies 1/16 per strategy, removal threshold 0.001, mutation introducing a random strategy at frequency 0.001 every 100 generations on average. Dominant outcomes across parameter grid were pPAS, GTFT, or AllD.",Directly summarized from 'IPD simulations' and 'Results of simulations' sections.,1
10.1023_a_1011612522206.md,"Simulation 2 – Example parameter set A (Figure 2a): K1=0.5, K2=2, L=0.1, T=5","Figure 2a explicitly described simulation outcome with these parameter values, where GTFT dominated.",0.95,"Costs: K1=0.5 (cost of giving P), K2=2 (cost of receiving P), L=0.1 (cost of giving AS); payoff matrix T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0; no dominance asymmetry.","Text under Figure 2a: '(a) K1=0.5, K2=2, L=0.1. GTFT dominated the population.'",1,False,"Numerical simulation with strategies, no human/animal participants.",1,False,"Theoretical simulation, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab; computation only.,1,True,Parameterized simulation run of the general model.,1,False,"This row refers specifically to a numeric example, not the analytical derivation.",1,2,Two-player IPD.,1,0,Infinite-horizon IPD with continuation probability w=0.99; number of rounds per match not fixed.,0.6,1,Binary actions C or D; modeled as all-or-nothing choice.,0.6,N/A,No endowment or contribution.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication channel.,1,0,Model does not include any informational feedback beyond state and signals.,1,N/A,Agents lack individual identities; only strategies matter.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism distinct from cooperation.,1,0,Horizon not displayed or known; interactions follow geometric continuation.,0.9,1,Punishment signal P with specified costs is available in relevant strategies.,1,0.5,K1 is defined as 'cost of giving P'; here K1=0.5.,1,0,"Only costs (K1, K2) are parameterized; no explicit linear mapping of punishment investment to loss.",0.3,0,No reward option is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Matrix payoffs instead of endowments.,1,N/A,"Outcome measure is which strategy dominates, not contributions.",1,N/A,No numeric contribution amounts.,1,N/R,No explicit normalized efficiency reported for this parameter set.,0.3,N/R,Only which strategy dominated is reported for this run; no average payoff given.,0.2,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,No human,Computational simulation only.,1,"Uses the general 16-strategy set and evolutionary dynamics described in the IPD simulations section. With K1=0.5, K2=2, L=0.1 under T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0, w=0.99, GTFT always came to dominate the population in this example run.",Summarized from Figure 2a caption and surrounding description.,1
10.1023_a_1011612522206.md,"Simulation 3 – Example parameter set B (Figure 2b): K1=1, K2=3, L=1, T=5",Figure 2b describes this simulation where pPAS and PAS coexisted but pPAS dominated in the long run.,0.95,"Costs: K1=1 (cost of giving P), K2=3 (cost of receiving P), L=1 (cost of giving AS); payoff matrix T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0; no dominance asymmetry.","Text under Figure 2b: '(b) K1=1, K2=3, L=1. Both pPAS and PAS remained in the population. pPAS dominated the population in the long run.'",1,False,Simulation with no human or animal subjects.,1,False,"Theoretical simulation, not empirical.",1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Single parameterized simulation run.,1,False,"Focus here is numeric simulation outcome, not ESS derivation.",1,2,Standard two-player IPD.,1,0,Repeated IPD with continuation w=0.99; rounds not fixed.,0.6,1,Binary choice C vs D.,0.6,N/A,No endowment/public goods.,1,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,N/A,No communication and no human participants.,1,0,Agents only rely on game states and signals.,1,N/A,No individual identities.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Horizon length not represented.,0.9,1,"P signal is used by pPAS, PAS, and deceptive strategies, with K1, K2 defined.",1,1,K1 is cost of giving P; here K1=1.,1,0,No separate punishment multiplier parameter.,0.3,0,No rewarding beyond cooperation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No token endowment.,1,N/A,"Outcome is strategy prevalence, not contributions.",1,N/A,Not applicable to IPD.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency reported for this specific parameter set.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative dominance of strategies is reported for this example.,0.2,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Computation only.,1,"Same 16-strategy evolutionary IPD as Simulation 1. With K1=1, K2=3, L=1, both pPAS and PAS persisted for long periods, but pPAS dominated the population in the long run.",From Figure 2b caption and text: 'Both pPAS and PAS remained in the population. pPAS dominated the population in the long run.',1
10.1023_a_1011612522206.md,"Simulation 4 – Low temptation condition T=3.5, R=3, P=1, S=0 (generic K1, K2, L)","Set of simulations with modified temptation payoff T=3.5 considered 'low temptation to defect', comparing outcomes with baseline T=5.",0.85,"Temptation payoff T=3.5 vs baseline T=5; costs of signals K1, K2, L varied over some range; no dominance asymmetry.","Section 'Simulations at low temptation to defect': 'We conducted simulations at another set of payoff values (T=3.5, R=3, P=1, S=0)…'.",0.9,False,Numerical simulations only.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,Simulation.,1,True,Described as 'simulations' with altered payoff matrix.,1,False,Focus is on simulation outcomes for changed payoffs.,0.9,2,Still an IPD game between two players.,1,0,Continuation probability w (implicitly 0.99 as before) defines an infinite-horizon game.,0.6,1,Binary choices C/D.,0.6,N/A,No endowment contributions.,1,N/A,Not a public-goods framework.,1,N/A,No human/animal subjects.,1,0,No such display in the model.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Horizon not modeled as information.,0.9,1,P and AS signals still present in low-temptation simulations.,1,0,K1 is still varied; no single value for this generic condition.,0.3,0,Cost parameters only; no explicit per-unit punishment multiplier.,0.3,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Matrix game with payoffs T,R,P,S.",1,N/A,"Simulation outcome is dominance of strategies, not contributions.",1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency reported for low-temptation runs.,0.3,N/R,"Average payoffs not numerically reported for low-temptation simulations, only which strategy dominated.",0.3,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,All runs are computational.,1,"Same 16-strategy evolutionary IPD model as baseline, but with modified temptation payoff T=3.5 (low temptation). Pure Pavlov can be ESS at this payoff set. Simulations 'ended in populations of pPAS under a wider range of conditions (K1, K2, and L). GTFT rarely became a majority of the population.' Example comparisons: for K1=0.5, K2=2, L=0.1, GTFT dominated when T=5, whereas pPAS dominated when T=3.5; for K1=0.1, K2=2, L=0.1, PAS dominated when T=5, whereas pPAS dominated when T=3.5.",From 'Simulations at low temptation to defect' section.,1
10.1023_a_1011612522206.md,"Simulation 5 – Dominance asymmetry condition (generic K1d, K1s, K2d, K2s, Ld, Ls)",Simulations where costs of signals differ between dominant and subordinate players; pPAS tested for ESS and dominance.,0.85,"Dominance-related asymmetry in signal costs: dominant has costs K1d, K2d, Ld; subordinate has K1s, K2s, Ls; payoff matrix T=5, R=3, P=1, S=0; no change in w.","Section 'Simulations with a dominance asymmetry' defines K1d, K2d, Ld and K1s, K2s, Ls and reports conditions under which pPAS is ESS and dominates.",0.9,False,Again simulations only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Explicitly 'simulations' with dominance asymmetry.,1,False,This row refers to computational verification of ESS conditions under asymmetry.,0.9,2,Dyads of two players with assigned dominance status.,1,0,Same infinite-horizon IPD with w=0.99.,0.6,1,Actions are C/D.,0.6,N/A,No contributions/endowments.,1,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,No informational summaries; only game state matters.,1,N/A,Dominance status is known but not individual identity in a human-subject sense.,0.9,N/A,No reward.,1,0,Horizon not displayed.,0.9,1,P and AS signals with asymmetric costs for dominant vs subordinate are present.,1,0,"Two different giving-costs (K1d, K1s) exist; values vary within defined inequalities so no single numeric value describes this generic asymmetry condition.",0.3,0,Cost parameters but no explicit damage multiplier per unit.,0.3,0,No reward signaling.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,Matrix game structure.,1,N/A,"Focus on dominance of pPAS vs other strategies, not contributions.",1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiencies reported for dominance asymmetry case.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative outcome (pPAS can dominate) is reported; no numeric average payoff.,0.3,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,No human,"Computational model with roles 'dominant' and 'subordinate', no empirical setting.",1,"Same 16-strategy evolutionary IPD model as in baseline, but each interaction is between a dominant and a subordinate individual. Dominance status is assigned independently of strategy; each individual is dominant or subordinate equally often (Bourgeois-like role assignment). Costs for signals differ: K1d, K2d, Ld for dominants; K1s, K2s, Ls for subordinates. Analytical condition for pPAS to be ESS: K1d ≤ K1s < 1.98, K2s + Ls ≥ K2d + Ld > 2, and Ld ≤ Ls < 1.98. Simulations showed that pPAS can dominate the population under these conditions, even when punishment by subordinates is more costly than the damage imposed on dominants (e.g., example parameter set K1d=0.5, K1s=1, K2d=0.5, K2s=2, Ld=1.5, Ls=1.5 where pPAS dominated).",From 'Simulations with a dominance asymmetry' section.,1
10.1007_s10614-008-9154-2.md,"Baseline spatial evolutionary CPR simulation (torus, naive learning, fixed parameters)","Main simulation setup in Sect. 3.1–3.2 with torus, naive imitation of most successful neighbor, fixed parameter set and 10x10 grid; all subsequent variations compare to this baseline.",0.95,"Initial strategy shares z0 = (mD/m, mE/m, mC/m) over the 3 strategies; spatial arrangement randomized on a 10x10 torus; resource dynamics suppressed (fixed N0).",Section 3.2 systematically varies initial shares across set Z and random spatial allocations while keeping other parameters fixed.,0.96,False,Study is purely theoretical/simulation-based with no human participants.,1.0,False,No controlled human or field experiment; only computational simulations of an evolutionary game model.,1.0,False,No laboratory or in-person environment; only simulations.,1.0,True,Spatial evolutionary dynamics are explored via numerical simulations on a grid (torus).,1.0,False,"Model is specified analytically but main results come from numerical simulations, not closed-form analytical equilibria.",0.8,100,"Most simulations use a 10x10 grid, so m = 100 (Sect. 3.1).",1.0,50,"Baseline runs in Sect. 3.2 are for 50 rounds, which is said to be sufficient for equilibrium.",1.0,1,"Each agent chooses between two fixed effort levels (xL or xH), i.e. discrete all-or-nothing relative to low/high; no continuous contribution choice.",0.8,N/A,"There is no endowment allocation or public-goods contribution structure; agents choose harvest efforts, not reallocations from an endowment.",1.0,N/R,"This is not a linear public goods game; payoffs derive from a concave production function F(X), so MPCR is not defined in the usual sense and no equivalent is reported.",1.0,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely simulated agents.,1.0,N/A,Information structure is coded in the learning rule (agents observe neighbors’ payoffs) but there is no experimental interface for humans.,1.0,N/A,Sanctions are modeled mechanically between strategies on the grid; there are no human identities to reveal.,1.0,N/A,"No explicit reward mechanism exists in the model, only punishment via enforcers.",1.0,N/A,Simulated agents do not have an interface showing remaining rounds; horizon is imposed by the researcher only.,1.0,1,"Enforcers punish neighboring defectors at cost γ, imposing sanction δ on them (Sect. 2.2).",1.0,0.1,Parameter γ = 0.1 is the cost per punished defector for an enforcer (Sect. 3.1).,1.0,0.4,Parameter δ = 0.4 is the sanction per enforcer on a defector (Sect. 3.1).,1.0,0,Model only includes punishment (sanctions) and not positive rewards for cooperation.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism is present in the model.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism is present in the model.,1.0,N/R,Agents do not have an explicit token endowment; they choose effort levels determining harvest and profits.,1.0,N/R,"Study reports only strategy shares (D, C, E) and payoffs conceptually; it does not define or report normalized contribution rates.",1.0,N/R,"No per-agent contribution amounts (efforts) are reported as outcome statistics; only initial parameters xL=2, xH=4 are specified.",1.0,N/R,Paper does not quantify efficiency as realized payoff relative to a maximum; only qualitative comparisons of equilibria are given.,1.0,N/R,Aggregate payoffs Π are defined theoretically but no numeric outcome values per condition are reported.,1.0,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents in simulations.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,All results are from computational simulations of artificial agents on a spatial grid.,1.0,"Spatial evolutionary common-pool resource game on a 10x10 torus; 3 strategies (Defection: xH=4, Cooperation: xL=2, Enforcement: xL with local punishment); production F(X)=α X^β N0^(1-β) with α=0.2, β=0.2, N0=500, w=0.2; learning via synchronous imitation of neighbor with highest payoff; 4950 initial share vectors z0 sampled (excluding pure and 2-strategy-only cases), 100 random spatial configurations each, for 50 rounds.",Summarized directly from Sects. 2.2 and 3.1–3.2 which define the structure and parameters of the baseline simulation.,0.98
10.1007_s10614-008-9154-2.md,Alternative learning rule simulation (sophisticated averaging) on torus,Section 3.2 runs additional simulations on the same torus varying only the learning rule to imitation of the most successful strategy on average.,0.95,Learning rule = sophisticated: agents imitate the strategy with highest average payoff in their neighborhood instead of the single best-performing neighbor; other parameters as in baseline torus simulation.,Explicitly described as using the ‘sophisticated learning rule’ of Noailly et al. (2007) with same parameters as baseline (Sect. 3.2).,0.96,False,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1.0,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment.,1.0,False,No physical lab or participants.,1.0,True,Results come from numerical simulations on a torus grid.,1.0,False,"Learning dynamics are evaluated via simulation, not analytically.",0.8,100,Uses the same 10x10 spatial grid as in the baseline (Sect. 3.2).,0.95,50,Described as ‘additional simulations on the torus’ using the same parameters and procedure as in Sect. 3.2 (50 rounds).,0.9,1,Same discrete effort choice xL vs xH as baseline; only learning rule differs.,1.0,N/A,No endowment-contribution setup; same as baseline.,1.0,N/R,Same production technology as baseline; no MPCR parameter is defined.,1.0,N/A,No human communication.,1.0,N/A,No human interface; agents observe neighbor payoffs programmatically.,1.0,N/A,No human identities; sanctions are automatic.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,Agents do not see a round counter; horizon is fixed externally.,1.0,1,Same enforcer-based punishment structure as baseline.,1.0,0.1,γ is unchanged from baseline.,1.0,0.4,δ is unchanged from baseline.,1.0,0,No rewards introduced in this variation.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/R,Same effort-based payoff model; no token endowment.,1.0,N/R,"Outcomes are described as frequencies of equilibria (D, CE, E), not contributions.",1.0,N/R,No per-agent effort or harvest statistics are reported for this variation.,1.0,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency metrics for this learning-rule comparison.,1.0,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not numerically summarized; only equilibrium frequencies are.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,This is a variant of the torus simulation with a different learning rule.,1.0,"Same spatial evolutionary CPR game and parameters as baseline (10x10 torus, α=0.2, β=0.2, N0=500, w=0.2, xH=4, xL=2, δ=0.4, γ=0.1, naive vs sophisticated punishment) but agents now imitate the strategy that is most successful on average in their neighborhood. With this rule, about 79% of runs converge to D-equilibria, 23% to CE-equilibria and 3% to E-equilibria, with no CDE or DE equilibria observed.",Directly summarized from the paragraph in Sect. 3.2 describing results under the sophisticated learning rule.,0.98
10.1007_s10614-008-9154-2.md,"Alternative effort-level calibration simulation (xH = XC/m, xL = XP/m)",Section 3.2 considers a configuration where defectors play Nash (xH=XC/m) and cooperators play Pareto (xL=XP/m) while keeping other aspects of the torus model fixed.,0.95,"Effort levels set to xH = XC/m ≈ 4.95 and xL = XP/m ≈ 0.67, representing defectors playing Nash equilibrium effort and cooperators playing Pareto efficient effort; other baseline parameters and naive learning on torus preserved.",Described explicitly as setting xH=XC/m and xL=XP/m with numerical equivalents given in a footnote.,0.96,False,Simulation only.,1.0,False,No empirical experiment.,1.0,False,No lab setting.,1.0,True,Numerical simulations on torus grid.,1.0,False,Outcomes obtained by simulation.,0.8,100,"Same 10x10 grid as baseline, not stated otherwise.",0.95,50,Frequencies are reported ‘in this case’ alongside baseline; they appear to follow the same 50-round design as Sect. 3.2.,0.9,1,Agents still choose between two discrete effort levels xL and xH.,1.0,N/A,Not a contribution-from-endowment game.,1.0,N/R,Technology is Cobb–Douglas; no MPCR defined.,1.0,N/A,No human communication.,1.0,N/A,"Simulated agents algorithmically observe neighbors, no interface.",1.0,N/A,No human identities.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No displayed horizon for agents.,1.0,1,Same enforcement structure as baseline.,1.0,0.1,γ not altered in this variant.,1.0,0.4,δ not altered.,1.0,0,Model still only has punishment.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/R,Effort choice model; no explicit endowment.,1.0,N/R,"Only frequencies of equilibria (D, DE, CDE) are reported.",1.0,N/R,No outcome-level efforts or harvests reported beyond parameter choice.,1.0,N/R,No efficiency statistics provided for this calibration.,1.0,N/R,Group payoffs are not numerically summarized.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,"Simulation only, variant of baseline setup.",1.0,"Same spatial evolutionary CPR game and torus setup as baseline, but with defectors choosing xH = XC/m and cooperators xL = XP/m (numerically xH ≈ 4.95, xL ≈ 0.67 given parameter values). With this specification, about 56% of runs converge to D-equilibria, 29% to DE-equilibria, 14% to CDE-equilibria, and 0% to CE and E equilibria.",Directly drawn from the paragraph in Sect. 3.2 and footnote giving numerical values.,0.98
10.1007_s10614-008-9154-2.md,Mutation robustness simulation from fixed CDE configuration,Section 3.4 analyzes the stability of CDE equilibria starting from a specific spatial distribution with mutations at rate α=0.005.,0.95,"Mutation probability α = 0.005 per agent per round, time horizon varied (100, 200, 1000, 10000, 20000 rounds); initial spatial configuration fixed with 25 C, 50 E, 25 D arranged in four quadrants.","Explicitly describes α and the fixed starting configuration, and reports time spent in different equilibria for various horizons.",0.98,False,Pure simulation.,1.0,False,Not an empirical experiment; computational study.,1.0,False,"No lab, no participants.",1.0,True,Numerical simulations with stochastic mutations and spatial dynamics.,1.0,False,"Stochastic stability investigated via simulation, not analytically.",0.8,100,Uses a 10x10 lattice with 25+50+25 players described in four blocks.,1.0,20000,Longest time horizon considered in Table 1 is 20000 rounds; results show time spent in equilibria up to that horizon.,1.0,1,Same discrete effort choice structure as baseline; not changed here.,1.0,N/A,No endowment contribution model.,1.0,N/R,Same production technology; MPCR not defined.,1.0,N/A,No human agents.,1.0,N/A,No experimental interface; only algorithmic observation.,1.0,N/A,No identities to reveal.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,Agents not informed of horizon.,1.0,1,Same local enforcement mechanism as core model.,1.0,0.1,γ unchanged.,1.0,0.4,δ unchanged.,1.0,0,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/R,"Efforts, not endowments, drive payoffs.",1.0,N/R,"Outcomes characterized as proportions of time in D, DE, CE, CDE equilibria, not by contributions.",1.0,N/R,No per-round effort statistics provided.,1.0,N/R,Stability is examined via time shares in different equilibria; efficiency not computed.,1.0,N/R,Group payoffs under mutations not summarized numerically.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Simulation of evolutionary dynamics with mutations.,1.0,"Starting from a specific spatial configuration on a 10x10 torus with 25 cooperators, 50 enforcers, 25 defectors arranged in four 25-cell blocks, a mutation probability α=0.005 per agent per round is introduced. For horizons up to 20000 rounds, the fraction of time spent in each equilibrium type (D, DE, CE, CDE) is computed over 100 simulation runs; e.g., after 10000 rounds, about 55% of time is in D-equilibria, 20% in DE, 0% in CE, and 25% in CDE (Table 1).",Direct synthesis of Sect. 3.4 and Table 1 description.,0.98
10.1007_s10614-008-9154-2.md,Mutation robustness simulation with randomized initial shares and spatial distributions,Second part of Sect. 3.4 generalizes the mutation analysis by randomizing initial strategy shares and spatial arrangements with mutations α=0.005.,0.94,"Initial strategy shares and spatial configurations randomized as in Sect. 3.2, with mutation probability α = 0.005 per agent per round and time horizon of 500 rounds.",Explicitly states that for each initial share they compute 100 runs of 500 rounds with mutations.,0.98,False,Simulation.,1.0,False,No human or field experiment.,1.0,False,No lab context.,1.0,True,Numerical simulations with stochastic mutation and random initial conditions.,1.0,False,Stability examined through simulated frequencies.,0.8,100,Same 10x10 torus as in Sect. 3.2; only mutations added.,0.95,500,They compute 100 runs of 500 rounds per initial share under mutations.,1.0,1,Effort choice remains discrete xL/xH.,1.0,N/A,No endowment contribution game.,1.0,N/R,Technology is unchanged from baseline.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,"Programmatic observation by agents, no UI.",1.0,N/A,No identities.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,Agents not given horizon information.,1.0,1,Same local sanctioning by enforcers as in baseline.,1.0,0.1,γ remains 0.1.,1.0,0.4,δ remains 0.4.,1.0,0,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/R,Effort decision model; no explicit endowment.,1.0,N/R,Outcome summary focuses on frequencies of equilibria across runs.,1.0,N/R,No reported effort/harvest statistics.,1.0,N/R,No efficiency measures reported.,1.0,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not tabulated.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Computation only.,1.0,"Using the same parameterization as baseline (10x10 torus, naive imitation, xL=2, xH=4, etc.) but adding mutations at rate α=0.005, and randomizing initial shares and spatial layouts as in Sect. 3.2. For each initial share, 100 runs of 500 rounds are simulated. On average, about 54% of runs converge to D-equilibria, 22% to DE-equilibria, 0% to CE-equilibria, and 24% to CDE-equilibria after 500 rounds.",Direct summary from the second part of Sect. 3.4.,0.98
10.1007_s10614-008-9154-2.md,"Sensitivity analysis: sanctions vs harvest productivity (α, δ) on torus",First part of Sect. 3.5 varies sanction level δ and productivity parameter α on a 10x10 torus.,0.9,"Sanction level δ varied between 0.1 and 1.5 and harvest productivity parameter α varied between 0.1 and 0.7; other parameters as in baseline (β=0.2, N0=500, w=0.2, xH=4, xL=2, m=100); naive imitation on torus.",Sect. 3.5 explicitly states the ranges for δ and α and that other parameters follow Sect. 3.2.,0.96,False,Simulation study.,1.0,False,No empirical experiment.,1.0,False,No lab environment.,1.0,True,Numerical simulations for multiple parameter configurations.,1.0,False,"Results shown as contour plots of frequencies, indicating simulation-based analysis.",0.8,100,"m=100 on a 10x10 grid is specified for most simulations, including this sensitivity case.",1.0,50,"Sect. 3.5 states ‘we performed simulation runs as in Section 3.2’, which used 50 rounds.",0.95,1,Effort choice remains between discrete xL and xH.,1.0,N/A,No endowment contribution frame.,1.0,N/R,Nonlinear CPR technology; no MPCR defined.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No user interface.,1.0,N/A,No identities.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,Horizon not shown to agents.,1.0,1,Sanctions δ and costs γ remain in effect; only δ is varied.,1.0,0.1,γ stays at 0.1; only δ and α are varied.,1.0,N/R,δ is varied across a range (0.1–1.5) rather than fixed; a single numeric value cannot represent the condition.,0.4,0,"Still only punishment, no rewards.",1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/R,Effort choice model; no endowment parameter.,1.0,N/R,Outcomes are frequencies of D-equilibria vs parameters; no contributions reported.,1.0,N/R,No per-agent effort data given.,1.0,N/R,No explicit efficiency analysis as function of α and δ.,1.0,N/R,Group payoffs not tabulated for parameter grid.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,No human,Sensitivity analysis via simulations.,1.0,"For each parameter pair (α, δ) with α in [0.1, 0.7] and δ in [0.1, 1.5], simulations as in Sect. 3.2 (10x10 torus, naive imitation, random initial shares multiples of 0.05) are run and the average frequency of convergence to D-equilibria is computed. Contour plots (Fig. 7a) show that D-equilibria are more frequent for low δ and high α, meaning weak sanctions and productive/valuable resources favor defection.",Summary of Sect. 3.5 description and Fig. 7a.,0.95
