10.1007_s00355-015-0884-5.md,Rich Punishment (RP),Treatment label in Table 1 and Section 2.1.1.,1,"Punishment availability: rich, multi-stage peer punishment with fixed IDs and endogenous number of punishment stages","RP has ≥1 punishment stages per period, determined endogenously; IDs fixed across all 30 periods (Table 1, Section 2.1.1).",1,True,Human participants in laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with specified rules and payoffs.,1,True,Run at the Experimental Economics Laboratory at the University of Melbourne.,1,False,No simulation-only study.,1,False,No purely analytical derivations as main method.,1,4,Four players simultaneously choose contributions (Section 2.1.1).,1,30,Game is repeated 30 times with fixed group composition (Section 2.1.1).,1,1,"Each player decides how much of their 20 ECU endowment to contribute; amount is a free (integer) choice, not binary.",1,0,Endowment is initially private; players must actively move contributions to the group account.,1,0.4,"Contributions multiplied by 1.6 and then distributed equally among 4 players, so 1.6/4.",1,0,No communication channel or messaging described.,0.9,1,"Players are informed of each member’s contribution and, in subsequent punishment stages, of the number of punishment points assigned by each player to each other player in the previous stage (Section 2.1.1).",1,1,"In further punishment stages every player is informed about ‘the number of points assigned by each individual player to each of the players’ in the previous stage, which identifies punishers and targets by fixed ID.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Design explicitly states 30 repetitions; typically conveyed to subjects.,0.7,1,"Multiple punishment stages exist, with peer punishment decisions each stage.",1,1,Assigning a punishment point costs the punisher 1 ECU (Section 2.1.1).,1,3,Each punishment point reduces the punished player’s income by 3 ECU (Section 2.1.1).,1,0,Only costly punishment is available; no positive rewards mentioned.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,20,"Each player has an initial endowment of 20 ECU for contribution stage (Section 2.1.1). The extra 125 ECU is an initial capital to cover potential losses, not the per-period endowment.",1,N/R,Only raw contributions in ECU are reported.,0,12.63,Table 4 Panel A: average contribution in RP across all periods is 12.63 ECU.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure reported.,0,23.9,Table 2 Panel A: ‘Average earnings’ in RP across all periods are 23.90 ECU per individual per period.,1,Australia,Lab at University of Melbourne.,0.9,N/R,Age not specified.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students (various disciplines),Participants are students at the University of Melbourne (Section 2.2).,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab using z-Tree.,1,"Linear public good game; 4 players; 20 ECU endowment each period; contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly redistributed; four-player group fixed for 30 periods with fixed IDs; contribution stage followed by potentially many punishment stages within the same period; in each punishment stage, player i can assign integer punishment points to each other player j, as long as both have non-negative earnings; each punishment point costs 1 ECU to punisher and 3 ECU to target; maximum total points a player can assign in a stage is limited by their current payoff; no limit on punishment a player can receive; new punishment stages continue as long as some punishment is assigned and conditions permit, up to a finite bound (maximum 34 stages). Detailed feedback on contributions and on who punished whom in the previous punishment stage enables strategies such as counter-punishment and punishment of non-punishers.",Summarizes RP design from Section 2.1.1 and related footnotes.,1
