ntities shown.,1,0,Agents are not human and do not see a round counter; horizon length is an external simulation parameter.,1,1,Model includes punishment cost E and transfer when one cooperates positively and the other negatively.,1,N/R,"E is a lump-sum penalty and transfer, not defined as cost-per-token choice; no per-unit cost concept.",0.3,N/R,Magnitude per unit of punishment expenditure not defined; E directly enters payoffs.,0.3,0,No additional costly rewarding stage; only punishment and standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/R,"No individual monetary endowment specified; only parameters R, c, E, S.",1,N/R,"Outcome variables are average probability of positive cooperation (p̄) and average payoff (Ū), not normalized contribution rates against an endowment.",1,N/R,No quantitative contribution amount is modeled; only cooperate/defect strategies.,1,N/R,They do not compute payoffs as fraction of maximum feasible payoff under full cooperation; only report Ū.,1,N/R,Only average per-node payoff Ū is reported; no total group payoff per round.,1,N/A,No human participants; country attribute not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; age not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; gender not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; education not applicable.,1,No human,Study is a numerical evolutionary simulation and analytical modeling only.,1,"Two-population evolutionary donation-style Prisoner’s Dilemma on empirical bipartite IUR patent network; players are industry side vs university & research institutes side, strategies are positively cooperate vs negatively cooperate, payoffs depend on excess return R=10, development cost c, punishment E, knowledge spillover S, distribution coefficient α, and updates follow a Fermi rule with noise ξ=0.1 until convergence of average positive cooperation probability p̄ and average payoff Ū.",Summarizes the core game and network structure from Sections 3 and 4.,1
