10.1016_j.physa.2017.01.086.md,"Simulation – Joint parameter sweeps (S, E, α) and degree-dependent effects","Section 4.4 and Fig. 5–9 examine relationships of node degree with p_i and joint variations of S, E, α.",0.9,"Multiple joint sweeps: (1) S varied with R=10, E=2.5<c=5, α=0.5; (2) S varied with R=10, E=4>c=2, α=0.5; (3) E varied with R=10, c=5, α=0.5, S=0; (4) α varied with R=10, c=4, S=0, E=2.5; all on empirical IUR network, inspecting dependence of individual p_i on node degree k_i",Fig. 5 summarizes these parameter sweeps; Figs. 6–9 add degree-wise plots.,0.9,False,Network data are empirical but dynamics are purely simulated.,1,False,No human experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,All results obtained by Algorithm 1 on empirical network.,1,False,Section 4.4 is simulation-based.,0.9,309,Same underlying network.,1,2000,Figs. 5–9 captions all state results obtained with 2000 iterations (except where otherwise specified earlier).,0.9,1,Binary cooperation decision per link.,0.6,N/A,No contribution endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR concept.,1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No explicit feedback or history summaries shown to agents.,0.6,N/A,No identity-based punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Agents do not know T.,1,1,All these sweeps involve punishment parameter E.,1,N/R,E is not decomposed into cost per unit.,0.3,N/R,Only overall penalty parameter E.,0.3,0,No reward mechanics.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No endowment.,1,N/R,"Main outcomes are node-level cooperation probabilities p_i, network average p̄, and average payoff Ū, not mapped to contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,Efficiency vs social optimum not computed.,1,N/R,Group totals or overall welfare not numerically reported.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Simulations using empirical network structure.,1,"Using empirical IUR network, simulations explore how node degree interacts with knowledge spillover S, punishment E, and distribution α. When E<c, increasing S sharply reduces cooperation of high-degree nodes; when E>c, high cooperation persists across S. Increasing E especially boosts cooperation among large-degree nodes. Varying α shows that highly unequal profit splits (α near 0 or 1) distort behavior of hubs, while α≈0.5 yields more balanced high cooperation across degrees.",Synthesizes key findings from Figs. 5–9 and the Section 4.4 narrative.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2010.12.044.md,"Simulation – evolutionary snowdrift game with punishers (N=30000, uniform initial distribution, r varied, α=0.1, β=0.5)","Only one numerical simulation setup with human-independent agents is described in detail: N=30000, initial fractions 1/3 each, α=0.1, β=0.5, r varied; used for Figs. 1–3.",0.9,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r varied in (0,1) with fixed punishment parameters α=0.1, β=0.5 and uniform initial fractions fC=fD=fP=1/3",The main parameter scanned in simulations is r (Figs. 1–3) for fixed α and β and fixed initial frequencies.,0.95,False,Model is an evolutionary game with simulated agents; no human or animal participants.,1.0,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; only numerical simulations and analytical equations.,1.0,False,No lab setting with human subjects; purely computational.,1.0,True,Authors repeatedly refer to numerical simulations with N=30000 agents and Monte Carlo–like updating.,1.0,True,Section 4 derives and analyzes replicator-dynamics differential equations capturing the same model.,1.0,30000,They state: “We take N=30000 …” for the simulations.,1.0,0,Model uses continuous evolutionary time steps until steady state; no discrete fixed number of game rounds as in standard experiments. Use 0 to indicate not applicable in this context.,0.4,1,"This is a 2-player snowdrift game at each interaction with fixed actions C, D, P rather than contribution amounts; the all-or-nothing vs. continuous contribution distinction for public goods does not apply, so coded as 1 (players choose a pure action).",0.4,N/A,"No endowment or contribution stage; agents just choose actions C, D, or P in a 2×2/3×3 matrix game.",1.0,N/R,"Snowdrift is a 2-player matrix game, not a linear public goods game with a defined MPCR.",1.0,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely simulated agents.,1.0,0,Agents observe only payoffs from sampled interactions; there is no interface with summaries as in lab experiments.,0.8,N/A,Punishment is implemented as payoff parameters β and α in pairwise encounters; no notion of identity revelation in a subject pool.,1.0,N/A,"There is no reward mechanism, only costly punishment.",1.0,0,Agents do not have meta-information about total time steps; it is a dynamical system not an experimental interface.,0.8,1,Model explicitly introduces a punisher type P who reduces D’s payoff by β at personal cost α.,1.0,0.1,Simulation parameter α is set to 0.1 in the main results (Figs. 1–3).,1.0,0.5,Simulation parameter β is set to 0.5 in the main results (Figs. 1–3).,1.0,0,No rewarding; only punishment is modeled.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/R,"Snowdrift payoffs are normalized (R=1, P=0, S=1−r, T=1+r); no explicit per-round endowment is defined.",0.6,N/R,"Outcome variables reported are frequencies of strategy types fC, fD, fP, not contributions or contribution rates.",1.0,N/R,"No notion of contribution amount; agents choose among discrete strategies C, D, P.",1.0,N/R,They do not compute overall efficiency as payoff relative to a maximum benchmark; only show type frequencies.,0.9,N/R,Average fitness/payoff is defined but not tabulated as a primary outcome; only indirectly appears in replicator equations.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents only.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Study is a computational evolutionary game model with analytical treatment; no empirical environment.,1.0,"Two-player evolutionary snowdrift game in a well-mixed infinite-like population with three strategies: Cooperate (C), Defect (D), and Punish (P). Payoffs normalized with R=1, P=0, S=1−r, T=1+r, and when P meets D, P pays cost α and D loses β (α<β). At each microscopic time step, a randomly chosen agent i and a reference agent j each play one random opponent, obtain payoffs Vi and Vj, and i adopts j’s strategy with probability w=(Vj−Vi)/(1+r) if Vj>Vi. Time is measured in evolutionary time steps where on average each of the N agents has one updating opportunity. Initial strategy frequencies are fC=fD=fP=1/3. The main reported outcomes are steady-state frequencies of each strategy and their time evolution for varying r.","Summarizes model rules, payoff structure, updating algorithm, and main observables as described in Sections 2 and 3.",1.0
10.1016_j.physa.2010.12.044.md,"Analytical condition – replicator dynamics with punishers (generic r, α, β; example α=0.2, β=0.8, r=0.2 phase portrait)","Section 4 analyzes the same three-strategy game via replicator dynamics; a specific parameter set α=0.2, β=0.8, r=0.2 is examined in detail in Fig. 4 for phase portraits and invasion analysis.",0.9,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r=0.2 with punishment parameters α=0.2, β=0.8 in replicator dynamics; varying initial frequencies (fC,fD,fP) over the simplex","Fig. 4 and subsequent text explicitly consider r=0.2, α=0.2, β=0.8 while scanning initial distributions across the triangular phase space.",0.95,False,"Replicator dynamics are analytical equations over frequencies, not based on empirical subjects.",1.0,False,"This is a theoretical dynamical system analysis, not an experiment.",1.0,False,No lab or field implementation; purely analytical.,1.0,False,"Although equations can be iterated numerically, Section 4 is framed as analytical replicator dynamics with fixed points and Jacobian analysis.",0.7,True,"Derives differential equations, fixed points, and stability via Jacobian eigenvalues and phase portraits.",1.0,0,Replicator dynamics describe infinite-population frequency limits; no finite N explicitly used.,0.9,0,Continuous-time differential equations; no discrete rounds.,1.0,1,"Same underlying snowdrift game with discrete strategies C, D, P; no graded contributions.",0.9,N/A,"Matrix game with strategy frequencies, not a contribution game.",1.0,N/R,Not a public goods game; no MPCR concept defined.,1.0,N/A,No agents or communication interface in analytical model.,1.0,0,Not applicable in analytical frequency dynamics; no experimental information display.,1.0,N/A,Punishment is encoded in payoff matrix; no concept of identity information.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1.0,0,Continuous-time model; no round count.,1.0,1,Replicator dynamics derived from the same payoff matrix including P strategy with parameters α and β.,1.0,0.2,In the phase portrait example (Fig. 4) they fix α=0.2.,1.0,0.8,In the same example they fix β=0.8.,1.0,0,Only costly punishment is modeled.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/R,"Payoffs normalized as R=1, P=0, S=1−r, T=1+r; no explicit endowment.",0.6,N/R,"Outcomes are equilibrium frequencies and fixed-point structure, not contribution rates.",1.0,N/R,No contribution amounts; discrete strategies only.,1.0,N/R,"Analysis focuses on stability and invasion thresholds, not efficiency metrics.",0.9,N/R,Average fitness is defined symbolically but not given as a numeric reported outcome for this specific condition.,0.7,N/A,No human participants in analytical model.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Purely analytical treatment without empirical implementation.,1.0,"Replicator dynamics for three-strategy snowdrift game with punishment in a well-mixed infinite population. Strategies: C, D, P. Payoff matrix normalized with R=1, P=0, S=1−r, T=1+r, and when P meets D, D’s payoff is reduced by β and P pays cost α (with α<β). Fitnesses are linear in current frequencies: FC=fC·1+fD·(1−r)+fP·1; FD=fC·(1+r)+fD·0+fP·(1+r−β); FP=fC·1+fD·(1−r−α)+fP·1. Dynamics follow df_i/dt = f_i( Fi − F̄ ), where F̄ is average fitness. For r=0.2, α=0.2, β=0.8, the phase portrait on the simplex of (fC,fD,fP) reveals a line of fixed points on the C–P edge, with a stable segment defined by fP > r/β, and a mixed C–D fixed point at (fC,fD,fP)=(1−r,r,0)=(0.8,0.2,0). Stability is analyzed via a 2D reduction and Jacobian eigenvalues, leading to invasion conditions for D against a C–P population.","Summarizes the analytical model structure, equations, parameterization, and main fixed-point findings for the highlighted parameter set.",1.0
10.1016_j.jebo.2014.11.004.md,"Baseline (exogenous buyer group, no communication)","Baseline treatment as labeled in Table 1; exogenous buyer group, no buyer-group entry or exclusion decisions, no communication.",1,Buyer group structure: exogenous (no entry/exclusion decision); Communication: NoTalk,Treatment factors described in design: Baseline vs Open vs Closed buyer group and Talk vs NoTalk.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human student subjects described in Procedures.,1,True,Controlled laboratory Cournot market with randomized assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the laboratory of the Duesseldorf Institute for Competition Economics (DICE).,1,False,Only human-subject lab experiments reported; no agent-based or numerical simulations.,1,False,"While the paper has a theoretical model, this record refers to the experimental treatment with human subjects.",1,3,Three-firm Cournot markets: “three-firm Cournot framework” and instructions say each firm with two others.,1,21,"After 7 initial periods, at least 20 more with random stopping 2/3; actual number 21–28. Asked to code a single value, so use minimum realized 21.",0.4,1,"Cournot quantity choice on [0,50]; subjects can choose any quantity in a continuous/discrete range, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Not a public goods or contribution game; no endowment allocation between private/public accounts.,1,N/A,"Cournot oligopoly, not a linear public good; MPCR not defined.",1,0,Table 1 shows communication 'No' for Baseline.,1,1,Feedback each period on quantities and profits of all firms is described; that is a summary of others’ actions and payoffs.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,0,"Random stopping rule from period 21 onward; actual number of periods not determined ex ante, so subjects could not know total remaining rounds.",0.8,0,No exclusion or other punishment option in Baseline; only quantity choice.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No costly rewarding option mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Subjects choose quantities; costs deducted from revenue. No fixed token endowment per period is specified.,0.2,N/R,"Outcome is quantity/output, not a contribution to a public good; no normalized contribution rate reported.",1,30,"Static Nash equilibrium output per firm is 30 (Table 2); Baseline average outputs are stated to be close to 30 but exact mean not reported. Use Nash benchmark as representative, noting it is approximate.",0.4,N/R,No explicit welfare efficiency ratios vs. maximum reported by treatment.,0.1,N/R,Profits and welfare discussed qualitatively; no numerical average payoffs per group by treatment reported.,0.1,Germany,Experiments conducted at University of Duesseldorf (Germany).,1,N/R,Only described as students from various departments; no age statistics.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,University students (various departments),Procedures specify 'Participants were students from various departments'.,1,On site,Computerized lab sessions using z-Tree at DICE laboratory.,1,"Three-player repeated Cournot quantity competition with linear inverse demand p = max{130 − Q, 0}. Constant marginal cost c = 10 for all firms (low cost). Actions are quantities in range [0,50]. Fixed matching of triads. At least 27 participants (9 triopolies). First 7 rounds standard Baseline; same structure continues thereafter.","Model and parameter description in Design and Model sections, plus procedures.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2014.11.004.md,"Open_NoTalk (open buyer group, no communication)",Treatment labeled 'Open_NoTalk' in Table 1.,1,"Buyer group structure: open buyer group (entry decision, no exclusion); Communication: NoTalk",Open buyer group vs closed vs baseline plus Talk vs NoTalk as treatment factors.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled experimental Cournot markets with treatment manipulation.,1,True,Run in DICE laboratory.,1,False,No computer-only simulations.,1,False,"This record refers to experimental treatment, not theoretical model.",1,3,Three-firm Cournot markets.,1,21,Seven initial periods plus at least 20 with random stopping (21–28 actual). Use minimum realized periods.,0.4,1,"Quantity choice on [0,50], not binary.",1,0,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,"Cournot game, no MPCR.",1,0,Table 1 indicates 'Communication: No' for Open_NoTalk.,1,1,"Subjects informed each period about buyer group participation decisions, quantities, and profits of firms.",0.9,0,No punishment or exclusion option in open buyer group (only entry).,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Random stopping with 2/3 continuation probability; total number of periods not known to subjects.,0.8,0,Open buyer group has entry but no exclusion or costly punishment decisions; punishment only via market quantities.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action.,1,0,No rewarding option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"No fixed per-period token endowment is specified, only quantity choice and derived profits.",0.2,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,30,Static Nash output per firm with low cost is 30 (Table 2). Text says Baseline and Open_NoTalk outputs are close to 30 and not significantly different; use 30 as representative.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio per treatment reported.,0.1,N/R,No numerical mean payoffs by treatment.,0.1,Germany,Same subject pool and lab as other treatments at University of Duesseldorf.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,University students (various departments),Stated for whole experiment.,1,On site,Lab sessions using z-Tree.,1,"Three-player repeated Cournot with linear demand p = max{130 − Q, 0}. Marginal cost is 10 for members of buyer group; 22 for any non-joining firm. Stage structure: Stage 1 entry decision into open buyer group; if at least 2 of 3 join, joiners have low cost and any outsider has high cost; if fewer, all have high cost. Then quantity choice. Almost all firms join so low costs virtually all the time.",Design section and Fig. 1 description of low-cost frequency.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2014.11.004.md,"Closed_NoTalk (closed buyer group with exclusion, no communication)",Treatment labeled 'Closed_NoTalk' in Table 1.,1,Buyer group structure: closed buyer group (entry + exclusion); Communication: NoTalk,Closed vs open buyer group and Talk vs NoTalk are experimental factors.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab Cournot game with treatment manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in DICE laboratory.,1,False,"Data from human subjects, not purely simulated agents.",1,False,Record pertains to experimental treatment.,1,3,Three-firm Cournot markets.,1,21,Seven initial periods plus at least 20; actual 21–28; use minimum realized.,0.4,1,"Quantity choice on [0,50].",1,0,Not a public goods setting.,1,N/A,Cournot oligopoly.,1,0,Table 1 lists communication as 'No' for Closed_NoTalk.,1,1,"Subjects get feedback on participation, exclusion, quantities, and profits each period.",0.9,1,"Exclusion messages identify which firm (e.g., 'firm 3 was excluded', 'firm 1 and 2 are still in the buyer group'); thus punished firm’s identity is revealed.",1,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,0,Random stopping rule; total number of remaining rounds not shown.,0.8,1,Closed buyer group has exclusion of a single firm as a targeted punishment for deviators.,1,N/R,Monetary cost per unit of exclusion is not parameterized as a simple 'cost per punishment point'; punishment is implemented via change in cost parameters.,0.2,N/R,Effect of exclusion is to change deviator’s marginal cost from 10 to 22 and others’ to remain at 10; no simple linear 'points reduced per coin' rate given.,0.4,0,No separate reward mechanic.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game framed in terms of costs and revenues; no fixed per-period token endowment is given.,0.2,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,28.5,Figure 2 and text indicate Closed_NoTalk average outputs lower than Baseline/Open_NoTalk (≈30) but above collusive 20. Visual inspection suggests around 28–29; record approximate midpoint 28.5 as representative.,0.3,N/R,No numerical welfare ratios reported by treatment.,0.1,N/R,No average profits by treatment reported numerically.,0.1,Germany,Same lab and subject pool.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,University students (various departments),Described for all treatments.,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab using z-Tree.,1,"Three-player repeated Cournot with linear demand p = max{130 − Q, 0}. Stage 1: entry into buyer group. Stage 2 (Closed only): if all three joined, firms simultaneously suggest one firm for exclusion; if two firms agree on same target, that firm is excluded and has high cost (c = 22) while others have low cost (c = 10). Stage 3: quantity choice. In this treatment, procedurally the first 7 rounds are Open buyer group without exclusion, then the Closed mechanism is introduced.",Design section and Table 1 plus description of Closed treatments and initial 7 Open rounds.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2014.11.004.md,"Open_Talk (open buyer group with communication, no exclusion)",Treatment labeled 'Open_Talk' in Table 1.,1,"Buyer group structure: open buyer group (entry decision, no exclusion); Communication: Talk (40s typed group chat each period before quantity choice, at buyer-group stage only)",Open buyer group plus communication enabled as described in communication treatments section.,1,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted in DICE lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulations only.,1,False,Refers to experimental condition.,1,3,Three-firm Cournot markets.,1,21,Seven initial periods plus at least 20; random stopping; use minimum realized.,0.4,1,"Continuous/discrete choice of quantity in [0,50].",1,0,Not a contribution/endowment allocation game.,1,N/A,Not a public good.,1,1,Communication 'Yes' for Open_Talk; 40 s typed messages each period.,1,1,Feedback each period on buyer group participation and on quantities and profits.,0.9,0,No exclusion/punishment mechanism in open buyer group.,1,0,No reward option.,1,0,Random stopping implies unknown remaining rounds.,0.8,0,Open buyer group allows joining/ not joining but no explicit punishment choice.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No explicit periodic endowment mentioned.,0.2,N/R,"Outcome is output, not contributions.",1,25,Figure 2 and text: Open_Talk output lower than Open_NoTalk (~30) but higher than Closed_Talk (~20); visual inspection suggests mid-20s; record approximate 25.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures reported numerically by treatment.,0.1,N/R,No numeric mean payoffs by treatment provided.,0.1,Germany,Same lab and pool.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0.1,N/R,Not specified.,0.1,University students (various departments),Given for entire experiment.,1,On site,Conducted in physical computer lab.,1,"Three-player repeated Cournot with open buyer group entry stage and 40-second group chat each period at the buyer-group stage. Chat is via instant messenger; subjects can send multiple typed messages to all in group; they are instructed not to explicitly agree on output targets, but qualitative and quantitative agreements nevertheless occur. If at least 2 join, joiners have low cost c=10 and any outsider c=22; if fewer, all c=22. Then quantity choice.",Communication treatments description and model design.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2014.11.004.md,Closed_Talk (closed buyer group with exclusion and communication),Treatment labeled 'Closed_Talk' in Table 1.,1,Buyer group structure: closed buyer group (entry + exclusion); Communication: Talk (40s typed group chat each period at buyer-group stage),Closed buyer group with communication as per 2x2 design and Table 1.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory study with random assignment to conditions.,1,True,Conducted at DICE laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation-only results for this condition.,1,False,"This entry concerns experimental treatment, not model.",1,3,Three firms per market.,1,21,Seven initial rounds plus at least 20; 21–28 realized; use minimum.,0.4,1,"Quantity choice in [0,50], not binary.",1,0,No public-good contribution structure.,1,N/A,Not a linear public goods game.,1,1,"Communication 'Yes' for Closed_Talk, 40s instant-messenger group chat at buyer-group stage.",1,1,"Subjects informed about buyer-group decisions, exclusion, quantities, and profits each period.",0.9,1,"Messages after exclusion identify which firm is excluded, so punishment targets are known.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Random stopping means total number of periods unknown to participants.,0.8,1,Closed buyer group allows exclusion of a specific firm as punishment.,1,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost to punisher specified; punishment implemented via change in cost parameters and requires coordination of two firms.,0.3,N/R,Effect of punishment is to switch deviator’s cost to c=22 while others stay at c=10; no linear 'damage per point spent' parameter is provided.,0.4,0,No explicit reward action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No fixed token endowment is described.,0.2,N/R,Not a contribution framework.,1,20,"Joint-profit maximizing (collusive) quantity per firm is 20 (Table 2). Text notes most groups in Closed_Talk had averages close to 20, and panel regressions show strong output reduction; use 20 as representative.",0.5,N/R,Welfare comparisons discussed qualitatively; no numeric efficiency measure for Closed_Talk.,0.1,N/R,No mean payoffs by treatment presented numerically.,0.1,Germany,Same experimental lab and recruitment.,1,N/R,Not stated.,0.1,N/R,Not stated.,0.1,University students (various departments),As for whole experiment.,1,On site,In-person lab sessions using z-Tree.,1,"Three-player repeated Cournot game with closed buyer group mechanism and 40-second instant-messenger chat at buyer-group stage. Stage 1: entry decision; Stage 2: if all three joined, each suggests a firm (if any) for exclusion. If two firms suggest the same firm, that firm is excluded and faces high cost c=22 while others keep c=10. Stage 3: quantity decision; then feedback on participation, exclusion, quantities, and profits. First 7 rounds implemented as Open buyer group before Closed mechanism introduced. Exclusion is rare under Talk but when it occurs, high-output deviators are typically targeted. Communication often includes explicit quantitative agreements on outputs, which are highly collusive.","Design, Section 6.2 and 6.3, and Table 4–6 descriptions.",1
