 between-group transfers, but weather shocks are varied in frequency (f_shock from 0 to 0.2) and mean size (μ_w). Each run lasts 10,000 periods and results are averaged over the last 5000 periods across 10 seeds. Dependent variables: long-run average frequency of resource-conserving individuals and number of group collapses. Higher frequency and magnitude of negative shocks increase collapse probability of defector-heavy groups and thereby increase selection for conservation.",Captures the experimental manipulation and observed qualitative outcomes described in Section 4.1.1 and Figs. 4–5.,0.9
10.1016_j.jtbi.2013.01.004.md,"Policy simulations varying defector effort, extraction cost, and penalty (baseline interaction structure)","Section 4.2 studies effects of changing high-effort level e_h, extraction cost w, and penalty on defectors d (with d = c) on conservation and collapse; conducted within the baseline model without added between-group mechanisms.",0.9,Between-group interaction type = none; high-effort level of defectors e_h varied from 0.002 to 0.009; extraction cost w varied from 0.1 to 0.9; penalty on defectors d (and punishment cost c) varied from 0.1 to 0.9; other parameters as in baseline (n = 20; N = 25; K = 70; e_l = 0.001; f_migration = 0.01; f_shock = 0.1; μ_w = 5; σ_w = 5; μ = 0.001).,"Ranges for e_h, w, and d are provided in Table 1 and Section 4.2; policy analysis does not introduce conflict or sharing, so interpreted as baseline between-group structure.",0.88,False,Purely computational parameter-sweep simulations.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Policy effects evaluated within the agent-based model.,1,False,"While some derivatives are given analytically, the main comparative results (Fig. 7) are simulation-based.",0.7,20,Baseline group size used when exploring policy parameters.,0.9,10000,Consistent run length for simulations.,0.98,1,Same effort-choice structure.,0.7,N/A,No human endowment game.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,No human-level punishment decisions.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,1,Punishment and penalties are core levers being varied.,0.98,N/R,"In this condition, cost c is varied (c = d) from 0.1 to 0.9 alongside penalty; no single fixed value applies.",0.9,N/R,Exact mapping of cost to penalty on defectors is embedded in payoff equations; no numeric multiplier independently reported.,0.4,0,"Policy analysis concerns penalties and extraction costs, not rewards.",0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Same as baseline.,0.7,N/R,Main DVs are long-run average frequency of conserving individuals and frequency of group collapse as functions of policy parameters.,0.7,N/R,No explicit per-agent harvest outcomes are tabulated.,0.6,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure.,0.7,N/R,Not reported numerically.,0.6,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,All manipulations are within the simulation.,1,"Within the baseline multi-group harvesting model, three key parameters are varied: (1) defector effort e_h (loosening or tightening limits on high effort), (2) extraction cost w (interpretable as a tax on resource extraction), and (3) penalty on defectors d, with d = c (cost of punishment). For each parameter, long-run average frequency of resource-conserving individuals and probability/frequency of resource exhaustion are computed (averaged like in earlier sections). Higher e_h intensifies within-group selection for defectors but increases collapse risk of defector-heavy groups, ultimately promoting conservation; increasing w or d reduces payoff disadvantage of conservation, decreases collapse probability, and increases conservation frequency.","Summarizes Section 4.2 and Fig. 7, focusing on what is varied and the qualitative outcome patterns.",0.92
