10.1016_j.jebo.2020.10.005.md,Herrmann et al. (2008) public good game with punishment – reanalysis via finite mixture model,"Paper explicitly states it exploits the data of Herrmann et al. (2008) and focuses on the repeated four-person public good game with punishment; all 16 subject pools are pooled in one econometric analysis, not separated by treatment.",1,Latent punishment type (Type N: never punish; Type AF: antisocial and free rider punishment; Type F: free rider punishment only) inferred from punishment behavior; cultural area/subject pool used as covariates and for descriptive comparisons but not as experimental treatments in this paper.,The study does not manipulate new experimental conditions; instead it statistically classifies subjects from an existing single treatment (PGG with punishment) into latent types and studies their distribution across cultures.,0.79,True,"Uses behavior of 1,120 human subjects from lab experiments reported in Herrmann et al. (2008).",1,True,Underlying data come from controlled public good game experiments with and without punishment.,1,True,Subjects are university students in 16 subject pools; these are standard laboratory public goods experiments.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only statistical modeling of human data.,1,False,"Finite mixture model is an econometric tool applied to data, not a standalone analytical game-theoretic model.",1,4,“Both games are played over ten periods in groups of four and a partner matching.”,1,10,Same sentence: games are played over ten periods.,1,1,"Standard Herrmann et al. (2008) design and wording (“decides how many tokens to spend” and ‘contributed to the public good’) imply continuous choice from 0 to 20, not all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,"Endowment of 20 tokens is given to subjects; they choose how many to contribute. By convention, default allocation is entirely private (0 in the public good) until they decide to move tokens.",0.85,0.4,"“Subjects are endowed with 20 tokens and the marginal per capita return is 0.4, i.e., each token contributed … yields a return of 0.4 tokens for every member of the group.”",1,0,"No mention of communication, and Herrmann et al. (2008) is known to be a no-communication PGG with punishment; communication would be a major treatment and would be reported.",0.9,1,"Punishment requires observing “all group members’ contributions in a given round”; standard design also shows resulting payoffs/punishments. While not fully detailed here, ability to target punishment implies feedback on others’ behavior and outcomes.",0.7,0,"Herrmann et al. (2008) uses anonymous, partner-matched groups without identity revelation of who punished whom; nothing in this paper suggests otherwise.",0.8,0,"No reward mechanism exists in this design, so no reward identities can be shown.",1,1,"They explicitly analyze “final period” effects, implying subjects knew the game length; PGG experiments with fixed 10 periods almost always inform subjects about the horizon.",0.85,1,Core of the study is “public good game with punishment”; subjects have “access to a punishment technology”.,1,1,"“Every token spent on punishment reduces the peer's payoff by three tokens, while costing one unit to the punisher.” Cost per punishment token is 1.",1,3,Same sentence: each punishment token reduces the target’s payoff by three tokens.,1,0,Only costly punishment is available; no mention of any positive reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this design.,1,20,“Subjects are endowed with 20 tokens …”,1,N/R,This paper focuses on punishment behavior and earnings; it does not report normalized contribution rates (fraction of endowment) numerically.,0.1,N/R,No explicit average contribution levels are reported in tokens; only qualitative statements and use of ‘cooperativeness’ from the no-punishment game as a regressor.,0.1,N/R,"They report mean earnings (e.g., 8.00 vs 25.51 tokens) but do not normalize by the theoretical maximum payoff to provide an efficiency ratio.",0.1,N/R,"They discuss average earnings per subject and regression of average earnings per group-period, but do not provide a single summary statistic for group payoff for the entire sample or by condition.",0.1,"USA, UK, Australia, Denmark, Germany, Switzerland, Belarus, Ukraine, Russia, Greece, Turkey, Saudi Arabia, Oman, South Korea, China",Table 1 lists 16 subject pools with their countries; all are included in the reanalyzed dataset.,1,N/R,"Paper notes that age is available in the underlying data and used in auxiliary regressions (Appendix A.6), but no age distribution or mean is reported here.",0.4,Mixed; proportion not reported,"Gender is included as a regressor in Appendix A.6 and is discussed (women slightly more likely to be Type AF), but no exact gender composition is provided.",0.6,University students,“In all locations subject pools are convenience samples (university students).”,1,On site,Herrmann et al. (2008) are standard in-person lab experiments conducted at universities; no indication of online implementation.,0.95,"Partner-matched repeated linear public good game with 4 players and 10 periods; endowment 20 tokens per period; each contributed token yields 0.4 tokens to each of 4 players (group return 1.6); punishment stage after contribution where each subject can assign integer punishment points (tokens) to each of 3 peers at cost 1 and effect 3; subjects first play a 10-period PGG without punishment, then a 10-period PGG with punishment; information includes all group members’ contributions each period; punishment and contribution decisions are censored at zero with very few at upper bound.","Summarized from Section 2.1 and methodological descriptions; some details (integer punishment tokens, standard linear PGG structure) inferred from the well-known Herrmann et al. (2008) protocol though not fully reprinted here.",0.85
10.18564_jasss.1568.md,"Simulation – No Punishment (NP), baseline public good game",Condition explicitly named in section 2.5 as 'No Punishment (NP)'.,1,Punishment regime: No Punishment (NP); Contribution cost C=1; Punishment efficiency E=0; K/Y not applicable (no punishment).,NP defined as society type with no punishment (E=0); C=1 stated for simulations underlying Figure 2 and 3; K and Y only defined for punishment conditions.,0.8,False,"All studies are agent-based computer simulations with virtual agents, not human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a simulation study.,1,False,No physical lab with human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Described as 'agent based computer simulations' using the Sociodynamica program.,1,False,"While equations are presented, main results come from numerical agent-based simulations.",1,50,Section 2.1: 'virtual population of 50 hunter-gatherers'; this is the default and only concrete population size used in described runs.,0.9,200,Figures 1–3 specify averages from simulations run to time step 200; NP is one of those three societies.,0.8,1,Agents can choose to contribute a proportion of their accumulated wealth (Table 1: 'Contribution. Was paid as a proportion of the wealth accumulated').,0.9,N/A,No human subjects; endowment allocation interface not applicable.,1,N/R,Text mentions a multiplication factor and alpha=1 but does not give the exact multiplier or group size used to compute MPCR.,0.2,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,0,No user interface; agents follow programmed rules and there is no description of summary feedback displays.,0.9,N/A,No identities displayed to human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism described separate from public good payoffs.,1,0,No display to agents or humans; number of time steps is a parameter of the simulation only.,1,0,NP defined as 'with no monitoring and punishment'.,1,N/A,No punishment in NP.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism described; only standard public good returns.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,3,Each individual collected 3 food units per time step (section 2.3).,1,N/R,"Figures report 'percentage of contributors' at population level, not average fraction of endowment contributed.",0.3,N/R,"Output summarized as percentage of contributors, not as mean number of units contributed.",0.3,N/R,No explicit ratio of actual to maximum group payoff reported.,0.1,N/R,Results figures summarize percentage of contributors only.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly computer simulations with virtual agents.,1,"Agent-based public good game with 50 agents, each getting 3 food units per time step, able to contribute part of accumulated wealth to a common pile; alpha (social synergy) fixed at 1 so that cooperation does not yield extra synergy; fitness via accumulated food and reproduction with mutation; no punishment or monitoring in this condition.",Summarized from sections 2.1–2.4 and definition of NP in 2.5.,0.9
10.18564_jasss.1568.md,"Simulation – Altruistic Punishment (AP), generic regime (Figure 1)",AP is defined in section 2.5 and compared with NP and CP in Figure 1 over different K/Y ratios.,1,Punishment regime: Altruistic Punishment (AP); K/Y ratio varied across values used in Figure 1; punishment efficiency E=60%; contribution cost C=1.,"Figure 1 caption states comparison of three societies over different K/Y ratios with E=60%; C=1 is specified for similar simulations in Figure 2 and 3, and Table 2; assumed same here.",0.7,False,Agent-based computer simulations only.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment.,1,False,Pure simulation.,1,True,"All conditions are implemented in Sociodynamica, an agent-based simulation.",1,False,"Results presented are numerical, not closed-form solutions.",1,50,Default population size described as 50; larger populations 'produced similar results' but not tied to the main figures.,0.9,200,"Figure 1: results are averages from simulations; Figures 2 and 3 specify 200 time steps, and text states similar procedure.",0.7,1,"Contribution C is 'paid as a proportion of wealth', indicating continuous choice rather than binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,No human interface for default contributions.,1,N/R,Multiplier and effective group size for the public good are not reported numerically.,0.2,N/A,No communication among human participants.,1,0,No GUI; agents do not view earnings summaries; rules are internal.,0.9,N/A,No human identities used.,1,N/A,No explicit reward beyond the shared public good.,1,0,Agents are not informed of total time steps.,1,1,AP condition defined by presence of altruistic punishment.,1,1,Table 1: cost to punisher Y equals contribution C; C=1 used in these simulations; so Y=1. In AP punishers personally bear this cost.,0.8,2,"Section 2.5: 'Punishment involves a detraction of food units twice the value of the withheld contribution'; withheld contribution is 1 unit, so cost to punished K=2 per unit of punishment.",0.8,0,No separate rewarding of contributors described; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,3,Each agent collects 3 food units per time step.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as percentage of contributors, not average fraction of wealth contributed.",0.3,N/R,No explicit mean contribution amounts given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff/max payoff) reported.,0.1,N/R,"Results are summarized as percentages of contributors, not payoffs.",0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations only.,1,"In Altruistic Punishment, individuals monitor encounters and can punish free-riders they meet. Punishment removes twice the withheld contribution (2 units) from the free-rider and imposes an equal cost on the punisher. Punishment efficiency E=60% here means 60% of free-riders are successfully punished in a period. Gene frequencies of contributor vs free-rider strategies evolve via reproduction and mutation over 200 time steps.","Summarized from AP definition in 2.5, Table 1, and description of evolutionary dynamics in 2.1–2.4 and Figure 1 caption.",0.9
10.18564_jasss.1568.md,"Simulation – Cooperative Punishment (CP), generic regime (Figure 1)",CP defined in 2.5 as 'Cooperative punishment (CP)' and compared versus NP and AP in Figure 1.,1,Punishment regime: Cooperative Punishment (CP); K/Y ratio varied across values used in Figure 1; punishment efficiency E=60%; contribution cost C=1.,Figure 1: three societies with varying K/Y and E=60%; CP is one; C=1 applies across these standard simulations.,0.7,False,Simulation with virtual agents only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment with human subjects.,1,True,Agent-based simulations in Sociodynamica.,1,False,Main results are simulated.,1,50,Default simulation population described as 50 agents.,0.9,200,Figure 1 uses same protocol as Figures 2 and 3 which specify 200 time steps.,0.7,1,Contribution C defined as proportion of wealth.,0.9,N/A,No human default choice.,1,N/R,Exact MPCR not numerically specified.,0.2,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No user-visible summaries in a GUI sense.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects whose identities could be revealed.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,0,Agents are not aware of the finite horizon.,1,1,CP is a punishment regime.,1,1,Table 1: Y=C and C=1; CP uses same Y as AP but CP shifts payment source to the common pile.,0.8,2,Section 2.5: 'Punishers and punished individuals paid the same cost as in AP'; in AP punishment detracts twice the withheld contribution (2 units).,0.8,0,Only punishment is modeled distinctly.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,3,Each agent collects 3 units per season/time step.,1,N/R,"Results report percentage of contributors in population, not normalized contribution size.",0.3,N/R,No explicit contribution amount statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,0.1,N/R,Only percentage of contributors is graphed.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Cooperative Punishment operates like AP in terms of harm to free-riders and total punishment cost, but the cost of punishment is paid from the common pile and shared among all group members. This socialization of punishment cost is intended to model co-operative punishment or mobbing. Punishment efficiency in Figure 1 simulations is E=60%, meaning a fixed fraction of free-riders is punished each period.",Summarized from CP description in 2.5 and Figure 1 caption.,0.9
