a, b.",1,N/A,"No human participants or experimental interface; communication is modeled abstractly as costless signals, not as chat between subjects.",1,0,Not an experimental design; no concept of participants seeing payoff summaries exists in the analytical model.,1,N/A,"No explicit punishment action; only withdrawal of help via standing, so no identity revelation mechanism in an experiment.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,No experimental interface; agents do not observe a fixed horizon.,1,1,Punishment is modeled implicitly as withdrawal of future help from individuals in bad standing; this is a sanctioning mechanism though not a token-based punishment stage.,0.8,N/R,Cost of punishing (withdrawing help) is not parameterized separately from the general cost of helping c.,0.4,N/R,"No per-unit punishment technology (e.g., tokens to reduce payoff) is specified; instead, losing standing affects future help.",0.4,0,No explicit extra reward mechanism; only help vs no help.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model is in fitness payoffs (1, 1−c, etc.); no explicit token endowment per period.",0.5,N/R,"Theoretical model analyzes stability of honest signaling, not contribution rates in a contribution game.",1,N/R,"No numeric contribution amount is defined, only binary help vs no help decisions.",1,N/R,Paper discusses evolutionary stability conditions rather than reporting normalized efficiency values.,1,N/R,No single aggregate group payoff per condition is reported; only relative fitness expressions.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model is purely theoretical with no empirical implementation.,1,"Repeated two-player Sir Philip Sidney signaling game: one signaler and one receiver alternate roles across periods with continuation probability w; signalers are deserving with probability p; receivers choose to help (cost c) or not, affecting signaler’s fitness via parameters a and b; signalers can be Honest (signal only when deserving) or Dishonest (always signal); receivers can Respond (help based on standing) or Never Respond; standing is updated based on detected lies and helping behavior; lie detection modeled via signal detection theory with probabilities e and ε derived from threshold d and difficulty M.",Summarizes the formal game structure and strategy sets as specified in Section 1.,1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2015.06.002.md,Community-level third-party monitoring and punishment (n > 1),Extended model in Section 2 adds third-party monitoring and punishment by a community of n individuals who aggregate evidence and enforce norms.,0.95,"Number of monitors n (community size), affecting aggregated cue variance and hence lie detection probability e and false accusation probability ε; other parameters as in base model (w, p, a, b, M, d). Condition here is third-party monitoring with n > 1 and normative sanctions.","Section 2 explicitly introduces n community members monitoring and punishing, and analyzes how varying n changes TL/TH via aggregated signals.",0.95,False,Still a theoretical extension; no human data are introduced.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; just extended analytical model and plots (Fig. 3).,1,False,No experimental setting.,1,False,The description is analytical (closed-form expressions for e and ε as functions of n with normal distributions); figures visualize these expressions.,0.7,True,"Model extends earlier analytical framework: defines e = 1 − F_n(d|lie), ε = 1 − F_n(d|truth) with variance 1/n, and computes resulting TL/TH; no discrete simulation is detailed.",1,2,Focal interaction each period is still between one signaler and one receiver; third parties observe but do not directly choose a help action in that focal dyad.,0.8,0,"As in the base model, interactions are repeated with continuation probability w; there is no fixed number of rounds.",0.6,1,Receiver’s action remains binary help vs no help; no graded contributions.,0.9,N/A,Not a contribution/public goods game.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,No experimental chat between human subjects; third-party communication is modeled abstractly as information pooling.,1,0,"No experimental interface; while individuals have information about others’ honesty, there is no design element like a displayed summary.",1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as community withdrawal of future help; there is no experimental display of punisher identity.,1,N/A,No explicit separate reward mechanism exists.,1,0,No concept of subjects being told the number of rounds.,1,1,"Section 2: third-party monitoring and punishment; individuals who do not participate in monitoring are punished by withdrawal of future help, and liars judged by consensus lose standing, leading to community-wide withholding of help.",1,N/R,"Costs for monitoring and punishing are conceptually invoked (e.g., 'individuals who don't participate are punished') but not parameterized numerically.",0.5,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment investment to payoff reduction is specified beyond loss of future help via bad standing.,0.5,0,"No distinct positive reward instrument is introduced; benefits come from receiving help, not from deliberate rewarding actions.",1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"As with the base model, payoffs are in fitness terms; there is no explicit per-period token endowment.",0.5,N/R,"The outcome measure is whether honest signaling can be evolutionarily stable (via TL/TH and benefit ratio), not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution variable.,1,N/R,Paper analyzes stability ranges; does not present normalized efficiency metrics.,1,N/R,No single aggregate group payoff is reported for this condition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The extended model is still entirely theoretical.,1,"Extension of the repeated Sir Philip Sidney game to a community of n individuals engaged in pairwise interactions each period. Each community member independently samples a cue about whether a given signal was truthful, from the same normal distributions used in the two-person model. Cues are aggregated (averaged); if the average exceeds threshold d, the signaler is judged a liar by the community and enters bad standing for all members, who then do not believe subsequent signals. Lie detection probability is e = 1 − F_n(d|lie) and false-accusation probability is ε = 1 − F_n(d|truth), where F_n has variance 1/n. Across the n simultaneous interactions in a period, the probability that at least one interaction detects a lie (or falsely accuses) is 1 − (1 − e)^n (or 1 − (1 − ε)^n). Standing and strategies (H/D for signalers; R/N for receivers) follow the same logic as in the base model, but punishment is now effectively community-wide.","Summarizes the specific additional structure introduced in Section 2 (n monitors, aggregation, adjusted e and ε, and community-based sanctions).",1
