10.1073_pnas.2106028118.md,Training condition – No rules,"Paper defines three between-population conditions: no rules, important rule, silly rule; this row captures the no rules condition.",1,Normative classification scheme: no berries are taboo; no marking or reward for punishment.,Condition differs only in which berries are taboo and associated punishment payoffs.,1,False,Study uses artificial agents in simulation only; no human participants.,1,True,Authors systematically manipulate conditions in a controlled simulation environment.,1,False,No physical or online lab with humans; purely computational.,1,True,Multiagent deep reinforcement learning in a Markov game is a computer simulation.,1,False,No closed-form mathematical model; results from numerical training.,1,8,Eight agents are selected to play in each episode from a population of 12.,1,1000,"Each episode consists of 1,000 time steps.",1,1,"Agents do not choose contribution levels; foraging/punishment are continuous actions, not discrete all-or-nothing contributions.",0.4,N/A,There is no public goods contribution structure; only individual foraging and punishment.,1,N/R,No linear public goods stage or multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,Agents are artificial and communicate only via environment mechanics; no explicit chat channel.,1,0,Text describes only local pixel observations; no reward or punishment summaries are displayed.,0.6,0,Agents only see others as colored sprites within a 2D view; no additional identity indicators beyond position/color are described.,0.6,0,No mechanism described that reveals which specific agent was rewarded.,0.6,0,No mention that agents observe episode length or remaining steps; they receive only pixel observations and rewards.,0.6,1,Agents have a punishing beam available in all conditions.,1,20,Using the punishing beam costs the punisher 20 points.,1,35,A successful punishing beam causes the punished agent to lose 35 points.,1,0,"There is no separate prosocial reward mechanism, only punishment and berry rewards.",1,N/A,No reward-giving action is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward-giving action is implemented.,1,N/R,Agents do not have a fixed per-episode endowment; they accumulate rewards via foraging.,1,N/R,No contribution decision to a public good is defined.,1,N/R,No contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,"Paper reports total group reward but not normalized efficiency relative to a theoretical maximum, nor enough detail to compute it.",0.3,N/R,Figures show trajectories qualitatively; exact numerical group payoffs per condition are not tabulated.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation uses only artificial agents in a virtual environment.,1,"Multiagent deep RL foraging task in a 33x12 pixel 2D world; berries give 4 reward; one berry type is poisonous and after 100 steps reduces future berry rewards to 1; berry respawn probabilities high so no resource competition; 12-agent population, 8 sampled per 1000-step episode; discount factor 0.99; independent neural-network policies trained with IMPALA-like actor-learner setup.",Summarizes key environment and training details that shape incentives but are not captured by other fields.,1
10.1073_pnas.2106028118.md,Training condition – Important rule (poisonous berry taboo),One of the three main conditions where only the poisonous berry is taboo.,1,Normative classification scheme: poisonous berry is taboo; agents who eat it are marked; punishing marked agents yields net +15 to punisher.,Condition differs in which berries are taboo and in payoffs for punishing marked agents.,1,False,Artificial agents only.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of environment rule content.,1,False,Not a physical/online human lab.,1,True,Deep RL simulation in a Markov game.,1,False,"Behavior learned numerically, not analytically.",1,8,Eight agents interact per episode.,1,1000,"Episodes last 1,000 time steps.",1,1,No discrete all-or-nothing contribution decision.,0.4,N/A,No public goods allocation.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human communication; only environment signals.,1,0,Agents observe only local pixels; no summary feedback mentioned.,0.6,0,Punishment is via local beam; no explicit identity display beyond position/color.,0.6,0,No mechanism for labeling who got rewarded.,0.6,0,No indication that agents see time remaining.,0.6,1,Punishing beam is available and especially incentivized for marked agents.,1,20,Cost of using the punishing beam is 20 points to punisher in all conditions.,1,35,Punished agent loses 35 points per successful beam.,1,1,"When punishing a marked agent, the punisher receives 35 points, effectively a conditional reward for enforcement.",1,20,Punisher pays 20 points to fire the beam even when receiving 35 from punishing a marked agent.,1,35,Successful punishment of a marked agent yields 35 points to punisher before subtracting the 20-point action cost.,1,N/R,No fixed starting tokens; rewards accrue from actions.,1,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,Only qualitative differences in collective return are shown; maximum possible payoff not specified to compute efficiency.,0.3,N/R,Figure 4F shows group return curves but does not provide exact numeric values.,0.3,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Artificial multiagent RL only.,1,"Same foraging world as no-rules condition (33x12 grid, 24 berry types, 1 poisonous type with 100-step delayed poisoning). Additional mechanic: consuming the poisonous berry marks the agent (color change visible only to others). Marking enables profitable punishment: if a marked player is hit by a punishing beam, punisher gains 35 points while still paying 20-point cost; marking is removed after punishment. Eating non-poisonous berries remains safe except for opportunity costs.","Captures condition-specific mechanics beyond the independent variable label, particularly marking and altered punishment payoffs.",1
10.1073_pnas.2106028118.md,Training condition – Silly rule (poisonous and harmless berries taboo),Third main condition where both the poisonous and an additional harmless berry are taboo.,1,Normative classification scheme: both the poisonous berry and one harmless berry are taboo; consuming either marks the agent; punishing marked agents yields net +15 to punisher.,"Condition extends important rule by adding a second, harmless taboo.",1,False,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,True,Environment’s rule content is exogenously manipulated across simulated populations.,1,False,No human lab setting.,1,True,Multiagent deep RL simulation.,1,False,Not solved analytically.,1,8,Eight agents interact per episode in all training conditions.,1,1000,"Each episode has 1,000 time steps.",1,1,No stylized binary contribution decision; behavior is continuous movement/beam use.,0.4,N/A,No endowment split between private and public accounts.,1,N/R,No MPCR is defined in this foraging task.,1,N/A,No humans; no chat channel.,1,0,Agents see only raw pixels locally; no explicit summary feedback described.,0.6,0,Agents infer interactions via local observations; no explicit identifier for punisher beyond seeing beams and sprites.,0.6,0,No explicit display of which agent obtained rewards.,0.6,0,No mention of time-remaining display; only pixel observations and rewards.,0.6,1,Punishing beam present and incentivized via rewards for hitting marked agents.,1,20,Cost of punishing beam is 20 points in all conditions.,1,35,Punished agent loses 35 points per successful hit.,1,1,"Punisher receives 35 points for punishing a marked agent, interpreted as an enforcement reward.",1,20,Punisher still incurs the 20-point action cost when punishing marked agents.,1,35,Reward magnitude to punisher for hitting a marked agent is 35 points.,1,N/R,No fixed initial token endowment per episode.,1,N/R,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,Only relative collective returns shown; theoretical maximum not specified for normalization.,0.3,N/R,Collective return trajectories are plotted but not provided as exact numeric values per condition.,0.3,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Entirely simulated artificial agents.,1,"Same base foraging world and training setup as in other conditions (12-agent population, 8 per 1000-step episode, 24 berry types, 1 poisonous with 100-step delayed poisoning). In addition to the poisonous berry taboo, one harmless berry type is also designated taboo. Eating either taboo berry marks the agent, visible only to others as a color change. Marked agents, when hit by a punishing beam, cause the punisher to receive 35 points while paying the 20-point cost; marking is removed after punishment. This doubles the number of potential taboo violations, increasing early opportunities to practice punishment.",Specifies how the silly rule extends the important rule by adding an additional harmless taboo with identical punishment mechanics.,1
10.1093_jeea_jvz008.md,Analytical small-group model with and without a specialized enforcer (Section 2),The paper develops a theoretical small-group repeated game model comparing a setting with k+1 producers (no enforcer) to a setting with k producers plus one enforcer and characterizes equilibria; this is the core analytical condition set in Section 2.,1,"Role structure and enforcement regime (all k+1 players are producers with only community enforcement via grim trigger vs k producers plus 1 enforcer with specialized punishment; also varying use of one-time enforcer punishment vs punishment+repentance and parameters of functions f, g, discount factor δ, and group size k).","The main comparative statics and equilibrium characterizations hinge on whether one player is assigned as an enforcer and on which enforcement strategy (grim trigger, one-time enforcer punishment, repentance) is used; these are the dimensions varied conceptually.",0.9,False,The work is purely theoretical with no human participants; it is a repeated game model with functions f and g.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment is run; results are derived analytically.,1,False,No lab or experimental setting is described; it is game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,There is no numerical or agent-based simulation; only analytical derivations and inequalities.,1,True,"The paper defines payoff functions, strategies, and proves propositions and theorems using formal analysis only.",1,0,"In the small-group model there are k+1 players in total, but k is a symbolic parameter and no fixed numeric value is chosen; since no concrete numeric configuration is instantiated, a single numeric playerCount cannot be given.",0.2,0,"The game is infinitely repeated (t = 0,1,2,...), not a finite number of rounds; a numeric count is not applicable.",1,1,"Each producer chooses a cooperation level x_i in R_+, a continuous choice, not an all-or-nothing decision.",1,N/A,There is no explicit endowment or public-good contribution frame with a default allocation; and the study has no human subjects.,1,N/R,"The model is framed as a prisoner's dilemma–like cooperation game with benefits f(x_i) to others, not as a linear public good with a defined marginal per capita return.",0.2,N/A,There is no experimental interface or human communication; the model is purely analytical.,1,N/A,No experimental feedback or UI is involved; agents are modeled with perfect monitoring but not via screens or summaries.,1,N/A,No human interface; identities and observability are embedded in the game structure but not as an experimental display choice.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism distinct from payoffs in the model and no display decisions.,1,N/A,The horizon is infinite and there is no notion of subjects being shown the number of rounds.,1,1,"In the enforcer version, a specialized enforcer chooses a punishment level y_i that reduces a producer’s payoff via g(y_i); in the no-enforcer comparison, punishment is absent but the condition as a whole includes cases with punishment.",1,N/R,"Punishment cost to the enforcer equals y_i per unit in utility terms, and harm to producers is g(y_i); there is no discrete per-token rate analogous to an experimental parameter.",0.3,N/R,Specialized enforcement is represented by a general concave function g(y); no single numeric multiplier is specified.,0.2,0,There is no separate costly reward or positive sanction technology; only cooperation benefits and coercive punishment are modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1,N/R,Agents choose costly effort x_i; no explicit endowment amount in tokens or money is defined.,0.3,N/R,"Theoretical results characterize equilibrium cooperation levels x, x*, x̂, etc., but not as normalized empirical contribution rates over an endowment.",0.4,N/R,"Equilibrium cooperation levels (e.g., x*, x̂) are defined implicitly as solutions to equations and not instantiated numerically except symbolically; no specific numerical contribution amount is reported.",0.4,N/R,The paper discusses social welfare and first-best benchmarks conceptually but does not report numeric efficiency ratios.,0.5,N/R,"Per-period and discounted payoffs are written as functions of x and y, but no concrete numeric payoffs are given.",0.5,N/A,No human participants are used; it is a theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No sample of people is studied.,1,N/A,No human participant pool exists.,1,No human,The work is a formal economic theory paper without empirical data.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated cooperation game in small groups of k+1 players. Two configurations: (i) all k+1 are producers; (ii) k producers plus 1 specialized enforcer. Stage game: producers choose continuous cooperation x_i ≥ 0, costing x_i and giving benefit f(x_i) to each other player; enforcer (if present) chooses punishment levels y_i ≥ 0, costing y_i to enforcer and imposing disutility g(y_i) on producer i. Functions f and g are increasing, strictly concave; f is bounded; players discount with factor δ. The paper characterizes optimal subgame-perfect equilibria (grim trigger without enforcer, and one-time enforcer punishment or punishment+repentance with an enforcer).","Summarized directly from Section 2.1–2.3 where the environment, payoffs, and strategies are defined.",1
10.1093_jeea_jvz008.md,Analytical large-group model with random matching and specialized enforcers (Section 3),"Section 3 generalizes the model to a large group of (k+l)n players with random matching and l enforcers per match, under perfect monitoring; this is a distinct analytical condition set.",1,"Population composition and enforcement regime in large groups: number of producers k and enforcers l per match, total population parameter n, and use of one-time enforcer punishment vs enforcer punishment plus (full or partial) repentance; functions f, g, and discount factor δ parameterize the environment.","Comparative results hinge on k, l, n and whether the equilibrium uses pure community enforcement (grim trigger with no enforcers) versus various one-time enforcer punishment structures.",0.9,False,The large-group model is also purely theoretical with no data collection from humans.,1,False,It is not a controlled behavioral experiment but an analytical repeated-game model.,1,False,No laboratory is involved; players are mathematical agents.,1,False,No computer simulation or numerical agent-based analysis is reported.,1,True,"The section derives equilibrium conditions and theorems (Theorem 3, 4) analytically.",1,0,"The total number of players is (k+l)n, where k, l, n are general positive integers; no specific numeric values are instantiated.",0.2,0,"The game is infinitely repeated over periods t = 0,1,2,...; a finite numeric round count is not defined.",1,1,"As in the small-group model, producers choose x_i ∈ R_+, a continuous effort level.",1,N/A,"There is no explicit endowment or pre-assigned contribution fraction, and no human subjects.",1,N/R,"The interaction is a general repeated cooperation game with benefit function f(x) to each partner, not a linear PGG with a specified MPCR.",0.2,N/A,No human communication protocol is modeled; information is given by monitoring structures.,1,N/A,"Perfect monitoring means matches’ outcomes are observed as part of the state, not as an experimental display decision.",1,N/A,Identification and observability are theoretical constructs; there is no experimental UI.,1,N/A,Rewards are not a separate mechanism here.,1,N/A,The horizon is infinite and players are not experimental subjects being informed of a finite length.,1,1,Enforcers in each match choose punishment levels y_{ji} that impose costs via g(y_{ji}).,1,N/R,"Punishment costs enforcer j an amount y_{ji}; this is specified in utility terms, not as a fixed monetary ‘cost per unit’ parameter.",0.3,N/R,The harm technology is given by a general concave function g(y); no single numeric tech multiplier is specified.,0.2,0,The model includes only costly punishment; rewards are not modeled as a separate instrument.,1,N/A,No costly reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of this analytical environment.,1,N/R,Players choose effort x_i; there is no defined initial token endowment.,0.3,N/R,"Equilibrium cooperation levels x*, x̂, check x, etc. are defined symbolically; there is no normalized empirical contribution rate reported.",0.4,N/R,No specific numeric values for equilibrium x are instantiated; they are characterized implicitly by equations.,0.4,N/R,The section discusses utilitarian social welfare and first-best cooperation x^FB but does not provide numeric efficiency ratios.,0.5,N/R,Per-match payoffs are given symbolically as functions of x and y; no numeric payoff is reported.,0.5,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,The analysis is theoretical without subjects.,1,N/A,No participants are sampled.,1,N/A,No human subject pool exists.,1,No human,The large-group model is a purely analytical repeated-game framework.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated cooperation game with a population of (k+l)n players: kn producers and ln enforcers. Each period, players are randomly partitioned into n matches, each with k producers and l enforcers. Cooperation stage: each producer in a match chooses a cooperation level x_i ≥ 0 (before seeing partner identities); choosing x_i costs x_i and benefits each other player in the same match by f(x_i). Punishment stage: each enforcer j in the match chooses punishment levels y_{ji} ≥ 0 toward each producer i; this costs y_{ji} to the enforcer and harms producer i by g(y_{ji}). Producers’ and enforcers’ stage payoffs are sums over their match partners, as specified in Section 3. Strategies like contagion, one-time enforcer punishment, and enforcer punishment plus repentance are analyzed under perfect monitoring to characterize maximal sustainable cooperation.","Summarized from Section 3.1, including player sets, matching, payoff functions, and the types of equilibria studied.",1
10.1093_jeea_jvz008.md,Analytical large-group model with private monitoring – general network monitoring (Section 4.1),Section 4.1 modifies the large-group model to include a stochastic monitoring network L_t and compares contagion vs one-time enforcer punishment under private monitoring; this constitutes a separate analytical condition.,1,Information structure via general network monitoring (distribution μ over monitoring networks L_t) and enforcement strategy type (pure contagion / community enforcement vs one-time enforcer punishment).,The key variation here is in what histories agents observe (captured by μ and L_t) and whether enforcement relies on contagion alone or uses specialized enforcer punishment.,0.9,False,No human data are collected; the section extends the analytical model to a private-monitoring environment.,1,False,This is not an experimental manipulation but a theoretical comparison of equilibria under different monitoring structures.,1,False,There is no lab or field deployment.,1,False,"Results are stated analytically using d_t, q_t, and equilibrium inequalities; no simulation runs are described.",1,True,"Theorems (e.g., Theorem 5) are proved using analytical expressions for sustainable cooperation under the monitoring structure.",1,0,"The underlying population is as in Section 3 ((k+l)n players), with k,l,n left as general symbolic parameters.",0.2,0,"Interactions occur over an infinite horizon t = 0,1,2,... .",1,1,Producers still choose x_i ∈ R_+; contribution is not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,N/A,"No endowments or default contributions are defined, and there are no humans.",1,N/R,The payoff technology remains f(x) for others per unit; no linear MPCR is specified numerically.,0.2,N/A,"The setting concerns information via observation networks, not communication between human subjects.",1,N/A,"Observation of past matches is modeled structurally, not as experimental summary screens.",1,N/A,"Monitoring specifies who observes which match, but there is no explicit UI decision about showing IDs.",1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,Infinite-horizon game; no finite-round information is displayed.,1,1,"As in the base model, specialized enforcers can choose costly punishment y_{ji}.",1,N/R,Costs to enforcers remain y_{ji} in utility terms; no discrete per-token rate is numerically specified.,0.3,N/R,Harm g(y) remains an abstract function; no numeric technology parameter is given.,0.2,0,Only punishment and contagion (withdrawal of cooperation) are enforcement channels.,1,N/A,Rewards are not considered.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/R,Effort x_i is the choice variable; no explicit monetary endowment exists.,0.3,N/R,"Theorems give expressions for the maximal x (hat x, x*) sustainable under different strategies, not normalized contribution rates.",0.4,N/R,No numeric solution for x* or hat x is instantiated in this subsection; they are defined implicitly.,0.4,N/R,"The comparison is about maximal sustainable cooperation levels (x) under different enforcement and monitoring, not reported as efficiency ratios.",0.5,N/R,Payoffs are discussed qualitatively; no explicit numeric group payoff is provided.,0.5,N/A,No empirical subjects are involved.,1,N/A,This is a theoretical extension.,1,N/A,There is no human participant pool.,1,N/A,No human data are gathered.,1,No human,The analysis is purely mathematical and uses no experiments or observational human data.,1,"Same underlying large-group cooperation game as in Section 3 ((k+l)n players, random matching into n matches with k producers and l enforcers per match), but now after each period a random monitoring network L_t is drawn from distribution μ. Player i observes match M_0’s outcome iff there exists j in M_0 with l_{i,j,t} = 1. Players always observe their own matches (l_{i,i,t} = 1). Based on this private monitoring, the paper defines contagion strategies (community enforcement) and one-time enforcer punishment strategies, and derives expressions for maximal sustainable cooperation under each, involving sequences d_t and q_t (expected number of infected producers over time). Theorem 5 gives conditions under which one-time enforcer punishment supports more cooperation than contagion (e.g., sufficiently effective punishment technology g or high discount factor δ).",Summarized from Section 4.1’s description of the general network monitoring environment and Theorem 5.,1
10.1093_jeea_jvz008.md,Analytical large-group model with private monitoring – observable last matches and informed enforcers (Section 4.2),"Section 4.2 considers a specific private-monitoring structure (observable last matches) and a variant with informed enforcers and nondiscrimination, analyzing optimality of one-time enforcer punishment equilibria.",1,Monitoring structure and information asymmetry: (i) observable last matches (each player sees own matches and each partner’s last match); (ii) variant where enforcers are perfectly informed while producers only see partners’ last matches; plus enforcement strategy type (contagion vs one-time enforcer punishment) and stability refinement.,"The central comparisons are across monitoring/information configurations (public vs private, producers vs enforcers’ information) and enforcement strategies.",0.9,False,This section remains purely theoretical; no actual agents participate.,1,False,No experimental design or data are presented; all results are theoretical equilibria.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment.,1,False,There are no numerical simulations; the theorems are proved analytically.,1,True,"Theorems 6, 7, 8 and definitions (e.g., Nondiscrimination, Stability) are analytical constructs.",1,0,"The underlying player set and matching are those of the general large-group model with symbolic parameters k, l, n.",0.2,0,The game is infinitely repeated.,1,1,Players still choose continuous cooperation levels x_i ≥ 0.,1,N/A,No experimental endowment structure is present; and there are no human subjects.,1,N/R,"As before, interactions are via a general function f(x); MPCR is not specified.",0.2,N/A,"Information is obtained by observation of matches, not communication among human subjects.",1,N/A,"Observability is defined via the monitoring structure (own and partners’ last matches), not via a UI choice.",1,N/A,Identities are implicit in the model; there is no experimental control over whether IDs are shown.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the enforcement technology.,1,N/A,Infinite horizon without subjects being told a number of rounds.,1,1,Specialized enforcers can punish deviating producers using g(y) technology.,1,N/R,"As elsewhere, punishment cost equals y in utility terms; no discrete numeric per-unit rate is parametrized.",0.3,N/R,Harm is via function g(y); no single numeric tech parameter provided.,0.2,0,"Enforcement uses only punishment and changes in cooperation, not explicit rewards.",1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward technology exists.,1,N/R,Effort x_i is the decision variable; there is no numerical endowment specification.,0.3,N/R,The results replicate the same maximal cooperation x* and x̂ as in perfect monitoring (Theorem 6) but do not translate them into normalized contribution rates.,0.4,N/R,The exact numeric values of x* are not instantiated; they remain solutions to equations as before.,0.4,N/R,"Discussion focuses on maximal cooperation levels and optimality among equilibria (including under Stability and Nondiscrimination), not numeric efficiency.",0.5,N/R,No numeric group payoff is calculated; only qualitative optimality is discussed.,0.5,N/A,There are no experimental or observational human data.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,Not applicable in a purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The section is analytical; environments are modeled mathematically.,1,"Base game as in Section 3, but with ‘observable last matches’ private monitoring: each period, a player observes the outcome of her own current and past matches; in addition, in each period she observes the outcome of each current partner’s most recent past match. Theorem 6 shows that with this monitoring, one-time enforcer punishment strategies and contagion strategies can sustain the same cooperation levels as under perfect monitoring (x* and x̂ are upper bounds in their respective classes). The section then introduces ‘informed enforcers’, who perfectly observe all past actions while producers still only observe last matches. Under a Nondiscrimination condition on producers’ strategies, Theorem 7 shows that if g′(y) ≥ m for all y, one-time enforcer punishment strategies maximize cooperation among all nondiscriminatory equilibria. The section also defines a Stability refinement (play returns to path after any single deviation in one period) and Theorem 8 states that the one-time enforcer punishment profile with cooperation x* and punishment y* is the most cooperative deterministic equilibrium satisfying Stability.","Summarized from Section 4.2, including the definitions of observable last matches, informed enforcers, Nondiscrimination, Stability, and Theorems 6–8.",1
10.1093_jeea_jvz008.md,Counterexample equilibrium: ‘three strikes and you’re out’ (3SYO) private-monitoring example (Section 4.2),"Within Section 4.2, the authors construct a specific numerical example (n = k = 2, l = 1, f(x)=100√x, g(y)=y, δ=0.1) and define a particular equilibrium labeled 3SYO to show that one-time enforcer punishment need not be optimal; this constitutes a distinct analytical condition with instantiated parameters.",1,"Monitoring asymmetry and punishment schedule in a specific parametric example: n = 2, k = 2, l = 1 (4 producers, 2 enforcers); producers observe their own matches and partners’ last matches; enforcers observe only their own matches (not partners’ last matches); cooperation levels differ by history (x1=493.830 on path, x2=494.102, x3=502.058 for repentance-like phases) and punishment level y=493.828; discount factor δ=0.1; benefit and punishment technologies f(x)=100√x, g(y)=y. The independent variation compared to one-time enforcer punishment is the use of a multi-step ‘three strikes and you’re out’ response combining enforcer punishment and eventual contagion after repeated shirking.",The counterexample fixes specific numeric parameter values and defines a structured multi-stage response to deviations (with up to three strikes) to demonstrate that a different strategy profile can sustain more cooperation (x1 ≈ 493.830) than the one-time enforcer punishment maximum (x* ≈ 493.8272).,1,False,"Despite using explicit numbers, this is still a theoretical equilibrium construction, not an experiment with participants.",1,False,No experimental procedure or behavioral data; it is an illustrative equilibrium example.,1,False,No lab environment exists; all values are hypothetical model parameters.,1,False,"The numbers x1, x2, x3, y are chosen analytically to satisfy incentive constraints; no simulation study is run.",1,True,The equilibrium construction is analytical and used to prove Proposition 3.,1,6,"In this example, n = k = 2 and l = 1, so there are (k+l)n = (2+1)*2 = 6 players: 4 producers and 2 enforcers.",1,0,The game is still an infinitely repeated game; no finite horizon is imposed in the example.,1,1,"Producers choose continuous cooperation levels x ≥ 0 (e.g. x1, x2, x3); not a binary choice.",1,N/A,There is no endowment-contribution framing; cooperation is continuous effort and there are no human subjects.,1,N/R,Payoffs are given by f(x)=100√x to each partner; no separate MPCR coefficient is defined.,0.3,N/A,This is a theoretical example; no human communication is modeled.,1,N/A,"Information is given via observation of own and partners’ last matches, not summaries.",1,N/A,"There is no explicit experimental interface; which enforcer punished whom is part of the game outcome, not a display-choice parameter.",1,N/A,No rewards appear in the example.,1,N/A,The horizon is infinite and not displayed to subjects (no subjects exist).,1,1,The example uses enforcer punishment at level y = 493.828 when specified conditions are met.,1,N/R,"In utility units, each unit of punishment y costs the enforcer y, since g(y)=y gives harm to the producer, but the exact mapping to a ‘cost per token’ is not given beyond this functional equality.",0.6,1,"Here g(y)=y, so each unit of enforcer effort yields one unit of harm to the punished producer; thus the punishment ‘multiplier’ is effectively 1.",0.8,0,There is no friendly reward mechanism; only cooperation levels and punishments and eventual contagion are used.,1,N/A,Rewards are not implemented.,1,N/A,No reward technology is specified.,1,N/R,"The example specifies effort levels x1, x2, x3 but not an explicit initial endowment; payoffs are in utility units from f(x) and costs x.",0.5,N/R,"x1, x2, x3 are in utility units of effort; there is no conversion to a normalized fraction of an endowment.",0.6,493.83,"On the equilibrium path in the 3SYO construction, producers choose x1 = 493.830 as the cooperation level; this is the key contribution amount sustained by the example and slightly exceeds the one-time punishment maximum x* ≈ 493.8272.",0.95,N/R,"The example is used to compare cooperation levels (x1 vs x*), not to compute efficiency ratios relative to first-best; f(x) and costs could allow such a computation, but the paper does not do it.",0.4,N/R,"Given f(x)=100√x and group composition, group payoff could be derived but is not reported in the text for this example.",0.4,N/A,This is a theoretical illustrative parameterization; no human data.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No subjects are recruited.,1,N/A,No human sample exists.,1,No human,The counterexample is fully theoretical.,1,"Specific parameterization of the large-group model to illustrate that a more complex equilibrium (three strikes and you’re out, 3SYO) can support more cooperation than the best one-time enforcer punishment equilibrium under private monitoring. Parameters: n = k = 2, l = 1 (so 4 producers, 2 enforcers), discount factor δ = 0.1, benefit function f(x) = 100√x, punishment technology g(y) = y. Monitoring: players observe own matches; producers additionally observe each partner’s last match; enforcers do not see partners’ last matches (producers have an informational edge). The one-time enforcer punishment equilibrium’s maximal cooperation x* satisfies x* = g((δ/(1−δ))*2*f(x*)) = (0.1/(0.9))*2*100√x*, giving x* ≈ 493.8272. The 3SYO equilibrium defines on-path cooperation x1 = 493.830, with off-path cooperation levels x2 = 494.102 and x3 = 502.058, and punishment level y = 493.828. Producers’ strategies: on path, play x1; after a first deviation (x < x1), play x2 for one period then return to x1; after a second deviation in specified matching circumstances, play x3 one period then return to x1; if a producer is seen deviating three times in a row in the specified pattern or if an enforcer fails to punish a deviation, producers switch to permanent zero cooperation (contagion). Enforcers’ strategies: punish a unique deviator at level y when she plays x < x1, unless two producers deviate simultaneously; if an enforcer fails to punish or sees a triple deviation pattern, he ceases to punish forever. This equilibrium exploits differences in beliefs between producers and enforcers under private monitoring, such that when a deviator hits the third strike, she simultaneously faces coercive punishment and future contagion, enabling a slightly higher sustainable cooperation level.","Summarized from the explicit ‘three strikes and you’re out’ example in Section 4.2, including numeric parameters and described strategies.",1
10.1017_ehs.2020.22.md,Dictator Game – Control (Unframed),Paper describes four Dictator Game conditions; this row is the unframed control condition labeled 'Control' in Table 1.,1,"Game framing: unframed control (anonymous recipient, no needy description, no supernatural punishment primes).",Condition defined in protocol section as the baseline with standard anonymous recipient and no additional framing.,1,True,Human Ik adults played Dictator Games in the field.,1,True,Controlled assignment to different framing conditions of a Dictator Game.,1,False,"Games were conducted in villages in Ik County, not in a laboratory setting.",1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations reported for this condition.,1,False,No purely analytical/mathematical model for this condition; it is a behavioral experiment.,1,2,Dictator and one anonymous recipient constitute a 2-player Dictator Game.,1,1,Standard Dictator Game; each participant makes a single allocation decision with no repetition mentioned.,1,1,"Participants could allocate 'some, none or all' of 20 coins, implying any integer share is allowed, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"All 2000 shillings are initially given to the dictator, who then decides how much to share; initial endowment is fully private.",1,N/R,Dictator Game has no group multiplier; marginal returns to giving are not framed as MPCR and not reported.,1,0,Instructions mention no communication between players; anonymity is emphasized.,1,0,No feedback or summaries of others’ behavior or payoffs described.,1,0,No punishment stage or identities; standard anonymous Dictator Game.,1,0,No reward stage; only one-way transfer and anonymity preserved.,1,0,One-shot game; there is no mention of showing any number of rounds.,1,0,Dictator Game has no mechanism for costly punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment option in the game.,1,N/A,No punishment option in the game.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism; transfers are the primary decision variable.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology like buying rewards for others.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,2000,Each player was given 2000 Ugandan shillings (twenty 100-shilling coins).,1,0.233,Table 1 reports mean proportion of endowment donated in Control as 0.233.,1,466,Mean proportion 0.233 × 2000 shillings = 466 shillings donated on average.,0.95,N/R,No efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum) is reported or inferable for this pure transfer game.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff data are reported by condition.,1,Uganda,"Participants are Ik adults living in Ik County, northeastern Uganda.",1,"Adults, mean age 34.3 years (SD = 13.9), 35% aged 18–25 across all conditions.",Descriptive age statistics reported for the full sample of 120 Ik Dictator Game participants.,0.9,"55 women, 65 men across all conditions; gender distribution by condition not reported.",Overall gender counts are given for the 120 participants but not broken down by treatment.,0.8,N/R,No information on education levels of participants is reported.,1,Field experiment,Games were run in villages in Ik County as part of anthropological fieldwork.,1,One-shot Dictator Game with 2000 UGX endowment in 20 coins; allocation between self and anonymous community member; money given to recipient was actually redistributed on another day; 30 participants in this condition; anonymity and no feedback.,Summarizes protocol details specific to this treatment from the Dictator Game Protocol and Table 1.,1
10.1017_ehs.2020.22.md,Dictator Game – Needy Recipient Frame,"One of four conditions, labeled 'NR' (Needy recipient) in Table 1 and described in the protocol.",1,"Game framing: recipient described as 'another member of your community who is in need' (e.g., old and hungry); no supernatural punishment primes.",Needy recipient script explicitly frames the recipient as needy without mentioning spirits.,1,True,Human Ik adults played this framed Dictator Game condition.,1,True,Condition is part of a randomized/assigned framing experiment.,1,False,"Implemented in villages during anthropological fieldwork, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,No non-human simulation for this condition.,1,False,Behavioral data only.,1,2,Dictator plus a single needy anonymous recipient.,1,1,Each participant made a single allocation decision; no repetition described.,1,1,"Allocation among 20 coins implies any integer amount, not limited to all-or-nothing.",1,0,Entire 2000 shillings initially allocated to dictator.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined for this transfer game.,1,0,No communication allowed; anonymity maintained.,1,0,No summaries or peer information.,1,0,No punishment phase.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism with identity revelation.,1,0,One-shot game without display of total or remaining rounds.,1,0,No punishment option.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,2000,Same 2000-shilling endowment for all conditions.,1,0.253,Table 1 lists mean proportion donated in NR condition as 0.253.,1,506,0.253 × 2000 shillings = 506 shillings mean donation.,0.95,N/R,No efficiency statistic reported for this condition.,1,N/R,Group payoff not reported by condition.,1,Uganda,"Ik participants reside in Ik County, Uganda.",1,"Adults, mean age 34.3 years (SD = 13.9) in full sample; condition-specific age data not reported.",Age summary is pooled over all 120 participants; no breakdown by condition.,0.8,"55 women, 65 men across all conditions; gender split in this condition not reported.",Only overall gender totals are given.,0.8,N/R,Education not discussed.,1,Field experiment,Run in Ik villages as part of fieldwork.,1,"One-shot Dictator Game; recipient framed as needy (e.g., 'old and hungry'); 2000 UGX endowment in 20 coins; allocations to needy recipients were actually distributed to elderly Ik individuals on another day; N = 30.",Details from condition-specific instructions and redistribution description.,1
10.1017_ehs.2020.22.md,Dictator Game – Supernatural Punishment Frame,Condition labeled 'Pun' (supernatural punishment) in Table 1 and defined in the protocol.,1,Game framing: questions about Earth spirits (kíjáwikᵃ) that may punish non-sharers and reward sharers; recipient described as anonymous community member (not specifically needy).,Supernatural punishment script precedes standard anonymous-recipient Dictator Game instructions.,1,True,Involves human Ik adults answering priming questions and playing the game.,1,True,Framing manipulated experimentally across conditions.,1,False,"Conducted in the field, not a laboratory.",1,False,No simulation for this treatment.,1,False,No purely analytical model of this condition.,1,2,Dictator plus one anonymous recipient.,1,1,"Single decision per player, as with other conditions.",1,1,Allocation over 20 coins; any split allowed.,1,0,Endowment fully given to dictator prior to allocation.,1,N/R,Not applicable for simple transfer game and not reported.,1,0,"Anonymity and lack of communication described; only scripted questions, no interaction with recipient.",1,0,No information about others’ choices or earnings is shown.,1,0,"No punishment mechanism or identity revelation; 'supernatural punishment' is a belief prime, not a game stage.",1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond the allocation itself.,1,0,No display of number of rounds in this one-shot game.,1,0,No strategic punishment choice; supernatural punishment is external to the game mechanism.,1,N/A,No player-controlled punishment.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology.,1,0,No separate costly reward option aside from giving.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,2000,Same 2000-shilling endowment applied.,1,0.29,Table 1 reports mean proportion donated for Pun condition as 0.29.,1,580,0.29 × 2000 shillings = 580 shillings mean donation.,0.95,N/R,No efficiency measure reported or directly inferable.,1,N/R,Group earnings not given.,1,Uganda,I