10.1017_ehs.2020.22.md,Dictator Game – Needy Recipient + Supernatural Punishment Combined Frame,"Fourth condition, labeled 'NR + Pun' in Table 1 and described as combining needy recipient and supernatural punishment framing.",1,"Game framing: questions about Earth spirits (kíjáwikᵃ) that punish non-sharers and reward sharers, plus recipient framed as 'another member of your community who is in need' (e.g., old and hungry).",Combined script contains both the supernatural questions and the needy recipient description.,1,True,Human Ik adults played this combined-framing condition.,1,True,Part of the same controlled framing experiment with four conditions.,1,False,Field setting in Ik villages.,1,False,No simulation used.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,2,Dictator plus one needy anonymous recipient.,1,1,Single allocation per participant.,1,1,Participants allocate up to 20 coins with no all-or-nothing restriction.,1,0,Full 2000-shilling endowment initially given to dictator.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined or reported.,1,0,No communication permitted; only scripted primes.,1,0,No summaries of others’ outcomes.,1,0,No actionable punishment feature; supernatural punishment is only a belief prime.,1,0,No separate reward stage.,1,0,Number of rounds not displayed in a one-shot game.,1,0,No strategic punishment mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,Punishment option unavailable.,1,N/A,Punishment option unavailable.,1,0,No distinct costly reward action aside from giving.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism.,1,2000,All conditions used a 2000-shilling endowment.,1,0.432,Table 1 lists mean proportion donated for NR + Pun condition as 0.432.,1,864,0.432 × 2000 shillings = 864 shillings mean donation.,0.95,N/R,Efficiency (relative to maximum payoff) not calculated or reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff statistics provided.,1,Uganda,Ik participants located in Uganda.,1,"Adults, mean age 34.3 years (SD = 13.9) over all conditions; no condition-specific ages given.",Age data pooled for the entire sample of 120.,0.8,"55 women, 65 men overall; condition-level gender breakdown not reported.",Only overall counts by gender are available.,0.8,N/R,No education information reported.,1,Field experiment,Conducted in rural Ik communities as part of fieldwork.,1,"One-shot Dictator Game with 2000 UGX in 20 coins; prior to allocation, participants primed with questions about Earth spirits’ punishing/rewarding sharing behavior; recipient framed as needy (e.g., old and hungry); allocations redistributed later to elderly or needy Ik; N = 30. This condition showed significantly higher generosity than control.","Synthesizes condition description, redistribution details, and reported significance (β = 0.346, p = 0.002).",1
10.1038_s41467-020-14712-3.md,Experiment 1 – Observer treatment (juvenile sees adult interact with fleeing plate),Experiment 1 has two between-subject treatments; this row is the observer treatment where juveniles watched adults cause plates to flee after prawn consumption.,1,Social observation of adult–plate interaction: observer treatment (adult could feed; plate fled immediately after prawn consumption). Plate response to cheating during tests: fleeing.,Independent variable is access to social information (observer vs control) with fixed plate response rule (fleeing) in tests; this row is the observer condition.,1,True,Live juvenile cleaner fish observed adult conspecifics and then were tested.,1,True,Manipulated social observation and plate behaviour under controlled lab conditions.,1,True,Fish were housed and tested in aquaria at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,False,No computational or agent-based model described.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as a standalone method.,1,1,Each juvenile made choices individually in its compartment; no strategic interaction with other agents within a game-theoretic round.,0.9,15,"Experiment 1 test phase: ""we conducted 15 test trials"" for each juvenile; treated as rounds.",1,1,Juveniles could choose item-by-item (three prawn and three flake items) and were not restricted to an all-or-nothing contribution.,1,N/A,No endowment or public vs private account; this is not a public goods or contribution game.,1,N/R,No group public good or multiplier structure is defined.,1,0,Fish had no means of communication manipulated in the design; only visual observation through partitions.,1,0,No payoff or behaviour summaries were displayed; juveniles only observed real-time interactions.,1,0,"No identities in the human sense; the punishing response was movement of the plate, not an identifiable agent; no identity revelation manipulation.",1,0,No explicit rewarding agent with identity.,1,0,No indication juveniles had information about the total number of trials.,0.9,1,"Negative response to cheating: plate is immediately removed ('fleeing'), reducing access to food; this functions as a payoff-reducing partner control mechanism.",1,N/R,"Cost per unit of punishment (e.g., tokens spent) is not defined in this biological setup.",1,N/R,"No numeric mapping from punisher action to victim payoff loss is given, only qualitative plate removal.",1,0,Only baseline feeding and negative responses (fleeing) are described; no additional costly prosocial reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Number of items a juvenile could potentially eat per trial is described (3 prawn, 3 flake) but not framed as an endowment in tokens; we leave as not reported.",0.7,N/R,"Outcome measured is 'feeding against preference score' (difference between observed and predicted flake choices), not a proportion of contribution to a public good.",1,N/R,"They report average flake items vs predicted and treatment effects, but not a clear raw mean number of cooperative choices per treatment in the text.",0.5,N/R,No ratio of actual to maximum group payoff is reported or computable in a multiplayer sense.,1,N/R,Total payoffs to groups are not defined; only individual food intake and derived scores.,1,Australia,"Fish were ""captured in the reefs surrounding Lizard Island"" which is in Australia.",1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),Subjects are explicitly described as juvenile cleaner fish with given body length instead of age in years.,1,N/R,Sex of individual fish was not reported.,1,N/A,Non-human animal subjects; education not applicable.,1,On site,"Conducted at Lizard Island Research Station with animals collected from nearby reefs and tested in aquaria; on-site lab, not online or field observation.",1,"Single-actor cooperative-cheating analogue: juvenile chooses between preferred (prawn, cheating) and less-preferred (flake, cooperative) items on a model client plate. Cheating triggers immediate plate removal, ending access; cooperative feeding prolongs access. Observer treatment juveniles watched adults experience plate fleeing in response to cheating before 15 individual test trials.",Summarises the key cooperative/cheating structure and contingency of plate behaviour for this condition.,1
