benchmark.,1,N/R,Group payoff not defined.,1,Australia,"Fish collected near Lizard Island, Australia.",1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),Same juvenile cohort as observer treatment.,1,N/R,Sex not given.,1,N/A,Non-human animal subjects.,1,On site,Aquarium experiment at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,"Same cooperative-cheating analogue as observer treatment: during 15 test trials plates fled immediately when prawn (cheating) was consumed and stayed when flake (cooperative) was eaten. Control juveniles lacked prior social information about plate responses, as adults could not interact with the plates during the demonstration phase.",Describes structure and how it differs from observer condition.,1
10.1038_s41467-020-14712-3.md,"Experiment 2 – Social learning phase: Observer, fleeing-responsive plate",One of four within-subject social-learning conditions in Experiment 2: observer × plate response type (fleeing).,1,"Social observation: observer (adult could feed on plates). Responsive plate reaction: fleeing (abrupt departure after prawn consumption). Plates: one tolerant, one fleeing-responsive.",Social learning phase had 2×2 design: observation (observer vs control) and plate response type (fleeing vs punishing); this row is observer–fleeing.,1,True,Juvenile cleaner fish participated as subjects.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of plate responsiveness and observational access.,1,True,Conducted in lab aquaria at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,No analytical-only study.,1,1,Juveniles made individual choices in test trials between two plates.,0.9,10,"For each treatment in the social learning phase, juveniles had ""a set of ten test trials"" choosing between plates.",1,1,Juveniles could choose one of two plates each trial; not restricted to all-or-nothing investment.,1,N/A,No endowment or public account.,1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,0,"During test trials an opaque barrier separated juveniles from adults, preventing social contact; no communication manipulation.",1,0,Juveniles saw direct interactions during observation but no outcome summaries.,1,0,Punishment implemented as plate fleeing; no separate identifiable punishing agent.,1,0,No explicit rewarder identity.,1,0,No mention that number of trials was signalled.,0.9,1,"Responsive plate fled abruptly after cheating, reducing feeding opportunity—analogous to punishment/fleeing control.",1,N/R,No numeric cost to punisher specified.,1,N/R,No quantitative mapping between act and payoff loss.,1,0,Tolerant plate simply allowed continued feeding; not modelled as costly prosocial reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/R,"Each plate had 2 prawn and 2 flake items, but not expressed as a subject endowment.",0.7,N/R,"Dependent variable is proportion of choices of tolerant vs responsive plates, not contribution.",1,N/R,"Choices of tolerant plates per 10 trials could serve as a raw measure, but treatment-specific means not numerically reported.",0.6,N/R,No group efficiency metric defined.,1,N/R,Group payoff is not defined; only individual plate choices and implied payoffs.,1,Australia,Same fish and location as Experiment 1 (Lizard Island).,1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),Juveniles from same cohorts participated in Experiments 2 and 3.,1,N/R,Sex not given.,1,N/A,Non-human animal subjects.,1,On site,Laboratory aquaria at a field research station.,1,"Social learning partner-choice task. Two model clients: tolerant plate (no negative reaction to cheating) vs fleeing-responsive plate (abruptly leaves after prawn consumption). In observer–fleeing condition, juveniles watched adults interact separately with each plate: tolerant plate allowed full feeding; fleeing plate left immediately after cheating. In subsequent 10 test trials, juveniles chose between the two plates; chosen plate’s reaction matched its type.",Summarizes cooperative structure and this specific condition.,1
10.1038_s41467-020-14712-3.md,"Experiment 2 – Social learning phase: Control, fleeing-responsive plate",Second social-learning condition with fleeing response but without observing actual feeding interactions.,1,"Social observation: control (adult blocked by transparent barrier; no feeding visible). Responsive plate reaction: fleeing (abrupt departure after prawn consumption). Plates: one tolerant, one fleeing-responsive.",Same plate response type (fleeing) as previous row but social information removed.,1,True,Live juvenile fish in controlled trials.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of observation vs matched plate-motion control.,1,True,Aquarium-based at research station.,1,False,No simulation.,1,False,No analytical-only results.,1,1,Individual juvenile choice per trial.,0.9,10,Ten test trials per treatment.,1,1,"Binary choice between two plates each round, not all-or-nothing contribution.",1,N/A,No endowment/public good.,1,N/R,No public goods game.,1,0,No communication during tests; opaque barrier used.,1,0,Juveniles did not see demonstrator outcomes; plate motion in control demonstrations was matched but without feeding.,1,0,Plate fleeing is the only negative reaction; no identity cue.,1,0,No rewarding agent.,1,0,No evidence fish knew total number of tests.,0.9,1,"During tests, fleeing-responsive plate departed after cheating, reducing access, analogous to punishment.",1,N/R,No tokenised cost.,1,N/R,No numeric mapping reported.,1,0,No extra prosocial reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Treatment not framed as endowment-based.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome is choice of tolerant vs responsive plate, not contributions.",1,N/R,Raw count of tolerant choices per treatment not numerically provided.,0.6,N/R,No group efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Group payoff not defined.,1,Australia,Same site as overall study.,1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),Same juveniles across Experiment 2 treatments.,1,N/R,Sex not provided.,1,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Aquarium experiment at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,"Identical partner-choice game to observer–fleeing condition during tests, but in the preceding demonstration phase adults were prevented from feeding; plate movements (gentle leaving, fleeing) and exposure time were yoked to an observer fish so juveniles had matched non-social motion cues without payoff information.",Clarifies how this control differs from observer condition and its game-like structure.,1
10.1038_s41467-020-14712-3.md,"Experiment 2 – Social learning phase: Observer, punishing-responsive plate",Third social-learning condition: juveniles observed adult interactions where the responsive plate punished (chased) rather than fled.,1,"Social observation: observer (adult could feed). Responsive plate reaction: punishing (plate chased cleaner ~3 s after prawn consumption). Plates: one tolerant, one punishing-responsive.",Varied both observation access and type of negative response; this row corresponds to observer–punishing combination.,1,True,Live juvenile fish as participants.,1,True,Experimental manipulation of punishment type and observation.,1,True,Conducted in laboratory aquaria.,1,False,No simulation models.,1,False,No pure analytical modeling.,1,1,Single juvenile decision-maker per trial.,0.9,10,Ten test choice trials per treatment.,1,1,"Binary plate choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No notion of contribution share.,1,N/R,No public goods framework.,1,0,Opaque barrier during tests; no communication.,1,0,Only direct experience and observation of interactions; no summaries.,1,0,Punishment administered by model client plate; no identity cues varied.,1,0,No explicit rewarding agent.,1,0,Number of test trials not signalled.,0.9,1,"Punishing plate ""would chase the cleaner for ~3 s"" after cheating, akin to direct punishment in the wild.",1,N/R,No numeric cost parameter reported for punisher.,1,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishing action to payoff reduction beyond qualitative description.,1,0,"Tolerant plate behaviour is baseline access, not a costly reward.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Plates had 2 prawn and 2 flake items each, but not treated as an endowment in the paper.",0.7,N/R,Outcome is choice proportion of tolerant vs punishing plates.,1,N/R,Exact mean number of tolerant choices per condition not numerically given.,0.6,N/R,No group efficiency reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not defined.,1,Australia,Same as rest of study.,1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),Single juvenile cohort across all Experiment 2 treatments.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Lab aquaria at field station.,1,"Partner-choice social learning game. Juveniles observed adults interacting with a tolerant plate (no negative reaction) and a punishing-responsive plate (chased cleaner for ~3 s after prawn consumption). After 20 such demonstrations, juveniles made 10 choices between the two plates; chosen plate’s response to cheating matched its type. This condition tested whether juveniles socially learn to prefer tolerant over punishing clients.",Highlights punishment form and cooperative interpretation.,1
10.1038_s41467-020-14712-3.md,"Experiment 2 – Social learning phase: Control, punishing-responsive plate",Fourth social-learning condition with punishing response but no visible adult–plate interaction.,1,"Social observation: control (adult blocked; plate motion and timing matched without feeding). Responsive plate reaction: punishing (simulated punishing movement pattern). Plates: one tolerant, one punishing-responsive.",Same punishment type as prior condition but with social information removed via transparent barrier; plate movements simulated out of reach.,1,True,Juvenile fish in controlled lab trials.,1,True,"Randomised order, within-subject manipulation of observation and plate reaction.",1,True,Aquarium at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No analytical model.,1,1,Each juvenile chose independently.,0.9,10,Ten test trials per treatment.,1,1,"Binary plate choice each trial, not all-or-nothing investment.",1,N/A,No contributions to a pool.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No opportunity for communication during test phase.,1,0,Juveniles only experienced direct interactions in test phase; control demonstrations did not convey payoff information.,1,0,Punishment enacted by model plate only; no identity manipulation.,1,0,No rewarding agent.,1,0,Rounding information not presented.,0.9,1,"During tests, punishing plate simulated chasing movement after cheating, akin to punishment.",1,N/R,No numeric cost parameter.,1,N/R,No quantified magnitude per unit.,1,0,No additional costly reward.,1,N/A,Reward not implemented.,1,N/A,Reward not implemented.,1,N/R,Not specified as endowment.,0.7,N/R,DV is tolerant vs punishing plate choice proportion.,1,N/R,Numerical mean choices not reported.,0.6,N/R,No efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Group payoff not defined.,1,Australia,Same experimental context.,1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),Same subjects.,1,N/R,Unreported.,1,N/A,Non-human subjects.,1,On site,On-site aquarium lab.,1,"Same partner-choice payoff structure as observer–punishing condition during tests. In matched control demonstrations, plates were confined and 'punishing' motion was simulated out of demonstrator’s reach, with timing and sequence yoked to an observer fish. Juveniles then made 10 choices between tolerant and punishing plates, experiencing their own payoffs through direct interaction only.",Clarifies absence of social payoff information while keeping motion cues.,1
10.1038_s41467-020-14712-3.md,Experiment 3 – Preferring demonstrator treatment (adult shows strong arbitrary plate preference),"One of two between-subject treatments in Experiment 3, where adults had been trained to prefer one coloured plate.",1,"Demonstrator preference regime: preferring demonstrator (adult trained so that one coloured plate always had 2 prawn items, the other 0; strong preference displayed). Juveniles observed this preference before tests.",Independent variable in Experiment 3 is adult demonstrator preference structure (preferring vs non-preferring); this row is preferring condition.,1,True,Juvenile cleaner fish observed adult choices and then made their own.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of demonstrator training and observational exposure.,1,True,Conducted in aquaria at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No analytical-only model.,1,1,Each juvenile made individual choices between two plates in tests.,0.9,10,"Observers ""were presented with a set of ten simultaneous choice tests"" between the two plates.",1,1,Binary choice per trial; not all-or-nothing contribution.,1,N/A,No endowment or contribution to a common pool.,1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,0,Only visual observation of adult during demonstration; no interaction during tests.,1,0,"Juveniles saw individual adult choices, not summary statistics.",1,0,No punishment mechanism or identities.,1,0,No costly rewards.,1,0,No indication of number of trials given.,0.9,0,Plates in Experiment 3 did not flee or punish; they simply provided hidden food.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,"Both plates in test phase ""equally rewarding"" (1 prawn each); no additional prosocial reward.",1,N/A,No distinct rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No distinct rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,Task not framed with an owned endowment.,0.7,N/R,"DV is copying of demonstrator plate preference, not contribution.",1,N/R,"They report that juvenile preferences did not differ between treatments, but not numeric means.",0.6,N/R,No group payoff or efficiency defined.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not applicable.,1,Australia,Same location (Lizard Island).,1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),Same juvenile cohort; N=10 in preferring demonstrator treatment.,1,N/R,Sex of fish not reported.,1,N/A,Non-human animals.,1,On site,Aquarium experiment at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,"Arbitrary social preference-copying task. Two monochromatic plates (red, green) each with hidden food on back. Adults in 'preferring demonstrator' group were pre-trained so that one plate always contained 2 prawn items and the other none, producing strong preference. Juveniles observed 14–20 such demonstrations per pair, then completed 10 test trials where both plates now each had 1 prawn item (equal payoff). This condition tested whether juveniles copy demonstrator’s arbitrary colour preference.",Summarises the structure and lack of cooperative context.,1
10.1038_s41467-020-14712-3.md,Experiment 3 – Non-preferring demonstrator treatment (adult approaches plates at random),Second Experiment 3 treatment where adults were trained to have no stable plate preference.,1,"Demonstrator preference regime: indifferent/non-preferring demonstrator (both plates equally rewarding during training and demonstrations, 1 prawn each; adult approached plates approximately at random). Juveniles observed these indifferent choices before tests.",Independent variable is demonstrator preference structure; this row is the non-preferring/indifferent condition.,1,True,Juvenile fish tested after social observation.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of adult training and observational exposure.,1,True,Conducted in lab aquaria at Lizard Island Research Station.,1,False,No simulation elements.,1,False,No analytical-only framework.,1,1,Individual juvenile decisions each trial.,0.9,10,Ten simultaneous choice tests per observer.,1,1,Single plate choice per trial; not all-or-nothing investment.,1,N/A,No contributions or public good.,1,N/R,Not a public goods context.,1,0,No communication channel; only visual observation.,1,0,"Juveniles saw discrete demonstrator choices, not summaries.",1,0,No punishment.,1,0,No separate rewarding agent.,1,0,Trials count not signalled.,0.9,0,No negative reactions (fleeing or chasing) in this experiment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,Both plates equally rewarding in test phase; no prosocial reward.,1,N/A,No separate reward action.,1,N/A,No separate reward action.,1,N/R,Not described in endowment terms.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome is whether juveniles’ preferences track indifferent demonstrators, not contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric mean of plate choices reported by treatment.,0.6,N/R,No cooperative group payoff structure.,1,N/R,Group payoff undefined.,1,Australia,Same experimental site.,1,Juvenile cleaner wrasse (average length 4.45 ± 0.64 cm),N=9 juvenile observers in indifferent demonstrator treatment.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/A,Non-human study subjects.,1,On site,Aquarium experimentation at a field research station.,1,"Same arbitrary preference-copying setup as preferring condition, except adults were trained so that both coloured plates were equally rewarding (1 prawn each) and their choices were encouraged to be random. Juveniles observed 14–20 such indifferent-choice demonstrations, then had 10 test trials where both plates had 1 prawn item. This condition served as a control for general effects of social exposure on juvenile plate preferences.",Explains how this condition differs from preferring demonstrator and its purpose.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_132_10007.md,"Synchronous updating – baseline (no punishment, L=0)",Condition with L=0 explicitly described as meaning no one will be punished; used as baseline in Figs. 1–3.,1,"Punishment parameter L=0 (no punishment), punishment fine α is irrelevant; enhancement factor r varies; synchronous updating.","Independent variables are L, α, r and updating rule; for this condition L=0, α has no effect, r is varied and updating is synchronous.",0.95,False,Study is a Monte Carlo simulation on agents located on a lattice; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human participants; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Model section and results describe lattice-based evolutionary simulations with time steps and Monte Carlo averaging.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations; results are numerical simulations.,1,10000,"Agents located on a 100×100 square lattice, so N = 100×100 = 10000.",1,21000,They average over 10^3 time steps after a transient of 2×10^4; total simulated steps per run ≈ 2.1×10^4.,0.7,1,"Strategies si ∈ [0,1] are continuous, so agents may contribute any amount in the interval rather than all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Standard PGG: individuals decide contributions each round; no initial allocation to public fund is described, implying all starts as private endowment.",0.6,0.2,"Payoff term is (r/5) Σ s_x; total return is r times total contribution, split equally among 5 individuals, so MPCR = r/5. For normalized unit contribution r=1 gives 1/5=0.2; they vary r but base marginal return per 1 unit is r/5. A single numeric entry is needed, so report per unit as r/5 at r=1.",0.4,0,No communication or messaging between agents is mentioned; only imitation based on payoffs.,1,0,Agents update based only on their own and a neighbor’s payoff; no summaries of others’ earnings described.,0.9,0,Simulation does not model identities or information about who punished whom; interactions are purely payoff-based.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism is included in the model.,1,0,Agents are not described as knowing the horizon; infinite/indefinite evolutionary process.,0.9,0,They state that for L=0 there is no punishment.,1,N/A,"No punishment in this condition, so cost per punishment is not applicable.",1,N/A,No punishment applies here.,1,0,Only punishment is modelled; no reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No rewards in any condition.,1,N/A,No rewards in any condition.,1,1,"Strategy si ∈ [0,1] is directly the contribution per group; standard CPGG assumes unit endowment normalized to 1.",0.6,N/R,"They report cooperation level ρ = Σ si(∞)/N but not normalized per endowment as a fraction; ρ can exceed typical 0–1 per-player fraction with multiple groups, so not directly a rate in the specified sense.",0.3,N/R,No explicit average si values or aggregate contributions are tabulated; only qualitative cluster centers and ρ curves shown.,0.2,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximal group payoff is reported or can be reliably inferred without full payoff distributions.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs are defined formulaically but no numerical averages are presented.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of computational simulations only.,1,"Spatial continuous public goods game on 5-person overlapping groups on a 100×100 lattice; each individual participates in 5 groups (self and 4 neighbors). Contributions are continuous si ∈ [0,1]; payoffs without punishment are Πij = −si + (r/5) Σx sx; synchronous updating uses Fermi imitation with noise K=0.5.",Summarized from the Model and Results sections.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_132_10007.md,"Synchronous updating – punishment, L=1",Condition with L=1 (one lowest contributor punished) under synchronous updating; appears in Figs. 1–5.,1,"Punishment parameter L=1; punishment fine α varied (e.g., 0.2, 0.3, other values); enhancement factor r varied; synchronous updating.",L is set to 1; α and r are varied in different figures; update rule is synchronous in primary analyses.,0.95,False,"Simulation with artificial agents on a lattice, no human subjects.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment; parameter sweeps in a computational model.,1,False,No physical lab or field setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on a 100×100 lattice with evolutionary updating.,1,False,No analytic solution provided.,1,10000,Same lattice size (100×100) used for all conditions.,1,21000,Same simulation length: 2×10^4 transient + 10^3 for averaging.,0.7,1,"Continuous contribution strategies si ∈ [0,1].",1,0,As in baseline; no initial mandatory contribution to public fund described.,0.6,0.2,Same payoff structure; MPCR per unit contribution is r/5; reported as base 0.2 for r=1.,0.4,0,No communication mechanism.,1,0,No aggregate feedback described.,0.9,0,Agents only respond via payoff-based imitation; identities of punishers/punished not modelled as information.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No horizon information provided to agents.,0.9,1,"In each group, the single lowest contributor is punished when L=1.",1,0.05,"Punisher cost per group is β = Lα/(5−L) = 1·α/4 = 0.25α. With α=0.2 (used in Fig.1), β=0.05. A single numeric entry is required; using this canonical example.",0.6,0.2,"Each punished individual pays fine α. In main plots α=0.2 (e.g., Fig.1), so a single punishment imposes 0.2 loss per unit event.",0.6,0,Model includes only punishment.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Same normalized unit contribution capacity as other conditions.,0.6,N/R,Only cooperation level ρ is plotted; not mapped to per-player normalized rate as defined.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mean si reported for L=1 beyond qualitative cluster centers.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency ratios reported.,0.2,N/R,No numerical payoffs reported for this condition.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation study.,1,N/A,Simulation study.,1,N/A,Simulation study.,1,No human,Entirely computational.,1,"In each 5-person group, the single lowest contributor (L=1) is punished by the other four; punished player loses fine α; each of the 4 punishers pays cost β = α/4. Strategy updating is synchronous using Fermi rule with K=0.5.",Derived from model definition of punishment and updating.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_132_10007.md,"Synchronous updating – punishment, L=2","Condition with two lowest contributors punished (L=2) under synchronous updating; appears in Figs. 1,2,4,5.",1,"Punishment parameter L=2; punishment fine α varied (e.g., 0.2, 0.8, 1.6); enhancement factor r varied (e.g., r=3 in Fig.4, r varied in Fig.1); synchronous updating.",L fixed at 2 while α and r are varied in different analyses with synchronous rule.,0.95,False,Agent-based simulation on lattice.,1,False,No human experimental data.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment.,1,True,As stated in Model and Results.,1,False,No analytical payoff evolution derived.,1,10000,100×100 lattice.,1,21000,Same time horizon for all synchronous runs.,0.7,1,Continuous strategies.,1,0,No initial public endowment described.,0.6,0.2,Same PGG payoff formulation; MPCR per unit contribution r/5; report base 0.2 at r=1.,0.4,0,No communication channel.,1,0,No mention of global summaries.,0.9,0,Punishment modeled as payoff adjustment only.,1,0,No rewards implemented.,1,0,No explicit horizon knowledge modeled.,0.9,1,Last L=2 individuals in each group are punished.,1,0.1333333333,"β = Lα/(5−L) = 2α/3. For example α=0.2 (used generically), β≈0.1333. A single numeric instance is reported from this canonical α.",0.6,0.2,Each punished player is fined α; for reference α=0.2 in some plots.,0.6,0,Only punishment is present.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,"Same normalized contribution interval [0,1].",0.6,N/R,"Only ρ curves and cluster centers provided, not a normalized rate per endowment as requested.",0.3,N/R,"Although some cluster strategy values (e.g., 0.157, 0.094, 0.021, 0.75, etc.) are mentioned, no global average contribution is reported.",0.4,N/R,No efficiency metric is computed.,0.2,N/R,No aggregated group payoff results reported.,0.2,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model.,1,"In each 5-person group, the two lowest contributors are punished; each punished player pays fine α; the 3 punishers share total cost 2α, so each punisher pays β = 2α/3. Strategy evolution uses synchronous Fermi updating with noise K=0.5.",From general punishment definition with L=2.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_132_10007.md,"Synchronous updating – punishment, L=3",Condition in which the three lowest contributors are punished (L=3) under synchronous updating; shown in Figs. 1–4.,1,"Punishment parameter L=3; punishment fine α varied (e.g., 0.2, 0.3, up to 0.9 in some analyses); enhancement factor r varied (e.g., r=4 in Fig.2, r=8 in Fig.4); synchronous updating.",L fixed at 3; α and r varied in different analyses with synchronous updating.,0.95,False,"Pure simulation, no humans.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical environment.,1,True,Lattice-based Monte Carlo.,1,False,No analytical model.,1,10000,100×100 lattice for all cases.,1,21000,Same time horizon applied.,0.7,1,Continuous contributions allowed.,1,0,No initial public pool allocation.,0.6,0.2,"Same PGG structure, MPCR per unit r/5; report base 0.2 at r=1.",0.4,0,No communication implemented.,1,0,No feedback summaries.,0.9,0,Only payoffs affect imitation.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents do not know total time steps.,0.9,1,Three lowest contributors in each group are punished.,1,0.3,"β = Lα/(5−L) = 3α/2. With representative α=0.2, β = 0.3 per punisher per group.",0.6,0.2,Fine per punished player is α; representative α=0.2.,0.6,0,Only punishment modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Same normalized contribution bounds.,0.6,N/R,"They report that ρ(t)→0 for L=3 and L=4, but not as a normalized per-endowment rate as defined; only time paths of ρ(t).",0.4,N/R,"They mention final strategies near 0 for L=3, but no quantitative average contributions across runs.",0.4,N/R,Efficiency ratio not computed.,0.2,N/R,No numerical payoffs shown.,0.2,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"In each group, three lowest contributors are punished; each punished player pays fine α; the remaining 2 punishers share total cost 3α, so each punisher pays β = 3α/2. For L=3 and L=4, punishers are minority and β>α, which the authors highlight as detrimental to cooperation.",From punishment cost definition and discussion in text.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_132_10007.md,"Synchronous updating – punishment, L=4","Condition with L=4 (four lowest contributors punished; punishers are minority) under synchronous updating; appears in Figs. 1,2,4,5.",1,"Punishment parameter L=4; punishment fine α varied (e.g., 0.1, 0.5, 0.9); enhancement factor r varied (e.g., r=8 in Fig.4 and 5); synchronous updating.",L fixed at 4; α and r varied in analyses using synchronous updating.,0.95,False,Agent-based simulation.,1,False,No human-subject data.,1,False,No lab/field context.,1,True,As described in Model.,1,False,No closed-form analysis.,1,10000,100×100 lattice.,1,21000,Same run length as other synchronous conditions.,0.7,1,Continuous contributions allowed.,1,0,No pre-assigned public allocation described.,0.6,0.2,Same PGG payoff structure; MPCR per unit r/5; reporting base 0.2 at r=1.,0.4,0,No chat or communication modelled.,1,0,No summaries mentioned.,0.9,0,Punishment not modeled as observable signal.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No finite-horizon information.,0.9,1,Four out of five group members (all but highest contributor) are punished when L=4.,1,0.8,"β = Lα/(5−L) = 4α/1 = 4α. For example, with α=0.2 used generically, β=0.8 per punisher per group.",0.6,0.2,Each punished player is fined α; illustrative α=0.2.,0.6,0,Only punishment mechanism present.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,1,"Same contribution bounds [0,1] as other conditions.",0.6,N/R,"ρ(t) decreases to 0 for L=4, but not explicitly turned into normalized contribution rate in the defined sense.",0.4,N/R,"Spatial snapshots show final strategies near 0 as α increases, but no explicit average contribution across system.",0.4,N/R,No efficiency metric provided.,0.2,N/R,No quantitative payoff data.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational.,1,"In each 5-person group, all but the highest contributor (L=4) are punished; each punished pays α; the single punisher pays β = 4α. Authors note that punishers are minority and pay higher cost than fines levied, which drives the system toward low-strategy clusters and eventually full defection for sufficiently large α.",Based on model description and interpretation around Figs. 4–5.,1
