10.1023_B_ACBI.0000043440.36462.1a.md,Non-structured society – varying % altruistic punishers,"Simulations without labor specialization where the independent variable is the proportion of altruistic punishers; used for Figures 1, 2, 3, 4, 7.",0.89,"Percentage of agents exercising altruistic punishment in a non-structured society (0–30%, exact levels not fully specified) and sensitivity to punishment (0–5 deciles of wf, with examples at sensitivity=1 and a range in Figure 2). This row represents the generic condition family where these parameters are varied.",Methods and Results sections specify that the percentage of altruistic punishers and sensitivity to punishment are systematically varied; exact discrete values are not fully enumerated.,0.8,False,"Study uses the agent-based computer simulation Sociodynamica with artificial agents, not human participants.",1,False,"This is a simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects.",1,False,No physical lab with human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly described as an agent-based computer simulation model (Sociodynamica).,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations are used for results; all outcomes come from simulations.,1,500,Methods: “simulations were run 200 times with 500 agents for 100 time-steps.”,1,100,Methods: each run lasts 100 time-steps; treated as the number of rounds.,1,1,"Agents donate a variable fraction (0–50%) of their current food wealth when conditions are met; contributions are continuous, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,"No explicit endowments or public/private account structure; agents accumulate resources via movement, not initial allocation. Also, no human participants.",1,N/R,This is not a public goods game with a linear return multiplier; no MPCR is defined.,1,N/A,Artificial agents follow fixed rules; no communication mechanism like human chat exists.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human subjects; purely internal simulation variables.,1,N/A,Agent identities are not a reported feature; there is no notion of revealed identity as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism is modeled beyond donations and punishment.,1,N/A,No human subjects or on-screen round counters; time-steps are internal to the simulation.,1,1,Key mechanism: altruistic punishers reduce wealth of low-generosity agents at a cost to themselves.,1,0.1,"Methods: “the cost to the punisher was fixed at 10% of its wf, and equaled the cost the punished agent paid.” Cost per punishment event is 10% of punisher’s current food wealth.",0.85,0.1,"Punished agent also loses 10% of its wf per punishment. Treated as 0.1 of the punished agent’s wealth per unit of punisher’s cost, since both costs are equal fractions.",0.7,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond generosity (donations); no costly reward stage described.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/R,Agents accumulate resources via movement and encounters with patches; initial wealth levels are not specified as numeric endowments.,0.5,N/R,"Outcomes reported are generosity distributions, aggregate wealth, and ages; no normalized contribution rate is provided.",0.3,N/R,Donations are defined as deciles of current wf but no specific mean/total donation amounts are numerically reported.,0.3,N/R,"Aggregate wealth (GDP) is reported qualitatively and in figures, but not as a normalized efficiency ratio relative to a defined maximum.",0.4,N/R,Aggregate total wealth is shown in figures but no explicit numerical values or summaries are listed in text.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation without human subjects.,1,"Agent-based society on a 2D toroidal world with 200 units of food and 100 units of minerals located in separate patches. 500 agents move via Brownian motion (speed 0–30 pixels/time-step), collecting resources; food decays at rate b=0.1 per time-step, minerals do not decay. Mineral wealth reduces probability of death from random catastrophes according to wm < rnd(0–1)*D. When two agents meet within 20 pixels and their food wealth differs by more than 2 units, the richer donates according to their generosity g (0–0.5 of wf). Low-generosity agents (g ≤ 1 decile, i.e., donate <10% of wf) can be punished by altruistic punishers, losing 10% of wf while the punisher pays the same cost. Sensitivity to punishment controls increase in generosity after being punished (0–5 deciles of wf). Non-structured society: all agents can collect both resources; no division of labor.",Summarizes the core structural rules as stated in Methods; focuses on the non-structured (no division of labor) setting in which % punishers and sensitivity to punishment are varied.,0.96
10.1023_B_ACBI.0000043440.36462.1a.md,Structured society – varying % altruistic punishers,"Simulations with labor specialization (farmers, miners, traders) where the independent variable is the proportion of altruistic punishers; used for Figures 5 and 6.",0.89,"Percentage of agents exercising altruistic punishment in a structured society with division of labor (0–30%, exact levels not fully specified).","Results describe that in structured societies (with farmers, miners, traders) the percentage of altruistic punishers is varied and its effect on generosity and aggregate wealth is observed (Figures 5 and 6).",0.82,False,Agent-based computer simulation with artificial agents only.,1,False,No empirical experimental protocol with human or field subjects; purely computational.,1,False,"Simulation environment, not a physical lab with participants.",1,True,Explicitly identified as simulations using the Sociodynamica agent-based model.,1,False,No formal mathematical solution of model dynamics; results are simulation-based.,1,500,"Methods: standard configuration is 500 agents per run; same size is referenced for structured societies (e.g., Figures 5 and 6 captions).",1,100,Each simulation run is 100 time-steps; structured societies use same runtime.,1,1,"As in the baseline model, donations are variable fractions (deciles) of wf, not binary.",0.9,N/A,No initial monetary endowments or public/private account structure; simulated agents accumulate resources via movement.,1,N/R,No public goods linear multiplier is defined in the structured society; resource and trade rules differ from MPCR-style games.,1,N/A,No human communication; agents follow behavioral rules without messaging.,1,N/A,No human interface elements such as feedback screens; purely internal state variables.,1,N/A,No notion of identity revelation as in human experiments; not reported or relevant for agent decisions.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism; only donations and punishment.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe rounds; time-steps are internal model cycles.,1,1,"Altruistic punishers operate here as in the non-structured case, punishing low-generosity agents.",1,0.1,Same model rule: punisher pays 10% of its wf per punishment; this applies in structured societies as well.,0.85,0.1,Punished agents also lose 10% of wf for each punishment; technology approximated as equal cost fraction.,0.7,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism is described in structured simulations.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Initial agent wealth is not numerically specified; agents gather resources after the start via labor and trade.,0.5,N/R,"Though average generosity is tracked, it is not normalized relative to a fixed endowment or maximum; no explicit numerical values are given.",0.3,N/R,"Donations occur as deciles of current wf, but no explicit aggregate or mean donation amounts are reported numerically.",0.3,N/R,"Aggregate wealth is reported via graphs; maximum possible wealth under full cooperation is not specified, so efficiency cannot be computed.",0.4,N/R,"“Aggregate total wealth (GDP)” is graphed, but no numerical values are tabulated in the text.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects; only artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects; only artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects; only artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects; only artificial agents.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations without human participants.,1,"Same base sociophysical world as in the non-structured model (2D toroidal world, resource patches, catastrophes, punishment rule), but with division of labor: farmers collect only food, miners collect only minerals, and traders specialize in trading. Traders exchange minerals for food with farmers and food for minerals with miners, and increase the value of minerals they trade by 50%, representing value-added through processing or productivity. Punishment of low-generosity agents (g ≤ 1 decile) by altruistic punishers operates across all roles (farmers, miners, traders). Structured societies exhibit different dynamics: lower aggregate wealth than non-structured ones, higher susceptibility of average generosity to the percentage of punishers, higher mortality (lower average age), and role composition shifts (more farmers, fewer miners/traders) as sensitivity to punishment increases.","Summarizes structured-society specific rules from Methods and interprets descriptive findings from Figures 5, 6, 8, 9, and 10.",0.95
10.1023_B_ACBI.0000043440.36462.1a.md,Non-structured society – varying sensitivity to punishment,Simulations without labor specialization where sensitivity to punishment is the main independent variable; used for Figures 2 and 7.,0.87,Sensitivity to punishment in a non-structured society (0–5 deciles of wf increase in generosity after punishment).,Methods: sensitivity to punishment regulates the increase in generosity (0–5 deciles) after punishment; Figure 2 varies susceptibility (sensitivity) and reports average generosity; Figure 7 varies susceptibility and reports age.,0.9,False,Computer simulation with artificial agents only.,1,False,No empirical experiment; purely simulation-based exploration.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment with humans.,1,True,Described as simulations using the Sociodynamica agent-based model.,1,False,"Results obtained from simulation runs, not derivations.",1,1500,"Results section for Figure 2 and Figure 1: “frequency distribution of two sets of 1500 agents” and “The average level of generosity shown by 1500 agents, after 100 time-steps, in simulations with different levels of susceptibility to punishment”.",0.95,100,Described generally that runs last 100 time-steps; Figure 2 caption: average after 100 time-steps.,1,1,"Donations are in deciles (0–0.5) of wf, implying continuous contribution levels.",0.9,N/A,No initial endowment in a public/private account sense; resources are acquired during the simulation.,1,N/R,No linear public good multiplication; not a standard PGG.,1,N/A,No human communication channel; rules are algorithmic.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents are not informed of global summaries.,1,N/A,No concept of identity disclosure like in human experiments.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond donations.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe time-steps; only the modeler does.,1,1,Sensitivity to punishment is only meaningful if punishment exists; altruistic punishers operate as in baseline model.,1,0.1,Same 10% wf cost to punisher per punishment applies.,0.85,0.1,Punished agent also loses 10% of wf per event; tech treated as equal fraction loss.,0.7,0,No separate costly reward stage modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Initial wealth of each agent is not specified numerically.,0.5,N/R,"Average generosity levels are reported qualitatively with graphs, but not as numeric normalized rates.",0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric donation amounts are given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio is computed.,0.4,N/R,These runs focus on generosity and age; aggregate wealth is not explicitly enumerated for each sensitivity level here.,0.3,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,"Artificial agents only; age is a simulation variable, not participant age.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations only.,1,"Non-structured society configuration: 2D toroidal world, 200 units of food and 100 units of minerals in separate static patches. 1500 agents move via Brownian motion, gathering resources. Food decays at b=0.1 per time-step; minerals do not decay. Catastrophic deaths occur randomly with probability decreasing in mineral wealth. When two agents are within 20 pixels and their food wealth differs by more than 2 units, the richer donates a fraction of its wf determined by its generosity g (0–5 deciles; 0–50% of wf). Altruistic punishers reduce the food wealth of low-generosity agents (g ≤ 1 decile) by 10% of wf at a 10% wf cost to themselves. Sensitivity to punishment defines how many deciles (0–5) agents increase their generosity after being punished. In these simulations, sensitivity is systematically varied, and outcomes such as average generosity (Figure 2) and agent age distributions (Figure 7) are observed.",Integrates the key structural rules from Methods with the specific manipulation of sensitivity to punishment in the non-structured setting.,0.96
10.1023_B_ACBI.0000043440.36462.1a.md,Structured society – varying sensitivity to punishment,"Simulations with labor specialization where sensitivity to punishment is varied; used for Figures 8, 9, 10, 11.",0.87,Sensitivity to punishment in a structured society with division of labor (0–5 deciles of wf increase in generosity after punishment).,"Results: Figure 9 and 10 explicitly analyze different levels of susceptibility (sensitivity) to punishment in societies with division of labor (farmers, miners, traders).",0.9,False,Purely agent-based simulations.,1,False,No human or field experiment; computational study.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,"Sociodynamica model is used, as in other conditions.",1,False,Simulation rather than analytical derivation.,1,500,Figures 8 and 9 captions: “Age achieved by 500 agents...” and “The average level of generosity shown by 500 agents... in simulations... when agents had division of labor.”,1,100,Outcomes measured after 100 time-steps for these structured-society runs.,1,1,"Generosity is in deciles of wf (0–0.5), not a binary decision.",0.9,N/A,No initial endowment structure like a PGG; resource gathering is dynamic.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier structure is defined.,1,N/A,"No human communication, only algorithmic interactions.",1,N/A,No human interface or summary feedback.,1,N/A,No concept of identity revelation in the agent rules.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents do not view round counters.,1,1,Sensitivity to punishment is only meaningful because punishers exist; structured societies use altruistic punishment just as non-structured ones do.,1,0.1,Same 10% wf punishment cost rule applies across both structured and non-structured societies.,0.85,0.1,Punished agents lose the same fraction of wf as punishers pay.,0.7,0,No separate costly reward rule is introduced in these runs.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/R,Initial wealth is not numerically specified.,0.5,N/R,"Reported outcomes: average generosity, role composition, ages; no normalized contribution rate is given.",0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric donation amounts per agent or role are reported.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency metric relative to a defined maximum output is computed; only aggregate wealth (in some figures) and role distributions are shown.,0.4,N/R,"These runs emphasize age, generosity, and role composition; GDP-like aggregate wealth for different sensitivities is not tabulated in the text.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,Agents are artificial; their simulated age is not a demographic variable.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Pure simulation without human involvement.,1,"Structured society model with division of labor: farmers gather only food, miners only minerals, traders specialize in exchanging resources between farmers and miners. Traders add 50% value to minerals they trade, modeling productivity. Fundamental world and punishment rules are the same as in the non-structured case: resources in patches (200 food, 100 minerals), Brownian motion, food decay b=0.1, catastrophes mitigated by mineral wealth, donations from richer to poorer when wf differences exceed 2 units, generosity g in deciles of wf, low-generosity agents (g ≤ 1) punished by altruistic punishers at a cost of 10% wf to both parties. In these runs, sensitivity to punishment (0–5 deciles increase in generosity after punishment) is varied and effects on average generosity (Figure 9), agent ages (Figure 8), role composition (Figure 10), and generosity distributions (Figure 11) are observed. Increasing sensitivity leads to more farmers and fewer miners/traders and reduces the fraction of agents with zero generosity.",Combines the structured-society mechanics from Methods with reported findings specific to sensitivity-to-punishment variations.,0.96
10.1002_bdm.2050.md,No punishment condition (standard public goods game),"Paper explicitly contrasts three game versions: (i) without punishment opportunities, (ii) with peer punishment, (iii) with democratic peer punishment. This is the no-punishment version.",1,Punishment system: no punishment (baseline public goods game without any punishment stage),"Only difference across conditions is presence and form of punishment stage; here, no punishment stage is added.",1,True,Human participants played public goods games in the lab.,1,True,Controlled laboratory manipulation of punishment system with random order of versions.,1,True,Described as laboratory study with participants in cubicles using z-Tree at the university.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulation reported.,1,False,No formal mathematical model developed as a standalone method.,1,4,"“As in a typical public goods game, four players constituted one group.”",1,6,“Each game version was played for six periods…”. This refers to each of the three versions separately.,1,1,"Players were “free to choose how many” of 20 MUs to contribute; implies any integer amount, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each player is endowed with 20 MUs in private and chooses how many to contribute; initial public fund is zero.,1,0.4,Each MU contributed is multiplied by 1.6 and each of 4 players receives one-fourth: MPCR = 1.6/4 = 0.4.,1,0,"No communication between participants is mentioned; only anonymous, computer-mediated decisions.",0.9,1,"After contribution, each player is given information about contributions, payoff of each other player from the public good, and their period and total payoff.",1,0,No punishment exists in this version; anonymity emphasized and no identities revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists; anonymity conditions described.,1,1,Participants are told beforehand that each version has six periods and that there are three different versions; implies they know total periods.,0.9,0,This is explicitly the version “without punishment opportunities”.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,0,No mention of any positive reward mechanism in any version.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,“Each player was endowed with 20 money units (MUs).”,1,0.4046,Average contribution across six periods is 48.55 MUs per participant (Table 1). Max possible = 6 periods × 20 MUs = 120. Rate = 48.55 / 120 ≈ 0.4046.,0.95,48.55,"Table 1 lists “Contribution (in MUs)” for no punishment as 48.55, summed across six periods.",1,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure (payoff / max payoff) is reported or derivable without additional variance details.,0.2,596.52,Total income in MUs per participant across six periods is 149.13 (Table 1). Group of 4 → 149.13 × 4 = 596.52 group MUs.,0.95,Germany,Study conducted at Ulm University and RWTH Aachen University in Germany; typical subject pool is local students.,0.9,M_age = 22.88; individual age range N/R,Sample section reports mean age only.,1,59 women; 61 men (assuming binary coding); other genders N/R,"Sample: “120 participants (M_age=22.88; 59 women)” implies 61 others, presumably men.",0.9,Primarily university students; exact distribution N/R,Typical lab sample at universities; not explicitly characterized but implied.,0.6,On site,Laboratory study with participants in cubicles at the university; interactions via z-Tree.,1,"Within-subjects design: all 120 participants played three 6-period public goods games (no punishment, peer punishment, democratic peer punishment) in random order; groups of four kept constant in membership but player numbers randomly reassigned each period; payoffs in MUs converted to Euro cents (1 MU = €0.01).",Summarizes procedural details from Method section relevant to game structure across conditions.,1
10.1002_bdm.2050.md,Peer punishment condition (standard peer punishment system),Paper labels one version as “with peer punishment”; this row reflects that treatment.,1,Punishment system: peer punishment with unilateral costly punishment (1 MU spent reduces target payoff by 3 MUs; 0–10 MUs per other player per period),Description of peer punishment stage specifies cost and technology.,1,True,Human participants played this version in the lab as part of the within-subjects design.,1,True,Punishment system experimentally manipulated; this is one of the controlled conditions.,1,True,Same laboratory setting and procedures as other versions.,1,False,No computer-only simulation; all decisions by human subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical model presented as an experiment.,1,4,Four players constituted one group in all public goods games.,1,6,Each game version (including peer punishment) was played for six periods.,1,1,Same contribution choice structure as in the base public goods game; free to choose any number of MUs up to 20.,1,0,Endowment begins entirely in private account; contributions are voluntary.,1,0.4,"Same multiplication rule (each MU ×1.6, split among 4) applies across all versions.",1,0,No communication or chat channel is mentioned in any condition; anonymity and computer mediation only.,0.9,1,"After contribution, players saw each player's contribution, public-good payoff, and their own period and total payoff; after punishment, they saw how much they punished, how much they were punished, and updated payoffs.",1,0,Interactions are anonymous; no identities revealed with punishment information.,1,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Participants were informed that each of three versions has six periods; thus they know the horizon.,0.9,1,“In the game variant with the peer punishment system… participants were given the option to punish each other group member.”,1,1,Participants invest their own MUs between 0 and 10 for each player; cost is 1 MU per punishment MU invested.,1,3,“Investment of 2 MUs decreases the payoff of another by 6 MUs” → 3×.,1,0,No positive reward option is provided in the peer punishment description.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this condition.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this condition.,1,20,Same 20 MU endowment per period as base game.,1,0.5882,Average total contribution = 70.58 MUs over six periods (Table 1). Max = 120. Rate = 70.58 / 120 ≈ 0.5882.,0.95,70.58,Table 1 entry for “Contribution (in MUs)” under Peer punishment.,1,N/R,Normalized efficiency not directly reported; deriving it would require more detailed payoff composition than provided.,0.2,449.32,Total income per participant = 112.33 MUs (Table 1). Group payoff = 112.33 × 4 = 449.32.,0.95,Germany,Same participant pool across all three versions in the within-subjects design; conducted at German universities.,0.9,M_age = 22.88; individual age range N/R,Single sample plays all conditions.,1,59 women; 61 men (assuming binary coding); other genders N/R,Same 120-person sample across conditions.,0.9,Primarily university students; exact distribution N/R,Lab subject pool at Ulm University and RWTH Aachen; not further specified.,0.6,On site,Same laboratory environment as described for the overall study.,1,"After the contribution and feedback stage, a punishment stage is added: each participant can spend 0–10 MUs to punish each of the three other group members; each MU spent reduces the target’s payoff by 3 MUs; punishment decisions and outcomes are reported each period; groups of four remain compositionally fixed across all 18 periods but player numbers are reshuffled each period.",Summarizes aspects unique to peer punishment version relative to others.,1
10.1002_bdm.2050.md,Democratic peer punishment condition,Paper labels third version as “democratic peer punishment system”; this row reflects that treatment.,1,Punishment system: democratic peer punishment (peer punishment intentions followed by group vote; punishment executed only if a majority of other group members vote yes),"Description: participants state intended punishments, see aggregated intended punishment, then vote yes/no; punishment executed if simple majority in favor.",1,True,Condition is one of three versions played by human participants in the lab.,1,True,Punishment system (including democratic mechanics) is manipulated experimentally within subjects.,1,True,Conducted in same lab setting with z-Tree as other conditions.,1,False,No simulated agents; all decisions made by humans.,1,False,No standalone analytical modeling as a method.,1,4,Groups of four in all public goods game versions.,1,6,"Each of the three versions, including democratic peer punishment, played for six periods.",1,1,Same contribution decision as in standard public goods game: any amount up to 20 MUs.,1,0,Endowment begins in private account; contributions are voluntary.,1,0.4,"Identical multiplication mechanism (×1.6, split among 4) across all versions.",1,0,"As in other versions, no mention of communication channels between participants.",0.9,1,"Participants see contributions, public-good payoffs, intended punishment totals per player, number of yes votes, and executed punishment and payoffs.",1,0,Interactions under anonymous conditions with no identity information revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Participants know there are three versions and each has six periods.,0.9,1,"Democratic peer punishment is still a peer punishment system, with intended punishment decisions and potential execution.",1,1,"If there is a majority to execute punishment, “every player had to bear the costs of their own intended punishment”; cost per MU invested is 1 MU.",1,3,"Intended punishment is aggregated in MUs and multiplied by same punishment factor of 3 (e.g., 3 MUs intended → 9 MUs reduction).",1,0,No reward option described in democratic peer punishment system.,1,N/A,"Condition has punishment only, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Same per-period endowment of 20 MUs as in other versions.,1,0.629,Average contribution = 75.47 MUs over six periods (Table 1). Max = 120. Rate = 75.47 / 120 ≈ 0.629.,0.95,75.47,Table 1 gives “Contribution (in MUs)” for democratic peer punishment as 75.47 (sum over six periods).,1,N/R,No standardized efficiency ratio reported; approximations would require detailed period-wise payoff breakdowns.,0.2,530.4,Total income per participant = 132.60 MUs over six periods (Table 1). Group payoff = 132.60 × 4 = 530.4.,0.95,Germany,Same German lab sample across all versions.,0.9,M_age = 22.88; individual age range N/R,Single participant pool plays this condition as part of the within-subjects experiment.,1,59 women; 61 men (assuming binary coding); other genders N/R,Same 120-person sample participates in all three versions.,0.9,Primarily university students; exact distribution N/R,Laboratory sample drawn from Ulm University and possibly RWTH Aachen students; not further specified.,0.6,On site,Condition implemented in identical on-site lab environment.,1,"Democratic peer punishment extends peer punishment with a voting stage: after stating intended punishment for each group member, participants see aggregated intended punishment (converted with factor 3) for each other and then vote yes/no on executing this aggregate for each target; the target cannot vote on their own case; punishment is executed if a simple majority of the three eligible voters (at least 2 yes votes) supports it; if executed, each punisher pays the cost of their own intended punishment and the punished player’s payoff is reduced by total intended punishment ×3; extensive feedback on intended and executed punishment, votes, and payoffs is shown each period.",Summarizes key institutional details distinguishing democratic peer punishment from standard peer punishment.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0208129.md,"Symmetric VOD, n=2, no penalty (rounds 1–15)","Condition defined by symmetric costs K=25 for all victims, group size n=2, and no penalty P=0 in first 15 rounds per Table 1 and design description.",1,Game type: symmetric VOD (all victims K=25); Group size n=2; Penalty P=0,"Independent variables varied in 2x2 design: symmetry (symmetric vs asymmetric), group size (2 vs 5), and penalty phase (0 vs 60). This row is symmetric, n=2, no penalty.",1,True,Human subjects played computerized stealing game in a lab.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions and controlled manipulations in laboratory sessions.,1,True,Conducted in Decision Science Laboratory at ETH Zurich.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,Paper contains theoretical model but this row concerns the empirical experiment with humans.,1,3,"One thief X plus n=2 victims per group, so total 3 players in each round.",1,15,Condition defined as first 15 rounds (no penalty) of the 30-round game.,1,0,Victims have binary choice: sanction or not; no continuous contribution levels.,1,0,No public goods contribution; all endowment effectively starts private; sanction is a costly act not a reallocation of an initial public fund.,0.7,N/R,Volunteer’s dilemma with sanctioning; no linear public good with MPCR defined.,1,0,No mention of communication; computerized anonymous decisions only.,0.9,1,Decision screens necessarily show whether stolen money is reclaimed for all and payoffs; standard in such z-Tree implementations though exact wording not quoted.,0.6,0,Anonymity stressed; no indication that victim identities are revealed when sanctioning.,0.9,0,No reward option in game.,1,1,Laboratory repeated-game designs and instructions with screenshots almost always state number of rounds; they call them “30 consecutive rounds”.,0.7,1,Victims can sanction thief at cost K to reclaim stolen amount.,1,25,Symmetric VOD: sanctioning costs K=25 MU for all victims.,1,50,"One sanction reclaims U=50 MU per victim regardless of how many sanction; thus 25 MU cost yields 50 MU collective good per victim (for the focal victim, reclaiming 50).",0.8,0,Only negative sanctioning option is described; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Paper specifies stolen amounts and costs in MU but not per-round endowment or initial account balance.,0,0.47,"Individual sanctioning rate in symmetric n=2 without penalty is reported as 0.47 (Fig 3). Since choice is binary sanction/no sanction, this is the fraction of maximum possible sanctioning.",0.95,N/R,"Only probabilities/rates of sanctioning reported, not average number of sanctions in MU.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios (group payoff / maximum) reported.,0,N/R,Paper does not report mean payoffs by condition.,0,Switzerland,"Subjects were students from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich, both in Switzerland.",1,"Mean 22.7, SD 3.0",Reported average age 22.7 years (sd=3.00).,1,59.5% female,Paper states 59.5% of participants were female.,1,University students,Participants recruited from University of Zurich and ETH Zurich subject pool.,1,On site,Conducted in physical laboratory (DeSciL at ETH Zurich).,1,"Stealing game with one thief X and two victims; X can steal 50 MU from each victim; if X steals, each victim independently decides whether to sanction (cost 25 MU) to reclaim 50 MU for all victims; no additional penalty in these rounds; random role assignment and stranger matching each round.",Summarizes description in Experimental game and Experimental design for this specific parameterization.,1
