10.1109_jstsp.2013.2263785.md,Simulation – Protocol 1 (designed reputation protocol),Section VI.A describes three protocols; Protocol 1 is the newly designed reputation protocol evaluated via simulation.,1,"Incentive protocol type = Protocol 1 (optimal reputation protocol from Theorem 2); cost c ∈ {1}, benefit b ∈ {3,5}, report error probability ε ∈ {0.01,0.05}; discount factor δ = 0.8; number of users N = 1000","Independent variables in the comparison are protocol (1 vs 2 vs 3), b, and ε; c, δ, N are fixed across conditions. This row corresponds to Protocol 1.",0.96,False,No human subjects; community evolution is simulated numerically.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a numerical experiment/simulation.,1,False,No lab or human environment; pure simulation.,1,True,"Section VI explicitly describes running experiments with N=1000 users interacting for 10^8 periods under each protocol, indicating a computer simulation.",1,False,"While the paper has analytical results, this condition refers specifically to the numerical simulation.",1,1000,“We deploy a number of N=1000 users in the community...” in Section VI.,1,100000000,“users in the community interact for 10^8 periods under each protocol.”,1,1,"Each period the server’s action z ∈ {0,1} is whether to supply the service at cost c; there is no divisible contribution level, so contribute-or-not is effectively all-or-nothing gift giving (binary provision). In this schema 1 denotes that players can contribute any amount; here they cannot, so the correct coding is 0. However the instructions force 1 or 0 based on contribution discreteness; since the action is binary (all-or-nothing), we set 0.",0.7,N/A,"There is no endowment allocation decision between public and private accounts; the game is a service provision (gift-giving) with cost and benefit, not a public goods contribution.",1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined; the interaction is a two-player asymmetric gift-giving game with fixed b and c, not a linear public goods game.",1,N/A,No human subjects; chat/communication among simulated agents is not described as a configurable feature.,1,N/R,Simulation description does not specify if agents observe summaries of others’ earnings; only that they know the community state.,0.4,N/R,"Identities are not modeled; only reputations are visible, and no explicit ID revelation for punishment is discussed.",0.6,N/R,"As with punishment, only reputations matter; no explicit modeling of identity revelation for rewards.",0.6,N/R,"Agents are modeled as infinitely repeated, farsighted decision makers; the simulation horizon 10^8 is an implementation detail, not information shown to agents.",0.7,1,Reputation scheme includes degradation to 0 with probability β when deviating from the social rule; this is a punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,"Punishment is implemented via reputation lowering, not via agents spending costly punishment points.",1,N/A,"No monetary/utility multiplier per punishment unit; punishment is probabilistic reputation reset, not parametric costly punishment.",1,1,"Compliance leads to reputation increase (reward) and better treatment in future, so a reward component exists via reputation scheme.",0.9,N/A,Reward via reputation increase is system-defined and costless for other agents; no costly peer rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit per-unit reward technology; the effect is through increased reputation state.,1,N/A,Stage game is cost c to provide service and benefit b to the other; no token endowment defined as in public goods games.,1,N/R,Outcome reported is average social welfare normalized by b−c; no direct contribution fraction is provided.,0.5,N/R,"They report only average social welfare over time, not average number of cooperative actions per period.",0.5,0.9328,"Table II: “Average Social Welfare ... normalized by the social optimum b−c” for Protocol 1 at c=1,b=3,ε=0.01 is 0.9328, which matches the definition of efficiency as realized welfare divided by maximum welfare.",1,N/R,Only normalized social welfare is reported; the raw group payoff is not provided.,0.5,N/A,No human participants; agents are simulated.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Community of users is modeled and simulated algorithmically; no recruitment or physical environment.,1,"Asymmetric gift-giving stage game between a server and a client with benefit b > 0 to the client and cost c > 0 to the server if z=1; users are randomly matched every period with uniform random matching; reputation set Θ={0,1} in simulations; reputation protocol parameters L=H=1 and β chosen in [(c/(bδ)), β_critical); users have discount factor δ=0.8; stochastic report errors with probability ε.","Summarizes the core game and protocol structure described in Sections II, V, and VI relevant for the simulation.",0.98
10.1109_jstsp.2013.2263785.md,Simulation – Protocol 2 (prior reputation protocol),Section VI.A: Protocol 2 is the reputation protocol designed in prior work [7]; evaluated alongside Protocols 1 and 3.,1,"Incentive protocol type = Protocol 2 (reputation protocol from prior work [7]); cost c ∈ {1}, benefit b ∈ {3,5}, report error probability ε ∈ {0.01,0.05}; discount factor δ = 0.8; number of users N = 1000",Same simulation environment; only the protocol changes from Protocol 1 to Protocol 2.,0.96,False,"Simulation with artificial agents, no humans.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical experiments only.,1,False,No physical lab or participants.,1,True,"Performance is evaluated “in our experiments” for N=1000 users interacting for 10^8 periods, indicating simulation.",1,False,This row concerns only simulation results for Protocol 2.,1,1000,VI: N=1000 users for the community in the experiments.,1,100000000,VI.A: “interact for 10^8 periods under each protocol.”,1,0,"Same underlying gift-giving game as Protocol 1 with binary decision to serve or not; per schema, this is all-or-nothing, coded 0.",0.7,N/A,No endowment/public good framing.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction or chat described.,1,N/R,Not specified whether agents see payoff/punishment summaries beyond community state.,0.4,N/R,Identities are not modeled explicitly; only reputations matter.,0.6,N/R,Same as punishment; not discussed.,0.6,N/R,Agents treat the interaction as indefinitely repeated; horizon visibility not described.,0.7,1,"Protocol 2 is a reputation-based indirect reciprocity protocol from [7], which also uses reputation updates including punishment.",0.9,N/A,"Reputation-based, not costly peer punishment.",0.9,N/A,No numeric punishment multiplier specified; punishment is embedded in reputation transitions.,0.9,1,Reputation protocols typically reward compliance via better future treatment; [7] is similar in spirit.,0.8,N/A,"Reward is via reputation, not vi