ity of paying to punish other players was present'.,1,N/R,Narrative does not specify fee-to-fine ratio per punishment point.,0.1,N/R,Magnitude of reduction of target’s payoff per punishment unit not reported.,0.1,0,Only punishment is described; no costly reward option.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,20,Punishment game is described as a variation of the 20-point public goods setup; Fehr & Gächter (2002) also typically used similar magnitudes.,0.7,N/R,"Only qualitative statement: 'deterioration ... is vastly diminished and, in fact, cooperation may increase'; no numeric rate.",0.2,N/R,No specific average token contributions reported.,0.1,N/R,No total payoff vs maximum payoff data provided.,0.1,N/R,No quantitative payoff outcomes given.,0.1,N/R,"The summarized description here does not state the country of the experimental subjects, although the underlying Fehr & Gächter study was European; not specified in this article.",0.3,N/R,No demographic details provided.,0.1,N/R,No demographic details provided.,0.1,N/R,Subjects not characterized as students or other populations in this summary.,0.1,On site,Standard economic lab implementation; article does not mention online or field context.,0.8,Ten-round linear public goods game with standard contribution stage followed by a costly punishment stage in which subjects can pay to reduce the payoffs of low contributors; 84.3% punished at least once and 74.2% of punishments targeted below-average contributors.,Summarizes structural and outcome details explicitly stated.,0.95
