ographics given.,0.1,N/R,No demographics given.,0.1,N/R,"Populations not described (e.g., undergraduates vs others) in this summary.",0.1,On site,Canonical WST studies are run in lab/classroom environments; online not discussed.,0.7,"Social-contract Wason Selection Task with four cards representing combinations of 'benefit accepted/not accepted' and 'cost paid/not paid'; rule is 'If you take the benefit then you pay the cost'. Performance is up to ten times better than in abstract WST, interpreted as evidence for cheater-detection mechanisms.",Direct summary of structure and main empirical pattern reported.,0.95
10.5840_beq200919433.md,"Ultimatum game – one-shot, anonymous interaction",Section 'The Ultimatum Game' describes a standard one-shot ultimatum game with anonymous proposer and responder splitting amount m.,0.95,Game structure: one-shot anonymous ultimatum bargaining over amount m with free choice of offer by proposer,Main structural independent features are one-shot vs repeated and anonymity; here one-shot and anonymous are emphasized.,0.9,True,Described as experiments involving two players dividing real money.,1,True,Controlled bargaining game with fixed rules and payoffs.,1,True,"Ultimatum games as described are standard lab experiments; no field implementation (e.g., in markets) is mentioned.",0.9,False,Human participants interacting over money.,1,False,"Description is empirical; theory is conceptual, not formal proof.",1,2,"Explicitly: 'The ultimatum game is a two-stage game, usually involving two participants'.",1,1,Described as a 'one-shot Prisoners' Dilemma' analogue and 'one-shot ultimatum game'; only one interaction.,1,1,"Proposer may 'offer any portion of m', implying continuous choices between 0 and m, not binary.",1,0,All of m is under proposer's control initially; nothing is automatically allocated to responder.,0.9,N/A,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return concept.,1,0,Players are 'unknown to one another' and no communication phase is described.,0.9,0,Only responder knows the proposer’s offer; no group summaries.,0.9,0,"Rejection of offers is implicit 'punishment', but identities are anonymous and there is only dyadic knowledge.",0.7,0,Acceptance of offer is implicit 'reward'; no identity revelation beyond dyad.,0.7,1,Described as one-shot; participants must know there will be no future rounds.,0.8,1,"Responder can 'punish' unfair offers by rejecting them, at a cost to self; this is central to interpretation as altruistic punishment.",1,N/R,Cost to punish depends on m and offered share; no specific numeric parameters provided.,0.2,N/R,Effect of rejection on proposer’s payoff (loss of their share) is proportional to offer; no fixed-per-unit tech described.,0.2,1,"Responder can 'reward' fair offers by accepting, granting proposer their share; discussed as altruistic rewarding/punishment structure.",0.8,N/R,"Accepting fair offer is usually beneficial to responder, not costly; no explicit cost per reward unit.",0.2,N/R,"Acceptance transmits offered share, but no unit-based reward rate is defined.",0.2,N/R,"Game uses generic 'amount of money, m'; no actual currency amount given.",0.1,N/A,Not a contribution-to-public-good structure.,1,N/R,"Offers are usually expressed as percentages (e.g., 40–50% of m); no absolute amounts reported.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency metrics reported.,0.1,N/R,"Typical sums of payoffs are not reported; only qualitative patterns (rejection rates, modal offers).",0.1,"Multiple (e.g., East Africa, Papua New Guinea, Indonesia, Central Asia, South America, Russia, Switzerland, Hungary, Germany, Austria, Netherlands)","Text: 'Experimental evidence ... has been found in ... East Africa, Papua New Guinea, Indonesia, Central Asia, South America ... Russia, Switzerland, Hungary, Germany, Austria, and the Netherlands.'",1,N/R,"Ages are not reported; some samples are small-scale societies, others lab participants.",0.1,N/R,No gender breakdown given.,0.1,Mixed (small-scale societies and participants from industrialized countries),"Text notes 'vast variety of cultural and economic situations' including small-scale societies and multiple countries, implying heterogeneous education levels.",0.6,On site,Ultimatum games in small-scale societies and labs are physically administered; field-like but still experimental; 'Field experiment' not explicitly used.,0.7,"Proposer offers a share of m; modal offers around 50%; offers below 25% have high rejection rates; responders often reject offers as high as 30%, interpreted as strong reciprocity (altruistic punishment) in a one-shot anonymous interaction.",Direct summary of structural and empirical features reported.,0.95
