10.5840_beq200919433.md,Moonlighting game – standard two-stage version,"Section 'The Moonlighting Game' defines a two-stage game with Players A and B, each starting with 12 points, with A choosing between -6 and +6 and B choosing between -6 and +18.",0.95,"Player A’s initial transfer choice in [-6, +6]; Player B’s subsequent transfer choice in [-6, +18]",Key varied decision variables that define conditions subjects face; experiments then analyze how B responds to A’s choice.,0.9,True,Game used in experiments with human participants to study reciprocity.,1,True,Controlled experimental game with fixed payoff functions.,1,True,Moonlighting games are standard economic lab tasks; no external field deployment mentioned.,0.9,False,Human-subject experiment.,1,False,Used empirically to test hypotheses about reciprocity and intentions.,1,2,Explicitly 'two-stage game involving two players'.,1,1,Described as a one-shot game: B’s choice follows A’s single action; no repetition mentioned.,0.9,1,"Players choose any integer in specified ranges, not a binary all-or-nothing choice.",1,0,Each player starts with own 12-point endowment; no shared pool.,0.9,N/A,Not a public-goods framework.,1,0,No communication between A and B is described.,0.9,0,Players only observe each other’s transfers; no summaries.,0.9,0,Punishment is implicit when B chooses negative values; identities remain anonymous within the dyad.,0.7,0,Reward (positive transfers) occurs within anonymous dyad.,0.7,1,One-shot; participants know there is a single interaction.,0.8,1,"Player B can choose a negative number, losing points and imposing three times that loss on A.",1,1,"When B selects -k, B loses k points; cost per punishment unit (in absolute terms) is 1 for B.",0.9,3,"When B selects -k, A loses 3k points; each unit of B’s cost reduces A’s payoff by 3.",0.95,1,"Both A and B can choose positive numbers, transferring points beneficially to the other.",1,1,"For positive choice x, the sender loses x points (A) or y points (B).",0.95,3,"If A chooses +x, B gains 3x points; A’s unit of cost creates 3 units of benefit. For B, positive transfers to A are 1:1, but the key 'multiplier' structure applies to A’s positive transfers.",0.8,12,"Explicit: 'In the first stage, each player receives 12 points.'",1,N/A,No contribution to a common pool; transfers are bilateral.,1,N/R,No specific average transfer sizes reported.,0.1,N/R,No numerical results on total surplus vs potential surplus.,0.1,N/R,No empirical outcome statistics provided.,0.1,N/R,Country or lab location not specified.,0.1,N/R,Demographics not reported.,0.1,N/R,Demographics not reported.,0.1,N/R,Population (students vs others) not described.,0.1,On site,Moonlighting games are standard incentive-compatible lab experiments; no online/field mention.,0.8,"Player A’s positive transfers signal trust/intentions and are tripled for B; Player B can respond by rewarding (positive transfers) or punishing (negative transfers that cost self and harm A threefold). Used by Falk, Fehr, and Fischbacher (2002) to disentangle the role of intentions vs outcomes in reciprocal behavior.",Summarizes structure and its application as described in the text.,0.95
10.5840_beq200919433.md,Trust game – baseline version without punishment option,"Section 'Trust Games' defines a two-player game where Player A transfers x, it is multiplied (e.g., by 3), and Player B returns y; no punishment option.",0.95,Presence of punishment option: absent; Player A chooses transfer x and desired-back transfer; Player B chooses actual back-transfer y,Baseline condition contrasted later with 'Trust With Punishment' where a fine can be imposed.,0.95,True,Described as involving human players with monetary endowments.,1,True,Controlled incentive-compatible game.,1,True,Fehr & List (2004) used lab-like settings with CEOs and students.,0.9,False,Human participants only.,1,False,"Experimental description, not a purely analytical model.",1,2,Game involves 'principal' (A) and 'agent' (B).,1,1,Standard trust game here is single-shot; repeated interaction is not mentioned.,0.8,1,Player A can transfer any amount x from 0 to 10; continuous choice.,1,0,Each player starts with own endowment; transfers are voluntary.,0.9,N/A,Not a symmetric public goods situation.,1,0,No communication between principal and agent described.,0.9,0,Only dyadic information (transfer and back-transfer) is known to players.,0.9,0,No explicit punishment option; only voluntary back-transfer by B.,0.95,0,Trust and back-transfer occur within anonymous dyad; no identity broadcast.,0.7,1,Game is one-shot; participants know payoff after one interaction.,0.8,0,No explicit punitive mechanism; B simply chooses y.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment step.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment step.,1,1,B’s positive back-transfer rewards A’s trust; this is central to measuring positive reciprocity.,0.95,1,"If B transfers y, B’s payoff decreases by y (10+3x−y); cost per rewarded unit is 1.",0.95,1,Each unit B transfers increases A’s payoff by 1 (10−x+y).,0.95,10,"Explicit example: 'both players are given an initial monetary endowment (e.g., 10 monetary units)'.",0.9,N/A,Not a public goods contribution setting.,1,N/R,No specific average transfers x reported; only that substantial positive reciprocity was observed in prior work.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoffs or efficiency metrics reported.,0.1,N/R,No numerical outcome data provided.,0.1,N/R,"Fehr & List’s article settings (e.g., country of CEOs) not repeated here.",0.2,N/R,Only that one group was 'CEOs' and another 'undergraduate students'; ages not specified.,0.3,N/R,No gender details provided.,0.1,Mixed (CEOs and undergraduate students),Text: 'Using subject pools that included seventy-six CEOs and 126 undergraduate students'.,1,On site,Trust game run with CEOs and students in experimental sessions; no indication of online administration.,0.8,"Player A transfers x (0–10) to B; experimenter multiplies by factor (e.g., 3); B chooses back-transfer y, determining final payoffs 10−x+y for A and 10+3x−y for B. Observed strong positive reciprocity even with high stakes in related studies.",Summarizes game structure and key conceptual outcome.,0.95
10.5840_beq200919433.md,"Trust game with punishment option (Trust With Punishment, Fehr & List 2004)","Section 'An Application of Trust Games' describes a variant where Player A can impose a fixed fine (e.g., 4) on Player B if back-transfer is below the desired level.",0.95,Presence of punishment option: present; A chooses whether punishment option is used when B’s back-transfer y < desired-back transfer,"Experiment compares trust game with and without punishment option, and within punishment condition compares use vs non-use.",0.95,True,Fehr & List studied real CEOs and students in this game.,1,True,Controlled lab-like experiment with manipulated presence/use of punishment.,1,True,Conducted as experimental sessions; no field implementation described.,0.9,False,Real human participants.,1,False,"Empirical design, not purely analytical.",1,2,Same principal-agent dyad as baseline trust game.,1,1,Described in single-shot terms; no repeated rounds noted.,0.8,1,Same as baseline trust game; A chooses any transfer amount x.,1,0,Each player retains own endowment unless transfers occur.,0.9,N/A,Not a public goods setting.,1,0,No communication between A and B is described.,0.9,0,Dyadic game only.,0.9,0,Use of fine is known only within dyad; no identity broadcasting.,0.7,0,Reward is back-transfer y; no identity broadcasting.,0.7,1,Subjects effectively know interaction is single-shot.,0.8,1,"Text: 'Player A is able to impose a fixed fine (e.g., 4) ... if the actual back-transfer falls short of the desired one'.",1,0,"Imposing fine reduces B’s payoff by 4 but does not alter A’s payoff expression beyond any behavioral response; no direct material cost to A is specified, so per-unit cost to punisher is 0.",0.7,4,Each activation of punishment reduces B’s payoff by a fixed 4 units.,0.9,1,"As in baseline trust game, B’s back-transfer rewards A’s trust.",0.95,1,B’s payoff decreases by y for each unit returned (10+3x−y).,0.95,1,Each unit B returns increases A’s payoff by 1.,0.95,10,"Same 10-unit endowment as baseline trust game, per the example.",0.9,N/A,Not a public goods contribution setting.,1,N/R,"No exact average transfers x or y reported; only directional effects (back-transfers higher with punishment option, and higher when A does not use punishment) are described.",0.2,N/R,No aggregated payoff data.,0.1,N/R,No numeric payoffs presented.,0.1,N/R,Fehr & List (2004) country context not repeated here.,0.2,N/R,Only groups identified as 'CEOs' and 'undergraduate students'; ages not specified.,0.3,N/R,Gender not reported.,0.1,Mixed (CEOs and undergraduate students),Same samples as baseline trust game: seventy-six CEOs and 126 undergraduates.,1,On site,Game run in controlled sessions with CEOs and students; online/field administration not mentioned.,0.8,"Payoffs: A: 10−x+y; B: 10+3x−y, minus fixed fine 4 if y < desired and A chooses punishment. Presence of punishment option increased back-transfers compared to no-punishment baseline, but within punishment condition, back-transfers were higher when A chose not to punish, interpreted as a 'hidden cost of incentives' and trust-enhancing effect of foregoing punishment.",Summarizes structural modification and main empirical findings from Fehr & List as described.,0.95
