10.1093_aler_aht010.md,Law-S (with sanctions),"Second experiment treatment Law-S: norm questions, legal frame, and sanctions.",1,Sanctioning opportunity and normative framing: Law-S (norm questions plus legal frame about customary law) with costly peer sanctions (1:1 cost-to-damage ratio),"Combines norm elicitation, legal frame, and sanctions; contrasts with Baseline-S and Norm-S.",1,True,Human-subject experimental treatment.,1,True,Randomized between-subjects design with explicit treatment manipulations.,1,True,Conducted at Bonn EconLab.,1,False,No non-human simulations mentioned.,1,False,Not a pure theoretical model.,1,4,"Instructions: participants randomly divided into groups of four, with members labeled 1–4.",1,30,Instructions: experiment consists of thirty periods.,1,1,Contribution is any integer 0–20 taler.,1,0,"Each period, endowment of 20 taler is entirely under the subject's control until they choose to contribute some portion.",1,0.4,Income from project equals total contributions times 0.4; with 4 players this is the marginal per capita return.,1,0,General rule: absolutely no communication between participants.,1,1,Stage 3: participants see how much each other group member contributed ‘in this period’.,1,1,"Subjects assign points to specific ‘group member 1’, ‘group member 2’, etc.; thus identities (anonymized by fixed number) of punishment targets are shown.",1,0,No reward or positive transfer mechanism in any stage.,1,1,Thirty periods explicitly announced at beginning.,1,1,Stage 3: possibility to ‘reduce the other players' income by distributing points’.,1,1,Each point distributed costs the sender 1 taler.,1,1,Each point reduces the recipient’s income by 1 taler.,1,0,Only negative sanctions implemented.,1,N/A,No reward system.,1,N/A,No reward system.,1,20,"Stage 1: ‘At the beginning of each period, each participant is given 20 taler’.",1,N/R,No normalized fraction summary by treatment is published.,0,N/R,Regression models and figures show relative differences but not a simple mean contribution for Law-S.,0,N/R,"Although average earnings are given, efficiency ratios vs. social optimum are not computed.",0,N/R,"Only mean individual payoff in Law-S (15.19€) is provided, not per-group totals.",0.1,Germany,Same Bonn EconLab setting in Germany.,0.7,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition stated for the second experiment sample of 72 students.,0,University students,Described as students; likely same university subject pool.,0.9,On site,Same EconLab infrastructure and in-person sessions.,1,"Three stages per period: Stage 1 public good contribution with 20-taler endowment and MPCR 0.4; Stage 2 norm elicitation (existence of norm, expected minimum 0–20) plus informational buttons showing past behavior; Stage 3 feedback on current contributions and decentralized peer sanctions using costly points with 1:1 cost and damage. Legal frame explains emergence of customary law from sufficiently similar behavior and that such norms are valid and binding.",Directly summarized from Law-S instructions in Appendix.,1
10.15195_v6.a14.md,"T1 – Automatic label revelation, no norm-enforcement stage",Condition T1 in Part 2: labels automatically revealed each round; no punishment/penalty stage.,1,Norm-enforcement stage absent; label revelation automatic,"Across Part 2 conditions, the presence of a norm-enforcement stage is varied (T1 vs T3), with label revelation method held (automatic).",1,True,Human student subjects in a computerized laboratory experiment.,1,True,"Random assignment to conditions, controlled payoffs, manipulation of punishment option.",1,True,"Conducted at Bologna Laboratory for Experiments in Social Science, on-site in a computer lab.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper includes theoretical analysis but T1 is an empirical treatment, not purely analytical.",1,2,Venture game is played in pairs: 'pairs of subjects interact in the VG'.,1,9,Part 2: 'subjects play 9 rounds of the VG' in both T1 and T3.,1,1,"Players choose among stay out, trust and cooperate, or trust and defect, not a continuous contribution; coded as ability to choose any strategic action, but not an all-or-nothing contribution public good. Using 1 as they are not forced into a single action.",0.4,0,Not a contribution game with endowments; no default allocation between private and public accounts. Set to 0 to indicate no automatic public contribution.,0.3,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined/reported.,1,0,Instructions and design mention no communication; only strategy method and on-screen decisions.,0.9,1,"After each round in T3/T4 partners are told partner’s VG decision and outcome; by design same VG feedback structure applies, so payoff/outcome info is shown.",0.7,0,No punishment stage exists in T1.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,0,"Subjects are told total rounds will be between 7 and 10, not the exact number; remaining rounds are not displayed.",0.9,0,Explicitly: in T1 there is no norm-enforcement stage and no penalty option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in T1.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in T1.,1,0,No mention of any costly positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Trust/venture game with fixed payoffs T,R,P,S; no per-round monetary endowment reported.",1,N/R,"No contribution measure; main DVs are trust/stay out/cooperate/defect decisions, but not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No continuous contribution variable in the venture game.,1,N/R,They discuss Pareto efficiency conceptually but do not report realized efficiency values.,1,N/R,Average or total group payoffs per condition are not numerically reported.,1,Italy,Experiment at University of Bologna with student subjects; implies Italy.,0.95,"Mean 24.7 years, SD 4.31",Reported: 'Subjects were 54 percent female and 24.7 years old on average (sd=4.31)'.,1,54% female,Reported aggregate gender share for full sample; conditions share same pool via random assignment.,0.95,University students,Subjects were 'student subjects from University of Bologna'.,1,On site,Conducted in the Bologna laboratory with in-person sessions.,1,"One-shot venture game implemented via strategy method over 9 rounds with random stranger matching. Payoffs: T = €8.5, R = €6.5, P = €4.5, S = −€1. Labels (dove/hawk) assigned from Part 1 based on defection frequency; in T1 labels are automatically revealed each round; earnings for this part based on one randomly selected round.",Summarizes design details specific to the condition and general venture game parameters.,1
10.15195_v6.a14.md,"T2 – Voluntary label revelation, no norm-enforcement stage",Condition T2 in Part 3: players can choose whether to reveal label; no punishment stage.,1,Norm-enforcement stage absent; label revelation voluntary,"Across Part 3 conditions, presence of norm-enforcement stage varies (T2 vs T4) with revelation choice held (voluntary).",1,True,Same human laboratory experiment with student subjects.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of information-sharing and punishment options.,1,True,Run in Bologna experimental economics lab.,1,False,No non-human simulation.,1,False,This is an empirical treatment.,1,2,Pairs of subjects interact in the venture game.,1,9,Part 3: 'Subjects play 9 rounds of VG' in both T2 and T4.,1,1,"Strategic choices are discrete (stay out, trust/cooperate, trust/defect) rather than a divisible contribution amount.",0.4,0,No endowment splitting into accounts; not a contribution game.,0.3,N/R,Not a linear public goods setting.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,1,"As in other treatments, players are informed of partner’s VG decision and outcome each round before any (hypothetical) penalty stage; in T2 there is no penalty but VG outcome feedback still occurs.",0.7,0,No punishment stage exists in T2.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Exact number of rounds not disclosed; only range 7–10.,0.9,0,Explicitly: T2 is 'without a norm-enforcement stage'.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No positive reward option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game defined via outcome payoffs T,R,P,S; no per-round endowment mentioned.",1,N/R,"Outcome variables are decisions to stay out, trust/cooperate, trust/defect, and revelation choices, not contributions.",1,N/R,No token contributions.,1,N/R,No numeric efficiency metrics reported by treatment.,1,N/R,No average group payoff reported for T2.,1,Italy,Same subject pool: University of Bologna students.,0.95,"Mean 24.7 years, SD 4.31",Aggregate demographics for the 186 participants.,1,54% female,"Same pool, randomly assigned to conditions.",0.95,University students,All participants are students.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at Bologna.,1,"Same venture game as other parts, but in T2 each round includes an information-sharing stage where subjects can choose to reveal their 'dove' or 'hawk' label. No penalty can be inflicted in this condition; SCI exists only as information about past behavior. Random stranger matching; earnings based on one randomly selected round of this part.",Summarizes details distinctive to voluntary revelation without punishment.,1
10.15195_v6.a14.md,T3 – Automatic label revelation with norm-enforcement stage,"Condition T3 in Part 2: labels automatically revealed, and hawks can be punished at end of each round.",1,Norm-enforcement stage present; label revelation automatic,Contrasts with T1 (no enforcement) while holding automatic revelation constant.,1,True,Human subjects in a lab experiment.,1,True,"Random assignment, explicit treatments, controlled incentives.",1,True,Conducted at Bologna lab.,1,False,No simulated agents.,1,False,This row describes the empirical T3 treatment.,1,2,Game is between two partners each round.,1,9,Part 2: 'Subjects play 9 rounds of VG' in T1 and T3.,1,1,Discrete strategic actions; no gradual contribution level.,0.4,0,No endowment-based contribution structure.,0.3,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,0.9,1,In T3 partners learn each other's VG decision and outcome before the punishment option; this is feedback on others’ outcomes.,0.9,1,"Punishment decisions are directed at the current partner, whose identity (as the paired partner) and label are known; punishment is inherently partner-identified.",0.8,0,No reward option.,1,0,Only a 7–10 rounds range announced; exact remaining rounds not shown.,0.9,1,Explicitly: 'hawks (and only hawks) can be inflicted a penalty ... at the end of each round'.,1,0.5,Inflicting the penalty costs the punisher €0.50.,1,4,Penalty is €4 imposed on punished hawk per punishment decision.,1,0,No positive payoff-increasing action for the partner.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Only outcome payoffs T,R,P,S and penalties are specified.",1,N/R,"DV is rate of trust/cooperation/defection, not fractional contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution choice.,1,N/R,No numeric efficiency outcomes by condition reported.,1,N/R,Mean group payoffs not provided.,1,Italy,Same Italian student subject pool.,0.95,"Mean 24.7 years, SD 4.31",Overall sample demographics apply.,1,54% female,"Same sample, randomized into T1/T3.",0.95,University students,Subjects are students at University of Bologna.,1,On site,Laboratory setting at Bologna.,1,"Venture game over 9 rounds with strategy method. Labels from Part 1 automatically revealed at start of each round. After learning partner’s VG decision and outcome, players can inflict a €4 penalty on hawk partners at a personal cost of €0.50. A conditional punishment choice ('no unless my partner chooses yes') allows creation of mutual assured destruction between two hawks.",Summarizes key elements specific to T3’s enforcement and conditional punishment.,1
10.15195_v6.a14.md,T4 – Voluntary label revelation with norm-enforcement stage,Condition T4 in Part 3: players choose whether to reveal label; hawks who reveal can be punished.,1,Norm-enforcement stage present; label revelation voluntary,Contrasts with T2 (no enforcement) while both have voluntary label revelation.,1,True,Human participants in controlled lab setting.,1,True,Manipulation of punishment and revelation chance with random assignment.,1,True,Run in an on-site laboratory at University of Bologna.,1,False,No computational simulation used for this condition.,1,False,Empirical treatment; theoretical analysis in appendix separate.,1,2,Players interact in pairs each round.,1,9,Part 3 specifies 9 rounds for conditions T2 and T4.,1,1,Discrete trust/cooperate/defect decisions.,0.4,0,No contribution endowment game.,0.3,N/R,MPCR not defined for this trust/venture game.,1,0,Design does not permit or mention communication.,0.9,1,Players see partner’s VG decision and outcome before deciding on penalty.,0.9,1,Punishment is targeted at the known current partner whose label/revelation decision is observed.,0.8,0,No rewarding.,1,0,Only a range of total rounds announced; no exact count visible.,0.9,1,Explicitly: 'Same as T2 but Hawks who reveal their label can be inflicted a penalty'.,1,0.5,Penalty imposer pays €0.50.,1,4,Revealed hawk suffers €4 penalty if punished.,1,0,No reward for partners is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Per-round endowment not specified; only outcome payoffs and penalties.,1,N/R,"Primary DVs are trust/cooperate/defect and reveal/not reveal, not numeric contributions.",1,N/R,No token choice.,1,N/R,Efficiency not numerically summarized for T4.,1,N/R,Group payoff measures are not reported by treatment.,1,Italy,Same University of Bologna student pool.,0.95,"Mean 24.7 years, SD 4.31",Overall sample stats; no evidence of systematic age differences by treatment.,1,54% female,Same sample composition across randomized treatments.,0.95,University students,All subjects are students.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at Bologna.,1,"In T4, each of 9 rounds includes an information-sharing stage where subjects decide whether to reveal their dove/hawk label. Only hawks who reveal can be punished after the VG if both chose trust. Punishment is €4 to the hawk at a cost of €0.50 to the punisher; conditional punishment option ('no unless my partner chooses yes') allows mutually assured destruction when both are revealed hawks and both trusted.",Captures how SCI is deliberately invoked: voluntary revelation plus punishment option specific to revealed hawks.,1
10.1016_s0167-2681(00)00159-1.md,"Static tournaments – baseline production function, five elementary programs, varying maximum sessions (1–20)","Section 3.1 describes a set of round-robin simulations with five behavioral programs under the baseline payoff function (F=2, Ci=1) and varying maximum number of production sessions from 1 to 20.",1,"Maximum possible number of production sessions for each team (1–20) under baseline joint-production technology (F=2, Ci=1); population of available programs fixed to five elementary strategies (Cooperator, Free-Rider, Tit-for-Tat, Prudence, Hit-and-Run).",They vary the potential duration (maximum sessions) while holding the payoff function and set of five programs fixed.,1,False,No human subjects; outcomes generated by computer-simulated programs.,1,False,This is a numerical simulation study rather than a controlled human experiment or field study.,1,False,No laboratory with human participants; all results from simulations.,1,True,They explicitly describe these as computer simulations of round-robin tournaments among programmed strategies.,1,False,"Although they present payoff functions, the core results here come from numerical tournaments, not closed-form analysis.",1,3,They repeatedly state these are three-person joint enterprises/teams.,1,20,They run 20 separate tournaments while varying the maximum sessions from 1 to 20; the upper bound (20) is the maximum possible sessions parameter they analyze in this block.,0.6,0,"Each player chooses binary actions (cooperate=1 unit of labor or defect=0; participate or exit), so contributions are all-or-nothing rather than a continuous amount.",1,N/A,"There is no human endowment of tokens; players choose labor effort and participation in a production function, so the public-goods default allocation concept does not apply.",1,2,"Total output Q=2 * sum(Li); each unit of Li raises team output by 2, split equally among 3 players. Marginal per capita return is 2/3 per player, but in public-goods terminology MPCR is often per unit to the group; here we record the per-unit group multiplier (2).",0.4,N/A,No human communication; strategies are hard-coded programs.,1,N/A,No human information display; only algorithmic updates.,1,N/A,"Punishment/exit are embedded in strategy rules, not chosen by identifiable human players.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with identities; only payoff consequences of strategies.,1,N/A,No human subjects with interfaces; the concept of displaying remaining rounds does not apply.,1,1,"Sanctions occur via exit and strategy-based defection responses (e.g., Tit-for-Tat turning to defection, Prudence/Hit-and-Run exiting when payoff is negative). These mechanisms reduce others’ future payoffs and serve as punishment in the model.",0.7,N/R,"Under the five-strategy baseline, punishment is via exit or defection, not a parameterized per-unit cost like in Targeted Retribution; no numeric punishment cost per unit is specified.",0.3,N/R,No explicit per-unit punishment technology (tokens-to-loss) is defined for these five strategies.,0.3,0,No separate costly reward or bonus mechanism is modeled; only cooperation in production.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/R,"They model payoffs from labor and fixed costs, not per-round individual endowments of tokens; no fixed endowment per period is specified.",0.2,N/R,"They report average scores/payoffs, not explicit contribution rates as proportions.",0.2,N/R,They do not tabulate average number of cooperating players or labor units per session by condition.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency ratios (actual vs. maximum possible output) are provided.,0.2,N/R,They only describe relative average scores graphically (Fig. 1) without numeric group-payoff aggregates.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; only simulated programs.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results here are generated by computer-implemented strategies without human subjects.,1,"Three-person prisoner’s-dilemma-like joint-production game with exit. Payoff function: Pi = (2 * sum(Li) - F)/3 - Ci * Li with baseline F=2, Ci=1. Actions: participate or exit; if participate, choose cooperate (Li=1) or defect (Li=0). If any exit, no production and everyone gets 0 that session.",Summarized directly from Section 2.1 and Table 1.,1
10.1016_s0167-2681(00)00159-1.md,"Static tournaments – baseline production function, nine programs including teaching/teachable and Targeted Retribution, varying maximum sessions (1–20)","Section 3.2 introduces four additional programs (Easy-Tester, Recalcitrant-Tester, Moral Suasion, Targeted Retribution) and reports 20 tournaments varying maximum sessions, under the same baseline production function.",1,"Maximum possible number of production sessions for each team (1–20) under baseline joint-production technology (F=2, Ci=1); population of available programs fixed to nine strategies (Cooperator, Free-Rider, Tit-for-Tat, Prudence, Hit-and-Run, Moral Suasion, Easy-Tester, Recalcitrant-Tester, Targeted Retribution).",They vary only the team longevity while holding the payoff structure and nine-strategy pool constant.,1,False,Computer simulation with programmed agents only.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; numerical simulations.,1,False,Not a lab study with human participants.,1,True,Described as a series of simulation tournaments among strategy programs.,1,False,Key findings arise from computational tournaments rather than closed-form derivations.,1,3,Same three-person team structure as in Section 3.1.,1,20,"They state a series of 20 tournaments varying maximum sessions from 1 to 20, analogous to Fig. 1.",0.6,0,Binary choice to cooperate with 1 unit of labor or defect with 0 units; also a binary participation/exit choice.,1,N/A,No token endowment framework with default allocation; only effort and exit choices.,1,2,"Same payoff function (Q=2 * sum(Li)) and parameters F=2, Ci=1 as in baseline.",0.6,N/A,No human or communication channel; purely algorithmic behavior.,1,N/A,No human interface with feedback summaries.,1,N/A,"Punishment is imposed mechanically by the Targeted Retribution program on identified defectors in the code, not via human-recognized identifiers.",1,N/A,No explicit costly rewarding with identities is modeled.,1,N/A,No display to participants; only parameters in simulation.,1,1,"Besides exit-based sanctions, this condition includes the Targeted Retribution program, which explicitly punishes a single defector by imposing a loss of 2 at own cost 0.2.",1,0.2,"Targeted Retribution ""imposes a penalty of 2 units on the defector at a cost of 0.2 units to itself""; cost per punishment act is 0.2.",1,2,Each punishment act imposes 2 units of loss on the target per 0.2 cost to the punisher; per-coin technology is 2 loss per punishment activation.,0.9,0,No costly positive rewards or bonuses are implemented; only punishment and exit.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/R,"Model specifies costs and outputs directly, without defining a fixed token endowment per player per session.",0.2,N/R,They plot average scores of programs (Fig. 2) but not contribution frequencies.,0.2,N/R,No explicit counts of cooperative acts are tabulated.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively but not computed as a numeric ratio.,0.2,N/R,Only relative performance curves are shown; no numeric group payoff levels.,0.2,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All behavior generated by computer code implementing nine strategies.,1,"Same three-person joint-production PDE game as baseline (Pi = (2 * sum(Li) - F)/3 - Li with F=2). Strategies now include teachable defectors (Easy-Tester, Recalcitrant-Tester), a cooperative exiter with temporary exit (Moral Suasion), and a punisher (Targeted Retribution) that exits if two defectors or if punishment fails to elicit cooperation.",Summarized from Section 2.2 and Section 3.2.,1
10.1016_s0167-2681(00)00159-1.md,"Static tournaments – low fixed cost (F=0.5), nine programs, varying maximum sessions (1–20)",Section 3.3 changes the fixed cost to F=0.5 while keeping nine programs and varying maximum sessions; this is a separate simulation condition.,1,"Joint-production difficulty via fixed cost F=0.5 (vs baseline F=2), with maximum possible sessions per team varied from 1 to 20; nine-strategy population unchanged.","They alter the production technology (lower fixed cost) while again varying team longevity, holding strategy set constant.",1,False,Simulation study only.,1,False,"Numerical tournament, not a human experiment.",1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Described as simulations with modified payoff structure.,1,False,Key outcomes from numerical tournaments (Fig. 3).,1,3,Still three-person teams (Table 3 and surrounding text).,1,20,"They state “a series of 20 tournaments” analogous to earlier figures, though not re-specified numerically; we infer same session-maximum variation.",0.5,0,"Binary cooperate (1 unit) vs defect (0), binary participation/exit.",1,N/A,No token endowment allocation; only contribution decisions.,1,2,Production function Q=2 * sum(Li) is unchanged; only fixed cost F is reduced.,0.8,N/A,No human interaction or messaging.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,Punishment occurs in code; no human identities.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No interface indicating number of rounds to human participants.,1,1,"Condition still includes Targeted Retribution as defined earlier, plus exit-based sanctions.",1,0.2,Targeted Retribution’s cost per sanction is unchanged (0.2) in this variant.,1,2,Targeted Retribution still imposes 2 units of loss per punishment act.,1,0,No separate rewarding technology is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Still no fixed token endowment per player per session described.,0.2,N/R,They show average scores (Fig. 3) but no explicit contribution rates.,0.2,N/R,No average number of cooperating players is given numerically.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency of joint production is discussed qualitatively but not quantified.,0.2,N/R,No explicit numeric group-payoff aggregates reported.,0.2,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,No human,Computer simulations only.,1,"Same three-person PDE game but with low fixed cost F=0.5. Payoff table (Table 3) shows that with all cooperation, each cooperator gets 0.833; single defector among two cooperators gets 1.166; cooperative players are more tolerant because negative payoffs only arise with two defectors.",Summarized from Section 3.3 and Table 3.,1
10.1016_s0167-2681(00)00159-1.md,"Evolutionary simulations – five-session teams, endogenous population shares, baseline production function, nine programs",Section 4.1 describes an evolutionary simulation where each generation plays a five-session tournament with the baseline payoff structure and nine programs; population shares update via a replicator dynamic.,1,"Evolutionary generation (1–20) with endogenous population shares; team maximum sessions fixed at 5; baseline joint-production technology (F=2, Ci=1); nine initial programs with equal starting frequencies; replicator dynamic with truncation for negative scores and reentry via shadow scores.","Only generation and resulting composition vary; game parameters (five sessions, F=2, nine-strategy pool) are fixed.",1,False,No human participants; strategy frequencies evolve computationally.,1,False,"Simulation of population dynamics, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field context with humans.,1,True,They explicitly describe using a modified simulation program implementing a (truncated) replicator dynamic over generations.,1,False,"While the replicator dynamic is defined analytically, outcomes in Fig. 4 are produced by simulation.",0.7,3,Teams remain three-person joint enterprises throughout.,1,5,They fix the maximum number of production sessions per team at five for this evolutionary run.,1,0,Same binary cooperation and participation structure as earlier simulations.,1,N/A,No endowed tokens allocation; production-based game.,1,2,"Uses the same baseline production function as in Section 3.2 (F=2, Q=2 * sum(Li)).",0.8,N/A,No communication among human agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; no feedback display.,1,N/A,Targeted Retribution targets defectors in code; identity display to humans is not relevant.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No human interface indicating remaining rounds.,1,1,Targeted Retribution and exit-based sanctions are part of the nine-strategy set in this evolutionary run.,1,0.2,Targeted Retribution retains cost of 0.2 units per punishment.,1,2,Targeted Retribution still imposes a 2-unit loss per act on the defector.,1,0,No separate costly reward technology modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No fixed per-round endowment is described for agents.,0.2,N/R,They track population shares and program scores rather than explicit contribution rates.,0.2,N/R,No numeric series for average cooperation per session is given.,0.2,N/R,They interpret viability and stability but do not present efficiency ratios.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs are embedded in scores but not tabulated numerically per generation.,0.2,N/A,"Simulated strategies, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulated strategies, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulated strategies, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulated strategies, no humans.",1,No human,Population dynamics entirely simulated.,1,Each generation: all possible three-person teams are formed from strategy programs based on current population shares. Each team can play up to five production sessions with exit-based team termination. Scores from these tournaments determine next-generation frequencies via a truncated replicator dynamic; programs with negative scores are set to zero frequency but can reenter if positive shadow scores arise.,"Summarized from Section 4 (definition of S_it, f_it, and reentry mechanism).",1
10.1016_s0167-2681(00)00159-1.md,"Evolutionary simulations – three-session teams, baseline Targeted Retribution, endogenous populations (cyclic anarchy case)","Section 4.2 analyzes evolutionary dynamics with maximum three production sessions per team, baseline sanction strength (Targeted Retribution penalty=2), and nine strategies.",1,Evolutionary generation under joint-production game with team maximum sessions fixed at 3; baseline Targeted Retribution punishment (loss 2 at cost 0.2); nine-strategy population; replicator dynamic with elimination of negative-score programs and reentry via shadow scores.,The main changed parameter from Section 4.1 is team longevity (3 instead of 5 sessions).,1,False,No human subjects involved.,1,False,Evolutionary computational simulation.,1,False,No lab study.,1,True,They explicitly simulate generations and population dynamics as in Fig. 5.,1,False,Results presented visually and described qualitatively.,0.7,3,Still three-person teams.,1,3,"They state this simulation uses ""at most three sessions"" per team.",1,0,Binary cooperate/defect and exit decisions.,1,N/A,No per-player token endowment.,1,2,"Production function unchanged (baseline F=2, Q=2 * sum(Li)).",0.8,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,Punishment targeting is internal to the algorithm.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human awareness of rounds.,1,1,Includes Targeted Retribution and exit-based punishing effects.,1,0.2,Targeted Retribution’s per-act cost remains 0.2.,1,2,Each punishment imposes 2 units of loss to a defector.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowments not specified.,0.2,N/R,They describe cycles of cooperation and defection but do not provide numeric contribution rates.,0.2,N/R,No explicit data series of contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,They discuss that joint production disappears in some generations but do not give efficiency numbers.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff data per generation are not shown numerically.,0.2,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Fully computational evolutionary model.,1,"Game: same three-person PDE with F=2. Evolutionary rule: replicator dynamic with elimination of negative-score strategies and reentry via low-frequency shadow scores on self-matched teams. With only three encounters per team, cooperative strategies cannot sustain themselves; populations cycle between all-free-rider and mixed/cooperative states, leading to periodic disappearance and reemergence of joint production.",Summarized from description of Fig. 5 in Section 4.2 and footnote 16.,1
10.1016_s0167-2681(00)00159-1.md,"Evolutionary simulations – three-session teams, strong Targeted Retribution (penalty=3), endogenous populations","Section 4.3 modifies the Targeted Retribution program to impose a penalty of 3 instead of 2 and repeats a three-session evolutionary simulation, observing stabilized cooperative populations.",1,"Strength of punishment by Targeted Retribution (penalty on defector increased from 2 to 3 units, cost still >0) in a three-session maximum team game with nine programs and replicator dynamic.","They change only the severity of the targeted sanction while keeping other aspects (three sessions, nine strategies, dynamics) constant.",1,False,Pure simulation.,1,False,"Computational evolutionary simulation, not a human experiment.",1,False,No human lab involvement.,1,True,"Section 4.3 clearly describes running a new simulation with modified punishment parameter, shown in Fig. 6.",1,False,Outcomes summarized qualitatively and via simulation figure.,0.7,3,Still three-person teams; same game.,1,3,"They say ""three production session evolutionary simulation"" mirroring the previous 3-session case.",1,0,Binary contribute/defect and exit choices remain.,1,N/A,No token endowments.,1,2,Production function and F=2 baseline are unchanged; only punishment parameter altered.,0.8,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No information interface to humans.,1,N/A,"Punishment targeting is algorithmic, not shown to human players.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human-facing round counter.,1,1,Targeted Retribution remains present and more severe in sanctioning.,1,N/R,"They only state that the punishment on defectors is increased from 2 to 3; they do not re-state the cost to Targeted Retribution (could still be 0.2 or changed), so we cannot be sure.",0.3,3,"They specify that Targeted Retribution now ""imposes a cost of 3 rather than 2 on single defectors""; we treat 3 as units of loss per punishment act.",1,0,No introduction of new reward mechanisms is mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowments are not discussed.,0.2,N/R,They discuss stabilized cooperative population shares but no explicit contribution rate time series.,0.2,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,"They qualitatively state that joint production is stabilized and cooperative strategies do better, but no numeric efficiency metric.",0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoff levels or averages are provided.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely simulation-based.,1,"Same three-person PDE setup (baseline F=2, Q=2 * sum(Li)) and nine strategies as in Section 4.2, but with a more powerful Targeted Retribution that imposes 3 units of loss on defectors. This higher sanction, combined with exit and other conditional responses, sufficiently reduces free-rider viability to produce a stable cooperative population structure similar to the five-session case.",Summarized from Section 4.3 and Fig. 6.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_128_28001.md,"Simulation – Public reputation scenario, baseline parameters","Single parameter set explicitly described for public reputation in Fig. 1: N=600, r=0.5, b=9, c=3, α=1, β=4, q=0.7, ε=0.1, μ=0.005, four initial strategies each at 1/4, nine norms N1–N9.",0.9,"Social norm n ∈ {N1,…,N9} under public reputation, with four initial strategies CC, CD, CP, DD each at frequency 0.25; other parameters fixed (N=600, r=0.5, b=9, c=3, α=1, β=4, q=0.7, ε=0.1, μ=0.005).",Across conditions they vary the second-order social norm; this row corresponds to the set of public-reputation simulations with those fixed baseline parameters.,0.9,False,Model is a computer simulation of evolutionary dynamics with no human participants described.,1,False,"It is not a controlled lab or field experiment with subjects, but a numerical simulation study.",1,False,No laboratory or on-site human experiment; only simulated agents.,1,True,They explicitly describe using a Wright–Fisher process and evolutionary time steps; all results are from simulations.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations reported; focus is on numerical simulations.,0.9,600,They state N=600 for Fig. 1 public reputation scenario.,1,300,"Each individual participates in I=rN interactions with r=0.5 and N=600, so I=rN=0.5×600=300 per evolutionary time step.",0.9,1,"Donation game allows discrete action choice C, D, or P rather than a continuous contribution amount; mapped as general action choice, so contributions are not restricted to all-or-nothing invest-all endowments. Use 1 to denote non-all-or-nothing in contribution sense.",0.4,N/A,"No explicit endowment or continuous contribution level; players simply choose C, D, or P in a donation game without a divisible endowment.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; only donation payoffs b and cost c are given.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication between simulated agents is not modeled as strategic chat.,1,N/R,Simulation description does not include any interface-like feedback such as summary of others' earnings; not meaningful for agents.,1,N/A,"No human participants or identity revelation; agents know only reputations, not identities in human sense.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism other than cooperation; no identity revelation concept.,1,N/R,"No interface to participants; number of rounds known only to modeler, not to simulated agents.",1,1,They include a punishment action P with cost α to donor and fine β to recipient.,1,1,For Fig. 1 they state α=1 as the cost of punishment to the donor.,1,4,Fine imposed on recipient is β=4 in these simulations.,1,0,There is no separate rewarding action; cooperation is not modeled as a costly reward beyond the basic donation game.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,The donation game specifies costs and benefits per interaction but not an overall per-round endowment.,0.7,N/R,They report cooperation rate (frequency of C choices) but not normalized to an endowment-based contribution schema.,0.8,N/R,"Only binary/ternary actions C, D, P; no continuous contribution amount.",1,N/R,They provide average payoff but not normalized by maximum possible payoff.,0.8,N/R,They present qualitative patterns and some figures; no explicit numeric values for average payoff are listed in text.,0.6,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely computational and theoretical with simulations only.,1,"Donation game with binary reputations (G,B); donors choose C, D, or P; C costs c=3 to donor and yields benefit b=9 to recipient; D yields zero payoffs; P costs α=1 to donor and imposes fine β=4 on recipient; action errors occur with probability ε=0.1; public reputation (all observers share same opinion); observation probability q=0.7; population size N=600; each time step each individual has I=rN=300 interactions with r=0.5; strategies S(G),S(B) ∈ {C,D,P} giving 9 types, but focus on CC, CD, CP, DD.",Summarized directly from description of model and Fig. 1 parameter specification for public reputation baseline.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_128_28001.md,"Simulation – Private reputation scenario, baseline parameters","Single parameter set explicitly described for private reputation in Fig. 1: N=600, r=0.5, b=9, c=3, α=1, β=4, q=0.7, ε=0.1, μ=0.005, four initial strategies each at 1/4, nine norms N1–N9, but with private reputations.",0.9,"Social norm n ∈ {N1,…,N9} under private reputation, with four initial strategies CC, CD, CP, DD each at frequency 0.25; other parameters fixed (N=600, r=0.5, b=9, c=3, α=1, β=4, q=0.7, ε=0.1, μ=0.005).",Analogous to public baseline but with private reputation updating; norms are the main independent variable across these simulations.,0.9,False,Only computer simulations; no human participants.,1,False,"Numerical simulation, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,They explicitly use Wright–Fisher process with simulated generations.,1,False,No analytical closed-form model used as main method.,0.9,600,They state N=600 for Fig. 1 private reputation scenario as well (same N as in public case).,1,300,"I=rN with r=0.5 and N=600, giving I=300 interactions per individual per evolutionary time step.",0.9,1,"Same donation-game structure with discrete action choice C, D, or P; not an all-or-nothing continuous contribution game.",0.4,N/A,No divisible endowment assigned; only action choices.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier defined.,1,N/A,No humans; communication not modeled as chat.,1,N/R,Simulation framework does not specify such feedback to agents.,1,N/A,No human identity revelation in simulation.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards; only donation actions.,1,N/R,Agents are not described as knowing total rounds; this construct applies to human experiments.,1,1,Punishment action P with cost α and fine β is available in private reputation case too.,1,1,For Fig. 1 they use α=1.,1,4,Fine is β=4 for these baseline simulations.,1,0,No separate reward stage is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,E