ically.,0.2,Italy,"Students from University of Bologna campuses in Emilia-Romagna, Italy.",1,"Mainly young adults: 95.2% aged 18–39, 4.8% aged 40–59, 0% aged 60+",Table 2 age distribution for Student sample.,1,"55.8% male, 44.2% female",Table 2: Male 55.8% for Student sample; remainder assumed female.,1,"1.0% 8th grade or lower, 55.8% high school, 43.3% college/Master/PhD",Education distribution for Student sample in Table 2.,1,On site,"Sessions run at the permanent BLESS lab, a standard on-site laboratory environment.",1,"Repeated linear public goods game with contributions restricted to {0,6,14,20}; strangers matching each period; 1 Euro per 40 tokens; no punishment stage.",Summarizes structural features for the student PGG-Standard condition.,1
10.1111_ecin.12187.md,Student – PGG-Punishment,Student treatment (university students sample) playing the Public Goods Game with peer punishment (PGG-Punishment).,1,"Subject pool = Student (University of Bologna); Punishment institution = Yes (PGG-Punishment, peer punishment allowed)",Varies both by participant pool and by presence of punishment stage.,1,True,Human student subject pool in an incentivized PGG with punishment.,1,True,Controlled random-rematching lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted at the BLESS laboratory.,1,False,No purely simulated agents or computational modeling reported as results.,1,False,Results stem from experimental data.,1,4,Same N=4 group size throughout.,1,8,Eight periods of PGG-Punishment after the eight PGG-Standard periods.,1,0,"Contribution choices {0,6,14,20} apply here as well.",1,0,"Each period, 20-token endowment is fully private before deciding contribution.",0.9,0.5,Same MPCR parameter a=0.5 for all games.,1,0,No communication allowed in any treatment.,1,1,Subjects see individual contributions and earnings of group members plus deduction points they received.,1,0,"""The punisher's identity was not revealed"" in the punishment game.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Fixed 8-period structure; likely common knowledge though not verbatim.,0.6,1,Second-stage peer punishment with deduction points present.,1,1,Each deduction point costs 1 token to the punisher.,1,4,Each point reduces target’s earnings by 4 tokens.,1,0,No possibility to increase others’ payoffs described.,1,N/A,Reward stage is absent.,1,N/A,Reward stage is absent.,1,20,Same endowment as in PGG-Standard.,1,N/R,"They report contribution in tokens, not share of endowment.",0.4,12.77,"Table 3: ""Average individual contributions"" for Student, PGG-Punishment, all periods = 12.77 tokens.",1,N/R,Profit/efficiency not summarized as normalized efficiency.,0.2,N/R,Only aggregated differences in per-period profits are mentioned qualitatively.,0.2,Italy,"Student participants from University of Bologna, in Emilia-Romagna, Italy.",1,"Mainly young adults: 95.2% aged 18–39, 4.8% aged 40–59, 0% aged 60+",Same Student sample as in other student condition.,1,"55.8% male, 44.2% female",From Table 2 for Student sample.,1,"1.0% 8th grade or lower, 55.8% high school, 43.3% college/Master/PhD",Education distribution for Student sample.,1,On site,"Conducted in the BLESS physical lab, not online or in natural field.",1,"Two-stage repeated PGG with discrete contributions {0,6,14,20}; strangers matching; anonymous peer punishment with up to 2 deduction points per target per period, each costing 1 token and reducing target’s payoff by 4; no revelation of punisher identity; 1 Euro per 40 tokens.",Summarizes institutional details of the student PGG-Punishment condition.,1
10.1007_s11071-023-08297-x.md,"Traditional punishment – f=0.5, r=3.0 (baseline comparison set over α, β)",Labeled as traditional punishment with fixed probability; Fig. 1a fixes f=0.5 and r=3.0 while varying α and β. Treated as one simulation condition family with fixed structural parameters.,0.8,"Punishment regime: traditional fixed-probability punishment; fixed punishment probability f=0.5; enhancement factor r=3.0; punishment cost α and fine β varied over ranges α∈[0.1,0.6], β∈[0.1,1.0].",Paper explicitly contrasts traditional punishment and graded punishment and states parameter values for this figure set.,0.9,False,"Study is an evolutionary Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled lab/field experiment; purely computational model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; all results from simulations.,1,True,Authors describe Monte Carlo steps and evolutionary rule on a 100×100 lattice; standard agent-based simulation.,1,False,No closed-form analytical results; only simulation-based phase diagrams and time series.,1,10000,Section 3: “a 100 × 100 square lattice… hence N=10^4”.,1,10000,They state system stabilizes after 10^4 MCS for most parameters and use last 1000 iterations for averages; treat 10^4 MCS as effective round count.,0.7,1,Each cooperator/punisher “donate one unit” vs defectors donate nothing; no partial contribution allowed.,0.9,N/A,"No human endowments; contributions are model actions, not pre-allocated funds.",1,0.6,"MPCR = r/G. Here r=3.0, group size G=5 (each player plus four neighbors), so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.95,N/A,No humans; no communication mechanism modeled.,1,0,Agents only use local payoff comparisons; no mention of observing summaries of others’ earnings or sanctions.,0.8,0,Model does not include identities or reputation; agents only see payoffs when imitating.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in model.,1,0,Agents do not have information about total MCS or remaining time; only reactive updating.,0.8,1,Punishment strategy P explicitly present in traditional punishment model.,1,N/R,In this condition set α is varied across simulations and not fixed to one value for the whole condition object.,0.5,N/R,Fine β per successful punishment is varied; no single fixed technical ratio for this aggregated condition.,0.5,0,Only punishment is modeled; no rewarding strategy or parameters.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator/punisher “need to donate one unit”; interpret as per-round endowment of 1 unit that can be given or kept.,0.8,N/R,They report only ρ_c (proportion of cooperators+punishers); this is not normalized contribution conditional on endowment and is not given numerically for this aggregate condition.,0.3,N/R,No raw average contribution per player reported; only densities.,0.1,N/R,No explicit payoff efficiency measures reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff values are not tabulated or plotted; only strategy proportions shown.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary model.,1,"Spatial public goods game on 100×100 square lattice with degree 4 (G=5); three strategies C, D, P; asynchronous updating via pairwise comparison with Fermi rule and noise κ=0.5; initial strategies uniform over {C,D,P}; punishment implemented with independent fixed probability f=0.5 for each punisher-defector pair.",Summarizes core structural features of the model from Sections 2–3.,1
10.1007_s11071-023-08297-x.md,"Graded punishment – f0=0.5, r=3.0 (baseline comparison set over α, β)",Labeled as graded punishment; Fig. 1b fixes initial punishment probability f0=0.5 and r=3.0 while varying α and β.,0.8,"Punishment regime: graded punishment with dynamic probability f_x^i=f0^x; initial punishment probability f0=0.5; enhancement factor r=3.0; punishment cost α and fine β varied over ranges α∈[0.1,0.6], β∈[0.1,1.0].",Section 2.2 defines graded punishment; Section 3 describes using f0=0.5 and r=3.0 in Fig. 1b.,0.9,False,Simulation study with artificial agents only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on spatial network.,1,False,No analytical model results.,1,10000,Same 100×100 lattice as for traditional punishment; Section 3 states N=10^4.,1,10000,System stabilized after 10^4 MCS for most parameters; used as effective round count.,0.7,1,Cooperators/punishers donate one unit; defectors donate nothing; no fractional actions.,0.9,N/A,No human endowment concept; simulation units only.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/G; r=3.0, G=5 hence 0.6.",0.95,N/A,No communication among simulated agents.,1,0,Agents only use local payoffs for imitation; no summary info is provided.,0.8,0,Model omits identity and information about who punished whom.,0.9,0,No rewards are modeled.,1,0,No round counter exploited by agents in update rule.,0.8,1,Punishment strategy P with graded probability present.,1,N/R,α varies across simulations within this parameter set; no single value.,0.5,N/R,Fine β per successful punishment is varied; no single fixed factor.,0.5,0,No reward or bonus strategy in this model.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,1,Each contributing player donates one unit; treat as unit endowment.,0.8,N/R,Only cooperation density ρ_c is shown qualitatively; no numeric table.,0.3,N/R,No explicit average contributions.,0.1,N/R,No payoff efficiency indices reported.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoff levels presented.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model only.,1,"Same spatial public goods framework as traditional punishment but with graded punishment: probability that a defector i is punished after x consecutive defections is f_x^i = 0 if x=0, else f0^x with f0=0.5; once i cooperates, x resets to 0. Payoffs and updating rules identical otherwise; κ=0.5, asynchronous pairwise imitation on lattice.",Summarizes modifications specific to graded punishment condition.,1
10.1007_s11071-023-08297-x.md,"Traditional punishment – f=0.2, r=3.5 (low punishment probability comparison set over α, β)",Fig. 2a fixes traditional punishment with f0=0.2 (static) and r=3.5 while varying α and β.,0.8,"Punishment regime: traditional fixed-probability punishment; fixed punishment probability f=0.2; enhancement factor r=3.5; punishment cost α and fine β varied (α∈[0.1,0.8] per text, β∈[0.1,1.0]).",Section 3: “we fix f0=0.2 and r=3.5 and draw comparison curves in Fig. 2.”,0.9,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Monte Carlo spatial evolutionary game.,1,False,No closed-form solution.,1,10000,Same 100×100 lattice used throughout.,1,10000,Use of 10^4 MCS for stabilization for most parameters.,0.7,1,Binary donate-one-unit vs defect.,0.9,N/A,No human endowments.,1,0.7,"MPCR = r/G; r=3.5, G=5 so 3.5/5=0.7.",0.95,N/A,No communication in simulation.,1,0,No evidence of summary feedback.,0.8,0,Identity not modeled.,0.9,0,No reward implemented.,1,0,No round information available to agents.,0.8,1,Punishment strategy exists with fixed probability f=0.2.,1,N/R,α varied over a range in this figure; not a single value.,0.5,N/R,Fine β varied; no single tech ratio.,0.5,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,1,Donate one unit when cooperating/punishing.,0.8,N/R,"Only cooperation density curves vs β, no numeric panel.",0.3,N/R,Not given.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0.1,N/R,Payoff levels omitted.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Same spatial PGG framework as other conditions, but fixed punishment probability f=0.2 and enhancement factor r=3.5; focus on coexistence regions and conditions for full cooperation under varying punishment cost α and fine β.",Summarizes aspects unique to this parameter set.,1
