10.1111_beer.12096.md,Exclusion game with ex-ante agreement on distribution rule (Faillo et al. 2015 treatment),Paper explicitly describes a treatment in Faillo et al. (2015) where subjects agree on a distribution rule before roles are assigned in the exclusion game.,0.9,Presence of an ex-ante veil-of-ignorance agreement on a distribution rule among three players in the exclusion game,"Condition is characterized by players agreeing on a distribution rule before knowing whether they are strong or weak players, i.e., presence of ex-ante agreement.",0.9,True,Described as a laboratory game with human subjects dividing a sum of money.,1,True,Referred to as an experimental setting and a treatment within an experiment.,1,True,Described as a laboratory simulation of veil-of-ignorance reasoning; no indication it is field or online.,0.8,False,"Involves human participants, not purely computational agents.",1,False,Not a purely theoretical or mathematical derivation; it is a behavioral experiment.,1,3,Exclusion game described as having groups of three players.,1,1,Described as a one-shot division of a sum of money in the exclusion game; no repetition mentioned.,0.6,1,"Players 'have to divide a sum of money among themselves', implying they can choose any feasible division, not only all-or-nothing contributions.",0.7,N/R,No public good or default allocation of endowment between private/public accounts is described.,0,N/R,"This is a bargaining/division game, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR reported.",0,0,Standard experimental economics method section emphasizes no direct communication as the norm; no mention of communication in this treatment.,0.6,0,No mention of feedback or summaries of others’ earnings or actions beyond the immediate outcome.,0.4,0,No punishment mechanism described in this exclusion/division game.,1,0,No explicit rewards given by peers; only allocation of an exogenous sum.,1,1,"Subjects in standard lab experiments are typically informed about the one-sho