10.1007_978-0-387-72596-3_7.md,Fehr & Gächter 2002 – Public-good game with altruistic punishment (stranger matching),This is the main punishment condition in Fehr & Gächter (2002) as described: repeated one-shot public-good game with a costly punishment stage and stranger matching.,1,"Punishment opportunity: present (0–10 MU per target, 1:3 fee-to-fine); Matching protocol: stranger (new 4-person group every round)",Condition differs from control by adding a costly punishment stage; stranger matching emphasized to rule out reputation.,1,True,Human laboratory participants with monetary incentives.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment opportunity within a lab game.,1,True,"Standard lab-based public-good game with MUs, not field or online.",0.95,False,No computer-only simulation involved for this condition.,1,False,Analytical modeling is discussed conceptually but not as the method for this condition.,1,4,Each group consists of four members.,1,6,Game repeated six trials; group membership changed each time.,1,1,Subjects could contribute any integer amount 0–20 MUs.,1,0,Endowment is received privately and then individuals choose contributions.,0.9,0.4,Each MU contributed returns 0.4 MU to each of 4 group members.,1,0,No mention of communication; Fehr & Gächter designs typically forbid communication to isolate punishment effects.,0.9,1,"After contribution decisions, participants were informed about each other’s investments.",0.95,0,Paper notes general anonymity of punishers in these experiments; punished know amounts but not identities.,0.9,0,No reward stage in this game.,1,1,Repeated-game design with references to final period suggests participants knew the horizon; standard in such studies though not explicitly stated.,0.6,1,Second-stage costly punishment decision between 0 and 10 MU per other member.,1,1,Each MU assigned costs the punisher 1 MU.,1,3,"For each point used, three points were subtracted from the target’s earnings; fine-to-fee ratio 3:1.",1,0,No mechanism to increase others’ payoffs described.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,20,Each trial subjects receive 20 MUs.,1,N/R,"Narrative reports that average contributions were higher than in no-punishment for all periods and that 39% contributed full endowment in final period, but no full-period mean or normalized rate is given.",0.2,N/R,"No numeric mean contribution by period reported, only proportions of full vs zero contribution.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate welfare or efficiency metrics reported.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff presented.,0,N/R,Country or city of the lab is not specified in the excerpt.,0,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,Gender not discussed.,0,N/R,"Likely university students, but the text does not specify.",0,On site,Standard lab monetary MU public-good experiment; no indication of remote or field implementation.,0.9,Two-stage repeated one-shot public-good game with 4-person stranger matching over 6 periods. Stage 1: contribute 0–20 MUs to group project; each contributed MU pays 0.4 MU to each group member. Stage 2: players can assign 0–10 punishment points to each other member; each point costs 1 MU and reduces target’s payoff by 3 MUs. Groups are reshuffled each period so no one meets the same participant twice.,Direct summary of the condition’s structural description.,1
10.1086_691630.md,Baseline – Appointed neutral authority,"Baseline public goods with punishment: neutral labels (participant A/B), authority appointed by experimenters.",1,Framing of authority role: neutral (participant A/B); authority selection: appointed; no policy announcement; no election; no prior experience,"Condition differs from others by neutral labels, appointed authority, no policy announcement, no election, and no prior experience.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with student participants.,1,True,Controlled lab design with random assignment to roles and treatments.,1,True,Conducted at Cologne Laboratory for Economic Research.,1,False,No computer-simulated agents; all decisions by humans.,1,False,"Paper reports empirical lab data, not purely theoretical analysis.",1,5,Groups consist of four active participants and one authority.,1,10,Baseline experiment is repeated 10 times.,1,1,Active participants can contribute any number of ECUs from 0 to 20.,1,0,Each participant receives a private endowment and chooses how much to contribute to the public project; all endowment starts private.,1,0.4,Every ECU contributed is multiplied by 0.4 and paid to each of 4 active participants; MPCR stated as 0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous public goods with no chat.,0.9,1,"Active participants learn individual contributions, punishment assigned, and income of all four members each period.",1,0,Experiment is anonymous; identities of subjects are never revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists; only punishment.,1,1,Experiment is described as repeated 10 times; standard practice and wording imply subjects know the horizon.,0.9,1,Authority can reduce active participants’ income by up to 80 ECUs per period.,1,1,Punishing an active participant costs the authority 1 ECU irrespective of severity.,1,80,Authority can at most reduce any one participant’s income by 80 ECUs; treat as max punishment capacity per target.,1,0,Design only includes income reductions; no option to increase others’ payoffs beyond public good.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,20,Four active participants receive an endowment of 20 ECUs each per period.,1,N/R,"Only directional statements (e.g., significantly more than 0, profit >20); no exact mean contribution reported.",0,N/R,No numeric average contribution level is given for the baseline.,0,N/R,Paper does not report total payoffs or efficiency ratios relative to maximum.,0,N/R,"Only individual earnings of authorities (mean €23.16) and active participants (€16.53) reported, not total group payoff in ECUs.",0.3,Germany,Experiment conducted at Cologne Laboratory for Economic Research (Germany) with local students.,0.9,Mean 23.87,Table 1 reports mean age 23.87 for Baseline.,1,57.78% female,Table 1 reports percentage female 57.78 for Baseline.,1,University students (various majors),Described as 90 student-participants of various majors.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory (Cologne Laboratory for Economic Research).,1,"Linear public good with 4 contributors (A) and 1 outsider authority (B). Authority gets fixed €15 plus 4 ECUs per period if no punishment; authority does not benefit from public good. Groups fixed across 10 periods. MPCR=0.4, group of 4, so full contribution yields each active 32 ECUs.",Summarizes the detailed payoff structure and role separation described in Section 3.,1
