roup.,1,University students (various majors),Same subject pool for both groups.,0.9,On site,Same physical laboratory as prior treatments.,1,Experienced judges (having served 10 periods with a first group) now decide over 10 periods for a new citizen group. This allows comparison of punishment behavior before and after experience.,Section 5.5: 'experienced judges' are those in the second phase with new citizens.,1
10.1109_tnsm.2015.2397883.md,Simulation – baseline VPEF with varying selection intensity β,"Condition described in Fig. 2: time averages of strategies C, D, P, L as a function of selection intensity β under VPEF parameters M=500, μ=0.0001, α=10, cs=10, ce=1, pb=1.",0.93,"Selection intensity β ∈ [0,1] under VPEF (M=500, μ=0.0001, α=10, cs=10, ce=1, pb=1)",Text for Fig. 2 explicitly varies β while holding other parameters fixed for the cycle-based simulation.,0.93,False,All results are from theoretical analysis and computer simulations of evolutionary dynamics; no human subjects or behavioral experiment is reported.,1,False,"These are numerical simulations and analytical results, not controlled experiments with human participants.",1,False,No lab or field setting with human participants; purely simulated model.,1,True,Authors state they use a cycle-based simulation model to evaluate VPEF under specified parameters.,1,True,Section III develops an analytical Markov-chain/EGT framework and Theorem 1; Fig. 2 overlays theoretical values with simulations.,0.96,500,Scenario caption for Fig. 2: 'M=500'.,1,0,They use an ongoing cycle-based simulation until time averages converge; exact number of rounds is not reported.,0.1,1,"Model specifies each participant 'voluntarily invests same amount of resource cs to common pool'; contribution level is fixed, not a continuous choice, matching all-or-nothing dichotomy in this schema.",0.8,N/A,No explicit endowment or initial allocation; this is a theoretical/simulation model without human accounts.,1,N/R,"They use a 'multiplexing/diversity factor' α and group size N, but MPCR (α/N) for the specific scenario is not provided because N is not given in the figure caption.",0.2,N/A,No human participants or communication stage; purely simulated strategies.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human subjects; model-level utilities only.,1,N/A,No notion of individual identity revelation in a human sense; just aggregate punishment in the payoff functions.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism distinct from the public good; only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No human interface or display of remaining rounds.,1,1,Punishers P impose penalty cp on defectors and pay punishment cost cu; costly punishment is central to VPEF.,1,N/R,They denote cu (punishment cost per punished defector) but do not assign a numerical value in Fig. 2 scenario.,0.2,N/R,Penalty cp is defined as pb * α * cs /(iG + iB + iP); no single scalar per-unit exchange rate is given for this scenario.,0.3,0,Model includes only public-good benefits and punishment; there is no separate costly reward action.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/R,Agents incur fixed cost cs for contributing and ce for entry but no explicit per-round endowment is defined.,0.2,N/R,"Outputs are reported as time averages (stationary probabilities) of strategies C, D, P, L, not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,"Each cooperator or punisher always contributes cs, but average aggregate contributions in the simulation are not tabulated.",0.3,N/R,"They define 'efficiency loss' as share of time in D and L states and give formula 4/(8+M), but do not present a normalized efficiency (0–1 payoff ratio) for this specific simulation.",0.4,N/R,"Payoff formulas Pi_j are provided, but no aggregate group payoff statistics are reported for Fig. 2.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of evolutionary game-theoretic analysis and computer simulations without human subjects.,1,"Common-goods game with four strategies (C: contribute only, D: defect, P: contribute and punish, L: loner not participating). Population size M=500. Groups of size N (not specified here) are sampled without replacement via hypergeometric distribution. Each participant who joins pays round-based entry fee ce=1, cooperators and punishers also pay contribution cost cs=10; resources are multiplied by factor α=10 and shared equally among participants. Punishers impose penalty cp on defectors with detection probability pb=1 while incurring punishment cost cu.","Summarized directly from Section III-C, Table I and the Fig. 2 caption; only elements needed for game structure are included.",0.95
10.1109_tnsm.2015.2397883.md,Simulation – varying defect-detection probability pb,"Condition described in Fig. 3: time averages of C, D, P, L as a function of probability pb that defectors are successfully found, with other parameters fixed.",0.93,"Defect detection probability pb ∈ [0,1] under VPEF (M=500, μ=0.0001, α=10, cs=10, ce=1)","Fig. 3 caption: 'Effect of the probability that defect peers are found on the time average', with parameters M=500, μ=0.0001, α=10, cs=10, ce=1.",0.96,False,Numerical simulation of an evolutionary game; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical controlled experiment; purely simulated.,1,False,No lab with human subjects.,1,True,Cycle-based simulation model is used to track time averages for varying pb.,1,True,"Curves in Fig. 3 include theoretical values derived from the analytical framework, as described around the figure.",0.9,500,Fig. 3 scenario states 'M=500'.,1,0,Number of simulation rounds is not reported; they simulate long enough to compute time averages.,0.1,1,"As in the main model, participants either contribute fixed amount cs or not at all.",0.8,N/A,No explicit endowment framework; theoretical agents only.,1,N/R,MPCR would be α/N but N is not specified for this scenario.,0.2,N/A,No communication stage among human players.,1,N/A,No user interface or human feedback.,1,N/A,Identity display is not modeled for humans.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,1,Punishers impose penalties cp that depend on pb; pb is the main independent variable.,1,N/R,Punishment cost cu is proportional to pb but the proportionality constant is not quantified for this scenario.,0.3,N/R,cp=pb * α * cs /(iG + iB + iP); no scalar mapping of cost to impact is given.,0.3,0,No rewarding behavior is modeled.,0.95,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/R,Only costs cs and ce are specified; no base endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Results are expressed as time shares of strategies, not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,"While cs is fixed per contributor, aggregate contributions over time for this condition are not numerically reported.",0.3,N/R,For this pb-variation simulation they only discuss performance qualitatively; no explicit normalized efficiency metric is provided.,0.3,N/R,No group payoff statistics or averages are provided for Fig. 3.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a parameter-sweep simulation of the evolutionary model.,1,"Same common-goods evolutionary game as baseline VPEF: four strategies (C, D, P, L), M=500, mutation probability μ=0.0001, multiplexing factor α=10, contribution cost cs=10, entry fee ce=1. Independent variable is pb, the probability that defectors are successfully detected and punished. The penalty cp per defector is cp=pb * α * cs /(iG + iB + iP), and the punishment cost cu borne by punishers is assumed proportional to pb.",Compiled directly from the description in Section IV-B and the Fig. 3 caption.,0.95
10.1109_tnsm.2015.2397883.md,Simulation – varying round-based entry fee ce,"Condition described in Fig. 4: theoretical and experimental time averages for C, D, P, L as a function of entry fee ce.",0.93,"Round-based entry fee ce (number of contributed files) under VPEF (M=500, μ=0.0001, α=10, cs=10, pb=1)","Fig. 4 caption: 'Effect of round-based entry fee on the time average.' Text states that entry fee represents the number of contributed files; parameters listed exclude ce (which is varied) but include M=500, μ=0.0001, α=10, cs=10.",0.95,False,Analysis is based on simulations with no human subjects.,1,False,"This is a simulation, not an empirical behavioral experiment.",1,False,No lab environment with human participants.,1,True,Cycle-based simulations produce 'experimental' curves compared to theory in Fig. 4.,1,True,Theoretical time averages are computed from the Markov-chain/stationary-distribution analysis for varying ce.,0.9,500,Fig. 4 scenario specifies M=500.,1,0,Only that a cycle-based simulation is used; no fixed number of rounds reported.,0.1,1,Contributors always invest cs if they join; loners and defectors invest nothing.,0.8,N/A,No endowment or default account structure is associated with human players.,1,N/R,MPCR is not numerically specified due to missing N for this figure.,0.2,N/A,No communication in simulation among human players.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No human-level identity revelation.,1,N/A,No reward system.,1,N/A,Not applicable to simulations without human subjects.,1,1,Punishers are present and impose penalties on defectors.,1,N/R,cu is present in the model but not assigned a numeric value for this ce-variation figure.,0.3,N/R,Impact of a unit of punishment is given via cp expression but no single scalar parameter is reported for this case.,0.3,0,Only punishment is modeled.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Agents only face costs cs and ce; no explicit initial endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes reported as fraction of time spent in each strategy, not in normalized contribution units.",0.2,N/R,Aggregate contribution amounts over time as a function of ce are not tabulated.,0.3,N/R,"Although they interpret higher P frequency as better performance, they do not compute explicit payoff-based efficiency numbers by ce.",0.3,N/R,No numerical group payoff statistics per ce value reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation-only study.,1,"Population of size M=500 evolves under imitation and mutation (μ=0.0001) among four strategies (C, D, P, L). Contribution cost cs=10; multiplexing/diversity factor α=10; defect detection probability pb=1 so defectors are always found and punished. Independent variable is the entry fee ce (modeled as number of files required to join). Time averages of being in near-homogeneous states (≥90% of same strategy) are measured as ce varies.",Summarized from Section IV-B and the Fig. 4 caption and discussion.,0.95
