humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,No human,Simulation on WS network only.,1.0,"WS network of size N = 1600 generated with K = 10 (each node initially linked to K/2 neighbors on each side) and reconnection probability 0.3; PDG payoff parameters R = 1, P = 0, S = −0.02, T = 1.02; initial strategies C/D with 50% probability; in each round, fraction p = 0.6 of players update strategies via Fermi imitation rule (κ = 1) using payoffs obtained from games with neighbors; revenge mechanism: if a mutual cooperator’s neighbor unilaterally defects between t and t+1, and the cooperator’s payoff is > 0, it becomes a revenger and punishes that neighbor, losing a fraction ρ_r = 0.2 of its current payoff per punished target, whereas each sufferer loses an amount scaled by ρ_s = 0.8 of revengers’ payoffs, with punishment order following node indices; they track the numbers of revengers and sufferers over t ∈ [0, 2000] and relate these to the evolution of cooperation frequency.",Compiled from Section 2 model description and Fig. 5(b) parameterization.,0.95
10.1016_j.amc.2023.128168.md,"Simulation on small-world network (WS), revenge mechanism: (ρ_r, ρ_s) = (0.2, 0.8), r in [0.035, 0.05], p = 0.4, N = 1600, K = 10, reconnection probability = 0.3","Condition used in Fig. 4(b) to study the effect of cost-to-benefit ratio r on cooperation frequency in WS with parameters (ρ_r, ρ_s) = (0.2, 0.8), p = 0.4, N = 1600, K = 10, reconnection probability = 0.3.",0.9,"Network type = WS small-world; revenge loss ratios ρ_r = 0.2, ρ_s = 0.8; cost-to-benefit ratio r varied from 0.035 to 0.05; updating fraction p = 0.4; network size N = 1600; mean degree parameter K = 10; rewiring probability = 0.3","From Fig. 4(b) caption: WS with K = 10, reconnection probability 0.3, p = 0.4, (ρ_r, ρ_s) = (0.2, 0.8), x-axis r from 0.035 to 0.05.",0.95,False,Simulated agents only.,1.0,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical parameter sweep.,1.0,False,No lab environment.,1.0,True,Results based on simulations varying r.,1.0,False,"r-dependence is obtained numerically, not analytically.",0.95,1600,Fig. 4(b) specifies N = 1600 in WS.,1.0,10000,They observe cooperation frequency until t = 10^4 to obtain stable results in Fig. 4(b).,0.9,1,Binary PDG strategies.,0.6,N/A,No contribution framework.,1.0,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects.,1.0,N/A,No UI for simulated agents.,1.0,N/A,Identity display not relevant.,1.0,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,1,"Revenge with ρ_r, ρ_s > 0 operates in these simulations.",1.0,N/R,Costs scale with payoff and number/order of punishments.,0.4,N/R,Sufferers’ losses depend on multiple revengers’ payoffs and order.,0.4,0,Only punishment aspects are modeled.,0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/R,Endowments not defined; payoffs come directly from PDG interactions.,0.3,N/A,Dependent variable is cooperation frequency fc and frequencies of revengers/sufferers.,1.0,N/A,No contribution decision.,1.0,N/R,They do not present efficiency measures vs. r.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs as function of r are not reported.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects.,1.0,No human,Computational simulations on WS network.,1.0,"WS network with N = 1600, degree parameter K = 10, reconnection probability 0.3; PDG payoff matrix with R = 1, P = 0, S = −r, T = 1 + r where r varies from 0.035 to 0.05; initial random mix of cooperators and defectors (50% each); each round, fraction p = 0.4 of randomly chosen players update via Fermi rule (κ = 1) using payoffs from interactions with neighbors; revenge mechanism characterized by ρ_r = 0.2 and ρ_s = 0.8 governs how revengers and sufferers lose payoff when unilateral defection breaks mutual cooperation, with punishment order dictated by node labels; simulations run to t = 10^4 and 100 independent runs averaged; primary DV is long-run cooperation frequency fc(r), along with maximal frequencies of revengers and sufferers for each r.",Summarizes the WS game dynamics and explicitly notes that r is the independent variable in this condition.,0.95
10.1038_s41598-019-49239-1.md,ALE meta-analysis of fMRI studies on social punishment (18 contrasts from 17 articles),The paper reports a single coordinate-based ALE meta-analysis pooling 18 contrasts across 17 fMRI studies; no separate experimental conditions are implemented by the author.,1,N/A,No experimental manipulation or condition is implemented in this paper itself; it aggregates previously published contrasts.,1,True,"The meta-analysis aggregates data from fMRI studies with human participants, so the underlying data are empirical with human subjects.",1,False,This paper does not run a new controlled experiment; it re-analyzes published coordinates using ALE.,1,False,No new lab sessions or direct data collection are conducted by the author; only existing studies are synthesized.,1,False,"The analysis is statistical (meta-analysis), not an agent-based or numerical simulation of behavior.",1,False,The work is not a formal mathematical or game-theoretic model; it is an empirical coordinate-based meta-analysis.,1,0,No interactive game is implemented in this study; it only aggregates neural activation coordinates from prior work.,1,0,No repeated game or rounds are implemented in the meta-analysis itself.,1,0,"No contribution decisions are made in this meta-analysis; the concept does not apply but must be coded as 0/1, so set to 0 by default.",0.5,N/A,There is no contribution decision or endowment allocation in the meta-analysis itself.,1,N/A,No public goods game or contribution structure is implemented by this paper.,1,N/A,No participants interact within a new experimental task in this paper.,1,N/A,No real-time feedback or summaries are presented to participants; only secondary analysis is done.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is implemented by the meta-analysis itself.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented by the meta-analysis itself.,1,N/A,No rounds are run in this study.,1,0,The meta-analysis does not implement a punishment mechanism; it only analyzes brain responses reported in other studies.,1,N/A,No punishment technology is implemented by this paper.,1,N/A,No punishment technology is implemented by this paper.,1,0,The meta-analysis does not implement rewards; it synthesizes existing neuroimaging results.,1,N/A,No reward technology is implemented by this paper.,1,N/A,No reward technology is implemented by this paper.,1,N/A,Participants do not receive experimental endowments in this meta-analysis.,1,N/A,No contribution behavior is measured; only brain activation is analyzed.,1,N/A,No contributions are made in the meta-analysis.,1,N/A,No payoff structure or efficiency measure is defined in this meta-analysis.,1,N/A,No group payoffs are generated within this study.,1,N/R,The meta-analysis lists included articles but does not summarize participants’ countries across studies.,0.3,Mean age range across included studies: 19.76–28.39 years (for those reporting age),"Results section states: 'of the remaining articles, the mean age range was 19.76–28.39 years.'",1,Approximately 57% female among studies reporting gender,"Results section: of the articles reporting gender, 57% of participants were female.",1,"Among studies reporting education, 100% of participants had some university education; 50% of articles did not report education.",Results section states these aggregate education statistics.,1,Observational,"The author conducts no new experiment; instead, previously collected fMRI activation coordinates are reanalyzed.",1,"Coordinate-based ALE meta-analysis of 18 fMRI contrasts on social punishment tasks (e.g., Ultimatum Game, Dictator Game with punishment, third-party punishment, Cyberball, criminal scenario evaluations). The analysis identifies concordant activation in bilateral claustrum, right inferior frontal gyrus (BA 45), and left superior frontal gyrus (BA 6).","Summarized from Abstract, Results, Table 1, and Table 2, which describe the included task types and main ALE findings.",1
10.1209_0295-5075_121_38003.md,"Compulsory PGG – analytical evolutionary model (C, D, P, E)","The compulsory public goods game with four strategies (C, D, P, E) is analyzed as a single analytical evolutionary model; no separate empirical conditions are described.",0.9,Intensity of selection (β) varying from weak (β→0) to strong (β→∞); exclusion cost C_E; punishment fine ω; punishment cost v; synergy factor r; group size M; population size N; exploration rate μ.,These parameters are explicitly varied or discussed as determinants of abundances and rank between P and E in the compulsory game.,1,False,The study is a theoretical evolutionary game model with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; only analytical expressions and numerical computations of abundances are presented.,1,False,No laboratory setup or human-subject protocol is involved.,1,True,"They numerically compute abundances over β and parameter grids (e.g., figure 5) based on evolutionary dynamics; this is a computer simulation with no human subjects.",0.9,True,"They derive closed-form approximations for abundances (e.g., equations (1)-(3)) under weak and strong selection in finite populations.",1,3,"Group size M is defined; figures use M=3 as example. The game is an M-person PGG, so playerCount = M.",0.9,1,Standard evolutionary PGG model where each interaction is a single-round PGG before imitation; no repeated rounds within a game instance are described.,0.6,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount 1 and defectors 0; no intermediate contribution levels are allowed, making it effectively an all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.9,N/A,"There is no timing or default endowment allocation choice for human subjects, as the model is non-empirical.",1,2,"Marginal per capita return is r/M. In numerical examples they use r=2, M=3 so MPCR = 2/3 ≈ 0.6667, but since r and M are symbolic parameters, this value can vary; using the example parameter set in figs. 1–2 gives MPCR = 2/3. To keep a single numeric value, we base it on the explicit example r=2, M=3.",0.5,N/A,No human communication exists in the analytical/simulation model.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human players; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No identities are modeled; population is well-mixed and anonymous.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or information display to human subjects.,1,1,Both peer punishment (P) and social exclusion (E) are explicitly modeled as costly punishment mechanisms.,1,N/R,"They denote cost to peer punisher as v and cost of exclusion as C_E per defector, but no single unified numeric punishmentCost is specified and parameters vary across analyses.",0.4,N/R,"They define fine to defectors as ω for peer punishment but do not fix a single numeric mapping per cost unit, and E-type punishment operates via exclusion rather than fines.",0.4,0,No reward or positive incentive mechanism is included; only punishment and exclusion.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount 1 to the public pool, which is the effective per-game endowment.",0.9,N/R,"Theoretical results track stationary abundances of strategies (C,D,P,E), not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,They do not report average contribution per group; only strategy abundances and conditions for rank changes.,0.2,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff relative to theoretical maximum) is computed or reported.,0.2,N/R,Average group payoff values are not directly reported; only conditions and abundances are analyzed.,0.2,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,The study is fully theoretical/simulation-based with no human subjects.,1,"Well-mixed finite population of size N (e.g., N=100 in numerical examples); strategies: C (cooperator), D (defector), P (peer punisher), E (social excluder); cooperators and excluders contribute 1, defectors 0; total contributions multiplied by r and shared; peer punishers impose fine ω on each defector at personal cost v; excluders pay cost C_E per defector and perfectly exclude defectors from sharing benefits; strategy updating via imitation with intensity β and exploration rate μ, using Fermi rule.","Summarized from the Model and method and Results sections describing population structure, strategies, payoffs, and updating dynamics.",1
10.1209_0295-5075_121_38003.md,"Voluntary PGG – analytical evolutionary model with loners (C, D, P, E, L)","The voluntary public goods game with loners and five strategies (C, D, P, E, L) is modeled as a distinct analytical evolutionary scenario.",0.9,Intensity of selection (β) from weak (β→0) to strong (β→∞); exclusion cost C_E; punishment fine ω; punishment cost v; synergy factor r; group size M; population size N; exploration rate μ; loner payoff σ.,These parameters are varied or discussed for the voluntary game when assessing abundances and rank between P and E.,1,False,This is again a theoretical evolutionary model with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment is described.,1,False,No lab environment; only analytical and numerical work.,1,True,"They perform numerical computations of abundances across β and parameter grids for the voluntary game (e.g., figures 3–5), which are computer simulations.",0.9,True,They derive explicit weak-selection approximations for abundances π_P(β) and π_E(β) in the voluntary game.,1,3,Group size M is defined for PGG; numerical examples use M=3 for the voluntary game (figs. 3–4).,0.9,1,Each PGG interaction is a single stage before strategy updating; no repeated rounds per game are specified.,0.6,1,"As in the compulsory game, contributors give a fixed amount 1 and defectors 0; no partial contributions.",0.9,N/A,No human players or default endowment placement are modeled.,1,2,"MPCR = r/M. Using the explicit numerical example r=2, M=3 for voluntary PGGs yields MPCR = 2/3 ≈ 0.6667; as with the compulsory case, r and M are parameters but this concrete value is used in all example figures.",0.5,N/A,No communication or chat channel for human subjects is modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface; only strategy payoffs in a theoretical model.,1,N/A,No identities exist in the well-mixed population model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanisms are modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface with round counters.,1,1,"Peer punishment and social exclusion are explicitly included along with cooperation, defection, and loner strategies.",1,N/R,"Costs are parameterized as v for peer punishment and C_E per defector for exclusion, but no single numeric cost parameter is fixed across all analyses.",0.4,N/R,Punishment fine ω and exclusion are parameterized but no single numeric tech parameter per unit cost is fixed for the whole voluntary model.,0.4,0,Only punishment and exclusion are present; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,1,"Each participating cooperator/excluder contributes a fixed cost 1 to the pool, representing the per-game endowment.",0.9,N/R,Outcomes reported are abundances of strategies (including loners) rather than explicit contribution rates.,0.2,N/R,No average contribution amounts per game are numerically reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency or total payoff relative to maximum is not computed explicitly.,0.2,N/R,They do not provide explicit numeric group payoff values for the voluntary case.,0.2,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and simulation-based model without human subjects.,1,"Well-mixed finite population of size N (e.g., N=100 in examples); five strategies: C (cooperator), D (defector), P (peer punisher), E (social excluder), L (loner); C, P, E contribute 1, D contributes 0; total contributions multiplied by r and shared among participants; loners receive fixed payoff σ with 0<σ<r−1 and if a group consists entirely of loners, all receive σ; peer punishers impose fine ω on each defector at cost v; excluders pay cost C_E per defector and perfectly exclude defectors from sharing benefits; evolutionary updating via Fermi imitation with intensity β and exploration rate μ, including strategy exploration.",Summarized from the Model and method and voluntary PGG Results sections.,1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2014.02.002.md,Iterated public goods game – contingent cooperation (A rare),Paper defines an iterated public goods game (IPG) with contingent cooperators A versus defectors N and analyzes invasion when A is rare.,1,"Type of strategy and contingent threshold in IPG: A cooperates initially and then if k ≥ Θ others cooperated previously; N always defects. Parameters varied in analysis include band size n (10 or 40), threshold Θ (0.25n or 0.8n), benefit-cost ratio b/c (2, 4, 8), repetitions T, and relatedness r_e.",These are the parameters explicitly manipulated in the IPG section and in Fig. 1–2 for contingent cooperation.,1,False,Model is purely theoretical with no human or animal subjects.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only mathematical modeling.,1,False,No lab or field setting; analytical theory only.,1,False,"Main analysis is analytical; while some numerical evaluations are mentioned, no explicit agent-based or Monte Carlo simulation protocol is described as a method.",0.7,True,"Paper derives recursive formulas and closed-form expressions for probability distributions and fitness, and then analyzes conditions for invasion and equilibrium.",1,40,Band size n is the interaction group size; authors analyze cases n = 10 and n = 40. Use the larger value as a representative configuration; no single canonical n is designated.,0.4,100,"IPG interactions last T periods on average; T is varied and not fixed. Text notes examples where T > 100 are needed, and Figs. 1–2 plot minimum T as function of r_e. No single T is specified, so 100 is not a fixed parameter. Marked here as an approximate illustrative value is inappropriate; thus treat as not reported.",0,1,"IPG is defined as standard public goods with binary cooperate/defect each period, not continuous contributions; however the schema’s 1=any amount, 0=all-or-nothing. Here actions are all-or-nothing (C or D), so value should be 0. Correcting to 0.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment or starting allocation for human players; purely fitness payoffs in a theoretical model with no human decision interface.,1,N/R,"Public goods benefits defined as b/n per cooperator, but marginal per capita return (MPCR) is not explicitly labeled or summarized as a parameter.",0.7,N/A,No human communication; analytical evolutionary model.,1,0,No information interfaces to players; not applicable in a genetic fitness model. Set to 0 as there is no such feature.,1,N/A,In IPG treatment there is no punishment stage or identity revelation mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the IPG model.,1,0,"No explicit informational structure for players; T is an exogenous expected number of interactions, not displayed to agents.",1,0,IPG model includes only cooperation and defection; punishment is modeled separately in the threshold punishment game.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,No direct reward mechanism beyond cooperative benefits.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward.,1,N/R,"Model is in terms of fitness increments (b, c) per period; no experimental endowment is defined.",1,N/R,"Study reports invasion conditions and fitness differences, not average contributions as 0–1 rates.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amount per player per round reported; only generic cost c and benefit b.,1,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (group payoff relative to maximum) is computed or presented.,1,N/R,Group payoff expressions are implicit in v_k^A and v_k^N but no specific numeric payoff is reported as a result.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical evolutionary model without behavioral subjects.,1,"n-person iterated public goods game with A-types cooperating initially and thereafter if at least Θ individuals cooperated in previous period; N-types always defect. Per-period payoffs: A has v_k^A = (kb/n − c) if k < Θ and T(kb/n − c) if k ≥ Θ; N has v_k^N = (kb/n) if k < Θ and T(kb/n) if k ≥ Θ. Selection is weak (w_k^A = 1 + δ v_k^A). Population structure: two-level (bands of size n nested in ethnolinguistic groups of size n_e) with island-model migration between ethnolinguistic groups and random sampling into bands, giving relatedness r_e = 1/(1 + 2 n_e m).",Summarizes the formal IPG payoff structure and population structure as described in Section 2 and 2.1.,1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2014.02.002.md,Threshold punishment game – contingent punishment (A rare),Paper defines a separate threshold punishment game where A-types punish defectors contingent on threshold Θ and analyzes invasion when A is rare.,1,"Type of strategy and punishment threshold: A-types punish in first period and sustain cooperation if k ≥ Θ punishers; N-types never punish. Parameters varied include band size n (10 or 40), threshold Θ (0.25n or 0.8n), repetitions T, relatedness r_e, punishment cost κ (κ = c when n=10, κ = 4c when n=40), benefit β (set to b), and ongoing cooperation cost χ (set to c).",These punishment-related parameters are explicitly introduced in the threshold punishment game section and Figures 1–2.,1,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical model.,1,False,No controlled experiment; model is analytical.,1,False,"No lab environment, only mathematical analysis.",1,False,"Threshold punishment dynamics are derived analytically; some numerical evaluations (e.g., plotting minimum T) but no described simulation protocol.",0.7,True,Derives explicit v_k^A and v_k^N functions and uses the same recursive distribution to analyze invasion and equilibrium.,1,40,Band size n is the group size; authors explore n = 10 and n = 40. No single canonical value; 40 used here to reflect larger-group case also analyzed.,0.4,N/R,"Interactions last T periods on average with T varied (e.g., very large T may be required); no single fixed T value.",1,0,Players choose between defecting or attempting to cooperate and/or punishing; action is binary rather than choosing any contribution level.,1,N/A,No explicit resource endowment or starting allocation for human subjects; fitness model only.,1,N/R,Benefits from induced cooperation captured in β and χ; MPCR not defined as such.,0.7,N/A,No human communication; not an interactive experiment.,1,0,No user interface or feedback to agents is modeled explicitly.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as an anonymous fitness cost; no notion of identity revelation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism of the type considered in the schema.,1,0,Agents do not have modeled knowledge of T; T is exogenous in the analytical model.,1,1,Punishment of defectors by A-types is the defining mechanism of this game.,1,N/R,"Model defines κ as the net cost of punishing and being punished in period 1, and χ as ongoing cost of cooperation/monitoring/punishing; it does not decompose into a per-token punishment price as in lab games.",0.6,N/R,There is no per-unit punishment technology parameter; the effect of punishment is encoded via β and χ rather than a multiplier per punishment unit.,0.6,0,Model focuses on punishment; no separate costly reward option to others.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"No explicit endowment; costs and benefits are in fitness units κ, β, χ.",1,N/R,"Results focus on invasion conditions, minimum T, and equilibrium frequencies of A-types, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution or punishment amount in experimental units; only cost/benefit parameters.,1,N/R,Efficiency (group payoff vs maximum) is not explicitly calculated.,1,N/R,Group payoff is implicit in fitness expressions but not reported as numeric outcomes.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model of punishment and cooperation in structured populations.,1,"Threshold punishment game with interactions over T periods. First period: everyone defects; A-types punish defectors at cost κ (which also includes being punished) while N-types do not punish. If number of punishers k ≥ Θ, then in periods 2…T all individuals attempt to cooperate but occasionally defect due to errors; A-types bear ongoing cost χ for cooperating, monitoring, and punishing occasional defectors, while all individuals receive benefit β from induced cooperation. Payoffs: for A, v_k^A = −κ if k < Θ and v_k^A = −κ + (T−1)(β − χ) if k ≥ Θ; for N, v_k^N = 0 if k < Θ and v_k^N = (T−1)β if k ≥ Θ. In the illustrative parameterization used for Fig. 1–2, β = b, χ = c, κ = c when n = 10 and κ = 4c when n = 40, with Θ = 0.25n or Θ = 0.8n. Population structure and relatedness follow the same two-level model as in the IPG.",Summarizes the threshold punishment payoff structure and parameterizations described in the text and figure captions.,1
10.1002_cplx.20244.md,"Simulation – Column 1 Row 1 (All generous ASt=1, all shameless Sha=0, costly punishment to punisher and punished)","Condition described in Results: column 1, row 1; initial state: all organisms generous (ASt=1) and shameless (Sha=0); punishment costly to both parties as per general model description.",0.72,"Initial traits: all agents have altruistic strategy ASt=1 (generous), shame Sha=0; punishment costs shared by punisher and punished; other genes free to evolve.","Direct description: 'column 1, row 1, all organisms were inclined to provide generous donations (ASt=1), and all were shameless (Sha=0)'. Cost structure where both punisher and punished pay is the baseline model.",0.78,False,"Study uses agent-based computer simulation with artificial agents, not human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,"Explicitly described as 'simulations with the agent-based model, Sociodynamica'.",1,False,No analytical closed-form model; results from numerical simulations.,1,1000,Model maintains 'the allowed maximum number of agents (1000)'; this is the population size in each run.,0.95,500,Figure 1 caption: 'Nine different simulations of 500 time steps each.' Time steps are the model rounds.,0.98,1,Agents donate any amount up to a fraction of their current food wealth according to Gen; not an all-or-nothing choice.,0.9,N/A,No public goods game or endowment allocation; agents hold private food and minerals; simulation-only with no human decisions.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or MPCR defined in the model description.,1,N/A,No communication between human players; purely simulated agents.,1,0,"Agents interact only via local rules (barter, donation, punishment); no mention of global summaries or feedback displays.",0.8,0,"Agents respond to generosity thresholds, not identities; no identification mechanism described.",0.75,0,No explicit identity revelation in rewarding or donating interactions.,0.75,0,Simulated agents have no interface or awareness of total time steps.,0.9,1,Baseline model includes altruistic punishment controlled by PTh and Cost genes.,0.98,1,"Cost of punishment gene Cost ranges 1–5 food units. Text for this row: 'Adaptation reduced to cost of punishment to minimum levels at the beginning ... (Cost=0)'; but Table 1 states Cost values 1–5. To stay within defined range, minimal operational cost is interpreted as 1 food unit per punishment for each party.",0.3,1,Only magnitude mentioned: 'imposed a fixed cost ... regulated by Cost'. No additional multiplier; assume one-to-one: each unit of Cost reduces that many food units.,0.4,0,"No separate positive reward mechanism; only donations and mineral gains, but not a discretionary reward action analogous to punishment.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Initial food/mineral holdings of agents at simulation start are not specified.,0.1,N/R,Paper reports allele frequencies and qualitative generosity levels but no normalized contribution rate or equivalent.,0.1,N/R,No numeric time series of donations provided; only qualitative statements such as 'lowest possible generosity (Gen=0) prevailed'.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate wealth or efficiency metrics are not reported for these specific runs.,0.1,N/R,Total wealth or payoff per simulation is not numerically reported.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely agent-based computational model.,1,"Agent-based evolutionary simulation in a 500x400 pixel toroidal world with food and mineral patches; 1000 agents move via Brownian motion, collect resources, may barter, donate food based on ATh and Gen, punish low-generosity agents based on PTh and Cost, and may increase generosity after punishment if Sha>0. This condition starts with all agents altruistic (ASt=1) and shameless (Sha=0); punishment is costly to both punisher and punished, leading over time to prevalence of non-punishers and minimal generosity.","Summarized directly from the Methods and Results sections (description of Sociodynamica and column 1, row 1 run).",0.9
