10.1016_j.comnet.2011.07.011.md,"Simulation – Comparison of average payoffs of P (punishment-based) vs R (reward-based, zero reward cost)","Section 6(3) and Fig. 15 compare average payoffs of P in punishment-based scheme with R in reward-based schemes (R rewards C and R vs only R, with cr=0).",0.9,"Comparison across schemes: (1) punishment-based with strategies {C, D, P, L}, parameters M = 500; N = 50; μ = 0.0001; β = 5; α1 = 10; cs = 1; ce = 0.1; cp = 0.2; cu = 0.1; (2) reward-based with ce = 0.1; α2 = 5; br = 0.1; cr = 0; other parameters as above, with two subcases: R rewards both C and R, and R rewards only R.","Figure 15 scenario: punishment-based scheme and reward-based scheme considered under these parameter sets, with α1 = α2 + N br to make schemes comparable.",0.82,False,Average payoffs computed from simulations and analytical stationary distributions.,1,False,Not an experiment with human participants.,1,False,"Simulation, not lab.",1,True,Average payoffs (trajectories in Fig. 15) obtained from running the evolutionary dynamics.,1,True,Analytical reasoning relates α1 and α2+N br and discusses theoretical equivalence of ideal reward-based and punishment-based performance.,1,500,Scenario: M=500 for both schemes.,1,100000,Average payoff curves over many rounds; earlier they specify 10^5 rounds for similar simulations.,0.7,1,In both schemes agents either contribute or not and possibly punish/reward; no degrees.,0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment in tokens.,1,N/R,"Comparison is in terms of average payoffs, not MPCR.",0.3,N/A,No human players.,1,N/R,Not an experimental UI variable.,0.3,N/A,Anonymity assumed; not modeled.,1,N/A,Anonymity assumed; not modeled.,1,N/R,Rounds not displayed to agents.,0.3,1,Punishment scheme side has active punishment.,1,0.1,cu=0.1 in punishment-based part of comparison.,0.9,0.2,cp=0.2 in punishment-based part.,0.9,1,Reward-based schemes have R with rewards.,1,0,"cr=0 in the reward-based comparison, as stated.",1,0.1,br=0.1 used for reward-based comparison.,0.9,N/R,No token endowments described.,0.3,N/R,"Key DV is average payoff of P vs R, not contribution rate.",0.3,N/R,Not reported; contribution levels implicit in strategies.,0.3,N/R,Effectiveness inferred via payoffs; efficiency ratio not provided.,0.3,N/R,"Graph in Fig. 15 shows average payoff of P or R individuals, not group payoff values.",0.2,N/A,Simulation-only.,1,N/A,Simulation-only.,1,N/A,Simulation-only.,1,N/A,Simulation-only.,1,No human,Comparison via simulation and analytic reasoning.,1,"Cross-scheme comparison aligning total system benefit by setting α1 = α2 + N br, so ideal full-P punishment state and full-R reward state deliver same system benefit when reward cost is zero. Simulations show that average payoff of P in the punishment-based scheme can be almost the same as in the ideal but infeasible reward-based case where R rewards only R with zero cost; when R must reward C as well (br to both), punishment-based scheme yields higher average payoff to cooperators/punishers than reward-based scheme.",Summarized from Section 6(3) and explanation of Fig. 15.,0.94
10.1016_j.joep.2017.03.007.md,UP – Unrestricted Punishment baseline,"Baseline treatment described in 3.1.1 as 'UP treatment, punishment is unrestricted' and used as benchmark.",1,Punishment institution: unrestricted decentralized peer punishment; any group member can punish any other member.,Section 3.1.1 explicitly defines UP as unrestricted punishment where everyone can punish everyone else.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 276 human subjects across treatments; UP is one of these treatments.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments and fixed game parameters.,1,True,Conducted at CEEL Lab of the University of Trento.,1,False,All data are from human subjects; no agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model as a standalone method; focus is experimental data.,1,4,Section 3.2: 'groups of size N=4'.,1,20,Section 3.1: 'played ... for 20 periods' for all treatments.,1,1,"Stage 1: each participant decides g_i ≤ 20 tokens; continuous 0–20 choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each participant receives 20 tokens endowment and then chooses how many to contribute; endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,Payoff: (20 - g_i) + 0.4 * sum g_j; MPCR is 0.4.,1,0,No communication allowed; instructions: 'you cannot communicate with other participants'.,1,1,"After contributions, subjects see all group members’ individual contributions; during punishment they see feedback on earnings; implies summary of others’ contributions and (in UP) punishment received.",0.9,0,Stage 2: 'Punishment is anonymous: subjects who get punished do not know the identity of the punisher.',1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Instructions mention 'The experiment is composed by 20 rounds' and round number appears on screen; so total rounds known.,1,1,UP includes a punishment stage where participants assign costly punishment points.,1,0,"Cost function Table 1: cost in tokens per total points is convex and non-linear (0,1,2,4,6,9,...). Cannot