10.1209_0295-5075_111_60003.md,Well-mixed population – analytical replicator dynamics with mutual punishment,Paper presents closed-form replicator equation analysis for well-mixed populations with mutual punishment characterized by temptation r and punishment fine α.,1,Temptation to defect r (0<r<1) and punishment fine α≥0 varied continuously; mutual punishment always present.,Model section defines SG with parameter r and punishment fine α; results describe cooperation fraction as function of r for different α and α–r phase diagram.,1,False,No human or animal participants; purely theoretical population model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment but an analytical evolutionary-game model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely mathematical.,1,False,Well-mixed part solved by replicator equation; no numerical agent-based simulation described for this case.,0.9,True,"They derive payoffs P_c, P_d and solve replicator dynamics, obtaining equilibria and stability conditions analytically.",1,0,Infinite well-mixed population assumed; no fixed finite player count.,1,0,Continuous-time replicator dynamics; no discrete repeated rounds.,1,1,"Not a contribution game; players choose one of two pure strategies C or D, not variable contribution levels; encoded as 1 since binary strategy not constrained contribution amount concept.",0.5,N/A,"Snowdrift game with strategy choice, not an endowment-contribution setup and no human subjects.",1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,0,No interface or feedback structure with subjects; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No identities or agents beyond anonymous types in analytical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identities.,1,0,No human interface or display of rounds.,1,1,Core mechanism is mutual punishment with fine α; includes α=0 as special case but model defined with punishment term.,1,N/R,They state cost is absorbed into punishment fine α; no separate cost-per-unit specified.,0.8,N/R,α directly subtracts from payoff for mismatched strategies; no discrete mapping like ‘fine per coin’ specified.,0.7,0,Only punishment is modeled; no reward term described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Stage-game payoffs normalized; no explicit endowment in tokens or money.,1,N/R,"Outcome variable is fraction of cooperators ρ_c, not contribution rate from an endowment.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts; binary strategies only.,1,N/R,They do not report efficiency as ratio of realized to maximum payoff.,1,N/R,Only individual payoffs and fraction of cooperators are analyzed; no group payoff reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical evolutionary-game model.,1,"Two-player snowdrift game with payoffs R=1, T=1+r, S=1, P=0; mutual punishment subtracts fine α for each pair of players with different strategies, symmetrically.",Model section defines payoff matrix M and punishment term with α for mismatched strategies.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_111_60003.md,Structured population – agent-based lattice simulation with mutual punishment,Section 'Results for structured populations' describes simulations on a 100×100 square lattice using Fermi updating with mutual punishment.,1,"Temptation to defect r and punishment fine α varied systematically over ranges (approximately r∈(0,1), α≥0).","Figures 3–6 and text analyze ρ_c as a function of r for different α, of α for different r, and provide α–r cooperation map.",1,False,Simulation of abstract agents on lattice; no human subjects involved.,1,False,"Numerical simulation, not an empirical experiment with subjects.",1,False,No physical lab or field setting; computational study.,1,True,Described as simulations on a square lattice with asynchronous updates and Fermi rule.,1,False,This part refers to stochastic simulations; pair-approximation analysis is covered conceptually but primary data are from simulations.,0.7,10000,Population on a 100×100 square lattice; every lattice point occupied by a player.,1,50000,"They state simulations run for 50000 time steps, each consisting of on average one strategy-updating event per player.",1,1,Agents choose between two pure strategies C or D; no graded contributions.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game and no human endowments.,1,N/R,Snowdrift pairwise game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,Artificial agents in simulation; no communication channel modeled.,1,0,No user interface; only local payoffs used for imitation.,1,N/A,No identities or observation features; only local strategic interactions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identities.,1,0,No human interface; agents do not observe horizon length.,1,1,Mutual punishment with fine α applied whenever neighboring strategies differ.,1,N/R,Cost for imposing punishment is said to be absorbed into punishment fine α; no separate per-unit cost specified.,0.8,N/R,Payoff formula subtracts α for each mismatched neighbor; not expressed as discrete ‘fine per coin spent’.,0.8,0,"Model contains only punishment, no reward terms.",1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/R,Simulation uses normalized game payoffs; no explicit endowment specified.,1,N/R,"Outcome variable is fraction of cooperators ρ_c, not a contribution rate from endowment.",1,N/R,Agents do not choose contribution amounts; only binary strategies C or D.,1,N/R,They report only ρ_c and phase structure; no explicit efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Average group or population payoff is not reported; only cooperation levels and phases.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational agent-based simulations with no human subjects.,1,"Agents occupy sites on a 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; each interacts with four nearest neighbors via snowdrift payoff matrix M=[[1,1−r],[1+r,0]] minus mutual punishment fine α for each neighbor with opposite strategy. Strategies are updated asynchronously with Fermi imitation rule W(s_x←s_y)=1/[1+exp(−(P_y−P_x)/K)] with K=0.1. Initial strategies are random with equal probability 0.5 for cooperators and defectors.",Detailed in 'Results for structured populations' and model section.,1
10.1098_rspb.2021.1773.md,Punishment – Anonymous/Random,"Condition where third-parties could punish unfair dictators, bystanders had no knowledge of Stage A behaviour, and matching for Stage C was random; corresponds to punishment × random allocation/no knowledge treatment.",0.95,"Signal type: punishment; Observability/partner-choice treatment: anonymous/random (no knowledge, random allocation)","Between-subject factors are signal type (punish vs help) and three-level treatment (anonymous/random, knowledge/random, knowledge/choice). This row is the punishment × anonymous/random cell.",0.98,True,"Uses MTurk human participants as dictators, third-parties, and bystanders.",1,True,Random assignment to roles and treatments with controlled payoffs and instructions.,1,False,Data collected online via MTurk rather than in a physical lab.,1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations reported.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as primary method.,1,3,"Stage A involves a dictator, a receiver, and a third-party; the strategic interaction relevant to punishment/help is between dictator and third-party with receiver passive. Group size taken as 3 including passive receiver.",0.7,1,Single-shot decisions in Stage A using the strategy method; no repeated rounds reported.,0.9,1,"Third-parties could invest any amount between $0.00 and $0.45 in $0.01 increments, i.e., continuous contribution not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Dictator and third-party both receive endowments in private accounts; nothing is initially in a public fund.,0.9,N/R,This is not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No communication or chat mentioned; decisions made in isolation.,0.95,0,"Participants are informed about a specific counterpart’s behaviour or allocation, not about payoff or punishment/reward summaries across others.",0.8,0,Online anonymous MTurk interactions; no identities revealed when punishment is applied.,0.95,0,No rewarding mechanism in the punishment condition.,1,0,One-shot structure; no mention of rounds or displaying remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Third-parties could pay to punish an unfair dictator in this condition.,1,1,"Fee-to-fine ratio is 1:1; one cent paid inflicts one cent loss, so cost per unit = 1.",1,1,"With a 1:1 fee-to-fine ratio, each unit spent causes one unit of damage; magnitude per coin = 1.",1,0,"Third-parties either punish or help, not both; this punishment condition has no helping/compensation option.",1,N/A,No help/reward mechanism in pure punishment condition.,1,N/A,No help/reward mechanism in pure punishment condition.,1,0.5,Third-parties were endowed with $0.50.,1,N/R,Study reports mean dollar investments but not normalized rates; a single summary rate for this treatment is not provided.,0.2,0.09,"For all punishment conditions combined, mean investment was $0.09; treatment-specific means are not reported, so this is the best available aggregate for punishment.",0.4,N/R,No analysis of efficiency (total payoff vs. maximum) is presented.,1,N/R,No raw group payoff figures by condition are reported.,1,N/R,"Participants are MTurk workers, but no country breakdown given.",0.2,N/R,No ages reported.,0.01,N/R,No gender information reported.,0.01,N/R,No education information reported.,0.01,Online,All participants were recruited via Amazon Mechanical Turk and completed the study online.,1,"Stage A: Dictator Game variant with unfair vs fair choice; third-party can pay from $0.00 to $0.45 (1:1 fee-to-fine) to punish unfair dictator; decisions via strategy method contingent on dictator being unfair. Stage C: some third-parties play Trust Game as trustees, but this punishment condition’s defining feature is unobserved third-party punishment.",Summarizes the game structure and decision context for this treatment.,0.95
