10.1109_tnse.2023.3279094.md,Analytical / simulation model of PGG with institutional incentives on regular networks,"Paper presents a theoretical evolutionary public goods game model on regular networks with institutional reward and punishment, analyzed via pair approximation and verified by numerical calculations; there are no distinct experimental conditions with human subjects.",0.9,"Allocation ratio of incentive budget to reward vs punishment (ω), efficiency of reward (a), efficiency of punishment (b), efficiency ratio α = a/b, per capita incentive δ, network degree k, multiplication factor r, cooperation cost c, selection intensity s, and cooperator frequency x","These parameters are systematically varied or treated as control variables when deriving optimal protocols and numerical results (e.g., α, δ, k, r, c, s, ω(x)); no human treatment conditions are defined.",0.95,False,Study is entirely theoretical and numerical with no mention of human participants or empirical data collection.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human or animal subjects is conducted; work is analytical with numerical calculations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only mathematical analysis and simulations.,1,True,"Authors provide numerical calculations of the derived dynamical equations (e.g., Fig. 1–5) without human subjects, which qualifies as computer simulation.",0.95,True,Core of the paper is derivation of dynamical equations and optimal protocols via pair approximation and analytical proofs (Theorems 1 and 2).,1,0,Population is assumed infinitely large on a regular network; no finite player count is specified because this is a theoretical model.,0.9,0,Model is formulated in continuous time with evolutionary dynamics rather than discrete experimental rounds.,0.9,1,"Each cooperator invests a fixed amount cost c and defectors invest nothing; there is no option of partial contributions, so contributions are effectively all-or-nothing at the individual level.",1,N/A,No human subjects or explicit experimental interface; notion of default allocation of endowment does not apply.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not explicitly defined; only public-benefit factor r and group size k+1 are specified, but MPCR is not named or used.",0.5,N/A,Model has no communication component and no human interface.,1,0,No information display to agents is modeled beyond payoff-based imitation; no summaries of others’ outcomes are described.,0.9,N/A,Institutional reward/punishment is modeled as payoff modifications; no identities or visibility structure are defined as there are no human players.,1,N/A,Same reasoning as for punishment; no identities.,1,0,Continuous-time evolutionary model; no explicit number of rounds is shown to agents.,1,1,Institution allocates part of fixed budget to punish defectors ((1−ω)(k+1)δ with efficiency b); ω=0 corresponds to pure punishment.,1,N/R,Model specifies that defectors’ payoffs are reduced by b(1−ω)(k+1)δ/(k+1−i) but does not define an explicit cost per punishment unit to any punisher; punishment is institutional.,0.6,N/R,"b is labeled as efficiency of punishment, but not as a per-unit technical effect in the sense requested; no clear mapping to ‘tokens spent per unit punishment’.",0.6,1,Institution allocates ω(k+1)δ to reward cooperators with efficiency a; ω=1 corresponds to pure reward.,1,N/R,"Institution has per capita incentive δ, but no explicit per-unit cost of reward in tokens is specified; cost is budgetary, not per individual action.",0.6,N/R,a is efficiency of reward but not decomposed into a clear per-unit technology parameter as defined in schema.,0.6,N/R,"Each cooperator invests cost c in each game, but a starting per-round endowment in tokens is not defined; only a cost parameter c.",0.7,N/R,"Analyses focus on fraction of cooperators x and payoffs, not normalized contribution rates per endowment.",0.6,N/R,"While cooperators contribute a fixed cost c per game, no empirical outcome statistics (means, distributions) are reported; equations treat c as parameter, not measured DV.",0.7,N/R,Paper discusses evolutionary outcomes and equilibria but does not report efficiency as ratio of actual to maximum possible payoff.,0.7,N/R,"Analytical expressions for individual payoffs exist, but no summary group payoff outcomes are reported as a primary dependent variable.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Work is analytical and simulation-based without human or field data.,1,"Public goods game on an infinitely large regular network with degree k; group size k+1; each cooperator pays fixed cost c, total contributions multiplied by r and equally shared; institutional incentive per group is fixed at (k+1)δ each round, allocated between rewarding cooperators and punishing defectors via state-dependent ratio ω(x); strategy updating via pairwise imitation with Fermi function under weak selection.",Summarizes the specified structural details of the theoretical PGG and evolutionary dynamics from the Model and Method section.,1
10.1007_s10144-013-0423-0.md,Entrance fee model – analytical condition (no explicit parameter treatment split),"Paper presents a single analytical model (entrance fee model) with varying parameters F, t0, α in sensitivity analyses but not as distinct experimental treatments with outcome data; treat as one analytical condition.",0.7,"Entrance fee q (continuous decision variable), number of traditional divers F, baseline tourist interest t0, sensitivity of tourists to resource level α, tourist sensitivity to fee β, catching efficiencies a (divers) and b (tourists), fish growth parameters r and K, resource price p.",These parameters are varied in the model and determine optimal entrance fee and tourist numbers; no discrete treatment labels are defined.,0.9,False,Model is purely mathematical with no human subjects; describes divers and tourists but only via differential equations.,1,False,No controlled experiment or intervention with human participants; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; theoretical model only.,1,True,Model uses numerical calculation of optimal q and steady states for given parameter sets; no agents but still a numerical simulation of differential equations.,0.8,True,Main results derived analytically from differential equations and optimization; explicit formulas and qualitative analysis.,1,0,No explicit finite number of players; tourists and divers represented by continuous variables T and F.,1,0,No repeated-game or round structure; continuous-time dynamics instead.,1,1,Contribution concept from public goods does not apply; model is not a contribution game. Use 1 by convention since continuous choices of effort/fee exist rather than binary all-or-nothing.,0.3,N/A,No human-subject contribution game structure; only resource dynamics and economic variables.,1,N/R,No public goods game; marginal per capita return not defined.,1,N/A,No human experiment; communication not modeled.,1,0,"No experimental interface; not applicable, but coded 0 as there is no feedback structure between human subjects.",0.3,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this model.,1,N/A,No rewards or peer identification modeled.,1,0,No discrete rounds; continuous time model. Use 0.,0.5,0,No sanctioning or punishment mechanism included in the entrance fee model.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward for cooperation; only economic earnings.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No endowment per player in a game-theoretic sense; only resource stock and economic parameters.,1,N/R,No contribution game; no such dependent variable.,1,N/R,No individual contributions defined.,1,N/R,Efficiency as defined for public goods games is not reported; model reports total benefit Φ but not normalized to a public-goods maximum.,0.7,N/R,Total benefit Φ is discussed conceptually but no single numeric value is reported as a result; various parameter examples exist without final numeric summary.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical ecological-economic model with no experimental implementation.,1,Bioeconomic model of a common fishing ground with traditional divers and tourists; fishing association chooses entrance fee q to maximize total benefit Φ = p a F X + q T given logistic resource growth and tourist demand depending on resource and fee.,Summary based directly on the entrance fee model description and equations (1–3).,1
10.1007_s10144-013-0423-0.md,Restricted fishing-ground model – analytical condition (no explicit parameter treatment split),Paper presents a single restricted fishing-ground model with cases (high/low migration m and α=0 or 1) but does not treat them as separate empirical treatments; aggregate as one analytical condition.,0.7,"Fraction of fishing ground open to tourists s, fish migration rate m between areas, tourist sensitivity to resource level α, baseline tourist interest t0, tourist per-capita disturbance b, diver effort F, fish growth parameters r and K, resource price p, tourist shop expenditure a_shop.",These variables determine optimal s* and outcomes; the paper explicitly examines qualitative cases for m (very high/low) and α (0 or 1).,0.9,False,Model is deterministic and theoretical with no human subjects.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with observed data; only modeling.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,True,Optimal s* is computed under different parameter regimes; system of ODEs is analyzed numerically and analytically.,0.8,True,"The model is analyzed using steady-state approximations and inequalities (e.g., conditions (7a,b) for s*).",1,0,"Divers and tourists modeled as aggregate effort and flows, not as discrete players.",1,0,Continuous-time ecological dynamics with no round structure.,1,1,"No contribution decision; continuous variables (s, T, F) instead of discrete contribution/no-contribution.",0.3,N/A,No public goods endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined; this is not a PGG.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0,No experimental interface or feedback between human subjects.,0.3,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No rounds defined.,0.5,0,"Model involves allocation of fishing ground, not punishment.",1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism; only profit outcomes.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No per-player endowment.,1,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,"Model reports total benefit Φ; not normalized against a public-goods maximum, and no numeric efficiency reported.",0.7,N/R,Total benefit Φ is discussed but specific numeric outcomes for each parametric case are not fully tabulated.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The restricted fishing-ground model is purely theoretical.,1,Two-area bioeconomic model with a protected area for local divers and a tourist area. Fish populations X1 and X2 follow logistic growth with migration m and are harvested by divers and tourists. Tourist numbers T depend on the tourist-area resource level X2 via T = t0 X2^α. Fishing association chooses fraction s of fishing ground open to tourists to maximize total benefit Φ = p a F/(1−s) X1 + a_shop T.,"Summary from equations (4a, 4b, 5, 6) and subsequent optimality conditions.",1
10.1007_s10144-013-0423-0.md,Awareness-raising model – dynamic optimization condition,Paper presents a single dynamic optimization model for allocating resources between conservation quality x and social concern y; no separate experimental treatments.,0.9,"Control variable u(t), the fraction of resources allocated to awareness-raising; parameters μ (decay rate of quality), v (decay rate of social concern), g0 (baseline support level), α (concavity of support function), f (efficiency of awareness activities), δ (discount rate), initial states x0 and y0.","u(t) is the decision variable optimized; model behavior depends on listed parameters, which are varied in examples.",1,False,No participants or empirical data; entirely theoretical.,1,False,"Optimization and dynamic programming, not an experiment.",1,False,No lab setting; theoretical model.,1,True,Dynamic programming is used to compute optimal trajectories numerically.,0.9,True,Pontryagin’s maximum principle is used to derive analytical expression for stationary social concern y*.,1,0,No explicit individual players; only aggregate variables for conservation quality and social concern.,1,0,Continuous-time infinite-horizon model; no discrete rounds.,1,1,"Control u(t) ranges continuously in [0,1]; not an all-or-nothing decision.",0.8,N/A,No public good endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,No interface or feedback to human subjects.,0.3,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No rounds.,0.5,0,Manager only allocates resources; no sanctions on individuals.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward to individuals; only social support function g(y).,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No per-player endowment variable.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are x(t) and y(t), not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution amount defined.,1,N/R,Objective is time-discounted integral φ = ∫ e^{-δ t} x(t) dt; no efficiency ratio reported.,0.8,N/R,Model focuses on conservation quality and social concern; no group payoff in tokens or money.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Dynamic optimization of a conservation management problem without empirical implementation.,1,"Dynamic optimization model where a manager allocates fraction u(t) of monetary support g(y)=g0 y^α to awareness-raising, which increases social concern y, and 1−u(t) to direct conservation improving quality x. Dynamics: dx/dt = (1−u) g − μ x, dy/dt = f u g − v y. Objective is to maximize discounted integral of x over an infinite horizon.",Directly summarizes equations (8–11) and text description.,1
10.1007_s10144-013-0423-0.md,Graduated punishment model – homogeneous population,Graduated punishment section analyzes two main cases: homogeneous and heterogeneous populations in terms of sensitivity parameter β. This entry captures the homogeneous case.,0.95,"Punishment level P, harm to society H, benefit from selfish action B, sensitivity to utility differences β (constant across players), evaluation error probabilities ε1 (false accusation) and ε2 (missed offense).",These parameters determine the optimal punishment P* in the homogeneous population analysis.,1,False,"Model is theoretical; player behavior is specified by quantal response equilibrium, not observed.",1,False,No experimental design or human data; only analytical welfare maximization.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,"Optimal P* is illustrated numerically as a function of H (e.g., curve in Fig. 7) based on the analytical expressions.",0.8,True,"Utilities and social welfare are derived analytically; optimal punishment characterized for B>H and B<H without errors, and with errors ε1, ε2.",1,0,Total number of players N appears symbolically; model uses fractions Ns/N and Np/N without specifying a concrete N.,1,0,"Static choice model (one-shot decision), not iterated rounds.",1,0,Players choose between two discrete options: prosocial or selfish; this is an all-or-nothing cooperation choice in standard public-goods terminology.,0.9,N/A,No formal endowment or default allocation; only utilities for two actions.,1,N/R,"Model is a generic social dilemma with harm H and benefit B, not an explicit linear public goods game with MPCR.",1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,"Players respond to utilities and punishment, not to feedback summaries; none mentioned.",0.7,N/R,Model specifies only that selfish players are punished; anonymity/identification of punished individuals is not discussed.,0.3,N/A,No rewards or positive sanctions in model.,1,0,Single decision; no horizon information.,1,1,Central focus is on a punishment level P applied to selfish players.,1,N/R,Model does not specify cost of administering punishment to punishers or an institution; only disutility P to selfish players is modeled.,0.4,N/R,Technology mapping cost to inflicted harm is not defined; P directly reduces utility of selfish players.,0.5,0,Only sanctions against selfish actions; no prosocial rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Utilities λ, B, H, P are specified, but no endowment per se.",0.7,N/R,Main outcome is optimal punishment P* and fraction Ns/N choosing selfish action; contribution rate per se is not reported numerically.,0.6,N/R,No token contributions; only binary action.,1,N/R,"Per capita social welfare Φ/N is defined, but no normalized efficiency measure in [0,1] is reported.",0.8,N/R,Φ/N is expressed symbolically; no single numeric payoff given for particular parameter sets in the homogeneous case.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Game is analyzed theoretically with quantal response; no experiment.,1,"Two-action social dilemma (prosocial vs selfish). Utilities: Up = λ − (Ns/N) H − ε1 P, Us = λ − (Ns/N) H + B − (1−ε2) P. Players follow quantal response: probability of selfish action is 1 / (1 + exp[−β (B−P_eff)]). Social welfare Φ/N = λ + (Ns/N)(B−P−H). Optimal punishment P* is chosen to maximize welfare; in homogeneous populations with QRE and evaluation errors, P* shows a large jump at low H and then increases slowly with H.","Summarized from equations (13a,b), (14–16) and narrative for homogeneous population curve in Fig. 7.",1
10.1007_s10144-013-0423-0.md,Graduated punishment model – heterogeneous population,Second main analytical condition in graduated punishment model where sensitivity β is heterogeneous (exponentially distributed).,0.95,"Punishment level P, harm to society H, benefit from selfish action B, distribution of sensitivity parameter β (exponential with mean β̄), evaluation error probabilities ε1 and ε2.",These are the key determinants of the optimal punishment in the heterogeneous population analysis.,1,False,"Still a purely theoretical model; heterogeneity is assumed, not measured.",1,False,"Analytical and numerical, not experimental.",1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,True,"Optimal P*(H) is numerically computed and graphed for heterogeneous β (e.g., curve in Fig. 7 and probabilities in Fig. 8b).",0.9,True,Expression for Ns/N involves integrating the logit choice over an exponential distribution of β; an approximate analytical formula P_approx ≈ B + sqrt(H / (ε1 β̄)) is derived.,1,0,"As before, model uses fractions and integrals over β, not a specified N.",1,0,One-shot decision model.,1,0,Players choose fully prosocial or fully selfish; binary choice.,0.9,N/A,No endowment schema.,1,N/R,Not a standard linear PGG with MPCR.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,No description of information on others’ behavior.,0.7,N/R,An institutional punishment is assumed but anonymity is not discussed.,0.3,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Single-decision framework.,1,1,Central variable P represents level of punishment to selfish actors.,1,N/R,Cost to punishers/institution is not specified.,0.4,N/R,Punishment directly reduces selfish players’ utilities by P_eff; no cost-to-impact mapping provided.,0.5,0,No positive incentives defined.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Model is formulated in terms of utilities λ, B, H, P; no explicit endowment.",0.7,N/R,Analysis yields the fraction of selfish players Ns/N and optimal punishment P*(H); contribution rate of prosocial actions is 1−Ns/N but exact numeric values are not tabulated.,0.6,N/R,Binary choice only; no token amounts.,1,N/R,"Per capita welfare Φ/N is defined, but the paper focuses on P*(H) shape rather than giving efficiencies.",0.8,N/R,No numeric group payoff reported for specific parameter combinations in heterogeneous case.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Graduated punishment model is purely theoretical in this case as well.,1,"Same two-action social dilemma as in homogeneous case, but players’ sensitivity β to utility differences is heterogeneous and follows an exponential distribution with mean β̄. Fraction selfish is Ns/N = ∫ [1 / (1 + e^{−β(B−P_eff)})] (e^{−β/β̄}/β̄) dβ. With evaluation errors ε1 and ε2, optimal punishment P*(H) is zero for small H, then jumps to a positive level and thereafter increases gradually with H, approximately following P_approx ≈ B + sqrt(H / (ε1 β̄)) for large H, yielding a graduated punishment pattern.","Summarizes integral expression for Ns/N, qualitative description of Fig. 7 heterogeneous curve, and approximation (Eq. 20).",1
