10.1073_pnas.1704032114.md,"Evolutionary RL Moran simulation – evolution of social rewards, varying punishment cost c","Authors embed RL agents with intrinsic biases into a Moran evolutionary process, varying c to see whether rigid punishment or theft evolves (Fig. 4B).",0.95,"Punishment cost c varied from 0.1 to 10; benefit of theft s fixed at 5; punishment severity p fixed at 15; intrinsic bias genotype (theft bias, punish-theft bias) allowed to mutate.",They state they vary c while holding other payoffs constant and allow evolution over theft and punishment biases.,0.95,False,Agent-based evolutionary simulation with RL agents.,1,False,No human subjects; not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab involved.,1,True,Described as Moran simulations of evolving Q-learning agents.,1,False,Uses numerical Moran process and cached game outcomes.,1,2,Each interaction is between a thief and a victim in a two-player game.,1,N/R,They mention ‘100 full games’ cached per genotype pairing but do not give the number of rounds per game.,0.2,1,Actions are discrete steal/punish choices; no graded contributions.,0.4,N/A,Not a contribution game and no humans.,1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,N/A,Agents do not communicate.,1,N/A,No summary feedback UI.,1,N/A,Agents lack identities beyond roles.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit knowledge of horizon presented.,1,1,Victim agents can choose to punish theft at cost c.,1,N/R,Punishment cost c is systematically varied over 0.1–10; single numeric value not applicable.,0.6,15,Severity p (loss to thief when punished) is fixed at 15 across simulations while c varies; treat 15 as punishment magnitude per punishment action.,0.7,0,No prosocial rewarding mechanism; only punishment and theft.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,Per-round endowment not specified in evolutionary simulations.,0.2,N/R,Primary outcomes are fraction of simulations where bias for punishing vs stealing evolves.,1,N/R,No contribution variable present.,1,N/R,They do not report aggregate efficiency metrics; focus on evolutionary prevalence of hedonic biases.,1,N/R,No group payoff statistic summarized.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary agent-based simulation only.,1,"Population of K agents in Moran process; each agent carries genotype (theft bias, punish-theft bias) that modifies experienced reward for stealing or punishing. Baseline reward equals objective game payoff; nonzero biases add or subtract fixed integer rewards for the corresponding action, effectively implementing rigid strategies like AS, AP, NS, NP when large. Each generation: all agents play each other as both thief and victim; fitness equals average payoff; one agent dies at random; one reproduces with probability proportional to exp(w f_i); offspring mutates genotype with probability μ. For tractability, outcomes of each genotype pair are precomputed by simulating 100 full games and cached.",Summarized from The Evolution of Social Rewards and RL Simulations sections.,0.9
10.1073_pnas.1704032114.md,Moran evolutionary simulation – strategy evolution with fixed θ (baseline evolutionary analysis simulation),"The ‘Moran Process Simulations’ section describes simulations where strategies from an extended space (including reactive strategies and a flexible strategy) evolve in a small population, varying θ from 0 to 1.",0.9,Parameter θ (probability thief learns to steal and victim stops punishing when two flexible agents meet) varied from 0 to 1; mutation rate μ and selection intensity w varied across robustness checks.,They explicitly vary θ; also explore different w and μ in SI.,0.9,False,No human participants; purely computational Moran process.,1,False,"Simulation, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,Described as Moran process simulations of strategy evolution.,1,False,This part reports numeric outcomes of stochastic simulations rather than closed-form ESS analysis.,1,2,Each game instance is between a thief and a victim.,1,N/R,They say agents ‘play each other’ and get average payoffs but do not specify rounds per match.,0.2,1,Action sets are discrete strategies (reactive and flexible) in a repeated binary choice game.,0.4,N/A,Not a public goods contribution setting; also simulation only.,1,N/R,No MPCR applicable.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,N/A,No human-visible summaries.,1,N/A,Identities not relevant in simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents not informed of number of rounds.,1,1,Game includes punishment option with cost c and harm p.,1,N/R,Parameters c and p sampled from ranges in Table S1; no single value for this condition.,0.3,N/R,"p is sampled/ranged, not fixed; exact magnitude depends on parameter draw.",0.3,0,"Model considers costly punishment, not reward.",1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,Per-round endowment not defined; only payoff increments.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes measured as which equilibrium strategy the population converges to, not contribution behavior.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They report proportion of simulations converging to each strategy, not efficiency scores.",1,N/R,Aggregate game payoffs not summarized as a DV.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Moran process over simulated agents only.,1,Population of K agents; each agent has a thief and victim strategy from an expanded space including all four reactive strategies and a flexible strategy (details in SI). Each generation: all agents play each other as thief and victim; fitness equals average payoff; one agent dies at random; another reproduces according to softmax over w·f_i; offspring mutates strategy pair with probability μ. Parameter θ encodes the probability flexible–flexible encounters end in AS/NP vs NS/AP equilibrium.,Summarized from Moran Simulations section.,0.85
10.1073_pnas.1704032114.md,"Behavioral experiment – focal game, thief role against rigid punisher","In the behavioral experiment, some MTurk participants were assigned as thieves in a focal game against an opponent who always punishes theft.",0.95,"Participant role = thief; opponent strategy = always punish theft; payoffs fixed at s=2, c=1, p=3.",Role (thief vs victim) and opponent type (always steal vs always punish) are manipulated; this row is the thief against rigid punisher condition.,0.95,True,Human participants from Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to roles and opponent conditions with controlled payoffs.,1,False,"Conducted online via Mechanical Turk, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Uses real human choices, not simulated agents.",1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,2,Each game is between a participant and one opponent.,1,20,They state ‘The focal game lasts 20 rounds’.,1,1,The thief chooses between two discrete options each round (steal vs do nothing).,0.7,0,"Each round offers a choice to steal +2 from opponent (−2 to partner) vs do nothing; participant’s money is not pre-committed to a public fund, so default contribution to counterpart is 0.",0.6,N/R,Not a public goods game with linear multiplier.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat between participants.,0.95,0,Participants see only partner’s current-round decision; no summaries of cumulative earnings/punishment reported.,0.7,0,Interaction is anonymous MTurk matching; no identities revealed beyond role and actions.,0.8,0,There is no reward stage and no identities.,1,0,They explicitly state ‘Participants do not know the game lengths’.,1,1,Opponent in this condition rigidly punishes theft; victim role generally has a punishment option.,1,1,Victim punishment costs −1 cent to punisher.,1,3,Punishing inflicts −3 cents on partner per 1 cent cost to punisher.,1,0,No positive reward action; only punishment and stealing.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,They say ‘endowed with money’ but do not specify initial total endowment.,0.3,N/R,Main DV is rate of stealing (thief) or punishing (victim) over rounds; they do not report normalized contribution to a public good.,0.9,N/R,Steal decision is binary (0 or 1 action per round); they report rates over time but no raw per-round totals in the text.,0.7,N/R,They do not compute efficiency as total joint payoff relative to maximum.,1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff reported.,1,N/R,"Participants are from Amazon Mechanical Turk; nationality not specified, though likely many are from US; cannot assume.",0.3,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,N/R,No education information given.,0.1,Online,"They explicitly state participants were recruited on Amazon Mechanical Turk, an online platform.",1,"Within-subjects broader design: each participant plays one focal game against an opponent who always steals or always punishes (this condition: thief vs rigid punisher), plus two background games against other opponent types (one as thief, one as victim). Game order is counterbalanced and controlled for in analysis. In each round as thief, participant chooses between ‘steal’ (+2 cents to self, −2 to partner) and ‘do nothing’ (0 to both). After each round, they are informed of partner’s decision. Participants do not know the game length (focal game 20 rounds, background games 10–20 rounds).",Summarized from Behavioral Experiment description and Fig. 5 caption.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.1704032114.md,"Behavioral experiment – focal game, victim role against rigid thief",Some MTurk participants were assigned to the victim role in the focal game against an opponent who always steals.,0.95,"Participant role = victim; opponent strategy = always steal; payoffs fixed at s=2, c=1, p=3.",Condition is complementary to thief vs rigid punisher: victim responds to an opponent who always steals.,0.95,True,Same human experiment with different role/opponent type.,1,True,Random assignment to roles and opponent strategies.,1,False,"Conducted on Amazon Mechanical Turk, not in-person.",1,False,Real human participants.,1,False,Not purely theoretical.,1,2,Game between victim participant and one thief opponent.,1,20,Focal game length is 20 rounds.,1,1,Victim chooses each round between punishing and not punishing theft (binary action).,0.7,0,No initial allocation to a shared resource; default effect on partner’s payoff is 0 unless punishing.,0.6,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication described.,0.95,0,Participants are only told about partner’s current action; no history summaries are mentioned.,0.7,0,Anonymity via MTurk; no identities given.,0.8,0,No prosocial rewards included.,1,0,They explicitly say participants do not know game lengths.,1,1,Victims can choose ‘punish’ each round at a cost.,1,1,Punishment costs −1 cent to victim.,1,3,Punishment imposes −3 cents on thief per 1 cent cost to victim.,1,0,No rewarding behavior defined.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,Initial monetary endowment not quantified.,0.3,N/R,"DV is the rate of punishing over rounds, but not expressed as contribution to a public good.",0.9,N/R,Punish choice is binary; aggregate punishment counts not given numerically in text.,0.7,N/R,No efficiency measure reported.,1,N/R,No combined payoff statistic reported.,1,N/R,"MTurk source mentioned, but country not specified.",0.3,N/R,Age not reported.,0.1,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0.1,N/R,No information on education.,0.1,Online,Conducted with Amazon Mechanical Turk participants.,1,"Participants in this focal condition always face a thief opponent who steals every round. In each round, the participant (victim) is told whether partner stole (always yes here) before choosing between ‘punish’ (−1 cent to self, −3 to partner) and ‘do nothing’ (0 to both for punishment stage, but they have already lost 2 from the theft). Participants also complete two background games against other opponent types, one as thief and one as victim; order is counterbalanced.",Based on Behavioral Experiment description and role-specific details.,0.9
10.1007_s10539-013-9401-3.md,"N/A – Philosophical/evolutionary theory paper, no distinct empirical condition","The article is a theoretical/philosophical treatment of moral judgment and its evolution; it does not report a specific experiment, simulation, or observational study with defined conditions.",1,N/A,No experimental manipulation or condition structure is described; the paper synthesizes and cites many external studies but does not itself run an experiment.,1,False,No new data with human participants are collected; the paper is a conceptual and evolutionary hypothesis-building article.,1,False,"No controlled experiment (lab, field, or online) is conducted by the author; only references to others’ experiments are made.",1,False,No lab study is run in this paper.,1,False,"No agent-based, computational, or numerical simulations are reported.",1,True,The work develops a formal/structured philosophical and evolutionary hypothesis with conceptual analysis but without new empirical data.,1,0,No game or interactive experiment with players is implemented; only references to public goods games in other papers.,1,0,No repeated game or experimental rounds are conducted in this article.,1,0,No contribution decision is implemented; this field is not applicable but numerical 0 required by schema. Conceptually N/A.,1,N/A,No human-subject contribution game is run; default contribution proportion does not apply.,1,N/R,No specific public goods game is implemented in this paper; MPCR is therefore not reported.,1,N/A,"No experiment with human subjects is conducted, so chat/communication settings do not apply.",1,0,No experimental interface exists; this configuration is inapplicable but set to 0 per schema.,1,0,No punishment stage in a game is implemented in this article.,1,0,No reward stage in a game is implemented in this article.,1,0,"No rounds exist, so nothing about rounds can be shown.",1,0,"Although punishment is discussed conceptually and via cited studies, no punishment mechanism is implemented by this paper itself.",1,N/A,No game with a defined punishment technology is run in this paper.,1,N/A,No punishment technology (effect per unit) is implemented.,1,0,The paper does not implement any reward scheme; it only cites other work.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward technology is specified.,1,0,No experimental endowment is given to participants because there are no participants.,1,N/R,No empirical contributions are measured in this article.,1,N/R,No game or contributions are observed.,1,N/R,No payoff outcomes from a cooperation game are reported.,1,N/R,No group payoffs are empirically measured.,1,N/A,There are no participants in this theoretical paper.,1,N/A,No human subjects are recruited.,1,N/A,No participant sample is described.,1,N/A,"No participants are involved, so education level is not applicable.",1,No human,The work is purely theoretical/analytical with no human-subject data collection.,1,N/A,"No specific game (e.g., public goods, prisoner’s dilemma) is implemented by the author; all games are only discussed via citations.",1
10.1007_s13235-013-0094-7.md,Analytical model – optional public good game with institutional punishment,Single mathematical model of an optional participation public good game under institutional penalties; no separate empirical conditions.,1,"Per capita institutional penalty δ; group size n; multiplication factor r; contribution cost c; participation fee σ; strategy composition (frequencies of cooperators x, defectors y, non-participants z).","Replicator dynamics are analyzed as functions of δ, n, r, c, σ and the population state (x,y,z).",1,False,Purely theoretical evolutionary game-theoretic analysis with no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only mathematical analysis and simulations implied by differential equations.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; model is theoretical.,1,False,"Main results derived analytically via replicator dynamics, not via explicit numerical or agent-based simulations.",0.8,True,Replicator equations and stability analyses are solved symbolically; appendices provide proofs.,1,5,"General model uses group size n≥2; illustrative figures and parameter examples use n=5 (e.g., Fig. 1 caption: 'n=5'). Using this concrete value as representative.",0.7,1,"Single-shot public good interaction per grouping; evolutionary dynamics arise from repeated reproduction, not repeated rounds within a game.",0.9,1,"Each participant can 'decide whether to invest a fixed amount c>0'; choice is contribute c or 0, not continuous; but players can always choose between the two options each time, not forced all-or-nothing participation in public fund vs outside. This corresponds to discrete contribution decision with no restriction on timing; coded as 1 to indicate non-compulsory contribution.",0.4,N/A,Endowment allocation between private and public accounts is not modeled for individuals; only investment decision of amount c is considered in a theoretical setting without explicit initial endowment split.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/n. In examples r=3 and n=5 (Fig. 1 caption), so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.9,N/A,No human communication; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No human interface; payoffs are computed by equations only.,1,N/A,Institutional punishment applied impersonally; no notion of identity visibility and no human subjects.,1,N/A,No implemented reward in this condition and no human subjects.,1,N/A,No human interface displaying game length.,1,1,This condition explicitly models institutional penalties imposed on defectors; cooperators avoid penalties.,1,N/R,"Model specifies total institutional penalty m·δ distributed among defectors; it does not specify the cost to the institution per unit of penalty in separate units (e.g., cost per token).",0,N/R,Paper defines that defectors 'have their payoffs reduced by mδ/n_D'; the ratio of payoff reduction per cost unit is not separated as a distinct 'technology' parameter.,0.3,0,This row is for the punishment case; reward incentives are treated separately in the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not present in this condition.,1,N/A,Rewards are not present in this condition.,1,N/R,"Players can invest a fixed contribution c and pay fee σ, but a total monetary endowment per round is not specified.",0.1,N/R,"Paper analyzes equilibria and trajectories in terms of frequencies of cooperators, defectors, and non-participants, not average individual contribution rates normalized by endowment.",0.2,N/R,Contribution level is fixed at c for cooperators and 0 for defectors; no aggregate or average numeric outcome is reported as a result variable.,0.2,N/R,"Discussion refers qualitatively to group welfare and maximum payoffs, but no explicit efficiency ratio value is reported.",0.3,N/R,"Provides payoff functions symbolically, not realized numerical group payoffs as empirical outcome data.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is an analytical evolutionary game theory paper with no empirical data.,1,"Optional participation public good game with group size n≥2; participants pay entrance fee σ>0 if they join; at least 2 participants required for game to occur; cooperators invest fixed amount c>0, returns multiplied by r>1 and shared among all m−1 other participants; institution sets total penalty m·δ allocated equally per defector as mδ/n_D; infinite, well-mixed population; replicator dynamics over strategies C, D, and N.",Summary of the model specification given in Section 2 (Model) and payoff definitions.,1
10.1007_s13235-013-0094-7.md,Analytical model – optional public good game with institutional rewards,Same mathematical framework as punishment condition but with institutional rewards instead of penalties.,1,"Per capita institutional reward δ; group size n; multiplication factor r; contribution cost c; participation fee σ; strategy composition (frequencies of cooperators x, defectors y, non-participants z).",Replicator dynamics for the reward case are analyzed as functions of these parameters and population state.,1,False,The study is theoretical with no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment is conducted.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation; all results are mathematical.,1,False,Focus is on analytical derivations of replicator dynamics; numerical examples only illustrate parameter choices.,0.8,True,Closed-form expressions and stability analyses for equilibria under rewards are provided.,1,5,"General model uses group size n≥2; figures and examples use n=5 (e.g., Fig. 1 caption).",0.7,1,"Single public good interaction per grouping; evolution comes from reproduction, not repeated rounds.",0.9,1,Each participant can either invest the fixed amount c or not; discrete contribution decision; modeled as binary action each interaction.,0.6,N/A,No explicit initial allocation of an endowment between private and public accounts is modeled.,1,0.6,Using example parameters r=3 and n=5 in figure captions gives MPCR = r/n = 3/5.,0.9,N/A,No human communication or interface.,1,N/A,No user interface; payoffs are analytic.,1,N/A,"This is a reward-only condition, and no human identities are modeled.",1,N/A,Rewards are institutional and impersonal in the analytical model; no concept of identity display.,1,N/A,No human participants; no display of rounds.,1,0,This condition analyzes institutional rewards only; defectors are unaffected by the institution.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,1,Institutional rewards are explicitly modeled: cooperators receive a share mδ/(k+1) of the reward fund.,1,N/R,"Total reward m·δ is divided among cooperators, but cost per unit of reward to institution is not separately parameterized.",0.2,N/R,Reward magnitude per resource unit spent by the institution is not decomposed into a distinct technological parameter.,0.3,N/R,Model specifies contribution cost c and fee σ but not total individual endowment.,0.1,N/R,"Outcomes are characterized as equilibrium strategy frequencies and coexistence vs dominance, not as numeric contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,"Contribution per cooperator is fixed at c, but the paper does not report aggregate or mean amounts as distinct outcome metrics.",0.2,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (e.g., 'group welfare becomes the maximum (r−1)c−σ'), but no explicit normalized efficiency index is tabulated as a dependent variable.",0.3,N/R,"Average payoffs are given as functions, not realized numeric payoffs as outcome data.",0.3,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,No human,The reward case is also entirely analytical.,1,"Same optional participation public good game structure as in punishment condition: n players; optional participation with entrance fee σ; at least two participants required; cooperators invest fixed c and returns are multiplied by r and shared among all other participants; institution sets total reward m·δ and shares it among cooperators so that each cooperator receives mδ/(k+1) where k is number of other cooperators; infinite, well-mixed population; replicator dynamics over strategies C, D, N with modified payoff terms for rewards.",Description based on Section 2.2 payoffs for reward case and subsequent dynamical analysis.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.90.042726.md,Model 3 – Public goods game with punishment under birth–death (BP) updating,Model 3 is the optional public goods game with peer punishment; one condition uses the birth–death (BP) updating rule when computing the Markov chain stationary distribution and summary frequencies.,1,"Updating rule = Birth–death (BP); mutation rate μ = 0.003953; population size N = 40; payoff parameters r = 2.8, c = 0.8, β = 1.5, γ = 0.6; strategies = {C, D, P, L} with loner payoff λ (value not specified).",These parameters and the updating rule are explicitly stated for the stationary distribution in Fig. 6 and surrounding text; λ is mentioned but its numerical value is not given.,0.95,False,The study uses evolutionary game simulations and Markov chains with strategy frequencies and no human subjects.,1,False,"This is a theoretical/simulation model, not a controlled behavioral experiment.",1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational/theoretical.,1,True,They numerically iterate the transition probability matrix and discuss stochastic simulations for a population of N=40.,1,True,They derive fitness functions and analyze mean-field ODEs and basins of attraction analytically for Model 3.,1,40,They state the Markov chain is computed for N = 40.,1,N/R,They model an ongoing evolutionary process; no finite number of game rounds or generations is specified.,0.2,1,"In the public goods game, each cooperator or punisher pays a fixed cost c; contribution choice is binary (contribute or not), which matches the ‘all-or-nothing’ definition where players cannot choose partial amounts.",0.9,N/A,No human endowment or starting allocation is modeled; this is an abstract payoff-based evolutionary game.,1,N/R,They give the multiplication factor r but do not specify explicit group size to compute MPCR = r / groupSize.,0.2,N/A,No human communication; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No interface to participants; model directly uses payoffs and fitness.,1,N/A,Punishment is a payoff term; there are no identities or displays to human players.,1,N/A,"There is no reward mechanism in this model, only punishment (and loners).",1,N/A,No human interface; rounds are time steps in Markov chain/ODEs.,1,1,Peer punishers P impose a fine β on each defector at personal cost γ.,1,0.6,"Each punisher pays cost γ per defector; γ is given as 0.6 so marginal cost per unit of imposed fine is γ/β = 0.6/1.5 but the direct per-defector cost parameter is γ = 0.6. Schema asks for coins per unit; here, per unit of inflicted fine β=1.5, cost is 0.6.",0.7,1.5,"Each defector receives fine β per punisher; β is given as 1.5, representing magnitude of punishment impact per unit.",0.9,0,Only punishment and loner strategies are modeled; no rewarding strategy is implemented in Model 3.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/R,"The model uses abstract costs and benefits (c, r, β, γ) but not an explicit per-round token endowment.",0.2,N/R,"They report stationary frequencies of strategies C, D, P; for BP, average n_C = 0.0899, n_P = 0.0899, n_D = 0.748, but do not convert this to an overall normalized contribution rate including group formation details.",0.5,N/R,"They do not specify per-player or per-round contribution amounts in absolute units, only strategy frequencies.",0.3,N/R,No total or maximum possible group payoff is reported for the stationary distribution.,0.2,N/R,They do not report aggregate payoffs in the stationary distribution; only composition of strategies is given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of analytical derivations and computer-based Markov chain/simulation analysis without human subjects.,1,"Optional public goods game with four strategies: cooperators (C) contribute cost c to the public good; defectors (D) do not contribute; punishers (P) contribute cost c and additionally pay cost γ per defector to impose fine β per defector; loners (L) opt out and receive fixed payoff λ. Public good contributions by C and P are summed, multiplied by r, and divided equally among all participants in the group (C, D, P). Fitness is proportional to payoff but truncated at 0 via max(0, ·). Evolutionary updating follows the Moran birth–death rule with mutation μ on a well-mixed population of size N=40, and the long-run stationary distribution is obtained by power iteration on the transition matrix.",Summarized from the description of Model 3 and the stationary-distribution analysis under BP.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.90.042726.md,Model 3 – Public goods game with punishment under death–birth (DP) updating,"Same evolutionary public goods game with punishment (Model 3), but using the death–birth (DP) updating rule for the Markov chain and stationary distribution.",1,"Updating rule = Death–birth (DP); mutation rate μ = 0.003953; population size N = 40; payoff parameters r = 2.8, c = 0.8, β = 1.5, γ = 0.6; strategies = {C, D, P, L} with loner payoff λ (value not specified).",All parameters are stated for Fig. 6 and text; only the updating rule differs from the BP and LD conditions.,0.95,False,The condition is part of a theoretical evolutionary simulation with no human subjects.,1,False,This is not an empirical experiment but a modeled Markov process.,1,False,No physical lab environment is involved.,1,True,They compute the stationary distribution numerically for N=40 under the DP updating rule.,1,True,They analyze mean-field dynamics and basins of attraction for Model 3 and then compare with stochastic Markov chain results.,1,40,"For the stationary distribution comparison across BP, DP, and LD, they fix N = 40.",1,N/R,An infinite-horizon evolutionary process is modeled; no fixed round count is specified.,0.2,1,"Contribution is modeled as a fixed cost c for C and P vs 0 for D and L, i.e., a binary contribute/not-contribute decision.",0.9,N/A,No explicit human endowment; this is a stylized evolutionary payoff model.,1,N/R,"Although r is given, the group size used in each PGG interaction is not specified, so MPCR cannot be computed.",0.2,N/A,No communication mechanism; no human agents.,1,N/A,There is no user interface; payoffs are directly computed from strategy frequencies.,1,N/A,Identity display is irrelevant in this agent-based Markov model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists and there are no participants.,1,N/A,No rounds are displayed to any agents; process is simulated programmatically.,1,1,"Peer punishment strategy P imposes costs γ and fines β, same game as in BP.",1,0.6,Punishers pay cost γ per defector; the paper specifies γ = 0.6.,0.9,1.5,"Each defector incurs fine β per punisher, with β = 1.5.",0.9,0,Only punishment (no rewards) is implemented in this PGG variant.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Players are not modeled with a finite endowment; they simply incur costs and receive benefits as payoffs.,0.2,N/R,They do not summarize results as an overall contribution rate; only stationary frequencies of strategies are reported.,0.5,N/R,No absolute contribution amounts per player or per round are given.,0.3,N/R,They do not report realized vs maximum group payoffs under DP.,0.2,N/R,"Only composition (n_D, n_C, n_P) in the stationary distribution is given; no group payoff statistics.",0.2,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,Condition is a computational evolutionary simulation.,1,"Same optional public goods game with punishment as in the BP condition. DP updating means death is biased inversely by fitness while births are uniform; they compute the stationary distribution of the resulting Markov chain for N=40 and μ=0.003953. Reported long-run average strategy frequencies under DP are n_D = 0.428, n_P = 0.406, n_C = 0.166, implying a majority of cooperative strategies (C and P).",Summarized from the DP-specific stationary distribution description and comparison to BP/LD.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.90.042726.md,Model 3 – Public goods game with punishment under link dynamics (LD) updating,"Same Model 3 game, but using link dynamics (LD) updating for both mean-field basins (Fig. 5) and the Markov chain stationary distribution (Fig. 6).",1,"Updating rule = Link dynamics (LD); mutation rate μ = 0.003953; population size N = 40; payoff parameters r = 2.8, c = 0.8, β = 1.5, γ = 0.6; strategies = {C, D, P, L} with loner payoff λ (value not specified).","LD is explicitly compared with BP and DP in the stationary distribution for N=40, μ=0.003953, r=2.8, c=0.8, β=1.5, γ=0.6.",0.95,False,No human subjects; this is a simulated evolutionary game.,1,False,The condition is not an empirical or field experiment.,1,False,"The work is theoretical and computational, not lab-based.",1,True,They perform numerical Markov chain analysis and stochastic simulations under LD.,1,True,They derive ODEs and depict basins of attraction for LD (Fig. 5).,1,40,"For the stationary distribution comparison, they set N = 40 for BP, DP, and LD.",1,N/R,The model runs as an ongoing evolutionary Markov chain; no definite number of rounds is specified.,0.2,1,Contribution decision in the PGG remains binary (pay cost c or not) in this condition.,0.9,N/A,There is no explicit human endowment; agents only have payoffs and fitness.,1,N/R,"r is specified but the number of players in each public-good group is not, so MPCR cannot be calculated.",0.2,N/A,Simulation has no agent communication channel.,1,N/A,There is no GUI; states are internal to the simulation.,1,N/A,No identities or displays exist in this computational model.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled and there are no human players.,1,N/A,No rounds are shown to agents; time is just discrete update steps.,1,1,"Punishment strategy P is present with parameters γ and β, same as in BP/DP.",1,0.6,Per-defector punisher cost γ = 0.6.,0.9,1.5,Per-defector fine magnitude β = 1.5.,0.9,0,"Model 3 includes only punishment (and loners), not reward strategies.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment per agent is used in the theoretical model.,0.2,N/R,They present stationary strategy frequencies but not an explicit aggregation to contribution rate.,0.5,N/R,Absolute contribution amounts are not separately reported.,0.3,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible payoffs is computed.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff statistics are not provided; only composition of strategies.,0.2,N/A,The study does not involve people.,1,N/A,The study does not involve people.,1,N/A,The study does not involve people.,1,N/A,The study does not involve people.,1,No human,Condition is entirely computational/theoretical.,1,"Same PGG with punishment game as in the BP and DP conditions. Under link dynamics, each update selects an interaction (link) and reproduction is biased by fitness along links. The mean-field analysis (Fig. 5) shows that LD yields basins of attraction intermediate between BP and DP. For N=40, μ=0.003953, the stationary distribution gives average frequencies n_P = 0.314, n_C = 0.145, n_D = 0.541, meaning defectors are a majority but cooperative strategies (C and P) are more frequent than under BP and less than under DP.",Summarized from the LD-specific description of basins and stationary distribution values.,1
