10.1098_rsbl.2013.1069.md,Treatment 1 – non-punishing cooperator (PAO1150-2) invading cheats,Paper’s experimental design section defines treatment 1 as PAO1150-2 invading a population of O:9 cheats.,1,"Invader type and linkage between cooperation and punishment: non-punishing cooperator only (PAO1150-2), no toxin production (cooperation without punishment).",Three treatments differ by strain composition and thus by presence/absence of punishment and linkage; this row is PAO1150-2 alone.,1,True,Wet-lab experiment with bacterial populations.,1,True,Controlled treatments where strain composition is manipulated.,1,True,Cultures grown in tubes of KB broth in orbital incubator; standard laboratory setting.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations described.,1,False,No formal mathematical model; only statistical analysis of empirical data.,1,3,"Competition among three strategy types at metapopulation level: PAO1150-2 cooperator, O:9 cheats, and indirectly other subpopulations; primary focal game is cooperator vs cheats (2), but invasion is within metapopulation of cheats, so we record focal interacting strains (cooperator + cheats) as 2; however, they are explicit about only invader and cheats in each subpopulation, so better interpreted as 2. Still, the instruction wants playerCount as number of interacting types; I set 3? This is ambiguous, so using 2 would be safer. Given ambiguity I choose 2.",0.4,9,They perform initial inoculation plus eight further transfers: “This selection procedure was repeated for a further eight transfers (approx. 70 bacterial generations).” Total selection rounds = 9.,0.9,1,"Bacteria grow and interact continuously; no discrete choice of contribution level is defined, so not an all-or-nothing contribution game. Per instructions, 1 denotes players can contribute any amount.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects and no explicit endowment or contribution mechanism as in economic games.,1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is quantified.,1,N/A,Bacteria do not use experimenter-mediated communication channels.,1,0,No information feedback mechanism analogous to summaries of others’ actions is described for bacteria.,1,N/A,No identities and no informational revelation in bacterial context.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Bacteria are not informed about the number of selection rounds.,1,0,Invading strain PAO1150-2 is a pyocin S2 knock-out and does not produce the toxin (no punishment).,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,"No explicit positive reward mechanism, only public good (siderophores).",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No notion of per-individual resource endowment is defined as in economic games.,1,N/R,"They measure frequencies of bacterial strains and extinction/fixation times, not continuous contribution levels.",1,N/R,No explicit measure of siderophore amount per cell reported in this experiment.,1,N/R,No payoff or yield measure normalized by a theoretical maximum is reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff variable is defined; they track strain frequencies and extinction times.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Experiment conducted on bacterial cultures only.,1,"Metapopulation of six replicate metapopulations; each metapopulation contains nine subpopulations (tubes). Each tube starts with approx. 10,000 O:9 cheats; in one subpopulation per metapopulation, 1% (circa 100 cells) of PAO1150-2 is added. Cultures grown 96 h, then pooled within metapopulation, diluted, and redistributed to nine fresh tubes; repeated for eight further transfers (approx. 70 generations). Outcome measures: time to extinction or fixation of invader, survival analysis with Weibull errors and Fisher’s exact test.",Summarizes detailed procedural description in Materials and methods.,1
10.1098_rsbl.2013.1069.md,Treatment 2 – punishing cooperator (PAO1) invading cheats (tight linkage),Experimental design defines treatment 2 as PAO1 invading O:9 cheats; PAO1 produces both siderophores and pyocin S2.,1,Invader type and linkage between cooperation and punishment: punishing cooperator (PAO1) with tight linkage between siderophore and toxin production.,This treatment tests invasion of strong reciprocator (cooperation + punishment linked) into cheats.,1,True,Bacterial growth experiment in the lab.,1,True,Strain composition is manipulated under controlled lab conditions.,1,True,Cultures in glass tubes of KB broth incubated at 37°C.,1,False,No simulation model used.,1,False,No standalone analytical model.,1,2,"Within each subpopulation, interaction is between PAO1 (invader) and O:9 cheats.",0.8,9,Initial inoculation plus eight transfers.,0.9,1,No discrete binary decision on contribution; bacterial secretion is continuous/constitutive.,0.4,N/A,Not a human endowment game.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return specified.,1,N/A,No participant communication channel.,1,0,No information feedback as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No identities in bacterial setting.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Bacteria not informed.,1,1,"PAO1 produces pyocin S2, which kills sensitive cheats; interpreted as punishment.",1,N/R,Cost of toxin production is discussed qualitatively but no numeric cost per unit is given.,1,N/R,"No quantitative effect per unit toxin (e.g., kills per molecule) reported.",1,0,Only public good (siderophores) and toxin; no targeted rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No economic-style endowment defined.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are invasion success and extinction times, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,No quantitative siderophore amount per cell reported.,1,N/R,No efficiency-like normalized payoff measure reported.,1,N/R,No total payoff variable; only population sizes/frequencies.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Bacterial cultures only.,1,"Six replicate metapopulations per treatment, each with nine subpopulations (tubes). Each tube starts with approx. 10,000 O:9 cheats; in one subpopulation per metapopulation, 1% (~100 cells) PAO1 is added. After 96 h growth at 37°C with shaking, all nine tubes in a metapopulation are pooled, diluted, and redistributed to nine fresh tubes with approx. 10,000 cells total; process repeated for eight more transfers (~70 generations). They track PAO1 frequency over time, recording whether it invades (fixes) or goes extinct and the time to these events. Survival analysis with Weibull error and Fisher’s exact test used to compare treatments; PAO1 invades in three of six metapopulations and has longer extinction times than other treatments.",Condenses methodological description relevant to this treatment.,1
10.1098_rsbl.2013.1069.md,Treatment 3 – mixed cooperators (PAO1 + PAO1150-2) invading cheats (broken linkage),Experimental design defines treatment 3 as a 1:1 mixture of PAO1 and PAO1150-2 invading O:9 cheats to mimic broken linkage.,1,"Invader type and linkage between cooperation and punishment: 1:1 mixture of punishing cooperator (PAO1) and non-punishing cooperator (PAO1150-2), representing broken genetic linkage.",This treatment specifically breaks linkage by introducing both strains together at low frequency.,1,True,Laboratory experiment on bacteria.,1,True,Controlled treatment with specified strain mix.,1,True,Conducted in glass tubes with KB broth at 37°C in incubator.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,No separate analytical model.,1,3,"Within subpopulations in the focal invaded tube, three strain types interact: PAO1, PAO1150-2, and O:9 cheats.",0.9,9,Initial inoculation plus eight transfers.,0.9,1,No discrete all-or-nothing contribution choice defined.,0.4,N/A,No human-style endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR given.,1,N/A,No communication channel for participants.,1,0,No information feedback as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No subject identities.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Bacteria not told number of transfers.,1,1,PAO1 in the mixture produces pyocin S2 that can kill cheats; overall system includes punishment.,1,N/R,Costs of toxin production not quantified numerically.,1,N/R,No numeric toxicity parameter per unit toxin reported.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism separate from cooperative siderophore production.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No endowment concept used.,1,N/R,"Measured variables are strain frequencies and extinction/fixation times, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No measurements of siderophore quantity per strain in this experiment.,1,N/R,No explicit group payoff vs maximum payoff ratio reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff not defined; only demographic outcomes.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Work exclusively with bacterial cultures.,1,"Six replicate metapopulations, nine subpopulations each. Each tube starts with approx. 10,000 O:9 cheats; in one subpopulation per metapopulation, a 1:1 mixture of PAO1 and PAO1150-2 is added at a total invader frequency of 1% (0.5% each, ~50 cells of each). After 96 h growth at 37°C with shaking, tubes in a metapopulation are pooled, diluted, and redistributed to nine fresh tubes (~10,000 cells total) and this selection cycle repeats for eight additional transfers (~70 generations). They record frequencies of both invader strains and cheats over time and determine whether cooperators invade or go extinct, and extinction times; extinction for cooperators is faster than in PAO1-only treatment and similar to PAO1150-2-only treatment.",Summarizes experimental procedure details relevant to mixed-strain treatment.,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2012.08.002.md,Baseline – peer punishment without third-party approval,Paper describes a baseline treatment where A and B can punish each other and C is only an observer.,1,Punishment enforcement mechanism: pure peer punishment (no third-party approval needed),"Only difference between treatments is whether C must approve punishment; here, no approval is needed.",1,True,"Laboratory experiment with human subjects A, B, and C.",1,True,Controlled lab design with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the CentER lab at Tilburg University.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all decisions by human subjects.,1,False,"Study reports experimental results, not purely analytical modeling.",1,3,"Groups consist of players A, B, and C.",1,20,Subjects play the game for 20 periods.,1,1,"First-stage decision is binary (cooperate/defect) but punishment choice is any integer up to 40 tokens; contribution dimension is all-or-nothing PD but punishment is continuous. Given schema focuses on contribution, mark as all-or-nothing allowed decisions overall.",0.5,0,"Standard PD with no explicit endowment allocation to public account; cooperation is an action, not moving an endowment. Default proportion interpreted as all in private account.",0.5,N/R,"Prisoner’s dilemma payoffs given only as matrix tokens, no linear public good or MPCR defined.",0,0,No mention of communication; standard PD lab design without chat.,0.8,1,"At end of each period, all decisions are revealed to each player, implying information about others’ choices and punishments.",0.9,1,"A and B play in fixed pairs with direct punishment; end-of-period feedback reveals each other's punishment proposals, so identities are known within the pair.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Game is explicitly finitely repeated for 20 periods; such designs normally inform participants of total periods. Text: “for 20 periods” suggests known horizon.,0.8,1,Second stage allows A and B to deduct tokens from each other.,1,1,Every 3 tokens deducted from the other costs the punisher 1 token.,1,3,Each 1 token cost to the punisher yields 3 tokens deduction for the target.,1,0,Only token deductions are described; no positive transfers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoff matrix (Table 1) is referenced but actual token payoffs and starting endowment per period are not provided in the text.,0,0.7,Text reports average cooperation rate of 0.70 in baseline over 20 periods.,1,N/R,"Only cooperation rate, not raw number of cooperation tokens, is reported.",0,N/R,No ratio of actual to maximum possible earnings is reported or derivable without full payoff table.,0,25.3,"Average earnings reported as 25.30 tokens per person in baseline; group payoff equals sum of A and B (and possibly C), but only per-person average is given. Using per-person payoff for this field.",0.6,Netherlands,"Experiment conducted at CentER lab, Tilburg University, which is in the Netherlands; typical subject pool is local students.",0.9,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,N/R,"No information on whether subjects were students or general population, though lab context suggests students; not stated explicitly.",0,On site,"Lab sessions run at CentER lab, implying in-person on-site participation.",1,"Finitely repeated 2-player prisoner’s dilemma with peer punishment and passive third party C. Stage 1: A and B simultaneously choose cooperate (Option I) or defect (Option II) using payoff matrix (40 tokens = 1 euro). Stage 2: A and B simultaneously choose integer punishment amounts (0–40 tokens deducted from the other). Each 3 tokens deducted costs the punisher 1 token; deducted tokens are destroyed. C observes but has no decision. C’s payoff each period is an independently drawn token amount from {15, 20, 30, 40}, uniform, independent of A and B’s actions; C’s earnings are not revealed until the experiment ends and A, B never see them. 37 groups in this treatment.",Direct description from procedure and results sections.,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2012.08.002.md,TPA – peer punishment with third-party approval,Paper describes a TPA (third-party approval) treatment where C can veto punishment proposals.,1,Punishment enforcement mechanism: peer punishment requiring third-party (C) approval for implementation,Only difference versus baseline is that C must approve proposed punishment for it to be implemented.,1,True,"Laboratory experiment with human subjects A, B, and C.",1,True,Controlled lab design with a treatment manipulation.,1,True,Conducted at the CentER lab at Tilburg University.,1,False,All decisions made by human subjects.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,3,"Groups consist of players A, B, and C.",1,20,Same finitely repeated game for 20 periods.,1,1,Same PD and punishment structure as baseline: binary cooperate/defect plus flexible punishment choice up to 40.,0.5,0,Same PD structure as baseline; no explicit initial allocation to a public account.,0.5,N/R,No linear public good defined; only PD payoffs.,0,0,No communication described; design mirrors baseline except for approval mechanism.,0.8,1,End-of-period feedback reveals all decisions to each player; A and B see each other’s punishment proposals even if C vetoes.,0.9,1,A and B in fixed pair know each other and see each other's punishment proposals; thus identity of punisher is known.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same 20-period finitely repeated design; participants are informed of the finite horizon.,0.8,1,A and B can propose deductions from each other; punishment exists though subject to approval.,1,1,"Same punishment technology: each 3 tokens deducted costs the punisher 1 token, if approved. If vetoed, no cost.",1,3,Each 1 token cost yields 3 tokens deduction for the target when implemented.,1,0,Only punishment via token deduction.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoff matrix not printed in text; starting tokens per period not stated.,0,0.47,Average cooperation rate reported as 0.47 in TPA treatment.,1,N/R,No raw cooperation counts provided.,0,N/R,Efficiency ratio not reported and cannot be calculated without the full payoff table.,0,23.52,Average earnings reported as 23.52 tokens per person in TPA; use this per-person payoff for this field.,0.6,Netherlands,"Experiment conducted at CentER lab, Tilburg University (Netherlands).",0.9,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,N/R,No explicit statement about education level; likely students but not stated.,0,On site,Lab sessions at CentER lab imply in-person participation.,1,"Same finitely repeated 2-player prisoner’s dilemma with third-party C as in baseline. Stage 1: A and B choose cooperate (Option I) or defect (Option II) via payoff matrix (40 tokens = 1 euro). Stage 2: A and B propose punishment amounts (0–40 tokens deducted) against the other; if implemented, every 3 tokens deducted cost the punisher 1 token and are destroyed. In TPA, C simultaneously decides whether to veto punishment decisions proposed by A and/or B. Punishment is implemented only if C does not veto; if vetoed, proposer incurs no cost. A and B still observe each other’s proposals even if C bans them. C’s payoff each period is an independent uniform draw from {15, 20, 30, 40}, independent of A and B’s actions; C’s earnings are revealed only at the end, and A and B never observe them. 38 groups in this treatment.",Directly from treatment description.,1
