10.1103_PhysRevE.97.062136.md,"Agent-based simulation – 1D Public Goods Game without punishment (varying r, β)","Paper describes agent-based simulations for the 1D public goods game without punishment, varying synergy r and selection strength β, summarized in Fig. 4.",1,"Synergy factor r (varied, increments Δr=0.1); selection strength β (multiple values); strategies available: Cooperate (C) or Defect (D) only.",Text and Fig. 4: simulations use different r and three different β values; only C and D strategies are available.,1,False,"Agent-based simulations with evolving strategies, no human participants.",1,False,"This is a computational simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects.",1,False,No physical lab or human subjects are involved; purely in silico.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent-based simulations of 1024 players on a 1D chain.,1,False,"This entry refers to the numerical agent-based simulations, not the closed-form Hamiltonian analysis.",1,1024,Methods: “a population of 1024 players … in a circular configuration.”,1,2000000,Methods: “we updated strategies 2 million times … then calculated the order parameter.” Each update is effectively a round.,0.9,1,"PG game imitation setting: each agent either cooperates (contributes) or defects (contributes nothing), but no partial contribution levels are described.",0.9,N/R,Model uses payoff formulas directly and does not specify an explicit initial allocation of endowment between private/public accounts.,0.5,N/R,The model uses synergy factor r but does not define linear MPCR as multiplier/group size in the usual experimental-econ sense.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; communication between simulated agents is not described as chat.,1,N/R,Simulation description does not discuss what information (summaries) agents observe beyond payoffs of focal and competitor.,0.3,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/R,"For simulated agents, no notion of displayed number of rounds is described.",0.2,0,Section III model and Fig. 4 do not include punishment; only C and D strategies.,1,N/A,Punishment not available in this simulation.,1,N/A,Punishment not available in this simulation.,1,0,No reward mechanism or rewarding strategies are described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/R,Model defines payoffs via r and contributions (cost 1) but does not state a per-round endowment as in lab experiments.,0.4,N/R,"Reported outcome is order parameter M (fraction cooperators minus defectors), not normalized contribution rate.",0.4,N/R,"Individual contributions are binary cooperate/defect and only aggregate fractions of cooperators are shown, not average tokens.",0.4,N/R,No ratio of total payoff to maximum possible group payoff is reported.,0.3,N/R,"Simulations report fraction of cooperators/order parameter vs r and β, not group payoff aggregates.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based simulations.,1,"1D circular lattice of 1024 agents; groups of size 3 (focal plus left and right neighbors); two strategies (C, D); strategy updating via Glauber-like adoption rule p(x←y)=1/(1+exp(−β(w_x−w_y))); synergy factor r varied; order parameter M=(P_C−P_D)/(P_C+P_D); critical r approximately 3 for cooperation.",Directly summarized from Sections III and Methods and the description of Fig. 4.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.97.062136.md,"Agent-based simulation – 1D Public Goods Game with punishment (varying r, ε, γ, fixed β=5)","Section IV and Fig. 6 describe agent-based simulations of a 1D public goods game with punishment and four strategies (C, D, M, I), varying punishment fine ε and cost γ with r.",1,"Synergy factor r (varied, increments Δr=0.1); punishment fine ε (three values); punishment cost γ (multiple values, but critical point depends only on ε); selection strength β=5; strategies: C, D, Moralist (M), Immoralist (I).","Text around Fig. 6: r varied with step 0.1, three different ε, costs γ varied but found not to affect critical point; β fixed at 5; four strategies allowed.",1,False,"Simulation with artificial agents and strategies, no humans.",1,False,"Computational model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field subjects; simulation only.,1,True,Explicitly defined as extension of the agent-based model with additional strategies and punishment.,1,False,This entry refers to the numerical agent-based validation of the analytical Hamiltonian model.,1,1024,Same population size as non-punishment case: “we use 1024 players in a population that is arranged linearly (see Methods)”.,1,2000000,Methods: each replicate updated strategies 2 million times; punishment extension described as an extension of this model with no change in run length.,0.9,1,"Each strategy either contributes (C, M) or not (D, I); no partial contributions mentioned.",0.9,N/R,Model defines contributions implicitly via payoffs; no explicit initial token allocation described.,0.5,N/R,"Synergy factor r is reported, but no explicit MPCR definition is given in standard experimental-econ terms.",0.4,N/A,Simulated agents do not have chat/communication in human sense.,1,N/R,No description of agents observing aggregate summaries aside from payoff comparisons for adoption.,0.3,N/R,"Simulation specifies that punishment is applied and costs/fines are paid, but does not specify what information about who punished whom is visible to agents.",0.2,N/A,No reward mechanism is included; only punishment.,1,N/R,No notion of displaying total or remaining rounds to simulated agents.,0.2,1,"Section IV explicitly introduces punishment with cost γ and fine ε and strategies that punish (M, I).",1,N/R,"Punishment cost γ per punishing player per punished defector is defined symbolically, but no specific numeric value is provided for γ in the simulations shown.",0.4,N/R,"Punishment fine ε per punished defector is defined; the exact mapping from ""one unit"" of cost to fine in numeric terms is not expressed as a simple per-token technology constant.",0.3,0,Only punishment is modeled; rewards are discussed only in the literature review.,1,N/A,No reward system implemented.,1,N/A,No reward system implemented.,1,N/R,Game uses contribution cost 1 and payoff formulas; an explicit per-round endowment is not reported.,0.4,N/R,"Outcome metric reported is M including four strategies, not normalized contribution fraction across all tokens.",0.4,N/R,Only fractions of each strategy and derived order parameter M are reported.,0.4,N/R,No ratio of total realized payoffs to cooperative maximum is presented.,0.3,N/R,Figures and text show order parameter M vs r and ε; group payoff statistics are not tabulated.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,No human,Simulation-based evolutionary model with artificial agents.,1,"1D circular lattice of 1024 agents; groups of 3 (focal plus two neighbors); four strategies: Cooperator (C), Defector (D), Moralist (M: cooperates and punishes), Immoralist (I: defects and punishes). Payoffs: Π_C = (r/3)(N_C+N_M+1) − 1; Π_D = (r/3)(N_C+N_M) − ε((N_M+N_I)/2); Π_M = Π_C − γ((N_D+N_I)/2); Π_I = Π_D − γ((N_D+N_I)/2). Strategy adoption via same Glauber rule p(x←y)=1/(1+exp(−β(w_x−w_y))) with β=5. Order parameter M = ((P_C+P_M) − (P_D+P_I)) / (P_C+P_D+P_M+P_I). Simulations run for 2×10^6 updates per replicate, 100 replicates per parameter combination. Critical synergy r decreases as punishment fine ε increases and is empirically independent of γ, consistent with analytic Eq. (40).","Summarized from Section IV, Methods, and Fig. 6 description.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.97.062136.md,"Analytical model – 1D Public Goods Game without punishment (single-site Hamiltonian, varying r, β)","Section III presents an exact Hamiltonian solution for the 1D public goods game without punishment, using a single-site energy minimization to model Darwinian evolution.",1,Synergy factor r; temperature T (via β=1/T).,Order parameter ⟨J_z⟩_β = tanh[β( r/3 − 1)/2] depends explicitly on r and β; these are the free parameters in the analytical model.,1,False,No data from human or agent-based empirical runs; closed-form analytical derivation.,1,False,"Mathematical analysis, not an experiment.",1,False,No lab setting or subjects.,1,False,"This entry represents the Hamiltonian closed-form solution, not the later numerical simulations.",1,True,Derives partition function Z and order parameter in closed form using statistical mechanics of spin chains.,1,N/R,Analytical single-site Hamiltonian treats a focal player with neighbors in the thermodynamic sense; no finite N is fixed for this calculation.,0.4,N/R,Static equilibrium model; number of rounds not defined.,1,1,Analytical PG game considers two pure strategies: cooperate (contribute cost 1) or defect (contribute 0).,1,N/A,No explicit endowment framework; pure payoff matrix analysis without human players.,1,N/R,Rewards use synergy factor r in payoff formulas; MPCR is not separately defined.,0.4,N/A,No human or artificial communication variable; purely analytical.,1,N/A,Static Hamiltonian description; no information display decisions.,1,N/A,No punishment in this model.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit round structure.,1,0,Only C and D strategies are present; no punishment.,1,N/A,Punishment absent.,1,N/A,Punishment absent.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism is defined.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Cost of cooperation is normalized to 1 but no explicit endowment is introduced.,0.6,N/R,"Analytical results focus on ⟨J_z⟩, representing difference between fractions of cooperators and defectors; they do not separately provide contribution rate as a normalized fraction.",0.5,N/R,Only binary strategies are considered; no numeric aggregate amounts shown.,0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum) is derived.,0.3,N/R,"While payoffs per configuration are defined, the paper does not report closed-form group average payoff in a summarized DV form.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely mathematical derivation.,1,"Analytical Hamiltonian model for a 1D public goods game on a linear chain with groups of size 3 (k=2). Strategies: cooperate (C) and defect (D). Payoffs: Π_C = (r/3)(N_C+1) − 1; Π_D = (r/3) N_C, where N_C is number of cooperative neighbors. Hamiltonian constructed with three-spin interactions but evaluated via an effective single-site partition function with matrices U and V. Resulting partition function Z = (1 + e^{−β r/3})^2 (1 + e^{−β(r/3 − 1)}). Order parameter (magnetization per focal player) ⟨J_z⟩_β = tanh[β( r/3 − 1)/2], yielding a critical synergy r_c = 3 (for k=2), matching standard evolutionary game theory.",Directly summarizing equations (11)-(21) and following discussion.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.97.062136.md,"Analytical model – 1D Public Goods Game with punishment (single-site Hamiltonian, varying r, ε, γ, β)","Section IV develops a closed-form Hamiltonian model for the 1D public goods game with punishment, including four strategies and deriving an explicit expression for ⟨J_z⟩_β.",1,Synergy factor r; punishment fine ε; punishment cost γ; temperature T (via β=1/T).,"Partition function components Z_C, Z_D, Z_M, Z_I and order parameter ⟨J_z⟩_β depend on r, ε, γ, β; final expression for ⟨J_z⟩_β depends on r, ε, β and is independent of γ.",1,False,No observed data; purely analytical thermodynamic treatment.,1,False,"Closed-form derivations, not experimental.",1,False,No lab context.,1,False,"This entry refers to the Hamiltonian solution, not the agent-based simulations used later for validation.",1,True,Model solved via Hamiltonian formalism and partition function decomposition.,1,N/R,Analysis is effectively for a single focal site in an infinite or unspecified-size system.,0.4,N/R,Static equilibrium model; no dynamic rounds counted.,1,1,"Four pure strategies either contribute (C, M) or do not (D, I); partial contribution not allowed.",1,N/A,No explicit endowment model; payoffs are directly based on contribution decisions.,1,N/R,"Synergy factor r appears, but no explicit MPCR is provided.",0.4,N/A,No agents or communication protocols defined; purely analytical.,1,N/A,Not applicable to analytical Hamiltonian.,1,N/A,No information display is modeled; only payoffs and energies.,1,N/A,Reward not modeled.,1,N/A,No round structure.,1,1,"Four-strategy game includes punishing strategies M and I, with cost γ and fine ε.",1,N/R,Cost of punishment per punished defector encoded by parameter γ but not normalized into a numeric cost per token in this framework.,0.4,N/R,Punishment fine parameter ε enters payoff expressions but is not expressed as a simple per-token technology scalar compatible with this schema.,0.4,0,Only punishment mechanisms are introduced; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,"Contributions cost 1 unit, but an initial endowment is not specified.",0.6,N/R,"Main analytical DV is the order parameter ⟨J_z⟩_β, defined as fraction(C+M) minus fraction(D+I); normalized contribution rate is not explicitly extracted.",0.5,N/R,Binary contributions only; no reported aggregate amount measure.,0.5,N/R,No explicit derivation of efficiency as ratio of realized to maximal payoff.,0.3,N/R,Hamiltonian formulation gives energy/payoff per configuration but not summarized as a single group payoff DV.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Mathematical model only.,1,"1D PG game with punishment on a linear chain; group size 3 (k=2). Four strategies: Cooperator (C), Defector (D), Moralist (M: C+punish), Immoralist (I: D+punish). Payoffs: Π_C = (r/3)(N_C+N_M+1) − 1; Π_D = (r/3)(N_C+N_M) − ε((N_M+N_I)/2); Π_M = Π_C − γ((N_D+N_I)/2); Π_I = Π_D − γ((N_D+N_I)/2). Hamiltonian constructed in extended 4D Hilbert space with projectors P_ij on strategy states and energy matrices F^(C), F^(D), F^(M), F^(I). Partition function Z = Z_C + Z_D + Z_M + Z_I with Z_C = 4(1+e^{−β r/3})^2, Z_D = e^{−β(r/3−1)}(1+e^{−β r/3})^2(1+e^{−βε/2})^2, Z_M = 4(1+e^{−β(r/3+γ/2)})^2, Z_I = e^{−β(r/3−1)}(1+e^{−β(r/3+γ/2)})^2(1+e^{−βε/2})^2. Order parameter measuring cooperation: ⟨J_z⟩_β = [1 − cosh^2(β ε/4) e^{−β(r/3+ε/2−1)}] / [1 + cosh^2(β ε/4) e^{−β(r/3+ε/2−1)}], which depends only on r, ε, β but not γ; reduces to tanh[β(r/3−1)/2] as ε → 0.",Summarizes equations (24)-(40) and discussion around Fig. 5.,1
10.3390_buildings12010019.md,Numerical simulation – baseline parameter setting,"Single evolutionary game model numerically simulated with a fixed baseline parameter vector (R=10, γ=0.45, α=1.5, β=0.6, E=2, a=1.5) before varying individual factors.",0.9,"Baseline parameter combination: R=10, γ=0.45, α=1.5, β=0.6, E=2, a=1.5; initial positive cooperation proportions x=0.5, y=0.5.",Table 3 reports these as the initial parameter values satisfying the inequalities; text notes initial positive cooperation proportion set to 0.5 when other factors are studied.,0.95,False,Study uses evolutionary game theory with numerical simulations in MATLAB; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human or field data; only model-based simulations.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly states numerical simulations with MATLAB2018b based on evolutionary game model.,1,True,"Derives replicator dynamics, equilibrium points, and inequality conditions analytically before simulating.",1,2,Model has two construction enterprises as players in a bilateral cooperation game.,1,20,Section 4 states the number of simulations is set to 20; interpreted as 20 time steps/iterations per run.,0.7,1,"Enterprises choose between two discrete strategies (positive vs negative cooperation), not continuous contributions; mapped to allOrNothing=1 since no graded amount choice exists.",0.7,N/A,No public goods or contribution endowment structure; only binary cooperation strategies in a simulation with no humans.,1,N/R,No public-good multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely computational agents.,1,0,Model payoffs are defined directly; no interface showing earnings/punishments is described.,0.9,N/A,Punishment is encoded as parameters a and E; no identities or information revelation between human subjects.,1,N/A,"Indirect income a is modeled, but there is no identity-based reward revelation among humans."