10.1086_700767.md,"Ostrom et al. 1992 – Condition A: Communication after 10 rounds, sanction-vote after experience","Paper refers to an experiment where groups play 10 rounds, then have 10 minutes face-to-face communication and a majority vote on adopting a sanctioning mechanism.",0.95,"Timing of communication and mechanism choice; prior experience of subjects with different fee-to-fine designs; this condition: play 10 rounds without sanctions, then communicate and vote on adopting sanctions",Condition distinguished by when communication and vote occur (after 10 rounds) and by subjects’ heterogeneous prior sanction experience.,0.9,True,Described as experiments with human subjects in common pool resource games.,1,True,Controlled lab-style game with specified treatments and votes.,1,True,Ostrom et al. (1992) are standard lab CPR experiments; face-to-face meetings occur on site.,0.9,False,"Involves human groups, not computer agents.",1,False,No purely analytical derivation described here.,1,8,Explicit: “Groups of eight subjects played a common pool resource game for 20 rounds.”,1,20,Explicitly stated 20 rounds.,1,1,Common pool resource games typically allow a range of extractions; no all‑or‑nothing restriction noted.,0.7,0,"In CPR designs, participants decide how much to withdraw from a shared resource; analog of default share in public fund is 0.",0.6,N/R,CPR payoff function details not provided in this text excerpt.,0,1,After 10th round “subjects met face-to-face for 10 minutes and were allowed to decide … whether to institute a ‘sanctioning mechanism’”.,1,N/R,No description here of feedback format about others’ extractions or earnings.,0,N/R,Text only mentions a ‘sanctioning mechanism’ and fee‑to‑fine ratio; does not state whether identities are revealed.,0,0,Only punishment via fees and fines is discussed; no rewards mentioned.,0.8,N/R,Paper excerpt does not specify whether subjects were told total or remaining rounds.,0,1,Groups vote on whether to institute a ‘san