rs. Combined with the first pre-play experiment, net yields reached about 94–95% of the maximum including fees and fines.",Restates description while distinguishing as second experiment.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0254860.md,Wasteful punishment model – Well-mixed population,Distinct model variant (wasteful punishment) analyzed in a well-mixed population using simulations and replicator dynamics.,1,Enhancement factor of public good r; punishment enhancement factor ρ; second-order punishment weight α; punishment cost c′ (with c=c′=1 unless otherwise varied); mutation rate v; group size g=9; population size N=10000 (simulations). This condition refers to the baseline wasteful punishment model in a well-mixed population across these parameter sweeps.,"Paper varies r, ρ, α, and c′ systematically (Figs 1 and 2) with fixed g=9, v=0.001, c=c′=1 unless otherwise stated, and N=10000 for phase-diagram simulations in well-mixed populations.",1,False,No human or animal subjects; evolutionary game-theoretic model analyzed by simulations and replicator equations.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only computational/theoretical analyses.,1,False,No lab or online participants; purely simulated agents.,1,True,"They run simulations in finite populations (e.g., N=10000) and also solve replicator dynamics equations.",1,True,They derive and solve replicator-mutation equations (closed-form dynamics) for infinite well-mixed populations.,1,9,"Group size g=9 is explicitly stated in multiple places (e.g., Fig 1 caption: 'Here, g=9').",1,1,"Game is played once per generation within groups; evolutionary model iterates generations, not repeated rounds within the same group.",0.7,0,"Each individual either cooperates (invest cost c) or defects; binary choice implies not an all-or-nothing contribution level continuum in tokens but a discrete cooperate/defect decision. By given coding, 1 denotes continuous any-amount contributions, 0 denotes only discrete all-or-nothing; here contributions are binary.",1,N/A,"No human players with default allocations; agents choose strategy each generation, not a UI with default allocations.",1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is r/g but r varies; no single numeric MPCR value is fixed for this condition.,0.7,N/A,No communication among simulated agents.,1,0,No information-display interface; agents interact only via payoffs and reproduction; no summaries shown.,1,0,"No notion of observable identities; punishment is institutional via pools, not targeted by recognized IDs.",1,0,No explicit individual-level rewards in wasteful model; only fines; no IDs.,1,0,Simulated evolutionary process; agents have no interface with round counters.,1,1,Core of the model is prosocial and antisocial punishment via punishment pools.,1,1,"Cost to contribute to punishment pool is c′, set to 1 unless varied (e.g., Fig 2).",1,N/R,Fine per unit contribution depends on ρ and group composition; no single fixed tech parameter (it’s effectively ρ times fractions (1−α) or α).,0.6,0,"In wasteful punishment model, unused punishment pool resources are wasted, not used for rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,1,Each cooperator invests cost c=1 into public good; interpreted as per-round endowment normalized to 1.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes reported as phases (C, D, PC, PD), not as normalized average contributions.",0.6,N/R,No explicit average contribution amount across population reported; only dominance of strategies.,0.6,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is numerically reported.,0.6,N/R,Paper does not provide explicit numeric average group payoff values for this model.,0.6,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and computational study with no empirical data.,1,"Public goods game with group size g=9; cooperators pay cost c to invest in a public resource, multiplied by r and shared equally. After PGG, public punishing game: cooperators may invest c′ in prosocial punishment pool; defectors may invest c′ in antisocial pool. Investments in each pool are multiplied by punishment enhancement factor ρ. In prosocial pool, fraction 1−α of resources punishes defectors and fraction α punishes non-punishing cooperators. In antisocial pool, fraction 1−α punishes cooperators and fraction α punishes non-punishing defectors. In wasteful model, unused punishment resources are destroyed.",Summarizes the defined game and punishment structure as described in the model section for the wasteful punishment variant.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0254860.md,Wasteful punishment model – Structured population,Same wasteful punishment rules but agents placed on a spatial lattice (structured population) and simulated.,1,"Enhancement factor r; punishment enhancement factor ρ; second-order punishment weight α; punishment cost c′ with c=c′=1; mutation rate v=0.001; group size g=9; lattice structure (200×200 or 300×300 first-nearest-neighbor lattice with Moore connectivity, periodic boundaries); population size N=40000 or 90000. This condition refers to the wasteful punishment model under spatial structure.","Paper distinguishes well-mixed vs structured populations and provides specific parameters for structured case (Fig 3: N=40000, 200×200; Fig 4: N=90000, 300×300).",1,False,No real participants; purely simulated agents on a lattice.,1,False,"Computational experiment, not a human or field experiment as defined.",1,False,Not conducted with human subjects in a lab or online.,1,True,"They run agent-based simulations on spatial lattices (e.g., 200×200, 300×300).",1,False,For structured populations they do not provide analytical (closed-form) dynamics; analysis is via simulation only.,1,9,"They keep group size g=9 in structured population as well (Fig 3 caption: 'Here, g=9').",1,1,"Game is played in each generation on local neighborhoods; repeated across generations, not multiple rounds within a fixed group.",0.7,0,Same binary cooperate/defect decision as in well-mixed case.,1,N/A,No human UI or default allocations; simulated strategies only.,1,N/R,"MPCR=r/g; r varies in phase diagrams, so no single value.",0.7,N/A,No communication among lattice agents.,1,0,No display mechanism; only local interactions and payoff-based reproduction.,1,0,No identities; punishment is via institutional pools.,1,0,No explicit rewards in wasteful model.,1,0,No explicit rounds known to agents.,1,1,Same prosocial and antisocial punishment pools as in the well-mixed wasteful model.,1,1,c′ is set to 1 in structured simulations (Fig 3 and 4 captions: c=c′=1).,1,N/R,Fine magnitude depends on ρ and local group composition; no single scalar per-coin mapping reported.,0.6,0,Wasteful punishment: unused pool resources are not redistributed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Same cost c=1 for cooperation as in well-mixed case, interpreted as unit endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Results described as dominance of strategy phases on lattice, not mean contribution fractions.",0.6,N/R,No explicit average contribution amounts reported.,0.6,N/R,"They discuss phases and transitions, not normalized efficiency metrics.",0.6,N/R,No numeric group payoff values for structured runs are reported.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation on a lattice; no empirical environment.,1,"Same game as wasteful well-mixed model (PGG plus public punishing game), but population resides on a 2D lattice (e.g., 200×200 or 300×300) with Moore neighborhood (first-nearest neighbors) and periodic boundaries. Groups are local neighborhoods of size g=9. Strategy update and reproduction occur via local interactions, leading to spatial pattern formation (e.g., domains and rock-paper-scissors-like dynamics).",Summarizes structured-population implementation and interaction topology from Results and figure captions.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0254860.md,Non-wasteful punishment model – Well-mixed population,"Variant where unused punishment pool resources are redistributed to contributors, analyzed in a well-mixed population (Fig 5).",1,Enhancement factor r; punishment enhancement factor ρ; second-order punishment weight α; punishment cost c′ (with c=c′=1); mutation rate v=0.001; group size g=9; population size N=40000 (simulations). This condition refers to the non-wasteful punishment model in a well-mixed population across these parameter sweeps.,"Fig 5 captions specify g=9, v=0.001, c=c′=1 and N=40000; the text describes varying r, ρ, and α.",1,False,No human/animal subjects; only simulations and replicator dynamics.,1,False,The study is theoretical/computational rather than an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment or human participants.,1,True,They run simulations for finite N=40000 in a well-mixed population.,1,True,They derive and solve replicator dynamics for the non-wasteful model (Methods and S1 Text).,1,9,"Group size g=9 again specified for non-wasteful model phase diagrams (Fig 5: 'Here, g=9').",1,1,"As in wasteful model, game is single-shot per interaction per generation.",0.7,0,Cooperate vs defect is a binary decision; no continuous contribution levels.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents have fixed strategies.,1,N/R,"MPCR=r/g, but r varies; not a single constant number.",0.7,N/A,No communication between agents.,1,0,No feedback UI; only payoffs affect reproduction.,1,0,Institutional punishment without identities.,1,0,"Rewards in this model are institutional redistribution to contributors, not targeted or identity-based.",1,0,No explicit round information displayed to agents.,1,1,"Same prosocial and antisocial punishment pools as before, but with non-wasteful use of resources.",1,1,c′=1 is explicitly stated for these phase diagrams.,1,N/R,"Punishment intensity depends on ρ, α, and group composition; no simple per-coin technology parameter is isolated.",0.6,1,"In non-wasteful model, if there is nobody to punish, punishment pool resources are divided among contributors; effectively a reward mechanism.",1,1,"Contributors pay cost c′ into the pool, which can yield rewards; cost per potential rewarded unit is the same c′ contribution.",0.6,N/R,Per-contribution reward depends on how much unused pool remains and number of contributors; not summarized as a single scalar.,0.5,1,"Same c=1 for contribution to public good, interpreted as normalized endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Outcomes described as phases C, D, PC, PD; no explicit average contribution proportions provided.",0.6,N/R,No numeric contribution levels beyond binary strategy are reported.,0.6,N/R,They compare which strategies dominate but do not compute explicit efficiency measures.,0.6,N/R,No explicit average payoffs are tabulated for the non-wasteful model.,0.6,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and computational for well-mixed population.,1,"Same basic PGG plus public punishing game as in wasteful model, but with resource conservation: in the prosocial punishment pool, fraction α targets non-punishing cooperators; if none exist, this fraction is redistributed among prosocial punishers. Fraction 1−α targets defectors; if none exist, it is redistributed among prosocial punishers. Analogously, in the antisocial pool, fraction α targets non-punishing defectors (otherwise redistributed among antisocial punishers), and fraction 1−α targets cooperators (otherwise redistributed). This occasional redistribution acts as a reward and mitigates second-order free-riding.",Summarizes the non-wasteful institutional rule changes relative to the wasteful model as described in the 'Non-wasteful punishment' section.,1
10.1098_rsos.150223.md,"Baseline – all defectors punished equally by all cooperators (Monte Carlo simulations, N=200)","Main model in §2.1 and Methods: all cooperators punish all defectors, simulations with N=200 used for fig. 1.",0.93,Punishment strength π and rationality β; initial condition: all players start as defectors,Text around fig. 1b: varies β and π; specifically contrasts initial all-cooperate vs all-defect. This row is the all-defect condition.,0.95,False,Study is a mathematical/computational model; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; only simulations and analytical work.,1,False,No lab or field setting with human subjects.,1,True,"Repeatedly states ‘Monte Carlo simulations of the model’ (e.g., fig. 1 caption).",1,True,Fixed-point analysis in Methods and fig. 1c–e uses closed-form equations without stochastic sampling.,0.9,200,Explicitly: ‘N=200 players (roughly the number of countries in the world)’ for simulations.,1,0,Time steps run until a stationary state; no fixed known number of rounds is specified.,0.2,1,Model is binary: each player chooses to cooperate or defect each time step; no continuous contribution.,1,N/A,No explicit public-goods contributions or endowment structure; abstract binary cooperate/defect in a simulation without human subjects.,1,N/R,Public-goods game is only described conceptually; no numerical multiplier or MPCR specified.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,0,No interface or feedback structure is modeled; only global fraction of cooperators matters.,0.9,N/A,No individuals observing IDs in a computer model; punishment is modeled as payoff term only.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,0,Players are not modeled as seeing number of time steps; purely mathematical dynamics.,0.9,1,Key parameter π is the punishment capacity and p_i enters payoff balance.,1,N/R,"π represents punishment inflicted, not decomposed into separate cost to punishers.",0.3,N/R,Model specifies total punishment p_i=π*n_p/n_f but not per-unit ‘technology’ relative to cost.,0.4,0,Only punishment mechanisms are discussed; no positive rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No token or monetary endowments in the abstract simulation.,1,N/R,"Outcome is proportion of cooperators ρ, not a contribution fraction from an endowment.",1,N/R,No token contributions modeled; only binary cooperate/defect decisions.,1,N/R,No explicit payoffs or efficiency ratios are reported; focus is on fraction of cooperators.,1,N/R,Collective payoffs are discussed qualitatively but not quantified.,1,N/A,No human participants; players are abstract agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational simulations and analytical work.,1,Abstract N-player social dilemma with binary states (cooperate/defect). Player predispositions h_i are heterogeneous and ordered linearly from most to least cooperative (h_i=-(i-2)/(N-2)). All cooperators punish all defectors; punishable set n_f equals number of defectors; punishers n_p equals number of cooperators. Transition probability to cooperate is P_i=0.5[tanh(β H_i)+1] with H_i=π n_p/n_f - (i-2)/(N-2). Simulations explore steady-state fraction of cooperators ρ under varying π and β and different initial conditions.,Summarizes model description in §2.1 and Methods.,1
10.1098_rsos.150223.md,"Baseline – all defectors punished equally by all cooperators (Monte Carlo simulations, N=200, initial all cooperate)","Same mechanism as first condition but with initial all-cooperate state, corresponding to fig. 1a.",0.93,Punishment strength π and rationality β; initial condition: all players start as cooperators,Text with fig. 1a specifies all N=200 players initially cooperate; β and π varied.,0.95,False,Computational model only.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Described as Monte Carlo simulations.,1,True,Fixed-point analysis accompanies simulations for same mechanism.,0.9,200,Fig. 1 description: N=200.,1,0,Runs until stationary state; no fixed number provided.,0.2,1,Binary cooperate/defect choices only.,1,N/A,No explicit endowments or public-good pot in the simulation.,1,N/R,Multiplier not quantified.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,No informational feedback structure is modeled.,0.9,N/A,No observable identities in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,0,Agents do not observe horizon length.,0.9,1,Punishment term p_i is used.,1,N/R,Cost to punishers not parameterized separately.,0.3,N/R,Only total inflicted punishment is parameterized.,0.4,0,Only punishment discussed.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No tokens/endowment.,1,N/R,"Outcome is ρ, stationary proportion of cooperators; not a normalized contribution from an endowment.",1,N/R,No per-round token contributions.,1,N/R,No explicit payoff or efficiency computation reported.,1,N/R,Collective payoff not quantified.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computational simulations.,1,"Same abstract social dilemma as in the all-defect baseline, with heterogeneous predispositions h_i and punishment p_i from all cooperators to all defectors. Initial condition is full cooperation (ρ=1). Study examines maintenance of cooperation under varying π and β.",Summarizes the specific initial-condition difference relative to the first baseline.,1
10.1098_rsos.150223.md,"Single file targeted punishment strategy (Monte Carlo simulations, N=200, initial all defect)",Section 2.2 and fig. 3a describe simulations where the ‘single file strategy’ is applied.,0.96,Punishment strength π and rationality β; punishment rule: single file strategy; initial condition: all players start as defectors,"Fig. 3a: ‘as figure 1b (all players initially defect), but now the single file strategy is applied’; β and π varied.",0.96,False,No human participants; computational model.,1,False,"Simulation study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field environment.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations explicitly mentioned for these strategies.,1,False,No separate fixed-point analytical treatment for single file in main text; results presented via simulations.,0.7,200,States ‘N=200 players’ for simulations on punishment strategies.,1,0,Simulations run until global cooperation or stationary state; number of time steps τ is measured but not fixed ex ante.,0.3,1,Binary cooperation/defection at each time step.,1,N/A,No explicit contribution levels from an endowment.,1,N/R,Abstract social dilemma; no MPCR specified.,1,N/A,No human communication modeled.,1,0,Agents don’t receive payoffs or summaries; only states and rules matter.,0.9,N/A,No identities or information revelation are modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No notion of displayed horizon.,0.9,1,Targeted punishment via single file rule is key mechanism.,1,N/R,Cost to punishers not separated from impact π.,0.3,N/R,"Total punishment still p_i=π n_p/n_f, but no separate ‘per coin’ tech.",0.4,0,"Only punishment, not rewards, implemented.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No token endowments.,1,N/R,Outcome measures are ρ (fraction of cooperators) and speed v=N/τ; not contributions from an endowment.,1,N/R,No numeric token contributions.,1,N/R,No payoffs or efficiency ratios reported.,1,N/R,Collective payoff not quantified.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulations only.,1,"Same underlying N-player social dilemma as baseline, but punishment is ‘targeted’ using the single file strategy: players are ordered by predisposition h_i; at any time, a defecting player i is punishable (at fault) only if player i−1 cooperates; a cooperator would be at fault if she defected and her predecessor cooperates. This reduces n_f, increasing punishment per targeted defector. Total punishers n_p are the cooperators; punishable set n_f is the subset of defectors meeting the rule. Simulations explore steady-state cooperation ρ and speed v=N/τ across β and π starting from universal defection.",Summarizes the specific targeted punishment rule and main outcomes for this condition as described in §2.2.,1
10.1098_rsos.150223.md,"Groups targeted punishment strategy (v=10, θ=0.8) (Monte Carlo simulations, N=200, initial all defect)",Section 2.2 and fig. 3b describe simulations under the ‘groups strategy’ with v=10 and θ=80%.,0.97,Punishment strength π and rationality β; punishment rule: groups strategy with group size v=10 and threshold θ=0.8; initial condition: all players start as defectors,"Text: ‘groups strategy’ with v=10, θ=80%; fig. 3b is ‘as in figure 1b, but under the groups strategy’ with all players initially defecting; β and π varied.",0.97,False,Computational only.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field environment.,1,True,Described and plotted as Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,Groups strategy evaluated via simulations; no fixed-point math given in main text.,0.7,200,"With v=10 and ‘20 groups’, N=20*10=200 as stated.",1,0,Simulations run until cooperation or stationary state; τ is measured ex post.,0.3,1,Binary cooperate/defect state per player per time step.,1,N/A,No explicit token contributions.,1,N/R,No multiplier or MPCR reported.,1,N/A,No human communication modeled.,1,0,No summaries shown in this abstract model.,0.9,N/A,No identity visibility modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,0,No round-count feedback to agents.,0.9,1,Punishment remains the main enforcement mechanism with targeted groups.,1,N/R,Costs not separately parameterized.,0.3,N/R,Total punishment p_i = π n_p/n_f still applies but no explicit tech ratio.,0.4,0,No positive rewards introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No token endowments in this model.,1,N/R,"Key DVs are fraction of cooperators ρ, gap Δ_ρ to maximum cooperation, and speed v=N/τ.",1,N/R,No token contributions modeled.,1,N/R,No payoff-based efficiency is reported.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoffs.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulations.,1,"Underlying social dilemma with heterogeneous predispositions h_i as before. Players are partitioned into 20 groups of size v=10: group 1 has players i=1..10, group 2: 11..20, etc. A defector in group m is punishable (at fault) at time t iff at least θ=0.8 of players in group m−1 are cooperating at time t. Group 1 defectors are always punishable if they defect, to ‘set the process off’. Punishers are cooperators; punishable set n_f is the subset of defectors whose preceding group meets the cooperation threshold. Total punishment capacity from punishers is distributed over n_f as in p_i=π n_p/n_f. Simulations measure regions of parameter space (π, β) in which global cooperation emerges from universal initial defection and compare attainability, Δ_ρ, and speed v=N/τ against the single file strategy.",Summarizes the groups strategy and outcomes per §2.2 and fig. 3.,1
