10.1016_j.jesp.2015.04.005.md,Study 4 – Third-party/judge punishment of proposers,Study 4 introduces third-party judges who can punish proposers; this row captures judge condition in all relevant proposer scenarios.,1,Proposer offer type and coin framing (within-subject): (a) direct 8/2 accepted vs (b) allegedly coin-determined 8/2 accepted vs (c) direct 8/2 rejected vs (d) allegedly coin-determined 8/2 rejected; DV of interest compares (a) vs (b).,Judges decide punishment across four proposer-responder outcome scenarios; core comparison is whether coin blame affects punishment.,0.95,True,"Human judges, proposers, and responders in lab.",1,True,Experimental manipulation of proposer framing and outcome with randomized matching to a judge.,1,True,Same laboratory setting and software.,1,False,No simulation-only analysis.,1,False,Not an analytical model study.,1,3,"Each relevant interaction involves proposer, responder, and one judge.",0.95,1,Single decision per judge per scenario; then one judge randomly matched to each proposer-responder pair for payoff computation.,0.9,1,"Judges choose a continuous punishment level in cents, not binary. However field is defined around contribution choices; schema requires 1 or 0; set 1 with low confidence as contributions are not all-or-nothing.",0.2,N/A,Not a public good contribution scenario.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier present.,1,0,No communication between roles reported.,0.9,0,"Judges are given descriptions of specific outcomes, not statistical summaries of others’ behavior.",0.9,0,No indication that proposers learn which judge punished them or judge identity.,0.9,0,"Judges can punish or reward, but identities remain anonymous.",0.9,0,No multi-round structure detailed.,0.9,1,Judges can spend up to 100 cents to reduce proposers’ payoffs (3 cents reduction per 1 cent they spend).,1,1,Judge pays 1 cent from their €5 per 3 cents of punishment to proposers.,1,3,Each 1 cent spent by judge decreases proposers’ payoff by 3 cents.,1,1,Judges are allowed to punish or reward proposers and responders; positive transfers are possible.,0.9,1,"By symmetry with punishment budget, presume 1 cent of judge’s endowment is used per 1 cent of reward, though exact reward exchange rate not detailed; using 1 with low confidence.",0.3,1,"Reward seems to be a 1:1 transfer from judge to target; not explicitly quantified, so placeholder 1.",0.3,5,Judges receive €5 and can use up to 100 cents for punishing or rewarding.,1,N/R,"DV is punishment amount in cents, not contribution fraction.",1,N/R,No contributions to a public pot are measured.,1,N/R,"Study reports mean punishments, not efficiency relative to maximum payoff.",1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs for triads are not reported.,1,Germany,Described as German students at same Cologne lab.,1,Mean age 24.0 years (SD = 3.5),Reported in Study 4 method.,1,61% female,Reported in Study 4 method.,1,University students,Described as students as in other lab studies.,0.95,On site,Carried out in experimental economics laboratory.,1,"Judges see scenarios describing proposer offer type (direct vs allegedly coin-based 8/2) and responder reaction (accept/reject). For each, they can spend up to 100 cents to punish or reward proposers and responders. Punishment: 3 cents removed from a target per 1 cent spent by judge. One judge is randomly matched with each proposer-responder pair for payoff computation.",Summarizes Study 4 design around third-party punishment.,1
10.1016_j.jesp.2015.04.005.md,Study 5 – Online vignette (Mechanical Turk responders),Study 5 is an online hypothetical ultimatum responder scenario; single between-scenario within-subject manipulation of coin blame vs no-coin.,1,"Scenario framing within-subject: direct 8/2 offer vs 8/2 offer where proposer claims to have flipped a coin; Online hypothetical, no real stakes.",Participants assess their hypothetical acceptance/rejection in two framed scenarios.,1,True,Human participants respond to hypothetical scenarios.,1,True,"Experimental manipulation of scenario framing with randomized order; though hypothetical, it is a controlled experiment.",0.9,False,"Conducted online via MTurk, not in physical lab.",1,False,No simulation model used.,1,False,Not an analytical paper.,1,2,"Vignette describes a standard two-player ultimatum game (proposer, responder).",1,1,Participants answer once for each of two hypothetical one-shot scenarios; no repeated interaction.,1,1,Responder’s choice is binary accept vs reject of the 8/2 offer.,1,N/A,"Hypothetical ultimatum, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,No multiplier or public good present.,1,0,No communication; participants only read text and answer.,1,0,Participants do not see others’ choices or payoffs.,1,0,Purely hypothetical; no identities exposed.,1,0,No separate reward choice.,1,0,Scenarios are one-shot; no rounds indicator.,1,1,"Rejection of the 8/2 offer in the vignette is framed as punishment, even though hypothetical and costless.",0.9,0,Decisions are hypothetical with fixed $0.5 participation payment; no monetary cost to participants for rejecting.,1,N/R,Payoff changes for hypothetical proposer not numerically specified.,0.5,0,No explicit reward action available besides accepting offer.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Vignette uses Study 1 instructions but stake size is not re-stated in Study 5 excerpt; cannot be certain it was €10 or translated amount.,0.4,N/R,Only rejection rates of unfair offers are reported.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency calculations for hypothetical outcomes.,1,N/R,Aggregate hypothetical payoffs not reported.,1,Mostly United States and India,"84% from the U.S., 13% from India, 3% other, as reported.",1,Mean age 30.8 years (SD = 10.2),Reported in Study 5 method.,1,36% female,Reported in Study 5 method.,1,Mixed general population (Mechanical Turk workers),MTurk sample described as demographically varied; education levels not specified.,0.4,Online,Study conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,Participants read English instructions adapted from Study 1 ultimatum game and indicate whether they would accept or reject a direct 8/2 offer and an 8/2 offer allegedly determined by a coin flip. Order of scenarios is randomized; participants receive a fixed $0.5 payment.,Summarizes online vignette procedure.,1
10.1186_1744-9081-9-3.md,Baseline standard PGG block (before punishment instruction),"First block is always a standard public goods game without punishment, used as baseline before any punishment instructions.",1,"Punishment condition: standard PGG, no punishment rule mentioned or in effect; position: first block before punishment instruction.",Condition differs from later blocks by absence of any punishment rule and by being fixed as the initial block.,1,True,Human undergraduates played the public goods games.,1,True,Authors describe a controlled laboratory experiment with randomized block orders.,1,True,Experiment conducted in a computer room with individual booths at University of Nottingham.,1,False,No agent-based or purely computational model; humans made all decisions.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as standalone method.,1,4,Each trial was played in anonymous groups of four.,1,13,Text states punishment blocks had 13 trials; Figure 1 indicates same total across all four one-shot-game blocks; assume 13 here.,0.6,1,"Participants could divide an endowment of 20 MUs between private and public account, implying any integer amount from 0 to 20.",1,0,Standard PGG structure: all 20 MUs start in private account; nothing is pre-allocated to public fund.,1,0.5,"Payoff formula: π_i = 20 - g_i + 0.5 * Σ g_j; with group size 4, marginal per capita return from public account is 0.5.",1,0,No mention of any communication; typical anonymous z-Tree PGG with no chat.,0.9,1,Participants received feedback about the group’s average contribution and their own earnings after each trial.,1,0,"No punishment mechanism in this block; also groups were anonymous, so identities were not revealed.",1,0,No reward mechanism described anywhere in the paper.,1,0,Participants were not explicitly informed how many blocks they would play; number of trials per block not said to be displayed.,0.5,0,Described as a standard PGG with no punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,