10.1186_1744-9081-9-3.md,Standard PGG block after punishment instruction,Fourth block: a standard PGG played after punishment instructions were introduced; used to assess spillover of punishment expectations.,1,"Punishment condition: standard PGG, no punishment rule active in that block, but participants had previously been informed that some blocks might include punishment.",Block is identical in game structure to standard PGG but occurs after punishment framing.,1,True,Human participants played this block as part of the lab study.,1,True,Within-subject manipulation of block type in controlled setting.,1,True,Same on-site computer lab.,1,False,No computer-only agents.,1,False,No separate analytical modelling.,1,4,Groups of four throughout all blocks.,1,13,Figure 1 suggests equal number of trials per block; punishment blocks explicitly have 13; assume same here.,0.6,1,Participants divide 20 MUs between private and public accounts.,1,0,Endowment starts fully in private account.,1,0.5,Same payoff function across all blocks.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,1,Participants saw average group contribution and own earnings each trial as in other blocks.,1,0,No punishment rule operated in this block.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Number of trials not said to be displayed.,0.5,0,Participants were informed before each block whether punishment rule applied; for this standard block it did not.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this block.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this block.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same as all other blocks.,1,N/R,Only relative comparisons vs other blocks reported; no normalized rate.,0,N/R,Graphical means in Figure 2 but no numeric values.,0.3,N/R,No per-block efficiency statistics.,0,N/R,No per-condition group payoff reported.,0,UK,Same University of Nottingham undergraduate sample.,0.9,"Mean 20.96, range 18–36",Single sample across conditions.,1,67.1% female,Single sample across conditions.,1,Undergraduate students,Same participant pool.,1,On site,Same lab environment.,1,"Standard one-shot PGG trials identical to the first baseline block; however, by this time participants had experienced instructions about possible punishment in some other blocks, so their expectations about punishment risk could spill over. Zero-contribution behaviour in this block is used to analyse relationship with BIS-anxiety.",Summarizes role of this block in design and analyses.,1
10.1140_epjb_s10051-021-00185-w.md,Replicator dynamics – N-player trust game with individual reward and punishment,"Single theoretical/simulation model analyzed: N-player trust game in an infinite well-mixed population with parameters N, p, α, r, RT, tv varied in analysis and figures.",1,"Incentive strength p ∈ [0,1] and reward coefficient α ∈ [0,1]; other fixed parameters in examples: N=20, r=1/3 or 1/5, RT=6, tv=1.",Section 2.2 defines p as incentive strength and α as reward coefficient; Sections 3–4 and Figs. 1–4 explicitly study dynamics as functions of p and α.,1,False,No human subjects; paper studies evolutionary dynamics via replicator equations in an infinite well-mixed population.,1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; purely theoretical and numerical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; model-based analysis only.,1,True,Uses numerical calculations of replicator dynamics and phase portraits (Figs. 1–4) without human participants.,1,True,"Derives closed-form payoffs, replicator equations, equilibrium conditions, and theorems analytically.",1,20,Model defines generic N>2; numerical examples and figures use N=20 (stated in captions of Figs. 1–4).,0.9,1,Trust game is played once per group formation within continuous-time replicator dynamics; no repeated-round decision for individuals.,0.7,1,"Investors pay a fixed amount tv; trustees choose among discrete strategies I, T, U rather than a continuous contribution level, so ‘any amount’ vs all-or-nothing contribution is not applicable; coded as 1 because contribution is not a binary fraction choice but a fixed transfer.",0.4,N/A,No human players or endowment allocation decision; investors automatically pay tv.,1,N/R,"Paper defines trusted value tv and multipliers RT, RU but not a standard public-goods MPCR.",1,N/A,No human interaction or communication; purely theoretical population model.,1,0,No information feedback structure is modeled beyond payoffs; nothing about showing summaries to agents.,0.9,N/A,Model has impersonally allocated reward and punishment; no identities or observation structure are modeled.,1,N/A,Same as punishment; no identity visibility modeled.,1,0,"No notion of discrete, known finite rounds in replicator dynamics.",1,1,Section 2.2 specifies that part of the incentive budget is used to punish untrustworthy trustees (U) when (1−α)p>0.,1,N/R,Model defines fines received by U but not an explicit cost-to-investor ratio per punishment unit in the sense of standard PGG punishment; investors’ incentive budget comes from returned funds but per-unit cost is not parameterized separately.,0.6,N/R,Fines depend on group composition through (1−α) p RT NI NT tv / ((N−NI)(NU+1)); no single constant ‘technology’ per punishment coin is defined.,0.6,1,Section 2.2: each T player receives a reward α p RT NI tv / kTU from investors’ incentive budget.,1,N/R,Reward is financed out of investors’ returned fund but there is no explicit per-unit cost parameterization like “1 cost gives β reward”.,0.6,N/R,Reward magnitude α p RT NI tv / kTU depends on group composition; no single fixed linear factor per invested unit is given.,0.6,N/R,Model defines trusted value tv paid by each investor but does not frame it as an initial endowment in standard experimental-economics terms.,0.7,N/R,"No empirical data or simulated averages of contribution fractions are summarized; paper focuses on frequencies of strategies (I,T,U) and attraction domains.",1,N/R,"No outcome statistics of contributions are reported, only theoretical expressions and qualitative dynamics.",1,N/R,"Although payoffs are analytically defined, the paper does not report normalized efficiency outcomes for parameter settings.",1,N/R,"Group payoff expressions can be derived, but no numeric or summarized group payoffs are presented as results.",1,N/A,No human participants; infinite well-mixed population in a theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographic variables do not apply.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographic variables do not apply.,1,N/A,No human participants; demographic variables do not apply.,1,No human,Study is entirely based on mathematical analysis and numerical calculations with no human subjects.,1,"N-player trust game in an infinite well-mixed population with three pure strategies: investor (I), trustworthy trustee (T), and untrustworthy trustee (U). Each investor pays tv>0 to trustees; total investor fund kI*tv is equally split among trustees (kTU=kT+kU). Trustworthy trustees return RT*kI*tv/kTU to investors and keep the same amount; untrustworthy trustees return nothing and keep RU*kI*tv/kTU with 1<RT<RU<2*RT. Investors allocate a fraction p of their returned fund as an incentive budget, split by fraction α to rewards for T and 1−α to fines for U. Evolutionary dynamics are modeled via replicator equations for frequencies x,y,z of I,T,U in an infinite population.","Summarizes model description from Sections 2.1–2.3 capturing game structure relevant to cooperation, trust, and incentives.",1
