10.1287_mnsc.1100.1271.md,N/A – purely theoretical/game-theoretic analysis with no distinct experimental conditions,"The paper develops general theorems and illustrative payoff-parameter examples (e.g., Prisoner’s Dilemma with parameters α, β; franchising game with α), but these are not implemented as distinct experiments or empirical conditions; they are analytical characterizations.",1,N/A,"No empirical or simulated treatments are run; the paper varies theoretical parameters (discount factor δ, forgetfulness probability λ, payoff parameters α and β), but not as realizations of experimental conditions.",1,False,"The work is entirely theoretical, using repeated-game models and analytical proofs; no human subjects are involved.",1,False,There is no controlled experiment; all results are derived analytically from specified payoff functions and equilibrium concepts.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation is described.,1,False,"No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; the examples (e.g., specific α, β values) are closed-form or algebraic derivations.",1,True,The paper proves theorems (Theorems 1–4) and provides closed-form conditions and payoff expressions; this fits analytical modeling.,1,2,"The model is explicitly a two-player repeated game (e.g., two firms, franchisor–franchisee, two players in Prisoner’s Dilemma).",1,-1,"The game is an infinitely repeated simultaneous-move game; finite round count is not applicable, so encoded as -1.",1,1,"In the Prisoner’s Dilemma example players choose between discrete actions, but in the franchising example efforts w and r are continuous; overall the framework allows general action sets, not an all-or-nothing public-good structure. Treat as non-all-or-nothing (1 by the schema definition, meaning ‘can contribute any amount’).",0.6,N/A,This is not a public goods or contribution game; there is no endowment allocation between private/public accounts.,1,N/A,"No public-good marginal per capita return is defined; the games are generic stage games (Prisoner’s Dilemma, franchising) with arbitrary payoffs.",1,N/A,No human experiment; communication between human subjects is not modeled.,1,N/A,Not an implemented experiment; no user interface exists to display earnings or punishment summaries.,1,N/A,Punishment is part of the strategy space in analytical models; there are no identities or displays as in experiments.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism with identity revelation is modeled.,1,N/A,The horizon is infinite and there is no interface; this control makes sense only for human-subject experiments.,1,1,Punishments are central: strategies like Nash reversion and modified Nash reversion specify punishment phases when deviations occur.,1,N/R,"Punishment is endogenous via action payoffs (e.g., play of \tilde{a}), not a separate costly punishment technology with explicit per-unit cost.",1,N/R,There is no separate punishment technology mapping punishment tokens to losses; punishments are realized directly through stage-game payoffs.,1,0,The paper discusses cooperation and punishment; there is no separate rewarding mechanism analogous to costly reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,"Games are defined directly in terms of payoffs, not via endowments of tokens to be allocated.",1,N/A,"The outcomes of interest are equilibrium payoffs and conditions for sustaining cooperation, not contribution rates in a contribution game.",1,N/A,No contribution or token-allocation structure is present.,1,N/R,"Although the paper compares cooperative and noncooperative payoffs conceptually, it does not compute or report an explicit efficiency ratio metric.",0.7,N/R,"Theoretical payoffs (e.g., π^C, π^D) are defined per player; total group payoff values are not summarized as a central reported DV.",0.7,N/A,No participants; the paper is theoretical.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,All results are derived from analytical models with forgetfulness in repeated games; no experimental or field environment is used.,1,"Analytical study of infinitely repeated two-player games with probabilistic forgetfulness each period (probability λ each player forgets, independently), focusing on conditions under which cooperation can be sustained via punishment strategies like Nash reversion and modified Nash reversion. Stage games include a generic Prisoner’s Dilemma with parameters α, β (Figure 1) and a symmetric franchising effort game (Bhattacharyya & Lafontaine 1995) with payoff functions π^e(w,r) = ln(α w r + 1) − w and π^r(r,w) = ln(α w r + 1) − r. The main parameters are discount factor δ and forgetfulness probability λ, and the core results characterize regions in (δ, λ) where full cooperation is an equilibrium.","Summarizes the game structures and key parameters described throughout Sections 3, 3.1, and 3.2.",1
