10.1017_ehs.2021.36.md,Theoretical models and conceptual games (no human subjects),"The paper is a theoretical and conceptual review; all described games (ultimatum, trust, insurance, PD, public goods, punishment) are used as illustrative models without any original empirical or simulation implementation.",1,N/A,No experimental manipulation or distinct treatment conditions are actually implemented; only conceptual discussion of various game structures.,1,False,The article contains no original data collection with human participants; it reviews existing empirical and theoretical work and develops conceptual arguments.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run by the authors; all games are discussed theoretically.,1,False,No laboratory or other empirical implementation is described by the authors.,1,False,"No agent-based, numerical, or computational simulations are reported; the paper refers to models analytically or conceptually.",1,True,"The paper develops and discusses formal game-theoretic models (e.g., ultimatum game, trust game, insurance game, PD, public goods, punishment) analytically without numerical simulation or data.",1,2,"Most core models (ultimatum game, trust game, insurance/friendship game, prisoners' dilemma) are explicitly two-player games. Public goods/punishment games with larger groups are discussed only generically; the simplest consistent value is 2.",0.6,1,Games are explicitly described as one-shot or sequential but not repeated (except where repeated games are discussed conceptually as a contrast).,0.9,1,"Players in the described games (e.g., proposer choosing among several splits, trustee choosing various return amounts, helper choosing whether to share) can select among multiple allocations, not just all-or-nothing contributions.",0.8,N/A,No endowment allocation interface for human subjects is implemented; these are purely theoretical payoff structures.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is only meaningful for implemented public goods games; he