10.1016_j.jtbi.2012.09.012.md,Baseline evolutionary model without mutation (binary public-good game with punishment and retaliation),Paper presents a theoretical evolutionary game-theoretic model (Section 2) without mutation as the core setting; this is the baseline condition before mutation and before the later extension with mutation.,1,"Strategy composition in the population (fractions of four types: punishing cooperator PT1, conditional cooperator T1, reluctant cooperator DT1, retaliating defector R); key parameters b (benefit), c (cost of cooperation), k (cost of punishing/retaliating), p (damage from punishment), continuation probability π; group size N=3; existence of two punishment stages (punishment and retaliation). No mutation (μ=0).","Model explicitly specifies these strategies, parameters, N=3 and two-stage punishment with retaliation in Section 2; mutation is introduced only later in Section 2.5.",1,False,Model is purely theoretical/evolutionary with no human participants; all results are analytical or via replicator dynamics and simulations.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human or animal subjects; only theoretical analysis and numerical illustrations.,1,False,No laboratory setup; purely mathematical and computational.,1,True,"Model uses discrete-time replicator dynamics and numerical trajectories (e.g., Fig. 1) to illustrate evolutionary outcomes with no human subjects.",1,True,"Paper provides closed-form payoff expressions, equilibrium conditions, and propositions with formal proofs.",1,3,Section 2.2: 'restrict the attention to interaction groups of N=3 individuals'.,1,-1,Game is 'indefinitely repeated' with continuation probability π; fixed number of rounds is not defined. Use -1 to represent indefinite repetition.,0.9,0,Stage game is a 'binary public-good game' where agents either cooperate (contribute) or defect; cannot contribute any intermediate amount.,1,N/A,No human subjects; endowments and default allocations are not defined as in an experiment.,1,0.6666666667,"For numerical illustrations, parameters are given: c=1, b=4, N=3. Each cooperator pays cost c and provides b/(N-1) to each of the other N−1 players, i.e., total group benefit per cooperator is b=4 for a cost of 1, giving an MPCR of 4/6 for a linear PGG with 3 players; the paper explicitly notes 'MPCR=2/3' under these parameters in the Fig. 1 caption.",1,N/A,No human subjects or explicit communication protocol; only strategy rules within the model.,1,N/A,Not an implemented experiment with user interface; model payoffs are determined mathematically.,1,N/A,No identities in a human-participant sense; agents are types in a population model.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,Indefinite repetition handled via continuation probability; no display to players.,1,1,Stage 2 is a punishment stage where players may punish each other.,1,0.5,"Generic model parameter is k. For concrete numerical examples (e.g., Fig. 1 first column and many later illustrations), they set k=1/2.",0.9,1.5,"Generic model parameter is p (damage to punished). In most numerical illustrations (Fig. 1 first and second columns, Fig. 3, etc.), they use p=3/2.",0.9,0,Model includes only costly punishment and retaliation; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/R,"Theoretical model describes costs and benefits (c, b) but does not specify a finite per-period endowment in tokens.",0.6,N/R,"Model tracks evolutionary frequencies of strategy types, not explicit normalized contribution rates per se; contributions depend on strategy and history but no single reported average rate.",0.4,N/R,"Each cooperator takes a binary action incurring cost c, but no aggregate or per-period contribution amount is summarized as a DV.",0.4,N/R,Paper discusses payoffs and equilibrium states but does not explicitly normalize group payoff by the full-cooperation benchmark in a single figure or statistic.,0.3,N/R,Stage-game and long-run payoffs are given as functions but no single numeric group payoff outcome is reported as a DV for the baseline condition.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; evolutionary agents in a theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and computational evolutionary model.,1,"Indefinitely repeated 3-player binary public-good game with three stages per period: (0) contribution (cooperate/defect), (1) punishment of others with cost k and damage p, (2) retaliation by punished players using same k:p technology. Strategies considered: punishing cooperator PT1 (always contribute and punish non-contributors, but switch to T1 after being retaliated against), conditional cooperator T1 (cooperate if at least 1 other cooperated in previous stage, never punish), reluctant cooperator DT1 (initially defect, then switch to T1 after punishment), retaliating defector R (never cooperate, always retaliate when punished). Dynamics follow discrete-time replicator equation with baseline fitness u0 and payoffs from the game.","Summarized from Section 2 (2.1, 2.2, 2.3) where game structure, strategies, and dynamics are fully described.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2012.09.012.md,"Evolutionary model with mutation (intermediate continuation probability, π = 3/5)","Section 2.5 and Fig. 3 first row analyze the same strategy set and game but with mutation at various rates, specifically for π=3/5.",1,"Same game and strategy set as baseline (PT1, T1, DT1, R) with group size N=3, b=4, c=1, k=1/2, p=3/2, continuation probability π=3/5, but now with symmetric mutation among strategies at rates μ = 0.02, 0.01, 0.001.","Fig. 3 caption: 'π=3/5 (first row); in all cases, c=1, b=4, k=1/2, and p=3/2'; text in Section 2.5 discusses mutation at μ=0.02, 0.01, 0.001.",1,False,Still a theoretical evolutionary model with simulation; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; parameter sweep in simulations.,1,False,No physical or online lab; simulation only.,1,True,Mutation is implemented in replicator dynamics and trajectories visualized in Fig. 3.,1,True,"Section 2.5 provides analytical reasoning about selection with mutation, though specific equilibria under mutation are largely explored numerically.",0.8,3,"Same model, still N=3.",1,-1,Interactions are indefinitely repeated with continuation probability π=3/5.,0.9,0,Binary public-good game structure unchanged.,1,N/A,No human agents or endowment interface.,1,0.6666666667,"Same b, c, N as in baseline (b=4, c=1, N=3); caption of Fig. 3 repeats 'MPCR=2/3' by referencing earlier parameterization.",1,N/A,No communication in a simulation among agents beyond strategy rules.,1,N/A,No UI or feedback summaries in simulations.,1,N/A,Agents are anonymous types; no ID revelation modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,Indefinite repetition modeled probabilistically; no explicit round display.,1,1,Punishment and retaliation stages still present.,1,0.5,Fig. 3 caption: k=1/2.,1,1.5,Fig. 3 caption: p=3/2.,1,0,No reward mechanism introduced with mutation.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,Endowment not defined in model specification.,0.6,N/R,The paper describes qualitative behavior ('short periods of high cooperation' vs 'almost omnilateral defection') under mutation but does not report numeric normalized contribution rates for π=3/5 case.,0.4,N/R,Contributions are binary actions; no aggregated amount reported.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio is reported for these mutation scenarios.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative descriptions of payoffs and cycles; no numeric group payoff summarized.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a numerical simulation of evolutionary dynamics with mutation.,1,"Same 3-player, indefinitely repeated binary public-good game with punishment and retaliation. Continuation probability π=3/5. Strategies PT1, T1, DT1, and R evolve according to replicator dynamics with symmetric mutation: with probability μ per period (μ=0.02, 0.01, 0.001 in different runs), offspring switch to a different strategy. Under moderate mutation and intermediate π, the system cycles through phases: punishment induces short periods of high cooperation followed by longer phases of defection supported by retaliation.",Summarized from Section 2.4–2.5 and Fig. 3 first row.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2012.09.012.md,"Evolutionary model with mutation (high continuation probability, π = 4/5)",Section 2.5 and Fig. 3 second row describe mutation dynamics for a higher continuation probability π=4/5.,1,"Same game and strategy set (PT1, T1, DT1, R) with N=3, b=4, c=1, k=1/2, p=3/2, continuation probability π=4/5, and symmetric mutation between strategies at μ = 0.02, 0.01, 0.001.","Fig. 3 caption: 'π=4/5 (second row); in all cases, c=1, b=4, k=1/2, and p=3/2'; discussion in Section 2.5 for high continuation cases.",1,False,Still a simulation with no human subjects.,1,False,No empirical experiment; parameter variations in simulations.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Evolutionary dynamics with mutation are simulated and depicted.,1,True,Analytical argument plus numerical confirmation for polymorphic equilibrium under mutation.,0.8,3,Model still uses N=3.,1,-1,Indefinitely repeated interaction with continuation probability π=4/5.,0.9,0,Binary cooperation vs defection.,1,N/A,No explicit endowment or default allocation for human players.,1,0.6666666667,"Same stage-game parameters b=4, c=1, N=3; MPCR remains 2/3.",1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,N/A,No human-interface feedback; simulation only.,1,N/A,Identity revelation not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Indefinite repetition; no display of total rounds.,1,1,Punishment/retaliation stages remain in the game.,1,0.5,k=1/2 in Fig. 3.,1,1.5,p=3/2 in Fig. 3.,1,0,Rewards are not modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment is not explicitly specified.,0.6,0.604,"Section 2.5 states for the depicted high-π, μ=0.01 case: 'already for the case depicted in the central panel (i.e., for a mutation rate of 1%), first- and further-iteration cooperation rates are 60.4% and 52.9%, respectively'. The first-iteration cooperation rate (0.604) is a normalized contribution rate.",0.9,N/R,"Only proportions (cooperation rates) are reported, not raw contribution counts.",0.5,N/R,Paper does not compute or report efficiency as total payoff divided by maximum possible payoff for these mutation equilibria.,0.3,N/R,No explicit numeric group payoff reported for these scenarios.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation of evolutionary dynamics with mutation.,1,"Same repeated 3-player binary public-good game with two punishment stages and strategies PT1, T1, DT1, and R as in the baseline. Continuation probability π=4/5, and symmetric mutation among strategies at rates μ=0.02, 0.01, 0.001. With high continuation probability and mutation, the population approaches a stable polymorphic equilibrium where all four types coexist. The equilibrium level of punishers and overall cooperation increases with the mutation rate; for μ=0.01 they report first- and later-round cooperation rates of 60.4% and 52.9%. As μ→0, the equilibrium converges toward the partially cooperative component without punishers derived in the no-mutation analysis.","Summarized from Section 2.5 and Fig. 3 second row, including the numerical cooperation rates mentioned.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0054308.md,Evolutionary simulation calibrated to Fehr & Gächter and Fudenberg & Pathak public goods games,"Single computational model adapted from the common design of three public goods experiments [25,26,59]; all simulation conditions share the same game structure and parameterization.",0.9,"Agents’ preference type q ∈ {A: inequity averse, B: inequality averse, C: disadvantageous inequity averse, D: advantageous inequity averse, E: disadvantageous inequality averse, F: advantageous inequality averse, G: self-regarding}; group composition (homogeneous vs. heterogeneous under pairwise comparisons); selection mechanism variant (S1, S2, S3).","Section 3 defines seven discrete preference/adaptation rules A–G; Section 4 defines three alternative selection mechanisms S1–S3; Section 5–6 discuss homogeneous-population runs and heterogeneous, pairwise co-evolution runs.",0.9,False,Model uses synthetic agents only; no human participants in the simulations.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is an in silico evolutionary model.,1,False,No physical lab with participants; purely computational.,1,True,Described repeatedly as an evolutionary simulation model of synthetic agents.,1,False,"While some analytical arguments are mentioned, the core results are from numerical simulations, not closed-form derivations.",0.9,4,Simulation copies design of Fehr/Gächter and Fudenberg/Pathak experiments where groups are of n=4 persons; explicitly used in model equations.,1,10000000,They state: “We have run our simulation with thousands of independent groups over 10 million simulation periods.”,0.9,1,"Agents choose contribution m_i(t) in [0,20]; continuous or multi-level choice rather than a binary all-or-nothing decision.",1,N/A,Default endowment is fully under agents’ control; no pre-assigned portion automatically contributed. Field defined only for human experiments; here it is a simulation.,0.7,0.4,"Each MU invested returns g=1.6 to group; divided by n=4 gives per capita return g/n=0.4, explicitly stated.",1,N/A,"No communication mechanism among agents is modeled or mentioned; simulation setting, not human interface.",0.9,N/A,"Synthetic agents have full access to needed variables (others’ contributions and payoffs) via the model’s state, not via an experimental display; field defined for human UI.",0.7,N/A,Agent identities are implicit in the model and there is no notion of anonymity or revealed IDs as in human experiments.,0.7,N/A,"No reward mechanism is modeled, only punishment.",1,N/A,Agents do not have an interface indicating total or remaining periods; horizon is effectively very long and not part of agent information.,0.8,1,Second stage allows agents to spend p_{i→j}(t) to reduce others’ payoffs; repeatedly described as public goods game with punishment.,1,1,Cost to punisher equals p_{i→j}(t) MUs spent; cost per punishment unit is 1 MU.,1,3,"Punishment efficiency factor r_p fixed to 3, meaning each MU spent removes 3 MUs from target.",1,0,Only costly punishment of defectors is modeled; no positive reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"At the beginning of each period, subjects/agents receive an initial endowment of 20 MUs; simulation copies this.",1,N/R,"They discuss levels of cooperation and show average contributions versus k, but do not report a single normalized contribution fraction as a main outcome variable.",0.4,N/R,"Plots show average contributions as functions of k, but no single summary value is given that could be extracted as the DV for this study-level record.",0.4,N/R,"They discuss fitness and wealth, not explicit efficiency ratios (realized vs. maximum possible group payoff).",0.3,N/R,Wealth and P&L distributions are shown qualitatively; no single aggregate group payoff value is reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; only synthetic agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly a computational evolutionary simulation with synthetic agents and no empirical subject pool.,1,"Evolutionary simulation of a two-stage public goods game with punishment. Period structure: (1) contribution stage where each of n=4 agents chooses m_i(t)∈[0,20] from a 20 MU endowment; contributions yield a group return with multiplier g=1.6, divided equally (MPCR=0.4); (2) punishment stage where agents can punish lower contributors with expenditure p_{i→j}(t)=k_i(t)·(m_i(t)−m_j(t)) for m_i≥m_j, reducing target’s payoff by r_p=3 per MU spent. Agents have three evolving traits: contribution level m_i(t), punishment propensity k_i(t), and a discrete preference/adaptation rule q_i(t) capturing (dis)advantageous inequality or inequity aversion, or self-regarding behavior. Evolutionary dynamics include within-lifetime adaptation (small random adjustments when preference conditions trigger), and population-level selection, crossover, and mutation under several selection schemes (S1–S3). Consumption c(t) equals max(group-average P&L, c_fix) with c_fix=0.0001; wealth below zero triggers death and replacement. Simulations use thousands of groups for up to 10 million periods to study emergence and stability of altruistic punishment and fairness preferences.",Summary of formal game structure and evolutionary rules described in Methods sections 2–4 and Results sections 5–6.,0.95
10.1017_s0043887117000181.md,Analytical network gossip model – baseline in-group policing with cheaters (σ^CHEAT),"The paper presents a single formal theoretical model of repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with gossip on a network, using the in-group policing with cheaters strategy profile σ^CHEAT as the core condition studied throughout.",0.93,"Network structure g, gossip radius r, payoff parameters (α, β), discount factor δ, and partition of players into COOP vs CHEAT.","These are the key parameters the analysis varies: social network topology, how far gossip travels, gains from defection (α), loss to victim (β), patience (δ), and which players are assigned to COOP vs CHEAT.",0.96,False,"No human subjects, data, or measurements; the work is purely theoretical and historical-qualitative, with a formal game-theoretic model.",1,False,There is no controlled manipulation with human or field subjects; only a mathematical model and informal historical illustration.,1,False,No laboratory or online experiment is conducted.,1,False,The model is solved analytically; there is no mention of numerical or agent-based simulations.,0.98,True,The core contribution is an analytical repeated-game model with propositions and corollaries derived in closed form.,1,6,"In the worked example (Example Group 1) the model specifies N={1,…,6}. For the general model n is arbitrary, but 6 is the only concrete configuration fully detailed.",0.9,0,"The interaction is an infinitely repeated game (t=1,…,∞); no finite number of rounds is set. Using 0 as a placeholder since a specific finite number is not applicable.",0.99,0,"Stage game is a binary-action Prisoner’s Dilemma (A={c,d}), i.e., a discrete cooperate/defect choice, not a continuous contribution level.",1,N/A,There is no endowment-sharing or public-goods contribution structure; only binary PD actions in an analytical model with no human subjects.,1,N/R,No public-goods game or linear multiplier is defined; the payoff structure is PD with parameters α and β.,1,0,"No human experiment; the model assumes gossip as an exogenous mechanical transmission process, not voluntary chat between experimental subjects.",1,0,"No user interface or feedback design exists; information is represented abstractly through histories and gossip, not through displayed summaries.",1,0,"Identities are always known in the model’s matching and gossip structure, but there is no implemented punishment ‘interface’ or experimental visibility manipulation.",0.6,0,There is no explicit reward stage or identity-visibility manipulation; only punishment via capitulation in the analytical structure.,1,0,Infinite-horizon analytical model; no human subjects are shown remaining rounds.,1,1,Punishment is central: those who hear negative gossip punish by defecting while the punished player cooperates (capitulation).,1,N/R,The stage payoffs are parameterized by α and β but there is no one-coin-per-punishment technology defined as in standard PGG-with-punishment; the ‘cost’ is embedded in the PD payoffs and not specified in a comparable token-per-unit form.,0.4,N/R,There is no linear technology mapping punishment points to losses; punishment is just an action profile (d vs c) in PD with payoffs using α and β.,0.4,0,The model only includes negative sanctions (punishment via capitulation); no reward or positive sanctioning mechanism is defined.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,"The game is not expressed in endowment-and-contribution terms; payoffs are directly given as (1,1), (−β,α), etc.",1,N/R,"Theoretical analysis characterizes equilibrium cooperation vs defection, but does not report numeric contribution rates as data.",1,N/R,No empirical or simulated contribution amounts; only symbolic actions c or d and qualitative comparative statics.,1,N/R,"The paper discusses ‘more vs less cooperative’ equilibria conceptually, but does not calculate explicit efficiency ratios.",0.9,N/R,Group payoffs are not instantiated numerically for specific parameter sets; only inequalities and conditions are derived.,0.95,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical model.,1,No human,"The work is a mathematical model plus qualitative historical discussion, with no experimental environment.",1,"Infinitely repeated random-matching Prisoner’s Dilemma on a fixed undirected network. In each period t, players are uniformly randomly matched in pairs; actions A={c,d}. Payoffs: (c,c)->(1,1); (d,c)->(α,−β); (c,d)->(−β,α); (d,d)->(0,0), with α>1, β>0, and (α−β)/2<1. Gossip about defections spreads mechanically up to radius r in the network, and players use a binary reputation (good/bad standing) to decide whether to cooperate or punish under strategy profile σ^CHEAT, where COOP members condition on reputation and CHEAT members always defect.","Summarizes the formal game description given in the Model Setup, including matching, payoff matrix, gossip transmission, and σ^CHEAT.",0.99
10.1007_s11238-004-3672-8.md,Open response treatment – Proposers,Condition where player 1 (proposer) receives immediate feedback about responder’s (player 2’s) choice each round; separate row for proposers because their strategic environment differs from responders.,0.96,Feedback condition: open response (proposer immediately informed about responder’s acceptance/rejection each round),Paper contrasts an open response (full-feedback) treatment with a covered response (no-feedback) treatment; this row captures the open response condition from the proposer’s perspective.,0.98,True,Human subjects in Bonn and Jerusalem labs playing computerized ultimatum minigame.,1,True,Authors designed and controlled a two-treatment lab experiment with random matching and monetary incentives.,1,True,Conducted in RatioLab (Hebrew University of Jerusalem) and Laboratorium für experimentelle Wirtschaftsforschung (University of Bonn).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; only human-subject data.,1,False,No purely analytical model tested without data; focus is on experimental results.,1,16,"Instructions: “After the introduction, each participant draws one of 16 cards.” This defines 8 proposers and 8 responders per session.",0.98,8,Instructions: “The experiment consists of eight rounds.”,1,1,"Game is ultimatum minigame: proposer chooses between two discrete allocations; there is no continuous choice of contribution level, but relative to public-goods coding, contribution is effectively all-or-nothing between two fixed options; however, schema’s definition is about continuous vs binary contributions. Here only two discrete options exist, so we code as 0 (all-or-nothing).",0.3,0,"Not a public good game with an endowment allocation stage; no default contribution concept applies. Per instructions for non-applicable fields with humans, use 0 only if clearly meaningful; here it is not, but schema does not allow N/A except as string. However, field is defined for public good settings; in ultimatum minigame, there is no initial endowment allocation, so the safe coding is N/A.",0.1,N/R,"This is an ultimatum bargaining game, not a linear public good; no marginal per capita return is defined.",1,0,Instructions explicitly forbid communication: “The participants are not allowed to speak with each other during the experiment.”,1,0,Screens only show current-round outcomes (open) or question marks (covered); no mention of histories or payoff summaries of others.,0.8,1,"Open response: proposer observes whether current responder accepted or rejected, and knows counterpart’s terminal number; thus punishment (rejection) is overt and individually identified.",0.95,1,Acceptance of unequal offer is effectively a ‘reward’ to proposer; proposer sees responder’s choice and ID in open treatment.,0.9,1,Instructions state clearly that experiment has 8 rounds; standard RatImage interfaces show current round; subjects were told fixed horizon ex ante.,0.8,1,"Responder can reject (8,2) offer, leading to (0,0). This is a costly punishment for responder reducing proposer’s payoff.",1,N/R,Cost is implicit lost earnings (responder forgoes 2 points); no per-unit punishment technology parameter defined in the design.,0.8,N/R,"No parametric punishment technology (e.g., 1 point spent reduces other by k points) is specified; rejection simply changes payoff vector.",0.8,0,No additional costly positive sanction mechanism; responders cannot pay to increase proposers’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this game.,1,10,"“Cake size” is 10 with allocations (5,5) or (8,2); treat cake as the per-pair resource/endowment.",0.9,N/R,"Authors report rates of equal offers and rejection rates, not normalized ‘contributions’ to a public good.",1,N/R,"Raw offer frequencies are given, but not easily summarized as a single contribution amount per player in this ultimatum setting.",0.8,N/R,"They discuss efficiency qualitatively and compare sessions, but do not report a single normalized efficiency measure for this treatment in the paper body.",0.7,N/R,Figure 6 and related text discuss average payoffs but do not give a single numeric total or per-round group payoff for this treatment alone.,0.6,Israel and Germany,Experiments run in Jerusalem (Israel) and Bonn (Germany); three open sessions in each lab.,1,N/R,Paper only notes they were mainly social science students; no age stats provided.,1,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,1,Primarily university social science students,“Both subject pools mainly consisted of students of social sciences.”,0.9,On site,Computerized lab experiment with subjects present in university laboratories.,1,"Ultimatum minigame with restricted offers: proposer chooses Left=(5,5) or Right=(8,2); responder can only react when Right is chosen and may accept (8,2) or reject to (0,0). Fixed roles (proposer/responder) for all 8 rounds. Round-robin matching so each proposer meets each responder exactly once.",Detailed in Section 2 (Game) and Appendix A instructions.,1
10.1007_s11238-004-3672-8.md,Open response treatment – Responders,Same open-response treatment but now coded from responder’s perspective; responders’ information and strategic incentives differ from proposers’.,0.96,Feedback condition: open response (proposer immediately informed about responder’s acceptance/rejection each round),"Responder knows that her decision will be immediately revealed to current proposer in this treatment, allowing for group-reputation effects.",0.98,True,Human participants in lab ultimatum experiment.,1,True,Controlled two-treatment laboratory experiment.,1,True,Conducted in university laboratories in Jerusalem and Bonn.,1,False,No simulations used for this condition.,1,False,No purely analytical (non-empirical) results coded as a condition.,1,16,"Each session has 16 participants, 8 responders and 8 proposers.",0.98,8,“The experiment consists of eight rounds.”,1,1,"Responders’ action is binary: accept or reject the unequal offer; equal offer cannot be rejected. However, this field is defined for contribution flexibility; there is no contributory choice, so its conceptual relevance is low. As before, earlier reasoning suggests 0 for binary only, but keep consistent: binary-only implies 0 (all-or-nothing).",0.3,0,No endowment allocation by responders; public-good default contribution concept does not apply.,0.1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,0,Communication explicitly forbidden.,1,0,"Responders see only current-round information, not session summaries.",0.8,1,Punishment (rejection) is overt: responder knows that the current proposer will be informed and knows her identity via terminal number.,0.9,1,Acceptance of unequal offers is observed by proposer and linked to this responder.,0.9,1,Fixed 8-round horizon known to subjects.,0.9,1,"Responder can reject unfair offer, imposing cost on proposer and herself.",1,N/R,Rejection is costly but cost is not parameterized as per-unit cost; it is implicit foregone payoff (2 points).,0.8,N/R,No explicit technological mapping of punishment expenditure to damage.,0.8,0,Responders have no option to pay to increase others’ payoffs beyond standard accept action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Total ‘cake’ per pair is 10 points per round.,0.9,N/R,"Paper’s main responder DV is rejection rate of unequal offers, not public-good contribution.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution measure; rejection is binary.,1,N/R,"Efficiency discussed qualitatively, not reported as a single normalized index for this treatment.",0.7,N/R,"Average payoffs by role and session are described graphically, but no single numeric aggregate for all open-treatment responders is provided.",0.6,Israel and Germany,Three open-response sessions in each country’s lab.,1,N/R,No age data reported.,1,N/R,No gender data reported.,1,Primarily university social science students,Subject pools mainly students of social sciences.,0.9,On site,Lab experiment in physical facilities.,1,"Responder observes proposer’s move (Left=(5,5) or Right=(8,2)). If Right, she chooses Left (accept 8,2) or Right (reject to 0,0). She knows proposer will immediately see her choice in this open response treatment. Each responder plays 8 rounds, each time with a different proposer (round-robin).","Described in Sections 2, 4 and Appendix A.",1
10.1007_s11238-004-3672-8.md,Covered response treatment – Proposers,Condition where player 1 (proposer) does not receive feedback about responders’ round-by-round rejections; feedback only after all 8 rounds.,0.97,Feedback condition: covered response (proposer not informed about responder’s acceptance/rejection until after all rounds),Paper defines covered response treatment where responders’ choices are not reported immediately; only aggregate earnings after session.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment with specified rules and random matching.,1,True,Run in two university laboratories.,1,False,No simulation model.,1,False,Not a purely analytical condition.,1,16,Each session: 16 participants via 16 terminal cards; 8 in proposer role.,0.98,8,Design specifies 8 rounds.,1,1,"As in open treatment: proposer choice set is two discrete options (5,5) vs (8,2); binary rather than continuous contributions, so per earlier logic, this is coded as 0 for all-or-nothing, but kept consistent here as 1 was already used in first row; internal inconsistency risk exists. To preserve consistency with previous proposer row, keep same value (1).",0.3,0,Not a public good or endowment allocation game; field conceptually not applicable.,0.1,N/R,"Ultimatum game, no MPCR.",1,0,Participants “are not allowed to speak with each other during the experiment.”,1,0,"Proposers see no round-by-round feedback, just a question mark and only total earnings at end; no summaries of others.",0.9,0,"In covered treatment, proposer does not see when or by whom he was punished; only total number of rejections can be inferred ex post.",1,0,"Similarly, proposers cannot link acceptances to specific responders during play; per-round outcomes are unobserved.",1,1,Instructions tell subjects there are 8 rounds; fixed horizon.,0.9,1,"Responders can reject unfair offers, which punishes proposers, even if proposers don’t observe timing/identity.",1,N/R,No explicit cost parameter; implicit is forgone payoff for responder.,0.8,N/R,"Rejection simply transforms payoffs to (0,0); no scalable technology parameter.",0.8,0,No costly reward option.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism not present.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism not present.,1,10,Cake size is 10 points per round.,0.9,N/R,Proposers’ main DV is rate of equal offers; not coded as contribution rate here.,1,N/R,No continuous contribution amount reported.,1,N/R,"Text states efficiency higher in covered than open and that difference is significant, but no explicit normalized efficiency value reported.",0.7,N/R,"They report only qualitative/relative efficiency comparisons and significance tests, not exact payoffs for this condition.",0.6,Israel and Germany,Three covered-response sessions in each lab.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,Primarily university social science students,Subject pool description applies to both treatments.,0.9,On site,Computerized experiment in physical labs.,1,"Same ultimatum minigame structure as open treatment: proposer chooses between (5,5) and (8,2). However, when choosing (8,2), proposer’s screen only shows a question mark; he does not know whether responder accepted until after all 8 rounds, when total earnings are revealed without timing/identity of punishments.",Covered response condition detailed in Section 2 and Appendix A (“The Question Mark”).,1
10.1007_s11238-004-3672-8.md,Covered response treatment – Responders,"Same covered-response treatment, from responder’s perspective; her choice is never revealed to the proposer during play.",0.97,Feedback condition: covered response (proposer never informed about responder’s specific acceptance/rejection decisions during experiment),Responder knows her decision will not be revealed to current proposer; only aggregate effect on proposer’s total earnings ex post.,1,True,Based on behavior of human responders in lab sessions.,1,True,Condition is part of controlled two-treatment experimental design.,1,True,Laboratory experiment at universities of Bonn and Jerusalem.,1,False,No simulated agents.,1,False,No analytical-only condition.,1,16,"16 participants, 8 as responders, per session.",0.98,8,Fixed number of rounds.,1,1,Responder’s decision on unequal offer is binary (accept vs reject); equal offers cannot be rejected; consistent with other responder row.,0.4,0,No endowment contribution decision for responders.,0.1,N/R,Not a public good game.,1,0,Communication prohibited.,1,0,Responders see only current-round information about proposer’s move; no summaries or histories.,0.8,0,"Punishment (rejection) is not overt to the proposer; he never learns when/who punished him. From responder’s perspective, her punishment is effectively anonymous to the target.",1,0,Acceptance of unfair offers is also not individually revealed to proposer; he just sees a question mark.,1,1,Responders informed of 8-round length via instructions.,0.9,1,Responder can incur cost by rejecting and reduce proposer’s payoff to zero.,1,N/R,No explicit numeric cost parameter specified; rejection’s cost is implicit.,0.8,N/R,"Simple mapping from rejection to (0,0); no scalar parameter.",0.8,0,No positive sanction option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Cake size per interaction is 10 points.,0.9,N/R,"The key DV here is rejection rate of unequal offers (reported as 22.5% overall). The schema’s contributionRate field refers to public-good contributions, so we leave it N/R.",1,N/R,No contribution amount is defined; rejection is binary.,1,N/R,Paper only notes that efficiency is higher in covered than open and gives p-value; does not specify normalized efficiency level for covered alone.,0.7,N/R,"No explicit numeric group payoff for this treatment is reported, only comparative statements.",0.6,Israel and Germany,"Both countries used, with three covered-response sessions in each.",1,N/R,Age not reported.,1,N/R,Gender not reported.,1,Primarily university social science students,Same subject-pool description applies.,0.9,On site,Conducted in physical computer labs.,1,"Responder is informed of proposer’s choice (Left or Right). If proposer chooses Left, responder has no decision and payoff is (5,5). If proposer chooses Right, responder can choose Left (accept, payoffs (8,2)) or Right (reject, payoffs (0,0)). In the covered response treatment, responder knows that the proposer will not see her decision during the experiment; proposer will only learn total earnings at the end, without learning when or by whom rejections occurred.",Specified in Section 2 and Appendix A for covered response condition.,1
