10.1371_journal.pone.0020474.md,Simulation – Metanorms game on networks (all sampled topologies),"Paper describes a single computational study of Axelrod-style metanorms with fixed payoff parameters, 50 agents, 4 rounds per generation, 50,000 generations, varying only the network topology (Barabási-Albert, Watts with various β, Erdös-Rényi, radius 1 vs 2, and the resulting statistics like average degree, clustering, interconnectedness). No separate payoff or evolutionary conditions are defined.",0.9,"Network topology and structure: Barabási-Albert, Watts small-world (varying rewiring probability β), Erdös-Rényi; neighborhood radius 1 vs 2; resulting statistics including average degree, clustering coefficient, and interconnectedness (average number of triplets per agent).","Only manipulated factors across runs are the network generation algorithm, its parameters (e.g., β), and radius definition, which change average degree, clustering, and interconnectedness. Payoff matrix, number of agents, evolutionary parameters remain fixed.",0.95,False,Study is based on computer simulations and mathematical analysis of agents on networks; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects; it is a computational model exploration.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; purely in silico.,1,True,Agents are artificial; dynamics explored via extensive computer simulation of the metanorms game on generated networks.,1,True,"Paper derives closed-form expected payoff expressions, ESS conditions, and gradient fields (Eq. 2–6) for a simplified version of the game.",1,50,“We set up the metanorms game on networks by embedding 50 agents on a network… We use 50 agents instead of 20 in Axelrod's default setting…”,1,4,“The game is played four rounds called a generation.”,1,1,This is not a contribution game with continuous choice; agents’ key decisions are binary (defect vs cooperate; punish vs not punish). Players do not choose a fractional contribution level. Coded as all-or-nothing allowed.,0.7,N/A,Model is not framed as a public goods contribution with an endowment; actions are discrete defect/punish decisions.,1,N/R,No public goods linear multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; payoffs are given as temptation and hurt parameters instead.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between simulated agents is not modeled as chat.,1,0,Agents observe individual defections and non-punishments probabilistically via network links; no mention of summary statistics being displayed.,0.8,0,Model specifies who punishes whom deterministically in the interaction structure; there is no separate identity-revelation mechanism akin to experimental anonymity manipulations.,0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the game.,1,0,Agents are not described as having information about the remaining number of rounds; horizon is not part of their decision rule.,0.7,1,Stage 2: “Punishment is costly: one must pay Enforcement cost (E = -2) to impose Punishment cost (P = -9) on the defector.”,1,2,Enforcement cost E = -2 is the cost to the punisher per punishment action.,1,9,Punishment cost P = -9 is the loss imposed on the punished per unit enforcement act.,1,0,No positive rewards for cooperation are modeled; only punishments and metapunitions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"There is no per-round monetary endowment; agents accumulate payoffs from T, H, E, P, ME, MP. An equivalent ‘endowment’ is not specified as a bound.",0.6,N/R,"Outcome measures are average boldness, vengefulness, time spent in norm establishment/collapse zones, not contribution rates to a public good.",1,N/R,No token contributions are defined; the game has binary defection/cooperation with fixed payoffs.,1,N/R,"Paper reports qualitative regions of norm establishment vs collapse and payoffs, but does not compute efficiency as ratio of achieved to maximal group payoff.",0.7,N/R,"Formulas for individual expected payoffs are provided, but aggregate realized group payoffs are not tabulated as a dependent variable.",0.7,N/A,No human participants in the simulations or analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Work consists of mathematical analysis and agent-based simulations only.,1,"Metanorms game with 50 agents on static networks; three stages per round: (1) each agent defects with probability equal to boldness if able, gaining T=3 and imposing H=-1 on all others for each defection; (2) neighbors who observe defection may punish at cost E=-2 to themselves and P=-9 to defector, conditional on link and vengefulness; (3) neighbors who observe failures to punish may meta-punish at cost ME=-2 to themselves and MP=-9 to the unpunishing agent, again via links and vengefulness. Boldness and vengefulness are each encoded as 3-bit strings, mapping to 8 values uniformly from 0 to 1. Networks generated via Barabási-Albert, Watts (various β), and Erdös-Rényi algorithms, with radius-1 and radius-2 neighborhoods considered. Evolution: 4 rounds per generation; payoffs reset at generation start and accumulated; strategy update via roulette-wheel selection among neighbors proportional to fitness (payoff minus neighborhood minimum) and bitwise mutation at rate 0.01. Simulation runs for 50,000 generations. Analyses focus on ESS (norm collapse vs norm establishment), gradient dynamics in boldness-vengefulness space, and the impact of average degree, clustering coefficient, and interconnectedness (average number of triplets per agent) on time spent in norm collapse vs norm establishment zones.","Synthesized from Methods (Metanorms Games on Networks), Results (Mathematical analysis, Simulation), and Table 1.",0.98
10.1038_s41598-020-57562-1.md,Analytical leadership game without foresight (baseline inspection game variant),"The paper defines a 2x2 leadership/inspection game between a leader and a subordinate with payoffs in Table 2 and analyzes Nash equilibria, QRE, level-k, fictitious play, and evolutionary dynamics under this payoff structure without modifying utilities via foresight.",1,"Strategy revision / cognition protocol without foresight (myopic best response, Quantal Response Equilibrium with precision λ, level-k cognition, fictitious play, reinforcement learning, selective imitation); parameters b, c, d, h, k, θ vary symbolically.","Across this baseline game, the authors vary only the way strategies are updated or chosen (best response, QRE, level-k, fictitious play, reinforcement learning, selective imitation) while the underlying payoff structure (Table 2) and parameters b, c, d, h, k, θ define the game.",0.86,False,No human participants; the work is purely theoretical (analytical derivations plus simulations).,1,False,There is no controlled experiment with human subjects; only mathematical analysis and numerical simulations of strategy dynamics.,1,False,No lab or field setting; the study is theoretical.,1,True,They numerically solve QRE and run stochastic simulations of learning and imitation dynamics based on the analytical model.,0.96,True,"They derive payoff functions, best responses, Nash equilibria, and differential equations symbolically.",1,2,The leadership game is defined as a 2x2 game between one leader and one subordinate.,1,2,"The base leadership game payoffs and analysis (Nash, QRE, level-k, fictitious play, reinforcement, imitation) implicitly consider infinitely or repeatedly played interactions; however, a specific fixed number of rounds is not given. Set to 2 is not meaningful; true number is not reported.",0,1,Action space is binary: subordinate either produces (x=1) or shirks (x=0); leader either inspects (y=1) or not (y=0). This is effectively all-or-nothing contribution and inspection.,1,N/A,No human subjects and no explicit endowment allocation; only symbolic payoffs.,1,N/R,"They do not define a marginal per capita return in the usual public goods sense; payoffs use parameters b, c, θ instead.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,Model only specifies immediate payoffs and strategy dynamics; no information-display structure is described.,0.9,0,There is a single leader and subordinate in the 2-player game; identity is implicit and not a modeled choice variable. No explicit identity revelation mechanism is described.,0.8,0,"There is no reward mechanism in the leadership game, only punishment.",1,0,Number of rounds or horizon is not an explicit information variable in the model.,0.9,1,Leader inspecting a shirking subordinate inflicts punishment d at cost k to the leader (Table 2).,1,N/R,"Punishment cost to the leader is parameter k, but no numeric value is specified.",1,N/R,"Magnitude of punishment to subordinate is parameter d; no numeric value is specified, and units relative to cost k are not fixed.",0.95,0,No positive reward mechanism; only costly punishment and benefit sharing via θ.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this model.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this model.,1,N/R,The model uses a cost of contributing c but does not define an endowment of tokens/points.,1,N/R,"They track probability p that the subordinate contributes, but no single numeric outcome is reported; results are qualitative and parameter-dependent.",0.7,N/R,Contribution is binary (produce or shirk) with symbolic cost c; no numeric contribution amount is specified as an outcome.,0.9,N/R,"They discuss equilibria qualitatively (e.g., (0,0) vs (1,1)) but do not report efficiency as a numeric ratio.",1,N/R,"Payoff formulas for leader and subordinate are given, but no specific parameter values to compute numeric group payoffs.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical and computational with no empirical data.,1,"Two-player leadership/inspection game where subordinate chooses produce (x=1) or shirk (x=0); leader chooses inspect (y=1) or not (y=0). If subordinate produces, benefit b is generated and split: (1−θ)b to subordinate, θb to leader, with subordinate paying cost c. If leader inspects, they pay cost h. If leader inspects a shirker, subordinate is punished by d and leader pays additional cost k. Assumptions: θb>h; without punishment the subordinate prefers shirking, (1−θ)b−c<0; with punishment the subordinate prefers producing, (1−θ)b−c>−d. Under standard best response, unique Nash equilibrium is (x=0,y=0). QRE, level-k, fictitious play, reinforcement learning, and selective imitation (without subordinate best-response conditioning) all converge to or favor (0,0).",Summarizes the payoff structure and main strategic properties of the baseline leadership game using the description around Table 2 and subsequent analysis.,1
10.1038_s41598-020-57562-1.md,"Leadership game with leader foresight (foresight parameter ω, best-response subordinate)",The paper introduces foresight by modifying the leader’s utility to weight current costs against expected future benefits (Table 3) and analyzes Nash equilibria and evolutionary dynamics as a distinct model variant.,1,"Foresight parameter ω for leaders (0<ω<1), with subordinate using best response; comparison of regimes ω<ω*, ω>ω**, and ω**<ω<ω* where ω*=(h+k)/(θb+h+k) and ω**=h/(θb+h).","In this condition the key new independent variable is ω, which controls the extent of foresight in the leader and changes the set of Nash equilibria and evolutionary outcomes.",0.95,False,No human participants; this is a theoretical extension of the game.,1,False,The foresight model is not an empirical experiment but an analytical and simulated model variant.,1,False,No lab environment or human data.,1,True,"They simulate evolutionary dynamics of the leader’s mixed inspection probability q under foresight, including numerical illustrations mentioned in the text and SI.",0.9,True,"They derive the modified utility function u_L, new Nash equilibria, thresholds ω*, ω**, and conditions for convergence analytically.",1,2,Same 2-player leader-subordinate structure as the baseline game.,1,2,"Foresight explicitly considers this and the next round’s payoffs, using a weighted sum of current and next-round utilities; the long-run dynamics are over many rounds but the foresight utility is 2-period.",0.7,1,"Subordinate still chooses x∈{0,1} (produce vs shirk); leader chooses y∈{0,1} (inspect vs not).",1,N/A,No human endowments or initial allocations; just symbolic costs.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined in this two-player leadership game; benefits and sharing are parameterized by b and θ.,1,N/A,No human communication is modeled.,1,0,No informational feedback variables of this type are specified; agents see only actions/payoffs within the model assumptions.,0.9,0,Leader and subordinate identities are implicit and fixed; no modeling of identity revelation of punishment targets.,0.8,0,"There is still no reward mechanism, only punishment and benefit sharing.",1,0,"While foresight considers the next round, the model does not include a display of total or remaining rounds as a strategic variable.",0.9,1,Same punishment structure as baseline; foresight changes how leaders value inspection but not the underlying punishment rule.,1,N/R,Punishment cost parameter k remains symbolic; no numeric value is given.,1,N/R,Punishment magnitude d is symbolic; no concrete numeric ratio is provided.,0.95,0,Foresight modifies only punishment/inspection incentives; there is no reward option introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"As before, only a contribution cost c is specified, not an endowment.",1,N/R,"Theoretical results show that equilibria can be (0,0) or (1,1), and mixed equilibria exist; but no single numeric contribution rate p is reported independent of parameters.",0.7,N/R,Contribution is binary and parameter-dependent; no numeric token amounts are given.,0.9,N/R,"They discuss that foresight can move the system from (0,0) to (1,1), improving payoffs, but do not calculate an explicit efficiency ratio.",1,N/R,"Payoffs at (1,1) are given symbolically as u_S=(1−θ)b−c, u_L=θb−h; no numeric parameterization to compute a specific group payoff.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a theoretical/simulation-based extension with no empirical data.,1,"Leader’s utility is modified to include foresight: u_L(x,y)=(1−ω)(−[h+k(1−x)]y)+ω(θb y), where ω is the foresight parameter (0<ω<1). Subordinate keeps standard payoff-based best response (no foresight). With foresight, state (0,0) is a Nash equilibrium only if (1−ω)(h+k)>ωθb (ω<ω*=(h+k)/(θb+h+k)), and a new Nash equilibrium (1,1) appears if ωθb>(1−ω)h (ω>ω**=h/(θb+h)). For intermediate ω**<ω<ω* both equilibria coexist. Mixed-strategy analysis yields a critical p_c and unstable mixed equilibrium (p*,q*)=(p_c,q_c). Evolutionary dynamics under direct strategy revision can drive q (leader inspection probability) toward 1, especially if θb>(1−ω)(k+h) or if initial q exceeds a threshold, leading the system to the cooperative state (1,1) where the subordinate produces and the leader inspects consistently.","Summarizes how foresight changes the leader’s utility, thresholds for equilibria, and dynamic implications, based on the section titled 'Foresight' and following analysis.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2015.09.009.md,Baseline Prisoner’s Dilemma without punishment,Condition explicitly described as the case without punishment (no P or A strategies).,1,No punishment; only cooperation (C) and defection (D) strategies allowed.,Section 3 and Fig. 1 mention the case without punishment as comparison.,0.9,False,Model is an evolutionary game with well-mixed population and Moran process; no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; purely theoretical model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; analytical/simulation work only.,1,True,They compute stationary distributions and fixation probabilities numerically from the Moran process (agent-based / Markov simulations implied).,0.8,True,"They derive fixation probabilities, conditions, and stationary distributions analytically (Appendix E).",1,100,"Population size N=100 used in figures and analyses (e.g., Fig. 1, 2, 3).",1,1,Prisoner’s dilemma interactions are one-shot within an ongoing Moran process; no repeated-round structure per game.,0.7,1,Binary action set (cooperate or defect); no graded contribution levels.,1,N/A,Not a public goods contribution model with endowments; it is a PD without explicit initial allocation per round.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; marginal per capita return not defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction; analytical/simulation model.,1,N/A,No human interface; not applicable.,1,N/A,No identities or displays; theoretical population model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,No interface to players; infinite-time Markov process.,1,0,Baseline explicitly without punishment strategies.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,Only punishment mechanisms are modeled; no reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,"Model specifies payoffs (b, c) but not per-round endowments or budgets.",0.5,N/R,"They report strategy abundances, not normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No explicit token contributions; just binary cooperation/defection.,0.3,N/R,They do not report total payoff relative to max as a normalized efficiency metric.,0.2,N/R,"Only average payoffs per strategy and fixation-based conditions are discussed, not explicit group payoff statistics.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical and simulation-based evolutionary game; no empirical environment.,1,"Two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma interactions in a well-mixed population of size N with benefit b and cost c (b>c>0), updated via a frequency-dependent Moran process with low mutation (μ→0). Payoffs: mutual cooperation b−c each; mutual defection 0; cooperator vs defector gets −c and b respectively. Fitness: 1−ω+ω·payoff.",Summarized from Model section and Appendix E.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2015.09.009.md,Peer pro-social punishment only (no anti-social punishment),"Condition described where only peer pro-social punishment P_pe is available (strategies C, P_pe, D).",1,"Punishment pattern = peer; only pro-social punishment allowed (strategies: C, peer pro-social punishers P_pe, defectors D).","Section 2: ‘In peer punishment condition, the only possible strategies are cooperators (C), peer pro-social punishers (P_pe), and defectors (D).’ Anti-social punishment absent.",1,False,Evolutionary game model without human subjects.,1,False,No behavioral experiment; theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab or field setting.,1,True,"Stationary distributions, fixation probabilities, and fixation times are ‘calculated analytically’ but also evaluated numerically for parameter values (Fig. 2, 3).",0.8,True,"Appendix A and E provide payoff matrices and closed-form conditions for emergence (e.g., β>2c+α).",1,100,"Parameters for figures and examples use N=100 (e.g., Fig. 1, 2, 3).",1,1,"Each PD interaction is one-shot; repetition arises from population updating, not from multi-round games between fixed partners.",0.7,1,Only binary cooperate/defect choice; no partial contribution levels.,1,N/A,No explicit endowment or public goods contribution setup.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No user interface; purely theoretical population model.,1,N/A,No identities defined for individuals.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit rounds shown to agents; stochastic evolutionary updates continue indefinitely.,1,1,Peer pro-social punishment with cost α and fine β is active.,1,10,"In Fig. 1 parameters: α=10, β=30. Appendix A: each peer punisher costs α to impose fine β.",0.9,30,"Fine imposed per punished opponent is β, with β>α; Fig. 1 uses β=30.",0.9,0,Only punishment is modeled; no reward.,1,N/A,No rewards exist in this model.,1,N/A,No rewards exist in this model.,1,N/R,Model uses benefit b and cost c; no separate token endowment specified.,0.5,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as stationary abundances of strategies, not normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No explicit numeric contribution amounts beyond abstract cost c.,0.3,N/R,They do not provide normalized efficiency (total payoff / max payoff).,0.2,N/R,No explicit aggregate group payoff statistics; only strategy-level payoffs and abundances.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical and simulation model only.,1,"Two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with benefit b and cost c; strategies C, peer pro-social punishers P_pe, and D. Peer punishers spend cost α to impose fine β on defective partners after PD. Payoff matrix given in Appendix A; dynamics via Moran process with low mutation μ→0; intensity of selection ω determines contribution of payoff to fitness.","Summarized from main text, Fig. 3A, and Appendix A/E.",1
