d numerical evolutionary analysis without human subjects.,1,"Combination of peer pro-social punishment and pool anti-social punishment. Appendix D gives payoffs for populations with peer pro-social punishers and pool anti-social punishers, e.g., P_Ppe(i) = [ (i−1)(b−c) + (−c−α)(N−i) ]/(N−1) − (N−i)β/i, P_Apo(i) = i(b−β)/(N−1) − α. Other population compositions use payoff formulas from Appendices A and B. Moran process dynamics with low μ and selection intensity ω.","Summarized from Appendix D, Section 3.2, and Fig. 4M–P, Fig. 5B.",1
10.1016_j.geb.2013.06.012.md,Main duopoly game – No communication,"Paper describes a single repeated Bertrand duopoly game with a between-markets communication manipulation; one quarter of the 100 markets had communication enabled, the rest did not. This row captures the no-communication condition.",0.95,Communication possibility: No communication between matched sellers.,Only treatment variation mentioned is whether subjects can post text messages through the website; this row records the condition without that option.,0.95,True,Human university students played the repeated price competition game.,1,True,Design involved random assignment of subjects to markets and to communication vs no-communication; payoffs determined by controlled payoff table.,1,True,Subjects attended an introductory lab briefing and then played via a dedicated website; this is best classified as a lab/online experiment rather than field or observational.,0.9,False,"Outcomes came from human play, not simulated agents.",1,False,"While theory is discussed, main results are from an experiment, not closed-form analysis alone.",1,2,Subjects were randomly paired into 100 duopoly markets; each market has two sellers.,1,28.1,Random continuation rule after round 12 with 5% stopping probability; they report markets lasted 28.1 rounds on average. Use this average as number of rounds.,0.7,1,"Players choose any integer price from 1 to 25 each round, not just a binary contribute/defect choice.",1,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game; no endowment allocation between private/public accounts.,1,N/R,"Game is a Bertrand duopoly with a payoff table, not a linear public good; no marginal per capita return is defined or implied.",1,0,This condition is the subset of markets without the communication option; they could not post messages.,1,1,"Subjects could see their total payoff and could click a link to see past choices of both sellers and associated payoffs, which constitutes feedback on others’ outcomes.",0.9,0,"There is no explicit punishment technology or choice; only prices are chosen. Identities are never revealed, only labeled as 'other seller'.",1,0,No explicit reward technology; only prices and resulting payoffs are observed anonymously.,1,0,Stopping is random with 5% chance after round 12; no indication that remaining rounds or final round were displayed.,0.8,0,"No stage of the game lets subjects directly spend to reduce another’s payoff; punishment is only via choosing lower prices, which is endogenous play, not a separate punishment stage.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism is implemented.,1,0,No separate reward action; only prices with derived payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are given by a price payoff table in 'lab dollars'; no per-round monetary endowment is specified, only example payoffs at particular prices.",0.3,N/R,"Outcome variable is pricing and earnings, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No contributions; only prices chosen each round.,1,N/R,They report payoffs in dollars and PDV of earnings but do not normalize by maximum feasible surplus in a way that allows constructing a 0–1 efficiency index.,0.3,N/R,"They report individual average earnings (S$3.30 per round) but not joint payoffs by treatment separately, so condition-specific group payoff cannot be extracted.",0.4,Singapore,Experiment conducted at the National University of Singapore with NUS students.,0.95,N/R,Described only as university students; no age range or mean reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition statistics reported.,0.01,University students (undergraduate; multiple majors),Subjects recruited from 50 different university classes at NUS; text notes undergraduate research assistant wage for comparison.,0.9,Online,"After a lab briefing session, all pricing decisions were made 'through the Internet' via a website over several months.",1,"Infinitely repeated (randomly stopped) differentiated Bertrand duopoly with integer prices 1–25 L$; one-shot Nash price p^n = 2 (payoff 45.02 L$), monopoly price p^m = 23 (payoff 270.65 L$), and a myopic best response of 21 against 23 yielding 525 L$; continuation probability 0.95 from round 13 onward (5% termination chance each round after 12). Occasionally (approx. every 5 rounds with state-dependent probability) one-period-ahead strategies s_it(p) were elicited instead of current prices.",Summarizes key structural features and payoffs of the repeated game from the design section.,1
