,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Design specifies payoffs per price pair but not an initial endowment per round in standard contribution-game terms.,0.3,N/R,"Main behavioral variables are prices, strategies, and earnings, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decision exists.,1,N/R,Paper compares earnings and PDV across strategies and treatments but does not report normalized efficiency values.,0.3,N/R,"While prices are higher with communication, the paper does not provide a numeric average group payoff by condition.",0.4,Singapore,All subjects are students at the National University of Singapore.,0.95,N/R,Only 'university students' is reported; no age data given.,0.2,N/R,No information about gender distribution.,0.01,University students (undergraduate; multiple majors),Recruited from 50 different university classes at NUS; comparison wage is for undergraduate research assistants.,0.9,Online,Same implementation via Internet website after in-lab briefing.,1,"Same infinitely repeated Bertrand duopoly as in the no-communication condition: two sellers choose integer prices 1–25 L$ each round using a fixed payoff table. One-shot Nash price p^n = 2 (45.02 L$), monopoly price p^m = 23 (270.65 L$), and a best-response price 21 against 23 yields 525 L$. Continuation probability is 0.95 per round after round 12. Occasionally (rounds 10, 15, 20, … depending on pre-drawn market length and randomization), one-period-ahead pricing strategies s_it(p) are elicited instead of a simple price. In this treatment, matched subjects can send one text message per round through the website; messages are screened to prevent identity revelation and are mostly used to coordinate on high prices.",Combines the core game description with the communication-specific details described in Section 2 and 4.6.,1
10.1023_a_1020598804674.md,Correlation simulation – varying correlation factor c,"Simulation described in 'Correlation' section and Figure 2, where only the correlation factor c is varied while other parameters (2-player PD, replicator dynamics) are held constant.",0.92,Correlation factor c in pairing: multiple levels including 0 (random pairing) up to values > 0.5 (exact tested values not numerically listed).,Text explicitly introduces a correlation factor c that biases pairings and Figure 2 plots dynamics 'with different correlation factors as altruists take over' starting from 1% altruists.,0.9,False,Pure computer/replicator simulations; no human or animal participants.,1,False,Not an experiment with assigned treatments to human/animal subjects; it is a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; all results are from simulations.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe running 'computer simulations' and 'replicator simulations' on variations of a two-player prisoner's dilemma.,1,True,"Section provides explicit payoff equations and closed-form conditions (e.g., equality of payoffs at c = 0.5), so both analytical and simulation components are present.",0.9,2,Described repeatedly as a '2-player prisoner's dilemma' and 'symmetric two-person game'.,1,10,General simulation description: 'Simulations are run for a sufficient number of rounds (usually ten) that a state is reached...'. Correlation simulation is presented as an example of these runs.,0.7,1,"Strategies are discrete 'altruist' vs 'selfish', not continuous contribution levels; players choose one of two pure strategies each interaction.",0.9,N/A,Not a public goods game; it is a 2x2 prisoner's dilemma with discrete strategies and no continuous endowment allocation; also simulation without humans.,1,N/R,"MPCR is not defined because the game is a standard 2-player PD, not a linear public goods game; no multiplier or group size for contributions reported.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely programmed strategies.,1,N/A,No human interface; only simulated payoffs and reproduction.,1,N/A,No punishment or identity revelation in this correlation-only simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human participants; no display of rounds.,1,0,Only altruist and selfish strategies in a one-shot PD; no punishment or sanctioning options.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this simulation.,1,N/A,No punishment technology defined.,1,0,No mechanism for positive rewards beyond PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward technology defined.,1,N/R,"Game is specified only via payoff matrix (e.g., outcomes (2,2), (1,1), etc.); no interpretation as 'endowment' tokens.",0.9,N/R,"Outcome of interest is fraction of altruists vs selfish agents over generations, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,No contributions in token units; only binary strategies.,1,N/R,"Paper reports qualitative robustness and takeover dynamics, not efficiency ratios relative to maximum possible payoff.",0.7,N/R,"Plots and discussion track population shares, not aggregate payoffs per generation.",0.8,N/A,No human participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"All results are from computational replicator simulations, not empirical data.",1,"Two-strategy symmetric 2-player prisoner's dilemma with payoffs such that altruism is strictly dominated; payoffs illustrated using outcomes like (2,2) for mutual altruism and (1,1) for mutual selfishness; starting population n = 1000 with initially 1% altruists, discrete generations with proportional reproduction.","Summarizes PD description, population size, initial composition, and dynamics rules from 'Background', 'Correlation', and 'Notes on simulations' sections and Figure 2 caption.",0.96
10.1023_a_1020598804674.md,Group selection simulation – basic intrademic group selection (IGS),Section 'Group selection' and Figure 3 describe a model where the population is partitioned into endogamous groups of varying size and duration without extra group-level benefits.,0.95,Group size (k) and number of generations between group recombinations (T); k varied up to at least 6–7 (for T = 10) and higher when T smaller; T varied including up to 10.,"Text: 'two significant variables: the size of the groups, and the number of stages that these groups stay together...'; Figure 3 shows altruist offspring as a function of group size and recombination interval.",0.9,False,Described explicitly as a simulation of group selection dynamics.,1,False,No experimental manipulation on human or animal subjects.,1,False,Simulation-only study.,1,True,"Partitioning, reproduction, and recombination are implemented via computer simulation per 'Notes on simulations'.",1,False,"For this IGS configuration, results are presented graphically and descriptively; no separate closed-form derivation for group-size thresholds.",0.7,2,"Within each group, individuals 'are paired off against one another to play some variation on the one-shot prisoner's dilemma', which is 2-player.",1,10,General simulations run 'usually ten' rounds; in this section they emphasize cases with 'ten stages allowed between recombination' as key parameter value.,0.75,1,Only discrete strategies: altruist vs selfish in each PD encounter.,0.95,N/A,Not a contribution game and no human players.,1,N/R,Game is a PD with fixed payoffs; no MPCR parameter given.,1,N/A,No human communication; simulated genetic types.,1,N/A,No on-screen information; population state only in the code.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in basic IGS model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,0,Only altruist vs selfish reproduction dynamics; no sanctions.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No explicit rewards beyond PD payoffs and group partitioning.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model is defined via offspring counts from payoff matrices; no numeric endowment per interaction.,0.9,N/R,"Outcomes concern proportion of altruists in population, not contributions.",1,N/R,No continuous contributions.,1,N/R,"They discuss whether altruism persists, not aggregate efficiency ratios.",0.8,N/R,"Figure 3 reports counts of altruist offspring, not total group payoffs.",0.8,N/A,Simulation; no participants.,1,N/A,Simulation; no participants.,1,N/A,Simulation; no participants.,1,N/A,Simulation; no participants.,1,No human,"Entire study is computational, with no data collection from humans.",1,"Population size n = 1000, initial composition 50% altruists and 50% selfish agents. Population is partitioned into randomly constituted endogamous groups of size k. Within each group, individuals are randomly paired to play a one-shot prisoner's dilemma each generation; payoffs determine number of offspring of each genotype. Groups breed internally for T generations before all offspring are recombined into a single population; process repeats until a stable mix is reached. No additional group-level benefits (e.g., predators) are included in this basic condition.",Summarizes 'Group selection' and 'Notes on simulations' sections and the description preceding Figure 3.,0.96
10.1023_a_1020598804674.md,Group selection simulation – IGS with predator-avoidance group benefit,Section in 'Group selection' following Figure 3 and summarized in Figure 4 introduces added group-level benefit where groups with ≥2 altruists avoid predators.,0.96,Group size (k) and number of generations between recombinations (T) under predator-avoidance benefit; predator efficiency is very high (near-certain elimination of vulnerable groups).,"Text states: 'if groups contain two or more altruists, then they are able to avoid the dangers of a predator if not, then they are exposed...' and explores impacts on sustainable group size for T ≈ 10.",0.9,False,Explicitly described as an altered simulation model.,1,False,No real-world interventions; purely simulated.,1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,True,Computer-based IGS simulation with added predator rule.,1,False,"Behavior explored via simulation output (Figure 4), not formulae.",0.8,2,Underlying interactions are still pairwise PD games inside groups.,1,10,They highlight results 'with group sizes of nearly ten if the number of turns between group recombinations is at least ten generations'; consistent with general 'usually ten' rounds.,0.8,1,Binary altruist vs selfish strategies each encounter.,0.95,N/A,Not a contribution game and no human subjects.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined; PD payoffs plus predator effect.,1,N/A,No communication; simulated genotypes.,1,N/A,No interface to agents.,1,N/A,"No punishment; predator is exogenous environmental risk, not a strategic punisher.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,"Predator elimination is modeled as environmental mortality, not a chosen punishment action between players.",0.95,N/A,No strategic punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Group benefit is survival from predators, modeled as avoiding elimination, not as an explicit costly reward technology.",0.9,N/A,No explicit rewarding actions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game outcomes expressed as offspring numbers and survival; no separate token endowment.,0.9,N/R,Focus is on frequency of altruists as function of k and T under predator benefit.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions.,1,N/R,"Paper reports how large groups can be while sustaining altruism, not efficiency ratios.",0.8,N/R,Only the number of altruist offspring is reported (Figure 4) and qualitative survival of groups; no explicit payoff totals.,0.8,N/A,Simulation; no humans.,1,N/A,Simulation; no humans.,1,N/A,Simulation; no humans.,1,N/A,Simulation; no humans.,1,No human,Computational evolutionary model.,1,"Same intrademic group-selection structure as basic IGS: n = 1000, initially 50% altruists and 50% selfish agents, random grouping into endogamous groups of size k, internal pairing via one-shot PD for T generations, then recombination. Additional rule: groups containing at least two altruists obtain an extra group-level benefit by avoiding a highly efficient predator that can wipe out groups lacking this minimum number of altruists.",Synthesizes modifications described immediately before and after Figure 4.,0.96
10.1023_a_1020598804674.md,Imitation + group selection simulation – IGS with imitator agents,Section 'Imitation' initially modifies the IGS model by adding imitator agents who adopt the dominant group phenotype; results displayed in Figure 5.,0.96,"Imitation success probability p_switch (set to about 0.9 in reported results), group size (k), and number of generations between recombinations (T).","Text: 'we assume that...imitators...have a specified chance of changing their phenotype...'; later, 'When the chance that imitators will switch...is set at ninety percent...'; Figure 5 varies group size and T with fixed high imitation probability.",0.9,False,All dynamics are simulated; no actual organisms.,1,False,"It is a modeling exercise, not an empirical trial.",1,False,Not lab-based.,1,True,Described explicitly as 'our simulation' implementing imitation within IGS.,1,False,No analytical derivations for imitation dynamics; only simulated outputs (Figure 5).,0.8,2,Agents in groups are paired for 2-player PD interactions.,1,10,They discuss behavior with 'ten generations...between group recombinations'; consistent with standard ten-round simulation structure.,0.8,1,"Altruist vs selfish is a discrete strategy choice; imitators copy a phenotype, not a continuous contribution.",0.95,N/A,Not a contribution or endowment-allocation game; plus no humans.,1,N/R,"Game is PD, not linear public goods.",1,N/A,Agents are automata; no communication.,1,N/A,No displays to agents.,1,N/A,No punishment in this imitation-only IGS variant.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,Only imitation and selection; no sanctioning mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment defined.,1,0,No explicit rewarding strategy.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model defined via reproductive payoffs; not via token endowments.,0.9,N/R,"Key outcome is altruist share in offspring population, not contribution proportion.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,"They assess 'ability to generate stable altruistic populations', not efficiency.",0.8,N/R,Figure 5 shows counts of altruist offspring across group sizes; not total group payoffs.,0.8,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Pure simulation.,1,"Population of size n = 1000 consists initially of one-third altruists, one-third selfish maximizers, and one-third imitators. Agents are partitioned into random groups, as in IGS. Within each group, imitators have a high probability (e.g., 90%) of adopting the most common phenotype in the group (‘vertical’ and ‘horizontal’ transmission). They then play pairwise one-shot PD games; payoffs yield offspring with inherited genotypes and phenotypes subject to imitation-based switching. Groups remain endogamous for T generations then recombine.",Summarizes description in 'Imitation' section and Figure 5 caption.,0.96
10.1023_a_1020598804674.md,Imitation-only simulation – random pairing without groups,"Second imitation model described after Figure 5, where population is not partitioned into groups and imitators copy the phenotype of partners in random pairings; results in Figure 6.",0.96,"Imitation probability p_imit (probability that an imitator adopts opponent phenotype in each encounter), varying from low to at least ≈0.9.","Text: 'If the chance that imitators will convert to the phenotype of their opponent is approximately ninety percent or higher, altruists come to occupy a substantial position...'; Figure 6 plots offspring populations vs propensity for imitation.",0.95,False,All dynamics generated by computer simulation.,1,False,No experimental manipulation on real subjects.,1,False,Not lab-based.,1,True,Described explicitly as 'this simulation' and presented as a model.,1,False,"Results obtained from simulation sweeps over p_imit, not from analytical solution.",0.8,2,"Agents are 'paired off randomly' to play simultaneous PD, which is 2-player.",1,10,General simulation framework uses approximately ten rounds to approach a steady state; this imitation-only model is presented as another instance of such simulations.,0.7,1,Binary altruist vs selfish strategies each round; imitators choose one phenotype to copy.,0.95,N/A,"No contributions or endowments, and no human players.",1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,N/A,No communication channel; simulation only.,1,N/A,No displays to agents.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,0,Mechanism is imitation only; no sanctions.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No rewards aside from PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No explicit token or monetary endowment; payoffs map to offspring counts.,0.9,N/R,"Outcome is mix of altruists, selfish agents, and imitators across imitation propensities, not contribution fraction.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They plot offspring populations vs imitation propensity, not efficiency metrics.",0.8,N/R,No aggregate payoff totals are reported; only composition of offspring populations.,0.8,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,No empirical subjects.,1,"Population consists of altruists, selfish maximizers, and imitators. Agents are paired randomly each generation (no group partitioning) to play a simultaneous one-shot PD. In each encounter, imitators have probability p_imit of adopting their opponent's phenotype; when two imitators with different phenotypes meet, they converge to a single phenotype with equal odds of altruist vs selfish. Payoffs determine offspring counts and inherited phenotypes, modified by imitation.",Summarizes narrative preceding and following Figure 6.,0.96
10.1023_a_1020598804674.md,Punishment simulation – IGS with enforcer agents (conformity via direct punishment),First punishment model under 'Punishment' section adds 'enforcer' agents who punish nonconformists within groups; output summarized in Figure 7.,0.96,"Group size (k), number of generations between recombinations (T), enforcer imitation probability (≈0.9), and confrontation success probabilities (25% risk of enforcer death vs 50% chance of eliminating nonconformer).","Text details parameters for enforcers (25% death risk, 50% chance to eliminate opponent) and sets imitator-like switching to 90%, then explores how large k can be for T=10.",0.9,False,Entirely a simulated evolutionary model.,1,False,No real-world experimental subjects.,1,False,Modeling only.,1,True,"Described as 'our first model' where they 'introduce a new agent, an enforcer'; results in Figure 7.",1,False,No analytical solution; reliance on simulation output.,0.8,2,"Within partitioned groups, agents still interact via pairwise PD games.",1,10,They interpret Figure 7 at 'ten generations per recombination'; consistent with earlier models.,0.85,1,"Strategies are discrete phenotypes plus discrete punishment actions, not continuous contributions.",0.95,N/A,Not a contribution game with human endowments.,1,N/R,Standard PD payoffs; MPCR is not defined.,1,N/A,No communication; only programmed interactions.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,Simulation agents are not modeled with identity awareness; no mention of identity revelation.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human display of round number.,1,1,Enforcers 'attempt to eliminate those individuals who do not conform' and accept a probabilistic cost; this is a punishment mechanism.,1,N/R,They provide probabilities (25% chance of enforcer death per confrontation) but no numeric cost in payoff units; cannot convert to a per-unit cost.,0.7,N/R,Technology is specified probabilistically (50% success chance) but not as a linear payoff ratio per punishment unit.,0.7,0,No explicit rewards beyond PD payoffs and benefits of increased conformity.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No endowment notion; offsprings counts derive from payoffs and punishment outcomes.,0.9,N/R,Outcome of interest is proportion of altruists vs selfish across k and T under punishment; no contributions.,1,N/R,No contributions measured.,1,N/R,"Authors emphasize maximum group sizes sustaining altruism, not efficiency statistics.",0.8,N/R,Figure 7 shows altruist offspring counts; no explicit total payoff metrics.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Punishment is implemented among simulated strategies, not real subjects.",1,"Extension of IGS with three agent types: altruists, selfish maximizers, and enforcers. Enforcers initially behave like imitators, adopting the dominant phenotype in their group with high probability (~90%). Additionally, when encountering nonconforming phenotypes, enforcers may initiate confrontations: each confrontation carries a 25% chance that the enforcer is eliminated and a 50% chance that the nonconformer is eliminated. Groups are randomly formed, remain endogamous for T generations, and then recombine; payoffs from PD games plus survivorship under punishment determine offspring.",Summarizes the punishment mechanism description in 'Punishment' section and Figure 7 caption.,0.96
10.1023_a_1020598804674.md,Punishment simulation – IGS with ostracism (collective punishment via exclusion),"Second punishment model in 'Punishment' section introduces ostracism, where altruists banish selfish agents from groups; output in Figure 8.",0.97,"Ostracism success probability p_banish (e.g., 0.5 and 0.75), group size (k), and number of generations between recombinations (T, e.g., 10).",Text: 'if altruists make up forty percent or more of any individual group...they are given a specified chance of being able to banish selfish agents'; later discusses results for 50% and 75% success rates at T = 10 and varying k (up to 200).,0.95,False,Fully simulated mechanism; no human data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,True,Described as 'our next model' with simulation results displayed in Figure 8.,1,False,No closed-form analysis; relies on simulation for behavior under different banishment probabilities.,0.8,2,"Within each group, interactions remain pairwise PD games.",1,10,They report results 'when...fifty percent and ten generations are allowed between recombinations' and 'with ten generations still allowed'; consistent with framework.,0.9,1,"Agent behavior is discrete: altruist vs selfish; ostracism is a discrete event, not a continuous contribution.",0.95,N/A,Not a contribution game; no humans.,1,N/R,"Game type is PD, not public goods.",1,N/A,Simulation only; no communication between agents.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,No modeled identity display; only type-based interactions.,0.9,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,1,Ostracism is a form of punishment where nonconforming selfish agents are banished from groups and must interact only with others similarly ostracized.,1,N/R,They specify that 'we assign a twenty-five percent chance that a single altruist will be killed in each collective attempt' but do not quantify costs in payoff units per punishment token.,0.7,N/R,Ostracism is implemented probabilistically (50% or 75% success) rather than as a linear punishment-per-token mechanism.,0.7,0,Model includes only punitive ostracism; no explicit rewarding.,1,N/A,No rewards exist.,1,N/A,No rewards exist.,1,N/R,"Framework uses reproductive success and survival probabilities, not explicit token endowments.",0.9,N/R,Primary DV is prevalence of altruists in offspring population as function of p_banish and group size.,1,N/R,No contributions used.,1,N/R,"They discuss robustness of altruism and maximal group size sustaining it, not efficiency metrics.",0.8,N/R,Figure 8 shows altruist offspring counts vs group size; aggregate payoffs are not provided.,0.8,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,N/A,No real participants.,1,No human,Model is purely computational.,1,"Extension of IGS where groups are randomly formed and remain endogamous for T generations as agents play pairwise PD games. If altruists make up at least 40% of a group, they may collectively attempt to ostracize selfish agents with specified success probability p_banish (e.g., 0.5 or 0.75). Successful ostracism expels selfish individuals into a separate 'banished' group; during their banishment period they interact only with other ostracized agents. Each collective ostracism attempt carries a 25% chance that a single altruist is killed. After T generations, all groups, including banished clusters, are recombined into a single population; reproductive success and survival determine next-generation composition.",Paraphrases detailed mechanism description leading up to and including Figure 8.,0.97
