icipant.,1,0.43,43% of participants contributed their endowment to the group account; normalized contribution rate equals 0.43.,1,2,Contributors allocate full €2; defectors allocate 0; cooperator contribution amount is €2.,1,N/R,No efficiency statistic is reported for this condition.,0,N/R,Mean group earnings not provided separately for this condition.,0,Netherlands,Same participant pool of Dutch students.,1,N/R,No explicit ages given.,0.3,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0,University students,All participants were students at or connected to VU University.,1,Online,Same online setup used for all main conditions.,1,"One-shot six-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with a subsequent punishment stage; payoff table corresponds to IPD case where the external comparison group has €0 in its group account (higher absolute payoffs than low-payoff PD), but there is no actual competing group in this condition; punishment budget and cap based on PD earnings.",Summarizes described structure of the high-payoff single-group condition.,1
10.1007_s12110-012-9136-x.md,Main experiment – Intergroup competition IPD with punishment,Condition referred to as 'competition condition' or 'intergroup n-person prisoner's dilemma' with punishment; 42 participants completed this condition.,1,Game structure: intergroup n-person Prisoner’s Dilemma (IPD) with two groups; Intergroup competition: present via competition over €43.20 group bonus; Punishment option: present,Distinct from single-group PD by inclusion of direct competition between two groups over a fixed monetary bonus while permitting punishment.,1,True,Involves real participants making incentivized intergroup choices.,1,True,Controlled random assignment to an IPD with specified payoff rules.,1,False,"Conducted online, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,No computational agents; only human participants.,1,False,No separate analytical model as a condition.,1,6,Each group in the IPD consists of six participants; participants interact within their group for contribution and punishment decisions.,1,1,One contribution decision in the IPD followed by one punishment stage.,1,0,Each participant chooses to allocate their whole €2 endowment either to group account (cooperate) or to private account (defect); no partial allocation.,1,0,Endowment is initially private; participants choose whether to move it to group account.,0.9,0.6,In all games putting endowment in the group account pays €0.60 for each of the six group members from the group bonus; implies marginal per capita return 0.60.,1,0,No mention of communication; anonymity and online protocol preclude chat.,0.9,1,Punishment stage requires that participants know who in their own group cooperated or defected and corresponding payoffs to target punishment.,0.9,0,No indication that punished players learn who punished them; anonymity emphasized.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Participants briefed on two-stage structure; game is explicitly one-shot before punishment.,0.8,1,Same punishment stage as other main conditions.,1,0.1,Punishment is in €0.10 increments.,1,0.3,Each €0.10 reduces target’s payoff by €0.30.,1,0,Only reductions (punishments) are allowed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,2,Each participant in the IPD receives €2 endowment.,1,0.53,53% of participants in the competition condition contributed their endowment to the group account; normalized cooperation rate 0.53.,1,2,Cooperators allocate full €2 to the group account; defectors give 0.,1,N/R,No summary metric of realized vs maximum feasible efficiency is reported for this condition.,0,N/R,Average group payoffs for the IPD with punishment are not reported.,0,Netherlands,Same Dutch student sample as other conditions.,1,N/R,No explicit age statistics provided.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students,Participants are described as students from VU University and previous experiments.,1,Online,IPD condition conducted within the same online platform.,1,"Two groups of six compete over a €43.20 bonus. Each participant chooses to place €2 in a group or private account. The group’s share of the bonus depends on the difference in total group-account contributions between the two groups as given in Table 1; all members of a group share its bonus equally regardless of individual contribution decisions. Defectors always earn €2 more than cooperators in their own group. After observing within-group contribution outcomes and payoffs, players can spend part of their stage-1 earnings to reduce other ingroup members’ payoffs, at a 1:3 cost-to-impact ratio, up to the target’s stage-1 earnings.","Condenses description of IPD structure, group competition mechanism, and punishment constraints.",1
