10.1007_s00355-014-0841-8.md,Baseline treatment,"Paper describes three treatments: Baseline, Punishment, Reward; this row is the Baseline condition.",1,Sanctioning institution: no punishment or reward available,"Only institutional feature varied across treatments is availability/type of sanctioning (none, punishment, reward).",1,True,Field experiment with human recreational fishermen as participants.,1,True,Controlled framed field experiment with random assignment to treatments and fixed rules.,1,False,"Conducted at an outdoor recreational fishing facility, not in a laboratory.",1,False,No numerical or computer simulation described.,1,False,No formal analytical model; study is empirical.,1,4,“Participants are placed in groups of four. Group membership is fixed for the entire session.”,1,3,Part 1 has three periods of 30 minutes each in which the social dilemma and feedback occur; cooperation is measured there.,1,0,"Not a token-contribution choice; action is continuous fishing effort and catch up to 2 fish per period, not a binary all-or-nothing decision.",0.8,0,Common-pool framing: no initial endowment allocated to a public account; cooperation is refraining from catching fish.,0.8,N/R,"No linear payoff function with MPCR is specified; payoffs depend on fishing time and fish caught, not a standard VCM.",0,0,Subjects “are not allowed to talk with the other participants” in each period of part 1.,1,1,"At end of each period, “each fisherman is informed of the number of fish caught by each of the other members of his group in the period.”",1,0,"No punishment exists in this treatment; set to 0 as not applicable in practice, while punishment mechanism is absent.",1,0,No reward exists in this treatment; identities not relevant.,1,1,Instructions explicitly state that part 1 has three 30-minute periods and part 2 at most 2.5 hours; participants are informed about this structure.,1,0,Baseline treatment has no opportunity to punish or reward; only fishing decisions affect others via time reduction.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No reward opportunities in Baseline treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No per-period token endowment is defined; state is fishing time and stocking of fish.,0,N/R,They report catch and effort levels but do not normalize as a contribution fraction relative to a defined endowment.,0,N/R,They provide tests on catch and effort but no single aggregate numerical value per treatment is reported.,0,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (payoff over maximum payoff) is provided.,0,N/R,Group payoffs (fish plus euros) are not numerically summarized by treatment.,0,Netherlands,Experiment conducted at Dutch recreational fishing facility 'De Biestse Oevers'; authors are based at Dutch universities.,0.9,N/R,No age information for fishermen provided.,0,"Predominantly male (fishermen), but not numerically reported","Text consistently refers to “fishermen”; however, no explicit gender breakdown is given.",0.4,N/R,No education or student status information given; only that they are regular customers.,0,Field experiment,Authors describe it as a framed field experiment in a natural recreational fishing facility.,1,Common-pool framed social dilemma: 3 periods of 30 minutes (part 1) where each fish caught reduces each other group member’s part-2 fishing time by 10 minutes; maximum 2 fish per period per player in part 1; part 2 allows unconstrained fishing for up to 150 minutes with €2 per fish and restocking every 30 minutes based on prior catch; 16 fishermen per session across 4 groups.,Summarized from the detailed baseline design in section 2.2 and the instructions.,1
10.1007_s00355-014-0841-8.md,Punishment treatment,"Paper labels one treatment as the Punishment treatment, with peer punishment enabled.",1,"Sanctioning institution: costly peer punishment available at 1:3 cost-to-impact ratio, no rewards","Relative to Baseline, players can spend their own part-2 minutes to reduce others’ time by three times as many minutes; no reward option.",1,True,Same field experiment with human fishermen.,1,True,Controlled treatment in framed field experiment.,1,False,Conducted at outdoor facility rather than laboratory.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,No analytical model provided.,1,4,Same group size as baseline: groups of four.,1,3,Three 30-minute periods in part 1 where cooperation and punishment occur.,1,0,Players choose ongoing fishing effort and up to 3 punishment blocks per period; not a binary contribution choice.,0.8,0,Common-pool structure unchanged; no initial contribution to a public pot.,0.8,N/R,No MPCR given; payoff is fishing time and catch-based.,0,0,Participants are not allowed to talk with other participants in each part-1 period.,1,1,"At end of each period, they see each other group member’s catch; additionally they see totals of punishment blocks assigned and received.",1,0,Punishment is anonymous: information presented “in a format which ensured that the identity of the assigning subjects remained anonymous.”,1,0,No rewards exist in this treatment.,1,1,Instructions specify three periods in part 1 and maximum 2.5 hours in part 2; known to participants.,1,1,"“In the Punishment treatment, individuals have an opportunity to punish others after observing how many fish they catch.”",1,5,“Punishments…can be imposed in blocks of 5 min… Subjects cannot spend more than a total of three blocks of 5 min… in a single period.” Cost is minutes of own part-2 time per block.,1,15,"Each 5-minute block “reducing the recipient's part 2 fishing time by 15 min”; i.e., 1 unit of cost yields 3 units of impact (5→15 minutes).",1,0,Punishment treatment only allows punishment; reward option is in separate treatment.,1,N/A,No rewards in this treatment.,1,N/A,No rewards in this treatment.,1,N/R,No discrete per-round token endowment; state is fishing time and part-2 minutes.,0,N/R,They compare catch and effort to baseline but do not report normalized contribution fractions.,0,N/R,No single numeric summary of catch or effort per treatment is given.,0,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency is reported.,0,N/R,Aggregate payoffs per group or treatment are not numerically reported.,0,Netherlands,Same location and subject pool as baseline (Dutch fishing facility).,0.9,N/R,No age data reported.,0,"Predominantly male (fishermen), but not numerically reported",Participants referred to as fishermen; no explicit gender statistics.,0.4,N/R,No education information provided.,0,Field experiment,Punishment treatment is implemented in the same framed field setting at the fishing pond.,1,"Same common-pool structure as baseline (3×30min periods in part 1 with max 2 fish per period, each fish reducing others’ part-2 time by 10 minutes; part 2 up to 150 minutes with €2 per fish). Additional stage after each period: each subject can spend up to three 5-minute blocks of own part-2 time to reduce one chosen group member’s part-2 time by 15 minutes per block; punishment decisions are made simultaneously and anonymously; feedback includes total punishment blocks received and total available part-2 time.",Summarized from section 2.3 describing the punishment treatment.,1
10.1007_s00355-014-0841-8.md,Reward treatment,"Paper labels one treatment as the Reward treatment, with peer rewards enabled.",1,"Sanctioning institution: costly peer reward available at 1:3 cost-to-impact ratio, no punishments","Relative to Baseline, players can spend own part-2 minutes to increase others’ part-2 time by three times as many minutes; no punishment option.",1,True,Same field experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled framed field experiment with treatment variation.,1,False,"Run at recreational fishing facility, not a lab.",1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Empirical study only.,1,4,Groups of four in all treatments.,1,3,Three 30-minute periods in part 1 where cooperation and rewards occur.,1,0,Actions are continuous fishing effort and up to 3 reward blocks; not binary contribution.,0.8,0,Same common-pool structure; no default allocation to a public account.,0.8,N/R,No linear public-good MPCR defined; payoffs depend on time and fish caught.,0,0,Communication between participants is prohibited during part 1.,1,1,"As in punishment treatment, participants see others’ catches and total number of reward blocks sent/received.",1,0,No punishment exists in this treatment.,1,0,Reward assignments are anonymous; only totals reported.,1,1,Participants are instructed about the three 30-minute periods and maximum 2.5 hours in part 2.,1,0,Reward treatment only allows rewards; no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,"“In the Reward treatment, they can reward others… to increase the part 2 fishing time of a designated group member.”",1,5,“Rewards can be imposed in blocks of 5 min… Subjects cannot spend more than a total of three blocks of 5 min on punishments or rewards in a single period.” Cost is minutes of own part-2 time.,1,15,"Each reward block “increase[s] the part 2 fishing time of a designated group member, by three times as many minutes…in blocks of 5 min (each… increasing… by 15 min).”",1,N/R,No comparable numeric token endowment specified; payoff is minutes and fish.,0,N/R,Paper reports effort and catch comparisons but not normalized contribution fractions.,0,N/R,"No single numeric contribution (e.g., average catch or effort) per treatment is tabulated.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios reported.,0,N/R,Aggregate monetary/fish payoffs by group or treatment are not given.,0,Netherlands,Same fishing facility and recruitment context as other treatments.,0.9,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0,"Predominantly male (fishermen), but not numerically reported",Participants described as fishermen; no explicit gender breakdown.,0.4,N/R,No education information.,0,Field experiment,Same framed field experimental setting at the recreational fishing facility.,1,"Same common-pool game as baseline (3×30min periods in part 1 with max 2 fish per period; each fish caught reduces each other group member’s part-2 time by 10 minutes; part 2 allows up to 150 minutes of unconstrained fishing with €2 per fish and restocking every 30 minutes). Additional stage after each period: each subject can spend up to three 5-minute blocks of own part-2 time to increase a chosen group member’s part-2 time by 15 minutes per block; decisions are simultaneous and anonymous; subjects can exceed 150 minutes of part-2 time, receiving €3 coupons per unused 30 minutes if time would extend beyond facility closing.",Summarized from section 2.3 including the description of coupons for excess fishing time.,1
