10.1016_j.amc.2024.128646.md,"Simulation – institutional reward only (ω=1), varying τ, r, and δ","Section 3.3 examines the reward case with ω=1 and varying δ, r, and τ.",0.95,"Institutional reward strength δ ≥ 0 (per capita incentive budget) and commitment threshold τ ∈ {0,1,2,3,4,5} varied with the PGG multiplication factor r over a range (phase diagrams in Fig. 7 and cuts such as τ=2, δ=0.1, varying r, and τ=2, r=4.1, varying δ). Weight parameter fixed at ω=1 (pure reward).","Section 3.3 describes ω=1 and explores r×δ diagrams for multiple τ, plus specific 1D scans.",0.9,False,Again a computational evolutionary model.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Not conducted with human subjects.,1,True,Uses same Monte Carlo lattice simulation framework.,1,False,No separate analytical solution.,0.9,5,Group size G remains 5; thresholds τ go up to 5 in this section as well.,0.8,100000,The total simulation time is always t=10^5 MCS (Section 2).,0.9,1,Same binary cooperate/defect action space with cost c=1.,1,N/A,No initial monetary endowment to human participants.,1,N/R,MPCR=r/G but r is varied across simulations; no single value specified.,0.4,N/A,No communication by human participants.,1,N/R,Interface concepts are not defined in simulation terms.,0.3,N/A,No punishment is applied in this reward-only condition (ω=1).,1,N/A,Agents do not have observable identities in a user interface; reward is implemented as payoff changes.,1,N/R,Agents are not informed of remaining rounds.,0.3,0,"ω=1 corresponds to pure reward, no punishment component.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,Section 3.3 discusses institutional reward for commitment-compliant players (ACC and ACD) with ω=1.,1,N/A,"δ is a collective institutional budget parameter, not a per-coin cost to individual ‘rewarders’.",0.5,N/R,"Reward per rewarded player is ωGδ/n_com with ω=1, but no simple fixed per-unit technology is specified.",0.5,N/R,Only the cooperation cost c=1 is mentioned; no explicit endowment.,0.5,N/R,Cooperation density is shown in phase diagrams (Fig. 7 and Fig. 8) but exact numeric values are not reported.,0.4,N/R,No tabulated contribution amounts.,0.5,N/R,Efficiency is not quantified.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff is not numerically reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a computational simulation study.,1,"Same spatial PGG model as in the baseline and punishment conditions (100×100 lattice, von Neumann neighbourhood, eight commitment strategies, cost c=1, Fermi update with K=0.1, uniform initial strategy distribution). Institutional reward targets commitment-compliant strategies ACC and ACD when prior commitment is formed; the total institutional budget per group is Gδ, and with ω=1 the full budget is distributed equally among compliant players. Analyses focus on how reward interacts with commitment thresholds τ and PGG difficulty (r) to sustain cooperation.",Reward mechanism structure and which strategies are rewarded are specified in Section 2 (incentive term) and elaborated in Section 3.3.,1
10.1038_s41598-017-12742-4.md,"Simulation – ⟨k⟩=4, Regular topology, no breaking/creating connections","Condition described in Results/Fig. 3(a,c,e,g) as initial regular topology with ⟨k⟩=4 without both activities of breaking and creating connections.",0.96,"Initial topology: Regular; Average degree ⟨k⟩=4; Coevolutionary rewiring: disabled; Punishment coefficient r varied over [0,1]; Peer-punishment probability based on payoff difference.","Model section plus Fig. 3 description specifying ⟨k⟩=4, regular, and left-column panels as without both activities of breaking and creating connections; r is varied on x-axis and probabilistic peer-punishment is core mechanism.",0.9,False,Study is explicitly a computer simulation with N=1000 players as vertices in networks; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or field data; it is an in silico evolutionary model.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,"Described repeatedly as a computer simulation with parameters N, generations, etc.",1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations as the main method; results are numerical.,1,1000,Model section: “N=1000”.,1,300,“One simulation run lasts until the number of generations reaches 300.” One generation is one round of game plus updating and rewiring.,0.95,1,"Prisoner’s dilemma with binary strategies (cooperator or defector); no continuous contribution choice, so no all-or-nothing contribution structure as in public goods games; coded as 1 (any strategic state is allowed by imitation each generation).",0.4,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game; standard pairwise prisoner’s dilemma with no endowment allocation.,1,N/A,MPCR applies to public goods games; here payoffs follow a 2x2 PD matrix with parameter b.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents interact via defined payoff and update rules only.,1,N/A,No identities displayed; interactions are algorithmic; punishment is automatic based on payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the implemented model.,1,N/A,No participants viewing a UI; simulation is controlled by code.,1,1,Core of model is probabilistic peer-punishment based on payoff difference; coefficient r controls punishment.,1,N/R,Model specifies players “inflict the cost r P(i) on the opponent” but does not map this to a per-unit token cost; not expressed in normalized units.,0.3,N/R,Magnitude of punishment per cost unit is not parameterized separately (cost equals inflicted amount rP(i)); cannot express as simple tech ratio.,0.3,0,Study discusses future work on reward but does not implement reward in current simulations.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the simulated model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the simulated model.,1,N/R,"Prisoner’s dilemma payoffs are defined by matrix A, not by an explicit per-round endowment.",0.2,N/R,Outcome variables reported are number of cooperators/defectors and average payoff; no normalized contribution rate defined.,0.2,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect strategy; no explicit numeric contribution amount reported.,0.2,N/R,Only average payoff and counts of cooperators/defectors are plotted; no efficiency ratio to a theoretical maximum is reported.,0.2,N/R,"Average payoff of all players is shown, but not the raw total group payoff.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract nodes in a network.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly a computer simulation study without human subjects.,1,"Pairwise prisoner’s dilemma on a regular network with N=1000 nodes and average degree ⟨k⟩=4. Payoff matrix A=[[1,0],[b,0]] with 1<b≤2 and b=1.5 in simulations. Initial strategies: ~50% cooperators and 50% defectors randomly distributed. Cumulative payoff over neighbors, probabilistic peer-punishment applied when neighbors have higher payoff, then synchronous imitation of best neighbor strategy each generation. No coevolutionary breaking/creating of links in this condition; network topology remains static regular.","Summarized from Model section and description of parameter settings, specializing to regular topology and no rewiring for this condition.",0.95
