10.1371_journal.pone.0040325.md,Stage 2 – Weak institution – Low groups,"Stage 2 has two fixed-institution conditions (weak, strong). Low-type and high-type groups are analyzed separately; this row is weak institution for low groups.",0.96,"Institution strength fixed at weak levels (A=1, F=2); group type = low contributors",Condition described as weak institution with A=1 and F=2; this row pertains to low groups (non-cooperative types).,0.98,True,Laboratory experiment with 80 human participants in phase two.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of institutional parameters in a lab public goods game.,1,True,Conducted at a laboratory for economic experiments at a Swedish university.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only human subjects.,1,False,"Although a model is analyzed, this row refers to the empirical game, not the formal analysis.",0.9,4,Groups of four players in all PG games.,1,4,Weak institution condition lasted four rounds with fixed parameters.,1,1,"Players choose 'how much of her endowment to contribute'; continuous 0–10, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment starts fully in private tokens; they decide how many to invest.,1,0.5,Each token invested yields 0.5 tokens to each of 4 players; marginal per capita return is 0.5.,1,0,No mention of communication; subjects isolated in cubicles with only computer instructions.,0.9,1,After each round they saw total contribution and average payoff; summary info about group provided.,0.96,0,No indication that identities of punished players were revealed; only aggregate counts reported.,0.9,0,"Feedback mentions whether 'you' got a reward and counts of punished cheaters, not identities.",0.9,1,"Instructions specify fixed numbers of rounds for each stage; standard lab practice is to display remaining rounds, and subjects were told when parts would end.",0.7,1,Cheaters monitored are 'automatically punished by a fine F'; here F=2.,1,0,Fine is automatic and costless to others; only the fined player loses F tokens.,1,2,Fi