10.1371_journal.pone.0040325.md,Stage 1 – Evolving institution – Low groups,Stage 1 evolving-institution condition with voting; this row for low-type groups.,0.96,"Institutional parameters A and F evolve via within-group voting starting from weak levels (A=1, F=2); group type = low contributors",Stage 1 described as evolving institution with voting on A and F; starting parameters given; this row is for low groups.,0.98,True,Human subjects played evolving regulated PG game.,1,True,Institutional parameter evolution is experimentally controlled through voting rules.,1,True,Same physical laboratory as other stages.,1,False,"Data from humans, not simulations.",1,False,Row describes empirical evolving-institution condition.,0.9,4,Groups of four.,1,16,Evolving institution condition lasted 16 rounds with voting after every second round.,1,1,Contribution choice is any amount between 0 and 10.,1,0,"Initial endowment 10 tokens each round, fully private at start.",1,0.5,Public goods technology unchanged: 1 token yields 0.5 to each player.,1,0,No free-form communication described; interaction only through voting interface and feedback.,0.9,1,"Feedback each round included total contribution, average payoff, counts of cheaters and punished, and number who monitored.",1,0,Only aggregate numbers of cheaters and punished cheaters shown; no identities.,0.9,0,Feedback indicates whether 'you' earned a reward but not which others did.,0.9,1,Design specifies 16 rounds with voting every second round; participants likely knew progression and/or rounds remaining.,0.7,1,Regulated PG game includes fines F for cheaters if detected; F evolves via voting.,1,0,Fines F are automatic collective sanctions costing only fined players; no per-punishment cost to others.,1,4,Fine F ranges over time; final-round mean F in low groups is 4.0 (SD 2.2). Using group mean as characteristic value.,0.7,1,Monitoring reward R=3 present and fixed.,1,0,Rewards are funded from project pot; no direct additional cost per reward.,0.85,3,Reward level R fixed at 3 tokens for all evolving-institution rounds.,1,10,"Each round, each agent receives 10 tokens.",1,0.379,Average contribution per member in evolving condition low groups (all periods) is 3.79; 3.79/10=0.379.,0.98,3.79,Table 2: contributions for evolving institution low groups averaged over all periods = 3.79.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric or maximum feasible payoff under varying A and F reported; parameters change over time.,0,12.85,Table 2: payoff in evolving institution low groups averaged over all periods = 12.85 tokens.,1,Sweden,Experiment site and recruitment in Sweden.,0.9,Mean 24 years; range N/R,Same sample characteristics.,0.9,"54 women, 62 men overall; per-condition breakdown N/R",No condition-specific gender distribution.,0.75,College students from miscellaneous study programs,Uniform recruitment pool.,0.95,On site,Same lab-based experimental environment.,1,"Regulated public goods game with N=4, endowment 10, multiplier M=2, acceptance threshold A and fine F jointly defining institution. Start at A=1, F=2; every two rounds, group members vote to increase, decrease, or keep A and F (separate votes). Monitoring cost C=1, reward R=3 fixed. Low groups end with mean F≈4 and A≈6 after seven votes.",Summarizes dynamic institutional rules and observed final parameter levels in low groups.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0040325.md,Stage 1 – Evolving institution – High groups,Evolving-institution Stage 1 for high-type groups.,0.96,"Institutional parameters A and F evolve via within-group voting starting from A=1, F=2; group type = high contributors",Same evolving condition with voting; this row for high groups.,0.98,True,Condition involves human play with evolving rules.,1,True,Institutional dynamics experimentally controlled.,1,True,On-site experimental lab.,1,False,Data from human subjects only.,1,False,Empirical condition separate from formal analysis.,0.9,4,Groups have four members.,1,16,Evolving institution played for 16 rounds.,1,1,Contributions can vary continuously from 0 to 10.,1,0,All 10 tokens are initially in private possession.,1,0.5,Each contributed token yields 0.5 tokens to each of 4 players.,1,0,No communication mechanism aside from voting screens.,0.9,1,"After each round, participants saw total contributions, average payoff, number of cheaters, number punished, and number monitoring.",1,0,Only aggregate counts of cheaters and punished cheaters provided; no IDs.,0.9,0,Own reward shown; others' identities not shown.,0.9,1,Subjects knew there were 16 rounds and votes every second round; likely saw or could infer remaining rounds.,0.7,1,"Fines F for cheaters present, with F evolving via votes.",1,0,Punishments cost only the punished; no marginal cost to others.,1,7.4,Average final fine F in high groups is reported as M=7.4 (SD 1.8); using this final value to characterize punishment technology.,0.7,1,Monitoring reward R=3 always available in regulated PG game.,1,0,Rewards pulled from the project pot; no per-reward cost to others.,0.85,3,Reward parameter R fixed at 3 tokens.,1,10,Each player receives 10 tokens at start of each round.,1,0.636,Average contribution per member in evolving condition high groups (all periods) is 6.36; 6.36/10=0.636.,0.98,6.36,Table 2: contributions for evolving institution high groups averaged over all periods = 6.36.,1,N/R,Efficiency cannot be determined precisely as A and F vary across time; no summary efficiency reported.,0,15.78,Table 2: payoff for evolving institution high groups averaged over all periods = 15.78.,1,Sweden,Same sample and location.,0.9,Mean 24 years; range N/R,Overall reported mean applies.,0.9,"54 women, 62 men overall; per-condition breakdown N/R",Gender not reported by condition.,0.75,College students from miscellaneous study programs,Same educational background across conditions.,0.95,On site,In-lab experiment.,1,"Regulated PG game, N=4, endowment 10, M=2. Start with weak institution A=1, F=2. Every two rounds, subjects vote separately on A and F with three options: increase by 1, decrease by 1, keep. Successful monitors get R=3, monitoring costs C=1. High groups generally increase both A and F over time; final average parameters A≈7.2, F≈7.4.",Summarizes evolving rules and observed institutional trajectory for high groups.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.95.052309.md,Untaxed three-species cyclic public goods game (baseline replicator dynamics),"Paper’s main model in Sec. III describes a three-species cyclic public goods game without taxation; numerical examples (e.g., Fig. 1, 2) are parameterizations of this condition.",1,"Public good returns r_A, r_B, r_C and common cost c; example parameter sets include (r_A=r_B=r_C=1.1, c=2) and (r_A=1.8, r_B=2.2, r_C=1.5, c=1). No punishment/tax.","Model equations (untaxed) depend on r_A, r_B, r_C, c; examples are explicitly given around Fig. 1.",1,False,Purely theoretical/ODE model; no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral or field experiment; only mathematical analysis and numerical phase portraits.,1,False,Not a lab or online experiment; just theory/simulation.,1,True,"Population dynamics simulated numerically via replicator ODEs (phase portraits, basins of attraction).",1,True,"Derives fixed points, eigenvalues, stability conditions, KKT conditions analytically.",1,3,"Three species/strategies U, V, W interacting in a cyclic public goods structure; effectively a 3-strategy game.",1,1,"Continuous-time replicator dynamics model a single indefinitely repeated evolutionary process, not discrete rounds; encoded as 1 for a single continuous interaction structure.",0.6,1,"Each species deterministically contributes to one good and freeloads on another; there is no individual-level contribution decision space, so flexible vs all-or-nothing is not meaningfully specified. Defaulted to 1 as contributions are effectively fixed, not binary-choice per round.",0.3,N/A,No individual endowments or discrete contribution decisions; purely population-level payoffs.,1,N/R,"Standard PGG notation N, r, c mentioned, but group size N is not fixed in the cyclic-species model; MPCR not explicitly defined nor computable for a per-individual stage game here.",0.3,N/A,No human participants or communication mechanism.,1,N/A,No interface or information display; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment or IDs; only fitness/payoff functions.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or user interface.,1,0,Baseline model has no tax/penalty term (no β parameters); only payoffs from three public goods.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No additional rewards beyond normal public-good returns.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Individual-level endowments are not defined in the cyclic three-species model; only contribution cost c appears.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes are species frequencies and population growth, not contribution fractions of endowment.",0.1,N/R,Model is continuous-time at population level; no per-round token contributions reported.,0.1,N/R,"They analyze population growth rate and stability, not efficiency defined as payoff relative to maximum possible.",0.2,N/R,Total population growth Ṁ and average payoff p̄ are discussed qualitatively; no numeric payoff levels are tabulated as outcome variables.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely mathematical/simulation study without subjects.,1,"Three species (A, B, C); each invests cost c in one public good (A, B, or C) with return r_A, r_B, r_C and freeloads on another good in a cyclic manner (A→B→C→A). Dynamics follow continuous-time replicator equations for species frequencies and total population size.","Summarized from Sec. III, equations (12)-(20) and surrounding description.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.95.052309.md,Taxed three-species cyclic public goods game with flat penalty tax (stabilization via β),"Section IV introduces an externally imposed penalty tax on each species and studies its effect; flat, symmetric tax β with examples (e.g., β=1.5 or 2, r=3, c=1) is a distinct treatment condition vs untaxed baseline.",1,"Penalty tax rates β_A, β_B, β_C (often symmetric β) applied proportional to species’ population shares; public good returns r_A, r_B, r_C and common cost c. Example symmetric cases: r_A=r_B=r_C=3, c=1 with β=1.5 (center), β=2 (stable).",Taxed dynamics defined via modified payoffs p̃_i = p_i − β_i * share; symmetric and asymmetric β scenarios discussed in Sec. IV; Corollary IV.2 focuses on symmetric β and r.,1,False,Still purely theoretical and simulation-based; no empirical subjects.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only mathematical and numerical analyses.,1,False,Not conducted in a physical or online lab; it is a model.,1,True,They simulate trajectories and phase portraits of the taxed replicator system (Fig. 3).,1,True,"Derive conditions on det(Ã) and β for stability, minimal growth, and collapse analytically.",1,3,"Same three-species cyclic public goods structure (U, V, W).",1,1,Continuous-time replicator dynamics with taxation; not structured as discrete rounds.,0.6,1,"As in untaxed model, individuals in each species are fixed contributors to one good and freeloaders on another; no discrete contribution decision level to categorize.",0.3,N/A,No per-round endowment allocation decision modeled.,1,N/R,Taxed model still uses r and c parameters without specifying a finite group size N in the cyclic setting; MPCR not explicitly defined or derivable in standard way.,0.3,N/A,No participants or communication.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,"Tax is an external penalty, not peer punishment with identities.",1,N/A,No explicit reward/punisher identities.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,1,A penalty tax proportional to population share is imposed on each species’ payoff (Sec. IV).,1,N/R,"Tax is modeled as a direct subtraction from growth rate (β_i * share), not as a cost per token of punishment in a standard PGG sense; no scalar cost per punishment unit specified.",0.4,N/R,No discrete mapping from punishment expenditure to loss for target; taxation directly reduces own growth.,0.4,0,Only penalties (taxes) are added; no added rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Individual endowments are not defined; only cost parameter c appears.,0.2,N/R,"Main outcomes are stability of interior fixed point, population growth, and coexistence; contributions per agent are not an outcome variable.",0.1,N/R,No per-round contribution quantities are reported.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to maximal) is not calculated; instead they analyze minimal growth and collapse thresholds.,0.2,N/R,"Group payoff is implicit via average payoff p̄ and growth z, but no numeric group payoff outcomes are tabulated.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Mathematical analysis and simulations only.,1,"Same three-species cyclic public goods structure as baseline, but each species’ payoff p_i is reduced by a tax term β_i times its population share (or common β in symmetric case). The tax alters the replicator dynamics and can make the interior coexistence equilibrium asymptotically stable, neutrally stable, or unstable depending on det(Ã) and β relative to r and c. In symmetric case c_A=c_B=c_C=c, β_A=β_B=β_C=β, r_A=r_B=r_C=r, coexistence u=v=w=1/3 is stable if β>rc/2, unstable if β<rc/2, and a center if β=rc/2.","Summarized from Sec. IV and Corollary IV.2, including Proposition IV.1 and IV.3.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.95.052309.md,Optimal control of tax rate in symmetric three-species cyclic public goods game (quasilinearized control problem),Section V treats β as a time-varying control variable γ(t)=β(t)−cr/2 in an optimal control framework to drive the system to the mixed-species equilibrium with minimal tax effort; this is a distinct simulation/analytical condition.,1,"Time-varying tax control γ(t) (equivalently β(t)) in symmetric case with c_A=c_B=c_C=c, r_A=r_B=r_C=r; weight parameter k in cost of control; time horizon t_f and initial state z_0. Constraint γ(t)≥0 (β≥cr/2).","Optimal control problem defines β (or γ) as the control variable, with cost functional including deviation from fixed point and quadratic control cost (Sec. V).",1,False,No behavioral data; pure optimal control analysis with numerical ODE solutions.,1,False,"This is a theoretical control design, not an applied experiment.",1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,True,They numerically solve the coupled state-adjoint ODEs and the derived second-order ODE for γ(t) (Figs. 4–6).,1,True,"Derive Hamiltonian, Euler-Lagrange conditions, quasilinearization, and a necessary ODE for γ(t) analytically.",1,3,"Control problem is built on same three-species model (u, v, w).",1,1,"Continuous-time optimal control over [0, t_f]; not discrete rounds.",0.7,1,Underlying game remains the same cyclic contribution structure; no new decision space at individual level.,0.3,N/A,No endowment allocation modeled.,1,N/R,Same as taxed game; MPCR not explicitly specified in this control formulation.,0.3,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No UI.,1,N/A,"Tax is a global control variable, not peer-to-peer punishment.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No experiment with participants.,1,1,"Tax continues to act as an external penalty affecting payoffs, now controlled optimally over time.",1,N/R,"Control cost is modeled via quadratic term (k β^2 or k γ^2) in the objective, but there is no micro-level punishment cost per unit in game-theoretic sense.",0.5,N/R,Tax directly subtracts from growth; no unit mapping of punishment to loss independent of control cost.,0.4,0,No rewarding scheme added in control problem.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Underlying model does not specify individual endowments.,0.2,N/R,Performance criteria are distance of state from mixed equilibrium and integrated tax cost; no contribution rates per agent.,0.1,N/R,No per-round contributions computed.,0.1,N/R,The objective mixes control cost and squared distance from equilibrium; efficiency relative to max payoff is not reported.,0.2,N/R,"Population payoff appears only indirectly via dynamics; outcome reported is behavior of state trajectories and control function, not aggregate payoff levels.",0.3,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Optimal control and ODE simulations only.,1,"Uses symmetric taxed three-species cyclic public goods game as the plant. Control problem: minimize integral over [0, t_f] of 0.5||z−z*||^2 + 0.5 k β^2 subject to replicator dynamics with tax β(t), where z=(u,v,w) and z* is mixed equilibrium (1/3,1/3,1/3). After substitution β=γ+cr/2, derive Hamiltonian, adjoint dynamics, and quasilinearized dynamics leading to a second-order ODE for γ(t): k γ¨ − (2/9) γ (γ^2 + C) = 0 with appropriate boundary conditions.","Summarized from Sec. V including definition of A(β), F, G, and Proposition V.4.",1
10.5751_es-07532-200228.md,Baseline – all groups (no institution),Baseline phase with no institutional interventions run for all 32 groups before treatments.,1,"Institutional condition: baseline without communication, traditional authority, punishment or institutional choice (no explicit intervention available).","Independent variable is the institutional setting; in this phase, no treatment was available.",1,True,"Human farmers from villages in Punjab, Pakistan played the irrigation game.",1,True,Controlled artefactual field experiment with randomized assignment to groups and fixed game structure.,1,False,"Conducted in village gathering places (dera) in the field, not in a laboratory.",1,False,"This condition used only human subjects, no computer-only simulation.",1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical derivations used as a stand‑alone method for this condition.,1,5,Each group consisted of five members.,1,7,Baseline phase always took seven rounds.,1,1,Players could invest any integer number of tokens up to 10 and choose any multiple of 5 minutes for extraction; not all‑or‑nothing.,1,0,Initial endowment of 10 tokens was private; players chose how much to invest into the CPR.,1,N/R,Only nonlinear (sigmoidal) production and payoff tables reported; no constant MPCR defined.,0.4,0,During baseline rounds players were not allowed to talk to each other.,1,1,"At end of each round, total earnings and extractions of each player were announced publicly.",1,0,No punishment mechanism existed in baseline.,1,0,No monetary reward mechanism existed in any phase.,1,0,Players were not aware of the number of rounds in each phase.,1,0,"Punishment and other treatments were only available in the treatment phase, not in baseline.",1,N/A,No punishment option existed in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment option existed in baseline.,1,0,No costly rewarding mechanism is described; only sanctions and communication.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,10,"At the start of each round, participants were provided with the same initial endowment of 10 tokens.",1,0.73,Average investment 7.30 tokens out of 10 => 7.30/10 = 0.73.,1,7.3,Average individual investment in baseline was 7.30 tokens.,1,0.74,"Average group earnings 77.01 tokens, which is reported as 74% of the social optimum.",1,77.01,Average group earnings for baseline rounds was 77.01 tokens.,1,Pakistan,"Experiments conducted with Punjabi farmers in Sargodha district, Punjab, Pakistan.",1,Mean 41 years (SD 14.4); adults only.,Table 1 reports average age; only adult farmers participated.,1,100% male,All participants were male.,1,63% received secondary education; mix from no formal schooling to university.,Table 1 and Appendix 1 detail education distribution.,1,Field experiment,Described as artefactual field experiments conducted in villages (dera).,1,Asymmetric irrigation CPR with 5 fixed positions (1=upstream head-user to 5=downstream tail-user). Two-stage decision each round: (1) invest 0–10 tokens in CPR; nonlinear production function yields 0–200 water minutes for group; (2) sequential extraction in multiples of 5 minutes (0–50) with S-shaped payoff from water; total payoff = uninvested tokens + tokens from water; individual extraction decisions and earnings publicly revealed each round.,Summary of the irrigation game rules and payoff functions in the baseline phase.,1
10.5751_es-07532-200228.md,Communication (C) – treatment phase,Groups assigned to communication treatment could buy time to talk during treatment rounds.,1,Institutional condition: face-to-face communication treatment available after extraction; individuals can buy 2 minutes of cheap talk with group members at cost 1 token per session.,Treatment C is defined as communication; only this institutional option differs from other conditions.,1,True,Same human farmer participants as baseline.,1,True,Controlled artefactual field experiment with random assignment of groups to treatments.,1,False,Conducted in village gathering places (field setting).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation for this condition.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,5,Same 5-player groups persisted into treatment phase.,1,9.5,Treatment phase varied randomly from 8 to 11 rounds; mean of the range used as best available numeric summary.,0.4,1,Same decision structure as baseline: continuous (discrete) choice of investment and extraction levels.,1,0,Endowment started in private account each round.,1,N/R,Nonlinear production; no constant MPCR reported.,0.4,1,"Under C treatment, players could talk to each other by purchasing communication.",1,1,Earnings and extractions of each player announced publicly every round in all phases.,1,0,No monetary punishment technology exists in pure C treatment; only communication.,1,0,No explicit reward option in any treatment.,1,0,Players were not informed about number of rounds in each phase.,1,0,"C treatment only allows communication, not monetary fines.",1,N/A,No punishment technology present.,1,N/A,No punishment technology present.,1,0,No separate positive reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward technology present.,1,N/A,No reward technology present.,1,10,Endowment per round remained 10 tokens.,1,0.73,Text reports treatment-phase investments did not significantly differ from baseline; use baseline average 7.30/10.,0.6,7.3,No separate treatment means reported; regression and non-significant tests suggest similar to baseline.,0.6,N/R,No separate efficiency or percentage-of-optimum reported by treatment.,0.2,N/R,Only baseline group earnings and pooled regression effects provided; no numeric means by treatment.,0.2,Pakistan,Same Punjabi farmer sample.,1,Mean 41 years (SD 14.4); adults only.,Same participants as baseline.,1,100% male,Same participants as baseline.,1,63% received secondary education; mix from no formal schooling to university.,Same participants as baseline.,1,Field experiment,Same artefactual field setting in villages.,1,"Same irrigation game as baseline, plus post-extraction decision to optionally buy a 2–3 minute cheap-talk session with any subset of group members at cost 1 token per session. Communication content unrestricted but nonbinding. Social information on individual extraction and earnings remained publicly disclosed.",Summarizes added communication stage and its cost while keeping core game constant.,1
10.5751_es-07532-200228.md,Traditional authority (TA) – treatment phase,Groups assigned to TA treatment had an elder observing and available for conflict resolution sessions.,1,Institutional condition: traditional authority treatment available; players may call an elder to mediate and impose informal social sanctions at cost 1 token per targeted player.,TA treatment is defined by availability of a traditional leader/panchayat-style mediation mechanism.,1,True,Same human farmer sample.,1,True,Part of the controlled artefactual field experiment.,1,False,"Conducted in field sites, not a conventional lab.",1,False,No purely simulated agents.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,5,"Each treatment group had 5 players, plus an elder who did not play the game.",1,9.5,Treatment phase length 8–11 rounds; use midpoint as numeric approximation.,0.4,1,Same investment and extraction decision spaces as baseline.,1,0,Endowment remained fully private before decisions.,1,N/R,Same nonlinear production function; MPCR not given.,0.4,1,TA sessions involve mediated group discussion guided by elder; functional communication channel.,1,1,"Public disclosure of each player's investment, extraction, and earnings continued.",1,1,"When TA is invoked, the elder and group discuss specific targeted players; their identities and actions are public.",1,0,No explicit reward technology; only informal social sanctions and discussion.,1,0,Total or remaining rounds were not revealed.,1,1,"TA enables informal social sanctions such as disapproval, embarrassment, demands for apology; counted as punishment mechanism.",1,1,Cost of invoking TA against a single player was 1 token; cost shared if multiple initiators targeted same player.,1,0,Informal sanctions; no direct deterministic fine in tokens per token spent. Encoded as 0 to indicate non-monetary payoff change per cost unit.,0.4,0,Only sanctions and guidance; no positive token transfers.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding institution.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding institution.,1,10,Same per-round endowment.,1,0.73,Authors report no significant differences in group investments between treatments; use baseline value.,0.6,7.3,No treatment-specific means reported; regression suggests stability relative to baseline.,0.6,N/R,No numeric efficiency for TA condition alone provided.,0.2,N/R,"Only relative statements (slightly higher provision and earnings, but not significant) for TA versus others.",0.2,Pakistan,Same farmer sample.,1,Mean 41 years (SD 14.4); adults only.,Same participants across conditions.,1,100% male,Same participants across conditions.,1,63% received secondary education; mix from no school to university.,Same sample as baseline.,1,Field experiment,Same artefactual field setting.,1,"Core irrigation game unchanged. After extraction, any player can pay 1 token to call a traditional authority (elder) against specific player(s). All players attend the session. Elder moderates discussion, can express disapproval, embarrass, demand apologies, or encourage commitments. Actions and recommendations are nonbinding and purely social, but information about players’ behavior and being targeted is public.",Describes TA mechanism and its cost in the context of the base game.,1
10.5751_es-07532-200228.md,External punishment (P) – treatment phase,Groups assigned to punishment treatment could request third‑party sanctions with probabilistic fines.,1,Institutional condition: external sanctioning (punishment) treatment available; any player can pay 1 token per target to request a probabilistic fine of 4 tokens on that target (2/3 probability).,P treatment is defined by introduction of an external monetary punishment mechanism.,1,True,Same human farmer participants.,1,True,Condition within artefactual field experiment with randomized group assignment.,1,False,Conducted in field setting (dera).,1,False,No non-human simulation.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,5,Each punishment group had 5 players.,1,9.5,Treatment phase length varied 8–11 rounds; use midpoint.,0.4,1,Players could choose any investment level (0–10) and extraction minutes in steps of 5 (0–50).,1,0,Endowment purely private at start of each round.,1,N/R,Nonlinear production; no MPCR reported.,0.4,0,"P treatment provides only punishment, no explicit communication opportunity beyond what already exists via public information.",1,1,Public announcement of each player's extraction and earnings each round continued.,1,1,Punishment is targeted at named players and handled publicly by experimenter; everyone sees who is punished.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Number of remaining rounds not shown.,1,1,External sanctioning rule P was available.,1,1,"Cost of invoking punishment against a single player was 1 token, shareable among initiators.",1,4,"If punishment occurs, targeted player pays fixed fine of 4 tokens, independent of initiator's cost (1 token). Encoded as 4 tokens lost per punishment event.",0.8,0,No reward technology was implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Endowment unchanged.,1,0.73,Regression and descriptive results indicate no significant change in investment level compared to baseline.,0.6,7.3,No distinct mean reported; no significant treatment effect on investments.,0.6,N/R,"Only direction of welfare change (decrease) reported, not exact proportion of social optimum.",0.2,N/R,Regression shows negative effect of P on earnings; numeric mean by treatment not reported.,0.2,Pakistan,Same sample.,1,Mean 41 years (SD 14.4); adults only.,Same sample.,1,100% male,Same sample.,1,63% received secondary education; mix from no formal schooling to university.,Same sample.,1,Field experiment,Same artefactual field setting.,1,"Irrigation game as in baseline, plus post-extraction option: a player can pay 1 token to request punishment of any number of other players, 1 token per target (costs shareable if multiple initiators target same player). For each target, there is a 2/3 probability that the target pays a fine of 4 tokens (and 1/3 probability of no fine), independent of their behavior. Fines are paid to the experimenter (not to initiators). Tail-users initiated most punishments, mainly targeting high extractors but sometimes low extractors as well.",Summarizes punishment mechanism details and its behavioral context.,1
10.5751_es-07532-200228.md,Institutional choice (IC) – treatment phase,"Groups in IC condition could choose among communication, traditional authority, and punishment in each treatment round.",1,"Institutional condition: institutional choice; each round, each player may individually select one of three interventions (communication, traditional authority, or punishment) against any subset of players, bearing the corresponding cost.","IC treatment is explicitly defined as allowing choice between C, TA, and P each round.",1,True,Same human farmers participated in IC groups.,1,True,One randomized treatment arm in the artefactual field experiment.,1,False,Conducted in field setting.,1,False,No non-human simulation.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,5,Each IC group consisted of 5 players plus an observing elder.,1,9.5,Treatment phase varied from 8 to 11 rounds; midpoint used.,0.4,1,Same investment and extraction options as in baseline.,1,0,All tokens initially private.,1,N/R,Production function unchanged; no MPCR reported.,0.4,1,IC allows use of C; groups could and did choose communication as one option.,1,1,"Investments, extractions, and earnings public in all rounds.",1,1,"When punishment is selected, targeted players are explicitly identified and processed by experimenter in front of group.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Number of rounds not disclosed.,1,1,Punishment was one of the selectable institutions under IC.,1,1,Same cost as P treatment: 1 token per target for punishment option; also 1 token for C and TA.,1,4,"When P is chosen and effective, target pays fine of 4 tokens with 2/3 probability, same as in P treatment.",0.8,0,No positive reward institution included in IC options.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Endowment unchanged.,1,0.73,Mann-Whitney tests show no significant differences in investments between IC and other treatments; use baseline rate.,0.6,7.3,No IC-specific mean provided; regression shows no significant change vs baseline.,0.6,N/R,No numeric efficiency specifically for IC condition.,0.2,N/R,Regression shows no significant group earnings effect of IC vs baseline; no numeric mean.,0.2,Pakistan,Same farmer population.,1,Mean 41 years (SD 14.4); adults only.,Same sample.,1,100% male,Same sample.,1,63% received secondary education; mix from no formal schooling to university.,Same sample.,1,Field experiment,Same artefactual field context.,1,"Core irrigation game plus individual institutional choice stage after extraction: each player can opt for Communication (C), Traditional Authority (TA), or Punishment (P) against any selected players, paying 1 token per treatment session/target (with cost-sharing among initiators targeting same player). C grants cheap talk; TA invokes elder-led mediation and informal social sanctions; P applies a probabilistic fine of 4 tokens (2/3 chance). Multiple different treatments can be used against different players in the same round, but not more than one treatment against the same player in a round.",Summarizes IC design combining three institutional mechanisms and their costs.,1
10.1007_s11211-017-0300-7.md,Non-punishment condition (standard public good game without punishment),Paper states there were two conditions: with (punishment) and without punishment; this refers to the no-punishment condition.,1,"Presence of punishment option: No punishment; fixed groups, 10 rounds, same payoff structure as Fehr & Gächter (2002)",Independent variable varied across conditions is availability of a costly punishment stage; here it is absent.,1,True,Human participants in a lab public good game.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions and controlled payoff structure in a lab setting.,1,True,Conducted at the Cologne Laboratory for Economic Research (CLER).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all decisions by human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model only; empirical data from games.,1,4,“Four participants interacted with each other in 47 fixed groups over ten rounds.”,1,10,Same quote specifies interaction over ten rounds.,1,1,Participants could contribute any integer 0–20 tokens; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment of 20 tokens given each round; subjects decide how much to contribute. Endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,"Income from project: 0.4 × total group contribution; each token yields 0.4 to each player, so MPCR = 0.4.",1,0,Instructions mention only on-screen decisions and feedback; no communication allowed.,0.9,1,After each round participants saw total contribution of all four members and their own return; this is a summary of others’ contributions/returns.,1,0,No punishment stage in this condition; hence no identities for punishment decisions.,1,0,No reward mechanism described; only public good contribution decisions.,1,1,Period number displayed on screens and subjects told there are ten periods; they know horizon.,1,0,Described explicitly as the non-punishment condition: only first-stage contribution decision.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,No positive reward or bonus allocation by participants described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"“In each round, participants received 20 monetary units… and had to decide how much to invest.”",1,0.459,Average contribution was 9.18 of 20 tokens in the non-punishment setting; 9.18/20 ≈ 0.459.,1,9.18,"Results section: “On average, participants contributed 9.18 of 20 tokens in the non-punishment setting.”",1,N/R,No explicit or computable measure of realized efficiency (realized group payoff vs. maximum) reported.,0,N/R,No aggregate group payoff values reported separately for this condition.,0,Germany,"Experiment conducted at University of Cologne, Germany; participants recruited there.",1,N/R,Age of participants not reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,0,University students (assumed),"Conducted in a university lab with online recruitment via ORSEE; typical pool is university students, but not explicitly stated.",0.5,On site,"Explicitly described as conducted in the Cologne Laboratory for Economic Research, a physical lab.",1,"Linear public good game with fixed groups of 4, 10 rounds, endowment 20 tokens per round (1 token = 1 Eurocent); contributions summed and multiplied by 1.6; each player receives 0.4 times total contribution; participants receive anonymized feedback on total contributions and own return after each round; plus 2.50 Euro show-up fee; horizon known and group composition fixed.","Summarized from Materials, Procedures, and Instructions sections describing payoff function, feedback, and implementation.",1
10.1007_s11211-017-0300-7.md,Punishment condition (public good game with costly punishment stage),"Same experiment, second condition where costly punishment is available after contributions.",1,"Presence of punishment option: Punishment available via second stage with deduction points; fixed groups, 10 rounds, same contribution stage as control",Independent variable is availability of a costly punishment decision after observing contributions; here it is present.,1,True,Human subjects in lab public good game with punishment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation (punishment vs no-punishment) with random assignment.,1,True,Conducted in CLER at University of Cologne.,1,False,All decisions by human participants.,1,False,No purely analytical model used instead of data.,1,4,“Four participants interacted with each other in 47 fixed groups over ten rounds.” Applies to both conditions.,1,10,Same fixed groups over ten rounds for both conditions.,1,1,Contribution decision identical: any integer 0–20 tokens allowed.,1,0,Endowment 20 tokens each period; subjects decide contribution; endowment initially private.,1,0.4,Contribution stage identical: income from project = 0.4 × total group contribution.,1,0,No communication described; only on-screen decisions and feedback.,0.9,1,"Participants see total group contribution and own return each round; in punishment stage, they also see each other member’s contribution level.",1,0,Participants see contributions of 'group members' but identities remain anonymous; no personal identifiers mentioned.,0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism described.,1,1,Screens display period number and text states 10 periods; horizon known.,1,1,“In the punishment condition… participants had the additional opportunity to deduct points from other participants…”,1,1,"Cost schedule table: 1 point allocated costs 1 ECU, 2 points cost 2 ECU, etc.; marginal cost per punishment point is 1 ECU.",1,0.3,"Each 'point' given reduces target’s income by 10%; income from first stage is multiplied by (10 - total points)/10. Maximum total reduction is 100% when received points ≥10. One monetary unit spent for one point can at most reduce target’s income by 30% of their first-stage income (3× as effective as in Fehr & Gächter, described as up to threefold reduction). They state: “each punishment point resulted in a maximum of threefold reduction for the punished group member.” We code tech as 0.3 (max 30% of target’s first-stage income per 1 ECU cost).",0.4,0,"Mechanism only allows deductions (punishment), not positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,Endowment per round identical across conditions: 20 monetary units.,1,0.917,Average contribution was 18.34 of 20 tokens in the punishment setting; 18.34/20 ≈ 0.917.,1,18.34,"Results: “On average, participants contributed… 18.34 tokens in the punishment setting.”",1,N/R,Efficiency (realized payoff relative to maximum without punishment costs) not reported or derivable without more data.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff by group or condition reported; only contribution and some punishment usage statistics.,0,Germany,"Same subject pool and location (University of Cologne, Germany).",1,N/R,Age not reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students (assumed),Conducted at a university lab using ORSEE; typical subjects are students though not explicitly stated.,0.5,On site,Described as lab experiment at CLER.,1,"Linear public good game replicated exactly from Fehr & Gächter (2002) using their z-Tree code. Fixed groups of 4, 10 rounds, 20-token endowment per round (1 token = 1 Eurocent). Contribution stage: each player chooses 0–20 tokens to contribute; total group contribution is multiplied by 1.6 and each player receives 0.4 times total contribution as income from project plus tokens kept. After stage 1, players see all four individual contributions (absolute and % of endowment). Stage 2: each player can assign 0–10 punishment points to each of the 3 others. Cost schedule: cost in ECU per recipient = {0,1,2,4,6,9,12,16,20,25,30} for 0–10 points respectively; total punishment cost is sum across recipients. Each point received reduces a target’s stage-1 income by 10%; if total received points ≥10, their stage-1 income is reduced by 100% (to zero). Period income = (stage-1 income × (10 - total received points)/10) − own punishment costs, with cap at full reduction when ≥10 points. No negative per-period incomes from punishment-induced losses alone are allowed beyond this; but costs of own punishment can make period income negative. Punishment used infrequently: in 78% of rounds no punishment; when used, mean deducted amount 3.24 points (SD 3.5, Median 2). 54 of 84 punished at least once; among those who punished more than once, they punished in 3.74 rounds on average. Plus 2.50 Euro show-up fee.",Summarized from punishment condition description and detailed instruction section.,1
10.1007_s10683-019-09609-y.md,Endogenous B10 treatment – Game A phases,Main treatment where groups can vote between Game A and Game B10; this condition describes behavior in Game A under endogenous choice aggregated across phases.,0.7,Institutional choice environment: endogenous; available institution: costless exclusion (B10) vs no exclusion (A); actual game played in this row: A (no exclusion).,"In B10 treatment, groups vote between A and B10 each phase. This row covers those group-phase segments where A was selected.",0.8,True,Human subjects in a lab experiment.,1,True,Randomly assigned treatments and controlled public goods games.,1,True,Sessions held in a computer lab at University of Magdeburg.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,"Although theory is discussed, this row refers to the empirical experiment with humans.",1,5,Groups of N=5 members that remain fixed throughout the experiment.,1,20,Four phases of five rounds each; in Game A there is no exclusion stage but still 5 rounds per phase.,1,1,Players choose how much to contribute from their endowment; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Standard linear public goods game; endowment given to private account and subjects decide contribution.,0.9,0.4,Stage game payoff defined with MPCR a=0.4.,1,0,No communication or chat mentioned; only contribution and (when applicable) exclusion voting stages.,0.95,1,"After each round, individual contributions are displayed on the screen (in random order).",1,0,No punishment or exclusion in Game A.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Standard in repeated public goods experiments with fixed known phases; control questions and instructions imply known horizon (4 phases × 5 rounds).,0.7,0,Game A has no exclusion or other punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment in Game A.,1,N/A,No punishment in Game A.,1,0,No reward stage or mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"In game A, players’ endowment is EA=10.",1,0.411,"Table 2: in B10 treatment, across all phases, average contribution in Game A is 41.1% of endowment.",1,4.11,41.1% of endowment 10 ⇒ 0.411×10=4.11 tokens average.,0.95,N/R,No normalized efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) reported for this condition separately.,0,14.1,"Table 2: average payoff in Game A (insiders and all equal) is 14.1 tokens per player per round; group payoff (sum) would be 5×14.1 but table reports per capita, so we note that.",0.7,Germany,"Sessions held at University of Magdeburg, Germany, with students from that university.",0.95,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,Paper does not report gender composition.,0,Undergraduate university students,Described as undergraduate students recruited from the general student population.,0.95,On site,Conducted in a physical computer lab.,1,Repeated linear public goods game; fixed groups of 5; no exclusion; partner design; four phases of 5 rounds; payoffs in tokens converted at €0.05 per token.,Summary of Game A structure in B10 treatment.,0.9
10.1007_s10683-019-09609-y.md,Endogenous B10 treatment – Game B10 phases,Main treatment where groups can vote between Game A and costless exclusion Game B10; this condition aggregates over all phases and groups when B10 is actually played.,0.8,Institutional choice environment: endogenous; available institution: costless exclusion (B10) vs no exclusion (A); actual game played in this row: B10 (costless exclusion with endowment 10).,"In B10 treatment, B10 has same endowment as A and includes exclusion stage; we focus on rounds where B10 is chosen.",0.9,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Randomized between-subject treatment (B10 vs B8) and controlled institutions.,1,True,Conducted in university computer lab.,1,False,No simulation in this treatment.,1,False,"Row refers to empirical condition, not theoretical models.",1,5,Initial group size N=5; groups can shrink due to exclusion but nominal player count is 5.,1,20,Four 5-round phases; in B10 there is an exclusion stage in rounds 1–4 of each phase but total rounds remain 20.,1,1,Continuous choice of contribution amount from endowment.,1,0,Endowment initially in private account; contributions chosen each round.,0.9,0.4,"MPCR a=0.4 is identical across A, B10, and B8.",1,0,No communication channel or chat described.,0.95,1,"After contributions, individual contributions are displayed to all players in random order.",1,0,Exclusion votes are anonymous; players see number of votes but not identities of voters.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite horizon and phase structure explained in instructions; standard to display or clearly communicate remaining rounds.,0.7,1,Game B10 includes an exclusion stage where players can vote to exclude a member for remaining rounds in the phase.,1,0,Casting an exclusion vote is explicitly stated to be at no cost.,1,1,"If a player gets >50% of co-players’ votes they are fully excluded from contributing and benefiting from the public good for remaining rounds of the phase; this is a discrete, high-magnitude punishment. We code tech as 1 to indicate a one-to-one application of an exclusion decision to full removal.",0.7,0,No reward or positive sanction option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,10,"In game B10, players’ endowment is EB10=10 (same as A).",1,0.7569,"Table 2, B10: across all phases, average contribution in Game B is 75.69% of endowment.",1,7.569,0.7569 × 10 token endowment ≈ 7.569 tokens average contribution.,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio relative to maximum group payoff reported per condition.,0,16.3,"Table 2: B10, Game B, across all phases, average payoff (insider) is 16.3 tokens; we record per-capita payoff as main outcome.",0.8,Germany,"Lab sessions at University of Magdeburg, Germany.",0.95,N/R,Only that participants are undergraduate students; no age stats.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,Undergraduate university students,Participants described as undergraduate students from general student population.,0.95,On site,Physical computer lab experiment.,1,"Repeated linear public goods game with exclusion institution: after seeing individual contributions in random order, each non-excluded player can cast at most one free vote to exclude one co-player for the remaining rounds in the phase; majority (>half of co-players) required; no exclusion stage in last round of each phase; excluded players still receive endowment but cannot contribute, benefit from the public good, or vote.",Summary of B10 game rules as implemented under endogenous choice.,1
10.1007_s10683-019-09609-y.md,Endogenous B8 treatment – Game A phases,Main treatment where groups can vote between Game A and costly exclusion Game B8; this condition aggregates all instances where Game A (no exclusion) is played.,0.8,"Institutional choice environment: endogenous; available institution: costly exclusion (B8, endowment 8) vs no exclusion (A, endowment 10); actual game played: A (no exclusion).","In B8 treatment, choice is between Game A and Game B8 each phase; this row covers phases where A is chosen.",0.9,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with exogenous treatment assignment.,1,True,Conducted in a university computer lab.,1,False,No simulations for this condition.,1,False,"This row describes empirical results, not theoretical analysis.",1,5,Groups of N=5 members fixed throughout experiment.,1,20,Experimental design with four phases × five rounds; same for B8 and B10 treatments.,1,1,Players choose any contribution from 0 to endowment.,1,0,Endowment starts privately; contributions are voluntary.,0.9,0.4,Same MPCR a=0.4 across all games.,1,0,No communication stage mentioned.,0.95,1,Individual contributions displayed after each round.,1,0,Game A has no punishment or exclusion.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Finite and phase-structured game, stated in instructions.",0.7,0,No exclusion or other punishment in Game A.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,0,No reward option in Game A.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"In Game A, endowment EA=10; same in B10 and B8 treatments.",1,0.4071,"Table 2: in B8 treatment, across all phases, average contribution in Game A is 40.71%.",1,4.071,0.4071 × 10 = 4.071 tokens.,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency index reported per condition.,0,14.1,"Table 2: B8, Game A, average payoff (insider and all) is 14.1 tokens per round per player.",0.8,Germany,Same lab and subject pool as other treatments.,0.95,N/R,Only general description as undergraduates.,0.2,N/R,Gender not mentioned.,0,Undergraduate university students,Participants are undergraduate students from general student pool.,0.95,On site,Computer lab sessions.,1,Repeated linear public goods game; no exclusion; four phases of 5 rounds; partner design; identical structure to Game A in B10 treatment but presented in sessions where choice is between A and costly B8.,Summarizes Game A as used in endogenous B8 treatment.,0.9
