10.1063_1.5085076.md,Public goods game without punishment – analytical thermodynamic-limit model,"Single analytical treatment of the two-player public goods game without punishment mapped to the 1D Ising model; no subconditions beyond parameter variation in r, c, β.",0.93,"Reward r, cost c, and inverse temperature β varied continuously in analytical expressions; no discrete treatment conditions.","Paper studies how game magnetization M changes as a function of r, c, and β; these are the only levers discussed for this model.",0.96,False,No human participants; purely theoretical/statistical-physics treatment.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; only analytic derivations.,1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,False,"Results are closed-form analytical mappings, not numerical or agent-based simulations.",0.96,True,Derives closed-form expressions for magnetization M via mapping to 1D Ising model; entire paper is analytical.,1,2,Explicitly described as two player public goods game; mapping is for a generic two-player game.,1,1,"Static, one-shot game analyzed; no repetition or temporal dynamics in the game definition.",0.9,1,Players choose between two pure strategies: provide or free ride; no intermediate continuous contribution level is modeled.,0.98,N/A,No human endowments or starting allocations; purely analytical with payoffs directly defined.,1,N/R,"Standard MPCR for linear PGG not explicitly given; only reward r, cost c, and factor k are stated, without explicit MPCR term.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects; communication not applicable.,1,N/A,Model is analytical with no feedback interface to players.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this version of the game.,1,N/A,No explicit reward/punisher identity structure; payoffs are symmetric and anonymous.,1,N/A,No user interface or repeated rounds with humans.,1,0,This section explicitly treats the public goods game without punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,1,Public good generates multiplied benefit k*c resulting in reward r to cooperators; r is the effective reward parameter.,0.9,N/R,"Model parameterizes reward via r and cost of providing via c, but does not define a separate per-unit reward cost parameter in the sense of a costly prosocial reward action.",0.5,N/R,No explicit conversion factor from a separate reward action to payoff; only the game’s built-in multiplication factor k and resulting r.,0.4,N/R,Endowments in tokens are not stated; only cost c and returns r are given.,0.3,N/R,"Outcome variable analyzed is magnetization M (difference in fractions providing vs free riding), not normalized contribution rate per endowment.",0.4,N/R,No explicit token-level contributions defined; only binary strategy choices.,0.4,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency as payoff relative to maximum possible; focus is on magnetization and phase transitions.,0.4,N/R,Only individual payoff entries and analytical magnetization are shown; no aggregate group payoff statistics reported.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/analytical model; no empirical environment.,1,"Two-player public goods game with binary strategies {provide, free ride}. Payoff matrix: (provide,provide) gives each player 2r; (free ride,free ride) gives 0; unilateral provide yields provider r−c/2 and free rider r+c/2. Parameters: reward r>0, cost c>0, with Nash equilibrium provide if r>c/2 and free ride otherwise. Mapped exactly to 1D Ising model with J=0 and h=(2r−c)/4, yielding game magnetization M = tanh(β(2r−c)/4).",Summarizes explicit payoff matrix and mapping equations given in Sec. III.,1
10.1063_1.5085076.md,Public goods game with punishment – analytical thermodynamic-limit model,"Single analytical treatment of the two-player public goods game with punishment mapped to the 1D Ising model; only continuous variation in r, c, p, β.",0.93,"Reward r, cost c, punishment p, and inverse temperature β varied continuously in analytical expressions.","Magnetization M is analyzed as a function of r, c, p, and β; these parameters define conditions within this model.",0.96,False,No human participants; analysis is theoretical.,1,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment; analytical derivations only.,1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,False,"Closed-form analytical mapping, not numerical simulation.",0.96,True,Uses exact mapping of payoff matrix with punishment to 1D Ising model and derives M analytically.,1,2,Explicitly: “two player Public goods game with punishment”.,1,1,"Model is static, single interaction; no repeated rounds.",0.9,1,Binary choice between provide and free ride; no gradation of contribution amounts.,0.98,N/A,No explicit endowments or default allocations; analytical model only.,1,N/R,"k (multiplication factor) and c are defined, but MPCR is not explicitly calculated or named.",0.4,N/A,No participants or communication stage.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,Punishment is an automatic payoff adjustment (−p) for defectors; no punisher identity exists.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarder identity; benefits are automatic from public good.,1,N/A,No rounds presented to human players.,1,1,"Model adds punishment p so that whenever a player free rides, payoff includes −p.",1,N/R,"Punishment p is a penalty to defectors, but there is no separate cost to punishers defined; it is not a costly punishment action by another player.",0.5,N/R,No parameter mapping coins spent on punishment to payoff reduction; p directly enters payoff.,0.5,1,"As in the no-punishment case, the public good creates benefits parameterized by r; this is treated as reward, and the paper discusses reward and punishment as triggers.",0.9,N/R,"Cost of producing the public good is c, but there is no distinct cost for providing extra rewards beyond cooperation itself.",0.5,N/R,No direct mapping from a separate reward action to payoff increment; r and k embed the benefit structure.,0.4,N/R,Endowment amounts are not specified; only cost c and benefit r.,0.3,N/R,Outcome analyzed: magnetization M (difference in fractions providing vs defecting); no normalized contribution measure.,0.4,N/R,Binary provide/free ride choice; no continuous contribution amount recorded.,0.4,N/R,Efficiency (group payoff vs max possible) is not computed; focus is on phase transitions in M.,0.4,N/R,Only individual payoff matrices and analytical M are discussed; no aggregated group payoff statistics.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Modeling work only.,1,"Two-player public goods game with punishment p>0 applied to defectors. Payoff matrix: (provide,provide) → (2r,2r); (free ride,free ride) → (−p,−p); if row provides and column free rides, row gets r−c/2, column r+c/2−p, and vice versa. Nash equilibrium: provide if r>c/2−p; free ride otherwise. Mapping to Ising gives transformed payoffs with J=0 and h=(2r−c+2p)/4, so game magnetization in thermodynamic limit is M = tanh(β(2r−c+2p)/4). Increasing punishment p shifts the critical value of r for the phase transition towards lower rewards, increasing cooperation.",Summarizes the explicit punished payoff matrix in Eq. (18) and the derived mapping in Sec. IV.B.,1
10.1111_j.1749-6632.2009.04542.x.md,Choi and Bowles (2007) evolutionary simulation of parochial altruism and war,"Only concrete, fully described game-like setup with explicit parameters is the long-range evolutionary simulation by Choi and Bowles described in the text.",0.97,"Frequencies of four genotype types (PA: parochial altruists, TA: tolerant altruists, TNA: tolerant selfish, PNA: parochial selfish) interacting under opportunities for trade, war, or no contact across 20 groups over 50,000 generations.",Text specifies two loci (A/NA and P/T) creating four types whose relative frequencies and interactions under trade/war/no-contact drive outcomes.,0.9,False,"Described explicitly as a long-range evolutionary simulation, not involving human participants.",1,False,"It is a computer/evolutionary simulation model, not a controlled behavioral experiment with human/field subjects.",1,False,No lab setting or human subjects; purely simulated populations.,1,True,"Text: 'In addition, in a long-range evolutionary simulation, Choi and Bowles found...' indicating an agent-based or numerical simulation.",1,False,Described as a simulation over generations rather than closed-form analytical derivations.,0.9,26,"Text: 'The simulation ran for 50,000 generations and allowed interactions between 20 groups ( n=26 members )...'. The playerCount is the number of members per group.",1,50000,"Text: 'The simulation ran for 50,000 generations'; interpreted as discrete iterations/rounds.",0.95,1,"Contribution choice structure (continuous vs all-or-nothing) is not described for simulated agents; this field is not directly applicable. Using 1 as default would be speculative, but schema disallows N/A for humans/nonhumans here. Marking as 1 with low confidence.",0.1,N/A,Simulation of genetic traits in intergroup interactions; no explicit endowment or default allocation decisions by agents are described.,1,N/R,"No marginal per capita return or linear public goods structure is described; model is about fitness from war/trade interactions, not standard MPCR.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between agents is not modeled as chat-like feature.,1,0,"No interface or feedback summaries are defined; agents interact via evolutionary fitness, not game screens.",0.95,0,No explicit punishment stage or identity revelation is mentioned in the description of the simulation.,0.95,0,No explicit reward stage or identity revelation is mentioned.,0.95,0,"Agents are not described as being informed about total generations; this is an evolutionary model, not a repeated game with displayed horizon.",0.95,0,Description focuses on war vs trade vs no contact and fitness effects of parochialism/altruism; no individual-level costly punishment mechanism is specified.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism present in the described model.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present in the described model.,1,0,No individual rewards for cooperation beyond fitness payoffs; no separate costly reward stage is specified.,0.9,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism in the simulation.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism in the simulation.,1,N/R,"Fitness payoffs are described in terms of evolutionary success, not token endowments per round.",1,N/R,Outcome measures reported: stable dominance of parochial altruists vs tolerant selfish types under war/trade regimes; no numeric contribution rates are given.,1,N/R,No explicit contributions in tokens or amounts are reported; only evolutionary frequencies of types.,1,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to cooperative optimum) is not quantified in the summarized description.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoff values are presented in the text; only qualitative dominance/regime descriptions.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulation with no human subjects or field context.,1,"Long-range evolutionary simulation over 50,000 generations with 20 groups of 26 members each. Two loci (altruism A/NA and parochialism P/T) yield four types (PA, TA, TNA, PNA). Groups have opportunities for trade, war, or no contact; fitness outcomes depend on type composition and interactions, leading to stable populations dominated by parochial altruists in warlike contexts and tolerant selfish types in trading contexts.",Summary of the only detailed game/simulation description in the paper.,0.98
