10.3982_te795.md,N/A – purely theoretical analysis of repeated prisoners’ dilemma,"The paper develops only analytical game-theoretic results (no separate experimental conditions, treatments, or simulations with agents).",1,N/A,"No experimental manipulation or simulation parameter grid with outcome data; only theoretical parameters (b, c, δ, L, etc.) within analytical models.",1,False,The work is entirely theoretical; no human subjects or empirical data are used.,1,False,"There is no controlled experiment; only theoretical definitions, lemmas, and propositions.",1,False,No lab setting or physical/online lab is described; the paper is theoretical.,1,False,"No numerical, agent-based, or computational simulations are reported; all results are analytical.",1,True,"The paper provides formal game-theoretic analysis and proofs about robustness in repeated games, with no data or simulations.",1,2,"The core game throughout is a two-player prisoners’ dilemma (PD) with A1 = A2 = {C, D}.",1,-1,"The main object is an infinitely repeated prisoners’ dilemma (infinitely many periods, discount factor δ∈(0,1)); not a finite number of rounds.",1,1,"Players choose between two discrete pure actions {C,D}; there is no continuous contribution variable. For our cooperation/punishment coding, this is neither a contribution game nor an all-or-nothing contribution frame, but forced dichotomous actions; coded as allowing the full action set rather than an all-or-nothing contribution constraint.",0.6,N/A,This is not a public-goods or contribution game; there is no divisible endowment or contribution default.,1,N/A,"No linear public-goods technology is defined; payoffs are given in PD matrices, not through a marginal per capita return on contributions.",1,0,The theoretical model does not include any communication or cheap-talk stage between players.,1,0,"Players observe public outcomes/signals or actions depending on the monitoring structure, but there is no notion of payoff/punishment summary displays as in experiments.",1,0,There is no explicit punishment action or identity revelation mechanism; only equilibrium strategies over C/D in PD.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,The horizon is infinite and common knowledge in the model; there is no interface element or information treatment about remaining rounds as in experiments.,1,0,"While ‘punishment’ is discussed conceptually within equilibrium strategies (grim trigger, selective punishment), there is no separate punishment stage or costly punishment action as in standard public-goods experiments.",0.9,N/A,No explicit costly punishment technology is specified; C and D are standard PD actions with fixed payoffs.,1,N/A,No parameter mapping punishment points to losses is defined.,1,0,There is no explicit rewarding action or stage distinct from PD choices.,1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward effect parameter is defined.,1,N/A,"The PD is specified directly in payoff units (e.g., 1, 0, -c, b), without an explicit per-period endowment that players allocate.",1,N/A,No empirical outcomes or simulated contribution rates are reported; only theoretical characterization of equilibria.,1,N/A,There is no contribution variable or data; actions are C/D in theory only.,1,N/R,"The paper characterizes sets of equilibrium payoffs and robustness properties but does not report numeric efficiency measures (e.g., ratios to maximum surplus) as data for specific parameter values.",0.6,N/R,"Payoff vectors are presented symbolically (e.g., (1,1), (0,0), (b,-c)); there are no realized group payoff statistics or averages for an empirical or simulated sample.",0.8,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is a theoretical analysis without any empirical or field component.,1,"Infinitely repeated 2x2 prisoners’ dilemma with payoffs: (C,C)→(1,1); (C,D)→(-c,b); (D,C)→(b,-c); (D,D)→(0,0), with b>1, c>0, b−c<2. Analysis of grim-trigger and selective-punishment strategies under complete and incomplete information, including perturbed games Γ_PD^L with temptation states and public/imperfect monitoring structures.",Summarized directly from the model description in Sections 2 and 5.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0057378.md,"Simulation – baseline responsive defectors with punishment (g=3, k=2, p=1, q=3, m=20)","Distinct simulation condition explicitly described as using typical economic experiment values g=3, k=2, p=1, q=3 and meetings m=20; main baseline in Fig. 1.",1,"Simulation parameters: group size g=3; cooperation multiplier k=2; punishment cost p=1; punishment impact q=3; meetings per generation m=20; defectors responsive to cumulative punishment; four genetically determined strategies DN, DP, CN, CP; mutation rate μ=0.01; 10 islands of N=3000 each; meta-population reproduction (local 0.8, global 0.2).",Summarizes all key parameters that define this condition and distinguish it from others.,1,False,Pure computer simulation with genetically determined strategies and no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment with subjects; it is a computational evolutionary model.,1,False,"No physical lab or human subjects, only simulations.",1,True,Author explicitly describes a simulation model with agents on islands and evolutionary updating.,1,False,"This specific condition refers to simulation runs, not the analytical derivations in the earlier section.",1,3,Stated that analysis focused on groups of size g=3; the representative baseline simulation uses g=3.,1,20,Meetings within groups are repeated for m=20 within each generation under standard conditions.,1,0,"Cooperation is a binary decision (cooperate or defect), not continuous contribution amounts, so contributions are all-or-nothing; per instructions 1 indicates continuous, 0 otherwise.",0.9,N/A,No human endowment allocation decision; agents have an abstract endowment and a binary cooperate/defect choice in a repeated game.,1,0.6667,MPCR = k/g = 2/3 ≈ 0.6667.,1,N/A,No communication among simulated agents; they follow fixed strategies.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback design; payoffs affect reproduction only.,1,N/A,Agents do not have identities or beliefs; punishment is algorithmic based on strategy and defection.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,Simulated agents do not observe or use explicit round-count information.,1,1,Punishment strategies P vs N are central to the model; punishers pay cost p to inflict cost q on defectors.,1,1,Baseline parameters: p=1.,1,3,Each act of punishment costs p=1 and inflicts q=3.,1,0,Model only includes costly punishment; no positive reward action is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,6,Agents begin each generation with an endowment of 6 points.,1,0.9394,Mean cooperation level reported as 93.94%; interpreted as proportion of cooperative actions.,0.95,N/R,"Each cooperative act invests cost i, but the exact i value for this baseline is not numerically specified; only that i is fixed and set to 1 for some analytical examples.",0.3,N/R,No explicit report of total group payoff relative to maximum cooperative payoff.,0,N/R,"Only average cooperation and punishment frequencies are reported, not aggregate payoffs.",0,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary simulation.,1,"Meta-population of 10 islands (N=3000 each); fixed groups of size g=3; responsive defectors who switch to cooperation when cumulative punishment cost exceeds net cooperation cost during interactions; four strategies (DN, DP, CN, CP) evolve via proportional reproduction with mutation μ=0.01; m=20 meetings per generation within fixed groups; local vs global reproduction mixing probabilities 0.8 and 0.2 respectively.",Summarizes additional design details relevant to understanding the simulation game structure and evolution protocol.,1
