10.1007_s10539-007-9065-y.md,"Theoretical complex game with TPP punishment – Scenario A (4 players, 2 defectors)","Named from section describing scenario (A): 4 players, 2 defectors, using two-person punishment game (TPP).",1,Punishment method: TPP (two-person costly punishment); Group size n = 4; Number of defectors in PG round = 2; Cost ratio in TPP = 1:2.,"Scenario A varies method (TPP), group size and number of defectors; these define this condition.",1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical payoff analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a thought-experiment/model.,1,False,No lab or field implementation; only analytical discussion.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported; only symbolic payoff calculations.,1,True,Derives inequalities and payoffs algebraically from specified matrices.,1,4,"Scenario A: '4 players, 2 of them defect in the PG round of the game'.",1,2,Complex game is composed of 'alternating rounds of a one-period n-person public goods game (PG) and a one-period two-person punishment game'; scenario describes one PG round followed by one punishment round.,0.8,1,"Players are described simply as cooperators or defectors in PG; no statement restricting them to all-or-nothing contributions, but standard PG modeling assumes full cooperate vs defect; coded as allowing binary choice, which fits 1 per instructions (can contribute any amount vs only all-or-nothing). Here only all-or-nothing is modeled, but schema maps 0 to 'only all-or-nothing'; set to 0.",0.3,N/A,No human subjects; default endowment handling not applicable.,1,N/R,Public goods stage payoff defined by benefit C and equal distribution but no explicit multiplier or MPCR reported.,0,N/A,No human subjects or interface; communication not applicable.,1,0,No informational interface described; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No identities or information structure modeled in analytical game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this scenario.,1,0,No mention of subjects being shown remaining rounds; theoretical one-shot per stage.,1,1,Scenario uses two-person punishment game (TPP) where cooperators directly punish defectors.,1,1,"Text: 'at cost to themselves but at an even greater cost to the punished, in a ratio of 1:2' for TPP; cost per punishment act to punisher normalized as 1.",0.7,2,Same sentence: cost ratio 1:2; thus each unit cost to punisher reduces target’s payoff by 2 units.,0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism described; only punishment and exclusion.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model defines cost of cooperation C but not an explicit endowment.,0,N/R,Analytical model; no specific numerical contributions or rates given beyond cooperative/defective strategies.,0,N/R,Only symbolic cost C; no numeric tokens.,0,N/R,Text compares relative payoffs and 'efficiency patterns' qualitatively but does not provide normalized efficiency values.,0.2,N/R,"Scenario gives individual payoffs (cooperators -2, defectors -4 after TPP) but not summed group payoff as a key dependent variable.",0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure analytical modeling without subjects.,1,"Two-stage game: Stage 1 is an n-person public goods game where cooperators pay cost C and benefit all equally; Stage 2 is a two-person punishment game (TPP) where each cooperator can punish defectors at personal cost 1 imposing cost 2 on each defector. In Scenario A (4 players, 2 defectors), each defector is punished by 2 cooperators, ending with payoff -4; cooperators end with -2 each after punishment.",Summarizes structure from the section 'A simple game for comparing punishment strategies' and scenario A calculations.,1
