y.,1,"Same two-stage structure: PG then TPP. In Scenario B (30 players, 10 defectors in PG), each of the 10 defectors is punished by 20 cooperators, resulting in -40 for each defector; the 20 cooperators get -10 each after punishment. Cooperation in the PG game is enforced when 30 − C > 0, indicating very strong punishment (over-punishment).",Summarizes Scenario B description and derived inequality (30 − C > 0).,1
10.1007_s10539-007-9065-y.md,"Theoretical complex game with TPP punishment – Scenario C (30 players, 20 defectors)","Named from section describing scenario (C): 30 players, 20 defectors, TPP method.",1,Punishment method: TPP (two-person costly punishment); Group size n = 30; Number of defectors in PG round = 20; Cost ratio in TPP = 1:2.,Scenario C is defined by high proportion of defectors under same TPP structure.,1,False,Purely analytical; no empirical data.,1,False,"Thought-experimental scenario, not run with subjects.",1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,False,No numeric/agent simulation.,1,True,Uses payoff calculations showing TPP cannot enforce cooperation when many defectors.,1,30,Scenario C: '30 players in total and 20 defectors'.,1,2,Same two-stage complex game (PG + punishment).,0.8,0,Only full cooperation vs defection allowed conceptually.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,No explicit multiplier/MPCR.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,0,Not modeled.,1,N/A,No identity structure modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No display/feedback mechanism.,1,1,Scenario C describes outcomes under TPP punishment.,1,1,Same TPP 1:2 cost ratio applies.,0.7,2,"Each unit of punishment costs 1 to punisher, 2 to punished.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism included.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Only a cooperation cost C is defined and used in inequalities.,0,N/R,No numeric contributions; just classification into cooperators/defectors.,0,N/R,Symbolic cost C only.,0,N/R,States that TPP cannot enforce cooperation beyond certain defector/cooperator ratio but does not compute efficiency ratio.,0.2,N/R,"Individual payoffs (defectors -20, cooperators -20 after TPP) are described; group sum not used as DV.",0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical scenario only.,1,"Two-stage game (PG then TPP) with many defectors. In Scenario C (30 players, 20 defectors), each defector is punished by 10 cooperators and ends with payoff −20; the 10 cooperators also end with −20 each. The condition for cooperation stability, −C > 0, cannot be satisfied because C > 0, so TPP cannot enforce cooperation when defectors are too numerous.",Summarizes Scenario C description and inequality '−C > 0'.,1
10.1007_s10539-007-9065-y.md,Theoretical complex game with TPR punishment (exclusion) – Scenarios A–C baseline (no exploitation in TPR),Text treats TPR version of the punishment round as one structure applied to Scenarios A–C when defectors cannot exploit cooperators; grouped here as a single condition because payoffs are stated to be independent of number of players and of proportion of defectors (given even number).,0.7,"Punishment method: TPR (two-person PD interaction with partner choice and exclusion of defectors); Defectors cannot exploit cooperators; Group size n is even (examples given: 4, 30) with varying numbers of defectors.",Independent variable is punishment method (TPR) and assumption about exploitability; group size and composition do not affect individual payoffs in this version.,0.8,False,Purely theoretical game analysis.,1,False,Not implemented as an experiment; structure discussed analytically.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,False,No computational implementation described.,1,True,Derives inequality 3 − C > 0 from payoff matrices for TPR.,1,N/R,"TPR analysis is stated to hold 'independently of the number of players (provided an even number)' and different n (4, 30) are mentioned; no single fixed n for this generalized condition.",0.4,2,Complex game has first a PG round then a TPR punishment round.,0.8,0,"As in TPP scenarios, only pure cooperation vs defection actions are modeled in PG and in TPR interactions.",0.8,N/A,No actual endowments or human subjects.,1,N/R,PG defined in terms of benefit C and equal distribution but no explicit MPCR.,0,N/A,No human interaction interface.,1,0,No feedback modeled.,1,N/A,No identity or information structure.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No interface to display rounds.,1,1,"Here, punishment is implemented via exclusion in TPR (refusing to cooperate with previous defectors).",1,0,"In TPR, cooperators simply choose partners among cooperators and defectors pair with each other; no explicit additional cost of punishing beyond opportunity cost already captured in PD payoffs. Modeled payoff for cooperators in TPR is +3, defectors 0; exclusion does not introduce extra cost compared to cooperating with cooperators.",0.4,3,"Relative to the counterfactual where defectors could have cooperated and gained 3, exclusion reduces each defector’s payoff from 3 to 0. Treating a unit 'act' of exclusion as costing 0 to punisher and 3 to the punished implies a 0:3 effect; for schema we approximate magnitude of loss per punished interaction as 3.",0.3,0,No positive rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Model defines stage payoffs (e.g., +3 in TPR cooperation) and cost C but no initial endowment per period.",0,N/R,No numeric contributions reported; only condition 3 − C > 0 for stability of cooperation.,0,N/R,Only symbolic cost C is mentioned.,0,N/R,Efficiency compared qualitatively ('stable and efficient punishment method') but not quantified.,0.3,N/R,"Individual payoffs in TPR (3 for cooperators, 0 for defectors) are stated, but group totals not highlighted as outcomes.",0.6,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical game-theoretic construction.,1,"Two-stage complex game: PG stage then TPR stage. In TPR version, 'cooperators choose their partners among the other cooperators in the PG game and play cooperatively. Defectors pair with each other and obtain 0 (zero).' Cooperators earn 3 each in TPR. This applies in scenarios A, B and C, 'independently of the number of players (provided an even number) and of the proportion of defectors.' Cooperation in the PG game can be enforced when 3 − C > 0.",Paraphrases the TPR description and enforcement condition.,1
