qualitative.,0,N/R,No numeric contribution data.,0,N/R,Text argues TPR is no longer an efficient punishment method but does not provide numerical efficiency values.,0.3,N/R,No explicit group payoff computations in Scenario D; only qualitative reversal of relative payoffs.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical thought experiment.,1,"Scenario D modifies the TPR stage: 'some defectors pair up with cooperators in the TPR round of the game and are able to exploit them.' Examples given include the use of violence or deception. When this happens, defectors no longer get 0 in TPR; depending on exploitation opportunities, inequality 3 − C > 0 will be invalidated and relative payoffs reversed, so TPR is no longer an efficient punishment method.",Summarizes narrative description of Scenario D.,1
10.1002_bdm.2012.md,Experiment 1 – Female partner,"Within Experiment 1, partner gender (female vs. male) is a within-subject factor; this row captures trials with female partners.",1,Partner behavior (defection vs. cooperation) with female partners; partner gender fixed as female; no variation in participant gender considered.,"For this condition, the manipulated feature distinguishing it from the parallel condition is partner gender=female; within this, partner behavior varies by trial.",0.9,True,Human university students played the game.,1,True,Laboratory-controlled social dilemma with programmed partner behavior and gender.,1,True,Conducted at Heinrich Heine University laboratory setting.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; partners are programmed but participants are human.,1,False,"Although a multinomial model is used, the focal data are from an experiment with human subjects.",1,2,Prisoner’s dilemma game between participant and one partner at a time.,1,40,"Participants interacted with 20 female and 20 male partners, each once, totaling 40 trials; this condition covers the 20 female-partner trials.",1,1,"Participants could choose to invest 30 cents (cooperate) or 0 cents (defect); binary choice, but schema defines 1 as can contribute any amount and 0 as all-or-nothing. Here, contribution is effectively all-or-nothing; however, contribution is not continuous. Set to 0 accordingly.",0.7,0,Endowment starts in participant’s private account; they choose to invest 0 or 30 cents into the game each trial.,1,0.3333333333,"Total investment is increased by a bonus of 1/3 of total investments and then split evenly. Each cent invested yields total = 1.333... cents shared by 2 players → MPCR = 1.3333/2 ≈ 0.6667 per cent? However, marginal per capita return is defined as multiplier/group size. Multiplier here is 4/3 (investment plus 1/3 bonus) so MPCR = (4/3)/2 ≈ 0.6667.",0.7,0,No mention of communication; only button choices and punishment input.,1,1,Participants saw partner’s gains/losses and updated account balance each trial.,1,0,Partners are anonymous photos; no indication that a stable identity beyond the face is tracked across trials or that punishment is attributed across rounds; moreover partners do not observe participant punishments in any strategic sense.,0.6,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,1,Standard lab PD implementations with fixed-trial tasks usually inform participants about total number of trials; not explicitly stated but likely given fixed 40 trials.,0.4,1,Participants had a costly punishment option each trial.,1,1,Participants could spend between 0 and 9 cents; each cent invested costs 1 cent to the participant.,1,10,"For each cent invested, 10 cents were subtracted from the partner’s account.",1,0,No mechanism to spend resources to increase partner’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,400,All participants started with an account balance of 400 cents.,1,N/R,"Cooperation probabilities (parameter C) are reported, but not converted to normalized contribution fraction per round for this subset.",0.6,N/R,Only cooperation probabilities and payoffs are given; no mean cents contributed aggregated per partner gender reported.,0.3,N/R,No total group payoff relative to maximum is provided.,0.1,N/R,"Only punishment amounts and some example payoffs are reported, not average group payoffs.",0.1,Germany,"Participants were students at Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Germany.",1,"18–40 (M=23, SD=4)",Age range and mean with SD reported for Experiment 1.,1,"Mostly female (61 female, 19 male)",Experiment 1 sample composition reported; condition does not subset by participant gender.,1,University students,Participants described as students at the university.,1,On site,Lab-based experiment with university students at terminals; not described as online.,0.9,Stage 1: simultaneous prisoner’s dilemma with invest 30 vs 0 cents; total investments increased by 1/3 and split equally. S