10.1016_j.physleta.2014.11.035.md,Analytical model of parochial altruism with two groups (well-mixed population),Paper presents a single theoretical evolutionary game model (no separate named experimental conditions with humans); treat the full analytical model as one condition.,0.92,"Model parameters: benefit of intragroup cooperation b; cost of intragroup cooperation c; cost of intergroup punishment α; effectiveness of intergroup punishment γ; mutation rate μ (low, intermediate, high limits); population size N; selection strength ω.",These parameters are systematically varied in the analysis and figures to study when parochial altruism (CP) is favored.,0.98,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical evolutionary game plus numerical calculations.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it is a theoretical/mathematical analysis with simulations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only theoretical modeling.,1,True,Uses numerical simulations based on a Moran process and fixation probabilities in a well-mixed population; no human participants.,0.96,True,"Derives closed-form conditions and fixation probabilities analytically (multiple inequalities and functions f(N), g(N)).",1,20,"Main text and figures use finite population sizes like N=10, N=20 and analyze large-N limits; choose N=20 as explicitly used baseline (e.g., Fig. 1: N=20, ω=0.0001).",0.7,1,Evolutionary Moran process with continuous update; no discrete repeated-game rounds. Treat each interaction set per update as a single-stage game.,0.6,1,"Strategies are binary (cooperate/defect, punish/not); there is no partial contribution level—effectively all-or-nothing choice each interaction.",0.8,N/A,Not a public goods or endowment-allocation setup; payoffs defined directly via PD and punishment matrices; also no human subjects.,1,N/R,"MPCR is specific to public goods games; here interactions are pairwise PD and punishment games, no MPCR defined.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication; evolutionary dynamics only.,1,0,Model agents do not have information displays; payoffs and evolution are automatic with no feedback presentation.,1,N/A,No identities or displays; punishment is modeled via payoff matrix between anonymous types.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,Agents are not shown any round information; process is infinite-horizon Moran updating without such constructs.,1,1,"Core of the model: intergroup punishment game with strategies P and N_P and parameters α, γ.",1,1,"Cost parameter α is the cost paid by a punisher per punishment; scale units so that cost per unit punishment is α, relative cost factor taken as 1.",0.7,1,"Effectiveness parameter γ is the harm inflicted to the punished; interpret 1 unit of cost α produces γ units of negative payoff, technology ratio implicit; set base unit as 1 for schema.",0.6,0,Only costly punishment is modeled; no positive reward or bonus mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/R,"No explicit per-period token or endowment amount; payoffs are directly given by PD and punishment parameters b, c, α, γ.",1,N/R,"Outputs are stationary probabilities and fixation metrics of strategies, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No monetary or token contributions reported; only strategy frequencies and payoffs.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency as total payoff relative to maximum; focuses on when CP is favored and strategy abundances.,1,N/R,"Average payoffs per strategy are discussed qualitatively, but no explicit group payoff numbers are given in the main text.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical/simulation-based with evolutionary dynamics; no empirical environment.,1,"Well-mixed finite population of size N divided into two observable-color groups (Red, Green); each individual uses one of four strategies: CP (in-group cooperation, out-group punishment), CNP (in-group cooperation, no out-group punishment), DP (in-group defection, out-group punishment), DNP (in-group defection, no out-group punishment). Intragroup interactions follow a Prisoner’s Dilemma with benefit b and cost c; intergroup interactions follow a costly punishment game with cost α to punisher and fine γ to target. Evolution follows a Birth–Death Moran process with mutation rate μ and selection intensity ω. Parochial altruism corresponds to strategy CP.",Summarizes the model structure and payoff matrices as described in the Model section.,1
10.1088_0253-6102_56_4_07.md,"Simulation – VPGG with C, D, L at K=0.8 (Fig. 1, Nature 2003 parameters)","Condition described when matching Semmann et al. [17] experiment using C, D, L and K=0.8 with N=6, c=1.25, r=3.6, k=1.25.",0.95,"Magnitude of rationality K = 0.8; strategies: C, D, L; group size N=6; contribution cost c=1.25; multiplier r=3.6; loner payoff k=1.25; finite population (M not specified here)","Paper explicitly states parameters for Fig. 1 including K, N, c, r, and k and considers only C, D, L.",0.95,False,Condition is a numerical implementation of approximate best response dynamics with finite populations and no human subjects.,1,False,No human participants or real-world interventions; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; it is a model simulation.,1,True,Authors ‘imitate the evolution procedure’ using approximate best response dynamics for specified parameters.,1,False,"This specific condition is presented as a numerical trajectory, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,6,Text for Fig. 1: parameters include N=6.,1,0,Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics; no discrete rounds defined.,0.7,1,Standard public goods game with cost c per participating cooperator; contributions are per cooperator not binary all-or-nothing at group level; players effectively choose participate & contribute c vs defect or be loner; contributions are not restricted to only all-or-nothing at the player level beyond this binary choice so coded as flexible (1).,0.6,N/A,No human endowments; strategies are abstract with fixed payoffs.,1,3.6,"Marginal per capita return is r/N_participants. However, they multiply total by r and divide by S participants; MPCR = r/S. For typical reference MPCR they often use r/N where N is group size, but here S varies. Using reported r=3.6 and typical N=6 from experiment being matched gives r/N=0.6; however MPCR is not clearly defined in this theoretical paper. To avoid over-inference, leave as N/R.",0,N/A,No human interaction; communication not modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface; only evolutionary state variables.,1,N/A,No explicit identities; punishment is aggregated in payoff equations.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No rounds or UI; continuous dynamics.,1,0,"This condition includes only C, D, L strategies; punishers are not present.",0.95,N/A,No punishment strategy present.,1,N/A,No punishment strategy present.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism is discussed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model uses fixed cost c but not an explicit endowment.,0.5,0,No explicit normalized contribution rate reported; dynamics and payoffs are given in absolute terms. Cannot infer stable average contributions as fraction of an endowment.,0,0,"Per cooperator contributes c=1.25; however, outcome statistics of average contributions per individual per interaction are not summarized; thus we do not enter a specific DV value.",0,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency relative to maximum payoff.,0,N/R,"Only individual payoffs P_c, P_d, P_l are compared; no explicit group payoff reported for this condition.",0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is a numerical simulation without human subjects.,1,Voluntary public goods game (VPGG) with strategies: cooperators (C) who pay cost c to contribute; defectors (D) who participate without paying; loners (L) who do not participate and receive fixed payoff k. Single participant (S=1) receives loner payoff k instead of PGG payoff.,"Described in the model section for VPGG without punishers with explicit payoff functions, applied to Fig. 1 parameter set.",1
10.1088_0253-6102_56_4_07.md,"Simulation – VPGG with C, D, P, L at K→0 (Fig. 2a)","Fig. 2(a) described as VPGG with C, D, P, L in simplex S3 for K=0.00001 approximating perfect rationality.",0.98,"Magnitude of rationality K ≈ 0 (0.00001); strategies: C, D, P, L; group size N=5; population size M=100; contribution cost c=1.0; multiplier r=3.0; loner payoff k=1.0; punishment parameters β=1.0, γ=0.3.","Parameters listed under Fig. 2: N=5, M=100, c=1.0, r=3.0, k=1.0, β=1.0, γ=0.3, and from top to bottom K=0.00001,0.5,2.5.",1,False,Finite population evolutionary dynamics without human participants.,1,False,"Controlled numerical study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab environment; simulations only.,1,True,Uses approximate best response dynamics in finite populations for specified parameters.,1,False,This figure reports numerical equilibrium frequencies rather than closed-form solutions.,0.9,5,Figure 2 caption: N=5.,1,0,Evolution is modeled in continuous time; no explicit round count.,0.7,1,"Each cooperator or punisher pays a fixed cost c per game; modeling uses continuous strategy frequencies but contribution choice is binary (contribute cost c or not). As per coding rule, treat as standard PGG (1).",0.6,N/A,No human endowments modeled.,1,N/R,Total public good is multiplied by r and divided among participants; MPCR would be r/S with variable S; no single reported value.,0,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,N/A,No user-level display; internal state only.,1,N/A,No identity structure; punishment is aggregated.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,Punishers P exist and impose β per defector at cost γ per defector.,1,0.3,Parameter γ=0.3 is the cost per punished defector to a punisher.,1,1,Parameter β=1.0 is punishment inflicted per defector per punisher.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism in model.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Only a cost c is defined; endowment is not specified.,0.5,0,"Cooperators and punishers always contribute c when participating, but the paper does not convert this into normalized contribution fraction over an endowment.",0,0,Per contributing player pays c=1.0; equilibrium average contributions not explicitly summarized as a DV.,0,N/R,No efficiency ratio reported.,0,N/R,"Figure reports strategy frequencies, not aggregate payoffs.",0,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Numerical simulations without human subjects.,1,"Voluntary public goods game with four strategies: cooperators (C) and punishers (P) contribute cost c to common pool; defectors (D) participate without contributing; loners (L) receive fixed payoff k and do not participate. Punishers additionally pay γ per defector to impose β punishment on each defector. For S=1 participant, the player instead receives loner payoff k.","Model section gives payoff functions p_c, p_d, p_p, p_l for arbitrary composition; Fig. 2(a) uses these with C,D,P,L in finite population.",1
