10.1037_dev0001183.md,Baseline condition – forced‑choice dictator game,"Paper describes three between-subjects conditions: Baseline, Direct Feedback, Reputation. This row captures the Baseline condition.",1,Third-party role: no additional experimenter present; no punishment possible; standard forced-choice dictator game allocations (3/3 vs 4/2; 3/3 vs 5/1; 4/2 vs 5/1).,Independent variable is presence/type of third party (none vs punisher vs observer) holding game structure constant.,1,True,Children aged 6–9 played dictator game; real payoffs in Skittles.,1,True,Participants pseudorandomly assigned to conditions with controlled procedures.,1,True,"Tested in laboratory space, public parks, museums, or zoo in an experimental setup; categorized as on-site lab-style experiment.",0.8,False,No computer/agent simulations; only human children.,1,False,No purely mathematical or analytical model reported.,1,2,Dictator and one absent peer recipient per game.,1,6,“The participant then began six forced-choice dictator game trials”.,1,0,Forced choice between two specified distributions each trial; not contribute-all-or-nothing to a public good.,1,0,Standard dictator game with tokens initially under dictator’s control; nothing pre-assigned to ‘public’.,0.7,N/A,Not a public goods game; no linear contribution multiplier.,1,0,No communication between child and recipient; only instruction not to speak to experimenter mentioned for other conditions.,0.9,0,Children only see own choice and resulting token allocations; no aggregate or others’ outcomes.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in Baseline.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,0,No indication that total number of trials/remaining rounds was displayed to children.,0.7,0,Baseline condition explicitly has no additional experimenter and no punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No mention of third-party rewards in any condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,6,"Each forced-choice trial divides 6 tokens between dictator and recipient (e.g., 3/3, 4/2, 5/1).",1,0.4,"Younger Baseline 6–7-year-olds chose fair option M=.19; older 8–9 Baseline M=.64. Overall Baseline proportion not given, but approximate combined mean around (0.19+0.64)/2≈0.415; rounded to 0.4 as rough estimate of fraction given to fairness. Very rough.",0.2,N/R,"Only proportions of choosing fairer option reported, not average tokens given.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff vs maximum payoff ratios reported.,0,N/R,No total tokens/Skittles per pair or condition reported.,0,United States,"Study conducted at Boston College (U.S.), recruiting in local labs, parks, museums, zoo presumably in U.S.",0.9,6–9 years (two groups: 6–7 and 8–9; overall sample M≈84 and 106 months in age groups across conditions),“Six to nine-year-old children (N=121)” split into 6–7 and 8–9 groups.,1,"Approximately 50% female (60 female, 61 male) across all conditions","Overall sample is 60 female, 61 male; allocation by condition not specified.",0.7,Primary school children (elementary-school age); specific grades N/R,Ages 6–9 correspond to primary school; schooling level not directly reported.,0.6,On site,"Data collected in laboratory space, public parks, museums, and a zoo with experimenters present.",1,Forced-choice dictator game with six trials. Each trial offers a choice between two distributions of 6 tokens between dictator and an absent peer: Trials 1–2: 3/3 vs 4/2; Trials 3–4: 3/3 vs 5/1; Trials 5–6: 4/2 vs 5/1. Tokens exchangeable for Skittles at end; recipients later paid according to actual allocations.,Summarized from ‘Dictator Game’ and procedure sections.,1
10.1037_dev0001183.md,Direct Feedback condition – third‑party punisher,Paper labels one experimental condition as ‘Direct Feedback condition’ where third party can punish.,1,"Third-party role: present adult punisher with costly punishment ability; after each choice, punisher can accept (green box) or punish (red box) dictator decisions.",Independent variable is presence/type of third party; this row corresponds to punisher condition.,1,True,Human children making incentivized decisions.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions with controlled intervention (punishment possibility).,1,True,"Conducted in controlled on-site environments (lab, parks, museums, zoo) with experimenters.",0.8,False,No non-human simulations used.,1,False,No stand-alone analytical model.,1,2,Dictator plus one absent recipient per game; third-party punisher is not a payoff player in the allocation but is an additional role.,0.9,6,Same six dictator game trials as other conditions.,1,0,Forced choice between two fixed allocations every trial.,1,0,Dictator chooses allocation of tokens; no initial assignment to public pot.,0.7,N/A,"Not a public good; dictator allocation, no multiplier.",1,0,Punisher and participant were told not to speak throughout the game; no communication with recipient.,1,0,"Child observes only own decision, punisher’s box action, and own/recipient bags; no aggregate summaries.",0.9,1,Punisher is physically present and known; child observes who punishes them.,1,0,No reward mechanism; punisher can only accept or punish.,1,0,No indication that total number of trials/remaining trials was displayed.,0.7,1,Direct Feedback condition explicitly includes costly punishment by third party.,1,1,"Each punishment requires punisher to put one of her own tokens into red box, which cannot be exchanged for Skittles; cost is 1 token per punishment.",1,1,"One token in red box results in dictator losing all their tokens for that trial (participant’s tokens removed, partner still receives theirs). Effective loss equals the dictator’s chosen amount (3–5), but standard unit mapping is 1 punisher token triggers full removal; set tech to 1 as one ‘unit’ of punishment action.",0.4,0,Punisher has option to accept (green box) but this is not an added reward over baseline; no separate costly reward described.,0.8,N/A,No explicit third-party reward scheme.,1,N/A,No explicit third-party reward scheme.,1,6,Same forced-choice dictator game structure with 6 tokens per trial.,1,0.8,"For Direct Feedback, younger group M=.84 and older group M=.77 on choosing fairer option; approximate overall ~0.8.",0.7,N/R,"Paper reports only proportion of fairer choices, not average tokens to recipient.",0.2,N/R,No total payoffs or efficiency ratios reported by condition.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoffs reported by condition.,0,United States,Study at Boston College with local testing sites in U.S.,0.9,6–9 years (two groups: 6–7 and 8–9) within Direct Feedback condition; exact n per condition not reported,Same sample frame; allocation across conditions not broken out but all conditions draw from 6–9-year-olds.,0.9,Approximately balanced male/female overall; distribution by condition N/R,"Total sample 60 female, 61 male; per-condition breakdown not given.",0.6,Primary school children (elementary-school age); specific grades N/R,Ages 6–9; schooling level not specified.,0.6,On site,Same data collection settings as other conditions.,1,"Six forced-choice dictator game trials with an adult female third-party punisher present. After each choice between 3/3 vs 4/2, 3/3 vs 5/1, or 4/2 vs 5/1, punisher chooses to accept by placing one of her tokens into green box (both players receive chosen tokens) or punish by placing token into red box (recipient still receives tokens, dictator’s tokens for that trial removed). Punisher’s red-box tokens are forfeited and cannot be redeemed for Skittles.",Summarized from Direct Feedback condition description.,1
