10.1016_j.jtbi.2006.05.004.md,Analytical evolutionary game: UA/ER/UD with involuntary defection,"The section 'Properties of the ER strategy' defines and analyzes a game among Unconditional Altruists (UA), Empathic Retaliators (ER), and Unconditional Defectors (UD) under parameters q, c, μ.",1,"Strategy type in population (UA, ER, UD); parameters: probability of being able to help q (0<q<1), relative cost of helping c (0<c<1), expected number of interaction cycles μ>0 modeled via Poisson distribution","Payoff matrix and replicator dynamics are expressed as functions of strategy type and parameters q, c, μ.",1,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical model and payoff matrices.,1,False,No controlled trial or data collection; only mathematical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory implementation; entirely analytical.,1,False,"Although Markov processes and Poisson distributions are used, results are given in closed form, not via numerical/agent-based simulations.",0.9,True,Derives closed-form payoff matrices and replicator equations using Markov process and game-theoretic analysis.,1,2,Direct reciprocity modeled as repeated interactions between a pair of individuals.,1,0,"Number of interaction cycles per lifespan is Poisson distributed with mean μ; no fixed finite number is specified, so a concrete round count cannot be given.",0.2,1,"Each 'round' is a binary help/defect decision; no continuous contribution levels are defined, so treat as standard binary PD-like cooperation rather than all-or-nothing contribution game.",0.4,N/A,No endowment or public-good structure; only binary help decisions in a Prisoner’s Dilemma–style model.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or interface; communication is not modeled.,1,0,"Model assumes perfect knowledge of past actions as part of the strategy state, but does not describe interface feedback like earnings summaries.",0.5,N/A,No explicit punishment institution with identity revelation; only strategy-encoded retaliatory defections.,1,N/A,No explicit positive reward mechanism beyond cooperation.,1,0,"Players are evolutionary strategies without explicit information about number of cycles (μ is a model parameter, not agent knowledge).",0.6,1,Retaliatory defection is interpreted as punishment for defection.,0.9,N/R,"Model does not decompose separate cost of punishing; cost c pertains to helping, not to punishing actions specifically.",0.2,N/R,"No explicit linear punishment technology (e.g., points per punishment token) is defined.",1,0,There is no modeled costly prosocial reward separate from standard cooperation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs are modeled as benefits (1) and costs (c) per interaction; no initial endowment is described.,1,N/R,"No explicit contributions or normalized contribution rate are reported; outcomes are evolutionary stability conditions, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,"Model only specifies binary help decisions and expected payoffs, not contribution quantities.",1,N/R,The paper does not compute aggregate efficiency ratios; it focuses on ESS conditions and payoffs per strategy.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are given per-player per-interaction and per-lifespan, not as an aggregate group payoff measure.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical evolutionary game with Markov processes; no empirical setting.,1,"Infinite-horizon direct reciprocity modeled as a Markov process with Poisson-distributed number of interaction cycles (mean μ). Strategies: Unconditional Altruist (always helps when able), Unconditional Defector (never helps), Empathic Retaliator (retaliates for defections except when opponent’s defection was preceded by own involuntary defection). Payoffs depend on probability of being able to help q, cost of helping c, and derived quantities θ, ρ, γ. Evolutionary stability analyzed via replicator dynamics.",Summary of model structure from Section 1 and Appendix.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2006.05.004.md,Analytical evolutionary game: UA/TFT/UD with involuntary defection,Section 2 analyzes a UA/TFT/UD game under IVD with a specific payoff matrix and ESS conditions.,1,"Strategy type in population (UA, TFT, UD); parameters: probability of being able to help q (0<q<1), relative cost of helping c (0<c<1), expected number of interaction cycles μ>0 via Poisson distribution","The payoff matrix for UA, TFT, and UD and subsequent ESS conditions depend on q, c, μ.",1,False,No empirical data; only theoretical payoffs and ESS analysis.,1,False,Not an experiment but a theoretical game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,Uses analytic expressions rather than numerical or agent-based simulations.,0.9,True,Derives closed-form payoff matrix and ESS conditions using Markov processes and Poisson lifespans.,1,2,"Again, direct reciprocity between pairs of individuals.",1,0,Number of interaction cycles is Poisson with mean μ; not a fixed finite round count.,0.2,1,Interactions are binary help/defect events; no graded contributions.,0.4,N/A,No endowment or public good; just PD-like help decisions.,1,N/R,Not a public goods framework.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,0,"Information is embedded in strategies’ memory of past interactions, not in explicit summaries.",0.5,N/A,No explicit punishment institution with identity revelation.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents are strategies in an evolutionary model; they are not told the number of rounds.,0.6,1,"TFT implements unconditional retaliation through defections following opponent defection, interpreted as punishment.",0.9,N/R,No separate cost for punishing is parametrized; costs are for helping only.,0.2,N/R,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No costly reward distinct from cooperation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No initial endowment is modeled; only per-interaction payoffs.,1,N/R,"Theoretical analysis of ESS, not of contribution fractions.",1,N/R,No token-based contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,"Efficiency measures are not reported; focus is on strategy stability conditions (χ1, χ2, χ3).",1,N/R,Group-level aggregate payoffs are not separately quantified.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game only.,1,"Infinite-horizon direct reciprocity game with involuntary defection. Strategies: Unconditional Altruist (UA), Tit-for-Tat (TFT), and Unconditional Defector (UD). Payoff matrix given in Eq. (5) parameterized by q, c, μ with derived quantities v and σ. ESS analysis yields conditions under which UA⊕TFT mixed strategy, TFT alone, or UD are evolutionarily stable, with χ1, χ2, χ3 functions of μ and q. Shows that stable cooperation via TFT and forbearing variants is unlikely in presence of IVD and mutations.",Condensed from Section 2 description of UA/TFT/UD with IVD.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2006.05.004.md,Analytical evolutionary game: SR/GI (Sympathetic Retaliator vs. Genuine Incapacity),Section 2 introduces a game between sympathetic retaliators (SR) and a genuinely incapacitated strategy (GI) and notes its payoff matrix equivalence to UA/UD.,0.95,"Strategy type (SR vs. GI); parameters implicitly same as UA/UD game: probability of being able to help q (0<q<1), cost of helping c (0<c<1), expected number of interaction cycles μ>0","SR/GI game is said to be identical in payoff structure to the UA/UD game referenced in Eq. (5), which uses q, c, μ.",0.8,False,No empirical observations; purely theoretical.,1,False,"Model comparison only, no experimental manipulation on subjects.",1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,False,No computational simulation reported; equality to UA/UD matrix is analytic.,0.9,True,The SR/GI game is analyzed conceptually by mapping to an existing analytical payoff matrix.,1,2,Game is between a pair: SR vs GI.,1,0,"As with other games, interactions are repeated with Poisson-distributed cycles; exact finite round count not set.",0.2,1,"Interactions represent binary help/defect decisions, like the UA/UD game.",0.5,N/A,No public-good style endowment or contribution fraction defined.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,N/A,No agents or interface.,1,0,Model does not discuss feedback displays; only strategy rules.,0.5,N/A,"No explicit punishment institution; SR uses selective retaliation, but identity revelation is irrelevant in a 2-player analytical model.",1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond cooperation.,1,0,Agents are evolutionary strategies; they are not modeled as observing number of rounds.,0.6,1,"SR is defined as a retaliatory strategy that punishes voluntary defectors but not involuntary ones, i.e., includes punishment.",0.9,N/R,No separate per-unit cost of punishment is parameterized; costs relate to helping.,0.2,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment tokens to payoffs.,1,0,No costly reward distinct from