10.1016_j.chaos.2023.114031.md,"Numerical simulation with NT=5, δ=0.02 (low punishment)","Condition described in numerical examples for NT>1 with specific parameter set Ni=10, Nt=5, r=0.8, R=10, T=5, δ=0.02 corresponding to Fig. 2.",0.93,"Punishment fine δ set to 0.02 with NT=5 (case 0 < δ ≤ (1−r)/NT, low punishment regime)",Text explicitly varies punishment fine δ and analyzes dynamics for particular values; this row is for δ=0.02.,0.95,False,Paper states it is a theoretical model with numerical simulations and that no data was used; no human participants.,1,False,"No controlled human or field experiment, only mathematical analysis and simulations.",1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational and analytical.,1,True,Section 3.2 explicitly presents numerical examples (simulations) of the dynamical system.,1,True,"Model is defined via payoff functions and replicator equations with theorems and proofs, i.e., formal analytical work plus simulations.",1,15,"NTG group defined by NI investors and NT trustees. For this condition, NI=10 and NT=5 so N=15.",0.95,10,"Expected number of rounds R=1/(1−ω). For parameter set, R=10 with ω=0.9 is explicitly stated.",0.98,1,"Investors pay 1 to each trustee each round; no indication of partial or continuous choice, so contributions are discrete fixed payments, not a choice over a range. However, this variable is defined for contribution flexibility; here no choice, so treat as can’t choose all-or-nothing. Using 1 to indicate not all-or-nothing would be misleading; given ambiguity, we mark as 1 since contributions are fixed per interaction, not binary per group account.",0.3,N/A,No human subjects and no public-goods style endowment allocation; model uses fixed payment of 1 from investor to each trustee per round.,1,N/R,"This is a trust game, not a standard public goods game; no marginal per capita return on a group account is defined.",1,N/A,No human players or communication protocol; purely evolutionary dynamics.,1,0,S