10.1016_j.physleta.2022.128138.md,"Mean-field evolutionary PD with quasi-defection (replicator dynamics, numerical analysis across parameter space)","Paper presents a single analytical/numerical model of a well-mixed infinite population with three strategies (C, D, QD) and analyzes evolution for various parameter settings (b, alpha, beta, gamma, delta). There are no distinct labeled experiments beyond parameter sweeps of the same model.",0.9,"Temptation b, cost α, reward β, punishment γ, lost benefit δ; initial condition x(0)=0.5, y(0)=0.25, z(0)=0.25 when specified. Paper varies these parameters to study cooperation levels and equilibria.","Model description and results section explicitly state varying b, α, β, γ, δ and using a specific initial condition for time series (Fig. 1 etc.).",1,False,No human or animal participants; purely theoretical and numerical analysis of ODEs.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a theoretical evolutionary game model analyzed analytically and via numerical integration.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; only mathematical/simulation work.,1,True,Section 3 states numerical simulation results; Appendix B details numerical integration using Runge-Kutta methods for the replicator dynamics.,1,True,Section 2 derives replicator equations and Section 3 and Appendix A provide stability analysis and equilibria analytically.,1,0,"Model assumes an infinitely large, well-mixed population; not a finite-player discrete game implementation.",1,0,Continuous-time replicator dynamics in an infinite horizon; no discrete rounds.,1,1,"Strategies are discrete (C, D, QD) but there is no partial contribution decision variable like in a PGG; the notion of contribution amount/all-or-nothing is not applicable, but following instructions we mark 1 for general freedom and explain.",0.4,N/A,Not a public goods contribution game with an endowment; model is a 2-player PD with fixed payoffs and infinite population dynamics.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is specific to public goods games; this is a prisoner's dilemma with specified payoffs b, β, γ, δ, α; no MPCR defined.",1,N/A,No human subjects or interface; communication not defined.,1,0,No information presentation to human players; model only defines payoffs and dynamics. Marked 0 as not used.,1,N/A,No identities or observation structure; infinite anonymous population.,1,N/A,No identities or observation structure; infinite anonymous population.,1,N/A,No human interface; continuous-time dynamics.,1,1,Quasi-defectors pay cost α to impose punishment γ on defectors; γ is the punishment parameter.,1,N/A,"Parameter α is both cost to reward and to punish for quasi-defectors; model not framed in tokens/coins, so per-unit cost in experimental tokens is not applicable.",1,N/A,Punishment parameter γ directly appears in payoff; not expressed as a technology mapping tokens to fines.,1,1,Quasi-defectors reward cooperators with benefit β while paying cost α.,1,N/A,Same α as general cost; not expressed as coin cost per reward unit.,1,N/A,Reward β is directly parameterized in payoff matrix; not a technology translating coins to benefits.,1,N/A,"No per-round endowment defined; payoffs are directly specified as R, S, T, P-like values (1, 0, b, -δ, etc.).",1,N/R,"Outcome of interest is proportion of cooperators, defectors, and quasi-defectors and their payoffs; there is no contribution decision variable.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions are chosen; only discrete strategies with fixed payoffs.,1,N/R,Paper reports fractions of strategies and qualitative payoffs; does not present normalized efficiency measure as defined in instructions.,0.6,N/R,"Average payoff expressions for strategies and population are given, but no single scalar reported as a central DV per condition.",0.6,N/A,No human participants; simulation/analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of mathematical analysis and computer-based numerical integration of ODEs without subjects.,1,"Infinite, well-mixed population playing a 2-player prisoner's dilemma extended with a quasi-defection (QD) strategy. Payoff matrix: C vs C: 1, C vs D: -δ, C vs QD: β-δ; D vs C: b, D vs D: 0, D vs QD: -γ; QD vs C: b-α, QD vs D: -α, QD vs QD: 0. Dynamics governed by replicator equations for frequencies x (C), y (D), z (QD) with z=1−x−y. Parameters: temptation b>1, cost α (0≤α≤1), reward β, punishment γ, lost benefit δ. Stability analysis identifies six equilibria including full D, full QD, coexistence of C and QD, and coexistence of all three strategies, depending on inequalities among b, α, β, γ, δ. Numerical integration via adaptive Runge-Kutta (4th/5th order) explores time evolution and stationary fractions across parameter sweeps.","Summarized from model description, payoff matrix table, and analytical derivations in Sections 2–3 and Appendix A–B.",1
