10.1016_j.econlet.2018.07.043.md,Standard treatment – Prisoner’s Dilemma with third-party punishment,"Paper states there are two treatments: the ""Standard"" and ""Visibility"" treatments. This row captures the Standard treatment.",1,"Visibility of third-party punishment: punishment decisions not observed by any other punisher (standard, low-visibility condition)","The independent variable manipulated between treatments is whether a third party punisher’s action choice is made known to another punisher. In the Standard treatment, it is not.",1,True,"Laboratory experiment with 96 student participants, 48 in this treatment.",1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the EXEC laboratory at the University of York.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,"While theory is discussed, the reported results are from a lab experiment, not a purely analytical model.",1,3,Each group has two PD players and one third party player.,1,1,The PD interaction is described as a one-shot game; no repetition within treatment is mentioned.,0.9,0,"PD players choose a discrete binary action (send 10 or not). This is not a continuous or multi-level contribution choice, so coded as not an all-or-nothing contribution game in the usual sense of public goods contributions.",0.6,0,Each PD player is endowed with 25 points privately and can choose to send 10; initial allocation is fully in the private account.,1,N/R,"This is a prisoner's dilemma, not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return to a continuous contribution is defined.",0.7,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous PD lab setup and focus on punishment visibility only.,0.9,0,Paper does not report feedback of others’ earnings or punishment summaries; only that realized punishment is applied. No evidence of aggregate or detailed summaries.,0.6,0,Identities of all subjects are not disclosed; no linkage of punishment to personal identity for PD players.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,One-shot interaction; paper does not mention displaying number of rounds.,0.8,1,Third party players have an endowment of 40 and can assign punishment points.,1,1,"For each punishment point assigned, one point is deducted from the punisher’s payoff.",1,3,"For each punishment point, three points are deducted from the target’s payoff.",1,0,Only punishment is described; no positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,25,Each PD player is endowed with 25 points at Stage 1; third parties have 40 but the main social dilemma endowment is 25.,1,0.719,PD players’ cooperation rate was 71.9% (23 out of 32) and was the same for both treatments; this is the fraction choosing to send 10 out of 10.,1,10,Cooperation is defined as sending 10 points; this is the contribution amount when cooperating.,1,N/R,The paper does not report total payoffs relative to maximum possible payoff in a normalized 0–1 efficiency metric.,0.3,N/R,Average or total group payoff is not reported numerically.,0.2,United Kingdom,"Experiment took place at the University of York, UK; participants are students there.",0.95,N/R,Only described as students; no age range reported.,0.2,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0.0,University students,Explicitly states a total of 96 students participated.,1,On site,Conducted at a physical laboratory (EXEC lab at University of York) using z-Tree.,1,"One-shot 2-player prisoner’s dilemma with actions Cooperate (send 10) or Defect (send 0). Payoffs in Stage 1: if both cooperate, each gets 15 (keep) + 30 (received) = 45; if i cooperates and j defects, i gets 15 and j gets 55; if both defect, each gets 25. A third party with 40 points can assign 0–20 punishment points per PD player under four scenarios (CC, DC, CD, DD) using the strategy method; each punishment point costs 1 to the punisher and 3 to the target.",Summarizes structural details of the PD and punishment stage as described in Sections 2.1–2.2.,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2018.07.043.md,Visibility treatment – Prisoner’s Dilemma with third-party punishment and visible sanctions,"Paper identifies a second treatment, the ""Visibility"" treatment, differing from the Standard treatment in the visibility of punishment behavior to another punisher.",1,Visibility of third-party punishment: each punisher’s punishment decisions are observed by a randomly matched punisher in another group (high-visibility condition),"In the Visibility treatment, each third party player is paired with another punisher and their respective punishment behavior is made known to the partner; this is the manipulated condition.",1,True,Same laboratory experiment with human student participants; 48 subjects in this treatment.,1,True,Controlled between-subjects design with random assignment to visibility condition.,1,True,Conducted in the EXEC laboratory at the University of York.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,Results reported are empirical; theory is supplemental.,1,3,Same group structure as Standard: two PD players and one third party player per group.,1,1,Described as a one-shot PD game; no repetition of the PD within a treatment.,0.9,0,Action is a binary cooperate/defect choice rather than a continuous contribution decision.,0.6,0,PD players receive 25 points privately; no initial allocation to a public account.,1,N/R,PD structure does not define a marginal per capita return as in standard linear public goods games.,0.7,0,No mention of communication or chat; only visibility of punishment to another punisher is manipulated.,0.9,0,No description of feedback summarizing others’ payoffs or punishments; only that a paired punisher directly observes punishment choices.,0.6,0,Identities are not disclosed in this experiment; visibility is anonymized between punishers and not identity-revealing to PD players.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present.,1,0,One-shot interaction; no mention that the number of rounds or remaining rounds is displayed.,0.8,1,Same third-party punishment mechanism as in Standard treatment.,1,1,"Each punishment point costs the punisher 1 point, same as in Standard treatment.",1,3,Each punishment point reduces the target’s payoff by 3 points.,1,0,Only punishment is used; no rewarding option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this treatment.,1,25,Same PD structure: each PD player has 25-point endowment; third party has 40 points for punishment decisions.,1,0.719,The paper states that PD players’ cooperation rates were the same for the two treatments at 71.9% (23 out of 32 PD players).,1,10,Cooperation involves sending 10 points in this treatment as well.,1,N/R,Efficiency as a ratio of realized to maximum possible payoffs is not reported.,0.3,N/R,"Although expected payoffs under different strategies are reported, an average realized group payoff is not.",0.2,United Kingdom,"Experiment conducted at the University of York, UK; participants are students there.",0.95,N/R,"Only indicates student status, no age data provided.",0.2,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0.0,University students,"States that 96 students participated, split across treatments.",1,On site,Conducted in a physical computer lab (EXEC laboratory) with z-Tree.,1,"Same one-shot 2-player prisoner’s dilemma with a third party as in the Standard treatment. The Visibility treatment is identical except that each third party is randomly and anonymously paired with another third party in a different group; they act independently toward different PD players but each observes the other’s punishment behavior. Identities of all subjects remain anonymous. Punishment is decided by the strategy method for four scenarios (CC, DC, CD, DD), with 0–20 punishment points available; each point costs 1 to the punisher and 3 to the target.","Summarizes modifications to the Standard treatment, as described in Section 2.2.",1
