10.1177_001872677002300105.md,"Segment III – Pre-talk (no communication, reward and punishment available)","Condition defined in Table 1 as Segment III (Pre-talk): reward (+) and punishment (−) buttons on, communication off, cooperative and individual task payoffs specified.",1,"Communication absent; B has unilateral reward (+) and punishment (−) options; cooperative payoff (A,B)=(0.1,0.7) cents per coordinated pull; individual payoff (A,B)=(0.2,0.2) cents per pull.",Segment III differs from V and VI only by absence of communication while keeping payoffs and influence buttons identical.,1,True,Human undergraduate participants worked on tasks for money.,1,True,"Conditions (payoffs, communication, reward/punishment availability) were experimentally manipulated in a controlled lab setting.",1,True,Conducted in experimental rooms at the University of Wisconsin with apparatus.,1,False,No mention of computer or agent-based simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No formal mathematical model or analytical derivations as a primary method.,1,2,"Study explicitly refers to dyads, subjects A and B.",1,0,Continuous free-operant responding over a 50-minute segment; no discrete rounds defined.,1,1,Subjects could pull the plunger at any time to earn on individual or cooperative task; not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.,0.9,0,"This is not a standard public goods endowment structure; subjects earn money via responses, so no initial endowment is placed in a public fund. By convention set to 0 (all in private).",0.4,N/R,"No group public-good return defined; payoffs are individual and differ by task, not via a linear public goods multiplier.",0,0,Table 1 indicates communication between subjects is off in Segment III.,1,0,"Apparatus had counters showing each subject’s own and the other’s cumulative earnings, but no mention of additional summaries or histories; coded as 0.",0.7,1,Dyadic interaction with fixed partners A and B; any change in counters due to B’s button press is necessarily attributable to that identified partner.,0.9,1,Same reasoning as for punishment: transfers are dyadic and attributable to B.,0.9,0,"No rounds; subjects see counters and lights, but no indication of remaining time or trials is described.",0.9,1,"Segment III: (−) button is on for B, allowing subtraction from A’s counter.",1,0,Pressing (−) subtracts one count from A’s total with no cost to B; explicitly noted that it costs B to reward but not to punish.,1,1,Each press of (−) reduces A’s counter by exactly one monetary unit (1 count).,1,1,"Segment III: (+) button is on for B, allowing transfers to A.",1,1,Pressing (+) subtracts one count from B and adds one count to A; thus cost per unit to B is 1 count.,1,1,Each unit of cost to B (one count) yields one count of benefit to A.,1,0,No initial lump-sum endowment; subjects start from zero and earn over time.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as proportion of time on cooperative vs individual tasks and amounts rewarded/punished, not normalized contributions.",0,N/R,No raw number of cooperative responses per subject for Segment III is reported in the text or tables.,0,N/R,No ratio of actual to maximal possible payoffs is reported or derivable without detailed response counts.,0,N/R,Cumulative earnings by segment are not provided.,0,USA,Subjects were undergraduates at the University of Wisconsin (USA).,1,Undergraduate freshmen and sophomores; exact age N/R,"Described as undergraduate freshmen and sophomores; typical age range early 18–20, but no exact ages.",0.7,"Dyads: 18 male pairs, 12 female pairs; no mixed-gender pairs.",Subjects section states 12 of the pairs were females and 18 were males.,1,University undergraduates (freshmen and sophomores),Explicitly stated in Subjects section.,1,On site,Participants were seated in adjacent experimental rooms with physical apparatus; classic lab setting.,1,"Continuous dyadic task-choice game over 50 minutes. Individual task: each reinforced response gives 0.2 cents to responder, followed by 5-second timeout. Cooperative task: reinforcement only for coordinated pulls within 0.5 s, giving 0.1 cents to A and 0.7 cents to B per cooperative response, followed by 5-second timeout. If either subject selects individual, individual task operates for both. B can transfer earnings to A (reward) or subtract from A’s earnings (punishment) at any time within constraints (no more than 80 cents subtracted per segment; reward button disabled when B’s total reaches 0 until he has earned 8 cents).","Summarizes procedural and payoff details from Setting, Procedures, and Table 1 focused on Segment III.",1
10.1177_001872677002300105.md,"Segment V – Talk (communication available, reward and punishment available)","Condition defined in Table 1 as Segment V (Talk): same payoffs and influence options as Segments III and VI, but communication on.",1,"Communication present; B has unilateral reward (+) and punishment (−) options; cooperative payoff (A,B)=(0.1,0.7) cents per coordinated pull; individual payoff (A,B)=(0.2,0.2) cents per pull.",Segment V adds verbal communication to the same underlying task and influence structure as Segment III.,1,True,"Same human dyads, now allowed to communicate verbally.",1,True,Communication is an experimentally manipulated situational variable between segments.,1,True,Same lab environment and apparatus.,1,False,No simulation used.,1,False,No analytical-only treatment.,1,2,Same dyadic structure as other segments.,1,0,50-minute continuous segment with free-operant responding.,1,1,Same continuous response opportunity as Segment III.,0.9,0,No initial public endowment; same structure as Segment III.,0.4,N/R,No linear public goods payoff structure reported.,0,1,Table 1 marks communication between subjects as on in Segment V.,1,0,Only individual counters described; no extra summaries.,0.7,1,Dyadic setting with identified partner B controlling punishment.,0.9,1,B’s identity as the transfer source is evident to A.,0.9,0,No rounds or explicit time remaining displays are mentioned.,0.9,1,Segment V: (−) button on for B.,1,0,Unchanged: B pays nothing per unit of punishment.,1,1,Each press subtracts one count from A.,1,1,Segment V: (+) button on for B.,1,1,Each press moves one count from B to A.,1,1,One count of benefit to A per count of cost to B.,1,0,Same earn-as-you-go structure.,1,N/R,"Reported DV is proportion of time on cooperation, but only by sex and across strategies; no segment-specific per-condition normalized contributions.",0.3,N/R,No raw counts of cooperative responses in Segment V are provided.,0,N/R,No efficiency metric reported or derivable without detailed timing data.,0,N/R,Segment-level cumulative earnings not reported.,0,USA,Same participants as entire study (University of Wisconsin undergraduates).,1,Undergraduate freshmen and sophomores; exact age N/R,Same subject pool as other segments.,0.7,"Dyads: 18 male pairs, 12 female pairs; no mixed-gender pairs.",Same composition across segments.,1,University undergraduates (freshmen and sophomores),Unchanged.,1,On site,Same lab setting.,1,"Identical to Segment III in payoff and influence structure, but subjects can communicate verbally throughout the 50-minute segment. Communication often used initially to discuss task contingencies and to propose agreements, then shifts to unrelated topics. Seven male groups and one female group first achieved steady-state cooperation during this segment, typically soon after reaching verbal agreement and beginning monetary transfers.","Summarizes Segment V details, including timing and role of communication, from Results and Discussion.",1
10.1177_001872677002300105.md,"Segment VI – Post-talk (no communication, reward and punishment available)","Condition defined in Table 1 as Segment VI (Post-talk): same as III and V except communication off; follows talk segment for most groups, but order reversed for six male groups.",1,"Communication absent; B has unilateral reward (+) and punishment (−) options; cooperative payoff (A,B)=(0.1,0.7) cents per coordinated pull; individual payoff (A,B)=(0.2,0.2) cents per pull; conducted after at least one prior exposure to the same payoff structure with/without communication.",Segment VI removes communication after prior segments while keeping same payoff and influence conditions.,1,True,Same human subjects continued into Segment VI.,1,True,Segment order and communication availability are experimental manipulations.,1,True,Same apparatus and rooms.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,No purely analytical study.,1,2,Dyadic A–B interaction.,1,0,50-minute continuous free-operant segment.,1,1,Same continuous response structure as other segments.,0.9,0,Same earn-as-you-go design; no initial public endowment.,0.4,N/R,No public goods multiplier defined.,0,0,Table 1 specifies communication off for Segment VI.,1,0,No additional summaries beyond counters mentioned.,0.7,1,Fixed dyad; B uniquely controls punishment on A.,0.9,1,Transfers are attributable to B in a two-person interaction.,0.9,0,No explicit display of total or remaining time/trials reported.,0.9,1,Segment VI: (−) button on for B.,1,0,"Punishment free to B, as in other segments.",1,1,One count deducted from A per punishment unit.,1,1,Segment VI: (+) button on.,1,1,One count cost to B per unit of reward.,1,1,One count benefit to A per unit cost to B.,1,0,No initial monetary endowment; earnings accumulate from zero.,1,N/R,"Although cooperation in talk and post-talk segments is said to be highly correlated, exact proportions of time on cooperation specific to Segment VI are not tabulated.",0.2,N/R,No raw counts of cooperative responses in Segment VI.,0,N/R,No efficiency measure given.,0,N/R,No segment-level earnings totals.,0,USA,Same US undergraduate pool.,1,Undergraduate freshmen and sophomores; exact age N/R,Same sample.,0.7,"Dyads: 18 male pairs, 12 female pairs; no mixed-gender pairs.",Same composition for all segments.,1,University undergraduates (freshmen and sophomores),Unchanged.,1,On site,Conducted in the same lab rooms.,1,"Same game as Segments III and V. Segment VI assessed whether cooperative and influence behaviors established during communication would persist once communication was removed. For all but one group, cooperative behavior and amounts given/taken in talk vs post-talk were highly similar (correlations: cooperation 0.98, amount given 0.97, amount taken 0.67).",Summarizes Segment VI’s role as a post-communication test drawing on Results section.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2011.01.005.md,Extortion game – principal–agent repeated game (single treatment),Paper reports a single experimental treatment: all pairs play the same 15-round extortion game with fixed roles and parameters; no separate control/treatment variants are described.,1,N/A,No independent variable is experimentally varied across conditions; only one game structure is used for all subjects.,1,True,Human participants (students) played the game in a computer laboratory.,1,True,Controlled laboratory economic experiment with random matching and fixed rules.,1,True,Conducted in the computer laboratory of European University Viadrina.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper includes theoretical predictions but the main study is an experiment with human subjects, not a pure analytical model study.",1,2,Each pair consists of one principal P and one agent A; 46 pairs formed from 92 subjects.,1,15,"Game is a finitely repeated game with T=15 rounds, explicitly stated multiple times.",1,1,"Principal can choose any demand x in [0,160] and any punishment y in [0,160], i.e., contributions/transfers are continuous within range, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,"All endowment (40 for P, 160 for A) starts in private accounts; nothing is pre-allocated to a public fund or transfer.",1,N/R,This is not a public goods game; no group multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,Subjects were not allowed to communicate; terminals were partitioned and no communication channels mentioned.,1,1,"Standard z-tree implementation with stage feedback is implied; subjects must see at least the other’s realized actions and payoffs to respond in subsequent rounds, but explicit description of summary screens not given. Assume they observe outcomes each round.",0.5,0,Matching is fixed and anonymous; each subject only knows their counterpart as A or B but not real identity. Punishments are observed in the pair but not linked to real identities beyond role labels.,0.75,0,No reward mechanism exists in the game; hence no reward identities can be shown.,1,1,"Time horizon T=15 is common knowledge; typical lab protocols show current round/remaining rounds, and econometric models use R_t=15−t, implying subjects knew the number of rounds left.",0.9,1,"After a rejection, P may punish A by choosing y in [0,160].",1,10,"If P chooses y>0, her payoff is reduced by 10 relative to no punishment (40 becomes 30), independent of y.",1,1,"Each punishment decision y destroys exactly y points of A’s income (π_A = 160 − y after rejection and y>0), i.e., 1 point destroyed per unit from P’s perspective.",1,0,Game only includes costly punishment (destroying A’s points); no option to reward or increase A’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,200,"Each round P has 40 and A has 160, totaling 200 per pair per round; since there are heterogeneous roles, total per game instance is reported.",1,N/R,"Paper reports demands, acceptance rates, punishment frequencies, and incomes, but not normalized transfer rates as a fraction of endowment.",0,N/R,"Demands (x) are the requested transfers, and accepted demands represent actual transfers, but no single aggregate average x per round over whole experiment is explicitly provided, only some conditional averages.",0.3,0.805,"Maximum combined earnings per pair over 15 rounds are €30 (2000 points if 1 cent=1 point); average combined earnings reported as €24.14, i.e., 24.14/30 ≈ 0.8047.",1,24.14,Average combined earnings per pair reported as €24.14 for the experiment.,1,Germany,"Experiment conducted at European University Viadrina in Frankfurt (Oder), Germany; subject pool is its students.",1,N/R,Only described as students from various faculties; no specific age range or mean age reported.,0,N/R,Paper does not report gender composition of the sample.,0,"University students (Cultural Science, Business and Economics, Law)",Subjects recruited from an email list of students from those faculties.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical computer laboratory with participants present in person.,1,"Two-player finitely repeated extortion game with fixed roles (P, A), 15 rounds, P endowed with 40 cents, A with 160 cents each round. Stage 1: P chooses demand x∈[0,160]. Stage 2: A accepts (P:40+x, A:160−x) or rejects. Stage 3 (after rejection): P chooses punishment y∈[0,160]; if y=0 then (P:40, A:160); if y>0 then (P:30, A:160−y). Matching is fixed for all 15 rounds; payoffs converted to euros (average €12.07).",Summarizes explicit game description and procedure from Section 2.,1
