10.1016_j.physa.2015.08.053.md,"Agent-based simulation – meta-norms game without social vaccine, varying T and E","Section 3.2 presents agent-based simulations of the meta-norms game without social vaccine, varying temptation T and enforcement cost E, summarized in Figs. 5 and 6.",0.98,"Meta-norms game; No social vaccine; Fixed parameters: N=20, H=-1, P=MP=-9, generations=10^4, mutation rate=0.01; Operational parameters: T ∈ {1,…,6}, E=ME ∈ {-6,…,-1}; Initial b_i and v_i uniform in [0,1]. This row represents all runs without social vaccine across the T and E grid.","Sec. 3.2 explicitly defines fixed and operational parameters and notes results across varied T and E without social vaccine, in Fig. 6.",0.9,False,"Agent-based simulation of artificial agents, no humans.",1,False,Computational experiment but not an empirical human experiment; schema’s METHOD_experiment refers to empirical controlled experiments.,0.9,False,"Simulation, not a physical lab or field setting.",1,True,Described as an agent-based simulation with GA updating over generations.,1,False,"This section uses numerical simulation, not derivations, to verify effects of parameter values.",1,20,Sec. 3.2: “The fixed parameters consist of the population size (N=20) …”.,1,4,Sec. 3.1: “Each agent plays the following process four times” per generation.,0.9,1,Agents choose to cooperate or defect in each interaction; binary choice coded as 1 (can choose either).,0.7,N/A,"No explicit endowment or default contributions, and no human players.",1,N/R,"Not a standard linear public goods game with contribution multiplier; payoffs encoded via T, H, E, P, etc.",1,N/A,No communication channel (and no humans).,1,N/R,Agents only see defections/punishments via probabilistic observation s; no summary interface.,0.9,N/R,Model assumes agents know who they are paired with but does not treat identity display as a parameter.,0.8,N/A,No reward or positive sanctioning.,1,N/R,Agents do not have an interface; they are programmed strategies.,1,1,Simulation implements both punishment and meta-punishment as in meta-norms game.,1,N/R,"While E and ME are varied (e.g., E=ME in [-6,-1]), the per-unit cost parameter in schema is not fixed across this condition; grid covers multiple values so no single number applies.",0.6,9,"P=MP=-9 are fixed, giving a magnitude of 9 cost to target per punishment event.",0.95,0,Only punishment and meta-punishment exist.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Simulation specifies payoffs directly, not via endowments.",1,0,"Text and Fig. 5 show that, in the long run without social vaccine, “the cooperation regimes collapse in all situations” and average boldness (defection probability) tends to 1; thus average contribution fraction tends to 0.",0.8,N/R,No token endowment; only binary cooperate/defect actions.,0.9,N/R,"They discuss collapse of cooperation and lower payoffs qualitatively, but do not provide normalized efficiency values.",0.7,N/R,"No numerical group payoff at final generation over T, E grid is listed.",0.7,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulation.,1,"Agents have strategies (b_i,v_i). In each generation, each agent plays four interaction cycles where they may defect or cooperate based on b_i, detect others’ defection with probability s (uniform [0,1] leading to expected detection proportional to b), punish based on v_i, and possibly meta-punish non-punishers. Evolutionary updating uses roulette selection proportional to squared payoff difference, uniform crossover, and mutation rate 0.01 over 10^4 generations.",Summarized from Sec. 3.1 and 3.2.,0.98
10.1016_j.physa.2015.08.053.md,"Agent-based simulation – meta-norms game with coercive social vaccine, varying T and E","Section 3.2 introduces social vaccine into the agent-based meta-norms simulations, shown in Figs. 7 and 8.",0.98,"Meta-norms game; Coercive introduction of social vaccine (a small number of agents always defect and never punish); Fixed parameters: N=20, H=-1, P=MP=-9, generations=10^4, mutation rate=0.01; Operational parameters: T ∈ {1,…,6}, E=ME ∈ {-6,…,-1}; Initial strategies for non-vaccine agents uniform in [0,1].",Sec. 3.2 describes same fixed and operational parameters as in no-vaccine case but now introduces social vaccine; figures compare with and without vaccine.,0.9,False,Agent-based simulation using artificial agents.,1,False,Computational rather than empirical experiment.,0.9,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,Explicitly an agent-based simulation with evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,"This condition is studied via simulation, not closed-form derivation.",1,20,Population size N=20 is kept as in the earlier simulations; social vaccine is a subset of these agents.,0.95,4,Same per-generation interaction structure: each agent plays the process four times.,0.9,1,Binary cooperate/defect choice in each interaction.,0.7,N/A,No explicit endowments or initial contributions; and no human subjects.,1,N/R,"Payoffs coded via T, H, E, P, etc., not via MPCR.",1,N/A,No human players and no explicit communication channel.,1,N/R,Agents only experience local detection; no centralized summary.,0.9,N/R,Identities not treated as a manipulable feature.,0.8,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Agents do not have round displays.,1,1,First- and second-order punishment remain part of the meta-norms game.,1,N/R,"E and ME vary across runs (grid over [-6,-1]); no single cost value applies to the entire condition.",0.6,9,"P=MP=-9 remain fixed, so each punishment reduces target payoff by 9.",0.95,0,No reward or positive incentives defined.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"As before, no endowments.",1,N/R,"Text indicates “cooperative regimes are achieved” and remain stable (Fig. 7 and 8 show low boldness), but no explicit final average boldness values are reported to translate into a precise contribution fraction across all T and E.",0.5,N/R,No token scale.,0.9,N/R,"Authors state that social vaccine establishes cooperative regimes with high average payoffs, but do not compute an efficiency ratio.",0.7,N/R,"No numerical group payoff outcomes for final generation across T, E grid.",0.7,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based computational experiment.,1,"Same interaction and evolutionary updating as in no-vaccine simulation, but with a social vaccine: some agents (at least one) are forced to adopt a perfect defector non-punisher strategy permanently. This keeps punishment activity and prevents vengefulness from decaying, so cooperative regimes are robust over wide ranges of T and E (as shown in Figs. 7 and 8).",Summarized from Sec. 3.2 discussion of Figs. 7–9.,0.95
