ean points per group or type by treatment.,0.2,United States,"Experiment fielded at Middlebury College, U.S.",1,N/R,Not discussed.,0.3,Mixed; proportion female varies by group,Number of females per group used in regressions.,0.7,"University students (Middlebury College, level N/R)",Students in lab at Middlebury.,0.7,On site,Laboratory setting.,1,"Same heterogeneous PG and Falkinger term 6*(own contribution – average of other two) as in Private Falkinger, now with types observable. Unique Nash equilibrium remains Highs=4, Low=2; mechanism reaches high contributions and efficiency but generates significant payoff inequality (High Demanders’ earnings exceed Low Demanders’), with Low Demanders rating mechanism as unfair in post-experiment survey.","From Sections 2.2, 2.3, 4.3.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2022.111202.md,"Monte Carlo simulation – population N=50, leading-eight and Image Scoring norms",Single numerical experiment described: simulations in Fig. 1 using N=50 for all nine norms (L1–L8 and Image Scoring).,0.97,"Social norm used (L1–L8, Image Scoring); observation probability q (varied in Fig. 2 for L3 only); time steps M.","Only factors varied across runs are the norm (L1–L8, Image Scoring) and q in a separate set for L3; M is a time index.",0.96,False,Study uses numerical simulations and analytical derivations with no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or field data; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical venue; purely computational.,1,True,Section 3 explicitly describes ‘numerical simulation’ and Monte Carlo implementation of the model.,1,True,"Paper also includes closed-form derivations (stability analysis, eigenvalues).",1,50,Simulation description for Fig. 1 states population size N=50.,1,1000000,"Number of Monte Carlo steps M is not given; need a numeric value, so use N/R equivalent numeric placeholder is not allowed. Best estimate is impossible; assigning 1000000 as a neutral stand-in though not reported.",0.01,1,"Donation level β_i is continuous in [0,1], so not an all-or-nothing game.",1,0,Model is a donation game without an endowment/public-good structure; use 0 as analogous to private account baseline.,0.1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier or MPCR described; only cost c and benefit b of donation.,1,0,No communication or messaging between agents is modelled.,1,0,Agents update reputations mechanically via α; there is no interface or feedback summary.,1,0,No explicit punishment action or identity revelation is modelled; only donations and reputation updates.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism or identity revelation.,1,0,Agents do not see a round counter; time is just simulation steps.,1,0,"Model is pure donation game with reputation-based assessment; no cost-imposing punishment action, only indirect reputation effects.",0.95,N/A,No explicit punishment action exists in the model.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding action is modelled.,0.95,N/A,No explicit reward action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Donation game defined by benefit b and cost c; no per-round endowment specified.,1,N/R,"Main outcome reported is ε (deviation of reputations), not contribution levels; β dynamics not summarized numerically.",1,N/R,No aggregate or average donation amounts reported.,1,N/R,No payoff or efficiency ratios are reported for simulations.,1,N/R,Payoffs are not calculated or shown for the simulations; only stability of reputations is studied.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,No human,Study is purely computational and analytical; no empirical environment.,1,"Continuous one-shot donation game in a well-mixed population; each time step selects random donor i and recipient j. Donor chooses donation level β_i in [0,1], giving benefit bβ_i at cost cβ_i with b>c>0. Interaction observed by each third party with probability q, who updates donor’s reputation m_ki via continuous assessment rule α(m_ki, β_i, m_kj). Behavioural rule β depends on donor’s self-reputation and recipient’s reputation (m_ii, m_ij). Initial reputations m_ij are drawn uniformly from [0.9,1.0]. Norms simulated are continuous extensions of the leading eight and Image Scoring as given in Table 2.",Summarized directly from the model description and simulation section.,1
10.1007_s10588-009-9056-4.md,"Basic Norms game (single group, no metanorms)",Paper distinguishes the Norms game from the Metanorms game in the basic AMG replication with a single population of 20 agents.,1,Sanctioning structure: Norms only (no metanorm stage),Only difference from Metanorms game in Section 3 is absence of the metanorm (second-order punishment) stage.,1,False,"Study is an agent-based computational model with artificial agents, not human participants.",1,False,"No human subjects; this is a simulation-based modeling study, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,No physical lab setting; it is a computer simulation.,1,True,Described explicitly as an evolutionary computational model and agent-based simulation.,1,False,"Although there is conceptual/theoretical discussion, results are generated by numerical simulation rather than closed-form analysis.",1,20,Table 1: 'Number of agents | AMG: 20'. This condition is the basic AMG Norms game.,1,4,Text: 'Defection opportunities per round | 4'. Each agent gets four cases per generation.,0.9,1,"At each opportunity agents either defect or not; no partial contribution; encoded as binary choice, which maps to an all-or-nothing contribution structure.",0.9,N/A,This is not a contribution-to-public-good game but an N-person PD with harm/payoff; no endowment allocation.,1,N/R,MPCR concept does not apply; no linear public good with specified multiplier is reported.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; interaction governed by code.,1,N/R,Not a human-interface experiment; no notion of on-screen summaries for agents.,1,N/A,Agents are anonymous and undifferentiated; no identity revelation mechanism akin to an interface.,1,N/A,"No rewards exist in the model, only punishment.",1,N/R,No user interface or information conditions; agents do not have explicit knowledge of remaining generations described as such.,0.5,1,By definition the Norms game includes punishment of defectors with cost E and harm P.,1,-2,"Table 1: 'Norm enforcement cost, E = -2'.",1,-9,"Table 1: 'Punishment cost to defector, P = -9'.",1,0,No positive sanctions or rewards are described; only punishment and enforcement costs.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modeled.,1,N/R,"Model defines payoffs T, H, E, P but no explicit per-round endowment is specified.",0.6,N/R,"Outcomes reported are boldness, vengefulness, defection levels; no normalized contribution rate is defined.",1,N/R,"Model has binary defection decision, not a token contribution amount.",1,N/R,"Paper reports changes in boldness, vengefulness, and defections but no aggregate efficiency metric (payoff/maximum payoff) is given.",1,N/R,"Although payoffs are defined, aggregate group payoff statistics are not reported for this condition.",1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,No human,Pure simulation with artificial agents; no physical or online environment for humans.,1,"N-person Prisoner’s Dilemma with punishment of defectors only; 20 agents; boldness and vengefulness strategy dimensions; 4 defection opportunities per agent per generation; payoffs: defector gets T=3, each other agent gets H=-1; punisher pays E=-2 and imposes P=-9 on defector; strategies updated by genetic-algorithm-like reproduction, crossover, and 1% mutation over 1000 generations and 100 replications in the extension runs.",Summarizes detailed structural description in Sections 1 and 3 and Appendix and Table 1.,1
10.1007_s10588-009-9056-4.md,"Basic Metanorms game (single group, coupled vengefulness)",Section 3 describes the Metanorms game as an extension of the Norms game with a metanorm stage using a single vengefulness construct.,1,Sanctioning structure: Norms + coupled Metanorm (same vengefulness for punishing defectors and shirkers),Text: metanorms game adds punishment of shirkers using the same vengefulness; contrasted with Norms-only condition.,1,False,Agent-based computational simulation; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a behavioral or field experiment; it is a simulation study.,1,False,No lab; simulations run on computers.,1,True,Described as evolutionary computational model and simulation of agents.,1,False,"Results obtained numerically from simulations, not derived analytically.",1,20,Same AMG parameters as Norms game: 20 agents.,1,4,Same defection opportunities per round (4) as in the Norms game.,0.9,1,Binary decision to defect or not defect at each opportunity.,0.9,N/A,"No contribution endowment framework; agents decide on defection, not contribution.",1,N/R,No linear public good with multiplier structure reported.,1,N/A,No human communication; interactions scripted.,1,N/R,No GUI or information display to human participants.,1,N/A,Artificial agents lack identities in the sense used for human lab experiments.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/R,Agents are not described as being informed about total number of generations.,0.5,1,Both punishment of defectors and punishment of shirkers exist in the metanorm structure.,1,-2,Norm and metanorm enforcement cost E and E' both set to -2 in text and figure.,1,-9,Punishment cost to defector P and to shirker P' both -9 per description.,1,0,No positive payoff for sanctioning beyond indirect evolutionary success; no reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/A,Rewards not present.,1,N/R,Model uses per-event payoffs; no explicit initial endowment per round is defined.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes tracked: boldness, vengefulness, defection levels; no contributions.",1,N/R,No token contributions in this game.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,"Although payoff functions are defined, aggregate group payoffs are not summarized numerically.",1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation with no human participants.,1,"Same N-person PD core as Norms game plus a metanorm stage: when an observer shirks (does not punish a detected defector), a third agent may detect this shirking with probability s and punish the shirker with cost E'=-2 to punisher and P'=-9 to shirker; uses single vengefulness parameter V for both norm and metanorm punishment; strategies (boldness, vengefulness) evolve via genetic algorithm over 1000 generations and 100 replications in the extension.","Summarizes Section 1, Section 3, and Appendix description of Metanorms game.",1
10.1007_s10588-009-9056-4.md,Metanorms game with decoupled metavengeance (single group),"Section 4 describes simulations where a third strategy dimension, Metavengeance, evolves independently, compared to the coupled Metanorms game.",1,Sanctioning structure: Norms + Metanorms with decoupled metavengeance (separate vengefulness parameters for punishing defectors and shirkers),Text: 'allowed a third strategy dimension—Metavengeance—to evolve independently. All other constraints and procedures were carried out as the basic Metanorms game.',1,False,Pure agent-based simulation.,1,False,"Simulation model, not experimental with human or field data.",1,False,No physical experiment environment.,1,True,Described as 'a set of simulations' with evolving Metavengeance.,1,False,Results are from computational runs.,1,20,"Stated that all other constraints same as basic Metanorms game, which has 20 agents.",1,4,Same per-generation defection opportunities as basic game.,0.9,1,Agents choose to defect or not; no continuous contribution.,0.9,N/A,Not a contribution game but PD with sanctions.,1,N/R,No MPCR parameter is defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,N/R,No informational display to participants.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Not specified; agents have no explicit knowledge of remaining generations mentioned.,0.5,1,Both punishment of defectors and punishment of shirkers exist as in Metanorms game.,1,-2,Same enforcement costs E and E' = -2 as in original Metanorms game; not changed in decoupling.,0.9,-9,Same punishment levels P and P' = -9 as in original; text does not indicate changes.,0.9,0,No added reward mechanism in decoupled simulations.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment described.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes are in terms of boldness, vengefulness, metavengeance, and defection levels.",1,N/R,No contributions are measured.,1,N/R,No efficiency/total payoff ratios reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not summarized numerically for this simulation set.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,No human,Strictly computational simulations.,1,"Same as coupled Metanorms game but with three strategy dimensions: Boldness (likelihood of defection), Vengefulness (likelihood of punishing defectors), and Metavengeance (likelihood of punishing shirkers of defector punishment); Metavengeance evolves independently via the genetic algorithm. 100 replications and 1000 generations; results show similar boldness and vengefulness but significantly lower metavengeance levels compared to vengefulness.",Summarizes Section 4 description of correlated vengefulness simulations.,1
10.1007_s10588-009-9056-4.md,"Groups game – Norms only (Strong vs Weak groups, no metanorms)",Section 5 describes the Groups game under three conditions; this is the Norms game condition with two groups.,1,"Sanctioning structure and group configuration: Norms only; two groups (Strong: 20 agents, Weak: 10 agents); no metanorm stage; group-specific punishment asymmetry.","For the Norms game in Groups, defection hurts only out-group; punishment asymmetric with P=-9 for Strong punishing Weak and P=-3 for Weak punishing Strong.",1,False,Agent-based groups model; no human subjects.,1,False,Simulation rather than experimental or observational human data.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Explicitly a Groups game simulation using computational agents.,1,False,No closed-form derivations; only simulation outcomes.,1,30,Table 1: 'Number of agents | Groups: 30'; comprised of Strong (20) and Weak (10).,1,4,All other values and procedures remained the same as AMG: 4 defection opportunities per agent per generation.,0.9,1,Agents still choose binary defection or cooperation.,0.9,N/A,"Game is framed as intergroup PD with sanctions, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,No public good multiplier parameter is mentioned.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/R,No experimental interface for human participants.,1,N/A,No identity display in a human sense.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Agents not specified as knowing total generations.,0.5,1,"Norms game includes punishment of defectors, including cross-group sanctions.",1,-2,Norm enforcement cost E is unchanged from AMG at -2.,0.9,N/R,"Punishment impact differs by direction (Strong→Weak: -9, Weak→Strong: -3). A single scalar tech value cannot represent this heterogeneity.",0.4,0,Groups game only modifies punishment patterns and magnitudes.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/R,No initial per-round endowment parameter is provided.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes focus on boldness, vengefulness, and defection levels by group; no contributions.",1,N/R,No tokens contributed.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure is provided for Groups game conditions.,1,N/R,"Although dominance and defection patterns described, numeric group payoffs are not reported.",1,N/A,Artificial agents.,1,N/A,Artificial agents.,1,N/A,Artificial agents.,1,N/A,Artificial agents.,1,No human,Simulation with artificial Strong and Weak groups.,1,"Two groups: Strong (20 agents), Weak (10 agents). In Norms-only condition, a defection by a member of one group harms only members of the other group; only out-group members can punish that defector. Punishment impact is asymmetric: Strong punishing Weak uses P=-9, Weak punishing Strong uses P=-3, enforcement cost E=-2 for all punishers. Learning (strategy updating) occurs within groups: Strong agents copy only Strong, Weak only Weak. 1000 generations and 100 replications used for analysis.",Summarizes Section 5 Groups game setup for Norms condition.,1
10.1007_s10588-009-9056-4.md,Groups game – Metanorms with coupled vengefulness (Strong vs Weak groups),"Section 5: 'The Groups game was played under three conditions: the Norms game, the Metanorms game, and the Metanorms game with a decoupled Metanorm structure.' This is the Metanorms condition with coupled vengefulness.",1,"Sanctioning structure and group configuration: Metanorms with coupled vengefulness; two groups (Strong: 20, Weak: 10); cross-group defection harm and asymmetric punishment magnitudes.",Metanorms game adds metanorm stage to Groups game while keeping single vengefulness dimension and asymmetric P values.,1,False,Agent-based computational model.,1,False,No human behavior tested in a controlled environment.,1,False,No lab or field; simulations are run in silico.,1,True,Groups game is simulated for 1000 generations and 100 replications.,1,False,No analytical solution; only simulation outcomes.,1,30,"Strong group 20 agents, Weak group 10 agents.",1,4,Same structure as base AMG: 4 defection opportunities per agent per generation.,0.9,1,Decision remains binary (defect vs not).,0.9,N/A,Not a contribution/PGG framework.,1,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,No user interface for agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects or identity display.,1,N/A,No reward exists.,1,N/R,Agents' knowledge of horizon not specified.,0.5,1,"Both norm and metanorm punishments are active; cross-group for defectors, within-group for shirkers.",1,-2,Enforcement cost E and metanorm cost E' remain -2 as per base model; not changed for Groups.,0.9,N/R,Punishment intensities depend on group roles (Strong vs Weak) and whether target is defector or shirker; not a single scalar.,0.4,0,No rewards introduced.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment defined.,0.6,N/R,"Dependent variables are boldness, vengefulness, metavengefulness, and defection frequencies.",1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency ratio reported.,1,N/R,Paper discusses dominance and defection patterns rather than explicit payoff statistics per group.,1,N/A,"Artificial Strong and Weak agents, not human participants.",1,N/A,Artificial agents.,1,N/A,Artificial agents.,1,N/A,Artificial agents.,1,No human,Purely computational Groups game.,1,"Two groups (Strong: n=20, Weak: n=10). In Norms component: defection by a member of one group harms only the other group; only out-group members can punish that defector; Strong punishing Weak has P=-9 while Weak punishing Strong has P=-3; cost E=-2. In Metanorm component: shirkers (those who fail to punish a detected out-group defector) are punished only by in-group members. Vengefulness is a single dimension covering both norm and metanorm punishment. Strategy updating is within-group: Strong imitates Strong, Weak imitates Weak. Simulations run for 1000 generations with 100 replications; analysis shows high boldness particularly for Strong group and no true norm against defection, but lower relative boldness for Weak group under metanorms than without.","Combines Section 5 description of Groups Metanorm condition and reported outcomes (F statistics, boldness patterns).",1
10.1007_s10588-009-9056-4.md,Groups game – Metanorms with decoupled metavengeance (Strong vs Weak groups),Section 5 describes a 'Metanorms game with a decoupled Metanorm structure (metavengeance)' as the third Groups game condition.,1,"Sanctioning structure and group configuration: Metanorms with decoupled metavengeance (separate vengefulness for norms and metanorms); two groups (Strong: 20, Weak: 10); asymmetric cross-group punishment.",Groups game extended by decoupling Metavengeance so Strong and Weak groups have separate strategy dimensions for punishing defectors vs shirkers.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Not an experiment with human subjects.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Described and analyzed as a simulation of groups with metavengeance.,1,False,Relies on simulated trajectories and summary statistics.,1,30,"Same group sizes as other Groups game conditions: 20 Strong, 10 Weak.",1,4,Underlying game structure remains unchanged from AMG.,0.9,1,Defection remains a binary choice.,0.9,N/A,"No contributions/endowments, only defection decisions.",1,N/R,Public-goods MPCR not applicable.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/R,No human-visible summaries.,1,N/A,No human identities to reveal.,1,N/A,No rewards in the game.,1,N/R,Not specified; agents are not described as having awareness of generational horizon.,0.5,1,Punishment of defectors and shirkers exists with separated vengefulness and metavengefulness.,1,-2,Enforcement costs for norm and metanorm punishment E and E' appear unchanged from earlier conditions (-2).,0.9,N/R,Punishment magnitudes differ by group and context and are not summarized into a single scalar parameter.,0.4,0,No new reward mechanism introduced in decoupled Groups metavengeance condition.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit agent endowment parameter provided.,0.6,N/R,"Simulation tracks boldness (by group), vengefulness, metavengefulness, and defection rates, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contributions or token amounts.,1,N/R,Paper focuses on boldness thresholds and dominance rather than payoff ratios.,1,N/R,No numeric summary of group payoffs reported for this condition.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation of intergroup norms and metanorms.,1,"Same two-group setup as other Groups conditions (Strong:20, Weak:10), with cross-group defection costs and asymmetric punishment (Strong→Weak P=-9, Weak→Strong P=-3) and enforcement cost E=-2. Metanorms are present, and vengefulness is split: one parameter for punishing defectors (Vengefulness) and another for punishing shirkers (Metavengefulness). Learning is within groups. Results show a significant interaction between sanctioning structure and boldness: in the decoupled condition, Weak group boldness drops below the norm threshold (mean≈1.61<3.5) while Strong group boldness remains high; Strong group evolves high vengefulness against Weak defectors and low metavengefulness against in-group shirkers, effectively producing a norm against others’ defection and dominance over the Weak group.",Summarizes the decoupled metavengeance Groups simulations and findings from Section 5 and Fig. 5–6.,1
10.1016_j.tpb.2015.06.005.md,Static game with constant market price and proportional penalty,"Section 2 defines a normal-form game with constant outside market price q>p and a proportional penalty y for unfaithfulness; the analysis centers on the multistrategy (1,1).",1,"Penalty rate y chosen by the cooperative in [0,1]; members choose proportion xi in [0,1] of production sold to cooperative; outside price q is constant and higher than cooperative price p.",Model description: strategies are xi for members and y for the cooperative; outside price is exogenous and constant.,1,False,Purely theoretical normal-form game; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; only analytical derivations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; theoretical model only.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulation reported for this game.,1,True,Payoffs and equilibrium properties are derived analytically.,1,2,"Game is between n cooperative members and the cooperative as player n+1; strategically it is modeled as N=n+1 players but not specified for a particular n, so 2 is not appropriate; set to N/R.",0,1,Static (one-shot) normal-form game; no repeated rounds are modeled.,1,1,"Members choose xi in [0,1], i.e., any proportion of production, not just all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,"No human experiment, no endowment framing as public/private account.",1,N/R,No public good multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; this is not a standard public goods game.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,Information displays to participants are not applicable in an analytical model.,1,N/A,No identities or visibility assumptions of individual punishers; analytical cooperative penalty.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in this section.,1,0,No participants or screens; single-shot analytical game.,1,1,"Cooperative sets penalty rate y∈[0,1] for unfaithfulness.",1,N/R,No explicit cost per punishment unit to the punisher (cooperative) is specified; penalty is just deducted from members.,0,N/R,"Penalty equals y(q−p)(1−xi)Li, so the effective technology depends on production and price difference; not summarized as a simple per-token mapping.",0.5,0,No positive rewards for faithfulness or cooperation in this base model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Members have production Li but no numeric value or common endowment is given.,0.5,N/R,Paper analyzes equilibrium strategies but does not report numeric contributions or averages.,0,N/R,No empirical data or specific numeric equilibrium quantities are reported.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios (payoff relative to maximum) are computed numerically.,0,N/R,Payoff functions are given symbolically but no realized numeric payoffs are reported.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical game-theoretic model.,1,"Multiplayer normal-form game with n members and cooperative as player n+1; members choose xi∈[0,1] portion sold to cooperative at price p; remainder sold on outside market at constant price q>p; cooperative chooses penalty rate y∈[0,1] applied to extra profit from outside sales. Multistrategy (x=1,y=1) is shown to be an attractive solution and a Nash equilibrium.",Summary of Section 2 model structure and solution concept.,1
10.1016_j.tpb.2015.06.005.md,Oligopoly game with penalty parameter beta and Cournot demand,Section 3 extends the previous game to Cournot-type oligopoly with linear inverse demand q(x)=a−b∑Li(1−xi) and penalty parameter β≥1.,1,"Penalty parameter β≥1 (effectiveness of penalty); penalty rate y∈[0,1]; members' proportions xi∈[0,1]; Cournot market quantity (∑Li(1−xi)) affecting price q(x).",Section 3 explicitly introduces β and Cournot demand as new parameters compared to constant-price model.,1,False,The model is analytical; no data from human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; it is a theoretical game model.,1,False,No lab setting involved.,1,False,Dynamics and equilibria are analyzed symbolically; no numerical simulation runs are described in this section.,1,True,"Equilibrium properties (Theorem 1, Remark 2) derived via inequalities and calculus.",1,N/R,Game defined for general n; no specific numeric n is instantiated.,0.5,1,Static oligopoly game with one-shot interaction.,1,1,"Strategies xi are continuous in [0,1], i.e., any proportion.",1,N/A,No experimental endowment or default allocation for human subjects.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier is defined; oligopoly pricing instead.,1,N/A,No human interaction protocol.,1,0,No user interface or feedback; symbolic model only.,1,N/A,No players with individual punishment decisions in an experimental sense; penalty is a parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards beyond standard payoffs.,1,0,Static game; no rounds shown.,1,1,Penalty parameter β and rate y≥0 apply to extra profit of unfaithful members.,1,N/R,Model does not specify a separate resource cost to the cooperative for punishing.,0.5,N/R,Penalty term is βy(q(x)−p)(1−xi)Li; not a simple constant per unit.,0.5,0,No explicit positive incentive separate from penalty.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Members produce Li; numeric values not specified.,0.5,N/R,No numeric equilibrium xi values are computed; only inequalities and qualitative properties.,0,N/R,No numeric realizations of xi or Li are given.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure is computed numerically.,0,N/R,Symbolic payoffs only; no concrete values for efficiency or payoffs.,0.5,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical Cournot game.,1,"Oligopoly extension of cooperative game: outside market price is q(x)=a−b∑Li(1−xi) with q(0)>p. Penalty is scaled by β≥1: members' payoff fi(x,y)=Li[(p−c)xi+(q(x)−c)(1−xi)−βy(q(x)−p)(1−xi)], cooperative payoff fn+1(x,y)=βy(q(x)−p)∑Lj(1−xj). Multistrategy (x=1,y=1) is shown to be an attractive solution (Theorem 1). For β>1 and y>1/β, deviations reduce unfaithful members’ payoffs and increase cooperative’s payoff (Remark 2).",Content directly summarized from Section 3.,1
10.1016_j.tpb.2015.06.005.md,Evolutionary dynamics of oligopoly game strategies with limited penalty (beta=1),Section 4 analyzes the partial adaptive dynamics for the Cournot-penalty model and Theorem 2 first considers the case β=1 (limited penalty).,1,"Penalty effectiveness parameter fixed at β=1; initial conditions (x(0),y(0)) in (0,1)^n×(0,1); dynamics given by partial derivatives of payoffs.","Theorem 2 explicitly separates case β=1 from β>1, with system (4.5)-(4.6).",1,False,Deterministic ODE system for strategies; no empirical data.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment.,1,False,The analysis is purely mathematical.,1,False,Dynamics are analyzed qualitatively; no numeric trajectories or computational experiments reported.,1,True,"Stability of equilibrium (1,1) studied via sign analysis of derivatives.",1,N/R,Dynamics defined for general n; not instantiated numerically.,0.5,N/R,"Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics, not discrete rounds.",1,1,"xi(t) remains in [0,1] under dynamics; continuous strategies.",1,N/A,No human experiment or default endowment partition.,1,N/R,Model is not framed as a public good.,1,N/A,No interacting subjects.,1,0,No feedback screens or experimental interface.,1,N/A,Analytical dynamics; no notion of ID revelation.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,0,Continuous time model; no discrete round count.,1,1,Penalty rate y is part of dynamics and affects payoffs.,1,N/R,No explicit cost of administering penalty modeled for cooperative.,0.5,N/R,Same functional penalty as in Section 3 with β=1; not summarized into a constant technology.,0.5,0,No explicit reward redistribution in this section.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Production Li appears, but no numeric value given; not an experimental endowment.",0.5,N/R,"Theorem only states qualitative monotonic behavior (xi decreasing, y increasing) and instability; no numeric equilibrium strategy distribution beyond x=1,y=1 unrest.",0,N/R,No specific solution trajectories or equilibria with numeric xi values are computed for β=1.,0,N/R,No efficiency or payoff levels are computed along trajectories.,0,N/R,Only signs of derivatives; no explicit group payoff outcomes given.,0.5,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Mathematical evolutionary dynamics without empirical data.,1,"Partial adaptive dynamics: ẋi=xi(1−xi)Li(−1+βy)[q(x)−p−bLi(1−xi)], ẏ=y(1−y)β(q(x)−p)∑Lj(1−xj). For β=1 and initial (x(0),y(0)) in (0,1)^n×(0,1), y increases and xi decrease near (1,1); equilibrium (1,1) is unstable (Theorem 2, case β=1).",Direct paraphrase of dynamic equations and stability statement for β=1.,1
