10.1371_journal.pone.0092336.md,Public Goods Game without punishment (standard PGG),Paper states that each group played six rounds of the standard Public Goods Game and six rounds of the Public Goods Game with punishment; this row corresponds to the standard Public Goods Game condition.,1,"Punishment option: absent (standard Public Goods Game, no punishment stage)",Only clear treatment difference described is presence vs absence of punishment; here it is the no-punishment variant.,1,True,Human participants (students) played incentivized games.,1,True,Controlled laboratory economic game with random rematching and predefined payoff rules.,1,True,Conducted in a university setting in a room with computer terminals; this matches a lab experiment.,1,False,"All results are from human subjects, no agent-based or purely simulated results reported.",1,False,"Although payoff functions are written, there is no standalone analytical model as a main method.",1,12,Methods: participants were divided into ten groups of twelve players; two groups had 11 plus 2 virtual players who behaved mechanically; group size from players’ perspective is 12.,0.9,6,Each group played six rounds of the standard Public Goods Game and six rounds of the Public Goods Game with punishment.,1,1,"Contribution X_i^k is defined over the continuous (or integer) interval <0,20> CZK, so any amount from 0 to full endowment is allowed.",1,0,Standard PGG: each round each player receives 20 CZK endowment and chooses how much to invest; default is entire amount in private account until contributed.,0.95,0.1667,"Payoff rule: y_i^k = (20 - X_i^k) + α * X̄^k, with α = 2 and group size n = 12. Marginal per capita return MPCR = α / n = 2/12 ≈ 0.1667.",1,0,Interactions only through a web application; no mention of communication or chat being allowed.,0.95,1,After each round players received information about all others’ contributions via the web app; this is a summary of others’ actions/earnings-relevant info.,0.9,0,In the no-punishment game there is no punishment; by definition identities of punishers cannot be shown.,1,0,No reward mechanism is described in any treatment.,1,1,"Standard in such lab designs and they discuss behavior over 6 rounds; though not stated explicitly, participants are almost certainly told the number of rounds.",0.7,0,This condition is explicitly the standard Public Goods Game without punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment stage exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment stage exists in this condition.,1,0,No reward or positive sanctioning option is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Methods: each player in each round received 20 CZK.,1,0.3055,Average contribution in standard PGG is 6.11 CZK out of 20 CZK endowment; normalized rate 6.11/20 ≈ 0.3055.,1,6.11,Results: in the standard Public Goods Game the average contribution to the fund was 6.11 CZK.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff vs maximum payoff ratio reported; cannot be derived without more detailed payoff distributions and punishment (which is absent here but they do not report total payoffs).,0.2,N/R,Paper does not report total group earnings or average payoff per player in this condition.,0.2,Czech Republic,"Conducted at Charles University in Prague; participants are students there, so country is Czech Republic.",1,Mean 21.46 years (SD 1.60); mostly university students.,"Methods: mean age 21.46, S.D. 1.60; subject pool mostly consisted of students.",1,"77 females, 41 males (N=118).",Methods: subject pool (118 in total with 77 females and 41 males).,1,"Mostly university students (Faculty of Science, Charles University in Prague).","Methods: subject pool mostly consisted of students of the Faculty of Science, Charles University in Prague.",1,On site,All players sat in the same room divided into separate computer cubicles; this is an on-site lab environment.,1,"Standard linear Public Goods Game with group size 12, endowment 20 CZK, contribution X_i^k ∈ [0,20], and payoff y_i^k = (20 − X_i^k) + 2·X̄^k. Contributions of all 12 players are displayed anonymously after each round. Random rematching between rounds; 6 rounds per group. No punishment or reward stage.",Summarizes explicit design details from the Methods and payoff formula.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0092336.md,Public Goods Game with punishment (PGG-P),Paper: each group played six rounds of the standard Public Goods Game and six rounds of the Public Goods Game with punishment; this row is the punishment condition.,1,Punishment option: present (Public Goods Game with punishment stage after contribution decisions),Independent manipulation is availability of punishment versus no punishment.,1,True,Same human participants played the PGG with punishment condition.,1,True,"Controlled lab game with defined stages, payoff functions, and random rematching.",1,True,"Conducted on-site in a computer lab-like setting, identical to the no-punishment condition.",1,False,No purely simulated or agent-based punishment game reported.,1,False,Use of payoff formulas is descriptive; no separate analytical model is studied.,1,12,"Groups of 12 players; two 11-player groups were filled up with 2 virtual players each who always contributed median and never punished; from design and strategic perspective, group size is 12.",0.9,6,Each group played six rounds of the Public Goods Game with punishment.,1,1,"Same contribution decision as in standard PGG: X_i^k ∈ <0,20> CZK.",1,0,Each round players receive 20 CZK and decide how much to contribute; default allocation is in private accounts until contributed.,0.95,0.1667,Contribution stage identical: public-good fund doubles total contribution (α=2) and is split evenly among 12 players; MPCR = 2/12 ≈ 0.1667.,1,0,Interactions via web application only; anonymity and no mention of communication or messaging options.,0.95,1,"After contributions, information about other players’ contributions is shown to each player before punishment decisions.",0.9,0,"Design emphasizes anonymity and random rematching; unlike designs allowing targeted counter-punishment, there is no indication that punished players learn who punished them.",0.85,0,No reward mechanism is present; only costly punishment points are available.,1,1,Experiment uses exactly 6 rounds and analyzes dynamics over rounds; standard practice is to inform participants about the fixed number of rounds.,0.7,1,This treatment is explicitly described as Public Goods Game with punishment.,1,N/R,"Cost function c(p) is given as a table of total costs for p=0..10 (e.g., c(1)=1, c(2)=2, c(3)=4, …), but not as a per-unit cost; schema asks for coins per unit, which varies with p, so a single numeric value is not defined.",0.3,0.1,"Each punishment point reduces the punished player’s basic payout y_i^k by γ·p, with γ=0.1, i.e. each unit of punishment reduces earnings by 0.1 units of the relevant payoff measure.",0.9,0,No option to positively reward or increase others’ payoffs is described; only punishment marks.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Each player in each round received 20 CZK (same as in standard game).,1,0.593,Results: players invested 11.86 CZK on average in the public fund under punishment; normalized by 20 CZK endowment: 11.86/20 ≈ 0.593.,1,11.86,Results: in the Public Goods Game with punishment players invested 11.86 CZK on average in the public fund.,1,N/R,No overall payoffs (after punishment costs and fines) vs theoretical maximum are reported; cannot compute efficiency from available data.,0.2,N/R,Paper does not provide average or total group payoffs for the punishment condition.,0.2,Czech Republic,"Same participant pool: students at Charles University in Prague, Czech Republic.",1,Mean 21.46 years (SD 1.60); mostly university students.,Same subject pool description applies to both conditions.,1,"77 females, 41 males (N=118).",Same participant pool across both game variants.,1,"Mostly university students (Faculty of Science, Charles University in Prague).","Methods specify this for the full sample, used in both treatment variants.",1,On site,Same on-site computer-lab setting as in the standard PGG condition.,1,"Two-stage Public Goods Game with punishment. Stage 1: each of 12 players receives 20 CZK and chooses contribution X_i^k ∈ [0,20] to a group fund. Payoff before punishment: y_i^k = (20 − X_i^k) + 2·X̄^k, where X̄^k is the group average contribution and the fund doubles total contributions. Contributions of all 12 players are shown anonymously after Stage 1. Stage 2: each player can assign p_{i→j}^k ∈ {0,…,10} punishment points to each other player j. The final payoff is Y_i^k = max(0, (1 − 0.1·∑_j p_{j→i}^k) · y_i^k) − ∑_j c(p_{i→j}^k), with γ=0.1 and a superlinear punishment cost function c(p) adopted from Fehr & Gächter (2000): c(0)=0, c(1)=1, c(2)=2, c(3)=4, c(4)=6, c(5)=9, c(6)=12, c(7)=16, c(8)=20, c(9)=25, c(10)=30. The player cannot lose more than their basic payout y_i^k from punishment fines, but punishment costs can drive net income below zero. Contributions and punishment decisions occur under strict anonymity with random rematching each round; 6 rounds total. Two groups include 2 virtual players each who always contribute the median of others’ contributions and never punish; these virtual players are not included in punishment-strategy analyses.","Compiled from Methods section, payoff equations, and Table 2 of the paper.",1
10.3389_fpsyg.2015.00983.md,Ultimatum Game – Alcohol-dependent responders,Single UG task with two between-subject groups; this row captures the alcohol-dependent (AD) participants as responders.,1,"Participant group: Alcohol-dependent (responder role only); Offer type within-subject: fair (40–50%), medium-fair (27–33%), unfair (17–22%) of stake from fictive proposer","Only manipulated factor between conditions is diagnosis group; within this group, offer fairness level varies across trials.",1,True,Human participants (26 alcohol-dependent individuals) performed UG decisions.,1,True,Controlled task with preprogrammed fictive offers and measured decisions/SCR; not observational.,1,True,Conducted at hospital/psychology lab with BIOPAC and computer interface; no indication of field context.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data from human participants.,1,False,No purely mathematical or closed-form modeling presented.,1,2,UG defined as two players (proposer and responder); text explicitly describes two players.,1,54,Participants played 54 single-round trials of the UG.,1,1,"Responder chooses accept or reject; contributions not continuous but binary regarding acceptance of proposed split. However, in this schema, contribution refers to giving; here, responder cannot choose partial amounts so classify as all-or-nothing=1 (binary decision).",0.6,0,"No voluntary contribution to a public fund; UG involves splitting a stake proposed by other, not a standard public goods contribution. Classified as 0 (all resources initially in proposer’s control/private).",0.4,N/A,Ultimatum game is not a linear public goods game with MPCR; not applicable.,1,0,Interaction was via computer with fictive proposers; no mention of communication allowed.,0.9,0,No indication that participants saw others’ earnings or punishment summaries; only their own offers.,0.9,0,"Punishment is implicit via rejection of offers; proposer identity shown only as neutral face photos, but punishment i