medium-fair (27–33%), and unfair (17–22%). No real monetary payment; neutral Caucasian faces (27 male, 27 female) displayed with each offer; responses via button box. Skin conductance responses recorded 0.5–4 s after offer onset.",Summary of task section and apparatus description.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2015.00983.md,Ultimatum Game – Control responders,Same UG task with healthy control participants as responders; separate condition due to between-subject group.,1,"Participant group: Healthy control (responder role only); Offer type within-subject: fair (40–50%), medium-fair (27–33%), unfair (17–22%) of stake from fictive proposer","Between-group factor is diagnosis (control vs alcohol-dependent); within this group, fairness level varies across trials.",1,True,Human participants (32 controls) performed UG decisions.,1,True,Controlled ultimatum game with preprogrammed fictive offers and physiological recording.,1,True,Run with BIOPAC in a psychology/medical lab environment; not field or online.,1,False,No simulation-only component.,1,False,No purely analytical modeling.,1,2,Ultimatum game has proposer and responder.,1,54,Participants played 54 single round trials of the UG.,1,1,Responder’s decision is binary (accept vs reject); no graded contribution choice.,0.6,0,UG setting involves proposer allocating a stake; responder has no public-goods-style endowment contribution.,0.4,N/A,UG is not a linear public good; MPCR concept not applicable.,1,0,No communication between participant and fictive proposers is described.,0.9,0,Participants only see current offer and proposer face; no summaries of others’ outcomes.,0.9,0,"Punishment arises via rejection; proposer identities only via static neutral face photos, not framed as identified punished individuals in a reputation sense; coded as 0.",0.6,0,No explicit reward option besides accepting an offer; no identity of rewarders reported.,1,0,Article does not state that number of remaining trials was displayed.,0.5,1,Rejection of offer is a costly punishment to proposer; discussed in text as punishing unfairness.,1,N/R,Numerical stake size not provided; cannot compute punishment cost per unit.,0,N/R,UG rejection simply voids the entire stake for both players; stake amount not quantified so cannot parameterize as technology.,0.3,0,No separate costly reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present; cost not applicable.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Stake per trial not numerically reported; only proportions of stake.,0,N/R,Only acceptance proportions and SCR are reported; no contributions.,0,N/R,No monetary/token contribution amounts reported.,0,N/R,Group efficiency measures (payoff relative to max) not reported.,0,N/R,No group payoff aggregates reported.,0,Belgium,Controls recruited from healthy community members around Université Libre de Bruxelles/Brugmann Hospital in Brussels.,0.9,"Mean 41.25 years (SD 10.08), adults",Table 1 reports mean age 41.25 (10.08) for control group; all subjects adults.,1,"24 male, 8 female",Table 1 gives gender (male/female) for controls as 24/8.,1,Mean 17.06 years (SD 3.14),Table 1 lists education in years for control group.,1,On site,Same lab/hospital setting and apparatus as AD group.,1,"Single-responder ultimatum game with 54 independent trials against fictive proposers; offer fairness categories as fair (40–50% to responder), medium-fair (27–33%), and unfair (17–22%). No real monetary payoffs; neutral Caucasian faces (27 male, 27 female) paired with offers; responses via button box. SCR recorded in 0.5–4 s window following offer onset.",Same UG task description applies to control group.,1
10.1007_s10816-013-9194-3.md,"Simulation condition – no indirect reciprocity (θ = 0), varying vision and whale probability",Paper distinguishes cases with θ=0 (no indirect reciprocity) versus θ>0 and explores vision and prob-beached-whale; this row captures the θ=0 scenario class.,0.9,No indirect reciprocity (θ = 0); varying vision v and prob-beached-whale P_bw,"Section ‘Stationary Regimes’ and Fig. 7 describe results “for the case without indirect reciprocity θ=0, when the parameter vision varies and the P_bw is fixed” with multiple P_bw values.",0.9,False,The study is explicitly an agent-based computer simulation with no human participants.,1,False,"It is a computational experiment, not a human-subject or field experiment in the usual empirical sense.",0.9,False,No physical lab with human subjects; purely in silico.,1,True,Described throughout as an agent-based model (ABM) computer simulation (NetLogo implementation).,1,False,"While equations are given, the core results use numerical simulation, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.9,82,"Footnote specifies people-density=0.002, which they state corresponds to 82 agents; these are the ‘people’ agents (households/canoes).",1,100000,Footnote: “The time limit for a simulation is 10^5 ticks”; each tick is the time step used for behaviour and measurement.,1,1,Agents do not choose a contribution amount; they choose a binary cooperative action (call or not call). This matches an all-or-nothing cooperation decision.,0.8,N/A,No public goods or endowments; agents have no divisible contribution to a public fund.,1,N/R,No linear public good with a reported marginal per capita return; payoffs are from functions of aggregation size and reputation.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; agents follow programmed rules.,1,0,Agents observe local actions (seeing calls/defection when present) but there is no explicit display of aggregate summaries like payoffs or punishments.,0.7,N/A,There is no explicit punishment action or punishment identity mechanism in the ABM.,1,N/A,"No explicit reward mechanism is modelled, only reputation and payoffs.",1,0,Agents have no parameter indicating knowledge of remaining ticks or total duration; not modelled.,0.7,0,"Although defection can reduce reputation indirectly, there is no explicit costly punishment or sanctioning action.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit costly rewarding action; only reputation and social-capital accumulation.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Agents are not endowed with tokens or money; state variables are meat and social-capital stocks whose initial levels are not given numerically (other than being reset to zero after imitation).,0.6,N/R,"Outcome tracked is ‘average cooperation C’ (fraction of cooperators) in the population, not contribution relative to an endowment.",0.9,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts per agent are reported; only probabilities and proportions of strategies.,0.9,N/R,They discuss fitness components (meat and social-capital) but do not report an aggregate efficiency ratio versus a theoretical maximum.,0.9,N/R,"No explicit numeric group payoff is reported for conditions; focus is on regime types (All Cooperation, All Defection) and cooperation levels.",0.9,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational ABM with no human subjects.,1,"Agent-based model of whale-beaching events on a 201×201 patch grid with beach-density=0.5; 82 people agents move (distance-walked-per-tick=4) searching for beached whales that appear with probability P_bw on beach patches. Each discovered whale may be made public (cooperate) or kept private (defect) with strategy prob-cooperation∈{0,1}. Public calls create signals with signal-range=50, enlarging my-range from vision v to signal-range. Payoffs are in meat and social-capital per tick, determined by aggregation size N, parameters α, μ (μ=0 for decreasing meat returns), and reputation Ri, with fitness Fi(t)=θ SC_i(t)+(1−θ)M_i(t). In this condition θ=0 so only meat matters; other parameters: prob-random-move=1, rounds-per-generation=50, prob-mutation=0.025, history-size=10, history-past-discount=0.8, tournament-size=5, initial population 50 cooperators and 50 defectors, 50 replications per parameter set, run length 10^5 ticks. Independent variables explored: vision v, prob-beached-whale P_bw; regimes classified by stationary cooperation C into All Cooperation (C≥0.9), All Defection (C≤0.1), Majority Cooperation (0.5≤C<0.9), Majority Defection (0.1<C<0.5).","Summarises detailed model description, parameters list, and experiment design from ‘Description of the WWHW Model’, footnote, and ‘Analysis and Results’.",1
10.1007_s10816-013-9194-3.md,"Simulation condition – indirect reciprocity present (θ > 0), varying vision and whale probability","Paper repeatedly contrasts runs with indirect reciprocity (θ>0) against θ=0; Figs. 6–8 analyse cases for θ>0, e.g., θ=0.1 and other values.",0.9,"Indirect reciprocity present (θ > 0; e.g., θ = 0.1 and other positive values); varying vision v and prob-beached-whale P_bw",They treat θ (social-capital-versus-meat-sensitivity) as a key parameter; describe ‘case with indirect reciprocity (θ=0.1)’ in Fig. 7 and graph average cooperation for ‘different values of θ’ in Fig. 8 while varying vision and fixing P_bw in panels.,0.9,False,"As above, this is part of the same ABM simulation study with no human subjects.",1,False,"Computational parameter sweeps, not empirical experiments.",0.9,False,No physical lab or human subjects.,1,True,"Agent-based model in NetLogo used to simulate behaviour under varied θ, vision, and P_bw.",1,False,"Results here are from simulation runs, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,82,Same model configuration: people-density=0.002 corresponds to 82 agents.,1,100000,Same 10^5 ticks run length per simulation.,1,1,"Binary cooperative decision (call vs. not call) persists; θ affects payoffs, not action granularity.",0.8,N/A,No explicit contributions to a public fund.,1,N/R,"No MPCR defined, as this is not a linear public goods game.",1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,Agents only use local observations and stored histories; no aggregate feedback screen.,0.7,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,No modelling of agents’ knowledge about remaining ticks.,0.7,0,"While low reputation reduces future gains, there is no explicit choice to punish at a cost.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,0,No separate reward action encoded; benefits emerge via reputation and aggregation but not through a reward decision.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,N/R,"Agents’ stocks begin at zero after imitation, but no fixed endowment like tokens is specified numerically.",0.6,N/R,"They track fraction of cooperators C, not contributions per endowment; C is used qualitatively but no numeric stationary values per parameter set are tabulated.",0.9,N/R,"Binary strategies, but no reported counts of cooperative acts per run.",0.9,N/R,No formal efficiency metric (payoff vs. optimum) is computed or reported.,0.9,N/R,"Results reported at the level of cooperation regimes, not aggregate numeric payoffs.",0.9,N/A,No humans in the simulation.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Same computational ABM study without human subjects.,1,"Same WWHW ABM as in the θ=0 case, but now θ>0 introduces indirect reciprocity by weighting social-capital in fitness: Fi(t)=θ SC_i(t)+(1−θ)M_i(t). Reputation Ri in [0,1] is computed from discounted histories of observed cooperation and being caught defecting with parameters history-size=10 and history-past-discount=0.8. Social-capital gain per tick is ΔSC_i(t)=Ri(1−e^{−α(N(t)−1)^2}), monotonically increasing with aggregation size N but saturating at Ri. Meat gain per tick is ΔM_i(t)=exp(−α((N(t)−1)−μ)^2) with μ=0, focusing on decreasing returns. Imitation occurs every rounds-per-generation=50 ticks via fitness-proportionate random tournaments (tournament-size=5); with probability prob-mutation=0.025 an exploring agent chooses a random strategy. Only last-public-prob-cooperation is copied (strategies become public only when actions are observed). Independent variables in these conditions include θ (various positive values, e.g., θ=0.1), vision v, and P_bw (0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.30). Results show that for θ>0, stationary regimes are typically All Cooperation (C≥0.9), with vision generally increasing cooperation, except specific low-vision cases where imitation of public strategies also favours cooperation.","Synthesises model specification and the role of θ>0, along with parameter sweeps described under ‘Stationary Regimes’, Figs. 6–8, and footnote on parameter settings.",1
10.1287_orsc.2021.1521.md,2012 lab-in-the-field one-shot Prisoner's Dilemma with CEOs,Single behavioral game described: one-shot PD played by CEOs in 2012 survey wave.,0.95,Framing of the dilemma: two textual frames with identical payoffs (combined in analysis),Respondents were randomly assigned to two frames describing the PD differently; authors report no framing effect and pool them.,0.9,True,Game played by human CEOs (entrepreneurs) in a survey context.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized one-shot PD with random assignment to frames.",0.95,True,Described as a laboratory-in-the-field experiment conducted with CEOs in their natural environment.,0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported for this PD.,1,False,"Study is empirical; analytical content is theoretical discussion only, not a formal model of this game.",1,2,Prisoner’s Dilemma payoff matrix is for an entrepreneur and a stranger (2 players).,1,1,Explicitly described as a one-shot PD game.,1,0,"In a standard 2x2 PD, players choose between two discrete actions (cooperate/defect), not a continuous contribution level.",0.95,0,Not a public goods contribution game; no endowment-allocation decision to a public account.,0.9,N/A,"MPCR applies to public goods games, not to a 2x2 PD with fixed payoffs.",1,0,No mention of any communication; described as anonymous one-shot PD with a stranger.,0.9,0,No indication that participants saw any feedback about others’ earnings or choices; one-shot interaction only.,0.9,N/A,No punishment stage or mechanism in the PD.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the PD.,1,1,Game is explicitly framed as one-shot; thus number of rounds is known.,0.9,0,Stage game is a standard PD with fixed payoff matrix and no post-decision punishment.,0.95,N/A,No punishment decision is available.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No stage for rewarding others beyond the basic PD move.,0.95,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology.,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix is reported in absolute CNY amounts (50, 100, 250, 400) but no initial endowment or show-up fee is specified.",0.4,0.48,"Table 2: 'Prisoner’s dilemma' variable mean 0.48, coded 1=cooperate, 0=defect. In a binary PD this is the fraction cooperating.",0.95,N/A,"Outcome is a binary cooperate/defect choice, not a continuous contribution magnitude.",0.95,N/R,No report of average total payoffs; cannot compute realized efficiency relative to mutual cooperation benchmark.,0.3,N/R,Paper reports only cooperation rate and not average payoffs of the PD outcomes.,0.3,China,CEOs are from manufacturing firms in the Yangzi River Delta region of China.,1,Mean 45.8 years (SD 8.04; range 29–69),Table 2 reports age descriptive statistics for the sample.,1,84% male (16% female),Table 2: 'Male' mean 0.84 (binary).,1,Mean 13.5 years of education (SD 2.9; range 0–25),Table 2 provides years of education statistics.,1,Field experiment,"Authors label it a 'laboratory-in-the-field behavioral game' with CEOs recruited from firms, not in a standard campus lab.",0.95,"2x2 one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma against an anonymous stranger from outside the local community; payoff matrix in CNY: (Defect,Defect)=100,100; (Defect,Cooperate)=400,50; (Cooperate,Defect)=50,400; (Cooperate,Cooperate)=250,250. Framed in two ways with no framing effect. PD was one of three games (Chicken, Battle of the Sexes) played in randomized order, but only PD outcomes analyzed here.","Payoff matrix from Table 1; one-shot, anonymity, and framing details from methods description and endnote 1.",1
