10.3389_fpsyg.2016.00799.md,Delton et al. 2011b – Direct reciprocity under uncertainty (agent-based simulation),Only concrete design described in article is an agent-based simulation from Delton et al. 2011b; this row captures that simulation study as one condition since specific parameterized treatments are not detailed here.,0.84,"Ecological and interaction parameters in agent-based model (costs and benefits of cooperation, length of interactions if repeated, a priori probability an interaction is repeated vs one-shot, possibility of one-shot vs repeated encounters)","Text notes that the simulation varied 'costs and benefits of cooperation, lengths of interactions if they are repeated, and the a priori probabilities of interactions being repeated or one-shot', but does not give exact numeric values or distinct named conditions.",0.78,False,Described explicitly as 'agent-based simulations'; no human participants are involved.,1,False,"It is a computational/agent-based evolutionary model, not a controlled experiment with subjects.",1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; purely simulated agents.,1,True,"Article states 'agent-based simulations of a human-like ecology, natural selection favored agents...' indicating a numerical simulation.",1,False,Results come from simulations rather than closed-form mathematical analysis.,1,0,Number of agents/players in each simulated group or population is not specified in this article.,0.2,0,"Simulation involves repeated or one-shot interactions over evolutionary time, but specific number of game rounds per interaction or per simulation run is not reported.",0.2,0,Not specified whether cooperation is binary or continuous in the Delton et al. 2011b model; cannot infer from this summary.,0.1,N/A,No human players and no explicit public-goods endowment structure is described; variable not applicable.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined or numerically reported; model is of direct reciprocity, not a standard linear public good.",0.1,N/A,No human interaction or communication; agent strategies are hard-coded and evolve.,1,0,"Simulation description does not mention any explicit informational interface like summaries; agents respond to modeled cues, not experimenter-displayed summaries.",0.3,N/A,Model concerns cooperation under uncertainty; punishment is not mentioned as part of this specific simulation.,0.9,0,"No explicit rewarding mechanism is reported; interactions are framed as cooperate/defect in reciprocity, so no identity-linked reward stage.",0.4,0,Agents do not have an experimenter interface or displayed number of rounds; design does not involve explicit disclosure of horizon to participants.,0.5,0,This particular simulation is about the evolution of cooperation in one-shot encounters via direct reciprocity under uncertainty; punishment is not part of the game described.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this simulation.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this simulation.,1,0,No explicit rewarding institution beyond the cooperate/defect payoffs; no separate reward stage is described.,0.8,N/A,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,0,Model is framed in terms of costs and benefits of cooperation in reciprocity; no fixed endowment per round is reported in this summary.,0.2,N/R,Outcome reported is qualitative: agents evolved to 'cooperate even with partners they would never see again'; no normalized cooperation rate is given.,0.2,N/R,"Simulation outcomes described qualitatively across parameter ranges, without specific contribution magnitudes.",0.2,N/R,No reported ratio of realized payoffs to maximum cooperative payoffs.,0.1,N/R,"Article reports evolutionary success of strategies, not raw payoff values.",0.1,N/A,No human participants in an agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly an 'agent-based simulation' with no human subjects.,1,"Agent-based evolutionary model of direct reciprocity under uncertainty. Interactions may be one-shot or repeated; parameters varied include costs and benefits of cooperation, expected length of repeated interactions, and prior probability that an interaction is repeated. Selection favors strategies that cooperate even in encounters that are actually one-shot.","Summary reflects the description: 'agent-based simulations of a human-like ecology' where 'interactions being repeated or one-shot' vary, and cooperation in one-shot encounters evolved.",0.86
10.3389_fpsyg.2016.00799.md,Krasnow et al. 2015 – Group cooperation with modest punishment (agent-based simulation),Article summarizes a simulation from Krasnow et al. 2015 on evolution of group cooperation with punishment; treated here as one modeled condition because specific parameterized treatments are not detailed.,0.84,"Presence of probabilistic, modest-cost punishment mechanism with quantitative individual differences in punitive tendency; ecological assumptions include that punishment recalibrates noncooperators to cooperate, life is long, and group sizes up to 25.","Text notes that once 'evolution [could] work on quantitative individual differences' in punitive tendencies and under assumptions about recalibration, long life, and probabilistic punishment, cooperation evolved in groups up to size 25.",0.8,False,Described purely as a model: 'We have modeled...' and 'the simulation allowed for the possibility of second-order free riding'.,1,False,"Computational evolutionary simulation, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,No laboratory environment; simulation only.,1,True,Explicitly referred to as a simulation where evolution operates on quantitative individual differences.,1,False,Results come from simulated populations rather than closed-form analysis.,1,25,"Article states that cooperation evolved 'including in groups of up to 25, the largest group size we tested'; this is the only concrete group size mentioned, so recorded as representative maximum group size.",0.6,0,Game is embedded in an evolutionary life-history model; specific number of within-life or across-life rounds per agent is not reported.,0.2,0,"Punishment is described as 'probabilistic rather than all-or-none'; this refers to whether punishment occurs, not necessarily contribution choice space. The contribution structure (binary vs continuous) is not specified.",0.2,N/A,"No explicit public-goods endowment framing or initial allocation is specified; simulation level, no human endowments.",1,N/R,Model involves group cooperation supported by punishment but no marginal per capita return is numerically reported.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects; agents do not 'chat' in the sense of experimental communication.,1,0,There is no mention of explicit informational summaries; agents respond according to strategies and payoffs.,0.3,N/A,Identity display to human participants is not meaningful in an agent-based simulation with no human experiment interface.,1,0,No separate rewarded stage is mentioned; focus is on punishment.,0.7,0,Agents are not informed by an experimenter; there is no 'display' of number of rounds.,0.8,1,Core feature of the model is punishment in groups; 'modest punishment can recruit much cooperation' and 'everyone in a group is a little willing to punish'.,1,N/R,"Punishment is described as 'modest' and 'probabilistic rather than all-or-none (lowering costs)', but no specific per-unit cost is given.",0.2,N/R,Effectiveness of punishment (impact per unit cost) is not quantified; only qualitative description that 'punishment recalibrates noncooperators to cooperate'.,0.2,0,Model focuses on punishment as mechanism to sustain cooperation; rewards are not mentioned.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,Model references costs and benefits of cooperation and punishment but does not specify a fixed per-round endowment.,0.2,N/R,Outcomes reported qualitatively as 'this punishment sustained cooperation'; no explicit proportion of cooperative contributions is reported.,0.2,N/R,Simulation does not report raw contribution quantities in this summary.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metric relative to maximum possible group payoff is provided.,0.1,N/R,"Results are described in terms of evolution of cooperation and punishment, not absolute payoffs.",0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Explicitly a simulation study ('we have modeled', 'the simulation allowed').",1,"Evolutionary simulation of group cooperation with punishment. Individuals have quantitative punitive tendencies. When someone behaves noncooperatively, group members may probabilistically punish, which recalibrates the target to cooperate in the future, preventing re-offense against the punisher. Life is long so that future cooperation can repay the cost of punishment. Punishment is modest in magnitude and probabilistic, which lowers its costs. Cooperation and some level of punishment evolve and are stable in groups up to size 25, even though second-order free riding is possible.",Paraphrases the description: 'Once we allowed evolution to work on quantitative individual differences... evolution easily and reliably created organisms with some willingness to punish (including in groups of up to 25)... punishment recalibrates noncooperators to cooperate... life is long enough that this later cooperation repeats... punishment is probabilistic rather than all-or-none... agents nonetheless evolved to be punitive and this punishment sustained cooperation.',0.9
