10.1371_journal.pone.0200965.md,Meta-sample of 96 network cooperation game conditions,"Paper aggregates 33 experiments comprising 96 experimental conditions from 18 prior studies; results are only reported at this pooled meta-analytic level, not per original condition.",0.9,"Structural and institutional features of original experiments jointly varied across conditions (network structure, network fluidity, reputation tracking, punishment institutions, game type, payoffs, group sizes, partners, periods).","The analysis treats each original treatment condition as a trial differing along these dimensions, but exact per-condition mappings are not reported in this paper.",0.8,True,All underlying studies are human-subject experiments on cooperation games.,1,True,All underlying data are from controlled laboratory/online experiments manipulating game/network conditions.,1,True,"Original studies are standard economic/social science experiments (lab or controlled online); paper treats them as experimental data, not field or observational data.",0.9,False,No agent-based or purely computational simulations are analyzed; only human experiments are used.,1,False,"Paper provides empirical meta-analysis, not formal analytical models.",1,0,Group sizes vary widely across the 96 conditions (from 8 to 625 players per group); no single value applies at this meta-analytic level.,1,0,Number of periods varies from 6 to >90 across experiments; the paper does not aggregate to a single value.,1,1,"Contribution decisions in PD, PG, and helping games are standard multi-level binary or continuous choices, not restricted to all-or-nothing full-endowment moves across all experiments.",0.7,-1,Not defined consistently across original studies and not reported in this meta-analysis.,0,-1,Marginal per capita returns differ by game and parameterization in each original experiment; the paper does not summarize a common MPCR.,0,0,None of the summarized designs mention direct communication or chat; standard network cooperation experiments typically disallow chat.,0.6,0,The meta-paper does not describe feedback detail (earnings or punishment summaries) consistently across underlying studies.,0,0,"Identity revelation for punishers/targets is not documented at the pooled level; designs differ and details are in original papers, not in this summary.",0,0,No reward mechanisms (only punishment or none) are described; punishment identity conditions are also not summarized.,0.9,0,The meta-paper does not specify whether participants were told the number of periods across all experiments.,0,1,"The pooled data explicitly include conditions with and without punishment institutions; at the meta-sample level, punishment is a key manipulated factor that is present in some conditions.",1,-1,Punishment costs vary across experiments (and some have no punishment); the meta-paper does not report a unified per-unit cost.,0,-1,Damage per punishment unit (multiplier) is not summarized and differs across underlying studies.,0,0,No reward institutions are mentioned; institutional variation is described only in terms of punishment vs no punishment and its forms.,0.95,-1,Reward mechanisms are absent in the summarized designs.,1,-1,Reward mechanisms are absent in the summarized designs.,1,-1,"Per-round endowments differ by original study (e.g., 10 or 20 tokens in various PG designs) and are not harmonized in this paper.",0,-1,This meta-study’s main outcome is inequality in final wealth (Gini); average cooperation is used descriptively in mechanisms but no normalized contribution rate is reported at pooled level.,0,-1,Raw contributions per condition are not tabulated; only qualitative findings on cooperation are summarized.,0,-1,"The study does not report efficiency (group payoff relative to maximum) at the pooled level; focus is on inequality (Gini, Theil).",0,-1,Only inequality in final earnings is summarized; average payoffs are referenced qualitatively but not given numerically for the pooled sample.,0,N/R,Underlying experiments were conducted in multiple countries/labs; the meta-paper lists author affiliations but not participant nationalities for each study.,0,N/R,Age ranges or means are not given in this summary article; such details are in the original experimental papers.,0,N/R,No gender composition information is provided in the meta-paper.,0,N/R,"Educational background (e.g., students vs general population) is not described at the pooled level.",0,On site,"All referenced studies are typical economic lab or controlled experiments (some possibly online), but exact modality for each is not harmonized; ‘On site’ chosen as the closest common category versus ‘Field’ or ‘Observational’.",0.6,"Meta-analysis of 96 experimental conditions from 18 prior network cooperation studies using Prisoner’s Dilemma, Public Good, and helping games, varying network structure (random, small-world, cycles, cliques, lattices, scale-free, pairs), network fluidity (fixed, randomly rewired, strategically updated), reputation tracking (various histories or none), and punishment institutions (no punishment, standard peer punishment, sequential, consensus, solo). Main dependent variable is final wealth inequality measured by the Gini coefficient at the group level.",Summarizes the key features of the design spectrum as explicitly described in Table 1 and the methods section at the level at which this paper reports results.,1
