10.1016_j.neucom.2024.128510.md,Monte Carlo simulation – square lattice PDG with punishment and periodic Q-learning (baseline parameter set),"Single core model on L×L lattice; baseline parameters repeatedly referenced (e.g., Fig. 4 uses α=0.1, γ=0.1, b=1.02, σ=1.5, ρ=0.3, β=0.8, ε=0.02) and varied one at a time in subsequent analyses.",0.86,"Temptation payoff b; period interval τ; punishment cost ρ; learning rate α; discount factor γ; aspiration level σ; exploration probability ε; network structure (square lattice, random, scale-free, small‑world). This row represents the general configuration for the square lattice case under the baseline values: b≈1.02–2, τ≈2–500, ρ≈0.1–0.7, α≈0.1–0.8, γ≈0.1–0.8, σ≈1.1–1.7, ε≈0.02–1, L≈100–200.","Paper systematically varies these parameters in figures (Figs. 4–13). Exact full grid not specified, but ranges for each are given.",0.7,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on networks; no human participants mentioned.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational simulations.,1,False,No physical or online lab; only in silico simulations.,1,True,They explicitly state they use Monte Carlo (MC) simulations of an evolutionary game model.,1,False,No closed‑form analytical derivations of equilibria; results all from numerical simulations.,0.95,10000,"Baseline lattice in main results uses L=100 (Fig. 4 caption: L=100), so N=L×L=100×100=10000. Some figures also use L=200, but 100 is the clearly specified baseline size.",0.7,100000,They state: “MC simulations are conducted … with a total step size of 1×10^5.” Interpreted as number of time steps (rounds).,1,1,"This is a two‑strategy Prisoner’s Dilemma; players choose C or D, not continuous contribution levels. For the requested coding, 1 indicates that ‘all or nothing’ restriction does NOT apply; however here actions are strictly binary. Given schema definition, closer analogue is that contributions are all‑or‑nothing so set field to 1.",0.4,N/A,"Model is a pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs R,S,T,P; there is no explicit endowment allocation into public vs private accounts.",1,N/R,This is not a public goods game; no group multiplier or MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects; agents do not have a communication channel beyond payoff‑based interactions.,1,0,Agents only observe neighbor payoffs when updating via Fermi; there is no global or historical summary information presented.,0.8,0,Punishment is modeled structurally via game matrices M2 and M3; there is no modeled notion of identities or visibility of who punished whom.,0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism in the model; only punishment.,1,0,Agents are not given meta‑information about horizon length T in the simulation description.,0.8,1,Core of the model is a punishment mechanism where cooperators choosing action P become punishers with cost ρ and fine β.,1,0.3,"Baseline analyses often fix ρ=0.3 (e.g., Fig. 6 caption: ρ=0.3; also used in other figures).",0.9,0.8,"Punishment amount on defectors is β; baseline value in multiple analyses is β=0.8 (e.g., Fig. 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 captions).",0.95,0,Model only includes costly punishment; no separate rewarding action or parameter is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Standard weak PDG payoff matrix (R=1, S=P=0, T=b) is used; no explicit endowment per round is defined.",0.6,1,"In PDG framing, cooperation corresponds to full ‘contribution’ and defection to zero. The primary DV is the fraction of cooperators f_c, which is effectively the average normalized contribution level; when they report e.g. f_c up to 1, that is full contribution.",0.85,N/R,"They only report the fraction of cooperators f_c, not raw tokens or amounts.",0.7,N/R,They do not compute overall efficiency as payoff relative to a maximum; only cooperation frequency is plotted.,0.9,N/R,"No aggregate payoff or welfare numbers are reported, only qualitative payoffs and cooperation levels.",0.95,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents.,1,No human,Work consists solely of Monte Carlo simulations on networks without human subjects.,1,"Spatial weak Prisoner’s Dilemma on an L×L square lattice with von Neumann neighborhood and periodic boundary conditions. Payoff matrix M1 uses R=1, S=0, P=0, T=b>1. Cooperative agents may choose action P (becoming punishers) or I (ignore), yielding transitions to games M2 and M3 with punishment cost ρ to punishers and fine β to defectors. Strategy updates combine Q-learning with periodic switching between Fermi imitation and aspiration-driven update every τ time steps. Agents are initialized with random C/D strategies and initial action I, Q-tables initialized to 0. Simulations typically run T=1×10^5 steps, with statistics (cooperation frequency f_c) averaged over last 5000 steps and 10 independent runs. Additional robustness checks are run on Erdős–Rényi random networks, Barabási–Albert scale‑free networks, and Watts–Strogatz small‑world networks of size N=2000 and mean degree 4.","Summarizes detailed model description from Section 2, Algorithm 1, and captions for Figs. 4–13.",0.98
10.3389_fnbeh.2020.00102.md,N/A – theoretical perspective review (no original experiment),"The article explicitly describes itself as a theoretical perspective paper and summarizes prior work; it does not report new experimental, simulation, or observational data.",1,N/A,No original conditions or manipulations are introduced; only prior studies are reviewed.,1,False,The paper does not collect or analyze new data from human participants; it is a conceptual/theoretical review.,1,False,No controlled experiment is conducted by the authors; all experimental details refer to other published studies.,1,False,There is no new lab or field experiment; methods described correspond to previous literature.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported.,1,False,"The work is narrative/theoretical, not a formal analytical model with proofs.",1,0,No new game or participant group is run; parameter is not applicable.,1,0,No iterative game is conducted by the authors; parameter is not applicable.,1,0,No contribution decision is implemented in this paper; configuration is not applicable.,1,N/A,No endowment or contribution structure is implemented; no human game is run by the authors.,1,N/R,The paper does not specify marginal per capita returns for any newly run game.,1,0,No interactive experimental platform is used; configuration not applicable.,1,0,No experimental feedback is implemented by the authors.,1,0,No punishment mechanism is implemented in a new experiment.,1,0,No reward mechanism is implemented in a new experiment.,1,0,There are no rounds in an implemented game.,1,0,"Although altruistic punishment paradigms are discussed, no new punishment option is implemented with participants.",1,N/A,No punishment is actually implemented by the authors.,1,N/A,No punishment technology is implemented.,1,0,The paper focuses on punishment; no new reward option is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward technology is implemented.,1,0,No endowment is given to participants in this paper; all numerical examples are from prior studies.,1,N/A,No contributions are measured; no new behavioral data are collected.,1,N/A,No contributions are measured.,1,N/R,No group payoff data are collected by the authors.,1,N/A,No groups or payoffs are realized in a new experiment.,1,N/A,No participants are recruited by the authors.,1,N/A,No sample is collected.,1,N/A,No sample is collected.,1,N/A,No sample is collected.,1,No human,The work is a theoretical perspective and literature review without new human or field data.,1,"The article reviews altruistic punishment and third-party punishment paradigms (e.g., de Quervain et al., Djamshidian et al.), but does not implement any new game; all task details (such as a trust game with £10 endowment, quadrupling, and costly punishment) refer to previously published studies.",Summary of how the paper discusses games while clarifying that these are from prior literature.,1
10.1016_j.leaqua.2023.101695.md,Meta-analysis unit: exogenous leadership vs simultaneous play in linear public goods games,Paper is a literature review/meta-analysis; the smallest coherent experimental unit with specified design parameters is the exogenous-leadership vs simultaneous comparison aggregated over 12 qualifying lab studies.,0.73,"Move structure and leadership: sequential play with one exogenously assigned leader (first mover) vs simultaneous contributions without a leader in linear public goods games; no additional leadership media (no punishment, reward, or cheap-talk leadership).","Section 'A plain meta-analysis of exogenous leadership' states inclusion criteria: exogenous sequential treatment with one leader and a simultaneous control, linear PG, no other medium of leadership, lab.",0.86,True,All underlying studies are lab public goods experiments with human subjects as described in the search and inclusion criteria.,0.99,True,The included studies are controlled laboratory experiments contrasting treatments.,0.99,True,Inclusion criterion (c) in meta-analysis section requires lab experiments; field experiments are treated separately.,0.99,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations are analyzed in the meta-analysis.,0.99,False,Analytical models are discussed but the focal results are from empirical lab studies.,0.99,4,"Most qualifying VCM lab studies use 4-person groups; explicitly mentioned for many (e.g., MPCR discussion for 4-player case, and many entries in Table 3). Using the modal design as representative; exact distribution over 3–8 not given.",0.55,16,"Repeated games; several key included studies use 16 pre-voting rounds (e.g., Güth et al. 2007, Levati et al. 2007, Rivas & Sutter 2011). Meta-analysis builds effect sizes from averages 'over all rounds of the repeated game'; 16 is the most typical number where stated.",0.6,1,Inclusion criteria require non-binary contribution decisions; standard linear VCM allows any amount between 0 and endowment.,0.98,0,Standard VCM: endowment is initially entirely private; no pre-funded public account is mentioned.,0.9,0.4,Typical MPCR noted in theory section for 4-player games ('usual MPCR of a=0.4') and many included studies in Table 3 use 0.4; exact meta-analytic average across 12 studies not directly reported.,0.6,0,Inclusion criteria for the plain meta-analysis exclude other 'mediums of leadership' like leading-by-words; no communication/cheap talk is part of these baseline sequential vs simultaneous comparisons.,0.9,1,"Standard PG experiments provide feedback on group contributions/earnings each round; though not restated, this is conventional and some included studies explicitly provide it.",0.7,0,"Meta-analysis excludes treatments with centralized punishment/reward; no punishment stage exists, so no punishment identities are shown.",0.98,0,Reward mechanisms are excluded in the plain exogenous-leadership meta-analysis; thus no reward identities can be shown.,0.98,1,"Standard finitely repeated VCM designs inform subjects of the number of rounds; not explicitly re-stated, but implied by typical lab protocol and by references to periods 1–16.",0.7,0,"Inclusion criterion (b) for the plain meta-analysis requires 'no other medium of leadership but leading-by-example'; punishment is discussed only in later WLS regressions, not in this exogenous-leadership set.",0.98,N/A,No punishment stage is present in the treatments used for the plain exogenous-leadership meta-analysis.,1.0,N/A,No punishment mechanism implemented.,1.0,0,Reward options are also excluded as 'other mediums of leadership' in the meta-analysis inclusion criteria.,0.98,N/A,No reward stage exists in these treatments.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1.0,N/R,"The meta-analysis text does not report the token endowment per round, and it varies by underlying study.",0.0,0.32,"The main meta-analytic effect is a standardized mean difference (Hedges g) of 0.32 in contributions between sequential (with exogenous leader) and simultaneous play; this is not directly a normalized contribution level but is the only comparable quantitative outcome reported for this aggregated unit, so recorded here as the key cooperation outcome.",0.7,N/R,The meta-analysis aggregates standardized differences across heterogeneous currency scales; no single raw amount is reported.,0.0,N/R,"Although the paper interprets higher contributions as higher efficiency, no normalized payoff-over-maximum efficiency index is reported for the meta-analysis subset.",0.0,N/R,Group earnings are discussed qualitatively but not reported as a single pooled value for the exogenous-leadership meta-analysis.,0.0,N/R,"Underlying studies are international (e.g., multiple European and other labs); the meta-analysis does not summarize participant countries.",0.0,N/R,No pooled age information is provided.,0.0,N/R,Gender composition is not summarized.,0.0,N/R,Meta-analysis does not aggregate education information; typical samples are students but this is not explicitly consolidated.,0.3,On site,All included studies are conventional lab experiments run before widespread online lab adoption and are described as 'lab experiments'; default assumption is physical lab sessions.,0.85,"Linear voluntary contribution mechanism (public goods game) with summation technology, finitely repeated, non-binary contributions; treatment compares simultaneous contributions with no leader to sequential play where one exogenously assigned first mover acts as leader, observed by followers.","Described in 'The voluntary contribution mechanism', 'A plain meta-analysis of exogenous leadership', and design criteria for WLS analysis.",0.96
