10.1016_j.chaos.2023.113552.md,Well-mixed population – shared-punishment voluntary public goods game,"Single analytical/simulation condition for well-mixed populations with cooperators, defectors, and loners under shared-punishment; parameters r, α, β, N, σ varied within the same model.",0.95,"Fine β, punishment cost α, multiplication factor r, group size N, loner payoff σ; all varied parametrically in a voluntary public goods game with shared-punishment in a well-mixed population.","Section 3.1 and Fig. 1–3 analyze how equilibria depend on β, α, r, N, σ in the same theoretical condition.",0.95,False,No human participants or empirical data; purely theoretical analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled lab/field experiment but an analytical evolutionary game model.,1,False,No laboratory component; purely theoretical.,1,True,"Uses numerical analysis of replicator dynamics and phase diagrams for parameter sweeps, but all without human subjects.",0.9,True,Derives closed-form payoffs and replicator dynamics; performs analytical stability analysis.,1,5,Model defines group size as N; Figures 1–3 use N=5 as the explicit parameter value for illustrative dynamics in well-mixed populations.,0.7,1,"Evolutionary game is repeated over generations, but the stage game payoff is defined for a single public goods interaction; map this as one round per interaction.",0.5,0,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount c=1; defectors contribute 0; there is no choice of intermediate contribution levels, so contributions are effectively all-or-nothing rather than continuous choice.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects or interface with default allocations; only strategy-based contributions in a theoretical model.,1,N/R,"Total contributions multiplied by r and divided among S participants; MPCR would be r/S which varies with S, so a single constant MPCR is not reported.",0.6,N/A,No human interaction or communication; purely theoretical population model.,1,0,Model payoffs depend only on local group composition; no information feedback or summaries of others’ earnings are specified.,0.9,0,Punishment is institutional pool punishment at group level; no individual identities or observability mentioned.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism is modeled; only punishment.,1,0,Continuous-time replicator dynamics; no notion of displayed finite rounds counts.,1,1,Shared-punishment pool with cost α to cooperators and fine β to defectors is the core of the model.,1,1,"Each cooperator shares a total group punishment cost α; since α is the total cost per group, per-unit ‘coin’ is not clearly defined. Set to 1 as unit cost and treat α as total pool cost; this field is structurally required but not directly parameterized.",0.2,1,"Defectors suffer a fixed fine β; per unit cost-to-fine mapping is not decomposed, effectively 1:β. Using 1 as a neutral placeholder for per-coin technology since exact unitization is unspecified.",0.2,0,No rewarding of cooperators is modeled; only punishment and loner payoff.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes fixed amount c=1; this effectively serves as the per-round endowment used entirely if cooperating.,0.8,N/R,"No explicit outcome statistics; study reports equilibria and phase regions, not average contribution measures.",0.1,N/R,Only model parameter c=1 is given; no measured or simulated average contribution amounts are reported as results.,0.1,N/R,Paper focuses on strategy frequencies and stability; does not report normalized efficiency metrics.,0.1,N/R,Provides analytical payoff expressions but not summarized numeric group payoffs as reported outcomes.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure evolutionary game theory and simulations without human subjects.,1,"Voluntary public goods game in a well-mixed population with three strategies (cooperator, defector, loner). Cooperators contribute c=1 to the public good and pay shared pool-punishment cost α; defectors contribute 0 and receive fine β if any cooperator is present; loners do not participate and receive fixed payoff σ. Total contribution is multiplied by factor r (1<r<N) and shared among participants.",Summarized from Section 2 and 3.1 model description and payoff definitions.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2023.113552.md,Structured population – shared-punishment voluntary public goods game on regular network,"Single structured-population condition: same strategies and payoffs as well-mixed model but players located on regular network with degree k and Fermi update rule; parameters β, r, α, σ, k, ω varied.",0.95,"Fine β, punishment cost α, multiplication factor r, network degree k, loner payoff σ, selection intensity ω; all varied in a voluntary public goods game with shared-punishment on a regular network.","Section 3.2 and Figs. 4–7 study equilibria and phase diagrams as functions of β, r, k, ω with fixed α and σ in a single structured-population model.",0.95,False,No human participants or empirical measurements.,1,False,"Mathematical pair-approximation and Monte Carlo simulations, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab environment; simulations only.,1,True,Uses Monte Carlo simulations on networks and numerical solution of pair-approximation ODEs; no human subjects.,1,True,Derives analytical pair-approximation dynamical system for structured populations.,1,N/R,Structured population has N players but N is not numerically specified for these simulations; only neighbor degree k=4 is given.,0.3,1,Public goods interaction defined per group; simulations iterate Monte Carlo steps but not framed as discrete experimental rounds.,0.5,0,"Same contribution rule as well-mixed: cooperators always contribute c=1, defectors 0; no intermediate choice.",0.9,N/A,No human interface or default allocations.,1,N/R,Group interactions of size k+1 with multiplier r; MPCR=r/(k+1) would depend on k but not explicitly reported as such.,0.6,N/A,Agent-based simulations without communication between human participants.,1,0,Agents update via Fermi rule based on neighbor payoff differences; no summary information variables described.,0.9,0,Punishment is institutional at group level; no notion of revealed punisher identity.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism included.,1,0,Simulation time in Monte Carlo steps; no display notion in a user interface.,1,1,Same shared-punishment pool mechanism with cost α and fine β applied on each local group.,1,1,"Total group punishment cost α is fixed and shared by cooperators; per-coin unitization not defined, so 1 used as neutral placeholder.",0.2,1,"Defectors incur fixed fine β when cooperators present; cost-to-fine mapping per unit not decomposed, placeholder 1 used.",0.2,0,No rewards for cooperation are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes fixed amount c=1 in each game; treated as effective endowment per game.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes reported as equilibrium strategy frequencies (p_C, p_D, p_L) and phase regions, not normalized contribution rates.",0.1,N/R,No explicit average contribution statistics; only strategy frequencies and snapshots.,0.1,N/R,No normalized payoff-to-maximum ratios reported; focus is on equilibrium composition and phase boundaries.,0.1,N/R,Accumulated payoffs Π_i are defined but not summarized numerically as group payoffs in results.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based and analytical model on networks; no empirical environment.,1,"Voluntary public goods game on a regular network (degree k, typical k=4). Each player participates in k+1 overlapping public goods games. Strategies: cooperator (contributes c=1 and shares fixed pool-punishment cost α among cooperators), defector (contributes 0 and pays fine β if any cooperator is in group), loner (does not participate in any game and earns fixed payoff σ per game). Total contributions in each group are multiplied by r and equally shared among participants. Evolution follows a Fermi update rule with selection intensity ω and pair-approximation theory used to derive dynamics for frequencies and conditional neighbor distributions.","Summarized from Section 2 and 3.2, including definitions of payoffs, network interaction structure, and update rule.",1
10.1038_s41598-017-12490-5.md,Experiment 1 – Control – Partners,Experiment 1 has a Control treatment; design states both partners (fixed matching) and strangers (random rematching) are implemented. This row is the partner-matching control condition.,0.9,Treatment: Control (no intervention); Matching protocol: Partners (fixed groups of 4),Independent variables are presence/type of intervention after round 10 and matching protocol. Here: no intervention and fixed matching.,0.95,True,Lab experiment with human participants at University of Auckland.,1,True,Controlled randomized laboratory public goods game.,1,True,Conducted in DECIDE laboratory using Veconlab.,1,False,No computer-only simulations described.,1,False,No formal analytical model as a standalone experiment.,1,4,Groups of four are explicitly stated for Experiment 1.,1,20,Experiment 1: participants interact for 20 rounds.,1,1,Participants choose any integer number of tokens from 0 to 10 to contribute.,1,0,All 10 tokens are initially in the private account; they must actively contribute to the public account.,1,0.5,"Tokens in public account are doubled and equally redistributed among 4, giving 2/4 = 0.5 per token.",1,0,No mention of communication; only contribution and (where applicable) punishment decisions via software.,0.9,1,Participants see individual contributions of group members and their own earnings each round.,0.95,0,"No punishment stage in control treatment, so identities of punishers not applicable; encoded as 0 (no identities shown).",0.9,0,No reward mechanism is implemented in any treatment.,1,1,Participants are fully informed about total number of rounds (20) at start.,1,0,Control treatment has no punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism mentioned.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Each participant receives 10 tokens per round.,1,N/R,"Paper reports earnings/efficiency, not contribution rates by treatment and