10.1007_s11403-007-0027-x.md,Agent-based evolutionary model of repeated public goods with punishment,"Section 6 describes a numerical agent-based model with parameters n, r1, r_p, T, adaptation and evolution rules.",1,"Punishment efficiency r_p (varied); group size n (varied e.g., n=8); agents’ punishment coefficients k_i and contributions m_i evolve under selection",Sect. 6 and Figs. 5–6 discuss dependence of evolved k and cooperation on r_p and n.,1,False,"Model uses artificial agents and simulated wealth, not human subjects.",1,False,This is not a controlled human experiment but a computational model.,1,False,No physical lab; purely simulated.,1,True,Explicitly described as an ‘agent-based model’ with repeated plays and computer simulations.,1,False,"Although theory guides it, the section itself is numerical simulation, not closed-form analysis.",1,8,Fig. 6 reports results ‘for n=8’; this is the configuration used in the r_p vs cooperation analysis.,0.9,500,They play the game over T = 500 periods.,1,1,Each agent’s contribution m_i is uniformly drawn between 0 and 20 initially and can take any value within range.,1,N/A,"No human endowment; simulated m_i and k_i are traits, not allocations of an initial endowment.",1,0.2,"Model mimics experimental public good with parameter r1 (< n). Example parameterization given: r1 = 1.6 and n = 8 in Fig. 6, so MPCR = r1/n = 1.6/8 = 0.2.",0.8,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,Agents do not have user interfaces; they respond via programmed rules.,1,N/A,Identity visibility is not modeled as a separate variable for non-human agents.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism and no human participants.,1,N/A,Agents are not ‘shown’ number of rounds; they are trivially iterated in code.,1,1,Agents punish each other according to Pu_{j→i} = k_i(m_j − m_i) for m_j > m_i.,1,1,"Loss from punishing is Pu_{i→j} itself (spent MUs), as in the corresponding experimental game.",0.9,N/R,"Text states they use same r_p as experiments (e.g., r_p = 3.5 or vary r_p), but does not explicitly define per-unit mapping in wealth update formula; assumed analogous but not clearly parameterized per MU.",0.4,0,Model includes only punishment-based feedback.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism simulated.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism simulated.,1,50,Initial ‘wealth’ is set to 50 MUs for each agent.,1,N/R,"They report distributions and quartiles of contributions m_i, but not normalized as rate of an endowment; wealth and contribution are separate state variables.",0.3,N/R,"Figures show quartiles of contribution, but no single aggregate figure is reported.",0.3,N/R,"They discuss stability and cooperation levels, not explicit efficiency ratios.",0,N/R,Total wealth dynamics are described qualitatively; no numerical summary of group payoff.,0,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly an ‘agent-based model’ with artificial agents.,1,"Simulation of repeated public goods game with punishment based on Fehr & Gächter structure: n agents indexed i; each has traits (m_i, k_i), initial wealth 50; at each of T = 500 periods, agents contribute m_i, receive returns from group project with parameter r1 (< n), then punish others per Pu_{j→i} = k_i (m_j − m_i) for m_j > m_i; wealth updated by earnings minus punishment costs and fixed consumption (initially 3 MUs, adaptively changed to keep average wealth stationary); if wealth W_i < m_i, agent is replaced by new agent with (m_j, k_j) drawn around population means; adaptation step lets failing agents randomly adjust (m_i, k_i). r_p is varied (e.g., r_p = 3.5) and n is varied (e.g., 2 ≤ n ≤ 16) to study evolved distribution of k and cooperation.","Summarizes Section 6 algorithm and parameter choices, including figures’ parameter sets.",1
