10.1016_j.ecolecon.2019.106432.md,Treatment trial – Reward rule,Condition where an externally imposed reward rule is introduced in the second (treatment) sequence after a common baseline trial.,1,Management institution design: externally imposed low monetary reward for pumping ≤ 1 hour; random monitoring with 1/5 probability.,Reward rule is one of three institutional designs varied across groups in the treatment trial; this row captures the reward condition.,1,True,Framed field experiment with 600 households (human participants).,1,True,"Controlled, randomized assignment of groups to four institutional treatments including reward.",1,False,"Conducted in villages/wards as a framed field experiment, not in a laboratory.",1,False,"Decisions made by human subjects, not by computer agents.",1,False,"Although a theoretical model is presented, this condition refers to the empirical experiment.",1,4,Four members in a village were randomly selected and grouped at a central point for the CPR experiment.,1,5,Two sequences with five periods each; this treatment trial sequence consists of five periods.,1,1,Participants choose any amount from 0 to 8 hours in 0.5 hour increments; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,"This is a CPR extraction game, not a contribution game; endowment is not pre-allocated between private/public accounts. Default is effectively all in private use.",0.3,N/R,No linear public good structure or MPCR is defined; payoffs come from a quadratic profit function with externalities via groundwater depth.,0,0,Communication is only explicitly allowed in the communication treatment; not mentioned for reward rule.,1,0,Participants are told their own profits and new groundwater level; no mention of others’ profits or punishment/reward summaries.,0.8,0,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,"The rewarded person is selected by die, but identities are not described as revealed to others; individual decisions are private.",0.6,0,Number of periods in each trial was not announced beforehand.,1,0,Only a positive reward is used; no fines.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in reward treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in reward treatment.,1,1,A bonus of 200 tokens is paid if monitored participant pumped ≤ 1 hour.,1,0,"Reward is paid by experimenter, not by other players; thus cost per reward token to participants is effectively zero.",0.8,1,Participant receives 200 tokens if compliant and selected; each rewarded token is 1 token of benefit to that individual (1:1).,0.9,N/R,No per-period token endowment is specified; only profits from pumping choices are described.,0,N/R,"Outcomes reported are pumping hours, user types, and welfare/efficiency; no normalized contribution rate is reported.",0,N/R,Paper reports average pumping choices graphically and via regression coefficients but not as raw group- or individual-level means by treatment.,0,0.95,Table 4 reports social efficiency score of 95% for reward in the treatment trial.,1,N/R,"Only relative welfare gains and efficiency scores are reported, not absolute payoffs in tokens.",0,India,"Experiment conducted with households along rural-urban interface of Bengaluru, India.",1,Mean ≈ 44.61 years (overall); reward subgroup mean 47.33 years,Table 1 provides mean ages overall and by treatment; reward treatment mean age is 47.33.,1,Approximately 62% male overall; 66.23% male in reward treatment,Table 1 lists gender shares overall and for each treatment.,1,Mean ≈ 5.96 years schooling overall; 5.54 years in reward treatment,Table 1 gives mean education years per treatment.,1,Field experiment,Described as a framed field experiment conducted in villages/urban wards rather than a lab.,1,Dynamic common-pool groundwater extraction game with 4 players per group; 2 sequences of 5 periods each; initial depth 100 ft; each hour of pumping = 10 water units; depth evolves via bathtub model using aggregated extraction; profits from quadratic benefit minus depth-dependent costs; random monitoring with 1/5 probability via 10-sided die; bonus 200 tokens if monitored player pumps ≤ 1 h.,Summarizes key structural and hydrological aspects of the CPR game under the reward institution.,1
