10.1177_0951629809103970.md,Analytical model – Baseline no-monitoring equilibria (σ1 and σ2),The paper presents an analytical repeated-game model and first analyzes equilibria σ1 (in-group cooperation only) and σ2 (spiral equilibrium) without monitoring.,0.9,"Equilibrium strategy structure without costly monitoring (σ1: in-group cooperation only, σ2: spiral inter-ethnic cooperation with conflict trigger); parameters include p (out-group interaction density), α, β, δ, Tin, Tout.",Independent conditions are different strategy profiles and associated parameter constraints; there is no experimental manipulation in the empirical sense.,0.9,False,No human subjects; the study is purely theoretical.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only theoretical equilibrium analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting; analytical model only.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulation is described; only closed-form reasoning.,0.9,True,The paper derives SPNE conditions and comparative statics analytically.,1,2,"Stage game is a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma, though embedded in larger populations n; per interaction it is 2 players.",0.9,0,"Infinite horizon repeated game (t = 1,2,...) with discount factor δ; no fixed finite number of rounds.",1,1,"Action set is binary {C,D}; there is no continuous contribution amount, so ‘can contribute any amount’ vs ‘all-or-nothing’ is best coded as allowing only discrete cooperation/defection, which maps to all-or-nothing=1 under the provided binary schema.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments; analytical PD with C/D actions and no human subjects.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined or computable from given payoffs.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication is not modeled as an experimental option.,1,0,"Information structure is described in terms of observability of actions and identities, not payoff summaries; no mention of summary feedback.",0.7,1,"Within a group, identities and actions of members in in-group interactions are perfectly observed; discovered defectors’ identities are known to all peers.",0.9,0,"No reward mechanism is modeled, only punishment (conflict and peer punishment).",1,0,Infinite-horizon repeated game with discounting; no finite known number of rounds is displayed or relevant.,0.9,1,Punishment via in-group punishment phases and out-group ethnic conflict is central to the equilibria.,1,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost of punishing action is defined; costs are implicit via foregone payoffs.,0.6,N/R,No parametric mapping from punishment investment to victim loss is given.,0.6,0,"Only sanctions (peer punishment, conflict) are modeled; no positive rewards.",1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs are normalized PD payoffs; no explicit per-period ‘endowment’ is defined.,0.7,N/R,No empirical data; cooperation probabilities are determined endogenously but not measured as a dependent variable.,0.6,N/R,No token contributions; binary actions only and no empirical measurement.,0.6,N/R,"Theoretical payoffs per period (e.g., 1 or 1-p) are compared qualitatively but not normalized as an explicit efficiency measure in [0,1].",0.6,N/R,Expected payoffs are given symbolically; no single numeric value is designated as a measured dependent variable.,0.7,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical model.,1,No human,Model is analytical with no empirical implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with random matching in two groups A and B of size n, with k out-group matches per period (p = k/n), payoffs (C,C)=(1,1), unilateral defection gives defector α>1 and cooperator -β<0, mutual defection (0,0); strategies σ1 and σ2 specify punishment phases Tin and Tout and conflict vs peace phases.",Summarizes the key structure of the baseline analytical model without costly monitoring.,1
10.1177_0951629809103970.md,Analytical model – Collective peer punishment without monitoring cost (σ3),"σ3 is introduced as an equilibrium of collective peer punishment supplementing the spiral equilibrium in the no-noise, no-monitoring-cost setting.",0.95,"Equilibrium strategy profile with collective peer punishment of all suspects after out-group defection (σ3), parameters: p, α, β, δ, Tin, Tout, n, k; no costly monitoring (mI=0).",Independent condition is that peer punishment is collective among suspects and there is no costly monitoring technology used.,0.95,False,"Purely theoretical equilibrium analysis, no human subjects.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment; analytical model.,1,False,No computational simulation is described for σ3.,0.9,True,SPNE conditions for σ3 are derived analytically (Lemma 2).,1,2,Interactions remain pairwise PD between two individuals.,0.9,0,Infinite-horizon repeated game; no fixed finite rounds.,1,1,"Action space is {C,D}; no partial contributions.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game; no humans.,1,N/R,Not a public-goods setting.,1,N/A,No human interaction interface; theoretical model.,1,0,Only actions and identities (in-group) and defections (out-group) are modeled; no payoff summaries.,0.7,1,"Within-group, identities and action histories are known; all suspects or revealed defectors are identifiable to peers.",0.9,0,No rewards are introduced.,1,0,Infinite-horizon setting; no displayed number of rounds.,0.9,1,Collective peer punishment phases and conflict punish defection.,1,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost; punishment is via reduced payoffs during punishment and conflict phases.,0.6,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment action to impact is parameterized aside from payoff structure.,0.6,0,No positive sanctions or rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/R,"Game is defined directly in terms of payoff outcomes, not endowments.",0.7,N/R,"Equilibrium behavior is cooperation vs defection, but no empirical dependent variable is estimated.",0.6,N/R,"No continuous amounts, no measured data.",0.6,N/R,The paper notes that σ3 yields per-period expected payoff 1 (in no-noise ideal) but does not formalize it as a normalized efficiency metric.,0.7,N/R,Group-level payoff under σ3 is discussed qualitatively; no single numeric DV is reported.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical equilibrium model only.,1,"Same repeated PD and random matching structure as baseline, but strategy σ3 adds collective peer punishment: after any out-group defection, all group members who interacted with outsiders in that period enter an in-group punishment phase, in addition to possible ethnic conflict of length Tout.",Captures the distinct mechanism in this condition (collective punishment of suspects).,1
10.1177_0951629809103970.md,Analytical model – Peer monitoring equilibrium without noise (σ4),"σ4 introduces costly peer monitoring with probability r(mI) of identifying out-group defectors and individual peer punishment, still in the no-noise case.",0.95,"Equilibrium with costly peer monitoring and individual peer punishment (σ4); independent parameter is monitoring effort mI (chosen to m*), with r(mI) increasing in mI, and other parameters p, α, β, δ, Tin, Tout.",The defining difference is the presence of costly monitoring and identification-based peer punishment compared to earlier equilibria.,0.95,False,Purely analytical; no subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory component.,1,False,No simulation reported for σ4; conditions given symbolically.,0.9,True,Proposition 1 derives incentive-compatibility conditions analytically.,1,2,Pairwise PD per interaction.,0.9,0,Infinite-horizon repeated game.,1,1,Binary C/D actions only.,0.6,N/A,No contribution endowments; no human subjects.,1,N/R,Not a linear PGG.,1,N/A,No communication protocol with humans.,1,0,"Monitoring reveals identities of defectors, not payoff summaries.",0.7,1,Identities of defectors revealed with probability r(mI) and become common knowledge within the group.,0.95,0,No rewards modeled.,1,0,Discounted infinite horizon; no displayed N.,0.9,1,Individual peer punishment and potential ethnic conflict punish deviations.,1,N/R,"Costs are modeled as lost payoff or monitoring cost mI, not as per-unit punishment costs.",0.6,N/R,"r(mI) is monitoring technology, not damage per punishment token; no direct punishment-production parameter.",0.6,0,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Game defined via payoffs; endowments not specified.,0.7,N/R,Equilibrium cooperation is predicted but not empirically measured.,0.6,N/R,No continuous contribution amounts or data.,0.6,N/R,"Expected per-period payoff (1−m*) is derived, but not framed as normalized efficiency.",0.7,N/R,"Group payoff is discussed symbolically (e.g., 1−m*) but not as a concrete numeric DV.",0.7,N/A,Theoretical model only.,1,N/A,Theoretical model only.,1,N/A,Theoretical model only.,1,N/A,Theoretical model only.,1,No human,Analytical game-theoretic model.,1,"Same repeated PD and matching structure, but with group-chosen monitoring effort mI that induces detection probability r(mI) for intentional out-group defectors. Identified defectors suffer individual peer punishment phases; conflict phases as in σ2.",Summarizes the distinctive costly monitoring feature of σ4.,1
10.1177_0951629809103970.md,Analytical model with noise – Modified spiral equilibrium (σ5),Section 4.2 defines σ5 as the spiral equilibrium modified for noisy interactions (ε>0) and tolerance Q.,0.98,"Equilibrium strategy with no monitoring and spiral enforcement under noise (σ5); independent parameters include noise ε, tolerance threshold Q, and standard parameters p, α, β, δ, Tin=1, Tout.","The distinctive independent condition is tolerance Q (maximum tolerated perceived defections) under misinterpretation probability ε, without peer monitoring.",0.98,False,The model remains analytical; no data from humans.,1,False,No experiment is conducted.,1,False,Not run in a lab.,1,True,"Table 2 reports computed per-period ex ante payoffs V5 for specific parameter values (n=1000, k=100, p=.1, δ=.95, Tout=10, α=1.2, β=2, various ε and Q), implying numerical evaluation of the analytical formulas.",0.9,True,"Equilibrium conditions (Lemma 3(i)) and payoff formulas are derived analytically, then numerically evaluated.",1,2,Interactions remain pairwise PD.,0.9,0,Infinite-horizon with discounting; ethnic conflict lasts Tout periods when triggered.,1,1,Binary C/D choices.,0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game; no human subjects.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are PD, not public goods.",1,N/A,No human communication channel.,1,0,"Players observe counts of perceived defections via the model, but no explicit payoff summary display is modeled.",0.7,1,"Within-group in-group identities and actions are perfectly observed; for out-group, only that at least Q+1 perceived defections occurred is known, not individual identities to outsiders, but peers know identities in their own group.",0.7,0,No rewards modeled.,1,0,Game is infinite-horizon; length Tout is a parameter but not information to players in an experiment.,0.9,1,Punishment via ethnic conflict lasting Tout periods still exists.,1,N/R,No explicit cost per punishment action; punishment is via reduced expected payoffs.,0.6,N/R,No parametric mapping from punishment tokens to loss.,0.6,0,No rewards introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are given directly, no separate endowment parameter.",0.7,N/R,"Contribution is cooperation action; equilibrium cooperation is theoretical, not simulated behavior of agents beyond equilibrium.",0.6,N/R,No continuous amounts used.,0.6,N/R,"Table 2 reports V5 payoffs, but not normalized as efficiency 0–1.",0.7,N/R,"V5 is per-period expected payoff per player, not explicit group-total payoff.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical plus numerical evaluation only.,1,"Same repeated PD with noise: with probability ε, a cooperation is perceived as defection by outsiders; each period there are k out-group matches, and the number of perceived defections #DI ~ Binomial(k, ε). Under σ5, groups remain at peace as long as perceived defections in each group do not exceed Q; if either exceeds Q, ethnic conflict of length Tout is triggered. Table 2 shows for ε=0, .01, .05 which (ε,Q) pairs support SPNE and the corresponding per-period payoffs V5.",Describes the main structural additions of noise and tolerance Q relevant to σ5.,1
10.1177_0951629809103970.md,Analytical model with noise – Collective peer punishment (σ6),σ6 is defined as the equilibrium of collective peer punishment under noise and tolerance Q.,0.98,"Equilibrium with collective peer punishment under noise (σ6); independent parameters ε (noise), Q (tolerance), and core parameters n, k, p, δ, Tout, α, β; no costly monitoring (mI=0).","Main differentiator is collective punishment of all suspects when perceived defections exceed Q, within a noisy environment.",0.98,False,No empirical data.,1,False,Not a real experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Table 2 includes V6 payoffs computed for parameter grid (various ε and Q) using the analytical formulas.,0.9,True,Lemma 3(ii) and Appendix derive conditions and payoff V6 analytically.,1,2,Still two players per PD encounter.,0.9,0,Infinite horizon with discounting and conflict length Tout.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect.,0.6,N/A,No endowed contributions and no human players.,1,N/R,Not a PGG.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,Model does not include payoff summary feedback.,0.7,1,"Within a group, it is known which members interacted with outsiders (suspects) and thus who is punished collectively.",0.9,0,No reward system.,1,0,"Infinite-horizon setting; Tout is a parameter, not a displayed count.",0.9,1,Collective peer punishment and ethnic conflict persist.,1,N/R,Punishment effects realized via reduced payoffs; no per-token cost parameter.,0.6,N/R,No punishment technology parameter; only the structure of the punishment phase.,0.6,0,No mention of rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment variable.,0.7,N/R,"Cooperation is modeled, but no empirical DV.",0.6,N/R,No continuous contributions.,0.6,N/R,"Table 2 lists V6 expected per-period payoffs for various ε and Q, but not as normalized efficiencies.",0.8,N/R,Reported payoffs V6 are per individual; group totals are not separately reported.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical plus numerical computations only.,1,"Noisy repeated PD with collective peer punishment: when at least Q+1 perceived defections occur in a group, all members who interacted with outsiders that period enter an in-group punishment phase; conflict rule is as in σ5. Table 2 shows V6 values for ε = .05, .01, 0 across Q, with dashes where SPNE cannot be sustained.",Highlights the difference from σ5 via collective punishment of suspects.,1
10.1177_0951629809103970.md,Analytical model with noise and costly monitoring – Peer monitoring equilibrium (σ7) with simulation parameterizations,"σ7 is the peer monitoring equilibrium under noise with tolerance Q and monitoring cost; Table 2 and 3 numerically evaluate V7 for different monitoring efficiencies c, tolerances Q, and conflict lengths Tout.",0.99,"Equilibrium with peer monitoring under noise (σ7); independent parameters include ε (0, .01, .05), tolerance Q, monitoring efficiency parameter c in r(m)=c(m/p)^{1/10}, conflict length Tout (1,10,100), and network parameters n=1000, k=100, p=.1, δ=.95, α=1.2, β=2.","Key dimensions explored numerically in Tables 2 and 3 are ε, Q, monitoring efficiency c, and Tout; these determine whether a SPNE exists and the payoff V7.",0.99,False,No real participants; model is theoretical and numerically evaluated.,1,False,No controlled experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,"Tables 2 and 3 give explicit numerical V7 values computed from the analytical formulas for chosen parameter combinations, corresponding to simulation/calculation of the model.",1,True,Proposition 2 provides the analytical SPNE condition and expressions for Vout and V7 before numerical evaluation.,1,2,Each interaction is between two individuals in a PD.,0.9,0,Infinite-horizon repeated game; conflict lasts Tout periods when triggered.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect choices.,0.6,N/A,No token endowments or public good contributions.,1,N/R,Not a public-goods payoff structure.,1,N/A,No human communication; purely theoretical and numerical.,1,0,"Information structure specifies action observability and monitoring outcomes, not payoff summary displays.",0.7,1,"Within a group, monitoring reveals the identity of intentional defectors with probability r(mI), and histories are known to all peers.",0.95,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Infinite horizon; players discount future but do not have a fixed known end.,0.9,1,Punishment via in-group individual punishment phases and ethnic conflict is central to σ7.,1,N/R,"Monitoring cost mI (per-period tax) is parameterized, but there is no per-unit cost of punishment tokens; punishment is behavioral (ostracism).",0.7,N/R,"Monitoring technology r(m)=c(m/p)^{1/10} governs detection, not direct punishment magnitude.",0.8,0,The model considers only sanctions.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment parameter; payoffs are direct.,0.7,N/R,Cooperation rates are not simulated beyond equilibrium; tables focus on payoffs.,0.6,N/R,No continuous contribution amounts are used.,0.6,N/R,V7 provides per-period expected payoffs but is not explicitly normalized as efficiency 0–1.,0.8,N/R,"Tables show individual V7 payoffs (e.g., .9095, .9847, .9969) for different parameters; group sums are not separately reported.",0.8,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Analytical model evaluated numerically.,1,"Noisy repeated PD with peer monitoring: each group chooses monitoring cost mI (determining r(mI)=c(m/p)^{1/10}), identities of intentional out-group defectors are revealed with probability r(mI) and punished individually via in-group punishment phases. Under σ7, groups tolerate up to Q perceived defections per period before triggering an ethnic conflict of length Tout. Table 2 reports V7 for ε∈{0,.01,.05}, Q from 0 up, and monitoring efficiencies c∈{.2,.1,.08} with fixed Tout=10. Table 3 varies Tout∈{1,10,100} for ε=.01 and c=.2 vs c=.08, reporting resulting V7.",Summarizes the structural aspects plus specific simulation parameterizations and outcomes reported.,1
10.1093_jleo_ewu019.md,No Jury - No Info,Baseline treatment label as defined in Section 2: 'In the baseline treatment (No Jury - No Info)...'.,1,"No Jury mechanism; No group information sharing (Info=0, Jury=0)",2x2 design varying Info (information sharing) and Jury (in‑group punishment). This condition has neither.,1,True,Human subjects in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with pre-specified treatments.,1,True,Run in a physical experimental laboratory at a university; subjects seated at computer terminals.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data are from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper reports empirical results, not purely formal theoretical model.",1,16,"Each session has 16 subjects (eight of each color, four senders and four receivers).",1,10,Subjects maintain roles over 'an entire 10-period session'.,1,1,Sender can send 'any integer amount between 0 and 10 ECU'; receiver can return any amount between 0 and the amount received.,1,0,Endowment is 10 ECU privately held by each player at start of each period; nothing is precommitted to a joint account.,1,N/A,"Trust/investment game, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",1,1,Receivers can send pre-play non-binding messages specifying intended return percentage; this is cheap-talk communication.,1,0,Baseline: 'subjects have no jury to enforce contracts and can only acquire group reputation information through their own experience.' No info on others’ outcomes.,1,0,No punishment mechanism exists; hence no punished identities displayed.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism in the design.,1,0,Subjects 'were not told the number of periods'; no evidence they see remaining periods.,1,0,No Jury and no other punishment option stated.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No in-group rewards or bonuses are described.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,10,Each sender and receiver 'are endowed with 10 experimental currency units (ECU)'.,1,0.5233,"Average amount sent is 5.233 ECU out of 10 (Table 1), so normalized sending rate is 5.233/10.",1,5.233,Table 1 'Amount Sent' mean for No Jury - No Info.,1,N/R,Paper does not report welfare as proportion of maximum possible; only raw earnings per period.,0.2,N/R,"Table 1 lists sender and receiver earnings per period, not summed per matching group; maximum group composition not clearly aggregated.",0.2,United States,Subjects recruited from 'a mid-sized university in the United States'.,1,N/R,Only described as university students; no specific age distribution reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0.0,University students,Subjects recruited randomly from the student body of a mid-sized university.,1,On site,Lab sessions with subjects seated at computer terminals and paid cash at end; not online.,1,"Repeated investment trust game with roles fixed (4 senders, 4 receivers per color), random rematching across 10 periods, tripling of amount sent, pre-play cheap-talk message from receiver indicating intended return percentage, only partner’s group color observable; no Jury, no group-level history table.",Summarized from Section 2 description of baseline trust game and matching protocol.,1
