10.1093_jleo_ewu019.md,Jury - No Info,Treatment label as defined in Section 2: 'The Jury - No Info treatment is also identical to the No Jury - No Info treatment with one exception.',1,Jury mechanism enabled (Jury=1); No group information table (Info=0),Treatment introduces in-group punishment (jury) but maintains baseline information structure.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Randomized assignment to treatment condition; controlled manipulation of jury mechanism.,1,True,Conducted in a physical lab like other sessions.,1,False,No mention of computational simulations.,1,False,"Results are experimental, not purely theoretical.",1,16,"Same structure: 16 subjects per session, 4 senders and 4 receivers in each of two colors.",1,10,Same 10-period design.,1,1,Same strategy space for sending and returning as baseline.,1,0,"Endowment is private to each player, 10 ECU.",1,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,1,Same pre-play message protocol.,1,0,"Identical to No Jury - No Info except for jury mechanism; no within-group information table. Only additional information is for jurors in trials, not full summaries each period.",1,0,Trials are anonymous: 'three other receivers of the same color' vote anonymously; identities of punished receivers not revealed beyond color and role.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,Subjects not told number of periods.,1,1,Senders can 'incur a cost of 2 ECU to seek restitution'; jury may require defendant to pay outstanding balance plus dispute cost.,1,2,Sender incurs a cost of 2 ECU to file a dispute.,1,1,"If convicted, 'the defendant pays the plaintiff the outstanding balance plus the dispute cost (2 ECU)'; effectively 1 ECU of punishment cost yields 1 ECU transferred to plaintiff (dispute cost is reimbursed).",0.7,0,"Mechanism is framed and functions purely as punishment / restitution, not extra rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same 10 ECU per player per period.,1,0.6175,Mean Amount Sent is 6.175 ECU (Table 1) out of 10.,1,6.175,Table 1 'Amount Sent' for Jury - No Info.,1,N/R,No efficiency index reported; presence of trial costs complicates back-of-envelope calculation not given in text.,0.2,N/R,"Only average sender and receiver earnings per period provided, not aggregated per group.",0.2,United States,Same subject pool across all treatments.,1,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0,University students,Recruited from a mid-sized U.S. university’s student body.,1,On site,Same laboratory setting; not online or field.,1,"Same repeated investment trust game as baseline with random rematching and pre-play messages, but after any instance where a receiver returns less than promised in their message, the sender can pay 2 ECU to bring a case before a jury of the three other receivers of the defendant’s color. Juries observe full case details and anonymously vote; majority for plaintiff forces defendant to pay the outstanding promised amount plus cover the 2 ECU dispute cost.",Summarized from description of Jury - No Info treatment and jury procedure.,1
10.1093_jleo_ewu019.md,Jury - Info,Treatment described as combining Jury mechanism with Info: 'The Jury - Info treatment identifies the joint effect of information sharing and in-group punishment by combining the within-group information sharing of the Info treatments with the Jury mechanism.',1,Jury mechanism enabled (Jury=1); Information sharing enabled (Info=1),"In 2x2 design, this is the cell with both mechanisms present.",1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized assignment to treatments with manipulation of Info and Jury.,1,True,Same lab setting and procedure as other treatments.,1,False,No simulation data.,1,False,Empirical results dominate; no purely analytical-only condition.,1,16,Same session size and composition as other treatments.,1,10,Same 10-period repeated investment game.,1,1,Senders/receivers choose any integer amount within bounds.,1,0,10 ECU private endowment.,1,N/A,Not a linear public good.,1,1,Same cheap-talk messaging as in other treatments.,1,1,Combines Jury with Info: within-group information table now 'also includes details of any trials'. Thus subjects see others’ transaction details.,1,0,"Information table includes transaction and trial details, but not individual identities beyond color/type; identities per se remain anonymous.",0.7,0,No explicit reward institution.,1,0,Subjects not told the number of periods.,1,1,Same jury-based punishment as in Jury - No Info.,1,2,Sender pays 2 ECU to initiate a dispute.,1,1,"As in Jury - No Info: conviction requires defendant to pay outstanding promised amount plus 2 ECU dispute cost, implying 1:1 cost-to-transfer ratio on the dispute cost component.",0.7,0,No institutionalized rewards beyond normal payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Same per-period endowment as other treatments.,1,0.6798,Mean Amount Sent is 6.798 ECU (Table 1) out of 10.,1,6.798,Table 1 'Amount Sent' for Jury - Info.,1,N/R,Paper does not provide normalized total surplus relative to maximum for this condition.,0.2,N/R,Only average sender/receiver earnings reported; not aggregated to group-level payoffs.,0.2,United States,Same subject pool.,1,N/R,No age info reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition.,0,University students,Subjects from student body of a mid-sized U.S. university.,1,On site,Conducted in the same physical lab context.,1,"Repeated 10-period investment trust game with fixed roles and random rematching, pre-play receiver messages, and both a jury dispute mechanism (as in Jury - No Info) and within-group information sharing (as in No Jury - Info). The group information table shows, for all same-color, same-role participants, detailed transaction outcomes and any trials (including whether disputes were filed and their outcomes).",Summarized from description of Jury - Info treatment.,1
10.1093_jleo_ewu019.md,Jury - Info - Pay,Robustness check treatment: 'we also report a treatment in which receivers choose whether or not to participate in trials... We call this the Jury - Info - Pay treatment'.,1,Jury mechanism with opt-in voting cost for receivers; Information sharing enabled,Same as Jury - Info but jurors must pay 2 ECU per trial to participate.,1,True,Human-subject robustness treatment in the lab.,1,True,Additional controlled treatment in same experimental design.,1,True,Run in same lab with same procedures.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,"Empirical treatment, not purely analytical.",1,16,Authors state they ran 3 sessions of each treatment including this robustness check; design elsewhere always uses 16 subjects per session.,0.9,10,Described as a robustness check on the Jury - Info treatment; likely retains same 10-period structure.,0.9,1,Game structure (investment trust) unchanged relative to main treatments.,1,0,Same initial endowments and control over sending decision.,1,N/A,Not a public-good contribution game.,1,1,Game remains the same trust game with pre-play messages; only jury participation cost changes.,0.9,1,"Described as a robustness check on Jury - Info, which includes information sharing; no contrary statement.",0.9,0,Jury mechanics same as Jury - Info except for juror participation cost; identities still anonymous beyond group color/type.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism added.,1,0,No indication this robustness treatment revealed the number of periods.,0.8,1,Still a jury treatment where senders can bring cheaters to trial.,1,2,"Sender dispute cost unchanged at 2 ECU; additional 2 ECU per trial applies to receivers who opt into jury duty (cost of voting, not of initiating punishment).",0.8,1,Punishment outcome remains same as Jury - Info; only voting participation cost changed.,0.7,0,No rewards added in robustness treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"Same game and payoffs as Jury - Info, only jury participation cost differs.",0.9,N/R,Main text only reports that robustness results 'support our other findings'; no summary statistics table for this treatment.,0,N/R,Table 1 excludes Jury - Info - Pay; no separate means reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency data for this robustness condition.,0,N/R,No outcome statistics reported specifically for this treatment.,0,United States,Same subject pool and lab as main treatments.,1,N/R,No age statistics provided anywhere.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,University students,Same recruitment procedure for all sessions.,1,On site,Run in the same laboratory context as others.,1,"Same repeated 10-period investment trust game with pre-play receiver messages, information sharing within group, and a jury mechanism as in Jury - Info, but here receivers must decide in advance whether to opt into serving on the jury at a cost of 2 ECU per trial, increasing the cost of managing group reputation.","Based on description: 'receivers choose whether or not to participate in trials. Before learning transaction details, receivers must choose whether to opt in at a cost of 2 ECU per trial.'",1
10.1111_j.1439-0310.2010.01781.x.md,Resident victim species vs. visiting victim species (between-species comparison),Paper reports a between-species comparison where the key condition is whether victim species are residents (enforced repeated game) or visitors (can avoid blenny territory). This is the main contrasted 'treatment' structure.,0.96,Victim residency status: resident species permanently within a blenny’s territory vs. visiting species with large home ranges that can avoid the blenny’s territory; local abundance varies continuously per species.,Resident/visitor distinction and variation in local abundance are explicitly described as the focal predictors for aggression towards blennies.,0.98,True,Data are direct field observations of live reef fish and blennies interacting on reefs.,1,False,Authors conducted observational focal sampling of natural interactions without manipulating conditions; no controlled assignment or treatment.,0.99,False,"Work conducted on natural reef patches in the sea using scuba, not in a laboratory or controlled tank.",1,False,"No agent-based, numerical, or computer simulations are reported.",1,False,"Study uses statistical analysis of observed data, not formal game-theoretic derivations or closed-form models as a primary method.",0.98,2,"Each recorded interaction involves one blenny and one victim fish, i.e., a dyadic interaction.",0.95,1,"Each scored event is a single interaction (attack/attempt/unprovoked chase and response); repeated-game structure is ecological, not an explicit sequential game with discrete rounds.",0.7,1,"Aggression is coded as presence/absence and not as a divisible contribution amount; there is no continuous contribution choice akin to public good tokens; by mapping to the schema, contribution concept is not applicable but default best fit is that there is no restricted discrete 'all-or-nothing' contribution choice in a PG sense.",0.3,N/R,No public goods contribution or token endowment framework is used; only behavioural responses (aggression) are measured.,1,N/R,No formal public goods game with multipliers or marginal per capita returns is implemented.,1,0,Non-human fish interact via natural behaviour; no experimental communication channel is introduced or controlled like chat.,1,0,Fish are not experimentally shown numerical summaries of others’ payoffs or actions.,1,0,"There is no experimental manipulation of identity revelation; interactions are naturally anonymous/recognition-based, not via an interface.",0.9,0,No explicit rewards or identity cues are experimentally displayed.,1,0,"Fish are not informed about a number of rounds; repeated interactions arise ecologically, not via an instructed finite-horizon game.",1,1,Aggressive chases by victims are explicitly interpreted as punishment that may alter blenny behaviour.,0.98,N/R,No quantitative fitness or energetic cost per aggressive act is measured or specified.,1,N/R,"No quantified functional effect of an aggressive act (e.g., units of payoff reduction) is reported.",1,0,No prosocial rewards or positive incentives are present; only aggression (punishment-like behaviour) and non-aggressive responses are described.,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this observational system.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this observational system.,1,N/R,There is no numeric individual endowment or token budget; only natural state variables like body size and position on reef.,1,N/R,"No normalized cooperation or contribution rate is defined; primary outcome is probability/percentage of aggression, not mapped to a 0–1 contribution choice in a game.",1,N/R,No monetary or token contributions are recorded; outcomes are behavioural frequencies.,1,N/R,No group payoff vs theoretical maximum payoff is calculated; only correlations and differences in aggression.,1,N/R,Group-level payoffs are not quantified in numeric units.,1,N/R,Participants are non-human fish in the Red Sea; the schema’s 'country' field for human participants does not apply.,1,N/R,Ages of individual fish are not reported.,1,N/R,"Sex is sometimes noted for specific analyses (e.g., male vs female P. squamipinnis) but not as a general sample descriptor analogous to human gender reporting; no global gender breakdown is given.",0.7,N/A,"Subjects are fish, so human educational attainment is not applicable.",1,Field experiment,"Observations took place on natural coral reef patches in Ras Mohammed National Park using scuba; this is a natural-field observational study. Among available categories, 'Field experiment' best matches non-lab, in situ research with animals, although there is no experimental manipulation.",0.9,"Natural asymmetric parasite–victim interactions: sabre-tooth blennies (always-defect cheaters) vs. non-predatory reef fish victims. Residents have enforced repeated interactions as they share a small reef patch territory with the blenny, whereas visitors can largely avoid the blenny’s territory. Aggression (chasing) by victims is interpreted as punishment that may induce future changes in blenny target choice, potentially generating by-product public goods when conspecifics benefit from a chaser’s action. Local abundance (group size) varies widely (singletons to shoals of >50), and aggression frequencies are analyzed as a function of residency status, local abundance, body size, and territoriality.","Summarizes the interaction structure and how it relates to repeated game and public goods/punishment concepts, as described in Introduction, Methods, and Discussion.",0.96
10.1007_s00199-008-0421-1.md,Theoretical community prisoners' dilemma model – baseline environment,"The paper presents a single formal model of a community-style repeated prisoners' dilemma with heterogeneous types, partner choice, and specified equilibrium refinements; there are no distinct empirical treatments or multiple separate models.",0.86,"Type distribution (α: opportunistic/strategic, β: bad/always defect, γ: good/always cooperate with α+β+γ=1); continuation probability of relationship ρ; discount factor δ; PD payoffs (a for C,C; b for D against C; −l for C against D; 0 for D,D). Analysis varies β and γ to characterize good, bad, and mixed-strategy equilibria.","These parameters are explicitly varied in propositions and comparative statics (e.g., existence of good equilibrium as a function of β, bad equilibrium as a function of γ, and mixed-strategy equilibria as a function of (α,β,γ), with a,b,l,δ,ρ treated as primitives).",0.96,False,The work is entirely theoretical with no human subjects or data collection; it analyzes equilibria in a formal game-theoretic model.,1,False,No controlled lab/online/field experiment is conducted; only theoretical analysis of a stylized game.,1,False,There is no laboratory setup; the study is a purely analytical model.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; results are derived analytically.,1,True,"The paper develops and solves an infinite-horizon game-theoretic model analytically, deriving value functions, steady states, and equilibrium conditions.",1,2,"The basic stage game is a prisoners' dilemma played within a partnership, i.e., a pair of players; the community is a continuum of such agents, but interaction is always bilateral.",0.98,0,"Time is discrete and horizon is infinite; the game is an indefinitely repeated interaction, not a fixed finite number of rounds. Using 0 here to indicate N/A numeric for infinite horizon.",0.9,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D; there is no continuous contribution decision or partial contribution; this maps to an all-or-nothing cooperation choice, encoded as 1 because any contribution level is not defined, so the usual distinction does not meaningfully apply.",0.6,N/A,There is no endowment allocation between private and public accounts; only action choices (C or D) in a prisoners' dilemma are modeled.,1,N/R,"The game is a prisoners' dilemma with payoffs a,b,l rather than a linear public good with a defined marginal per capita return; no MPCR is specified or derivable.",1,N/A,There are no human participants or procedural details like communication; this is a theoretical model.,1,N/A,"Information structure is modeled as perfect monitoring within pairs and no information across matches, but there is no experimental interface or summary screen; no humans.",1,N/A,Punishment is endogenous via matching with likely defectors rather than an explicit punishment stage with IDs; no human interface.,1,N/A,"Reward is via entry/maintenance of high-payoff long-term relationships, not an explicit reward operator tied to identities in an experiment.",1,N/A,Infinite-horizon theoretical game; no display of remaining periods as in an experiment.,1,0,"There is no explicit costly punishment action; punishment is indirect through dissolution of relationships and higher likelihood of meeting defectors, not a dedicated punishment stage or operator.",0.95,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism with a defined per-unit cost exists.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology is defined; sanctions arise via equilibrium matching patterns.,1,0,There is no explicit costly reward action; higher payoffs from long-term cooperative relationships are endogenous outcomes of play rather than a separate reward technology.,0.95,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with a per-unit cost is present.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology; rewards are higher continuation values from cooperation.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are specified directly as a,b,−l,0 per stage, not as returns on an endowment; no endowment size is defined.",1,N/R,"Theoretical analysis characterizes strategies (C or D) and equilibrium existence, not average contribution rates in a public good sense.",1,N/R,Actions are binary (C or D) without a numeric contribution level; no aggregate numeric contributions are reported.,1,N/R,"Theoretical welfare comparisons are given symbolically as W(β), W(γ), but no normalized efficiency metric (payoff divided by maximum possible) is explicitly computed.",0.8,N/R,Per-period community welfare functions are provided in symbolic form but not as concrete numeric values; no single numeric group payoff is reported.,0.9,N/A,No human participants; model agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,No human,The study consists solely of an analytically solved game-theoretic model without empirical implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon community prisoners' dilemma with continuum of agents and random matching into bilateral partnerships each period; players can either stay with current partner or separate and be rematched; three types of agents: good (always C), bad (always D), and opportunistic (best-responding) with type distribution (α,β,γ); within-partnership monitoring is perfect but no information flows across relationships; exogenous breakup probability 1−ρ; discount factor δ; payoff parameters satisfy 0<a<b, l>0, and b−l<2a; analysis focuses on steady-state equilibria satisfying fresh start, zero tolerance, and quick familiarity, and characterizes good (always cooperate), bad (always defect in S), and mixed-strategy equilibria as functions of the type distribution and parameters.","Summarizes the formal game structure, timing, and key equilibrium refinements directly stated in Sections 2–5.",0.98
10.1103_PhysRevE.76.026114.md,Mean-field analytical model with constant detection probability α,Paper presents a theoretical mean-field evolutionary game model analyzed under the assumption of a constant fraud detection probability α; this is the primary analytical condition in Section III.A.,1,"Detection probability α (0 ≤ α ≤ 1), penalty ρ, cost of cooperation c, benefit b, number of rounds n; strategy frequencies of discriminators (x), defectors (y), scammers (z). This condition assumes α is constant and independent of z.","Model outcomes are analyzed as functions of α, ρ, c, b, n, and initial composition (x,y,z) with α treated as a fixed parameter.",1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical evolutionary game model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; analytical study of differential equations.,1,False,No lab setting; theoretical analysis only.,1,False,"Although figures may be plotted numerically, the core analysis is closed-form/analytical mean-field dynamics, not an explicit agent-based or numerical simulation described as such.",0.6,True,"Derives and analyzes replicator equations and reputation dynamics analytically (closed-form expressions for payoffs, fixed points, thresholds α1, α2, etc.).",1,3,"Model tracks three strategy types (discriminators, defectors, scammers) in an infinite well-mixed population, effectively three player types.",0.7,7,General analysis uses parameter n (number of interaction rounds per generation). A specific value n=7 appears in Fig. 4 parameter set and is a concrete configuration; here we record that concrete numerical example.,0.6,1,"In each donor-recipient encounter the donor 'has the possibility to pay the cost c to support the recipient'; no discrete choice set over partial amounts is specified, implying effectively a binary cooperate/defect decision rather than a continuous contribution rate; for coding, players can either contribute c or 0, so it is not an all-or-nothing public goods contribution level across multiple tokens in the usual sense; however, the schema defines 1 if players can contribute any amount and 0 if only all-or-nothing. Here contribution is a fixed cost c, i.e., all-or-nothing; but there is no variable amount. This does not map cleanly; set to 1 to indicate not a constrained public-goods discrete-token game.",0.3,N/A,No human players and no explicit endowment allocation between private and public accounts is modeled; only cost c and benefit b parameters.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined; model is framed as indirect reciprocity with donor cost c and recipient benefit b, not a linear public goods MPCR.",1,N/A,No human interaction or communication; analytical population model.,1,0,"No information feedback structure of this type is modeled; only global reputation states and payoffs, no per-round summary display to players.",0.9,N/A,Punishment is modeled as an automatic penalty ρ applied upon detection with probability α; there are no individual punishers or identities.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism separate from cooperation is modeled.,1,0,Players are not modeled as cognizant agents with information about n; n is an exogenous parameter in the analytical model.,1,1,"Scammers, when detected with probability α, 'have to pay a penalty proportional to the number of potential donors, ρ(x+z)'; this is a punishment mechanism.",1,N/A,Punishment is an exogenous penalty term ρ(x+z)/2 in payoffs; there is no notion of a punisher paying a cost per unit of punishment in the sense of peer punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit mapping from punisher expenditure to target loss is modeled; only a direct penalty parameter ρ.,1,0,No separate rewarding institution; only benefits from cooperation b and penalty ρ.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,Model specifies only cost c and benefit b; no explicit per-round endowment or wealth constraint is given.,1,N/R,No empirical outcome measures like average contribution rate are reported; analysis concerns relative payoffs and stability regions.,1,N/R,No numerical contributions beyond parameter c are reported as outcome variables.,1,N/R,Efficiency (normalized total payoff relative to maximum) is not explicitly calculated or reported.,1,N/R,"Only average payoffs by strategy (𝓟_C, 𝓟_D, 𝓟_S) are analyzed, not aggregate group payoff as a central DV.",1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is an analytical evolutionary game model without human or animal subjects.,1,"Indirect reciprocity/image scoring model with three strategies: discriminators help those with good reputation at cost c giving benefit b; defectors never help; scammers fake cooperation, maintaining good reputation while exploiting public good until detected with probability α and punished with penalty ρ(x+z). Dynamics modeled on two timescales: within-generation reputation/payoff updates over n rounds and between-generation replicator dynamics for strategy frequencies.",Summary of Sections II and III.A where the game structure and dynamics are defined.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.76.026114.md,Mean-field analytical model with adaptive detection probability α(z) = βz,"Section III.B introduces a more realistic condition where detection probability increases with scammer abundance, α = βz, and analyzes resulting fixed points and dynamics.",1,"Detection function α(z) = β z with β > 0; penalty ρ; cost of cooperation c; benefit b; number of rounds n; strategy frequencies of discriminators (x), defectors (y), scammers (z).","Key variation from the constant-α case is making α an increasing function of scammer fraction z, parameterized by β; other parameters c, b, ρ, n are as before.",1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,False,"Condition is a parameterization of analytical equations, not an experiment.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,"Dynamics and fixed points are discussed analytically; while Fig. 4 is a numerical illustration, the method is not presented as a computational simulation method per se.",0.6,True,"Analyzes replicator dynamics with α(z)=βz and general strictly increasing f(z), deriving coexistence conditions and fixed point scammer fractions analytically.",1,3,"Same three strategy types (discriminators, defectors, scammers) in an infinite population.",0.9,7,"For Fig. 4, parameters include n=7, b=1.0, c=0.5, ρ=1.0, β=1; we record this concrete configuration for this adaptive-detection illustration.",0.9,1,Same donate-or-not structure: donor either pays cost c or not; no graded contribution specified. Schema mapping is imperfect; set to 1 to indicate not a classic discrete-token all-or-nothing PGG.,0.3,N/A,No explicit endowments for human players.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return defined; game is framed via c and b.,1,N/A,No human players or communication modeled.,1,0,No explicit display of others’ outcomes; only population fractions enter payoffs.,0.9,N/A,"Punishment is automatic upon detection, not administered by identified individuals.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents are not modeled as seeing n.,1,1,"As before, scammers detected with probability α(z) pay penalty proportional to (x+z).",1,N/A,No separate punisher cost specified; ρ is direct penalty to scammers.,1,N/A,No mapping from punisher action to target loss.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment per player.,1,N/R,No empirical contribution rate reported; interest is in equilibrium fractions and conditions for coexistence/fixed points.,1,N/R,No outcome variable as raw contribution amount; cooperation is a binary decision with fixed cost c.,1,N/R,Total efficiency not numerically reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff not given as primary DV; analysis focuses on relative payoffs and fixed points.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical population-dynamics model.,1,"Same indirect reciprocity/image scoring game as in the constant-α model, but fraud detection probability is adaptive: α(z) = β z, or more generally any strictly increasing function f(z). This destabilizes pure-strategy corners and yields stable coexistence fixed points where scammers coexist with discriminators or defectors. For coexistence with discriminators only, scammer fraction is α1/β where α1 = c/(b+ρ); for coexistence with defectors only, scammer fraction is α2/β where α2 = (b/(b+ρ)) (1 - 2/n + 2^{1-n}/n).",Summary of Section III.B and Fig. 4 description.,1
10.1017_s1355770x19000457.md,Complete Network (CN),One of three between-subject monitoring/punishment network treatments: Complete Network (perfect monitoring and punishment).,1,"Network type = Complete Network (CN); density 100%, undirected, in-degree=3, out-degree=3.",Independent variable is the monitoring/punishment network structure; this row is for the CN condition.,1,True,Lab experiment with 136 human student subjects across treatments; CN is one of them.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized stranger matching and fixed treatment per session.,1,True,Conducted at LSE Behavioural Research Lab (on-site laboratory).,1,False,"All decisions made by human subjects via z-Tree, not simulated agents.",1,False,"Paper reports an experiment and regressions, not purely analytical derivations as the main result.",1,4,“four-person network groups” and Table 2 lists Group size (n)=4 for all treatments.,1,15,"“An experimental session consisted of 15 decision-rounds, and this was common knowledge.”",1,1,"Strategy space is “0,1,…,24,25 tokens” allocated between common and private account; continuous/discrete choice over full range, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Subjects receive 25 tokens each round and decide allocation; all endowment initially under their control (private) until allocated to common account.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear CPR payoff F(X)=aX−bX² with individual share x_i/X; no single marginal per capita return value reported.,1,0,No mention of any communication or chat; interactions anonymous via decision screens only.,1,1,"In stage two, each subject sees for monitored neighbors: “how many tokens subjects in N_i placed into their private and group accounts in stage one” and later total cost of punishment given/received; this is feedback on others’ choices and sanctioning.",0.9,0,"Identity anonymized: “interacted anonymously…”, stranger matching, and no indication that IDs of punishers or specific sources of punishment are revealed.",0.9,0,No rewards in design; only punishment stage.,1,1,"“An experimental session consisted of 15 decision-rounds, and this was common knowledge.” Implies subjects know total number of rounds.",0.9,1,Stage two is explicitly a punishment stage where deduction points can be assigned.,1,1,“at a personal unit cost of c_a=1 per deduction point assigned.”,1,3,“Each punishment point received reduces j’s payoff by c_r=3.” Fee-to-fine ratio 1:3.,1,0,No mechanism to increase others’ payoffs; only deduction points described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,25,“subjects received an endowment of 25 tokens” at start of each round.,1,0.615,"For CN, “average amount of tokens allocated to the common account… was 61.5 per cent of the endowment or 15.37 tokens.” 61.5% = 0.615.",1,15.37,Same sentence reports 15.37 tokens for CN pooled across rounds.,1,N/R,Paper reports net payoffs but does not normalize by theoretical maximum group payoff to provide an efficiency ratio.,0.7,689.44,Average individual net payoff in CN is 172.36 tokens (Table 3). Group has 4 members: 172.36*4≈689.44.,0.95,United Kingdom,Experiment conducted at LSE in London; subjects recruited from LSE student pool. Country of lab and institution is UK.,0.9,N/R,Described as LSE students; no age statistics reported.,1,Approximately 59% female; remainder male/other (all treatments pooled).,"Footnote states: “Of the total sample, 59.35 per cent was female…” for the full 136-subject sample across treatments; not broken down by treatment.",0.7,"University students (16.15% economics majors, remainder other disciplines).",Participants are registered students at LSE; footnote: 16.15 per cent studied economics.,0.9,On site,Run in LSE Behavioural Research Lab with subjects at computer terminals.,1,"Nonlinear common-pool resource game with 4 players, 15 rounds, strangers matching. Stage 1: each gets 25 tokens and chooses x_i∈[0,25] to common account; private account yields w=4 per unallocated token; CPR return F(X)=aX−bX² with a=0.25, b=0.25? (Table 2 lists a=0.25, b=5 but CPR payoff parameters used yield Nash appropriation 16 tokens and Pareto 10 tokens giving payoffs 189 and 225). Individual CPR payoff share proportional to x_i/X. Stage 2: each player can assign deduction points to all 3 others (complete, undirected network). Cost 1 per point to punisher; target loses 3 per point; maximum punishment bounded by target’s stage 1 earnings. One random round paid; beliefs about others’ stage-1 allocations incentivized (4 tokens per correct guess).","Summarizes key game structure, matching, payoff function, and punishment mechanism described in Sections 2.1 and 2.2 and Table 2.",0.9
10.1017_s1355770x19000457.md,Undirected Circle Network (UCN),One of three between-subject monitoring/punishment network treatments: imperfect Undirected Circle Network.,1,"Network type = Undirected Circle Network (UCN); density 66%, undirected, in-degree=2, out-degree=2.",Independent variable is network structure; this row is for the UCN condition with lower density and symmetric ties.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment; UCN is a treatment.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random rematching and fixed UCN network per session.,1,True,Conducted at LSE Behavioural Research Lab.,1,False,Human subjects only.,1,False,"Main results are experimental, not purely analytical.",1,4,Four-person network groups in all treatments.,1,15,15 decision rounds for each session in all treatments.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer number of tokens 0–25 to common account.,1,0,"Endowment given to each subject who then allocates to common vs private account, same as CN.",0.9,N/R,Nonlinear CPR technology; no single MPCR reported.,1,0,No communication implemented.,1,1,"Subjects can see monitored neighbors’ stage-1 allocations and their own punishment flows summary, analogous to CN.",0.9,0,Anonymity and stranger matching; no identification of punishers reported.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Total of 15 rounds is common knowledge in all treatments.,0.9,1,Stage-two punishment decision stage exists in UCN as well.,1,1,Same c_a=1 per deduction point across all networks.,1,3,Same c_r=3 per point received; 1:3 fee-to-fine ratio.,1,0,No rewarding.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,25,Same 25-token endowment each round in all networks (Table 2).,1,0.5804,Average appropriation in UCN is 14.51 tokens (Table 3). Divide by 25-token endowment: 14.51/25≈0.5804.,1,14.51,"Table 3, row “UCN-All” reports 14.51 tokens.",1,N/R,Net payoffs reported (188.77) but no normalized efficiency measure vs max possible group payoff.,0.7,755.08,Average individual net payoff in UCN is 188.77 tokens (Table 3). Group of 4: 188.77*4≈755.08.,0.95,United Kingdom,Same subject pool and lab as CN: LSE in London.,0.9,N/R,Age not reported; all are students.,1,Approximately 59% female; remainder male/other (all treatments pooled).,"Only aggregate gender share given for full 136-subject sample, not separately by treatment.",0.7,"University students (16.15% economics majors, remainder other disciplines).",Same overall sample composition applies to all treatments.,0.9,On site,Same laboratory setting.,1,"Same nonlinear 4-player CPR game and punishment technology as CN. Network differs: undirected circle on 4 nodes (A,B,C,D) where each player has degree 2. Example: A monitors and can punish B and D; A is monitored by B and D, but not C. Network density 66% vs 100% in CN. Monitoring and punishment opportunities restricted to immediate neighbors; all other rules (endowment 25, F(X)=aX−bX², w=4, cost 1 per point, fine 3 per point, stranger matching, 15 rounds, belief elicitation with incentives) unchanged.",Derived from Figure 1 and description of UCN in Section 2.2 and Table 2; all parameters otherwise held fixed across networks.,0.95
10.1017_s1355770x19000457.md,Directed Circle Network (DCN),One of three between-subject monitoring/punishment network treatments: imperfect Directed Circle Network.,1,"Network type = Directed Circle Network (DCN); density 50% of undirected equivalent; directed ties with in-degree=1, out-degree=1.","Independent variable is monitoring/punishment network; DCN introduces directed, asymmetric ties.",1,True,Human lab experiment; DCN is one treatment.,1,True,Controlled design with random stranger matching and exogenous assignment to DCN network.,1,True,Conducted in LSE Behavioural Research Lab.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Primary results are experimental.,1,4,Four-person network groups across all treatments.,1,15,15 decision rounds common to all treatments.,1,1,"Subjects choose 0–25 tokens to common account, not binary.",1,0,Same endowment allocation decision as CN/UCN.,0.9,N/R,Same nonlinear CPR; MPCR not explicitly given.,1,0,No communication implemented.,1,1,Subjects can view monitored neighbor’s stage-1 allocation plus their own punishment costs/receipts summary.,0.9,0,Anonymity; no identification of individual punishers described.,0.9,0,No rewards in the design.,1,1,Number of rounds (15) is common knowledge.,0.9,1,Stage-two punishment exists; each subject can punish their single out-neighbor.,1,1,Same c_a=1 per deduction point.,1,3,Same c_r=3 per received point; fee-to-fine 1:3.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,25,Same 25-token endowment each round.,1,0.592,Average appropriation in DCN is 14.8 tokens (Table 3). 14.8/25≈0.592.,1,14.8,"Table 3, row “DCN-All” lists 14.8 tokens.",1,N/R,Average net payoff reported but not normalized to maximum possible cooperative payoff.,0.7,757.04,Average individual net payoff in DCN is 189.26 (Table 3). Group-of-4 payoff ≈189.26*4=757.04.,0.95,United Kingdom,Same LSE student subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Age not reported; only that participants are students.,1,Approximately 59% female; remainder male/other (all treatments pooled).,Only aggregate gender share given for the full sample; cannot disaggregate by treatment.,0.7,"University students (16.15% economics majors, remainder other disciplines).",Same composition as total sample applies to this treatment.,0.9,On site,Conducted in the same physical lab.,1,"Same underlying nonlinear 4-player CPR and punishment as CN/UCN. Network differs: directed circle on 4 nodes (A,B,C,D) with asymmetric monitoring/punishment. Example: A can monitor/punish B; B C; C D; D A. If tie runs from i to j, then i can monitor j but not vice versa. Each subject has exactly one potential target and one potential punisher (in-degree=1, out-degree=1). All other design details (endowment 25, payoff function F(X)=aX−bX², w=4, c_a=1, c_r=3, maximum punishment bounded by target’s stage-1 earnings, 15 stranger-matched rounds, incentivized belief elicitation, random-round payment) are identical across treatments.",Based on Figure 1(c) description of DCN and general design section stating all institutional features fixed except network structure.,0.95
10.1007_s00199-018-1146-4.md,No communication – Δ = 1 (high flexibility),Condition identified as treatment with Δ=1 without communication in Section 2.2 and Table 2.,1,Action flexibility Δ=1 (high flexibility); no pre-play communication allowed.,"Across treatments Δ is varied (1,2,3). Separately, communication is either allowed or not; this condition is the no-communication, Δ=1 case.",1,True,Human subjects in CentERlab at Tilburg University.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Run in CentERlab at Tilburg University (a physical laboratory).,1,False,All results based on human subjects; no purely computational simulations reported.,1,False,Paper includes theory discussion but this condition refers to the lab experiment with humans.,1,2,Two players interact in a Cournot duopoly in all treatments (Section 2.1).,1,10,Indefinitely repeated with random termination; expected number of periods is 10 for Δ=1 (Δ/(1−δ) with δ=e^{−0.1}≈0.90). Use expected length as proxy.,0.6,0,"Action set is discrete {3,4}; subjects cannot choose any amount along a continuum or only ‘all’ vs ‘nothing’. This is a restricted discrete-choice setup, not an all-or-nothing public good.",0.7,N/A,"This is a Cournot quantity-setting duopoly, not a contribution game with an endowment.",1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined or reported.,1,0,Described as treatment without communication; no chat window provided.,1,1,"At end of each block of Δ periods, subjects received realized prices and their own profits; they never see partner’s quantity but do see payoff-relevant price history. Interpreted as summary feedback about outcomes.",0.7,0,No punishment mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,Indefinite horizon with random continuation; paper does not indicate that total or remaining periods are shown.,0.7,0,No explicit costly punishment stage or choice; only market payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism in the stage game.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,80,Subjects were given a starting endowment of 80 points to cover potential losses.,1,0.277,"Table 2, cooperation rate (all periods) for Δ=1 is 0.277. Cooperation defined as choosing collusive quantity (q=3).",1,N/R,They report frequencies of cooperation (q=3) but not average quantity levels expressed as amounts per period.,0.3,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible joint payoffs is reported.,0.1,N/R,Paper provides expected earnings benchmarks but not realized average total profits by treatment.,0.2,Netherlands,Experiment conducted at Tilburg University (Netherlands); subjects recruited via local student email list.,0.9,N/R,Only described as students; no age statistics reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,University students,Subjects recruited from an email list of students interested in experiments at Tilburg University.,0.9,On site,"Run in CentERlab, a physical laboratory at Tilburg University; subjects came to the lab.",1,"Indefinitely repeated Cournot duopoly with homogeneous product. Action set q∈{3,4}. Demand P_t = 12 − (q1t + q2t) + ε_t with ε_t ~ N(0,1.3^2). Marginal cost 0, fixed cost 16 per period. Imperfect public monitoring via noisy price; quantities unobserved. Random termination after each block of Δ periods with continuation probability δ=e^{−0.1Δ}. Here Δ=1 so actions can be changed every period; δ≈0.90; expected length 10 periods. Seven supergames (matches) per subject with random rematching and no repetition of partners.","Compiled from Sections 2.1 and 2.2, plus discounting description.",1
10.1007_s00199-018-1146-4.md,No communication – Δ = 2 (intermediate flexibility),Condition identified as treatment with Δ=2 without communication in Section 2.2 and Table 2.,1,Action flexibility Δ=2 (intermediate flexibility); no pre-play communication allowed.,"Δ varied across treatments; this row is the no-communication, Δ=2 condition.",1,True,Same subject pool and lab setup as other treatments.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with treatment manipulation of Δ.,1,True,Conducted in CentERlab at Tilburg University.,1,False,Not a computer-only simulation.,1,False,"This row is empirical, though paper also contains theoretical appendix.",1,2,Duopoly throughout.,1,11.1,"Expected number of periods is Δ/(1−δ); for Δ=2, δ=e^{−0.2}≈0.82, so 2/(1−0.82)=11.1 as stated in design.",0.9,0,"Same Cournot discrete choice {3,4}.",0.7,N/A,No endowment-contribution structure.,1,N/R,Not applicable to Cournot game and not reported.,1,0,This is from the set of treatments without communication; chat only added later in Section 3.1.,1,1,"Same feedback structure: after each block of Δ=2 periods, subjects see realized prices and own profits.",0.7,0,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Indefinite horizon; no indication that remaining periods are revealed.,0.7,0,No punishment stage-game choice.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No rewarding option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,80,Same starting endowment applies to all treatments.,1,0.241,"Table 2, cooperation rate (all periods) for Δ=2 is 0.241.",1,N/R,No average quantities or number of cooperative periods translated into amounts reported.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency ratios not reported.,0.1,N/R,No treatment-level realized payoff totals reported.,0.2,Netherlands,Same lab and recruitment pool.,0.9,N/R,No age data given.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition given.,0.1,University students,Same as other treatments.,0.9,On site,Conducted in physical lab at Tilburg University.,1,"Same indefinitely repeated Cournot duopoly with noisy public price signal. Here Δ=2: chosen quantity applies for 2 periods; after each 2-period block, players observe prices and own profits from those 2 periods, then face a continuation probability δ=e^{−0.2}≈0.82. Expected length is 11.1 periods per match. Seven matches per subject, random rematching, no repeated pairings.",Specialization of general design parameters for Δ=2.,1
