10.3389_fnbeh.2016.00180.md,"DEC / λ = 1 (perfect signal, decentralized punishment)",Paper defines six between-subject treatment combinations by institution (DEC vs CEN) and signal accuracy λ; this row is the DEC/1 condition.,1,Punishment institution: decentralized (peer-to-peer). Signal accuracy λ = 1 (perfect information).,Independent variables are institution and signal accuracy as described in the 2×3 factorial design; values for this condition are DEC and λ=1.,1,True,Human participants in lab public-good game.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with imposed treatments.,1,True,Conducted in University of Bonn EconLab using z-Tree.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported; all results from human-subject sessions.,1,False,Paper contains theoretical discussion but no formal analytical model as a separate study.,0.9,5,Groups of five: four C-participants and one Authority in all treatments.,1,30,Participants interact over 30 periods in a partner design.,1,1,C-participants choose any even number 0–20; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each C-participant receives endowment of 20 tokens and then chooses contribution; all start in private account.,1,0.4,They explicitly state a marginal per capita return of 0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous lab PGG with only numerical feedback.,0.9,1,"End of each round, participants learn income from public good, punishment received, and final payoff, which aggregates others' actions.",0.9,0,"In DEC, recipients cannot identify individual punishers; implies no identity revelation.",1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Game is finitely repeated with 30 known periods; standard design and analysis assume subjects know horizon.,0.8,1,Second-stage punishment is central to all treatments.,1,1,In DEC each punishment point sent costs 1 unit to the punisher.,1,3,Each punishment point received reduces target’s income by 3 units in DEC.,1,0,Only punishment points; no rewarding described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each C-participant receives an endowment of eg = 20 tokens each round.,1,N/R,Paper reports contributions graphically and via tests but not as a single normalized average 0–1 for each treatment.,0,N/R,Average contribution levels per treatment are not given numerically in the text or tables.,0,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed and plotted, but no numeric average per treatment is reported.",0,N/R,Total earnings or group payoffs by treatment are not numerically reported.,0,Germany,Experiments run in University of Bonn lab with local student subject pool.,0.9,N/R,"Only described as student participants, mostly undergraduates; no age statistics.",0,N/R,They state they did not control for sex; no composition shares reported.,0,Mostly undergraduate students from various fields,Explicitly described as student participants (mostly undergraduate from various fields of study).,1,On site,Conducted in physical experimental laboratory (EconLab) with z-Tree.,1,"Linear public good game with four contributing players and one non-contributing authority; partners design; punishment endowment 10 for C-participants and 40 for authority; DEC: only C-participants can punish each other, reducing their own payoff and the authority’s.",Summarizes structural features not captured by other fields.,1
10.3389_fnbeh.2016.00180.md,"CEN / λ = 1 (perfect signal, centralized punishment)",This row corresponds to the centralized punishment and perfect signal condition CEN/1.,1,Punishment institution: centralized (single authority punisher). Signal accuracy λ = 1 (perfect information).,Independent variables are institution and signal accuracy; here CEN and λ=1.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab treatment.,1,True,"Conducted at EconLab, University of Bonn.",1,False,No simulation-only study.,1,False,No separate analytical experiment.,0.9,5,Same 5-player groups (4 C-participants + 1 Authority) across all treatments.,1,30,Repeated interaction over 30 periods in partners design.,1,1,Contribution choice set 0–20 in steps of 2.,1,0,Endowment 20 tokens given then contribution chosen.,1,0.4,Marginal per capita return explicitly 0.4.,1,0,No communication reported.,0.9,1,"End-of-round feedback on income from public good, punishment, and final payoff.",0.9,0,No indication that authority’s identity is linked to specific punishment; subjects only know the authority exists. DEC explicitly states punishers not identifiable; no contrary info for CEN.,0.7,0,No rewards.,1,1,Finitely repeated 30-round design is standardly known; no evidence of hidden horizon.,0.8,1,Authority can distribute punishment points.,1,1,Each punishment point distributed by A reduces A’s payoff by one.,1,3,Each point reduces punished subject’s payoff by 3 units in CEN as well.,1,0,No rewarding described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,C-participants’ contribution endowment 20 tokens per period; authority does not contribute.,1,N/R,No numeric normalized contribution fraction per treatment reported.,0,N/R,Average contribution numbers by treatment not provided in text/tables.,0,N/R,Efficiency discussed and compared but no explicit average value per treatment.,0,N/R,Group earnings per treatment not numerically reported.,0,Germany,Sessions run in University of Bonn lab with local student pool.,0.9,N/R,"Only that participants are students, mostly undergraduates.",0,N/R,They state experiments did not control for sex; no composition given.,0,Mostly undergraduate students from various fields,Same subject pool description applies across all treatments.,1,On site,Conducted in EconLab using z-Tree.,1,"Centralized punishment: only the authority can assign punishment points (max 30 per target) financed by all others via externality (each point also reduces other participants’ payoffs by 1/3); partners design, same linear public good structure as DEC.",Captures institution-specific payoff structure and limits.,1
