10.1007_s00265-014-1741-8.md,Baseline,Treatment label in Table 1; no group competition and no ostracism.,1,Group competition: No; Ostracism: No,From Table 1 columns 'Group competition' and 'Possibility to ostracize'.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 240 human subjects.,1,True,Controlled randomized laboratory design with treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the University of Hamburg laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,"All results from human subjects, no agent-based or numerical simulation described.",1,False,"Paper reports empirical experiment; formal model only referenced in ESM, not the focus here.",0.9,12,Each society has 12 subjects; treatments are defined at the 12-person society level.,1,20,“The total earnings equaled the sum of net pay-offs over all 20 periods”.,1,1,Standard public goods game with allocation of 20 MUs between private and project accounts; no restriction to all-or-nothing mentioned.,0.9,0,Subjects are ‘endowed with 20 MUs that can be allocated’; default is all in private account before decision.,0.9,N/R,Table 2 gives MPCR for each possible group size; MPCR is not a single fixed value for the treatment.,1,0,Subjects were in separate cubicles and communication was prevented; no chat or communication option described.,1,1,After each period subjects see average contributions in all existing entities and individual contributions in own group.,1,0,No punishment mechanism; only ostracism in other treatments. Identities are anonymous; only ID letters shown. So punishment ID not applicable but set to 0 as no punishment.,0.9,0,No explicit reward mechanism described.,1,0,Paper does not state that remaining or total number of periods is displayed on screen.,0.5,0,"No costly punishment stage; only optional exit and, in other treatments, ostracism.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No positive reward or bonus mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,“Each subject is endowed with 20 monetary units (MUs)”.,1,0.725,"Mean contribution 14.50 MUs reported; endowment 20 MUs, so 14.5/20 = 0.725.",1,14.5,“Mean contribution to the group account in treatment Baseline is 14.50 MUs”.,1,N/R,No explicit normalized efficiency measure reported.,1,N/R,Figure 1c qualitatively describes net average pay-offs but no numeric values.,0.3,Germany,"Experiment conducted at the University of Hamburg; participants are likely mostly in Germany, but exact nationality not given.",0.7,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age statistics reported.,1,"125 female, 115 male across all treatments",“The vast majority of 125 female and 115 male participants…”; distribution by treatment not specified.,0.8,Mostly undergraduate university students,Described as “undergraduate students studying a wide range of disciplines”.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at University of Hamburg with participants in cubicles and paid in cash.,1,"Dynamic public goods game with endogenous group formation and free migration; initial 4 groups of 3 within 12-person society; MPCR varies with current group size; subjects can exit to become free-agents, apply to join other groups, or form new groups; mergers require mutual approval and are resolved by an algorithm prioritizing larger entities; in this treatment there is no intergroup competition and no ostracism voting.","Summarizes design elements specific to the Baseline condition (no competition, no ostracism).",1
10.1007_s00265-014-1741-8.md,Competition,Treatment label in Table 1; features group competition without ostracism.,1,Group competition: Yes; Ostracism: No,From Table 1 columns for Competition treatment.,1,True,Same laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Randomized lab treatment with controlled manipulation of group competition.,1,True,Implemented in the Hamburg lab with z-Tree.,1,False,No non-human simulation.,1,False,Behavioral lab study; analytical work in ESM not treated as separate condition.,0.9,12,Each ‘society’ is 12 subjects in all treatments.,1,20,Same 20 periods for all treatments.,1,1,Same public goods allocation decision of 0–20 MUs; no all-or-nothing restriction.,0.9,0,Endowment is received privately; contributions are voluntary transfers to project account.,0.9,N/R,"MPCR depends on group size per Table 2, not fixed.",1,0,No communication allowed; separated cubicles.,1,1,"Average contributions in all entities shown each period, plus individual contributions in own group.",1,0,No punishment stage separate from contribution and competition.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No mention that remaining rounds are indicated on screen.,0.5,0,Between-group comparison reallocates payoffs but is not an individual punishment choice.,1,N/A,No formal punishment stage.,1,N/A,No formal punishment stage.,1,0,No individual reward option beyond project account returns and competition transfers.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,20,Same endowment of 20 MUs in all treatments.,1,0.681,Mean contribution is 13.62 MUs; divide by endowment 20 → 13.62/20 ≈ 0.681.,1,13.62,“Mean contribution to the group account in treatment Competition 13.62 MUs”.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,"Net average pay-offs only graphed, not numerically reported.",0.3,Germany,All treatments share the same subject pool at University of Hamburg.,0.7,N/R,"Age not reported, only student status.",1,"125 female, 115 male across all treatments",Gender composition aggregated for whole sample; per-treatment not given.,0.8,Mostly undergraduate university students,Same pool for all treatments.,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab with cubicles and cash payments.,1,"Same dynamic public goods game and free migration as Baseline, plus between-group comparison option: in each period, if a strict majority in any entity votes for comparison, two randomly matched entities are compared. The entity with higher total MUs allocated to the project account wins half the difference between the project accounts; the losing entity loses that amount. The transfer is split equally among members. No external monetary inflows/outflows; equilibrium monetary payoffs held constant across treatments.",Describes the added group competition feature specific to this treatment.,1
10.1007_s00265-014-1741-8.md,Ostracism,Treatment label in Table 1; has ostracism without group competition.,1,Group competition: No; Ostracism: Yes,"From Table 1, ‘Ostracism’ treatment has no group competition and possibility to ostracize.",1,True,Same lab experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled treatment varying ostracism option.,1,True,Conducted at University of Hamburg laboratory.,1,False,No simulation-only condition.,1,False,Not an analytical model condition.,0.9,12,12-person societies in every treatment.,1,20,Earnings summed over 20 periods for all subjects.,1,1,Subjects allocate any amount of 20 MUs; no all-or-nothing restriction stated.,0.9,0,Endowment starts in private account and can be allocated to project account.,0.9,N/R,MPCR function of current group size per Table 2 rather than fixed treatment parameter.,1,0,No communication allowed; in cubicles with no interaction.,1,1,After each period subjects learn average contributions in all entities plus individual contributions in their own group.,1,0,Ostracism votes are internal to group; IDs are letters constant across experiment but no mention that who-voted-whom is revealed. Punishment per se is ostracism; no individual punishment with revealed identity beyond group knowledge.,0.5,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,No statement that remaining rounds are displayed.,0.5,1,Ostracism by majority vote is a form of social punishment via exclusion.,1,N/R,No explicit individual cost per ostracism vote or per expulsion reported; likely costless voting but not numerically specified.,0.4,N/R,Effect of ostracism (becoming free-agent) is qualitative; no numeric mapping per unit of ‘punishment’ is defined.,0.4,0,No explicit payoff-increasing reward action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,20 MUs per subject in all treatments.,1,0.9185,Mean contribution 18.37 MUs; 18.37/20 ≈ 0.9185.,1,18.37,“Mean contribution in treatment Ostracism is 18.37 MUs”.,1,N/R,Efficiency ratio not reported.,1,N/R,"Only qualitative comparison of net average pay-offs across treatments, no numbers.",0.3,Germany,Same subject pool at University of Hamburg.,0.7,N/R,Age distribution not provided.,1,"125 female, 115 male across all treatments",Overall gender composition reported only for the full sample.,0.8,Mostly undergraduate university students,Participants described as undergraduate students.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions with physical attendance and cash payments.,1,"Same dynamic public goods game with free migration as Baseline; in addition, after each period’s contribution stage, all group members are informed about individual allocations to the project account in their group and can vote by majority to ostracize any member. Ostracized individuals become free-agents with the standard options to apply to other groups, form new groups, or remain alone. All group members are informed after each period how many votes suggested their own ostracism, regardless of outcome. There is no intergroup competition in this treatment.",Summarizes ostracism mechanism that defines this treatment relative to Baseline.,1
10.1007_s00265-014-1741-8.md,Competition + ostracism,Treatment label in Table 1; combines group competition and ostracism.,1,Group competition: Yes; Ostracism: Yes,From Table 1 for ‘Competition + ostracism’ treatment.,1,True,Same lab experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Treatment manipulation of both competition and ostracism.,1,True,Run in onsite lab at University of Hamburg.,1,False,No simulations without human subjects.,1,False,Not an analytical-only condition.,0.9,12,Each society has 12 subjects.,1,20,Same 20 periods across treatments.,1,1,Continuous allocation of up to 20 MUs; no all-or-nothing restriction.,0.9,0,Endowment is initially in private account; contributions are voluntary transfers.,0.9,N/R,MPCR depends on group size per Table 2.,1,0,No communication; cubicles and no interaction.,1,1,Subjects see average contributions of all entities and individual contributions within own group after each period.,1,0,"Ostracism is voted anonymously in the description; who-voted-whom is not reported. Group members see numbers of votes suggesting their ostracism, but not identities.",0.6,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,0,No explicit mention of round counter on screen.,0.5,1,"Ostracism is available by majority vote and changes group membership, functioning as social punishment.",1,N/R,No numeric per-unit cost of ostracism voting specified.,0.4,N/R,Ostracism’s effect is qualitative (member becomes free-agent); not parameterized per cost unit.,0.4,0,No explicit reward action beyond standard payoffs and competition transfers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,20 MUs per subject across all treatments.,1,0.878,Mean contribution is 17.56 MUs; 17.56/20 ≈ 0.878.,1,17.56,“Mean contribution to the project account in treatment Competition + ostracism 17.56 MUs”.,1,N/R,Efficiency ratio not provided.,1,N/R,"Net pay-offs only described qualitatively and in figure, not numerically.",0.3,Germany,Same Hamburg lab subject pool.,0.7,N/R,Age not reported.,1,"125 female, 115 male across all treatments",Only overall gender composition provided.,0.8,Mostly undergraduate university students,Participants are undergraduate students.,1,On site,Sessions in physical lab with cash payments.,1,"Combines both additional mechanisms: group competition and ostracism. After allocation decisions, a majority in any entity can vote to engage in between-group comparison, with transfers based on differences in total contributions to the project account, as in the Competition treatment. Additionally, after learning individual contributions in the group, members can vote by majority to ostracize others; ostracized members become free-agents. Feedback about number of votes suggesting each member’s ostracism is provided after each period.",Summarizes how both competition and ostracism operate jointly in this treatment.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2014.06.041.md,"Tax-based strong altruistic punishment, no tax (A=0)","Condition described in Results and Fig. 1(a) as the case without a tax, i.e., A=0, for the tax-based strong altruistic punisher model.",1,"Tax amount A in punishment model; here A=0 (no tax). Other parameters fixed: M=15, r=3, c=1, H_SP=2.5, ω=0.7, ε=0.5.",Paper varies A while keeping other model parameters fixed; Fig. 1 compares A=0 versus A=0.2.,1,False,Purely theoretical evolutionary game model plus numerical calculations; no human subjects or data collection.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with participants; it is analysis of equations and numerical simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only mathematical and numerical analysis.,1,True,"They use numerical calculations of replicator dynamics (ODEs) on strategy frequencies C, D, SP.",1,True,"Sections 2, Appendix A derive analytical replicator equations and stability conditions.",1,15,Model group size M; explicitly given for figures as M=15.,1,1,"Evolutionary, infinitely repeated population game without discrete rounds; map to a single-stage PGG per interaction.",0.4,1,Each individual either cooperates by contributing fixed cost c or defects by contributing nothing (binary choice).,1,0,Model endowment is fully in private possession before choosing to contribute cost c to the public pool.,0.8,0.2,"MPCR = r/M. With r=3 and M=15, MPCR = 3/15 = 0.2.",1,0,No communication or signaling mechanisms are mentioned; continuous well-mixed population model.,1,0,No information feedback about others is modeled beyond payoffs and strategy frequencies.,1,0,Model operates at strategy level in a well-mixed population; no identities or observability structure.,1,0,This punishment condition includes no reward institution.,1,0,No finite-horizon or round information; continuous-time replicator dynamics.,1,1,Strong altruistic punishers contribute to a punishment pool and impose penalties ω and ε.,1,2.5,Punishers contribute H_SP to the punishment pool; parameter given as H_SP=2.5 in figures.,1,0,"Model specifies total penalty per defector/ cooperator as ω M_SP and ε M_SP, but not per-cost effectiveness (conversion per unit H_SP).",0.2,0,"This model variant includes only punishment, no reward fund.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in punishment-only condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in punishment-only condition.,1,1,"Cooperators contribute a fixed cost c; with c=1 in figures, interpret as contributing full endowment of 1 unit.",0.7,N/R,"They analyze frequencies of strategies, not contributions per player normalized by endowment.",0,N/R,Individual contribution is fixed at c for cooperators but aggregate contributions are not tabulated as outcome measures.,0.3,N/R,Paper does not report overall payoff relative to social optimum as a numeric measure.,0,N/R,Group payoff expressions exist symbolically but no specific numerical outcome values are given as results.,0.2,N/A,No human participants in analytical/simulation study.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical and computational; no empirical environment.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population public goods game with three strategies: cooperators (C), defectors (D), and strong altruistic punishers (SP). Group size M=15; enhancement factor r=3; cooperation cost c=1; punishment pool contribution H_SP=2.5; per-punisher penalties: ω=0.7 to defectors, ε=0.5 to cooperators; tax A=0 (traditional strong altruistic punishment). Evolution governed by replicator dynamics on frequencies x1, x2, x3.","Summarizes core model structure and parameters for this condition from Sections 2.2, 2.4, Appendix A, and Fig. 1(a).",1
10.1016_j.physa.2014.06.041.md,"Tax-based strong altruistic punishment, with tax (A=0.2)","Condition described in Results and Fig. 1(b) as the case with a tax, i.e., A=0.2, for the tax-based strong altruistic punisher model.",1,"Tax amount A in punishment model; here A=0.2. Other parameters fixed: M=15, r=3, c=1, H_SP=2.5, ω=0.7, ε=0.5.",Paper varies A and explicitly analyzes A=0.2 in figures for punishment model.,1,False,No human data; only theoretical model and numerical calculations.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; simulation/ODE analysis.,1,False,No physical lab or field; purely theoretical.,1,True,Uses numerical integration of replicator dynamics and phase portraits (simplex flow).,1,True,"Derives payoffs, replicator equations, equilibria, and stability analytically in Appendix A.",1,15,Group size parameter M; explicitly set to 15 for figures.,1,1,Continuous replicator dynamics on one-shot PGG interactions; mapped as single-round game structure.,0.4,1,"Binary choice: cooperate by contributing cost c, or defect by contributing nothing.",1,0,Endowment is held privately until a cooperator pays cost c to the pool.,0.8,0.2,MPCR = r/M = 3/15.,1,0,No modeled communication; only strategy imitation via payoffs.,1,0,Model at population level; no feedback interface.,1,0,No individual identities; only frequencies.,1,0,No reward mechanism in this punishment model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon replicator dynamics without explicit round counts.,1,1,Tax-based strong altruistic punishers contribute H_SP and impose penalties on D and C.,1,2.5,Punishers pay H_SP=2.5 into the punishment pool; constant across A.,1,0,"Penalty ω and ε are specified per punisher, not per unit H_SP; conversion rate not given.",0.2,0,This condition involves punishment only.,1,N/A,No reward pool.,1,N/A,No reward pool.,1,1,Cooperators incur cost c=1; treated as contributing full unit endowment.,0.7,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as strategy frequencies, not contribution rates.",0,N/R,Aggregate contribution or its trajectory is not summarized numerically.,0.3,N/R,No explicit comparison of realized payoffs to social optimum.,0,N/R,Model gives payoff formulas but not specific numeric group payoff results.,0.2,N/A,No human participants are modeled.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical and numerical study only.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population public goods game with strategies C, D, and SP. Parameters: M=15, r=3, c=1, H_SP=2.5, ω=0.7, ε=0.5, tax A=0.2. Each individual pays tax A into a tax pool; total tax is divided equally among punishers. Payoffs Π_C, Π_D, Π_SP defined in Section 2.2; expected payoffs F_C, F_D, F_SP given in Appendix A. Evolution follows replicator dynamics, leading to coexistence of cooperators and punishers and elimination of defectors at sufficient A.","Summarizes the punishment model including tax redistribution mechanism for A=0.2, as discussed around Fig. 1(b), Fig. 2(b), and Appendix A.",1
10.1016_j.physa.2014.06.041.md,"Tax-based strong altruistic reward, no tax (A=0)","Condition described in Results and Fig. 3(a) (and Fig. 4(a)) as the case without a tax, i.e., A=0, for the tax-based strong altruistic rewarder model (which reduces to traditional strong altruistic reward).",1,"Tax amount A in reward model; here A=0 (no tax). Other parameters fixed: M=15, r=3, c=1, H_SR=2.5, γ=0.7, η=0.5.",Paper varies A while keeping other parameters fixed; Figs. 3(a) and 4(a) use A=0 for reward model.,1,False,The study is a theoretical evolutionary game model plus numerical simulations.,1,False,No controlled experiment with participants.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting used.,1,True,"They numerically compute trajectories of replicator dynamics and simplex flow diagrams for C, D, SR.",1,True,"Appendix B presents analytical derivation of payoffs, equilibria, and stability.",1,15,Group size M is given as 15 for figures.,1,1,One-shot PGG interaction embedded in continuous-time evolutionary dynamics.,0.4,1,Individuals either contribute fixed cost c (cooperate) or nothing (defect).,1,0,Endowment is private; cooperators choose to pay cost c to public pool.,0.8,0.2,"Same PGG parameters: r=3, M=15 → MPCR=3/15.",1,0,No communication is present in the model structure.,1,0,No interface or feedback to agents except payoffs; purely mathematical model.,1,0,No punishment institution in this reward-focused condition.,1,0,No identities; only frequencies of strategies in a well-mixed population.,1,0,No notion of displayed rounds in continuous replicator dynamics.,1,0,"This model condition includes only strong altruistic rewarders, without punishment pool.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,1,Strong altruistic rewarders contribute to a reward pool and increase payoffs of cooperators and rewarders.,1,2.5,Rewarders contribute H_SR to the reward pool; parameter given as H_SR=2.5.,1,0,"Model defines added payoff γ M_SR to cooperators and η M_SR to rewarders per additional rewarder, but not per unit H_SR.",0.2,1,"As in punishment model, cooperators pay cost c=1, interpreted as full endowment.",0.7,N/R,"Outcome measures are strategy frequencies, not contribution fractions.",0,N/R,No explicit numerical reporting of contributions over time.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency relative to social optimum is discussed qualitatively but not quantified.,0,N/R,Group payoff formula exists but no numeric outcomes presented.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical and numerical modeling only.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population public goods game with strategies C, D, and SR. Parameters: M=15, r=3, c=1, H_SR=2.5, γ=0.7, η=0.5, tax A=0 (traditional strong altruistic reward). Rewarders pay H_SR to reward pool and give extra payoff γ per rewarder to cooperators and η per rewarder to other rewarders. Payoffs Π_C, Π_D, Π_SR given in Section 2.3; expected payoffs F_C, F_D, F_SR in Appendix B. For A=0, model tends to defection dominance; reward alone insufficient to sustain cooperation.","Summarizes the reward-only model without tax as described in Section 2.3, Appendix B, and discussed around Fig. 3(a) and Fig. 4(a).",1
10.1016_j.physa.2014.06.041.md,"Tax-based strong altruistic reward, with tax (A=0.2)","Condition described in Results and Fig. 3(b) (and Fig. 4(b)) as the case with a tax, i.e., A=0.2, for the tax-based strong altruistic rewarder model.",1,"Tax amount A in reward model; here A=0.2. Other parameters fixed: M=15, r=3, c=1, H_SR=2.5, γ=0.7, η=0.5.",Paper explicitly compares A=0 vs A=0.2 for reward model in figures.,1,False,The work is theoretical and computational.,1,False,No experimental manipulation of human subjects.,1,False,No lab or field context.,1,True,"Numerical integration and visualization of replicator dynamics are used (time evolution plots, simplex diagrams).",1,True,Appendix B contains analytical equilibrium and stability analysis.,1,15,M=15 specified for this set of numerical examples.,1,1,Single-stage PGG with continuous evolutionary updating.,0.4,1,Binary cooperate/defect choice with fixed contribution cost c.,1,0,Endowment controlled individually; cooperators decide to pay c to public pool.,0.8,0.2,"Same PGG core: r=3, M=15 so MPCR=3/15.",1,0,No communication modeled.,1,0,Model does not involve any display of other players' outcomes.,1,0,No punishment institution in reward model.,1,0,Agents are anonymous in the well-mixed population.,1,0,No discrete finite rounds; continuous-time dynamics.,1,0,Reward-only model with no punishment pool.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,1,Tax-based strong altruistic rewarders pay into a reward pool and distribute rewards.,1,2.5,Rewarders contribute H_SR=2.5 to reward pool in this parameterization.,1,0,"Rewards per rewarder are γ and η units to cooperators and SRs, not specified per cost unit.",0.2,1,Cooperation cost c=1 interpreted as contributing full endowment.,0.7,N/R,No explicit reporting of contribution fractions over endowment.,0,N/R,"Numerical results focus on frequencies of strategies, not contribution magnitudes.",0.3,N/R,Efficiency is not numerically summarized.,0,N/R,Group payoff is not provided as a numeric outcome.,0.2,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,No human,The study is purely mathematical and computational.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population public goods game with strategies C, D, and SR. Parameters: M=15, r=3, c=1, H_SR=2.5, γ=0.7, η=0.5, tax A=0.2. Each individual pays tax A; total tax is divided equally among rewarders. Rewarders also pay H_SR into a reward pool. Cooperators gain γ per rewarder and rewarders gain η per rewarder. Replicator dynamics on x1, x2, x3 describe evolution; for sufficient A, defectors are eliminated and cooperators and rewarders coexist, yielding higher cooperation than in the punishment model.","Summarizes reward model with tax redistribution mechanism for A=0.2 based on Section 2.3, Appendix B, and discussion related to Figs. 3(b) and 4(b).",1
