n extraction decision number 0–125 but no per-period monetary endowment is specified.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports extraction levels and yield index, not normalized contribution (which would be 1 - extraction/125).",0.4,N/R,"Aggregate statistics for extraction are described (e.g., Nash equilibrium 112.5, social optimum 50) but treatment-specific averages are not tabulated.",0.3,N/R,"They compute 'yield' (aggregate payoff / payoff at social optimum) and note efficiency parallels yield, but do not give numeric averages by treatment.",0.6,N/R,"Only theoretical group payoffs at Nash and social optimum are given, not realized averages by treatment.",0.6,"USA (students at Purdue University, West Lafayette)",Experiment conducted at Purdue University in the USA; sample described as university students there.,0.95,Mostly 18–23,Procedures: '94% of our subjects are between the ages of 18 and 23'.,1,N/R,Gender composition is not reported.,0.1,"University students (undergraduates, diverse majors)",Procedures: '100% are university students' with majors such as Engineering and Business.,1,On site,Conducted in the Vernon Smith Experimental Economics Laboratory using z-Tree on lab computers.,1,Nonlinear common-pool resource extraction game with concave private benefit B(Ei)=44-0.002(125-Ei)^2 and linear damage D=alpha*E with alpha=0.05; unique dominant-strategy Nash extraction 112.5; social optimum extraction 50.,Details on payoff functions and Nash/social optimum are given in Section 3.1.,1
