reatment are provided.,0.6,N/R,Realized group payoffs for this treatment are not tabulated.,0.6,"USA (students at Purdue University, West Lafayette)",Same subject pool as all treatments.,0.95,Mostly 18–23,Applies to entire sample of 192 student subjects.,1,N/R,Gender information not given.,0.1,"University students (undergraduates, diverse majors)",All participants are university students.,1,On site,Same physical laboratory setting described for whole study.,1,Nonlinear CPR extraction game with same payoff structure as other treatments; includes both punishment (deduction points) every period and two 5-minute anonymous group chats (before periods 11 and 21).,Combined description in Section 3.2 and detailed Appendix instructions labeled 'Swords with Covenants Treatment'.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2021.102448.md,Second part – Normative disagreement condition,"Paper describes two experimental conditions in the second set of 10 rounds: one where newcomers and incumbents are sorted for normative disagreement (""disagreement condition"") and one for agreement. This row covers the disagreement condition in rounds 11–20.",1,Group-level sorting of members by normative views: newcomers normatively disagree with incumbents (newcomer has dissimilar normative view to the two incumbents).,"Section 3.3 explains that in this condition, after first 10 rounds, a member with similar normative views to co-members is replaced by a newcomer with a dissimilar normative view, creating normative disagreement between newcomer and two incumbents.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with 192 human participants at Utrecht University.,1,True,"Participants were randomly assigned to conditions and to groups, and the independent variable (sorting for disagreement vs agreement) was experimentally manipulated.",1,True,Conducted in the Experimental Laboratory for Sociology and Economics (ELSE) with participants in individual cubicles.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model as the primary method; paper reports empirical lab data.,1,3,Groups of three members in the public goods game (N=3).,1,10,Second part consists of a second set of 10 rounds after membership change; this condition refers to rounds 11–20.,1,1,"Contributions c_i ∈ {0,1,…,20}; participants can choose any integer amount, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Each round, each individual receives an endowment of 20 MU and decides how much to contribute; all 20 start in private account.",1,0.5833333333,Two members have m_i=0.50 and one has m_i=0.75; sum of returns M=1.75. MPCR = M/N = 1.75/3 ≈ 0.5833.,1,0,No communication or chat is mentioned; interaction is via computerized decisions only.,0.9,1,"After contributions, ""the contributions and payoffs of each member are communicated to all group members"", which is a summary of others' actions and earnings.",1,0,They explicitly state participants do not see who punished whom to curb revenge motives.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the design; hence no reward identities shown.,1,0,"Participants are told there will be two parts of 10 rounds, but no indication that remaining rounds are displayed on screen each round.",0.5,1,Second stage each round allows assigning punishment points p_ij.,1,1,Each assigned punishment point costs 1 MU to the punisher.,1,3,Each received punishment point reduces payoff of punished member by 3 MU.,1,0,Only punishment points are described; no positive reward option is mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,20,Each individual receives an endowment of 20 MU each round.,1,0.69,Fig. 3c text reports proportion of endowment contributed after membership change: 0.69 in disagreement condition (average rounds 11–20).,1,13.8,Contribution rate 0.69 of endowment 20 MU implies 0.69*20 = 13.8 MU average contribution.,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) reported; insufficient data by condition to compute precisely.,0,N/R,Paper does not report average payoffs by condition for rounds 11–20.,0,"Mixed – mainly Utrecht University students (87 Dutch, 105 other nationalities overall sample)","Section 3: almost all participants attending Utrecht University; 87 Dutch nationality, 105 other countries. No breakdown by condition; assume similar mix by random assignment.",0.7,Mean 24 years (overall sample),Average age of participants was 24 years; no condition-specific ages reported.,0.8,"66% female, remainder mainly male, 3 other (overall sample)","Overall sample: 127 female (66%), 62 male, 3 other; random assignment implies similar distribution per condition.",0.8,Mostly university students at Utrecht University,Participants recruited among students at Utrecht University; almost all attending courses there.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical lab (ELSE) with participants in individual cubicles; not online.,1,"Public goods game with heterogeneous returns (two low-return players m=0.50, one high-return m=0.75; M=1.75). Peer punishment stage each round with p_ij∈{0,…,10}. Two low-return incumbents, one low-return newcomer after member switch; high-return player always incumbent. Minimal group colors (blue/orange) used to mark prior groups; member identities anonymous but type (high vs low return) known. No information on others’ normative views; contributions and payoffs of each member shown after each round.",Summarizes structural details from sections 3.1 and 3.3 that are not captured in other fields.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2021.102448.md,Second part – Normative agreement condition,"This row covers the second condition in the second set of 10 rounds, where newcomers and incumbents are sorted for normative agreement (""agreement condition"").",1,Group-level sorting of members by normative views: newcomers normatively agree with incumbents (newcomer has similar normative view to the two incumbents).,"Section 3.3 (lower part of Fig. 1 description) states that in this condition, a member with a dissimilar normative view is replaced by a newcomer with a similar normative view, creating normative agreement between newcomer and incumbents.",1,True,"Same human-subject laboratory experiment, different condition.",1,True,Condition assignment and sorting rule are experimentally manipulated.,1,True,Conducted at ELSE lab with participants in cubicles.,1,False,No simulations involved.,1,False,"Study reports experimental data, not an analytical model.",1,3,Same 3-player group structure.,1,10,Second part of experiment consists of 10 rounds after membership change (rounds 11–20).,1,1,Contributions can be any integer 0–20 MU.,1,0,All 20 MU endowment start privately; participants decide contribution.,1,0.5833333333,"Same heterogeneous returns structure: two m=0.50, one m=0.75, so M=1.75 and MPCR=M/N≈0.5833.",1,0,No mention of communication between participants.,0.9,1,Contributions and payoffs of each member are communicated to all members each round.,1,0,"Punishers and targets remain anonymous; ""we do not let participants see who punished whom"".",1,0,No rewards implemented.,1,0,Design mentions two 10-round parts but does not state that remaining rounds are displayed each round.,0.5,1,Second-stage peer punishment available each round.,1,1,Each punishment point costs 1 MU to punisher.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces recipient payoff by 3 MU.,1,0,"Only punishment, no explicit reward option.",1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,20,Each participant receives 20 MU per round.,1,0.75,"Fig. 3c reports that after membership change, proportion of endowment contributed is 0.75 in agreement condition (average rounds 11–20).",1,15,Contribution rate 0.75 of 20 MU endowment implies 0.75*20 = 15 MU average contribution.,0.95,N/R,No condition-specific efficiency measure reported and insufficient payoff detail to infer accurately.,0,N/R,Group payoff data by condition are not reported.,0,"Mixed – mainly Utrecht University students (87 Dutch, 105 other nationalities overall sample)",Same participant pool as other condition; random assignment implies similar composition.,0.7,Mean 24 years (overall sample),Average age 24 for entire sample; not broken down by condition.,0.8,"66% female, remainder mainly male, 3 other (overall sample)",Overall gender distribution; randomization suggests similar by condition.,0.8,Mostly university students at Utrecht University,Participants recruited among Utrecht University students.,0.9,On site,Same lab environment (ELSE) with in-person sessions.,1,"Same public goods game with heterogeneous returns (two low-return m=0.50, one high-return m=0.75; M=1.75) and anonymous peer punishment (p_ij∈{0,…,10}). Minimal group colors mark prior groups; member switch yields one newcomer and two incumbents, but here newcomer’s normative view matches incumbents’. Contributions and payoffs shown each round; identities of punishers not revealed.","Summarizes design aspects that apply equally but are not captured elsewhere, emphasizing that only sorting rule differs from disagreement condition.",1
10.1098_rspb.2021.1611.md,Mutual-Punishers scenario – all players (non-immune),Baseline condition replicating Fehr & Gächter where all 4 group members can punish and none are immune; paper labels this the 'Mutual-Punishers' scenario.,1,Scenario: Mutual-Punishers; Immunity: non-immune (all 4 players can punish and can be punished),Independent variables are scenario type (who can punish) and immunity status; here everyone is non-immune and can punish.,1,True,Human participants in lab public-goods game with punishment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatment scenarios.,1,True,"Conducted at HEC-LABEX facility, University of Lausanne.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation described.,1,False,No formal analytical model only; primary results are from experiment.,1,4,Groups of four in all scenarios.,1,5,Repeated game for five rounds under perfect-stranger matching.,1,1,"Contribution decision was 0–20 MU, continuous integer range, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each individual given 20 MU endowment and chooses how many to contribute; endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,All contributions multiplied by 1.6 and shared among 4; MPCR = 1.6/4 = 0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous PGG with no chat.,0.9,1,"After contributions, individuals were informed of individual contributions and pay-offs of each group mate.",1,0,"Participants learn only how many MU were deducted, not which individual(s) punished them.",1,0,No reward mechanism in design.,1,1,"They were told they would play a repeated game for five rounds, implying knowledge of total rounds.",0.9,1,Second phase allows paying to deduct earnings from others.,1,1,Cost of punishment was 1 MU to deduct 3 MU.,1,3,Each 1 MU spent deducts 3 MU from target.,1,0,No option to pay to increase others’ payoffs described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Individuals were given 20 MU each round for the contribution decision.,1,0.435,Mean contributions stable between 42% and 45%; approximate mid-point 43.5% used as overall rate.,0.6,8.7,ContributionRate (0.435) × endowment (20 MU) ≈ 8.7 MU.,0.6,N/R,Text only states that punishment was ultimately destructive; no exact efficiency ratio reported.,0,N/R,Mean final earnings by scenario or per round not broken down.,0,Switzerland,"Participants at University of Lausanne, Switzerland.",1,"Mostly under 26; 134 <20, 257 age 20–25, 23 age 26–30, 2 age 30–35, 3 >35, 1 not reported",Age distribution explicitly reported for full sample.,1,"202 female, 215 male, 2 other, 1 declined",Gender distribution explicitly reported for full sample.,1,Mostly students at University of Lausanne (UNIL) or EPFL,Participants mostly students enrolled at UNIL or EPFL.,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab (HEC-LABEX facility) using z-Tree.,1,Perfect-stranger matching across 5 rounds; punishment phase with per-target budget up to 10 MU (6 MU in sessions 1–2); each player has punishment budget 30 MU (18 MU in sessions 1–2); maximum earnings from contribution stage 44 MU; negative payoffs per round prevented.,Summarizes additional structural details described in Methods.,1
10.1098_rspb.2021.1611.md,Immune-Punisher scenario – immune punisher role,Condition where one group member is 'immune punisher'—immune to punishment but able to punish all three group mates.,1,"Scenario: Immune-Punisher; Immunity: immune punisher (1 of 4 players per group, can punish all 3 others, cannot be punished)",Independent variables are scenario structure and immunity status; here the immune punisher role is focal.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to roles within controlled game scenarios.,1,True,Conducted at HEC-LABEX lab.,1,False,No simulations reported for this condition.,1,False,"Results based on behavior, not purely analytical model.",1,4,Groups of four in all scenarios.,1,5,Repeated game for five rounds under perfect-stranger matching.,1,1,"Contribution choice 0–20 MU, not binary.",1,0,20 MU endowment fully under individual control before contribution.,1,0.4,Same linear public goods technology across scenarios (1.6 multiplier divided by 4).,1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,0.9,1,Participants informed of each group mate’s contributions and pay-offs before punishment decisions.,1,0,"Victims learned only total MU deducted, not identity or number of punishers.",1,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,Participants told they would play five rounds of the game.,0.9,1,Immune punisher can pay to deduct others' earnings.,1,1,Punishment cost 1 MU per 3 MU fine.,1,3,Each MU spent deducts 3 MU from target.,1,0,No rewarding option mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same endowment as in baseline: 20 MU.,1,0.24,Immune individuals in this scenario declined from 31% to 17%; mid-point of 24% used as approximate mean over 5 rounds.,0.6,4.8,ContributionRate (0.24) × 20 MU = 4.8 MU.,0.6,N/R,Efficiency for this subgroup not reported; only qualitative statement about destruction from punishment in overall sample.,0,N/R,No separate payoff statistics by role and scenario.,0,Switzerland,Same participant pool for all scenarios.,1,"Mostly under 26; 134 <20, 257 age 20–25, 23 age 26–30, 2 age 30–35, 3 >35, 1 not reported",Global sample description applies to all roles.,1,"202 female, 215 male, 2 other, 1 declined",Global gender distribution for all participants.,1,Mostly students at University of Lausanne (UNIL) or EPFL,Same student pool in all sessions.,1,On site,Lab experiment at HEC-LABEX.,1,"In Immune-Punisher scenario, 1 immune punisher per group can punish all 3 group mates but cannot be punished; 3 non-immune punishers can punish only the other 2 non-immune players. Same punishment budgets and limits as baseline (30 MU per round; up to 10 MU per target, subject to session-specific caps).",Summarizes role-specific structure from figure 1 and text.,1
