10.1098_rspb.2021.1611.md,Sole-Punisher scenario – non-immune non-punishers,Condition for the three non-immune players per group in Sole-Punisher scenario who cannot punish and can be punished by the sole punisher.,1,"Scenario: Sole-Punisher; Immunity/role: non-immune non-punisher (3 of 4 players per group, cannot punish, can be punished by the sole punisher)","Independent variables are scenario structure and whether the individual has the punishment ability; here, non-punishing role is focal.",1,True,Human participants in lab game.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of who can punish.,1,True,Experiment at HEC-LABEX facility.,1,False,No computer-only simulations.,1,False,Empirical rather than purely analytical.,1,4,Groups consist of four players.,1,5,Design used five repeated rounds.,1,1,Contribution range 0–20 MU.,1,0,Initial endowment entirely private.,1,0.4,Same multiplier 1.6 and group size 4.,1,0,No discussion or messaging facility mentioned.,0.9,1,Non-punishers also receive information on others' contributions and payoffs.,1,0,Punishment remains anonymous to punishees.,1,0,No rewards stage in any scenario.,1,1,Participants know the game will last five rounds.,0.9,1,"Punishment exists in the game, though these players cannot administer it.",1,N/A,"These non-punisher roles are not allowed to punish, so cost per punishment they could choose is not applicable.",1,N/A,Cannot administer punishment from this role.,1,0,No rewards available.,1,N/A,Reward stage absent.,1,N/A,Reward stage absent.,1,20,All players receive 20 MU per round.,1,0.355,Non-immune individuals in Sole-Punisher scenario declined from 46% to 25%; approximate mean ≈ 35.5%.,0.6,7.1,ContributionRate (0.355) × 20 MU ≈ 7.1 MU.,0.6,N/R,No efficiency statistics reported by role.,0,N/R,Group or individual earnings for this role not specified.,0,Switzerland,Same Swiss participant pool.,1,"Mostly under 26; 134 <20, 257 age 20–25, 23 age 26–30, 2 age 30–35, 3 >35, 1 not reported",Reported age distribution for all conditions combined.,1,"202 female, 215 male, 2 other, 1 declined",Overall gender composition.,1,Mostly students at University of Lausanne (UNIL) or EPFL,Same recruitment base in all scenarios.,1,On site,Laboratory setting.,1,"In Sole-Punisher scenario, 3 non-punisher players per group can be punished by the sole punisher but have no option to punish others; otherwise face same contribution game and information structure.",Derived from description of the sole-punisher scenario; only one group member can punish.,1
10.3390_g10010011.md,Donation-punishment game under linear expected utility theory,"Paper analyzes one theoretical donation-punishment game with two alternative payoff-evaluation frameworks; this condition corresponds to the linear expected utility case (γ=1, α=1, λ=1 implicitly).",0.86,"Payoff evaluation framework = linear expected utility theory; punishment strength s and error rate ε varied; cost of cooperation c=1, punishment cost r=1, benefit b=4; strategies available: CP, CN, DP, DN.","Model section defines parameters (c=r=1, b=4), strategies, and states that results are compared between linear expected utility and prospect theory while varying s and ε.",0.92,False,No human participants; infinite well-mixed population analyzed mathematically.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; purely theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; analytical evolutionary game model.,1,False,Main text uses analytical derivations and replicator dynamics; agent-based simulations mentioned only as future work.,0.9,True,Closed-form payoff expressions and replicator equations on an infinite population are derived and analyzed.,1,2,Donation(-punishment) game is defined for two randomly matched players.,1,1,"Each encounter is a single donation-punishment sequence; evolution occurs via replicator dynamics over many encounters, not repeated-game rounds between fixed partners.",0.8,0,"Players have binary choices: cooperate or defect, punish or not punish; no continuous contribution level.",1,N/A,Not a public goods contribution with an initial allocation; theoretical pairwise donation game without human subjects.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined because this is not a linear public goods game but a donation game with fixed benefit b and cost c.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback to human participants; model is not implemented with subjects.,1,N/A,No identities; population is anonymous and infinite.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No user interface; evolutionary process is continuous time.,1,1,Model includes a peer punishment phase where defectors can be punished.,1,1,Parameters are set to c=r=1 in analysis; r is the cost per punishment.,1,N/R,"Paper specifies that punishment reduces the opponent’s payoff by s, but does not clarify if this is per unit of cost r or a one-off per punishment; mapping to 'per coin' technology is undefined.",0.6,0,Only peer punishment is modeled; rewards are not included in this game.,1,N/A,No reward option in the model.,1,N/A,No reward option in the model.,1,N/R,"Game is parameterized by benefits and costs (b, c, r, s); no explicit per-round monetary/token endowment is defined.",0.7,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as evolutionary stability of strategies and phase diagrams, not as numerical contribution rates.",0.7,N/R,No specific numerical contributions are reported beyond binary cooperate/defect.,0.9,N/R,"Paper discusses stability of cooperative vs defective states, but does not compute normalized efficiency values.",0.7,N/R,"Only relative payoffs and dominance relations are analyzed, not absolute expected group payoffs.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; infinite theoretical population.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game theory with infinite population and no subjects.,1,"Two-player donation-punishment game in an infinite well-mixed population. Parameters: cooperation cost c=1, benefit to partner b=4, punishment cost r=1, punishment damage s (varied), symmetric implementation errors with probability ε affecting both cooperation and punishment actions. Four pure strategies: CP, CN, DP, DN. Evolution governed by replicator dynamics under linear expected utility (no probability or value distortion).",Summarized from Sections 2.1–2.2.1 and results’ parameter specification.,0.96
10.3390_g10010011.md,Donation-punishment game under prospect theory,"Same strategic donation-punishment game analyzed, but expected payoffs are evaluated with prospect theory (probability weighting and value function).",0.9,"Payoff evaluation framework = prospect theory with parameters γ=0.65 for probability weighting, α=0.88 and λ=2.25 for value function; punishment strength s and error rate ε varied; cost of cooperation c=1, punishment cost r=1, benefit b=4; strategies available: CP, CN, DP, DN.","Section 2.2.2 defines w(x) with γ=0.65 and v(x) with α=0.88, λ=2.25 and states these are used in the following analysis; same base game and parameters as in linear case.",0.98,False,Model uses no human participants; it is a theoretical evolutionary model.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only mathematical analysis and vector-field plots.,1,False,No lab or field setting; fully theoretical.,1,False,"Dynamics studied by analytical conditions and vector fields; although numerical solving of equations is used, there is no explicit agent-based or Monte Carlo simulation with agents.",0.85,True,Expected payoffs under prospect theory and resulting replicator dynamics are derived and analyzed symbolically; parameter regions for stability are characterized.,1,2,Same two-player donation-punishment interaction as linear case.,1,1,Each encounter is a one-shot donation-punishment game; evolution via replicator dynamics over many encounters.,0.8,0,"Actions are binary (cooperate/defect, punish/not punish).",1,N/A,Not a human-subject public goods game; no initial allocation between private and public accounts.,1,N/R,Game is not formulated as a linear public goods problem; thus MPCR is not defined or reported.,1,N/A,No human interaction or communication.,1,N/A,No user interface; no human subjects.,1,N/A,No identities of players beyond strategy types in an infinite population.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is included.,1,N/A,No displayed rounds; evolutionary process in continuous time.,1,1,Same peer punishment institution as in linear case; only evaluation of outcomes differs.,1,1,"Parameter r, the cost to punish, is set to 1 in Section 3.",1,N/R,"Punishment reduces opponent’s payoff by s when applied, but the mapping to a per-unit technology parameter (damage per cost unit) is not specified.",0.6,0,"Model includes only punishment, not reward; rewards mentioned only in related literature.",1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,"Game payoffs are determined by fixed parameters b, c, r, s; there is no explicit token endowment per round defined.",0.7,N/R,"Outcomes are characterized in terms of stability of cooperative vs defective strategies (CP, CN, DN) and regions where cooperation evolves, not via explicit numerical contribution fractions.",0.7,N/R,Contribution is binary and not summarized with numeric mean amounts.,0.9,N/R,"Although cooperative states are more profitable, efficiency (payoff normalized by maximum) is not explicitly calculated.",0.7,N/R,Paper focuses on inequalities between strategy payoffs and their stability rather than reporting explicit expected group payoffs.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Prospect-theoretic effects are modeled analytically; no behavioral experiment is run.,1,"Same two-player donation-punishment game as in the linear case, embedded in an infinite well-mixed population with symmetric implementation error ε on cooperation and punishment actions. Parameters c=1, r=1, b=4, punishment strength s varied, strategies CP, CN, DP, DN (with DP later shown dominated). Unlike linear expected utility, expected payoffs are computed using prospect theory: objective probabilities in the payoff matrix are transformed by a weighting function w(x)=x^γ/[x^γ+(1−x)^γ]^{1/γ} with γ=0.65, and monetary outcomes are transformed by a value function v(x)=x^α for gains and −λ(−x)^α for losses, with α=0.88, λ=2.25, reference point at payoff 0. Replicator dynamics on strategy frequencies are then analyzed to determine regions where DN is globally stable, CN is globally stable, or both are bistable as functions of ε and normalized punishment strength s/s_max.","Summarizes Sections 2.1–2.2.2 and Figure 3 description, emphasizing the prospect-theoretic modifications.",0.98
10.1111_geer.12103.md,Day 1 – Basic public goods game (no additional rules),The paper describes an initial 25-round play of a standard public goods game on Day 1 before any designed rule sets are implemented.,1,"Institutional rule set: basic game with no punishment, no communication, no additional rules.",Condition differs from later ones by having no extra rules; the institutional environment is the main independent variable across conditions.,0.9,True,Human students played the game in the laboratory.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with predetermined rules in a lab.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory setting at the University of Erfurt.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported for this condition.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model analyzed as a separate condition.,1,4,Basic game was a standard public goods game with four players.,1,25,Subjects played the basic game over 25 rounds in a partner design.,1,1,"Players could contribute any integer amount between 0 and 20, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each player had an endowment of 20 tokens from which they chose a contribution; all tokens start in private accounts.,1,0.4,Sum of contributions was multiplied by 1.6 and evenly divided among 4 players: 1.6/4 = 0.4.,1,0,No communication mechanisms mentioned for the basic game.,0.9,0,"Subjects were informed only about the sum of contributions, not about others’ earnings or other summaries.",0.8,0,No punishment mechanism exists in the basic game.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism exists.,1,1,"In repeated public goods experiments, total rounds are typically announced; 25-round horizon is clearly specified in the design.",0.7,0,There was neither punishment nor any other additional non-standard rule feature.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No explicit reward or bonus mechanism is described for contributions.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,20,Each player contributed from an endowment of 20 tokens.,1,N/R,Paper does not report average contributions in the basic game.,0,N/R,No numerical summary of contributions for the basic game is provided.,0,N/R,Efficiency for this initial basic-game round is not tabulated or summarized numerically.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff numbers are reported.,0,Germany,Students were recruited at the University of Erfurt (Germany).,1,N/R,No age information given beyond being university students.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Economics students at a German university,The paper states that 24 students of economics were recruited.,1,On site,Described as a laboratory setting and seminar-type courses conducted at the university.,1,Partner matching across 25 rounds; feedback limited to sum of contributions per round; no additional rules or costs.,Based on description of Day 1 procedure.,0.9
10.1140_epjb_e2020-100618-x.md,"Simulation – Baseline PGG without payoff transfer (a=0, b=0)","Figure 6 explicitly compares '(0,0)' as traditional public goods game baseline with no reputation-based payoff transfer.",1,"Transfer mechanism off: a=0, b=0; synergy factor r varied; transfer strength s N/A","Authors state that (0,0) represents traditional PGG. Here the independent variables are presence/absence of transfer (a,b) and the synergy factor r.",1,False,"Study uses numerical simulations on a lattice, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; only simulations.,1,True,Described as 'numerical simulations' on an L×L lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution; only model definition plus simulations.,1,10000,"Lattice size L=100, so total players L^2 = 100^2 = 10000.",1,10000,They state each simulation runs at least 10000 steps until cooperation stabilizes; use 10000 as the minimum used throughout.,0.7,1,"Each cooperator donates c_i=1 and defectors donate 0; no partial contributions allowed, so contribution choice is all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment allocation between private/public; purely model tokens.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/(group size). They use von Neumann neighborhood n=4 so group size = n+1 = 5. With r=3 (default) MPCR=3/5=0.6. For varying r this value changes, but baseline parameter set around r=3 gives 0.6.",0.7,N/A,No human communication; lattice agents do not chat.,1,N/A,No human interface; summaries not applicable.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage with identities; also no humans.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage with identities; also no humans.,1,N/A,No display to human participants.,1,0,Baseline PGG without transfer has no punishment or transfer of payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward/bonus mechanism when a=b=0.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator donates c_i=1; this serves as the per-round endowment in model units.,0.8,N/R,"They report only fraction of cooperators ρ, not normalized contribution per player.",0.6,N/R,Only ρ (fraction of cooperators) is plotted; no average contribution amount reported.,0.6,N/R,No direct or computable measure of group efficiency is provided.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative payoffs and ρ are discussed; no numeric group payoff.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Numerical simulations with agents on a lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a 100×100 lattice with von Neumann neighborhood (n=4). Each player participates in n+1=5 groups. Initial strategies: 50% cooperators, 50% defectors. Strategy update uses Fermi rule with noise k=0.1.",Summarized from Section 2 model description and simulation settings.,1
10.1140_epjb_e2020-100618-x.md,"Simulation – Inert transfer ratios (b=0.2, a varied)","Section 3 describes 'inert transfer ratios' with b fixed at 0.2 and increasing a, illustrated in Figure 1.",1,"Transfer parameters: b=0.2, a varied (0<a<1); synergy factor r=3; transfer strength s=0.5.",Text: 'here b is fixed at 0.2' and 'we take the inert transfer ratios of b=0.2 as an example'; default parameters listed earlier.,1,False,Purely numerical simulations.,1,False,Not a controlled human experiment.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Authors repeatedly refer to 'numerical simulations'.,1,False,No closed-form analysis.,1,10000,L=100 so L^2=10000.,1,10000,Each simulation runs at least 10000 steps; used as representative horizon.,0.7,1,"Contribution choice is binary: c_i=1 for cooperators, 0 for defectors.",1,N/A,No human initial endowments split between accounts.,1,0.6,"Synergy factor r=3 and group size 5 (n+1), so MPCR=3/5.",1,N/A,No communication between human subjects.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No explicit identity-based punishment phase with humans.,1,N/A,No explicit identity-based reward phase with humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable without humans.,1,1,Payoffs of low-ranking (transferred) players are reduced by proportion s; this functions as punishment.,1,0.5,Transfer strength s is defined as 'the proportion of the transferred payoff of the individual to its total payoff'; set to 0.5 in simulations.,1,0.5,Each transferred player loses fraction s of its payoff; effective punishment magnitude per unit own payoff equals s.,0.7,1,Collected payoff forms a bonus fund divided among accepted players; this is a reward.,1,0,Accepted players receive bonus without paying own cost; their 'cost' per reward unit is zero.,0.9,N/R,Paper does not specify per-unit reward multiplier relative to some expenditure; only that fund is equally divided among accepted players.,0.4,1,Cooperators donate c_i=1; treat this as unit endowment.,0.8,0,They report that 'cooperation rate will quickly decay to zero ... defectors finally occupy the entire population'; thus final fraction of cooperators ρ≈0.,0.95,0,"With ρ→0 and only cooperators contribute 1, average contribution amount per player tends to 0.",0.9,N/R,No explicit normalized efficiency metric reported for these conditions.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative outcomes (extinction of cooperators) shown; no numeric payoff values.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Lattice-based agent simulation.,1,"Reputation-based payoff transfer with b=0.2 and varying a. After each round, players are ranked by accumulated reputation scores; lowest-ranked fraction b are 'transferred' (lose fraction s of their payoff), and top fraction a are 'accepted' (share the bonus fund). Evolution by Fermi imitation with k=0.1.",Summarized from Section 2 and Figure 1 description.,1
10.1140_epjb_e2020-100618-x.md,"Simulation – Conditional transfer ratios (a=0.3, b varied)","Section 3 defines 'conditional transfer ratios' with a fixed at 0.3 and increasing b, illustrated in Figure 2.",1,"Transfer parameters: a=0.3, b varied; synergy factor r=3; transfer strength s=0.5.","Text: 'Taking the ratios of the a to 0.3 as the example' with increasing b; other parameters set as k=0.1, L=100, n=4, r=3, s=0.5.",1,False,No human participants.,1,False,Simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Described as numerical simulations.,1,False,No analytical derivations of dynamics.,1,10000,L=100 so L^2=10000 players.,1,10000,Simulations run at least 10000 steps.,0.7,1,"Binary contribution choice c_i∈{0,1}.",1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,0.6,"r=3, group size=5 so MPCR=3/5.",1,N/A,Agents do not communicate like humans.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,No identities for human punishment.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,1,Low-ranked 'transferred' players lose a fraction s of their payoff.,1,0.5,Transfer strength s=0.5 is the proportion removed from transferred players' payoffs.,1,0.5,Being selected as transferred reduces own payoff by fraction s.,0.7,1,Collected transferred payoff is redistributed to accepted high-ranking players.,1,0,Accepted players incur no cost to receive bonus.,0.9,N/R,Paper does not specify per-unit reward factor; only equal split among L^2·a accepted players.,0.4,1,Cooperators donate c_i=1 each round.,0.8,N/R,"They provide qualitative and graphical ρ for multiple b values (some go to zero, others to medium/high), but no single numeric value per condition is given.",0.3,N/R,"Only fraction of cooperators ρ is plotted, without precise numeric endpoints.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency metric computed.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs are discussed only qualitatively.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Agent-based lattice simulation.,1,"Conditional ratios with a=0.3, b varied (e.g., b=0.3,0.5,0.6,0.7). Cooperation dies out for b≤0.5; for b=0.6,0.7 cooperation emerges to a moderate stable level. Spatial snapshots in Figure 4 illustrate dynamics for (b=0.5,a=0.3) and (b=0.7,a=0.3).",Summarized from explanation of conditional transfer ratios in Section 3 and Figures 2 and 4.,1
10.1140_epjb_e2020-100618-x.md,"Simulation – Effective transfer ratios (medium a and b, r=3, s=0.5)","Section 3 defines 'effective transfer ratios' where a and b are both medium (0.3<a<0.7, 0.3<b<0.7), with examples in Figure 3 and snapshots in Figure 5.",1,"Transfer parameters: a,b both in (0.3,0.7), multiple combinations (including (0.4,0.6), (0.5,0.5), etc.); synergy factor r=3; transfer strength s=0.5.",Text: 'effective transfer ratios in which b(0.3<b<0.7) and a(0.3<a<0.7) are medium' and 'here the values in parentheses represent the combination of b and a'.,1,False,Agent-based numerical simulation.,1,False,No human or field experiment.,1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,They perform numerical simulations on a regular lattice.,1,False,Results based on simulation runs.,1,10000,"L=100, so L^2=10000.",1,10000,At least 10000 steps per parameter group; use 10000 as typical.,0.7,1,Binary cooperation decision c_i=1 or 0.,1,N/A,No human endowments or default allocations.,1,0.6,"r=3, group size=5 → MPCR=3/5.",1,N/A,No chat or communication stage.,1,N/A,No user interface to show summaries.,1,N/A,No explicit IDs and human punishment visibility.,1,N/A,No human identification.,1,N/A,Rounds not displayed to humans.,1,1,Low-ranked players have part of payoff transferred away.,1,0.5,s=0.5 fraction of payoff is taken from each transferred player.,1,0.5,Punishment magnitude equals s fraction of the target's payoff.,0.7,1,Collected payoff forms bonus fund distributed to high-ranked accepted players.,1,0,Accepted players pay nothing to receive bonuses.,0.9,N/R,No explicit per-unit mapping from costs to rewards; just redistribution rule.,0.4,1,Cooperator donation c_i=1 serves as per-round resource.,0.8,N/R,"They state that 'the level of cooperation is high in each combination', approaching ρ≈1 for some (e.g., (0.5,0.5)), but no exact numeric ρ per combination is reported.",0.4,N/R,Only graphs and qualitative descriptions of ρ; not enough for precise numeric value.,0.3,N/R,"Efficiency not computed, even though cooperation is near-global in some cases.",0.3,N/R,Group payoff values are not numerically reported.,0.3,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,No human,Numerical simulation environment only.,1,"Effective transfer ratios with medium a and b yield high and often stable cooperation levels. Optimal case at (b=0.5, a=0.5) produces near-global cooperation rapidly; another example (b=0.4, a=0.6) also yields large-scale cooperation. Spatial snapshots in Figure 5 show accepted cooperators (green) dominating the lattice.",Summarized from Section 3 discussion of effective transfer ratios and Figures 3 and 5.,1
10.1140_epjb_e2020-100618-x.md,Simulation – Effective transfer ratios with varying synergy factor r,"Figure 6 analyzes fraction of cooperators as a function of synergy factor r under several effective transfer ratios and compares with baseline (0,0).",1,"Synergy factor r varied; multiple effective transfer ratios (a,b) including (0,0), (0.4,0.6), (0.5,0.5) and others; transfer strength s=0.5.","Text: 'Figure 6 depicts the effect of the synergy factor r on the fraction of cooperation when effective transfer ratios are applied' and legend includes (0,0).",1,False,Simulation on lattice without human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Results generated via numerical simulations.,1,False,No analytical solution for dependence on r.,1,10000,L=100 → L^2=10000.,1,10000,At least 10000 steps per parameter set.,0.7,1,Same PGG structure: cooperate (1) or defect (0).,1,N/A,No human endowment split.,1,N/R,r is varied across a range; a single numeric MPCR is not fixed for this condition.,0.3,N/A,No communication stage.,1,N/A,No human UI.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,1,"For effective transfer ratios, punishment via payoff transfer is present.",1,0.5,Transfer strength s is kept at 0.5 in Figure 6 ('Other parameter settings: ... s=0.5').,1,0.5,Each transferred agent loses fraction s of its payoff regardless of r.,0.7,1,Bonus fund from transferred payoffs is redistributed to accepted players.,1,0,Accepted players incur no cost.,0.9,N/R,No explicit numeric reward multiplier reported.,0.4,1,Cooperator donation remains c_i=1 in all r conditions.,0.8,N/R,"Figure 6 shows curves of ρ vs r; specific numeric ρ at each r for each (a,b) is not tabulated.",0.3,N/R,Only fraction of cooperators ρ is depicted.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported even though r varies.,0.3,N/R,No numeric payoffs reported as function of r.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Shows that reputation-based payoff transfer allows cooperation to emerge at much smaller r than in traditional PGG. At (a=0.5,b=0.5) global cooperation is reached already at r≈2.7, while baseline (0,0) requires r≈4.6 to reach near ρ=1.",Summarized from narrative description of Figure 6.,1
10.1140_epjb_e2020-100618-x.md,Simulation – Transfer strength variation at fixed b=0.4,Figure 7(a) studies cooperation for different transfer strengths s at b fixed to 0.4 and varying a.,1,"Transfer strength s ∈ {0.4,0.5,0.6,0.7}; transfer parameter b=0.4; a varied including small values (e.g., a=0.1); r=3.","Caption: 'effect of a set of transfer strengths (s=0.4,0.5,0.6,0.7)... (a) ... small reward ratio (a=0.1) when b is fixed at 0.4'.",1,False,All results are from simulations.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Authors explicitly mention 'simulations'.,1,False,"Outcomes derived numerically, not analytically.",1,10000,L=100 lattice used throughout.,1,10000,Same general simulation protocol (≥10000 steps).,0.7,1,Contribution c_i=1 or 0.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0.6,Synergy factor r=3; group size=5 → MPCR=3/5.,1,N/A,No communication.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No human identities in punishment.,1,N/A,No human identities in reward.,1,N/A,Not displayed to agents.,1,1,Low-ranked transferred players lose a fraction s of payoff.,1,N/R,"s varies across {0.4,0.5,0.6,0.7}; a single numeric cost cannot represent all conditions in this panel.",0.3,N/R,Magnitude of punishment per unit payoff varies with s; no single value.,0.3,1,Transferred payoffs redistributed to accepted players.,1,0,Accepted players do not pay cost; s only affects transferred players.,0.9,N/R,"Reward per accepted player depends on s and a,b each run.",0.3,1,Cooperative contribution remains c_i=1.,0.8,N/R,Figure 7 portrays ρ qualitatively versus transfer ratios and s; no exact numeric values reported for b=0.4 case.,0.3,N/R,Only fraction of cooperators shown graphically.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency index reported.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically given.,0.3,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,No human,Numerical simulation.,1,"Panel (a) in Figure 7 shows that with strong transfer strength (s=0.7) and small reward ratio a=0.1 at b=0.4, ρ can reach a high level; as s decreases, fewer transfer ratios maintain cooperation.",Summarized from Figure 7(a) caption and discussion.,1
