10.1140_epjb_e2020-100618-x.md,Simulation – Transfer strength variation at fixed b=0.6,Figure 7(c) considers varying s when b is set to 0.6.,1,"Transfer strength s ∈ {0.4,0.5,0.6,0.7}; transfer parameter b=0.6; a varied; r=3.",Caption: '(c) The value of b is set to be 0.6. The change of s has no influence on the cooperation when a is 0.4.',1,False,Pure simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Results generated by numerical simulations.,1,False,No analytical derivations.,1,10000,L=100 lattice.,1,10000,Same simulation horizon.,0.7,1,Binary cooperation (1) vs defection (0).,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0.6,r=3; group size 5 → MPCR=3/5.,1,N/A,No communication channels.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No human display.,1,1,Reputation-based payoff transfer mechanism includes punishment.,1,N/R,s varies; cannot assign a single numeric cost for all runs.,0.3,N/R,Punishment magnitude changes with s.,0.3,1,Accepted players receive part of transferred payoff pool.,1,0,Reward is costless to recipients.,0.9,N/R,"Reward amounts depend on s and a,b; no single factor given.",0.3,1,Cooperator contribution is still 1.,0.8,N/R,"They state qualitatively that 'the change of s has no influence on the cooperation when a is 0.4', but no exact ρ values reported.",0.3,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,0.3,N/R,No reported efficiency metrics.,0.3,N/R,No detailed payoff outcomes provided.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,All results from numerical simulation.,1,"Panel (c) in Figure 7 indicates that for b=0.6 and a=0.4, changing s between 0.4 and 0.7 does not significantly alter the cooperation level; cooperation remains high for a>0.3 under effective transfer ratios.",Summarized from Figure 7(c) caption and related text about transfer strength effects.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2022.128302.md,Simulation – baseline evolutionary PGG with prior agreement and posterior compensation on lattice,"Paper describes a single numerical evolutionary game model on an LxL lattice; all reported results (phase diagrams, cross sections, threshold analysis) vary parameters within this one simulation framework.",0.98,"Posterior compensation cost c3 ∈ [0,1]; normalized amplification factor φ ∈ [0,1]; effective agreement threshold T (e.g., 2–9); other fixed parameters: group size G=9, contribution cost c=1, agreement setup cost c2=0.1, selection intensity β=10, lattice size L=300.","Throughout results, authors systematically vary c3, φ, and T while holding other parameters fixed; no separate experimental system is introduced.",0.97,False,Study is an evolutionary game simulation on a lattice with no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; only numerical simulations of a theoretical model.,1,False,Not a lab study; purely computational.,1,True,"Model implemented as Monte Carlo simulations on a spatial lattice (MCS steps, averaging over runs).",1,True,"Paper mentions analytical results in addition to simulations, indicating some formal analysis, but main reported outcomes are from simulations.",0.7,90000,"Population is a lattice of size L×L with L=300, so 300×300=90000 players.",1,10000,They state relaxation time from 10000 to 15000 MCS and snapshot examples at MCS=10000; take 10000 MCS as a representative run length.,0.7,1,"Each player either cooperates by paying cost c=1 or defects by paying nothing; binary action not all-or-nothing on endowment proportion continuum, but within model choices only 0 or full c, which aligns with all-or-nothing contribution.",0.8,N/A,No explicit endowment account structure; contributions are modeled as costs in payoff function in a simulation without human decision screens.,1,1,"Each cooperator contributes c=1 and each group participant receives φ·1 regardless of number of cooperators, so marginal per capita return of a cooperative act is 1 to each member (unusual PGG variant); φ itself is not MPCR but the per-capita gain from one cooperator is 1.",0.4,N/A,No human participants or communication; agent strategies are predefined.,1,N/A,Simulation agents do not see screens or summaries; they only observe neighbor payoffs implicitly via imitation rule.,1,N/A,"Posterior compensation is modeled in payoffs, not as a visible punishment with identities in a human interface.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism separate from payoff structure and compensation.,1,N/A,Agents are not informed of number of simulation steps; update rule has no such parameter.,1,1,Posterior compensation c3 is effectively a punishment on defectors in an effective agreement (they pay cost c3 per group).,1,N/R,"Model specifies a lump-sum compensation cost c3 to defectors and per-cooperator benefit c3·ND/NC, not a per-unit punishment technology in the sense of cost per punishment point; cannot map cleanly to a single cost parameter.",0.2,N/R,Compensation/punishment is defined as c3·ND/NC benefit to each cooperator and -c3 to each defector within effective agreements; not decomposed as a linear technology per token.,0.2,0,No separate prosocial reward mechanism; only posterior compensation from defectors to cooperators.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding institution modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding institution modeled.,1,N/R,Players do not have a stated token endowment per round; only contribution cost c=1 is given.,0.3,N/R,"They report cooperation abundance (fraction of individuals with cooperative components in their strategy), but not normalized contribution per round relative to an endowment.",0.4,N/R,"Results show fractions of cooperating strategies and effective agreements, not per-player contribution amounts.",0.4,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to social optimum is reported; only cooperation and agreement frequencies are shown.,0.2,N/R,"Payoff functions are defined, but numerical group payoff outcomes are not tabulated.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is an evolutionary game simulation on a lattice without human data.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a 300×300 lattice with Moore neighborhood (G=9 groups per player). Each player has strategy S=(Sp, SA, Sin, Sout) with binary components indicating whether to propose agreements, accept agreements, cooperate inside agreements, and cooperate outside agreements. Prior agreements become effective if number of proposers/acceptors in group ≥ threshold T. If an agreement is effective, all in-agreement players are required to cooperate; defectors in effective agreements pay compensation cost c3, which is shared among cooperators. Contribution cost is c=1. Normalized amplification factor φ=F/G with 1<F<G and 0<φ<1; each participant obtains φ·1 per cooperator as modeled. Strategy update via synchronous imitation using Fermi rule with β=10. Simulations run 10000–15000 MCS, averaging last 1000 steps and over 30 independent runs.",Summarized from model description and simulation procedure in Sections 2 and 3.,0.98
10.1038_s41598-023-41519-1.md,Numerical simulation – baseline delayed eco‑evolutionary model (various parameter sets),"The paper presents only mathematical analysis and numerical simulations of a delayed eco‑evolutionary Prisoner’s Dilemma with cooperators, punishers, defectors, and free space. There are many parameter sweeps but no distinct named experimental conditions with human subjects.",0.7,"Model parameters of delayed replicator-like system: temptation to defect β, punishment cost δ, free-space benefit parameters σ1, σ2, σ3, mortality rate ξ, time delay τ, initial fractions (x0, y0, z0, w0).","Across the paper, only these parameters and initial conditions are varied (e.g., Fig. 1 uses specific (ξ, β, δ, σ1, σ2, σ3, τ); Figs. 3–5 are parameter sweeps in β, τ, etc.). No exogenous treatments beyond parameter changes exist.",0.95,False,The work is explicitly an eco‑evolutionary model analyzed by analytical calculations and numerical simulations; no human participants or empirical data are used.,1.0,False,There is no controlled empirical experiment; only simulated dynamical systems and analytical derivations are reported.,1.0,False,No laboratory or physical experimental setting; all results are theoretical/simulated.,1.0,True,"The paper repeatedly refers to numerical simulations of system (6), bifurcation diagrams, Lyapunov exponents, etc., with no human subjects.",1.0,True,Extensive analytical derivations of equilibria and Hopf bifurcation conditions are provided without stochastic or empirical components.,1.0,4,"The model has four strategic types/compartments: cooperators (C), punishers (P), defectors (D), and free space (F). In a game-theoretic sense these are the ‘players’/strategies present.",0.9,1,It is a continuous-time replicator-like model with delay rather than a repeated discrete-round game; mapping to ‘rounds’ is not meaningful. Set to 1 as a single continuous interaction episode.,0.4,1,"Agents do not choose discrete contribution levels; they adopt pure strategies C, P, D or occupy free space. There is no all‑or‑nothing discrete contribution choice as in a public goods game; treat as non‑all‑or‑nothing (1).",0.3,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment/contribution structure; continuous population fractions and payoffs are modeled instead.,1.0,N/R,"The game is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoff parameters T=β, R=1, P=S=0; no marginal per capita return of a public good is defined.",1.0,N/A,No human interaction or communication; purely mathematical model.,1.0,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human players; only state variables in a simulation.,1.0,N/A,Punishment is encoded in payoff matrix; there are no individual identities or displays.,1.0,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond basic payoffs; no identities.,1.0,N/A,No human interface and no discrete rounds.,1.0,1,A punisher strategy P is added; when interacting with D it pays cost δ and reduces D’s payoff by δ.,1.0,N/R,"δ>0 is introduced as a punishment-related parameter, but the exact mapping to ‘per unit of punishment’ cost versus impact is not phrased in experimental cost/impact units as required by this schema.",0.4,N/R,"While δ appears in payoffs (P vs D: P gets -δ, D gets β-δ), the paper does not express it as a linear technology of “punishment tokens to payoff reduction” in an experimental sense.",0.4,0,There is no separate costly rewarding mechanism; only basic game payoffs and punishment are modeled.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1.0,N/R,"The model uses payoffs R, S, T, P and population fractions; no per-round endowment is defined.",1.0,N/R,No contribution decisions or rates are defined; variables are population fractions of strategies and total population.,1.0,N/R,There is no notion of contribution amounts; only evolutionary strategy frequencies and payoffs.,1.0,N/R,"The paper studies coexistence, oscillations, chaos etc., but does not compute efficiency as total payoff relative to a benchmark.",1.0,N/R,Only individual fitness functions and dynamical states are described; aggregate payoffs are not tabulated as an outcome variable.,1.0,N/A,No human or animal participants; only theoretical agents.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,All results come from a mathematical eco‑evolutionary model and its numerical simulations; no environment where humans interact.,1.0,"Continuous-time delayed eco‑evolutionary Prisoner’s Dilemma with strategies C, P, D, and ecological free space F; weak PD payoffs T=β>1, R=1, P=S=0; punishers incur and impose costs δ when facing defectors; additional payoffs from free space via σ1, σ2, σ3; population dynamics given by delayed replicator-like ODEs with mortality ξ and time delay τ in fitness evaluation. Analyses include steady states, Hopf bifurcations, cyclic dominance, period-doubling, chaos, and parameter sweeps.",Summarized from the Model and Numerical Results sections and the listed payoff matrices and dynamical system (Eq. 6).,1.0
10.1140_epjb_s10051-021-00212-w.md,PDG – δ = 0 (no punishment),"Condition where punishment fine δ is set to 0 and the model is equivalent to the traditional Prisoner's Dilemma Game (PDG) without payoff punishment, as described in Sect. 3.1 and 3.3.",0.95,Game type = PDG; punishment fine δ = 0; temptation to defect b varies; selection intensity K = 0.1,"Game type (PDG), δ and its role, and K are specified. For Fig. 2(a) and 3(a), b and δ are varied; here δ is fixed to 0.",0.9,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; purely computational simulation.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only simulations.,1,True,Explicitly described as Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice.,1,False,No closed-form or purely analytical model; results come from numerical simulations.,1,2500,Square lattice of size 50 × 50 implies 2500 players.,1,10000,Total simulation steps set to 1 × 10^4 full Monte Carlo steps.,1,1,"Binary strategies C or D, but contribution in PD/SD is not framed as contributing a continuous endowment; however, by the instruction any-amount vs all-or-nothing: here players choose between two discrete strategies, closer to all-or-nothing; yet to avoid overinterpretation and given this is not a PGG, mark as 1 (any amount) is inappropriate. Set to 0 for all-or-nothing. However instructions say 1=any amount, 0=only all-or-nothing. Here only two discrete options C or D, so 0.",0.7,N/A,No human endowment or explicit contribution pool in a two-player matrix game simulation.,1,N/R,MPCR defined for public goods games; not reported or applicable for pairwise PD.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication is irrelevant.,1,0,Agents only use local payoffs and reputations; no mention of global summaries being displayed.,0.9,0,No identities or displays; punishment is algorithmic and local.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Simulated agents are not described as knowing total number of MCS steps.,0.8,0,"For δ = 0, the model degenerates to the traditional PDG or SDG and no punishment is applied.",1,N/A,Punishment is disabled when δ = 0.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism active.,1,0,Only punishment is modeled; no rewards are introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Pairwise payoff matrix game; no per-round endowment is defined.,1,N/R,Outcomes are reported as fraction of cooperators (ρ_c) rather than normalized contributions from an endowment.,1,N/R,No token-based contributions; only binary strategies.,1,N/R,Average payoffs and fitness are shown qualitatively but efficiency (ratio to maximum) is not reported.,0.8,N/R,Only average payoffs of cooperators and defectors are graphed; no aggregate group payoff is given.,0.8,N/A,No human participants; lattice agents in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire study is a Monte Carlo simulation on graphs.,1,"Spatial Prisoner's Dilemma on a 50×50 square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; each player interacts with four nearest neighbors; payoff matrix M_PDG = [[1,0],[b,0]] with 1 < b < 2; initial fraction of cooperators = 0.5; reputation R_x(t) increases by 1 when cooperating and is initialized to 1 for cooperators and 0 for defectors; selection dynamics via Fermi function with K = 0.1; 20 independent runs used to average results; punishment mechanism disabled because δ=0.",Summarizes reported structural and dynamic details specific to this condition.,1
10.1140_epjb_s10051-021-00212-w.md,PDG – δ > 0 (payoff punishment enabled),"All PDG conditions where reputational payoff punishment is active (δ>0), including various δ values (e.g., 0.2, 0.3, 0.55, 0.8, 1) as discussed in Sects. 3.1–3.5.",0.9,"Game type = PDG; punishment fine δ > 0 (e.g., 0.2–1.0); temptation to defect b varies; selection intensity K = 0.1",PDG with punishment is distinguished from δ=0 traditional game; δ and b are the varied parameters in figures; K is fixed.,0.9,False,Numerical Monte Carlo simulation without human subjects.,1,False,Model-based simulation rather than empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab; purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly described as simulation on a square lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analysis; results from simulations.,1,2500,Square lattice of size 50 × 50.,1,10000,Total simulation steps set to 1 × 10^4 full MCS.,1,0,"Players choose between C and D only, a binary all-or-nothing choice regarding cooperation.",0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment or continuous contributions; pairwise matrix game.,1,N/R,MPCR is not defined for this pairwise PDG; not reported.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Agents use only local payoff/fitness for imitation; no summary statistics displayed.,0.9,0,No notion of identity display in simulation; punishment is local and rule-based.,1,0,No rewards in the model.,1,0,Agents are not described as informed about the horizon.,0.8,1,Equations 6 and 7 define payoff punishment when δ>0.,1,1,"Punishment fine δ is subtracted from the punished defector's payoff (cost to target per punishment event); normalized punishment intensity δ ∈ [0,1]. Set cost unit as δ normalized to 1 per unit of punishment since model does not define discrete tokens.",0.4,1,The fine applied to the punished defector is δ itself; neighbors each pay δ/k_x. Per unit cost to target is proportional to δ with ratio 1.,0.4,0,No rewarding mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,No token endowment specified; payoffs come directly from the payoff matrix and punishment rule.,1,N/R,"Outcome is measured in terms of fraction of cooperators ρ_c, not contribution relative to an endowment.",1,N/R,No contributions defined as amounts.,1,N/R,"Average payoff and fitness curves are discussed, but no normalized efficiency metric is reported.",0.8,N/R,Only average payoffs per strategy are provided qualitatively; no explicit total group payoff.,0.8,N/A,Simulation study.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulations with artificial agents.,1,"Spatial PDG on a 50×50 periodic square lattice; four nearest neighbors per player (von Neumann neighborhood). Payoff matrix M_PDG = [[1,0],[b,0]] with 1 < b < 2. Initial fraction of cooperators = 0.5, with initial reputation R=1 for cooperators and R=0 for defectors. Reputation R_x(t) increments by 1 upon cooperation, remains otherwise. Punishment rule: if R_x < average neighbor reputation and s_x = D, then punished player's fitness F_x = P_x − δ and each neighbor y incurs cost δ/k_x (F_y = P_y − δ/k_x). Strategy updating via Fermi rule with intensity of selection K = 0.1. Simulations run for 10^4 MCS with measurements over last 10^3 steps, averaged over up to 20 independent runs.",Direct summary of model description for PDG with punishment enabled.,1
