chanism.,1,8,Each member still has 8 months endowment each period even in ostracism phase; ostracised individuals simply stop participating.,1,N/R,Only raw months are reported.,0,3.76,"Average over groups 9–16 in Table 2, rounds 16–30: (3.28+3.34+3.72+3.78+3.79+4.12+3.83+4.21)/8 ≈ 3.76 months.",0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure (relative to social optimum payoffs) reported for ostracism treatment.,0,N/R,Mean payoffs per group or treatment not given numerically.,0,Ghana,Same Ghanaian fishing community and school-based subject pool.,1,Mean 18.5 years; teenagers and young adults,Same sample as other phases.,1,Mixed; about 34.0% female in treated groups,Table 3: Female = 0.340 for treated groups.,1,Senior Secondary School students (first to third year),All participants are students at Anyako Secondary School.,1,On site,Conducted in school halls on paper with instructors.,1,"Same CPR game as baseline but with added ostracism stage each period: after seeing individual and total months, each subject can vote (by ID) to exclude one peer or none; majority rule of at least 50% of group (e.g., 4 of 8) required; excluded members leave permanently and earn zero in remaining rounds; each exclusion imposes a fixed ECU cost per period on remaining members (e.g., 27 ECU when 1 ostracised, 56 when 2, etc.), deducted from their payoffs.",Summarizes ostracism rules and cost structure from Appendix B and main text.,1
10.1007_s00191-010-0204-2.md,RM1 – Analytical equilibrium analysis (Section 3),Paper analytically studies equilibria under the specific two-state reputation mechanism RM1 in Section 3.,1,Reputation mechanism fixed as RM1; discount factor δ varied; benefit of unilateral defection b and loss from being defected against c as parameters; population restricted to one Markov strategy at a time.,"Section 3 derives perfect and robust equilibria under RM1 as δ, b, c vary, holding the Markov strategy homogeneous in the population.",1,False,No human or biological subjects; purely theoretical game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it is an analytical model of strategies and equilibria.,1,False,No lab setting; entirely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported in this section; all results are analytical.,1,True,Equilibrium characterizations and propositions are derived via formal mathematics.,1,2,Prisoners’ Dilemma is modeled as pairwise interaction: “individuals are matched in pairs”.,1,0,"Indefinitely/sporadically repeated game with infinite expected number of interactions; not a fixed finite number of rounds, so coded as 0.",0.8,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D; not an all-or-nothing continuous contribution structure; for this schema, contribution amount choice beyond C/D is not defined, so we treat as non-all-or-nothing PGG equivalent.",0.6,N/A,"Model is a Prisoners’ Dilemma with fixed payoffs, not a contribution game with an endowment split.",1,N/R,"No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; only PD payoffs b, 1, 0, -c.",1,0,No communication between individuals is modeled beyond reputation mechanism; choices depend only on states.,1,0,Agents observe only opponent’s reputation state; no summary of others’ earnings or punishment histories is modeled.,1,0,No notion of individual identity or revealed punisher identity; only states G/B are public.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identity; only reputation transitions.,1,0,Agents face an infinite horizon with discounting; no explicit feedback about remaining rounds is modeled.,1,1,Playing D against Bad opponents is interpreted as punishment and affects future reputation; reputation loss functions as punishment technology.,1,N/R,"Model specifies PD material payoffs (b, -c, etc.) but does not isolate a per-unit cost of punishment tokens.",0.6,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment investment to reduction in others’ payoffs beyond the base PD payoff structure.,0.6,0,Mechanism is based on punishment and penitence via reputations; no costly rewarding action is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,"Model normalizes payoffs to 1, 0, -c, b but does not specify per-round endowments.",0.7,N/R,"Outcomes are expressed as payoffs and cooperation frequencies, not normalized contribution fractions of an endowment.",0.7,N/R,Only binary action C vs D; no token amount is specified.,0.9,N/R,"Long-run population payoff is characterized (e.g., 1/(1−δ)) but not explicitly normalized relative to a defined maximum in the sense of this schema.",0.6,N/R,They derive long-run per-individual payoffs; group totals are not separately reported as raw amounts.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model.,1,No human,Purely analytical reputation and game-theoretic model without empirical data.,1,"Two-action Prisoners’ Dilemma with payoffs normalized: mutual cooperation yields 1 each, mutual defection yields 0 each, unilateral defector gets b>1 and cooperator gets −c<0. Infinite-horizon matching with discount factor δ; reputation states Good/Bad updated by RM1; Markov strategies over four reputation-pair states.",Summarizes key structural details specified in Sections 1–3 and Table 1 defining RM1 and strategy space.,1
10.1007_s00191-010-0204-2.md,RM2 – Analytical equilibrium analysis (Section 3),Section 3 defines the two-state reputation mechanism RM2 (Table 2) and derives its robust perfect equilibrium properties.,1,Reputation mechanism fixed as RM2; discount factor δ varied; parameters b and c as in canonical PD; homogeneous Markov strategy CCDC in the population.,"For RM2, the paper studies the condition on δ under which the Markov strategy CCDC is a robust perfect equilibrium with efficient payoff.",1,False,No actual participants; conceptual population of strategies.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with observations; purely theoretical.,1,False,No lab or physical environment.,1,False,Equilibrium results are derived analytically without numerical simulation for this section.,1,True,Characterization of equilibria and payoffs with RM2 is via algebra and inequalities on δ.,1,2,Same pairwise matching structure as for RM1.,1,0,Infinite expected number of interactions (sporadically repeated PD with discounting).,0.8,1,"Action set is {C,D}, not continuous contributions.",0.6,N/A,Model is not framed as a contribution from an endowment.,1,N/R,"No MPCR described; base game is PD, not linear public good.",1,0,No communication channel is modeled.,1,0,Agents condition only on partner’s reputation and own state; no payoff summary displays.,1,0,No personal identities; only states G/B are known.,1,0,No dedicated reward mechanism with identity.,1,0,Infinite horizon model; no round count shown.,1,1,Defecting against Bad types is interpreted as punishment and is treated differently in the reputation mechanism.,1,N/R,Per-unit cost of punishing is not separately parameterized beyond the PD payoff parameters.,0.6,N/R,No explicit token-based reduction technology; only action-payoff mapping.,0.6,0,No costly prosocial rewarding action in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,"Only normalized PD payoffs specified, not endowments.",0.7,N/R,Outcomes described as long-run payoffs and proportion of Good reputations; not as contribution rates.,0.7,N/R,No numerical token contributions reported.,0.9,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (efficient robust equilibria with payoff 1/(1−δ)) but not normalized as a 0–1 ratio according to a defined maximum in the schema.,0.6,N/R,Paper reports long-run per-individual payoff 1/(1−δ); does not give aggregate group payoff as a separate number.,0.7,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,Analytical equilibrium study only.,1,"Two-action PD with parameters b>1 and c>0; RM2 differs from RM1 by allowing reputation improvement (Bad to Good) when both players are Bad and the action is C. Under RM2, strategy CCDC is a robust perfect Nash equilibrium for δ>max{b−1,c}/(1+c) with long-run per-player payoff 1/(1−δ) and all players Good in steady state.",Summarizes the defining features of RM2 and its equilibrium outcome as reported in Section 3 and Table 2.,1
10.1007_s00191-010-0204-2.md,RM3 – Analytical equilibrium analysis (Section 3),Section 3 introduces RM3 (Table 2) and analyzes robust perfect equilibrium under this mechanism.,1,Reputation mechanism fixed as RM3; discount factor δ varied; PD parameters b and c; homogeneous strategy CDDC in population.,"For RM3, the paper identifies CDDC as robust perfect equilibrium and gives the δ condition; b and c are payoff parameters.",1,False,No observed human behavior; theoretical constructs only.,1,False,Not an experiment; purely analytic derivation.,1,False,No lab or experimental site.,1,False,No simulation runs reported for RM3; closed-form inequalities only.,1,True,"Uses analytic methods for equilibria with RM3, including condition δ>(b−1)/b.",1,2,Same pairwise PD as in other mechanisms.,1,0,Indefinite horizon with discounting.,0.8,1,Binary actions C/D; no graded contribution choice.,0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game with an initial endowment.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier defined.,1,0,Model has no communication variable.,1,0,No feedback except reputation states.,1,0,No personal identifiers modeled.,1,0,No explicit rewards or identities.,1,0,Infinite horizon; no round information shown.,1,1,D against Bad players functions as punishment in the reputation framework.,1,N/R,Model does not distinguish separate resource cost of punishment tokens.,0.6,N/R,No explicit parameter mapping punishment expenditure to damage; only action-payoff mapping.,0.6,0,Reputation improvement from C is not modeled as a costly reward paid by others.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Only normalized PD outcomes specified.,0.7,N/R,"Outcomes are characterized by payoffs and reputation distributions, not contribution ratios.",0.7,N/R,No quantity of contribution tokens specified beyond binary C/D.,0.9,N/R,They state that equilibrium is efficient with payoff 1/(1−δ) but do not express it as efficiency ratio 0–1 as per schema.,0.6,N/R,"Only per-capita long-run payoff stated, not group sums.",0.7,N/A,Theoretical model without subjects.,1,N/A,Theoretical model without subjects.,1,N/A,Theoretical model without subjects.,1,N/A,Theoretical model without subjects.,1,No human,Analytical model of strategies and reputations only.,1,"RM3 differs from RM2 by eliminating penitence when a Bad player meets a Good player: reputation remains Bad regardless of C or D in that row. Under RM3, strategy CDDC is a robust perfect Nash equilibrium for δ>(b−1)/b, with long-run payoff 1/(1−δ) and all players Good in the stable steady state.",Summarizes RM3 definition and the main equilibrium property as described in Section 3 and Table 2.,1
10.1007_s00191-010-0204-2.md,RM1 – Replicator dynamics (Section 4),Section 4 studies replicator dynamics under RM1 for a restricted set of Markov strategies.,1,"Reputation mechanism RM1; population composition over four Markov strategies {DD, CD, DC, CC} evolves via replicator dynamics; discount factor δ varied with δ>δ*; small drift ε and adjustment speed κ_t as parameters.","Section 4 specifies RM1, restricts to strategies {DDDD, CDDD, DCDD, CCDD}, defines state variables x_m, ψ_m, and analyzes replicator dynamics as δ varies.",1,False,No experimental or observational data; a dynamical systems model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; purely theoretical dynamic analysis.,1,False,No lab; equations describe evolution of strategy frequencies.,1,False,"The section gives analytical propositions about the dynamics; no numeric simulations, figures, or parameter sweeps are reported.",0.9,True,Replicator dynamics and equilibrium properties are derived through algebraic and limit arguments.,1,2,Matching remains pairwise between individuals for the PD stage game.,1,0,Within each period there are infinite potential matching rounds; not a fixed finite number.,0.9,1,Action space is binary (C or D) rather than continuous contribution levels.,0.6,N/A,No endowment allocation; just action choices in PD.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier defined.,1,0,Dynamics depend on payoffs and reputations; communication is not modeled.,1,0,Agents condition only on reputations; no explicit feedback about others’ incomes is specified.,1,0,There are no personal identifiers in the dynamic model; only types and reputations.,1,0,No distinct reward mechanism with known rewarder identity.,1,0,Infinite-horizon interaction in each period; players do not observe a countdown.,1,1,"Under RM1, defecting against Bad opponents is treated as punishment without damaging one’s own reputation; punishment thus exists via action D against B.",1,N/R,The cost of punishing is embedded in PD payoffs but not specified as a separate marginal cost parameter.,0.6,N/R,No explicit mapping of punishment units to opponent payoff loss; model focuses on action payoffs and reputation.,0.6,0,"No separate rewarding action; cooperation may improve reputation, but that is not modeled as costly reward paid by others.",1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/R,Payoffs are normalized PD payoffs with no separate endowment parameter.,0.7,N/R,"Outcomes are cooperation frequencies and type proportions, not normalized contributions of an endowment.",0.7,N/R,Binary C/D choices; no token-level contributions reported.,0.9,N/R,"They show that the dynamic converges to near-universal cooperation (hence efficient), but do not quantify efficiency as a numeric 0–1 ratio in the paper.",0.6,N/R,Analysis focuses on per-strategy payoffs and growth; no explicit group-payoff figure reported.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Replicator dynamics model over strategy types in an idealized population.,1,"Population of strategies M* = {DD, CD, DC, CC} under RM1. Time divided into periods; within each period, infinite expected matches with discount δ; after each match, agents exit with probability 1−δ. Reputations (Good/Bad) update under RM1, and strategy frequencies evolve via discrete-time replicator dynamics with growth rate π_m(t) based on normalized accumulated payoffs and small drift ε. Proposition 4: for sufficiently high δ, from any internal initial distribution, the dynamic converges to near-homogeneous CC (and CD with Good reputations), leading to approximately 100(1−ε)% cooperation.",Condenses the structural assumptions and main dynamic result from Section 4.,1
10.1007_s00191-010-0204-2.md,RM1 – Clan-based reputation mechanisms (Section 5),Section 5 extends the RM1 model to multiple clans with intra-clan reputation effects and inter-clan anonymous interactions.,1,Reputation mechanism RM1; matching parameter r_k (probability of intra-clan match) varied; discount factor δ varied; PD parameters b and c; homogeneous clan using CCDD.,Proposition 5 derives conditions on δ and r_k under which CCDD is a perfect Nash equilibrium and gives associated payoffs.,1,False,Analysis is theoretical; no observed clans or human data.,1,False,No experimental manipulation or measurements; only analytic characterization.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab.,1,False,Section presents closed-form expressions and conditions; no computational simulations.,0.9,True,Proposition 5 and surrounding text are analytic derivations involving r_k and δ.,1,2,Matching remains pairwise; each match is between two individuals (possibly from same clan).,1,0,Same sporadically repeated PD with infinite expected matches via discounting.,0.8,1,Two-action PD (C or D).,0.6,N/A,No contribution-from-endowment framing.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier.,1,0,No communication modeled; clans differ by reputation mechanism only.,1,0,Only intra-clan reputations are tracked; no summary of others’ payoffs displayed.,1,0,Players see reputations and clan membership implicitly via r_k; individual identities are not modeled.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Infinite horizon game; no announcement of remaining rounds.,1,1,"As with RM1, defecting against Bad clan members is punishment; against outsiders, D is dominant but reputations unaffected.",1,N/R,No explicit cost-per-unit for punishment; embedded in PD payoffs and matching structure.,0.6,N/R,No detailed punishment technology beyond action-payoff mapping.,0.6,0,No separate rewarding action described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,Model describes normalized PD payoffs; no separate endowment.,0.7,N/R,"Outcomes given as long-run payoffs r_k/(1−δ) and equilibrium conditions, not contributions.",0.7,N/R,Actions are C or D only.,0.9,N/R,Equilibrium payoffs are characterized but efficiency is not normalized as 0–1 relative to a maximum per this schema.,0.6,N/R,They give per-member long-run payoff r_k/(1−δ); group totals for finite clan size are not specified.,0.7,N/A,No empirical subjects or real clans.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model of clans and matching probabilities.,1,"Extends RM1 to a setting with clans. Reputation changes only from within-clan interactions; interactions outside one’s clan do not affect reputation and D is a weakly dominant action against outsiders. With intra-clan match probability r_k, CCDD is a perfect Nash equilibrium when δ > max{b−1,c} / [r_k(1+c) + (1−r_k)max{b−1,c}], and the long-run per-member payoff for a clan of cooperators is r_k/(1−δ). Larger r_k (more within-clan interactions) facilitates cooperation at lower δ.","Summarizes new structural element (r_k and clans), equilibrium condition, and long-run payoff from Proposition 5.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2013.08.006.md,P – standard punishment (no redistribution),Paper labels this treatment as the standard punishment treatment P and contrasts it with the redistributive punishment treatment RP.,1,Punishment technology: standard costly punishment where each 0.25 E$ spent reduces a target’s earnings by 1 E$ and the deducted amount is destroyed (no redistribution).,Only difference across conditions is whether punishment losses are burned (P) or redistributed (RP); within this row we specify the P setting.,1,True,Human undergraduate subjects played a computerized public goods game.,1,True,Controlled laboratory VCM with random assignment to treatments and fixed groups.,1,True,Sessions conducted in a computer classroom at Brown University.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Although theoretical discussion is provided, main results are from experiments, not closed-form models.",1,4,Design states there are 4 members in each group.,1,20,Design specifies 20 periods in an experimental session.,1,1,Subjects choose a contribution from 0 to 10 E$; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each period subjects receive an endowment and decide how much to allocate; endowment starts in private account.,0.9,0.4,Payoff function and text state MPCR is 0.4 (1.6 total multiplier divided by 4).,1,0,Text notes decisions were made without communication.,1,1,"In punishment stage, each subject learns amounts each other put in group account and can punish them; implies feedback on others’ contributions and hence earnings components.",0.8,0,Subjects do not see who punished whom by how much; only net punishment is shown.,1,0,No rewards exist in P treatment.,1,1,Number of periods is finite and known; standard VCM implementations display remaining periods; inferred but not explicitly described on screen.,0.6,1,Second stage allows costly punishment of other group members.,1,0.25,Costs 0.25 E$ to reduce another’s earnings by 1 E$.,1,1,Each unit of punishment reduces target’s earnings by 1 E$; this is the punishment magnitude per 1 E$ of punishment variable in payoff function.,1,0,No mechanism to increase others’ earnings; only burning-type punishment.,1,N/A,No costly reward technology in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Payoff function specifies subject is endowed with 10 experimental dollars each period.,1,0.709,Average contribution per period is 7.09; endowment is 10; normalized rate = 7.09/10.,1,7.09,Table 1: Avg. contribution per period in P treatment is 7.09.,1,0.805,Social optimum is 16 E$ per person (if all contribute 10 and no punishment). Average earnings per period are 12.88; efficiency ≈ 12.88/16.,0.9,51.52,Average earning per period is 12.88 per subject; group has 4 members; 12.88*4 = 51.52.,0.95,United States,"Subjects recruited from Brown University in Providence, RI, USA.",1,N/R,Only described as drawn from general undergraduate student body; no precise age stats.,0.4,Slight majority female,Text: female subjects slightly outnumbered males; no exact percentages given.,0.6,"Undergraduate students at Brown University (mixed majors, economics slightly overrepresented)",Subjects drawn from general undergraduate student body; proportion of economics majors slightly exceeds campus average.,0.9,On site,Sessions run in a physical computer classroom with subjects seated at desks.,1,"Partners design voluntary contribution mechanism with punishment; four-person fixed groups; linear public good with multiplier 1.6; punishment stage with constraints: (i) cannot spend more on punishment than pre-punishment earnings, (ii) earnings floored at zero, (iii) cannot unilaterally spend enough to drive another’s earnings below zero; no communication.",Summarized from design section and footnotes describing structure and constraints.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2013.08.006.md,RP – redistributive punishment,Paper labels this treatment as redistributive punishment (RP) and contrasts it with P.,1,"Punishment technology: redistributive costly punishment where each 0.25 E$ spent reduces a target’s earnings by 1 E$, and the 1 E$ deducted must be transferred as a reward to one or both of the other group members.",Independent variable is whether punishment losses are destroyed (P) or redistributed (RP); here we specify the RP configuration.,1,True,Human undergraduate subjects in a VCM with punishment and reward-like redistribution.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment and fixed groups.,1,True,Conducted in Brown University computer classroom with on-site participants.,1,False,No simulation-only results reported.,1,False,Focus is experimental; theory is informal rather than formal analytical modeling.,1,4,Same design as P: groups of four.,1,20,RP treatment also played for 20 periods in fixed partner groups.,1,1,Contribution choice is any amount 0–10 E$; not binary.,1,0,Same endowment and decision structure as P: endowment starts privately and subjects decide contribution.,0.9,0.4,Payoff function for RP includes same public good term with MPCR 0.4.,1,0,Design explicitly states decisions were made without communication.,1,1,Subjects observe each other’s contributions and see net rewards/punishments affecting earnings.,0.8,0,"Subjects only see net change due to rewards and punishments, not who punished whom or by how much.",1,0,Redistributed amounts’ individual sources and destinations are not revealed; only net effect shown.,1,1,"Number of periods is finite and known; likely displayed similarly to P, though not explicitly described.",0.6,1,Second stage allows subjects to reduce others’ earnings at a cost to themselves.,1,0.25,Same cost structure as P: 0.25 E$ per 1 E$ reduction of target’s earnings.,1,1,Each unit of punishment reduces target’s earnings by 1 E$; same magnitude as P.,1,1,"By design, every dollar of punishment must be redistributed as a reward to other group members.",1,0,Redistributing the 1 E$ deducted from a punished subject to others involves no extra cost beyond the 0.25 E$ punishment cost; marginal cost per 1 E$ reward is therefore 0.,0.95,1,Each 1 E$ taken from a punished subject is transferred 1:1 to another member as reward.,1,10,RP payoff function also begins with (10 - C_i); same 10 E$ endowment.,1,0.903,Average contribution is 9.03; endowment is 10; normalized contribution rate = 9.03/10.,1,9.03,Table 1: Avg. contribution per period in RP treatment is 9.03.,1,0.949,Social optimum earnings are 16 E$ per person. Average earnings per period are 15.18 including transfers; efficiency ≈ 15.18/16.,0.9,60.72,Average earning per period is 15.18 per subject; 4-member group: 15.18*4 = 60.72.,0.95,United States,Subjects were Brown University undergraduates in the U.S.,1,N/R,Only described as undergraduates; no more specific age statistics provided.,0.4,Slight majority female,Text states female subjects slightly outnumbered males.,0.6,"Undergraduate students at Brown University (mixed majors, economics slightly overrepresented)",Same subject pool description as P treatment.,0.9,On site,RP sessions also took place in the computer classroom with on-site participants.,1,"Partners design VCM with redistributive punishment; four-person fixed groups; linear public good with multiplier 1.6 and MPCR 0.4; second stage where each 0.25 E$ spent reduces a target’s earnings by 1 E$ and obliges the punisher to transfer that 1 E$ to one or both of the remaining group members; same punishment constraints as P; no communication; subjects see only net punishment/reward they receive, not its decomposition by source or type.","Summarized from RP payoff function, design text, and footnotes explaining redistribution and information structure.",1
