10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,Replicator dynamics – mini-trust game without reputation,Section 3 and 6 define the minimal trust game and analyze its evolutionary dynamics without any reputation or perturbation parameter.,1,"Game structure: mini-trust game; no reputation (μ = 0); strategies G1=(donate, return), G2=(no donate, return), G3=(no donate, no return), G4=(donate, no return). Parameters constrained by c < β < b = r·c.",Condition defined by absence of reputation and the specified strategy/payoff structure.,1,False,Paper explicitly states analysis is via replicator equation and evolutionary game dynamics; no human subjects described.,1,False,No randomized or controlled behavioral experiment; purely theoretical model.,1,False,No lab setting; mathematical analysis only.,1,False,"This section uses closed-form replicator dynamics, not numerical simulations.",0.9,True,Payoff matrices and replicator dynamics solved analytically.,1,2,Trust game described as two-person game with proposer and responder; symmetrized but still pairwise.,1,1,"Trust game specified as one-shot; evolutionary process is across generations, not repeated rounds for a pair.",0.9,1,Proposer chooses between two discrete actions (donate fixed amount c or not); no continuous choice of contribution level.,1,N/A,"Concept of default public vs private endowment not applicable in this stylized trust game with analytical agents, no explicit human endowment framing.",1,N/R,Multiplier r is given but group size is 2 and the mapping to standard MPCR definition is not explicitly made; they only state b = r·c.,0.4,N/A,No human interaction; analytical model.,1,0,No feedback or information displays; only payoff structure and replication.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this trust-game variant.,1,N/A,"Rewards are endogenous returns, not a separate stage with observable identity; and no human interface.",1,0,No notion of displayed round count in an analytical model.,1,0,Only donation and return actions; no fines or s