10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,Replicator dynamics – reduced ultimatum game without reputation,"Section 7 analyzes a simplified ultimatum game with two offer levels and two responder aspiration levels, without reputation.",1,"Game structure: reduced ultimatum game; no reputation (μ = 0); proposer offers h or l (0 < l < h < 1); responder accepts only h or both h and l; strategies G1=(h,h), G2=(l,h), G3=(l,l), G4=(h,l).",Condition defined by this baseline payoff matrix before adding reputation perturbation.,1,False,"Described as evolutionary dynamics of strategies, no experimental subjects.",1,False,Controlled lab experiment is not conducted; only theory.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment.,1,False,Analysis is symbolic using replicator dynamics in S4 simplex.,0.9,True,They derive conditions for fixed points and Nash equilibria analytically.,1,2,Ultimatum game is between proposer and responder.,1,1,"Stated as one-shot, two-person game; evolutionary updates occur across generations, not repeated rounds.",0.9,0,"Proposer chooses between two discrete fractions h and l, both between 0 and 1; not an all-or-nothing contribution decision.",1,N/A,"Trust/ultimatum framing, not a standard public good with initial endowment structure.",1,N/R,No linear public good multiplier is defined in ultimatum game representation.,1,N/A,"No communication modeled, and no human subjects.",1,0,No feedback interface; only payoffs used for replication.,1,N/A,"No explicit punishment stage; responder rejection is part of base payoff, not an added mechanism.",1,N/A,No explicit positive reward mechanism separate from the split.,1,0,No interface; analytical model.,1,1,"Responder can reject an offer, imposing a cost on proposer and themselves; interpreted as punishment in later sections.",0.9,N/R,"Resonder's opportunity cost equals foregone share, but not parameterized as cost per unit.",0.5,N/R,Size of proposer's loss per unit of responder cost is not represented in a simple linear technology parameter.,0.4,0,"Higher offer is baseline decision, not modeled as a separate reward stage for contributors.",0.8,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism beyond choosing h or l.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/R,Endowment is referenced qualitatively but not specified numerically.,0.3,N/R,"They track frequencies of strategies, not normalized contribution rates to a public good.",0.4,N/R,No explicit units for offers h and l are given.,0.3,N/R,They discuss payoff levels qualitatively but no explicit efficiency ratios.,0.4,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not fixed numerically.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Evolutionary game-theoretic model only.,1,Proposer chooses high offer h or low offer l; responder strategy is to accept only high offers (h) or accept both offers (l). Four symmetric composite strategies G1–G4; replicator dynamics yields no interior fixed points and long-run convergence either to fully selfish or Nash-equilibrium segments on edge G1–G4.,Summarizes reduced ultimatum game structure and dynamic outcome in this baseline condition.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,Replicator dynamics – reduced ultimatum game with reputation,Section 8 introduces reputation in the reduced ultimatum game via parameter μ that alters payoffs when fair proposers meet mild responders.,1,"Game structure as reduced ultimatum game; reputation probability μ > 0 that a fair (h,h) proposer knows a mild (h,l) responder accepts low offers and thus offers l instead of h. Strategies G1–G4 unchanged; μ perturbs one payoff entry.",Condition is defined by introduction of μ and resultant modified payoff matrix.,1,False,"Analysis via replicator dynamics, no subjects.",1,False,No experimental manipulation on people; parameter μ is theoretical.,1,False,Not a lab or field study.,1,False,They derive qualitative dynamics from analytical expressions; no numerical simulation outputs are shown here.,0.9,True,"They compute transformed parameters R,S,r,s as functions of μ and derive existence of line of fixed points.",1,2,Same 2-player ultimatum structure as baseline.,1,1,"Underlying game is one-shot; reputation reflects information from earlier interactions in population, not repeated rounds between same pair.",0.8,0,"Two possible offer fractions h, l; not an all-or-nothing public good contribution.",1,N/A,"Ultimatum framing, no initial public/private allocation.",1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,"Information about reputation is modeled probabilistically, not via chat; no participants.",1,0,"Reputation exposure is via parameter μ, not via observable earnings summaries.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment stage distinct from acceptance decisions.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanic.,1,0,No display concept.,1,1,Rejection of offers still functions as punishment analog; reputation simply changes when low offers are made.,0.9,N/R,Cost structure not restated in parametric punishment terms.,0.4,N/R,Punishment mapping not parameterized as β per unit cost.,0.4,0,No explicit reward stage.,0.8,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment.,0.3,N/R,"Outcome described in terms of convergence to fair or selfish strategies, not contribution rates.",0.5,N/R,"No numeric values for h, l.",0.3,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized vs maximal surplus.,0.4,N/R,Payoffs defined symbolically only.,0.4,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,No human,Purely analytical evolutionary dynamics.,1,Same reduced ultimatum game; reweighted payoff when G1 proposer meets G4 responder with probability μ that proposer knows responder’s history and offers l instead of h. Transforms replicator dynamics to a bistable system with attractors at pro-social (G1) and selfish (G3) states.,Describes how reputation modifies payoffs and dynamics.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,"Replicator dynamics – 2-player public good with punishment, no reputation, no errors",Sections 9–10 first analyze a 2-player public good (donation) game with conditional punishment but without reputation and without implementation errors.,1,"Game structure: 2-player public good with punishment; no reputation (μ = 0); no implementation errors (ε = 0). Stage 1: cooperate (donate b = r·c) or defect; Stage 2: punish defectors or not. Parameters with c < β (fine) and γ (punisher fee); strategies G1=(cooperate,punish), G2=(defect,punish), G3=(defect, no punish), G4=(cooperate, no punish).",Condition corresponds to the base payoff matrix before adding reputation (μ) or error (ε).,1,False,No human experiments; theoretical evolutionary game.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,"They use analytical calculation of R,S,r,s and invariant manifolds.",0.9,True,Replicator dynamics fully characterized analytically.,1,2,They explicitly consider N=2 case.,1,1,"Game is described as two-stage interaction; repetition occurs only across evolutionary time, not repeated rounds between same pair.",0.9,1,Each player either contributes fixed amount c or not; no intermediate continuous contributions.,1,N/A,"Analytical two-stage donation game, not framed with default endowment split.",1,N/R,They specify donation b = r·c but do not express MPCR explicitly as r/N in this two-player setting.,0.5,N/A,"No players, no chat.",1,0,"No information display aside from type knowledge introduced later; in baseline, no reputation.",1,N/A,"Who punishes whom is inherent in payoff structure; identities not a modeled dimension, and no human interface.",1,N/A,No rewards in this punishment-only game.,1,0,No displayed round counts.,1,1,Explicit second-stage punishment imposes fine β at personal cost γ.,1,N/R,Punisher cost γ is given but not normalized per fine unit (β).,0.7,N/R,Technology ratio β/γ not explicitly treated as such.,0.6,0,"Only punishment, no positive rewards; rewards are treated separately later.",1,N/A,No rewards in this game.,1,N/A,No rewards in this game.,1,N/R,"They never specify initial endowment in currency units, only costs c, β, γ, b.",0.3,N/R,They discuss convergence to defectors with no punishment but do not quantify contribution rates.,0.5,N/R,c left symbolic.,0.3,N/R,"Efficiency implications described qualitatively (asocial outcome), no explicit ratio.",0.4,N/R,Aggregate payoff not fixed numerically.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Theoretical evolutionary dynamics.,1,"Donation game equivalent to a 2-player public goods with punishment: donate b=r·c at cost c in stage 1; in stage 2 punish defectors at personal cost γ to impose fine β. Four composite strategies define whether to cooperate and/or punish; replicator dynamics without reputation leads eventually to asocial non-contributing, non-punishing state G3 despite existence of a Nash-equilibrium segment when c<β.","Summarizes structure and outcome in no-reputation, no-error case.",1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,Replicator dynamics – 2-player public good with punishment and reputation (no errors),Section 10 introduces reputation into the 2-player public good with punishment via parameter μ while still assuming no implementation errors.,1,"Same public good with punishment game as baseline; add reputation: with probability μ, cooperators (e1) defect against known nonpunishers (f2). Parameters: μ > 0, c < β, etc.; strategies G1–G4 unchanged.",This condition is defined by μ>0 altering payoffs for interactions between cooperators and nonpunishers.,1,False,No real subjects; analytical treatment.,1,False,Parameter μ is theoretical; no experimental manipulation.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,False,"They describe qualitative dynamics based on transformed R,S,r,s, not on numeric simulations.",0.9,True,"They compute sign pattern of R,S,r,s and deduce bistability between G1 and G3.",1,2,Same N=2 framework.,1,1,Game is still a two-stage donation-punishment interaction; repeated only across population history.,0.9,1,Binary cooperate/defect choice for contribution.,1,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation structure.,1,N/R,"As before, MPCR not specified.",0.5,N/A,Reputation probability μ abstracts from any explicit communication.,1,0,"Reputation is modeled as a probability of knowing types, not as displayed histories or earnings.",0.9,N/A,Identity of punishers isn’t a modeled variable; only type information matters probabilistically.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,0,No display; analytic model.,1,1,Fine β at cost γ remains in second stage.,1,N/R,γ is present but not normalized.,0.7,N/R,No explicit per-unit technology parameter.,0.6,0,Only punishment incentives here.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment defined.,0.3,N/R,"They state dynamics becomes bistable with attractors at full cooperation plus punishment (G1) and full defection without punishment (G3), but do not report numeric rates.",0.6,N/R,Donations remain parameter c; no numeric value.,0.3,N/R,No explicit computation of group efficiency under reputation.,0.4,N/R,Aggregate payoffs left symbolic.,0.4,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary game theory.,1,"Same four strategies G1–G4 in donation-punishment game, but cooperators sometimes exploit known nonpunishers with probability μ. This changes sign of r and breaks edge of fixed points, producing bistable dynamics with attractors at G1 (cooperate and punish) and G3 (defect and no punishment).",Captures how reputation shifts dynamics relative to baseline.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,"Replicator dynamics – 2-player public good with punishment, errors but no reputation","Section 11 first introduces implementation errors (ε) into the public good with punishment, still without reputation.",1,"Same 2-player donation-punishment game; implementation error ε > 0: players intending to donate (e1) fail with probability ε; no reputation (μ = 0). Strategies and punishment parameters β, γ as before.",Condition defined by payoff matrix including ε but excluding μ.,1,False,Still a theoretical replicator model.,1,False,No actual games run with people.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,False,Section provides symbolic payoff matrix and qualitative dynamics (case (b) of Fig. 2).,0.9,True,They inspect sign of r after introducing ε and deduce global convergence to G3.,1,2,Still in N=2 framework.,1,1,"Game per interaction is still two-stage; repetition only across history, not explicit repeated plays per pair.",0.9,1,Players intend to donate or not; donation is of fixed amount c.,1,N/A,No endowment allocation given.,1,N/R,MPCR not defined.,0.5,N/A,Theoretical model.,1,0,No explicit info sharing; errors occur but types not known.,0.9,N/A,Identity not modelled as separate dimension.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No interface.,1,1,Conditional punishment with fine β and fee γ remains.,1,N/R,γ appears symbolically but not normalized.,0.7,N/R,Ratio not treated as a technology parameter.,0.6,0,Only punishment incentives.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No endowment given.,0.3,N/R,"They show that asocial type G3 wins, implying zero cooperation asymptotically, but no numeric rate is reported.",0.6,N/R,c not numeric.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency not quantified explicitly.,0.4,N/R,No numeric group payoff.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical replicator dynamics with error parameter ε.,1,"Implementation errors mean intended donors fail with probability ε, which modifies R,S,r while leaving s unchanged. This makes punishment dominated and drives dynamics to asocial G3 despite possible punishment option.",Summarizes the role of ε in changing dynamics before adding reputation.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,"Replicator dynamics – 2-player public good with punishment, errors and reputation","Later in Section 11, reputation is added on top of implementation errors, yielding condition on γ for which r<0 and pro-social regime can emerge.",1,"2-player public good with punishment, with implementation errors (ε>0) and reputation effects; parameter μ = wε / (1 - w(1-ε)) for learning nonpunisher types, where w is continuation probability; condition for r<0 given by γ < [w(1-ε)b]/[1 - w(1-ε)].",Section specifies probability structure for μ and inequality involving γ under which reputation reverses dominance of punishment.,1,False,"Still theoretical, no subjects.",1,False,"w, ε, μ, γ are not experimentally assigned to participants.",1,False,No lab implementation.,1,False,They derive condition r<0 analytically.,0.9,True,Use algebra on payoff entries with ε and μ to sign r and draw consequences.,1,2,Same game with 2 players.,1,1,Stage game is two-stage; repeated with geometrically distributed number of rounds captures reputation formation but no explicit discrete round interface.,0.8,1,Donation decision is binary.,1,N/A,No explicit allocation of endowment.,1,N/R,MPCR unspecific.,0.5,N/A,"Reputation knowledge modeled probabilistically, not via chat among actual players.",1,0,"Players may learn about punishment behavior, but not via designed summary displays.",0.8,N/A,"Model only tracks whether a type is punisher or not, not identity revelation.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Number of past rounds k appears in probability calculations but not displayed to agents in a user interface.,1,1,Fine β and fee γ remain.,1,N/R,γ remains symbolic.,0.7,N/R,No explicit β per γ ratio reported.,0.6,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment specified.,0.3,N/R,"They only state that if punishment cost γ is not too high (inequality holds), r<0 and reputation can stabilize pro-social behavior; no numeric rates.",0.6,N/R,No concrete value for c.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency implications discussed qualitatively.,0.4,N/R,No numeric payoff reported.,0.4,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,"Evolutionary game with theoretical parameters ε, μ, w.",1,"Errors (ε) create occasional defections that allow learning about punishers vs nonpunishers. Reputation parameter μ = wε/[1-w(1-ε)] describes probability of having observed nonpunishment. Under condition γ < [w(1-ε)b]/[1 - w(1-ε)], r becomes negative so reputation restores bistable dynamics favoring possible pro-social outcomes.",Summarizes how errors and reputation interact to change sign of r and thereby dynamics.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,"Replicator dynamics – 2-player public good with rewards, no reputation",Section 12 first defines a public good game augmented with rewards but without reputation (μ = v = 0).,1,"2-player public good with rewards; no reputation (μ = 0, v = 0). Stage 1: contribute c or not; Stage 2: reward contributors at cost γ giving benefit β, with 0 < c < β and 0 < γ < b; strategies G1=(contribute,reward), G2=(no contribute,reward), G3=(no contribute,no reward), G4=(contribute,no reward).","Condition defined by payoff matrix with β,γ,c,b but without μ or v perturbation.",1,False,No participants.,1,False,Theoretical scenario only.,1,False,Not lab based.,1,False,"Uses same analytical machinery as trust game (matrix (5) and R,S,r,s).",0.9,True,They map game to mini-trust game and use replicator analysis from earlier sections.,1,2,Public good game with N=2 players explicitly.,1,1,One-shot two-stage interaction (contribution then reward).,0.9,1,Binary decision to contribute fixed amount c or not.,1,N/A,No endowment split discussed.,1,N/R,"They mention b as benefit to others from contribution, but not MPCR explicitly.",0.5,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,No explicit feedback; only payoffs drive replication.,1,N/A,No punishment here.,1,N/A,Rewarder types exist but identity display not modeled.,1,0,Theoretical single-shot game.,1,0,Only positive rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Second stage allows rewarding donor at cost γ and benefit β.,1,N/R,γ is given as total cost per reward but not decomposed per unit; schema asks per-unit; cannot infer without further structure.,0.7,N/R,Mapping from cost γ to benefit β not expressed as a linear per-unit technology.,0.6,N/R,Endowment not pseudo-specified.,0.3,N/R,"They state that dynamics is the same as in trust game, leading to eventual defection; no numeric rates.",0.6,N/R,c unspecified numerically.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,0.4,N/R,Only symbolic payoffs used.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical replicator dynamics only.,1,"Public good with rewards structurally equivalent to mini-trust game; rewarders pay cost γ to give benefit β to contributors. Replicator dynamics without reputation mirrors trust-game case, converging to asocial non-contributing, non-rewarding state G3.",Summarizes mapping and dynamics outcome.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,"Replicator dynamics – 2-player public good with rewards and two-sided reputation (μ small, v>0, r>0)","Section 12 then introduces reputation on both sides via μ and v; first discusses case of small μ so that r>0 and v>0, yielding cyclic dynamics.",1,"Same 2-player public good with rewards; add reputation: μ is probability a cooperator (e1) knows a nonrewarder (f2) and so defects; v is probability a defector (e2) knows a rewarder (f1) and so contributes. Condition considered here: μ small so that r = γ - bμ > 0, and v>0 so s=(γ-b)v<0; strategies G1–G4 unchanged.",This particular parameter regime produces cyclic orientation and interior fixed line with periodic/spiral orbits.,1,False,Still a theoretical evolutionary model.,1,False,No randomized control on participants.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,False,"They qualitatively describe dynamics (e.g., heteroclinic cycles) based on signs; no numerical paths printed, though conceptually akin to simulation.",0.8,True,"Classification of dynamics via saddle-like surfaces W_K and signs of R,S,r,s.",1,2,Same 2-player setting.,1,1,One-shot two-stage game; reputation arises from repeated interactions in population.,0.8,1,Binary contribution decision.,1,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation.,1,N/R,"As before, MPCR not explicitly defined.",0.5,N/A,"Type knowledge abstracted into μ and v, no chat in a lab environment.",1,0,"Reputation is modeled probabilistically, not via displayed histories.",0.8,N/A,No punishment in this rewards game.,1,N/A,"Rewarder vs nonrewarder types exist, but model abstracts from explicit identity revelation; only probabilistic type knowledge via v.",1,0,No interface; game is theoretical.,1,0,This condition concerns rewards only.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Rewarders pay γ to provide β benefit.,1,N/R,γ present but not normalized in per-unit terms.,0.7,N/R,Technology ratio not given explicitly.,0.6,N/R,No numeric endowment.,0.3,N/R,They describe complex dynamics with heteroclinic cycles and line of fixed points; no steady contribution rate.,0.5,N/R,c unspecified numerically.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency discussed only qualitatively via prevalence of strategies.,0.4,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff values.,0.4,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary theory only.,1,"With μ small so r>0 and v>0, edges of simplex are cyclically oriented, yielding a line of interior fixed points; on W1, orbits are periodic, while for K≠1 dynamics spirals towards or away from this line and spends long times near corners, with frequent shifts driven by random fluctuations.",Summarizes characteristic dynamic behavior in this parameter regime.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,"Replicator dynamics – 2-player public good with rewards and strong reputation against nonrewarders (μ large, r<0)","End of Section 12 briefly treats the case μ high enough to make r=γ - bμ negative, leading to global attraction of G1.",1,Same 2-player public good with rewards; reputation parameter μ sufficiently large that nonrewarder status is almost surely known and r = γ - bμ < 0; v possibly positive but not central here. This corresponds to situation where failure to reward becomes public knowledge.,"They assume high μ, specifically μ > β/b as indicative threshold in discussion, making r negative and altering dynamics.",0.9,False,Still theoretical analysis.,1,False,No real participants.,1,False,Not implemented in lab.,1,False,They infer dynamics analytically (all orbits converging to G1).,0.9,True,They identify sign-change in r and deduce that G1 becomes global attractor in W_K.,1,2,Same 2-player reward game.,1,1,One-shot two-stage structure; reputation arises from repeated history but not explicit in rounds.,0.8,1,Binary contribute/not choice.,1,N/A,No explicit endowment structure.,1,N/R,MPCR not provided.,0.5,N/A,"Reputation is public knowledge, but modeled as high μ, not as chat in human experiment.",1,0,Knowledge that someone does not reward is assumed but not modeled as a specific summary interface.,0.8,N/A,No punishment here.,1,N/A,"Being a nonrewarder is publicly known; still, identity display is not framed as a separate experimental variable.",0.8,0,No displayed round counts.,1,0,Rewards only.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Reward stage active.,1,N/R,γ is cost parameter but not normalized for our schema.,0.7,N/R,β/γ not expressed as such.,0.6,N/R,Endowment not specified.,0.3,N/R,"They note all orbits converge to pro-social state G1 (full contribution and rewarding), implying contribution rate 1 in limit, but not as a reported numeric DV.",0.7,N/R,No numeric value of c.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency increase implied but not quantified.,0.4,N/R,Aggregate group payoff not turned into a number.,0.4,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,No human,Purely theoretical claim about global attraction of social strategy.,1,"In this high-μ regime, failure to reward is almost always known, making it highly unlikely that nonrewarders receive donations. This flips sign of r, so replicator dynamics on each W_K converges to G1: a fully pro-social state where agents contribute and reward.",Describes effect of strong reputation against nonrewarders on dynamics.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,"Individual-based simulation – ultimatum game, anonymous treatment",Figure 8a (left) describes an individual-based simulation of the ultimatum game where players are anonymous.,0.95,"Information condition: anonymous. 1,000 simulated players with random strategies (p: offer size, q: aspiration level) each play 50 games against random partners per generation; no reputation or history-based offer adjustment.",Text for Fig. 8 specifies anonymity as distinguishing condition; no use of co-player history in offers.,0.95,False,"Simulated fictitious players and strategies, no human participants.",1,False,"Simulation study, not an experiment with human subjects.",1,False,Not executed in a physical lab with humans.,1,True,"Described explicitly as individual-based simulations with 1,000 fictitious players.",1,False,This part uses computational simulation instead of only closed-form derivations.,1,2,Ultimatum game is 2-player; pairings are random across population.,1,50,Text: each simulated player plays 50 games against randomly chosen co-players per generation.,1,0,"Offer size p is continuous (or at least multi-valued) between 0 and 1, not just all or nothing.",0.9,N/A,"Ultimatum offer, not standard public good endowment decision.",1,N/R,Ultimatum game has no public good multiplier.,1,N/A,Simulated anonymous agents cannot communicate; and no human interface.,1,0,Anonymous treatment; no reputation or display of others’ histories; only payoffs used internally for replication.,0.9,N/A,"Responder rejection is inherent; no explicit identity display, especially under anonymity.",1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,0,Simulated agents are algorithmic; no UI feature like round display.,1,1,Rejecting offers still functions as costly punishment in ultimatum game.,0.9,N/R,Cost to rejecting responder equals foregone payoff; not parameterized numerically in simulation description.,0.4,N/R,No explicit parameter mapping rejector cost to proposer loss reported.,0.4,0,No explicit reward stage beyond offer split.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Utilities described in percentages; actual endowment unit not given.,0.4,N/R,"Simulation tracks average (p,q) over time, not contribution rate in a public goods sense.",0.5,N/R,"They mention population averages of p,q but do not report numeric terminal values in the text.",0.5,N/R,"Plots show strategy evolution, not efficiency metric.",0.4,N/R,No aggregate payoffs reported.,0.4,N/A,Fictitious simulated players only.,1,N/A,Fictitious players only.,1,N/A,Fictitious players only.,1,N/A,Fictitious players only.,1,No human,Simulation on computer with artificial agents; no human data.,1,"Strategies are continuous pairs (p,q) for offer and aspiration; 1,000 agents initialized randomly, play 50 random-pair ultimatum games per generation; frequencies updated by replicator dynamics. Under anonymity, mean (p,q) first moves toward center, then drifts towards (0,0) along diagonal.",Summarizes structure and main qualitative result of anonymous simulation condition.,1
10.1007_978-3-540-85436-4_5.md,Individual-based simulation – ultimatum game with reputation-based offers,Figure 8b (right) describes a companion simulation where proposers condition their offers on responders’ past accepted offers.,0.95,"Information condition: reputation-based. Same 1,000 fictitious players and (p,q) strategies, each playing 50 games vs random partners; proposers now offer min(p, p_min_accepted_by_responder), i.e., they use their own p constrained by offers previously accepted by that co-player.","Text states: players know the past of their co-players, and offer the minimum of their own p and offers previously accepted by co-player.",0.95,False,"Still a computer simulation, no real participants.",1,False,"Simulated agents, not human experiment.",1,False,Not a lab study.,1,True,"Explicitly described as individual-based simulations with 1,000 fictitious players.",1,False,"Outcomes described graphically and qualitatively, not from closed-form analysis.",1,2,Ultimatum game with proposer and responder per interaction.,1,50,Same as anonymous treatment: each player plays 50 games per generation.,1,0,"Offers p are continuous fractions, not all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,Ultimatum setting.,1,N/R,No linear public good.,1,N/A,"Simulated knowledge of past offers, no explicit chat among subjects.",1,1,"Condition assumes players ""know the past of their co-players"" and base offers on previously accepted offers, akin to having access to a summary of that responder’s acceptance history.",0.8,N/A,No separate punishment stage with identity revelation; only history of acceptance is used.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,0,Simulation agents do not have UI; number of rounds per generation is fixed but not displayed.,1,1,Offer rejection still functions as punishment of proposers.,0.9,N/R,Costs are implicit in rejected offers; no numeric param reported.,0.4,N/R,No explicit technology parameter.,0.4,0,No explicit reward stage.,0.9,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment not given numerically.,0.4,N/R,"Simulation reports evolution of offers p and thresholds q, not contribution rates as fraction of endowment.",0.5,N/R,"They qualitatively describe path: average p,q creep up to slightly below 50%; numeric values not given.",0.5,N/R,"Efficiency not explicitly calculated; only directional change in p,q.",0.4,N/R,No aggregate payoffs reported.,0.4,N/A,Fictitious simulated players.,1,N/A,Fictitious simulated players.,1,N/A,Fictitious simulated players.,1,N/A,Fictitious simulated players.,1,No human,Fully computerized simulation with artificial agents.,1,"Same 1,000 agents with (p,q) strategies and 50 random games per generation; offers now depend on individual reputations (history of accepted offers). Evolution begins similarly to anonymous case but then p and q climb upwards along diagonal to just below 50%, reflecting impact of reputation on fairness norms.",Summarizes rules and qualitative outcomes of the reputation-based simulation.,1
10.1007_978-3-319-15515-9_15.md,Herrmann et al. (2008) public goods game – No-punishment condition,Footnote 10 describes the baseline 10-round PGG before introducing punishment; this is a distinct condition.,0.99,Punishment availability: no punishment stage; standard linear public goods game only,Condition is defined by absence of punishment compared to later punishment condition.,0.99,True,Human participants in lab PGG across multiple countries are described.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized economic game with random groupings and specific rules.",1,True,Described as an anonymous economic game played in laboratories.,1,False,"This condition uses human subjects, not only computer agents.",1,False,Not a purely mathematical or analytical model; it is an experiment.,1,4,Footnote 10: groups of four participants.,1,10,Footnote 10: 'Ten rounds were played as described above' before punishment is introduced.,1,1,"Participants could 'either keep all ... or contribute some portion' of the endowment, implying a continuous choice, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Standard PGG: endowment (20 ECU) is initially entirely in private account; contributions are voluntary.,0.95,0.4,"Total contributions multiplied by 1.6 then divided equally among 4 players, so MPCR = 1.6/4 = 0.4.",1,0,Participants were separated by partitions and unable to directly interact; no communication is mentioned.,0.95,1,End of each round: they must infer earnings since contributions are public and payoff rule is common knowledge; but paper does not explicitly say earnings are shown. Marking as shown because payoffs are needed to calculate next decisions.,0.4,0,No punishment exists in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism is described for any condition.,1,1,"Typical lab PGGs inform subjects of the fixed horizon (10 rounds), but not stated explicitly; assume yes.",0.4,0,This is the pre-punishment stage: only contribution decisions.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,0,"Only punishment is ever mentioned, no positive reward mechanism.",0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each participant received 20 experimental currency units per round.,1,N/R,This chapter cites the paradigm but does not report average contribution fractions.,0.01,N/R,No numeric results for mean contributions are provided here.,0.01,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency statistics are reported.,0.01,N/R,No numeric payoff outcomes are given.,0.01,Multiple countries (exact list N/R),"Herrmann et al. ran the game 'across societies' and variation is linked to countries and GDP, but this chapter does not list the specific countries.",0.4,N/R,No demographic details are given here.,0.01,N/R,No gender composition is described.,0.01,N/R,No information on education or student status in this chapter.,0.01,On site,Explicitly described as an anonymous economic game in laboratories with physical partitions.,1,"Linear public goods game in groups of four; each round, each participant received 20 ECU and chose any contribution to a public account. Sum of contributions multiplied by 1.6 and split equally among all four regardless of contribution. No punishment option in this condition; 10 rounds with random or fixed groups not specified here.",Summarized from detailed procedural description in footnote 10.,1
10.1007_978-3-319-15515-9_15.md,Herrmann et al. (2008) public goods game – Punishment condition,"Footnote 10 describes an additional 10 rounds with a punishment stage, which is a separate condition.",0.99,Punishment availability: costly peer punishment enabled after contribution stage,This condition differs from baseline by adding a punishment decision after contributions.,1,True,Same human-subject lab setup as the no-punishment condition.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of availability of punishment within the same experimental paradigm.,1,True,Again described as an anonymous laboratory economic game.,1,False,"Uses human participants, not just simulations.",1,False,Not purely analytical modeling.,1,4,Same groups of four as baseline; no change in group size reported.,1,10,Footnote 10: 'also ten rounds with the addition of punishment' following the first 10 rounds.,1,1,Contribution decision identical to baseline: participants could contribute some portion of endowment.,0.9,0,Endowment starts in private account; contributions voluntary.,0.95,0.4,Same multiplier and group size: 1.6/4 = 0.4.,1,0,Partitions prevent direct interaction; no communication channel is described.,0.95,1,"After contributions, participants see each other's contributions before punishing. They must also know own payoffs to understand punishment impact, but explicit payoff display is not stated; we mark summaries as shown at least for contributions.",0.8,0,Punishment was anonymous: 'Participants did not receive information about who deducted points from their payoff.',1,0,No reward option exists; only punishment.,1,1,"Likely told the number of rounds as in standard PGG protocols, though not explicitly stated.",0.4,1,Participants could spend ECU to deduct from others' payoffs; this is costly punishment.,1,1,Each ECU spent on sanctioning is a direct cost to the punisher (they 'spent' up to 10 ECU).,1,3,Each ECU spent causes 3 ECU to be deducted from target's payoff.,1,0,Only negative sanctions are described; no mention of prosocial rewards.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same per-round endowment of 20 ECU as baseline.,1,N/R,Chapter does not report average contribution fractions in the punishment condition.,0.01,N/R,Numeric means are not included in this chapter; only qualitative effects are discussed.,0.01,N/R,No explicit aggregate efficiency reported here.,0.01,N/R,No quantitative payoff results given.,0.01,Multiple countries (exact list N/R),Same Herrmann et al. cross-society subject pool; specific countries not listed.,0.4,N/R,Age distributions not provided.,0.01,N/R,Gender composition not mentioned.,0.01,N/R,No description of education or student status.,0.01,On site,Same lab setting with physical partitions as baseline.,1,"Two-stage linear public goods game in groups of four over 10 rounds. Each round: each participant gets 20 ECU, chooses a contribution (0–20) to the public good; total contribution multiplied by 1.6 and divided equally among all group members. After seeing all others’ contributions, each participant can spend up to 10 ECU assigning punishment points to any other group member. For each ECU spent, the targeted player's payoff is reduced by 3 ECU. Punishment is anonymous; received deductions cannot exceed PGG earnings.",Summarized from detailed description in footnote 10.,1
10.1007_978-3-319-15515-9_15.md,MacLean et al. (2010) yeast cooperation – Mixed-strategy population,"MacLean et al. are described as combining empirical experiments and models on two isogenic yeast strains, one overproducing and one underproducing digestive enzymes. The main focus is the mixed population outcome.",0.8,Population composition: mixture of altruistic overproducing strain and free-riding underproducing strain; exact proportions vary dynamically,"Key variable is relative frequency of enzyme overproducers vs underproducers in the population, which determines dynamics.",0.7,False,"Although wet-lab experiments exist in the original paper, this chapter only reports conceptual outcomes and treats them together with models; no specific experimental configuration is detailed enough to code as an empirical design.",0.5,False,From this chapter’s description we cannot separate specific controlled lab conditions; focus is on evolutionary dynamics theory.,0.6,False,Not enough procedural information about lab context; chapter uses the study as an illustrative case of evolutionary dynamics.,0.5,True,The chapter explicitly states they 'demonstrate with both empirical experiments and models' that selection operates to yield optimal group benefit; here we treat the referenced dynamics as a simulation/model condition.,0.8,True,Described as models explaining why a mixed population best tracks environmental change; this is theoretical/evolutionary dynamics analysis.,0.8,N/A,"This is a population genetics style model of many yeast cells, not discrete 'players'.",1,N/A,Evolutionary time is continuous or generational rather than round-based game play.,1,1,"At the strain level, yeast are either altruistic overproducers or free-riding underproducers; individuals do not choose intermediate efforts.",0.7,N/A,No per-round human endowment; this is a biological public-good model.,1,N/R,No explicit marginal per capita return parameter is reported in this chapter’s summary of the model.,0.01,N/A,No human or agent communication stage; microbial interaction is via diffusion of enzymes.,1,N/A,No information display; this is not a decision experiment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the yeast model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit rounds or horizon; evolutionary process is ongoing.,1,0,"Cooperation and free-riding are modeled via public good production, not direct sanctions.",1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,"Benefits are via access to pre-digested food, not an explicit reward mechanism chosen by agents.",1,N/A,No voluntary reward allocation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No discrete endowment; resources are environmental nutrients.,1,N/R,Chapter discusses relative over/underproduction qualitatively but gives no numeric rates.,0.01,N/R,No explicit quantitative enzyme production levels reported here.,0.01,N/R,They state that the mixed population 'benefits optimally' and provides 'most efficient exploitation of available resources' but do not quantify efficiency.,0.2,N/R,No explicit total population payoff numbers provided.,0.01,N/A,No human participants; yeast strains in lab.,1,N/A,No human age.,1,N/A,No human gender.,1,N/A,No human education.,1,No human,Evolutionary model and microbial experiments; no human subjects.,1,"Evolutionary dynamics model of two isogenic yeast strains: an altruistic strain that overproduces costly extracellular digestive enzymes and a free-riding strain that underproduces. Enzyme production is a public good because pre-digested food can be used by any nearby yeast. The model shows that selection maintains a mixture of the two strains: when altruists are rare, free-riders starve; when free-riders are rare, excess public good allows free-riders to invade. This mixed composition yields near-optimal exploitation of fluctuating resources given rapid birth–death cycles and changing environmental optima.",Paraphrased from the conceptual description in Sect. 15.4.2.,0.9
10.1038_srep42735.md,Replicator-equation analysis – generic asymmetric 2×2 bribery game on inter-dependent regular networks,Theoretical section derives replicator equations for a generic asymmetric 2×2 game on inter-dependent networks as the analytical baseline for the bribery game.,0.86,"Game payoffs (a′, b′, c′, d′, p′, q′, r′, s′), network degrees k1, k2, interaction degrees λ1, λ2, update rule (death–birth, imitation, birth–death)","Equations (1–2) show dependence on payoff entries, degrees k1, k2 and λ1, λ2 through τ1, τ2 and specify three update rules.",0.86,False,No human participants; purely mathematical derivation.,1,False,Not an empirical or controlled behavioral experiment; it is analytical theory.,1,False,No lab or field setting; analytical work only.,1,False,This condition is purely analytical; simulations are described in a separate section.,1,True,Explicit derivation of replicator equations for structured asymmetric games using pair approximation.,1,0,No explicit finite group of simultaneous players; continuum of agents in replicator dynamics.,1,0,"Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics, not discrete repeated rounds.",1,1,"Citizens’ and officers’ strategies are categorical (pay/complain types), not contribution levels; public-goods style contribution structure not applicable.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects and no contribution game structure.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no group multiplier.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication stage.,1,0,Model agents update via payoffs; no display of summaries as in experiments.,0.8,N/A,No explicit notion of observable identities; analytical payoff-only model without humans.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identities in analytical model.,1,0,"Continuous-time model, no round information.",1,1,Corrupt officers are penalized with probability k and penalty p_o when prosecuted.,1,N/R,Per-unit cost structure not defined as in peer punishment; only total penalty p_o is used.,0.3,N/R,No linear mapping from spent punishment to loss; penalty p_o is exogenous.,0.3,1,"Citizens may receive refund r of the bribe when prosecution succeeds, which functions as a reward for complaining.",0.7,N/R,No explicit cost per unit of refund besides complaint cost t; no separate reward-cost parameter.,0.4,N/R,"Refund amount r is directly specified, not derived from a technology parameter.",0.4,N/R,Model uses fixed payoff parameters v and c rather than an initial endowment concept.,0.4,N/A,No notion of contribution; analytical outcome is strategy frequencies.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts are defined.,1,N/R,"Paper reports phase regions and fixation of strategies, not explicit efficiency ratios.",0.4,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not summarized as a single reported measure.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical derivation without empirical implementation.,1,"Asymmetric bribery game between officers and citizens on inter-dependent regular networks; analytical reduction to replicator equations for a generic 2×2 asymmetric game and a specialized 4-strategy bribery game with strategies O1 (honest officer), O2 (corrupt officer), C1 (apathetic payer), C2 (payer-complainer).",Summarizes the analytical game structure explicitly described in Methods and Results.,0.96
10.1038_srep42735.md,"Simulation – symmetric populations, refund r = b, 5-strategy model, CN = 2, ON = 2, varying IN","Single simulation study with N_O = N_C = 100, ON = CN = 2, refund r = b, exploring several values of interaction-network degree IN (1, 2, 4, 100).",0.96,"Interaction-network degree IN for symmetric populations with refund (IN = 1, 2, 4, 100) while scanning bribe amount b and complaint cost t over [0,1].","Figures 2a–d vary IN; text: “for increasing IN with parameters NO=100, NC=100, ON=2, CN=2, r=b … with (a) IN=1, (b) IN=2, (c) IN=4 and (d) IN=100 … we vary both b and t between 0 and 1 with steps of 0.01”.",0.96,False,"Agent-based simulations with model agents, no human subjects.",1,False,"Computational experiment, not an empirical behavioral experiment.",1,False,No physical lab or field setting.,1,True,Described under “Stochastic Simulations”; uses agent-based simulations on networks.,1,False,"This condition uses numerical simulations, not closed-form analysis.",1,200,Symmetric case uses N_O = 100 officers and N_C = 100 citizens.,1,10000000000,"They iterate “for 10^10 generations or until any one of the officers’ strategies gets fixed, whichever is earlier.”",0.9,1,"Agents choose pure strategies (O1/O2, C1/C2/C3) rather than continuous contributions; mapping to all-or-nothing contributions does not apply, but citizens may either pay or refuse; coded as free-choice (1).",0.5,N/A,No endowment allocation or contribution game.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,0,Model specifies only payoffs and imitation dynamics; no communication or messaging stage.,0.9,0,Agents only compare own and a neighbour’s payoff; no aggregate summaries.,0.9,0,No notion of identity visibility or targeted punishment; only role-based interactions.,0.8,0,"Refund is automatic, not conditioned on knowing others’ identities.",0.8,0,Agents do not receive information about total remaining generations; evolution proceeds autonomously.,0.9,1,Corrupt officers are penalized by p_o with probability k when prosecuted.,1,N/R,"Penalty p_o is a fixed loss to the officer, not cost paid by another agent; no per-unit punishment-cost parameter.",0.4,N/R,No linear punishment-technology mapping specified.,0.4,1,"In refund scenario, bribe is refunded to complaining citizens (r = b) upon successful prosecution.",1,N/R,No explicit cost for funding refunds beyond complaint cost t; no separate reward pool.,0.4,N/R,"Refund size r is directly parameterized, not derived from spendable reward tokens.",0.4,N/R,"Agents have fixed baseline payoff parameters (v, c) but no explicit initial endowment per round is described in contribution terms.",0.5,N/A,No contribution variable; outcomes are strategy fixation regions.,1,N/A,No continuous contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,"Paper reports which strategies fix for each (b,t,IN) not normalized efficiency metrics.",0.4,N/R,No aggregate payoff values are summarized numerically across all agents.,0.4,N/A,"Simulation with artificial agents, not humans.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is computational, not run with human subjects.",1,"Five-strategy asymmetric bribery game with strategies O1 (honest officer), O2 (corrupt officer), C1 (apathetic, pay silently), C2 (pay and complain), C3 (refuse and complain) on inter-dependent regular networks. Symmetric populations: N_O = 100 officers, N_C = 100 citizens; officer and citizen networks are 1-D rings of degree ON = 2 and CN = 2. Interaction network connects each citizen to IN officers (IN ∈ {1,2,4,100}); refund scenario r = b, asymmetric punishment p_o > 0, p_c = 0, v = 1, c = 1, p_o = 2, k ∈ (0,1). For each IN, they sweep bribe b and complaint cost t on [0,1] in steps of 0.01 and classify which strategies fix in equilibrium.",Summarizes detailed simulation configuration as described under ‘Stochastic simulations’ and in captions of Figs 1–2.,0.96
10.1038_srep42735.md,"Simulation – symmetric populations, refund r = b, 5-strategy model, IN = 1, ON = 2, varying CN","Simulation study where degree of citizen network CN is varied (4, 6, 50) for symmetric populations with refund and IN fixed at 1 (Figure 3 and 5).",0.96,"Citizen-network degree CN for symmetric populations with refund (CN = 2, 4, 6, 20, 50) while scanning bribe amount b and complaint cost t over [0,1].","Text: “we varied CN … while keeping IN=1. … compare Fig. 2a,b with Fig. 3a,b … with (a) CN=4, (b) CN=6 and (c) CN=50.” Fig. 5 further shows CN = 2,4,6,20,50 for a fixed (b,t).",0.96,False,Agent-based simulations with model agents.,1,False,"Computational, not empirical.",1,False,No lab setting; purely in silico.,1,True,Described as stochastic simulations on networks.,1,False,"Outcomes generated numerically, not by closed-form solutions.",1,200,"Same symmetric-population configuration as before: N_O = 100, N_C = 100.",1,10000000000,Same update rule and termination condition: up to 10^10 generations or earlier fixation.,0.9,1,"Strategy choice, not continuous contribution; coded as non-all-or-nothing contributions not applicable.",0.5,N/A,No contribution game.,1,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,0,No communication modeled.,0.9,0,Agents only observe payoffs of randomly sampled neighbours during imitation.,0.9,0,No identity revelation in punishment process.,0.8,0,"Refund is automatic, no identity display.",0.8,0,No information about total or remaining generations is provided in the model.,0.9,1,Asymmetric penalty p_o on corrupt officers upon prosecution.,1,N/R,Penalty is exogenous; no cost borne by other agents per punishment ‘unit’.,0.4,N/R,No per-token punishment technology defined.,0.4,1,Refund scenario with r = b.,1,N/R,No separate cost for rewarding beyond t and implicit institutional resources.,0.4,N/R,Refund is parameterized by r; no transformation from resource to reward specified.,0.4,N/R,"Baseline payoffs (v, c) not framed as endowments.",0.5,N/A,Focus is on the fraction of phase space with honest-officer fixation and dynamics of strategy clusters.,1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"Paper examines f_ho (fraction of parameter space with honest officers) and fixation probability near phase boundary, not efficiency.",0.4,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not reported numerically.,0.4,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational experiment.,1,"Five-strategy asymmetric bribery game on inter-dependent regular networks, with symmetric populations (N_O = 100, N_C = 100), ON = 2, IN = 1, r = b, v = 1, c = 1, p_o = 2, p_c = 0. The degree of the citizen network CN is varied (2,4,6,20,50), and for each CN they compute phase diagrams over the b–t grid (0–1, step 0.01) and the fraction f_ho of this space where honest officers fix. Additional simulations at a specific (b,t) near phase boundary (e.g., b = 0.70, t = 0.70) show time-evolution of spatial strategy clusters for different CN.",Summarizes configuration and outcomes described in text around Figs 3–5.,0.96
10.1038_srep42735.md,"Simulation – symmetric populations, no refund r = 0, 5-strategy model, varying CN","Simulation scenario described as ‘Scenarios without refund’ with r = 0 and varying CN; results mainly in Supplementary Figures S2, S3, S4.",0.86,"Refund condition (r = 0) and citizen-network degree CN (varied over multiple values) while scanning bribe amount b and complaint cost t over [0,1].",Text: “we therefore consider a situation when the bribe-giver does not receive any refund (r=0). The phase diagram for various values of CN (Figure S2)… A peak in the fraction … on varying CN.”,0.86,False,Agent-based simulations only.,1,False,"Computational, not empirical.",1,False,"In silico, not lab based.",1,True,Described as stochastic simulations on networks.,1,False,"Numerical, not closed-form.",1,200,"Same base symmetric setup (N_O = 100, N_C = 100) is used unless specifically changed; no change is mentioned for this scenario.",0.8,10000000000,Same simulation procedure and stopping rule used across all scenarios.,0.8,1,"Strategy selection, not contributions; same reasoning as other simulations.",0.5,N/A,No contribution game.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication modeled.,0.9,0,Agents only observe local payoffs.,0.9,0,No identity-based punishment mechanism.,0.8,0,"No refunds (r=0), so no rewards to identify.",0.9,0,No round-number information in the model.,0.9,1,Officers still face penalty p_o upon prosecution; only refund is removed.,1,N/R,Penalty magnitude p_o is not framed as a cost per punishment token.,0.4,N/R,No punishment technology parameter.,0.4,0,They explicitly set r = 0 and note that bribe-givers do not receive a refund.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism; hence no reward cost.,1,N/A,No reward/refund implemented.,1,N/R,Same bribery payoff structure; no explicit endowment.,0.5,N/A,"Outcomes are phase diagrams and f_ho over (b,t,CN), not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"Paper discusses regions where bribery is sustained vs eradicated, not explicit efficiency metrics.",0.4,N/R,No quantitative aggregate payoff reported.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Agent-based, in silico.",1,"Same five-strategy asymmetric bribery game on inter-dependent regular networks as in the refund case, but with r = 0 (no refund). Asymmetric penalty persists (p_o > 0, p_c = 0). They vary CN and compute phase diagrams over b and t in [0,1] and the fraction of phase space with honest-officer fixation, observing a substantial shrinkage of the honest region and V-shaped phase boundaries due to optimization of bribe demand by corrupt officers.","Summarizes the ‘Scenarios without refund’ simulation description, which is structurally similar to the refund case but with r = 0.",0.86
10.1038_srep42735.md,"Simulation – symmetric populations, 4-strategy model without C3, with and without refund, varying CN","Authors explicitly consider reduced models dropping C3-type citizens, both with and without refund (Figures S3 and S4), to compare with the 5-strategy model.",0.82,"Citizen-type set (C1, C2 only), refund condition (r = b vs r = 0), and citizen-network degree CN; b and t scanned over [0,1].",Text: “we … explored bribery games with (Figure S3) and without (Figure S4) refund by dropping C3 type citizens from the initial population. The results are similar to the 5-strategy model and here too we observe a peak … for moderate value of CN.”,0.82,False,Again entirely simulated agents.,1,False,"Computational, not empirical.",1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Described as extended simulations in Supplementary Information.,1,False,Numerical analysis only.,1,200,"They state N_O = 100, N_C = 1000 only when introducing asymmetric populations; for baseline models they use N_O = 100, N_C = 100. The 4-strategy extensions are presented as variants of the symmetric model, so 100+100 is most consistent.",0.7,10000000000,Same simulation framework and stopping condition for all variants.,0.8,1,Strategy choice framework; no partial contributions.,0.5,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No PGG structure.,1,0,No communication modeled.,0.9,0,Local payoff comparison only.,0.9,0,No explicit identity visibility in punishment.,0.8,0,"Refund, when present, is automatic; no identity cues.",0.8,0,No feedback on remaining generations.,0.9,1,Same asymmetric officer penalty is maintained in the reduced models.,1,N/R,"As before, no per-unit punishment cost parameter.",0.4,N/R,Penalty p_o is exogenous.,0.4,N/R,"They explicitly study both with-refund (r = b) and without-refund (r = 0) cases in this 4-strategy model but do not separate them in the main text. At this aggregation level, whether reward exists depends on the particular subcondition.",0.3,N/R,Reward/refund is present only 