10.1007_s11403-015-0146-8.md,Weakly augmented social norm (GGBGBG) – punishment optional,Condition where punishment (P) is feasible but not strongly incentivized; norm labeled weakly augmented GGBGBG.,1,"Social norm type: weakly augmented (GGBGBG), punishment option available but defection against bad recipients still assigned good reputation; strategies: CC, CD, CP, DD.",This condition fixes the social norm to GGBGBG and allows punishment as an additional action.,1,False,No human data; purely theoretical and computational.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is model analysis.,1,False,No lab study.,1,True,"Uses numerical simulation (Runge–Kutta, separatrix computation, sampling many initial conditions) to analyze dynamics and basin sizes, including reported q = 0.60 for the CESS attraction basin.",1,True,"Analytically derives steady-state reputation distributions, payoffs, and replicator equations under this norm.",1,2,Same donor–recipient pair structure as in the general model.,1,0,Continuous evolutionary time with repeated random matching; no fixed round count.,1,1,"Action set is discrete {C,D,P}, with each implying full cooperation, defection, or punishment; no partial contributions.",0.5,N/A,Not a contribution game with an initial endowment allocation.,1,N/R,No group public-good multiplier defined.,1,N/A,No communication stage with human agents.,1,0,"Model assumes agents learn via payoff comparisons, not via displayed summaries.",0.5,0,"Reputations are public, but identities of punishers are not separately modeled as being revealed; only outcomes and reputations matter.",0.5,0,No rewarding institution.,1,0,No finite-horizon rounds.,1,1,Punishment P is explicitly available and norm describes reputation updates following punishment.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost α is a parameter, but no single numeric value is fixed for the general model; specific illustrative values vary (e.g., α = 1 in some figures).",0.5,N/R,"Punishment imposes cost β on recipients, but magnitude per unit cost is not expressed as a separate linear technology parameter.",0.5,0,No explicit costly positive reward action separate from cooperation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Endowment per round is not modeled; c,b,α,β act as flow parameters.",1,N/R,They report cooperation ratio trajectories and basin sizes but do not give a single normalized contribution rate value for this condition.,0.5,N/R,Contributions are binary actions with abstract cost c; no token amounts given.,1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed via expected payoff and comparisons (e.g., Fig. 7b), but no normalized 0–1 efficiency statistic is reported.",0.5,N/R,Expected payoff functions are symbolic; no single outcome value reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is a parameterization of the theoretical model only.,1,"Donor–recipient game with three actions (C, D, P). Cooperation costs c and yields benefit b. Punishment costs α to donor and β to recipient. Reputation is binary (G/B), public, and updated under norm GGBGBG: cooperators toward any recipient get good reputation; defectors toward bad recipients get good reputation; punishing bad recipients yields good reputation; punishing good recipients yields bad reputation. Strategies allowed: CC, CD, CP, DD. Evolution follows replicator dynamics, with reported CESS at CD and basin ratio q ≈ 0.60 for parameter example b=3, c=2, α=1, β=4, μ=0.02.","Summarized from Sections 2.1–2.2 and 3.2, including description of the weakly augmented norm and the numerical example.",1
10.1007_s11403-015-0146-8.md,Strongly augmented social norm (GGBBBG) – punishment encouraged,Condition where punishment is available and explicitly incentivized by the social norm; labeled strongly augmented GGBBBG.,1,"Social norm type: strongly augmented (GGBBBG), punishment option available and defection against bad recipients yields bad reputation; strategies: CC, CD, CP, DD.","This condition fixes the norm to GGBBBG; it differs from the weakly augmented norm in how defection against bad recipients is evaluated, encouraging punishment.",1,False,No experimental or observational human data.,1,False,The work is theoretical and simulation-based.,1,False,No lab interaction.,1,True,"Uses numerical integration and sampling to characterize basins (q ≈ 0.81), trajectories (e.g., Fig. 5, 7d–e), and convergence speeds.",1,True,"Provides analytic steady-state reputation distributions, payoffs, and replicator equations plus eigenvalue analysis for stability.",1,2,Same dyadic donor–recipient structure as in other norms.,1,0,Continuous-time evolutionary model without discrete rounds.,1,1,"Action space is a discrete choice between C, D, P; no partial levels of cooperation/punishment.",0.5,N/A,No endowment-allocation stage.,1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier defined.,1,N/A,No communication with human agents.,1,0,"Strategy revision uses payoff comparisons conceptually, but no explicit summary display is modeled.",0.5,0,"Reputation is public, but the model does not separately model identity revelation of punishers.",0.5,0,No explicit costly rewards.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary process; no displayed rounds.,1,1,Punishment action P is included and evaluated by the norm.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost α is symbolic; specific numeric examples (e.g., α=1) are illustrative only.",0.5,N/R,"Recipient cost β is specified, but not as a distinct per-unit technology parameter.",0.5,0,No separate rewarding mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game defined via costs/benefits per interaction, not via finite token endowments.",1,N/R,"Cooperation ratios and basins are described (e.g., CESS at CP, basin q≈0.81 and faster convergence) but no single normalized contribution rate is numerically reported.",0.5,N/R,No token-based contribution measure.,1,N/R,"Expected payoff paths are plotted and compared with weakly augmented norm (Fig. 7e), but no explicit 0–1 efficiency value is reported.",0.5,N/R,Group-average payoff is functional and time-dependent; not summarized as a single number for the condition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition exists only within theoretical/simulation framework.,1,"Donor–recipient game with actions C, D, P. Cooperation costs c and yields benefit b. Punishment costs α to donor and β to recipient. Reputation is binary and public; norm GGBBBG assigns reputations such that defection against bad recipients yields bad reputation while punishing bad recipients yields good reputation, creating a stronger incentive to punish. Strategies allowed: CC, CD, CP, DD. Under parameter example b=3, c=2, α=1, β=4, μ=0.02, the cooperative ESS is CP, and the attraction basin size for CESS is q ≈ 0.81, larger than in other norms. Convergence to cooperation is faster but long-run efficiency can be lower due to costly punishment when most reputations are good.",Compiled from Sections 2.1–2.2 and 3.2–3.3 where the strongly augmented norm and its numerical properties are discussed.,1
