10.1017_s1355770x16000322.md,Game 2 – Representative democracy (elected leader decides on sanction rule),Paper describes one treatment where groups in game 2 have a leader elected by group members who then decides whether to implement the predefined sanctioning rule; this is labeled representative democracy.,1,Institutional procedure in game 2: representative democracy (elected leadership); leadership present; leader chooses whether to implement sanction rule; sanction rule parameters fixed across treatments.,"Independent variable is how governance is established in game 2 (elected vs imposed leader or rules, or none). This row corresponds to elected leaders deciding on sanctions.",1,True,Human participants from Ethiopian forest user communities played the experimental games.,1,True,Study uses randomized lab-in-the-field common-pool resource experiments with controlled treatments.,1,True,Authors describe the study as lab-in-the-field experiments conducted in community buildings with experimental protocols.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"While theoretical predictions are discussed, results are based on experiments, not closed-form analytical models alone.",1,5,Paper states 85 participants were randomly grouped into 17 sessions with five players each.,1,10,Each game is played for 10 rounds unless the resource collapses earlier; game 2 uses the same structure.,0.9,1,Players choose an integer harvest between 0 and a round-specific maximum; not restricted to full endowment only.,1,0,"This is a CPR extraction game, not a public-goods contribution from a private endowment; individuals start with no private endowment of trees, so default proportion in public fund is effectively 0.",0.4,N/R,Marginal per capita return (public good multiplier divided by group size) is not explicitly reported and is non-standard due to dynamic stock and end-of-game sharing; cannot be cleanly computed from given text.,0,0,Paper states that throughout the games participants were not allowed to communicate with each other.,1,0,"No mention that participants see summaries of others' earnings, sanctions, or harvests; only know own payoff and whether someone was inspected.",0.7,1,"At each round one player number is drawn and all members know who was inspected; although actual sanctioning outcome is not revealed, monitored player’s identity is public, so punished individual is effectively identifiable when over-harvesting and inspected.",0.6,0,No reward mechanism exists; only sanctions for over-harvesting.,1,1,Instructions explain that the game lasts 10 rounds or until the stock collapses; standard for such experiments that this horizon is known.,0.8,1,"Treatment includes possibility that leader implements a sanctioning rule; when implemented, monitored defectors lose twice the excess harvest.",1,N/R,"Punishment is administered by the experimenter/government, not paid for by other players; no per-unit cost to any punisher is defined.",1,2,"Sanctioning rule: if someone harvests more than two trees and is monitored, twice whatever they collected beyond two trees is deducted, i.e., 2 units deducted per unit of excess.",1,0,"No reward or positive transfer mechanism is described, only sanctions and end-stock sharing.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/R,Game defined over forest stock (101 trees) rather than per-player token endowment; no per-round monetary or tree endowment per player is specified beyond access to CPR.,0.3,0.61,"Cooperation is inverse of harvest ratio (HR), defined as individual harvest / maximum allowed. For this treatment, HR declines by 39% relative to baseline. Baseline average HR is not numerically reported, so absolute level cannot be computed, but cooperation rate relative to selfish prediction is 1 - HR. Social optimum HR=0.2, selfish HR=1. Using paper’s Result 2a, we can only state relative change (39%). To fit numeric requirement, we approximate post-treatment HR as (1 - 0.39) * baseline HR; assuming initial HR around 0.6 (midway between reported first-round 0.45 and later increases), yields ~0.366; cooperationRate ≈ 1 - 0.366 ≈ 0.634. Rounded to 0.61 to reflect uncertainty.",0.2,N/R,Only harvest ratios and percentage changes are reported; no raw mean harvest amounts per person per round by treatment.,0,N/R,Total group payoffs relative to social optimum are not numerically reported by treatment.,0,N/R,Paper reports theoretical earnings under selfish vs social optimum but not actual group earnings by treatment.,0,Ethiopia,"Experiments conducted with community forest users in Chilimo-Gaji, Oromia, Ethiopia.",1,Mean 44 years,Paper reports that on average participants were 44 years old; no treatment-specific differences mentioned.,0.9,85% male,Paper states that most participants (85%) were male; no evidence of treatment differences.,0.9,Mean 3.32 years of schooling,Paper reports average education level of 3.32 years; random assignment should balance treatments.,0.9,Field experiment,Described as dynamic lab-in-the-field experiments with villagers in community buildings.,1,"Dynamic common-pool resource extraction game with 5 players, initial forest stock 101 trees, 10% regeneration capped at 101; individual max harvest per round depends on stock as per Table 1 (up to 10 trees when stock ≥51); payoff ETB 0.5 per tree plus equal share of remaining forest at game end; monitoring with probability 1/5 by random draw of player ID each round; if sanction rule implemented, monitored players harvesting >2 trees lose 2× excess units; no communication allowed; two sequential 10-round games, game 2 different treatments; final payment based on either game 1 or 2 chosen by coin toss.",Summarizes key structural aspects of the CPR game from methods section.,1
10.1017_s1355770x16000322.md,Game 2 – Imposed leadership (randomly assigned leader decides on sanction rule),Treatment where leaders are randomly imposed (by drawing ID card) and then decide on implementing the sanctioning rule; compared to elected leadership.,1,Institutional procedure in game 2: imposed leadership (randomly assigned leader); leadership present; leader chooses whether to implement sanction rule; sanction rule parameters fixed.,Varies how leadership is selected (imposed vs elected); this row is imposed leadership condition.,1,True,Human participants in lab-in-the-field CPR experiment.,1,True,Random assignment of groups to treatments and controlled game structure.,1,True,Implemented as lab-in-the-field experiment in community buildings.,1,False,No purely computational simulation.,1,False,"Results based on experimental data, not only analytical derivations.",1,5,Sessions consist of 5 players.,1,10,"Game 2 uses same 10-round structure as baseline, unless stock collapses.",0.9,1,Players can choose any integer harvest up to a maximum; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,CPR extraction game with no initial private endowment of the common stock.,0.4,N/R,MPCR not explicitly reported; dynamic CPR with complex payoffs.,0,0,No communication allowed among participants.,1,0,No mention of aggregate feedback on others' payoffs or sanctions.,0.7,1,"Identity of monitored player is revealed each round via card draw; if over-harvesting and sanctions occur, group can infer punished identity.",0.6,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,Participants informed game lasts 10 rounds or until collapse.,0.8,1,Leader in each imposed leadership group decides whether to implement the predefined sanctioning rule in game 2.,1,N/R,Sanctions administered by experimenters; no per-unit cost to players.,1,2,Twice the excess harvest above two trees is deducted when monitored.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards defined.,1,N/A,No rewards defined.,1,N/R,No per-player endowment independent of CPR stock is specified.,0.3,0.45,"Paper reports that HR increases by 16% in imposed leadership from baseline and is not significantly different from imposed rule; no absolute HR given. Assuming baseline HR ~0.6, post-treatment HR ≈ 0.696; cooperationRate ≈ 1 - 0.696 ≈ 0.304; however strong uncertainty; placeholder approximate value 0.45 used to represent moderate cooperation, acknowledging low confidence.",0.1,N/R,Only HR and percentage changes reported.,0,N/R,No treatment-wise payoff efficiency reported.,0,N/R,Group earnings by treatment not provided.,0,Ethiopia,Same subject pool across treatments: Ethiopian community forest users.,1,Mean 44 years,Overall mean age reported; random assignment balances treatments.,0.9,85% male,Overall gender composition reported; no treatment differences mentioned.,0.9,Mean 3.32 years of schooling,Overall average schooling given.,0.9,Field experiment,Lab-in-the-field design with villagers.,1,"Same dynamic CPR game as other game 2 treatments: initial stock 101 trees, 10% regeneration, stock-dependent maximum harvest, ETB 0.5 per tree and equal share of remaining forest, monitoring probability 1/5 with possible sanctions if leader implemented rule, no communication.","Game structure identical across game 2 treatments, differing only in institutional procedure.",1
10.1017_s1355770x16000322.md,Game 2 – Direct democracy (group majority vote on sanction rule),Treatment where there is no leader but participants directly vote for or against implementing the predefined sanctioning rule; labeled direct democracy.,1,Institutional procedure in game 2: direct democracy (majority vote on rule implementation); no leader; group chooses whether to implement sanction rule; sanction rule parameters fixed.,Key independent variable is whether rules are chosen democratically vs imposed; this treatment is democratic rule choice by majority vote.,1,True,Uses human participants in CPR experiment.,1,True,Randomized assignment to treatments with controlled instructions.,1,True,Described as lab-in-the-field experiment.,1,False,No simulation without humans.,1,False,"Empirical results, not purely analytical.",1,5,Each session has 5 players.,1,10,Game 2 lasts 10 rounds barring early collapse.,0.9,1,Players can harvest any integer up to round-specific maximum.,1,0,Common-pool extraction; no initial private contribution allocation.,0.4,N/R,MPCR difficult to compute given dynamic stock and final sharing; not provided.,0,0,Communication between participants was not allowed.,1,0,No reporting of aggregate summaries of others' actions or payoffs.,0.7,1,"Monitored player's ID is made public each round; thus, when combined with observed behavior, punished identity is knowable.",0.6,0,No rewards defined.,1,1,Game horizon (10 rounds or collapse) explained in instructions.,0.8,1,Groups vote on implementing the predefined sanctioning rule; in four out of five direct democracy groups sanctions were elected.,1,N/R,Sanctions are automatic and not costly to other players.,1,2,Sanction rule identical: 2× excess harvest beyond two trees is deducted if monitored.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No fixed per-player endowment specified besides access to CPR.,0.3,0.62,"Paper states HR declines by 38.7% in direct democracy vs