10.1016_j.jmp.2021.102640.md,Gas Station game – Virtual Bargaining Equilibrium condition,"Paper defines a stylized extensive-form Gas Station game and characterizes the VBE set {(Pay, (π, G F^c F; 1−π, G F^c F^c)): π ≥ 0.6}. Treat this as one theoretical condition.",0.9,"Game structure: sequential trade with optional costly punishment; virtual bargaining equilibrium solution concept (no renegotiation); threat probability π in [0.6,1]","Only variation discussed within this condition is π, constrained to ensure payment; solution concept (VBE vs SVBE) is key independent modeling assumption.",0.86,False,Purely formal game-theoretic analysis; no human participants described.,1,False,No controlled study with human subjects; only stylized game model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; entirely theoretical.,1,False,"No computational/agent-based simulation results reported, only analytical derivations.",0.96,True,"Payoffs, equilibria, and VBE/SVBE sets are derived analytically.",1,2,Game is between attendant A and motorist M.,1,1,"Single finite extensive-form interaction (provide gas, pay, fight); not repeated.",0.96,1,"Choices are discrete actions, not contribution levels; contribution framing not applicable but protocol allows binary actions rather than constrained continuous contributions.",0.3,N/A,"Not a contribution/public goods game; analytical, no human endowments.",1,N/R,No public good or multiplier structure defined.,1,N/A,"No human subjects; communication only discussed conceptually, not implemented.",1,0,No mention of feedback or summaries of others’ outcomes; game is complete information with fixed payoffs.,0.8,N/A,No actual agents; identity revelation not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this game.,1,0,"Finite, known extensive form; but no interface/subjects, so display notion not applicable.",0.7,1,"Attendant can choose F (fight), described as mutually damaging conflict (cost 10 to both).",1,10,Text: “The cost of fighting is 10 for both players.”,1,0,No explicit punishment technology ratio; conflict just imposes fixed cost 10 on both with no additional loss multiple; set to 0 as no per-unit mapping.,0.4,0,No positive-cost rewards; only trade and punishment.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism not present.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism not present.,1,N/R,"Model gives payoffs and costs (e.g., gas cost 4, payment 6, value 8), but no explicit initial endowment per player.",0.6,N/R,No contributions or cooperation rates measured; purely theoretical.,1,N/R,No experimental data on amounts; only fixed payoffs from strategy profiles.,1,N/R,They note that trade is mutually advantageous vs no-trade equilibrium but provide no normalized efficiency metric.,0.7,N/R,"Can compute example payoffs for specific profiles, but no single aggregate group payoff is reported as a dependent variable.",0.6,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All analyses are formal game-theoretic models with no data collection.,1,"Sequential extensive-form game between attendant and motorist: attendant chooses G or G^c; if G, motorist chooses Pay or Not Pay; then attendant chooses F or F^c. Parameters: attendant gas cost 4, motorist gas benefit 8, payment 6 from motorist to attendant, fight cost 10 to each. Unique SPE is no trade (G^c, no fight, no pay). VBE yields trade with payment sustained by potentially costly punishment with probability π ≥ 0.6 after nonpayment.","Summarizes described structure, moves, and numerical payoffs from Fig. 1 and Table 1.",1
