rminal payoffs like (256,256) can be computed, but not treated as empirical DV.",0.7,N/A,No participants used.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Formal analysis only.,1,"Two players A and B; two piles of money start as ($1,$4). On her turn, a player either takes the larger pile (t) and ends the game, or passes (p), in which case both piles double and the other player moves. Figure 2 shows a 6-stage version; general analysis covers arbitrary finite n ≥ 4. Unique SPE (and Nash equilibria) predict taking at the first move. Proposition 1 shows that in the unique VBE, each player chooses p at all decision nodes, effectively doubling the piles to the end.",Directly summarizes game description and the stated Proposition 1(a).,1
