10.1016_j.jmp.2021.102640.md,Finitely repeated modified Prisoner’s Dilemma – Turn-taking Virtual Bargaining Equilibrium condition,"Section 3.3 introduces a modified PD with Table 4 payoffs and Proposition 3(a): in any VBE, both players follow a turn-taking sequence (c,d)/(d,c) enforced by punishment.",0.95,Game structure: finitely repeated modified 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma (Table 4) with even number of stages; virtual bargaining equilibrium with turn-taking agreement and punishment (defecting after deviation),Independent structural features are the altered payoff matrix and the VBE solution concept applied to a finite repeated game with turn-taking strategies.,0.93,False,No experimental implementation of this modified PD; citations to other experiments are external.,1,False,The paper only theorizes about turn-taking; no data.,1,False,Not run in a lab.,1,False,Analytical proof in Proposition 3; no simulation.,0.96,True,Turn-taking VBE is proved in Appendix (Proposition 3).,1,2,Two players in modified PD.,1,4,"They assume an even number of stages > 4 so that turn-taking can occur at least twice. Exact n not fixed, but 4 is the minimal even n > 4 (i.e., 6); using an exact value is speculative; record lower even bound illustratively as 4 with low confidence.",0.2,1,Actions are c or d only.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No public-good structure.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,0,No interface; theoretical.,0.8,N/A,"Punishment is implemented as future defection in strategies, not as a separate action in an experiment.",1,N/A,No separate reward operator.,1,0,"Even finite horizon is assumed common knowledge, not displayed.",0.8,0,‘Punishment’ here is implemented within the PD as choosing d after deviation; no separate costly punishment stage.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment cost parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit rewards beyond baseline payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No separate rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,"Stage-game payoffs (Table 4) are (c,c)=(-6,-6); (c,d)=(-10,0); (d,c)=(0,-10); (d,d)=(-7,-7). No initial endowment specified.",0.7,N/R,"Outcome is a predicted turn-taking pattern, not empirical cooperation rates.",1,N/R,No empirical contributions.,1,N/R,"They compare payoffs (e.g., turn-taking vs (c,c) every round), but do not compute normalized efficiency metrics.",0.7,N/R,"Total payoff under turn-taking can be computed symbolically (e.g., -5n total across both players), but not used as an empirical DV.",0.7,N/A,No experimental participants.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,No human,"Turn-taking in modified PD is presented as a theoretical prediction and linked to other empirical literatures, but not tested here.",1,"Modified stage-game PD (Table 4) with payoffs (Player1,Player2): (c,c)=(-6,-6); (c,d)=(-10,0); (d,c)=(0,-10); (d,d)=(-7,-7). Game is repeated a finite even number of times (>4). Turn-taking agreement: players alternate between (c,d) and (d,c), starting with (c,d), and if either deviates, the opponent punishes by defecting for the rest of the game. Proposition 3(a): this turn-taking agreement with punishments is a VBE; in any VBE, both players follow the turn-taking sequence along the equilibrium path.",Paraphrases Section 3.3 and Proposition 3(a) plus its proof sketch.,1
