10.1016_j.jmp.2021.102640.md,Repeated Bertrand competition – Static game Virtual Bargaining condition,Appendix recaps Melkonyan et al. (2018)’s one-shot Bertrand game and states the unique VBE price p^V between Nash price p^N and collusive price p^*.,0.9,"Game structure: one-shot 2-firm differentiated Bertrand duopoly with linear demands q1 = a − b p1 + c p2, q2 = a − b p2 + c p1, b ≥ c > 0, marginal cost 0; virtual bargaining equilibrium in static game",Independent features are demand/cost parameterization and the choice of VBE as solution concept.,0.9,False,This is a theoretical recap; no empirical data.,1,False,"No experiment, only derivation.",1,False,No lab setup.,1,False,Closed-form analytical solution; no simulations reported.,0.96,True,VBE price p^V is solved symbolically.,1,2,Two firms in the duopoly.,1,1,Static (one-shot) pricing game.,1,1,Action is a continuous price; ‘allOrNothing’ coding is not conceptually appropriate but default to 1 to indicate no discrete contribution constraint.,0.2,N/A,Not a contribution/public goods setting.,1,N/R,No public goods element.,1,N/A,No agents or interface.,1,0,No interface or feedback described.,0.8,N/A,No punishment or IDs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,One-shot game; no notion of rounds visible to participants.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism; only prices affecting demand and profit.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Profits are inherent outcomes, not optional rewards.",1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Firms have implicit capacity and zero marginal cost; no initial endowment specified.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome is a price (p^V), not a contribution.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They compare welfare between Nash, VBE, and collusive prices qualitatively; no normalized efficiency ratio given.",0.7,N/R,"Joint profits at p^V are characterized qualitatively as intermediate, but no numeric value provided.",0.7,N/A,"Theoretical firms, not humans.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Static Bertrand VBE is purely analytical.,1,"One-shot 2-firm differentiated Bertrand game with linear demands q1 = a − b p1 + c p2, q2 = a − b p2 + c p1, b ≥ c > 0, marginal costs 0. Unique Nash equilibrium price: p^N = a / (2b − c). Collusive price that maximizes joint profit: p^* = a / (2b − 2c). From Melkonyan et al. (2018), unique VBE price: p^V = a(2b + c) / (2(2b^2 − c^2)), with p^N < p^V < p^* and corresponding joint profits between Nash and collusion.",Collects formulas given in the Appendix section on Bertrand competition.,1
10.1016_j.jmp.2021.102640.md,Repeated Bertrand competition – Virtual Bargaining Equilibrium condition,"Appendix discusses finitely repeated Bertrand competition and notes that in any VBE, firms choose the collusive price vector p^* each period, enforced by punishment strategies (e.g., grim trigger).",0.9,Game structure: finitely repeated 2-firm differentiated Bertrand game with same linear demands; virtual bargaining equilibrium in dynamic game with collusive price p^* enforced by punishments,"Key independent modeling features: repeated interaction, VBE in extensive-form game with commitment and trigger punishments.",0.9,False,No repeated-Bertrand experiments reported; only theoretical results.,1,False,No data or randomization.,1,False,Pure theory.,1,False,"Dynamic VBE is derived analytically, not via simulation.",0.96,True,Appendix formally describes VBE and SVBE outcomes for the repeated Bertrand game.,1,2,Two firms.,1,3,"They assume n ≥ 3; exact n not fixed, so we set to the minimal 3 with low confidence.",0.3,1,Action is choosing a real-valued price; no contribution constraint.,0.2,N/A,No contribution endowments.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No humans or interface.,1,0,No feedback interface.,0.8,N/A,No punishment mechanism with IDs; punishments are just price cuts within strategies.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,"Finite horizon n assumed common knowledge among firms, but not displayed.",0.8,0,"Punishment is operationalized as switching to low Bertrand prices in future periods, not as a separate costly action.",0.9,N/A,No unit punishment technology; punishments are part of pricing strategies.,1,N/A,No punishment operator.,1,0,Collusive prices generate higher profits but are not modeled as rewards.,1,N/A,No separate reward action.,1,N/A,No separate reward action.,1,N/R,Firms’ costs and demand parameters are given; no financial endowment.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome is collusive pricing, not a contribution behavior.",1,N/R,No contribution metric.,1,N/R,"They note that VBE yields first-best joint profits for firms vs SVBE and SPE, but no efficiency relative to social welfare is computed.",0.7,N/R,Joint profits at p^* per period are implied but not numerically reported.,0.7,N/A,Firms are theoretical actors.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,No human,Repeated Bertrand model is entirely analytical.,1,"Finite-horizon repeated version of the differentiated Bertrand duopoly with linear demands q1 = a − b p1 + c p2, q2 = a − b p2 + c p1, b ≥ c > 0, zero marginal cost. In SPE, both firms set Nash price p^N in every period. Appendix shows that in any VBE, firms tacitly collude on the joint-profit-maximizing price p^* in every period, enforced by punishment strategies such as grim trigger (reverting to p^N for the rest of the game after a deviation).",Summarizes the VBE description for repeated Bertrand in the Appendix.,1
10.1016_j.jmp.2021.102640.md,Repeated Bertrand competition – Subgame Virtual Bargaining condition,"Appendix states that in the unique SVBE of the repeated Bertrand game, firms choose the virtual bargaining equilibrium price of the static game p^V in each period and thus achieve an intermediate collusive outcome.",0.9,Game structure: same finitely repeated 2-firm differentiated Bertrand game; subgame virtual bargaining equilibrium (SVBE) with period-by-period VBE pricing p^V,"Independent feature is the SVBE solution concept applied to the repeated game, implying myopic virtual bargaining in each stage.",0.9,False,No data collected.,1,False,Theoretical characterization only.,1,False,No lab or field site.,1,False,No simulation runs reported.,0.96,True,SVBE behavior is deduced analytically in the Appendix.,1,2,Two firms.,1,3,Same n ≥ 3 assumption as VBE repeated Bertrand condition; exact n unspecified.,0.3,1,Firms choose continuous prices.,0.2,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,No experimental interface.,0.8,N/A,No punishment mechanism separate from pricing strategies.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Finite horizon is assumed knowledge to firms but not displayed.,0.8,0,No dedicated punishment action; firms simply play p^V each stage.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,Higher prices generate profits but not modeled as optional rewards.,1,N/A,No reward operator.,1,N/A,No reward operator.,1,N/R,Firms’ conditions identical to previous Bertrand conditions.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome is an intermediate collusive price level, not a contribution.",1,N/R,No contribution.,1,N/R,"They state SVBE joint profits fall between SPE (Nash every period) and VBE (full collusion), but no numeric efficiency ratio given.",0.7,N/R,Joint profits at p^V per period are implicitly known but not numerically reported as DV.,0.7,N/A,"Firms are theoretical, not real participants.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Repeated Bertrand SVBE is modeled analytically only.,1,"Same repeated differentiated Bertrand structure as in the VBE repeated Bertrand condition. Appendix notes that under SVBE, firms cannot commit to multi-period punishments and instead choose in each period the VBE of the static Bertrand game, p^V. This yields collusion that is weaker than under VBE (p^*) but stronger than under the SPE (p^N).","Summarizes Appendix discussion: ‘In the unique SVBE, the two firms choose the virtual bargaining equilibrium of the static game in each period… prices and joint profits under SVBE fall strictly between those under subgame perfect and VBE.’",1
