ding UCPun participants.",1,"36% male, 64% female",Applies to overall subject pool; no treatment-specific breakdown given.,1,University students; economics and related majors excluded,Same recruitment criteria for all treatments.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at University of Jena.,1,"Same basic one-shot linear public good with strategy-method elicitation. All subjects also submit conditional punishment tables (0–10) as a function of a counterpart’s contribution. In UCPun, after roles are realized, the computer randomly selects one of the three UDMs; that player’s punishment table is used to punish the CDM, conditional on the CDM’s realized contribution. Punishment is costless and capped at 10 points.",Based on treatment description in Section 2 and detailed screen/instruction text for UCPun.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,Three-role model – Baseline system (no sanctions),"Paper defines a three-role model (generators, ITF, landfill sites) and first analyzes the baseline system without sanctions.",1,"Sanction system: none (baseline, no fines or manifest-based sanctions)","Independent variable across conditions is the type of sanction system; here, no sanctions are applied.",1,False,Study is a mathematical model using replicator dynamics with firms as players; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; purely theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory setting; analytical model only.,1,True,Authors state they carried out numerical (Monte Carlo) simulations of the replicator dynamics.,1,True,Core of the paper is derivation and analysis of replicator equations and equilibrium conditions.,1,3,"Three roles (generator group, ITF group, landfill site group) are modeled as interacting player populations.",1,1,"Model represents a one-shot interaction structure per encounter; evolutionary change occurs continuously, not in discrete game rounds.",0.7,1,Players choose between cooperation and defection but also face continuous payoffs; no evidence of discrete all-or-nothing contribution caps as in PGG.,0.6,N/A,Not a public goods game with endowments; model uses cost/benefit parameters instead.,1,N/R,No linear public-goods multiplier or MPCR is defined; payoffs are role-specific costs and benefits.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication component.,1,0,Model does not include any informational feedback or summary displays; only payoffs via equations.,1,N/A,No peer punishment or identity revelation; only centralized environmental costs.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,0,No notion of displayed finite rounds in the continuous-time replicator model.,1,1,"Environmental restoration fees r*g_i and s*r*g_i are imposed on dumpers by the local administrative organ, functioning as centralized sanctions.",1,N/R,Per-unit cost to an enforcing player is not modeled; only fines/restoration fees on violators are specified.,1,N/R,No conversion rate from punisher expenditure to target loss is defined.,1,0,"Model includes only costs/fines, no positive rewards.",1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/R,"No token endowments are defined; payoffs are parameterized by b, x1, etc.",1,N/R,"Outcomes are equilibrium strategy frequencies, not normalized contribution rates to a public good.",1,N/R,No discrete contribution amounts; only binary strategies and continuous payoffs.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency as payoff relative to a maximum cooperative benchmark.,1,N/R,Total group payoff is not aggregated as a primary reported dependent variable; focus is on stability of equilibria.,1,N/A,No human participants; firms are theoretical players.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analysis is purely mathematical/simulation-based with no empirical data collection.,1,"Asymmetric three-population evolutionary game with generators (cooperator/defector), ITF (C-C, C-D, D-C, D-D), and landfill sites (cooperator/defector), interacting sequentially along a linear division of labor with environmental damage from illegal dumping.",Summarizes the structure given in the Baseline system in the three-role model section.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,Three-role model – Actor responsibility system,Paper introduces an actor responsibility system where detected illegal dumpers are fined f with detection probability d; this is analyzed separately from the baseline.,1,Sanction system: actor responsibility (central fine f applied probabilistically d to each dumper in any role),"Condition differs from baseline only by the addition of expected fine d*f in payoff matrices A1, A2, C1, C2, B1.",1,False,No human participants; firms are modeled as strategy types in an evolutionary game.,1,False,"It is a theoretical intervention in the model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,Not conducted in a physical or online lab with people.,1,True,Authors report Monte Carlo simulations and numerical analyses for all three systems including this one.,1,True,The actor responsibility system is incorporated via modified payoff matrices and analyzed using replicator equations.,1,3,"Same three-role structure (generator, ITF, LS) as baseline.",1,1,Same continuous-time evolutionary framework as baseline; no repeated finite rounds.,0.7,1,Strategy space unchanged from baseline; players choose cooperation vs defection types without partial contributions.,0.6,N/A,Not a PGG with initial endowments; strategic choice is legal treatment vs illegal dumping.,1,N/R,No multiplier of a shared public good is specified.,1,N/A,No agents with communication; pure payoff dynamics.,1,0,No summary information mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,Central fines do not involve identification decisions between strategic players.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,"Evolutionary, not finite-round play; no round counter.",1,1,"In addition to baseline environmental restoration fees, a fine f with probability d is imposed on any dumper; modeled as expected value d*f in payoffs.",1,N/R,Cost to the sanctioning authority is not modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit mapping from enforcement spending to fine magnitude; f and d are exogenous.,1,0,"Only fines, no positive incentives.",1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,Endowments are not described; payoffs start from production benefit b and costs.,1,N/R,"Outcomes tracked as equilibria (e.g., (C,C-C,C)) not as normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions reported.,1,N/R,"Paper compares stability of cooperation vs defection, not efficiency ratios.",1,N/R,Does not report aggregate payoffs of all three roles as a DV.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Mathematical model with simulations; no field or lab data.,1,"Same three-role asymmetric game as baseline, but illegal dumping in any role is additionally sanctioned by an expected fine d*f. Detection probability d is small, and analysis shows this system has limited impact when d*f is negligible.","Derived from Actor responsibility system in the three-role model section, including modified payoff matrices.",1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,Three-role model – Producer responsibility system,"Paper defines a producer responsibility system using manifests, where generators are fined f1 when manifests are not returned; payoffs and dynamics are analyzed separately.",1,"Sanction system: producer responsibility (manifest-based fine f1 imposed on generators conditional on probabilities p2, p3, p4, q3, q4)",Condition adds expected fines F1 and F2 onto generator payoffs A1 and A2 based on manifest return probabilities; differs from baseline and actor-responsibility regimes.,1,False,It is a purely theoretical evolutionary game with no subjects.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with participants; only model parameter changes.,1,False,Not a lab or online experiment.,1,True,Monte Carlo and numerical simulations of replicator dynamics are reported also for this system.,1,True,Closed-form equilibrium and stability conditions are derived for this sanction regime.,1,3,Same three interacting role-populations as in the other three-role conditions.,1,1,"Continuous-time evolutionary adjustment over repeated generations; no discrete, known number of rounds per game.",0.7,1,Choice structure (cooperate vs defect in each role) unchanged by introduction of manifests.,0.6,N/A,No endowment allocation; interactions are contractual and environmental.,1,N/R,Model is not framed as a linear public good with MPCR.,1,N/A,No communication channel exists in the model.,1,0,"Manifest flow is a contractual/legal mechanism, not informational feedback about others' payoffs.",1,N/A,Only the generator is fined when the manifest is missing; identity revelation between strategic peers is not modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards are part of this system.,1,0,No explicit round feedback; replicator dynamics over continuous time.,1,1,Generators pay a fine f1 when manifests are not returned; expected fines F1 and F2 are added to generator payoffs.,1,N/R,Resource cost to the local administrative organ is not in the equations.,1,N/R,"Mechanism is defined by parameters f1 and probabilities p2, p3, p4, q3, q4 but not as a mapping from punisher cost to damage.",1,0,No positive incentives are absent; only punishments via manifest failure.,1,N/A,There are no rewards.,1,N/A,There are no rewards.,1,N/R,"No tokenized endowment; only benefits b and costs x1, c_mid, etc.",1,N/R,"The main outcomes are stability of cooperative vs dumping equilibria (e.g., (C,C-C,C)), not contribution fractions.",1,N/R,Model does not measure contributions in units; it uses strategy labels and parameters.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not numerically computed; only qualitative comparison of cooperation promotion between sanction systems.,1,N/R,Total payoff across roles is not reported as a core outcome.,1,N/A,No human participants in the model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The producer responsibility system is evaluated through mathematical analysis and simulations only.,1,"Same asymmetric three-role industrial waste dumping game, augmented with a manifest chain. Generators, ITFs, and landfill sites must pass manifests; failure leads to generator fines f1 with probabilities determined by p2, p3, p4 (defectors failing to pass manifests) and q3, q4 (accidental failures by cooperators). Analysis compares stability of cooperative equilibria (e.g., (C,C-C,C)) versus dumping under this system and finds conditions where it promotes cooperation more than the baseline and actor responsibility systems.",Summarizes Producer responsibility system in the three-role model and subsequent comparison results.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,No-role model – Baseline system,Appendix A defines a no-role model with only generators who can treat waste themselves; first analyzed without sanctions.,1,"Role structure and sanction system: single-role (generators only), no fines (baseline)",Independent variable here is both the absence of division of labor (no-role) and absence of fines; this is the reference for the single-role case.,0.9,False,"Purely analytical replicator model, no subjects.",1,False,"No experimental protocol, only theoretical analysis.",1,False,Not run with human participants in a lab.,1,False,Appendix A only presents analytical results for the no-role model; simulations are discussed later for three-role models.,0.8,True,Replicator equation is solved analytically: u=0 is stable regardless of parameters.,1,1,Only generators exist; ITF and landfill roles are absent in the no-role model.,1,1,"Single population with continuous-time evolutionary adjustment, no discrete repeated rounds.",0.7,1,Each generator chooses between cooperation (legal treatment) and defection (illegal dumping); there is no gradation of contribution.,0.9,N/A,No endowment or public account structure.,1,N/R,This is not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No agents or communication.,1,0,No information feedback is modeled.,1,N/A,No punishment between strategic players; only environmental damage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,"Infinite-horizon evolutionary dynamics, not finite repeated rounds.",1,1,"Environmental restoration expense r*g is imposed on all generators as external cost, analogous to a central sanction.",0.9,N/R,Enforcer’s cost is not modeled.,1,N/R,No transformation rate between spending and fine is given.,1,0,There are no rewards; only costs and benefits.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Model is framed in terms of production benefit b and treatment cost c, not as an endowment.",1,N/R,"Outcome is whether cooperation can evolve (stability of u=1 vs u=0), not a reported fraction.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts quantified.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric provided.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoff is not given as a dependent variable.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical population model without data collection.,1,"Single-population evolutionary game where generators choose between legal treatment (cooperation, cost c) and illegal dumping (defection) causing environmental damage r*g affecting all. Tragedy of the commons holds when c/r < g, leading to stable full defection (u=0).",Summarizes Appendix A baseline no-role model description.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,No-role model – Actor responsibility system,Appendix A extends the no-role model by adding an actor responsibility fine f with detection probability d to defecting generators.,1,"Role structure and sanction system: single-role (generators only), actor responsibility fine d*f on defectors",Condition differs from no-role baseline by introducing expected fine d*f against illegal dumping.,1,False,Mathematical analysis only.,1,False,No experimental design with participants.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,No simulations are described for the no-role actor-responsibility extension; results are analytical.,0.8,True,Stability conditions u=1 stable if d*f>c and u=0 stable otherwise are derived analytically.,1,1,Only generators are modeled.,1,1,Continuous replicator dynamics with no discrete rounds.,0.7,1,Generators choose cooperate vs defect; no partial contributions.,0.9,N/A,No endowment/public account structure.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,No agents communicating.,1,0,No informational feedback channel.,1,N/A,Central authority punishes; strategic identification is not modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Infinite horizon evolutionary model.,1,1,Defectors pay expected fine d*f in addition to environmental cost r*g(1-u).,1,N/R,Enforcement costs are not captured.,1,N/R,Only fine magnitude and detection probability are parameters; no tech mapping.,1,0,Only punitive fines; no rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments are not specified.,1,N/R,"Outcome is stability of cooperative vs defective monomorphic populations, not a numeric rate.",1,N/R,No contributions are measured.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not calculated.,1,N/R,Group payoff not reported.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,The no-role actor responsibility model is purely analytical.,1,"Same single-population illegal dumping social dilemma as no-role baseline, with an added expected fine d*f on defectors. Cooperation (u=1) is stable if d*f > c; otherwise defection (u=0) remains stable.",Summarizes Appendix A actor responsibility section.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,No-role model – Producer responsibility system,Appendix A further extends the no-role model by adding a producer responsibility fine f1 with probability p on defectors who fail to submit manifests.,1,"Role structure and sanction system: single-role (generators only), producer responsibility fine p*f1 on defectors",Condition differs from prior no-role conditions by replacing actor responsibility fine with manifest-based fine p*f1.,1,False,Theoretical model; no data.,1,False,No experimental procedures.,1,False,Not in a laboratory.,1,False,Appendix A solution is analytical; no simulations described for this variant.,0.8,True,Condition for cooperation (u=1 stable if p*f1>c) is derived analytically from modified payoffs.,1,1,Only generators exist.,1,1,Replicator dynamics with continuous time.,0.7,1,Binary choice of legal treatment vs illegal dumping.,0.9,N/A,No resource endowment structure.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,No feedback summaries.,1,N/A,Central fine; no peer punishment or identity revelation.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary model.,1,1,Defectors pay an expected manifest-based fine p*f1 in addition to environmental damage.,1,N/R,No costs for the authority are modeled.,1,N/R,"Fine magnitude and probability are exogenous, no enforcement technology is modeled.",1,0,Only negative sanctions exist.,1,N/A,No reward regime.,1,N/A,No reward regime.,1,N/R,Endowment is not specified.,1,N/R,"DV is binary stability of cooperation vs defection, not a reported rate.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts are computed.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics not provided.,1,N/R,Group payoff is not reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical extension of the no-role model.,1,"Same no-role illegal dumping dilemma, with a manifest-based producer responsibility fine f1 applied to defectors with probability p (defector fails to submit real manifest). Cooperation (u=1) is stable if p*f1 > c; otherwise defection dominates. Comparison suggests that when d is very small and p is high, producer responsibility can outperform actor responsibility in promoting cooperation.",Summarizes producer responsibility part of Appendix A.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,Two-role model – Baseline system,"Appendix B introduces a two-role model with generators and landfill sites only, first analyzed without fines.",1,"Role structure and sanction system: two roles (generators and landfill sites), no fines (baseline)",Independent variation from three-role models is role reduction to generators and landfill sites; this is the baseline without added fines.,1,False,The two-role model is a theoretical evolutionary game.,1,False,No hands-on experiment.,1,False,Not conducted with participants.,1,False,Appendix B shows only analytical derivations for the two-role model.,0.8,True,Replicator equations for u1 and v1 are solved and equilibria characterized analytically.,1,2,Generators and landfill sites are the two interacting populations.,1,1,Continuous-time replicator model; no discrete repeated finite rounds.,0.7,1,Both generators and landfill sites choose between cooperate and defect; no partial contributions.,0.9,N/A,No public good endowment allocation.,1,N/R,Not framed as a linear PGG.,1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No informational display mechanisms modeled.,1,N/A,No peer punishment.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary model.,1,1,Environmental restoration expense s*r*g0 on generators and r*g0 on landfill sites functions as a centralized sanction against dumping.,0.9,N/R,Cost of imposing these expenses is not modeled.,1,N/R,No enforcement technology parametric mapping.,1,0,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,"Endowment not specified; payoffs use b, x1, ct parameters.",1,N/R,"DV of interest is stability of cooperative equilibrium (u1=1, v1=1) vs dumping equilibria.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts given.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoff is not reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Two-role model is analytical, with no empirical data.",1,"Asymmetric two-population game: generators choose to cooperate (commit waste to landfill at cost x1) or defect (illegal dumping), landfill sites choose to cooperate (treat waste at cost ct) or defect (illegal dumping). Environmental restoration cost s*r*g0 (generators) and r*g0 (landfill sites) applies to dumpers. Three equilibria exist: (1,1), (1,0), (0,*) with stability depending on comparisons of x1, ct, and g0.",Summarizes Appendix B baseline two-role model.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,Two-role model – Actor responsibility system,Appendix B adds an actor responsibility fine f with detection probability d to the two-role model’s dumpers.,1,"Role structure and sanction system: two roles (generators, landfill sites), actor responsibility fine d*f on dumpers",Condition changes only by including expected fine d*f in payoffs to both generator and landfill defector strategies.,1,False,Theoretical model only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field subjects.,1,False,Appendix B describes only analytical solutions for the two-role actor responsibility system.,0.8,True,Replicator dynamics with modified payoffs including d*f are solved for equilibrium conditions.,1,2,Generators and landfill sites remain the two roles.,1,1,Same replicator framework as other analytical variants.,0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect strategies for both roles.,0.9,N/A,No endowment division.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No summary info channel.,1,N/A,Central authority punishes dumpers; identity revelation is not strategic.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary model.,1,1,"Defectors in either role now face added expected fine d*f, on top of baseline environmental costs.",1,N/R,Enforcement costs not modeled.,1,N/R,No mapping from enforcement effort to d or f is given.,1,0,Only punishment present.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,Endowment not defined.,1,N/R,"DV is stability of cooperative equilibrium (1,1) under conditions involving ct, x1, d*f, etc.",1,N/R,Contributions not numerically reported.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical extension of the two-role model.,1,"In the two-role setting (generators, landfill sites), actor responsibility adds an expected fine d*f to any dumper. Cooperation equilibrium (u1=1,v1=1) is stable if (ct - d*f)/r < g0 and (x1 - d*f)/(s*r) < g0, while (1,0) is stable when (ct - d*f)/r > g0 and x1 - d*f < 0.",Summarizes actor responsibility part of Appendix B.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2017.10.007.md,Two-role model – Producer responsibility system,"Appendix B modifies the two-role model with a producer responsibility system where fines f1 are conditioned by manifest-related probabilities p1, p2, q1.",1,"Role structure and sanction system: two roles (generators, landfill sites), producer responsibility fine f1 with probabilities p1, p2, q1","Condition changes from two-role baseline by adding expected fines involving f1, p1, p2, q1 in generator payoffs.",1,False,No empirical data; purely analytical.,1,False,No experimental procedures.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,False,Appendix B presents only equations and stability conditions for this variant.,0.8,True,"Stability conditions for equilibria (1,1), (1,0), (0,*) are derived analytically with parameters f1, p1, p2, q1.",1,2,"Same two roles (generators, landfill sites).",1,1,Continuous-time replicator dynamics.,0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect strategies remain.,0.9,N/A,No endowment splitting.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,No summary information mechanism.,1,N/A,Central mechanism; identification between strategic players not relevant.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary model.,1,1,Generators face expected fines involving f1 and manifest failure probabilities; landfill payoffs unchanged.,1,N/R,Cost of the fine-imposing authority not modeled.,1,N/R,No mapping of enforcement effort to sanction magnitude.,1,0,No reward system.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/R,No fixed endowment is specified.,1,N/R,"Again, DV is equilibrium stability, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,No contributions measured.,1,N/R,No efficiency computations provided.,1,N/R,Group payoff is not presented.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Fully analytical treatment of the two-role producer responsibility case.,1,"Two-role asymmetric game with manifest-based producer responsibility. Generators incur extra expected costs in payoffs A via terms involving f1, p1 (ls-defector not handing manifest back), p2 (g-defector not handing manifest to authority), and q1 (ls-cooperator accidentally failing). Landfill site payoffs remain as in baseline. Equilibria (1,1), (1,0), (0,*) have stability conditions depending on these parameters, with (1,1) stable when ct/r < g0 and (x1 - f1(p2 - q1))/(s*r) < g0.",Summarizes producer responsibility part of Appendix B.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2014.09.001.md,BASE – Rounds 1–10,"Baseline treatment with α=1.2, β=0.5 in the first sequence of ten rounds.",1,"Treatment: BASE (α_1-10 = 1.2, β_1-10 = 0.5); Phase: rounds 1–10 (pre-change)",Condition defined by parameter combination and time segment before any change.,1,True,Human subjects in a computerized public-good experiment.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized lab experiment with treatments.",1,True,Conducted in Lakelab at University of Konstanz.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,No purely analytical or formal model as main method.,1,4,Groups of four subjects in all treatments.,1,10,First sequence of ten rounds considered as this condition.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer contribution from 0 to 20.,1,0,Endowment starts fully in private account; subjects actively move points to project.,1,0.3,α/4 with α=1.2 gives 1.2/4 = 0.3 marginal per capita return.,1,0,Instructions explicitly state decisions made without possibility of communicating.,1,1,"End-of-stage summaries include others’ contributions, incomes, punishment given and received.",1,0,Identities anonymized and randomly relabeled each round; only role numbers shown.,1,0,No reward mechanism in design.,1,1,Participants told there are 20 rounds and that after 10 a parameter might change.,1,1,Stage 2 allows assigning deduction (punishment) points.,1,0.5,Baseline punishment cost parameter β=0.5 in all rounds.,1,1,Each punishment point lowers target’s income by 1 unit.,1,0,Design only includes deduction points; no positive transfers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each participant receives 20 points at beginning of each round.,1,N/R,Paper reports average contributions qualitatively; no exact numeric average for BASE rounds 1–10.,0.3,N/R,Only descriptive phrases like ‘about 50 percent of the endowment’; no exact mean provided.,0.3,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency ratios reported.,0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes per treatment and phase.,0.1,Germany,Experiment conducted at University of Konstanz (Germany) with local student subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Only described as students; no age statistics given.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,University students (various disciplines),States 164 students from various disciplines participated.,1,On site,Lab sessions in Lakelab with subjects physically present.,1,"Repeated linear public-good game with fixed partners, 2-stage rounds (contribution then punishment). Base parameters: α=1.2, β=0.5 in all rounds; additional 90-point initial endowment for possible losses; belief elicitation before rounds 1 and 11 (unincentivized).",Summarizes structural details described under design and instructions for BASE condition.,1
