ibutions falling between rounds 11–15 and 16–20.",Summarizes key behavioral differences driven by change in punishment cost.,1
10.1016_j.amc.2022.127612.md,"Analytical and numerical replicator dynamics with SC, C, and D in an infinite well-mixed population","Paper presents a single evolutionary game model (SC, C, D) analyzed by replicator dynamics plus numerical simulations over parameters b, alpha1, alpha2, gamma in an infinite well-mixed population.",1,"Temptation to defect b (1 < b ≤ 2), reward strength α1 (0 ≤ α1 ≤ 1), punishment strength α2 (0 ≤ α2 ≤ 1), and SC cost γ (0 ≤ γ ≤ 1) varied in the payoff matrix and replicator dynamics.","Model explicitly defines these four parameters and varies them systematically in analytical conditions and numerical figures (e.g., b ∈ {1.4,1.6,1.8}, α1, α2 ∈ [0,1], γ fixed at 0.2 for simulations).",1,False,"Study uses infinite well-mixed population, replicator equations, and numerical simulations without human or animal participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; work is theoretical plus numerical analysis of differential equations.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; purely mathematical/simulation-based.,1,True,"Section 3.2 explicitly states ""numerical simulations"" of the replicator dynamics in the simplex S3 over parameter combinations.",1,True,Section 3.1 derives equilibria and their stability analytically using replicator equations and Jacobian analysis.,1,2,Base interaction is a pairwise weak prisoner's dilemma (2x2 extended to 3x3 with SC) as shown in Tables 1 and 2.,1,1,"Evolution modeled in continuous time via replicator dynamics on strategy frequencies; underlying game is a one-shot pairwise PD repeatedly played via population mixing, not discrete repeated rounds. Use 1 as per single-stage payoff matrix.",0.6,1,"Strategies are discrete types (SC, C, D) with fixed behavior rather than variable contribution levels; contribution is not a continuous decision variable, so players do not choose partial contributions. Treat as all-or-nothing (pure strategies).",0.7,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment allocation between private/public accounts; only payoffs per interaction are modeled.,1,N/R,"Model is based on prisoner's dilemma payoff matrix, not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.",1,N/A,No human players or communication; infinite well-mixed analytical model.,1,0,No information about displaying others’ earnings or punishments; not part of the theoretical model.,1,N/A,Punishment is encoded in payoff parameters α2 and γ; there are no identities or information display assumptions.,1,N/A,Rewards are encoded via α1; no notion of revealed identities.,1,0,No rounds or user interface; continuous-time dynamical system.,1,1,Punishment is modeled by parameter α2 reducing defectors’ payoffs when interacting with SC; section 2 describes this as punishment.,1,N/R,"SC pays a generic cost γ whenever it exposes, but no per-unit punishment cost in the sense of a linear punishment technology is defined; cannot map to a single 'coins per unit' number.",0.4,N/R,"Payoff matrix entries encode combined effects of punishment and cost (e.g., D vs SC: D gets b−α2); no separate per-unit mapping from punishment expenditure to target loss is given.",0.4,1,Reward mechanism via α1 that increases payoffs when cooperators interact with SC or each other is explicitly modeled.,1,N/R,"The SC’s cost γ is paid for obtaining exposure qualification, not explicitly decomposed as per-unit cost of reward to others.",0.4,N/R,"Rewards appear directly as additive payoff α1, without describing how much reward per unit of cost is allocated.",0.4,N/R,"No token endowment or budget per player is specified; only normalized payoff values in the PD matrix (e.g., 1, b, 0).",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported are equilibrium fractions of strategy types (SC, C, D), not contribution rates relative to an endowment.",1,N/R,Model does not track numerical contributions; C and SC are simply cooperative strategies with fixed behavior.,1,N/R,"Paper focuses on strategy frequencies and stability, not aggregate efficiency normalized to a maximum payoff.",1,N/R,"Average payoffs for strategies are expressions in x, y, z, but no explicit aggregate group payoff statistics are reported as main results.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; theoretical model only.,1,No human,Study is based on replicator dynamics and numerical simulations in an infinite well-mixed population with no human subjects.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population playing a weak Prisoner’s Dilemma extended to three strategies: punishing-evil-and-promoting-good (SC), ordinary cooperation (C), and defection (D). Payoff matrix includes parameters: temptation to defect b (1 < b ≤ 2), reward α1, punishment α2, and SC cost γ. Dynamics governed by three-strategy replicator equations with analytical equilibrium and stability analysis plus numerical phase portraits and parameter sweeps.",Summarizes key structural aspects of the game and dynamics explicitly defined in Sections 2 and 3.,1
