10.1016_j.jmateco.2013.07.003.md,Analytical model of infinitely repeated random-matching Prisoners’ Dilemma with contagious grim-trigger punishment and monitoring parameter a,"Paper presents a single formal model parameterized by population size N, discount factor β, and monitoring breadth a (0 = private, N−2 = public). There are no distinct experimental treatments with human subjects; instead, conditions are analytical cases of the same model.",0.99,"Monitoring breadth a determining size of observation set O_i(t,a) (a = 0: private monitoring; 1 ≤ a ≤ N−2: imperfect public monitoring; a = N−2: public monitoring). Additional parameters varied in comparative statics: discount factor β, payoff parameters (c,d,g,l), economy size N, and public randomization probability q.","Section 2 defines a as the key monitoring parameter. Sections 5–6 analyze how equilibrium conditions (β*, l(β,k), etc.) vary with β, N, γ=g/(c−d), l, and q (public randomization device).",0.97,False,"The work is purely theoretical: no human participants, no data collection; all results are analytical derivations about continuation payoffs and equilibrium conditions.",1,False,No controlled experiment is run; the paper cites experiments elsewhere but only develops theoretical results.,1,False,No lab or field implementation; only theory.,1,False,"Although transition matrices and contact rates are discussed, there is no numerical or agent-based simulation; results are closed-form or analytical.",0.98,True,"Model is formulated and solved with matrices, recursive equations, and proofs; all results are closed-form or analytical characterizations.",1,2,"Stage game is a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma; population size is N agents randomly paired into n = N/2 matches, but each game is bilateral.",1,-1,Game is infinitely repeated / indefinite duration with continuation probability β each period; finite round count is not defined.,1,0,"Players choose a discrete action C or D; there is no continuous contribution choice, so the all-or-nothing contribution concept does not apply in the public-goods sense.",0.7,N/A,No endowment allocation or contribution decision as in a public goods game; only C/D actions in a Prisoner’s Dilemma.,1,N/R,"No linear public goods structure or MPCR is defined; payoffs are specified directly as (d, d−l, d+g, c).",1,0,Agents are anonymous and cannot communicate; stated in the model description.,1,0,"Agents may observe others’ actions in O_i(t,a) but there is no mention of payoff or punishment/reward summaries being shown.",0.95,0,"Agents cannot observe identities of others (anonymity); thus, any defection/punishment is not linked to a persistent identity.",1,0,No reward mechanism is modeled; only C/D actions and contagious punishment via defection.,1,0,Game length is indefinite with continuation probability β; no indication that players observe a remaining-horizon counter (theoretical agents simply know β).,0.9,1,"Grim-trigger social norm implements decentralized, unforgiving punishment: upon observing any defection, agents switch permanently to D; this is the core punishment mechanism studied.",1,N/A,"Punishment is endogenous to the strategy (switching to D), not a separate costly decision with a per-unit cost parameter as in a standard punishment stage.",0.95,N/A,"There is no explicit punishment technology (e.g., each punishment point reduces others’ payoffs by X); punishment is simply future play of D causing lower continuation payoffs.",0.95,0,No positive sanctioning or reward choice is modeled; only cooperation C and defection/punishment D.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoffs of the stage game are given directly as (d, d−l, d+g, c); no initial token endowment per round is specified.",1,N/A,"No contribution level is modeled; action space is {C,D} in a Prisoner’s Dilemma, and the paper does not report empirical contribution rates.",1,N/A,No experimental contribution amounts are measured; theoretical results concern payoffs and equilibrium conditions.,1,N/R,"Although the efficient outcome (C,C) is identified, the paper does not compute or report an efficiency metric as total payoff over maximum possible for empirical data.",0.8,N/R,"Theoretical payoffs per player are given, but no empirical group payoff statistics are reported; the schema requests realized group payoff as a DV, which is absent.",0.9,N/A,No human participants; pure theory.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of analytical derivations without any human-subject component.,1,"Infinite-horizon random-matching Prisoners’ Dilemma with N = 2n ≥ 4 anonymous agents. Each period, agents are randomly paired to play a binary-action stage game with payoffs π_DD = d, π_CD = d − l, π_DC = d + g, π_CC = c where c > d, g > 0, l ≥ 0. Agents discount future with common factor β ∈ (0,1). Monitoring structure: each agent observes actions of self, current opponent, and a additional anonymous agents O_i(t,a), with a ∈ {0,…,N−2}; a = 0 is private monitoring, a = N−2 is public monitoring. Agents follow a grim-trigger social norm: start in state C, play C unless they ever observe D in their observation set, then switch to state D forever (unless modified by public randomization in Section 6.2). Contagious punishment spreads via random matching and observation, captured by an upper-triangular transition matrix Q over the number of defectors k. Key theoretical object is the contact rate φ_k, the normalized expected rate at which a defector meets cooperators over the continuation game, used to express continuation payoffs as convex combinations of static payoffs and to derive bounds on β and l for sustaining cooperation under various monitoring regimes and with/without a public randomization device.","Summarizes the formal game, strategy (grim trigger), matching and monitoring process, and main analytical constructs used in the paper.",0.99
10.1098_rspb.2018.1508.md,"Simulation scenario Sc (coop-rep only, baseline parameters)","Paper defines three simulation scenarios: Sc (only coop-rep), Sp (only pun-rep), Scp (both). Baseline parameters listed in Methods and referred to in Results and Table 2.",1,"Reputation system availability: only cooperation-based reputation (coop-rep) available; other parameters fixed at baseline (N=100, m=30, r=1.6, k=0.2, p=0.5, ρ=0.01, φ=0.05, γ=0.3, μ=0.01).",Scenario Sc is defined as the condition where players may use only coop-rep. Baseline parameter values are taken from Table 1 and text 'baseline specification with γ=0.3 and ρ=0.01'.,1,False,Study uses computer simulations of evolutionary dynamics; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational model.,1,False,No lab setting; only in silico simulations.,1,True,Author explicitly states 'Using computer simulations' and describes repeated simulations over generations.,1,False,Analytical results are in a separate subsection (b); this row represents the simulation scenario.,1,2,Each interaction is between two individuals in a Prisoner's Dilemma; 'equally divided within each pair'.,1,30,Each individual faces an expected m=30 interactions during lifetime; used as effective number of rounds per individual.,0.8,1,"Players decide whether or not to contribute a fixed amount y; binary choice all-or-nothing, but schema defines 1 as 'can contribute any amount'; using 1 to denote non-all-or-nothing would be inaccurate, so set 0 for all-or-nothing and 1 otherwise. Here choice is strictly binary, so all-or-nothing=0.",0.7,N/A,"Model is a Prisoner's Dilemma framed as contributing y, not a standard PGG with an initial endowment allocation.",1,0.8,"Public good multiplier r=1.6, group size=2; MPCR=r/2=0.8.",1,N/A,"No human subjects; gossip is a modelled mechanism, not participant communication.",1,0,No explicit display of others’ earnings or summaries in the simulation description.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; identity revelation is not a UI feature.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modelled; no human interface.,1,0,Agents do not know the number of interactions that have occurred; total rounds unknown to them.,1,1,"Second stage allows punishment at cost k>0, inflicting p on co-player.",1,0.2,Table 1 lists k=0.2 as cost of punishing.,1,2.5,Each punishment costs k=0.2 and inflicts p=0.5; ratio p/k=0.5/0.2=2.5.,1,0,Model only includes costly punishment; no rewarding stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Contribution amount y is referenced but its numerical value is not given.,0,N/R,Results report 'share of cooperative acts' qualitatively and in figures but not as a single baseline scalar for this condition.,0.2,N/R,No absolute contribution amount reported; only frequencies of cooperation.,0,N/R,No reported ratio of realized payoffs to maximum possible.,0,N/R,Only qualitative discussion of payoffs and evolutionary success; no numeric group payoff.,0,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computer simulation only.,1,Two-player Prisoner's Dilemma framed as public good contribution y with multiplier r=1.6 and punishment stage; costly reputation acquisition via gossip about coop-rep only.,Summarized from Methods; this scenario restricts reputation use to coop-rep.,1
10.1098_rspb.2018.1508.md,"Simulation scenario Sp (pun-rep only, baseline parameters)",Scenario Sp defined where only pun-rep can be used; baseline parameters as in Table 1 and text.,1,"Reputation system availability: only punishment-based reputation (pun-rep) available; other parameters fixed at baseline (N=100, m=30, r=1.6, k=0.2, p=0.5, ρ=0.01, φ=0.05, γ=0.3, μ=0.01).",Text: 'in Sp only pun-rep can be used' and 'baseline specification with γ=0.3 and ρ=0.01'.,1,False,Computer simulation; no humans.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,In silico.,1,True,Uses evolutionary computer simulations over generations.,1,False,Analytical results separated elsewhere.,1,2,Interactions are between pairs.,1,30,Expected m=30 interactions per individual.,0.8,0,Binary choice to contribute y or not; all-or-nothing setting.,0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment split at start.,1,0.8,MPCR=r/2 with r=1.6 and group size 2.,1,N/A,No human chat.,1,0,No UI-like feedback described.,0.7,N/A,No human player IDs.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Agents cannot condition on number of interactions.,1,1,Punishment stage with cost k and impact p.,1,0.2,k=0.2 baseline.,1,2.5,Impact/cost=0.5/0.2.,1,0,Only punishment is modelled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,y is unspecified.,0,N/R,Cooperation rates described as 'significantly more common' but no scalar baseline given.,0.2,N/R,No token amounts reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency index reported.,0,N/R,No numeric payoffs reported.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Two-player PD with contribution y, multiplier r=1.6, punishment cost k=0.2, impact p=0.5; gossip-based acquisition of punishment reputation (pun-rep) only.",Scenario Sp restricts reputational conditioning to pun-rep.,1
