10.1098_rspb.2018.1508.md,Analytical restricted model – invasion and stability analysis,Section 3(b) describes an analytical model restricting strategies to the five most frequent types and considering non-punishers vs responsible punishers.,1,"Strategy composition (five first-stage strategy types: d-none, c-none, c-pun(-)d, c-coop(-)a, c-coop(-)d) and punishment type (non-punishers vs responsible punishers); information cost ρ varied (including ρ=0), avoidance cost φ, punishment severity p, and multiplier r.","Analytical section examines conditions for invasion and ESS across strategy combinations, with parameters r, p, ρ, φ varied in inequalities (e.g., p>1−r/2, φ<k+1−r/2).",0.9,False,No human data are used.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; formal analysis.,1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,False,"This subsection explicitly presents 'analytical results' based on payoff comparisons and ESS conditions, not simulation output.",1,True,Derives ESS conditions and invasion possibilities using analytical payoff expressions.,1,2,Analytical model is based on the same two-player PD with punishment as the simulations.,1,1,Analytical payoffs are per interaction (stage game) against predominant type rather than multi-round trajectories.,0.6,0,Same underlying game: binary choice to contribute y or not.,0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation; payoffs defined relative to cooperation/defection actions.,1,0.8,Still uses r=1.6 and pair size 2; MPCR=r/2.,0.9,N/A,No human interaction; information costs ρ model gossip abstractly.,1,0,No display to agents; analytical setting.,0.9,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Stage-game analysis; no round information.,1,1,Considers responsible punishers with cost k and impact p.,1,0.2,Analytical section refers to same parameters as simulations; k=0.2 from Table 1.,0.9,2.5,Punishment impact/cost ratio remains p/k=0.5/0.2.,0.9,0,Only punishment is discussed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Analytical form uses contribution y but no numeric value.,0,N/R,"Analytical section discusses possibility of invasion and stability, not explicit average cooperation levels.",0,N/R,No concrete token amounts; only qualitative cooperation/defection.,0,N/R,No efficiency metric is computed.,0,N/R,Payoffs are used symbolically in inequalities; no numeric group payoff reported.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Purely theoretical analytical model.,1,"Analyses a reduced set of strategies in the same two-stage PD with punishment, considering non-punishers and responsible punishers; assumes γ=1 (full observability) and derives ESS and invasion conditions in terms of r, p, ρ, φ.",Summarized from section 3(b) description of the analytical framework.,1
10.1038_s41893-022-00877-w.md,"Simulation – (li) Local public goods, individual institution choice, high efficiency, long memory, global information","Condition corresponds to scenario (li) with high institution efficiency (F_I=5), long memory (M=30), and global information as described for Fig. 2.",0.9,Public good scale: local (n<Z); Institution decision scale: individual; Memory length: long (M=30); Information: global; Institution efficiency: high (F_I=5); Quorum: N/A (individual choice).,"Independent dimensions explicitly varied are scale of public good, scale of institutional decision, memory length, and information scope; F_I and other parameters fixed in main results.",0.9,False,Study uses computational simulations of agents; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a model-based simulation.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely computational.,1,True,Described as an evolutionary/computational model with repeated simulations.,1,False,Analytical results mentioned are in Supplementary Section B; main text figures reflect simulations.,0.8,100,Population size Z=100 for local scenarios per Fig. 2 caption.,1,50000,Results averaged over a period of 5×10^4 generations taken after a period of 5×10^4 generations.,0.9,1,"Model defines a single contribution amount c; agents choose C or D, effectively an all-or-nothing decision; coded as 1 because players cannot choose intermediate amounts.",0.7,N/A,"No human endowment allocation interface; model directly specifies contributions, not default allocations.",1,0.5,"Caption: n=5, F/n=0.5, so marginal per capita return is 0.5.",1,N/A,No communication or chat in agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,"Agents have abstracted memory/information, not a user interface with summaries.",1,N/A,No identities or displays; model-level interactions only.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,"No participants or interface, so nothing is displayed.",1,1,Core of model is a punishment institution that fines defectors.,1,0.1,Membership fee c_I with c_I/c=0.1 and c=1 implies c_I=0.1.,1,5,Institution efficiency F_I=5 multiplies collected fees into fines on defectors.,1,0,Only punishment institutions are modelled; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Contribution cost c=1; each cooperating agent effectively has at least 1 unit to potentially contribute.,0.7,N/R,Figure 2 shows qualitative average levels but no exact numerical cooperation fractions.,0,N/R,Only binary cooperate/defect with amount c; no reported average counts per group for this specific condition.,0,N/R,Aggregate payoffs or efficiency ratios are not numerically reported.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff data for this condition in the main text.,0,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational agent-based simulations only.,1,"Agent-based public-goods game with punishment institution; local public goods (n=5) sampled from Z=100; players vote Y/N, with individual institution choice and global information; parameters: F/n=0.5, c_I/c=0.1, c=1, F_I=5, β=10, μ=0.01; memory length M=30; results averaged over late 5×10^4 of 10^5 generations.",Summarized from Methods and Fig. 2 caption.,1
