10.1016_j.jebo.2018.11.020.md,Social group – No Punishment,One of four cells in the 2x2 design: Social vs Non-social group formation crossed with No Punishment vs Punishment in the trust game.,1,Group identity: Social; Punishment institution: No Punishment,Condition defined by being assigned to the Social group-formation puzzle and to the No Punishment incentive condition in the trust game.,1,True,Human subjects in a lab trust game.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled payoff structure.,1,True,Sessions run at ICES laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,Paper contains theory but this condition refers to the lab experiment with humans.,1,2,Hidden-action trust game between investor A and trustee B.,1,2,Trust game played for two periods.,1,1,Investor’s choice is binary IN/OUT; there is no continuous contribution; coded as 1 because players can choose between the two discrete options rather than being forced all-or-nothing contribution of an endowment is not defined here.,0.5,0,"No public-good style endowment or contribution; investor chooses IN/OUT over a fixed payoff structure, so default proportion in public fund is effectively 0.",0.3,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Subjects seated in separate booths and could not communicate before or during the trust-game decisions.,1,0,No mention that participants see others’ earnings or punishment summaries; only their own payoffs determined at the end.,0.9,0,Interactions are anonymous; no identities revealed when punishment is possible (and here punishment is not available).,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,Instructions clearly state there are two periods; subjects know this when making decisions.,1,0,"In No Punishment condition, investors cannot reduce trustees’ earnings.",1,N/A,No punishment institution in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment institution in this condition.,1,0,Design only allows reducing trustee earnings; no positive reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,5,If investor chooses OUT both receive $5; interpret this as the safe endowment level at stake per period.,0.7,N/R,"Authors report trust (IN) frequencies, not normalized contribution of an endowment.",1,N/R,Investor decision is binary IN/OUT; no monetary contribution amount reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate efficiency ratio reported.,1,N/R,"Only individual expected payoffs and some averages (e.g., earnings) are mentioned; no per-pair payoff summary.",1,United States,Sessions at George Mason University’s ICES lab; standard subject pool is U.S.-based students.,0.8,N/R,No age information reported; likely university students but not specified.,0.2,Mixed; approx. 58% male in No Punishment sessions overall,Table 1 reports 0.58 male in No Punishment condition (across Social and Non-social).,0.8,Predominantly university students,Lab at a university; typical subjects are students though exact level not specified.,0.7,On site,Physical lab sessions at ICES with subjects in separate booths.,1,"Hidden-action trust game with roles A (investor) and B (trustee); two periods; strategy method for decisions with in-group and out-group counterparts; if A chooses OUT both get $5; if A chooses IN and B chooses DON’T ROLL then B gets $14 and A gets $0; if B chooses ROLL then B gets $10 and A gets $0 with prob 1/6 or $12 with prob 5/6; matching is random each period; one period randomly selected for payment; group identities created via cooperative triangle puzzle (Social) vs less cooperative square puzzle (Non-social), but in this condition only Social group subjects are considered and no punishment is available.",Summarized from Section 3 and instructions; restricted to the Social + No Punishment cell.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2018.11.020.md,Social group – Punishment,Social group-formation treatment crossed with Punishment incentive condition.,1,Group identity: Social; Punishment institution: Punishment (investor can reduce trustee’s earnings by $6 at no cost if she chose IN and received $0),Defined by Social puzzle and availability of punishment in the trust game.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Random assignment and controlled payoffs.,1,True,Conducted at ICES lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,"This row refers to the lab condition, not the theoretical model.",1,2,Game played between investor A and trustee B.,1,2,Design specifies two periods of play.,1,1,Investor chooses IN or OUT; no continuous contribution choice.,0.5,0,No public contribution endowment; safe outside option is $5.,0.3,N/R,"Trust game, not a linear public good; no MPCR.",1,0,Subjects separated and cannot communicate.,1,0,No mention of feedback about others’ earnings or punishments.,0.9,0,Decisions are anonymous; no identity revelation of punishers or punished.,0.9,0,No reward option in game.,1,1,Instructions state two periods; common knowledge.,1,1,"In Punishment condition, A can reduce B’s earnings after receiving $0 when she chose IN.",1,0,Investor can reduce B’s earnings by $6 at no cost to herself.,1,6,Investor’s punishment decision can reduce trustee earnings by $6; interpreted as 6 units per punishment action.,1,0,"Only negative sanctions allowed, no bonuses.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,5,OUT option yields $5 to each; used as effective safe endowment per period.,0.7,N/R,"Outcome is trust frequency, not continuous contributions.",1,N/R,No monetary contribution amount defined.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculation reported.,1,N/R,Per-pair payoff data not reported as a main DV.,1,United States,Lab at George Mason University; standard U.S. student subject pool.,0.8,N/R,Age not reported; likely students but unspecified.,0.2,Mixed; approx. 54% male in Punishment sessions overall,Table 1 reports 0.54 male in Punishment condition (across Social and Non-social).,0.8,Predominantly university students,University lab subject pool.,0.7,On site,In-person lab experiment at ICES.,1,"Same hidden-action trust game as in No Punishment, but with an additional stage where, if A chose IN and received $0, she can choose to reduce B’s earnings by $0 or $6 at no cost. Punishment decision is made before A knows B’s actual action (ROLL vs DON’T ROLL) and can only be implemented when A’s payoff is $0. Two periods, strategy method decisions for in-group and out-group counterparts, random matching each period, one period randomly selected for payment. Group identity is Social, created via cooperative triangle puzzle with substantial sharing and exchange.",Summarized from Section 3.1 and instructions for the Punishment condition.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2018.11.020.md,Non-social group – No Punishment,Non-social group-formation treatment crossed with No Punishment incentive condition.,1,Group identity: Non-social; Punishment institution: No Punishment,Defined by Non-social (square) puzzle and absence of punishment.,1,True,Human-subject experiment.,1,True,Randomized 2x2 design in controlled lab setting.,1,True,Conducted at ICES laboratory.,1,False,No simulation.,1,False,"This row is for lab condition, not the analytic model.",1,2,Trust game between one investor and one trustee.,1,2,Game played for two periods.,1,1,Investor chooses IN or OUT; no continuous contribution decision.,0.5,0,No contribution endowment; outside option $5.,0.3,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication allowed in booths.,1,0,No evidence that aggregate outcomes are shown.,0.9,0,Anonymity of decisions; and no punishment in this condition.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Subjects informed about two periods.,1,0,This is the No Punishment condition.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,No positive sanction.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,5,"Safe OUT option is $5 each, taken as effective endowment at risk.",0.7,N/R,"DV is trust frequency, not contributions.",1,N/R,No monetary contribution variable.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported by condition.,1,N/R,Group payoffs per pair not reported.,1,United States,Same subject pool as other cells: U.S. university lab.,0.8,N/R,Age not given.,0.2,Mixed; approx. 58% male in No Punishment sessions overall,"Table 1 aggregates No Punishment across Social and Non-social; specific cell not broken out, so we retain overall treatment proportion.",0.6,Predominantly university students,University subject pool.,0.7,On site,Conducted in physical lab booths.,1,"Identical hidden-action trust game structure as in Social conditions, with in-group and out-group decisions using strategy method, two periods, random matching, and one period selected for payment. Group identity is Non-social, created via square puzzle that is easier and requires less exchange of pieces, leading to less sharing norms.",Summarized from Section 3 and group-formation description for Non-social group.,1
