10.1016_j.jebo.2018.11.020.md,Control – Punishment (no prior group interaction),"Control_P treatment in Appendix B: no group-identity formation, with punishment.",1,"Group identity formation: None (Control, no puzzle interaction); Punishment institution: Punishment (investor can reduce trustee’s earnings by $6 at no cost upon receiving $0 after choosing IN)",Appendix B describes a Control_P treatment with punishment but without group formation.,1,True,Lab control with human subjects.,1,True,Randomized punishment vs no-punishment in control setting.,1,True,Conducted in same ICES lab.,1,False,No simulation.,1,False,This is an experimental control condition.,1,2,Same A-B trust game.,1,2,"Two periods, as in main treatments.",0.9,1,Binary trust choice IN/OUT.,0.5,0,No continuous contribution institution.,0.3,N/R,Not a public goods setup.,1,0,No communication mentioned; same lab procedures.,0.9,0,No mention of additional feedback.,0.9,0,Anonymity and no identity revelation; punishment affects only payoffs.,0.9,0,No reward available.,1,1,Structure matches main two-period game.,0.9,1,Control_P includes punishment as in main Punishment treatments.,1,0,Investor can reduce trustee earnings at no cost.,1,6,Punishment reduces trustee earnings by $6.,1,0,No positive reward.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,5,Same payoff structure: OUT yields $5 to each.,0.7,N/R,"Trust frequency (0.69) is reported, but not normalized as contribution rate.",1,N/R,No monetary contribution.,1,N/R,No efficiency measures for control are reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff not given.,1,United States,Same lab and likely same subject pool.,0.8,N/R,Age data not provided.,0.2,N/R,No gender breakdown provided specifically for control conditions.,0.2,Predominantly university students,Same recruitment context as main experiment.,0.7,On site,Conducted in same physical lab as other treatments.,0.9,"Same hidden-action trust game and punishment stage as main Punishment treatments, but without preceding group-formation puzzle. No group labels are in play; participants simply interact as anonymous A and B. Reported overall trust under Punishment in Control is mean = 0.69, which is not significantly higher than 0.56 in Control_NP.",Summarized from Appendix B description of control treatments and Fig. A1.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2019.05.019.md,Well-mixed population – replicator dynamics with shared punishment cost,The paper analytically studies a public-goods game with shared punishment cost in a well-mixed (well-mixed population model) setting using replicator dynamics.,1,"Fine on defectors (β), total punishment cost (α), group size (N), and return rate (r) determining evolutionary outcomes via replicator dynamics","Section 3.1 varies β, α, N, and fixes r to analyze stability of equilibria and basins of attraction in the well-mixed population.",1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical analysis of evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only mathematical analysis and simulations.,1,False,No lab or field implementation; theoretical population model.,1,False,"This specific part (replicator dynamics in well-mixed population) is analytical; numerical illustrations of fixed points are based on closed-form ODE, not agent-based simulation.",0.8,True,Derives and analyzes replicator equations and fixed points analytically for well-mixed populations.,1,5,"Group size N is a parameter with examples N=5 or 10 in Figs. 1–3; choose N=5 as a representative condition explicitly used in figure captions with α=0.5, β and r specified.",0.6,1,"Evolutionary game is modeled as repeated random group formation with payoffs feeding into replicator dynamics; each ‘round’ is effectively one public goods interaction, so the stage game is single-shot per interaction.",0.5,0,"Players choose to be either a cooperator contributing c=1 or a defector contributing 0; no continuous contribution level choice, so it is a binary all-or-nothing decision.",1,N/A,"No human subjects or default UI state; contributions are strategy-defined, not pre-allocated tokens.",1,0.3,"In linear public goods game, MPCR = r/N. For the common parameter set in Figs. 1–3, r=1.5 and N=5 so MPCR = 1.5/5 = 0.3.",0.9,N/A,No human participants or communication mechanism.,1,0,Model specifies only payoff functions and dynamics; no observation structure like earnings summaries is defined.,0.9,N/A,"No identities; punishment is modeled as a cost/fine term, not directed with observable identities.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary dynamics; no concept of known finite round count.,0.9,1,Core of the model is punishment with shared cost α and fine β in the payoff functions.,1,1,"Total punishment cost is α; common parameter α=0.5 is the total group cost, which is spread across cooperators. Per-unit specification is not clearly mapped to ‘per punishment unit’; set to α as the effective cost scale.",0.4,1,Fine per defector is β (fixed fine independent of number of cooperators); effectively 1 unit of fine parameter per punishment event.,0.4,0,No rewarding mechanism is modeled; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator’s investment is c=1; interpret this as the per-round endowment available to contribute in the binary choice.,0.9,N/R,They analyze frequencies of cooperators x but do not report a specific numeric contribution rate for a particular parameter set as an outcome.,0.4,N/R,"While each cooperator contributes 1, no aggregate average contribution per group is reported as a result metric.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is calculated or reported numerically.,0.3,N/R,"Group payoff formula can be derived, but no specific numeric group payoff values are reported as outcomes.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Purely theoretical evolutionary model, no data collection environment.",1,Two-strategy linear public goods game (cooperator contributes c=1 and pays shared punishment cost α/n_c; defector contributes 0 and suffers fixed fine β). Group size N>=2; benefits multiplied by r and divided equally among N. Dynamics governed by replicator equation in an infinite well-mixed population.,Summarizes the model specification from Section 2 and 3.1.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2019.05.019.md,Structured population – analytical weak-selection pair-approximation model,"Section 3.2 derives analytical dynamics on structured populations using pair approximation and weak selection, with players placed on regular graphs.",1,"Fine on defectors (β), total punishment cost (α), return rate (r), regular graph degree (k), and selection intensity (s) affecting coexistence equilibria","The structured population analysis varies β while fixing α, r, N, and parameterizes dynamics by degree k and selection intensity s.",1,False,No human or animal subjects; it is an analytical population model plus simulations.,1,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment; mathematical analysis of evolutionary dynamics on graphs.,1,False,No lab or field implementation; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,False,This specific row represents the analytical pair-approximation dynamics (equations for p_C and q_{C|C}); simulations are handled in a separate condition.,0.9,True,Derives closed-form ODEs for strategy and pair frequencies under weak selection on regular graphs.,1,10000,"They state: 'In structured population of size N=10000, players locate in a regular graph with degree k=4 or 8' when comparing to well-mixed case.",1,1,"As in the well-mixed case, the public goods interaction is single-shot per game; evolutionary time is continuous, not discrete repeated rounds.",0.5,0,Same two-strategy public goods game: cooperate with c=1 or defect with 0.,1,N/A,No actual subjects or initial endowment allocation; strategies fully determine contributions.,1,0.3,"Benefits in each group: r*n_c/(k+1). For structured population figures they fix r=1.5 and k=4 (group size=5) or k=8 (group size=9). For k=4, MPCR = r/(k+1) = 1.5/5 = 0.3, which matches the well-mixed example and one of the primary structured cases.",0.7,N/A,No communication or human interface.,1,0,Model specifies only payoffs and update rules; no informational feedback structure of summaries is modeled.,0.9,N/A,No explicit identities or directed punishment decisions; punishment is institutional and group-based.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,0,Infinite evolutionary dynamics with no finite round horizon.,0.9,1,"Same shared-cost punishment mechanism as in the well-mixed model, applied within each local public goods game.",1,1,"Total punishment cost per group is α shared among cooperators; as before, we represent α as effective punishment cost scale.",0.4,1,Defectors suffer a fixed fine β per game; treat β as one 'unit' of punishment effect per sanctioned defector.,0.4,0,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,"As in the global model, each cooperator's contribution is c=1 per game.",0.9,N/R,The analytical structured model yields equilibrium p_C as a function of parameters but numerical values for specific parameter sets are not tabulated.,0.4,N/R,"Can be computed as p_C*c per player, but not explicitly reported numerically for any one parameter set.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure (ratio to optimal group payoff) is provided.,0.3,N/R,"Group payoff formulas over local games are given, but no numeric values for particular equilibria are listed.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical model without empirical data.,1,"Players occupy vertices of a regular graph of degree k; each participates in k+1 overlapping public goods games. In each game, cooperators contribute c=1 and share a punishment cost α equally among cooperators; defectors contribute 0 and incur a fine β. Total payoff is the sum over k+1 games. Strategy updating follows pairwise comparison with Fermi rule under weak selection, approximated using pair dynamics (p_C, q_{C|C}).",Summarizes Section 3.2 and Appendix A description of the structured population model.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2019.05.019.md,"Structured population – Monte Carlo simulations on regular networks (cycle, lattice, random regular)","Figure 6 describes Monte Carlo simulations on regular networks (cycle, spatial lattice, random regular graphs) with varying degrees and selection intensities, producing cooperation levels as outcomes.",1,"Network topology (cycle, spatial lattice, random regular), degree k (2, 4, 8), selection intensity s (0.5, 1, 2, 4), punishment cost α, fine β",Panels (a)-(i) vary α and β on axes and compare across different k and s and network types to show cooperation levels.,1,False,These are agent-based Monte Carlo simulations with no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; computational simulations of evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,No lab or field setting; in silico.,1,True,They state 'Each simulation result is obtained by averaging over the last 10^3 Monte Carlo steps of the total 10^4 steps for 10 independent realizations.',1,False,"This condition refers specifically to numerical Monte Carlo studies, not the analytical weak selection equations.",1,10000,They specify N=10000 in structured population simulations in Fig. 5 and in the description for Fig. 6.,1,10000,They run 'total 10^4 steps' and measure averages over the last 10^3 Monte Carlo steps.,1,0,"Each player chooses between cooperate (contribute c=1) and defect (0), as defined by the game.",1,N/A,"In simulations, players start with random strategies; there is no 'default' contribution UI state or endowment allocation as in human experiments.",0.8,0.3,"They fix r=1.5 and k varies (group size k+1). For k=4 (the central lattice case in multiple panels), MPCR=r/(k+1)=1.5/5=0.3.",0.7,N/A,Simulated agents; no communication protocol.,1,0,Agents update using local payoff comparisons (Fermi rule) without global summaries.,0.9,N/A,Punishment is institutional and not targeted with identity information; agents only see payoffs.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Simulated agents do not have explicit knowledge of horizon; updates continue for a fixed simulation duration but not modeled as known to agents.,0.8,1,"Simulations implement the same shared-cost punishment mechanism (α cost shared, β fine) as the analytical model.",1,1,"Total punishment cost per group is α; as before, represented as the effective punishment cost parameter.",0.4,1,Defectors incur fixed fine β per game; treated as 1 unit of fine parameter per sanctioned defector.,0.4,0,"Simulations include only punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Each cooperating agent contributes c=1 per public goods game.,0.9,N/R,Outcomes are reported as 'cooperation levels' (fraction of cooperators) across α-β parameter space but specific numeric values for any one setting are not tabulated.,0.5,N/R,"Contribution per cooperator is 1, so average contribution = cooperation level, but numeric values for specific conditions are only shown graphically without exact numbers.",0.5,N/R,"The simulations focus on cooperation levels, not explicitly on efficiency metrics.",0.3,N/R,Group payoff is not reported; only strategy frequencies are presented.,0.3,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Numerical simulations only.,1,"Population of N=10000 agents arranged on different regular network topologies: cycle (k=2), spatial lattices (k=4,8), and random regular graphs (k=2,4,8). Each Monte Carlo step: randomly select a focal player and neighbor, compute accumulated payoffs from k+1 public goods games with shared-cost punishment, and update focal strategy with Fermi rule using selection intensity s. Simulations run for 10^4 steps; cooperation level is averaged over last 10^3 steps and across 10 realizations for each (α,β,k,s,network) condition.",Summarizes the simulation protocol described around Fig. 6.,1
10.1038_srep31927.md,Analytical stochastic evolutionary model with increasing returns to scale,"Single analytical model specified under 'Analytical results' and 'Methods', examining PGG with increasing returns to scale.",1,"Coefficient of increasing returns to scale α varied (e.g., plotted over range; key thresholds around α = 1.3 and α = 1.6). Other parameters fixed at M=100, N=5, c=1, r=3, δ=1, γ=0.3, ω=0.1, μ→0.",They calculate relative time in homogeneous states as a function of α with other parameters fixed as stated in Fig. 2 caption.,1,False,No human participants; purely mathematical and stochastic evolutionary analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a theoretical model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; analytical work only.,1,False,"This object refers to analytical derivations, not the later computer simulations.",1,True,"They derive payoffs, fixation probabilities, and stationary distributions in closed form.",1,5,"Sample size N=5 is the group size in each PGG, from Fig. 2 caption.",1,1,Model payoffs defined per period; evolutionary process is many repetitions of a one-shot PGG. Represented as 1 round for the stage game.,0.7,1,"Each contributor pays fixed cost c if they contribute (binary contribute/defect choice), not a continuous amount; treated as all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.8,N/A,No endowment allocation model with human players; purely theoretical without explicit initial allocation between private and public accounts.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly given; production uses Cobb–Douglas cr(X+Z)^α / n; marginal return per contributor is nonlinear and not a single constant MPCR.,0.6,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modeled.,1,0,No information feedback structure of human experiments is modeled; payoffs determined analytically only.,1,N/A,No identities or observability modeled; only aggregate payoff functions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,0,No explicit interface or display to players; theoretical model only.,1,1,Punishers pay cost γ per defector Y and defectors receive punishment δZ.,1,N/R,"Model uses parameters γ (cost per punished defector) and δ (punishment strength), but there is no discrete 'coin per punishment point' unit to map to required metric.",0.6,N/R,"Punishment effect δ is defined, but not in the requested per-coin-spent metric.",0.6,0,"Only punishment, no reward, appears in payoff functions.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Contributors pay cost c but initial endowment in tokens not specified.,0.5,N/R,"Analytical results report relative time in homogeneous states, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,Each cooperator/punisher contributes c but aggregate amounts or averages are not presented as outcomes.,0.8,N/R,They do not present total payoffs as fraction of maximum; only fixation probabilities and occupation times.,1,N/R,"Total group payoff P is part of model definition, but numerical outcome summaries are not reported.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical evolutionary game model.,1,"Finite well-mixed population of size M=100 with three strategies (cooperators, defectors, punishers). Each period, N=5 individuals play a PGG with Cobb–Douglas production P = c r (X+Z)^α Y^β with β=0, α>1 for increasing returns. Payoffs per player type: Px = cr(X+Z)^α / n − c; Py = cr(X+Z)^α / n − δZ; Pz = cr(X+Z)^α / n − c − γY. Evolution follows a frequency-dependent Moran process with exponential fitness F = exp(ωP) and mutation rate μ→0.",Summarizes the core structure of the analytical model from the Methods section.,1
10.1038_srep31927.md,Simulation – constant returns to scale (α = 1.0),Simulation condition in Fig. 3a described as 'With constant returns to scale (α=1)'.,1,"Coefficient of returns to scale α = 1.0 (constant returns). Other parameters: M=100, N=5, initial composition X=30, Y=40, Z=30, c=1, r=3, δ=1, γ=0.3, ω=0.5, μ=0.001.",Exact parameter set given in Fig. 3 caption; α specified for this panel.,1,False,Simulation with artificial agents using a Moran process; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; computational simulation.,1,False,No laboratory or field; run on computer.,1,True,Described as 'multi-agent computer simulations' based on a Moran process.,1,False,"This object refers to the numeric simulation, not the analytical derivations.",1,5,N=5 participants in each PGG as per Fig. 3 caption.,1,100000,"Robustness section says strategy frequency is averaged over 100,000 periods, indicating simulations typically run for 100,000 periods; use that as approximate number of rounds.",0.7,1,Agents are of fixed types contributing cost c or not; binary cooperate/defect choice.,0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation to public/private accounts in agent-based simulation.,1,N/R,Production is nonlinear Cobb–Douglas; MPCR is not a single fixed scalar and not reported.,0.6,N/A,Artificial agents; no communication modeled.,1,0,Simulation code computes payoffs but there is no notion of displayed summaries to agents.,1,N/A,No identities; population is anonymous and well-mixed.,1,N/A,No reward available.,1,0,No user interface; agents do not observe round counts.,1,1,Punishers pay punishment cost γ and defectors are punished by δ per punisher.,1,N/R,Cost parameter γ=0.3 exists but no explicit token-per-punishment mapping defined.,0.6,N/R,"Punishment strength δ=1, but not described as multiplier per investment unit.",0.6,0,Model only includes punishment; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Agents incur contribution cost c when cooperating, but starting endowment not specified.",0.5,N/R,Simulation outcomes described qualitatively (rock–paper–scissors-like oscillations); no explicit contribution rates provided.,1,N/R,No numerical report of mean contributions per round.,1,N/R,"They focus on dominance and extinction of strategies, not on efficiency metrics.",1,N/R,Group payoffs not summarized numerically for this condition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Multi-agent computer simulation with no human subjects.,1,"Finite well-mixed population with M=100 agents of three fixed strategies (cooperators, defectors, punishers). Each period, N=5 randomly drawn agents play a PGG with constant returns to scale (α=1): production P = c r (X+Z) where X is cooperators, Z punishers. Payoffs as in analytical model, with punishment parameters δ=1, γ=0.3. Evolution follows frequency-dependent Moran process with exponential fitness F = exp(ωP), selection strength ω=0.5, mutation rate μ=0.001, and initial composition X=30, Y=40, Z=30. For α=1, simulations produce rock–paper–scissors-like oscillations and no stable cooperation.",Summarizes the simulation setup described in 'Simulation results' and Fig. 3a caption.,1
10.1038_srep31927.md,Simulation – increasing returns to scale (α = 1.8),Simulation condition in Fig. 3b described as 'With increasing returns to scale (α=1.8)'.,1,"Coefficient of increasing returns to scale α = 1.8. Other parameters: M=100, N=5, initial composition X=30, Y=40, Z=30, c=1, r=3, δ=1, γ=0.3, ω=0.5, μ=0.001.",Exact parameter set given in Fig. 3 caption; α specified for this panel.,1,False,"Multi-agent simulation, not using human beings.",1,False,Not an empirical or field experiment.,1,False,"Simulation is computational, not lab-based.",1,True,Explicitly described as computer simulation based on a Moran process.,1,False,"This refers to numerical runs, although supported by analytical theory.",1,5,N=5 in Fig. 3.,1,100000,"Robust tests compute average strategy frequency over 100,000 periods; interpret as typical run length.",0.7,1,Agents either contribute fixed cost c or defect.,0.9,N/A,No modeled endowment split in simulation; only contribution cost parameter c.,1,N/R,Production is P = c r (X+Z)^α; MPCR changes with X+Z and is not reported as a scalar.,0.6,N/A,No communication among artificial agents is described.,1,0,No interface or feedback displays simulated to agents.,1,N/A,No identities in simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents are not modeled as observing round numbers.,1,1,Punishment parameters δ and γ are active in payoff calculation.,1,N/R,γ=0.3 gives cost functional form but not expressed per punishment unit in requested sense.,0.6,N/R,"δ=1 defines damage per punisher, but not mapped to cost-per-effect metric.",0.6,0,No reward is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Only contribution cost c is mentioned; initial endowments not specified.,0.5,N/R,"Outcomes reported as dominance of punishers and extinction of others, without numeric contribution statistics.",1,N/R,No explicit averages of contributions are given.,1,N/R,"No numerical efficiency measure is reported, only qualitative statements about resolving dilemmas.",1,N/R,Group payoff trajectories not summarized numerically.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is a computer simulation without human subjects.,1,"Finite well-mixed population with M=100 agents of three types (cooperators, defectors, punishers). Each period, N=5 randomly sampled agents play a PGG with increasing returns to scale (α=1.8): production P = c r (X+Z)^α. Payoffs as in analytical model with c=1, r=3, δ=1, γ=0.3. Evolutionary updating through a frequency-dependent Moran process with exponential fitness F = exp(ωP), selection strength ω=0.5, mutation rate μ=0.001, initial composition X=30, Y=40, Z=30. For α=1.8, after transient oscillations, punishers come to dominate and maintain dominance, resisting invasion by other strategies.",Summarizes the increasing-returns simulation described in Fig. 3b and 'Simulation results' section.,1
