10.1126_sciadv.abj7377.md,"Experiment 2 – PATS, high accuracy, big investment, authority benefits from public good","Experiment 2 condition with big investment, high realized accuracy, PATS, and authority share-of-pot compensation.",0.8,Authority investment: big; Realized accuracy: high; Enforcement rule: PATS; Authority compensation: 40% share of common pot,"Combination of investment, accuracy, enforcement rule, and compensation mechanism defines this treatment condition.",0.8,True,Part of Experiment 2 human-subject data.,1,True,Randomized control of treatments.,1,True,Performed in lab settings.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,Empirical condition description.,1,5,Standard 5-player groups.,1,20,Same as other Experiment 2 conditions.,1,0,Binary contribution.,1,0,All tokens in private account initially.,1,0.4,Linear public good MPCR.,0.9,0,Chat not used.,0.9,0,No mention of summary information.,0.7,0,Identities not revealed.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,1,20 periods planned and likely announced.,0.8,1,PATS enforcement applied.,1,24,24 tokens deducted per punished citizen.,1,24,Punishment size unchanged.,0.8,0,No reward institution.,1,N/A,Reward absent.,1,N/A,Reward absent.,1,20,Endowment same as rest of experiments.,0.9,3.47,"3.47 of 4 for high accuracy & big investment (Fig. 6), pooling over compensation; here used as approximate rate.",0.4,69.4,3.47 contributors × 20 tokens.,0.4,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,United States,US university labs.,0.9,N/R,Not reported for Experiment 2.,0.3,N/R,Not reported for Experiment 2.,0.2,University students,Subject pool of university students.,0.8,On site,Laboratory environment.,1,"Under big investment in Experiment 2, high accuracy (20% error) occurs with 75% probability; authority here also receives 40% of public good, and this is known by the citizens when they make their contribution choices.",Combines accuracy and compensation details from Experiment 2 description.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2020.126867.md,"Simulation – baseline thresholded punishment and reward (a=1, b=3, c_p=0.2, c_r=0.2, u_p=0.5, u_r=0.5)","Uses the common parameter set explicitly given for Fig. 4 (a and b) where a=1, b=3, c_p=0.2, c_r=0.2, u_p=0.5, u_r=0.5 and the environmental gain factor r is varied.",0.9,"Environmental gain factor r varied across a range; punishment fine-to-cost ratio α=u_p/c_p varied (e.g., α=1, 1.2, 2.4) with β fixed; reward benefit-to-cost ratio β=u_r/c_r varied with α fixed; two-threshold incentive mechanism (a=1, b=3) with both punishment and reward active.","From Fig. 4 caption and related text: they vary r, α=u_p/c_p and β=u_r/c_r while keeping a and b fixed at 1 and 3. The mechanism combines punishment and reward based on thresholds.",0.9,False,"Study is an evolutionary game simulation on L×L square lattices, no human participants.",1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; all results are from computational simulations of a model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,"They describe Monte Carlo simulations on structured populations (L from 120 to 600, relaxation time up to 10^6 steps).",1,False,No closed-form or purely analytical derivations reported for main results; focus is on numerical simulations.,0.9,14400,"Players are on L×L square lattices with L from 120 to 600. A concrete value is not fixed, but a commonly used size (e.g., L=120) implies N=120×120=14400. Since a single exact N is not specified, this is approximate.",0.3,1000000,They state: relaxation time was varied from 10^4 to 10^6 Monte Carlo steps; using the upper bound 10^6 as representative for convergence.,0.3,1,"In each PGG, cooperators invest a fixed amount C and defectors invest 0; there is no continuous choice between 0 and C. This is effectively an all-or-nothing contribution structure.",0.8,N/A,No human endowments; model uses payoff C as a fixed investment parameter rather than an initial allocation between accounts.,1,N/R,They specify an environmental gain factor r but do not define group size-based MPCR explicitly as r/5 or similar.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; communication is not modeled.,1,0,"Agents update based on local payoffs via a Fermi rule; no notion of on-screen summaries, only local payoff comparisons.",0.8,0,Model only needs local neighbor relations; there is no concept of identity revelation beyond adjacency.,0.8,0,"Same as for punishment: rewards are applied to neighbors in the lattice, with no identity or labeling mechanism.",0.8,0,Simulation agents do not see a number of remaining rounds; dynamics are Markovian and stationary.,0.9,1,Egoistic punishment is a core part of the model; agents with H<a punish defectors.,1,0.2,Given explicitly as c_p=0.2 in the parameter listing for Fig. 4.,1,2.5,"They specify u_p=0.5 and c_p=0.2, so α=u_p/c_p=0.5/0.2=2.5 when using that exact combination; this is the fine per unit cost.",0.7,1,Altruistic reward is explicitly modeled: agents with H>b reward cooperating neighbors.,1,0.2,Given explicitly as c_r=0.2 in the parameter listing for Fig. 4.,1,2.5,"They specify u_r=0.5 and c_r=0.2, so β=u_r/c_r=0.5/0.2=2.5 under this baseline set.",0.7,N/R,"Each cooperator invests a payoff amount C, but the numeric value of C is never specified.",0.2,N/R,"They report ρ_c, the fraction of cooperators, not normalized contribution conditional on endowment per se.",0.4,N/R,Only strategy frequencies (ρ_c) are shown; the underlying absolute investment C is unspecified.,0.2,N/R,They do not report total payoffs relative to maximum achievable payoff; focus is on cooperation level ρ_c.,0.2,N/R,No explicit numbers for group payoff are given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results derived from Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice with no empirical sample.,1,Public goods game on an L×L square lattice with von Neumann neighborhoods and overlapping groups of size 5. Each player participates in 5 public goods games per generation. Cooperators invest a fixed well-being amount C; total contribution in each group is multiplied by environmental gain factor r and shared equally. Subjective well-being thresholds a and b determine whether egoistic punishment (H<a) of defectors or altruistic reward (H>b) of cooperators in the neighborhood is applied. Strategy updating uses a Fermi imitation rule with K=0.5 based on total payoff T_i collected from all games and incentives.,"Summarizes the detailed model description from the Model section, including network structure, group size, thresholds, and update rule.",0.95
10.1016_j.physleta.2020.126867.md,Simulation – threshold variation with fixed incentives (Fig. 2: varying a at b=8; varying b at a=0),"Fig. 2 examines ρ_c as a function of r for different values of lower limit a with fixed b=8 (panel a) and for different upper limit b with fixed a=0 (panel b), with c_p=c_r=0.2, u_p=u_r=0.5.",0.9,"Environmental gain factor r varied; in panel (a) lower threshold a varied with upper threshold fixed at b=8; in panel (b) upper threshold b varied with lower threshold fixed at a=0; punishment and reward costs/benefits fixed at c_p=c_r=0.2, u_p=u_r=0.5.",Explicitly stated in Fig. 2 caption and surrounding text.,1,False,Same simulation framework on lattice as described in Model section.,1,False,No empirical or field experiment; purely computational.,1,False,Not a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on L×L lattice with overlapping groups of size 5.,1,False,Results are presented as numerical curves; no analytical solution for these parameter sweeps.,0.9,14400,Same simulation environment; N=L×L with L from 120 to 600. A precise N not fixed; 14400 (L=120) used as a representative value.,0.3,1000000,Relaxation time for simulations varied up to 10^6 Monte Carlo steps; use upper bound as representative.,0.3,1,"Contribution choice is binary: cooperator invests C, defector invests 0.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment setting.,1,N/R,They define r but never explicitly give MPCR as multiplier divided by group size.,0.4,N/A,No human agents.,1,0,No presentation layer; agents only use local payoff comparisons.,0.8,0,Identities beyond lattice position are irrelevant in the model.,0.8,0,Same as for punishment.,0.8,0,No notion of displayed rounds in simulation.,0.9,1,Egoistic punishment active when H<a.,1,0.2,From Fig. 2 caption: c_p=0.2.,1,2.5,u_p=0.5 and c_p=0.2 so u_p/c_p=2.5 under these settings.,0.7,1,Altruistic reward occurs when H>b.,1,0.2,From Fig. 2 caption: c_r=0.2.,1,2.5,u_r=0.5 and c_r=0.2 so u_r/c_r=2.5 under these settings.,0.7,N/R,C is not numerically specified.,0.2,N/R,"Reported outcome is ρ_c as a function of r and thresholds, not normalized contributions per endowment.",0.4,N/R,Absolute contributions are not tabulated.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency ratios reported.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff values are not given in figures or text.,0.2,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,No human,Computational evolutionary game simulations only.,1,"Same underlying PGG-on-lattice model as baseline: L×L square lattice with periodic boundaries, von Neumann neighborhoods, overlapping groups of size 5, Fermi imitation with K=0.5, threshold-based egoistic punishment and altruistic reward. Here, the focus is on how varying the subjective well-being thresholds a and b, along with r, affects the stationary cooperation level ρ_c.",Directly follows from the common model description plus the modifications described for Fig. 2.,0.95
10.1016_j.physleta.2020.126867.md,Simulation – threshold interval variation at fixed r (Fig. 3: varying threshold interval for r=1.5 and r=2.5),"Fig. 3 investigates ρ_c as a function of the interval between lower and upper thresholds for fixed environmental gain factors r=1.5 (panel a) and r=2.5 (panel b), with c_p=c_r=0.2 and u_p=u_r=0.5.",0.9,"Threshold interval (b−a) of travel well-being varied by changing a and/or b; environmental gain factor fixed at r=1.5 in panel (a) and r=2.5 in panel (b); punishment and reward parameters held fixed at c_p=c_r=0.2, u_p=u_r=0.5.","From Fig. 3 caption and text: ρ_c plotted as function of the threshold interval for different values of a and b, with specified r values.",1,False,No human subjects; simulated population on a network.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory context.,1,True,Same Monte Carlo evolutionary game model as elsewhere in the paper.,1,False,Results reported numerically through simulations.,0.9,14400,Same L×L lattice setting; numerical L not fixed; 14400 used as representative (L=120).,0.3,1000000,Relaxation time can be up to 10^6 Monte Carlo steps; used as characteristic number.,0.3,1,Binary strategy: either contribute C or 0.,0.8,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly specified; only r is discussed.,0.4,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No user interface or summaries in simulation; agents use payoff differences.,0.8,0,No identity revelation modeled.,0.8,0,Same as above.,0.8,0,Agents have no information about total steps.,0.9,1,Egoistic punishment is part of the incentive mechanism.,1,0.2,From Fig. 3 caption: c_p=0.2.,1,2.5,"u_p=0.5, c_p=0.2 so u_p/c_p=2.5.",0.7,1,Altruistic reward mechanism active.,1,0.2,From Fig. 3 caption: c_r=0.2.,1,2.5,"u_r=0.5, c_r=0.2 so u_r/c_r=2.5.",0.7,N/R,Investment C not numerically specified.,0.2,N/R,Outcome is ρ_c (fraction of cooperators) rather than normalized contribution per endowment.,0.4,N/R,No raw contribution values are reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency is not directly computed or plotted.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs are not tabulated.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational modeling.,1,"Same PGG-on-lattice setup with threshold-based incentives. The new focus is on how the width of the subjective well-being interval [a,b] (i.e., b−a) influences the stationary cooperation level ρ_c at fixed environmental gain factors r=1.5 and r=2.5.",Derived from Fig. 3 description combined with the core model definition.,0.95
10.1016_j.physleta.2020.126867.md,"Simulation – spatiotemporal dynamics at r=1.8 and r=2.8 (Fig. 5, a=1.2, b=4.5)","Fig. 5 presents spatiotemporal distributions of strategies and incentives for two specific values of r (1.8 and 2.8) with thresholds a=1.2, b=4.5 and fixed incentive parameters.",0.95,"Environmental gain factor r set to 1.8 in one condition and 2.8 in another; thresholds fixed at a=1.2, b=4.5; punishment and reward parameters c_p=c_r=0.2, u_p=u_r=0.5.","From Fig. 5 caption: a=1.2, b=4.5, c_p=c_r=0.2, u_p=u_r=0.5; other parameters: r=1.8 for panels (a)-(h) and r=2.8 for panels (i)-(p).",1,False,Simulation on lattice; narratives describe evolution over Monte Carlo steps.,1,False,No real-world experimental manipulation.,1,False,No lab participants.,1,True,Snapshots generated from dynamic Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,"Visual results from computation, not closed-form.",0.9,14400,Same L×L lattice framework; exact L not given for these snapshots; 14400 used as representative (L=120).,0.3,1000000,They vary relaxation time up to 10^6 Monte Carlo steps; snapshots likely taken during such runs.,0.3,1,"Cooperators invest a fixed C, defectors invest 0.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment decision.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly given.,0.4,N/A,No communication among human agents.,1,0,Simulation output only; no agent-level feedback beyond local payoffs.,0.8,0,"Punishment depends only on neighborhood relations, not on identity display.",0.8,0,Same as punishment.,0.8,0,Agents do not observe any countdown of iterations.,0.9,1,Egoistic punishment is active for H<a in both r conditions.,1,0.2,From Fig. 5 caption: c_p=0.2.,1,2.5,u_p=0.5 and c_p=0.2 so u_p/c_p=2.5.,0.7,1,Altruistic reward is active for H>b in both r conditions.,1,0.2,From Fig. 5 caption: c_r=0.2.,1,2.5,u_r=0.5 and c_r=0.2 so u_r/c_r=2.5.,0.7,N/R,Value of C not reported.,0.2,N/R,They qualitatively describe that ρ_c≈0.7 for r=1.8 and ρ_c=1 for r=2.8 but do not provide precise numeric values; normalized contribution rate is not separately defined.,0.4,N/R,No absolute contribution numbers are tabulated.,0.2,N/R,They do not compute payoff relative to maximum efficiency; only spatial patterns and ρ_c are discussed.,0.2,N/R,No explicit payoff sums for groups are given for these runs.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Numerical evolutionary game study.,1,"PGG on L×L square lattice with von Neumann neighborhoods, group size 5, threshold-based egoistic punishment and altruistic reward. For r=1.8, simulation converges to coexistence of cooperators and defectors with about 70% low-carbon travelers; punishment initially arises among defectors, then spreads, and eventually cooperators use punishment to expand. For r=2.8, cooperators become dominant and occupy the whole population; rewarding is prevalent among them while defectors are punished and eliminated. Snapshots illustrate co-evolution of strategies (low-carbon vs non-low-carbon) and incentive behaviors (punish, reward, none).",Summarizes the narrative explanation of Fig. 5 in the Results section.,0.95
10.1016_j.amc.2024.128864.md,Monte Carlo simulation – baseline spatial PD with smart cooperators,"Single modeling framework described in Section 2; all figures and parameter sweeps (T, W, U, G) use the same underlying simulation protocol on an L×L lattice.",0.9,"Temptation to defect T, reward for cooperation W, punishment for defection U, extra cost of smart cooperators G; sucker’s payoff fixed at S = -0.1.","Model section specifies T, W, U, G as the free parameters, with S fixed to -0.1; results systematically scan these parameters (e.g., T–W planes for various G,U; one-dimensional sweeps in T, U, G).",1,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of strategies on a lattice, no human or animal participants mentioned.",1,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; purely computational simulation of an evolutionary game.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; numerical simulations only.,1,True,They repeatedly mention Monte Carlo (MC) steps on an L×L lattice and refer to their work as computational experiments.,1,False,"No closed-form derivations, only qualitative explanation of simulation outcomes.",1,1000000,"Population is an L×L square lattice with L=1000 or 1200. They say: “Typically we use 10^4 MC steps… where the linear system size is L=1000 or L=1200.” Using the main size 1000×1000 gives N=1,000,000; 1200 is for checks. Use the typical L=1000 as the main configuration.",0.7,10000,They state: “Typically we use 10^4 MC steps to reach the stationary state.” Treat MC steps as rounds of interaction.,0.9,0,"Players are unconditional strategies (C, D, M) in a 2×2-type payoff matrix; there is no choice of contribution amount, so the all-or-nothing contribution structure is not applicable in a public-goods sense.",0.6,N/A,"Model is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with fixed payoffs, no explicit endowment or contribution decision.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; this is a pairwise PD payoff matrix.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication is not modeled.,1,N/A,Agent-based lattice simulation; no on-screen feedback to human subjects.,1,N/A,"Punishment is embedded in payoffs, not a decision by identifiable human players.",1,N/A,"Rewards are modeled as payoff terms, no identification of human rewarders.",1,N/A,Simulation agents are not informed; round information display is a human-interface concept.,1,1,Payoff matrix includes parameter U such that D receives -U when facing M; this is a punishment mechanism.,1,N/R,"Cost of administering punishment is absorbed into G, a fixed extra cost to M, but there is no per-unit punishment decision or per-token cost specification.",0.5,N/R,Model does not define a scalable punishment per unit spent; U directly sets the payoff loss to D.,0.8,1,Payoff matrix includes W such that cooperators meeting M receive 1+W; this is a reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Cost of rewarding is represented by G, a lump-sum cost to M, but no per-unit reward cost is given.",0.5,N/R,There is no mapping from ‘coins spent’ to reward units; W is directly the reward size.,0.8,N/R,"Model defines payoffs R, S, T, P but not an explicit token endowment per round.",1,N/R,"Outcome measures are stationary fractions of strategies, not contributions; in a 2×2 PD with pure strategies there is no partial contribution rate.",1,N/R,No token contributions are modeled; only strategic interactions and payoffs.,1,N/R,"They do not report average total payoff or efficiency ratios, only fractions of C, D, M.",1,N/R,No aggregate payoff figures are provided; only qualitative discussion of dominance and fractions.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a numerical Monte Carlo simulation of strategies on a lattice.,1,"Spatial evolutionary Prisoner’s Dilemma on an L×L square lattice with periodic boundary conditions and three unconditional strategies: defector (D), cooperator (C), and smart cooperator (M). Payoffs: R=1, P=0, S=-0.1, T variable; M receives 1+W-G vs M, 1-G vs C, -G vs D; C receives 1+W vs M, 1 vs C, S vs D; D receives -U vs M, T vs C, 0 vs D. Strategy update rule: pairwise comparison with Fermi imitation probability 1/(1+exp[(Pi_i-Pi_j)/K]) with K=0.1. Small mutation rate μ=10^-6 allows random strategy changes. System typically run for 10^4 MC steps on lattices of size L=1000 or 1200 to reach a stationary state; outcome is the stationary fraction of each strategy as functions of T, W, U, and G.","Summarizes explicit model specification from Section 2 and simulation protocol (lattice, strategies, payoff matrix, update rule, noise K, mutation μ, system size, runtime).",1
10.1027_1864-9335_a000460.md,Study 3 – Dictator Game (help-giving condition),Study 3 includes a Dictator Game specifically linked to help-giving; treated as a separate condition.,1,Economic game type: Dictator Game; participant role: dictator; decision: amount to share (0–10.70 €) with another participant.,Only key manipulated feature is the game structure and choice to share; no between-subject conditions described.,0.9,True,Human participants made decisions in an economic game.,1,True,Controlled lab-like task with scripted opponents and defined payoff rules.,0.9,False,"Games were conducted in an online session, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,"Decisions are from human subjects, not simulated agents.",1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model is described for this task.,1,2,Dictator and one other participant (‘another participant who had not been chosen to receive a reward’).,0.9,1,Single dictator decision is reported; no repeated rounds mentioned.,0.8,1,Participants could ‘share the amount (freely)’ implying any amount from 0 to full endowment.,0.9,0,All 10.70 € are initially allocated to the dictator’s private account; sharing is voluntary.,0.9,N/R,No linear public goods structure or multiplier is defined for this bilateral transfer game.,1,0,No communication or messaging between dictator and recipient is mentioned.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ earnings or actions is described.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism exists in this game.,1,0,Recipient identity is not described as revealed; only a generic ‘another participant’.,0.7,0,No information about displaying the number of rounds; appears as a one-shot choice without such display.,0.7,0,"Dictator Game includes only sharing, no punitive option.",1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,0,There is no structured costly rewarding mechanism beyond the main transfer decision itself.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism is implemented.,1,10.7,Participants were ‘randomly selected as winners of a reward of €10.70’.,1,N/R,Only mean giving in euros is reported (3.79); rate relative to endowment is not explicitly used as DV.,0.7,3.79,Table 4 reports ‘DG giving’ M = 3.79 (SD = 2.63).,1,N/R,No information on maximum possible joint payoff or efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Group or total payoff is not reported for this game.,1,Germany,Earlier studies explicitly ‘German-speaking’ and recruited in Germany; Study 3 does not re-specify but appears to use the same population.,0.7,"M = 29.87, SD = 13.57, range 19–75",Reported under Study 3 Methods for the combined sample.,1,"63% female, 0% diverse",Reported in Study 3 Methods.,1,75% students,Study 3: ‘75% students’.,1,Online,Economic games conducted ‘in another online session’.,1,"One-shot unincentivized Dictator Game; fictitious money, no real payouts; opponents were staged. Participants chose how much of a 10.70 € endowment to share with an anonymous non-winner.",Summarized from the Study 3 Methods description of the Dictator Game.,1
10.1027_1864-9335_a000460.md,Study 3 – Public Goods Game with punishment option,Study 3 describes a specific public goods game with a punishment stage used to probe peer punishment.,1,Economic game type: 4-player public goods game with punishment; independent decision variables: contribution (0–5 €) and punishment spending on free riders.,Only manipulation is game structure including punishment option; no between-subject variations reported.,0.9,True,Human subjects played the public goods game online.,1,True,Controlled game with defined payoff rules and a constructed punishment mechanism.,0.9,False,Games were administered via an online session rather than an in-person lab.,1,False,"Decisions are from human participants, not computer simulations.",1,False,No pure analytical model is used here.,1,4,Participants ‘played in groups of four pursuing an investment project’.,1,4,Three practice rounds plus a fourth round used for data collection are described.,0.9,1,Participants ‘were asked to invest’ from an endowment; free-riders who ‘did not contribute their fair share’ implies a range of possible contributions including zero.,0.8,0,Each player had an ‘initial endowment of €5’ to decide how much to invest; all starts in private account.,1,0.75,"Sum of contributions is tripled and evenly distributed to four players, so MPCR = 3/4 = 0.75.",1,0,No communication between players is mentioned; only investment and punishment decisions.,0.9,1,"Participants can identify ‘unfair group members, that is, free riders who did not contribute their fair share’, which requires some feedback about others’ contributions or payoffs.",0.7,0,Identities are not described as revealed; unfair group members are described generically.,0.6,0,No reward stage or identity revelation for positive actions is described.,0.9,0,The text does not specify that the total or remaining number of rounds was displayed.,0.6,1,Explicit ‘punish unfair group members’ option with a fee-to-fine ratio of 1:3.,1,1,Fee-to-fine ratio ‘1:3’ implies each 1 € spent by punisher costs 1 € to them.,1,3,Fee-to-fine ratio 1:3 means each 1 € spent reduces target’s payoff by 3 €.,1,0,Only punishment option is described; no positive rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,5,Players had ‘an initial endowment of €5’.,1,N/R,No mean contribution is reported; only punishment costs are summarized.,0.6,N/R,Average euro contribution to the project is not provided.,0.6,N/R,No total group payoffs or efficiency ratios are reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff statistics are not given.,1,Germany,Study 1 and 2 explicitly use German samples; Study 3 is presented as a continuation with the same institution and language; no other country mentioned.,0.7,"M = 29.87, SD = 13.57, range 19–75",Reported in Study 3 overall sample description.,1,"63% female, 0% diverse",Study 3 Methods specifies gender composition.,1,75% students,Study 3 Methods: ‘75% students’.,1,Online,Games were run ‘in another online session’.,1,"Four-person public goods game with fictitious euros, no real payoffs. Each player endowed with 5 €, chooses investment; total contributions are tripled and evenly shared. In the (data) round with punishment, participants can spend money to reduce payoffs of identified free riders, with each 1 € spent reducing the target’s payoff by 3 €. Only the fourth round is used for analysis.",Synthesized from Study 3 description of the ‘Public Goods Game with punishment option’.,1
10.1027_1864-9335_a000460.md,"Study 3 – Third-Party Intervention (moral courage game, original version)",Study 3 initially implemented a third-party intervention game before modifying instructions; these data (N=38) are treated as the original condition.,0.95,Scenario version: original third-party intervention; offender is unfair but no explicit minority victims or passive bystanders are highlighted.,Text distinguishes an original setup that correlated more with Peer Punishment and a modified version designed to target Moral Courage.,0.95,True,Human participants decided whether to send a message to an unfair powerful player.,1,True,Scripted scenario with controlled information and a binary behavioral choice.,0.9,False,Game administered online as part of an online session.,1,False,"Decisions are made by human participants, not simulated agents.",1,False,No analytical modeling is involved.,1,N/R,Exact number of ‘other players’ in the group is not specified; only that one person controls payouts for participant and ‘other players’.,0.4,1,Single scenario ending in an opportunity to send a message; no repetitions mentioned.,0.9,0,No continuous contribution; only a binary choice to send or not send a message.,1,0,"No endowment-contribution structure; participant’s own payoff is controlled by the powerful player, not via a public goods decision.",0.9,N/R,This is not a standard public goods or linear contribution game.,1,1,Participants ‘were allowed to send a message to this person’ which counts as directed communication.,1,1,"Participants observe how the powerful player allocates and removes money (‘kept a large sum’, ‘took 11.50 € from other players’, ‘excluded some other players’).",0.9,1,The offender is a distinct person with a unique role (controller of payouts); participants know they are messaging this specific person.,0.8,0,No structured rewarding of specific individuals is described.,0.9,0,No mention that any number of stages or rounds was displayed.,0.8,1,"Sending a confrontational message to a powerful unfair player, at risk of personal loss, functions as costly punishment/complaint.",0.8,N/R,"While the offender ‘had the power to respond… by excluding the participants from playing and from being paid off’, the probability or magnitude is not specified; no per-unit cost is defined.",0.4,N/R,No parameterized punishment technology like fee-to-fine ratio is provided.,0.4,0,No option to spend own resources to reward others is included.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,"Amounts mentioned (9.40 €, 11.50 €) refer to sums controlled by the powerful player, not clearly to the participant’s initial endowment.",0.4,N/R,"DV is a binary intervention decision, not a contribution amount.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution is made; DV is whether participants intervened.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric is reported or derivable from the description.,1,N/R,Total group payoff is not summarized.,1,Germany,Same recruitment context as earlier studies at a German university; no other country is mentioned.,0.7,"M = 29.87, SD = 13.57, range 19–75",Study 3 overall sample characteristics apply to all tasks unless otherwise stated.,1,"63% female, 0% diverse",Study 3 sample description.,1,75% students,Study 3 specifies that 75% of participants were students.,1,Online,Third-Party Intervention was part of the ‘other online session’.,1,"Third-party moral courage scenario (original). A powerful player determines payouts for the participant and unnamed other players. The player keeps 9.40 € they could have shared, removes 11.50 € from others without justification, and excludes some players from payouts. Participants can choose whether to send a message to this player, who can retaliate by excluding the participant from playing and payoff. This version (first 38 datasets) correlated more with the Peer Punishment scale than with Moral Courage, prompting later modifications.",Paraphrased from the Study 3 description of the initial Third-Party Intervention setup.,1
10.1027_1864-9335_a000460.md,"Study 3 – Third-Party Intervention (moral courage game, modified version)","After N=38, instructions for the Third-Party Intervention were changed to better target moral courage, creating a modified condition (N=30 used for analyses).",1,"Scenario version: modified third-party intervention; adds minority-sounding victim names, visible non-intervening bystanders, and group-wide message visibility.","Text explicitly lists modifications (minority names, passive others, group message visibility) to distinguish from original version.",1,True,Human participants make a real-time decision to send or not send a message in an online scenario.,1,True,Scripted interactive scenario with controlled manipulations and binary response.,0.95,False,"Conducted in an online session, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Involves real participants, not simulated agents.",1,False,No purely analytical modeling.,1,N/R,Still described only as ‘other players’ and group members; exact number not specified for the modified version.,0.4,1,Single decision to send or not send a message in the modified scenario; no repeated rounds indicated.,0.9,0,Binary DV: intervention (1) or no intervention (0); no gradual contribution.,1,0,No structured endowment-to-public-account decision; only a message choice.,0.9,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,1,"Participants may ‘send the offender a message, one that would be presented to all members of the group’.",1,1,"Participants observe unfair allocation, theft, and exclusions; also see pseudonyms of others who failed to act.",0.9,1,Participants send a message to a specific unfair decision-maker (offender) whose identity/role is salient.,0.8,0,No option to reward others is described.,0.9,0,No indication that round count was displayed to participants.,0.8,1,"Sending a public message confronting the powerful offender, with risk of being excluded, functions as costly punishment/intervention.",0.9,N/R,Retaliation (‘excluding the participants from playing and from being paid off’) is possible but probabilistic or conditional costs are not quantified.,0.4,N/R,No per-unit mapping between cost and harm to offender is defined.,0.4,0,No rewarding option is described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,"No explicit endowment for this scenario; amounts refer to sums the offender controls, not participant’s own initial endowment.",0.4,N/R,"Measured DV is binary intervention, not a continuous contribution.",1,N/R,No contribution tokens or euros; only an intervention decision.,1,N/R,No efficiency measure is reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff data for this scenario are not provided.,1,Germany,Same Study 3 sample of German-speaking participants from a German university context.,0.7,"M = 29.87, SD = 13.57, range 19–75",Study 3 sample description applies to both original and modified versions unless otherwise stated.,1,"63% female, 0% diverse",Study 3 overall sample.,1,75% students,Study 3 overall sample.,1,Online,Game is part of the online economic games session in Study 3.,1,"Modified Third-Party Intervention designed to measure moral courage. A powerful player unfairly keeps 9.40 €, takes 11.50 € from other players, and excludes some from payouts. Names of excluded players are manipulated to sound like minority-group members. Pseudonyms of other participants who could have, but did not, send a message are shown, indicating group acquiescence. Participants decide whether to send a message to the offender that will be presented to the entire group, despite the offender’s power to exclude them from play and payment. This modified version (N=30) yields a binary intervention measure that correlates selectively with the Moral Courage subscale.",Summarizes the additional elements introduced after the initial 38 datasets as described in Study 3.,1
10.1007_s11229-020-02890-y.md,Dunn et al. (2008) spending money on self vs. others,Only concrete experimental design described: participants randomly assigned to spend money on themselves or on others.,0.86,Spending target: self vs. others (other people/charity),Text: “people were randomly assigned to spend money on themselves or to spend it on others”.,0.96,True,Random assignment of “a large sample of people” to spending conditions implies human subjects.,0.98,True,Random assignment to conditions = controlled experiment.,0.98,False,"Setting not specified; Dunn et al. (2008) is typically a field/online style experiment, but this paper does not say, so cannot code as lab.",0.2,False,Human random assignment; not a numerical/agent-based model.,0.99,False,"Reports empirical brain/behavior evidence, not formal derivations for this study.",0.99,1,Each participant receives instructions about how to spend money; no interaction structure is described. Treated as an individual decision task.,0.3,1,Described as a one-shot spending assignment; no repetition mentioned.,0.6,1,"Game-like contribution structure (discrete choice set) not described; task is to ‘spend money on self vs. others’. Cannot infer restricted strategy space, so treat as general/any-amount possible.",0.2,N/R,No endowment or initial account structure (public vs. private) specified; not clearly a public-good style game.,0.0,N/R,Not a linear public good; no multiplier or group size reported.,0.0,0,No mention of communication between participants in this description.,0.4,0,No feedback about others’ behavior or payoffs is described.,0.4,0,No punishment mechanism described.,0.99,0,No structured reward mechanism (beyond possible emotional rewards) described.,0.99,0,One-shot style task; no information about rounds is mentioned.,0.6,0,Task is about spending allocation; no costly punishment option is mentioned.,0.99,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1.0,0,No formal peer-to-peer reward option; only the experimental manipulation of spending target.,0.9,N/A,No formal reward scheme.,1.0,N/A,No formal reward scheme.,1.0,N/R,Amount of money given to subjects not reported in this article.,0.0,N/R,"Outcome reported here is ‘reported (significantly) greater happiness’, not spending fractions in a strategic game.",0.0,N/R,No detailed data on how much was given vs. kept is reported.,0.0,N/R,No group payoff structure defined; not a standard public goods efficiency measure.,0.0,N/R,Group payoffs not reported or defined.,0.0,N/R,The article cites Dunn et al. (2008) but does not state where the sample was drawn from.,0.0,N/R,Described only as “a large sample of people”. No age information.,0.0,N/R,No gender composition details.,0.0,N/R,No education description for this experiment (separate from the correlational Westlake et al. result).,0.0,On site,"The Dunn et al. (2008) study is widely known as an in-person experiment, but this paper does not specify. Coding ‘On site’ with low confidence rather than ‘Online’.",0.3,Happiness comparison after random assignment to spend an experimentally provided sum on oneself vs. on others; primary DV is self-reported happiness rather than cooperative payoff structure.,Summarized from: “a large sample of people were randomly assigned to spend money on themselves or to spend it on others … the group that was asked to spend it on others reported (significantly) greater happiness”.,0.95
10.1057_s41599-020-0419-2.md,"T0 – Integrated society, CDP class (punishment allowed)","Treatment T0 is the integrated society model; CDP class are players with cooperation, defection, and punishment options.",1,"Socioeconomic integration (integrated matching) and strategy set: CDP (can cooperate, defect, or punish); random SES assignment.","Across treatments, integration/segregation and which SES group holds CDP vs CD are manipulated; for this row, T0 integrated and CDP class.",1,True,Human student participants played the Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma in a lab.,1,True,"Controlled lab experiment with between-subject treatments T0, T1, T2.",1,True,Conducted in a computer lab at Yunnan University of Finance and Economics.,1,False,"This row refers to the human laboratory experiment, not the agent-based model.",1,False,No closed-form analytical model; only empirical data and simulations.,1,116,T0 included 116 students; treatments are between-subject; all in T0 face the same integrated condition.,0.9,82.5,They report an average of 82.5 iterated rounds per session using a 75% continuation rule.,0.6,0,"IPD with choices among cooperation, defection, and punishment implies discrete action options, not a continuous contribution; ‘any amount’ vs all-or-nothing is not applicable, but per instructions 0=only all-or-nothing.",0.4,0,Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma has no contribution endowment structure; by convention we treat as 0 (all payoff from private account).,0.3,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined for this IPD payoff matrix.,0,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous IPD with only choices revealed.,0.9,1,"After each round, each participant was shown partner’s choice together with total payoff scores, i.e., outcome summary.",1,0,Interactions are anonymous; no indication that identities of punishers were revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,Experiment duration and stopping rule were unknown to players to avoid e