10.1016_j.geb.2009.07.007.md,Section 3.1 – Public goods game (theoretical model),"Single underlying public goods game described in Section 3.1; no separate empirical conditions, purely theoretical.",1,"Marginal per capita return γ (1/n < γ < 1); number of players n ≥ 2; complier share κ ∈ (0,1); guilt cost g > 0","Section 3.1 specifies payoffs as u_i = γ Σ c_j − c_i with 1/n < γ < 1, n ≥ 2, and parameters κ and g determining behavior.",1,False,No human subjects; Section 3.1 presents a purely theoretical game-theoretic model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with participants; it is an analytical treatment of a stylized game.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; only analytical derivations.,1,True,Section 3.1 derives propositions analytically from a formal game-theoretic model.,1,2,"Model states n ≥ 2; a specific n is not fixed, so no single numeric value fits. Using minimal n (2) as a placeholder is arbitrary; set to N/R effectively.",0,1,"One-shot public goods game, no repetition mentioned.",0.9,1,"Each player chooses c_i ∈ R_0^+; contributions are continuous, not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human subjects and no explicit endowment allocation mechanism; theoretical continuous contribution choice.,1,N/R,"γ is the MPCR by definition, but no single numeric γ is specified; only inequality 1/n < γ < 1.",0.5,N/A,No human subjects or procedural features like communication; purely analytical.,1,0,Information structure not specified beyond payoffs; no mention of feedback on others’ earnings; model does not hinge on such summaries.,0.3,N/A,No punishment stage in this baseline public goods game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Single-shot analytical game; no user interface or display of number of rounds.,1,0,This subsection is explicitly the public goods game without punishment; punishment introduced only in 3.2.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this game.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this game.,1,0,No explicit reward technology is defined; only standard public good payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Players choose contributions c_i ≥ 0; no explicit initial endowment level is specified in tokens or money.,0.2,N/R,"Model derives qualitative/comparative statics, not numeric contribution levels normalized by endowment.",0.2,N/R,"Predictions are symbolic (e.g., c̄_g) and depend on parameters; no single numeric amount.",0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio is computed; only qualitative statements that total payoffs increase in contributions.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff expressions are symbolic; no single numeric group payoff is reported.,0.3,N/A,No participants; theoretical analysis only.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical analysis only.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical analysis only.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical analysis only.,1,No human,Section 3.1 is a purely analytical game-theoretic model with no empirical implementation.,1,"Linear public goods game with n ≥ 2 players; payoff u_i = γ Σ_j c_j − c_i, with 1/n < γ < 1; players are either selfish or compliers with private types and guilt cost g when deviating from norm.",Restates the formal structure given in Section 3.1.,1
