10.1016_j.geb.2009.07.007.md,Section 3.5 – Ultimatum game with envy (theoretical model),Single ultimatum game structure analyzed in Section 3.5 with compliers and selfish types; no empirical data.,1,"Envy parameter α > 0; complier share κ ∈ (0,1); guilt cost g; total pie normalized to 1","Section 3.5 reuses envy utility from dictator game and defines x*, x^s, etc. in terms of α, κ, g.",1,False,"The paper’s ultimatum games are theoretical, used to match experimental stylized facts.",1,False,"No new experimental data; only theoretical propositions (e.g., Proposition 5).",1,False,No lab implementation described.,1,False,No simulations.,1,True,All predictions are derived analytically (thresholds for offers and acceptance).,1,2,Standard two-player ultimatum game: proposer and responder.,1,1,Single offer and accept/reject decision.,1,1,Proposer chooses a continuous offer x; not restricted to binary choices.,1,N/A,No real endowment process in an implemented experiment; theoretical split of normalized pie.,1,N/R,Ultimatum game is not a public good; MPCR not defined.,1,N/A,No pre-play communication modelled.,1,0,One-shot two-player game; no summaries beyond the observed offer.,0.5,N/A,"No explicit punishment technology beyond rejection, which is part of the game’s basic structure, not a punishment stage.",1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,0,Single-shot game; no rounds displayed.,1,0,"Rejection changes payoffs but is inherent to the ultimatum structure, not a separate costly punishment technology as defined in the schema.",0.7,N/A,No separate costly punishment choice with adjustable intensity and monetary cost.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology parameter like φ or p.,1,0,"Offers are part of the base game, not an add-on reward mechanism.",1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,1,Total ‘cake’ is normalized to 1; proposer allocates x to responder.,1,N/R,"The main variable is offer x; theoretical expressions vary with α, κ, g; no sin