iveness, compassion, fairness, strength).",0,UK,Participants drawn from University of Nottingham.,1,Mean age 26.78 years; heterosexual women,Study 3 sample: 161 heterosexual women with mean age 26.78 (s.d. 8.14); roughly half assigned to short-term.,0.9,Female; heterosexual,Non-heterosexual women were excluded from analyses.,1,Mixed; predominantly university students and affiliates,Recruited in the university context; detailed education beyond that not given.,0.6,On site,Questionnaires administered at the university.,0.9,"Women in the short-term condition imagine Player C as a potential short-term partner. Using the same 3PPC vignette, they rate the man’s attractiveness, compassion, fairness, and strength (each 1–7) for each of four behaviors (punish, compensate, both, nothing). Trait empathic concern and a single-item left–right political ideology scale are also measured.",Summarizes design details and measured constructs for the short-term arm of Study 3.,1
10.1098_rsos.181441.md,Study 3 – Long-term relationship condition – Women’s ratings,Complementary between-subject condition in Study 3 where women judge men as potential long-term partners.,0.95,Between-subject: relationship context = long-term relationship; within-subject: male 3PPC preference (punish vs compensate vs both vs nothing),Women randomly allocated to the long-term condition rate a male C across four 3PPC behaviors.,0.95,True,Human female raters provide responses.,1,True,Random allocation to long-term versus short-term context and factorial manipulation via vignettes.,0.95,True,Conducted on site at the university.,0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations.,1,False,"Empirical rating study, not analytical modeling.",1,3,"Same 3PPC vignette: three roles (A, B, C).",1,1,Based on the one-shot 3PPC game from Study 1.,0.95,1,"Underlying game allows Player C to spend 'some, none or all' of £5.",0.8,0,No automatic contribution; C’s entire £5 is discretionary.,0.7,N/R,No standard public-good MPCR defined.,0,0,Scenario does not include communication channels.,0.9,0,Only the unfair interaction and C’s response are known.,0.9,0,Punishment targets anonymous Player A.,0.8,0,Compensation targets anonymous Player B.,0.8,1,One-shot scenario known to participants.,0.9,1,Punishing is among the four behaviors being rated.,1,1,Same as the Study 1 underlying 3PPC game.,0.9,2,Each £1 punishes A by £2 in the referenced game.,0.9,1,Compensation is another of the four behaviors.,1,1,Each £1 spent on compensation increases B's payoff by £2.,0.9,2,Inherited from Study 1’s 3PPC game.,0.9,5,C has £5 in the underlying game description.,0.9,N/R,No actual economic decisions; only ratings.,0,N/R,Participants do not allocate funds.,0,N/R,No payoff data reported.,0,N/R,Only perception measures are collected.,0,UK,Same University of Nottingham sampling frame.,1,Mean age 26.78 years; heterosexual women,Overall Study 3 sample has mean age 26.78; long-term subsample (n=81) is drawn from this distribution.,0.9,Female; heterosexual,Non-heterosexual women were excluded.,1,Mixed; predominantly university students and affiliates,Recruited in a UK university setting; detailed levels not specified.,0.6,On site,Questionnaire-based experiment at the university.,0.9,"Women in the long-term condition imagine Player C as a potential long-term partner. They rate his attractiveness, compassion, fairness, and strength (1–7) for each of the four 3PPC behaviors (punish, compensate, both, nothing). Trait empathic concern and political ideology (0=left, 10=right) are also recorded and related to ratings.",Summarizes design and measures for the long-term arm of Study 3.,1
10.1016_j.jet.2017.05.005.md,Baseline repeated trust game on a fixed investor network,"The paper presents a single theoretical model: a repeated trust game with N investors, one agent, and an exogenously fixed social network G determining information flows and punishments. There are no separate empirical or simulated conditions.",1,"Network structure and monitoring parameters: network G over investors (e.g., complete components, incomplete components, minimally connected, star); monitoring radius d (1 or N); observation of non-participation with probability q; investors’ degree |N(i,G)| and component size |C(i,G)|.","Across the analysis, payoffs and feasible cooperation levels are compared as functions of G and of the monitoring parameters (d,q). These are the only elements varied across cases; there is no experimental treatment with human subjects.",1,False,The paper develops a purely theoretical repeated-game model with networks; no human participants or data collection are described.,1,False,No controlled behavioral or field experiment is run; only theoretical analysis is provided.,1,False,There is no laboratory or other physical experimental setting; the work is analytical.,1,False,"No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; all results are analytic (theorems, lemmas, proofs).",1,True,"The paper presents formal game-theoretic and network-theoretic analysis, proving results about equilibria and optimal networks.",1,0,"No fixed numeric N is used; the model assumes an arbitrary N ≥ 2 investors plus one agent. Since there are no human subjects, N is theoretical.",0.7,0,The interaction is an infinitely repeated game (rounds t ≥ 1 with discount factor δ). There is no finite number of rounds.,1,1,"Investors choose an investment level a ∈ ℝ₊; this is a continuous choice, not an all-or-nothing contribution.",1,N/A,There is no endowment-allocation framing with public vs. private accounts; it is a trust/investment game without human subjects.,1,N/R,"The paper does not describe a linear public goods technology or an MPCR; payoffs are given by generic functions f(a), g(a), v(a), l(a).",1,N/A,No communication stage between human participants exists; there are no human players.,1,N/R,The formal model specifies only whether investors observe actions/signals; it does not describe payoff or punishment summary screens as in lab software.,1,N/R,Punishments are modeled as refusal to trade by informed investors; there are no identities or interfaces in an experimental sense.,1,N/R,There are no explicit rewards or IDs revealed; only continuation play in the theoretical model.,1,N/R,Agents are assumed to know the discount factor and infinite horizon; there is no display of remaining rounds as in an experiment.,1,1,Punishment is central: investors can refuse to trade in future rounds after observing a defection; the network determines who can punish.,1,N/R,Punishment is modeled as foregone future gains from trade; there is no discrete cost-per-punishment-token parameter.,1,N/R,There is no parametric mapping from punishment points to victim loss; effects are embedded in continuation payoffs via δ and investment choices.,1,0,The model considers only cooperation versus defection and community sanctions (punishments); no explicit reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,Investors choose an investment level a ∈ ℝ₊ but there is no explicit finite per-period endowment specified.,1,N/R,"As there is no empirical data, no realized contribution rates are reported; only equilibrium investment levels a_i are characterized.",1,N/R,The paper derives theoretical upper bounds and equilibrium actions (a_i) symbolically; it does not report numeric outcome data.,1,N/R,No empirical efficiency statistics (realized payoff/maximum) are computed; only welfare comparisons across network structures are given theoretically.,1,N/R,"Group welfare is analyzed symbolically (e.g., ∑ u_i(σ,G)), but no numeric outcomes from data are reported.",1,N/A,There are no human participants; thus no participant country information exists.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,No human,The paper is purely theoretical with no experimental or field implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated trust game with N ≥ 2 investors and one agent. In each round, one investor is randomly and uniformly selected to interact with the agent. The investor chooses participate (P) or not (NP); if P, he selects investment a ∈ ℝ₊. The agent then chooses cooperate (C) or defect (D). Payoffs are given by functions f(a), g(a), v(a), l(a) satisfying Condition 1; static best response of the agent is to defect, so cooperation requires community enforcement. A social network G over investors determines who observes defections and refusals to trade; monitoring technology is parameterized by distance d and probability q of observing non-participation. Results characterize equilibrium investment bounds and optimal network architectures (complete components, minimally connected networks, stars) under different information-flow assumptions.","Summarizes the core structure of the theoretical game, including player roles, action sets, payoff functions, and the role of the network and monitoring parameters, as described in Sections 2–4.",1
10.1016_j.socec.2019.02.006.md,CPR – No Theft,"One of the four cells in the 2×2 design {CPR, PG} × {No Theft, Theft}; this is the CPR condition without outsiders.",1,"Game type: CPR (common-pool resource); Theft condition: No Theft (no outsiders, no sanctions stage).",Design section explicitly describes CPR vs PG and No Theft vs Theft as crossed treatment factors.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 120 student subjects making decisions for monetary payoffs.,1,True,Randomly assigned subjects to treatments and used controlled payoff structures.,1,True,Conducted in a university lab using z-Tree; on-site sessions of 90 minutes.,1,False,All results are from human-subject sessions; no separate computer-only simulations.,1,False,"Paper contains theory but this condition refers to the lab experiment, not the analytical model.",1,4,“Subjects were divided into independent groups of four and remained in their groups… Groups played fifteen rounds of either a CPR or PG game” for the No Theft treatments.,1,15,Groups played fifteen rounds of either a CPR or PG game.,1,1,"Insiders chose how much of a 50-unit endowment to allocate; implies a continuous (0–50) choice, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Subjects start with an endowment and choose how much to invest; endowment is initially in private account.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear surplus function V(G); no constant marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,No communication or chat is mentioned; authors later note absence of explicit coordination mechanisms like communication.,1,1,"Insiders learned their final payoffs each round and were given payoff tables; outsiders do not exist in this condition. Exact feedback detail on others’ earnings is not fully spelled out, but standard in such designs and partially implied.",0.5,0,No punishment stage in No Theft treatments.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism described anywhere.,1,1,"Finite horizon of 15 rounds is common knowledge from instructions in such experiments, though not quoted verbatim.",0.8,0,Punishment (“Deductions”) only appears in theft treatments; No Theft treatments end after payoff calculation.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this condition.,1,0,Only ‘Deductions’ (negative sanctions) are described; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism.,1,50,Parameterization section sets e_hi = e_ho = 50; procedures say identical endowment each round.,1,0.2766,"Average CPR allocation to group account is 36.17 with endowment 50. Contribution rate = 36.17/50 ≈ 0.7234 to CPR (appropriation from resource). Cooperation index for CPR is defined as 1 − g/e. Reported mean cooperation index is 0.28, so g/e ≈ 0.72. But DV_contributionRate is defined as fraction of endowment contributed (to group account). For CPR, “allocation” is appropriation from CPR (group account), not contribution. To maintain consistency with instructions, use cooperation index as normalized pro-social measure: 0.28.",0.4,36.17,"Table 3: ‘Allocation’ in CPR, No theft is 36.17.",1,N/R,They report profits and benchmarks but not realized efficiency as ratio to social optimum.,1,N/R,Table 3 reports average individual profit (97.06) but not group-level totals.,1,United States,Experiment run at University of Massachusetts-Amherst in the U.S.,1,N/R,Age used as a control variable but no summary stats reported.,1,N/R,Gender used as control but no distribution reported.,1,Undergraduate students,“120 subjects were recruited from the undergraduate population at the University of Massachusetts-Amherst.”,1,On site,Physical lab sessions with cash payment and z-Tree at University of Massachusetts-Amherst.,1,"Nonlinear CPR game with four insiders, partners matching, 15 periods, endowment 50, private good return c=1, group surplus V(G)=aG−bG^2 with a=6, b=0.025. Payoff: π_h = c(e−g_h) + (g_h/G) V(G). No outsiders, no sanctions.",Summarized from model parameterization and procedures for CPR No Theft control.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2019.02.006.md,PG – No Theft,Second cell in the 2×2 design: public good game without outsiders.,1,"Game type: PG (public good); Theft condition: No Theft (no outsiders, no sanctions stage).",Design section: treatments are CPR vs PG crossed with No Theft vs Theft.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled incentives and randomized assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in university laboratory using z-Tree; in-person sessions.,1,False,This condition uses only human participants.,1,False,Analytical model exists but this row refers to the lab condition.,1,4,“Subjects were divided into independent groups of four…” for No Theft controls.,1,15,Groups played fifteen rounds of either a CPR or PG game.,1,1,Subjects choose ‘how much’ of the 50-unit endowment to invest; implies a continuous decision.,0.9,0,Endowment starts in private account; contributions are active choices.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear public good with V(G)=aG−bG^2; no constant MPCR reported.,1,0,No communication allowed; authors explicitly note lack of coordination mechanisms like communication.,1,1,Subjects see final payoffs and payoff tables; exact detail of others’ outcomes not fully specified but some aggregate info implied.,0.5,0,No punishment possible in No Theft conditions.,1,0,No reward mechanism is used.,1,1,Fifteen-round horizon is part of instructions; common knowledge.,0.8,0,Sanctions (“Deductions”) only in Theft treatments.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No positive reward actions implemented.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,50,Parameter e_hi = 50 and procedures describe identical endowment each round.,1,0.3478,"In PG, Allocation is contribution to group account. Average allocation 17.39 with endowment 50, so 17.39/50 ≈ 0.3478.",1,17.39,"Table 3: PG, No theft Allocation = 17.39.",1,N/R,They do not report realized efficiency as share of social optimum surplus.,1,N/R,"They report individual profit 102.67, not group total.",1,United States,Conducted at University of Massachusetts-Amherst.,1,N/R,Age only used as control; no descriptive statistics given.,1,N/R,Gender only appears as a regression control.,1,Undergraduate students,Subjects drawn from undergraduate population.,1,On site,In-lab monetary experiment with subjects present and paid in cash.,1,"Nonlinear public good game with four insiders, partners matching, 15 periods, endowment 50, private good return c=1, group surplus V(G)=aG−bG^2 with a=6, b=0.025. Payoff: π_h = c(e−g_h) + (1/n_i) V(G). No outsiders, no sanctions.",Summarized from model and procedures for PG No Theft control.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2019.02.006.md,CPR – Theft,Third cell in the 2×2 design: CPR game with outsider theft and insider sanctions.,1,Game type: CPR; Theft condition: Theft (two outsiders can poach surplus; insiders can sanction).,"Design: {CPR, PG}×{No Theft, Theft}; this is CPR with Theft.",1,True,Human subjects (insiders and outsiders) in lab.,1,True,Randomized treatment structure and incentivized decisions.,1,True,Executed in a university experimental economics lab with z-Tree.,1,False,No mention of agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,"This row refers to the experimental condition, not the theoretical benchmarks.",1,4,Each group of six has four insiders and two outsiders; playerCount refers to number of players in the social dilemma (insiders).,1,15,Same repeated structure as No Theft: 15 rounds.,1,1,Insiders and outsiders both choose continuous effort from endowment; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,0.9,0,Endowment is given at start of each round and subjects actively decide how much to invest; baseline in private account.,0.9,N/R,CPR surplus is nonlinear; no single MPCR reported.,1,0,No communication allowed; authors explicitly remark on lack of coordination mechanisms.,1,1,Insiders are informed of outsiders’ decisions and resulting payoffs and see their own and cumulative payoffs; implies some cross-player payoff information each round.,0.7,1,"Insiders choose deductions for each individual outsider, so they must know which outsider is which (IDs shown).",0.9,0,No reward mechanism is provided.,1,1,Fifteen-round horizon is part of instructions; typical in such lab designs.,0.8,1,Insiders can levy costly ‘Deductions’ on each outsider after observing theft.,1,1,They use Gächter and Fehr (2000) 1:3 sanction technology: 1 ED spent reduces outsider payoff by 3 ED. Thus cost per punishment point is 1 ED.,1,3,Explicitly: ‘We used the 1:3 sanctions technology …’ meaning each ED spent cuts 3 ED from outsider.,1,0,Only negative ‘Deductions’ are available; no positive transfers.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,50,Parameterization sets e_hi = e_ho = 50; procedures confirm same for insiders and outsiders.,1,0.34,"CPR cooperation index reported as 0.34. For CPR, index = 1 − (g/e), so normalized ‘good’ behavior is this index. DV_contributionRate defined as normalized contribution; for CPR, pro-social behavior is lower appropriation, i.e., index value 0.34.",0.5,32.82,"Table 3: CPR, Theft Allocation = 32.82.",1,N/R,No explicit measure of realized efficiency vs maximum in presence of theft and sanctions.,1,N/R,Only individual profits reported (73.78) for insiders; full group payoff not tabulated.,1,United States,All sessions at University of Massachusetts-Amherst.,1,N/R,Age only appears as a covariate.,1,N/R,Gender used as a regression control; no aggregate reported.,1,Undergraduate students,All subjects were undergraduates at UMass-Amherst.,1,On site,In-person lab environment with real payments.,1,"Six-person groups: four insiders in a nonlinear CPR game and two outsiders. Partners matching for 15 rounds. Insiders: endowment 50, choose harvest g_h from CPR vs private good with return c=1, surplus V(G)=aG−bG^2 with a=6, b=0.025, CPR payoff π_hi = c(e−g_h)+ (g_h/G)V(G) minus equal share of any theft. Outsiders: endowment 50, choose effort x toward ‘take’ vs private good; max each can take is 25% of insiders’ surplus (so at most 50% total loss). After outsiders’ decisions, insiders observe each outsider’s theft and payoff and can assign costly deductions with 1:3 technology, limited by their own initial payoffs.",Synthesized from model and procedures for theft treatments.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2019.02.006.md,PG – Theft,Fourth cell in the 2×2 design: public good game with outsider theft and insider sanctions.,1,Game type: PG; Theft condition: Theft (two outsiders can steal surplus; insiders may punish).,Explicitly described as PG Theft treatment in 2×2 design.,1,True,Uses real human insiders and outsiders in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized game with random assignment to treatments.",1,True,Conducted in the econ lab at a U.S. university using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation/agent-based component here.,1,False,"This row reflects experimental condition, not theory.",1,4,Four insiders form the PG group within each six-person set.,1,15,Same number of rounds as other conditions.,1,1,Insiders choose ‘how much’ to contribute; continuous decision.,0.9,0,Endowment entirely under subject control; contributions are active choices.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear PG; no single marginal per capita return reported.,1,0,"No communication, voting, or chat in design.",1,1,"Insiders see outsiders’ decisions and payoffs, and both insiders and outsiders see their own and cumulative payoffs; implies some feedback on others’ outcomes.",0.7,1,"Insiders sanction each outsider individually, requiring identity of each outsider.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,1,Fifteen-round horizon stated in instructions.,0.8,1,Insiders have a Deductions stage to reduce outsiders’ earnings.,1,1,Same 1:3 sanctions technology as CPR Theft; 1 ED of cost per deduction point.,1,3,Gächter and Fehr (2000) 1:3 technology used in both theft treatments.,1,0,No positive sanctions described.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist.,1,50,e_hi = e_ho = 50 in parameterization; outsiders and insiders have same endowment.,1,0.3242,PG allocation is contribution. Average allocation 16.21; endowment 50; 16.21/50 ≈ 0.3242.,1,16.21,"Table 3: PG, Theft Allocation = 16.21.",1,N/R,Efficiency vs social optimum under theft and sanctions is not directly computed.,1,N/R,They provide average individual profit (81.55) but not total group earnings.,1,United States,Same subject pool and location as other treatments.,1,N/R,"Not summarized, only used as regressor.",1,N/R,Gender appears only as a regression covariate.,1,Undergraduate students,All subjects from undergraduate population.,1,On site,Lab experiment in person with cash payouts.,1,"Six-person groups: four insiders play a nonlinear public good game, two outsiders can steal. Partners matching for 15 rounds. Insiders: endowment 50, decide contribution g_h to Account 1 (public account) vs private good with return c=1; surplus V(G)=aG−bG^2 with a=6, b=0.025; payoff π_hi = c(e−g_h) + (1/4)V(G) minus equal share of any theft. Outsiders: endowment 50, choose effort x to ‘take’ from insider surplus vs private good; cannot take more than 25% of surplus each (max 50% total). After theft, insiders see each outsider’s action and payoff and can assign costly deductions with 1:3 technology, constrained by their own initial payoff; final payoffs incorporate sanctions and are accumulated across periods.",Summarizes the PG Theft treatment from procedures and parameterization.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2015.10.001.md,Control – baseline Prisoner's Dilemma,Control treatment described in Section 4 with no trust-building or punishment mechanism; only the PD stage (Table 3).,1,"Trust-building/punishment mechanism: none (no vulnerability choice, no punishment stage).",Treatment variable is presence/type of vulnerability/punishment mechanism; Control has none.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 240 human subjects across 10 sessions; this treatment uses human participants.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab treatment within an experimental design.,1,True,Conducted in the CREED laboratory at the University of Amsterdam.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations are reported.,1,False,"This record refers to the empirical treatment, not the theoretical model.",1,2,Pairs of subjects play a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma each round.,1,50,Each session ran 50 rounds with random rematching.,1,0,"Binary choice: Cooperate (C) or Defect (D), no continuous contribution amount.",1,0,Standard Prisoner’s Dilemma with no endowment allocation decision to a public account.,1,N/A,"Not a public goods game; payoffs given directly by PD matrix, so MPCR not defined.",1,0,No communication between subjects is mentioned; only information via history screens.,0.9,1,Social history screen with recent outcomes/actions for all pairs in matching group is always visible.,1,0,No punishment mechanism in this treatment; IDs are never revealed and partners are anonymous.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in any treatment; identities not revealed.,1,1,Subjects know there are 50 rounds; standard in such designs and implied by wording about rounds 1–50.,0.8,0,Control: game consists only of PD stage; no punishment stage or mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No reward or positive transfer mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No per-round token endowment to allocate; payoffs directly from PD matrix. They do have a starting capital of 500 points but not an endowment per round to contribute.,0.7,0.043,"In second half (rounds 26–50) ‘cooperation rate is around 4%’; table shows 4.25%. Treat cooperation decision C as contribution=1, so rate≈0.043.",0.9,N/R,"Only binary C vs D is reported, not a numeric contribution amount.",1,0.4743,Table 6 Panel B: Control efficiency second half = 47.43% (of maximum 110 points per pair).,1,N/R,"Only normalized efficiency percentages are provided, not raw average payoffs by treatment and half.",0.6,Netherlands,"Experiment conducted at University of Amsterdam, Netherlands; typical subject pool is local students.",0.9,N/R,Paper does not report ages; only that subjects are mainly undergraduate students.,1,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,1,Mainly undergraduate university students from various fields,"Explicitly stated: 'Subjects were mainly undergraduate students from various fields (e.g. economics, business, psychology, law)'.",1,On site,Conducted in physical CREED laboratory; not online.,1,"Two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs: (C,C)=(55,55), (C,D)=(5,70), (D,C)=(70,5), (D,D)=(25,25); 24 subjects per session, 3 matching groups of 8, strangers-style rematching within group forbidding same partner in consecutive rounds; social history screen with last 10 rounds’ actions and outcomes in matching group.",Directly from Table 3 and design description in Section 4.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2015.10.001.md,Gradual – endogenous vulnerability with gradual mechanism,Treatment 'Gradual' described in Section 4 using the gradual vulnerability mechanism via an automated increasing punishment-level clock.,1,"Trust-building/punishment mechanism: Gradual endogenous vulnerability (clock raising possible punishment levels up to 50, with drop-out and 4-second pause allowing partner to match).",Experimental treatments differ by trust-building mechanism; this row is the Gradual variant.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects under the Gradual treatment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with random rematching and explicit instructions.,1,True,Conducted in CREED laboratory at University of Amsterdam.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation for this treatment.,1,False,Analytical model is separate; this entry concerns the empirical treatment.,1,2,Pairs of two players interact in each round.,1,50,Same design across treatments: 50 rounds per session.,1,0,PD choice is discrete (C or D).,1,0,No pre-allocated contribution; only PD actions and/or punishment.,1,N/A,Not a linear public good; PD with fixed payoffs.,1,0,No communication option beyond observation of history information is mentioned.,0.9,1,"Social history screen shows recent possible punishment levels, PD actions, and punishment decisions for all pairs in matching group.",1,0,Subjects cannot identify whom they are matched with; only anonymous history is shown.,1,0,No reward stage and partners remain anonymous.,1,1,Subjects know experiment length (50 rounds) and analysis refers to 'first half' and 'second half' in instructions/results.,0.8,1,Third stage in each round where subjects can punish partner at a cost.,1,4,"If a subject punishes, she incurs a cost of 4 points.",1,1,Partner loses an amount equal to his own possible punishment level chosen in stage 1; one unit of punishment choice translates one-to-one into deduction units.,1,0,"Only costly punishment is available, no reward mechanism.",1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,0,No per-round contribution endowment; they start experiment with 500 points globally and then earn/loss points via PD and punishment.,0.7,0.3625,"Cooperation rate in second half for Gradual = 36.25% (Table 4 Panel A); treat C as full contribution, D as zero.",1,N/R,"Only binary actions reported, no numeric contribution amount.",1,0.6005,Table 6 Panel B: Gradual efficiency second half = 60.05% of maximum 110 points per pair.,1,N/R,"Only normalized efficiency percentages given by treatment and half, no raw mean payoffs.",0.6,Netherlands,"CREED lab at University of Amsterdam, Netherlands.",0.9,N/R,Not reported; only that they are mainly undergraduates.,1,N/R,Gender composition not mentioned.,1,Mainly undergraduate university students from various fields,Same subject pool description applies to all treatments.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at CREED.,1,"Three-stage game each round. Stage 1: automated clock raises own possible punishment level from 0 to 50; subject can stop clock at an integer level. If one stops, clock pauses 4 seconds for partner to optionally stop at same level; otherwise continues until partner stops or 50 is reached (auto-drop at 50). Stage 2: standard PD with payoffs (C,C)=(55,55), (C,D)=(5,70), (D,C)=(70,5), (D,D)=(25,25). Stage 3: each may punish partner; punisher pays 4 points; partner loses an amount equal to their own chosen punishment level from Stage 1. Random rematching in fixed groups of 8, no immediate rematch with same partner, social history screen shows last 10 rounds’ punishment levels, actions and punishments from all pairs in matching group.",Direct from Gradual treatment description in Section 4 and Table 3.,1
10.1016_j.geb.2015.10.001.md,Leap – endogenous vulnerability with leap mechanism,Treatment 'Leap' described in Section 4 where subjects simultaneously choose an own possible punishment level (leap in the dark).,1,Trust-building/punishment mechanism: Leap endogenous vulnerability (simultaneous one-shot choice of own possible punishment level 0–50).,Independent variable is mechanism type; this is the Leap variant.,1,True,Laboratory experiment treatment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled treatment varying mechanism while holding other features constant.,1,True,Run in CREED lab at University of Amsterdam.,1,False,No simulation used for this treatment.,1,False,Treatment is empirical; theory analyzed separately.,1,2,Two-player PD each round.,1,50,Same 50-round sessions.,1,0,Binary cooperate/defect decision.,1,0,No continuous contributions; only PD actions and punishment.,1,N/A,Not a public good game.,1,0,No mention of any messaging or discussion; only decision screens and history.,0.9,1,"Social history screen visible with recent punishment levels, actions, and punishments for all pairs.",1,0,Subjects never learn partner identity in history screen.,1,0,No rewards and anonymity maintained.,1,1,Experiment length is common knowledge and analysis refers to halves of the 50 rounds.,0.8,1,Third-stage punishment identical to Gradual treatment.,1,4,Punisher incurs 4-point cost per punishment decision.,1,1,Partner loses an amount equal to his own possible punishment level from stage 1 per punishment.,1,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,0,"No per-round endowment to allocate; all payoffs come from PD and punishment, with initial capital 500 points overall.",0.7,0.265,Cooperation rate in second half for Leap = 26.50% (Table 4 Panel A).,1,N/R,Only binary C vs D actions observed.,1,0.5364,Table 6 Panel B: Leap efficiency second half = 53.64%.,1,N/R,Raw payoffs per pair are not tabulated by treatment; only efficiency proportions.,0.6,Netherlands,Same lab and subject pool as other treatments.,0.9,N/R,Not reported for any treatment.,1,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,1,Mainly undergraduate university students from various fields,Applies to entire experimental subject pool.,1,On site,Conducted in the CREED laboratory.,1,"Three-stage game per round. Stage 1: both subjects simultaneously choose an integer own possible punishment level between 0 and 50 (leap in the dark). Stage 2: Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs (C,C)=(55,55), (C,D)=(5,70), (D,C)=(70,5), (D,D)=(25,25). Stage 3: optional punishment; if a subject punishes, she pays 4 points and partner loses an amount equal to his own stage-1 punishment level. Parameters: cooperation-supporting conditional cooperator range begins at own punishment level ≥15. Random rematching within fixed groups of 8 with no immediate rematch; social history screen displays last 10 rounds’ punishment levels, actions, and punishments for all pairs (ordered by own punishment of player 1).",Direct from Leap description and overall design in Section 4.,1
