10.1155_2020_8276392.md,"Simulation – Baseline (Xinjiang county parameterization, x=0.5, y=0.5, z=0.5)",Authors set a specific parameter set from a Xinjiang county (Table 5) and initial cooperation probabilities x=y=z=0.5 and numerically simulate the evolutionary game; treated as baseline condition.,0.94,"Initial cooperation probabilities: local government x=0.5, social organizations y=0.5, poverty group z=0.5; payoff parameters from Table 5 (C1=1200, C2=560, C3=890, C4=650, W1=100, W2=450, W3=300, k1A1=20, S1=200, S2=100, S3=1100, S4=400, (1−k2)A2=300).",Condition defined by initial state and fixed parameter values used for all subsequent comparative simulations.,0.9,False,No human decision making; only parameterization from survey data feeding an evolutionary game model.,1,False,No controlled assignment of human subjects; it is a numerical simulation of differential equations.,1,False,No laboratory or field experiment with participants.,1,True,Authors use Matlab to simulate the evolutionary dynamics with specified parameters.,1,True,"Paper derives closed-form evolutionary dynamics, equilibrium conditions, and stability analytically.",1,3,"Evolutionary game between three agents: local government, social organizations, poverty group.",1,0,"Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics, not discrete repeated rounds in a game with human players.",0.9,1,"Strategies are binary (cooperate / not), but contributions are not amounts; public goods framing with contribution levels is not used. Coded as 1 meaning this PGG-specific flag is not restrictive here.",0.3,N/A,No human endowment or contribution mechanism; not a PGG contribution game.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return or explicit public goods multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,N/A,No human interface or feedback; purely modeled payoffs.,1,N/A,"Punishment is represented as payoff parameters, not identity-based actions.",1,N/A,"Rewards/subsidies are modeled as parameters, not identity-linked observables.",1,N/A,No participant interface; continuous-time model.,1,1,Negative payoffs k1A1 and (1−k2)A2 represent penalties/negative income for noncooperation.,1,N/R,Model specifies magnitudes of negative payoffs but not a separate cost-to-impact ratio per punishment unit as in PGG punishment schemes.,0.2,N/R,"No explicit linear punishment technology (e.g., 1 coin reduces 3 tokens) is defined.",0.2,1,"Subsidies/support (C1, W3, S3, S4) act as rewards for cooperative behavior.",0.9,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost of granting rewards is specified; only net payoffs are parameterized.,0.2,N/R,No explicit mapping of reward units to payoffs as a technology parameter.,0.2,N/A,"Model concerns payoffs and support parameters, not divisible endowments given to strategic agents.",1,N/A,"No notion of contribution relative to endowment; focus is on cooperation probabilities and stability, not amount contributions.",1,N/A,No token/monetary contribution decisions; strategies are cooperate vs not.,1,N/R,They discuss Pareto optimality qualitatively but do not compute an efficiency ratio of realized vs maximal payoffs.,0.6,N/R,"Model provides symbolic payoffs; simulations report evolution of strategies, not aggregate numeric payoff outcomes.",0.5,N/A,No human participants; only contextual data from Xinjiang surveys used to set parameters.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results come from analytical derivations and Matlab simulations of an evolutionary game model.,1,"Three-player evolutionary game among local government, social organizations, and poverty groups. Each actor chooses between cooperative and noncooperative strategies. Payoff parameters include government support (C1), local government costs (C2), external benefits (C3), opportunity cost losses (C4), social organization basic benefits and costs (W1, W2), support they receive (W3), and punishment-like negative payoffs (k1A1, (1−k2)A2). Dynamics are governed by replicator equations in continuous time, and Matlab is used to simulate trajectories for different initial cooperation probabilities and parameter variations.",Summarizes the structure of the modeled strategic interaction as described in Sections 3–5 and Table 5.,0.96
10.1155_2020_8276392.md,Simulation – Varying superior government support C1,"Section 5(2), Figures 4–5: authors vary C1 while holding other parameters at baseline and examine evolutionary paths.",0.96,"Superior government support to local government C1 varied (e.g., 1200, 1300, 1400, 1500, 10000) with baseline parameters otherwise; initial cooperation x=0.5, y=0.5, z=0.5.",Text under Figures 4–5 lists multiple C1 values while keeping other parameters and initial conditions fixed.,0.9,False,Numerical simulation of model; no new human data.,1,False,"Parameter sweep in a simulation, not an experiment with human subjects.",1,False,Not conducted in a behavioral lab.,1,True,Uses Matlab to simulate replicator dynamics for different C1 values.,1,True,Relies on earlier analytical derivation; simulation illustrates those results.,1,3,Same three agents as baseline model.,1,0,Continuous replicator dynamics; no discrete rounds.,0.9,1,Binary strategies cooperate/noncooperate; not a contribution-amount game.,0.4,N/A,No contributions or endowments.,1,N/R,Model has no public goods multiplier.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No interface or displayed summaries.,1,N/A,"Punishment is parametric, not implemented as targeted actions.",1,N/A,Rewards are part of payoff parameters only.,1,N/A,No rounds shown to participants.,1,1,Negative payoff parameters remain part of the model in these simulations.,1,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost technology is introduced when varying C1.,0.2,N/R,Punishment is not parameterized as a linear technology.,0.2,1,C1 is itself a reward/subsidy from superior government to local government.,1,N/R,Cost to superior government is not included in this three-player payoff matrix.,0.3,N/R,They vary the magnitude of C1 but not a separate transformation rate.,0.3,N/A,No endowment decisions.,1,N/A,No contribution decision; outcome variables are strategy probabilities.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,They analyze whether cooperation is achieved but do not quantify efficiency under different C1.,0.5,N/R,"Graphs depict evolution of x, y, z, not aggregate payoffs as numbers for each C1.",0.5,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Matlab simulations only.,1,Same three-player evolutionary game as baseline. Independent variable is the magnitude of superior government support C1 to the local government. Finding: raising C1 speeds local government’s convergence to active cooperation but does not induce cooperation in social organizations or poverty groups; overall tripartite cooperation is not achieved solely by increasing C1.,Summarizes model and main qualitative result from Figures 4–5 discussion.,0.94
10.1155_2020_8276392.md,Simulation – Increasing support to poverty groups S3 and S4,"Section 5(2), Figures 6–7: simulations where local government support to poor (S3) and social organization support to poor (S4) are increased.",0.95,"Support to poverty groups: S3 (local government support) and S4 (social organization support) increased above baseline (S3=1100, S4=400), with other parameters at Table 5 values; initial x=0.5, y=0.5, z=0.5.",Text explicitly analyzes effect of improving S3 and S4 on evolutionary outcomes.,0.88,False,Simulation study with no human decisions.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Matlab-based simulation of replicator dynamics under varied S3 and S4.,1,True,Relies on analytically derived dynamics; simulations visualize.,1,3,Same triadic game structure.,1,0,Continuous-time model.,0.9,1,Binary cooperate/noncooperate strategies.,0.4,N/A,No contribution mechanics.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,"Punishment is encoded in payoffs, not as targeted actions.",1,N/A,"Rewards are generic subsidies, not identity-linked.",1,N/A,No rounds displayed.,1,1,"Negative payoffs (k1A1, (1−k2)A2) remain in the model.",1,N/R,No explicit per-unit cost when S3 and S4 are varied.,0.2,N/R,No functional punishment technology parameterized.,0.2,1,S3 and S4 represent rewards/support for cooperative poor groups.,1,N/R,Model does not distinguish resource cost to providers from net payoffs.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mapping of support units to payoffs beyond S3 and S4 values.,0.3,N/A,No endowment concept.,1,N/A,"Outcomes are cooperation probabilities, not contribution rates.",1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,"They focus on whether poor groups shift to cooperation and on stability, not on numeric efficiency.",0.5,N/R,No numerical aggregate payoff results for varied S3/S4 are reported.,0.5,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Numerical simulations.,1,"Same three-player evolutionary game. Here the independent manipulation is increasing S3 (support from local government to poor) and S4 (support from social organizations to poor). Qualitative result: stronger S3 and S4 accelerate and eventually induce a shift of the poverty group from passive to active cooperation, while local government behavior is mostly unchanged and social organizations may converge faster to noncooperation if bearing higher support costs.",Based on narrative around Figures 6–7 about effects of increasing S3 and S4.,0.9
10.1155_2020_8276392.md,Simulation – Increasing government support to social organizations W3,"Section 5(2), Figures 8–9: W3 (support from local government to social organizations) is increased to see effects.",0.95,"Local government support to social organizations W3 increased above baseline W3=300, with other parameters from Table 5; initial x=0.5, y=0.5, z=0.5.",Text explicitly studies influence of improving W3 on evolution.,0.9,False,Simulation only.,1,False,"Parameter sweep in simulation, not an experiment with people.",1,False,Not lab-based.,1,True,Matlab simulations with W3 varied.,1,True,Interpretation uses underlying analytical model.,1,3,Same triadic model.,1,0,Continuous time.,0.9,1,Binary strategy choice.,0.4,N/A,No contribution scheme.,1,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No displayed summaries.,1,N/A,Punishment not modeled as directed actions.,1,N/A,Rewards (W3) are generic subsidies.,1,N/A,No rounds concept.,1,1,Negative payoff parameters retained.,1,N/R,No explicit punishment technology.,0.2,N/R,Not specified.,0.2,1,W3 acts as a reward/subsidy to social organizations for cooperation.,1,N/R,Cost to local government of W3 not modeled separately.,0.3,N/R,No general reward technology coefficient.,0.3,N/A,No endowment decisions.,1,N/A,Only cooperative strategy probabilities.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,Only qualitative behavior noted: social organizations still do not cooperate sufficiently; no efficiency figures.,0.5,N/R,No explicit payoff trajectories reported for W3 changes.,0.5,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Simulation study.,1,"Three-player evolutionary game as before. Here W3 (support from local government to social organizations) is increased to examine whether social organizations start to cooperate. Reported insight: even with increased W3, social organizations often do not select the cooperative strategy because their own subsidy to the poor and insufficient governmental support make cooperation unattractive; thus, government support to social organizations must be substantially increased to change their strategic choice.",Paraphrases authors’ discussion linked to Figures 8–9.,0.9
10.1155_2020_8276392.md,Simulation – Increasing negative income (penalties) k1A1 and (1−k2)A2,"Section 5(3), Figures 10–11: authors vary negative income (losses) to social organizations and poor groups for noncooperation.",0.95,"Negative payoff parameters for noncooperation: k1A1 (loss to social organizations) and (1−k2)A2 (loss to poverty groups) increased above baseline (k1A1=20, (1−k2)A2=300); other parameters baseline; initial x=0.5, y=0.5, z=0.5.",Text explicitly analyzes raising the negative earnings of social organizations and poverty groups.,0.9,False,No human experimentation.,1,False,Parameter variation in simulation.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,Matlab simulations of replicator system under modified penalty parameters.,1,True,Within analytical evolutionary game framework.,1,3,Same model triad.,1,0,Continuous dynamics.,0.9,1,Binary cooperation choice.,0.4,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No interface summaries.,1,N/A,Punishment not agent-targeted in a human context.,1,N/A,No identities; parameters only.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,This condition explicitly manipulates penalty magnitudes.,1,N/R,Cost to the punishing side is not modeled as separate from the negative payoff to the punished.,0.3,N/R,They scale penalties directly; no cost-effect ratio is specified.,0.3,1,"Support parameters like C1, W3, S3, S4 remain in force.",1,N/R,Reward costs not separated from payoffs.,0.3,N/R,No explicit reward technology discussed here.,0.3,N/A,No endowment decisions.,1,N/A,Outcome of interest is strategic cooperation probabilities under higher penalties.,1,N/A,No amount contributions.,1,N/R,"They describe that increasing penalties promotes cooperation, but no efficiency ratio is computed.",0.5,N/R,No numerical total payoffs reported under altered penalties.,0.5,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Simulation.,1,"Same three-player evolutionary model, but the negative income parameters k1A1 for social organizations and (1−k2)A2 for poverty groups when they do not cooperate are increased. Results: larger penalties make noncooperation less attractive; social organizations and poverty groups eventually switch from noncooperative to cooperative strategies once losses pass a threshold, showing that moderate increases in punishment can bind behavior and promote cooperation.",Summarizes qualitative findings noted in Section 5(3).,0.94
10.1155_2020_8276392.md,Simulation – Increasing basic benefits W1 and S1,"Section 5(4), Figures 12–13: authors vary basic economic benefits of social organizations (W1) and of poor groups (S1).",0.95,"Basic benefits of cooperation increased: W1 (social organizations’ basic benefits from targeted poverty alleviation) and S1 (poverty groups’ basic benefits from cooperation) raised above baseline W1=100, S1=200, with other parameters at Table 5 values; initial x=0.5, y=0.5, z=0.5.",Text explicitly: 'increasing the basic economic benefits of social organizations and poverty groups' and analyzes impacts.,0.9,False,"Numerical model, no human subjects.",1,False,Simulated parameter manipulation.,1,False,No lab involved.,1,True,Matlab simulations of replicator system as W1 and S1 increase.,1,True,Analysis grounded in evolutionary game equations.,1,3,Unchanged triadic setting.,1,0,Continuous-time trajectories.,0.9,1,Strategies are all-or-nothing cooperation decisions.,0.4,N/A,No endowment contributions.,1,N/R,No PGG return rate.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,Penalty not identity-targeted.,1,N/A,Rewards are modeled parameters.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds for humans.,1,1,Negative income parameters still exist.,1,N/R,Not decomposed.,0.3,N/R,Not specified.,0.3,1,"W1 and S1 themselves are benefits from cooperating, akin to rewards.",1,N/R,No explicit accounting of who bears cost of increased W1/S1.,0.3,N/R,No explicit technology mapping resource to reward.,0.3,N/A,No endowments defined.,1,N/A,"Outcome variables are cooperation probabilities (x, y, z); no contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,They describe that increasing W1 and S1 leads towards a Pareto-optimal cooperative state but do not compute efficiency numerically.,0.6,N/R,No reported numeric group payoffs across W1/S1 levels.,0.5,N/A,No sample of human participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Purely simulated.,1,"Three-player evolutionary game. Here basic benefits from cooperation for social organizations (W1) and for poverty groups (S1) are increased. Findings: as W1 and especially S1 rise, the evolutionary equilibrium shifts from (1,0,0) to (1,0,1) and finally to full cooperation (1,1,1). Social organizations initially fluctuate but converge to cooperation when W1 is high enough (around 500 in the plotted example), while poverty groups rapidly shift to full cooperation as S1 increases, indicating that boosting endogenous economic returns to the poor is crucial for achieving Pareto-optimal multiagent collaboration.",Based on narrative around Figures 12–13 describing evolution from partial to full cooperation.,0.94
10.1093_oep_gpy045.md,"J-P (Joint Decision, Partner)","Joint-decision treatment with partner matching as named in Section 2.1 ('Joint Decision, Partner', J-P).",1,Decision unit = pair (joint decision via chat); Matching protocol = partner (fixed pair–pair match for all 15 rounds); Identity within pair = known,"Independent variables varied across treatments are decision unit (pair vs individual), matching protocol (partner vs stranger), and within-pair identity/communication features.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects at University of Michigan.,1,True,Authors randomly assigned subjects to treatments and controlled incentives and matching.,1,True,Conducted in a university laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental design and regressions, not closed-form analytical models as a standalone study.",1,2,Two decision-making units (pairs) interact in each group in a two-player public goods game.,1,15,“The number of interactions is 15 in all of the treatments.”,1,1,Pairs choose any non-negative integer contribution from 0 to 40; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment is given as private points; players must actively allocate to group account.,1,0.8,Each pair and its counterpart each obtain 0.8 per point contributed to group; group size is 2 so MPCR is 0.8.,1,1,“given one minute to freely discuss ... using an electronic chat system in each period.”,1,0,Paper describes feedback about partner and counterparts’ contributions/payoffs only implicitly via payoffs; no summaries or histories are described.,0.4,0,No punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Game is finitely repeated with 15 known periods; standard design implies subjects are told number of periods.,0.7,0,Baseline two-player public goods game without any punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward or bonus mechanism beyond standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,40,“each pair is endowed with 40 points.”,1,0.8,Text: contributions increase and remain around 80% of endowment until period 13; using that as typical sustained level.,0.5,32,80% of 40-point endowment = 32 points; matches described sustained level.,0.5,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is not explicitly reported for this treatment.,0,N/R,Only average payoffs across treatments are briefly mentioned; no treatment-specific group payoff level.,0,USA,Sessions conducted at University of Michigan (USA).,0.95,N/R,No age statistics reported; typical student sample but not specified.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0,Predominantly university students,Conducted at a university lab with standard subject pool; not explicitly specified but implied.,0.6,On site,Conducted in physical laboratory using z-Tree; not online.,1,"Two-player linear public goods game with pairs as decision units. Payoff per pair: π_i,t = 40 − C_i,t + 0.8·(C_i,t + C_j,t); each member receives half. Integer contributions 0–40. Partner matching: fixed pair–pair group for all 15 periods. Within-pair identity known; 1-minute text chat each period before making joint decision via individual proposals with random tie-breaking if disagreement.",Summarizes detailed structural features described in Sections 2 and 2.1.,1
10.1093_oep_gpy045.md,"J-S (Joint Decision, Stranger)","Joint-decision treatment with stranger matching as named in Section 2.1 ('Joint Decision, Stranger', J-S).",1,Decision unit = pair (joint decision via chat); Matching protocol = stranger (random re-matching of pairs each round); Identity within pair = known,Varies matching protocol relative to J-P while keeping joint decision and identity within pair.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment with random matching and incentives.,1,True,Run in the lab at University of Michigan using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations.,1,False,No standalone analytical model experiment.,1,2,Groups consist of two pairs (two decision-making units).,1,15,Same 15 periods as all treatments.,1,1,Pairs choose any integer contribution from 0 to 40.,1,0,Endowment entirely under private control initially.,1,0.8,Same payoff technology as J-P; MPCR reported as 0.8 in all four main treatments.,1,1,Same joint decision procedure with 1-minute electronic chat within pair every period.,1,0,No mention of history or summary displays beyond immediate outcomes.,0.4,0,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Finitely-repeated game with 15 periods likely announced to subjects.,0.7,0,No punishment stage is described.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,40,Each pair is endowed with 40 points in J-P and J-S treatments.,1,0.5325,Overall average contribution is reported indirectly as 21.3 points in comparison to I-S-Punishment (21.4); 21.3 / 40 ≈ 0.5325.,0.6,21.3,Section 4.5: “overall average contribution in the I-S-Punishment treatment (21.4 points) is almost identical to that in the J-S treatment (21.3 points)” so we take 21.3.,0.7,N/R,Efficiency not numerically reported per treatment.,0,26.4,"Near end of 4.5: “average payoffs were 26.4, 22.6 and 24.5 points in the J-S, J-