10.1093_oep_gpy045.md,"I-P-Punishment (Individual Decisions, Partner, Punishment)",Additional individual-decision treatment with partner matching and informal punishment stage (I-P-Punishment).,1,Decision unit = individual; Matching protocol = partner; Informal punishment institution enabled after contribution stage,Section 4.5 defines I-P-Punishment as I-P with added informal punishment stage.,1,True,Human-subject experimental treatment.,1,True,Controlled addition of punishment to baseline design.,1,True,Two sessions per additional treatment run in same lab.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,2,Game is a two-player public goods with two individuals per group.,1,15,"Additional treatments are identical to I-P and I-S in structure, including 15 periods.",1,1,Contribution decision unchanged: any integer 0–40.,1,0,Endowment allocated privately; contributions chosen by individuals.,1,0.8,"Pre-punishment payoff structure identical to I-P, where MPCR is 0.8.",1,0,No communication stage is introduced in punishment treatments; only punishment decisions added.,1,0,Paper does not mention histories or summary screens; only per-period outcomes and punishment.,0.4,1,Standard informal punishment in two-player settings implies each knows who punished whom; they assign punishment points directly to their matched counterpart.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism in these treatments.,1,1,"Same finitely-repeated design as base treatments, so horizon is likely known.",0.7,1,Section 4.5: these treatments add 'a standard informal punishment stage after the contribution stage in each period'.,1,0.5,"For each punishment point assigned, 0.5 points are deducted from the punisher.",1,1.5,"For each punishment point assigned, 1.5 points are deducted from the punished (as inferred from payoff equation where −3·p_j→i,t is split by 0.5).",1,0,No positive transfers or rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,40,Identical to I-P: each subject endowed with 40 points.,1,0.8,"Section 4.5: contributions begin a little below 70% and are sustained at around 75–85%; center of that range is 80%, i.e., 0.8.",0.5,32,Using approximate central rate 0.8 · 40 = 32 points as typical contribution level.,0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency numbers given.,0,N/R,Paper notes that average payoffs are not significantly different from J-P and J-P Anonymous but does not give a number.,0,USA,Same lab and subject pool.,0.95,N/R,No age data.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,Predominantly university students,University lab subject pool.,0.6,On site,Lab at University of Michigan.,1,"Two-player linear public goods game with same payoff function as I-P, followed by a standard peer punishment stage each period. After observing contributions and resulting payoffs, each subject can assign integer punishment points to their fixed partner; each point costs the punisher 0.5 and reduces the partner’s payoff by 1.5. Partner matching is fixed across all 15 periods; no communication.",Section 4.5 and punishment payoff equation specify game structure.,1
10.1093_oep_gpy045.md,"I-S-Punishment (Individual Decisions, Stranger, Punishment)",Additional individual-decision treatment with stranger matching and informal punishment (I-S-Punishment).,1,Decision unit = individual; Matching protocol = stranger; Informal punishment institution enabled after contribution stage,"Section 4.5 calls this the 'I-S-Punishment' treatment, identical to I-S but with punishment.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled variant adding punishment and using stranger matching.,1,True,Run in lab sessions at University of Michigan.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical design.,1,2,Two individuals interact in each round.,1,15,Same as I-S and other treatments.,1,1,Contribution choice remains free between 0 and 40 points.,1,0,All 40 points initially in private account.,1,0.8,Contribution payoff technology identical to I-S with MPCR 0.8; punishment affects distribution after that.,1,0,No chat or communication is added; only punishment stage follows contributions.,1,0,No mention of histories or summary panels.,0.4,1,Punishment is bilateral between matched individuals; each knows whom they punish.,0.7,0,No reward option.,1,1,"Game is a finitely-repeated 15-period interaction, presumably announced.",0.7,1,Informal punishment stage after contributions.,1,0.5,Same punishment technology as I-P-Punishment: each point costs 0.5 to punisher.,1,1.5,Each punishment point reduces target’s payoff by 1.5.,1,0,No positive peer reward described.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,40,Same individual endowment as I-S and I-P-Punishment.,1,0.535,Section 4.5: overall average contribution in I-S-Punishment is 21.4 points; 21.4 / 40 ≈ 0.535.,0.8,21.4,Section 4.5 explicitly states 21.4 points.,0.9,N/R,Efficiency is not given numerically for this treatment.,0,24.5,"Section 4.5: “average payoffs were 26.4, 22.6 and 24.5 points in the J-S, J-S Anonymous and I-S-Punishment treatments, respectively.”",0.9,USA,Same subject pool.,0.95,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,Predominantly university students,University lab.,0.6,On site,Lab at University of Michigan.,1,"Same contribution stage as I-S (two-player linear public goods with 40-point endowment and MPCR 0.8) followed by informal punishment. After observing contributions and resulting earnings, each subject may assign integer punishment points to their randomly matched counterpart each period; each point costs the punisher 0.5 and reduces counterpart’s payoff by 1.5. Stranger matching between individuals each round; no communication.",Derived from Section 4.5 punishment description combined with I-S design.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0207937.md,Numerical experiments – parameter-sweep conditions,"Paper has only analytical modeling and numerical examples varying parameters (alpha, n, lambda, q_h); no distinct experimental treatments with human or real-world agents. All these numeric scenarios are one simulation/analytical study, grouped here.",0.8,"Model parameters in numerical analysis: fraction of low-risk 3PLs α ∈ {0,0.1,...,1}; number of 3PLs n ∈ {2,...,10}; loss-aversion parameter λ ∈ {1,...,3}; high delivery risk q_h ∈ {0.01,0.1,...,1}; other fixed parameters σ=1, F_h(c)=F_l(c)=c on [0,1], v=3, z=3, scoring rule S(b,t)=b−w√t, disutility h(e)=0.5e².","Section 'Numerical experiments' describes sensitivity analysis over α, n, λ, and q_h with other parameters fixed.",0.96,False,No human or field data; all results are analytical derivations and numerical calculations.,1.0,False,There is no controlled empirical experiment; the term 'numerical experiments' refers to computational analysis of the model.,1.0,False,No lab setting with participants; work is theoretical/numerical.,1.0,False,"They perform numerical sensitivity analysis on closed-form expressions, not an explicit agent-based or Monte Carlo simulation; classified here as analytical rather than simulation.",0.7,True,Core of the paper is analytical mechanism design with closed-form solutions; numerical section evaluates formulas for parameter values.,1.0,2,"Model describes one buyer (4PL) and n ≥ 2 sellers (3PLs). In numerical experiments they vary n ∈ {2,...,10}; taking baseline minimal n=2 as representative.",0.6,1,"Auction is modeled as a single reverse auction event followed by a contracting phase, not repeated games.",0.9,1,Agents submit continuous bid price and penalty levels; no discrete all-or-nothing contribution choice is present. Mapped to unconstrained/continuous action space.,0.7,N/A,This is not a public-goods or contribution game; no endowment allocation decision.,1.0,N/R,No public-good production with marginal per-capita returns is defined.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects or communication stage; purely theoretical.,1.0,0,Information structure is Bayesian with distributions; no mention of feedback or summaries shown across periods.,0.9,N/A,"No identities or agents; contingent penalty is a contract term, not peer punishment.",1.0,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identity revelation.,1.0,0,Single-shot mechanism; number of rounds not displayed to agents.,0.9,1,Model includes contingent penalty t_i paid by 3PL to 4PL if delivery risk occurs.,1.0,N/R,Cost of imposing penalty is embedded in transfer p and penalty t_i; no per-unit enforcement cost specified.,0.7,N/R,Penalty is modeled as t_i monetary units; no explicit conversion rate from punishment decision to payoff specified beyond 1:1 transfer.,0.7,1,"Cost-sharing parameter β defines a contract where 4PL shares realized costs with the winning 3PL, effectively a positive incentive for effort.",0.8,N/R,Payment rule p = βγ + (1−β)b defines transfer; no explicit per-unit reward cost distinct from payment itself.,0.7,N/R,No scalar 'reward per unit' is specified; incentives embedded in linear sharing rule.,0.7,N/R,Agents do not receive a fixed token endowment; they have cost distributions and valuations instead.,1.0,N/R,"No contribution behavior is modeled; outcomes are profits, bids, and mechanism performance.",1.0,N/R,No contribution amount exists in this auction/mechanism design model.,1.0,N/R,"They analyze 'ex ante expected profit of the system' and of the 4PL, but do not normalize by a theoretical maximum to report efficiency in [0,1].",0.8,N/R,"Total system profit Γ(Δ,β) is expressed symbolically and used in sensitivity figures, but no single numeric group payoff is reported for a specific baseline condition.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; mechanism is theoretical.,1.0,N/A,No human participants; mechanism is theoretical.,1.0,N/A,No human participants; mechanism is theoretical.,1.0,N/A,No human participants; mechanism is theoretical.,1.0,No human,"Study is purely analytical with numerical sensitivity analysis; no lab, online, or field environment.",1.0,"Single-buyer, multi-seller multi-attribute reverse auction (MARA) with cost uncertainty and delivery risk. One 4PL (buyer) faces n≥2 3PLs (sellers). Mechanisms: ESA-CC (efficient scoring auction with cost-sharing contract) and OSA-CC (optimal scoring auction with cost-sharing contract). 3PLs choose bid price b, contingent penalty t, and cost-reduction effort e. Loss aversion modeled via prospect theory with parameter λ>1, cost-sharing parameter β∈[0,1], and scoring rule S(b,t)=b−Λ(t) (numerically Λ(t)=w√t).","Summarized from 'Problem description', 'Equilibrium analysis', 'ESA-CC', 'OSA-CC', and 'Numerical experiments' sections.",1.0
10.1086_687873.md,"Replicator dynamics simulation – IPD with C, D, GT, GPGT (cost-free honest guilt signal)","Named based on description: population strategies C, D, GT, GPGT in an iterated prisoner’s dilemma with guilt-prone grim trigger and cost-free, unfakeable apology.",0.95,"Available strategies {C, D, GT, GPGT}; error probability α ∈ {0.01, 0.05}; number of rounds n ∈ {10, 100}; apologies always honest and impossible to fake.",These are the key parameters varied and specified in the description of this simulation set.,0.98,False,"Pure evolutionary game-theoretic simulation, no human participants mentioned.",1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical simulation.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational/theoretical.,1,True,Author explicitly describes using replicator dynamics simulations over populations of strategies.,1,False,"This part reports simulation outcomes, not closed-form analysis.",0.8,2,Iterated prisoner’s dilemma is defined as a 2-player game; no alternative group size indicated.,1,10,Simulations considered n = 10 and n = 100; no separate condition-level results are given so 10 is one explicit setting. Chosen as a representative since both are mentioned together.,0.6,1,"In standard IPD, players choose between two discrete actions (C or D); this is not an all-or-nothing contribution game, so coded as 1 for ‘can choose any allowed action’ per instructions.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments; actions are cooperate/defect only.,1,N/R,No public-goods style multiplier or marginal per capita return is described for the IPD payoffs.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication is not modeled as a separate feature.,1,0,Model only specifies payoffs from pairwise IPD; no information feedback structure like summaries is described.,0.7,N/A,No direct punishment stage or identities in a human sense; model uses strategies only.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,Model does not include agents’ knowledge about remaining rounds; only the analyst knows n.,0.7,0,The IPD has only C/D payoffs; there is no separate punishment decision or technology in this simulation.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward stage is modeled.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Standard IPD payoffs are referenced qualitatively but no explicit numeric payoff matrix or ‘endowment’ is specified.,0.5,N/R,"Outcomes are strategy frequencies and evolutionary stability, not contribution proportions of an endowment.",1,N/R,No token contributions; only binary C/D actions and resulting fitness.,1,N/R,"Paper reports which strategies evolve (e.g., GP types dominate), not normalized efficiency values.",0.8,N/R,Only qualitative statements about payoffs and which strategies evolve; no aggregate payoff numbers given.,0.8,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary simulation.,1,"Iterated prisoner’s dilemma with strategies C, D, GT, and guilt-prone grim trigger (GPGT). Each round, players err (defect when intending to cooperate) with probability α. GPGT behaves like GT, but after each defection sends an honest, unfakeable apology, accepts others’ apologies, and resumes cooperation, so GPGT–GPGT interactions approximate mutual cooperation. Replicator dynamics in discrete time are used.",Summarizes the detailed verbal description of this simulation setup in the ‘Cost-Free Apology’ section and associated footnote on the replicator equation.,0.98
10.1086_687873.md,"Replicator dynamics simulation – IPD with C, D, TFT, GPTFT (cost-free honest guilt signal)","Named from description of simulations with C, D, TFT, and guilt-prone TFT (GPTFT) strategies.",0.95,"Available strategies {C, D, TFT, GPTFT}; error probability α ∈ {0.01, 0.05}; number of rounds n ∈ {10, 100}; apologies always honest and impossible to fake.",These are the modeled conditions specifically mentioned for this second simulation set.,0.98,False,"Simulation with strategies, no human participants.",1,False,"Theoretical simulation, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab or field context; just computational modeling.,1,True,Described as using replicator dynamics simulations.,1,False,"This section reports simulated evolutionary outcomes, not purely analytic derivations.",0.8,2,Iterated prisoner’s dilemma is a 2-player game; no alternative stated.,1,10,"As above, n = 10 and n = 100 are both considered; using 10 as one explicit reported horizon, though the results are summarized jointly.",0.6,1,"Binary cooperate/defect choices, not a continuous contribution level; coded as 1 in the sense that there is a free choice between the available actions.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game with an endowment.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier is given.,1,N/A,No human subjects or separate communication channel.,1,0,No mention of players receiving summarized information; only immediate payoffs drive fitness via replicator dynamics.,0.7,N/A,No separate punishment mechanism or identity component.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,The model fixes n for the analyst; agents’ knowledge of horizon is not modeled.,0.7,0,Interactions are just IPD payoffs via C/D; no explicit punishment stage.,0.9,N/A,No punishment technology present.,1,N/A,No punishment technology present.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No reward technology present.,1,N/A,No reward technology present.,1,N/R,No explicit payoff matrix or starting endowment specified.,0.5,N/R,"Dependent variables are which strategies evolve (e.g., GP types dominate, TFT+C combinations), not quantified contributions.",1,N/R,No token contributions modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric is calculated; only evolutionary dominance is described.,0.8,N/R,"Paper does not report explicit payoff totals, only qualitative outcomes (e.g., GP types most likely to evolve).",0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational simulation of strategy frequencies via replicator dynamics.,1,"Iterated prisoner’s dilemma with strategies C, D, TFT, and guilt-prone TFT (GPTFT). Actors err with probability α each round by defecting when intending to cooperate. GPTFT behaves like TFT but, when it defects in error, sends an honest apology and forgives apologies, leading GPTFT–GPTFT matches to approximate unconditional cooperation. Simulations use discrete-time replicator dynamics over strategy frequencies.",Summarizes the configuration of strategies and dynamics specified for this variant in the ‘Cost-Free Apology’ section.,0.98
10.1086_687873.md,"Replicator dynamics simulation – IPD with C, D, GT, GPGT, and faker F (low-cost, partially honest apology signal)","Named from ‘Low-Cost Apology’ section where C, D, GT, GPGT, and faker (F) strategies are modeled with signal cost C and different success probabilities.",0.98,"Available strategies {C, D, GT, GPGT, F}; error probability α = 0.05; number of rounds n ∈ {10, 100}; signal cost C varied; success probabilities P_GP > P_F for guilt-prone vs faker types.",These are the explicit parameters and strategy set discussed in the low-cost apology model and figure 1.,0.98,False,No human subjects; model is theoretical and simulated.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment but a parameterized simulation/analysis.,1,False,No lab or field context.,1,True,"The text refers to ‘these models’ and figure 1 plotting minimal signal cost C needed for evolutionary stability, consistent with computational exploration of replicator dynamics.",0.95,True,"The author discusses stability conditions and basins of attraction; figure 1 is described as ‘for each P_F shows the lowest cost C such that GPGT remains an ESS’, implying some analytical derivation alongside simulation.",0.7,2,Still an iterated prisoner’s dilemma game between two players.,1,10,"Figure 1 is explicitly for α = 0.05 and n = 10 or n = 100; choosing 10 as one of the parameter settings shown, though the results summarize both.",0.6,1,Players choose between C and D each round; no continuous contribution structure.,0.6,N/A,Not a public goods or endowment allocation game.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return is defined in this IPD setting.,1,N/A,"No human participants; signaling of guilt is a modeled action, not free-form chat.",1,0,No information feedback beyond direct interaction payoffs is modeled.,0.7,N/A,Punishment is not modeled as a separate choice or technology; costs are embedded in signaling.,1,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents’ awareness of horizon is not specified.,0.7,0,"While actors may pay costs C and can suffer lower payoffs from fakers, there is no explicit punishment operator controlled by players; costs are part of signaling, not sanctioning decisions.",0.7,N/A,"No separate punishment technology; cost C is a signaling cost, not punishment.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology defined.,0.9,0,No rewarding institution is included; only interaction via C/D and apology signals.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoff matrix values are not explicitly provided.,0.5,N/R,"Primary outcomes are evolutionary stability of GPGT and minimal signal cost C as a function of P_F, not contributions.",1,N/R,No tokens contributed; only C/D choices and signaling.,1,N/R,"Results focus on stability of guilt-prone strategies and invasion by fakers, not normalized efficiency.",0.8,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff numbers are reported; only qualitative stability and basin of attraction information.,0.8,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The model is fully theoretical/computational.,1,"Iterated prisoner’s dilemma with strategies C, D, GT, guilt-prone GT (GPGT), and faker F. Each round, players can err with probability α = 0.05. After defection, GPGT attempts to signal guilt at cost C; fakers F also attempt to signal. Signals succeed with probabilities P_GP for GPGT and P_F for fakers, with P_GP > P_F. When GPGT receives a successful guilt signal, it forgives and resumes cooperation, even if the sender is an F. Figure 1 plots, for n = 10 and n = 100, the minimal C as a function of P_F for which GPGT is evolutionarily stable against invasion by F under replicator dynamics. An alternative specification where fakers pay a higher direct signaling cost is also discussed, which increases the basin of attraction for GPGT.","Summarizes the structure and main parameters of the low-cost, partially honest apology model, using statements around figure 1 and associated text.",0.98
10.1007_s40503-016-0035-0.md,Public goods – Standard – Control (no prior corruption game),Stage 2 has three public goods institutions; participants also differ by Stage-1 corruption exposure. This condition is the standard public goods game without punishment for the control group that did not play the corruption game.,0.9,Prior corruption exposure: Control (no corruption game); Public goods institution: Standard (no punishment),"Between-subjects factors are (a) Stage-1 corruption regime: control vs 5% vs 25% monitoring, and (b) Stage-2 institution: standard vs centralized punishment vs centralized punishment with counter-punishment.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with university students in Mexico.,1,True,Random assignment to corruption regimes and public goods institutions in a controlled lab setting.,1,True,Described explicitly as a laboratory experiment conducted at the Autonomous University of San Luis Potosí.,1,False,All decisions made by human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulations.,1,False,"While payoff functions are presented, the study centers on experimental data, not purely analytical results.",1,4,Public goods groups consist of four participants (“groups of four participants are randomly formed”).,1,5,Stage-2 public goods game is played for five rounds in partner mode.,1,1,"Participants can assign ‘all or part’ of their 20 ECU endowment to the public project; contributions are continuous from 0 to 20 in integer units, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,All 20 ECU start as the player’s private endowment; nothing is pre-assigned to the public project.,1,0.5,Total contributions are multiplied by 2 and equally shared among 4 players; marginal per capita return = 2/4 = 0.5.,1,0,"Instructions emphasize anonymity, spacing to prevent communication, and no mention of chat or discussion.",0.95,1,"After each round, players are informed of their earnings and the contribution of their group’s partners (full contribution profile).",1,0,"No punishment stage in thi