10.1111_evo.12031.md,Analytical model – transition from selfish to altruistic punishment,Section ‘The transition of punishment’ presents condition (eq. 10) for altruistic punishment to outcompete selfish punishment within groups.,1,"Competing enforcement types: selfish punishers vs altruistic punishers; Population includes altruists and selfish individuals; Transition condition given by inequality involving C, n_s, I, e, R, S, m.",Eq. 10 explicitly compares altruistic punishment against selfish punishment for within-group advantage.,1,False,Derives closed-form condition; no subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,False,This entry covers analytical inequality (eq. 10).,1,True,Inequality 10 is a formal expression comparing strategies.,1,5,Uses same group size n as baseline (Table 1).,0.9,10,I=10 iterations baseline; inequality includes I.,0.9,1,"Same cooperation rule: altruists contribute R, selfish do not.",1,0,Resource R starts private.,0.8,2,MPCR=m/n with m unspecified; 2 used as low-confidence placeholder.,0.1,0,No communication component.,1,0,No such features described.,1,0,Identities not modeled.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No feedback on remaining iterations.,1,1,Both altruistic and selfish punishment mechanisms are present.,1,2,C=2 baseline per selfish target in Table 1; used in analytical condition as generic C.,1,N/R,Punishment as expulsion; no linear damage per cost unit provided.,0.1,0,No reward modeled.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,R=10 (Table 1).,1,N/R,No numeric DV reported; only conditions of strategy dominance.,0,N/R,No explicit amounts summarized.,0,N/R,Efficiency not explicitly quantified.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff statistics provided.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Analytical theory only.,1,"Both altruistic and selfish punishment operate in a multigroup PGG. Eq. 10 gives when altruistic punishers have higher fitness than selfish punishers given group composition and parameters C, S, m, I, e.",Summarized from ‘The transition of punishment’ section and equation 10.,0.9
10.1111_evo.12031.md,Simulation – baseline multigroup model with altruistic punishment only,"Figure 3a, Figure 4, and related text describe NetLogo simulations where altruistic punishers are present and selfish punishers are absent under baseline parameters.",1,"Punisher type: altruistic punishers allowed, selfish punishers disallowed; Initial frequencies of altruism and punishment varied over grid; Baseline parameters n=5, I=10, R=10, C=2, D=0.7, S=0.5.",Text states simulations ‘with selfish punishment absent’ and mentions baseline values in Table 1; Figure 4 shows invasion ranges over initial altruism and punishment frequencies.,1,False,Agent-based computer simulations; no human subjects.,1,False,"It is a computational experiment, but per schema, METHOD_experiment refers to empirical experiments with human/field data.",1,False,No physical lab with participants.,1,True,Model implemented in NetLogo; agents interact in silico.,1,False,"This row refers to simulation runs, separate from analytical derivations.",1,5,Group size n=5 baseline (Table 1) used in simulations unless varied in specific sections.,1,10,Iterations I=10 within each group per generation (Table 1; Figure 3 caption mentions 10 iterations as baseline).,1,1,"Agents are either altruists, contributing full R, or selfish, contributing 0.",1,N/A,No human endowments; purely simulated agents.,1,2,"As with analytical model, MPCR = m/n; m numeric value not given. 2 kept as low-confidence placeholder.",0.1,N/A,"No communication channel among agents is modeled; also, no human subjects.",1,0,Agents do not observe earnings or punishment summaries; only internal state and random draws.,1,0,No explicit identity or displayed IDs in the simulation rules.,1,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,Agents are reactive; no UI or knowledge about remaining iterations is described.,1,1,Altruistic punishers pay C*n_s per round and may expel selfish group members; detection via D and number of punishers.,1,2,Baseline C=2 (20% of R=10) in Table 1; used in baseline simulations.,1,N/R,Punishment technology is probabilistic expulsion; magnitude per token is not parameterized as a multiplier.,0.2,0,Simulations focus on punishment only; no rewarding behavior.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,R=10 resources allocated each iteration per agent (Table 1).,1,N/R,"Plots report frequencies of strategies and invasion ranges, not mean contribution fractions.",0,N/R,"Outcome measures in figures are strategy frequencies, not token amounts.",0,N/R,The paper discusses group fitness qualitatively but does not report numeric efficiency ratios.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes are tabulated.,0,N/A,Agents are simulated; no human participants.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computer simulation only.,1,"Population size N=10,000; strategies: altruistic punishers, altruistic non-punishers, selfish non-punishers, selfish punishers (disabled in this condition). Groups of size 5 form randomly; agents play a two-phase public-goods game for 10 iterations per generation. Mutation rate M allows switching among strategies, but selfish punishment is excluded in this set of runs. Invasion ranges over initial altruism and punishment frequencies are tested 10 times per point; success defined as altruism establishing in 100% of runs.","From Table 1, Figure 1 (algorithm description), Figure 3a, Figure 4 captions and text.",1
10.1111_evo.12031.md,Simulation – baseline multigroup model with selfish punishment only,Figure 3b and Figure 4 describe NetLogo simulations where selfish punishers are present and altruistic punishers are absent under baseline parameters.,1,"Punisher type: selfish punishers allowed, altruistic punishers disallowed; Initial frequencies of altruism and punishment varied; Baseline parameters n=5, I=10, R=10, C=2, D=0.7, S=0.5.",Text notes ‘with altruistic punishment absent’ in simulations examining selfish punishment and compares invasion ranges (Fig. 4).,1,False,Agent-based simulation only.,1,False,No empirical subjects.,1,False,Not a human lab experiment.,1,True,NetLogo agent-based model.,1,False,"This row refers to simulation outcomes (Figure 3b, 4).",1,5,Baseline n=5.,1,10,Baseline I=10 iterations per generation.,1,1,"Binary strategies: altruists contribute all R, selfish agents contribute none.",1,N/A,No human endowment behavior.,1,2,Same underlying PGG with multiplier m>1; numeric m not given; 2 as low-confidence assumption.,0.1,N/A,No modeled communication plus no human subjects.,1,0,No evidence of feedback summaries among agents.,1,0,No identities or observations of who punished whom.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No representation of displayed round count.,1,1,Selfish punishers incur cost to expel cheaters; simulations explore their ability to establish altruism.,1,2,Baseline C=2 per selfish target (20% of R=10).,1,N/R,Punishment modeled as probabilistic expulsion; no per-unit fine reported.,0.2,0,Only punishment strategies; no reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,R=10 per iteration per agent baseline.,1,N/R,Reported outcomes focus on frequency trajectories and invasion ranges.,0,N/R,No token-level DV.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency numbers.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff summary.,0,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computer simulation.,1,"Same ABM as altruistic punishment case, but altruistic punishment is disabled and selfish punishment enabled. Invasion ranges (Fig. 4) demonstrate selfish punishers can invade more selfish populations than altruistic punishers. Fitness trajectories over a single generation (Fig. 3b) show how selfish punishers and altruistic non-punishers surpass selfish non-punishers as cheaters are expelled.","Consolidated from Figure 3b, Figure 4, and text describing selfish punishment simulations.",1
10.1111_evo.12031.md,Simulation – baseline transition dynamics with selfish then altruistic punishment,"Figure 5a–b describe simulations where selfish punishers invade first and, with mutation to altruistic punishment allowed, altruistic punishers later replace them.",1,"Mutation to altruistic punishment: allowed vs disallowed; Initial conditions: 22% altruism, 10% punishment, baseline parameters; In one condition selfish punishment only, in other selfish plus mutation to altruistic punishment.","Figure 5 caption: panel (a) selfish punishment only; (b) same parameters but mutation to altruistic punishment allowed, tracking transition.",1,False,Purely computational agents.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,NetLogo ABM with evolutionary mutation among strategies.,1,False,"These are time-series simulation results, not closed-form derivations.",1,5,Baseline group size n=5 applies.,1,10,Baseline I=10 iterations per generation; caption notes generations last 10 iterations under baseline.,1,1,Same binary cooperation rules as elsewhere.,1,N/A,No human endowment behavior.,1,2,"Underlying PGG with m>1, n=5; numerical m not given, using 2 as low-confidence placeholder.",0.1,N/A,No communication or human interface.,1,0,Simulation code description lacks any feedback of others’ payoffs.,1,0,No identity modeling.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents don’t receive iteration count information.,1,1,Selfish punishers (and later altruistic punishers when allowed by mutation) expel cheaters.,1,2,Baseline C=2.,1,N/R,"Expulsion-based punishment, but no per-unit tech parameter.",0.2,0,No rewards considered.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,R=10 resources per iteration baseline.,1,N/R,"Time series show frequencies of altruists and punishers, not contributions.",0,N/R,No explicit DV on token contributions.,0,N/R,Paper notes altruism reaching ~75% or >99% but does not report efficiency values.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff statistics.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Initial condition: 22% altruists and 10% punishers in population. In (a), selfish punishers can invade and a stable equilibrium with ~75% altruism is reached; altruistic punishment is excluded from mutation. In (b), under the same starting conditions, mutation to altruistic punishment is allowed; altruistic punishers eventually replace selfish punishers and altruism is maintained at >99%. 50 replications used; error bars show standard errors; some runs extended to 1000 generations to confirm stability.",From Figure 5 caption and related text.,1
10.1111_evo.12031.md,"Simulation – punishment cost variation (C = 0.5R, C = 0.2R, baseline C = 0.2R*2)",Section ‘Cost of punishment’ and Figure 6 vary cost of punishment C from baseline (20% of R) to 40% and 5% of R for both altruistic and selfish punishment invasion plots.,1,"Punishment cost C as proportion of endowment R: baseline C=2 (0.2R), high cost C=4 (0.4R), low cost C=0.5 (0.05R); Punisher type considered separately for altruistic vs selfish punishment invasion plots.",Text explicitly describes varying C from 20% to 40% and 5% of resources each iteration; Figure 6 shows separate panels for altruistic and selfish punishment.,1,False,Parameter sweeps in a NetLogo agent-based simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Computational invasion plots are based on simulation outcomes.,1,False,Focus is on simulation invasion plots rather than analytic inequalities.,1,5,Group size n baseline stays at 5 in these cost variation simulations.,1,10,No change to I is described in this section; baseline I=10 carries over.,0.9,1,Contribution rules unchanged.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,2,Underlying PGG unchanged; numeric m unspecified; 2 retained as low-confidence placeholder.,0.1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No summaries modeled.,1,0,No identity features described.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No round count displayed.,1,1,Both altruistic and selfish punishment invasion plots rely on punishment being enabled.,1,2,"For this row we record baseline setting C=2; section also examines C=4 and C=0.5 as alternative values, but these are part of the same cost-variation treatment family.",1,N/R,Mechanism remains probabilistic expulsion with cost C per cheater.,0.2,0,No reward introduced in cost-of-punishment study.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,R fixed at 10.,1,N/R,"Outcomes summarized as invasion borders, not average contributions.",0,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts.,0,N/R,"Plots show parameter regions of successful invasion, not efficiency metrics.",0,N/R,No group payoff numbers provided.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation parameter sweep.,1,"Figure 6 shows how changing C (20%, 40%, 5% of R) affects invasion ranges for altruistic vs selfish punishment. Lower cost increases effectiveness of punishment and particularly benefits altruistic punishment; higher cost hinders punishment, especially altruistic punishment due to double costs.",From ‘Cost of punishment’ section and Figure 6 description.,1
10.1111_evo.12031.md,"Simulation – solitary behavior cost variation (S = 0.3, 0.5, 0.7)","Section ‘Costs of solitary behavior’ and Figure 7 vary S from 0.5 to 0.3 and 0.7, examining both altruistic and selfish punishment.",1,"Solitary payoff factor S: baseline 0.5, lower 0.3, higher 0.7; Punisher type: altruistic vs selfish punishment considered separately; Other parameters baseline.",Text: 'we altered the costs of exile by varying S from baseline 0.5 to 0.3 and 0.7'; invasion plots in Fig. 7 for both punishment types.,1,False,Agent-based simulations.,1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Parameter sweep in NetLogo model.,1,False,Results presented graphically from simulations.,1,5,n maintained at 5.,1,10,I baseline unchanged in this section.,0.9,1,Cooperation rules unchanged.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,2,Public goods structure same as baseline; exact m not given.,0.1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No feedback.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,No reward.,1,0,No round display.,1,1,Both punishment strategies examined under different S.,1,2,C remains at baseline 2; only S varied.,1,N/R,Mechanism unchanged.,0.2,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,R=10 fixed.,1,N/R,"Results reported as invasion borders based on initial frequencies, not contributions.",0,N/R,No token amounts.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency values.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoffs.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computer simulation.,1,"S determines payoff of expelled individuals as R*S per remaining iteration. Lower S (0.3) makes expulsion more costly and increases effectiveness/evolvability of punishment; higher S (0.7) reduces punishment effectiveness. Across S values, selfish punishment invades a wider parameter space than altruistic punishment.",From ‘Costs of solitary behavior’ section and Figure 7.,1
10.1111_evo.12031.md,"Simulation – iteration number variation (I = 6, 10, 14)","Section ‘Iterations’ and Figure 8 vary the number of iterations I from 10 to 6 and 14, examining both punishment strategies.",1,"Number of iterations per generation I: 6, 10, 14; Punisher type: altruistic vs selfish punishment considered separately; other parameters baseline.","Text: 'we varied the number of iterations (I) within a generation from the baseline value of 10, to 6 and 14'; Figure 8 shows invasion plots.",1,False,Simulation parameter sweep.,1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,NetLogo ABM with varied I.,1,False,Results shown graphically from simulations.,1,5,n remains 5.,1,10,"Baseline is 10; figure also examines 6 and 14, but for this record we store the default baseline value.",0.8,1,Contribution structure unchanged.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,2,Same PGG; exact m unspecified.,0.1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No summaries.,1,0,No explicit identities.,1,0,No reward.,1,0,No round-display behavior.,1,1,Both punishment types investigated under different I values.,1,2,C kept at baseline.,1,N/R,Mechanism unchanged.,0.2,0,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,R=10 baseline.,1,N/R,"Invasion borders, not contributions.",0,N/R,No numeric contribution data.,0,N/R,Efficiency not reported numerically.,0,N/R,No group payoff numbers.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation.,1,"Increasing I (14) increases group selection strength and helps both punishment strategies invade; decreasing I (6) weakens group selection, hindering punishment. Despite expectations, higher I also aids selfish punishers when rare because they retain exclusive exploiter status for more rounds.",From ‘Iterations’ section and Figure 8 description.,1
10.1111_evo.12031.md,"Simulation – group size variation (n = 3?, 5, 7?; qualitative)",Section ‘Group size’ and Figure 9 vary group size n around baseline; exact alternative values not numerically stated but variation is described qualitatively.,0.6,"Group size n: baseline 5, smaller and larger values (exact numbers not reported in text excerpt); punisher type: altruistic vs selfish punishment examined separately.","Text: 'we varied group size (n)' and discusses smaller vs larger groups; Figure 9 shows invasion plots for different n, but specific numeric alternatives not visible in text.",0.6,False,Agent-based simulations.,1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,NetLogo ABM with varied group size.,1,False,Section reports simulation invasion plots.,1,5,"Baseline n=5; paper notes simulations where n is decreased and increased, but no explicit alternative numeric values in the excerpt; 5 recorded as the central baseline.",0.6,10,I not changed in this section; baseline 10 iterations assumed.,0.9,1,Contribution rules unchanged.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,2,"Underlying m and n change MPCR, but m not reported; 2 maintained as approximate placeholder for baseline.",0.1,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No summaries.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,No reward.,1,0,No round display.,1,1,Punishment strategies evaluated under different group sizes.,1,2,C baseline retained.,1,N/R,Mechanism unchanged.,0.2,0,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,10,R=10 fixed.,1,N/R,Only invasion regions by initial strategy frequencies shown.,0,N/R,No numeric contributions.,0,N/R,No efficiency metrics.,0,N/R,No group payoff data.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation.,1,"Reducing group size increases between-group variance and makes punishment more effective, expanding the invasion region for both altruistic and selfish punishment. Increasing group size has the opposite effect; across n values, selfish punishment remains more effective at establishing altruism in predominantly selfish populations.",From ‘Group size’ section and Figure 9 narrative.,1
10.5751_es-07493-200323.md,"Irrigation lab experiment – baseline communication, rounds 1–10",Paper describes a single experimental treatment (five-player irrigation game with neighbor-only chat and changing uncertainty after round 10). Analyses for this paper are restricted to the first 10 rounds without environmental uncertainty.,0.9,"No between-group experimental manipulation; within-game variation in positions (A–E) and in later rounds (11–20) environmental variability, but only stable-environment rounds 1–10 are analyzed.",Methods describe one treatment; only positions and later environmental variability differ. Authors explicitly restrict analysis to rounds 1–10 with constant depreciation and water supply.,0.9,True,Human subjects (115 undergraduate students) participated in lab sessions.,1,True,Design is a controlled behavioral lab experiment with random assignment to groups and fixed payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted at Arizona State University lab with recruited undergraduates.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations without humans are reported; computer only implements the game.,1,False,"Although Nash and cooperative outcomes are discussed, no standalone analytical model experiment is run.",0.9,5,Explicitly stated: five-player irrigation game with positions A–E.,1,10,"While 20 actual rounds were played, analyses and reported outcome thresholds use only rounds 1–10 without environmental uncertainty.",0.9,1,"Investment: participants can invest 'all, zero, or a portion' of 10 tokens; extraction: can open/close gate continuously over time, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each round players receive an endowment of 10 tokens in their private account and must actively decide how many to invest in the public infrastructure.,1,N/R,"Standard public goods MPCR not defined; infrastructure and water production follow nonlinear, thresholded functions rather than linear multiplication by group size.",0.3,1,Each round begins with a 60-second communication phase via text chat where participants may discuss anything within rules.,1,1,"After investment, screen summarizes neighbors' investments, infrastructure efficiency, water delivery capacity, and supply.",0.9,0,No explicit punishment mechanism exists; only informal threats in chat. Thus no punishment identities or actions are shown via a punishment stage.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism exists beyond regular payoffs.,1,1,"Not directly stated, but standard protocol for these computerized experiments and time-based dashboard implies awareness of round progression; assume remaining rounds or at least round number is visible.",0.5,0,"No costly punishment or sanctioning action is available in the payoff structure; 'enforcer' refers to threats like strikes, not a separate punishment technology.",1,N/A,No formal punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No formal punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit costly rewarding option separate from normal earnings.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism.,1,10,"At beginning of each round, participants are endowed with 10 tokens.",1,N/R,Only total investment per group across 20 rounds and correlations with extraction are reported; no average fraction of endowment invested per round is provided.,0.2,25.191,"Mean total group investment over 20 rounds is 251.91 tokens (Appendix 1). Dividing by 10 rounds analyzed and 5 players yields ~5.038 tokens per player per round. As a per-round group amount, 251.91/20 ≈ 12.595; however, the design section compares to 25 tokens per round total under cooperation. To avoid over-interpreting 1–10 vs 1–20, we use average group investment per round over all 20 rounds: 251.91/20 ≈ 12.5955; per-player per-round ≈ 2.5191. Here we report mean per-player per-round contribution amount 2.5191, rounded to 3 decimals.",0.4,N/R,They report group earnings and compare to a cooperative benchmark (978 tokens in 10 rounds) but do not provide observed efficiency as a fraction of maximum for the analyzed subset (rounds 1–10) per group.,0.3,77.661,Mean final earnings per group over full 20 rounds is 776.61 tokens (Appendix 1). Per-round group payoff ≈ 776.61/20 = 38.8305; per-player per-round ≈ 7.7661. Here we report average per-player per-round payoff.,0.4,United States,Participants were undergraduate students at a major university in the United States (Arizona State University).,1,Mean 20 years (undergraduate students),Exit surveys report average age 20 years; recruited from undergraduate student pool.,1,49% female,Exit surveys: 49% of 115 students were female.,1,Undergraduate students (mixed majors),Participants randomly recruited from database of undergraduate students from all majors.,1,On site,Behavioral lab sessions at Arizona State University; not an online or field implementation.,1,"Five-player asymmetric irrigation common-pool resource game with upstream–downstream positions (A–E). Each round: 60s text chat; then investment stage with 10-token endowment, linear accumulation of infrastructure stock that depreciates by 25 units per round; S-shaped production function from infrastructure to water delivery (0–40 cf/s). Appropriation stage: 50s real-time gate opening/closing, max gate flow 25 cf/s, group water supply capped at 30 cf/s (1500 cf per round). Crop earnings from 0–20 tokens based on total water applied (150–899+ cf). Groups start with infrastructure stock 75 (75% efficiency). In rounds 1–10, depreciation and river supply are constant; variability introduced only after round 10.",Summarizes key structural and payoff features from Investment and Appropriation stage descriptions and Table 2 and Table 3.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2017.10.016.md,"Simulation – p=0 (no punishment, traditional PD)",Condition where willingness to punish p is set to 0; described as the traditional version without punishers.,1,Willingness to punish p = 0; temptation to defect b varied; punishment fine β=0.5; punishment cost γ=0.1,"Model section and Fig. 1–4 vary p across 0, 0.4, 0.6, 1 and b over a range; β and γ fixed.",0.95,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled lab/field experiment; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical site; purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly described as Monte Carlo simulations on a 100×100 lattice.,1,False,No analytical closed-form results or proofs; only simulation.,1,10000,"Square lattice of size 100×100 implies 10,000 players.",1,50000,They run 5×10^4 Monte Carlo steps and measure over last 5×10^3.,1,0,"This is a pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with binary strategies C, D, P, not a contribution game.",1,N/A,Not a contribution/public goods framework; no endowments or contributions.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication among agents not modeled as chat.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback to human players; purely algorithmic updates.,1,N/A,No identities or displays; interactions determined by lattice neighborhood.,1,N/A,No identities or explicit rewards shown.,1,N/A,No participants or interface to show the number of rounds.,1,0,"When p=0, punishers never impose fines; effectively no punishment in payoffs.",0.95,N/A,Punishment not active when p=0.,1,N/A,Punishment not active when p=0.,1,0,Only punishment is modeled; no positive rewards for sanctioning.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game; payoffs derived directly from PD matrix.,1,N/A,"Outcome is density of cooperators ρc, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contributions are made; binary strategies only.,1,N/R,They do not report any efficiency or total payoff normalized by maximum.,1,N/R,"Only densities and cluster statistics are reported, not group payoffs.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results from Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice.,1,"Pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma on a 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundary; payoffs R=1, T=b (1<b≤2), P=0, S=0; three strategies C, D, P initially in equal proportions; interaction neighborhood size n=4; update rule: if Px<Py, x adopts y’s strategy with probability (Py−Px)/(n×Δ); parameter K=0.1 appears in caption of Fig. 4 but main dynamics use proportional imitation form.",Summarized directly from the Model section and figure captions.,0.9
