10.3982_te2404.md,Theoretical model – repeated anonymous random matching game G^M_δ with trust-building and community enforcement,Paper develops a single formal model of a repeated anonymous random matching game G^M_δ with two communities and phases I–III; there are no separate empirical treatments or simulations.,1,"Not an empirical experiment; theoretical analysis varying underlying stage game G (must be in class g with a strict Nash equilibrium and a one-sided incentive profile), community size M, discount factor δ, and phase lengths T^I and T^II.","Model characterizes equilibria as functions of parameters G, M, δ, T^I, and T^II; no treatment assignment to human subjects.",1,False,No human participants; paper is purely theoretical game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,No controlled lab/field experiment is conducted; only theoretical propositions and proofs.,1,False,No laboratory setup or human-subject sessions are described.,1,False,"Although Markov chains and matrices are used analytically, there is no numerical or agent-based simulation reported.",1,True,"Main content is formal analytical modeling and proofs (sequential equilibrium, contagion matrices, lemmas, propositions).",1,2,Stage game is a two-player game G; repeated random matching pairs one buyer and one seller each period.,1,-1,The repeated anonymous random matching game G^M_δ is infinitely repeated (t ∈ ℕ). Use -1 to indicate infinite horizon.,1,1,"Players choose actions from finite action sets A1, A2 (e.g., Q_H vs Q_L, B_H vs B_L); these are discrete choices but not constrained to an all-or-nothing contribution format; by convention mark as 1 (not all-or-nothing contribution game).",0.6,N/A,This is not a public goods or contribution game; no endowment-to-public-account structure.,1,N/R,No linear public goods technology or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,"No human experiment; communication between players is only modeled as strategic play, not as chat.",1,N/A,"Information structure is modeled (players only observe own matches), but there are no experimental screens or summaries for subjects.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment action or identity revelation in an experimental interface; punishment is via playing stage-game Nash.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism with identities.,1,N/A,Infinite-horizon theoretical model; no interface displaying remaining rounds to participants.,1,1,‘Punishment’ is modeled as community enforcement via Nash reversion (grim-trigger-like) after deviations.,1,N/R,No numerical per-unit cost of punishment is specified; costs are embedded in payoffs of the Nash equilibrium versus cooperative profiles.,0.5,N/R,"No parametric technology (e.g., 1 coin reduces 3 coins) is defined; punishment is simply play of the stage-game Nash action.",0.5,0,There is no explicit rewarding technology; only cooperative play and punishment via Nash reversion.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a separate mechanism.,1,N/R,"Stage games like the product-choice game specify payoffs directly, not via an explicit monetary endowment per period.",0.5,N/A,No contribution decision or public good is modeled; main outcomes are equilibrium payoffs and cooperation sustainability.,1,N/A,No contribution amount is defined.,1,N/R,Paper discusses payoffs close to efficiency conceptually but provides no numeric efficiency measure over an experimental outcome.,0.7,N/R,Equilibrium payoff sets are characterized theoretically; no single numeric group payoff outcome is reported.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical.,1,No human,"Model only; no experiment conducted in lab, field, or online with subjects.",1,"Infinitely repeated anonymous random matching between two equal-sized communities (C1 and C2 with M players each) playing a finite two-player stage game G in class g. Players observe only their own match histories (private monitoring), matching is uniform and independent over time, and strategies involve three phases (I trust-building with profile (hat a1, hat a2), II with (a1*, a2), III with profiles in A_{a*} achieving target payoff v). Deviations trigger community enforcement via Nash reversion after specified delays.","Summarizes key structural features of the theoretical game relevant to cooperation and punishment, based on Sections 2–3.",1
10.1073_pnas.1400827111.md,Tokyo Managers' Union – Field case study (observational),The paper reports one detailed observational case study of the Tokyo Managers' Union; no separate experimental conditions or lab/field manipulations are described.,1,N/R,The study is descriptive/observational; it does not define or manipulate specific independent variables across conditions.,1,True,The study uses real-world data and interviews with human union members and staff.,1,False,"There is no controlled intervention, randomization, or experimental manipulation; it is a field case study.",1,False,No laboratory setting is used; all data are from real-world union activities and interviews.,1,False,"Although a theoretical model is discussed, the reported empirical component is not a computer simulation.",1,False,"The paper contains theoretical discussion, but the question here concerns the empirical case; the object refers to the field/observational part, not a purely analytical model.",0.7,6,"For the formal model of the union (used to explain Mr. A’s behavior), the paper specifies: in each period there are six players (two young, two middle-aged, and two old players). This best maps to the game-structure configuration.",1,-1,"The model has an infinite time horizon t = 1,2,3,..., so a finite number of rounds is not defined.",1,1,"Helping is modeled as a binary action per target (a player can help at most one player per period); there is no continuous choice of contribution level, so contributions are effectively all-or-nothing.",0.7,0,"There is no explicit endowment allocation structure described; by convention for such repeated helping models, resources start as fully private unless stated otherwise.",0.3,N/R,"The model specifies benefit b and cost c per help, but does not define a marginal per capita return in the public-goods sense.",1,1,Members communicate via email and face-to-face requests; communication is central to arranging help in the model and in the field context.,0.9,0,The union does not keep or share detailed records of who helped whom; members lack such summaries.,1,0,There is no formal punishment technology with identities shown; sanctions are indirect via lack of help.,1,0,There is no explicit formal reward mechanism distinct from help; thus no identity display setting is defined.,1,0,Neither in the model nor in the field case do members know a fixed number of periods/rounds.,1,0,No explicit costly punishment mechanism is modeled; non-help is an absence of reward rather than a defined punishment technology.,0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment technology exists.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology exists.,1,1,Receiving help (attendance at one’s activities) is the positive reciprocal incentive in the model.,0.9,N/R,The model uses a generic cost c per help but does not specify it numerically.,1,N/R,The model specifies benefit b per help but does not give a numeric value or ratio for a reward technology.,1,N/R,No per-period token or monetary endowment for players is numerically specified in the model.,1,N/R,The case study and model do not report normalized contribution rates; they only describe counts of helps and qualitative patterns.,1,N/R,"No numeric contribution amounts (e.g., number of helps per player in aggregate statistics) are systematically reported, only some example counts for Mr. A.",0.9,N/R,No measure of efficiency relative to a theoretical maximum payoff is reported or computed.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff figures for the group are reported; only qualitative statements about the benefit of cooperation.,1,Japan,The union is a Japanese community union located in Tokyo; all described members are from Japanese workplaces.,1,N/R,Ages of union members or interviewees are not reported.,1,N/R,Genders of members are not systematically reported; Mr. A is male but that is not representative data.,0.9,N/R,Educational attainment of members is not reported.,1,Field experiment,"The setting is the real-world Tokyo Managers' Union; while there is no intervention typical of an experiment, among the provided categories it is closest to a field context with real organizations rather than lab, online, or purely observational in the sense of passive data; however, the lack of manipulation makes this ambiguous.",0.4,"Overlapping-generations repeated helping game: infinite-horizon, in each period two new players enter and each player stays exactly three periods (young, middle-aged, old). Players who need help are middle-aged and old; in each period each player can help at most one other player. Young players know only the list of those needing help and, upon helping, observe who else helped the same person; this is the sole observability. Payoff is (number of helps received)*b − (number of helps provided)*c. An equilibrium strategy consistent with Mr. A’s behavior: when young, help a randomly selected player in need; when middle-aged, help someone you met while helping when young; when old, do not help. With elaborations, this is an equilibrium if b > 8c.",Summarized directly from the model description and equilibrium construction provided in the text and footnotes.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.115095.md,Monte Carlo simulations – baseline APTM PGG on hypergraphs,The paper reports a single computational study using Monte Carlo simulations of a public goods game with an adaptive punishment transition mechanism (APTM) on hypergraphs; no distinct experimental treatments with humans are described.,0.95,"Simulation parameters of the APTM public goods game on q-uniform hypergraphs (e.g., punishment probability ω, punishment cost ρ, punishment intensity β, exploration probability ε, learning rate α, discount factor γ, synergy factor r, contribution c, hyperedge size q). This condition refers to the general baseline configuration used across figures, with N=10000 agents, T=1e5 steps, and averaging over last 5000 steps.","The study systematically varies several parameters (ω, ρ, β, ε, α, γ, r, c, q) in separate plots but treats them as part of one simulation framework rather than separate labeled experiments; here the independent variables are the game and learning parameters in this baseline simulation model.",0.9,False,The study uses Monte Carlo simulations of artificial agents on hypergraphs with Q-learning; no human or animal participants are involved.,1,False,"This is a computational simulation study within evolutionary game theory, not a controlled behavioral or field experiment with empirical subjects.",1,False,No laboratory setting or human laboratory experiment is reported; all results come from simulations.,1,True,The paper repeatedly refers to Monte Carlo simulations of agents on hypergraphs implementing Q-learning and APTM.,1,False,"Although payoff equations are derived, the core results are from numerical Monte Carlo simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.9,10000,"They state: ""These simulations are conducted using a population of N=10000 throughout the experimental trials.""",1,100000,"They state: ""a series that totals 10^5 steps""; interpreted as the number of simulation steps/rounds T.",0.95,1,"Cooperators ""contribute a fixed amount, c"" and defectors contribute nothing; there is no indication that partial contributions are allowed, so contributions are effectively all-or-nothing between 0 and c.",0.9,N/A,This is a theoretical/simulation PGG without explicit endowments or default allocation between public and private accounts as in lab experiments with money; not applicable.,0.9,N/R,"They specify a synergy factor r and group size q (payoff term r*N_C*c/q), but never explicitly define MPCR (marginal per capita return). MPCR could be inferred as r/q, but it is not directly labeled or used as such, so it is marked not reported.",0.4,N/A,"No human communication is modeled; agents interact only via game payoffs and learning, so chat/communication is not applicable.",1,N/R,"The simulation describes payoff-based learning and local imitation but does not specify any explicit display of summaries or histories beyond payoffs, and such interface concepts are not meaningful for non-human agents.",0.3,N/A,"There are no human identities; agents are anonymous nodes in a simulation, so identity revelation of punishers is not defined.",1,N/A,"No reward mechanism exists in the model, only punishment; identity display of rewarders is therefore not applicable.",1,N/R,"In a simulation without human interface, information about total or remaining rounds isn’t described; not reported.",0.3,1,"Punishers and punishment mechanisms (probability ω, cost ρ, intensity β) are central to the APTM model.",1,N/R,"The model defines ρ as punishment cost and β as punishment intensity/resources per punishment event, but does not clearly distinguish ""cost per coin"" versus ""coin"" units; thus a clear per-unit punishment cost compatible with the specified schema cannot be extracted.",0.3,N/R,"Punishment intensity β affects defectors' penalties, but its exact mapping to ""punishmentTech"" (magnitude per coin spent) under an experimental token framework is not specified.",0.3,0,"The game includes only cooperators, defectors, and punishers; there is no mention of any positive reward mechanism.",1,N/A,"Reward mechanisms are absent, so reward costs do not apply.",1,N/A,No reward technology/mechanism is modeled.,1,N/R,"Agents contribute a fixed amount c if they cooperate, but the underlying endowment or maximum available resources per round is not defined in the paper.",0.4,N/R,The primary outcome reported is the fraction of cooperators f_C; there is no explicit reporting of average contribution as a fraction of endowment beyond this binary strategy frequency.,0.4,N/R,"While c is a parameter for cooperator contribution, the paper does not report average actual contributions per agent or group as an outcome metric, only the fraction of cooperators.",0.4,N/R,Group efficiency (total payoff relative to maximum possible) is discussed qualitatively but not quantified or plotted as a numerical dependent variable.,0.3,N/R,No explicit numeric group payoff outcomes are reported; only the fraction of cooperators f_C is visualized and discussed.,0.3,N/A,There are no human participants; only simulated agents on hypergraphs.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,The study is entirely based on Monte Carlo simulations of agents and hypergraphs without any interaction with human subjects or real-world field data.,1,"Public goods game on q-uniform preferential-attachment hypergraphs with two strategies (C, D) and an adaptive punishment transition mechanism: only cooperators can act as punishers (action P vs I) with punishment probability ω, punishment cost ρ, and punishment intensity β. Agents update strategies via the Fermi rule based on average payoffs across hyperedges and update punishment actions using an adaptive Q-learning algorithm with learning rate α, discount factor γ, and exploration probability ε. Population size N=10000, hyperedge size q varies (e.g., 4–7), contribution c and synergy factor r determine payoffs, and simulations run for 10^5 steps with results averaged over the last 5000 steps and 20 independent runs. The key reported outcome is the steady-state fraction of cooperators f_C as a function of r, ω, ρ, β, ε, α, γ, c, and q.","Summarizes the core structural and dynamic elements of the simulated APTM public goods game and how results are computed, directly from the model and algorithm descriptions.",1
