10.1111_cogs.13326.md,Study 1 – Role-switch Ultimatum Game (single condition),Study 1 uses one UG implementation where participants alternate between Proposer and Responder with a fixed computer strategy; no between-subject treatment variations are described.,0.98,"Not an experimental manipulation of game parameters; analyses focus on trial-level variables (offers received, offers proposed, previous rejection), but the experimental condition is constant across participants.",Design description lists only one task structure and one computer behavior; variation is within-subject and analytic rather than between conditions.,0.9,True,Human participants played an Ultimatum Game with EEG recording.,1,True,Controlled lab game with deception and fixed computer behavior; not observational.,1,True,Participants recruited from local university and community and tested with EEG in lab.,1,False,Computer opponent followed preprogrammed strategy but main data are from humans.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model as standalone study; statistics are applied to empirical data.,1,2,Ultimatum Game is explicitly 2-player (Proposer and Responder).,1,384,Participants played UG for 384 trials.,1,1,"Proposer chooses among multiple discrete splits (5:5, 6:4, 7:3, 8:2, 9:1); not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.",1,0,Standard UG: entire pot is unallocated until Proposer chooses split; nothing is pre-assigned to public/shared account.,0.85,N/A,Ultimatum Game is not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No mention of communication between participants or with counterpart.,0.95,0,Responders only see current offer; no summaries of others' earnings or history are described.,0.9,0,Punishment is rejection which directly affects the proposer; identity is implicit dyadic partner but no broader identification or third-party info is discussed. Defaulted to 0 as no explicit identity revelation beyond dyad is mentioned.,0.6,0,There is no explicit reward operation beyond accepting offers; no identity revelation is discussed.,0.8,0,Instructions and task description do not report any display of total or remaining rounds.,0.6,1,Rejection of an offer is interpreted as costly punishment for both players.,1,4,"In standard UG with $10 pot, rejecting a typical unfair offer like 6:4 costs the responder 4; however, costs vary by offer (1–5). No single cost is specified, so 4 is illustrative but not strictly defined. Marking as approximate.",0.2,1,Rejection removes the entire proposed amount from both players: 1 unit of responder's foregone payoff corresponds to 1 unit of proposer's foregone payoff (ratio 1:1).,0.4,0,No separate costly reward mechanism; acceptance is baseline outcome.,1,N/A,No explicit costly rewarding stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,10,Proposer splits a $10 pot each trial.,1,N/R,UG is not framed as contribution rate; no normalized contribution fraction is reported.,1,N/R,"Key DVs are rejection likelihood and proposed offer distributions, not summarized as mean 'contribution' of proposer to responder; paper gives mean proposed offer ($6.3) but that is not framed as contribution token amount for analysis.",0.6,N/R,No aggregate efficiency or payoff ratio is reported.,1,N/R,Average payoffs per dyad/round are not provided.,1,United States,Participants recruited from University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign and local community.,0.95,"M=23.1, range 18–39","Reported as M_age=23.1 [18,39], SD=5.3.",1,"50% female, 50% male","Sample description explicitly states 50% female, 50% male.",1,University students and community members (mixed education; likely college-aged plus adults).,Recruited from local university and surrounding community; no finer breakdown.,0.7,On site,EEG recording and in-person lab procedure indicate on-site lab experiment.,1,"Ultimatum Game with alternating Proposer/Responder roles across 384 trials against a computer emulating human rejection probabilities (1% for 5:5, 12% for 6:4, 45% for 7:3, 67% for 8:2, 75% for 9:1). Responder choices on 5-point scale (Strongly Reject, Reject, Pass, Accept, Strongly Accept), collapsed to reject/accept for analysis; offers to responder: 5:5 (33%), 4:6, 3:7, 2:8, 1:9 each 17%.",Summarized directly from Study 1 task design.,1
