10.1111_cogs.13326.md,"Study 3 – Public Goods Game with punishment (single condition, reanalysis of Herrmann et al. 2008)",Study 3 reanalyzes an existing PGG with punishment dataset; no experimental treatments are introduced within this paper.,0.98,"No new experimental manipulation by current authors; underlying Herrmann et al. (2008) design: 4-player PGG with punishment, repeated 10 rounds with fixed group membership across 16 cities.",Text describes only a single PGG-with-punishment structure reanalyzed for expectation modeling; punishment vs no-punishment variant exists in original but the current paper uses only the punishment version.,0.9,True,Human participants in 16 cities played a repeated PGG with punishment.,1,True,Original study is a controlled lab experiment on public goods with punishment.,1,True,Herrmann et al. (2008) PGGs were conducted in lab sessions in various cities.,0.95,False,"Data come from human subjects, not simulated agents.",1,False,Current paper uses multilevel regressions on empirical data.,1,4,Task explicitly described as 4-player PGG.,1,10,Participants played the 4-player PGG for 10 rounds with the same group.,1,1,Participants could contribute 'tokens' up to their endowment (20 tokens) with no restriction to full or none; amounts treated as continuous/discrete 0–20.,0.95,0,Standard PGG: endowment starts in private account; contributions moved voluntarily to public pot.,0.95,0.4,"Each round, 20 tokens per player; contributions multiplied to 160% (factor 1.6) and distributed equally among 4 players. MPCR = 1.6 / 4 = 0.4.",0.95,0,No communication described; typical for this design.,0.9,1,"After each round, participants saw how much each other player contributed and could punish them; implies visibility of others' contributions each round (a form of summary).",0.9,0,Participants knew punishments directed at them but not who punished them ('Participants were told of punishments directed toward them but were not told who specifically punished