10.1088_1742-5468_abb236.md,"Spatial lattice SPGG – baseline reputation model (lattice, w=0.001, P1=1, P2=1, varying Z and r)","Main spatial lattice setup described in Sections 2–3 with default ω=0.001, P1=1, P2=1; r and Z varied to generate curves such as figure 1(d) and figure 2.",0.9,"Enhancement factor r (varied over a range, exact values not all reported) and reputation threshold Z ∈ {10,30,50,90,98}, with fixed P1=1, P2=1, ω=0.001 on an L=100 lattice.","Figure 1(d) explicitly reports varying r and Z with P1=1, P2=1, ω=0.001 and L=100; these are the main independent variables for this baseline condition.",0.9,False,The study is described as Monte Carlo simulations of an evolutionary SPGG (agent-based model) without human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; only computational simulations are used.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Monte Carlo method on lattice and well-mixed populations is explicitly stated.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria are presented; focus is on numerical simulation results.,0.9,10000,"Lattice size L=100, so number of individuals is L×L=100×100=10000.",1,10000,"They state that for most parameter combinations, the system stabilizes in 10^4 MCS and averages are taken over the final 100 MCS; treat 10^4 as the effective number of simulated rounds.",0.8,1,"Players choose between pure strategies C (contribute fixed c=1) and D (contribute 0); no continuous contribution choice, thus all-or-nothing between 0 and full c. Coding 1 as players can contribute any amount does not fit; they have a binary choice only, so set to 0? However schema defines 1 if can contribute any amount, 0 if only all-or-nothing; here it is all-or-nothing, so value is 0.",1,N/A,No human subjects; endowments and defaults are implicit in the simulation and not described as a default allocation between accounts.,1,N/R,"They report an enhancement factor r and group size G, but G is not numerically specified, so MPCR=r/G cannot be computed.",0.3,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely algorithmic strategy updates.,1,0,Agents update strategies based on local payoffs and reputations; no interface or feedback summaries to human players.,1,N/A,"Punishment and reward are implemented through reputation changes, not via targeted, identified human punishers or a user interface.",1,N/A,Analogous to punishment; no human identities.,1,0,No human interface; agents are not shown the number of rounds.,1,1,Defection against good neighbors (D/G) is punished by −5 to reputation; D/B is punished by −P2; both are punishment mechanisms.,1,N/R,"Punishment is implemented as an exogenous decrease in reputation, not as a costly action for a punisher with well-defined token costs.",0.2,N/R,No explicit conversion factor from punishment effort to payoff reduction (only reputation changes).,0.2,1,Cooperation with good neighbors (C/G) yields +1 reputation; C/B yields +P1 reputation; these are reward mechanisms.,1,N/R,Rewards are implemented as reputation increases without a modeled cost for a rewarding party.,0.2,N/R,No mapping from reward actions to payoff; only to reputation.,0.2,N/R,They specify a fixed contribution c=1 for cooperators but do not specify total endowment per round.,0.3,N/R,"They report frequency of cooperators ρc, not normalized contribution rate conditional on endowment.",0.4,N/R,Only strategy frequencies are reported; per-capita or per-group contribution amounts are not tabulated.,0.3,N/R,"Although payoffs are defined, no aggregate efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum) is explicitly reported.",0.3,N/R,"They discuss average payoffs by strategy in figure 2(b) qualitatively, but no exact numeric group payoff values are given.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulations in silico.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a regular L×L lattice (L=100) with group size G (not numerically specified), periodic boundaries, cooperators contributing c=1 and defectors contributing 0, enhancement factor r>1, second-order reputation evaluation with threshold Z, reputation bounds [0,100], and strategy update via Fermi rule p = ω_j / (1+exp((Π_i−Π_j)/K)) with K=0.5 and ω_j depending on whether neighbor j’s reputation exceeds Z.",Synthesized from Section 2.1–2.3 and simulation description in Section 3.,1
