10.1016_j.joep.2019.102190.md,"Treatment 1 – MCR Control (no comment, no enforcement)",Stage 2 Treatment 1 in Table 1 is labeled 'MCR - Control' with no comment phase and no enforcement.,1,Comment mechanism: none; Enforcement: none; MCR chosen by rule-maker without input,2×2 design varies comments (present/absent) and enforcement (present/absent). This treatment is the baseline with neither.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 215 student participants.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with between-subjects treatment assignment.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab at Michigan State University.,1,False,All decisions made by human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model estimated without human data.,1,5,Each group consisted of four regular players and one rule-maker (5 total).,1,25,Stage 2 comprised five games with five rounds each (Rounds 6–30).,1,1,"Players divided a 25-point endowment between private and group accounts, implying any integer amount is allowed.",1,0,All 25 points are initially under the player's control; nothing is pre-allocated to the group account.,0.9,0.4,Text: 'The marginal per capita return (MPCR) was set at 0.4.',1,0,Subjects were not permitted to communicate in any way other than their decisions; no chat/cheap talk stage mentioned.,1,1,End-of-round summary showed anonymous contributions of other group members by player.,1,0,Punishment is exogenous third-party fine; identities of punished players are not described as revealed to others.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism exists in any treatment.,1,1,Standard oTree public-goods implementations and instructions that games have 5 rounds imply subjects know round number; not explicitly but strongly implied.,0.7,0,Enforcement (probabilistic fine) only appears in enforcement treatments; this is control without enforcement.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No rewarding mec