10.1016_j.joep.2019.102190.md,Treatment 4 – MCR Comment with Enforcement (comment and enforcement),Stage 2 Treatment 4 in Table 1 is labeled 'MCR - Comment with Enforcement'.,1,Comment mechanism: private comments to rule-maker before MCR; Enforcement: probabilistic fine (50% chance of 25-point fine) if contribution < MCR; MCR chosen after reading comments,Combines both comment phase and probabilistic enforcement as described.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Randomized controlled lab experiment.,1,True,Sessions run in a computer lab.,1,False,No non-human agent simulation.,1,False,Predominantly empirical analysis.,1,5,Four contributing players plus one rule-maker.,1,25,Five games × five rounds in Stage 2.,1,1,Flexible allocation of 25 points across accounts.,1,0,No default contribution to the group; all endowment initially in private control.,0.9,0.4,MPCR is 0.4 for all games in the experiment.,1,0,Comment messages are private to the rule-maker and not among players; other communication is forbidden.,1,1,"Summary screen includes other players’ anonymous contributions by player, sanction info, and own earnings.",1,0,Only own sanction status is explicitly mentioned; others’ identities are not stated as revealed.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Game and round numbers known; consistent with design and software.,0.7,1,This is an enforcement treatment with the probabilistic fine for under-compliance.,1,25,Fine amount equals the 25-point initial endowment for each punishment event.,1,25,Each application of punishment reduces the punished player’s payoff by 25 points.,0.9,0,No positive rewards are implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,25,All players receive 25 points each round.,1,N/R,"Normalized average contribution is not directly reported, though treatment effect (+5.99 points vs control) is given.",0,N/R,Absolute mean contribution by treatment is not tabulated; only regression APEs are reported.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency figure for this treatment.,0,N/R,Mean group payoff not provided.,0,USA,Same U.S.-based undergraduate sample.,1,N/R,No age data reported.,0,N/R,No breakdown by gender.,0,Undergraduate students,Participants are undergraduates at Michigan State University.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions in a campus computer lab.,1,"Linear VCM with third-party rule-maker and non-binding MCR, plus both comment and enforcement mechanisms. At the start of each Stage 2 game, the four players submit private, open-ended comments about their preferred MCR to the rule-maker. The rule-maker reads these and then selects an integer MCR used for all five rounds of that game. After each round, under-contributors (contribution < MCR) have a 50% chance of receiving a 25-point fine. Feedback includes personal contribution, MCR, own sanction status and fine level, others’ anonymous contributions, and total earnings.",Combines the description of both comments and enforcement as implemented in the same treatment.,1
10.1162_ARTL_a_00251.md,Baseline simulation – well-mixed population (single group),"Condition described in Results section as baseline: a well-mixed population consisting of a single group (Ng = 1) with parameters E=0.75, G=20, M=0.",0.95,"Population structure: well-mixed (single group, Ng=1); migration rate M=0; institutional efficiency E=0.75; initial group size G=20.","These parameters define the key condition for Figure 2, contrasting with structured population cases.",0.95,False,Model is explicitly described as an individual-based simulation with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a computational model.,1,False,No physical lab or human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Section 6.1 states that analysis proceeds by individual-based simulations.,1,False,"Although equations are given, behavior is explored via simulations, not closed-form analysis for this condition.",0.9,20,Well-mixed population consisting of a single group uses baseline parameter G=20 as group size.,0.9,1,Within each generation social interactions (political + economic game) occur once before reproduction; treated as one round per generation.,0.8,0,"Players either cooperate (produce B) or defect (produce 0), a binary choice rather than continuous contributions, so not an all-or-nothing contribution level in the sense of allowing any amount; but since the schema defines 1 as 'can contribute any amount', we set 0.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment or default allocation; contributions are modeled abstractly as traits.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not explicitly given; only total benefit B=0.9 and cost C=0.1 are specified; mapping to MPCR not stated.,0.5,N/A,No communication between agents is modeled.,1,0,Agents do not observe explicit summaries; they only experience payoffs through the modeled fitness functions.,0.9,N/A,No identities or information revelation are modeled; punishment is institutional and anonymous.,1,N/A,No identity-based information abo