10.1162_ARTL_a_00251.md,"Diminishing returns model – varying migration rate M (beta=300, gamma=0.0075)","Figure 5c presents sensitivity to migration rate M under the diminishing-returns model with E=0.75, G=20.",0.9,"Migration rate M varied under diminishing returns carrying capacity model (β=300, γ=0.0075) with structured population (Ng≈50), G=20, E=0.75.",Figure 5c examines how cooperation responds to M; caption notes that tragedy is averted even for large M.,0.85,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Parameter sweep simulations.,1,False,No lab or human participants.,1,True,Long-run averages from simulations over 1×10^6 generations.,1,False,Behavior evaluated numerically.,0.9,20,G=20 according to parameters for Figure 5c.,0.9,1,One interaction per generation.,0.8,0,Binary actions.,0.8,N/A,No endowment modeled.,1,N/R,Not defined.,0.5,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No summaries modeled.,0.9,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,0,Horizon not specified for agents.,0.9,1,Punishment mechanism active.,1,N/A,"Cost is in foregone productive public good, not per-agent tokens.",1,N/R,No separate parameter beyond E.,0.6,1,Reward mechanism active.,1,N/A,Funded at group level.,1,N/R,No separate reward technology parameter.,0.6,N/R,Not described.,0.5,N/R,"Figure 5c indicates that tragedy is averted for large M, but does not give numeric cooperation levels per M.",0.4,N/R,No raw numbers.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency metrics.,0.4,N/R,No summarized payoffs reported.,0.4,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Purely computational simulation.,1,Diminishing returns carrying capacity with β=300 and γ=0.0075; Ng≈50; G=20; E=0.75; migration rate M varied; B=0.9; C=0.1; rho0=2; mu=0.001; sigma=0.1; time averages over 1×10^6 generations; result: tragedy of political game is averted even for large M and individuals prefer rewards over punishments.,Summarizes model features and findings for Figure 5c from text.,0.9
10.1016_j.jtbi.2016.02.012.md,Metapopulation simulation – baseline public goods with punishment and conformist bias,"Single metapopulation simulation framework described in Methods; all reported results (varying N, F_ST, D', etc.) are parameter sweeps within the same core model.",0.94,"Group size N (4, 16, 64, 128, 256); initial within-group association F_ST at three loci (0.01–0.99); initial between-trait association D′ between loci (−0.5, 0, 0.5); distinct F_ST at conformity vs cooperation/punishment loci in some runs.","Results section explicitly states that simulations vary N, F_ST at each of the three loci, and D′ values; Fig. 4 varies F_ST for conformist locus separately from cooperation/punishment.",0.98,False,Study is a metapopulation simulation with haploid individuals and no human or animal participants.,1,False,"The work is theoretical/simulation-based, not a controlled behavioral or lab experiment with subjects.",1,False,No laboratory setting or participants; purely computational model.,1,True,Methods and Appendix A repeatedly describe this as a metapopulation simulation with explicit lifecycle rules.,1,False,"While some analytical expressions are given (e.g., F_ST formula, time to equilibrium), the main results are from numerical simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions of the game.",0.86,4,"Smallest reported group size N is 4; model uses N as number of individuals per group. Since the schema requires a single number and the paper varies N, we record the minimum value.",0.6,100000,Simulations in Fig. 2 are run for 10^5 time steps; we treat each time step (one full lifecycle) as a round of the public goods interaction.,0.9,1,"Individuals can either cooperate or not; no indication of a continuous contribution level. In this encoding, 1 = can contribute any amount, 0 = only all-or-nothing. Here behavior is binary, i.e., all-or-nothing, but schema forces a 1/0 choice; we mark as 1 to indicate a choice exists, though it is discrete.",0.3,N/A,"No human subjects and no explicit endowment allocation between public/private accounts; model is evolutionary with costs and payoffs, not endowed tokens.",1,N/R,"Payoff function is given in terms of costs c_C, c_R, c_P and normalizer z; no standard linear public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is specified.",0.3,N/A,No human players or communication; evolutionary simulation only.,1,0,Individuals update via conformist or payoff-based copying using local payoffs; there is no concept of on-screen summaries or feedback as in lab experiments.,1,N/A,No identities or visibility structure between individuals beyond strategy frequencies; inapplicable to non-human simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this model.,1,0,No agents are informed about the number of time steps; they follow fixed learning rules.,1,1,Second locus is punishment vs no-punishment; payoffs include costs c_R for punishers and c_P for punished noncooperators.,1,0.2,Table 1 reports c_R = 0.2 as the cost of punishing.,1,0.8,"In payoff, punished individuals pay cost c_P; Table 1 gives c_P = 0.8, which is the magnitude of loss inflicted per punishment event.",0.9,0,Model includes only costly punishment of noncooperators; no positive reward or bonus mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/R,Agents do not receive a per-round token endowment; they have payoffs determined by costs and group composition. No numeric endowment is specified.,0.3,N/R,"Outcomes reported are allele frequencies of cooperation, punishment, and conformist bias, not per-round contribution rates normalized by endowment.",0.2,N/R,Model is binary cooperate vs not; no numeric contribution amount per individual per round is summarized in results.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric (group payoff relative to maximum) is reported; only allele frequencies and F_ST dynamics are shown.,0.2,N/R,Group-level payoffs are implicit in conflict probabilities but no aggregate payoff numbers are reported as dependent variables.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire study is a computational metapopulation simulation with no empirical data collection.,1,"Public-goods-type interaction in a metapopulation of n=128 groups with haploid individuals carrying three loci: (1) cooperation vs noncooperation with cost c_C=0.2 and error rate e=0.02; (2) punishment vs no-punishment with cost to punisher c_R=0.2 and cost to punished noncooperator c_P=0.8; (3) conformist vs payoff-based social learning. Lifecycle per time step: social interaction determines payoffs; stochastic group conflict with probability ε=0.015 where groups with higher frequency of cooperators are more likely to win and replace losing groups; social learning where conformists copy modal cooperation/punishment and payoff-based learners copy or innovate with probability μ=0.01; reproduction with mortality q=0.1, sexual reproduction without recombination (r_kl=0) and mutation rate ν=0.001; and payoff-biased migration with rate m=0.01.","Details taken from Sections 2.1, 2.3, Table 1, and Appendix A which describe the public-goods-type interaction, costs, lifecycle, and parameter values.",1
10.1111_ecin.12766.md,PUN game – Complete network (CO),"Paper defines four network treatments (complete, circle, line, star). This row is the punishment (PUN) phase in the complete network.",1,"Network structure: complete (4 players all mutually linked, all are degree 3)",Between-subjects factor is network structure with four levels; this condition is the complete network benchmark.,1,True,Human subjects recruited at LINEEX played the PUN game.,1,True,Network and rules are exogenously imposed and controlled in lab sessions.,1,True,"Conducted at LINEEX laboratory, University of Valencia.",1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,"Study is experimental, not a purely theoretical/analytical model.",1,4,Groups G are composed by four agents in all treatments.,1,20,After 20 VCM rounds there was a surprise restart and subjects played 20 rounds of the PUN game.,1,1,"Subjects can allocate any fraction of 50 ECUs to the group account; continuous from 0 to 50, not all-or-nothing discrete choice.",1,0,Each round subjects are endowed with 50 ECUs in a private account and then decide how much to contribute; initial allocation is fully private.,1,0.5,"Each ECU contributed yields 0.5 ECU to each of 4 group members, giving total return 2; marginal per capita return is 0.5.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard computerized VCM with no communication stage.,0.9,1,End of round feedback includes earnings from private and group accounts and total group contribution in PUN game; this summarizes others’ behavior/payoffs.,1,0,"Environment is described as ‘identity-free, fully anonymous’; only aggregate number of punishment points received is displayed, so no identities.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,1,"Design uses fixed 20-round blocks; standard z-Tree experiments and explicit references to period numbers (e.g., P20 dummy) imply subjects know the current and final round.",0.9,1,PUN game adds a second punishment stage where subjects can assign 0–10 punishment points to each neighbor.,1,2,"Cost schedule K(P_in) given in Table 1: 1 point costs 2 ECUs. This is the marginal cost at low levels; costs are convex, but 2 is the per-point cost at P=1.",1,0.1,Each point received reduces first-stage earnings by 10%.,1,0,Only costly punishment points are described; no positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,50,"At the beginning of each round, subjects are endowed with 50 ECUs.",1,0.77,Table 4 reports average contribution of 38.485 ECUs in PUN game for complete network; dividing by 50 ECU endowment gives 0.7697 ≈ 0.77.,0.95,38.485,"Table 4, All periods PUN, Complete network.",1,N/R,No explicit normalized efficiency (payoff divided by maximum) is reported or easily reconstructible from aggregated data.,0.2,N/R,"Only average individual profits per period (e.g., 73.649) are reported, not group totals across 4 players.",0.3,Spain,"Sessions conducted at LINEEX, University of Valencia, Spain.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age range or mean reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0,Undergraduate students in business and economics,144 subjects recruited from undergraduate courses in business and economics.,1,On site,"Conducted in a physical laboratory (LINEEX) using z-Tree software, implying in-person lab sessions.",1,"Repeated linear public goods game with partner matching in a complete 4-node undirected network; 20 PUN rounds after 20 prior VCM rounds without punishment. In stage 1 of each round, subjects choose contributions from 0–50 ECUs; in stage 2, they can assign 0–10 punishment points to each of the 3 neighbors. Feedback includes own and neighbors’ contributions (all others here), total punishment received, punishment costs, and total group contribution. Groups and positions remain fixed.",Summary of structural and procedural details specific to the complete network as described in design section.,1
10.1111_ecin.12766.md,PUN game – Circle network (CI),Circle network is one of four network treatments; this row corresponds to its PUN phase.,1,"Network structure: circle (4 players in a ring, each degree 2)",Figure 1 and text define the circle treatment as four D2 subjects linked in a ring.,1,True,Human subjects played the PUN game in circle networks.,1,True,Network structure exogenously imposed in a controlled lab environment.,1,True,Sessions conducted at LINEEX laboratory.,1,False,No non-human simulation reported.,1,False,Study is behavioral/experimental.,1,4,Groups of four agents in all network treatments.,1,20,"20 PUN rounds after the 20 VCM rounds, same across networks.",1,1,Any fraction of 50 ECUs can be allocated to the group account; continuous choice.,1,0,Endowment starts in private account and subjects decide how much to move to group account.,1,0.5,Same VCM technology in all networks: each ECU contributed pays 0.5 ECU to each of 4 members.,1,0,No communication stage is described.,0.9,1,Subjects see earnings from accounts and total group contribution at round end.,1,0,Environment is anonymous; only aggregate punishment points received are shown.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present.,1,1,Fixed 20-round block with explicit references to period number; standard to inform subjects.,0.9,1,PUN game includes stage where subjects assign punishment points to neighbors.,1,2,Punishment cost function in Table 1 applies to all networks; 1 point costs 2 ECUs.,1,0.1,Each punishment point reduces target’s first-stage earnings by 10%.,1,0,Only costly punishment is implemented.,1,N/A,There is no reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,50,All subjects receive 50 ECUs each round.,1,0.511,"Table 4: All periods PUN, Circle average contribution 25.553; 25.553 / 50 ≈ 0.511.",0.95,25.553,Table 4 provides 25.553 ECUs for Circle in PUN game.,1,N/R,Normalized efficiency not reported and not straightforward to infer including punishment costs.,0.2,N/R,Only average individual profits (62.035 ECUs) are given; total group payoff not directly reported.,0.3,Spain,Same subject pool and lab for all network treatments.,1,N/R,"Age not specified, only that they are undergraduates.",0,N/R,No gender information provided.,0,Undergraduate students in business and economics,Same recruitment pool as other treatments.,1,On site,Conducted in LINEEX laboratory using z-Tree.,1,"Repeated linear public goods game with partner matching in a 4-node undirected circle (ring) network; each subject has 2 neighbors. The PUN phase has 20 rounds following 20 VCM rounds without punishment. In each PUN round, subjects contribute 0–50 ECUs then can assign 0–10 punishment points to each of their 2 neighbors. Information and punishment opportunities are limited to neighbors determined by the circle.",Restatement of design details particular to the circle (degree 2 neighbors and restricted observation/punishment).,1
10.1111_ecin.12766.md,PUN game – Line network (LI),Line network (treatment LI) is one of the four network structures; this row is its PUN phase.,1,"Network structure: line (4 players in a path; 2 players degree 2, 2 players degree 1)",Text defines line network as two D2 and two D1 subjects connected in a line (path).,1,True,Human subjects played PUN game in line-structured groups.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with exogenous network structure.,1,True,"Conducted at LINEEX, University of Valencia.",1,False,No simulation used.,1,False,"Study is experimental, not purely analytical.",1,4,Groups of four in all network treatments.,1,20,20 rounds of PUN game after initial 20 VCM rounds.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer amount from 0 to 50 ECUs; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment is initially private; contributions move ECUs into group account.,1,0.5,Same VCM payoff function E_i=50-c_i+0.5*(sum contributions) is used in all networks.,1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,0.9,1,Feedback includes total group contribution and own earnings each round.,1,0,"Only aggregate punishment received is displayed, in an anonymous environment.",1,0,No rewards exist in design.,1,1,"Fixed known horizon (20 rounds) and explicit period counters (e.g., P20 dummy) imply subjects are aware of number of rounds.",0.9,1,Second stage allows assignment of punishment points to neighbors.,1,2,Same cost schedule K(P_in) across treatments; 1 point costs 2 ECUs.,1,0.1,Each punishment point received reduces first-stage earnings by 10%.,1,0,Only punishment options exist.,1,N/A,No reward option in the game.,1,N/A,No rewards are implemented.,1,50,"Each round, subjects receive 50 ECUs.",1,0.52,"Table 4: All periods PUN, Line average contribution 25.991; 25.991/50 ≈ 0.52.",0.95,25.991,Table 4 reports 25.991 ECUs for line network in PUN game.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency figure given; only average profits.,0.2,N/R,Only mean individual profit (61.413 ECUs) reported; group totals not reported.,0.3,Spain,All treatments run at Spanish lab.,1,N/R,Age distribution not provided.,0,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,0,Undergraduate students in business and economics,Same pool mentioned for entire experiment.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at LINEEX with physical sessions.,1,"Repeated public goods game with punishment in a 4-node undirected line (path) network. Two interior players (degree 2) and two endpoints (degree 1). In PUN rounds, each player observes and can punish only linked neighbors. Groups and positions are fixed over 20 VCM and 20 PUN rounds.",Details specific to line network’s heterogeneity in degrees and restricted observability/punishment.,1
10.1111_ecin.12766.md,PUN game – Star network (ST),Star network (treatment ST) is one of four network structures; this row is its PUN phase.,1,Network structure: undirected star (1 central player degree 3; 3 peripheral players degree 1),Design section defines star as one D3 subject (center) and three D1 subjects; undirected links.,1,True,Human subjects participated in star network PUN game sessions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with imposed star network.,1,True,Run at LINEEX experimental economics laboratory.,1,False,No simulations described.,1,False,"Behavioral experiment, not an analytical-only study.",1,4,All networks are defined on groups of four subjects.,1,20,20 rounds of PUN game with punishment after initial 20 VCM rounds.,1,1,Any fraction of 50 ECUs can be contributed; not binary all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment starts fully in private account each round.,1,0.5,Same linear VCM technology across networks; 0.5 per capita return from public account.,1,0,No communication or chat functionality is mentioned.,0.9,1,Subjects see total group contribution and own earnings; central and peripheral players can infer others’ aggregate contributions.,1,0,"Identity-free, fully anonymous environment; only total punishment received is displayed.",1,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Fixed known length of 20 PUN rounds and explicit reference to last period dummy (P20) suggest round count was known to subjects.,0.9,1,PUN game’s second stage enables punishment points to be assigned along network links.,1,2,"Table 1 describes cost per punishment points; 1 point costs 2 ECUs, used in all networks.",1,0.1,"Each punishment point reduces first-stage earnings by 10%, with max 100% reduction.",1,0,Only punitive sanctions are available.,1,N/A,No reward options.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Each subject endowed with 50 ECUs per round.,1,0.7175,"Table 4: All periods PUN, Star average contribution 35.877; 35.877/50 ≈ 0.7175.",0.95,35.877,Table 4 reports 35.877 ECUs for star network in PUN game.,1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed qualitatively and via average profits, but not normalized as payoff/max payoff.",0.2,N/R,Only average individual profits per period (76.570 ECUs) reported; group totals not given.,0.3,Spain,Same lab and recruitment location for all treatments.,1,N/R,No information on age distribution.,0,N/R,Paper does not provide gender breakdown.,0,Undergraduate students in business and economics,Subjects recruited from undergraduate business and economics courses.,1,On site,Described as experimental sessions at LINEEX laboratory.,1,"Repeated public goods game with punishment on a 4-node undirected star. One central player (D3) linked to all 3 peripheral players (D1), who are only linked to the center. In PUN stage, players can only observe and punish neighbors (center-periphery links). Positions (central vs peripheral) are randomly assigned but fixed over 20 VCM and 20 PUN rounds. Results emphasize that central players punish prosocially and do not retaliate, while peripheral players increase contributions when punished.",Captures structural specifics and key behavioral features tied to star network condition.,1
10.1098_rsbl.2014.0388.md,Third-party punishment – typical defector condition,"Paper describes a single experiment with two conditions created ex-post: 'typical defector' (three defectors, one cooperator) and 'atypical defector' (three cooperators, one defector). This row is for the typical defector condition observed by player 5.",1,Group composition/norm: typical defector condition with 3 defectors and 1 cooperator in the PGG observed by player 5.,"Independent variable manipulated is whether defection or cooperation is typical in the group (descriptive norm). Here, defection is typical (3 defectors, 1 cooperator).",1,True,Human subjects recruited via Amazon Mechanical Turk played the game.,1,True,Authors describe it as an experiment with random role assignment and controlled payoff structure.,1,False,"Data collected online via Amazon Mechanical Turk, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; all data from human participants.,1,False,No formal mathematical or closed-form model presented as the main method.,1,5,Players 1–4 played the PGG and player 5 was the observer/punisher.,1,1,Single one-shot PGG with one subsequent punishment decision; no repeated rounds mentioned.,0.9,1,"Each PGG player had a single investment token that could be invested either in public or private opportunity (binary decision, not a continuous amount). Coded as 1 because they can choose between two discrete options, not fractional contributions, but schema defines 1 as 'can contribute any amount'; however, this is closest available and required numeric; leaving at 1 with low confidence.",0.3,0,Players decide where to invest their token; endowment starts entirely in their private control (equivalent to private account).,0.9,0.2,"Public investment yields $0.20 to each of the 4 PGG players; total return = 0.80. Group size for PGG is 4, so MPCR = 0.80/4 = 0.20.",1,0,No communication or chat between players mentioned; typical MTurk PGGs of this type do not allow chat.,0.9,1,Player 5 observes the decisions of players 1–4 and can see their earnings implicitly to choose whom to punish. This counts as seeing others’ outcomes/decisions.,0.8,0,No indication that PGG players were informed about who punished them; only player 5 chooses punishment and payoff reductions. Identities of punishers not discussed.,0.5,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,Design appears strictly one-shot and there is no mention of displaying number of rounds.,0.8,1,Player 5 could pay a cost to reduce earnings of any of the four PGG players.,1,0.05,Player 5 could pay a fixed cost of $0.05 to punish.,1,0.15,"Paying $0.05 reduced each punished player’s earnings by $0.15, independent of number of targets.",1,0,No option for player 5 to increase others’ payoffs; only punishment described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1.35,"Each PGG player received 1 investment token. Assuming one token equals a monetary value is unclear, but the explicit monetary endowments are $1.05 for player 5 and effectively 1 token for PGG players. To report a numeric value, sum of explicit monetary endowment for player 5 ($1.05) and one investment token approximated to its maximum private value $0.30 would be 1.35 per 5-player group role, but this is ambiguous. Using 1.35 with low confidence because schema requires a number; paper does not clearly define a unified endowment across roles.",0.2,N/R,No average fraction of endowment invested in public option reported; only proportions punished/rejected by type.,0,N/R,Paper does not report how many players chose public vs private options in each condition beyond the constructed group composition (3 vs 1); no aggregate token counts given.,0.2,N/R,No explicit or inferable ratio of actual to maximum group payoff reported.,0,N/R,No raw payoff averages per group reported.,0,USA,Subjects were all based in the USA (MTurk restriction).,1,Adult; exact distribution N/R,Only that age was collected and used as a covariate; no descriptive statistics given.,0.4,"664 males, 380 females, 6 unspecified across whole sample",Sample composition explicitly reported for the 1050 subjects.,1,N/R,Education not mentioned; only age and gender discussed.,0,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk (online labour market).,1,"One-shot 4-player public goods game with binary choice (public vs private investment). Public investment yields $0.20 to each of 4 players including investor (total $0.80), private yields $0.30 to investor only. Groups with 3 defectors + 1 cooperator constructed ex-post. A fifth player with $1.05 observes contributions and can pay $0.05 once to reduce earnings of any subset of players by $0.15 each, independent of number punished. After punishment, observer rates each player on a 7-point scale for willingness to play a future game; ratings recoded to a binary rejection measure.",Summarizes detailed structural features directly stated in the methods.,1
10.1098_rsbl.2014.0388.md,Third-party punishment – atypical defector condition,"Same experiment; this row is for the 'atypical defector' condition (three cooperators, one defector) observed by player 5.",1,Group composition/norm: atypical defector condition with 3 cooperators and 1 defector in the PGG observed by player 5.,"Independent variable manipulated is whether defection or cooperation is typical; here, cooperation is typical (3 cooperators, 1 defector), making defection atypical.",1,True,Same human-subject online experiment as in the typical defector condition.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of group composition/norm.,1,False,"Conducted on Amazon Mechanical Turk, an online platform.",1,False,No computer-only simulations used.,1,False,No formal analytical model as primary method.,1,5,As in typical condition: 4 PGG players plus 1 observer/punisher.,1,1,Same one-shot PGG and one punishment decision.,0.9,1,"Same binary investment choice (public vs private) as in typical condition; schema forces 1/0, using 1 with same caveat.",0.3,0,Endowment (investment token) starts under player’s control; they choose where to invest.,0.9,0.2,Identical PGG payoff structure: public investment yields $0.20 to each of 4 players.,1,0,No communication channel described.,0.9,1,Player 5 observes all four PGG decisions.,0.8,0,No information that PGG players learn who punished them.,0.5,0,No reward option in the design.,1,0,One-shot interaction; no rounds information displayed.,0.8,1,Player 5 can pay to reduce earnings of any PGG players.,1,0.05,"Fixed cost of $0.05 to punish, same as typical condition.",1,0.15,"Each punished player loses $0.15, regardless of number punished.",1,0,No rewarding mechanism included.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,1.35,Same endowment structure as typical condition; see that condition for detailed reasoning. Using same numeric approximation.,0.2,N/R,"As groups are constructed to have 3 cooperators and 1 defector, the nominal contribution rate is fixed by design, but the paper does not treat it as an outcome and gives no additional statistics.",0.3,N/R,"Number of public vs private choices is fixed by design (3 vs 1), but no aggregate token or monetary contribution data reported as outcomes.",0.3,N/R,No calculation or report of realized vs maximum group payoffs.,0,N/R,Raw payoff data per group or condition not given.,0,USA,All MTurk subjects were based in the USA.,1,Adult; exact distribution N/R,Age collected and used in models but no descriptive statistics reported.,0.4,"664 males, 380 females, 6 unspecified across whole sample",Overall sample composition applies to both conditions since assignment to conditions is random/ex-post.,1,N/R,No mention of education variables.,0,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,"Same as typical defector condition: one-shot 4-player PGG with binary investment choice and third-party punisher (player 5) with fixed-cost punishment option and subsequent partner-choice ratings. In this condition, groups are ex-post constructed so that 3 PGG players cooperate (public investment) and 1 defects (private investment), making the defector's behavior atypical relative to the group norm. Player 5 in this condition (n=108) observes such groups.",Condition differs only in ex-post group composition and resulting descriptive norm; all other structural features identical.,1
10.1080_0013791x.2011.650839.md,Carrot – high reward (Rounds 1–10),Carrot treatment first 10 rounds are explicitly described as the high-reward phase.,1,Leader behavior: reward-only (carrot); Reward/punishment magnitude: high (superior’s 1 lab $ decreases subordinate payoff by −1 to superior and +5 to subordinate per unit in Table A1 / methods).,Independent variables varied across conditions are leader behavior type and size of reward/punishment. This row is the carrot treatment in its high-magnitude phase.,1,True,Undergraduate business students participated in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled economic laboratory experiment with randomized assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in an economic laboratory at a university with networked computers.,1,False,Computer acts as superior but human subjects make decisions; not a pure simulation.,1,False,No formal analytical or purely theoretical model tested without data.,1,2,One subordinate (human) and one superior (computer) per game as described in one-superior/one-subordinate setting.,1,10,First 10 of total 20 rounds are the high-reward phase for carrot treatment.,1,1,Subordinates can freely choose any budget cost in $5 increments within constraints; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Not a public-goods or contribution game; no endowment allocation between private/public accounts.,1,N/A,Not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No communication between participants mentioned; only interaction is with computer.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ earnings or reports; only own payoffs discussed.,0.9,0,Only one human and a computer superior; no identities of other punishers exist.,1,0,Only computer superior; no other human identity to reveal.,1,1,Instructions state exactly 20 rounds and explicitly mention first and second 10 rounds; thus total rounds known.,0.9,0,Carrot treatment is reward-only; no punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in carrot treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment in carrot treatment.,1,1,Carrot treatment allows superior to reward subordinates based on slack level.,1,1,Methods: superior can reward at a cost of $1 (lab dollar) per unit.,1,5,First 10 rounds: each $1 cost to superior increases subordinate earnings by $5 (high reward).,1,N/R,"No fixed per-round monetary endowment to subordinate is described; payoff is 100 + slack ± rewards, not a standard divisible endowment.",0.3,0.471,"Use 1 − PSR as cooperation measure. Mean PSR for carrot first 10 rounds is 0.471 (Table 1), so contributionRate ≈ 1 − 0.471 = 0.529. However, schema defines DV_contributionRate as actual provided value; since only PSR is reported, best direct mapping is to treat PSR as 1−contribution. To avoid misrepresentation, this field reflects PSR complement? To prevent confusion, set as N/R.",0,N/R,"Raw budget reports not provided, only PSR statistics.",0,N/R,No aggregate payoff ratios versus maximum reported.,0,N/R,"Only average individual earnings reported, not group totals by treatment/phase.",0,Taiwan,Participants were students at a large private university in Taiwan; author affiliated with Taiwanese institution.,1,N/R,Paper only notes they are undergraduate business students; no age statistics.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Undergraduate business students in managerial accounting course,Explicitly stated in Experimental Methods.,1,On site,Conducted in an economic laboratory with networked computers; typical on-site lab setting.,1,One-superior/one-subordinate budget reporting game. Project revenue fixed at 1000; actual cost uniformly distributed between 700 and 900 in 5-unit increments. Subordinate privately observes actual cost and chooses a reported budget cost in 5-unit increments subject to experimental constraints; superior must accept report. Subordinate payoff each round: 100 + (reported cost − actual cost) + rewards (no punishments). Superior payoff: (1000 − reported cost) − rewards cost.,Summarized from Experimental Methods and Appendix instructions.,1
10.1080_0013791x.2011.650839.md,Carrot – low reward (Rounds 11–20),Carrot treatment second 10 rounds are explicitly described as the low-reward phase.,1,Leader behavior: reward-only (carrot); Reward/punishment magnitude: low (superior’s 1 lab $ increases subordinate earnings by $2).,Independent variables are behavior type and reward magnitude; this is the carrot treatment with low reward in second 10 rounds.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment as other treatments.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with within-treatment change in reward magnitude.,1,True,Conducted in an on-site economic lab.,1,False,Human participants present.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,2,One subordinate and one computer superior per game.,1,10,Second 10 of total 20 rounds for carrot treatment.,1,1,Budget choice is continuous in 5-unit increments; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,No contribution account structure; budgeting game.,1,N/A,Not a public-goods game.,1,0,No communication channels between subjects described.,0.9,0,No reporting of others’ outcomes; only own earnings matter.,0.9,0,No human punisher identity beyond computer.,1,0,Computer superior; no additional identity.,1,1,Subjects told experiment lasts exactly 20 rounds.,0.9,0,"Carrot is reward-only, no punishment.",1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,1,Carrot treatment includes rewards.,1,1,Cost to superior per unit reward remains $1.,1,2,"After first 10 rounds, reward magnitude changes to $2 per $1 cost.",1,N/R,No clear per-round endowment; payoffs based on budget slack and rewards.,0.3,N/R,Only PSR is reported; no direct contribution proportion defined.,0,N/R,No raw budget values reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0,N/R,Group payoffs by condition not tabulated.,0,Taiwan,Same participant pool as other treatments.,1,N/R,Only educational status given.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0,Undergraduate business students in managerial accounting course,Same as other treatments.,1,On site,Economic laboratory at a university.,1,Same budget reporting game as in carrot high-reward phase; only change is that each $1 reward cost to the superior now increases subordinate earnings by $2 instead of $5.,Described in Experimental Methods and Appendix (change after 10 rounds).,1
10.1080_0013791x.2011.650839.md,Stick – high punishment (Rounds 1–10),Stick treatment first 10 rounds are described as high-punishment phase.,1,Leader behavior: punishment-only (stick); Reward/punishment magnitude: high (each $1 cost decreases subordinate earnings by $5).,Leader behavior and punishment magnitude are experimental manipulations; this row is stick with high punishment.,1,True,Human-subject economic lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled payoffs.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory setting.,1,False,Includes human decision makers.,1,False,Not purely theoretical.,1,2,One subordinate and one computer superior.,1,10,First 10 of 20 rounds in stick treatment.,1,1,Budget can vary in 5-unit steps; not binary.,1,0,No public-good contribution structure.,1,N/A,Game is not a linear public good.,1,0,No mention of communication among participants.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ earnings.,0.9,0,Only a computer superior punishes; no other humans.,1,0,No rewards exist in stick treatment.,1,1,Subjects know there are 20 rounds with first and second 10 specified.,0.9,1,Stick treatment is punishment-only.,1,1,Superior punishes at a cost of $1 per unit.,1,5,First 10 rounds: each $1 cost decreases subordinate earnings by $5.,1,0,Stick is punishment-only; no reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment; payoffs are 100 + slack − punishments.,0.3,N/R,Only PSR reported; contribution fraction not directly defined.,0,N/R,Budget amounts by round not reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency metric or total surplus ratios provided.,0,N/R,Group payoffs by treatment/phase not listed.,0,Taiwan,Same sample as overall experiment.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0.3,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0,Undergraduate business students in managerial accounting course,As stated in Experimental Methods.,1,On site,Economic laboratory at a university.,1,"Same budget reporting structure as carrot treatment, but superior can only punis