10.1007_s10588-008-9053-z.md,Simulation – Linear neighborhood – Punishment enabled (M varied 0.5–6.0),Condition family where linear neighborhood used and punishment multiplier M increased from 0.5 to 6.0; analyzed together for punishment effects and transition ~M=1.5.,0.9,"Neighborhood type: Linear (2 neighbors); Punishment multiplier M ∈ {0.5,1.0,1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5,4.0,4.5,5.0,5.5,6.0}",Table 5 lists values of M; text notes runs under each neighborhood type for each M and a bifurcation threshold around M=1.5 for linear.,0.9,False,Purely simulated agents.,1,False,"Computational simulation, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No laboratory with humans.,1,True,Described as agent-based model and simulations across parameter values.,1,False,"Though analytical discussion exists, outcomes for this condition are simulated.",1,625,Linear neighborhood uses default N=625.,1,30000,"Each run is 30,000 generations.",1,1,"Offers are continuous in [0,1].",1,0,Proposer initially has full endowment of 1.,0.9,N/A,"Ultimatum game, not public goods.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No UI or feedback displays for humans.,1,N/A,Identities are not modeled as observed labels; punishment is internal to model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human-facing interface.,1,1,By increasing M above 0 agents became endowed with ability to punish neighbors; applies for all M>0 in this condition.,1,N/R,"Cost of punishment π_O is an evolving trait in [0,1]; no single fixed cost per unit reported.",0.3,N/R,Punishment impact is π_O * M on proposer; π_O is endogenous; no fixed technology parameter aside from M itself and this row pools multiple M values.,0.3,0,Model only includes third-party punishment; no altruistic rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Standardized endowment of 1 per game for proposers.,1,N/R,"For punishment-enabled runs, only qualitative trends (transition to ~99% offers) are reported; no numeric mean offers per M given for linear case.",0.2,N/R,No precise offer amounts per M value reported; only qualitative ~99% mentioned.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency metrics not reported.,0,N/R,Group payoff not numerically reported.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations.,1,"Ultimatum game with third-party punishment on a linear (2-neighbor) toroidal lattice. Each agent has traits (ω, α, τ, π). As proposer P, agent makes 3 offers per generation to neighbors. As observer O, an agent who observes an offer ω_P below its punishment threshold τ_O pays cost π_O and reduces P’s fitness by π_O * M. Selection via local imitation of fitter neighbors with mutation (probability 0.1, Gaussian noise sd 0.01). For linear neighborhoods, as M increases from 0.5 upward, a rapid transition occurs around M≈1.5 from low offers (similar to no-punishment case) to very high offers (~99%).","Summarized from Sections 2–3 and Table 7, focusing on linear neighborhood with punishment enabled.",1
