10.1016_j.chaos.2023.114011.md,"Non-delayed model – internal equilibrium E2* (coexistence of C,D,E, Fig. 1(f) and parameter base for Figs. 3–6, 8–17, 19(f))","System (7) with parameter set in equation (35), featuring stable internal equilibrium E2*≈(0.6302,0.1660,0.6302) used as baseline for many comparative figures, including reward/punishment sweeps and delayed model.",0.99,"Payoff/feedback parameters as in (35): a0=3,b0=0,c0=4,d0=4.1,r=4,g1=0.2,g2=0.1,p=0.7,a1=4,b1=3,c1=5,d1=2,ε=1,β=0.6. Initial condition (e.g. (0.75,0.05,0.6)) chosen in the attraction basin of E2*.","This parameter combination defines the E2* regime; subsequent figures vary r,p,g1,g2 around this base but this configuration itself is a distinct condition.",0.98,False,No human or animal data.,1.0,False,"The condition is analyzed via theory and simulation, not experimental manipulation of subjects.",1.0,False,Not run in a physical lab.,1.0,True,Trajectories under this parameter set are simulated and shown (e.g. Fig. 1(f)).,1.0,True,They derive general existence and stability conditions for E2* and identify Hopf bifurcation with respect to p.,1.0,2,Same 2-player payoff matrix Π as in model definition.,0.95,1,Continuous-time replication over a one-shot pairwise game.,0.7,1,"Only discrete strategic types C,D,E exist.",0.4,N/A,Contribution not modeled as a divisible resource.,1.0,N/R,No explicit PGG multiplier/MPCR.,1.0,N/A,No human players.,1.0,N/A,No experiment interface.,1.0,N/A,Model is anonymous at population level.,1.0,N/A,Rewards similarly impersonal.,1.0,N/A,No round-based experiment.,1.0,1,"Punishment intensity p=0.7 and cost g2=0.1 are present, so encouragers punish defectors.",1.0,N/R,g2 is specified but not scaled to a per-unit sanction technology within a discrete-token setting.,0.5,N/R,p is a payoff reduction but not described per unit cost.,0.5,1,Reward r=4 with cost g1=0.2 is specified when E meets C.,1.0,N/R,g1 is a cost parameter without token-based unit definition.,0.5,N/R,Ratio between r and g1 is not formalized as a reward technology.,0.5,N/R,No endowment concept introduced.,1.0,N/R,"Outcomes focus on long-run frequencies (x,y,z) and public good level m; no normalized contribution variable.",1.0,N/R,No explicit contribution amount per player.,1.0,N/R,"They discuss survival, coexistence, and oscillations but not payoff efficiency ratios.",1.0,N/R,No reported aggregate payoffs for this condition.,1.0,N/A,No empirical study.,1.0,N/A,No empirical study.,1.0,N/A,No empirical study.,1.0,N/A,No empirical study.,1.0,No human,Purely theoretical and simulated model.,1.0,"Same game structure as baseline; parameters here: a0=3, b0=0, c0=4, d0=4.1, r=4, g1=0.2, g2=0.1, p=0.7, a1=4, b1=3, c1=5, d1=2, ε=1, β=0.6. Internal equilibrium E2*≈(x,y,m)=(0.6302,0.1660,0.6302) with z=1−x−y≈0.2038 is locally asymptotically stable in the non-delayed model; C, D, and E coexist with C dominant. This parameter set is used as the base when analyzing dependence on reward r, punishment p, reward/punishment costs g1,g2, and as the equilibrium point whose stability is later tracked under various delay combinations in the delayed model (10).",Consolidated from equation (35) and multiple figure descriptions referring back to this parameter set.,1.0
10.1016_j.chaos.2023.114011.md,"Delayed model – Case I (τ1 = 0, τ2 variable; Hopf at τ2 ≈ 1.21, Figs. 8–9, 14, 17, 18(a)-(c))",Condition where only production delay τ2 varies (information delay τ1=0) around internal equilibrium E2* for the delayed system (10).,0.99,"Production delay τ2 in dm/dt = εβ(x(t−τ2) − m), with τ1 fixed at 0 and payoff/feedback parameters as in the E2* base set (a0=3,b0=0,c0=4,d0=4.1,r=4,g1=0.2,g2=0.1,p=0.7,a1=4,b1=3,c1=5,d1=2,ε=1,β=0.6).","In Section 5.2 Case (I), they explicitly set τ1=0 and vary τ2, identifying a critical value τ2_0≈1.21 for Hopf bifurcation and also explore very large τ2 (e.g., 3, 40).",0.99,False,No human or biological experiments; delay differential equations only.,1.0,False,"Parameter sweep in simulations, not an empirical experiment.",1.0,False,No lab/field context.,1.0,True,They numerically integrate the delayed system (10) for various τ2 values and plot time series and phase portraits.,1.0,True,"Section 4.1 provides analytical characteristic equation and Theorem 4.1 for τ1=0, τ2>0, including stability and Hopf conditions (m13<0, etc.).",1.0,2,Underlying game is unchanged.,0.95,1,Continuous-time delay dynamics still reflect a repeated one-shot interaction structure.,0.7,1,"Strategy types (C,D,E) remain discrete.",0.4,N/A,No explicit endowment/contribution decision.,1.0,N/R,MPCR is not part of the delay analysis either.,1.0,N/A,No agents.,1.0,N/A,No experimental interface.,1.0,N/A,No identities.,1.0,N/A,No identities.,1.0,N/A,No discrete rounds are displayed; time is continuous.,1.0,1,"Same punishment structure (p=0.7,g2=0.1) as in E2* base.",1.0,N/R,Cost parameter g2 unchanged but still not tied to discrete cost-per-punishment units.,0.5,N/R,Punishment technology is not respecified under delay.,0.5,1,"Reward parameters (r=4,g1=0.2) unchanged in delayed model.",1.0,N/R,"Cost structure not altered by delay, still not per-unit defined.",0.5,N/R,No explicit technology mapping under delay.,0.5,N/R,Endowment not part of the delay specification.,1.0,N/R,Outcome variables remain frequencies of strategies and public good level.,1.0,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1.0,N/R,"They discuss stability vs. oscillation, not efficiency under delay.",1.0,N/R,No group payoff reported for particular τ2 values.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,No human,Numerical study of a delay differential equation system.,1.0,"Delayed version of the coevolutionary game (system (10)) with information delay τ1 and production delay τ2. In Case (I), τ1=0, τ2>0. Internal equilibrium E2* from the non-delayed base persists, and Theorem 4.1 shows that for τ2∈(0,τ2_0) with τ2_0≈1.21, E2* is locally asymptotically stable; for τ2>τ2_0, a supercritical Hopf bifurcation yields stable periodic oscillations in x,y,z,m. Example values: τ2=0.5 (Fig. 8) gives damped oscillations converging to E2*; τ2=3 (Fig. 9) yields stable limit cycles where all strategies cyclically dominate; very large τ2=40 (Fig. 18(a)-(c)) eventually drive cooperators extinct and defectors dominate (convergence to E1).","Summarized from Section 4.1 Case (2), Theorem 4.1, and numerical illustrations in Section 5.2.",1.0
10.1016_j.chaos.2023.114011.md,"Delayed model – Case II (τ1 variable, τ2 = 0; Hopf at τ1 ≈ 0.3882, Figs. 10–11, 15, 17, 18(d)-(f))",Condition where only information delay τ1 varies (production delay τ2=0) around E2* in delayed system (10).,0.99,"Information delay τ1 in fitness evaluation fC,fD,fE depending on x(t−τ1),y(t−τ1),m(t−τ1); τ2 fixed at 0; payoff parameters as in E2* base.","Section 4.1 Case (3) and Theorem 4.2 explicitly treat τ1 as bifurcation parameter with τ2=0; Section 5.2 explores τ1=0.1,0.6, etc.",0.99,False,Mathematical model with numerical simulations.,1.0,False,No empirical manipulation of subjects.,1.0,False,Purely computational/theoretical.,1.0,True,Time series and phase portraits are generated for various τ1 values.,1.0,True,Theorem 4.2 derives conditions involving m26 and I3I1+I4I2 for Hopf bifurcation as τ1 crosses τ1_0≈0.3882.,1.0,2,Underlying game remains two-player.,0.95,1,Continuous-time repeated interaction setting.,0.7,1,"Discrete strategies C,D,E.",0.4,N/A,No explicit contribution decision.,1.0,N/R,Not applicable here either.,1.0,N/A,No human interaction.,1.0,N/A,No experimental UI.,1.0,N/A,Population-level anonymous interaction.,1.0,N/A,No identities for reward either.,1.0,N/A,No discrete rounds are displayed.,1.0,1,Same p and g2 as E2* base.,1.0,N/R,Cost parameter unchanged but not quantifiable as per-unit.,0.5,N/R,Tech not defined.,0.5,1,Reward structure same as in non-delayed E2* baseline.,1.0,N/R,No per-unit mapping.,0.5,N/R,No explicit technology ratio.,0.5,N/R,No endowment concept.,1.0,N/R,Outcomes are strategic frequencies and public good.,1.0,N/R,No contribution amount reported.,1.0,N/R,Efficiency is not computed.,1.0,N/R,No aggregate payoffs under delays.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,No human,Delay-differential-equation simulations only.,1.0,"Delayed system (10) with τ2=0, τ1>0. Theorem 4.2 shows that for τ1∈(0,τ1_0) with τ1_0≈0.3882, E2* is locally asymptotically stable; if τ1>τ1_0, E2* becomes unstable and a supercritical Hopf bifurcation yields stable periodic oscillations. Example simulations: τ1=0.1 (Fig. 10) show damped oscillations converging to E2*; τ1=0.6 (Fig. 11) show persistent cycles where the three strategies cyclically dominate. Large information delay with τ1=20, τ2=0 (Fig. 18(d)-(f)) ultimately drives the system to E0 (extinction of both strategies C and D, m=0), after a transient cooperative peak.",Summarized from Theorem 4.2 and numerical examples in Section 5.2 Case (II) and Fig. 18(d)-(f).,1.0
10.1016_j.chaos.2023.114011.md,"Delayed model – Case III (τ1, τ2 both variable; Hopf at τ1* ≈ 0.3555 when τ2 = 0.1, Figs. 12–13, 16–18(g)-(i))","Condition with both delays positive, focusing on τ1 as bifurcation parameter with a fixed small τ2=0.1 around E2* in delayed system (10).",0.98,Joint delay configuration with production delay τ2 fixed at 0.1 and information delay τ1 varied; base payoffs as in E2*; critical τ1*≈0.3555 defines Hopf threshold for given τ2.,"Section 4.1 Case (4) (Theorem 4.3) and Section 5.2 Case (III) explicitly analyze τ1 as variable with τ2 in (0,τ2_0); numerics take τ2=0.1 and vary τ1.",0.98,False,No empirical component.,1.0,False,Controlled parameter change in simulations only.,1.0,False,Not in a lab setting.,1.0,True,State trajectories and bifurcation diagrams are computed numerically for this two-delay setting.,1.0,True,"Theorem 4.3 analytically characterizes stability and Hopf for joint delays using coefficients m31–m311, T1,T2,T3,T4.",1.0,2,Same underlying stage game.,0.95,1,Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics.,0.7,1,Discrete strategy types.,0.4,N/A,No explicit contributions.,1.0,N/R,Not defined.,1.0,N/A,No human experiment.,1.0,N/A,No interface.,1.0,N/A,No individual identities.,1.0,N/A,No individual identities.,1.0,N/A,No round display.,1.0,1,"Same p,g2 as E2* base; delays do not change presence of punishment.",1.0,N/R,Cost not reparameterized by delay.,0.5,N/R,No explicit punishment technology parameterization.,0.5,1,"Same r,g1 as E2* base; delays only in dynamics.",1.0,N/R,Cost not per-unit.,0.5,N/R,No technology ratio.,0.5,N/R,Endowment not modeled.,1.0,N/R,"Frequencies x,y,z and m remain primary outcomes.",1.0,N/R,No token contributions.,1.0,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1.0,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not quantified for these delays.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Dynamic-systems analysis and simulation only.,1.0,"Delayed system (10) with both delays: τ1>0 in the replicator components via fC,fD,fE evaluated at t−τ1; τ2>0 in the public-good dynamics dm/dt=β(x(t−τ2)−m). In Case (III), they fix τ2=0.1 (with τ2<τ2_0) and vary τ1. Theorem 4.3 indicates that E2* is locally asymptotically stable for τ1∈(0,τ1_0*) with τ1_0*≈0.3555, and unstable for τ1>τ1_0*; a supercritical Hopf bifurcation leads to stable periodic orbits. Simulations show: τ1=0.1, τ2=0.1 (Fig. 12) gives convergence to E2*; τ1=0.5, τ2=0.1 (Fig. 13) yields stable cycles. Jointly large delays τ1=20, τ2=20 (Fig. 18(g)-(i)) first produce cyclic dominance of C and D and ultimately lead to dominance of D (strategy C extinction), demonstrating that large combined delays are harmful to cooperation.","Summarized from Section 4.1 Case (4), Theorem 4.3, and the numerical results in Section 5.2 Case (III) and Fig. 18(g)-(i).",1.0
