10.1016_j.physa.2021.126839.md,Complete punishment network,"One of four punishment network treatments: complete, circle, homo-pairwise, hetero-pairwise.",1,"Punishment network structure = Complete (each subject can punish all 3 others; 3 potential punishers per subject: 1 same-MPCR, 2 different-MPCR)",Design section defines four network structures and describes connections for the complete punishment network.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 192 human participants.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and protocols.,1,True,Sessions conducted at National Taipei University laboratory using zTree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation mentioned.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental design and statistical analysis, not formal analytical modeling.",1,4,All subjects play a four-player linear public good game.,1,15,Two-stage game repeated over 15 periods.,1,1,"Subjects allocate 20-token endowment between private and group accounts; continuous from 0 to 20, not binary.",1,0,Endowment is given to each subject as private tokens; nothing pre-allocated to public account.,0.9,0.25,"Two players have MPCR 0.3 and two have 0.7; marginal per capita return (multiplier divided by group size) is group-average MPCR: (0.3+0.3+0.7+0.7)/4 = 0.5, but in heterogeneous VCM literature MPCR is individual-specific. For a single scalar, use expected MPCR across subjects = 0.5; divided by 2? However schema wants MPCR as used in payoff function. Here payoff is MPCR_i * sum c_j. Effective per-capita return per token/unit contributed from the perspective of a randomly drawn participant is 0.5. To stay consistent with definition, use 0.5.",0.5,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous VCM with no chat.,0.9,1,Participants see aggregate group contribution and their own payoff after stage one and total punishments given/received after stage two.,0.9,0,Design states subjects only know total number of punishments assigned and received; identities of punishers not revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism in experiment.,1,1,"Standard lab practice and 15 fixed periods; although not explicit, participants usually informed of total periods.",0.6,1,Peer punishment stage two described for all treatments.,1,1,Subject must pay cost of 1 token per punishment assigned.,1,3,Each punishment reduces punished subject's payoff from stage one by three tokens.,1,0,Only costly peer punishment is implemented; no reward mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each subject receives 20 tokens as endowment at beginning of stage one.,1,0.148,Table 1: group contribution 11.84 out of total endowments 80; 11.84/80 = 0.148.,1,11.84,Table 1: group contribution in complete network is 11.84 tokens.,1,0.5383,"Max group payoff per period is 160 tokens (all contribute 20, no punishment). Table 7: average group payoff 86.13. Efficiency = 86.13/160 ≈ 0.5383.",0.95,86.13,Table 7: group payoffs in complete punishment network = 86.13.,1,Taiwan,"Experiment conducted at National Taipei University, Taiwan; participants are students there.",0.95,N/R,Only notes that participants are students; no age distribution or range reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0,University students,Participants recruited from students enrolled at National Taipei University.,1,On site,Conducted in university lab with zTree and physical seating with privacy partitions.,1,"Four-player linear VCM with heterogeneous individual MPCRs (two players with MPCR 0.3, two with 0.7). Stranger matching across 15 periods; individual MPCR randomly reassigned each period. Stage 1: contribute 0–20 from 20-token endowment to group account; payoff π_i^1 = 20 - c_i + MPCR_i * sum_j c_j. Stage 2: peer punishment on a complete directed network where each player can punish all three others; punishment binary per target (0 or 1). Each punishment costs punisher 1 token and reduces target’s stage-1 payoff by 3 tokens. Final payoff is sum of stage 1 and 2. Identities of punishers not revealed; subjects see only aggregate contributions in stage 1 and total punishments given/received in stage 2.",Summary of rules from Sections 2.1 and 2.2 specialized to complete network case (all others in N_i).,1
