1,No human,Entirely agent-based simulations.,1,"Same game and population setup. Within each unit, all cooperative agents (contributors) punish all agents with rational egoist profile. Each punisher incurs cost = its endowment divided by number of neighbors; each egoistic agent pays fine of 40% of its endowment. All punishment costs and fines are added to the public good. Results: this 'collegial' peer-punishment mechanism yields the highest overall aggregate contribution levels; cooperation increases with larger span of control due to stronger induced group pressure and positive perceptions, and is relatively independent of the number of hierarchical levels.","Mechanism from Section 5.4 item 4; qualitative results from Observations 1, 4, and 5 and Fig. 6–9.",0.95
