10.1017_s0266267119000233.md,Illustrative public goods game without punishment,"Paper describes a canonical public goods game (group size 10, contribution 10, multiplier 5) as an illustrative laboratory setup, not reporting original data.",0.88,"Presence of public good contribution opportunity without punishment; group size = 10, individual contribution option = 10 monetary units, multiplier = 5","Author specifies population size ten, contribution value $10, multiplier five, and no punishment stage.",0.9,True,"Described as a staple of experimental economics with human participants, citing Ledyard 1995.",0.92,True,Public goods games are described as laboratory experiments with controlled payoffs.,0.95,True,Referred to as economics lab experiments; cites lab survey Ledyard 1995.,0.95,False,"Human lab experiment, not a computer-only simulation.",0.98,False,Game is presented as experimental; payoffs are not purely analytical results.,0.8,10,Explicitly: 'for a population size of ten'.,1,1,Example is described as 'if this game is played a single time'; later discussion of iterations is separate.,0.9,0,"Footnote 6 notes that in many such games individuals choose how much to contribute, implying a continuous/any-amount decision rather than all-or-nothing.",0.7,0,Standard PGG: all endowment starts private; contributions are voluntary from private to public.,0.85,0.5,MPCR = multiplier / group size = 5 / 10 = 0.5.,0.95,0,No mention of communication; canonical linear public goods games typically disallow chat.,0.6,1,"In the iterative discussion players are made aware of contribution patterns; although the single-shot illustrative case does not iterate, typical implementation at least reveals group outcome; set to 1 given context.",0.6,0,No punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism is described.,0.95,1,Game described as known to be single-shot; participants in such experiments are informed of the number of rounds.,0.7,0,"Baseline game with only contribution decision, no punishment phase.",0.98,N/A,No punishment in this game.,1,N/A,No punishment in this game.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each player has the option to contribute $10 or keep it; interpreted as endowment.,0.9,N/R,No quantitative outcome data reported for this illustrative game.,0.01,N/R,"Only theoretical incentives discussed, not observed contributions.",0.01,N/R,No empirical efficiency numbers reported.,0.01,N/R,"Only example of potential earnings, no actual outcome data.",0.01,N/R,Country or lab location of participants not specified for this generic example.,0.01,N/R,No demographic information given.,0.01,N/R,No demographic information given.,0.01,N/R,Not reported; likely students but not stated.,0.1,On site,"Described as 'economics lab' experiments, which are typically in-person laboratory sessions.",0.85,Standard linear public goods game with group size 10; individual chooses whether and how much to contribute from a $10 endowment to a common pot multiplied by 5 and equally redistributed; no punishment or reward stage.,Summarized from the descriptive example in section 3 and footnote 6.,0.9
