They state C_high P_high ‘induced the highest cooperation rate’, but do not provide an explicit numeric cooperation index for this corner in text.",0.4,N/A,"Simulation tracks cooperation acts, not token amounts.",1,N/R,"They report a ‘cooperation index’, not efficiency vs maximum payoff.",0.3,N/R,"Simulation results focus on cooperation rates and strategies, not summed payoffs.",0.3,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in simulation.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based model.,1,"Finite population of size N=12 on same heterogeneous network as behavioral tPDG: 3 central nodes, 9 peripheral. Two types of agents: classical agents using 10-strategy pool (combinations of always-cooperate, always-defect, tit-for-tat, punishment-driven cooperation with always-punish, always-nonpunish, conditionally-punish), and manipulated central agents whose cooperation and punishment rates are exogenously set. Each generation, all connected pairs play 30 rounds of the two-stage PD with punishment; fitness is accumulated payoff. After each generation, worst-performing agent replaces its strategy with a new draw from appropriate pool; selection strength ω=8 in main runs. Cooperation index computed as proportion of cooperative actions at start of each generation, averaged over last 50,000 of 100,000 generations.",Summarizes model structure and evolutionary dynamics from Evolutionary modeling section.,1
