10.1371_journal.pone.0044432.md,Replicator dynamics with payments to punishers (righteousness game),Single modeling setup described: a four-strategy evolutionary game analyzed via continuous-time replicator dynamics with parameterized punishment and a small payment e to punishers.,0.97,"Punishment of defecting non-punishers p; punishment of defecting punishers q; fixed parameters t=4, r=2, s=1, c=0.2, e=0.01; p and q varied from 1 to 10 in increments of 0.25 in numerical simulations.","Methods and Results state that analysis focuses on parameters p and q, and that simulations are run for p and q between 1 and 10 in increments of 0.25 with fixed values of t, r, s, c, e.",0.98,False,Study uses analytical derivations and numerical simulations of replicator dynamics; no human or animal subjects.,1.0,False,No controlled lab/field experiment; only mathematical and simulation-based analysis.,1.0,False,No laboratory setting with participants; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1.0,True,They numerically simulate the replicator dynamics system across parameter ranges of p and q and report basins of attraction.,1.0,True,They derive equilibria and stability conditions analytically with payoff matrices and replicator equations.,1.0,2,"The interaction is a 2-player Prisoner's Dilemma–type game, as indicated by the payoff matrix between two individuals with strategies C, D, H, K.",0.95,1,Standard evolutionary game-theory and replicator dynamics assume a stage game repeated over generations; per-interaction structure is one-shot; set to 1 for lack of explicit repeated discrete rounds.,0.6,1,Model is formulated directly as a Prisoner's Dilemma with fixed payoffs rather than continuous contribution amounts; all-or-nothing cooperation/defection choice.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments or explicit public-goods contribution structure; purely payoff matrix with cooperation/defection.,1.0,N/R,"Model is a Prisoner's Dilemma with payoffs r, s, t; no marginal per capita return of a public good is defined.",1.0,N/A,No human subjects or communication; evolutionary dynamics only.,1.0,0,No information feedback between individuals beyond payoffs in the theoretical payoff matrix.,0.9,N/A,No explicit identity or information structure; model abstracts away from who punishes whom at the individual level.,1.0,N/A,No explicit reward/identity mechanism apart from small payment e embedded in payoffs.,1.0,0,No notion of displayed rounds in an evolutionary differential-equation model.,1.0,1,Punishment costs p and q are core parameters; strategies H and K are punishers and incur cost c to punish.,1.0,N/R,Model specifies cost to the punisher as c>0 but does not map this to a discrete 'per-unit' coin-based technology; no direct analog to per-token cost.,0.7,N/R,"Model provides punishment impacts on defectors (p and q) and cost c, but not as a discrete per-unit technology like 'target loses β per 1 cost'.",0.7,1,Small payment e>0 from cooperators to punishers effectively rewards punishers.,0.9,N/R,Parameter e enters the payoff matrix but is not expressed as a separate cost vs. transfer technology; not a standard per-unit cost in tokens.,0.7,N/R,The small payment e is integrated into payoffs but not as an explicit 1-to-β transfer technology.,0.7,N/R,"No endowment or token budget is defined; only payoff parameters r, s, t, p, q, c, e.",1.0,N/R,Outcome variables are frequencies of strategies and basins of attraction; no contributions relative to endowment are specified.,1.0,N/R,No token-based contributions are modeled; only cooperate/defect strategies in a 2x2-like payoff matrix.,1.0,N/R,"They discuss relative wellbeing and payoffs of equilibria, but do not normalize group payoff by a theoretical maximum to report efficiency 0–1.",0.8,N/R,Text discusses 'net wellbeing' and 'collective payoff' qualitatively but does not provide explicit numeric group payoff values for conditions.,0.8,N/A,No human (or specific animal) participants; purely theoretical and simulation-based.,1.0,N/A,No human sample.,1.0,N/A,No human sample.,1.0,N/A,No human sample.,1.0,No human,Study consists solely of analytical derivations and numerical simulations with no human or field environment.,1.0,"Four-strategy evolutionary Prisoner’s Dilemma-style game with strategies: cooperative non-punisher (C), defecting non-punisher (D), cooperative punisher (H), defecting punisher (K). Payoff matrix parameterized by r, s, t (PD payoffs), p and q (punishment severities for defectors and corrupt punishers), c (cost of punishing), and e (small payment from cooperators to punishers). Dynamics studied via continuous-time replicator equation with 4-strategy simplex; equilibria labeled as defection (z_D), corruption (z_K or z_CK), and righteousness (z_H or z_HK). Numerical simulations estimate basins of attraction over p,q∈[1,10].",Summarizes formal game structure and dynamic analysis as described in Methods and Results.,0.98
