10.1177_0959354308091842.md,Conceptual summary of ultimatum game evidence,The paper does not report its own experiment; it summarizes typical results from many ultimatum game studies as one conceptual condition.,0.74,Ultimatum game role (proposer vs responder) and offer size as fraction of pie,The only varying elements described are the player role and the offer proportion; no specific treatment manipulations are reported.,0.7,True,Summarizes behavior of human subjects in ultimatum games (“numerous experiments conducted throughout the world”).,0.92,True,Ultimatum games are controlled lab experiments with randomized roles and fixed rules.,0.95,True,"Ultimatum games referenced (Fehr, Gintis, etc.) are standard economic lab experiments; no field/online setting is indicated.",0.8,False,No computer/agent-based simulations are described; only human experiments are referenced.,0.99,False,"The section is empirical/behavioral, not formal modeling.",0.95,2,Ultimatum game is explicitly described as between a proposer and a responder.,1,1,Described as one-shot anonymous interactions; ultimatum games here are single-round.,0.9,1,"Standard ultimatum games allow any split of the pie, i.e., continuous or multiple discrete amounts, not just all-or-nothing; text does not contradict this.",0.7,0,"In an ultimatum game there is no public good endowment; the pie starts entirely under the proposer’s control, analogous to a 0 proportion preallocated to a public account.",0.4,N/R,"Ultimatum game is a bargaining game, not a public goods game; no MPCR is defined or reported.",1,0,"Standard ultimatum experiments referenced are anonymous with no communication; the text emphasizes anonymity and one-shot nature, with no mention of chat.",0.85,0,Only two players and a single outcome; no mention of earnings summaries beyond direct offers/outcomes.,0.8,0,"Punishment is implicit via rejection, and interactions are anonymous; identities are not revealed.",0.9,0,Positive outcomes (accepted offers) do not involve identity revelation; interactions are anonymous.,0.9,1,"Described as a one-shot game, so participants must know there is a single round.",0.7,1,"Responder can punish unfair offers by rejecting, causing both to get nothing; this is the sanctioning mechanism discussed.",0.95,N/R,"No specific pie size or payoff units are given, so the numeric cost per unit of punishment cannot be calculated.",0.2,N/R,The magnitude of loss imposed per unit of punisher’s cost is not specified numerically.,0.2,0,No separate rewarding option in ultimatum games is mentioned; only division of the pie and the possibility of rejection.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the described ultimatum games.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the described ultimatum games.,1,N/R,"The absolute monetary amount of the pie is not reported; only proportions are discussed (e.g., 40–50%).",0.2,N/R,"Outcome is offer and rejection behavior, not contribution to a public good; no normalized contribution rate is defined.",1,N/R,"Only proportions of the pie (40–50%, offers <30%) are reported, not the underlying token or currency amounts.",0.4,N/R,No aggregate efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is reported for these games.,0.8,N/R,No numerical payoffs for the pair (sum of proposer and responder earnings) are provided.,0.2,N/R,The paper states that experiments were conducted “throughout the world” but does not specify countries.,0.3,N/R,No information about participants’ ages is provided.,0.01,N/R,No information about participants’ gender composition is provided.,0.01,N/R,"No information about participants’ education (e.g., students vs general population) is provided.",0.01,On site,"Standard ultimatum game experiments in the cited literature (e.g., Fehr, Gintis) are typically conducted in physical labs; the paper does not mention online or field settings.",0.65,"One-shot, anonymous ultimatum game where a proposer divides a monetary pie and a responder can accept (both paid according to split) or reject (both receive nothing). Typical findings summarized: most proposers offer 40–50% of the pie; offers below 30% are frequently rejected; about 25% of participants behave self-regardingly while about 50% exhibit strong reciprocity.",Paraphrased from the section labeled “Evidence” describing ultimatum games and behavioral regularities.,0.98
10.1177_0959354308091842.md,Conceptual summary of public goods games with altruistic punishment,"The paper summarizes evidence from Fehr & Gächter (2002) public goods experiments with punishment, without detailing specific treatments; treated here as one conceptual condition.",0.72,Presence of costly punishment option in a public goods game,The only distinct manipulation mentioned is that participants can punish uncooperative behavior at a cost; no other treatment dimensions are specified.,0.78,True,Refers to Fehr and Gächter’s public goods experiments with human participants.,0.98,True,Described as public goods games designed to test punishment behavior under controlled conditions.,0.98,True,"Fehr & Gächter (2002, Nature) are classic laboratory public goods experiments; the paper does not suggest field conditions.",0.95,False,No indication of agent-based or numerical simulation; behavior is from human subjects.,0.99,False,The section discusses empirical findings rather than a formal analytical model.,0.98,N/R,"The group size for the summarized public goods games is not stated in this paper, even though Fehr & Gächter (2002) used groups of 4; external knowledge cannot be assumed.",0.3,N/R,The number of periods in the public goods games is not reported here.,0.3,1,"Standard public goods experiments of this type allow any contribution from 0 up to the endowment; the paper does not contradict this, but does not state it explicitly.",0.6,0,"In typical linear public goods games, each participant’s endowment starts in their private account, from which they decide how much to contribute; no alternative initial allocation is mentioned.",0.6,N/R,The marginal per capita return from the public account is not specified in this text.,0.3,0,The summary emphasizes one-shot and anonymous interactions and does not mention any opportunity for communication.,0.8,1,"In public goods games with punishment, subjects must observe others’ contributions (and often payoffs) to target punishment; contributions of others must be displayed in some form, which counts as a summary of others’ behavior.",0.75,0,"Interactions are said to be between “completely anonymous strangers,” implying that punishment does not reveal identities; though standard designs use ID numbers, personal identity is not shown.",0.7,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism is mentioned; only punishment of uncooperative behavior is discussed.,0.9,N/R,The description does not indicate whether participants are told the total number of rounds in the Fehr & Gächter games.,0.3,1,The paper explicitly notes that participants were willing to punish uncooperative behavior at their own cost.,1,N/R,The cost per punishment unit to the punisher is not numerically described.,0.25,N/R,The punishment technology (how much the target loses per unit spent) is not quantified.,0.25,0,Only punishment of uncooperative behavior is mentioned; no positive reward option is described.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism is present in the summarized design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present in the summarized design.,1,N/R,The size of each participant’s endowment in the public goods game is not reported.,0.3,N/R,"The paper states that participants punish uncooperative behavior and that punishment increases cooperation, but does not report actual contribution rates as fractions of endowment.",0.4,N/R,No specific token or monetary contribution amounts are given.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes are described qualitatively (higher cooperation, group survival chances) without numeric efficiency levels.",0.4,N/R,No numeric group payoff statistics are provided for these public goods experiments.,0.2,N/R,Countries involved in Fehr & Gächter’s experiments are not specified in this article.,0.3,N/R,No information on subject ages is provided.,0.01,N/R,No information on gender composition is provided.,0.01,N/R,"No information on educational background (e.g., students) is given.",0.01,On site,Fehr & Gächter (2002) public goods experiments are standard lab studies with subjects physically present; the paper does not indicate online or field implementation.,0.9,"Iterated public goods game with an additional stage allowing participants to pay to punish uncooperative (low-contributing) group members. Interactions are described as between anonymous strangers, aimed at removing possibilities of repeated interaction or reputation, and yet many participants engage in costly punishment of defectors, exemplifying strong reciprocity.",Summarized from the “Evidence” section referring to Fehr & Gächter (2002) and later discussion on how punishment by strong reciprocators stabilizes cooperation.,0.9
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.115778.md,C + IAC + D system on square lattice (spatial PGG with inequity-averse cooperators),"Main simulation model with strategies C, IAC, and D on a square lattice described in Section 2 and analyzed in Section 3.1.",1,"Synergy factor r (e.g., 3.5, 4), punishment cost coefficient α, punishment fine coefficient β; strategy set {C, IAC, D}","Phase diagrams and dynamics are reported as functions of r, α, β for the C+IAC+D strategy set.",1,False,Study uses numerical simulations of evolutionary games on a lattice; no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational simulation.,1,False,No laboratory with human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe running simulations on a square lattice for up to 8×10^8 generations.,1,False,"They present model equations but all results are from numerical simulations, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,40000,Square lattice with L=200 implies 200×200=40000 individuals.,1,800000000,Simulations run for 10^8–8×10^8 generations; use upper bound as maximum number of rounds.,0.8,0,Standard PGG where cooperators contribute fixed amount c=1 and defectors 0; no intermediate choices available.,1,N/A,No human endowments or default allocations; contributions are fixed strategic rules in a simulation.,1,0.7,"MPCR = r / group size. Group size is 5. They analyze r=3.5 as a key case, so MPCR=3.5/5=0.7.",0.9,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely simulated evolutionary dynamics.,1,0,Agents update via payoff-based imitation; no display of summaries is modeled.,0.9,N/A,"No identities in the simulation; punishment is based on payoffs and local neighborhood, not IDs.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this system; only punishment is modeled.,1,0,Agents follow fixed evolutionary rules; there is no interface showing remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Second stage explicitly models punishment by inequity-averse individuals.,1,N/R,Punishment cost is α·(payoff difference); α varies across conditions and no single numeric value applies.,0.2,N/R,Punishment fine is β·(payoff difference); β varies by condition; no single fixed per-unit technology reported.,0.2,0,Model only includes punishment; no reward term or positive sanction described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this system.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this system.,1,N/R,They set contribution c=1 for cooperators but do not specify an explicit per-round endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Results are reported as fractions of strategy types, not explicit contribution rates relative to endowment.",0.2,N/R,Cooperators contribute c=1 by rule; no aggregate or average contribution amounts are tabulated as outcomes.,0.3,N/R,"Paper analyzes phases and fractions of strategies, not explicit efficiency metrics.",0.1,N/R,No numerical group payoff statistics are reported; only qualitative payoff relations.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents.,1,No human,All results are from computational simulations without humans.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a L=200 square lattice with periodic boundaries; each individual plays in 5-person groups (self and four neighbors). Cooperators (C, IAC) contribute c=1, defectors (D) contribute 0. IACs incur punishment costs and fines based on payoff differences (parameters α, β). Strategy updating via pairwise comparison with Fermi rule (K=0.5).","Summarized from the model description in Section 2 (population structure, game rules, punishment, and updating).",1
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.115778.md,C + SIAC + D system on square lattice (spatial PGG with smarter inequity-averse cooperators),"Alternative simulation where IAC is replaced by SIAC, which only punishes defectors while keeping inequity-based punishment intensity.",1,"Synergy factor r=3.5, punishment cost coefficient α, punishment fine coefficient β; strategy set {C, SIAC, D}","Phase diagram in Fig. 4 is described for r=3.5 with α, β varied and strategies C, SIAC, D.",1,False,Numerical agent-based simulation; no human data.,1,False,"Simulation study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab with human subjects; only simulated agents.,1,True,They explicitly generate a phase diagram using simulations on the same lattice structure.,1,False,"Results are numerical phase diagrams, not analytical solutions.",0.9,40000,Same lattice size L=200 as in the main model.,1,800000000,They use the same simulation protocol (10^8–8×10^8 generations) for all systems.,0.8,0,"Cooperators contribute fixed c=1, defectors 0; no continuous choice.",1,N/A,No human endowment allocation; contributions determined by strategy.,1,0.7,Same PGG as base model with r=3.5 and group size 5 → MPCR=3.5/5.,0.9,N/A,No communication mechanism among simulated agents.,1,0,No explicit information display modeled; agents only use own and neighbor payoffs for updating.,0.9,N/A,Agents do not have IDs; interactions are local and anonymous in the lattice.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is included.,1,0,Simulation rules do not include knowledge of total or remaining rounds.,0.9,1,SIAC agents punish defectors based on payoff inequity.,1,N/R,Cost is α·(payoff difference); α varies; no single numeric constant applies.,0.2,N/R,Fine is β·(payoff difference); β varies by condition.,0.2,0,Reward is not modeled in this system.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Only c=1 is specified; no explicit endowment per period.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes are in terms of strategy fractions, not normalized contributions.",0.2,N/R,Not reported as a separate dependent variable; fixed by strategy.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric is provided.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs are discussed qualitatively but no numeric statistics are given.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Purely computational simulation.,1,"Same spatial PGG as C+IAC+D system, but SIAC agents can identify defectors and only punish them, reducing internal conflict with cooperators; punishment cost and fine remain proportional to payoff differences (parameters α, β). Strategy updating uses the Fermi rule with K=0.5 on an L=200 lattice.",Summarized from Section 3.1 where SIAC is introduced and Fig. 4 described.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.115778.md,C + IAD + D system on square lattice (spatial PGG with inequity-averse defectors),"Simulation model with strategies C, IAD, and D described in Section 3.2 and Fig. 11.",1,"Synergy factor r (e.g., 3.5), punishment cost coefficient α, punishment fine coefficient β; strategy set {C, IAD, D}",Phase diagram for C+IAD+D is given as a function of α and β at r=3.5.,1,False,Model is simulated; no human participants.,1,False,"Not an empirical experiment, but a numerical simulation.",1,False,No lab subjects; only simulated agents.,1,True,Authors state they conduct simulations on a lattice for many generations.,1,False,Results are phase diagrams and spatial snapshots from simulations.,0.9,40000,Same lattice size L=200.,1,800000000,Same simulation horizon (10^8–8×10^8 generations) as described for all systems.,0.8,0,"Cooperators contribute c=1, defectors (including IAD) contribute 0.",1,N/A,No human endowments; contributions are rule-based.,1,0.7,Key analysis for r=3.5 in a 5-person group yields MPCR=3.5/5.,0.9,N/A,No communication modeled among agents.,1,0,No explicit reporting of others' earnings; only local payoff-based imitation.,0.9,N/A,"No identities, only local neighborhoods.",1,N/A,Reward is not part of this system.,1,0,Agents have no modeled knowledge of total or remaining generations.,0.9,1,IAD agents punish higher-paid neighbors according to inequity aversion rule.,1,N/R,Cost is α times payoff difference; α varies; no single constant per-unit cost reported.,0.2,N/R,Fine is β times payoff difference; β varies by condition.,0.2,0,No reward or positive sanction mechanism described for this system.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Only the contribution amount c=1 is given; endowment per round is not specified.,0.2,N/R,"They track fractions of cooperators versus defectors, not explicit normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,Not separately reported beyond the rule that C contributes c=1.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency is not quantitatively calculated; focus is on coexistence phases.,0.1,N/R,No tabulated group payoff outcomes; only qualitative discussion of payoffs.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation-only study.,1,"Same spatial PGG structure as other systems: L=200 square lattice with periodic boundaries; 5-person overlapping groups (self and four neighbors), synergy factor r (e.g., 3.5), cooperators contribute c=1, defectors and IADs contribute 0. Inequity-averse defectors (IADs) punish neighbors with higher PGG payoffs with cost α and fine β scaled by payoff differences. Strategy updating uses Fermi imitation with K=0.5.","Summarized from Section 2 and Section 3.2, including Fig. 11 and related discussion.",1
10.1177_105971230601400306.md,Rigid agents – baseline genetic algorithm,"Section 2.1–2.2 describes rigid agents without plasticity; Section 3.1 reports results for this rigid population as a distinct baseline condition (Figure 1a, Figure 2).",1,Agent type: rigid (no phenotypic plasticity); selection algorithm: tournament with S=500; population size N=100; stake M=1000; mutation probability per gene=0.05; no additional plasticity parameters.,Model parameterization for this condition is explicitly specified in Sections 2.1–2.2 and reiterated in Results 3.1.,1,False,Study uses agent-based evolutionary simulations with artificial agents only.,1,False,No human or field experiment; this is a computational model analysis.,1,False,No laboratory with human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,True,The paper repeatedly refers to evolutionary simulations and an implemented agent-based model.,1,False,No closed-form mathematical analysis; results are from simulations.,1,100,"They state using 100 agents in multiple places (e.g., Section 3.1, for comparison with Sánchez and Cuesta).",0.9,20000000,They report running simulations for up to 20 million generations; we map one reproduction cycle to one ‘round’ of evolutionary updating.,0.5,1,"Proposers can offer any integer amount from 0 to M, not just all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,"This is an ultimatum bargaining model, not a public goods or contribution game.",1,N/A,No public good or multiplier structure; payoffs are simple splits of M.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between agents is not modelled.,1,N/A,Not a human experiment; information displays are not part of the design.,1,N/A,There is no punishment stage or identities in a human sense; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage beyond standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents are not informed about simulation length in a cognitive sense.,1,0,"There is no separate costly punishment action; ‘altruistic punishment’ is interpreted via rejection thresholds, not a punishment technology.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or cost parameter.,1,N/A,No punishment technology is modelled.,1,0,No extra rewarding stage beyond accepting an offer.,1,N/A,No explicit costly rewarding action.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,1000,The total money at stake in each game is M=1000 for all simulations (Section 3).,1,N/R,"They report average offers and thresholds as percentages of M, but do not provide a single final normalized rate for this baseline besides describing convergence to very small offers; no numeric summary is given.",0.2,N/R,No explicit final average amount offered is numerically reported for the rigid baseline in tokens.,0.2,N/R,They do not report average realized payoffs or efficiency ratios for this condition.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics are provided.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary simulations.,1,"Two-player ultimatum game; agents have genes go (offer) and gr (minimum acceptable offer) in [0, M]. Random matching each game; if offer >= gr, proposer receives M - offer and responder offer; otherwise both receive 0. Tournament-style genetic algorithm every S=500 games, with fitness measured as accumulated payoff over last S games; offspring inherit genome with 5% per-gene mutation by ±1.",Summarized from Sections 2.1–2.2 and 3.1 describing the rigid-agent model.,1
10.1177_105971230601400306.md,Rigid agents – Sánchez & Cuesta algorithm implementation,"Section 3.1 explicitly distinguishes the authors’ rigid model (Figure 1a) from their implementation of Sánchez and Cuesta’s (2005) model (Figure 1b), which uses a different genetic algorithm.",1,"Agent type: rigid; selection algorithm: global best reproduces, worst removed (Sánchez & Cuesta rule); population size N=100; stake M=1000; S=500; mutation probability per gene=0.05 (1/20).","Section 3.1 states M=1000, S=500, 100 agents, and a one-in-twenty chance of mutating each gene when reproducing Sánchez and Cuesta’s model.",1,False,Computational agent-based model only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment with subjects.,1,False,No laboratory/human setting.,1,True,They reproduce another simulation model algorithmically.,1,False,"Results are simulation-based, not analytical.",1,100,They report using 100 agents for their implementation of Sánchez and Cuesta’s model.,1,10000000,"They describe behavior after around 10 million generations for rigid agents, including the Sánchez & Cuesta implementation (Figure 2 caption references 10 million generations).",0.6,1,"Same ultimatum game structure: continuous integer offers in [0,M].",1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No linear public good.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/A,Purely simulated.,1,0,No separate costly punishment action; reciprocity is via rejection thresholds.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,1000,M=1000 for the whole set of simulations.,1,N/R,They state that offers and thresholds fluctuate around non-zero altruistic values but do not provide final numeric averages.,0.2,N/R,No explicit final average offer amount in tokens is reported.,0.2,N/R,No payoff or efficiency statistics are given.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not reported.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from computer simulations.,1,"Same ultimatum game as baseline, but genetic algorithm differs: in each generation, the agent with lowest fitness is removed and the agent with highest fitness is duplicated, with 1/20 mutation probability per gene by ±1; fitness is inherited in Sánchez & Cuesta’s original, whereas authors note differences in their implementation but still follow a global best/worst rule.",Summarized from Section 2.2 comparison and Section 3.1 description of their implementation of Sánchez and Cuesta’s model.,0.8
10.1177_105971230601400306.md,Plastic agents – Type 1 plasticity (lifelong adaptability),Section 2.3.1 defines Type 1 plastic agents (plastic throughout lifetime); Section 3.2.1 reports results for this condition (Figure 3 and Figure 4).,1,"Agent type: Type 1 plasticity (phenotype adapts after every game, no bounds); population size N=100; stake M=1000; S=500; mutation probability per gene=0.05; adaptation rule: proposer increases/decreases offer by 1 after rejection/acceptance, responder increases/decreases threshold by 1 after rejection/acceptance.",All these parameters and the learning rule are defined in Sections 2.2–2.3.1 and referenced again in 3.2.1.,1,False,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,"It is a computational experiment, not an empirical experiment with subjects.",1,False,No lab/subjects.,1,True,Agent-based evolutionary simulations with learning.,1,False,No analytical closed-form model is presented.,1,100,Section 3.2.1: ‘average of 10 runs of 100 agents’.,1,20000000,Section 3.2.1: each run lasts 20 million generations.,1,1,Same ultimatum game structure allowing any integer split of M.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No public good.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No separate punishment stage.,1,N/A,No separate reward stage.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe a round counter.,1,0,"Altruistic punishment is realized via rejection thresholds, not a dedicated punishment choice.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit rewarding action.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,1000,Stake M=1000 for all simulations.,1,0.4,Section 3.2.1: ‘they learn to offer around 40%’; 40% of M corresponds to normalized contribution rate 0.4.,0.9,400,"M=1000, and they report learnt offers around 40% of the money at stake; 0.4 * 1000 = 400.",0.9,N/R,No payoff or efficiency stats are reported for this condition.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not given.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same ultimatum game and genetic algorithm as rigid baseline, but each agent has a phenotype (po, pr) initially equal to (go, gr). After each game: if offer was rejected, proposer increases po by 1; if accepted, decreases po by 1. If a responder’s offer was rejected, it increases pr by 1; if accepted, it decreases pr by 1. Phenotypic changes are not inherited; offspring inherit only genotype (go, gr) subject to mutation.",Summarized from Sections 2.3 and 3.2.1.,1
