10.1177_105971230601400306.md,Inherited plasticity – Boolean plasticity gene,Section 2.3.4: first inherited plasticity experiment introduces a Boolean gene (plastic or not); results in Section 3.3 (Figure 10).,1,"Agent type: rigid vs plastic determined by Boolean gene; plastic gene=1 implies Type 1-like plasticity; gene is inherited with 5% mutation (flip); other parameters: N≈100, M=1000, S=500, mutation probability per strategy gene=0.05.",Boolean gene mechanism described in 2.3.4; plastic agents follow plasticity rules; mutation rate 5% stated; other baseline parameters from earlier sections; N is not explicitly restated but likely 100.,0.8,False,Simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Agent-based computational model.,1,False,No analytical derivation.,1,100,They consistently use 100 agents elsewhere; not restated for this experiment but likely unchanged.,0.7,N/R,Number of generations for the Boolean gene experiment is not explicitly specified; only that plasticity disappears within about 1000 generations.,0.3,1,Same ultimatum game.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No public good.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No punishment tech.,1,N/A,No reward tech.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,No explicit costly punishment mechanic.,1,N/A,No punishment tech.,1,N/A,No punishment tech.,1,0,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward tech.,1,N/A,No reward tech.,1,1000,M=1000 across simulations.,1,N/R,They only report that plasticity disappears and behavior becomes similar to rigid baseline; no numeric offers.,0.2,N/R,No numeric mean offers given.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency statistics.,0.1,N/R,No payoff statistics.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computational simulation.,1,"Each agent has an additional Boolean gene indicating plasticity (1=plastic, 0=rigid). Plastic agents adapt after every game like Type 1 plastic agents; rigid agents do not adapt. The plasticity gene is inherited from the parent with 5% mutation probability (flip). Plasticity frequency rapidly declines to zero, making the population effectively rigid.","Summarized from Section 2.3.4 and 3.3, including Figure 10 description.",1
10.1177_105971230601400306.md,Inherited plasticity – Probabilistic plasticity gene α with β=0.005,Second experiment in 2.3.4 replaces Boolean gene by continuous α; Section 3.3 and Figure 11a describe case with mutation rate β=0.005.,1,"Agent type: plasticity controlled by real-valued gene α∈[0,1]; α is adaptation probability per game; mutation rate on α is β=0.005; other parameters: N≈100, M=1000, S=500, mutation on strategy genes=0.05.","Mechanism and α,β parameters described in 2.3.4; Figure 11a caption: β=0.005.",0.9,False,Simulation-only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Agent-based computational simulation.,1,False,No analytical derivation.,1,100,Likely same N as previous simulations; not restated but implied consistency.,0.7,N/R,Simulation duration for α gene experiments is not explicitly stated.,0.3,1,"Ultimatum game offers in [0,M].",1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No public good.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,0,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,1000,M=1000 applied globally.,1,N/R,Figure 11a shows trajectories but no numeric stable averages of offers/thresholds are reported.,0.2,N/R,No numeric mean offers given.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metrics.,0.1,N/R,No payoff statistics.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation-only.,1,"Each agent has a gene α∈[0,1] representing the probability of adapting after each game using the same ±1 plasticity rule. α is inherited with mutation rate β=0.005, meaning the offspring’s α is uniformly drawn between α(1−β) and α(1+β). For this β, the average α remains small and strong reciprocity is still observed.",Summarized from 2.3.4 and 3.3 plus Figure 11a caption and description.,1
10.1177_105971230601400306.md,Inherited plasticity – Probabilistic plasticity gene α with β=0.025,Figure 11b caption: mutation rate β=0.025; Section 3.3 notes α stabilizes around 4% and strong reciprocity persists.,1,Agent type: probabilistic plasticity with α gene; mutation rate on α is β=0.025; adaptation occurs with probability α after each game; other base parameters as in β=0.005 condition.,β=0.025 specified; α mechanism as in 2.3.4; rest from earlier description.,0.9,False,Simulation-only.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Agent-based computational model.,1,False,No analytical derivation.,1,100,Likely same N as other simulations.,0.7,N/R,Duration not explicitly stated.,0.3,1,"Ultimatum offers in [0,M].",1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No public good.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,0,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment tech.,1,N/A,No punishment tech.,1,0,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward tech.,1,N/A,No reward tech.,1,1000,M=1000.,1,N/R,"They indicate higher offers and thresholds than in β=0.005, but no numeric averages are given.",0.2,N/R,No numeric mean offers provided.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not quantified.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff not reported.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation-only.,1,"Same α-based probabilistic plasticity as previous α experiment, but with higher mutation rate β=0.025. They report that average α fluctuates around 0.04 (4%), and that offers and acceptance thresholds are higher than in the lower-β case while strong reciprocity persists.",From 3.3 and Figure 11b description.,1
10.1177_01902725221132517.md,Small group – information-only – sparse network,"Condition defined by group size (small: 3–5 participants), phase type (information-only: no punishment), and sparse random network (density ≈ 0.43).",1,"Group size: small (3–5); Network density: sparse (Erdős–Rényi, target density 0.43); Punishment: absent (information-only phase).","Design manipulates group size (small vs large), network density (sparse vs fully dense), and punishment (information-only vs information-and-punishment).",1,True,Human participants in a z-Tree public goods experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to group size and network density; controlled lab task.,1,True,Sessions took place in laboratory space at the research center on campus.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model as primary method; paper reports an experiment.,1,3.81,Mean small group size given as 3.81 (range 3–5). Using mean as representative.,0.7,7,Each density phase within information-only rounds lasted seven rounds.,1,1,Participants chose any integer contribution from 0 to 20 points.,1,0,All 20 points begin as personal endowment and must be actively contributed to the group fund.,1,N/R,"Multiplier is 2 and payoff is redistributed equally, but exact group size for each session varies; paper does not provide MPCR per condition. Authors note MPCR differs by group size but do not report specific numeric MPCRs.",0.4,0,Participants were explicitly prevented from communicating about behaviors.,1,1,"After each round, participants saw a list with each member’s identifier, visible alters’ contributions and kept amounts, and their own earnings.",1,N/A,Punishment not available in information-only rounds.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in any phase.,1,0,Participants were not told in advance how many rounds they would complete to avoid end-game effects.,1,0,Information-only rounds had no deduction/punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,Only costly punishment (deductions) described; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each participant received an endowment of 20 points per round.,1,N/R,"Paper reports contributions in points, not normalized fractions, by condition.",0.2,N/R,Condition-level mean contributions not tabulated in main text; referenced as Table S1 in supplemental materials.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios or comparable calculations reported by condition.,0.1,N/R,Earnings are discussed only qualitatively; no numeric payoffs by condition given.,0.1,United States,"Participants recruited from a subject pool at a U.S. university (Duke University, NC, USA).",0.95,Adult students and community members (exact ages N/R),Subject pool described as adult university students and nonstudent community members; no age statistics.,0.6,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0.1,Mixed: university students and nonstudent community members,Pool consisted of adult university students and nonstudent community members; no further breakdown.,0.8,On site,Participants physically arrived at the research center labs and were escorted to private rooms; interaction via computers.,1,"Linear public goods game: 20-point endowment, contribution 0–20 to group fund; contributed points doubled and redistributed equally to all group members; points kept in personal fund not doubled. Groups randomized to small size (3–5, mean 3.81). Sparse network is an Erdős–Rényi graph with tie probability 0.43; ties fixed within this phase. Letter identifiers used; contributions and kept amounts of tied alters are individually displayed after each round; untied alters appear with blank contribution fields.",Summarizes core structure and network implementation directly from procedure.,1
