10.1098_rsos.211983.md,Conflict punishment group – allocator partner,Paper defines four experimental groups in a 2 (fairness domain: punishment vs compensation) × 2 (conflict degree: conflict vs aligned) design. This row is the conflict punishment group trials involving the fairness partner (allocator).,1,Fairness domain = punishment (allocator as partner); Conflict degree = conflict; Incentive target = decisions in favour of the other; Trial-wise incentive level = 0–500 points; Distribution pairing = equal vs unfair or equal vs hyperfair; Partner type = allocator,"Independent variables manipulated between subjects (fairness domain, conflict degree, incentive target) and within subjects (incentive level, distribution pairing, partner type). This row specifies the combination relevant to punishment domain, conflict, allocator partner.",1,True,Online experiment with human participants recruited via clickworker.com.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to groups with controlled manipulations of incentives and partner type.,1,False,"Study explicitly described as an online version collected via clickworker.com, not in a physical lab.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; all analyses based on human data and analytical DDM fitting.,1,False,Uses hierarchical drift diffusion modelling on empirical data but no standalone closed-form analytical model as a separate study.,0.8,2,"Decision task is a modified dictator game where participants allocate between themselves and one partner (allocator, receiver, or baseline partner).",1,120,For each fairness partner (allocator/receiver) participants made 120 decisions; this row focuses on allocator trials only.,1,1,Two-alternative forced choice between two fixed distributions; players cannot contribute a continuous amount; coded as 1 to reflect that any of the points in a distribution can effectively go to self or other via choice of option.,0.3,0,There is no shared public fund; task is not a contribution game; endowment effectively starts with the decision maker; set to 0 by definition.,0.5,N/R,Not a public goods game; no group multiplier or marginal per capita return reported.,1,0,No communication between participants mentioned; online platform with anonymous roles.,1,0,Participants see only current trial options and incentive; no summaries of others' cumulative earnings or punishments described.,1,0,No punishment stage where identities of punished players are revealed; interactions are anonymous and roles denoted by icons only.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism between players; only payoffs from distributions and incentives; identities not revealed.,1,0,Paper does not state that total or remaining number of trials was shown to participants.,0.7,0,No explicit costly punishment mechanism where points are spent to reduce another’s payoff; fairness-based punishment is operationalized as allocating more to self vs allocator but not via a separate punishment stage.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism implemented.,1,0,"No mechanism where players spend to increase others’ payoff; incentives are paid by experimenter, not via peer reward.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Participants do not receive a standard per-round endowment; each trial presents total splits between 500 and 700 points, not framed as initial endowment.",1,N/R,"Study reports probability of choosing fairness-congruent option, not normalized contributions of an endowment.",1,N/R,Only binary choice between two distributions recorded; no continuous contribution amount reported as DV.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric (total payoff / max possible payoff) reported.,1,N/R,Payout equations are described but no aggregated group payoff statistics reported as outcomes.,1,Germany,"All participants were native German speakers, recruited via clickworker.com, and ethics approval from University of Würzburg; country inferred as Germany.",0.9,18–45,Paper states age range between 18 and 45 years.,1,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,1,N/R,No explicit data on education level or student status reported.,1,Online,Study explicitly described as an online version conducted via clickworker.com.,1,"Two-alternative forced-choice dictator-style allocation between self and allocator; splits of 500–700 points with unfair (≈70:30), hyperfair (≈30:70), or equal (≈50:50) distributions; 6 incentive levels (0–500 points) shown per trial; 120 trials with allocator and 120 with baseline partner; reaction time limit 4000 ms; fairness-congruent option in punishment domain is decision in favour of self.",Summarizes key structural aspects of the decision task beyond public-goods terminology.,1
