10.3389_fpubh.2022.881330.md,System I – Static condition (fixed reward and penalty),"Paper defines ""system I"" as the baseline evolutionary game with fixed reward S and penalty P (static condition).",1,Penalty level P relative to supervision cost I and net fraud gain (C+ΔL−S); static (non-dynamic) reward and penalty.,"System I varies relationships between P, I, C, ΔL, S and analyzes resulting equilibria under fixed (static) incentives.",1,False,Study is an analytical evolutionary game model with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only theoretical modeling and simulations.,1,False,No lab or field experiment; purely theoretical and computational.,1,True,Uses Matlab simulations of replicator dynamics without human subjects.,1,True,"Derives replicator equations, Jacobian, equilibrium, and stability conditions analytically.",1,2,Model involves two strategic populations: local governments and PHSIs.,1,0,"Continuous-time replicator dynamics, not discrete repeated rounds; no finite round count.",0.7,1,"Strategies are binary, but x and y are continuous proportions in [0,1]; not an all-or-nothing contribution structure of a public goods game.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments; theoretical supervision model with no human players.,1,N/R,No public-goods production function or marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol.,1,0,Model does not include information feedback about others’ payoffs; only population proportions.,0.9,N/A,No individuals or identities; population-level model.,1,N/A,No individual-level identification in the model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary dynamics; no round information.,1,1,Penalty P is a core parameter affecting payoffs and dynamics.,1,N/R,Model specifies penalty P but not a per-unit cost structure like coins-to-impact; not mapped to standard punishment-cost parameter.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment spending to impact magnitude per unit.,0.3,1,Reward S is given when governments positively supervise and PHSIs self-discipline.,1,N/R,Reward S is modeled as transfer; no per-unit cost parameter specified.,0.3,N/R,No conversion factor from reward spending to benefit per unit defined.,0.3,N/A,Not a resource-contribution game; no individual endowments.,1,N/R,"Outcome is strategy proportions x and y, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,"Model tracks behaviors (self-discipline vs fraud, supervision) not token amounts.",1,N/R,No aggregate efficiency ratio (payoff relative to maximum) is computed.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are derived symbolically (L, ΔL, G1, etc.) without numerical aggregate group payoff reported.",1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical and simulation study only; no data collection from people.,1,"Two-population evolutionary game between local governments (strategies: positive vs passive supervision) and public health service institutions (PHSIs) (strategies: self-discipline vs fraud) with fixed reward S and fixed penalty P; payoffs parameterized by L, ΔL, C, I, G1; replicator dynamics analyzed and simulated for cases P<I, I<P<C+ΔL−S, and P>max(I, C+ΔL−S).",Summary of the model description and Simulation 1–3 under static incentives.,1
10.3389_fpubh.2022.881330.md,System II – Dynamic penalty mechanism,"Paper defines ""system II"" as the evolutionary game when penalty becomes dynamic P(x) = (1−x)P.",1,Dynamic penalty: effective penalty P(x) = (1−x)P increases with proportion of fraud (1−x); reward S remains fixed.,"Section ""Dynamic Penalty Mechanism"" specifies that punishment is proportional to fraud behavior and replaces P with P(x).",1,False,No human data; purely theoretical and simulated.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Analyzes and simulates replicator dynamics of the modified system.,1,True,"Derives new replicator equations, equilibrium (x_II*, y_II*), and stability via Jacobian.",1,2,Same two populations as in system I.,1,0,Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics.,0.7,1,Strategies are binary choices; proportions continuous.,0.6,N/A,No endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,No public goods production.,1,N/A,No agents/humans to communicate.,1,0,Model omits informational displays of others’ behavior or payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No individual-level identities.,1,N/A,No individuals.,1,0,No rounds in replicator dynamics.,1,1,Dynamic penalty P(x) is central to system II.,1,N/R,"Penalty is modeled as payoff parameter, not cost per punishment unit.",0.3,N/R,No explicit conversion ratio from spending to penalty magnitude defined.,0.3,1,Reward S still present and fixed.,1,N/R,No explicit cost-per-unit for reward.,0.3,N/R,No per-unit reward technology specified.,0.3,N/A,Not a resource allocation game.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are x and y, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts modeled.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency ratios.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff levels under dynamic penalty reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely theoretical and computational.,1,"Same two-population supervision game as system I, but penalty term becomes P(x) = (1−x)P so that punishment intensity endogenously depends on the fraction of fraudulent PHSIs; new replicator dynamics F_II(x), F_II(y) are analyzed; when P exceeds a threshold, interior equilibrium (x_II*, y_II*) is asymptotically stable (ESS) under dynamic penalty.",Summarizes modifications and main stability result for dynamic penalty mechanism.,1
10.3389_fpubh.2022.881330.md,System III – Dynamic reward mechanism,"Paper defines ""system III"" as the case where reward becomes dynamic S(x) = (1−x)S.",1,Dynamic reward: effective reward S(x) = (1−x)S increases with proportion of fraud (1−x); penalty P remains fixed.,"Section ""Dynamic Reward Mechanism"" states reward is proportional to fraud share and substitutes S(x) for S.",1,False,No empirical data; purely model-based.,1,False,No experiment with subjects.,1,False,No laboratory or field setup.,1,True,"Uses simulations (e.g., Figure 5 and 6 use parameters under dynamic mechanisms) alongside analysis.",1,True,"Derives F_III(x), F_III(y), equilibrium (x_III*, y_III*), and proves asymptotic stability.",1,2,Local governments and PHSIs.,1,0,Continuous replicator dynamics.,0.7,1,Binary strategies with continuous population shares.,0.6,N/A,No endowments or voluntary contributions.,1,N/R,No public-goods structure.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,No modeled display of others' outcomes.,0.9,N/A,No individuals or IDs.,1,N/A,No IDs.,1,0,Not a finite-round game.,1,1,Penalty P remains part of payoffs.,1,N/R,No explicit cost-per-unit; P treated as payoff parameter.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mapping from cost to punishment magnitude.,0.3,1,Reward becomes dynamic S(x) but remains a central instrument.,1,N/R,Costs not decomposed per rewarded unit.,0.3,N/R,No explicit per-unit technology.,0.3,N/A,No endowment-based contributions.,1,N/R,"Outcome is evolution of x and y, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution units.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoffs presented for this mechanism.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Modeling and simulation only.,1,"Same supervision game; reward to self-disciplined PHSIs becomes S(x) = (1−x)S, higher when many PHSIs are fraudulent; replicator system F_III(x), F_III(y) derived; interior equilibrium (x_III*, y_III*) exists when P exceeds a threshold and is asymptotically stable. Comparative analysis finds x_III* > x_IV* > x_II*, so dynamic reward yields the highest steady share of self-discipline.",Condenses the paper’s description of system III and its comparative outcome.,1
10.3389_fpubh.2022.881330.md,System IV – Dynamic penalty–reward mechanism,"Paper defines ""system IV"" as case where both penalty and reward are dynamic: P(x) and S(x).",1,Dynamic penalty and reward: P(x) = (1−x)P and S(x) = (1−x)S both depend on proportion of fraud (1−x).,"Section ""Dynamic Penalty-Reward Mechanism"" states both P and S are functions of x and substitutes P(x), S(x) into payoff matrix.",1,False,No empirical data collection.,1,False,No experimental design with subjects.,1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,True,"Simulated along with other dynamic mechanisms (e.g., Figure 4).",1,True,"Derives F_IV(x), F_IV(y), interior equilibrium (x_IV*, y_IV*), and proves asymptotic stability.",1,2,Two strategic populations.,1,0,Continuous evolutionary dynamics.,0.7,1,Binary strategy sets for each population.,0.6,N/A,No contributions or endowments.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,Information displays not modeled.,0.9,N/A,No individual IDs.,1,N/A,No individuals or IDs.,1,0,Infinite-horizon dynamics.,1,1,Penalty P(x) is present.,1,N/R,"No explicit per-unit cost, only aggregate penalty parameter.",0.3,N/R,Punishment technology not parameterized in standard units.,0.3,1,Reward S(x) persists as incentive.,1,N/R,No decomposition of reward into cost per unit.,0.3,N/R,Reward technology not specified.,0.3,N/A,No endowment decisions.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are strategy proportions, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution measures.,1,N/R,Efficiency not calculated.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoff for this mechanism.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Fully theoretical and simulated.,1,"Same supervision game; both penalty and reward are functions of x: P(x)=(1−x)P, S(x)=(1−x)S; replicator system F_IV(x), F_IV(y) derived; for sufficiently large P, interior equilibrium (x_IV*, y_IV*) exists and is asymptotically stable. Comparative analysis (Figure 4) shows dynamic reward-only > dynamic penalty–reward > dynamic penalty in promoting self-discipline (x_III* > x_IV* > x_II*).",Summarizes the structure and main comparative result for dynamic penalty–reward mechanism.,1
