10.1016_j.physleta.2022.127969.md,Well-mixed population replicator dynamics with nonlinear punishment,Paper’s Section 3.1 models a well-mixed population public goods game with nonlinear punishment using replicator dynamics; this is the only well-mixed analytical condition.,1,"Nonlinear punishment factor ω (ω<1 discounted, ω=1 linear, ω>1 synergistic), group size G, multiplication factor r, punishment cost α, punishment fine scale β; this row represents the generic analytical condition over these parameters.","Model explicitly varies ω, G, r, α, β in analysis and phase diagrams (e.g., Fig. 1 uses ω=0.5 vs 1.5; Fig. 2 varies G; Fig. 3 varies ω, r, G); they are the theoretical independent variables.",1,False,No human participants; study is purely theoretical/analytical with differential equations.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only mathematical modeling.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; analytical model only.,1,False,"Section 3.1 derives closed-form replicator dynamics; figures are phase diagrams from analytical conditions, not described as agent-based or numerical simulations with discrete agents.",0.8,True,Replicator equations and equilibria are derived analytically for well-mixed populations.,1,5,"Generic model uses group size G; specific illustrations (e.g., Fig. 3a) fix G=5. As an analytic condition, group size is a parameter; use a representative numeric example G=5 where specified.",0.4,1,"Evolutionary game is modeled as repeated population updating, not discrete experimental rounds; a single-stage public goods game underlies the dynamics. Set to 1 as there is one stage per encounter.",0.5,1,"Each player chooses to cooperate (contribute c=1) or defect (contribute 0); no continuous contribution choice, so contribution is effectively all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human subjects or experimental implementation; initial allocation of tokens between public/private accounts is not defined.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is r/G but the paper does not name or emphasize it as MPCR and uses general variables; they state r(1<r<G) and payoffs r*Nc/G.,0.9,N/A,No human experiment or communication protocol.,1,0,No information feedback structure with human players; not applicable in an analytical model. Set to 0 by default.,0.5,N/A,No identities in an analytical population model; punishment is anonymous and purely payoff-based.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,No user interface or rounds in a human sense; evolutionary time is continuous.,1,1,"Model assumes cooperators pay cost α to punish defectors, who suffer fines depending on β and ω.",1,1,Text: 'cooperators contribute a fixed cost α to the punishment pool.' α is a unit cost parameter; Fig. 1 and others use α=0.5 as an example. Record generic unit cost parameter as 1 token baseline.,0.3,1,"Each cooperator imposes a fine sequence scaled by β and ω; for the first cooperator, fine is β/(G−1). With contribution α, effective tech is proportional to β. Use 1 as normalized tech per cost unit since exact per-cost mapping is not specified.",0.2,0,Only punishment is modeled; no rewards for cooperation are introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes amount c to the public good and c is set to 1 without loss of generality; interpret this as full endowment per game.,0.9,N/R,"Model tracks frequency of cooperators x, not average contribution as a fraction of an explicit endowment in a finite population experiment. While contribution equals x in normalized terms, no explicit DV defined.",0.6,N/R,"Payoffs use contributions of 1 from cooperators, but no separate summary statistic for average contribution amount is reported.",0.7,N/R,No explicit measure of efficiency (total payoff / maximum payoff) is presented.,1,N/R,"Formulas for individual payoffs are given, but there is no specific numerical or summarized group payoff statistic reported as a DV.",0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical (evolutionary game / replicator dynamics) without empirical data.,1,"Two-strategy public goods game (C or D) with group size G, multiplication factor r (1<r<G); cooperators contribute c=1 to public good and pay punishment cost α; defectors contribute nothing; punishment fines to defectors accumulate nonlinearly with factor ω and base fine scale β.",Summary of Section 2 model definition for the well-mixed case.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2022.127969.md,Structured population replicator dynamics with nonlinear punishment on regular networks,Section 3.2 models the same public goods game on regular networks with degree k and nonlinear punishment; this is distinct from the well-mixed case.,1,"Network degree k (regular network), nonlinear punishment factor ω (ω<1 discount, ω=1 linear, ω>1 synergy), multiplication factor r, punishment cost α, punishment fine scale β.","Structured population model explicitly examines how k, ω, β (and also r, α) influence equilibria (e.g., Fig. 4 varying parameter combinations, Fig. 5 varying β and k, Fig. 6 varying k and β).",1,False,No empirical data or human subjects; purely theoretical population-dynamics analysis.,1,False,No controlled behavioral or field experiment; only mathematical modeling.,1,False,No lab environment; model on abstract regular graphs.,1,False,"Replicator equation on graphs is derived using pair-approximation and weak selection; figures show phase diagrams/analytical equilibria, not detailed agent-based simulations.",0.8,True,Appendices A–C derive replicator dynamics analytically on regular networks; main text discusses analytical equilibria.,1,5,"Local game size is k+1 for each focal plus its k neighbors. k varies, but as an example, when k=4 (used in Fig. 5 and 6 captions), group size is 5. Use 5 as representative size.",0.5,1,"As in the well-mixed case, underlying interaction is a single-stage PGG per group; evolutionary updates repeat indefinitely but not in discrete experimental rounds.",0.5,1,Same binary choice as in Section 2: cooperate by contributing c=1 or defect and contribute 0.,1,N/A,No experimental endowment allocation with human subjects is defined.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return in each local group is r/(k+1), but MPCR is not labeled or emphasized as such.",0.9,N/A,No human communication; purely theoretical.,1,0,No user interface or feedback to players is modeled; variable not meaningful here and set to 0.,0.5,N/A,No identities or observability of who punishes; only aggregated payoff effects in the model.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism is included.,1,0,No explicit rounds are displayed; time is continuous in replicator dynamics.,1,1,Same punishment structure as in Section 2 is used on networks: cooperators pay α and defectors receive nonlinear fines governed by β and ω.,1,1,Cooperators 'contribute a fixed cost α to the punishment pool'; α remains the generic per-game punishment cost. Use 1 as normalized unit.,0.3,1,"Defectors experience fines that depend on β, ω, and the number of cooperators in overlapping groups; exact per-cost technology is not separately parameterized beyond β and ω. Record as normalized 1.",0.2,0,No positive incentives or rewards are added in the structured population model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"As in the base model, each cooperator contributes c=1 to each public good it participates in.",0.9,N/R,"Model tracks fraction of cooperators pC; though this can be seen as contribution rate in normalized units, the paper does not define a separate contribution-rate DV.",0.7,N/R,Individual contribution amount is fixed at 1 for cooperators; no aggregate or average contribution amount is reported as a DV.,0.8,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric (ratio of total payoff to maximum possible payoff) is computed or reported.,1,N/R,"Payoff expressions for individuals and accumulated payoffs over k+1 games are given, but no specific average group payoff or numerical summary is presented as a result.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,"Structured population model is a theoretical replicator equation on graphs, not an empirical experiment.",1,"Players occupy nodes of a regular graph with degree k and participate in k+1 overlapping public goods games (one centered on themselves and one on each neighbor). Strategies are cooperate (contribute c=1 and punishment cost α) or defect. Payoffs accumulate across all local games; strategy updating follows pairwise comparison under weak selection with Fermi rule expansion, leading to a replicator equation depending on pC and conditional neighbor frequencies.",Summary of Section 3.2 and Appendix A–C description of structured population public goods game with nonlinear punishment.,1
10.1007_s40656-017-0151-4.md,Fehr and Gächter (2002) – One-shot public goods with costly punishment,Paper describes a seminal experiment by Fehr and Gächter (2002) where an extra costly punishment option is added to a standard public goods game. Only this single treatment is described with any detail.,0.87,Availability of costly punishment option (present); public goods game in one-shot interactions (repeated across periods),"The only manipulation mentioned is that, in addition to contribute/not contribute, subjects can incur personal costs to punish non-contributors.",0.8,True,Described explicitly as a public goods experiment with human subjects.,1,True,"Controlled public goods game in a lab-like setting, introducing a treatment condition.",0.95,True,Referred to as a (laboratory) public goods experiment in economics; no indication of field or online implementation.,0.8,False,"Human subjects, not an agent-based or numerical simulation.",1,False,No formal analytical model of this experiment is described in the excerpt; it is an empirical lab study.,0.9,4,"Standard Fehr & Gächter (2002, Nature 415:137–140) public goods game uses groups of 4; this paper does not restate group size but clearly refers to that study.",0.8,10,"Fehr & Gächter (2002) standard design has 10 periods; this paper notes a one-shot game 'repeated several times' but does not give the exact number, inferred from original study.",0.75,1,"Described as a public goods experiment with options of contributing vs. not contributing, consistent with standard continuous contribution between 0 and endowment in Fehr & Gächter (2002).",0.7,0,"In standard public goods games, endowments start in private accounts; no pre-allocation to public account is mentioned.",0.8,0.4,"In Fehr & Gächter (2002), each token contributed is multiplied by 1.6 and divided among 4 players, giving MPCR = 1.6/4 = 0.4; this paper does not restate it.",0.75,0,"No communication is mentioned, and standard Fehr & Gächter (2002) design disallows communication.",0.8,1,Punishers must observe who contributed vs. free-rode and typically see others’ contributions/earnings in Fehr & Gächter (2002); not restated here but required for targeted punishment.,0.7,0,"In Fehr & Gächter (2002), punishment is anonymous; identities of punishers are not revealed to targets.",0.75,0,No reward mechanism exists in this treatment; therefore identities of rewarders cannot be shown.,1,1,"Standard Fehr & Gächter (2002) procedure informs subjects about the number of periods; paper notes that the one-shot game is repeated several times, implying knowledge of repetition length.",0.7,1,Core design feature: 'subjects were given the opportunity to incur personal costs in punishing non-contributors'.,1,1,"In Fehr & Gächter (2002), each punishment point costs the punisher 1 monetary unit.",0.75,3,"In Fehr & Gächter (2002), each punishment point reduces the target’s payoff by 3 monetary units.",0.75,0,Only costly punishment of non-contributors is described; no rewarding option is mentioned.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this treatment.,1,20,Fehr & Gächter (2002) use an endowment of 20 tokens per period; not specified here but inferred from original study.,0.75,N/R,This paper only qualitatively reports that contributions increase with punishment and decrease without; no numerical contribution levels are given.,0.2,N/R,No specific token amounts or averages are reported.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency statistics are reported.,0.1,N/R,No numerical payoff outcomes are given.,0.1,N/R,Country or lab location of subjects in Fehr & Gächter (2002) is not stated in this article.,0.1,N/R,No age information is provided.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information is provided.,0.1,N/R,This article does not mention whether participants were students or another population.,0.1,On site,"Described as a public goods experiment from 2002; at that time such experiments were typically run in physical laboratories rather than online, and Fehr & Gächter (2002) is known as a lab experiment.",0.8,"Standard linear public goods game with groups of 4, endowment of 20 tokens per period, contribution multiplied by 1.6 and evenly redistributed; after contribution stage, players can assign costly punishment points to others, reducing others’ payoffs at a higher rate than their own cost.","Game structure and parameters are taken from Fehr & Gächter (2002), the exact study referenced as the 'classic seminal study'; this article gives only a brief verbal description.",0.75
