10.3982_te2063.md,Analytical model – general environment,"The paper presents a general theoretical principal–agent model with transfers and social preferences, not split into multiple labeled experiments; this entry covers the baseline analytical environment described in Section 2.1.",0.92,"Agent action a ∈ {C (pro-social), D (self-interested)}; information structure over outcomes z via densities f(z|C), f(z|D); principal’s social preference parameters α (inequity aversion), δ (weight on ex post vs ex ante fairness); transfer cost parameter λ; transfer bound T_max.",These are the key variables that are varied conceptually across propositions and comparative statics in the analytical model.,0.96,False,"No human subjects, data collection, or experiments are reported; the paper is purely theoretical.",1,False,There is no controlled experiment; all results are derived from a formal game-theoretic model.,1,False,No lab or field setting is used; work is theoretical only.,1,False,No numerical/agent-based simulations are presented; analysis is analytical.,1,True,The paper develops and analyzes a formal game-theoretic model with proofs and propositions only.,1,3,"Model includes an active agent A, a passive group player P, and a principal who chooses transfers.",1,1,The core game is a one-shot subjective performance evaluation problem; no repeated-game interaction is modeled within the baseline framework (relational contracts are only in related literature).,0.9,1,"Agent chooses between two discrete actions C and D, not continuous contributions, but the all-or-nothing field is defined relative to contribution levels in PGGs; here there is no contribution choice. Marked as 1 to indicate not constrained to all-or-nothing contributions in a PGG sense is inapplicable; however schema requires 1 or 0, so set to 1 with this caveat.",0.4,N/A,"There is no endowment-contribution public good structure; the agent chooses actions C or D affecting payoffs, so default contribution proportion is not applicable.",1,N/R,No public goods game or linear payoff structure with an MPCR is specified.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication among players in an experiment; this is an analytical model.,1,0,Players do not observe others’ histories or summaries; only the principal observes z and chooses transfers; no repeated-round feedback.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects and no identity revelation; transfers are modeled abstractly and not linked to identifiable individuals.,1,N/A,Same reasoning as punishment identity; not applicable in a purely analytical model.,1,0,Single-stage game; notions of total or remaining rounds are not part of the model.,1,1,Transfers T_z can be positive (from A to P) and are interpreted as punishments; paper discusses punishment explicitly.,1,N/R,"Transfer cost is modeled via λ and deadweight loss λ|T_z|, not as a per-unit punishment cost to the punisher in tokens; no numeric value is given.",0.4,N/R,Transfers T_z directly change payoffs of A and P with deadweight loss; there is no discrete punishment technology parameter analogous to ‘points lost per point spent’.,0.5,1,"Transfers T_z can be negative (from P to A), which correspond to rewards; reward behavior is analyzed (e.g., ‘rewards and punishments’ in title).",1,N/R,"As with punishment, transfer cost is captured by λ|T_z|, not a separate per-unit reward cost parameter.",0.4,N/R,No discrete reward technology like ‘extra tokens per reward point’ is specified; transfers directly add/subtract payoffs.,0.5,N/R,Model payoffs u_A and u_P are defined as deviations from outside options; there is no fixed token endowment as in a standard PGG.,0.9,N/R,No contribution variable or rate (0–1 share of endowment) is defined; the main behavioral variable is probability π(C) of taking action C.,1,N/R,There are no numeric contributions; only binary actions C or D.,1,N/R,"Although efficiency is discussed conceptually (e.g., first-best surplus), no single numeric efficiency outcome is computed for a specific parameterization in the general model.",0.6,N/R,"Group payoff u_A + u_P is analyzed symbolically, but no specific numeric value is reported for a particular calibrated environment in the general framework.",0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Paper is purely theoretical with no empirical or simulation component.,1,"Principal–agent model with one active agent A choosing binary action C or D that stochastically determines payoffs (u_A, u_P) and public signal x; principal observes z=(u,x) and chooses transfer T_z ∈ [−T_max, T_max] between A and passive player P with deadweight loss λ|T_z|; principal has inequity-averse social preferences with parameter α and cares about ex post vs ex ante fairness weighted by δ; equilibrium defined as Bayes–Nash in (π,T).",Summarizes the core structure of the analytical game as described in Section 2.1.,0.98
10.3982_te2063.md,Numerical example – Negative externality environment (γ = −1/2),"Section labeled “An example” specifies a discrete environment with γ = −1/2, action-dependent payoff distributions and explicit numbers to illustrate punishment-without-guilt under the ex ante optimal contract.",0.96,"Action a ∈ {C,D}; parameter γ = −1/2 (negative externalities); outcome z ∈ {−1,1} with Pr(z=−1|C)=1/4, Pr(z=1|C)=3/4, Pr(z=−1|D)=3/4, Pr(z=1|D)=1/4; social preference parameters δ = 0, α ≥ 1; transfer cost λ ∈ (0,2).",These are the explicit values and ranges given in the worked example.,1,False,"This is a numerical illustration of the analytical model, not based on data from human subjects.",1,False,No experimental manipulation with participants; it is a parameterized theoretical example.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation.,1,False,Outcomes and equilibria are derived analytically; no Monte Carlo or agent-based simulation is described.,1,True,The example computes expected payoffs and optimal transfers symbolically from the model.,1,3,"Same player set as general model: agent A, passive player P, principal.",1,1,The example is a one-shot game with a single realization of z.,1,1,"Agent chooses between two discrete actions C and D, not partial contributions; contribution-rate concept is not used, but schema requires a value, so set to 1 with this caveat.",0.4,N/A,No endowment and contribution structure; only binary actions affecting payoffs.,1,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; MPCR is not defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication in this analytical example.,1,0,Only a single period with no history; no summaries of others are relevant.,1,N/A,Analytical model; no identities or visibility manipulation.,1,N/A,"As above, identity of rewarder/punisher not modeled.",1,0,Single-stage example; round count not shown or relevant.,1,1,Positive transfers T_z>0 from A to P are allowed and are interpreted as punishment; lemma notes T*(z=-1)>0 in this negative externality example.,1,N/R,Transfer cost parameter λ is specified but not as a discrete ‘cost per punishment point’; exact per-unit mapping is not given numerically.,0.5,N/R,"Payoffs are u_A^T = u_A − T − λT^+, u_P^T = u_P + T − λT^−; no simple linear punishment technology parameter is isolated.",0.6,1,"Negative transfers T_z<0 (P to A) are allowed in the general transfer space; though this example focuses on punishment, the mechanism includes rewards.",0.8,N/R,Same transfer cost λ applies to all transfers; no per-unit reward cost parameter.,0.5,N/R,No separate technology parameter for rewards beyond the transfer equation.,0.6,N/R,"Agent’s and passive player’s payoffs are defined as u_A=−[z+γ], u_P=5[z+γ]; no initial token endowment is stated.",0.9,N/R,Outcome of interest is whether C or D is implemented and whether punishment occurs; no contribution rate is defined.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions are made; binary action only.,1,N/R,"While expectations of total surplus are discussed qualitatively, no explicit scalar efficiency index (0–1) is calculated for this specific parameter set.",0.6,N/R,"Expected payoffs are partially given (e.g., E[u_A|D]=1, E[u_P|D]=−5), but the paper does not summarize a single group payoff metric for this example as a main DV.",0.7,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,This is a parameterized analytical illustration without human subjects.,1,"Discrete version of the general game: z ∈ {−1,1}. Payoffs: u_A = −[z+γ], u_P = 5[z+γ], with γ = −1/2 in this negative externality environment. Action C increases probability of z=1 (Pr(z=1|C)=3/4) compared to D (Pr(z=1|D)=1/4). Principal’s preferences: δ=0 (cares only about ex ante fairness), α≥1, transfer cost λ∈(0,2). Ex ante optimal contract implements C and, when γ=−1/2, sets T*(z=−1)>0, illustrating punishment on the equilibrium path even though C is taken with probability 1.",Summarizes the illustrated structure and numerical parameters of the example as described in the ‘An example’ section.,1
10.3982_te2063.md,Numerical example – Positive externality environment (γ = 1/2),"Later in the example section, the authors set γ = 1/2 to create a positive externality environment and compute expected payoffs and transfer schemes under intent-based justice.",0.96,"Action a ∈ {C,D}; parameter γ = 1/2 (positive externalities); same z ∈ {−1,1} with Pr(z=−1|C)=1/4, Pr(z=1|C)=3/4, Pr(z=−1|D)=3/4, Pr(z=1|D)=1/4; δ = 0, α ≥ 1, λ ∈ (0,2).","The text explicitly reuses the same z distribution and varies γ to +1/2 to flip to positive externalities, maintaining δ, α, λ ranges.",1,False,Again a purely theoretical numerical illustration with no data.,1,False,No participant-level manipulation.,1,False,No laboratory setup.,1,False,Results are derived analytically from the model equations.,1,True,Closed-form expectations and incentives are computed for this parameterization.,1,3,"Same trio of players (A, P, principal) as in the general model and earlier example.",1,1,Single-shot realization of z and transfer choice.,1,1,"Binary action choice (C or D), not continuous contributions; field is only loosely analogous here.",0.4,N/A,No endowment-contribution structure.,1,N/R,No linear public goods technology given.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0,No multi-round feedback.,1,N/A,Identity not modeled.,1,N/A,Identity not modeled.,1,0,Single-stage interaction.,1,1,Positive transfers T_z>0 from A to P are feasible in the transfer space; punitive transfers are discussed generally under intent-based justice.,0.9,N/R,"Cost specified via λ, but no discrete per-unit punishment cost.",0.5,N/R,Transfer equations incorporate punishment; no separate technology parameter.,0.6,1,"In this positive externality example, optimal transfers to sustain C involve rewards: e.g., T(z=1) = −8/(2+λ) from P to A.",1,N/R,"Again only λ|T| is given, not a per-unit reward cost.",0.6,N/R,Rewards operate through T_z directly; no separate multiplier is stated.,0.6,N/R,"Payoffs are defined as u_A = −[z+γ], u_P = 5[z+γ]; no explicit endowment.",0.9,N/R,"DV is whether C can be sustained as equilibrium (π(C)=1) under intent-based justice, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,"The example shows that with γ=1/2, under suitable transfers, E[u_A^T|C] > E[u_A^T|D], but it does not provide a normalized efficiency number.",0.6,N/R,"Expected payoffs like E[u_A|C]=−1 and E[u_P|C]=5 are given, but not summarized as a separate DV.",0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely analytical numerical example.,1,"Same discrete environment as the negative externality example but with γ = 1/2, so that D is the status quo action and C creates a positive externality (E[u_A|C]=−1, E[u_P|C]=5; E[u_A|D]=0, E[u_P|D]=0). Under intent-based justice with δ=0, α≥1, λ∈(0,2), for π(C) close to 1 the principal sets transfers T(z=−1)=0 and T(z=1)=−8/(2+λ), which makes E[u_A^T|C]=−1+6/(2+λ) and E[u_A^T|D]=2/(2+λ); since λ<2, this yields E[u_A^T|C]>E[u_A^T|D], so a pure-strategy equilibrium with π(C)=1 exists.",Directly summarizes the described calculations for the γ = 1/2 case in the manuscript.,1
