10.1371_journal.pone.0034051.md,Mathematical model – first-order punishment only,"Condition described as games with only first order punishment (peer punishment allowed, no second-order pool punishment), linked to Figure 1A and corresponding analytical conditions.",0.88,Presence of first-order peer punishment only; coverage coefficient Cc varied across full range; second-order punishment not allowed.,Text contrasts games with only first order punishment versus games with second order punishment (Figure 1A vs 1B) and studies how varying Cc changes outcomes.,0.86,False,No human participants; purely theoretical and computational modeling of strategies on a lattice.,1,False,"Work consists of mathematical analysis and simulations, not controlled experiments with subjects.",1,False,No laboratory or field setting; only models and simulations.,1,True,"They run computational simulations of populations on a spatial lattice (e.g., Figure 2, Figure 3).",0.96,True,"They derive payoff equations, invasion conditions, and phase transition thresholds analytically.",0.99,0,"Denoted as M players on a lattice, but specific numeric value of M not reported.",0.4,0,Iterated population dynamics described but no discrete finite number of rounds specified.,0.2,1,Each player chooses whether to contribute amount c or not; no indication of multiple contribution levels beyond full c vs 0.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects and no initial endowment structure; model uses direct cost c rather than an endowment allocation.,1,N/R,They define contribution c multiplied by factor r and divided among players; MPCR = r/(N-1) but N and r not numerically specified so exact value cannot be given.,0.5,N/A,No human interaction or communication; model is agent-based and analytical.,1,0,Agents update via payoffs in the model; no interface or information display structure like summaries described.,0.8,0,Punishment is modeled as payoffs; no notion of identity revelation is discussed.,0.9,0,Rewards are only mentioned as possible future extensions; no reward mechanism in this condition.,0.98,0,No user interface or explicit information about number of rounds; infinite or long-run dynamics in a model.,0.95,1,Condition explicitly involves peer punishment with cost γ and fine g.,1,N/R,"Peer punishment cost per punished defector is γ, but no numeric value for γ is given.",0.6,N/R,"Peer punishment fine per defector is g, but no numeric value for g is reported.",0.6,0,Only punishment mechanisms (peer and pool) are modeled; rewards appear only as a suggestion for future work.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,Model uses contribution cost c but does not define a finite endowment per round.,0.3,N/R,"Outcomes focus on long-term frequencies of strategies (cooperators, defectors, punishers, non-participants), not explicit normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,"Contribution level is fixed at c for cooperators, but no numeric contributions are reported as results.",0.4,N/R,They discuss maintenance of the commons and phase transitions but do not provide explicit efficiency ratios relative to theoretical maximum.,0.3,N/R,"Payoff formulas are given symbolically, but no aggregate numeric group payoff outcomes are reported.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical and computational work.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is based on mathematical modeling and agent-based simulations only.,1,"Spatial public goods game on square lattice (torus) with M players; group size N drawn from within interaction range pi; strategies include cooperators, defectors, non-participants, peer punishers; voluntary participation with outside option sigma; evolutionary imitation dynamics with selection strength s and mutation rate mu; coverage coefficient Cc summarizes relation of punishment range pp to interaction range pi and modulates effective punishment parameters.",Summarizes structural aspects of the public goods game and evolutionary process described in Materials and Methods and Results for the first-order punishment case.,0.95
10.1371_journal.pone.0034051.md,"Mathematical model – second-order punishment allowed (pool punishment, non-global games)","Condition described as games with second order punishment (pool punishment with potential second-order sanctions), linked to Figure 1B, Figure 2C–E, and Figure 3B.",0.9,Presence of second-order pool punishment in addition to peer punishment and other strategies; coverage coefficient Cc varied across full range.,They analyze cases where pool punishers can engage in second-order punishment and contrast zones where peer vs pool punishment dominate depending on Cc.,0.88,False,No human subjects; model is theoretical and simulation-based.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; only modeling.,1,False,Work is not conducted in a lab with participants.,1,True,"They run computational simulations of populations including pool punishers on a spatial lattice (e.g., Figure 2E, Figure 3B).",0.97,True,They derive analytical conditions (inequalities (2)–(4)) for invasion and maintenance under second-order punishment.,0.99,0,"Number of players denoted by M, but no concrete numeric value is given.",0.4,0,Model studies long-run evolutionary dynamics; no explicit finite number of game rounds.,0.2,1,Players either contribute full amount c or 0; no graded contribution levels indicated.,0.7,N/A,No endowments for human subjects; model uses a cost c but not an initial allocation.,1,N/R,"MPCR would equal r/(N-1) per standard PGG, but r and N have no numeric values.",0.5,N/A,No humans and no communication mechanism.,1,0,No interface with summaries; agents only observe payoffs for imitation.,0.8,0,Identities of punishing agents are not modeled as information; only payoffs matter.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism implemented; only punishment.,0.98,0,No displayed round information in a non-human simulation.,0.95,1,"Condition explicitly includes both peer and pool punishment, with second-order punishment possible via institutions.",1,N/R,Peer punishment cost γ and pool punishment contribution β per round are defined symbolically but without numeric values.,0.6,N/R,"Pool punishment fine per punisher is b, peer punishment fine is g, but no numeric values reported.",0.6,0,Rewards are not implemented; they are mentioned as potential future extensions.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/A,No rewards in the implemented model.,1,N/R,Players pay costs c and β but no explicit finite endowment per round is given.,0.3,N/R,"Results are expressed in terms of long-term frequencies of strategy types and maintenance of commons, not normalized contribution fractions.",0.3,N/R,"Cooperators and punishers contribute c, but no numeric contributions or averages are reported as outcome measures.",0.4,N/R,"They qualitatively discuss protection and maintenance of the commons, but do not quantify efficiency as payoff relative to a maximum.",0.3,N/R,Payoff functions are given but no specific aggregate payoffs are presented as results.,0.4,N/A,No human participants in the simulations or analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is implemented solely via mathematical and computational methods.,1,"Spatial public goods game on square lattice (torus) with M players; group of size N formed from players within interaction range pi; strategies include cooperators, defectors, non-participants, peer punishers, and pool punishers; pool punishers contribute β each round to a punishment pool that fines defectors by b per pool punisher; second-order punishment of non-punishing cooperators by pool punishers is possible; optional participation with outside payoff σ; evolutionary imitation dynamics with selection strength s and rare mutation μ; coverage coefficient Cc captures fraction of potential punishment pairs within punishment range pp and scales effective punishment parameters b, β, g, γ; phase transitions in dominant strategies as Cc varies, including non-global games where pi is finite (Figure 3B).","Summarizes the extended game with pool punishment and second-order effects, including spatial and evolutionary features, as applied in non-global games.",0.96
10.1098_rspb.2021.2174.md,"GER_NAT_NS (Germany, National, No Sanctions)","Directly taken from table 1: treatment name GER_NAT_NS described as National Germany, no sanctions, nationality revealed.",1,"Sanction availability: No sanctions (NS); Interaction level: National; Nationality info: Revealed; Country composition: 6 Germans (3 in each of two German cities); Game: collective-risk social dilemma with threshold T=2100, no sanction stage.",Independent variables are treatment factors varied across conditions as summarized in table 1 and methods section.,1,True,Human participants (university students) played the game.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab-style economic experiment with pre-specified treatments.,1,True,"Conducted with university students in experimental sessions via Internet in real time, typical lab experiment setting, not natural field.",0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results from human play.,1,False,"Paper includes theoretical benchmarks but this treatment is empirical, not purely analytical.",1,6,“Groups of six participants interacted in the CRSD…”.,1,10,“Participants interacted over 10 periods with the same partners…”.,1,1,"Participants could contribute “up to 50 tokens”, implying any amount from 0–50 rather than binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Each participant was endowed with 60 tokens; they choose contributions up to 50, so all endowment starts in private account before decision.",0.95,N/R,Game is a collective-risk threshold game where contributions reduce catastrophe probability; no linear per-capita return (MPCR) is specified.,0,0,Interaction was via Internet with information screens; no mention of any communication or chat being allowed.,0.9,1,"“Participants received information on each of the other group members' contributions in all the previous rounds, as well as the current PLA determined by total contributions.”",1,0,"Interactions were anonymous and only numeric labels (1–6) were used; no indication that real identities were revealed. IDs (1–6) were already known before any punishment stage, but there is no punishment in NS treatments; thus, showing punishment target identity is N/A here but coded as 0 per schema.",0.7,0,No reward mechanism is present in any treatment.,1,1,"Standard repeated-game protocols display period number; 10 fixed periods were known ex ante; while not explicitly stated, this is typical and necessary. Treated as shown.",0.6,0,This is a No Sanctions (NS) treatment; only contribution stage exists.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in NS treatments.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in NS treatments.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism is described anywhere in the paper.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,60,“Each participant was endowed with 60 tokens in each period.”,1,N/R,Paper reports PLA and some descriptive comparisons; does not provide an overall average fraction of endowment contributed per treatment.,0,N/R,Exact mean token contributions per treatment are not given in the main text.,0,N/R,"They discuss payoffs and note that payoffs were higher in NS than S in supplement, but no normalized efficiency measure per treatment is reported.",0,N/R,No raw average payoffs per treatment are provided in the excerpt.,0,Germany,Table 1: GER_NAT_NS is National Germany; all 6 players in each group are Germans.,1,N/R,Demographic details are said to be in electronic supplementary material S1.1 and S1.2; not in provided text.,0,N/R,"Gender composition only in supplementary material, not given here.",0,University students,Methods: “three of whom were university students in one city and three in another”; participants are described as university students.,0.9,On site,"Participants were university students in specific cities using lab-style internet terminals; not an online panel done remotely, nor a field study.",0.9,"Collective-risk social dilemma with catastrophe probability. Threshold T=2100 total tokens over 10 periods (group of 6) to achieve certain safety (PLA=1). Total contribution C relative to T determines probability P of avoiding 75% loss to each player's private account: P = min{C/T, 1}. Each period endowment: 60 tokens, max 50 to collective fund, remaining (including 10 automatically private) kept in private account. Loss, if triggered at end of period 10 when C<T, reduces private accounts to 25%.","Summarized from the description of CRSD, threshold, PLA formula, loss magnitude, and endowment details.",1
10.1098_rspb.2021.2174.md,"GER_NAT_S (Germany, National, Sanctions)","Directly from table 1: GER_NAT_S, National Germany, sanctions yes, nationality revealed.",1,Sanction availability: Sanctions possible (S); Interaction level: National; Nationality info: Revealed; Country composition: 6 Germans; Game: CRSD with threshold T=2100 plus second-stage sanctioning each period using remaining 10 tokens.,"Treatment factors are sanction availability, level of interaction, and information about nationality, as laid out in table 1 and methods.",1,True,Human subjects experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized treatment in an economic experiment.,1,True,University students participated in lab-style sessions via Internet within sites.,0.9,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,"This row refers to empirical treatment, not the theoretical benchmarks.",1,6,Groups of six participants interacted in the CRSD.,1,10,Ten periods of interaction are specified.,1,1,Contributions to the collective fund can be any amount up to 50 tokens.,1,0,All 60 tokens are initially in the private account; up to 50 can be reallocated each period.,0.95,N/R,"No per-capita linear return is given, only threshold-probability relation.",0,0,No mention of chat or communication beyond structured feedback.,0.9,1,"Participants receive detailed information on each group member's contributions in all previous rounds, and in S treatments, also sanctions assigned by each member to others.",1,1,"In S treatments, participants “received information on the sanctions assigned by a group member to any other group member.” Since numeric labels 1–6 identify members and are known, this effectively reveals which ID punished which ID.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Ten periods with fixed horizon; standard to display period number; not explicitly stated but implied.,0.6,1,“The CRSD in the S treatments took place in two stages… each group member could use the remaining 10 tokens … to reduce other group members' private accounts.”,1,N/R,"They state that sanctioning uses remaining 10 tokens and that tokens spent are deducted from the punisher’s private account, but the exact mapping between tokens spent and cost per unit is not specified in the excerpt; sanctioning cost structure is said to be in supplementary table S6.",0.1,N/R,"Only qualitative description: target loses more than proportionally with more punishers; exact magnitude per coin is in supplementary table S6, not provided here.",0.1,0,No rewarding phase exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,60,Each participant endowed with 60 tokens per period.,1,N/R,Exact normalized contributions per treatment are not reported in main text.,0,N/R,No mean token contributions by treatment provided.,0,N/R,They state payoffs were significantly higher in NS than S treatments in supplements but give no efficiency statistic by treatment.,0,N/R,Raw payoff data per treatment not in provided text.,0,Germany,GER_NAT_S is National Germany treatment; all players are Germans.,1,N/R,Details in supplementary material only.,0,N/R,Not reported in main text.,0,University students,Described as university students in participating cities.,0.9,On site,Standard lab sessions with students in specific universities interacting via Internet in real time.,0.9,"Same CRSD as NS, with additional stage: in each of the 10 periods, after contribution stage, each participant can spend up to their remaining 10 tokens to impose sanctions on any other group member. Tokens spent on sanctioning are deducted from the punisher’s private account. The punished player’s private account is reduced by an amount that increases more than proportionally in the total tokens spent on sanctioning them by others. Final payoff equals private account result from contributions and risk, minus any sanctioning costs and plus/minus sanctions received.",Summarizes sanctioning stage description and its integration with CRSD.,1
