10.1098_rspb.2021.2174.md,"INT_Blind_NS (International, Blind, No Sanctions)","Directly from table 1: INT_Blind_NS, International, no sanctions, nationality not revealed.",1,"Sanction availability: No sanctions (NS); Interaction level: International; Nationality info: Blind (not revealed); Country composition: mixed groups of 6 (3 Germans in one city, 3 Russians in another) but participants believe they are likely same-country; Game: CRSD with T=2100 and no sanction stage.","International Blind NS defined in methods: one city in Germany, one in Russia; in Blind, nationality of other city not revealed and believed same-country by most.",1,True,Human participants from Germany and Russia.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with multiple treatments including this one.,1,True,Same lab-like environment; participants in university labs connect via Internet.,0.9,False,No agent-based simulations.,1,False,This is empirical treatment.,1,6,Groups of six participants.,1,10,Ten periods repeated interaction.,1,1,Contribution choice is continuous up to 50 tokens.,1,0,All 60 tokens initially in private account every period.,0.95,N/R,"CRSD with probability of catastrophe, not linear MPCR.",0,0,No communication between Germans and Russians beyond experimental feedback.,0.9,1,Participants see contribution histories of all group members and PLA.,1,0,"No sanctions in NS, so punishment IDs not relevant.",1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Fixed 10 periods known; likely displayed.,0.6,0,INT_Blind_NS is a No Sanctions treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,Rewards are never available.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,60,Same 60-token endowment each period in all treatments.,1,N/R,PLA comparisons between Blind and Open are given but not explicit average contributions normalized by endowment.,0,N/R,No numeric mean contribution per treatment in provided text.,0,N/R,No efficiency measure for this specific treatment is reported.,0,N/R,Raw payoffs per treatment not given.,0,Germany and Russia,International treatments have 3 German and 3 Russian participants per group; this treatment pools them under Blind information.,1,N/R,Age distribution only in supplementary materials S1.1–S1.2.,0,N/R,Gender details only mentioned as balanced but not specified.,0,University students,All participants from participating universities.,0.9,On site,"Participants in labs in German and Russian cities, connected via Internet.",0.9,"International CRSD between German and Russian students; groups of six with 3 per city. In Blind condition, participants are not told that the other city is in another country, and ex-post questionnaires confirm most believed partners were domestic. Game mechanics identical to other NS CRSD treatments: 60 tokens, up to 50 to collective fund, T=2100 threshold for certain safety, PLA based on C/T, potential 75% loss.",Summarizes specific international-blind manipulation plus core CRSD features.,1
10.1098_rspb.2021.2174.md,"INT_Blind_S (International, Blind, Sanctions)","Directly from table 1: INT_Blind_S, International, sanctions yes, nationality not revealed.",1,Sanction availability: Sanctions possible (S); Interaction level: International; Nationality info: Blind (not revealed); Country composition: mixed groups of 3 Germans and 3 Russians; Game: CRSD with T=2100 plus sanctioning stage each period.,From treatment label and explanation of Blind international sanctions condition.,1,True,Human subjects from two countries.,1,True,Part of the controlled multi-treatment experimental design.,1,True,University lab-type sessions in Germany and Russia.,0.9,False,Results derived from human behavior.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical treatment.,1,6,Groups of six with 3 per city.,1,10,Ten interaction periods.,1,1,Participants choose contribution level between 0 and 50.,1,0,Endowments start fully private.,0.95,N/R,No MPCR parameter given.,0,0,No communication capability described.,0.9,1,"Contribution histories and, in S treatments, sanctioning actions from each member are shown.",1,1,"Information on which numbered participant sanctioned which other numbered participant is provided, as in other S treatments.",0.9,0,No rewards.,1,1,Fixed horizon of 10 periods; standard to display to subjects.,0.6,1,Sanctioning stage exists as part of S treatments.,1,N/R,Sanctioning cost schedule only referenced as being in supplementary table S6.,0.1,N/R,Only qualitative statement that losses to sanctioned increase more than proportionally.,0.1,0,No reward phase defined.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,60,Same per-period endowment as other treatments.,1,N/R,"While sanction effectiveness is analyzed, contribution levels by treatment as normalized share are not provided in this excerpt.",0,N/R,Exact average contributions not reported.,0,N/R,Efficiency measure per treatment not included.,0,N/R,No explicit payoff data per treatment.,0,Germany and Russia,International interaction with 3 Germans and 3 Russians in each group.,1,N/R,Age details in supplementary material.,0,N/R,Not reported in main text.,0,University students,Participants drawn from universities in both countries.,0.9,On site,Lab-style experiment in universities with internet connection between labs.,0.9,"International CRSD identical to other S treatments: after contribution stage, sanctioning stage using remaining 10 tokens. In Blind condition, nationality of other city not revealed; ex-post data show most believed partners to be domestic. Sanctions are observable and sanc-tioned losses grow more than proportionally with total sanctions received.",Combines general CRSD+sanctions structure with Blind manipulation in international setting.,1
10.1098_rspb.2021.2174.md,"INT_Open_NS (International, Open, No Sanctions)","From table 1: INT_Open_NS, International, no sanctions, nationality revealed.",1,"Sanction availability: No sanctions (NS); Interaction level: International; Nationality info: Open (participants know other city is in other country); Country composition: 3 Germans, 3 Russians per group; Game: CRSD with T=2100, no sanction stage.",Methods describe International Open treatments where German and Russian participants are aware the other city is in the other country.,1,True,Human-subject international experiment.,1,True,Controlled design with manipulated information on nationality and sanction availability.,1,True,Conducted at university labs in Germany and Russia.,0.9,False,No computer-only simulations.,1,False,"Empirical treatment, not analytical only.",1,6,Groups of six with 3 per city.,1,10,Ten periods repeated.,1,1,Contribution decision any amount up to 50 tokens.,1,0,Endowment entirely private before allocation.,0.95,N/R,"Game is not linear public good, so MPCR undefined in text.",0,0,No communication channel indicated; participants only see contributions and PLA.,0.9,1,Feedback includes histories of other members' contributions and PLA.,1,0,No sanctioning in NS treatments.,1,0,No reward option.,1,1,Finite 10-period game; likely communicated.,0.6,0,INT_Open_NS is decribed as no sanctions treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,60,CRSD endowment invariant across treatments.,1,N/R,They report mean PLA in this treatment (~0.65) but not normalized contributions.,0.2,N/R,No mean token contributions given for this treatment.,0,N/R,No efficiency computation provided for specific treatments.,0,N/R,Treatment-level payoffs not given.,0,Germany and Russia,International groups with 3 Germans and 3 Russians.,1,N/R,Age information only in supplement.,0,N/R,Not specified here.,0,University students,Participants described as students from universities in both countries.,0.9,On site,Laboratory sessions at respective universities.,0.9,"International Open CRSD: Germans and Russians know that the other sub-group is in the other country, though exact location not disclosed. Players also know that IDs 1–3 are in one city and 4–6 in the other, making partner location inferable. Game mechanics: 10 periods, 60-token endowment, up to 50 tokens per period to collective fund, T=2100 threshold over 10 periods, PLA = min{C/T,1}, 75% loss if catastrophe occurs.",Summarizes Open international information structure combined with core CRSD.,1
10.1098_rspb.2021.2174.md,"INT_Open_S (International, Open, Sanctions)","From table 1: INT_Open_S, International, sanctions yes, nationality revealed.",1,"Sanction availability: Sanctions possible (S); Interaction level: International; Nationality info: Open (participants know other city is in other country); Country composition: 3 Germans, 3 Russians; Game: CRSD (T=2100) plus sanctioning stage each period.",Described as International Open S treatment: sanctions available and nationality revealed.,1,True,Human participants from Germany and Russia.,1,True,Part of the designed experimental treatments.,1,True,Conducted in on-site lab environments.,0.9,False,No computational simulations.,1,False,"This row represents empirical data, not theoretical solution.",1,6,"Six-player groups, 3 per city.",1,10,Ten periods of repeated interaction.,1,1,Contribute any amount up to 50 tokens.,1,0,All 60 tokens are private before contribution.,0.95,N/R,"No linear MPCR is specified, only probability-threshold relation.",0,0,No mention of communication; only structured feedback.,0.9,1,"Participants see contributions of each member in all previous rounds and, in S treatments, information on sanctions assigned between each pair.",1,1,"Sanctions assigned between labeled participants (1–6) are displayed, revealing punisher and punished IDs.",0.9,0,Reward mechanism absent.,1,1,Fixed 10-period horizon; typically shown to participants.,0.6,1,This is an S (sanctions) treatment with explicit sanctioning stage.,1,N/R,Exact cost per token of punishment unit not in excerpt; schedule is in supplementary table S6.,0.1,N/R,Only described qualitatively as more-than-proportional loss to sanctioned player with aggregate sanctions.,0.1,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,60,Same 60-token endowment per period as other treatments.,1,N/R,"They report PLA and comparisons, but not normalized contributions as fraction of endowment in text.",0,N/R,No average token contributions are reported numerically.,0,N/R,"Discussion mentions PLA and that many S groups exceed cooperative solution, and that payoffs are lower under S, but no explicit efficiency value.",0.1,N/R,No explicit average payoffs per treatment in the excerpt.,0,Germany and Russia,Each international group is composed of Germans and Russians.,1,N/R,Not in provided text.,0,N/R,Not given.,0,University students,Participants recruited from universities in both countries.,0.9,On site,Lab-like sessions in universities with international Internet-linked groups.,0.9,"Same CRSD and sanctioning system as other S treatments, but with explicit knowledge that partners are in the other country. They find that mean PLA in INT_Open_S is significantly higher than in INT_Open_NS and similar to German National S, indicating sanctions plus internationalization raise cooperation, especially among Russian participants whose contributions converge to Germans’ over time.",Summarizes distinguishing Open international plus main behavioral finding for this treatment.,1
10.1037_npe0000011.md,Baseline treatment (B),"Paper defines three treatments: B (baseline), SRS, and MRS; this row is the baseline condition.",1,Reward/sanction option: none (no second-stage rewards or sanctions),Only dimension varied across treatments is presence/type of second-stage reward/sanction technology.,1,True,Human student subjects in lab experiment at Osaka University.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with experimental treatments.,1,True,Conducted in lab at Osaka University with computers and z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations described.,1,False,"Study reports experimental results, not purely analytical modeling.",1,2,"""There are two subjects, a and b"" and ""There were 10 pairs of subjects"".",1,2,Two rounds conducted in each treatment; subjects informed about second round only after first round finished.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer investment between 0 and 20; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Standard VCM: entire endowment is initially private; subjects decide how much to invest in public good.,1,0.7,"Public-good payoff parameter explicitly stated as a = 0.7, the marginal per capita return.",1,0,Communication among subjects prohibited; no messaging in B.,1,0,"Only own and partner’s investment and own payoff shown; “Neither the other's realized payoff nor the outcomes in pairs, other than his or her own, are shown.”",1,0,"No reward/sanction stage exists in B, hence no punishment identities displayed; coded 0 as not shown.",1,0,"No rewards in B, so no identities shown.",1,0,Design relies on surprise second round; implies total number of rounds not shown in advance.,0.9,0,Baseline explicitly lacks any reward or sanction stage.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment option in this treatment.,1,0,Baseline lacks any reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward option in this treatment.,1,20,Each subject has endowment w_i = 20 units of private good.,1,N/R,Only average contribution levels are discussed qualitatively; no numeric values reported to compute normalized rate.,0.2,N/R,Figure 1 values not numerically given in text; only relative comparisons reported.,0.2,N/R,No total group payoff or efficiency ratios reported.,0.1,N/R,Only examples and average monetary earnings across all treatments reported; not separated by treatment.,0.1,Japan,Experiment conducted at Osaka University in Japan with campus-wide recruitment.,1,N/R,Paper describes students but gives no age statistics.,0.2,N/R,No information on gender composition provided.,0,University students (various majors),Described as student subjects with diverse backgrounds including fields other than economics.,0.9,On site,Lab sessions at Osaka University using computers and experimenter reading instructions.,1,"Two-player linear public-good (VCM) with endowment 20, continuous integer contributions 0–20, payoff u_i = x_i + 0.7*y, payoffs scaled by 100 in table; 21x21 game-theoretic payoff matrix shown to subjects; partners fixed across 2 rounds; strangers across pairs; anonymity within session.",Summarizes core structural features explicitly described in the method and design sections.,1
10.1037_npe0000011.md,Social rewards and sanctions treatment (SRS),Paper defines treatment with free-form social messages as SRS.,1,Reward/sanction option: costless social messages with subjective evaluation (positive/neutral/negative) after first-stage contributions,Second-stage mechanism is free-form written message evaluating partner’s contribution; this is only difference vs B.,1,True,Human student subjects in lab experiment at Osaka University.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with between-treatment manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in a physical lab with computers and experimenter.,1,False,No simulation-only study.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,2,Same VCM structure as B: pairs of two subjects.,1,2,Two rounds in each treatment; same temporal structure as B.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer investment between 0 and 20.,1,0,Same VCM; endowment starts fully as private good.,1,0.7,"Same payoff function u_i(x_i, y) = x_i + 0.7*y as in baseline.",1,1,Treatment explicitly allows free-form written messages evaluating partner.,1,0,"As in B, only own/partner investment and own payoff are revealed; no additional summaries described.",0.9,1,"Pairs are one-to-one; each subject knows which partner sent the message, so identity of ‘sanctioner’ is revealed.",0.9,1,Same as for sanctions: sender is identifiable within the fixed pair.,0.9,0,"Same overall design: second round not announced until first finishes, so number of rounds not shown ex ante.",0.9,1,Negative social messages function as social sanctions; paper analyzes them as sanctions.,1,0,Social messages incur no monetary cost to sender: “They incur no cost.”,1,1,No direct monetary transformation; use 1 as neutral magnitude since tech not defined in units; still allows recording that it exists.,0.2,1,Positive social messages are social rewards; explicitly discussed as such.,1,0,Messages are costless to send.,1,1,No explicit payoff conversion; record a neutral 1 to indicate existence despite non-monetary nature.,0.2,20,Same basic VCM as B; each subject endowed with 20 units.,1,N/R,Only relative contribution levels and significance tests vs B and MRS are given; no numeric amounts to normalize.,0.2,N/R,Average contributions by round shown only in a figure without numeric values.,0.2,N/R,No report of aggregate payoffs or efficiency ratios by treatment.,0.1,N/R,Only individual earnings summarized over all treatments; not separated for SRS.,0.1,Japan,Same subject pool and location as B.,1,N/R,No age data reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,University students (various majors),Same recruited student pool as in other treatments.,0.9,On site,Conducted in lab sessions at Osaka University.,1,"Same two-player linear VCM as B, followed by a social-message stage: after seeing both investments and own payoff, each subject writes a free-form text message evaluating partner’s contribution; messages are displayed to the partner, who then classifies the message as positive, neutral, or negative. Partners are fixed across the 2 rounds; social messages only after round 1 (used to study impact on round-2 contributions).",Summarizes the additional social-reward/sanction stage relative to baseline.,1
10.1037_npe0000011.md,Monetary rewards and sanctions treatment (MRS),Paper defines treatment with monetary point rewards/sanctions as MRS.,1,"Reward/sanction option: costly monetary points in [-10,10] transferred after first-stage contributions (1:1 cost-benefit)",Second stage allows subjects to assign monetary points (positive or negative) to partner; this is treatment manipulation.,1,True,Uses human student subjects in a laboratory experimental setting.,1,True,Controlled experimental design with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted on-site at Osaka University lab using computers.,1,False,No description of simulation-only methods.,1,False,Study is primarily empirical despite some game-theoretic reasoning.,1,2,Same pairwise VCM as in other treatments.,1,2,Two rounds in each treatment; MRS not an exception.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer investment between 0 and 20.,1,0,Same basic VCM with entire endowment initially private.,1,0.7,Same underlying public-good technology used in all treatments.,1,0,No mention of any verbal or written communication in MRS; only numerical point choices.,1,0,Subjects see partner’s assigned points and own final payoff; no broader summaries of others’ outcomes.,0.9,1,"Each subject observes “the point number awarded by his or her partner”; within a fixed pair, this identifies the punisher/rewarder.",0.9,1,Same reasoning as for sanctions; partner as unique source of points.,0.9,0,Same surprise-second-round structure as B and SRS; number of rounds not announced initially.,0.9,1,"Negative points (in [-10,0)) reduce partner’s payoff and own payoff; described as monetary sanctions.",1,1,One negative point reduces own payoff by 1 experimental currency unit.,1,1,Each negative point reduces partner’s payoff by 1; “cost-benefit performance ... is one-to-one.”,1,1,Positive points increase partner’s payoff and reduce own payoff; described as monetary rewards.,1,1,Each positive point reduces own payoff by 1.,1,1,Each positive point increases partner’s payoff by 1 (one-to-one transfers).,1,20,Same first-stage VCM with endowment 20 units.,1,N/R,"Paper reports only inferential tests and qualitative description of contribution levels, not numeric averages.",0.2,N/R,Average contributions in MRS are depicted in a figure without numbers and not tabulated in text.,0.2,N/R,No direct or computable efficiency measures provided for MRS.,0.1,N/R,Final payoffs by treatment not numerically reported; only overall average and min/max across all subjects.,0.1,Japan,"Same lab, same university, same recruitment pool.",1,N/R,No age stats reported.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not mentioned.,0,University students (various majors),Same recruited student population as for other treatments.,0.9,On site,Same on-site lab environment at Osaka University.,1,"Same two-player linear VCM as B, followed by a monetary reward/sanction stage: after seeing stage-1 results, each subject chooses an integer point p_i in [-10,10]; own final payoff is u_i - |p_i| + p_j, where u_i is first-stage payoff and p_j is partner’s chosen points. Points are worth one unit of endowment from the first-stage game; positive p_i are rewards (zero-sum transfers), negative p_i are costly sanctions. Theoretical subgame-perfect equilibrium is y_i = 0, p_i = 0.",Summarizes details of the monetary point mechanism described in the design section.,1
