10.1371_journal.pone.0069871.md,"Ex-Pos (Exit, Positive Incentive)","Table 1 and instructions: Exit - Positive Incentives, Ex-Pos.",1,Exit option allowed; positive incentive (reward 5 MU to helpers); voluntary participation,Combines exit option with 5 MU bonus for helping.,1,True,Human lab treatment.,1,True,Part of experimental factorial design.,1,True,On-site lab experiment.,1,False,No simulation used.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,6,Groups of six.,1,10,10 treatment periods.,1,0,Binary helping choice (and exit decision).,1,0,Endowment in private account.,1,0.4,Helping technology unchanged.,1,0,No communication.,1,1,Aggregate group result communicated.,0.9,0,No punishment exists.,1,0,Rewards administered centrally; identities of helpers not revealed.,0.8,1,Instructions state the continuation for 10 more rounds.,1,0,No fines.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,Helpers receive 5 MU bonus.,1,5,Bonus size per help is 5 MU.,1,5,Each help action yields 5 MU directly to helper.,0.8,100,Treatment restarts with 100 MU endowment.,1,0.49,Table 2: helping proportion in Ex-Pos = 0.49.,1,N/R,Only proportion reported.,0,0.69,Text: Ex-Pos profit equals 69% of theoretical optimum (Fig. 3b).,0.9,1031.28,Table 2: final profit mean =171.88 MU; group payoff=171.88*6.,0.9,Italy,Same lab site.,1,"Mean 23.21, SD 2.70",Table 2: Participant age for Ex-Pos.,1,58% female,Table 2: Female proportion in Ex-Pos = 0.58.,1,University students (Faculty of Economics),Same recruitment.,1,On site,Lab experiment.,1,"Binary helping game with 10 MU cost giving 20 MU total benefit to other participants, plus a 5 MU bonus to helpers; each round, players can opt out, receiving neither cost nor benefit nor bonus; if only one participates, the round is skipped.",From Exit - Positive Incentives instructions and design description.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0077041.md,Analytical evolutionary model of public goods game with punishment,"The paper develops a closed-form evolutionary/utility model of a public good game with punishment, not a new lab experiment.",1,"Group size n, punishment efficiency r, return factor g, agents’ preference type (purely self-regarding vs. disadvantageous inequity averse)","Model outcomes are derived as functions of n, r, g and the assumed preference type.",1,False,No human subjects are used in the theoretical model itself.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; the paper analyzes equations and dynamics.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; it is a theoretical model.,1,False,This article presents analytical derivations; numerical simulations are said to be in a companion article and not detailed here.,0.8,True,"The core contribution is an analytical evolutionary/expected-utility model with closed-form expressions (e.g., k*, bifurcation k+).",1,4,Section 'Description of the empirical data set' and throughout model calibration they specify n=4.,1,1,"The modeled game is explicitly described as a 'standard one-shot-interaction public goods game with punishment'; evolutionary reproduction happens across generations, not repeated rounds within a game.",1,1,Contributions m_i are continuous nonnegative amounts of MUs; no all-or-nothing restriction is stated.,1,0,"Agents are endowed with MUs and decide how much to contribute; endowment starts in private account, not in the public good.",0.9,0.4,"They state g=1.6 and n=4 in the empirical section, giving MPCR=g/n=1.6/4=0.4, which is used as the benchmark for the model.",0.9,0,The theoretical model does not include any communication or signaling stage.,0.9,1,Model assumes agents observe others’ contributions and payoffs to decide punishment; conceptually equivalent to seeing outcome summaries.,0.8,1,"Punishment is modeled as directed from agent i to j based on pairwise contribution deviations, implying knowledge of who punished whom.",0.8,0,"There is no reward stage, only punishment.",1,0,Single-shot interaction; no information about remaining rounds is relevant or modeled.,1,1,Punishment is a central mechanism in the public goods game with punishment.,1,1,They define that each MU spent by the punisher imposes r MUs loss on the punished; cost per punishment unit is 1 MU.,1,3,They state punishment efficiency r≈3; each MU spent reduces the punished’s payoff by r MUs.,0.9,0,"No positive reward mechanism is discussed, only costly punishment.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,The evolutionary model itself does not fix a per-period endowment in MUs; endowment is only given for empirical experiments used for validation.,0.6,N/R,"The analytical results characterize stable strategies (e.g., m^a, m^b) symbolically but do not report a normalized contribution fraction like m/E in numeric form.",0.4,N/R,"Theoretical steady states m^a and m^b are expressed symbolically (e.g., c_fix/(g−1), median of beliefs) without numeric amounts.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio (actual/maximum group payoff) is numerically reported for the model.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs are analyzed functionally via fitness and P&L expressions but no single numeric payoff outcome is reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants are used in the analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This entry describes a non-empirical analytical model.,1,"One-shot public goods game with punishment; agents choose contribution m_i and punishment propensity k_i; payoffs: −m_i + (g/n)·sum(m_j) − punishment costs and damages; group size typically n=4, return factor g between 0 and n (empirically calibrated to g=1.6), punishment efficiency r>1 (empirically r≈3); evolutionary dynamics via selection, crossover, mutation over strategies [m,k]; two regimes separated by k+ = 1/(n·r − r − 1); optimal punishment for inequity-averse agents k_ieq* = 2/(n·(r−1)).",Summarizes the formal structure and key parameter relationships of the modeled game.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0077041.md,"Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002) and Fudenberg & Pathak (2010) pooled – Stranger treatment with punishment","Section 'Description of the empirical data set' explains that data from three independent lab public goods experiments with punishment [19–21] are pooled; all share the same core treatment structure (stranger, punishment). The paper does not separate results by these experiments, so they are represented as one empirical condition here.",0.7,Presence of costly punishment (enabled) in a repeated public goods game; stranger matching; observed vs unobserved punishment information in one study pooled together.,"Across all pooled data, punishment is available; groups are rematched (stranger); the observed/unobserved punishment feedback variants in [21] are reported as behaviorally similar and pooled.",0.8,True,The section 'Description of the empirical data set' describes three lab experiments with human subjects.,1,True,These are controlled laboratory public good games.,1,True,They are referred to as 'laboratory experiments' with standard public-good-with-punishment designs.,1,False,"The empirical data come from human-subject experiments, not from simulations.",1,False,"This row describes the empirical condition, not the analytical model.",1,4,All three experiments used groups of n=4 subjects.,1,10,"Experiment 1: 10 periods stranger + 10 partner; Experiment 2: 6 stranger; Experiment 3: 10 stranger. The paper pools them; 10 is the most common and is explicitly given for two experiments. Exact effective number per subject in the pooled data is not clearly specified, so 10 is an approximate dominant value.",0.5,1,"Subjects ""could decide on the amount m_i of MUs to contribute"" from an endowment of 20 MUs, implying a continuous/any-amount choice.",1,0,"At the beginning of the stage, each individual is endowed with 20 MUs in a private account and chooses how much to contribute; no initial allocation to the public good.",1,0.4,"They state that the sum of contributions is multiplied by g=1.6 and ""redistributed in equal shares"" to 4 subjects, giving an MPCR of 1.6/4=0.4.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat among participants in any of the described experiments.,0.9,1,"After the contribution stage, subjects are ""informed about the contributions of their group mates"" and, in punishment, observe punishment-related information; this is equivalent to seeing others’ outcomes.",0.9,1,"Subjects can punish specific ""other group members"" based on their contributions, implying that the identity (within group) of each target is known to the punisher. Standard public goods punishment designs reveal within-group identities.",0.8,0,"There is no reward option, only punishment.",1,1,"These are standard finite-horizon public goods experiments (6 or 10 periods); such designs typically inform subjects about total periods, though the article does not state it explicitly. Marked as shown with moderate confidence.",0.6,1,All three experiments are 'public goods games with punishment' and include a punishment stage.,1,1,"They state ""for each MU spent by the punisher"" the punished loses r MUs, so cost per unit of punishment is 1 MU.",1,3,"For experiments 2 and 3 they explicitly say ""each MU spent... caused a harm of approximately r=3 MUs""; experiment 1 has a slightly different implementation but ""can be considered to be approximately equal to the factor 3"" and is pooled.",0.9,0,No positive reward option is described in any of the experiments.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,N/A,Rewards are not available.,1,20,"In each experiment, ""individuals were endowed with 20 monetary units (MUs)"" at the beginning of the contribution stage.",1,N/R,The paper does not provide numeric average contribution rates; it only notes that contributions remain positive and roughly stable over time.,0.2,N/R,No explicit means or medians of contributions in MUs are reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff/maximum payoff) measure is numerically reported.,0.2,N/R,The article does not report numeric group payoffs from these experiments.,0.2,N/R,The article does not state where the original experiments [19–21] were run; only that they are 'western cultural area' and human subjects.,0.2,N/R,No age information is provided for the participants in the three experiments.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition is reported.,0.1,N/R,The educational background of participants is not described.,0.1,On site,"These are described as laboratory experiments, which are typically conducted in physical labs rather than online; no indication of online implementation is given.",0.8,"Two-stage repeated public goods game with punishment. Stage 1: Each of 4 players receives 20 MUs and chooses contribution m_i to a public good. Total contributions are multiplied by g=1.6 and evenly split, giving each player 0.4 MUs per MU contributed to the public pool. Stage 2: After observing others’ contributions, players can spend MUs to reduce others’ payoffs; each MU spent reduces the target’s payoff by approximately 3 MUs. Treatments: stranger matching in all experiments (groups reshuffled each period); one experiment also has a partner-matching arm and one has two feedback variants (observed vs unobserved punishment), which are pooled here.",Summarizes the design details given in the 'Description of the empirical data set' section.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0083225.md,Two-group analytical model: cooperators and defectors (parameter a varied),Paper defines and analyzes the case of two coexisting groups (cooperators x1 and defectors x2) with parameter a controlling resources consumed by defectors.,1,"Relative amount of resources consumed by defectors (a), varied continuously in [0, ∞) with analytical focus on 0 ≤ a ≤ 1 and a > 1","Only systematically varied quantity in this two-group model is parameter a, representing resources (cost) of defectors relative to cooperators.",1,False,No human or biological subjects are studied; this is a theoretical dynamical system.,1,False,No controlled experiment; purely mathematical analysis of differential equations.,1,False,"No laboratory or field setting, only equations.",1,True,"They study deterministic ODE models, sometimes with numerical illustration; this falls under simulation/no-human modeling.",0.9,True,They derive closed-form fixed points and stability conditions for the ODE system.,1,2,Model explicitly states two groups: cooperators (x1) and defectors (x2).,1,1,"Continuous-time dynamical system, not a repeated discrete game; encoded as a single evolving interaction.",0.5,1,"No discrete contribution choices; group fractions vary continuously, analogous to continuous contributions.",0.7,N/A,No individual endowments or contribution game; analytical population model without humans.,1,N/R,No public goods payoff structure or marginal per capita return specified.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,0,No information feedback between strategic individuals; only global dynamics defined by equations.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,0,Continuous-time infinite-horizon model; no rounds to be shown.,1,0,Regulators/punishers are absent in this two-group specification; only cooperators and defectors.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewarding institution or prosocial incentives modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model does not use per-agent endowments, only group population fractions.",1,N/R,"They analyze equilibrium group fractions, not contributions in a PGG sense.",1,N/R,No token contributions defined.,1,N/R,"They discuss stability and existence of cooperators/defectors, not payoff efficiency metrics.",1,N/R,Payoffs are not explicitly calculated; only utilities and population fractions.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation-based model of population fractions.,1,"Analytical Lotka–Volterra-type utility rate equations for two trait groups (cooperators x1 and defectors x2). Parameter a = |A2|/A1 captures resources consumed by defectors. They derive logistic growth for cooperators alone, extinction for defectors alone, and two evolutionary stable states under coexistence: (1) x1* = 1, x2* = 0 when a > 1; (2) coexistence with x1* = (1 + a^(5/2))/(1 + a^2), x2* = [(1 − sqrt(a))/(1 + a^2)] a^(3/2) when 0 ≤ a ≤ 1. Maximal stable defector fraction is about 0.083 (~8.3%).",Summarizes the specified equations and fixed-point analysis for the two-group case.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0083225.md,"Three-group analytical model: cooperators, defectors, regulators (parameters a and b varied)","Paper defines and analyzes a system with three coexisting trait groups x1 (cooperators), x2 (defectors), x3 (regulators) and studies stable equilibria across a–b parameter space.",1,"Relative amount of resources consumed by defectors (a) and by regulators (b), varied continuously with regions: (I) 0 ≤ a < ((2 b^2 sqrt(b) − b^2 + 1)/(1 + b^2))^2, 0 ≤ b < 1; (II) a ≥ that threshold, 0 ≤ b < 1; (III) 0 < a < 1, b > 1; (IV) a ≥ 1, b ≥ 1","Only systematically varied quantities in the three-group model are the nonnegative parameters a and b, which control resource consumption (costs) of defectors and regulators.",1,False,Three-group model is a theoretical dynamical system without direct human or biological subject data.,1,False,No controlled manipulation of subjects; purely mathematical analysis.,1,False,Not a lab or field study.,1,True,"They analyze and sometimes illustrate numerically a system of ODEs for group fractions x1, x2, x3.",0.9,True,"Closed-form fixed points and stability regions in (a,b) are derived.",1,3,"Model explicitly uses three trait groups: cooperators, defectors, regulators.",1,1,Continuous-time evolution; no discrete rounds.,0.5,1,"Fractions x1, x2, x3 vary continuously; analogous to continuous strategy spaces rather than all-or-nothing.",0.7,N/A,No endowment or contribution game context; population fractions only.,1,N/R,No PGG payoff or marginal return defined.,1,N/A,"No agents, communication, or interface.",1,0,Model does not include informational feedback between individuals.,1,N/A,"Punishment is represented via regulators’ parameters, not as individual-level actions or identities.",1,N/A,No explicit rewarding institution.,1,0,Infinite-horizon continuous dynamics.,1,1,Regulators (x3) are defined as agents who maintain order and punish defectors and outsiders; this is a punishment institution embedded in the dynamics.,1,N/R,"Cost is modeled at an aggregate level via parameter b (resources consumed by regulators), but no per-unit punishment cost is given.",0.7,N/R,Strength or impact per unit punishment is not parameterized separately from b and interaction coefficients.,0.7,0,No prosocial reward mechanism; only punishment via regulators.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No individual payoffs or endowments are specified.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are stable group fractions x1*, x2*, x3*, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contributions defined.,1,N/R,They do not compute an explicit efficiency ratio; focus is on stability and maximum allowed fractions of defectors/regulators.,1,N/R,"Group payoff is not quantified numerically; instead they treat utilities and parameter a,b qualitatively.",1,N/A,The three-group model is generic and not tied to a specific empirical country in its derivation.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical/simulation model only.,1,"Three-dimensional utility rate ODE system for trait groups: cooperators x1, defectors x2, regulators x3. Dynamics: dx1/dt = (1 − x1 − sqrt(a) x2 − sqrt(b) x3) x1; dx2/dt = (−a + sqrt(a) x1 − (1/a) x2 − sqrt(a b) x3) x2; dx3/dt = (−b + sqrt(b) x1 + sqrt(a b) x2 − (1/b) x3) x3. Parameters a = |A2|/A1 and b = |A3|/A1 are nonnegative and represent relative resource consumption/costs of defectors and regulators. Four types of evolutionary stable equilibria appear depending on (a,b): (I) coexistence of all three groups (x1*, x2*, x3* all > 0) when 0 ≤ b < 1 and 0 ≤ a < ((2 b^2 sqrt(b) − b^2 + 1)/(1 + b^2))^2; (II) only cooperators and regulators (x2* = 0) when a is above that threshold with 0 ≤ b < 1; (III) only cooperators and defectors (x3* = 0) for 0 < a < 1, b > 1; (IV) only cooperators (x2* = x3* = 0) when a ≥ 1 and b ≥ 1. They compute global maxima of stable fractions: max_a,b x2* ≈ 0.083 and max_a,b x3* ≈ 0.083, implying defectors and regulators cannot exceed ~10% each in a self-organized stable society.","Summarizes the specified three-group equations, parameter meanings, stability regions, and reported numerical maxima.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0083225.md,"Four-group analytical model with outsiders (parameters a, b, c, φ; no influx: φ = 0 case emphasized)","Paper defines general four-group dynamical system including outsiders (x4) with parameters a, b, c and influx φ, though detailed stability analysis focuses on subsets.",0.8,"Relative resource consumption parameters a = |A2|/A1 (defectors), b = |A3|/A1 (regulators), c = |A4|/A1 (outsiders), and outsider influx φ = Φ4/(A1 N0), conceptually varied over nonnegative values; much of the discussion sets φ = 0 to model closed societies.","For the four-group system, these are the defined nonnegative parameters controlling utilities and influx; they present general equations in terms of a, b, c, φ.",0.8,False,No explicit data or subjects used in defining the four-group equations.,1,False,"Pure model specification, not empirical.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,"They define ODEs for four groups; while they defer detailed analysis of outsiders to future work, the structure is a simulation-ready model.",0.7,True,"Four-group equations are derived analytically from utility principles, though equilibria with outsiders are not fully solved here.",0.8,4,"They specify four trait groups: cooperators x1, defectors x2, regulators x3, outsiders x4.",1,1,Continuous-time dynamical system.,0.5,1,"Continuous-valued group fractions, not discrete all-or-nothing moves.",0.7,N/A,No endowment or individual contributions modeled.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return.,1,N/A,No agents or communication.,1,0,No feedback of other players’ outcomes within a game framework.,1,N/A,"Punishment is institutional via regulators, no identities.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No discrete rounds.,1,1,Regulators x3 punish defectors and outsiders by construction.,1,N/R,Costs captured only via aggregate parameter b; no per-action cost defined.,0.7,N/R,No explicit technical mapping from punishment to payoffs beyond sign/size of interaction coefficients.,0.7,0,No positive incentives modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowments not part of the model.,1,N/R,No contribution decisions; only population fractions.,1,N/R,No token contributions.,1,N/R,They do not derive efficiency metrics for the four-group setting in this paper.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not quantified.,1,N/A,Generic theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical equations.,1,"Full four-group utility rate equations with outsiders x4 and influx Φ4: dx1/dt = (1 − x1 − sqrt(a) x2 − sqrt(b) x3 − sqrt(c) x4) x1; dx2/dt = (−a + sqrt(a) x1 − (1/a) x2 − sqrt(a b) x3 − sqrt(a c) x4) x2; dx3/dt = (−b + sqrt(b) x1 + sqrt(a b) x2 − (1/b) x3 + sqrt(b c) x4) x3; dx4/dt = (−c + sqrt(c) x1 + sqrt(a c) x2 − sqrt(b c) x3 − (1/c) x4) x4 + φ, where a, b, c ≥ 0 and φ ≥ 0. Outsiders (x4) exploit cooperators and defectors, are suppressed by regulators, and can receive external influx φ. While detailed equilibrium analysis with outsiders is postponed to another paper, the system generalizes the earlier 2- and 3-group models.","Summarizes equations (10–13) in dimensionless a,b,c,φ form for four groups, including outsiders.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0083225.md,"Country-level comparative stability application: Israel (a = 0.255, b = 0.329)","Section 'Application to human populations' computes specific parameters and initial fractions for Israel and compares them to model equilibrium, interpreting distance D as a stability metric.",1,"Country-specific parameters for Israel: a = 0.255 (budget share for defectors/social protection), b = 0.329 (budget share for regulators: defense, public order, governance); initial group fractions x1(0) = 0.779, x2(0) = 0.124, x3(0) = 0.097 as of 2008.","For this condition, (a,b) and initial (x1,x2,x3) are instantiated from empirical data for Israel.",1,True,"Uses real demographic, labor, and budget data for Israel to parameterize the model.",1,False,No experimental manipulation; descriptive/analytical use of secondary data.,1,False,Data at national scale; not a lab study.,1,True,Empirical parameters are plugged into the three-group ODE model to compute stationary point and distance from equilibrium; also used in later noise simulations.,1,True,"They analytically compute the stable fixed point for given a,b and the Euclidean distance D between empirical and theoretical fractions.",1,3,"At country level they consider cooperators, defectors, regulators only; outsiders x4 and influx are excluded for this application.",1,1,Static snapshot of 2008 data compared to equilibrium prediction; no repeated game rounds.,0.8,1,Model variables are continuous fractions.,0.9,N/A,No lab game with endowments; national aggregates instead.,1,N/R,No explicit public goods payoff structure.,1,N/A,Not an interactive experiment.,1,0,Countries are not players in a game in this context; no interfaces.,1,N/A,"Punishment via regulators is modeled structurally, not via individual identity revelation.",1,N/A,No reward system modeled.,1,0,No experimental time horizon displayed.,1,1,"Regulators in the model (police, army, bureaucracy) enforce order and punish defectors/outsiders.",1,N/R,Only aggregate budget share b = 0.329 is given; no marginal cost per punishment.,0.8,N/R,No explicit mapping from cost to sanction magnitude beyond model coefficients.,0.8,0,Model includes regulation/punishment but no reward institutions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Macroeconomic model; no per-agent endowment.,1,N/R,"Outcome reported is distance from equilibrium D, not contributions.",1,N/R,No tokens or contributions measured.,1,N/R,"They interpret lower D as higher stability and note concordance with GDP per capita, but do not define an efficiency ratio.",0.8,N/R,"Group payoffs are proxied qualitatively by GDP per capita, but no numeric DV linked directly to the model is computed.",0.8,Israel,They explicitly work out the case of Israel using 2008 data.,1,N/R,"They use aggregate demographic categories (pensioners, labor force, etc.) but no age distribution beyond that.",0.6,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,1,N/R,Education levels not reported.,1,Observational,Country-level data and budget shares are observed from official statistics; no intervention.,1,"Israel 2008: Total population 7.34M. Defectors defined as pensioners (9.8% of population), unemployed (2.46%), and prisoners (0.177%), giving x2(0) = 0.124. Regulators are army (0.185M), police (0.035M), and bureaucrats (16.5% of 2.96M labor force ≈ 0.488M), totaling 0.22M + 0.488M = 0.708M ≈ 9.65% of population, so x3(0) = 0.097. Cooperators fraction x1(0) = 1 − (x2 + x3) = 0.779. Parameter a is taken as fraction of budget spent on social protection: 25.5%, so a = 0.255. Parameter b is fraction spent on national defense (16.4%), public order and safety (3.8%), and state governing (12.7%), totaling 32.9%, so b = 0.329. Plugging these into the three-group model yields a stable equilibrium with x1* = 0.933, x2* = 0.050, x3* = 0.073 (values from Table 1). The Euclidean distance from equilibrium D ≈ 0.173, the smallest among ten countries studied, interpreted as relatively high structural stability.","Directly summarizes the Israel-specific parameterization, definitions of group categories, and reported equilibrium and distance from Table 1.",1
