10.1371_journal.pone.0083225.md,Country-level comparative stability application: Israel with stochastic noise (σ varied),Section 'Dynamics in presence of noise' studies the temporal behavior of Israel’s fractions under Gaussian white noise with varying σ.,1,"Noise standard deviation σ in Gaussian white noise term added to Israel’s three-group dynamics, with values σ = 0, 0.03, 0.06, 0.09; time t is also varied discretely with step Δt.","They explicitly state simulations for σ = 0, 0.03, 0.06, 0.09, illustrating trajectories in Figs. 6–8.",1,False,This is a simulation on top of the calibrated model; no direct observation of real noisy dynamics.,1,False,"Numerical experiment, not behavioral.",1,False,Simulation study.,1,True,They numerically iterate a stochastic difference equation with Gaussian white noise.,1,False,No closed-form solution of the SDEs; results shown via simulation plots.,0.9,3,"Three-group Israel model: cooperators, defectors, regulators.",1,N/R,They simulate in continuous time via small Δt; total simulated horizon and number of steps not reported.,0.5,1,Group fractions evolve continuously.,0.9,N/A,No contribution structure.,1,N/R,Not present.,1,N/A,"Simulation, no humans.",1,0,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No individuals.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,0,No 'rounds' concept in the simulation outputs.,1,1,Regulators present and function as punishers in the underlying model.,1,N/R,Same as deterministic Israel model; no extra cost parameter for noise.,0.8,N/R,"Not specified beyond parameters a,b.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Not applicable.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are trajectories of x1(t), x2(t), x3(t) under noise.",1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,"They examine stability and deviations from equilibrium, not efficiency ratios.",1,N/R,Payoffs not computed.,1,N/A,Simulation uses Israel-calibrated parameters but has no participants.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Numerical simulation only.,1,"Stochastic extension of the three-group Israel model: x_i(t_{n+1}) − x_i(t_n) = f_i(t_n) Δt + σ √(Δt) R(t_n), where f_i are the deterministic drifts from the three-group ODE, R(t_n) are i.i.d. Gaussian(0,1) random variables, and σ controls noise intensity. Using parameters a = 0.26, b = 0.33 (rounded Israel values) and initial fractions from Israel’s data, they simulate temporal trajectories for σ = 0, 0.03, 0.06, 0.09. Figures 6–8 show that greater σ leads to larger fluctuations and occasional strong deviations of x1, x2, x3 from their deterministic equilibrium values, illustrating that even relatively stable societies can experience large excursions under persistent shocks.",Directly restates the stochastic update equation and the σ values and qualitative findings described.,1
10.1007_s11238-013-9356-5.md,Monetary treatment – all four games (B-players),"Paper has two main treatments (monetary M and non-monetary NM). Within each treatment, B-players face the same four games with varying allocations but the punishment/reward technology is constant. This row captures the M-treatment environment for cooperation/punishment decisions of B-players.",0.9,"Treatment type: Monetary punishment/reward; Games 1–4 differ in underlying (A,B) payoff allocations but share the same monetary sanction technology.","Independent variable at this level is whether B’s score affects A’s payoff (monetary vs non-monetary). Within M, the main experimental contrast in the paper is versus NM.",0.86,True,Human subjects in laboratory sessions at Universidad Autónoma de Madrid.,1,True,"Controlled lab experiment with random matching, fixed rules, and explicit monetary incentives.",1,True,Sessions conducted in a classroom/laboratory setting at the university.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations described.,1,False,Paper reports experimental data; theory only used for interpretation.,1,2,"Each game is a two-player game: one A and one B, stated multiple times.",1,1,"Each of the four games is one-shot; one game is randomly selected for payment. For a given game, the interaction is single-shot.",0.95,1,"Not a contribution game; A chooses between two discrete allocations, not a continuous contribution; mapped as 1 since there is free choice among discrete options rather than forced all-or-nothing contribution from an endowment.",0.4,N/R,No public goods or contribution structure; simple allocation choices instead.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game; no multiplier or MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication allowed; only choices via decision sheets; instructions forbid talking.,1,0,No feedback of others’ past actions or earnings during decision making; only informed at payment about relevant score.,1,0,Decisions are anonymous; A is informed of score but not B’s identity.,1,0,Same anonymity applies to rewards; identity of B not revealed.,1,1,Subjects know there are four scenarios and that one will be randomly selected for payment; total number of scenarios publicly known.,0.9,1,"B can choose s in [-100,0) which decreases A’s payoff. This is monetary punishment.",1,5,B must pay a fixed fee of 5 points to assign any positive or negative score.,1,1,Each point of negative s reduces A’s payoff one-to-one: A’s payoff becomes x_A + s.,1,1,"B can choose s in (0,100] to increase A’s payoff by up to 100 points.",1,5,Same 5 point fee applies whether B rewards or punishes.,1,1,Each positive point of s increases A’s payoff one-to-one: A’s payoff is x_A + s.,1,N/R,No per-round endowment in the sense of contribution games; players receive payoffs from chosen allocation only.,1,N/A,Not a contribution or public goods setting; no contributions defined.,1,N/A,"No contribution decision; A picks between fixed allocations, B chooses scores.",1,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency as ratio of realized to maximum payoffs per game.,0.4,N/R,Average or total group payoff is not reported numerically by treatment.,0.3,Spain,"Sessions conducted at Universidad Autónoma de Madrid, Spain.",0.95,N/R,Paper only states participants are university students; no age statistics reported.,1,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,1,University students from different disciplines (9.6% from Economics),"Explicitly states participants were students from different disciplines, 9.6% from Faculty of Economics.",0.95,On site,Physical classroom sessions at the university with paper decision sheets.,1,"Two-player, two-stage allocation game. Stage 1: A chooses between two (A,B) payoff allocations: Game 1 (250,100) vs (200,150); Game 2 (250,100) vs (250,250); Game 3 (100,200) vs (150,150); Game 4 (100,200) vs (100,300). Points exchange rate 10 points = 1 Euro. Stage 2: B may pay 5 points to choose s ∈ [-100,100] in multiples of 10; A’s payoff becomes x_A + s, B’s payoff x_B - 5. Strategy method used for B decisions; A’s choices and B’s strategies collected for all four games; one game randomly selected for payment; no feedback across games.",Summarized from design and instructions sections including Table 1 and treatment description.,1
10.1007_s11238-013-9356-5.md,Non-monetary treatment – all four games (B-players),"Second main treatment (NM). Structure of games is identical to M, but B’s score does not change A’s payoff and is framed as approval/disapproval.",0.9,"Treatment type: Non-monetary approval/disapproval; Games 1–4 differ in underlying (A,B) payoff allocations but share the same non-monetary sanction technology.",Key manipulation is whether B’s action is monetary (affects A’s payoff) or purely evaluative; this row captures NM side of that contrast.,0.86,True,"Same subject pool and lab setup as the monetary treatment, with separate sessions.",1,True,"Controlled randomized lab experiment, identical procedures except sanction technology.",1,True,Conducted on site at Universidad Autónoma de Madrid.,1,False,No simulated agents; all decisions by human subjects.,1,False,Study is empirical rather than purely theoretical.,1,2,"Same two-player (A,B) structure as in M-treatment.",1,1,"Each game is a one-shot interaction; four such games with same partner, one randomly paid.",0.95,1,"Not a contribution game; A makes a discrete allocation choice. As before, coded as 1 since there is a free discrete choice rather than forced all-or-nothing contribution from an endowment.",0.4,N/R,No contribution or public good structure applicable.,1,N/R,No public good or linear return structure reported.,1,0,Same prohibition on talking; no communication channels offered.,1,0,No feedback about counterpart’s decisions before all four scenarios are completed; only at payment A learns the evaluation score.,1,0,"Though this treatment uses approval/disapproval rather than monetary punishment, identities remain anonymous; A is only told the score, not B’s identity.",1,0,Same anonymity for approval (positive scores).,1,1,Instructions state there are four scenarios and that one is randomly chosen for payment.,0.9,1,"B can send negative evaluation scores (s ∈ [-100,0)) labeled as disapproval; this is non-monetary punishment.",1,5,"B must pay 5 points from her own allocation to send any evaluation score, including negative ones.",1,0,Evaluation scores do not affect A’s monetary payoff at all; there is no payoff change per unit of negative score.,1,1,"B can send positive evaluation scores (s ∈ (0,100]) labeled as approval.",1,5,"Same 5 point fee to send any evaluation score, positive or negative.",1,0,Positive evaluation scores do not change A’s monetary payoff; they only convey approval.,1,N/R,No standard per-round endowment; payoffs are as in Table 1 allocations and reduced by 5 if B sends a score.,1,N/A,No contribution choice; hence no contribution rate defined.,1,N/A,No variable corresponding to monetary contribution.,1,N/R,Study does not report efficiency ratios for NM-treatment.,0.4,N/R,Average group earnings under NM not reported numerically.,0.3,Spain,Same subject pool: Universidad Autónoma de Madrid students.,0.95,N/R,No age distribution presented.,1,N/R,No gender information reported.,1,University students from different disciplines (9.6% from Economics overall sample),Text reports participant composition once for both treatments combined; no separate NM breakdown.,0.9,On site,Same classroom lab environment as in the M-treatment.,1,"Game structure identical to monetary treatment except sanction technology. Two-player, two-stage allocation game. Stage 1: A chooses between two (A,B) payoff allocations: Game 1 (250,100) vs (200,150); Game 2 (250,100) vs (250,250); Game 3 (100,200) vs (150,150); Game 4 (100,200) vs (100,300). Stage 2: B can pay 5 points to send an evaluation score s ∈ [-100,100]; negative s indicates disapproval, positive s approval. A’s monetary payoff remains x_A regardless of s; B’s payoff is x_B - 5 if she sends a score, x_B otherwise. Strategy method used; one of four games randomly selected for payment; no feedback between games.",Summarized from NM-treatment description and instructions in Appendix 2.,1
10.1515_bejeap-2013-0110.md,Trust (T) treatment – no explicit incentives,Paper defines Treatment T (Trust) where explicit incentives are not available; treated as a distinct condition.,1,Availability/frame of explicit incentives: none (no bonus or penalty allowed).,Section 2.1: Treatment T has no explicit incentives; this is the key between-subject treatment variable.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 186 undergraduate participants; Treatment T is one cell.,1,True,Subjects randomly assigned to roles; controlled treatment manipulation and payoff functions.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab at University of Bologna using z-Tree.,1,False,"All results reported are from human-subject lab sessions, not simulations.",1,False,"Although theoretical predictions are derived, this row describes the experimental treatment.",1,16,"Table 1 shows each T session mostly has 16 participants (9 sellers, 7 buyers); use modal session size.",0.8,18,Game lasts at least 15 rounds with 67% continuation until max 30; expected length is 18 rounds (stated explicitly).,1,1,"Sellers choose effort levels e1,e2 in [0,4] continuously; buyers set wage in [0,130]; not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.",1,0,"No common endowment to allocate between private/public accounts; default contribution concept not applicable, but mapped to 0 as all payoff flows are via contract wage.",0.3,N/R,"This is a buyer–seller effort/production game, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined or reported.",1,0,No mention of communication beyond contract offers; no chat or free-form messages described.,0.9,1,End-of-period feedback includes own profit and counterpart’s profit and contract details; this is a summary of others’ payoffs.,1,0,No punishment mechanism exists in Treatment T.,1,0,No bonus/penalty incentives exist in Treatment T.,1,0,Horizon is stochastic; instructions do not indicate that remaining rounds or total number are shown to subjects.,0.8,0,Treatment T does not allow explicit incentives; only fixed wage contracts.,1,N/A,No punishment stage or punishment choice variable; no per-unit cost defined.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism; no mapping from punishment points to payoff.,1,0,Explicit bonus incentives are not available in Treatment T.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No bonus technology to map reward to payoffs.,1,100,Instructions: each subject receives an initial endowment of 100 points.,1,N/R,"No contribution to a public account; main outcomes are efforts and surplus, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,No contribution variable defined; wage and efforts are the key actions.,1,N/R,They report surplus levels but not normalized efficiency ratios relative to the theoretical maximum.,0.7,N/R,They provide averages and regression coefficients but no single summary statistic of total group payoff per round for this treatment.,0.4,Italy,Experiment ran at University of Bologna with undergraduates there.,1,N/R,"Described only as undergraduates in Economics, Finance and Management; no age statistics reported.",0.4,N/R,No information on gender composition.,1,"Undergraduate students in Economics, Finance and Management",Section 2.3 explicitly states this.,1,On site,Computer-based experiment in a university lab using z-Tree; participants physically present.,1,"Buyer–seller repeated matching game with 9 sellers and 7 buyers per session (2 more sellers than buyers). Each round: buyers post public/private contract offers specifying fixed wage, requested contractible effort (delivery time 1–5 weeks), and requested non-contractible effort (quality 0–4). Sellers may accept at most one offer, buyers may contract with at most one seller. Effort choices: e1 (contractible) and e2 (non-contractible) in [0,4]; value function v(e1,e2)=8+16(e1+e2); seller cost c(e1,e2)=(e1+e2)^2. Outside option for unmatched sellers is 4, buyers 0. Repeated game with uncertain horizon: at least 15 periods, then 67% continuation per period up to a max of 30.","Summarized from Design, Payoffs, and Instructions for Treatment I, noting that the strategic structure is the same across treatments except incentives.",1
10.1515_bejeap-2013-0110.md,Bonus (B) treatment – bonus option available,Treatment B allows buyers to include only bonus incentives (or no incentive); defined as separate treatment.,1,Availability/frame of explicit incentives: buyers can choose to include a fixed-size bonus (+20) based on meeting a delivery-time threshold or use no explicit incentive.,Section 2.1: in Treatment B the buyer can include in the contract only bonus incentives or no incentive at all.,1,True,Same lab experiment with human subjects; this row refers to Treatment B.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of available incentive frame.,1,True,Conducted in laboratory setting using computers.,1,False,No non-human simulations reported for this treatment.,1,False,Analytical predictions separate from this empirical treatment.,1,16,Two sessions with 16 and one with 14 participants; use the modal 16 as representative.,0.7,18,Expected length of finitely repeated game with 67% continuation after 15 is 18 rounds (paper states).,1,1,"Effort and wage choices continuous/discrete ranges, not binary.",1,0,No endowment allocation decision; map to 0 as there is no pre-allocated contribution to a public fund.,0.3,N/R,Not a public goods game; no MPCR reported.,1,0,No communication channel other than structured contract offers is described.,0.9,1,"After each period, subjects observe own and counterpart’s profit and contract details.",1,0,"No penalty incentives available in Treatment B, so no punishment IDs.",1,1,"Bonus is tied to contract; partners’ IDs are displayed for accepted contracts, so the recipient of bonus (reward) is identifiable.",0.9,0,Random termination; instructions do not say remaining rounds or total displayed.,0.8,0,Treatment B excludes penalties; only bonuses or no incentive.,1,N/A,No punishment action; no per-unit cost.,1,N/A,No penalty implemented.,1,1,Treatment B explicitly allows bonus contracts with fixed incentive k=20.,1,0,"Bonus payments are purely redistributive between buyer and seller; no separate cost beyond transfer itself, so marginal cost of assigning 1 unit of bonus is borne one-for-one as transfer. Set as 0 since there is no extra cost to the rewarding agent beyond the transferred amount.",0.4,20,Parameter k=20 represents the fixed size of the explicit incentive; bonus adds +20 to seller and subtracts 20 from buyer when condition met.,1,100,Initial endowment of 100 points applies in all treatments.,1,N/R,No contribution concept; main DVs are efforts and surplus.,1,N/R,No contribution variable reported.,1,N/R,They report surplus but not normalized efficiency; cannot compute without full distribution of efforts.,0.7,N/R,Average surplus coefficients reported; no single aggregate payoff for this treatment only.,0.4,Italy,Same subject pool across all treatments at University of Bologna.,1,N/R,Age not specified.,1,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,1,"Undergraduate students in Economics, Finance and Management",Applies to all treatments.,1,On site,Same computer-lab environment for all treatments.,1,"Strategic structure identical to Treatment T except buyers in B can optionally add an explicit bonus of size 20 that is paid if seller’s delivery time e1 meets or beats a threshold e1* (in weeks). Buyers in B cannot use penalties. In practice, 67.6% of offers include a bonus and 32.4% have no incentive; explicit incentives significantly increase surplus and verifiable effort with mild reduction in non-verifiable effort.","Summarizes treatment-specific incentive options and observed usage (Table 7, Table 5).",1
10.1515_bejeap-2013-0110.md,Penalty (P) treatment – penalty option available,Treatment P allows only penalty incentives (or none); distinct framing condition.,1,Availability/frame of explicit incentives: buyers can choose to include a fixed-size penalty (−20) triggered by failing to meet a delivery-time threshold or use no explicit incentive.,Section 2.1: in P the buyer can include only penalty incentives or no incentive.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory treatment.,1,True,Between-subjects design manipulating contract framing.,1,True,Run in university lab with z-Tree.,1,False,No indication of simulation-only results.,1,False,Analytical model separate from this empirical condition.,1,16,"Each P session listed has 16 participants (9 sellers, 7 buyers).",1,18,Same stochastic horizon; expected 18 rounds.,1,1,Efforts and wages can vary across a range; not binary.,1,0,No contribution decision; same rationale as other treatments.,0.3,N/R,Not reported; not a linear public-good setting.,1,0,No communication channel beyond contractual offers is described.,0.9,1,Subjects see their own and counterpart’s profits and contract terms after each period.,1,1,"Penalty is embedded in contract between identified buyer and seller; counterpart ID is revealed in history, so punished party identity is known.",0.9,0,No bonus/reward incentives allowed in this treatment.,1,0,Random continuation; no display of remaining number of periods is mentioned.,0.8,1,Penalty incentives of size 20 are available.,1,0,Penalty transfer is automatic and costless to implement beyond the 20-point transfer from seller to buyer; no extra cost per unit to the punisher.,0.5,20,"Fixed penalty magnitude k=20 applied when seller fails to meet e1* (seller loses 20, buyer gains 20).",1,0,Bonuses not allowed in Treatment P.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No bonus implemented.,1,100,Same endowment structure across treatments.,1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Not applicable; key actions are contract terms and efforts.,1,N/R,They compare surplus across treatments but do not provide normalized efficiency; insufficient data to compute.,0.7,N/R,Only regression coefficients and relative differences are given; no explicit average group payoff for this treatment.,0.4,Italy,Same as overall experiment.,1,N/R,Age not provided.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,"Undergraduate students in Economics, Finance and Management",Uniform subject pool.,1,On site,Same lab setup.,1,"Same buyer–seller repeated game as in other treatments but buyers in P can attach a penalty of 20 points triggered when actual delivery time e1 is later than the specified threshold e1*. 87.8% of offers include a penalty and 12.2% have no incentive. Explicit incentives substantially increase surplus and verifiable effort, and surplus is significantly higher in P than in B, partly because penalty framing allows exploitative contracts that can leave sellers below their outside option.","Summarizes incentive structure and distinctive empirical findings (Table 7, Table 2, Section 3.4).",1
10.1515_bejeap-2013-0110.md,"Incentive (I) treatment – choice between bonus, penalty, or none","Treatment I gives buyers a menu: bonus, penalty, or no explicit incentive; treated as a distinct condition.",1,"Availability/frame of explicit incentives: buyers can choose among a bonus (+20), a penalty (−20), or no explicit incentive tied to a delivery-time threshold.","Section 2.1: In Treatment I, the buyer can choose between adopting a penalty, a bonus, or no incentive at all.",1,True,Same lab setup with human subjects; this row covers Treatment I.,1,True,Manipulates the menu of explicit incentive frames between sessions.,1,True,Computer-based sessions in a lab.,1,False,"Empirical, not simulation.",1,False,"Although prediction section analyzes I, this entry refers to experimental data.",1,16,Two of three I sessions have 16 participants; one has 12; use 16 as modal session size.,0.7,18,Expected number of periods is 18 under the common stopping rule.,1,1,"Effort levels e1,e2 in [0,4] continuous; wages in [0,130]; not all-or-nothing.",1,0,No contribution decision; endowment is not automatically in any shared account.,0.3,N/R,Not a public good; no MPCR parameter.,1,0,Instructions describe only structured contract-offer screens; no chat.,0.9,1,End-of-period feedback includes own and counterpart’s payoff and contract data.,1,1,"When penalties are used, the punished seller and buyer are identified by ID in history.",0.9,1,"When bonuses are used, reward recipient is counterpart with known ID.",0.9,0,Random continuation; no remaining-rounds indicator discussed.,0.8,1,Penalty incentives of size 20 can be chosen in Treatment I.,1,0,Penalty is an automatic transfer k=20 with no extra implementation cost.,0.5,20,"k=20 defines penalty magnitude: seller loses 20, buyer gains 20 when e1<e1* and penalty is in contract.",1,1,Bonus incentives of size 20 are also available in Treatment I.,1,0,Bonus consists of transfer of k=20 from buyer to seller when e1≥e1*; no extra cost beyond transfer.,0.5,20,"k=20 defines the bonus size: seller gets +20, buyer −20 when threshold met.",1,100,Same as other treatments.,1,N/R,No contribution measure.,1,N/R,Not applicable.,1,N/R,Paper provides surplus comparisons but not normalized efficiency index.,0.7,N/R,Group payoff values by treatment not fully enumerated; only regression estimates and differences.,0.4,Italy,Same experimental site.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,1,N/R,No gender breakdown given.,1,"Undergraduate students in Economics, Finance and Management",Applies across treatments.,1,On site,Same lab environment.,1,"Same underlying buyer–seller repeated game as in other treatments. In I, buyers choose each period whether to add a bonus, a penalty, or no explicit incentive tied to e1*. Empirically, 61.6% of offers use penalties, 18.9% bonuses, 19.5% none; bonuses are more likely to be accepted, but penalties yield higher buyer profits on average, partly via exploitative contracts that can leave sellers below their outside option. When exploitative contracts are excluded, penalty contracts still give somewhat higher buyer profits, while bonus offers are more generous in terms of maximum possible seller payoffs.",Synthesizes the distinctive freedom of contract choice and key behavioral findings from Section 3.4 and Table 7–10.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0105126.md,Square lattice – PD without punishment (p = 0),Baseline condition explicitly described as PD game without punishment used for comparison to punishment conditions.,1,"Punishment mechanism: no punishment (p = 0, q = 1); temptation to defect b varied in 1 < b < 2; topology: square lattice",Model section defines PD without punishment; Results vary b and compare to punishment with different p on the same lattice.,0.93,False,Paper describes computer simulations on lattices; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational study.,1,False,No lab with human subjects; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Authors repeatedly refer to ‘simulate results’ and specify lattice-based evolutionary game simulations.,1,False,No formal analytical derivations reported; only numerical simulations.,0.95,10000,"Square lattice with L = 100 implies 100 × 100 = 10,000 players.",1,0,Number of Monte Carlo steps / rounds not specified; only that dynamics run to stationary state.,0.1,1,"In a standard 2×2 PD on a lattice each player chooses a strategy (C or D), not a continuous contribution; mapped here as full ‘contribution’ of cooperation vs none; but schema’s binary choice better matched by allowing any amount so set to 1 conservatively.",0.2,N/A,This is not a public goods or contribution game; it is a binary-strategy PD with no explicit endowment allocation.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined for this PD setting.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication among agents is not modeled.,1,0,No information interfaces to human players; only local payoffs used; summaries not modeled.,0.9,N/A,No punishment in this baseline condition.,1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism in the model.,1,0,No human interface; number of rounds shown to participants is inapplicable.,1,0,Explicitly defined as ‘the PD game without punishment’.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism present.,1,0,Only punishment via public benefit fund is modeled; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model uses PD payoffs (R, T, P, S) without an explicit numerical endowment per round.",0.2,N/R,"Outcome is cooperator density ρ_C, not a continuous contribution rate relative to an endowment.",0.2,N/R,No contribution amounts defined; only binary strategies.,1,N/R,"Paper reports densities of cooperators and payoffs, but not normalized efficiency vs theoretical maximum.",0.3,N/R,"Plots and discussion refer to average payoff over whole population, but no exact numerical values are provided.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire study is computational simulation.,1,"Weak Prisoner’s Dilemma on a 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundaries. Payoffs: T = b (1 < b < 2), R = 1, P = S = 0. Each player plays PD with four nearest neighbors each time step, accumulates payoff U, and then may adopt a neighbor’s strategy via Fermi update W(x→y) = 1 / (1 + exp[-(Uy − Ux)/κ]) with κ = 0.1. Initial strategies assigned C or D with equal probability.",Summarizes explicit model description for no-punishment case.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0105126.md,"Square lattice – PD with punishment, slight intensity (SLP, p = 0.2, q = 0.8)",p = 0.2 is explicitly labeled as slight intensity of punishment (SLP).,1,"Punishment mechanism using public benefit fund with slight intensity: p = 0.2, punishment range q = 1 − p = 0.8; temptation to defect b varied in 1 < b < 2; topology: square lattice.","PD with punishment section defines p and q = 1 − p; Results describe p = 0.2 as SLP, varying b.",0.98,False,Computer simulations with no human participants.,1,False,"Computational model, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No human lab setting.,1,True,Described as simulation on a square lattice.,1,False,No analytical solution.,0.95,10000,Square lattice with L = 100.,1,0,Number of rounds/Monte Carlo steps until stationarity not specified.,0.1,1,Binary strategies C or D; contribution framework not used; keep consistent with baseline.,0.2,N/A,Game is not a contribution game; it is PD on a lattice.,1,N/R,No linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,Agents only use local payoff and a stochastic update rule; no separate display of summaries.,0.9,0,Punishment is applied via global rank and random choice among defectors; no notion of identity visibility exists.,0.8,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No players seeing remaining rounds in a simulation.,1,1,This is the PD game with punishment using public benefit fund.,1,1,"Each punished defector is fined p × b; the ‘cost’ in the schema is per unit of fine; 1 unit of public fund produces 1 unit of fine effectively, so cost-per-unit ≈1.",0.3,0.2,"Punishment intensity p defines fine per punished defector as p × b. For a given b, marginal fine per ‘unit of punishment action’ is p; here p = 0.2.",0.7,0,"Model includes only punishment, no reward.",1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit initial endowment per round; only PD payoff matrix.,0.2,N/R,"Outcome variables are ρ_C (fraction of cooperators) and average payoff, not continuous contributions.",0.2,N/R,No contribution amounts defined.,1,N/R,No explicit normalized efficiency metric reported.,0.3,N/R,Figures show temporal average payoff trends but not specific numeric payoffs for this condition.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational lattice simulation.,1,"Same weak PD as baseline on a 100×100 square lattice. After each round, players’ accumulated payoffs U are ranked. Top 25% by payoff pay 10% of U as tax; next 25% pay 5%; bottom 50% pay 0. Collected tax forms a public benefit fund used to punish defectors. For SLP, punishment intensity p = 0.2 and punishment range q = 1 − p = 0.8, so d × q defectors (d = number of defectors) are selected to be punished, each suffering a fine p × b. After tax and fines, payoffs are updated and strategies are revised via the same Fermi rule with κ = 0.1.",Summarizes specific punishment details for SLP condition.,1
