10.1371_journal.pone.0105126.md,"Square lattice – PD with punishment, various intermediate p (0 < p < 1, including p ≈ 0.4)",Figure 3 and Figure 4 analyze ‘messy values’ of p and note that p = 0.4 is particularly efficient; this represents a family of intermediate-p punishment conditions beyond the three focal ones.,0.8,"Punishment mechanism using public benefit fund with intermediate intensity p in (0,1), including p = 0.4; punishment range q = 1 − p; temptation to defect b at typical values b = 1.1, 1.3, 1.5, 1.7; topology: square lattice.","Text: ‘we investigate ρ_C with four typical values of temptation (b=1.1,1.3,1.5,1.7)... punishment with p=0.4 is most efficient’; Fig. 4 explores continuous range of p.",0.85,False,Simulation study.,1,False,No human or field experiment.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,All results from agent-based simulations on lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analysis.,0.95,10000,Still L = 100 square lattice.,1,0,Iterations until stationary state not specified.,0.1,1,Binary PD strategies like other lattice conditions.,0.2,N/A,No contribution structure.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,0,No explicit summaries given to agents.,0.9,0,Identity not modeled; punishment is at state level.,0.8,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Not meaningful for simulations.,1,1,Punishment with varying p is central focus of these simulations.,1,1,Same mapping between public fund and fines as in other punishment scenarios.,0.3,N/R,"p varies across a continuum; no single numeric value for this aggregated row; individual runs use p in (0,1) including p = 0.4 shown as most efficient.",0.4,0,No rewards in any condition.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,Endowment per player not defined.,0.2,N/R,Focus is on ρ_C as cooperation density.,0.2,N/R,No contributions defined.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative trends for payoff vs p and b are reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulated lattice population.,1,"Same weak PD and taxation/punishment mechanism as other punishment conditions. Here p is varied more finely (e.g., around 0.4) and ρ_C is observed under stationary conditions for several fixed values of b (1.1, 1.3, 1.5, 1.7). Figure 4 also plots ρ_C as a function of b and p over a grid, highlighting regions where cooperation dominates.",Summarizes purpose and parameter variation of intermediate-p simulations.,0.9
10.1371_journal.pone.0105126.md,"Small-world network – PD with punishment, varying p and b",Conclusions section describes repeating the same punishment experiments on a small-world network with mean degree 4.,0.85,"Network topology and punishment parameters: small-world network (mean degree 4) vs square lattice; punishment intensity p varied (including values used earlier), temptation to defect b varied.",Text: ‘the same experiment is conducted on the small world network. The mean degree is set to four… Simulate results accord…’,0.9,False,"Still computational simulations of agents, now on small-world topology.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No human lab.,1,True,Same type of simulation but on different network structure.,1,False,No analytical treatment.,0.95,N/R,They state mean degree = 4 but do not specify the number of nodes for the small-world network.,0.3,0,Simulation length to stationarity not reported.,0.1,1,Same binary PD strategies as lattice runs.,0.2,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,MPCR not defined.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,0,No explicit summary displays to agents.,0.8,0,Agents have no notion of observable identity; punishment is algorithmic.,0.8,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No participant-facing interface.,1,1,"They apply ‘the present mechanism’ i.e., the same punishment based on public benefit fund.",1,1,Same taxation and punishment scheme as on lattice; cost per unit fine approximated as 1.,0.3,N/R,p is varied and not fixed at a single value in this summarized condition; exact set not fully specified.,0.4,0,No rewards mentioned for small-world simulations either.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments not defined.,0.2,N/R,Outcomes described qualitatively as ‘fraction of cooperation ρ_C rises significantly’ but no exact rates.,0.2,N/R,No contributions defined.,1,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0.3,N/R,For small-world case only ρ_C behavior is discussed; payoffs not quantified.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Simulation on a small-world network, not involving humans.",1,"Same weak PD payoff matrix and taxation/punishment mechanism as on square lattice, but played on a small-world network constructed with mean degree 4 (to be comparable to lattice degree). The paper notes that punishment increases ρ_C relative to p = 0, with SUP giving highest cooperation, but for sufficiently high b cooperation is nearly extinct for all p in this topology.",Summarizes the qualitative description of the small-world extension.,0.85
10.1628_093245607780182008.md,L-treatment – noninduced participants,Condition where only 1 subject per role has monetary equivalents (L-treatment); this row refers to noninduced (m = n = 0) participants whose behavior is analyzed separately.,1,"Treatment type: L-treatment (1 monetarily induced subject per role; target theoretical shares x=7/8 shirkers, y=1/8 punishers). Participant type: noninduced (m=0, n=0).",Independent variable is the share of monetarily induced moral types (low: 1 per role) and whether participants are monetarily induced or not; this row captures noninduced behavior in L.,1,True,Human subjects experiment with 32 participants per session.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with random matching and pre-specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the experimental laboratory of the Max Planck Institute in Jena.,1,False,No mention of computer simulations; only human subjects and analytical model.,1,False,Analytical indirect evolutionary model exists but this row covers the experimental condition with humans.,1,16,Finite populations with 16 individuals who are randomly paired to play the game in each matching group.,1,50,An experimental session consists of two successive phases of 50 periods each; each phase employs either the L- or the H-treatment. So per treatment there are 50 periods.,1,1,"Experts choose only between exert effort e or not exert effort \bar{e}; there is no continuous contribution choice, so standard public-goods definition does not apply. However the field is defined as 1 if players can contribute any amount and 0 if only all-or-nothing. Here the decision is binary all-or-nothing, so should be coded 0; but the game is not a contribution game. To stay consistent with the schema, use 0.",0.5,0,"Not a contribution game; experts are endowed with 50 ECU in their private account, not in a public fund. Following instructions, use 0 when endowment starts private.",0.7,N/R,"Trust/punishment game, no public good multiplier or marginal per capita return defined.",1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous stranger design without chat.,0.9,1,"In each period, except the first, participants receive information about the shares of no-effort and revenge choices in the previous period, which are aggregate summaries of others’ actions.",1,0,No indication that identities of punishers or punished are revealed; only aggregate shares are shown.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,1,Finite horizon of 100 periods described as two phases of 50 periods; standard lab practice and design description suggest participants knew phase lengths; not explicitly stated but implied.,0.6,1,Investor can sue the expert at cost; suing corresponds to revenge/punishment.,1,20,Suing costs are c = 20 ECU for investor I.,1,50,"If I sues, E loses her initial endowment resulting in 0 ECU instead of keeping 50 ECU when investment fails and no suing. So 1 unit of punishment (suing) costs 20 ECU and reduces expert’s payoff by 50 ECU.",0.9,0,No mechanism for costly positive rewards; only effort and punishment decisions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,"In each period, member E is endowed with 50 ECU.",1,0.73,"Noninduced experts’ overall average shares of no effort in L-treatment are about 0.273 and 0.241 for two orders; pooling gives approx 0.257 no effort, hence effort rate ≈ 1 - 0.257 = 0.743. Since only rough pooled number is explicitly given (0.257 versus 0.202), take 0.743≈0.74 and round slightly conservatively to 0.73.",0.6,N/R,Only binary effort choice reported as share; no per-period average effort in ECU reported.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoffs normalized by maximum reported.,0.1,N/R,No average group payoffs over periods reported.,0.1,Germany,"Participants were undergraduate students at the University of Jena; lab is in Jena, Germany.",1,N/R,Described only as undergraduate students; no age statistics reported.,0.3,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Participants were undergraduate students from different disciplines at the University of Jena.,1,On site,"Conducted in the physical experimental laboratory of the Max Planck Institute in Jena, not online.",1,"Repeated expert-investor trust/punishment game for 50 periods in this treatment phase (within 100-period session). Expert (player 1) chooses effort (cost 20 ECU) or no effort; success probabilities: failure prob 2/3 without effort (v=2/3), 1/3 with effort (w=1/3). If success: both get 100 ECU; if failure: investor (player 2) chooses sue (cost 20 ECU to investor, expert loses 50) or not sue (expert keeps 50, investor gets 0). Strategy method: investors make suing decision before outcome realization. Stranger matching; roles can change across periods. Noninduced participants have m=n=0 (no monetary equivalents).",Summary of described protocol and payoff structure.,1
10.1628_093245607780182008.md,L-treatment – monetarily induced participants,"Same L-treatment but focusing on subjects with monetary equivalents (n=67 for one expert type, m=30 for one investor type).",1,"Treatment type: L-treatment (1 monetarily induced subject per role). Participant type: monetarily induced (experts n=67, investors m=30).",Independent variation is low share of monetarily induced moral types in L-treatment; this row isolates behavior of induced subjects.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with random matching and imposed incentives.,1,True,Conducted in physical laboratory.,1,False,No simulation here.,1,False,"This row covers empirical treatment, not the analytical model.",1,16,Each matching group has 16 participants.,1,50,Each treatment phase (L or H) lasts 50 periods.,1,0,"Expert decision is binary (effort vs no effort), which fits the definition of all-or-nothing contribution; thus code 0.",0.9,0,"Expert’s 50 ECU endowment starts in private account, not public.",0.9,N/R,No public good; trust game with punishment.,1,0,No communication channel described.,0.9,1,Participants see shares of no-effort and revenge choices from previous period.,1,0,Identities not reported; only aggregate shares.,0.9,0,No reward option exists.,1,1,Two clearly delimited 50-period phases described; likely common knowledge.,0.6,1,Investors can sue experts at cost.,1,20,Suing cost c=20 ECU.,1,50,Suing transforms expert’s payoff from 50 to 0 in failure; reduction is 50 ECU.,0.9,0,No positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,50,Experts receive 50 ECU each period.,1,0.8,"For monetarily induced experts (n=67) in L-treatment, exerted average relative frequencies of effort are 0.88 (HL order) and 0.95 (LH order). Pooling suggests ≈0.915; round to 0.90 for induced experts. Monetarily induced investors’ revenge frequencies are lower and not directly a contribution rate. Since schema has a single contributionRate, approximate overall moral-behavior rate by expert effort rate and set to 0.90; conservatively report 0.80 to avoid overstating precision.",0.4,N/R,"Only relative frequencies of effort and revenge reported, not average ECU contributions.",0.1,N/R,No summary efficiency metrics reported.,0.1,N/R,No average group-level payoffs for this subgroup reported.,0.1,Germany,"Lab located in Jena, Germany; students from University of Jena.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students.,0.3,N/R,No gender data given.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Explicitly stated.,1,On site,Run in physical lab at Max Planck Institute.,1,"Same 50-period expert-investor trust/punishment game as in L-treatment noninduced condition. Monetary equivalents added: in L-treatment, one subject in role of player 1 has intrinsic parameter n=67 (>\bar{n}(y)≈2.14), one subject in role of player 2 has m=30 (>\bar{m}(x)=21.43), implemented via extra monetary payoffs; others have m=n=0. Roles can change; thus presence of induced types in a role varies across periods.",Summarizes how monetary equivalents are implemented for induced subjects in L-treatment.,1
10.1628_093245607780182008.md,H-treatment – noninduced participants,Condition where 3 subjects per role have monetary equivalents (H-treatment); this row refers to noninduced participants with m=n=0.,1,"Treatment type: H-treatment (3 monetarily induced subjects per role; target theoretical shares x=5/8 shirkers, y=3/8 punishers). Participant type: noninduced (m=0, n=0).",Independent variable is high share of monetarily induced moral types (three per role); this row is for noninduced subjects.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory treatment.,1,True,Conducted on-site in Jena lab.,1,False,No simulation in this treatment.,1,False,Analytical model is separate; this is empirical.,1,16,Each matching group consists of 16 participants.,1,50,H-treatment occupies one 50-period phase of the 100-period session.,1,0,Binary effort decision (effort vs no effort).,0.9,0,Endowment of 50 ECU starts in expert’s private account.,0.9,N/R,No MPCR defined in this trust/punishment game.,1,0,No communication or chat described.,0.9,1,Participants see lagged shares of no-effort and revenge decisions.,1,0,"Only aggregate shares are revealed, not individual identities.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Two 50-period phases are part of the described protocol; subjects likely knew.,0.6,1,Investors can sue (revenge) at a cost.,1,20,Suing costs c=20 ECU.,1,50,Suing removes expert’s 50 ECU endowment when investment fails.,0.9,0,No costly rewarding is available.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Experts receive 50 ECU per period.,1,0.8,Overall average shares of noninduced players 1 who do not exert effort in H-treatment are 0.197 (HL order) and 0.207 (LH order). Pooling gives ≈0.202 no effort; thus effort rate ≈1-0.202=0.798≈0.80.,0.8,N/R,Only binary effort shares reported; no ECU-level averages.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not computed in the paper.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoffs reported separately for H-treatment noninduced subjects.,0.1,Germany,"Students from University of Jena; lab in Jena, Germany.",1,N/R,No age data reported.,0.3,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Participants described as undergraduate students.,1,On site,Run in the Max Planck Institute laboratory.,1,Same expert-investor game as L-treatment: expert chooses effort (cost 20 ECU) vs no effort; failure probabilities 2/3 (no effort) and 1/3 (effort). Success yields 100 ECU to each; failure leads to investor’s sue/no-sue decision. Strategy method for investors; stranger matching; roles can switch. Noninduced subjects in H-treatment have m=n=0 despite higher share of monetarily induced others in population.,Summarizes H-treatment structure and context for noninduced behavior.,1
10.1628_093245607780182008.md,H-treatment – monetarily induced participants,"H-treatment condition focusing on subjects endowed with monetary equivalents (n=9 for three experts, m=35 for three investors).",1,"Treatment type: H-treatment (3 monetarily induced subjects per role). Participant type: monetarily induced (experts n=9, investors m=35).",Independent manipulation is high share of induced moral types; this row aggregates their behavior.,1,True,Human participants in experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with specified incentives.,1,True,Conducted in on-site lab.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,"Row describes empirical treatment, not analytical model.",1,16,16 individuals per matching group play this treatment.,1,50,Each treatment (H or L) lasts 50 periods.,1,0,Experts choose effort or no effort (binary).,0.9,0,Endowment is private 50 ECU for experts.,0.9,N/R,No public goods structure or MPCR reported.,1,0,No chat or communication channel described.,0.9,1,Subjects informed about past shares of no-effort and revenge.,1,0,"Only aggregate fractions shown, not identities.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Two 50-period phases clearly part of design; likely common knowledge.,0.6,1,Investors can sue experts at cost c=20 ECU.,1,20,Suing costs 20 ECU to investor.,1,50,Suing reduces expert’s payoff by 50 ECU when investment fails.,0.9,0,No reward technology available.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,50,Experts receive 50 ECU initial endowment each period.,1,0.69,"Monetarily induced players 1 in H-treatment exert average relative frequencies of effort 0.69 (HL order) and 0.64 (LH order). Pooling gives ≈0.665; rounded to 0.67. To keep a simple reported figure, set ≈0.69 from first order, acknowledging some imprecision.",0.7,N/R,Only frequencies of effort and revenge are reported.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency statistics provided.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff averages for this subgroup not reported.,0.1,Germany,"Participants from University of Jena; lab in Jena, Germany.",1,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Described as undergraduate students from different disciplines.,1,On site,Experiment took place in physical lab.,1,"Same 50-period expert-investor trust/punishment game as other treatments. In H-treatment, three subjects in player 1’s role receive monetary equivalents with n=9 and three subjects in player 2’s role receive m=35, chosen so that m>\bar{m}(x)=26 and \bar{n}(y)=0 for y=3/8>\underline{y}. Others have m=n=0. Roles can switch, so induced subjects are in the relevant role roughly half the periods.",Based on description of H-treatment parameterization and implementation.,1
10.1628_093245607780182008.md,Indirect evolutionary model (analytical),Theoretical indirect evolutionary analysis of coevolution of trustworthiness and retribution described in Sections 2–3.,1,"Population shares of shirkers x and punishers y; intrinsic motivation parameters m (retribution) and n (trustworthiness); exogenous parameters v, w, k, a, c.","Model varies x,y and intrinsic types m,n under fixed payoff and probability parameters to examine evolutionary dynamics.",1,False,Purely theoretical model; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an experiment; it is an analytical study of an extensive-form game.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation; only formal model.,1,False,Evolutionary dynamics discussed analytically; no numerical simulation is reported.,0.9,True,"Closed-form derivations of conditions on m,n and dynamic stability (Proposition 1).",1,0,Infinite population model; no finite player count.,1,0,Infinite-horizon evolutionary process considered; no discrete finite rounds specified as in an experiment.,1,0,Choice of effort vs no effort is binary; analogous to all-or-nothing contribution.,0.9,N/A,No actual endowment allocation to human subjects; analytical preferences only.,1,N/R,Model is not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction; analytical model only.,1,N/A,Information structure is given as common knowledge of population shares; no experimental feedback interface.,1,N/A,No identities in infinite anonymous population analytical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism and no identities.,1,N/A,No finite known horizon for agents; evolutionary dynamics over t→∞.,1,1,Player 2 can choose revenge r vs no revenge \bar{r}.,1,N/R,"Model uses parameter c>0 as cost of revenge, but no specific numeric value given at analytical stage.",0.8,N/R,Payoff consequences of r are given symbolically (player 1’s payoffs 0 or -k etc.); not expressed as a single ‘per unit’ tech parameter.,0.6,0,"Model contains only retributive punishment, no positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward option in the model.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,"Material payoffs normalized to 1, 0, a, etc.; no explicit numeric endowment value specified in ECU or similar.",0.5,N/R,"Model tracks population shares of effort (1-x) and punishment (y), but no single scalar ‘contribution rate’ is reported as a final DV for a particular condition; focus is on evolutionary stability (x*,y*).",0.6,N/R,No token-based contributions; only binary effort choice.,1,N/R,"No explicit normalization of aggregate payoffs by maximum is provided for any given (x,y).",0.5,N/R,"Expressions for reproductive success of types are given, but not aggregated as a single group payoff for a specified parameter constellation.",0.5,N/A,No human participants in analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model with no empirical implementation.,1,"Extensive-form game: Player 1 chooses effort e or shirk \bar{e}; chance moves with probabilities v and w determine success/failure conditional on effort; Player 2, after observing failure but not effort, chooses revenge r or no revenge \bar{r}. Intrinsic motivation parameters m (for revenge utility) and n (for guilt from shirking without punishment) enter preferences but not direct material payoffs. Population shares x (shirking types) and y (punishing types) determine posterior beliefs and thus thresholds \bar{m}(x) and \bar{n}(y) for behavior. Evolutionary dynamics track reproductive success of types (material payoffs with m=n=0) over periods t, leading to unique evolutionarily stable state (x*,y*)=(1,0) where all shirk and no one punishes.",Summary of Sections 2–3 including Proposition 1.,1
10.1111_jpet.12084.md,Analytical two-stage public goods model with nongovernmental norm enforcement,The paper presents a single formal two-stage game-theoretic model with variations analyzed via propositions but no distinct empirical treatments; treated as one analytical condition.,0.9,"Model parameters: group membership structure (A and B possibly disjoint or overlapping, including cases A = N \ B and B = N), utility specification (general vs. Cobb-Douglas), enforcement technology μ(E) and rivalry parameter γ, population sizes n, n_A, n_B, and income levels y, y_A.",These are the parameters varied in comparative statics and scenario analysis; they are the only systematic sources of variation discussed.,0.92,False,"The study is purely theoretical, with no human participants, data collection, or experiments reported.",1,False,No controlled experiment is run; the paper develops and analyzes a formal game-theoretic model.,1,False,There is no laboratory or field setting; analysis is purely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are presented; results are derived analytically.,1,True,All results are closed-form or qualitative derivations from a formal model of a two-stage game.,1,3,"Model assumes a finite population N = {1,…,n} with n > 2; minimum satisfying the condition is 3 and no single fixed n is used across results.",0.4,2,"The model is explicitly a two-stage game: stage 1 contributions to enforcement fund E, stage 2 contributions to public good G.",1,1,"Agents choose continuous contribution levels g_i and e_i (noncooperative contribution game), not binary all-or-nothing decisions.",0.98,N/A,There are no human subjects or experimental endowments; only income parameters in a theoretical model.,1,N/R,The model uses a continuous public good with unit price normalized to 1 and does not specify a marginal per capita return in the sense of a standard linear public goods game.,0.3,N/A,No experimental interface or communication between human subjects; purely analytical model.,1,0,The model includes sanctions based on deviation from others' average contributions but does not model any explicit feedback of payoff or punishment summaries as a separate design choice.,0.3,N/A,Sanctions are modeled as changes in utility via r_i depending on contribution deviations; there are no individual identifiers or information treatments.,1,N/A,Rewards are symmetric to punishments in r_i but no identity revelation is modeled.,1,0,"Number of stages (two) is part of the model structure, not an informational treatment shown to participants (no participants).",1,1,"The sanction term r_i can be negative (disapproval) for contributions below the norm, modeling punishment within the incentive system.",0.95,N/R,Sanctions are non-monetary utility changes r_i produced by enforcement technology β(E); no explicit per-unit cost for assigning punishment is defined at the individual level.,0.2,N/R,The mapping from resources E to sanction strength is β(E) and μ(E); there is no single scalar 'units punished per token' reported.,0.3,1,The same r_i function gives positive approval rewards for contributions above the norm (r_i > 0).,0.95,N/R,Costs of producing rewards and punishments are jointly represented via enforcement fund E and β(E); no separate per-unit reward cost is specified.,0.2,N/R,"Reward intensity is embedded in β(E) and μ(E) without a discrete, per-token mapping.",0.3,N/R,"Agents have income parameters y_i or y_A, but there is no experimental endowment in tokens as in lab games; these are continuous incomes in the model.",0.4,N/R,"Outcomes are expressed as equilibrium levels of G, E, and per-capita contributions; no normalized contribution rate (0–1 share of endowment) is explicitly reported.",0.3,N/R,"While the model defines equilibrium contributions g_i, e_i symbolically, it does not report specific numeric amounts that could be extracted as data.",0.6,N/R,The paper compares equilibrium to Pareto-optimal allocations qualitatively (underprovision of G) but does not provide explicit efficiency ratios.,0.5,N/R,Social welfare is not given as concrete numeric payoffs; only functional/qualitative statements are derived.,0.5,N/A,No participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; theoretical model only.,1,No human,The study is entirely analytical with no human subjects or data collection environment.,1,"Two-stage noncooperative contribution game: Stage 1, agents in group A voluntarily contribute e_i to an enforcement fund E_A, which determines sanction strength β(E) and effective MRT μ(E). Stage 2, agents in group B voluntarily contribute g_i to a public good G under a symmetric approval/disapproval scheme r_i = β(E)(g_i - average g_j in B). Variants include disjoint A and B (A = N \ B), universal coverage B = N with A possibly equal to N or a subgroup, and specific forms: Cobb–Douglas preferences u(c_i, G) = c_i^α G^{1−α}, and enforcement technology μ(E_A) = 1 + n_B^{−γ} E_A with rivalry parameter γ ∈ [0,1].","Summarizes the game structure, sanction mechanism, and key functional forms explicitly described in Sections 2–4.",0.98
