10.1038_srep12797.md,General non-cooperative games – Analytical condition with social penalty on non-cooperators (NC),"Generalized analytical treatment of non-cooperative games with cooperators C and non-cooperators NC, where a penalty α_NC is added to NC actions.",0.95,Presence and magnitude of social penalty on non-cooperators α_NC ≥ 0; two cases based on calculated ESS without penalty p_cal*: (1) 0 ≤ p_cal* ≤ 1 and (2) p_cal* ≥ 1.,Section 'Non-cooperative games in general' defines p_cal* and compares ESS with and without penalty depending on α_NC.,0.9,False,This is a general analytical extension; no empirical data.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only theoretical reasoning.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,False,Discussion remains analytical; no computational model is described.,0.9,True,General conditions on W_NC(p) and W_C(p) and their ESS shifts under α_NC are derived.,1,N/R,Framework is generic over non-cooperative games; number of players is not specified.,0.3,1,Treats static ESS in an evolutionary setting; effectively one-shot interaction.,0.7,0,Players are categorized as cooperators vs non-cooperators NC; interpreted as discrete strategy choice rather than continuous contribution.,0.7,N/A,Not a specific contribution game and no human participants.,1,N/R,No specific public goods structure or multiplier is provided.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Analytical model; summaries are not part of the setup.,1,N/A,Penalty is abstract; no notion of observable identities.,1,N/A,No rewards considered in this generalization.,1,0,Static ESS framework; no rounds displayed.,1,1,Central claim is that penalty on non-cooperators (α_NC) promotes cooperation.,1,N/R,Penalty α_NC is generic; no numeric cost specified.,0.5,N/R,No technology parameter relating expenditure to penalty magnitude is given.,0.5,0,"General discussion focuses purely on punishments, not rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model is abstract over many games; no endowment specified.,0.4,N/R,"Outcome is equilibrium density of non-cooperators vs cooperators, not normalized contributions.",0.9,N/R,No specific contribution amounts modeled.,1,N/R,General theorem does not quantify efficiency; focuses on ESS shifts.,0.7,N/R,Group payoffs are not explicitly computed in the general result.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical generalization only.,1,"Consider general non-cooperative games with cooperators C and non-cooperators NC, with payoffs W_NC(p) and W_C(p) as functions of NC frequency p. Let p_cal* solve W_NC(p_cal*) = W_C(p_cal*) without penalty. With social penalty α_NC applied to NC actions, ESS with penalty p_Pnl* satisfies W_NC(p_Pnl*) − α_NC = W_C(p_Pnl*). If 0 ≤ p_cal* ≤ 1, then p_Pnl* < p_cal*, so equilibrium shifts toward more cooperators and may reach pure C if α_NC is large. If p_cal* ≥ 1 (pure NC optimal without penalty), sufficiently large α_NC can again yield p_Pnl* < 1 (more cooperators); otherwise p_Pnl* = 1 (pure NC).",Summarizes the general analytical statement connecting penalties to shifts in ESS composition.,0.96
