payoffs are generic benefits vs penalties.,0.5,N/R,"Outcome focus is on equilibrium crime rate (hawk frequency) as a function of b and q, not on contributions.",0.9,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,"Paper qualitatively discusses that low q cannot be compensated by high b, but no formal efficiency ratio is given.",0.6,N/R,"Group-level welfare under different b, q combinations is not quantified.",0.6,N/A,"Model has no explicit individual data; Japanese traffic law example is descriptive, not modeled with participants.",1,N/A,No human subjects in the model.,1,N/A,No human subjects in the model.,1,N/A,No human subjects in the model.,1,No human,Analytical criminological extension of the hawk–dove model.,1,"Penalty on a suspect’s criminal action is modeled as α_Sus = b·q, where b is sentence (≥0) and q is arrest ratio (0 ≤ q ≤ 1). Applying this to hawk–dove with V ≥ C yields equilibrium sentence b* = (V − p_H C)/(2q) for given hawk frequency p_H. If q is very low, even very large b cannot effectively reduce criminal activities, whereas sufficiently high q allows lighter sentences to suffice.",Summarizes the specific functional form α_Sus = b·q and the derived equilibrium sentence b* in the hawk–dove context.,0.96
