10.1002_bdm.2208.md,"Study 1 – Third-party observer (Player E), unfair transfer condition, all countries pooled",Study 1 analyzes Player E behavior in the unfair scenario across 10 countries; countries are treated mainly as controls rather than separate experimental treatments.,0.82,"Scenario: unfair transfer (Player C keeps 100/100 Talers, sends 0); Player role fixed to E (third-party observer).",Only the unfair transfer scenario and Player E decisions were analyzed for prosocial behavior.,0.98,True,Human participants from online panels in 10 countries.,1,True,Controlled incentivized economic game with random assignment to player roles and predefined scenarios.,1,False,"Data collected via an online panel provider (Toluna), not in a physical lab.",1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations described.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical modeling as a standalone method.,1,3,"Groups consisted of Player C (perpetrator), Player D (victim), and Player E (observer).",1,2,Player E made decisions for two scenarios: one fair and one unfair transfer. These are effectively two decision rounds.,0.9,1,"Player E could allocate 'some or all' of their 50 Talers across keep, punish, and compensate, implying a continuous (0–50) choice, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment starts fully in Player E’s private account; they decide how much to transfer as punishment/compensation.,1,N/R,This is not a standard public good game; no marginal per capita return from a group fund is defined.,1,0,Interactions were anonymous and implemented via online survey; no communication or chat is mentioned.,0.96,0,"Participants only saw basic scenario information, not payoff summaries or histories of others.",0.9,0,Game is anonymous; no identifying information about who punished whom is available.,0.96,0,Compensation transfers are anonymous; no identities revealed.,0.96,0,The text does not state that the total number of decision scenarios or remaining rounds was displayed to participants.,0.7,1,"Player E could transfer Talers as punishment to Player C, reducing C’s payoff.",1,1,Each Taler used for punishment is deducted from E’s 50-Taler endowment (cost of 1 per punishment Taler).,1,3,Punishment transfers were tripled: each punishment Taler reduced Player C’s payoff by 3 Talers (1:3 efficiency).,1,1,"Compensation is effectively a reward to the victim, increasing D’s payoff.",1,1,Each Taler used for compensation reduces Player E’s endowment by 1.,1,3,"Talers transferred as compensation were tripled, so each Taler increased D’s payoff by 3.",1,50,Player E received an endowment of 50 Talers for each decision scenario.,1,N/R,They report mean punishment and compensation in Talers; no normalized fraction of endowment is reported.,0.2,N/R,"Although mean transfers are reported (e.g., men: 14.15 vs 9.05 Talers), no single overall contribution amount is defined as the primary DV; the paper separates punishment and compensation and by gender.",0.3,N/R,No analysis or reporting of group efficiency (total payoff relative to maximum) is provided.,1,N/R,Group-level total payoffs are not reported.,1,"Indonesia, Colombia, Mexico, Chile, Russia, United States, China, Japan, Spain, Sweden",Study 1 draws samples from 10 specified countries via Toluna and pools them for the main analysis.,1,18–86,"Table 1 shows age ranges per country; overall lower bound is 18, upper bound 86.",0.95,Male and female; participants identifying with other genders excluded.,Participants indicating a gender other than male or female (n=3) were excluded from analyses.,1,General population; not restricted to students; detailed education not reported.,Samples were recruited to be representative in age and gender within each country; education characteristics not given.,0.6,Online,Data were collected via the Toluna online panel provider.,1,"Third-party compensation/punishment game using strategy method; Player C chooses fair (50/100) or unfair (0/100) transfer; only unfair condition decisions from Player E analyzed; transfers of Player E (keep, punish C, compensate D) are tripled in impact on C and D.",Summary of procedural details relevant to understanding the game structure and incentives.,1
10.1002_bdm.2208.md,"Study 2 – Medium punishment efficiency, separate rounds and combined round (Germany, all Player E decisions)",One of the two experimental punishment-efficiency conditions in Study 2; combines all three rounds for medium-efficiency participants as analyzed.,0.86,"Punishment efficiency = medium (1:3); behavioral option across rounds: compensation-only round, punishment-only round, and combined round (keep, compensate, punish).",Study 2 varied punishment efficiency between subjects (medium vs high) and behavioral option within subjects across three rounds; this row captures the medium-efficiency condition.,0.94,True,Human student participants in a lab setting.,1,True,Random assignment to punishment-efficiency condition and controlled game structure.,1,True,Data collected in the on-site laboratory of the University of Cologne.,1,False,No simulation-only components.,1,False,No purely analytical modeling.,1,3,"Same Player C, D, E triads as in Study 1; bonus payment computed in matched groups of three.",1,3,"Medium-efficiency participants played: (1) compensation-only round, (2) punishment-only round, (3) combined compensation/punishment/keep round, all in unfair scenario analyses.",1,1,"In each round, Player E could allocate 'some or all' of their Talers, indicating a continuous choice over 0–50 Talers, not a binary decision.",1,0,Endowment starts wholly with Player E; no automatic contribution to others.,1,N/R,Not a public goods setting; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Laboratory computer-based decisions; no communication channels are described.,0.9,0,Participants saw scenarios and their own decisions; no feedback on others’ accumulated outcomes.,0.85,0,Anonymous interactions; identities not revealed.,0.96,0,Compensation targets are anonymous victims; no identity information.,0.96,0,"Round order and number are implied by procedure, but no explicit statement that total/remaining rounds were shown on screen.",0.6,1,Punishment exists in the punishment-only round and in the combined round.,1,1,Each punishment Taler reduces Player E’s endowment by 1.,1,3,Medium punishment efficiency condition used the same 1:3 factor as Study 1.,1,1,Compensation exists in the compensation-only round and in the combined round.,1,1,Each compensation Taler costs Player E one Taler of their endowment.,1,3,Compensation efficiency remained 1:3 (Talers tripled) as in Study 1.,1,50,The basic compensation/punishment game structure (50 Talers for Player E) was retained in Study 2; no change of endowment is reported.,0.9,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as Talers transferred, not normalized percentages of endowment, and not split by efficiency condition.",0.2,N/R,"The paper reports averages pooled over both efficiency conditions (e.g., men punishment M=10.64; women M=9.07), not separately for the medium-efficiency condition.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency (payoff/maximum) measure is reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff totals are reported.,1,Germany,Study 2 was carried out with a student sample in Germany.,1,18–64,Study 2 reports participants aged 18–64 years.,1,Male and female; one participant with other gender excluded.,One non-binary participant and three under 18 were excluded; remaining participants were 51% female.,1,University students,Study 2 uses a student sample recruited at the University of Cologne laboratory.,0.95,On site,"Data collected in the on-site laboratory, not online.",1,"Third-party compensation/punishment game in which Player E responds to unfair transfers. In medium-efficiency condition, three unfair-scenario rounds: (1) compensation-only (keep or compensate), (2) punishment-only (keep or punish), (3) combined (keep, punish, compensate). Punishment and compensation Talers each had 1:3 effectiveness. Victim gender was varied within subjects (same vs opposite gender).",Captures key structural and procedural features of the medium-efficiency treatment.,1
10.1002_bdm.2208.md,"Study 2 – High punishment efficiency, separate rounds and combined round (Germany, all Player E decisions)",Second between-subjects punishment-efficiency condition in Study 2 with increased punishment effectiveness.,0.9,"Punishment efficiency = high (1:5); behavioral option across rounds: compensation-only round, punishment-only round, and combined round (keep, compensate, punish).","Study 2 manipulated punishment efficiency between subjects; here, the high-efficiency condition where each punishment Taler reduces the perpetrator’s payoff by 5.",0.97,True,Human student participants in the same lab experiment as the medium-efficiency condition.,1,True,Random assignment to punishment efficiency with controlled instructions and incentives.,1,True,Conducted in the on-site lab at the University of Cologne.,1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations.,1,False,No analytic-only study component.,1,3,"Groups composed of Player C, Player D, and Player E for bonus calculation, as in Study 1.",1,3,"High-efficiency participants also played compensation-only, punishment-only, and combined rounds.",1,1,Player E could allocate any amount of their Talers (0–50) within the allowed options each round.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely in private account of Player E.,1,N/R,This is not a public-goods framework.,1,0,No communication between participants reported.,0.9,0,No display of other players’ payoffs or previous actions is described.,0.85,0,Anonymous interactions.,0.96,0,Victims’ identities are not revealed beyond role and gender.,0.96,0,The article does not clarify if the total number of rounds was explicitly displayed.,0.6,1,Punishment decisions were available in the punishment-only and combined rounds.,1,1,Each punishment Taler still costs Player E one Taler.,1,5,"In the high punishment efficiency condition, each punishment Taler reduced the perpetrator's payoff by a factor of 5 (1:5 efficiency).",1,1,Compensation transfers are available in the compensation-only and combined rounds.,1,1,Cost of using Talers for compensation remains 1 per Taler.,1,3,Only punishment efficiency was increased; compensation efficiency remained 1:3 (tripling Talers).,0.95,50,Same basic game and endowment structure as in Study 1 and the medium-efficiency condition; no change reported.,0.9,N/R,No normalized rates by endowment are reported for high-efficiency participants.,0.2,N/R,Mean transfers are reported pooled across efficiency conditions and not separated for the high-efficiency treatment.,0.3,N/R,No explicit group efficiency metrics.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not presented.,1,Germany,Same German student sample and lab as medium-efficiency condition; assignment differed only in punishment efficiency.,1,18–64,Overall Study 2 age range applies to both efficiency conditions; no differences by condition noted.,1,Male and female; one participant with other gender excluded.,Same gender composition as overall Study 2 sample.,1,University students,Participants were recruited in the university laboratory; described as a student sample.,0.95,On site,Conducted in the on-site laboratory.,1,"Identical third-party compensation/punishment structure to medium-efficiency condition, but with higher punishment effectiveness (1:5). Player E made decisions in three unfair-scenario rounds (compensation-only, punishment-only, combined). Victim gender was varied within participants (same vs opposite gender).",Summarizes the aspects that distinguish this high-efficiency treatment while keeping shared structure with Study 2.,1
10.1017_ehs.2022.32.md,Homogeneous Gitano groups,One-shot public goods game with peer punishment; condition with ethnically homogeneous groups composed only of Gitanos.,1,Group ethnic composition: homogeneous Gitano (4 Gitanos per group),Between-subjects factor is ethnic composition; this condition is all Gitano members.,1,True,Human participants played incentivized public goods game with punishment.,1,True,Controlled/randomized economic game with treatment conditions.,1,True,Conducted in organized sessions in town venues; standard lab-in-the-field public goods experiment.,0.9,False,No computer/agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No formal purely analytical model as standalone study.,1,4,“anonymous four-person groups” repeatedly stated.,1,1,“one-shot public goods game with peer punishment (PGP)”.,1,1,Standard PGG with contribution from €0 to €10 endowment; no all-or-nothing restriction mentioned.,0.95,0,Participants decide how much of €10 endowment to allocate to group pot; all starts in private account.,1,0.5,Explicit: “endowment of €10 (marginal per capita return = 0.5)”.,1,0,No mention of communication; anonymous standard PGP; implies no chat allowed.,0.95,1,"After contribution, participants “received feedback on their group partners' contributions and earnings in a colour-based fashion”.",1,0,"Punishment is anonymous; only scarf colour known, not personal identity.",0.95,0,No reward stage exists; only punishment.,1,1,One-shot game explained in instructions; participants know there is a single round.,0.9,1,Peer punishment stage where participants spend earnings to reduce others' earnings.,1,1,“€1 spent on punishment reduced the target individual's earnings by €3.” Cost per unit is €1.,1,3,Same sentence implies impact is 3 times cost.,1,0,"Design only allows reduction of others’ earnings, no positive reward option.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,10,Explicit: “endowment of €10”.,1,0.6,Text: “Contribution level