10.1371_journal.pone.0166101.md,Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with punishment – Mixed-gender student sample,Single experimental design described; all 240 students played the same iterated PD with punishment in the lab. No separate treatment conditions reported.,0.98,Gender of participant (male vs. female) within a single iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with punishment structure,The only systematic factor analyzed as explaining behavioral variation is gender; game payoffs and procedures were otherwise identical.,0.9,True,Human undergraduate students participated in the study.,1,True,"Researchers controlled payoffs, matching, and repetition in a z-Tree implemented game.",1,True,Conducted in a visually partitioned computer lab using networked computers.,1,False,No agent-based or purely computational simulations were reported; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model or closed-form derivations as the primary method; focus is on experimental data analysis.,1,2,Described as a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma; subjects were assigned to dyads.,1,9,Fig 3 caption: “Each interaction had a maximum of nine repeated rounds with the same dyad of players.”,0.95,1,"Action choice is discrete among cooperation, defection, or punishment, not a continuous contribution. However, within the requested coding, 1 corresponds to ‘can contribute any amount’; this does not map well, but all-or-nothing public-good style is not used. Coding as 1 to avoid mislabeling as all-or-nothing PG contribution.",0.3,0,"Standard PD structure with no shared endowment/public fund; no initial allocation to a public account. By convention for PD, treat as 0.",0.4,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined or reported.,1,0,Only anonymous interaction via z-Tree choices; no mention of any communication channel.,0.9,1,"After each round, “the choices made were shown to the participants together with the updated payoff scores,” i.e., subjects see others’ actions and payoffs.",0.9,0,"Interactions were anonymous; no indication that identities of punishers were revealed, only actions and payoffs.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists; hence no reward identities can be shown.,1,0,"“Each subject was not told how many rounds were involved in each pair's interaction,” implying no display of remaining or total rounds.",1,1,Third option each round is punishment; payoff matrix includes punishment outcomes.,1,N/R,"Game is defined by discrete payoffs (Table 1), not by cost-per-punishment-unit; no simple ‘per coin’ punishment cost reported.",0.2,N/R,"Similarly, no linear technology mapping punishment spending to losses; only outcome payoffs are tabulated.",0.2,0,"Only cooperation, defection, and punishment options; no reward mechanism is described.",1,N/A,No reward option in the game.,1,N/A,No reward option in the game.,1,N/R,Payoffs are given as round-by-round point outcomes in Table 1; no per-round or initial point endowment is specified.,0.2,0.2903,Overall cooperation frequency reported as 29.03%; mapped to contribution rate 0.2903 in PD context.,0.95,N/R,"Binary/ternary action (C, D, P) rather than a numeric amount from an endowment; no contribution amounts reported.",1,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible total payoffs is reported or derivable without full distribution of outcomes.,0.2,N/R,Only average individual payments (~28.3 RMB) and SD ranges are mentioned; no aggregate group payoff reported.,0.3,China,"Students from universities in Kunming, Yunnan Province, China.",1,17–24,Explicitly: “aged between 17 and 24.”,1,"125 male, 115 female","Explicit counts provided: “240 students, 125 men and 115 women.”",1,Undergraduate university students,Described as undergraduates at three universities.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical computer lab with subjects seated at individual computers.,1,"Two-player iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with three discrete actions per round (Cooperate, Defect, Punish). Payoff matrix (row player, column player): CC=(1,1); CD=(-2,3); CP=(-5,1); DC=(3,-2); DD=(0,0); DP=(-3,-2); PC=(1,-5); PD=(-2,-3); PP=(-5,-5). Dyads interact for a random number of rounds up to 9, with continuation probability 0.75 after each round; matching across ‘interactions’ is random. After each round, both players see both actions and updated cumulative payoffs. Monetary payment equals accumulated points times 0.2 RMB; average earning 28.3 RMB.","Summarized directly from Methods and Table 1, plus continuation probability description.",1
