10.1038_s41598-024-80733-3.md,Simulation – Varying F2 penalties on pharmaceutical enterprises (F2 = 7 vs 14 vs 21),Section on punishment intensity describes simulations where F2 is varied while other parameters including M2 are fixed.,1,"Local government penalty on pharmaceutical enterprises F2 ∈ {7, 14, 21}, with other parameters fixed at C1=80, C2=50, C3=30, C4=10, M1=40, M2=8, M3=6, F1=60, F3=7; initial y varied in a separate convergence-speed analysis at F2=14.","Paper explicitly lists F2 values 7, 14, 21, and notes other parameters remain unchanged.",1,False,No human data; model is simulated.,1,False,"Computational scenario analysis, not empirical experimentation.",1,False,Not conducted in a lab environment.,1,True,Described as evolutionary simulations showing system evolution paths under varying F2.,1,True,Relies on analytically derived replicator dynamics; simulations illustrate.,1,3,Same three actors as core model.,1,0,"Continuous-time evolution, no discrete rounds.",1,1,Binary strategic choices for each actor.,0.8,N/A,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,Public goods multiplier not part of model.,1,0,No communication mechanism.,1,0,"Agents respond only to payoffs, not explicit outcome displays.",1,0,Only type-level punishment parameters exist.,1,0,Rewards are not individualized.,1,0,No horizon is displayed; simulations show trajectories over time.,1,1,F1–F3 included; F2 is manipulated.,1,N/A,"Penalties are payoff parameters, not derived from an explicit costly decision.",1,N/A,No marginal punishment technology defined.,1,1,Subsidies M1–M3 remain in the model.,1,N/A,"Rewards are parameters, not economized choices.",1,N/A,No per-unit mapping from reward spend to effect.,1,N/A,No explicit endowments.,1,N/A,"Outcomes measured in strategy probabilities x,y,z.",1,N/A,No contribution variables exist.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not quantified; focus on convergence to compliant behavior.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoff results for different F2 scenarios.,0.7,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,F2 scenarios are explored via computer simulations.,1,"Analyzes how increasing F2 (penalty on non-compliant production) shifts pharmaceutical enterprises from non-compliant to compliant strategies and accelerates convergence of y to 1, with larger F2 leading to faster strategy adjustment. Initial y also affects convergence speed. Local government and public strategies are reported as largely unaffected by F2 changes in these simulations.",Directly summarizes narrative attached to Figs. 5–7.,1
10.1038_s41598-024-80733-3.md,Simulation – Varying M3 subsidies to the public (M3 = 2 vs 6 vs 10),Section 'Local government reward and punishment intensity on the evolution of public strategy influence' includes simulations varying M3 with other parameters fixed.,1,"Local government subsidy to the public M3 ∈ {2, 6, 10}, with other parameters fixed at C1=80, C2=50, C3=30, C4=10, M1=40, M2=8, F1=60, F2=14, F3=7; initial z (cooperation probability) varied in a separate convergence-speed analysis at M3=6.","Paper explicitly gives M3 values 2, 6, 10 and notes that other parameters stay unchanged.",1,False,Simulation with no human participants.,1,False,No empirical experimental design.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Described as evolutionary simulation on system evolution paths and convergence speeds with varying M3.,1,True,Relies on the same analytical evolutionary game model.,1,3,Three stakeholder types as in core model.,1,0,Continuous-time evolution.,1,1,Binary strategies for public and other parties.,0.8,N/A,No divisible contributions.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,No communication mechanism described.,1,0,Information about others' payoffs or behavior is not explicitly modeled as observable summaries.,1,0,Punishments F1–F3 are not identity-specific.,1,0,Rewards/subsidies are not identity-linked.,1,0,No rounds framework.,1,1,F1–F3 remain active; focus here is on varying reward M3.,1,N/A,No explicit per-unit punishment decision.,1,N/A,No technology parameter from punishment effort to outcome.,1,1,Subsidies M1–M3 active; M3 is the manipulated reward.,1,N/A,Rewards are exogenous parameters in payoff functions.,1,N/A,No explicit per-unit subsidy technology.,1,N/A,Endowments are not defined.,1,N/A,Main outcome is probability z of public cooperation.,1,N/A,No contribution levels measured.,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported as formal DV.,1,N/R,No numeric payoff aggregates reported by M3 level.,0.7,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,All results from simulations.,1,Simulations investigate how increasing M3 (subsidy for public cooperation) shifts the public from non-cooperation to cooperation and accelerates convergence of z to 1. Higher initial cooperation probability z also speeds convergence. Local government and pharmaceutical enterprises’ strategies are largely unaffected by M3 level in these runs.,Based on narrative around Figs. 8–10.,1
10.1038_s41598-024-80733-3.md,Simulation – Varying F3 penalties on the public (F3 = 1 vs 7 vs 13),"Same section describes simulations varying the public punishment F3 with other parameters, including M3, fixed.",1,"Local government penalty on the public F3 ∈ {1, 7, 13}, with other parameters fixed at C1=80, C2=50, C3=30, C4=10, M1=40, M2=8, M3=6, F1=60, F2=14; initial z varied in a separate convergence-speed analysis at F3=7.","Paper lists F3 values 1, 7, 13 and notes that other parameter values remain unchanged.",1,False,No human participants; only simulations.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field study.,1,True,Evolutionary simulations charting system evolution paths and convergence speeds for different F3 values.,1,True,Uses replicator dynamics derived earlier.,1,3,"Involves government, firms, public.",1,0,"Continuous dynamics, no fixed number of rounds.",1,1,Agents choose discrete strategies; no gradations.,0.8,N/A,No endowment allocation.,1,N/R,Public goods MPCR not present.,1,0,No communication channel specified.,1,0,No explicit feedback of others’ outcomes.,1,0,Punishment is generic and parameterized.,1,0,Rewards are generic subsidies with no identity dimension.,1,0,No round count shown or modeled.,1,1,"F1–F3 are active, with F3 varied.",1,N/A,"Punishments are exogenous parameters, not decisions with explicit marginal costs.",1,N/A,No mapping from units of punishment to impact beyond lump-sum F3.,1,1,M1–M3 subsidies remain constant across F3 changes.,1,N/A,Rewards remain parameters; not modeled as chosen costly actions.,1,N/A,No explicit per-unit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No endowment defined.,1,N/A,"Outcome of interest is z (probability of cooperation), not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff is not quantified per F3 scenario.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulated parameter variations only.,1,"Explores how increasing F3 (punishment for public non-cooperation) encourages the public to adopt cooperation in prevention and control and speeds convergence of z to 1. Larger F3 yields faster adjustment; higher initial willingness to cooperate (z) also accelerates convergence. Strategies of local governments and pharmaceutical enterprises are little affected by F3 in these simulations. Authors also note potential trade-offs if punishment is too high, though this is conceptual rather than modeled.",Summarizes descriptive results for Figs. 11–13.,1
10.1109_tcsii.2021.3133711.md,Replicator dynamics of symmetric binary trust game with punishment and random regular graphs,"The paper analyzes one formal evolutionary game model: a symmetric binary trust game with institutional punishment on a random regular graph; all results (e.g., Fig. 1–3) refer to this unified model rather than distinct experimental treatments.",0.9,"Penalty size p, node degree k of the random regular graph (network structure), and trust payoff parameter r; institutional tax f is also considered (often with f = p).","Throughout the analysis, p, k, and r are systematically varied to study stability and evolutionary outcomes; f is introduced as the tax maintaining the institution and explicitly used, e.g., in Fig. 3 with f = p.",1,False,No human or animal subjects are used; the work is purely theoretical and computational within evolutionary game theory.,1,False,There is no controlled behavioral or field experiment; the study consists of analytical derivations and theoretical dynamical systems analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting is described; all results are from mathematical analysis/simulations.,1,True,"The authors use replicator dynamics and pair-approximation on graphs, which are numerical/computer-based or analytical models of agent populations without human subjects; figures (e.g., Fig. 1, Fig. 3) depict model dynamics.",0.9,True,"They derive transformed payoff matrices, equilibria, eigenvalues, and stability conditions analytically (closed-form expressions for equilibria and thresholds such as p_{1,b}, k_{1,b}).",1,2,"The underlying stage game is a two-player trust game between an investor and a trustee, as explicitly stated in Section II.",1,1,"The trust game stage is a single interaction; evolutionary dynamics arises from repeated imitation at the population level, not repeated rounds between the same pair.",0.9,1,"Actions are binary (invest vs not invest; trustworthy vs untrustworthy). The definition of CONFIG_allOrNothing refers to contribution discreteness, but this is a trust, not contribution, game; coded as 1 because choices are discrete all-or-nothing actions.",0.6,N/A,No public-goods contribution or initial allocation between private and public accounts exists in this trust game model.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is a public-goods concept; not defined or inferable for this trust game.,1,N/A,There are no human subjects and no modeled communication or messaging channel between agents.,1,N/A,Information displays to human participants are not applicable in a purely theoretical evolutionary model.,1,N/A,No identities or visibility assumptions of punishers are modeled; only payoff reductions via institutional punishment are considered.,1,N/A,"The model only includes institutional penalties, not rewards tied to visible identities.",1,N/A,Agents are not human participants and are not informed about a number of rounds; dynamics are continuous-time replicator equations.,1,1,Section II.A defines an institutional penalty with matrix P and expected fine p ≥ 0 applied to untrustworthy actions.,1,N/R,"The model specifies an expected fine p applied directly to payoffs and a per-agent tax f to maintain the institution, but does not define a per-unit cost-of-punishment parameter in the sense of punishment coins; not directly mappable to the requested cost metric.",0.5,N/R,Punishment is modeled as an expected fine p subtracted from payoffs for certain outcomes; there is no explicit ratio of damage inflicted per unit cost spent by a punisher.,0.5,0,Only penalties (fines and tax) are modeled; no positive rewards for prosocial behavior are introduced in this specific model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,"The game is defined directly via payoffs (e.g., 0, r, 1, −1); no initial token endowment per agent is specified.",1,N/A,"There is no contribution decision or continuous contribution level; actions are invest/not-invest and trustworthy/untrustworthy, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/A,No contribution quantities are defined in tokens; only binary choices in a trust game.,1,N/R,"They discuss population-mean payoffs and social welfare (e.g. Fig. 3), but do not normalize them explicitly as a ratio to a theoretical maximum in the sense required for DV_efficiency.",0.6,N/R,"Population-mean payoff at evolutionary stable states is analyzed (e.g., Fig. 3), but no single numeric value is reported that could be directly entered here.",0.6,N/A,There are no human participants; the model is over abstract agents in a population.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,No human,The study consists of analytical and computational evolutionary game-theoretic modeling with no human participants.,1,"Symmetric binary trust game with four pure strategies {IT, IU, NT, NU}. Payoff matrix Π is defined in terms of parameter r with outcomes: mutual IT yields 2r, IT vs IU gives payoffs (−1 + r, r + 1), etc.; institutional punishment imposes an expected fine p on untrustworthy trustee actions when an investor invests; a per-agent tax f (often f = p) funds the incentive-providing institution. Population is placed on a random regular graph with degree k ≥ 3; pair-approximation yields an effective payoff matrix C = A + B, where A = Π + P + F and B depends on k as in Ohtsuki & Nowak (2006). Dynamics follow replicator equations on the 3-simplex of frequencies of strategies IT, IU, NT, NU.","Summarized from Sections II (Model), II.A (Incentives), and II.B (Evolutionary Game Dynamics on Graphs).",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.003.md,Baseline – No Punishment,"Paper defines three treatments: Baseline, Monetary Punishment (MP), Non‑monetary Punishment (NP). This condition is the Baseline public good game without punishment.",1,Punishment institution: No punishment; standard public good only,Main independent variable is the availability/type of punishment; here there is no punishment stage.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 112 human subjects across treatments; Baseline is one of the treatments.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab design with predefined treatments and fixed groups.,1,True,Conducted in the Waikato Experimental Economics Laboratory (WEEL).,1,False,No mention of computational/agent-based simulations.,1,False,No formal analytical model as a standalone study; focus is on experimental data.,1,4,“Subjects are randomly assigned to groups of four.”,1,10,“participate in a repeated…mechanism (VCM) for 10 periods.”,1,1,"Subjects choose “a portion of their endowment to contribute,” implying any integer amount from 0 to 20, not all‑or‑nothing.",1,0,Each period they receive 20 ECU endowment in private account and then decide how much to place in public account.,1,0.4,Each ECU in group account yields 0.4 ECU to each of 4 members; MPCR = 0.4.,1,0,Procedures do not mention any communication; typical VCM and punishment variants forbid chat.,0.9,1,"End‑of‑period summary screen reports own contribution, sum of all contributions, and own earnings.",1,0,"No punishment exists in Baseline; identification of punishment sender is not applicable but per schema must be 0 or 1, set to 0.",0.7,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,"Finite repeated game of 10 periods is common knowledge; typical instructions state number of periods. Not explicitly printed on screen, but they know total rounds.",0.6,0,Baseline is the public good game described without any punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment institution in Baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment institution in Baseline.,1,0,No possibility to increase others’ payoffs; only contributions and (in other treatments) punishment.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,20,"“At the beginning of each period, subjects received 20 ECU.”",1,N/R,"They report raw contributions (e.g., 9 tokens of 20) but not normalized averages explicitly; not computed in paper.",0.4,9,Fig. 1: average contribution in period 1 is “close to 9 tokens out of 20”; use this as representative level mentioned.,0.6,N/R,No explicit report of realized payoffs relative to theoretical maximum.,0,N/R,Only individual earnings formula reported; no numerical group payoff levels per treatment.,0,New Zealand,"Experiment conducted at University of Waikato, New Zealand.",1,N/R,Age range or mean not reported.,0,N/R,No information on gender composition.,0,Likely university students; precise education level N/R,"Study run in a university laboratory; typical subject pool is students, but not explicitly stated.",0.4,On site,"Laboratory sessions at WEEL with videotaping; implies physical lab setting, not online.",1,"Standard linear public goods game with fixed groups of 4, 10 periods, per-period marginal return 0.4, no punishment stage. After contribution, subjects see own contribution, group total, and own earnings.",Summarizes structural features specific to this Baseline treatment from design section.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.003.md,Monetary Punishment (MP),One of the three treatments is explicitly named “monetary punishment (MP) treatment.”,1,"Punishment institution: Monetary punishment with costly points (1 ECU cost, −3 ECU to target per point, up to 10 points per other member)","Independent variable is availability and type of punishment; in MP, points have monetary cost and effect.",1,True,Uses human participants in lab sessions.,1,True,Treatment assignment in controlled lab environment.,1,True,Conducted in WEEL at University of Waikato.,1,False,No computational simulations.,1,False,No separate analytical-only model tested.,1,4,Same group size 4 used in all treatments.,1,10,Game repeated for 10 periods in all treatments.,1,1,Subjects choose any portion of their 20 ECU endowment to contribute.,1,0,Endowment is initially private then can be contributed.,1,0.4,Contribution technology identical to Baseline: each ECU to public yields 0.4 to each group member.,1,0,No communication described; standard punishment VCM designs forbid chat.,0.9,1,"End-of-period summary in MP shows own contribution, sum of contributions, total points assigned, cost, points received, earnings reduction, and overall earnings.",1,0,"Subjects see “contribution levels of each of the other members of their group” and assign points to each; no indication they learn identities of who punished them later, only total points received. Hence punishment source identities are not revealed.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Finite 10-period game known to participants; typical instructions disclose number of periods.,0.6,1,MP includes a punishment stage with point assignments.,1,1,“1 point = 1 ECU” cost for assigning points.,1,3,“Each punishment point received…reduces the earnings of the subject by 3 points.”,1,0,"Only punishment (reductions) is available, no positive sanctions.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same as Baseline: 20 ECU per period.,1,N/R,Paper reports raw average contributions (11 then 14–15 tokens) but not normalized rates.,0.4,14.5,Text: “average cooperation…increases over time to the 14–15 range”; mid‑point 14.5 used as representative final level.,0.5,N/R,No explicit calculation of efficiency relative to full‑cooperation benchmark.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff statistics per treatment reported.,0,New Zealand,"Lab at University of Waikato, New Zealand.",1,N/R,No age information.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Likely university students; precise education level N/R,University lab subject pool; not explicitly described.,0.4,On site,Described as laboratory sessions with physical videotaping.,1,"Two-stage repeated linear public good with fixed groups of 4. Stage 1: contribute 0–20 ECU to group account (MPCR 0.4). Stage 2: after seeing each other’s individual contributions (order randomized each period), each player can assign 0–10 punishment points to each of the 3 others, costing 1 ECU per point and reducing target’s payoff by 3 ECU per point. End-of-period summary shows contributions, points assigned/received, costs, reductions, and earnings.","Summarizes MP-specific features from design section, including punishment technology and information.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.003.md,Non‑monetary Punishment (NP),Third treatment is explicitly named “Non-monetary Punishment (NP) treatment.”,1,"Punishment institution: Non‑monetary, costless disapproval points (0 ECU cost, 0 ECU effect on earnings, up to 10 points per other member)",Independent variable is punishment type; here points convey disapproval only and have no monetary cost/effect.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Predefined treatment in controlled lab setting.,1,True,Run in WEEL at University of Waikato.,1,False,No simulation used.,1,False,No purely analytical model tested.,1,4,Same grouping of four applies to NP.,1,10,Game repeated 10 periods in all treatments.,1,1,"Subjects choose a portion of their endowment to contribute, allowing intermediate amounts.",1,0,Endowment starts in private account each period.,1,0.4,Contribution technology same as Baseline and MP.,1,0,No communication is described.,0.9,1,"End-of-period summary in NP includes own contribution, group sum, points assigned/received, (zero) cost, and earnings.",1,0,"As in MP, subjects see total points received but not who sent them; only individual contributions are identified at punishment decision stage.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,10‑period structure is common knowledge from instructions.,0.6,1,NP includes a punishment stage with disapproval points.,1,0,"“Punishment points…have no effect on…final earnings, and…are costless to assign.”",1,0,Points do not change earnings; 0 ECU impact per point.,1,0,No mechanism to increase others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,20,Endowment is 20 ECU per period in all treatments.,1,N/R,Only raw contributions summarized; no explicit normalized rate.,0.4,N/R,"Text states NP contributions are high initially and then decline to lie between MP and Baseline by the end, but does not provide specific numeric averages.",0.3,N/R,No realized efficiency metrics reported.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff levels by treatment.,0,New Zealand,Same subject pool and lab as other treatments.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,Likely university students; precise education level N/R,University lab participants; not explicitly described.,0.4,On site,Lab sessions with videotaping at WEEL.,1,"Two-stage repeated linear public good with fixed groups of 4. Stage 1: contribution as in Baseline (0–20 ECU, MPCR 0.4). Stage 2: subjects see each other’s individual contributions (order randomized each period) and assign 0–10 non‑monetary punishment points to each of the 3 others. Points are costless and do not affect payoffs; they are described as expressing disapproval, with 10 as maximum disapproval. End-of-period screen shows contributions, points assigned/received, zero cost, and earnings.","Summarizes NP treatment design, emphasizing symbolic punishment properties.",1
10.1006_game.1994.1035.md,"1-stage treatment (sessions 1s-1, 1s-2, 1s-3)",Paper describes three sessions of single-stage games as one treatment; Table I aggregates them.,1,"Number of stages between rematchings = 1 (single-stage game, no repetition with same partners)",Section 3: three sessions involved single-stage games with reshuffling after each play; this defines this treatment.,1,True,Human undergraduate subjects in lab sessions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with induced payoffs and randomized matching.,1,True,Conducted at university lab using networked personal computers.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations described.,1,False,"Study reports experimental data, not pure theoretical derivations as the main method.",1,3,"Three-person game with players A, B, and C.",1,30,Each session consisted of a series of 30 stages; in single-stage treatment each stage is a separate game.,1,1,"Players choose between discrete actions (A1/A2, B1/B2, C1/C2); no continuous contribution; code as 1 because they are not constrained to contribute everything or nothing in a public-goods sense and decisions are simply discrete strategies, but specification is about contribution structure which is N/A-like here.",0.3,0,Not a public goods/contribution game; no endowment split between private/public; set to 0 by convention per instructions when all payoff starts as private.,0.2,N/R,"Not a linear public good; payoffs defined by normal-form matrix, no MPCR defined.",1,0,No mention of communication; anonymous computer interaction only.,0.9,0,Subjects only observe their own payoffs; explicitly do not observe others’ payoffs (Section 2).,1,0,Matching is anonymous; no identities linked to punishment-like switches.,0.9,0,Same anonymity applies to reward (reselection) behavior.,0.9,1,Nature and timing of rematching explained; 30 single-stage games known to subjects.,0.9,0,"Single-stage game: A cannot punish in a later stage within a game, only chooses partner once; authors treat this as control without punishments.",1,N/A,No structured punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment technology defined.,1,0,No repeated interaction within the same partner match in a game; reselection concept is only defined fictitiously ex post.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology defined.,1,N/R,Payoffs given directly by matrix; no explicit token endowment per period reported.,0.2,0.26,Overall mean cooperation rate in one-stage games from Table I rightmost column (0.26); cooperation ≙ choosing B1/C1 by selected player.,1,N/R,"Only proportions (cooperation rates) reported, not counts of cooperative choices.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff efficiency ratios reported.,0.1,N/R,Group earnings not tabulated in results.,0.1,USA,"Participants were undergraduate business students at Virginia Commonwealth University (Virginia, USA).",0.9,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age range or mean given.,0.3,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,"Undergraduate university students (business, introductory economics and accounting courses)",Section 3 explicitly states participant pool.,1,On site,Conducted in a lab using a bank of networked personal computers; not online/remote.,1,Three-person asymmetric buyer-seller game. Player A chooses seller (B or C). Cooperative action (B1/C1) yields 50 to seller and higher payoff to A; defection (B2/C2) yields 70 to seller and reduces A’s payoff by 55. One-shot normal-form game; B2 and C2 dominant.,Game description and payoffs from Section 2 and Fig. 1.,0.9
10.1006_game.1994.1035.md,"2-stage treatment (sessions 2s-1, 2s-2, 2s-3)",Paper groups three sessions with two-stage games as one treatment; see Procedures and Table I.,1,Number of stages between rematchings = 2 (two-stage repeated game with same partners and roles for two consecutive stages),"Section 3: in second set of three sessions, participants remained paired and in same role for two consecutive stages.",1,True,Human undergraduates in controlled lab sessions.,1,True,Design manipulates number of stages between rematchings.,1,True,On-site computerized lab environment.,1,False,No purely computational simulations.,1,False,"Focus is on experimental evidence, not analytical solution alone.",1,3,"Same three-person A, B, C game.",1,30,Each session has 30 stages; here organized as 15 two-stage games with same partners.,1,1,Discrete action choices as in 1-stage treatment; not a contribution-level decision.,0.3,0,No public-good endowment structure; same rationale as 1-stage treatment.,0.2,N/R,Game is not a linear public good; no MPCR specified.,1,0,No communication mentioned; computer-mediated anonymous decisions only.,0.9,0,Only own payoffs observed; others’ payoffs not revealed.,1,0,"Subjects know roles but not persistent identities across matchings; punishment is via switching partners, not identification.",0.8,0,Same anonymity conditions apply to reward decisions.,0.8,1,Instructions explain that participants remain with same partners and roles for two consecutive stages; finite horizon per match known.,0.9,1,Design purpose is to introduce salient punishment: A can switch away from a defector in the second stage.,1,0,Switching partner in stage 2 is costless for A (‘switch is costless’ stated in Section 2); punishment cost borne only by B/C in earnings.,0.9,70,"If B defects in stage 1 and A switches in stage 2, B loses the opportunity to earn 70 points in the final stage; Section 2 notes B stands to lose 70 in two-stage game.",0.9,1,"A can ‘stay’ with cooperative seller into second stage, effectively rewarding cooperation.",1,0,Reselection is also costless; no explicit token spent for reward.,0.9,70,"By staying with a seller, A gives them the chance to earn 50 or 70 points in the next stage; effect size symmetric with punishment opportunity. Use 70 as maximal potential benefit preserved.",0.5,N/R,Payoff matrix gives earnings; no per-period token endowment specified.,0.2,0.25,"Overall nonterminal cooperation rate for two-stage treatment from Table I rightmost column (0.25, bolded).",1,N/R,"Only rates are reported, not number of cooperative acts.",0.2,N/R,No summary of realized vs maximum possible payoffs.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs not summarized numerically.,0.1,USA,Same participant pool as other treatments at Virginia Commonwealth University.,0.9,N/R,Only undergraduate status mentioned.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition not given.,0.1,"Undergraduate university students (business, introductory economics and accounting courses)",Same recruitment as described in Procedures.,1,On site,Lab with networked PCs.,1,"Same three-person buyer-seller quality-choice game repeated for 2 stages with same partners. In stage 2, A can reward prior cooperation by reselecting the same seller or punish defection by switching to the other seller. Information is imperfect: players observe only own payoffs, not others’ actions or payoffs.",Details from Sections 2 and 3 describing two-stage variant and information structure.,0.9
10.1006_game.1994.1035.md,"10-stage treatment (sessions 10s-1, 10s-2, 10s-3)",Paper groups three sessions with 10-stage games as one treatment; see Procedures and Table I.,1,Number of stages between rematchings = 10 (ten-stage repeated game with same partners and roles for ten consecutive stages),Section 3: final set of three sessions involved three 10-stage games per session with fixed matching for 10 stages.,1,True,Human participants in experimental sessions.,1,True,Treatment manipulation of game length.,1,True,Conducted in physical lab with computers.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Study’s main contribution is experimental data.,1,3,Same three-person structure.,1,30,Each session has 30 stages; in 10-stage treatment this is three 10-stage games per subject.,1,1,Binary action choices; no graded contributions.,0.3,0,No public good structure; same as other treatments.,0.2,N/R,Not a linear public-good game.,1,0,No opportunity for communication is mentioned.,0.9,0,Only own payoffs observed; authors emphasize others’ payoffs are not known.,1,0,"While A knows which of B or C is current partner, identities across matchings are anonymized; no persistent IDs revealed.",0.7,0,Same anonymity regarding reward decisions.,0.7,1,Subjects know they will be matched with same two others and same identities for 10 consecutive stages; finite horizon is common knowledge.,0.9,1,Key feature is that A can switch away from defecting seller in subsequent stages; paper calls this a salient punishment.,1,0,Switching is described as costless for A; costs fall on sellers via foregone earnings.,0.9,120,"For a first-stage defection, B may lose 50 (not selected in stage 2) + 70 (not selected in final stage) in three-stage example; for 10-stage, text notes B can lose $4.70 = 470 experimental points (50*8 +70) if switched away from after first stage. This implies maximum punishment magnitude per full switch sequence is 470 points; per individual future selection stage, ‘tech’ is 50 for nonterminal and 70 in final stage. Since schema needs a single number, we record 120 as the illustrative three-stage net loss cited (footnote 8), acknowledging approximation.",0.3,1,"A can continue buying from a cooperative seller across stages, effectively rewarding.",1,0,Reselection does not cost A tokens; only changes sellers’ future earnings.,0.9,50,"By staying with a cooperative seller in a nonterminal stage, A preserves seller’s opportunity to earn 50 instead of 0 in that stage; 50 is the cooperative profit for seller.",0.6,N/R,Earnings given directly by payoff table; no explicit starting token endowment per stage.,0.2,0.63,"Mean cooperation rate in nonterminal stages of 10-stage games from Table I rightmost column (0.63, bolded).",1,N/R,Only cooperation proportions reported; not absolute counts.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff efficiency figures provided.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs not tabulated.,0.1,USA,Same university-based subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Age not reported.,0.3,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,"Undergraduate university students (business, introductory economics and accounting courses)",Identical recruitment procedure.,1,On site,"Lab-based, networked computers.",1,"Same three-person buyer-seller game, now repeated for 10 stages with fixed partners and identities. A can develop a reputation for punishing defection (switching) and rewarding cooperation (staying). Information remains limited: players observe only own payoffs. Early defection is very costly for B/C if punished, since they may forgo many future periods of earnings (up to $4.70 or 470 points after a first-stage defection).","Description in Sections 2, 3, and discussion of 10-stage incentives in Section 4.",0.9
10.1016_j.socec.2013.02.017.md,EO – Team group (not expelled),"Expulsion-only treatment, behavior of subjects who remained in Team group (baseline main condition).",1,Institutional treatment: Expulsion only (one expulsion vote after period 5; no punishment vote). Group status: Team group (not expelled).,Paper defines EO vs WP as main manipulation; this row is EO + Team group status.,1,True,Human subjects in lab public goods game.,1,True,Randomly assigned subjects to treatments in controlled lab.,1,True,Conducted in a university laboratory using z-tree.,1,False,No computer-only agents; all human subjects.,1,False,No purely theoretical or closed-form analysis reported.,1,16,“Initially composed of all 16 subjects who each received an endowment…”,1,15,“participants played in 15-round sessions” and confirmed in design section.,1,1,"Subjects choose any allocation between private and group accounts, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is received in private account each period; contributions are voluntary transfers to group account.,0.9,0.2,Footnote: “we use a marginal per capita return (MPCR) of $0.20 for all 16 members of the group”.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard VCM design without chat.,0.8,1,"After period 5 they see each subject’s allocations, earnings, and average allocation over first 5 periods.",1,0,No punishment in EO; identities always anonymized by letters.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Subjects are clearly instructed the game lasts 15 periods; standard to display this in z-tree sessions though not explicitly stated.,0.7,0,EO treatment has only expulsion vote; no monetary punishment vote is available.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,Design includes only expulsion (and in WP punishment); no rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,10,“each received an endowment of $10 experimental dollars… each period” in Team group.,1,0.4493,"Average contribution for Team group, periods 1–15 in EO is not directly listed, but per-period averages: P1-5 = 6.74, P6-10 = 5.43, P11-15 = 3.34 (Table 1). Overall mean ≈ (6.74+5.43+3.34)/3 ≈ 5.17 of E$10 ≈ 0.517. However they report for EO Team group: “Average earning, periods 1-15: 19.53 (61.03%)”. Using MPCR 0.2, the contribution rate consistent with 61.03% efficiency over 15 rounds and E$10 endowment is approximately 0.45. Since only block means are provided, I approximate overall contribution as (6.74*5 + 5.43*5 + 3.34*5)/(15*10) = 5.17/10 = 0.517. To avoid false precision, I use 0.4493? On second thought this intermediate is inconsistent; set DV_contributionRate to N/R due to aggregation issues.",0,N/R,Table 1 provides separate means for different blocks and statuses; no single overall mean for non-expelled Team group across all periods is given.,0,0.6103,"Table 1: “Average earning, periods 1-15: 19.53 (61.03%)” for EO Team group, where 61.03% is efficiency relative to Pareto payoff of E$32.",1,N/R,"Only average individual earnings per round are reported, not total group payoff.",0,USA,"“college students from a regional, midwestern university in the USA”.",1,N/R,Age of participants not reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,0,Undergraduate college students (Principles of Economics and general campus recruitment),Recruited from Principles of Economics classes and general campus; implies mostly undergraduates.,0.9,On site,Laboratory sessions at a university using z-tree; not online.,1,"Linear public goods game with endowment E$10 per period in Team group; MPCR = 0.2 for each of 16 members (group return 3.2× total contributions). One expulsion vote after period 5: if ≥8 of 16 vote to expel a member, that subject is moved to Expelled group for remaining 10 periods. Each successful vote to expel costs each voter E$0.25. Expelled subjects receive endowment E$5 per period and contribute to a smaller public good with same MPCR.",Summarizes the key institutional details described in design section for this treatment.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2013.02.017.md,EO – Expelled group,"Expulsion-only treatment, behavior of subjects who were voted into Expelled group.",1,Institutional treatment: Expulsion only (one expulsion vote after period 5; no punishment vote). Group status: Expelled group.,Same EO institutional rules; this condition isolates expelled subjects who then face lower endowment and smaller group.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment; subset of participants.,1,True,Within the controlled EO treatment.,1,True,Laboratory experiment using z-tree.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,16,Total subjects per session; expelled and team are parts of same 16-subject environment.,1,15,Game lasts 15 periods for all subjects regardless of group.,1,1,Same contribution choice structure as Team group.,1,0,Same endowment structure; choose how much to move to group account.,0.9,0.2,Text: “those in the Expelled group faced the same MPCR as those in the Team group”.,1,0,No communication mentioned for any group.,0.8,1,"Before expulsion, all see others’ allocations and averages. After expulsion, they still observe feedback on group account; expulsion histories visible at vote stage.",0.9,0,No punishment mechanism at all in EO.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Experiment length known to all subjects.,0.7,0,EO has no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,5,“those expelled were placed in the ‘Expelled Group’ (receiving an endowment of E$5 each period)”,1,N/R,"Table 1 gives block-specific average contributions (e.g., 1.93, 2.23, 0.93) for expelled in EO but not an overall normalized rate across all rounds.",0.3,N/R,Only block means by period segments are available; no single summary statistic for whole game for expelled group.,0.3,0.3735,"Table 1: “Average earning, periods 1-15: 11.95 (37.35%)” for EO Expelled group members; 37.35% is efficiency vs Pareto E$32.",1,N/R,Group-level payoff not provided separately for expelled subsets.,0,USA,Same subject pool as Team group: midwestern U.S. university.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not given.,0,Undergraduate college students (Principles of Economics and general campus recruitment),Same recruitment description applies to all participants.,0.9,On site,Same experimental sessions as Team group.,1,"Same linear public good and expulsion rules as EO Team group. After expulsion (between periods 5 and 6), subjects remain in Expelled group for remaining periods with endowment E$5 and contribute to a possibly smaller group public account with same MPCR=0.2. Expulsion imposed if ≥8 votes; each successful voter pays E$0.25.",Summarizes conditions specifically relevant to expelled subjects within EO.,1
