te likely typo in table where percentage matches value; take 13.42% as reported efficiency.)",0.7,N/R,Group-level payoff not given.,0,USA,Same subject pool.,1,N/R,Not provided.,0,N/R,Not provided.,0,Undergraduate college students (Principles of Economics and general campus recruitment),Same recruitment process.,0.9,On site,Same lab experiment context.,1,"Expelled group members are removed from Team group after majority expulsion vote following period 5; each voter against them pays E$0.25 when expulsion passes. Expelled members receive E$5 per period, contribute to a smaller public good game with same MPCR=0.2 and no access to the later punishment vote that applies to Team group members.",Summarizes institutional environment specifically for expelled subjects in WP treatment.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2015.07.032.md,Version I – population with T isolate groups (analytical condition for CP under low mutation),"The paper’s first main model (version I) analyzes parochial altruism (CP) in a population divided into T isolate groups, deriving conditions for CP to be favored under low mutation.",0.9,Number of groups T in a well-mixed population of isolate groups; mutation regime fixed to low mutation (μ→0); strategy considered is parochial altruism (CP).,The main parameter varied in this part is T (number of groups) while focusing on low mutation and CP’s stationary abundance.,0.9,False,"The study uses an evolutionary game-theoretic model with Moran process, no human participants.",1,False,No controlled lab/field experiment; purely theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory implementation; only analytical derivations.,1,False,This section presents analytical conditions and symbolic calculations; no numerical/agent-based simulation results are described for Version I.,0.9,True,Derives closed-form conditions for CP to be favored using fixation probabilities and weak selection theory.,1,200,They state an example figure with N=200 (Fig. 2) and results generally for large N; use 200 as concrete population size where numerical abundances are illustrated.,0.6,1,Moran process is generational; interactions occur per generation. There is no repeated-stage game in the usual sense; treat as one-shot interaction per pair.,0.4,1,"Prisoner’s dilemma and punishment games are discrete action games (C/D, P/NP) rather than contributions on a continuum; players choose one of two pure actions each encounter.",0.9,N/A,No endowment allocation or continuous contributions; only binary cooperate/defect and punish/non-punish.,1,N/R,No public goods or linear contribution mechanism; hence MPCR is not defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocols.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human participants.,1,N/A,No identities in a behavioral experiment sense; only strategy-level interactions in a model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identity information for humans.,1,N/A,Not an implemented experiment with a user interface.,1,1,Out-group game is a punishment game with strategies P and N_P; punisher pays cost α to impose fine γ.,1,N/R,They specify cost parameter α but not as ‘cost per coin’ in experimental units; mapping to a numeric cost per punishment unit is conceptual only.,0.3,N/R,They specify fine γ but not in discrete units (per coin); only inequality γ>α.,0.3,0,Only punishment (negative payoff) is modeled; no positive rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No rewards defined in the model.,1,N/R,"Game payoffs b, c, α, γ are defined directly as payoffs/costs, not from an explicit endowment.",0.2,N/R,"Outcome variables are fixation probabilities/abundances and analytical conditions, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No token-based contributions; only binary C/D choices.,1,N/R,"They discuss abundance and conditions for selection, not total efficiency or payoff relative to maximum.",1,N/R,Group-level payoff statistics are not reported; only symbolic payoffs and selection conditions.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure theoretical evolutionary model without empirical implementation.,1,"Finite well-mixed population of size N subdivided into T isolate groups, each with a tag; in-group interactions: 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs (C,C): b−c, (C,D): −c, (D,C): b, (D,D): 0 where b>c>0; out-group interactions: symmetric punishment game with actions P and N_P, payoffs (P,P): −α−γ, (P,N_P): −α, (N_P,P): −γ, (N_P,N_P): 0 where γ>α>0. Evolutionary dynamics follow a frequency-dependent Moran process with mutation μ and weak selection ω→0; strategies: CP, CNP, DP, DNP distinguished by in-group cooperation and out-group punishment behavior.",Summarizes the specified game structure and evolutionary dynamics of Version I.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2015.07.032.md,Version I – population with T isolate groups (analytical condition for CP under high mutation),Same Version I structure but analyzing the regime of high mutation (μ→1) for CP’s abundance.,0.9,Number of groups T in a well-mixed population of isolate groups; mutation regime fixed to high mutation (μ→1); strategy considered is parochial altruism (CP).,The independent variation here is T while focusing on high mutation effects in Version I.,0.9,False,"Analytical evolutionary model, no human subjects.",1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab-based implementation.,1,False,"Results are analytic inequalities for high mutation, not simulations.",0.9,True,"Derives condition (T−1)α+c<0 and notes it is never satisfied under b>c, γ>α.",1,200,They illustrate with N=200 in Fig. 2 for the same structural model; population size is treated as large.,0.6,1,"As in Version I, single-stage pairwise interactions per generation within the Moran process.",0.4,1,Actions restricted to C/D and P/N_P.,0.9,N/A,No continuous contributions or initial allocation.,1,N/R,Model is not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,"No humans, no communication.",1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No identities of human players.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,Not an implemented experiment.,1,1,Same punishment game structure as in low mutation case.,1,N/R,Cost parameter α defined symbolically; no mapping to experimental units.,0.3,N/R,Fine γ is symbolic; no discrete per-unit technology described.,0.3,0,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment-based payoffs.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes described in terms of strategy abundances, not contributions.",1,N/R,No token contributions defined.,1,N/R,They do not compute efficiency measures.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoffs reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is a theoretical evolutionary analysis.,1,"Same Version I structure: N agents in a well-mixed Moran process, subdivided into T isolate groups. Interaction rules and payoff matrices identical to low-mutation case. This condition focuses on the high mutation limit where each strategy appears with similar frequency and CP is analyzed via H_CP.",Clarifies that only mutation regime changes; game and dynamics remain the same.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2015.07.032.md,Version I – population with T isolate groups (analytical condition for CP under arbitrary mutation),"Intermediate mutation regime for Version I, combining low and high mutation results to obtain a general condition G_CP>0.",0.9,Number of groups T; mutation rate μ (arbitrary between 0 and 1); strategy considered: CP in Version I.,"Condition depends jointly on T and μ, with N and parameters b,c,α,γ.",0.9,False,"Purely analytical model, no empirical data.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory environment.,1,False,"The arbitrary μ results are derived symbolically via linear combination formula, not from simulations.",0.9,True,Uses expression G_CP=4T L_CP + Nμ H_CP and solves for b threshold.,1,200,"They use N=200 as an illustrative value when plotting strategy abundance, within this same model.",0.6,1,Single-stage interactions per generation; evolutionary time is continuous over generations.,0.4,1,Actions are discrete C/D and P/N_P.,0.9,N/A,No contribution endowment framing.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,N/A,No interface/feedback to subjects.,1,N/A,No agents with observable IDs in experimental sense.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Not a finite-round experiment.,1,1,Punishment game for out-group interactions remains part of the model.,1,N/R,"Only symbolic α given, not in coin units.",0.3,N/R,Fine γ given symbolically.,0.3,0,No rewarding actions exist.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments are not defined; payoffs are direct.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes are selection conditions, not contribution measures.",1,N/R,No continuous contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not computed.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoffs reported.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,This is a theoretical analytical treatment.,1,"Same Version I structure. Arbitrary mutation condition for CP: CP is favored if b>max(c,[(T+1)/(T−1)+Nμ]c+[1+Nμ(T−1)]α−γ). For small μ below a threshold, CP can be favored if b>c and c lies in a parameter-dependent interval; for larger μ the threshold on b increases.",Summarizes the key analytical expression referencing μ and T.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2015.07.032.md,"Version II – population with T groups, individuals may have multiple tags (CP(T,i) under low mutation)","The second model version allows individuals to belong to multiple groups; Table 2 and accompanying text give low-mutation conditions for CP(T,i).",0.9,"Number of groups T; number of tags i an individual carries (1 ≤ i ≤ T−1), considering parochial altruism strategy CP(T,i) under low mutation (μ→0).",The main structural variables here are T and the affiliation level i.,0.9,False,"No human data, only analytical derivations.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab-based implementation.,1,False,"Table 2 and general formula for g2(T,i) result from algebraic analysis, not numerical simulation runs.",0.9,True,"Uses multi-phenotype payoff matrices and conditions from Antal et al. (2009) to derive when CP(T,i) is favored.",1,N/R,They mention “large population size” and use N symbolically; no specific numeric N is fixed in Version II results.,0.4,1,"As before, interactions are pairwise games in each generation; no explicit repeated-game structure.",0.4,1,Action sets remain binary (C/D for in-group; P/N_P for out-group).,0.9,N/A,No endowment allocation.,1,N/R,"Prisoner’s dilemma and punishment games, not a linear public good.",1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,N/A,No displays to human subjects.,1,N/A,No explicit agent identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Not a human experiment.,1,1,Out-group punishment game P vs N_P is still the interaction for pairs with no common tag.,1,N/R,"α is symbolic, not tied to concrete units.",0.3,N/R,"γ is symbolic, no per-unit description.",0.3,0,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Results emphasize whether CP(T,i) is favored, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,No continuous contributions modeled.,1,N/R,No efficiency metrics computed.,1,N/R,No numeric group payoffs are provided.,1,N/A,"The study is theoretical, with no participants.",1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Version II is an analytical evolutionary model.,1,"Version II generalizes Version I by allowing individuals to possess multiple group tags. Two individuals are in-group members if they share at least one tag; otherwise they are out-group members. In-group interactions use the same 2-player PD (payoff matrix with b,c); out-group interactions use the same punishment game (α,γ). Each base strategy (CP, CNP, DP, DNP) has 2^T−1 phenotypes corresponding to different tag sets. For low mutation and large N, CP(T,i) is favored if b>c when c∈(0,(γ−α)/(g2(T,i)−1)) and b>g2(T,i)c+α−γ otherwise, where g2(T,i)=(2^{T+1}−2)/(2^{T−i}−1)−1.",Summarizes Version II game structure and the key low-mutation condition mentioned in the results.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2015.07.032.md,"Version II – population with 2 groups (CP(2,1) under low mutation)","Specific case of Version II with T=2 and individuals with one tag, with explicit low-mutation condition (7) for CP(2,1).",0.95,"Number of groups T=2; affiliation level i=1 tag; mutation regime low (μ→0); strategy CP(2,1).","Text explicitly labels CP(2,1) and gives its condition under low mutation.",1,False,Analytical evolutionary model only.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,Theoretical work.,1,False,"Condition (7) is derived analytically, not via simulations.",0.95,True,"Uses weak-selection, large-N analysis for a specific T and i.",1,N/R,Only ‘large population size’ is specified; no N given for this Version II case.,0.4,1,Same generational interaction framework as other model parts.,0.4,1,Binary actions C/D and P/N_P.,0.9,N/A,No contribution-based payoffs.,1,N/R,No public good.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,No identities of human punishers.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Model-only.,1,1,Same punishment game as the general Version II model.,1,N/R,α defined symbolically only.,0.3,N/R,γ defined symbolically only.,0.3,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit endowment specification.,0.2,N/R,"Outcome is whether CP(2,1) is favored, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,Binary cooperation game; no amount variable.,1,N/R,No efficiency metrics.,1,N/R,No numeric group-level payoffs reported.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,"The CP(2,1) case is part of the analytical Version II model.",1,"For T=2, individuals may have one tag (Y or M) or both (YM). A CP(2,1) individual cooperates with in-group partners and punishes out-group partners but belongs to only one group. Low-mutation condition for CP(2,1) to be favored: b>c if c∈(0,(γ−α)/4), else b>5c+α−γ.",Summarizes the case-specific condition (7) directly quoted in the text.,1
