10.1016_j.amc.2018.02.017.md,"Analytical and numerical game-theoretical model of a marketing cooperative in fisheries (continuous-time, real-time delivery, general case)",The paper presents a unified analytical normal-form game plus coupled dynamic system (fishing effort + strategy dynamics) as the main model; it is not split into separate experimental conditions but is one general model analyzed theoretically and via simulations.,0.9,"Penalty rate β (≥1), oligopoly effect parameter b (small), time-dependent catches L_i(t), members’ strategies x_i(t)∈[0,1], cooperative’s punishment strategy y(t)∈[0,1]","These are the central parameters and strategic variables that are varied or analyzed in the model equations; β and b are key exogenous parameters, x_i and y are the strategic choices.",0.9,False,No human subjects or empirical data are used; the work is purely theoretical plus numerical simulation of differential equations.,1,False,There is no controlled experiment in the empirical sense; only mathematical modeling and simulations.,1,False,No lab or field setting with human participants is involved.,1,True,The paper explicitly states that simulations illustrating the theoretical study have been programmed in Matlab; all results come from simulated dynamics of the model.,1,True,"The core of the paper is formal game-theoretic and dynamical-systems analysis (e.g., proofs of theorems, closed-form solutions of the fishing model).",1,4,"Example 1 explicitly considers n=3 member enterprises plus the cooperative as player n+1, giving 4 players in the illustrative simulations; the general model uses generic n but the concrete parameterized examples use n=3.",0.8,1,"The underlying game is a continuous-time or one-shot normal-form game (strategies x_i in [0,1], y in [0,1]); there is no discrete repeated-round structure like in standard public goods games. Represented as a single-stage game with continuous strategy adaptation.",0.7,1,"Each member’s strategy x_i can take any real value in [0,1], meaning continuous contribution choice rather than all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,"There are no human players and no initial token endowments in accounts; the model is about production and marketing quantities, not allocations from an initial endowment.",1,N/R,The model is not a standard linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined or derivable in the usual sense.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication stages exist in the model.,1,N/A,Information-display details for human subjects are irrelevant in this purely theoretical/simulation model.,1,N/A,There is no notion of identity revelation to human subjects; punishment is modeled as a parameterized penalty term.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identity display to human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects and no round-based interface.,1,1,"The cooperative can punish unfaithful members with penalty proportional to extra revenue, with rate βy(q(t,x)−p); β≥1 is the penalty parameter.",1,N/R,The model specifies payoffs directly including penalty terms but does not define a separate cost per punishment ‘coin’ to the punisher in the public-goods sense.,0.6,N/R,Punishment intensity is parameterized by β and y in continuous payoff functions; it is not described as a discrete technology with a per-unit conversion rate from cost to inflicted loss.,0.6,0,Only penalties for unfaithfulness are modeled; there is no positive reward mechanism mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,There are no per-period monetary or token endowments; players’ quantities are production outputs L_i(t) from the fishing model.,1,N/R,"While x_i represents the fraction of production sold to the cooperative, the paper does not report outcome statistics (means, distributions) over x_i as data; only qualitative convergence properties are discussed.",0.7,N/R,"No empirical or numerical summary (e.g., average L_i(t)x_i) is reported as a data outcome; figures are qualitative.",0.7,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum) is computed or reported.,1,N/R,"No aggregate payoff statistics across simulations are tabulated; only functional forms and qualitative behavior (e.g., stability of (1,1)) are presented.",0.9,N/A,There are no human participants; only firms and the cooperative exist in the model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical and simulation-based without human subjects.,1,"Multi-person normal-form game between n fishing enterprises (members) and one cooperative. Members choose x_i∈[0,1] as the fraction of their time-dependent production L_i(t) sold to the cooperative at contracted price p; the remainder is sold on the free market at price q(t,x)=a−b∑_j L_j(t)(1−x_j). The cooperative chooses punishment intensity y∈[0,1]; unfaithful members (x_i<1) incur penalty proportional to their extra revenue, with rate β≥1. Payoffs: for member i, f_i(t,x,y)=L_i(t){(p−c)x_i+(1−x_i)[q(t,x)−c−βy(q(t,x)−p)]}; for the cooperative, f_{n+1}(t,x,y)=βy(q(t,x)−p)∑_j L_j(t)(1−x_j). Fishing production L_i(t)=E_iρ_i z_i(t) arises from a logistic fishing effort model z_i=r_i z_i(1−z_i/K_i)−E_iρ_i z_i. An attractive solution concept (a special Nash equilibrium with a distinguished player—the cooperative) is used; (x_i=1 for all i, y=1) is shown to be an attractive solution. Coupled partial adaptive dynamics is defined: x_i=x_i(1−x_i)∂f_i/∂x_i and y=y(1−y)∂f_{n+1}/∂y. Theorems show instability of (1,1) when β=1 and stability (global attraction from y≥1/β) when β>1 and b is small. Additionally, a discrete-time delivery variant aggregates catches L_i^m over periods and uses analogous dynamics.","Summarizes the formal game structure, strategies, payoff functions, and dynamic adjustment rules described in Sections 2–5.",1
10.1016_j.amc.2018.02.017.md,"Simulation condition – Example 1 and Theorem 2 (β = 1, weak oligopoly effect, continuous-time, real-time delivery)",Example 1 plus Theorem 2 describe and simulate a specific parameterization with β=1 (limited penalty) showing instability of the attractive solution; this constitutes a distinct simulation condition.,0.95,"Penalty rate β = 1 (limited penalty), oligopoly parameter b = 0.0087 (weak effect), n = 3 members with heterogeneous fishing parameters, initial biomasses z(0) set below or above z* (e.g., (450,450,450) and (970,970,970)), initial strategies x(0)=(0.5,0.7,0.8), y(0)=0.9",These values are explicitly given in Example 1 and are the parameters varied/used to illustrate Theorem 2.,1,False,This is a numerical simulation of differential equations with no human subjects.,1,False,No empirical or controlled behavioral experiment; only simulation of a theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory setting or subjects.,1,True,Example 1 and its figures are generated by Matlab simulations of the coupled fish and strategy dynamics.,1,True,Theorem 2 analytically proves instability for β=1; Example 1 provides numerical illustrations.,1,4,Example 1 specifies three enterprises plus the cooperative as player n+1.,1,1,"The dynamics are continuous in time, not structured as discrete repeated rounds.",0.8,1,"Members choose continuous x_i(t) in [0,1].",1,N/A,No human endowments; quantities are production outputs.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,Identity revelation not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds for human subjects.,1,1,"Penalty term with β=1 is included, though shown to be insufficient for stability.",1,N/R,No separate cost parameter to cooperative is specified beyond payoff formula.,0.6,N/R,Penalty formulation is continuous; no discrete tech ratio is defined.,0.6,0,Only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No token endowments in a public-goods sense.,1,N/R,"The simulation illustrates trajectories of x_i(t) moving away from 1, but no summary statistics are reported.",0.7,N/R,No numeric outcome tables; only plots.,0.7,N/R,Efficiency is not computed or reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff time series are not tabulated.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Mathematical model and computer simulation only.,1,"Specific parameters: n=3 members. Fishing dynamics: z_i=r_i z_i(1−z_i/K_i)−E_iρ_i z_i, with K_1=1100, K_2=1000, K_3=1050; r_1=0.25, r_2=0.21, r_3=0.23; ρ_1=0.01, ρ_2=0.015, ρ_3=0.013; E_1=2, E_2=1, E_3=3. Penalty parameter β=1 (limited penalty). Market parameters: contracted price p=1.5, import price a=2.5, oligopoly slope b=0.0087. Initial biomasses examples: z(0)=(450,450,450)<z*=(969,952.38,871.96), and z(0)=(970,970,970)>z*. Initial strategies: x(0)=(0.5,0.7,0.8), y(0)=0.9. Strategy dynamics follow the general equations (4.3)-(4.4); under β=1, the equilibrium (x,y)=(1,1) is unstable, with members’ strategies moving away from full faithfulness as shown in Figs. 3 and 5.",Condenses the parameterization given under Example 1 that defines this simulation condition.,1
10.1016_j.amc.2018.02.017.md,"Simulation condition – Example 2 and Theorem 3 (β = 1.15, weak oligopoly effect, continuous-time, real-time delivery)","Example 2 plus Theorem 3 analyze and simulate the case with effective penalty (β>1), using the same base parameters as Example 1 except for β; this defines a distinct condition.",0.95,"Penalty rate β = 1.15 (effective penalty), oligopoly parameter b = 0.0087 (weak effect), same fishing parameters and initial conditions as Example 1 unless otherwise stated, initial (x(0), y(0)) in [0,1]^n × [1/β,1]",The text specifies β=1.15 and that the base parameter system of Example 1 is reused; Theorem 3 assumes initial y≥1/β.,0.95,False,This is a model simulation without human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory setting.,1,True,Figures 6–9 illustrate the dynamics under β>1 using Matlab simulations.,1,True,Theorem 3 provides an analytical convergence proof; Example 2 provides simulations.,1,4,The same 3-member cooperative plus cooperative player model is used.,1,1,"Continuous-time dynamics, no discrete repeated rounds.",0.8,1,"x_i(t) ∈ [0,1] is continuous.",1,N/A,No human endowment structure.,1,N/R,Not a public goods MPCR setting.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No subject interface.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human rounds.,1,1,Penalty term with β=1.15 is present and effective.,1,N/R,No separate unit cost for punishment defined.,0.6,N/R,"Penalty intensity is continuous via β,y but no explicit tech ratio.",0.6,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No public-goods-style endowment.,1,N/R,"While figures show x_i(t) converging to 1, no numerical averages or summary statistics are reported.",0.7,N/R,No numeric tables; only trajectory plots.,0.7,N/R,Efficiency metrics are not provided.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not summarized numerically.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure model simulation.,1,"Same structure as Example 1: three heterogeneous members with parameters K_1=1100, K_2=1000, K_3=1050; r_1=0.25, r_2=0.21, r_3=0.23; ρ_1=0.01, ρ_2=0.015, ρ_3=0.013; E_1=2, E_2=1, E_3=3. Market: p=1.5, a=2.5, b=0.0087. Penalty parameter β increased to 1.15. Initial strategies (x(0),y(0)) chosen in [0,1]^3×[1/β,1]; specific initial values are not fully enumerated but the example uses the base system of Example 1. According to Theorem 3, for any such initial condition, (x(t),y(t)) monotonically increases and converges to (1,1), stabilizing full faithfulness to the cooperative; this behavior is illustrated in Figs. 6–9 for both z(0)<z* and z(0)>z* cases.",Summarizes the specific effective-penalty condition used in Example 2 and Theorem 3.,1
10.1016_j.amc.2018.02.017.md,"Simulation condition – Example 3, real-time delivery (β = 1.15, continuous-time delivery)",Example 3 explicitly distinguishes between a 'real-time delivery' case and a 'discrete-time delivery' case using β=1.15; the real-time version is a distinct simulation condition.,0.95,"Penalty rate β = 1.15, continuous-time 'real-time delivery' of catch (L_i(t)=E_iρ_i z_i(t)), base parameter system as in Example 2",Example 3 states it starts from the basic parameter system of Example 2 with β=1.15 and contrasts real-time vs discrete-time delivery.,0.95,False,Computer simulation only.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab participants.,1,True,"Fig. 10 shows strategy dynamics for real-time delivery, generated via simulation.",1,True,"The behavior (convergence to (1,1)) also follows analytically from Theorem 3.",1,4,Still using the 3 members + cooperative example framework.,0.95,1,"Continuous-time game with strategy dynamics, no discrete rounds.",0.8,1,Continuous strategy spaces for x_i and y.,1,N/A,No human endowment allocated.,1,N/R,Not a public goods MPCR structure.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No subject interface.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human experimental rounds.,1,1,Penalty mechanism with β=1.15 is active.,1,N/R,No discrete cost parameter defined separately.,0.6,N/R,"Penalty defined continuously via β,y; no explicit cost-to-harm ratio.",0.6,0,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,No token endowment.,1,N/R,"Fig. 10 qualitatively shows x_i(t) approaching 1, but no numerical outcome averages are given.",0.7,N/R,No tables of L_i(t)x_i(t).,0.7,N/R,No efficiency statistic reported.,1,N/R,No explicit group payoff series or summary.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The scenario is entirely simulated.,1,"Same base parameters as Example 2 (β=1.15, three heterogeneous members, same r_i, K_i, ρ_i, E_i, p, a, b). Catches are delivered continuously in 'real time', so L_i(t)=E_iρ_i z_i(t) enters directly into the inverse demand q(t,x) and payoff functions at each time t. Strategy dynamics follow (4.3)-(4.4) without time aggregation of L_i. Fig. 10 shows the paths of x_i(t) and y(t) converging more quickly to (1,1) compared to the discrete-time delivery case.",Based on the description in Example 3 and Figure 10.,1
10.1016_j.amc.2018.02.017.md,"Simulation condition – Example 3, discrete-time delivery (β = 1.15, period length Δ = 0.5, M = 10)","Example 3 constructs and simulates a discrete-time delivery version with specified Δ and M, leading to different (slower) dynamics; this is a separate simulation condition.",0.98,"Penalty rate β = 1.15, discrete-time delivery with accumulation periods of length Δ = 0.5, number of periods M = 10 (total horizon T = 5), aggregated catches L_i^m over each period, base parameter system as in Example 2","The text for Example 3 states β=1.15, Δ=0.5, M=10, T=5 and uses the base system of Example 2.",1,False,"Again, this is only numerical simulation.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment with subjects.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Fig. 11 displays simulated strategy dynamics under discrete-time delivery.,1,True,"Construction of the autonomous system for (x^m,y^m,z^m) is analytical; convergence intuition follows from previous theory though no separate theorem is formalized.",0.8,4,Still the same 3 members plus cooperative setting.,0.95,10,"The discrete-time delivery model aggregates behavior over M=10 periods in [0,T]; these can be interpreted as 10 effective decision/update periods.",0.9,1,"Strategies x_i^m and y^m are continuous within [0,1].",1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,Not a standard public goods setup.,1,N/A,No communication stage.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,"No actual human round display; M is part of the model, not a UI element.",1,1,Penalty mechanism persists with β=1.15; only the aggregation of L_i changes.,1,N/R,No discrete cost for punishment specified.,0.6,N/R,No explicit tech ratio for punishment.,0.6,0,No reward in the model.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,No endowment in the sense of a public good game.,1,N/R,"Fig. 11 shows that strategies still tend toward (1,1), but no numeric averages per period are given.",0.7,N/R,No numeric time series of L_i^m x_i^m reported.,0.7,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,No group payoff stats across periods are provided.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely simulated system.,1,"Total catch of member i during period [mΔ,(m+1)Δ] is L_i^m=∫_{mΔ}^{(m+1)Δ}E_iρ_i z_i(t)dt, where z_i(t) follows the fishing effort model. This integral is given in closed form in Appendix formulas (A.5)-(A.6), depending on whether z(0)<z* or z(0)>z*. By substituting L_i^m into the payoff and strategy dynamics, the authors construct an autonomous system for (x^m, y^m, z^m) with update equations: x^m=u(x^m,y^m,z^m), y^m=v(x^m,y^m,z^m), z^m=w(x^m,y^m,z^m). At the end of each accumulation period, the endpoint state becomes the initial condition for the next period. Parameters chosen: Δ=0.5 (half a month), M=10, T=5. Fig. 11 suggests that, in this discrete-time delivery case, strategy dynamics still tends to the attractive solution (1,1), but convergence is slower than under real-time continuous delivery.",Summarizes Example 3’s discrete-time delivery construction and parameterization.,1
