10.1016_j.copsyc.2021.08.033.md,"Hilbe et al. 2014 – human vs. computerized ZD extortion (strong/mild, symmetric PD)","Review describes one economic experiment [11] where human participants play 60-round iterated PD against an extortionate ZD computer strategy (strong or mild extortion). Conditions are variants of the same basic setup; paper text here does not separate them with full parameter details, so they are grouped.",0.5,Type of opponent strategy: extortionate ZD (strong or mild) implemented by a computer; human is always the adapting player.,"Text: “participants were matched with an extortioner… Figure 1… In mild extortion, the extortionate strategy responds with a higher probability of C to the human player's C than in strong extortion.” Independent variation is the extortion parameter (strong vs mild).",0.7,True,Human participants interacted with a programmed strategy in an economic experiment.,1,True,Controlled iterated PD with assigned strategies and payoffs.,1,True,Described as an economic experiment with students; no indication of online or field. Such work is typically lab-based.,0.7,False,"Human-subject experiment, not purely computational, although the opponent is a computer strategy.",1,False,No formal derivations described here; focuses on behavioural experiment.,1,2,Standard Prisoner’s Dilemma with “two players” (human vs computer).,1,60,Text: “after acceding to extortion in the first half of 60 rounds”.,1,0,"Standard PD is a binary cooperate/defect choice, not a continuous contribution.",1,0,Binary C/D PD does not use an endowment contribution frame; effectively no default contribution to public fund.,0.3,N/R,Payoff matrix details (multiplier and group size) are not given in this review excerpt.,0,0,No mention of communication; iterated PD experiments with computer opponents typically prohibit chat.,0.6,0,Only own and opponent payoffs as a function of cooperation are discussed; no indication of additional summaries beyond usual feedback.,0.3,0,No explicit punishment stage or identity revelation; one human and one programmed opponent.,0.9,0,No separate reward mechanism or identity revelation reported.,0.9,0,Text does not specify whether remaining rounds were displayed.,0,0,No explicit punishment action; costly punishment is only implicit via choosing D in the PD.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward option beyond standard PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Specific PD payoff matrix or round endowment not provided.,0,N/R,"Results reported qualitatively (cooperation fractions, payoffs) and in figures not included here; no numeric averages provided in text.",0,N/R,Binary C/D with no token amounts specified.,0,N/R,No aggregate efficiency or payoff ratio reported numerically.,0,N/R,"Only relative and graphical payoffs mentioned, no exact totals.",0,N/R,Not stated in the review; Hilbe et al. 2014 were based at Max Planck (Germany) but participant nationality not specified here.,0,N/R,Not reported in the review text.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Review only notes 'students' elsewhere for another experiment but not explicitly for this one.,0.3,On site,Described as an economic experiment with lab-style design; no indication of online implementation.,0.7,Iterated 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma for 60 rounds against a pre-programmed extortionate zero-determinant strategy (strong or mild). Human subjects initially accede (high cooperation) then reduce cooperation as implicit costly punishment; extortioners always achieve higher payoff at any given human cooperation level.,Summarized directly from section 'Extortion subdues human players but is finally punished in symmetric games' and Figure 1 description.,1
10.1016_j.copsyc.2021.08.033.md,"Becks & Milinski 2019 – T0 symmetric iterated PD (no incentive, egalitarian)","Review describes an experiment [19] with two treatments: T0 (egalitarian, no incentive) and T1 (asymmetric incentive bonus). This row is T0.",0.9,Incentive structure: no extra monetary incentive; symmetric power between players (T0).,"Text: “Under completely symmetric power conditions, an iterated Prisoner's Dilemma (T0) was compared with the same game where an incentive was assigned… (T1).”",1,True,Experiment with student participants.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of bonus incentive in iterated PD.,1,True,"Economic experiment with students, no mention of field or online context.",0.8,False,Human-subject experiment.,1,False,No purely analytical component in described part.,1,2,Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma between two players.,1,N/R,Review does not state the number of rounds for this experiment.,0,0,Standard PD with C/D actions.,1,0,"Binary PD, no public-goods endowment structure.",0.4,N/R,Payoff matrix parameters not stated.,0,0,No mention of communication; iterated PD lab experiments typically disallow chat.,0.6,0,Only own and partner payoffs/cooperation are discussed; no mention of extra summaries.,0.3,0,Two anonymous players with no explicit punishment mechanism.,0.9,0,No explicit reward mechanism separate from PD payoffs.,0.9,0,No information on whether the horizon was shown.,0,0,No costly-punishment stage; only PD choices.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward option beyond PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Specific PD payoffs/endowments are not specified.,0,N/R,"Figure 2c reports cooperation and payoffs graphically, but no numeric summary is in the text.",0,N/R,"Binary PD actions, no token amounts given.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure given.,0,N/R,"Only relative payoffs/regressions plotted, no numeric totals.",0,N/R,Not given in the review; authors are in Germany but participant nationality not stated.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,"Students (likely university-level), more specific level N/R",Text: “An experiment [19] tested with students whether extortioners can be found in reality…”,0.8,On site,"Economic experiment with students, typical lab setting; no hint of online field implementation.",0.8,"Two-player iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with symmetric payoffs and no extra competitive bonus. Participants not informed about ZD strategies. Under these egalitarian conditions, generous ZD-like strategies emerged, letting partners succeed until mutual cooperation was reached; extortion was predicted to be disciplined.",Summarized from section describing T0: “In egalitarian iterated Prisoner's Dilemmas with no incentive (T0) generous ZD strategies prevailed… generous players let their coplayers succeed till both reach mutual cooperation (Figure 2c).”,1
10.1016_j.copsyc.2021.08.033.md,Becks & Milinski 2019 – T1 asymmetric iterated PD (bonus incentive for competitive superiority),"Same experiment [19] as T0, but with asymmetric incentive (bonus) assigned to one randomly chosen player (T1).",1,Incentive structure: one randomly chosen player publicly receives an additional monetary incentive (bonus) for becoming competitively superior (T1).,Text: “the same game where an incentive was assigned in public only to one player chosen randomly (T1)… whether extortion can be favoured by offering one player an incentive to gain extra money if she manages to become competitively superior over her partner.”,1,True,Human students played the game under manipulated incentive conditions.,1,True,Random assignment of incentive; controlled comparison with T0.,1,True,Same economic experiment with students as T0; presumably lab-based.,0.8,False,Human-subject experiment.,1,False,No purely analytical model here.,1,2,Iterated PD between two players; one of them has the bonus incentive.,1,N/R,Number of rounds not given for this experiment.,0,0,Standard PD choices: cooperate or defect.,1,0,Binary PD; no contribution endowment framing.,0.4,N/R,Payoff matrix not reported in this review.,0,0,No mention of communication allowed.,0.6,0,No additional statistics beyond own/group payoffs mentioned.,0.3,0,No explicit punishment stage; only two anonymous players.,0.9,0,"Bonus assignment is public but is not a reward chosen by players, and there is no separate reward action with identity revelation.",0.6,0,Not mentioned if horizon known.,0,0,No explicit punishment option separate from defection.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,There is an exogenous bonus reward offered to one player for achieving competitive superiority.,0.8,N/A,Bonus is exogenous; players do not pay to bestow rewards.,1,N/A,No per-unit reward technology; fixed external bonus.,1,N/R,Per-round payoffs or starting endowments not detailed.,0,N/R,Figure 2d shows cooperation vs payoff but no textual numeric values.,0,N/R,Actions are C/D; no numeric token contributions provided.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency statistics reported.,0,N/R,"Only relative/regression payoffs in Figure 2b,d.",0,N/R,Review does not state nationality.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,"Students (likely university-level), more specific level N/R",Same participant pool as T0: students.,0.8,On site,Same lab-based economic experiment as T0.,0.8,"Two-player iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with one randomly selected player receiving a public bonus incentive for having a higher payoff than the partner. Extortionate strategies frequently emerged: bonus players often adopted extortion, outcompeting coplayers. Extortioners “refused to become disciplined” and did not increase cooperation after partners’ cooperation; in most groups they even decreased cooperation in response to C.","From text: “When one player was assigned an additional monetary incentive (bonus) (T1), he/she often adopts the extortion strategy: extortioners outcompete their coplayers (Figure 2d)… they did, however, not increase to respond with C after their partners' C… instead… even decreased cooperation.”",1
10.1016_j.copsyc.2021.08.033.md,"Hilbe et al. 2016 – asymmetric power PD, treatment without replacement","Hilbe, Hagel & Milinski 2016 [20] described two treatments: 'without replacement' and 'with replacement'. This row is the no-replacement treatment.",1,"Power structure: double player interacts with two single players, but has no option to replace coplayers between blocks (treatment without replacement).","Text distinguishes “the treatment 'without replacement' and the treatment 'with replacement', respectively.” In the former, double players cannot change coplayers.",1,True,Economic experiment with human subjects simulating employer–employee relations.,1,True,Controlled assignment to treatments varying replacement power.,1,True,"Described as an economic experiment; typical for lab setting, no field/online notes.",0.8,False,"Human participants, not purely simulated agents.",1,False,"Focus on behavioural outcomes, not analytical derivations.",1,3,"Text: “a repeated game between two types of players, the so-called double player and two single players.”",1,N/R,Within-block and total rounds not fully specified; only that replacement decisions (in the other treatment) occur every 10 rounds.,0.3,0,Each round is a Prisoner’s Dilemma between double and each single player (C or D).,1,0,"Binary C/D PD, no contribution endowment.",0.4,N/R,Payoff matrix for PD not specified in the review.,0,0,No mention of communication; multi-player PD experiments generally disallow chat.,0.6,1,"Text: “After each round, all subjects including the inactive player are informed about the decisions of all group members including those of the double player.” Implies feedback summary of others' actions; likely also payoffs.",0.8,0,No separate punishment stage or identity-linked sanctions; employer/employee roles are known but there is no punishment mechanism.,0.8,0,No explicit rewarding option; only PD payoffs with role asymmetry.,0.9,1,"Replacement decisions in the other treatment occur every 10 rounds, suggesting block structure and knowledge of 10-round blocks. But total number of blocks not given.",0.4,0,No explicit costly punishment stage; only PD choices.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism; only standard PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Per-round earnings or starting endowment not described.,0,N/R,Review states classification rates (number of extortioners) but not aggregate cooperation fractions in this treatment.,0,N/R,"C/D PD, no token counts specified.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric is given.,0,N/R,"Only relative payoffs (advantages) are described in aggregate, no numbers.",0,N/R,Not specified.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,"Recruitment details (e.g., students vs general public) are not stated in the review.",0,On site,Described as an economic experiment simulating a company; typical for lab setting.,0.8,"Three-person asymmetric iterated PD: one 'double player' (employer) simultaneously plays a PD with each of two 'single players' (employees) each round, choosing possibly different actions toward each; single players only interact with the double player. In the 'without replacement' treatment, the double player cannot replace employees over time. Extortion classification criteria: (i) double’s cooperation rate at least 10 percentage points lower than average of single players; (ii) double’s strategy incentivizes coplayers to cooperate. In this treatment, only one double player met extortion criteria, indicating limited extortion when replacement power is absent.","From text: description of company simulation, roles, and note that “In the treatment without replacement, only one participant… satisfied both criteria.”",1
10.1016_j.copsyc.2021.08.033.md,"Hilbe et al. 2016 – asymmetric power PD, treatment with replacement","Second treatment in Hilbe, Hagel & Milinski 2016 [20], where double players can replace single players every 10 rounds.",1,Power structure: double player can periodically replace one active single player with an inactive one (treatment with replacement).,"Text: “The second treatment is similar except that there are three single players… Every 10 rounds, double players can decide whether they want to continue to play with the two currently active single players or whether they want to choose one… to be replaced by the inactive player… There is the treatment 'without replacement' and the treatment 'with replacement', respectively.”",1,True,Same human-subject experiment as no-replacement treatment.,1,True,Random or controlled assignment to replacement vs no-replacement conditions.,1,True,"Economic experiment simulating labour market, likely lab-based.",0.8,False,Human-subject experiment.,1,False,"Behavioural data only, not analytical derivations.",1,4,"One double player plus three single players, with one single inactive at any time: all four are part of the group.",1,N/R,"Only block size (every 10 rounds there is a replacement decision) is mentioned, not total rounds.",0.3,0,Per-round interactions are PDs (C or D) between double and each active single.,1,0,"PD game, not a contribution game.",0.4,N/R,No payoff matrix provided.,0,0,No communication reported; feedback is via actions/payoffs.,0.6,1,"Text: “After each round, all subjects including the inactive player are informed about the decisions of all group members including those of the double player.”",1,0,No explicit punishment mechanism separate from interaction; only role-based power asymmetry.,0.8,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,0.9,1,"Replacement decisions occur “every 10 rounds”; implies participants are aware of 10-round blocks, though total rounds unknown.",0.5,0,"No costly punishment action; only PD choices and replacement decisions by double player, which is more akin to partner selection than punishment in this coding scheme.",0.8,N/A,No explicit per-unit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit per-unit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward option is described.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Token endowments or payoffs per action are not specified.,0,N/R,Figures 3 and 4 show cooperation rates and payoffs graphically but no numeric averages or exact values in text.,0,N/R,"C/D PD, no token contributions reported.",0,N/R,Efficiency metrics are not reported.,0,N/R,Only relative payoff comparisons (extortioners vs nonextortioners) and regression lines shown.,0,N/R,Not stated.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,No details in the review; likely students but not specified.,0.3,On site,Economic laboratory experiment simulating a company with employer and employees.,0.8,"Asymmetric employer–employees PD with replacement option. One 'double player' simultaneously plays a PD with each of two active 'single players' each round, from a pool of three singles (one inactive at a time). After each 10-round block, the double player can keep the current two singles or replace one with the inactive single. All participants are informed about all group members’ decisions each round. Extortion classification: (i) double’s cooperation rate at least 10% lower than that of single players; (ii) strategy incentivizes coplayers to cooperate (single players’ payoffs increase with their cooperation). In this treatment, almost half of double players were extortionate, and extortioners were less cooperative than nonextortioners but obtained more cooperation from coplayers and higher payoffs. Single players who cooperated less or less than the other single were more likely to be replaced. Extortioners thus amplified coplayers’ incentives to cooperate and captured a disproportionate share of joint payoffs.","Synthesized from description of company simulation, replacement mechanism, extortion classification, and Figures 3 and 4 discussion.",1
