10.1016_j.physa.2022.128097.md,Task 2 – SECRET sanctions (unobservable sanctions),Task 2 (rounds 11–20) in sessions 1 and 2 is called SECRET sanctions treatment with unobservable sanctions.,1,Sanctioning option: available; Feedback on sanctions: delayed until end of experiment (unobservable during play); Treatment: SECRET,Only difference from STANDARD is timing/observability of sanction feedback; sanctions exist but are not revealed until the end.,1,True,Human participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled design with specific sanctioning treatments and regrouping.,1,True,Run at Tilburg University using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation mentioned.,1,False,Focus is empirical; theoretical discussion but no standalone analytical model.,0.9,4,Four-player repeated public goods game.,1,10,Task 2 consists of 10 rounds (rounds 11–20).,1,1,"Contributions xi ∈ {0,…,10}.",1,0,10 tokens per round to allocate between public and private accounts.,0.95,0.4,Same payoff function as in Task 1.,1,0,No communication feature reported.,0.9,1,"After each Stage 1, subjects observe contributions of others in their group.",1,0,Subjects only learn total sanction points received per round at the end; no indication that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.8,0,No reward option exists.,1,1,Subjects are informed they will stay in the new group for 10 rounds of Task 2.,0.9,1,Stage 2 allows assigning deduction points.,1,1,Each sanction point comes from Stage 2 endowment; cost of 1 per point to sender.,1,3,Each assigned point decreases receiver’s payoff by three points.,1,0,No positive payoff-increasing actions toward others described.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"10 tokens for contribution stage, plus an additional 10 tokens S=10 for sanctioning stage (sanction endowment). Endowment field refers to main contribution endowment.",0.95,N/R,"They state subjects achieve more than 25% of maximum group contributions in SECRET treatment with sanctions, but no exact mean fraction or per-condition number.",0.3,N/R,Figure 1 shows averages graphically but no numeric table.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures reported by treatment.,0,N/R,"Average payoffs in Task 2 not provided per treatment, only that they do not differ significantly.",0.1,Netherlands,University in the Netherlands; same subject pool as Task 1.,0.95,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,0,"Bachelor’s and Master’s students in economics, law, and business",Same subject pool description applies to all tasks.,1,On site,Computerized lab at Tilburg University.,1,"Two-stage rounds: Stage 1 contribution decision, then observe all individual contributions; Stage 2 sanctioning using a separate 10-token endowment (S=10); subjects randomly regrouped into new fixed groups of 4 at start of Task 2; anonymous integer labels 1–4 reshuffled each round; sanctions assigned each round but cumulative sanctions per round revealed only after period 20; no strategic reputation effect via feedback within Task 2.",Compiled from Task 2 and SECRET treatment description.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2022.128097.md,Task 1 – No sanctions (pre-phase for STANDARD treatment),Task 1 (rounds 1–10) without sanctions for subjects later assigned to STANDARD sanctions sessions (sessions 3 and 4). Design identical to other Task 1 but different later treatment assignment.,0.95,"Sanctioning option: none; Treatment assignment: STANDARD sessions, pre-sanction phase","Same no-sanction public goods game, but for those who will later be in STANDARD treatment with observable sanctions.",0.9,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled design.,1,True,Conducted on-site in a computer lab.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No standalone analytical model.,0.9,4,Four-player game.,1,10,Task 1 has 10 rounds.,1,1,Contributions 0–10 allowed.,1,0,10 tokens received and allocated between public/private.,0.95,0.4,Same payoff function.,1,0,No communication mentioned.,0.9,1,Subjects observed contributions of all others after each round.,1,0,No punishment exists in Task 1.,1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Participants told they will stay in same group for all 10 rounds.,0.9,0,Without sanctions.,1,N/A,No sanctions.,1,N/A,No sanctions.,1,0,No reward scheme.,0.95,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,10 tokens per round as in Task 1 description.,1,N/R,No separate numeric average for STANDARD pre-phase reported.,0.1,N/R,Only qualitative statement that contributions converge to full free riding over these 10 rounds.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency calculations per phase.,0,N/R,Payoffs not broken down by phase and treatment.,0.1,Netherlands,Same subject pool.,0.95,N/R,No age detail.,0.4,N/R,Not reported.,0,"Bachelor’s and Master’s students in economics, law, and business",Same as for other tasks.,1,On site,University lab experiment.,1,Fixed groups of 4 for 10 rounds; random relabeling each round; neutral wording; observation of entire vector of others’ contributions; this phase precedes introduction of observable sanctions for these subjects.,From Task 1 description and session table.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2022.128097.md,Task 2 – STANDARD sanctions (observable sanctions),Task 2 (rounds 11–20) in sessions 3 and 4 is called STANDARD sanctions treatment with observable sanctions.,1,Sanctioning option: available; Feedback on sanctions: immediate and observable in same round; Treatment: STANDARD,"Independent variable is information about sanctions; here, subjects are informed each round how many sanction points they received.",1,True,Human lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled variation of sanction feedback timing.,1,True,On-site computerized experiment.,1,False,No purely simulated data.,1,False,"Empirical design, no standalone analytical model.",0.9,4,Groups of four.,1,10,Task 2 spans 10 rounds.,1,1,Integer contributions 0–10.,1,0,10-token endowment allocated between public and private each round.,0.95,0.4,Same linear public good payoff function.,1,0,No communication channel described.,0.9,1,Contributions of all group members are shown each round.,1,0,"Standard treatment feedback: subjects learn how many sanctioning points they received, but no indication that identities of senders are revealed.",0.7,0,No reward option.,1,1,Subjects told they will remain in the new group for 10 rounds (Task 2).,0.9,1,Stage 2 allows deduction points in every round.,1,1,Sanction points drawn from Stage 2 endowment S=10; 1 point costs 1.,1,3,Each deduction point decreases receiver’s payoff by 3 points.,1,0,Sanctioning is only payoff-reducing; no rewards described.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,10 tokens in Stage 1 per round for contribution decision; plus 10-token separate sanction endowment S=10 in Stage 2; endowment field refers to main contribution endowment.,0.95,N/R,"They note that contributions are above 50% of maximum group contributions in STANDARD treatment with sanctions and increase over time, but no exact normalized contribution rate.",0.4,N/R,"Numerical group means only shown in figures, not tabulated.",0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios reported.,0,N/R,They only state that individual payoffs in Task 2 do not differ significantly between treatments; no levels given.,0.1,Netherlands,Same lab and subject pool.,0.95,N/R,Not specified.,0.4,N/R,Not reported.,0,"Bachelor’s and Master’s students in economics, law, and business",As in experiment description.,1,On site,Computerized experiment in university lab.,1,"Two-stage repeated public goods game; participants regrouped into new fixed groups of four between Task 1 and Task 2; integer contributions 0–10 in Stage 1; Stage 2: each subject assigns 0–10 deduction points across other members subject to total ≤10; each deduction point costs 1 and reduces recipient by 3; subjects receive feedback each round on total number of deduction points received and resulting payoffs, allowing for strategic (reputation-based) punishment motives.",Summarizes STANDARD treatment details from design section.,1
10.1086_720287.md,hi-S (High social information with sanctioning),Authors define four treatments; hi-S is high social information plus weak social sanctioning (sec. 2.2–2.3).,1,Social information: High (message that many previously chose group account); Sanctioning: Present (voting to exclude a strategy from 1-point bonus).,2×2 design varying social information (high vs low) and sanctioning (with vs without). This row is the high-info plus sanctioning cell.,1,True,Lab-in-the-field experiment with fishermen as participants (sec. 2.5).,1,True,Randomized treatment assignment and controlled payoff structure (sec. 2).,1,False,"Conducted in community centers or at landing sites in villages (lab-in-the-field), not a conventional lab.",1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations described.,1,False,Paper includes a theoretical model but this condition refers to the empirical game with human subjects.,1,3,Three-player prisoner's dilemma; groups of three (sec. 2.1).,1,7,"One-shot procedure plus six additional rounds, described as seven rounds of a repeated one-shot game (sec. 2.1, 2.4).",1,0,Decision is binary: allocate all 4 points to private or all 4 to group; no partial contributions (sec. 2.1).,1,0,Endowment of 4 points is owned by participant and allocated to either private or group account; starts in private control.,0.8,0.6667,"Each 4-point cooperation yields 2 points to each of 3 players (=6 total). MPCR = 6/4/3 = 0.5? But definition here is marginal per capita return per contributed point: 2 points to each player per 4 contributed? Actually 4 points moved to group generate 2 to each player = 6 total, so per 4 contributed: 6/4=1.5 group, per capita 1.5/3=0.5. However standard MPCR in binary PD is payoff increase to each from one unit in group; here each ‘contribution unit’ is 4 points, giving +2 each; per point contributed: 2/4=0.5. So MPCR=0.5; using that.",0.7,0,No communication allowed; explicitly stated (sec. 2.5).,1,1,"After each round, participants see allocation decisions of all group members and total points they earned (sec. 2.3).",1,0,"Sanctioning is anonymous and directed at a strategy, not individuals; group members not identified (sec. 2.3, 2.5).",1,0,"One-point bonus is group-wide mechanism; no identities revealed; only rule and who was excluded are shown, not identities (anonymity and shields, sec. 2.3, 2.5).",0.8,1,"After first round, participants are informed experiment will continue for six additional rounds (sec. 2.4), implying known finite horizon.",1,1,Weak social sanctioning via exclusion from 1-point bonus implemented (sec. 2.3).,1,0,Voting is costless; no deduction from voter’s payoff is mentioned. Sanction is exclusion from bonus to targets only.,0.8,1,"If excluded, target loses 1-point bonus; one unit of ‘sanction’ (majority rule to exclude a strategy) removes 1 point from each excluded player.",0.9,1,Each participant may receive a bonus of one point unless excluded (sec. 2.3).,1,0,Bonus is exogenous; no cost to any player to create or allocate it.,0.9,1,Reward magnitude is 1 point per eligible participant.,0.9,4,Each participant receives an endowment of four points per round (sec. 2.1).,1,0.54,"Average cooperation (choosing group account) in hi-S is 54% over game (abstract and sec. 4.1). With binary all-or-nothing and 4-point endowment, fraction of endowment contributed equals cooperation rate.",0.9,2.16,Each cooperator contributes full 4 points. Average contribution per player per round = 0.54 * 4 = 2.16 points.,0.8,N/R,No explicit or computable report of group payoff relative to social optimum over time.,0,N/R,No raw average group earnings reported by treatment.,0,Tanzania,"Fishermen at Lake Victoria, Tanzania (abstract and sec. 2.5).",1,Mean 37.95 years (SD 12.03; range 18–93).,"Table 3 summarizes age for full sample; treatments are balanced across sessions (sec. 2.5, 2.6).",0.9,Approximately 98% male (12 women out of 588 participants).,Table 3: only 12 women among 588 participants; treatments balanced by random assignment.,0.9,N/R,Paper notes little formal education qualitatively but no distribution or levels reported.,0,Field experiment,Lab-in-the-field in 20 villages at community centers/landing sites (sec. 2.5).,1,"Three-player binary prisoner’s dilemma with 4-point endowment; cooperate = allocate all 4 to group account, yielding 2 points to each group member; defect = allocate all 4 to private account; perfect-stranger matching over 7 one-shot rounds; social information message about previous cooperation; weak sanctioning via majority vote on which strategy (cooperate/defect/none) is excluded from 1-point bonus.",Summary of design details from sections 2.1–2.4.,1
10.1086_720287.md,low-S (Low social information with sanctioning),Authors define low-S as low social information plus weak social sanctioning (sec. 2.2–2.3).,1,Social information: Low (message that many previously chose private account); Sanctioning: Present (voting to exclude a strategy from 1-point bonus).,This is the low-info plus sanctioning cell of the 2×2 design.,1,True,Same field experiment with fishermen participants.,1,True,Part of the randomized controlled experimental design.,1,False,Lab-in-the-field at community locations.,1,False,No computer-only agents.,1,False,Empirical condition with human subjects.,1,3,Same three-player PD as other treatments.,1,7,Identical repeated one-shot structure.,1,0,Binary allocate-all decision as in sec. 2.1.,1,0,Endowment initially under individual control; binary allocation.,0.8,0.5,Same payoff structure; per point contributed yields 0.5 to each player.,0.7,0,No communication allowed in any treatment.,1,1,Participants see allocation decisions and total own payoff each round (sec. 2.3).,1,0,Sanctioning is anonymous and rule-based; no identities revealed.,1,0,Bonus receipt/exclusion reported without identities; anonymity ensured.,0.8,1,Participants told there will be six additional rounds after first.,1,1,Weak social sanctioning included in all -S treatments.,1,0,Voting is costless; only excluded players lose the 1-point bonus.,0.8,1,Exclusion removes 1-point bonus from each excluded member.,0.9,1,Bonus of one point per participant unless excluded.,1,0,"Reward supplied exogenously, no cost to group members.",0.9,1,Eligible participants receive 1 point.,0.9,4,Same 4-point endowment.,1,0.42,Average cooperation in low-S is 42% (abstract and sec. 4.1). Binary all-or-nothing implies contribution fraction equals cooperation rate.,0.9,1.68,Average contribution per player per round = 0.42 * 4 = 1.68 points.,0.8,N/R,No efficiency metric by treatment reported.,0,N/R,No mean group payoff by treatment reported.,0,Tanzania,Same participant pool: Tanzanian Lake Victoria fishermen.,1,Mean 37.95 years (SD 12.03; range 18–93).,Table 3 describes full sample; sessions randomized into treatments.,0.9,Approximately 98% male (12 women out of 588 participants).,Same sample across treatments.,0.9,N/R,No quantitative education breakdown.,0,Field experiment,Lab-in-the-field environment in villages.,1,Same three-player binary prisoner’s dilemma and repeated perfect-stranger matching as hi-S; low social information message says many previously chose private account; weak sanctioning via majority vote on which strategy is excluded from 1-point bonus.,Low-S differs only in social information content from hi-S.,1
