10.1111_j.1467-9779.2011.01519.x.md,Baseline – No Sanction/Reward,"Paper defines three treatments: baseline, sanction, reward. This row corresponds to the baseline treatment without any second-stage punishment or reward option.",1,"Institutional environment: no punishment or reward stage; only investment decisions in X (collective security), Y (national protection), Z (alternative project).",Independent variable is the presence/absence/type of institution. In this treatment there is no sanction or reward mechanism.,1,True,"Human subjects in a lab experiment at CREM, University of Rennes I.",1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and fixed groups.,1,True,Conducted in laboratory setting at CREM with computerized interface (z-Tree).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation mentioned; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Though a theoretical model is presented, this row refers to the experimental treatment with human data.",1,4,Groups of size four are stated explicitly (“group assignments … groups of size four”; model sets n=4).,1,40,Each session had 40 periods; partner matching over 40 periods applies to all treatments.,1,1,"Subjects can choose any integer allocation of 20 tokens across X,Y,Z (0–20 each subject to budget), not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Endowment starts entirely under individual control in private accounts X,Y,Z; nothing is pre-assigned to collective account.",0.9,N/R,Not a standard linear VCM; contributions reduce a probability of loss nonlinearly; no marginal per capita return reported.,1,0,No possibility of communication mentioned; standard computerized anonymous VCM-like protocol.,0.9,1,"After each period, subjects learn occurrence of terrorist attack and whether they personally are hit; implies feedback on payoffs/losses. Exact payoff table visibility isn’t specified, but outcome summaries per period are given.",0.7,0,No punishment stage exists in this treatment; set to 0 per definition.,1,0,No reward stage exists; set to 0.,1,1,"Subjects are told there will be 40 periods, and backward induction arguments rely on common knowledge of finite horizon.",1,0,"Baseline treatment has only first-stage allocation to X,Y,Z; no second-stage punishment.",1,N/A,No punishment available in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment available in baseline.,1,0,No reward mechanism in baseline.,1,N/A,No reward available.,1,N/A,No reward available.,1,20,In each period participants receive d=20 UME to allocate; long-run wealth W=6000 is a separate baseline stock.,1,N/R,"Paper reports average group contributions in tokens for X, Y, Z but not normalized contribution rates per endowment.",0.6,11.32,Table 2 reports average group investment in X per treatment; baseline average=11.32 out of max 80 per group per period.,1,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized vs maximum possible group payoff is provided.,1,3989.16,Section 3.4: baseline first-stage average payoff 3989.16 UME. This appears to be per subject; group-level not given but we report the clearly stated figure.,0.7,France,"Subjects recruited from University of Rennes I, Rennes, France; hence participants are located in France.",0.9,N/R,Only that they are undergraduates in business and economics; no age range reported.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not specified; only that gender dummies are used in regressions.,0.3,Undergraduate students in business and economics,Subjects recruited from undergraduate courses in business and economics at the university.,1,On site,Lab experiment physically conducted at CREM using lab computers; not online.,1,"Four-person groups with partner matching; 40 periods. Each period, each subject allocates 20 UME between X (collective security reducing probability of an attack for the group), Y (private protection reducing own conditional probability of being hit given an attack), and Z (safe private account, 1:1 return). Initial world attack probability p* = 0.7, total possible reduction a = 0.16 if all tokens in X; loss if hit is c = 600 UME drawn against a baseline wealth W = 6000 UME.","Summarizes key structural parameters (n, W, d, p*, a, c) and decision structure as described in Sections 2.1–2.3 and Appendix.",1
10.1111_j.1467-9779.2011.01519.x.md,Sanction – Punishment Institution,Second treatment is explicitly called the sanction treatment; identical first stage but adds second-stage costly punishment.,1,Institutional environment: costly punishment stage enabled after observing others’ X contributions; no reward option.,Independent variable manipulated is the availability of a punishment institution versus baseline and reward treatments.,1,True,Uses human subjects from same subject pool in lab environment.,1,True,Controlled variation of institutional rule (sanction stage) across sessions.,1,True,Conducted at CREM lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No mention of simulations in this treatment.,1,False,"Refers to experimental data, not the analytical model.",1,4,Same group size n=4 in all treatments.,1,40,All sessions had 40 periods under partner matching.,1,1,"Identical allocation possibilities over X,Y,Z as baseline (0–20 tokens).",1,0,Tokens start fully under private control; X is funded only by voluntary allocation.,0.9,N/R,Game uses probabilistic damage reduction rather than linear MPCR public good.,1,0,No communication stage is described; instructions forbid communication.,0.9,1,Stage 2 screen shows each group member’s contribution to X; subjects also see their earnings and whether an attack and hit occurred.,0.9,0,Paper notes subjects cannot observe individual sanction decisions across periods and punishment is effectively anonymous; recipients see total points but not who punished.,0.8,0,No reward institution in sanction treatment.,1,1,40-period finite horizon is common knowledge across treatments.,1,1,Second stage allows assigning 0–10 punishment points to each other member; costly to punisher and reduces target’s earnings.,1,5,"Cost is nonlinear; from Table A3 the marginal cost for first point is 5 UME and increases with more points. Since schema needs a single number, we report per-point cost for the first unit and note nonlinearity.",0.5,20,"Each punishment point reduces target’s payoff; from Table A3, 1 point reduces target by 20 UME; effect is linear in points until capped.",0.9,0,Sanction treatment uses punishment only; reward mechanism appears only in third treatment.,1,N/A,No reward stage here.,1,N/A,No reward stage here.,1,20,Per-period endowment d=20 UME in all treatments.,1,N/R,"Only raw token investments in X, Y, Z are reported per treatment; normalized rates not given.",0.6,35.86,Table 2: average group investment in X in sanction treatment is 35.86 tokens (SD 7.53).,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio relative to maximum cooperative benchmark is given.,1,3264.04,Section 3.4: average payoff in punishment treatment is 3264.04 UME (appears to be per subject final payoff).,0.8,France,"Same subject pool and location as baseline (University of Rennes I, France).",0.9,N/R,Only education level (undergraduate) given.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.3,Undergraduate students in business and economics,Sanction treatment sessions use same recruitment pool.,1,On site,Same lab at CREM using computers and z-Tree.,1,"Identical first-stage game to baseline: 4-player repeated security game with allocation of 20 UME among X (collective security), Y (national protection), Z (safe private). Second stage: after seeing others’ X contributions (order randomized), each subject can assign 0–10 punishment points to each of the three partners. Costs and impacts per Table A3 (nonlinear costs to punisher, linear damages up to 10 points, then capped at 600 UME). Partner matching over 40 periods.","Summarizes specific institutional differences (punishment stage, cost structure, information) as described in Sections 2.3 and Appendix A.4.",1
10.1111_j.1467-9779.2011.01519.x.md,Reward – Reward Institution,Third treatment is called the reward treatment; structurally same as sanction treatment but with rewarding instead of punishing.,1,Institutional environment: costly reward stage enabled after observing others’ X contributions; no punishment option.,Key manipulated feature is that second-stage points increase recipients’ payoffs instead of decreasing them.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment like other treatments.,1,True,Controlled treatment varying institutional rules in lab.,1,True,Conducted at CREM lab using z-Tree.,1,False,"No simulation, purely empirical.",1,False,"This row represents experimental data, not theory.",1,4,Same n=4 groups as in other treatments.,1,40,All sessions last 40 periods.,1,1,"Subjects divide 20 tokens freely over X,Y,Z each period.",1,0,No automatic allocation to X; all 20 UME start privately controlled.,0.9,N/R,"Public aspect is via probability reduction, not a constant MPCR.",1,0,No communication stage or messaging reported; communication is prohibited.,0.9,1,Stage 2 displays each member’s X contribution; payoffs and random attack/hit outcomes are reported.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in reward treatment.,1,0,"Rewards are anonymous like sanctions: recipients see total reward points, not who rewarded (inferred from discussion of blind reciprocity and absence of identity info).",0.7,1,Subjects know they play 40 periods under partner matching.,1,0,Reward treatment replaces punishment with reward; no sanction option.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,1,Second stage allows assigning 0–10 reward points that increase recipients’ payoffs at a cost to sender (Table A4).,1,5,Cost schedule mirrors punishment: first reward point costs 5 UME to sender; costs are nonlinear but we report first-unit cost as representative.,0.5,20,"From Table A4, 1 reward point gives +20 UME to target; linear in points up to cap.",0.9,20,Same per-period endowment d=20 UME.,1,N/R,Only average token amounts in X reported; rates as fraction of endowment not separately reported.,0.6,54.89,Table 2: average group investment in X in reward treatment is 54.89 tokens (SD 10.89).,1,N/R,Paper gives average payoffs and probabilities but not explicit efficiency ratios vs social optimum.,1,8040.19,"Section 3.4: higher final payoff of 8040.19 UME in reward treatment, described as average payoff and compared to baseline.",0.8,France,Same subject pool at University of Rennes I.,0.9,N/R,Age range not reported.,0.4,N/R,Gender distribution not described.,0.3,Undergraduate students in business and economics,Recruitment identical across treatments.,1,On site,All sessions run in CREM lab using computers.,1,"Same 4-player, 40-period stochastic-loss public good structure as other treatments. After investing 20 UME over X, Y, Z and realization of attack and individual hit, subjects see X contributions of all group members and can assign 0–10 reward points to each other member. Costs and benefits follow Table A4: increasing marginal cost to rewarder, linear benefit to target up to maximum of 600 UME per 10 points. Partner matching and anonymity of individual rewarding preserved.","Summarizes distinctive features of reward institution from Section 2.3 and Appendix A.5, including cost/benefit structure and timing.",1
