10.1016_b978-0-12-404681-8.00010-8.md,Sell & Son (1997) / Sell et al. (2002) – Static public good condition,"The chapter explicitly describes a standardized linear public good condition adapted from Marwell & Ames and Isaac et al., used by Sell & Son (1997) and Sell et al. (2002) to compare public goods and resource goods. This row captures that public good condition.",0.86,Type of good and initial location of tokens: public good; tokens start in private fund; static (no replenishment).,"The manipulation described compares public vs resource goods by where tokens start (private vs group fund) with identical payoffs; this row is the public good, static case.",0.86,True,Described as laboratory experiments with human subjects making contribution decisions.,0.96,True,Random assignment to public vs resource good conditions and controlled payoff structures.,0.96,True,Described as experiments using computerized screens or paper record sheets; no indication they are field experiments.,0.9,False,All descriptions involve human participants; no mention of agent-based or purely computational models.,0.99,False,"This section discusses implemented experiments, not purely formal models.",0.99,4,When exemplifying the setting they use a 4-person group (“if there are four members of a group…”); this appears to describe the design used for these comparisons.,0.7,N/R,"For these particular public vs resource goods comparisons, the chapter mentions static vs repeated vs dynamic designs, but does not state the exact number of decision periods for the static public-good case.",0.2,1,"Standard linear public goods setting with tokens; subjects can allocate any number of tokens between funds, not just all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,"For the public good setting, “tokens appeared in the blue box marked ‘private fund’” initially; thus 0 proportion is initially in the group fund.",0.98,0.75,"Example given: 4-person group, 1¢ in private fund vs 3¢ in public fund per token; MPCR = (3 / 4) = 0.75. This example is presented as instantiating the property of the social dilemma for “this standard linear public goods setting,” implying it matches the experimental parameterization.",0.7,0,No mention of communication or discussion between subjects for these conditions; focus is purely on structural differences in the good.,0.8,1,"Record sheets indicated past results so each subject could see what behaviors had occurred previously, implying they saw at least aggregate outcomes.",0.75,0,No punishment mechanism is described for these public vs resource good comparisons.,0.99,0,No reward mechanism is described.,0.99,N/R,The description does not indicate whether subjects were informed of the total number of rounds for these specific static public-good conditions.,0.2,0,No possibility of punishment or sanctions is mentioned within this design description.,0.98,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism is mentioned.,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,The chapter notes that subjects receive an “initial endowment” in tokens but does not specify the exact number for these studies.,0.2,N/R,"The chapter only reports qualitative comparative results (resource > public in cooperation), not exact contribution fractions.",0.1,N/R,No quantitative contribution amounts are given.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency statistics (payoff relative to maximum) are presented.,0.01,N/R,No numerical payoff data are reported.,0.01,N/R,The chapter does not specify the country or lab location for these particular experiments beyond generic references and that some were in cultures without computers.,0.1,N/R,No information on age ranges or student status for these experiments.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information given for these conditions.,0.1,N/R,The chapter does not state whether the participants were university students or another population for these experiments.,0.1,On site,"These are controlled, in-person experiments (computers or paper record sheets) conducted in lab-like environments, not online or field settings.",0.85,"Standard linear public good with individual vs group fund; static (no replenishment), identical payoffs as matched resource-good condition; subjects can freely allocate tokens between private and group funds; information on past behavior and replenishment (if applicable) shown on record sheets.",Summarizes structural details explicitly given for this condition.,0.86
10.1016_b978-0-12-404681-8.00010-8.md,Sell & Son (1997) / Sell et al. (2002) – Static resource good condition,The chapter describes a matched resource-good condition where tokens start in the group fund and can be withdrawn to the private fund; payoffs match the public-good condition. This row captures that resource-good condition.,0.86,Type of good and initial location of tokens: resource good; tokens start in group fund; static (no replenishment).,Described as the counterpart to the public-good setting with only the framing and initial token location changed.,0.86,True,Same experiments and human subjects as in the public-good condition.,0.96,True,Random assignment to public vs resource good conditions with controlled structure.,0.96,True,Computerized and paper-based decision tasks in an experimental setting.,0.9,False,"Human-subject experiments, not simulations.",0.99,False,Implemented experiments rather than purely analytical modeling.,0.99,4,Same 4-person group example and design as public-good condition; the resource-good setting is matched in all structural aspects except framing and initial token location.,0.7,N/R,Number of decision periods for this specific static resource-good condition is not reported.,0.2,1,Subjects can choose how many tokens to withdraw from the group fund to the private fund; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,0.95,1,"For the resource good setting, “tokens appeared first in the group fund” on the subject’s screen or in the group-fund column on paper; that is, 100% start in the shared fund.",0.98,0.75,"The payoffs for private vs group fund are stated to be exactly the same as in the public-good version; thus MPCR is the same (0.75 in the 4-person, 1¢ vs 3¢ example).",0.7,0,No discussion or communication among participants is mentioned for these conditions.,0.8,1,"Record sheets indicated previous results and replenishment information, allowing participants to see what behaviors had occurred in prior trials.",0.75,0,No punishment mechanism is included or referenced.,0.99,0,No explicit reward mechanism is part of the design.,0.99,N/R,The text does not state whether the total number of rounds was displayed.,0.2,0,The design is described purely in terms of allocation between group and private funds with no sanctioning options.,0.98,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward option is available.,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,The exact initial number of tokens in the group fund is not given.,0.2,N/R,"Only qualitative statements that cooperation (i.e., restraint from withdrawing) is higher here than in the public-good condition.",0.1,N/R,No numerical data for take/keep amounts.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency metrics reported.,0.01,N/R,No payoff figures given.,0.01,N/R,"The chapter notes that in some cultures computers were not available, implying cross-cultural work, but does not specify which country for this particular condition.",0.1,N/R,No age information provided.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition mentioned for these experiments.,0.1,N/R,No explicit statement that these were student samples or otherwise.,0.1,On site,Conducted with subjects in person using computers or paper records in controlled settings.,0.85,Standard linear ‘take-some’ resource-good game structurally isomorphic to the public-good version: tokens start in shared group fund; subjects may withdraw tokens to private fund; static (no explicit replenishment function in this particular comparison); identical monetary payoffs to the public-good treatment; prior behavior and outcomes displayed on record sheets.,Summarizes structural details explicitly given for the resource-good condition.,0.86
10.1016_b978-0-12-404681-8.00010-8.md,Sell & Wilson (1999) – Baseline repeated public good without grim trigger,The chapter describes Sell & Wilson (1999) treatments that differ by presence of a grim trigger and by discount parameters (probability of continuation). This row corresponds to a repeated public-good setting without the grim trigger punishment rule at a given discount parameter.,0.7,Trigger strategy institution: no grim-trigger rule; repeated interaction with probabilistic continuation (discount parameter specified for the treatment).,The independent variables in Sell & Wilson (1999) are expectations of future interaction (discount parameter) and threat of grim-trigger group punishment; this condition has no grim trigger.,0.7,True,Sell & Wilson (1999) is explicitly discussed as an experiment with human subjects making repeated public-good decisions.,0.96,True,Controlled manipulation of discount parameter and grim-trigger rule in a lab experiment.,0.96,True,"Conducted as a structured experiment with chips in a basket, decisions over repeated trials, and controlled environment.",0.9,False,"Human participants, not simulated agents.",0.99,False,"Experimental test of folk-theorem-related predictions, not purely analytical.",0.99,N/R,Group size for Sell & Wilson (1999) is not specified in the chapter excerpt.,0.2,N/R,A random stopping rule based on a discount parameter is described but no expected or maximum rounds are numerically reported for this treatment.,0.3,1,Described as standard public-good style decisions over endowment; no indication that contributions were restricted to all-or-nothing.,0.75,0,Follows the Marwell & Ames / Isaac & Walker linear public-good paradigm where tokens start as a private endowment and must be contributed to the public fund; the chapter links Sell & Wilson’s work conceptually to this structure.,0.6,N/R,The chapter does not report the specific marginal per capita return used by Sell & Wilson (1999).,0.2,0,"No mention of communication or discussion; focus is on probabilistic continuation and trigger punishment, not on communication treatments.",0.75,N/R,The description does not clearly state what summary information about others’ contributions or payoffs was provided each round.,0.3,0,In the no-trigger baseline there is no collective punishment rule; hence no identities for punishers or punished.,0.95,0,No explicit reward mechanism is part of this design.,0.95,0,"Subjects are told only a continuation probability each period (“likelihood or probability that each trial would be the last”), implying there is no fixed, known endpoint displayed.",0.8,0,"This baseline treatment lacks the grim-trigger punishment rule, so there is no explicit institutionalized punishment.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No separate reward institution mentioned.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Exact token endowment per period is not described.,0.2,N/R,The chapter states only that the presence of a grim trigger increases cooperation relative to this baseline; no numeric cooperation levels for the baseline are reported.,0.1,N/R,No raw contribution amounts reported.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency metrics given.,0.01,N/R,No payoff sums provided.,0.01,N/R,No country of subject pool reported for Sell & Wilson (1999) in this excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No age information given.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition is not discussed.,0.1,N/R,No information about whether participants were students or other groups.,0.1,On site,Described as a lab-style experiment with chips in a basket and repeated in-person decisions.,0.85,"Repeated public-good game with probabilistic continuation: in each period, subjects decide how much to contribute to a group fund; after decisions, a chip is drawn from a basket representing the continuation probability (e.g., 19 red, 1 white for 0.95 continuation). In this baseline, no institutional grim-trigger punishment is in force.",Summarizes the continuation-probability mechanism and the absence of the trigger rule for this treatment.,0.8
10.1016_b978-0-12-404681-8.00010-8.md,Sell & Wilson (1999) – Repeated public good with grim-trigger punishment rule,"The chapter reports that ‘when the threat of a trigger that mandates defection forever… exists, cooperation is higher than when it is not present (Sell & Wilson, 1999)’. This row captures the treatment where this grim trigger is implemented.",0.8,Trigger strategy institution: grim-trigger rule present (group mandated to defect forever after any noncooperation); repeated interaction with probabilistic continuation (given discount parameter).,Independent variables are presence of grim trigger and discount parameter; this condition includes the grim trigger.,0.8,True,Same human-subject experimental study as the baseline Sell & Wilson (1999) condition.,0.96,True,Controlled manipulation of institutional trigger rule in a lab experiment.,0.96,True,Conducted in-person with structured rules and random stopping via chip draws.,0.9,False,Not a computer simulation; involves human subjects.,0.99,False,Empirical test rather than purely analytical derivation.,0.99,N/R,Group size is not stated in the text for Sell & Wilson (1999).,0.2,N/R,The number of realized or expected rounds under the random-stopping procedure is not reported.,0.3,1,"Same underlying public-good contribution structure as the baseline, i.e., continuous contribution choice from the endowment.",0.75,0,Follows the standard linear public-good format with an endowment in a private account to be possibly contributed to the public fund.,0.6,N/R,Specific marginal per capita return for this experiment is not provided.,0.2,0,The description focuses on the institutional threat of punishment and continuation probability; no communication treatments are mentioned.,0.75,N/R,The excerpt does not detail feedback information (individual vs aggregate contributions/earnings) per round.,0.3,0,The grim trigger is a rule-based collective punishment (everyone defects after a deviation) rather than individualized punishment with identities revealed; identities of deviators or punishers are not described as being revealed.,0.7,0,No separate reward institution is discussed.,0.95,0,"Same random stopping procedure as the baseline; subjects know only the probability that each period is the last, not a fixed round count.",0.8,1,"Presence of a ‘grim trigger’ that mandates permanent defection after any noncooperation is effectively a punishment institution for defectors, implemented at the group level.",0.9,N/R,The chapter does not specify any explicit cost per unit of punishment or whether group members bear individual costs when the trigger is activated.,0.2,N/R,"The ‘technology’ of punishment (e.g., payoff reduction per punitive action) is not numerically specified; only the qualitative rule (‘defect forever after any noncooperation’) is described.",0.2,0,No formal reward mechanism in addition to the trigger rule is indicated.,0.95,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not reported.,0.2,N/R,"The chapter states that cooperation is higher with the grim trigger than without, but does not provide numeric contribution rates.",0.1,N/R,No specific contribution amounts are given.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency statistics reported.,0.01,N/R,No raw group payoff values reported.,0.01,N/R,Country of participants for Sell & Wilson (1999) is not provided in this excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No age information given.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition is not described.,0.1,N/R,No educational background information reported.,0.1,On site,Same in-person lab-style setting as the baseline Sell & Wilson (1999) treatment.,0.85,"Repeated public-good game with probabilistic continuation; after any detected noncooperation (e.g., insufficient contribution), a ‘grim-trigger’ rule mandates that the group revert to permanent defection in all subsequent rounds, serving as a collective punishment threat.",Summarizes the key institutional difference from the baseline: presence of the grim-trigger punishment rule.,0.85
10.1016_j.jebo.2011.01.018.md,"Local punishment, x = 0 (full separation)",Condition with local punishment in group A and matching parameter x=0 as defined in Section 3.3 and implemented as one of four main treatments.,1,Matching viscosity x = 0; punishment local; group membership A vs B endogenous,"Across main treatments only the matching parameter x varies (0, 1/3, 2/3, 1) with local punishment fixed; within this condition, group membership is chosen by subjects.",0.96,True,Human subjects (students) played the game in the lab.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized lab experiment with specified payoff structure and treatments.",1,True,Conducted in experimental sessions at University of Cologne with computer terminals and z-Tree.,1,False,"Although partners are simulated draws from empirical distributions, human decisions drive outcomes; not a pure simulation study.",1,False,Paper contains theory but this condition refers to the experimental implementation.,1,8,“One population consisted of 8 subjects.” The game is played within these populations.,1,100,“played a prisoner's dilemma game for 100 rounds.”,1,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D; no continuous contribution choice, so standard PD, mapped as unconstrained binary choice (coded 1 as not ‘all endowment or nothing’ contribution pool).",0.7,0,"Standard PD with no endowment allocated to a public account; all payoffs determined by action profile, so effectively all value starts in private account.",0.7,N/A,"Not a linear public goods game; it is a 2x2 prisoner’s dilemma with fixed payoffs, so MPCR not defined.",1,0,Instructions forbid communication between participants.,1,0,"Subjects are only informed of partner’s group, action, and own payoff each round; no summary of others’ earnings or punishments.",0.9,0,Punishment is automatic and local in group A; partners are anonymous ‘representative members’; no identities revealed.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in any treatment.,1,1,Instructions state the e