10.1086_700098.md,"Internal punishment (INT), rounds 11–20",Paper defines an internal punishment treatment (INT) introduced after round 10; behavior in rounds 11–20 under this rule is the core condition.,1,"Punishment institution = Internal peer punishment (players observe all extractions each round and can buy punishment: cost 1, effect −3 on target; identities of punishers hidden)",Only institutional feature varied across main treatments is type of punishment institution; here it is internal peer punishment.,1,True,Human forest users from Namibian villages participated in a framed field experiment.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized framed field experiment with imposed rules and treatments.",1,False,Conducted in villages (lab-in-the-field) rather than a conventional lab.,0.95,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model as primary method; focus on experimental data.,1,5,“The experiment was played in groups of five subjects.”,1,20,“...lasted for 20 rounds.” INT applies in rounds 11–20 but game length is 20.,1,1,"“Every individual i had to decide to extract any integer amount between zero and 20 units” indicates a continuous integer choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Subjects decide how much to extract from 0–20; remaining units stay in common forest. Endowment effectively starts as private extraction potential (nothing is pre-placed in public account).,0.7,0.4,"“Resources left in the common forest were doubled and shared among the five players, which leads to a marginal return from the public good of 0.4.”",1,0,No communication phase described; decisions “taken in private and never revealed” beyond programmed feedback.,0.9,1,"In INT, after each round extractions of all players are announced and kept on a whiteboard; this is a summary of others’ actions and payoffs are implicitly inferable.",0.95,0,“Note that participants were not informed about who punished them.”,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in any treatment.,1,1,Standard in finite-round experiments and implied by instructions about 20 rounds; not explicitly stated but very likely told.,0.7,1,Internal peer punishment is the defining feature of INT.,1,1,“Punishment in our experiment cost the punisher one unit of resources and reduced the punished player's amount of resources by three units.”,1,3,Same sentence: each 1 unit spent reduces target’s payoff by 3 units.,1,0,No positive reward option is described; only fines/punishments.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Each individual can “extract any integer amount between zero and 20 units”; interpretable as a 20-unit per-round extraction endowment.,1,N/R,They report harvest (extraction) and group harvest; no explicit normalized contribution (non-harvest) rate reported.,0,23.95,"They define cooperation as low harvest; table 2 gives “Average Group Harvest” 23.95 in rounds 11–20 for INT. Per-person average harvest is 23.95/5 ≈ 4.79, but table value is group level. The clearest reported quantity is average group harvest 23.95, which we record here as the main behavioral amount, noting it is group-level.",0.5,N/R,"They discuss earnings qualitatively and give mean earnings per round, but not as a fraction of maximum possible efficiency.",0,7.33,"Table 2 reports “Individual Earnings (Tokens)” = 7.33 in INT for rounds 11–20. Group payoff per round would be 5×7.33, but they don’t report that explicitly; we record the reported individual per-round payoff as the primary payoff measure.",0.8,Namibia,Subjects are forest users in Kavango regions in the northeast of Namibia.,1,N/R,No age distribution or mean age reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,Mixed; rural subsistence farmers and forest users with varied education levels,They describe “rural subsistence farmers and forest users” and note sociodemographic variation; education levels not quantified.,0.6,Field experiment,Framed field experiment run in four villages with local forest users.,1,"Common-pool resource / extraction game framed as forest use; 5 players choose integer harvest 0–20 trees from a common forest for 20 rounds. Resources left: doubled and shared equally (MPCR=0.4). Group threshold of 50 units: if total extraction X>50, no group return and each harvester’s private harvest payoff is halved. Two symmetric Nash equilibria at xi=10 and xi=20; social optimum at X=0. INT from round 11: perfect monitoring of extractions, public history of extractions on whiteboard; players can buy costly punishment that reduces others’ payoffs; about one-third of punishment points are antisocial. Average group harvest in INT rounds 11–20 is 23.95; threshold exceeded in 2 groups once. Average individual earnings 7.33 tokens per round; average total penalty received 5.75 tokens (1.74 ASP, 4.01 PSP).",Summarizes detailed design elements and descriptive outcomes specific to INT condition from Sections III–IV and Table 2.,1
10.1086_700098.md,"External punishment (EXT), rounds 11–20",Paper defines external punishment treatment (EXT) introduced after round 10; behavior in rounds 11–20 under this rule is the comparative condition.,1,"Punishment institution = External centralized punishment (random monitoring with 20% probability per person per round; if monitored and harvested >0, entire round payoff confiscated and fine equal to harvested amount imposed)",Key institutional difference vs INT is centralized random monitoring and fixed sanction rule.,1,True,Same participants and sessions as INT but with external monitoring rule.,1,True,Controlled framed field experiment with randomized treatments.,1,False,"Conducted in village field setting, not standard lab.",0.95,False,No simulation models.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,5,Same group size across treatments: groups of five subjects.,1,20,Game lasts 20 rounds; EXT applies in rounds 11–20.,1,1,Choice of any integer 0–20 remains the same.,1,0,Same extraction framework as INT; no pre-assigned public contribution.,0.7,0.4,Public good production rule identical across treatments.,1,0,No communication allowed in either treatment.,0.9,0,"In EXT, they do not report public revelation of each player’s extraction history on a board; only in INT does that feature appear. In baseline/EXT sessions, subjects can see each other but individual decisions are said to be private and never revealed.",0.8,0,"Punishment comes from an impersonal external authority (die and rule), not from identified peers; no individual punisher identity to reveal.",0.95,0,No rewards exist.,1,1,Game length of 20 rounds is part of instructions; likely known to subjects.,0.7,1,External fines for harvesters are implemented from round 11 in EXT.,1,0,"Sanctioning is not chosen or paid for by participants; fines are imposed by experimenters/civil authority analogue; participants bear fines only as reduced payoffs, but they do not pay to punish others.",0.9,1,"When monitored and found harvesting, player “received no profits from individual harvest or the group account and in addition had to pay a fine as high as the amount of resources he had harvested.” Effective net loss relative to unmanaged payoffs equals harvested amount, so the fine magnitude is 1 unit per harvested unit, ignoring lost positive earnings. We record the explicit fine parameter (1:1).",0.7,0,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Per-round extraction choice 0–20 units unchanged.,1,N/R,They report harvest and earnings but not normalized contribution rates.,0,29.28,"Table 2 gives “Average Group Harvest” 29.28 for EXT in rounds 11–20. As with INT, we record this group harvest level as the main behavioral amount, recognizing it is at group level.",0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0,12.11,Table 2: “Individual Earnings (Tokens)” = 12.11 in EXT for rounds 11–20. This is the reported payoff measure.,0.9,Namibia,Same subject pool across treatments.,1,N/R,Age statistics not reported.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0,Mixed; rural subsistence farmers and forest users with varied education levels,Same as INT; no more detailed breakdown.,0.6,Field experiment,Same framed field experiment in villages.,1,"Same CPR extraction game as INT: 5 players, 20 rounds, integer harvest 0–20 from common forest. Public-good return: units left are doubled and shared (MPCR=0.4). Threshold: if total extraction X>50, public return zero and private payoffs halved. In EXT (rounds 11–20), participants are informed that if more than zero resources are harvested, one randomly selected player in each round (20% monitoring probability) is inspected. If monitored and harvesting >0, that player loses all profits from individual harvest and group account for that round and pays an additional fine equal to their harvested amount. Sanction is anonymous: only the experimenter/“external monitor” imposes it. Mean group harvest in EXT rounds 11–20 is 29.28; threshold surpassed in 2 groups once and 1 group four times. Average individual earnings 12.11 tokens per round; average penalty received per player per round 1.87 tokens.",Summarizes EXT-specific punishment rule and descriptive statistics from design and Table 2.,1
10.1086_700098.md,"Baseline no-punishment stage, rounds 1–10 (pooled across future INT and EXT groups)",Rounds 1–10 are conducted without any punishment rules and before subjects learn their treatment; this is effectively a separate no-punishment condition shared by all groups.,1,Punishment institution = None (no internal or external sanctions; only CPR payoff structure with threshold and public-good component),Paper explicitly states that in first 10 rounds no rules (sanctions) were in place; treatments introduced only after round 10.,1,True,Same human subjects and game environment.,1,True,This stage is part of the controlled experimental protocol.,1,False,Field setting in villages.,0.95,False,No simulations.,1,False,"Empirical, not purely analytical.",1,5,Same 5-person groups apply in baseline stage.,1,10,Baseline no-punishment stage is defined as rounds 1–10.,1,1,Same extraction choice 0–20 per round.,1,0,Same extraction game; no initial contribution to public pool.,0.7,0.4,Forest return rule does not differ across stages.,1,0,No communication is reported; decisions are private.,0.9,0,"In baseline rounds, they state that “all individual decisions were taken in private and never revealed”; no whiteboard history or public listing.",0.95,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this stage.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,"Subjects likely informed about total of 20 rounds, but not explicitly differentiated here; assumed known.",0.7,0,“Behavior in the first 10 rounds where no rules were in place” indicates no sanctions.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward option in any stage.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same extraction capacity 0–20 units per round.,1,N/R,Only harvest and earnings summarized; no normalized contribution rate.,0,32.84,"Table 2 reports “Average Group Harvest” = 32.84 in rounds 1–10 (baseline). As above, we record this group-level harvest as the main behavioral amount.",0.8,N/R,They provide average individual earnings 13.21 tokens but not in efficiency ratio form.,0,13.21,Table 2: “Individual Earnings (Tokens)” = 13.21 in rounds 1–10 (baseline).,0.9,Namibia,Same subjects as later stages.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,Mixed; rural subsistence farmers and forest users with varied education levels,Same pool as in later stages.,0.6,Field experiment,Same framed field experiment setting in Kavango villages.,1,"Baseline stage uses the same CPR extraction game: 5 players, 10 rounds without any punishment institution. Each round, individuals secretly choose integer harvest 0–20 from a common forest. Remaining resources are doubled and split equally (MPCR=0.4). If total extraction X>50, no public return and private harvest payoffs are halved. Subjects do not yet know that punishment institutions (INT or EXT) will be introduced later; treatments are revealed only after round 10. Table 2 indicates average group harvest 32.84; threshold surpassed in 5 groups once, 1 group twice, and 1 group four times. Average individual earnings per round are 13.21 tokens.",Summarizes baseline game details and descriptive statistics before punishment is introduced.,1
10.1111_jpet.12540.md,"Fehr & Gächter (2000) – Public goods, stranger treatment, no-punishment condition, round 10",Section 5: analyzes final round (10) of Fehr & Gächter (2000) public goods game without punishment in stranger treatment; used to calibrate φ and γ.,1,Punishment institution: no punishment available; standard linear public good,Condition differs from punishment treatment only by absence of punishment stage; contributions only.,1,True,Uses human subjects from Fehr & Gächter (2000).,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with imposed game structure.,1,True,Fehr & Gächter (2000) is a laboratory public goods experiment.,1,False,No computer-only agents; all data are from human subjects.,1,False,"This row refers to experimental treatment, not the theoretical model.",1,4,Section 5: 'They study a public goods contribution game with four players.',1,10,They analyze 'the final round of 10 in the stranger treatment.',1,1,Contributions are continuous: 'Participants choose contribution levels 0 ≤ x^i ≤ 20'.,1,0,Standard linear public good where each subject chooses how much of private endowment (20 tokens) to contribute; no automatic allocation to public fund.,0.9,0.4,"Payoff u^i = v0 − c x^i + v Σ_{j≠i} x^j with v=0.4, so each token in public account yields 0.4 to each group member (MPCR=0.4).",1,0,No mention of communication; standard Fehr & Gächter design disallows communication.,0.9,1,"Public goods experiments normally reveal others’ contributions and payoffs; Fehr & Gächter (2000) is standard in that respect, though not re-described here.",0.7,0,No punishment stage exists in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism is described for this game.,1,1,Standard repeated public-good designs including Fehr & Gächter inform subjects of total number of periods (10); implied but not reiterated here.,0.8,0,This is the 'no-punishment condition'.,1,N/A,No punishment available in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,0,Only punishment is discussed in this paper; no rewards in Fehr & Gächter (2000) design.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,v0=20 is the private endowment in Fehr & Gächter (2000).,1,1.9,"Average contribution in final round is x=1.9 tokens (Section 5). Normalized by endowment 20 gives 1.9/20≈0.095; but user asks for 'fraction (0–1)' yet empirical number given is 1.9; to avoid fabrication, we report raw 1.9 as stated.",0.4,1.9,Section 5: 'The average contribution in the no-punishment condition is x=1.9.',1,N/R,Paper gives welfare comparisons mainly for punishment treatment; efficiency for no-punishment round 10 not computed.,0,N/R,Token utilities for no-punishment treatment are given as 21.1 but not broken down by round; round-10 group payoff not provided.,0.3,N/R,Fehr & Gächter (2000) location not re-specified; likely Switzerland but not stated in this paper.,0,N/R,No demographic details from Fehr & Gächter (2000) repeated here.,0,N/R,Not discussed.,0,University students,Described as 'Western college students' for the class of games studied (Section 4.3).,0.7,On site,Fehr & Gächter (2000) is a conventional physical laboratory experiment.,1,Linear public goods game; utility u^i=20−0.6 x^i+0.4 Σ_{j≠i} x^j; stranger matching each round.,Payoff function and matching protocol described in Section 5; they focus on stranger treatment.,1
10.1111_jpet.12540.md,"Fehr & Gächter (2000) – Public goods, stranger treatment, punishment condition, round 10","Section 5: analyzes punishment treatment of Fehr & Gächter (2000) public goods game in final round of 10, stranger treatment.",1,Punishment institution: costly peer punishment available with 10% impact per point; same public good as control.,Section 5.1: describes punishment stage added after contribution stage; independent variable is presence of punishment.,1,True,Uses human subjects from Fehr & Gächter (2000).,1,True,Controlled randomized treatment in a lab game.,1,True,Standard controlled lab public goods experiment.,1,False,"Empirical human-subject data, not simulated.",1,False,"This row corresponds to an experimental treatment, not the analytical model.",1,4,Same base game as no-punishment: 'public goods contribution game with four players.',1,10,They analyze 'the final round of 10 in the stranger treatment.',1,1,Contributions continuous between 0 and 20 (0 ≤ x^i ≤ 20).,1,0,As in the no-punishment treatment: subjects allocate any part of 20-token endowment to public good.,0.9,0.4,Same linear public goods payoff with v=0.4.,1,0,No communication stage described in Fehr & Gächter (2000) punishment design.,0.9,1,Standard punishment public good designs reveal others’ contributions and punishment received to compute payoffs; implied though not fully re-specified.,0.7,0,Paper does not state that identities of punishers are revealed; Fehr & Gächter (2000) typically uses anonymous punishment.,0.7,0,No rewards in punishment treatment; only costly sanctions.,1,1,Total number of rounds (10) is part of experimental instructions in original study.,0.8,1,Section 5.1: describes punishment points purchasable after contributions.,1,1,Each punishment point costs 1 token up to 2 points (linear region they use for calibration).,1,0.1,Each punishment point reduces target’s payoff by 10%.,1,0,Only punishment points are implemented; no rewarding scheme.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same endowment v0=20 as in no-punishment.,1,12.3,"Average contribution in punishment treatment final round is x=12.3 (Section 5). Normalized rate would be 12.3/20≈0.615; but only raw value is directly given, so we report 12.3 to avoid unstated calculations.",0.4,12.3,"Section 5: 'contributions were much higher, at x=12.3.'",1,N/R,"They discuss welfare in tokens relative to no-punishment, but not as normalized efficiency ratio vs. first best.",0.3,23.3,Section 5.4: 'Welfare is reported as 10% higher than the token utility of 21.1 received in the treatment without punishment ... which is to say 23.3 tokens.',1,N/R,Country of lab (Fehr & Gächter 2000) not specified here.,0,N/R,No direct age information; only that they are 'Western college students' in related contexts.,0,N/R,Not discussed.,0,University students,Section 4.3 indicates subject pool for these games is Western college students.,0.7,On site,Fehr & Gächter (2000) is a physical lab experiment.,1,"Linear public goods game; u^i=20−0.6 x^i+0.4 Σ_{j≠i} x^j; after contributions, each player may buy punishment points against others at cost 1 per point (linear up to 2 points); each point reduces target payoff by 10% (capped at 10 points). Stranger matching between rounds.",Section 5 and 5.1 provide payoff and punishment details.,1
10.1111_apce.12343.md,"O-VCM (Homogeneous, no punishment)",Condition label defined in footnote 6: O-VCM represents the VCM condition with homogeneous subjects.,1,MPCR homogeneity = homogeneous (all MPCR=0.5); punishment mechanism = none (VCM only),Homogeneous MPCR=0.5 defined in Experimental Design; VCM condition has no punishment stage.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 192 student subjects; this condition uses a subset.,1,True,Controlled lab public good game with randomized matching and treatments.,1,True,Sessions conducted at National Taipei University lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation mentioned.,1,False,"Study reports experimental results, not purely analytical models.",1,4,Design specifies groups of four subjects.,1,6,Each condition has six decision periods; Part I for VCM is Periods 1–6.,1,1,Subjects choose any integer tokens from 0 to 20 for group account; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,All 20 tokens start under subject control and are allocated between private and group accounts.,1,0.5,Homogeneous treatment assigns MPCR=0.5 to each subject.,1,0,No communication or chat is described; only feedback screens and instructions.,0.9,1,"At end of each period, subjects are informed of aggregate contributions to group account.",1,0,No punishment in this condition; identities not applicable.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,1,Instructions state each part has six periods; standard z-Tree implementations inform subjects of period numbers; inferred.,0.7,0,VCM condition is explicitly described without any punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No reward or positive sanction mechanism is described anywhere.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Each subject is endowed with 20 tokens at the start of each period.,1,0.864,Average group contribution is 17.28 tokens (Table 2); max group contribution is 4*20=80; normalized rate =17.28/20 (per capita) =0.864.,0.9,17.28,Table 2 'Group contribution' for O-VCM is 17.28.,1,0.608,Efficiency defined as group earnings per period relative to max 160 tokens. Table 7 shows 97.28 average group payoffs; 97.28/160 ≈ 0.608.,0.95,97.28,Table 7 lists O-VCM group payoffs as 97.28 tokens.,1,Taiwan,Experiment conducted at National Taipei University in Taiwan; student participants there.,1,N/R,Only that participants are university students; no age range reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,0,University students,Participation open to all students enrolled in National Taipei University.,1,On site,"Subjects checked in, seated with partitions in the university lab; not online.",1,Stranger matching with random rematching each period; homogeneous MPCR=0.5; feedback includes own payoff and aggregate group contribution each period.,"Specified in Experimental Design, VCM condition description, and procedure.",1
10.1111_apce.12343.md,"O-IP (Homogeneous, individual punishment)",Condition label from footnote 6: O-IP represents the individual punishment condition with homogeneous subjects.,1,"MPCR homogeneity = homogeneous (all MPCR=0.5); punishment mechanism = individual peer punishment (cost 1, impact 3)",Homogeneous MPCR defined in design; IP condition defined as VCM plus individual punishment stage.,1,True,Same subject pool and lab setup as other conditions.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with explicit rules and random matching.,1,True,Conducted at National Taipei University using z-Tree.,1,False,No indication of computer-only agents.,1,False,"Analysis is empirical with regressions, not closed-form modeling only.",1,4,Groups of four subjects used throughout.,1,6,"Each condition (including IP) has six decision periods (e.g., Part II Periods 7–12).",1,1,Stage 1 identical to VCM; subjects choose any 0–20 tokens to group account.,1,0,Endowment starts fully in private account and is allocated by subject.,1,0.5,Homogeneous treatment specifies MPCR=0.5 for all.,1,0,No communication stage or chat described.,0.9,1,In Stage 2 subjects see aggregate group investment and each group member's contribution.,1,1,"Subjects can punish 'any member' after observing each member's contribution, implying identities within group are known for assigning punishment.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Instructions describe three parts of six periods; typical implementation reveals period number; inferred.,0.7,1,Individual punishment stage explicitly described.,1,1,Punisher pays cost of one token for each punishment assigned.,1,3,Each punishment received reduces punished subject's payoff by three tokens.,1,0,"Only costly punishment is available, no reward.",1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,20,"Same 20-token endowment per period as in VCM, Stage 1.",1,1.482,Average group contribution 29.64 (Table 2); per-capita 7.41; normalized rate 7.41/5 (since max 20) =1.482? This seems inconsistent; instead compute per-capita rate: 7.41/20 ≈ 0.3705. But field requires 0–1; typical definition is per-endowment. Use 29.64/(4*20)=29.64/80≈0.3705.,0.5,29.64,Table 2 reports group contribution 29.64 for O-IP.,1,0.626,Average group payoff 100.22 (Table 7); max 160; efficiency=100.22/160≈0.626.,0.95,100.22,Table 7 group payoffs for O-IP.,1,Taiwan,"National Taipei University, Taiwan.",1,N/R,Only student status mentioned.,0,N/R,No gender details.,0,University students,Participants are students at National Taipei University.,1,On site,Physical lab sessions with check-in and partitioned seating.,1,"Stranger matching each period; two-stage game: VCM contribution then individual punishment decisions; feedback includes aggregate group investment, individual contributions, own payoff and punishment outcomes.",Described in Experimental Design and IP condition sections.,1
10.1111_apce.12343.md,"O-MP (Homogeneous, majority-voting punishment)",Footnote 6: O-MP represents the majority-voting punishment condition with homogeneous subjects.,1,"MPCR homogeneity = homogeneous (all MPCR=0.5); punishment mechanism = majority-voting punishment (cost 1 per other member, impact 9 if ≥2 votes)",Homogeneous MPCR specified; MP condition defined as majority-voting punishment mechanism.,1,True,Lab experiment with student subjects.,1,True,"Randomly matched, controlled treatment in lab.",1,True,Conducted at National Taipei University using z-Tree.,1,False,No mention of simulation models.,1,False,Analytical section supplements but main results are experimental.,1,4,Groups consist of four subjects.,1,6,Each condition (including MP) has six decision periods.,1,1,Contribution decision in Stage 1 same as VCM; any 0–20 tokens.,1,0,Endowment starts in private account and is allocated by subject.,1,0.5,Homogeneous groups have MPCR=0.5.,1,0,No chat or communication described.,0.9,1,Subjects informed of aggregate group investment and each member's contribution before voting.,1,1,"Each subject votes on whether to punish 'each of the other three group members', implying identification of group members.",0.9,0,No reward implemented.,1,1,Parts and periods clearly defined; typical interface shows period count; inferred.,0.7,1,MP condition is a punishment condition implemented via majority voting.,1,1,Each of the other three group members bears cost 1 token for an executed punishment.,1,9,"If punished, subject’s Stage 1 payoff reduced by nine tokens.",1,0,Only costly punishment exists; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment per period in all conditions.,1,0.4195,Average group contribution 33.56; per-capita 8.39; normalized by 20: 8.39/20≈0.4195; equivalently 33.56/80≈0.4195.,0.95,33.56,Table 2 'Group contribution' for O-MP is 33.56.,1,0.635,Average group payoff 101.56; divide by max 160 => 101.56/160≈0.635.,0.95,101.56,Table 7 group payoffs for O-MP.,1,Taiwan,"Location National Taipei University, Taiwan.",1,N/R,No age info beyond being students.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not given.,0,University students,Participants are university students at National Taipei University.,1,On site,Described as lab experiment with physical attendance.,1,Stranger matching; two-stage game per period: VCM contribution then majority voting on punishments; subject cannot vote on own punishment; punishment executed if at least two of three others vote to punish.,Detailed in MP condition section.,1
