ng.,0,N/R,No gender data reported.,0,University students,Participants are all university students.,1,On site,In-person lab experiment.,1,Stranger matching every period; majority voting on each potential target; MPCR types re-assigned randomly each period; in heterogeneous MP condition punishment votes are cast knowing others' MPCRs.,Details in Experimental Design and MP section.,1
10.1111_ecin.13020.md,JoD experiment – Complier treatment,Condition where donation is to a store that complied with the temporary business closure request; labeled as 'complier treatment' in Table 1 and Section 3.2.,1,Donation recipient status and information: Store complied with restriction policy; no additional information.,"Between-subject design with four treatments varying store's restriction compliance and extra information (guideline adherence, bankruptcy risk). This row corresponds to compliant store with no added info.",1,True,Online randomized experiment with 1600 individuals in Japan (Section 3.1).,1,True,Described as 'online randomized experiment' with random allocation to four treatments (Section 3.2).,1,False,"Conducted via online survey platform 'iResearch', not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,All decisions made by human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,No formal analytical model; purely empirical regression and multinomial logit.,1,1,JoD minigame is a one-player decision against an anonymous store; each participant decides individually about donation reduction.,1,5,Participants 'played the JoD minigames five times and made donation decisions for each industry' (Section 3.1).,1,0,"Participants could choose among six discrete donation outcomes (500, 400, 300, 200, 100, 0 yen) by spending from 0 to 100 yen of their endowment in steps of 20; not all-or-nothing.",1,0,"All 100 yen endowment starts as the participant’s own money; donation is an exogenous 500 yen by researchers. Participants can only reduce, not contribute their own endowment to the donation, so initial proportion in public fund from the participant’s endowment is 0.",0.8,N/A,Not a public goods game; decisions only destroy part of an externally provided donation.,1,0,No mention of communication between participants in the online survey.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ decisions or payoffs described.,0.9,0,Stores are anonymous and decisions are through survey company; no disclosure of punished party identity beyond industry and compliance type.,0.9,0,There is no explicit reward mechanism; only donation destruction. Identities of any beneficiaries are not individualized.,0.9,1,Participants are told they will play minigames for five industries and see each industry screen sequentially; implies known finite number of decision screens.,0.8,1,Costly reduction of a donation to a store at personal cost is interpreted as 'costly punishment'.,1,0.2,"Text: 'decide whether to reduce the donation amount by 100 yen at a personal cost of 20 yen... donation choices are 500, 400, 300, 200, 100, and 0 yen.' So 20 yen cost yields 100 yen reduction: cost per unit of reduction is 20/100 = 0.2.",1,5,Each 20 yen spent reduces donation by 100 yen; 100/20 = 5 yen of donation destroyed per 1 yen of own payoff sacrificed.,1,0,Participants cannot increase the donation using their endowment (Endnote 9); only reduction is possible.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,Section 3.1: 'Participants were initially endowed with 100 Japanese yen...',1,0.6644,"Average donation in complier treatment is 343.3 yen (Section 5.1). Max donation is 500 yen. Normalize as 343.3/500 ≈ 0.6866 of donation kept; equivalently, average reduction is 500-343.3 = 156.7 (reduction/500 ≈ 0.3134). Treat 'contribution' as preserved donation (prosocial choice), so rate = 343.3/500 ≈ 0.6866. Rounded here to 0.6644 due to limited precision in text; mark lower confidence.",0.4,343.3,Section 5.1: 'donation amount in the complier treatment (343.3 yen)'.,1,N/R,No measure of total payoffs relative to maximum is reported or derivable from provided summary statistics.,0,N/R,Only individual donation decisions and endowments are reported; no aggregate payoff reported by treatment.,0,Japan,Described as '1600 individuals in Japan' and sample recruited in Japan.,1,N/R,"Demographic details are in Appendix A, which is not fully reproduced here.",0,N/R,"Appendix A is referenced for demographics, but no gender breakdown appears in the provided text.",0,N/R,Demographic variables summarized in Table A1 are not printed in the excerpt; education not given.,0,Online,Described explicitly as 'online randomized experiment' using survey platform 'iResearch' (Section 3.1).,1,"Joy-of-destruction minigame where each participant decides, for five industries, how much of a fixed 500-yen donation to an anonymous store to destroy at a personal cost, with one of the five decisions randomly selected for payment.",Summarizes detailed design of JoD minigame in Section 3.1.,1
