fied in the provided sections.,0,N/R,Not specified in the provided sections.,0,N/R,Not reported in main text excerpt.,0,Online,Part of the same online randomized experiment.,1,"Same JoD structure, but instructions add: 'The store faced bankruptcy risk if they had followed the request.'",Section 3.2 provides the extra line that characterizes the moral norms treatment.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3084342.md,Evolutionary game simulation – baseline parameter set (Table 6),"The paper presents a single evolutionary game model analyzed via MATLAB simulations using the parameter assignments in Table 6; all subsequent figures vary one parameter at a time from this baseline, so the baseline configuration is treated as the main condition.",0.9,"Not a comparative experiment; single baseline parameterization: R1=2, R2=1, R4=12, R5=11, R6=2, R7=1, R9=2, R10=1, M=1, N=1, J=1, K=1, C1=2, C2=2, C3=3. Subsequent analyses vary initial intentions (x0,y0,z0) and single parameters (M,N,J,K,C1,C2,C3,R1−R2,R4−R5,R6−R7,R9,R10) one at a time.",The model is purely theoretical; no between-condition empirical manipulation. Independent variables are game parameters and initial strategy probabilities used in simulations.,1,False,No human participants; the study is an evolutionary game-theoretic model with MATLAB simulations.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; it is a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only mathematical modeling and simulations.,1,True,The paper explicitly states that MATLAB simulation analysis is used on the evolutionary game model.,1,True,"Sections III and III.C derive payoff functions, replicator dynamics, Jacobian matrices, eigenvalues, and stability conditions analytically.",1,3,"Tripartite game among core enterprises, start-up enterprises, and the government.",1,0,"Continuous-time replicator dynamics over time t, not discrete rounds as in repeated games.",0.7,1,"Strategies are discrete (positive/negative), but there is no continuous contribution choice akin to a public goods contribution; all-or-nothing definition does not apply well, coded as allowing any amount by default.",0.3,N/A,"No public good or contribution endowment structure; model is about strategic choices, subsidies, punishments, and payoffs.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely theoretical model.,1,0,"Information structure is described as asymmetric, but there is no interface or notion of displaying summaries as in lab experiments.",0.5,N/A,"No identities or user interface; punishment is a model parameter, not an implemented mechanism with IDs.",1,N/A,"Same as punishment; subsidies are parameters, not implemented with identities.",1,0,No discrete rounds or display thereof in the model.,1,1,"Model includes punishment parameters N (core → startup), J (gov → core), and K (gov → startup).",1,N/R,"Costs of punishing per se (e.g., per unit N, J, K) are not separately parameterized; only overall regulation and supervision costs C1 and C3 are specified.",0.3,N/R,"No explicit mapping «1 unit cost → X units fine» is given; N, J, K enter directly as payoff adjustments.",0.3,1,"The model includes subsidies M (core → startup), H (gov → core), and I (gov → startup).",1,N/R,"No explicit per-unit cost of providing subsidies is separated from payoffs, beyond general regulation/supervision costs.",0.3,N/R,No explicit conversion from expended resources to subsidy impact; subsidies directly affect payoffs.,0.3,N/R,No concept of initial monetary or token endowment for agents is defined.,1,N/A,"No contribution decision; model outcomes are strategy probabilities and stability, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution variable is modeled.,1,N/R,"Although system-level welfare is discussed qualitatively, no formal efficiency ratio (actual/maximum payoff) is reported or computed.",0.5,N/R,"Payoff functions for each player type are given, but no aggregate group payoff statistics are reported as results.",0.8,N/A,No human participants; thus no participant demographics.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is an evolutionary game-theoretic and simulation study without empirical data collection.,1,"Three-player evolutionary game among core enterprises, start-up enterprises, and the government with binary strategies for each: core enterprises choose positive vs. negative performance; start-up enterprises choose positive vs. negative entrepreneurship; the government chooses positive vs. negative regulation. Payoffs depend on base benefits (R1–R7, R9, R10), subsidies (M,H,I), punishments (N,J,K), and costs (C1–C3). Dynamics follow replicator equations for strategy probabilities x, y, z over continuous time. Analytical stability analysis identifies eight pure-strategy equilibria E1–E8 with conditions (1)–(8). MATLAB simulations then explore effects of varying initial intentions (x0, y0, z0) and parameters (M, N, J, K, C1, C2, C3, R1−R2, R4−R5, R6−R7, R9, R10) on convergence paths.","Summarizes the strategic structure, payoff components, and dynamic formulations described in Sections III and IV.",1
