10.1103_PhysRevE.100.052304.md,Infinite well-mixed population – D + CP (defectors and cooperator-driven punishers),Authors analyze evolutionary dynamics for six strategy combinations; this condition is explicitly labeled as the 'D+CP' case in Sec. III A and Appendix B.,1,Synergy factor r; punishment fine α; feedback sensitivity A; strategies available: defectors (D) and cooperator-driven punishers (CP),"Within this condition, only D and CP strategies are allowed while r, α, and A are varied to study phase behavior and selection gradients.",1,False,Study is purely theoretical/simulation-based with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only analytical replicator dynamics.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person sessions; mathematical modeling only.,1,False,"This specific condition is treated via replicator equations for an infinite well-mixed population, which is analytical rather than agent-based numerical simulation.",0.9,True,Appendix A defines replicator equations for infinite well-mixed populations; D+CP is one of those analytically solved cases.,1,1000000,Infinite well-mixed population is assumed; represented here as a very large number.,0.3,1,Replicator dynamics are continuous-time and do not use discrete game rounds; encoded as a single repeated interaction in the model.,0.3,1,"In the PGG each cooperator contributes '1' and defectors contribute '0', but punishers and cooperators are not making a discrete choice of all vs nothing each round beyond this fixed rule; modeled as standard continuous-type PGG decisions. However, contribution level is fixed, so the available action is binary; coded as 1 since text does not frame it as an all-or-nothing choice variable.",0.3,0,Public good game where cooperators pay a cost of 1 from their own endowment; endowment is implicitly private before contribution.,0.4,N/R,"They define group return as r times total contributions divided equally among G; MPCR = r/G. However, G is not explicitly fixed for i