/A,Only simulated agents.,1,N/A,Only simulated agents.,1,N/A,Only simulated agents.,1,N/A,Only simulated agents.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation on networks without human participants.,1,"Full strategy set on lattice: TC, D, CP, DP. Group formation, payoff computation and Fermi imitation as in Sec. II. Study examines how mixing CP and DP with TC and D affects nondefection phases, mutualism, and optimal feedback sensitivity A.","Summarized from Sec. III B and four-strategy networked results in Figs. 7, 10, 11(d), 13.",1
10.1007_s11127-018-0622-5.md,Simulation – Reference scenario (low-welfare mode),"Paper describes this as the reference scenario: beta=5, RAC=0.5, RWP=0 (initial), It=5, X̄^AD=0.1 leading to domination of AD-types and low welfare (left panel of Fig. 2).",0.95,"Tax redistribution parameters and defectors-threshold: R^{AC}=0.5, R^{WP}=0 initially (no support for punishers), delay factor \bar{X}^{AD}=0.1; inflow I_t=5, beta=5; open-border inflow of all three types each period.","These parameters define this baseline condition; payoff parameters and migration are fixed; main manipulated dimensions relative to other simulations are R^{AC}, R^{WP}, and \bar{X}^{AD}.",0.92,False,No human subjects; purely theoretical evolutionary model with computer simulations.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a model-based simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only simulated agents.,1,True,Section 3 explicitly states that they run simulations of the evolutionary dynamics.,1,True,"Paper includes closed-form derivations for thresholds (e.g., \bar{X}^{WP}=(c-d)/(p-d)), alongside simulations.",0.9,300,"In each period t society has P_t = P_t^{AC}+P_t^{WP}+P_t^{AD}. Initial values P_0^{AC}=100, P_0^{WP}=100, P_0^{AD}=0; plus fixed inflow I_t=5 of EACH type per period (so +15 per period). To characterize the basic game environment they start with 200; using 300 as a typical mid-simulation size is arbitrary, so effectively population size is variable. Set to 300 as an approximate but this is not explicitly given as fixed.",0.2,1000,Average society welfare is calculated on the basis of 1000 periods in Fig. 2 description.,0.95,1,"Players in the model choose a fixed strategy (AC, AD, WP) in a prisoner’s dilemma; there is no continuous contribution level, so the notion of partial vs all-or-nothing contribution does not apply. However, mapped to cooperation choice, it is binary; encoded as 1 meaning any strategy choice is possible for types, but this mapping is imperfect.",0.2,N/A,No human participants and no explicit endowment allocation between private/public accounts; theoretical PD payoffs only.,1,N/R,"Model uses PD payoff parameters (a,b,c,d,p) rather than a linear public goods MPCR.",1,N/A,Simulation of evolutionary game; no communication between agents.,1,0,Agents adapt via replicator dynamics based on payoffs; no interface or feedback summaries as in lab experiments.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as payoff changes; no identities or observability structure like in human experiments.,1,N/A,"Tax redistribution is an aggregate ingroup transfer, not targeted identifiable rewards.",1,0,Simulated dynamics; no human awareness of horizon.,1,1,Willing Punishers impose costly direct punishment on Always Defectors (payoff p<a for both).,1,N/R,"They denote punishment costs as p^{WP} and effect on AD as p^{AD} with resulting payoff p=-1, but do not provide numeric cost per punishment unit in token terms; only resulting post-punishment payoffs.",0.5,N/R,Model gives resulting payoffs (p=-1) not a separate technology ratio of loss per cost unit.,0.5,1,Ingroup tax redistribution R^{AC} adds payoff to ingroup members; functionally a reward mechanism.,0.9,N/R,"They model a lump-sum tax τ and redistribution R_t^i, but do not specify τ numerically or per-unit cost of reward beyond budget neutrality.",0.5,N/R,No explicit mapping from one unit of tax to incremental payoff per ingroup member beyond R_t^i/P_t^i.,0.5,N/R,"Agents have no initial token endowments; only payoff parameters (a,b,c,d,p).",1,N/R,No contributions to a public good; only cooperation/defection strategies in PD and state variables (type shares).,1,N/R,Same reason as contributionRate; only payoffs and population shares are tracked.,1,N/R,They discuss ‘average welfare’ (average payoff π^S) but do not normalize it by a theoretical maximum to give efficiency 0–1.,0.7,1.28,Left panel of Fig. 2 (Mode 1) reports ‘Average welfare in society π^S=1.28’ for this reference low-welfare scenario.,0.95,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical simulation without human subjects.,1,"Evolutionary 3-type prisoner’s dilemma with payoffs a=0, b=1, c=4, d=6, p=-1; replicator dynamics with beta=5; open borders modeled as migration inflow I_t=5 of each type per period; ingroup-outgroup tax redistribution with R^{AC}=0.5 when X^{AD}<=0.1, R^{WP}=0 when X^{AD}>0.1; this scenario evolves to domination of AD (X^{AD}≈0.87, X^{AC}≈0.09, X^{WP}≈0.04).",Summarizes the key structural aspects and outcome metrics reported for this reference simulation (mode 1 in Fig. 2).,0.96
10.1007_s11127-018-0622-5.md,Simulation – High-welfare cyclical mode (baseline high-welfare scenario),"Right panel of Fig. 2 shows a scenario with R^{AC}=0.5, R^{WP}=1.0, \bar{X}^{AD}=0.1, leading to domination of non-AD types and high welfare with cycles.",0.95,"Tax redistribution parameters and defectors-threshold: R^{AC}=0.5, R^{WP}=1.0 (support for punishers once X^{AD}>0.1), delay factor \bar{X}^{AD}=0.1; inflow I_t=5, beta=5; open-border inflow each period.",These differentiate this high-welfare mode from the low-welfare reference; other structural parameters are held constant.,0.95,False,Model-based simulation with no human participants.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment; numerical simulation of differential equations/replicator dynamics.,1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,True,Section 3 explicitly runs simulations for various parameter sets.,1,True,Analytical results on thresholds accompany the simulations.,0.9,300,"Population is dynamic; initial P_0^{AC}=100, P_0^{WP}=100, P_0^{AD}=0 and each period I_t=5 of each type is added. No fixed total P_t is given; 300 entered as an approximate mid-simulation size only, thus very low confidence.",0.2,1000,Average welfare values reported in Fig. 2 are based on 1000 periods.,0.95,1,"Actions are binary cooperate/defect (and punishment embedded in type), not continuous contributions. All-or-nothing public good contribution concept does not strictly apply but binary action is closer to unrestricted than to mandated all-or-nothing in contribution sense.",0.2,N/A,No public goods endowment structure; theoretical PD only.,1,N/R,"Linear public good with MPCR is not modeled; instead, PD parameters (a,b,c,d,p) are used.",1,N/A,No human communication; population evolves via replicator dynamics.,1,0,"Agents adapt through payoff-based replication, not through viewing summaries or histories.",1,N/A,No identity structure; punishment is a payoff rule tied to types.,1,N/A,Ingroup tax advantage is not individually identified.,1,0,No human awareness; infinite/long-run dynamics simulated for 1000 periods.,1,1,Willing Punishers impose costly direct punishment on defectors (resulting payoff p=-1 for both).,1,N/R,"They specify overall post-punishment payoff p=-1 and note p^{AD}>p^{WP}>0 as costs, but not numeric per-unit cost.",0.5,N/R,No explicit fine-to-cost ratio reported.,0.5,1,"Tax redistribution R^{AC}, R^{WP} constitutes additional payoff to ingroup members (a reward).",0.9,N/R,Size of τ or per-capita reward cost is not given numerically; only aggregate R^i parameters.,0.5,N/R,Redistribution rule R_t^i/P_t^i-τ is given symbolically but not as a numeric technology parameter.,0.5,N/R,There is no explicit per-period token endowment; payoffs derive from game and redistribution.,1,N/R,No explicit contribution decisions or rates; outcome metrics are population shares and payoffs.,1,N/R,Same as above; no token contributions measured.,1,N/R,They report average welfare but do not normalize it to a theoretical maximum.,0.7,3.44,Right panel of Fig. 2 (Mode 2: high-welfare society) reports ‘Average welfare in society π^S=3.44’.,0.95,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Pure simulation environment.,1,"3-type evolutionary prisoner’s dilemma with payoffs a=0, b=1, c=4, d=6, p=-1; replicator dynamics with beta=5; open-border migration I_t=5 of each type per period; tax redistribution with R^{AC}=0.5 when X^{AD}<=0.1 and R^{WP}=1.0 when X^{AD}>0.1; delay threshold \bar{X}^{AD}=0.1. Long-run averages: X^{AC}≈0.62, X^{WP}≈0.28, X^{AD}≈0.10, payoffs π^{AC}=3.46, π^{WP}=3.43, π^{AD}=3.23, average welfare π^S=3.44; system cycles between AC- and WP-led ingroups but stays in a high-welfare regime.",Summarizes structural parameters and reported outcome statistics for the high-welfare cyclical mode in Fig. 2.,0.97
