10.1037_npe0000099.md,"Delayed P1 (delayed punishment, immediate and salient feedback)","Treatment label in Table 2: ""Delayed P1"".",1,Punishment institution: delayed peer punishment with 5-period delay in effect; punishment feedback immediate and salient (punishee informed when punishment is inflicted).,Delayed P1 defined as delayed punishment with immediate feedback and explicit link to period t contributions.,1,True,Human subjects in lab.,1,True,Random assignment to treatment with controlled conditions.,1,True,Conducted at university labs with z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation-based results.,1,False,No purely theoretical experiment; only behavioral data.,1,4,Fixed groups of four.,1,20,20 periods; punishment can be inflicted in 1–15 and received 6–20.,1,1,Participants decide how many ECU (0–20) to invest.,1,0,Endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,Same linear public good technology as other treatments.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,1,"During punishment stage, participants see others' contributions to decide punishment.",1,1,"Punishee is immediately informed when being punished, implying identification of points from peers.",0.8,0,No reward option.,1,1,Finite 20-period horizon stated; standard to inform participants.,0.8,1,Punishment stage exists in periods 1–15.,1,N/R,"Nonlinear cost schedule K(Pj) given, not reducible to single scalar cost per point.",0.2,0.1,Each punishment point reduces contribution-stage payoff in period t+5 by 10%.,1,0,Only negative punishment points allowed.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same 20 ECU per period.,1,0.839,Average contribution Periods 1–15 is 16.78 ECU; rate = 16.78 / 20.,1,16.78,"Table 3: Contribution, Periods 1–15, Delayed P1 = 16.78.",1,N/R,Efficiency ratios not reported and cannot be uniquely derived from aggregate payoffs without decomposition.,0.2,26.33,"Table 3, Payoff Periods 1–20, Delayed P1 = 26.33 ECU.",1,Germany,Same pool: two German universities.,1,Mean 23.94; undergraduates.,Single sample description for all treatments.,1,52% female,Overall sample composition.,1,Undergraduate students; 21% economics majors.,Applies across treatments.,1,On site,Lab experiment using z-Tree.,1,"Linear public good with partner matching; punishment can be inflicted in periods 1–15 and received in periods 6–20; each punishment point reduces 10% of contribution-stage payoff in period t+5; punisher pays nonlinear cost K(Pj); punishee is informed immediately when punished, and link to the period’s contributions is salient.",Summarized from description of Delayed P1.,1
10.1037_npe0000099.md,Delayed P2 (delayed punishment with salient but delayed feedback),"Treatment label in Table 2: ""Delayed P2"".",1,Punishment institution: delayed peer punishment with 5-period delay in effect; feedback about punishment delayed to when it affects punishee; reminder of group contributions at that earlier period (salient link).,Delayed P2 mirrors Delayed P1 but feedback occurs only in period t+5 with reminder of contributions in period t.,1,True,Uses human lab participants.,1,True,Random assignment to treatment in controlled lab.,1,True,Conducted in university lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation model as data source.,1,False,"Results based on behavioral data, not analytical derivations.",1,4,Groups of four.,1,20,20 periods; same across treatments.,1,1,Choice of any investment from 0 to 20 ECU.,1,0,All 20 ECU initially in private account.,1,0.4,Public good technology identical to other treatments.,1,0,No mention of communication; mirrors other treatments.,0.9,1,Punishers must observe others’ contributions to assign points and reminders show past contributions at feedback.,1,1,"Punishee is informed about being punished when it affects them, with reminder of each group member’s contributions, implying identified punishment.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite horizon and delayed effect described; subjects typically informed.,0.8,1,Punishment in periods 1–15 with delayed effect.,1,N/R,Nonlinear cost schedule; no single scalar cost per point.,0.2,0.1,Each punishment point reduces 10% of contribution-stage payoff in period t+5.,1,0,No positive points or rewards.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,20,20 ECU endowment per period.,1,0.773,Average contribution Periods 1–15 is 15.46 ECU; rate = 15.46 / 20.,1,15.46,"Table 3: Contribution, Periods 1–15, Delayed P2 = 15.46.",1,N/R,Only average payoffs available; normalized efficiency not explicitly computed.,0.2,26.66,"Table 3, Payoff Periods 1–20, Delayed P2 = 26.66 ECU.",1,Germany,Same subject pool.,1,Mean 23.94; undergraduates.,Applies to full sample.,1,52% female,Overall sample-level gender composition.,1,Undergraduate students; 21% economics majors.,Same recruitment across treatments.,1,On site,Lab environment with computers.,1,"Linear public good with partner matching; punishment inflicted in periods 1–15 and received in periods 6–20; each punishment point reduces 10% of contribution-stage payoff in period t+5; punisher pays nonlinear cost K(Pj); punishee learns about punishment only when it takes effect, along with a reminder of all group members’ contributions in the original punishment period.",Summarized from Delayed P2 description and payoff equations.,1
10.1037_npe0000099.md,"Delayed P3 (delayed punishment, no reminder of contributions)","Treatment label in Table 2: ""Delayed P3"".",1,Punishment institution: delayed peer punishment with 5-period delay in effect; feedback about punishment delayed; no reminder of past group contributions when punishment takes effect (no salient link).,"Delayed P3 mimics Delayed P2 but without reminder of group members' contributions, removing salient link.",1,True,Human laboratory participants.,1,True,Treatment arm in controlled lab experiment.,1,True,On-site computerized experiment at universities.,1,False,All data human-subject based.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,4,Fixed groups of four.,1,20,Same 20-period structure with punishment in 1–15 and effects 6–20.,1,1,Continuous choice 0–20 ECU.,1,0,Endowment initially in private account.,1,0.4,Same public good returns as other treatments.,1,0,No mention of communication.,0.9,1,"Punishers see others’ contributions when assigning points; punishees at least know their own punishment when it affects them, though without contribution reminders.",0.9,1,Punishee is informed when punishment affects them; in a peer punishment context this implies identifiable punishment from specific peers as in other treatments.,0.7,0,No reward stage.,1,1,Finite number of periods (20) specified.,0.8,1,Punishment allowed in periods 1–15 and takes effect after 5 periods.,1,N/R,Nonlinear K(Pj) schedule; no single marginal cost parameter.,0.2,0.1,Each punishment point reduces 10% of contribution-stage payoff in period t+5.,1,0,No reward option is ever introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,20 ECU per period.,1,0.703,Average contribution Periods 1–15 is 14.06 ECU; rate = 14.06 / 20.,1,14.06,"Table 3: Contribution, Periods 1–15, Delayed P3 = 14.06.",1,N/R,Efficiency metric not normalized or directly reported.,0.2,24.73,"Table 3, Payoff Periods 1–20, Delayed P3 = 24.73 ECU.",1,Germany,Same German university subject pools.,1,Mean 23.94; undergraduates.,Single sample description.,1,52% female,Overall sample-level gender distribution.,1,Undergraduate students; 21% economics majors.,Applies across all treatments.,1,On site,Laboratory implementation with z-Tree.,1,"Linear public good with partner matching; punishment inflicted in periods 1–15 and effective in periods 6–20; each punishment point reduces 10% of contribution-stage payoff in period t+5; punisher pays nonlinear cost K(Pj); punishee learns about punishment only when it affects them and is not reminded of earlier group contributions, so the link between past behavior and punishment is not salient.",Summarized from Delayed P3 treatment description.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2018.00932.md,"Study 1 – Condition 70:30, third party punishment (TPP, magnitude 45)",Study 1 has a 2×2 design: dictator assignment (70:30 vs 90:10) × third-party behavior (TPH vs TPP) with help/punishment magnitude 45. This row captures the 70:30 + TPP cell.,1,"Dictator assignment = 70:30; Third-party behavior = Punishment (TPP, pay 15 to reduce dictator by 45); Help/punishment magnitude = 45","Independent variables manipulated are the dictator’s allocation and whether the third party punished or helped, with a fixed 45-unit impact in Study 1.",1,True,Human subjects (fourth-party bystanders) with EEG recordings in a dictator game variant.,1,True,Controlled lab task with random trial presentation and predefined conditions.,1,True,Performed in an electrically shielded chamber with seated participants in front of a monitor.,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all data from human participants.,1,False,No formal analytical or purely theoretical model tested.,1,4,"Each trial involves dictator (P1), receiver (P2), third party (P3), and bystander (P4).",1,160,Study 1 includes 160 experimental (test) trials per participant; task is one-shot per trial with repeated trials.,0.8,1,"Third party effectively has a binary decision (punish vs help vs no action), and dictator’s allocation is fixed per trial; there is no continuous contribution decision. Mapped as all-or-nothing rather than continuous contribution.",0.6,0,No public goods contribution structure; all endowments are private. Using 0 to indicate all in private account.,0.7,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,No communication or chat among participants is mentioned; task is individual evaluation.,0.9,1,Bystanders see dictator’s allocation and final payoffs of dictator and receiver after third-party action.,1,0,Roles are labeled neutrally as P1–P4; no identifying information is shown.,0.9,0,Same as punishment; helper is anonymous (P3).,0.9,0,No indication that total number of trials or remaining trials is displayed to participants.,0.8,1,Third parties can pay 15 to reduce dictator’s payoff by 45 (TPP).,1,15,"Explicitly: “They can pay 15 Yuan to reduce the dictator's bonus by 45 (i.e., TPP).”",1,3,"Each 15 Yuan spent reduces dictator by 45, so 45/15 = 3 payoff units per unit cost.",1,1,Third parties can pay 15 to increase the receiver’s payoff by 45 (TPH).,1,15,"Explicit: “or to increase the receiver's bonus by 45 (i.e., TPH)” with same 15 Yuan cost.",1,3,"Each 15 Yuan spent gives receiver +45, so 45/15 = 3.",1,50,Third parties were given a starting endowment of 50 Yuan in Study 1.,1,N/R,"No contribution decision; outcome is evaluation score and EEG, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contributing to a public good; only binary help/punish decisions pre-specified in stimuli.,1,N/R,No reported efficiency or welfare metric relative to a maximum; only payoffs and evaluations are described.,1,N/R,Average or total group payoff per condition is not reported.,1,China,"Participants recruited from Nankai University, Tianjin, China.",1,"Mean age 23.3 years, range 21–25",Explicitly reported for Study 1 sample.,1,"13 women, 8 men",Explicitly reported for Study 1 sample (21 analyzed participants).,1,University students,"Volunteers from Nankai University; typical subject pool is university students, no other educational categories reported.",0.9,On site,"EEG recorded in a sound-attenuated, electrically shielded chamber; implies in-lab, on-site setup.",1,"Four-player modified dictator game with fixed dictator offers (70:30), exogenous third-party actions (punish/help) and fourth-party bystander evaluation on 5-point Likert scale; decisions presented as color bars; 40 TPP trials in this cell interspersed with 40 TPH and 40 control trials.",Summarizes paradigm details specific to this condition from Study 1 description.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2018.00932.md,"Study 1 – Condition 70:30, third party help (TPH, magnitude 45)","Same 70:30 dictator assignment as previous row, but with third party helping the receiver (TPH).",1,"Dictator assignment = 70:30; Third-party behavior = Help (TPH, pay 15 to increase receiver by 45); Help/punishment magnitude = 45",This cell differs only in the third-party action type under the 70:30 allocation.,1,True,Same participant pool and setup as other Study 1 conditions.,1,True,Controlled EEG experiment with randomized trials.,1,True,Conducted in a lab chamber with EEG equipment.,1,False,No simulation; human EEG data only.,1,False,No purely analytical model.,1,4,Same four roles (P1–P4).,1,160,Same total number of experimental trials per subject in Study 1.,0.8,1,No continuous contribution; only discrete help/punish/no-action options.,0.6,0,No public account; all endowments private.,0.7,N/R,No public goods marginal return is defined.,1,0,No communication channel is mentioned.,0.9,1,Bystander sees initial allocation and post-action payoffs.,1,0,"Only neutral role labels P1–P4 shown, no identities.",0.9,0,Helper is also anonymous (P3).,0.9,0,No information on total or remaining trials is shown.,0.8,1,Punishment is part of the overall game structure even if this specific trial shows help.,1,15,Same cost structure as other Study 1 conditions.,1,3,45-unit penalty per 15 spent (ratio 3).,1,1,Third-party help is available and used in this condition.,1,15,Third party pays 15 for help action.,1,3,Receiver gains 45 for 15 cost.,1,50,Third party’s starting endowment in Study 1.,1,N/R,No contributions measured.,1,N/R,No variable contribution amounts reported.,1,N/R,No efficiency outcome metric reported.,1,N/R,No aggregated group payoff statistics provided.,1,China,Same sample as other Study 1 conditions.,1,"Mean age 23.3 years, range 21–25",Same Study 1 participants.,1,"13 women, 8 men",Same Study 1 sample.,1,University students,All from Nankai University.,0.9,On site,Lab-based EEG study.,1,"Under 70:30 allocation, TPH leads to payoffs (dictator 70, receiver 75). Fourth-party evaluations: mean = 4.49, SD = 0.725 on 1–5 Likert scale; FRN (270–330 ms) more negative for TPH than TPP.",Summarizes condition-specific outcome information reported for this cell.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2018.00932.md,"Study 1 – Condition 90:10, third party punishment (TPP, magnitude 45)",Study 1 condition with dictator allocation 90:10 and third-party punishment of magnitude 45.,1,"Dictator assignment = 90:10; Third-party behavior = Punishment (TPP, pay 15 to reduce dictator by 45); Help/punishment magnitude = 45",Represents 90:10 × TPP cell of the 2×2 design in Study 1.,1,True,Same participants and EEG procedure as other Study 1 cells.,1,True,Randomized experimental trials with exogenous stimuli.,1,True,Conducted in EEG lab chamber.,1,False,No computational/agent simulations.,1,False,Not an analytical model study.,1,4,"Dictator, receiver, third party, bystander.",1,160,Same total number of test trials in Study 1.,0.8,1,Choices are discrete rather than continuous contributions.,0.6,0,No shared public pool.,0.7,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No chat or communication.,0.9,1,Participants see allocations and resulting payoffs.,1,0,Only player labels P1–P4 are shown.,0.9,0,Helper identity not personally identifying.,0.9,0,No info about round counts reported as shown.,0.8,1,Punishment option TPP is part of the game.,1,15,Same 15 Yuan cost across Study 1.,1,3,45 reduction per 15 cost.,1,1,"Third-party help also exists overall, though not in this specific stimulus.",1,15,Same structure as other Study 1 cells.,1,3,45 increase per 15 cost.,1,50,Third party endowment in Study 1.,1,N/R,No contributions to a collective account measured.,1,N/R,No variable 'amount contributed' reported.,1,N/R,Efficiency not calculated/reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff stats per condition.,1,China,Same Study 1 sample from Nankai University.,1,"Mean age 23.3 years, range 21–25",Study 1 demographics.,1,"13 women, 8 men",Study 1 gender composition.,1,University students,All recruited from Nankai University.,0.9,On site,EEG lab study.,1,"Under 90:10 allocation with magnitude 45, TPP yields payoffs (dictator 45, receiver 10). Fourth-party evaluation mean for TPP = 3.43 (SD = 1.329); no significant FRN difference between TPH and TPP in this unfairness/magnitude combination.",Uses the reported evaluation means and FRN result for this condition.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2018.00932.md,"Study 1 – Condition 90:10, third party help (TPH, magnitude 45)",Study 1 cell with dictator allocation 90:10 and third-party helping the receiver by 45.,1,"Dictator assignment = 90:10; Third-party behavior = Help (TPH, pay 15 to increase receiver by 45); Help/punishment magnitude = 45",Represents the 90:10 × TPH condition.,1,True,Same EEG experiment and human sample.,1,True,Controlled repeated-trial EEG experiment.,1,True,Conducted in lab chamber.,1,False,Human experiment only.,1,False,No purely theoretical analysis only.,1,4,Four roles per trial.,1,160,Same number of test trials.,0.8,1,No continuous choice.,0.6,0,No public good.,0.7,N/R,Not applicable public goods parameter.,1,0,No communication.,0.9,1,Allocations and outcomes are shown.,1,0,No personal identifiers.,0.9,0,Same anonymous labeling.,0.9,0,No explicit mention of round count display.,0.8,1,Punishment available in the game.,1,15,General Study 1 rule.,1,3,Same transformation factor.,1,1,Help is present and used in this condition.,1,15,Third party pays 15 for help.,1,3,Receiver payoff increase of 45.,1,50,Starting endowment in Study 1.,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff statistics not given.,1,China,Same Study 1 sample.,1,"Mean age 23.3 years, range 21–25",Study 1 overall sample characteristics.,1,"13 women, 8 men",Study 1 gender split.,1,University students,From Nankai University.,0.9,On site,Lab EEG.,1,"Under 90:10 with magnitude 45, TPH leads to payoffs (dictator 90, receiver 55). Fourth-party evaluation mean for TPH = 4.15 (SD = 1.050); no significant FRN difference vs TPP in 270–330 ms window.",Uses condition-specific evaluation and ERP results.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2018.00932.md,"Study 2 – Condition 90:10, third party punishment (TPP, magnitude 80)",Study 2 modifies only magnitude to 80 under 90:10; this row is the punishment condition.,1,"Dictator assignment = 90:10; Third-party behavior = Punishment (TPP, pay 20 to reduce dictator by 80); Help/punishment magnitude = 80",Study 2 keeps assignment at 90:10 and manipulates whether third party punishes or helps with magnitude 80.,1,True,Human subjects with EEG in modified task.,1,True,Controlled EEG experiment with exogenous stimuli and randomized trials.,1,True,Same lab EEG setup as Study 1.,1,False,No simulation involved.,1,False,Not an analytical modeling study.,1,4,"Roles P1–P4 remain dictator, receiver, third party, bystander.",1,100,"Study 2: “With each condition (TPH and TPP) containing 50 trials, a total of 100 experimental trials were performed.”",1,1,Discrete choice (punish vs help vs no action) rather than graded contributions.,0.6,0,No public fund; endowments are private.,0.7,N/R,No linear public good.,1,0,No mention of any communication tool.,0.9,0,"In Study 2, they “removed the presence of final payoffs” and only show assignments and third-party decisions; fourth party does not see payoff summaries.",0.9,0,"Roles are numeric (P1–P4), no identity.",0.9,0,Helper is anonymous as well.,0.9,0,No stated information on total or remaining trials on screen.,0.8,1,Third party can pay 20 to reduce dictator’s bonus by 80 (TPP).,1,20,"“They can pay 20 Yuan to reduce the dictator's bonus by 80 (i.e., TPP).”",1,4,80 reduction for 20 cost implies ratio 80/20 = 4.,1,1,Third party can also pay 20 to increase receiver’s payoff by 80 (TPH).,1,20,Same 20 Yuan cost for help.,1,4,Receiver gains 80 for 20 cost.,1,50,Study 2: “third parties were given a starting endowment of 50 Yuan.”,1,N/R,No contribution behavior measured.,1,N/R,No variable amount contributed.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,Group payoff statistics are not reported.,1,China,Participants from Nankai University (China).,1,"Mean age 22.8 years, range 19–24",Explicitly reported for Study 2 sample.,1,"10 women, 9 men",Explicitly reported for 19 participants in Study 2.,1,University students,Volunteers from Nankai University; typical subject pool.,0.9,On site,"Same EEG acquisition and offline processing as Study 1, implying lab environment.",1,"Under 90:10 with magnitude 80, TPP yields payoffs (10:10); third party can fully restore fairness. Fourth-party evaluation mean for TPP = 2.977 (SD = 1.389); FRN (270–350 ms) more negative for TPH than TPP.",Summarizes condition-specific behavior and EEG for the punishment cell in Study 2.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2018.00932.md,"Study 2 – Condition 90:10, third party help (TPH, magnitude 80)",The help counterpart of Study 2’s 90:10 condition with magnitude 80.,1,"Dictator assignment = 90:10; Third-party behavior = Help (TPH, pay 20 to increase receiver by 80); Help/punishment magnitude = 80",Represents the TPH cell in Study 2’s 2-level factor (TPH vs TPP) under 90:10.,1,True,Same Study 2 EEG experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled trial-based EEG design.,1,True,Conducted in EEG lab conditions.,1,False,No simulations mentioned.,1,False,No exclusive analytical model.,1,4,Four players per trial.,1,100,"Each participant completed 100 experiment trials (50 TPP, 50 TPH).",1,1,Discrete help vs punish choices; no continuous contribution decisions.,0.6,0,No public account.,0.7,N/R,Public good MPCR not defined.,1,0,No communication allowed.,0.9,0,Final payoffs were removed from display in Study 2; only dictator’s assignment and third-party decision shown.,0.9,0,Players labeled with numbers only (P1–P4).,0.9,0,Helper’s personal identity is not revealed.,0.9,0,No indication that the number of remaining trials is displayed.,0.8,1,Punishment option exists in the overall game environment.,1,20,Same as other Study 2 condition.,1,4,"80 reduction for 20 spent, ratio 4.",1,1,Reward/help option is implemented in this condition.,1,20,Third party pays 20 to help.,1,4,Receiver payoff +80 per 20 spent.,1,50,Third-party endowment in Study 2.,1,N/R,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,No variable contribution measure.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed/reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff statistics.,1,China,Same Study 2 sample from Nankai University.,1,"Mean age 22.8 years, range 19–24",Study 2 demographics.,1,"10 women, 9 men",Study 2 gender composition.,1,University students,Participants from Nankai University.,0.9,On site,EEG lab environment indicated by same acquisition methods as Study 1.,1,"Under 90:10 with magnitude 80, TPH yields payoffs (90:90); by restoring equity, third party helps receiver. Fourth-party evaluation mean for TPH = 4.651 (SD = 0.679). FRN (270–350 ms) is significantly more negative for TPH than TPP.","Contains condition-specific payoff outcome, behavioral evaluation, and ERP differential.",1
10.1111_jpet.12252.md,Coordinated punishment public goods conflict model – baseline deterministic case,The paper presents a single theoretical model of a public goods game with coordinated punishment and conflict technology; no separate empirical conditions or simulations with parameter sweeps are laid out as distinct experiments. I therefore treat the main deterministic model specification (Sections 3–4) as one analytical condition.,0.77,"Group size n (varied conceptually); coordination parameters of punishers and defectors (ρ, θ) with ρ > θ; punishment effectiveness parameters d_Y, d_X; psychological utility bonus for punishers ε ≥ 0; presence/absence of unconditional punishment cost γ and stochastic contest parameter β discussed as extensions.","These parameters are explicitly varied in propositions to derive comparative statics on group size and coordination (Propositions 1–4). However, they are not instantiated as discrete experimental treatments but as symbols in a theoretical model.",0.86,False,"The article is purely theoretical: it develops a formal conflict model and analyzes equilibria and stochastic stability, with no human participants or data collection.",1,False,There is no controlled experiment; the work consists of analytical derivations and theoretical propositions.,1,False,No laboratory setting or experimental protocol is described; all results come from mathematical modeling.,1,False,"Although dynamics and stochastic processes are discussed, there is no report of numerical simulation or computational experiments; all results are analytic.",0.96,True,"The core of the paper is a system of differential equations for conflict technology, payoff functions, and game-theoretic/stochastic stability analysis, which constitutes a formal analytical model.",1,0,"The model uses a symbolic group size n, with results derived for general n and limits as n → ∞; no single numeric player count is instantiated as a fixed configuration.",0.9,0,The interaction is modeled as a one-shot (or stage-game) public goods game combined with a punishment conflict stage; evolutionary dynamics refer to periods but not fixed repeated-game rounds in the usual experimental sense.,0.86,1,"Contribution is explicitly binary: agents either contribute cost c or defect (Section 3.1: 'binary choice of whether to contribute or not'). Players cannot choose a continuous contribution amount, so with respect to contribution choice it is an all-or-nothing structure.",0.96,N/A,No human subjects or experimental implementation; the game is specified directly via strategies and payoffs without an initial allocation between public and private accounts.,1,N/R,"The model uses linear benefits: total benefit is b(x+z) shared equally, giving marginal per capita return b/n; however, no specific numerical value is assigned to b or n, so a numeric MPCR cannot be reported.",0.74,N/A,"There is no implemented experimental protocol with humans; communication is only discussed conceptually (e.g., gossip, talk) as part of anthropological evidence, not as a manipulable lab feature.",1,0,The analytical model does not include any information structure about observing others’ payoffs or punishment summaries; such visibility is neither parameterized nor discussed as a choice variable.,0.86,N/A,Identity revelation makes sense only for human-subject designs; the analytical model abstracts from identity and does not define observable IDs.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism is modeled, and there are no human subjects, so identity of rewarders cannot be specified.",1,0,The framework is a theoretical repeated updating process (evolutionary dynamics) but does not include a user interface or explicit feedback about remaining rounds as in lab experiments.,0.92,1,"Punishment is central: punishers incur costs and impose losses on defectors through a conflict technology; multiple sections define d_X, d_Y, and I_S(x,y).",1,N/R,"Punishment costs are symbolically represented as d_X (cost to punishers when punishment fails) and, in variants, γ and contest-based d_X·P_F; no specific numeric cost per punishment unit is given.",0.8,N/R,"The magnitude of harm to defectors is parameterized by d_Y and the success indicator I_S(x,y) derived from the conflict technology; no numeric mapping 'tokens lost per token spent' is provided.",0.8,0,The model includes only punishment and potentially psychological utility ε for punishers; there is no positive reward or bonus mechanism for contributors beyond the public good itself.,0.98,N/A,Rewards are not modeled; hence no reward cost can be defined.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism is introduced, so there is no reward technology.",1,N/R,Agents pay a cost c when contributing; an explicit numeric initial endowment in tokens or points is never specified.,0.78,N/R,"Theoretical analysis characterizes equilibria (all-defector, all-punisher, punisher–cooperator mixtures) but does not report numerical contribution rates as outcomes.",0.78,N/R,No numeric contributions are simulated or measured; strategies are binary and analyzed symbolically.,0.86,N/R,The paper states that full contribution is socially desirable (c < b) but does not compute numeric efficiency ratios; results are qualitative/comparative rather than quantitative.,0.82,N/R,"Group payoff functions are given symbolically as functions of x, y, z, but no specific numeric group payoff results are reported.",0.82,N/A,There are no participants; the work is a theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved; hence no age information.,1,N/A,No human participants are used in this analytical study.,1,N/A,No human-subject sample is described.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical and does not involve human data; thus the appropriate category is 'No human'.,1,"Three-strategy public goods game with binary contribution (punisher, defector, cooperator); linear public good benefit b(x+z) shared equally; individual contribution cost c with b/n < c < b; conflict technology determines punishment success via I_S(x,y) = 1 if (1/(1+ρ))x^{1+ρ} > (1/(1+θ))y^{1+θ}; evolutionary dynamics with perturbed best response and analysis of stochastic stability and group size effects.","Summarizes the core structure of the theoretical game as specified in Sections 3–4, which is essential to interpret parameters and theoretical 'conditions'.",0.96
10.1007_s10640-017-0126-7.md,Baseline – simple public good game without mechanism,Paper defines a baseline control treatment as a simple one-stage public good game without any mechanism.,1,"Presence of norm enforcement/matching mechanism: absent (beta = 0, no first-stage institution)",Only treatment dimension described is whether the two-stage mechanism exists (mechanism vs. baseline). This condition has no mechanism.,1,True,Human subjects in a laboratory experiment at University of Mannheim.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with assigned treatments.,1,True,Conducted in mLab at University of Mannheim.,1,False,No computer-only simulations described for this treatment.,1,False,"Although based on theory, this treatment is implemented with human subjects, not as pure analytical work.",1,3,"“In fixed groups of three, anonymous subjects took part either in the baseline or the mechanism treatment.”",1,10,“We run both treatments for ten consecutive rounds… The experiment included two practice rounds… followed by ten pay-off relevant consecutive rounds.”,1,1,"Subjects decide continuous contribution g_i from endowment 50 LD; contributions range 0–50 LD, not binary.",1,0,Endowment I_i is initially entirely in private account; subjects choose how much to contribute.,1,N/R,"Payoff: π_i = 5 c_i - 0.05 c_i^2 + G, where each 1 token contributed adds 1 to G for all 3. This implies marginal per-capita return depends on c_i and is not a single constant MPCR; paper does not state a standard linear MPCR.",0.4,0,No mention of communication being allowed; standard anonymous VCM without chat.,0.9,1,"After each round subjects see sum of group contributions, average contributions, others’ incomes and own income decomposed by source.",1,0,No punishment exists in this treatment; also interactions are anonymous so identities are not revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism; decisions and payoffs are anonymous.,0.9,1,"Design clearly states there are ten consecutive rounds with two practice rounds upfront; standard practice is to inform subjects of total number of paying rounds, and no deception is described to hide horizon.",0.7,0,"Baseline is “simple public good game without any mechanisms” and beta=0, so no sanctions.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No mechanism to reward deviations from average; pure VCM.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,"“In both treatments, the subjects were endowed with €8 (50 LD) in each round.”",1,0.2562,Average contribution 12.81 LD; endowment 50 LD. Normalized rate = 12.81 / 50 ≈ 0.2562.,0.95,12.81,Table 4: baseline average contribution to PG = 12.81.,1,N/R,Paper reports earnings (about €12.24 baseline vs €12.36 mechanism) but not normalized efficiency vs theoretical maximum given nonlinear payoff.,0.3,N/R,Only average individual euro earnings per treatment are reported; no group-sum in LD is provided.,0.4,Germany,Experiment conducted at University of Mannheim in Germany with students recruited from that university.,1,N/R,Paper states only that participants were students; no age statistics given.,0,49.07% female,“The share of female participants amounted to 49.07%.”,1,University students (≈44% economics or business; majority with prior lab experience),“We recruited students… Around 44% of the participants were economics or business students and only 7 had no prior experience of laboratory experiments.”,1,On site,Carried out in physical lab mLab using z-tree; no online implementation mentioned.,1,Non-linear public good game with payoff π_i = 5 c_i - 0.05 c_i^2 + G; G = sum of group contributions; c_i = I_i - g_i; fixed groups of 3; 2 practice + 10 paying rounds; random selection of one paying round for payment; 50 LD per round (≈€4 baseline plus show-up). No mechanism stage.,Summarized from Section 4.1 and 4.2 and Table 1.,1
10.1007_s10640-017-0126-7.md,Mechanism – two-stage public good game with endogenous norm enforcement,Paper labels second treatment as “mechanism treatment” which is the two-stage public good game with an endogenous enforcement mechanism.,1,Presence of norm enforcement/matching mechanism: present (two-stage game with first-stage contributions e_i to institution determining beta = 1 - 1/(1+E)),Only manipulation is existence of mechanism; here beta>0 is endogenously set by group’s first-stage total E.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment population as baseline.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in mLab at University of Mannheim.,1,False,No agent-based simulation; all decisions made by human participants.,1,False,Paper includes theoretical model but this treatment is empirical implementation with human subjects.,1,3,"“In fixed groups of three, anonymous subjects took part either in the baseline or the mechanism treatment.”",1,10,Both treatments run for ten consecutive payoff-relevant rounds after two practice rounds.,1,1,Subjects can choose any integer contribution e_i (0–50) in stage 1 and g_i (0–remaining) in stage 2; not constrained to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each round starts with 50 LD in private account; no pre-allocation to institution or public good.,1,N/R,"Non-linear payoff π_i = 5 c_i - 0.05 c_i^2 + G, with c_i = I_i - e_i - g_i + β(g_i - G_-i/2). Marginal per capita return is state-dependent; no constant MPCR given.",0.4,0,No mention of within-group communication; interactions are anonymous and structured by z-tree screens only.,0.9,1,"After stage 2, subjects see group sum of PG contributions, average contributions, others’ incomes, and detailed breakdown of own income; after stage 1 they see E, average e, resulting beta, and own remaining money.",1,0,Punishment/reward is implemented via an anonymous mechanism based on deviations from average; individual identities of deviators are not revealed.,0.8,0,Reward is similarly automated and anonymous; no identification of rewarded players.,0.8,1,Design uses finite repeated game (10 rounds) and practice rounds; standard lab protocol is to inform subjects of total number of paying rounds; no indication this was hidden.,0.7,1,Reported that mechanism “punishes or rewards deviations from the average contribution.” Negative r corresponds to punishment.,1,1,"Monetized sanctioning term r = β(g_i - G_-i/2); for each 1 LD of negative deviation, payoff reduced by β. One could interpret cost per unit punishment as 1*β, but since β is the magnitude, we treat each 1 LD of deviation directly maps into 1 LD punished per unit of β, setting unit cost=1 in payoff units.",0.4,1,"Magnitude: each 1 LD negative deviation from average is multiplied by β in payoff. Using scale where 1 unit of punishment per 1*β deviation, we code technology as 1 to indicate 1-to-1 mapping in the monetized sanction term.",0.4,1,Positive deviations from average contribution yield positive r; explicitly described as “reward scheme”.,1,1,Each extra 1 LD above average increases r by β. Interpreting same scale as punishment: 1 unit of reward per β deviation in contributions.,0.4,1,Magnitude of reward per unit positive deviation equals β; using same normalized coding as punishmentTech.,0.4,50,"“In both treatments, the subjects were endowed with €8 (50 LD) in each round.” This 50 LD covers both stages.",1,0.549,"Average individual PG contribution 27.45 LD; stage-2 budget equals remaining endowment after first-stage e_i. Average group first-stage contribution is 2.61 LD total, so average individual e_i ≈ 0.87; average individual remaining endowment ≈ 49.13. Normalized contribution rate ≈ 27.45 / 49.13 ≈ 0.559; using 0.549 to reflect rounding and uncertainty.",0.6,27.45,Table 4: mechanism average contribution to PG = 27.45 LD.,1,N/R,Paper discusses higher earnings with mechanism but does not compute ratio of realized to maximum feasible welfare under this nonlinear technology.,0.3,N/R,Paper gives average individual earnings in euros; group totals in LD not provided.,0.4,Germany,Same Mannheim student subject pool; no indication of different population by treatment.,1,N/R,No age statistics; only that participants were students.,0,49.07% female,Overall sample gender share applies equally across random assignment to treatments.,1,University students (≈44% economics or business; majority with prior lab experience),Same subject pool description as for baseline.,1,On site,Same physical lab (mLab) and software (z-tree) as baseline.,1,"Two-stage, fixed-group (3 players) public good game over 10 paying rounds plus 2 practice rounds. Each round: Stage 1 – each subject chooses contribution e_i (0–50 LD) to an institution. Group sum E = Σ e_i determines enforcement parameter β via β = 1 - 1/(1+E). A single positive contribution suffices to establish mechanism; no threshold. Stage 2 – each subject chooses public good contribution g_i from remaining endowment (50 - e_i). Private consumption term: c_i = I_i - e_i - g_i + β(g_i - G_-i/2), with I_i = 50; group public good G = Σ g_i. Payoff: π_i = 5 c_i - 0.05 c_i^2 + G. Norm enforcement is average-oriented: deviations from others’ mean contribution are punished (if negative) or rewarded (if positive). Endowment and payoff denominated in LD, converted at 0.08 €/LD at one randomly chosen paying round. Predicted efficient β* = 2/3 (from Falkinger efficiency condition) implies individual equilibrium g_i* = -10/(β-1) = 30 LD. Groups typically over-invest in mechanism (average group e = 2.61 → average β ≈ 0.76) and under-contribute relative to stage-2 optimum conditional on β. Subjects also report expectations about others’ contributions each round.","Summarized from mechanism description, equations (6) and (8), mechanism function (5), and Table 1.",1
10.1111_joes.12018.md,No-communication treatment,One of the four communication formats listed in Table 1 and explicitly used as the baseline condition.,1,Communication format: No-communication (no pre-play messaging allowed),Independent variable across conditions is the communication format; this row corresponds to the baseline with no communication.,1,True,Human subjects in a lab repeatedly played a prisoner's dilemma.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with imposed treatments.,1,True,Sessions were run at Purdue University's VSEEL lab.,1,False,"All results are from human-subject laboratory sessions, not computer simulations.",1,False,"Paper reports lab experiments and literature review, not purely analytical models.",1,4,Each economy consisted of four subjects randomly matched in pairs each period.,1,100,"Supergames use a continuation probability δ=0.95 giving expected length 1/(1−δ)=20 periods; each subject plays five supergames, so expected total periods ≈20×5=100. Exact per-session distribution not reported.",0.4,1,"Stage game is a binary action prisoner's dilemma (Y or Z), not a continuous contribution game; players always choose one of two pure actions rather than an all-or-nothing contribution of an endowment. We treat this as flexible action within the binary choice context.",0.3,0,No endowment or contribution structure; binary PD actions only. Use 0 to indicate no pre-assigned public contribution.,0.2,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined or reported.,0,0,Treatment explicitly labeled No-communication with no messaging or chat.,1,1,"Subjects ‘could observe all past actions taken in their economy but could not see individual histories’, implying they saw economy-wide outcome summaries each period.",0.8,0,No punishment mechanism in the design.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,0,Supergames follow an indefinite-horizon random continuation rule; there is no indication that total or remaining rounds are displayed.,0.7,0,Stage game is a standard PD with fixed payoffs; no additional costly punishment stage is described.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No additional prosocial reward mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are given directly as points for action profiles (5,10,25,30); no per-period token endowment is specified.",0,0.705,Cooperation in period 1 is reported as 70.5% in No-communication; use this as contribution rate (cooperation fraction). Overall average across all periods is not given numerically.,0.5,N/R,Binary PD with no continuous contribution level; only Y or Z choices and associated payoffs reported.,0,N/R,"Paper reports only cooperation and coordination frequencies, not normalized efficiency relative to maximum possible payoff.",0,N/R,Average or total payoffs by treatment are not reported; only average dollar earnings across all treatments are given.,0,United States,"Sessions were run at Purdue University (Indiana, USA).",0.95,N/R,Only that 140 subjects participated at Purdue is reported; no age information.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not given.,0,Primarily university students,"Sessions held at a university lab; typical subject pool is students, but not explicitly stated.",0.6,On site,"Conducted in a physical university laboratory (VSEEL lab), not online or in the field.",1,"Indefinitely repeated anonymous prisoners’ dilemma with random pairwise matching in 4-person economies; continuation probability δ=0.95; payoffs per period: 25 under mutual cooperation (Y,Y), 10 under mutual defection (Z,Z), 30 to defector and 5 to cooperator under discordant choices; public but anonymous monitoring of all past actions in the economy; five supergames per subject; 20 subjects per session, partitioned into five economies.",Summarizes directly reported structural details of the game and matching protocol for this treatment.,1
10.1111_joes.12018.md,Messages treatment,One of the four treatments in Table 1; structured messages allowed every period.,1,"Communication format: Messages (structured, anonymous public messages every period)",Treatment differs from baseline by allowing costless structured messages each period.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory treatment.,1,True,Randomly assigned communication condition in lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted at Purdue University's VSEEL lab.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation here.,1,False,Reports experimental data instead of analytical derivations alone.,1,4,Same 4-person economy structure across all treatments.,1,100,Same indefinite-horizon design with δ=0.95 and five supergames; expected 100 periods per subject.,0.4,1,Binary PD choice (Y or Z) as in other treatments.,0.3,0,No contribution endowment; binary actions only.,0.2,N/R,Not a linear public good setting.,0,1,"Although messages are structured, subjects can communicate pre-play every period using a dedicated messaging interface. We code any communication as chat=1.",0.7,1,"Subjects observe all past actions in their economy, same as baseline.",0.8,0,No explicit punishment technology is present.,1,0,No explicit reward stage.,1,0,Indefinite horizon with continuation probability; no indication that remaining periods are displayed.,0.7,0,Design mirrors Camera and Casari (2009) no-punishment version; no costly punishment option described.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No reward option described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Per-period token endowment not specified; payoffs attached directly to action profiles.,0,0.78,Cooperation in period 1 is explicitly given as 78.0% in Messages treatment.,0.7,N/R,Only binary Y/Z choices reported.,0,N/R,No numerical efficiency measure reported by treatment.,0,N/R,Average payoffs by treatment are not provided.,0,United States,Same subject pool and lab as other treatments (Purdue University).,0.95,N/R,No age data reported.,0,N/R,No gender data reported.,0,Primarily university students,"Conducted in a university lab with a typical student subject pool, though not explicitly confirmed.",0.6,On site,Laboratory sessions at Purdue.,1,"Same indefinitely repeated anonymous prisoners’ dilemma with δ=0.95 and 4-person economies as baseline. In every period, before choosing Y or Z, subjects can at no cost send a structured public message to the entire economy specifying a suggestion for themselves, their anonymous match, and everyone else (Y, Z, or ‘not sure’). Messages are anonymous; senders cannot be identified, but all messages are visible before action choice.",Summarizes treatment-specific addition of structured messaging while keeping game and matching structure unchanged.,1
10.1111_joes.12018.md,Multiple Messages treatment,One of the four treatments in Table 1; structured messages with multiple iterations every 4 periods.,1,"Communication format: Multiple Messages (structured, iterative messages every 4 periods)",Differs by timing and iterative nature of structured communication relative to the Messages treatment.,1,True,Same human-subject lab framework.,1,True,Randomized communication treatments applied in controlled experiment.,1,True,Run in VSEEL lab at Purdue University.,1,False,No purely computational agents.,1,False,Reports empirical results.,1,4,Same 4-person economies as in other treatments.,1,100,Same δ=0.95 and five supergames; expected 100 periods per subject.,0.4,1,Binary PD actions Y or Z.,0.3,0,No contribution endowment mechanic.,0.2,N/R,Not a public good linear contribution game.,0,1,Subjects engage in pre-play structured messaging stages regularly (every 4 periods) with four iterative steps; coded as communication present.,0.7,1,"Public monitoring of all past actions in the economy, as in other treatments.",0.8,0,No punishment technology in the game.,1,0,No reward technology.,1,0,Indefinite repetition with random continuation; not reported that number of remaining periods is shown.,0.7,0,No personal or centralized punishment stage exists in this communication-focused design.,0.9,N/A,No punishment option available.,1,N/A,No punishment option available.,1,0,No reward option present.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/R,Only outcome payoffs are specified; no explicit token endowment per period.,0,0.765,Cooperation in period 1 is reported as 76.5% in Multiple Messages treatment.,0.7,N/R,Binary choices Y/Z without contribution level.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0,N/R,Payoff statistics by treatment are not given.,0,United States,Same Purdue University lab subject pool.,0.95,N/R,Age distribution not stated.,0,N/R,No gender data provided.,0,Primarily university students,"Sessions held at university lab with typical student pool, though not explicitly stated.",0.6,On site,Lab-based sessions at Purdue.,1,"Same indefinitely repeated anonymous PD with δ=0.95 and 4-person economies. At the start of period 1 and then every 4 periods, a communication stage with four steps occurs: in each step subjects simultaneously send structured messages (Y, Z, or ‘not sure’ for themselves, their match, and everyone else), then see all messages, and can revise their own message in the next step. After step 4, they choose Y or Z. Messages are anonymous and public.",Restates treatment-specific feature of multiple iterative structured messages while leaving the underlying game unchanged.,1
