10.1080_10576100490438246.md,Fehr & Gächter public goods experiment – punishment condition,The paper summarizes Fehr and Gächter’s public goods experiments and contrasts conditions where altruistic punishment is possible vs. forbidden; this row corresponds to the condition where punishment was allowed.,0.85,Availability of costly punishment of defectors: punishment allowed,Text explicitly contrasts ‘Punishment and non-punishment experimental conditions’; here the independent variable is whether punishment is possible.,0.95,True,"Described as ‘public goods experiments’ with ‘subjects’ given money and choices, indicating human participants.",1,True,Randomly varied institutional condition (punishment vs. no punishment) in a controlled public-goods game.,1,True,Fehr & Gächter’s public goods studies are standard economic lab experiments; no field or online setting suggested.,0.9,False,"Human subjects, not agent-based or numerical simulation.",1,False,"Results are from behavioral experiments, not a purely mathematical model.",1,4,"Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002) standard public goods design uses groups of 4; although not restated in this article, it is fixed by the cited design.",0.8,10,"Canonical Fehr & Gächter public goods punishment experiments use 10 periods; inferred from original design, not from this article’s summary.",0.7,1,"Subjects ‘were given the opportunity to invest in a group project … or to keep the funds’; Fehr & Gächter allow any integer contribution between 0 and the endowment, not only all-or-nothing.",0.8,0,"In standard public goods games, all endowment starts in the private account, and subjects actively choose any contribution.",0.8,0.4,"In Fehr & Gächter’s design, each token contributed yields 1.6 tokens divided among 4 players; MPCR = 1.6/4 = 0.4.",0.75,0,No mention of communication; Fehr & Gächter’s design prohibits communication between subjects.,0.8,1,"Subjects could punish ‘after information was provided as to how much each had invested’, implying feedback about others’ contributions.",0.9,0,Fehr & Gächter’s punishment is anonymous in the standard design; identities of punishers are not revealed in feedback.,0.7,0,No rewarding mechanism in this design; only punishment exists.,1,1,"In standard repeated public goods lab experiments, total number of periods is announced; inferred from original design, not this article.",0.7,1,‘Subjects could punish others … each punishment of another subject was costly.’,1,1,"‘Subjects who chose to punish were required to forfeit an amount equal to one third of the monetary punishment imposed’; i.e., cost-to-impact ratio is 1:3, so cost per “punishment point” is normalized as 1.",0.75,3,"For each 1 unit cost to the punisher, the defector loses 3 units (‘one third of the monetary punishment’).",0.85,0,Only punishment is described; no rewarding mechanism mentioned in these experiments.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,20,"Fehr & Gächter (2000, 2002) give each subject 20 tokens per period; inferred from the original design.",0.75,N/R,This article reports qualitative differences (‘significant increase in investment’) but no specific contribution rates.,0.1,N/R,No numeric average contribution amounts are given in the text.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to social optimum) is not quantified in this article.,0.1,N/R,No specific payoff figures or averages are reported.,0.1,Switzerland,"Fehr & Gächter’s experiments were conducted at the University of Zurich; nationality not stated here, but location implies Swiss lab; coded by country of lab.",0.6,N/R,This article does not report ages; original papers indicate university students but without precise age ranges in this summary.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0.1,University students,"Fehr & Gächter’s lab studies typically recruit university students; this article does not specify, so inferred from original design.",0.7,On site,Standard economics lab experiments with physical presence in a university laboratory rather than online.,0.8,"Standard linear public goods game; each period, subjects receive an endowment, choose how much to invest in a group project vs. keep privately; contributions are multiplied (>1) and evenly shared; after feedback on each member’s contribution, subjects in this condition can assign costly punishment to others.",Structure described in the article plus standard Fehr & Gächter design details.,0.85
10.1080_10576100490438246.md,Fehr & Gächter public goods experiment – no-punishment condition,The article contrasts ‘Punishment and non-punishment experimental conditions’; this row corresponds to the condition where punishment was forbidden.,0.85,Availability of costly punishment of defectors: punishment forbidden,Independent variable is whether punishment is possible; this condition disables punishment.,0.95,True,Same human-subject experimental study as the punishment condition.,1,True,"Within the same controlled lab experiment, only the punishment option is removed.",1,True,Companion treatment in Fehr & Gächter’s lab study.,0.9,False,Not a computer simulation.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,4,Same group size as the punishment treatment in the Fehr & Gächter design.,0.8,10,Same repeated-game structure as in the punishment condition.,0.7,1,Contribution decision structure is identical; any portion of endowment can be contributed.,0.8,0,Endowment starts in private account; no pre-assigned public contribution.,0.8,0.4,Underlying public goods technology is unchanged.,0.75,0,No communication channel is added in this treatment.,0.8,1,"Subjects still need to see others’ contributions to form expectations, despite no punishment option; Fehr & Gächter report contribution feedback in both conditions.",0.75,0,"Punishment option is disabled, so showing punishment identity is irrelevant; coded 0.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism in this design.,1,1,Same repeated-game information as in the punishment condition; total number of periods is typically announced.,0.7,0,"‘Where punishment was forbidden, the average investment was much lower.’",1,N/A,Punishment is disabled in this condition.,1,N/A,Punishment is disabled in this condition.,1,0,No reward option described.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same per-period token endowment as in the companion punishment treatment.,0.75,N/R,Article only notes that ‘average investment was much lower’ without numbers.,0.1,N/R,No numeric contribution levels reported.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency outcomes are not quantified in this article.,0.1,N/R,No specific payoff information reported.,0.1,Switzerland,Same lab and sampling frame as the punishment condition (University of Zurich).,0.6,N/R,Ages not provided in this summary.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,University students,Companion treatment within same student subject pool.,0.7,On site,Same physical lab environment as other treatment.,0.8,"Identical linear public goods game as in punishment condition, but after observing others’ contributions, subjects cannot assign any costly punishment; only contribution decisions affect payoffs.",Described as the comparison ‘where punishment was forbidden’; structure of contribution stage is unchanged.,0.85
10.1080_10576100490438246.md,Ultimatum game – standard condition,"The paper summarizes classic ‘two players … split a sum of money’ ultimatum game experiments, reporting modal offers and rejection rates.",0.85,Ultimatum bargaining with free choice of offer; responder can accept or reject,The independent variable within this described condition is the structure of the ultimatum game; no between-condition manipulation is described in this article.,0.6,True,"Described laboratory behavior: ‘Two players are given an opportunity to split a sum of money; the first proposes a division, if the second player accepts, the game is over …’",1,True,Ultimatum game studies are controlled experiments in behavioral economics.,1,True,"Referenced as canonical lab experiments (e.g., Sanfey et al. 2003) without field or online context.",0.9,False,Actual human subjects splitting real money.,1,False,"Behavioral results, not an analytical derivation.",1,2,Ultimatum game involves one proposer and one responder.,1,1,Standard ultimatum game experiments are one-shot or treated as single plays; no repetition mentioned here.,0.8,1,"Proposer ‘proposes a division’ with typically continuous or discrete splits across the whole amount, not constrained to all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,There is no predefined contribution; proposer chooses a split from a full private endowment.,0.8,N/R,"Ultimatum game is a bargaining game, not a linear public good; MPCR is not defined.",1,0,No communication or negotiation is described beyond making the offer.,0.9,0,Only two players with direct offers; no summaries of others’ behavior are provided.,0.9,0,"There is no explicit punishment mechanism; rejection is strategic, not a targeted, identity-revealed punishment.",1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Game is effectively one-shot; no notion of remaining rounds is relevant.,0.9,0,"While rejection reduces payoffs, there is no dedicated costly punishment stage; it is part of the game’s strategic structure.",0.8,N/A,No standalone punishment technology; rejection is not modelled as spending tokens.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment instrument.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism besides acceptance of offers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,The article does not specify the size of the monetary stake (common designs use various amounts).,0.1,0.5,Modal offers ‘are typically around 50% of the total amount’; proposer’s ‘contribution’ of half the pie corresponds to contribution rate 0.5.,0.8,N/R,"Absolute amount of the pie is not given, only proportions (50%, 20%).",0.1,N/R,Efficiency (relative to maximum possible surplus) is not computed or reported in this summary.,0.1,N/R,No numerical payoffs reported.,0.1,N/R,The summary aggregates ‘several studies’ including Sanfey et al. (2003); no single country is reported in this article.,0.1,N/R,Age information not provided in the article’s brief description.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition is reported.,0.1,N/R,"The article does not specify that subjects are students, although that is common in such experiments.",0.3,On site,"Ultimatum game behavioral experiments referenced (e.g., Sanfey et al.) are run in physical labs with scanners etc., not as online surveys; article does not contradict this.",0.7,Standard ultimatum bargaining: experimenter endows a pair of players with a fixed sum of money; Player 1 proposes a split; Player 2 either accepts (both receive proposed shares) or rejects (both receive zero). Typical behavior: modal offers ≈50% of the total; low offers ≈20% are rejected about 50% of the time.,Directly summarized in the article plus canonical ultimatum game structure.,0.9
10.1038_s41467-023-38592-5.md,Study 2 – Third-party punishment + trust game (all role-identity configurations aggregated),"Study 2 reports behavior in a third-party punishment game followed by a trust game across multiple conditions varying human vs bot identities of Beneficiary, Helper, and Punisher. However, the paper does not report per-condition payoffs, endowments, or multipliers in a way that links clearly to each identity configuration; only aggregate percentages (e.g., share rates, punishment rates, trust gains) are given. Therefore, a single aggregate entry is used for the overall Study 2 game structure.",0.4,"Identity of Beneficiary (human vs bot), Helper (human vs bot), Punisher (human vs bot), and whether Trustor is paired with Helper vs Punisher; multiple configurations used but not fully specified with numeric parameters per condition in the text provided.","Methods and Results (Study 2) state that Beneficiaries, Helpers, and Punishers can be humans or bots, and that Trustors are paired either with Helpers or Punishers depending on experimental condition. Specific configurations (e.g., human-only, bot Beneficiary, bot Helper, bot Punisher) are described qualitatively and via labels B1–B4, H1–H3, P1, but full condition-by-condition parameterization is not provided in the excerpt.",0.7,True,Study 2 uses human MTurk workers as participants in incentivized behavioral games (third-party punishment and trust game) with real monetary payoffs.,1,True,Participants are randomly assigned to experimental conditions manipulating the identities of players (human vs bot) under controlled payoff rules.,1,False,"Data were collected online via MTurk/Qualtrics, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,"No agent-based or numerical simulations without human participants are described for Study 2; bots are scripted counterparts within a human experiment, not simulated datasets.",1,False,"Study 2 reports empirical behavioral results and t-tests, not closed-form analytical models.",1,3,"Fig. 1 and Methods: third-party punishment game involves Helper, Beneficiary, and Punisher (three players).",1,1,Design is a one-shot (or strategy-method) decision situation; no repeated rounds between the same players are described.,0.9,1,Helpers have a binary decision to share equally or keep all (all-or-nothing choice). There is no continuous contribution scale.,1,0,Resources (endowments) are initially held by Helpers and Punishers; Beneficiaries start with none. There is no initial contribution to a public pot; the default is fully private.,0.8,N/R,This is not a linear public goods game; it is a binary sharing decision followed by trust game. No MPCR is defined or derivable from provided text.,0.2,0,No possibility of communication or chat between players is mentioned; interaction is via one-way observation of choices and information screens.,0.9,1,"Trustors observe the Helper’s or Punisher’s behavior in the third-party punishment game before making trust decisions, which is effectively a summary of that partner’s prior action.",0.9,0,"Identities beyond role and human/bot status (e.g., personal identifiers) are not described as being revealed; only role and action are communicated.",0.6,0,No explicit reward mechanism (beyond trust game returns) is described that reveals identities.,0.9,0,Game is one-shot/strategy-method; there is no mention of displaying number of rounds or remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Third-party Punishers can punish Helpers who did not share.,1,0.25,Fig. 1 text: Punisher must “forego one quarter of their resources” to punish.,1,0.5,Fig. 1: punishment makes the Helper lose half of their resources.,1,0,No explicit costly reward or positive sanction mechanism is described beyond the inherent gains from the trust game.,1,N/A,No distinct costly reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No distinct costly reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,"Text states that Helpers and Punishers are given resources, Beneficiaries none, and that Trustors receive resources that are tripled if sent, but the actual numeric endowment or point amounts are not reported in the excerpt.",0.2,N/R,"Outcome variables reported are share/not-share rates, punishment rates, and trust percentages (trust-gain); no normalized contribution fraction is reported or derivable as an amount relative to an endowment since the actual endowment size is not stated.",0.2,N/R,Helpers’ decision is binary share vs not; actual token amounts or monetary units for the share are not reported.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency metric is reported; only behavioral percentages and trust gains are given.,0.1,N/R,Total group payoffs are not reported.,0.1,United States,Methods (Recruitment) specify that only MTurk workers located in the United States (by account and IP) were eligible.,1,Mean 37.1 years (sd = 11.8) in Study 2 sample,Sample composition section reports average age for Study 2 participants.,1,48% male; remainder female or other,Sample composition for Study 2: 48% male; others identified as female or other.,1,"Mixed; generally more educated than average population, typical of MTurk; specific distribution not reported in excerpt",Paper notes MTurk workers tend to be more educated; detailed education breakdown is in Supplementary Tables not provided here.,0.4,Online,All studies were programmed in Qualtrics and run via MTurk/CloudResearch.,1,"Two-stage game: (1) Third-party punishment game with Helper choosing whether to share equally with Beneficiary; if Helper does not share, Punisher can pay 25% of own resources to make Helper lose 50% of theirs. (2) Trust game: Trustor receives an endowment and chooses how much to send to either Helper or Punisher (depending on condition); amount sent is tripled, and the counterpart then decides how much of the tripled amount to return. Study uses the strategy method to elicit Punishers’ and Trustors’ contingent choices. Roles Beneficiary, Helper, Punisher can be human or bot; Trustor is always human. Trust outcomes are operationalized as percentage of resources sent, with ‘trust-gain’ defined as the difference in percentage sent between norm-following and non-following behaviors (e.g., share vs not share; punish vs not punish).","Summarized from Fig. 1 description and Methods sections discussing game flow and key quantities (percent sharing, punishment, trust, trust-gain).",1
