10.1073_pnas.2408802121.md,Numerical solution – Baseline equilibrium (no institution),"Condition where the institution is removed by setting p2 = 0 and no institutional incentives (ρ not applied); used as 'baseline equilibrium' in numerical section and Fig. 6A, Fig. 7.",1,"Institutional setting: no institution (p2 = 0, ρ not applied); cooperation difficulty δ^b and population patience μ varied continuously.",Baseline case defined by p2 = 0 and absence of institutional incentives; δ^b and μ are the two axes in Fig. 6A and the same parameter grid underlies Fig. 7 comparisons.,1,False,"No human participants; purely mathematical and numerical model described under 'Model', 'Numerical Solution', and 'Materials and Methods'.",1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment but a theoretical game-theoretic analysis plus numerical computation.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only formal modeling and simulations.,1,True,"Authors 'compute equilibrium outcomes' using Mathematica over parameter ranges, which is numerical simulation of the model.",1,True,"Closed-form equilibrium conditions and thresholds (e.g., δ̂^b(θ)) are derived analytically.",1,2,Stage game involves one actor and one chooser in a trust game; institution game involves many actors but key strategic interaction is dyadic trust game.,0.6,0,Game is infinitely repeated ('infinitely many interactions'); no finite round count.,1,1,"Decisions are binary (trust/distrust, reciprocate/cheat, contribute/free-ride); there is no continuous contribution level, so all-or-nothing contribution is effectively imposed.",0.9,N/A,Not a standard public goods endowment-contribution setting and there are no human subjects.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; model is trust and institutional incentive based.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,0,Model specifies that choosers only observe last-round actions with certain probabilities; no payoff or punishment summary information is modeled.,0.8,N/A,In this baseline condition there is no institution and thus no punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No institutional rewards or rewards mechanism in baseline.,1,0,Infinite-horizon repeated game; no information about a total or remaining number of rounds is given to agents.,0.9,0,Institution is 'removed' and thus no institutional punishment; trust game itself has no punishment action.,1,N/A,No punishment technology in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment technology in this condition.,1,0,No institutional reward mechanism in baseline; only reputational benefits.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward action or technology.,1,N/A,No institutional reward technology.,1,N/R,"Model specifies payoffs (k, r, c1, c2, b) but not a per-round endowment for players.",1,N/R,"Numerical results report 'rate of cooperation' (trust+reciprocation), not contribution rates to the institution, which is off in this condition.",1,N/R,No numeric levels of contributions are reported; only qualitative that actors never contribute in baseline.,1,N/R,"They report 'expected payoff' and changes, but not normalized efficiency relative to a maximum cooperative benchmark.",1,N/R,Expected individual payoff is discussed qualitatively; no explicit numeric payoffs are given.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and numerical analysis with no subjects.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated trust/institution game with large population of actors and one-shot choosers; key fixed parameters in numerical baseline include q = 0.5 (probability of trust game), r = 2 (benefit to actor from being trusted), b = 1 (benefit to chooser from reciprocation), p1 = 0.25 (observation in trust games), and p2 = 0 (no observation in institution game, disabling institution). Difficulty of cooperation δ^b varies by setting c1 = (p1 q r) δ^b = δ^b / 4 and k = c1; actors' discount factors follow truncated normal with mode μ ∈ [0,1], SD σ = 0.25.",Summarizes the parameterization in 'Numerical Solution' when the institution is switched off (p2 = 0).,1
10.1073_pnas.2408802121.md,Numerical solution – Institution equilibrium with inefficient institution (ρ = 1/3),"Condition (b) in 'Numerical Solution': institution equilibrium with an inefficient institution, efficiency parameter ρ = 1/3, shown in Fig. 6B.",1,Institutional setting: inefficient institution with efficiency ρ = 1/3; δ^b (difficulty of cooperation) and μ (population patience) varied continuously.,Authors explicitly vary ρ between 1/3 and 3 while also scanning δ^b and μ; this row corresponds to ρ = 1/3.,1,False,Same theoretical model as baseline; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; parameter-sweep of a model.,1,False,No lab or field context.,1,True,Equilibrium outcomes numerically computed with Mathematica over δ^b and μ for this parameterization.,1,True,"Analytical equilibrium conditions (δ̂1, δ̂2, etc.) are derived and then instantiated at ρ = 1/3.",1,2,Trust game remains dyadic; institution effects are aggregated but based on many dyadic trust games.,0.6,0,Game is infinite-horizon repeated.,1,1,"Actions are discrete (reciprocate vs cheat, contribute vs free-ride).",0.9,N/A,Not a human endowment-contribution task.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR is defined; institutional efficiency is captured by ρ instead.,1,N/A,Communication not modeled; no humans.,1,0,Choosers observe only previous-round actions with given probabilities; no payoff or sanction summaries are modeled.,0.8,0,"Institutional punishment is impersonal; reputations encode only action types (cheater, etc.), not specific punishers' identities.",0.7,0,"Similarly, any institutional rewards (if β > 0) are generic; identities are not modeled.",0.7,0,"Agents do not know the round index, only continuation; infinite horizon.",0.9,1,"In the institutional implementation used in Figs. 6 and 7, β = 0 and incentives are split between 'punishing cheaters' (γ > 0) and monitoring; this includes punishment.",1,N/A,There is no per-player choice to spend on punishment; punishment arises automatically from institutional contributions via γ.,1,N/R,"γ per trust game is derived from ρ, c2, q, n2, n; no simple per-unit 'tech' ratio is reported independently.",0.7,0,"The particular institution studied numerically sets β = 0, so there are no rewards for reciprocators.",1,N/A,No rewards in this implementation.,1,N/A,No rewards in this implementation (β = 0).,1,N/R,No per-round endowments specified; only action-specific costs and benefits.,1,N/R,They do not plot or tabulate the fraction of institution contributors separately; focus is on rate of cooperation in trust games.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution counts are given; contributions cost c2 but rates are not reported.,1,N/R,Outcome metrics include 'rate of cooperation' and 'expected payoff'; no explicit efficiency ratio vs. maximum is provided.,1,N/R,Expected payoff is plotted as change vs. baseline in Fig. 7 but without numeric scale or absolute group payoffs.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model-based numerical analysis only.,1,"Same repeated trust/institution game as baseline, but with institution active. Efficiency parameter ρ = 1/3. Institution allocates incentives equally between punishing cheaters and monitoring trust games: β = 0, γ = (1/2) ρ c2 (1−q) n2 / (q n), π1 = (1/2) ρ (c2 / c1) (1−q) n2 / (q n). Fixed parameters: q = 0.5, r = 2, b = 1, p1 = 0.25, p2 = 0.75. Costs vary with δ^b: c1 = (p1 q r) δ^b = δ^b / 4, k = c1, c2 = c1 / 3. Actors' discount factors follow a truncated normal with mode μ ∈ [0,1] and σ = 0.25.",Direct extraction from 'Numerical Solution' section for the inefficient institution case (ρ = 1/3).,1
10.1073_pnas.2408802121.md,Numerical solution – Institution equilibrium with efficient institution (ρ = 3),"Condition (c) in 'Numerical Solution': institution equilibrium with an efficient institution (ρ = 3), used in Fig. 6C and Fig. 7 comparisons.",1,Institutional setting: efficient institution with efficiency ρ = 3; δ^b (difficulty of cooperation) and μ (population patience) varied continuously.,ρ is explicitly set to 3 in the third numerical case; δ^b and μ vary along the axes.,1,False,No human data; purely theoretical/numerical model.,1,False,Model parameterization instead of empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field participants.,1,True,Equilibrium outcomes over the δ^b–μ grid are computed with Mathematica at ρ = 3.,1,True,Analytical expressions for thresholds and equilibrium conditions underlie the numerical results.,1,2,Core strategic interaction is between one chooser and one actor in the trust game.,0.6,0,Infinite-horizon game.,1,1,Actions are binary choices.,0.9,N/A,No human endowments; not a standard PGG.,1,N/R,Cooperation is modeled via trust and institutional incentives; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication.,1,0,Only last-round behavior observations are modeled; no summaries.,0.8,0,"Institutional punishment is generic, not attributed to identified individuals.",0.7,0,No explicit rewarding institution in this parameterization (β = 0).,0.8,0,Infinite horizon with no round counter given to agents.,0.9,1,Institution allocates half of multiplied contributions to punishing cheaters (γ > 0).,1,N/A,No individual-level decision to spend on punishment; it is generated automatically from contributions.,1,N/R,"While γ is a function of contributions and ρ, no single reported scalar describes 'units punished per coin spent' in the requested sense.",0.7,0,β = 0 in the numerically explored institution; there are no rewards for reciprocators.,1,N/A,No rewards in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward technology in this condition.,1,N/R,Model defines action-specific costs/benefits but not a general endowment per period.,1,N/R,"Numerical figures present cooperation rate in trust games, not contribution rates to the institution.",1,N/R,No numeric series on contributions is reported.,1,N/R,"They analyze 'expected payoff' and changes, but not normalized efficiency vs. maximum cooperative payoff.",1,N/R,Only relative increases/decreases in expected payoff are shown in Fig. 7B.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely model-based.,1,"Same repeated trust/institution game architecture and parameterization as the inefficient-institution case, but with higher efficiency ρ = 3. Institution allocates incentives equally between punishing cheaters and monitoring: β = 0, γ = (1/2) ρ c2 (1−q) n2 / (q n), π1 = (1/2) ρ (c2 / c1) (1−q) n2 / (q n). Fixed parameters: q = 0.5, r = 2, b = 1, p1 = 0.25, p2 = 0.75. Costs set by δ^b: c1 = δ^b / 4, k = c1, c2 = c1 / 3. Actors’ discount factors distributed as truncated normal with mode μ (0–1) and σ = 0.25. Figures 6C and 7 (institution condition) are computed under this setting.",Directly summarizes the efficient institution setting described under 'Numerical Solution' (ρ = 3).,1
10.1063_5.0256342.md,Monte Carlo simulation – graded punishment mechanism on regular lattice,Single agent-based Monte Carlo simulation model with graded punishment on a 32x32 regular lattice is the core condition studied throughout; no separate human or control treatments with different rules besides parameter sweeps and brief comparison to a traditional punishment variant.,0.88,"Graded punishment trust game parameters on regular lattice: punishment cost α (1.0–2.0 in Fig.1, and 0.5, 0.8, 1.0 in Fig.2), punishment amount β (0, 0.4, 0.8, 1.0), reputation threshold R_i (100 vs 200), dilemma intensity r_UT (0–1), network topology fixed to 32×32 square lattice with average degree 4, trustee gain R_T=6; initial strategy proportions ρ_I=0.2, ρ_PI=0.1, ρ_T=0.3, ρ_U=0.4.","Paper systematically varies α, β, R_i, r_UT on regular lattice; other elements held fixed for this main mechanism.",0.86,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on networks; no human participants are mentioned.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; it is a computational simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; entirely in silico.,1,True,Authors repeatedly refer to Monte Carlo simulation experiments on agent-based network model.,1,False,"While they define payoff functions, results are obtained via simulations, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,1024,"They state: “32×32 square lattice network, which denotes the trust game of N=1024 agents.”",1,20000,They say: “each experiment arrives at the stationary state after around 10000 time steps… we perform all experimental results for 20000 MCS and averaged over the last 5000 time steps.” Thus 20000 update steps per run.,0.95,1,"Agents choose one of four discrete strategies (I, T, U, PI) each round, not a continuous contribution amount; in this schema, 1 corresponds to ‘players can contribute any amount; 0 if only all-or-nothing’. Here there is no scalar contribution decision but pure strategy choice, so closest is all-or-nothing style; encode as 1 per instructions that 0 is reserved for binary contribute/not-contribute decisions in standard public goods, while here trust investment t_v is fixed.",0.3,N/A,No human endowment allocation decision; investment amount t_v is fixed for relevant strategies; also purely simulated.,1,N/R,"They define payoffs via R_T and R_U, but do not describe a linear public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return in the standard sense; MPCR not directly defined.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; communication between agents is not modeled.,1,0,"Agents only observe neighbors’ payoffs for imitation; no display of summaries, earnings, punishments as in human UI.",0.8,0,"Punishment is modeled via payoff parameters α, β and reputation thresholds; no notion of identity revelation is used.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism is included; only punishment affects payoffs.,1,0,"Time steps are part of simulation control, not shown to agents as information.",0.9,1,"Core of the model is a graded punishment mechanism via punishment-type investors and parameters α, β, n_PI.",1,0.5,"Baseline illustrative graded-punishment runs (Figs. 2(a), 4, 5, 6 and many discussions) use α=0.5; while α is varied in some sweeps, a representative cost in the main mechanism is 0.5. Here we enter that canonical value.",0.6,0.8,"Punishment impact per event is β; they often use β=0.8 in time-series and snapshots (Figs. 2, 4). Take β as magnitude of punishment per unit cost; no finer mapping is defined, so use β as tech.",0.6,0,Model only imposes costs on untrustworthy trustees via punishment; no explicit material reward action is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,"They state: “trust assets provided … t_v (t_v>0). In this paper, we will set t_v=1.” Interpret this as per-round ‘endowment’ invested by investors and PI.",0.95,N/R,"They track densities of strategies (ρ_I, ρ_PI, ρ_T, ρ_U) and total wealth; they do not report a normalized contribution rate in [0,1] relative to an endowment as in standard PGG.",0.2,N/R,"Investment per investor is fixed t_v=1, but no aggregate or average contributions are explicitly reported as an outcome variable; only strategy densities and wealth are discussed.",0.3,N/R,"They mention ‘total wealth’ and qualitative levels of trust, but do not compute efficiency as realized payoff divided by maximum possible payoff.",0.2,N/R,"Figure 6 shows ‘total wealth’ under various α and network types, but exact numerical values are only approximate from bar charts and not tabulated; schema requires precise values, so mark as not reported.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human subjects; only simulated agents.,1,No human,All results from Monte Carlo simulations on networks.,1,"N-player networked trust game on static 32×32 square lattice (average degree 4) with four strategies: investor (I), trustworthy trustee (T), untrustworthy trustee (U), and punishment-type investor (PI). Per-round investment t_v=1 for I and PI. Payoffs: W_I = (R_T K_T*/K_TU*) t_v − t_v; W_T = (R_T (K_I*+K_PI*)/K_TU*) t_v; W_U = (R_U (K_I*+K_PI*)/K_TU*) t_v − β n_PI; W_PI = (R_T K_T*/K_TU*) t_v − t_v − α K_U*. Reputation-based graded punishment: n_PI=0 if reputation > 0.8 R_i, n_PI=1 baseline, n_PI=2 if reputation < 0.4 R_i. Reputation update: +1 for I or T, −1 for U, +2 for PI; max reputation 100; initial reputation at half max. Trustworthy gain R_T=6; untrustworthy gain R_U defined via r_UT=(R_U−R_T)/R_T with 1<R_T<R_U<2R_T. Strategy update via proportional imitation rule using payoff comparison with random neighbor; probability proportional to positive payoff difference divided by φ, the maximum possible payoff difference. Simulations run 20000 MCS, last 5000 averaged, 30 independent runs for averages. Initial strategy fractions: ρ_I=0.2, ρ_PI=0.1, ρ_T=0.3, ρ_U=0.4.",Summarizes detailed game rules and simulation protocol directly stated in model and results sections.,0.96
10.1063_5.0256342.md,Monte Carlo simulation – traditional (non-graded) punishment mechanism on regular lattice,They design a ‘comparative experiment’ where the same parameters as Fig. 2(a) are used under a traditional punishment mechanism to compare with graded punishment (Fig.3). This constitutes a distinct simulated condition.,0.82,"Traditional punishment trust game parameters on regular lattice: punishment cost α=0.5, punishment amount β=0.8, reputation threshold R_i=100, dilemma intensity r_UT=0.6, network topology fixed to 32×32 square lattice with degree 4, trustee gain R_T=6; initial strategy proportions ρ_I=0.2, ρ_PI=0.1, ρ_T=0.3, ρ_U=0.4.","They state that all parameters in the traditional-punishment run (Fig.3) are consistent with Fig.2(a), which has α=0.5, β=0.8, r_UT=0.6, R_T=6 and given initial fractions; only punishment rule (graded vs traditional) differs.",0.9,False,"As with the main condition, this is a Monte Carlo agent-based simulation.",1,False,"Comparative simulation, not human or field data.",1,False,No physical lab setting; simulation only.,1,True,They explicitly refer to applying the same experimental parameters in the traditional punishment mechanism and ‘record the dynamic evolution process’ via simulation.,1,False,Outcomes are graphically presented from simulations.,0.9,1024,They maintain the same network (32×32 lattice) as in the graded punishment simulations.,1,20000,"Simulation protocol (20000 MCS, last 5000 averaged, 30 runs) is common to all reported experiments.",0.95,1,"Agents choose among discrete strategies (I, T, U, PI); there is no continuous contribution decision. As in the graded case, this best maps to discrete all-or-nothing style in this schema.",0.3,N/A,No human contribution endowment decision; fixed t_v investment in a simulation.,1,N/R,Same payoff structure with R_T and R_U; no MPCR is defined.,0.2,N/A,Simulation with no human communication.,1,0,Agents update based on neighbor payoff comparisons only; no UI-style summary display.,0.8,0,No explicit identity revelation is modeled; punishment affects only payoffs.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism modeled here either.,1,0,Time horizon is not information available to agents in the model.,0.9,1,Traditional punishment is still present; only grading by reputation-dependent n_PI is removed or simplified.,1,0.5,Same α=0.5 as in the matched graded-punishment condition (Fig.2(a)); they explicitly state all relevant parameters are consistent.,0.95,0.8,"Same β=0.8 as matched graded condition; traditional vs graded differs in how often punishment applies, not magnitude per event.",0.95,0,"Traditional condition is punishment-only; no explicit rewarding behavior but possibly reputation bonus, which is not modeled as a resource reward.",1,N/A,No monetary reward action.,1,N/A,No monetary reward action.,1,1,Investment amount t_v remains 1 in all variants of the model.,0.95,N/R,They compare densities of strategies and investment group ρ_I+PI between traditional and graded punishment; they do not report a normalized contribution rate variable.,0.3,N/R,t_v is fixed but aggregate contributions are not tabulated as an explicit dependent variable.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative discussion of cooperation and trust; no explicit efficiency measure reported.,0.2,N/R,For traditional vs graded comparison they plot the density of investors; they do not plot or tabulate total wealth specifically broken out for traditional punishment.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same N-player networked trust game on static 32×32 lattice with strategies I, T, U, PI, t_v=1, payoff functions with R_T and R_U as in the graded model. The key difference is that the punishment mechanism is ‘traditional’, i.e., punishment probability or intensity does not depend on graded reputation thresholds (no explicit case n_PI=0 or 2 based on reputation ranges). Initial reputation, update rules, and proportional imitation dynamics are kept the same as in the graded condition. They compare time evolution of strategy fractions and investment density ρ_I+PI between this traditional mechanism and the graded mechanism using identical parameter settings.","Description inferred from comparison section: they state all parameters are identical to Fig.2(a) except that the ‘traditional punishment mechanism’ replaces graded punishment; we do not have a separate explicit formula, so describe difference qualitatively.",0.7
10.1063_5.0256342.md,Monte Carlo simulation – graded punishment on scale-free network,"They run the same graded punishment model on a scale-free network (average degree 4) and compare total wealth levels to the regular lattice (Fig.6), which forms a distinct network-topology condition.",0.8,"Network topology and punishment parameters under graded punishment: scale-free network with average degree 4 vs regular lattice; punishment cost α in {0.1, 0.4, 0.7, 1.0}, punishment amount β=1.0, dilemma intensity r_UT=0.6, R_T=6; initial strategy proportions ρ_I=0.2, ρ_PI=0.1, ρ_T=0.3, ρ_U=0.4.","Fig.6 explicitly compares total wealth between scale-free and regular lattice networks for α=0.1,0.4,0.7,1.0 at β=1.0, r_UT=0.6, <k>=4.",0.9,False,"Again, these are Monte Carlo simulations with artificial agents.",1,False,No empirical experimental manipulation with humans; computational experiment only.,1,False,"In silico simulations, not lab.",1,True,Monte Carlo simulation on networks explicitly mentioned; network structure (scale-free vs regular) is varied.,1,False,Outcomes (total wealth bars) obtained numerically.,0.9,N/R,They specify <k>=4 for the scale-free network but do not report the number of nodes; likely 1024 for comparison but not explicitly stated.,0.3,20000,"They state the general simulation protocol (20000 MCS and last 5000 averaged) for ‘all experimental results’, which reasonably includes scale-free runs.",0.9,1,Same discrete four-strategy choice model as on lattice; no continuous contribution level.,0.3,N/A,No human endowment allocation; fixed t_v=1.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined; trust game structure same as regular lattice case.,0.2,N/A,No human communication; agents only interact through network payoffs.,1,0,Same imitation rule with local payoff comparison; no explicit summaries.,0.8,0,No identity revelation modeled; punishment via payoffs only.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism in model.,1,0,Agents not given round-count information.,0.9,1,They describe this as graded punishment mechanism applied on both network types; α and β are set and used.,1,0.55,"Fig.6 examines four distinct values α=0.1,0.4,0.7,1.0. Since a single scalar is required here and no single α is canonical in this comparison, a representative mid-range (approximate mean 0.55) is used, but the heterogeneity is better reflected in indep_var; numeric field here is thus approximate.",0.3,1.0,They state in Fig.6 caption: ‘penalty amount β=1.0’. Use this as punishment magnitude per unit.,0.95,0,No mention of explicit reward actions; only punishment represented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Model-wide t_v=1 still applies; they did not change endowment when altering network topology.,0.95,N/R,"For this topology comparison they report only ‘total wealth’ at stationarity, not normalized contribution measures.",0.2,N/R,"Contributions per investor are fixed, but no explicit aggregate contribution values are reported for the scale-free case.",0.3,N/R,They compare absolute total wealth levels between networks but do not normalize by maximum achievable wealth to define efficiency.,0.3,N/R,Fig.6 provides bar-chart approximations (~4500 vs ~3700) but no exact numerical data; thus exact group payoff is not formally reported.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Network-based simulation only.,1,"Same graded punishment N-player trust-game rules as on the regular lattice (strategies I, T, U, PI; t_v=1; payoffs parameterized by R_T=6, R_U via r_UT; graded punishment based on reputation thresholds with n_PI in {0,1,2}; reputation update; proportional imitation). Only network topology changes to a scale-free network with average degree 4, while α is varied across {0.1,0.4,0.7,1.0} and β fixed at 1.0. They report that at the end of evolution, total wealth on the regular lattice is around 4500 whereas on the scale-free network around 3700, indicating lower trust/wealth levels on the scale-free topology under otherwise identical parameters.",Summarizes network and parameter differences specific to the scale-free versus lattice comparison as described near Fig.6.,0.88
10.1037_pspi0000474.md,"Study 1 – Pooled social dilemma observations (PD and PGD, all structural configurations)","The paper is a meta-analysis; the fundamental observational unit is each cooperation rate extracted from a study. Study 1 pools 2,340 cooperation rates across PDs and PGDs with varying structural features. No single, well-defined experimental condition with fixed parameters (e.g., group size, MPCR) can be isolated.",0.4,"Meta-analytic moderators: conflict of interests (K index, continuous), group size (continuous), sequentiality (simultaneous vs. sequential), communication (yes vs. no; unidirectional vs. bidirectional), show-up fee (yes vs. no), game payment (incentivized vs. hypothetical; monetary vs. nonmonetary; lottery vs. nonlottery), symmetry (symmetric vs. asymmetric), sanctions (present vs. absent; punishment vs. reward), matching design (partner vs. stranger), deception (yes vs. no), plus sample and methodological covariates.","Study 1 is a three-level meta-regression where these structural variables are used as predictors of cooperation rates across studies and treatment cells, rather than cleanly separated experimental conditions with fixed values.",0.7,True,The meta-analysis aggregates lab and field studies with human participants.,1,True,All underlying primary studies are experimental games (social dilemmas); Study 1 is an observational synthesis of experimental evidence.,0.9,True,"Most primary studies are lab experiments; some are online or field-like, but the modal and emphasized design is lab-based experimental games.",0.7,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations without human participants are synthesized.,1,False,There is no formal analytical model; analyses are statistical meta-regressions on empirical data.,1,4.35,"The paper reports the average group size across cooperation rates as M = 4.35 (SD = 9.51, range 2–324). This is heterogeneous across observations; the mean is recorded explicitly.",1,0,Number of rounds per game varies widely and is not summarized (only whether cooperation was measured on first vs. all trials). A single numeric value cannot be inferred.,0,1,"Both dichotomous (all-or-nothing) and continuous contribution games are included (choice type: 1,107 dichotomous; 1,233 continuous). Because many observations allow any amount of contribution, we code as allowing any amount rather than strictly all-or-nothing.",0.4,N/R,Whether endowments start in private vs. public accounts is not reported at the meta-analytic level.,0,N/R,"MPCR is mentioned conceptually for linear PGDs, but no pooled numeric MPCR is reported.",0,0,"Communication is a moderator: 2,096 cooperation rates without communication and 208 with communication. The majority baseline configuration is no communication, but values vary by study; here we record the modal state.",0.6,0,Whether participants saw summaries of others’ earnings/punishments is not coded or summarized in the article.,0,N/R,The meta-analysis codes presence of sanctions but not whether identities of punished individuals are revealed.,0,N/R,The identity visibility in reward mechanisms is not reported.,0,0,The article does not report whether subjects were informed about total or remaining rounds in the primary studies.,0,0,"Sanction (punishment and/or reward) is coded as a structural variable: 2,013 rates without sanctions, 287 with, 40 mixed. The pooled dataset is mixed; at baseline most observations have no sanctions, so we code 0 for the overall configuration.",0.5,N/A,"At the aggregate level we do not specify a punishment technology; many games have no punishment and, for those that do, costs vary and are not pooled.",0,N/A,Magnitude of punishment per unit cost varies by study and is not summarized.,0,0,Sanctions include both punishment and rewards but frequencies are only broken out in Study 2 effect-size meta-analyses; the Study 1 pooled configuration is predominantly no sanctions.,0.4,N/A,Reward cost per unit is not summarized across studies.,0,N/A,Magnitude of rewards per unit cost is not summarized.,0,N/R,Endowment levels are not aggregated or reported at the meta level.,0,0.486,Study 1 reports the overall mean cooperation rate across all PDs and PGDs as 0.486 (48.6%). This is defined as proportion of cooperative choices (binary) or contributed endowment (continuous).,1,N/R,Only normalized cooperation rates (proportions) are pooled; raw contribution amounts in tokens/points are not summarized.,0,N/R,No pooled efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum possible) is reported.,0,N/R,Group payoff is not aggregated across studies.,0,"Multiple (70+ societies; e.g., United States, China, Japan and others)","Studies were collected from a wide range of societies (English, Chinese, Japanese documents; cross-societal work is referenced). No dominant single country is specified for Study 1.",0.6,Mean age 24.99 years (range 18–55 across samples),"Table 3 reports age (M) = 24.99, SD = 6.667, range 18–54/55, based on the subset of studies that reported age.",1,Mean male proportion 0.50 (range 0–1 across samples),"Table 3 reports gender (male %) M = 0.50, SD = 0.242, range 0–1 for 1,293 cooperation rates.",1,"Mixed student (economics, psychology, other disciplines) and nonstudent samples","Academic discipline is coded: economics, psychology, other, and nonstudent samples; no single type dominates all observations.",0.9,On site,"Most primary studies are described as laboratory settings (k = 2,061 lab vs. 104 online and 162 other). Thus the pooled environment is predominantly on-site laboratory.",0.9,"Meta-analysis of 2,340 cooperation rates from 1,527 studies using two main social dilemma paradigms: Prisoner’s Dilemma (binary and continuous, 2-person and n-person) and Public Goods Dilemma (linear continuous and step-level). Structural features varied across observations in seven Ostromian dimensions (participants, positions, actions, outcomes, control, costs/benefits, interactions).",Summary is taken from the Study 1 description of included paradigms and structural variables.,1
