10.1016_j.physa.2018.09.194.md,PG without punishment – analytical model,Paper develops an analytical Public Goods (PG) game without punishment; treated here as one simulation/analytical condition.,0.9,"Public goods parameters: number of players N (general), multiplication factor b, cost c, rationality/noise parameter β; no punishment (γ = 0).","Model varies N, b, c, and β in analytic expressions for cooperation density; punishment not included in this part.",0.9,False,No human participants; purely theoretical/statistical-physics style treatment.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a theoretical/modeling study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely analytical/simulation work.,1,True,"Although formulas are derived analytically, the framework is used with Monte Carlo simulations (Appendix), so this condition is best classified as simulation/no-human.",0.9,True,"Core results (Hamiltonians, partition functions, cooperation risk) are derived analytically for PG without punishment.",1,1024,"Text uses general N but specific numerical/Monte Carlo illustrations for PG without punishment use N = 1024 (e.g., Fig. A.6).",0.6,1,"Model explicitly considers a single instance of a public goods game shared by N players (“single PG game, with a single resource pool”).",0.9,1,"Players choose between two pure strategies: cooperate (pay cost c) or defect (pay 0); no partial contributions allowed, so it is all-or-nothing at the strategy level.",1,N/A,No human endowment process; analytical binary choice model only.,1,N/R,"They specify a multiplier b and equal sharing among N, but do not explicitly define MPCR; could be b/N, but this is not labeled as such.",0.5,N/A,No human subjects or communication mechanism; abstract model.,1,N/A,No user interface or human feedback; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No players with identities; no punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No interface; one-shot analytical game.,1,0,This base PG model does not include punishment; that is added later in the PGP section.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,Only punishment (later) is modeled; no positive reward to cooperators.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Players ‘forfeit the cost c from their own assets’, but absolute endowment level is never defined.",0.3,N/R,Theoretical focus is on cooperation density ⟨n⟩ rather than normalized contribution relative to a numeric endowment.,0.4,N/R,"Binary choice “contribute cost c or 0” is modeled, but no aggregate numeric contribution is summarized as a reported DV.",0.5,N/R,They discuss payoffs and NE but do not report efficiency as ratio of realized to maximum possible payoff.,0.4,N/R,Payoff operators are given symbolically; no specific numerical group payoff outcomes reported as a DV for this condition.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely mathematical model and simulations; no lab/online human experiment.,1,"Public Goods game with N players, single shared pool, binary strategies (cooperate: pay cost c; defect: pay 0), pool multiplied by b and shared equally. Cooperation risk μ = c − b/N included in Hamiltonian for NE consistency; no punishment (γ = 0).",Summarizes the PG structure and risk operator as described in Sections 2 and related formulas.,0.95
10.1016_j.physa.2018.09.194.md,PG with punishment (PGP) – analytical model,Section 3 introduces Public Goods with Punishment (PGP) via punishment parameter γ and derives a separate Hamiltonian; treated as distinct condition.,0.95,"Public goods parameters: N players (general, with numerical focus at N = 1024), multiplication factor b, cost c, rationality/noise parameter β; punishment reduction factor γ (0 ≤ γ ≤ 1).","Model explicitly varies N, b, c, β, and γ in the PGP Hamiltonian and in analytical expressions for cooperation density.",0.95,False,No human participants; theory and simulations only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; it is a theoretical/simulation study.,1,False,No physical lab with human subjects; computational/analytical work.,1,True,"They perform Monte Carlo simulations of the PGP (e.g., Fig. 2, Fig. 3, Fig. 4, Fig. 5) using Metropolis algorithm and compare with analytical results.",1,True,"Derive Hamiltonian with punishment, partition function Z, analytical inequalities and equations for ⟨n⟩, thresholds β* and c′.",1,1024,Figures 2–5 explicitly state N = 1024 players for PGP simulations.,1,1,"As in base PG, PGP is a single public goods interaction with N players and one shared pool; β tunes rationality but not repeated rounds.",0.9,1,Same binary choice (cooperate vs defect) with added punishment; no partial contributions mentioned.,1,N/A,No explicit initial monetary endowment or default allocation defined for human players.,1,N/R,They still specify returns via factor b and equal split among N; MPCR would be b/N but it is not labeled as such in a way typical for experiments.,0.5,N/A,No communication among agents; simulation of strategy updates governed by β and punishment.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents’ interactions handled through Hamiltonian and update dynamics.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as a payoff reduction factor (1 − γ) for defectors; no notion of personal identity visibility.,1,N/A,No positive reward mechanism is specified.,1,N/A,No explicit repeated rounds interface; β controls equilibrium distribution rather than finite horizon play.,1,1,Section 3 explicitly introduces punishment via modifying defectors’ payoffs by factor (1 − γ).,1,N/A,"Punishment is parameterized as a payoff reduction factor γ, not as a costly choice by punishers; no per-unit cost reported.",0.3,1,"Defector payoff operator becomes (1 − γ)·Π_k^(D), so payoffs to defectors are multiplied by (1 − γ); interpreted as a linear punishment technology of factor (1 − γ).",0.7,0,Only punishment (reducing defectors’ payoffs) is modeled; no extra rewards for cooperators.,1,N/A,No rewards are used.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model references ‘assets’ and cost c but no explicit endowment amount is given.,0.3,N/R,"The central DV is cooperation density ⟨n⟩ as function of β, c/b, γ, not normalized contributions relative to numeric endowment.",0.4,N/R,"Each cooperator pays cost c, but no aggregate contribution is reported numerically as an outcome metric.",0.5,N/R,"They analyze payoffs, risk, and phase transitions but do not express results as efficiency ratios relative to maximum possible payoff.",0.4,N/R,Focus is on cooperation density and phase transitions; no tabulated or graphed group payoff values as the main DV.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulations and analytical calculations only.,1,"Public Goods with Punishment (PGP): N players, one shared pool; each cooperator pays cost c, total contributions multiplied by b and shared equally. Defectors’ earnings are reduced by factor (1 − γ). Hamiltonian includes two-body terms proportional to γ, generating correlations and phase transitions. Cooperation risk with punishment is μ′ = μ − (γ b/N) Σ_{ℓ≠k} ⟨n_ℓ⟩; they analyze thresholds such as β* = ln(2)/(2c − b) and cost threshold c′ ≈ 2/3 b at γ = 1 for N = 1024.","Summarizes PGP structure, punishment mechanism, and key analytical features mentioned in Section 3 and related figures.",0.95
10.1016_j.physa.2018.09.194.md,"PG with punishment (PGP) – Monte Carlo simulation, γ = 0 (baseline)",Fig. 2a compares curves for different γ including γ = 0; this is the no-punishment baseline within the simulation framework used for PGP analysis.,0.9,Punishment factor γ = 0; N = 1024 players; c/b and β varied; Metropolis updating.,Fig. 2 specifies N = 1024 and different γ; γ = 0 corresponds to baseline PG dynamics in the same simulation setup.,0.9,False,Simulation only; no humans.,1,False,"Numerical Monte Carlo study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,"Runs on computer, not in a human lab.",1,True,Explicitly described as Monte Carlo simulations with Metropolis algorithm (10^7 realizations).,1,False,This specific condition refers to numerical Monte Carlo results relative to the analytical model; the DV plotted is simulation-based.,0.7,1024,Fig. 2 caption: “N = 1024 players in a single public goods game.”,1,1,"Described as a single public goods game; simulations sample equilibrium distributions via many Monte Carlo steps, not repeated game rounds.",0.9,1,Same binary cooperate/defect choice as in analytical model; no partial contributions.,1,N/A,No explicit monetary endowment for simulated agents.,1,N/R,"Simulation uses same PG structure with multiplier b and N players, but MPCR is not specifically labeled or parameterized.",0.5,N/A,Agents update strategies via Metropolis/logit rules; no communication channel modeled.,1,N/A,Simulation does not include user-visible summaries; only internal state and global averages.,1,N/A,No human identities; moreover here γ = 0 so no punishment is active.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism or identities.,1,N/A,No game interface; Monte Carlo steps are not presented as rounds to subjects.,1,0,"This baseline simulation case uses γ = 0, i.e., no punishment effect.",1,N/A,No punishment when γ = 0.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism is inactive.,1,0,No positive reward system modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Simulation follows the abstract payoff operators, not explicit endowments.",0.3,N/R,"Outcome of simulations is reported as density of collaborators ⟨n⟩, not normalized contribution relative to a specified endowment.",0.5,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect is simulated but no numeric total contributions are tabulated.,0.5,N/R,Simulation plots focus on ⟨n⟩ vs β and parameters; efficiency is not reported.,0.4,N/R,Group payoffs are not plotted or tabulated for this simulation condition.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is a Monte Carlo simulation over model states.,1,Baseline PG dynamics implemented via Metropolis updates (10^7 realizations) to sample the Boltzmann distribution; compares empirical ⟨n⟩ from simulations with analytical prediction without punishment for various β and cost/benefit ratios.,Derived from Fig. 2 and Appendix text describing Metropolis vs Logit updates for PG.,0.9
10.1016_j.physa.2018.09.194.md,"PG with punishment (PGP) – Monte Carlo simulation, γ > 0","Figures 2–5 analyze PGP numerically for positive punishment factors (e.g., γ = 1); this forms a distinct simulation condition from γ = 0.",0.95,"Punishment factor γ > 0 (notably γ = 1 in detailed phase-transition analysis), N = 1024, cost-benefit ratio c/b varied (e.g., c/b = 0.75, c ≈ 0.664, 0.665 when b = 1), rationality β varied; Metropolis updates.","Fig. 2–5 and related text explicitly mention γ, specific c/b values, b = 1, N = 1024, and varying β in Monte Carlo simulations.",0.95,False,No human subjects; purely computational.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; it is a simulation study.,1,False,Runs on a computer; no physical lab setting.,1,True,Text labels these as Monte Carlo simulations with Metropolis algorithm using 10^7 realizations.,1,False,"This entry refers specifically to Monte Carlo outcomes (phase transitions, curves in Figs. 2–5) rather than closed-form derivations.",0.8,1024,Fig. 2–5 captions specify N = 1024 players.,1,1,"Just as in the analytical description, these simulations model a single PG interaction, sampled via many Monte Carlo steps to equilibrium.",0.9,1,Agents can be cooperators or defectors; no partial contribution model is mentioned.,1,N/A,"Simulation of abstract strategy states, not endowed accounts.",1,N/R,"Although returns are defined through b and N, MPCR is not reported explicitly within the simulation section.",0.5,N/A,No communication; only stochastic updates based on payoffs and β.,1,N/A,No explicit exposure of others’ payoffs; model-level dynamics only.,1,N/A,No player identities; punishment is implemented via generic reduction factor γ on defectors’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Monte Carlo steps are technical iterations, not user-visible rounds.",1,1,Punishment factor γ > 0 reduces defectors’ earnings as per Π_k^(P) = (1 − γ) Π_k^(D).,1,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as exogenous payoff reduction, not as costly decisions by punishers; no cost parameter defined.",0.4,1,Linear multiplicative punishment on defectors’ payoffs by factor (1 − γ).,0.8,0,Simulations focus solely on punishment; no separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/R,"Simulation uses payoff operators, not explicit endowments.",0.3,N/R,Simulations record density of cooperators ⟨n⟩ for given parameter sets; not expressed as normalized contribution per endowment.,0.6,N/R,"While each cooperator pays c, only cooperation densities are reported in figures; no explicit token counts.",0.5,N/R,"Results are framed in terms of phase transitions and cooperation density, not efficiency ratios.",0.4,N/R,"Group payoffs with punishment are discussed qualitatively (e.g., payoff decrements) but not reported numerically in the simulation figures.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulations with Metropolis algorithm.,1,"Monte Carlo simulations of PGP with N = 1024 and punishment γ > 0 (often γ = 1, b = 1) show that punishment can accelerate cooperation or defection depending on c/b and β. A critical inverse temperature β* = ln 2 / (2c − b) marks crossings of cooperation density between γ = 0 and γ = 1. For γ = 1 and costs just below c′ ≈ 2/3 b, ⟨n⟩ increases continuously with β (cooperative phase); for costs slightly above c′, ⟨n⟩ drops sharply with β, indicating a first-order-like transition to non-cooperative phase, with λ-shaped variance peak around β ≈ 2.2.",Summarizes key Monte Carlo findings from Figs. 2–5 and associated discussion.,0.95
