10.1016_j.chaos.2018.03.029.md,"BA-SF – jealous sanctions, specific example (p = 0.2, β = 1, D_g = 0.5, D_r = 0)","Condition underlying Fig. 3B and Fig. 4B: BA-SF, D_g=0.5, D_r=0, p=0.2, β=1.",0.98,"Network = BA-SF (⟨k⟩ = 8); jealous sanction enabled (p = 0.2, β = 1); payoff parameters D_g = 0.5, D_r = 0.",Explicitly stated in captions for Figs. 3 and 4.,0.99,False,Simulation-based study.,1,False,No observational or lab experiment.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Condition is one numerical simulation parameter set.,1,False,No analytical solution presented.,1,10000,Global N=10^4 for all simulations.,1,0,"Time series shown (e.g., 6th step) but total steps to equilibrium not reported.",0.2,1,Binary C or D strategies.,0.9,N/A,No contribution framework.,1,N/A,"Pairwise PD, not PGG.",1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No displays; multi-agent simulation.,1,N/A,No human participant identities.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or human IDs.,1,N/A,No human agents or UI.,1,1,Jealous sanctions applied with p=0.2 whenever jealousy condition is met.,1,N/R,Cost coefficient c_s is introduced but its specific numerical value for this condition is not reported; only β=1 and p=0.2 are given.,0.2,N/R,"Penalty magnitude p·π_i relative to cost c_s·π_i characterized by β = p/c_s = 1, but c_s not given; cannot translate to a simple unit cost-to-penalty scalar.",0.3,0,Only costly jealous sanctions that reduce payoff are modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No external endowment; only evolving payoffs.,1,N/A,Metric of interest is cooperation fraction P_c.,1,N/A,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,"Although they remark that heavy jealousy suppresses total social payoff, they provide no normalized efficiency value for this condition.",0.2,N/R,No time series of total payoffs or averages per agent reported numerically.,0.2,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Condition is part of a computational experiment.,1,"BA-SF network with ⟨k⟩=8 and N=10^4. PD parameters D_g = 0.5 and D_r = 0 yield T = 1 + D_g = 1.5, S = −D_r = 0, R=1, P=0. Jealous sanction rule: if π_i < π_j < 2π_i, agent i sanctions j with payoff-based probability (π_j − π_i)/π_i (textual description) and then π_i' = (1 − c_s)π_i, π_j' = π_j − pπ_i, with p=0.2 and β = p/c_s = 1. Strategy update: imitation max (IM), synchronous. Initial condition: half C and half D randomly distributed on BA-SF. They analyze 100 independent realizations, classifying episodes into successful (cooperation prevails) or failed (defection dominates) and examine sanction types (C→D, D→D, etc.) over time and degree pairs of punisher-victim at specific time steps.",Details compiled from model section and figure captions for this specific parameter set.,0.98
10.1016_j.jtbi.2016.03.017.md,Theoretical model – PRECISE network knowledge,"Paper defines a network knowledge environment labeled PRECISE, under which equilibrium conditions for cooperation are derived (Proposition 1). Treated as one modeling condition.",1,"Network knowledge environment = PRECISE (all agents know full network g with common knowledge); other structural parameters (n, r, T, α, β, g) held generic.",Only clear variation across conditions is agents’ knowledge of the network (PRECISE vs COARSE).,1,False,No human subjects or data collection; purely theoretical/game-theoretic model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; analytical derivation of equilibria.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only mathematical theory.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations described.,1,True,Paper provides closed-form equilibrium conditions and proofs (Appendix A) without simulations or data.,1,0,"Model defines a finite group of size n but does not fix a numeric value; since n is a symbolic parameter and there are no actual players, use 0 and treat game as theoretical.",0.5,0,Game is infinitely repeated; there is no finite number of rounds in the design.,1,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions c and d, not a continuous contribution; this fits the binary/all-or-nothing cooperation choice classification.",0.9,N/A,"No endowment-allocation stage or public good; model is a repeated prisoner’s dilemma, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,"No linear public goods structure or marginal per capita return defined; only PD payoffs (1, -β, α, 0).",1,N/A,"No human subjects or interface; communication is modeled as deterministic gossip along network links, not optional chat by participants.",1,0,Model specifies that victims send gossip messages about specific misbehavior; there is no notion of aggregate earnings or punishment summaries.,0.9,1,"Messages include the offender’s identity and round (""{i, t}""); thus identities of punished deviators are always known by recipients.",1,0,No reward mechanism is modeled; only punishment via capitulation is present.,1,0,Game is infinitely repeated; no interface or feedback about remaining rounds is discussed.,1,1,Section 6 describes punishment as capitulation: offenders must play c while punishers play d for T rounds.,1,N/R,"Model uses PD payoffs (α, β) but does not parameterize cost of punishing per se (per unit or per token).",0.7,N/R,"No explicit ""per coin"" or multiplier specification for punishment; only outcome payoffs when playing c or d.",0.7,0,Only sanctioning/punishment is modeled; no rewarding or positive incentives.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in this model condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in this model condition.,1,N/R,Payoff matrix is specified but no per-period endowment in tokens/points is defined.,0.8,N/R,Paper focuses on conditions for full cooperation (all choose c) but does not report simulated or empirical contribution rates.,1,N/R,"No numeric contributions or averages are reported, only theoretical strategy profiles.",1,N/R,"States that full cooperation is globally efficient, but no numeric efficiency ratios are reported.",0.9,N/R,"No aggregate expected payoff values are calculated, only inequalities on parameters for cooperation.",0.9,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No participants; analytical model only.,1,No human,"Work is entirely theoretical; no lab, online, or field implementation with humans.",1,"Infinitely repeated pairwise prisoner’s dilemma with random matching (probability of any pair per period = 1/(n−1)), symmetric discount factor δ<1, payoffs: (c,c)→(1,1); (c,d)→(−β,α); (d,c)→(α,−β); (d,d)→(0,0). Gossip spreads deterministically across an undirected, unweighted social network g at rate r degrees per round. In-group policing strategy σ^NWIGP prescribes T-round capitulation punishment following deviations.",Summarized directly from Sections 4–6 and Appendix A (model setup and strategies).,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2016.03.017.md,Theoretical model – COARSE network knowledge,"Paper defines a second network knowledge environment labeled COARSE, with different information assumptions and equilibrium conditions (Proposition 2). Treated as separate modeling condition.",1,"Network knowledge environment = COARSE (agents know only node set N and the mean Tr-neighborhood size (1/n) Σj #N_j^{Tr}(g), with this being common knowledge); structural parameters (n, r, T, α, β, g) held generic.",Condition differs from PRECISE only in what agents know about network structure (precise vs coarse average neighborhood information).,1,False,"As with PRECISE, this is an analytical treatment; no human data.",1,False,No experimental manipulation with subjects; only theoretical variation in assumptions.,1,False,Not conducted in any lab setting.,1,False,No computational simulations under COARSE are reported.,1,True,Proposition 2 and 3 derive closed-form equilibrium conditions analytically under COARSE.,1,0,Group size is denoted by symbolic parameter n; no concrete numeric group size or actual players.,0.5,0,Infinitely repeated game; no fixed finite number of rounds.,1,1,"Same underlying prisoner’s dilemma action set {c,d} as under PRECISE.",0.9,N/A,No contribution endowment structure; only binary cooperate/defect choices.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return parameter; structure is not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,"Gossip transmission is part of the modelled environment, not a participant option in an implemented interface; and there are no human subjects.",1,0,"Agents receive specific gossip about deviators, not summaries of others’ earnings or actions.",0.9,1,"As in PRECISE, messages m_{j,i,t} include offender identity i and time t.",1,0,No rewards are part of this condition either.,1,0,"Infinite horizon game, no finite horizon information given to agents.",1,1,Same in-group policing strategy σ^NWIGP with T-round capitulation punishment is used under COARSE.,1,N/R,Cost of punishment is embedded in PD payoffs and discounting but not parameterized as a standalone cost per token.,0.7,N/R,No explicit numeric mapping between punishment effort and penalty magnitude beyond the PD payoff matrix.,0.7,0,No reward schemes under COARSE either; only punishment.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent; cost per reward unit thus not applicable.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/R,Model specifies payoffs but not per-round endowment in tokens/points.,0.8,N/R,"While condition for full cooperation is derived, no quantitative contribution rates are simulated or reported.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions given (only theoretical behavior c vs d).,1,N/R,Result is qualitative (cooperation easier and thus potentially higher group fitness) but without numeric efficiency metrics.,0.9,N/R,No explicit expected group payoff levels are computed or tabulated for COARSE vs PRECISE.,0.9,N/A,No empirical participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No empirical participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No empirical participants; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No empirical participants; theoretical model only.,1,No human,"Condition is part of the same analytical framework as PRECISE, without human subjects.",1,"Same infinitely repeated, randomly matched prisoner’s dilemma as in PRECISE, with identical payoffs and gossip dynamics. Difference lies in information structure: agents know only the roster N and the average Tr-neighborhood size (1/n Σj #N_j^{Tr}(g)), and this is common knowledge. Equilibrium conditions for sustaining full cooperation under σ^NWIGP involve this average neighborhood size instead of the minimum Tr-neighborhood, making cooperation potentially easier to sustain.","Summarized from Section 7 and Proposition 2 and 3, which compare PRECISE vs COARSE information environments.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0267153.md,High Partner Choice condition – US participants – first-round truster role,Condition is defined by High Partner Choice manipulation; restricted to US participants and first-round truster role to avoid carry-over effects as in authors’ analyses.,0.88,Partner choice condition: High (participants told they could switch partners after 2 rounds); Society: US; Role/order: participant was truster in round 1.,"Independent variables explicitly manipulated (partner choice condition, society) plus role/order subset used for clean behavioral measures.",0.88,True,Human participants from US via MTurk.,1,True,Random assignment to High vs Low Partner Choice with controlled game structure.,1,False,"Study conducted online via crowdsourcing platforms, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Although partners were simulated, focal data are from human decisions.",1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model as the primary method in this condition.,1,2,Dyadic Trust Game with Punishment between truster and responder.,1,2,Behavioral outcomes of interest for this subset are from the two initial TGP interactions before possible switching; paper states two interactions with same partner before switch option.,0.8,1,Truster could send any amount 0–100 in 10-point increments; responder could return 0–100% in 10% increments.,1,0,All 100 points endowment starts under truster’s control; nothing pre-allocated to joint pool.,1,1.5,"Points sent P are tripled (3P) and shared between two players, so marginal per capita return is 3/2.",1,0,No mention of any communication channel between players.,1,0,"Participants only see partner’s immediate decisions (percent returned, punishment), no aggregate summaries reported.",0.8,0,Partners are anonymous; no identities attached to punishment decisions.,0.9,0,"No explicit rewarding mechanism; only trust, reciprocation, and costly punishment.",1,1,Instructions told participants they would interact twice with same partner and in High co