10.18564_jasss.3166.md,Simulation – PP-type game,Paper defines PP-type games as having first-order punishment of defectors and second-order punishment of non-punishers; cooperation dynamics and maxima reported specifically for PP-type.,1,Game type: PP (first-order punishment P and second-order punishment PP enabled); Intermediate parameters μ (incentive cost-effect ratio) varied from 0.0 to 2.0 in steps of 0.1; δ (discount factor of costs) varied from 0.0 to 1.0 in steps of 0.1.,Simulation scenarios systematically vary μ and δ for each game type; here the configuration is PP-type.,1,False,Agent-based computer simulation with N agents; no human participants mentioned.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational model exploration.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; entirely simulated agents.,1,True,Described explicitly as an agent-based simulation model.,1,False,"Although some formulas are given, results are from numerical simulations rather than closed-form analytical solutions.",1,20,Section 5.1: 'The number of agents is fixed to 20 in all simulations.',1,1000,Section 5.1: 'All of the simulation lengths were 1000 steps'; steps correspond to repeated meta-sanction game rounds before measuring cooperation.,1,1,"Agents choose probabilistically to cooperate or defect each round; contribution is binary (provide appropriate knowledge or not), which operationally matches an all-or-nothing contribution decision.",0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment or token account; only costs and benefits in payoff equations for simulated agents.,1,N/R,Model uses parameters κ0 and ρ0 and count of cooperators N0; no marginal per capita return (multiplier divided by group size) is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication channel; purely simulated agents.,1,N/R,Simulation description does not specify any interface-like feedback such as earnings summaries.,1,N/A,No identities or interface in agent-based simulation akin to human experiments.,1,N/A,"As above, no explicit notion of revealed identities.",1,N/R,No notion of agents being informed about total steps or remaining rounds.,1,1,PP-type defined as having System P (punish defectors) and System PP (punish non-punishers).,1,N/R,"Paper defines symbolic cost parameters κp and κpp via intermediate δ and κ0, but does not give a single numeric per-unit 'cost' comparable to tokens.",0.6,N/R,"Benefit/penalty of punishment ρp, ρpp parameterized by μ but no discrete tokens-per-cost unit specified.",0.6,0,"PP-type game, by definition, includes first-order punishment and second-order punishment of non-punishers; no reward systems (R, PR, RP, RR) mentioned as active.",1,N/A,No reward mechanisms present in PP-type game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanisms present.,1,N/R,"Model uses a cost κ0 for cooperation, not a fixed endowment of tokens.",1,0.985,Figure 6: maximum cooperation rate in PP-type games is 0.985 at μ=2.0 and δ=0.7; cooperation rate is defined as average of agents’ cooperation parameters bi at step 1000.,1,N/R,Model has binary cooperate/defect decision; no token quantity contributed.,1,N/R,No measure of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum possible) is reported.,1,N/R,"Simulation tracks individual payoffs for evolution, but aggregate group payoff statistics are not reported.",1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,Meta-sanction game with public-goods stage followed by first-order punishment of defectors and second-order punishment of non-punishers; agents have cooperation rate b_i and reaction rate r_i that evolve via roulette selection and genetic operators.,"Summarized from sections describing meta-sanction framework, agent strategies, and evolution process for PP-type configuration.",1
10.18564_jasss.3166.md,Simulation – PR-type game,PR-type games described as having first-order punishment and second-order rewards for punishers; cooperation dynamics and robustness highlighted (Figures 7 and 9).,1,Game type: PR (first-order punishment P and second-order reward PR enabled); Intermediate parameters μ varied from 0.0 to 2.0 in steps of 0.1; δ varied from 0.0 to 1.0 in steps of 0.1.,Simulation explores full μ–δ grid for each game type; this row is for PR-type.,1,False,Agent-based simulation; no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical or field experiment.,1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly an agent-based simulation model.,1,False,"Results from simulations, not closed-form analysis.",1,20,Same N=20 agents for all simulations (Section 5.1).,1,1000,All scenarios run for 1000 steps before evaluating cooperation (Section 5.1).,1,1,"As in other game types, agents either cooperate (provide knowledge) or defect each round.",0.7,N/A,No explicit resource endowment per round.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return on contributions is specified.,1,N/A,No humans; no communication channel.,1,N/R,No UI or feedback structure akin to human experiments is described.,1,N/A,No explicit identities in the agent-based model.,1,N/A,As above.,1,N/R,Agents are not described as knowing the horizon length.,1,1,"PR-type has System P (punish defectors) and System PR (reward punishers), so punishment is present as a first-order sanction.",1,N/R,"Costs κp and κpr are parameterized by δ and κ0, but no single numeric token cost is given.",0.6,N/R,Punishment impact ρp is defined via μ but no discrete conversion useful as a token 'technology' is specified.,0.6,1,PR-type includes second-order rewards for punishers (System PR); those are reward mechanisms.,1,N/R,Costs κpr for PR are defined symbolically; no standalone numeric cost per reward token.,0.6,N/R,Reward magnitude ρpr defined through μ but not as a simple per-token multiplier.,0.6,N/R,Only costs and benefits appear; no explicit resource endowment.,1,0.986,"Figure 6 table: PR row, '-' column (i.e., PR-type without additional reward systems) has maximum cooperation rate 0.986.",1,N/R,No numeric amount per cooperative act.,1,N/R,No reported payoff-efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not summarized.,1,N/A,No humans; only agents.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulation.,1,"Meta-sanction game with public-goods stage plus first-order punishment of defectors (P) and second-order rewards for punishers (PR). Agent strategies (cooperation b_i, reaction r_i) evolve via roulette selection, crossover, and mutation. PR-type is shown to be highly robust for cooperation over a wide μ–δ parameter range.",Summarized from descriptions of PR-type game structure and results (Figures 7 and 9).,1
10.18564_jasss.3166.md,Simulation – PB-type game,PB-type games described as having both first-order punishment and reward and relevant second-order incentives; highlighted as capable of reaching cooperative regimes and used in comparisons.,1,"Game type: PB (Systems P and R at first order, plus associated second-order systems PP and PR); Intermediate parameters μ varied from 0.0 to 2.0 in steps of 0.1; δ varied from 0.0 to 1.0 in steps of 0.1.",Figure 6 and robustness table (Figure 9) report outcomes for PB-type games across μ–δ grid.,1,False,Model is an agent-based simulation.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment.,1,False,No physical lab or human subjects.,1,True,Explicitly presented as simulation results.,1,False,Results are numerical.,1,20,Section 5.1 applies generally to all simulations.,1,1000,All episodes run for 1000 steps.,1,1,Agent decision is binary cooperate/defect each period.,0.7,N/A,No token endowments used.,1,N/R,Not defined for this model.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,N/R,No user interface described.,1,N/A,No explicit identities corresponding to human participants.,1,N/A,Same reason as above.,1,N/R,Simulation does not describe agents as being aware of horizon.,1,1,PB-type defined as System B at first order (includes P and R) plus relevant second-order sanctions; hence punishment is present.,1,N/R,Only symbolic κ parameters provided.,0.6,N/R,Only symbolic ρ parameters.,0.6,1,First-order rewards to cooperators are part of PB-type (System R) and there are also associated second-order incentives (PR and RR or analogous combinations).,1,N/R,Costs κr etc. defined symbolically via μ and δ.,0.6,N/R,"Reward magnitude parameters ρr, ρpr, etc. not expressed as a simple per-unit technology.",0.6,N/R,No explicit per-round token endowment.,1,0.986,"Figure 6: PB row, '-' column shows maximum cooperation rate 0.986.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions per cooperator.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed or reported.,1,N/R,No group payoff statistics reported.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Fully computational agent-based model.,1,Meta-sanction game where cooperators can be rewarded and defectors punished at first order (System B = combination of P and R). Second-order sanctions include both meta-punishments for non-punishers and meta-rewards for punishers (and/or rewarders) according to the PB definition. PB-type is capable of achieving high cooperation and has robustness comparable to PR-type as noted in robustness checks.,"Summarized from definitions of PB-type games and results discussion (Sections 3.10, 5.6, 5.9).",0.9
10.18564_jasss.3166.md,Simulation – PB+R-type game,PB+R-type games singled out as complex systems with first-order rewards but no second-order incentives for rewards; Figures 5 and 10 and discussion section focus on their failure to sustain cooperation.,1,"Game type: PB+R (Systems P and R at first order, Systems PP and PR at second order, plus additional first-order rewards R without corresponding second-order incentives for rewards); Intermediate parameters μ varied from 0.0 to 2.0 in steps of 0.1; δ varied from 0.0 to 1.0 in steps of 0.1. Specific trajectory example discussed for μ=2.0, δ=1.0.","Described as 'PB+R-type' with controlled μ and δ; example dynamics shown for μ=2.0, δ=1.0.",0.9,False,Simulation study; no human participants.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,"No lab, field, or online humans.",1,True,Agent-based simulation with evolving strategies.,1,False,Results generated numerically.,1,20,General simulation setup (Section 5.1).,1,1000,Standard length of each simulation run.,1,1,Same binary cooperation decision as other game types.,0.7,N/A,No token endowment model.,1,N/R,MPCR not defined.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,No such feature described.,1,N/A,No identities; agent-based.,1,N/A,Same as above.,1,N/R,Horizon visibility not discussed.,1,1,"PB+R-type contains Systems P and PP, so punishment of defectors and non-punishers exists.",1,N/R,Only parameterized cost symbols.,0.6,N/R,Only symbolic ρ parameters.,0.6,1,PB+R includes first-order rewards for cooperators (R) and second-order rewards for punishers (PR).,1,N/R,"Rewards costs κr and κpr specified symbolically, not numerically.",0.6,N/R,Reward magnitude parameters not linked to a specific unit technology.,0.6,N/R,No explicit endowment tokens per step.,1,0,"Discussion and Figure 10 describe that in PB+R-type games, cooperation 'is never dominant under any condition' and example trajectory shows cooperation and reaction rates eventually declining to 0.0, implying that in equilibrium cooperation collapses. Using 0 as characteristic contribution rate outcome.",0.7,N/R,No quantitative contribution amount reported.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics not provided.,1,N/R,Group payoff not summarized for PB+R-type.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational study.,1,"Complex meta-sanction configuration including first-order punishments of defectors, first-order rewards for cooperators, second-order punishments of non-punishers, and second-order rewards for punishers, but without second-order incentives for rewarding behavior. Dynamics show initial increase in reaction and cooperation rates followed by decline to zero as agents evolve towards lower reaction rates and occasional defectors face no effective sanctions; cooperation cannot be sustained regardless of μ and δ in explored ranges.",Summarized from 'Disturbance by reward system' section and Figures 5 and 10.,0.9
