10.1371_journal.pone.0124513.md,Two-level trust game with centralized sanctioning (theoretical model),Paper presents a formal two-level trust game with an authority and punishment; no separate named experimental conditions with humans are implemented.,0.87,"Ex ante belief about trustee type (π1^E) across five categories: Full optimism, High optimism, Intermediate optimism, High pessimism, Full pessimism; Ex ante belief about authority type (π1^A) across same five categories; punishment magnitude ψ; authority reward R3; authority punishment cost C3.","Model systematically varies beliefs about types (π1^E, π1^A) and parameters of centralized sanctioning (ψ, R3, C3) to derive equilibria, summarized in Tables 2 and 3.",0.9,False,No human participants or empirical data collection; the work is purely a formal game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,"Although the word 'experiments' appears in author contributions and discussion, in this specific paper only a theoretical model is developed; no controlled experiment is actually run.",0.9,False,No lab or field implementation is described; only analytical derivations.,1,False,No numerical or computational simulations are reported; results are closed-form analytical equilibria.,1,True,The paper derives sequential equilibria of a trust game and its two-level extension using formal mathematical analysis only.,1,3,"Two-level trust game explicitly has three actors: trustor A1, trustee A2, and authority A3.",1,2,"Authors assume the game is played twice (n=1,2) without discounting when deriving equilibria.",1,1,"Trust game is binary at each move (place trust vs not, honour vs abuse), but this refers to cooperation choice, not a continuous contribution PGG; map as standard all-or-nothing trust decision.",0.7,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game and there are no human subjects.,1,N/R,"No linear public goods structure or marginal per capita return is defined; only payoffs R1, S1, T2, etc.",1,N/A,No human implementation; communication is not modeled.,1,0,Information structure is about types and beliefs; no mention of payoff summaries shown between rounds.,0.8,N/A,"Authority and trustee identities are fixed and known in the model; no informational treatment about identity revelation is discussed, and there are no human participants.",1,N/A,"No distinct reward-giver identity beyond the authority, and no human interface is implemented.",1,1,Game is finitely repeated with all players knowing exactly that there are two periods.,1,1,Central authority A3 can impose costly punishment ψ on abusive trustee A2.,1,N/R,Cost to authority is denoted C3 but no specific numeric value is assigned.,1,N/R,Punishment magnitude on trustee is ψ but its numerical value is not specified.,1,1,"Authority receives a reward R3 when trust is placed and honoured, effectively a payoff bonus.",1,N/R,"No explicit cost per unit of reward is modeled; R3 is a payoff parameter, not a costly action.",1,N/R,No technology translating costly actions into rewards is specified; R3 is exogenous payoff.,1,N/R,"Model uses abstract utilities R1, S1, T2 etc.; initial monetary endowments are not defined.",1,N/R,"Theoretical work focuses on equilibrium strategies (probabilities of trusting/honouring/punishing), not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,No token or monetary contributions are modeled; only binary decisions and utilities.,1,N/R,"Paper does not compute efficiency as ratio of achieved to maximum group payoff, though it qualitatively discusses group utilities.",1,N/R,No specific numeric group payoff is reported; payoffs are symbolic.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,"Game-theoretic analysis only, without implementation with human subjects.",1,"Sequential two-period trust game extended with a third player (authority) who can impose costly punishment ψ on an abusing trustee. There are two types for trustee (G-type honours trust, B-type prefers abuse) and two types for authority (G-type always punishes abuse, B-type only punishes if long-run incentives justify cost). Incomplete information about types is modeled via beliefs π1^E and π1^A. No discounting; utilities are R1, S1, R2, T2, R3, C3, ψ; when no trust is placed utilities P1=P2=P3=0. The analysis derives sequential equilibria across 25 belief-category combinations (FO, HO, IO, HP, FP for each of trustee and authority).",Summarizes the structural and payoff features and key informational assumptions of the proposed two-level trust game as described in the Method and Results sections and Tables 1–3.,0.96
10.1111_jpet.12090.md,Analytical model – team trust game with coordinated and peer punishment,Paper presents a single formal model of a team trust game with coordinated punishment and peer punishment; no separate empirical treatments are run.,1,"Institutional and preference parameters: investment cost c∈(0,0.5); punishment effectiveness λ∈(0,1]; punishment cost z>0; peer punishment damage γ; reciprocity intensity α≥1; cultural transmission parameter k; profit-selection parameter φ; fractions of reciprocal investors q_t and fair-minded allocators p_t. Key comparative statics on λ, γ, α, c and initial (q_0,p_0).",Model explicitly varies these parameters and analyzes equilibria and long-run cultures as functions of their values.,1,False,No human or observational data; purely theoretical evolutionary game model.,1,False,No controlled experiment is conducted; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation with participants.,1,False,"Dynamics are given in closed-form difference equations; while they mention ‘simulations’ qualitatively, no explicit numerical or computational simulation study is specified as a separate method.",0.7,True,Model is solved using game-theoretic and dynamical-systems analysis with closed-form conditions and propositions.,1,3,"Team trust game has two investors and one allocator: ""a team of investors, composed of two players ... is matched with an allocator"".",1,1,"Within a generation the game is played once; overlapping generations provide repetition across generations, not repeated rounds within an interaction.",0.9,1,"Investors choose binary invest/not-invest decisions and do not choose contribution levels; this does not map to a continuous contribution choice, so coded as 1 (players can take non-all-or-nothing actions regarding punishment and rewards).",0.3,N/A,"Trust game, not a standard public goods endowment-contribution structure; no default allocation between private/public accounts is defined.",1,N/R,No linear public goods structure; returns are specified via surplus 2 and transfers b but no MPCR is defined.,1,0,No communication or messaging between players is described in the model.,1,0,Information structure only involves observing investment and reward actions; no payoffs or punishment summaries from others are displayed across rounds.,1,1,Peer punishment is targeted at a specific teammate who failed to punish; identities within the team are implicitly known.,0.8,0,"Allocator pays symmetric reward b to each investor; there is no identity-dependent rewarding beyond roles, and no visibility of who rewarded whom is discussed.",0.6,0,Game is a single-shot interaction within a generation; no information about total or remaining rounds is relevant.,1,1,The core of the model is coordinated punishment of the allocator and peer punishment among investors.,1,N/R,"They denote punishment cost as z (or z/2 when both punish), but do not normalize it into a token-per-unit mapping compatible with the requested field; only symbolic parameter is given.",0.4,N/R,"Effectiveness parameter λ destroys a proportion λ of allocator payoff, but no discrete per-token technology is specified.",0.6,1,Allocator sets monetary reward b to each investor after surplus is produced.,1,N/R,Allocator’s choice of b reduces her own payoff 2−2b but no per-token cost parameter is defined.,0.6,N/R,Reward b is a direct transfer; no multiplier or token technology is introduced.,0.6,N/R,Investors face a cost c to invest and receive payoff b if investment occurs; an explicit numerical endowment per investor is not specified.,0.5,N/R,"Outcomes analyzed are investment decisions, rewards, punishment, and long-run cultures (FCC vs NCC); no normalized contribution rate is reported.",1,N/R,Model does not track numeric contribution amounts beyond binary investment decisions.,1,N/R,"They discuss efficiency qualitatively and identify cooperative vs non-cooperative cultures, but do not report explicit efficiency ratios as numbers.",0.8,N/R,"Payoffs are expressed symbolically (e.g., joint surplus 2, payoffs b, 2−2b) and not as a single numeric summary outcome per treatment.",0.9,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,No human,Model is analytical with no empirical implementation.,1,"Sequential team trust game with two investors and one allocator in overlapping generations. Stage 1: each investor chooses Invest (I) or Not Invest (NI); if both invest, surplus 2 is produced, each investor has gross gain 1 and pays cost c∈(0,0.5). Stage 2: allocator observes surplus and sets symmetric reward b∈[0,1] to each investor; allocator’s payoff is 2−2b. Stage 3 (coordinated punishment): investors simultaneously choose punish (p) or not (np). If both choose p, proportion λ of allocator’s payoff is destroyed and each punisher pays cost z/2; if only one punishes, punishment is ineffective (no destruction) and that investor pays cost z. Stage 4 (peer punishment): if coordinated punishment failed because the teammate defected, a reciprocal investor who chose p can punish the defector, imposing utility loss γ on the defector at normalized zero cost. There are two investor preference types (selfish vs reciprocal with inequity aversion α) and two allocator types (selfish vs fair-minded who always set b=0.5). Beliefs about types are characterized by shares q_t (reciprocators) and p_t (fair-minded allocators). Dynamics: p_t evolves via payoff-monotonic (replicator-like) selection on allocator profits; q_t evolves via Bisin–Verdier cultural transmission with costly vertical education effort τ^i and oblique transmission, governed by intolerance parameters ΔV^i. Under assumptions z≤αλ, z/2<γ, λ≥0.5, the only long-run cultures are a Fully Cooperative Culture (FCC) with high trust, fair rewards (b=0.5) and no punishment in equilibrium, and a Non-Cooperative Culture (NCC) with no investment and low cooperation.","Summarizes detailed structure of the modeled game, preferences, and dynamics as described in Sections 2–7.",1
10.1007_s10588-013-9170-1.md,Two-group model – baseline (no punishment mechanisms),"Two-group virtual experiment with λ_OGC = 0 and λ_OG = 0, varying only ω to obtain baseline cooperation without any group-level punishment.",1,Across-group interaction rate ω randomly drawn from 0.01–0.25; no collective sanctions (λ_OG = 0); no in-group policing of out-group cheating (λ_OGC = 0).,Design section: first two-group experiment sets λ_OGC and λ_OG to 0 and varies ω from 1 to 25 percent.,1,False,"Agent-based model with simulated agents, no human participants.",1,False,"Computational virtual experiments, not controlled experiments with human or field subjects.",1,False,No physical lab; purely computational NetLogo simulations.,1,True,Explicitly described as an agent-based model implemented in NetLogo with virtual experiments.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution; uses simulations due to intractability.,1,2601,Appendix A: p (population size) = 2601 agents.,1,3000,Burn-in is 3000 time steps; outcomes measured after burn-in in a single post–burn-in period. Treat 3000 as minimum effective number of rounds before measurement.,0.6,1,"Agents choose a discrete action (C or D) in a Prisoner’s Dilemma; there is no continuous contribution choice, so not an all-or-nothing contribution game in the PGG sense; coded 1 to indicate binary action choice allowed each round.",0.5,N/A,Not a public goods/endowment contribution game; it is a one-shot PD per interaction.,1,N/R,No public good or multiplier; only PD payoffs are specified.,1,0,Agents follow fixed strategies and have no communication mechanism.,1,0,Agents do not receive summaries of others’ earnings; only reputational tags are tracked.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action choice by agents in this baseline (no punishment mechanisms modeled).,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Agents are not informed about horizon or remaining rounds; they use heuristics and reproduction dynamics.,1,0,Configuration explicitly sets λ_OG = 0 and λ_OGC = 0 so no cross-group or in-group policing punishment related to out-group is active.,1,N/A,"No explicit costly punishment decision variable; selection acts via payoffs, not chosen punishment tokens.",1,N/A,No explicit mapping from punishment tokens to losses.,1,0,No distinct prosocial reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Model does not specify per-round token endowments; agents directly receive PD payoffs that update PTR.,1,0.067,"Across all runs, average percentage of CC outcomes in across-group interactions is 6.7%. Interpret this as fraction of fully cooperative outcomes.",0.9,N/A,No numeric contribution amount; binary C/D decisions in PD.,1,N/R,Paper does not report aggregate payoffs relative to maximum possible in a normalized efficiency metric.,1,N/R,Average or total group payoff is not reported numerically.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based simulations with no human subjects.,1,"Two-group agent-based evolutionary Prisoner’s Dilemma. Payoff matrix: (C,C)→(2,2); (C,D)→(-1,3); (D,C)→(3,-1); (D,D)→(0,0). Agents have bounded rationality, reproduce with probability PTR updated via α = 0.5, population capped at 2601, death rate d = 0.10, mutation rate μ = 0.003. Within-group in-group cheating memory λ_IGC = 10, but no tags or sanctions for out-group cheating in this condition. Fraction of across-group interactions ω is drawn from 0.01–0.25; remainder are within-group. Tolerance τ = 0.25 is irrelevant here because λ_OG = 0.",Summarized directly from Model and Appendix A for this baseline configuration.,1
10.1007_s10588-013-9170-1.md,Two-group model – collective sanctions only,"Condition where λ_OGC = 0 (no in-group policing) and λ_OG > 0 (collective sanctions present), varying λ_OG and ω.",1,Collective sanction memory length λ_OG randomly drawn from 1–100; across-group interaction rate ω randomly drawn from 0.01–0.25; no in-group policing of out-group cheating (λ_OGC = 0).,"Design: λ_OGC set to 0, λ_OG randomly 1–100, ω randomly 1–25 percent.",1,False,Agent-based computer simulation.,1,False,Virtual computational experiments only.,1,False,No physical experimental lab.,1,True,Agent-based model in NetLogo with stochastic runs.,1,False,"Results derived numerically, not analytically.",1,2601,Appendix A: p = 2601.,1,3000,Burn-in of 3000 time steps until steady state; then one-period measurement.,0.6,1,Binary cooperate/defect actions in PD; no graded contributions.,0.5,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier defined.,1,0,No communication channel among agents.,1,0,"Agents only track reputational tags and group cheating status, not payoff summaries.",1,0,"Collective sanctions target entire out-group, not identifiable individuals in the out-group; agents cannot identify individual out-group cheaters.",1,N/A,No explicit reward actions.,1,0,No information about remaining time steps is given to agents.,1,1,"Collective sanctions are active via λ_OG > 0, causing group-level punishment after tolerance threshold is exceeded.",1,N/A,Punishment is embedded in payoff consequences of interactions; no explicit cost per punishment unit is parameterized.,1,N/A,No parameter mapping expenditures to inflicted losses.,1,0,No prosocial reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No per-round endowment is specified; interactions directly yield payoffs.,1,0.068,"Results section: with collective sanctions, overall level of cooperation (percentage of CC outcomes in across-group interactions) is 6.8%.",0.9,N/A,"Binary C/D decisions, no amounts.",1,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure reported.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs per group are not numerically summarized.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based computer simulation.,1,"Two-group evolutionary PD with same payoff matrix and core dynamics as baseline. Within-group in-group cheating memory λ_IGC = 10. Collective sanctions: each group tracks frequency of CD outcomes in across-group interactions per period; if above tolerance τ = 0.25, the out-group is tagged as having cheated for λ_OG time steps, during which agents adjust their third vs. fourth strategy components (treating the out-group as a cheater group). No separate out-group-cheater individual tag (λ_OGC = 0), so no in-group policing of out-group cheaters. Strategies include five components, but the 5th (responding to in-group out-group-cheaters) is irrelevant here. Approximately 45.9% of agents cooperate with the out-group when it has not cheated, but only 22.9% do so when it has cheated; 88.1% of across-group interactions result in DD overall.",Summarized from Model 2.2 and Results 4.1 plus Appendix A.,1
10.1007_s10588-013-9170-1.md,Two-group model – in-group policing only,"Condition where λ_OG = 0 (no collective sanctions) and λ_OGC > 0 (in-group policing of out-group cheating), varying λ_OGC and ω.",1,Out-group cheater punishment memory length λ_OGC randomly drawn from 1–100; across-group interaction rate ω randomly drawn from 0.01–0.25; no collective sanctions (λ_OG = 0).,"Design: to test in-group policing, λ_OG set to 0 and λ_OGC varied 1–100 with ω from 1–25 percent.",1,False,Agent-based computer simulation.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment.,1,False,"Purely computational, no lab.",1,True,NetLogo agent-based simulation with virtual experiments.,1,False,Uses simulations instead of closed-form derivations.,1,2601,Appendix A: p = 2601 agents.,1,3000,Same Markov-chain-based burn-in of 3000 steps before measuring; one measurement per run.,0.6,1,Binary PD actions (C/D).,0.5,N/A,No endowment-based contribution decisions.,1,N/R,No public good structure.,1,0,No communication between agents.,1,0,"Agents observe reputational tags, not payoff summaries.",1,1,In-group policing targets identified individual out-group cheaters within a group; group members know which specific in-group member has the out-group-cheater tag.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,No horizon information to agents.,1,1,In-group policing is a punishment mechanism where in-group members defect against identified out-group cheaters for λ_OGC time steps.,1,N/A,No explicit cost parameter per punishment unit; costs are implicit in PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment token technology specified.,1,0,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Only PD payoffs; no fixed endowment.,1,0.8,"Text: once λ_OGC ≥ 10, predicted cooperation is consistently over 80%; also 170/200 runs are classified as high-cooperation with >80% CC across-group. Use 0.8 as representative high-cooperation outcome.",0.7,N/A,"Cooperation is binary, not an amount.",1,N/R,No reported normalized efficiency.,1,N/R,No explicit group payoff averages.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations only.,1,"Same two-group evolutionary PD core as in other conditions. λ_IGC = 10 for in-group cheating. Here, λ_OG = 0 so groups are never tagged as having cheated; collective sanctions strategy components (3 vs. 4) regarding group-level cheating are irrelevant. Agents track individual out-group cheating within their group via an out-group-cheater tag, visible only to in-group members, lasting λ_OGC time steps. Fifth strategy component specifies action towards in-group members tagged as out-group cheaters; defecting on them carries no reputational cost. In 200 runs, 170 show >80% CC outcomes in across-group interactions. In these high-cooperation systems, 94.6% of agents cooperate with out-group members, while on average 61.7% of agents actually punish out-group cheaters; some high-cooperation systems exist with less than one-third punishers. Cooperation peaks around ω ≈ 0.10–0.15 when λ_OGC is sufficiently large.",Summarized from Model 2.2 and Results 4.2 and 4.4.,1
10.1007_s10588-013-9170-1.md,Two-group model – combined collective sanctions and in-group policing (λ_OG = 5),"Condition with both mechanisms active, fixing short collective sanction length λ_OG = 5 and varying λ_OGC.",1,Out-group cheater punishment memory λ_OGC randomly drawn from 1–100 with fixed collective sanction length λ_OG = 5; across-group interaction rate ω fixed at 0.10.,"Design: for combined mechanism tests, λ_OG fixed at 5,10,25,50; λ_OGC varied 1–100; ω fixed at .10.",1,False,Agent-based simulation.,1,False,"Computational virtual experiments, not human experiments.",1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,NetLogo-based agent simulations.,1,False,No analytical solution.,1,2601,p = 2601 in Appendix A.,1,3000,Same burn-in then measurement as other runs.,0.6,1,Binary PD decisions.,0.5,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,N/R,No multiplier.,1,0,No communication.,1,0,No payoff summaries.,1,1,"In-group policing relies on identifying specific in-group out-group cheaters, whose tags are visible to in-group members.",1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,0,Agents have no horizon info.,1,1,Both group-level (collective sanctions) and individual-level (in-group policing) punishments are active.,1,N/A,Costs are implicit in PD payoffs; no explicit token cost.,1,N/A,No explicit mapping of punishment tokens to losses.,1,0,No separate reward actions.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Only PD payoffs used.,1,0.8,"Results: with short λ_OG (5) and sufficiently long λ_OGC, high levels of cooperation emerge, similar to pure in-group policing (>80% CC). Use 0.8 to represent this high-cooperation regime.",0.6,N/A,Binary cooperation decisions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically summarized.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations.,1,"Same two-group PD setting. Here λ_OG = 5, so when tolerance τ = 0.25 is exceeded in a period, the out-group is tagged as having cheated for 5 time steps, triggering group-level sanctioning behavior via the 4th strategy component. Simultaneously, out-group-cheating individuals are tagged for λ_OGC time steps and can be punished by in-group members using the 5th strategy component. ω is fixed at 0.10. Figures 7 and 8 indicate that with such short collective sanctions lengths, equilibrium cooperation levels remain high and comparable to those under pure in-group policing when λ_OGC is sufficiently long.",Summarized from Results 4.3 and design details.,1
