10.1007_s10588-013-9170-1.md,Two-group model – combined collective sanctions and in-group policing (λ_OG = 25),Condition with intermediate-length collective sanctions (λ_OG = 25) plus in-group policing.,1,Out-group cheater punishment memory λ_OGC randomly 1–100 with fixed λ_OG = 25; ω fixed at 0.10.,Design: λ_OG fixed at 25 for one set of 200 runs; λ_OGC varied 1–100; ω = .10.,1,False,Simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Agent-based computational runs.,1,False,No closed-form analysis.,1,2601,p = 2601.,1,3000,Same burn-in and measurement approach.,0.6,1,Binary PD moves.,0.5,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,N/R,No public good.,1,0,No chat.,1,0,No summaries displayed.,1,1,In-group policing uses individual tags.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No horizon information.,1,1,Both punishment mechanisms present.,1,N/A,No tokenized punishment cost.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,Not specified.,1,0.6,"Text: when λ_OGC > 20, CC outcomes fall to between 50 and 70 percent for intermediate λ_OG. Approximate central value 0.6.",0.7,N/A,Binary cooperation.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Same structure as other combined conditions with ω = 0.10 and λ_OGC varied. Here λ_OG = 25, meaning once tolerance τ is exceeded, sanctions persist for 25 time steps. Results (Fig. 9) show that when λ_OGC > 20 and λ_OG is this intermediate length, predicted CC outcomes in across-group interactions are only about 50–70%, lower than in the short λ_OG conditions and lower than in pure in-group policing, indicating that intermediate-length collective sanctions hinder the effectiveness of in-group policing.",Summarized from Results 4.3.,1
10.1007_s10588-013-9170-1.md,Two-group model – combined collective sanctions and in-group policing (λ_OG = 50),Condition with long collective sanctions (λ_OG = 50) plus in-group policing.,1,Out-group cheater punishment memory λ_OGC randomly 1–100 with fixed λ_OG = 50; ω fixed at 0.10.,Design: λ_OG fixed at 50 for one batch of combined-mechanism runs; λ_OGC and ω as specified.,1,False,Simulation.,1,False,Not empirical.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Agent-based model.,1,False,No analytical solution.,1,2601,p = 2601.,1,3000,Same burn-in rule.,0.6,1,Binary PD.,0.5,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR concept.,1,0,No chat.,1,0,No summaries.,1,1,In-group policing uses visible individual tags.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No horizon info.,1,1,Collective sanctions plus in-group policing both active.,1,N/A,No explicit per-unit cost.,1,N/A,No punishment token technology.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,Not given.,1,0.5,"Results: for the longest λ_OG, with λ_OGC > 20, CC share is approximately 50%.",0.8,N/A,Binary cooperation.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulated agents only.,1,"Same structure as other combined conditions, but λ_OG = 50 so collective sanctions last a long time once triggered. ω = 0.10; λ_OGC varies. Figure 10 shows that when λ_OGC > 20 with such long collective sanctions, predicted CC outcomes in across-group interactions fall to approximately 50%. The text concludes that intermediate and long λ_OG values lower groups’ ability to identify and punish out-group cheaters and thus reduce inter-group cooperation relative to in-group policing alone.",Summarized from Results 4.3.,1
