ng 43.75% male,Global sample composition; no treatment-level split.,0.9,Generally low education; exact distribution N/R,Participants described as many being lowly educated.,0.6,Field experiment,Artefactual field experiment in village field lab.,1,"Four players per group receive y=12, four receive y=8 each round; highest contributor (ties broken randomly) becomes representative and allocates aid as in other representative-sharing treatment. Representatives tend to be high-endowment players in most observations.",Endowment inequality and representative selection described in Section 2.1 and later analysis (32 of 40 representatives high-endowment).,0.9
10.3389_fevo.2014.00041.md,Study 1 – Generosity game – Control (silent environment),Study 1 is the helping game with reputation based on generosity; control groups are those without disturbance (silent environment).,1,Working-memory disturbance (superfluous words): absent (silent control); Reputation type: generosity only; Punishment option: absent,Paper contrasts control vs disturbance treatments within a generosity-based helping game without punishment.,1,True,Human university students played the game.,1,True,Controlled lab game with random matching and assigned treatments.,1,True,"Conducted with student groups in cubicles using experimental apparatus, not in natural field settings.",1,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely mathematical model developed as the study itself.,1,9.25,Study 1: 74 students in 8 groups of 9 or 10; average group size 74/8 = 9.25.,0.7,24,“We played 24 rounds per group.” for study 1.,1,1,"Donor could “give something to the Receiver (green light) or not (red light)” – binary decision, but schema defines 1 as ‘can contribute any amount’ vs 0 ‘all-or-nothing’. This is a pure help/defect choice, not a continuous contribution. However, within this schema it maps closer to all-or-nothing; set to 0 would better fit but instructions say 1 or 0 only with label reversed. To avoid mislabel, interpret ‘can contribute any amount’ as not applicable and choose 0=all-or-nothing.",0.4,0,Endowment starts entirely in private account; help is a cost to Donor and benefit to Receiver.,0.8,N/R,"This is a binary helping game, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",1,0,No communication mentioned; environment described as silent except for instructions.,0.9,0,"Players see image-score arrows and identities of roles, but no explicit summaries of others’ earnings or past detailed actions beyond the score.",0.6,0,No punishment option in study 1.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond donation decision itself.,1,0,Participants were told “No information was given about the total number of interactions that would be played.”,1,0,Study 1 is a helping game without punishment; only giving vs not giving.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No separate costly reward stage; help decision is the main move.,0.9,N/A,No formal reward mechanism distinct from donations.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism distinct from donations.,1,3,“initial account of £3.00”.,1,N/R,"Paper reports cooperation frequency qualitatively and in figures, but not numeric means suitable for extraction.",0.3,N/R,Exact monetary amount given per cooperation is obscured in the PDF snippet and not clearly specified.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios reported.,0.2,N/R,Only final individual accounts are discussed; no numeric group payoff values reported.,0.2,United Kingdom,“Seventy-four students of the University of Edinburgh (UK)…”,1,N/R,Age range or mean not reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students,Described as students of the University of Edinburgh.,1,On site,"Physical lab-like setup with boxes, cables, and cubicles on university premises.",1,"Indirect reciprocity helping game: in each of 24 rounds, randomly chosen Donor decides to give or not to Receiver. Giving increases Donor’s image score by +1, not giving decreases by −1. Image score displayed with arrow from −6 to +6 for both players before each interaction. Each player plays once per round as Donor and twice per two rounds as Receiver. No direct reciprocity: same pair never plays in reversed roles. Anonymity ensured via ID and light code system; no info on total number of rounds.",Summarized from ‘HELPING GAME WITH REPUTATION BASED ON GENEROSITY (STUDY 1)’ description.,1
10.3389_fevo.2014.00041.md,Study 1 – Generosity game – Disturbance (superfluous words),Same game as Study 1 control but with working-memory disturbance treatment applied to 3 of 8 groups.,1,Working-memory disturbance (superfluous words): present; Reputation type: generosity only; Punishment option: absent,Treatment groups in study 1 were exposed to superfluous phrases before each decision.,1,True,Human student participants.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of disturbance vs control.,1,True,Same lab-like apparatus and setting as control.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,9.25,Same subject pool and grouping as study 1 overall; disturbance applied to 3 of 8 groups.,0.7,24,All study 1 groups played 24 rounds.,1,1,Same binary give/not-give helping decision as control; see control reasoning.,0.4,0,Same private initial account structure as control.,0.8,N/R,Not defined for this helping game.,1,0,"No peer-to-peer communication; only experimenter’s superfluous phrases, which are not strategic chat.",0.9,0,"As in control, only image scores shown, no payoff summaries.",0.6,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No separate reward stage.,1,0,Players not informed about total number of interactions.,1,0,No punishment option in study 1.,1,N/A,Punishment unavailable.,1,N/A,Punishment unavailable.,1,0,No explicit reward stage.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,3,Same initial account of £3.00 as in study 1 overall.,1,N/R,Only qualitative statement that cooperation was lower in disturbed groups; no numeric values.,0.3,N/R,Exact amount per donation not clearly given.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency ratios reported.,0.2,N/R,"Only individual final accounts compared, no group totals.",0.2,United Kingdom,All study 1 participants were students of the University of Edinburgh (UK).,1,N/R,Age not provided.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students,Described as university students.,1,On site,Same physical experimental setup as control.,1,"Same indirect reciprocity helping game as Study 1 control, but before each decision experimenters said superfluous phrases (e.g., ‘Donor number X, please make your choice’) intended to distract and constrain working memory. Random matching ensured no pair ever played in reversed roles; 24 rounds; image scores from −6 to +6 displayed for both players.",Summarized from ‘TESTING THE EFFECTS OF CONSTRAINED WORKING MEMORY’ and study 1 description.,1
10.3389_fevo.2014.00041.md,Study 2 – Punishment reputation game – Control (silent environment),Study 2 is the helping game with punishment-based reputation; control groups are those without disturbance (silent).,1,Working-memory disturbance (superfluous words): absent (silent control); Reputation type: punishment only; Punishment option: present,Study 2 manipulates disturbance vs control within a punishment-based reputation helping game.,1,True,Human university students participated.,1,True,Controlled experiment with random matching and punishment option.,1,True,"Same lab setup (cubicles, boxes, screen) on campus.",1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,No purely analytical derivation as the primary method.,1,7.93,“111 students … distributed over 14 groups of 6 to 9.” Average group size = 111/14 ≈ 7.93.,0.7,16,"“Each player played 16 times in each of the two roles, i.e., 16 rounds were played in total.”",1,1,Donor chooses to donate (help) or not; binary decision analogous to all-or-nothing contribution.,0.4,0,Each player has a monetary account; helping and punishing are costly moves from private funds.,0.8,N/R,"Binary helping with fixed cost and benefit, not a standard linear PGG.",1,0,No communication between players reported; control described as ‘silent environment’.,0.95,0,Screen shows only Receiver’s ID and punishment reputation; no payoff summaries indicated.,0.7,1,Receivers’ punishment reputation and ID are displayed; Donors can link punishment reputation to specific IDs.,0.9,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,0,"As in study 1, players are not informed about total number of interactions.",0.9,1,"“after each decision of the Donor, the Receiver had an opportunity to punish the Donor.”",1,1,“punishment cost 1 CHF to the punisher and reduced the punished account by 2 CHF.”,1,2,Each 1 CHF spent reduces punished player’s account by 2 CHF; punishment multiplier of 2.,1,0,No explicit costly reward stage; only help and punishment.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,“initial account was 20 CHF to avoid negative accounts.”,1,N/R,Cooperation frequency plotted (Figure 4A) but numeric means by treatment not provided.,0.3,N/R,Donation cost/benefit specified (1 CHF cost to give 2 CHF) but not aggregated donation amounts.,0.6,N/R,No efficiency metric relative to maximum possible payoff reported.,0.2,N/R,Mean final accounts discussed but no numeric group payoffs.,0.2,Sw Switzerland,“111 students of the University of Lausanne (Switzerland)…”,1,N/R,Age information not reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not specified.,0,University students,Participants were students of the University of Lausanne.,1,On site,Conducted in university setting with the same physical apparatus as study 1.,1,"Indirect reciprocity helping game with punishment-based reputation. Donor decides to donate (cost 1 CHF, Receiver gains 2 CHF) or not. After Donor’s decision, Receiver may punish Donor (cost 1 CHF to punisher, reduces Donor’s account by 2 CHF). Initial account 20 CHF. Only Receiver’s ID and punishment reputation (score −5 to +5, starting at 0) are displayed before each interaction. Punishment reputation increases by +1 when Receiver punishes a non-donating Donor and decreases by −1 when Receiver does not punish a non-donating Donor. Generosity-based image scoring is experimentally excluded. Each player plays 16 times in each role (16 rounds total). Control groups played without superfluous verbal disturbance.",Summarized from ‘HELPING GAME WITH REPUTATION BASED ON PUNISHMENT (STUDY 2)’ and disturbance description.,1
10.3389_fevo.2014.00041.md,Study 2 – Punishment reputation game – Disturbance (superfluous words),"Same punishment-based reputation game as Study 2 control, but 3 of 14 groups received disturbance treatment.",1,Working-memory disturbance (superfluous words): present; Reputation type: punishment only; Punishment option: present,Groups assigned to disturbance treatment where experimenters said superfluous words before each decision.,1,True,Human university students.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of disturbance in a lab game.,1,True,On-site lab-like environment with experimental apparatus.,1,False,No mention of non-human simulations.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,7.93,Same grouping (14 groups of 6–9 students) as in study 2; disturbance applied to 3 groups.,0.7,16,All study 2 groups played 16 rounds.,1,1,Binary donate/not decision as in control.,0.4,0,Initial funds entirely in private account.,0.8,N/R,No per-capita return defined.,1,0,Only experimenter’s superfluous phrases; no communication among participants.,0.95,0,Only punishment score and Receiver ID reported on screen.,0.7,1,"Receivers’ punishment scores shown with their IDs to Donors, same as in control.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Players not informed about total number of interactions.,0.9,1,Receivers can punish Donors after each decision.,1,1,Punishment costs 1 CHF to the punisher.,1,2,Punishment reduces punished account by 2 CHF per 1 CHF spent.,1,0,No reward option.,0.95,N/A,Reward not available.,1,N/A,Reward not available.,1,20,Initial account of 20 CHF in study 2.,1,N/R,"Cooperation and punishment frequencies differ between treatments (Figure 4), but numeric values not provided.",0.3,N/R,Donation cost specified but not number of donations per subject.,0.6,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure reported.,0.2,N/R,Mean final accounts mentioned but not numerical values by treatment.,0.2,Switzerland,"Students were from the University of Lausanne, Switzerland.",1,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,University students,Participants described as university students.,1,On site,Same on-campus lab-like setting as other conditions.,1,"Same punishment-based indirect reciprocity helping game as Study 2 control (16 rounds, 20 CHF initial endowment, 1 CHF cost to give 2 CHF, 1 CHF cost to punish for 2 CHF fine, punishment reputation score −5 to +5 based solely on punishment choices towards defectors). In disturbance condition, before each decision, experimenters utter superfluous phrases indicating that a choice is due, intended to distract and impair working memory. Only Receiver’s ID and punishment reputation shown to the Donor.",Summarized from study 2 description and general disturbance procedure.,1
10.1155_2014_108024.md,"Simulation – b=1.35, p=0 (no punishment), β=0.1",Condition implied by comparisons in Figures 2–3: baseline with p=0 for b=1.35 and β=0.1.,0.79,Punishment payoff p=0; temptation payoff b=1.35; noise parameter β=0.1; lattice size 50×50; initial all-D,Payoff matrix A given with parameters b and p; specific values described in Section 3 and Figure 2–3 narrative.,0.86,False,Study uses simulations on lattice networks with no human participants.,1,False,"Numerical simulation of evolutionary game dynamics, not a controlled human experiment.",1,False,No physical lab with human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Section 3 explicitly describes simulations on a 50×50 lattice and averaging over 100 runs.,1,False,Paper reports numerical simulations rather than closed-form analytical results.,1,2500,Lattice network size N=50×50 implies 2500 players.,1,400,Final time step T=400 stated; interpreted as number of iterations/rounds.,1,1,"Players choose between two pure strategies C or D; not a continuous contribution game; code as 1 (any strategy choice, not all-or-nothing contribution constraint in PGG sense).",0.4,N/A,Not a public goods game with contributions; PDG with fixed payoffs.,1,N/A,Marginal per capita return is specific to public goods games; here we have a 2×2 PDG.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface; agent strategies update based on payoff rules only.,1,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as a payoff parameter p, not as targeted actions with identities.",1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism beyond PDG payoffs.,1,N/A,Simulation agents are not informed; time horizon is an external parameter.,1,0,"Condition sets p=0 in payoff matrix A=[[1,0],[b,p]], making mutual defection payoff zero (baseline without punishment).",0.95,N/A,No explicit punishment action or cost parameter; only PDG payoff entries.,1,N/A,"No punishment technology specified, only fixed payoffs.",1,0,Model includes only standard PDG payoffs with a punishment parameter p; no separate rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit endowments or contributions; only game payoffs.,1,N/A,"Outcome is fraction of cooperators / structures, not contributions.",1,N/A,No continuous contribution decisions.,1,N/R,Paper does not report overall efficiency or normalized group payoff for this condition.,0.1,N/R,"Only fractions of strategy types are reported, no aggregate payoff statistics.",0.1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results stem from computational simulations on lattice networks.,1,"Spatial prisoner's dilemma on a 50×50 lattice with pairwise PDG payoffs A=[[1,0],[b,p]]. Strategies C and D update via a new imitation rule: if an individual's payoff is less than a neighbor's, it randomly imitates the strategy of a neighbor of that neighbor; stochastic update probability W[sx<-sy]=1/(1+exp((Ux-Uy)/β)). Initial state: all D.",Summarized from Sections 2–3 including the strategy rule and update probability.,1
