10.1111_j.0020-8833.2004.00306.x.md,Analytical comparison of probabilistic trigger vs. Contrite Tit-for-Tat (CTFT) designs in a noisy repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma,Paper only develops formal game-theoretic models (trigger and CTFT) and compares their treaty values analytically; there are no separate empirical conditions beyond these modeled designs.,1,"Dispute resolution design (probabilistic trigger vs. Contrite Tit-for-Tat with generosity), payoff parameters b and c, noise level ε, and discount factor ω","Models vary punishment scheme (trigger vs. CTFT) and parameters b, c, ε, ω to examine treaty value.",1,False,No human participants; the paper is purely theoretical and analytical.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only mathematical modeling and derivations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only formal analysis.,1,False,"Although numerical examples are given (tables of values), they are closed-form calculations, not agent-based or numerical simulations.",0.9,True,"The core of the paper is analytical game theory, deriving equilibria and value functions.",1,2,Stage game is explicitly defined as a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma.,1,0,"Game is an infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with discounting, not a finite number of rounds; encode as 0 per instructions when not a standard finite-round experiment.",0.9,0,"Players choose between two discrete actions (Cooperate C or Defect D), which is neither a continuous contribution choice nor an all-or-nothing contribution to a public good; set to 0 (no continuous choice).",0.7,N/A,"No human subjects, no endowment or public account structure; analytical Prisoner’s Dilemma.",1,N/R,No linear public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human interface or feedback; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,No players with identities in an experimental sense; punishment is modeled abstractly.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism revealing identity to players; theoretical payoffs only.,1,N/A,Infinite-horizon analytical model; no interface displaying remaining rounds.,1,1,"Core of the paper is punishment schemes (retaliation via defection, probabilistic triggers, CTFT redress) in the repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma.",1,N/R,"Punishment is embedded in payoff parameters (b, c) of the Prisoner’s Dilemma; there is no per-unit punishment technology specified as in tokens per point.",0.8,N/R,Paper does not define a numeric mapping from punishment expenditure to loss; punishment effects are encoded in PD payoffs.,0.8,0,No separate reward mechanism where players can spend to reward others; only cooperation/defection outcomes with redress encoded via CTFT structures.,0.8,N/A,No explicit costly reward action exists.,1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,Utilities are normalized payoffs of a Prisoner’s Dilemma; there is no explicit token endowment.,1,N/A,No human contributions or public good; only analytical payoffs.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions with magnitudes; only C or D choices.,1,N/R,"They compute a ‘treaty value’ V_i as long run expected value, not normalized efficiency vs. maximum payoff; no explicit efficiency metric defined.",0.8,N/R,"Only individual long-run values V_i and rates v_i are reported; no aggregate group payoff figures for a specific numeric game beyond illustrative values, and these are parameterized, not a single outcome measure.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is solely mathematical game-theoretic analysis, without empirical implementation.",1,"Infinite-horizon noisy repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with two players. Stage-game payoffs: mutual cooperation normalized to 0; unilateral defection yields 1 to defector and −b_i to cooperator; mutual defection yields −c_i to both, with b_i > c_i > 0 and b_i > 1. Noise parameter ε: with probability ε an intended cooperation is observed as defection. Players discount future with factor ω. Designs compared: (1) probabilistic trigger schemes with trigger probability q and return probability r, determining states CO (cooperation) and PN (punishment), and (2) Contrite Tit-for-Tat (CTFT) with generosity incorporating states of guilt/innocence (II, IG, GI, GG) and a probability q of being judged guilty upon unjustified defection. Long-run treaty value V_i is defined as the ergodic long-run expected discounted payoff and compared across designs via first-order decline rates in noise ε (v_i).",Summarizes the formal game description and main mechanisms as explicitly defined in the paper.,1
