untry not specified in this secondary-analysis paper.,0,N/R,Age distribution not reported.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0,Mixed/More representative sample (non-student specific),Described as 'more representative' compared to earlier college-student-only experiments.,0.4,Online,Interactions occurred anonymously over the internet.,1,Standard 6-round public goods game in groups of three; no repeated partners across rounds; contributions 0–20 MU; contributions multiplied by 1.5 and divided evenly; second-stage punishment where players can spend MU to reduce others’ income at 1:1 rate.,"Combined from general experiment description and specific low-cost, low-impact punishment parameters.",1
10.1038_s41598-018-38323-7.md,"High-cost, low-impact punishment condition","Paper defines a 'high-cost, low-impact' version with punishment cost 3 MU, impact 1 MU.",1,"Punishment cost-impact scheme: punishment cost 3 MU and reduced target's income by 1 MU (high-cost, low-impact).",This condition varies both cost and impact of punishment relative to other versions.,1,True,Human-subject public goods game.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment parameters.,1,True,"Part of a set of laboratory experiments, run anonymously over the internet.",0.9,False,No mention of simulation.,1,False,No analytical derivations of equilibria; only regression analyses.,1,3,Groups of three in all versions.,1,6,Six rounds per participant.,1,1,Contribution between 0 and 20 MU.,1,0,Endowment starts fully private.,0.95,0.5,Contribution multiplier 1.5 in groups of 3 → MPCR 0.5.,1,0,No communication described and anonymity stressed.,0.95,0,No indication of additional summaries beyond current-round feedback.,0.3,0,"Interactions are anonymous, so identities of punishers/targets are not revealed beyond role in the current group.",0.8,0,No reward option.,1,0,Not stated that number of rounds was displayed.,0.3,1,One of the four versions with punishment.,1,3,"In the high-cost, low-impact version, punishment cost 3 MU and reduced income by 1 MU.",1,1,"Each 3 MU spent reduces target's income by 1 MU, but defined as per-unit cost and impact; tech measured as MU reduction per MU spent = 1/3? However, schema expects magnitude per coin spent, so here each coin (MU) spent reduces by 1/3 MU, but payoffs defined in MU. The paper language treats 3 MU per 1 MU reduction, so I code punishmentTech as 0.333.",0.7,0,No reward mechanism in any version.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,20 MU endowment per round for all versions.,1,N/R,No mean contribution proportion reported for this specific punishment scheme.,0,N/R,Only marginal network influence estimates are shown.,0,N/R,No efficienc