dent specific),Same more-representative internet sample as other conditions.,0.4,Online,Anonymous interactions over the internet.,1,"6 rounds, groups of three, no repeated partners; contributions 0–20 MU, multiplied by 1.5 and divided evenly; second-stage punishment where each 3 MU spent reduces a chosen group member’s income by 3 MU (high-cost, high-impact).","Derived from general design and specific description of the high-cost, high-impact punishment scheme.",1
10.3389_fpsyg.2021.678237.md,Romano et al. (2021) continuous PD – aggregated across treatments within societies,"The paper uses Romano et al. (2021) PD data as one cooperative-behavior measure; design is identical across treatments and societies, and they aggregate over treatments and partners at the society level.",1,"Partner type (same-nation partner vs. outgroup-national partner vs. unidentified stranger), within-subject across 12 one-shot PD rounds; this paper uses each society’s average percentage of endowment given across all partner types.",Partner type is the only treatment factor described in the PD; authors explicitly say they average across treatments and all participants from a society.,0.9,True,"Uses behavioral decisions from more than 18,000 human participants in 42 societies.",1,True,"Continuous PD decisions in a controlled, pre-specified payoff structure; partner types experimentally manipulated.",1,False,"Study is described as an online experiment, not conducted in a physical laboratory.",1,False,All data from human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulation is analyzed here.,1,False,No formal closed-form or purely theoretical model is presented as the main method for this PD measure.,1,2,Standard PD with two agents; each participant was assigned a single partner in each game.,1,12,"“Each participant played the game 12 times, each time with a different partner.”",1,1,"Participants chose how many MUs (0–10) to give; contributions are continuous, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each participant was given 10 MUs as an endowment; all starts in their private account and they decide how much to give.,1,1,"Amount sent is doubled, then received by the partner. Group size is 2, so MPCR = 2/2 = 1.",0.9,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard anonymous PD design typically disallows it and none is described.,0.7,0,Description only notes knowing own endowment and multiplier; no feedback of others’ cumulative earnings or histories is mentioned.,0.6,0,"No punishment stage exists in this PD, so there are no punishment identities to reveal.",1,0,No reward mechanism is present in the described PD.,1,1,"Although not stated explicitly, online repeated-game experiments virtually always inform participants of the number of rounds (12); however the paper does not specify this.",0.4,0,Only simultaneous give/keep decisions with doubling are described; no sanctioning or punishment stage is mentioned.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,There is no separate costly rewarding or bonus mechanism; only the standard PD transfer and doubling.,1,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,10,"“In each game, both participants were given 10 Monetary Units (MUs).”",1,N/R,This paper reports society means as percentage of endowment given but does not list numeric values; they are taken from Romano et al. (2021) and not reproduced here.,0.2,N/R,Only percentages of the 10-MU endowment are mentioned; no raw MU means are given in this article.,0.2,N/R,No total or maximum possible payoffs are reported in this article; efficiency is not computed.,0.1,N/R,"The paper only uses average percentage given, not payoffs.",0.1,62 societies overall; PD behavior measure is available for 40 of these (country list not reproduced in this paper).,"The article states the Romano PD data cover 42 societies, of which behavior data are available for 40 in their merged dataset, but specific countries are not listed in the text.",0.6,N/R,Age distributions for Romano et al. participants are not reported in this secondary-analysis paper.,0.1,N/R,Gender breakdown is not given here.,0.1,N/R,No information on educational background is provided for Romano et al. (2021) participants in this article.,0.1,Online,"Described explicitly as “a recent online experiment with more than 18,000 participants in 42 societies.”",1,"One-shot continuous PD repeated 12 times with different partners; 10 MU per round; any amount 0–10 can be transferred and is doubled for the recipient; no punishment or reward; partner types (in-group, out-group, unidentified) vary across rounds; this paper analyzes per-society mean percentage given across all treatments.",Summarized from the PD method description in the Cooperative Behavior section.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2021.678237.md,ISMN cooperation scenario – cooperative norm (appropriateness of non-cooperation in CPR animation),"In the ISMN data, one key condition is the cooperative norm rating: appropriateness of Purple’s non-cooperative harvesting of the full common pool resource in the animation scenario.",1,Scenario: CPR animation where one agent (Purple) defects by harvesting all remaining resources after others took turns; participants rate appropriateness of Purple’s action.,Only one cooperation scenario is used for this norm measure; there is no experimental variation attached to this specific analysis in this paper.,1,True,"Norm ratings are collected from more than 22,000 human participants across 57 societies.",1,True,Participants respond to standardized animated scenarios and questions in a controlled survey protocol across societies.,1,False,"Data are collected as part of an international online survey (ISMN), not an in-lab session.",0.9,False,Animations are stimuli for human respondents; no agent-based simulation outcomes are analyzed.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical model constitutes this condition; it is survey-based empirical data.,1,4,"The CPR animation shows four triangle agents (Blue, Green, Pink, Purple).",1,1,"This is a single illustrated harvesting episode, not an iterated game with multiple discrete rounds.",0.9,1,"The norm scenario is an animation, not a decision task with numeric choices; contribution discreteness is not defined. Treat as allowing arbitrary harvest choices conceptually rather than a binary decision.",0.4,0,"No human endowments; resources initially all in the common pool before any agent harvests. For human participants, this is a rating task, not a contribution decision.",0.3,N/R,No explicit payoff or multiplier structure is described for the animation; only qualitative harvesting is shown.,0.1,0,Participants independently rate appropriateness; no peer communication in the survey context is mentioned.,0.9,1,"Participants see the entire sequence of all agents’ harvests in the animation, effectively summarizing others’ behavior before judging.",0.8,0,This norm condition has no punishment act; only Purple’s over-harvesting is shown.,1,0,No reward behavior is depicted; only harvesting actions.,1,0,The scenario does not frame behavior as a multi-round game with remaining rounds; just a one-off sequence.,0.9,0,"In this specific norm item, there is no response to Purple; only Purple’s own action is rated.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism is modeled.,1,0,No explicit reward actions occur in the animation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Resource quantities are shown visually as circles but not numerically specified; not used as a payoff parameter.,0.2,N/A,No human contributions are made; participants only rate appropriateness of a depicted action.,1,N/A,"Survey ratings, not costly contribution amounts, are collected.",1,N/R,No numerical payoffs in this scenario; only normative ratings.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs are not quantified in the animation.,0.1,57 societies (country list not reproduced here) with ISMN cooperation scenario data; this paper uses society-mean standardized ratings for 51 societies in the merged dataset.,The ISMN includes 57 societies; cooperative norm measures are available for 51 of the 62 societies used in this paper.,0.7,N/R,This article does not summarize age distributions from the ISMN.,0.1,N/R,Gender breakdowns are not provided in this article.,0.1,N/R,Education levels for ISMN respondents are not reported here.,0.1,Online,ISMN is described as a multi-society study using animations suitable for cross-cultural online data collection; it is not presented as an in-lab or field study.,0.9,"Participants view an abstract CPR animation with four agents sequentially harvesting from a common pool; the final agent (Purple) violates the cooperation norm by taking all remaining resources in one move. Participants rate the appropriateness of Purple’s action on a 0–5 scale, later standardized within persons; society means represent strength of the cooperative norm against non-cooperation.",Summarized from the Cooperative Norm and Metanorms description in the ISMN section.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2021.678237.md,ISMN cooperation scenario – metanorm against physical confrontation (appropriateness of Blue confronting Purple),This condition is the ISMN item where participants rate appropriateness of Blue’s physically confrontational response to Purple’s non-cooperation in the CPR animation.,1,Response type: physical confrontation by Blue against the non-cooperator Purple in the CPR animation; participants rate appropriateness of Blue’s action.,The independent factor at issue is the specific response to the violation (physical confrontation) in a fixed scenario.,1,True,Based on human survey responses in 57 societies.,1,True,Standardized scenario-based ratings under controlled survey conditions.,1,False,"Part of an international online survey project, not specified as lab-based.",0.9,False,No non-human simulation; animations serve as stimuli only.,1,False,Empirical rating data; no analytical-only treatment.,1,4,"Same CPR animation with four triangle agents (Blue, Green, Pink, Purple).",1,1,Single depicted episode: initial harvesting including Purple’s violation followed by Blue’s confrontation.,0.9,1,"Not a choice game for participants; Blue’s confrontation is a fixed depicted act, not a parameterized contribution.",0.4,0,"No human contributions; resources initially central, then allocated visually; human task is rating.",0.3,N/R,Payoffs are not quantified for this scenario.,0.1,0,Respondents do not interact; they just rate Blue’s action.,0.9,1,Participants observe both Purple’s non-cooperation and Blue’s reaction before rating.,0.9,1,Blue’s identity (color/position) is explicitly visible in the animation; viewers can see exactly which agent confronts Purple.,1,0,There is no rewarding behavior; only punishment-like confrontation.,1,0,No framing as multiround; scenario is a single sequence.,0.9,1,Blue’s physical confrontation is an informal punishment response to Purple’s norm violation.,1,N/R,The animation does not specify costs to Blue for confronting Purple.,0.1,N/R,No quantitative mapping from punishment effort to losses for Purple is defined.,0.1,0,No reward mechanism or positive response is modeled here.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Resource pool size and any personal endowments are not numerically specified.,0.1,N/A,"Outcome is a normative rating of punishment appropriateness, not cooperation decisions.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions are made.,1,N/R,No numeric efficiency measure for the group is defined in this metanorm scenario.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs are not reported or computable from the qualitative animation.,0.1,57 societies in ISMN; metanorm against physical confrontation in the cooperation scenario is available for 51 of the 62 societies used in this paper.,Text specifies 57 societies overall for ISMN and that metanorm measures are available for 51 of 62 societies in the merged dataset.,0.8,N/R,Age is not summarized in this article.,0.1,N/R,Gender distribution is not provided.,0.1,N/R,Educational background is not reported here.,0.1,Online,ISMN is an international survey using online-compatible animations; no lab or field description is given.,0.9,"After viewing Purple’s over-harvesting in the CPR animation, participants see Blue physically confront Purple, making Purple lurch backward. They rate appropriateness of Blue’s confrontation on a 0–5 scale (extremely inappropriate to extremely appropriate); ratings are standardized per respondent. Society-mean standardized ratings index strength of the metanorm against physical confrontation (higher inappropriateness implies stronger metanorm).",Summarized from the Metanorm against physical confrontation description.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2021.678237.md,ISMN cooperation scenario – metanorm in favor of gossip (appropriateness of gossiping about Purple),This condition uses the ISMN item where participants rate appropriateness of using gossip as a response to Purple’s non-cooperation in the CPR animation.,1,Response type: gossiping about the non-cooperative Purple (speaking to others) after the CPR norm violation; participants rate appropriateness.,The manipulated/queried factor is the specific response (gossip) to the same underlying violation scenario.,1,True,Human participants across many societies provide ratings.,1,True,Standardized vignettes/animations with pre-defined response options rated under controlled survey conditions.,1,False,"Administered within ISMN survey framework, likely online, not lab-based; not described as a lab experiment.",0.9,False,No non-human simulation is involved.,1,False,"Empirical survey data, not an analytical model.",1,4,"Same four-agent CPR animation (Blue, Green, Pink, Purple).",1,1,"Single violation and potential gossip response are contemplated, not an iterated interaction.",0.9,1,No participant contribution decisions; rating task only.,0.4,0,Resources start in common pool; human respondents have no endowment.,0.3,N/R,No payoff structure is numerically described.,0.1,0,Participants do not communicate with each other; they only give individual ratings.,0.9,1,Participants observe the norm violation and know that gossip is directed at Purple; they see the overall context before rating.,0.8,0,"Here, the potential response is gossip to unspecified others; no specific punisher identity is depicted in the simple rating question.",0.7,0,No rewards or rewarders are part of this metanorm item.,1,0,No reference to multiple rounds or game length.,0.9,1,Gossip about norm violators is conceptualized as a social sanctioning (metanorm) mechanism in the article.,1,N/R,Costs of gossip to the gossiper are not parameterized.,0.1,N/R,No explicit payoff effects of gossip on targets are quantified.,0.1,0,No formal rewarding of cooperators is considered here.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No numeric endowment for agents is specified.,0.1,N/A,"Outcome is normative approval of gossip, not cooperative contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions exist in this item.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not defined or measured for this metanorm.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff outcomes are reported.,0.1,57 societies in ISMN; gossip metanorm in cooperation scenario is available for 51 of the 62 societies in this paper’s merged dataset.,Same coverage as other cooperation metanorm measures.,0.8,N/R,Age is not described in this article.,0.1,N/R,Gender information is not provided.,0.1,N/R,Educational information is not reported.,0.1,Online,ISMN is described as using simple animations suitable for cross-cultural research; such work is typically online survey-based and not connected here to lab settings.,0.9,"Following the CPR norm violation by Purple (taking all remaining resources), participants assess the appropriateness of using gossip—talking to others about Purple’s behavior—as a response. Ratings on the 0–5 scale (standardized within respondents; reversed so high = more appropriate) are averaged per society to index the strength of metanorms in favor of gossip.",Derived from the description of the gossip metanorm measure in the ISMN section.,1
