10.1111_ecin.12974.md,Happiness treatment – Public good with punishment and incidental happiness videos,Paper describes two between-subjects treatments: happiness vs. anger; this row corresponds to the happiness treatment.,1,Induced incidental emotion: happiness (video clips after contribution stage each round),Only design difference between treatments is the valence of induced incidental emotion via videos (happiness vs. anger).,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 72 human subjects in this treatment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted at Sogang Experimental Economics Laboratory (SEE Lab).,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,Study is experimental rather than purely theoretical.,1,3,Groups of three subjects played the public good game with punishment.,1,5,Game was repeated five times with stranger matching.,1,1,"Participants chose ""how much to contribute"" from a 20-token endowment, implying any amount between 0 and 20.",1,0,Each subject was endowed with 20 tokens privately and then decided how much to contribute to the group account; all tokens start in private accounts.,1,0.6,Marginal per capita return for the group account was explicitly set at 0.6.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat; z-Tree implementation with only decisions and feedback.,0.9,1,"After contribution, subjects saw each member's token earnings, each contribution, and total group contributions; after punishment they saw punishment received and given and final earnings.",1,1,Feedback included punishment points received from each other member along with the corresponding member's temporary ID.,1,0,"No reward mechanism in design, so no reward identities shown.",1,1,"Subjects knew in advance that the game was repeated five times; standard to display round number in z-Tree when horizon is known, though not explicitly stated.",0.7,1,Second-stage costly punishment is central feature; subjects can assign punishment points.,1,1,Punishment cost was one token per punishment point.,1,3,One punishment point reduced the target’s token earnings by three tokens.,1,0,Design mentions only punishment; no reward or positive points available.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,20,Each subject was endowed with 20 tokens in each round.,1,N/R,Paper reports raw contributions (tokens) and trends but does not provide normalized contribution rates explicitly.,0.3,N/R,Reports various means by round and subgroup; no single overall treatment-level mean contribution amount is given in a directly usable form.,0.3,N/R,Welfare and efficiency are discussed qualitatively; no numeric efficiency ratio is provided.,0.2,N/R,Only individual average earnings in KRW are reported pooled; no separate treatment-level group payoffs in tokens.,0.2,South Korea,"Experiment conducted at Sogang University in Seoul, South Korea; subjects are standard lab participants there, likely Korean.",0.9,N/R,"Only described as subjects; no age statistics reported, though likely university students.",0.2,N/R,Paper does not report gender breakdown.,0.1,"University students (undergraduate/graduate, mixed majors)","Describes use of student subjects (e.g., references to department such as Economics, and separate pretest with graduate students); lab at a university.",0.7,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental laboratory (SEE Lab) using z-Tree; payments made in cash on site.,1,"One-shot public good with punishment repeated 5 times under stranger matching; group size 3; each round: contribution stage from 20-token endowment to a group account with MPCR 0.6, then 2-minute treatment-specific video (happiness clips), then punishment stage where each subject can assign up to 10 punishment points to each of the two others (cost 1 token per point, reduces target’s contribution-stage earnings by 3 tokens per point). Earnings are truncated at zero if deductions exceed contribution-stage earnings, plus 30 extra tokens added at the end. Matching pools of 6 subjects per session used to increase independent observations. Emotion ratings (1 very happy – 7 very angry) collected after all rounds.",Summarizes detailed procedural description and payoff equations specific to this treatment (emotion content of videos).,1
10.1111_ecin.12974.md,Anger treatment – Public good with punishment and incidental anger videos,Second treatment in the between-subjects design where incidental anger is induced.,1,Induced incidental emotion: anger (video clips after contribution stage each round),Design identical to happiness treatment except videos in each round induce anger instead of happiness.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 72 human subjects in this treatment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with specified treatments.,1,True,Performed at Sogang Experimental Economics Laboratory.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,Not a purely analytical/theoretical study.,1,3,Groups of three subjects played a PG game with punishment.,1,5,Game repeated five times with stranger matching.,1,1,Subjects choose any contribution level from 0 to 20 tokens from their endowment.,1,0,Endowment is entirely in private tokens at the start of each round.,1,0.6,Marginal per capita return for the group account explicitly set at 0.6.,1,0,Design does not include communication; only decisions via z-Tree.,0.9,1,"Feedback after contribution and punishment includes others' contributions, payoffs, punishment given/received with IDs.",1,1,Subjects observe punishment points received from each member along with temporary ID.,1,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Participants were informed the game would be repeated five times; likely displayed as round information.,0.7,1,Voluntary costly punishment stage after each video.,1,1,Punishment cost is one token per punishment point.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces target's contribution-stage earnings by three tokens.,1,0,Only punishment; no rewarding option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available.,1,20,Each subject endowed with 20 tokens per round.,1,N/R,"Paper provides raw contributions and trends, not normalized rates for the treatment as a whole.",0.3,N/R,Reports individual-round and subgroup means; no single summary mean per treatment easily extracted.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency and welfare differences mentioned qualitatively; no numeric efficiency metric reported.,0.2,N/R,Average earnings reported only pooled; no treatment-wise group payoff in tokens.,0.2,South Korea,"Experiment run at Sogang University in Seoul, South Korea.",0.9,N/R,No age summary statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,Gender distribution not provided.,0.1,"University students (undergraduate/graduate, mixed majors)","Conducted in a university lab using student subject pool; regressions reference department (Economics), implying university students as participants.",0.7,On site,Physical lab sessions at SEE Lab with in-person cash payment.,1,"Identical public good with punishment structure as happiness treatment: 3-person groups, 5 stranger-matched rounds within submatching pools of 6. Each round: 20-token endowment, contribute to group account (MPCR 0.6), then ~2-minute anger-inducing video watched by all 3 group members, then punishment stage where each subject can assign up to 10 punishment points to each of two others (cost 1 token per point, reduces target’s earnings by 3 tokens per point). Earnings truncated at zero if deductions exceed contribution-stage earnings; 30 extra tokens added after all rounds; final cash earnings = 150 KRW × (30 + sum of final token payoffs). Retrospective emotion ratings (1 very happy – 7 very angry) completed after all 5 rounds.","Summarizes design and payoff structure, highlighting treatment-specific incidental anger induction.",1
10.1007_s10614-020-10017-1.md,Baseline model – standard simulation scenario,Authors label this as the 'baseline model' and use the 'standard simulation scenario' parameters from Table 1.,1,"Punishment regime: no punishment (s=0, γ=0)",Section 3.2 defines the baseline model as s=0 and γ=0; compared against other punishment regimes.,1,False,Model is explicitly described as an agent-based simulation with autonomous agents and no human participants.,1,False,"This is a computational simulation study, not a controlled human-subject experiment.",1,False,No physical or online lab with human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,True,"Described repeatedly as an agent-based model implemented in NetLogo with 10,000 simulations of 20,000 rounds.",1,False,"While analytical properties are discussed, the reported results here are from simulations rather than closed-form solutions.",0.9,4,Standard simulation scenario in Table 1 sets group size to 4; also reiterated in model description.,1,20000,"Table 1 and Section 4: each simulation lasts 20,000 rounds.",1,1,"Contribution space S={0,k,2k,...,W} allows any multiple of k up to W, i.e., not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human subjects or default allocation; contributions are chosen by simulated agents from their endowment.,1,0.5,Section 3.2 sets α (MPCR) to 2/4 = 0.5 for the standard scenario.,1,N/A,No communication among agents is modelled; also there are no human subjects.,1,N/R,Not applicable to agents in the usual UI sense and not specified as an information treatment.,0.2,N/A,"Baseline model has no punishment system (s=0, γ=0).",1,N/R,No peer-to-peer rewards or identity features described in the simulation.,0.2,N/R,No human interface; not specified whether agents 'know' remaining rounds in any sense.,0.1,0,"Baseline model explicitly defined as having no voting or punishment (s=0, γ=0).",1,N/A,No punishment in baseline model.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,Only standard TPGG payoff structure; no additional reward mechanism beyond public good returns.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,30,"Section 3.2: W is initial endowment of each agent, with W=30 for standard scenario.",1,N/R,Paper reports mean raw contributions but not normalised rates (0–1) explicitly.,0.2,15.51,Table 3: 'Contribution mean' in provision cases for baseline model is 15.51.,1,N/R,They report mean rewards and provision rates but not efficiency as ratio to theoretical maximum.,0.2,N/R,"Only per-agent mean rewards are provided, not total group payoffs.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; all agents are artificial.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations in NetLogo with no human subjects.,1,"Threshold public goods game without punishment; 4 agents each have endowment 30, choose contribution in {0,k,2k,...,30}; public good provided if total contributions ≥ 60; returns W - c^i + α * sum_j c^j when threshold met, else W - c^i.","Summarised from Sections 3.1, 3.2 and Table 1.",1
10.1007_s10614-020-10017-1.md,"Cooperation model – standard simulation scenario (full punishment, s=1)","Section 3.2 describes a 'cooperation model' as the special case with s=1; Table 3 reports this as 'Cooperation model, s=1'.",1,"Punishment regime: cooperative full punishment (s=1, γ=10)",Cooperation model has no voting; punishment is always applied (s=1) and γ=10 per Table 1 and Section 3.2.,1,False,"Agent-based simulation with artificial agents, not humans.",1,False,No human experimental protocol; purely computational.,1,False,Not conducted with human subjects in a lab.,1,True,Described as an agent-based model using NetLogo with many simulated runs.,1,False,Reported outcomes are from simulations rather than closed-form analysis.,0.9,4,Standard simulation scenario for all three model variants uses group size 4 (Table 1).,1,20000,"Each simulation lasts 20,000 rounds (Table 1, Section 4).",1,1,"Same contribution space S={0,k,2k,...,W} as baseline; no restriction to all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human players or default allocation; agents choose contributions from their endowment.,1,0.5,α=2/4=0.5 in Section 3.2 and Table 1.,1,N/A,No communication among agents and no humans.,1,N/R,Information structure for agents not specified at this UI-like level.,0.2,0,"Punishment is implemented as a mechanism on contributions, not as identified peer-to-peer punishment; no identities are referenced.",0.6,N/R,No separate reward or identity structure described.,0.2,N/R,Not specified whether agents are modelled as knowing horizon; no human interface.,0.1,1,"Cooperation model uses cooperative punishment applied whenever contributions below threshold, with γ>0.",1,10,"Section 3.2: γ represents unit cost of the punishment system per agent when s=1, with γ=10 in standard scenario.",1,1,"From payoff formula and explanation: free/cheap riders pay s*(T/n - c^i); with s=1 this is exactly the shortfall T/n - c^i; cost to group is nγ; thus one can interpret 1 token of shortfall leading to 1 token fine for deviator, so tech set to 1 as best approximation.",0.5,0,No explicit reward mechanism aside from standard public good returns.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,30,"W=30 in the standard simulation scenario (Section 3.2, Table 1).",1,N/R,"Only raw contribution means are reported, not normalised as a fraction of endowment.",0.2,15.63,Table 3: 'Contribution mean' for the cooperation model in provision cases is 15.63.,1,N/R,They provide expected mean rewards and provision probabilities but not efficiency as ratio to max possible payoff.,0.2,N/R,Only per-agent payoffs are given; group total not explicitly reported.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model only.,1,Threshold public goods game with cooperative punishment: PG provided if total contributions ≥ 60; payoffs follow baseline TPGG plus punishment system where each period all 4 agents pay investigation cost γ=10 (when s=1) and any agent contributing below T/n=15 pays additional punishment equal to T/n - c^i.,"Summarised from Section 3.2, including the payoff specification and explanation that the cooperation model sets s=1 and γ>0.",1
10.1007_s10614-020-10017-1.md,Endogenous shared punishment model – standard simulation scenario (no activists),"Main new model in Section 3.2; Table 3 lists it as 'Endogenous shared punishment model' with s∈{0,1/4,2/4,3/4,1}.",1,"Punishment regime: endogenous shared punishment with voting (s ∈ {0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1}, γ=10), no activists","Section 3.2 defines s as share of pro-punishment votes in {0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1} with γ=10; Section 7 distinguishes later variants with activists.",1,False,Study uses artificial agents in an ABM; no human participants.,1,False,"Controlled variation is in a simulation, not a human experiment.",1,False,No lab or field with humans.,1,True,"Model implemented and explored via NetLogo simulations (10,000 runs, 20,000 rounds each).",1,False,"Although some game-theoretic properties are discussed, main results reported are from simulations.",0.9,4,"Standard simulation scenario sets group size to 4, used for all three main models.",1,20000,"Simulations for this model also use 20,000 rounds (Section 4, Table 1).",1,1,"Contribution choice set remains S={0,k,2k,...,W}, same as baseline model.",1,N/A,No human subjects; agents decide contribution from full private endowment.,1,0.5,"α=0.5 in the standard scenario (Section 3.2, Table 1).",1,N/A,No explicit communication channel; no human UI.,1,N/R,Information available to agents besides own payoff is not described at this level of detail.,0.2,0,Punishment is automatic and based on contributions; no individual identifiers or targeted peer punishment are mentioned.,0.6,N/R,No separate reward stage with identities described.,0.2,N/R,Horizon awareness of agents is not specified.,0.1,1,Central feature is an endogenous shared punishment system determined by voting (s).,1,10,γ=10 per agent under full pro-punishment voting (s=1) in the standard scenario.,1,1,"Punishment fine to a free/cheap rider is s*(T/n - c^i); cost per agent is sγ. Under s=1, one unit of under-contribution yields 1 unit of fine; thus tech approximated as 1.",0.5,0,No additional reward treatment; only public good returns and lower punishment when above threshold.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,30,Same W=30 as in standard scenario (Table 1).,1,N/R,They present contribution means and distributions but not explicitly normalised rates.,0.2,15.56,Table 3: 'Contribution mean' in provision cases for endogenous shared punishment model is 15.56.,1,N/R,Efficiency measure as share of maximum possible payoff is not directly reported.,0.2,N/R,Only mean per-agent payoffs and weighted means are provided.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Model is an agent-based simulation without human participation.,1,"Threshold public goods game with endogenous shared punishment: four agents, each with endowment 30, contribute in steps to a public good with threshold T=60 and MPCR α=0.5. Each period, agents choose a contribution and vote for/against punishment. The share s of pro-punishment votes (0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1) determines both the per-agent investigation cost sγ (γ=10) and the fine on under-contributors, s(T/n - c^i). Payoffs differ depending on whether total contributions meet threshold and whether individual contributions are above or below T/n=15, as given in the four-case payoff function in Section 3.2.",Condensed from the payoff definition and description of voting and punishment in Section 3.2.,1
10.1007_s10614-020-10017-1.md,"Endogenous shared punishment with activists – 1 activist, 3 regular players",Section 7 and Table 4 describe a variant of the endogenous shared punishment model with ratio A:RP = 1:3.,1,"Player composition: 1 activist and 3 regular players under endogenous shared punishment (s endogenous, γ=10)","Section 7: introduces activists and reports results separately for ratios 1:3, 2:2, 3:1.",1,False,Still purely agent-based simulations; activists are a type of artificial agent.,1,False,No human-subject experimental protocol.,1,False,Not a human lab or field study.,1,True,"Described as a simulation experiment with 5000 simulations of 20,000 rounds.",1,False,"Outcomes reported from simulation runs, not only theory.",0.9,4,Ratio 1 activist : 3 regular implies total of 4 players; group size from standard scenario is 4.,1,20000,"Section 7: 'A total of 5000 simulations, each lasting 20,000 simulation rounds'.",1,1,"Same underlying TPGG with contribution space S={0,k,2k,...,W}; activists are restricted from low contributions but choice set structure unchanged.",0.9,N/A,No human players; contributions are chosen by agents from their private endowment.,1,0.5,Uses the same standard simulation parameters (α=0.5) unless otherwise stated; none given for activist runs.,0.9,N/A,No communication among agents; simulation only.,1,N/R,Level of informational feedback to agents not specified beyond own payoff and group outcome.,0.2,0,"Punishment associated with contribution level relative to threshold, not with identifiable counterparts.",0.6,N/R,No explicit identity-linked reward mechanism described.,0.2,N/R,Horizon knowledge of agents not detailed.,0.1,1,Still the endogenous shared punishment framework with voting on punishment.,1,10,Punishment system cost parameter γ remains 10 as in standard scenario; activists only change contribution bounds.,0.9,1,Punishment formula unchanged from endogenous shared punishment model.,0.5,0,No new reward mechanism is introduced with activists.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,30,Endowment parameter W not changed in activist section; default remains 30.,0.9,N/R,Only raw contribution averages (overall and by type) are reported.,0.2,15.61,Table 4: 'Contribution mean' in provision cases for 1:3 treatment is 15.61.,1,N/R,Efficiency metric not given as fraction of maximum payoff.,0.2,N/R,"Table 4 reports mean rewards per agent, not total group payoffs.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation experiment with artificial agents including activists.,1,"Same endogenous shared punishment TPGG as in the main model (4 agents, threshold 60, MPCR 0.5, voting-based punishment with cost γ=10), but one agent is an activist whose contribution strategy space is restricted to contributions at or above the per-capita provision threshold (15) and excess contributions; activists cannot cheap- or free-ride. Regular players retain full contribution strategy set. Roth–Erev learning governs both contribution and punishment votes.",Section 7 describes activists as players who 'neglect cheap/free riding' and are not allowed to ride cheaply/freely; structure of game otherwise identical to endogenous shared punishment.,1
