10.1086_709105.md,Field dataset – all group-lending transactions 2006–2011,"Single quantitative field dataset of 741 loans in 406 groups (1,884 borrowers) with 5,487 repayment transactions; no separate experimental conditions or treatment arms are defined.",1,"Observed structural features of borrowing groups: (a) structural cohesion (continuous proxy from Euclidean minimum spanning tree of residential GPS coordinates), including a squared term; (b) existence of disconnected subgroups (binary); and (c) interaction between cohesion and disconnected subgroups. Additional observed controls: group composition, financial characteristics, loan characteristics, loan officer identifiers.",Paper specifies these as the main explanatory variables in hierarchical models; they are not manipulated but naturally varying in an observational field study.,1,True,"Uses real repayment records and survey/GPS data from 1,884 human borrowers in Sierra Leone.",1,False,No random assignment or controlled intervention; authors explicitly describe it as field/observational analysis of an existing microfinance program.,1,False,"Data collected from real-world microfinance operations, not from a lab setting.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported; quantitative part is purely statistical analysis of field data.,1,False,"Although a theoretical framework is developed, the reported empirical section here is not a formal analytical model; it is data-driven statistical analysis.",1,4.58,"Mean group size reported in Table 2: ‘Group size 4.58 (SD 0.49, min 4, max 5)’.",1,6,"Loans scheduled for monthly repayment over 6–12 months; since exact distribution per loan is not given, the minimum number of scheduled payments is 6. Exact per-loan rounds are not fully specified.",0.3,1,"Borrowers can make partial payments of arbitrary size (some as low as 5% of the scheduled amount), indicating continuous contribution choices rather than all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment (loan principal) is given directly to each borrower’s own business; nothing is automatically placed in the joint repayment fund beyond a separate mandated 10% deposit account. Standard PGG default of private control applies.,0.6,N/R,No marginal per capita return is defined in game-theoretic terms; only loan amounts and repayment obligations are described.,0,1,"Borrowers interact freely in markets, neighborhoods, religious organizations, and in group meetings; enforcement relies on ‘pressuring’, ‘cajoling’, and discussions, implying unrestricted communication.",0.9,1,"Group members know others’ repayment behavior and can observe who is late or defaulting; enforcement is based on awareness of others’ performance, although no formal numeric summary screen exists.",0.8,1,"Punishments (social pressure, shaming, ostracism, going to elders/police) are applied face-to-face and target identifiable group members; anonymity is never mentioned.",1,1,"Positive reinforcement (‘encouraging’, ‘supporting’) is interpersonal and necessarily identifies recipients; no anonymous rewards are described.",0.8,1,Loan contracts specify the full repayment schedule (6–12 monthly payments) ex ante; borrowers know the number and timing of rounds.,0.9,1,"Extensive qualitative evidence of informal sanctions: social pressure, public shaming, involvement of elders/police, exclusion from group, and ostracism from markets or networks.",1,N/R,"Costs are time, effort, damaged relationships, possible counter-punishment; no numeric token/monetary per-unit cost is specified.",0,N/R,No formal mapping from ‘units’ of punishment to reductions in a target’s payoff is quantified; only qualitative descriptions exist.,0,1,"Interviewees mention ‘encouraging’ and ‘supporting’ compliant members; these are informal social rewards, not monetary, but nonetheless positive sanctions.",0.7,N/R,"Social rewards (encouragement) have undefined, non-monetary costs; no numeric per-unit cost is given.",0,N/R,No quantitative effect size of rewards on recipients’ payoffs is reported.,0,300000,"Each borrower receives between 300,000 and 1.5 million SLL; 300,000 SLL is the minimum individual loan amount explicitly reported. Exact per-loan distribution not given.",0.4,N/R,"Outcomes are measured as days early/late or default, not as proportion of income or loan repaid in normalized 0–1 terms.",0,N/R,"While scheduled and partial payment amounts exist in principle, the paper only reports days early/late and does not tabulate raw contribution magnitudes.",0,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff is computed or reported.,0,N/R,"Analysis focuses on repayment timing (days overdue, default hazard) and not on total group earnings or payoffs.",0,Sierra Leone,"Study context is ‘group lending in Sierra Leone’; all borrowers are clients of a microfinance institution headquartered there, with GPS data from a single Sierra Leonean city.",1,N/R,Age is never reported or summarized in the paper.,0,84% female; 16% male,"They state ‘In this data set, 84% of the borrowers are female.’ Remaining share is implicitly male.",0.9,N/R,Authors note educational attainment data are unreliable and are not used; no distribution or summary is provided.,0,Field experiment,"Data are from real-world microfinance operations with naturalistic variation; this best fits ‘Field experiment’ vs ‘Observational’, given that it is an in-the-field behavioral dataset. However, no randomization is present, so it is essentially an observational field study.",0.5,"Joint-liability group lending in a Sierra Leonean microfinance institution. Groups of about 4–5 borrowers self-select under instructions to choose members they ‘know and trust’. Direct kin are not allowed in the same group. Each member must operate an individual microbusiness and receives an equal share of a group loan, typically 300,000–1,500,000 SLL per borrower, with total group loans of 1.2–7.5 million SLL. Loans are scheduled for monthly repayment over 6–12 months. If any member does not repay, the entire group is liable and can lose access to future credit. 10% of each loan is placed immediately into a group deposit account intended as a buffer, but this is rarely used to cover arrears. Default (non-full repayment) occurs in 6.2% of loans. Enforcement is entirely informal, via social pressure, public shaming, involvement of elders or police (mainly for embarrassment), expulsion from the borrowing group, exclusion from market spaces, and selective ostracism from social networks. Structural cohesion and disconnected subgroups are proxied with Euclidean minimum spanning trees over high-resolution residential GPS data; social affiliation surveys (religious organizations, markets, schools, savings groups, tribe) validate spatial measures as proxies for social embeddedness.","Synthesized factual description from Data and Methods, Statistical Analysis, and Ethnographic Analysis sections, focusing on strategic structure relevant to cooperation and punishment.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0202878.md,Treatment B (Base),Paper defines four treatments; Treatment B is the baseline ROSCA without voting or punishment.,1,Institutional rules: no voting; no punishment (forfeiture) rule.,Independent variables are institutional mechanisms varied across treatments: presence/absence of voting and punishment.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with student subjects.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted at Tokyo Institute of Technology laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,"This treatment uses human subjects, not just simulations.",1,False,No purely analytical model; this is an empirical treatment.,1,4,Groups of four members in all treatments.,1,10,Each session consisted of ten rounds.,1,0,"In each period, subjects choose either GIVE 100 or NOT GIVE 0; no intermediate amounts.",1,0,"Each period, 100 points are given to each subject as private endowment; zero is pre-assigned to fund.",1,N/A,"This is a ROSCA/rotating indivisible good, not a standard linear public goods game with MPCR.",1,0,No communication between subjects is described; only information screens.,0.9,1,"After each payout, all members were informed which members had contributed and who received payouts; table in Fig 1 shows this history.",1,0,There is no punishment mechanism in Treatment B.,1,0,There is no explicit reward mechanism distinct from normal payouts.,1,1,Subjects were informed up-front that they would play ten rounds and four periods per round.,1,0,Treatment B uses neither voting nor punishment rule; no formal punishment institution.,1,N/A,No formal punishment option.,1,N/A,No formal punishment option.,1,0,Only standard ROSCA payouts; no separate costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,100,"Each period, experimenter gave 100 points to all group members.",1,0.44,Table 3 reports average β0 (before payout) in Treatment B as 0.44 over 10 rounds.,1,44,"Endowment is 100; 0.44 * 100 = 44 average contributed before payout. After payout not separable in single scalar, so use β0-based amount.",0.8,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency as ratio of realized to maximum possible payoff.,0,N/R,Only individual mean payoffs are given; group-level totals by treatment not reported.,0.3,Japan,Subjects recruited from Tokyo Institute of Technology in Japan.,1,N/R,Described only as undergraduate and graduate students; no age statistics.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,Mainly undergraduate students; some graduate students,Paper: 'Subjects consisted mainly of undergraduate students' and also says undergraduate and graduate students.,0.9,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory with subjects at lab computers using z-Tree.,1,"Four-person ROSCA with four periods per round; fixed order of payout randomly determined at start of each round; discount factor δ=0.3; recipient’s payoff: π = 100(m_C / (1+δ)^{n-4} + 1); groups randomly rematched each round; 20 subjects per session, 5 groups.",Summarizes structural details of the ROSCA game and matching protocol.,1
