10.1111_jlca.12283.md,Ethnographic observational study of collective action and support networks in Pucucanchita,Single field study described: collective action participation and three social support networks measured in one Quechua community.,0.95,Household-level variation in collective action investment (total hours over 2 years),Paper analyzes how differing household contributions (hours) to collective action relate to support network size and structure.,0.9,True,Study is based on ethnographic fieldwork with human participants in a Peruvian village.,1,False,No controlled manipulation or randomized assignment; naturalistic observation and interviews.,1,False,"Research occurred in a rural village, not in a laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations reported for this field site; only references to others' simulations.,1,False,No formal mathematical model is developed; theoretical discussion only.,1,24,Community described as having 24 households; Figure 2 uses N=24 households as units of analysis.,0.9,0,No discrete game rounds; ongoing real-world collective action and support events.,1,1,Contributions are measured in continuous hours; no indication of binary/all-or-nothing choices.,0.8,N/A,No experimental endowment or contribution decision; ethnographic observation only.,1,N/R,No formal public-goods payoff structure or marginal per capita return is specified.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or game; question of in-game communication not applicable.,1,N/A,No controlled information display; real-world knowledge and gossip instead.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism is implemented by a researcher.,1,N/A,No explicit experimental reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"No finite, known number of game rounds.",1,0,Author explicitly states that institutional punishment of undercontributors is nonexistent; only informal exclusion is discussed.,1,N/A,No formal punishment mechanism with specified costs.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology or multiplier.,1,0,No separate experimental reward mechanism; benefits arise from real-world reciprocity and reputation.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism with token costs.,1,N/A,No defined reward multiplier.,1,N/R,No experimental monetary or token endowment; households have real herds and crops instead.,1,N/R,"Contributions are reported in hours, not normalized as a fraction of a maximum; no such rate is provided.",1,102,Average time invested per household in collective action is 102 hours across 22 events (reported in text).,0.9,N/R,No calculation of collective payoff relative to a theoretical maximum is provided.,1,N/R,"No explicit aggregate payoff (e.g., total meat or money value) is quantified.",1,Peru,Field site is a Quechua community in the altiplano of southern Peru.,1,N/R,No age ranges or means reported for adult participants/household heads.,1,N/R,"Text notes both men and women participate, but no composition or proportions are given.",0.6,N/R,No educational attainment information reported.,1,Field experiment,"On-site empirical fieldwork in a natural community setting; not a lab or purely observational database, but also no intervention. Among provided categories, 'Field experiment' best matches an on-site field study with human subjects.",0.7,"Ethnographic, non-experimental study of collective action and reciprocal support networks (agricultural aid, health advice, food sharing) in a 24-household Quechua community; household collective action investment (hours across 22 events) is correlated with normalized indegree centrality in combined support networks (Pearson r=0.672, one-tailed p<.001, N=24). No formal game, tokens, or controlled punishment/reward mechanisms.",Summarizes key cooperation-relevant structural details and the main quantitative relationship reported.,0.95
10.1007_s11846-016-0209-9.md,"JO→JO (control, no additional information)",Paper defines JO→JO as control condition where superior only observes joint output x and cannot buy additional information (Table 1; Sect. 4.1).,1,Information condition: JO→JO (superior observes only joint output; no option to buy additional information).,Information condition is the manipulated treatment; this row captures the JO→JO condition as described in Table 1 and Sect. 4.1.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 144 student participants (Sect. 4.1).,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized assignment and manipulated information conditions (Sect. 4.1).,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory with computer network at University of Osnabrück (Sect. 4.1).,1,False,All data from human subjects; no agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,"Although theory is developed, main study is an experiment, not purely analytical.",1,3,Groups of three: one superior and two subordinates (Sect. 4.1).,1,16,Experiment lasted 16 rounds (Sect. 4.1).,1,1,"Subordinates choose integer effort from 0–10, i.e., a continuous range, not binary all-or-nothing (Sect. 4.1).",1,0,No public-good style endowment allocation; each subordinate has own payoff function with fixed salary and cost of effort. Default fraction in any shared pool is effectively zero.,0.5,N/A,Not a public goods game with linear return multiplier; team output directly forms bonus pool.,1,0,"No communication or chat between participants mentioned; they interacted anonymously via computer (Sect. 4.1, 4.2).",0.9,1,"At end of each round, subordinates receive all information the superior had for her allocation decision plus own output and payoff (Sect. 4.2), which summarizes relevant outcomes.",0.8,1,"Superior allocates identifiable amounts to each named subordinate within the triad; each subordinate learns her own allocation, implying identity of who punished/rewarded whom within group.",0.7,1,"Same as punishment: rewards are allocations from the superior, whose role is known within the fixed triad.",0.7,1,Design uses fixed 16 rounds and cycles of 4 rounds; standard practice and instructions/quiz strongly imply subjects were informed about total rounds and cycle length.,0.7,1,"Superior can allocate less than a subordinate’s contribution, effectively punishing via reduced share of joint output (norm enforcement frame throughout the paper).",0.9,0,"Superior’s wealth is unaffected by allocation; only pays for information, not for severity of punishment/reward (Sect. 2, 4.1). Thus marginal cost per unit of punishment is zero.",0.9,1,One point less allocated to a subordinate is one point less in their payoff; linear 1:1 mapping between reduced allocation and payoff loss.,0.9,1,"Superior can allocate more than a subordinate’s contribution, effectively rewarding high effort (Sect. 3.2, 5.2).",0.9,0,Superior’s payoff independent of allocation; changing allocation composition has no cost to superior (only info cost).,0.9,1,Each extra unit allocated to a subordinate increases that subordinate’s payoff by 1 point.,0.9,7,Subordinate payoff per round includes a fixed salary of 7 points before effort cost and allocation (Sect. 4.1). This is the guaranteed per-round endowment.,1,N/R,They report average effort levels (0–10) but not normalized as a fraction 0–1.,0.7,3.48,"Table 2: in JO→JO condition, mean subordinate effort is 3.48 (average over both subordinates per group). Effort is the costly contribution choice.",1,N/R,No reported ratio of realized to maximum possible joint payoffs.,1,N/R,"They report only efforts, information values, and some pay ranges, not average group payoff by condition.",0.7,Germany,"Participants were students from the University of Osnabrück, Germany (author affiliation; Sect. 4.1 and address footnote).",0.9,N/R,Only described as students; no age statistics reported.,1,48% female,Sect. 4.1: 48% of the 144 participants were female.,1,University students; 39% major in economics or business,Sect. 4.1 gives composition of majors; all are students at the university.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions with participants on computers at the university (Sect. 4.1).,1,"Single-period team production with two subordinates choosing integer efforts 0–10 incurring cost c(e)=0.7e. Two random shocks (0–10) added to efforts form contributions x_i=e_i+θ_i and joint output x=x1+x2. Superior observes only joint output, cannot buy additional information, and allocates the entire bonus pool x between the two subordinates. Subordinate payoff per round: 7 + allocation − 0.7e; superior gets fixed 25, unaffected by allocation.",Summary from Sect. 2 and 4.1 restricted to aspects not captured in other config fields.,1
