10.1007_s11846-016-0209-9.md,JO→FI (additional full information from joint output),"Table 1 defines JO→FI where superior can acquire full information FI={e1,e2,θ1,θ2}.",1,"Information condition: JO→FI (superior starts with joint output; can buy FI={e1,e2,θ1,θ2}, i.e., individual efforts and individual shocks, plus implied contributions).",Defined as JO→FI in Sect. 2 and Table 1.,1,True,Same human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of full information.,1,True,Conducted in university lab.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Empirical lab data.,1,3,Triads.,1,16,Common design.,1,1,Effort 0–10 integer.,1,0,No shared endowment pool.,0.5,N/A,Team output equals sum of contributions; not a standard MPCR.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,1,Subordinates see all information held by superior for that round (Sect. 4.2).,0.8,1,Roles and allocations are individually known in triad.,0.7,1,Same identity structure.,0.7,1,Fixed 16-round design communicated in instructions and quiz.,0.7,1,Superior uses full information to punish low effort (allocations below contributions).,0.9,0,Punishment only changes subordinate payoffs; superiors only pay for info.,0.9,1,One unit change in allocation changes payoff by one.,0.9,1,Superior rewards high effort by allocating more than contribution.,0.9,0,No marginal cost to superior for reward.,0.9,1,Linear mapping allocation→payoff.,0.9,7,Fixed salary 7.,1,N/R,Only raw efforts reported.,0.7,6.81,Table 2: mean subordinate effort in JO→FI is 6.81.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure.,1,N/R,Not reported by condition.,0.7,Germany,Same subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Not provided.,1,48% female,Overall composition.,1,University students; 39% major in economics or business,Same participants.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment.,1,"Same team production and payoffs. In JO→FI, superior uses BDM (WTP 0.0–5.0) to decide on purchasing FI={e1,e2,θ1,θ2}. If bought, she sees individual efforts and individual shocks (and thus x1,x2) before allocating x. Empirical analysis shows allocations highly sensitive to differences in efforts and used to reward high and punish low effort (Sect. 5.2, Table 4).","Additional details specific to full-information condition from Sect. 2, 4.1, 5.2.",1
10.1007_s11846-016-0209-9.md,IC→FI (additional full information given individual contributions),Table 1: IC→FI where superior already knows joint output and individual contributions and can buy full information.,1,"Information condition: IC→FI (superior initially has {x,x1,x2} and can buy FI={e1,e2,θ1,θ2}).",Defined in Table 1 and Sect. 2 as IC→FI.,1,True,Same lab experiment.,1,True,Condition is part of manipulated design.,1,True,Conducted in on-site lab.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Focus on experimental evidence.,1,3,Same triad structure.,1,16,Constant design.,1,1,Effort choice is not binary.,1,0,No initial allocation to a common pool.,0.5,N/A,No MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication allowed/mentioned.,0.9,1,"Subordinates receive the information the superior had, including any purchased FI.",0.8,1,Allocations are individualized in known triads.,0.7,1,Same as for punishment.,0.7,1,Fixed round structure; instructions likely informed participants.,0.7,1,"Superior can punish norm violations via allocation, enhanced by full information.",0.9,0,Only info cost; allocation shares are costless for superior.,0.9,1,Allocation changes map 1:1 into payoff changes.,0.9,1,Superior rewards high effort when full info acquired.,0.9,0,Reward has no personal cost beyond any info acquired.,0.9,1,Linear technology.,0.9,7,Subordinate fixed salary.,1,N/R,Only raw efforts reported.,0.7,7.59,Table 2: subordinate effort mean in IC→FI is 7.59.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,No condition-level group payoff data.,0.7,Germany,Same subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Not given.,1,48% female,Overall sample stats apply.,1,University students; 39% major in economics or business,Same participants.,1,On site,Lab study.,1,"In IC→FI, superior already has x,x1,x2 before BDM. She then states WTP (0.0–5.0) to acquire FI={e1,e2,θ1,θ2}. Positive mean WTP (1.96) shows superiors value extra effort/shock separation even when they know contributions (Table 2, 3), consistent with norm enforcement.","Details specific to IC→FI drawn from Sect. 2, 4.1, 5.2, Table 2-3.",1
10.1007_s11846-016-0209-9.md,IE→FI (additional full information given individual efforts),"Table 1: IE→FI where superior initially knows x,e1,e2,θ and can buy θ1,θ2.",1,"Information condition: IE→FI (superior initially has {x,e1,e2,θ} and can buy FI by learning individual shocks θ1,θ2).",Defined in Table 1 and Sect. 2 as IE→FI.,1,True,Part of same human-subject experiment.,1,True,Condition is experimentally manipulated.,1,True,Conducted in university laboratory.,1,False,No simulation component.,1,False,Focus on empirical data.,1,3,Same triad composition.,1,16,Experiment length.,1,1,Effort 0–10.,1,0,No public endowment.,0.5,N/A,No marginal per capita return parameter defined.,1,0,No chat/communication reported.,0.9,1,Subordinates see whatever info superior had for that round.,0.8,1,Same identity structure as other conditions.,0.7,1,Same for rewards.,0.7,1,Fixed number of rounds and cycles.,0.7,1,Superior can use full information to adjust allocations punitively.,0.9,0,Only info costs; allocation is budget-neutral to superior.,0.9,1,Unit changes in allocation directly change subordinate payoff.,0.9,1,Superior can reward high effort via allocation.,0.9,0,No marginal reward cost to superior.,0.9,1,Linear technology.,0.9,7,Fixed salary of 7 per subordinate per round.,1,N/R,Reported as raw effort levels only.,0.7,8.61,Table 2: mean effort in IE→FI condition is 8.61.,1,N/R,No reported efficiency measure.,1,N/R,Not broken out by condition.,0.7,Germany,Participants from University of Osnabrück.,0.9,N/R,Age distribution not provided.,1,48% female,Overall sample statistic.,1,University students; 39% major in economics or business,Same subject pool.,1,On site,Lab sessions.,1,"In IE→FI, superior already knows x,e1,e2 and aggregate shock θ without paying. She then states WTP in BDM to obtain θ1,θ2 (and thus x1,x2). Positive mean WTP (1.00; Table 2-3) shows superiors value knowing which subordinate got which shock even when they already know efforts, supporting norm enforcement.","Information condition description and behavioral interpretation from Sect. 2, 5.2, Table 2-3.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0120394.md,Single Public Goods Game – Rounds 1–5 (No Punishment Condition),Paper describes one 10-round public goods game; rounds 1–5 are explicitly a phase without punishment opportunities.,1,Punishment opportunity = absent; Tournament payoff scheme with rank-based fixed payments; Full information on others’ round contributions and cumulative profits.,Only factor that changes over time is introduction of punishment from round 6; tournament payoffs and information structure are constant.,1,True,Human participants (80 undergraduate students) played the computerized game.,1,True,Controlled economic game with randomized groups and predefined rules.,1,True,"Computerized z-Tree experiment, participants in separate rooms at a university.",1,False,No agent-based or purely computational model described.,1,False,No formal mathematical model or closed-form analysis as primary method.,1,4,“The participants played in groups of four; the composition of the groups remained stable throughout the games.”,1,5,"This condition spans rounds 1–5, explicitly noted as the phase before punishment is introduced.",1,1,"Players decide “whether they would like to contribute some of these points…and if so, how many” from 1000 points, implying any amount between 0 and 1000 is possible.",1,0,All 1000 points each round are initially on private accounts; players actively choose an amount to move to the public account.,1,0.5,"Total contributions are summed and doubled, then split among 4. Each 1 point contributed yields 2/4 = 0.5 points to each player, so MPCR = 0.5.",1,0,"No mention of communication; players in separate rooms, information only via on-screen messages.",0.9,1,"Each round, participants see others’ contributions and others’ total profits (“total profit of the other group members given at the end of the round”).",1,0,No punishment in this phase; by design later they “did not receive information about the identities of their punishers.”,0.9,0,No reward mechanism is described anywhere in the experiment.,1,1,“The game consisted of 10 rounds. The players were informed of the number of the current round by on-screen messages.”,1,0,First five rounds are explicitly “without punishment condition”; players had no knowledge of punishment possibility before sixth round.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this phase.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this phase.,1,0,No positive reward or costly reward option described; only contributions and (later) punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1000,"“At the beginning of each round, the participants received 1000 points.”",1,N/R,"Paper reports that contributions decrease and gives ANOVA results and a figure, but no exact average contributions relative to 1000 are numerically reported.",0.2,N/R,Only qualitative trends and a graph are provided; no exact round-wise means or totals in points are given.,0.2,N/R,No ratio of actual to maximum possible group payoff is reported or derivable without average contributions.,0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff values per round or condition are presented.,0.1,Hungary,"Participants are “undergraduate students of a Hungarian university”; experiment conducted at University of Pécs, Hungary.",1,"19-25 (Mean 22.0, SD 2.11)",“Aged 19 to 25 (Mean ± SD = 22.0 ± 2.11).”,1,"32 male, 48 female","Explicitly reported: “80 undergraduate students… (32 male, 48 female)”.",1,Undergraduate university students,“Eighty undergraduate students of a Hungarian university… were recruited.”,1,On site,"Participants were in separate rooms at a Hungarian university using z-Tree, indicating an in-person lab setting rather than online.",1,"Tournament incentives: after 10 rounds, players are ranked by total points and paid fixed HUF amounts: 1st=5000, 2nd=3000, 3rd=2000, 4th=1000; group composition fixed; feedback each round on own profit, own total, others’ contributions, others’ totals; no punishment option in rounds 1–5.",Summarizes payoff structure and feedback rules that characterize the game in this phase.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0120394.md,Single Public Goods Game – Rounds 6–10 (Costly Punishment Condition),Same 10-round public goods game; rounds 6–10 are explicitly described as the phase with costly punishment opportunities.,1,"Punishment opportunity = present (costly reduction of others’ profits up to 500 points per target, 25% cost to punisher); Tournament payoff scheme with rank-based fixed payments; Full information on others’ round contributions and cumulative profits; Identities of punishers anonymous.",Only structural change from first phase is introduction of costly punishment from round 6 with specified parameters; other aspects remain constant.,1,True,Same human participant sample in the continuation of the game.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment opportunity within the same repeated game.,1,True,Same z-Tree lab environment at the university.,1,False,No non-human simulation is used.,1,False,No purely analytical model is presented as a separate condition.,1,4,Groups of four remain fixed throughout all 10 rounds.,1,5,This condition covers the second half of the 10-round game (rounds 6–10).,1,1,Contribution decision in each round remains identical: choose how many of the 1000 points to contribute.,1,0,"Endowment is still 1000 points per round on private account, with voluntary transfer to public account.",1,0.5,"Contribution technology (sum, double, divide by 4) does not change.",1,0,No communication channel added in the punishment phase; instructions only via on-screen messages.,0.9,1,"As before, players see others’ contributions and total profits each round; additionally they see total punishment received and cost of punishment imposed for themselves.",1,0,“They did not receive information about the identities of their punishers.”,1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Players continue to be informed of the current round number out of 10.,1,1,"From round 6, “they would be able to reduce the total profit accumulated in the other group members' private accounts… The maximum amount of punishment… was 500 points… Punishment was also costly: 25% of the imposed amount was subtracted from the punisher's private account.”",1,0.25,Each 1 point of punishment costs the punisher 0.25 points (“25% of the imposed amount was subtracted from the punisher's private account”).,1,1,"Imposed punishment directly reduces target’s private account point-for-point up to 500 points; no multiplier described, so 1 unit reduction per 1 unit spent-before-cost.",0.9,0,No provision for increasing others’ payoffs; only reduction/punishment is available.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1000,"Endowment remains “At the beginning of each round, the participants received 1000 points.” including rounds 6–10.",1,N/R,"They report that the decline in contributions from round 6–10 is not significant and show a figure, but no numeric average contributions or normalized rates are given.",0.2,N/R,No exact values of contributions in points per round are tabulated.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric or enough numeric detail to compute it for this phase.,0.1,N/R,No round-wise or total average group payoff in points is reported.,0.1,Hungary,Same participant pool and location as in rounds 1–5.,1,"19-25 (Mean 22.0, SD 2.11)",Same sample continues into punishment rounds.,1,"32 male, 48 female",No change in composition across rounds; all 80 play all 10 rounds.,1,Undergraduate university students,Same as overall sample description.,1,On site,Same lab environment using z-Tree in separate rooms.,1,"Tournament incentives identical to first phase: after 10 rounds, final rank by total points determines fixed monetary payoffs (1st=5000 HUF, 2nd=3000 HUF, 3rd=2000 HUF, 4th=1000 HUF). In rounds 6–10, after contributing, each player can assign up to 500 punishment points to each of the three other players; punishment reduces targets’ accumulated profit and costs the punisher 25% of the imposed amount. Players see total punishment received and total punishment cost each round but not who punished them.",Summarizes punishment mechanism and payoff scheme specific to the punishment phase.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0041338.md,High cooperation level condition (HC),"Condition explicitly labeled as High Cooperation level condition (HC), with specific contribution ranges for personas A, B, and C.",1,"Cooperation level of the group = High (average of B and C contributions between 5,000–9,999 JPY); non-cooperator A contributes 5,000–6,000 JPY; punishment choice among 0, 1×, or 4× the amount A did not contribute.",Paper defines cooperation level based on average contributions of B and C and specifies ranges for HC and A’s contribution; punishment options are common across conditions but cooperation level is the key contextual independent variable.,1,True,Seventeen healthy male volunteers performed the task in the MRI scanner.,1,True,"Task with controlled conditions (HC, LC, CC, UC) and randomization, designed and administered by experimenters.",1,True,Conducted in an fMRI scanning environment at a research institute (laboratory setting).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only human subjects’ decisions.,1,False,No purely mathematical or closed-form model; analysis is statistical on fMRI/behavioral data.,1,1,Each subject acts as a single third-party punisher observing three personas; only the subject makes active decisions.,0.7,25,"One session had 4 conditions (HC, LC, CC, UC) with 5 punishing events per condition, repeated 5 times, yielding 100 events total; thus 25 events per condition.",0.8,0,"Subjects choose among three discrete punishment levels (none, moderate, severe) rather than contributing all-or-nothing to a public good.",1,0,"No endowment allocation for the punisher; the contribution game is hypothetical among personas, and subjects only choose punishments.",0.3,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; only contribution amounts and punishment amounts are specified.,0,0,No communication among participants or between participant and personas is mentioned.,0.9,1,Subjects view a scene where three personas’ individual contributions are shown and know that all know each other’s contributions.,0.8,0,Only the leftmost persona (A) is ever punished; no mention that any identity information is revealed to that persona or others since personas are fictive.,0.6,0,"No rewarding mechanism is included, only punishment options.",1,0,No indication that subjects were told the number of total events or remaining rounds on-screen.,0.5,1,Core of the task is choosing a penalty level for the non-cooperator.,1,N/R,The paradigm specifies penalty amounts imposed on A but does not describe any cost to the punisher for administering punishment.,0.2,N/R,"Only relative amounts (amount not contributed, fourfold that) are given; no per-unit transformation from expenditure to punishment is defined.",0.2,0,No option to reward any persona is described; only punishments.,1,N/A,Reward option not present.,1,N/A,Reward option not present.,1,N/R,"Subjects do not receive a specified monetary endowment for the game; contributions of personas are exogenous and described as up to 10,000 JPY.",0.2,N/A,No contributions by the human subject; only punishment decisions are measured.,1,N/A,Human subject does not contribute; only observes personas’ contributions.,1,N/R,No group payoff or efficiency metric is reported or derivable for the personas’ facility-building game.,0.1,N/R,"No explicit payoffs to the group or to personas from the contributions are given, only conceptual public facility.",0.1,Japan,"Study conducted at National Institute of Radiological Sciences in Chiba, Japan; all affiliations and ethics committees are Japanese.",0.9,"Mean age 26.4 years (SD = 5.0), adult men",Subjects section: 'seventeen men [mean age 26.4 ± (SD) 5.0 years]'.,1,Male only,Explicitly states that all subjects were healthy men.,1,N/R,No information on student status or years of education is given.,0,On site,"Participants physically present in MRI facility; this is a lab-based fMRI experiment, not online or field.",1,"Third-party punishment fMRI task; three fictive personas (A: non-cooperator, B and C: cooperators) contribute up to 10,000 JPY to a public facility. In the High Cooperation condition, B and C’s contributions average between 5,000–9,999 JPY and A contributes 5,000–6,000 JPY (still the lowest). Subject chooses within 6000 ms among No Punishment, punishing the amount A did not contribute (Moderate), or four times that amount (Severe). Behavioral DV: converted mean punishment level (0=No, 1=Moderate, 2=Severe) with mean 0.97 (SD=0.21) in HC.",Summarized directly from Materials and Behavioral Results sections for this specific cooperation level.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0041338.md,Low cooperation level condition (LC),"Condition explicitly labeled as Low Cooperation level condition (LC), with specific contribution ranges for personas A, B, and C.",1,"Cooperation level of the group = Low (average of B and C contributions between 0–4,999 JPY); non-cooperator A contributes 800–1,500 JPY; punishment choice among 0, 1×, or 4× the amount A did not contribute.","Paper defines cooperation level based on B and C; defines LC range and A’s contribution range for LC, with identical punishment choice set as other conditions.",1,True,Same fMRI experiment and participants as HC condition.,1,True,Condition within the same controlled experimental design.,1,True,Conducted on site in MRI lab.,1,False,No computer-only agents; human subjects only.,1,False,"Analysis is empirical/statistical, not purely analytical.",1,1,One human punisher per session; personas are not interactive participants.,0.7,25,Same session structure as HC: 25 events per condition.,0.8,0,"Punishment choice among three discrete levels, not all-or-nothing contribution.",1,0,No punisher endowment; personas’ contributions are exogenous to subject’s incentives.,0.3,N/R,No contribution multiplier or per capita return reported.,0,0,No communication channel in description.,0.9,1,Subject sees all three personas’ contribution amounts each trial.,0.8,0,Only persona A is target and is a fictive character; no information that identities are revealed in a way relevant to strategic behavior.,0.6,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No mention that number of total or remaining events is displayed.,0.5,1,Same three-level punishment choice as HC.,1,N/R,No explicit cost to punisher described.,0.2,N/R,"Penalty is specified relative to A’s un-contributed amount, not as a linear transform of punisher’s expenditure.",0.2,0,No rewarding option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No subject monetary endowment for decisions specified.,0.2,N/A,"Outcome of interest is punishment, not contribution by subject.",1,N/A,No subject contributions.,1,N/R,No payoff or efficiency measure reported.,0.1,N/R,Payoffs from contributions or punishments are not quantified.,0.1,Japan,Same sample as HC condition.,0.9,"Mean age 26.4 years (SD = 5.0), adult men",Same subject pool as in HC.,1,Male only,All subjects are male volunteers.,1,N/R,No education information provided.,0,On site,Same MRI lab environment as HC.,1,"Same third-party punishment fMRI paradigm as HC, but with low cooperation context: in the Low Cooperation condition, cooperators B and C contribute between 0–4,999 JPY on average, and non-cooperator A contributes 800–1,500 JPY (still the lowest). Subject chooses between No, Moderate (amount A did not contribute), or Severe punishment (four-fold of Moderate). Behavioral DV: converted mean punishment level (0–2) with mean 0.18 (SD=0.30) in LC.",Summarized from Materials and Behavioral Results for the LC cooperation level.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0041338.md,Control cooperation level condition (CC),"Condition explicitly labeled as Control Cooperation level condition (CC), where all three personas contribute equally.",1,"Cooperation level of the group = Control/Full cooperation (A, B, and C each contribute 10,000 JPY); punishment choice among 0, 1×, or 4× the amount A did not contribute (here zero).","Paper defines CC such that all three personas contribute 10,000 JPY, i.e., full cooperation; punishment choice menu same as other conditions.",1,True,Same human subjects in same experiment.,1,True,CC is a within-subject condition in the controlled fMRI experiment.,1,True,MRI lab setting.,1,False,Human participants are used.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,1,One human punisher per session observing personas.,0.7,25,"One of four conditions in 100-event session, with 25 events per condition.",0.8,0,"Three discrete punishment options, not all-or-nothing contributions.",1,0,No punisher endowment or contribution decision.,0.3,N/R,No public goods multiplier specified.,0,0,No communication is described.,0.9,1,"Subject sees all three personas’ contributions, now all equal at 10,000 JPY.",0.8,0,Persona A is target; scenario uses fixed positions but no indication that identifying information is shown back to any agent; personas are fictive.,0.6,0,No rewarding option.,1,0,No evidence that number of remaining events was displayed.,0.5,1,"Same punishment decision menu, though inequity is zero.",1,N/R,No cost to the punisher is reported.,0.2,N/R,Penalty defined as function of A’s non-contribution (which is zero in CC); no per-unit transformation relative to punisher resources.,0.2,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Subject endowments are not specified.,0.2,N/A,No subject contributions.,1,N/A,No subject contributions.,1,N/R,No payoff metrics reported.,0.1,N/R,Payoffs from the fully cooperative contributions are not quantified.,0.1,Japan,Same subject pool as other conditions.,0.9,"Mean age 26.4 years (SD = 5.0), adult men",Same participants as in HC and LC.,1,Male only,All subjects are male.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0,On site,Same fMRI lab.,1,"Control cooperation condition in same third-party punishment fMRI paradigm: all three personas A, B, and C contribute the maximum 10,000 JPY each to the public facility, eliminating inequity. Subjects still choose among No, Moderate (amount not contributed, here zero), and Severe (four-fold of Moderate) punishments. Behavioral DV: converted mean punishment in CC is 0 (SD=0), i.e., subjects never punish.",Summarized from Materials and Behavioral Results for CC condition.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2017.01.006.md,"PX (Baseline: public goods + expropriation, homogeneous groups, M=3)",Baseline treatment PX described in Section 5 and Table 1 with homogeneous groups and expropriation threshold M=3.,1,"Institutional structure: public goods stage followed by expropriation stage (M=3, homogeneous groups).",PX differs from P and X by including both stages; differs from PX-M1/M2 by M=3; differs from -Het by homogeneity.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects at UK universities.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized assignment to treatments.,1,True,Sessions run at universities using zTree; standard lab environment.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,"This row refers to the empirical PX treatment, not the theoretical model.",1,4,“Subjects were randomly allocated into groups of four.”,1,20,“They played twenty rounds.”,1,1,"Public goods stage: subjects could contribute any amount from 0 to 50 ECU, standard linear PGG; not restricted to all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Each subject endowed with 50 ECU and could choose how much to contribute; endowment starts in private account.,1,0.35,Total contributions multiplied by 1.4 and divided equally among 4; MPCR = 1.4 / 4 = 0.35.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard anonymous PGG without communication.,0.9,1,"Feedback on individual contributions was given, and in expropriation stage subjects learned how many people targeted each group member.",1,1,Color labels and group membership visible; they know which group members targeted whom via counts per member. Identities within small fixed group are effectively known.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,1,"Standard repeated-game lab design with fixed 20 rounds; although not explicitly stated, subjects are typically informed of total rounds.",0.7,1,Expropriation stage functions as a punishment mechanism where a target loses 50 ECU when successfully targeted.,1,0,“Targeting involved no monetary cost.” Therefore cost per targeting coin is 0.,1,50,"If at least 3 subjects target same subject, target is expropriated, losing 50 ECU. So each successful collective punishment causes a 50 ECU loss.",1,1,Expropriators each gained 15 ECU when expropriation succeeds; this is a positive payoff to punishers.,1,0,No cost to targeting; expropriators’ gain of 15 ECU is net of costs since k=0.,1,15,Each successful expropriator gains 15 ECU.,1,100,"Each round: 50 ECU for public goods stage plus a further 50 ECU for expropriation stage, so total 100 ECU per round.",1,0.524,Mean contributions in PX were 26.20 ECU. Max possible per round is 50 ECU. So 26.20 / 50 ≈ 0.524.,0.9,26.2,Reported directly: “Mean contributions per group are significantly higher in PX than P (26.20 vs. 11.67)”. 26.20 is interpreted as mean individual contribution.,0.9,N/R,No overall payoff efficiency relative to maximum is reported or derivable from text.,0,N/R,No explicit average group payoff information reported.,0,United Kingdom,"“Sessions were run at two UK universities.” Subjects are likely UK-based, though possibly of diverse origin; country of lab is UK.",0.9,N/R,No age range or mean age reported.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0,University students,"Subjects recruited via university hroot pools; typical participants are students, although not explicitly labeled.",0.7,On site,Laboratory sessions conducted physically at two UK universities; no indication of online implementation.,1,"Repeated linear public goods game (20 rounds) with fixed groups of 4, endowment 50 ECU per public goods stage, MPCR 0.35, followed each round by a costless-targeting expropriation stage with threshold M=3 where a unanimously (3+) targeted player loses 50 ECU and each expropriator gains 15 ECU.",Summarizes procedural and payoff-relevant details of PX treatment as described in Section 5.,1
