10.1073_pnas.1606456113.md,Homogeneous population envelope game – theoretical analysis,Paper analyzes Nash equilibria of an abstract two-player repeated envelope game in a homogeneous population; no separate empirical conditions.,0.95,"Game parameters in homogeneous population: payoff for cooperation a (varied; e.g., region c_l < a < c_h), temptation to defect when low c_l = 1, when high c_h = 10, payoff to player 2 from cooperation b = 1.5, payoff from defection d = -5, continuation probability w = 0.4, probability of low temptation p = 0.6. Strategies of player 1 (cooperate without looking; look and conditionally cooperate; always defect) and player 2 (end if P1 looks; end if P1 defects).",Extracted from explicit parameter listing and strategy descriptions for Fig. 2.,0.96,False,Study is purely game-theoretic and uses no human participants.,1.0,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only theoretical game analysis.,1.0,False,No lab or field implementation; analytical work only.,1.0,True,Authors state they ran simulations using the replicator equation to test dynamic stability of equilibria.,0.86,True,"Core results derived via analytical game-theoretic and equilibrium analysis, including payoff tables and sequential equilibria.",1.0,2,Envelope game explicitly defined as an interaction between player 1 and player 2.,1.0,0,"Infinite-horizon repeated game with continuation probability w; no fixed number of rounds, so coded as 0.",0.7,0,"Not a standard contribution game; players choose between actions (cooperate/defect/look), not contribution levels; use 0 to indicate all-or-nothing cooperation decision.",0.6,N/A,No public goods or endowment structure; purely strategic actions in a 2x2 style game; and no human participants.,1.0,N/R,Not a public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1.0,0,Model does not include any communication stage between players.,0.95,0,No feedback summaries about others' earnings are modeled; only own and partner payoffs.,0.95,N/A,No identity structure 