10.1073_pnas.1606456113.md,Heterogeneous population envelope game – two-type analytical model,Paper extends the envelope game to heterogeneous populations with at least two types of player 1 differing in payoff for cooperation a_U and a_F.,0.96,"Population heterogeneity with two types of player 1: unfavorable type (1_U) with payoff for cooperation a_U and favorable type (1_F) with payoff a_F. Parameters used in Fig. 3: a_U and a_F varied; b = 1.5, c_l = 1, c_h = 10, d = -5, w = 0.4, p = 0.6. Independent variables are the pair (a_U, a_F) and strategy profiles of both types and player 2 (e.g., end if P1 looks vs end if P1 defects).",Directly from section 'The Envelope Game in Heterogeneous Populations' and Fig. 3 description.,0.97,False,Model of types and strategies; no human participants.,1.0,False,Theoretical sequential equilibrium analysis; not an empirical experiment.,1.0,False,No laboratory setting; purely analytical and simulated dynamics.,1.0,True,"Authors indicate they ran simulations using the replicator equation to test stability of equilibria, especially in heterogeneous populations.",0.9,True,Derive general conditions and regions (manipulative vs preferential) using analytical game-theoretic methods with types.,1.0,2,Still an interaction between a single player 1 (of varying type) and a single player 2.,1.0,0,"Repeated game with continuation probability w; expected number of rounds 1/(1-w), but no fixed N.",0.7,0,"Actions are discrete (cooperate/defect/look), not continuous contributions.",0.7,N/A,Not a contribution game and has no human subjects.,1.0,N/R,No MPCR defined; not a public goods setting.,1.0,0,No communication modeled.,0.95,0,Model does not provide per-round summary feedback beyond direct payoffs.,0.9,N/A,Only one opponent; no notion of identity visibility for punishment.,1.0,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism is introduced.,1.0,0,Players know discount factor/continuation probability; not a displayed fixed number of remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Player 2 can end the game based on observed behavior; interpreted as punishment in both homogeneous and heterogeneous analyses.,0.98,N/R,"No explicit numeric cost parameter; cost is foregone continuation, not parameterized.",0.4,N/R,"No fine-grained technology (e.g., 1 token causes 3-point loss) specified.",0.4,0,No additional rewards besides normal cooperative payoffs.,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,N/R,"No endowments defined; players receive payoffs a, b, c_l, c_h, d directly per round.",0.8,N/R,"Relevant outcomes are cooperation frequencies by type and expected payoffs, not normalized contributions.",0.9,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts are defined.,0.9,N/R,"Paper characterizes existence of aversion to looking and mechanisms, not overall efficiency ratios.",0.7,N/R,Does not report single aggregate group payoff numbers; only comparative and conditional payoffs.,0.7,N/A,Theoretical heterogeneous population; no real participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Model and simulations only.,1.0,"Two-player repeated envelope game with incomplete information about player 1’s type; population composed of at least two types differing in payoff from cooperation (a_U, a_F). Player 2 chooses strategies (e.g., end if P1 looks) to maximize expected payoffs given beliefs about types. Main mechanisms studied: manipulation via punishment and preferential interactions enabling discrimination between favorable and unfavorable types.",Summarizes heterogeneous-type extension described around Fig. 3.,0.98
10.1073_pnas.1606456113.md,Infinite-type heterogeneous population – prevalence of aversion to looking,"Section 'Prevalence of Aversion to Looking in Heterogeneous Populations' and Fig. 4 analyze a population with infinitely many types, focusing on how interval width δ_a affects aversion to looking.",0.95,"Distribution of player 1 types: payoff for cooperation a distributed uniformly over an interval of width δ_a, with interval center varied between 0 and 15. Common game parameters: b = 1.5, c_l = 1, c_h = 10, d = -5, w = 0.4, p = 0.6. Independent variable is heterogeneity width δ_a.",Directly taken from Fig. 4 caption describing infinite types and δ_a.,0.98,False,Only analytical and possibly numerical evaluation of model across type distributions.,1.0,False,Not an empirical or lab experiment; parametric exploration of a model.,1.0,False,No lab environment; only theory/simulation.,1.0,True,"Figure shows proportion of cases in which aversion to looking exists as function of δ_a, obtained via analytical derivation and likely numerical integration/simulation over parameter space.",0.8,True,"Authors mention an analytical derivation for the line in Fig. 4 in SI Appendix, plus general proof that adding types can only enable aversion to looking.",1.0,2,Game remains a 2-player interaction; only type distribution changes.,1.0,0,Same repeated-game structure with continuation probability w; no fixed number of rounds.,0.7,0,"Actions are discrete choices, not continuous contributions.",0.7,N/A,Not a contribution game and has no human subjects.,1.0,N/R,No public goods or MPCR.,1.0,0,No communication modeled.,0.95,0,Model does not include display of summaries; only theoretical payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No separate identities in a multi-player sense; single opponent.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,0,"Players condition on continuation probability, not on displayed remaining rounds.",0.9,1,Game still allows player 2 to end the interaction as punishment or protective action.,0.95,N/R,No explicit numeric punishment cost parameter is defined.,0.4,N/R,No punishment technology ratio is parameterized.,0.4,0,No model of explicit rewarding behavior.,0.98,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1.0,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1.0,N/R,"As before, no endowment structure in the game.",0.8,N/R,"Dependent variable is proportion of parameter combinations where aversion to looking exists, not contributions.",0.95,N/R,No numeric contributions defined.,0.95,N/R,"Figure 4 reports prevalence of aversion to looking, not payoff efficiency.",0.7,N/R,No single group payoff measure; only classification over parameter space.,0.7,N/A,Infinite-type theoretical population; no real subjects.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Entire analysis is theoretical/analytical (with possible numerical evaluation).,1.0,"Extends heterogeneous envelope game to a continuum of player 1 types with payoff for cooperation a uniformly distributed over an interval of width δ_a. Main dependent outcome: fraction of parameter realizations in which a sequential equilibrium with aversion to looking exists, as a function of heterogeneity width.",Based on Fig. 4 description and surrounding text.,0.96
10.1073_pnas.1606456113.md,Costly detection of looking – theoretical extension,Section 'Aversion to Looking When Detecting Looking Is Costly' introduces an extension where player 2 pays a cost to detect information gathering.,0.9,"Addition of a positive cost for player 2 to detect whether player 1 has looked. Game otherwise as envelope game with parameters a, b, c_l, c_h, d, w, p; cost magnitude unspecified but treated as > 0.","Based on textual description stating that detecting looking is costly, even infinitesimally.",0.85,False,Purely theoretical claim about equilibria with costly detection; no data.,1.0,False,Analytical exploration of modified game; not an experiment.,1.0,False,No lab implementation.,1.0,False,This subsection refers to equilibrium existence and stability; no mention of additional simulations specifically for costly detection.,0.6,True,Authors assert results based on equilibrium analysis (pure vs mixed equilibria) and stability in homogeneous vs heterogeneous populations.,0.95,2,Same two-player envelope game.,1.0,0,Repeated game with continuation probability; no fixed N.,0.7,0,"Binary cooperation/defection decisions, not contribution amounts.",0.7,N/A,Not a public goods game and no humans.,1.0,N/R,MPCR concept not applicable here.,1.0,0,No communication stage added in this extension.,0.9,0,No mention of feedback displays.,0.9,N/A,Only dyadic interaction; identity issues not modeled.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,0,Repeated game with discount factor; no fixed horizon displayed.,0.9,1,"Player 2 still can end the game as punishment, although now detecting looking is costly.",0.9,N/R,"The cost referenced is for detecting looking, not directly parameterized as cost per punishment or token; no numeric value given.",0.3,N/R,No numeric mapping between punishment actions and opponent losses provided.,0.3,0,No reward mechanism introduced in the costly-detection extension.,0.98,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/A,No rewards.,1.0,N/R,No endowment discussed.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes concern existence/stability of aversion to looking, not contribution rates.",0.9,N/R,No contributions defined.,0.9,N/R,"Subsection discusses sustainability of equilibria, not efficiency metrics.",0.7,N/R,No numeric group payoff values reported.,0.7,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,No human,Purely theoretical extension.,1.0,"Modified envelope game where player 2 must incur a (possibly infinitesimal) cost to detect whether player 1 has looked in the envelope. Shows that in homogeneous populations such a cost eliminates pure-strategy equilibria with aversion to looking, whereas in heterogeneous populations aversion to looking can remain stable if its benefits exceed the detection cost.",Paraphrased from section discussing costly detection of looking.,0.96
10.1073_pnas.1606456113.md,Preference for looking – heterogeneous population with unreliable cooperators and defectors,Section 'Preference for Looking' and Fig. 5 analyze conditions under which player 2 prefers player 1 to look before defecting.,0.95,"Two-type heterogeneous population with player 1 types X and Y. Type Y: fixed payoff for cooperation a_Y = 3, chosen so that Y is an unreliable cooperator (looks and cooperates when temptation low). Type X: payoff for cooperation a_X varied. Payoff to player 2 from cooperation b varied on y-axis. Other parameters: c_l = 1, c_h = 10, d = -5, w = 0.4, p = 0.6. Strategies expanded to include: for P1, 'defect with looking', 'defect without looking'; for P2, 'end if P1 defects when temptation is low or defects without looking' vs 'end if P1 defects when temptation is low (regardless of looking)'.",Directly from Fig. 5 description and associated text defining strategies and parameter roles.,0.98,False,Study of equilibria in extended heterogeneous envelope game; no human data.,1.0,False,Not an experiment but theoretical parametric analysis.,1.0,False,No lab or field setting.,1.0,False,"Fig. 5 appears derived analytically from conditions; text refers to 'see SI Appendix, section 6 for derivations' rather than simulations.",0.7,True,Conditions for aversion vs preference for looking are derived in SI Appendix; figure marks analytic regions.,1.0,2,Still a two-player repeated envelope game.,1.0,0,Repeated game with continuation probability; no fixed number of rounds.,0.7,0,Choices are to cooperate or defect (with/without looking); no continuous contribution choice.,0.7,N/A,Not a contribution game; no human participants.,1.0,N/R,MPCR not defined for this game.,1.0,0,No communication modeled beyond implicit signaling via actions.,0.9,0,Model has no feedback summaries or histories beyond what is encoded in strategies.,0.9,N/A,Only one counterpart; no explicit identity revelation of punishers.,1.0,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1.0,0,Infinite-horizon setting parameterized by w; no displayed remaining rounds.,0.9,1,"Player 2 can end the game contingent on combinations of player 1’s looking and defecting actions, effectively punishing certain behaviors.",0.95,N/R,No explicit numeric punishment cost parameter; costs are opportunity costs of ending the game.,0.4,N/R,No specific punishment technology defined.,0.4,0,"Continuation of interaction is the default benefit, not a separate reward mechanism.",0.9,N/A,No distinct rewarding action.,1.0,N/A,No distinct rewarding action.,1.0,N/R,No endowment structure described.,0.8,N/R,"Dependent variables are the existence of preference vs aversion for looking over (a_X, b) space, not contributions.",0.95,N/R,No contribution amounts in tokens/points.,0.95,N/R,"Paper discusses net benefits of interactions with unreliable cooperators vs all-defectors, but does not report normalized efficiency metrics.",0.7,N/R,"No explicit total group payoff values given; focus is on sign and relative magnitude of payoffs (e.g., p b + (1-p) d > 0).",0.7,N/A,"Model population, no human subjects.",1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,N/A,No humans.,1.0,No human,"Theoretical, not empirical.",1.0,"Extended envelope game in a heterogeneous population where interactions with unreliable cooperators can be net beneficial for player 2 (p b + (1-p) d > 0). Analyzes regions of parameter space (a_X, b) where player 2 exhibits aversion to looking vs preference for looking; preference for looking arises when at least one type always defects, at least one type is an unreliable cooperator, and interactions with unreliable cooperators are sufficiently beneficial.",Condensed from 'Preference for Looking' section and Fig. 5 narrative.,0.97
10.1177_1043463111434700.md,Game of kula – analytical model,"The paper presents only a formal, infinite-horizon non-cooperative game called the 'game of kula'; there are no distinct empirical treatments or simulations.",0.98,"Not applicable – purely analytical game-theoretic model; no experimentally manipulated independent variable or parameter schedule beyond generic n, u(·), and δ.","The model analyses general properties for arbitrary number of players n, payoff functions u, and discount factor δ, without fixing specific parameter values as experimental conditions.",0.96,False,The work is entirely theoretical with no human participants or data collection.,1,False,No controlled experiment is conducted; it is a theoretical construction with propositions and proofs.,1,False,No lab or experimental setting is described; only abstract game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,"Although simulations by Ziegler are cited, this paper itself does not implement simulations.",1,True,The paper defines games and proves propositions mathematically using algebra and repeated-game theory.,1,0,"The model allows an arbitrary n ≥ 2 and analyses special cases n=2,3; no single fixed number of players per condition as in an experiment.",0.5,0,The game of kula is defined explicitly as an infinite-horizon repeated game (indefinite repetition).,1,1,Players choose between two discrete actions A and B each round rather than contributing a continuous amount; mapped here to 'all-or-nothing' contribution options.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects or monetary endowments; only abstract actions A and B with utilities.,1,N/R,The game is not framed as a linear public goods game with a marginal per capita return; only utility rankings are specified.,1,N/A,No communication stage or human implementation is described; purely analytical.,1,0,"Information assumption is 'observed actions and perfect recall'; utilities are known, but there is no notion of payoff or punishment summaries being displayed to human players.",0.7,N/A,"Punishment is represented as others choosing B in the strategy profile, not as an implemented punishment mechanism with identities.",1,N/A,Rewards are not implemented as a separate mechanism; only actions A and B affect payoffs.,1,0,Infinite-horizon analytical game; no interface where total or remaining rounds are displayed to subjects.,1,0,"There is no explicit costly punishment technology; 'punishment' is simply playing B according to strategies, not a separate decision with cost and impact parameters.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism is modelled.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology (mapping from punishment points to losses) is specified.,1,0,There is no separate rewarding action with distinct cost/benefit parameters beyond choosing A versus B.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology is defined.,1,N/R,"The model uses abstract utility payoffs u(x,y), not endowments in tokens or money.",1,N/R,No empirical data or simulation outputs are reported; only theoretical existence of equilibria.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions or actions frequencies are measured; A/B are abstract actions.,1,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (Pareto-dominance) but not quantified as a 0–1 ratio.,0.8,N/R,"No concrete payoff numbers are given for the model’s equilibria; only inequalities among u(A,A), u(B,A), etc.",1,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a theoretical game-theoretic analysis without any implemented experiment or field study.,1,"Non-cooperative infinite-horizon game with n ≥ 2 players arranged in a circular permutation; two actions A (gift/cooperate) and B (abstain/defect). Utilities satisfy prisoner's-dilemma-like inequalities. Two inverse circular games (permutations ρ and τ) alternate over time, generating flows analogous to the kula exchange. Analyses include one-shot 'circular external effects' game and its repeated version (game of kula), with trigger, generalized Pavlov, and double-trigger strategy equilibria representing direct, indirect, and mixed reciprocity norms.",Summarizes the formal structure and strategic norms directly described throughout sections I–V.,1
