punishment c is defined as c = p × N × Px / Σφx Px; not a simple linear cost-per-unit that can be summarized as a single scalar for all states.,0.3,N/R,Fine is f=βPx; no simple fixed mapping from a spent unit to imposed fine is defined as a constant parameter.,0.3,0,Only punishment is discussed; no rewarding of cooperators modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,"Model specifies payoffs R,T,S,P but does not frame these as endowments/allocations per round.",0.2,N/R,"Outcome measure is fraction of cooperators ρc, not framed as contribution relative to an endowment.",0.2,N/R,No token-based contribution; binary strategies only.,1,N/R,They report average payoff qualitatively and payoff distributions but not normalized efficiency relative to maximum possible.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative statements and distribution plots of payoffs; no single group payoff value reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; agents on lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational Monte Carlo simulation with agents; no empirical environment.,1,"Spatial PD on L×L (L=100) square lattice with periodic boundary conditions; agents have strategy C or D, initialized at random with equal probability. Payoffs: R=1, T=b (1<b≤2), S=P=0. Each time step: select focal agent x, compute payoff Px from interactions with four nearest neighbors; compute neighbors’ payoffs similarly. Punishment cost for cooperator or unpunished defector: c = p × N × Px / Σφx Px, where φx is x plus its neighbors, N=L². If x is a punished defector, fitness is Gx = Px − c − f, where f=βPx (0≤β≤1). Otherwise, Gx=Px−c. Strategy update: x randomly selects neighbor y; if Gx ≥ Gy, keep strategy; if Gx < Gy, adopt y’s strategy with probability W = (Gy − Gx)/ΔGmax, where ΔGmax is maximal possible fitness difference between x and neighbors. Simulations run for 20000 time steps; fraction of cooperators ρc measured as average over last 1000 steps and averaged over up to 20 independent runs per parameter set.",Directly summarized from Model and Simulation Results sections.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2013.07.016.md,SD – varying p and β on 100×100 lattice,"Single simulation setup described for Snowdrift (SG/SD) on a 100×100 lattice; r, p, and β varied with specific values in figures.",0.9,"Game: Snowdrift game (SD); cost-to-benefit ratio r ∈ [0,1] (examples r=0.4, 0.6); punishment proportion p ∈ [0,1] (e.g., 0.3, 0.5, 0.6); punishment strength β ∈ [0,1] (e.g., 0.2, 0.4, 0.5).","Model section specifies SD parameterization; figures for SD vary r, p, and β analogous to PDG.",0.9,False,"Monte Carlo simulations with agents, no human participants.",1,False,Not an empirical controlled experiment.,1,False,No physical laboratory interaction.,1,True,Described as simulations on a 100×100 lattice.,1,False,No analytical solution presented; results are numerical.,1,10000,"Same lattice size L=100, N=L²=10000.",1,20000,"Same simulation protocol: 20000 total steps, last 1000 used for measurement.",0.9,1,"Agents choose between C and D; no graded contribution. By provided coding, treat as not constrained to an all-or-nothing contribution amount choice.",0.4,N/A,No endowment or contribution mechanism defined.,1,N/R,"Pairwise snowdrift payoffs R,T,S,P but no MPCR defined.",1,N/A,No human subjects; no communication.,1,0,Agents only rely on local fitness comparisons; no payoff dashboards.,1,N/A,No identities; punishment implemented as fitness reduction.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents not informed about horizon; fixed update rule.,1,1,Same proportional punishment model (cost c and fine f=βPx) applied to SD.,1,N/R,Cost c defined as c = p × N × Px / Σφx Px; cannot be summarized as a single constant per-unit cost.,0.3,N/R,Fine f=βPx; no constant per-unit mapping given.,0.3,0,Only punishment considered.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/R,Interactions defined via payoff matrix; no explicit endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Outcome is fraction of cooperators ρc, not contribution relative to endowment.",0.2,N/R,Binary actions C/D only; no amount-level contributions.,1,N/R,They describe enhanced average payoff and payoff distributions but not normalized efficiency.,0.3,N/R,No explicit numeric group payoff values reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational agent-based simulations only.,1,"Spatial Snowdrift (SG/SD) game on an L×L (L=100) square lattice with periodic boundary conditions. Strategies: C or D, initialized at random with equal probability. Payoffs: R=1, T=1+r, S=1−r, P=0 with 0≤r≤1. At each step, a focal agent x plays the SD game with its four nearest neighbors to obtain payoff Px; neighbors’ payoffs computed similarly. Punishment and cost structure identical to PDG case: cooperators and unpunished defectors have fitness Gx = Px − c, punished defectors have Gx = Px − c − f, where c = p × N × Px / Σφx Px and f = βPx, N=L², φx is x plus its neighbors, and 0≤β≤1. Strategy update via local imitation: x picks random neighbor y; if Gx ≥ Gy, x keeps its strategy; if Gx < Gy, x adopts y’s strategy with probability W = (Gy − Gx)/ΔGmax. Simulations run for 20000 time steps, with fraction of cooperators ρc averaged over last 1000 steps and over up to 20 independent runs per parameter combination.",Summarized from model description where SD payoffs and same punishment mechanism are introduced.,1
