10.1007_s10640-023-00759-5.md,Stage 2 – Baseline treatment (BT),"Authors define four treatments: Baseline (BT), Sanctioned quota (SQT), Threshold information (TIT), and Sanctioned quota + Threshold information (SQT-TIT). This row corresponds to the BT condition in Stage 2, which is the incentivized part of the experiment.",1,Policy intervention: none (baseline); Resource dynamics: no threshold (concave growth for elephants and pastures); Quota: none; Information about threshold: none.,"Treatments differ by presence of sanctioned quota, threshold information, and associated resource dynamics. BT has no policy intervention and no thresholds, as summarized in Table 1 and section 4.1.2.",1,True,Framed field experiment with 384 villagers; BT is one of the human-subject conditions.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments with controlled payoff structure and rules; described as framed field experiment.,1,False,"Conducted in villages (CAMPFIRE communities) as a framed field experiment, not in a laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all results from human play.,1,False,"Although theoretical model is discussed, this row refers to the empirical treatment.",1,4,Section 4.1 describes groups of four participants sharing the resource; 96 groups total implies 384/4.,1,20,"Games are time-limited to about one hour with unknown stopping time; not a fixed known number of rounds. Instructions show table up to 50 but that is a form, not actual realized rounds. Number of actual rounds is not reported.",0,1,"Participants choose any integer from 0 up to current stock (request sheet shows 0–50, and text: 'You can choose a number between zero and the current stock of elephants'). This allows flexible amounts, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,"This is a common-pool extraction (harvest) game, not a contribution game. Endowment is in private decision of harvest; there is no initial allocation into a public fund. By convention for CPR take games, coded as 0 (all in private account).",0.7,N/R,Marginal per capita return of contributing to a public good is not defined in a CPR extraction game and no equivalent normalized MPCR is reported.,0,1,"Participants 'were allowed to communicate face-to-face during any moment of the game, except when they made decisions'.",1,1,"After each round, assistants 'communicated this information orally and in writing to the groups, and to individuals, using a balance sheet' including total harvest and new stock sizes, which are group-level summaries.",1,0,No punishment institution exists in BT; by convention set identity revelation to 0.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in any treatment.,1,0,Participants were told that the game length (time horizon) was unknown and not given the number of rounds.,1,0,BT has no sanctions or quotas; punishment is only introduced in SQT and SQT-TIT.,1,N/A,No punishment institution in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment institution in baseline.,1,0,No rewards for punishing or additional positive incentives beyond normal payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/R,"Game describes access to a common stock of 50 elephants, not a private per-round token endowment; no fixed 'endowment' per player is specified.",0.2,N/R,"Authors report average efficiencies and stock levels, but not normalized individual contribution (or harvest) rates as 0–1 shares of endowment.",0,N/R,They do not report mean individual harvest per round or total harvest per group; only depletion cases and stock trajectories.,0,0.42,Table 5 reports 'Average efficiency for elephant stock' in BT Stage 2 as 0.420.,1,36.83,Table 2: 'Group payoff' for Baseline treatment has mean $36.83.,1,Zimbabwe,Study conducted with CAMPFIRE communities in Zimbabwe.,1,Mean 37.5 years (SD 13.59); Median 35,Table 2 shows these values for Baseline treatment.,1,"31% male, 69% female","Table 2: Gender [1=M] mean 0.290 in BT; thus 29% male, 71% female. Text also says approximately 69% female overall; we report BT-specific proportion.",0.9,Mean 7.66 years of schooling (SD 4.05); Median 9,"Table 2 Baseline treatment, Education [# years].",1,Field experiment,"Described as framed field experiment with villagers in their communities, not online or lab.",1,"Dynamic common-pool resource (elephants) extraction game with linked public pasture benefits; initial common stock 50 elephants, discrete growth function without threshold; each elephant harvested pays $0.25; pasture stock generates group payoff $0.10 per unit split equally; if resource exhausted or over-harvested, terminal payoff rules apply; communication allowed; Stage 1 was unpaid training and excluded from analysis; Stage 2 used same BT rules but with monetary incentives in this treatment arm.",Summarized from sections 4.1–4.2 and instructions; this row covers the baseline environment without any quota or threshold information.,1
10.1007_s10640-023-00759-5.md,Stage 2 – Sanctioned quota treatment (SQT),"One of the four experimental treatments; this row captures the policy condition with sanctioned quota but without threshold in the resource dynamics, in Stage 2.",1,"Policy intervention: sanctioned quota (TAC) with individual stochastic monitoring and moratorium sanctions; Resource dynamics: no threshold (concave growth); Quota: group TAC_t = X_t − 20 elephants; Information about threshold: none (participants focused only on elephant quota, no explicit pasture-threshold information).","Section 4.1.2 details SQT as a quota-based, sanction-backed policy with no threshold in growth; Table 1 and text distinguish resource dynamics from TIT and SQT-TIT.",1,True,"Same framed field experiment with villagers, with this policy applied.",1,True,Controlled randomized assignment of groups to SQT and rules specific to this treatment.,1,False,"Run in CAMPFIRE villages as framed field experiment, not a university lab.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation here.,1,False,Analytical model exists but not what this row represents.,1,4,Same group size as all treatments.,1,20,Actual number of rounds not reported; time-limited with unknown horizon. Cannot infer a specific number.,0,1,Same harvesting decision range as BT: any integer from 0 to current stock.,1,0,Same CPR extraction framing as BT; no public fund contribution mechanism.,0.7,N/R,MPCR is not defined or reported for this CPR game.,0,1,Communication allowed in all treatments.,1,1,Groups received round-by-round information on total harvest and stocks via oral report and balance sheet.,1,1,"In quota treatments, the experimenter throws a die; numbers 1–4 correspond to specific participants who, if controlled and found violating quota, forfeit harvest and face moratorium. Identity of punished individuals is observable to group given face-to-face setting and individualized instructions.",0.85,0,No reward scheme exists.,1,0,End time unknown to participants; they are not informed about remaining rounds.,1,1,Described sanctioned quota with punishment: forfeiting harvest and temporary harvest prohibition with probability 2/3 of being controlled if stock falls below 20 and quota exceeded.,1,1,"Cost defined as own forfeited harvest units in current round and loss of ability to harvest in next round(s). However, paper does not specify a numeric token-per-punishment ratio, so we approximate cost per unit of sanction as 1 lost unit of harvest for each unit over quota is not explicitly defined. Strict numeric mapping not given.",0.2,1,"Punishment effect is losing entire harvest of the round and zero harvest in subsequent moratorium round(s); but magnitude per 'coin' of punishment is not specified. We conservatively code 1 as a placeholder, noting low confidence.",0.2,0,No positive sanctions or bonus rewards present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Common stock of 50 elephants at start, but no per-player token endowment or fixed private resource; thus endowment per player is not defined.",0.2,N/R,No normalized individual harvest/contribution rates reported.,0,N/R,No mean or median harvest quantities reported per person or group.,0,0.71,Table 5 shows 'Average efficiency for elephant stock' in SQT Stage 2 as 0.710.,1,45.17,Table 2: 'Group payoff' mean for Sanctioned quota treatment is $45.17.,1,Zimbabwe,Same participant pool across treatments.,1,Mean 36.58 years (SD 13.58); Median 37,Table 2 SQT age statistics.,1,"31% male, 69% female","Table 2: Gender [1=M] mean 0.310 in SQT, i.e., 31% male.",1,Mean 7.22 years of schooling (SD 3.62); Median 7,Table 2 SQT education values.,1,Field experiment,Same framed field setting as BT.,1,"Dynamic CPR game with elephants and linked pasture benefits. Initial elephant stock 50, concave growth without threshold; pasture dynamics linked similarly. Each harvested elephant pays $0.25; pasture units are worth $0.10 to the group, equally shared. Treatment-specific: in each round with stock above 20, the group receives a total allowable catch TAC_t = X_t − 20. Participants are informed of the quota each round and may choose to respect it or not. If total harvest exceeds TAC and/or stock falls below 20, there is a 2/3 probability that one randomly selected player (via die roll) is controlled; if caught cheating, that player forfeits their elephant harvest for that round and is banned from harvesting for the next round (and longer for repeated offenses). Communication remains allowed except during decision moments.",Compiled from institutional design (4.1.2) and instructions for sanctioned quota treatment.,1
10.1007_s10640-023-00759-5.md,Stage 2 – Threshold information treatment (TIT),This row represents the treatment where participants receive information about a threshold/regime shift in resource dynamics but no quota policy.,1,Policy intervention: information provisioning about threshold/regime shift; Resource dynamics: threshold (non-concave growth) for elephants and pastures with a critical stock level at 20; Quota: none; Monitoring/punishment: none.,Section 4.1.2 describes TIT as informing participants about a threshold at stock 20 below which growth falls sharply; Table 1 labels it 'Threshold information treatment' with thresholds and no quota.,1,True,"Same framed field experiment with villagers, assigned to TIT.",1,True,Randomized between-group treatment with controlled rules.,1,False,"Conducted as field sessions in villages, not a conventional lab.",1,False,"Outcomes based on human decisions, not a simulation.",1,False,"This row concerns empirical treatment, not theoretical model calibration.",1,4,All treatments use four-person groups.,1,20,Exact realized rounds not reported; horizon is time-based and unknown to participants.,0,1,"Harvest choice is any integer from 0 to current stock, same interface as BT.",1,0,Same CPR extraction structure; no contribution account.,0.7,N/R,No MPCR measure for a public good contribution is reported.,0,1,Communication allowed in all treatments.,1,1,Groups receive total harvest and stock updates every round.,1,0,No punishment mechanism present in TIT.,1,0,No dedicated reward institution.,1,0,Game endpoint unknown to participants.,1,0,No sanctions or moratoria in TIT; only information.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,0,No additional reward policy.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Same CPR framing as other treatments: common stock, no individual token endowment.",0.2,N/R,No normalized harvest rate per player reported.,0,N/R,No descriptive stats on average harvest per player or group reported.,0,0.559,Table 5: 'Average efficiency for elephant stock' in TIT Stage 2 is 0.559.,1,41.62,Table 2 gives 'Group payoff' mean for Threshold information treatment as $41.62.,1,Zimbabwe,All groups from CAMPFIRE communities in Zimbabwe.,1,Mean 37.04 years (SD 12.15); Median 35,Table 2 values for TIT.,1,"31.7% male, 68.3% female",Table 2: Gender [1=M] mean 0.317 for TIT.,1,Mean 7.67 years of schooling (SD 3.57); Median 7,Table 2 TIT education values.,1,Field experiment,Same framed field village setting.,1,"CPR game with elephants and linked pasture stocks governed by non-concave growth with a threshold at stock level 20. When elephant stock falls below 20, growth rates of both elephants and pastures drop sharply (e.g., elephants: growth falls from 7 to 1; pastures: sharp decline and 'explosion in bushes'). Participants are explicitly informed of the existence and location of this threshold and the associated drastic regime shift. No quotas or sanctions; participants choose harvest each round, observe updated elephant and pasture stocks and payoffs, and can communicate freely except during decision entry.",Derived from 4.1.1–4.1.2 and detailed treatment-specific instructions for the threshold information treatment.,1
10.1007_s10640-023-00759-5.md,Stage 2 – Sanctioned quota + Threshold information treatment (SQT-TIT),"Fourth treatment combining both policy interventions: sanctioned quotas and threshold information, with threshold resource dynamics.",1,Policy intervention: combination of sanctioned quota (TAC) and threshold information; Resource dynamics: threshold (non-concave growth) for elephants and pastures with threshold at 20; Quota: TAC_t = X_t − 20 elephants when stock > 20; Monitoring/punishment: as in SQT; Information about threshold/regime shift: provided.,Table 1 and section 4.1.2 describe SQT-TIT as combining SQT and TIT institutions under threshold dynamics.,1,True,"Human-subject framed field experiment, same sample structure.",1,True,Random assignment of groups to SQT-TIT with defined rules.,1,False,Conducted in communities (field) rather than a lab.,1,False,Outcome data come from participants' decisions.,1,False,"This row describes the empirical treatment, not the theoretical analysis.",1,4,Same 4-person groups in all treatments.,1,20,Number of rounds is not fixed ex ante for participants; games end when time or depletion occurs. No explicit round count given.,0,1,"Harvest decision is flexible from 0 to current stock, as in other treatments.",1,0,Same CPR extraction framing with no initial public fund.,0.7,N/R,MPCR is not reported or directly applicable for this CPR game.,0,1,Communication allowed across all treatments.,1,1,Groups receive feedback on total harvest and updated elephant and pasture stocks in each round.,1,1,"Same quota enforcement as SQT: a die determines which of the four participants is controlled; punished individuals forfeit harvest and are banned. This is observable to group members, so identities of punished participants are effectively revealed.",0.85,0,No reward mechanism introduced.,1,0,Experimental sessions have unknown end round to participants.,1,1,Quota violations below stock 20 trigger potential controls with sanctions: current harvest forfeited and temporary harvest prohibition.,1,1,"As in SQT, the quantified cost per unit is not specified; punishment is forfeiture of entire harvest and future moratorium. We code 1 token-equivalent cost per unit with low confidence since no explicit ratio is given.",0.2,1,Punishment reduces punished player's payoff fully for that round (loss of all harvest) and to zero in subsequent banned rounds; exact 'per coin' impact is not numerically specified. Placeholder 1 with low confidence.,0.2,0,No explicit reward system beyond standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Common stock at start, but no per-person endowment.",0.2,N/R,No normalized harvest rates are reported.,0,N/R,The paper presents no descriptive stats for mean harvest quantities.,0,0.767,Table 5 reports 'Average efficiency for elephant stock' in SQT-TIT as 0.767.,1,45.96,Table 2: 'Group payoff' mean for 'Sanctioned quota and threshold information treatment' is $45.96.,1,Zimbabwe,All treatments use participants from the same Zimbabwean CAMPFIRE communities.,1,"Mean 37 years (SD 13.30); Median 37.16 (reported as 37.16 in table, likely mean; median given as 37 in heading).",Table 2 values for SQT-TIT; some rounding inconsistency but approximate age distribution is clear.,0.9,"30.8% male, 69.2% female",Table 2: Gender [1=M] mean 0.308 in SQT-TIT.,1,Mean 6.97 years of schooling (SD 4.27); Median 6,Table 2 SQT-TIT education data.,1,Field experiment,Same framed field context in villages.,1,"Same CPR game with elephants and pastures under non-concave growth with a critical threshold at 20. Participants are informed about the threshold and the drastic drop in growth beyond it. Additionally, a group-level sanctioned quota (TAC) is applied: TAC_t = X_t − 20 when X_t>20. At the beginning of each round, groups are told the quota. If the elephant stock falls below 20 and/or total harvest exceeds the quota, a six-sided die is thrown: numbers 1–4 map to individual players who may be controlled; if the selected player is found to have harvested more than their share relative to the quota, they forfeit their elephant harvest in the current round and are banned from harvesting in the next round (and for increasing periods with repeated offenses). Die outcomes 5–6 imply no one is controlled. Communication is allowed throughout except when filling request sheets.","Synthesized from quota implementation and threshold information sections ({4.1.1–4.1.2}, instructions for TIT and for quota treatments).",1
10.1063_5.0088493.md,"Square lattice – random umpires – varying ρ_f and r (α=1.5, β=0.2)","Section III A, Fig. 2: square lattice, random initial strategies and umpires, α=1.5, β=0.2, ρ_f and r varied.",1,"Synergy factor r ∈ {1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5,4.0,4.5} and fraction of fair umpires ρ_f ∈ [0,1] with players/umpires randomly placed.","Explicitly described around Fig. 2(a,b).",1,False,Monte Carlo simulation model; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human/field experiment; purely computational simulations.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; computational study.,1,True,Authors explicitly state use of Monte Carlo simulations on networks.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations as primary results; all main results from simulations.,0.9,10000,"Square lattice L×L with L=100 (Sec. III A), hence 100×100=10000 players.",1,20000,Each simulation runs for 20000 steps (rounds) before averaging.,1,1,Model uses binary strategies: cooperate (contribute fixed cost 1) or defect (contribute 0); not a continuous contribution model.,1,0,Endowment concept not explicitly defined; effectively each cooperator pays its own cost from private resource; public pool starts empty.,0.7,0.2,"Payoff term for each game: sum_k x_k * r / 5; each cooperator’s unit contribution yields r/5 to each player, so MPCR=r/5. For configuration we record the structural factor 1/5.",0.9,0,No communication or signaling between players mentioned.,1,0,Model only includes local payoffs; no information about others’ histories or summary statistics.,1,0,No identities; players only see payoffs and imitate neighbors via Fermi rule.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,No human interface; agents do not observe time horizon.,1,1,Fair umpires impose fine α on defectors.,1,1,"Defectors overseen by fair umpire pay fine α; each defector pays α per game. For baseline α=1.5, but structurally cost-per-unit is 1×α so we treat base coin as 1.",0.6,1,Each unit of fine directly reduces defector payoff one-for-one; no multiplier.,0.8,0,No prosocial reward or bonus in model; only fines and bribes.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"Per game, cooperators contribute cost 1 (x_i term) while defectors contribute 0; this can be interpreted as unit endowment per game.",0.8,N/R,Only aggregate fraction of cooperators ρ_c reported; no normalized contribution per endowment beyond this binary measure.,0.4,N/R,"While each cooperator contributes 1 conceptually, figures report only ρ_c, not average per-player contribution amounts.",0.5,N/R,They discuss social efficiency qualitatively but do not provide explicit efficiency ratios.,0.4,N/R,Total or average payoffs are not tabulated or plotted.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational network simulations.,1,"Players on a 100×100 square lattice (von Neumann neighbors). Each player organizes a PGG with self and 4 neighbors (group size 5). Cooperators contribute 1, defectors 0. Total contributions multiplied by synergy factor r and shared equally. Two-layer multiplex: each player linked to an umpire. Umpire is fair with probability ρ_f; otherwise corrupt. In each game, if umpire fair, each defector pays fine α; if corrupt, each defector pays bribe β (0<β<1<α). Players update synchronously by imitating a random neighbor using Fermi rule with noise K=0.1. Initial fractions: ρ_c=ρ_d=0.5. Umpires randomly fair/corrupt for given ρ_f.",Summarized from model description in Sec. II and parameter settings in Sec. III A.,1
10.1063_5.0088493.md,Square lattice – spatial distributions of umpires (random vs centralized vs staggered) at ρ_f=0.5,"Section III B, Fig. 4(a) and snapshots Fig. 5 with ρ_f=0.5 comparing three distributions.",1,"Spatial distribution of fair vs corrupt umpires: random, centralized clusters, or staggered (evenly mixed), with fraction of fair umpires ρ_f=0.5 and varying r.",Explicitly defined around Fig. 3 and Fig. 4(a).,1,False,Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,Computational study.,1,False,No lab/humans.,1,True,Results obtained via simulations on square lattice.,1,False,No analytical solution presented for these distributions.,0.9,10000,"Square lattice L×L, L=100 used throughout Sec. III.",1,20000,"Same Monte Carlo procedure as elsewhere: 20000 steps, averaging last 2000.",1,1,Binary cooperate/defect strategies with fixed cost 1 for cooperation.,1,0,Public pool starts from contributions; individual cost deducted from private payoff.,0.7,0.2,Same PGG structure: each unit contribution yields r/5 per player.,0.9,0,No communication modeled.,1,0,Only local payoff information used for imitation.,1,0,No identity or visibility of who punishes whom; only parametric interactions.,1,0,No reward.,1,0,Agents do not observe round horizon.,1,1,Fair umpires levy fine α on defectors.,1,1,Same payoff structure; cost is α per defector per fair-umpire game; base unit treated as 1.,0.6,1,Each unit of fine subtracts equally from defector payoff.,0.8,0,No rewarding.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Unit cost of cooperation remains 1.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes reported as ρ_c only, across r and spatial distributions.",0.5,N/R,Per-capita contribution not explicitly calculated; can be inferred as ρ_c but not reported numerically.,0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures given.,0.4,N/R,Group payoffs not tabulated.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Network simulations only.,1,"Same two-layer PGG-umpire model on 100×100 lattice as in baseline. α=1.5, β=0.2 fixed. Fraction of fair umpires fixed at ρ_f=0.5. Only spatial pattern of fair vs corrupt umpires differs: (1) random distribution, (2) centralized distribution where all fair umpires form a contiguous block and all corrupt umpires another, separated by a line, and (3) staggered distribution where fair and corrupt umpires are evenly and discretely interleaved. Players initially random with ρ_c=0.5. Synergy factor r is varied and resulting steady-state ρ_c compared across distributions.",Summarized from descriptions of Fig. 3–5.,1
10.1063_5.0088493.md,Square lattice – spatial distributions of umpires (random vs centralized vs staggered) at ρ_f=0.25,"Section III B, Fig. 4(b) and snapshots Fig. 5 for ρ_f=0.25.",1,"Spatial distribution of fair vs corrupt umpires: random, centralized, or staggered, with fraction of fair umpires ρ_f=0.25 and varying synergy factor r.",Described in Sec. III B with specific discussion of ρ_f=0.25 in Fig. 4(b).,1,False,Simulation-only study.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on lattice described in Sec. II–III.,1,False,No closed-form solution; results numerical.,0.9,10000,Same 100×100 lattice as elsewhere.,1,20000,All simulations use 20000 steps then average.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect contributions.,1,0,Contributions taken from private payoff; public pool initially empty.,0.7,0.2,"Same payoff structure with group size 5, factor r.",0.9,0,No communication.,1,0,Agents only know local payoff differentials.,1,0,Identity not modeled.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No display to agents.,1,1,Fair umpires fine defectors with α.,1,1,Fine α per defector per fair-umpire game; treated as 1×α unit cost.,0.6,1,Fine subtracts linearly from defector payoff.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,Cooperation cost per game is 1.,0.8,N/R,They report ρ_c as outcome measure; not expressed as normalized per-endowment rate.,0.5,N/R,No numerical averages of contributions presented.,0.5,N/R,Social efficiency only discussed qualitatively.,0.4,N/R,No aggregate payoff numbers reported.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Computation only.,1,"Same two-layer PGG model on a 100×100 lattice with α=1.5, β=0.2. Fraction of fair umpires ρ_f fixed at 0.25. Spatial distributions defined as in previous condition: random, centralized, and staggered. Players start with ρ_c=0.5 and evolve under Fermi imitation (K=0.1). Synergy factor r varied; study focuses on how sign and magnitude of differences between distributions in ρ_c depend on r.","Summarized from Sec. III B, focusing on ρ_f=0.25 case.",1
10.1063_5.0088493.md,"Square lattice – varying fine cost α and bribe cost β at ρ_f=0.5, r=1.3","Section III C, Fig. 7(a) and Fig. 8(a,b) with r=1.3, ρ_f=0.5, α and β varied.",1,"Fine cost α and bribe cost β varied over ranges with fixed fraction of fair umpires ρ_f=0.5, synergy factor r=1.3, random umpire distribution.","III C: explores wide parameter space for α and β; Fig. 7(a) for r=1.3, ρ_f=0.5.",1,False,Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,Not human or field experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Section II–III: Monte Carlo algorithm.,1,False,Results generated numerically on lattice.,0.9,10000,Square lattice with L=100.,1,20000,Same simulation length and averaging as earlier sections.,1,1,"Binary contribution decision; cost 1 if cooperate, 0 otherwise.",1,0,No default contribution to public good; contributions come from individual cost.,0.7,0.2,"Group size 5, multiplier r; MPCR=r/5.",0.9,0,No communication modeled.,1,0,No global or summary feedback.,1,0,"Agents only use payoffs, not identity of punisher.",1,0,No reward.,1,0,No visible horizon.,1,1,Fair umpires impose fine α; α is an experimental parameter.,1,1,Each defector pays α per fair-umpire game; base unit cost is 1×α.,0.6,1,Linear fine impact.,0.8,0,No rewards involved.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,"Cooperator’s cost is 1 per game, as before.",0.8,N/R,"Outcome measured as ρ_c vs α,β; not normalized per endowment in text.",0.5,N/R,Not explicitly tabulated; only fraction of cooperators shown.,0.5,N/R,"No explicit efficiency metric; only boundaries (e.g., ρ_c=0.5 isocline) discussed.",0.4,N/R,No numerical payoffs reported.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation runs only.,1,"Two-layer square lattice model as before. Fraction of fair umpires fixed at ρ_f=0.5. Synergy factor fixed at r=1.3 (small r). Umpires distributed randomly. α ∈ (1, ∞) and β ∈ (0,1) varied over a grid; they report that for these data the ρ_c≈0.5 contour is near 2α+3β−3.3=0. Simulations show ρ_c increases monotonically in both α and β, with β having stronger effect; cooperators dominate (ρ_c≈1) when β exceeds a threshold (~0.71) independent of α.","Condensed from Sec. III C, including equation and threshold description.",1
10.1063_5.0088493.md,"Square lattice – varying fine cost α and bribe cost β at ρ_f=0.5, r=2.5","Section III C, Fig. 7(b) reports ρ_c as function of α and β for r=2.5, ρ_f=0.5.",1,"Fine cost α and bribe cost β varied with fraction of fair umpires ρ_f=0.5 and synergy factor r=2.5, random umpire distribution.","III C, Fig. 7(b) caption: r=2.5; α,β varied.",1,False,Computation only.,1,False,Not human experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations are used.,1,False,"No analytical solution for α,β panel.",0.9,10000,Same lattice size as main experiments.,1,20000,Simulation protocol unchanged.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect.,1,0,No automatic public contribution; contributions arise from strategy.,0.7,0.2,"Group size 5, PGG same form.",0.9,0,No communication channel.,1,0,No aggregated information shown.,1,0,Punishers not individually identified to agents.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,No horizon information.,1,1,Fair umpires impose α fines.,1,1,Cost to defector per game is α; structural unit cost 1×α.,0.6,1,Linear fine implementation.,0.8,0,No reward.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,Cooperation cost remains 1.,0.8,N/R,"Outcome is ρ_c across α,β; no explicit normalized rate reported.",0.5,N/R,Not numerically tabulated.,0.5,N/R,No efficiency metric displayed.,0.4,N/R,Payoffs not graphed.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation-only.,1,"Same two-layer lattice structure and update rule as baseline. ρ_f fixed at 0.5, r fixed at 2.5. Umpires randomly assigned as fair/corrupt consistent with ρ_f. α and β varied; Fig. 7(b) shows that ρ_c increases with both parameters and is generally higher than in r=1.3 case, often exceeding 0.6 even for modest α,β.",Based on description around Fig. 7(b).,1
10.1063_5.0088493.md,"Scale-free network – random umpires – varying ρ_f and r (α=1.5, β=0.2)","Appendix, Fig. 9 describes results for a scale-free network analogous to Fig. 2.",1,"Network topology (scale-free with BA construction) and synergy factor r, fraction of fair umpires ρ_f varied, with α=1.5, β=0.2.","Appendix: BA algorithm used; M=10000, k=4; ρ_f and r varied like square lattice case.",1,False,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,Not human data.,1,False,No laboratory environment.,1,True,Networks generated by Barabasi-Albert algorithm; dynamics simulated.,1,False,Results are simulation-based.,0.9,10000,Appendix: network size M is set to 10000.,1,20000,They state other parameter settings and number of experiments same as for square lattice (which used 20000 steps).,0.9,1,Same binary cooperator/defector strategies.,1,0,Public goods pool defined identically; contributions added from private costs.,0.7,N/R,Group size for each PGG on scale-free network is not explicitly specified; likely degree+1 but varies; no single MPCR value provided.,0.3,0,No communication mechanism introduced.,1,0,Agents use only local payoffs and imitation rule.,1,0,No identity features modeled.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents not given horizon.,1,1,Fair umpires fine defectors as in lattice model.,1,1,"Same payoff function, with α fine for defectors; base coin=1.",0.6,1,Linear fine application.,0.8,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewarding.,1,N/A,No rewarding.,1,1,Cooperation cost remains 1 per game.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes shown as ρ_c(r,ρ_f); not converted to normalized contribution rate beyond this.",0.5,N/R,No explicit amounts tabulated.,0.5,N/R,Efficiency differences between square and scale-free discussed qualitatively (lower thresholds) but not as numerical efficiency ratios.,0.4,N/R,No payoff numbers in Appendix figures.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations on generated networks.,1,"Two identical scale-free networks generated via Barabasi-Albert algorithm act as player and umpire layers. Network size M=10000, average degree k=4. Construction: each player and umpire node correspond one-to-one across layers. Dynamics identical to square lattice model except neighborhood structure: each player plays PGGs organized by neighbors in scale-free topology; supervised by corresponding umpires. α=1.5, β=0.2, initial ρ_c=0.5, ρ_d=0.5, umpires random with given ρ_f. Results in Fig. 9 show ρ_c as function of r and ρ_f, with cooperation thresholds generally lower than on square lattice.",From Appendix description: BA network details and comparison to square lattice.,1
10.1063_5.0088493.md,Scale-free network – varying fine cost α and bribe cost β at ρ_f=0.5,"Appendix, Fig. 10 shows ρ_c as function of α and β on scale-free network with ρ_f=0.5.",1,"Fine cost α and bribe cost β varied on a BA scale-free network with fraction of fair umpires ρ_f=0.5; synergy factor r same as in corresponding square lattice experiments (r=1.3 for panel a, r such that curves in b).","Appendix states other parameter settings same as Figs. 2 and 8; Fig. 10 mirrors α,β dependence.",0.7,False,Simulation-only.,1,False,Not empirical human data.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,BA scale-free network simulations as described in Appendix.,1,False,"No analytical results given for α,β tradeoffs.",0.9,10000,M set to 10000 in Appendix and reused.,1,20000,Simulation protocol same as square lattice; not restated but implied.,0.9,1,Binary strategies as before.,1,0,Same PGG payoff structure.,0.7,N/R,Group size varies with node degree on scale-free network; single MPCR not specified.,0.3,0,No communication.,1,0,Only local interactions and payoffs.,1,0,No identities.,1,0,No rewards.,1,0,Agents not given horizon.,1,1,Fair umpires levy fine α on defectors.,1,1,Same α-based fine mechanism.,0.6,1,Linear deduction from payoff.,0.8,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards to cost.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Cooperator pays cost 1 per PGG instance.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes reported as ρ_c(α,β); not normalized beyond this.",0.5,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts given.,0.5,N/R,"No efficiency ratio plotted for scale-free α,β experiments.",0.4,N/R,Group payoffs not reported.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations on BA networks.,1,"Two-layer BA scale-free multiplex identical in topology for players and umpires (M=10000, average degree k=4). Fraction of fair umpires fixed at ρ_f=0.5. α and β varied as in square lattice Fig. 8; Fig. 10(a,b) show ρ_c as function of α and β. Authors note that results are similar to square lattice but thresholds for cooperation are lower on scale-free networks, consistent with prior work that such networks promote cooperation.",Summarized from Appendix description and Fig. 10 caption.,1
10.1016_j.euroecorev.2024.104682.md,Single-group to multilevel – No punishment – Part 1 single-group PGG,"Condition A in Fig. 1: starts in single-group PGG, no punishment; first 10 rounds are single-group game.",0.97,Game structure: single-group PGG; Order: single-group then multilevel; Punishment: absent,"Design section describes 2x2 between-subjects: start game (single-group vs multilevel) × punishment (yes/no). This row is the block with single-group first and no punishment, during the single-group phase.",0.97,True,Behavioral lab experiment with 220 human participants at Utrecht University.,1,True,Researchers manipulate punishment and order of game types in controlled lab setting.,1,True,Conducted at Experimental Laboratory for Sociology and Economics (ELSE) on-site.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations described.,1,False,No formal analytical model as standalone study; focus is on experimental data analysis.,1,4,Groups of four participants in all PGGs.,1,10,Participants first play 10 rounds of either a single-group or multilevel PGG before group change.,1,1,"Participants allocate a 20 MU endowment between private, local, and global accounts in any amounts 0–20.",1,0,Endowment is received in a private account and then allocated; nothing is pre-committed to public accounts.,0.9,0.7,Single-group PGG: four members all same local group; each MU to local returns 0.7 to each of 4 ⇒ MPCR=0.7. Global contributions are inefficient and rarely used.,0.8,0,Participants were told not to communicate; no chat or communication tool mentioned.,1,1,"After each round, participants see how much each group member contributed to local and global accounts; this is feedback on others' actions/earnings.",0.9,0,"Punishment not available in this condition; by design in punishment conditions, punishment is anonymous.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,0,Number of rounds described as unknown to participants (between 8 and 12) to reduce endgame effects.,0.9,0,This is explicitly the no-punishment condition (Fig. 1A).,1,N/A,Punishment not available in this condition.,1,N/A,Punishment not available in this condition.,1,0,No peer reward option described anywhere.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"Each round, participants receive an endowment of 20 MU.",1,0.705,"Average total contribution in single-group PGG without punishment, single-group-to-multilevel condition is 14.10 MU (Table 1). Rate = 14.10 / 20 ≈ 0.705.",0.95,14.1,"Table 1, single-group PGGs, 'Total contributions' column under 'Without punishment Single-group to multilevel' = 14.10.",1,0.705,"In single-group PGG, efficiency-maximizing is full local contribution (20). Group payoff is linear in total contributions, so realized efficiency equals average contribution / 20 = 14.10/20.",0.7,N/R,Paper does not report aggregate group MU per round or per game.,0.1,"Mixed – majority Dutch, others various countries","Sample: 114 Dutch, 106 from various other countries; conditions assigned randomly with no further breakdown.",0.7,Mean 24 years (overall sample),Participants were on average 24 years old; no condition-specific age given.,0.9,"153 female, 63 male, 4 other (overall sample)",Gender breakdown given for entire sample; random assignment across conditions.,0.9,Mostly Utrecht University students,Almost all participants were taking courses at Utrecht University.,0.9,On site,Physical lab sessions at ELSE with individual cubicles.,1,"Single-group public goods game: all 4 players same color (one local group). Accounts: private (MPCR 1), local (MPCR 0.7), global (MPCR 0.5). Only local contributions are collectively efficient; global mostly unused. No punishment stage.",Summarized from Design section including payoff examples.,1
