10.1080_00220485.2018.1551097.md,Semester-long fisheries game – baseline open-access setting (no instructor-imposed policies),"Paper describes a single, semester-long classroom game with a common-pool fishery framed social dilemma; there are many endogenous student-created variations, but no distinct, systematically compared experimental treatments imposed by the authors. Treat this as one baseline condition.",0.8,"Open-access fishery with dual prize incentives; no exogenously imposed quotas, punishment, or aquaculture by instructor. Students can freely choose extraction, communication within fishery, and may endogenously create institutions (e.g., costly punishment, ITQs, aquaculture).","The core design has an unmanaged open-access fishery plus prize scheme. Other features (ITQs, aquaculture, punishment) are student-devised variations, not pre-specified factorial conditions.",0.8,True,Game is played with undergraduate students in real classes.,1,True,"Instructor sets rules, payoffs, information, and runs repeated decision rounds; this is a controlled classroom experiment, even though it is used pedagogically.",0.95,True,"Conducted in classroom / university setting, not in the field with real-world fishers.",0.95,False,No computer/agent simulations without humans are described.,1,False,"While a stock growth function is presented, the paper does not present a separate analytical model study; focus is on the implemented game.",0.9,5,"Groups (‘fisheries’) are between 5 and 10 students. The stock function and examples repeatedly assume 5 as the illustrative group size; use lower bound as representative, noting heterogeneity.",0.5,12,"Appendix notes: ‘historically, we have had 12 to 15 rounds a semester.’ The exact count in any given implementation is not fixed; choose 12 as conservative typical value, but this is approximate.",0.4,1,Each student can choose any integer catch between 0 and 10 fish; not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.,1,0,"There is no initial mandatory ‘contribution’ to a public pool; students decide how much to harvest (extract) from a shared stock. By the public-goods analogy, all endowment (harvest capacity) starts in their private control.",0.6,N/R,"This is a common-pool extraction game with a dynamic stock function, not a linear public goods game with a defined marginal per capita return parameter.",1,1,Students ‘discuss their harvest decisions with their group members’ before submitting private choices; this is cheap-talk communication.,1,1,"Instructor provides ‘total catch of each fishery, the fish stock available for the next round for their own fishery and the same for all other fisheries’ plus rankings of individual catches by ID.",1,0,"Baseline game has no formal punishment stage. Later, one cohort devised a costly punishment (coast guard) system but punishments are implemented by instructor without revealing individual identities to peers; IDs remain anonymous.",0.6,0,"Prizes are based on ID-coded cumulative catch; IDs, not real names, are displayed, so personal identities are not revealed to peers in reward allocation.",0.7,0,Total number of rounds is deliberately not announced; game end is determined via coin toss and kept unknown to prevent backward induction.,1,1,"The core instructor-designed game has no peer punishment. However, at least one cohort ‘set up a costly punishment system’ by sacrificing fish to create a coast guard who prevents poachers from playing. Since this is part of actual implemented play, costly punishment exists endogenously.",0.7,N/R,"Students ‘sacrificed some fish to the regulator’ to fund the coast guard, but the per-unit mapping from sacrificed fish to punishment strength is not quantified.",0.4,N/R,"Effectiveness is described qualitatively (poachers’ boats seized; poachers cannot play for several rounds), but no numeric conversion from cost to penalty units is given.",0.4,1,Dual monetary rewards: $25 gift card per member of best-managed (highest total catch) fishery and $50 gift card for top individual catcher.,1,N/R,"Rewards are fixed-value gift cards, not continuous per-unit rewards per contribution or extraction; per-unit cost is not defined.",0.5,N/R,"The ‘technology’ of rewards is a discrete prize for highest performance, not a linear mapping per unit; no such parameter is reported.",0.5,10,Each student ‘has a boat that allows them to catch a maximum of 10 fish’; this is the per-round individual capacity/endowment.,1,N/R,Paper reports illustrative strategy paths but provides no systematic aggregate contribution/extraction statistics.,0.2,N/R,"No summary statistics (means, medians) of individual or group catches over rounds or cohorts are reported.",0.2,N/R,"Although the social optimum is discussed (steady-state harvest at 50% of carrying capacity), no realized efficiency measures or averages relative to this optimum are presented.",0.2,N/R,Group performance is discussed qualitatively; no numeric payoff aggregates are reported.,0.2,USA,Authors are at University of Iowa and University of Nebraska-Lincoln and mention ‘students in the United States’; classes appear to be at US universities.,0.9,N/R,"Participants are undergraduates in economics/environmental courses, but no age distribution is provided.",0.4,N/R,No information on gender composition is given.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Game is used in ‘undergraduate environmental economics course’ and ‘interdisciplinary environmental studies courses’; described as undergraduate setting.,0.95,On site,Described as an in-person classroom game with physical buckets and post-it notes; online implementation is only suggested as a possible variant.,1,"Common-pool resource (open-access fishery) extraction game with dynamic, piecewise linear stock function: S_{t+1} = min[10 n_{t+1}, max{2[(S_t - X_t)/n_t] n_{t+1}, n_{t+1}}]. Group-specific fish stock is 10 * n_t carrying capacity; individual capacity is 10 fish per round. Groups of size 5–10, multiple parallel fisheries, repeated over ~12–15 rounds in a semester. Dual prize structure (group-level and individual-level) creates tension between sustainable group management and individual over-harvesting. Information: full feedback on each fishery’s total catch and next-round stock, and ranking of individual cumulative catches by anonymized student ID. Communication within groups is allowed but nonbinding. Overfishing beyond available stock is corrected by instructor according to specified rules (either uniform downward adjustment or largest-catch-first reduction). Students may poach between fisheries by misplacing post-its and can propose bottom-up institutional changes (e.g., ITQs, aquaculture, costly punishment via coast guard). Game endpoint is uncertain, determined via repeated coin toss near semester end.","Summarizes the detailed game rules, stock function, information structure, overfishing adjustment, and endogenous institutional innovations described throughout the paper and appendices.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0263028.md,Control – deterministic punishment (β = 3),Control condition described as having fixed punishment enhancement factor β = 3.,1,Punishment noise amplitude σ = 0; punishment enhancement factor β fixed at 3,Independent variable is punishment noise; control has no noise (deterministic β = 3).,1,True,Human participants recruited via Prolific played the game.,1,True,Controlled randomized assignment to punishment-noise conditions.,1,False,"Game conducted online using oTree and Prolific, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Data come from human subjects, not simulations.",1,False,No purely analytical/mathematical model as main method.,1,4,Participants played in groups of four.,1,20,22 rounds played but first two were practice and did not count toward final income; main repeated game is 20 rounds.,0.9,1,"Players could contribute any amount from 0 to 10 MU, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each round participants were given 10 Coins and could choose any amount; endowment starts in private account.,1,0.5,Public good multiplier r = 2 divided by group size 4 gives MPCR = 0.5.,1,0,No communication or chat between participants is mentioned.,0.9,1,"After stage 2, participants saw effects of reduction payments and punishment received by others, implying summaries of others' punishment/earnings.",0.8,0,Groups were anonymous and only contributions/punishment per player index were shown; no identities revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,The actual number of rounds was not communicated to avoid end-game effects.,1,1,Second-stage costly punishment explicitly implemented.,1,1,Participants spent 1 MU (Coin) per unit they chose to punish; this is the cost per coin.,1,3,Each MU spent to punish translated into a fine of 3 MU in control.,1,0,No option to increase others’ income; only reduction/punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Each round participants received 10 MU/Coins to allocate between private and public accounts.,1,N/R,Paper reports averages and distributions but no single normalized rate per condition that can be extracted numerically.,0,N/R,Only qualitative/graphical information on average contributions is provided; no specific numeric mean per condition.,0,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to maximum possible) not numerically reported per condition.,0,10.1,Mean payoff per round in control reported as 10.1 MU (Fig 2 inset). For a representative player this is also average group payoff per player.,1,"Multiple (41 countries, mainly Prolific online sample)",320 participants from 41 countries are reported; individual country breakdown not given.,0.9,N/R,Age information not reported.,0,"Sex ratio ≈ 2:1 (male:female or vice versa, direction not specified)",Paper notes sex ratio ≃ 2:1 but not which sex is majority.,0.5,N/R,No education information reported.,0,Online,Experiments conducted using oTree and participants recruited via Prolific.,1,Stranger matching; 20 paid rounds plus 2 unpaid practice rounds; groups of 4 reshuffled each round; initial show-up endowment 200 Coins (£0.01 each) plus 10 Coins per round; public good multiplier r = 2; punishment up to 10 Coins per target player per round; number of rounds not revealed to subjects.,Summarizes additional procedural and payoff structure details from Methods.,1
