10.1038_s41598-021-04217-4.md,Prisoner's Dilemma with punishment – No-load condition,Paper describes a single study with two between-subjects conditions: no-load vs cognitive-load. This row is the no-load condition.,1,Cognitive load manipulation: no concurrent tone-classification task (no-load condition),Independent variable is presence vs absence of continuous tone classification task; this condition had no additional task.,1,True,Human participants played an incentivized Prisoner's Dilemma game.,1,True,Cognitive load was experimentally manipulated between participants; controlled game structure.,1,True,Participants arrived at the laboratory and used lab computers/headphones.,1,False,"Although partners were computer-controlled, the focal data are human decisions.",1,False,No purely analytical or closed-form model as the only method; main results are empirical/model-based estimates.,1,2,Standard simultaneous Prisoner's Dilemma with a participant and one partner.,1,14,"Participants played 20 one-shot trials with different partners; first 3 interactions with each type (cooperator/defector) were training and excluded from analysis, leaving 14 analyzed trials.",0.8,0,Choices are binary cooperate (invest 30 cents) or defect (invest 0); no continuous contributions.,1,0,Endowment (400 cents) is held in private account; players choose whether to invest 30 cents.,1,0.3333333333,Bonus is 1/3 of total investments and split equally; each invested cent returns 1/3 cent to each player; MPCR = (1/3)/1 = 1/3.,1,0,No mention of any communication or chat; only computer interface.,1,0,"Participants see both players’ account balances and gains/losses for the current trial, but no aggregate summaries across rounds.",0.7,0,Partners are anonymous faces; no stable identifiers beyond current face; punishment is not linked to persistent IDs.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,Text does not indicate that total or remaining number of trials is displayed.,0.5,1,"After each Prisoner's Dilemma outcome, participants can spend cents to reduce partner’s payoff.",1,1,Participants invest 0–9 cents; each cent spent reduces own account by 1 cent.,1,10,"For each cent invested, 10 cents are subtracted from partner's account.",1,0,Only costly punishment is described; no option to increase partner’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,400,"At beginning, each participant was endowed with 400 cents.",1,N/R,Model reports cooperation parameter C but not as a raw fraction of endowment or explicit numeric value.,0.3,N/R,"Per-round cooperation is binary (0 or 30 cents), but average amount is not numerically reported.",0.3,N/R,No aggregate payoff ratio vs maximum is provided.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs across rounds are not given as numeric outcomes.,0.1,Germany,"Study conducted at Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Germany.",1,Mean age 23 years (SD = 4),Reported in Participants section.,1,"125 female, 81 male (total N=206 across both conditions)","Participant section reports 206 participants, 125 female; no further breakdown by condition.",0.7,University students or individuals receiving course credit/monetary compensation (exact distribution not reported),Participants recruited at a university and could receive course credit or monetary compensation.,0.6,On site,Participants arrived at the laboratory and used lab equipment and headphones.,1,"One-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma with costly punishment, 20 unique partners, 10 cooperators and 10 defectors per participant (first 3 of each type as training). Cooperation: investing 30 cents; defection: investing 0. Bonus is 1/3 of the sum of investments, added to the pot and split equally. Mutual cooperation: both gain 10 cents; mutual defection: no change; unilateral cooperation: cooperator loses 10 cents, defector gains 20 cents. Partners are computer-controlled and always morally punish unilateral defection with 1–9 cents; they never punish otherwise.","Summarizes detailed procedural description of PD payoff structure, partner behavior, and trial design.",1
10.1038_s41598-021-04217-4.md,Prisoner's Dilemma with punishment – Cognitive-load condition,Same game as no-load but with added continuous tone classification task to induce cognitive load.,1,Cognitive load manipulation: concurrent continuous tone-classification task (cognitive-load condition),Independent variable is cognitive load; this condition performed the tone task during the game.,1,True,Human participants played the game under cognitive load.,1,True,Between-subjects manipulation of cognitive load.,1,True,Conducted in a university lab with computers and headphones.,1,False,Human subjects generate primary data.,1,False,No purely analytical-only study.,1,2,Dyadic Prisoner's Dilemma with participant and one partner.,1,14,"Same trial structure as no-load condition: 20 one-shot interactions, first 3 of each partner type as training, leaving 14 analyzed trials.",0.8,0,Binary cooperate (invest 30 cents) vs defect (0).,1,0,Endowment starts in private account; players decide to invest.,1,0.3333333333,Same payoff structure as in no-load condition; bonus is 1/3 of total investments.,1,0,No communication facility is described.,1,0,Only current-trial balances and gains/losses shown; no cumulative summaries.,0.7,0,Partners are represented by transient faces without persistent identifiers; punishment not linked to stable IDs.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,Text does not report any display of total or remaining number of trials.,0.5,1,Same costly punishment option as no-load condition.,1,1,Participants spend 1 cent of own payoff per punishment unit.,1,10,Each cent invested reduces partner's balance by 10 cents.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent.,1,400,Same initial endowment as in no-load condition.,1,N/R,Only qualitative statement that cooperation was lower than in no-load; no numeric rate reported.,0.3,N/R,Average invested cents per round are not numerically reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric provided.,0.1,N/R,No numeric group payoff values reported.,0.1,Germany,"Same participant pool; study at Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Germany.",1,Mean age 23 years (SD = 4) across full sample; condition-specific means not reported,Only overall age described; no per-condition breakdown.,0.7,"125 female, 81 male (total N=206 across both conditions; condition-specific gender not reported)",Gender counts given only for full sample.,0.7,University students or individuals receiving course credit/monetary compensation (exact distribution not reported),Same recruitment context as no-load condition.,0.6,On site,Same lab setting as no-load condition.,1,"Identical Prisoner’s Dilemma with punishment as in no-load condition, but participants simultaneously performed a continuous tone classification task. Tones C1, F3, B6 mapped to A/S/D keys; tones repeated every second until correct classification. Average performance: 92% correct (SD=5) with mean RT=1560 ms (SD=397). Participants received feedback on tone task accuracy and RT after each trial. Four participants with <75% accuracy were excluded.",Summarizes the cognitive-load manipulation and its performance metrics while noting identical game structure.,1
10.1093_scan_nsab124.md,Revised repeated ultimatum game (rrUG),One of the two main game conditions; ultimatum version with external punishment via monetarily effective rejection.,1,Game type with external punishment: revised repeated ultimatum game (rejection by responder voids payoffs).,Study contrasts rrUG (punishment institution present) vs rrDG (no monetary effect of rejection). This row is the rrUG condition.,1,True,Human participants played the games while EEG was recorded.,1,True,Controlled laboratory task with randomized offers and specified rules.,1,True,Conducted in experimental rooms at Fuzhou University with EEG equipment; not a field or online study.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only human task plus EEG analysis.,1,False,No formal analytical/mathematical model developed; focus is empirical EEG and behavior.,1,2,Dyads of one proposer and one responder played each game.,1,120,Each game (including rrUG) was played 120 times.,1,0,"Offers were exogenously set to 2:8, 5:5, or 8:2 by the computer; proposers could only confirm or give up, not choose any amount.",0.9,N/A,"No public-goods style contribution; this is a bargaining/allocation task, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR reported or applicable.,1,0,Participants were in separate rooms with no mention of communication channels.,0.9,0,Outcome screens showed only current trial earnings or hash marks; no summaries of others’ cumulative outcomes or punishments.,0.9,0,"Punishment occurs via rejection but is inherent in the dyad; identities are fixed and known only as roles, no separate punishment identity display.",0.7,0,No reward mechanism exists in the game.,1,0,Procedure description does not mention displaying remaining or total rounds to participants.,0.5,1,"In rrUG, responder rejection makes both players earn nothing, which is treated as an external punishment institution.",1,N/R,Cost per unit of punishment is not framed in token terms; rejection just voids the entire offer.,0.3,N/R,Magnitude of punishment per unit cost is not parameterized; rejection results in zero payoff without a numeric mapping.,0.3,0,No costly reward or bonus mechanism for peers is included.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,10,"Each trial presents a divided color pie representing 10 CNY (total to be split 2:8, 5:5, or 8:2).",1,N/R,No contributions relative to an endowment are reported; game is about accepting or rejecting exogenous offers.,1,N/R,"Behavioral DV reported is mean offer and rejection rates, not voluntary contribution amounts.",1,N/R,No aggregate efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is reported or derivable from provided statistics.,0.3,N/R,Only mean offers and rejection rates are provided; average dyad payoffs per game are not reported.,0.3,China,Participants were recruited from Fuzhou University in China.,1,"Mean 19.52 years (SD 1.89), healthy male university students",Participant description: 'Forty-four healthy male participants (aged 19.52 ± 1.89 years) were recruited from Fuzhou University.',1,All male,Text specifies 'Forty-four healthy male participants'; females were excluded intentionally.,1,University students,"Participants were recruited from Fuzhou University, implying current university enrollment.",0.9,On site,Participants were seated in separate experimental rooms with lab EEG systems at the university.,1,"Dyadic revised repeated ultimatum game with exogenously drawn offers (2:8, 5:5, 8:2) over 120 trials; proposer either confirms or gives up the computer-chosen offer; responder accepts or rejects; in rrUG, rejection makes both earn 0 for the trial, acceptance implements the shown division; if proposer gives up, trial does not count toward earnings.",Summarized from Task and Procedure sections describing rrUG rules.,1
10.1093_scan_nsab124.md,Revised repeated dictator game (rrDG),Second main game condition; dictator-style version without external punishment (rejection has no monetary effect).,1,Game type without external punishment: revised repeated dictator game (rejection by responder has no monetary effect).,Study contrasts rrDG (no monetary effect of rejection) vs rrUG (monetary effect of rejection). This row is the rrDG condition.,1,True,Human dyads played rrDG while EEG data were recorded.,1,True,Controlled laboratory economic game with random offers and fixed rules.,1,True,Conducted in experimental rooms at the university with EEG systems.,1,False,No computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical model; focus on empirical EEG and behavior.,1,2,Same dyadic structure of proposer and responder as in rrUG.,1,120,"Each game, including rrDG, was played 120 times.",1,0,"Offers are fixed by the computer (2:8, 5:5, 8:2); proposer can only confirm or give up, not choose arbitrary splits.",0.9,N/A,Not a contribution/public goods setting; fixed-sum allocation game.,1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier; not reported.,1,0,Participants were isolated in separate rooms with no described communication.,0.9,0,"Outcome screen only shows current trial earnings or hash marks, no cumulative summaries.",0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in rrDG; responder rejection is non-monetary only.,1,0,No costly reward institution described.,1,0,No mention that total or remaining rounds are displayed.,0.5,0,"In rrDG, responders’ rejection has no monetary effect; authors treat this as absence of external punishment.",1,N/A,No monetary punishment mechanism exists in this condition.,1,N/A,No monetary punishment present.,1,0,No peer reward mechanism is available.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,10,"Same 10 CNY pie as in rrUG, divided 2:8, 5:5, or 8:2.",1,N/R,"Outcomes measured are mean offers (exogenous) and rejection rates, not contributions.",1,N/R,No voluntary contribution amounts are reported.,1,N/R,No efficiency measure is reported and insufficient data to compute average payoffs.,0.3,N/R,"Behavioral section reports mean offers and rejection rates, not mean total payoffs.",0.3,China,Same participant pool as rrUG from Fuzhou University in China.,1,"Mean 19.52 years (SD 1.89), healthy male university students",Identical participant group played both games.,1,All male,Same all-male sample used in both conditions.,1,University students,Recruited from Fuzhou University.,0.9,On site,Same on-site laboratory environment as rrUG.,1,"Dyadic revised repeated dictator game using exogenous offers (2:8, 5:5, 8:2) across 120 trials; proposer confirms or gives up computer-chosen offer; responder accepts or rejects; in rrDG, responder’s acceptance/rejection has no monetary effect, the offer is executed whenever the proposer confirms; if proposer gives up, trial is excluded from payoff calculations but kept for data analysis.",Summarized from Task and Procedure sections describing rrDG rules.,1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,Study 1 – Baseline honor-culture simulation with all 4 strategies present,"Study 1 describes a baseline ABM condition with Strong Reciprocators (Honorables), Trusters, Exploitive Egoists, and Opportunists all starting at 25% each and parameters constrained so Coop gain < Sucker payment < Punish Amount.",1,Honor culture payoff structure with punishment exceeding cheating gains (Coop gain < Sucker payment < Punish Amount); all four agent types initially at 25%.,Baseline simulation is defined by these payoff constraints and initial equal proportions.,1,False,"Agents are computer-simulated, not human participants.",1,False,"Simulation study using parameter sweeps, not a controlled human or field experiment.",1,False,No human lab setting; purely computational.,1,True,Described explicitly as an agent-based model implemented in NetLogo.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution; results from numerical simulation.,1,4800,"Each simulation starts with 4,800 agents in two-dimensional space.",1,30000,"They “ran the simulation to 30,000 turns,” one interaction opportunity per turn.",1,1,"Model uses abstract payoffs (Coop gain, Sucker payment, etc.) without explicit discrete contribution choices; treat as non-all-or-nothing continuous/strategic choice.",0.3,N/A,Public-goods style contribution endowments are not modeled; interactions are payoff-matrix based.,1,N/R,No linear public-goods multiplier or MPCR is defined; only pairwise payoff parameters.,1,N/A,No human subjects; no communication channel is modeled.,1,N/A,Simulation agents do not have an interface; such feedback is not applicable.,1,N/A,Agents do not have identities in a human-perceptual sense.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards beyond payoff structure; no identities.,1,N/A,Agents do not have awareness of time horizon.,1,1,Strong Reciprocators and Exploitive Egoists punish; Punish Amount and Feud rounds are key parameters.,1,N/R,Model specifies Punish Amount as a payoff to the punished; explicit cost-to-punisher per unit is not separately parameterized.,0.2,N/R,No explicit mapping of punisher expenditure to victim loss is provided beyond aggregated Punish Amount.,0.2,0,"Only punishment, appeasement, and cooperation are modeled; no separate reward stage.",1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist as a mechanism.,1,N/R,"Agents have ‘resources’ 0–15 as life/reproduction capital, not per-round monetary endowments in a contribution game.",0.4,N/R,"Outcomes tracked are proportions of strategy types and voluntary cooperation counts, not normalized contribution fractions.",1,N/R,No explicit per-round contribution amounts are reported.,1,N/R,They do not compute total payoff relative to a theoretical maximum efficiency benchmark.,1,N/R,"They report proportions of strategies and counts of interactions, not aggregate payoff sums.",1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model only.,1,"Agent-based evolutionary game with four fixed strategies (Strong Reciprocator/Honorable, Truster, Exploitive Egoist, Opportunist), random pairwise interactions each turn, resource-based death at 0 and reproduction at 15, carrying capacity 5000, 1000 independent runs of 30000 turns under honor culture constraint Coop gain < Sucker payment < Punish Amount.","Summarizes detailed interaction, movement, reproduction, and payoff structure as described in Study 1.",1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,Study 1 – Simulation without Opportunists,Authors explicitly run a variant where Opportunists are removed from the initial and subsequent population to examine their role.,1,"Removal of Opportunist strategy; initial population split among Strong Reciprocators, Trusters, Exploitive Egoists only under honor-culture payoff constraint.",Condition differs from baseline by absence of Opportunists; other rules unchanged.,1,False,Agent-based simulation with no humans.,1,False,"Computational parameter variation, not a human experiment.",1,False,Pure simulation.,1,True,"Same NetLogo ABM as baseline, with one strategy type removed.",1,False,No closed-form derivation of outcomes.,1,4800,"They state all simulations begin with 4,800 agents; no exception for this variant.",0.9,30000,Same run length is used for all Study 1 simulations.,1,1,Same abstract payoff-matrix interaction structure as baseline.,0.3,N/A,No contribution endowment game.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,Not applicable to simulation agents.,1,N/A,No identities as perceived by humans.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,Agents lack awareness of horizon.,1,1,Strong Reciprocators and Exploitive Egoists still punish.,1,N/R,Separate punisher cost is not specified.,0.2,N/R,Only aggregate Punish Amount appears.,0.2,0,No modeled rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Resources 0–15 as in baseline, but not reported separately for this variant.",0.4,N/R,Outcomes are proportions of strategies; no explicit contributions.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not computed.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Agent-based model.,1,"Same ABM as baseline, but Opportunist type is entirely removed. After evolution over 30,000 turns and 1,000 runs, final mean composition: 3.5% Honorables, 78.6% Trusters, 16.8% Exploitive Egoists; Honorables nearly disappear and dynamics approximate a Hawk–Dove game.",Summarizes Table 3 line ‘No Opportunists’ and associated text.,1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,Study 1 – Simulation without Honorables,Authors run another variation where Strong Reciprocators (Honorables) are removed to assess their necessity.,1,"Removal of Honorable/Strong Reciprocator strategy; initial population consists of Trusters, Exploitive Egoists, Opportunists only under honor-culture payoff constraint.",Differentiated from baseline by absence of Honorables; other parameters unchanged.,1,False,No human subjects; computational agents only.,1,False,Simulation parameter change only.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,Described as simulation variant within Study 1.,1,False,No analytical solution.,1,4800,Same starting number of agents is used unless otherwise indicated.,0.9,30000,"Same 30,000 turns as baseline.",1,1,Same non-binary interaction framework.,0.3,N/A,No contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Not relevant to agents.,1,N/A,No identities in human sense.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents lack such information.,1,1,Exploitative Egoists still punish; honor constraint still used.,1,N/R,Punisher’s cost not given.,0.2,N/R,Only Punish Amount outcome is specified.,0.2,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Same resource 0–15 framework implied, but not restated.",0.4,N/R,"Outcome is composition of strategies, not contributions.",1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not computed.,1,N/R,Not provided.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same ABM as baseline but with Strong Reciprocators removed. After evolution, population is dominated by Opportunists (70.6%) and Exploitive Egoists (27.6%), with Trusters essentially eliminated and no positive reciprocity remaining.",Based on Table 3 line ‘No Honorables’ and supporting text.,1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,Study 2 – Social capital simulation with free punishment parameter (full sample over 3000 runs),"Study 2 describes a second ABM where Punish Amount is unconstrained relative to Sucker payment and Coop gain, run 3,000 times.",1,"Wide random variation of eight payoff parameters including Punish Amount and Appease offer, with only Sucker payment > Coop gain constraint; initial four strategies each at 25%.",Study 2 manipulates these ecological parameters to examine effects on voluntary cooperation (social capital).,1,False,Again an agent-based model; no human participants.,1,False,"Computational parameter analysis, not human experiment.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,"Described explicitly as using the same ABM with modified constraints and 3,000 simulation runs.",1,False,"Results are from regression analyses on simulation outputs, not analytical solution.",1,4800,"They state they use the same basic model from Study 1; initial 4,800 agents unchanged.",0.9,30000,They keep the same run length per simulation instance unless noted otherwise.,0.9,1,Still pairwise payoff interactions; no all-or-nothing constraint.,0.3,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,Simulation agents do not communicate.,1,N/A,Not meaningful for nonhuman agents.,1,N/A,Identities not modeled as in human games.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,Agents lack horizon awareness.,1,1,"Punish Amount, Feud rounds remain parameters; punishing strategies unchanged.",1,N/R,Only punished party’s cost via Punish Amount; punisher’s cost not parameterized separately.,0.2,N/R,No explicit cost-to-damage multiplier is specified.,0.2,0,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Same resource-life system; not re-specified.,0.4,N/R,"Main DV is total amount of voluntary cooperation (frequency of HH, HT, TT interaction types in last round).",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts reported.,1,N/R,They do not normalize payoffs against maximum cooperative outcome.,1,N/R,"They analyze counts of voluntary and coerced cooperation, not aggregate payoffs.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulations only.,1,"Same four-strategy ABM as Study 1 but removes constraints Punish Amount > Sucker payment and Punish Amount > Coop gain. Only Sucker payment > Coop gain is enforced. 3,000 runs. Main regressions show that increasing Punish Amount (severity) and Appease offer (standard for avoiding punishment) both significantly reduce total voluntary cooperation; severity of punishment also reduces total cooperation (voluntary+coerced). Effects are mediated by changes in prevalence of Strong Reciprocators, Trusters, Exploitive Egoists, and Opportunists as reported in Table 4.",Summarizes Study 2 method and key DV definition (voluntary cooperation index).,1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,"Study 2 – Subcondition: Punish Amount < Sucker payment, feud still worst outcome (Chicken-like cases with Opportunists present)",Table 5 breaks out a subset of Study 2 runs where Punish < Sucker but Feud rounds × Punish > Sucker (feud remains worst outcome) and Opportunists are included.,1,Subset of Study 2 parameter draws where Punish Amount < Sucker payment and Feud rounds × Punish Amount > Sucker payment; Opportunists initially present.,These parameter inequalities define this Chicken-like subset; composition results are reported separately in Table 5.,1,False,Still part of the ABM.,1,False,Subset analysis of simulation runs.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Derived from agent-based simulation output.,1,False,No closed-form solution.,1,4800,Same underlying ABM as Study 2.,0.9,30000,Inherited from base model.,0.9,1,Same interaction rules.,0.3,N/A,No explicit contributions.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Agents lack such info.,1,1,Punish Amount and feuds still operate; only magnitude constraints differ.,1,N/R,Not separately given.,0.2,N/R,No unit cost-to-harm parameter.,0.2,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Resources as in base, not restated.",0.4,N/R,"DV is composition and voluntary cooperation, not contributions.",1,N/R,Not given.,1,N/R,Not computed.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Subset of simulation runs.,1,"Within Study 2, this subset has Strong Reciprocators 49.0%, Trusters 23.6%, Exploitive Egoists 10.6%, Opportunists 15.3% at the end of simulations (Table 5). Despite Punish Amount < Sucker payment, feuds remain the worst outcome due to Feud rounds multiplier, so payoffs resemble Chicken; Strong Reciprocators become the most common type.",Directly from Table 5 and surrounding explanation.,1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,"Study 2 – Subcondition: Punish Amount < Sucker payment, sucker payoff worst (PD-like cases with Opportunists present)","Table 5 also reports cases where Punish < Sucker and Feud rounds × Punish < Sucker, so the ‘Sucker’s payoff’ is worst.",1,Subset of Study 2 parameter draws where Punish Amount < Sucker payment and Feud rounds × Punish Amount < Sucker payment; Opportunists initially present.,These inequalities define the PD-like region examined in Table 5.,1,False,Still ABM results.,1,False,"Subset of simulations, not a separate human experiment.",1,False,No lab.,1,True,Agent-based computational subset.,1,False,No closed-form results.,1,4800,Same model as Study 2.,0.9,30000,Inherited from Study 2.,0.9,1,Same interaction structure.,0.3,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,Agents unaware of horizon.,1,1,"Punishment still included as a parameter, though relatively mild.",1,N/R,Not specified.,0.2,N/R,No such mapping reported.,0.2,0,No explicit reward stage.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Same life-resource system as base.,0.4,N/R,Outcome reported as proportions of strategy types.,1,N/R,Not specified.,1,N/R,Not calculated.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation subset.,1,"In this PD-like subset with Opportunists present, final composition is 64.5% Strong Reciprocators, 20.6% Trusters, 11.4% Exploitive Egoists, and 2.8% Opportunists (Table 5). Here, feuds are less costly than being the sucker, making the Sucker’s payoff worst and enabling Strong Reciprocators to reach majority status while punishment is relatively mild.",Directly from Table 5 and explanation about worst outcome switching from feud to Sucker payoff.,1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,"Study 3 – Human experiment: Medium punishment, Opportunists present","Within Study 3’s 2×3 design (Opportunists present vs absent × low/medium/high punishment), this condition has Opportunists available and uses median punishment.",0.95,"Human participants choose among 4 strategies (Honorable, Truster, Exploitive Egoist, Opportunist) over 50 rounds; punishment parameter in payoff matrix set to median level from Study 1 distribution; Opportunists available.","Text states punishment was manipulated at low, median, high; this row isolates the medium-punishment, four-strategy case used as a comparison baseline in Table 6 and Table 7.",0.9,True,This is the in vivo experiment using Amazon MTurk participants.,1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to punishment-level and Opportunist-presence conditions; controlled payoff matrices and instructions.,1,False,"Conducted online via MTurk, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Although other ‘players’ were bots, focal data are human decisions.",1,False,No closed-form derivations; behavioral experiment.,1,35,"Each human participant is told they play with 34 other players including themselves, i.e., groups of 35 (1 human + 34 bots).",1,50,Participants played 50 rounds of the strategy-choice game.,1,1,"Participants select a discrete strategy each round, not continuous contribution amounts; not a binary contribute/no-contribute endowment choice, so encoded as non-all-or-nothing.",0.4,0,"There is no initial public fund; players start each round with zero points and earn or lose based on pairwise interactions, analogous to all resources being in private accounts.",0.4,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; interactions are pairwise matrix payoffs.,1,0,No communication between participants is mentioned; only payoff and distribution feedback screens.,1,1,"Participants always see what their paired partner chose and both payoffs each round; some also see distribution of strategies in the population, which is a summary of others’ choices.",0.8,0,Other players are anonymous; no identities revealed when punishment is effectively applied via strategy interaction.,1,0,No separate reward stage or identity information.,1,1,Participants are told there will be 50 rounds; standard practice and wording imply a known horizon.,0.7,1,"Two strategies explicitly retaliate (Strategies 1 and 3), with payoffs incorporating punishment parameters.",1,N/R,The per-unit cost for punishing to the punisher is not reported; only total payoffs are in the matrix.,0.2,N/R,Magnitude of harm per point cost is not decomposed; only net point outcomes are shown.,0.2,0,Only cooperation and punishment are discussed; no prosocial reward action stage.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/R,Participants do not receive a per-round token endowment; they accumulate points through payoffs; initial point level each round is effectively zero but not specified as an endowment.,0.3,N/R,"DV is voluntary cooperation probability inferred from strategy choice proportions, not normalized contribution fraction of a numeric endowment.",1,N/R,No token contributions to a common pool; only strategy choices and resulting payoffs.,1,N/R,They do not compute actual payoffs vs maximum possible as a fraction.,1,N/R,"Results emphasize proportions of strategies and derived cooperation index, not group payoffs; average additional compensation ($3.28) is across all conditions combined, not this cell.",0.2,United States,All participants are described as U.S. adults on MTurk.,1,Mean 42.36 (SD 13.03) years; adults only,Reported overall sample stats; design did not significantly vary by condition in text; use full-sample values.,0.9,44.7% female; remainder primarily male with a small % other/unspecified,"Overall gender balance reported for full sample, not by condition.",0.9,N/R,Education distribution is not reported.,1,Online,Study run via Amazon MTurk with a web-based game server.,1,"Factorial design: presence vs absence of Opportunists × punishment level (low, medium, high). In this medium-punishment, Opportunists-present condition, participants choose among 4 labeled strategies (Strategy 1 Honorable, 2 Truster, 3 Exploitive Egoist, 4 Opportunist) each of 50 rounds, facing simulated partners whose choices follow distributions based on the Study 1 simulation. Payoff matrix is fixed and shown each round. Average strategy use in medium punishment: Honorable 0.26, Truster 0.15, Exploitive Egoist 0.08, Opportunist 0.51 (Table 6). Voluntary cooperation index is computed as P(H)² + P(T)² + 2·P(H)·P(T) from these human-chosen strategy frequencies.","Condenses Study 3 procedure and medium-punishment, four-strategy outcome numbers.",1
10.1037_pspa0000301.md,"Study 3 – Human experiment: Low punishment, Opportunists present",Same 4-strategy human game as above but with punishment set to low (1.2 SD below Study 1 mean).,0.95,4-strategy human choice game with punishment parameter set to low level (1.2 SD below Study 1 mean Punish Amount); Opportunists available.,Described in ‘Validating the Punishment → Voluntary Cooperation Hypothesis’ as the low-punishment condition.,1,True,Same MTurk experiment as other Study 3 cells.,1,True,Random assignment to punishment-level conditions.,1,False,Online MTurk setting.,1,False,"Humans choose, bots implement environment.",1,False,Behavioral experiment.,1,35,Same 1 human + 34 bot players per game.,1,50,Same for all Study 3 conditions.,1,1,Discrete strategy selection.,0.4,0,No public endowment pool.,0.4,N/R,Not a public goods setting.,1,0,No communication allowed.,1,1,All see partner’s choice and payoffs; some see global distributions.,0.8,0,No IDs.,1,0,No reward actions.,1,1,Game is described as 50 rounds.,0.7,1,Punishing strategies 1 and 3 available.,1,N/R,Not decomposed.,0.2,N/R,Not decomposed.,0.2,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No per-round token endowment.,0.3,N/R,"Cooperation measured via strategy frequencies, not endowment fractions.",1,N/R,No explicit contributions.,1,N/R,Not calculated.,1,N/R,Not reported per condition.,0.2,United States,All participants are U.S. MTurk workers.,1,Mean 42.36 (SD 13.03) years; adults only,Only overall sample stats provided.,0.9,44.7% female; remainder primarily male with small % other/unspecified,Reported for full sample.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,1,Online,Conducted via MTurk.,1,"Same interface and bots as other punishment levels. Low punishment is defined using a Punish Amount 1.2 SD below the Study 1 mean. Observed mean strategy use: Honorable 0.30, Truster 0.14, Exploitive Egoist 0.07, Opportunist 0.48 (Table 6). Punishment correlates negatively with voluntary cooperation across the three punishment levels, r = -0.17, so this low-punishment condition has the highest voluntary cooperation probability among the three.",Directly from Table 6 and punishment-correlation description.,1
