punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,Only costly deductions are mentioned; no positive reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,"“At the start of each round, all insiders and outsiders received an identical endowment of 50 Experimental Dollars (EDs).”",1,N/R,"Paper reports harvest (investment in shared account) and poaching, but not normalized contribution fractions.",0.2,29.74,Table 2: Assigned-No Punishment insiders’ mean harvest = 29.74.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio or enough aggregate payoff/maximum data per treatment to compute it.,0.2,N/R,"Only average individual payoffs by role and treatment are reported, not total group payoff.",0.3,United States,Subjects recruited from undergraduate population at University of Massachusetts Amherst in the USA.,0.95,N/R,No age information provided; only that subjects are undergraduates.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.01,Undergraduate students,Subjects recruited from undergraduate population at the university.,0.95,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental laboratory using z-Tree.,1,"Common-pool resource game with 4 insiders and 2 outsiders in fixed groups of 6. Each round insiders receive 50 EDs and choose investment in The Account; total insider investment generates surplus V(G)=aG−bG² with a=6, b=0.025, which is shared among insiders proportional to their investment. Outsiders observe The Account and may each withdraw up to 25% of The Account (so maximum total poaching is 50% of The Account). Outsiders also have 50 EDs to allocate between private good (return 1) and poaching (linear in surplus with w=0.01 in the theoretical model). In this treatment property rights (insider vs outsider) are randomly assigned after the slider task, and no punishment or deductions are available. 15 repeated rounds with insider-only pre-harvest chat each round.","Summary drawn from design description, parameter listing, and stage description specific to Assigned and No Punishment.",1
10.1016_j.joep.2021.102452.md,Assigned – Punishment,One of four treatments: Assigned allocation with punishment stage enabled.,1,Property-right allocation: Assigned (random); Punishment: Peer punishment available to insiders (1:3 deduction technology).,Differs from other treatments by random allocation and presence of sanctions.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment; this is a treatment arm.,1,True,Controlled between-subject manipulation of allocation method and punishment.,1,True,Conducted in university experimental economics laboratory.,1,False,No separate simulation reported for this condition.,1,False,Analytical benchmarks exist but this record refers to the empirical treatment.,1,6,"Groups of six: four insiders, two outsiders, fixed across 15 rounds.",1,15,Same across all four treatments.,1,1,Continuous choice of investment and poaching amounts.,1,0,Endowment starts in private account each round before decisions.,1,N/R,Nonlinear surplus; no constant marginal per capita return stated.,1,1,Insiders communicate via chat at the start of each round in every treatment.,1,1,"All players view The Account value, total investment, total outsider withdrawals, and payoffs before punishment decisions.",0.9,0,Instructions mention IDs (Type and ID numbers) but do not indicate revelation of real identities; sanctions directed by ID only.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Subjects receive full instructions for both parts and total number of rounds; standard in lab experiments.,0.6,1,"In Punishment treatments, insiders can levy deductions on insiders and outsiders.",1,1,Standard 1:3 technology: paying 1 ED reduces target payoff by 3 ED.,1,3,"For each 1 ED spent, target loses 3 ED.",1,0,Only costly deductions exist; no positive rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Per-round endowment of 50 EDs for all insiders and outsiders.,1,N/R,"Harvest reported only in EDs, not normalized to endowment.",0.2,28.07,Table 2: insider harvest in Assigned-Punishment is 28.07.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric or sufficient information for exact computation by treatment.,0.2,N/R,Only average individual insider and outsider payoffs are reported.,0.3,United States,Same recruitment pool: UMass Amherst undergraduates.,0.95,N/R,No age statistics provided.,0.1,N/R,No gender breakdown mentioned.,0.01,Undergraduate students,Described recruitment from undergraduate population.,0.95,On site,Physical experimental lab (Cleve E. Willis Experimental Economics Laboratory).,1,"Same CPR-with-poaching game as in Assigned-No Punishment: 4 insiders, 2 outsiders, 50 ED endowment per round, insiders invest in The Account which yields surplus V(G)=6G−0.025G² shared proportionally. Outsiders may each withdraw up to 25% of The Account after observing its value. In this treatment, property rights (Type 1 vs Type 2) are randomly assigned after the slider task. After poaching decisions, insiders have a punishment stage where each insider can spend from their initial payoff to impose ‘Deductions’ on any insider or outsider, using a 1:3 punishment technology (1 ED spent reduces target payoff by 3 ED). Sanction budget equals the insider’s initial payoff for that round. Final payoffs reflect investment returns, poaching, and net sanctions.",Summarizes design specifics including punishment for the Assigned-Punishment arm.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2021.102452.md,Earned – No Punishment,Treatment with earned allocation of property rights and no punishment stage.,1,Property-right allocation: Earned via slider task performance (top 4 become insiders); Punishment: No punishment available.,Differs by allocation mechanism (real-effort-based) and absence of punishment.,1,True,Part of the same human-subject laboratory study.,1,True,Between-subject randomized assignment to Earned vs Assigned and Punishment vs No Punishment.,1,True,Run in UMass Amherst experimental economics laboratory.,1,False,No separate simulation condition reported for this treatment.,1,False,"Treatment is empirical implementation, not pure theory.",1,6,Groups of six with four insiders (top four scores) and two outsiders (bottom two scores).,1,15,Uniform across treatments.,1,1,Continuous investment and poaching decisions allowed.,1,0,Subjects start with 50 EDs privately each round.,1,N/R,Nonlinear CPR technology; no constant MPCR parameter reported.,1,1,Insider communication stage exists in all treatments.,1,1,"Subjects see Account value, total insider investments, total outsider withdrawals, and initial payoffs before next round.",0.9,0,"No punishment in this treatment, so no punishment-related identity revelation.",1,0,Rewards not part of design.,1,1,Participants received full instructions including both parts and total 15 rounds before the experiment.,0.6,0,Treatment labeled No Punishment; stages 4 and 5 removed.,1,N/A,No sanctioning technology.,1,N/A,No sanctioning technology.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,50,Stated per round for insiders and outsiders.,1,N/R,"Harvest statistics given as raw amounts, not as proportion of endowment.",0.2,29.59,Table 2: Earned-No Punishment insider harvest mean = 29.59.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric or sufficient payoff data to derive exact efficiency reported.,0.2,N/R,Only role-specific average payoffs are provided.,0.3,United States,Same subject pool as other treatments.,0.95,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.01,Undergraduate students,Subjects recruited from undergraduate population.,0.95,On site,Same laboratory environment applies to all four treatments.,1,"Game structure identical to Assigned-No Punishment except for allocation of property rights. All subjects perform a 2-minute slider task with continuous scoring; in Earned treatments, the four highest scorers within each group of six are assigned as insiders (Type 1) holding property rights to the CPR, while the remaining two become outsiders (Type 2). No punishment or deduction stage is present. Insiders can only coordinate via chat and harvest decisions; outsiders can only choose poaching amounts after observing The Account.",Summarizes modifications related to earned allocation and absence of punishment.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2021.102452.md,Earned – Punishment,Treatment with earned property-right allocation and punishment stage enabled.,1,Property-right allocation: Earned via slider task (top 4 insiders); Punishment: Peer punishment (1:3) available to insiders.,Combination of real-effort-based insider selection and punishment technology.,1,True,Part of the same human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments in controlled lab conditions.,1,True,Conducted at UMass Amherst experimental lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation-only data for this treatment.,1,False,"This record captures the empirical treatment, not analytical derivations.",1,6,"Four insiders and two outsiders per group, fixed across 15 rounds.",1,15,Described as uniform across conditions.,1,1,Participants can allocate any amount from 0 to 50 in whole units; not limited to all-or-nothing choices.,1,0,Endowment initially held privately; insiders choose investment amount.,1,N/R,Nonlinear surplus; no single marginal per capita return parameter provided.,1,1,Insiders have 60-second chat at the beginning of each round.,1,1,"After poaching and before punishment, all see The Account, total investment, withdrawals, and initial payoffs.",0.9,0,Sanctions are assigned to player IDs (Type and ID). Paper does not report revealing real identities; punishment is anonymous beyond role and ID.,0.8,0,No positive reward option in game.,1,1,Subjects received instructions for both parts and know the game lasts 15 rounds.,0.6,1,"In Punishment treatments, insiders can pay EDs to reduce others’ payoffs.",1,1,Standard 1:3 sanction technology: cost of 1 ED per unit of punishment.,1,3,Each ED spent by an insider reduces target’s payoff by 3 EDs.,1,0,Only deductions; no specification of rewarding others.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Each insider and outsider receives 50 ED each round.,1,N/R,Contribution reported as raw harvest; not normalized.,0.2,26.5,Table 2: insider harvest in Earned-Punishment is 26.50.,1,N/R,No efficiency index per treatment reported.,0.2,N/R,Group-level payoffs not provided; only individual means.,0.3,United States,Same subject pool and location.,0.95,N/R,Not specified.,0.1,N/R,Not specified.,0.01,Undergraduate students,Subjects recruited from undergraduates via ORSEE.,0.95,On site,Conducted in physical experimental lab.,1,"Same CPR-with-poaching structure and parameters as other treatments. All subjects perform a 2-minute slider task with refined continuous scoring; in Earned treatments, within each group the four highest scoring subjects become insiders (Type 1) and the remaining two are outsiders (Type 2). After observing The Account, outsiders choose poaching amounts up to 25% of the Account each. In this treatment, insiders then proceed to a punishment stage where they can allocate part of their initial payoff to impose ‘Deductions’ on any insider or outsider. One ED spent reduces the target’s payoff by three EDs; an insider’s maximum sanction budget is their initial payoff that round. Communication among insiders happens before harvest; no communication with outsiders.",Summarizes elements specific to Earned allocation and punishment stage.,1
