10.1057_s41599-019-0249-2.md,System IV: Public goods game with democratic punishment (individual costs),System IV in Table 1 is 'Public goods game with democratic punishment (individual costs)'.,1,Punishment system = Democratic peer punishment; Voting = Yes; Cost sharing = No (individual punishment costs),Described as democratic peer punishment with individual costs and voting for execution of punishment.,1,True,Human participants played this condition.,1,True,One of the experimentally manipulated institutional designs.,1,True,Implemented in the same lab setting via z-Tree.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No analytical modeling as separate experiment.,1,4,Four players per group.,1,10,Ten periods for each system.,1,1,Same contribution decision space as base game.,1,0,"20 MU private endowment, contribute any portion.",1,0.4,Public good multiplier 1.6 split among 4 players.,1,0,No chat or discussion allowed is reported.,0.9,1,"Players see each other’s contributions, payoffs, and total intended punishment amounts before voting; receive info on executed punishment and payoffs afterward.",1,0,Voting and punishment decisions refer to anonymous player labels; no evidence of identity revelation.,0.7,0,No rewards.,1,1,Participants informed ex ante of number of periods and versions.,0.9,1,Democratic peer punishment stage where players propose and vote on punishment.,1,1,"In the democratic system with individual costs, 'all players had to bear the costs of their own intended punishment' if a majority voted for execution.",1,3,"Based on the underlying peer punishment design, each MU invested reduces the target's payoff by 3 MU; democratic system adds voting but retains this structure.",0.8,0,Only costly punishment is described.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Endowment is 20 MU in all game versions.,1,N/R,Only cumulative contribution in MUs is reported.,0.3,144.2,Table 2: 'Contribution (in MUs)' = 144.20 (53.56) for 'Democratic peer punishment with individual costs'.,1,