ith number of active members (80, 60, 40, 20 ECU for N=4,3,2,1). Stage 2: majority/unanimity voting to exclude others for 3 periods (reduced to 2 or 1 periods in final rounds as common knowledge). Excluded players receive 10 ECU per exclusion period and observe group outcomes.",Exo3P treatment description.,1
10.1111_ecin.12720.md,Endo – Endogenous exclusion length (1–3 periods),"Treatment named 'Endo'; length of exclusion chosen by voters, 1–3 periods.",1,"Exclusion mechanism: group voting; each voter chooses both whether to exclude and exclusion length in [1,3]; implemented length is median (or shorter in specified cases).","Only treatment where exclusion length is endogenized via voting, with up to 3 periods.",1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to treatment; controlled protocol.,1,True,Run at GATE-Lab.,1,False,No computational simulation.,1,False,Not analytical-only.,1,4,Groups of four.,1,20,20 periods.,1,1,"Withdrawal in [0,20] ECU.",1,1,Endowment in group account each period.,1,0.4,Same group account MPCR.,1,0,No chat/communication mentioned.,0.9,1,Feedback on all members' withdrawals and earnings each period.,1,1,"After voting, group members learn which IDs are excluded and for how many periods; number of votes not revealed.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,20 periods announced; period counter shown.,1,1,Voting to exclude for 1–3 periods constitutes punishment.,1,0,Voting is costless; only indirect costs via group account and incentives.,1,10,"Excluded members receive fixed 10 ECU per exclusion period, same as other exclusion treatments.",0.3,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"For N=4, group account 80 ECU, giving each up to 20 ECU withdrawal.",1,N/R,Only raw withdrawals presented.,0,7.28,Table 1: 'Average withdrawal (groups with N>2)' Endo is 7.28 ECU.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency metric reported.,0.3,22.51,"Table 1: 'Average payoff (in ECU, all players)' Endo is 22.51 ECU per person.",1,France,"Same subject pool, French lab.",1,Mean 28.60; distribution not fully reported,Table A1: Mean age 28.60 in Endo.,1,50.0% male,Table A1: Males (%) 0.50 for Endo.,1,"66.7% students (mainly engineering, business, medical schools)",Table A1: Students (%) 0.667 for Endo.,1,On site,Same lab setting.,1,"Two-stage per period. Stage 1: taking game with group account 80/60/40/20 for N=4/3/2/1 as in Exo3P. Stage 2: for each other member, player decides whether to exclude and, if yes, for 1–3 periods; if at least two vote to exclude, implemented length is median among positive votes with rules for ties and two-voter cases. Excluded members get 10 ECU per exclusion period, cannot act, but observe group outcomes.",Endo treatment description and detailed voting rules.,1
10.1038_s41598-020-60349-z.md,Baseline Wildlife-Viewing Game (WVG) without punishment,Paper develops a single formal two-player wildlife-viewing game (WVG) without punishment as the core model; all SVO and parameter variations are within this game.,0.96,"Player social value orientations (SVOs: individualistic, competitive, prosocial) and game parameters (tour fee B, tour cost C, guarantee coefficient δ, encounter probability P, vigilance coefficient θ) varied across analyses within the same model.","Model outcomes are analyzed for all SVO combinations (I,C,P) and for ranges of δ, P, θ, with fixed B and C in figures; these are the formal independent variables of the game-theoretic analysis.",0.9,False,The study is purely theoretical/mathematical; no human participants or behavioral data are used.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; only a formal game-theoretic model is analyzed.,1,False,No lab or field setting; the work is theoretical.,1,False,"Although numerical examples and parameter sweeps are provided, they are closed-form analyses of a game, not an agent-based or numerical simulation with agents.",0.78,True,"Core of the paper is mathematical derivation of equilibria, Pareto efficiency, and PINE conditions for a formal 2-player game.",1,2,The WVG is explicitly defined as a two-player non-cooperative game with players 1 and 2.,1,1,They state the WVG is a single-shot game in the Discussion.,1,1,Players choose between two pure strategies: conduct a tour (T) or not (F); this is an all-or-nothing decision regarding tour provision.,1,N/A,Model is not a contribution game/public goods setting; no endowment allocation decision.,1,N/R,"No public goods multiplier or MPCR is defined; payoffs derive from fees, costs, and probabilities.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; purely analytical.,1,0,Information about others’ earnings or actions is not modeled; only simultaneous choices with payoff matrices.,0.7,N/A,No individual-level punishment decisions or identities; only symmetric payoff structures.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,One-shot analytical game; no notion of displaying remaining rounds.,1,0,This baseline version does not include an external punishment term ε; that is introduced only later in the punishment extension.,0.98,N/A,No explicit punishment decision or cost parameter in the baseline game.,1,N/A,No punishment technology is modeled.,1,0,No reward mechanism or positive sanctioning is described.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/R,"Model specifies tour cost C and fee B, but no per-period endowment analogous to tokens in a contribution game.",0.8,N/R,"Outcomes are payoffs and equilibrium types (tour vs no tour), not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No contribution quantity is defined; the action is binary (conduct tour or not).,1,N/R,They analyze Pareto efficiency and PINE qualitatively; no explicit normalized efficiency metric (0–1) is reported.,0.8,N/R,"They provide example payoff matrices but no single aggregated group payoff outcome is defined as a DV, and it depends on parameters and SVOs.",0.7,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical modeling.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is a formal analytical model with no empirical environment.,1,"Two-player non-cooperative wildlife-viewing game with binary actions (conduct tour T or not F). Payoffs depend on tour fee B, cost C, guarantee coefficient δ, encounter probability P, vigilance coefficient θ, and players’ social value orientations (SVOs: individualistic (1,0), competitive (1/√2,-1/√2), prosocial (1/√2,1/√2)). Objective payoffs U_i are expected monetary profits; subjective payoffs V_i are linear combinations of own and other’s objective payoffs. Social dilemmas are defined via Pareto-inefficient Nash equilibria (PINE).","Summarizes formal structure of the WVG as defined in Methods, including strategy set, payoff functions, and SVO-based transformation.",1
10.1038_s41598-020-60349-z.md,Wildlife-Viewing Game (WVG) with fair punishment on tour concentration,"Punishment extension is defined as a distinct game G_CC^ε with modified payoff matrix U_ε and V_ε, analyzed specifically for its impact on PINE.",0.96,"Presence and magnitude of symmetric external punishment ε > 0 applied to both players when both choose T; SVOs with focus on competitive–competitive market and same game parameters (B, C, δ, P, θ).","Methods define U_ε and V_ε by subtracting ε from both players’ payoffs in the (T,T) cell and then analyze equilibria as ε varies, especially for competitive players.",0.94,False,Punishment is introduced only in the analytical model; no experimental enforcement or human data.,1,False,"Still a formal theoretical game-theoretic analysis, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No laboratory or in-person implementation.,1,False,Analysis remains closed-form with parametric examples; no agent-based or stochastic simulations.,0.78,True,"Theorems and conditions for Nash equilibria, Pareto efficiency, and PINE are derived analytically for U_ε and V_ε.",1,2,Punishment-augmented game retains the same two-player structure as the baseline WVG.,1,1,Extension with punishment is still a single-shot game.,1,1,Players still choose between conducting a tour (T) and not conducting (F).,1,N/A,Not a contribution/public goods game.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human implementation or communication.,1,0,Model does not describe feedback about others’ past outcomes; simultaneous moves only.,0.7,N/A,"Punishment is an exogenous symmetric penalty applied automatically in (T,T), not a decision by identifiable players.",1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,0,Single-shot theoretical game; no rounds for players to be informed about.,1,1,"This condition explicitly introduces punishment parameter ε > 0 on outcome (T,T).",1,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as an external penalty to payoffs, not as a costly action taken by players spending units; no per-unit punishment cost is defined.",0.95,N/A,No mapping from invested punishment to inflicted loss; ε directly specifies loss.,0.95,0,Only punishment term ε is added; no reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"As in baseline WVG, payoffs are based on fees and costs; no explicit initial endowment is specified.",0.8,N/R,DV is whether equilibria are PINE or Pareto efficient; no contribution rate defined.,1,N/R,No quantity of contribution modeled; action is binary.,1,N/R,They discuss how punishment changes Pareto-efficient regions and PINE but do not report a normalized efficiency measure.,0.85,N/R,"They show example total payoffs (e.g., total payoff changing from 0.22 to 0.35 or -1.38) but not as a single general DV; it varies with parameters.",0.75,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Punishment extension is fully analytical without human implementation.,1,"Same WVG structure as baseline (two players, strategies T or F, payoffs via B, C, δ, P, θ, and SVO-based subjective payoffs), but with an additional symmetric external punishment ε > 0 subtracted from both players’ objective payoffs when both choose T: U_ε(T,T) = (U_1^{TT}−ε, U_1^{TT}−ε). Punishment leaves the competitive players’ subjective payoff matrix unchanged but shrinks the (T,T)-Pareto region, thereby expanding the parameter region where (T,T) is a PINE in competitive–competitive markets.",Summarizes the punishment mechanism and its analytical implications as described in the Results and Theorem 5.,1
10.1007_s10640-020-00526-w.md,"T0 – Pure control (no monitoring, no SMS)","One of three randomized village-level conditions: T0 pure control, T1 monitoring only, T2 monitoring+SMS.",1,"Village-level assignment: no community monitoring, no SMS rule reminders (T0).",T0 is baseline condition in the three-arm RCT; no interventions implemented.,1,True,Household and village data collected from human participants via surveys and on-the-ground assessments.,1,True,Villages randomly assigned to treatments; T0 is control arm within RCT.,1,False,"Intervention implemented in real villages/forests in Uganda, not a lab setting.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,No purely theoretical or analytical model as primary design.,1,10,"Within each study village, 10 households were surveyed at baseline and endline; treat these surveyed households as players in the empirical common-pool situation.",0.8,1,"Field intervention over time, but no discrete repeated game rounds; model as single aggregate decision environment.",0.5,1,Households can harvest continuous quantities of forest products; not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.,1,0,Not a token-contribution game; all forest stock effectively starts as unharvested (private accounts concept inapplicable). Use 0 as default private holding.,0.2,N/R,No experimental payoff structure with stated marginal per capita return; real-world forest benefits not parameterized.,1,0,No experimental communication channel specified; natural village communication is not an experimental feature.,0.8,0,Participants not given experimental feedback tables about others’ extractions or sanctions; only researchers aggregate data.,0.9,0,Existing community sanctions pre-date study and are not experimentally manipulated; no experimental identity revelation setting.,0.7,0,No experimental reward mechanism implemented.,1,0,No mention that subjects were told a fixed number of periods or rounds.,0.9,1,Formal and informal sanctions for breaking forest rules exist in almost all villages at baseline.,1,N/R,"Paper describes fines, confiscation, violence, etc., but no standardized marginal cost per unit to punisher.",1,N/R,No numeric mapping from units of punishment to reduction in target payoff.,1,0,Only sanctions and penalties mentioned; no rewards for compliance.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No standardized experimental endowment of tokens or money.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are knowledge, perceived/actual sanctions, compliance, and forest use; no normalized contribution variable.",1,N/R,Study measures forest extraction (use) rather than contributions to a public account.,1,N/R,No total-payoff vs. maximum-payoff efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate monetary payoff reported as outcome; outcomes are indices and forest loss.,1,Uganda,"Study villages located in Central, West, and South-West Uganda.",1,N/R,Age used as a control but no summary or range given in main text.,0.6,N/R,Gender used as control; no overall gender composition reported.,0.6,N/R,Education used as control; no descriptive stats in main text.,0.6,Field experiment,Randomized interventions in real communities managing actual forests.,1,"Common-pool forest management setting in 110 Ugandan villages. T0 villages have existing locally-determined use rules and sanctions but receive no added community monitoring structure and no SMS reminders. Forest products include fuelwood, poles, water, medicinal plants, mushrooms, etc. Outcomes measured via household surveys, satellite-based deforestation, and on-the-ground forest assessments. Village-level management rules and sanctioning institutions are endogenous and heterogeneous across villages but not experimentally varied in this arm.","Synthesized from description of setting, rules, sanctions, products, and measurement strategy.",1
10.1007_s10640-020-00526-w.md,T1 – Community monitoring only,Second randomized condition: 50 villages assigned community monitoring without SMS; denoted T1.,1,Village-level assignment: community forest monitoring treatment only (T1); no SMS rule reminders.,Section 4.2 describes monitoring as a separate treatment; 50 villages receive it without SMS.,1,True,Uses household surveys and forest measurements from human communities.,1,True,Villages randomly allocated to this treatment arm.,1,False,Conducted in real-world forest villages.,1,False,No computational simulation.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical study.,1,10,Design mirrors T0: 10 baseline households surveyed per village.,0.8,1,Ongoing field exposure; no discrete experimental rounds.,0.5,1,Same real-world extraction environment; continuous harvest choices.,1,0,No token endowment; same inference as T0.,0.2,N/R,No specified MPCR in payoffs.,1,0,"No experimental communication channel beyond normal village interaction and monitoring meetings, which are part of treatment but not strategic in a game sense.",0.7,1,"Under community monitoring, six monitors report information about collective forest use in village meetings and display findings on a public poster.",1,0,"Monitors report aggregate forest use and threats, not identities of violators.",0.9,0,No reward scheme implemented.,1,0,No fixed finite horizon informed to participants.,0.9,1,"As in T0, existing formal and informal sanctions remain in place.",1,N/R,No per-unit sanction cost reported.,1,N/R,No numeric transformation from punishment acts to payoff reductions.,1,0,No rewards introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No standardized endowment.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are indices of knowledge, scrutiny, sanctions, compliance, and forest use, not contributions.",1,N/R,No explicit contribution variable.,1,N/R,Efficiency relative to maximum group payoff not reported.,1,N/R,No monetary group payoff outcome.,1,Uganda,Same sample of Ugandan villages.,1,N/R,Age included as control but no descriptives provided.,0.6,N/R,Gender included as control but composition not reported.,0.6,N/R,Education used as control; no distribution given.,0.6,Field experiment,Village-level randomized community monitoring intervention.,1,"In T1 villages, six community members are selected as monitors. They systematically measure forest use and threats monthly along specified transects, then report aggregate information in village meetings and on a public poster. This increases second-party monitoring and public information about collective use. Forest rules and sanction institutions themselves remain endogenously set by community and/or NFA. T1 is the primary comparison group for estimating the marginal effect of SMS reminders (T2 vs T1).",From Section 4.2 description of community monitoring treatment.,1
10.1007_s10640-020-00526-w.md,T2 – Community monitoring + SMS rule reminders,Third randomized condition: 10 villages assigned both community monitoring and SMS reminders; denoted T2.,1,Village-level assignment: community monitoring plus monthly SMS reminders of local forest use rules (T2).,Section 4.1 and 4.2: 10 villages receive both interventions; Monitoring×Rules term in ANCOVA captures this.,1,True,Intervention delivered via SMS to consenting households; data from human subjects.,1,True,Villages randomly assigned to this treatment in the RCT.,1,False,Implemented in real forest-dependent communities.,1,False,No simulation model.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,10,Baseline: 10 households surveyed per village; at endline more households in SMS villages but core village size per design is 10 surveyed. Use 10 for comparability with other arms.,0.6,1,"Monthly SMS over about a year but analyzed as one treatment condition, not explicit repeated rounds.",0.5,1,Continuous forest extraction choices as in other arms.,1,0,No experimental endowment; same coding as other arms.,0.2,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,0,"No added interactive chat channel; SMS are one-way reminders from project, not peer communication.",0.9,1,All T2 villages also have community monitoring with public posters and village meetings on forest use.,1,0,Neither monitoring reports nor SMS disclose identities of punished individuals.,0.9,0,No rewards mechanism introduced.,1,0,Duration of SMS campaign not framed as finite repeated game for participants.,0.9,1,Same underlying forest rule regime with formal and informal sanctions.,1,N/R,"Sanction costs (fines, time, social costs) not quantified.",1,N/R,No numeric mapping from sanctions to payoffs.,1,0,No rewards introduced as part of SMS or monitoring.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No standardized endowment.,1,N/R,"Key DVs: standardized indices of knowledge, attentiveness (discussions), scrutiny, sanctioning, compliance, forest use, deforestation. No contribution variable.",1,N/R,No experimental contributions; only real extraction quantities which are many-dimensional.,1,N/R,Study does not compute payoff efficiency relative to maximum cooperative outcome.,1,N/R,No explicit monetary group payoff reported; focus on environmental and behavioral outcomes.,1,Uganda,Same Ugandan village sample.,1,N/R,Age controlled for but no descriptive summary values.,0.6,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0.6,N/R,Education used as control only.,0.6,Field experiment,RCT with SMS and community monitoring in real villages.,1,"In T2 villages, households are exposed both to community monitoring (as in T1) and to a monthly SMS intervention reminding them of locally specific forest use rules. SMS content is customized by village to reflect its actual rule set (e.g., allowed days for fuelwood collection, bans on poles, charcoal, tree cutting, forest entry, tools, grazing, agricultural expansion). Example: ‘Dear [name], please remember that community members can only collect firewood on Wednesdays and Saturdays. Thank you for obeying your community's rules.’ SMS are sent to consenting households (70 in total across 10 T2 villages; 40 of these surveyed at endline). The main treatment contrast in the paper is T2 vs T1, interpreted as the marginal effect of SMS reminders on knowledge, perceived and actual sanctions, compliance with rules (indices of violating harvesting bans), and forest use (self-reports plus high-resolution Sentinel-2 based forest loss near households and along transects).","Summarizes SMS content, targeting, and measurement strategy described in Sections 4 and 5.",1
10.1093_qje_qjaa035.md,"Baseline extortion game (theoretical model, no investigations or dyadic relationships)",Core model in Section II and Proposition 2 describing the extortion game without additional instruments.,1,Presence of commitment to action-contingent threats (extortion allowed); long-run principal interacting with sequence of short-run agents in a trust game.,The only varied feature relative to the no-extortion benchmark is that agents can commit to threats μ_t; within this model there are no further treatments or parameter changes at condition level.,0.9,False,Pure game-theoretic model with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only analytical modeling.,1,False,No lab or field setting; fully theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported.,1,True,Equilibrium characterized via propositions and proofs in continuous-time/infinitely repeated game framework.,1,2,Each stage involves one long-run principal and one short-run agent (dyad) in the trust game.,1,0,"Game is infinite horizon with t ∈ {0,1,2,...}; no fixed finite number of rounds.",1,1,"Agent chooses effort e_t ∈ ℝ_+ and principal transfer s_t ≥ 0 as continuous amounts, not all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,Not a public goods contribution game and no human experiment; concept not applicable.,1,N/R,No linear public goods structure; payoffs are e_t - s_t and s_t - c(e_t).,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication is modeled as costless messages but not experimental chat among participants.,1,0,Agents observe only own interaction and public messages; no mention of aggregate payoff summaries.,0.9,0,Model does not distinguish identities beyond principal vs current agent; no concept of identity revelation for punishers.,0.8,0,No separate rewarders distinct from principal; no identity revelation discussed.,0.8,0,Infinite horizon with discounting; no information about a terminal round is modeled.,1,1,Punishment is implemented as future agents reducing principal’s continuation payoff based on messages; coordinated punishments central to model.,1,N/R,"Cost of punishing is implicit via continuation payoffs and effort, not specified as per-unit token cost.",0.7,N/R,No linear technology mapping punishment points to payoff changes; only general continuation payoff differences.,0.7,1,Principal can reward agents via positive transfers; these act as rewards in trust game.,1,1,"Each unit of transfer s_t reduces principal’s current payoff one-for-one (e_t - s_t), so marginal cost per unit is 1.",1,1,"Each unit of transfer increases agent’s payoff one-for-one (s_t - c(e_t)), so marginal benefit per unit is 1.",1,N/R,Model does not define a discrete endowment; effort and transfers are continuous variables without bounded endowments.,0.8,N/R,"No contribution game; main results are about equilibrium effort levels and transfers, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,Outcomes reported as general conditions (e_t = 0 in equilibrium); no numeric effort amounts specified.,1,N/R,Paper discusses surplus qualitatively (e - c(e)) but no normalized efficiency measure is fixed numerically.,0.9,N/R,Total surplus formulas given symbolically but no specific numerical payoffs to record.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire paper is theoretical; no empirical or experimental data collection.,1,"Infinite-horizon trust game between a long-run principal and a sequence of short-run agents with public cheap-talk messages; agents can precommit to action-contingent threats (μ_t) that determine messages m_t based on transfers, leading to coordinated punishments by future agents; cost of effort c(e) strictly increasing, strictly convex with c(0)=c'(0)=0; discount factor δ ∈ [0,1). Equilibrium result (Proposition 2): in all equilibria of this extortion game, e_t = s_t = 0 in every period.",Summary of the key structural aspects of the baseline model as specified in Sections II–III and Proposition 2.,1
10.1093_qje_qjaa035.md,"No-extortion benchmark game (agents choose messages after transfers, no commitment to threats)",Defined as the 'no-extortion game' in Section III and contrasted with the extortion game in Proposition 1.,1,Timing of messaging: agents cannot precommit to threats; messages chosen after transfers (no extortion possible).,"Only difference from extortion game is that m_t is chosen at end of period without commitment μ_t, eliminating action-contingent extortion.",1,False,Theoretical benchmark model only.,1,False,No experimental or observational data.,1,False,Not conducted in a lab setting; purely analytical.,1,False,No computational simulations reported.,1,True,Equilibrium characterized in Proposition 1 with proofs.,1,2,Same principal-agent dyad per period as in baseline model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon repeated interaction (t ≥ 0).,1,1,Effort e_t and transfer s_t are continuous nonnegative choices.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments.,1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication is a theoretical cheap-talk device.,1,0,Agents see all messages but not payoff summaries; only principal observes all e_t and s_t.,0.9,0,No modeling of identities for punishment beyond roles.,0.8,0,Rewards (transfers) are bilateral; no identity revelation discussion.,0.8,0,"Discounted infinite game, so no finite round information.",1,1,Coordinated punishments via future agents’ behavior based on messages (Proposition 1 construction).,1,N/R,Punishment cost appears only via future surplus loss; no per-unit token specification.,0.7,N/R,No explicit linear technology of punishment defined.,0.7,1,Principal can reward effort with positive transfers.,1,1,Transfer reduces principal’s period payoff one-for-one.,1,1,Transfer increases agent’s payoff one-for-one.,1,N/R,No fixed token endowment; continuous variables instead.,0.8,N/R,Not formulated as contribution rate; equilibrium effort denoted e* symbolically.,1,N/R,Effort level e* characterized parametrically (min of e^FB and solution to c(e)=δe); no numerical value.,1,N/R,"Total surplus e* - c(e*) discussed, but not normalized as efficiency index.",0.9,N/R,Group payoff given symbolically (principal plus agent surplus) without specific numbers.,0.9,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,Analytical theoretical model only.,1,"Infinite-horizon trust game as in the baseline but without commitment to threats: in each period agent t chooses effort e_t, principal chooses transfer s_t, then agent sends message m_t chosen at end of period; agents cannot use action-contingent precommitted threats. Communication supports coordinated punishments that sustain positive effort e* with transfers s_t = c(e*), where e* = min{e^FB, max e such that c(e)=δe}.",Description from Section III and Proposition 1.,1
10.1093_qje_qjaa035.md,Extortion game with public effort signals (effort investigations),Section IV.A introduces an extension of the extortion game where a noisy public signal of effort y_t is observed.,1,"Public signal of effort: binary signal y_t ∈ {0,1} with Pr(y_t=1 | e_t)=γ(e_t), γ(e) strictly increasing; agents can condition threats on (s_t, y_t).",Primary change from baseline extortion game is introduction of effort-dependent public signal used in threats.,1,False,"This is a theoretical extension, not an empirical investigation.",1,False,No actual experiment with subjects; analyses are analytic propositions.,1,False,No lab environment; abstract model.,1,False,"Results derived analytically, not via simulation.",1,True,Proposition 3 analytically characterizes equilibria and rents.,1,2,Same principal and current agent per period as baseline.,1,0,Infinite horizon setting persists.,1,1,Continuous effort e_t and transfer s_t decisions.,1,N/A,Not framed as contribution of an endowment.,1,N/R,No linear public good with MPCR.,1,N/A,No human experiment; communication captured by theoretical messages and signals.,1,0,Players observe public signal y_t and messages but not payoff summaries.,0.9,0,Model does not discuss identifying who punishes; only one principal and sequence of agents.,0.8,0,Transfers remain bilateral principal-agent; no identity variation to show.,0.8,0,Infinite game; no explicit round-count information.,1,1,Future agents punish principal based on message m_t and observed effort signal y_t.,1,N/R,Punishment implemented via continuation payoffs; no explicit linear cost parameter.,0.7,N/R,Technology summarized through L(y) = δ/(1-δ)(Π̄(y)-Π̲(y)); no simple per-unit mapping.,0.7,1,Principal still uses transfers as rewards.,1,1,Each unit of transfer costs principal one unit.,1,1,Each unit of transfer increases agent’s utility one unit.,1,N/R,No explicit finite endowment given.,0.8,N/R,Outcomes expressed via optimal effort e solving arg max{e - c(e) - ū(e)}; no normalized contribution rate.,1,N/R,"Effort level characterized symbolically, not numerically.",1,N/R,"Efficiency tradeoff described via surplus minus agent rents, but no specific numeric efficiency index.",0.9,N/R,Principal-optimal surplus is derived symbolically; no numbers.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model with public effort signals.,1,"Same infinite-horizon extortion game as baseline, but after transfer s_t a public binary signal y_t ∈ {0,1} of effort is realized with Pr(y_t=1|e_t)=γ(e_t), γ strictly increasing and usually concave. Threats μ_t can map (s_t, y_t) to messages. Leverage L(y) depends on y, and expected leverage E[L(y)|e] increases with effort. Proposition 3: if γ is weakly concave, agent’s equilibrium payoff when on-path effort is e is at least ū(e) = max{0, [c'(e)/γ'(e)]γ(e) - c(e)}, which is increasing in e; principal-optimal on-path effort maximizes e - c(e) - ū(e) subject to a dynamic enforcement constraint.",Summarizes Section IV.A and Proposition 3.,1
10.1093_qje_qjaa035.md,Extortion game with public transfer signals (transfer investigations),Section IV.B introduces a variant with a public signal x_t of the transfer s_t.,1,"Public signal of transfer: binary signal x_t ∈ {0,1} with Pr(x_t=1 | s_t)=φ(s_t), φ strictly increasing; threats μ_t can condition on (s_t, x_t).",Key variation from baseline extortion game is the introduction of transfer-dependent signal used with threats.,1,False,Model only; no observed data.,1,False,No controlled experiment.,1,False,Pure theory.,1,False,Results derived symbolically.,1,True,Proposition 4 gives necessary conditions for positive effort analytically.,1,2,Principal and one agent per period.,1,0,Infinite horizon continues.,1,1,"Continuous e_t, s_t.",1,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No linear public good.,1,N/A,No subjects; signals are hard-coded in model.,1,0,Only public info is x_t and messages.,0.9,0,No multiple punishers with identities.,0.8,0,Single principal; no identity variation.,0.8,0,Infinite game assumption; no display of remaining rounds.,1,1,"Future agents punish via strategies based on (m_t,x_t).",1,N/R,"Costs embedded in continuation values, not per-unit tokens.",0.7,N/R,No explicit parametrization of punishment technology.,0.7,1,Transfers still serve as rewards.,1,1,Marginal cost of s_t to principal is 1.,1,1,Marginal benefit of s_t to agent is 1.,1,N/R,No fixed money endowment specified.,0.8,N/R,Focus is on conditions for positive effort; no normalized rate.,1,N/R,Effort e_t either zero or constrained by conditions in Proposition 4; no numerics.,1,N/R,"Discussion about inefficiency given stringent φ, but no explicit metric.",0.9,N/R,Surplus not expressed numerically.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Theoretical transfer-signal extension.,1,"Extortion game as baseline, plus a public binary signal x_t ∈ {0,1} realized after s_t with Pr(x_t=1|s_t)=φ(s_t), φ strictly increasing and twice continuously differentiable. Agent’s threats μ_t map (s_t, x_t) into messages m_t. Proposition 4: for any equilibrium with positive on-path effort e_t>0, there must exist s* > 0 and ŝ ∈ [0, s*) such that c(e_t) ≤ s* - ŝ, φ''(s*) ≤ 0, and φ'(s*) = [φ(s*) - φ(ŝ)]/(s* - ŝ). If φ is strictly concave on ℝ_+, then these conditions cannot be satisfied and every equilibrium has e_t = 0.",Based on Section IV.B and Proposition 4.,1
10.1093_qje_qjaa035.md,Extortion game with dyadic relationships (principal-agent coordination game after messaging),Section V defines the extortion game with dyadic relationships by adding a post-message coordination game between principal and agent.,1,"Presence and strength of dyadic relationship: symmetric 2x2 coordination game with payoffs (v_H, v_H) on (h,h) and (v_L, v_L) otherwise; parameter difference v_H - v_L determines relationship strength.","Main new ingredient relative to baseline is addition of dyadic coordination game with parameters v_H, v_L.",1,False,Still a purely theoretical construction.,1,False,No involving human experimental design.,1,False,Abstract model only.,1,False,Equilibria solved analytically.,1,True,Proposition 5 analytically characterizes bounds on effort and principal-optimal equilibria.,1,2,Principal and agent t interact including coordination game.,1,0,Underlying extortion game remains infinite-horizon.,1,1,"Effort and transfers continuous; coordination game actions h,l are discrete but not contribution choices.",1,N/A,Not a public goods contribution structure.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0,Agents see messages; coordination game outcomes are private to principal and current agent.,0.9,0,No multiple punishers with identities; punishments partly via bilateral coordination outcome.,0.8,0,"Rewards in dyadic game (h,h) are between same two players; no identity variation.",0.8,0,Infinite horizon remains; no rounds announced.,1,1,Punishment occurs via both continuation payoffs from messages and low payoff v_L outcome in dyadic game.,1,N/R,Costs embodied in switching from v_H to v_L and in continuation payoff reductions; no explicit per-unit punishment cost.,0.7,N/R,No linear mapping of punishment units to payoff loss specified.,0.7,1,"Rewards via transfers and high-coordination outcome (h,h) yielding v_H.",1,1,Transfer cost remains one-for-one; coordination reward v_H is not associated with a choice variable cost parameter.,0.8,N/R,No simple linear reward technology; v_H and v_L are exogenous payoffs.,0.7,N/R,No finite endowment in tokens specified.,0.8,N/R,Outcome of interest is upper bound on effort via c(e_t) ≤ 3(v_H - v_L); no normalized contribution rate.,1,N/R,Effort e_t symbolically constrained by cost function and v_H - v_L; no numeric value.,1,N/R,Efficiency discussed qualitatively; no normalized measure.,0.9,N/R,Principal’s maximum payoff bounded by e* - c(e*) + 2v_H - v_L; no specific numbers.,0.9,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,N/A,No empirical data.,1,No human,Section V remains theoretical.,1,"Baseline extortion game is modified so that after message m_t is sent, principal and agent t play a symmetric 2x2 coordination game with actions h and l and payoffs: (h,h) yields (v_H, v_H); any outcome involving l yields (v_L, v_L) with v_H > v_L. Agent can also transfer s_t^A ≥ 0 to principal at stage of choosing s_t. Total period payoffs: principal e_t - s_t + s_t^A + v_t, agent s_t - s_t^A - c(e_t) + v_t. Proposition 5: in any equilibrium c(e_t) ≤ 3(v_H - v_L) in every period; letting e* = min{e^FB, solution of c(e)=3(v_H - v_L)}, there exists δ̄<1 such that for δ ≥ δ̄ any principal-optimal equilibrium has on-path effort e_t = e* for all t.",Condensed from Section V and Proposition 5.,1
10.1093_qje_qjaa035.md,Costly extortion game (heterogeneous cost of committing to threats),Section VI defines a 'costly extortion game' where each agent draws a private cost k_t to invest in extortion capability.,1,Agent’s private cost of commitment k_t to extortion: k_t ~ G(·); decision to invest in extortion (play extortion game) or not (play no-extortion game).,Core variation is stochastic individual cost of obtaining extortion capability; investment choice is the condition’s key independent factor.,1,False,Still purely theoretical.,1,False,No behavioral experiment run.,1,False,No lab or field context.,1,False,Equilibria derived analytically; no numerical simulations.,1,True,Proposition 6 gives closed-form characterization for principal-optimal leverage and effort decisions.,1,2,Principal and a single agent per period as before.,1,0,Infinite-horizon dynamic game.,1,1,Effort and transfers continuous; investment in extortion is binary but not a contribution choice.,1,N/A,Not a public goods context.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human experimental platform.,1,0,Agents observe messages; no payoff summary signals beyond what is described.,0.9,0,No multiplicity of punishers with identities; structure unchanged.,0.8,0,Bilateral rewards; no identity variation.,0.8,0,Infinite horizon persists.,1,1,Both extorting and non-extorting situations rely on coordinated punishments via leverage differences L.,1,N/R,Punishment via continuation payoffs; no explicit unit cost given.,0.7,N/R,Technology of punishment is summarized by leverage L and continuation payoffs.,0.7,1,Principal continues to reward via transfers c(e_t) or L.,1,1,Transfer cost remains one-for-one.,1,1,Transfers increase agent utility one-for-one.,1,N/R,No numeric resource endowment specified.,0.8,N/R,Main outcomes are effort e_t for non-investing agents and frequency of investment; no normalized contribution rate.,1,N/R,Effort for non-extorting agents is c^{-1}(L_t); symbolic only.,1,N/R,Paper describes tradeoff between extortion prevalence and effort; no explicit 0-1 efficiency measure.,0.9,N/R,Principal’s payoff Π* is defined as max over L; no numeric realization.,0.9,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,No human,Costly extortion model is theoretical only.,1,"Each period t starts with agent t privately observing cost k_t ≥ 0 drawn from distribution G(·). Agent chooses whether to invest in extortion. If he invests, he effectively plays the extortion game (can commit to threats) and pays cost k_t; if not, he plays the no-extortion game. Principal observes investment decision but other agents only see message m_t. Leverage L_t = δ/(1-δ)(Π̄ - Π̲) is chosen in principal-optimal equilibrium. Proposition 6: in each period of a principal-optimal equilibrium, L_t solves max_{L ≥ 0} {(1 - G(L)) c^{-1}(L) - L} subject to L ≤ [δ/(1-δ)]((1 - G(L)) c^{-1}(L) - L). Agent invests if k_t < G(L_t); if he invests, he exerts e_t=0 and is paid s_t = L_t; if he does not, he exerts e_t = c^{-1}(L_t) and is paid s_t = L_t.",Summarizes Section VI and Proposition 6.,1
10.1016_j.euroecorev.2013.11.012.md,Base analytical model – repeated trust game with two groups,Paper presents a single analytical model of an infinitely repeated trust game with majority (x) and minority (y) groups; no separate empirical conditions are described.,0.99,"Firm discount factor δ; group size shares γ_x and γ_y (majority/minority); probabilities of bias φ_x, φ_y; enforcement penalties π_x, π_y; information parameters θ_p^q.",Model characterizes equilibria as functions of these parameters (Section 2 and 3).,0.98,False,Pure game-theoretic and analytical treatment with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled lab/field experiment; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or experimental data are collected.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulation results are reported; all results are analytical.,1,True,Model consists of formal repeated-game analysis with derived equilibria and propositions.,1,2,Stage game is between one individual and one firm; repeated with many individuals but each period is a two-player interaction.,0.95,-1,Game is infinitely repeated; not a finite number of rounds.,1,1,"Individual chooses between trusting (fixed investment c) or not; there is no continuous contribution level, so the binary trust decision is effectively an all-or-nothing investment.",0.9,N/A,Not a public goods or contribution game; analytical model with no human endowment allocation mechanism.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined; this is a trust/hold-up game, not a linear public goods game.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely theoretical.,1,0,Players observe public history of cheating in baseline but there is no notion of payoff/punishment summaries as in experiments.,0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or IDs; reputation is modeled via game history.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Infinite-horizon analytical model; round information is common knowledge but not modeled as a UI variable.,0.9,0,No costly punishment stage; only endogenous refusal to trust as a reputational sanction.,0.95,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,"No costly reward mechanism; firm’s fair behavior is part of equilibrium strategies, not a separate reward action.",0.95,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,"Model specifies an investment cost c and payoffs α, 1−α, β but does not define a finite endowment of tokens/money for an individual.",0.7,N/R,"No empirical data; model characterizes trust/no-trust decisions and payoffs, not observed contribution rates.",1,N/R,"No empirical data; investment c is a parameter, not a measured outcome.",1,N/R,Paper discusses welfare qualitatively; does not report numerical efficiency measures.,0.9,N/R,"Payoffs are expressed symbolically (functions of α, β, δ etc.), not as empirical outcome measures for specific parameter values.",0.9,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is purely theoretical with no data collection environment.,1,"Infinite-horizon one-sided trust/hold-up game between a firm and randomly matched individuals from two observable groups (majority x and minority y). Individuals decide whether to trust (incurring cost c) or not; firm chooses to cheat or be fair. Payoffs: if trust and fair, individual α−c>0, firm 1−α; if trust and cheat, individual −c, firm 1; no trust yields 0 to both. The firm may become inept with small probability ε and/or biased against a group with probability φ_p. Equilibrium concepts include solidarity trigger, discriminatory trigger, coalition-proofness, and various enforcement and information extensions.","Summarized from Sections 2 and 3 where the stage game, matching process, and extensions are defined.",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0248599.md,"Baseline (IS; No peer) – Individual sanctions, no peer punishment","Table 1 labels this treatment as 'Baseline (IS; No peer)', which is individual sanctions without peer punishment.",1,Sanction regime: Individual sanctions (IS); Peer punishment: absent; Collective sanctions: absent,2×2 design in Table 1 crosses individual vs collective sanctions with presence/absence of peer punishment; this cell is individual sanctions without peer punishment.,1,True,"Laboratory experiment with human participants at CELSS, Columbia University.",1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment manipulating sanction regimes and peer punishment.,1,True,Conducted in Columbia Experimental Laboratory in the Social Sciences (CELSS).,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations described.,1,False,Contains a payoff formula but no standalone analytical model study.,1,3,“Participants were divided into groups of three.”,1,15,“The experiment consisted of 15 periods.”,1,1,Subjects could invest any number of tokens between 0 and 20; continuous decision.,1,0,Each period participants receive an endowment of 20 tokens and then decide how much to invest; endowment starts private.,0.9,0.5,Payoff term a∑g_j with a=0.5; standard linear PGG implies MPCR=a.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard anonymous VCM; identities not revealed.,0.9,0,Paper does not report feedback on others’ payoffs or punishments beyond what is necessary; no evidence of summary feedback.,0.6,0,Identities within group were not revealed to avoid retaliative strategic punishment; thus punishers’ identities not shown.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Participants were informed of the number of periods in advance.,1,0,This treatment is labeled 'No peer punishment'; only external individual sanctions operate.,1,N/A,No peer punishment stage in this treatment.,1,N/A,No peer punishment mechanism present.,1,0,Only costly deduction (punishment) is available in some treatments; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in design.,1,20,“Participants were divided into groups of three and were provided with an endowment of 20 tokens each.”,1,N/R,Paper reports mean contributions in tokens but not normalized by endowment.,0.2,8.2,Table 3 reports 'Mean' contribution for Baseline as 8.20 tokens.,1,N/R,No explicit or computable ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff reported by treatment.,0.1,N/R,"Only overall average earnings by broader categories (IS vs CS, peer vs no peer) are given, not by specific treatment.",0.2,United States,"Experiment conducted at Columbia University (New York, USA); participants are Columbia undergraduates.",0.9,N/R,Described as 2nd- and 3rd-year students but no age statistics reported.,0.6,Mixed,"Gender comparisons (men vs women) are reported, implying both male and female participants.",1,2nd- and 3rd-year university students at Columbia University,Paper states all participants were 2nd- and 3rd-year students at Columbia University.,1,On site,Lab experiment at CELSS with standard z-Tree and ORSEE recruitment; typical physical lab sessions.,0.9,"Linear public good game with group size 3, endowment 20, continuous contributions. Contributions to public project yield a*Σg with a=0.5. External individual sanction: if group is checked with probability 1/3 and an individual’s contribution is ≤10 tokens (threshold T=11), that individual loses 7 tokens for that period. No peer punishment stage.",Summarized from Method and payoff equation description for this treatment.,1
