10.3389_fpsyg.2021.676064.md,"Study 1a – Punishment treatment (keep vs punish, amounts 0/3/5 RMB)","Study 1a describes third parties in two treatments; this row covers the punishment-treatment scenarios where the third party can keep or punish with 0, 3, or 5 RMB.",1,"Intervention method fixed to punishment (choice between keep and punishment); transfer amount levels: 0, 3, 5 RMB; third parties not free to choose between punishment and compensation.","Method (punishment vs compensation) and transfer amount (0,3,5) are manipulated; in this treatment, only punishment vs keep is available.",1,True,Human college students rated reputations (fourth parties).,1,True,"Controlled lab vignette experiment with manipulated factors (method, amount).",1,True,Run on computers in a controlled university setting (Eprime 2.0 mentioned for Study 1a as well).,0.9,False,No computational agents; only human raters.,1,False,No purely mathematical model; empirical analyses only.,1,4,"Scenario involves dictator, recipient, third party, and participant as fourth party rater.",1,1,Single hypothetical dictator-game scenario per trial; no repeated interaction structure.,0.9,1,"Third party could choose any transfer amount up to 10 RMB (keep vs continuous transfer), not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,No public-goods contribution; all endowment starts in private account of third party.,1,N/A,"Dictator game with third-party transfer, not a public-goods game with MPCR.",1,0,No communication between any players described.,1,0,Fourth parties only see scenario text with one dictator allocation; no payoff summaries across others.,1,0,No identities revealed; hypothetical third parties in scenarios.,0.9,0,No rewards; only punishment vs keep in this treatment.,1,0,No repeated-game horizon information; just ratings over vignettes.,1,1,Third party can transfer RMB to reduce dictator’s payoff.,1,1,"For each RMB to punish, third party pays 1 RMB from 10-RMB endowment.",1,1,"For each RMB, dictator loses 1 RMB (1:1 effect).",1,0,No option to increase recipient’s payoff in punishment treatment.,1,N/A,No reward/compensation option in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward/compensation option in this treatment.,1,10,Third parties endowed with 10 RMB each.,1,N/R,No contributions to a public good; outcome is reputational ratings only.,1,N/R,Study 1a reports reputational scores as DV; no behavioral contribution by participants.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported or derivable for a hypothetical vignette.,1,N/R,No payoffs for raters; only hypothetical third-party actions; group payoff not analyzed.,1,China,Participants recruited from Shanghai Dianji University in China.,1,"17-23 (M=19.42, SD=1.40)",Age range and mean reported for Study 1a sample.,1,"44 male, 59 female","Study 1a: 103 students, 44 male; rest inferred female.",0.95,College students,Sample described as college students from Shanghai Dianji University.,1,On site,Conducted on computers (Eprime) with university students physically present.,0.9,"Dictator allocates 10 RMB as 8:2; third party has 10 RMB and must choose between keep and punishment (0,3,5 RMB) with 1:1 loss to dictator; participants (fourth parties) rate reputation (niceness, trustworthiness, group-concernedness, respectability) on 1–7 scales across 24 randomized trials.",Summarizes core vignette and rating structure for this condition.,1
