10.1016_j.physa.2015.12.128.md,"Simulation – Pure strategies, varying lifespan","Paper describes one series of simulations where only pure strategies are allowed and lifespan L is varied (5, 10, 20, 30) with fixed other parameters.",0.9,"Strategy space: pure strategies only; Lifespan L ∈ {5,10,20,30}; Waiting time W=2; Payoff matrix R=3, T=5, S=0, P=1; Population size N=100; Mutation rate=0.02","Independent variables explicitly mentioned around Fig. 1: upper four histograms (pure strategies), with lifespans 5,10,20,30 and the listed fixed parameters.",1,False,Simulation study with evolving strategies; no human or animal participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral or field experiment; it is a numerical evolutionary simulation.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors repeatedly state they run simulations with specified parameters and Moran process dynamics.,1,False,"This condition corresponds to numerical simulations, not the theoretical NSS analysis.",1,100,Population size N=100 stated in Fig. 1 caption.,1,800000,They state convergence takes from 6×10^4 to 8×10^5 rounds; use upper bound as maximum rounds before convergence for this series.,0.4,1,"Strategies specify cooperation probability p∈[0,1]; implies continuous contribution/choice rather than discrete all-or-nothing contributions. Map to non all-or-nothing.",0.6,N/A,"This is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with binary actions C/D, not a public goods contribution setting.",1,N/A,"Prisoner’s Dilemma, not a linear public goods game, so MPCR concept does not apply.",1,N/A,No human participants; communication between simulated agents is not modeled as chat.,1,0,Agents have no observation of histories beyond last round and no payoff summaries are mentioned.,0.7,N/A,"Punishment is via automatic waiting after breakup, not directed by identity recognition; and no human interface.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with identities; payoffs follow the PD matrix mechanically.,1,0,Agents are evolutionary automata; no notion of displayed remaining rounds.,0.9,1,Conditional dissociation with waiting time W acts as a punishment; authors explicitly frame it as punishment.,1,1,"During waiting, payoff is O which is set such that being single is no better than being defected; in simulations they set waiting payoff to 0 and P=1, so relative cost per round vs mutual defection is approximately 1.",0.4,2,"Waiting time W=2; each breakup causes both players to lose 2 rounds of opportunity, treated here as 2 punishment units.",0.6,0,No costly rewarding or bonus mechanism is described; only PD payoffs and waiting punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,"Prisoner’s Dilemma specified only by payoffs R,T,S,P; no endowment or budget per round is defined.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported as frequencies of surviving strategies, not normalized cooperation rates.",0.2,N/R,They do not report per-round number of cooperators or similar contribution counts.,0.2,N/R,No ratio of actual to maximum group payoff is reported.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate group payoffs are not tabulated; only qualitative comparisons are discussed.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is an agent-based/evolutionary simulation without human subjects.,1,"Iterated 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with conditional dissociation: strategies (p,q) where p is cooperation probability each round and q is probability to continue after being defected; random matching in a well-mixed population using a Moran process with lifespan L and waiting time W=2.",Summarized from Model and simulation description around Sections 2 and 3.1 and Fig. 1.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2015.12.128.md,"Simulation – Mixed strategies, varying lifespan","Paper describes a second series of simulations where mixed strategies are allowed and lifespan L is varied (5, 10, 20, 30) with same other parameters; these are the lower four histograms in Fig. 1.",0.9,"Strategy space: mixed strategies allowed over [0,1]^2; Lifespan L ∈ {5,10,20,30}; Waiting time W=2; Payoff matrix R=3, T=5, S=0, P=1; Population size N=100; Mutation rate=0.02",Explicitly stated for the lower four histograms in Fig. 1 and Section 3.1.,1,False,Numerical simulations with evolving strategy parameters; no human participants.,1,False,This is not an empirical experiment; it is an in silico evolutionary model.,1,False,No physical laboratory or field site is used.,1,True,Authors run 100 simulations per parameter set with Moran process dynamics.,1,False,"This condition is part of the numerical simulations, distinct from the analytical NSS work.",1,100,Population size N=100 specified in Fig. 1 caption.,1,800000,They report convergence between 6×10^4 and 8×10^5 rounds; use maximum as an upper bound per run.,0.4,1,Strategies are parameterized by real-valued cooperation probability p; action is binary but not an all-or-nothing contribution game; mapped as non all-or-nothing.,0.6,N/A,Not a public goods contribution framework; only binary C/D choices.,1,N/A,MPCR is undefined for a 2×2 Prisoner’s Dilemma.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication interface.,1,0,Agents only use one-period memory of opponent’s last action; no payoff or punishment summaries are presented.,0.7,N/A,"Punishment is structural (waiting after breakup), not targeted by identity, and this is not a human interface.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism beyond PD payoffs.,1,0,Simulated agents do not observe a displayed remaining number of rounds; lifespan is a model parameter only.,0.9,1,Waiting after conditional dissociation is explicitly framed as punishment.,1,1,Waiting payoff is 0 whereas P=1; per round of waiting costs approximately 1 payoff unit relative to mutual defection.,0.4,2,"Waiting time W=2; thus each breakup entails 2 waiting rounds, interpreted as 2 punishment units.",0.6,0,No separate rewarding institution or costly reward action is modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,"Game is specified via payoff matrix, not via endowments.",1,N/R,"Results are in terms of regions of strategy space where populations end, not explicit average cooperation rates.",0.2,N/R,No per-round cooperation counts or contributions are reported.,0.2,N/R,They do not report efficiency as payoff relative to cooperative optimum.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate payoffs across the population are not tabulated.,0.2,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based numerical simulation only.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with conditional dissociation where each agent’s strategy is (p,q): p is probability to cooperate each time she plays, q is probability to continue after being defected. Population is well-mixed and matched in random pairs, with Moran process selection, finite lifespan L, waiting time W=2, and mutation rate 0.02.",Summarized from Sections 2 and 3.1 and Fig. 1 description for mixed-strategy simulations.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2015.12.128.md,Simulation – Effect of waiting time W,"Section 4 and Fig. 2 describe simulations varying waiting time W while holding other parameters fixed (N=100, lifespan=100).",0.9,"Waiting time W varied over multiple values (increasing to large W); Lifespan L=100; Population size N=100; Payoff matrix R=3, T=5, S=0, P=1; mutation rate not explicitly restated, presumed small as in earlier simulations; strategy space appears to allow mixed strategies.","Fig. 2 caption: 'The effect of waiting time. When waiting time increases, both probabilities increase. Population size N=100, lifespan=100. Each point ... average of 100 simulation results.' The earlier default payoff matrix is kept.",0.8,False,These are again computational simulations with evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,Not an empirical or field experiment.,1,False,No lab setting; purely in silico.,1,True,They run 100 simulations per parameter setting of W.,1,False,This portion reports numerical results instead of analytical derivations.,1,100,Fig. 2 caption: Population size N=100.,1,N/R,For the waiting-time simulations the number of rounds until measurement is not stated; only that each point averages 100 simulations.,0.1,1,"Same underlying (p,q) strategy parametrization with probability of cooperation; not an all-or-nothing contribution game.",0.6,N/A,"Binary cooperation in PD, no continuous endowment allocation.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication channel.,1,0,"Model specifies only one-round memory of actions, no exposure to payoff or punishment summaries.",0.7,N/A,"Punishment is endogenous waiting, not based on identified punishers.",1,N/A,No explicit rewards implemented.,1,0,Agents do not see remaining life; lifespan is fixed but not an observed round counter.,0.9,1,Varying waiting time W directly manipulates severity of conditional dissociation punishment.,1,N/R,Exact per-unit cost is not parameterized separately; W itself is varied and payoff during waiting is described qualitatively; no numeric value for waiting payoff in this section.,0.2,N/R,"They vary W (duration of punishment), but do not express a fixed mapping from cost units to harm units for this series.",0.2,0,No mention of any costly reward; only cooperation payoffs and waiting punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Model is specified via PD payoffs R,T,S,P, not initial endowments.",1,N/R,Outcomes reported as average cooperation and continue probabilities but not as normalized contribution relative to an endowment; exact values not tabulated.,0.3,N/R,No counts of cooperative actions are tabulated.,0.2,N/R,They only discuss that cooperation becomes more competitive; no explicit efficiency measure is given.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics are reported in Section 4.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Simulations of evolving agents only.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with conditional dissociation and variable waiting time W. Strategies (p,q) denote cooperation and continuation probabilities. Population of N=100 evolves via Moran process with lifespan 100; they measure how equilibrium cooperation and continuation probabilities vary as W increases.",Summarized from Section 4 and Fig. 2 caption: 'The effect of waiting time.',1
10.1016_j.physa.2015.12.128.md,Analytical model – NSS characterization (infinite lifespan),Section 3.2 and Appendix present analytical results deriving Neutrally Stable Strategies for the conditional dissociation model with infinite lifespan.,1,"Analytical parameters: waiting time w (fixed arbitrary); PD payoffs R,T,S,P satisfying T>R>P>S≥0 and T+S<2R; strategies (p,q) ∈ [0,1]^2; lifespan assumed infinite.",Section 2 defines the model and Section 3.2 derives NSS types I and II under these general parameters and infinite repetition.,1,False,Purely theoretical analysis with no empirical data or simulations in this subsection.,1,False,No controlled experimental manipulation; formal game-theoretic modeling and derivations.,1,False,Not a lab or field study.,1,False,"This subsection uses analytical calculation of stationary distributions and optimization conditions, not numerical runs.",1,True,They explicitly derive stationary distribution of a Markov chain and characterize NSS sets with calculus.,1,N/A,Analysis assumes an infinite population limit for evolutionary stability; no specific finite N is used.,1,N/A,Interactions are modeled as infinitely repeated (stationary distribution of Markov chain); not a finite number of rounds.,1,1,Model is binary PD action C/D governed by cooperation probability p; maps to non all-or-nothing contribution setting.,0.6,N/A,No endowment contribution framework; only action choice.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human participants or explicit communication in the analytical model.,1,0,Players only condition on last-round action; no payoff or punishment summaries are assumed.,0.8,N/A,Punishment is via anonymous separation and waiting; no identity-based targeting.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,0,Game is modeled as infinite horizon; no displayed remaining rounds.,1,1,Waiting after breakup is explicitly treated as punishment in the analytical derivation (payoff O ≤ S).,1,N/R,Analysis leaves waiting payoff O unspecified aside from inequality; no numeric cost provided.,0.1,N/R,Waiting time w is a parameter but they do not define a discrete 'punishment unit' mapping cost to harm.,0.2,0,Only PD payoffs and waiting punishment; no separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate rewarding.,1,N/A,No separate rewarding.,1,N/A,"Game specified via payoff matrix, not endowments.",1,N/R,"Analytical results classify stable strategies (0,1) or (1,q) but do not report an aggregate contribution rate for a finite population.",0.3,N/R,No explicit per-round cooperation counts are given; payoffs are in expectation form.,0.2,N/R,They focus on stability conditions; no explicit efficiency metrics relative to maximum cooperation.,0.3,N/R,"They specify individual expected payoffs u^{p,q}(x,y) but not group-level aggregate payoff measures.",0.3,N/A,Analytical model of abstract agents; no human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical game-theoretic analysis.,1,"Analytical characterization of Neutrally Stable Strategies in iterated PD with conditional dissociation and waiting time w. Uses Markov chain over playing and w waiting states to derive stationary distribution and expected payoff u^{p,q}(x,y). Shows two NSS sets: I) {(0,1)} (tolerant defectors) and II) {(1,q) | q ≤ (R+wR−T)/(wR)} (cooperators with limited tolerance).",Summarized from Section 3.2 and Appendix derivation.,1
10.1038_s41598-023-37286-8.md,Third-Party Dictator Game – Costly punishment condition,The scanning task had two payment conditions: Costly (punishment reduces own payoff) and Free (Control). This row describes the Costly punishment condition.,1,"Payment condition = Costly; Social distance levels = {1,2,3,5,10,20,50,100}; Inequity levels = {90–10, 85–15, 80–20}; punishment options = {0,5,10,15,20,25,30}","Payment condition (Costly vs Free), social distance, and inequity were manipulated within the TP-DG. This row captures the Costly level of the payment condition factor, with all SD and inequity levels available.",1,True,Human undergraduates played the TP-DG in an fMRI scanner.,1,True,"The study used randomized, controlled task conditions (Costly vs Free, SD, inequity) in a lab/fMRI setting.",1,True,Participants were scanned in a 3T MRI scanner in a controlled lab environment.,1,False,No agent-based or numeric-only simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Although computational models are used, they are fit to behavioral data and not purely analytical proofs.",1,3,"Each scenario involved a dictator (A), a recipient (S), and the participant as third-party observer.",1,288,6 sessions × 54 trials each = 324 total trials; 48 target (unfair) trials per session → 288 unfair target trials where main DV (punishment) is analyzed. Filler trials are excluded from main analysis.,0.7,1,"Participants chose punishment from multiple discrete amounts (0–30 CNY), not all-or-nothing.",1,0,"This is a punishment task, not a contribution game; endowment starts entirely in the observer’s private account.",0.8,N/R,No public good or marginal per capita return is defined in this modified TP-DG.,1,0,No communication between players was described; only individual decisions in the scanner.,1,0,Participants saw only the dictator’s current allocation and their own options; no summary statistics of others’ behavior or payoffs were reported.,0.9,0,Dictators and recipients were represented generically (A and S and SD icons); no identification of who punished whom was revealed to other players.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism was present in the task.,1,0,No mention that the total number of trials or remaining trials was displayed to participants.,0.7,1,Core manipulation: participants could pay to punish dictators.,1,1,"In the Costly condition, spending p CNY from the observer reduced the dictator’s payoff by 3p; thus cost per punishment unit to the punisher is 1.",1,3,"For every 1 CNY spent by the third-party, the dictator’s payoff was reduced by three-fold (3 CNY).",1,0,Participants had only punishment options; no reward to increase dictator or recipient payoff.,1,N/A,There was no reward mechanism.,1,N/A,There was no reward mechanism.,1,50,"Each trial, participants were endowed with 50 CNY.",1,N/R,"Primary DV is punishment amount, not a contribution to a public good; no normalized contribution rate reported.",1,6.15,Mean punishment amount in the Costly condition was reported as 6.15 ± 0.72 CNY.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric (realized vs maximum group payoff) was reported or derivable from aggregate data provided.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports only punishment levels and neural data, not average total payoffs to all three players.",0.2,China,Participants were Chinese undergraduates recruited at South China Normal University.,1,"Mean = 20.42, SD = 1.48",Reported for the final sample of 31 participants.,1,"16 men, 15 women","Final sample included 31 participants, including 16 men; remaining 15 inferred as women since no other genders were mentioned.",0.8,Undergraduate university students,Participants were described as Chinese undergraduates.,1,On site,Participants completed the task in an fMRI scanner at a research facility.,1,"Modified Third-Party Dictator Game. Dictator (A) has 100 CNY and chooses allocations (unfair: 90–10, 85–15, 80–20; quasi-fair fillers: 65–35, 60–40, 55–45) to recipient (S). Third-party observer starts with 50 CNY per trial and selects a punishment level from {0,5,10,15,20,25,30}. In the Costly condition, punishing by p costs the observer p and reduces the dictator’s payoff by 3p (not below 0). Social distance between observer and dictator takes values {1,2,3,5,10,20,50,100}, displayed via a 101-icon scale; recipient always at SD 100.",Summary of the task structure specific to the costly punishment blocks drawn from the Methods section.,1
10.1038_s41598-023-37286-8.md,Third-Party Dictator Game – Free punishment (Control) condition,The same TP-DG task included a Free punishment condition (Control) where punishment did not reduce the observer’s payoff.,1,"Payment condition = Free (non-costly Control); Social distance levels = {1,2,3,5,10,20,50,100}; Inequity levels = {90–10, 85–15, 80–20}; punishment options = {0,5,10,15,20,25,30}","Payment condition (Costly vs Free), SD, and inequity were varied; this row captures the Free (non-costly) level of the payment condition factor.",1,True,"Same human participant experiment, different payment condition.",1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of payment condition within subjects.,1,True,Conducted in an fMRI lab setting.,1,False,No separate simulation-only condition.,1,False,"Analyses are empirical and model-based, not pure analytical derivations.",1,3,"Same triadic structure (dictator, recipient, third-party) as in the Costly condition.",1,288,Trial structure and number of unfair target trials are symmetric across Costly and Free blocks.,0.7,1,Multiple discrete punishment options (0–30 CNY) as in the Costly condition.,1,0,Observer’s 50 CNY endowment is fully in a private account; no public fund.,0.8,N/R,No public good is defined; only punishment of a dictator.,1,0,No communication between players is described.,1,0,Only current trial allocations and punishment options are displayed.,0.9,0,Punishment choices are not communicated to other players within the experiment.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No evidence that total or remaining trials were displayed.,0.7,1,Participants could choose to punish dictators; in this condition punishment is free.,1,0,"In the Free punishment condition, participants could punish the dictator without any cost to their own endowment.",1,3,The task description states generally that the dictator’s payoff is reduced three-fold relative to the third-party’s expenditure; in Free condition the effective cost to the punisher is 0 but the dictator reduction per nominal punishment unit remains 3.,0.7,0,No reward or positive transfer option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,50,"Each trial, participants received 50 CNY also in Free blocks.",1,N/R,No contribution behavior is defined; the DV is punishment level.,1,8.32,Mean punishment amount in the Control (non-costly Free) condition was reported as 8.32 ± 0.74 CNY.,1,N/R,Efficiency relative to a maximum payoff was not reported.,0.3,N/R,"Average total payoffs across dictator, recipient, and observer for Free trials were not given.",0.2,China,Same participant pool as the Costly condition.,1,"Mean = 20.42, SD = 1.48",Same participants took part in both conditions.,1,"16 men, 15 women",Same final sample as in the Costly condition.,0.8,Undergraduate university students,Same participant pool.,1,On site,Performed in the MRI scanner in a controlled lab.,1,"Same modified TP-DG as in the Costly condition, but punishment is non-costly: third-party observers start with 50 CNY per trial and can choose a punishment level from {0,5,10,15,20,25,30} that reduces the dictator’s payoff (threefold per nominal unit) without affecting their own payoff. Social distance between observer and dictator again takes values {1,2,3,5,10,20,50,100}, recipient S always at SD 100. Unfair allocations are 90–10, 85–15, 80–20; quasi-fair filler allocations 65–35, 60–40, 55–45.",Summarizes the structure of the Free punishment control blocks based on the Methods section.,1
