f costs from partner's withdrawal or social sanctions is not quantified.,1,N/R,No numeric mapping between punishment expenditure and impact given.,1,0,"Institution primarily adds deterrent costs for deviation and coordination benefits, not explicit rewards.",0.8,N/A,No formal reward mechanism specified.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism specified.,1,N/R,No explicit endowments or resource units; payoffs are in relative fitness terms.,1,N/R,"Outcome of interest is correlated equilibrium at mutual monogamy, not a contribution measure.",1,N/R,No contributions quantified.,1,N/R,Efficiency improvements discussed qualitatively; no ratio reported.,1,N/R,Group fitness advantages are discussed but not numerically specified.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely conceptual correlated-equilibrium model of marriage norms.,1,"Conceptual extension of the monogamous vs opportunistic game where the institution of marriage acts as a 'choreographer' in Aumann's sense, correlating partners' strategies toward mutual monogamy. Deviation risks loss of partner's cooperation and (often) social punishment, effectively making mutual monogamy a correlated equilibrium.",Summarizes author’s discussion of marriage as a correlated equilibrium and social norm.,1
10.1086_684166.md,"Matrix A1 – Degenerate Augmented Stag Hunt (r = 0, p = 0)",Paper explicitly labels A1 as a 3×3 game obtained from the Augmented Stag Hunt with α = 1/4 and r = p = 0.,1,"Cost parameters of punishment: punisher cost r = 0, punishee cost p = 0 (b and c not applicable).","Within-section comparison varies r and p across matrices A1–A5 while keeping α = 1/4; this condition has r = 0, p = 0.",1,False,Study is purely theoretical/evolutionary game analysis with replicator dynamics and payoff matrices; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only mathematical models and numerical simulations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only theoretical/simulation work.,1,True,"Author uses deterministic discrete replicator dynamics and replicator with mutation, implemented in MatLab and Dynamo.",1,True,Provides formal matrix specifications and analytical propositions in appendix in addition to numerical orbits.,1,2,All games are described as two-player (symmetric two-player Augmented Stag Hunt).,1,1,"Stage game is a one-shot Stag Hunt/augmented game; replicator dynamics model repeated population interactions, not repeated rounds within a match.",0.9,1,"Players choose among discrete pure strategies C, D, P, not a continuous contribution level; contribution notion from public goods doesn’t apply, so coded as general strategic choice (treated as any-strategy rather than binary all-or-nothing contribution).",0.6,N/A,There is no endowment or contribution to a public good; only abstract payoffs.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,No feedback structure of this kind is specified; interactions are abstract payoff mappings.,1,N/A,No identities or information structure for human players; punishment is just a payoff transformation.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism is modeled, only punishment-like payoffs in some matrices.",1,0,"No user interface; dynamics are mathematical, not participant-facing.",1,1,"Strategy P (punish) is present, though here r = p = 0 so punishment is costless/degenerate.",1,0,Condition definition: r = p = 0 so punisher cost r = 0.,1,0,Punishee cost p = 0 per act given r = 0 and p = b r + c; in A1 it’s specified r = p = 0.,1,0,"No strategy or parameter for rewarding, only punishment.",1,N/A,No reward option in model.,1,N/A,No reward option in model.,1,N/R,"Game is specified directly in payoffs, not via endowments.",1,N/R,"Outcomes of interest are basins of attraction (measure of initial states leading to each attractor), not contributions.",1,N/R,No tokens contributed; only strategy frequencies.,1,N/R,"Paper reports payoffs and basin sizes, not normalized efficiency indices.",1,N/R,"While payoff matrices list per-player payoffs, no single aggregate group payoff for equilibria is highlighted as a DV for the numeric examples; focus is on attraction basins.",0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists solely of analytical derivations and computer simulations without subjects.,1,"3×3 symmetric coordination game (Augmented Stag Hunt) with strategies C (cooperate/acquiesce), D (defect), P (cooperate then punish). Here punishment is costless (r = p = 0), so P is effectively a clone of C. Deterministic and stochastic replicator dynamics used to compute basins of attraction: for A1, m(Δ_D) ≈ 0.75 and m(Δ_Ξ) ≈ 0.25, where Δ_Ξ is the basin of polymorphic strategies mixing C and P.",Summarizes the structure and reported results around matrix A1 from section 3.,1
10.1086_684166.md,"Matrix A2 – Augmented Stag Hunt (r = 1, b = 1, c = 0, p = 1)","Paper defines A2 as an Augmented Stag Hunt with α = 1/4, r = b = 1, c = 0, hence p = 1.",1,"Punishment parameters: punisher cost r = 1, multiplier b = 1, fixed term c = 0, punishee cost p = 1 (α = 1/4).",Section 3 explicitly states this parameter set when introducing matrix A2.,1,False,No participants; only theoretical and computational analysis.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical simulations of replicator dynamics.,1,False,No lab or field setting is involved.,1,True,Deterministic discrete replicator and replicator with mutation (δ = .01) are run on A2.,1,True,A2 is derived from the general analytical payoff matrix; results discussed with theoretical interpretation.,1,2,Augmented Stag Hunt is defined as a 2-player symmetric game.,1,1,Model is a one-shot 2-player game embedded in population dynamics; no explicit repeated rounds per pair.,0.9,1,"Action space is discrete (C, D, P) rather than a continuous contribution level.",0.6,N/A,No endowment or contribution process is modeled.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No communication or humans involved.,1,0,No user interface or earnings feedback structure; only payoff matrices.,1,N/A,No identities; punishment is represented only as payoff reductions.,1,N/A,No reward option in model.,1,0,No round counter in a subject interface; purely theoretical dynamics.,1,1,"Strategy P is present and now carries nonzero costs (r = 1, p = 1).",1,1,Definition of the A2 condition specifies r = 1.,1,1,Punishee cost p = 1 when a player using P punishes a defector.,1,0,Model only introduces punishment; no symmetric reward strategy.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game payoffs are direct; no initial token endowment described.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are discussed in terms of basins of attraction, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contributions quantified; only strategies and payoffs.,1,N/R,Paper reports relative basin sizes; does not compute efficiency ratios.,1,N/R,"Although per-player payoffs appear in A2, no single aggregate payoff outcome is reported as a primary numerical result for this condition.",0.8,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Condition is a parameterization of a theoretical game simulated via replicator dynamics.,1,"3×3 Augmented Stag Hunt with α = 1/4 where P punishes a defector with cost r = 1 to punisher and p = 1 to punishee. Normalized payoff matrix A2: [[9,4,9],[8,8,4],[9,0,9]]. Deterministic replicator and replicator with mutation (δ = .01) are analyzed. Reported basin sizes: m(Δ_D) ≈ 0.56, m(Δ_Ξ) ≈ 0.44, where Δ_Ξ is the basin of Nash equilibria mixing C and P.",Summarizes the specific payoff matrix and reported numerical basin measures for A2.,1
10.1086_684166.md,"Matrix A3 – Augmented Stag Hunt (r = 1/4, p = 5/4)","Paper defines A3 as Augmented Stag Hunt with α = 1/4, punisher cost r = 1/4, punishee cost p = 5/4.",1,"Punishment parameters: punisher cost r = 1/4, punishee cost p = 5/4 (b and c chosen implicitly so that p = 5/4).",Section introducing A3 states these particular values and interprets them relative to hare/deer payoffs.,1,False,Only evolutionary game-theoretic analysis and simulations; no human data.,1,False,"A3 is a parameterized matrix used in simulations, not an experiment.",1,False,No lab or field environment.,1,True,Replicator dynamics (deterministic and with mutation δ = .01) applied to A3.,1,True,A3 derived from general analytical specification; qualitative claims about net losses and equilibrium structure.,1,2,Same two-player Augmented Stag Hunt framework.,1,1,Single-shot underlying game with population dynamics.,0.9,1,"Choice over discrete strategies C, D, P, not a contribution continuum.",0.6,N/A,No endowment or contribution framework.,1,N/R,No MPCR reported; not a standard public good.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,0,No presentation/feedback in a subject interface.,1,N/A,No identities; punishment is internal to payoff matrix.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No explicit round display; dynamic is mathematical.,1,1,"Strategy P is present and costly (r = 1/4, p = 5/4).",1,0.25,Given directly: punisher’s cost r = 1/4.,1,1.25,Given directly: punishee’s cost p = 5/4 = 1.25.,1,0,Model only features punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoff matrices specify utilities directly.,1,N/R,Main reported outcomes: basin sizes m(Δ_D) and m(Δ_Ξ). No contribution variable.,1,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,Efficiency not numerically computed; focus is on probability of evolving to optimal equilibria (in terms of basins).,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix A3 = [[10,5,10],[9,9,4],[10,4,10]] is given, but no single numeric group payoff is treated as DV; emphasis is on dynamic stability and attraction.",0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The condition is a simulated evolutionary game.,1,"Augmented Stag Hunt with α = 1/4, punisher’s cost r = 1/4, punishee’s cost p = 5/4. Normalized payoff matrix A3: [[10,5,10],[9,9,4],[10,4,10]]. Deterministic replicator and replicator with mutation δ = .01 show that m(Δ_D) ≈ 0.32 while m(Δ_Ξ) ≈ 0.68, so polymorphic C/P strategies have a much larger basin than defecting D.",Summarizes structure and reported basin-of-attraction measures specific to A3.,1
10.1086_684166.md,"Matrix A4 – Augmented Stag Hunt (r = 1/2, b = 5/2, c = 0)","Paper introduces A4 with α = 1/4, b = 5/2, c = 0 and punisher’s cost r = 1/2.",1,"Punishment parameters: α = 1/4, b = 5/2, c = 0, punisher cost r = 1/2 so punishee cost p = b·r + c = (5/2)(1/2) = 5/4.","Section describing A4 explicitly sets α, b, c, and r; p is defined as b r + c, yielding 5/4.",1,False,"No empirical data, only theory and simulation.",1,False,A4 is not a subject experiment; it is a parameter configuration for simulations.,1,False,No laboratory environment.,1,True,Replicator dynamics (deterministic and with δ = .01) are run on matrix A4.,1,True,A4 built analytically from general punishment function p = b r + c and then analyzed with dynamics.,1,2,Continues same two-player Augmented Stag Hunt framework.,1,1,Underlying game is a single interaction per pair; repetition is via population dynamics.,0.9,1,"Action is discrete strategy selection, not divisible contribution.",0.6,N/A,No public-good contribution setting.,1,N/R,Not a standard public good.,1,N/A,No communication among human subjects.,1,0,No subject interface; dynamic is represented analytically.,1,N/A,No identities or observability; punishment is via payoff function.,1,N/A,No rewarding.,1,0,No explicit rounds to show to subjects.,1,1,Strategy P with positive costs is present.,1,0.5,Given that r = 1/2 for A4.,1,1.25,"With b = 5/2, r = 1/2, c = 0, p = b r = 5/4 = 1.25.",1,0,Only punishment modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Payoffs given directly, no endowments.",1,N/R,Key reported outcomes: m(Δ_D) and m(Δ_Ξ); no contributions.,1,N/R,No tokens or contribution amounts.,1,N/R,Efficiency isn’t numerically derived as a ratio; analysis is about relative attracting power.,1,N/R,"A4 payoff matrix [[10,5,10],[9,9,4],[10,3,10]] is specified, but group payoff is not singled out as a DV for this numeric example.",0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulated evolutionary model only.,1,"Augmented Stag Hunt with α = 1/4, b = 5/2, c = 0, r = 1/2 (so p = 5/4). Normalized payoff matrix A4: [[10,5,10],[9,9,4],[10,3,10]]. Deterministic and stochastic replicator dynamics applied. Reported basins: m(Δ_D) ≈ 0.39, m(Δ_Ξ) ≈ 0.61, indicating polymorphic C/P strategies are more likely to evolve than D.",Condenses the condition-specific matrix and numeric basin sizes given in text for A4.,1
10.1086_684166.md,"Matrix A5 – Augmented Stag Hunt (r = 5/4, b = 5/2, c = 0)","Paper defines A5 with same α, b, c as A4 but higher punisher cost r = 5/4; provides the resulting matrix.",1,"Punishment parameters: α = 1/4, b = 5/2, c = 0, punisher cost r = 5/4, so punishee cost p = b·r + c = (5/2)(5/4) = 25/8.","Section states r = 5/4 while keeping b = 5/2, c = 0; p is defined as b r + c.",1,False,Condition is part of a theoretical/simulation study; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; it’s a payoff parameterization used in simulations.,1,False,No laboratory implementation.,1,True,Replicator dynamics (deterministic and with δ = .01) run on A5 matrix.,1,True,A5 is analytically constructed from the punishment function and then analyzed by dynamics.,1,2,Same two-player Augmented Stag Hunt framework throughout.,1,1,Game is single-shot between two players; repetition only at population level.,0.9,1,"Players pick among discrete pure strategies C, D, P, not a share of an endowment.",0.6,N/A,No contribution or endowment is modeled.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No display of others’ outcomes; just theoretical payoffs.,1,N/A,Identities and observability not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No experimental interface; hence no round display.,1,1,"P strategy exists with substantial costs: r = 5/4, p derived via b r + c.",1,1.25,Punisher cost r = 5/4 = 1.25 for A5.,1,3.125,"Given b = 5/2 and r = 5/4, p = (5/2)*(5/4) = 25/8 = 3.125.",1,0,Only punishment modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Utilities given directly via payoff matrix.,1,N/R,"DV is the measure of basins of attraction, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contributions modeled.,1,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency metrics for A5.,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix A5 = [[35,25,35],[33,33,8],[35,15,35]] is shown, but group payoff isn’t isolated as a DV; focus is on dynamic outcomes.",0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation of theoretical game only.,1,"Augmented Stag Hunt with α = 1/4, b = 5/2, c = 0, r = 5/4 (p = 25/8). Normalized payoff matrix A5: [[35,25,35],[33,33,8],[35,15,35]]. Deterministic and stochastic replicator dynamics (δ = .01) show m(Δ_Ξ) ≈ 0.74 and m(Δ_D) ≈ 0.26. Punishee’s net loss becomes very large, further increasing the probability that polymorphic C/P strategies evolve instead of D.","Captures the condition-specific parameters, matrix, and reported basin sizes for A5.",1
