ges not reported.,Results of Study 2: “mean age = 37.24”.,1,45% female; remaining male/other not specified.,Study 2 results: “45% females”.,1,N/R,Education not summarized for Study 2.,0.1,Online,Study 2 also run on AMT with online surveys.,1,"Participants read the same PD instructions as Study 1 but make no PD decisions and no monetary punish/reward choices. They answer comprehension questions, then rate Adam on a 1–5 star scale under two hypothetical choices: (a) Adam cooperates; (b) Adam defects. Average rating when Adam cooperates is 4.19 (men) vs 4.24 (women); when Adam defects: 2.96 (men) vs 2.86 (women). Overall, Adam’s cooperative behavior is rated 4.22 on average, defection 2.92.",Summarizes vignette and the main reported rating outcomes.,0.95
10.1007_s40881-021-00099-4.md,Study 2 – Rate-Anna condition (third-party rating of PD choice),Study 2 methods: second between-subject condition is Rate-Anna.,1,Framing of target's gender (Anna = female); hypothetical PD choice (cooperate vs defect) within-subject; outcome is 1–5 star rating.,Between-subject factor is target name; within-subject variation in hypothetical PD choice.,0.9,True,Human AMT respondents provide star ratings.,1,True,Random assignment and controlled vignettes.,1,False,Online AMT implementation.,1,False,Not simulated.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,3,Underlying described PD involves Anna and Participant B with an implicit observer.,0.8,1,Same one-shot PD description as Study 1.,0.9,1,PD decisions are binary; raters themselves do not contribute.,0.7,0,PD is as in Study 1; endowment presumably private.,0.4,N/R,PD parameters not provided.,0,0,No communication described.,1,0,"Raters see no payoff summaries, only hypothetical choices.",0.9,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,Stars do not identify individuals or affect payoffs.,1,0,Single hypothetical interaction.,0.9,0,Only non-incentivized ratings are given.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No monetary reward; only ratings.,1,N/A,No cost associated with ratings.,1,N/A,Ratings do not translate into payoffs.,1,0,Participants receive only a fixed fee (30 cents).,1,N/R,No contributions.,1,N/R,No monetary actions besides accepting participation fee.,1,N/R,No payoff structure to evaluate efficiency.,1,N/R,No interactive payoffs.,1,N/R,Country not stated.,0,Mean age 37.24; individual ages not reported.,Study 2 sample description.,1,45% female; remaining male/other not specified.,Study 2 sample description.,1,N/R,No education breakdown.,0.1,Online,Conducted via AMT.,1,"Participants read PD instructions with Anna and Participant B, pass four comprehension questions, then rate Anna’s hypothetical cooperation and defection on a 1–5 star scale. Average rating when Anna cooperates is 4.21; when she defects, 2.88. Splitting by rater gender: for cooperation, 4.24 (men) vs 4.17 (women); for defection, 2.73 (men) vs 3.07 (women).",Summarizes vignette and main rating outcomes for Anna.,0.95
10.1007_s00191-014-0375-3.md,Sequential punishment model – sequential-move version,The core model in Section 3 is explicitly called the 'sequential punishment model' with a two-move sequential game embedded in a cultural evolution framework; this condition is the sequential-move version contrasted later with a simultaneous-move version.,1,Interaction timing in the nested punishment game: sequential moves with Player 1 then Player 2 (sequential-move punishment).,The paper contrasts a nested two-move sequential punishment game with an otherwise similar simultaneous-move punishment game; timing/sequential structure is the key dimension varied.,1,False,The paper presents a theoretical evolutionary game-theoretic model and Price-equation analysis with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled lab/field experiment or random assignment is conducted; only theoretical models are analyzed.,1,False,There is no laboratory or field setting; the work is purely theoretical.,1,False,"Although evolutionary dynamics are discussed, they are handled analytically via payoff and Price equations, not via explicit numerical or agent-based computer simulations.",0.9,True,"The model is solved using payoff matrices, Nash equilibria, and Price equations, with closed-form expressions for conditions under which altruism can evolve.",1,3,"Section 3 defines the sequential punishment model as having three players, with two active decision-makers and a third as a passive recipient.",1,1,"The sequential punishment interaction is described as a finite two-move sequential game within each period; no repetition of the stage game within a period is modeled, so it is a single-round interaction per grouping.",0.9,1,"Players choose only whether or not to inflict harm (a binary choice), not a continuous contribution level; this aligns more with 'any amount' being not applicable but since the schema forces 0/1, we code 1 and note the binary nature.",0.3,N/A,No public-goods contribution or endowment allocation is modeled; only harm/no-harm decisions in a theoretical game without human subjects.,1,N/R,No public-goods structure or linear return from a group account is defined in this model.,1,N/A,"There are no human subjects or experimental interface, so communication rules are not applicable.",1,0,The model assumes full knowledge of others’ altruism coefficients but does not include displays of earnings or punishment summaries as in an experiment; this is a theoretical construct.,0.7,1,"Players know who punished whom because strategies explicitly refer to punishing Player 1, 2, or 3; thus identities of punished and punisher are implicit in the structure.",0.9,N/A,"There is no reward mechanism in the model, only harm/punishment opportunities.",1,0,The interaction is modeled as a one-shot sequential game within each period; no information about remaining rounds is discussed.,0.8,1,The core decision is whether players inflict harm on others; this is treated as punishment/social control.,1,0,"Player 1 gains felicity benefit \hat{\pi} when punishing and the punishee loses 1; there is no direct cost to the punisher beyond opportunity cost, so explicit cost per punishment is zero.",0.9,1,Each unit of punishment leads to a felicity loss of 1 for the target; thus 1 unit 'spent' (conceptual) results in 1 unit of harm.,0.9,0,"The model includes only opportunities to inflict harm, not to provide positive rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,No monetary or token endowments per player are specified; only felicity payoffs and a fixed felicity baseline f are discussed.,1,N/R,There is no contribution to a public good; the main behavioral variable is punishing vs not punishing and the evolution of altruism levels.,1,N/R,No contribution amounts are defined in tokens or points.,1,N/R,"The paper analyzes social welfare E(W) but does not present a normalized efficiency measure in [0,1] relative to a public-goods maximum.",0.8,N/R,"Although expected social welfare expressions are provided, no single numeric payoff per group is fixed; they are symbolic functions of q, n, and \hat{\pi}.",0.9,N/A,No human participants are involved; it is a purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,The study is a theoretical evolutionary model with no empirical data collection.,1,"Three-player sequential punishment game embedded in a cultural group-selection model. Players 1 and 2 each may inflict harm once in sequence. Punisher gains felicity benefit \hat{\pi} (0<\hat{\pi}<1); punishee suffers felicity loss 1. Player 2 can direct harm either to Player 1 or Player 3, allowing for a punishment scheme deterring Player 1 from harming. Individuals have two possible phenotypes: high altruism H (\theta_H \ge \hat{\pi}) and low altruism L (\theta_L < \hat{\pi}). Population split into groups; group selection via Price equations with migration/dispersal and intergroup variance in altruism.","Summarizes the specified structure in Sections 3–5, including payoff structure, phenotypes, and group-selection framework.",1
10.1007_s00191-014-0375-3.md,Sequential punishment model – simultaneous-move version (comparison condition),Section 4 introduces an 'almost identical model' where punishment choices are made simultaneously rather than sequentially; this is treated as an alternative nested game used for comparison.,1,Interaction timing in the nested punishment game: simultaneous moves by both players (simultaneous-move punishment).,The only stated structural change from the baseline sequential model is that both individuals choose whether to punish in a single simultaneous move.,1,False,This is another analytical theoretical model with no human participants.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with subjects; only model comparison.,1,False,The model is not implemented in a lab or field setting.,1,False,"Results are derived analytically via payoff matrices and Price equations, not via computational simulation runs.",0.9,True,The properties and evolutionary conditions of the simultaneous-move model are derived analytically and contrasted to the sequential case.,1,3,"The simultaneous-move version retains the same underlying structure: three individuals, with two potentially punishing and one passive (Player 3).",1,1,The nested punishment interaction is a one-shot simultaneous decision within each grouping; no repetition is modeled.,0.9,1,"Players choose whether to punish or not in a binary way, similar to the sequential case; contribution amounts to a public good are not part of the model, but the schema forces a 0/1 code.",0.3,N/A,No contributions or endowments are modeled; only punishment decisions in a theoretical setting.,1,N/R,There is no public good or linear return; thus MPCR is undefined.,1,N/A,No experimental communication structure is specified; the model is purely analytical.,1,0,Theoretical agents know types but the model does not specify summaries of others’ earnings or punishments as in an implemented experiment.,0.7,1,Strategies specify that person 1 punishes person 2 or person 2 punishes person 3; identities of punisher and punishee are thus inherent in the model.,0.9,N/A,No positive reward mechanism is defined.,1,0,The simultaneous punishment game is one-shot; no horizon information is relevant.,0.8,1,"Players may choose to inflict harm, framed as punishment, in the simultaneous-move version.",1,0,"As in the sequential case, a punisher gains felicity \hat{\pi} when punishing, while the punishee loses 1; no explicit cost to the punisher per unit of punishment is modeled.",0.9,1,Each punishment action imposes a felicity loss of 1 on the target; we treat this as one unit of harm per unit of punishment.,0.9,0,Only harm/punishment options exist; no rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards in the game.,1,N/A,No rewards in the game.,1,N/R,No endowment amounts are specified; only felicity payoffs are modeled.,1,N/R,No contributions to a public good are defined; the primary outcome of interest is the evolution of altruism and use of punishment.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution decisions are modeled.,1,N/R,"Social welfare E(W_sim) is derived as a function of q and \hat{\pi}, but no normalized efficiency metric (0–1) relative to a maximum is given.",0.8,N/R,"Only symbolic expected welfare expressions (e.g., E(W_sim)) are provided; no fixed numeric group payoff per condition is specified.",0.9,N/A,No human participants are involved; it is a theoretical comparison model.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,The simultaneous-move model is also purely theoretical and analytical.,1,"Three-player punishment game where both individuals who may punish choose simultaneously whether to punish. Each individual with low altruism phenotype L (\theta_L<\hat{\pi}) punishes; high altruism phenotype H (\theta_H\ge\hat{\pi}) does not punish. Default assumptions: person 1 punishes person 2 and person 2 punishes person 3 when they choose to punish. Payoff matrices (Fig. 2) show individuals’ felicity and social welfare. Evolutionary dynamics are analyzed using Price equations, showing that the simultaneous-move version yields a less stringent condition for altruism to evolve than the sequential-move version.",Summarizes the described simultaneous punishment setting and its role as a comparison in the Price-equation analysis.,1
