ual discussion of cooperation and punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward technology is instantiated.,1,0,No experimental endowment is provided because no game is conducted.,1,N/A,No contributions are measured; there are no participants in a game.,1,N/A,No public goods or contribution data are collected.,1,N/A,No payoff structure or experimental outcomes to compute efficiency.,1,N/A,No groups playing games; no payoffs measured.,1,N/A,No human participants are recruited.,1,N/A,No sample of human subjects.,1,N/A,No human participant pool.,1,N/A,No empirical sample.,1,No human,The work is a conceptual/theoretical review with no human-subject or field data collection.,1,N/A,"No specific game or experimental paradigm is implemented; only prior literature (e.g., public goods with punishment) is cited at a high level without parameters.",1
10.1111_j.1439-0310.2012.02075.x.md,Field observations of anthias–blenny interactions on patch reefs 1–5,Single observational field study; same protocol across five reef patches without distinct experimental treatments.,0.95,"No manipulated independent variable; observational comparison of individuals’ spatial positions and, for some analyses, reef patch identity (1–5).",Authors describe only natural variation in spatial use and site fidelity; no experimental manipulation or treatment labels.,0.95,True,Wild fish (anthias and blennies) were individually marked/identified and observed in situ.,1,False,No controlled intervention on behavior or payoffs; purely observational spatial and behavioral recording in the field.,0.95,False,"Work was conducted on natural reef patches in Ras Mohamed National Park using scuba, not in a laboratory or tank.",1,False,"No agent-based, numerical, or other computer simulations reported.",1,False,"Paper reports empirical field observations and standard statistical tests, not an analytical game-theoretic or mathematical model.",1,0,No formal game with defined players or groups of decision-makers; natural shoals of ~100–200 anthias are mentioned but not as a controlled game configuration.,0.2,0,No repeated game structure; continuous observation periods (hours) rather than discrete rounds.,1,0,No contribution decision structure is defined; question is not applicable but encoded as 0 per numeric schema.,0.1,N/A,No endowment or contribution mechanism; not a formal contribution game.,1,N/R,No payoff multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined in this observational field study.,1,0,No designed communication channel; natural fish interactions are not a configured ‘chat’ feature.,0.8,0,Fish are not presented with summary feedback; this concept is not part of the design.,1,0,No experimental punishment stage or identity revelation protocol; only natural chasing behavior.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism implemented by researchers.,1,0,Subjects are not informed about number of rounds; there is no round-based structure.,1,1,"Natural ‘punishment’ is the anthias’ chasing of blennies after attacks, central to the study; although not experimentally configured, punishment behavior exists.",0.9,N/A,No token/coin cost structure; costs are biological and not quantified as game parameters.,1,N/A,No parametric mapping from punishment investment to payoff impact is specified.,1,0,No explicit rewarding behavior or mechanism is implemented or quantified.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/A,No reward technology or impact function is part of the design.,1,N/A,No experimental monetary or token endowments; natural foraging context only.,1,N/R,No quantified contribution decisions to a public good are measured; only spatial positions and attack/punishment-related behaviors.,1,N/R,No numeric amount of contribution is defined or recorded.,1,N/R,No payoff efficiency relative to a game-theoretic optimum is calculated.,1,N/R,"Group-level payoffs are not quantified; analysis focuses on spatial overlap, site fidelity, and movement after attacks.",1,Egypt,"Study conducted at Ras Mohamed National Park, Sinai, Egypt; fish are the observed participants.",1,N/R,Ages of individual fish are not reported.,1,Mixed; protogynous hermaphroditic species with adult females and males; analyses emphasize females for some measures,Species is protogynous; shoals have on average eight adult females per male and females are focal in some attack analyses; no exact sex composition per patch is provided.,0.6,N/A,Non-human animal subjects; education level not applicable.,1,Field experiment,"In-situ behavioral observations and light manipulation (marking, grid installation) on natural reef patches; standard classification is ‘field experiment’ rather than lab or purely observational survey.",0.7,"No formal public goods game; natural host–parasite system where anthias’ punitive chases after blenny attacks create a public-good-like reduction in attack probability, with spatial structure (individual home ranges and blenny site fidelity) hypothesized to make punishment more self-serving for frequently exposed individuals.","Authors explicitly frame system in public goods and punishment terms but do not implement a controlled game; they measure 3-D spatial distributions, home range overlap, and post-attack movements on five reef patches.",0.95
10.3390_g12030063.md,Study 1 – Partners treatment – Punishment phase,Authors describe a Haifa public goods experiment with two conditions: 'partners' and 'strangers'. This row refers to the punishment phase (first six rounds) of the partners treatment.,1,Group matching protocol = Partners (fixed groups of 4); Punishment option = Enabled,Independent variables distinguishing this condition are fixed vs changing group composition and presence of punishment; here: fixed composition (partners) with punishment enabled.,1,True,Lab experiment with 192 Israeli student participants.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted at University of Haifa using z-Tree in computer lab.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described for Study 1.,1,False,"Study 1 reports behavioral experiment, not closed-form analytical model.",1,4,Groups of four members played the game in both treatments.,1,6,Punishment phase consisted of six periods; reported separately from the no-punishment phase.,1,1,Participants could contribute any amount between 0 and 20 MUs.,1,0,Each participant received an endowment of 20 MUs and then chose how much to contribute; all endowment initially private.,1,0.4,Each MU contributed yielded 0.4 MUs for each group member; MPCR is marginal return to one contributor.,1,0,Instructions explicitly prohibit communication.,1,1,"After contribution, participants saw total contributions and then individual contributions of others, plus income information.",1,0,Participants were not informed who had punished them.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,1,"Instructions state that the part is divided into six periods and the interface shows 'Step 1 out of 6', so total number of periods is displayed.",1,1,Second stage of each period allowed assigning deduction points.,1,1,Each deduction point assigned costs the punisher 1 MU.,1,3,Each deduction point reduced punished member's earnings by 3 MUs.,1,0,No mechanism to incr