10.3389_fpsyt.2023.1131769.md,Analytical evolutionary model – Ultimatum Game (UG) network,The paper presents only analytical/replicator-dynamics models calibrated to prior experimental data (Li et al. 2022) for the UG on a heterogeneous bipartite network.,1,"Inequality aversion parameters of central responders (α1) and peripheral responders (α2), fitted separately for oxytocin (OT) vs placebo (PL) experimental groups from Li et al. (2022). This row describes the general model, not a specific pharmacological condition.","Model explicitly varies α1 and α2 (derived from q1, q2) between OT and PL groups, using experimental means as inputs.",1,False,No new human data are collected; prior experimental means are used only as calibration targets for an analytical model.,1,False,The work is theoretical/analytical and does not manipulate conditions in a new behavioral experiment.,1,False,No laboratory sessions are conducted in this paper; it analyzes existing data via theory.,1,False,"UG section uses closed-form replicator dynamics and stability analysis, not numerical agent-based simulation.",1,True,The UG portion develops utility matrices and replicator dynamics and performs stability analysis analytically.,1,18,Networked UG described: nine proposers and nine responders.,1,60,They use mean minimum acceptance offers over 60 rounds of the UG experiment from Li et al. (2022).,0.9,1,"Proposers choose a resource offer p (and rational type uses s<0.5); responders choose a minimum q. These are continuous amounts, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,Not a public-goods or contribution game; it is an ultimatum bargaining game.,1,N/R,UG is not framed as a contribution multiplied by a factor divided by group size; MPCR is not defined.,1,N/A,No human gameplay occurs in this paper; communication options of the original experiment are not modeled.,1,N/A,Model does not specify feedback or summaries presented to human participants.,1,N/A,"UG rejection is modeled as costly punishment, but no identity-revelation interface is specified in this analytical work.",1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism is implemented in UG model.,1,N/A,The analytical model does not include information display about total or remaining rounds.,1,1,Rejection of unfair offers by responders is explicitly interpreted as costly punishment of proposers due to inequality aversion.,0.9,N/R,Model acknowledges that responders forego payoffs when rejecting offers but does not parametrize a per-unit punishment cost.,0.5,N/R,No explicit linear mapping from punishment cost to target fine is provided for UG; only utility terms are given.,0.5,0,UG formulation has no separate costly reward/bonus action beyond acceptance of offers.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Each proposer–responder pair shares a fixed amount of resources normalized to 1; proposer’s share is 1−p and responder’s p when deal occurs.,0.9,N/R,"Main behavioral outputs are offers (s) and minimum acceptance thresholds (q1, q2), not framed as contribution rates.",1,N/R,"Paper does not report raw offer distributions from the underlying experiment; only mean q1, q2 and the predicted s are discussed.",0.7,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio (realized / maximum possible payoff) is computed or reported.,1,N/R,Group-level total payoffs are not reported; only strategic parameters are fitted.,1,N/A,This is an analytical model with parameters fitted to external experimental data; no new participants.,1,N/A,No human subjects in this paper.,1,N/A,No human subjects in this paper.,1,N/A,No human subjects in this paper.,1,No human,All UG work is theoretical/analytical with no new experimental sessions.,1,"Bipartite heterogeneous network (9 proposers, 9 responders); proposers each connected to 2 central and 2 peripheral responders. Responders classified as central (degree 6) or peripheral (degree 3). Utilities incorporate payoff and disadvantage inequality aversion α1, α2; replicator dynamics over strategies R/F for proposers and C/D for responders.",Summarizes described network and strategic structure relevant to game configuration.,1
10.3389_fpsyt.2023.1131769.md,Analytical evolutionary model – Two-stage Prisoner’s Dilemma with costly punishment (tPDG) network,"The tPDG section specifies payoff/utility matrices and replicator dynamics calibrated to experimental data from Li et al. (2022), without collecting new behavioral data.",1,"Social preference parameters: inequality aversion (α1, α2) and dilemma aversion (β1, β2) for central vs peripheral players, fitted separately for oxytocin (OT) and placebo (PL) groups. This row covers the general analytical model.",These parameters are explicitly estimated from stable strategy proportions in OT vs PL groups and are the main varying inputs.,1,False,The paper does not run a new tPDG experiment; it only uses previously published summary statistics.,1,False,No randomized assignment or behavioral protocol is implemented within this article.,1,False,"All tPDG work here is theoretical, not a new lab or field experiment.",1,False,They derive and analyze replicator dynamics and perform stability checks; no agent-based or Monte Carlo simulation is reported.,1,True,"Core of the tPDG section is analytical construction of utility matrices and replicator differential equations, with Jacobian eigenvalue analysis.",1,12,"The tPDG network has three central nodes and nine peripheral nodes, i.e., 12 players total.",1,N/R,They mention using the mean proportions of strategies of the last 20 rounds from the prior experiment but do not state total rounds.,0.5,1,"Stage I is a binary cooperate/defect choice, but punishment in stage II is quantified with cost C and fine F; overall model is not framed as an all-or-nothing contribution game.",0.7,N/A,"This is a pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with punishment, not a public-goods contribution setting.",1,N/R,No linear public-goods multiplier divided by group size is specified.,1,N/A,Analytical model does not specify any communication protocol between (real) participants.,1,N/A,"The modeling framework includes knowledge of neighbors’ actions/payoffs only in utility expectations, not as an experimental interface.",1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as a strategic action with cost C and fine F; anonymity/identification is not addressed.,1,N/A,There is no separate prosocial reward mechanism beyond cooperation itself.,1,N/A,The analytical model does not model on-screen display or knowledge of horizon.,1,1,Stage II explicitly allows players to choose costly punishment of neighbors who defected in stage I.,1,N/R,Matrix uses generic symbols C (cost) and F (fine) without numeric values.,0.6,N/R,Magnitude of punishment per unit cost is symbolically F−C; no concrete numeric mapping per coin is given.,0.6,0,Model focuses on costly punishment of defectors; there is no separate costly reward action for cooperators.,1,N/A,No explicit reward option.,1,N/A,No explicit reward option.,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix uses generic symbols R, S, T, P and normalized C, F; an initial token endowment is not specified.",0.4,N/R,"Primary DV is cooperation rate (proportion using C+N or C+P), but no normalized contribution variable is defined.",0.8,N/R,"They report strategy proportions, not raw amounts invested or contributed.",1,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized vs maximal group payoff is computed.,1,N/R,Group-level payoffs are not reported; analysis focuses on strategy frequencies and parameter estimates.,1,N/A,No new participants in this paper; it only references external experimental work.,1,N/A,No human subjects tested.,1,N/A,No human subjects tested.,1,N/A,No human subjects tested.,1,No human,"The tPDG portion is purely analytical, building on earlier lab work but not running new experiments.",1,"Heterogeneous undirected network with 3 central nodes (degree 8) and 9 peripheral nodes (degree 4). Two-stage game: Stage I Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs T, R, P, S (T>R>P>S); Stage II optional costly punishment of neighbors who defected. Strategies reduced to C+N, C+P, D+N (D+P dominated). Utilities include inequality aversion (α1, α2) and dilemma aversion (β1, β2). Mean-field reduction to 2-node system (central, peripheral) with weighted self-loops.",Summarizes structural and strategic assumptions needed to understand the modeled game.,1
10.3389_fpsyt.2023.1131769.md,Analytical evolutionary model – Trust Game (TG) network,"The TG section builds a utility-based replicator model on a heterogeneous network, fitted to trust-game data from Li et al. (2022), without running new experiments.",1,"Trustee return ratio r (relative to multiplication factor g) and trustee altruism parameter λ, plus central vs peripheral investors’ investment levels (T^C, T^P) and investment probabilities, considered under oxytocin vs placebo in the referenced experiment. This row describes the general model.","Stability conditions and classification of experimental groups are driven by r relative to g, and λ>1 vs λ<1; OT is modeled as changing T and λ but not r.",1,False,The TG analysis is fully theoretical with calibration to existing data; no new human sample is collected.,1,False,No direct manipulation of subjects in this paper; only mathematical modeling.,1,False,The TG work is not a new lab or field experiment.,1,False,"They use analytical replicator dynamics and stability, not numerical simulations.",1,True,"TG section sets up symbolic utility matrices, derives replicator equations, and performs stability analysis.",1,18,"Networked TG uses same heterogeneous network as UG: 3 central investors, 6 peripheral investors, 9 trustees (3+6+9=18).",1,N/R,They reference repeated rounds and stable points by group but do not give the total number of rounds for the TG experiment.,0.5,1,"Investors choose a ‘certain proportion’ of endowment to invest (continuous variable T), and trustees choose a return proportion r; not an all-or-nothing decision.",0.9,N/A,"Trust game is bilateral investment/return, not a standard linear public goods contribution.",1,3,"Investment is multiplied by factor g=3 in the TG, effectively the marginal return on invested units before sharing.",0.9,N/A,Trust-game model does not specify any communication between real participants.,1,N/A,Analytical model mentions access to choices/payoffs conceptually but not as an interface variable.,1,N/A,There is no explicit punishment stage/mechanism in the TG model.,1,N/A,"While investment and reciprocal return are beneficial, there is no separate costly reward action whose identity visibility is discussed.",1,N/A,The theoretical model does not incorporate or report round-horizon feedback.,1,0,The TG model has no costly punishment option; primary mechanism is trust/investment and reciprocal return.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Although investment may be interpreted as rewarding trustees, there is no separate modeled costly reward technology akin to structured reward games.",0.7,N/A,No explicit reward action beyond the core trust-game transfers.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology is defined in addition to the standard TG payoff structure.,1,1,Investor’s initial endowment is normalized to 1 in the utility specification (payoffs like 1−T^C etc.).,0.9,N/R,"Main DVs are investments T^C, T^P and return ratios r, not expressed as normalized rates in this article.",0.7,N/R,"The paper does not provide numerical values for average investments or returns, only qualitative statements and stability categories.",0.7,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric is computed.,1,N/R,Total payoffs across the TG network are not reported.,1,N/A,No new participants are studied; only prior data are referenced.,1,N/A,No human subjects directly involved in this modeling work.,1,N/A,No human subjects directly involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects directly involved.,1,No human,TG results are theoretical; experimental data come from Li et al. (2022) but are not re-collected or extended here.,1,Heterogeneous bipartite-like network: 3 central investors each connected to 6 trustees; 6 peripheral investors each connected to 3 trustees; 9 trustees each connected to 4 investors. Investors choose whether to invest (I vs NI) some fraction T of their unit endowment; trustees decide whether to return (R vs NR) fraction r of the tripled investment (g=3). Trustee utility includes an altruism term λ·(returned amount). Replicator dynamics over trustee reciprocity and investor trust show that network-wide dynamics depend on r relative to 1/g and λ.,Summarizes structure and payoff features that define the analytical TG model.,1
