titution.,1,N/R,"As in baseline periods, buyers’ values and sellers’ costs change via shock but specific monetary endowments are not reported.",0.4,N/R,No contribution decision; key outcomes are post-shock prices and convergence.,1,N/R,No public good contributions.,1,N/R,Article states prices adjust quickly after the shock but does not quantify efficiency or surplus.,0.4,N/R,No report of aggregate post-shock earnings.,0.4,Australia,Same University of Melbourne classroom participant pool.,0.95,N/R,"No age range specified; likely university-aged, but not reported.",0.5,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,University students,Continuation of same student sample in the classroom experiment.,0.9,On site,Laboratory/classroom experiment at the University of Melbourne.,0.95,"Same double auction market as baseline but with an unannounced change in both supply and demand schedules in period 6, raising the competitive equilibrium price from $7 to $10 while leaving equilibrium quantity unchanged (6 or 7 units); prices of traded goods adjust quickly to the new equilibrium.",Summarizes the post-shock environment and observed adjustment described in the text.,1
10.1111_1468-0262.00230.md,Analytical model of repeated common-interest games with perturbations (baseline general game G),The paper studies a general two-player repeated common-interest stage game G with perturbations; this is the core theoretical environment analyzed throughout Sections 2–3.,0.9,"Presence and type of perturbation strategies in supports (S1, S2): nondraconian (sup β_i(s_i) < 1) vs draconian (sup β_i(s_i) = 1); perturbation probability ξ > 0; choice of target payoff vector v with v_i > v_i.","Theorems are comparative statements about which payoff vectors are consistent in Nash equilibrium depending on whether S1, S2 are draconian or not, and on perturbation probability and target payoff v.",0.9,False,No human participants; purely game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation; purely analytical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported.,1,True,"The paper develops formal definitions, lemmas, and theorems about strategies and equilibria in repeated games.",1,2,"Framework explicitly states a two-player game: 'between two players, 1 and 2.'",1,0,Infinite repetition of the stage game; no fixed finite number of rounds. Encoded as 0 for 'infinite'.,0.9,1,"Each player chooses actions from finite sets A1, A2 without restriction to binary contribute/all-or-nothing choices; they can play any pure action each period.",0.8,N/A,There is no public goods or contribution structure with endowments; general stage game payoffs g(a) are used.,1,N/R,No linear public good or marginal per capita return is defined; payoffs are arbitrary rational numbers in a common-interest game.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely theoretical model.,1,0,"Information structure is that both players perfectly observe past actions (full history), not 'summaries' of earnings/punishments; the requested UI-like feature is not applicable.",0.8,N/A,No player identities or punishment interface as in experiments; strategies are abstract maps from histories to actions.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology distinct from regular actions.,1,1,Players know the game is infinitely repeated with no terminal date; effective horizon is common knowledge.,0.7,1,Min-max actions A_i^m are used to punish the opponent to their minmax payoff (0); draconian strategies min-max opponents frequently.,1,N/R,Punishment is embedded in the stage-game action payoffs; there is no separate marginal cost parameter per punishment unit.,1,N/R,No separate technology parameter mapping punishment expenditure to losses; punishment is just choosing min-max actions.,1,0,No distinct reward actions/technology are introduced beyond efficient cooperative actions a*; only punishment via min-max is formalized.,0.9,N/A,There is no explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,There is no explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model does not specify endowments or tokens; only action payoffs g_i(a1,a2).",1,N/R,"No contribution game; payoffs and results are about efficiency and min-max, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No notion of contribution amount exists in the model.,1,N/R,"They discuss 'efficient' (Pareto-dominant) payoffs v*, but do not report numerical efficiency ratios for any specific instance.",1,N/R,"No numeric group payoffs are fixed (aside from symbolic v*, v_i).",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is purely theoretical and analytical; no empirical environment.,1,"General two-player common-interest stage game G with finite pure action sets A1, A2, rational payoffs g(a), strongly Pareto-dominant efficient payoff vector (v1*, v2*) > 0 attained at action pair a*; min-max actions A_i^m yield opponent payoff 0; infinite repetition with complete observation of past actions; perturbation probability ξ > 0 that a player’s strategy is drawn from distribution μ_i over pure strategies; focus on draconian strategies (β_i(s_i)=1) that min-max with maximal frequency versus nondraconian sets where sup β_i(s_i) < 1; key results: Theorem 1 shows nondraconian supports cannot force efficiency, Theorem 2 constructs a single draconian strategy s1 that induces efficient outcomes in all Nash equilibria of the perturbed repeated game under a min-max equality condition.",Summarizes the central formal game environment and strategic concepts because there is no standard experimental game like a linear public good.,1
10.1111_1468-0262.00230.md,"Analytical example 1: Stage game with actions {T,B} x {L,R}",Section 2 presents a concrete 2x2 payoff matrix example to illustrate reservation payoffs v_i.,1,"Fixed stage-game payoff matrix with actions T,B for player 1 and L,R for player 2; perturbation distribution μ2 gives positive probability to strategy 'always L'.",Only variation discussed is whether μ2 has positive probability on 'always L'; the payoffs of the matrix are fixed.,0.9,False,Matrix used illustratively in analytical derivation; no experiment.,1,False,No controlled experimental implementation with human subjects.,1,False,Purely theoretical example.,1,False,No simulation; payoffs analyzed analytically.,1,True,Example is used to compute reservation payoffs and illustrate definitions analytically.,1,2,Payoff table is between two players.,1,0,Example is embedded in the infinitely repeated game framework; no finite horizon given.,0.9,1,"Players choose among pure actions T,B and L,R; not an all-or-nothing contribution setting.",0.8,N/A,This is not a contribution/public-good game.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return defined; payoffs are arbitrary.,1,N/A,Theoretical example; no communication protocol.,1,0,"Players are assumed to observe full history of actions, not summary feedback.",0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism beyond choosing stage-game actions.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Infinite repetition with common knowledge.,0.7,1,"Min-max actions and strategies that can hold a player to reservation/min-max payoffs are discussed (e.g., strategies ensuring at least (1-ξ)v_i+ξv_i).",0.8,N/R,No distinct punishment cost parameter; costs embedded in payoff matrix.,1,N/R,No separate punishment technology defined.,1,0,No explicit rewarding actions separate from efficient outcomes.,0.9,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,No endowment notion; payoffs directly specified.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,"They compute reservation payoffs (v2=1, v1=0) but do not compute efficiency ratios.",1,N/R,Group payoff is not summarized as a single reported dependent variable; only cell payoffs are given.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical example only.,1,"Stage game payoff matrix: Player 1 actions T,B; Player 2 actions L,R. Payoffs: (T,L) = (1,8); (T,R) = (-2,-1); (B,L) = (-1,-6); (B,R) = (0,0). Min-max payoffs normalized so pure min-max is 0. Reservation payoffs computed: v2 = 1, v1 = 0. With μ2 giving positive probability to strategy 'always L', any pure-strategy equilibrium must give player 2 at least 1 on average because 1 must eventually play T at least half the time against always L.",Directly summarizes the described illustrative matrix and its role in the reservation-payoff argument.,1
10.1111_1468-0262.00230.md,"Analytical example 2: Stage game Γ with actions {γ1, γ2, γ3}","Section 4 introduces a specific symmetric 3x3 game Γ with special payoffs, used to extend Theorem 1 logic under Turing-machine perturbations.",1,"Fixed stage-game payoff matrix Γ with actions γ1, γ2, γ3 for both players; perturbation distributions μ1, μ2 over Turing machines with nondraconian supports satisfying AS1; constructed modified perturbations μ1', μ2' with added alter-ego machines; parameter K > 0 payoff when only one nonhalting machine is used.","Within this example, only the perturbation support properties and the constant K are varied conceptually; the matrix itself is fixed.",0.9,False,Turing machines and strategies analyzed theoretically; no behavioral data.,1,False,"Not an experiment, but a theoretical constructed example.",1,False,No lab implementation.,1,False,"No simulation; arguments are analytical about equilibria of Γ^∞(μ1', μ2', ξ).",1,True,All reasoning uses formal definitions of machines and equilibrium payoffs.,1,2,Γ is a two-player normal-form game.,1,0,Analysis is for infinite repetition Γ^∞ with perturbations; no finite T specified.,0.9,1,Players select among three discrete actions each period; not an all-or-nothing contribution model.,0.8,N/A,No endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,No public good or MPCR specified.,1,N/A,Theoretical Turing-machine environment; no communication stage.,1,0,"Players (machines) observe histories of actions, not numerical summaries; no UI summary feature.",0.8,N/A,Punishment is embedded in action γ3 and payoff structure; no IDs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism beyond cooperative outcomes.,1,1,Infinite-horizon game is common knowledge.,0.7,1,"Strategy s_i: play γ2 as long as opponent plays γ2; if not, switch to γ3 forever. Given payoffs, γ3 min-maxes deviations, serving as punishment.",1,N/R,No explicit parametric cost; punishment is via lower payoffs encoded in matrix.,1,N/R,No separate punishment intensity parameter.,1,0,"Game has cooperative actions (γ1,γ1) with payoff (3,3) and (γ2,γ2) with (2,2), but no separate rewarding technology.",0.9,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Model does not use endowments; payoff matrix directly specifies utility.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,"They note that payoffs (2,2) are inefficient relative to (3,3), but do not provide normalized efficiency numbers.",1,N/R,"While matrix entries like (3,3) and (2,2) imply group payoffs 6 and 4, no summary DV measure is reported.",0.9,N/A,"Players are Turing machines, not humans.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Environment consists of Turing machines playing repeated games; no empirical setting.,1,"Stage game Γ payoff matrix with actions γ1, γ2, γ3 for each player: (γ1,γ1)=(3,3); (γ1,γ2)=(0,0); (γ1,γ3)=(0,1); (γ2,γ1)=(0,0); (γ2,γ2)=(2,2); (γ2,γ3)=(0,1); (γ3,γ1)=(1,0); (γ3,γ2)=(1,0); (γ3,γ3)=(0,0). Additional payoff rule: if Xi fails to halt when played against itself, player i gets 0 and opponent j gets K>0; if both fail to halt after some history, both receive 0. They construct computable strategies s_i: 'Play γ2 as long as opponent has always played γ2; if opponent deviates, switch to γ3 forever.' Under suitable modified perturbations μ1', μ2' with nondraconian support satisfying AS1, (s1,s2) is an equilibrium of Γ^∞(μ1', μ2', ξ) yielding long-run payoffs close to (2,2) as ξ → 0, which is inefficient relative to (3,3). This illustrates that, even with Turing-machine restrictions and nonhalting possibilities, nondraconian perturbations cannot ensure efficiency unless alter-ego-type machines are excluded by assumptions like AS3.",Directly summarizes the game structure and the constructed equilibrium used to extend Theorem 1’s logic.,1
10.1017_ehs.2023.15.md,Random Allocation Game – SELF vs DISTANT,The paper describes a Random Allocation Game (RAG) with a SELF vs DISTANT condition where coins can go either to the participant (SELF) or an anonymous distant co-religionist (DISTANT).,1,"Game type: Random Allocation Game; Recipient contrast: SELF vs DISTANT; Deity belief measures (moral concern proportion, punitiveness, knowledge breadth) as predictors, not experimental manipulations.",The only experimental manipulation at the game level is which recipients the two cups represent (SELF vs DISTANT). Deity-related variables are used as regressors but are not assigned experimentally.,0.93,True,Economic games were played by human participants across 15 field sites.,1,True,"The RAG is a controlled economic game with an assigned endowment, a fair die, and specified recipient cups.",1,False,"Games were run as part of fieldwork in diverse communities, not in a physical laboratory; described as field sites.",0.95,False,No computer or agent-based simulations were used; all data are from human subjects.,1,False,"While Bayesian models are applied, there is no standalone analytical/game-theoretic model examined as an experiment.",0.98,2,Each decision is between two recipients: SELF and a DISTANT co-religionist.,0.9,30,"Participants are endowed with 30 coins and roll the die once per coin, implying 30 allocation decisions.",1,1,"For each coin, participants can effectively allocate it to either cup; over 30 coins this allows any integer amount from 0 to 30 to each recipient, not just all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,All coins start under the participant’s control and are then allocated; no initial amount is pre-placed in any public/shared account.,0.8,N/R,No multiplier or marginal per capita return is described; allocations are 1:1 transfers.,1,0,No mention of communication among participants; games are described as played alone.,0.95,0,Participants are not described as seeing others’ payoffs or allocations; focus is on anonymous recipients and solitary play.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism exists in the game.,1,0,"No reward mechanism exists beyond the allocations themselves, and no identity revelation is mentioned.",0.95,1,"Participants are endowed with a known number of coins (30) and roll once per coin, so the total number of decisions is transparent.",0.9,0,Game only involves allocation of coins between two recipients; no option to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment option is present.,1,N/A,No punishment option is present.,1,0,There is no separate rewarding technology beyond allocating coins; no additional reward action is available.,0.95,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,30,The RAG description states that participants were endowed with 30 coins.,1,N/R,Paper reports distributions of number of coins allocated to DISTANT but does not provide a single summary contribution rate across participants.,0.4,N/R,Only graphs of distributions are shown; no explicit means or medians by condition are provided.,0.3,N/R,No total payoffs or efficiency ratios relative to maximum are reported.,1,N/R,Group-level payoffs are not presented in the text.,1,"15 sites: Brazil, Vanuatu, Tanzania, Peru, Democratic Republic of Congo, Fiji, Mauritius, India, Kenya, Papua New Guinea, Russia (Tyva Republic).","Countries inferred from the site list in Table 1 (e.g., Cachoeira, Brazil; Coastal Tanna, Vanuatu; Hadza, Tanzania; Huatasani, Peru; Kananga, DRC; Lovu and Yasawa, Fiji; Marajó and Cachoeira, Brazil; Mauritius; Mysore, India; Samburu and Turkana, Kenya; Sursurunga, Papua New Guinea; Tyva Republic, Russia).",0.98,N/R,Age ranges or averages are not reported in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition is not described in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,No information on education levels is given in the excerpt.,0.1,Field experiment,"Data were collected at diverse field sites in small-scale and other real-world communities, using standardized behavioral games.",0.98,"Random Allocation Game measuring impartial rule-following; participants endowed with 30 coins, a fair die with two outcomes, and two labeled cups (SELF and DISTANT). For each coin, they privately think of a desired recipient, roll the die, and allocate according to the die outcome or opposite cup; recipients other than SELF are anonymous and geographically distant co-religionists; stakes approximately one local daily wage in total across games, with a show-up fee of about 25% of daily wage.",Direct summary of the RAG SELF vs DISTANT description in Section 2.2 and the stakes description.,1
10.1017_ehs.2023.15.md,Random Allocation Game – LOCAL vs DISTANT,The paper describes a Random Allocation Game (RAG) with a LOCAL vs DISTANT condition where coins can go either to a local co-religionist or an anonymous distant co-religionist.,1,"Game type: Random Allocation Game; Recipient contrast: LOCAL vs DISTANT; Deity belief measures (moral concern proportion, punitiveness, knowledge breadth) as predictors, not experimental manipulations.",The primary experimental manipulation is which two recipients are contrasted (LOCAL vs DISTANT). Deity-related measures vary at the individual level but are not manipulated.,0.93,True,Same human-participant field protocol as the SELF vs DISTANT RAG.,1,True,A controlled economic game with fixed rules and recipient identities.,1,False,Conducted in community field sites rather than a laboratory.,0.95,False,No simulations are described.,1,False,Game is empirical; analytical work is only for statistical modeling.,0.98,2,Each play allocates coins between a LOCAL co-religionist and a DISTANT co-religionist.,0.9,30,Same RAG structure: 30 coins and one decision per coin.,1,1,"Over 30 binary allocations, participants can choose any integer split between LOCAL and DISTANT recipients.",0.95,0,Coins start with the participant; nothing is pre-allocated.,0.8,N/R,No multiplier or MPCR is specified.,1,0,No communication among players is mentioned; play is solitary.,0.95,0,No feedback or summaries of others’ decisions are reported.,0.9,0,There is no punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism with identity revelation is used.,0.95,1,"Participants know they have 30 coins to allocate, making the number of decisions transparent.",0.9,0,Only allocation decisions; no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No distinct reward technology outside of allocation choices.,0.95,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,30,RAG endowment is specified as 30 coins for participants.,1,N/R,The paper provides distributions of coins to DISTANT but no summary rate reported per condition.,0.4,N/R,Exact average/median number of coins to DISTANT is not reported for this condition.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency metric relative to maximum possible payoff is given.,1,N/R,Group payoff data are not presented.,1,"15 sites: Brazil, Vanuatu, Tanzania, Peru, Democratic Republic of Congo, Fiji, Mauritius, India, Kenya, Papua New Guinea, Russia (Tyva Republic).","Same pooled multi-site sample as for the other games, based on Table 1 site list.",0.98,N/R,Age information is not included in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,Gender breakdown is not reported.,0.1,N/R,No education data are described.,0.1,Field experiment,Games were run in situ across 15 cultural field sites.,0.98,"Random Allocation Game measuring impartial rule-following; participants receive 30 coins, a fair die with two outcomes, and two labeled cups (LOCAL co-religionist and DISTANT co-religionist). For each coin, they privately select a desired recipient, roll the die, and allocate according to outcome rules; recipients are anonymous, with LOCAL vs DISTANT differing in geographical proximity but religious affiliation matched. Stakes across games approximate one local daily wage with a show-up fee around 25% of daily wage.",Summarizes the LOCAL vs DISTANT RAG condition based on Section 2.2 and Table 1.,1
