n exists.,0.8,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism beyond the allocation.,1,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism beyond the allocation.,1,10,DG description: participants are endowed with a stack of 10 coins.,1,N/R,Only distributions by number of coins to DISTANT are shown; no single normalized mean fraction is reported.,0.4,N/R,Mean or median coins sent to DISTANT in SELF vs DISTANT DG are not specified.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency ratio is reported.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not given.,1,"15 sites: Brazil, Vanuatu, Tanzania, Peru, Democratic Republic of Congo, Fiji, Mauritius, India, Kenya, Papua New Guinea, Russia (Tyva Republic).",DGs were played by the same multi-site sample listed in Table 1.,0.98,N/R,No age statistics in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition is not discussed.,0.1,N/R,Educational background is not mentioned.,0.1,Field experiment,DGs were conducted in the same field settings as the RAGs.,0.98,Standard Dictator Game; participants are endowed with 10 coins and asked to allocate them between themselves (SELF) and a DISTANT anonymous co-religionist in any way they like. The DG measures self- vs distant-partiality; no randomness (die) is involved. Stakes across all games approximate one local daily wage with a show-up fee of about 25% of that wage; game order is counterbalanced.,Directly based on the DG description in Section 2.2 and general protocol details.,1
10.1017_ehs.2023.15.md,Dictator Game – LOCAL vs DISTANT,The paper describes a Dictator Game (DG) with a LOCAL vs DISTANT condition where participants allocate a 10-coin endowment between a local co-religionist and an anonymous distant co-religionist.,1,"Game type: Dictator Game; Recipient contrast: LOCAL vs DISTANT; Deity belief measures (moral concern proportion, punitiveness, knowledge breadth) as predictors, not experimental manipulations.",The manipulated feature at the game level is the recipient pairing (LOCAL vs DISTANT). Belief variables are observational predictors.,0.93,True,Same human-subject DG protocol in the field as other conditions.,1,True,"Controlled, incentivized allocation task with specified recipients.",1,False,"Implemented in real-world field sites, not in a lab.",0.95,False,No agent-based or simulated data.,1,False,"Analytical work is limited to statistical modeling, not a separate analytical experiment.",0.98,2,Each DG allocation is between a LOCAL and a DISTANT co-religionist.,0.9,1,One allocation decision per DG endowment of 10 coins.,0.9,1,Dictators can allocate any number of the 10 coins to either recipient; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,0.95,0,All coins start under dictator control; nothing is initially placed with either recipient.,0.9,N/R,No contribution multiplier is specified.,1,0,No communication between dictator and recipients is reported.,0.95,0,Participants are not informed of others’ play or payoffs.,0.9,0,No punishment option is in the DG.,1,0,No separate reward technology with identity reveal is used.,0.95,1,"Participants clearly face a single 10-coin allocation, not repeated rounds.",0.9,0,There is no choice to actively reduce another’s payoff.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism beyond choosing allocations.,0.8,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,10,Participants are endowed with 10 coins in the DG.,1,N/R,The article shows distributions of allocations but does not provide a summary rate statistic per condition.,0.4,N/R,No explicit mean/median allocations to DISTANT in LOCAL vs DISTANT DG are given.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency metrics are not reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not detailed.,1,"15 sites: Brazil, Vanuatu, Tanzania, Peru, Democratic Republic of Congo, Fiji, Mauritius, India, Kenya, Papua New Guinea, Russia (Tyva Republic).",Same field site list as in Table 1 applies to DG LOCAL vs DISTANT.,0.98,N/R,Age details are not provided in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,Gender of participants is not described.,0.1,N/R,Education information is absent.,0.1,Field experiment,DGs were conducted in the same cross-cultural field protocol.,0.98,"Standard Dictator Game; participants receive 10 coins and allocate them between a LOCAL co-religionist and a DISTANT co-religionist in any split. Used to measure in-group (local) vs distant partiality. Stakes across the full set of games correspond to about one local daily wage, plus a show-up fee (~25% of daily wage); order of games is counterbalanced.",Summarizes DG LOCAL vs DISTANT condition based on Section 2.2 and general protocol description.,1
10.1142_s0129183121501278.md,"Simulation – PGG-N (normal public goods game, no rewiring, no punishment)",Paper contrasts baseline normal public goods game (PGG-N) with rewiring (PGG-M) and punishment (PGG-P); treat PGG-N as a distinct condition.,1,"Game type: PGG-N (no leaders rewiring mechanism, no punishment); Enhancement factor β varied; other parameters fixed (N=1000, e=10, ν=10, K=0.1).","PGG-N is defined as the normal public goods game without LRM or punishment; Fig. 3 varies β and compares to PGG-M; Fig. 5 compares PGG-N, PGG-M, and PGG-P at β=0.3.",0.9,False,Study uses agent-based simulations on BA networks with 1000 individuals; no human participants are mentioned.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational model.,1,False,No laboratory or in-person setting; only simulations.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent-based simulations on networks.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations; results are numerical.,1,1000,"Section 3: ""The population consists of 1000 individuals"".",1,5000,"Section 3: ""5000 time-steps after discarding 2000 initial transient steps""; interpret effective analyzed rounds as 5000.",0.8,0,"Model section states each player decides to contribute c=e (cooperate) or c=0 (defect), i.e., binary all-or-nothing choice.",1,N/A,"No human subjects; initial contributions are strategic choices, not default allocations.",1,N/R,Payoff uses multiplier β on total contribution but group size varies with degree; marginal per capita return not explicitly given or directly inferable as single value.,0.3,N/A,No human communication; agent-based simulation only.,1,0,Agents update using Fermi rule based on neighbor payoffs; no interface concepts like summaries are described.,0.7,N/A,No punishment mechanism in PGG-N.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,0,Agents are not described as observing time horizon; steps are part of simulation engine only.,0.7,0,PGG-N is normal game without punishment; punishment only introduced in PGG-P.,1,N/A,No punishment available in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment available in this condition.,1,0,Only punishment and rewiring are discussed; no reward option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,"Section 3: ""The initial endowment e is set as e=10"".",1,N/R,Figures show qualitative evolution of contribution but no numerical final rates are given.,0,N/R,"Average contributions over β and time are plotted, but exact values are not tabulated.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,0,N/R,Average payoff curves are shown but no specific numeric outcome is provided.,0,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from computational simulations without human subjects.,1,"Scale-free BA network with N=1000, m0=3; each player participates in groups defined by themselves and neighbors when group size > ν (ν=10 in main simulations); strategies updated by Fermi rule with K=0.1; enhancement factor β in (0,1); initial cooperator share 50%.",Summarized from Sections 2.1–2.4 and 3.,1
10.1142_s0129183121501278.md,"Simulation – PGG-M (public goods game with leaders rewiring mechanism, baseline parameters)","PGG-M (with LRM) is the central treatment compared to PGG-N; Fig. 3 uses ν=10, p_l=0.1, T_th=0.3 across β; treat as main LRM condition.",1,Game type: PGG-M (leaders rewiring mechanism enabled); Trust threshold T_th=0.3; leaders rewiring probability p_l=0.1; minimum degree ν=10; enhancement factor β varied.,"Fig. 3 caption specifies ν=10, p_l=0.1, T_th=0.3 for PGG-M while varying β.",1,False,Agent-based simulation; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; computational model.,1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,Described as agent-based model and simulations on networks.,1,False,"No analytical solution, only numerical simulations.",1,1000,"Section 3: ""The population consists of 1000 individuals"" used for all simulations.",1,5000,"Section 3: ""5000 time-steps after discarding 2000 initial transient steps""; these are the analyzed periods.",0.8,0,Contribution choice is binary: cooperate (c=e) or defect (c=0).,1,N/A,No human subjects and no default token placement.,1,N/R,Only enhancement factor β is given; group sizes vary with degree so a single MPCR is not provided.,0.3,N/A,No communication among human participants; only programmed updating.,1,0,Simulation agents use local payoffs for strategy updating; no notion of presented summaries.,0.7,N/A,No punishment in PGG-M; mechanism is rewiring only.,1,N/A,No reward option described.,1,0,Agents are not modeled as observing remaining time-steps.,0.7,0,PGG-M uses leaders rewiring without punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewards defined in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Section 3 specifies e=10 for all players and simulations.,1,N/R,Only graphical comparisons of average contributions versus β and T_th are shown; no exact final values.,0,N/R,"Figures show trends, not exact token amounts.",0,N/R,"Although payoff is plotted, efficiency ratio is not computed or reported.",0,N/R,Average payoff per group member is plotted versus β but without numeric table.,0,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model only.,1,Same PGG structure as PGG-N but with leaders rewiring mechanism: some focal players designated as leaders can cut all links to current neighbors and form a new group of same size when the proportion of cooperators ρ in the group falls below trust threshold T_th; rewiring is attempted with probability p_l at each time step. Underlying network is BA scale-free with m0=3; minimum degree ν=10 for group formation; Fermi updating with K=0.1; initial 50% cooperators.,"Summarized from Sections 2.1–2.4, 2.3, and Fig. 3 caption.",1
10.1142_s0129183121501278.md,Simulation – PGG-P with punishment parameter s=1,"Fig. 5 compares PGG-N, PGG-M, and PGG-P with different punishment cost s; each s represents a distinct punishment condition.",0.95,Game type: PGG-P (leaders can punish defectors) with punishment parameter s=1 (punisher cost per sanction); punishment multiplier k=2; β=0.3; ν=10; p_l=0.1; T_th=0.3.,"Fig. 5 caption: β=0.3, ν=10, p_l=0.1, T_th=0.3 for PGG-M; it also states ""We set k=2 for PGG-P where s denotes the amount player i uses to sanction"". The figure includes curves for different s values (starting from s=1).",0.7,False,Still the same agent-based simulation framework; no humans.,1,False,"Computational, not an empirical experiment with subjects.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,PGG-P is implemented within the agent-based simulation.,1,False,No analytical expressions derived.,1,1000,Same simulation population size as stated in Section 3 for all models.,1,5000,Same time horizon: 5000 time-steps after 2000 transients.,0.8,0,Contribution decision remains c=e or c=0.,1,N/A,No human default allocation.,1,N/R,β and group size vary; MPCR is not explicitly given.,0.3,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No interface; only payoff-based updating.,0.7,0,"Identity visibility is not modeled; punishment is based on strategy (defector) and leader status, not identity revelation.",0.5,N/A,No rewards mentioned.,1,0,Agents are not modeled as knowing or responding to finite horizon.,0.7,1,"Section describing PGG-P: ""The punishment is introduced after every game. Leaders have the opportunity to punish defectors"".",1,1,"Fig. 5: s denotes amount used to sanction; for this condition, s=1 so cost per punishment is 1 to the punisher.",0.7,2,"Text: ""if a leader punishes a defector player, by reducing his payoff with k·s ... We set k=2 for PGG-P""; so each unit s costs 1 and reduces other's payoff by 2.",1,0,No reward mechanism is introduced in PGG-P.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Same e=10 is used for all simulations.,1,N/R,Fig. 5 shows contribution over time but not numeric values.,0,N/R,Only qualitative time series are provided.,0,N/R,Efficiencies are not computed.,0,N/R,Group payoffs with punishment are not numerically summarized.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulation.,1,"Same public goods structure and network as PGG-M, but with punishment: after every game, leaders can punish defectors, reducing defector payoff by k·s and incurring cost s to themselves (k=2, s=1 here). Other parameters: β=0.3, ν=10, p_l=0.1, T_th=0.3, BA scale-free network with N=1000.",Based on punishment description and Fig. 5 caption; assumes PGG-P shares base parameters with PGG-M for that comparison.,0.8
10.1142_s0129183121501278.md,Simulation – PGG-P with punishment parameter s=3,Intermediate punishment cost level shown in Fig. 5 between s=1 and high-cost s=5; treat as distinct condition because contribution dynamics differ with s.,0.7,Game type: PGG-P with punishment parameter s=3; punishment multiplier k=2; β=0.3; ν=10; p_l=0.1; T_th=0.3.,Fig. 5 shows multiple PGG-P curves; text mentions that contribution increases as s increases until it exceeds PGG-M and specifically that s>=5 is needed to match PGG-M. The figure visually suggests an intermediate s (likely 3); encoded as s=3 to represent a medium punishment-cost condition.,0.4,False,Same simulation framework; no humans.,1,False,"Computational, not empirical.",1,False,No lab.,1,True,Agent-based model.,1,False,No analytical results.,1,1000,Same N=1000 as other simulations.,1,5000,Same time horizon description applies.,0.8,0,Binary contribution decision maintained.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,Not numerically reported.,0.3,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,No UI/feedback beyond payoffs.,0.7,0,Identity visibility not modeled.,0.5,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents lack explicit knowledge of horizon.,0.7,1,Punishment is central in PGG-P.,1,3,"Condition defined by s=3, cost to punisher per sanction.",0.4,2,k=2 fixed for PGG-P.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Same e=10.,1,N/R,Relative ordering is discussed but no a specific numeric value.,0,N/R,Only plotted qualitatively.,0,N/R,Not computed.,0,N/R,Not tabulated.,0,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulation.,1,"Same as PGG-P description with s=3: after games, leaders can punish defectors, reducing payoff of target by k·s=6 and paying cost s=3 themselves. Other structural/network parameters as in PGG-M comparison (β=0.3, ν=10, p_l=0.1, T_th=0.3, N=1000).",Derived from general PGG-P description and assuming intermediate s condition in Fig. 5.,0.4
10.1142_s0129183121501278.md,Simulation – PGG-P with high-cost punishment s=5,"Text explicitly emphasizes that s>=5 is required for PGG-P to reach or exceed PGG-M contribution levels and that this is very costly, implying a specific high-cost treatment.",0.95,Game type: PGG-P with punishment parameter s=5 (high cost); punishment multiplier k=2; β=0.3; ν=10; p_l=0.1; T_th=0.3.,"Text: ""For reaching PGG-M contribution level, we have to put s>=5"" and describes consequences when s=5 as example of high cost.",0.9,False,Agent-based simulation.,1,False,"Computational, not empirical.",1,False,No lab.,1,True,Part of the simulation study.,1,False,No analytical modeling.,1,1000,Same population size as other simulations.,1,5000,Same time-steps reported.,0.8,0,Players still choose c=e or c=0.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No concept of display; only local payoffs.,0.7,0,No modeling of ID revelation.,0.5,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,No horizon information to agents.,0.7,1,Punishment mechanism is active.,1,5,s=5 means a punisher forfeits 5 units per sanction.,1,2,k=2 for PGG-P; target loses 2·s=10 per punishment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,Endowment e=10 for all players.,1,N/R,"Contribution trajectories are compared qualitatively, showing that high s produces higher contributions, but values are not given.",0,N/R,Not numerically specified.,0,N/R,No efficiency measure (benefit minus punishment costs) is computed.,0,N/R,Group payoffs under high-cost punishment are not tabulated.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Purely computational simulation.,1,"Same PGG-P structure: after each game, leaders may punish defectors, losing s=5 of their own payoff to subtract k·s=10 from target’s payoff. Model parameters otherwise match PGG-M comparison scenario (β=0.3, ν=10, p_l=0.1, T_th=0.3, N=1000). This condition achieves contribution levels comparable to or exceeding PGG-M but at high cost to punishers.",Restates the described high-cost punishment scenario used to argue that punishment is less attractive than rewiring.,0.95
