10.1561_102.00000099.md,Unequal – No Punishment,"Cell of 2×2: Unequal endowments, no punishment.",1,"Endowment distribution: Unequal (Low=40 ED, High=60 ED; two of each per group); Punishment institution: No Punishment",Unequal endowments treatment without punishment stage.,1,True,Lab experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled experimental design.,1,True,Run in experimental economics laboratory.,1,False,No non-human simulations as separate conditions.,1,False,"Empirical treatment, not only analytical.",1,4,Groups of four in all treatments.,1,15,Fifteen periods played.,1,1,"Subjects chose extraction amount along a range, not restricted to 0 or full endowment.",0.9,0,Subjects allocate from own endowment between group and private accounts; default is private.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear payoff function; MPCR not a single constant.,1,0,No communication stage mentioned.,0.9,1,"After decisions, subjects saw group aggregate extraction and own earnings.",0.95,0,No punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward institution.,1,1,"Likely told total number of periods (15), as in similar designs.",0.6,0,This is explicitly the No Punishment treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment possible.,1,N/A,No punishment possible.,1,0,No reward mechanism described in any treatment.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,N/A,Rewards not implemented.,1,200,Group endowment fixed at 200 ED with heterogeneity: two Low (40) and two High (60). For heterogeneous case we record total as 200 to express heterogeneity; details in other_game_info.,0.8,N/R,"They report extractions by type and treatment, not normalized rates.",1,31.75,Table 3: average extractions in Unequal No Punishment (all periods) = 31.75 ED (pooled over Low and High).,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0.6,105.91,Table 2: average period earnings in Unequal No Punishment (all periods) = 105.91 ED per subject.,0.9,USA,University of Massachusetts Amherst.,1,N/R,Only undergraduate status mentioned.,0.7,N/R,No gender information.,1,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate population.,1,On site,Run in a physical lab.,1,"Same CPR structure: V(G)=6G−0.025G², w=1, group size 4, 15 periods. Endowment heterogeneity: in each group two Low subjects with 40 ED and two High with 60 ED, fixed across periods. Subjects know distribution but not which ID is which endowment. No punishment stage.",Summarizes unequal endowment and lack of punishment for this treatment.,1
10.1561_102.00000099.md,Unequal – Punishment,Cell of 2×2: Unequal endowments with punishment.,1,"Endowment distribution: Unequal (Low=40 ED, High=60 ED; two of each per group); Punishment institution: Peer Punishment enabled (1:3 technology)",Heterogeneous endowments combined with peer punishment.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Randomized controlled treatment in laboratory.,1,True,Implemented at experimental economics lab.,1,False,"Results are from human subjects, not a simulation-only study.",1,False,Analytical benchmarks but empirical data for this condition.,1,4,Groups of four in all treatments.,1,15,Fifteen periods in experiment.,1,1,Continuous choice of extraction from endowment.,0.9,0,"Endowment is under subject control, then allocated between group and private accounts.",0.9,N/R,Nonlinear CPR technology; MPCR not constant.,1,0,No mention of communication.,0.9,1,Subjects saw group aggregate extraction and individual earnings after each period.,0.95,1,"Punishers assign deduction points to each group member by a random ID they observe, so IDs are shown for punishment targeting.",0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism exists.,1,1,Likely informed of 15-period horizon; consistent with literature though not spelled out.,0.6,1,Punishment stage with deduction points is available.,1,1,Each punishment point costs sender 1 ED.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces receiver payoff by 3 ED.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,200,Total group endowment 200 ED with heterogeneity: two 40 ED (Low) and two 60 ED (High).,0.8,N/R,"Only extraction levels in ED reported, not normalized fraction of endowment.",1,28.81,Table 3: average extractions in Unequal Punishment (all periods) = 28.81 ED (pooled).,1,N/R,Realized efficiency relative to social optimum not reported.,0.6,100.77,Table 2: average period earnings in Unequal Punishment (all periods) = 100.77 ED per subject.,0.9,USA,Study run at a US university lab.,1,N/R,Age not provided; only undergraduate status.,0.7,N/R,No gender information.,1,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate population.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment in physical lab.,1,"CPR game with V(G)=6G−0.025G², private return w=1, group size 4, 15 periods. Heterogeneous endowments: in each group two Low (40 ED) and two High (60 ED), fixed identities but hidden; subjects know the distribution but not which ID corresponds to which endowment. After extraction decisions, subjects observe each group member’s extraction and random ID (but not endowments), then assign punishment points costing 1 ED each, reducing target payoff by 3 ED. Payoffs in a period can be negative because punishment is only constrained by initial earnings.",Summarizes key structural and informational aspects specific to unequal with punishment treatment.,1
10.1007_s11238-015-9527-7.md,Theoretical cheap-talk signaling model of social preferences,The paper presents only a formal game-theoretic model and equilibrium characterization (Theorem 1) with no distinct empirical treatments; treat the core modeled game as a single analytical condition.,0.9,Sender’s type parameter β_ij (friendliness/spitefulness level) relative to endogenous threshold β*; message choice in an open-ended cheap-talk space,The only dimension that varies in the model’s analysis is the agent’s social-preference type β_ij and the resulting partition of message space into ‘friendly’ vs ‘unfriendly’ messages around β*.,0.9,False,"No human participants, data collection, or experiments are described; the paper is purely theoretical.",1.0,False,There is no controlled manipulation with human or field data; only a formal model and equilibrium concepts are used.,1.0,False,No lab or experimental setting is reported; analysis is theoretical.,1.0,False,The paper does not report any numerical or agent-based simulations; it relies on analytical derivations.,1.0,True,"Utility functions, Bayesian games, and neologism-proof equilibrium are derived analytically with proofs but no numerical simulation or data.",1.0,2,The core game analyzed in Sect. 3 is ‘a game of cheap talk with a single talker’ where player i sends a message to player j; this is effectively a 2-player interaction.,0.9,1,The cheap-talk interaction considered is a single-stage game: player i chooses one message; no repeated rounds are modeled.,0.9,1,"The model does not constrain message choice to a binary/all-or-nothing action; the sender can pick any message in a large (uncountable) message space, so choice is effectively continuous/free rather than all-or-nothing.",0.8,N/A,No public-goods-style endowment and contribution decision is modeled; only cheap-talk messages are chosen.,1.0,N/R,No linear public goods production or MPCR parameter is defined in the model.,1.0,N/A,"‘Cheap talk’ here is part of the theoretical game definition, not a human communication protocol; there are no human participants.",1.0,0,The model specifies beliefs and priors analytically; it does not include any experimental feedback screens showing summaries of payoffs or punishments.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or identity revelation mechanism is implemented in the modeled game.,1.0,N/A,No explicit reward stage is modeled; only messages that affect beliefs and utilities.,1.0,0,The interaction is a one-shot analytical game; no design choice about displaying the number of rounds to participants exists.,1.0,0,"The focal cheap-talk model has no explicit costly punishment action; punishment is only discussed informally in the broader discussion, not implemented in the game.",0.9,N/A,No punishment option is part of the formal game structure analyzed.,1.0,N/A,No punishment technology or mapping from punishment points to losses is specified.,1.0,0,"The analytical model does not include a separate, explicit reward action; utilities change via beliefs about social preferences instead.",0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism with explicit cost/payoff technology is modeled.,1.0,N/A,No parametric reward technology is given.,1.0,N/R,There is no endowed monetary token or payoff structure like in public goods or dictator games; payoffs are abstract utilities.,1.0,N/A,The model does not involve contribution decisions to a public good; only the choice of a message is analyzed.,1.0,N/A,No contribution amount variable exists in the cheap-talk model.,1.0,N/R,The paper does not define or compute efficiency as a fraction of maximum group payoff; focus is on signaling and equilibrium types.,1.0,N/R,No numerical or closed-form group payoff statistic is given; only individual utility forms and comparative statics are provided.,1.0,N/A,No human participants are involved; thus no participant demographics exist.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects; no ages reported.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects; gender composition is irrelevant.,1.0,N/A,No sample of human participants is recruited; there is no education information.,1.0,No human,"The work is purely theoretical with no experiments, field data, or online studies.",1.0,"Two-player Bayesian cheap-talk game with a single sender (player i) and one receiver (player j); sender has private type parameters a_ij and λ_i determining β_ij, chooses a costless message from an uncountable message space; material outcome x is exogenous and independent of the message; utilities incorporate outcome utility, social image concerns via function S of expected β_ji, and other-regarding preferences via β_ij u_j; equilibrium concept is neologism-proof (or announcement-proof) refinement of perfect Bayesian equilibrium, yielding a partially separating equilibrium with threshold β* partitioning friendly (β_ij > β*) and unfriendly (β_ij < β*) types’ message sets.","Summarized from Sections 2–3 and the statement and proof sketch of Theorem 1, which fully describe the modeled game structure and equilibrium characterization.",0.96
10.1038_s41598-020-79731-y.md,"Simulation – No punishment (β=0, γ=0) across r∈[3,6], ζ∈[0,1]","Single simulation condition family described: β=0, γ=0 while r and ζ are varied in Fig. 1.",0.94,"Synergy factor r varied from 3 to 6 (step 0.2) and selfishness ζ varied from 0 to 1 (step 0.1); no punishment (β=0, γ=0).",Explicit description under 'Without punishment' computational model section.,0.97,False,"Agent-based computational model with evolving agents, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely simulation.,1,False,No laboratory human study; purely computational.,1,True,Described as computational agent based evolutionary model.,1,False,This part uses numerical simulations rather than closed-form derivations.,0.9,5,They analyze groups of k+1 players with k=4 used for illustration; same structure used in simulations as in Hintze 2015 model.,0.7,1,Standard one-shot public goods interaction per game in these evolutionary models; no repeated-round description.,0.7,1,Each individual either contributes 1 unit or 0; binary choice not continuous fraction.,0.95,N/A,No human subjects; endowment framing not applicable.,1,N/R,They report only synergy factor r and group size; do not explicitly give MPCR=r/(k+1).,0.4,N/A,No humans; communication not modeled.,1,0,Agents receive payoffs via programmed rules; no interface or feedback summaries.,0.9,N/A,"Punishment option exists as a gene but with β=0,γ=0; no identity/observability concept in simulation.",1,N/A,No rewarding mechanism in the model.,1,0,Agents do not see a UI; generations continue until fixed number but not as 'rounds' to players.,0.9,1,"Punishment gene pP is present, but punishment fine and cost are set to zero (β=0, γ=0).",1,0,Explicitly: 'we set β=0 and γ=0' for no punishment condition.,1,0,"Fine β is set to 0, so effect per punishment unit is 0.",1,0,"Model includes only punishment, no reward or positive sanctions.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,Each cooperator contributes 1 unit; this is the effective per-round endowment relevant to contribution.,0.9,N/R,They discuss probability to cooperate qualitatively and via heatmaps but do not report specific numeric averages.,0.5,N/R,"Only probabilities to cooperate are given, not expected contribution counts.",0.4,N/R,No explicit ratio of actual to maximal group payoffs reported.,0.4,N/R,Net earnings and payoffs are defined symbolically but no concrete simulation averages are reported.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly a computational evolutionary agent-based model.,1,"Agent-based evolutionary public goods game with group size k+1 (k≈4), binary cooperate/defect actions, genes pC and pP mutating over 100,000 generations; 100 independent replicate runs per (r, ζ) combination; payoffs redistributed based on selfishness parameter ζ; punishment parameters set to β=0, γ=0.",Summarizes the simulation protocol as described in 'Computational evolutionary model' and Fig. 1 caption.,0.96
10.1038_s41598-020-79731-y.md,"Simulation – With punishment: 6 β,γ-scenarios across r, ζ (cooperation–punishment phase diagrams)",Described as six experiments where β is increased from 0 to 1 and γ reduced from 1 to 0 in Fig. 2.,0.9,"Synergy factor r, selfishness ζ, and punishment parameters β and γ jointly varied. Six combinations of β and γ from (β=0, γ=1) toward (β=1, γ=0); r and ζ varied on grids as in no-punishment case.",Section 'With punishment' states six different combinations of cost and fine were tested; same analysis protocol as Fig. 1.,0.8,False,Computational agent-based evolutionary model; no human data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical simulations.,1,False,Not a lab study.,1,True,Explicitly agent-based evolutionary simulations.,1,False,"This condition refers to numerical results, not closed-form derivations.",0.9,5,Simulation uses same public goods structure analyzed mathematically with k+1 players and illustration for k=4.,0.7,1,Each generation consists of single public goods interactions; no repeated rounds per group.,0.7,1,"As in base model, agents either contribute 1 unit or 0.",0.95,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,MPCR=r/(k+1) implied but not numerically given.,0.4,N/A,No humans; no communication modeled.,1,0,No user interface; agents simply receive payoffs.,0.9,N/A,Identity/reputation is not modeled; punishment is purely payoff-based.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,0,No human-visible rounds; fixed generations but not displayed.,0.9,1,"Punishment gene pP and positive β,γ values are used; punishment is part of the payoff.",1,N/R,"Exact γ grid values for all six experiments are not listed, only that γ decreases from 1.0 to 0.0 across them.",0.5,N/R,"Exact β values for each of the six experiments not fully enumerated, only described as increasing from 0 to 1.",0.5,0,Only punishment is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,1,Same basic public goods game: contribution is 1 unit.,0.9,N/R,Phase diagrams display probabilities to cooperate qualitatively (color scale) but no numeric table is reported.,0.5,N/R,"Only probabilities, not average contributions, are reported.",0.4,N/R,They discuss when cooperation evolves but do not report explicit efficiency ratios.,0.4,N/R,No explicit numerical group payoff outcomes from simulations.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational simulations.,1,"Agent-based evolutionary public goods game as in Hintze 2015 with punishment: agents have probabilities pC (cooperate) and pP (punish); 100 replicate evolutionary runs per parameter combination; 100,000 generations each; payoffs include public goods payoff, punishment fines β and costs γ, and redistribution based on selfishness ζ; six different (β,γ) combinations explored with varying synergy r and selfishness ζ; cooperation and punishment probabilities at convergence plotted as phase diagrams.",Summarizes 'With punishment' simulation protocol and Fig. 2 description.,0.95
10.1038_s41598-020-79731-y.md,"Simulation – Local β,γ sweeps for fixed r, ζ (fine vs cost on critical point)",Described under 'Further inspection' and Fig. 3 as additional simulations varying β and γ for selected r and ζ.,0.88,"Punishment fine β and cost γ varied over a grid for selected fixed synergy r values and selfishness ζ values; r, ζ combinations chosen close to critical points.","Text: 'For selected values of synergy r and degrees of selfishness ζ already close to the critical point, varying cost and fine shows... (see Fig. 3)'.",0.86,False,"Still within computational agent-based framework, no human players.",1,False,"Simulation study, not empirical.",1,False,No lab with human subjects.,1,True,Described explicitly as computational model investigating β and γ effects.,1,False,"This entry refers to numerical phase diagrams, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,5,Same underlying model as other simulations: groups of k+1 players with k≈4.,0.7,1,Same one-shot public goods interaction per generation.,0.7,1,Agents choose to contribute 1 or 0.,0.95,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,Not numerically specified; only r and k conceptually defined.,0.4,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,No UI; only internal payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No identity or observability modeled.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,0,Agents do not observe round counts.,0.9,1,Punishment with varying β and γ is explicitly studied.,1,N/R,Exact γ values in the sweep are not listed.,0.5,N/R,Exact β grid is not provided.,0.5,0,Only punishment considered.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,1,Same base game: one unit contribution possible.,0.9,N/R,Fig. 3 presents heatmaps of probability to cooperate (Pc) but no numeric averages are tabulated.,0.5,N/R,Only Pc is visualized; no raw amounts.,0.4,N/R,Effect on critical point discussed but not as explicit efficiency ratio.,0.4,N/R,No numeric group payoff outputs.,0.4,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,N/A,No human agents.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulations only.,1,"Same evolutionary PGG with punishment as other simulations; for a few fixed (r, ζ) combinations close to the cooperation critical point, β (fine) and γ (cost) are varied in a 2D grid. For each (β,γ), 100 replicate runs of 100,000 generations are performed, and long-run probabilities to cooperate (Pc) and punish (Pp) are plotted (white=1, black=0) to show that β shifts the critical point more strongly than γ.",Summarizes the protocol tied to Fig. 3 and its description.,0.95
10.1038_s41598-020-79731-y.md,"Analytical model – No punishment (β=0, γ=0) with selfishness ζ",Section 'Critical points without punishment' derives payoffs and critical rC with β=γ=0.,0.98,"Selfishness parameter ζ∈[0,1], synergy factor r, group size k+1; no punishment (β=0, γ=0).","Analytical expressions P_C, P_D, and rC(ζ,k) derived under these variables.",0.98,False,Purely mathematical analysis; no subjects.,1,False,Not an experiment but analytical modeling.,1,False,No lab component.,1,False,"This entry refers only to analytical derivation, not simulations.",1,True,Closed-form derivations of payoffs and critical points for the public goods game with selfishness parameter.,1,5,"General formula uses k+1 players; they provide explicit interpretation for k=4, so typical case is 5 players.",0.7,1,Standard one-shot PGG analyzed; no repetition in model.,0.9,1,Each individual can either 'cooperate by making a contribution of 1 unit' or defect and contribute 0.,1,N/A,No human experiment; concept of default contribution not applicable.,1,N/R,They define payoffs using r and k but do not explicitly name MPCR=r/(k+1).,0.5,N/A,No players or interface.,1,0,"Analytical model, no information structure for players.",1,N/A,Punishment not present in this analytical condition.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,0,No game display or rounds for subjects.,1,0,"Assumes β=γ=0 and N_M=N_I=0, so no punishment.",1,N/A,Punishment is not part of this condition.,1,N/A,Punishment not modeled here.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the analytical model.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,1,Cooperators contribute 1 unit; that is their effective per-game stake.,0.95,N/R,"Analytical model yields conditions (rC) for cooperation to be favorable, not actual equilibrium contribution rates.",0.6,N/R,Does not produce quantitative contributions beyond binary action definition.,0.6,N/R,"Net and individual payoffs are expressed symbolically, but not normalized to maximum payoff to report efficiency.",0.6,N/R,"Net group earnings are given as (r−1)n, but not as an empirical or parameterized DV for specific conditions.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical derivations.,1,"One-shot public goods game with k+1 players; each cooperator contributes 1 to a common pool; total contributions are multiplied by a synergy factor r and shared equally among all players. Introduces selfishness parameter ζ∈[0,1] defining degree of individual vs group-level payoff redistribution. Derives payoffs P_C and P_D and the critical synergy r_C=ζ·k+1 below which defection is optimal and above which cooperation can be favored.",Captures the structure of the analytical model without punishment as described in the 'Critical points without punishment' section.,0.98
10.1038_s41598-020-79731-y.md,"Analytical model – With punishment (β>0, γ≥0) and selfishness ζ","Section 'Critical points with punishment' derives rC as function of β, γ, ρP, ζ, and k.",0.98,"Punishment fine β, punishment cost γ, density of punishers ρP, selfishness ζ∈[0,1), synergy factor r, group size k+1.","Expression for rC includes β, γ, ρP, ζ and k; model analyzes their influence on cooperation.",0.98,False,Mathematical derivation only.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with subjects.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,"This record pertains to the analytical expression, not the simulations that later confirm it.",1,True,Analytical expression rC=ζ(1−βρP)(k+1)−ζ+1−(1−ζ)(β+γ)ρP is derived.,1,5,General model uses k+1 players; illustrative numerical example uses k=4 (so 5 players).,0.7,1,One-shot PGG framework.,0.9,1,Players either contribute 1 unit (cooperate) or 0 (defect).,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,N/R,Not labeled or computed explicitly as MPCR.,0.5,N/A,No players with communication.,1,0,"Theoretical model only, no observability of others’ payoffs modeled.",1,N/A,"Model specifies that defectors are punished by punishers, but does not include identity revelation as a parameter.",1,N/A,No reward system.,1,0,No dynamic rounds for agents.,1,1,Defines defectors receiving fine β/k from each punisher and punishers paying cost γ/k per defector.,1,N/R,γ is a free symbolic parameter; no specific numeric value is fixed for this analytical condition.,0.7,N/R,β is a free symbolic parameter; no numeric value fixed.,0.7,0,Only punishment is considered.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,"As before, each cooperator contributes 1 unit.",0.95,N/R,Analytical threshold rC distinguishes regions where cooperation can invade but does not give explicit equilibrium contribution frequencies.,0.6,N/R,Binary contribution structure only; no quantitative DV reported.,0.6,N/R,Effect of punishment and ζ on rC discussed; efficiency not explicitly defined as output variable.,0.6,N/R,Net group payoff is affected by punishment but not reported as a numeric DV.,0.6,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Theoretical analysis only.,1,"Extends the one-shot PGG with selfishness ζ to include costly punishment. Defectors and immoralists receive a punishment fine β/k from each punisher; moralists and immoralists pay a punishment cost γ/k per defector. Defines density of punishers ρP=(NM+NI)/k and derives the critical synergy rC=ζ(1−βρP)(k+1)−ζ+1−(1−ζ)(β+γ)ρP, showing how inclusiveness (1−ζ), punishment fine β, and cost γ jointly affect the conditions for cooperation.",Summarizes the structure of the analytical punishment model as described in 'Critical points with punishment'.,0.99
10.1002_bdm.2243.md,"Experiment 1 – Punishment condition (CRD vs PGD, taking vs giving)","Experiment 1 has one between-subject factor: sanction type (punishment vs reward). This row captures the punishment condition, which includes both CRD and PGD games faced within-subjects.",1,"Social dilemma type within-subjects (CRD vs PGD), sanction type between-subjects fixed to punishment, sanction technology: decrement points (cost 1, target loses 3).",Independent variables are social dilemma type (CRD vs PGD) varied within each participant and sanction type fixed at punishment for this condition; punishment tech is defined in the sanctioning stage description.,1,True,Human student participants took decisions in lab social dilemma tasks.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with manipulated sanction type and social dilemma type.,1,True,Conducted in the laboratory at Leiden University; participants seated in cubicles with computers.,1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations; only human decisions.,1,False,No formal analytical model as the primary method; focus is on behavioral data analysis.,1,4,Groups consisted of four-person groups in both CRDs and PGDs.,1,30,"Cooperation stage: 15 CRDs + 15 PGDs, each one-shot; participants made 30 cooperation decisions.",1,1,"Each game allowed six discrete contribution/withdrawal levels from 0% to 100%, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"In PGDs, all points start as personal endowment; in CRDs, points start in group account. For this field, default is share in public fund relative to individual endowment; here individual endowment starts at 0 in PGD and all in group in CRD, but standard definition (endowment starts private) implies 0.",0.3,0.5,Both CRDs and PGDs: points left/contributed are doubled and divided equally among four group members; MPCR reported explicitly as 0.5.,1,0,No mention of any communication; participants made decisions individually in cubicles.,0.9,0,Participants were not informed about fellow members’ cooperation decisions before sanctioning; they only saw hypothetical options and did not see earnings summaries.,0.8,0,Interactions were anonymous and groups formed post hoc; no indication that identities of punishers/rewarders were revealed.,0.9,0,Same anonymity applies to reward condition; identities not mentioned as revealed.,0.9,1,Participants were instructed that they would face multiple (15 CRDs and 15 PGDs) one-shot games; implies knowledge of the number of decision tasks.,0.7,1,Punishment condition used decrement points to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,1,Assigning a decrement point cost the punisher 1 point per punished group member.,1,3,Each decrement point reduced target’s payoff by 3 points.,1,0,"This row is the punishment treatment; only decrement points were available, no increment points.",1,N/A,Reward mechanism not available in punishment condition.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism not available in punishment condition.,1,N/R,"In CRDs, points start in group account; in PGDs, personal endowment varied from 5 to 75 points; no single fixed endowment reported that can summarize across games.",0.5,N/R,"They report mean percentage left in group (CRD) and given (PGD) pooled across punishment and reward; not separated by sanction type, so cannot assign a numeric value for this condition only.",0.2,N/R,"Only percentages and relative cooperativeness indices are reported, not raw point contributions by condition.",0.1,N/R,No report of realized efficiency (payoff vs social optimum).,0,N/R,No group or individual payoff statistics per condition are reported.,0,Netherlands,Participants were students from Leiden University in the Netherlands.,1,"M=21.75, SD=2.82, range N/R",Reported mean and SD; no age range.,1,"60 women, 24 men",Counts reported explicitly for Experiment 1 sample.,1,University students,Participants described as students from Leiden University.,1,On site,"Participants came to the laboratory, sat in individual cubicles with computers.",1,"Within-subject CRD vs PGD one-shot games (15 of each). CRD: group account; each player can take up to one-fourth of group account, with 6 options (0–100% in 20% steps). Remaining group points doubled and shared equally. PGD: each player endowed with 5–75 points (step 5) and can give 0–100% (20% steps) to group account; contributed points doubled and shared. Strategy method sanctioning: for each of 180 potential actions (30 games × 6 options), subject pre-specifies whether to assign a decrement point to any group member choosing that option; time limit 5 s per sanctioning decision; groups formed ex post and payoffs computed, but actual payoffs replaced by fixed bonus for practical reasons.","Summarizes task structure, choice granularity, and payoff mapping described in the Experiment 1 method.",1
10.1002_bdm.2243.md,"Experiment 1 – Reward condition (CRD vs PGD, taking vs giving)",Same experimental structure as punishment condition but with reward technology instead of punishment; sanction type is reward between subjects.,1,"Social dilemma type within-subjects (CRD vs PGD), sanction type between-subjects fixed to reward, sanction technology: increment points (cost 1, target gains 3).",Independent variables are social dilemma type (CRD vs PGD) varied within each participant and sanction type fixed at reward for this condition; reward tech is defined in the sanctioning stage.,1,True,Human student participants in lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to punishment vs reward conditions; CRD vs PGD within-subject.,1,True,Conducted in university laboratory cubicles.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Primarily empirical; no standalone analytical model.,1,4,Four-person groups in all games.,1,30,15 CRDs + 15 PGDs one-shot games in cooperation stage.,1,1,Six discrete levels 0–100% allowed.,1,0,Same reasoning as for punishment condition; endowment conceived as private points in PGD; group account holds common resource in CRD.,0.3,0.5,Left/contributed points doubled and split; MPCR reported as 0.5.,1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,0.9,0,No feedback about others’ choices or earnings between stages; sanctioning based on hypothetical choices.,0.8,0,Punishment mechanism not present in this condition; identities not discussed.,1,0,No indication that rewarded players learned who rewarded them; interactions were anonymous with groups formed after data collection.,0.9,1,Instructions described that they face 15 CRDs and 15 PGDs; implies subjects know number of games.,0.7,0,Only increment (reward) points available; no decrement points in this condition.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism disabled.,1,N/A,Punishment mechanism disabled.,1,1,Reward condition used increment points to increase others’ payoffs.,1,1,Assigning an increment point cost the rewarder 1 point per rewarded group member.,1,3,Each increment point increased target’s payoff by 3 points.,1,N/R,"Endowment in PGDs varied 5–75; no single canonical endowment. In CRDs, property initially in group account.",0.5,N/R,Reported mean contributions by social dilemma type pooled across sanction type; not separated for reward-only participants.,0.2,N/R,No raw mean points per condition reported.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency statistics reported.,0,N/R,No payoff outcomes per condition provided.,0,Netherlands,Same sample as Experiment 1 punishment condition.,1,"M=21.75, SD=2.82, range N/R",Sample description applies to whole Experiment 1.,1,"60 women, 24 men","Reported overall; assignment to punishment vs reward not split by gender, but same pool.",1,University students,Students from Leiden University.,1,On site,Lab cubicles with PCs.,1,"Identical game structure to punishment condition: 15 CRDs and 15 structurally equivalent PGDs with six discrete options (0–100% in 20% steps). In sanctioning stage, for each game-option combination, participants pre-specify whether to assign an increment point to any group member choosing that option; each increment point costs 1 and gives 3 to target; max 3 points cost per game and 9 points received. Strategy method with 5-second time limit per decision; about 2.14% of sanction decisions missing due to timeout. Groups formed ex post; for practical reasons, actual sanction earnings replaced by fixed €2 bonus.","Summarizes aspects relevant to reward condition, as described in Experiment 1.",1
10.1002_bdm.2243.md,"Experiment 2 – Punishment condition, self-interest focus (CRD-taking vs PGD-keeping; taking vs keeping and taking vs giving/ leaving vs keeping treatments where CRD/PGD actions both emphasize self-interest)","Experiment 2 has between-subject factors: sanction type and decision focus (self-interest vs collective interest). This row captures all punishment-condition participants assigned to self-interest decision focus (i.e., treatments where CRD actions were framed as taking and PGD actions as keeping). The paper collapses across treatments when coding decision focus, so we follow that aggregation.",0.7,Sanction type: punishment; Decision focus: self-interest (CRD decision framed as taking; PGD decision framed as keeping); Social dilemma type within-subjects (CRD vs PGD).,Decision focus factor defined as whether actions emphasize self-interest vs collective interest (taking/keeping vs leaving/giving). This row corresponds to punishment + self-interest focus combination.,0.7,True,Human students in lab-based social dilemma tasks.,1,True,Random assignment to sanction type and to one of four treatments that map to decision focus conditions.,1,True,Conducted in psychology lab with participants seated in cubicles.,1,False,No simulation models reported.,1,False,Behavioral experiment without formal analytic model as main method.,1,4,Four-person groups in both CRDs and PGDs.,1,30,As in Experiment 1: 15 CRDs and 15 PGDs presented as one-shot games.,1,1,Six discrete choices between 0% and 100% allowed per game.,1,0,"PGD endowments begin as personal points; CRD property initially in group account; mapping to this field remains ambiguous, defaulted to 0 as private starting point.",0.3,0.5,Identical payoff structure: left/given points doubled and shared; MPCR=0.5.,1,0,No communication mentioned between group members.,0.9,0,No feedback on others’ decisions or earnings; strategy method uses hypothetical contingencies.,0.8,0,Anonymized grouping and payoff computation; identities of punishers not disclosed.,0.9,0,Reward mechanism not used in this condition; no identity information reported.,1,1,"Participants informed about engaging in multiple CRDs and PGDs; structure mirrors Experiment 1, implying known number of decision tasks.",0.7,1,Punishment condition uses decrement points to reduce others’ earnings.,1,1,Each decrement point costs punisher 1 point.,1,3,Each decrement point reduces the punished member’s payoff by 3 points.,1,0,No increment points; only punishment enabled.,1,N/A,Reward disabled in punishment condition.,1,N/A,Reward disabled in punishment condition.,1,N/R,PGD endowments vary from 5 to 75 points; CRD uses varying group account sizes; no single endowment.,0.5,N/R,Cooperation statistics (percentages) are reported aggregated across decision focus and sanction type; no separate numeric values for this specific cell (punishment + self-interest).,0.2,N/R,No mean raw point contributions provided per condition.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency (payoff / max payoff) not reported.,0,N/R,No payoff statistics per condition.,0,Netherlands,Students from Leiden University in the Netherlands.,1,"M=21.41, SD=3.86, range N/R",Mean and SD reported for Experiment 2 sample.,1,"167 women, 73 men",Counts provided for Experiment 2 overall sample.,1,University students,Participants were students from Leiden University.,1,On site,Described as conducted in the laboratory with cubicles and PCs.,1,"Same structural games as Experiment 1: 15 CRDs and 15 PGDs, with two verbal versions each. In this condition, CRD decisions are framed in the taking version (""how many group points to take"") and PGD decisions in the keeping version (""how many personal points to keep""); both emphasize self-interest. Six options per game (0–100% in 20% steps) corresponding to degree of cooperativeness. Strategy-method sanctioning: for each of 30 games × 6 options, participants decide whether to assign a decrement point to any group member choosing that option; decision screens show one specific choice option of a specific game. 5-second time limit per sanctioning decision; ~1.3% of sanction decisions missing from timeouts. Groups matched after all data collected; this experiment was fully incentivized and participants were paid based on actual earnings via bank transfer.",Combines Experiment 2 description with specific taking/keeping framing that induces self-interest focus in this cell.,0.8
10.1002_bdm.2243.md,"Experiment 2 – Punishment condition, collective-interest focus (CRD-leaving vs PGD-giving; leaving vs giving and taking vs giving/leaving vs keeping treatments where CRD/PGD actions both emphasize collective interest)",Captures punishment-condition participants whose decision focus is coded as collective interest (CRD actions framed as leaving; PGD actions as giving). Decision focus is defined and analyzed as a factor; this row corresponds to punishment + collective-interest focus.,0.7,Sanction type: punishment; Decision focus: collective interest (CRD decision framed as leaving; PGD decision framed as giving); Social dilemma type within-subjects (CRD vs PGD).,Decision focus 'collective interest' corresponds to leaving in CRD and giving in PGD; this row is for punishment condition with that focus.,0.7,True,Human participants in lab experiment.,1,True,Randomized between-subjects on sanction type and treatment mapping to focus; within-subject on social dilemma type.,1,True,"In-lab, computerized tasks.",1,False,No simulations.,1,False,Data analyzed with mixed models but no analytical model as standalone method.,1,4,Groups of four.,1,30,15 CRDs + 15 PGDs in cooperation stage.,1,1,Six contribution/withdrawal levels (0–100%).,1,0,Same ambiguity; best to retain as 0.,0.3,0.5,Left/given points doubled and shared.,1,0,No communication feature described.,0.9,0,No interim summaries of others’ outcomes.,0.8,0,No indication that punished know who punished them.,0.9,0,Reward mechanism absent.,1,1,Participants told about series of social dilemma games; same as Experiment 1.,0.7,1,Decrement points enabled.,1,1,Decrement point costs 1 point.,1,3,Each decrement reduces target by 3.,1,0,Only punishment enabled.,1,N/A,Reward disabled in this condition.,1,N/A,Reward disabled in this condition.,1,N/R,Endowments vary; no single value.,0.5,N/R,Cooperation rates by sanction type × decision focus not numerically reported.,0.2,N/R,No mean point contributions per cell.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency data.,0,N/R,No payoff outcomes by condition.,0,Netherlands,Same Experiment 2 pool.,1,"M=21.41, SD=3.86, range N/R",Applies to all Experiment 2 participants.,1,"167 women, 73 men",Overall sample composition.,1,University students,Leiden University students.,1,On site,Lab-based experiment.,1,"Game payoffs as in Experiment 1. In this decision-focus condition, CRD cooperation framed as leaving group points in the common resource; PGD cooperation framed as giving personal points to the group account. Six options (0–100% in 20% steps) per game, mapped to percentage cooperativeness. Strategy-method punishment decisions for each game-option; time limit 5 s; about 1.3% of decisions missed due to timeouts. Groups matched post hoc; payoffs from both cooperation and sanctioning stages determined actual monetary earnings, paid via bank transfer.",Summarizes Experiment 2 structure with emphasis on leaving/giving framing for collective-interest focus.,0.8
10.1002_bdm.2243.md,"Experiment 2 – Reward condition, self-interest focus (CRD-taking vs PGD-keeping)","Represents participants in Experiment 2 assigned to reward condition (increment points) and to decision-focus level 'self-interest' (taking in CRD, keeping in PGD).",0.7,Sanction type: reward; Decision focus: self-interest (CRD framed as taking; PGD framed as keeping); Social dilemma type within-subjects (CRD vs PGD).,"Independent variables are sanction type (reward), decision focus (self-interest), and within-subject social dilemma type.",0.7,True,Uses human participants.,1,True,Randomized conditions and controlled design.,1,True,Performed in university lab.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,No pure analytical model.,1,4,Four-player groups.,1,30,Participants faced 15 CRDs and 15 PGDs.,1,1,Multiple discrete contribution/withdrawal options.,1,0,Same as other Experiment 2 cells.,0.3,0.5,Reported constant MPCR.,1,0,No chat/communication.,0.9,0,No other players' outcome summaries shown.,0.8,0,Punishment disabled in this condition.,1,0,Anonymity maintained; no mention of revealing rewarder identity.,0.9,1,As in Experiment 1; they knew they would complete multiple one-shot games.,0.7,0,Only reward (increment) points available.,1,N/A,Punishment unavailable.,1,N/A,Punishment unavailable.,1,1,Reward condition with increment points.,1,1,Increment point costs rewarder 1 point.,1,3,Each increment point gives target 3 points.,1,N/R,Endowments vary from 5 to 75 across PGDs; group account size varies in CRDs.,0.5,N/R,Cooperation means not reported by sanction type × focus.,0.2,N/R,No raw contribution data by condition.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency not provided.,0,N/R,No payoff statistics by condition.,0,Netherlands,Same Experiment 2 sample.,1,"M=21.41, SD=3.86, range N/R",Reported for whole sample.,1,"167 women, 73 men",Overall Experiment 2 counts.,1,University students,Leiden University students.,1,On site,Lab setting.,1,"Identical payoff structure to other Experiment 2 conditions. CRDs framed as taking from group account; PGDs framed as keeping personal points, reinforcing focus on self-interest. Six discrete options representing 0–100% cooperativeness. Strategy-method reward decisions: for each game-option combination, participants indicate whether to assign an increment point to any group member who chose that option. Each increment costs 1 and grants 3 to the recipient; up to 3 points cost per game and 9 gained. 5-second limit for each sanctioning decision; ~1.3% of sanction data missing. Groups formed randomly after data collection; this experiment used actual performance-based payments via bank transfer.",Tailors Experiment 2 description to reward + self-interest focus framing.,0.8
10.1002_bdm.2243.md,"Experiment 2 – Reward condition, collective-interest focus (CRD-leaving vs PGD-giving)","Represents Experiment 2 participants in reward condition with collective-interest decision focus (leaving in CRD, giving in PGD).",0.7,Sanction type: reward; Decision focus: collective interest (CRD framed as leaving; PGD framed as giving); Social dilemma type within-subjects (CRD vs PGD).,Independent variables as defined in Experiment 2 design.,0.7,True,Human subjects study.,1,True,"Randomized, controlled design.",1,True,Conducted in lab environment.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,Primarily empirical.,1,4,Four-person groups.,1,30,Same 15 CRDs + 15 PGDs cooperation decisions.,1,1,Six options from 0% to 100%.,1,0,Same rationale as other rows.,0.3,0.5,Constant MPCR.,1,0,No evidence of chat.,0.9,0,Participants did not observe others' payoffs or decisions in aggregate.,0.8,0,Punishment disabled in this condition.,1,0,No indication of identity revelation regarding rewards.,0.9,1,As elsewhere; participants knew they would make many game decisions.,0.7,0,No decrement points.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,1,Increment points allowed.,1,1,Increment costs 1 point.,1,3,Increment grants 3 points to target.,1,N/R,Game endowments vary; not summarized as single value.,0.5,N/R,No numeric breakdown by sanction type × decision focus.,0.2,N/R,Raw contributions not reported per cell.,0.1,N/R,Efficiency not discussed.,0,N/R,Payoffs not given per treatment.,0,Netherlands,Same Experiment 2 participant pool.,1,"M=21.41, SD=3.86, range N/R",Overall sample statistics.,1,"167 women, 73 men",Applies to whole sample.,1,University students,All drawn from university.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at Leiden University.,1,"Same CRD and PGD payoff structures as other Experiment 2 cells. Here, CRD decisions are framed as leaving group points; PGD decisions as giving personal points. Six options per game representing 0–100% cooperation. Strategy-method reward: participant specifies for each game and choice option whether a group member choosing that option should receive an increment point. Each increment costs 1 point and gives 3 to the recipient; up to 3 points cost and 9 gained per game. 5-second time limit per sanction decision; about 1.3% missing decisions. Groups matched ex post; actual combined cooperation and reward outcomes used for real monetary payments via bank transfer.",Focuses on reward plus collective-interest framing within Experiment 2 design.,0.8
10.1007_s12144-019-0129-3.md,Punishment condition – Ultimatum Game (proposer role),Paper explicitly describes a punishment situation corresponding to the ultimatum game; this row captures proposer behavior when rejection/punishment is possible.,1,"Punishment situation: punishment (ultimatum game); Offer categories available: absolutely unfair (87–93:13–7), relatively unfair (67–73:33–27), fair (53–50:47–50); Pre-set rejection ratios for offers (90%/40%/0% for absolutely unfair/relatively unfair/fair)",Condition differs from non-punishment primarily by presence of punishment (responder may reject) and associated rejection probabilities; offer menu is the same in both conditions.,0.96,True,Twenty undergraduates participated and made allocation decisions.,1,True,Task is a controlled economic game with randomly ordered trials and manipulated punishment condition.,1,True,Participants sat in a shielded room in the laboratory with EEG recording.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations; all decisions by human participants.,1,False,No formal mathematical model developed; study is behavioral + EEG.,1,2,Both ultimatum and dictator games are defined with one proposer and one responder; text describes splitting money between two players.,1,240,Each participant acted as proposer in 240 trials total; punishment and non-punishment were randomly intermixed. At the condition level this corresponds to 240 one-shot interactions because a new partner is used each trial.,0.8,1,"Proposers choose among three categories (absolutely unfair, relatively unfair, fair) rather than contribute a divisible amount to a public good; the all-or-nothing coding is not directly applicable but by definition they choose discrete allocations, not a binary contribute/nothing decision. Following schema, 1 indicates they can choose among more than two options.",0.4,0,"This is not a public goods contribution game; the entire 100 Yuan is initially under proposer control, analogous to 0 proportion in a public fund.",0.5,N/R,Not a public goods game with a multiplier; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication between proposer and responder; responders are previous participants and identified only by number.,1,0,No mention of showing summaries of others’ earnings or past behavior; only current responder number and outcome are shown.,1,0,"Punishment is implemented as rejection causing zero payoffs; responders are anonymous and identified only by serial number, with no identity revealed in association with punishment decisions.",0.9,0,No explicit rewarding actions by other players; only allocations and accept/reject outcomes are involved.,1,0,Instructions and procedure do not mention informing participants about the total number of trials or remaining trials during play.,0.6,1,"In the ultimatum game responder can reject the offer, resulting in zero payoff for both, described explicitly as punishment condition.",1,N/R,Rejection is binary and not framed in cost-per-punishment-unit terms; no per-unit cost specified.,1,N/R,No parametric mapping from punishment points to losses is reported; rejection simply yields zero for both players.,1,0,No mechanism for responders to spend resources to increase others’ payoffs; only default payoffs from accepted offers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,100,Participants as proposers allocate a total of 100 Yuan between themselves and the responder.,1,N/R,Study reports choice ratios for unfair vs fair categories but does not report mean offered amount as a fraction of endowment.,0.5,N/R,"Only category choice counts (e.g., number of fair vs unfair offers) and not average monetary amounts offered are provided for this condition.",0.5,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to maximum is reported or derivable from the information given.,0.5,N/R,Group (proposer+responder) earnings are not reported.,0.5,China,"Participants were recruited from Zhejiang University in Hangzhou, People’s Republic of China.",1,"20-27 (M=22.9, SD=1.86)",Age range and mean with SD are explicitly reported.,1,"10 female, 10 male","Sample described as 20 undergraduates, 10 females.",1,Undergraduate students,All participants are described as undergraduates from Zhejiang University.,1,On site,"Participants sat in a comfortable sofa in a shielded room with EEG equipment, indicating an in-lab, on-site experiment.",1,One-shot framed repeated ultimatum interactions: each of 240 trials pairs proposer with a new responder (previous participants’ recorded responses); three discrete offer categories with variable exact splits within each category; pre-set rejection ratios applied using stored responder data; one randomly selected trial is paid out for real.,Summarized from task and procedure description including use of previous responders’ data and payment scheme.,0.96
10.1007_s12144-019-0129-3.md,Non-punishment condition – Dictator Game (proposer role),Paper explicitly describes a non-punishment situation corresponding to the dictator game; this row captures proposer behavior when rejection/punishment is impossible.,1,"Punishment situation: non-punishment (dictator game); Offer categories available: absolutely unfair (87–93:13–7), relatively unfair (67–73:33–27), fair (53–50:47–50); Rejection ratios effectively 0% for all offers (responders cannot reject).",Condition differs from punishment by absence of rejection/punishment mechanism; offer menu remains identical.,0.96,True,Same 20 human participants acted as proposers in both punishment and non-punishment conditions.,1,True,Within-subject manipulation of punishment vs non-punishment in a controlled lab economic game.,1,True,Conducted in a shielded lab room with EEG recording.,1,False,"Decisions are made by human subjects, not simulated agents.",1,False,No purely analytical modeling presented.,1,2,Dictator game is played between one proposer and one responder splitting 100 Yuan.,1,240,"Each participant completed 240 one-shot allocation decisions (half non-punishment, half punishment) with a new partner in each trial.",0.8,1,"As in the punishment condition, proposers choose among three discrete allocation categories rather than a binary decision; following schema, this is treated as allowing non-binary choices.",0.4,0,"Not a contribution game; proposer initially controls full 100 Yuan, analogous to 0 in a public fund.",0.5,N/R,No public good multiplier; not reported.,1,0,No possibility of communication with responders; they are previous participants linked only via serial numbers.,1,0,"Participants see only current responder number, situation type, allocation options, and own outcome.",1,0,There is no punishment in the dictator game; thus identities associated with punishment cannot be shown.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism by responders; payoffs follow directly from chosen allocations.,1,0,Procedure does not mention displaying total or remaining trials to the participants.,0.6,0,Non-punishment condition explicitly corresponds to dictator game where responders cannot reject offers.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism available.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism available.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism; transfers are simply the chosen allocations.,1,N/A,No distinct reward technology.,1,N/A,No distinct reward technology.,1,100,"As in punishment condition, proposers allocate a total of 100 Yuan.",1,N/R,Reported outcomes are counts of choices by category; mean share to responder is not reported as a fraction of 100.,0.5,N/R,"Only mean choice numbers (e.g., number of unfair vs fair selections) are given, not average amounts transferred.",0.5,N/R,No calculation of total payoffs relative to maximum possible is reported.,0.5,N/R,Group payoff figures are not provided.,0.5,China,Same participant pool: undergraduates at Zhejiang University in China.,1,"20-27 (M=22.9, SD=1.86)",Same participants in both conditions.,1,"10 female, 10 male",Same participant pool as punishment condition.,1,Undergraduate students,Participants are all undergraduates from Zhejiang University.,1,On site,Non-punishment trials were interleaved with punishment trials in the same on-site EEG lab session.,1,"One-shot framed repeated dictator interactions: in each of 240 trials participants act as proposers facing a new responder (using recorded decisions from earlier responders); three discrete offer categories with variable splits within categories; in non-punishment trials, any chosen allocation is automatically implemented, and one random trial is paid for real.",Summarized from description of non-punishment (dictator) situation and payment rule.,0.96
10.1038_s41598-021-88663-0.md,Fear induction condition,"One of three between-subjects inductions (Fear, Anger, Control) applied before the suite of economic games.",1,"Induction type = Fear (anticipatory Trier Social Stress Task, simulated job interview)",Primary manipulated factor; this row corresponds to the Fear group.,1,True,Human participants recruited from Max Planck Institute database.,1,True,Random assignment to emotion inductions and incentivized decisions in controlled lab setting.,1,True,Sessions took place in a computer room at the institute with cubicles.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,No purely analytical or mathematical modeling as primary method.,1,2,"Most cooperative and punishment paradigms (dictator, trust, ultimatum, impunity, 2PP, 3PP) are standard 2-player games. Group size for public good and commons dilemmas not specified, but a single canonical player count is required; using 2 as the modal size.",0.2,1,"Participants make one incentivized decision per game (or a small set averaged), and are paid for one randomly selected decision; no repeated-round interaction with feedback.",0.4,1,"Participants could decide 'how much' to transfer, donate, contribute, etc., implying continuous/graded contributions rather than binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Standard implementations of these games start with full endowment in private accounts; no indication of pre-allocation to public funds.,0.6,N/R,No numerical multiplication factor or group size reported for the public good game; cannot compute MPCR.,0,0,No mention of communication or chat; games administered via computer terminals in cubicles.,0.9,1,Punishment games require observing others’ transfers; responder games require seeing offers; thus at least outcome-relevant summaries of others’ actions/earnings are shown.,0.8,0,Standard 2PP/3PP experiments and SVO setup use anonymous others. No mention of revealing identities.,0.8,0,"No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond standard transfers, and interactions are with anonymous others.",0.9,1,Participants know they are completing a finite suite of tasks and will be paid for one randomly chosen decision; implies known task set/round count.,0.7,1,2nd and 3rd party punishment games are included.,1,N/R,No token/point cost per punishment unit provided in the main text.,0,N/R,No information about how much the target loses per punishment unit.,0,0,"Only punishment, transfer, and donation paradigms are described; no explicit peer reward mechanism.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/R,Exact per-game monetary endowments not described in the main text.,0,N/R,Only factor scores and qualitative patterns are reported; no normalized contribution rates for public good or similar games.,0,N/R,"Raw amounts in individual games (e.g., public good contributions) are not numerically reported.",0,N/R,No efficiency or payoff ratio measures reported.,0,N/R,Total group payoffs are not presented.,0,Germany,"Participants were recruited from the Max Planck Institute for Human Cognitive and Brain Sciences, Leipzig, Germany, and ethics approval is from University of Leipzig.",0.9,"Mean age 26.5 (SD 4.4), range 18–65 (sample eligibility criterion)",Fear group-specific mean and SD reported; eligibility range stated.,1,"25 males, 31 females",Group-specific gender counts reported for the Fear induction (N=56).,1,N/R,No educational attainment or student status information reported.,0,On site,Sessions conducted in a physical computer room with cubicles and separate induction rooms.,1,"Within-subject battery of incentivized games: dictator, trust (1st and 2nd mover), charitable donations, public good, common resource dilemma, Zurich prosocial game, 2nd and 3rd party punishment, ultimatum (1st and 2nd mover), impunity; plus a separate non-social lottery risk task. Paid for one randomly selected decision.",Summarized from ‘Game theoretic paradigms’ and design description.,1
