10.1371_journal.pone.0222724.md,Leader condition – punishment phase,Condition where one randomly selected group member is designated leader and has sole punishment ability during the 9-round punishment phase.,1,Punishment institution type = Leader (single designated punisher immune from punishment),Paper contrasts leader vs peer punishment institutions; this row is the leader institution condition in the punishment phase.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants from University of Michigan.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions and controlled manipulation of punishment institution.,1,True,Conducted in university laboratory; participants escorted to private rooms and used z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or computational simulations described.,1,False,No formal mathematical or purely theoretical model as the primary method.,1,4,Four-person groups explicitly stated multiple times.,1,9,Punishment phase repeated for nine rounds.,1,1,Participants decide how many MUs from 0 to 20 to contribute; continuous within that range.,1,0,All 20 MUs are initially in the personal (private) fund; participants choose how much to move to group fund.,1,0.5,"Contributions to group fund are doubled (multiplier 2) and split among 4 members, so MPCR = 2/4 = 0.5.",1,0,No mention of communication; only computer-mediated contribution and punishment decisions.,0.9,1,"After each round, participants could view their own and others' contributions and earnings, both before and after punishment.",1,1,"In the punishment phase results screen, group members could see, by identifier, who punished whom in peer punishment; in leader condition, leader identity known and able to punish others, and others see deductions from leader. Identities are via unique letters, not anonymous aggregates.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism in design.,1,0,Participants were not told in advance how many rounds; no indication that remaining rounds were displayed.,0.9,1,Leader can spend MUs to deduct others' MUs in this phase.,1,1,It cost one MU to deduct 3 MUs from another participant.,1,3,One MU spent deducts 3 MUs from target.,1,0,Only deduction (punishment) described; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"Each round, each group member received an endowment of 20 MUs.",1,N/R,"Paper reports regression coefficients and qualitative trends, but not average normalized contribution proportion by condition for the punishment phase.",0.3,N/R,Figures show trajectories but not explicit mean MU contributions per round per condition.,0.3,N/R,"Earnings after sanctions are discussed comparatively, but no normalized efficiency (payoff/max payoff) is reported.",0.2,N/R,Text only notes that earnings were higher than in peer punishment holding contributions constant; no raw group payoff values given.,0.3,United States,Participants recruited from University of Michigan in the United States.,1,N/R,Demographics include age but only used as control; no descriptive stats reported.,0.2,N/R,"Gender collected and used as control, but no distribution reported.",0.2,University students,Participants recruited from the general student population at the University of Michigan.,0.9,On site,Participants arrived at the laboratory and were escorted to private rooms with lab computers (z-Tree).,1,"Standard linear public good game with continuous contributions, MPCR=0.5, 4 players, 2 phases before this (9 non-punishment rounds then 9 punishment rounds); in leader condition one randomly selected leader can spend up to 10 MUs per round to deduct up to 30 MUs in total from others; leader cannot be punished and retains role; identifiers anonymized with letters; feedback each round on individual contributions, pre- and post-punishment earnings.",Summary of structural design elements for this condition extracted from Methods.,1
