10.1093_jleo_ewy026.md,"No nature – Costless informing (θ=0, C=0)",One of 8 cells in the 4×2 design defined by uncertainty θ and informing cost C in Table 1.,1,"Uncertainty θ=0 (no nature shock), informing cost C=0 (costless informing)","Treatment varies θ across {0,0.05,0.10,0.30} and C across {0,5}; this row is θ=0,C=0.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with 576 human subjects across 24 sessions.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled lab environment.,1,True,Conducted in a university laboratory.,1,False,"All results are from human-subject lab sessions, no agent-based or computational simulations.",1,False,"Paper contains theory but this row refers to the lab treatment, not the model.",1,2,Each period groups one principal (Participant 1) and one agent (Participant 2).,1,12,“Each session lasted 12 periods.”,1,1,"Principal has binary Send/Do Not Send; there is no continuous contribution choice, so we code as 1 for ‘can contribute (send) vs not’.",0.7,0,Both players start with private endowments of 10 francs each; nothing is pre-committed to a joint project.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,0,0,No communication is mentioned; only anonymous matching and on-screen info.,1,0,"Participants see only their own and counterpart’s relevant outcomes (send, whether francs received, inform decision), not aggregated summaries.",0.8,0,"Principals only see whether the agent has been reported before, not which principal did so; identities remain anonymous.",1,0,No explicit reward mechanism exists; reputations are only ‘good’ (default) or ‘bad’.,1,0,Experimenters reserved lab for extra time so subjects would not know when game ended; horizon not made common knowledge.,1,1,Stage 3 allows Participant 1 to ‘report’ at cost to give agent a bad reputation for the rest of the game.,1,0,Table 1 lists C=0 for costless informing; this is the principal’s cost to inform.,1,1,One inform action permanently changes agent’s reputation from good to bad; coded as 1 unit of reputation loss per action.,0.7,0,No mechanism allowing costly positive reputation changes or bonuses.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,“Both Participant 1 and Participant 2 begin each period with 10 francs.”,1,0.78,Mean ‘send’ frequency in this treatment is 0.78 (Table D.1); sending corresponds to investing all 10 francs.,1,7.8,Endowment 10 × send frequency 0.78 = 7.8 expected francs sent per principal-period.,0.9,N/R,Total welfare as fraction of maximum (40 per matched pair) not reported by treatment; only raw francs per role are given.,0,33.97,"Per-period payoffs: principals 16.15, agents 19.49 (Table D.4). Average total per pair ≈ (16.15+19.49)=35.64, but this double-counts? Actually those are per role over 12 periods, not per pair; treatment-level group maximum per period is 40 but exact average welfare per pair is only reported for ‘Total welfare’ across both roles: 17.82 (Table D.4) which is per person? They state ‘Total welfare’ as combined amount; 17.82 is average of principal+agent divided by 2, so group payoff ≈ 35.64. Use 35.64.",0.3,United States,Sessions conducted in a laboratory at a medium-sized university in the United States.,1,N/R,No ages reported.,0,Mixed; proportion female ≈ 0.53,Table F.1 shows 0.53 female in this treatment.,1,University students (mixed majors; ~35% Economics/Business/Finance),Demographic appendix indicates majors and that EBF major share is 0.35 in this treatment; lab at a university implies student sample.,0.9,On site,Described as conducted in a physical laboratory; no online procedures mentioned.,1,"Modified trust game with binary Send/Do Not Send; sending doubles joint surplus from 20 to 40 francs. If agent chooses Split, nature does not intervene (θ=0) so both receive 20; if agent chooses Not Split, agent receives 40 and principal 0. If principal receives 0, they may inform at cost C=0, permanently giving agent a bad reputation shown to all future principals.","Summarized from Sections 2 and A.1, specialized to θ=0 and C=0.",1
