10.3389_fphy.2018.00156.md,"Authoritative third-party punishment – monomorphic population (risk-neutral, β = 0)",Condition described in Section 2.1 analyzing ESS of cooperators vs defectors under authoritative punishment with risk-neutral utility (β = 0).,1,Risk attitude β = 0 (risk-neutral); punishment observation probability z; fine F; temptation payoff T.,Section 2.1 varies β conceptually and derives z*_neutral for given T and F under risk-neutrality.,0.9,False,"Purely theoretical evolutionary game model, no human subjects.",1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; it is a mathematical analysis.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment; purely theoretical.,1,False,"Section 2.1 is analytical (closed-form ESS conditions), not numerical simulation based.",0.9,True,Derives ESS condition z > (T − 1)/F and its utility-based generalization analytically.,1,2,Weak prisoner’s dilemma between two players (Section 2).,1,1,Each interaction is a single-shot weak prisoner’s dilemma; no repetition within the model.,0.9,1,"Players choose between two discrete actions C or D, not constrained to all-or-nothing public-good framing; mapped as free choice between options.",0.7,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments; only C/D choices in prisoner’s dilemma.,1,N/R,"Model is a 2x2 prisoner’s dilemma, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modeled.,1,0,Information structure only concerns payoffs and punishment; no summaries of others’ outcomes are modeled.,0.8,0,Authority punishment is impersonal; no identity revelation is modeled.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,Infinite-horizon population dynamics with no notion of displayed round count.,1,1,Defectors are fined F with probability z by an authority (Section 2).,1,N/R,The authority’s cost of punishing is mentioned qualitatively but no numeric cost parameter per punishment is defined in this canonical 2-player model.,0.4,N/R,Only total fine F to defectors is given; no separate scaling per unit cost token is defined.,0.4,0,No positive reward mechanism is modeled; only fines for defection.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in the model.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in the model.,1,N/R,Payoffs are defined directly in the matrix; no explicit endowment per round.,0.8,N/R,"Outcome measures are frequencies of cooperators/defectors and ESS conditions, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,Model uses binary C/D choices with payoffs; no explicit contribution amount variable.,1,N/R,"Paper analyzes stability conditions and observation probability, not normalized efficiency statistics.",1,N/R,"Group payoff distributions are discussed implicitly via payoffs, but no summarized DV is reported.",0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical evolutionary game dynamics.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population; 2-player weak prisoner’s dilemma with payoffs: (C,C) = (1,1); (D,C) = (T,0); (C,D) = (0,T); (D,D) = (0,0). Authoritative third-party observes each game with probability z and fines each defector F.",Directly from Section 2 description.,1
10.3389_fphy.2018.00156.md,"Authoritative third-party punishment – monomorphic population (risk-averse, β = -1, illustrative T = 2)",Condition highlighted in Figure 1 and text: risk-averse individuals (β = -1) under authoritative punishment in monomorphic cooperator population.,0.9,Risk attitude β = -1 (risk-averse); T = 2; punishment observation probability z; fine F.,Figure 1 uses β = -1 and T = 2; text discusses effect of β < 0 on required z.,0.9,False,Theoretical evolutionary model.,1,False,No behavioral experiment.,1,False,Not a lab or field study.,1,False,"Analysis of ESS threshold z*_biased; Figure 1 is based on analytic expression, not agent simulation.",0.8,True,Utility function u_s and z*_biased derived symbolically.,1,2,Two-player weak prisoner’s dilemma.,1,1,Single-shot encounters in a repeated population process.,0.9,1,"Binary choice between C and D, no restricted all-or-nothing contribution endowment.",0.7,N/A,No endowment-based contribution.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,No agents/participants.,1,0,Not modeled.,0.8,0,No identity information modeled.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No round display modeled.,1,1,Defectors are fined F with probability z.,1,N/R,Authority’s execution cost is only discussed qualitatively.,0.4,N/R,Only fine magnitude F is specified.,0.4,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit per-round endowment.,0.8,N/R,"Outcomes are ESS conditions for cooperation, not reported as contribution rates.",1,N/R,Binary C/D actions only.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,1,N/R,No aggregated payoff metric provided.,0.6,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Analytical evolutionary model.,1,"Same weak prisoner’s dilemma as in Section 2.1 with T = 2 used for illustration in Figure 1. Utility: u_C = 1, u_D = (1/β) log[z e^{β(T−F)} + (1−z) e^{βT}], and ESS condition z > (1 − e^{−β(T−1)}) / (1 − e^{−βF}) with β = −1.","Directly from equations for u_C, u_D, and z*_biased with parameter choice in Figure 1.",1
