are not described as having knowledge of total or remaining rounds; they are evolutionary strategies, not deliberating subjects.",0.9,0,Model 1 explicitly avoids punishment by forcing investors to always accept the highest known profitable offer (MAO fixed at 1/b).,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward/bonus decisions; trustees’ higher return rates are not modeled as chosen costly rewards per se.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism separate from basic game payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Each investor begins each interaction with one unit of fitness to keep or invest.,1,N/R,"Outcome measures reported are average trust t, average return rate r, and investor fractional payoffs; no normalized contribution rate is defined.",1,N/R,"Investors either invest 1 or 0, but no aggregate contribution amounts are tabulated as a dependent variable.",1,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency as group payoff relative to maximum; focuses on average payoffs and evolved traits.,1,N/R,No explicit total group payoff statistics are reported; only averages per agent type.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely computational evolutionary model with no behavioral lab or field component.,1,"One-shot trust game with partner choice: Ni=500 investors and Nt=500 trustees play x=500 one-shot TG rounds per generation for g=500 generations; trust trait t in [0,1] controls probability of investing in unknown trustees; trustee return rate r in [0,1] determines fraction returned when trusted; investment multiplier b=3; each round investor is matched with k random trustees; each trustee’s r is known with probability q; investor chooses highest known profitable return (r>=1/b) or, if none profitable, invests randomly in an unknown trustee with probability t; otherwise keeps endowment. Evolution via pairwise comparison process with selection intensity β=20, mutation probability μ=0.01, and copy-error magnitude ν=0.005.",Summarizes the game structure and evolutionary parameters directly described in Sections 3.1–3.2.,1
10.1007_s13235-017-0230-x.md,Model 2 – Evolving minimum acceptable offer d with trust,Section 4 introduces Experiment 2 (Model with evolving MAO d) where both trust t and demand d evolve alongside trustee return rate r.,1,"Information transparency q and number of potential partners k; investor traits trust t and demand d, and trustee return rate r evolve.","Again q and k are exogenous parameters (varied in Fig. 2), while t, d, and r are evolving state variables.",1,False,This is an evolutionary agent-based simulation with no human subjects.,1,False,"The term 'Experiment 2' refers to a simulation scenario, not a human behavioral experiment.",1,False,No laboratory setting; all results generated in silico.,1,True,"Agents with parameters t, d, r evolve via pairwise comparison under payoffs from simulated trust games.",1,False,Model explored numerically rather than analytically.,1,1000,Same base population sizes Ni=500 and Nt=500 are used unless otherwise stated; Section 4 does not change them.,0.95,500,Uses same evolutionary schedule as Model 1: x=500 rounds per generation.,0.95,0,"As in Model 1, investors either invest their one unit or keep it; no partial investment choice is described.",0.9,N/A,Not a continuous contribution setting and involves no human players.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR reported; only b=3 in a dyadic trust interaction.,1,N/A,No communication among agents; and no humans.,1,0,Agents observe at most individual trustees’ return rates r when available; no aggregate summaries.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment signals or identities; punishment is only implicit via partner choice.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Agents are evolutionary strategies without modelled knowledge of horizon length.,0.9,0,"Although rejecting profitable offers is interpreted as 'costly punishment', there is no separate punishment stage or cost-per-punish action; our punishmentExists flag refers to dedicated punishment mechanisms.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment technology or per-unit cost is parameterized.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward decisions beyond trustees’ chosen return rate r.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Same trust game structure: investors start with one unit each round.,1,N/R,"Key outcomes are evolved d (MAO), t, r, and 'fractional payoff'; no normalized contribution metric across a group is defined.",1,N/R,Individual investments are binary (1 or 0) but not reported as an explicit DV.,1,N/R,Paper compares average payoffs between conditions (evolving vs fixed d) but does not compute efficiency as total payoff over maximum possible.,1,N/R,Reports average investor fractional payoffs and payoff differences; no aggregate group payoff provided.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,All dynamics occur in a computational model.,1,"Same base trust game as Model 1 (Ni=500, Nt=500, x=500, g=500, b=3, pairwise comparison with β=20, μ=0.01, ν=0.005), now with an additional investor trait demand d∈[0,1] representing the minimum acceptable offer (MAO). In each round, investor sees k trustees, knows j of their return rates with probability q for each. Decision rule: (1) Among known trustees (i≤j), investor chooses the one with maximal r_i if that max r_i ≥ d; (2) if no known r_i meets d and investor has trust t>0, then she randomly selects among unknown trustees (i>j) with probability t/(k−j) each; (3) otherwise, investor does not invest and keeps 1. Trustee returns r·b and keeps (1−r)·b. Traits t, d, r evolve via the same pairwise comparison process, allowing co-evolution of trust and fairness demands.",Summarizes the modifications relative to Model 1 as described in Section 4.1.,1
10.1007_s13235-017-0230-x.md,Model 3 – Evolving minimum acceptable offer d with trust forced to zero (t = 0),Section 5 describes a model where trust is fixed at t=0 to test whether rejecting profitable offers can evolve without trust.,1,Information transparency q and number of potential partners k; investor demand d and trustee return rate r evolve while trust t is fixed at 0.,q and k are exogenous parameters; d and r evolve under the constraint t=0.,1,False,Again an evolutionary simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,"Model variant within the simulation framework, not a human experiment.",1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,Agents with traits d and r evolve via simulated evolutionary dynamics; t is fixed to 0.,1,False,No closed-form derivations; uses numerical simulations.,1,1000,"Based on continuity with prior models: Ni=500 investors and Nt=500 trustees remain unless changed, and no change is stated in Section 5.",0.9,500,Uses same evolutionary schedule as previous models (x=500 per generation).,0.9,0,Interaction structure remains a 0/1 investment choice.,0.9,N/A,Not a contribution game with humans.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No communication or human subjects.,1,0,"As before, only some individual r values are known; no aggregate feedback.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,0,No modeled knowledge of horizon length.,0.9,0,"As in Model 2, rejecting profitable offers is interpreted conceptually as punishment but there is no parameterized punishment mechanism.",0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment action.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment action.,1,0,No explicit costly reward decision stage.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward action.,1,1,Same trust game structure retained.,0.95,N/R,Outcomes reported: evolved MAO d and payoff comparisons; no contribution-rate DV.,1,N/R,Binary investment decision not tabulated as a DV.,1,N/R,"Paper compares average payoffs for evolved vs fixed d under t=0, but not efficiency metrics.",1,N/R,No total group payoff is provided.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Same underlying trust game and evolutionary parameters as Model 2, but investor trust is fixed at t=0 for all investors. Decision rule: If any known trustee has return rate r_i≥d, investor selects the one with maximal r_i among known trustees (i≤j). For unknown trustees (i>j), probability of selection is 0; if no known r_i meets d, investor keeps the 1 unit and does not invest. Demand d and trustee return rate r evolve via the same pairwise comparison process; simulations show that d rarely rises above 1/b and that rejecting profitable offers is not adaptive without trust.","Summarizes constraints and changes described in Section 5, including the altered selection probabilities.",1
10.1007_s13235-017-0230-x.md,Sensitivity analysis – Extended k up to 15 (baseline Model 1/2 structure),Appendix A extends the number of potential partners k up to 15 to test robustness; underlying game and evolutionary setup remain as in Models 1 and 2.,1,"Number of partners k extended from 1–15 and information transparency q; investor trust t, demand d (in one set), and trustee return rate r evolve.","Appendix A explicitly varies k beyond 5 while scanning over q; it reports trust t, return r, MAO d, and payoff differences.",1,False,Still an agent-based evolutionary simulation.,1,False,"Simulation robustness check, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab/field setting.,1,True,Same simulation framework with extended parameter range for k.,1,False,Results come from numerical runs across parameter grid.,1,1000,Appendix A does not alter Ni=Nt=500 from the main text.,0.95,500,Evolutionary schedule retained from main models.,0.9,0,Underlying game unchanged: binary invest/keep decision.,0.9,N/A,No human contributions.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No communication and no humans.,1,0,Information structure (individual r known with probability q) is unchanged; no summaries.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,0,Agents are not modelled as observing game horizon.,0.9,0,No separate punishment mechanism introduced in the sensitivity analysis.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Underlying trust game unchanged.,0.95,N/R,"Reported outputs include t, r, d, and payoff differences; no contribution-rate measure.",1,N/R,Binary investment; not reported as a DV.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,Only payoff differences at the investor level are reported.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Computational robustness analysis.,1,"Appendix A extends the number of potential partners per round to k∈{1,…,15}. Two sets of simulations: (1) MAO d fixed at 1/b, evolving t and r (trust and return rates); (2) MAO d allowed to evolve alongside t and r. For each (q,k) pair, they run 5 independent runs, averaging over the last 400 generations. Results show that as k increases, trustees’ return rates r increase due to competition; trust t becomes unnecessary (drifts) when information is high and many partners are available; and demanding fair offers (d>1/b) remains adaptive only when trust is under selection, with partial information and partner choice.",Summarizes the extended-parameter sensitivity experiment described in Appendix A.,1
10.1007_s13235-017-0230-x.md,Sensitivity analysis – Larger populations Ni=Nt=5000,Appendix B modifies population sizes to 5000 investors and 5000 trustees while keeping other aspects of the model the same.,1,"Information transparency q and number of potential partners k; population sizes Ni and Nt increased to 5000; t, d, r evolve in respective runs.",Appendix B states the change in population size and repeats the evolving vs fixed MAO setups across q and k.,1,False,Still a simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,"Robustness simulation, not empirical.",1,False,No lab component.,1,True,Agent-based evolutionary simulation with altered Ni and Nt.,1,False,Results are numerical.,1,10000,"Appendix B sets Ni=Nt=5000, total agents=10000.",1,500,Appendix B does not modify x=500 rounds per generation.,0.9,0,Same trust-game 0/1 investment choice.,0.9,N/A,No human contributions.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,Information structure unchanged: agents can know individual r with probability q.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,0,No modeled horizon awareness.,0.9,0,No explicit punishment mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same trust game.,0.95,N/R,"As before, no contribution-rate DV.",1,N/R,Binary investments not reported as DVs.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported mirror main models (t, r, d, payoff differences).",1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff reported.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Computational test of scaling population size.,1,"Appendix B repeats two simulation setups (evolving d vs fixed d=1/b) with Ni=Nt increased to 5000 while keeping b=3, x=500, g=500, β=20, μ=0.01, ν=0.005, and the same trust-game decision rules. Results (Fig. 6) show essentially identical patterns for trust t, return rate r, MAO d, and payoff differences as in the main text, indicating robustness to larger populations.",Summarizes the robustness manipulation and main conclusions.,1
10.1007_s13235-017-0230-x.md,"Sensitivity analysis – Varying investment multiplier b (2,4,5)","Appendix C varies the investment multiplier b to 2, 4, and 5, examining how trust and MAO co-evolution depend on b.",1,"Investment multiplier b ∈ {2,4,5}, information transparency q, and number of partners k; investor trust t, demand d, and trustee return rate r evolve in the evolving-MAO runs.","Appendix C describes rerunning the two main models (evolving vs fixed d) for different values of b, across the usual q,k grids.",1,False,All results from simulations.,1,False,"The 'tests' are parameter sweeps, not human experiments.",1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Same evolutionary simulation with different b values.,1,False,Results from numerical experiments.,1,1000,Appendix C keeps Ni=Nt=500 as in the main text.,0.95,500,Evolutionary schedule unchanged.,0.9,0,Same 0/1 invest structure.,0.9,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,Information is via individual known r values; no summaries.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,0,No modeled horizon knowledge.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism beyond partner choice.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,1,"Investors still start with one unit, though it is now multiplied by various b values upon investment.",0.95,N/R,"Appendix C focuses on MAO d, payoff differences, and comparisons across b; no contribution rate DV.",1,N/R,Binary investments not quantified as a DV.,1,N/R,"They compute 'fraction of maximum reward b' as an investor-level payoff measure, but not group efficiency.",1,N/R,No aggregate group payoffs reported.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,No human,Computational parameter sweep.,1,"Appendix C reruns both the evolving-MAO and fixed-MAO (d=1/b) simulations for b=2,4,5. For each b, they compute average evolved MAO d and the fractional payoff difference between evolving and fixed MAO cases across q and k. Black regions in payoff-difference plots indicate identical fraction of maximum reward across b values when MAO evolves. They find that as b increases, (1) the parameter region where blind trust and high MAO (d>1/b) are jointly adaptive expands, and (2) the relative payoff advantage of these strategies over d=1/b increases, even though the fraction of maximum reward achieved when both evolve remains similar across b.",Summarizes Appendix C’s manipulation and qualitative findings.,1
10.1007_s13235-017-0230-x.md,Sensitivity analysis – Varying selection intensity β,Appendix D varies the selection intensity parameter β to test robustness of co-evolutionary results.,1,"Selection intensity β ∈ {10,5,1,0.5}; also information transparency q and number of partners k; traits t, d, r evolve (in evolving-MAO vs fixed-MAO comparisons).","Appendix D explicitly states testing β=10,5,1,0.5 and shows payoff-difference heatmaps similar to earlier figures.",1,False,Simulated evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,No human experiment.,1,False,Not lab-based.,1,True,Same agent-based model with different β settings.,1,False,Uses numerical simulation.,1,1000,No change to Ni=Nt=500 mentioned in Appendix D.,0.95,500,Number of rounds per generation remains 500.,0.9,0,Same invest/keep decision.,0.9,N/A,No human contributions.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,Information via individual r only.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,0,No modeled knowledge of horizon.,0.9,0,Underlying model has no explicit punishment mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,1,Base game unchanged.,0.95,N/R,"DV is payoff difference between evolving and fixed MAO cases, not contribution rate.",1,N/R,Binary investments not aggregated as DV.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure.,1,N/R,Group-level payoff not reported.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,N/A,Simulation.,1,No human,Parameter-sweep simulation.,1,"Appendix D modifies the selection intensity β in the logistic imitation rule ρ=1/(1+e^{−β(πa−πb)}), considering β=10,5,1,0.5 (baseline is 20). For each β, they compare investors’ average fractional payoff when demand d evolves vs when d is fixed at 1/b, across q and k. They illustrate that blind trust and high MAOs still co-evolve and confer advantages even with relatively weak selection (β down to 1), though payoff differences weaken as β decreases. At β=0.5, the advantage is small but still present in some regions.",Summarizes Appendix D’s manipulation and main finding on robustness to weaker selection.,1
