10.1140_epjb_e2018-90042-8.md,"Model 1 – Regular lattice PGG with heterogeneous defectors, r=1.4",Original model; this row for r=1.4 from Figs. 1 and 2.,0.9,"Network: 100x100 regular lattice (Von Neumann, degree 4), MPCR r=1.4, no punishment, initial cooperators 50%, defectors 50%, ξ ~ Normal[0,1]",Condition-specific r and shared parameters.,0.9,False,Simulations only.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Described as Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,Outcomes are simulated.,0.9,10000,100×100 lattice.,1,10000,They run 10000 steps.,1,1,Non-binary contributions.,1,0,Endowment initially private.,0.8,N/R,MPCR not numerically specified as r/N per condition.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,0,Only payoffs used for updates.,0.8,N/A,No punishment in Model 1.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Round information not modeled as observable.,0.8,0,Punishment is added only in Model 2.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,c=1.,0.8,1,"Fig. 1: when r=1.4, all players contribute and resources reach maximum; assume full cooperation.",0.8,N/R,No numeric total given.,0.2,1,Full cooperation yields maximal efficiency.,0.8,N/R,Group payoff not numerically reported.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Same model as other Model 1 conditions. At r=1.4, heterogeneous defectors convert to cooperators very quickly and all agents become cooperators, making the public pool resources maximal and stable.",From qualitative description around Fig. 2.,1
10.1140_epjb_e2018-90042-8.md,"Model 2 – Lattice with punishment, r=1.2, s=2p, p=0.2","Model 2 introduces punishment; this condition corresponds to case s=2p and p=0.2 in Fig. 3a,3d.",0.9,"Network: 100x100 regular lattice (Von Neumann, degree 4); MPCR r=1.2; punishment enabled with altruistic cooperators fraction ≈10%; punishment cost p=0.2; defectors’ loss s=0.4 (s=2p); ξ ~ Normal[0,1]",Combines common Model 2 settings with specific p and s values.,0.9,False,Agent-based simulation.,1,False,No human or field experiment.,1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,They state many simulations and Monte Carlo steps.,1,False,"Outcomes from simulation, not solved analytically.",0.9,10000,Uses same 100×100 lattice as Model 1 (not changed in Sec. 2.2).,0.9,10000,They carry out evolutionary processes by 10000 steps and repeat 10 times.,1,1,"Contributions: cooperators donate 1, heterogeneous defectors donate λ(ξ) with 0<λ(ξ)<1, defectors 0.",1,0,Each round agents decide donation from their unit endowment.,0.8,N/R,"r=1.2 provided, but MPCR as r/N not explicitly reported.",0.4,N/A,No humans involved.,1,0,Agents rely on local payoffs; no global displays.,0.8,0,Punishment is algorithmic; no notion of identity revelation is mentioned.,0.8,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Agents not modeled as knowing game horizon.,0.8,1,Section 2.2 introduces punishment by altruistic cooperators toward defectors.,1,0.2,Punishment cost to AC is p·ξ; here p=0.2 so cost per unit ξ is 0.2.,1,0.4,Defectors’ loss is s·ξ; condition specifies s=2p=0.4 so loss per unit ξ is 0.4.,1,0,Only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,c=1 as basic contribution unit.,0.9,N/R,They provide only qualitative evolution and a generic sum-of-donations trace; no numeric final fraction or normalized rate given by condition.,0.2,N/R,Fig. 3d shows qualitative curves without numeric values.,0.2,N/R,No explicit payoff or efficiency calculation reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically given.,0.1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Purely computational experiments.,1,"Model 2 builds on Model 1 by adding altruistic cooperators (AC) who are a subset of cooperators. Initially cooperators (including AC) and defectors are each 50%, with AC about 10% of all players. Cooperators (including AC) contribute 1; heterogeneous defectors contribute λ(ξ) = (1−ξ^2); complete defectors contribute 0. Each AC randomly supervises one group member; if that member is a defector, AC pays punishment cost p·ξ and defector loses s·ξ with effective probabilities derived in Eq. (π_A C, π_D). Strategy update via Fermi rule with k=1 as in Model 1. For s=2p and p=0.2, equilibrium shows coexistence of heterogeneous defectors and altruistic cooperators; all cooperators become altruistic and complete defectors vanish, with heterogeneous defectors as main donors.",Summarizes Sec. 2.2 and 3.2.1 for this parameter set.,1
