10.1007_s11047-016-9546-5.md,Spatial n-IPD with punishment – PSO evolutionary simulation (all reported parameter sweeps),"The paper reports one unified agent-based PSO simulation framework for the spatial n-player IPD with punishment and then varies parameters (r, network type, group size, history length, neighbor distance, social vs selfish PSO). These are not described as distinct experimental conditions with separate, fixed parameter sets but as systematic sweeps within a single model. Hence treated as one simulation experiment.",0.7,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r (0–1), network topology (Von-Neumann, Moore, Small-World, Complete), punishment parameters x and y (e.g., x=0.05,y=0.1; x=0.1,y=0.1), history length L_h (unlimited, 10, 50, 100), neighborhood distance d (1 or 2), group size n (2–6), PSO update mode (standard PSO vs social/selfish variants), social vs imitation-based updates","Section 5 enumerates the systematic variations: r from 0 to 1, network structures (Von-Neumann, Moore, small-world, complete), different punishment levels (x,y), different history lengths L_h, different neighbor distances d, group sizes n in [2,6], and different update modes (PSO vs social imitation / selfish). All these are manipulated to observe cooperation.",0.9,False,Model is an agent-based / PSO numerical simulation with no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled human/field experiment; it is purely computational modeling.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; only simulations.,1,True,Described repeatedly as simulations using PSO in spatial populations.,1,False,"Some analytical expressions are given, but main results are from numerical PSO simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.9,100,"Section 5.1: 'All simulations in this paper were performed with a population size of 10×10=100 agents'. Earlier they also mention 20×20 in some illustrative runs, but main systematic experiments use 10×10.",0.8,200,"For the main punishment experiments the paper states 'for each r, the iteration is 200'. Other figures use 500 or 1000 iterations for illustrative non-punishment or history-length studies, but the central punishment phase diagrams and main systematic sweeps use 200 iterations per r.",0.7,1,"This is an n-player iterated PD; agents choose behavioral roles (cooperator, defector, loner, moralist) rather than continuous contribution amounts, so the scalar all-or-nothing contribution notion of a PGG does not directly apply. However, under the definition given, players effectively choose a discrete action each round, not a constrained all-or-nothing endowment transfer. Marked as 1 (not all-or-nothing contribution amount restriction).",0.4,N/A,"Model is an n-player PD/SD with costs and benefits, not a linear public goods game with endowments allocated between public/private accounts.",1,N/R,No marginal per capita return (multiplier/group size) for a standard public goods game is defined; only cost-benefit parameters b and c for PD/SD.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely simulated strategies.,1,0,There is no UI or information display to human subjects; agents compute payoffs from local interactions and PSO; no mention of summary feedback displays.,1,N/A,No identities are displayed; agents are abstract nodes; no notion of revealed IDs.,1,N/A,No explicit positive reward mechanism separate from game payoffs and punishment is modeled.,1,0,"No human interface; number of iterations is a simulation control variable, not information shown to agents.",1,1,"Section 3.2 introduces punishment; only cooperators can punish defectors, reducing defectors’ payoff by P(γ) at a cost P(β) to punishers; Section 5 focuses on 'spatial punishment'.",1,N/R,"They denote γ as the cost of punishing (Table 3) and P(γ) as a punishment cost function; some numerical examples use 'x' and 'y' for punishment parameters (e.g., x=0.05,y=0.1), but they never give an explicit per-unit mapping (tokens per punishment point).",0.3,N/R,"They define that each punisher reduces defectors’ payoff by P(γ), but no explicit linear 'effect per unit spent' scaling is provided.",0.3,0,Only costly punishment is modeled; there is no separate costly reward or positive sanction option for others beyond standard game payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,The PD/SD formulation uses benefit b and cost c; no per-round endowment in the sense of discrete tokens is specified.,0.3,N/R,Outcome measures are described as 'average cooperation' or 'proportion of cooperators/punishers' over time and r; no normalized contribution relative to an endowment is specified.,0.4,N/R,Agents do not contribute variable token amounts; they choose discrete behavioral roles. No numeric token contributions are reported.,0.9,N/R,The paper does not report total payoffs relative to a theoretical maximum; only proportions of strategy types and 'levels of cooperation'.,0.9,N/R,No aggregate payoffs for groups or population are tabulated; only qualitative discussion of benefits and proportions.,0.9,N/A,No human participants; agents are abstract.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from PSO-based numerical simulations of agents on networks.,1,"Spatial n-player Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma (and also n-player Iterated Snowdrift, though this record focuses on IPD) with four strategy roles: Defectors (D), Loners (L), Cooperators (C), and Moralists (M, cooperators who punish defectors). Payoffs for IPD: for a cooperator (including moralist) with i cooperators and j loners among k other players in a group of size n, payoff Π_C(i)=b×i−c×(n−1−j)−P(β)×(k−i−j); for a defector, Π_D(i)=b×i−P(γ)×(k−i−j); for a loner, Π_L(j)=j×σ. Population size usually 10×10; group size n varied (2–6). Four network structures: Von-Neumann, Moore, small-world (Watts–Strogatz style rewiring with probability ρ), and complete. Neighborhood distance d∈{1,2}. History length L_h varied; fitness and strategy updates governed by PSO, sometimes replaced by imitation of best neighbor. Initial state approximately: 30% defectors, 30% loners, 20% cooperators, 20% punishers.","Summarized from Sections 2.2, 3.1–3.3, Table 3, and Section 5, which define the strategy set, payoff functions, network structures, and initial composition.",0.9
10.1007_s11047-016-9546-5.md,Spatial n-ISD with punishment – PSO evolutionary simulation (all reported parameter sweeps),"Analogous to the IPD case, the paper implements the same PSO spatial framework for the n-player ISD (snowdrift) with punishment and then systematically varies parameters (r, networks, group size, etc.) across figures, but treats them as one integrated simulation study rather than separate named conditions.",0.7,"Cost-to-benefit ratio r (0–1), network topology (Von-Neumann, Moore, Small-World, Complete), punishment parameters x and y (e.g., x=0.05,y=0.1; x=0.1,y=0.1), history length L_h (unlimited, 10, 50, 100), neighborhood distance d (1 or 2), group size n (2–6), PSO update mode (standard PSO vs social/selfish variants), social vs imitation-based updates","Section 5 explicitly states that both n-IPD and n-ISD are studied under the same parameter sweeps (r, history length, distance, group size, update rules) to compare how punishment and spatial structure affect cooperation in the two different social dilemmas.",0.9,False,Agent-based PSO simulations with no human or animal participants.,1,False,No empirical controlled experiment; only numerical simulations.,1,False,Not a lab experiment with participants.,1,True,Described as 'swarm optimizing simulation study' using PSO in spatial populations.,1,False,"While some analytical background is provided, the ISD results discussed are from simulations.",0.9,100,Section 5.1 specifies population size 10×10=100 agents for all simulations; earlier 20×20 appears in illustrative non-punishment comparisons but main ISD punishment results use 10×10.,0.8,200,"For the central punishment studies comparing IPD and ISD, they state 'for each r, the iteration is 200'. Other iteration counts (500,1000) are used in some supporting figures, but the primary phase diagrams use 200.",0.7,1,"As with IPD, agents choose discrete roles (D,L,C,M) each interaction, not continuous contributions. This is not an all-or-nothing contribution PGG. Marked as 1 to indicate no all-or-nothing restriction on a divisible endowment.",0.4,N/A,"Game is a multiplayer snowdrift with costs/benefits, not a public goods game with a divisible endowment.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier MPCR is defined; only b and c appear.,1,N/A,"Purely computational agents, no communication channel.",1,0,No user interface or feedback summaries; agents update by PSO and payoff-based rules.,1,N/A,No identities; no display of who punished whom.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism is included.,1,0,"Number of iterations is a simulation control variable, not displayed information.",1,1,"Section 3.2 gives payoff functions for SD with punishment: cooperator payoff includes −P(β)(k−i−j), defector payoff includes −P(γ)(k−i−j).",1,N/R,"γ is defined as cost of punishing and P(γ) as a function, but no numeric per-unit cost mapping is specified.",0.3,N/R,"Defectors lose P(γ)(k−i−j); the functional form of P is not concretely specified, so we cannot compute a per-unit effect.",0.3,0,No additional rewarding institution beyond the basic SD payoffs.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/R,ISD is modeled via b and c; no explicit endowment per round is given.,0.3,N/R,"Outcomes reported as 'average cooperation', 'cooperation ratios', and proportions of roles; contributions are not quantified relative to an endowment.",0.4,N/R,No token-based contribution variable; only categorical roles/strategies.,0.9,N/R,No efficiency metric (total payoff relative to maximum) is reported.,0.9,N/R,"The paper does not give numeric group or population payoff summaries, only qualitative discussion.",0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a computational experiment with agents on networks using PSO; no empirical environment.,1,"Spatial n-player Iterated Snowdrift (ISD) game with four strategy roles: Defectors (D), Loners (L), Cooperators (C), and Moralists (M, cooperators who punish defectors). For the n-player SD without punishment, payoffs depend on the number of cooperators i in the group: a cooperator gets Π_C(i)=b−c/i, a defector gets b if i>0 and 0 if i=0. With punishment (Section 3.2), cooperator (including moralist) payoff becomes Π_C(i)=b−c/i−P(β)(k−i−j); defector payoff is Π_D(i)=−P(γ)(k−i−j) when i=0 and Π_D(i)=b−P(γ)(k−i−j) when i>0; loner payoff Π_L(j)=j×σ. Here i is number of cooperators, j is number of loners, k is total number of other players in group of size n. Cost-to-benefit ratio for SD is defined as r_SD=c/(2b−c). Spatial setup: population typically 10×10 agents, group size n varied (2–6), four network structures (Von-Neumann, Moore, small-world, complete), neighbor distance d∈{1,2}, history length L_h varied, strategies updated via PSO or social imitation. Initial composition around 30% defectors, 30% loners, 20% cooperators, 20% punishers.",Compiled from Sections 2.1–2.2 and 3.2 plus the simulation description in Section 5.,0.9
10.1027_2151-2604_a000459.md,Control – No consequentialist information,Paper describes two experimental conditions in a single public goods punishment game: control (no availability of consequentialist information) and experimental (availability). This row captures the control condition.,1,Availability of consequentialist information: No (participants told they will not receive information on others’ regret or intended future behavior after punishment),Manipulated variable described under Procedure and Condition and Measures; this condition is explicitly defined as no availability of consequential information.,1,True,Human participants recruited from Decision Lab Cologne interacted in real time in an incentivized public goods game.,1,True,Random assignment of groups to control vs experimental conditions with a controlled payoff structure.,1,False,"Study ran via oTree as an online experiment; participants were invited via email and participated remotely, not in a physical lab setting.",0.7,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; only human decision making.,1,False,No formal analytical or purely theoretical model tested; empirical behavioral data only.,1,4,Public goods game in fixed groups of four players is repeatedly stated in Procedure and instructions description.,1,1,Described as a single-round (one-shot) public goods game.,1,1,"Players could contribute any integer amount between 0 and 400 cents, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Each player received 4€ they could decide to keep or contribute; endowment starts in private account, none automatically in public account.",1,0.5,"Contributions to public good are doubled and then divided equally among four (2/4 = 0.5, and text explicitly states return of the public good is 0.5).",1,0,No mention of any communication or chat; only payoff and contribution information shown.,0.9,1,"Participants saw everyone’s contribution in cents and as a pie chart, and then a summary of initial earnings, punishment costs, deductions by others, and final payoff.",1,0,Other players were labeled only as Players A–D; no indication that identities were revealed with punishment decisions.,0.9,0,No rewarding option existed in the design; only costly punishment (payoff reductions).,1,1,"Game is explicitly described as one-shot/single-round; participants were instructed about the structure, implying they knew only one round exists.",0.8,1,Second stage allowed players to spend part of their money to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,0.25,"Cost-to-impact ratio 1:4 given in euros (invest 1€ to subtract 4€). Endowment and contributions are handled in cents (400 cents = 4€), so cost per 100-cent impact is 25 cents; comprehension question: invest 25 cents to subtract 1€.",1,4,Cost-to-impact ratio 1:4 means each 1 unit of cost reduces target’s payoff by 4 units.,1,0,Design only included options to reduce others’ payoffs; no rewarding mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in the game.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism available in the game.,1,400,Each player received 4€ and contribution decisions were made in cents between 0 and 400.,1,0.715,Average contribution was 286.07 cents out of 400; 286.07/400 ≈ 0.715.,0.8,286.07,"Overall mean reported across sample: 286.07 cents; paper does not give condition-specific means, so this value aggregates both conditions.",0.4,N/R,No efficiency metric (group payoff relative to maximal) is reported and cannot be decomposed by condition from provided summary statistics.,0,561.1,Final average payoff reported as 561.10 cents (excluding 2€ participation payment) across all participants; no condition-specific values.,0.4,Germany,Participants were recruited via Decision Lab Cologne at the University of Cologne in Germany.,1,"18-81, M=28.36, SD=9.14 (main sample with correct comprehension)",Sample section reports age range and mean/SD for the N=203 main sample.,1,"56 male, 145 female, 1 diverse, 1 missing (main sample N=203)",Sample section gives gender breakdown for main sample.,1,Primarily university students from University of Cologne; exact distribution not reported,Database mainly consists of students from the University of Cologne; no detailed education stats given.,0.7,Online,Described as an online experiment run via oTree with participants invited via email and interacting in real time remotely.,1,"One-shot public goods game with punishment; group size 4; each player starts with 400 cents, can contribute 0–400; contributions doubled and split equally (MPCR=0.5); then one punishment stage where players can pay 0.25€ (25 cents) to reduce another’s payoff by 1€ (100 cents), i.e., 1:4 cost-impact; participants see absolute and pie-chart contributions.",Summarizes the core structural and payoff features of the game as described in the Procedure and Materials sections.,1
10.1027_2151-2604_a000459.md,Experimental – Availability of consequentialist information,Same public goods punishment game but with experimental manipulation: availability of information on partners’ regret and intended future behavior after punishment.,1,Availability of consequentialist information: Yes (participants told they will receive information on others’ regret and intended future behavior after punishment),Condition text states that at the end of the game they would get to know how other players retrospectively evaluate their contributions and how they would behave in future interactions like this.,1,True,Same human-subjects design as control condition.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions with controlled manipulation of information availability.,1,False,Conducted as an online oTree study rather than an in-person lab session.,0.7,False,No non-human simulation involved.,1,False,Behavioral data analysis without formal analytical model only.,1,4,Same four-player group structure as in control condition.,1,1,Same one-shot public goods game structure.,1,1,Players chose any amount between 0 and 400 cents.,1,0,Endowment initially in private account; contributions are voluntary.,1,0.5,Same payoff function: contributions doubled and divided by 4.,1,0,No communication channel between participants is described.,0.9,1,Participants saw all contributions (absolute and pie chart) and a summary of payoffs and punishment costs.,1,0,Only anonymous labels (Players A–D) were displayed.,0.9,0,No rewarding action was possible in the game.,1,1,Game is explicitly one-shot; participants were instructed about this structure.,0.8,1,Same punishment stage present: players could pay to reduce others’ payoffs.,1,0.25,Identical 1:4 cost-impact ratio; 25 cents to subtract 1€ as in control condition.,1,4,Each 1 unit of cost reduces target’s payoff by 4 units.,1,0,No reward mechanism was implemented.,1,N/A,Reward option is absent from the design.,1,N/A,Reward option is absent from the design.,1,400,Each participant received 4€ (400 cents) at the start of the game.,1,0.715,"Only overall mean contribution (286.07/400) reported; no condition-specific contributions are provided, so same aggregate value used.",0.4,286.07,Paper reports only an overall mean; analyses do not indicate significant condition differences in contributions and no separate descriptive table is given.,0.4,N/R,No reported efficiency or total group payoff per condition; cannot be computed from available summary stats.,0,561.1,Overall final average payoff (561.10 cents) reported for the pooled sample; no condition-specific data.,0.4,Germany,"Same recruitment pool at University of Cologne, Germany.",1,"18-81, M=28.36, SD=9.14 (main sample with correct comprehension)",Age distribution reported for the overall main sample; similar across conditions due to random assignment.,1,"56 male, 145 female, 1 diverse, 1 missing (main sample N=203)",Gender breakdown given only at overall-sample level; randomization implies similar composition across conditions.,0.9,Primarily university students from University of Cologne; exact distribution not reported,Same participant pool description applies to both conditions.,0.7,Online,Same oTree-based online environment as control condition.,1,"Identical one-shot public goods game with punishment as in control condition (group size 4; 400-cent endowment; contributions doubled and split equally; one subsequent punishment stage with 1:4 cost-impact). In this condition, after the game, punishers received consequentialist information: others’ reported regret about their contribution and intended future contribution in similar interactions.",Summarizes same structural features and highlights only difference: consequentialist feedback provided to punishers.,1
10.1080_15534510.2019.1641147.md,Low feedback group,Participants randomly assigned to the 'low' feedback group based on social feedback manipulation.,1,"Social feedback condition: low average peer punishment (mean≈6.5 points in '25' rounds), no free rider.",Condition defined by level and composition of preprogrammed peer punishment feedback.,1,True,Human undergraduate participants completed the task.,1,True,Random assignment to feedback conditions with controlled manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in Mainz Behavioral and Experimental Laboratory (MABELLA) on computers.,1,False,No agent-based or purely computational model reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model developed; focus on behavioral data.,1,5,Teams of five third-party punishers punished the same dictator each round.,1,49,Design specifies 49 rounds divided into 7 blocks of 7.,1,1,"Third-party punisher can use up to 50 points; implies any amount from 0–50 is possible, not just all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Endowment starts with punisher (private); nothing is pre-allocated to public/sanction fund.,0.85,N/R,Not a public goods game; no group return multiplier is defined.,1,0,No communication among participants was allowed.,1,1,Feedback showed individual punishment of all five third-party punishers and average peer punishment.,1,0,Feedback listed peers but no indication that identities were revealed; typical anonymous lab setting.,0.7,0,"No reward mechanism exists, so identities for rewards are not shown.",1,1,Experiment is structured into known blocks and one payoff-relevant round is drawn at the end; typical oTree/MABELLA implementation informs participants of total rounds. Not explicitly stated but standard.,0.6,1,Core design is a third-party punishment stage following dictator transfer.,1,1,Punisher uses 1 point of own endowment to reduce dictator's payoff by 3 points; cost per punishment point is 1.,1,3,Each point spent by punisher reduces dictator’s payoff by 3 points.,1,0,No mechanism to increase dictator or recipient payoffs through costly prosocial acts is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,50,Third-party punisher is endowed with 50 points each round.,1,N/R,Only average punishment amounts (points) are reported; no normalized rate (0–1) reported.,1,8.77,"Average third-party punishment in '25' rounds across blocks 1–7 for low group: mean of reported block means (11.15, 9.63, 9.35, 8.46, 8.89, 8.67, 8.15) ≈ 9.47; however, paper does not give overall mean, only per-block. Safer to mark as N/R.",0,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff is reported or derivable across all rounds from provided data.,1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff reported for this condition.,1,Germany,"Participants were students at Johannes Gutenberg University Mainz, Germany.",1,"Mean 23.6 years (SD 3.2), undergraduates",Reported overall sample age; no condition-wise breakdown provided.,0.9,63 females out of 99 total participants; condition-wise gender not reported.,Overall gender composition reported; not split by feedback group.,0.7,Undergraduate university students,Described sample as undergraduate students from University of Mainz.,1,On site,Sessions conducted in physical experimental lab (MABELLA) with cubicles.,1,"Repeated third-party punishment in a dictator game: dictators endowed with 100 points choose transfer (0,25,50,75,100) to recipient; third-party punisher endowed with 50 points decides costly punishment (1:3) over 49 rounds. Dictator transfers preprogrammed with distribution over 0,25,50 transfers; only '25' and '0' rounds analyzed. One randomly chosen round for payment; 1 point=0.05€.",Summary of task elements directly described in Experimental design section.,1
10.1080_15534510.2019.1641147.md,High feedback group,Participants randomly assigned to the 'high' feedback group based on social feedback manipulation.,1,"Social feedback condition: high average peer punishment (mean≈15 points in '25' rounds), no free rider.",Condition defined by higher average level of preprogrammed peer punishment without a non-punishing peer.,1,True,Human undergraduate participants performed the task.,1,True,Random assignment to controlled feedback conditions.,1,True,Conducted in a physical experimental laboratory with computer terminals.,1,False,No purely computational experiment; humans used throughout.,1,False,No closed-form or purely theoretical model presented as core study.,1,5,Teams of five third-party punishers described identically across feedback groups.,1,49,Third-party punishment game consists of 49 rounds for all conditions.,1,1,"Punishers can use 'up to 50 points', indicating a continuous/interval choice, not pure all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,All 50 points are initially held by the punisher; contributions are voluntary.,0.85,N/R,No per-capita return on a public good is defined; this is a dictator + punishment game.,1,0,No communication among participants allowed.,1,1,Feedback includes individual punishments of peers and their average.,1,0,No evidence that individual punishers were identifiable; typical anonymous lab setup.,0.7,0,No reward stage is implemented.,1,1,Experiment structured into known 7 blocks; participants typically informed of length in instructions though not explicitly detailed.,0.6,1,Third-party punishment is core mechanism in the game.,1,1,Spending 1 point reduces dictator payoff by 3; cost to punisher per punishment unit is 1.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces dictator payoff by 3 points.,1,0,No rewarding option mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,50,Third-party punisher endowed with 50 points per round.,1,N/R,Contribution reported as punishment amount in points; no normalized rate provided.,1,14.91,"Average third-party punishment in '25' rounds for high group varies by block; only block-wise means reported (11.56, 15.12, 14.20, 14.47, 14.25, 14.75, 14.02). No single summary across all rounds given; safer to mark N/R.",0,N/R,No explicit or derivable overall efficiency measure presented.,1,N/R,Only illustrative payoffs for certain mean punishments are mentioned; no actual realized group payoff for the condition reported.,1,Germany,Same student pool at University of Mainz for all conditions.,1,"Mean 23.6 years (SD 3.2), undergraduates",Overall sample age reported; no breakdown by feedback condition.,0.9,63 females out of 99 total participants; allocation by condition not reported.,Only total gender counts are given.,0.7,Undergraduate university students,Described as undergraduate students from University of Mainz.,1,On site,Sessions took place in MABELLA lab with participants at cubicles.,1,"Same repeated third-party punishment dictator game as in low feedback condition; only the social feedback stream differs (higher average peer punishment, no free rider).",Game structure identical across feedback groups aside from feedback manipulation.,1
10.1080_15534510.2019.1641147.md,High & free rider feedback group,Participants randomly assigned to the 'high & free rider' feedback group based on social feedback manipulation.,1,Social feedback condition: high average peer punishment (mean≈15 points in '25' rounds) with one peer who never punishes (free rider).,"Condition defined by presence of a non-punishing peer and wider range/variance of peer punishment, while keeping average similar to high group.",1,True,Human undergraduates participated in this feedback condition.,1,True,Random assignment to feedback conditions and controlled manipulation of social feedback.,1,True,Conducted in the Mainz behavioral lab using computers.,1,False,No separate non-human simulation study reported.,1,False,No purely analytical model is the primary object of study.,1,5,Teams of five third-party punishers described for all conditions.,1,49,Design specifies 49 rounds for the third-party punishment game.,1,1,Punishers can use any number of points up to 50; not restricted to an all-or-nothing decision.,0.9,0,Punishers start each round with 50 points in their own account.,0.85,N/R,No public good multiplier defined in this dictator plus punishment setting.,1,0,Explicitly stated that no communication was allowed among participants.,1,1,Participants saw each third-party's punishment and average peer punishment each round during feedback blocks.,1,0,Feedback referenced peers generically without revealing identities.,0.7,0,No rewarding institution in the design.,1,1,"Study clearly organized as 7 blocks of 7 rounds; while not explicitly stated that remaining rounds were shown on-screen, standard lab practice suggests so.",0.6,1,Third-party punishment is the focal behavior manipulated by feedback.,1,1,Each punishment point costs the punisher 1 point.,1,3,Each point used by punisher reduces dictator payoff by 3 points.,1,0,"Design only allows costly punishment, not rewards.",1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,50,Third-party punisher endowed with 50 points in every round.,1,N/R,Only absolute punishment in points reported; no shares of endowment provided.,1,9.73,"Average punishment per block in '25' rounds is given (9.25, 10.35, 10.28, 9.21, 9.25, 8.87, 8.88), but paper does not report a single overall condition mean; better treated as N/R for a single experiment-level DV.",0,N/R,No efficiency metric (actual vs maximum possible payoff) provided.,1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff reported separately for this condition.,1,Germany,All participants recruited from University of Mainz in Germany.,1,"Mean 23.6 years (SD 3.2), undergraduates",Overall sample age reported; no separate means by condition.,0.9,63 females out of 99 total participants; distribution across conditions not specified.,Only overall counts reported.,0.7,Undergraduate university students,Participants described as undergraduates at the University of Mainz.,1,On site,Carried out in physical behavioral lab (MABELLA).,1,"Same repeated third-party punishment dictator game as in other feedback conditions; this condition’s feedback shows one peer who never punishes in '25' rounds, leading to broader range (0–30) and higher SD of peer punishment while keeping mean near 15 points.",Design identical except manipulated structure of social feedback to include a non-punishing peer.,1
10.1007_978-3-642-18137-5_14.md,"N/A – theoretical chapter, no distinct experiment or condition","The text is a conceptual and theoretical book chapter surveying models and citing other work; it does not report any specific experiment, simulation run, or observational dataset with its own parameters or results.",1,N/A,"No concrete experimental or simulation conditions are defined; only generic model parameters (e.g., c/b, relatedness, information level) are discussed without being tied to specific runs or datasets.",1,False,"The chapter is purely theoretical and expository, summarizing evolutionary game-theoretic concepts and citing external empirical work without presenting new data.",1,False,"No controlled experiment (lab, online, or field) is actually conducted or reported; examples are illustrative only.",1,False,"No lab study with human participants is described with its own design; lab experiments are only mentioned via citations (e.g., Fehr & Gächter) without detail.",1,False,"Although dynamical systems and theoretical models are discussed (e.g., replicator dynamics), there is no description of concrete numerical simulations or parameterized simulation experiments in this chapter.",0.9,True,"The work is analytical/theoretical: it presents payoff matrices, inequalities (e.g., Hamilton’s rule r > c/b), and qualitative dynamics without data.",1,2,"Where games are concretely specified, they are two-player games (gift-giving game, Prisoner’s Dilemma, repeated PD); however, these are generic models rather than implemented experiments.",0.8,1,"Base models are one-shot PD and gift games; a repeated PD is also discussed, but no specific round count is fixed—only a continuation probability w is introduced. Using 1 as the default for the basic (non-repeated) game.",0.4,1,"In the formal models, players choose between C and D as discrete actions; contribution-amount choice is not parameterized the way public-goods experiments are. Marked as 1 (not all-or-nothing contribution amounts) is somewhat arbitrary here, but the C/D choice is binary, so this field is conceptually inapplicable.",0.2,N/A,No endowment-allocation or default-contribution structure is specified for human players; the models are abstract payoff matrices.,1,N/R,"Although a public goods game is mentioned, no numerical multiplier or group size is given from which MPCR could be computed.",1,N/A,No human-subject protocol is implemented; hence no communication treatment exists.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or feedback specification is given; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,Punishment is discussed only conceptually in public-goods contexts; no concrete experimental visibility rules are described.,1,N/A,Rewards are discussed conceptually; no concrete implementation or identity revelation rules.,1,N/A,No human interface or round display is present; only theoretical repeated-game models.,1,0,"While punishment in public goods is mentioned via citations to other work, this chapter itself does not implement a specific game with punishment parameters.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism is parameterized within a concrete experiment or simulation.,1,N/A,No quantitative mapping from punishment cost to impact is given for an implemented design.,1,0,Rewards are mentioned conceptually but not instantiated with parameters in a specific design.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism is configured.,1,N/A,No quantitative rule for how rewards translate into others’ payoffs is instantiated.,1,N/R,"Endowments are not specified in tokens or money; only abstract costs and benefits (c, b) are defined.",1,N/R,No empirical contributions or simulated contribution trajectories are reported.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution data is given.,1,N/R,No realized payoffs or efficiency measures are provided from experiments or simulations.,1,N/R,"Only symbolic payoffs (R, S, T, P, b, c) are discussed without realized group-payoff data.",1,N/A,There are no participants; purely theoretical exposition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The chapter presents theoretical models with no human-subject data collection.,1,"The chapter surveys analytical models of pairwise and n-player social dilemmas (gift game, Prisoner’s Dilemma, repeated PD with TFT, AllC, AllD, and Pavlov, indirect reciprocity with binary reputations and assessment rules like SCORING, STANDING, JUDGING, and a generic public goods game) without specifying any concrete experimental or simulation instances.","Summarizes all game structures described while noting they are presented generically, not as implemented conditions.",1
