condition.,1,0,"No incentives, so no rewards.",1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent in this condition.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism is absent in this condition.,1,N/R,Model specifies maximum contribution c and costs but no explicit endowment per individual.,0,N/R,"Analytical focus is on fraction of cooperators x, not per-round contribution rate relative to endowment.",0,N/R,Only abstract parameter c and cost functions specified; no realized contributions reported.,0,N/R,No explicit normalized efficiency measure reported.,0,N/R,Expressions for individual payoffs are given; no aggregate numerical group payoff for a specific parameter set.,0,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely theoretical without empirical data.,1,"Nonlinear multi-player game combining public goods and N-player snowdrift with threshold τ; well-mixed infinite population; parameters include group size N, benefit factor r, max contribution c, and threshold τ. Without incentives, replicator dynamics show full defection for r < N.",Summarized from Sections 2 and 3.1 where the game and results without incentives are defined.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2020.126904.md,Analytical model – Consensual incentives (punishment and/or reward),Section 3.2 analyzes evolutionary dynamics when consensual incentives (punishment or reward) can be endogenously employed.,1,"Consensual incentives with parameters: punishment preference p, reward preference q, consensus threshold T, punishment cost α, punishment/reward effect β, resources threshold τ, benefit factor r, group size N","Model introduces p, q, T, α, β, τ, r, N as key parameters controlling incentive use and game payoffs under incentives.",1,False,Incentives are modeled analytically; no human data.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; purely theoretical derivations and qualitative analysis.,1,False,No lab setting; no empirical implementation.,1,False,"Although figures show phase portraits, they stem from analytical expressions; no stochastic simulation procedure described.",0.8,True,Payoffs with incentives are expressed symbolically and analyzed via replicator dynamics.,1,5,Configuration for Fig. 3 (which exemplifies consensual incentives) states N=5.,0.9,1,"As in standard replicator models, each evolutionary update is based on a one-shot group interaction.",0.7,1,"Contributions are 'not ""all-or-nothing"" but nonlinearly dependent on the number of cooperators'.",1,N/A,No human endowment allocation; only theoretical cost and benefit functions.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return not explicitly defined; only total benefit factor r and N appear.,0,N/A,No human participants; communication is not modeled.,1,0,Information about others' payoffs or incentives is not part of the analytical specification.,0.9,0,Model specifies fines and costs but not revelation of identities.,0.8,0,Model specifies rewards but not identity disclosure.,0.8,0,No notion of displayed finite horizon; continuous-time replicator dynamics.,1,1,Consensual punishment is one possible incentive; defectors can be fined depending on consensus.,1,0.3,"Parameter α=0.3 in examples (e.g., Fig. 3 caption: α=0.3) is the per-unit cost to cooperators of punishing.",0.9,1,Parameter β=1 in main examples is the per-unit fine magnitude relative to cost; use β as punishment effect.,0.8,1,Consensual reward of cooperators is explicitly modeled via β and q.,1,0.3,The same α parameter governs cost of rewarding (α(n_c−1)); α=0.3 in example settings.,0.9,1,"β appears as award per other cooperator; in examples β=1, used here as reward magnitude per unit cost.",0.8,N/R,"Individuals can contribute up to c, but no explicit initial endowment amount in tokens or money is given.",0,N/R,"Outcomes reported as basins of attraction for cooperation, not normalized contributions relative to endowment.",0,N/R,Model uses symbolic c and cost functions; no specific realized contribution levels reported.,0,N/R,"Paper discusses promotion of cooperation and basins of attraction, not explicit efficiency ratios.",0,N/R,"While payoffs are defined, no numerical group payoff values are reported for particular parameter combinations.",0,N/A,No human participants; theoretical analysis only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is part of the same analytical model with no empirical implementation.,1,"Well-mixed infinite population; N individuals interact in a nonlinear multi-player game with saturation in public benefits and threshold τ between public goods and N-player snowdrift regimes. Consensual incentives: each cooperator independently prefers punishment with probability p, reward with probability q (p+q≤1). An incentive (punishment or reward) is implemented if the fraction of cooperators favoring it exceeds consensus threshold T. Punishment: each cooperator favoring punishment pays cost α·n_d to fine each defector by β·n_c; reward: each cooperator favoring reward pays cost α·(n_c−1) to give β·(n_c−1) to each cooperator. Evolution follows replicator dynamics on fraction of cooperators x with payoffs π_C and π_D derived in Section 3.2 and Appendix A/B. Parameters in examples: r=3, c=1, α=0.3, β=1, N=5; T varied (1/2, 2/3,...); p and q varied such that p+q may equal 1 or less; τ varied between 1 and N to interpolate between N-player snowdrift and public goods games.","Summarized from model description (Section 2) and consensual incentives specification with equations and parameter choices (Figs. 2–4, Tables 1–2).",1
