hment emergence are reported.,0.3,N/R,No numerical averages of per-round contributions are presented.,0.3,N/R,No explicit measure comparing actual to maximum possible group payoff.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff levels are not summarized numerically in the results.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Entirely computational agent-based simulation.,1,"Finite, well-mixed population M = 100; agents of three types: cooperators (contribute, no punishment), defectors (no contribution, potentially punished), punishers (contribute and punish defectors). Initial composition X = 30, Y = 40, Z = 30. Contribution cost c = 1; PGG multiplier r = 3. Punishment: each punisher incurs cost γ for each defector and each defector suffers cost δ per punisher. Transforming rule for payoffs to fitness includes costly signaling efficacy α = 0.3 and λ = 5 so that altruistic punishment yields more social attention than nonpunishing contributions. Selection strength ω = 0.5; mutation rate μ = 0.001. Moran reproduction/imitation with mutation at each period.",Summarized from main methods section and specific parameter statements for Fig. 1(c) and Fig. 3(c).,0.98
