10.1016_j.jebo.2023.04.004.md,"Pool punishment (10P) – voluntary tax, first-order punishment only",Paper defines pool punishment treatment labelled 10P; this row captures that treatment as implemented.,1,"Punishment institution: pool punishment available via voluntary tax; no second-order punishment. Tax per payer: 0.5 tokens if 1 payer, 0.25 if 2, 0.15 if 3. Penalty of 1 token for harvests above collectively chosen threshold when institution is active.",10P is characterized by the option to pay a tax to establish a sanctioning institution; only over-harvesters are punished.,1,True,Human subjects in the same lab experiment; 21 groups in 10P treatment.,1,True,"Random assignment to groups, treatment-specific rules, controlled payoffs.",1,True,Conducted at University of Warsaw lab using z-Tree.,1,False,"This treatment uses human decisions, not simulated agents.",1,False,"Although a theoretical model predicts low tax payment probability, this row is empirical implementation.",1,3,Same grouping as baseline: three-person groups.,1,30,Common protocol: actual experiment lasted at most 30 periods.,1,1,Subjects could extract fractions of tokens up to 1/n of resources.,1,0,Same CPR structure: no pre-assigned contribution; payoff only from privately harvested tokens.,0.7,N/R,No linear public good multiplier defined; dynamic CPR instead.,0,0,No communication described; identical interface to baseline plus punishment stages.,0.9,1,"After harvesting, subjects observed harvests of each co-player, then voted if institution existed.",1,0,Identities of group members never revealed; feedback is anonymous and in random order.,1,0,No rewards; anonymity maintained.,1,0,Subjects were not told exact number of periods; only that total experiment under 1.5 hours.,1,1,Pool punishment institution can exist if at least one player pays tax; then over-harvesters incur a 1-token penalty.,1,1,"When active, each punished individual loses 1 token regardless of number of taxpayers; cost is 1 token per punishment to the punished player.",1,1,Each unit of punishment (institution decision) reduces punished player’s earnings by 1 token; no further multiplier.,1,0,Design only includes penalties; no reward option mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,45,Common resource stock at start fixed at 45 tokens as in baseline.,1,0.23,Table 2(a): Mean fraction of harvested resources in 10P is 0.23 (±0.17) before resource exhaustion.,1,3.45,Table 2(a): Mean harvest in 10P is 3.45 (±3.38) tokens before resource exhaustion.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0,10.86,Table 2(a) reports Total Payoffs = 10.86 (±7.67) per subject in 10P; group-level total would be higher but table does not disaggregate; we record reported value.,0.5,Poland,All treatments run at University of Warsaw.,1,Mean age 30.41 (±16.9) years,Mean age for 10P treatment given in design section.,1,63% female,Females constituted 63% of subjects in 10P.,1,48% economics students or graduates; remainder other fields or non-students,"For 10P treatment, 48% were economics students or graduates.",0.9,On site,Same lab setting as other treatments.,1,"Same dynamic CPR game as baseline (logistic growth with r=0.1, K=80, initial stock 45, groups of 3, max individual harvest 1/3 of current stock, continuous extraction). Additional two-stage structure each period: (1) before harvesting, each subject decides whether to pay a tax (0.5 tokens if 1 payer, 0.25 each if 2 payers, 0.15 each if 3 payers); if at least one pays, a sanctioning institution is established for that period; (2) after observing others’ harvests (anonymous), each subject may vote on a harvest threshold; one vote is randomly selected (Random Dictator rule), and all players whose harvest exceeds that threshold lose 1 token. No second-order punishment of tax evaders.","Summarizes punishment mechanics, tax schedule, random dictator voting, and relation to baseline CPR.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.04.004.md,Second-order punishment (20P) – voluntary tax with punishment of over-harvesters and tax evaders,Paper defines second-order punishment treatment labelled 20P; includes extra penalty on tax evaders.,1,"Punishment institution: pool punishment via voluntary tax plus second-order punishment. Tax per payer: 0.5 tokens if 1 payer, 0.25 if 2, 0.15 if 3. When institution active, penalty of 1 token for harvests above chosen threshold and additional 1-token penalty for each tax evader.",Key treatment difference is additional punishment for tax evasion in 20P.,1,True,Same human-subject experimental design; 22–23 groups reported for 20P.,1,True,Controlled treatment manipulation within lab CPR game.,1,True,Conducted on-site in computer lab with z-Tree.,1,False,"This row is for empirical treatment, not the theoretical model.",1,False,Analytical predictions exist but are separate from the experiment.,1,3,Groups of three persons each in all treatments.,1,30,Maximum of 30 periods as in other treatments.,1,1,Participants can choose any (fractional) harvest up to 1/3 of current stock.,1,0,Same CPR extraction structure; no initial contributions to public account.,0.7,N/R,No linear MPCR is defined; growth is logistic.,0,0,No communication facility described.,0.9,1,"After harvesting, subjects observe others’ harvests and then vote when institution exists.",1,0,Identities remain anonymous and harvests shown in random order; punishments apply without identity revelation.,1,0,No rewards available; identities are never revealed.,1,0,Subjects not told the exact number of periods.,1,1,Sanctioning institution can be established and punishes both over-harvesters and tax evaders when active.,1,1,Each applicable penalty (over-harvesting or tax evasion) reduces subject’s payoff by 1 token.,1,1,One unit of punishment causes a 1-token loss; no magnification factor.,1,0,"Second-order treatment adds more penalties, not rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,45,Initial common-pool stock is 45 tokens as in other treatments.,1,0.27,Table 2(a): Mean fraction of harvested resources in 20P is 0.27 (±0.13).,1,4.28,Table 2(a): Mean harvest in 20P is 4.28 (±4.43) tokens.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio presented.,0,4.58,Table 2(a) reports Total Payoffs = 4.58 (±5.84) per subject in 20P; used as payoff outcome measure.,0.5,Poland,All participants recruited at University of Warsaw.,1,Mean age 32.1 (±16.9) years,Mean age for 20P treatment reported in design section.,1,69% female,Females constituted 69% of subjects in 20P.,1,39% economics students or graduates; remainder other fields or non-students,"For 20P treatment, 39% were economics students or graduates.",0.9,On site,Same lab context as other treatments.,1,"Same dynamic CPR framework as other treatments (logistic growth with r=0.1, K=80, initial stock 45, groups of 3, continuous extraction up to 1/3 of stock). Each period: (1) before harvesting, each subject decides whether to pay a tax (0.5, 0.25, or 0.15 tokens per payer depending on 1, 2, or 3 taxpayers); if at least one pays, the sanctioning institution is established; (2) after seeing others’ harvests (anonymous), each subject may vote on a harvest threshold; one vote selected at random determines a cutoff; all players with harvest above this cutoff lose 1 token; additionally, any subject who did not pay the tax in a period where at least one person paid loses another 1 token as a penalty for tax evasion. If stock falls below 1 token, game ends immediately and all accumulated earnings from this part are lost.","Combines CPR dynamics, tax schedule, first- and second-order punishment rules, and termination condition.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.027.md,Round 1 – Baseline public goods game,First round is a standard linear public goods game played in all 147 villages before any variation is introduced.,1,Village had Radio Reception in 1994 (proxy for conflict exposure); age cohort at time of genocide (<=14 vs >=15) used in heterogeneity analyses,Main treatment variable is village-level radio reception; age at genocide is key moderator for contributions and other outcomes.,1,True,Human participants played incentivized games in villages.,1,True,Controlled lab-in-the-field public goods game with randomized participant selection and fixed rules.,1,True,"Game conducted on site in village settings as a lab-in-the-field experiment, not in naturally occurring markets or institutions.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation is described.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model used as a treatment condition.,1,12,Twelve individuals per village game session are repeatedly mentioned.,1,1,This row refers only to the first round baseline decision; second round variants are separate conditions.,1,1,"Participants could contribute any amount from 0 to 400 RWF in 100 RWF increments, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is received privately as coins; nothing is pre-committed to the group account.,1,0.25,Payoff function v_i = 400 - c_i + (3/12)*Σ c_j implies MPCR = 3/12 = 0.25.,1,0,No communication between participants reported; privacy stressed instead.,0.9,0,Only total group contributions and equal returns are computed; no mention of showing individual or summary earnings aside from one’s own.,0.8,0,No punishment stage in baseline round.,1,0,No reward stage in baseline round.,1,1,"Participants were told the activity would be played two times, so total rounds were known.",0.9,0,Baseline first round has no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this round.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this round.,1,0,No reward option in baseline round.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,400,Each participant received 4×100 RWF coins as endowment per round.,1,0.64,Average contribution 255 RWF out of 400 RWF endowment; 255/400 = 0.6375 ≈ 0.64 reported as 64% of 400.,1,255,Figure 2 text: average contribution was 255 RWF.,1,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum group payoff is reported or derivable from aggregates.,0,N/R,Only average individual contributions and some earnings info are given; no average group payoff.,0,Rwanda,"Games conducted in Rusizi district, Rwanda.",1,"Mean age 35 in 2013; heterogeneous ages, split into cohorts <=14 vs >=15 at time of 1994 genocide","Section 4.1 reports average age 35, and later sections describe age-at-genocide distribution and cohorts.",1,74% female,Section 4.1 notes women represent 74% of participants.,1,Mean 4.5 years of education,Section 4.1 reports average education is 4.5 years.,1,On site,Lab-in-the-field experiments run in person in 147 villages with local staff.,1,Linear public goods game; contribution choices in 100 RWF increments from 0 to 400; contributions triple and are evenly shared; one-shot within this round with known second round to follow; random village-level exposure to genocide-related radio used as quasi-exogenous treatment.,Summarizes key structural features of the baseline round as described.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.027.md,Round 2 – Baseline repeated version,"Second round in 76 villages repeated the same baseline public goods game without punishment, reward, or risk.",1,Village had Radio Reception in 1994; game version = Baseline repeat; age cohort at time of genocide used in heterogeneity analyses,Radio reception is main historical treatment; this condition is the baseline second-round version in Table 3 column (1).,1,True,Human subjects played the second round.,1,True,Second round follows controlled experimental protocol.,1,True,On-site lab-in-the-field continuation of the first-round game.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No analytical-only treatment.,1,12,Same session of 12 players continues into round 2.,1,1,This condition refers only to the second-round decision in villages assigned the baseline repeat.,1,1,Contribution choice set identical to baseline: 0–400 in 100 increments.,1,0,"Endowment again received privately, nothing pre-committed.",1,0.25,Payoff structure identical to baseline: contributions tripled then equally divided (3/12).,1,0,No communication stage introduced in round 2 description.,0.9,0,"No mention of displaying others’ earnings beyond public counting of coins, which already occurred in round 1.",0.7,0,No punishment in the baseline repeat version.,1,0,No reward mechanism in baseline repeat.,1,1,Participants knew there would be exactly two rounds from the outset.,0.9,0,No penalty stage in this version.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,No reward stage in this version.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,400,Same 4×100 RWF endowment per round.,1,N/R,Text notes that round-2 contributions in baseline villages are 10% lower than round 1 but does not give exact mean.,0.3,N/R,"Only relative decrease compared to round 1 is reported, not the absolute mean for this subsample.",0.3,N/R,No aggregate payoff efficiency reported by version.,0,N/R,No mean group payoff for second round baseline version is provided.,0,Rwanda,Same sample and setting as round 1.,1,Mean age 35 in 2013; age-at-genocide cohorts (<=14 vs >=15) present with similar composition as overall sample,Second round uses same participants as first round in those villages.,1,74% female,Same participant pool as first round.,1,Mean 4.5 years of education,Same as round 1 sample.,1,On site,Continuation of on-site lab-in-the-field sessions.,1,"Second round is a repeated one-shot linear public goods game with same structure as round 1; average contributions are about 10% lower than in round 1, indicating learning; assignment to this version is random at the village level with baseline overweighted overall.",Summarizes key features of the baseline repeat second round and its assignment.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.027.md,Round 2 – Penalty version,"Second-round public goods game with individual punishment option, implemented in 25 villages.",1,Village had Radio Reception in 1994; game version = Penalty; age cohort at time of genocide (<=14 vs >=15) in heterogeneity analyses; partner contribution level for within-version behavior,Radio reception is main historical treatment; penalty version is specific institutional environment; partner contribution enters as key determinant of punishment.,1,True,Human participants made contribution and punishment decisions.,1,True,Controlled game with random assignment of version at village level and anonymous pairing.,1,True,Conducted as lab-in-the-field experiment in villages.,1,False,No simulation model.,1,False,No analytical-only treatment.,1,12,Public good is still within the same 12-person group from round 1.,1,1,This condition is the one second-round decision in villages assigned to the penalty version.,1,1,Contribution decision remains any amount from 0 to 400 in 100 increments.,1,0,Participants again receive full 400 RWF privately.,1,0.25,Contributions are still tripled and divided across 12 participants (3/12).,1,0,No communication reported; interaction is via anonymous one-to-one matching.,0.9,0,"Players observe only their paired partner’s contribution, not group-level summaries of payoffs or sanctions.",0.9,0,"Matching is anonymous; identities are not revealed, only partner’s contribution is shown.",1,0,No reward option exists in the penalty version.,1,1,"Participants knew there would be two rounds, and this is the second.",0.9,1,Participants can give a penalty to an anonymously matched player at a personal cost.,1,50,Giving a penalty costs 50 RWF to the punisher.,1,100,"Penalty removes 100 RWF from the punished player’s earnings, stated fine-to-fee ratio 2.",1,0,Only penalties possible in this version.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,400,Same endowment structure continues in second round.,1,N/R,Paper reports that mean contributions are about 11% above baseline in this version but gives no exact average contribution.,0.3,N/R,No explicit mean contribution level by version reported; only relative differences.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency levels for penalty game are not provided.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff by version is reported.,0,Rwanda,Same sample of Rwandan villagers.,1,Mean age 35 in 2013; age-at-genocide cohorts present; penalty-specific regressions split by <=14 vs >=15 at time of genocide and also by gender in robustness checks,Penalty version uses a subset of the same participant pool; Table 6 and Appendix H discuss age and gender heterogeneity.,1,74% female overall; penalty subsample similar though exact share is not separately reported,No separate gender breakdown for the penalty subsample; assume similar to overall since selection is random across villages.,0.7,Mean 4.5 years of education (overall sample); penalty subsample similar,No distinct education statistics by version; village assignment is random.,0.7,On site,Same lab-in-the-field setting.,1,"After contributions, each player is anonymously matched one-to-one, sees the partner’s contribution, and can choose once to impose a 50 RWF cost that reduces partner’s payoff by 100 RWF; punishment is binary; norm enforcement analyzed via a linear probability model of ‘gave penalty’, with partner’s low contributions strongly predicting punishment and exposure to conflict increasing punishment, especially among those who were older (>=15) during the genocide.",Summarizes added punishment stage and key behavioral findings relevant to this condition.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.027.md,Round 2 – Reward version,"Second-round public goods game with individual reward option, implemented in 22 villages.",1,Village had Radio Reception in 1994; game version = Reward; age cohort at time of genocide (<=14 vs >=15); partner contribution level within version,Radio reception is primary historical treatment; reward version defines institutional environment; partner’s contribution determines likelihood of rewarding.,1,True,Human subjects make contribution and reward decisions.,1,True,Controlled second-round game with exogenous version assignment.,1,True,On-site experimental sessions within villages.,1,False,No simulation treatment.,1,False,No analytical-only treatment.,1,12,Same 12-player groups continue from round 1.,1,1,One second-round decision in villages assigned reward version.,1,1,Contribution choice from 0 to 400 RWF in 100-RWF steps.,1,0,Endowment again entirely private.,1,0.25,Group fund mechanics same as baseline: tripling and equal division.,1,0,No pre-decision communication is described.,0.9,0,"Participants observe only contribution of their partner, not group summaries or IDs.",0.9,0,No punishment in this version.,1,0,Matching is anonymous; only partner’s contribution is known.,1,1,Participants were told beforehand there would be two rounds.,0.9,0,"Only rewards, no penalties.",1,N/A,No punishment possible.,1,N/A,No punishment possible.,1,1,Players can reward their matched partner at a personal cost.,1,50,Giving a reward costs 50 RWF to the giver.,1,100,"Reward adds 100 RWF to the recipient’s payoff, giving a 2:1 reward-to-fee ratio.",1,400,Participants receive 400 RWF again in this round.,1,N/R,"Text notes contributions are about 15% more than baseline, but exact mean contribution is not reported.",0.3,N/R,No numerical mean contribution for this version is given.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency metric for reward version is given.,0,N/R,Group payoff by version not reported.,0,Rwanda,Same village sample.,1,Mean age 35 in 2013; age-at-genocide cohorts <=14 vs >=15 analyzed for reward-giving behavior,Reward version regressions in Table 6 split sample by young vs old at genocide.,1,74% female overall; reward subsample expected to be similar,No separate gender shares reported for this version; assignment is random at village level.,0.7,Mean 4.5 years of education (overall sample); reward subsample similar,No version-specific education data; rely on overall description.,0.7,On site,Same on-site experimental setup.,1,"After contributing, each participant is anonymously paired one-to-one, sees partner’s contribution, and decides once whether to pay 50 RWF to give the partner an extra 100 RWF; 81% of individuals give a reward overall, with more rewards for higher-contributing partners; exposure to conflict increases rewarding behavior mainly among those who were older at the time of the genocide, though average contributions in this version are not significantly linked to conflict exposure once first-round behavior is controlled for.",Summarizes reward mechanism and central behavioral findings for this treatment.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2023.11.027.md,Round 2 – Risk version,"Second-round public goods game with risky group fund multipliers, implemented in 24 villages.",1,Village had Radio Reception in 1994; game version = Risk; allocation between Fund A and Fund B; age cohort at time of genocide (<=14 vs >=15),Radio reception is main historical treatment; risky two-fund structure defines this version; age cohorts used for heterogeneous treatment effects.,1,True,Human participants made risky contribution decisions.,1,True,Controlled modification of public goods game with randomized assignment to risk version.,1,True,Lab-in-the-field implementation in Rwandan villages.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,No separate analytical condition.,1,12,Same 12-person groups as in other versions.,1,1,Single second-round decision in villages assigned risk version.,1,1,"Players can decide how much (0–400 RWF, increments of 100 RWF) to contribute; they may also split that between two funds.",1,0,Full 400 RWF endowment initially private.,1,0.25,"One fund has MPCR = 1/12 ≈ 0.0833; the other has MPCR = 5/12 ≈ 0.4167; coin flip selects which applies, so expected MPCR is (1/2)*(1/12+5/12) = 3/12 = 0.25, equal to baseline.",1,0,No communication stage described; only choices and random coin flip.,0.9,0,"Participants are not described as seeing others’ payoffs, only their own earnings post-realization.",0.7,0,No punishment in risk version.,1,0,No reward in risk version.,1,1,Participants know that this is the second of two rounds.,0.9,0,No penalty mechanism described.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No reward mechanism in this version.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,400,Same per-round endowment.,1,N/R,"Text reports no perceptible difference in average contributions relative to baseline overall, but no exact mean is provided.",0.3,N/R,Mean contribution in risk version is not numerically reported.,0.3,N/R,No efficiency summary for risk version is given.,0,N/R,Average group payoff not reported.,0,Rwanda,Same region and sample.,1,"Mean age 35 in 2013; contributions in risk version analyzed by young (<=14 at genocide) vs old (>=15) cohorts, with both showing increased risk-taking associated with conflict exposure",Table 6 columns (7)–(8) examine heterogeneous effects on risk-version contributions by age-at-genocide.,1,74% female overall; risk subsample expected to be similar,No version-specific gender shares; assume approximate similarity due to random assignment.,0.7,Mean 4.5 years of education (overall sample); risk subsample similar,No risk-version-specific education stats; assume similar composition.,0.7,On site,Same on-site experimental sessions.,1,"Participants choose how much of their 400 RWF to allocate to two group funds (A and B); a fair coin flip determines which fund gets MPCR = 1/12 and which gets MPCR = 5/12; totals for each fund are multiplied accordingly and divided equally among 12 participants; expected MPCR matches baseline but introduces risk over public returns; conflict exposure (radio reception) is associated with significantly higher contributions (about +47 RWF) in this risky environment, consistent with more risk-seeking preferences across age cohorts.",Summarizes risky structure and main conflict-related behavioral results for this treatment.,1
10.1088_0253-6102_62_5_03.md,"Simulation – spatial voluntary PGG with punishment, varying γ and r, baseline parameters",Single simulation model on 100×100 lattice; conditions differ only by punishment cost γ and interest rate r (and in Fig. 3 by memory ω). Treated as one experiment family with parameterized independent variables.,0.8,"Punishment cost γ ∈ {1.0, 0.5, 0.3, 0.1}, interest rate r ∈ (2,5), memory ω ∈ [0.01,0.8], mutation/exploration rate μ=0.0001; comparison of resulting frequencies and payoffs of C, D, P, L","Text and figure captions specify γ values for panels (a–d) and (e–h) in Fig. 1, r is scanned; Fig. 3 varies ω and r for γ=1 or 0.1. All are parameter variations within the same simulation model.",0.9,False,Model is an evolutionary game on a 100×100 lattice with Monte Carlo simulation; no human participants.,1.0,False,No controlled experiment with human subjects; it is a numerical simulation study.,1.0,False,Not a lab or field experiment; purely computational.,1.0,True,They explicitly describe running simulations on a spatial lattice with Monte Carlo time steps.,1.0,False,"Although payoff formulas are given, results are based on numerical simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions.",0.9,5,A single PGG includes the player and his four nearest neighbors (n_c + n_d + n_l + n_p = N = 5).,1.0,100000,They state results were obtained by averaging over the last 10000 Monte Carlo time steps of the total 100000.,1.0,1,"Strategies are discrete types (C, D, L, P); there is no continuous contribution choice level, so all-or-nothing in terms of contributing the fixed amount or not.",0.9,0,Standard PGG formulation with players deciding whether to contribute a fixed unit from their endowment; no initial allocation in public pool indicated.,0.7,0.8,"Marginal per capita return is r/N; they use N=5 and discuss low r values like r=2.1, so MPCR = r/5. For canonical level r=4 (mid-range) MPCR=0.8; since r varies, we report representative MPCR at r=4.",0.4,0,No mention of any communication or signaling among players; only payoffs and mixed-strategy updates are modeled.,0.95,0,Individuals only know their own payoff in each round; no information about others’ earnings or actions is provided.,1.0,0,Model is symmetric and anonymous on a lattice; punishers affect payoffs but there is no identity or labeling beyond local strategy types.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism is present; only punishment is modeled.,1.0,0,Simulation agents are not given explicit knowledge of total time steps; updating is based only on last-round payoff vs σ.,0.8,1,Punishers P reduce payoffs of defectors by β and cooperators by αβ at cost γ and αγ; entire paper centers on punishment.,1.0,1.0,"Cost per punished neighbor for a punisher is γ toward defectors and αγ toward cooperators. Baseline γ=1 with α=0.1, so unit cost scale is set by γ; we report γ as the punishment cost parameter.",0.7,1.2,Punishment strength is β toward defectors and αβ toward cooperators; they set β=1.2 and α=0.1. Report core punishment magnitude β.,0.9,0,Only punishment (reduction of payoffs) and loner option are modeled; no positive reward mechanism is introduced.,1.0,N/A,"No reward mechanism, so no cost parameter.",1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1.0,1,Standard PGG normalization where cooperators invest a fixed amount; payoff formulas subtract 1 for contributors (−1 term) implying unit contribution cost.,0.9,N/R,They report frequencies of cooperators and punishers (contributors) but not an explicit normalized contribution rate aggregated over C and P; not summarized as a single scalar.,0.4,N/R,Only qualitative descriptions and example payoffs are given; no numeric average contribution per player is tabulated.,0.3,N/R,They show average payoffs but do not normalize them by the theoretical maximum payoff from full cooperation without punishment.,0.3,N/R,"Figures present average payoff per individual (population average), not explicit total group payoffs.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,Study is a computational simulation on a lattice with Monte Carlo steps.,1.0,"Spatial voluntary public goods game on a 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundary; four pure strategies: cooperators (C) contribute 1 and do not punish, defectors (D) do not contribute or punish, punishers (P) contribute 1 and punish non-punishers (defectors by β, cooperators by αβ) at costs γ and αγ, and loners (L) do not participate and get fixed payoff σ=1. Interest rate r satisfies 2 < r < 5. Initial mixed strategies: each site assigned one of L,C,D,P with equal probability 1/4. Agents use mixed strategies Sx(pl,pc,pd,pp) updated via a self-adjusting rule comparing last payoff Pi to σ with memory parameter ω and random exploration rate μ=0.0001. Frequencies and average payoffs are measured after equilibration over last 10000 of 100000 Monte Carlo steps.","Summarized directly from Methods and figure captions (σ, α, β, γ, r, ω, μ, lattice size, initial conditions, mixed-strategy updating).",1.0
10.1007_978-3-642-00128-4_6.md,Shariff and Norenzayan (2007) – God concept prime,Paper explicitly cites Shariff and Norenzayan (2007) and describes a condition where God concepts are involved in a dictator game increasing prosocial allocations.,0.93,Priming condition: God concepts,Described contrast between when 'God concepts are involved' vs. 'neutral or no concepts' in an anonymous dictator game.,0.93,True,Human subjects play an anonymous dictator game with monetary allocations.,0.99,True,Dictator game with experimentally manipulated primes (God vs neutral/no prime) is a controlled lab experiment.,0.99,True,Shariff and Norenzayan (2007) is a standard psychology laboratory experiment; no indication of field setting.,0.9,False,"Described study uses human participants, not simulated agents.",1,False,No formal mathematical modeling; it is an empirical game.,1,2,Dictator game involves one allocator and one recipient; text mentions 'anonymous people' in singular as recipients of allocation.,0.8,1,Dictator game as described is one-shot; no repetition is mentioned.,0.9,1,Standard dictator games allow any portion of the endowment to be given; no restriction to all-or-nothing stated.,0.8,0,In dictator games the endowment initially belongs fully to the dictator (private account).,0.9,N/R,Dictator game is not a linear public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined or reported.,1,0,No communication or discussion between dictator and recipient is mentioned; standard anonymous dictator games prohibit chat.,0.85,0,Only a single anonymous recipient is referenced; no information about others’ actions or payoffs is described.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism in a standard dictator game.,1,0,No explicit reward-sanction stage is described; only unilateral giving.,0.95,0,One-shot dictator game; no indication that any 'number of rounds' information is relevant or displayed.,0.9,0,Dictator game in description does not allow recipients to punish.,0.98,N/A,No punishment stage in this game.,1,N/A,No punishment implemented.,1,0,"There is no post-play costly reward scheme; giving itself is the primary decision, not a separate reward technology.",0.9,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond initial allocation.,1,N/A,No defined reward technology.,1,N/R,Text mentions 'money' but gives no specific amount or unit.,0.1,N/R,"Paper only states that allocations are higher in God-prime condition, without numerical values.",0.1,N/R,No exact amount of money shared is reported in the text.,0.1,N/R,No group payoff or efficiency ratio reported.,0.1,N/R,No total payoff numbers described.,0.1,N/R,"The excerpt does not include sample location; original Shariff and Norenzayan (2007) was North American, but this is not stated here, so not inferred.",0.1,N/R,Age range of participants is not reported.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information provided.,0.1,N/R,Text does not state if they were students or other groups.,0.1,On site,Shariff and Norenzayan (2007) is a lab-based psychology experiment; lab corresponds to an on-site controlled setting rather than online or field.,0.85,"One-shot anonymous dictator game where participants receive an endowment and may allocate part to an anonymous recipient, with primes involving God concepts vs neutral or no concept preceding the allocation decision.","Summarized from description: 'anonymous dictator game', 'subjects allocate more money to anonymous people when God concepts are involved than when neutral or no concepts are present'.",0.95
