10.1007_s11127-007-9269-3.md,"Analytical evolutionary game model with Fair (F), Cheater (C), and Unpleasant (U) player types","Paper presents only formal evolutionary game-theoretic models (Tables 1–3, Figures 1–3) comparing types F, C, and U; no human-subject experiment or numerical simulation is described.",1,"Type composition and parameter values in an evolutionary game: player strategy types (Fair, Cheater, Unpleasant) and payoff parameters α (baseline fair-fair payoff), β (cost of being cheated), φ (cost of punishing), γ (cost of being punished), with cases distinguished mainly by β < φ vs β > φ.","Model varies relative costs of being cheated vs punishing (β and φ) and includes three player types with different behavioral rules; these are the key independent dimensions generating different equilibria (ESS, internal equilibria, cycles).",1,False,No human participants; all results are derived from theoretical payoff matrices and evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment; it is a theoretical/evolutionary analysis.,1,False,No lab or field data; purely theoretical.,1,False,"Although population dynamics are discussed qualitatively and with diagrams, no explicit computational or agent-based simulations are reported.",0.9,True,"Model consists of closed-form payoff matrices, algebraic conditions for ESS, and qualitative phase-diagram reasoning (winding numbers) without numerical simulation.",1,0,No discrete group size is specified; interactions are modeled pairwise in a large population evolutionary game.,1,0,Model assumes ongoing evolutionary dynamics rather than discrete repeated rounds of a game.,1,1,"Players are not contributing amounts to a public good but choosing discrete strategies (F, C, U) in pairwise encounters; contribution-amount granularity is not defined, so mark as allowing general strategies.",0.3,N/A,"No human players or explicit public-goods endowment allocation; model is about payoff from interaction types, not token allocation.",1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction or communication stage; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or feedback to humans.,1,N/A,Identity revelation is not modeled for human subjects; strategies are abstract types.,1,N/A,No reward stage or human identities modeled.,1,N/A,No discrete-round experiment.,1,1,Central feature of the model is costly punishment (parameters φ and γ) by Unpleasant (and conceptually by Altruistic) types.,1,N/R,Punishment cost is represented symbolically by φ; no specific numeric value is assigned.,1,N/R,"Effectiveness of punishment is modeled via payoff terms (e.g., γ cost to punished), but no numeric multiplier per punishment unit is provided.",1,0,"Only punishment (costs φ, γ) is modeled; no positive reward or bonus mechanism is introduced in the formal model.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model normalizes payoffs (e.g., cheating payoff = 1) but does not specify a token endowment per player.",1,N/R,No explicit contribution decision to a public good; outcomes are evolutionary population shares and payoffs.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts are modeled.,1,N/R,"Paper discusses relative payoffs and stability, not explicit efficiency ratios compared to a maximum payoff benchmark.",1,N/R,Payoffs are per-encounter and per-type; aggregate group payoff levels are not given as a primary reported dependent variable.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely theoretical with no empirical data collection.,1,"Analytical evolutionary game of pairwise interactions among three strategy types: Fair (cooperate, no punishment), Cheater (defect to gain payoff 1, no punishment), and Unpleasant (cheat and punish identifiable Cheaters). Payoff parameters: α in [0,1) as fair–fair baseline; β < α is cost of being cheated; φ < γ are costs of punishing and being punished, with cases distinguished by β < φ vs β > φ. The analysis derives conditions under which C, U, or mixed F–U or internal polymorphic states are evolutionarily stable using ESS conditions and Saari-style qualitative dynamics/winding numbers.",Summarizes the structure of the theoretical game as described in Tables 1–3 and surrounding text.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2019.121796.md,Analytical and simulation model of public goods game with fairness mechanism and peer punishment,Paper presents a single theoretical model (Sections 2–3) and then simulates its dynamics under varying parameters (Section 4). No distinct human-subject experimental conditions are reported.,1,"Model parameters varied in simulations: income factor k1 ∈ [3,5]; fairness factor k2 ∈ [0.5,1]; punishment cost C either fixed (e.g., 2, 5, 6, 10) or scale-dependent C = k3 n² with k3 ∈ {1/400, 1/600, 1/800}; punishment fine δ ∈ (1,2.5]; cluster size n varied (e.g., 20, 50, 64, 65, 80, 100); initial number of cooperators nc varied between n/4 and 3n/4; group partition in two-group game: n1, n2 = n − n1.","Extracted all exogenous parameters explicitly varied in simulation figures and analysis: k1 in Fig.1, 2, implicit elsewhere; k2 in Fig.3, Fig.7; C and δ in Figs.1–4,7; n and nc ranges in Sections 3.2 and 4.",0.95,False,No human subjects; study consists of analytical derivations and MATLAB simulations of the model.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human participants or field data is described; only theoretical and computational work.,1,False,No laboratory or field experiment with participants; purely model-based.,1,True,Section 4 explicitly states that the cluster evolutionary process is simulated and figures are generated using MATLAB based on the model.,1,True,"Sections 2 and 3 derive closed-form expressions for utilities, equilibrium conditions, and propositions using algebra and calculus.",1,50,"A representative simulation configuration is n = 50 used repeatedly in Section 4.2 and Fig.3–4. Exact player count varies in different simulations (e.g., 20, 50, 64, 65, 80, 100), but 50 is a central illustrative case.",0.7,50,"Text for Fig.3–4 and Fig.6–7 states “with 50 steps,” which correspond to discrete time steps/iterations of the dynamical process.",0.9,1,"Players are either cooperators (qc = 1) or defectors (qd = 0), implying a binary all-or-nothing contribution decision rather than a continuous amount choice.",1,N/A,No human subjects or experimental interface with default allocations; purely theoretical contributions qc and qd.,1,N/R,"Public goods return uses income factor k1 (1 < k1 < n), but marginal per capita return (multiplier divided by group size) is never explicitly defined or computed.",0.3,N/A,No human interaction or communication; model has no communication mechanism.,1,0,"Model does not include information displays; agents only respond to utilities and numbers of cooperators/players, with no notion of shown summaries.",0.9,N/A,No identities or information structure are modeled; peer punishment is anonymous in an aggregate sense within a well-mixed population.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is included; only punishment is modeled.,1,0,No interface or awareness of total steps for agents in the formal model; horizon is not part of their decision rules.,0.9,1,Core of the model is a peer punishment mechanism where cooperators pay cost C and defectors pay fine δ.,1,5,"Representative fixed-cost setting in simulations: Section 4.2 uses k1 = 3, C = 5, δ = 2, n = 50 to study multiplayer equilibrium; C also varies (1–6, 10) and as C = k3 n², but 5 is a typical fixed scenario.",0.7,1,"Model defines fine δ paid by each defector when punishment is implemented. One can interpret each unit of total punishment cost C spread among cooperators as financing an equal per-defector fine δ; per-unit cost-to-fine mapping is not normalized, but taking 1 as the technical multiplier is a neutral coding choice since actual ratio C/δ depends on n and nc.",0.3,0,Only punishment (cost C and fine δ) is discussed; no reward or positive sanctioning is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model normalizes contributions with qc = 1, qd = 0, but does not specify an explicit monetary/point endowment per player.",0.4,N/R,"Simulations report cooperation level as equilibrium number of cooperators nc (or B) relative to n in figures, but no explicit normalized scalar time-averaged contribution rate is tabulated; curves are qualitative.",0.4,N/R,"Total contributions equal nc in the model, but simulations and text do not provide specific numeric equilibrium nc values beyond ranges and qualitative statements.",0.4,N/R,"Although utilities and total payoffs can be derived from k1, nc, and n, the paper does not explicitly calculate or report normalized efficiency measures.",0.3,N/R,"Group payoff equals sum of individual utilities, which can be expressed analytically, but numerical group payoff values are not provided in text or figures.",0.3,N/A,No participants; purely theoretical and simulated study.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Work consists of analytical modeling and MATLAB simulations only.,1,"Well-mixed public goods game with n players; binary strategies: cooperate (contribute qc = 1) or defect (qd = 0); total contributions multiplied by income factor k1 (1 < k1 < n) and equally shared among all n players. Fairness mechanism introduces fairness utility Uf = k2 (μ − qi − 1) σ² based on mean μ and variance σ² of contributions, with 0 < k2 < 1. Peer punishment: if both cooperators and defectors exist and cooperators choose punishment, cooperators share total punishment cost C evenly so each cooperator’s effective contribution is qc' = 1 + C/nc; each defector pays fine δ and has contribution proxy qd' = δ. Population dynamics: number of cooperators nc and total population size n (cluster size) evolve as players switch strategies to maximize total utility (profit + fairness) and may enter/leave the cluster depending on sign of utility. Punishment cost may depend on n as C = k3 n². Stability concepts: social dilemma (all defect), full cooperation (all cooperate), vibratory equilibrium (nc fluctuates between B−1 and B), and stabilization of n when extension/exit dynamics settle.",Summarized main structural aspects of the theoretical public goods game and evolutionary dynamics as described in Sections 2–4 and Appendices.,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2020.109155.md,PN – Punishment community P(PN),PN treatment includes a punishment community and a no-sanction community; notation P(PN) is used in the paper.,1,Institutional regime in PN: punishment vs no-sanction; this row: punishment community P(PN),"Treatments differ by available sanctioning institutions; within PN, communities differ by presence of punishment.",0.9,True,Laboratory experiment with 156 human participants.,1,True,Controlled lab study with random assignment into matching groups and predefined treatments.,1,True,Conducted at WISO Research Laboratory at the University of Hamburg.,1,False,No mention of agent-based or numerical simulations; all results from human-subject experiment.,1,False,Paper reports empirical results rather than formal analytical model.,1,12,“We use 12-people matching groups who interact anonymously.” Communities are formed within 12-person matching groups.,0.8,30,“which are repeated for 30 periods.” referring to the three-stage game.,1,1,"Standard public goods game with 20-token endowment; no restriction to all-or-nothing is mentioned, so continuous choice from 0 to 20 is implied.",0.8,0,Each player receives an endowment of 20 tokens and then decides how many to contribute; endowment starts in private account.,1,0.1333,Tokens contributed are multiplied by 1.6 and split equally among all members of the group; MPCR = 1.6 / 12 ≈ 0.1333 given 12-player groups.,0.8,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard anonymous interaction in z-tree.,0.9,1,"After sanctioning, players receive feedback including contribution, income after PGG, received and distributed points, and total payoffs.",1,0,"Design stresses anonymity and changing player IDs; reputation building not possible, so identities of punishers are not revealed.",0.9,0,This community has only punishment; no reward mechanism.,1,0,No explicit information that remaining or total rounds are displayed in the interface.,0.5,1,P(PN) is defined as community with a punishment system.,1,1,“players may assign points… reducing their own income by one and reducing … income … by 3 tokens.” Cost is 1 per punishment point.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces target’s income by 3 tokens.,1,0,"PN punishment community has only punishment, no reward option.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this community.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this community.,1,20,“each player receives an endowment of 20 tokens.” for the public goods stage.,1,0.87,"Average contribution reported as 17.4 tokens in P(PN); endowment is 20, so 17.4/20 = 0.87.",0.9,17.4,"“the punishment community in the PN treatment condition performing best, with an average contribution of 17.4.”",1,N/R,No explicit or computable normalized efficiency measure reported for this community.,0,N/R,Paper compares profits across communities but does not report a specific numeric mean payoff for P(PN).,0,Germany,Experiment conducted at University of Hamburg (Germany); typical subject pool is local students.,0.9,N/R,Age range or mean age not reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,0,University students,"Conducted in a university experimental lab; typical participants are students, though not explicitly stated.",0.6,On site,Sessions at a physical university laboratory using z-tree.,1,"Three-stage game per period: (i) community selection within 12-person matching group, (ii) public goods contribution from 20-token endowment with multiplier 1.6 shared equally among group, (iii) sanctioning stage with additional 20-token endowment enabling peer punishment. Anonymity is enforced by changing player IDs; no reputation building.",Summary of design description in Section 2.,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2020.109155.md,PN – No-sanction community N(PN),In PN treatment players choose between a punishment system and a community without any sanctioning system (no-sanction community).,1,Institutional regime in PN: punishment vs no-sanction; this row: no-sanction community N(PN),Communities differ only by presence or absence of punishment; this condition has no sanctioning institution.,0.9,True,Same human-subject experiment as other PN communities.,1,True,Part of controlled lab experiment with random matching groups and prescribed rules.,1,True,Conducted in WISO Research Laboratory at University of Hamburg.,1,False,No simulation results reported.,1,False,No analytical model used as primary method.,1,12,12-person matching groups; communities form endogenously within these groups.,0.7,30,Game repeated for 30 periods.,1,1,"Same public goods game structure as in other communities, allowing continuous contributions from the 20-token endowment.",0.8,0,Endowment initially under participant control; they decide how much to contribute.,1,0.1333,Same PGG parameters: contribution tokens multiplied by 1.6 and equally split among group members; MPCR = 1.6 / 12.,0.8,0,No communication channel described.,0.9,1,"Feedback on contributions, income, and total payoffs is provided for both communities regardless of sanctioning.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this community.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this community.,1,0,Interface details about showing remaining rounds are not reported.,0.5,0,This PN community is explicitly described as “without any sanctioning system.”,1,N/A,No punishment in this community.,1,N/A,No punishment in this community.,1,0,"PN contrasts punishment with no-sanction, not with reward.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this community.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this community.,1,20,Same PGG endowment of 20 tokens per period applies to all communities.,1,N/R,Paper indicates contributions in non-punishment community remain low but does not provide a numeric mean.,0,N/R,No explicit average contribution amount reported for the no-sanction community.,0,N/R,No normalized efficiency measure provided.,0,N/R,Only qualitative information that profits are lower than in other communities; no specific figure given.,0,Germany,Lab at University of Hamburg in Germany.,0.9,N/R,Age not reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not mentioned.,0,University students,Standard subject pool for a university experimental lab; not explicitly reported but likely.,0.6,On site,Sessions physically run in a university laboratory.,1,"Same three-stage structure per period as other conditions. Players in no-sanction communities also receive an additional 20-token endowment in the stage that would allow sanctioning, but cannot assign punishment or reward points.",Footnote states that in communities without sanctioning players also receive the additional endowment.,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2020.109155.md,PR – Punishment community P(PR),In PR treatment players choose between a punishment and a reward community; P(PR) denotes the punishment community.,1,Institutional regime in PR: punishment vs reward; this row: punishment community P(PR),"Within PR, communities differ by sanction type; this condition uses punishment.",0.9,True,Part of the same human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of available sanctioning institutions.,1,True,Conducted at WISO Research Laboratory at the University of Hamburg.,1,False,"Results are empirical, not simulated.",1,False,No purely analytical results reported.,1,12,12-person matching groups are used; communities exist within these groups.,0.7,30,Three-stage game repeated for 30 periods.,1,1,"Same PGG as other communities, permitting any contribution from 0 to 20.",0.8,0,Endowment starts in private account; players choose contributions.,1,0.1333,"Same PGG parameters: multiplier 1.6, group size 12, MPCR=1.6/12.",0.8,0,No communication is described.,0.9,1,Players receive feedback on current and past performance measures for both communities.,1,0,Anonymity with changing IDs prevents reputation; identities of punishers are not tracked or revealed.,0.9,0,"This community has only punishment, not rewards.",1,0,The interface is not described as showing remaining periods.,0.5,1,Defined as punishment community in PR.,1,1,Each punishment point costs one token to the punisher.,1,3,Each punishment point reduces the target’s income by 3 tokens.,1,0,"P(PR) allows only punishment, reward community is separate.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in punishment community.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in punishment community.,1,20,Same PGG stage with 20-token endowment.,1,N/R,Paper reports that P(PN) has significantly higher average contributions than P(PR) and R(PR) but does not provide a numeric mean contribution for P(PR).,0,N/R,No explicit average contribution amount for P(PR) reported.,0,N/R,No normalized efficiency metric given for this community.,0,N/R,"Profits in R(PR) and P(PR) are compared statistically, but numeric mean payoff in P(PR) is not reported.",0,Germany,Study implemented in University of Hamburg lab.,0.9,N/R,No age statistics given.,0,N/R,Gender composition not discussed.,0,University students,Standard laboratory subject pool at a university; inferred.,0.6,On site,Sessions run in a university research lab.,1,"In PR, players can migrate between punishment and reward communities over 30 periods. Migration into P(PR) is gradual; threat of punishment suffices to maintain high contributions with relatively low actual use of punishment points, especially early on.",Based on description of migration patterns and use of punishment points in P(PR).,1
10.1016_j.econlet.2020.109155.md,PR – Reward community R(PR),"In PR treatment, R(PR) denotes the reward community, contrasted with punishment community P(PR).",1,Institutional regime in PR: punishment vs reward; this row: reward community R(PR),Between-community variation within PR is reward vs punishment; this condition uses rewards.,0.9,True,Same human-subject lab study.,1,True,Controlled experimental comparison of institutional regimes.,1,True,"Conducted at WISO Research Laboratory, University of Hamburg.",1,False,No simulation-only results.,1,False,Primarily empirical; no standalone analytical model.,1,12,12-person matching groups overall; communities form within these groups.,0.7,30,Game repeated 30 periods.,1,1,Same PGG decision structure; any amount between 0 and 20 can be contributed.,0.8,0,Endowment initially entirely private; contribution decision moves tokens into public good.,1,0.1333,Tokens contributed are multiplied by 1.6 and divided among the group of 12.,0.8,0,No communication tools are described.,0.9,1,"Players receive feedback for both communities including contributions, incomes, and sanctioning points.",1,0,Community uses rewards only; no punishment in R(PR).,1,0,"Anonymity with changing IDs; no reputation building, so identities of rewarders are not revealed.",0.9,0,No statement that interface shows round count or remaining periods.,0.5,0,R(PR) has a reward system instead of punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment in this community.,1,N/A,No punishment in this community.,1,1,R(PR) is defined as the reward community where players may assign positive points.,1,1,“In the reward system players may assign points… reducing their own income by one and increasing the income of targeted community members by 3 tokens.” Cost is 1 per reward point.,1,3,Each reward point increases recipient’s income by 3 tokens.,1,20,Participants receive 20-token endowment for the public good in each period.,1,0.595,Average contribution is 11.9 tokens; divided by 20-token endowment gives 11.9/20 ≈ 0.595.,0.9,11.9,“The reward community in our experiment achieves a relatively stable level of contribution at ca. 60% of the full endowment level (11.9).”,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0,N/R,"Paper notes that profits in R(PR) exceed those in P(PR) overall, but does not provide a numeric mean payoff for R(PR).",0,Germany,Lab located at University of Hamburg.,0.9,N/R,Age statistics not provided.,0,N/R,Gender breakdown not reported.,0,University students,Typical subject pool for a university lab; not explicitly stated.,0.6,On site,Lab sessions in a physical research laboratory.,1,"In PR, over 80% initially choose the reward community R(PR), peaking at about 90% around period 15 before some migrate to the punishment community. Reward points are frequently assigned early but decrease over time; in later periods, mainly high contributors reward others, generating income inequality and triggering migration.",Summary of migration and usage patterns of reward points described in Results.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2021.09.024.md,"Ghana – 1C (1-on-1, complete contract enforcement)",Treatment 1C is the 1-on-1 game with enforced contracts; Table 1 reports separate outcomes for Ghana sessions.,1,"Country = Ghana; Matching = 1-on-1; Enforcement = Complete (C); Reputation info = No; Market size = 1 employer, 1 worker",Directly from Sections 2.1–2.2 defining 1C and from implementation section specifying Ghana sample.,1,True,Human participants (students) in Ghana played repeated employer–worker games.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with predefined treatments.,1,True,Conducted in Osu (Accra) using a mobile lab platform (LabBox) in a controlled environment.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,"Paper is empirical; theory only for interpretation, not as a separate analytical study.",1,2,1-on-1 treatment involves one employer and one worker per game.,1,5,Each game is played for five periods.,1,1,"Employer chooses any non-negative wage; worker chooses among three discrete effort levels, not an all-or-nothing contribution. Coding 1 for ability to choose partial levels.",0.8,0,"Not a contribution game; workers start with an endowment in private account and choose costly effort. By convention for non-PGG labor games, use 0.",0.7,N/R,"Not a linear public goods game; employer revenue from effort is non-linear (5,20,40). MPCR not defined.",1,0,"No communication channel between subjects is mentioned; only wage/effort choices and optional praise/criticism are in a different study, not here.",0.9,1,At end of a period subjects see a summary of actions and associated payoffs for self and the matched partner; this is a summary of other’s actions/payoffs.,0.9,0,"No punishment stage or identity-linked sanctions; only wage changes, which are bilateral offers, not framed as punishment IDs.",1,0,No explicit reward stage beyond normal wage offers.,1,1,Game length of 5 periods is common knowledge as they discuss finitely repeated games and last-period behavior.,0.9,0,"No explicit costly punishment mechanism; wage reductions are endogenous contract terms, not a separate punishment technology.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism implemented.,1,0,No explicit costly reward stage beyond normal wages.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,130,"Each game: worker starts with 70 points, employer with 60 points; total per matched pair is 130. Using group endowment since contributions/effort affect joint surplus.",0.8,N/R,They report compliance and effort but not normalized as a fraction of an endowment.,0.3,N/R,"No single numeric average effort level reported for 1C; only wage, acceptance, compliance, and payoffs.",0.3,N/R,They discuss efficiency qualitatively; but no explicit ratio of realized to maximum surplus per treatment is provided.,0.3,N/R,"Table 1 gives separate employer and worker average payoffs but not total per pair; could be summed (12.8+16.2=29) but this is average, not clearly labeled as group payoff. Avoid computing as DV.",0.4,Ghana,This row is for Ghana sessions.,1,N/R,Age not reported; only that participants are students.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University/college students,Explicitly states participants were recruited among students from colleges and universities in Accra.,1,On site,"Physical tablet-based sessions in Accra, not online, and not natural field context.",1,"Gift-exchange style repeated employer–worker game framed as labor contract; 1 employer and 1 worker matched for a 5-period game, then rematched for 4 games total. Worker’s effort levels L,M,H produce employer benefits of 5,20,40 and worker costs of 0,2,6 respectively. In 1C, enforcement forces effort to equal requested level; worker can only accept/reject offer. Strategy-method rehiring schedule is still elicited but not behaviorally relevant for effort.",Summarized from Sections 2.1–2.3 and parameter description.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2021.09.024.md,"Ghana – 1E (1-on-1, unenforced contract, free effort)",Treatment 1E is 1-on-1 with free worker effort; Table 1 lists Ghana 1E separately.,1,"Country = Ghana; Matching = 1-on-1; Enforcement = Imperfect (E, worker free to choose effort); Reputation info = No; Market size = 1 employer, 1 worker",Section 2.2 defines 1E and notes Ghana implementation.,1,True,"Same subject pool and procedures as 1C, but different enforcement.",1,True,Randomized treatment in a controlled lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted in controlled tablet-based sessions in Accra.,1,False,"Human behavior, no pure simulations.",1,False,No stand-alone theoretical experiment.,1,2,1 employer + 1 worker per game.,1,5,Each game has five periods; they play four such games but the treatment is at game level.,1,1,Wage is continuous (>=0); effort is 3-level discrete; not all-or-nothing.,0.8,0,Same labor setting as 1C; no public endowment.,0.7,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication channel mentioned.,0.9,1,End-of-period summary of actions and payoffs shown.,0.9,0,No assigned punishment stage.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond wage.,1,1,"Game is finitely repeated and subjects’ end-game behavior is analyzed, implying known horizon.",0.9,0,No dedicated costly punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,130,Same per-game pair endowment (70 worker + 60 employer).,0.8,N/R,"They report compliance shares and regression of effort on wage, but not a normalized contribution metric.",0.3,N/R,"No single average effort level (e.g., numeric coding of L,M,H) is reported.",0.3,N/R,They present employer and worker average payoffs (Table 1) but no explicit efficiency ratio.,0.3,N/R,Could add average payoffs (0.0+13.1=13.1) but authors never label this as group payoff; avoid derived DV.,0.4,Ghana,Row restricted to Ghana sessions; treatment 1E is played in Ghana and UK separately.,1,N/R,Ages not provided.,0,N/R,No gender statistics.,0,University/college students,Same recruitment frame as 1C.,1,On site,Same Ghana LabBox sessions.,1,"Same employer–worker repeated game as 1C, but in 1E workers may choose low, medium, or high effort after accepting a contract, independently of the requested effort. Rehiring stage elicits employers’ conditional wage schedules for each possible effort and workers’ reservation wages; these can automatically determine next-period contracts if conditions are met.",Summarized from Section 2.1 and 2.2; differs from 1C only in enforcement of effort.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2021.09.024.md,"Ghana – 3C (3-on-3, complete contract enforcement)",Treatment 3C is the 3-on-3 game with enforced contracts; Table 1 reports Ghana 3C only.,1,"Country = Ghana; Matching = 3-on-3; Enforcement = Complete (C); Reputation info = No; Market size = 3 employers, 3 workers; Competition = Yes",Section 2.1–2.2 defines 3C as 3-on-3 with enforced effort; competition emerges via multiple employers and workers.,1,True,"Same subject pool in Ghana, different treatment.",1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of market size and enforcement.,1,True,Implemented in Ghana LabBox sessions.,1,False,All human subjects.,1,False,No pure theory experiment.,1,6,Three workers and three employers per game (3-on-3).,1,5,Each game still 5 periods.,1,1,Same wage and discrete effort choices as 1C/1E; not binary all-or-nothing.,0.8,0,"Labor contract, not a PGG.",0.7,N/R,No linear public good.,1,0,No communication tool mentioned; only wage/effort and market interface.,0.9,1,End-of-period summary screens for actions and payoffs still present.,0.9,0,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite 5-period game is known to subjects.,0.9,0,No formal costly punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment tech.,1,N/A,No punishment tech.,1,0,No additional reward beyond wage.,1,N/A,No reward tech.,1,N/A,No reward tech.,1,390,"Per game, 3 workers×70 = 210 and 3 employers×60 = 180, totaling 390 points per 3-on-3 group.",0.8,N/R,They report 100% compliance in 3C but do not translate to normalized contribution rate.,0.4,N/R,Requested effort distribution and actual induced effort (due to enforcement) not summarized numerically.,0.3,N/R,"Though 3C is essentially efficient conditional on requested effort, no explicit efficiency ratio is reported.",0.3,N/R,"Table 1 reports average per-employer and per-worker payoffs, but not aggregated per 6-player group.",0.4,Ghana,3C not run in UK; only Ghana sessions are reported.,1,N/R,Age not provided.,0,N/R,No gender data.,0,University/college students,Same Ghana student pool as other treatments.,1,On site,Same in-person sessions.,1,"3 employers simultaneously make offers to 3 workers, then see each other’s offers and can revise once. Workers are approached in random order to choose among offers; each employer can match with at most one worker and vice versa. In 3C, if a worker accepts, effort is forced equal to requested effort. After each period, strategy-method rehiring schedules and worker reservation wages are elicited; these can generate automatic continuation contracts in the next period.",Taken from detailed 3-on-3 description in Section 2.1 and Appendix A.,1
