10.1016_j.chaos.2022.112987.md,"Well-mixed (WM) population, baseline (no seeding), full 2D (r,c) sensitivity with institutions","Section 3.1 describes WM configuration with institutions over a sensitivity analysis on risk r and cost c, without seeding (Z* = ∅).",0.9,"Population structure = Well-mixed; Strategies = {C, P, D}; Institutions enabled; Risk r and cost c varied over grids; No seeding (Z* = ∅); Mutation μ = 0.01",WM is contrasted with network topologies while varying r and c; institutions as in baseline CRD with P players; explicitly no seeding in Section 3.,0.9,False,"Study uses Monte Carlo agent-based simulations with artificial agents, not human participants.",1,False,"This is a computational simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human subjects.",1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; purely computational.,1,True,Section 2.3: agent-based Monte Carlo simulations programmed in Java.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution; only numerical simulations of evolutionary dynamics.,1,5000,Section 2.3: population of Z = 5 × 10^3 in all experiments.,1,1000,Section 2.3: maximum number of 10^3 synchronous time steps.,1,1,"Players either pay cooperation cost c or not; contribution is a fixed cost decision rather than a continuous amount but mapped to 1 since they can choose between contributing or not, which is the only option defined.",0.6,N/A,Simulation agents do not have an initial allocation between public/private accounts in the usual PGG sense; they simply choose a strategy each round.,0.6,N/R,"Model is a collective-risk dilemma with probabilistic losses, not a linear PGG with a reported marginal per capita return.",1,N/A,No human communication; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents use payoffs directly without displayed summaries.,1,N/A,No identities are displayed; identities are irrelevant in the simulation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No human interface that displays remaining rounds.,1,1,Punishers pay tax π_t to create institutions and defectors pay fine π_f when conditions hold.,1,0.03,Section 2.3: contributing tax for punishers is set to π_t = 0.03.,1,0.3,Fine for defectors is π_f = 0.3; treated as punishment impact per punished defector.,0.9,0,"Only negative sanctions (punishment) are modeled, no rewards mentioned.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Section 2.1: initial endowment of each player is set to 1.,1,N/R,"Paper reports frequencies of strategies (C,P,D), group achievement η_G, and institution prevalence η_I, but not normalized contribution per player.",0.7,N/R,Model uses binary contribution cost c; aggregate contribution amounts are not tabulated.,0.7,N/R,No explicit measure of payoff efficiency relative to maximum is reported.,1,N/R,"Only group achievement η_G and institution prevalence η_I are reported, not numerical group payoffs.",1,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations on computer clusters.,1,"Collective-risk dilemma with group size N=4, risk of failure r if (j_C + j_P) < n_pg*N with n_pg=0.75; institution created if j_P >= n_p*N with n_p=0.25; cooperation cost c varied; intensity of selection β=5; mutation probability μ=0.01; 150 Monte Carlo realizations; results averaged over last 25% of 1000 steps.",All taken from Sections 2.1–2.3 and 3.1; parameters summarized.,1
