10.1016_j.amc.2023.128247.md,Model 1 – Public goods game with labor subsidy for executors (no corruption),"Paper defines this as the first public goods game model (Section 2.1) without corruption; all analyses in Sections 3.1, 4 (Figs. 1–2, 5(a), 5(c), 6(a)-(c)) use this model.",0.97,"Tax rate T, labor subsidy share α, reward-punishment weight β, group size N, synergy factor F, contribution cost c; executors always available; no corruption (p q = 0).","Model 1 payoffs (Eqs. 1–3, 9–11) explicitly include T, α, β, N, F, c; corruption parameters p, q, h, k do not appear, meaning no corruption.",0.96,False,"The study uses infinite well-mixed populations and replicator dynamics (Section 2.3), with no human participants or empirical data.",1,False,"This is a theoretical evolutionary game-theoretic analysis, not a controlled behavioral experiment.",1,False,No lab or field environment; purely mathematical modeling and simulations.,1,True,"Section 4 states that the Runge–Kutta method (ode45 in Matlab) is used to solve the ODEs and obtain numerical results (Figs. 1–2, 5(a), 5(c), 6(a)-(c)).",1,True,"Sections 2–3 provide closed-form payoff expressions, derive replicator equations, equilibrium points, and stability theorems analytically.",1,5,"Parameter values for figures using Model 1 specify N = 5 (e.g., Fig. 1, 2, 5, 6).",0.95,1000,Section 4: simulations run until steady state and note 't = 1000 with over 4000 time steps'; t is the continuous-time horizon; treated here as number of simulated rounds.,0.7,1,"Model assumes a fixed contribution cost c for cooperators and executors; decision is whether to cooperate/execute or defect, not a continuous contribution level; coded as all-or-nothing.",0.8,0,Standard public goods formulation: individuals start with private endowment and may contribute cost c; no automatic allocation to the common pool.,0.7,0.6,"Marginal per capita return = F / N. For figures, F = 3 and N = 5 (e.g., Fig. 1 caption), so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.95,0,No communication or discussion among individuals is mentioned; only strategy frequencies evolve via replicator dynamics.,0.95,0,Theoretical infinite-population model; individuals do not observe explicit summaries of others’ payoffs; only payoffs enter replicator dynamics.,0.9,0,No notion of identity; population is anonymous and well-mixed; nothing indicates identity revelation.,0.95,0,"As with punishment, rewards are pooled and anonymous in the model.",0.95,0,Agents are not modeled as knowing or reacting to a finite time horizon; replicator dynamics have infinite time without explicit round information.,0.95,1,Executors fine defectors using part of tax revenue; punishment term appears in π_D^1 with factor (1−β)(1−α)NT γ_P.,1,1,Section 2.1: parameters γ_R and γ_P (reward and punishment leverage) are set to 1; this implies one unit of punishment per unit of resource from the pool; interpreted as a 1:1 cost–impact factor.,0.7,1,"With γ_P = 1 and costs invested equal to fines harvested, each unit paid from the execution pool yields one unit of punishment impact; coded as 1.",0.7,1,Executors reward cooperators; reward term in π_C^1 includes (1−α)βNT γ_R.,1,1,"Section 2.1: γ_R = 1, costs invested equal rewards harvested; interpreted analogously to punishment as 1 unit cost per 1 unit reward.",0.7,1,"With γ_R = 1 and cost equals benefit to cooperators, technology multiplier per unit resource is 1.",0.7,N/R,Model specifies contribution cost c = 1 but does not define a total endowment or wealth per player.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports steady fractions of strategy types (ρ_C, ρ_D, ρ_E) but not normalized contribution rates relative to an endowment.",0.2,N/R,No aggregate or per-round contribution amounts are reported; only parameter c and strategy frequencies.,0.2,N/R,Total group payoff and maximum feasible payoff are not numerically summarized; no efficiency ratios given.,0.1,N/R,"Expected payoffs P_C^1, P_D^1, P_E^1 are given symbolically, but no specific numeric group payoff is reported as a result metric.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is purely analytical and simulation-based without human data.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population public goods game with three strategies: cooperators, defectors, executors. Cooperators and executors pay cost c to common pool; contributions multiplied by F and equally shared. All players pay tax T; fraction α of NT returned to executors as labor subsidy, remaining (1−α)NT split between reward (β) and punishment (1−β) pools. Executors reward cooperators and punish defectors with leverage γ_R = γ_P = 1. Evolution governed by replicator dynamics in continuous time, numerically solved by Runge–Kutta (ode45) until steady state.","Summarized from Sections 2.1, 2.3, and 4.",0.99
10.1016_j.amc.2023.128247.md,Model 2 – Public goods game with labor subsidy and corruption mechanism,"Section 2.2 introduces a second model that extends Model 1 by allowing corruption (bribery) between defectors and executors; analyses in Sections 3.2, 4 (Figs. 3–4, 5(b), 5(d), 6(d)-(f)) use this.",0.97,"Tax rate T, labor subsidy share α, reward-punishment weight β, group size N, synergy factor F, contribution cost c, effective bribery probability pq, bribe cost k, illegal income h.","Model 2 payoff formulas (Eqs. 4–6, 29–31) add parameters p, q, k, h to the same core parameters as Model 1; simulations specify pq, k, h in captions (e.g., Fig. 3).",0.97,False,No human subjects; infinite population and replicator dynamics.,1,False,"The study is theoretical with numerical ODE solutions, not an experimental manipulation with participants.",1,False,No laboratory or field setting; simulations only.,1,True,"Section 4 describes numerical solution via Runge–Kutta (ode45) for the extended system (Eqs. 33–35), producing Figs. 3–4, 5(b), 5(d), 6(d)-(f).",1,True,"Section 3.2 derives expected payoffs, equilibrium existence (Theorem 4), and stability conditions (Theorem 5) analytically.",1,5,"For simulations with corruption they keep N = 5 (see captions of Figs. 3, 4, 5, 6).",0.95,1000,Same simulation procedure as Model 1: 't = 1000 with over 4000 time steps' defines the simulation horizon.,0.7,1,"As in Model 1, players choose strategies C, D, or E, each implying a fixed cost pattern; no partial contributions.",0.8,0,Structure of the public goods interaction is unchanged from Model 1: contributions are voluntary costs paid into a common pool.,0.7,0.6,"MPCR = F / N; corruption model uses same F = 3, N = 5 (e.g., Fig. 3 caption), so 3/5.",0.95,0,No communication among individuals is modeled; only strategies and payoffs with bribery.,0.95,0,"Infinite population, anonymous replicator dynamics; individuals do not see summaries.",0.9,0,"No identities or pairwise observability introduced when corruption is added; bribes depend on probabilities, not on knowing who punished whom.",0.95,0,Rewards remain pooled and anonymous as in Model 1.,0.95,0,Agents are not modeled with knowledge of or behavior contingent on a finite or known horizon.,0.95,1,"Punishment of defectors remains, but is weakened by corruption; π_D^2 includes a punishment term scaled by (1−pq) and bribery term −pq N_E k.",1,1,"γ_P is unchanged from Model 1 and set to 1; corruption changes whether punishment is applied, not its per-unit technology.",0.7,1,Same reward/punishment leverage γ_P = 1; cost from punishment pool translates 1:1 into fines when punishment occurs.,0.7,1,Reward for cooperators remains as in Model 1 (π_C^2 includes reward term).,1,1,γ_R remains 1; cost from reward pool yields equivalent reward units.,0.7,1,Reward technology is unchanged by corruption and uses γ_R = 1.,0.7,N/R,"As in Model 1, only contribution cost c is specified; no total endowment per player is defined.",0.3,N/R,"Outcomes are given in terms of fractions of strategies (ρ_C, ρ_D, ρ_E) and not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,No explicit aggregate contribution amounts under corruption are reported.,0.2,N/R,"Although payoffs with corruption are defined, the paper does not present explicit efficiency metrics or ratios.",0.1,N/R,No numerical group payoff results are tabulated; focus is on equilibrium composition and cooperation levels.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The corruption-extended model is likewise analyzed only via mathematics and simulations.,1,"Same base public goods structure as Model 1 (N-player PGG with tax-based reward and punishment and labor subsidy to executors), plus corruption: each defector chooses to bribe with probability p, paying cost k, and each executor accepts bribe h with probability q; effective bribery probability is pq. If bribery succeeds, defectors avoid punishment and executors obtain illegal income h. Payoffs π_C^2, π_D^2, π_E^2 incorporate both reduced expected punishment for defectors and additional illegal income for executors. Evolution is governed by replicator dynamics; numerical integration uses Runge–Kutta (ode45) until steady state, with initial condition (x, y, z) = (0.2, 0.6, 0.2) in figures.","Summarized from Section 2.2, corresponding replicator system in 3.2, and simulation descriptions in Section 4 (Figs. 3–4, 5(b), 5(d), 6(d)-(f)).",0.99
10.1016_j.jesp.2024.104695.md,Study 1 – Direct punishment condition,Study 1 has a between-subjects factor (punishment type) with a direct punishment condition; this row captures that condition.,1,Punishment type: direct punishment (participant decides whether to deduct 300 yen from distributor after each observed distribution),Only manipulation in Study 1 is punishment type (direct vs no punishment). This row corresponds to direct punishment.,1,True,Human participants recruited via Lancers completed the SS-TPPG.,1,True,Random assignment to punishment conditions and controlled task structure.,1,False,"Data collected via online crowdsourcing platform, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model as a standalone method.,1,1,Each participant makes individual choices about pre-recorded interactions between a distributor and recipient; no strategic interaction among multiple participants.,0.7,30,"“In the sequence of decision-making tasks, participants engaged in 30 iterations…”",1,1,Not a contribution game; there is no choice of continuous contribution level.,1,N/A,No public goods or contribution structure; third-party punishment over dictator-like allocations.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no multiplier defined.,1,0,No possibility of communication among participants is mentioned.,1,0,Participants only see one distribution per trial; no summaries of others’ earnings or past punishments are described.,0.9,0,Identities of distributors/recipients are not given; only anonymous roles are displayed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in this study; thus identities cannot be revealed for rewards.,1,1,Participants were informed of the total number of trials before beginning the task.,1,1,Direct punishment condition explicitly allows deduction of 300 yen from the distributor.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost is time/effort via a real-effort task, not a numeric token/monetary unit; no per-punishment numeric cost is specified.",0.3,N/R,"Punishment deducts 300 yen from the distributor, but there is no numeric mapping of punished amount per unit of cost (as units are not numeric tokens).",0.4,0,No mechanism to increase the distributor’s payoff is described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,1000,Distributors ‘had received 1000 yen and had to decide whether to give 500 or 100 yen to the recipients.’,1,N/R,"Not a contribution game; primary DV is choice rate of unfair deck and punishment rate, not contribution.",1,N/R,No contributions are made by participants.,1,N/R,No group payoff or efficiency metric is reported or derivable from description.,0.2,N/R,"Only individual behavior metrics (deck choice, punishment) are reported; no numeric payoffs across players are provided.",0.2,Japan,Participants had Japanese as their first language and were recruited via a Japanese crowdsourcing service; authors are at a Japanese university.,0.9,"23–77 (M = 44.39, SD = 9.70)",Reported in Study 1 Participants and design section.,1,"179 male, 101 female, 0 other",Reported in Study 1 Participants and design section.,1,N/R,No education information reported.,1,Online,Participants recruited via the online crowdsourcing service Lancers; no physical lab mentioned.,1,"Situation-Selective Third-Party Punishment Game (SS-TPPG): each trial, participant selects between two decks (80% fair/20% unfair vs 20% fair/80% unfair allocations of 1000 yen as 500 or 100 to recipient). After observing distribution, participant chooses whether to punish (deduct 300 yen from distributor), with real-effort typing task cost proportional to number of punishments. 30 trials; deck fairness probabilities are known; distributions are pre-determined and fictitious.",Summary of core task features from Study 1 method section.,1
