ions (500 vs 100 to recipient from 1000). After observing the distribution, participant reports recipient’s amount; no punishment option.",Derived from Study 2 methods for no punishment condition.,1
10.1016_j.jesp.2024.104695.md,Study 3 – Direct punishment condition,Study 3 includes three conditions; this row covers the direct punishment condition.,1,Punishment type: direct punishment; card selection mode each trial (self-choice vs random option) as in Study 2,"Between-subject manipulation is punishment type: direct, indirect, or none.",1,True,Human participants recruited via online crowdsourcing platform.,1,True,Random assignment to three punishment-type conditions; controlled procedure.,1,False,Experiment done online using Lancers.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical game-theoretic model.,1,1,Individual-level decision-making over pre-set distributions.,0.7,20,"Study 3 uses the SS-TPPG variant from Study 2, with 20 trials.",0.9,1,No contribution choice; binary punishment choices only.,0.7,N/A,No contribution to a public account.,1,N/A,No public goods or multiplier.,1,0,No interaction between participants.,1,0,No cumulative earnings or punishment summaries shown.,0.9,0,No identifying information about distributors/recipients mentioned.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Procedure mirrors Study 2 where total number of trials is known; no change indicated.,0.9,1,Direct punishment condition allows participants to deduct from distributors as in prior studies.,1,N/R,"Assumed same real-effort cost structure as Studies 1–2, but numeric per-punishment cost not specified.",0.3,N/R,Text states direct punishment option but does not restate numerical deduction per punishment for Study 3.,0.3,0,No reward mechanism mentioned in any Study 3 condition.,1,N/A,Reward not available.,1,N/A,Reward not available.,1,1000,SS-TPPG structure carried over from Studies 1–2; Study 3 section implies same distributions.,0.9,N/R,Primary DVs: expected unfair deck choice rates and punishment rates.,1,N/R,Participants do not contribute tokens.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,0.2,N/R,Payoffs are not calculated or reported.,0.2,Japan,Participants are Japanese and recruited via same Japanese crowdsourcing service.,0.9,"21–76 (M = 43.19, SD = 11.02)",Reported in Study 3 Participants and design section.,1,"188 male, 138 female, 4 other",Reported in Study 3 Participants and design section.,1,N/R,Education not mentioned.,1,Online,Same crowdsourcing platform (Lancers) in mid-January 2024.,1,"Study 3 uses SS-TPPG with same deck structure as Study 2 (fair deck 80% fair/20% unfair vs unfair deck 20% fair/80% unfair; allocations of 500 vs 100 from 1000-yen endowment). 20 trials with option each trial to choose deck or use random option; expected unfair deck choice rate computed using 1.0, 0.0, and 0.5 probabilities for unfair deck depending on choice. Direct punishment condition: after observing distribution, participant can directly punish the distributor (details of cost and deduction presumed same as previous studies).",Synthesized from Study 3 description plus references to earlier SS-TPPG variants.,0.9
10.1016_j.jesp.2024.104695.md,Study 3 – Indirect punishment condition,Study 3 adds an indirect punishment condition distinct from direct and no punishment.,1,Punishment type: indirect punishment (option to give negative evaluation about distributor affecting future interactions; no explicit material cost); card selection mode (self-choice vs random option),"Between-subject manipulation is punishment type; here, participants can administer indirect punishment.",1,True,Uses human participants performing SS-TPPG tasks.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to indirect punishment vs other conditions.,1,False,Run via online crowdsourcing platform.,1,False,No simulation models.,1,False,No analytical solution used as main method.,1,1,Individual-level play against pre-set scenarios.,0.7,20,"Study 3 states SS-TPPG modified from Study 2, which used 20 trials; no different number mentioned for indirect punishment.",0.9,1,No contribution decision exists.,0.7,N/A,No endowment allocation decisions by participants.,1,N/A,Not a public goods structure.,1,0,No communication channels.,1,0,Only current trial info; no past summary described.,0.9,0,"Although participants believe feedback will be passed to future participants, no personal identities for current distributors are shown.",0.8,0,Only negative evaluations are mentioned as indirect punishment; no reward option.,1,1,Same SS-TPPG structure as in Study 2 where total trials known; Study 3 does not indicate otherwise.,0.9,1,Participants can express dislike/negative evaluation of distributors which is framed as potentially affecting future interactions (indirect punishment).,1,0,Text explicitly notes that indirect punishment condition ‘did not involve explicit costs akin to those in the direct punishment condition.’,0.9,N/R,Effect of negative evaluation on distributor’s future outcomes is not quantified.,0.4,0,Indirect punishment condition only mentions negative evaluations as sanctions; no mention of positive evaluations used as rewards in the main task.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism in the main SS-TPPG.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1000,Indirect punishment is layered on same SS-TPPG distribution structure from previous studies (1000-yen endowment).,0.9,N/R,No contributions; DVs are expected unfair deck choice rate and indirect punishment rates.,1,N/R,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,No payoff efficiency metric reported.,0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoffs provided.,0.2,Japan,Same Japanese crowdsourcing platform; participants described as Japanese.,0.9,"21–76 (M = 43.19, SD = 11.02)",Study 3 demographics apply across all three conditions.,1,"188 male, 138 female, 4 other",Study 3 reports aggregate gender across all conditions.,1,N/R,No education details.,1,Online,Conducted via same crowdsourcing platform as previous studies.,1,"SS-TPPG with 20 trials and same deck probabilities as Study 2 (fair vs unfair deck, 80/20 vs 20/80 fair/unfair distributions of 1000 yen as 500 vs 100 allocation). Each trial starts with choice between self-selecting a deck or random selection (0.5 probability of unfair deck). In the indirect punishment condition, after viewing the distribution, participant can express dislike/negative evaluation of the distributor. They are told this feedback will be conveyed to future participants and may affect how those participants interact with the distributor. No explicit monetary or effort cost is attached to giving negative evaluations.",Summarizes indirect punishment mechanism and task structure from Study 3 SS-TPPG modifications.,1
