on not reported.,1,Online,Study 3 conducted via Lancers crowdsourcing service.,1,"Same SS-TPPG as other Study 3 conditions: 20 trials, option each trial between self-selection of deck vs random selection; decks differ in probability of fair vs unfair allocations of a 1000-yen endowment (500 vs 100). In the no punishment condition, after observing distribution, participants do not have any direct or indirect punishment option; core task is to observe and (by analogy with prior no punishment conditions) report or acknowledge the recipient’s amount.","Study 3 states that no punishment condition is analogous to prior studies but with same structural modifications (random option, indirect punishment info removed).",0.8
10.1007_s12144-023-05029-7.md,Study 1 – Within-subject 2×2×2 design (all conditions),"Study 1 is a single repeated-measures design where all 38 participants experience all combinations of distributor identity (ingroup/outgroup), recipient identity (ingroup/outgroup), and fairness (equal/unfair); parameters of the game do not vary across these conditions, only the social identities and allocation fairness do.",0.96,"Within-subject factors: distributor's identity (ingroup vs. outgroup), recipient's identity (ingroup vs. outgroup), fairness (equal vs. unequal allocation).",Abstract and methods explicitly describe a 2 (distributor's identity: ingroup vs. outgroup) × 2 (recipient's identity: ingroup vs. outgroup) × 2 (fairness: equal vs. unequal) within-subjects design.,1,True,Human psychology students played the Third-Party Punishment Game.,1,True,Participants were randomly exposed to controlled game stimuli with manipulated identities and fairness.,1,False,"Data were collected remotely at participants’ homes using E-Prime Go, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Game opponents were simulated, but focal data are from human participants.",1,False,No analytical or purely theoretical model; empirical behavioral data only.,1,3,"Third-Party Punishment Game is explicitly described as involving player A, player B, and player C.",1,20,"Methods: 'The game consists of 20 rounds (16 unfair allocations, 4 equal allocations)'.",1,1,"Player C 'is given an endowment that s/he can either spend all or part of it on punishing player A or keep it all', implying any integer amount 0–20.",0.95,0,"This is a punishment game, not a public-goods game; there is no contribution to a public account, so endowment starts entirely private.",1,N/R,No public good or linear return is defined; only punishment technology is specified.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat between participants; interaction is only via programmed screens.,0.95,0,Participants only see A’s decision and identities each round; no summary of others’ cumulative earnings or punishments is described.,0.9,0,Only department labels (ingroup vs outgroup) of A and B are shown; no personal identity of punisher C is revealed to others.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in this game.,1,0,Paper does not state that remaining or total number of rounds is displayed on screen.,0.5,1,Core of the design is costly third-party punishment by player C.,1,1,Player C chooses 'a number between 0 and 20' from her 20-unit endowment; each unit spent is a unit of cost to C.,1,3,Three times the portion that player C spent is deleted from player A’s endowment.,1,0,Only deletion of A’s points is described; no positive transfer to A or B.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"Player C: 'an endowment that s/he can either spend all or part of it ... choose a number between 0 and 20', so endowment is 20 units.",1,N/R,No normalized contribution rate is reported; only punishment amounts are given.,1,9.15,Mean punishment in unfair conditions is M=9.15; we treat the amount spent on punishment as the 'contribution' to norm enforcement.,0.9,N/R,No total payoffs or efficiency metrics are reported.,1,N/R,Group monetary outcomes are not reported.,1,Turkey,Participants were 'undergraduate psychology students at Mersin University in Turkey'.,1,Mean 20.68 years (SD = 1.16; range 18–23),Participant section of Study 1 reports these age statistics.,1,"20 female, 18 male",Participant section explicitly states gender counts.,1,Undergraduate psychology students,Sample is described as undergraduate psychology students at Mersin University.,1,Online,"Data were 'remotely collected'; participants used E-Prime Go at home, not on-site lab.",1,"Third-Party Punishment Game with Player A holding 40 units and deciding allocation to B; Player C holds 20 units and can spend 0–20 units to delete 3× spent units from A’s endowment. 20 rounds: 16 unfair allocations (A keeps 36–39, B gets 1–4 of 40) and 4 equal allocations (20–20 split). Identities: ingroup (Psychology) vs outgroup (English Language and Literature). One-shot interactions across rounds with new (simulated) A and B each round.",Summarized directly from the Study 1 methods description of the Third-Party Punishment Game and identity manipulations.,1
10.1007_s12144-023-05029-7.md,"Study 2 – Ingroup distributor, ingroup recipient",Study 2 uses a 2 (recipient’s identity: ingroup/outgroup) × 2 (distributor’s identity: ingroup/outgroup) between-subjects factor crossed with fairness within-subject; this row captures the condition where both distributor (A) and recipient (B) are ingroup (psychology students).,0.95,Between-subject: distributor's identity = ingroup; recipient's identity = ingroup. Within-subject: fairness of allocation (unfair vs. equal).,Study 2 methods: '2 (recipient's identity: ingroup or outgroup) x 2 (distributor's identity: ingroup or outgroup) x 2 (fairness of allocations: unfair or equal)' ANCOVA with the last factor repeated.,1,True,Undergraduate students participated in an online third-party punishment game.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of social identities and fairness in a game setting.,1,False,"Data collected remotely in participants’ own environments using E-Prime, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,"Opponents are simulated, but focal data are from human player C.",1,False,No purely analytical model; behaviorally measured responses only.,1,3,"Same Third-Party Punishment Game structure as Study 1: players A, B, and C.",1,8,"Study 2 game: 'The eight-round game consists of 4 rounds of unfair (TRY 1,2,3, and 4) and 4 rounds of equal (TRY 20TL) allocation'.",1,1,"As in Study 1, player C can spend 'tickets' in any amount from 0 to 20 each round (implicitly same choice set).",0.8,0,Endowment consists of raffle tickets privately held by player C; there is no public account.,1,N/R,No public good or marginal per capita return is defined; only punishment effect on A’s payoff.,1,0,No communication between players is mentioned.,0.9,0,Participants see A’s decision and identities; no cumulative summary displays described.,0.9,0,Only group membership labels of A and B appear; C’s identity is not revealed.,0.9,0,There is no reward mechanism.,1,0,No indication that the interface displays remaining or total rounds.,0.5,1,Core of the game is costly third-party punishment by spending raffle tickets.,1,1,Player C spends raffle tickets; each ticket spent is a unit of cost to C by reducing their own chance to win.,1,3,"As in Study 1: 'spending a ticket would deduct a portion of TRY 3 from player A's budget', i.e., 3 per ticket.",1,0,No option to reward A or B is described.,1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,N/A,No reward option exists.,1,20,Participants: '20 tickets would be allocated to them for this raffle in each round'.,1,N/R,No normalized fraction of endowment spent on punishment is reported by condition.,1,6.69,"For unfair allocations to ingroup recipients, mean punishment is reported as M = 6.69 (SD = 4.11); this condition has ingroup recipients, matching this row. Equal allocations M=1.58. We report the unfair-case mean as the main contribution/punishment amount.",0.95,N/R,No group payoff or efficiency ratios are reported.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoff outcomes are provided.,1,Turkey,Participants are undergraduate psychology students at several universities in Turkey (authors’ affiliations and context).,0.95,Mean 21.16 years (SD = 2.82; range 18–45),Study 2 participant section reports these age statistics.,1,"139 female, 35 male",Study 2 participant section explicitly provides these counts.,1,Undergraduate psychology students,Participants described as undergraduate psychology students at several universities.,1,Online,Data were 'remotely participated'; experiment run via E-Prime on participants’ computers.,1,"Third-Party Punishment Game framed with raffle tickets: in each round, player C receives 20 tickets that determine chances in a raffle for five prizes of TRY 50. Player A has 20 TRY to allocate between self and B (unfair: A keeps 19–16, B gets 1–4; equal: 10–10 or equivalently 'TRY 20TL' to B as stated). Spending a ticket both reduces C’s raffle chance and deducts TRY 3 per ticket from A’s budget. Eight total rounds (4 unfair, 4 equal) in random order. Identities: distributor (A) and recipient (B) can each be ingroup (psychology) or outgroup (other department) between subjects. Participants are told allocation between A and B is independent of the raffle to separate motives.","Summarizes Study 2 methods: raffle framing, ticket endowment, punishment technology, and identity manipulations.",0.95
