10.1016_j.amc.2022.127578.md,Analytical invasion-interface model – expected invasion difference E(C←D)−E(D←C),"Condition in Fig. 7: analytical mini spatial model of invasion along interface, computing expected invasion difference as function of r and p_C.",0.94,Synergy factor r and cooperator proposal level p_C varied; defector proposal level fixed at p_D=0; acceptance thresholds satisfy 0 ≤ q_D ≤ p_D < q_C ≤ p_C ≤ 0.5; noise level κ=0.1 fixed.,"Fig. 7 description: E as function of parameters r and p_C; they set p_D=0, κ=0.1 and constrain q_D, q_C relative to p_D, p_C.",0.98,False,No human subjects.,1,False,"Derivation and/or numerical evaluation of invasion probabilities, not an empirical or agent-based experiment in the main lattice.",0.95,False,No lab context.,1,False,"Described as a mini spatial analytical model focusing on expected invasion difference; does not specify running Monte Carlo over full lattice, but rather computing E based on local configuration.",0.7,True,They derive expected difference E(C←D)−E(D←C) at an interface analytically as function of parameters; figure shows phase boundaries from these calculations.,0.85,8,Interface configuration shown in inset panel: a small cluster of cooperators at a corner with surrounding defectors; typical local neighborhood for invasion calculation likely includes focal and its nearest neighbors (~8 sites in Moore neighborhood). Approximated as 8 interacting sites.,0.3,1,Model evaluates single-step invasion probabilities at an interface rather than repeated-round dynamics.,0.7,1,Underlying game is binary cooperate vs defect with bargaining; same structure as main model.,0.7,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,MPCR is not stated; r is used directly.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,0,Analytical invasion model uses only local configuration; no information summaries.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment stage is separately modeled; fairness-driven rejection is embedded in payoff structure.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,0,Single-step invasion calculation; no notion of displayed rounds.,0.9,1,Rejection of unfair offers within the payoff structure is still interpreted as punishment.,0.8,N/R,No explicit numerical punishment cost parameter specified in this analytical model.,0.3,N/R,No explicit punishment technology parameter.,0.3,0,No separate rewarding mechanism is modeled.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment is not explicitly parameterized in the invasion calculation.,0.3,N/R,"Outcome is invasion advantage E, not average cooperation rate in a population.",0.4,N/R,"Model focuses on invasion probabilities, not raw contributions.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency metric; only invasion advantage sign E>0 or E<0.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff not reported; emphasis is on invasion boundaries.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical/numerical calculation of invasion in a theoretical model; no humans.,1,"Mini spatial model of invasion along the interface of cooperator and defector domains: cooperator at corner with strategy [1,p_C,q_C], surrounding defectors [0,p_D,q_D] with p_D=0 and 0≤q_D≤p_D<q_C≤p_C≤0.5; uses Fermi imitation with κ=0.1 to compute expected difference E=E(C←D)−E(D←C); phase boundaries in (r,p_C) plane show when cooperators have invasion advantage vs defectors, contrasted against classical PD boundary independent of p_C; illustrates that lower p_C (more unfair cooperator offers) can reduce r-threshold for cooperation when r is below classical boundary but can hinder cooperation at high p_C.",Summarized from description of Fig. 7.,0.99
10.1016_j.socec.2023.102081.md,Meta-analysis pooled baseline (no hierarchical design variable active),"Represents the standard, leaderless PGG baseline conditions used for comparison in the meta-analysis when design variables (leadership-related mechanisms) are absent.",0.86,"Hierarchical design variables absent (no punishment, no reward, no monitoring, symmetric information, simultaneous moves, random leader selection not applied, partner matching as default when applicable)",Baseline corresponds to standard PGG without hierarchical mechanisms; text repeatedly contrasts each moderator’s ‘present’ condition with a ‘baseline’ or ‘absent’ condition.,0.74,True,All included source studies are incentivized lab(-in-the-field) experiments with human participants per Section 3.1.,1,True,Meta-analysis only includes controlled PGG experiments with treatments and baselines.,1,True,Eligibility requires ‘controlled lab environment (including lab-in-the-field experiments)’; no field-only or observational designs are included.,0.97,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations are described; all results stem from human-subject experiments.,1,False,"Although analytical formulas are discussed, the meta-analysis is not based on purely analytical models.",1,4,"Group sizes range from 3–8 (Table 1, Table 2). A single representative value is needed; 4-person groups are by far the most frequent (most k in Table 2).",0.55,10,Number of iterations ranges from 1 to 30; 10 rounds is the modal value in Table 2 (15 effect sizes). Used as typical for baseline.,0.55,1,"Canonical PGGs in referenced literature (standard baseline Eq. (1)) allow continuous choice of contribution from 0 up to endowment, not all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Standard PGG baseline: endowment starts in private account and players decide how much to move to public good.,0.96,N/R,MPCR is mentioned conceptually but specific numerical values across the meta-analysis sample are not reported.,0.2,0,"Communication is not described as part of included hierarchical PGG designs; communication appears only in an external reference (Kingsley & Muise, 2018) that is not necessarily in the final meta sample.",0.4,1,Standard PGGs and hierarchical variants report others’ contributions/earnings so that leaders can monitor or punish; payoff feedback is necessary. Assume summaries/feedback are shown.,0.6,0,Meta-analysis does not indicate that identities of punished subjects are revealed; typical lab PGGs use anonymity.,0.3,0,"Similarly, identity of rewarded subjects is typically not individually revealed; not reported in text.",0.3,1,"Standard repeated PGGs normally inform subjects of total number of rounds; not stated, but common protocol.",0.5,0,Baseline is the ‘no punishment’ or ‘absent’ condition against which punishment-present treatments are compared (Section 4.1).,0.98,N/A,Punishment mechanism is absent in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment in baseline.,1,0,Baseline for reward comparisons is when rewards are absent (Section 4.1.2).,0.98,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,Endowment E is described conceptually but no numerical value is reported for the pooled meta-analysis.,0.2,0.5,"Text: baseline contributions typically decline from ‘around 50% to 18%’ over time in Fig. 1 description. Taking the early-round level as characteristic baseline cooperation, set to 0.5.",0.6,N/R,"Only percentages of endowment are reported, not raw token amounts.",0.2,N/R,Meta-analysis focuses on contribution levels; no direct or derivable efficiency ratios are reported at the pooled level.,0.1,N/R,Pooled meta-analysis does not report aggregate payoffs across studies.,0.1,"Mixed (China, Germany, Denmark, France, India, Norway, United States, United Kingdom)",Table 1 lists CO codes and country names; meta-analysis deliberately pools 7 countries. UK appears in O’Gorman et al.; Norway in Cappelen et al.,1,N/R,Meta-analysis does not report participant age ranges; individual papers are not detailed here.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition data reported.,0.1,Likely university students; exact distribution N/R,"Most PGG lab experiments use student samples, but the paper does not explicitly summarize education levels for the meta sample.",0.4,On site,All included studies are ‘controlled lab environment (including lab-in-the-field)’ conducted before/around 2020; on-site labs are the norm. No mention of fully online experiments.,0.7,"Canonical linear public goods game baseline: subjects receive an endowment each round, choose how much to contribute to a public account, total contributions multiplied by factor G>1 and divided equally among N players; no hierarchical roles or leader discretion over allocation.",Matches Eq. (1) and description of ‘standard baseline PGG’ used for controls in the meta-analysis.,0.95
10.1016_j.socec.2023.102081.md,Meta-analysis pooled punishment-present hierarchical treatments,"Represents all treatment conditions across the 20 studies in which a centralized punishment mechanism is available to a leader/manager, as coded under moderator ‘Punishment present’.",0.96,"Punishment present (centralized punishment mechanism available to leader/manager; other hierarchical variables as in underlying studies, mixed)",Section 4.1: ‘design variable of punishment… stronger when punishment is present…’ with d and N reported for ‘Present’ vs ‘Absent’.,0.96,True,Same empirical human-subject dataset as baseline; punishment is a treatment variation.,1,True,All source conditions are controlled lab experiments with manipulated punishment availability.,1,True,Eligibility required lab(-in-the-field) environment.,0.97,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,Not a purely analytical treatment; empirical data only.,1,4,Punishment-present treatments span several group sizes (3–8). 4-person groups are most common (Table 2).,0.55,16,"In punishment analysis, contributions are discussed ‘to round 16’ (Fig. 1) indicating many punishment studies use 16 rounds; chosen as representative.",0.6,1,All underlying PGGs use continuous contribution decisions.,0.9,0,"Same canonical PGG structure as baseline: endowment in private account, choose contribution.",0.96,N/R,MPCR values for included punishment studies are not reported in the meta-summary.,0.2,0,"Punishment treatments described do not emphasize communication; design focus is on punishment rights, not chat.",0.4,1,Leaders must observe others’ contributions to punish free riders; implies feedback/summary information.,0.8,0,"Centralized punisher knows whom to punish, but identities are typically anonymous to other players; paper does not detail identity revelation.",0.35,N/A,"Conditions are defined by presence of punishment, not rewards; assume no reward option unless noted, so reward identity visibility is not applicable.",0.7,1,Repeated games with known finite horizon are standard; text assumes awareness of ‘round 16’ etc.,0.6,1,By definition of punishment-present moderator: ‘when punishment is present’.,1,N/R,"Meta-analysis later distinguishes ‘costly vs. free punishment’, indicating heterogeneity; this pooled punishment-present condition mixes both, so a single numeric cost cannot be specified.",0.2,N/R,Magnitude of punishment per token spent not summarized across studies.,0.2,0,"This moderator isolates punishment presence; some underlying studies may also allow rewards, but the meta-description of the punishment analysis does not specify concurrent rewards. To avoid over-assumption, set to absent.",0.3,N/A,Reward mechanism not assumed active in the punishment-present meta-contrast.,0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled for this pooled punishment-present analysis.,0.7,N/R,No pooled endowment level reported.,0.2,0.7,"Section 4.1: with punishment, contributions ‘from around 55% in the first round to approximately 70% by round 16, thereafter declining to around 65% in the final round’. A characteristic level is about 70% of endowment.",0.7,N/R,Only percentages shown; no raw token values pooled.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not numerically summarized for punishment vs baseline.,0.1,N/R,Pooled payoffs not reported.,0.1,"Mixed (China, Germany, Denmark, France, India, Norway, United States, United Kingdom)",Punishment-present treatments are a subset of the 20-study sample spanning these countries.,1,N/R,Not summarized in meta-analysis.,0.1,N/R,Not summarized.,0.1,Likely university students; exact distribution N/R,Standard for lab experiments; not explicitly summarized.,0.4,On site,Same lab(-in-the-field) context as baseline.,0.97,"Linear public goods game with centralized punishment: after contribution stage, a designated leader/manager can impose monetary penalties on low contributors (e.g., exclusion from next round or token fines), often at personal cost or using a punishment budget, with remaining payoffs then realized.","Description in Section 2.1.1 (e.g., O’Gorman et al., Andreoni & Gee, etc.) and Section 4.1 generalizes the structure across punishment-present treatments.",0.9
