 present’ explicitly requires a reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Some studies impose direct or indirect costs (e.g., Sutter & Rivas 2014); costs vary and are not summarized numerically in the meta-analysis.",0.3,N/R,Magnitude of reward per token spent not pooled.,0.3,N/R,Not reported at pooled level.,0.2,0.6,"Fig. 2: contributions in both baseline and reward start around 60%; rewards ‘could marginally increase group contributions in the formative rounds but were unable to prevent…decline’, and final-round reward contributions are ‘significantly higher’ than baseline but lower than punishment. A characteristic mid-range level ≈60%.",0.55,N/R,Only percentages of endowment given.,0.2,N/R,No pooled efficiency measure reported.,0.1,N/R,Not summarized.,0.1,"Mixed (China, Germany, Denmark, France, India, Norway, United States, United Kingdom)",Reward-present conditions arise in several of the 20 studies spanning these countries.,1,N/R,Not summarized.,0.1,N/R,Not summarized.,0.1,Likely university students; exact distribution N/R,Standard experimental practice; not aggregated here.,0.4,On site,Same lab-based setting.,0.97,"Linear public goods game where, after observing contributions or outputs, a leader/manager can allocate monetary rewards (bonuses) to group members using a reward budget or own tokens; any unspent budget or retained tokens may augment leader’s own payoff in some designs.","Summarizes reward mechanisms described in Section 2.1.2 and related studies (Gürerk et al., Sutter & Rivas, Drouvelis et al.).",0.9
