10.1016_j.chaos.2024.114859.md,M0 – Null model without minority rater punishment,"Baseline contribution game with PRHDS, labelled M0 in the Model section.",1,Minority rater punishment mechanism: none (M0); rating weight w and multiplier r varied over ranges; random exploration rate u and population composition p varied in different simulations.,"M0 is defined as the version without any MRP; parameters r, w, u, and mixture of IIIs/IDIs are systematically varied in Results.",0.86,False,The study explicitly states it is based on computer simulations and evolutionary dynamics on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational model.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; simulations only.,1,True,Model implemented via Monte Carlo steps on L^2 lattice with strategy update rules; repeatedly referred to as simulations.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria; dynamics explored numerically.,0.94,N/R,"Population size N=L^2 with L varied (e.g., 50,100,200,400), but no single canonical size for all simulations; they only specify ranges, not a fixed standard.",0.4,N/R,"They mention evolution up to 10^4–10^5 Monte Carlo steps, but not a single fixed number for all simulations.",0.4,0,"Contribution decision S_i^1 is binary (0 or 1), but this is a public goods decision, not an all-or-nothing budget allocation over a continuous endowment; by definition here, all-or-nothing refers to contribution amounts; they model only contribute vs defect, so players do not choose any intermediate amount. This corresponds to all-or-nothing contribution, so encoding as 0 (since 1 was defined as 'any amount allowed').",0.7,N/A,No explicit human endowment allocation; individuals either pay a fixed cost 1 or 0; this field is defined only for human-subject endowment framing.,1,N/R,"They specify each cooperator bears cost 1 and generates r units of collective benefit with r in [1,|G|], but group size |G| not specified explicitly, so MPCR=r/|G| cannot be computed.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modeled.,1,0,Simulated agents compute payoffs from local interactions; no interface or feedback summaries analogous to experimental display.,0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment choice by peers; only rating and endogenous redistribution; identity revelation not applicable as in lab experiments.,1,N/A,Rewards are implicit via heterogeneous distribution; no explicit reward action revealing identities.,1,0,Agents do not observe a round counter; dynamics are Markovian without such state.,0.7,1,"PRHDS creates de facto punishment via reduced share for low-rated individuals; additionally, in M0 no explicit MRP, but negative heterogeneous distribution works as disguised punishment.",0.7,0,They emphasize PRHDS avoids lose-lose punishment and is budget-balanced; punishment is via share reduction without extra cost beyond contribution cost.,0.8,1,Model unit of benefit and cost normalized; share reduction translates 1:1 at marginal level between share and payoff; exact scaling not specified but effectively linear.,0.4,1,"High ratings correspond to larger shares, i.e., disguised reward mechanism under budget balance.",0.9,0,"Reward comes from redistribution of fixed collective benefit, no external budget or extra cost; they note budget balanced reward.",0.8,1,Higher score increases proportional share linearly via weight w; marginal mapping normalized.,0.5,1,Each cooperator bears 1 unit cost; treated as unit endowment at risk.,0.8,N/R,"They report fractions of strategy types (PC, EC, etc.) and overall fraction of cooperators F_C but no single numeric value for all conditions.",0.5,N/R,Binary contribution (0 or 1) but no aggregate average explicitly tabulated numerically.,0.5,N/R,No explicit reporting of ratio of achieved to maximal group payoff.,0.3,N/R,They use payoffs P_iG in dynamics but do not report mean group payoff numerically.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study consists of agent-based simulations on lattice; no physical or online human environment.,1,"Public-goods-like contribution game on L^2 lattice; binary contribution S_i^1∈{0,1}; rating stage with binary scores S_ij^{2c}, S_ij^{2d}∈{0,1}; payoffs P_iG = r * sum_j S_j^1 * (w*A_iG+1-w)/Σ_k(w*A_kG+1-w) - S_i^1. Four basic rating types (prosocial, egoistic, generous, antisocial) combined with cooperate/defect choices into eight strategies (PC, EC, GC, AC, PD, ED, GD, AD). Strategy update via imitation with noise K=0.1 and random exploration with rate u on a lattice network.",Summarized directly from Model and Results sections.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.114859.md,M1 – Fixed unit monetary minority rater punishment,Extended model M1 introduces a fixed fine c for each time an individual is a minority rater.,1,"Minority rater punishment mechanism: fixed unit fine per minority event (M1); punishment intensity c varied (e.g., 0, 0.25, 1); random exploration rate u and other PRHDS parameters varied.",M1 is defined by parameter c≥0; Results explicitly discuss effects of different c and u.,0.9,False,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulations with no human subjects.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment; computational model.,1,False,"No laboratory environment, only simulations.",1,True,Evolutionary dynamics of strategies on a lattice network explored numerically for M1.,1,False,No analytical solution of dynamics; only numerical results.,0.94,N/R,"Population size N=L^2 with L varied (e.g., 50–400), no single canonical N.",0.4,N/R,"They simulate up to 10^4–10^5 MCS in some figures, but no single standard across all.",0.4,0,Contribution decision is binary contribute vs defect as in M0; no continuous contribution choice.,0.7,N/A,No explicit divisible monetary endowment for human subjects; fixed cost 1 for cooperation.,1,N/R,"Same contribution technology as M0 with r∈[1,|G|]; |G| not fixed, so MPCR not uniquely given.",0.4,N/A,Simulation with no human communication.,1,0,Agents do not observe aggregate summaries; only local payoff differences used in update rule.,0.8,N/A,MRP is automatic via model rules; no explicit decision by other agents with identity revelation.,1,N/A,Rewards occur via redistribution; not as explicit targeted rewards with identities shown.,1,0,No explicit modeling of agents seeing round count.,0.7,1,"In addition to implicit punishment via PRHDS, M1 adds explicit monetary punishment c*T_iG imposed by a third party.",1,1,Each time being a minority rater reduces payoff by c; effectively cost per punishment unit is c. Normalizing unit punishment to 1 per event yields linear mapping.,0.6,1,"Each minority event reduces payoff by exactly c, so 1 unit of punishment expenditure yields 1*c payoff loss; normalized as 1 for ratio.",0.6,1,Same PRHDS-based disguised rewards as in M0.,0.9,0,"Rewards remain endogenous redistributions; M1 only adds exogenous fines, not external rewards.",0.8,1,Same linear dependence of payoff share on scores via w parameter as in M0.,0.5,1,Each cooperator still pays cost 1 into the public good.,0.9,N/R,"Fractions of cooperators F_C under different c,u,r,w shown graphically but no universal numeric summary.",0.5,N/R,Binary contributions; average amounts implicit in F_C but not numerically tabulated.,0.5,N/R,No explicit aggregate efficiency numbers reported for M1 conditions.,0.3,N/R,Group payoffs are internal to dynamics; results focus on strategy fractions instead.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results for M1 are from computer simulations.,1,"Same contribution and rating structure as M0, but final payoff modified to P_iG|M1 = CB_G * (w*A_iG+1-w)/Σ_j(w*A_jG+1-w) - S_i^1 - c*T_iG, where T_iG counts how many times i is a minority rater in the game. The parameter c≥0 controls punishment intensity; larger c increases penalty per minority event. Simulations explore how varying c and random exploration u affect fractions of strategies, cyclic dominance, and robustness of cooperation.",Directly from definition of M1 in Model and related Results discussion.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.114859.md,M2 – Monetary share reduction minority rater punishment,Extended model M2 introduces a reduction in distribution share for minority raters parameterized by ρ.,1,"Minority rater punishment mechanism: reduction in distribution share per minority event (M2); punishment intensity ρ varied (e.g., 0, 0.25, 1); random exploration rate u and other PRHDS parameters varied.","M2 is defined by parameter ρ∈[0,1]; Results analyze cooperation as function of ρ and u.",0.9,False,Agent-based simulations on lattice; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; computational.,1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,Evolution simulated via Monte Carlo steps with update rules and M2 payoff function.,1,False,"Dynamics explored numerically, not analytically solved.",0.94,N/R,"Population size N=L^2, with L sometimes 50,100,200,400; no single N for the condition.",0.4,N/R,Reported horizons (10^4–10^5 MCS) vary by figure; not one fixed standard.,0.4,0,Contribution decision remains binary contribute vs defect; no continuous contribution choice.,0.7,N/A,"No explicit divisible endowment allocation for human subjects, only unit cost for cooperation.",1,N/R,"Same as M0: r∈[1,|G|] but |G| not fixed, so MPCR not uniquely known.",0.4,N/A,No modeled communication between humans.,1,0,Agents only use local payoff information for imitation; no interface summarizing others' outcomes.,0.8,N/A,Punishment is algorithmic via decreased shares; no explicit actor identity displayed.,1,N/A,Rewards remain implicit in redistribution; no explicit targeted reward with identities.,1,0,Model does not include agents observing the number of remaining rounds.,0.7,1,"M2 specifies that each time an individual is a minority rater, their share is multiplied by (1 - ρ*T_iG/(|G|-1)), reducing payoff.",1,1,"Each minority event proportionally reduces own share; effectively, per-unit punishment cost is linear in ρ and CB_G, normalized as 1 for unit mapping.",0.5,1,Punishment effect scales linearly in ρ*T_iG/(|G|-1) on individual's share; normalized as one unit per punishment parameter.,0.5,1,"As in M0/M1, higher scores receive larger shares; PRHDS provides disguised rewards.",0.9,0,Redistribution-based reward; no external reward budget introduced in M2.,0.8,1,Payoff share is product of rating-based term and punishment factor; reward part still proportional to w*A_iG+1-w.,0.5,1,Cooperator cost remains 1 as in base model.,0.9,N/R,"They provide visual heatmaps and curves of F_C over ρ and u, but no single numeric summary for this condition.",0.5,N/R,Binary contributions; mean not explicitly tabulated.,0.5,N/R,No reported efficiency values for M2.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff trajectories not numerically reported.,0.3,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,M2 results are all from computational experiments.,1,"Same underlying contribution and rating game as M0, but with payoff P_iG|M2 = CB_G * [(w*A_iG+1-w)*(1 - ρ*T_iG/(|G|-1))]/Σ_j[(w*A_jG+1-w)*(1 - ρ*T_jG/(|G|-1))] - S_i^1. Parameter ρ∈[0,1] determines how much a player’s share is reduced for being a minority rater. The total reduction per player is bounded so that share is never reduced by more than 100%. Punishment is endogenous and limited by collective benefit CB_G, which affects how GD–AD vs PC–EC–ED camps can use punishment.",Summarized from the definition of M2 and discussion of its effects.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.114859.md,M3 – Non-monetary punishment via reduced rating voice for minority raters,"Extended model M3 reduces the weight of ratings given by minority raters, controlled by parameter φ.",1,"Minority rater punishment mechanism: reduction in rating weight (voice) per minority event (M3); punishment intensity φ varied (e.g., 0, 0.25, 1); random exploration rate u and other PRHDS parameters varied.","M3 is defined by parameter φ∈[0,1] affecting the weight of ratings; Results compare M3 to M0, M1, M2.",0.9,False,"Based on computer simulations, not on human data.",1,False,Not a human/field experiment.,1,False,No lab implementation; purely computational.,1,True,Agent-based simulations of M3’s payoff and rating equations on lattice.,1,False,"No analytical solution presented, only simulations.",0.94,N/R,"As with other models, N=L^2 with L varied; no single fixed N.",0.4,N/R,MCS horizons vary by simulation; not uniquely specified.,0.4,0,Contribution choice is binary contribute vs defect; no continuous amount.,0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment fraction; agents only decide to incur cost 1 or not.,1,N/R,Same r and |G| structure as other models but |G| not fixed.,0.4,N/A,No human communication modeled.,1,0,Agents have no explicit information about others’ overall outcomes beyond payoffs of neighbors used for imitation.,0.8,N/A,"Punishment is via lowered rating weights, not explicit sanction choices with identities.",1,N/A,Rewards stem from redistribution; no explicit rewarding actor with identity information.,1,0,No modeling of observation of remaining rounds.,0.7,1,"M3 defines that when a player is often a minority rater, the weight of their ratings (their voice) is decreased via factor (1 - φ*T_jG/(|G|-1)).",1,0,Described as a non-monetary punishment; it reduces influence on scoring but not own payoff directly outside redistribution; thus no explicit additional cost parameter like c or ρ.,0.8,1,Each minority event scales down rating weight linearly via φ*T_jG/(|G|-1); normalized as 1 for mapping.,0.5,1,Reward via PRHDS persists; higher scores still yield higher shares.,0.9,0,Rewards remain budget-balanced redistributions.,0.8,1,Payoff still determined via w*A_iG|M3 + 1 - w in proportional share; linear in score.,0.5,1,Cooperator cost is 1 in all models.,0.9,N/R,"Graphs compare F_C across M3 and other models, but no single numeric value reported.",0.5,N/R,Binary contributions; no explicit numeric average in tables.,0.5,N/R,No efficiency statistics given for M3.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff not summarized numerically for M3.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,M3 is evaluated solely via simulated evolutionary dynamics.,1,"Contribution stage and distribution stage as in M0, but rating aggregation and payoffs modified: A_iG|M3 = Σ_{j≠i}[S_i^1*S_ji^{2c} + (1-S_i^1)*S_ji^{2d}] * (1 - φ*T_jG/(|G|-1))/(|G|-1). Payoff P_iG|M3 = CB_G * (w*A_iG|M3 + 1 - w)/Σ_j(w*A_jG|M3 + 1 - w) - S_i^1. Parameter φ∈[0,1] measures how strongly the voice of minority raters is reduced. Results indicate M3 does not significantly improve cooperation compared to M0.",Summarized from M3 definition and corresponding Results discussion.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 1 – Baseline parameterization,"Uses the baseline values given in Table 1 (N=50,000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, S=0.4, M=1e-3, x=0.5, P=0.30) as a distinct configuration; time-series in Figure 2 describes outcomes under these conditions.",1,Baseline values: population size N=50000; group size n=5; iterations I=10; endowment R=10; punishment cost C=5; detection probability D=0.7; solitary payoff factor S=0.4; mutation rate M=0.001; power symmetry x=0.5; proportion powerful P=0.30; initial strategy frequencies as in Table 1.,"Taken directly from Table 1, described as baseline values for the simulations.",1,False,Agent-based evolutionary game-theoretical model with simulated agents; no human or animal subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment but a computational simulation study.,1,False,No physical lab or participant setting; purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly described as an agent-based simulation implemented in NetLogo.,1,True,Paper includes analytical equations (1–9) underlying the simulation; same model run here under baseline parameters.,0.8,5,Group size n=5 in Table 1; interactions occur in groups of size n.,1,10,Iterations per group per generation I=10 in Table 1.,1,1,"Model specifies altruists contribute their entire share R and selfish contribute 0; no option for intermediate contributions, which is an all-or-nothing decision.",0.9,0,All resources are initially privately endowed (R) and then altruists may contribute; nothing starts in public fund.,0.8,2,"MPCR = m/n; baseline m not explicitly given, but standard public good parameterization with R=10, multiplier m and n=5; paper does not report m explicitly, so MPCR cannot be computed confidently.",0,0,No communication or signaling between agents described in the model.,1,0,Agents do not receive payoff summaries; only their own fitness is tracked internally by the model.,0.9,0,"No identities or reputations are modeled; punishment is based purely on strategies, not revealed IDs.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,Agents do not have explicit knowledge of I or remaining iterations; no such information is modeled.,0.8,1,Punishment phase follows cooperation; punishers pay cost C to expel selfish individuals.,1,5,Cost of punishing each selfish individual C=5 in Table 1.,1,1,"Punishment removes the target from group interactions, but the model does not define a per-token damage multiple; set to 1 as a neutral placeholder since detailed tech not specified.",0,0,Model includes only costly punishment and expulsion; no positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward stage in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism specified.,1,10,Resources allocated each iteration R=10 in Table 1.,1,N/R,Paper reports frequencies of altruistic versus selfish phenotypes but not normalized contribution rates as a numerical outcome.,0,N/R,"Contributions are R for altruists, 0 for selfish, but no aggregate or average contribution amounts are tabulated as a reported DV.",0.3,N/R,Efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) is discussed qualitatively but no numeric efficiency index is reported.,0,N/R,Model tracks fitness but the paper does not provide explicit numeric average group payoff outcomes.,0,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based NetLogo simulation with no physical or online human environment.,1,"Agent-based evolutionary public goods game with four strategies (altruistic nonpunisher, altruistic punisher, selfish nonpunisher, selfish punisher), punishment via expulsion, asexual reproduction proportional to fitness, mutation rate 0.001, and power asymmetry where proportion P of individuals have reduced punishment cost and reduced probability of being punished by factor x. Simulations run for 3,000 generations (30,000 iterations).",Summarizes distinctive structural features of the modeled game from The Model section and Table 1.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 2 – Power symmetry and distribution sweep (with selfish punishers allowed),Figures 3 and associated text describe simulations varying symmetry of power x from 0.05–1.0 and proportion powerful P from 0.05–0.50 while holding other parameters at baseline and allowing all four strategies.,1,"Independent variables: symmetry of power x ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; proportion of powerful individuals P ∈ [0.05, 0.50]; all other parameters at baseline (N=50000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, S=0.4, M=0.001, initial strategy frequencies as in Table 1).",Text: 'Solitary (S) and symmetry of power (x) were varied from 0.05 to 1.0 in increments of 0.05. The distribution of power ... (P) was varied from 0.05 to 0.5.' Figure 3 holds S=0.4 and varies x and P.,1,False,Agent-based simulation only.,1,False,"Computational parameter sweep, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab environment; pure simulation.,1,True,Agent-based NetLogo simulations over a grid of parameter values.,1,True,Based on analytical model but outcomes are from simulation runs.,0.8,5,Group size n=5 is constant across these simulations (baseline value).,1,10,Iterations I=10 held at baseline.,1,1,"Same cooperation rule: altruists contribute all R, selfish contribute 0.",0.9,0,Endowment starts private each iteration as in baseline model.,0.8,N/R,"Multiplier m is not numerically reported, so MPCR cannot be calculated.",0,0,No communication mechanism included.,1,0,No feedback of others’ payoffs beyond their strategic effects.,0.9,0,No identity-level modeling.,0.9,0,No rewards present.,1,0,Agents are not modeled as seeing remaining iterations.,0.8,1,Punishment is a key phase in every iteration.,1,5,Punishment cost C=5 across these runs.,1,1,"Magnitude per unit cost not separately parameterized; punishment results in expulsion, same as baseline.",0,0,Only punishment modeled.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,R=10 fixed.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as frequencies of altruism and punishment strategies (phenotypes), not explicit normalized contribution rate numbers.",0.2,N/R,No numeric contributions reported; only qualitative descriptions of 'total altruism fixed (>99%)' etc.,0.2,N/R,Paper contrasts efficiency qualitatively between equilibria but no numeric efficiency metric is tabulated.,0,N/R,Average group payoff not numerically reported for this sweep.,0,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational agent-based simulations.,1,"For each (x, P) combination, the model runs for 3,000 generations (30,000 iterations) with 10 replicate runs. Outcomes categorized into three equilibria: (1) pure selfish nonpunishers; (2) dimorphic altruistic nonpunishers + selfish punishers where selfish punisher conditional strategy reaches fixation (>99%); (3) dimorphic altruistic nonpunishers + altruistic punishers where total altruism (altruistic phenotypes) reaches fixation (>99%). x controls both reduction in punishment cost and reduced probability of being punished for powerful individuals; P is fraction of population tagged as powerful each generation.",Summarizes design and main equilibria as described around Figure 3 and Results.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 3 – Power symmetry and solitary value sweep (with selfish punishers allowed),Figure 4 describes simulations varying symmetry of power x and solitary payoff factor S with P fixed at 0.30 and selfish punisher strategy allowed.,1,"Independent variables: symmetry of power x ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; solitary payoff factor S ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; proportion powerful P=0.30; all other parameters at baseline (N=50000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, M=0.001).",Text specifies S and x varied 0.05–1.0; Figure 4 holds P=.3 and varies x and S.,1,False,No human or animal subjects.,1,False,Parameterized simulations rather than empirical experiments.,1,False,No lab; simulation only.,1,True,Agent-based model run across grid of x and S values.,1,True,Simulation grounded in analytical equations but outcome is computational.,0.8,5,Group size n=5 constant.,1,10,Iterations I=10 constant.,1,1,Binary contribution decision as in base model.,0.9,0,Endowment starts private.,0.8,N/R,Multiplier m not numerically reported.,0,0,No communication modeled.,1,0,Agents receive no summarized info about others.,0.9,0,No personal identities in the model.,0.9,0,No reward phase.,1,0,Remaining rounds are not part of agents' state.,0.8,1,Punishment is part of every iteration.,1,5,C=5 fixed; x scales cost for powerful but base unit cost is 5.,1,1,Expulsion as the punishment effect; no numeric tech beyond that.,0,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,10,R=10.,1,N/R,Paper reports total altruism >99% vs reduced levels qualitatively but not numeric contribution rates.,0.2,N/R,No numeric contributions per agent or per group given for this sweep.,0.2,N/R,"Efficiency differences (e.g., 'effective but not efficient') discussed qualitatively.",0,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes reported.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation-only study.,1,"For each (x, S) combination with P=0.30, simulations (10 replicates) converge to one of three equilibria: (1) altruistic nonpunishers + altruistic punishers with total altruism fixed (>99%), in region of high x and low S (i.e., high power symmetry and severe punishment); (2) altruistic nonpunishers + selfish punishers where selfish punisher conditional strategy is fixed and total altruism is high but <100%, occurring with low x (high disparity) and intermediate S; (3) pure selfish nonpunishers when power disparities are high and punishment consequences weak. S determines how costly solitary life is (lower S = harsher punishment).",Derived from narrative and regions in Figure 4 and related results text.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 4 – Power distribution and solitary value sweep (with selfish punishers allowed),Figure 5 reports simulations varying distribution of power P and solitary value S with symmetry x fixed at 0.5 and selfish punishers present.,1,"Independent variables: proportion powerful P ∈ [0.05, 0.50]; solitary payoff factor S ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; symmetry of power x=0.5; other parameters baseline (N=50000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, M=0.001).",Text: 'Solitary (S) and symmetry of power (x) were varied from 0.05 to 1.0... The distribution of power (P) was varied from 0.05 to 0.5.' Figure 5 holds x=0.5 and varies P and S.,1,False,No human data.,1,False,"Simulation parameter sweep, not empirical.",1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,Agent-based simulations over grid of P and S.,1,True,"Same model equations, numerically simulated.",0.8,5,Group size n=5 fixed.,1,10,I=10 fixed.,1,1,Altruists contribute full R; selfish contribute none.,0.9,0,No initial public account.,0.8,N/R,Multiplier m not given.,0,0,No communication.,1,0,No explicit information-sharing modeled.,0.9,0,No individual IDs.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,0,Rounds not part of agents' perceptions.,0.8,1,Punishment present in interactions.,1,5,Baseline C=5.,1,1,Expulsion-based punishment; no finer tech value.,0,0,Rewards not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,10,R=10.,1,N/R,Reported outcomes focus on region boundaries (altruistic vs selfish enforcement and collapse to selfishness) rather than numeric contribution rates.,0.2,N/R,No numeric contributions per condition presented.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not numerically evaluated.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not reported numerically.,0,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"For each (P, S) at x=0.5, simulations (10 replicates) lead to: (1) altruistic punishment equilibrium (altruistic nonpunishers + altruistic punishers) under high P (widely distributed power) and harsh punishment (low S); (2) selfish punishment equilibrium (altruistic nonpunishers + minority powerful selfish punishers) when power is concentrated (low P) and punishment is moderate; (3) pure selfish nonpunishers when punishment is weak (high S) and/or power highly concentrated. Dashed lines show total altruism levels within selfish-punisher region.",Summarizes Figure 5 description.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 5 – Symmetry sweep and participation differences for powerful vs nonpowerful selfish individuals,Figure 6 presents simulations varying x from 0.05 to 1.0 and measuring difference in proportion of rounds spent in group interactions between powerful and nonpowerful selfish individuals.,0.9,"Independent variable: symmetry of power x ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; other parameters at baseline (N=50000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, S=0.4, M=0.001, P as in baseline unless otherwise stated).",Text: 'a separate set of simulations varying x from 0.05 to 1 (at 0.05 increments and replicated 10 times)'. Baseline values implied for others.,0.9,False,No real subjects.,1,False,Simulation runs.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Agent-based simulations systematically varying x.,1,True,Based on analytical conditions for w_sp > w_ap but evaluated via simulation.,0.8,5,Same base model group size.,1,10,Base model I=10.,1,1,Contribution rule unchanged.,0.9,0,Endowment is private at start of each iteration.,0.8,N/R,Multiplier m not reported.,0,0,No modeled communication.,1,0,No feedback channel.,0.9,0,"Strategies, not IDs, determine interactions.",0.9,0,Rewards absent.,1,0,Rounds not shown to agents.,0.8,1,Punishment is central to these runs.,1,5,C=5 baseline.,1,1,"Expulsion effect, no numeric tech parameter.",0,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,10,R=10 baseline.,1,N/R,These runs focus on differential participation (rounds in group) rather than contributions.,0.1,N/R,Contribution not reported.,0.1,N/R,"DV here is proportion of rounds in group for selfish types, not efficiency.",0,N/R,Group payoff not given.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All runs are computational.,1,"Ten replicate runs for each x; measured the difference in proportion of rounds spent participating in group interactions between powerful vs nonpowerful selfish nonpunishers and selfish punishers. As x decreases (greater power disparity), powerful selfish individuals avoid punishment more and spend a higher fraction of rounds in groups than nonpowerful counterparts. Quadratic fits show strong relationship (R^2 ≈ .80–.85) between x and these participation differences.",Summary based on description accompanying Figure 6.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 6 – Power symmetry and distribution sweep without selfish punishers,"Figure 7 shows results when the selfish punisher strategy is prohibited, varying symmetry x and proportion powerful P while holding other parameters at baseline.",1,"Independent variables: symmetry of power x ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; proportion powerful P ∈ [0.05, 0.50]; selfish punisher strategy s_p disallowed; initial population 50% altruists, 50% selfish, with 10% punishers all altruistic; other parameters baseline (N=50000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, S=0.4, M=0.001).",Text: 'we ran the model across the previous parameter space but prohibiting the existence of the selfish punishment strategy' and then describes initial frequencies; Figure 7 varies x and P with S=0.4.,1,False,No empirical subjects.,1,False,Simulation study.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Agent-based model simulations with modified strategy space.,1,True,Analytical model underlies simulations.,0.8,5,n=5 remains.,1,10,I=10 remains.,1,1,Same contribution rule.,0.9,0,Same endowment structure.,0.8,N/R,m not reported.,0,0,No chat modeled.,1,0,No summary info to agents.,0.9,0,No identities.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,0,Agents not given round info.,0.8,1,Altruistic punishment remains.,1,5,C=5 baseline.,1,1,Punishment via expulsion.,0,0,No reward strategy.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,10,R=10 baseline.,1,N/R,DV reported is whether altruism is fixed (>99%) or population falls to pure selfishness; not a numeric contribution rate.,0.3,N/R,No absolute contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not quantified.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not tabulated.,0,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational modeling.,1,"Allowed strategies: altruistic nonpunisher, altruistic punisher, selfish nonpunisher. Without selfish punishers, populations either (1) maintain altruism via altruistic punishment (dimorphic altruistic nonpunishers + altruistic punishers with total altruism fixed >99%) when x and P are sufficiently high (little power disparity and broad distribution), or (2) collapse to pure selfish nonpunishers when power disparity grows (low x and/or low P). This contrasts with the case including selfish punishers, where such conditions could instead yield a selfish-punisher equilibrium.",Summary based on 'A World Without Corruption' and Figure 7 description.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 7 – Symmetry and solitary value sweep without selfish punishers,"Figure 8 shows simulations without selfish punishers, varying symmetry x and solitary value S with P fixed at 0.30.",1,"Independent variables: symmetry of power x ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; solitary payoff factor S ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; selfish punishers disallowed; proportion powerful P=0.30; other parameters baseline (N=50000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, M=0.001).","Figure 8: 'holding all other variables at baseline conditions (most notably, P=.3), variation of symmetry x and solitary value S'. Selfish punishers are stated as prohibited in this series.",1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,Simulation parameter sweep.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,Agent-based modeling.,1,True,Built from analytical model; outcomes from simulation.,0.8,5,n=5 fixed.,1,10,I=10 fixed.,1,1,"Altruists contribute all, selfish 0.",0.9,0,"Endowment is private at start, as in base model.",0.8,N/R,Multiplier m not reported.,0,0,No chat.,1,0,No summary feedback.,0.9,0,No agent identities.,0.9,0,Rewards not included.,1,0,Agents not modeled as seeing the number of rounds.,0.8,1,Altruistic punishment remains possible.,1,5,C=5.,1,1,Punishment via expulsion; no separate tech parameter.,0,0,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,R=10.,1,N/R,Outcomes described as parameter regions where altruism is fixed (>99%) vs pure selfishness; no numeric contribution rate given.,0.3,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,0,N/R,Group payoff not reported.,0,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,All runs are computational.,1,"For each (x, S) with P=0.30 and no selfish punishers, simulations lead either to (1) fixation of altruism (>99% altruism in cooperation phase) when symmetry is high and consequences of punishment are sufficiently strong (low S), or (2) fixation of pure selfish nonpunishers when symmetry is low and/or consequences are weak (high S). It shows that without corruption (selfish punishment), societies with power disparities are more vulnerable to collapse into selfishness.",Based on description for Figure 8.,1
10.1177_1474704918756993.md,Simulation set 8 – Power distribution and solitary value sweep without selfish punishers,Figure 9 describes simulations without selfish punishers varying P and S with x fixed at 0.5.,1,"Independent variables: proportion powerful P ∈ [0.05, 0.50]; solitary payoff factor S ∈ [0.05, 1.0] in increments of 0.05; selfish punishers disallowed; symmetry of power x=0.5; other parameters at baseline (N=50000, n=5, I=10, R=10, C=5, D=0.7, M=0.001).","Figure 9: 'holding all other variables at baseline conditions (most notably, x=.5), the proportion of powerful individuals (P) and solitary value (S) ...' with selfish punishers prohibited as per section title.",1,False,No real participants.,1,False,Simulation study.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Agent-based NetLogo simulations.,1,True,Same analytical structure used; outputs from simulations.,0.8,5,n=5 constant.,1,10,I=10 constant.,1,1,"Altruists contribute all, selfish 0.",0.9,0,Endowments private at each iteration.,0.8,N/R,m value not specified.,0,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,No summary info.,0.9,0,No identifiers.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,0,Number of rounds not part of agent state.,0.8,1,Altruistic punishment remains.,1,5,C=5 baseline.,1,1,Punishment implemented as expulsion; no additional tech parameter.,0,0,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,R=10 baseline.,1,N/R,"Outcomes specified in terms of whether altruism vs selfishness fixates, not numeric rates.",0.3,N/R,No contribution amounts presented.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not evaluated numerically.,0,N/R,Group payoff not reported.,0,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model.,1,"For each (P, S) at x=0.5, without selfish punishers, simulations yield either: (1) evolution of altruism maintained by altruistic punishment (white region in Figure 9) when power is widely distributed (high P) and punishment consequences are strong (low S); or (2) fixation of pure selfish nonpunishers (gray region) when consequences are weak (high S) and/or power is concentrated (low P). Shows that concentrating power undermines altruistic enforcement in absence of corruption strategies.",Based on narrative for Figure 9 in 'A World Without Corruption'.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2019.109398.md,"T1 – Unequal initial wealth, visible opponent wealth",Paper defines treatment group T1 as unequal initial endowments (50 vs 100) with visible opponent wealth.,1,"Initial wealth distribution = unequal (half 50, half 100); Wealth visibility = visible",T1 is described as having unequal initial endowments and visible opponent wealth information.,1,True,Human students at Northwestern Polytechnical University participated.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized assignment to treatments and specified payoffs.,1,True,Conducted at a university with students in controlled sessions.,0.95,False,"Uses human subjects, no agent-based or numerical simulation reported.",1,False,No formal mathematical or closed-form analytical model used as primary method.,1,40,"Total of 80 in treatments split into T1 and T2; T1 therefore has 40 students, half poor and half rich.",0.95,0,Number of rounds not fixed; continuation probability 0.75 each round. Exact realized average or maximum number of rounds not reported.,0.3,1,"Prisoner’s Dilemma with discrete strategies C, D, P; no continuous contribution amount. Players choose among discrete options, not contribute ‘any amount’; coded as 1 because contribution is a binary C vs non-C decision, but schema’s definition better aligned with continuous contribution; however game is not an all-or-nothing contribution to a public good, so using 1 to denote free action is ambiguous. To avoid misclassification, set 1 meaning agents freely choose among strategies, not constrained to match opponent.",0.2,0,Standard PD with payoffs per encounter; no continuous endowment allocation to public vs private. Treat as 0: no default contribution to any shared pool.,0.3,N/R,Pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma with fixed payoff matrix; marginal per capita return of a public good not defined.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard PD lab protocols typically prohibit communication.,0.8,0,"Only visibility of opponent’s wealth is m