10.1016_j.econlet.2012.04.048.md,Single-shot 3-player prisoners’ dilemma with punishment and random replacement of one PD decision,Paper describes only one experimental game: a 3-player PD with punishment where one randomly chosen player’s PD decision is replaced by a chance move.,1,"Role/behavioral type and counterpart’s intentionality: choices and punishment are elicited via strategy method for all combinations of (i) subject’s own PD choice (cooperator vs defector), (ii) punished partner’s PD outcome (cooperate vs defect), and (iii) whether the partner’s outcome is intentional vs randomly determined.",Design varies whether a punished partner’s action is intentional or random and analyzes punishment by cooperators vs defectors toward cooperators vs defectors under both intentional and non-intentional outcomes.,0.8,True,Human subjects participated in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab game with randomized roles and payoff-relevant decisions.,1,True,Conducted in the laboratory of the Max-Planck-Institute of Economics in Jena.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations are reported.,1,False,Study does not present a separate formal analytical model as a treatment.,1,3,Explicitly described as a three-player prisoners’ dilemma.,1,1,Each subject only played one round of the game.,1,1,"Paper does not restrict PD decisions to all-or-nothing contributions; standard PD is binary cooperate/defect, but contribution magnitude beyond this is not discussed. Following schema, 1 is used for ability to choose among actions, not a forced single action.",0.4,0,"Standard PD with no initial contribution to a public account; payoffs depend directly on actions, with no default allocation to a group fund.",0.6,N/R,"Design is a prisoners’ dilemma, not a linear public goods game; no multiplier or marginal per capita return is reported.",1,0,"No opportunity for communication between subjects is mentioned; standard PD lab experiments usually prohibit chat, and anonymity is