r strategies within the game, not as a separate punishment stage revealing identities.",1,N/A,No reward stage or identity revelation; only strategic actions within the Prisoners' Dilemma.,1,N/A,Indefinite horizon with continuation probabilities; no interface indicating remaining rounds.,1,1,"Deviation from cooperation is punished by t rounds of playing the inefficient Nash equilibrium (defect, defect); this is the trigger-strategy punishment mechanism.",1,N/R,Punishment is not a costly action chosen in a separate stage; it is an equilibrium strategy profile (reversion to Nash) with no per-unit punishment cost parameter.,1,N/R,No explicit punishment technology mapping cost to inflicted loss; punishment is simply playing the stage-game Nash equilibrium.,1,0,Model only uses punishment via trigger strategies; no positive rewards for others’ cooperation are modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,"Stage game is specified directly in terms of payoffs, not as allocation from an endowment.",1,N/R,"Paper analyzes existence of cooperative equilibria, cones of cooperation, and spectral radii; no numeric contribution rates in a contribution game are reported.",1,N/R,Cooperate/defect decisions not framed as numeric contribution amounts; no such data reported.,1,N/R,"While efficiency is discussed conceptually, no numeric efficiency values (ratio of achieved to maximum payoff) are reported.",1,N/R,Specific numeric repeated-game payoffs are not computed as outcome data; the paper focuses on conditions for existence of equilibria.,1,N/A,No human participants in this analytical study.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical with no human-subject component.,1,"Generalized Prisoners' Dilemma stage game with unique inefficient Nash equilibrium σ* normalized to payoff 0, efficient strategy τ* that strictly Pareto dominates σ*, trigger strategy S_t with t rounds of punishment (including grim trigger t = ∞), time-dependent continuation probabilities β_k ∈ [0,1], discount factor δ, and publicly correlated strategies that linearize equilibrium conditions into matrix inequalities involving M_t with spectral radius μ_t = ∑_{k=1}^t (ℰ δ)^k, where ℰ is an asymptotic geometric average of β.",Summarizes key structural elements of the theoretical repeated-game model described throughout the paper.,0.98
10.1111_j.1468-5876.2011.00544.x.md,Analytical model – Penance contract in finitely repeated Prisoners' Dilemma,Single analytical game-theoretic model: finitely repeated PD with penance contract (y^p) and penance strategy (σ^p); no separate empirical conditions.,0.95,"Contractual device: presence of penance contract y^p with fine X>max[L,M]; baseline is finitely repeated PD without contract (discussed but not separately solved here).","Key variation is introduction of side-payment contract y^p with fine X; L,M,T are parameters but not treatment conditions.",0.88,False,Pure game-theoretic analysis; no human subjects or data collection.,1,False,No controlled experiment; only theoretical model and proofs.,1,False,No lab or field setting; theoretical analysis only.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations are described.,1,True,The paper develops and solves an analytical game-theoretic model with formal lemmas and theorems.,1,2,Model is explicitly a two-player repeated Prisoners' Dilemma (players 1 and 2).,1,2,"Horizon is a generic finite integer T≥2; no single value is fixed. Use minimal admissible T=2 is not appropriate; instead we cannot specify a concrete numeric T. Per instructions, use 2 as lower bound representative since field requires a number.",0.3,1,"Actions are discrete (c or d), not divisible contributions; but within this framework players freely choose either action each round. Interpreted as not all-or-nothing contribution game; coded as 1 since players can choose among available actions without restriction.",0.4,N/A,Not a public-goods or contribution game; no endowment allocated between private/public accounts.,1,N/R,"Model is a 2x2 PD with fixed payoffs, not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely analytical.,1,0,Information structure only specifies observed action histories; no mention of payoff or punishment summaries being shown separately.,0.7,1,"Players observe entire action history and know who deviated; contract fines the deviator specifically, so identity of punished player is effectively known.",0.9,0,Model has fines (negative transfers) only; no explicit rewards or positive transfers framed as rewards.,1,1,Finite horizon T is common knowledge in finitely repeated game; players know the number of periods.,0.95,1,Penance contract imposes fine X on a deviator in final period.,1,N/A,Model specifies transfer X but not marginal cost per punishment decision as in experimental design; cost is embedded in payoff function rather than a choice variable.,0.6,N/A,No parametric mapping of punishment tokens to payoff reductions; fine X is applied directly in payoffs.,0.7,0,Side payments are fines for deviators; no positive rewards for others beyond avoiding fines.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/R,Model directly specifies payoffs for action profiles; no initial resource endowment per period is defined.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are actions and payoffs in a PD, not contributions; no normalized contribution rate is defined.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts; only binary actions c and d.,1,N/R,"Paper discusses efficiency qualitatively (e.g., full collusion) but does not provide a normalized efficiency metric 0–1 tied to a maximum payoff benchmark.",0.6,N/R,"Total payoffs can be derived symbolically, but no single numeric outcome is reported since analysis is general in T, L, M, X.",0.6,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,N/A,No human participants; analytical model only.,1,No human,Purely theoretical game-theoretic analysis; no empirical environment.,1,"Two-player finitely repeated Prisoners' Dilemma with actions c (cooperate) and d (defect). Stage-game payoffs: (c,c)->(1,1); (d,c)->(M+1,-L); (c,d)->(-L,M+1); (d,d)->(0,0), with L>0, M>0, and L+1>M. Horizon T is finite (T≥2). Penance strategy σ^p prescribes (c,c) initially; after deviations, deviator plays c and opponent d in following period; asymmetric treatment when both deviate. Penance contract y^p imposes fine X>max[L,M] on any player who deviates from σ^p in final period T, with balanced budget side payments.",Summarized directly from model description and contract/strategy definitions in Sections 2–3.,1
10.1111_j.1468-5876.2011.00544.x.md,Analytical model – Trigger contract in finitely repeated Prisoners' Dilemma,Same underlying repeated PD but with different contract (trigger contract ŷ) and trigger strategy (σ̂); treated as a distinct analytical condition.,0.95,"Contractual device: presence of trigger contract ŷ with fine X>max[L,M] on first deviator from (c,c).","Main variation relative to penance condition is the alternative contract and associated trigger strategy; other parameters (L,M,T,X) are shared.",0.9,False,No human data; theoretical characterisation of equilibria under trigger contract.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; analytical model only.,1,False,No laboratory or field implementation.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,True,Proposition 4 analytically proves that the trigger strategy is the unique subgame-perfect equilibrium under ŷ.,1,2,Same two-player PD as in the penance-contract model.,1,2,"Horizon is a generic finite integer T≥2, identical structure as before; a specific numeric T is not fixed, but field requires a number, so we enter the minimum admissible value.",0.3,1,"Binary choice between c and d each period; within this binary framework, players freely choose each round; coded consistently with first condition.",0.4,N/A,No contribution or endowment allocation is modeled.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction protocol; theoretical model.,1,0,Players observe action history; no explicit summaries of others' earnings or punishments are modeled beyond that.,0.7,1,"Trigger contract fines 'the first player to select d'; given observed history, identity of punished deviator is known.",0.95,0,Only fines; no reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite horizon T is common knowledge in the finitely repeated game.,0.95,1,"Trigger contract explicitly imposes fine X on first deviator from (c,c).",1,N/A,Fine X is embedded in the payoff structure; no separate marginal punishment cost parameter like in lab designs.,0.6,N/A,No token-based punishment technology; fines are directly specified.,0.7,0,No positive rewards; just penalties and their counterparts due to balanced budget.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Stage game payoffs are specified directly; there is no explicit endowment.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are actions and payoffs, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decision variable is defined.,1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed qualitatively (full cooperation vs. (d,d)), but no formal normalized 0–1 efficiency measure is provided.",0.6,N/R,"Aggregate payoffs can be expressed symbolically but no single scalar outcome is given; analysis is generic in T,L,M,X.",0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical comparison of contracts; no empirical data.,1,"Same 2-player finitely repeated PD as in penance-contract condition. Trigger strategy profile σ̂: play (c,c) as long as no one has ever played d; once any deviation from (c,c) occurs, both play (d,d) forever. Trigger contract ŷ: with fine X>max[L,M], the first player to select d (while all previous outcomes were (c,c)) pays −X and the other receives +X; if both select d simultaneously at first deviation, transfers are 0; if (c,c) is played in all periods, transfers are 0.",Summarized from Section 4.2 describing the trigger contract and strategy.,1
10.1111_j.1468-5876.2011.00544.x.md,Analytical model – Generalized penance contract in generic 2-player game,Section 5 introduces a generalized penance strategy σ^{gp} and contract y^{gp} for a broader class of two-player games; treated as a separate analytical condition.,0.9,"Game structure and contract: general 2-player stage game with actions (c_i,d_i,e_i) and generalized penance contract y^{gp} with fine X above specified payoff differences.",Independent structure relative to PD: broader action sets and payoff conditions; still uses penance-style contracting.,0.85,False,Extension remains purely theoretical; no subjects or data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment but a theoretical generalization.,1,False,No lab/field context.,1,False,No simulation is conducted.,1,True,Section 5 analytically generalizes previous results to a broader class of games with similar proofs.,1,2,"Still a two-player game G=(A1,A2,u1,u2).",1,2,"As before, T is a finite integer ≥2; no specific numeric T is chosen, but field requires a number, so minimum admissible value is entered.",0.3,1,"Players select among multiple discrete actions (c_i,d_i,e_i); they freely choose an action each period.",0.5,N/A,Not a contribution game; arbitrary 2-player game with specified actions and payoffs.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods framework.,1,N/A,No human implementation.,1,0,Only history of actions matters; no mention of additional payoff summaries being provided.,0.7,1,"Contract fines the player deviating from σ^{gp} in final period; with complete history observed, identity is known.",0.9,0,"As before, only fines and their budget-balancing counterparts; not framed as rewards.",1,1,Finite horizon T is part of the model and common knowledge.,0.95,1,Generalized penance contract y^{gp} imposes fine X on deviators in final period.,1,N/A,Fine X integrated in payoffs; no marginal punishment cost parameter separate from X.,0.6,N/A,No explicit punishment technology mapping tokens to payoffs.,0.7,0,Mechanism is fines for deviators; no separate rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Paper specifies payoffs directly; no explicit endowment.,1,N/R,General game does not define contributions.,1,N/R,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,"Efficiency is discussed (e.g., u(c1,c2) efficient), but no numeric normalized efficiency is provided.",0.6,N/R,Payoffs are left as symbolic u_i functions; no single numeric outcome.,0.7,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Extended analytical model only.,1,"General 2-player stage game G=(A1,A2,u1,u2). For each player i, three actions are specified: cooperative c_i, defective d_i, and costly e_i. Assumptions: u1(c1,c2)+u2(c1,c2) ≥ u1(a)+u2(a) for all a∈A (efficiency of (c1,c2)); and for each i and for each opponent action a_j∈{c_j,d_j}, u_i(c_i,c_j)−u_i(e_i,d_j) > max_{a_i∈A_i} [u_i(a_i,a_j)−u_i(c_i,a_j)], ensuring single-period penalty via costly action deters deviation. Generalized penance strategy σ^{gp}: play (c1,c2) initially and whenever previous outcome equals σ^{gp}(previous history); if player 1 deviated in previous period, next period outcome is (e1,d2); if only player 2 deviated, next period outcome is (d1,e2). Generalized penance contract y^{gp} mirrors y^p: in final period T, any player deviating from σ^{gp} pays −X and the other receives +X; if both deviate or both follow σ^{gp}, transfers are 0. X must exceed certain payoff differences to ensure incentives, analogous to X>max[L,M] in PD.","Summarized from Section 5, which specifies actions, payoff conditions, generalized penance strategy, contract, and assumptions on X and η.",1
