10.1016_j.jrurstud.2020.08.013.md,Simulation – spatial PD with conditional neutral punishment (generic parameter setting),"Single numerical model on an L×L lattice; all reported results (Figs.1–6) are parameter sweeps of the same simulation rules. No distinct experimental treatments beyond continuous parameters b, α, β.",0.9,"Temptation to defect b, punishment fine β, punishment cost α (varied continuously over ranges; e.g., Fig.1: b vs β with α=0.01; Fig.2: α vs β with b=1.3; Fig.4–6: b varied with fixed α,β)","Model description and results sections explicitly state sweeping over b, α, β to examine cooperation levels.",1,False,Study uses evolutionary game simulations on a square lattice; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled lab/field experiment; only numerical simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely computational.,1,True,Explicitly described as numerical simulations with Monte Carlo steps on a lattice.,1,False,"Although a critical condition b−β=1−α is derived, the core results rely on numerical simulations rather than a full analytical model.",0.9,40000,Population is on an L×L lattice with L=200; thus 200×200=40000 players.,1,55000,They simulate for 5×10^4 Monte Carlo steps for relaxation and then average over the last 5×10^3 steps to compute cooperation level; total dynamic length per run is 5×10^4 + 5×10^3 = 5.5×10^4.,0.8,1,"Binary strategies: cooperate (1) or defect (0); not an all-or-nothing contribution amount variant, but in this PD context players choose between two pure actions, which corresponds to discrete all-or-nothing cooperation choices.",0.7,N/A,"This is a 2×2 prisoner's dilemma with payoffs, not a contribution game with endowments and public/private accounts.",1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined or reported for this PD payoff matrix.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between players is not modeled.,1,0,"Simulation agents do not receive explicit summaries, only local payoffs and neighbors’ strategies via lattice interactions; no global or summary feedback is modeled.",0.9,0,"Agents interact only with fixed neighbors on a lattice; identities are implicit and unchanging, and no separate identity revelation about who punished whom is modeled.",0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,Agents do not observe number of Monte Carlo steps or horizon; time information is not part of their decision rule.,0.9,1,Second-stage conditional neutral punishment is the core mechanism: players may pay cost α to impose fine β on opposite-strategy neighbors when their payoff is below neighbors’ average.,1,1,"In the payoff update, up_x = p_x − M×β − N×α; each punished neighbor costs the punisher α and fines the neighbor β. Hence 1 coin of punishment causes a cost of α to the punisher.",0.8,1,"Each punishment action (cost α) inflicts a fine β on the target; thus per unit cost, effective fine magnitude is β/α. As the schema expects a per-coin magnitude, we set the punishment ‘tech’ to β/α but it varies with parameters; we approximate as 1 here because α and β are separate independent variables and no fixed ratio is used.",0.2,0,"Only punishment (cost α, fine β) is modeled; no positive reward or bonus mechanism.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"The PD is defined via payoff matrix A with entries (1,0,b,0); no explicit endowment per round is described.",0.3,N/R,"Outcome variable reported is fraction of cooperators ρ_c, not framed as contribution rate relative to an endowment.",0.8,N/R,Model is not a contribution game with variable amounts; actions are C or D.,1,N/R,They do not report total payoffs relative to maximum possible payoffs; only fractions of cooperators and cluster statistics.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not reported; only cooperation levels and cluster measures are shown.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely computational agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study conducted via numerical simulations on a computer; no lab, online, or field environment with human subjects.",1,"Two-strategy spatial Prisoner’s Dilemma on a 200×200 square lattice with degree 4; payoff matrix A=[[1,0],[b,0]], imitation update via Fermi rule with selection intensity K=0.1; conditional neutral punishment: if player’s payoff is below neighbors’ average, it pays cost α per opposite-strategy neighbor to impose fine β on each.",Summarized from Methods section and parameter descriptions in Results.,1
10.1515_amcs-2015-0054.md,"Numerical simulations – evolutionary history-1 strategies, bg=0",Paper describes one numerical-evolutionary analysis; conditions differ only by bg (0 vs 0.5). This row captures the bg=0 setting.,0.9,"Bonus for giving the resource bg = 0; other parameters fixed: c_pt = 1.5, c_st = 2.25, number of stages l = 100, mutation probability = 0.01, population size = 100, generations = 2000, strategy space = history-1 strategies, selection = tournament or imitation with mutation operators (flip or randomisation).","Section 5.3 and Table 3 specify parameter values and that bg took values {0, 0.5}. All other listed parameters held constant across bg conditions.",0.93,False,Numerical simulations with strategies and evolutionary algorithm; no human participants involved.,1,False,"This is a computational experiment, but within the schema 'experiment' refers to empirical experiments with subjects or field data.",0.9,False,No physical lab or human subjects; purely in silico.,1,True,Section 5 explicitly describes numerical simulations and evolutionary algorithm over strategy space.,1,False,Analytical results are in Section 4 but this row refers to the numerical simulations only.,0.95,2,Give and Take is defined throughout as a two-player game; simulations pit strategies in pairwise games among population members.,0.98,100,"Table 3 lists l, number of stages, as {100}.",0.98,1,"Not a contribution game with continuous decisions; actions are discrete (give, take, none). The all-or-nothing contribution flag is not conceptually applicable but game allows any of the discrete actions each round; coded 1 as not restricted to a single forced action.",0.4,N/A,No public good or contribution endowment; purely possession of an indivisible resource in a stochastic game.,1,N/A,Not a public goods or linear contribution game; marginal per capita return is not defined.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication between agents not modelled as a separate configurable channel.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents have perfect knowledge of previous moves via modeled history-1 strategies but not via displayed summaries.,1,N/A,No identities or interface; also no explicit punishment stage beyond built-in take action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism distinct from standard payoffs; no identities.,1,N/A,Artificial agents are programmed with l=100 but no display to human subjects.,1,1,Take action is explicitly interpreted as built-in punishment with costs c_pt to taker and c_st to subject.,1,1.5,Table 3: c_pt (cost perform action take) = {1.5}.,0.98,N/R,"Magnitude of payoff reduction per unit of punishment expenditure not parameterized separately; c_pt and c_st are fixed costs per take action, not per unit of an independent punishment decision variable.",0.3,1,Bonus bg for giving resource is a positive payoff component analogous to a reward for the recipient of the cooperative action.,0.9,0,Giver receives a bonus bg and does not incur an explicit cost for giving beyond opportunity cost of not holding resource; no separate reward-cost parameter is defined.,0.6,0,"Reward magnitude is bg itself, which is set to 0 in this condition; mapping from reward units to payoff is direct but schema expects a multiplier per coin spent, not applicable here, approximated as 0.",0.4,N/A,Game payoffs arise from holding a resource and action bonuses/costs; there is no per-round monetary endowment.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions as in public good games.,1,N/A,No contribution tokens; only actions give/take/none.,1,N/R,"Simulations report counts and ranks of strategies, not aggregate efficiency measures relative to a theoretical maximum.",0.4,N/R,Paper focuses on ranking strategies by their frequency and analysing properties; does not report aggregate payoffs for the population or per game explicitly.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; agents are anonymous computational strategies.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational evolutionary simulations.,1,"Two-player Give and Take game; history-1 strategy space encoded as 10-bit binary vectors (8 state-contingent action parameters plus 2 initial-state parameters); 1024 possible strategies, 194 'nice' strategies that give when playing against themselves; population of 100 strategies evolves over 2000 generations with payoffs accumulated over round-robin pairings of 100-stage games; selection via tournament size 2 or imitation, mutation probability 0.01 using either bit-flip or full randomisation.","Synthesised from Sections 5.1–5.3, definition of strategy coding and simulation procedure.",0.96
10.1515_amcs-2015-0054.md,"Numerical simulations – evolutionary history-1 strategies, bg=0.5",Same numerical-evolutionary setup as previous condition but with bonus bg=0.5; treated as separate condition because bg is the key varied parameter.,0.9,"Bonus for giving the resource bg = 0.5; other parameters fixed: c_pt = 1.5, c_st = 2.25, number of stages l = 100, mutation probability = 0.01, population size = 100, generations = 2000, strategy space = history-1 strategies, selection = tournament or imitation with mutation operators (flip or randomisation).","Table 3: bg tested at {0, 0.5} while other listed parameters unchanged.",0.93,False,No human or field data; just computer simulations.,1,False,"Within this schema, 'experiment' refers to human/field experiments; this is computational.",0.9,False,No physical laboratory or human participants.,1,True,Described as numerical simulations and evolutionary algorithm (Section 5).,1,False,Analytical derivations are separate (Section 4); current row focuses on numerical results.,0.95,2,Game definition and simulations use two-player Give and Take interactions.,0.98,100,Table 3 parameter l = 100 stages.,0.98,1,"Decision each round is among discrete actions (give, take, none); all-or-nothing contribution notion not directly relevant but actions are unrestricted within that discrete set.",0.4,N/A,No contribution endowments or public good accounts.,1,N/A,Not a linear public goods game.,1,N/A,No explicit communication between computational agents beyond their programmed strategies.,1,N/A,No UI; agents just respond based on previous stage actions.,1,N/A,No subject identities or displays; punishment is via take action only.,1,N/A,"No explicit reward targeting or identities, only built-in bonus for giving.",1,N/A,Agents are programmed for l=100; no visual display for human subjects.,1,1,Take action functions as punishment with costs c_pt and c_st.,1,1.5,Table 3: c_pt (cost perform action take) = 1.5.,0.98,N/R,"No separate scaling parameter; c_pt and c_st are flat per-take costs, not per coin of a distinct punishment decision.",0.3,1,Positive bonus bg=0.5 for giving resource corresponds to a reward-like incentive.,0.98,0,Giver earns bg for giving; no explicit cost parameter for distributing rewards.,0.6,0.5,Each give action grants a bonus of bg=0.5 directly to the giver; interpreted as magnitude of reward per 'unit' of reward action.,0.7,N/A,No per-round monetary endowment defined; only possession-based payoff and bonuses/costs.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions.,1,N/A,No contribution tokens or amounts.,1,N/R,"Authors summarise which strategies dominate (e.g., nice strategies that give immediately) but do not compute efficiency relative to a maximal cooperative benchmark.",0.4,N/R,Results focus on histograms of strategy occurrences and ranking; aggregate payoffs are not reported numerically.,0.3,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Entirely computational evolutionary simulations.,1,"Same simulation design as bg=0 condition: two-player Give and Take game; strategies with history of length 1 encoded into 10 binary parameters (8 contingent on previous joint action, plus 2 for first stage given/without resource); 1024 possible strategies, 194 'nice' (give when facing themselves); evolutionary algorithm with population size 100 over 2000 generations, mutation probability 0.01 (either single-parameter flip or full randomisation), selection via tournament size 2 or imitation, round-robin interactions with all other individuals each generation, game length 100 stages. Results: top strategies under bg=0.5 are predominantly 'nice' strategies that give the resource right after receiving it, and they tend to take only as retaliation in specific histories.","Compiled from Sections 5.1–5.3, including definition of nice strategies and discussion of Figures 4 and 5 and Table 4.",0.96
10.1016_j.jecp.2023.105858.md,Variant 1 – Monitoring funded by separate institutional tax pool,Paper defines 'Variant 1' where monitors are reimbursed from a separate pool of institutional taxes paid by agents with l_τ=1; treated as one analytical condition.,0.93,"Funding mechanism for monitoring: separate tax pool (β fraction of institutional taxes C_τ paid by agents with l_τ=1); agents unilaterally decide whether to pay tax, monitor, and cooperate.","Independent manipulation is how monitoring is financed and the unilateral tax decision, contrasted later with Variant 2 funded from the common-pool resource.",0.94,False,No human or animal participants; purely theoretical equations and evolutionary game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,No controlled empirical or simulation experiment; only analytical derivations.,1,False,No laboratory or online experiment with participants.,1,False,"Although the paper discusses ABM in general, this variant is treated only with analytical equations; no executions or numerical simulations are described.",0.86,True,"Variant 1 is developed and studied via explicit utility functions and inequalities, using evolutionary game theory.",1,N/R,Model uses a generic group size n in equations but does not instantiate it with a numerical value.,0.9,N/R,Uses repeated interactions and generations t but no specific finite or infinite number T is fixed for this variant.,0.7,1,Agents choose whether or not to cooperate (binary l_C) rather than contributing a continuous amount; this corresponds to an all-or-nothing contribution choice.,0.9,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment allocation across private/public accounts; abstract cost C_C to provision α units.,1,N/R,"Model uses linear public goods with benefit α per cooperator and group size n, but does not define or label MPCR explicitly.",0.6,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; abstract analytical model.,1,0,No interface or feedback structure about others’ earnings is described; only abstract fitness/payoff expressions.,0.85,N/A,No identities or observability of punishers are modelled; only sanctions s applied to free-riders.,0.95,N/A,No rewarding mechanism or identification of rewarders; only monitoring and sanctions.,1,0,No user interface or information display is relevant in an analytical model.,1,1,"Free-riders that are monitored are sanctioned at level s, explicitly described as a sanction cost C_F.",1,N/R,"Model defines a sanction magnitude s applied to free-riders but not cost per punishment point for the punisher; monitors are paid from tax pool, not paying per sanction.",0.7,N/R,No explicit mapping of 1 unit of punisher expenditure to sanction magnitude; only overall sanction s used.,0.7,0,No rewarding scheme is introduced in Variant 1; only reimbursement of monitors and sanctions for free-riders.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modelled.,1,N/A,Rewards are not modelled.,1,N/R,No per-round endowment or token amount is specified; only baseline utility u0 and costs/benefits.,0.8,N/R,They derive conditions under which cooperation is favoured but do not report numeric equilibrium contribution fractions.,0.65,N/R,No specific equilibrium n_C or α values are instantiated numerically.,0.7,N/R,No explicit ratio of achieved group payoff to maximum possible payoff is computed numerically.,0.7,N/R,Utility/payoff is expressed symbolically; no numerical group payoff is reported.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical evolutionary game-theoretic analysis without agents in a lab or field setting.,1,"n-player linear public goods game for provisioning a common-pool resource; binary decisions: cooperate (provision at cost C_C, each cooperator adds α units) or defect; additional binary traits: pay institutional tax (cost C_τ) and monitor (cost C_M = pδ). Free-riders monitored with probability proportional to p n_M / n and sanctioned with magnitude s. Monitoring in Variant 1 is reimbursed from a separate institutional tax pool: B_M = β n_τ C_τ / n_M.",Summarised directly from Section 5.1 and 5.2 equations and descriptions.,0.98
10.1016_j.jecp.2023.105858.md,Variant 2 – Monitoring funded from common-pool resource,Paper defines 'Variant 2' where monitors are paid directly from the common-pool resource; treated as distinct analytical condition.,0.96,Funding mechanism for monitoring: fixed fraction β of common-pool resource; no separate tax C_τ (C_τ=0); agents decide whether to cooperate (l_C) and monitor (l_M).,Key difference from Variant 1 is the institutional rule on how monitoring is financed (collective decision using common-pool vs unilateral tax pool).,0.96,False,No human or animal subjects; analytic evolutionary game theoretic treatment.,1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment; no data collection or random assignment.,1,False,No laboratory or online human experiment conducted.,1,False,Variant 2 is explored with equations and inequalities; simulations are mentioned generically for ABM but not run for this model.,0.86,True,"They derive formal conditions (e.g., αβ/δ > C_C/s and β > δ C_C/(α s)) using evolutionary game theory.",1,N/R,"Group size is represented symbolically by n, but no concrete value is chosen.",0.9,N/R,Repeated rounds/generations t are assumed but no specific number of rounds is fixed.,0.7,1,"As in base model, cooperation is a binary decision l_C∈{0,1}, i.e. either provision α units or not.",0.9,N/A,No endowment allocation between private and public accounts for human players; purely analytical.,1,N/R,Linear public good is specified via α per cooperator and n players but MPCR is not identified numerically or symbolically as such.,0.6,N/A,No human agents or explicit communication channel; political game to choose β is discussed abstractly only.,1,0,No interface for showing payoffs is modelled; only payoff equations.,0.85,N/A,Model does not distinguish identities of sanctioners; punishment is institutional via monitoring and sanctions s.,0.95,N/A,No rewards are modelled.,1,0,No experimental interface; only theoretical analysis.,1,1,"As in base model, monitored free-riders incur sanction s; Variant 2 only changes payment source for monitors.",1,N/R,Sanction magnitude s is defined but not tied to a specific per-unit cost of punishment effort by an agent.,0.7,N/R,No explicit technology ratio from punisher expenditure to sanction magnitude is specified.,0.7,0,No reward or positive bonus mechanism is modelled; only reimbursement of monitors and sanctioning of defection.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/R,Model specifies baseline utility u0 and costs/benefits but not a numeric endowment per round.,0.8,N/R,They identify conditions under which full cooperation is an equilibrium but do not report numerical contribution rates.,0.7,N/R,No concrete numerical values for n_C or α are instantiated to yield a contribution amount.,0.7,N/R,Discussion of social welfare is qualitative; no efficiency ratio is calculated.,0.7,N/R,Only symbolic payoffs are presented; group payoff not given as a specific number.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely analytical evolutionary game theory; no experimental setting.,1,"Same underlying n-player linear public goods game as base model: each agent can cooperate (pay cost C_C to add α units to CPR) or defect, and can also monitor (cost C_M = pδ). In Variant 2, no institutional tax is paid (C_τ=0). A fraction β of the common-pool resource is used to pay monitors: group benefit share becomes B_G(t) = (1−β)(1/n) α n_C(t), and each monitor receives B_M = α β n_C / n_M. Free-riders monitored with probability p n_M / n suffer sanction s. Paper derives conditions such as αβ/δ > C_C/s and β > (δ C_C)/(α s) for full cooperation with sufficient monitoring.",Summarised from Section 5.3 equations (9)–(14) and associated description.,0.99
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2021.104429.md,"PrivateLow (κ = 0.2, monitoring private)","One of four treatments in the 2×2 design, distinguished by low monitoring cost (κ=0.2) and private monitoring decisions, labeled in text as PrivateLow.",1,Monitoring cost κ = 0.2 (Low); Monitoring observability = Private,Treatment varies two between-subject factors: cost per monitoring unit (Low vs High) and whether monitoring decisions are public vs private. This condition combines Low cost with Private observability.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 240 human student subjects split across treatments.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments and fixed parameters.,1,True,Conducted at the Wiso-Lab of the University of Hamburg.,1,False,No agent-based or computational-only simulations reported.,1,False,Paper contains theoretical discussion but main results here are from lab experiment with humans.,0.9,4,“Subjects play in fixed groups of four (partner design).”,1,20,“for a pre-announced twenty periods.”,1,1,"Contributions g_i ∈ {0,…,20}; any integer amount allowed, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each period endowment is 22 ECU held privately; players then choose how much to contribute. So initial share in public good is 0.,1,0.4,“Each ECU contributed to the public good yields a benefit of 0.4 ECU (the marginal per capita return) to every player in the group.”,1,0,"No communication or chat is mentioned; only monitoring, contribution, and punishment stages.",0.95,1,"After each period, players learn stage two payoff, total punishment points received, and final payoff, which summarize others’ impact on them.",0.8,0,"“Players learn only the total sum of punishment points received, but do not learn the number or identity of the punisher(s).”",1,0,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,1,Game length of 20 periods is pre-announced; subjects therefore know total number of rounds.,0.9,1,Stage three allows spending ECU on punishment points to reduce others’ earnings.,1,1,“Each punishment point ... reduces the punisher's income by one ECU.” Cost per point =1.,1,3,“Each punishment point assigned to another player leads to a deduction of three ECUs from the punished player's account.”,1,0,No positive reward or bonus mechanism is described; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward stage or mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward stage or mechanism.,1,22,"“At the beginning of each period, each player receives an endowment of 22 ECU.”",1,14.73,"Table 1 reports average contribution of 14.73 ECU. Normalized rate = 14.73/20 ≈ 0.7365. However the schema asks for a 0–1 fraction while the example they give in text uses raw contributions in that slot; because the JSON field is numeric and examples like Table 1 are central, I record the raw mean here to avoid fabrication but note this mismatch.",0.5,14.73,"From Table 1, row ‘Contribution’, treatment PrivateLow, mean 14.73 ECU.",1,N/R,"Rightmost panel of Fig.1 and Table 5 discuss ‘efficiency gains’, but exact average efficiency value by treatment is not numerically reported.",0,26.63,"Table 1, row ‘Earnings’, PrivateLow mean 26.63 ECU per subject; group payoff is 4× this, but the DV is at group level or subject-level is not specified. I record the reported per-subject earnings.",0.6,Germany,Experiment conducted at University of Hamburg (Germany); subjects are mostly local students.,0.95,N/R,Only described as mostly undergraduate students; no age statistics given.,0,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,0,Mostly undergraduate students from various fields,“with mostly undergraduate students from various fields.”,1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory (Wiso-Lab) with subjects seated and instructions read aloud.,1,"Voluntary contribution mechanism with partner matching; groups of 4 fixed across 20 periods. Stage 1: monitoring choice m_i ∈ {0,1,2} at cost κ=0.2 per unit, affecting signal accuracy λ_i = 1/2 + (1/4)m_i. Stage 2: contribution choice g_i ∈ {0,…,20} from 22 ECU endowment; MPCR=0.4. Signals s_j about others’ contributions can be accurate with probability λ_i or a random alternative number otherwise; players sharing same m_i see identical signals. Stage 3: peer punishment; each ECU spent imposes 3 ECU loss on target and costs 1 ECU to punisher, up to 10 ECU per target. Monitoring choices are private in this treatment; others do not observe m_i.","Summarized from detailed design description for this specific treatment condition, setting κ=0.2 and private monitoring.",1
