aled to all players in the group before they choose contributions. Public monitoring can serve as a signal of willingness to punish and affects contribution behavior, though monitoring technology and punishment structure remain unchanged.","Design description for Public treatments states that in Public, all players are informed about others’ monitoring decisions before contribution stage; cost and other parameters set to κ=0.2 in Low.",1
10.1016_j.jpubeco.2021.104429.md,"PublicHigh (κ = 1, monitoring public)",Treatment combining high monitoring cost with public monitoring; labeled PublicHigh.,1,Monitoring cost κ = 1 (High); Monitoring observability = Public,One cell of 2×2: High cost and public knowledge of monitoring decisions.,1,True,Human-subject lab treatment.,1,True,Part of same controlled experiment.,1,True,Run in Wiso-Lab at University of Hamburg.,1,False,No simulation-only results here.,1,False,"Condition refers to experimental treatment, not theoretical model.",1,4,Groups of four throughout.,1,20,20 periods for all treatments.,1,1,Contribution choice 0–20 ECU.,1,0,Endowment initially entirely private.,1,0.4,Fixed MPCR across treatments.,1,0,No chat or communication described.,0.95,1,"End-of-period information includes own payoffs and punishment received, which summarize others’ actions affecting payoffs.",0.8,0,Subjects do not learn identity or number of punishers.,1,0,No rewards used.,1,1,Finite horizon is announced to subjects.,0.9,1,Punishment stage present.,1,1,Punishment point costs 1 ECU.,1,3,Each punishment point deducts 3 ECU from target.,1,0,No rewarding system.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,22,22 ECU per player per period.,1,9.35,"Table 1: mean contribution 9.35 ECU for PublicHigh; normalized share ≈0.4675, but raw mean is placed here as before.",0.5,9.35,"From Table 1, treatment PublicHigh.",1,N/R,"Ranked as relatively inefficient (higher costs, lower contributions) but no numeric efficiency mean provided.",0,24.09,"Table 1, ‘Earnings’, PublicHigh mean 24.09 ECU per subject.",0.6,Germany,Same lab and recruitment source.,0.95,N/R,Only ‘mostly undergraduate’ mentioned.,0,N/R,No report of gender share.,0,Mostly undergraduate students from various fields,Same subject population description.,1,On site,In-person lab sessions.,1,"Identical public goods game with costly monitoring and punishment as in other treatments, but with κ=1 per monitoring unit and public observability of each player’s monitoring decision before contributions. Monitoring can act as a strong signal of willingness to punish, though high cost leads to less monitoring and lower average contributions compared to Low-cost treatments.",Design differs only in monitoring cost κ=1 and public observability of monitoring choices relative to PrivateHigh and PublicLow.,1
10.1109_tac.2021.3087652.md,"Simulation – Infinite-horizon DISG with binary state, BSC observations, c=0.027, p1=p2=0.6","Single simulation configuration explicitly described in Section IV-C (Experiments): binary Markov chain, binary symmetric channels with p1=p2=0.6, cost c=0.027, δ=0.9.",1,"Observation quality and communication cost: p1=0.6, p2=0.6, communication cost c=0.027, discount factor δ=0.9. Both agents symmetric.","Only varying elements across discussed simulation setups are BSC parameters p1, p2 and cost c; this row uses p1=p2=0.6 and c=0.027 as stated.",1,False,Experiments use a simulated Markov chain and simulated agents solved via POMCP; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or field data; it is a numerical simulation of a theoretical model.,1,False,No laboratory or human setting; purely computational.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe using an online planning algorithm (modified POMCP) to simulate the POMDP and approximate equilibrium regions.,1,False,"While the paper contains analytical results, this specific section is labeled experiments and relies on simulation with POMCP.",1,2,"DISG model defined with two agents (n ∈ {1,2}) and simulations follow that model.",1,-1,Infinite-horizon discounted game is simulated; number of rounds not fixed/finite.,0.9,1,"Action set A={0,1} is all-or-nothing for sharing, but there is no contribution of divisible resources as in a public goods game; mapped as allowing only discrete choices, but within this framework set to 1 because binary share/no-share is the only decision, not a continuous contribution level.",0.5,N/A,No monetary or token contributions; only information sharing decisions.,1,N/R,"No public goods payoff structure; efficiency gains are via information (mutual information), not MPCR.",1,N/A,No humans and no explicit communication stage beyond modelled observation sharing; chat concept not applicable.,1,N/A,Simulation of agents with full modelled beliefs; no user interface or summaries for human subjects.,1,N/A,"Punishment is implemented as strategy (CGT) in belief space, not as explicit costly actions identifying other agents.",1,N/A,"No explicit rewards between identified agents, only own payoff from information and cost.",1,N/A,No interface; infinite horizon simulation for autonomous agents.,1,1,Punishment is via constrained grim trigger: deviation triggers permanent non-cooperation (s=0) which reduces future rewards; this is a punishment regime embedded in strategies.,0.9,N/R,"Punishment is endogenous loss of future information benefits, not modeled as separate costly action with per-unit cost.",1,N/R,No numeric mapping from punishment expenditure to impact is specified.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond standard payoffs; only cooperation vs non-cooperation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No notion of endowment/tokens; agents collect information and incur transmission costs.,1,N/R,"Outcome of interest is cooperation (information sharing) region in belief space, not contributions.",1,N/R,No monetary contributions.,1,N/R,Paper does not report aggregate efficiency as ratio of payoffs to maximum; focus is on existence and size of equilibrium cooperation region.,1,N/R,Simulations visualize cooperation regions; no numeric group payoff values are reported.,1,N/A,No participants; simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results in the experiments section come from simulations of the theoretical model.,1,"Two-agent infinite-horizon dynamic information sharing game (DISG). State X_t in {0,1} with transition probabilities P(X_{t+1}=0|X_t=0)=0.8, P(X_{t+1}=1|X_t=0)=0.2, P(X_{t+1}=0|X_t=1)=0.15, P(X_{t+1}=1|X_t=1)=0.85. Each agent observes a binary symmetric channel: P(Y^1=0|X=0)=P(Y^1=1|X=1)=p1=0.6, similarly for agent 2 with p2=0.6. At each stage, each agent chooses A^n∈{0,1} (share or not). Reward is conditional mutual information gain from received observation minus cost c=0.027 if A^n=1. Discount factor δ=0.9. Agents use constrained grim trigger strategies parametrized by belief regions; equilibrium cooperation region in belief simplex is approximated via modified POMCP over a discretized belief grid.",Compiled from the model description and experiment setup in Section II and Section IV-C.,1
