r token or unit in a generic framework.",0.4,N/R,"η is the fine to defectors, but no per-unit technology mapping to spent tokens is defined.",0.4,1,Government subsidy α rewards three-faced individuals when punishing defectors.,1,N/R,"Reward α is an exogenous subsidy, not the result of agents spending reward tokens.",0.3,N/R,No token-based reward conversion specified.,0.3,N/A,No individual endowment is defined; payoffs are direct from the payoff matrix.,1,N/A,No contributions or public goods; only strategy frequencies.,1,N/A,No contribution amounts modeled.,1,N/R,"They analyze fractions of strategies and stability, not explicit efficiency ratios.",0.3,N/R,Average payoff is defined (\bar{P}) but no particular numeric value is emphasized as a reported outcome.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation study without human subjects.,1,"Three-strategy prisoner’s dilemma in an infinite, well-mixed population with cooperation (C), defection (D), and three-faced (T) strategies; payoffs R=1, P=S=0, T=b (1<b≤2). T acts as defector vs C (payoff b−δ to T, 0 to C), as punisher vs D (T gets α−γ−δ, D pays fine η), and as cooperator vs T (1−δ each). Dynamics follow replicator equations for x,y,z with x+y+z=1.",Summarized from Section 2 ‘Model’ and Table 1.,1
