10.1016_j.physa.2024.129631.md,"Structured population simulation – spatial lattice, varying δ (α=0.2 for δ=0.01, α=1.5 otherwise)",Condition corresponding to Fig. 7 showing snapshots on L×L square lattice with different δ values and two α settings.,0.95,"Role-switching cost δ ∈ {0.01, 0.2, 0.35, 0.6, 1.15}; for δ=0.01, α=0.2; for δ∈{0.2,0.35,0.6,1.15}, α=1.5; other parameters b=1.1, γ=0.8 (or 0.4 for δ=0.01), η=0.8, selection intensity K=0.1.","Fig. 7 caption: δ values listed; (a) uses b=1.1, α=0.2, γ=0.4, η=0.8; (b)-(e) use b=1.1, α=1.5, γ=0.8, η=0.8; text states all simulations use K=0.1 on L×L lattice.",1,False,Agent-based simulation with no human subjects.,1,False,"Numerical simulation, not empirical experiment.",1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations on a 100×100 square lattice with Fermi update rule.,1,False,This part is purely computational; no new analytical derivation specific to lattice beyond generic payoffs.,0.8,10000,L×L lattice with L=100 implies 100×100=10000 players.,1,10000,‘All simulations are performed ... and the whole simulation process is carried out in 10000 Monte Carlo time steps.’,1,1,"Each agent has a fixed strategy from {C,D,T}; no continuous contributions; encoded as general non-all-or-nothing contribution field; we follow same coding as other non-contribution PD models.",0.3,0,"Each player’s payoff comes from interactions with neighbors; there is no notion of initial contribution to public pool; by convention, we set default contribution proportion to 0 (all payoff potential initially private).",0.3,N/A,"Pairwise PD on lattice, not public goods.",1,0,No communication among simulated agents.,1,0,Agents receive payoffs locally; there is no display of global summaries.,1,0,"Punishment is implicit in matrix payoffs; agents observe only neighbor strategies and payoffs, not explicit IDs.",0.7,0,Government subsidy is applied automatically; no identity display.,0.7,0,Artificial agents are updated by fixed schedule; no interface showing remaining rounds.,1,1,"Three-faced agents punish defectors in their neighborhood, with cost γ and fine η.",1,N/R,γ is per interaction punishment cost but not framed in generic coin units.,0.5,N/R,η is fine per interaction but no explicit per-coin technology is defined.,0.5,1,"When T punishes D, T gets government subsidy α; present in both parameter sets (α=0.2 or 1.5).",1,N/R,Reward is exogenous subsidy; no cost to rewarding agent specified.,0.3,N/R,No explicit reward mechanism per coin.,0.3,N/R,Model does not define per-period endowment; only payoff accumulation.,0.4,N/R,"No contributions defined; only strategy ratios. This field is not applicable, but since there are agents we use N/R rather than N/A per instructions.",0.4,N/R,No contribution amounts modeled.,0.4,N/R,"They report only qualitative dominance and coexistence patterns, not normalized efficiency.",0.3,N/R,Group or average payoff not numerically reported for spatial simulations.,0.4,N/A,"Agents are simulated, not human participants.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice.,1,"Agents occupy nodes of a 100×100 square lattice with periodic boundaries and 4 von Neumann neighbors. Each agent’s strategy ∈ {C,D,T}. Payoffs per pairwise interaction given by Table 1. At each Monte Carlo step, a randomly selected agent i and neighbor j compare payoffs p_i and p_j (sum over neighbors), and i adopts j’s strategy with probability W_{i←j} = 1 / (1 + exp[(p_i − p_j)/K]) with K=0.1. Simulations run for 10000 time steps, examining how varying δ affects spatial coexistence and dominance of C, D, and T.",Summarized from ‘structured population’ section and Fig. 7 caption.,1
10.1016_j.chaos.2024.114699.md,"Simulation – NRNG, Random selection (RS)",Distinct simulation condition defined by non-intervened groups not updating policy status (NRNG) and central institution using random selection (RS) of intervened groups.,0.97,Selection method = Random selection (RS); Non-intervened group behavior = NRNG (no SP updating); Fraction of intervened groups p (varied 0–1); Normalized multiplier ω (varied); Policy cost coefficient μ (varied); Penalty β (varied).,"Paper systematically varies selection method (RS/DS/SS), NRNG vs RNG, p, ω, μ, and β and reports outcome surfaces and curves by these parameters.",0.97,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a birandom geometric graph with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled lab/field experiment; it is a computational model.,1,False,No physical lab or field environment; purely in silico.,1,True,Authors describe iterating 10^4 Monte Carlo Steps on generated BRGG networks; all results are from simulations.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria; results are numerical.,0.95,1500,Model section sets N = 1500 player nodes.,1,10000,They iterate 10^4 MCSs and average over the last 10^3 as the final result; interpret 10^4 as number of rounds.,0.9,1,"Each cooperator contributes fixed c=1 and defectors 0; no option to vary partial contribution, so action is binary cooperated/defected rather than a continuous amount; coded as ‘can contribute any amount’=1 is inaccurate but schema only distinguishes all-or-nothing vs continuous. Given two discrete options (0 or 1), this is closer to all-or-nothing; set to 0.",0.6,N/R,No explicit endowment allocation between public and private accounts; only rule is that cooperators pay cost c=1.,0.2,N/R,They define group-specific multiplier r_G = 1 + ω(|G|-1) but do not report marginal per capita return r_G/|G| explicitly and it varies by group size.,0.5,N/A,No human subjects; communication not modeled.,1,N/R,"Simulation does not specify any UI-like feedback such as summaries, only payoffs for imitation dynamics.",0.2,N/A,No identities in simulation; punishment is anonymous and rule-based.,1,N/A,"Reward is implemented as redistribution of fines, not individually identified.",1,N/R,Agents do not have explicit knowledge of horizon; number of MCS is a simulation control parameter only.,0.3,1,Intervened and spontaneous groups implement fines β on defectors when sp=1; this is a punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,Cost of punishment for punishers is not modeled as a separate per-unit expense; instead there is a separate policy cost μ|G| borne per member in some groups.,0.9,N/R,"They state each defector is fined β but do not express it as ‘fine per 1 unit of cost to punisher’, since punisher cost is zero.",0.8,1,"Fines collected from defectors are distributed equally to cooperators, which functions as an endogenous reward.",1,N/A,"Reward is funded by collected penalties, not by extra costly actions of cooperators or institution.",0.9,N/R,Amount rewarded per fined unit depends on number of cooperators and defectors and is not given as a simple rate per cost.,0.7,N/R,Game is framed in terms of per-round contribution cost c=1 and payoffs; initial endowment per player per round is not specified.,0.2,N/R,"Main outcome is fraction of cooperation F_c, which is effectively the fraction of players choosing s_i=1, but no single numeric value is reported for a specific parameter set in text.",0.4,N/R,Only binary strategies (cooperate/defect) with c=1 are defined; no aggregate contribution amount is numerically reported.,0.4,N/R,They report total social welfare W_t but not normalized efficiency relative to theoretical maximum.,0.3,N/R,"They discuss total welfare W_t and average payoffs, but no specific numeric group payoff for a given parameter set.",0.3,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from Monte Carlo simulations on graphs.,1,Regional public goods game on a birandom geometric graph (BRGG) with N=1500 players and M=1000 public good nodes. Groups are players within circular influence regions (radius d=0.06). Cooperators pay cost c=1; group benefit multiplier r_G = 1 + ω(|G|-1); payoffs depend on group size and policy status sp (0 or 1). Intervened groups (sp=1) have institutional reward-punishment with fine β to defectors and redistribution to cooperators; policy cost for intervened groups is μ|G|^2 but borne by central institution; common groups (sp=0) have no policy. NRNG: non-intervened groups never change SP.,Summarized from model description in Sections 2.1–2.3 for this specific NRNG+RS condition.,1
