10.3389_fpubh.2024.1373747.md,"Scenario 1 – Low government benefit, low penalty, moderate construction cost","Paper labels this as Scenario 1 in the simulation analysis with specific parameter values C1=8, R1=6, L1=4, C2=6, A=5, P=3, R2=7, C3=2, C4=6, L=4.",1,"Parameter setting: C1=8, R1=6, L1=4, C2=6, A=5, P=3, R2=7, C3=2, C4=6, L=4; condition -C1−L1+R1+1<0 holds","Scenario 1 is defined by this particular parameter vector and inequality, which drives the evolutionary outcome.",1,False,No human participants; the study analyzes deterministic evolutionary game dynamics and then runs numerical simulations on differential equations.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a computational simulation of a mathematical model.,1,False,No lab or physical setting; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,True,Section 4 is titled 'Simulation analysis' and describes 'simulation experiment' results for each scenario based on parameter values.,1,True,The paper derives closed-form replicator dynamics and stability conditions before simulating them.,1,3,"Three strategic populations: government, agricultural producers, consumers.",1,0,Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics without discrete rounds; not a repeated finite game.,0.8,1,"Players choose between discrete strategies (e.g., Regulate vs Not regulate) rather than continuous contribution levels; mapped as not an all-or-nothing contribution game, but here contribution concept is inapplicable. Using 1 because they are not restricted to a contribution fraction framework.",0.3,N/A,No human subjects or contribution endowment structure; evolutionary game over discrete strategies.,1,N/R,No public goods structure or marginal per capita return specified.,1,N/A,No human interaction; only model variables.,1,0,No information-display mechanism is modeled; only payoffs and strategies in replicator dynamics.,1,0,Punishment is parameter P applied at population level; no identities or individual-level observability discussed.,1,0,"Incentive A is modeled as a payoff parameter, not as an identity-linked reward.",1,0,No explicit rounds or displayed horizon; continuous-time evolutionary model.,1,1,Parameter P denotes penalties for not constructing the traceability system; L is additional loss to producers; these are modeled punishments.,1,N/R,"Model uses lump-sum penalty parameter P and loss L, not a per-unit cost of punishing borne by a punisher.",1,N/R,No mapping of punishment investment to impact per unit; only direct payoffs.,1,1,"Parameter A is 'Government incentives for agricultural producers to choose ""Build""'.",1,N/R,"No explicit cost per unit reward; government reward A is modeled as producers' gain, not decomposed.",1,N/R,No per-unit mapping from rewards to benefits; A enters directly as payoff.,1,N/R,No concept of endowment or tokens; only payoffs/costs.,1,N/A,No contribution or public good; outcome is strategy proportions and stability.,1,N/A,No contribution quantities measured.,1,N/R,No efficiency ratio relative to a benchmark is computed; only qualitative welfare interpretations.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff or social welfare number reported for this scenario.,1,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Analysis and simulations are performed on mathematical models, not with human subjects.",1,"Three-population evolutionary game among government (Regulate/Not regulate), agricultural producers (Build/Not build a digital traceability system), and consumers (Satisfied/Dissatisfied) with payoffs defined via parameters C1, R1, L1, C2, A, P, R2, C3, C4, L; Scenario 1 satisfies -C1−L1+R1+1<0 and converges to equilibrium (Not regulate, Not build, Satisfied).",Summarizes game structure and the specific stable strategy described for Scenario 1.,1
10.3389_fpubh.2024.1373747.md,"Scenario 2 – High regulation benefit, high construction cost, moderate penalty","Paper labels this as Scenario 2 in the simulation analysis with C1=8, R1=9, L1=4, C2=8, A=5, P=3, R2=7, C3=2, C4=6, L=4.",1,"Parameter setting: C1=8, R1=9, L1=4, C2=8, A=5, P=3, R2=7, C3=2, C4=6, L=4; conditions C1+L1−R1−1<0 and −C2+A+P<0 hold","Scenario 2 is defined by this parameter set and the given inequalities, determining the evolutionary equilibrium.",1,False,No human participants; simulation of replicator dynamics.,1,False,"The term 'simulation experiment' refers to numerical experiments on equations, not lab/field experiments.",1,False,No physical lab; computations only.,1,True,Scenario 2 is explicitly part of the 'simulation experiment' section.,1,True,Uses previously derived analytical model and stability conditions; simulations illustrate them.,1,3,Same three strategic populations as overall model.,1,0,Continuous-time dynamics; not discrete rounds.,0.8,1,Model is not a contribution game; binary strategies used rather than continuous contributions.,0.3,N/A,No endowment or contribution structure.,1,N/R,No public good or multiplier described.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication.,1,0,No such information feedback is modeled.,1,0,"Punishments are parameters, not identity-based peer sanctions.",1,0,Rewards are modeled as payoff parameters only.,1,0,No explicit number or display of rounds.,1,1,Penalty parameter P and loss L for not building traceability system remain present.,1,N/R,No separate cost for imposing punishment is specified.,1,N/R,No per-unit punishment effect described.,1,1,Government incentive A is included for producers who build.,1,N/R,No explicit cost to government per unit incentive.,1,N/R,Reward is direct payoff A without technological mapping.,1,N/R,No token or resource endowment; only payoff parameters.,1,N/A,No contribution concept in this model.,1,N/A,No contributions measured.,1,N/R,"Scenario outputs focus on strategy probabilities, not explicit efficiency metrics.",1,N/R,No aggregate payoff for all players is computed for Scenario 2.,1,N/A,"The 'participants' are modeled populations, not real individuals.",1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,No human,Numerical simulations on mathematical model only.,1,"Same three-population evolutionary game as Scenario 1. Scenario 2 sets higher government benefit R1 and higher producer construction cost C2 with the inequalities C1+L1−R1−1<0 and −C2+A+P<0. Simulation shows government regulation and consumer satisfaction converge to 1, while producers converge to Not build, yielding equilibrium (Regulate, Not build, Satisfied).","Summarizes the condition and resulting ESS noted as (1,0,1) in the text.",1
