10.1016_j.amc.2024.129156.md,Analytical model – endogenous punishment (0 < p < 1),"Condition where cooperators punish defectors with probability p (0<p<1), labeled as endogenous punishment in the text.",1,"Punishment regime: endogenous stochastic punishment with punishment participation probability p ∈ (0,1), cost α, fine β; network degree k and enforcement factor r varied analytically.","Independent variables are punishment parameters (p, α, β) and network structure (k, r); this row focuses on nonzero p with 0<p<1.",1,False,Model is theoretical with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; only analytical derivations and theoretical phase diagrams.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,"Main results derived analytically (closed-form g(x), rc, ro, pc, pt).",0.7,True,"Explicit analytical expressions for payoffs, dynamics, and thresholds under endogenous punishment.",1,2222222222,Infinite population assumption.,1,2222222222,Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics; not a finite-round game.,1,1,Players choose between C (contribute fixed c) and D (contribute 0); no partial contribution choice.,1,2222222222,No explicit per-round endowment or starting allocation; model specifies a cost c for cooperation only.,1,N/R,MPCR could be r/(k+1) but is not defined or used under that name; thus treated as not reported.,0.6,2222222222,No human interface; communication not modeled.,1,0,No explicit observation of others’ payoffs beyond what is implicit in evolutionary updating.,1,0,Punishment is probabilistic and ‘not overt’ to avoid retaliation; identities are not modeled as revealed.,0.8,2222222222,No reward mechanism; not applicable.,1,0,No notion of displayed remaining rounds in the analytical model.,1,1,Cooperators punish defectors with probability p>0 at cost α imposing fine β.,1,N/R,"Cost per punished defector is α, but the scale of payoff units is not mapped to tokens/coins as in an experiment; numeric value depends on parameter choice (e.g., α=0.3 in examples) rather than a single experimental constant.",0.6,N/R,"Fine per punished defector is β, but no fixed numeric technology parameter in standardized units; examples use β=1 but not as a universal setting.",0.6,0,Only punishment incentives are modeled.,1,2222222222,No reward; not applicable.,1,2222222222,No reward; not applicable.,1,N/R,"As in the baseline, only a cost c for cooperation is defined; no explicit per-round endowment.",0.6,N/R,"Primary outcome variables are the frequency of cooperation x, threshold pc, and basin of attraction sizes; not normalized to an endowment-based contribution rate.",0.7,N/R,No specific numeric steady-state contributions are tabulated.,0.7,N/R,"Although average payoff can be computed from given formulas, no explicit efficiency ratio is reported.",0.6,N/R,Expressions for payoffs exist but no focal summarized group payoff values are given as results.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical evolutionary model.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a regular network of degree k (group size k+1). Cooperators contribute c; defectors contribute 0. Total contribution multiplied by r and shared evenly. Endogenous punishment: each cooperator independently chooses to punish with probability p, paying cost α per punished defector and imposing fine β per punishing cooperator on each defector. Evolutionary dynamics follow death-birth updating on graphs under weak selection with pair approximation; conditions for bistability, thresholds rc, ro, pc, and pt are derived.",Summarizes the endogenous punishment model as described in Methods and Results.,1
10.1016_j.amc.2024.129156.md,Analytical model – deterministic punishment (p = 1),"Special case mentioned explicitly where p=1 so that all cooperators always punish, termed deterministic punishment.",1,"Punishment regime: deterministic punishment with p = 1, cost α, fine β; network degree k and enforcement factor r varied analytically.","Independent variable here is the punishment regime with p fixed at 1, contrasted with endogenous (0<p<1) and no punishment (p=0).",1,False,No human participants; purely theoretical treatment of p=1 case.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,"Results (e.g., x* for p=1) are given analytically.",0.7,True,Closed-form expression for unstable equilibrium x* under p=1 and analysis of its dependence on α and β.,1,2222222222,Infinite population on a regular network.,1,2222222222,Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics; no discrete finite rounds.,1,1,Same underlying PGG structure: contribute c or 0.,1,2222222222,No explicit endowment structure; not applicable.,1,N/R,MPCR terminology not used; only r and k appear.,0.6,2222222222,No human subjects.,1,0,No user interface or information display modeled.,1,0,"As in endogenous case, punishment identities are not part of the model; punishment is mechanistic.",0.8,2222222222,No reward process.,1,0,No concept of displayed rounds.,1,1,"Punishment is always executed by cooperators (p=1) at cost α, fine β.",1,N/R,"Punishment cost per defector is α, but only symbolic and example numeric values (α=0.3) given; not a single experimental constant.",0.6,N/R,Fine per defector per punisher is β; examples use β=1 but not as a unique experimental parameter.,0.6,0,No rewards considered.,1,2222222222,Reward not modeled.,1,2222222222,Reward not modeled.,1,N/R,"Only cost c is defined, no explicit endowment.",0.6,N/R,"Focus is on equilibrium cooperation frequency x* and its dependence on parameters, not normalized contributions.",0.7,N/R,No explicit numeric contributions reported.,0.7,N/R,E