10.1016_j.chaos.2025.116297.md,"Observation probability S_i = 0.05, Barabási–Albert, N=100",Section 3.4: Influence of S_i—explicit case S_i=0.05.,0.9,"Observation probability S_i = 0.05; Network topology = Barabási–Albert; N=100; T=3; ε=0.1, α=0.1, γ=0.9",Text: ‘when S_i is extremely low (0.05 for example)…’.,0.9,False,Simulation study.,1,False,No human experiment.,1,False,Computational.,1,True,S_i variation in simulation.,1,False,Not analytical.,1,100,Assumed N=100.,0.7,20000,Same MC protocol; not stated otherwise.,0.7,1,Same decision space.,0.8,N/A,No endowment game.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No humans/chat.,1,0,Agents only observe own payoff.,0.9,0,No explicit identity mechanism.,0.8,0,No rewards.,1,0,No description of observing remaining rounds.,0.8,1,Punishment and metapunishment continue to exist.,1,2,E=-2 constant.,1,9,P=-9 constant.,1,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No initial endowment.,0.3,N/R,Qualitatively: ‘community continued to maintain good order’ and order collapse requires more cycles; no explicit cooperation fraction.,0.3,N/R,No contributions quantified.,1,N/R,No efficiency statistics.,0.2,N/R,No group payoff numbers.,0.2,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation.,1,"Metanorm game with very low observation probability S_i=0.05. Findings: community still maintains good order; number of cycles required for order collapse increases as S_i decreases, showing that lower observability delays collapse but cooperation persists.",Based on S_i variation discussion in Section 3.4.,0.7
10.1007_s11538-025-01422-4.md,Two-strategy PDE – defectors vs altruistic punishers,"Paper’s core model is a PDE for multilevel selection with two strategies (defectors D and altruistic punishers P), used analytically and numerically throughout (Sects. 3, 5, 6).",1,"Model parameters of altruistic punishment and multilevel selection: cost of cooperation c, benefit b, punishment strength p, fixed punishment cost q, per-interaction punishment cost k, and relative strength of between-group competition λ; plus functional form of group-level victory probability ρ(z,u).",These are the only quantities varied to study different regimes; they define the ‘conditions’ in this theoretical/simulation framework.,1,False,No human or animal subjects; purely mathematical modeling and numerical simulation.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical PDE and stochastic-process analysis.,1,False,No laboratory or experimental setting; all work is analytical/numerical.,1,True,"Uses numerical solutions of PDEs and simulations of multilevel dynamics without human subjects (e.g., Figs. 2–12).",1,True,"Main results are closed-form PDEs, steady states, thresholds, and theorems (Theorems 1–2, Propositions 2–5).",1,2,Within-group interactions are pairwise donation games between two individuals (Sec. 2.1).,1,1,"Game is an abstract stage game; evolutionary dynamics iterate in continuous time, not repeated strategic rounds; encode as single-stage interaction.",0.7,1,"Strategies are discrete (D vs P) but there is no choice of a continuous contribution amount; contribution concept is not parameterized as a decision variable, so this field is treated as allowing general (non-binary-amount) modeling.",0.3,N/A,No explicit endowment or contribution decision by players; model specifies fixed donation-game payoffs rather than contribution choices.,1,N/R,"Donation game is parameterized by benefit b and cost c, not by a marginal per capita return in a linear public goods sense.",1,0,No communication or signaling mechanism between agents is modeled.,1,0,Agents do not observe payoffs or histories; model is purely evolutionary with fitness-based reproduction.,1,N/A,"Punishment is represented as payoff parameters p,k,q in a population-level evolutionary model; no individual identities are visible.",1,N/A,"No rewarding mechanism is modeled, only punishment.",1,0,No user interface or subject display exists; number of evolutionary time steps is not shown to agents.,1,1,Core independent mechanism is altruistic punishment: punishers incur costs (k or q) to impose a punishment p on defectors (Sec. 2.1–2.2).,1,N/R,Model has two cost parameters: per-interaction cost k and fixed cost q. There is no single coin-per-punishment cost compatible with this schema.,1,N/R,"Punishment effect on defectors is parameter p, but not expressed as a linear multiplier per unit of punishment expenditure in a token sense.",1,0,No positive incentives or rewards are modeled; only donation benefits and punishment.,1,N/A,"Rewards are absent, so cost is not applicable.",1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,"Donation game is defined via benefits and costs (b,c), not via explicit endowments of tokens or money.",1,N/R,"Outputs are fractions of strategy types (e.g., fraction of punishers z) and average payoffs; no normalized contribution rate is defined.",1,N/R,Model does not track individual contribution magnitudes; cooperation is a binary strategy in a donation game.,1,N/R,Paper reports average group payoff G(z) and its long-run expectation but does not normalize by a theoretical maximum to define an efficiency ratio.,0.8,N/R,G(z) is defined symbolically; specific numerical group payoffs for particular parameter sets are plotted but not tabulated as a single DV.,0.8,N/A,No human participants; only theoretical agents in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All work is analytical PDE and stochastic-process modeling plus numerical simulation.,1,"Continuous-time evolutionary model in a group-structured population. Within each group, individuals play a donation game: cooperators and altruistic punishers pay a cost c to give benefit b to partners. Defectors pay no cost and give no benefit. Altruistic punishers additionally punish defectors with strength p, paying either a per-interaction cost k and/or a fixed cost q. Within-group evolution follows replicator dynamics driven by payoff differences. Between-group competition is modeled either by a two-level replicator term with net group reproduction rate G(z) or by pairwise group conflicts with victory probability ρ(z,u) depending on group composition or payoff (including Fermi and Tullock-type update rules). Key outputs include steady-state densities over z, threshold between-group selection strength λ* for maintaining punishment, and long-run average payoff.","Summarizes the full theoretical game and multilevel selection structure used in the two-strategy PDE, as described in Sects. 2–6.",1
10.1007_s11538-025-01422-4.md,"Three-strategy PDE – cooperators, defectors, altruistic punishers","Section 3.2 and Section 7 define and simulate a three-strategy PDE (C, D, P) on the simplex, with pairwise group competition and additive group-level victory functions.",1,"Same punishment and selection parameters (b,c,p,k,q,λ) as in the two-strategy model, but with an additional pure-cooperator strategy C; plus choice of additive group-level victory function ρ(x,y;u,v) via net group reproduction rate 𝒢(x,y) (e.g., 𝒢=1−y or 𝒢=G(x,y)/(G*−G*)).",Three-strategy model extends the two-strategy PDE by adding the cooperator strategy and alternative group-level reproduction functions; these are the only factors varied.,1,False,No data from human or animal experiments; only mathematical and computational analysis.,1,False,Modeling and simulations are not framed as experimental treatments but as parameter explorations.,1,False,No lab setting; purely in silico.,1,True,"Finite-volume numerical schemes on the 2D simplex are used to approximate PDE solutions (Sec. 7, App. E.2).",1,True,The trimorphic PDE is derived analytically from a multilevel birth–death process; some qualitative comparisons with analytical edge results are made.,1,2,"Within-group interaction remains a pairwise donation game, with C and P cooperating and D defecting (Sec. 2.1, 3.2).",1,1,The interaction is a single-stage donation game repeated implicitly through evolutionary time; not a repeated-game design.,0.7,1,"Strategic choice is which type to be (C,D,P), not a continuous contribution decision; no all-or-nothing contribution constraint is modeled.",0.3,N/A,No endowments or contribution proportions are modeled; actions are cooperate vs defect within a donation-game payoff structure.,1,N/R,Public-good marginal returns are not specified; only donation benefits and costs are used.,1,0,No explicit communication or signaling between individuals or groups.,1,0,Agents do not observe others’ payoffs or actions; only evolutionary dynamics matter.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled via payoff parameters; no observable identities or directed punishment decisions.,1,N/A,No reward is modeled.,1,0,"No experimental interface, so nothing about rounds is displayed to agents.",1,1,"Altruistic punishers P operate exactly as in the two-strategy case, with punishment strength p and costs q,k (Sec. 2, 7).",1,N/R,"Two cost parameters (k per interaction with defectors, q fixed per punisher); they cannot be reduced to a single cost per punishment unit for this schema.",1,N/R,Punishment effect is p in payoff terms; not framed as a linear punishment-technology multiplier per cost unit.,1,0,Only punishment is considered; no rewarding strategy or mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,Individuals do not have modelled endowments; payoffs are directly defined via costs and benefits.,1,N/R,"Outcomes are distributions over strategies (x,y,z) and payoffs; no explicit measure of contribution rate is derived.",1,N/R,Each cooperative act has fixed cost c and benefit b; the model does not track varying contribution amounts.,1,N/R,"Analysis focuses on average group payoff ⟨G(x,y)⟩ and fractions of types; efficiency relative to a maximum is not explicitly normalized and reported.",0.8,N/R,"G(x,y) is explicitly derived and used, but numerical group payoff values for particular parameter sets are not given as a single reported DV; they are plotted as functions over parameter space.",0.8,N/A,Simulation agents are not human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Trimorphic PDE is solved numerically; no empirical environment.,1,"Three-strategy extension of the donation game with altruistic punishment. Strategies: C (cooperator) pays cost c to give benefit b to partner; D (defector) pays no cost, gives no benefit; P (altruistic punisher) pays cost c and gives benefit b, and also punishes defectors: defectors lose p and P pays either per-interaction cost k and/or fixed cost q each period. Within groups, fractions (x,y,z) of C,D,P evolve via three-strategy replicator dynamics derived from payoffs πC(x,y), πD(x,y), πP(x,y). Between-group competition occurs via pairwise conflicts: groups with compositions (x,y) and (u,v) meet, and win with probability ρ(x,y;u,v)=1/2+1/2[𝒢(x,y)−𝒢(u,v)], where 𝒢 reflects either (i) fraction of non-defectors 1−y (Boyd et al. 2003 case) or (ii) normalized average payoff G(x,y)/(G*−G*). Winning groups reproduce (copy) and replace losers. Numerical finite-volume schemes on the simplex are used to study how long-run cooperation and punishment depend on p,k,q and λ, and to compare three-strategy dynamics with two-strategy edge cases (D–C and D–P).","Condenses the three-strategy PDE structure, payoff functions and group competition rules from Sects. 2.1, 3.2, 5.4, and 7.",1
10.1007_s10479-017-2412-8.md,Strategy method treatment,One of two main experimental treatments; uses the strategy method for receiver’s third-stage decisions.,1,Elicitation method = Strategy method,Only between-condition manipulation is elicitation method (strategy vs direct-response). This row captures the strategy method condition.,1,True,Human student participants played the multi-round punishment game in a lab.,1,True,Design with randomized assignment to treatments and controlled payoff structure.,1,True,Sessions conducted in a computer laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations reported; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper includes theoretical discussion and a fitted learning model, but main results are from lab experiment with humans.",0.9,2,Game is played by two parties: player S (sender) and player R (receiver).,1,40,Experimental design specifies that each session was run for 40 rounds.,1,0,"Not a contribution game; players choose among discrete actions (S1/S2, R1/R2, then reward/punish). No continuous contribution choice.",1,N/A,"No endowment-contribution public good structure; decisions are action choices in a payoff matrix, not contributions.",1,N/A,Not a public goods or linear contribution game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,No opportunity for communication other than the structured message M1/M2; instructions forbid talking and no chat tool is mentioned.,1,0,Feedback each round only includes own and current opponent’s decisions and payoffs; no aggregate summaries over others reported.,0.9,0,"Matching is anonymous and opponent identities are not revealed; players only see decisions, not IDs.",1,0,Same anonymity applies to reward decisions; receivers see only actions and payoffs of the current anonymous opponent.,1,0,Subjects were not informed of the number of rounds to avoid end-game effects.,1,1,"Receiver action R6 (changing (12,3) to (2,2)) is explicitly described as punishment.",1,1,"If receiver punishes, his payoff goes from 3 to 2 (loss of 1 unit).",1,10,"Punishment changes sender payoff from 12 to 2, a reduction of 10 units per 1 unit cost to receiver.",1,1,"Receiver action R4 (changing (6,9) to (8,7)) is explicitly described as reward.",1,2,"Reward changes receiver payoff from 9 to 7, a loss of 2 units.",1,2,"Reward changes sender payoff from 6 to 8, an increase of 2 units per 2 units cost to receiver (1:1 rate, coded as +2 per reward action).",0.8,N/R,Payoffs are defined directly by outcome states; no per-round initial token endowment is specified.,0.4,N/R,"No contribution or cooperation rate variable defined; outcomes are action frequencies and payoffs, not fractional contributions.",1,N/R,"No token contributions are made; only discrete actions (messages, S1/S2, R1/R2, reward/punish).",1,N/R,"They report ex-post payoffs by message-action combination, but do not normalize by a theoretical maximum efficiency for a contribution benchmark.",0.6,N/R,"Table 5 reports average combined payoffs conditional on message and action, not an overall aggregate measure per round or per group.",0.6,China,"Subjects were students at a university in China (Tsinghua University, Beijing); currency CNY used.",1,N/R,Participants described only as graduate and undergraduate students; no age range or mean age reported.,0.3,N/R,No information on gender composition is provided.,0.1,Undergraduate and graduate students at a university,Subjects were recruited from graduate and undergraduate students in various majors at a university.,1,On site,Sessions executed in a computer laboratory with physical attendance; not online.,1,"Two-player, three-stage punishment game repeated for 40 rounds with random matching (no same opponent in successive rounds). Stage 1: sender sends cheap-talk message M1/M2 about intended action. Stage 2: sender chooses S1 or S2; receiver chooses R1 or R2. If R1: both get (2,2). If R2: provisional payoffs (6,9) for (S1,R2) or (12,3) for (S2,R2). Stage 3: receiver may reward S1 by R4 (change (6,9) to (8,7)) or accept (R3), and may punish S2 by R6 (change (12,3) to (2,2)) or accept (R5). Feedback each round: full history of that round’s decisions and payoffs for the pair. Roles fixed, matching random, no history of opponents shown.",Summarized from Sections 3.1 and 3.3 and the Appendix instructions.,1
10.1007_s10479-017-2412-8.md,Direct-response method treatment,Second main experimental treatment; uses direct-response method for receiver’s third-stage decisions.,1,Elicitation method = Direct-response method,Only between-condition manipulation is elicitation method (strategy vs direct-response). This row captures the direct-response condition.,1,True,Human student participants played the multi-round punishment game in a lab.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Sessions conducted in a computer laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No purely simulated agents; results come from human subjects.,1,False,"While the paper contains a learning model, this condition itself is an empirical lab experiment.",0.9,2,Game is between player S (sender) and player R (receiver).,1,40,Each session of each treatment consists of 40 rounds of the three-stage game.,1,0,Players choose discrete actions; there is no continuous contribution choice.,1,N/A,Not a contribution or public goods framework; no default allocation between private and public accounts.,1,N/A,No linear public goods structure; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,Instructions explicitly prohibit talking and no messaging channel is described beyond the game’s one-way cheap-talk message.,1,0,Feedback limited to own and current opponent’s decisions and payoffs each round; no summary info about others.,0.9,0,Anonymity and random matching; no identifying information about who punished whom.,1,0,Same anonymity applies to rewarding decisions; only actions and payoffs for the anonymous opponent are shown.,1,0,Subjects were not informed about the number of rounds.,1,1,"Receiver can choose R6 to change (12,3) to (2,2), explicitly interpreted as punishment.",1,1,Punishment reduces receiver payoff from 3 to 2 (loss of 1 unit).,1,10,"Punishment reduces sender payoff from 12 to 2, a drop of 10 units per 1 unit cost.",1,1,"Receiver can choose R4 to change (6,9) to (8,7), explicitly interpreted as reward.",1,2,Reward reduces receiver payoff from 9 to 7 (loss of 2 units).,1,2,Reward increases sender payoff from 6 to 8 (gain of 2 units per 2 units receiver cost; coded as +2 per reward action).,0.8,N/R,No explicit per-round initial endowment defined; only outcome payoffs are specified.,0.4,N/R,"Study reports frequencies of messages, actions, punishments, rewards, and payoffs; no contribution rate defined.",1,N/R,"There is no contribution amount decision, only discrete strategy choices.",1,N/R,They give ex-post payoffs by outcome but do not compute efficiency ratios relative to a cooperative benchmark.,0.6,N/R,"Combined payoffs per message-action cell are reported (Table 5), but not an overall DV summarized as group payoff across all rounds.",0.6,China,"Subjects were students at a Chinese university; payoffs in CNY and affiliation is Tsinghua University, Beijing.",1,N/R,Only student status (undergraduate/graduate) mentioned; no numeric age information.,0.3,N/R,Gender composition is not discussed.,0.1,Undergraduate and graduate students at a university,Subjects recruited from graduate and undergraduate students in various majors.,1,On site,All sessions run in a computer laboratory.,1,"Identical three-stage punishment game and 40-round random matching structure as in the strategy method treatment. Only difference is elicitation method in stage 3: under direct-response, receiver observes the realized (S action, R2) outcome and then immediately decides whether to accept or change the result (reward or punish). No unexecuted contingent third-stage decisions are elicited.",Summarized contrast from Section 3.1 between direct-response and strategy methods; all other protocol aspects are the same.,1
10.1111_1468-0106.12034.md,No-audit treatment,"One of three main treatments; no audit procedure, standard voluntary contribution mechanism.",1,"Audit scheme: no audit (audit probability 0, no monitoring fund)",Treatment varies presence and nature of auditing; this condition has none.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with undergraduate subjects.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab sessions with specified treatments.,1,True,Conducted in computer lab of Department of Public Finance at National Chengchi University.,1,False,"All results from human-subject lab experiment, no separate simulation.",1,False,Paper has theoretical section but experimental results are empirical; this entry is for the experiment.,1,4,Groups of size n=4 in all treatments.,1,20,Each session involves 12 subjects who make decisions for 20 rounds.,1,1,"Subjects choose any report R_i with 0 ≤ R_i ≤ w_i; continuous choice, not binary.",1,0,All income starts as private; only reported part is taxed and moved to public X account.,1,0.5,Marginal per capita return of the public good is m=0.5 and group size is 4; MPCR is defined as this m in the VCM.,1,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous rematching VCM with no chat.,0.9,1,Result report shows each member’s declaration and X-account investment and group totals.,1,0,No punishment/audit in this treatment; also rematching is anonymous.,1,0,No reward mechanism described.,1,1,"Standard repeated-game lab experiments typically inform subjects of total rounds; though not explicit, assume known horizon of 20 as design feature.",0.6,0,"No audit or fines, thus no punishment mechanism.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No individual rewards; only public good returns.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology.,1,70,"True income is randomly 40, 60, 80, or 100; average per subject per round is (40+60+80+100)/4 = 70 points.",0.8,0.372,"Average compliance rate for rounds 1–20 in no-audit treatment, Table 2 row (1).",1,7.503,"Average taxes reported (points) in rounds 1–20, Table 2 row (5); tax is 30% of reported income so this equals average contribution to X via tax.",1,N/R,No explicit report of payoffs relative to full-cooperation maximum across treatments.,0,N/R,Only average individual earnings per round reported; group total not directly given.,0.3,Taiwan,Sessions conducted at National Chengchi University in Taiwan.,1,N/R,Only that subjects are undergraduate students; no age range or mean reported.,0.4,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Undergraduate students,All subjects are undergraduate students at National Chengchi University.,1,On site,Lab sessions in a physical computer laboratory.,1,"Tax compliance framed VCM with heterogeneous income (40, 60, 80, 100), flat tax rate t=0.3 on reported income, public good return m=0.5 per tax point to each of 4 players. No audit or fines.",Summarizes structural payoffs for this treatment from design section.,1
10.1111_1468-0106.12034.md,Endogenous-audit treatment,Main treatment where subjects can transfer tax paid to a group monitoring fund determining audit probability.,1,Audit scheme: endogenous audit (group monitoring contributions determine number audited; penalty rate θ=3),Independent variable across treatments is the audit scheme; this row corresponds to endogenous auditing.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with specified endogenous audit rules.,1,True,Conducted in the computer lab at National Chengchi University.,1,False,No separate agent-based or numerical simulation reported for this treatment.,1,False,Theoretical predictions exist but this row is for the experimental implementation.,1,4,Groups of size n=4 in each round.,1,20,Each session has 20 rounds.,1,1,Continuous declaration choice 0 ≤ R_i ≤ w_i and continuous monitoring contribution 0 ≤ c_i ≤ t R_i.,1,0,"Income initially private; only taxed part goes to public X account, some of which can be shifted to monitoring fund.",1,0.5,Every point in X yields 0.5 to each group member; same as no-audit treatment.,1,0,No mention of communication channels; random anonymous rematching used to reduce reputation effects.,0.9,1,"After stage 1, subjects observe others’ declarations; after stage 2, they see total monitoring contributions and number audited.",1,0,"Audits are random; no indication that identities of auditors or contributors to monitoring are revealed beyond group membership, which is anonymous.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism beyond public good; no identities revealed for rewards.,1,1,Same 20-round repeated structure; standard to inform subjects of horizon though not explicitly stated.,0.6,1,Endogenous audit with fines operates as a punishment for under-reporting.,1,1,Each point contributed to monitoring fund costs the contributor 1 point from X (one-for-one transfer).,0.9,3,"Penalty rate θ=3 meaning fine is three times the evaded tax; plus evaded tax is collected. Per unit of evaded tax uncovered, the cheater loses 1 (evaded tax) + 3 (fine) = 4; but technological fine factor itself is 3.",0.8,0,No individual reward option; only punishment via audits.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,70,"Same random income levels 40, 60, 80, 100; average 70.",0.8,0.879,"Average compliance rate for rounds 1–20 in endogenous-audit treatment, Table 2 row (1).",1,18.501,"Average taxes reported (points) in rounds 1–20, Table 2 row (5); equals average contribution to public account before monitoring transfers.",1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio vs. maximum given for this treatment.,0,N/R,Only individual average earnings (86.012 points) reported; group total not explicitly given.,0.3,Taiwan,Sessions conducted at National Chengchi University in Taiwan.,1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no ages reported.,0.4,N/R,Gender composition not provided.,0,Undergraduate students,All subjects are undergraduate students.,1,On site,Lab experiment in physical computer lab.,1,"Two-stage tax compliance game. Stage 1: income w_i ∈ {40,60,80,100} assigned; subject reports R_i (0 ≤ R_i ≤ w_i); tax t=0.3 applied to R_i and put in X (public good) with MPCR m=0.5 to each of 4 players. Stage 2: subject chooses monitoring contribution c_i with 0 ≤ c_i ≤ tR_i, shifting that amount from X to Y (group monitoring fund). Sum of c_i maps to number s of audited members via thresholds (0,2,8,18,32) for s=0..4. Audited under-reporters pay back evaded tax plus fine with penalty rate θ=3. Collected evaded taxes and fines go into X. Subjects see own audit status, total monitoring points, number audited, and payoff each round. Average realized audit probability ≈0.586 over 20 rounds.",Compiled from design description and Table 1.,1
