phases constructed using Blackwell approachability.",Summarized from Sections 2 and 3 where the model and equilibrium construction are defined.,1
10.1007_978-0-387-72596-3_8.md,Control – standard NPD (full feedback),Paper describes a control condition where five groups played the standard NPD game with full feedback.,1,Institution type: standard NPD; Feedback: full,Condition differs from APD games by absence of authority option; feedback specified as full information.,1,True,Human participants in lab groups making decisions for monetary payoffs.,1,True,Controlled laboratory game with random assignment to conditions and manipulated feedback/institution.,1,True,Conducted in computer-controlled lab at University of Haifa with private booths.,1,False,"All results are from human subjects, not computer agents.",1,False,"Although game-theoretic equilibria are discussed, main results are experimental, not closed-form analysis only.",1,6,Text: “42 groups of 6 players each”; also matrices defined for n=6.,1,280,“each game was played for 280 trials”; experiment ended after 280 trials or 1.5 hours.,1,1,"Players choose to contribute their entire 15 NIS allocation or keep it; no partial contributions mentioned, so contribution choice is all-or-nothing and modeled as discrete action, which maps to 1 (can contribute any amount) vs 0 (all-or-nothing). However schema defines 1 for any amount and 0 for all-or-nothing, so here it is all-or-nothing.",0.7,0,Endowment is initially in private allocation; contribution is an active move from private to public good.,1,0.8,"Each 1 NIS contributed is multiplied by 4 and divided equally among 5 others → each other gets 0.8 NIS; for contributor, marginal per capita return from their own contribution is 0 (they don't benefit from their own), but MPCR for others is 0.8. Using standard public-good definition, MPCR = multiplier/group size = 4/5 = 0.8.",0.9,0,No communication or chat described; only feedback and individual decisions.,1,1,"Full feedback: participants receive number of others choosing each option and payoffs resulting from each choice, which is a summary of others’ earnings/choices.",1,0,No punishment mechanism in control NPD; thus no punished identities.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism beyond standard public good; identities not discussed.,1,1,Players told game will be played for many rounds and see ongoing trials; exact display of remaining rounds not explicitly stated but likely; mark as shown with moderate certainty.,0.6,0,Standard NPD game has only contribute vs keep; no fines or sanctions.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding option; only standard payoffs from public good.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,15,"Instructions: “In each round, you will receive an allocation of 15 NIS.”",1,0.157,"Text: “contribution rate (15.7%, SD=7.82%) obtained under the control condition.”",1,N/R,Only rates reported; no absolute number of contributions per trial given.,0,N/R,Efficiency for NPD control not explicitly reported or derivable from aggregate earnings.,0,N/R,Average earnings by condition not separately given for control.,0,Israel,Participants recruited at University of Haifa (Israel) campus.,1,18–41 (median 24),Participants described as 18 to 41 years old with median age 24.,1,"169 female, 83 male",Participants section reports these counts.,1,University students,"Recruited via billboard ads at University of Haifa campus; typical subject pool is students, though not explicitly labeled.",0.7,On site,Conducted in lab at university with private booths and experimenter room; not online.,1,Iterated 6-player N-person Prisoner’s Dilemma with all-or-nothing contribution of 15 NIS each round; each contribution multiplied by 4 and distributed evenly among other 5 players; no authority option.,Summarizes control game description from instructions and footnote.,1
