10.1016_j.biosystems.2018.10.015.md,Replicator dynamics simulation – β = 1.2,"Fig. 1 describes one simulation run with β = 1.2 and c = 1, contrasted with another run at β = 2.0, with same initial composition.",1,"Punishment level β set to 1.2; contribution cost c = 1; initial strategy counts k1=5 (C), k2=25 (P), k3=10 (D), population size N=40",Independent variable varied across the two simulations in Fig. 1 is β; c and the initial composition are held constant.,1,False,Study uses mathematical analysis and computer simulations of strategy frequencies via replicator equations; no human subjects described.,1,False,"No controlled assignment of human subjects; it is a numerical experiment (simulation), not an empirical experiment with participants.",1,False,No lab or field setting with human participants; purely computational.,1,True,Fig. 1 explicitly shows results from simulations using replicator equations in a finite population.,1,True,Core of paper is closed-form analysis of dominance conditions; simulations apply those analytical payoffs. This condition uses both analytical and simulation methods.,1,40,"Fig. 1: ""The population size is N=40 with initially k1=5, k2=25, k3=10.""",1,1,"Replicator dynamics update strategy counts over iterations, but there is no discrete, repeated public goods stage game with fixed round count; safest to treat as one-stage payoff structure per generation.",0.3,1,Model PGG is framed as binary choice: each round individuals decide whether to contribute an amount c or not; no intermediate amounts described.,0.8,N/A,No human participants or default allocation of endowment; purely theoretical strategic types.,1,N/R,"Only group multiplier r and group size N are mentioned; MPCR = r/N could be computed, but no specific numeric r given.",0.2,N/A,No human interaction or communication; simulation over strategy frequencies.,1,0,"No informational structure for human feedback described; evolutionary updating based on fitness, not on observed summaries.",1,N/A,No identities; population-level model without agents observing who punished.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,No human subjects or interface; notion of displayed remaining rounds is inapplicable.,1,1,Core model includes altruistic punishers who impose a penalty β on free riders at personal cost η=β.,1,1.2,Assumption η=β and in this simulation β=1.2; cost per punisher equals β.,1,1.2,"Each punisher is willing to pay cost η=β to inflict total penalty β on free riders; β=1.2 here, so each punisher’s total imposed penalty is 1.2.",0.9,0,Model only includes costly punishment; no rewarding or positive incentives described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,"Each contributing player contributes an amount c; example and simulations set c=1, so endowment per player normalized to 1.",0.8,N/R,"Simulation tracks frequencies of strategy types (C, P, D) over time but does not report aggregate contribution rate values.",0.3,N/R,No specific numeric contributions per round are reported as results; only qualitative dominance and trajectories shown.,0.3,N/R,No explicit reporting or calculation of payoff efficiency relative to maximum cooperative payoff.,0.2,N/R,Model gives generic payoff formulas but no numeric group payoff outcomes for this simulation.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation and analytical model with no human subjects.,1,Public goods game with three fixed strategies: Cooperators (C) contribute c; Punishers (P) contribute c and pay cost η=β to punish free riders with penalty β; Defectors (D) contribute nothing. Pot is multiplied by r (1<r<N) and equally shared among N players. Population evolves under discrete-time replicator dynamics with fitness equal to strategy returns.,Summarizes the game and evolutionary process as defined in the model description and Section 2.,1
10.1016_j.biosystems.2018.10.015.md,Replicator dynamics simulation – β = 2.0,"Second simulation in Fig. 1 uses β = 2.0 with same initial conditions and c=1, contrasted with β=1.2.",1,"Punishment level β set to 2.0; contribution cost c = 1; initial strategy counts k1=5 (C), k2=25 (P), k3=10 (D), population size N=40",Independent variable between the two simulations is the punishment parameter β; other parameters are held constant.,1,False,No human or animal subjects; purely theoretical and computational.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; numerical experiment over equations.,1,False,No laboratory setting with human subjects.,1,True,Described explicitly as simulations of replicator equations in a finite population (Fig. 1).,1,True,Simulation uses analytically derived payoff functions and dominance conditions.,1,40,"Fig. 1: population size N=40 with k1=5, k2=25, k3=10 initially.",1,1,Replicator dynamics applied iteratively but not structured as finite repeated rounds of a PGG; treated as single-stage payoff per generation.,0.3,1,Players either contribute an amount c or defect by contributing zero; no partial continuous contribution described.,0.8,N/A,No actual endowment allocation for human players; simulation only.,1,N/R,"Symbolic multiplier r and group size N given, but no numeric r; hence MPCR cannot be specified numerically.",0.2,N/A,No human interaction or communication process.,1,0,Agents do not observe payoff summaries; selection based on fitness in replicator dynamics.,1,N/A,No individual identities or observation stage in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,No human interface; concept does not apply.,1,1,Altruistic punishment is central: punishers impose penalties on free riders at cost β.,1,2,Assumption η=β and this simulation sets β=2.0; cost per punisher equals β.,1,2,Each punisher in total inflicts penalty β on free riders while paying cost β; here β=2.0.,0.9,0,Only punishment is modeled; no rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Contribution cost c set to 1; typical interpretation in this model is that each cooperator and punisher contributes c from a normalized unit endowment.,0.8,N/R,"Paper does not provide explicit numerical contribution rates, only strategy dominance behavior and trajectories.",0.3,N/R,No explicit numeric contributions per iteration reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures given for simulation outcomes.,0.2,N/R,"Although payoffs are formulaically defined, no aggregate group payoff is numerically reported for this condition.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation and analytical model only.,1,"Same public goods game model as in the β=1.2 condition: three strategies (C, P, D), with punishers contributing c and paying cost β to punish free riders with total penalty β; pot multiplied by r and shared equally. Evolution governed by discrete-time replicator dynamics using the specified payoffs.",Game structure is identical to the other simulation; only β value differs.,1
10.1073_pnas.2322072121.md,Analytical evolutionary game-theoretic model of local vs global indirect reciprocity,The paper presents a single formal model analyzing strategies across local and global public goods games with a mutual aid game; no separate named experimental conditions beyond parameter/strategy variations.,0.92,"Strategy set for PGG (G: global cooperation, L: local cooperation, D: defect, plus LG, M, O in SI) and MAG (c, g, l, d, m, pm, p), group structure (number of local groups a, local group size n_l, global size n_g=a·n_l), invasion fraction β of mutants, and payoff parameters (c_p, c_m, b_g, b_l, b_m, error rate e). This row summarizes the general model rather than a single parameterization.","The model systematically varies strategies, group structure, invasion fraction, and payoff parameters; these constitute the independent variables determining outcomes.",0.96,False,The study is purely theoretical with no human participants; it analyzes strategy invasion using adaptive dynamics.,1,False,No controlled behavioral or field experiment is conducted; only mathematical analysis and simulations of strategies.,1,False,There is no laboratory or online experiment with human subjects.,1,True,The authors analyze invasibility of strategies and dynamics over parameter ranges consistent with numerical/agent-based or numerical evaluation of analytic expressions; all without human subjects.,0.88,True,Core results are derived from explicit fitness functions (Eqs. 6–8) and invasion conditions (Eqs. 3–5) using adaptive dynamics; this is a formal analytical model.,1,0,The model defines n_g (global group size) and n_l (local group size) symbolically with n_g = a·n_l but does not fix them to one numeric value across the paper; therefore a single numeric player count cannot be specified.,0.2,0,The model assumes repeated interactions with reputation over rounds but does not specify a fixed number of rounds; it analyzes equilibrium behavior instead.,0.2,1,"In the main model players choose between discrete actions: always contribute to global, always to local, or defect. Although SI mentions 25%, 50%, 75% intermediate contributions, contribution is not restricted to an all-or-nothing between 0 and full to a single pool; multiple contribution proportions are allowed in extended strategies.",0.6,N/A,There is no initial allocation choice by human subjects; endowment allocation is defined purely as strategy actions in a theoretical model.,1,N/R,The model uses returns parameters b_g and b_l and costs c_p rather than explicitly defining a marginal per capita return; MPCR is not directly specified.,0.3,N/A,No human communication is modeled; agents follow fixed strategies without explicit communication.,1,0,The model assumes agents know previous actions/reputation needed for strategies but does not include UI-like summaries; this variable is not meaningful in a non-human analytical model.,0.3,N/A,"Punishment is implemented via reputation and aid decisions, not via explicit costly punishment identifying individuals in an interface.",1,N/A,"Reward is operationalized as aid in the MAG, not as an explicit reward mechanism with identity revelation.",1,0,There is no user interface; the model analyzes equilibrium behavior over an indefinite or large number of rounds.,1,0,There is no separate costly punishment stage; incentives operate via conditional aid (indirect reciprocity) rather than a punishment technology.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology is modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology is modeled.,1,1,Aid in the mutual aid game (MAG) functions as a costly prosocial reward: paying cost c_m yields partner benefit b_m.,0.95,N/R,"The model defines the generic cost of aiding as c_m but does not give a fixed numeric value across all analyses, only example parameterizations (e.g., c_m=1 in figure captions).",0.5,N/R,The model denotes benefit from aid as b_m but does not specify a single numeric magnitude per cost unit across all conditions.,0.5,N/R,The model specifies a cost of contributing to the PGG (c_p) rather than a total endowment amount per round.,0.3,N/R,"The outcomes are reported in terms of evolutionary stability and invasibility of strategies, not in normalized contribution rates; no single numeric rate is presented.",0.2,N/R,Theoretical payoffs and invasion conditions are described symbolically; no specific aggregate contribution amounts are reported as numeric DVs.,0.2,N/R,The paper discusses relative stability and potential payoffs but does not compute or report efficiency as total payoff divided by maximum possible payoff.,0.2,N/R,"Fitness/payoff expressions F(i,j) are given symbolically rather than as concrete numeric group payoffs in particular conditions.",0.2,N/A,No human participants are involved; the model is purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,The work consists of analytical and simulation modeling without human or field data.,1,"Global public goods game and local public goods game played simultaneously with cost c_p to contribute; returns b_g to the global pool and b_l to the local pool, divided evenly among respective groups (global group of size n_g = a·n_l and local groups of size n_l). After PGGs, players engage in a mutual aid game (MAG) within local groups: each pays cost c_m to give partner benefit b_m with implementation error e, and reputations (good/bad) are updated under a standing norm or other leading eight norms. Strategies for PGG: G (always global), L (always local), D (defect), LG (split contributions), M and O (conditional on MAG aid in SI). Strategies for MAG: c (always aid), g (aid global cooperators), l (aid local cooperators), d (never aid), m (aid those who aid others), pm (aid those who contributed to PGG and aided in MAG), plus p (aid all PGG cooperators) in SI. Evolutionary dynamics analyzed via adaptive dynamics and invasion analysis for rare mutants and for an invading fraction β in one local group.","Summarizes the game structure as described in Materials and Methods, Tables 2–4, and model description.",0.98
