10.1177_00483931211008432.md,"N/A – purely theoretical/analytical paper, no distinct experimental condition","The paper is a conceptual/philosophical analysis of commons, property relations, and trust; it cites others’ empirical work but does not report its own experiment, simulation, or observational dataset with conditions.",1,N/A,No experimental or observational conditions are manipulated or compared; the work is theoretical.,1,False,"No human-subject data are collected; all references to behavior and commons (e.g., Ostrom’s field cases, lab experiments) are secondary citations.",1,False,The article does not implement a controlled experiment; it only discusses others’ experiments conceptually.,1,False,There is no lab or field experimental protocol conducted by the author.,1,False,"No agent-based, numerical, or computational simulations are presented.",1,True,"The article develops a conceptual/analytical account of property structures, trust, and externalities, with diagrams illustrating thought experiments (e.g., fishers A and B) rather than data.",1,0,"No actual game with human players is implemented; any mention of A, B, or communities is illustrative.",1,0,No repeated-game procedure is run; temporal structure is only discussed conceptually (present vs future generations).,1,0,Not applicable because no contributions or strategy space are implemented in an actual game.,1,N/A,No human-subject contribution decision or default allocation is specified; theoretical setting only.,1,N/R,No public goods game or linear payoff structure is specified from which an MPCR could be derived.,1,N/A,No experimental interface or communication protocol; only theoretical discussion.,1,0,No experimental feedback structure is implemented; hence no summaries of others’ behavior or payoffs are shown.,1,N/A,"Punishment is discussed conceptually (e.g., cutting nets) but not implemented in a controlled game with identity revelation choices.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism is structured as an experimental treatment.,1,0,There is no experimental interface where number of rounds could be displayed.,1,0,"Although punishment and mutual monitoring are discussed, there is no formal game or mechanism implemented by the author.",1,N/A,No numerical punishment technology or cost structure is defined as part of an experiment or simulation.,1,N/A,No parameterized mapping from punishment effort to impact is specified.,1,0,"Rewards are not operationalized as a game mechanism, only norms and sanctions are discussed.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism is defined.,1,N/A,No reward technology is parameterized.,1,0,"The illustrative fish-stock examples (e.g., 6 units of fish) are conceptual; there is no subject endowment in an implemented game.",1,N/R,No contributions are measured; there are no behavioral data.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions or actions from participants are reported.,1,N/R,No empirical payoff data are provided from which efficiency could be computed.,1,N/R,No realized group payoffs are reported; only conceptual examples of gains and externalities.,1,N/A,No human participants are recruited for a study by the author.,1,N/A,No participant pool exists in this theoretical paper.,1,N/A,"No participants, so no gender composition.",1,N/A,No participant sample or educational background is described.,1,No human,"The work is theoretical/analytical with no human subjects, experiments, or field data collected by the author.",1,"The paper develops a conceptual model of commons governance, mutual monitoring, and patrimonial property structures, using stylized examples (e.g., fishers A and B, irrigation systems) and diagrams, but does not specify or run any formal game or experimental protocol.","Summarizes that any games or dilemmas mentioned (Prisoner’s Dilemma, commons dilemmas, public goods) are discussed abstractly or via citations to prior literature rather than implemented.",1
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Baseline upper bound n=8 (ideal cooperation among 8 cooperative of 12 participants),Section 4.1 and Fig. 1 describe an ideal scenario with 12 participants of which 8 are cooperative and 4 selfish; the curve n=8 is used as an upper bound when only 8 cooperative participants share among themselves and perfectly avoid lending to selfish ones.,0.94,Participant composition and cooperation scope: 12 total participants with 8 cooperative and 4 selfish; cooperative participants share resources only among the 8 cooperative participants (no sharing with selfish participants).,"Explicitly described in Section 4.1 and again when introducing Fig. 1: 12 VPN operators, 8 cooperative, 4 selfish; in the n=8 upper bound scenario, they assume cooperatives can perfectly recognize selfish participants and share only with cooperatives.",0.94,False,"Study is a computer simulation of VPN operators, not human subjects.",1,False,No controlled experiment with human or field data; purely simulated game-theoretic model.,1,False,No lab or field setting with human participants; only simulations.,1,True,Section 4 explicitly refers to simulations of strategies in a repeated game model.,1,True,"Paper includes explicit analytical payoff definitions and inequalities, but this specific condition is primarily used as a calculated upper bound; still based on analytical model.",0.8,12,"Section 4.1: 'In total, we have 12 VPN operators (participants)'.",1,100000,Section 4.1: '100000 sequential requests are made.' Requests correspond to iterations/rounds.,0.95,1,"Each participant either offers exactly one resource or zero at a given iteration (binary decision), not a continuous contribution proportion.",0.8,N/A,No human subjects; game is not framed as initial public vs private endowment.,1,N/R,"No public goods multiplier or MPCR specified; model is in terms of discrete resource offers and payoffs a,b,c,d.",1,N/A,No human subjects; no notion of chat or explicit communication interface.,1,0,"Simulation agents have access to historic records and counters for other agents but there is no mention of showing payoff summaries; their internal state is algorithmic, not an interface.",0.7,N/A,No explicit punishment stage with targeted identity revelation as in human experiments; punishment is endogenous via strategies.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage analogous to public goods games.,1,0,Agents play a fixed number of iterations in simulation; no evidence of them being given horizon information as a variable.,0.7,0,No costly punishment action; punishment is indirect refusal to lend resources.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward stage; helping is just the cooperative action itself.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model defines 'one resource' offers but not an overall per-round or initial endowment level.,0.6,N/R,"Outcome is expressed as 'total gain' (payoff) and number of fulfilled requests, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,"They track offers of single resources and fulfilled requests, not aggregated contribution amounts per agent.",0.8,N/R,"They present expected total gains and upper bounds, but not efficiency as ratio of actual to theoretical maximum in 0–1 form.",0.7,626500,"Section 4.1: for n=8 scenario, derived upper bound of expected total gain of cooperative participants is '≈ 626500'.",0.95,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from computational simulations of strategies among virtual VPN operators.,1,"Asynchronous prisoner's dilemma style resource borrowing game among 12 agents; 8 cooperative and 4 selfish. At each iteration, exactly one randomly chosen participant makes a request for one resource (request probability pr=1/12 per participant). Non-requesting cooperative participants each offer one resource with probability pa=1/4. Purely selfish participants never offer. Payoffs: sharing cost a=-1 for lender when sharing unused resources; c=0 if refusing; borrower's gain from fulfilled request b=1/pr; borrower's loss from unfulfilled request d=-1. This scenario is used to compute an upper bound on the cooperative agents' total gain assuming perfect recognition of selfish agents and sharing only among cooperatives.",Summarized the key game and payoff parameters for this baseline scenario from Section 4.1.,0.96
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Upper bound n=12 (all 12 participants cooperative),Section 4.1 and Fig. 1 also define a second 'n=12' upper bound scenario where all 12 participants behave cooperatively.,0.94,"Participant composition: 12 total participants, all 12 cooperative and willing to share resources with each other.",Explicitly described: '12 cooperative participants sharing their available resources with any of them.',0.96,False,Computer simulation only.,1,False,"Analytical/simulation scenario, not an empirical controlled experiment.",1,False,No lab or human setting.,1,True,Derived via simulation/analytical calculations as described near Fig. 1.,1,True,Uses analytical formula with same payoff parameters to compute expected total gain; framed as theoretical upper bound.,0.9,12,Scenario explicitly: '12 cooperative participants'.,1,100000,Uses same overall simulation horizon of 100000 sequential requests as base scenario.,0.9,1,Same one-resource-or-nothing decision per offer as base scenario.,0.9,N/A,Simulation with no human monetary endowments.,1,N/R,No public-goods MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or explicit communication channel.,1,0,No explicit display of earnings or punishments; only algorithmic access to state.,0.7,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Horizon not treated as information variable for simulated agents.,0.7,0,No costly punishment action; refusal to lend is just the non-cooperative action.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment.,1,0,No separate reward institution distinct from basic cooperation.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/R,No initial or per-round endowment specified.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes tracked as gains from fulfilled requests, not contribution fractions.",1,N/R,Not reported as aggregate contributions.,0.8,N/R,Efficiency relative to maximum not given as normalized metric.,0.7,699550,"Section 4.1: for n=12 scenario, upper bound of expected total gain of the original eight cooperative participants is '≈ 699550'.",0.95,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely simulated agents.,1,"Same asynchronous borrowing game as baseline: at each iteration, one randomly chosen participant requests one resource (pr=1/12). All 12 participants are cooperative, and when not requesting, each offers one resource with probability pa=1/4. Payoffs identical to baseline (a=-1, c=0, b=1/pr, d=-1). Under this full-cooperation assumption, the expected total gain over 100000 iterations for the focal 8 participants is approximately 699550.",Summarizes the condition as described under the 'second scenario' near Fig. 1.,0.95
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Model I: Traditional Tit-for-Tat with fixed history (maximum / average variants),"Section 4.2 presents 'Model I: the traditional Tit-for-Tat with fixed history', with two update rules for offers (max and average of last k offers). Fig. 2 reports performance for these Tit-for-Tat with fixed history variants.",0.95,"Strategy: Traditional Tit-for-Tat with fixed history of length k=coeff/pa; offer rule either (a) offers min{Aj(t), max_h H_{j,i}(h)} or (b) min{Aj(t), (1/k) * sum_h H_{j,i}(h)}. Participant composition: 12 participants (8 cooperative, 4 selfish) as in base scenario.",Section 4.2 defines the strategy and alternative offer functions; base scenario parameters still apply.,0.94,False,Simulation of strategies among artificial agents.,1,False,No empirical experiment; numerical experiments are simulations.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,Model I is evaluated via simulation (Fig. 2).,1,False,"Here focus is on simulated trajectories (total gain over time), not closed-form analysis.",0.7,12,Same 12 participants scenario as defined in Section 4.1.,0.95,100000,They keep the same '100000 sequential requests' setup in Section 4; used across model comparisons.,0.9,1,"Each offer is either some integer number of resources (often 0 or 1), with no partial continuous contributions.",0.8,N/A,No human monetary endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,Simulated agents; no chat.,1,0,"Agents only access history needed for strategy, no mention of complete payoff summaries.",0.7,N/A,No explicit costly punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Finite horizon not given to agents as a parameter.,0.7,0,No separate punishment phase; sanction is refusal to share.,0.9,N/A,No explicit costly punishment.,1,N/A,No explicit costly punishment.,1,0,"Cooperation is inherent action, not a separate reward mechanism.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,N/A,No explicit reward.,1,N/R,Per-round or initial endowments not specified.,0.6,N/R,They track total gains but not contribution fractions.,1,N/R,Not reported explicitly; focus is on payoffs and deadlock emergence.,0.7,N/R,No explicit computation of efficiency relative to an optimal benchmark.,0.7,N/R,Fig. 2 is qualitative; no concrete numeric value for total gain is printed.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are simulations.,1,"Same base asynchronous borrowing game with 12 participants (8 cooperative, 4 selfish). Request process: at each iteration, one participant i is randomly selected to request one resource Ri(t); request probability pr=1/12. Non-requesting cooperative participants j have unused resources Aj(t) with probability pa=1/4 and decide offers based strictly on fixed-length history Hj,i(1..k) of i's past offers to j, with k=coeff/pa. Two update rules are explored: offering up to the maximum or up to the average of the last k offers (capped by Aj(t)). Selfish participants never offer. Simulation shows that this Tit-for-Tat with fixed history leads to mutual deadlocks where agents stop lending.",Summarizes the model description in Section 4.2.,0.96
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Model II: Generous Tit-for-Tat (various coeff and threshold settings),"Section 4.3 describes the 'generous Tit-for-Tat' model, with Figs. 3 and 4 showing performance for different generosity thresholds (e.g., Coop with 1 offer, Coop with 2 offers) and coefficients.",0.95,"Strategy: Generous Tit-for-Tat. Cooperative participant j with unused resources Aj(t) offers according to: (i) if some non-empty H_{j,i}(h) in last k observations, offer min{Aj(t), max_h H_{j,i}(h)}; (ii) every k requests, offer Aj(t) minus a bonus even if history is empty; otherwise offer 0. Parameter k=coeff/pa with various coeff (e.g., 4, 16) and thresholds for recognizing cooperators (requiring 1 vs 2 offers in history). Participant composition: 12 participants (8 cooperative, 4 selfish).","Detailed in Section 4.3 and illustrated in Figs. 3 and 4, including the use of thresholds (minimum number of offers in history) and coefficient values.",0.92,False,All results are from simulated agents.,1,False,"These are computational experiments, not empirical experiments with human subjects.",1,False,No physical or online lab with humans.,1,True,The performance of generous Tit-for-Tat is evaluated via simulations shown in Figs. 3 and 4.,1,False,They reason qualitatively and via simulation plots rather than closed-form results for this model.,0.7,12,They continue using the same 12-participant base scenario.,0.95,100000,Simulations use 100000 sequential requests as baseline; applied across strategy comparisons.,0.9,1,"Agents decide whether or not to offer one resource unit, not a fractional contribution.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment setup.,1,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,N/A,No communication stage with humans.,1,0,"Agents only use necessary history (Hj,i) and counters; no explicit payoff summaries shown.",0.7,N/A,No explicit identity-based punishment as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,0,"Horizon is a simulation parameter, not given to agents.",0.7,0,No separate costly punishment institution; sanction is denial of requests.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment institution.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment institution.,1,0,"Helping is intrinsic cooperative action, not a structured reward mechanism.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/R,No specific per-agent endowment given.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes tracked as total gain and fraction of fulfilled requests, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,They discuss number of offers per history window but not overall contribution amounts as DV.,0.7,N/R,They compare cooperative total gains to theoretical upper bound but do not report a 0–1 efficiency metric.,0.7,N/R,Figs. 3 and 4 give qualitative curves; text notes 'close to the optimal upper bound' but no explicit numeric total stated.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational simulation.,1,"Base asynchronous prisoner's dilemma borrowing game with 12 participants (8 cooperative, 4 selfish) and 100000 requests. Per-iteration: one randomly selected participant i requests one resource; each non-requesting cooperative participant j has unused resource with probability pa=1/4. In Generous Tit-for-Tat, j uses a finite history Hj,i of length k=coeff/pa of offers from i to j. If Hj,i contains a positive offer, j offers up to the maximum previous offer capped by Aj(t). If Hj,i contains no positive offers, then j still lends some resources every k requests (a 'bonus') regardless of i's past behavior, to avoid permanent deadlocks. Selfish participants can exploit generosity; variants add thresholds requiring at least 1, 2, or more offers from i in Hj,i within the k-window for j to regard i as cooperative; increasing coeff increases history length and reduces risk of misclassification.",Summarizes Section 4.3 and Figs. 3–4.,0.96
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Model III: Altruistic Tit-for-Tat,Section 4.4 explicitly introduces 'Model III: the altruistic Tit-for-Tat' and Fig. 5 presents its performance.,0.96,"Strategy: Altruistic Tit-for-Tat. Participant j offers to i if i has not refused the last k requests from j; i is deemed cooperative if i has made at least coeff/2 offers in j's last k requests. When j has unused resources, sharing cost aA=-1; when j has no unused resources but avoids refusing k-th consecutive request to a cooperative i, j altruistically lends engaged resources at cost aNA=-1/pr. Parameters: pa=1/4, pr=1/12, coeff values such as 4 and 16 explored, with varying thresholds for cooperative recognition (e.g., expecting at least 8 offers in last 64 requests when coeff=16). Participant composition: 12 participants (8 cooperative, 4 selfish).",Section 4.4 defines these payoffs and recognition thresholds; Fig. 5 compares cooperative participants for different coeff and threshold settings.,0.92,False,Strategy is evaluated in silico.,1,False,"Numerical experiments are simulations, not empirical experiments.",1,False,No lab or field study.,1,True,Section 4.4 relies on simulated performance (Fig. 5).,1,False,"They provide some inequality to argue average gain positive, but performance is essentially assessed via simulation.",0.7,12,Same participant set used across models.,0.95,100000,They keep the 100000 sequential requests scenario to compare strategies.,0.9,1,"Agents decide to offer discrete resources (0 or 1) and sometimes engaged resources, but still a binary decision to give or not.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human communication channel.,1,0,Agents only maintain necessary histories and counters.,0.7,N/A,No explicit punitive mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Not part of agent information set.,0.7,0,"Punishment occurs indirectly via denying future resource requests, not as a distinct costly action.",0.9,N/A,No explicit per-unit punishment cost.,1,N/A,No explicit per-unit punishment technology.,1,0,Cooperation itself is not framed as a separate reward mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No explicit structured rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit structured rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment level not specified.,0.6,N/R,"Metric of interest is total gain and number of victims (agents who fail often), not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,Not explicitly reported.,0.7,N/R,"They note altruistic Tit-for-Tat is 'slightly better' than generous in total gain, but no normalized efficiency provided.",0.7,N/R,Fig. 5 is graphical; no numeric total gain value is provided.,0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,All results are simulated.,1,"Same 12-participant, 100000-request asynchronous borrowing game. Altruistic Tit-for-Tat modifies generous strategy: a participant j will lend from available unused resources with cost aA=-1 as long as not refusing k consecutive requests from a participant i recognized as cooperative (>=coeff/2 offers in last k of i's responses to j). If j has no unused resources but faces a cooperative i whom j would otherwise refuse for the k-th time, j can altruistically lend engaged resources incurring cost aNA=-1/pr to avoid future punishment. Selfish participants who never offer are never recognized as cooperative and receive almost no resources. Cooperative participants still achieve positive average gains.",Summarizes Section 4.4 and Fig. 5.,0.96
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Model IV: Reputation-based Tit-for-Tat (fixed and adaptive delta),"Section 4.5 describes 'Model IV: reputation-based Tit-for-Tat', and Figs. 6, 7, and 8 illustrate fixed delta and adaptive delta versions.",0.96,"Strategy: Reputation-based Tit-for-Tat. For each ordered pair (i,j), i tracks NR_i(t) (its total number of requests made up to t) and NO_{i,j}(t) (offers received from j). Reputation metric is r_{i,j}=NO_{i,j}(t)/NR_i(t). Participant j offers Aj(t) to i if r_{i,j} > (1−δ)·pa. Selfish agents with low offer rates eventually have r below threshold and are denied. Parameters: pa=1/4, pr=1/12. Scenarios include fixed δ (e.g., 0.5 or 0.1) with various initialization intervals (during which all are treated as cooperative) and an adaptive δ schedule δ_{i+1}=δ_i−0.1(δ_i−τ) every 500 iterations converging to τ=0.1. Participant composition: 12 participants (8 cooperative, 4 selfish).","Taken from the formal definition (Eq. (1)), reputation rule, and description of fixed vs adaptive delta in Section 4.5.",0.95,False,No human data.,1,False,Computational simulations only.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Model IV evaluated via simulations (Figs. 6–8).,1,True,"They also derive Chernoff bounds for reputation concentration and misclassification probability, in addition to simulations.",0.85,12,Same 12 agents framework used.,0.95,100000,Simulations appear to continue using 100000 iterations; explicit in base scenario and consistent across strategy comparisons.,0.9,1,Agents decide whether or not to lend one resource (no graded continuous contributions).,0.8,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Agents maintain only counters needed for reputation; no payoff summaries.,0.7,N/A,No explicit punishment stage.,1,N/A,No explicit reward stage.,1,0,Finite horizon not given to agents as parameter.,0.7,0,Punishment is via not lending if reputation low; no separate costly sanction action.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment cost parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward system.,1,N/A,No explicit reward system.,1,N/R,Endowment not specified.,0.6,N/R,Focus is on total gain and misclassification; contribution rates not provided.,1,N/R,No aggregate contributions reported.,0.7,N/R,They show that total gain approaches upper bound but do not report an explicit efficiency index.,0.7,N/R,Figs. 6–8 show trajectories but no numeric total gain value is printed.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational model.,1,"12-agent, 100000-iteration asynchronous borrowing game. Each agent i keeps NR_i(t) (its total requests) and NO_{i,j}(t) for every other agent j. Reputation r_{i,j}=NO_{i,j}/NR_i. After an initialization phase where everyone is presumed cooperative, agent j will lend any available Aj(t) to i only if r_{i,j} > (1−δ)pa. Chernoff bounds show that for cooperative j with offer probability pa, r_{i,j} will concentrate around pa and exceed threshold with high probability as NR_i grows, while selfish j with much lower offer probability will fall below threshold. Fixed δ values (0.5 or 0.1) with varying initialization length, and an adaptive δ schedule converging to τ=0.1, are evaluated. Results: cooperative participants’ total gain is close to the ideal upper bound; selfish participants must raise their offer probability close to pa to avoid being classified as selfish.",Condenses the full description in Section 4.5 and associated figures.,0.97
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Model V: Altruistic-reputation-based Tit-for-Tat,"Section 4.6 presents 'Model V: the altruistic-reputation-based Tit-for-Tat', combining the altruistic and reputation-based strategies.",0.95,"Strategy: Altruistic-reputation-based Tit-for-Tat. During an initial interval of length chosen for δ=0.5 (e.g., 1000 iterations), participants use the altruistic Tit-for-Tat rule with coeff=16, requiring that a cooperative participant has made at least 8 offers in last coeff/pa=64 requests of an agent to be deemed cooperative. After this initialization, the reputation-based rule with adaptive δ schedule (δ_i+1=δ_i−0.1(δ_i−τ), τ=0.1) gradually replaces pure altruistic detection. Participant composition: 12 participants (8 cooperative, 4 selfish).","Directly from Section 4.6, which describes using altruistic strategy for the initial interval then switching to adaptive reputation strategy.",0.92,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab study.,1,True,Combined strategy performance assessed in simulation; text notes result is as in Fig. 8(b).,1,False,Composition of earlier strategies; performance assessed via simulations.,0.7,12,Same 12-agent setting.,0.95,100000,Used within same overall 100000-request simulation scenario.,0.9,1,Agents offer discrete units according to same underlying game.,0.8,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Agents rely on localized histories and reputations; no payoff summaries interface.,0.7,N/A,No explicit punishment institution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,0,Horizon not provided as part of strategy input.,0.7,0,"Punishment is via refusing future requests, not as a separate action.",0.9,N/A,No explicit cost of punishment beyond opportunity cost of not lending.,1,N/A,No distinct punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward.,0.9,N/A,No separate reward.,1,N/A,No separate reward.,1,N/R,Endowments not specified.,0.6,N/R,DV is primarily cooperative participants’ total gain; contribution rates not reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate contribution numbers reported.,0.7,N/R,"They state the final strategy 'has all the advantages' and produces 'very good overall gain', but no 0–1 efficiency metric.",0.7,N/R,"They state 'result is as in Fig. 8(b)', which shows trajectories but no numeric total gain.",0.4,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Simulation of agents.,1,"Same 12-agent, 100000-iteration asynchronous borrowing game. For the first ~1000 iterations (chosen based on δ=0.5 analysis), agents apply altruistic Tit-for-Tat with coeff=16, requiring that a cooperative counterpart has at least 8 offers in the last 64 requests. This makes it harder for purely selfish agents to be misclassified as cooperative in the early phase, reducing short-term exploitation. After this initialization, they switch to the adaptive reputation-based strategy with δ decreasing over time toward τ=0.1 (δ_{i+1}=δ_i−0.1(δ_i−τ) every 500 iterations). The combined strategy delivers cooperative participants’ total gains similar to the pure reputation-based model while more aggressively denying early benefits to purely selfish agents.",Summarizes Section 4.6.,0.96
10.1016_j.comnet.2011.11.013.md,Simulation – Simultaneous request scenario: one selfish 'greedy' participant,Section 4.7 describes a 'Simultaneous request scenario' focusing on effects of a single selfish participant who may be greedy (and possibly generous) relative to cooperative participants. Fig. 9 presents 'Greedy and generous' and 'Greedy' cases.,0.95,"Scenario variation: simultaneous requests with one selfish participant. Cooperative participants: one randomly selected cooperative participant requests one extra resource per iteration with probability pr=1/8; other cooperative participants each offer their unique resource with probability pa=1/4. Selfish participant: in each iteration, may offer with probability pa* and request with probability pr*; if pr*>pa*, he is 'clearly selfish'. Two key parameterizations: (1) Greedy & Generous: pr*=2/3, pa*=1/3 (>pa); (2) Greedy: pr*=2/3, pa*=1/4 (=pa). Condition under test: whether the strategy (e.g., reputation-based and selection by best reputation when offers scarce) can distinguish and penalize the selfish participant when multiple requests happen simultaneously.",Section 4.7 explicitly sets these probabilities and distinguishes 'Greedy and generous' vs 'Greedy' cases.,0.95,False,Scenario is simulated with artificial agents.,1,False,Not an empirical human experiment.,1,False,No lab context.,1,True,Results presented through simulation curves in Fig. 9.,1,False,This section reasons qualitatively and via simulation rather than analytically proving results.,0.7,1,"At the focal condition level, this row corresponds to the single selfish participant strategy setting (greedy or greedy+generous) contrasted against the cooperative population. The total population is one selfish plus multiple cooperative participants, but the independent variable is defined at the selfish condition (pr*, pa*). For structured extraction, playerCount is set to 1 for the focal condition.",0.4,100000,"Though not restated, this scenario is described as an extension of prior simulations, which use 100000 iterations.",0.7,1,"Selfish participant either offers one resource or not, and either requests or not.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,"Selection among simultaneous requests uses reputations (NO/NR ratios), not displayed summaries.",0.7,N/A,No explicit punishment institution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,0,Finite horizon not relevant to agents' decisions.,0.7,0,Punishment is implemented implicitly by discarding requests from low-reputation selfish participant when scarce resources.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment cost parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment unspecified.,0.6,N/R,"Outcome of interest is impact of selfish participant on total gain of cooperative participants, not normalized contribution rate.",1,N/R,"Number of offers by selfish and cooperative participants discussed probabilistically, not as recorded DV.",0.7,N/R,"They compare total gains under different classification strategies, but no efficiency ratio reported.",0.7,N/R,Fig. 9 shows relative total gains (curves) but not numeric totals.,0.4,N/A,No human participation.,1,N/A,No human participation.,1,N/A,No human participation.,1,N/A,No human participation.,1,No human,Simulated agents only.,1,"Extension of reputation-based selection to cases with simultaneous requests. Cooperative side: one cooperative participant is randomly chosen and requests one extra resource per iteration with probability pr=1/8; other cooperative participants each offer with probability pa=1/4. Selfish participant: on each iteration, chooses among offering with probability pa*, requesting with probability pr*, or idle with probability 1−pa*−pr*. Parameter choices: 'Greedy' case pr*=2/3, pa*=1/4; 'Greedy & Generous' case pr*=2/3, pa*=1/3. When total available offers are insufficient to satisfy both the cooperative and selfish requests, selection is based on reputation: cooperative participants can choose to prioritize higher-reputation cooperators vs the selfish agent. If the selfish participant is indistinguishable in reputation, cooperative participants’ total gain falls. If the strategy successfully distinguishes him (e.g., taking into account different request frequencies or weighted reputation), his requests can be systematically deprioritized when conflicting with cooperative requests, resulting in negative gain for the selfish agent and an incentive to reduce pr* and behave like a cooperative participant.","Based on Section 4.7's description of the greedy greedy+generous cases and their impact, including the recommended discrimination rule.",0.96
10.1038_s41598-023-33167-2.md,"Experiment 1 – Uniform groups, punishment block",Experiment 1 has groups of four that are either uniform or pluriform; in one of two 20-round blocks punishment is available. This row captures the condition: uniform group structure with punishment present.,0.96,Group structure: uniform (all same academic background); Punishment: present (peer punishment allowed in this 20-round block),Independent variables manipulated are group structure (uniform vs pluriform) and presence of punishment (block with punishment vs block without). This row is the uniform + punishment combination.,0.95,True,Human subjects (psychology and pedagogy students) played incentivized public goods games in the lab.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions and controlled manipulation of group structure and punishment opportunities.,1,True,Conducted in laboratory cubicles at Leiden University with computer terminals.,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations described for this experiment.,1,False,No formal closed-form or purely analytical model reported; results are empirical.,1,4,Experiment 1 used groups of four participants.,1,20,Each punishment/nost-punishment block contained 20 rounds; this row refers to one punishment block.,1,0,Contribution decision is binary: contribute full 20 MU or keep all (all-or-nothing).,1,0,Endowment of 20 MU is initially private and can be contributed; nothing starts in public account.,1,0.4,Group multiplier is 1.6 and group size is 4; MPCR = 1.6 / 4 = 0.4.,1,0,No mention of communication or chat; decisions made individually in cubicles.,0.95,1,"After contribution decisions, participants were informed about each other's choices and later about total DP each member received, which are summaries of others’ actions.",0.9,0,"Participants only learned total DP per member, not who imposed them, to prevent retaliation and reputation formation.",1,0,"No reward mechanism was available, so no reward identities are shown.",1,1,Standard multi-round PGGs typically state number of rounds; they explicitly say two blocks of 20 rounds were used. Exact on-screen display not specified but likely shown in round counters.,0.6,1,"In one block, participants had the opportunity to assign decrement points to punish others.",1,1,Each decrement point cost the punisher 1 MU.,1,3,Each decrement point reduced target’s payoff by 3 MU; cost–impact ratio 1:3.,1,0,Only decrement points (punishment) are described; no positive reward.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this experiment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in this experiment.,1,20,"In each round, endowed participants received 20 MU.",1,0.8009,Reported mean relative contribution for uniform groups with punishment present is 80.09%. Converted to proportion 0.8009.,1,N/R,Only relative contribution percentages reported; not the average number of contributors or MU per round.,0.2,N/R,They report relative contributions and wealth but not explicit efficiency measures or enough detail to compute payoff-to-maximum ratio cleanly across noisy endowments.,0.2,N/R,"They state pluriform vs uniform groups' wealth differed, but no numeric payoffs per group or per round are given.",0.2,Netherlands,Participants were students at Leiden University in the Netherlands.,0.95,N/R,Age of first-year students not provided.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,First-year university students in Psychology or Pedagogical Science,Participants were recruited among first-year Psychology and Pedagogical Science students at Leiden University.,1,On site,Participants were seated in individual cubicles in the lab with personal computers.,1,"Two 20-round blocks of binary-contribution public goods game with noisy endowments (15% of rounds: zero endowment, private information). One block includes peer punishment (up to 5 decrement points per other from an extra 15 MU each round, 1:3 cost ratio). Group structure is uniform: all four members share same academic background.",Summarizes key structural details beyond requested config fields directly from methods description.,1
