10.1038_s41598-022-23454-9.md,Analytical–computational network model of monitoring and punishment,"Paper presents a single formal model analyzed with analytical derivations and large-scale random sampling of networks of size N=6, 12, and 120; no distinct human-subject conditions are described.",0.9,Monitoring efficiency m; punishment efficiency p; public goods efficiency b; group size N; contribution threshold c_t; punishment reserve c_p; network structure (directed monitoring/punishment links) subject to constraints on min in-degree and max out-degree.,"Model systematically varies monitoring efficiency, punishment efficiency, and examines different network structures and group sizes under fixed game and institutional parameters.",0.96,False,No human participants; study is a theoretical game-theoretic/network model with analytical results and computational sampling of network topologies.,1,False,Not a controlled empirical experiment; it is a theoretical and computational analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting with participants; only modeling and simulations.,1,True,"Author generates one million random networks for each parameter set (e.g., N=6, N=12, N=120) to evaluate hierarchy scores, which is a computer simulation with no human subjects.",0.95,True,"Core results derived analytically (e.g., conditions in Eqs. (1)-(3), hierarchy bounds in Appendix S9).",1,6,"One focal set of analyses uses N=6 (e.g., “For illustration, Fig. 4A shows socially-optimal networks for organizations of N=6 individuals”). Although other N are also analyzed (12, 120), this row represents the N=6 case as an example condition.",0.8,1,Model is a one-shot linear public goods game with monitoring and punishment; no repeated-round structure is described.,0.8,1,"Individuals choose how to allocate up to a threshold c_t across monitoring, punishment reserves, and public good contributions; contributions are continuous up to c_t, not restricted to all-or-nothing.",0.8,N/A,No human participants or pre-set endowment allocation; only strategic allocation in a model.,1,N/R,Paper specifies public goods efficiency b and group size N but does not explicitly define MPCR = (multiplier/group size) as such; value is parameter-dependent and not a single reported constant.,0.2,N/A,Model is non-empirical and does not include communication among human players.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback stage with human subjects; summaries not applicable.,1,N/A,No modeled anonymity or identity revelation; the model enforces punishment deterministically via network links without informational UI aspects.,0.9,N/A,"No rewarding institution is modeled, only punishment.",1,N/A,"No human interface, and the game is effectively one-shot; nothing to show.",1,1,Punishment is a core component with efficiency parameter p and punishment reserve c_p; punishing free-riders is central to the model.,1,N/R,Cost is represented generically by the punishment efficiency p (resources allocated are multiplied by p and subtracted from target’s payoff); no single numeric cost-per-unit is fixed across all conditions.,0.3,N/R,Punishment effect per unit cost is captured by parameter p but specific numeric values are not reported in the main text for any particular condition.,0.3,0,The model only includes punishment as an enforcement mechanism; no positive reward or bonus for cooperators is described.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/R,"Individuals are described as contributing up to a contribution threshold c_t and holding punishment reserve c_p, but a separate initial endowment in tokens/points is not specified numerically.",0.3,N/R,"Model explores conditions for universal threshold contribution vs universal free-riding, but no numeric contribution rates (0–1) for specific parameter sets are tabulated.",0.4,N/R,"Contributors contribute exactly c_t in equilibrium, but concrete numeric values of c_t are not provided in this text.",0.4,N/R,"Social efficiency Π_N is discussed conceptually, and Eq. (1) compares universal contribution vs free-riding, but no single normalized efficiency value (0–1) is reported for specific conditions.",0.4,N/R,Group payoff Π_N is defined and compared analytically but not reported as explicit numeric outcomes for particular parameter sets in the main text.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely based on formal modeling and computer-generated network samples; no empirical environment.,1,"Linear public goods game with N individuals; each contribution is multiplied by public goods efficiency b ≥ 1 and distributed equally to all other N−1 individuals. Individuals are embedded in a directed monitoring/punishment network with costs determined by monitoring efficiency m and punishment efficiency p. Contributors must allocate at least a contribution threshold c_t across monitoring costs, punishment reserve c_p, and direct public good contributions; free-riders contribute nothing and may be punished.",Summary of the formal game and institutional structure as specified in the model section and Table 1.,0.98
10.1111_ajae.12372.md,Numerical simulation – baseline calibrated to Western La Mancha Aquifer,"Paper describes an empirically calibrated numerical study of a groundwater-extracting community based on the Western La Mancha Aquifer; there is a single calibrated simulation setting, not multiple experimental conditions.",0.9,"Model parameters of social pressure, sanctioning costs, network cohesiveness, resource dynamics, and enforcement (e.g., τ, cf, α0, α1, α2, v(k), Υ, R, ψ, n); no discrete treatment vs control conditions with human subjects.","The study varies model parameters in analytical and numerical analysis (Table 1 sensitivity), but there are no human treatments; independent variables are theoretical parameters.",0.9,False,"No human subjects take decisions; the ‘empirical part’ refers to calibration using published field data, not to running an experiment with participants.",1,False,There is no controlled experiment with random assignment or observed behavior; only an analytical model plus numerical simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting or lab subjects are mentioned.,1,True,"They solve a bioeconomic model and run numerical simulations calibrated to aquifer data (e.g., phase diagrams in Figures 2–3, sensitivity analysis Table 1).",1,True,"Large parts of the paper develop and analyze an analytical model with closed-form conditions (equilibria, propositions, Jacobian, etc.).",1,0,No actual players; n denotes the number of agents in the model only.,1,0,"Dynamic time t in differential equations, not discrete rounds with players.",1,0,"Contribution concept does not apply; model is about continuous extraction choices, but no game with contribution options is implemented with players.",1,N/A,No human endowment or contribution mechanism; purely modeled extraction decisions.,1,N/A,No linear public-goods payoff structure or MPCR is defined; model is a CPR extraction game with continuous payoffs.,1,N/A,No human interaction or communication stage.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human subjects.,1,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as social pressure, not implemented as an experimental interface with identities.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism with identities in an experiment.,1,N/A,No rounds to display; continuous-time model.,1,1,Social pressure ωi represents costly sanctions imposed by compliers on defectors; also legal fines θi are modeled.,1,N/R,"Model specifies generic sanctioning cost function γi(k_i,c_i) with parameters v(k_i), α0, α1, α2 but not as a simple ‘cost per punishment unit’ compatible with the requested field.",0.4,N/R,Effectiveness of punishment (how much payoff is reduced per cost unit) is embedded in utility and ωi; no single numeric ‘tech factor’ is given.,0.4,0,"Only negative sanctions (social pressure, fines) and subsidies of sanctioning costs are discussed; no positive rewards to others for cooperation.",0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No per-period monetary endowment to human players; only implicit farm profits and water extraction in the model.,1,N/A,No contribution game with human subjects; cooperation is modeled as choosing the compliant extraction w^C rather than w^D.,1,N/A,No experimental contribution amounts; only modeled water demands and shares of compliers.,1,N/R,They discuss socially optimal vs second-best outcomes and basins of attraction but do not report a scalar efficiency ratio (payoff / maximum) for a specific condition.,0.6,N/R,"Group payoffs are implicit in π^C(s), π^D(s) and not reported as aggregate numeric outcomes per scenario.",0.6,N/A,"No human participants; aquifer is in Spain but that refers to calibration data, not experimental subjects.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study consists of an analytical model and numerical simulations calibrated with field data; no lab, online, or field experiment with people.",1,"Bioeconomic differential-equation model of a large community of n agents extracting from a common aquifer. Agents choose between compliant extraction w^C(s) and defecting extraction w^D(s); compliers can exert costly social pressure on defectors based on share of compliers c, local cohesiveness τ, and stock level s. Legal enforcement via fines/subsidies θ is also modeled. System dynamics given by coupled ODEs for c and s, solved analytically and via numerical simulation for different parameter baselines and sensitivities.","Summarizes the structure and key elements of the modeled game as described in Sections 3–4, including social networks, social pressure, and resource dynamics.",1
