10.1002_ird.2704.md,"Baseline – Rounds 12–21 (no communication, no penalties)",Paper distinguishes baseline sessions from treatment sessions; dynamic analysis and equilibria B21-B23 refer to rounds 12–21 in baseline games.,0.9,"Institutional setting: Baseline (no communication, no penalties) in rounds 12–21",Only institutional condition varied across sessions is presence/absence/type of communication or penalties; this row captures the baseline condition in the later stage used for comparison.,0.9,True,Field experiment with 235 farmers making allocation decisions.,1,True,Controlled field experiment with randomized treatment allocation.,1,False,"Conducted in rural villages with farmers, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Uses human participants, no purely computational simulations.",1,False,Empirical field experiment; analytical model presented separately but not this row.,1,5,"47 sessions with 235 farmers implies 235/47 = 5 players per session, matching standard Cárdenas et al. irrigation-game protocol referenced as basis.",0.9,21,“47 sessions with 21 rounds of allocation decisions in each session.”,1,1,Endowment “to be allocated either for private consumption or to a public irrigation fund” without restriction to all-or-nothing; standard protocol allows any integer amount.,0.8,0,Endowment is given to each player and they choose how much to allocate to public fund; default is fully private until contributed.,0.8,N/R,"Paper states that “depending on the size of the irrigation fund, water availability and thus returns … increased” but gives no numerical mapping or multiplier.",0,0,Baseline explicitly contrasted with “communication treatment” where farmers are allowed to communicate.,1,1,Standard Cárdenas et al. protocol and need for players to observe group investment for dynamic responses imply group investment is displayed; exact wording not given but necessary for autoregressive behavior.,0.7,N/A,No punishment mechanism in baseline.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Standard field public-good designs and Cárdenas protocol inform participants of the fixed number of rounds; not explicitly stated but needed for learning discussion.,0.6,0,Baseline is without penalty treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment in baseline.,1,0,No mention of any positive reward technology; only penalties are discussed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Paper does not specify the numeric coupon endowment per round.,0,N/R,"Only reports approximate equilibrium collective investment levels in coupons, not normalized rates.",0,25,Table 3 text: “equilibrium level of investment decreased from 29 to 25” and refers to B22 as the stable equilibrium in rounds 12–21 of baseline; use 25 coupons as representative group investment.,0.7,N/R,Efficiency discussed conceptually but no numeric maximum payoff or normalized efficiency value provided.,0,N/R,Total payoffs in coupons or money are not reported.,0,Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan,“We recruited the players from 12 villages of South Kazakhstan and Samarkand… 120 participants were from Samarkand’s 6 villages.”,1,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0,Predominantly male; more than 98% in Samarkand and 86% in South Kazakhstan were male,Explicit percentages reported for male participants by region.,1,N/R,No information on education levels.,0,Field experiment,Described as a field experiment with water users in rural Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan.,1,Field public-good irrigation game: each of 5 farmers per session receives a coupon endowment each round and allocates between private consumption and a public irrigation fund; returns from farming increase with group investment. 21 rounds per session; no communication or penalties in baseline; analysis focuses on dynamic equilibria of collective investment in rounds 12–21.,Summarizes described structure and features of baseline condition from experimental setting section and results.,0.9
