ilibrium under communication in rounds 12–21.,0.8,N/R,No numeric mapping of investment to payoffs or efficiency reported.,0,N/R,No total payoff values provided.,0,Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan,"Same participant pool as baseline, across all treatments.",1,N/R,Age not reported.,0,Predominantly male; more than 98% in Samarkand and 86% in South Kazakhstan were male,Same composition across all treatment types drawn from the same villages.,1,N/R,No education statistics reported.,0,Field experiment,Same rural field setting for all treatments.,1,"Same irrigation public-good game as baseline (5 farmers, 21 rounds, endowment allocated between private account and public irrigation fund). From round 12, groups can communicate before decisions, enabling peer monitoring and coordination; no monetary sanctions are available.",Combines description of general game with the specific institutional change (communication) for this treatment.,0.9
10.1002_ird.2704.md,Low-penalty treatment – Rounds 12–21,"Paper distinguishes “low-penalty” irrigation games with corresponding recursion diagram (Figure 5) and equilibria LP21, LP23 for rounds 12–21.",1,"Institutional setting: Low monetary penalty for not contributing, introduced from round 12; no communication",Treatment described as “penalized them if they did not contribute (‘penalty treatment’)”; text and Table 2 distinguish low- and high-penalty games.,0.95,True,"Same field experiment with farmers, now under low penalty condition.",1,True,Penalty level is an experimentally controlled treatment.,1,False,Field setting in villages.,1,False,Not a computer simulation.,1,False,Empirical treatment.,1,5,Same session size as other conditions.,0.9,21,Same 21-round protocol with treatment from round 12.,1,1,Contribution decision structure unchanged from baseline.,0.8,0,Endowment starts private; players choose contribution to public fund.,0.8,N/R,No numeric MPCR specified.,0,0,"Penalty treatments are described separately from communication; no mention of combined communication-plus-penalty condition, so assume no communication here.",0.8,1,Dynamic investment path depends on observing past collective investment; needed under penalties as well.,0.7,0,"Penalty is implemented as an exogenous rule (“top-down rules”), not peer punishment; no indication that specific defectors’ identities are revealed; assume anonymous enforcement.",0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism in penalty treatments.,1,1,Same fixed-horizon experimental structure likely explained to participants.,0.6,1,“Penalized them if they did not contribute (‘penalty treatment’).”,1,N/R,Paper distinguishes low vs high penalty qualitatively but does not give numeric per-unit cost or fine size.,0,N/R,No specific mapping from penalty units to payoff reduction reported.,0,0,Only penalties are mentioned; no positive sanctions.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not numerically specified.,0,N/R,Only coupon amounts of equilibria are discussed.,0,24,Text: “both LP21- and LP24- equilibrium values of investment (14 and 24) are less than the B22-equilibrium (25).” LP24 is the higher of the stable low-penalty equilibria; use 24 coupons as representative collective investment under low penalties in rounds 12–21.,0.7,N/R,No numeric efficiency measures presented.,0,N/R,No group payoff values given.,0,Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan,Same sample frame across all treatments.,1,N/R,Age data not provided.,0,Predominantly male; more than 98% in Samarkand and 86% in South Kazakhstan were male,Same pool of farmers assigned to different treatments.,1,N/R,Education not reported.,0,Field experiment,Same field setting.,1,"Same irrigation public-good game as baseline (5 farmers, 21 rounds). From round 12, an exogenous low monetary penalty is applied to farmers who do not contribute (or undercontribute) to the public irrigation fund; no communication is allowed. Analysis shows two post-treatment stable equilibria (LP21≈14, LP24≈24 coupons), both below baseline B22=25.",Summarizes how the low-penalty treatment modifies baseline rules and the associated equilibria as described in results.,0.9
10.1002_ird.2704.md,High-penalty treatment – Rounds 12–21,Paper separately analyses “high-penalty irrigation games” with recursion diagram Figure 6 and equilibrium HP21 for post-treatment rounds.,1,"Institutional setting: High monetary penalty for not contributing, introduced from round 12; no communication",Treatment is described as penalty with a higher severity than low-penalty games; no communication mentioned.,0.95,True,"Same field experiment, high-penalty sessions.",1,True,Penalty severity manipulated experimentally.,1,False,Conducted in the field among farmers.,1,False,Human-subject experiment.,1,False,"Refers to empirical condition, not model.",1,5,Same as other experimental sessions.,0.9,21,21 rounds per session; high penalty introduced from round 12.,1,1,Contribution decision space unchanged.,0.8,0,Endowments initially private; players decide contribution.,0.8,N/R,No numeric mapping from public fund to payoffs.,0,0,High-penalty games are discussed separately from communication games; no indication of concurrent communication.,0.8,1,"As with other conditions, dynamic best-response interpretation requires feedback about previous collective investment.",0.7,0,Penalties are characterized as exogenous/top-down rules rather than peer-imposed; identities of penalized individuals likely not revealed.,0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same fixed 21-round structure likely announced to participants.,0.6,1,High-penalty treatment explicitly penalizes non-contributors more severely.,1,N/R,No numeric cost or fine size for high penalty is given.,0,N/R,No quantitative mapping between penalty and payoff reductions reported.,0,0,No rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not numerically stated.,0,N/R,"Only equilibrium investment amounts in coupons, not fractions, are reported.",0,23,"Text: “values of the HP11- and HP13-equilibria (26 and 42, respectively) are greater than the HP21-equilibrium (23)… B22-equilibrium amount (25) is greater than the HP21-equilibrium amount (23).” Use 23 coupons as representative collective investment under high penalties in rounds 12–21.",0.8,N/R,No numeric efficiency reported.,0,N/R,Group payoff levels not provided.,0,Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan,Same experimental participant pool.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0,Predominantly male; more than 98% in Samarkand and 86% in South Kazakhstan were male,Treatment allocation randomized across same male-dominated farmer population.,1,N/R,No data on education.,0,Field experiment,Same rural field experimental setup.,1,"Same irrigation public-good game as baseline (5 farmers, 21 rounds). From round 12, a high-severity exogenous monetary penalty is applied to non-contributors. Results show a post-treatment stable equilibrium HP21≈23 coupons, which is lower than both pre-treatment equilibria (26 and 42 coupons) and the baseline B22=25, indicating that strong penalties crowd out cooperation.",Summarizes rule change (high penalty) and key dynamic equilibrium outcomes described in Section 6.4.,0.9
10.1038_s41467-024-49505-5.md,Analytical and simulation framework for multi-strategy multiplayer games on regular graphs,"The paper develops general analytical replicator equations and compares them qualitatively to prior numerical simulations, but does not introduce distinct experimental treatments in the sense of separate conditions with different parameterizations; the whole framework constitutes one theoretical condition.",0.7,"Graph degree k (number of neighbors), number of strategies n, payoff parameters of the multiplayer game (e.g., public goods: cost c, synergy factor r; punishment games: punishment cost α and fine β; pool punishment vs peer punishment structure), and update rule (pairwise comparison vs death–birth, though main text focuses on pairwise comparison).","Throughout the paper they vary k, n, and game-specific parameters (r, c, α, β) in the analytical expressions and phase diagrams, and also consider two update rules; these are the core independent variables driving dynamics.",0.9,False,"The study is purely theoretical, deriving analytical equations and discussing prior simulation results; no human or biological subjects are used.",1,False,There is no controlled empirical or laboratory experiment; all results are mathematical derivations and references to earlier simulations.,1,False,No laboratory or controlled physical environment with participants is involved; all work is analytical and computational/theoretical.,1,True,"The core contribution is analytical, but the work explicitly targets and reproduces properties of numerical simulations on graphs; the model is an agent-based-like evolutionary process on graphs without human subjects, which falls under simulation.",0.9,True,The main results are closed-form replicator equations and thresholds derived using pair approximation and weak selection; this is formal analytical modeling.,1,5,"The generic framework considers k neighbors plus the focal individual, so each multiplayer game is a (k+1)-player game. In worked examples (e.g., Fig. 3 and others) they set k=4, so player count is 4+1=5 for those illustrative public goods games.",0.8,1,"The evolutionary process is modeled in continuous time over infinitely many elementary steps rather than discrete repeated rounds of a finite game; each interaction is a single-shot multiplayer game, effectively one round per interaction.",0.6,1,"Players are not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions; strategies specify fixed behaviors (e.g., cooperators contribute cost c, punishers also pay α, etc.), so the game is not an all-or-nothing choice over a continuum of contributions in the experimental sense. By the given coding, 1 denotes that players can contribute any amount; here contribution levels are fixed by strategy but not restricted to a binary all-or-nothing decision in the public-goods-contribution sense, so we code as 1 rather than a constrained binary all-or-nothing decision.",0.4,N/A,There are no actual endowments allocated between private and public accounts for human participants; the model specifies strategy payoffs directly in terms of costs and benefits without a default allocation.,1,N/R,"They specify the public goods payoff via a synergy factor r and group size k+1, and derive conditions like r>k+1; however, the marginal per capita return (MPCR = r/(k+1)) is not explicitly labeled or calculated as such in the text.",0.6,N/A,No human participants or communication are involved; interactions are defined only through payoff functions on graphs.,1,0,There is no concept of feedback displays to agents; fitness depends only on payoffs from games and not on shown summaries.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as a payoff modification parameterized by α and β; there is no identity revelation mechanism or observation structure because there are no human players.,1,N/A,"Similarly, in reward or punishment models there is no concept of identity revelation to agents, only payoff changes.",1,0,Agents do not observe a finite horizon or number of rounds; the dynamics are infinite-horizon evolutionary processes.,1,1,A central focus is peer and pool punishment in public goods games; punishment mechanisms with cost α and fine β are explicitly modeled.,1,N/R,"The paper uses a generic punishment cost symbol α and fine β, and gives example values like α=0.7 and β varying, but no single fixed numerical punishment cost for all analyses is specified that could be entered as a single scalar here.",0.5,N/R,"The mapping from each unit of punishment cost to reduction in the target’s payoff is not given as a fixed ratio; instead, generic parameters α and β specify cost and fine but their ratio is not fixed across conditions.",0.4,1,"They state that the framework is applied to reward mechanisms (n=3, Supplementary Note 3) and discuss reward as a mirror to punishment, even though detailed results are in the Supplementary Information.",0.7,N/R,"Reward cost parameters are only mentioned generically and detailed definitions are in Supplementary Note 3, which is not fully provided in the excerpt; no specific numeric value is reported.",0.3,N/R,The exact mapping from reward cost to benefit (reward technology) is not numerically specified in the main text; it is only qualitatively described.,0.3,N/R,"The public goods game is parameterized by cost c and synergy factor r, but no explicit initial endowment per player is stated.",0.5,N/R,"They analyze evolutionary frequencies of strategies and phase diagrams, not normalized contribution rates; contributions are strategy-defined and not reported as aggregate normalized rates.",0.5,N/R,"The model uses a fixed contribution cost c for cooperators and punishers, but no aggregate or per-player contribution amounts over time are reported as outcome variables.",0.6,N/R,"They discuss social dilemmas and conditions for resolving them, but do not compute or report explicit efficiency metrics like group payoff relative to maximum.",0.5,N/R,"Payoff functions for individuals are given, but no aggregate group payoffs are reported as specific numerical outcome variables.",0.6,N/A,There are no human participants; all agents are abstract nodes on a graph.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,No human,"All analyses concern evolutionary dynamics on graphs with abstract agents; there is no online or on-site human experiment, nor a field or observational human study.",1,"Infinite population on a regular graph where each node has k neighbors; strategies updated via pairwise comparison (main text) or death–birth (Supplementary); weak selection limit δ → 0+; includes multi-strategy multiplayer public goods games with cooperation, defection, peer punishment, pool punishment, reward, and multi-stage investment; analysis uses pair approximation and Balls-and-Boxes combinatorics to derive replicator equations valid for arbitrary n-strategy (k+1)-player games.",Summarizes key structural aspects of the theoretical multiplayer game model described throughout the paper.,1
10.1111_vox.13088.md,Study 1 – One-shot 3PPC game condition (all participants as Player C),Study 1 uses a single one-shot third-party punishment-and-compensation (3PPC) game with all participants in the same decision environment.,1,No between-subject experimental manipulation; all participants face the same unfair offer scenario as Player C. Individual differences in blood/organ donor status used only as predictors.,"The paper describes no randomized conditions in Study 1; donor and organ-donor status are measured variables, not experimental manipulations.",1,True,Human participants played a hypothetical 3PPC game for real monetary stakes.,1,True,"Controlled economic game with specified payoffs and a standardized scenario; although donor status is observational, the game itself is an experimental task.",0.8,True,"Described as played in private, anonymously, within university ethical procedures; consistent with on-site lab/psychology testing.",0.8,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulation reported.,1,False,No purely formal/mathematical model without data is reported.,1,3,"3PPC game explicitly states three players: A, B, and C.",1,1,Described as a standard one-shot 3PPC game.,1,1,Player C can spend 'some of this' £5 in £1 units to compensate and/or punish; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,"By analogy, the entire £5 endowment starts in Player C’s private account; nothing is initially allocated to any public/punishment/compensation account.",0.8,N/R,MPCR is a public goods game construct; here there is no group contribution multiplied and shared. Not reported or applicable.,1,0,No mention of communication; decisions made in private and anonymously.,0.9,0,Participants only see the specified unfair transfer (A gives £2 of £10); no mention of summary info about others’ payoffs beyond this scenario.,0.8,0,Game is anonymous; identities of Players A and B are not revealed.,0.9,0,Same anonymity applies to compensation towards Player B.,0.9,1,"Participants are told it is a one-shot game, so the number of rounds (1) is known.",1,1,Player C can choose to punish Player A at a cost.,1,1,Every £1 Player C spends to punish reduces A’s allocation by £2; cost per punishment unit to C is £1.,1,2,Each £1 spent reduces Player A’s allocation by £2.,1,1,Compensation is possible: C can increase B’s payoff at a personal cost.,1,1,Every £1 Player C spends to compensate increases B’s allocation by £2; cost per compensation unit is £1.,1,2,Each £1 spent increases Player B’s allocation by £2.,1,5,Player C has £5 to potentially spend on compensation and/or punishment.,1,0.326,"Interpret ‘contribution’ as average total amount C spends across all who choose any costly action. From text: compensation-only £1.63 (n=156), punish-only £2.43 (n=30), compensate+punish £2.95 total (1.45+1.50; n=50), do-nothing £0 (n=136). Weighted mean spend = (156*1.63 + 30*2.43 + 50*2.95 + 136*0) / 372 ≈ 1.63. Divide by endowment 5 ⇒ 1.63/5 ≈ 0.326.",0.6,1.63,Weighted average total spend of Player C across all participants computed as in DV_contributionRate_reason ≈ £1.63.,0.6,N/R,No aggregate group payoff or explicit efficiency measure reported; cannot compute total surplus vs. maximum from available information.,0.2,N/R,"Group-level total payoffs for A, B, and C not fully enumerated for the sample; only some average individual outcomes for subsets are given.",0.2,UK,"Participants recruited under School of Psychology, University of Nottingham, UK; no other country indicated.",0.9,"Mean 22.12 years (SD 4.05); adult participants, likely university population.",Sample description explicitly reports mean age and SD.,1,52% female; 48% male.,Sample described as 52% female; remaining assumed male given no other categories reported.,0.9,Predominantly university students (exact distribution not reported).,Study conducted in a university psychology setting with young adult sample; student status implied but not explicitly quantified.,0.5,On site,Described as played in private and anonymously under university ethics; no indication of online administration.,0.9,"Third-party punishment-and-compensation (3PPC) game; Player A has £10 and gives £2 to B (unfair 80:20 split). Player B starts with £0 and must accept. Player C (participant) has £5 and can choose one of four mutually exclusive options: compensate-only, punish-only, compensate and punish, or do nothing. Decisions are hypothetical for A and B, but C’s payoff is real for a random subset of participants.",Summarized from the procedural description of the 3PPC game in Study 1.,1
10.1111_vox.13088.md,Study 2 – Pure Control (PC) message condition,One of three randomized message conditions in Study 2; participants saw only the baseline recruitment image and slogan.,1,"Message condition: Pure Control (PC) – image with cartoon blood drop and text 'Donate Blood.. Save Life' only, no additional guilt or AIA wording.",Design section describes three message conditions; this row corresponds to the pure control condition.,1,True,Human participants rated messages and chose whether to take donor information.,1,True,One-way between-groups design with random allocation to three message conditions.,1,True,Described as a small-scale laboratory-based experiment under school ethics; no mention of online platform.,0.9,False,No simulation component.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,1,Each participant individually evaluates a message and decides whether to take information; no strategic interaction.,1,1,Single exposure to a message followed by immediate ratings and behavioural proxy.,1,1,Participants can either take the information leaflet or not; but this is not a contribution game. Default to 1 as there is no constrained contribution magnitude.,0.3,0,No endowment or contribution structure exists; set to 0 by convention.,0.2,N/R,No public goods or return multiplier structure.,1,0,No interaction or communication between participants reported.,1,0,Participants only see the recruitment advert; no info about others’ behaviour or outcomes.,1,0,No punishment mechanism is part of this design.,1,0,No explicit rewarding between participants; just an informational choice.,1,0,It is a one-off survey-like exposure; no indication that a 'number of rounds' is displayed.,0.8,0,No costly punishment or sanctioning option in this intervention study.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No structured reward mechanism analogous to a game; the leaflet is not a payoff change.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Participants 