10.1038_s41598-021-87019-y.md,Imposed treatment – fixed interaction neighborhood,Paper describes a baseline treatment called Imposed where all six individuals in a group are forced to play with each other.,1,Interaction structure: imposed fixed neighborhood (each subject plays 5 pair-wise stag-hunt games with all others in group of six),Main manipulated factor is neighborhood choice vs imposed interaction. This condition has no neighborhood choice.,1,True,Human subjects in lab sessions playing repeated stag-hunt games.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and instructions.,1,True,Conducted in BEElab at Maastricht University (laboratory setting).,1,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No purely mathematical or analytical model as a standalone study.,1,6,Groups of six individuals; each subject interacts with five others.,1,30,Both treatments played the games repeatedly for 30 rounds.,1,1,"Subjects choose between two discrete actions (blue or green), not a contribution amount; all-or-nothing field interpreted as they cannot choose intermediate levels so set to 1 for unconstrained contribution not appropriate; however schema defines 1 if players can contribute any amount and 0 if only all-or-nothing. Here choice is binary only, so set to 0. But question: this is not a contribution game; safer to treat as all-or-nothing=0.",0.4,0,"Not a contribution game with endowments to allocate; default contribution proportion therefore set to 0 as all payoff comes from game outcomes, not pre-allocated to public fund.",0.2,N/R,"This is a stag-hunt coordination game with fixed payoffs (95/75/5/90), not a linear public goods game; MPCR not defined or reported.",1,0,No mention of any communication allowed; only choices via computer interfaces.,0.9,1,Left panel provided information of behavior in previous rounds including actions and earnings in each round and cumulative earnings.,1,0,No explicit punishment mechanism; only anonymous information about others’ actions; identities labeled but no punishment stage.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism apart from game payoffs.,1,1,Subjects knew there were 30 rounds (“30 rounds of stag-hunt games”).,0.9,0,No costly punishment stage; only coordination game outcomes determine payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward option is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs are directly from stag-hunt outcomes per dyad; no initial token endowment per round is specified.,0.2,N/R,No contributions; outcomes are frequencies of efficient/inefficient/miscoordination and welfare gains.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game; no numeric contributions recorded.,1,N/R,They report normalized welfare gain per dyad but not an explicit efficiency ratio (total payoff divided by maximum).,0.6,N/R,Only report normalized welfare gains; no raw group payoff values given.,0.6,Netherlands,All subjects were students of Maastricht University in the Netherlands; likely mostly Dutch-resident student population.,0.8,Mean age 22.7 (overall); median age 22 in main experiment part; range N/R,Methods: mean age 22.7; Results mention median age 22 for n=108 subjects.,1,43% female,Reported in Methods: women = 43%.,1,University students (Maastricht University),All subjects were students of Maastricht University.,1,On site,Conducted in BEElab at Maastricht University with participants in cubicles.,1,"Two-action 2x2 stag-hunt played in all pairwise dyads in a group of six; payoffs per dyad: (Efficient,Efficient)=(95,95); (Safe,Safe)=(75,75); (Efficient,Safe)=(5,90); (Safe,Efficient)=(90,5). Each player chooses one action (blue=efficient, green=safe) that applies to all dyads simultaneously; total payoff per round is sum over 5 dyads. 30 repeated rounds with fixed groups.",Directly summarized from description of game and payoffs in Imposed treatment.,1
10.1038_s41598-021-87019-y.md,Free treatment – free neighborhood choice,Paper describes a treatment called Free that differs from Imposed by allowing neighborhood choice.,1,Interaction structure: free neighborhood choice (subjects propose interaction partners; games played only if proposals reciprocated),Independent variable is neighborhood choice vs imposed interaction; this condition enables neighborhood choice.,1,True,Human subjects in lab sessions playing repeated stag-hunt games with dynamic interaction neighborhoods.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with a formal treatment manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in the BEElab at Maastricht University.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations presented.,1,False,Focuses on experimental data; no standalone analytical model.,1,6,Same group size as Imposed: groups of six.,1,30,Treatment Free also played for 30 rounds with fixed groups.,1,1,"As in Imposed, subjects choose between two fixed actions (blue/green); given schema definition, binary action implies only all-or-nothing so coded as 0. However this is a coordination game, not contribution, making this field somewhat ill-fitting.",0.4,0,No initial contribution allocation; only action choices and neighborhood proposals determine payoffs.,0.2,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR reported or implied.,1,0,No communication between subjects is mentioned; only proposals and actions via interface.,0.9,1,"Left panel showed behavior in previous rounds (actions, earnings, cumulative earnings, interaction neighborhoods).",1,0,"No explicit punishment stage; exclusion occurs via neighborhood proposals, not a punishment action with IDs shown as such.",1,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,1,Subjects knew game length of 30 rounds.,0.9,0,"No formal punishment technology (costly points to reduce others’ payoffs); exclusion is embedded in the matching technology, not a separate punishment action.",0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward stage where subjects can spend to increase others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs arise from dyad outcomes; no fixed per-round token endowment is specified.,0.2,N/R,No contributions measured; outcomes are coordination types and welfare gains.,1,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,They define normalized welfare gain per dyad but not a standard efficiency ratio; exact mean welfare gain values by treatment and period blocks are reported but not converted to 0–1 efficiency relative to maximum possible in group.,0.7,N/R,Only normalized welfare gains are given; raw payoffs or tokens per group are not reported.,0.6,Netherlands,All sessions across treatments share the same subject pool: Maastricht University students in the Netherlands.,0.8,Mean age 22.7 (overall); median age 22; range N/R,Same pool and sample across treatments (subjects randomly and equally distributed across Imposed and Free).,1,43% female,Overall sample composition applies; subjects were equally and randomly distributed across treatments.,1,University students (Maastricht University),All subjects were students of Maastricht University.,1,On site,Sessions conducted in BEElab at Maastricht University with subjects physically present.,1,"Same 2-action pairwise stag-hunt as Imposed with payoffs per dyad: (Efficient,Efficient)=(95,95); (Safe,Safe)=(75,75); (Efficient,Safe)=(5,90); (Safe,Efficient)=(90,5). In each round, subjects choose an action (blue/green) and simultaneously propose which group members to interact with. A dyadic game is played only if both players mutually propose to interact; otherwise both get 0 from that dyad. Not interacting is strictly dominated in monetary terms by any interaction outcome because 0 is lower than the minimum game payoff (5). Groups fixed for 30 rounds.","Summarized from description of Free treatment (interaction proposals, reciprocation requirement, cost of no-play).",1
