10.1371_journal.pone.0236544.md,Contest – Baseline (no response),Treatment described as Baseline with opponent group present; no reward or punishment possible.,1,Contest environment: Opponent group present; Response option: None (Baseline).,"2×4 design: first dimension contest vs non-contest; second dimension response (none, punishment, reward, reward & punishment). This condition is contest × baseline.",1,True,Human subjects in computerized lab experiment at BEElab.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments and controlled manipulation of contest and response institutions.,1,True,Conducted in BEElab at Maastricht University with subjects in cubicles.,1,False,"All decisions made by human participants, no purely simulated agents.",1,False,"Paper includes theory but this treatment is a lab experiment, not purely analytical.",1,4,“Players are randomly sorted in groups of four.” Each group K has four players.,1,15,“The experiment is conducted over 15 rounds.”,1,1,"Players choose how much of 100-token endowment to invest; any integer amount allowed, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each round players receive endowment B=100 tokens in private account and then decide how much to invest; starts private.,1,N/R,"Tullock contest lottery, not linear public good; marginal per capita return not defined in standard MPCR sense.",1,0,No mention of communication between participants; only decision screens described.,0.9,1,"Players see whether their group won, others’ contributions, opponent team’s total tickets, and own detailed payoff summary each period.",1,0,"“Note that players do not get to know from whom they get response points assigned.” In Baseline, no punishment exists; set to 0 as identities never revealed.",1,0,Same anonymity statement for reward; baseline has no reward mechanism but identities are never revealed.,1,1,Total of 15 rounds is disclosed and commonly known; typical z-Tree implementation shows current/remaining rounds.,0.9,0,Baseline treatment explicitly has no further interaction or response; only extra tokens added to own account.,1,N/A,No punishment in Baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment in Baseline.,1,0,Baseline has no possibility to assign tokens to others; tokens are presented as extra tokens to self.,1,N/A,No reward in Baseline.,1,N/A,No reward in Baseline.,1,100,“Each player receives an endowment (budget) of B=100 tokens…”,1,N/R,Only means like ‘Average group level contribution over all treatments is about 142’ are given; not normalized per person by endowment per treatment.,0,N/R,"Figures (e.g., Fig 1, Fig 2) are graphical; exact per-treatment averages not reported numerically for Baseline contest.",0,N/R,Paper reports overspending and income losses vs Nash/social optimum but not normalized efficiency per treatment.,0,N/R,"Only average euro earnings by environment, not by each treatment; Baseline contest not separately given.",0,Netherlands,"Experiment conducted at BEElab, Maastricht University, Netherlands; majority of subjects likely in Netherlands.",0.9,N/R,Only described as “healthy adult subjects” and mostly students; no age statistics.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Mostly university students of Business and Economics,“Most of them students of Business and Economics.”,0.9,On site,Conducted in physical lab (BEElab) with subjects seated in cubicles.,1,"Tullock team contest; two groups of 4 compete. Each token buys one lottery ticket for the group. Opponent group invests; contest success function p_K = sum_K v_k / (sum_K v_k + sum_M v_m) unless all invest zero, then 1/2. Winning group members each get prize z=100 tokens. No human response stage: participants simply receive 50 extra tokens per round added to their account.",Direct description of contest environment and Baseline response stage in Experimental design.,1
