s punish) but no explicit numeric mean for this treatment.,0,N/R,Table 5 reports overspending vs Nash but not efficiency ratio.,0,N/R,Mean income by environment and special note on contest R&P only; not separate for contest–reward.,0,Netherlands,"Same subject pool across treatments at Maastricht University, Netherlands.",0.9,N/R,No explicit age summary.,0,N/R,Gender composition not stated.,0,Mostly university students of Business and Economics,Recruitment description applies to all treatments.,0.9,On site,Same BEElab lab setting.,1,Same team Tullock contest as Baseline. Stage 3: each player receives F=50 response tokens. They may assign any number of these to any subset of their three teammates; each token given costs the giver 1 token and increases the recipient’s payoff by 1 token. Unassigned response tokens are kept by the giver and added to their account at end of round. Players cannot carry response tokens across rounds.,Direct description of Reward treatment in Experimental design; constant cost=1 discussed later.,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0236544.md,Contest – Punishment only,Treatment described as Punish with opponent group present.,1,Contest environment: Opponent group present; Response option: Punishment only.,2×4 design; this condition is contest × punishment.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Manipulated contest and response regimes.,1,True,Conducted at BEElab with participants in cubicles.,1,False,Not a simulation-only study.,1,False,"This record concerns the empirical treatment, not the model.",1,4,Groups of four.,1,15,All treatments run for 15 rounds.,1,1,Contribution choice is an amount from 0–100 tokens.,1,0,Endowment initially private.,1,N/R,Rent-seeking contest rather than linear public good.,1,0,No chat or communication described.,0.9,1,"Participants view others’ contributions, opponent tickets, outcome, and detailed payoff.",1,0,Anonymity: players do not know who sanctioned them.,1,0,"No reward here; in general, sources of response are anonymous.",1,1,Total number of rounds is common knowledge; typically displayed.,0.9,1,Punish treatment: players can assign deduction points to teammates.,1,1,Cost for response-giving is 1 per point (explicit for punishment example).,1,1,Each punishment point reduces target’s payoff by exactly the amount spent (1:1).,1,0,"Only deduction (punishment) is available, no positive transfer.",1,N/A,Reward is not possible.,1,N/A,Reward is not possible.,1,100,Same contest stage endowment.,1,N/R,Exact average contribution for contest–punish is not reported numerically.,0,N/R,Only relative comparison: in non-contest environment reward > punishment; no concrete numbers for contest–punish.,0,N/R,"Overspending vs Nash reported, but not efficiency ratio per treatment.",0,N/R,Mean incomes by environment and for R&P noted; contest–punish not separate.,0,Netherlands,Same pool and location.,0.9,N/R,No age details.,0,N/R,No gender information.,0,Mostly university students of Business and Economics,Applies across treatments.,0.9,On site,BEElab lab experiment.,1,"Same contest as other contest treatments. Stage 3: each player receives F=50 response tokens that can be spent to assign deduction points to any subset of their three teammates. For each point assigned, the giver pays 1 token and the target’s account is reduced by 1 token. If punishment would drive a player’s round payoff negative, it is truncated at zero. Unused response tokens are kept and added to own account at end of round; cannot be carried over periods.",Description of Punish treatment and cost specification; note on truncation at zero.,1
