10.1371_journal.pone.0236544.md,Non-contest – Reward and Punishment (R&P),Reward & Punishment treatment implemented in environment without opponent group.,1,"Contest environment: No human opponent (non-contest, 25 blank tickets); Response option: Reward and Punishment (R&P).",Non-contest × R&P cell in 2×4 setup.,1,True,One of the lab treatments with human subjects.,1,True,Random assignment and controlled conditions.,1,True,On-site experiment at BEElab.,1,False,Not a simulation-only setting.,1,False,This record covers empirical data.,1,4,Groups of four in all environments.,1,15,Same round count.,1,1,Contribution choice is a free amount.,1,0,All 100 tokens start in private account.,1,N/R,Tullock-style contest vs blank tickets; not standard MPCR.,1,0,No chatter or communication permitted or described.,0.9,1,"Feedback includes teammates’ contributions, winning probability (with blank tickets), and payoffs.",1,0,Anonymity rule applies to punishment in R&P.,1,0,Anonymity also applies to reward in R&P.,1,1,Total and progression of rounds likely shown; total count disclosed.,0.9,1,R&P allows punishment option.,1,1,Re-use of constant cost 1 per punishment point.,1,1,One-to-one deduction from target’s payoff.,1,1,R&P also allows rewards.,1,1,Same cost structure for reward.,1,1,One-to-one increase to recipient’s payoff.,1,100,B=100 per period for investment.,1,N/R,No numeric mean contribution rate per player provided for this treatment.,0,N/R,Only overspending vs Nash benchmark in Table 5 and graphical patterns given.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure per treatment reported.,0,N/R,Treatment-specific average earnings not provided.,0,Netherlands,Same subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Age distribution not stated.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0,Mostly university students of Business and Economics,Applies across treatments.,0.9,On site,"Conducted in BEElab, Maastricht University.",1,"Non-contest Tullock game: p_K = sum_K v_k / (sum_K v_k + 25) against 25 blank tickets; prize z=100 paid to each member if group wins; prize forfeited if blank ticket drawn. In Stage 3, each player receives F=50 response tokens that can be used to either reward or punish teammates, subject to the rule that a given teammate cannot be both rewarded and punished in the same period. Cost 1 per token to giver; reward gives +1, punishment −1 (with payoff truncation at zero). Unused tokens are kept by the giver in that round only.",Combines non-contest environment description with R&P response rules.,1
10.1007_s10640-020-00419-y.md,Treatment 0 – No threshold (baseline CPR game),"Paper defines three experimental treatments; Treatment 0 (T0) is the baseline without tipping point, Table 2 and Section 3.1.",1,Nature of threshold: No threshold (no economic sanction or Blim implemented).,Only difference across treatments is nature/knowledge of tipping point (Table 2); for T0 there is explicitly no threshold.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 51 student subjects described in Section 3.2.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized lab protocol with defined treatments.",1,True,"Conducted at LEEM experimental laboratory, University of Montpellier.",1,False,Human subjects; no purely computational simulations reported for this treatment.,1,False,"While a theoretical model is discussed, Treatment 0 refers to the implemented lab game with humans, not an analytical solution.",1,3,Groups are composed of 3 subjects (Table 1; Section 3.1).,1,15,"Game had unknown horizon to subjects, but analysis is explicitly restricted to the first 15 rounds; using 15 as standardized comparable horizon.",0.6,0,Each player chooses an integer harvest between 0 and 5 units; intermediate amounts allowed (Section 3.1).,1,0,"This is a CPR appropriation game (harvesting from a stock), not a contribution-to-public-good game; default contribution proportion concept does not apply but by convention set to 0 (all in private).",0.3,N/R,"No linear public good is defined; payoffs come from a non-linear bioeconomic harvest model, so MPCR is not meaningful.",0,0,"Face-to-face communication is not allowed; only non-binding pledges via the interface (Section 3.1, 3.2).",1,1,"At end of each round, participants are informed about everyone's decisions, cumulative profit, and track records of total catch and profit (Section 3.2).",1,0,No explicit punishment technology or sanctions by peers; only endogenous stock and profit changes. Identities of any ‘punisher’ are not defined.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism is implemented.,1,0,"Subjects do not know the number of rounds; only maximum duration is known and end-time is varied (Section 3.1, 3.2).",1,0,"There is no per-player punishment decision or sanction; only the natural dynamics and profits. The ‘trigger strategy’ is behavioral, not a programmed punishment technology.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No rewarding option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Players have a starting capital of 50 monetary units, but that is money, not a per-round harvest endowment like tokens; maximum harvest capacity is 5 resource units per round but no per-round ‘endowment’ in the public-good sense is defined.",0.4,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as harvests, biomass, profits, and harvest/myopic ratios, not as normalized contributions to a public good.",0,N/R,"Tables and figures provide averages (e.g., average group harvest 1.49 units in T0) but not consistently enough to define a single DV for the treatment; paper emphasizes ratios and biomass rather than a single aggregate.",0.5,N/R,No explicit measure of realized group payoff relative to maximum possible is reported.,0,N/R,"Table 4 shows average group profit (10.31) for T0 across groups and rounds used in analysis, but this aggregates both games and not clearly per group or per round; ambiguous to encode as a single DV value.",0.4,France,"Experiment conducted at University of Montpellier, France; student subjects likely from that country.",0.9,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,Undergraduate university students,Section 3.2: subjects coming from the undergraduate student population.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental laboratory (LEEM) with participants present; computer-based via oTree but not online recruitment.,1,"Dynamic common-pool resource (fishery) harvesting game with 3 players per group, unknown horizon (analysis first 15 rounds), discrete stock with logistic growth (r=0.15, K=70), exogenous price (p=10) and cost depending on biomass, maximum individual harvest 0–5 units per round, initial biomass 52 units; non-binding pledges and belief elicitation before actual harvest decisions; subjects observe others' decisions and cumulative profits each round.",Summarized from Sections 3.1 and 3.2 and Table 1.,1
10.1007_s10640-020-00419-y.md,Treatment 1 – Known tipping point (certain Blim),Treatment 1 (T1) is described in Table 2 as the treatment where subjects know there is a threshold and its position.,1,Nature of threshold: Known economic tipping point at Blim=20 units; subjects know its existence and exact location.,Table 2 plus parameter table (Table 1) where Blim=20; Section 3.1 describes T1 as known tipping point.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment as other treatments.,1,True,Controlled assignment to Treatment 1 conditions.,1,True,"Conducted at LEEM experimental laboratory, University of Montpellier.",1,False,No non-human simulation for T1.,1,False,"T1 pertains to implemented experiment, not just analytical model.",1,3,Groups of 3 subjects (Table 1; Table 2).,1,15,"As for other treatments, analysis limited to first 15 rounds of game; game horizon unknown to subjects.",0.6,0,Same harvest choice set 0–5 units as in baseline; continuous integer choices.,1,0,Appropriation game; no contribution default; coded 0 as no initial public contribution.,0.3,N/R,"Non-linear CPR harvest game, not a linear public good with MPCR.",0,0,Same communication restrictions as other treatments: no face-to-face; only pledges.,1,1,"Participants observe everyone's decisions, cumulative profit, and track records each round (Section 3.2).",1,0,No explicit punishment stage or identity of punisher.,1,0,No explicit reward technology.,1,0,Unknown end-period; maximum duration only.,1,1,Crossing Blim (B_t < Blim) triggers an automatic fixed economic sanction (fixed cost α per player) representing a ban on foreign trade; this is an endogenous economic punishment built into payoff function.,1,30,Fixed cost parameter α = 30 (10^7 € units) per subject when biomass falls below Blim (Table 1 and payoff equations).,1,1,"Each player that period loses α (30 units) regardless of who caused the threshold crossing; cost per sanctioned unit is effectively 1×α for the player. There is no scaling per coin, so we record magnitude factor as 1.",0.5,0,No positive sanctions or bonuses implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Subjects start with capital of 50 monetary units and harvest from stock, but there is no fixed per-round endowment of harvestable units in the standard PGG sense.",0.4,N/R,"Key DVs are harvest/myopic ratio, biomass, profits, and threshold crossings, not contributions.",0,N/R,Table 4 reports average group harvest 1.54 units for T1 (across groups and rounds) but not clearly as a single DV per condition suitable for a unique scalar here.,0.5,N/R,No normalized efficiency index (realized vs. maximum possible payoff) is reported.,0,N/R,"Table 4 reports average group profit 2.90 for T1, but aggregation across groups and rounds makes it ambiguous as a single DV.",0.4,France,Same subject pool and location as T0.,0.9,N/R,No ages reported.,0,N/R,No gender data reported.,0,Undergraduate university students,Section 3.2.,1,On site,Physical lab at University of Montpellier; oTree on computers.,1,"Same dynamic 3-player fishery harvest game as T0, but with a known biomass threshold Blim=20 units; if biomass Bt < Blim, an additional fixed cost α=30 (10^7 € units) per player is subtracted from profit that period, representing an economic sanction (trade ban). Subjects are explicitly informed about the existence and location of this tipping point.","Derived from Table 1, Table 2, and payoff equations in Section 3.1.",1
10.1007_s10640-020-00419-y.md,Treatment 2 – Uncertain tipping point (ambiguous Blim range),Treatment 2 (T2) is introduced in Table 2 and Section 3.1 as the treatment with uncertain threshold location.,1,"Nature of threshold: Tipping point with uncertain location; players know there is a threshold but only that Blim lies in a 40% uniform uncertainty range [Blim_min, Blim_max] around Blim=20 units.","Section 3.1: T2 introduces uncertainty around the position of Blim drawn within a 40% uniform uncertainty range [Blim^min, Blim^max] centered on Blim; subjects know a range with equal possibility (Table 2).",1,True,Same human-subject lab protocol; 15 subjects total in T2 (Table 2).,1,True,Controlled assignment of groups to Treatment 2 conditions.,1,True,Conducted at LEEM laboratory.,1,False,Not a simulation-only study.,1,False,"Empirical treatment, not purely analytical.",1,3,Groups of 3 subjects (Table 2).,1,15,"As with other treatments, analysis restricted to first 15 rounds; horizon unknown to subjects.",0.6,0,Same harvest choice set 0–5 units.,1,0,Appropriation game; no contribution default.,0.3,N/R,"Non-linear CPR harvest, not linear public goods.",0,0,No face-to-face communication; only pledges.,1,1,Same feedback as other treatments: others' decisions and cumulative profits displayed.,1,0,Sanction is automatic via payoff; no identified punisher.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,0,Unknown stopping time; only maximum duration.,1,1,"As in T1, crossing into the sanction region (relative to Blim range) triggers a fixed cost representing economic sanctions, though the actual Blim is drawn from a range.",1,30,Fixed cost parameter α = 30 per player is applied when biomass is in or below the sanction region (Section 3.1).,1,1,Sanction subtracts α from each player's profit when applicable; magnitude coefficient per sanction event effectively 1.,0.5,0,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,"Starting capital is 50 monetary units, but no fixed endowment of harvestable units each round.",0.4,N/R,"Outcomes focus on harvest decisions, myopic ratios, biomass and profits.",0,N/R,"Table 4 reports average group harvest 1.42 units for T2, but it aggregates data; not encoded as a single scalar DV here.",0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0,N/R,"Table 4 shows average group profit 0.40 for T2, but aggregation across groups and rounds makes it ambiguous.",0.4,France,Same lab and recruitment pool.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,0,N/R,Not reported.,0,Undergraduate university students,Same as other treatments.,1,On site,Physical lab experiment at University of Montpellier.,1,"Same dynamic fishery CPR game as T1 with 3 players and logistic stock dynamics, but subjects face an ambiguous economic tipping point: they know there is a biomass threshold that triggers a fixed cost α=30 per player, but they are only told that Blim is uniformly drawn from a 40% uncertainty range around 20 units. Payoff function incorporates a probability-weighted expected sanction when stock is within [Blim_min, Blim_max].","Summarized from Section 3.1, including the piecewise profit function under uncertainty.",1
10.3389_fnins.2019.01160.md,Unilateral DLPFC – Right anodal (R+),"Condition label taken from Table 1 and text describing unilateral DLPFC stimulation modes (R+, R−, L+, L−, U-Sham).",1,"tDCS stimulation mode: unilateral right DLPFC anodal (F4 anode, Pz return), 1.5 mA for 20 min",Independent variable is stimulation montage/polarity; this row corresponds to right anodal unilateral stimulation.,1,True,Human student participants played the 3-player prisoner’s dilemma under tDCS.,1,True,Random assignment to stimulation conditions and controlled game environment.,1,True,"Conducted at the Center for Economic Behavior and Decision-making, Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No formal mathematical model as primary method.,1,3,3-player prisoner’s dilemma; payoff function defined for three players.,1,12,Participants played 12 rounds of the 3-player prisoner’s dilemma.,1,0,"Binary choice between Strategy A (cooperate) and Strategy B (defect), not continuous contributions.",1,0,"No endowment allocation decision; PD framed as discrete strategies, so default contribution proportion treated as 0 (no automatic contribution).",0.3,N/R,Game is not a linear public goods game; marginal per capita return not defined or reported.,0.2,0,No mention of communication; standard anonymous computerized play.,0.9,1,"After choices, strategies of group members (or another group) were revealed anonymously for punishment decisions, which is a summary of others’ behavior.",0.9,0,"Punishment decisions were anonymous; only amount and whether punishment is revealed/naive are mentioned, not identities.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,"Typical economic lab design with fixed known 12 rounds; text implies known horizon though not explicitly stating display, so coded as shown.",0.6,1,Both second-party and third-party monetary punishment phases are central to the design.,1,1,Each token spent by punisher costs them 1 token: ‘Every token that the punisher spends will deduct three tokens from the punished player.’,1,3,Each token spent deducts three tokens from punished player.,1,0,No reward/positive sanction option described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"Baseline payoff is 20 when all cooperate; payoffs given as 20 + 2*Di − 4*sum(Dj), implying an effective baseline of 20 tokens per round.",0.7,N/R,Only cooperation rates (probability of choosing Strategy A) are reported; no normalized contribution fraction beyond binary choice.,0.4,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect with no token contribution amounts reported.,0.4,N/R,Group efficiency relative to maximum not numerically reported or derivable from summary statistics.,0.2,N/R,Average payoffs not reported.,0.1,China,"Participants were students from universities in Xiasha University Town, Hangzhou, China.",1,Mean 20.78 years; 95.7% between 18 and 24; range 17–31,Age statistics reported in Participants section.,1,"54 males, 108 females in total sample; gender balanced across stimulation modes",Numbers given in Participants and Table 1; equal male/female allocation per mode.,1,University students,All participants were students from universities in the Xiasha University Town.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory using z-Tree with participants present.,1,"3-player prisoner’s dilemma with binary strategies A (cooperate) and B (defect); payoff Pi = 20 + 2*Di − 4*sum(Dj≠i). After each choice stage, either second-party punishment (SPP) within group or third-party punishment (TPP) between groups occurs, randomly selected each round (6 SPP, 6 TPP). One randomly chosen punisher per group implements punishment on one randomly chosen target. Punishment range 0–8 tokens, 1:3 cost-to-impact. For each potential target, subjects choose punishments separately under revealed and naive information conditions; one condition is randomly implemented. One of 12 rounds is randomly selected for payment.",Summarizes core structure and timing of the experimental game and punishment procedures as described in Experiment Design.,1
