10.1007_s10657-019-09615-8.md,Main experiment – Homo (homogeneous professional groups),Paper explicitly defines two treatments: Homogenous Treatment (Homo) and Heterogeneous Treatment (Hetero). This row refers to Homo.,1,Group composition information: participants told group is as professionally homogeneous as possible (same-field peers) and matched accordingly.,Design section: 'In the Homogenous Treatment (Homo) subjects were assigned into groups as homogeneous as possible and told that this was the case.',1,True,Laboratory experiment with 136 human participants described in Design and Procedures.,1,True,"Controlled manipulation of treatment (Homo vs Hetero) with randomized matching, typical lab experimental protocol.",1,True,Subjects recruited from university subject pool and participated in a computerised lab setting; payouts in PLN.,1,False,No mention of agent-based models or computer-only simulations; all results are from human-subject sessions.,1,False,No formal analytical model or closed-form derivations of behaviour; only statistical analysis of experimental data.,1,4,"Design: 'played in groups of four, with fixed matching.'",1,10,Design and instructions: 'In each of 10 periods' and 'The experiment will consist of ten periods.',1,1,"Participants can allocate any integer amount of 0–20 to Public and Individual accounts; examples show interior allocations like 5, 7, 12.",1,0,Endowment is given as 20 points to each participant with no automatic allocation to the Public Account: 'must decide ... how to distribute them.' All 20 start in private account conceptually.,1,0.375,"Design: 'The points collected in the Public Account were multiplied by 1.5 and distributed equally among all group members, implying the Marginal per Capita Return of .375.' MPCR = 1.5 / 4.",1,0,No mention of any communication or chat; instructions state only decisions via computer without communication.,0.9,1,"Participants see others' contributions each period and the total punishment received; payoff breakdown also shown: 'Each participant finds out how much the other group members have just allocated...'; 'you will see how it follows from the value of the Public Account, your Individual Account and the number of Punishment Points given and received.'",1,0,Instructions: 'You will NOT know who exactly allocated how many of them.' Thus punishment is anonymous.,1,0,"No reward mechanism is present, so identities of 'rewarders' are not applicable; coded as 0 as per instructions for mechanisms that do not exist.",1,1,"Instructions clearly state there are 10 periods and describe a countdown clock per decision screen; standard z-Tree-style implementations show period numbers. Even if not screenshot-explicit, participants are told the fixed horizon (10 periods).",0.9,1,Design: 'public goods game with punishment'; detailed 'Punishment Phase' described.,1,1,Instructions: 'Each Punishment Point you allocate costs you one point.',1,3,Instructions: 'Each Punishment Point that you receive costs you three points.' So 1 point spent yields 3-point deduction.,1,0,No mechanism allowing participants to spend to increase others' payoffs; only punishment discussed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,Design and instructions: 'In each of 10 periods participants were given 20 points.',1,N/R,Paper reports investments (0–20) and trajectories but does not provide a single numeric mean contribution as a fraction of endowment per treatment.,0,N/R,"While figures show distributions and trajectories qualitatively, no exact mean contribution numbers per treatment are tabulated.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) statistics reported.,0,N/R,"Only example payoffs (e.g., 40 PLN if all free-ride) are given; no actual average group payoff statistics per treatment.",0,Poland,"Authors are at University of Warsaw; currency is PLN and EUR approximations for Poland; funding from National Science Centre, Poland.",1,"Mean 24.4 years, median 22.5 years","Design: 'On average, the participants were 24.4 years old (median 22.5).' This refers to the full sample across treatments.",1,Approximately 56% female,Design: 'Some 56% were female.',1,"University students and graduates in economics/management, medical sciences, law; about 70% students, remainder graduates; some 'other' fields.","Design: 'Only individuals registered in the subject pool as representing one of three educational backgrounds: economics/management, medical sciences, law were invited... About 70% of them were students.' Graduates implied by 'graduate' dummy.",1,On site,Standard physical lab experiment at University of Warsaw with in-person payments in cash; no indication of online implementation.,0.95,"Linear public goods game with fixed groups of 4, 10 periods, endowment 20 points per period, contributions to Public Account multiplied by 1.5 and shared equally. After observing others' contributions, each participant can assign up to 3 punishment points per period across others, costing 1 point per punishment point given and reducing recipient's payoff by 3 per point. Matching is fixed; identities remain anonymous; punishment is anonymous. In Homo treatment, groups are formed to be as homogeneous as possible by field of study and subjects are told this.",Summary of detailed procedural description in Design and Instructions specific to the Homo condition (group-composition information).,1
