10.1007_978-3-642-13983-3_11.md,"Gürerk, Irlenbusch & Rockenbach (2006) – Sanctioning Institution (SI)",Endogenous choice section describes a treatment where subjects choose to join a sanctioning institution (SI) in each period.,0.95,Institutional framework: sanctioning institution (SI) with costly peer punishment and rewards; endogenous community choice,Independent variable is whether subjects are in SI vs SFI; this row is SI condition.,0.95,True,Laboratory public goods experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled experimental design manipulating institution choice.,1,True,Explicitly described as a laboratory experiment.,1,False,No simulation; all decisions made by subjects.,1,False,No purely analytical model is the focus.,1,N/R,"Group size in each institution (SI, SFI) not explicitly reported.",0.3,30,Text: “A public goods game is played over 30 repetitions.”,1,1,"Each player may contribute between 0 and 20 MUs, indicating continuous choice.",1,0,Players are endowed with 20 MUs and “may contribute between 0 and 20 MUs”; default is full private account.,1,N/R,"It is stated that “Each group member equally profits from the public good,” but the factor is not quantified.",0.4,0,No communication or chat channel mentioned; focus is on institutional choice and sanctioning.,0.7,1,"Text: “After the players have simultaneously made their contribution decisions, they are informed about the contributions of each member in the own group… At the end of the period each participant receives detailed (but anonymous) information about each of the other participants from both institutions.”",1,0,"Information is “detailed (but anonymous)”; thus, punishment not linked to identity.",0.9,0,Positive sanctions exist but information is anonymous; identities not revealed.,0.8,1,Experiment describes a 30-period horizon; subjects are likely informed; standard in such designs.,0.8,1,"In SI, “each player in SI may positively or negatively sanction other members of SI.” Negative sanctio