ot given.,0.3,N/R,Text says that groups with punishment keep contributed fractions of current wealth at a constant level; numeric rates not provided.,0.4,N/R,No average contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,Narrative describes wealth growth paths and hints that higher wealth and contribution levels would be achieved with slightly longer horizon; no explicit efficiency values.,0.3,N/R,No specific payoff numbers provided.,0.2,N/R,Not identified.,0.2,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,N/R,Not described.,0.2,On site,Within context of laboratory economic experiments.,0.9,"Dynamic linear public-good game where players’ wealth evolves over time based on contributions and payoffs; initial punishment is strong, leading to early disadvantage relative to no-punishment groups. Over time, contributions as a share of wealth remain constant and punishment levels fall, enabling groups with punishment to catch up and surpass no-punishment groups in wealth, given a sufficiently long time horizon.",Directly describes dynamic effects of punishment as given in the chapter.,1
10.1007_s11284-013-1066-5.md,Deterministic coupled dynamics with internal and external social pressure (cases 1–5 parameter analysis),"The paper presents a single mathematical-simulation model of coupled socioeconomic and bioeconomic dynamics, analyzed across parameter regimes (cases 1–5) but always with both internal (φ, γ) and external (λ) social pressure terms present. There are no human experiments or distinct treatment implementations, only analytical and numerical exploration of this one model.",0.9,"Model parameters governing social pressure and fishing: internal pressure scale φ, internal relationship strength γ, external pressure scale λ, fishing coefficients f_C and f_N (or F_C and F_N), stock parameters r, K, biomass threshold ȳ, behavioral parameters β and s. Different qualitative cases (1–5) correspond to inequalities involving these parameters.","Independent variables are the model parameters varied in the analysis (φ, γ, λ, F_C, F_N, K, r, ȳ, β, s). The authors classify five dynamic cases based on inequalities involving these parameters and then discuss how changing φ, γ, λ, and F_N relative to F_C affects outcomes.",0.96,False,The study is purely theoretical: a coupled differential equation model with no human participants or empirical data collection.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; the paper derives and analyzes equations and their dynamics.,1,False,There is no laboratory setting; all results come from mathematical analysis and simulations.,1,True,"The authors analyze ODEs and present phase portraits, trajectories, and limit cycles (e.g., Figs. 2–3), implying numerical simulation of the model without human subjects.",0.96,True,"They derive isoclines, equilibria, and stability conditions analytically (e.g., cases 1–5 defined by inequalities, Jacobian eigenvalue discussion).",1,0,"Number of players n appears as a parameter in F_C = n f_C and F_N = n f_N, but no specific numeric value is given; with no human subjects this is not a literal player count.",0.5,0,"The model is continuous-time (d x/dt, d y/dt) rather than a finite repeated game with discrete rounds.",1,1,"Players choose between two discrete strategies (C vs N); there is no continuous contribution decision, but in this CPR context the binary choice is closer to an all-or-nothing cooperation decision. However, since this is not a standard contribution game and there are no humans, this coding is approximate.",0.4,N/A,No endowment or contribution structure with human players is modeled; it is a theoretical fishery exploitation model.,1,N/R,The model is not framed as a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction or communication is modeled explicitly.,1,N/A,No human-facing information structure is described; it is an abstract differential equation model.,1,N/A,Punishment is modeled as an impersonal psychological cost (social pressure) α in the utility of noncooperators; no identities or observability among real participants.,1,N/A,There is no explicit reward mechanism in the sense of targeted rewards between individuals.,1,N/A,Continuous-time model; no human interface with displayed number of rounds.,1,1,"Noncooperators incur a psychological punishment term α (“social pressure”) in their utility (U_N = y f_N − α); this acts as a punishment mechanism, albeit psychological and modeled parametrically.",0.95,N/R,"α is modeled as a direct disutility to noncooperators; there is no explicit separate cost borne by punishers per unit of punishment, so a standard punishment cost parameter is not defined.",0.4,N/R,"The mapping from any punisher’s action to α is not modeled as a per-unit technology; α is directly parameterized by φ, γ, λ, x, and y.",0.4,0,Only a negative utility term (social pressure on noncooperators) is included; no explicit positive rewards or bonuses are modeled.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,Players do not receive a fixed per-period endowment of tokens; instead they harvest from a biomass y via fishing coefficients.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are fraction of cooperators x and biomass y, not normalized contribution rates out of an endowment.",1,N/R,There are no discrete contribution amounts; behavior is captured by continuous fraction x of cooperators.,1,N/R,The paper discusses MSY and socially desirable states but does not report a normalized efficiency metric (payoff relative to maximum) in 0–1 form.,0.7,N/R,Utilities are modeled for individual strategies; total group payoff is not reported numerically as a DV in tables or figures.,0.8,N/A,There are no human participants; it is a theoretical model and simulation.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,No human,"Study consists of analytical and numerical modeling only; no lab, field, or online environment with participants.",1,"Coupled continuous-time model of a local fishery where players choose between cooperating with a stock-management rule (C) or not (N). Utilities: U_C = y f_C, U_N = y f_N − α with f_N > f_C. Social pressure α > 0 when biomass y < ȳ, decomposed into internal component φ ((ȳ−y)/ȳ)(1+γ x) and external component λ ((ȳ−y)/ȳ)(1−x). Behavior dynamics follow a stochastic best-response (SBR) process with parameter β (utility sensitivity) and s (adjustment speed): dx/dt = s(1/(1+exp[−β(U_C−U_N)]) − x). Biomass follows a Gordon–Schaefer logistic harvest model: dy/dt = r(1−y/K) y − [F_C x + F_N (1−x)] y, with F_C = n f_C, F_N = n f_N and F_C set to r/2 to represent MSY when all cooperate. The authors derive x- and y-isoclines, classify five qualitative dynamic cases (1–5) based on intersections and stability (full cooperation, no cooperation, intermediate cooperation, and bistable regimes), and examine how changes in φ, γ, and λ shift the system between cases, emphasizing that sufficiently strong internal and external social pressure together can yield full cooperation and sustainable stock levels.","Summarizes the key structural elements of the theoretical model and its game-theoretic and ecological coupling as described in the Model, Equilibria and stability, and Effect of parameter values sections.",0.98
10.1371_journal.pone.0166437.md,Model 1 – Modified Trust Game (analytical),Single analytical game-theoretic model (Model 1) of a repeated modified trust game without matching; no separate experimental treatments.,1,"Recipient competence level a; donor generosity strategy (H, M, L); recipient intention (C vs D). This row summarizes the analytical model across the full range of a.","Model 1 varies competence a and considers three donor strategies (high, middle, low generosity) and two recipient intentions (cooperate/defect).",0.9,False,No human participants; purely theoretical analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a mathematical model.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely analytical.,1,False,"Model 1 is solved analytically using Markov chains and Nash equilibrium, not via computational simulation.",1,True,Authors explicitly describe mathematical analysis with closed-form equilibrium conditions (C1–C3) and Markov chain payoffs.,1,2,"Model 1 is a dyadic, two-player trust relationship (donor and recipient).",1,100,They state: “The entire game comprises 100 sub-game rounds…”.,1,1,"Players decide only whether to trust, to cooperate, or to defect; there is no choice of contribution amount, so the all-or-nothing contribution restriction is not applicable. By convention, set to 1 when continuous contribution choice is absent and only binary moves exist.",0.5,N/A,"No public goods or contribution allocation; it is a trust game with discrete choices, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,Model is not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication stage.,1,0,Information structure limited to each player’s observation of past outcomes; no mention of summary statistics being shown.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment stage or identity revelation; strategies encode exit/forgiveness but not targeted punishment choices.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Game length is fixed and common knowledge in the analytical setup (100 sub-game rounds).,0.9,0,No costly punishment action; only exit vs continuation decisions.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No costly reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model only specifies payoffs P, R, T for outcomes; it does not describe an explicit per-round endowment in tokens.",0.6,N/R,No contribution amount or rate is defined; outcome is binary trust/cooperate decisions and resulting payoffs.,1,N/R,No contributions in the sense of public goods tokens.,1,N/R,"They discuss Pareto efficiency qualitatively ((H,C) Pareto efficient) but do not report efficiency as a 0–1 numeric measure.",0.7,N/R,"They define per-player payoffs (P=1, R=4, T=6), but not aggregate group payoff statistics as reported DVs.",0.7,N/A,No human participants in analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical model with no empirical data.,1,"Repeated two-player modified trust game with 100 sub-game rounds, payoffs P=1, R=4, T=6, competence a_j as success probability, exogenous breakup probability d=0.1, donor strategies H/M/L differ in forgiveness of past failures, recipient chooses C or D; donor history memory restricted to last outcome.","Summarizes structural features explicitly specified in the Model 1 description (game tree, payoff matrix, strategy definitions, Markov-chain-based payoffs).",1
10.1371_journal.pone.0166437.md,Model 2 – Agent-based simulation at competence a = 0.55,Model 2 is an agent-based simulation where competence a is systematically varied; 0.55 is discussed as a case where cooperation is near the threshold but generally fails and ST dominates.,0.95,"Recipient competence level a = 0.55; donor trust strategies ST, MG, EG, SG; recipient intention types x ∈ {0, 0.5, 1}.",Simulation explicitly varies competency a and tracks proportions of four donor strategies and three recipient intention levels; this row captures the treatment with a = 0.55.,0.95,False,"Simulation with artificial agents, not human subjects.",1,False,"This is a numerical simulation model, not a controlled behavioral experiment with participants.",1,False,No physical lab or field environment; entirely computational.,1,True,They state: “We construct the agent-based simulation with C++ …”; competence and strategies evolve under imitation dynamics.,1,False,"Model 2 results are obtained via numerical ABM, not via closed-form analysis.",1,500,"Benchmark case is 250 donors and 250 recipients, totaling 500 agents.",1,100,"They state: “After this process repeats L rounds, the roles of both players evolve” and set L=100; each evolution consists of 100 rounds.",0.9,1,"Agents choose discrete actions (trust vs not; honor vs defect), not continuous contribution levels.",0.9,N/A,"No endowment allocation between private/public accounts; trust game structure, not a contribution game.",1,N/R,"Trust game with fixed payoffs, not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.",1,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,0,Agents access only each candidate partner’s last-round history (C or D); no global summaries are described.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism with identities; only matching and exit based on history.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Round-based simulation where L=100 is fixed and part of the model specification; agents implicitly operate in a finite-horizon setting.,0.7,0,"No costly sanctions; negative consequences arise only via exit and partner choice, not via a punishment action.",1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,"No costly, explicit rewarding of partners; only cooperative outcomes via the trust game.",1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/R,"Payoff structure (P=1, R=4, T=6) is inherited from Model 1, but a per-round endowment is not described.",0.6,N/R,"Outcome metrics are proportions of strategy types and intentions, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable; only payoffs and strategy frequencies.,1,N/R,They do not report aggregate payoff efficiency for the simulation at a=0.55.,0.8,N/R,"Text summarizes which strategies dominate, not the average total payoffs.",0.8,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computer simulation.,1,"Agent-based trust model with 250 donors and 250 recipients. In each round, donors select partners from a matching pool based on matching generosity (ST: choose only partners with good history C; MG: choose C or D; EG: only C; SG: C or D). After playing the modified trust game (P=1, R=4, T=6, competence a=0.55, exogenous breakup probability d=0.1), donors decide whether to continue the relationship based on exit generosity (ST and MG: exit if partner defects; EG and SG: continue even after defection). Recipients have intention types x ∈ {0, 0.5, 1} which determine probability of honoring trust. All agents share the same competence a=0.55. After L=100 rounds, strategies evolve via imitation dynamic with mutation rate M=0.025. At a=0.55, cooperation is near but below the threshold: the Strict Trust (ST) strategy dominates and cooperative intentions remain comparatively low.","Compiled from Model 2 description, parameter settings (P,R,T,B,d,M,L), and discussion around Fig 3–5 where a=0.55 is used as a representative low-competence case.",0.95
10.1371_journal.pone.0166437.md,Model 2 – Agent-based simulation at competence a = 0.65,Competence a=0.65 is analyzed as a typical case above the cooperation threshold where EG and SG coevolve and cooperative intentions are high.,0.95,"Recipient competence level a = 0.65; donor trust strategies ST, MG, EG, SG; recipient intention types x ∈ {0, 0.5, 1}.",Simulation examines different values of competence a; 0.65 is highlighted in figures and text as a stable cooperative regime.,0.95,False,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,False,"Numerical simulation, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,No physical lab; code-based simulation.,1,True,They explicitly build an agent-based simulation in C++ and run trials at multiple competence values.,1,False,"Results at a=0.65 are obtained from simulation runs, not formulae.",1,500,Benchmark population size is fixed at 250 donors plus 250 recipients.,1,100,Each evolutionary update occurs after L=100 rounds.,0.9,1,"Only discrete choices (trust vs not, honor vs defect) exist; no graded contributions.",0.9,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,Trust-game payoff structure; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human communication step.,1,0,Agents see only partner’s last action result (history C or D); no summarized feedback specified.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment institution.,1,N/A,No explicit reward institution.,1,1,Finite horizon L=100 is part of the simulation setup and thus effectively common knowledge to the modeler; agents operate in repeated rounds with fixed evolutionary update interval.,0.7,0,No costly punishment decisions; only exit vs continuation.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit costly rewarding beyond standard cooperative payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Per-round endowment is not specified; only outcome payoffs P, R, T are given from Model 1 and reused in Model 2.",0.6,N/R,Key DVs are proportions of donors’ strategy types and recipients’ intention types; no contribution rate variable.,1,N/R,No contribution amount; discrete trust decisions instead.,1,N/R,"Authors focus on coexistence of strategies (EG, SG) and cooperation levels; efficiency statistics are not reported.",0.8,N/R,"Simulation output summaries concern strategy distributions, not aggregate payoff values.",0.8,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Agent-based computer model.,1,"Same ABM structure as for a=0.55: 250 donors and 250 recipients; donors adopt one of four strategy profiles combining matching and exit generosity (ST, MG, EG, SG) based on partner’s last-round history; recipients have intention types x ∈ {0, 0.5, 1} that determine probability of honoring trust; competence is fixed at a=0.65 and common to all recipients; payoffs P=1, R=4, T=6, and exogenous breakup probability d=0.1 from the modified trust game; after L=100 rounds, strategies update by imitation with mutation rate M=0.025. At a=0.65, cooperative intentions become high and stable; Exit Generous (EG) and Super Generous (SG) strategies coevolve and together sustain generous trust, while Strict Trust (ST) loses dominance.","Synthesizes description of Model 2 process, parameterization, and specific behavior for a=0.65 from discussion around Fig 3–6.",0.95
10.1016_j.jebo.2014.08.005.md,Analytical ranking profile model with community and bilateral enforcement,"The paper presents a single formal theoretical model of repeated helping with ranking profiles, analyzed under community enforcement and bilateral enforcement; there are no distinct empirical conditions or parameterized simulations treated as separate experiments.",0.9,"Ranking profile structure (e.g., Latin square vs. non–Latin-square, Bilateral Friendship Form vs. other profiles); enforcement regime (community enforcement vs. bilateral enforcement); discount factor δ, success probability p, benefit v, and cost c as continuous parameters.","The analysis varies the structure of ranking profiles and the enforcement mechanism and derives conditions in terms of δ, p, v, c under which full cooperation is sustainable.",0.95,False,The paper is purely theoretical with no human participants or empirical data.,1.0,False,No controlled behavioral experiment is conducted; only theoretical analysis is provided.,1.0,False,There is no laboratory or online experiment; the work is analytical.,1.0,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations are reported; the results are derived analytically.,1.0,True,"The paper develops and analyzes a formal repeated-game model with proofs (lemmas, propositions) and no numerical simulation or data.",1.0,4,"The general model allows any n≥3, but concrete ranking profiles are illustrated with 3, 4, and 7 players. Since no single n is fixed for a run, this numeric choice is illustrative; 4-player examples (Figs. 2 and 4) are central for Latin square and BFF discussion.",0.2,-1,"The game is infinitely repeated (time t=1,2,... with discount factor δ), so there is no finite number of rounds; encoded as -1 to indicate an infinite horizon.",1.0,1,"Helping decisions are binary e_j^i∈{0,1}, but this is not a public-goods contribution slider; the all-or-nothing field is defined for contribution magnitude choice, which does not exist here. Using 1 as the closest analogue (binary action) while noting the mismatch.",0.3,N/A,"There is no endowment-allocation or contribution mechanism; the model is about costly binary help, not contributions to a public pool.",1.0,N/R,No public-goods production function or marginal per-capita return is defined; only benefit v and cost c of bilateral help are specified.,1.0,N/A,There are no human subjects or experimental interface; communication options are not modeled.,1.0,N/A,This is a theoretical model without an experimental display; no feedback interface is described.,1.0,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as changes in future helping probabilities within the strategy profile, not as an experimental feedback option with identities shown.",1.0,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism with identity revelation is specified; only continued cooperation vs. cessation of help.,1.0,N/A,There is no user interface; the horizon is infinite and not ‘shown’ to participants.,1.0,1,Strategies include grim-trigger community enforcement and bilateral punishment where victims cease to help deviators in the future; this is a punishment mechanism in the theoretical game.,1.0,N/A,"Punishment occurs via withdrawal of future help (foregoing benefits), not by spending a costly punishment token; no numeric per-unit punishment cost is defined.",1.0,N/A,There is no parametrized punishment technology transforming coins into harm; punishment is embedded in the continuation play.,1.0,0,The model has only helping vs. not helping and punishment via stopping help; no separate costly reward stage or technology is introduced.,1.0,N/A,No explicit costly rewarding action exists.,1.0,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1.0,N/R,Players do not receive an explicit per-period token endowment; instead they face binary help decisions with benefit v and cost c; no numeric endowment is specified.,1.0,N/A,There are no experimental measurements of contribution behavior; the analysis is purely theoretical and not based on observed contribution rates.,1.0,N/A,"No contributions are recorded; the main outcomes are equilibrium conditions (e.g., threshold δ) rather than empirical amounts.",1.0,N/R,"Theoretically, full cooperation is socially optimal, but no numeric efficiency measure (payoff ratio) is reported as an empirical DV.",1.0,N/R,The paper derives expected payoffs symbolically but does not present empirical payoff data; there is no single numeric group payoff reported as a DV.,1.0,N/A,No human participants are used; the model is purely analytical.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,N/A,No participants.,1.0,No human,"The work consists of formal game-theoretic analysis without human subjects, field data, or lab implementation.",1.0,"Infinite-horizon repeated helping game on a community of n players with fixed ranking profiles (ordering of whom to ask for help). Each period a uniformly random player receives a problem; approached helpers choose effort e∈{0,1}. If a helper exerts effort, the problem is solved with probability p, yielding benefit v to the helped player and cost c to the successful helper. Ranking profiles determine the sequence of approached helpers; Latin-square profiles equalize expected helping costs, and Bilateral Friendship Forms ensure pairwise symmetry. Full cooperation is supported via community grim-trigger strategies or via bilateral enforcement with pair-specific punishments.","Summarizes the core theoretical game structure, player actions, payoffs, and key structural notions (Latin squares, BFF, enforcement regimes) as described in Sections 2–4.",1.0
10.1007_s11432-010-4145-z.md,Experiment 1 – Relationship between user number N and punishment times k in FCAR,"The paper labels an experimental evaluation: 'Experiment 1. In finite punishment strategy, analyzing the relationship of user number N and punishment times k.'",1,Number of users N and punishment times k varied jointly under FCAR finite punishment strategy,Experiment 1 explicitly studies the relationship between N and k in the FCAR algorithm using finite punishment strategy.,0.86,False,No human participants; experiment is algorithmic/simulation over network flow control parameters.,1,True,"They run controlled tests of FCAR varying N and k, described as 'Experiment 1'.",0.9,False,No physical or human lab setting; only programmatic/network simulation.,1,True,"Described overall as 'experiment and simulation'; here, Experiment 1 explores theoretical FCAR behavior via parameter sweeps, implying simulation.",0.86,False,This part is not purely closed-form derivation but an implemented algorithmic experiment.,0.8,0,N is varied and not fixed to a single value; no single player count applies to this condition-wide summary.,0.7,0,They discuss total stages T as an input but do not specify a numeric value in Experiment 1.,0.5,1,"Flow rates x_i are continuous quantities; users can choose any (non-negative) flow rate up to capacity, not just all-or-nothing.",0.95,N/A,Not a public goods contribution setting and no human endowment.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined; utility is x_i^β / d_l.,1,N/A,No human interaction; only automated algorithm.,1,0,No user interface or feedback summaries to human participants; purely algorithmic.,1,N/A,No identities of human punishers; punishment is a network control mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards and no human identities.,1,0,"No human subjects or UI, so nothing is displayed.",1,1,Finite punishment strategy is central: cooperative users 'punish' deviating users by changing their rates.,1,N/A,No exp