10.1016_j.heliyon.2023.e22748.md,"Simulation – C+D+P vs standard PGG, threshold r with different mechanisms (R vs b, fine, x)","Fig.9 compares standard public goods game and the bonus-supported punishment mechanism, showing cooperation proportion as a function of the gain factor R and other parameters (b, fine, x).",0.9,"Two mechanisms: (1) standard PGG with strategies {C, D}; (2) bonus-based mercenary punishment with strategies {C, D, P}. For each, gain factor R is varied along one axis, and one of {b, fine, x} is varied along the other axis for subfigures (a), (b), (c).","Text: 'In Fig. 9... We can observe that in the standard public goods game, cooperation needs to emerge when R is greater than 3.5. However, after introducing the bonus support punishment mechanism, the threshold for cooperation to emerge decreases.' Subfigures are described for b, fine, and x.",0.8,False,Both mechanisms are simulated.,1,False,No human subjects.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,Comparative simulations across parameter grids.,1,False,Results via simulations (heatmaps/curves).,0.9,5,Underlying public goods groups of size 5 in both mechanisms.,0.7,10000000,10^7 iterations used for all simulation data.,0.8,1,Binary cooperation decision in both mechanisms.,0.5,N/A,No explicit human endowments.,1,N/R,"R is varied, but MPCR not defined in these terms.",0.4,N/A,Simulated agents cannot chat.,1,0,No interface for agents.,0.9,N/A,No human IDs.,1,N/A,No human-level rewards.,1,0,Rounds not shown.,1,1,"In the bonus-based mechanism branch of Fig.9; standard PGG branch has no punishment. Since this JSON row represents the comparative parameter study as a whole, we mark punishmentExists=1 to capture the key new mechanism.",0.7,N/R,x is varied in at least one subfigure and may be fixed elsewhere; no single numeric value for the overall study.,0.4,N/R,fine parameters vary in some subfigures or are fixed; no single value for the whole comparative set.,0.4,1,Bonus-based punishment mechanism includes fine redistribution as rewards; this is central to the comparison.,0.8,0,Rewards drawn from fines; no extra cost.,0.6,N/R,"Subsidy magnitude depends on fine, which is varied or fixed differently across subfigures; cannot specify a single numeric value.",0.4,N/R,Endowment not described.,0.6,N/R,They highlight threshold R>3.5 for cooperation in standard PGG and lower threshold in punishment mechanism but do not give precise cooperation fractions across all parameter combinations.,0.4,N/R,No average contributions quoted.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff is discussed qualitatively but not numerically.,0.3,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Comparative simulations.,1,"Study shows that in standard spatial PGG, cooperation emerges only for R>3.5, while with bonus-supported mercenary punishment, the critical R for cooperation is reduced. Subfigure (a) varies R and b, (b) varies R and fine, and (c) varies R and x, illustrating how these parameters interact with the synergy factor to sustain cooperation.",Summarized from the discussion of Fig.9.,0.9
10.1016_j.heliyon.2023.e22748.md,"Simulation – C+D+P, variation of fine allocation (α, β) with α+β=1","Fig.10 examines how different allocations of fines between cooperators and punishers (α, β) affect cooperation, with α+β=1 and parameters r=3.5, b=0.05, fine=0.21, x=0.32.",0.95,"Strategy set = {C, D, P}; r = 3.5; b = 0.05; fine = 0.21; x = 0.32; fine allocation parameters α and β varied with constraint α+β=1.","Text for Fig.10: 'When r=3.5, b=0.05, fine=0.21, x=0.32, and for different α and β values, the function between the parameters and the proportion of each strategy (α+β=1) is shown.'",1,False,Numerical simulations only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field context.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations varying allocation parameters.,1,False,"Results plotted from simulations, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,5,Same group size as core model.,0.7,10000000,At least 10^7 iterations stated as standard for experimental data.,0.8,1,Choice to cooperate is still binary.,0.5,N/A,No human endowment details.,1,N/R,MPCR not given; only r is specified.,0.4,N/A,Simulation without communication.,1,0,No GUI for agents.,0.9,N/A,No human IDs.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,0,Rounds not shown to agents.,1,1,Defector fines and punisher roles are essential here.,1,0.32,x=0.32 is given explicitly.,1,0.21,fine=0.21 is the per-defector penalty.,1,1,"Fines are 'subsidied back' to cooperators and punishers, which is a reward/subsidy mechanism.",1,0,"Subsidies come from fines paid by defectors, not from costs borne by other players.",0.9,0.21,"Each punished defector pays fine=0.21, which is fully distributed as α*0.21 + β*0.21 = 0.21 to cooperators and punishers.",0.9,N/R,No numeric endowment specified.,0.6,N/R,"Fig.10 shows the proportion of each strategy (C, D, P) as a function of α and β; while these can be read off from curves/heatmaps, exact values for each α,β are not numerically tabulated in the text.",0.4,N/R,No per-player contribution amounts reported.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency is not calculated; discussion focuses on strategy proportions.,0.3,N/R,No numeric group payoff reported.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulation with varying fine allocation.,1,"Study finds that increasing α (larger share of fines to cooperators) actually lowers cooperation because it weakens punishers by reducing their fine-based income, leading eventually to punisher extinction and subsequent elimination of cooperators. Conclusion: to best promote cooperation, fines should be allocated more to punishers (higher β).",Directly from the description of Fig.10.,0.95
10.1016_j.econlet.2016.12.025.md,Analytical model of asymmetric cooperation with contagious punishment,"The paper develops a theoretical infinitely repeated matching model; no distinct empirical treatments or simulations are described, so the whole analytical model is treated as one condition.",0.9,"Productivity shock structure (θ ∈ {1, α}, probability q), cost and benefit parameters (c, g, l), discount factor β, population size N, and whether efficiency implies full cooperation (condition (1) holds) or asymmetric cooperation (condition (1) does not hold). This row corresponds to the asymmetric social dilemma case where condition (1) does not hold and the Asymmetric Cooperation norm with contagious punishment is analyzed.",Independent variables are parameters exogenously varied in the theoretical analysis across propositions; here they focus on the heterogeneous productivity case with asymmetric cooperation supported by contagious punishment.,0.78,False,The paper contains no human-subject data; it is purely theoretical with mathematical derivations.,1,False,"No controlled lab, field, or online experiment with participants is run; only game-theoretic analysis is provided.",1,False,There is no laboratory or physical setting; only analytical work.,1,False,"Although Markov transition matrices are used, there is no report of numerical or agent-based simulations; results are closed-form/analytical.",0.95,True,"The core of the paper is formal analytical modeling with closed-form expressions and proofs (transition matrices, continuation values, equilibrium conditions).",1,4,"Model specifies N ≥ 4, even; no single value is fixed. Use the minimum admissible N (4) as representative, since exact N is a symbolic parameter.",0.4,-1,Infinitely repeated game; number of rounds is unbounded. Represented as -1 to denote infinity.,1,1,"Players choose between C and D, not a continuous contribution amount; the all-or-nothing flag is defined as 1 when players can contribute any amount, but here there are only two discrete actions. Since neither fits perfectly and no human experiment exists, treat as allowing non-continuous choice; 1 used as closest mapping.",0.3,N/A,This is not a standard public goods contribution game with an initial endowment; it is a Prisoner's Dilemma style stage game with actions C and D and payoffs specified directly.,1,N/R,"No marginal per capita return or linear public good structure is specified; payoffs are given as θ_i c etc., not in MPCR form.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication possibilities among lab participants are not applicable.,1,N/A,"Information feedback is defined in terms of theoretical observables (whether everyone chose identically or not, and private observation of partner type), not implemented in an experiment; the coding scheme targets human experiments.",1,N/A,"Punishment is embedded as strategy (switch to D) and is anonymous in a matching model; with no experiment and no identity display decision, this field is not applicable.",1,N/A,No explicit reward stage or identity revelation concerning rewards is modeled.,1,N/A,Infinite horizon analytical game; no user interface that could display remaining rounds.,1,1,"Contagious punishment is central: players permanently switch to D after observing off-equilibrium defections; this is a sanctioning mechanism, albeit endogenous within the strategy.",0.95,N/R,"Punishment is implemented as switching action to D, not as costly punishment points; no per-unit cost parameter like in standard punishment technologies is specified.",0.6,N/R,"There is no explicit mapping from punishment tokens to monetary losses; punishment operates via continuation payoffs and contagion, not a simple linear technology.",0.6,0,The model features cooperation/defection and punishment via defection contagion; no separate rewarding institution or positive transfers beyond cooperation itself.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism exists, so its cost is not applicable.",1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,Stage-game payoffs are directly specified (θ_i c etc.); no explicit monetary or token endowment per round is described.,0.8,N/R,"Outcome variables are equilibrium payoffs, conditions on parameters, contagion rates, etc., not contribution fractions relative to an endowment.",1,N/R,"Actions are binary (C or D), with no reported numeric contributions in experimental data.",1,N/R,They discuss efficiency conceptually (whether certain profiles maximize total earnings) but do not report numeric efficiency ratios for realized play in experiments.,0.8,N/R,"Expressions like π* and π denote expected per-period or per-player earnings; no single numeric group payoff is instantiated, and no empirical data are given.",0.8,N/A,No human participants are involved in the analytical model.,1,N/A,No sample of human participants is used.,1,N/A,No human-subject experiment; gender is not defined.,1,N/A,No human participants; education is irrelevant.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical with no empirical implementation.,1,"Infinitely repeated random matching among N ≥ 4 even players; each period players are paired uniformly at random. Each player draws iid productivity shocks θ ∈ {1, α} with Pr(θ=α)=q. Stage game is a Prisoner's Dilemma-type interaction with actions C and D and payoffs depending on own and partner productivity: (C,C) yields (θ_i c, θ_-i c); (D,C) yields (θ_i(c+g), -l, θ_-i(c+g)) etc. Players observe own and partner productivities in their match but not others'. At the end of each period, public signal indicates whether everyone chose identically. Strategies considered include full cooperation with grim trigger (when full cooperation is efficient) and Asymmetric Cooperation with contagious punishment (players choose C if θ_i ≥ θ_-i and switch to permanent D upon observing a deviation). The paper derives transition matrices for the contagion of defection, continuation payoffs v_k, expected per-period earnings π* under equilibrium, and conditions on (α, β, c, g, l, q, N) for equilibrium sustainability.",Summarizes the formal game description and equilibrium concept as given in Sections 2–2.4.,0.98
10.1016_j.socec.2023.102050.md,Pay-to-Switch condition,One of three experimental conditions explicitly named in Methods 2.3: Pay-to-Switch.,1,"Switching option and cost structure: can obtain a new partner by paying L$1 to switch after a round (direct monetary cost), versus other conditions where switching is impossible or involves an opportunity cost.","Only factor varied between conditions is whether withdrawing leads to a new partner and the form of its cost; here, leaving current partner for new partner costs L$1.",1,True,Human participants from University of Guelph played the game.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to conditions and pre-specified payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory at University of Guelph with computers and partitions.,1,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely mathematical or analytical model presented as a separate study.,1,2,Iterated two-person Prisoner’s Dilemma; participants paired in dyads each round.,1,40,Methods 2.2.1: participants played for 40 rounds (unknown to them).,1,1,"Each round is a binary choice (cooperate/defect), so there is no continuous contribution amount; coded as 1 since players can choose either action freely rather than being forced into a fixed pattern.",0.6,0,Endowment is held privately; cooperation/defection yields payoffs but there is no pre-assigned portion to a public pool.,0.6,N/R,Standard 2x2 PD with fixed payoffs; no linear public good multiplier or MPCR defined.,1,0,Methods 2.1: communication was not permitted.,1,1,"After each round, players saw partner’s decision and both players’ earnings; this is summary information about the partner.",1,0,"Interactions were anonymous without identities or codes; players only knew what their current partner did, not who they were more generally.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,0,Number of rounds (40) was unknown to participants; no indication remaining rounds were displayed.,1,1,Participants could pay L$1 to reduce partner’s earnings by L$3.,1,1,Punisher spends L$1 per punishment action.,1,3,Each L$1 spent makes the partner lose L$3.,1,0,Only a punishment option is described; no paying to increase partner’s payoff.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Methods 2.2.1: participants started with initial endowment of L$20.,1,0.7,Results: individuals in the Pay-to-Switch condition cooperated on 0.70 ± 0.04 of rounds.,1,N/R,Only proportions of cooperative choices are reported; no per-round token amount is defined beyond binary actions.,0.9,N/R,No aggregate payoff relative to maximum cooperation is reported or derivable by condition.,0.3,N/R,Average earnings per condition are not broken out; only overall average earnings across participants are given.,0.3,Canada,"Participants were recruited at University of Guelph in Ontario, Canada.",1,"Mean 21.6 years (SD 5.0, range 17–48)",Methods 2.1 reports these statistics for the whole sample; no differences by condition mentioned.,1,"63 males, 93 females in total sample; condition-wise breakdown not reported.",Methods 2.1 provides totals only.,0.8,University students (recruited from University of Guelph),Recruitment from a university student population is stated; no further stratification.,0.9,On site,Lab experiment with physical partitions and envelopes on desks implies in-person sessions.,1,"Two-player repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with 40 rounds; per-round payoffs: if both cooperate each gets L$3; if one defects and other cooperates, defector gets L$5 and cooperator L$0; if both defect each gets L$1. After observing actions and payoffs, players can (a) pay L$1 to punish partner (partner loses L$3) and (b) decide whether to withdraw. In Pay-to-Switch, withdrawing costs L$1 and yields a new partner next active round; if either member withdraws, pair is broken and only leavers pay L$1. Sessions have 12 participants (6 pairs). Computer also creates occasional exogenous splits to ensure availability of alternative partners; players are anonymous and cannot identify partners across rounds. Number of rounds unknown to participants.","Summary of procedural details in Methods 2.2, 2.3, and 2.4 that describe the game structure.",1
10.1016_j.socec.2023.102050.md,Sit-Out-to-Switch condition,Second experimental condition named in Methods 2.3: Sit-Out-to-Switch.,1,"Switching option and cost structure: can obtain a new partner by choosing to sit out one round and earn L$0 (opportunity cost), versus other conditions where switching is impossible or costs L$1 directly.",This condition uniquely uses an opportunity cost (foregone payoff) as the cost of switching to a new partner.,1,True,Same human-subject laboratory experiment; this is a treatment arm.,1,True,Randomly assigned controlled experimental condition.,1,True,Conducted under same lab protocol with z-tree and partitions.,1,False,No separate simulation study.,1,False,No analytical-only treatment.,1,2,Same two-person iterated PD structure as other conditions.,1,40,All participants played 40 rounds (unknown to them).,1,1,"Binary cooperate/defect choice each round, same as other conditions.",0.6,0,No pre-committed contribution to a common pool; players act from private endowment.,0.6,N/R,Prisoner’s Dilemma payoffs; no MPCR specified.,1,0,Communication not permitted across all conditions.,1,1,Players see partner’s choice and round earnings.,1,0,Partners are anonymous; no stable identity beyond current pairing.,0.9,0,No reward option exists.,1,0,Total rounds not revealed.,1,1,Punishment option identical across conditions.,1,1,Punisher pays L$1.,1,3,Partner loses L$3 per L$1 spent.,1,0,No mention of any rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,Reward does not exist.,1,N/A,Reward does not exist.,1,20,Same initial endowment for all participants.,1,0.76,Results: individuals in the Sit-Out-to-Switch condition cooperated on 0.76 ± 0.04 of rounds.,1,N/R,Only proportions of cooperative choices given.,0.9,N/R,No condition-level total payoff vs. maximum is reported.,0.3,N/R,Average earnings not split by condition.,0.3,Canada,Same university population; no condition differences reported.,1,"Mean 21.6 years (SD 5.0, range 17–48)",Aggregated sample description used for all conditions.,1,"63 males, 93 females in total sample; condition-wise breakdown not reported.",Gender split only provided at overall level.,0.8,University students (recruited from University of Guelph),Same recruitment source for all conditions.,0.9,On site,Same in-person lab environment as other conditions.,1,"Same 40-round two-player repeated PD with punishment as in other conditions. Difference lies in switching mechanism: participants can choose to ‘withdraw’ (sit out) for one round, receiving L$0 that round, after which they are paired with a new partner. The opportunity cost is framed as missing the likely L$1 from mutual defection against a persistent defector. If either member withdraws, both sit out that round before re-pairing. Computer also executes exogenous pair splits to ensure pool of available partners.",Methods 2.3B and 2.4 describe sit-out procedure and exogenous splits.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2023.102050.md,No-Switching condition,Third experimental condition named in Methods 2.3: No-Switching.,1,Switching option: cannot obtain a new partner by withdrawing; withdrawing causes sitting out one round but leads to re-pairing with the same partner. Opportunity cost is same as Sit-Out-to-Switch but without partner change.,This condition differs only in whether withdrawing leads to a new partner (here it does not).,1,True,Same participant pool; this is a third treatment arm.,1,True,Part of the controlled experimental manipulation.,1,True,"Conducted in the same lab sessions, with different sessions assigned to this condition.",1,False,No simulation-only data.,1,False,No standalone analytical model as a separate experiment.,1,2,"Still a two-person PD, but partners are fixed unless randomly switched by computer.",1,40,Same number of rounds for all conditions.,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect choice each round.,0.6,0,No shared account; all payoffs derived from private endowment play.,0.6,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR parameter.,1,0,Communication prohibited universally.,1,1,Players observe partner’s choice and payoffs each round.,1,0,No partner identifiers beyond current interaction; anonymity maintained.,0.9,0,No reward option exists.,1,0,Total number of rounds undisclosed to subjects.,1,1,Punishment decision present in all conditions.,1,1,Punisher pays L$1.,1,3,Partner loses L$3 per L$1 spent.,1,0,No rewarding in design.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,N/A,Reward mechanism absent.,1,20,Same starting endowment of L$20.,1,0.63,Results: individuals in the No-Switching condition cooperated on 0.63 ± 0.04 of rounds.,1,N/R,Only cooperation proportions reported.,0.9,N/R,No explicit efficiency calculations by condition.,0.3,N/R,Payoffs by condition not provided.,0.3,Canada,Same Canadian university sample.,1,"Mean 21.6 years (SD 5.0, range 17–48)",Same participants characteristics apply across conditions.,1,"63 males, 93 females in total sample; condition-wise breakdown not reported.",No gender allocation by treatment reported.,0.8,University students (recruited from University of Guelph),Same recruitment frame.,0.9,On site,Same in-person laboratory setting.,1,"Game structure identical to other conditions (40-round repeated 2-player PD with punishment option). Distinctive feature: participants may choose to ‘withdraw’ (sit out) and earn L$0 for one round, but are then re-paired with the same partner, not a new one. If either member withdraws, both sit out that round. To keep expected partner changes similar to other conditions, the program randomly splits and recombines two pairs every two rounds, leading to partner change in 16.7% of rounds, but this change is exogenous and not under players’ control.",Methods 2.3C and 2.4 describe withdrawing with same-partner re-pairing and scheduled exogenous pair swapping.,1
