10.1016_j.physa.2023.129282.md,Simulation – Baseline donor-recipient game with punishment and migration,"Single evolutionary simulation model described: lattice donor-recipient (PD) game with four strategies, punishment, and weighted migration; all figures vary parameters within this same baseline setup.",0.9,"Dilemma strength r; population density ρ; migration weight τ; punishment fine β; punishment cost γ; presence vs absence of migration (ρ=1 vs ρ<1). This condition represents the general mobile-population model with parameters typically fixed at l=100, α=2 (migration sensitivity), β=0.3, γ=0.1, ρ variable, τ variable and r variable as explored in Figs. 1–5.","Paper describes one model and explores outcomes as a function of r, ρ, τ, β, γ; no distinct treatment labels beyond parameter sweeps, so list the independent variables and note typical baseline parameterization.",0.86,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of agents on a lattice; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human subjects; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory human-subject setup; purely simulation.,1,True,Authors explicitly mention simulations on a lattice network and Monte Carlo time steps.,1,False,"Although a payoff matrix and functions are defined, results are obtained via numerical simulation, not closed-form analysis.",0.9,10000,"Lattice size N = l × l with l = 100, so N = 100 × 100 = 10000 sites; population size n = ρN varies, but the maximum number of players (sites) is 10000.",0.9,50000,"They state: “we typically run more than 50,000 Monte Carlo time steps … and obtain the fraction … by averaging the last 3000 steps.” Use 50000 as the reported lower bound for time steps.",0.7,1,"Strategies are discrete (C, D, CP, DP); there is no continuous contribution choice. All-or-nothing contribution concept for PG games does not directly apply, but mapped here as 1 (players choose a fixed action rather than a divisible contribution).",0.4,N/A,"No human subjects and no endowment–contribution decision; agents choose strategies, not contribution levels.",1,N/R,"This is a pairwise donor–recipient (PD) game, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR is defined or derivable in the usual sense.",0.2,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,0,Model specifies only local payoff-based imitation and utility-based migration; no display of summaries or earnings information is described.,0.9,N/A,No identities or information revelation are modeled; punishment is purely payoff-based in simulations without agents ‘seeing’ IDs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,Agents do not have information about total time steps; dynamics are Markovian without round counter awareness.,0.9,1,"Second stage allows punishment with cost γ and fine β; four strategies include punishers (CP, DP).",1,0.1,"Baseline simulations: “we set the punishment cost γ at 0.1” and also “Other parameter are set to γ = 0.1, β = 0.3, τ = 0.5” etc. Although γ is varied in Fig. 5, the primary baseline is γ = 0.1.",0.8,3,"Punishment efficiency is measured by β/γ. Baseline: β = 0.3, γ = 0.1, so β/γ = 3, meaning each unit cost imposes three units of fine.",0.9,0,Model includes only punishment; no positive rewards are mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Donor-recipient PD specified via costs and benefits (c, b, r) but no numerical per-round endowment variable is defined.",0.2,N/R,"Outcomes reported as fractions of strategies (C, D, CP, DP), not normalized contribution rates.",0.2,N/R,No explicit token/point contributions are modeled beyond fixed cost c; no aggregate contribution amount is reported.,0.2,N/R,Paper reports fractions of strategies and qualitative cooperation levels; no efficiency measure (payoff relative to maximum) is provided.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate payoff or average payoff statistics are reported; only strategy frequencies.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely computational with agent-based simulations.,1,"Pairwise donor-recipient Prisoner’s Dilemma on a 100×100 lattice with periodic boundaries and von Neumann neighborhood; four strategies (C, D, CP, DP) play a two-stage game with punishment (cost γ, fine β). Strategy updating by imitate-the-best among neighbors; migration on empty sites driven by weighted utility U = τI + (1−τ)L with sensitivity α = 2 and population density ρ = n/N.",Summarizes key structural details of the evolutionary game and migration process explicitly described in the model section.,0.98
