10.1016_j.socec.2023.102091.md,Complete punishment network,One of four treatments; label taken from Section 2.1 and Table 1.,1,"Punishment network structure = complete (each subject can punish 3 others: 1 same-MPCR, 2 different-MPCR)",Independent variable is the punishment network; here the complete network as described in Section 2.1.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with 192 human participants.,1,True,Controlled randomized laboratory design with treatments and monetary incentives.,1,True,"Conducted at National Taipei University lab using zTree, with on-site sessions.",1,False,No computer-only agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model as standalone study; focus is on experimental data.,1,4,Four-player linear public good game (Section 2.1).,1,15,Two-stage game repeated over 15 periods (Section 2.2).,1,1,Subjects allocate 0–20 tokens any way between private and group account; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Endowment of 20 tokens given to private account and then allocated; nothing starts in group account.,0.9,0.25,"MPCR_i is 0.3 for two players (A,B) and 0.7 for two players (C,D). Group return from 1 token to group is 0.3*2+0.7*2=2 tokens, divided among 4 players gives MPCR=2/4=0.5 at group level, but marginal per capita return for each individual is their own MPCR. Requested field is MPCR of public good for a representative player; average of heterogeneous MPCRs is (0.3+0.3+0.7+0.7)/4=0.5. However, MPCR is normally defined as multiplier/group size; multiplier here 2, group size 4, so MPCR_group=0.5. Using 0.5.",0.6,0,No communication or chat mentioned; only observation and punishment decisions.,0.9,0,"Subjects see others’ allocations and total punishments they assign/receive, but not summaries of others’ earnings or punishments beyond direct connections; this is not a general summary display.",0.6,1,Punishment is directed to specific connected group members whose contributions and MPCR are shown; implies identification of w