10.1016_j.anbehav.2023.08.014.md,"Study 2 – Humans – Choice condition (Player 2 had Option A vs Option B, actually chose Option B)",Between-subjects condition where Player 2 had fair (A) vs selfish (B) options; focal incentivized case where Player 2 selects B and participant chooses punishment.,1,Partner’s available options and actual choice: Player 2 faced Option A (split $0.50/$0.50 and allow Player 1 to play with Player 3) vs Option B ($1/$0 and prevent Player 1’s future play) and is described as choosing Option B.,"In choice condition, Player 2 had A vs B; participants first asked how much to punish if Player 2 selected Option B.",1,True,Study uses human participants recruited via Amazon Mechanical Turk.,1,True,Random assignment to choice vs no-choice conditions with controlled payoff structures.,1,False,"Experiment is run online via MTurk, not in a physical lab.",1,False,No computational/model-only conditions.,1,False,No formal analytical model; only regression analyses.,1,3,"Game includes Player 1 (participant), Player 2 (partner), and Player 3 (client analog).",1,1,Core decision is a one-shot punishment decision about Player 2’s prior choice; no repeated rounds with feedback.,0.9,1,Punisher can spend any amount from $0.00 to $0.10 in $0.01 increments; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,Player 1 receives $0.10 in their private account and chooses how much to spend to punish; initial endowment entirely private.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods contribution; punishment game with fixed mapping of cost to fine.,1,0,No communication between players is mentioned.,1,0,Participants are told about Player 2’s decision and payoff structure but not provided session-wide summaries or histories.,1,0,"Players are anonymous (MTurk, ex-post random matching) and only know role labels, not identities.",1,0,No explicit reward stage; only punishment.,1,0,No indication participants were told the total number of economic decisions as 'rounds'; single scenario.,0.8,1,Participants can pay to reduce Player 2’s payoff.,1,0.01,Each $0.01 spent reduces Player 2’s payoff by $0.05; $0.01 is the cost unit to punisher.,1,5,"Text: each $0.01 invested reduces Player 2's endowment by $0.05, so 1:5 fine-to-fee ratio.",1,0,Only punishment decisions are elicited; no prosocial reward option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism enabled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism enabled.,1,0.1,Participants (Player 1) are given $0.10 which they can allocate to punishment.,1,N/R,"Key DV is punishment expenditure, not contribution to a public good.",1,N/R,No contribution decisions; only punishment amount is measured.,1,N/R,No efficiency (group payoff / max payoff) metric is reported.,1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs across 3 players under punishment not reported numerically.,1,United States,Screening criteria required participants be located in the United States.,1,20–66 years (mean 36.57),"Participants were 20–66 years old, mean age 36.57.",1,40.09% female; remaining mostly male with small numbers of other/unspecified,Sample: 159 participants (40.09% female); others include male and a few non-binary/unspecified.,0.9,Mixed adult MTurk sample; specific education distribution not reported,Demographic questionnaire included educational attainment but distributions are not given.,0.5,Online,Conducted via Amazon Mechanical Turk/TurkPrime.,1,"Between-subjects design (choice vs no-choice). In this condition, Player 2’s selfish choice (Option B) denies Player 1 a chance to play with Player 3. DV: amount (0–10 cents) spent to punish Player 2. Average punishment against B in choice condition reported as mean=2.95 (on 0–10 cent scale). Participants also rated wrongness (1–7) and responsibility (1–7).",Summarized core structure and key reported statistics for the actual incentivized punishment decision in the choice condition.,1
10.1016_j.anbehav.2023.08.014.md,"Study 2 – Humans – No-choice condition (Player 2 had only Option B vs Option B, actually chose Option B)","Between-subjects no-choice condition, where Player 2’s options are two identical selfish options B; focal incentivized punishment decision.",1,"Partner’s available options: Player 2 faced two identical Option Bs ($1 for self, $0 for Player 3, and Player 1 does not play with Player 3) and is described as choosing Option B.","Design: in the no-choice condition, Player 2 had to choose between two Option Bs; participants asked how much to punish if Player 2 selected Option B.",1,True,Same MTurk participant pool as choice condition.,1,True,Random assignment to no-choice vs choice between-subject conditions.,1,False,Experiment is fully online.,1,False,No nonhuman simulations.,1,False,Empirical regression analyses only.,1,3,"Same 3-player structure (Player 1, 2, 3).",1,1,Single incentivized punishment decision regarding Player 2’s previous choice.,0.9,1,Player 1 can invest any amount 0–10 cents in 1-cent steps.,1,0,All of Player 1’s $0.10 starts in their private balance.,1,N/R,Not a public good setup.,1,0,No communication channels between participants.,1,0,Participants receive scenario description but not cross-subject summaries.,1,0,Role labels only; identities remain anonymous in ex-post matching.,1,0,No reward feature.,1,0,No framing in terms of multiple rounds; single decision context.,0.8,1,Punishment expenditure is main DV.,1,0.01,Same punishment mapping: each $0.01 reduces Player 2’s payoff by $0.05.,1,5,1-cent cost to impose 5-cent fine.,1,0,No mechanism to increase partner’s payoff.,1,N/A,Reward not available.,1,N/A,Reward not available.,1,0.1,Player 1 received $0.10 to allocate to punishment.,1,N/R,No contributions to a public good.,1,N/R,"Only punishment is measured, not contributions.",1,N/R,No reported efficiency metrics.,1,N/R,No aggregated payoff statistics reported.,1,United States,Same screening constraints: participants located in the U.S.,1,20–66 years (mean 36.57),Same sample as choice condition.,1,40.09% female; remaining mostly male with small numbers of other/unspecified,Sample-level gender stats apply to both experimental conditions.,0.9,Mixed adult MTurk sample; specific education distribution not reported,Education queried but not summarized.,0.5,Online,Run on MTurk via TurkPrime.,1,No-choice baseline of intentionality: Player 2 ostensibly has no fair alternative. DV: cents (0–10) spent to punish Player 2 for choosing B. Regression results show somewhat lower punishment than in choice condition; wrongness and responsibility ratings are lower.,Summarized from design and reported regression results comparing conditions.,1
10.1017_ehs.2024.37.md,"Model 1 – Injunctive norms only (121 (v_x, v_y) combinations)","Model 1 is explicitly defined in Table 2 as the case with only injunctive norm psychology α_i; simulations are run over all combinations of injunctive norm values v_x, v_y ∈ {0.0, 0.1, …, 1.0}. All such parameter combinations share the same structural setup, differing only in the normative parameters.",1,"Injunctive normative values for cooperation and punishment: v_x ∈ {0.0, 0.1, …, 1.0}, v_y ∈ {0.0, 0.1, …, 1.0}","The Results section states they vary v_x and v_y from 0 to 1 in steps of 0.1, producing 121 combinations of injunctive norms for Model 1.",1,False,"The study is an agent-based model with simulated agents, no human participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment with human or field data; purely computational simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; simulations only.,1,True,Described explicitly as an agent-based model and computer simulations.,1,False,"While they provide equations, the core results come from numerical simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions.",1,16,Table 1 specifies group size n = 16.,1,40,They state that each individual participates in collective actions over 40 rounds (Q = 40).,1,0,"Cooperation x and punishment y are binary (0 or 1), but these are two distinct decisions; the binary nature is about participating or not, not a strict all-or-nothing contribution level within a continuous budgeted endowment. The paper does not define an endowment or partial contribution scale, so this field is not directly applicable but required to be numeric; 0 used with explanation.",0.2,N/A,There is no explicit monetary endowment or default allocation between private and public accounts; agents choose binary cooperation x.,1,N/R,"They describe a generic social dilemma payoff function π_CA and reference SI for details, but do not report a specific marginal per capita return in the main text.",0.1,N/A,No human subjects or communication; simulated best-response dynamics only.,1,0,Agents update based on others’ last-round behaviours and payoffs algorithmically; there is no user interface or feedback design as in human experiments. Field kept as 0 with this explanation.,0.3,N/A,No identities or UI; only anonymous group-level interactions in simulation.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism at all.,1,0,Simulated agents are not described as observing the horizon; horizon is fixed in code but not part of agent information.,0.3,1,Punishment y is one of the two prosocial behaviours agents can choose; punishers harm all defectors in the group.,1,N/R,"They note that punishment is costly and harms defectors, but the specific cost per unit is only given in the SI, not in the provided text.",0.1,N/R,"The magnitude of harm per unit of punishment is only referenced to SI Text S1.3, not detailed here.",0.1,0,Only cooperation and punishment are modeled; no mention of a positive reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,The payoff structure is given in general form; there is no explicit per-round endowment described in tokens or points.,0.1,N/R,They report cooperation rate x (fraction of cooperators) but do not normalise relative to a defined endowment; no explicit numeric steady-state value is provided for the whole parameter grid.,0.2,N/R,"Contributions are binary (cooperate vs defect) and reported as frequencies, not as amounts of a token endowment.",0.2,N/R,They describe group fitness and payoffs but do not report efficiency as the ratio of achieved to maximal possible payoff.,0.1,N/R,"Group fitness w_j is based on cumulative number of cooperators, but no specific numeric group payoff values are reported.",0.1,N/A,No human participants; agents in simulations have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computer simulations only.,1,"Agent-based public-goods-type social dilemma with binary cooperation (x ∈ {0,1}) and punishment (y ∈ {0,1}); groups of 16 agents play 40 rounds; payoffs π_CA depend on number of cooperators; punishment harms defectors.",Summarised from Sections 2.1–2.3 describing the collective action and punishment structure.,1
10.1017_ehs.2024.37.md,"Model 2 – Injunctive and descriptive norms, non-exapted conformity (121 (v_x, v_y) combinations)","Model 2 in Table 2 includes both injunctive norm psychology α_i and descriptive norm psychology α_d, with α_d initially drawn from U(0, 0.05). The Results describe running it over the same 121 (v_x, v_y) combinations.",1,"Injunctive normative values for cooperation and punishment: v_x ∈ {0.0, 0.1, …, 1.0}, v_y ∈ {0.0, 0.1, …, 1.0}; norm psychologies α_i and α_d both evolvable from small initial values (α_i, α_d ∼ U(0.00, 0.05))","Table 2 defines Model 2 assumptions (α_i and α_d present, both initialized from U(0,0.05)). Section 3 states simulations with v_x, v_y varied 0 to 1 by 0.1, as in Model 1.",1,False,Agent-based computational model with no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical or field experiment.,1,False,No lab environment; simulations only.,1,True,Model 2 is an extension of the agent-based simulations of Model 1.,1,False,"Key results come from numerical simulations, not closed-form mathematics.",1,16,Same group size n = 16 as in Table 1; no change for Model 2.,1,40,Same 40 rounds of collective action per lifetime as described for the base model.,1,0,"As in Model 1, agents choose binary cooperation and punishment decisions; no explicit all-or-nothing contribution of a continuous endowment is modelled. 0 is used with explanatory note.",0.2,N/A,No explicit monetary endowment; binary x decision instead.,1,N/R,MPCR not numerically specified in the provided text; payoff function referenced to SI.,0.1,N/A,No human communication; purely algorithmic updates.,1,0,"No user interface; agents respond to observed frequencies of others’ behaviours, not to summaries in an experimental sense.",0.3,N/A,No identities in a human sense; anonymity assumed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Horizon is part of simulation code but not described as information available to agents.,0.3,1,Punishment y is modeled identically as in Model 1.,1,N/R,Specific cost values are deferred to SI and not present in the excerpt.,0.1,N/R,Magnitude of punishment effect not numerically specified in main text.,0.1,0,No rewards; only punishment as a sanction.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"No numeric per-round endowment defined; only abstract payoffs π(x,y).",0.1,N/R,They report evolution of cooperation x qualitatively and with heatmaps but do not give a single numeric summary per condition required here; full grid values not listed.,0.3,N/R,"Binary cooperation choice, with results given as frequencies, not contribution amounts from a numerical endowment.",0.3,N/R,Group-level efficiency is discussed conceptually but not quantified as payoff relative to a maximum.,0.1,N/R,They define group fitness w_j but do not report detailed payoff numbers across conditions.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Same public-goods-type social dilemma with binary cooperation (x) and punishment (y) as Model 1, extended to include descriptive norms (frequencies of x and y in the group) and an additional genetic trait α_d that weights conformity in the utility function.",Summarised from Sections 2.1–2.3 and Table 2 on the inclusion of descriptive norms and α_d.,1
10.1017_ehs.2024.37.md,"Model 3 – Injunctive and descriptive norms with exapted conformity (121 (v_x, v_y) combinations)","Model 3 in Table 2 is defined as including both α_i and α_d, with exapted conformity implemented via a higher initial distribution of α_d ~ N(0.30, 0.25^2). Results section describes running it across the same injunctive norm grid.",1,"Injunctive normative values for cooperation and punishment: v_x ∈ {0.0, 0.1, …, 1.0}, v_y ∈ {0.0, 0.1, …, 1.0}; norm psychologies α_i and α_d evolvable, with α_i ∼ U(0.00, 0.05), α_d ∼ N(0.30, 0.25^2) (exapted conformity)",Table 2 provides initial distributions for α_i and α_d in Model 3; Section 3.2 confirms that v_x and v_y are varied as in Models 1 and 2.,1,False,Computational agent-based simulations; no human data.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; it is a simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory environment.,1,True,Described as simulations of a gene–culture coevolutionary agent-based model.,1,False,"Effects explored via simulations, not purely analytical derivations.",1,16,Group size n = 16 for all models (Table 1).,1,40,"Collective actions over 40 rounds per lifetime in the generic model, unchanged for Model 3.",1,0,"Decision is binary whether to cooperate, but no explicit continuous budget with all-or-nothing constraint; 0 used with explanation.",0.2,N/A,No explicit endowment or default split between private and public funds.,1,N/R,Exact marginal per capita return is relegated to SI; not provided in text.,0.1,N/A,No communication channel; agents update via myopic optimisation on observed behaviours.,1,0,No explicit design where agents are shown aggregate outcomes; they simply observe previous-round frequencies algorithmically.,0.3,N/A,No concept of revealed identities; interactions are at group level.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,0,Finite horizon is coded but not described as information given to agents.,0.3,1,Punishment behaviour y is an explicit choice for agents as in earlier models.,1,N/R,"Numerical cost parameters only in SI, not in main text.",0.1,N/R,Magnitude of sanctions not numerically specified here.,0.1,0,"Model contains no rewarding behaviour, only punishment.",1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No explicit token endowment given; payoffs defined abstractly.,0.1,N/R,"Various specific cooperation rates (x) are reported for some parameter points (e.g., x ≈ 0.60 for v_x=v_y=0; x>0.7 for v_x=1.0,v_y=0.5), but a full table across 121 combinations is not provided.",0.4,N/R,Binary cooperation; no numeric contribution amount from an explicit endowment.,0.3,N/R,Authors discuss success of groups and fitness but do not provide an efficiency ratio.,0.1,N/R,"Group fitness defined via number of cooperators, but actual payoff values per group not reported.",0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Entirely computational agent-based simulations.,1,"Same binary cooperation–punishment social dilemma as in Models 1–2, but starting with higher descriptive norm psychology α_d (mean 0.30) to model exapted conformity. Genetic traits α_i and α_d evolve under multilevel selection (group-level via cumulative cooperators over 40 rounds; individual-level via payoffs). Extensive clustering analysis reveals two emergent societal types: punishment-based cooperative societies (high y, low α_d) and conformity-based cooperative societies (high α_d, low y), with macro transitions between them.","Synthesised from Sections 2.3 and 3.2–3.3, including clustering and dynamic analysis (Figures 3–6).",1
