10.1016_j.joep.2023.102675.md,Cathodal tDCS – No-cost TP-PD task,One of three between-subject stimulation conditions; no-cost is one of two within-subject tasks.,1,"tDCS condition = cathodal; task type = no-cost; norm violation cases presented: Defector–Cooperator, Defector–Defector, Cooperator–Defector",Condition defined by stimulation polarity and within-subject task; norm violation types are scenarios within the task.,1,True,Human undergraduate participants.,1,True,Random assignment to stimulation conditions with controlled game tasks.,1,True,Conducted in Reinhard Selten Lab at Nankai University using z-tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation described.,1,False,No formal analytical model as a standalone study condition.,1,3,"Groups consist of Players A, B, and C (third party).",1,2,Each TP-PD task included two rounds.,1,1,"Punishment points can vary continuously from 0 up to 20, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,PD with transfer decision; endowment starts fully in private account for A and B; no pre-placed public fund.,0.9,N/A,"Prisoner’s dilemma with discrete transfer and tripling, not a linear public goods game with well-defined MPCR.",1,0,No communication or chat between players mentioned; assistants predetermined A and B actions.,0.9,1,"Player C observes A’s and B’s actions and resulting payoffs before assigning punishment, implying payoff information is displayed.",0.8,0,"No indication that player identities are revealed; players are generic A, B, C and group members differ each round.",0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,1,"Lab experiments with fixed two rounds and counterbalanced order; standard z-tree implementation typically shows stage/round, but not explicitly stated.",0.5,1,Third-party punishment (deduction points) is the core of the task.,1,0,"In the no-cost task, C did not bear any cost for deduction decisions.",1,3,"For every deduction point assigned, 3 points are deducted from the punished player’s payoff.",1,0,Only punishment (deductions) is described; no possibility to increase others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,40,Player C receives an endowment of 40 points in the second stage.,1,N/R,Outcome is punishment and emotional response; no contribution variable for Player C.,1,N/R,No contribution decision by third party; A and B’s transfers are not the dependent variable of interest.,1,N/R,No efficiency or surplus ratio reported.,1,N/R,Only per-point monetary conversion and mean payoff for C overall are reported; no condition-specific group payoff.,0.6,China,"Participants were undergraduates from Nankai University, China.",1,"Mean 20 years, range 18–23",Explicitly reported in Subjects section.,1,54.44% female,Explicitly reported proportion of female subjects.,1,Undergraduate students,All subjects recruited were healthy undergraduate students.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical lab (Reinhard Selten Lab) with tDCS equipment and z-tree.,1,"3-player PD with third-party punishment; Stage 1: A and B choose keep 10 or transfer 10 (tripled). Stage 2: C observes actions and assigns up to 20 deduction points per player, each deducting 3 points from the target and costing 0 in this no-cost task. Two rounds with predetermined A/B actions (Defector–Defector and Defector–Cooperator/Cooperator–Defector combinations). C also reports emotional response on a 5-point scale (−2 to 2).",Summary of the task rules as described for the no-cost TP-PD task.,1
10.1016_j.joep.2023.102675.md,Sham tDCS – No-cost TP-PD task,Sham is the baseline between-subject stimulation condition; combined with no-cost within-subject task.,1,"tDCS condition = sham; task type = no-cost; norm violation cases presented: Defector–Cooperator, Defector–Defector, Cooperator–Defector",Condition defined by zero effective stimulation and no-cost task.,1,True,Human participants in lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to sham vs active tDCS.,1,True,Performed in Reinhard Selten Lab with z-tree software.,1,False,Purely behavioral and neuromodulation experiment.,1,False,No separate analytical model as a condition.,1,3,Same TP-PD task structure across all stimulation conditions.,1,2,Each TP-PD task includes two rounds.,1,1,Continuous choice of deduction points 0–20; not binary.,0.9,0,Same PD transfer setup as in other conditions.,0.9,N/A,Not a linear public good.,1,0,No communication described.,0.9,1,C observes A and B’s actions and payoffs before punishing.,0.8,0,"No identity revelation beyond labels A, B; groups change each round.",0.7,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Likely known fixed two rounds; not explicitly reported.,0.5,1,Third-party punishment decision in second stage.,1,0,No-cost task: C does not pay for deductions.,1,3,Each deduction point reduces target payoff by 3 points.,1,0,No way to increase A or B payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,40,C’s endowment is 40 points in stage 2.,1,N/R,No contribution variable as DV for C.,1,N/R,"Study reports punishment levels (TPP), not transfers as DV.",1,N/R,No efficiency outcomes reported.,1,N/R,No condition-specific group payoffs reported; only average C payoff overall.,0.6,China,Undergraduates from Chinese university.,1,"Mean 20 years, range 18–23",Same subject pool across all tDCS conditions.,1,54.44% female,Reported for the full sample.,1,Undergraduate students,Described recruitment.,1,On site,Physical lab with tDCS and z-tree.,1,"Same 3-player PD with third-party punishment as in c