10.1016_j.jtbi.2025.112043.md,"Analytical model – CD norms (cooperate with good, defect against bad)","Paper analytically studies separate classes of social norms; CD norms with S(G)=C, S(B)=D are treated as one main condition.",0.9,"Social norm class with punishment option but prescribed actions C vs good (G) and D vs bad (B); assessment rule parameters R(X,A); environmental parameters {b, c, α, β, q, ξ, μ}.","Within the analytical model, the main varying factors for this condition are the CD action rule and corresponding assessment rules plus environment parameters.",0.9,False,The study is purely theoretical; no human participants are used.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only mathematical analysis.,1,False,No lab or field implementation; only theory.,1,False,Main text presents analytical derivations; numerical confirmation is mentioned but no explicit simulation design is described.,0.7,True,Model is defined by equations and ESS conditions are derived mathematically.,1,2,Each interaction is a pairwise donation game between one donor and one recipient.,1,0,"Population interacts in an infinitely repeated, randomly paired process; no fixed finite number of rounds is defined.",0.7,1,"Donor’s choice is among discrete actions C, D, or P; there is no notion of a continuous contribution level typical of public goods games. Marked as 1 to indicate non-all-or-nothing contributions are not applicable in this binary-action donation-game framework.",0.4,N/A,No human players and no divisible endowment or contribution are modeled.,1,N/A,This is not a public goods game; marginal per capita return is not defined.,1,0,No communication between players is modeled.,1,0,Model only specifies that reputations are public; no payoff or punishment summary displays are modeled.,0.9,0,Identity revelation is not modeled; only reputation states G/B are public.,0.9,0,No explicit rewarding action distinct from cooperation and punishment is modeled.,1,0,Infinite ongoing interactions; no explicit information about total or remaining rounds.,1,1,"Punishment action P is part of the action set {C,D,P} and has explicit costs/benefits.",1,N/R,"Cost of punishment denoted α>0, but no specific numeric value is given.",1,N/R,"Loss imposed on recipient is β>0, but no specific numeric magnitude is provided.",1,0,"Model has benefit b from cooperation, but no separate costly rewarding action is defined.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Donation game is defined in cost-benefit terms (c, b) without an explicit per-round endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Outcomes are cooperation levels (fraction of good/cooperators) and payoffs, not normalized contributions from an endowment.",0.7,N/R,Only binary decision to cooperate (pay cost c) vs defect/punish is modeled; no token amount is specified.,0.8,N/R,"Paper compares payoffs across norms analytically (π_CD, etc.) but does not report normalized efficiency as defined in the instructions.",0.7,N/R,"Only per-player average payoffs are given (e.g., π_CD); no aggregate group payoff for a finite group is specified.",0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical analytical model with infinitely large population; no empirical environment.,1,"Indirect reciprocity model with infinitely large well-mixed population playing pairwise donation games with binary reputation (G/B). Actions are C, D, or P; cooperation costs c and yields benefit b; punishment costs α and inflicts β. Public second-order social norms specify action rule S(X) and assessment rule R(X,A). Two error types: assessment error μ (social resolution q=1−2μ) and detection error where defection is seen as cooperation with probability ε_DC (detectability ξ=1−ε_DC).",Summarizes the formal game structure as described in the Model section.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2025.112043.md,"Analytical model – CP norms (cooperate with good, punish bad)","Paper separately analyzes CP norms with S(G)=C, S(B)=P as another main condition.",0.9,"Social norm class with punishment option and prescribed actions C vs good (G) and P vs bad (B); assessment rule parameters R(X,A); environmental parameters {b, c, α, β, q, ξ, μ}.","This condition is defined by the CP action rule plus assessment rules and environment parameters, contrasted against CD and DD norms.",0.9,False,No human participants are involved; analysis is theoretical.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment is run.,1,False,No lab setting; only equations.,1,False,Main results for CP norms are derived analytically; simulations are not described in detail.,0.7,True,ESS conditions and cooperation levels for CP norms are derived with formal inequalities and equations.,1,2,"As in the CD case, interactions are pairwise donation games between donor and recipient.",1,0,Infinite repeated random matching; no fixed number of rounds.,0.7,1,"Action space is discrete {C,D,P}; there is no scalar contribution level.",0.4,N/A,No endowment-based contribution decision is modeled.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Model has no communication channel.,1,0,Only reputations are public; no summaries of payoffs or punishments.,0.9,0,Identities beyond reputation states are not modeled.,0.9,0,No reward action is present.,1,0,Ongoing evolutionary process without explicit round-count information.,1,1,Punishment P is used strategically against bad recipients and has explicit cost and inflicted loss.,1,N/R,Cost α>0 is specified symbolically but not numerically.,1,N/R,"Loss β>0 on punished recipient is symbolic, no numeric value.",1,0,No separate rewarding action is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game is defined via cost c and benefit b, not via explicit token endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Outcomes are expressed as cooperation level h* and payoffs, not as fraction of a numeric endowment.",0.7,N/R,Model is binary cooperate vs not; no numeric contribution amount is specified.,0.8,N/R,"Although payoffs for CP norms are derived, efficiency as payoff divided by maximum possible is not explicitly reported.",0.7,N/R,Only individual average payoff π_CP is provided; no finite group payoff reported.,0.8,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical model only.,1,"Same indirect reciprocity setup as CD norms, but the resident social norm prescribes S(G)=C and S(B)=P. Being assessed as good is worth (b+β) more than being bad because bad recipients are punished (−β). ESS conditions for CP norms are derived in terms of assessment rules R(X,A) and parameters q, ξ, b, c, α, β; cooperation level h* is 1−μ for the most efficient CP norms listed in Table 3.",Summarizes the specific CP configuration described in Section 3.2 and Table 3.,1
10.1016_j.jtbi.2025.112043.md,Analytical model – DD norms (always defect),DD (ALLD) norms are explicitly treated as ESS and serve as a benchmark condition with zero cooperation.,0.9,"Social norm class with action rule S(G)=D, S(B)=D (ALLD); assessment rules can be arbitrary under DD; environmental parameters {b, c, α, β, q, ξ, μ}.",DD norms are defined by always defecting irrespective of recipient reputation; the paper notes they are always ESS and compares their efficiency.,0.95,False,The DD case is studied analytically within the same theoretical framework.,1,False,No empirical manipulation or data.,1,False,No lab or field data.,1,False,DD payoffs are derived as special analytical case.,0.7,True,Payoff π_DD=0 is derived mathematically as part of analytical comparison.,1,2,Still the same pairwise donation-game structure.,1,0,Infinite repeated random interactions as in the rest of the model.,0.7,1,Only discrete action D is actually chosen; no gradated contributions.,0.4,N/A,No contribution decision from an endowment.,1,N/A,Not a public goods setting.,1,0,No communication modeled.,1,0,Only reputations matter; DD has trivial reputation dynamics but no payoff summary displays.,0.9,0,"In DD, punishment action is not used; identities not modeled.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No finite horizon.,1,1,"Punishment action P exists in the overall model, even if resident DD players choose D; P is available to potential mutants.",0.9,N/R,"Cost α defined at model level, but not numerically specified.",1,N/R,"Magnitude β defined at model level, but not numerically specified.",1,0,No separate rewarding action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Costs and benefits defined without explicit per-round endowment.,0.8,0,"Under ALLD, no one cooperates; cooperation level is zero, corresponding to zero contribution relative to maximal cooperation.",0.95,0,"Defectors never pay cost c, so effective cooperative contribution per interaction is zero.",0.9,N/R,Model reports π_DD=0 but not normalized efficiency as fraction of maximum possible payoff.,0.7,0,"Per-player payoff under DD is π_DD=0; for a two-player interaction, total payoff is also 0.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical benchmark within the same theoretical framework.,1,"Same infinite-population indirect reciprocity setting, but resident action rule is S(G)=D and S(B)=D (ALLD). Any assessment rule entries are allowed, and DD norms are always ESS with payoff π_DD=0 and zero cooperation.","Summarizes DD configuration as described when comparing π_CD, π_CP, and π_DD.",1
10.1016_j.evolhumbehav.2025.106655.md,"Observational analysis of professional men’s soccer fouls 2016–2019 (nine leagues, all conditions pooled)",The paper reports one unified observational dataset of fouls across nine professional leagues over three seasons; there are no experimentally manipulated conditions or subconditions with different rules or incentives.,1,"Cultural background and institutional context measured at foul, player, and country level (e.g., prevalence of revenge motifs in folklore of victim’s or perpetrator’s country; GPS negative reciprocity; country-level views on prison as vengeance; violence motifs; civil war exposure; pastoralism; other cultural indices; whether initial foul or player has yellow card; minute of game; player positions; season; league; referee’s propensity to issue yellow cards).",All variation is naturally occurring; key explanatory variables are cultural indices by country of origin plus in-game and referee characteristics. No treatment assignment is described.,1,True,Data derive from observed behavior of human professional soccer players in real matches.,1,False,No controlled intervention or random assignment; analyses use naturally occurring fouls and sanctions in league play.,1,False,"Behavior is recorded in real-world professional matches, not a laboratory or lab-in-the-field setting.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations are described; only statistical analysis of observed events.,1,False,"While regression and hazard models are used, there is no standalone formal game-theoretic or closed-form analytical model developed as a distinct study.",1,22,Standard professional soccer has 11 players per team on the field; the paper does not state a different number.,0.9,1,Each match is a single continuous interaction (90+ minutes); there is no discrete repeated-game structure defined as multiple rounds.,0.7,1,"Players cannot choose a continuous contribution level; the notion of ‘contribution’ as in public goods does not apply, but if mapped, actions (foul/non-foul, retaliate/non-retaliate) are discrete rather than constrained to all-or-nothing contributions. Coding as 1 to reflect non-all-or-nothing contribution not being enforced is somewhat arbitrary.",0.2,N/R,The study is not a contribution game; no endowment allocation between private and public accounts is defined.,1,N/R,No public-goods or linear-benefit structure is specified; this is not a public goods or similar contribution game.,1,0,"Players may communicate verbally in normal play, but there is no structured experimental chat or communication channel defined as part of a game protocol.",0.8,0,"Players in real matches do not receive formalized numerical summaries of others’ payoffs, punishments, or rewards as part of an experimental interface.",0.9,1,Retaliation and referee cards are observed on the field; identities of fouler and victim are publicly visible to all players.,1,0,"There is no formal reward mechanism, only fouls and sanctions such as yellow/red cards and free kicks.",1,1,"Players know match duration (90 minutes plus stoppage time), and the time (minute) of each foul is central to the analysis; effectively, remaining time is common knowledge.",0.9,1,Two punishment modes are observed: informal retaliation by victims (fouls in response to previous fouls) and formal sanctions (yellow/red cards) by referees.,1,N/R,The paper does not quantify the payoff cost of committing a retaliatory foul or of receiving a yellow/red card in tokens or money.,1,N/R,"There is no numerical mapping from punishment effort to target’s payoff reduction; effects are in terms of game state and deterrence, not token losses.",1,0,"No structured positive reward mechanism (e.g., costly rewards) is studied; only fouls and penalties.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"No per-player monetary or token endowment is specified; stakes are professional career incentives and match outcomes, not experimental endowments.",1,N/R,"The core outcomes are retaliation incidence, timing, and repeat fouling, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts are defined; only binary or count outcomes of fouls/retaliation.,1,N/R,The study does not define or calculate efficiency as payoff relative to a cooperative benchmark.,1,N/R,"Team or group payoffs (e.g., goals, points, earnings) are not modeled quantitatively as a dependent variable in the reported regressions.",1,"Multi-country (players from 139–140 countries; leagues in England, France, Germany, Italy, Spain, Mexico, USA, Argentina, Brazil)","Section 2.1 notes nine leagues in these countries and 10,928 perpetrators from 139 countries and 11,150 victims from 137 countries.",1,Adult professional male players; exact age distribution not reported (age used as covariate).,"Described as professional men's soccer players; age of victim is included as a covariate, but no summary statistics for age are given in main text.",0.9,Male,"The dataset is explicitly from ""men's soccer leagues"" and notes exclusion of women’s leagues due to data availability.",1,N/R,No information on education levels is provided.,1,Field experiment,"Although not an experiment, among the provided categories this is closest: behavior is observed in a real-world field environment (professional leagues) rather than online, lab, or purely observational without structured rules. However, strictly speaking it is non-experimental observational field data.",0.4,"Observational study of 230,113 foul incidents from 9,531 professional men’s soccer matches (nine top leagues, 2016–2019). Key DVs: whether a foul by player p against opponent q is retaliated later in same game by q against p; whether a given foul is itself a retaliation; time (minutes) until retaliation; probability of repeated fouls by original perpetrator on same victim vs third players; probability that third players foul the victim. Key IVs: country-level prevalence of revenge motifs in folklore (Michalopoulos & Xue 2021) for victim or perpetrator; GPS negative reciprocity (overall, revenge, altruistic); survey-based views on prison as vengeance; violence motifs; civil war exposure; pastoralism; various cultural dimensions (risk taking, long-term orientation, Hofstede dimensions, Inglehart–Welzel axes); referee-level propensity to issue yellow cards. Foul-level covariates: minute of game, season, victim age, field positions of victim and perpetrator (goalkeeper/defender/midfielder/forward and their interactions), league, FIFA ranking of victim’s national team, numerous socioeconomic and geoclimatic country controls. Analyses include linear probability models, logit regressions, Cox proportional hazards for timing of retaliation, and minute-level panels around retaliation events to study deterrent effects of retaliation and yellow cards.","Summarizes game structure, dataset, outcome measures, and main covariates as described in Sections 2–6.",1
10.1038_s41598-025-87322-y.md,"Bottom-up electoral institution model (local electoral punishment/reward, baseline parameters for Fig. 2)",Distinct simulation condition explicitly described with full parameter set in Fig. 2 caption.,1,"Collective risk level r=0.8 under bottom-up electoral local institution with parameters Z=70, N=8, n_pg=6, n_I=2, b=1, c=0.1, δ=2, π_t=0.03, μ=1/Z, β=5",Fig. 2 caption lists these parameter values; r=0.8 is the focal risk value for the depicted attractor and trajectories.,1,False,Paper describes a stochastic evolutionary game-theoretic model and computer simulations; no human participants.,1,False,No controlled lab or field experiment; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only simulations.,1,True,"Model implemented via computer simulations and Markov process numerics (e.g., stationary distribution, trajectories).",1,True,"Includes explicit analytical model and derivations of payoffs, transition probabilities, and stationary distribution, though results visualized via simulation.",0.9,70,Population size Z=70 given in Fig. 2 caption.,1,1,Evolution is modeled as continuous Markovian time steps rather than discrete repeated game rounds; effectively a single-stage game repeatedly sampled.,0.4,1,Each player either contributes a fixed fraction c of endowment or nothing depending on strategy; contributions are not restricted to all-or-nothing choice in terms of tokens but strategy-based; treated as flexible contribution rule.,0.5,0,Endowment b is initially fully in private possession; pro-social strategies then decide to contribute fraction c to the CRD.,0.8,N/R,"No explicit multiplier of the public good relative to group size is given; CRD here is threshold with risk, not linear MPCR.",0,N/A,No human subjects; communication not applicable.,1,0,No notion of participants observing others’ summaries in the simulation model.,1,N/A,No identities or information structure for human subjects; not applicable to simulation agents.,1,N/A,Same as punishment; no individual identity revelation modeled.,1,0,No explicit display of total or remaining rounds in a simulation with no human subjects.,1,1,Electoral institution can allocate Δ_RP to punish defectors depending on majority of punishers; punishment is an explicit mechanism.,1,N/R,"Tax π_t funds a pool that can be converted into punishment, but marginal cost per punished unit to punishers vs effect on defectors is not given as a simple per-unit parameter.",0.2,N/R,Model specifies total amount Δ_RP redistributed or subtracted but not as a standardized per-unit punishment technology parameter.,0.2,1,Electoral institution can allocate Δ_RP to reward pro-social strategies in majority rewarder or tie cases.,1,N/R,"Again, only aggregate Δ_RP and tax π_t are specified; per-unit cost to reward per recipient is not separately parameterized.",0.2,N/R,"Total reward divided among pro-social individuals, but no explicit technology ratio per taxed unit to rewarded payoff is provided beyond δ multiplier; does not map directly to requested per-coin-tech parameter.",0.3,1,Initial endowment b=1 given in Fig. 2 caption.,1,N/R,Paper reports composition at cooperative attractor but not explicit average contribution fraction across population normalized by endowment.,0,N/R,No numeric average contribution amount per player or group is reported for this parameter set.,0,N/R,"They define group achievement η_G elsewhere, but do not provide its numeric value for r=0.8 in Fig. 2.",0,N/R,No explicit payoff values for groups are reported for the depicted cooperative attractor.,0,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation model.,1,"Collective Risk Dilemma with threshold n_pg=6 in groups of size N=8; four strategies (C, P, R, D); pro-social strategies contribute fraction c=0.1 of endowment b=1; additional tax π_t=0.03 from P and R funds local electoral institution; institution formed if at least n_I=2 P/R in group; institutional pool multiplied by δ=2 before redistribution; risk r=0.8 of losing endowment if threshold not met; finite well-mixed population of size Z=70; stochastic evolutionary dynamics with mutation μ=1/Z and selection intensity β=5.",Summarized from Methods and Fig. 2 caption parametric description.,1
