10.1038_s41598-025-87322-y.md,"Local electoral institutions (bottom-up, dual reward/punishment) (Fig. 3, blue solid line)",Main new model in paper; in Fig. 3 described as 'Local Electoral institutions' distinct from other three curves.,1,"Presence of local electoral institutions that can implement reward or punishment based on majority of P vs R; risk r varied; η_G≈0.5 reached at r*≈0.640; parameters Z=140, N=8, n_pg=6, n_I=2, b=1, c=0.1, δ=2, π_t=0.03, μ=1/Z, β=2",Results section states r*≈0.640 for this model and that its nature (reward/punishment) is decided via majority voting; Fig. 3 caption gives shared parameters.,1,False,Model-based study without human participants.,1,False,No empirical experimental protocol.,1,False,No lab; computational only.,1,True,η_G vs r curve computed via simulations and stationary distribution for the electoral model.,1,True,Model and transition probabilities are analytically specified; simulations implement this.,0.9,140,Fig. 3 caption uses Z=140 for all conditions including electoral institutions.,1,1,Evolution is ongoing; CRD is a single-stage game within an evolutionary Markov process.,0.4,1,"Prosocial strategies contribute fraction c of b; not strictly all-or-nothing binary at decision level, but fixed rule; treated as flexible contribution.",0.5,0,"Endowment b initially private, then prosocials contribute c·b.",0.8,N/R,Threshold-risk structure without explicit MPCR parameter.,0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,No informational interface modeled.,1,N/A,Punishment is applied by institution without modeled observation/identity features.,1,N/A,Similar for rewards; no identity display.,1,0,Not applicable to simulation agents.,1,1,Electoral institution may choose a sanctioning nature or mixed reward/punish in tie; thus punishment is available.,1,N/R,Tax π_t levied on P and R funds both rewards and punishments; no explicit per-unit punishment cost parameter extracted.,0.2,N/R,"Pool Δ_RP computed via δ, π_t, and j_PR; not summarized as standard per-unit technology metric.",0.3,1,"Institution can also be rewarding or mixed, providing positive incentives.",1,N/R,Same pooled funding via π_t; no independent reward-cost parameter given.,0.2,N/R,Aggregate reward pool definition does not convert to per-unit technology parameter as requested.,0.3,1,b=1 per Methods and Fig. 3 caption.,1,N/R,Paper does not provide explicit contribution shares under electoral institutions; only η_G and qualitative composition at attractor (Fig. 2 for different Z).,0,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts reported.,0,N/R,They discuss improved cooperation and r* thresholds but no explicit efficiency ratio; η_G is plotted but not tabulated.,0,N/R,No raw payoff values for groups under electoral institutions are given.,0,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation environment.,1,"Collective Risk Dilemma in finite well-mixed population Z=140, group size N=8, threshold n_pg=6, endowment b=1, contribution fraction c=0.1; four strategies C, P, R, D; pro-social strategies contribute to CRD, D does not; P and R pay additional tax π_t=0.03 to a local electoral institution formed when at least n_I=2 P/R present; institution revenue Δ_RP=δ·π_t·j_PR with δ=2; majority rule among P and R determines nature: if P>R then Ds are punished using full Δ_RP; if P<R then Cs, Ps, Rs are rewarded using Δ_RP; if tie, half of Δ_RP rewards prosocials and half punishes defectors; risk r of losing endowment if CRD threshold not met; evolutionary dynamics governed by selection-mutation Markov process with μ=1/Z and β=2; outcome metric η_G (population average group achievement) computed from stationary distribution as function of r.",Synthesizes detailed institutional description in Methods and parameter values in Fig. 3 and main text.,1
10.1177_10434631231219608.md,"No sanctioning (baseline, first 10 periods)",Paper describes an initial 10-period CPR game without any sanctioning mechanism before subjects are reassigned to sanctioning conditions.,1,"Sanctioning mechanism: none (no punishment stage, no sanction endowment)",Only difference from later treatments is absence of sanctioning stage and associated endowment.,1,True,Human subjects in a computerized laboratory CPR game.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions and controlled lab protocol.,1,True,Conducted at ELSE lab at Utrecht University.,1,False,All data from human participants; simulations only suggested as future work.,1,False,No formal analytical solution used as primary method; only informal discussion of equilibria.,1,4,Game is explicitly described as having four appropriators (fishermen) per group.,1,10,First part of experiment: 10 periods without sanctioning.,1,1,"Players choose appropriation effort x_it with 0 ≤ x_it ≤ 50, a continuous/any-amount decision.",1,0,Standard CPR appropriation: all 50 units are in private endowment; there is no pre-allocated public contribution.,1,N/R,They specify benefit function and resource dynamics but do not express marginal per capita return in standard linear PGG terms.,0,0,No mention of communication; typical z-Tree CPR experiments disallow chat and only provide outcome feedback.,0.8,1,Subjects observe what other fishermen have done at end of period (appropriation and resulting resource/payoffs).,0.9,0,No punishment stage in this baseline; mechanism N/A but coded 0 as there is no display of punishment identities.,0.9,0,No rewards are part of the design.,1,1,Participants knew they played 10 periods in the first part; common in lab instructions though not explicitly quoted.,0.7,0,Stage without any sanctioning mechanism by design.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,0,Only punishment is discussed; no reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Each appropriator has an endowment of 50 units each period to invest in fishing.,1,N/R,Outcomes reported as appropriation effort and resource size; no normalized cooperation rate given.,0,36.546,Table 1 reports mean individual appropriation = 36.546 in the 10 periods without sanctioning.,1,N/R,They discuss profit levels but do not normalize by theoretical maximum efficiency.,0,140,They state that in the first part each subject could earn 140 units per period if everyone cooperated (30 appropriation). For a group of four this is 4*140.,0.7,"Mostly Netherlands (61% Dutch), remainder mixed other nationalities",Sample description: 61% Dutch; others not itemized.,0.9,"Mean 23 years (students and non-students, varying years/stages)",They state the average age is 23; no further distribution given.,1,67% female,Sample description reports 67% of subjects were female.,1,87% students (various disciplines and years),They report 87% of subjects were students.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical lab (ELSE) with participants recruited via ORSEE.,1,"Dynamic common-pool resource (fishing ground) with stock R_t, initial resource R0=600, renewal R_t = min(600, 1.25*(R_{t-1} - (R_{t-1}/R0)*sum x_it)); individual benefit b_it = (4/R0 * R_{t-1})*x_it + (50 - x_it); overexploitation if total appropriation >120; sustainable individual appropriation = 30.",Summarizes core CPR dynamics and payoff structure described in Methods.,1
