10.1016_j.chaos.2025.116135.md,Five-strategy spatial public goods game with bribery on square lattice,"Paper describes a single Monte Carlo simulation model: a five-strategy spatial PGG with bribery on a square lattice; all reported parameter sweeps (r, α, β, γ) are within this one modeling framework.",0.95,"Synergy coefficient r (e.g., 3.5, 4), punishment cost α (fixed at 0.1), punishment fine β (varied), bribery amount γ (varied); initial state type (random vs patch-like) used for some runs.","Model section defines r, α, β, γ as key parameters. Results present phase diagrams and trajectories as functions of β and γ under fixed r and α, and later vary r. Initial networks (random vs patch-like) are described as alternative starting conditions but belong to the same simulation condition family.",0.96,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of strategies on a lattice with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; purely computational evolutionary game model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only simulations are run on a supercomputing system.,1,True,Authors explicitly state they use Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice and report numerical phase diagrams.,1,False,No closed-form analytical or proof-based results; all outcomes are numerical simulations though payoff functions are written down.,0.95,40000,Square lattice with side length 200 implies 200×200=40000 individuals.,1,100000000,"Text gives various illustrative run lengths (e.g., 10^7, 10^8, 1.53×10^8, 2.78×10^8). A fixed canonical number is not specified; 10^8 is a representative order-of-magnitude for reaching steady states. Using 1e8 as an approximate typical run length.",0.3,1,Model describes cooperation as contributing a cost c (single level) versus zero; no choice of a continuous range of contributions is given. This is effectively an all-or-nothing contribution decision.,0.8,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment allocation between private and public accounts; purely payoff-based evolutionary game.,1,N/R,"Total cooperative contributions in a group are multiplied by r and shared among 5 players; per-group MPCR would be r/5, but r is varied and no single value characterizes the condition. The paper does not state MPCR as a fixed parameter.",0.4,N/A,No human communication; agent strategies update via imitation based on payoffs only.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to humans; only model-internal payoffs.,1,N/A,No identities or information revelation; just strategy states on lattice.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism with identity revelation; only punishment and bribery.,1,N/A,Agents are not informed of a round horizon; simulation runs until steady state.,1,1,Peer punishment is central: punishers and bribe-recipients impose fines β at cost α on relevant defectors.,1,0.1,"α is defined as punishment cost and is fixed at 0.5 in noise K but α itself stated as 0.1 in Results (e.g., r=3.5 and α=0.1); no other α used in reported figures.",0.95,N/R,"β is the fine, but its value is systematically varied and not fixed to a single number for the condition, so no single magnitude per unit cost can be specified.",0.4,0,Model only contains costs and fines and bribery transfers; no positive reward mechanism is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/R,Payoff equations use a contribution cost c but its numerical value is not specified; no explicit endowment per round is given.,0.2,N/R,"Results report average fractions of strategies and phase memberships, not explicit normalized contributions relative to an endowment.",0.2,N/R,"Cooperators, punishers, and bribe-recipients pay a cost c but c’s numeric value and aggregate contributions are not given.",0.1,N/R,No ratio of realized group payoffs to maximum possible payoff is reported; only qualitative discussion of contribution level and cooperation.,0.1,N/R,Group or population-average payoffs are not tabulated; only strategy frequencies and phases are described.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"All results come from Monte Carlo simulations run on a supercomputing system, with no empirical data collection.",1,"Five-strategy spatial public goods game on a 200x200 square lattice with periodic boundaries. Each player is in 5 overlapping groups (self-centered and four nearest neighbors). Strategies: C (cooperator investing cost c), P (punisher investing cost c and paying cost α per defector to impose fine β), R (bribe-recipient investing cost c, accepts bribe γ from bribers instead of punishing them but still punishes non-bribing defectors with fine β at cost α), D (defector investing nothing and suffering fines from P and R), G (briber/defector paying bribe γ to neighboring R to avoid punishment; still punished by P). Payoff per group: rc(N_C+N_P+N_R)/5 minus relevant costs and fines as specified in the model equations. Each Monte Carlo step: randomly pick focal i, random neighbor j, i adopts j’s strategy with probability 1/(1+exp[(π_i−π_j)/K]) with K=0.5. Initial strategies distributed randomly or in patch-like clusters. Simulations run until absorbing pure phases or long-run coexistence oscillations; each configuration replicated 20 times.","Summarizes the detailed model specification from the Models and Results sections, including strategies, lattice structure, payoff equations, and updating rule.",0.99
10.1073_pnas.2500960122.md,"Baseline two-player harvesting game (evolutionary dynamical-systems simulation, main parameterization)","Single core simulation setup described as the minimal model with fixed parameters α=1, β=2/3, κ=0.2, T=1000; N and μ varied in analyses.",1,"Population size N and mutation rate μ of decision parameters (S,O), varied within ranges 30 ≤ N ≤ 3000 and 0.03 ≤ μ ≤ 0.3. Core game parameters fixed at α=1, β=2/3, κ=0.2, T=1000.","Paper repeatedly discusses how evolution depends on N and μ (e.g., Div6 vs Div8/2 emergence) while other parameters are held fixed except for robustness checks.",1,False,No human or animal participants; all results from numerical simulations.,1,False,"Study is a computational model, not a controlled behavioral or field experiment.",1,False,No lab or in-person environment; purely in silico.,1,True,They implement and run an evolutionary dynamical-systems model via source code (GitHub repository referenced).,1,True,"Paper includes formal derivations (Propositions 1–3, analytical calculation of modes), but central results rely on simulations; still counts as having an analytical component.",0.9,2,Core game is explicitly called a two-player harvesting game where N players are randomly matched into pairs; each game is within a pair.,1,1000,Generation defined as T game steps; they set T=1000 for simulations.,1,1,"Action ai is binary (1 harvest, 0 wait); no intermediate contribution levels.",1,N/A,"No public good or contribution endowment structure; players harvest from a regenerating resource, not contribute to a public fund.",1,N/R,Model is not framed as a linear public good; payoffs depend on logistic resource growth and harvest fractions; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication mechanism; only algorithmic decision functions.,1,N/A,"Simulation agents do not receive summary feedback screens; only state variables x, y_self, y_opponent enter decision function.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment action or identity revelation; punishment is modeled implicitly via overharvesting.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism in the simulation.,1,N/A,Agents are not described as knowing T or remaining steps.,1,0,"No separate costly punishment action; what authors call 'punitive harvesting' is just the same harvest action used strategically, not a distinct punishment technology.",0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism; therefore no defined per-unit punishment cost.,1,N/A,No explicit mapping from punishment points to payoff reductions.,1,0,"Model includes only harvesting/waiting, no rewarding of others.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward technology specified.,1,N/R,"There is no fixed per-round monetary or token endowment; players have a state variable y that evolves, but no constant per-round endowment is defined.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported are fitness, modes, and resource dynamics, not normalized contribution or harvest rates.",1,N/R,They do not summarize average harvested quantity per step numerically; only qualitative descriptions and mode fitness values.,1,N/R,"They compare fitness across modes qualitatively and give some fitness numbers in Table 2, but do not normalize by a theoretical maximum payoff to report efficiency 0–1.",0.7,N/R,They report individual fitness values and differences but no explicit aggregate group payoff per game or per generation.,0.8,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a computational evolutionary game simulation without human subjects.,1,"Evolutionary dynamical-systems game with two players sharing a regenerating resource with logistic growth r(x)=2x−x², harvest fraction β=2/3 applied multiplicatively to the resource, and player-state decay κ=0.2. Each player has a deterministic binary decision rule a_i = 1{x + S_i y_self + O_i y_opponent > 0} that evolves via mutation and selection across generations in a population of size N, with random matching into N/2 pairs each generation. Fitness is the time-average of y over T=1000 steps. Modes (limit cycles) of socio-ecological dynamics such as synchronized or alternating harvesting (e.g., Sync4, Div6, Div8/2) emerge and are analyzed, along with iso-mode regions in (S,O) space and ε-evolutionary robustness of strategies.",Summarizes the core structure and dynamics of the simulated game from the Model and Results sections.,1
