orted, only average bonuses by condition (e.g., Both condition average $3.83 total bonuses).",0.2,United States,Same MTurk recruitment restrictions.,1,"Mean 38.49 years, SD 11.73 across all conditions",Only overall demographics reported.,0.7,"51% female, 48% male, 1% other across all conditions",No Both-condition-specific gender stats.,0.7,N/R,Education present only in supplementary tables.,0.1,Online,Same online environment as other treatments.,1,"Reward rate drops from 73% in reward-only human groups to 46% in Both human groups; punishment rate around 3% in Both. Combining rewards and punishments is inefficient for human partners (no credible increase in contributions; estimated decrease of 1 token on average, p=0.170).",Reflects the key behavioral frequencies and inefficiency result for human-partner Both condition.,0.95
10.1016_j.isci.2025.112833.md,Reward+Punishment (Both) – all partners purported machine,Both condition where participants can both reward and punish and are told all partners are machines.,0.95,Sanction structure: Both (peer rewards and punishments available); Information: individual contributions + combined sanction stage; Purported group composition: all machine partners,Captures combined use of reward and punishment plus machine-partner belief.,0.95,True,Human participants interacting in IPGG with full sanction set.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of sanctions and partner composition.,0.98,False,Online experimental platform.,1,False,"Data from actual participants, not simulations.",1,False,Focus is empirical; analytical formulation is descriptive.,1,4,Group size fixed at 4.,1,20,20 rounds across all conditions.,1,1,Choice of any contribution amount between 0 and 20 MUs.,0.9,0,Endowment is under full control of participant each round.,0.95,0.4,1.6 multiplier / 4.,1,0,No communication stage mentioned.,0.95,1,"Players are told same info as Feedback plus sanction stage, i.e., each player's contributions.",1,1,Per-partner punish-or-reward decisions imply identity of target and likely of source; typical PGG design with labeled co-players.,0.8,1,Same individual-level structure for rewards.,0.8,1,"UI displays progression across up to 40 possible rounds, actually 20 played.",0.7,1,"Participants can punish at cost 4, harming others by 12.",1,4,Given parameter for cost of punishing.,1,12,Given parameter for harm from punishment.,1,1,"Participants can reward at cost 4, benefiting others by 12.",1,4,Given parameter for cost of rewarding.,1,12,Given parameter for benefit from reward.,1,20,20 MUs/round plus 50 MUs starting funds for rewards/punishments.,1,N/R,Only relative elevation of contributions and closure of machine penalty described.,0.1,N/R,"Paper states that when partners are assumed to be machines, combining rewards and punishments leads to higher contributions than rewards or punishments alone by 1.8 tokens on average (p=0.025), but does not provide the mean contributions themselves.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported by condition.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs not decomposed by human vs machine and condition in main text.,0.2,United States,Study restricted to US MTurk workers.,1,"Mean 38.49 years, SD 11.73 across all conditions",Demographics not broken down by condition.,0.7,"51% female, 48% male, 1% other across all conditions",Only overall gender composition provided.,0.7,N/R,Condition-level education not in main text.,0.1,Online,Same MTurk/CloudResearch online environment.,1,"Reward rate for machine-partner groups is 31% in Both vs 47% in reward-only; punishment rate about 4%. Machine penalty is finally suppressed in Both condition: 95% CI for contribution gap is −1.5 to 2.3 tokens. Combining rewards and punishments is uniquely effective when partners are assumed to be machines, increasing contributions by 1.8 tokens on average vs pooled reward+punishment-only (p=0.025), and eliminating exploratory reduction in contributions after receiving multiple rewards.",Summarizes distinctive behavioral features and core outcome (elimination of machine penalty) for this condition.,0.98
10.1177_00375497231171138.md,"Cellular automata simulation baseline setting (b=22, c=19, δ=4, ζ=0.35)","Paper describes a specific AnyLogic 8.7 cellular automata run with fixed parameters Numberofpeople=100, b=22, c=19, δ=4 (thus ζ=0.35) as the main illustrative simulation; treated as one simulation condition.",0.94,"External payoff parameters and population composition: benefit b=22, cost c=19, penalty δ=4 (ζ=0.35<0.5), Numberofpeople=100, initial cooperation share=0.5, trait proportions (easygoing=0.2, neutral=0.2, independent=0.6), neighbor-influence cooperation switch probabilities (0.5, 0.3, 0.1 for easygoing, neutral, independent).",These parameters are explicitly specified in Section 5.1 as the chosen values for the simulation illustrated in Figures 4–6.,0.97,False,No human participants; behavior is generated by a cellular automata model in AnyLogic.,1,False,"This is a computational simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human subjects.",1,False,No lab or physical setting; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe developing a cellular automata model in AnyLogic 8.7 to simulate employee group behavior.,1,True,Study also derives an evolutionary game model and cusp catastrophe equations analytically; this condition is part of an integrated analytical–simulation approach.,0.86,100,Section 5.1: “we set Numberofpeople = 100 … suppose that cell space is a 10×10 grid”.,1,100,Section 5.1: “simulation time has 100 time stages”.,1,1,"Model only has a binary strategic choice (cooperation vs conflict); there is no continuous contribution level, so contributes ‘all’ of a behavior choice or ‘nothing’.",0.9,N/A,This is not a public goods or contribution game with an endowment; agents simply choose between two behaviors.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return is defined; not a standard public goods game.,1,N/A,"Agents influence neighbors via probabilistic switching rules, but there is no explicit communication protocol like chat in a human experiment.",1,N/A,No user interface; simulated agents locally observe neighbors but are not ‘shown’ summaries.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment choices between identifiable agents; penalty δ is a fixed parameter in payoff function.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism between identified agents.,1,N/A,Simulated time steps are not presented to human participants.,1,1,"Penalty parameter δ represents punishment from digital supervision for conflict behavior (Section 4, Table 1).",1,N/R,"Model specifies punishment magnitude δ but not the cost to a punisher per unit; punishment arises from ‘digital supervision’, not from punishing agents.",0.4,4,"In the baseline simulation, δ=4 is the per-conflict penalty applied to a conflicting employee; treated as punishment impact per unit of conflict.",0.8,0,Model has benefits b but no reward decision or extra-cost reward mechanism; only penalty is discussed as ‘punishment’.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix uses perceived benefit b and cost c, not a standard token endowment per round.",0.7,N/R,"Outcome of interest is number/ratio of cooperating agents (Cp, ratio_Cp) and Behavior_State; not framed as contributions from an endowment.",0.9,N/R,No numerical contribution amounts are reported; binary behavior choice only.,1,N/R,Efficiency (group payoff relative to maximum) is not computed; only perceived payoffs and counts are shown.,1,N/R,"Aggregate group payoff is not numerically reported; only average perceived payoff per behavior (MeanVcp, MeanVcf).",0.9,N/A,No human participants in the simulation condition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is based on evolutionary game theory, cellular automata simulation in AnyLogic, and catastrophe modeling with no human subjects.",1,"Cellular automata on a 10×10 grid; each cell is an employee (agent Person) with a binary behavior (cooperation=1, conflict=0) and a psychological trait (easygoing, neutral, independent). Neighborhood is von Neumann (four neighbors). At t=0, 50% of employees cooperate. Transition rules: employees compare perceived payoffs of cooperation and conflict based on payoff matrix with b=22, c=19, δ=4; if Vcp>Vcf they choose cooperation, else conflict. Additionally, when more than 75% of neighbors cooperate, employees may irrationally switch to cooperation with probabilities 0.5, 0.3, 0.1 for easygoing, neutral, independent types, respectively. Simulation runs for 100 discrete time stages, tracking Cp, Cf, MeanVcp, MeanVcf, and counts of behavior reversals.",Summarizes the core dynamic rules and structure of the AnyLogic cellular automata model described in Section 5 and Appendix 3.,0.96
10.1177_00375497231171138.md,Cusp catastrophe fitting over 112 simulated parameter combinations,"Section 6 describes generating 112 experimental datasets by varying b, c, δ to change ζ and then fitting a cusp catastrophe model; treated as a separate simulation condition at the modeling level.",0.9,"Varied payoff parameters summarized by control factor ζ and resulting ratio_Cp, MeanVcp, MeanVcf, ratio_Chara2, ratio_Chara3 across 112 simulation runs; these serve as predictors in the cusp model for Behavior_State.","Section 6.1: state variable is Behavior_State, v is determined by ζ and ratio_Cp, u by MeanVcp, MeanVcf, ratio_Chara2, ratio_Chara3; 112 data points are generated by adjusting b, c, δ.",0.9,False,"Data are outputs of the cellular automata simulations, not human behavior.",1,False,"This is a model-fitting exercise on simulated data, not an experimental manipulation with subjects.",1,False,No physical lab or participants; analysis is computational.,1,True,All 112 observations come from running the AnyLogic simulation under different parameter values.,1,True,"Fitting of the cusp catastrophe model (equations for u*, v*, bifurcation condition) is a formal analytical modeling step using maximum likelihood.",0.95,100,Section 5.1 indicates Numberofpeople=100 is the base simulation size; not stated to vary across the 112 runs.,0.8,100,Section 5.1: simulation time has 100 time stages; not indicated that time horizon changes in the 112 runs.,0.8,1,Each agent still chooses between cooperation and conflict only.,0.95,N/A,No endowments or divisible contributions in the model.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined.,1,N/A,Neighbor influence is algorithmic; no explicit communication or chat channel.,1,N/A,No displays to human participants; only internal state variables.,1,N/A,Punishment is a parameter (δ) rather than chosen by identifiable agents.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No participants to display remaining rounds to.,1,1,Penalty δ is present in all payoff matrices; control factor ζ = (c-b)/((c/2)+δ-b) is defined for all simulations.,0.95,N/R,Cost to a punisher is never defined; punishment is exogenous supervision.,0.5,N/R,"Across the 112 runs δ varies via ζ, but specific δ values for each run are not listed, so a single numeric cannot be assigned.",0.4,0,No additional rewarding mechanism beyond baseline benefits b.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,"Model uses perceived benefits and costs, not fixed token endowments.",0.7,N/R,"Outcome coded as Behavior_State and ratio_Cp, not a normalized contribution from an endowment.",0.9,N/R,No token contributions reported.,1,N/R,Model fit focuses on Behavior_State; efficiency is not computed.,1,N/R,"Only average perceived payoffs (MeanVcp, MeanVcf) and Behavior_State are used in the fit.",0.9,N/A,No human participants in the 112 simulated datasets.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All data are from simulations and subsequent analytical fitting.,1,"Across 112 runs of the AnyLogic cellular automata model, benefit b, cost c, and penalty δ are systematically varied, summarized by the control factor ζ. For each run, the simulation produces time series of Cp, MeanVcp, MeanVcf, and trait ratios. From these, Behavior_State is computed as a discrete variable (0,1,2) based on ratio_Cp and relative MeanVcp vs MeanVcf. These summary statistics form the dataset for fitting linear, logistic, and cusp catastrophe models using the R Cuspfit package, with u determined by MeanVcp, MeanVcf, ratio_Chara2, ratio_Chara3 and v by ζ and ratio_Cp.",Summarizes how the 112 simulated conditions are constructed and used in the cusp catastrophe analysis.,0.92
10.1177_00375497231171138.md,Analytical evolutionary game model (replicator dynamics) for individual behavior,Section 4 develops a general evolutionary game model with payoff matrix and replicator dynamic function F[x(t)]; treated as a purely analytical condition without specific numeric parameterization.,0.88,"Symbolic payoff parameters: benefit b (>c), cost c, penalty δ; state variable x(t) as proportion of cooperators. Key inequalities: δ>(c/2) vs δ≤(c/2) determining ESS and threshold ζ=(c-b)/((c/2)+δ-b).","The independent variables in the analytical model are the parameters b, c, δ and the current cooperation proportion x(t); propositions are derived over symbolic ranges.",0.92,False,Model is entirely theoretical with no data collection.,1,False,No experimental manipulation or data; only mathematical derivations.,1,False,No laboratory or participants.,1,False,"This specific part involves closed-form derivations, not computational runs.",1,True,Replicator dynamics and ESS conditions are derived analytically from the payoff matrix.,1,2,Payoff matrix (Table 1) is defined for two employees in a symmetric game; population-level dynamics are captured via x(t) but base interaction is pairwise.,0.8,N/R,Replicator dynamics describe continuous or discrete time evolution; no finite number of rounds is specified.,0.7,1,"Players choose between two pure strategies (cooperation, conflict); no continuous contribution choice.",1,N/A,No endowment or contribution amount in the formal model; only behavior types.,1,N/R,Model is not a linear public goods game; no MPCR is defined.,1,N/A,Analytical model contains no communication mechanism.,1,N/A,No information-display assumptions beyond payoff perception.,1,N/A,"Punishment δ is impersonal digital supervision, not assigned by identified individuals.",0.9,N/A,No explicit rewarding agents.,1,N/A,No notion of displayed rounds in a purely analytical model.,1,1,Penalty δ for conflict behavior is central to the propositions.,1,N/R,Cost to any punisher is not modeled; δ directly reduces payoff of conflict chooser.,0.5,N/R,δ is a symbolic parameter without fixed numeric value in this general model.,0.8,0,No separate reward mechanism; only benefit b and penalty δ.,0.95,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/R,"Payoff is modeled as perceived benefit minus cost/penalty, not allocations from an endowment.",0.8,N/R,"Key dependent variable is x(t), the proportion choosing cooperation, not defined as contribution from an endowment.",0.9,N/R,No continuous contribution amounts exist.,1,N/R,Model does not compute group efficiency metrics; only compares payoffs and stability conditions.,1,N/R,Aggregate group payoff is not derived explicitly.,0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely mathematical derivations.,1,"Two-player symmetric game with strategies cooperation and conflict. Payoff matrix: if both cooperate, each gets b − c/2; if one cooperates and the other conflicts, cooperator gets b − c, defector gets b − δ; if both conflict, both get 0. Assumes (b−c)>0. Let x(t) be proportion of cooperators in the population. Using evolutionary game theory, the replicator dynamic is F[x(t)] = x(t)[1−x(t)] [b−c + ((c/2)+δ−b)x(t)]. Proposition 1: if δ > c/2, cooperation is the ESS. Corollary 1: if δ ≤ c/2, cooperation vs conflict depends on x(t) relative to threshold ζ = (c−b)/((c/2)+δ−b).",Summarizes the core analytical structure and key derived conditions from Section 4 and Appendices 1–2.,0.98
10.1209_0295-5075_acb07d.md,Simulation condition – traditional PDG with punishment and no exit,"Fig. 1 and text contrast the ""traditional case"" where individuals are always involved in interaction and no exit occurs, with the same spatial PDG and punishment parameters.",0.88,"No adaptive exit (exit probability fixed at 0); aspiration level N/A; sanction-to-cost ratio β/α=3; dilemma strength b varied; punishment fine β=0.3, cost α=0.1; strategies C, D, P on square lattice",Traditional case is explicitly described as without exit; β/α=3 mentioned for traditional case in Fig. 4 caption; same PDG and punishment parameters as model section; b is varied in figures.,0.86,False,"Study is based on Monte Carlo simulations of agents on a lattice, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a numerical simulation study.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,They explicitly use Monte Carlo synchronous simulations on a square lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution; only model definition and simulations.,1,10000,"Population is N players on an L×L lattice with L=100–500; figures appear to use a typical size, 100×100=10000. Use representative lower bound.",0.3,500000,They state that averages of last 5000 time steps over more than 5×10^5 MC steps are taken; total steps per simulation are 5×10^5.,0.8,1,"This is a pairwise PDG with discrete strategies C, D, P; there is no continuous contribution decision, so the contribution-choice field is best coded as all-or-nothing enabled (binary cooperate or not).",0.6,N/A,"No endowment allocation decision by agents; payoffs arise directly from PDG parameters b and c, and this is a simulation with no explicit contribution pool.",1,N/R,This is not a public-goods game; there is no MPCR defined for PDG.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication not applicable in agent-based simulation.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents update only via payoff-based imitation in simulation.,1,N/A,