10.1209_0295-5075_acb07d.md,Simulation condition – adaptive exit with varied aspiration level A and fixed β/α=3,"Main condition where adaptive exit is enabled; aspiration level A and dilemma strength b are varied, with β=0.3, α=0.1 (β/α=3). Used for Figs. 1–3 and parts of Fig. 2.",0.9,"Adaptive exit enabled (individual dynamic exit probability θ∈(0,0.99)); aspiration level A varied in [0,1]; sanction-to-cost ratio β/α=3 (β=0.3, α=0.1); dilemma strength b varied; strategies C, D, P on square lattice","Model section defines adaptive exit; Fig. 1 studies various A; parameters α=0.1, β=0.3; β/α=3 is default except where β/α is explicitly varied.",0.9,False,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulations without human subjects.,1,False,"Simulation study, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,Monte Carlo synchronous simulation on spatial lattice.,1,False,No analytical derivations of equilibria; all results via simulation.,1,10000,Simulations use L=100–500; figures likely use a standard L such as 100. Use representative size 100×100.,0.3,500000,They simulate more than 5×10^5 MC time steps per run and average over last 5000 steps.,0.8,1,Binary strategies C/D/P analogous to all-or-nothing cooperation decision in PDG.,0.6,N/A,No explicit contribution pool or initial allocation; only PDG strategy payoffs.,1,N/R,"Model is PDG, not public-goods; MPCR not defined.",1,N/A,Agents do not communicate; no human subjects.,1,N/A,No UI or feedback displays; only payoff-based imitation in code.,1,N/A,Punishment is encoded in payoffs; there is no identity revelation notion.,1,N/A,No reward is modeled.,1,N/A,No human-facing interface; time horizon not shown to agents.,1,1,Punisher strategy P fines defectors.,1,0.1,α=0.1 is specified as cost to punisher.,1,0.3,β=0.3 is specified as fine imposed per punishment event.,1,0,Only punishment is introduced as a prosocial mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/R,Payoffs are defined via b and c; no explicit per-round endowment is given.,0.2,N/R,"Outcomes reported as fractions of C, D, P and overall cooperators (F_C+F_P), not normalized contributions.",0.1,N/R,No explicit contribution amount separate from strategy choice.,0.1,N/R,They do not compute payoff efficiency relative to a maximum; focus is on strategy fractions and exit probabilities.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate group payoff time series or values are given.,0.1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,No human,Entire study uses Monte Carlo simulations of artificial agents.,1,"Spatial prisoner's dilemma with strategies C (cooperate), D (defect), and P (prosocial punisher) on an L×L lattice with von Neumann neighborhood and periodic boundaries. Payoff matrix: C vs C: b−c; C vs D: −c; C vs P: b−c; D vs C: b; D vs D: 0; D vs P: b−β; P vs C: b−c; P vs D: −c−α; P vs P: b−c. Parameters: α=0.1, β=0.3, b−c=1, sensitivity γ=0.05. Dynamics: synchronous Monte Carlo updating with stages for participation (based on θ), interaction among active neighbors, imitation-based strategy updating via payoff comparison, and adaptive exit-probability updating according to a Bush–Mosteller-type rule with aspiration level A and stimulus s_i(t)=tanh[γ(π_i−A b k_i)]. Exit probability θ is capped at 0.99.",Directly summarizes the model section and adaptive exit mechanism for the main treatment with varying A and fixed β/α.,0.98
