99)” in Table 2.,1,University students (mean semester 4.816),Semester entry for Pun-IM in Table 2.,1,On site,Conducted at WiSo experimental laboratory.,1,"Stage 1: C recommends contributions. Stage 2: A, B, C contribute simultaneously. After contributions, C observes individual contributions of A and B and can punish either or both using 3:1 technology (1 point cost to C reduces target’s payoff by 3 points).","Punishment treatment, Individual Monitoring description, and Table 1.",1
10.1016_j.amc.2022.127567.md,Analytical evolutionary public goods model with consensual peer punishment and preference reversal,"Paper describes a single mathematical/analytical model of a public goods game with consensual peer punishment and probabilistic preference reversal (parameters N, r, c, T, p, q1, q2, τ1, τ2, α, β). No separate empirical conditions with human subjects are reported.",1,"Parameters of the evolutionary public goods game: group size N, synergy factor r, contribution c, punishment consensus threshold T, cooperator punishment preference level p, punishment cost α, punishment fine β; preference reversal probabilities q1 and q2; thresholds for triggering preference reversal τ1 and τ2.","Model dynamics and comparative statics are studied by varying these parameters (e.g., sections 3.1 and 3.2, Figs. 2–5, Table 1). They are the levers that define different scenarios in the analytical results.",1,False,The paper explicitly states it is a theoretical/mathematical model and that no data was used. The model is defined on an infinite well-mixed population with replicator dynamics and no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects or samples is conducted; only theoretical analysis and equations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,"Although figures illustrate dynamics, they are based on analytical/replicator equations rather than described as agent-based or Monte Carlo simulations without humans. Core contribution is analytical formulae.",0.7,True,"Section 2 derives payoff functions and replicator dynamics; results are framed in terms of analytical conditions for equilibria and thresholds (e.g., Δπ(x), qt).",1,5,"Generic model uses parameter N; specific parameterizations for results often use N=5 (e.g., Fig. 2 and Fig. 3 state N=5). Use this as representative group size for the analyzed scenarios.",0.7,1,Standard one-shot public goods interaction repeated over evolutionary time via replicator dynamics; no repeated rounds within a single game instance are described.,0.8,1,"Each cooperator contributes a fixed amount c if cooperating or 0 if defecting, implying binary choice to contribute or not rather than choosing any continuous amount.",1,0,"In standard public goods formulation used, each individual decides whether to contribute c from their own endowment; initial endowment is fully private before contribution.",0.8,0.6,"Marginal per capita return is r/N. In many illustrative parameter sets, r=3 and N=5 (e.g., Figs. 2, 3, 5), so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.8,0,No communication or messaging between individuals is modeled; only probabilistic decisions and thresholds.,1,0,Model specifies payoffs and punishment but does not include any feedback about others' earnings or actions beyond what is implicit in the public goods outcome.,1,0,No identities or individual labeling are present; population is anonymous and well-mixed.,1,0,"There is no reward mechanism; only punishment is modeled, and no identities.",1,0,No explicit rounds or horizon are communicated to agents; dynamics are evolutionary and continuous in time.,1,1,Central focus is consensual peer punishment; defectors are fined when consensus and preferences conditions are met.,1,0.3,"Punishment cost per punisher per defector is α; many parameter examples use α=0.3 (e.g., Figs. 2–5).",0.8,1,"Defectors each are imposed a fine β·n_ep; cost to each punisher is α·n_d. Thus, each punisher pays α per defector and imposes β per punisher on each defector. Using β=1 from multiple parameter sets gives punishment impact per cost unit of β/α ≈ 1/0.3 ≈ 3.33, but the schema defines tech as magnitude per coin spent; most natural is β/α. However β and α vary by example, so set to N/R.",0.2,0,"The model includes only punishment, no rewarding mechanism.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,N/R,"Individuals contribute a fixed amount c if cooperating (often c=1 in examples), but total endowment per player is not explicitly defined (could be c or higher); not specified.",0.3,N/R,"As an analytical evolutionary model, the outcome variable is the fraction of cooperators x over time, but no single summary value of average contribution rate in equilibrium is numerically reported.",0.4,N/R,No explicit numerical equilibrium contributions are tabulated; only qualitative conditions and thresholds are given.,0.2,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency (total payoff relative to maximum) numerically; it discusses evolution of cooperation qualitatively.,0.2,N/R,Average payoffs πC and πD are derived symbolically but no single numeric group payoff is reported as a main outcome measure.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; infinite well-mixed population is purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is a mathematical model with no empirical data or subjects, as stated in Data Availability and methods.",1,"Infinite well-mixed population; N players randomly selected to play a linear public goods game. Each cooperator contributes fixed c>0; total contributions multiplied by r with 1<r<N and shared equally. Consensual peer punishment: in a first (democratic) stage, cooperators indicate preference for punishment with probability p; punishment is employed if n_p/n_c ≥ T. In a second (individual) stage, actual execution of punishment may differ due to preference reversal: cooperators who initially preferred punishment may drop out with probability q1 when cooperator fraction n_c/N < τ1; cooperators who initially preferred no punishment may start punishing with probability q2 when n_c/N ≥ τ2. Actual number of punishers n_ep is binomial. If punishment is employed, each defector receives fine β·n_ep and each punisher pays cost α·n_d. Evolutionary dynamics follow replicator equation on strategy frequencies with payoffs πC and πD derived as functions of parameters.","Summarizes the formal model description in Section 2, including public goods structure, consensual punishment rule, preference reversal mechanism, and payoff and evolutionary dynamics.",1
10.1016_j.physa.2022.127913.md,"Monte Carlo simulations on regular lattice (triangular lattice, k=6)","Paper treats results on regular lattice (RL) as a distinct setting in Sec. 3.1, with fixed topology and parameters r, A, α varied.",1,"Network topology = regular triangular lattice (k=6); synergy factor r ∈ {3.5, 4.0, 4.5}; feedback sensitivity A ∈ [0,1]; punishment fine α varied; initial fractions of D, CP, DP random.","RL topology is fixed; r, A, α are scanned in phase diagrams (Fig. 2) and text; strategies D, CP, DP are present from random initial conditions.",1,False,Study uses an evolutionary game model with Monte Carlo simulations and replicator dynamics; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled lab/field experiment with participants; it is a computational study.,1,False,No physical lab or human subjects are involved.,1,True,Numerical Monte Carlo simulations on networks explicitly described in Sec. 2.1 and 3.1.,1,True,Replicator dynamics and mean-field/replicator equations used for analytical boundaries; applies to this topology as well.,0.9,40000,"Text: 'the size of RLs or the small-world networks based on 2D RLs is N=200×200', so N=40000.",1,0,Infinite-time evolutionary process until steady state; no discrete fixed number of game rounds as in finite experiments.,0.4,1,Each cooperator/punisher contributes 1 if cooperative; defectors 0. No indication of discrete all-or-nothing choice over full endowment; strategy type fixes contribution.,0.6,N/A,Agent strategies determine contributions; there is no dynamic endowment allocation or default contribution state for human participants.,1,N/R,Only r (synergy factor) and group size Gi are given; MPCR=r/Gi could vary with Gi. They do not state a single fixed MPCR numeric value.,0.5,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated agents.,1,0,"No notion of screens or feedback to human players; not applicable, set to 0 for absence.",1,N/A,No identities or user interface; punishment is defined in payoff equations only.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,0,No display of rounds; simulation runs until steady state.,1,1,Core of the model is prosocial pool punishment by CP and DP strategies.,1,N/R,Model assumes fines on defectors covered by costs to punishers but does not give cost per punishment unit explicitly; only fine α per defector is specified.,0.3,N/R,Punishment fine per defector is α; they do not specify a multiplier mapping from spent cost to fine.,0.3,0,No rewarding scheme appears; only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,1,Each cooperator/punisher 'makes a contribution of 1 to the public good'; treat 1 as the per-round contribution endowment.,0.8,N/R,"They report fractions of strategies (ρ_CP, ρ_DP, ρ_D) but not normalized contribution rates separately.",0.3,N/R,No explicit average contribution amounts per group or player are tabulated; only strategy densities.,0.3,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff normalized by maximum is provided.,0.2,N/R,Group or population average payoffs are discussed qualitatively but no numeric values given.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; country not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational simulations and analytical work.,1,"Public goods game on a regular triangular lattice (degree k=6) with overlapping groups of size Gi=k+1; three strategies (D, CP, DP); synergy factor r with 1<r<Gi; probabilistic pool punishment where CP punishment probability g_CP = A*N_P/G and DP punishment probability g_DP = A*N_D/G; strategy update via pairwise comparison (Fermi rule) with noise κ=0.1; synchronous Monte Carlo steps until steady state.",Summarizes the PGG and punishment rules plus update dynamics as described in Sec. 2.1 for the RL case.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2022.127913.md,"Monte Carlo simulations on regular random networks (RRNs, k=6)",Sec. 3.2 discusses results on regular random networks separately from RLs and small-world networks.,1,"Network topology = regular random network with degree k=6; synergy factor r ∈ {3.5, 4.0, 4.5}; feedback sensitivity A ∈ [0,1]; punishment fine α varied; initial strategy fractions random.","RRN topology fixed with k=6; r, A, α varied in Fig. 4 and discussed; strategies D, CP, DP initially random.",1,False,No human subjects; evolutionary simulations on graphs.,1,False,"This is a computational study, not a behavioral experiment.",1,False,No lab setting or participants.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations explicitly described in Sec. 2.1 and used in Sec. 3.2.,1,True,Semi-analytical boundaries using replicator/mean-field approximations are presented for RRNs (Fig. 4).,1,40000,Text: 'The size of employed RRNs is N=40000'.,1,0,"Simulations proceed until steady state, not a fixed number of rounds.",0.4,1,"Agent strategy types (D, CP, DP) determine contribution 1 or 0; no indication of fractional choice decision per round.",0.6,N/A,No human endowments or initial allocations; contributions hard-coded by strategy.,1,N/R,r and group size G are mentioned but MPCR is not explicitly computed or fixed as a single number.,0.5,N/A,No communication among human players; agents follow programmed rules.,1,0,No UI; only internal payoffs matter.,1,N/A,No player identities; not a human experiment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,0,No round display; infinite-horizon simulation.,1,1,Same CP and DP punishment scheme as in RL case.,1,N/R,"As before, only fines α on defectors are specified, not explicit cost per unit punishment.",0.3,N/R,Mapping of expenditure to fine is not detailed; model just assumes fines covered by costs.,0.3,0,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,Each cooperative agent contributes 1 per PGG; treat this as unit endowment.,0.8,N/R,Only densities of strategies and dominance regions are reported.,0.3,N/R,No average contributions per agent are numerically summarized.,0.3,N/R,They talk about 'public goods' and advantages qualitatively but not normalized efficiency metrics.,0.2,N/R,"Payoff gaps and qualitative higher vs lower payoffs are discussed, not explicit group payoff numbers.",0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Computational simulations only.,1,"Public goods game on a regular random network with degree k=6, N=40000; each node plays in Gi=k+1 overlapping groups; strategies D, CP, DP; contribution 1 from each punisher/cooperator; synergy factor r; probabilistic pool punishment with g_CP = A*N_P/G and g_DP = A*N_D/G; defectors fined α if at least one punisher acts; strategy update via Fermi rule with κ=0.1 until steady state.",Summarizes model instantiation on RRN as in Sec. 2.1 and 3.2.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2022.127913.md,Monte Carlo simulations on small-world networks based on 2D regular lattice (rewiring model),Sec. 3.3 first uses Watts–Strogatz-type small-world networks generated by rewiring edges with probability p from a 2D RL.,1,"Network topology = small-world network via rewiring edges of 2D triangular lattice (k=6, N=200×200); randomness p ∈ [0,1]; synergy factor r ∈ {3.5, 4.0, 4.5}; feedback sensitivity A (notably A=0.235); punishment fine α varied.","Topology constructed by rewiring with probability p (Sec. 2.1, 3.3, Fig. 6, Fig. A.12); r, A, α varied in figures.",1,False,"Numerical simulations on networks, no human participants.",1,False,"Computational study, not a human experiment.",1,False,No lab environment or human subjects.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations repeatedly referenced in Sec. 3.3 and figures.,1,True,"Pair-approximation-based analytical payoff gaps (Δ_DPL-D etc.) are derived for small-world case (Fig. 9, Eq. (10)).",1,40000,Small-world networks are 'based on 2D RLs' with N=200×200 as stated earlier.,1,0,Evolutionary simulations continue until strategy densities reach steady state.,0.4,1,"Same strategy-based contribution rule: CP and DP contribute 1, D contributes 0.",0.6,N/A,No dynamic endowment allocation; contributions fully determined by strategies in a non-human simulation.,1,N/R,"As before, r and group size are given but MPCR not explicitly reported.",0.5,N/A,No communication mechanism among agents.,1,0,No visual/game interface.,1,N/A,No identities or UI; simulation only.,1,N/A,No rewards in model.,1,0,No rounds shown; only simulation time steps.,1,1,Same CP and DP punishment processes apply.,1,N/R,Costs to punishers are implicit and not numerically specified per unit.,0.3,N/R,Fine α per defector defined; no explicit technology parameter mapping cost to fine.,0.3,0,Only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,1,Each cooperative player contributes 1 per game as before.,0.8,N/R,"They report fractions of Ps, DPs, CPs; not explicit normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No numerical contribution totals per group/player tabulated.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively as 'optimal public goods' but not quantified as payoff/maximum.,0.3,N/R,No explicit group payoffs are given.,0.3,N/A,No human participants involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulations on networks without humans.,1,"Public goods game on small-world networks created by rewiring each edge of a 2D triangular lattice with probability p, preserving degree k=6 and avoiding self/multi-edges; group size G=k+1; strategies D, CP, DP; contribution 1 by CP and DP; synergy factor r (1<r<G); probabilistic pool punishment g_DP=A*N_D/G and g_CP=A*N_P/G; defectors fined α when at least one punisher acts; strategy update via Fermi rule W_{j←i}=1/[1+exp((Π_i−Π_j)/κ)] with κ=0.1; simulation continues until densities of CP, DP, D reach steady state; analysis includes classification of nodes by presence of shortcuts (local vs shortcut-linked) and pair-approximation payoff gaps Δ_DPL-D and Δ_CPL-D.",Combines the general model description with the specific small-world construction and analyses in Sec. 3.3 and Fig. 7–9.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2022.127913.md,"Monte Carlo simulations on small-world networks based on 2D regular lattice (extra-connection model, Appendix A.13)","Fig. A.13 describes small-world networks 'where extra connection is added to each individual with probability p', a different small-world construction from the rewiring model.",0.95,"Network topology = small-world network based on 2D triangular lattice (k=6) by adding an extra connection to each node with probability p; randomness p ∈ [0,1]; synergy factor r ∈ {3.5, 4.0, 4.5}; feedback sensitivity A=0.235; punishment fine α varied.","Appendix Fig. A.13 specifies this alternative construction and lists r, A, α as varied parameters.",0.95,False,"Again a computational variant, no participants.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Described as numerical simulations in Appendix on this alternative small-world network.,1,False,Appendix Fig. A.13 is presented as simulation evidence; no separate analytical treatment is detailed for this construction beyond carrying over intuition.,0.6,40000,Based on the same 2D RL with N=200×200 as stated; adding edges does not alter node count.,0.95,0,"Simulations run to steady state, as in main small-world case.",0.4,1,Same underlying PGG and strategies; contribution 1 or 0 fixed by strategy.,0.6,N/A,No human allocation; strategy-determined contributions.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly computed; same payoff structure as main model.,0.5,N/A,No communication.,1,0,No UI.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Rounds not displayed.,1,1,Uses same CP/DP punishment mechanics.,1,N/R,"As before, cost per punishment not numerically defined.",0.3,N/R,Fine per defector α exists; cost technology unspecified.,0.3,0,No rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,Same PGG contribution rule as main text.,0.8,N/R,"Appendix shows fractions of Ps, DPs, CPs vs p, not explicit contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No contributions quantified separately from strategy densities.,0.3,N/R,No normalized efficiency given.,0.2,N/R,No group payoff values shown in Appendix figure.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation-only Appendix study.,1,"Same public goods game and punishment rules as main text: nodes on a 2D triangular lattice (base degree k=6) are given an extra connection with probability p, generating a small-world topology with increased average degree; overlapping groups of size G based on local neighbors plus focal; strategies D, CP, DP; contribution 1 by CP and DP; synergy factor r; probabilistic punishment with sensitivity A and fine α; strategy update via Fermi rule until steady state; Appendix Fig. A.13 examines fractions of Ps, DPs, CPs versus p for various r and α, demonstrating optimal peaks in DP prevalence similar to the rewiring-based small-world case.",Summarizes specifics of the extra-connection small-world construction and its use in simulations.,1
10.1016_j.physa.2022.127913.md,Replicator dynamics analysis (well-mixed approximation),Sec. 2.2 ('Theory') specifies replicator equations and analytical payoffs Π_S to derive boundaries between punisher- and defector-dominated regions; this is a separate analytical experiment without networks.,0.95,"Population state given by densities f_CP, f_DP, f_D in [0,1]; group size G; synergy factor r; feedback sensitivity A; punishment fine α; composition weights w_DP, w_CP for mixed punisher strategy X.","Replicator dynamics treat fractions of strategies and parameters A, α, r, G as variables; boundaries are found by solving Π_X=Π_D.",0.9,False,Purely mathematical model; no simulations or human data required for the equations themselves.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; it's theoretical analysis.,1,False,No lab or participants.,1,False,This condition refers specifically to analytic replicator equations; simulations are described elsewhere.,0.9,True,Section 2.2 derives closed-form expressions for expected payoffs and boundaries using replicator dynamics and combinatorics.,1,0,Well-mixed infinite-population approximation; no finite player count is specified.,0.7,0,"Continuous-time replicator dynamics over time t, not discrete rounds of a game.",0.8,1,Same underlying PGG with contributions 1 for cooperative/punisher strategies; this assumption carries into payoff formulas.,0.8,N/A,Analytical model does not define endowments or defaults for human subjects.,1,N/R,Replicator analysis uses r and G symbolically but never defines MPCR separately.,0.7,N/A,No communication or interface; abstract mathematical dynamics.,1,0,No UI concepts.,1,N/A,No notion of player identity in equations.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,0,No rounds displayed or relevant in theory.,1,1,"Punishment enters payoffs via α, g_CP, g_DP in Π_S expressions.",1,N/R,"Costs to punishers are implicit in payoff formulas but not parameterized separately from α, A.",0.4,N/R,No explicit cost-to-fine multiplier parameter is introduced analytically.,0.4,0,Only punishment appears in equations.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,Contribution 1 per cooperator/punisher used in payoff derivation Π_P^g.,0.8,N/R,Replicator model tracks densities f_S; contribution rate isn't reported as a distinct output.,0.5,N/R,No explicit aggregate contributions are output; contributions are embedded in payoff formulas.,0.5,N/R,They do not compute Π̄ normalized by a maximum as a reported dependent variable; use boundaries instead.,0.5,N/R,Average payoff Π̄ is part of equations but not reported numerically as a result.,0.5,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Pure mathematical modeling.,1,"Well-mixed public goods game with three strategies (D, CP, DP); groups of size G; contributions of 1 from CP and DP; synergy factor r; punishment probabilities g_CP and g_DP dependent on fractions of punishers and defectors; expected payoffs Π_D', Π_DP', Π_CP' are computed via combinatorial sums over possible group compositions; replicator equations df_S/dt = f_S(1−f_S)(Π_S−Π̄); boundary between P- and D-dominated regions obtained by solving Π_X = Π_D with Π_X = Σ w_S Π_S and weights w_CP, w_DP set by estimated densities.",Condenses the key analytical model elements in Sec. 2.2.,1
10.1108_tpm-03-2020-0021.md,NC – Not costly to punish treatment,"Treatment NC in Table 5: first period group incentives, second period individual incentives where punishing is not costly.",1,Cost of punishment in second period = Not costly (NC treatment),Only manipulated factor across treatments is whether second-period punishment is costly or not.,1,True,Human participants recruited from graduate-level business courses.,1,True,Random assignment to two treatments in a controlled lab setting with induced payoffs.,1,True,Participants interacted over a computerized network in sessions run by an experimenter; this is standard lab protocol.,0.9,False,No agent-based or computer-only simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper contains a theoretical model but the reported results are from an experiment with human subjects, not purely analytical derivations.",0.9,2,Two managers form each team in the two-period game (“two managers work or do not work hard”).,1,15,Participants played 15 rounds of one of the two-period games.,1,0,Action set is binary: Work or Do Not Work; cannot choose intermediate contribution levels.,1,0,"No endowment allocation between private/public accounts is used; payoffs are expected utilities from effort choices, so default contribution concept does not apply and is set to 0 as effectively all payoff is private.",0.2,N/R,This is not a standard linear public goods game and no marginal per capita return is defined or can be inferred.,0,0,No mention of communication between subjects; only feedback screens are described.,0.9,1,"After each round, strategies and outcomes from both periods and total points in current and all rounds combined were revealed, which is a summary of others’ actions and payoffs.",1,1,"Each round is an anonymous pair but within a round, each manager observes partner’s action (or can infer it) and thus who effectively ‘punished’ whom; identities within round are known.",0.6,0,No explicit reward mechanism; only effort choices and resulting payoffs.,1,1,"Design uses a fixed, known horizon of 15 rounds; standard practice implies this is told to subjects, though not explicitly quoted.",0.7,1,"Punishing defined as not working in the second period, reducing partner’s payoff.",1,0,"In NC, when punishing is costless, the punisher receives same payoff as if both worked; described as “costless to punish”.",1,60,Text: being punished reduces expected utility by 60 compared to both working in second period (loss of 60). Interpreted as 60 utility points harm per punishment act.,0.7,0,No mechanism for costly rewarding or bonus contributions; only work vs not work.,1,N/A,No reward stage in design.,1,N/A,No reward stage in design.,1,N/R,"Game is framed in expected utility points, not as allocating an endowment; no fixed endowment per period is specified.",0,0.656,"First-period working rate in NC across all rounds is 65.6%, treated as normalized contribution rate.",1,N/R,Only binary choice (work/not work) with no token amount per work; no numeric ‘amount’ of contribution reported.,0,N/R,No efficiency measure (payoff vs maximum) reported or derivable from aggregate data provided.,0,N/R,Only individual expected utilities and some outcome frequencies are given; average group payoff by treatment is not reported.,0,USA,"Study run at Farmingdale State College (New York, USA) and Ohio State University department support is acknowledged; implies US-based subjects.",0.8,N/R,Only described as recruited from graduate-level business courses; no age statistics.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,0,Graduate business students,Participants recruited from graduate-level business courses.,1,On site,"Participants interacted over a computerized network with experimenter-led instructions and payment, implying lab/onsite sessions rather than remote online participation.",0.8,"Two-player, two-period effort game repeated for 15 rounds with random rematching each round. First period uses group incentives with payoffs: (Do Not Work, Do Not Work)=(30,30); (Do Not Work, Work)=(90,20); (Work, Do Not Work)=(20,90); (Work, Work)=(80,80). Second period uses individual incentives with payoffs from Table 1 Panel B (not costly case). Actions: binary Work vs Do Not Work, using neutral labels 'Two' and 'One'. Feedback after period 1 then period 2; random rematching each round to simulate one-shot horizon.","Summarized from method, model, Table 1 and procedure descriptions specific to NC treatment.",1
10.1108_tpm-03-2020-0021.md,C – Costly to punish treatment,"Treatment C in Table 5: first period group incentives, second period individual incentives where punishing is costly.",1,Cost of punishment in second period = Costly (C treatment),Only manipulated factor across treatments is whether second-period punishment is costly or not.,1,True,Human participants recruited from graduate-level business courses.,1,True,Random assignment to treatments with controlled payoff structure.,1,True,Same lab/computer-network setup as NC treatment.,0.9,False,No non-human simulations for this treatment.,1,False,"Results based on experimental data, not solely analytical derivations.",0.9,2,Two managers in each team as in NC treatment.,1,15,Participants played 15 rounds of one of the two-period games.,1,0,Binary work choice; no continuous contribution level.,1,0,"No explicit endowment-splitting; payoffs are direct utilities from effort choices, treated as private.",0.2,N/R,No linear public goods structure or MPCR statistic reported.,0,0,No subject communication is described; only feedback screens and questionnaires.,0.9,1,"After each round, actions/outcomes from both periods and total points are revealed, including partner’s outcomes.",1,1,"Within each pair and round, each manager observes (directly or via inference) partner’s effort and hence who punished.",0.6,0,No explicit reward mechanism implemented.,1,1,"Fixed 15-round horizon; standard to inform subjects, though not verbatim quoted.",0.7,1,Punishing is not working in second period; this is central to the costly-punishment treatment.,1,60,"When a manager is punished in costly treatment, both managers incur the same cost of 60 in expected utility compared to both working.",0.7,60,Being punished reduces each manager’s expected utility by 60 relative to both working; interpreted as 60-point harm per punishment event.,0.7,0,No separate costly rewarding option in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,No per-period monetary endowment is specified; utilities derive from effort choices and contract payoffs.,0,0.567,"First-period working rate in C across all rounds is 56.7%, treated as normalized contribution rate.",1,N/R,Only binary work/not work choices; no numeric token contribution reported.,0,N/R,No aggregate efficiency ratio reported or derivable from available summary statistics.,0,N/R,No average group payoff per round or per treatment is provided.,0,USA,"Same subject pool and institutions as NC treatment, located in the USA.",0.8,N/R,Only that they are graduate-level business students; no age data reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition information.,0,Graduate business students,Participants recruited from graduate-level business courses.,1,On site,Same lab-style setup as NC treatment with computer network sessions.,0.8,"Two-player, two-period effort game repeated 15 rounds with random rematching each round. First period uses same group incentive payoffs as NC: (Do Not Work, Do Not Work)=(30,30); (Do Not Work, Work)=(90,20); (Work, Do Not Work)=(20,90); (Work, Work)=(80,80). Second period uses individual incentives with payoffs from Table 1 Panel B (costly to punish case). Actions: binary Work vs Do Not Work, neutrally labeled. Feedback after each period; participants see both players’ actions/outcomes and cumulative points.","Summarized from model description, Table 1 panel B (costly), and procedure for the C treatment.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2019.06.019.md,VCM (baseline voluntary contribution mechanism),Baseline condition where participants choose numeric contributions without type report; explicitly labeled VCM.,1,"Reporting/punishment institution: no type report, no punishment, standard VCM baseline","Across conditions, only the reporting mechanism and punishment stage vary; this row is the no-report, no-punishment baseline.",1,True,Human subjects (Middlebury undergraduates) in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with between-condition manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in a computer lab with z-Tree and physical dividers.,1,False,No mention of computer simulations; all data from human play.,1,False,"Paper reports experimental results, not purely theoretical analysis.",1,3,Fixed groups of three in all conditions.,1,10,Participants played a 10-period public goods game.,1,1,Participants could contribute any integer amount from 0 to 4 tokens; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,Standard VCM style: subjects choose how many of their tokens to contribute; endowment starts private.,0.9,N/R,Payoffs given via non-linear tables by type; no explicit linear MPCR.,1,0,No communication stage; only numeric contribution and outcome screens.,1,1,End of each period shows each group member’s contribution in anonymous boxes; summary of others’ contributions.,1,0,No punishment stage in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,1,Standard lab practice; game length (10 periods) is clearly described; participants know period number.,0.9,0,Baseline VCM has no punishment phase.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this condition.,1,0,No positive reward option described in any condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,4,"Each period, participants could contribute up to four tokens; implies per-period endowment of 4 tokens.",1,N/R,"Paper reports cooperation measure and raw contributions indirectly, but not a simple fraction-of-endowment per condition.",0.3,N/R,Mean raw tokens contributed per condition are not tabulated.,0.2,N/R,Welfare relative to maximum can be inferred qualitatively but not as a precise 0–1 efficiency statistic per condition.,0.3,N/R,"Figure 4 gives approximate excess over Nash, but not exact numeric averages per condition in a table for VCM alone.",0.4,United States,"Participants are Middlebury College undergraduates in Vermont, USA.",1,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age statistics reported.,0.4,N/R,"Gender composition not reported, only that gender is used as a control in regressions.",0.3,Undergraduate students,Explicitly states they are Middlebury College undergraduate students.,1,On site,Lab sessions with z-Tree and physical dividers at the college.,1,"Heterogeneous types (Blue, Red) randomly reassigned each of 10 periods; Blue dominant strategy 2, social optimum 4; Red dominant strategy 0, social optimum 2; payoffs via non-linear tables; fixed matching; points converted at 50 points = $1 plus $5 show-up; practice rounds in homogeneous groups before main game.",Summarizes structural features of the public goods game common to all conditions and specific strategic details.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2019.06.019.md,Mechanism (type-report to binding mechanism),Condition labeled Mechanism where contribution is implemented via reported type to a mechanism.,1,Reporting/punishment institution: report type to binding mechanism that maps messages to fixed contributions; no voluntary numeric choice; no punishment,Differs from VCM by requiring a type message that deterministically sets contribution; no punishment stage.,1,True,Same human subject pool and lab setting as other conditions.,1,True,Between-condition lab experiment with controlled instructions.,1,True,Computerized z-Tree sessions on site.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation described.,1,False,No purely analytical experiment; empirical data only.,1,3,Same 3-person groups as baseline.,1,10,Same 10-period design as stated for all conditions.,1,1,Action set is five discrete contribution levels (0–4) mapped from five messages; not restricted to just 0 or all.,1,0,Conceptually same endowment structure as VCM; contributions are taken from a 4-token endowment based on reported message.,0.9,N/R,Non-linear payoff tables; no linear MPCR parameter.,1,0,"Only structured message ""I am a [Color] Type""; no free-form communication.",1,1,Group members see each individual’s contribution each period; in this condition message is perfectly inferred from contribution mapping.,1,0,No punishment phase present.,1,0,No rewarding institution used.,1,1,Subjects know there are 10 periods; typical in such z-Tree experiments.,0.9,0,Mechanism condition has no peer punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,4,"Mechanism contributions are 0–4 tokens, same as the underlying VCM game.",1,N/R,Paper does not provide contribution as fraction of endowment by condition separately.,0.3,N/R,No table of mean token contributions per period for Mechanism alone.,0.2,N/R,"Group profits above Nash are described (e.g., 27.5 points per period in Mechanism from Fig. 4) but maximum-payoff benchmark not numerically given to compute 0–1 efficiency.",0.4,159.5,Fig. 4 states group payoffs exceed Nash (132) by 27.5 points in Mechanism; 132 + 27.5 = 159.5 average per-period group payoff.,0.8,United States,Same subject pool across all conditions.,1,N/R,"No ages given, only undergraduate status.",0.4,N/R,Not reported by condition.,0.3,Undergraduate students,Same Middlebury College undergraduate pool.,1,On site,Same lab environment as VCM.,1,"Subjects must type a constrained message ""I am a [Color] Type""; mapping: Green→0, Yellow→1, Red→2, Purple→3, Blue→4 tokens. Mechanism automatically deducts corresponding contribution; messages are non-verifiable, lying allowed and emphasized; group members only see resulting contributions, not true types.",Specifics given in Section 2.2 and Fig. 2.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2019.06.019.md,"Revelation (type-report to group, no punishment)",Condition labeled Revelation where subjects report type to group members and then freely choose contribution; no punishment.,1,"Reporting/punishment institution: report type (one of five colors) to group members, then choose voluntary contribution; no punishment",Distinct from Mechanism because messages do not bind contributions; distinct from Punishment because no punishment stage.,1,True,Same human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions and fixed matching; controlled tasks.,1,True,Run in the lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation reported.,1,False,Empirical experiment rather than pure theory.,1,3,Groups of three throughout.,1,10,Same 10-period game.,1,1,"After messaging, participants ""can then voluntarily contribute any amount"" from 0 to 4 integer tokens.",1,0,Endowment still four tokens per period; contribution chosen after message.,0.9,N/R,Same heterogeneous non-linear payoff tables; no linear MPCR.,1,0,"Only restricted choice among five labels (Red, Blue, Green, Yellow, Purple); explicitly designed to avoid prosocial effects of free-form communication.",1,1,"End-of-period screen shows, for each anonymous group member, their message and contribution.",1,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No rewards implemented.,1,1,"Finite, known 10 periods; standard and implied.",0.9,0,"Revelation condition has no punishment phase, only reporting plus contribution.",1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,4,Same token endowment constraints as core game (0–4 contribution).,1,N/R,Only cooperation (% of distance from dominant strategy to optimum) is reported; no standard rate field.,0.3,N/R,Raw mean contribution by condition is not given in tokens.,0.2,N/R,"Relative welfare numbers provided (e.g., excess over Nash) but not enough to compute a clean 0–1 efficiency for each condition alone.",0.4,163,Fig. 4: Group payoffs exceed Nash (132) by 31 points in Revelation; 132 + 31 = 163.,0.8,United States,Same U.S. undergraduate subject pool.,1,N/R,No age stats.,0.4,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,Undergraduate students,Same Middlebury student pool.,1,On site,Same lab context as other conditions.,1,"Participants choose one of five labels as a message (Red, Blue, Green, Yellow, Purple). In Revelation these labels have no direct payoff meaning; Green/Yellow/Purple are described as equally meaningless and effectively represent refusal to reveal type. Messages are entered before contribution but revealed to others only at the end of the period alongside the actual contribution to avoid commitment/""promise"" effects.",Details given in Revelation condition description in Section 2.2.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2019.06.019.md,Punishment (type-report to group with costly peer punishment),"Condition labeled Punishment, identical to Revelation but with an added punishment stage.",1,"Reporting/punishment institution: report type to group, then choose contribution, then optional costly peer punishment with 1:3 cost-impact ratio",Differs from Revelation only by addition of punishment stage; message and contribution structure same.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment with added punishment phase.,1,True,Same lab setting using z-Tree.,1,False,Empirical data only.,1,False,No separate analytical model experiment.,1,3,Three-person fixed groups.,1,10,Same 10-period design.,1,1,Voluntary contribution 0–4 tokens after messaging.,1,0,Endowment is privately held initially; contributions decided each period.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear heterogeneous payoffs; no MPCR specified.,1,0,Only pre-defined color message; no free chat.,1,1,"End-of-period screen shows each group member’s message and contribution, then subjects decide punishments based on these.",1,1,Punishers select which specific group member to punish after viewing that member’s message-contribution pair; thus identities within group (anonymous IDs) are known at that stage.,0.9,0,No positive reward mechanism.,1,1,"Finite 10-period game is common knowledge, as elsewhere in the experiment.",0.9,1,Participants can pay to reduce earnings of group members after seeing behavior.,1,1,They can pay some amount to reduce the other’s earnings by three times the amount paid; so 1 token spent costs punisher 1.,1,3,Punishment ratio is 3:1; each unit spent reduces target’s payoff by 3.,1,0,Only punitive reductions available.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,4,Contribution decision still from 0–4 tokens; no indication endowment differs by condition.,1,N/R,Only relative cooperation and regression coefficients are provided; no normalized contribution rate per se.,0.3,N/R,Mean token contributions in Punishment condition not reported directly.,0.2,N/R,Text notes that Punishment groups earn slightly less than Nash but no exact maximum payoff stated to compute efficiency ratio.,0.4,N/R,"Fig. 4 indicates group profits are slightly below Nash 132, but exact average not given for Punishment.",0.4,United States,Same Middlebury U.S. undergraduate pool.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0.4,N/R,Not broken out.,0.3,Undergraduate students,Same recruitment source.,1,On site,Same lab infrastructure.,1,"After seeing each group member’s message and contribution, participants can spend any non-negative amount (bounded by earnings) to reduce one or both other members’ payoffs; each unit spent reduces the target’s payoff by three units. Evidence shows punishment is modest (~4 points) for under-contributing relative to message but much larger (~8–11.5 points) for blatantly false or mismatched messages; punishment does not significantly increase subsequent cooperation.",Summarizes punishment design and main punishment-related findings from Sections 2.2 and 3.4.,1
10.1016_j.euroecorev.2024.104795.md,"Experiment – Control Treatment (relevant false headline on Page 2, one non-corrective endorsing comment shown)",Main experiment has two treatments differing in exposure to corrective comments on Page 2. This row captures the Control Treatment as described under 'Treatments' and Fig. 2/Fig. A.1.,1,Exposure to corrective comments on relevant false headline: Control Treatment – only one non-corrective endorsing comment shown below the false headline on Page 2,Independent variable is exposure to corrective comments (meta-norm nudge). Control: no corrective comments; only one non-corrective comment.,1,True,Online experiment with 660 human participants recruited via Prolific.,1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to treatments with controlled stimuli; clearly a randomized experiment.,1,False,"Conducted online via Prolific/Qualtrics, not in a physical lab.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; all results from human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model tested as an experiment; paper reports empirical behavioral study.,1,1,Each participant interacts individually with a pre-generated thread; no strategic interaction with contemporaneous others in real time. Treated as single decision-maker environment.,0.9,4,"Participants go through four consecutive forum pages, each with an initial post and comments.",1,1,Commenting is free-form text and voluntary; no binary all-or-nothing contribution structure.,1,0,"No endowment or public goods contribution structure is used; default contribution proportion not applicable but per instructions must be numeric, so set to 0 with note.",0.01,N/R,No public goods game or linear contribution mechanism; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,Participants cannot communicate with each other; they only see pre-existing comments and optionally post their own.,1,0,"Participants see individual exemplar comments but no summaries of others’ earnings, punishments, or aggregate behavior.",1,0,"No identification of specific punishers; comments are anonymized, no user IDs reported.",1,0,No reward mechanism is implemented.,1,1,Participants are told they will go through four pages; structure of four consecutive pages is explicit in instructions.,0.9,1,Punishment is modeled as posting corrective comments to norm violators (fake news sharers); this is central to design and explicitly defined as social punishment.,1,0,"No monetary or token cost for commenting; only time/effort cost, which is outside this numeric token-cost concept.",0.9,0,"No parametrized punishment technology (e.g., x tokens reduce y tokens of target) is defined.",1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism for others’ behavior in the interaction stage.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism, so no cost.",1,N/A,"No reward mechanism, so no technology.",1,0,No per-round endowment of tokens/points; only a fixed participation payment and potential bonus unrelated to per-round allocation choices.,0.9,N/R,"Study measures probability of leaving corrective, endorsing, or any comment, not contributions to a public good.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amounts are reported.,1,N/R,No payoff-maximizing benchmark or efficiency metric is defined in the design.,1,N/R,No group payoff is defined or reported.,1,United Kingdom,Both Experiment and Pre-study recruited adult participants residing in the UK via Prolific.,1,Mean age ≈ 40.88 years; adults only,Section 2.2 and Appendix A.2 report mean age 40.88; participants are adult Prolific users.,1,Approximately 50.94% female; gender-balanced sample,Appendix A.2 notes sample was gender balanced with 50.94% female; Control had 46.75% female but overall is stated as balanced.,0.9,About 60.94% completed some form of tertiary education,Appendix A.2 reports 60.94% of sample with tertiary education; applies to both treatments.,0.9,Online,Experiment conducted online via Qualtrics with Prolific participants.,1,"Online forum-like task with four pages. Each page: one real-world news headline as initial post plus at least one pre-collected comment. On Page 2, participants see a false headline (A or B) followed by one non-corrective endorsing comment. Participants may optionally write a free-text comment on each page. Pre-study of 1000 UK adults measured prior beliefs about several headlines (accuracy, engagement, divisiveness) and was used to stratify randomization by ‘knowledge type’. Main outcomes: whether a participant leaves a corrective, endorsing, or any comment on Page 2 and Page 4; perceived social appropriateness (personal and normative expectations), and empirical expectations about corrections.","Summarized procedural details from Sections 2.1–2.2 and related appendices, specific to the Control condition (no corrective comments shown on Page 2).",1
10.1016_j.euroecorev.2024.104795.md,"Experiment – Corrective Treatment (relevant false headline on Page 2, one non-corrective plus two corrective comments shown)",Main experiment’s second treatment where participants observe corrective comments in response to the relevant false headline on Page 2.,1,"Exposure to corrective comments on relevant false headline: Corrective Treatment – one non-corrective endorsing comment plus two corrective comments shown below the false headline on Page 2 (three comments total, randomized order)",Independent variable is exposure to others’ corrective comments. Corrective Treatment adds two corrective comments to the same endorsing comment seen in Control.,1,True,Same online human-participant experiment as in Control Treatment.,1,True,Random assignment to Corrective vs Control conditions.,1,False,"Experiment run online, not in a lab setting.",1,False,No non-human simulations are part of this treatment.,1,False,No purely analytical (non-empirical) study here.,1,1,Each subject faces pre-defined content individually; no interactive multi-player game.,0.9,4,"Same four-page sequence (true, false, true, false headlines).",1,1,"Comment behavior is unconstrained text, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,No monetary endowment/public-good; proportion concept not applicable but numeric field set to 0.,0.01,N/R,No public good or payoff multiplier in design.,1,0,Participants cannot chat with one another; only see static comments.,1,0,Design does not display aggregate behavior or payoff summaries.,1,0,Corrective comments displayed are anonymous; no user identifiers are revealed.,1,0,No rewarding stage.,1,1,Participants are transparently taken through four pages; structure is part of the instructions/design.,0.9,1,"As in Control, punishment is operationalized as corrective comments against fake news; in this treatment participants additionally observe others’ punitive actions.",1,0,No token/monetary cost per corrective comment; only time/effort cost.,0.9,0,No formal mapping from punishment to payoffs is specified.,1,0,No positive incentive scheme for helping or compliance.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No per-round token endowment; incentives are participation fee plus accuracy-based bonuses.,0.9,N/R,"Outcomes are comment behaviors and norm perceptions, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No numeric contribution amount defined.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric is described.,1,N/R,No joint payoff calculated.,1,United Kingdom,Same sampling frame as Control: UK residents via Prolific.,1,Mean age ≈ 40.88 years; adults only,"Appendix A.2 gives overall mean age; randomization conditional on knowledge type, not age, so applies similarly here.",1,Approximately 50.94% female overall; Corrective treatment had 55.21% female,"Appendix A.2 shows 55.21% female in Corrective, 46.75% in Control; overall sample near-balanced.",0.95,About 60.94% completed some form of tertiary education (58.99% in Corrective treatment),"Appendix A.2 reports 58.99% tertiary education in Corrective and 62.85% in Control, 60.94% overall.",0.95,Online,Conducted online with Prolific participants using Qualtrics survey platform.,1,"Identical structure to Control: four-page forum-like task, with two true and two false headlines. On Page 2, participants see a randomly chosen false headline (A or B) as the relevant false headline, followed by one non-corrective endorsing comment and two corrective comments selected from a pre-defined set, shown in randomized order (three comments total). On other pages, structure is identical to Control (true headline + endorsing comment on Pages 1 and 3; non-relevant false headline + endorsing comment on Page 4). Participants may freely choose whether to comment on each page. Main behavioral outcomes (e.g., Ic, Ie, It) and norm-elicitation procedures are the same, used to estimate treatment effects of observed corrections on enforcement behavior and meta-norms.","Summarizes all aspects that differ from and are shared with Control, focusing on the addition of two corrective comments on Page 2.",1
10.1016_j.euroecorev.2024.104795.md,Alternative design – One-corrective vs one-endorsing comment on Page 2 (Constant number of comments),"Appendix A.10 describes an additional experimental treatment where number of comments on Page 2 is held constant at one, but their nature (corrective vs endorsing) is varied. Compared against original Control (one endorsing) to rule out confound of number of comments.",0.9,Nature of single observed comment on relevant false headline on Page 2: Corrective vs Endorsing (both with exactly one comment displayed),Alternative design manipulates whether the single visible comment is corrective instead of endorsing while keeping number of comments equal (one).,0.9,True,Described as additional data from 200 participants in a modified but still online human-subject experiment.,1,True,"Participants are randomly assigned to the new treatment where the single Page 2 comment is corrective, and compared to initial Control condition.",0.9,False,Also conducted online via Qualtrics/Prolific.,1,False,No simulations mentioned.,1,False,Not an analytical model; it is empirical.,1,1,Same individual exposure structure as main experiment: each participant sees static content.,0.9,4,"Design is said to 'exactly correspond to the Corrective treatment as described above, except' for the number of comments on Page 2; thus still four pages.",0.9,1,Participants still optionally post free-form comments; not all-or-nothing.,1,0,No endowment structure; field kept as 0 for numeric requirement.,0.01,N/R,No public goods or MPCR.,1,0,"No interactive chat, same as main design.",1,0,No summaries of others' behavior or payoffs shown.,1,0,Displayed corrective comment (when present) is anonymous.,1,0,No explicit rewards.,1,1,Structure mirrors main experiment with four pages; participants effectively see the finite sequence.,0.9,1,"Punishment remains defined as corrective comments; in the alternative treatment, a single corrective comment by 'others' is shown.",1,0,No token/monetary cost per corrective comment for participants.,0.9,0,No explicit payoff impact mapping.,1,0,No reward stage is introduced in the alternative design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No per-round endowment; incentive structure similar to main experiment.,0.8,N/R,Outcomes are comment behaviors not contributions.,1,N/R,No numeric contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric described.,1,N/R,No group payoffs.,1,United Kingdom,"Additional 200 participants are said to be in a setting 'corresponding to the Corrective treatment', implying same recruitment platform and country (Prolific UK adults) as main sample.",0.8,Adults recruited via Prolific; specific mean age not reported for this subsample,Text notes 200 additional participants but does not give age summary; likely similar Prolific UK adult pool.,0.5,N/R,Gender composition of the 200-participant additional sample is not reported.,0,N/R,Education distribution for this subsample not reported.,0,Online,Described as add-on to same online Qualtrics/Prolific setting as primary experiment.,1,"Alternative design mirrors the main experiment's Corrective treatment in all respects (four-page forum, same set of headlines and procedures) except Page 2 displays only one comment below the relevant false headline instead of three. In this additional treatment, that single comment is always corrective. Behavior is compared to the original Control Treatment, where Page 2 shows one non-corrective endorsing comment. Purpose is to test whether the treatment effect on corrective behavior is driven by the nature (corrective vs endorsing) rather than the number of comments. Main DV is again Ic (indicator for leaving a corrective comment on Page 2); Table A.10.1 and A.10.2 show that seeing a single corrective comment increases both probability and normative expectations of corrective behavior relative to a single endorsing comment.","Summarizes specifics from Section 3.3 and Appendix A.10, which describe this additional experimental manipulation.",1
10.1038_s41598-022-18797-2.md,First model – group dispersal range = 0,"Condition described where group dispersal range slider is set to 0; 15 repeated runs, cheaters do not share dispersal costs.",1,Group dispersal range = 0; dispersal costs = 8; mutation rate = 0; other parameters at default values,First model experiments vary group-dispersal-range with other listed defaults; this row corresponds to group-dispersal-range = 0.,1,False,Paper describes agent-based NetLogo simulation models with no human participants.,1,False,"Computational simulation, not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects.",1,False,No physical lab or human subjects; purely in silico.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent-based NetLogo simulation.,1,False,No formal closed-form analytical model; results generated numerically.,1,80,Number-Agents (started number) is 80 in first model experiments.,0.9,50000,"Text notes one run reached stop limit of 50,000 steps; appears to be the maximum tick limit used.",0.6,1,"Agents harvest resource amounts, not binary contribute-all-or-nothing decisions; contributions (harvest) are continuous up to limits.",0.7,N/A,No human players or explicit contribution game; resource harvesting in ABM does not map to initial contribution allocation.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or group return structure is defined; only local resource growth.,1,N/A,No human communication; simulation agents follow fixed rules.,1,0,No user interface for agents; they do not observe payoff summaries in a game-theoretic sense.,0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism or identities in this first model.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding mechanism.,1,0,Model runs for a fixed number of ticks internally; no human subjects to whom rounds are shown.,1,0,First model is about dispersal and resource harvesting; no punishment described.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this model.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this model.,1,0,No rewards mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N