10.1177_1046496404266546.md,"Unequal-access, punishment-rule condition",One of four experimental cells in the 2 (distribution of access: unequal/equal) × 2 (sanctioning system: equality rule/punishment rule) design.,1,"Group access distribution: unequal (1 high-access, 3 low-access members); Sanctioning system: punishment rule (single highest harvester over previous 5 trials fined $1.40)",Condition defined by unequal access manipulation and punishment-rule sanctioning system as described in the design and procedure.,1,True,Involves 208 human participants in 52 four-person groups.,1,True,Random assignment to 2×2 factorial conditions with controlled instructions and payoffs in a lab.,1,True,Conducted in laboratory cubicles with networked computers at a university.,1,False,"Although resource is computer-simulated, core study uses human decisions, not standalone agent-based simulation.",1,False,No formal mathematical modeling presented as a separate study.,1,4,Groups of four participants were used in all conditions.,1,25,Main analyses refer to 25 trials; some groups continued beyond 25 but DV definitions use 25th trial.,0.9,1,"Participants chose any integer number of trees within their access range (0 to quota), not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,"This is a common-pool harvesting task, not a contribution game; default-contribution concept not applicable but per instructions use 0 when resource is initially common and no private endowment is pre-allocated to public.",0.3,N/R,No linear public goods multiplier or MPCR is defined; payoffs depend on individual harvest and fines.,0,0,Participants were separated and explicitly could not communicate.,1,1,At end of every fifth trial they were told average number of trees taken and points earned by their group.,1,0,No indication that the identity of the fined participant was revealed; only personal feedback on own fine.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism in the design.,1,0,Participants were not told how many harvesting trials there would be.,1,1,Punishment rule imposes monetary fines for overharvesting.,1,1.4,Fine magnitude under punishment rule is $1.40 per block; cost borne directly by fined individual.,1,1.4,Each application of the punishment rule deducts $1.40 from the fined player’s earnings; we treat tech as fine magnitude per 'unit' of sanction.,0.7,0,No rewarding or positive sanction mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1.5,Each participant had an initial credit of 1.50 points (~$1.50).,1,N/R,"Study reports harvesting (extraction), not contributions; no normalized cooperation rate reported.",0,N/R,Only averages across conditions and by access level for subsets of trials are given; not separable by full condition over full game in a way consistent with this schema.,0.2,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff is reported or derivable without full time series.,0,7.33,"Mean total profit per group in punishment-rule conditions (collapsed over access) is $7.33; access distribution did not interact significantly, so we assign this as approximate for this condition.",0.6,Australia,"Participants were students at La Trobe University, Melbourne, Australia.",1,"18-50 (M=21.11, SD=4.97; majority ≤21)",Age range and mean with SD reported; majority 21 or younger.,1,"66 males, 142 females; majority of groups had 3 or more females",Counts and group-composition percentages provided.,1,University students (various disciplines and levels),All participants were student volunteers at an Australian university.,1,On site,Participants attended a university laboratory and sat in cubicles with networked computers.,1,"Common-pool resource (harvesting) game with a free-running, regenerating shared resource. Initial stock 85 trees, max 150; each tree worth 9 points (9 cents). One high-access member (0–6 trees per trial) and three low-access members (0–2 trees per trial). Resource regenerates 5% of remaining trees each trial. When total requested trees exceed remaining stock, remaining trees are split equally and game ends. Punishment rule: after each block of 5 trials, the participant who harvested the most trees in that block is fined $1.40 (if tied, one randomly selected). No communication; feedback each trial on own harvest and resource stock; every 5th trial on group averages.","Summarizes task structure, payoffs, regeneration, and feedback specifically for unequal-access punishment-rule groups.",1
10.1177_1046496404266546.md,"Unequal-access, equality-rule condition",One of four experimental cells: unequal access paired with equality-rule sanctioning system.,1,"Group access distribution: unequal (1 high-access, 3 low-access members); Sanctioning system: equality rule (all harvesters over previous 5 trials share equally in $1.40 fine; if only one harvester, that person pays full $1.40)",Condition defined by unequal access and equality-rule sanctioning instructions.,1,True,Uses human participants making harvesting and voting decisions.,1,True,Random assignment in controlled factorial design.,1,True,Laboratory cubicles with networked computers at La Trobe University.,1,False,Human-subject experiment despite computer-simulated resource.,1,False,No separate analytical modeling.,1,4,Groups of four in all cells.,1,25,Analyses of group profit and resource level focus on 25th trial.,0.9,1,Participants can choose any integer number of trees up to their quota.,1,0,Common-pool extraction context; no explicit contribution endowment.,0.3,N/R,No linear public good multiplier specified.,0,0,Participants could not communicate or meet.,1,1,Every fifth trial they saw average group harvest and earnings; equality-rule subjects also saw number of fellow harvesters.,1,0,No mention that fined individuals were identified.,0.9,0,No rewards.,1,0,Number of trials was not revealed.,1,1,Equality rule is a fining system (shared fine).,1,1.4,"Total fine per block is $1.40, divided among harvesters if more than one.",1,1.4,Total sanction per block is $1.40; treated as the magnitude of punishment applied per sanctioning event.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1.5,Each participant started with 1.50 points.,1,N/R,Only extraction (harvesting) is reported.,0,N/R,Harvesting amounts over 12 trials by access level are reported but not separable cleanly at condition level over the full game.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency ratio reported or inferable without full time series.,0,8.89,"Mean total profit for groups in equality-rule conditions (collapsed over access) is $8.89; access distribution did not interact significantly for profit, so we assign this as approximate.",0.6,Australia,"Participants from La Trobe University, Melbourne.",1,"18-50 (M=21.11, SD=4.97; majority ≤21)",Sample description applies to all conditions.,1,"66 males, 142 females; majority of groups had 3 or more females",Reported for full sample.,1,University students (various disciplines and levels),All participants were students.,1,On site,Laboratory-based study at a university.,1,"Common-pool resource game with free-running regenerating plantation: initial 85 trees, max 150, 5% regeneration each trial, each tree worth 9 points (9 cents). Unequal access: one high-access member (0–6 trees/trial) and three low-access members (0–2 trees/trial). If group requests exceed stock, remaining trees split equally and no more profit possible. Equality-rule sanctioning: after each block of 5 trials, all participants who harvested any trees share equally in a $1.40 fine; if only one harvester, that person pays full $1.40. Each trial, participants see own harvest and remaining trees; every 5 trials they see group average harvest and earnings and, in this condition, the number of other harvesters.",Summarizes rules and feedback specific to unequal-access equality-rule groups.,1
10.1177_1046496404266546.md,"Equal-access, punishment-rule condition",One of four experimental cells: equal access with punishment-rule sanctioning system.,1,Group access distribution: equal (all 4 members 0–3 trees per trial); Sanctioning system: punishment rule (single highest harvester over previous 5 trials fined $1.40),Condition defined by equal access manipulation and punishment rule.,1,True,Human-subject resource-harvesting experiment.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to factorial conditions.,1,True,Ran in on-site computer lab.,1,False,Not a purely computational model.,1,False,No analytical derivation as standalone study.,1,4,Groups of four participants.,1,25,25th trial used as reference for group profit and remaining trees.,0.9,1,Participants can choose 0–3 trees each trial.,1,0,"Common-pool extraction, no explicit contribution endowment.",0.3,N/R,No public-good multiplier defined.,0,0,No communication allowed.,1,1,Feedback on group averages every fifth trial.,1,0,No identities of fined individuals were said to be revealed.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Total number of trials was not disclosed.,1,1,Punishment rule fining system in place.,1,1.4,Fine amount is $1.40 to the highest harvester each block.,1,1.4,Fine magnitude per sanctioning event.,0.7,0,No reward system.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1.5,Initial credit of 1.50 points for all participants.,1,N/R,No normalized cooperation measure reported.,0,N/R,"Paper reports for equal-access participants that they harvested M=11.34 trees (SD=3.79) over 12 trials in punishment condition, but this is aggregated over both equal-access groups in punishment-rule condition; still, this is by access status not cell-level overall; to avoid misclassification we mark N/R.",0.3,N/R,Efficiency relative to maximum possible payoff not computed.,0,7.33,Groups in punishment-rule conditions averaged $7.33 profit; no access main effect or interaction reported for group profit.,0.6,Australia,"Sample from La Trobe University, Melbourne.",1,"18-50 (M=21.11, SD=4.97; majority ≤21)",Applies to full sample.,1,"66 males, 142 females; majority of groups had 3 or more females",Reported for combined sample.,1,University students (various disciplines and levels),All participants were students.,1,On site,Conducted in on-site lab with computers.,1,"Free-running common-pool resource: initial 85 trees, max capacity 150, 5% regeneration each trial. All four group members have equal access (0–3 trees per trial). Each tree worth 9 points. If group over-requests relative to stock, remaining trees divided equally and no further harvesting possible. Punishment rule: after each block of 5 trials, the participant who harvested the most trees in that block is fined $1.40 (ties broken randomly). Participants see their own harvest and remaining trees each trial and group average harvest and earnings every fifth trial.",Task structure and feedback described specifically for equal-access punishment-rule groups.,1
10.1177_1046496404266546.md,"Equal-access, equality-rule condition",Fourth cell in the 2×2 design: equal access and equality-rule sanction.,1,"Group access distribution: equal (all 4 members 0–3 trees per trial); Sanctioning system: equality rule (all harvesters over previous 5 trials share equally in $1.40 fine; if only one harvester, that person pays full $1.40)",Defined by equal access and equality-rule sanctioning.,1,True,Human participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to this condition.,1,True,Laboratory environment with computer terminals.,1,False,Not a simulation-only study.,1,False,No analytical model reported as a separate experiment.,1,4,Four-person groups.,1,25,25th trial used for group outcome analyses.,0.9,1,Each participant can choose any number 0–3 trees per trial.,1,0,Extraction setting; no default contribution pool.,0.3,N/R,No marginal per capita return for contributions defined.,0,0,Participants could not see or speak with each other.,1,1,"Every fifth trial, average group harvest and earnings were displayed; equality-rule condition also showed number of fellow harvesters.",1,0,No identification of particular fined individuals reported.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,0,Participants were not informed of total number of trials.,1,1,Equality rule imposes a fine on harvesters.,1,1.4,"Total fine per 5-trial block is $1.40, shared among those who harvested.",1,1.4,Total sanction magnitude per fining event.,0.7,0,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1.5,All participants started with 1.50 points.,1,N/R,"Study reports extraction, not normalized contribution.",0,N/R,"It reports that equal-access participants in equality-rule condition harvested M=13.67 trees (SD=7.18) over first 12 trials, but this is by access type and early rounds only; full-condition DV is not provided.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency measure provided.,0,8.89,"Group profit for equality-rule conditions reported as M=$8.89; no access interaction, so used for this cell.",0.6,Australia,All participants from an Australian university.,1,"18-50 (M=21.11, SD=4.97; majority ≤21)",Global sample description.,1,"66 males, 142 females; majority of groups had 3 or more females",Sample-level gender distribution.,1,University students (various disciplines and levels),Participants recruited as student volunteers.,1,On site,Described as laboratory-based at a university.,1,"Free-running common-pool resource with 85 initial trees, capacity 150, 5% regeneration per trial, trees worth 9 points (9 cents). All four participants have equal access of 0–3 trees per trial. If requested trees exceed remaining stock, remaining trees are split equally and game ends. Equality-rule sanctioning: after each 5-trial block, all participants who harvested any trees share equally a $1.40 fine, or one harvester pays full $1.40 if alone. Participants receive per-trial feedback on own harvest and remaining trees, and every fifth trial on group average harvest and earnings and the number of other harvesters.",Summarizes rules and feedback for equal-access equality-rule condition.,1
10.1007_s40822-020-00144-3.md,Lab – Low MPCR (0.30) Stage Game,Laboratory implementation has a session-level treatment with Low MPCR=0.30 affecting both the VCM and subsequent Stage game; this row captures the low-MPCR condition for human A–F dyads.,1,MPCR treatment in VCM and Stage game: Low MPCR = 0.30,"Text: “The MPCR is either Low (0.30) or High (0.75), depending on the session-level treatment.” This condition is the low-MPCR case.",1,True,Undergraduate subjects in NYU Abu Dhabi SSEL participated in the lab experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to sessions with different MPCRs and controlled game structure indicate a lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted at NYU Abu Dhabi’s Social Science Experimental Laboratory (SSEL).,1,False,No purely computational or agent-only simulations are described for this condition.,1,False,"Study uses experimental games, not closed-form analytical models.",1,4,"VCM groups of four, then Stage game payoffs explicitly reference two active and two passive players.",1,10,Subjects play the VCM for ten rounds and then the Stage game ten times.,1,1,"VCM and Stage game contributions (e.g., 0–20 in VCM, 5 vs 15 in Stage game) are amounts, not binary; players can choose any amount in VCM.",0.9,0,"In a standard VCM, endowment starts in private account and players decide how much to allocate; no indication of automatic public allocation.",0.9,0.3,Low MPCR treatment is explicitly 0.30.,1,0,No communication or chat among participants is mentioned; standard computerized anonymous VCM and Stage game.,0.9,1,Subjects see other players’ endowments before VCM decisions and see terminal node and payoffs each round in the Stage game.,0.9,0,Interactions are anonymous computer-mediated choices; no indication that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,No explicit reward mechanism; only boycott-style punishment.,1,1,Subjects complete quizzes ensuring understanding of “games” and “rounds”; typical oTree implementation and repeated-game framing strongly suggest they know there are 10 rounds.,0.8,1,Nature node with boycott cost is described as ‘boycott or punishment stage’.,1,10,"“Due to the costs associated with holding a boycott, the Activist's final payoff is reduced by a fixed cost (of ten points).”",1,1,"Boycott success changes Firm’s payoff structure but no per-point fine rate is specified; treat as 1 unit tech to indicate existence, not magnitude.",0.3,0,No mechanism where players can pay to increase others’ payoffs; only punishment via boycott.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,20,RET maximum of 20 points; “most subjects earn the same endowment” and Stage game example uses contributions summing to 40 for the two active players (consistent with 20 each).,0.8,N/R,Mean contribution rate in this specific Low-MPCR Stage game condition is not numerically reported.,0,N/R,Only example payoffs (15 vs 5) are given; no aggregate contributions by condition are reported.,0,N/R,No normalized welfare or efficiency measure is reported.,0,N/R,Only example node payoffs are provided; no average payoffs per treatment.,0,United Arab Emirates,"Lab sessions conducted at NYU Abu Dhabi; undergraduate subject pool is drawn from that campus. Institutional location is UAE, though students may be international.",0.7,N/R,Only described as standard undergraduate subject pool; no age statistics.,0,N/R,No gender composition for the lab pool is provided.,0,Primarily undergraduate students,Described as a standard undergraduate subject pool at NYUAD.,0.9,On site,"Sessions in the NYUAD SSEL, a physical laboratory.",1,"Three-part design: (1) real-effort typing task to earn endowments (max 20 points); (2) 10-round VCM in groups of four with random rematching and session-level MPCR=0.30; (3) 10 repetitions of an extensive-form Activist–Firm Stage game with two passive players, boycott stage with Nature p=0.75 success, boycott cost 10 for Activist, strategy method elicitation.",Concise summary of key design elements governing cooperation and punishment.,1
