10.3390_g12020041.md,CPG theoretical model condition,"The paper defines a class of games called 'CP against a Public Good (CPG)' characterized by parameter restrictions 0 < c < G, but does not specify a single numeric parameterization; treat this theoretical condition as one model.",0.71,Type of collective punishment game structure: CPG with parameters satisfying 0 < c < G (costly resistance producing a public good if j ≥ k).,Definition 2 labels games with 0 < c < G as 'CP against a Public Good (CPG)'; this is the main structural independent variable distinguishing this condition.,0.88,False,"The study is entirely theoretical, with no human participants or empirical data collection.",1,False,No controlled experiment is run; only game-theoretic modeling and equilibrium analysis are presented.,1,False,There is no lab or field experiment; only analytical modeling.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; only analytical derivations and illustrative figures.,0.96,True,"The paper develops a formal game-theoretic model, derives equilibria, and uses analytical equilibrium selection methods (QRE limits, Linear Tracing Procedure).",1,5,General model uses n agents; one explicit parameterization for illustration in Figure 1 uses n=5. Use this as the clearest concrete configuration.,0.7,1,"The CP game is a static, two-stage game with a single simultaneous choice by agents; no repeated rounds are modeled.",0.96,0,"Each agent’s decision is binary (resist vs comply), i.e., all-or-nothing contribution to resistance rather than a continuous amount.",0.97,N/A,"No human subjects, and the model is not framed as a contribution of an endowment; binary actions are resistance vs compliance, not reallocations of an initial fund.",1,N/R,"The model is a threshold public good / public bad game with parameters G, c, k, n, and x; there is no linear public good multiplier or MPCR specified.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; purely analytical.,1,0,The model does not include feedback or summaries of others’ payoffs or actions; agents form expectations but do not observe histories or summaries.,0.86,N/A,Punishment is collective and anonymous within the model; no identities or observability assumptions about individual punishers or punished are modeled.,0.95,N/A,"The model features only punishment x, not rewards.",1,0,The game is one-shot; there is no concept of multiple rounds or displaying remaining rounds.,0.96,1,"By definition, the CP game includes a collective punishment x ∈ [0, M] determined by the principal.",1,N/R,The model specifies a punishment level x directly affecting utilities but does not model a separate per-unit cost of punishment to the punisher like in standard PGG punishment; such a cost is not parameterized.,0.78,N/R,There is no linear technology mapping punishment spending to impact; x itself is the per-agent loss when CP is triggered.,0.84,0,"The model analyzes only collective punishment, not reward mechanisms; rewards are mentioned only in literature review.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/R,"The model is not framed in terms of monetary endowments or token amounts; only utilities (0, -c, -x, G-c, G-x) are specified.",0.86,N/R,No empirical data; the paper derives equilibrium probabilities of resistance π but does not report normalized contribution rates from experiments.,0.9,N/R,No numeric contributions or resistance counts from human data are reported; only symbolic probabilities and parameters.,0.9,N/R,Theoretical welfare comparisons are discussed qualitatively but no explicit normalized efficiency metric (0–1) is computed or reported.,0.9,N/R,"Group payoffs are given symbolically (depending on j, x, c, G) but no single numeric outcome is reported as a result variable for a specific parameter set.",0.86,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,N/A,There are no human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical/analytical without any human-subjects component.,1,"Principal-agent threshold game with n agents and one principal. Principal chooses collective punishment level x ∈ [0, M]. Each agent chooses resist (bear cost c) or comply. Let j be number of resisters. If j = 0, all utilities 0. If 0 < j < k, CP is applied and agents receive utility -c - x if resisting and -x if complying. If j ≥ k, regime breaks down and a public good of size G is produced: resisters get G - c, compliers get G. Two subclasses: CPG with 0 < c < G (costly resistance producing a public good) and CPB with G < c < 0 (profitable resistance producing a public bad). The paper analyzes symmetric mixed-strategy equilibria and equilibrium selection via Quantal Response Equilibria limits and Harsanyi-Selten Linear Tracing Procedure.","Summarizes the formal game definition (Definition 1, Definition 2, and subsequent analysis) relevant to cooperation and punishment structure.",1
10.3390_g12020041.md,CPB theoretical model condition,The paper defines a second class of games called 'CP against a Public Bad (CPB)' characterized by parameter restrictions G < c < 0; treat this as a distinct theoretical condition.,0.71,Type of collective punishment game structure: CPB with parameters satisfying G < c < 0 (profitable resistance producing a public bad if j ≥ k).,Definition 2 labels games with G < c < 0 as 'CP against a Public Bad (CPB)'; this structural difference is the key independent variable for this condition.,0.88,False,No human subjects are involved; the analysis is entirely theoretical.,1,False,No experimental manipulation with participants; only comparative statics and analytical equilibrium selection.,1,False,No lab or field environment is used; methods are analytical.,1,False,Figure 1 is illustrative based on analytical functions with chosen parameters; no separate simulation model or Monte Carlo analysis is described.,0.92,True,"The CPB condition is analyzed via formal game-theoretic derivations, characterizing equilibria and their selection (Proposition 1–3, Conclusions 2 and 4).",1,5,"General model allows arbitrary n, but Figure 1 for CPB explicitly uses n=5 (with k=3, c=-0.15, G=-1) to illustrate equilibria.",0.7,1,"As with CPG, CPB is modeled as a single-shot simultaneous-move game among agents after the principal sets x.",0.96,0,Agents choose a binary action: resist or comply; this is an all-or-nothing decision rather than a continuous contribution level.,0.97,N/A,No human-subject public good experiment with initial endowments; the model is in utility space with binary actions.,1,N/R,The model describes threshold-induced externalities (public bad) instead of a linear MPCR-type public good technology.,1,N/A,No empirical implementation or communication mechanism; purely theoretical.,1,0,Agents base decisions on expectations over others’ strategies; there is no modeled feedback of payoff summaries.,0.86,N/A,"Punishment is applied collectively and automatically, with no identity revelation modeled.",0.95,N/A,"The CPB game, like CPG, only includes punishment x and no reward mechanism.",1,0,Single-shot game; there is no notion of multiple rounds to display.,0.96,1,"By definition, collective punishment x is the central instrument considered by the principal in both CPG and CPB games.",1,N/R,The model includes punishment level x affecting agent utilities but not an explicit cost-of-punishing parameter for the principal or others.,0.78,N/R,x itself determines the loss each agent suffers under CP; no linear mapping from spent points to punishment impact is defined.,0.84,0,"Only punishment, not rewards, is modeled in the CPB structure.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is present.,1,N/R,"Utilities are modeled directly via c, G, x; there is no explicit token endowment or budget constraint.",0.86,N/R,"Equilibria are probabilities of resistance π, but no empirical contributions or normalized rates from experiments are measured.",0.9,N/R,No numeric contribution or resistance counts from data; only symbolic equilibrium values.,0.9,N/R,"While the paper qualitatively discusses efficiency and welfare effects, it does not provide a normalized efficiency index.",0.9,N/R,Group payoffs are given symbolically as functions of parameters; no specific numeric outcome is singled out as a DV.,0.86,N/A,No human participants are involved in this theoretical condition.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a purely analytical game-theoretic model without empirical implementation.,1,"Principal-agent threshold game with n agents and one principal. Principal chooses collective punishment x ∈ [0, M]. Each agent decides whether to 'resist' (individually profitable action generating a public bad if sufficiently many resist) or comply. Let j be the number of resisting agents. If j = 0, all agents have utility 0. If 0 < j < k, collective punishment is applied: resisting agents get utility -c - x, complying agents get -x. If j ≥ k, the regime or standard is violated and a public bad with externality G (G < 0) is produced; utilities are G - c for resisters and G for compliers. In CPB, parameters satisfy G < c < 0, so individual resistance has private benefit (-c) but generates negative externalities (-G) when collective resistance is high. The paper derives multiple equilibria and examines how varying x affects equilibrium resistance probability under Quantal Response Equilibrium limits and the Linear Tracing Procedure.",Summarizes the formal CPB game definition and how it differs structurally from CPG while retaining the same analytical framework.,1
10.3390_g12010017.md,Fractional punishment optional public good game – analytical model,Paper develops a single mathematical model of an optional public good game with a fractional punishment parameter d; no separate empirical conditions are run.,0.96,"Punished fraction of defectors d (0 ≤ d ≤ 1), with analytical thresholds d1 = (n − r)/(r(n − 1)) and d2 = (n − r)/(nσ + n − r); examples use n = 5, r = 3, σ = 1, and specific values d ∈ {0, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0.16667, 0.2, 0.22, 0.25, 0.2857, 0.5, 0.99}",Independent variable explicitly defined as parameter d controlling the fraction of defectors punished; text and figures analyze dynamics for various d values and thresholds.,1,False,Study is a theoretical evolutionary game/replicator-dynamics analysis without human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment is conducted; only mathematical analysis and illustrative phase diagrams.,1,False,No laboratory or online experiment with participants is reported.,1,False,"While figures show phase portraits and numerical solutions, the core method is analytical derivation of replicator dynamics; no standalone numerical simulation study is defined as a separate method.",0.7,True,"Paper derives payoff functions, replicator equations, equilibrium conditions, lemmas, and theorems analytically.",1,5,"Model defines group size n; figures and numerical examples repeatedly use n = 5 (e.g., captions: 'Parameters N=5, r=3, σ=1').",0.95,0,"Infinite/continuous-time evolutionary replicator dynamics, not a finite repeated game; use 0 to denote N/A for round count.",0.9,1,Each player 'must decide if either contributes or not with a positive amount c to the common pool' implying binary cooperate/defect decision; model does not allow partial continuous contributions beyond c vs 0. Encoded as 1 meaning players CAN choose any of the discrete options but not an all-or-nothing constraint on continuous amounts; schema forces binary so closest is 1.,0.6,0,"Endowment is implicitly in private possession; cooperators decide to pay cost c=1 into pool, defectors do not. So initial endowment fully private.",0.9,N/R,"Marginal per capita return (multiplier divided by group size) could be r/n, but they never explicitly define MPCR or interpret r/n as such; to avoid over-inference this is left N/R.",0.5,0,No human players or communication stage; purely analytical model.,1,0,No informational feedback structure for individuals is modeled; only payoffs and strategy frequencies.,1,0,Punishment is institutional and random over defectors; identities are irrelevant in the model and never discussed.,1,0,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics; no notion of displaying remaining rounds.,1,1,"Core of the paper is introducing punishment of a randomly selected fraction of defectors, parameterized by d.",1,N/R,"Cost structure to individuals is not modeled as a per-unit cost borne by punishers; punishment is funded by an institutional fee pool, but no per-token cost parameter is specified.",0.4,N/R,"Effect of punishment is described qualitatively as reducing sanctioned defectors' payoff to zero, but no linear per-unit mapping from punishment investment to payoff reduction is parameterized as 'technology'.",0.6,0,Model only includes punishment of defectors; no positive rewards for cooperators are specified.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,1,Each contribution is c = 1; effectively each cooperator pays 1 unit; treated as per-round endowment normalized to 1.,0.8,N/R,"Theoretical model tracks frequencies of strategies (x cooperators, y defectors, z loners) as state variables, but no single numerical outcome for contribution rate is summarized as a dependent variable.",0.5,N/R,"While each cooperator contributes c=1, the paper does not report aggregate or average contributions as an outcome variable per condition.",0.5,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure (group payoff / maximum possible) is computed or summarized.,0.5,N/R,"Payoff functions px, py, pz are derived, but no specific numerical group payoff outcomes are given for parameter sets as a dependent variable.",0.5,N/A,No human participants; purely analytical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is purely theoretical/evolutionary game analysis without empirical data.,1,"Optional public good game with three strategies: cooperators (contribute c = 1), defectors (contribute 0), and loners (receive fixed payoff σ). Group size n (examples use n = 5). Total contributions by cooperators are multiplied by r (1 < r < n) and split equally among participants (cooperators and defectors). A fraction d of defectors is randomly selected and has payoff reduced to zero; remaining defectors get standard public good payoff. Population evolution follows continuous-time replicator dynamics over strategy frequencies (x, y, z). Loners receive payoff σ with 0 < σ < r − 1. Analysis focuses on equilibria, stability, and thresholds d1 and d2 affecting global attraction of full cooperation.",Summarizes core structural features of the modeled game as described in Sections 2 and 3.,1
10.3390_g11040045.md,Analytical model – finitely repeated game: Homo oeconomicus vs emotional player,Paper analyzes a finitely repeated prisoner’s dilemma between one Homo oeconomicus and one emotional player as a distinct interaction case.,0.93,"Player type composition: Player 1 = Homo oeconomicus (neutral, no transitions), Player 2 = emotional (3 states F/N/H with deterministic transitions based on last observed action). Time horizon T finite (e.g., T=2, T=3, general T>2).",Section 3.1 explicitly defines this pairing and compares different finite horizons.,0.96,False,No human participants or data; purely theoretical game-theoretic analysis.,1,False,No controlled experiment is conducted; only analytical derivations.,1,False,"No lab or field setting, purely theoretical.",1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported.,1,True,Model is solved using analytical stochastic-game methods and equilibrium proofs.,1,2,"Model specifies two-player interaction I={1,2}.",1,2,"Within this finitely repeated case, conditions are first worked out explicitly for T=2; T=3 and T>2 are discussed but 2-period game is the clearest concrete instance.",0.7,1,"Actions are binary (C or D), not continuous contribution levels; mapping to contribution framework treated as general discrete choice so coded as allowing any of the two actions rather than mandatory full contribution.",0.6,N/A,"This is a prisoner’s dilemma with fixed payoffs, not a contribution game with an endowment.",1,N/R,No public good or linear multiplier is defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication protocols not applicable.,1,0,Stage game payoffs depend only on current actions and states of mind; no feedback summaries are modeled.,0.8,N/A,No explicit costly punishment mechanism distinct from choosing D.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism is modeled.,1,1,Finite horizon T is common knowledge in the analysis of the finitely repeated game.,0.9,0,Punishment is implicit via choosing D within PD payoffs; there is no separate costly punishment technology.,0.95,N/A,No separate punishment action with token costs exists.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology defined.,1,0,No distinct costly reward stage or action.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,"Only normal-form payoffs (b,c,d,0) are given; no explicit per-period endowment.",0.8,N/R,Outcomes are utilities/payoffs from C/D actions; no aggregate contribution rate reported.,1,N/R,"Model does not specify numeric contributions, only discrete actions.",1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios are computed; only utility vectors and equilibrium conditions.,0.8,N/R,Group payoff expressions are implicit (sum of utilities) and not given as final numeric outcomes.,0.7,N/A,Theoretical model with no participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical model without empirical implementation.,1,"Two-player prisoner’s dilemma with payoffs: (C,C) → (c,c); (D,D) → (d,d); (C,D) → (0,b) for the cooperator and defector respectively, with b>c>d>0. Player 2’s utility adds α times the other’s material payoff when friendly and −γ times when hostile. Emotional player’s state transitions deterministically from neutral to friendly after observing C, to hostile after observing D; hostile transitions back to neutral after observing C, otherwise stays.",Summarizes game form and emotional-state structure from Section 2 and Figure 1.,1
10.3390_g11040045.md,Analytical model – finitely repeated game: two emotional players (deterministic transitions),Section 3.1 separately analyzes a finitely repeated interaction between two emotional players.,0.96,"Player type composition: Player 1 = emotional, Player 2 = emotional, both with three states F/N/H and deterministic transitions based on last observed action. Time horizon T finite with T≥2.","Explicitly described under ‘Finally, consider the finitely repeated interaction between two emotional players.’",0.96,False,No experimental or observational data with humans.,1,False,This is an analytical treatment of a stochastic game.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,False,No computer simulations are reported.,1,True,"Properties (e.g., Proposition 3) are derived analytically.",1,2,Model is a two-player game.,1,2,"Proposition 3 states T≥2; no single concrete T is fixed, but minimal finite horizon is 2, used here as representative.",0.6,1,"Action space is {C,D} per period; mapped as discrete cooperation/defection rather than contribution levels.",0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game with an endowment but a PD with fixed outcome payoffs.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,"Histories affect states, but there is no modeling of summary feedback displays.",0.8,N/A,No separate costly punishment stage is implemented.,1,N/A,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite horizon T is common knowledge in finitely repeated game analysis.,0.9,0,"Punishment is via D within the PD payoffs, not a designated punishment technology.",0.95,N/A,No explicit per-unit punishment cost parameter.,1,N/A,No punishment-technology multiplier defined.,1,0,No distinct reward action beyond cooperation choice and its payoffs.,1,N/A,No costly reward instrument.,1,N/A,No reward technology is modeled.,1,N/R,"Only normal-form payoffs are specified, not initial endowments.",0.8,N/R,"No reported contribution statistics; only equilibrium play (C,C) under certain conditions.",1,N/R,Actions are binary and payoffs are symbolic; no numeric contribution level.,1,N/R,Efficiency is discussed qualitatively but not quantified.,0.7,N/R,"Utilities like (c, c+αc) are symbolic; no fixed numeric group payoff is reported for this condition.",0.7,N/A,Model uses no participants.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,Theoretical analysis only.,1,"Same PD material payoffs as baseline. Both players have identical emotional utility functions u_i = π_i + φ(m_i)π_j with φ(F)=α>0, φ(N)=0, φ(H)=−γ<0. Deterministic transitions per Figure 1: from neutral to friendly after observing C, to hostile after D; from friendly stay if C, to hostile if D; from hostile to neutral if C, stay if D. Proposition 3 shows that if c(1+α)>b then full cooperation (C,C) can be sustained in all periods via strategic manipulation of states of mind.",Summarizes structure and key equilibrium property for this specific player-type composition.,1
10.3390_g11040045.md,Analytical model – infinitely repeated game: Homo oeconomicus vs emotional player (Grim Trigger),Section 3.2.1 analyzes an infinitely repeated interaction between one Homo oeconomicus and one emotional player under Grim Trigger strategies as a specific condition.,0.97,"Player type composition: Player 1 = Homo oeconomicus, Player 2 = emotional (F/N/H states). Strategy profile: Grim Trigger (both start cooperating and revert to (D,D) forever after any deviation). Horizon: infinite with common discount factor δ.","Explicitly identified in 3.2.1 as ‘First consider the game between one Homo oeconomicus, player 1, and one emotional player, player 2’ with Grim Trigger.",0.98,False,The section presents theoretical equilibrium conditions; no human data.,1,False,No designed behavioral experiment; purely analytical proof.,1,False,No lab implementation.,1,False,No simulations reported; analysis uses inequalities and folk theorem.,1,True,Uses stochastic game theory and one-shot deviation principle to characterize equilibria.,1,2,Game defined for players 1 and 2.,1,N/R,Interaction is infinite-horizon; number of rounds is unbounded rather than a finite T.,1,1,Choice each period is between C and D; no finer contribution grid.,0.7,N/A,Framework is not a contribution game with an endowment.,1,N/R,No public-good multiplier defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects or communication channel modeled.,1,0,Players observe full action history (perfect monitoring) but no separate earnings summary displays are modeled.,0.8,N/A,No explicit costly punishment stage distinct from the main action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Infinite horizon; there is no terminal round or remaining-rounds count.,0.9,0,"‘Punishment’ is implemented via switching to D in the PD, not via a separate punishment technology with cost multipliers.",0.95,N/A,No punishment tokens or cost-per-unit parameters.,1,N/A,No formal punishment technology exists.,1,0,"Rewards are implicit via choosing C, not separate costly reward actions.",1,N/A,No distinct reward cost specified.,1,N/A,No reward technology is defined.,1,N/R,"Stage-game payoffs are parameters (b,c,d,0); no underlying endowment is mentioned.",0.8,N/R,Theoretical condition for cooperation but no numeric rate is reported.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution units are modeled.,1,N/R,"Although cooperative and non-cooperative outcomes are compared qualitatively, no explicit efficiency ratio is stated.",0.7,N/R,"Utilities like (c, c+αc) are symbolic, not instantiated numerically for this condition.",0.7,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,No human,Purely theoretical infinite-horizon game.,1,"Infinite-horizon PD with same material payoffs. Grim Trigger: both choose C as long as no one deviates; any deviation leads to perpetual (D,D). Player 1 (Homo oeconomicus) has deviation constraint c ≥ (1−δ)b + δd as in the standard PD. Emotional player’s constraint is (1−δ)c + δ(1+α)c ≥ (1−δ)b + (1−δ)δ(1+α)d + δ²(1−γ)d. Minimum δ for subgame perfection is determined by the Homo oeconomicus, and Proposition 4 shows δ̲_H^GT = δ̲^GT (same as benchmark with two Homo oeconomicus).",Condenses the main technical characterization specific to this condition.,1
10.3390_g11040045.md,Analytical model – infinitely repeated game: two emotional players (Grim Trigger),Same Grim Trigger strategy profile analyzed for the case of two emotional players in Section 3.2.1 and Proposition 5.,0.97,"Player type composition: both players emotional (three states of mind, deterministic transitions). Strategy profile: Grim Trigger. Horizon: infinite, discount factor δ.",Described as ‘Next consider the game between two emotional players’ with Grim Trigger.,0.97,False,No subject data.,1,False,Theoretical equilibrium analysis only.,1,False,Not run in a lab.,1,False,No computational experiments.,1,True,Subgame perfection conditions derived using inequalities like (4).,1,2,Two players in the model.,1,N/R,Game is infinitely repeated.,1,1,Binary action choice C or D only.,0.7,N/A,No contribution endowment.,1,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,0,Perfect monitoring of actions but no mention of separate summary displays.,0.8,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Infinite horizon; no finite number of rounds to display.,0.9,0,"Punishment is embedded in choosing D with emotional utilities, not a dedicated punishment technology.",0.95,N/A,No cost-per-punishment parameter.,1,N/A,No punishment multiplier.,1,0,Choosing C yields higher payoffs but is not a separate costly reward operation.,1,N/A,No reward parameter.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Payoffs described without specifying endowments.,0.8,N/R,No empirical contribution statistics; only equilibrium cooperation possibilities.,1,N/R,No numeric contribution per se.,1,N/R,"Sets of feasible and individually rational utilities are described, but efficiency is not reported as a scalar DV for this condition alone.",0.7,N/R,"Various utility vectors (e.g., cooperative (c,c+αc)) mentioned symbolically, not as final numeric group payoffs.",0.7,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Analytical stochastic game.,1,"Under Grim Trigger, both emotional players must satisfy condition (4): (1−δ)c + δ(1+α)c ≥ (1−δ)b + (1−δ)δ(1+α)d + δ²(1−γ)d. Proposition 5 states the minimal discount factor δ̲_E^GT for two emotional players is lower than δ̲^GT for two Homo oeconomicus and decreases in α and γ, so emotions facilitate sustaining cooperation under Grim Trigger.",Highlights the main comparative-static result for emotional vs standard players under Grim Trigger.,1
10.3390_g11040045.md,"Analytical model – infinitely repeated game: Homo oeconomicus vs emotional player (mutual minmax, deterministic transitions)",Section 3.2.2 considers mutual minmax strategies for the pairing of one Homo oeconomicus and one emotional player with deterministic emotional transitions.,0.96,"Player type composition: Player 1 = Homo oeconomicus, Player 2 = emotional with states F/N/H. Strategy: mutual minmax with punishment length L>1 (both play C in cooperative phase; if deviation occurs, both play D for L periods, then return to C given conditions). Horizon: infinite, discount factor δ.",Proposition 6 explicitly refers to ‘one Homo oeconomicus and one emotional player’ using mutual minmax with L>1.,0.97,False,"Analytical equilibrium derivations, no participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment is run.,1,False,Not a lab or field experiment.,1,False,No simulations are reported.,1,True,"Uses inequalities (7),(9)-(12) etc. and Proposition 6.",1,2,Two players considered.,1,N/R,Infinite-horizon repeated game.,1,1,"Each period, choose either C or D.",0.7,N/A,No explicit endowment or partial contributions.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0,Perfect monitoring of actions but no separate summary display modeled.,0.8,N/A,Punishment realized as both choosing D; no separate punishment stage revealing identities.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards stage.,1,0,Infinite horizon.,0.9,0,Mutual minmax uses action D rather than a dedicated costly punishment operator; no punishment tech parameters.,0.95,N/A,No per-unit punishment cost parameter is defined.,1,N/A,No punishment technology parameter.,1,0,"Rewards embedded in cooperative payoffs, not modeled as separate costly rewards.",1,N/A,No such parameter.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Endowments not described.,0.8,N/R,No numeric contributions are reported; only strategy conditions.,1,N/R,No explicit contributions.,1,N/R,Relative ease/difficulty of sustaining cooperation is discussed instead of explicit efficiency indices.,0.7,N/R,Symbolic payoff vectors only.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Theoretical infinite-horizon model.,1,"Mutual minmax: players choose C in cooperative phase; after any deviation, play (D,D) for L periods, then resume C. For the Homo oeconomicus, the no-deviation condition is b−c ≤ (c−d)(δ+…+δ^L) as in the benchmark. For the emotional player, additional terms appear due to friendly (α) and hostile (γ) utilities, e.g. for deviation in neutral state with L>2: b−c ≤ (1+α)(c−d)δ + ((1+α)c−(1−γ)d)(δ²+…+δ^L) + δ^{L+1}(α+γ)c + δ^{L+2}αc. Proposition 6: if αb < (1+α)d and condition (13) does not hold, then the minimal discount factor δ̲_H^MM exceeds δ̲^MM from the benchmark; emotions can make cooperation harder to sustain under mutual minmax.",Summarizes strategy structure and main comparative result for this player-type pairing.,1
10.3390_g11040045.md,"Analytical model – infinitely repeated game: two emotional players (mutual minmax, deterministic transitions)",Section 3.2.2 and Proposition 7 analyze mutual minmax with two emotional players.,0.97,"Player type composition: both players emotional with F/N/H states and deterministic transitions. Strategy: mutual minmax with punishment length L>1. Horizon: infinite, discount factor δ. Transition-based emotions with parameters α>0 and γ>0.",Described as ‘Next consider the case of two emotional players’ and formalized in Proposition 7.,0.97,False,No human experiment or data.,1,False,Only theoretical modeling.,1,False,No lab study.,1,False,No computational experiments.,1,True,"Equilibrium conditions derived using detailed inequalities (7),(9),(10),(11),(12),(13).",1,2,Two emotional players.,1,N/R,Infinite repetition.,1,1,Discrete C or D each period.,0.7,N/A,No endowment-based contributions.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,0,Perfect monitoring but no explicit summary display; analytical game.,0.8,N/A,No explicit costly punishment separate from D.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward.,1,0,Infinite-horizon game; no fixed T.,0.9,0,‘Punishment’ defined behaviorally as mutual D; no separate punishment technology.,0.95,N/A,No separate punishment tokens or costs.,1,N/A,No punishment multiplier parameter.,1,0,Rewards via C only; no special reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,Not defined.,1,N/R,No endowment mentioned.,0.8,N/R,No numeric contributions; only conditions for sustaining cooperation.,1,N/R,Not a contribution model.,1,N/R,"Comparative statements about ease of sustaining cooperation are given, not explicit efficiency numbers.",0.7,N/R,"Utility payoffs for strategies are algebraic expressions, not instantiated numerically.",0.7,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,Analytical theoretical model only.,1,"Mutual minmax with emotional players has conflicting emotional effects: friendly state makes deviation more costly but also punishing more painful; hostile state both obstructs returning to cooperation and strengthens threat credibility. For L>1, no-deviation conditions are (9), (10), and (12). Proposition 7: If αb < (1+α)d and condition (13) holds, then the minimal discount factor δ̲_E^MM is less than δ̲^MM for two Homo oeconomicus and decreases in γ (emotions facilitate cooperation). If (13) does not hold, δ̲_E^MM > δ̲^MM (emotions obstruct cooperation).",Captures the key comparative results for two emotional players under mutual minmax.,1
10.3390_g11040045.md,"Analytical model – infinitely repeated game: two emotional players with stochastic transitions (mutual minmax, Appendix A)",Appendix A extends the mutual minmax analysis to emotional players with stochastic state transitions (parameter v).,0.97,"Player type composition: two emotional players with stochastic transition probabilities p=q=w=1, v<1 determining speed of leaving hostile state. Strategy: mutual minmax with punishment length L>1. Horizon: infinite with discount factor δ. Transition parameter v varied (resentfulness).",Appendix A defines stochastic transitions and analyzes how varying v affects the minimal δ for mutual minmax equilibrium (Proposition A1).,0.97,False,No empirical implementation; pure theory.,1,False,No experiment or data collection.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,False,"While probabilities are involved, results are closed-form; no numerical simulation is reported.",0.95,True,Inequalities (A1)-(A3) and Proposition A1 give analytical comparative statics in v.,1,2,Two emotional players.,1,N/R,Infinite repeated game.,1,1,Actions per period are C or D.,0.7,N/A,Not a contribution setting.,1,N/R,No MPCR.,1,N/A,Theoretical model; no communication protocol.,1,0,Histories are known but no explicit summary displays.,0.8,N/A,No separate punishment stage.,1,N/A,No distinct reward action.,1,0,Infinite horizon; number of rounds not finite.,0.9,0,"Mutual minmax punishment is via D within PD, not via separate punishment technology.",0.95,N/A,No punishment technology defined.,1,N/A,No punishment multiplier.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism distinct from cooperative play.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,Not applicable.,1,N/R,No endowment mentioned.,0.8,N/R,No empirical or numeric contribution outcomes.,1,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/R,"Appendix focuses on how v affects minimal δ, not on efficiency numbers.",0.7,N/R,No explicit numeric group payoff level is derived.,0.7,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human data.,1,No human,All results are theoretical and pertain to abstract players.,1,"Emotional players with stochastic transitions (Figure A1): after observing C, they move from hostile to neutral with probability v (0<v≤1), making them more or less resentful. For L>1, conditions (A1)-(A3) generalize deterministic-case inequalities (9),(10),(12) by incorporating v. Proposition A1: if αb < (1+α)d, the minimal discount factor δ̲_EES^MM required for mutual minmax equilibrium is decreasing in v. More resentful players (lower v, longer hostile spells) require more patience to sustain cooperation.",Summarizes how stochastic transitions and v affect equilibrium under mutual minmax.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.102.052414.md,Version I – Consensual punishment with coercion (p = 1),The paper explicitly defines Version I as the case where cooperators are compulsory to comply with the electoral outcome (p=1) under consensual punishment.,1,"Cooperators' preference for punishing defectors q ∈ [0,1]; consensus threshold T ∈ [0,1] for pool punishment employment; other parameters fixed (e.g., r=3, c=1, G=B=0.7, γ=β=0.7, N and M as given in specific analyses).","Version I systematically varies q and T while treating others as fixed parameters in analytical and numerical exploration of cooperation abundance (e.g., Fig. 4).",0.9,False,No human participants; the work is a theoretical evolutionary game model analyzed analytically and numerically.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical model with Markov-chain and fixation probability analysis.,1,False,No lab setting or human subjects; only mathematical and computational analysis.,1,True,"They compute stationary distributions and abundances via a frequency-dependent Moran process using numerical solutions of Markov chains (e.g., ‘calculated analytically based on the numerical solution of equation φA=φ’).",1,True,They derive closed-form expressions for expected payoffs πC and πD and conditions for cooperation using analytical calculations (see Appendices A and B).,1,5,"In several parameter specifications for Version I (Fig. 3, Fig. 4) they set N=5 as the number of participants in a PGG group; this is the canonical group size used in their comparative plots.",0.7,1,"Model describes a single-stage PGG interaction embedded in an evolutionary process, not repeated rounds with the same players.",0.7,1,Cooperators ‘each contribute c’ and defectors ‘contribute nothing’; there is no indication of partial or variable contribution levels beyond full c vs 0.,0.9,N/A,"No human experimental interface or pre-allocation of endowment between accounts, as this is a theoretical model.",1,0.6,"Marginal per capita return is r/N. They frequently use r=3 and N=5, giving MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.9,N/A,No human players or communication; purely theoretical.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human subjects.,1,N/A,No identities; punishment is modeled abstractly at population level.,1,N/A,"No rewards in the model, only punishment.",1,N/A,No experimental interface; infinite-horizon evolutionary process.,1,1,Core of the model is consensual pool punishment when consensus condition is met.,1,0.7,"For Version I, typical parameters (Fig. 4) use G=B=0.7 and γ=β=0.7; pool punishment cost per cooperator is G=0.7.",0.9,N/R,"They state that defectors suffer fine B*N_V proportional to number of punishers, but no per-unit mapping from cost to fine (i.e., fine per unit cost) is given in a normalized form.",0.5,0,Only punishment mechanisms (pool and peer) are modeled; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,1,"Each cooperator contributes cost c to the common pool; they typically set c=1, implying endowment normalized to 1 when using all-or-nothing contributions.",0.9,N/R,They track abundance of cooperation (frequency of cooperators) rather than contribution rate as a continuous DV; no explicit normalized contribution rate is reported.,0.5,N/R,Contribution is fixed at c for cooperators and 0 for defectors; no aggregated token contributions are reported as outcome statistics.,0.5,N/R,"They discuss fitness differences and abundance of cooperation, not aggregate efficiency ratios relative to maximum possible payoff.",0.4,N/R,Group payoff expressions are implicit in payoff formulas but no summarized group payoff values are reported as main DVs.,0.4,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Purely theoretical and computational evolutionary model, not run with human subjects.",1,"Well-mixed finite population of size M (e.g., M=50) using a public goods game with two strategies (C,D). Groups of size N (e.g., N=5) are randomly sampled. Cooperators pay cost c which is multiplied by enforcement factor r and shared equally. Pool punishment is used if the fraction of cooperators voting for punishment exceeds threshold T; all cooperators must comply (p=1). Dynamics follow a frequency-dependent Moran process with selection intensity ω and mutation rate μ; fixation probabilities define an embedded Markov chain on homogeneous states whose stationary distribution gives cooperation abundance.","Summarizes the structural features of Version I as described in the Model and Section III.A, including evolutionary update rule and punishment institution.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.102.052414.md,Version II – Consensual punishment with freedom (0 ≤ p < 1),"The paper explicitly defines Version II as the case where cooperators are voluntary to comply with the electoral outcome (p∈[0,1)), introducing both pool and peer punishment depending on consensus.",1,"Cooperators' preference for punishing defectors q ∈ [0,1]; cooperators' preference for complying with electoral outcome p ∈ [0,1); consensus threshold T ∈ [0,1] for pool punishment employment; other parameters fixed (e.g., r=3, c=1, G=B=0.7, γ=β=0.7, N and M as specified).","Section III.B and Fig. 5 vary q, p, and T systematically to study cooperation abundance under consensual punishment with freedom.",0.95,False,The study uses simulated evolutionary dynamics without human participants.,1,False,No controlled lab/online experiment; purely theoretical modeling and numerical exploration.,1,False,No laboratory environment or human recruitment.,1,True,They numerically compute stationary distributions and cooperation abundance for different parameter combinations using a Moran process and Markov chains.,1,True,"Closed-form expected payoff expressions for cooperators and defectors are derived (πC, πD) as functions of q, p, T and other parameters.",1,40,"In Section III.B and Fig. 5 (emergence of cooperation under consensual punishment with freedom) they specify N=40, M=50, and vary other parameters.",0.9,1,"Single-shot PGG per interaction; evolutionary dynamics repeated over time, not repeated-game rounds with fixed groups.",0.7,1,Cooperators contribute a fixed cost c and defectors contribute 0; no partial contribution choice is modeled.,0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment management or interface; purely modeled contributions.,1,0.075,"MPCR is r/N. For Version II, core analyses use r=3 and N=40 (as specified in Fig. 5), so MPCR = 3/40 = 0.075.",0.9,N/A,No communication between simulated agents.,1,N/A,No feedback to human subjects; only model-level payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit identities or observability choices for agents.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism at all.,1,N/A,No displayed rounds; only evolutionary time steps.,1,1,Model explicitly includes both pool and peer punishment depending on whether consensus on punishment is reached.,1,0.7,They specify G(B)=0.7 and γ(β)=0.7 (Table I parameters used in figures); pool punishment cost G = 0.7 per punisher.,0.9,N/R,"Fines are proportional to number of punishers or defectors (β, B parameters) but not summarized as a single ‘units of fine per cost unit’ figure.",0.5,0,Only punishment mechanisms modeled; no prosocial rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanisms present.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,1,"Each cooperator incurs cost c to contribute; with c=1, the natural normalization is an endowment of 1 with all-or-nothing cooperation.",0.9,N/R,Outcome of interest is cooperation abundance (probability state is C) rather than per-round contribution fractions.,0.5,N/R,Contributions are fixed at c for cooperators; the paper does not report aggregate contribution statistics as DVs.,0.5,N/R,They do not compute or report efficiency as total payoff divided by maximum attainable payoff.,0.4,N/R,"Although payoffs are given formulaically, they are not summarized as main outcome variables for groups.",0.4,N/A,No human participants involved; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely model-based evolutionary simulations.,1,"Well-mixed finite population of size M (e.g., M=50) using a public goods game with two strategies. Groups of size N=40 are randomly formed. Each cooperator pays cost c=1, multiplied by r=3 and shared equally. Cooperators express preference for punishing (q) and for complying with electoral outcome (p). If fraction of cooperators voting for punishment exceeds T, pool punishment is applied by cooperators who comply with the electoral outcome; each pays cost G and defectors incur fines proportional to number of punishers. If consensus is not reached, some cooperators who intended to punish but do not comply instead engage in autonomous peer punishment with costs γ and fines β. Evolutionary dynamics follow the frequency-dependent Moran process with selection intensity ω and mutation μ, leading to a stationary distribution over homogeneous C and D states.","Summarizes core structure of Version II, including dual punishment modes, voluntary compliance, and evolutionary update rule.",1
10.1103_PhysRevE.102.052414.md,"Autonomous peer punishment benchmark (T = 1, p = 0)","Section V defines autonomous punishment as implemented in the model by setting T=1 and p=0, so cooperators opting for punishment directly punish defectors individually (peer punishment).",1,"Cooperators' preference for punishing defectors q ∈ [0,1]; punishment effectiveness β (and B with B=β); other parameters fixed (e.g., r=3, c=1, N=5, M=50, G=γ=1, ω=0.01).","Fig. 7 compares autonomous vs consensual punishment across parameter space (q, β) under fixed other parameters, with T=1 and p=0 representing autonomous peer punishment.",0.95,False,The benchmark is part of the same theoretical/simulation model with no human data.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment but a modeled condition.,1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,True,Stationary distributions and cooperation abundances are computed numerically for this parameterization.,1,True,"Payoff expressions specialize to the autonomous punishment case under T=1, p=0 and are used in analytical conditions (e.g., critical q_c(β)).",0.9,5,"Fig. 7 specifies N=5 when comparing consensual and autonomous punishment across (q, β).",1,1,Single PGG per interaction embedded in the Moran evolutionary process.,0.7,1,Cooperators contribute fixed cost c; defectors contribute 0.,0.9,N/A,No explicit endowment allocation in an experiment.,1,0.6,"With r=3 and N=5 (stated in Fig. 7 for this comparison), MPCR = r/N = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No human communication; modeling only.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No explicit agent identities modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,No human subjects or on-screen round counts.,1,1,Autonomous peer punishment is the only punishment institution in this benchmark.,1,1,"For Fig. 7 they set G=γ=1 and B=β; under pure peer punishment (T=1, p=0), relevant cost is γ=1 per punisher.",0.9,N/R,"They vary β (fine parameter) and state fines are proportional to number of punished defectors, but do not define a unified cost-to-fine ratio per token.",0.5,0,No reward or positive incentives included.,1,N/A,Rewards are absent.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,"They set c=1; with all-or-nothing contributions, endowment is naturally normalized to 1.",0.9,N/R,"Outcome tracked is steady-state cooperation abundance, not per-round contribution fraction.",0.5,N/R,No explicit reporting of average contributions; only cooperation frequencies.,0.5,N/R,They do not present efficiency metrics for this benchmark; focus is on relative abundance of cooperation under different punishment forms.,0.4,N/R,"Group payoff can be inferred from payoff formulas, but no specific numerical group payoff results are reported.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Condition is part of the theoretical simulation model with no empirical implementation.,1,"Finite well-mixed population of size M=50. In each interaction, N=5 individuals play a PGG: cooperators pay c=1, multiplied by r=3, and shared evenly. Consensus threshold T=1 implies that pool punishment is never triggered; with p=0, any cooperator who prefers punishment and is selected acts as an autonomous peer punisher. Each punisher pays cost γ=1 per punished defector, and each defecting target is fined β (with B=β). Strategy updating follows a frequency-dependent Moran process with selection intensity ω=0.01 and mutation rate μ, yielding a Markov chain over homogeneous C and D states whose stationary distribution provides cooperation abundance. This condition serves as the autonomous punishment baseline in comparisons (Fig. 7).",Summarizes autonomous punishment setting as defined in Section V with the specific parameterization used in the comparison figure.,1
