10.1007_978-981-13-2769-8_2.md,Universal scaling and reciprocity mechanisms – analytical & simulation framework,"Single integrated theoretical framework covering 2x2 games, reciprocity transformations, and illustrative simulations (e.g., Fig. 2.4) rather than separable empirical conditions.",0.73,"Game payoff parameters (R, S, T, P) summarized as dilemma parameters D_g, D_r and scaled D_g', D_r'; reciprocity mechanism type (none, direct reciprocity, indirect reciprocity, kin selection, group selection, network reciprocity); mechanism-specific parameters w, q, r, m, n, k; for Fig. 2.4 network reciprocity with lattice degree k=8 and varying (R,P).","Paper explicitly varies D_g, D_r, D_g', D_r' and reciprocity mechanisms plus parameters w,q,r,m,n,k; Fig. 2.4 additionally varies (R,P) across three PD games on an 8-neighbor lattice.",0.9,False,No human or animal participants; all work is analytical derivation and computer simulation.,1,False,No controlled laboratory or field experiment with subjects; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,False,No lab setting with participants; only models and simulations.,1,True,"Multiple places explicitly describe simulations (e.g., spatial PD on lattice N=10^4, multi-agent simulations) without human subjects.",1,True,"Derives replicator dynamics, equilibria, transformed payoff matrices, and conditions analytically.",1,2,Core framework is symmetric 2-player 2-strategy games; multiplayer PGG discussed later is conceptual and not tied to a distinct experiment here.,0.86,1,"One-shot 2x2 interactions under evolutionary dynamics; no repeated game per dyad (except in conceptual definition of direct reciprocity, but not a separate experimental condition).",0.8,1,Players choose between two pure strategies C or D; no continuous contribution level like a public goods amount.,0.98,N/A,No endowment/contribution structure with human players; purely strategic C/D choices.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined; games are generic 2x2 with payoffs (R,S,T,P).",1,N/A,"No human subjects or communication; agent strategies update via rules, not chat.",1,0,"Agents observe payoffs locally via update rules, but there is no UI or experimental display; field defined for human experiments only.",1,N/A,No identity revelation because there are no human subjects or explicit punishment stage with IDs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism with identities in an experiment.,1,0,"No experimental interface; evolutionary time is modeled mathematically, not shown to participants.",1,0,"Within this universal scaling section there is no explicit costly punishment or reward decision; only evolutionary updating. (Punishment appears in later chapter sections, not in this unified scaling treatment.)",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism instantiated in the scaling/simulation examples here.,1,N/A,"No punishment implemented, so no technology parameter.",1,0,No explicit prosocial reward mechanism in these models.,0.9,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No reward implemented.,1,N/A,No per-round monetary endowment; abstract payoffs only.,1,N/A,There is no contribution decision; only C/D strategy frequencies.,1,N/A,No contribution quantities modeled.,1,N/R,Paper presents cooperation fractions and phase diagrams but does not normalize group payoff by a maximum to report efficiency as defined.,0.65,N/R,Average payoffs are discussed in some contexts but not as a single reported scalar DV per condition.,0.6,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All work is theoretical or agent-based simulation with no human or field data.,1,"Symmetric 2x2 games with strategies C and D; evolutionary dynamics given by replicator equations in infinite well-mixed and transformed games under five reciprocity mechanisms (direct, indirect, kin, group selection, network reciprocity). Simulations include spatial PD on lattices (e.g., k=8, N=10^4) using imitation-max updating, exploring equilibrium cooperation across D_g, D_r, and scaled D_g', D_r'.","Summarizes the core game structure and evolutionary setting described throughout 2.1 and related figures (e.g., Figs. 2.4–2.9, Table 2.3).",0.94
