on and Figure 6.,0.9
10.1007_s10588-015-9205-x.md,Theta-pi model – baseline homogeneous population,Paper presents a single formal decision-theoretic model (theta-pi) with various parameter regimes; no distinct named experiments or empirical treatments. This row captures the core homogeneous-population configuration that all analyses build on.,0.9,"Model parameters of the dyadic game: temptation T, reward R, punishment P, sucker payoff S; institutional parameters theta (empathy, 0–1) and pi (probability of being caught, 0–1); elite share K in some sections. This row represents the generic symmetric, homogeneous case without specifying a single numeric setting.","The paper analytically varies T, R, S, P, theta, pi (and later K) to characterize equilibria; these are the only levers in the model.",1,False,"No human subjects, no data collection; purely theoretical.",1,False,No controlled empirical manipulation; only analytical derivations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; it is a theoretical model.,1,False,"Although agent-based and simulation work are discussed in related literature, this paper itself uses algebraic/analytical reasoning, not numeric/agent simulations.",0.9,True,The work derives closed-form conditions and phase diagrams from a non-cooperative game-theoretic model.,1,2,"Core interaction is a dyadic, symmetric game between two parties (farmer and counter-party; binary transactions).",1,1,Each decision situation is a one-shot transaction (no explicit repetition within the game; iterated PD is mentioned only in background).,0.9,1,"Players choose among discrete actions (sit/make/take); there is no continuous contribution decision, so the all-or-nothing vs. continuous contribution distinction from public goods games is not applicable but maps best to allowing any of the available discrete moves.",0.5,N/A,No human endowment or contribution structure; not a public goods or contribution game.,1,N/R,"Model is a 2×2 (plus sit) prisoners’ dilemma–type payoff structure, not a linear public good; MPCR is never defined.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,Players are assumed to know parameters and distributions at equilibrium but there is no notion of round-by-round summaries or feedback; public-goods-style feedback not modeled.,0.8,N/A,Punishment is modeled as an impersonal probability pi of being caught; there are no identities.,1,N/A,No explicit reward operator analogous to targeted peer reward; only higher payoffs from cooperation.,1,0,One-shot analytical game; no displayed horizon.,1,1,Coercive (pi) institutions represent third-party law enforcement that punishes cheaters with payoff S while making restitution; this is a punishment mechanism.,1,N/R,Cost to enforcer or to punisher is explicitly said not to enter the decision; only victim’s and predator’s payoffs are modeled.,1,N/R,"Model specifies resulting payoffs (T, R, P, S) and probability pi, but not a linear mapping from punishment points spent to damage.",1,0,There is no separate reward action or technology; higher R from cooperation is simply the cooperative payoff.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding institution or costly reward action.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,"Model discusses utilities/payoffs R, T, S, P but not per-round endowments in tokens or money.",1,N/R,Outcome metrics are equilibrium E (fraction of makers) and aggregate wealth; no notion of contribution relative to an endowment.,1,N/R,No token-based contributions are defined.,1,N/R,Paper reasons about ‘market collapse’ vs. ‘efficient market’ qualitatively and via phase diagrams but does not compute efficiency as a normalized payoff ratio.,0.8,N/R,Aggregate social wealth is qualitatively discussed; no specific numerical group payoff is reported for any parameter set.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical/theoretical work; no empirical setting.,1,"Non-cooperative game-theoretic decision tree with three actions (sit, make, take) in symmetric dyadic interactions. Payoff matrix is prisoners’ dilemma–type with inequalities T>S, S<P, 2R > T+S. Institutions enter via theta (weight on counterpart’s utility) and pi (probability that a taker is caught and punished with restitution). Elite/non-elite structure is added by varying exposure to pi or theta across subpopulations.",Summarizes the structure of the theoretical game as described across Sections 3 and 4.,1
