10.1007_s10955-012-0661-0.md,"Section 4 – Convergence-percentage simulations (varying tau and n, WP_C=0, WC_T=0.1, a=0.01)","Top-left panel of Fig. 3: a_i=0.01, WP_C=0, WC_T=0.1, alpha=0.1, pA_min=0.01; initial p_C=1, p_A=0.5; multiple tolerance and player counts.",0.9,"Tolerance tau varied from 0 to at least 0.95; number of workers n in {17, 33, 65, 129}; punishment WP_C = 0; computing cost WC_T = 0.1; aspiration a_i = 0.01; learning rate alpha = 0.1; minimum auditing probability pA_min = 0.01; initial cheating probabilities p_Ci = 1; initial master auditing probability p_A = 0.5","Text for Fig. 3: 'Initial condition in all cases was p_c=1 for all workers, p_A=0.5. Parameters in all panels WC_T=0.1, alpha=0.1, pA_min=0.01. Left panels a_i=0.01, top panels WP_C=0. Four numbers of workers indicated by legend.' Tau sweeps shown on x-axis.",1,False,Batch of simulation runs only.,1,False,No human experiment.,1,False,Not lab-based.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations to estimate convergence percentages.,1,False,Uses theory but this result is simulation-based.,0.9,17,"One of the n values used in top-left panel (curves for n=17,33,65,129); this row represents the n=17 condition within that panel.",0.9,1000,Figure 3 caption: 'Convergence percentage of 50 realizations in 1000 rounds.',1,1,Same master–worker decision structure.,0.3,N/A,No endowment framework.,1,N/R,Not applicable.,1,N/A,No human chat.,1,N/R,Not discussed.,0.3,N/A,"No punishment WP_C=0, no identity.",0.95,N/A,No identities/humans.,0.95,N/R,Agent horizon knowledge not reported.,0.2,0,WP_C=0.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,WB_Y=1,1,N/R,Reward cost not given.,0.3,N/R,Per-unit tech not given.,0.3,N/A,No endowment.,1,N/R,Key DV is convergence percentage (probability of eventual correctness in finite horizon).,0.5,N/R,Not measured.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,N/R,Not reported.,0.3,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulations.,1,"For each tau value, 50 simulation realizations with 17 workers. Parameters: WB_Y=1, WC_T=0.1, WP_C=0, a_i=0.01, alpha=0.1, p_A^min=0.01, initial p_Ci=1, initial p_A=0.5. Convergence defined as reaching eventual correctness within 1000 rounds. Results show that for tau up to about 0.6–0.7, convergence percentage is 1, while beyond that it declines and depends on n (worse for larger n).",Summarized from Fig. 3 top-left description and detailed discussion including comments about n and tau.,0.9
10.1080_13504851.2011.572844.md,No punishment treatment,"Paper describes three treatments: no punishment (baseline), voluntary punishment, quasi-voluntary punishment.",1,Punishment institution: no punishment stage; coalition and compliance stages only.,Independent variable is the punishment institution; this treatment has no punishment option.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects forming 4-person groups.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab treatments with specified stages and payoffs.,1,True,Described as experiments with subjects in 4-person groups; standard economics lab design; no indication of field setting.,0.9,False,"All data from human subjects, no agent-based or numerical simulations described.",1,False,Theoretical equilibria discussed but not as a separate analytical study; core is an experiment.,1,4,Groups are explicitly stated to be 4-person.,1,12,“20 subjects forming 4-person groups which are randomly reshuffled for 12 periods.”,1,0,Endowment decision is discrete: either keep or contribute entire $7; no partial contributions mentioned.,0.95,0,Subjects begin with an endowment they can choose to contribute; by default it is private.,0.9,N/R,"Public benefit of contributing endowment is $3 with group size 4, but paper does not specify whether $3 is per capita or total; cannot safely compute MPCR.",0.3,0,"No mention of communication or chat; only coalition, compliance (and in other treatments punishment) stages.",0.9,0,No evidence subjects see payoff or punishment summaries beyond necessary stage information; not described.,0.4,0,No punishment stage exists in this treatment; thus no punishment identities.,1,0,No reward mechanism is described in any treatment.,1,0,Whether subjects were informed of total periods is not stated.,0.2,0,Baseline is explicitly called “no punishment” and has only coalition and compliance stages.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,N/A,No punishment institution.,1,0,Only costly punishment via trade is discussed in other treatments; no rewards anywhere.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,7,“The private value of the endowment is $7 while the public benefit of contributing the endowment is $3.”,1,N/R,Average public good provision is discussed but numerical level or fraction of endowment is not reported.,0.2,N/R,Only relative comparisons and regression coefficients on provision are provided; no raw average reported.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency or payoff ratio is provided or can be computed without knowing exact public good payoff structure.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs are not tabulated; only qualitative statements about joint payoffs being maximized by full participation and contribution.,0.2,N/R,"Institution (Appalachian State University, USA) is given for author, not subject pool; subject nationality not reported.",0.3,N/R,No demographics reported.,0.1,N/R,No demographics reported.,0.1,N/R,"Likely university students but not explicitly stated, so cannot code.",0.3,On site,Standard lab-style economic experiment with groups and monetary payoffs; no indication of online implementation.,0.8,"Two-stage voluntary coalition public good game. Stage 1: discrete choice to join coalition; coalition forms if at least 2 join, else all keep $7. Stage 2: if coalition forms, members decide whether to comply and contribute entire $7; nonmembers automatically keep endowment. Underlying n-player prisoners’ dilemma; joint payoffs maximized if all four join and contribute.",Summary of design description in Section II for baseline (no punishment) treatment.,1
10.1080_13504851.2011.572844.md,Voluntary punishment treatment,Second treatment described is explicitly called “voluntary punishment treatment”.,1,Punishment institution: voluntary bilateral trade-based costly punishment available to all members and nonmembers; no commitment requirement.,Independent variable is punishment institution; in this treatment all players can voluntarily withhold trade as sanction.,1,True,Uses human subjects in 4-person groups; same sample size structure as other treatments.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with explicit payoff structure and random rematching.,1,True,Same lab-style design as baseline; no field elements suggested.,0.9,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,"Analytical SPNE is part of experiment design, not a standalone analytical study.",1,4,Groups are 4-person in all three treatments.,1,12,Treatments share the same 12-period strangers design.,1,0,Coalition/compliance decision remains discrete all-or-nothing contribution of entire $7.,0.95,0,Same endowment structure as baseline; contributions are active decisions from private endowment.,0.9,N/R,Same underlying public good specification as baseline; MPCR cannot be inferred from ambiguous $3 benefit.,0.3,0,"No communication stage; only coalition, compliance, punishment (trade) stages.",0.9,0,"Paper only mentions that, when making trading decisions, players see others’ coalition and compliance decisions, not payoffs or punishment summaries.",0.6,1,"Punishment is implemented as bilateral trade decisions between identified group members; players decide whether to trade with each of the other three, implying they know identities of counterparts they punish (by not trading).",0.9,0,"Trade is mutually beneficial but framed as default action; design and text refer to ‘not trading’ as punishment, not trading as reward. No explicit reward institution.",0.7,0,Information given to subjects about total periods is not reported.,0.2,1,Third stage allows players to withhold mutually beneficial trade; explicitly described as a form of costly punishment.,1,4,"If both choose to trade, they each receive $4; if at least one chooses not to trade, both receive $0. Hence, unilaterally not trading costs the punisher $4 compared with mutual trade.",1,4,"By not trading, punisher reduces target’s payoff from $4 to $0, a $4 reduction per ‘unit’ of punishment (a refused trade).",1,0,"No explicit costly reward action is provided, only opportunity to withhold beneficial trade as punishment.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,7,Same underlying game across treatments with $7 private endowment.,1,N/R,Figure 1 shows average provision but no numeric values are provided.,0.2,N/R,Only regression coefficients and relative comparison to baseline reported; no means.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency is not computed or reported.,0.2,N/R,No table of payoffs; only theoretical joint payoff statements and sanctions frequencies.,0.2,N/R,Country of subject pool is not specified.,0.3,N/R,No age information given.,0.1,N/R,No gender information given.,0.1,N/R,No explicit statement about student status or education.,0.3,On site,Same lab environment as other treatments; no reference to online implementation.,0.8,"Three-stage voluntary coalition public good game. Stage 1 (coalition): discrete choice to join coalition; forms if ≥2 join, else all keep $7. Stage 2 (compliance): if coalition forms, members choose whether to contribute $7 endowment; nonmembers keep $7 by default. Stage 3 (punishment): each player chooses whether to ‘trade’ with each of the other three. If both choose to trade, each gets $4; if at least one refuses, both get $0. Players observe others’ coalition and compliance decisions before trading; refusing to trade is a costly punishment for noncompliance or nonparticipation.",Direct summary of treatment description in Section II.,1
10.1080_13504851.2011.572844.md,Quasi-voluntary punishment treatment,Third treatment is referred to as “quasi-voluntary punishment”.,1,"Punishment institution: same trade-based punishment stage as voluntary treatment, but coalition members must ex ante commit to punishing (not trading with) noncompliant coalition members.",Only difference from voluntary punishment is the commitment requirement for coalition members to punish noncompliant members.,1,True,Described as one of three experimental treatments with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment with specified rules and payoffs.,1,True,"Same setting and structure as other treatments, which are standard lab experiments.",0.9,False,No simulations mentioned.,1,False,"Contains SPNE characterization, but data are empirical.",1,4,All treatments use 4-person groups.,1,12,Same 12 periods for each treatment.,1,0,Coalition members decide whether to contribute full $7 or not; no partial contributions are described.,0.95,0,Endowment starts with player and is contributed only if they choose to comply.,0.9,N/R,Same underlying payoff parameters as other treatments; MPCR ambiguous.,0.3,0,No communication option mentioned in any treatment.,0.9,0,"Subjects are described as observing coalition and compliance decisions before trading, but not payoff or punishment summaries.",0.6,1,Punishment is via bilateral trade decisions with each of the three named group members; identity of punished counterpart is inherent in the decision.,0.9,0,"No reward mechanism; mutual trade is default beneficial outcome, not framed as rewarding.",0.7,0,Paper does not state whether total periods were disclosed.,0.2,1,"Same punishment/trade stage as voluntary treatment, with added commitment requirement.",1,4,"As in voluntary punishment: refusing a trade reduces own payoff from $4 to $0, a $4 cost.",1,4,"Refusing trade reduces target’s payoff from $4 to $0, a $4 sanction per refusal.",1,0,No costly reward institution exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,7,Same underlying game: $7 private endowment.,1,N/R,"Text notes that public good provision increased significantly relative to other treatments, but does not provide numeric averages.",0.2,N/R,"Only regression coefficients (e.g., β2^pg = 1.00) reported; no mean contribution levels.",0.2,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0.2,N/R,No payoff table; only theoretical SPNE payoffs discussed qualitatively.,0.2,N/R,Country of subjects not stated.,0.3,N/R,No age information.,0.1,N/R,No gender information.,0.1,N/R,No explicit statement of education level.,0.3,On site,Same lab experiment structure as other treatments.,0.8,"Three-stage voluntary coalition public good game with commitment to punish. Stage 1: discrete choice to join coalition (forms if ≥2 join). Stage 2: coalition members choose whether to comply by contributing $7; nonmembers keep $7. Stage 3: as in voluntary punishment, each player decides whether to trade with each other player; mutual trade yields $4 each, otherwise $0. Difference: coalition members commit ex ante to not trading with any noncompliant coalition member, making punishment for noncompliance credible and strong enough to induce full compliance in SPNE.",Summary of quasi-voluntary punishment description in Section II.,1
