10.1186_1471-2148-11-345.md,Gender-difference replicator dynamics model,Methods (Replicator dynamics) and Results (Gender differences) describe a separate two-gender model studied via simulations.,0.99,"Sex-specific costs and benefits of extra-pair mating (l, z, y, a) for males vs. females; random pairing between genders","They vary l, z, y (and potentially a) across genders to study resulting trait frequencies.",0.95,False,The gender-difference study is simulation-based with no human subjects.,1,False,Not an experiment; it is a theoretical/simulation extension.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,They explicitly say they used replicator dynamics simulations for the two-gender model.,1,False,"For gender differences they state analytical adaptive dynamics became unwieldy and they relied on simulations, not closed-form solutions.",0.8,2,Model assumes random pairing between one male and one female; effective interaction is between two players.,0.7,1,Fitness computed per generation with replicator dynamics; no repeated strategic rounds.,0.6,1,Strategies are discrete pure strategies but selection operates via continuous frequencies; still not an all-or-nothing contribution game.,0.6,N/A,No endowment or contribution.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human players or communication.,1,N/A,No interface to agents.,1,N/A,Punishment modeled as a parameter y; no explicit identity display.,1,N/A,No reward component.,1,N/A,Continuous-time selection with no rounds.,1,1,"They require condition y > z(1 − β − s) in at least one gender for polymorphism, implying punishment present.",0.95,N/A,"Punishment is a fitness penalty to adulterers; no explicit punisher cost parameter in this replicator model beyond the earlier c_P, c_G policing/guarding costs.",0.7,N/A,No discrete punishment action.,0.7,0,No prosocial rewards modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,No endowment construct.,1,N/A,No contribution variable; DVs are trait frequencies across genders.,1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They discuss polymorphism and gender differences in strategy frequencies, not efficiency metrics.",0.5,N/R,Average fitness values not tabulated as outcome measures.,0.5,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,"Male and female are modeled strategy types, not human subjects.",1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Simulation study with replicator dynamics.,1,"Two-sex version of the earlier mating game, with 8 pure strategies for each sex (M+/−, G+/−, P+/− as before). Random pairing between males and females; sex-specific payoffs E_i computed, then frequencies updated via replicator equation ḟ_i = f_i(E_i − Ē). Costs and benefits of EPM (l, z) and punishment y can differ by gender. Simulations show that as long as condition y > z(1 − β − s) and a > c_P is satisfied for at least one gender, both genders exhibit stable or oscillatory polymorphism in monogamy, guarding, and policing traits. Greater male z and l and/or smaller male y lead to higher male polygamy frequencies, while differences in a alone have little effect on gender differences in traits.",Summarizes description under 'Gender differences' and replicator dynamics methods.,0.95
