10.1063_5.0215702.md,"Punishment condition: p sweep with c varied, ρ=0.2 (includes c=0.3 continuous case)","Figure 2(a) includes curves for multiple costs (e.g., c=0.2,0.3,0.4,0.5) at ρ=0.2; text contrasts low vs high cost behavior. Here we isolate the high-cost side at ρ=0.2, e.g., c=0.3 where transition is continuous at ρ=0.2 but discontinuous at ρ=0.4.",0.8,"Punishment parameters: ρ=0.2, c=0.3, p varied; temptation b=0.1; Q-learning RL PD on L×L lattice",Select condition c=0.3 at ρ=0.2 from Fig. 2(a) to contrast with same c at higher ρ.,0.8,False,Agent-based simulations only.,1,False,No human experiment.,1,False,Simulation environment.,1,True,All results are from computational model.,1,False,No purely analytical solution.,1,10000,Default L=100 used for main parameter sweeps.,0.9,10000000,They use at least 10^7 steps before averaging.,0.8,1,Binary C/D choices.,0.9,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No public goods structure.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No display to agents.,1,N/A,No identities as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Horizon unknown to agents.,1,1,"Punishment enabled with c>0, ρ>0, p>0.",1,0.3,High-cost curve in Fig. 2(a) explicitly mentions c=0.3 as example.,1,1,Penalty per punishment is p and cost per punishment is c; encode linear mapping as 1.,0.4,0,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,PD payoffs only.,1,N/R,Values of f_C vs p are plotted but not tabulated in text.,0.4,N/R,Binary action only.,0.5,N/R,No efficiency numbers for this cost level.,0.5,N/R,Group payoffs not explicitly given.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Part of same simulation framework.,1,"Same RL PD lattice model. ρ=0.2 implies moderate chance that cooperators punish defectors. Cost c=0.3 is high enough that punishment effectiveness depends strongly on p; at ρ=0.2 this produces a continuous increase in f_C, unlike the discontinuous jump at higher ρ.",Summarized from comparative discussion of Figs. 2(a) and 2(b).,0.9
10.1063_5.0215702.md,"Cost sweep with strong punishment p=1, ρ=0.2 (hysteresis and bistability)",Figure 4(b) presents hysteresis for p=1 as c is varied upward and downward at ρ=0.2.,0.95,"Punishment parameters: p=1, ρ=0.2, cost c varied from 0 to 1; temptation b=0.1; Q-learning RL PD on L×L lattice","In Fig. 4(b), p is fixed to 1 and c is varied; initial conditions differ in up/down sweeps, producing hysteresis.",0.95,False,Numerical agent-based simulations.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment.,1,False,Simulation setting only.,1,True,Described as simulation with Monte Carlo steps.,1,False,"Results from simulations, not purely analytic.",1,10000,No change in L stated; use default L=100.,0.9,10000000,Simulation protocol reuses at least 10^7 steps before measurement.,0.8,1,Actions are C or D.,0.9,N/A,No continuous contribution.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No communication between humans.,1,N/A,No UI.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,Agents not told horizon.,1,1,"Punishment enabled with p=1, ρ>0, and varying c.",1,N/R,"In this sweep, c varies continuously from 0 to 1; a single numeric value cannot summarize the condition.",0.4,1,"Penalty per punishment is p=1, so one unit of punishment reduces defector payoff by 1.",0.8,0,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No endowment; PD payoffs matrix-based.,1,N/R,Fig. 4 shows cooperation level f_C vs c and hysteresis but does not provide explicit numeric values beyond qualitative description.,0.4,N/R,Binary action only.,0.5,N/R,No efficiency reported for this cost sweep.,0.5,N/R,No group payoff data tabulated.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is still within the simulation framework.,1,"Same RL PD lattice setup. Punishment intensity fixed at p=1, punishment probability ρ=0.2. Cost c is increased from 0 to 1 and then decreased back from 1 to 0, starting from random initial conditions at endpoints only. This produces hysteresis in f_C vs c, indicating bistability between high cooperation and almost full defection for intermediate c.",Summarized from Fig. 4 and its description of bistable region and hysteresis.,1
