10.1063_5.0215702.md,"Punishment probability sweep ρ with p=0.6, varying cost c (Fig. 8(c))",Figure 8(c) mirrors 8(b) but with p=0.6.,0.9,"Punishment probability ρ varied, with p=0.6, b=0.1, cost c taking multiple values in separate curves",Main varying parameter is ρ; curves for different c at p=0.6.,0.8,False,Simulation-only study.,1,False,No human subjects experiment.,1,False,Simulation.,1,True,Agent-based Q-learning model.,1,False,No closed-form analysis.,1,10000,Same default lattice size.,0.9,10000000,Standard simulation length.,0.8,1,C/D binary action.,0.9,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No public goods.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No UI.,1,N/A,No human identities.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,Horizon unknown to agents.,1,1,Punishment is active with p=0.6 and ρ>0.,1,N/R,Multiple cost values represented; single numeric summary not possible.,0.4,0.6,Penalty per punishment is p=0.6; encode as 0.6.,0.8,0,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,PD game.,1,N/R,No explicit numeric f_C values for ρ given.,0.4,N/R,Binary action only.,0.5,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0.5,N/R,Group payoff not given.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation conditions.,1,"Same RL PD lattice model. For low c, increasing ρ tends to increase f_C. For high c at p=0.6, strong ρ can make punishment too costly, eventually eliminating cooperators.",From the descriptive text about impact of ρ at different p and c in Fig. 8.,0.9
10.1017_s0007123423000716.md,Survey condition – Vox t-shirt,All respondents evaluated a picture of a man wearing a Vox (radical-right) t‑shirt; this is the focal condition for sanctions against counternormative views.,1,Party logo on t-shirt of depicted individual = Vox (radical right),Key randomized/stimulus dimension is which party logo appears on the shirt; here it is fixed to Vox for this condition.,1,True,"Online survey of 1,182 Spanish residents with human respondents judging scenarios.",1,True,Respondents were randomly assigned which additional party shirt (PSOE/Podemos) and order of images; treatment is experimentally varied stimuli.,0.9,False,"Data collected via an online survey company (Bilendi), not in a physical lab setting.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; only human survey data.,1,False,No purely formal/mathematical model is tested as an experiment.,1,1,Each respondent evaluates a vignette individually; no interactive game between multiple players is implemented.,1,1,Single-shot vignette judgment per party image; no repeated interaction or rounds.,1,1,"There is no contribution game; normative questions are continuous rating scales, so the all-or-nothing distinction does not apply but closest is that no discrete restriction exists.",0.3,N/A,No endowment or contribution structure; this is not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No public goods or monetary return structure is present.,1,0,Respondents cannot communicate with each other; they only answer survey questions.,1,0,"Participants are not shown payoff or behavior summaries of others, only information about an image and scales; they get incentive information for second-order questions but not behavior summaries.",1,0,There is no actual punishment implementation or identity revelation; only hypothetical sanction questions.,1,0,No reward mechanisms are implemented.,1,0,No iterative game; thus nothing about number of rounds is displayed.,1,0,Respondents only state perceived appropriateness and willingness to sanction; they do not actually impose costly punishment within a game with payoffs.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment technology with monetary costs.,1,N/A,No formal mapping from punishment actions to payoffs.,1,0,No option to reward the depicted individual is studied.,1,N/A,No implemented rewards.,1,N/A,No implemented rewards.,1,N/A,"Although survey points are used as incentives for guessing others’ responses, there is no endowment for a social dilemma game.",1,N/A,No contribution decision is made; only ratings of appropriateness and willingness to sanction.,1,N/A,No token/point contributions to a public good are measured.,1,N/A,No group payoff structure; thus efficiency is undefined.,1,N/A,No group payoffs are calculated.,1,Spain,The study is described as a survey of the Spanish population conducted in Spain.,1,Adult sample with quotas on age groups; exact range N/R,They state quotas for age groups but do not provide exact minimum/maximum ages.,0.6,Mixed; quotas for gender (male and female),They mention quotas for gender; proportions not exactly reported.,0.9,Mixed; includes college and non-college respondents,"They model a dummy for having attended college, implying both groups are present; distribution not fully reported.",0.8,Online,Survey was run online by the company Bilendi.,1,"Online vignette experiment where each respondent views a standardized image of a 30-year-old man in a public square wearing a Vox t-shirt, plus a close-up of the shirt. Respondents rate first-order and second-order norm perceptions (social/moral appropriateness and harmfulness), perceived appropriateness of various direct and indirect sanctions, their own willingness to engage in such sanctions, and emotional reactions. Second-order expectations are incentivized with 35 points convertible to money if the modal answer is guessed correctly. All respondents view the Vox condition and also one additional party condition (PSOE or Podemos), with party and order randomized.",Summarizes the core design features relevant to cooperation/punishment-type behavior in this non-game survey setting.,1
10.1017_s0007123423000716.md,Survey condition – PSOE t-shirt,Half the respondents additionally evaluated the same man wearing a PSOE (centre-left mainstream) t‑shirt; this is a comparison condition.,1,Party logo on t-shirt of depicted individual = PSOE (centre-left mainstream),This condition varies the party logo relative to the Vox baseline; party identity is the manipulated attribute.,1,True,Human respondents in an online survey evaluate this vignette.,1,True,Assignment to see PSOE vs Podemos as the second image and order of images is randomized.,0.9,False,Conducted online via Bilendi rather than in a physical lab.,1,False,No artificial agents or simulations.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,1,"Individual, non-interactive judgments per vignette.",1,1,Each party vignette is rated once; no repeated rounds.,1,1,No contribution decision; rating scales are continuous. All-or-nothing distinction is not applicable but closest category is unconstrained response.,0.3,N/A,No public good/endowment structure.,1,N/A,No marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,Respondents cannot talk to one another.,1,0,They are not shown outcome summaries of other participants’ sanction choices.,1,0,No real punishment action or revealed identities.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,0,No multi-round interaction.,1,0,Only hypothetical willingness to sanction; no implemented costly punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment technology implemented.,1,N/A,No punishment mapping to payoffs.,1,0,No reward actions possible.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No endowment decisions within a game context.,1,N/A,No contribution choices.,1,N/A,No contribution choices.,1,N/A,No group payoffs.,1,N/A,No group payoff.,1,Spain,Same Spanish online sample as Vox condition.,1,Adult sample with quotas on age groups; exact range N/R,Same respondents as in Vox condition.,0.6,Mixed; quotas for gender (male and female),Same sampling design as overall survey.,0.9,Mixed; includes college and non-college respondents,Same survey sample and education distribution as for Vox.,0.8,Online,Same Bilendi online survey environment.,1,"Within-subject vignette where respondents (a subset of the total sample) view the same 30-year-old man in a public square wearing a PSOE t-shirt. They answer the same batteries about first-order/second-order norm perceptions, appropriateness of different sanctions, self-reported willingness to sanction, and emotions, allowing comparison of sanctions toward mainstream (PSOE) versus counternormative (Vox) preferences within individuals.",Describes how this comparison condition is used analytically in the paper.,1
10.1017_s0007123423000716.md,Survey condition – Podemos t-shirt,Half the respondents additionally evaluated the same man wearing a Podemos (radical-left) t‑shirt; this is another comparison condition.,1,Party logo on t-shirt of depicted individual = Podemos (radical left),This condition varies party identity to a radical-left but not generally stigmatized party.,1,True,Human participants rated this vignette in the online survey.,1,True,Randomization determines whether a respondent’s second image is Podemos or PSOE and the viewing order.,0.9,False,"Conducted online, not in a physical lab.",1,False,Not a simulation study.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,1,Each rating is made individually without interaction.,1,1,One-shot ratings per party vignette.,1,1,No contribution decision; rating scales are continuous; closest category is unconstrained.,0.3,N/A,No public good or endowment.,1,N/A,No MPCR defined.,1,0,No communication between respondents.,1,0,No feedback about others’ sanctioning behavior.,1,0,No real punishers or punished identities.,1,0,No rewarding options.,1,0,Single vignette per party; no rounds are shown.,1,0,Sanctions are only hypothetical; no implemented punishment mechanism with costs.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No mapping to payoffs.,1,0,No reward action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No endowment allocation decision.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No group payoffs.,1,N/A,No group payoffs.,1,Spain,Same Spanish sample as other conditions.,1,Adult sample with quotas on age groups; exact range N/R,Identical sampling frame as for Vox and PSOE conditions.,0.6,Mixed; quotas for gender (male and female),Same panel design.,0.9,Mixed; includes college and non-college respondents,Same national sample with varied education.,0.8,Online,Conducted via Bilendi online platform.,1,"Within-subject vignette in which a subset of respondents see the man in a public square wearing a Podemos t-shirt. They answer t