10.1038_s41598-023-50414-8.md,Experiment 1 – Cooperating-majority condition (with partner punishment),Experiment 1 has two between-subjects base-rate conditions; this row refers to the condition where partners cooperated in 60% of trials.,1,Partner cooperation base rate: cooperating-majority (partners cooperate in 60% and defect in 40% of trials); partner punishment present (partners always punish participants’ unilateral defection).,Between-groups manipulation of partner cooperation/defection base rates and fixed programmed partner punishment behavior.,1,True,Human participants from an online panel played the game.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions with controlled manipulations of partner behavior.,1,False,"Study conducted via online panel provider, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Although partners were simulated, human subjects made decisions; not a pure simulation study.",1,False,Data analyzed with multinomial models but no standalone analytical model as the primary method.,1,2,Prisoner’s Dilemma is described as interaction between two players each trial.,1,25,"Participants played 30 trials, five of which were training trials; thus 25 payoff-relevant trials.",1,1,Players choose between two discrete actions (cooperate or defect); no continuous contribution level as in a public goods game.,1,0,"This is a standard PD with fixed outcome payoffs, not a contribution game where endowment can be pre-allocated to a public fund; all points are in private account until changed by payoffs.",0.7,N/A,"Marginal per capita return is a public goods game concept; here payoffs are defined by a PD payoff matrix, not linear contributions.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat; only one-shot simultaneous decisions each trial.,1,0,"Participants only see current partner’s decision, outcomes, and punishment feedback; no summary statistics over others.",1,0,Partners are anonymous photos; no IDs linking punishment history across trials are reported.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Participants are instructed about playing 30 trials; implies they know the total number of rounds.,0.9,1,A costly punishment option is available every trial.,1,1,"Participants can invest 1, 2, or 3 points to deduct 10, 20, or 30; cost per 10-point deduction is 1 point.",1,10,Each 1 point invested deducts 10 points from partner’s account (10:1 ratio).,1,0,Only punishment options are described; no positive reward choices.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,150,"At the start of the experiment, participants were endowed with 150 points.",1,0.7,"Discussion states: in cooperating-majority condition, participants cooperated in 70% of trials.",1,N/R,"Only cooperation probabilities (percent of trials) are reported, not average point amounts (binary decision).",0.9,N/R,No explicit or computable measure of total payoff relative to maximum cooperative payoff is reported for this condition separately.,0,N/R,"Only individual average final account balances are given across entire experiment, not by condition or group.",0,Germany,"Participants were recruited via a German online panel provider (mingle) and study conducted at Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Germany.",0.9,"18-88 (M=49, SD=15)",Reported for the full Experiment 1 sample; age by condition not separated.,1,"305 female, 239 male",Reported for full Experiment 1 sample; not broken down by condition.,1,N/R,Demographic questions mentioned but no education breakdown reported.,0,Online,Data collected via online panel provider mingle; instructions and game presented online.,1,"Repeated simultaneous one-shot Prisoner’s Dilemma with changing simulated partners each trial. Payoff matrix: mutual cooperation +10 each; mutual defection 0; if participant cooperates and partner defects: participant −10, partner +20; if participant defects and partner cooperates: participant +20, partner −10. Partners cooperate in 60% and defect in 40% of trials. After each PD outcome, both participants and programmed partners can choose punishment: invest 0–3 points to deduct 0–30 points from the other (10 points deducted per point invested). Programmed partners always punish participants’ unilateral defection by randomly deducting 10, 20, or 30 points.",Summarizes key PD and punishment structure and programmed partner behavior for this condition.,1
