10.1093_pnasnexus_pgad054.md,Migration with discrimination against migrants in normative interactions,Simulation variant where migrants’ traits do not affect normative payoff calculations (zero social capital case).,1,Migration rate m; source mean z; source variance U; similarity coefficient S; learning error E; discrimination rule excluding migrants from normative payoff expectations.,These aspects define this simulation condition; others follow base migration model.,0.9,False,"Numerical simulation, no human subjects.",1,False,Not a human experiment.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Described as simulation adjusting normative payoff calculation for migrants.,0.95,False,Behavior described qualitatively; no closed-form solutions reported.,1,2,Members interact in dyads as in base migration model.,0.9,N/R,Number of generations not provided.,0.2,1,Trait is continuous.,1,N/A,No contribution game.,1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,N/A,Simulation with no communication.,1,0,No summaries displayed.,1,N/A,No punitive action choice.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Rounds not presented to agents.,1,0,Payoffs modified by discrimination rule but no explicit punishment decisions.,1,N/A,No punishment action.,1,N/A,No punishment action.,1,0,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No endowment-based payoffs.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency metrics given; only convergence behavior.,1,N/R,Group payoff not reported.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Implemented in Matlab or similar as part of simulation suite.,1,"Same migration process as base migration simulation, but normative payoff for a trait is computed from expected interactions with original residents only, effectively giving migrants no social capital; despite this, focal population’s mean norm still converges to source population’s mean unless migrants are never used as social learning models.",Paraphrases description in section on discrimination against migrants.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgad054.md,One-sided similarity-based payoffs simulation,Simulation where only deviations on one side of the mean are penalized normatively.,1,One-sided payoff rule assigning payoff 1 to larger trait and exp(−(x_i−x_j)^2 / (2S)) to smaller trait; migration rate m; presence/absence and strength of direct effects; cognitive/demographic parameters as in base model.,"Defining independent condition is the asymmetry in similarity-based payoff plus background forces (none, migration, or direct effects).",0.9,False,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab involvement.,1,True,Authors report that one-sided similarity-based payoffs are operationalized in simulations.,1,False,"Behavior is described qualitatively, not via closed-form expressions.",1,2,"Payoff rule defined for pairs (x_i, x_j).",1,N/R,Number of simulated generations is not stated.,0.2,1,Continuous norm values.,1,N/A,No contribution game.,1,N/R,No public good or MPCR.,1,N/A,No communication step.,1,0,No summaries shown in simulation design.,1,N/A,No discrete punishment actions; payoffs are continuous evaluations.,1,N/A,No discrete rewards.,1,0,Agents do not observe number of rounds.,1,0,"One-sided payoffs represent normative evaluation, not explicit punishment decisions.",1,N/A,No punishment decision variable.,1,N/A,No punishment decision variable.,1,0,No explicit reward decision variable.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No tokens/endowment.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,No group efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,"Outcomes discussed qualitatively (e.g., norm pushed infinitely) but no numeric group payoff.",1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,All dynamics computed via simulation of artificial populations.,1,"For each interacting pair, the larger trait gets normative payoff 1, the smaller gets exp(−(difference)^2 / (2S)); without other forces or with only low-rate migration, distribution drifts indefinitely in the favored direction; with strong direct effects or demographic processes, one-sided normative payoffs can maintain an equilibrium norm different from direct-effect optimum or migrant norm, but this equilibrium is still independent of initial conditions.",Summarizes operational definition and qualitative outcomes for this simulation condition.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgad054.md,Discretized similarity-based payoffs with migration (narrow vs. wide categories),Simulation where continuous behavioral space is partitioned into categories of width c and similarity-based payoffs depend on category centers; two conditions shown: narrow c=1.07 and wide c=3.21.,1,"Category width c (1.07 vs 3.21); migration rate m; source mean z=3.21; source variance U=3.24; similarity coefficient S=4; learning error E=1; initial focal means 0 or 6.42; initial variances (3.28 for c=1.07, 2.79 for c=3.21).",Figure 2 and text list these parameter values; central manipulated factor is category width c.,1,False,Bin-based numerical simulation of trait distributions; no human participants.,1,False,Not a human experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No lab subjects or setting.,1,True,Explicitly described as simulation in discretization section and illustrated in Fig. 2.,1,False,"Outcomes obtained by numerical iteration, not closed form.",1,2,Individuals interact in pairs; similarity-based payoff based on categories of the two traits.,1,N/R,Number of generations not specified; only equilibrium outcomes vs migration rate are shown.,0.2,1,Underlying trait is continuous; contributions not restricted to discrete options.,1,N/A,No contribution structure.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,0,Simulation agents do not see summaries.,1,N/A,No identity-based punishment.,1,N/A,No identity-based reward.,1,0,Agents are not given round counts.,1,0,Similarity-based payoffs depend on category centers but no explicit punishment decisions.,1,N/A,No punishment action variable.,1,N/A,No punishment action variable.,1,0,No explicit reward actions.,1,N/A,No reward action variable.,1,N/A,No reward action variable.,1,N/A,No token endowment.,1,N/A,No contributions are modeled.,1,N/A,No contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,Efficiency not measured; outcomes are equilibrium mean norms as function of migration rate.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically reported.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Trait distributions and payoffs computed numerically; no human sample.,1,"Continuous trait space is partitioned into fixed-width categories of size c; in norm effects, all traits in category receive payoff equal to payoff at category center, making payoff a step function; under migration from source (z=3.21, U=3.24, S=4, E=1), when c is narrow (1.07) equilibrium mean in focal population converges to source mean even at low migration; when c is wide (3.21) and focal populations start at different initial means (0 or 6.42), they maintain norms near initial values at low migration rates, preserving between-group variation.",Summarizes discretization mechanism and key outcomes shown in Fig. 2.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgad054.md,Multiple cognitive attractors with direct effects (close vs. sparse spacing),"Simulation analyzing norm evolution when cognitive payoff has multiple Gaussian peaks (cognitive attractors) with different spacing, under direct effects.",1,"Distance between adjacent cognitive attractors (close vs sparse, e.g., distance 1 vs 6 as in initial variance note); direct effect optimum θ=2.39; similarity coefficient S=4; learning error E=1; initial focal means 0 and 6; initial variances 3.28 for distance 1, 2.79 for distance 6.","Figure 3 reports θ, S, E and variances for two attractor spacings; attractor spacing is main manipulated factor.",1,False,Simulation with modeled cognitive payoff; no human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,Computation only.,1,True,Authors state they model this by modifying base model and analyze with simulations in Fig. 3.,1,False,Qualitative outcomes are reported from simulations; no closed-form solution given.,1,2,"Still based on pairwise interactions as in base model, with added cognitive payoff.",0.9,N/R,Number of simulated generations not reported.,0.2,1,Norm trait continuous over behavioral space.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,No MPCR parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit communication.,1,0,No summaries are shown to agents.,1,N/A,No discrete punishment stage.,1,N/A,No discrete reward stage.,1,0,Rounds not displayed.,1,0,"Cognitive payoff modifies direct payoff, not punishment decisions.",1,N/A,No punishment choices.,1,N/A,No punishment choices.,1,0,No explicit rewarding actions.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No monetary tokens.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency metrics not reported; main DV is equilibrium mean norm.,1,N/R,Only cognitive payoff landscape and equilibrium means shown.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human subjects.,1,No human,Model implemented computationally without subjects.,1,"Cognitive payoff is sum of multiple Gaussian peaks (cognitive attractors) ranging from 0 to 1; with closely spaced attractors, populations with different initial norms converge to direct-effect optimum θ=2.39; with sparsely spaced attractors, populations starting at different initial norms (0 and 6) remain near different cognitive attractors until direct-effect strength is high enough that both converge to compromise between θ and nearest attractor.",Summarizes mechanism and outcomes depicted in Fig. 3.,1
