tment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this treatment.,1,0,No costly reward mechanism; only the trust-game transfers and automatic 12-point additions.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology beyond standard game transfers.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,12,‘Each member of the group is given 12 points’ at start of each round as decision endowment.,1,0.475,Using all-round average sent 5.70 of 12: 5.70/12 ≈ 0.475.,0.9,5.7,Table 2: ‘Average sent by senders’ in Baseline is 5.70 points.,1,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximum theoretical group payoff reported and would require detailed state-by-state data.,0.2,N/R,Only average individual earnings reported; no explicit per-group total payoff.,0.2,Finland,"Experiment conducted at University of Turku, Finland; participants are local university students.",0.95,Mean 27.4 (SD 6.35); mostly undergraduate and graduate students,"Participant description: ‘mostly undergraduate and graduate students… mean age 27.4, s.d. 6.35’.",1,58% female,Sample description: ‘58 percent female’.,1,Mostly undergraduate and graduate university students,Sample described as ‘mostly undergraduate and graduate students of the local university’.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical decision-making laboratory with assigned seats and lab computers.,1,Three-player trust game with two senders and one responder; senders choose 0–12 to send; amounts tripled; responder allocates among three players; at stage 3 all get extra 12 points; roles fixed over six rounds; random stranger matching each round; one round randomly paid (2 points = 1 euro).,Summarized from design and instructions sections.,1
