dition.,1,N/R,Condition-specific group earnings are discussed qualitatively in S1 File but not numerically in main text excerpt.,0.3,United Kingdom,Same UCL lab participant pool.,0.5,N/R,Age distribution not provided.,1,Mixed; both male and female participants,Results in Table 1–2 etc. separately report male and female outcomes in this condition.,1,University students (implied),"Lab at a university, typical subject pool.",0.6,On site,Same lab setting as other conditions.,1,Same driving game as Control plus an external fine: any participant choosing Fast who is not in an accident faces a 25% probability of paying a £4 fine that reduces their payoff in that round.,From condition description: “Participants who chose Fast were fined with C=£4 with a 25% chance each round. This fine only applied to participants who were not in an accident in that round.”,1
10.1371_journal.pone.0275383.md,Endogenous punishment – Peer-funded fine on Fast drivers,Condition 4 in “Policy conditions”: players can contribute to a punishment fund that fines Fast drivers.,1,"Policy condition = Endogenous punishment (Fast drivers face fine £X with 25% probability if not in accident; X depends on others’ £1 contributions, each adding 2.5 to X)","Defines the key independent manipulation: endogenous, group-determined punishment of Fast drivers.",1,True,Human lab experiment; participants decide on both driving and whether to pay into a punishment fund.,1,True,Condition is randomly assigned and manipulates the presence and nature of peer punishment.,1,True,Conducted in lab sessions at UCL.,1,False,No purely simulated results specific to this condition.,1,False,"Row is about the implemented experimental treatment, not the analytical model.",1,10,Same N=10 parameter as in the Game section.,0.7,21,Same 17–25 random horizon with continuation probability.,0.4,0,"Driving choice is among three options; the punishment-fund contribution is a binary £1 vs £0 choice, not a contribution of entire endowment vs zero.",1,0,No default allocation into a public fund; punishment contributions are voluntary and separate from driving payoff endowment.,0.4,N/R,"This is not a standard linear public goods game; the fund affects fines on Fast players, not a shared benefit.",1,0,No communication stage reported.,1,1,"As in other conditions, participants see others’ past driving choices and payoffs.",1,0,"Punishment is pooled through a fund; no indication that contributors know which Fast players were fined, nor that fined individuals observe who punished them.",0.6,0,No rewards.,1,0,Randomly ended between 17 and 25 rounds; exact final round not announced in advance.,1,1,Endogenous policy explicitly introduces a group-funded fine on Fast drivers.,1,1,Each punisher pays a fixed cost of £1 into the punishment fund when choosing to contribute.,1,2.5,"Fine amount X equals number of contributors times 2.5, so each £1 contribution yields £2.5 of fine imposed on each punished Fast driver with 25% probability.",1,0,No mechanism for rewarding others exists.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,28,"Same baseline per-round earning structure as other conditions; extra £1 contribution is a deduction from period payoff, not a change in base endowment.",0.7,N/R,"Paper reports rate of punishment-fund contributions in passing (e.g., 13.5% by females vs 8.6% by males) but not as a main outcome in tables; plus it is not a contribution to a public good in the sense defined in the schema.",0.5,N/R,No aggregate or mean contribution amounts reported in main text.,0.5,N/R,No normalized efficiency metrics relative to maximal surplus are reported.,1,N/R,Condition-level group earnings are mentioned to be in S1 File but not quantified in excerpt.,0.3,United Kingdom,Same UCL lab as other treatments.,0.5,N/R,No age summary statistics in excerpt.,1,Mixed; both male and female participants,"Gender-specific results reported (e.g., Table 1, 2, participation in punishment).",1,University students (implied),Lab at a university.,0.6,On site,Laboratory sessions in person.,1,"Same driving game as Control plus a peer punishment stage. Each round, along with choosing Fast/Slow/Auto and beliefs, participants also choose whether to pay £1 to a punishment fund. The fund size determines a fine X=2.5×(number of contributors) imposed with 25% probability on each Fast chooser who is not in an accident in that round.",From the Endogenous policy description in Policy conditions section.,1
10.1057_s41599-022-01436-6.md,"T1 – Single group of 9, no subgroups, no incentives, no sanctions","Treatment T1 description: 9 players invest individually in a single group with no subdivision, incentives, or sanctioning.",1,Group structure and incentives: single group of 9 players; no subgroups; no incentives; no sanctioning opportunity.,"T1 is baseline condition varying presence of subgroups, incentives, and sanctions relative to T2–T4.",1,True,Human student volunteers played the game.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted in university computer labs using z-Tree with partitions.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical/mathematical model tested here.,1,9,Each experimental group (country) consists of 9 volunteers in T1.,1,10,Participants contribute in each of ten rounds; target checked after ten rounds.,1,0,"Each round players can choose among €0, €2, or €4; not restricted to all-or-nothing.",1,0,Initial €40 endowment is in personal account; climate account starts at zero.,1,N/R,No linear public goods multiplier; threshold with catastrophe risk instead.,1,0,Only decisions via computer; no communication mentioned.,0.8,1,"Players must know group’s total contributions and target attainment for the game to work; implies summary feedback, though not fully detailed.",0.6,N/A,No punishment mechanism in T1.,1,N/A,No reward/incentive mechanism at individual level in T1.,1,1,"Contributions are made for a known, fixed ten rounds; typical design and wording imply subjects knew this.",0.9,0,No sanctioning opportunity in T1.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,N/A,Punishment not available.,1,0,No incentive or refund mechanism for any subgroup or individual in T1.,1,N/A,No reward system.,1,N/A,No reward system.,1,40,Each participant received an initial endowment of €40.,1,N/R,Paper reports contributions qualitatively and via counts of 0/2/4 but not an overall normalized average per treatment.,0.3,N/R,Exact mean total euros contributed per player in T1 not given; only aggregate tests versus target.,0.3,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible payoffs reported.,0.1,N/R,No mean monetary payoff per group reported; only whether target met or not and some side-account earnings in other treatments.,0.1,Germany,"Participants recruited from universities in Kiel, Hamburg, Köln and Greifswald, Germany.",1,undergraduate students,Described as undergraduate students; no numeric age range.,0.8,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,All participants were undergraduate students at German universities.,1,On site,Computerised lab experiment with participants physically present and separated by partitions.,1,"Collective-risk social dilemma on climate mitigation with catastrophe risk: groups of 9 each have €40 endowment, choose €0/€2/€4 per round over 10 rounds to a climate account. Group target is €180 (equivalent to average €2 per player per round). If target reached, each keeps remaining personal money; if not, they lose remaining money with 90% probability. All contributions fund real-world climate mitigation advertisements.",Summarizes core structure of the threshold public good and payoff rule from Methods.,1
10.1057_s41599-022-01436-6.md,"T2 – Polycentric with 3 fixed subgroups, incentives (refund for subgroup performance), no sanctions","Treatment T2 description: 9 players split into 3 fixed subgroups of 3, with incentives linked to subgroup reaching sub-target; no sanctioning.",1,Group structure and incentives: 3 fixed subgroups of 3 within group of 9; subgroup-level refund incentives for reaching sub-targets; no sanctioning opportunity.,"T2 differs from T1 by subgroup structure and presence of incentivizing refunds, and from T3 by lack of sanctions.",1,True,Human student volunteers participated.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment within same experimental study.,1,True,Conducted on-site in labs using z-Tree.,1,False,No computer-only agents.,1,False,No closed-form theoretical model tested here.,1,9,Each group simulating a country consists of 9 volunteers.,1,10,Ten rounds of contributions with target checked after round 10.,1,0,"Players choose €0, €2, or €4 each round.",1,0,"All €40 initially in personal account, none in climate account.",1,N/R,Threshold-with-risk structure; no linear marginal per capita return specified.,1,0,No communication channel reported; typical for such z-Tree designs.,0.8,1,Subgroups must be informed whether they reached sub-targets and group whether global target is met.,0.7,N/A,No punishment mechanism available in T2.,1,0,Refund is automatically given for subgroup performance; no mention that individual identities of rewarded players are revealed beyond their own payoff.,0.6,1,Ten-round horizon is common knowledge.,0.9,0,No sanctioning opportunity in T2.,1,N/A,Punishment not enabled.,1,N/A,Punishment not enabled.,1,1,Explicit increasing extra monetary reward to each player when subgroup reaches specified sub-targets.,1,0,Players do not pay to issue rewards; refunds are exogenous bonuses based on subgroup performance.,0.9,8,Maximum reward is €8 per player if subgroup reaches €60 or more; treat this as magnitude of incentive per successful subgroup member.,0.9,40,Same initial endowment of €40 as other treatments.,1,N/R,"Paper provides frequency of 0/2/4 contributions and tests, but not an explicit normalized mean fraction per player in T2.",0.3,N/R,Only states that groups assembled significantly more than €180 on average; exact mean per player not provided.,0.4,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximum payoff.,0.1,N/R,Mean monetary payoff per group not reported; only side-payoff via incentives (€6.40 ± 0.24 per player) is given.,0.3,Germany,Same participant pool as T1: German universities.,1,undergraduate students,Participants are undergraduate students from multiple German universities.,0.8,N/R,No details on gender distribution.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Stated in Participants and procedure.,1,On site,Same computerized lab setup as other treatments.,1,"Same collective-risk climate threshold game as T1 (group target €180 after ten rounds, €40 endowment, €0/€2/€4 choices), but the 9 players are partitioned into 3 fixed subgroups of 3. Each subgroup has a notional sub-target of €60. If after ten rounds subgroup contributions reach at least €52, each member receives a refund that increases stepwise with subgroup total: at €52 (€0.50), €54 (€1.00), €56 (€2.00), €58 (€4.00), €60+ (€8.00). Refund is paid after game regardless of whether global target €180 is reached. Catastrophe risk if global target not met remains 90% loss of remaining personal money.",Summarizes additional incentive scheme and subgroup structure from the T2 description.,1
10.1057_s41599-022-01436-6.md,"T3 – Polycentric with 3 fixed subgroups, sanctioning opportunity, no incentives","Treatment T3 description: 9 players split into 3 fixed subgroups of 3, no incentives, but subgroups can impose costly sanctions on another subgroup by majority vote.",1,Group structure and sanctions: 3 fixed subgroups of 3 within group of 9; subgroup-level sanctioning opportunity via majority vote; no refund incentive.,Main manipulated features relative to T4 (sanctions vs none) and T2 (sanctions vs incentives).,1,True,Human undergraduates participated.,1,True,Controlled treatment in lab study.,1,True,"Same lab, z-Tree setup.",1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,No formal model only; this is empirical.,1,9,Each group simulating a country consists of 9 volunteers.,1,10,Ten rounds of contributions and sanction decisions.,1,0,Same €0/€2/€4 contribution options.,1,0,€40 starts in personal account.,1,N/R,Threshold with catastrophe risk; no MPCR reported.,1,0,No chat or communication option reported.,0.8,1,Each subgroup is shown the contribution of each subgroup to allow sanction decisions; implies feedback of subgroup totals.,1,0,"Votes are cast anonymously; no indication that identities of punished subgroup members or voters are revealed, only which subgroup is sanctioned.",0.8,N/A,No reward/incentive mechanism in T3.,1,1,Ten-round horizon is part of instructions as in other treatments.,0.9,1,Each subgroup can sanction another subgroup by majority vote.,1,0.5,"If at least 2 members vote to sanction, each player of the active (sanctioning) subgroup pays €0.50 from their extra expenses account.",1,2,Each player of the sanctioned subgroup pays €2.00 from their extra expenses account when sanctioned.,1,0,T3 is explicitly described as T2 without incentives but with sanctions.,1,N/A,No rewards offered.,1,N/A,No rewards offered.,1,40,Each player receives initial endowment of €40 plus an additional €15 'extra expenses' account used only for sanction costs.,1,N/R,Paper compares contributions and frequencies but does not give normalized fraction of endowment.,0.3,N/R,States contributions per group are on average close to €180 in T3 but does not provide exact mean.,0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure reported.,0.1,N/R,"Mean final payoffs per group or per player are not fully reported; only average sanction costs (€1.486 paid, €5.944 received) are given.",0.4,Germany,Same multi-university German sample across treatments.,1,undergraduate students,Undergraduate students from several German universities.,0.8,N/R,Gender composition unspecified.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Stated explicitly.,1,On site,On-site computerized lab experiment as for other treatments.,1,"Same climate threshold game as T1 and T2 (group target €180, €40 endowment, €0/€2/€4 choices, 10 rounds, 90% loss risk if target not reached). The 9 players are partitioned into 3 fixed subgroups of 3. After each round, each subgroup observes the contribution of each subgroup and can vote to sanction one other subgroup. If at least 2 of 3 members vote to sanction a specific other subgroup, each member of the sanctioning subgroup pays €0.50 and each member of the sanctioned subgroup pays €2.00 from an additional 'extra expenses' account (€15 initial). Remaining extra-expenses money is paid out after the game irrespective of climate target outcome. Only reaching the global target (sum over all subgroups) averts the 90% loss; subgroup thresholds alone do not determine climate outcome.","Summarizes T3 specifics: subgroups, voting rule, sanction costs, extra account, and global threshold rule.",1
