 specified.,0.1,Undergraduate university students,Explicitly stated.,1,On site,Same lab setting for all treatments.,1,"Same core climate threshold game as T1–T3 (group target €180, €40 endowment, €0/€2/€4 choices for 10 rounds, 90% loss risk if target not achieved). The 9 players are partitioned into 3 fixed subgroups of 3. There are no subgroup incentives (refunds) and no sanctioning opportunities. Subgroups do not have independent thresholds affecting payoffs; only the global target matters for averting the 90% loss.",Summarizes that T4 isolates polycentric structure without incentives or sanctions.,1
10.3390_ijerph192416578.md,Cross-sectional survey – cooperative attitude groups (noncooperative vs cooperative vs punishment),"Single observational study where cooperative attitude is categorized into three groups: noncooperative, cooperative, punishment.",1,"Cooperative attitude toward sharing a class task with an unfamiliar classmate after betrayal (levels in this row: noncooperative / cooperative / punishment, treated as categorical exposure in regression).",Paper explicitly defines three cooperative attitude types and uses them as the main explanatory variable for risky behaviors and vaccine hesitancy.,1,True,Uses survey data from human medical students.,1,False,No controlled manipulation or random assignment; observational cross-sectional survey.,1,False,"Data collected via Google Forms as part of a class, not a controlled laboratory game environment.",1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No formal mathematical model; purely statistical analysis of survey responses.,1,0,No interactive game or strategic interaction with payoffs; only a hypothetical vignette question.,1,0,No repeated rounds of a game; only one hypothetical situation.,1,0,Not applicable; there is no contribution decision with gradations or all-or-nothing choices. Set to 0 to reflect absence of such a structure.,0.7,N/A,No endowment or contribution mechanism; observational survey only.,1,N/R,No public goods game or payoff multiplier is used.,1,0,Participants answer individually to an online questionnaire; no communication between participants reported.,0.9,0,No feedback about other participants’ choices or earnings is given; only self-report survey.,1,0,No punishment mechanism is implemented; only a survey attitude category labeled 'punishment'.,1,0,No reward mechanism in the study design.,1,0,Not a repeated game; respondents are not informed about rounds.,1,0,No actual costly punishment option with payoffs; only a self-reported punitive attitude.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment technology.,1,N/A,No implemented punishment technology.,1,0,No reward or bonus mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"No tokens, money, or points are used.",1,N/R,No contribution decisions; dependent variables are risky behaviors (eating out) and vaccine hesitancy.,1,N/R,No contribution decisions are measured.,1,N/R,No payoff structure or efficiency measure is defined.,1,N/R,No group payoff is defined.,1,Japan,Participants are fourth-year medical students at Tokyo Medical and Dental University in Japan.,1,"Primarily 21–26; categorized as: entered immediately after high school (~22), gap years (~23), prior university graduates (~26).",Section 3 describes age categories and approximate ages instead of exact ages.,0.9,"Mix of male and female; 62 male (63.3%), 36 female (36.7%).",Reported in Table 1 demographics.,1,Fourth-year medical students at a single Japanese medical university.,Described in Methods and throughout as fourth-year medical students at Tokyo Medical and Dental University.,1,Online,Questionnaire administered via Google Forms in 2021.,1,"Observational cross-sectional survey on cooperative attitude in a hypothetical class-task scenario and its association with high-risk COVID-19 behaviors (eating/drinking out frequency, vaccine hesitancy); no economic game or payoff-based interaction.","Methods describe only survey questions, hypothetical vignette, and Poisson regression analyses without any strategic game.",1
10.3390_su141912809.md,"Baseline model with gossip (no punishment or compensation, P_N)","Control group defined in Section 3.2 as P_N, where no punishment or compensation is applied when gossip contradicts facts.",1,"Coping mechanism for gossip: P_N (no punishment or compensation); interpersonal relationship structure varies by scenario (IT, MT, ET, HT); other parameters fixed as reported.",Independent variable is the mechanism for handling gossip; this row corresponds to the baseline P_N condition described explicitly.,0.93,False,"Study uses agent-based numerical simulations on scale-free networks, not human participants.",1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; only computational simulations.,1,False,No lab setting with human subjects; purely simulated agents.,1,True,"Described as numerical simulations on networks with agents updating strategies; many runs of 10,000 steps.",1,False,"While equations specify update rules, core results are from simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions over parameter space.",0.9,200,Section 3 specifies initial population N=200.,1,10000,"Section 3: 'each simulation ran at least 10,000 steps'; use 10,000 as representative horizon.",0.9,1,"Contributions c_i are random in [0,1] and update as continuous amounts, so contribution is not all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,"Endowments are not explicitly endowed in a public vs private account; c_i is a contribution decision variable starting random in [0,1], implying all endowment initially private.",0.4,N/R,They report synergy factor r and network degree but not explicit group size per game for MPCR computation.,0.2,N/A,No human communication; agent rules are algorithmic.,1,0,Agents update based on own payoff and neighbors’ payoff via Fermi rule; no interface or explicit summaries typical of experiments are described.,0.6,N/A,No human identities; punishment is an internal parameter change (influence reduction) for gossipers.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism disclosed to human subjects; only simulated influence updates.,1,0,"Agents are not described as seeing round counts; this is a simulation parameter, not agent information.",0.7,0,Control group P_N explicitly: 'we do not give agents any form of punishment and compensation.',1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in P_N.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in P_N.,1,0,No explicit reward/bonus tokens; only reputational and influence updates inherent to dynamics.,0.9,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"They define contributions c_i in [0,1] but do not define an explicit endowment per round in tokens or money.",0.3,N/R,They report total contributions (TC) at equilibrium qualitatively (≈70) but not normalized per-endowment rates.,0.2,70,Figure 4b: TC under varying conformity is 'always close to 70'; for baseline P_N with C_gf=0.5 this applies approximately.,0.4,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff is reported.,0.1,N/R,They discuss payoff functions but do not report aggregate payoff values.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,No human,Study is a numerical simulation without human or field data.,1,"Public goods game on a scale-free network with N=200 agents, average degree a_vn=4, synergy factor r=2.0, contribution c_i initially uniform on [0,1], proportion of gossipers q=0.1, each gossiper talks about z=4 individuals per round; heterogeneous interpersonal relationships parameterized by (n1, n2, n3) for IT, ET, MT structures.",Summarized from Section 2 and the initial conditions in Section 3.,1
10.3390_su141912809.md,Punishment only mechanism (P_O),"Section 3.2 defines P_O as 'punishment only', penalizing gossipers spreading incorrect information without compensating affected agents.",1,"Coping mechanism for gossip: P_O (punishment only, no compensation); tolerance degree d=0.4, penalty time h=5, rebirth coefficient θ=0.5; conformity between gossip and facts C_gf=0.5; interpersonal relationships (IT, MT, ET) varied across panels.",These are the parameters specified in Figure 5 caption for comparing mechanisms.,0.95,False,Agent-based numerical simulations only.,1,False,Not a controlled human experiment.,1,False,No lab or human environment.,1,True,Results from numerical simulations on networks.,1,False,They rely on simulations for outcomes.,0.9,200,Same baseline N=200 used for all simulations in Section 3.,1,10000,"Each simulation ran at least 10,000 steps.",0.9,1,"Contributions vary continuously in [0,1].",0.95,0,No explicit pre-allocation to public fund; contributions chosen from own resources.,0.4,N/R,Synergy factor r reported but not group size per game; MPCR not given.,0.2,N/A,No human communication; algorithmic updates only.,1,0,No UI or summaries for agents; only payoff-based imitation.,0.6,N/A,Punishment is applied automatically to gossipers’ influence; no identities in human sense.,1,N/A,No explicit social rewards communicated.,1,0,Rounds are not informational variables for agents.,0.7,1,P_O penalizes gossipers who spread incorrect information; influence set to zero for h rounds per rules in Section 2.2.,1,N/R,Cost parameter per punishment to the punisher is not modeled in payoff units; only influence reductions are specified.,0.2,N/R,"They specify qualitative tech: I_{t+1}=...=0 for h rounds then θ I_t, but no scalar punishment-per-token metric.",0.3,0,P_O only includes punishment; no compensation or explicit reward to victims.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in P_O.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in P_O.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not specified.,0.3,N/R,Only total contributions (TC) at equilibrium are described qualitatively across mechanisms.,0.2,N/R,"For mechanisms comparison in Figure 5, exact TC values per mechanism are not numerically labeled.",0.2,N/R,No efficiency ratios reported.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate payoff numbers reported per mechanism.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational model.,1,"Same PGG network setup as baseline (N=200, a_vn=4, r=2.0, q=0.1, z=4, φ=0.1). Gossipers classified as unqualified if a/(a+b) > d, then punished for h=5 rounds with influence I set to 0, then reborn with influence θ I_t where θ=0.5. No compensation to affected agents.",Derived from Section 2.2 and Figure 5 parameters.,1
10.3390_su141912809.md,Punishment with reputation compensation mechanism (P_R),"Section 3.2 defines P_R as punishment with reputation compensation, penalizing gossipers and restoring the reputation of affected agents.",1,"Coping mechanism for gossip: P_R (punishment plus reputation compensation); tolerance degree d=0.4, penalty time h=5, rebirth coefficient θ=0.5; conformity C_gf=0.5; interpersonal relationships (IT, MT, ET) varied.","Figure 5 caption lists shared parameters for P_N, P_O, P_R, P_M comparisons.",0.95,False,Agent-based simulations only.,1,False,Not empirical with humans.,1,False,No lab experiment.,1,True,Results based on simulated agents on networks.,1,False,Not primarily analytical derivations of equilibria.,0.9,200,Same system size as all simulations.,1,10000,"Each simulation ran at least 10,000 steps.",0.9,1,"Continuous contribution choice c_i in [0,1].",0.95,0,No explicit initial fraction in public fund; default assumed private.,0.4,N/R,MPCR not reported numerically.,0.2,N/A,No communicative human interface.,1,0,No mention of display of others’ outcomes to agents beyond payoff-based imitation rule.,0.6,N/A,Punishment is internal to simulation; no identities shown.,1,N/A,"Reputation compensation is an internal state change, not an identity-revealing reward.",1,0,Round information is not modeled as known to agents.,0.7,1,Gossipers who spread incorrect information are punished similarly to P_O.,1,N/R,No explicit numeric cost terms for punishers.,0.2,N/R,"Same influence-reset mechanism as in Section 2.2, but not summarized as a simple scalar tech parameter.",0.3,1,Mechanism includes compensation in the form of reputation restoration for wrongly affected agents.,0.95,N/R,No numerical per-unit cost of reputation compensation is defined.,0.2,N/R,They conceptually describe reputation restoration but do not formalize a per-unit reward factor v like in P_M.,0.3,N/R,Endowment not specified.,0.3,N/R,Only qualitative comparison: P_R is the worst mechanism for speed and similar to P_O/P_N in TC.,0.2,N/R,Exact TC not numerically specified for P_R in any figure.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,0.1,N/R,No explicit payoff totals reported per mechanism.,0.1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Simulation study.,1,"Same PGG and gossip structure as baseline. Under P_R, unqualified gossipers are punished (parameters d=0.4, h=5, θ=0.5) and affected agents have their reputation restored when harmed by incorrect gossip. No monetary transfers take place.",Summarized from mechanism description of P_R and Figure 5 parameters.,1
10.3390_su141912809.md,Punishment with monetary compensation mechanism (P_M),P_M is defined as punishment plus monetary compensation to agents harmed by incorrect gossip.,1,"Coping mechanism for gossip: P_M (punishment plus monetary compensation); tolerance degree d=0.4, penalty time h=5, rebirth coefficient θ=0.5; conformity C_gf=0.5; compensation parameter v=0.01; interpersonal relationships (IT, MT, ET) varied.","P_M specification in Section 2 and parameters from Section 3 & Figure 5 (v=0.01, d=0.4, h=5, θ=0.5, C_gf=0.5).",0.96,False,All results from simulations.,1,False,Not a human experiment.,1,False,No laboratory context.,1,True,Agent-based model executed numerically.,1,False,Outcome properties are studied via numerical runs.,0.9,200,Baseline N=200 used for all simulations.,1,10000,"Each simulation ran at least 10,000 steps.",0.9,1,"Contributions are continuous in [0,1].",0.95,0,No explicit partial pre-commitment to the public good.,0.4,N/R,MPCR not explicitly reported.,0.2,N/A,No human communication feature.,1,0,No described feedback interface; agents rely on local payoff comparison.,0.6,N/A,Punishment is automatic; no IDs shown.,1,N/A,Monetary compensation is internal to simulation; no identity revelation.,1,0,Rounds are not explicit information to simulated agents.,0.7,1,Unqualified gossipers penalized with influence set to 0 for h rounds and then partially restored.,1,N/R,No explicit token cost per unit of punishment is defined; only influence changes.,0.2,N/R,"Technological intensity of punishment described structurally, not as scalar factor per coin.",0.3,1,P_M explicitly provides monetary compensation m v to agents affected by incorrect gossip.,1,0.01,"Compensation value per incident is v (0.01); although framed as compensation, it functions analogously to reward magnitude per incident.",0.6,N/R,Total compensation is m v where m is number of incidents and v=0.01; no separate multiplicative tech (impact-per-coin) is defined.,0.4,N/R,Endowment size not specified.,0.3,N/R,They analyze TC and composition of donation but not normalized contribution rate relative to endowment.,0.3,N/R,"Figure 5 indicates P_M achieves the highest TC and high-level donation (>0.7) exceeds 70%, but no numeric TC values for P_M are labeled.",0.3,N/R,No efficiency ratios computed.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff not numerically summarized for P_M.,0.1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,No human,Simulation only.,1,"Same PGG network and gossip structure. Under P_M, if a gossiper is unqualified (a/(a+b) > d), they are punished (influence reset to 0 for h rounds, then θ I_t) and all agents harmed by their incorrect gossip receive monetary compensation m v (with v=0.01 and m equal to the number of times harmed). P_M yields the fastest generations-to-equilibrium and highest total contributions among mechanisms, with more than 70% of agents donating between 0.7 and 1 in IT, MT, and ET.",Summarized from mechanism description and Figure 5.,1
10.3390_e24091289.md,"Simulation – U=15, full punishment parameter grid",Single evolutionary simulation framework; one environmental condition is the required contribution to resist risk U=15 while varying punishment intensities t1 and t2 over specified ranges.,0.94,"Risk threshold U=15; central punishment intensity t1 ∈ [2, 9.5]; peer punishment intensity t2 ∈ [1, 6.5]; other parameters fixed (N=100, n=10, m=10, u=5, w1=2, w=4, w2=1, β=0.1, α=0.05).","Model description specifies U is chosen from {15,25,35} and later figures describe t1 and t2 ranges with step sizes; other parameters are fixed across simulations.",0.96,False,"Study is an evolutionary game simulation with agents, no human participants.",1.0,False,No controlled behavioral or field experiment; only computational simulations.,1.0,False,No laboratory or in-person sessions; purely computational model.,1.0,True,Authors repeatedly refer to 'simulation' and define an agent-based evolutionary process.,1.0,False,No closed-form analytical derivations reported; results are numerical simulations and visualizations.,0.96,100,They choose a group size of N=a·n=100 with a=10 subgroups of n=10 individuals.,1.0,N/R,They discuss multi-rounds game and evolutionary process but do not state the exact number of rounds.,0.2,1,"Collaborators always contribute a fixed u=5 while defectors contribute 0; there is no continuous choice set, so contributions are effectively all-or-nothing between 0 and 5.",0.9,N/A,No human endowment allocation interface; contributions are algorithmically determined by strategy type in a simulation.,1.0,N/R,"This is not a standard public goods game with a linear multiplier; there is only a threshold U and success/failure payoffs, so MPCR is not defined.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects; no communication channel exists.,1.0,N/A,No user interface; agents interact via payoff rules only.,1.0,N/A,No identities or displays in the computational model.,1.0,N/A,There is no reward mechanism in the model.,1.0,N/A,No human participants and no round display interface.,1.0,1,Both central and peer punishment mechanisms are explicitly modeled.,1.0,N/R,Schema expects a single numeric cost but model has two: central support cost w1=2 and peer punishment cost w2=1; no single aggregate cost is defined.,0.3,N/R,There are two punishment technologies: central (each D loses t1) and peer (each act makes D lose t2) with different intensities; no single combined magnitude is provided.,0.3,0,Model includes only punishments; no rewarding or positive sanctions.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism in the simulations.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1.0,10,"In each round, each individual receives m=10 units of resources.",1.0,N/R,"They report 'collaboration rate' as the percentage of people who successfully resist risk (population-level outcome), not average contribution fraction; no normalized individual contribution rate is given.",0.3,N/R,"Individual collaborator contribution u=5 is a parameter, not a measured dependent variable; realized subgroup average contributions are not tabulated.",0.4,N/R,They discuss cost-benefit ratios and resource wastage but do not report total payoff divided by maximum possible payoff.,0.2,N/R,"Figures show collaboration rate, enhancement, cost-benefit ratios, and resource wastage, but no raw payoff totals per group.",0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,All results come from simulations of an evolutionary game model without human subjects.,1.0,"Evolutionary risk-resistant threshold game with four strategies (C, CP, PP, D); population N=100 split into 10 subgroups of size 10 each round; collaborators contribute u=5 from endowment m=10 toward threshold U=15; central punishment requires total CP support ≥ w=4 (each CP pays w1=2) to fine each D by t1; peer punishers pay w2=1 per punished D, causing loss t2 to that D; strategy updating via imitation based on resources with Fermi rule (β=0.1) and mutation rate α=0.05.",Summarizes the key structural aspects of the game relevant to cooperation and punishment under the U=15 risk condition.,0.98
10.3390_e24091289.md,"Simulation – U=25, full punishment parameter grid",Same simulation model with environmental risk parameter raised to U=25 while varying t1 and t2 as described.,0.94,"Risk threshold U=25; central punishment intensity t1 ∈ [2, 9.5]; peer punishment intensity t2 ∈ [1, 6.5]; other parameters fixed (N=100, n=10, m=10, u=5, w1=2, w=4, w2=1, β=0.1, α=0.05).","Model states U is selected from {15,25,35}; figures 2–6 explicitly present separate panels/columns for U=25 with same punishment grids.",0.96,False,Computational evolutionary simulations only.,1.0,False,No behavioral or field experiment is conducted.,1.0,False,"Work is entirely in silico, not in a lab setting with humans.",1.0,True,All results are from agent-based/evolutionary simulations.,1.0,False,No analytical solution or proof is presented for outcomes; focus is on numerical exploration.,0.96,100,Global population size N=100 is fixed across all simulations.,1.0,N/R,They refer to evolutionary process and stable state but do not specify how many rounds/generations are simulated.,0.2,1,Agents are either collaborators contributing u=5 or defectors contributing 0; no intermediate contribution levels.,0.9,N/A,Not a human-interface game; contributions stem from fixed strategies.,1.0,N/R,"Payoff structure is threshold-based success/failure, not a linear public goods multiplier; MPCR is undefined.",0.9,N/A,No communication channels for simulated agents.,1.0,N/A,No UI or feedback display; only payoff-based imitation.,1.0,N/A,Model does not include identity revelation or observability components.,1.0,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects to whom round numbers could be shown.,1.0,1,Central and peer punishments are both active mechanisms in all conditions.,1.0,N/R,"Two separate per-actor costs (w1=2 for central support, w2=1 per peer punishment) exist; no single numeric cost variable across punishments.",0.3,N/R,"Central punishment fines each D by t1; peer punishment fines by t2 per act; s