10.3389_fpsyg.2022.794953.md,Study 1 – One-round PGG – Intuition punishment decision,"Condition defined by game type (one-round PGG) and decision time (intuition, 5 s limit) in Study 1.",1,Game type: one-round PGG; Decision time: intuition (5 s time pressure); Within-subject factor: later deliberation condition not applied here.,"Study 1 manipulated game type between subjects and decision time within subjects; this row isolates the one-round, intuition condition.",1,True,Used undergraduate human participants making incentivized decisions.,1,True,Random assignment to conditions with controlled payoff structure and time pressure.,1,True,"Conducted in classrooms with controlled instructions, not in participants’ natural environment.",1,False,No computer/agent-based simulation; all decisions by humans.,1,False,No formal purely analytical model reported for this condition.,1,4,Participants divided into groups of four for the PGG.,1,1,One-round PGG condition explicitly described as a one-time event.,1,1,"Participants asked how much of JPY 800 to contribute; implies continuous choice from 0 to 800, not all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,"Standard PGG: all JPY 800 initially private, then participants choose how much to contribute; no automatic public contribution.",0.9,0.5,"Total contributions doubled then divided equally among four; per contributed JPY 1 each member gets JPY 0.5, so MPCR = 0.5.",1,0,No mention of communication; classroom setting with private decision sheets and anonymity emphasized.,0.9,1,Feedback sheet showed group total contribution and individual selfish member’s payoff; summary of others’ outcomes.,1,0,Only “selfish person” identified; no identifying information about which participant punished.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,0,Participants told it is one-time; no dynamic display of remaining rounds during punishment stage.,0.8,1,Participants could pay to deduct from the selfish person’s payoff.,1,1,"Options show each JPY 100 paid leads to JPY 300 deducted; cost per 1 unit of deduction is 1/3. Here we record cost per 1 unit spent, which is 1 token of own money.",0.8,3,Punishment efficiency is three times the amount the participant paid for the punishment.,1,0,"No option to increase payoffs of others, only to deduct.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,800,Each participant was given JPY 800 in the PGG.,1,N/R,Only reported that mean contribution did not differ between one-round and repeated; no numeric mean provided.,0,N/R,Contribution means not numerically reported for each condition.,0,N/R,No aggregate payoff or efficiency statistic reported.,0,N/R,No mean group payoff reported beyond illustrative fake feedback.,0,Japan,Participants were Japanese female undergraduates; authors from Japanese universities.,1,"Mean 18.85, SD 0.73; undergraduates",Explicitly reported for Study 1 participants.,1,All female,Ninety-one Japanese female undergraduates.,1,Undergraduate students,Participants described as undergraduates.,1,On site,Conducted in a classroom setting with physical instruction sheets.,1,"Public goods game with 4 members; each receives JPY 800, chooses contribution; contributions doubled and split equally. Punishment stage one week later with fake feedback about one free rider; intuition punishment decision within 5 s using discrete options (0–400 JPY cost, 0–1,200 JPY deduction).",Directly summarized from Study 1 procedure for one-round condition and intuition stage.,1
