n was JPY 508.70, indicating lower punishment under deliberation than under intuition.",Summarized from Study 2 SPPG description and results.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2022.794953.md,Study 2 – TPPG – Intuition punishment decision,Third-party punishment game with time-pressured punishment decision.,1,Game type: Third-party punishment game (third party is not direct victim); Decision time: intuition (5 s time pressure).,Study 2 between-subject factor is partyness (TPPG vs SPPG); this row represents TPPG intuition stage.,1,True,Human subjects in TPPG scenario.,1,True,Random assignment to roles and game types; punishment decision under time constraint.,1,True,In-person classroom experiment.,1,False,No simulations used.,1,False,Not an analytical-only condition.,1,3,"Participants divided into groups of three: proposer, recipient, third-party.",1,1,Described as a one-time game; no repetition.,1,1,"Proposer chooses any share between JPY 0 and 1,200 for recipient; third party chooses among punishment levels; not all-or-nothing.",0.8,0,"Not a PGG; all endowments start private (JPY 1,200 with proposer, JPY 400 with third party, implied 0 with recipient).",0.7,N/R,No public good multiplication.,1,0,No communication allowed between roles.,0.9,1,"Third party informed that proposer kept JPY 1,200 for themselves; summary of others’ payoffs.",1,0,Punishers’ identities not shared.,0.7,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Game is one-shot; no indicator of multiple rounds.,0.9,1,Third party can pay to deduct from proposer’s payoff.,1,1,Pay 100 JPY to deduct 300; same as SPPG.,0.8,3,Deduction equals three times cost.,1,0,No reward options.,1,N/A,Rewards not used.,1,N/A,Rewards not used.,1,400,Third parties are given JPY 400 by the experimenter.,1,N/A,No PGG-style contributions.,1,N/A,Third party does not contribute; only punishes.,1,N/R,No efficiency indices reported.,0,N/R,No summary of combined payoffs.,0,Japan,Same Study 2 Japanese undergraduate population.,1,"Mean 19.83, SD 0.71; undergraduates",Study 2 description.,1,All female,All participants female.,1,Undergraduate students,Sample characteristics.,1,On site,Classroom setting.,1,"Third-party punishment game based on dictator structure: proposer has JPY 1,200 and keeps all per fake feedback; recipient’s share is 0; third party has JPY 400 and may pay 0–400 (in steps of 100) to deduct 0–1,200 (3× cost) from proposer. Intuition punishment decision must be made within 5 s. Mean amount deducted from proposer’s payoff in TPPG intuition condition was JPY 821.74.",Directly from Study 2 TPPG instructions and reported results.,1
10.3389_fpsyg.2022.794953.md,Study 2 – TPPG – Deliberation punishment decision,Third-party punishment game with deliberative punishment decision without time constraint.,1,Game type: Third-party punishment game; Decision time: deliberation (no time limit).,Within-subject time manipulation; this row refers to TPPG deliberation stage.,1,True,Same participants as TPPG intuition condition.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of time pressure.,1,True,In-person classroom environment.,1,False,No simulated agents.,1,False,Not analytical-only.,1,3,"Proposer, recipient, and third-party.",1,1,One-shot TPPG; no repetition.,1,1,"Same choice sets as TPPG intuition (continuous share choice for proposer, discrete punishment for third party).",0.8,0,No initial contribution to public fund; this is not a PGG.,0.7,N/R,No public good multiplier.,1,0,No chat or communication provided.,0.9,1,Third party sees feedback about proposer’s decision and payoff.,1,0,Punisher’s identity not revealed.,0.7,0,No reward option.,1,0,"One-shot game, no displayed number of rounds.",0.9,1,Third party can punish selfish proposer without time limit.,1,1,Same punishment technology: pay 100 JPY to deduct 300 JPY.,0.8,3,Deduction equal to three times cost.,1,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,N/A,Rewards not available.,1,400,Third parties have endowment of JPY 400.,1,N/A,No contribution behavior in a public goods sense.,1,N/A,Third party only punishes.,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not reported.,0,Japan,Same Study 2 Japanese sample.,1,"Mean 19.83, SD 0.71; undergraduates",Study 2 description.,1,All female,Study 2 sample all female undergraduates.,1,Undergraduate students,Sample description.,1,On site,Conducted in classroom.,1,"Same TPPG setup as intuition condition. Here, third parties choose punishment level without time pressure. Mean amount deducted from proposer’s payoff in TPPG deliberation condition was JPY 913.04, higher than under intuition.",Directly from Study 2 TPPG procedure and result values.,1
10.1525_sop.2004.47.1.109.md,"Simulation – Accelerating production, N=10, Sc=75, Si=150, varying M and initial cooperators","Main simulation condition described with N=10, Sc=75, Si=150, costs 100/3/3, E2=E3=0.2; core results in Figures 1–3 varying monitoring efficacy M and initial cooperation at levels 1 or 2.",0.95,Agent monitoring efficacy M (0–1) and number of initial cooperators at level 1 (0–10) or level 2 (0–10) under accelerating production function,Text and Figures 1–2 explicitly vary M and initial cooperators; production function specified as accelerating EA=(1−M′)^{N(1−P)}.,0.98,False,Study is a computer simulation with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a theoretical simulation model.,1,False,No laboratory or physical setting; purely computational.,1,True,Described repeatedly as a simulation model; agents are simulated group members.,1,False,"While equations are used, core results derive from numerical simulation rather than closed-form solutions.",0.9,10,Standard parameter values explicitly given as group size N=10 for Figures 1–3.,1,6,Model cycles through members until equilibrium; text notes that with N=10 equilibrium is always reached within six complete cycles. Use 6 as effective maximum iterations.,0.7,1,"Agents choose among 6 discrete behavior combinations; no continuous contribution amount is modeled, so treat as all-or-nothing choice at each cooperation level.",0.9,N/A,No endowment or public-good contribution pool for human participants; abstract compliance model.,1,N/R,"No marginal per capita return is defined; payoffs involve sanctions and costs, not linear public goods returns.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication stage in the simulation.,1,0,Simulated agents best-respond to current state; no interface or feedback summaries are discussed.,0.9,0,Identity and observability of who is punished are not modeled; sanctions come from an external agent only.,0.9,0,Rewards/sanctions are applied by an agent; no notion of revealed identities within group is modeled.,0.9,0,Agents do not have information about a finite horizon; they myopically best-respond until equilibrium.,0.9,1,The model includes individual and collective sanctions that can be interpreted as punishments or rewards.,1,N/R,"Cost to group members of punishing others is modeled via K2, K3, but cost to the agent per sanction unit is not parameterized as a per-unit cost in payoff units in the core model.",0.6,N/R,Magnitude of punishment per unit cost for the punisher is not formulated as a simple linear technology parameter.,0.6,1,Author states sanctions can be treated as either negative (punishments) or positive (rewards); structure is symmetric.,0.9,N/R,Analogous to punishment; no explicit per-unit cost parameter for rewards to the agent.,0.6,N/R,No linear mapping from cost to reward magnitude is parameterized.,0.6,N/R,No per-period monetary or token endowment for players is defined; only costs K1–K3 and sanctions appear.,1,N/R,Outcomes are expressed as compliance levels and payoffs; no normalized contribution rate to a public account is reported.,1,N/R,No token contributions are modeled; only binary/multilevel cooperation choices at three levels.,1,N/R,"No ratio of achieved to maximum group payoff is explicitly computed, although payoffs are discussed qualitatively.",0.7,N/R,"Figure 3 shows equilibrium payoff qualitatively, but exact numeric payoff values are not given in the text provided.",0.6,N/A,No human subjects are involved; agents are simulated.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational simulation with no human participants or physical setting.,1,"Simulation of group-mediated social control with N=10 symmetric agents choosing among six discrete strategies that combine cooperation/defection at three levels (compliance with external agent, enforcement of others’ compliance, opposition to enforcement). Parameters: monitoring efficacy M, individual sanction Si=150, collective sanction Sc=75, costs K1=100, K2=3, K3=3, internal control efficacies E2=0.2, E3=0.2. Starting state usually full defection; some runs introduce a specified number of initial cooperators at level 1 or 2. Production function for primary public good (compliance) is accelerating: EA=(1−M′)^{N(1−P)}.",Summarizes detailed model description and standard parameter set used in the main results section for this condition.,0.98
10.1525_sop.2004.47.1.109.md,"Simulation – Accelerating production, N=10, varying Sc with Si=150",Robustness analysis varying collective sanction Sc from 25 to at least 475 with individual sanction fixed at 150.,0.9,Collective sanction Sc (25–≥475) with individual sanction Si=150; monitoring efficacy M varied; initial cooperation varied; accelerating production function,Section 'Varying Individual and Collective Sanctions' describes varying Sc over a range with other parameters at Heckathorn values.,0.95,False,Computer simulation only.,1,False,"Theoretical robustness checks within the simulation, not human experiments.",1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Parameter sweeps in the simulation model.,1,False,Outcomes obtained numerically from simulations.,0.9,10,States 'given the Heckathorn values for all other parameters'; Heckathorn base uses N=10.,0.95,6,Same dynamics as base model; equilibrium reached within six cycles for N=10.,0.7,1,Behavioral choice structure unchanged.,0.95,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,No linear public good.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,0,No feedback interface.,0.9,0,No identity information in sanctions.,0.9,0,Same as punishment.,0.9,0,No round information modeled.,0.9,1,Collective and individual sanctions are central parameters.,1,N/R,Agent’s per-unit sanction cost not parameterized.,0.6,N/R,No explicit punishment technology parameter.,0.6,1,Sanctions can be interpreted as rewards; symmetric treatment.,0.9,N/R,Not parameterized.,0.6,N/R,Not specified.,0.6,N/R,No token endowment.,1,N/R,"Outcomes described as compliance levels, not contributions.",1,N/R,No numeric contributions.,1,N/R,Efficiency not numerically reported.,0.8,N/R,Payoffs under different Sc are not listed numerically.,0.6,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Computational robustness analysis.,1,"Same group-mediated social control simulation as base condition with N=10, costs K1=100, K2=3, K3=3, internal control efficacies E2=E3=0.2, accelerating production function. Independent variation of collective sanction Sc from 25 upward (noted range 25–≥475) with individual sanction fixed at Si=150 to see when the catastrophe (compliance collapse at intermediate M) appears or disappears.",Summarizes what is varied and what stays fixed for this robustness condition.,0.95
