10.1002_pchj.259.md,Baseline (B) – No third-party sanctions,"Paper defines four treatments: baseline (B), TP, TR, TP/R; this row covers the baseline without third-party sanctions.",1,"Third-party sanction condition: Baseline (no third-party punishment or reward); group composition: 4 PGG-players, no TP-players.",Condition differs from others only by absence of third-party sanction stage and by group size (4 instead of 4+4).,1,True,Human participants (students) played the public goods game in this treatment.,1,True,Controlled lab-style public goods game with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted on-site in a university setting with standardized instructions and tokens; not a field or online study.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical/mathematical model analyzed in lieu of data.,1,4,"“In the B treatment, each group consisted of four participants.”",1,10,“played the 10-period simultaneous-move PGG” and “finitely repeated PGG for 10 periods”.,1,1,"Each player chose gi with 0 ≤ gi ≤ 20, so any amount was possible, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each member is endowed with 20 tokens in private and decides how many to contribute; all endowment starts private.,1,0.4,"Total contributions G multiplied by k=1.6 and split among 4: each receives (1.6/4)*G=0.4G, so MPCR=0.4.",1,0,Participants “could not talk with each other during the experiment.”,1,1,After each round each player saw contributions of other group members and their own earnings on a feedback card.,1,0,"No punishment stage in this treatment; identities of any punisher not applicable, but field defined as 0 for non-existing mechanism.",0.7,0,No reward stage in this treatment; same logic as punishment.,0.7,1,Participants were told “This experiment consists of 10 rounds” in instructions; implies they know total number of rounds.,1,0,Baseline treatment has only contribution stage with no sanctions.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism in baseline treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,20,“Each group member was given y=20 tokens at the beginning of each round.”,1,N/R,Only raw contribution amounts (tokens) and group means are reported; no normalized proportion reported and insufficient per-round detail to reconstruct overall fraction.,0.4,7.65,"Results section: final-period mean contribution in B treatment is 7.65 (SD 3.46, N=10). Use as representative DV.",0.8,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff reported or derivable from given aggregates.,0.2,N/R,"Only average individual earnings across all treatments (¥ amounts) are reported, not separated by treatment.",0.2,China,"Participants were students from Zhejiang Sci-Tech University, which is in Hangzhou, China; wage comparison given for China.",1,N/R,Described only as undergraduate or graduate students; no age range or mean reported.,0.3,"Mixed; 137 males, 143 females across all treatments",Overall sample gender counts reported but not by treatment; assume similar mix across treatments.,0.6,Undergraduate and graduate students (non-economics majors),“All participants were undergraduate or graduate students from all disciplines except economics.”,1,On site,"Ran with physical tokens, cards, and in-person consent at the university; no indication of online interface.",1,"Standard linear public goods game with 4 players, 20-token endowment, continuous contributions, multiplier 1.6, returns equal for all; feedback each round includes all group members’ contributions and own earnings.",Summarizes the structural description given under B treatment design.,1
10.1002_pchj.259.md,TP – Third-party punishment only,One of four treatments; this row captures the treatment labeled TP in the paper.,1,Third-party sanction condition: TP (third-party punishment only); group composition: 4 PGG-players + 4 TP-players; TP-players can assign deduction points only.,Condition defined by presence of punishment-only decision stage with third parties after contributions.,1,True,Human students acted as PGG-players and TP-players.,1,True,Randomly assigned groups and controlled payoff structure in lab-style game.,1,True,Conducted physically at a university lab environment with tokens and paper cards.,1,False,No computer simulations described.,1,False,No purely analytical modeling of this treatment without data.,1,8,"“In the other three treatments, each group consisted of eight participants, half of whom played the PGG … and the others played the role of third parties.”",1,10,All treatments used 10 periods/finitely repeated PGG.,1,1,Same contribution decision as baseline: 0 ≤ gi ≤ 20.,1,0,PGG-players receive 20-token private endowment each round; decide contribution.,1,0.4,Same public goods stage: contributions multiplied by 1.6 and shared among 4; MPCR=1.6/4.,1,0,Global rule: participants could not talk with each other during the experiment.,1,1,"After each round PGG-players saw all group contributions, deduction points received, and their own earnings.",1,0,Third parties are separate participants; PGG-players see deduction points but not which TP-player imposed them.,0.8,0,No rewards exist in this condition.,1,1,"Instructions for TP/R treatment explicitly mention 10 rounds; structure is shared across sanction treatments, so total rounds are known.",0.9,1,TP-players can “punish … by assigning deduction points.”,1,1,Assigning one deduction point costs the TP-player one token.,1,3,“Assigning one deduction point … deducted three tokens from the sanctioned player's payoff.”,1,0,Only punishment (deduction points) available in TP treatment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,20,PGG-players: same 20-token endowment at start of each round; TP-players get 32 tokens but CONFIG_endowment refers to players in main game.,1,N/R,"Mean contribution level reported (12.36) but not normalized to 0–1 fraction; could be 12.36/20=0.618 but not explicitly reported, so left N/R.",0.3,12.36,"Reported as average level of cooperation in TP treatment (M=12.36, SD=2.64, N=10).",0.9,N/R,No efficiency ratio presented; only contributions and some earnings comparisons.,0.2,N/R,No treatment-specific group payoff totals are reported.,0.2,China,Same participant pool across treatments (Chinese university).,1,N/R,No age distribution reported; only student status.,0.3,"Mixed; 137 males, 143 females across all treatments","Only aggregate gender split given, not by treatment.",0.6,Undergraduate and graduate students (non-economics majors),Applies uniformly across treatments.,1,On site,Same experimental setting as baseline; physical presence in university.,1,"Each group has 4 PGG-players and 4 TP-players. In each of 10 rounds, PGG-players contribute to a 4-person public good (k=1.6). Then all 4 TP-players independently assign integer deduction points to each PGG-player at cost 1 token per point, reducing that PGG-player’s payoff by 3 tokens per point. Only one TP-player’s decision is randomly implemented for payoffs, but all TP-players pay their own costs and do not know whether their decision was implemented.",Summarizes structural details specific to TP treatment from method section.,1
10.1002_pchj.259.md,TR – Third-party reward only,One of four named treatments; this row covers the TR (third-party reward) treatment.,1,Third-party sanction condition: TR (third-party reward only); group composition: 4 PGG-players + 4 TP-players; TP-players can assign increment points only.,Defined by presence of reward-only stage and no punishment.,1,True,Human participants serve as PGG-players and third-party rewarders.,1,True,Controlled experimental design with explicit payoff rules.,1,True,Run in-person at Zhejiang Sci-Tech University using physical tokens and cards.,1,False,No simulation work reported for this treatment.,1,False,No analytical-only modeling.,1,8,Same 4+4 structure as TP and TP/R treatments.,1,10,All treatments repeated for 10 rounds.,1,1,Contributions are continuous from 0 to 20 tokens.,1,0,PGG-players start with 20 tokens privately each round.,1,0.4,Same PGG production technology as other treatments.,1,0,Communication prohibited for all participants.,1,1,"PGG-players see contributions, increment points received, and own earnings on feedback card each round.",1,0,No punishment option exists in TR treatment.,1,0,No indication that PGG-players learn which TP-player rewarded them; only number of increment points shown.,0.8,1,"Shared 10-round structure; instructions for TP/R explicitly state 10 rounds, implying similar knowledge here.",0.9,0,TR treatment only allows increment (reward) points.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,TP-players “had the opportunity to reward the PGG-players … by assigning increment points.”,1,1,Assigning one increment point costs TP-player one token.,1,3,"If PGG-player receives one increment point, “their earnings would be increased by three tokens.”",1,20,Each PGG-player given 20 tokens at beginning of each round; TP-players receive 32 but CONFIG refers to game endowment.,1,N/R,"Mean contribution reported (10.33), but no normalized rate expressed; not explicitly computed in paper.",0.3,10.33,"Reported as average level of cooperation in TR treatment (M=10.33, SD=3.37, N=10).",0.9,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure for TR condition.,0.2,N/R,Treatment-specific group earnings not provided.,0.2,China,Same sample pool as other treatments.,1,N/R,Not specified beyond student status.,0.3,"Mixed; 137 males, 143 females across all treatments",Only aggregate numbers; assignment to treatments not broken down.,0.6,Undergraduate and graduate students (non-economics majors),Uniform description across entire sample.,1,On site,Same lab-like implementation as other experimental treatments.,1,"Each round: four PGG-players play the same 4-person PGG (20-token endowment, k=1.6). Then four TP-players each receive 32 tokens and assign non-negative integer increment points to each PGG-player at cost 1 token per point, increasing that PGG-player’s payoff by 3 tokens per point. Only one TP-player’s reward vector is randomly selected to be payoff-relevant for PGG-players, but all TP-players pay costs and are unaware of implementation.",Synthesizes TR-specific procedural details from method section.,1
10.1002_pchj.259.md,TP/R – Combination of third-party punishment and reward,Fourth treatment where TP-players can both punish and reward (TP/R).,1,Third-party sanction condition: TP/R (third-party punishment and reward simultaneously available); group composition: 4 PGG-players + 4 TP-players; TP-players can assign both deduction and increment points.,Condition differs by offering both sanctions to third parties.,1,True,Human participants in mixed roles (PGG-player and TP-player) in this condition.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of sanction availability.,1,True,Conducted at Zhejiang Sci-Tech University with in-person procedures and monetary incentives.,1,False,No simulation component described.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,8,Same 4 PGG-players + 4 TP-players structure as TP and TR treatments.,1,10,"Instructions for TP/R explicitly: “The experiment consists of 10 rounds, and each round … has two stages.”",1,1,PGG-players choose any gi from 0 to 20 tokens.,1,0,20-token private endowment at start of each round.,1,0.4,"Same PGG technology: contributions multiplied by 1.6, divided among 4 players.",1,0,All participants forbidden to talk during the experiment.,1,1,"Feedback cards show others’ contributions, deduction and increment points, and own earnings.",1,0,PGG-players are informed of “the number of deduction points and increment points” but not which TP-player imposed them.,0.8,0,Identities of specific rewarders not mentioned; only totals displayed.,0.8,1,TP/R instructions explicitly state that there are 10 rounds.,1,1,TP-players can assign deduction points under TP/R.,1,1,“One token spent by the TP-player … reduced the PGG-player's payoff by three tokens.” Same 1-token cost as TP.,1,3,Explicitly: one token reduces PGG-player’s payoff by three tokens.,1,1,TP-players can also assign increment points under TP/R.,1,1,One token spent increases PGG-player’s payoff by three tokens; cost is one token per increment point.,1,3,“One token spent by the TP-player increased … the PGG-player's payoff by three tokens.”,1,20,PGG-players receive 20 tokens per round; TP-players get 32.,1,N/R,Mean contribution reported (14.39) but not expressed as a fraction of 20; not explicitly normalized.,0.3,14.39,"Reported as average level of cooperation in TP/R treatment (M=14.39, SD=2.02, N=10).",0.9,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric; only contributions and some earnings/rank transformations.,0.2,N/R,Group or treatment-specific payoff totals not given.,0.2,China,Same Chinese university sample.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0.3,"Mixed; 137 males, 143 females across all treatments",Same gender pool; no per-treatment split.,0.6,Undergraduate and graduate students (non-economics majors),Same description for all participants.,1,On site,"Explicit reference to written instructions, physical tokens, and face-to-face sessions at the university.",1,"Each of 10 rounds has two stages. Stage 1: 4 PGG-players play a 4-person linear PGG (20-token endowment, 0–20 contribution, multiplier 1.6, equal sharing). Stage 2: 4 TP-players each receive 32 tokens and can assign non-negative integer deduction and/or increment points to each PGG-player at cost 1 token per point; each point changes the PGG-player’s payoff by −3 (deduction) or +3 (increment). For each PGG-player, one TP-player’s full vector of sanctions is randomly selected to determine that player’s implemented punishment and reward for payoffs, but all TP-players pay the cost of points they assign and remain uninformed about whether their decision was implemented.",Summarizes TP/R-specific rules from method and appendix instructions.,1
