10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Baseline – No-Conflict treatment,Paper labels first study as 'baseline experiment' with two treatments: no-conflict and conflict. This row is the no-conflict treatment.,1,"Conflict possibility: No (K = ∞); other parameters fixed (w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100).",Treatments differ only in conflict technology (K=∞ vs K=0.1). All other game parameters held constant.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants at LABEX.,1,True,Random assignment to roles and treatments; controlled lab game.,1,True,Run in HEC Lausanne laboratory (LABEX).,1,False,All results from human-subject lab sessions; no agent-based or numerical simulation reported.,1,False,Paper contains theoretical framework but this row describes empirical lab treatment.,1,2,Each interaction is between one manager and one worker (principal-agent pair).,1,15,“The experiment lasts for a total of 15 periods.”,1,1,"Workers choose binary effort, but contribution in sense of money to principal is not chosen; cooperation is effort choice, not token contribution. Public-goods all-or-nothing notion not directly applicable; coded as any-amount allowed is not meaningful. Set to 1 given unconstrained conflict choice absent.",0.2,N/R,No endowment allocation between public/private accounts; not a contribution game.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Baseline experiment explicitly has 'lack of any communication'.,1,0,Participants only learn own payoff each round; no mention of summaries of others’ payoffs or conflicts.,0.9,0,Pairs are anonymous; no mention that identity of punisher is revealed to manager beyond knowing their matched worker decided on conflict.,0.7,0,Bonus is chosen by uniquely matched manager; no separate rewarding party identity to show.,0.8,1,Standard z-Tree implementation usually informs about total periods; text does not contradict. Assume subjects know 15 periods.,0.7,0,"No-conflict treatment removes conflict stage; K = ∞, so punishing option not available.",1,N/A,Punishment not available in this treatment.,1,N/A,Punishment not available in this treatment.,1,1,Manager can pay discretionary bonus B=50 to worker.,1,50,"Bonus payment b ∈ {0, B=50} is a direct transfer from manager to worker with that face value.",1,1,"Each bonus unit is a 1:1 transfer: manager loses 50, worker gains 50.",1,100,Worker receives fixed wage w=100 each period. That is the certain monetary base before effort cost and bonus.,1,N/R,"Main cooperative action is binary effort; paper reports proportion of high effort, not normalized contribution of monetary endowment.",1,0.404,Use high-effort frequency as cooperation measure. Baseline no-conflict: 'rate with which workers choose high effort rises from 40.4% in the no-conflict treatment to 47.5% in the conflict treatment.',1,N/R,They report average total surplus (233.8) but not normalized by first-best (310) per treatment explicitly as ratio.,0.7,233.8,“Total surplus decreases insignificantly from 233.8 in the no-conflict treatment to 221.6 in the conflict treatment.”,1,Switzerland,Sessions conducted at HEC Lausanne (Switzerland). Subject pool is local university students.,0.9,N/R,Paper only notes student subject pool; no age statistics reported.,1,N/R,No gender composition or balance information reported.,1,University students (various fields),Recruited from regular subject pool at HEC Lausanne 'covering all fields of study'.,1,On site,Physical laboratory sessions at LABEX (HEC Lausanne).,1,"Principal-agent subjective evaluation game: worker chooses effort e∈{0,1} at cost c=10; output r∈{150,350} with Prob(r_H|e=1)=0.85, Prob(r_H|e=0)=0.15; manager and worker observe private noisy signals with p=q=0.75; manager offers fixed wage 100 and discretionary bonus 50; in this treatment, no punishment stage.",Summarizes Section 2.1 and 3.1 implementation for this treatment.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Baseline – Conflict treatment,"Same baseline experiment, but treatment where conflict is enabled (K=0.1).",1,"Conflict possibility: Yes (K = 0.1, worker can initiate costly conflict); other parameters fixed (w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100).",Only difference from no-conflict is presence and cost of conflict stage.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory treatment.,1,True,Randomized lab experiment with controlled manipulation of conflict technology.,1,True,Run in LABEX at HEC Lausanne.,1,False,No computer-only simulations for this treatment.,1,False,"Row represents empirical condition, not the theoretical analysis.",1,2,One manager and one worker per interaction.,1,15,Same 15 periods as described for baseline experiment.,1,1,Effort is binary; no continuous contribution. Public-good all-or-nothing concept not directly applicable; keep coding consistent with other treatments.,0.2,N/R,No initial contribution allocation between public/private accounts.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,0,Baseline experiment has no communication stage.,1,0,Subjects learn only their own payoff each period; no mention of others’ outcomes.,0.9,0,Manager knows only that her matched worker initiated conflict; broader identity revelation not indicated.,0.7,0,Bonus source is the unique manager; no special identity revelation mechanism.,0.8,1,Likely common knowledge of 15 periods in z-Tree implementation; not contradicted by text.,0.7,1,Conflict treatment: 'the full game is played'; worker can initiate conflict reducing manager payoff.,1,10,"Worker cost k(d)=d*F*K with F=100, K=0.1, so a unit conflict (d=1) costs 10.",1,100,Manager’s damage f(d)=d*F with F=100; a unit conflict reduces manager payoff by 100.,1,1,Manager has discretionary bonus option B=50.,1,50,Bonus transfer from manager to worker is 50.,1,1,1:1 transfer of tokens via bonus.,1,100,Worker’s fixed wage w=100 per period.,1,N/R,"Paper reports effort frequencies and surplus, not normalized contribution rates.",1,0.475,High-effort frequency in baseline conflict treatment is 47.5%. Used as effort-based cooperation measure.,1,N/R,They report average total surplus (221.6) but not surplus divided by first-best (310).,0.7,221.6,“Total surplus decreases insignificantly from 233.8 in the no-conflict treatment to 221.6 in the conflict treatment.”,1,Switzerland,Sessions at HEC Lausanne (Swiss university).,0.9,N/R,No age summary statistics provided.,1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,1,University students (various fields),Recruited from LABEX subject pool covering all fields of study.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at HEC Lausanne.,1,"Same subjective evaluation game as in baseline no-conflict, but adds stage where worker can initiate conflict d∈{0,1} (continuous [0,1] in first two experiments) after observing own signal and manager’s bonus. Conflict reduces manager’s payoff by 100 and costs worker 10.",Summarizes key structure with conflict stage for this treatment.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Communication experiment – No-Conflict treatment,Second experiment labeled 'communication experiment' with no-conflict and conflict treatments; this is no-conflict condition.,1,"Conflict possibility: No (K = ∞); Structured preplay communication allowed each period via predefined messages about effort/bonus (no conflict messages for workers here). Game parameters: w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100.","Relative to baseline no-conflict, adds communication stage; conflict stage still removed.",1,True,Human-subject lab sessions in 2013.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of communication and conflict availability.,1,True,Conducted at LABEX.,1,False,No simulation/agent-based results.,1,False,This row is empirical treatment.,1,2,Dyadic manager-worker interactions.,1,15,Design reused from baseline: 15 periods.,1,1,Effort is binary; no continuous contribution decision.,0.2,N/R,Not a public good game.,1,N/R,No MPCR defined.,1,1,"“Communication experiment” adds communication stage each period; structured, not free-form.",1,0,Text only mentions feedback of own payoff; no summaries of others' behavior.,0.9,0,No punishment stage in no-conflict treatment.,1,0,Manager identity as rewarder is implicit within anonymous pair; no additional identity revelation.,0.8,1,Likely common knowledge 15 periods; same as baseline.,0.7,0,"As in baseline no-conflict, conflict stage removed (K=∞).",1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,1,Manager can pay bonus 50.,1,50,Same contract as baseline.,1,1,Bonus is direct transfer.,1,100,Base wage w=100.,1,N/R,"They track high-effort frequency and surplus, not normalized rates.",1,0.518,“rate with which workers choose high effort rises from 51.8% in the no-conflict treatment to 58.5% in the conflict treatment.”,1,N/R,"Average total surplus 243.7 reported, but not normalized by first-best.",0.7,243.7,“Total surplus decreases insignificantly from 243.7 in the no-conflict treatment to 237.7 in the conflict treatment.”,1,Switzerland,Same lab and subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,University students (various fields),Same LABEX pool.,1,On site,Lab experiment at HEC Lausanne.,1,Same subjective evaluation game as baseline; added initial communication stage where manager and worker send predefined strategy messages (manager: about bonus policy; worker: about effort; no conflict messages since no punishment stage).,Summarizes communication experiment specifics for no-conflict condition.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Communication experiment – Conflict treatment,Communication experiment’s treatment where conflict is available and workers also communicate conflict strategy.,1,"Conflict possibility: Yes (K = 0.1, worker can initiate costly conflict); Structured preplay communication each period via predefined messages about effort, conflict, and bonus. Game parameters: w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100.","Relative to communication no-conflict, only change is presence of conflict stage and worker conflict message set.",1,True,Human lab treatment.,1,True,Controlled lab manipulation.,1,True,At LABEX.,1,False,"Data from participants, not agents.",1,False,Empirical condition.,1,2,"One manager, one worker per match.",1,15,Same as other experiments.,1,1,No continuous contribution decision; binary effort.,0.2,N/R,No allocation of endowment to public account.,1,N/R,Not a public good.,1,1,Communication stage present with predefined messages for both parties.,1,0,No mention of aggregated info about others; feedback is individual.,0.9,0,Manager sees if current worker punished but not longer-run identity info; no special disclosure mentioned.,0.7,0,Reward (bonus) comes from matched manager; no framing about revealing identity beyond pair.,0.8,1,Likely known that game has 15 periods; same platform.,0.7,1,Conflict treatment uses 'full game'; worker can trigger conflict.,1,10,"Same parameters: F=100, K=0.1 => worker cost 10 per conflict.",1,100,Manager loses 100 per d=1.,1,1,Manager can pay bonus 50.,1,50,B=50.,1,1,1:1 transfer.,1,100,Fixed wage w=100.,1,N/R,Main cooperation variable is effort; they report its frequency.,1,0.585,“rate with which workers choose high effort rises from 51.8% in the no-conflict treatment to 58.5% in the conflict treatment.”,1,N/R,"They give total surplus 237.7, but not normalized by first-best.",0.7,237.7,“Total surplus decreases insignificantly from 243.7 in the no-conflict treatment to 237.7 in the conflict treatment.”,1,Switzerland,Same lab and pool.,0.9,N/R,Not provided.,1,N/R,Not provided.,1,University students (various fields),Same LABEX subject pool.,1,On site,Laboratory communication experiment.,1,"Same subjective evaluation game as baseline with added communication. Workers also send a second part of message about intended conflict strategy (always punish, punish if no bonus, punish if no bonus and good signal, never punish, or no message). Conflict stage identical to baseline conflict (continuous d in {0.0,…,1.0}).",Summarizes communication plus punishment structure.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Agreement experiment – No-Conflict treatment,Third study labeled 'agreement experiment' with code of conduct and two treatments; this is no-conflict.,1,"Conflict possibility: No (K = ∞); Nonbinding electronic code of conduct (pay-for-output norm) each period; if either party refuses to sign, trade does not occur and both get outside option 40. Game: same parameters w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100.","Relative to baseline no-conflict, adds agreement stage with code of conduct enforcing PO norm, and outside option.",1,True,Human-subject lab experiment in 2017.,1,True,Controlled treatment variation of institutional environment.,1,True,Run at LABEX.,1,False,No simulation models for this treatment.,1,False,"Empirical, not pure theory.",1,2,Single worker and manager per match.,1,15,All experiments reuse 15-period horizon.,1,1,Binary effort; no continuous contribution choice.,0.2,N/R,No contribution structure.,1,N/R,No linear public-good mechanism.,1,0,Agreement experiment does not add communication; only agreement signing.,1,0,Only own payoff feedback described.,0.9,0,No conflict stage in no-conflict treatment.,1,0,No special identity display for rewarding.,0.8,1,Likely informed about 15 periods.,0.7,0,No-conflict treatment; K=∞ and no conflict stage.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,1,Manager can pay bonus 50.,1,50,Same contract.,1,1,1:1 transfer.,1,100,Fixed wage w=100.,1,N/R,Cooperation measured as high-effort share.,1,0.491,“rate with which workers choose high effort rises from 49.1% in the no-conflict treatment to 52.4% in the conflict treatment.”,1,N/R,Average total surplus 243.1 given but not normalized.,0.7,243.1,“total surplus decreases from 243.1 in the no-conflict treatment to 224.5 in the conflict treatment.”,1,Switzerland,Same lab and subject pool.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,University students (various fields),LABEX pool description unchanged.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions.,1,Same subjective evaluation game plus pre-stage agreement: both parties asked to sign code of conduct prescribing PO norm (worker exerts high effort; manager pays bonus iff high signal). Refusal leads to no trade this period and outside option 40 each.,Summarizes agreement mechanism in no-conflict setting.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Agreement experiment – Conflict treatment,Agreement experiment’s conflict-enabled treatment with same code of conduct plus conflict stage.,1,"Conflict possibility: Yes (K = 0.1); Nonbinding code of conduct each period prescribing PO norm, plus outside option 40 if either party refuses to sign; conflict stage available. Game parameters: w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100.",Adds conflict stage and grievance-like commitment in the code of conduct; same as agreement experiment description.,1,True,Human lab experiment.,1,True,Treatment variation in environment within lab.,1,True,At LABEX.,1,False,No model-only simulation.,1,False,Empirical treatment row.,1,2,"One manager, one worker.",1,15,Same round structure.,1,1,Binary effort only.,0.2,N/R,No contribution to a public pot.,1,N/R,Not a public good.,1,0,"Only agreement stage, no communication about strategies beyond signing.",1,0,Only own payoff provided.,0.9,0,Worker’s sanctions affect current manager; no mention of identity revelation beyond that.,0.7,0,No explicit statement that manager identity is disclosed; within pair implicit.,0.8,1,Likely known 15 rounds.,0.7,1,Agreement conflict treatment plays full game with conflict stage.,1,10,"Same parameters F=100, K=0.1.",1,100,Manager loses 100 per conflict.,1,1,Manager bonus option remains.,1,50,Bonus magnitude unchanged.,1,1,1:1 token transfer.,1,100,Fixed wage.,1,N/R,They report high-effort fraction.,1,0.524,“rate with which workers choose high effort rises from 49.1% in the no-conflict treatment to 52.4% in the conflict treatment.”,1,N/R,"Average surplus 224.5, not normalized.",0.7,224.5,“total surplus decreases from 243.1 in the no-conflict treatment to 224.5 in the conflict treatment.”,1,Switzerland,Same environment.,0.9,N/R,Not given.,1,N/R,Not given.,1,University students (various fields),Same LABEX pool.,1,On site,Laboratory setting.,1,"Same as agreement no-conflict (code of conduct, outside option) plus standard conflict stage as in earlier experiments: worker can initiate conflict after observing own signal and bonus; in this experiment conflict choice is binary (d∈{0,1}).","Text: 'in the other two experiments as a binary choice (d∈{0,1}).' for experiments 3 and 4.",1
10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Grievance experiment – No-Conflict treatment,Fourth study 'grievance experiment' keeps code of conduct but no-conflict treatment has no formal grievance or punishment stage.,1,"Conflict possibility: No (K = ∞); Same nonbinding code of conduct as agreement experiment each period (PO norm), refusal leads to outside option 40; no grievance/punishment stage in this treatment. Game parameters: w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100.","Grievance experiment replicates agreement design but adds grievance only in conflict treatment; here, this is the parallel no-conflict.",1,True,Human-subject lab experiment in 2018.,1,True,Treatment manipulation of grievance institution and conflict availability.,1,True,At LABEX.,1,False,Empirical only.,1,False,Describes empirical treatment.,1,2,One worker and one manager.,1,15,Same 15 periods.,1,1,Binary effort game.,0.2,N/R,Not a contribution game.,1,N/R,Not public-good based.,1,0,Only code of conduct stage; no explicit messaging.,1,0,Individual payoff feedback only.,0.9,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No extra identity revelation noted.,0.8,1,Likely known to participants.,0.7,0,No-conflict treatment lacks grievance/conflict stage.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,1,Manager can pay bonus 50.,1,50,Same contract parameters.,1,1,1:1 transfer.,1,100,Wage w=100.,1,N/R,They use high-effort rate.,1,0.441,“rate with which workers choose high effort rises from 44.1% in the no-conflict treatment to 64.4% in the conflict treatment.”,1,N/R,"Average surplus 237.3, not ratio.",0.7,237.3,“total surplus is also slightly higher in the conflict treatment” with values 237.3 (no-conflict) and 243.7 (conflict).,1,Switzerland,Same site.,0.9,N/R,Not given.,1,N/R,Not given.,1,University students (various fields),Same subject pool description.,1,On site,Lab at HEC Lausanne.,1,Same as agreement experiment (PO code of conduct plus outside option); no grievance process or punishment stage in this treatment.,Grievance experiment’s no-conflict mirrors agreement no-conflict except for implementation time.,1
10.1287_mnsc.2022.03085.md,Grievance experiment – Conflict (Grievance) treatment,Fourth study’s treatment with both code of conduct and institutionalized conflicts via grievance process.,1,"Conflict possibility: Yes (K = 0.1) via formal grievance procedure; Same nonbinding code of conduct (PO norm) each period, with outside option 40 if not signed. Conflicts initiated only through filing formal grievance confirming manager violated code (no bonus despite positive subjective signal). No verification; material payoffs same as earlier conflict treatments. Game parameters: w=100, B=50, c=10, r_L=150, r_H=350, γ1=0.85, γ0=0.15, p=0.75, q=0.75, F=100.","Grievance treatment adds a framing/institution: conflict via complaint linked to code of conduct, but technology (cost, impact) unchanged.",1,True,Human-subject lab experiment with grievance framing.,1,True,Institutional framing and conflict availability experimentally manipulated.,1,True,Sessions at LABEX.,1,False,No purely computational model.,1,False,"Section describes empirical condition, not only math analysis.",1,2,Manager-worker dyads.,1,15,Same structure.,1,1,Binary effort only.,0.2,N/R,No public good contributions.,1,N/R,Linear public good MPCR not defined here.,1,0,"Only grievance/complaint framing, no communication messages.",1,0,Participants receive own payoffs; no information about others’ histories.,0.9,0,Complaint filed within dyad; no additional identity disclosure beyond current match.,0.7,0,No separate identity display of rewarder; same manager.,0.8,1,Likely known 15 periods; same environment.,0.7,1,Workers can punish managers by filing grievance that triggers same monetary conflict as earlier treatments.,1,10,"Conflict technology unchanged: F=100, K=0.1 → cost 10 per grievance.",1,100,Manager payoff reduced by 100 per grievance.,1,1,Bonus payment available.,1,50,Bonus B=50.,1,1,One-to-one transfer.,1,100,Worker's wage w=100.,1,N/R,Main variable is high-effort rate.,1,0.644,“rate with which workers choose high effort rises from 44.1% in the no-conflict treatment to 64.4% in the conflict treatment.”,1,N/R,"Total surplus 243.7 reported, but no normalized efficiency index.",0.7,243.7,Result 4a notes total surplus rises from 237.3 to 243.7.,1,Switzerland,Same lab and pool.,0.9,N/R,No age statistics.,1,N/R,Not reported.,1,University students (various fields),Same description of subject pool across experiments.,1,On site,Laboratory grievance experiment.,1,"Identical subjective evaluation game and PO code of conduct as agreement experiment. In conflict treatment, worker sanctions manager by filing a formal grievance confirming that manager violated code (no bonus despite worker’s positive signal). No verification or material effect of lying beyond conflict payoffs; conflict choice is binary (d∈{0,1}).",Summarizes grievance framing and how conflicts are institutionalized.,1
10.1038_s41598-025-98728-z.md,"Simulation – Our model with reputation mechanism, Scenario 1, Z ∈ [0,20], L=100",Identifies the main Monte Carlo simulation of the new model for initial Scenario 1 across all reputation thresholds on a 100x100 lattice.,0.95,Reputation threshold Z (0–20) in our model; initial strategy proportions Scenario 1: k_TP ≈ k_UP ≈ k_TC ≈ k_UC ≈ 0.25N; lattice size L=100,Z is systematically varied; Scenario 1 initial mix and lattice size are explicitly specified.,0.97,False,"Study uses agent-based Monte Carlo simulations with 10,000 or 25,0000 agents and no human subjects.",1,False,No controlled experiment with human participants; purely computational model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; only simulations.,1,True,Explicitly described as Monte Carlo simulation and agent-based modelling on a lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations of equilibria; focus is numerical simulation.,0.9,10000,"Edge length L=100, square lattice so N=L^2=10,000.",1,10000,"They run simulations for 10,000 time steps and analyze last 1,000.",1,1,"Players choose among 4 discrete strategies (TP, UP, TC, UC); there is no continuous contribution choice characteristic of public goods games, so continuous/all-or-nothing contribution notion is inapplicable; coded as 1 because no restriction to binary contribution amounts exists within this trust-game setting.",0.3,N/A,"Trust game with pairwise payoffs, not a contribution game with an endowment.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between simulated agents not modelled as chat.,1,N/A,Simulation agents update based on local payoffs; no user interface or feedback summaries.,1,N/A,Reputation-based reward/punishment is automatic; no concept of visible identities.,1,N/A,"As above, no explicit identity revelation in a UI sense.",1,N/A,Agents do not have a displayed round counter; horizon is fixed in code only.,1,1,Players with R < Z are punished via payoff term scaled by (R−Z)/Z.,1,N/R,Punishment is endogenous via reduced payoff; no explicit per-unit punishment cost parameter.,0.2,N/R,Magnitude of punishment not parameterized as a simple linear tech like ‘beta per coin’.,0.2,1,"Players with R > Z receive higher payoffs, described as rewards.",1,N/R,No explicit resource or coin cost to give rewards; embedded multiplicatively in payoffs.,0.2,N/R,Reward multiplier depends on (R−Z)/Z; no single scalar technology is given.,0.2,N/A,"Game uses payoff parameters B, S, Temp, X but no per-round endowment tokens.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions; only discrete trustworthiness and role strategies.,1,N/A,"As above, model is not a contribution game.",1,N/R,They report total revenue but not normalized by maximum possible revenue.,0.3,N/R,"Total revenue W is discussed qualitatively and plotted, but no exact numeric values for specific Z are tabulated.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely agent-based simulation with Monte Carlo method.,1,"Square lattice network with periodic boundaries; each agent has 4 neighbors (von Neumann neighborhood); four strategies (TP, UP, TC, UC); synchronous update; proportional imitation update rule modified from Fermi; payoffs defined by B=25, S=20, Temp=40, X=10 and scaled by reputation term (R−Z)/Z; reputation scores R∈[0,20] updated +1 for trustworthy (TP,TC) and −1 for untrustworthy (UP,UC); initial strategies random with equal proportions; simulations run 10,000 time steps, 10 runs; results averaged over last 1,000 steps.",Summary of game structure and parameters given under model and experimental setup for Scenario 1.,1
