10.1016_j.jedc.2024.104987.md,N/A – purely theoretical/analytical model,"The paper develops a theoretical model of temporary partnerships in an infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma and analyzes equilibrium conditions; it does not report any empirical experiment, simulation, or observational condition.",1,N/A,"No experimental treatments or empirical conditions are implemented; only parameters of a theoretical model (e.g., T, δ, g, l, N) are varied analytically.",1,False,The study contains no human-subject data; all results are theoretical derivations.,1,False,There is no controlled experiment; the paper is entirely analytical/theoretical.,1,False,No lab or field experiment is conducted; only theory is presented.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported; the analysis uses closed-form expressions and Markov matrices.,1,True,"The paper provides formal analytical results (theorems, lemmas, closed-form payoffs) for an infinitely repeated game with temporary partnerships.",1,2,"The stage game is a two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma played within each temporary partnership, though the population has N=2n players.",1,-1,"The interaction is infinitely repeated (time t=1,2,…); use -1 to indicate infinite horizon.",1,0,"Players choose between two discrete actions {C,D}, not a continuous contribution amount; this is not an all-or-nothing contribution public-good structure.",1,N/A,"There is no endowment-allocation or contribution decision; actions are cooperate/defect in a Prisoner’s Dilemma, not a contribution game.",1,N/A,"The setting is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoff parameters g and l, not a linear public good with a marginal per capita return.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No information interfaces for human subjects are described; only theoretical payoffs are analyzed.,1,N/A,"Punishment is modeled as strategy-contingent actions in an anonymous population, not as a chosen mechanism revealing identities to human subjects.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled beyond cooperative/defective actions in the Prisoner’s Dilemma.,1,N/A,"There is no user interface; agents in the model know the structure analytically, and the horizon is infinite.",1,1,Punishment is implemented via strategy-contingent play of D (direct punishment within a partnership and community punishment across partnerships).,1,N/R,The model does not specify a separate cost parameter per unit of punishment; the cost is implicit in foregone cooperative payoffs and lower continuation values.,0.6,N/R,There is no explicit punishment technology scaling paid cost to imposed loss; punishment is simply choosing D which yields specified PD payoffs.,0.8,0,No separate reward mechanism beyond cooperation is modeled; only C and D actions with associated PD payoffs.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism is included.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology is defined.,1,N/R,"Payoffs are specified directly as 1, 0, 1+g, -l in the stage game; there is no mention of an endowed amount per period.",0.7,N/A,No empirical dependent variables are measured; only theoretical efficiency/cooperation properties are derived.,1,N/A,There is no contribution amount choice; only discrete PD actions.,1,N/R,"Although efficiency of full cooperation vs. defection is discussed conceptually, no numeric efficiency outcome from data is reported.",0.8,N/R,No empirical payoff data are reported; only theoretical payoffs and continuation values.,1,N/A,No participants are recruited; the study is theoretical.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,The paper presents only a theoretical model with no human or field data.,1,"Infinitely repeated random-matching Prisoner’s Dilemma with N=2n≥4 players, temporary partnerships of fixed length T≥1, private monitoring across partnerships but perfect monitoring within a partnership; actions {C,D} with payoffs: (C,C)→(1,1), (C,D)→(-l,1+g), (D,C)→(1+g,-l), (D,D)→(0,0). Analysis compares a combined direct-plus-cyclical community sanction strategy to a grim trigger strategy for sustaining cooperation.",Summarizes the theoretical game structure and key strategic mechanisms described in Sections 2–3.,1
