10.1063_5.0253511.md,Spatial optional public goods game with loners' punishment (full four-strategy model),"Single computational evolutionary model on a square lattice with four strategies (C, D, LN, LP); all reported phase diagrams and parameter sweeps are variants of this model.",1,Synergy factor r; loner payoff δ; punishment cost α; punishment fine β; all varied over ranges in phase diagrams within the same four-strategy spatial PGG model.,"The paper systematically varies r–δ, α–β, and r–δ at fixed β to obtain phase diagrams and discusses how cooperation changes with these parameters.",1,False,No human participants; work is based on Monte Carlo simulations of evolutionary dynamics on a lattice.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No laboratory or online participants; only simulations.,1,True,"Authors explicitly describe Monte Carlo simulations on a square lattice with periodic boundaries, evolutionary imitation via Fermi rule, and long runs over up to 10^9 rounds.",1,False,They do not derive closed-form analytical solutions; focus is on numerical phase diagrams and spatial snapshots.,1,102400,"They state using a square lattice with maximum side length L = 320; typical simulations in this literature use that size, so N = L^2 = 102400. No other size is specified for main results.",0.7,1000000000,They state that all evolutions run for no more than 10^9 rounds until an absorbing or cyclic state is reached; this is the stated upper bound for rounds.,0.9,1,"Contributors invest a fixed cost c when choosing to cooperate, but defectors invest 0; players do not choose arbitrary contribution levels beyond this binary choice, so standard continuous-allocation vs. binary definition does not neatly apply. However, in this coding scheme 1 denotes that players can contribute any amount and 0 that only all-or-nothing; here the choice is strictly all-or-nothing (cooperate with fixed c or not), so this should be 0. Setting 1 would be incorrect; thus assign 0. (NOTE: field must be 1 or 0; using 0 to represent binary contribution.)",0.9,N/A,No human subjects or default endowment allocation; strategies are purely behavioral rules in simulations.,1,N/R,"They define payoff as rc * NC / (NC + ND); MPCR = r/(group size of participants), but effective participant group size varies and MPCR is not explicitly reported as a single value.",0.5,N/A,No human interaction; no communication mechanism.,1,N/A,No user interface; only simulated payoffs.,1,N/A,No identities or displays to human participants; punishment is modeled mechanically.,1,N/A,No rewards mechanism and no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation agents are not informed of total rounds.,1,1,Loners of type LP impose sanctions on cooperators; punishment cost α and fine β are explicit model parameters.,1,N/R,"The model uses parameter α as the cost per punished cooperator to each LP, but α is varied; no single numeric value can represent it for the whole study.",0.8,N/R,"Punishment fine per punished cooperator is β, but β is varied across conditions; no single value summarizes it.",0.8,0,No mechanism where players pay to reward others; only loner punishment of cooperators is modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/R,"Players invest a fixed cost c if they cooperate, but c’s numeric value is not reported; only inequalities like 0 < δ < (r − 1)c are given.",0.5,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as stationary fractions of strategies and phase types, not as contribution rates normalized to endowment.",0.6,N/R,Only strategy frequencies and qualitative spatial patterns are reported; no aggregate contribution amounts per round are given.,0.6,N/R,The paper discusses dominance of phases and coexistence but does not report normalized efficiency (total payoff relative to maximum).,0.6,N/R,No explicit aggregate payoff statistics (means per group) are reported in text or figures; only qualitative payoffs enter the imitation rule.,0.6,N/A,No human participants; simulation agents have no country.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"All results are from Monte Carlo simulations; no lab, online, field, or observational human data.",1,"Spatial optional public goods game on a square lattice with periodic boundaries; each individual belongs to 5 overlapping groups (self-centered plus four neighbors). Strategies: Cooperators (C) contribute fixed cost c; Defectors (D) do not contribute; Traditional loners (LN) opt out and receive fixed income δ; Punishing loners (LP) opt out, receive δ, but pay cost α per neighboring cooperator they punish and impose fine β on each such cooperator. Public goods game occurs only if at least two participants (C or D) are present in a group; total cooperative contributions rc * NC are shared equally among C and D participants. Strategy updating uses pairwise comparison with Fermi rule and noise K = 0.5; initial conditions mostly random across 4 strategies or patch networks; system size up to L = 320, runs up to 10^9 Monte Carlo steps, 20 independent runs per parameter set.",Summarized directly from Section II (model description) and simulation protocol details.,1
