10.1007_s00355-025-01596-9.md,"Part 1 – Player B punishment decision, IMMEDIATE treatment","Part 1 is the only part with interpersonal punishment; IMMEDIATE is one of two treatments (Sec. 3, 3.4, 5.1.2). This row aggregates over the four scenarios in IMMEDIATE.",0.78,Cooling-off treatment: IMMEDIATE (no 10-minute questionnaire delay before punishment decision),Two treatments differ only by cooling-off delay before Stage 2 (Sec. 3.4). Independent variable is presence vs. absence of delay; this row is IMMEDIATE.,0.86,True,Human subjects in a lab experiment at BonnEconLab (Sec. 3.5).,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and specified payoffs (Sec. 3).,1,True,Conducted at BonnEconLab using z-Tree; participants recruited via hroot and came to the lab (Sec. 3.5).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,"Although a theoretical model is presented, this row refers to the empirical experiment only.",0.9,2,Part 1 is a two-player game between Player A and Player B (Sec. 3.1).,1,1,The game in Part 1 is played once per matched pair; no repetition of the A–B interaction is described.,0.9,0,"Player B chooses punishment p from {0,60,120,180,240,300}; this is a discrete multi-level choice, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,There is no public-goods contribution or initial allocation between private/public accounts in this dyadic lottery-punishment game; by convention set to 0 as endowments are effectively private payoffs.,0.4,N/R,No public good or contribution technology is used; thus no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,No communication between A and B is mentioned; only information about choices/outcomes is provided.,0.95,0,Players are informed only about their pair’s choices and outcomes; no summaries of others’ earnings or punishments are reported.,0.9,0,Matching is anonymous; no indication that identities of punishers are revealed.,0.9,0,No reward option exists; only punishment is available.,1,1,"Subjects know the structure of the experiment at the outset, including that Part 1 consists of the described one-shot lottery and punishment stages (Sec. 3).",0.7,1,Player B can deduct p points from A at a cost p/4 (Sec. 3.1).,1,0.25,"Cost to B is p/4, so cost per 1 point reduction in A’s payoff is 1/4 (Sec. 3.1).",1,1,Each unit of p reduces A’s payoff one-for-one (Π_i^A = π_i^A − p).,1,0,No option for B to increase A’s payoff is described; only deduction is possible.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1500,"Both lotteries allocate payoffs that sum to 1500 points in all outcomes (1350+150, 750+750), described as division of an endowment amounting to 1500 points (Sec. 3.1).",1,N/R,No contribution decision; only punishment choices are measured.,1,N/R,No contribution decision; only lottery choice and punishment level are reported.,1,N/R,They do not report efficiency as ratio of realized to maximal payoffs; analysis focuses on punishment levels and beliefs.,0.7,N/R,Average group payoff (A+B) is not reported for IMMEDIATE separately.,0.4,Germany,Experiment conducted at BonnEconLab (University of Bonn) in Germany (Sec. 3.5).,0.95,N/R,Age data collected (questionnaire) but no summary statistics reported.,0.3,"Mixed: 191 males, 189 females overall; treatment-specific gender split for IMMEDIATE not reported",Overall sample composition given in Sec. 3.5; allocation to IMMEDIATE vs DELAY is not broken down by gender.,0.7,University students (course of study recorded),Questionnaire records course of study; typical BonnEconLab subject pool is university students; no other population described.,0.75,On site,Sessions at BonnEconLab using lab software z-Tree; implies in-person lab sessions.,0.95,"Two-player lottery–punishment game. Stage 1: Player A chooses lottery Selfish (S) or Considerate (C). Selfish: outcome U=(1350,150) with 80% and E=(750,750) with 20%. Considerate: U with 20%, E with 80%. Stage 2: Player B observes A’s lottery choice and realized allocation (SE, SU, CE, CU) and chooses punishment p∈{0,60,120,180,240,300}, reducing A’s payoff by p and B’s by p/4. Beliefs on lottery choice and punishment elicited with incentives (200 points for correct prediction).",Summary of detailed design in Sec. 3.1.,1
10.1007_s00355-025-01596-9.md,"Part 1 – Player B punishment decision, DELAY treatment","Same Part 1 game as IMMEDIATE, but with a delay manipulation before B’s punishment decision (Sec. 3.4).",0.78,Cooling-off treatment: DELAY (≈10-minute unrelated questionnaire before punishment decision),"Treatment DELAY adds a set of unincentivized questions taking about 10 minutes before Stage 2, compared to IMMEDIATE (Sec. 3.4).",0.9,True,Same human-subject lab experiment; this row isolates the DELAY treatment.,1,True,Random assignment to IMMEDIATE vs DELAY; controlled payoffs.,1,True,Conducted at BonnEconLab using z-Tree (Sec. 3.5).,1,False,No simulation model reported.,1,False,"This row refers to empirical data, not the theoretical model.",0.9,2,Same two-player A–B interaction as IMMEDIATE.,1,1,Same single interaction structure as IMMEDIATE.,0.9,0,"Same multi-level punishment choice p∈{0,60,120,180,240,300}.",1,0,No contribution or public good component; same as IMMEDIATE.,0.4,N/R,No public-good technology.,1,0,No communication introduced in DELAY; only extra questionnaires.,0.95,0,Information structure identical to IMMEDIATE; no summaries of others’ outcomes.,0.9,0,Anonymity/matching procedure unchanged.,0.9,0,No reward option.,1,1,Participants informed about the structure of parts; Part 1 remains a one-shot interaction.,0.7,1,Same p-based punishment option as in IMMEDIATE.,1,0.25,Cost to B is p/4 as in IMMEDIATE.,1,1,Each unit of p reduces A’s payoff one-for-one.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,1500,Same 1500-point joint endowment distribution as in IMMEDIATE.,1,N/R,No contribution measure.,1,N/R,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,Efficiency ratios not reported by treatment.,0.7,N/R,Average group payoff not reported separately for DELAY.,0.4,Germany,Same subject pool as IMMEDIATE at BonnEconLab in Germany.,0.95,N/R,Age statistics not broken out by treatment.,0.3,"Mixed: 191 males, 189 females overall; treatment-specific gender split for DELAY not reported",Same overall composition; no separate figures for DELAY.,0.7,University students (course of study recorded),Same recruitment context and questionnaire as IMMEDIATE.,0.75,On site,Same BonnEconLab setting.,0.95,"Identical two-player lottery–punishment game as in IMMEDIATE (A chooses Selfish vs Considerate; B observes scenario SE, SU, CE, or CU and chooses punishment p∈{0,60,120,180,240,300} with cost p/4). Treatment DELAY inserts ≈10-minute non-incentivized questionnaires (risk attitudes, CRT, past experiment participation) between outcome revelation to B and B’s punishment decision.",Sec. 3.1 and 3.4 describe same game and the delay manipulation.,1
10.1007_s00355-025-01596-9.md,Part 2 – Social norm elicitation (punishment and lottery appropriateness ratings),Part 2 elicits social appropriateness ratings for punishment levels and lottery choices; it is an incentive-compatible coordination task but not an interaction game with payoffs between players.,0.85,"Scenario evaluated (SE, SU, CE, CU) and action type (punishment level p∈{0,60,120,180,240,300} or lottery choice S vs C); matched partner gender varied but has no effect on punishment norms","Subjects rate 26 items: six punishment levels for each of four scenarios plus two lottery choices; matching partner’s gender is announced but has negligible effect except one case for women’s rating of Selfish (Sec. 3.2, 5.2).",0.82,True,Human subjects provide ratings and receive incentives based on matching another subject’s rating (Sec. 3.2).,1,True,"Incentivized coordination game to elicit norms, with randomized order of items.",1,True,Conducted in the same lab sessions at BonnEconLab (Sec. 3.5).,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,This row concerns empirical norm-elicitation data.,0.95,2,The norm items reference behavior of Player A and Player B in a two-player game; coordination incentive is based on comparing a participant’s rating to that of one other subject.,0.7,1,Each subject gives a set of 26 ratings in one block; there is no repeated strategic interaction over rounds.,0.8,0,Ratings cover multiple discrete punishment levels and both lottery choices; not an all-or-nothing decision.,1,0,Norm task does not involve contributions or endowments; we set to 0 by convention.,0.3,N/R,No public good or linear production; this is a rating task.,1,0,Subjects do not communicate; they independently rate items.,0.95,0,Participants are not shown others’ earnings or full distributions; only know matching rule.,0.9,0,No actual punishment occurs in Part 2; only hypothetical scenarios are rated.,1,0,No reward decisions are rated beyond lottery choices; no identities are revealed.,1,1,"Instructions explain that one of the 26 ratings will be randomly selected for payment, but there is no multi-round interaction.",0.8,1,Scenarios being rated involve Player B’s punishment options from Part 1.,1,0.25,Norm items are explicitly about the same game where cost is p/4; thus implied cost technology in the rated scenarios.,1,1,Each unit of p in the underlying game reduces A’s payoff by 1; norms are conditional on that structure.,1,0,Underlying game has no reward; norms about rewards are not elicited.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1500,Norm items refer to the same underlying lottery game with total endowment 1500 points.,0.9,N/R,"Outcome variables are norm ratings, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,No efficiency measure reported for norm ratings.,0.9,N/R,Payment in Part 2 is 1200 points if rating matches another’s; average payoffs from this task are not reported.,0.3,Germany,Same subject pool as Part 1 at BonnEconLab.,0.95,N/R,Age statistics not reported for Part 2 separately.,0.3,"Mixed; ratings analyzed separately by gender (e.g., gender differences in norm ratings in Table 4 and Figs. 2–5)","Paper reports separate analyses for men and women in norm ratings; overall 191 males, 189 females.",0.9,University students (course of study recorded),Same sample as Part 1; course of study collected in questionnaire.,0.75,On site,Part 2 conducted in same lab sessions as Part 1 at BonnEconLab.,0.95,"Norm-elicitation coordination task based on Krupka and Weber (2013). Each subject rates social appropriateness on a 6-point scale (0–5) for: (i) each punishment level p∈{0,60,120,180,240,300} in each of the four scenarios (SE, SU, CE, CU); and (ii) the lottery choices Selfish vs Considerate. One out of 26 items is randomly selected; the subject’s rating is compared to that of a randomly selected other subject (whose gender is announced before rating); if their ratings match, the subject earns 1200 points, else 0.","Design summarized in Sec. 3.2, with details on incentives and items.",1
