icacy framing for this cell.,1
10.1002_ejsp.3171.md,"Study 5 – High fairness, high outcome efficacy",Condition with high fairness and high outcome efficacy in Study 5.,1,Perceptions of economic fairness: high; Outcome efficacy: high (message may change allocation because game is still ongoing),Fairness manipulation high plus high outcome efficacy instructions.,1,True,Human-subject experiment in game setting.,1,True,Part of randomized 2×2 design.,1,True,Same token-based lab experiment environment as other Study 5 groups.,0.7,False,No simulation.,1,False,No analytical-only modeling.,1,3,"Dictator, recipient, third-party punisher.",0.95,1,Single punishment decision after one allocation.,0.9,0,Decision is whether or not to send the disapproval message.,1,N/A,No public good contribution.,1,N/R,Not relevant.,1,0,Only a fixed sanction message can be sent; no chat.,1,0,Only one instance is considered.,1,0,Identity of punisher is not indicated as disclosed.,0.7,0,No rewarding.,1,1,Framed as ongoing but single-shot; number of rounds is effectively one.,0.8,1,Binary non-monetary punishment via costly message.,1,10,Message costs 10 tokens.,1,0,Punishment is social; no direct fine in tokens for A stated.,0.8,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,50,Third party begins with 50 tokens.,1,N/R,"Measure is punishment, not contribution.",1,0.8068,"Punishment rate in high fairness, high efficacy group: 80.68%; recorded as mean of binary punishment.",0.95,N/R,Not provided.,0.2,N/R,No group payoff numbers specified.,0.2,China,Same as rest of Study 5.,0.9,"M = 29.07, SD = 8.30, adults",Study 5-level data.,1,66.67% female,Study 5-level data.,1,N/R,Not reported.,0.2,On site,Same as other Study 5 cells.,0.7,"Same non-monetary punishment paradigm as other Study 5 conditions: third party may pay 10 tokens to send 'I think your allocation scheme is unfair'. Under high efficacy, participants are told that A may change the distribution and they expect B’s tokens to rise (mean estimate 17.49).",Includes outcome efficacy manipulation details relevant to this cell.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgaf150.md,Chain reciprocity study – Baseline condition (participants believe all alters are human),Chain reciprocity study has one between-subject manipulation: baseline vs hybrid. This row is the baseline condition.,1,Condition: baseline (participants told they interact with 3 other participants; display shows 4 humans) while actual network composition is 2 bots + 1 human alter; RBR present in repeated 4-person chain network.,Baseline vs hybrid is the primary experimental manipulation in the chain reciprocity study; bots are covert in baseline.,1,True,Human participants recruited via Prolific.com make token-giving decisions.,1,True,Participants randomly assigned to baseline vs hybrid with controlled network structure and programmed bots.,1,False,"Study conducted online via Prolific, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Bots are preprogrammed agents embedded in a human experiment, not a standalone simulation study.",1,False,No formal analytical model as the primary method.,1,4,Closed four-agent network A-B-C-D is explicitly described.,1,11,They intended 12 rounds but due to a programming error they obtained valid data for 11 rounds.,1,1,Ego could choose any number of tokens from 0 to 10 to send.,1,0,Each round participants receive a token endowment that they control; nothing is preallocated to others.,0.9,0.5,"Tokens sent are doubled for the recipient; group size is 4, so MPCR = 2/4 = 0.5.",0.9,0,No mention of any communication channel among participants.,1,0,Participants only see what their alter gave in the current round; no summaries of earnings or punishments.,1,0,No punishment mechanism in this study.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond standard giving; no IDs of rewarders shown.,1,0,Participants were not told ahead of time how many rounds there would be.,1,0,Only positive token transfers are possible; no costly punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism beyond the main giving decision.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,10,"Outcome variable is number of tokens from 0 to 10 that ego can send, implying a 10-token endowment per round.",1,N/R,"Paper reports mean tokens given and model coefficients, not normalized contribution proportions.",0,N/R,Table 1 gives regression intercepts and effects but no condition-specific raw means for baseline alone.,0,N/R,No efficiency ratios (actual/maximum group payoff) reported.,0,N/R,Group-level payoff statistics are not reported.,0,United States,Eligibility required participants to be located in the United States.,1,"Mean 37.6 years, 18+ required",They report average age 37.6 and minimum age 18.,1,54% female,Reported in Materials and methods for the chain reciprocity study.,1,N/R,They explicitly state they did not analyze any other demographic information beyond age and gender.,1,Online,Both studies took place online with participants recruited via Prolific.com.,1,"Repeated 4-person indirect reciprocity chain. Sequence: A gives 0–10 tokens to B; D observes A→B then gives to A; C observes D→A then gives to D; B observes C→D then gives to C; then loop continues. Tokens sent are doubled for the recipient, kept tokens are not multiplied. Participants never see what was given to them, only what their current alter previously gave to someone else. Actual network composition is 2 bots (strong reciprocators: mirror alter’s previous giving; if A in first round, give 10) and 2 human participants, but baseline participants are told all 3 alters are human and see only human icons.",Summarized from the detailed task description in Materials and methods.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgaf150.md,Chain reciprocity study – Hybrid condition (participants know 2 alters are bots),Same chain reciprocity structure but between-subject condition where bots are disclosed and visually marked.,1,Condition: hybrid (participants told group contains one other participant and two bots; onscreen network shows which positions are bots vs human); actual network composition 2 bots + 1 human alter; RBR present in repeated 4-person chain network.,Hybrid vs baseline is manipulated; in hybrid bots are known and visually identified.,1,True,Human Prolific participants interact with bots and another participant.,1,True,Random assignment to hybrid vs baseline with controlled bots' behavior.,1,False,Study conducted online.,1,False,"Bots are part of an experiment, not a standalone simulation study.",1,False,No purely analytical model as the study itself.,1,4,Closed four-agent network A-B-C-D.,1,11,Valid data for 11 rounds due to programming error.,1,1,Choice from 0 to 10 tokens.,1,0,Endowment starts fully under the participant’s control each round.,0.9,0.5,"Sent tokens doubled, group size 4 → MPCR=2/4.",0.9,0,No communication described.,1,0,"Only previous giving of the specific alter is shown, no aggregate summaries.",1,0,No punishment option.,1,0,No separate reward option or revealed reward IDs.,1,0,Participants not told how many rounds.,1,0,Only giving positive tokens.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,10,Participants choose 0–10 tokens to send from an endowment of 10.,1,N/R,No normalized rates by condition reported.,0,N/R,"Regression shows that hybrid reduces giving by 0.54 tokens on average, but absolute hybrid mean not given.",0.2,N/R,No efficiency measures reported.,0,N/R,No group payoff statistics per condition.,0,United States,Eligibility restricted to US-located participants.,1,"Mean 37.6 years, 18+ required",Reported average age and minimum age requirement.,1,54% female,Reported for the chain reciprocity sample.,1,N/R,Education not collected or analyzed beyond age and gender.,1,Online,Conducted via Prolific with online interface (SMARTRIQS).,1,"Same 4-person indirect reciprocity chain as baseline, but participants are explicitly told the group consists of one other participant and two bots and see icons marking bot vs human positions. Bots follow a strong reciprocity rule: give the same number of tokens that their alter previously gave; if a bot is in A in round 1, it sends all 10 tokens. Participants never see tokens received, only their alter’s previous giving to someone else. Real-time interaction with one other participant plus bots; idle or unmatched participants are silently replaced by bots with the same strong reciprocity behavior.",Summarized hybrid-specific aspects from Materials and methods.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgaf150.md,"One-shot giving study – Control decisions (private giving, all bot/human compositions pooled)",One-shot study has 4 control decisions (private giving with different bot/human compositions). This row captures the control condition set where reputational giving is not possible; compositions vary within-subject.,0.9,"Decision type: Control (RBR not possible; ego’s giving decision is private). Within this type, 4 within-subject scenarios crossing: (i) alter B (recipient) is participant vs bot; (ii) alter C (who simultaneously decides whether to give to ego) is participant vs bot.",Control decisions are defined by absence of reputational giving and by manipulation of whether B and C are bots or participants.,1,True,Involves human Prolific participants making binary give/keep decisions.,1,True,Within-subject manipulation of decision types and alter identities under controlled instructions.,1,False,Online experiment.,1,False,"Bots/other participants serve as counterparts in an experiment, not a simulation-only study.",1,False,No primary analytical modeling.,1,3,"Each scenario involves ego, the alter they can give to (B), and another alter who can give to ego (C).",0.9,4,All participants first completed a set of four control decisions.,1,0,In one-shot study each round endowment is 1 token and decision is binary: give token to alter or keep it.,1,0,"Ego starts with full control of one token, which can be sent or not; no default allocation to public.",0.9,2,"Tokens sent to someone else are doubled while kept tokens are not; group interaction is dyadic for payoff purposes, so MPCR for giving = 2/1 = 2.",0.9,0,No communication among players is described.,1,0,Ego does not observe any others’ past behavior in control decisions.,1,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward decisions beyond main giving; no identities of rewarders shown.,1,0,Instructions do not state that total number of decisions is displayed on the decision screen.,0.5,0,Only positive giving vs keeping own token.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No distinct reward stage; giving is the only action.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,1,Participants receive a one-token endowment in each one-shot decision round.,1,N/R,Control decisions’ giving rates can be inferred relative to reputational giving via odds ratio but raw rates are not reported.,0.2,N/R,"Only logistic regression odds ratios, no raw mean giving probabilities by condition.",0.1,N/R,No efficiency metrics computed.,0,N/R,Payoffs are described but no aggregate payoff reported.,0,United States,Same Prolific recruitment criteria as chain reciprocity; US-only.,1,"Mean 41.7 years, 18+ required",Reported for one-shot giving study.,1,49% female,Reported for one-shot giving sample.,1,N/R,Education not reported for either study.,1,Online,Study conducted online via Prolific.,1,"Each control round: ego receives 1 token, decides to send it to alter B or keep it. If sent, B’s payoff increases by 2 tokens (doubling rule); if kept, ego’s token is not multiplied. Simultaneously, an independent alter C (participant or bot) decides whether to give their token to ego; ego never learns that outcome. RBR is not possible because C does not observe ego’s action and no one observes C.",Summarized from one-shot giving study control condition description.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgaf150.md,One-shot giving study – Reputational giving decisions (all bot/human compositions pooled),Reputational giving is analyzed as a condition vs control in Table 2; compositions of B and C are within this decision type.,0.9,"Decision type: Reputational giving (A’s giving to B is observed by C who then decides whether to give to A). Within this type, 4 within-subject scenarios crossing: (i) B is participant vs bot; (ii) C is participant vs bot.",This condition implements reputational giving; paper reports main and interaction effects of RBR possible and whether B or C are bots.,1,True,Human participants make decisions that can affect their earnings; some alters are bots.,1,True,Within-subject randomization of decision type and alter identities.,1,False,Online experiment.,1,False,Bots act but are not the sole focus as a simulation.,1,False,Empirical behavioral analysis.,1,3,"Roles A (ego), B (recipient), and C (observer who can give to A).",1,4,Participants completed four reputational giving decisions.,1,0,Binary decision: send 1 token or keep.,1,0,Endowment is fully private until ego chooses to send it.,0.9,2,Sent tokens are doubled for the recipient; 1-token endowment → MPCR=2.,0.9,0,No communication or chat functionality mentioned.,1,0,"Ego is told structurally that C will see A’s decision, but does not see payoffs or summaries.",1,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,RBR occurs through standard giving by C; IDs are role labels A/B/C only.,0.8,0,Not specified that number of remaining decisions is displayed.,0.5,0,Only positive giving choices.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No separate reward apart from the standard giving decision.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,1,Each decision uses a one-token endowment.,1,N/R,"They report that OR for giving when reputational giving is possible vs control is 4.03, but not baseline probabilities.",0.2,N/R,Binary outcome; logistic models without raw giving fractions by cell.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency stats reported.,0,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not provided.,0,United States,US-only Prolific sample.,1,"Mean 41.7 years, 18+ required",Reported for one-shot giving study.,1,49% female,Reported gender breakdown.,1,N/R,Education not reported.,1,Online,Conducted via Prolific.,1,"In each reputational giving round, ego in role A receives 1 token and decides whether to give it to B (participant or bot). If given, B receives 2 tokens. After A’s decision, role C (participant or bot) observes whether A gave to B, and then decides whether to give their own token to A or keep it. A is informed about this structure but does not learn C’s actual choice. RBR is possible because A can gain a reputation with C and potentially receive a doubled token.",Summarized from one-shot study description and Table 2 narrative.,1
10.1093_pnasnexus_pgaf150.md,One-shot giving study – Rewarding reputations decisions (all bot/human compositions pooled),Rewarding reputations decisions are analyzed separately in Table 3 and involve distinct information structure.,1,"Decision type: Rewarding reputations (ego in role C decides whether to give to A after learning whether A gave to B). Within this type, 8 within-subject scenarios crossing: (i) A is participant vs bot; (ii) B is participant vs bot; (iii) A previously gave token to B vs did not give.",These choices specifically test the rewarding reputations process with manipulated identities and A’s prior action.,1,True,Human participants decide whether to reward/punish alters based on reputational info.,1,True,Within-subject manipulation of previous action (A gave vs not) and identities of A and B.,1,False,Online environment.,1,False,Not a pure simulation study.,1,False,No formal analytical solution as main method.,1,3,"Roles A, B, and C, with C making the current decision.",1,8,Eight rewarding reputations decisions (2×2×2 design) per participant.,1,0,Binary 0/1 decision whether C gives their single token to A.,1,0,C’s token is entirely in their control until they choose to give it.,0.9,2,"As in other one-shot decisions, a given token to A is doubled.",0.9,0,No communication between roles described.,1,0,"C only knows whether A helped B, not any summary statistics.",1,0,No explicit punishment option; not identity-based.,1,0,"Rewarding occurs through C’s giving itself; roles are labeled, but no special display of rewarder identity beyond role.",0.8,0,Total number of decisions not stated as being displayed.,0.5,0,"C can withhold giving, but there is no costly punishment action that reduces A’s payoff below default.",0.9,N/A,No separate punishment action.,1,N/A,No separate punishment action.,1,0,Rewarding is integrated into the main giving decision; no secondary reward choice.,0.9,N/A,No additional reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No additional reward mechanism.,1,1,C has a 1-token endowment as in other one-shot decisions.,1,N/R,"They report odds ratios (e.g., OR=25.66 for effect of A giving) but not overall probabilities by cell.",0.2,N/R,Binary decisions; only regression outputs reported.,0.1,N/R,No efficiency numbers presented.,0,N/R,No group payoff summary per condition.,0,United States,US-based Prolific sample.,1,"Mean 41.7 years, 18+ required",Provided for the one-shot giving study.,1,49% female,Reported gender distribution.,1,N/R,Education was not collected/reported.,1,Online,Study implemented online via Prolific.,1,"In each rewarding reputations decision, ego is in role C. C is told whether A gave their 1-token endowment to B (so B received 2 tokens) or kept it. C then decides whether to send C’s own token to A (doubling to 2 for A) or keep it. Identities of A and B (participant vs bot) are manipulated. This allows testing whether C rewards/punishes A’s prior generosity/selfishness differently when A and/or B are bots vs humans.",Summarized from description of rewarding reputations decisions and Table 3 discussion.,1
10.1016_j.physleta.2024.130196.md,"Simulation – population (C+M), baseline combined reward/punishment, no reward withdrawal","Condition where only cooperators (C) and manipulators (M) are present, with manipulators both rewarding and punishing (μ=β=γ), and ξ=1 so no reward withdrawal, as described in Section 2 and Fig. 4(a).",0.9,"Population composition: C+M only; synergy factor r ∈ [1,5]; manipulation cost γ with μ=β=γ; reward withdrawal parameter ξ=1","Model description and Fig. 4(a) specify C+M population, variation of r from 1 to 5, and γ with μ=β=γ, ξ=1.",0.9,False,Study is an evolutionary simulation on a square lattice with no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with human or field data; purely computational Monte Carlo simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; simulations only.,1,True,"Square lattice, Monte Carlo updating, parameters k, L, etc. clearly indicate numerical simulations.",1,False,"Although payoff functions are written analytically, main results are from simulations rather than closed-form analysis.",0.9,5,"Each individual plays a public goods game with its four neighbors centered on itself, so each group has G=5 players.",1,10000,"They use 2,000–10,000 iterations until convergence; using upper bound as representative since exact per-condition iteration count is not fixed.",0.3,1,"Model defines contribution as c but does not allow partial vs. full choice; each cooperator contributes a fixed amount c and defectors and manipulators contribute 0, effectively an all-or-nothing decision between contribute c or 0.",0.8,N/A,No human endowment framing; evolutionary game where strategies directly determine contributions.,1,N/R,"Synergy factor r and group size G=5 are given, but c is not numerically specified, so marginal per capita return r*c/G per unit of contribution cannot be turned into a numeric value; formula would be r/5.",0.4,N/A,No communication; also no human participants.,1,N/R,Not applicable to agents in simulations; no interface described.,1,N/A,No identities or displays; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No identities or displays; simulated agents only.,1,N/R,No user interface; purely algorithmic iterations.,1,1,Manipulators punish defectors when present; model defines punishment via β and γ.,1,N/R,γ is the cost to the reward-punishment pool per manipulator but is varied; no single numeric value represents this condition.,0.2,N/R,Fine β per γ is defined conceptually but here β=γ and γ varies; a single numeric parameter cannot be assigned.,0.2,1,Manipulators reward cooperators when no defectors are in the group.,1,N/R,Same γ is used as cost for reward; but it is systematically varied so not a single value.,0.2,N/R,Reward μ per γ is defined but μ=γ and γ varies; no single numeric magnitude.,0.2,N/R,Model specifies contribution c but not the endowment amount.,0.3,N/R,"Figures report fractions of strategies (C, D, M) but not normalized contribution rates; in C+M populations, contribution equals fraction of C but not given numerically per condition.",0.4,N/R,No explicit numeric contribution amounts reported; only symbolic c.,0.3,N/R,Group payoff vs. maximum is discussed qualitatively but not reported numerically.,0.2,N/R,Payoff formulas are given but no numeric aggregate payoffs are tabulated for this condition.,0.3,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results from computational simulations on a lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game on L×L square lattice (L=200) with periodic boundary conditions; group size G=5 (focal plus four neighbors); strategies C, D, and M; payoff update via Fermi imitation rule with noise k=0.1; manipulators pay cost γ to a pool that is used as punishment (fine β) if defectors present or as reward (μ) if only cooperators present; in this condition, initial strategies restricted to C and M.",Summarized from Section 2 model description and C+M-specific statements and panels in Fig. 4(a).,1
