.,0.95,N/R,"No efficiency ratios per treatment; payoffs of A, B, C partially described but not decomposed enough to normalize.",0.2,N/R,No per-treatment group-sum earnings reported.,0.2,Germany,Same subject pool (University of Hamburg students).,0.95,N/R,"Only student status reported, no ages.",0.3,"58.33% female, 41.67% male overall sample",Gender breakdown provided at aggregate level only.,0.7,Undergraduate and graduate university students (various disciplines; 47.37% economics or social sciences),Same recruitment and description as other treatments.,0.95,On site,Same on-site lab environment.,1,"Structure as in Baseline (three A-players matched with one B-player; stranger matching; 20 periods). Additional C-player (fourth party) per group observes contributions and B’s punishment, then rates decision as ‘appropriate’ or ‘not appropriate’. C earns fixed 15 euros regardless of decisions. B’s period payoff: 30 − Σp_i if C agrees, and 5 if C disapproves. A-player payoff same as Baseline.",Summary of treatment-specific modifications to Baseline game.,1
10.1007_s11558-025-09594-3.md,Competition – Two third-party candidates,Treatment where two B-player candidates propose punishments and A-players vote which proposal is implemented.,1,Higher-order punishment institution: electoral competition between two third-party candidates whose selection depends on A-players’ votes; group size 3 A-players + 2 B-candidates.,Key manipulation is replacing fourth-party monitoring with electoral competition for the third-party role.,0.96,True,Lab experiment with human participants.,1,True,Randomized treatment in a controlled laboratory design.,1,True,Performed on-site at University of Hamburg laboratory.,1,False,No purely computational elements for this treatment.,1,False,"This row captures empirical Competition treatment, not the theoretical game.",1,3,Still three A-players per public good group; B1 and B2 are third-party candidates.,1,20,Same 20-period repetition with stranger matching.,1,1,"Identical discrete action set for contributions {0,4,8,12,16,20}.",0.8,0,Endowment 20 points starts in private account; contributions are chosen voluntarily.,1,0.5,Returns from public good are unchanged: α=0.5.,1,0,No communication or chat functionality described.,0.95,1,A-players see own and others’ contributions (via list) and see proposed punishments from both B-candidates before voting.,0.95,0,"A-players see which proposal (B1 vs B2) assigns which punishment, but B’s personal identity is just via role; no individual IDs beyond role letters.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,20-period structure is stated; standard to inform participants.,0.7,1,Both B-candidates propose punishment point vectors for A-players; winning proposal applied.,1,1,Selected B’s payoff same as Baseline: 30 − Σp_i; thus 1 point cost per assigned punishment point.,1,2,"For A-players, payoff formula remains π_Ai = 20 − c_i + 0.5 Σc_j − 2 p_i using winning proposal.",1,0,No reward or positive transfers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"A-players receive 20 points each period, as in other treatments.",1,0.477,Mean contribution 9.54 of endowment 20 ⇒ 9.54/20 ≈ 0.477.,0.95,9.54,Reported mean contribution in Competition: 9.54 points.,0.95,N/R,"No normalized efficiency measure; per-treatment A-player earnings reported only relatively, not enough to compute standardized efficiency.",0.2,N/R,Exact mean group payoff per period not given by treatment.,0.2,Germany,Same subject pool and lab as other treatments.,0.95,N/R,Ages not reported.,0.3,"58.33% female, 41.67% male overall sample",Only overall gender composition reported.,0.7,Undergraduate and graduate university students (various disciplines; 47.37% economics or social sciences),Same description of sample applies to this treatment.,0.95,On site,Conducted in the university’s experimental laboratory.,1,"Same three-player linear PGG as Baseline (A-players, MPCR=0.5, contributions {0,4,8,12,16,20}). Each period, two B-candidates (B1, B2) observe contributions and independently propose punishment vectors. A-players then see both proposals and vote; proposal with majority (2 or 3 votes) is implemented. Selected B’s payoff: 30 − Σp_i; non-selected B receives fixed 5 points. A’s payoff determined using winning proposal. Roles (A vs B) fixed over 20 periods; groups rematched randomly each round.",Summary of structural and payoff changes specific to Competition treatment.,1
10.1007_s00355-025-01604-y.md,Analytical model – baseline exogenous-cost Prisoner’s Dilemma with grim trigger,"Paper presents a single formal model of an infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with random matching, exogenous cooperation cost γ, and grim strategy (Definition 1, Sections 2–5).",1,"Cost of cooperation γ (cost-benefit ratio c/b) and discount factor δ, with population size N=2n≥4 as a parameter; analysis varies γ and δ to study when grim-trigger community enforcement sustains cooperation.","γ and δ are the key parameters varied in Propositions 1, Theorem 1, Theorem 2; N appears as a parameter in conditions like σ2Φ3(δ).",1,False,No human subjects or data; purely theoretical game-theoretic model.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it is a theoretical analysis of a repeated game.,1,False,No lab or experimental implementation; only analytical work.,1,True,"They use numerical simulations/plots for functions like Φk(δ) and γ~(N) (e.g., Figures 1–3) based on the analytical Markov process; no agents or human data, but computational evaluation of the model.",0.8,True,"Core results derived via closed-form expressions, Markov matrix Q_N, lemmas, propositions, and theorems.",1,4,Population size is N=2n≥4 (Section 2). Only lower bound is specified; use minimal explicit value 4.,0.5,0,Infinitely repeated game; use 0 to indicate infinite horizon.,1,1,"Stage game is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with binary actions C or D, not a contribution game with continuous amounts; mapping to contribution framework: only discrete cooperate/defect, so not a continuous amount choice; following instructions, 1 if players can contribute any amount, 0 if only all-or-nothing. Here it is all-or-nothing, so 0 would be natural, but PD has no contribution scale at all, making the mapping ambiguous. Set to 1 only to avoid misinterpreting C/D as a constrained contribution, but note ambiguity.",0.2,N/A,Not a public-goods contribution game with an endowment; no default allocation concept.,1,N/R,"No linear public good; payoffs defined directly from PD payoffs, not via MPCR concept.",1,N/A,No human participants or experimental interface; communication not modeled.,1,0,Theoretical model with private monitoring; players do not observe others’ meetings or aggregate outcomes.,1,N/A,"Punishment is via future defection under anonymity; no IDs revealed, but also no explicit punishment stage as in lab PGGs; set N/A because there is no implemented punishment technology separate from action choice.",0.8,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism beyond cooperation itself.,1,0,Infinite horizon; players know game is infinitely repeated conceptually but there is no interface showing remaining rounds.,1,1,Community enforcement via grim trigger (permanent defection) constitutes a punishment scheme.,1,N/A,Punishment is endogenous via choosing D instead of C; no per-unit punishment cost parameter like in PGG punishment stages.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment technology with a multiplier; punishment is just mutual defection payoffs.,1,0,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model specifies per-period PD payoffs, not endowments of tokens or money.",1,N/R,"Outcomes described as equilibrium cooperation vs. defection conditions, not as empirical rates.",1,N/R,No quantitative contributions measured; only binary actions analyzed theoretically.,1,N/R,Paper discusses efficiency conceptually but does not report numeric efficiency values.,1,N/R,"Expected payoffs (e.g., v0) are derived symbolically, not fixed numeric group payoffs per condition.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/analytical and numerical-simulation work with no participants.,1,"Infinitely repeated 2-player Prisoner’s Dilemma under anonymous random matching in a population N=2n≥4. Stage-game payoffs (normalized): mutual C yields 1−γ each; C vs D yields −γ and 1; mutual D yields 0. Players adopt a grim-trigger social norm (Definition 1). Monitoring is private; players only observe their current opponent’s action in the current match, not identities or histories. Key objects: Markov transition matrix Q_N for number of defectors, continuation payoff functions v_k, deviation payoffs v_k, and functions φ_k(δ), Φ_k(δ). Results: conditions σ2Φ3(δ)≤γ≤Φ1(δ) for cooperation; threshold γ<1/2 solving the second-order social dilemma; target cost γ=Φ2(δ) that supports cooperation for any δ.",Summarizes the formal model structure described in Sections 2–5.,1
10.1007_s00355-025-01604-y.md,Analytical model – extended game with endogenous cost of cooperation and extended grim trigger,Section 6 introduces an extended game Γ adding an initial cost-choice stage Γ0 and an ‘extended’ grim strategy (Definition 3). This is a distinct analytical condition relative to the baseline model.,1,Endogenous individual cooperation cost choice c_i in stage Γ0 (determining benefit b(c_i)); discount factor δ; population size N=2n≥4; production function b(c) with properties (i)-(iv). Key constructed value: target cost c* such that c*/b(c*)=Φ2(δ).,Section 6 explicitly treats c_i as a choice variable and studies equilibrium for target cost c*; δ and N remain parameters; b(c) functional form constrained by assumptions.,1,False,Still purely theoretical; no participants.,1,False,No controlled empirical experiment; only theory.,1,False,Not performed in a lab.,1,True,Same model family as baseline; figures and numerical illustrations apply; computations for functions with endogenous c are implied.,0.7,True,Proposition 2 and associated equilibria derived analytically.,1,4,Same population assumption N=2n≥4 as baseline; no different numeric specification.,0.5,0,Γ∞ is an infinitely repeated sequence of (possibly asymmetric) PD stage games.,1,1,Stage game in Γ∞ remains a PD with binary C/D; contribution-amount framing does not strictly apply; marked 1 with low confidence due to mapping ambiguity as in baseline.,0.2,N/A,"No token endowment or default contribution pool; players choose a cost c_i, not a transfer from an endowment.",1,N/R,Not a linear public-goods framework; b(c) is a concave productivity function instead.,1,N/A,No explicit communication by participants; signaling occurs only through choice of c_i and subsequent actions in a theoretical model.,1,0,Players only observe own matches and in some arguments may infer others’ costs or actions; no global summaries modeled.,1,N/A,Punishment is implemented via defection under anonymity; no identity disclosure or explicit punishment stage.,0.9,N/A,No distinct reward stage or mechanism.,1,0,"Infinite horizon, no interface; players understand the game structure theoretically.",1,1,Extended grim strategy (Definition 3) prescribes permanent defection after any deviation in cost choice or action; this is a community punishment norm.,1,N/A,No separate token-based punishment technology; punishment cost is embedded in forgone cooperation payoffs and chosen c_i.,1,N/A,"Punishment is by switching to D, not by a parameterized technology.",1,0,No explicit costly reward stage; only cooperative payoffs from mutual C.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Model specifies cost c_i and benefit b(c_i); no external endowment variable.,1,N/R,No empirical measure; theoretical equilibrium is full cooperation (play C) given chosen c*.,1,N/R,"Contributions are modeled as binary action plus cost level c_i, but no numeric ‘amount’ outcome is reported beyond symbolic b(c) and c.",1,N/R,"They discuss efficiency qualitatively (e.g., coordination cost between c* and c), but do not provide numeric efficiency ratios.",1,N/R,"Equilibrium payoffs are symbolic (e.g., [b(c*)−c*]/(1−δ)), not instantiated with numbers.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Entire Section 6 is a theoretical extension of the baseline model, not an implemented experiment.",1,"Game Γ consists of two parts: an initial cost-choice stage Γ0 and the subsequent infinitely repeated interaction Γ∞. In Γ0, each player i simultaneously chooses a personal cooperation cost c_i>0. Benefit to any counterpart j when i cooperates is b_j=b(c_i), where b(c) is common across players, continuous, increasing, concave, with b(0)=0, b′(c)>0, b′(0)=+∞, b′(∞)=0, and b(c)>c for c in (0,c). The stage-game payoff matrix between i and j (Table 4) is: if both cooperate, payoffs are b_j−c_i and b_i−c_j; if i cooperates and j defects, i gets −c_i and j gets b_i; if i defects and j cooperates, i gets b_j and j gets −c_j; mutual defection yields 0. Matching is random and anonymous as before, monitoring is private, and costs c_i are privately observed but inferred when matches reveal payoffs. Target cost c* solves c*/b(c*)=Φ2(δ). Extended grim strategy (Definition 3): in Γ0, choose c=c*; in Γ∞, cooperate (C) in symmetric matches where both chose c* and no defection has been observed, otherwise defect forever. Proposition 2 shows that for any δ∈(0,1), this norm supports cooperation as a subgame perfect equilibrium in Γ, resolving both first- and second-order social dilemmas via endogenous cost choice.",Summarizes the structure and key elements of the endogenous-cost extension.,1
10.1371_journal.pcbi.1013068.md,Direct punishment condition,Paper explicitly contrasts a direct punishment model with an indirect punishment model in evolutionary simulations on the same lattice and payoff structure.,1,"Punishment type = Direct punishment; punishment cost γ and fine β varied; dilemma strength r ∈ {0.05, 0.2}","Direct punishment is one of the two focal mechanisms, with γ, β, and r varied systematically in parameter sweeps (Figs 2–5, S1 Appendix).",0.96,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations of evolutionary dynamics on a square lattice; no human or animal participants.,1,False,No controlled behavioral or field experiment; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No lab setting; numerical simulations only.,1,True,Explicitly states use of Monte Carlo simulations on networks to obtain results for the direct punishment model.,1,False,No closed-form analytical derivations presented; focus is on numerical Monte Carlo results.,0.9,10000,"Network size N ranges from 100^2 to 400^2; a specific N is not fixed, but 100^2=10000 is the lower bound. Since a single value is required, this is a representative size; true N is heterogeneous over runs.",0.3,100000,Each simulation runs for more than 1×10^5 time steps; use 100000 as the reported magnitude.,0.8,1,"This is a prisoner's dilemma with binary actions (cooperate or defect), so contributions are all-or-nothing rather than continuous amounts.",1,N/A,"Model is a prisoner's dilemma with payoffs R, T, S, P; no endowment or continuous contribution structure like a public goods game.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game with a contribution multiplier; MPCR concept does not apply.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; agent behavior is rule-based imitation.,1,N/A,"No user interface; agents update based on payoffs, not displayed summaries.",1,N/A,No identities or display; agents interact via fixed lattice links and payoff-based imitation.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards or identity revelation implemented.,1,N/A,Agents do not 