10.1371_journal.pcbi.1013068.md,Indirect punishment condition,"Paper defines an alternative model where punishment targets second-order neighbors instead of direct neighbors, referred to as indirect punishment, and compares it to direct punishment.",1,"Punishment type = Indirect punishment (second-order neighbors); punishment cost γ and fine β varied; dilemma strength r ∈ {0.05, 0.2}","Indirect punishment is the second focal mechanism; same parameters γ, β, r are swept to compare with direct punishment (Figs 2–5, S1 Appendix).",0.96,False,Condition is implemented purely via Monte Carlo simulations with no human subjects.,1,False,This is not a behavioral or field experiment but a computational model.,1,False,No physical lab; code executed computationally.,1,True,Authors explicitly state they use Monte Carlo simulations to obtain results for the indirect punishment model.,1,False,No analytical solution or closed-form derivations provided for dynamics; focus on numerical results.,0.9,10000,"Network size N ranges from 100^2 to 400^2; exact N varies. 100^2=10000 is the lower bound and used here as a representative value, though simulations include larger N.",0.3,100000,Each simulation runs for more than 1×10^5 time steps; use 100000 as representative magnitude.,0.8,1,"As in the direct punishment model, agents make binary cooperate/defect choices in a Prisoner’s Dilemma.",1,N/A,No contribution endowment framework; binary PD actions only.,1,N/A,"Not a public goods game, so MPCR is inapplicable.",1,N/A,No human agents or communication functionality.,1,N/A,Simulated agents are not shown earnings summaries; they update via payoff-based imitation.,1,N/A,No explicit representation of identities or their revelation.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this model.,1,N/A,Agents do not observe the number of elapsed or remaining time steps.,1,1,"Second stage includes costly punishment, with cost γ and fine β, now targeting defecting second-order neighbors.",1,0.01,Same γ parameter as in di