10.1007_s10458-025-09698-5.md,"TPP-S (third-party punishment with partner selection, Scheme 2)",Condition label inferred from Section 4.1 list of combinations; this row corresponds to 'TPP-S third-party punishment with partner selection' under the successful just-punishment reward scheme (Scheme 2).,0.93,Social mechanisms: third-party punishment + partner selection + reputation; Just-punishment reward scheme: Scheme 2 (net +2 reward for just punisher).,Independent variables are which social mechanisms are enabled and which just-punishment reward scheme is used; TPP-S is defined in Section 4.1 and Scheme 2 is defined in Section 3.2.4 and selected in 4.3.,0.95,False,Study uses simulated MARL agents only; no human participants are involved.,1,False,"This is a computer simulation study, not an empirical experiment with human or field data.",1,False,No lab setting; purely computational simulations.,1,True,Section 3 describes a 'series of simulations' with MARL agents.,1,False,No formal analytical or closed-form modeling; results are from simulations.,1,5,"Section 3.2.4: 'In each of the experiments, the population consisted of five agents.'",0.98,10,"Section 3.2: 'Each simulation consists of 2000 episodes, with ten rounds per episode.'",1,1,"In the Iterated Prisoner's Dilemma, agents choose between cooperate or defect each round; no continuous contribution level as in a public goods game; encoded as allowing any (binary) choice rather than all-or-nothing contributions.",0.7,N/A,No endowment allocation between private/public accounts; game is IPD with fixed payoff matrix.,1,N/A,"MPCR applies to public goods games; this study uses an Iterated Prisoner's Dilemma payoff matrix, not a linear public good.",1,0,No communication or chat between agents is described; decisions are based on states and reputations only.,0.98,0,"Agents observe reputations and past actions in their state, but not explicit summaries of others' earnings or punishments.",0.8,1,"Punishment is targeted at specific agents (A or B), and punishment decisions condition on specific agents' previous actions; therefore the 'identity' of the punished is effectively known.",0.8,0,"No peer-to-peer rewards are modeled, only environmental rewards and punishments.",1,0,No indication that agents observe episode length or remaining rounds; state definitions include reputations and actions only.,0.7,1,"Third-party punishment stage is enabled in this condition (Section 3.2.4, list in 4.1).",1,10,Section 3.2.4: 'the decision to perform punishment ... reduces the reward of the punishing agent by ten.',1,3,Section 3.2.4: punishment 'reduces the reward of the punished agent by three.',1,0,No peer rewards or prosocial reward actions; only punishment and base IPD payoffs plus intrinsic just-punishment bonuses.,1,N/A,No reward action is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Game is specified directly by payoffs (R=3,S=0,T=4,P=1); no per-round token endowment is defined.",0.6,N/A,"Outcome metrics are cooperation frequency, punishment, reputation, and reward; no continuous contribution rate.",1,N/A,No monetary contribution amounts; binary cooperate/defect moves.,1,N/R,Paper reports 'societal reward per episode' but does not normalize by the theoretical maximum payoff explicitly.,0.55,N/R,"They track societal reward per episode, but no single scalar value for final or average payoff is reported numerically.",0.4,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational MARL simulations.,1,"Population of 5 independent DQN agents repeatedly play the Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma for 10 rounds per episode over 2000 episodes. Partner selection stage: each agent selects an interaction partner using reputations (public scalar integers updated by cooperative, defective, and punishing actions). Prisoner’s Dilemma payoffs: (C,C)=(3,3); (C,D)=(0,4); (D,C)=(4,0); (D,D)=(1,1). Third-party punishment stage: For each pair A,B, two non-involved agents P and K may punish A and B respectively, based on the last game’s actions. Punishing agent pays 10 reward units and target loses 3. Just punishment (punishing a defector) yields an additional +12 reward to the punisher in Scheme 2 (net +2); unjust punishment of a cooperator carries no bonus and reduces punisher reputation by 3 (just punishment increases reputation by 2). Reputation enters partner selection and the game-playing state; punishment state observes last actions only. Each agent uses up to three separate DQNs (partner selection, play, punish) with 1 hidden layer of 128 ReLU units and shared hyperparameters across agents.",Summarized core simulation and payoff structure from Sections 3.1–3.3 and 4.1–4.3 for this specific mechanism combination.,0.94
10.1007_s10458-025-09698-5.md,"TPP (third-party punishment only, Scheme 2)",From Section 4.1 list: 'TPP third-party punishment'; using Scheme 2 as per 4.3.,0.95,Social mechanisms: third-party punishment + reputation (no partner selection); Just-punishment reward scheme: Scheme 2 (net +2 reward for just punisher).,This condition disables partner selection while retaining third-party punishment and reputation; Scheme 2 selected as successful reward scheme.,0.95,False,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,False,Not an empirical human experiment.,1,False,No human lab setting.,1,True,MARL simulation as described in Section 3.,1,False,No closed-form analysis used for results.,1,5,Same population size across all primary experiments (Section 3.2.4).,0.98,10,Each episode has ten rounds (Section 3.2).,1,1,Binary cooperate/defect choice in each PD round; coded as a non-contribution game but closest to all-or-nothing in this schema.,0.7,N/A,No contribution endowments.,1,N/A,IPD not linear public good.,1,0,No communication stage or messages among agents are described.,0.98,0,"Agents see reputations and last actions, not aggregate outcomes.",0.8,1,Punishers explicitly choose whether to punish a specific target (A or B) based on that agent's previous move.,0.85,0,No direct rewarding between agents.,1,0,No mention that agents observe round count.,0.7,1,Third-party punishment is active by definition in TPP.,1,10,Cost to punisher fixed at 10 units for all punishment-enabled conditions.,1,3,Punished agent loses 3 units upon being punished.,1,0,No separate reward action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Rewards given directly as payoffs rather than from an explicit endowment.,0.6,N/A,"Outcome metric is cooperation frequency, not contribution rate.",1,N/A,No explicit contributions.,1,N/R,"They do not provide normalized efficiency numbers, only societal reward trajectories.",0.55,N/R,No single summarized payoff level per condition is numerically reported.,0.4,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Purely computational MARL setting.,1,"Same IPD and punishment structure as TPP-S, but without partner selection stage. Stage 1 pairing is random: 'If partner selection is not included... every agent is randomly paired with another agent in the population.' Reputations (scalar integers) are globally visible and updated based on cooperation/defection and just vs unjust punishment. Third-party punishers P and K are randomly chosen non-participants for each pair A,B each round and may punish based on last actions. DQN architecture and hyperparameters for play and punishment models are as described in Section 3.3; partner-selection DQN is absent in this condition.",Derived by removing partner selection from the general simulation description.,0.94
10.1007_s10458-025-09698-5.md,"DP-S (direct punishment with partner selection, Scheme 2)",From Section 4.1: 'DP-S direct punishment with partner selection'; Scheme 2 as per 4.3.,0.95,Social mechanisms: direct punishment + partner selection + reputation; Just-punishment reward scheme: Scheme 2 (net +2 reward for just punisher).,Condition enables direct punishment and partner selection alongside reputation; Scheme 2 is the adopted just-punishment reward scheme in the main results.,0.96,False,Only simulated RL agents.,1,False,Not an empirical human/field experiment.,1,False,Simulation environment.,1,True,Condition is one of several MARL simulations.,1,False,No analytical derivations for outcomes.,1,5,Population of five agents as specified for all experiments (Section 3.2.4).,0.98,10,Ten rounds per episode (Section 3.2).,1,1,Decision each round is between C and D; no graded contributions.,0.7,N/A,No contributions/endowments.,1,N/A,No public goods multiplier.,1,0,No explicit communication between agents.,0.98,0,States contain only reputations and recent actions; no summaries.,0.8,1,Direct punishment targets specific counterpart in the interaction (A punishes B or vice versa).,0.85,0,No explicit reward actions.,1,0,Round/episode count not included in state definition.,0.7,1,Direct punishment stage is present in this condition.,1,10,Punisher loses 10 reward units when punishing.,1,3,Punished agent loses 3 units per punishment.,1,0,No reward-as-action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Game uses fixed payoff matrix; no explicit starting endowment.,0.6,N/A,Not a contribution game; outcomes measured via cooperation fraction.,1,N/A,No invested amounts.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency ratio is reported.,0.55,N/R,Figures show societal reward trajectories but provide no single scalar summary.,0.4,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Fully simulated MARL.,1,"Same IPD parameters and punishment payoffs as other conditions. Stage 1: partner selection is enabled; each agent selects an interaction partner for the next 10 rounds using reputations only. Stage 2: agents play IPD using a DQN whose state S_play for direct-punishment populations is defined as a 2D vector of own and partner's previous actions (no reputation). Stage 3: direct punishment; after each PD game, A may punish B and B may punish A, based on S_punish containing the previous two actions only. Reputation is updated by +1 for cooperation, -1 for defection, +2 for just punishment, and -3 for unjust punishment. Just punishment also yields +12 reward (on top of the -10 cost) to the punisher under Scheme 2 (net +2). All agents have partner-selection, play, and punish DQNs with one hidden layer of 128 ReLU units and shared hyperparameters.",Condition-specific state definitions (no reputation in play/punish states) and partner-selection usage drawn from Section 3.3 and Appendix B.,0.95
10.1007_s10458-025-09698-5.md,"DP (direct punishment only, Scheme 2)",From Section 4.1: 'DP direct punishment'; Scheme 2 per 4.3.,0.95,Social mechanisms: direct punishment + reputation (no partner selection); Just-punishment reward scheme: Scheme 2 (net +2 reward for just punisher).,Partner selection is disabled in DP while direct punishment and reputation remain; Scheme 2 is the operative reward scheme.,0.95,False,Simulation-only condition.,1,False,No empirical human or field data.,1,False,Not a physical lab study.,1,True,Part of the MARL simulation suite.,1,False,No analytical results; only simulations.,1,5,Population size same across main experiments.,0.98,10,Ten rounds per episode.,1,1,Binary C/D choice each round.,0.7,N/A,No contribution accounts.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication phase included.,0.98,0,"Agents only see local state variables, not aggregate statistics.",0.8,1,Agents punish their own interaction partner based on their actions.,0.85,0,No direct reward actions.,1,0,No text indicating round count is in the state.,0.7,1,Direct punishment is active in DP.,1,10,Punisher pays 10 reward units when punishing.,1,3,Punished loses 3 units.,1,0,No reward actions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs drawn from PD matrix rather than token endowments.,0.6,N/A,No contribution variables; only actions C/D.,1,N/A,No tokens contributed.,1,N/R,No normalized efficiency statistic given.,0.55,N/R,"Only trajectories of societal reward, no single reported numbers.",0.4,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Computational MARL setup.,1,"Same as DP-S but with random partner assignment instead of partner selection. Each episode: (1) agents are randomly paired (no selection DQN used); (2) each pair plays IPD for 10 rounds, using S_play consisting of own and partner's last actions; (3) after each round, direct punishment decisions are made based on S_punish (previous actions). Reputation and reward update rules and DQN architecture match those described for DP-S.",Derived DP-S description minus partner selection.,0.95
10.1007_s10458-025-09698-5.md,"TPPDP-S (third-party + direct punishment with partner selection, Scheme 2)",From Section 4.1: 'TPPDP-S third-party punishment with direct punishment and partner selection'; uses Scheme 2 as in main results.,0.96,Social mechanisms: third-party punishment + direct punishment + partner selection + reputation; Just-punishment reward scheme: Scheme 2 (net +2 reward for just punisher).,Condition enables both punishment types plus partner selection and reputation under Scheme 2.,0.96,False,No human subjects; multi-agent RL simulation.,1,False,Not an empirical controlled experiment with people.,1,False,No human lab environment.,1,True,Condition is a simulation treatment.,1,False,No analytical solution method.,1,5,Same 5-agent population size.,0.98,10,Ten rounds per episode.,1,1,Binary actions in IPD.,0.7,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,No MPCR concept here.,1,0,No described communication.,0.98,0,Agents have local reputations/actions only.,0.8,1,Both direct and third-party punishers choose specific targets A or B based on their last actions.,0.9,0,No separate reward action.,1,0,Round count is not mentioned as part of state.,0.7,1,Both punishment modes are active.,1,10,Same cost structure for any punishment action.,1,3,Each punishment reduces target's reward by 3.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Payoffs specified directly.,0.6,N/A,Not a contribution framework.,1,N/A,No tokens or amounts.,1,N/R,No explicit normalized efficiency metric.,0.55,N/R,Only curves for societal reward are plotted; no summary figure.,0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All conditions are simulated MARL.,1,"Partner selection is enabled as in TPP-S and DP-S. After selection, during each of 10 rounds per episode, each pair A,B plays the IPD then both direct and third-party punishment opportunities exist: (i) A may punish B and B may punish A (direct punishment); (ii) two additional non-involved agents P and K are sampled to judge A and B respectively (third-party punishment), all using the same punishment DQN. The punishment state S_punish consists of the last actions of A and B; reputations are not included. For play-state inputs, populations using combined punishment use S_play ∈ R^4 consisting of own and partner reputations and previous actions (Appendix B). Reward and reputation updates follow the same rules as other conditions with Scheme 2: punisher loses 10, target loses 3; just punishment yields a +12 bonus for the punisher; reputation +2 for just, -3 for unjust; playing C gives +1 reputation, D gives -1.",Combined description based on sections defining direct and third-party punishment and Appendix B's note that combined punishment uses the 'third-party style' S_play (with reputation).,0.96
10.1007_s10458-025-09698-5.md,"TPPDP (third-party + direct punishment only, Scheme 2)",From Section 4.1: 'TPPDP third-party punishment with direct punishment'; without partner selection; uses Scheme 2 in main text.,0.96,Social mechanisms: third-party punishment + direct punishment + reputation (no partner selection); Just-punishment reward scheme: Scheme 2 (net +2 reward for just punisher).,"Condition activates both punishment types and reputation but not partner selection, under Scheme 2.",0.96,False,No human subjects.,1,False,Simulation study.,1,False,Not a lab experiment.,1,True,MARL simulation condition.,1,False,Outcomes not analytically derived.,1,5,Population of 5 used for all primary simulations.,0.98,10,Ten rounds per episode.,1,1,Binary C/D actions in IPD.,0.7,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,Not applicable for PD.,1,0,No communication channels in description.,0.98,0,No aggregate outcome displays.,0.8,1,"Punishment targets are explicit (A, B) for direct and third-party punishers.",0.9,0,No separate rewards.,1,0,Rounds not in state definition.,0.7,1,Both direct and third-party punishment are enabled.,1,10,Cost structure identical across punishment-enabled conditions.,1,3,Target loses 3 reward units per punishment.,1,0,No reward action.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Not specified; payoffs come directly from matrix and punishment scheme.,0.6,N/A,Not a contribution framework.,1,N/A,No numerical contributions.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency values given.,0.55,N/R,Only time series of societal reward plotted.,0.4,N/A,Only artificial agents.,1,N/A,Only artificial agents.,1,N/A,Only artificial agents.,1,N/A,Only artificial agents.,1,No human,Computational experiment.,1,"Same combined punishment structure as TPPDP-S but without partner selection. At the start of each episode, agents are randomly paired. In each of 10 rounds, each pair plays the IPD (S_play includes own and partner reputation and previous actions as in combined-punishment conditions) and then both direct and third-party punishment can occur: A/B choose whether to punish each other; additionally, two non-participant agents are assigned as third-party punishers for A and B, all using a shared punishment DQN that observes only the last two actions. Reward and reputation updates mirror those in TPPDP-S under Scheme 2.",Same as TPPDP-S with random pairing rather than partner selection.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.2319925121.md,"Repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma – 3-strategy set (ALLC, TFT, ALLD)","Condition described as standard 3-strategy space in repeated PD, used for Fig. 1C–D (“biased strategy set of three strategies”).",0.95,"Strategy set restricted to three memory-1 strategies: ALLC, TFT, ALLD","Authors explicitly compare outcomes for different choices of strategy set; here they use only ALLC, TFT, ALLD.",0.98,False,Only simulations/ODEs of evolutionary dynamics; no human participants.,1,False,"The study is theoretical/simulation-based, not a controlled behavioral experiment.",1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,True,They numerically simulate stochastic imitation dynamics and analyze replicator dynamics.,1,True,They present and analyze the replicator equation and its fixed points for this strategy set.,0.9,2,Repeated prisoner’s dilemma is played pairwise between two agents.,1,N/R,Game repeats with continuation probability δ; expected number of rounds 1/(1−δ) is given but no fixed finite round count.,0.7,1,"In PD each round players choose C or D; there is no continuous contribution level, so contribution-choice is effectively all-or-nothing.",0.8,N/A,Not a public goods or endowment allocation game; no initial allocation between public/private accounts.,1,N/A,"Not a linear public goods game, but a 2×2 prisoner’s dilemma with payoffs R,S,T,P.",1,N/A,No human subjects or explicit communication; agents are strategies in a game-theoretic model.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human participants; purely modeled payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in this repeated PD model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No human participants; agents are not shown information.,1,0,Standard repeated PD without explicit costly punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit reward stage follows the PD interaction.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Stage-game is defined directly by payoff matrix (R,S,T,P); no per-round monetary endowment.",1,N/R,They report average percentage of cooperation but not numeric values in the text; only qualitatively (cooperation overestimated).,0.4,N/A,"Binary C/D actions, no token amounts.",1,N/R,"They discuss cooperation levels, not normalized efficiency numbers.",0.3,N/R,No explicit numerical long-run payoff statistics reported; only qualitative dynamics.,0.3,N/A,No human or animal subjects.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The model is purely theoretical/simulation-based with artificial agents.,1,"Repeated prisoner’s dilemma with payoff parameters R=3, S=0, P=1, T=4 and continuation probability δ varied; dynamics studied under replicator equation and stochastic imitation in population of size N (e.g., N=20) with mutation μ=10⁻³ and intensity of selection β=1.0.",All these parameters are reported explicitly with Fig. 1 description.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.2319925121.md,Repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma – full memory-1 strategy set,Condition described as including “all strategies that are conditioned on the opponent's last move” and contrasted with the 3-strategy set in Fig. 1C–D.,0.9,"Strategy set expanded to all memory-1 strategies conditioned on opponent’s last move (beyond ALLC, TFT, ALLD)",Authors state they include all such strategies and report its effects on cooperation.,0.9,False,No human participants; purely evolutionary simulations/analytical reasoning.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; parameter sweeps and simulations.,1,False,No physical lab setting.,1,True,They ‘include all other strategies’ and inspect average cooperation via imitation dynamics (ref. 55).,0.95,True,Some statements on equilibrium abundance and Nash equilibria with strong vs weak selection rely on analytical results in ref. 55.,0.8,2,Still a repeated PD between two agents.,1,N/R,"As above, repeated with probability δ; no fixed round count.",0.7,1,Actions remain binary cooperate/defect each round.,0.9,N/A,Not a PGG or continuous contribution environment.,1,N/A,Game is not a public good game.,1,N/A,No human subjects or chat.,1,N/A,No interface; simulated strategies.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,Model remains a repeated PD without costly punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,Payoffs defined directly via PD matrix.,1,N/R,"They state cooperation is reduced compared to 3-strategy set and show curves in Fig. 1C–D, but no numeric values.",0.4,N/A,No token amounts; binary actions.,1,N/R,Not explicitly reported.,0.3,N/R,No numeric payoff statistics summarized.,0.3,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,N/A,No empirical subjects.,1,No human,Only theoretical and simulation work with artificial strategies.,1,"Same repeated PD as 3-strategy case (R=3, S=0, P=1, T=4, continuation probability δ). Strategy space includes all memory-1 strategies conditioned on opponent’s last move; analysis focuses on how including neutral or dominated strategies alters cooperation under weak and strong selection (ref. 55).",Parameters and qualitative findings described in the section ‘All strategies of a given memory size’.,0.9
10.1073_pnas.2319925121.md,Optional Public Goods with Punishment – 4-strategy Hauert et al. set,"Described as ‘original set with 4 strategies’ introduced by Hauert et al. (33): Cooperators, Punishers, Defectors, Loners.",0.98,"Strategy set restricted to 4 strategies: C, P (cooperate+punsih defectors), D, L; only cooperators can punish and only defectors can be punished.",Explicitly listed in text and Table 1 as 4-strategy restriction.,1,False,Evolutionary simulations of strategy dynamics; no human data.,1,False,"The analyses are simulations/theoretical, not controlled behavioral experiments.",1,False,No lab environment described.,1,True,They use an imitation process in finite populations and compute long-term cooperation levels (Fig. 2B–C).,1,True,"They discuss evolutionary dynamics, cycles, and long-term abundances referencing analytical work in refs. 33 and 34.",0.8,5,They specify group size n=5 in Fig. 2 caption.,1,N/R,"Public goods interactions repeat over evolutionary time, but no discrete repeated-round count is provided.",0.4,1,"Each individual either contributes fixed amount c or 0, so contribution is all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,"No endowed participants; contributions are choices per round, not reallocations of an endowment.",1,0.6,"In the PGG, each contribution c is multiplied by r and shared among n: MPCR = r/n. Using r=3.0 and n=5 from Fig. 2, MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.95,N/A,"Simulated evolutionary agents, not human subjects.",1,N/A,No experimental interface; no feedback screens discussed.,1,N/A,Model does not represent identities or information disclosure; only payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No human interface.,1,1,"Second stage allows peer punishment; punishers pay cost γ, defectors incur fine β.",1,1,"Parameters given as β=1.0, γ=1.0 in Fig. 2; γ is cost per unit punishment.",0.95,1,"Fine β=1.0 imposed on each punished defector, interpreted as punishment impact per unit cost.",0.9,0,Only costly punishment is modeled; no positive rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Each contribution is c=1.0, but total per-round endowment per player is not described as such; unclear if 1 is full endowment.",0.5,N/R,"They show ‘average percentage of cooperation’ across simulations in Fig. 2, but no numeric values in text.",0.4,N/A,"Contribution is fixed at c=1.0 when cooperating; but they summarize outcomes via cooperation percentages, not token amounts.",0.6,N/R,They describe high cooperation and cycles but no normalized efficiency metrics.,0.3,N/R,Long-run mean payoffs not reported numerically.,0.3,N/A,No human or animal participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,All results from theoretical models and simulations in finite populations.,1,"Optional public goods game: players can Cooperate (contribute c=1), Defect (contribute 0), or be Loners (receive σ=1.0 and do not participate). Contributions of participants are multiplied by r=3.0 and equally divided among n=5 participants. Punishers cooperate and punish defectors at cost γ=1.0, imposing fine β=1.0. Population size N=100, intensity of selection 1.0; dynamics show cycles where punishment-supported cooperation, defection, and loners alternate (ref. 33).",All parameter values and qualitative dynamics summarized around Fig. 2 and in the text on ‘Punishment in optional public good games.’,0.95
10.1073_pnas.2319925121.md,Optional Public Goods with Punishment – 9-strategy García & Traulsen set,Table 1 lists 9-strategy case (“Loners cannot punish or be punished”) corresponding to ref. 34; also referred to as ‘set with 9 strategies’.,0.98,"Strategy set expanded to 9 strategies where loners cannot punish or be punished; cooperators/defectors may have punishment options, loners only choose participation option.","Authors explicitly state ‘loners not taking part in the game are unable to monitor ... and thus cannot punish ... or be punished, leading to nine strategies’.",1,False,Model and results are simulation/theory-based.,1,False,No empirical experimental protocol with subjects.,1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,They study ‘average percentage of cooperation’ in an imitation process for 9-strategy set (Fig. 2B–C).,1,True,"Discussion references analytical understanding (e.g., that 4 and 9 strategy sets match for strong selection).",0.75,5,Same PGG group size n=5 as in Fig. 2.,1,N/R,Number of interaction rounds not specified.,0.4,1,Contribution decisions remain binary cooperate/defect.,1,N/A,No initial endowments modeled.,1,0.6,"Same PGG parameters r=3.0, n=5; MPCR=r/n.",0.95,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No experimental UI.,1,N/A,Identity revelation not modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,1,Peer punishment is still part of the game; only loners are excluded from punishing and being punished.,1,1,"Uses same parameterization β=1.0, γ=1.0 as 4- and 24-strategy comparisons (Fig. 2).",0.9,1,Fine per punishment β=1.0.,0.9,0,No peer rewards introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"As before, each cooperator contributes c=1 but no explicit endowment statement.",0.5,N/R,Fig. 2B–C plot cooperation level but no numeric table in text.,0.4,N/A,"Outcome tracked via cooperation frequency, not contribution tokens.",0.6,N/R,No efficiencies reported.,0.3,N/R,No explicit payoff statistics.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Evolutionary model over artificial strategies.,1,"Same optional PGG as in 4-strategy case (r=3.0, c=1.0, σ=1.0, β=1.0, γ=1.0, n=5, N=100, intensity of selection 1.0). Strategy strings have four elements: participation choice (C/D/L) and three binary punishment bits (for punishing cooperators, defectors, loners). In 9-strategy restriction, loners neither punish nor are punished, reducing 24 possible strategies to 9. Under strong selection, 4- and 9-strategy sets yield similar cooperation levels.",Derived from Fig. 2A and associated discussion as well as Table 1 description.,0.9
