ntensity of selection 1.0). Strategy encoded as four-element string: participation type (C/D/L) and three binary punishment bits indicating whether to punish cooperators, defectors, or loners. With no restrictions, all 3×2³=24 strategies are allowed (Rand & Nowak 2011), leading to widespread antisocial punishment and a collapse of cooperation.",Directly from Fig. 2A plus accompanying narrative and Table 1.,0.95
10.1073_pnas.2319925121.md,Indirect Reciprocity – Exogenous social norm (stern judging only),"Described in Fig. 3B as ‘Exogenous norms assume one highly cooperative norm [""stern judging"" (64)], thus agents only choose their Action Rules (4 strategies)’.",0.98,"Reputation norm exogenously fixed to stern judging; agents only evolve action rules (Cooperator, Discriminator, Antidiscriminator, Defector).",Explicitly contrasted with endogenous-norm condition where both norms and action rules evolve.,1,False,This is a simulation model of indirect reciprocity; no human subjects.,1,False,The setting is computational; not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,They simulate evolutionary dynamics and report ‘average percentage of cooperation reached in the long-term’ in Fig. 3B.,1,True,"The framework builds on analytical work (Santos et al. 2018; Ohtsuki & Iwasa), combining analysis with simulations.",0.7,N/R,"Indirect reciprocity model assumes a well-mixed population with pairwise donation-game interactions but the population size is given (50), not the group size per se.",0.4,N/R,Interactions repeat over evolutionary time with random matching; no fixed-round repeated game specified.,0.4,1,"Donation game is a binary decision to help (pay cost c, give benefit b) or not; thus contribution is all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,Not a PGG with continuous contributions; binary help decision only.,1,N/A,"Donation game, not linear public good.",1,N/A,No human participants or communication channel.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,No explicit costly punishment; reputations are updated by social norm.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism; only reputational changes.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,"Model does not include material punishment, only reputational updating.",1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,Benefits are via reputation and helping; no separate costly rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Donation game payoffs defined via cost c and benefit b; no fixed endowment per round.,1,N/R,Fig. 3B plots average percentage of cooperation but values are not provided numerically in the text.,0.4,N/A,"Binary help decision; model reports cooperation frequency, not token amounts.",0.7,N/R,No normalized efficiency metric explicitly reported.,0.3,N/R,"They focus on cooperation rates, not aggregate payoffs.",0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Population of artificial agents simulated with indirect reciprocity dynamics.,1,"Indirect reciprocity with binary reputations and donation game: four action rules (Cooperators, Discriminators, Antidiscriminators, Defectors). Social norm is fixed to ‘stern judging’, a highly cooperative norm taken from Santos et al. (2016). Population size is 50; other parameters follow reference 65. After each interaction, donor’s reputation is updated according to stern judging; evolutionary dynamics over action rules determine long-run cooperation.",Directly from Fig. 3A–B caption and surrounding text on exogenous norms.,0.9
10.1073_pnas.2319925121.md,Indirect Reciprocity – Endogenous social norms and action rules,Described in Fig. 3B as ‘Endogenous norms let agents choose a combination of four Actions Rules as well as a Social Norm (4×16=64 strategies)’.,0.98,"Both action rules (4 types) and social norms (16 binary norms) evolve endogenously, yielding 64 possible strategies.",Explicit explanation in Fig. 3B and reference to Xu et al. (65).,1,False,Simulation of evolutionary dynamics; no human data.,1,False,The study is not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab component.,1,True,They note ‘simulation models confirm’ and present cooperation levels from an imitation/evolutionary process.,1,True,"Framework builds on analytical characterization of norms and equilibrium conditions (Santos et al. 72, Ohtsuki & Iwasa).",0.7,N/R,"As with exogenous case, interactions are pairwise in well-mixed population; only population size (50) is specified.",0.4,N/R,Number of repeated encounters not specified; evolutionary time with many interactions.,0.4,1,Same binary donation (help vs not) as standard indirect reciprocity models.,0.9,N/A,Not a PGG; binary helping choice.,1,N/A,"Donation game setting, not linear PGG.",1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Punishment per se not modeled; only reputations.,1,N/A,No material rewards.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,"Only reputational changes affect future cooperation, not explicit material punishment.",1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,"Benefits are outcome of helping, not costly rewards.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,"Payoffs determined by donation (cost c, benefit b).",1,N/R,"Fig. 3B shows that cooperation collapses when norms are endogenous, but does not provide explicit numeric levels.",0.4,N/A,Binary help vs not; outcomes reported as share of cooperative acts.,0.7,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric.,0.3,N/R,"Focus is on cooperation levels and norm cycling, not payoffs.",0.3,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,N/A,No empirical participants.,1,No human,All dynamics simulated in silico over artificial agents.,1,"Same indirect reciprocity framework as exogenous case but with endogenous norms: each strategy specifies both an action rule (one of 4) and a social norm (one of 16 possible binary norms), giving 64 strategy combinations. Population size is 50; other parameters follow Xu et al. (2019). Agents interact via donation games, update reputations via their chosen norm, and reproduce according to payoffs. Neutral drift between strategies with different norms but identical action payoffs leads to cycling of norms and collapse of cooperation; similar results hold for more complex norms (ref. 66).",Summarizes textual description around Fig. 3A–B and citations 65–66.,0.9
10.1016_j.jebo.2025.107071.md,"6-Vote-0.75 (Complete punishment institution, MGR=0.75)",Uses treatment label and key parameters as in Table 1: complete institution (6-Vote) with MGR 0.75.,1,Punishment institution type: complete (6-Vote); Marginal group return (MGR)=0.75,"Condition differs by institution type (complete vs incomplete) and by MGR level; this row is the complete, low-MGR condition.",1,True,Online experiment with 654 student participants overall; this treatment includes human subjects.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab-style game with between-subject treatment assignment.,1,False,"Experiment was conducted online (oTree, remote participation), not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,All decisions made by human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,"While theory is discussed, the reported results are from an experiment with human subjects.",1,6,Groups of n=6 in the punishment game in all treatments.,1,24,"Punishment game played over 24 rounds, divided into six phases of four rounds.",1,1,Players choose how much (0–100) to contribute; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each round players receive an endowment (100 tokens) in a private account; must choose how much to contribute.,1,0.125,"Payoff: (100 - g_i)(1-p) + α Σ g_j, n=6. MGR = nα = 0.75 ⇒ α = 0.75/6 = 0.125; MPCR is α.",1,1,Participants could ask questions through a private computer chat with the experimenter during the games.,1,1,"After each round, individual contributions, deductions, and payoffs of red and blue players are displayed to all group members.",1,0,"Within subgroups, members are displayed in random order and cannot be tracked; no identities linked to deductions.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,1,Timeline figure and standard oTree practice; phases and remaining rounds are typically displayed; inferred but not explicit.,0.6,1,Formal punishment institution can be implemented and is enforced automatically once chosen.,1,0,Paper explicitly states that enforcement is free of cost; deductions are not costly to punishers.,1,1,Punishment rate p is the percentage deducted from amount not contributed; 100% deduction per unit implies 1 token deducted per 1 token withheld.,1,0,"Design only includes formal punishment, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,"Payoff function for punishment game is π_i = (100 - g_i)(1-p) + α Σ g_j, implying per-round endowment of 100 tokens.",1,N/R,Exact normalized contributions by treatment are not numerically reported in the excerpt.,0,N/R,"Only qualitative descriptions and approximate percentages are provided, not precise averages by treatment.",0,N/R,No explicit efficiency or payoff-as-fraction-of-maximum values reported by treatment.,0,N/R,Group earnings by treatment are not reported numerically.,0,Germany,"Students were recruited from universities in Kassel and Magdeburg, both located in Germany.",1,N/R,Age was collected in questionnaire but no summary statistics reported in the provided text.,0,N/R,Gender collected but no distribution reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Experiment conducted with undergraduate students from the general student population.,1,Online,Experiment conducted online using oTree; payments via PayPal.,1,"Two-stage study: first a one-shot 6-player public goods game with endowment 250 and MPCR=1/3; then the 24-round punishment game. In this treatment all 6 players vote each phase on a punishment rate p∈[0,1]; one of the two middle proposals (3rd or 4th) is randomly selected. Implemented p applies to all 6 players for 4 rounds of that phase; deductions apply only to un-contributed endowment and are not redistributed. MGR=0.75 makes full cooperation unprofitable; weakly dominant strategy is zero punishment and zero contributions.",Summarizes key structural aspects and theoretical prediction for this condition based on design section.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2025.107071.md,"6-Vote-1.5 (Complete punishment institution, MGR=1.5)",Uses treatment label and MGR from Table 1 for complete institution with MGR 1.5.,1,Punishment institution type: complete (6-Vote); Marginal group return (MGR)=1.5,Condition varies MGR while keeping punishment institution complete.,1,True,Human-subject online experiment.,1,True,Controlled game with randomized between-subject treatments.,1,False,Conducted online rather than in a physical lab.,1,False,No computational agents; all decisions by humans.,1,False,Analytical predictions exist but results are empirical.,1,6,Groups of six players in the punishment game.,1,24,Six 4-round phases.,1,1,Continuous contribution choice g_i from 0 to 100.,1,0,Endowment given privately; contributions are chosen.,1,0.25,MGR = nα = 1.5 with n=6 ⇒ α = 1.5/6 = 0.25.,1,1,Participants could ask questions via private computer chat.,1,1,"Round feedback includes contributions, deductions, and payoffs for all red and blue players.",1,0,Randomized ordering within subgroups prevents mapping deductions to individuals.,1,0,No reward option exists.,1,1,Phase-based design with fixed 6 phases likely shown; inferred.,0.6,1,Formal punishment rate chosen and enforced each phase.,1,0,Enforcement is free of cost by design.,1,1,Punishment rate is proportion of un-contributed endowment deducted from payoff (1:1).,1,0,Only punishment institution is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,Same payoff function as other 6-Vote treatments: (100 - g_i)(1-p) + α Σ g_j.,1,N/R,Graphical trends only; no numeric average contribution fraction by treatment is provided.,0,N/R,Only stated that contributions are very high with deterrent punishment; no exact mean.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency metrics per treatment.,0,N/R,Treatment-level average payoffs not reported.,0,Germany,Participants from two German universities.,1,N/R,Age collected but not summarized.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Participants described as undergraduate students.,1,Online,"Experiment run online with oTree, recruitment via ORSEE/hroot, payment via PayPal.",1,"Same 6-player, 24-round public goods game with endogenous formal punishment as 6-Vote-0.75, but with MGR=1.5 (α=0.25). All six players vote on a punishment rate p∈[0,1]; one of the two middle proposals is selected each phase. With deterrent punishment (≥76%) full contributions are individually optimal. Weakly dominant prediction: deterrent punishment and full contributions. Empirically, median punishment 100%, average 91.58%, deterrent punishment about 88% of the time and maximum punishment about 85%; contributions are near full when punishment is deterrent.",Summarizes design and main behavioral outcomes for this MGR level from Table 2 and text.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2025.107071.md,"6-Vote-2.5 (Complete punishment institution, MGR=2.5)",Uses treatment label and MGR from Table 1 for complete institution with highest MGR (2.5).,1,Punishment institution type: complete (6-Vote); Marginal group return (MGR)=2.5,Same institution as other 6-Vote treatments but with higher MGR.,1,True,Human-subject online experiment.,1,True,"Randomized treatment assignment, controlled payoff structure.",1,False,Conducted remotely (online).,1,False,No artificial agents.,1,False,Results are empirical rather than purely theoretical.,1,6,Groups of six players.,1,24,Six phases × 4 rounds.,1,1,Continuous contribution between 0 and 100.,1,0,Endowment initially private.,1,0.4167,MGR = nα = 2.5 with n=6 ⇒ α = 2.5/6 ≈ 0.4167.,1,1,Private chat with experimenter allowed.,1,1,"Round feedback shows contributions, deductions, payoffs for all subgroup members.",1,0,"Participants cannot track individual histories, only subgroup aggregates.",1,0,No reward institution.,1,1,Fixed number of phases and rounds likely known; inferred.,0.6,1,Formal punishment is available and chosen by all 6 players.,1,0,Punishment is enforced exogenously without cost.,1,1,p% of withheld endowment is deducted; 1:1 deduction.,1,0,No reward feature mentioned.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Same payoff function structure as other punishment-game treatments.,1,N/R,Only general description: contributions very high when punishment deterrent.,0,N/R,No numeric means per treatment reported.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency figures by treatment.,0,N/R,Group payoff data not reported by treatment.,0,Germany,Two German universities supplied the subject pool.,1,N/R,No summary ages provided.,0,N/R,No gender distribution provided.,0,Undergraduate students,Participants described as undergraduate students.,1,Online,Run online via oTree with PayPal payments.,1,"6-player, 24-round public goods game with endogenous complete formal punishment. MGR=2.5 (α≈0.4167). In each phase, all 6 players propose p∈[0,1]; random choice of 3rd or 4th ordered proposal applies group-wide. Deterrent punishment thresholds derived in paper: ≥59% for MGR=2.5. Weakly dominant prediction: deterrent punishment and full contributions. Observed median punishment 100%, average 87.63%; deterrent punishment ~88% and maximum punishment ~76.67% of the time; contributions nearly full under deterrent punishment.",Combines design information with summary statistics from Table 2 and theoretical analysis.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2025.107071.md,"3-Vote-1.5 (Strictly incomplete punishment institution, MGR=1.5, red subgroup bound)","Uses treatment label and description from Table 1: strictly incomplete (3-Vote), MGR 1.5.",1,"Punishment institution type: strictly incomplete (3-Vote, only red players vote and are punished); Marginal group return (MGR)=1.5",Condition varies both institution incompleteness and MGR.,1,True,Human-subject online experiment.,1,True,Between-subject randomized treatments with controlled game.,1,False,Conducted online.,1,False,No non-human agents.,1,False,Outcomes are experimentally measured.,1,6,Groups consist of three red and three blue players.,1,24,Six phases × 4 rounds.,1,1,Players choose g_i freely between 0 and 100.,1,0,Endowment given to private account at start of each round.,1,0.25,Same MGR=1.5 as in 6-Vote-1.5 ⇒ α = 1.5/6 = 0.25.,1,1,Participants could ask clarifying questions via private chat.,1,1,"After each round, contributions, deductions, and payoffs of red and blue players are displayed to all six players.",1,0,Only subgroup-level anonymous display; members randomized in display order.,1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Phase structure fixed and likely displayed; inferred.,0.6,1,Only red subgroup can vote to implement punishment that applies to red players.,1,0,Formal enforcement is exogenous and costless.,1,1,Selected punishment rate p deducts p proportion of withheld tokens from punished subgroup’s members.,1,0,No reward option described.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Same 100-token endowment and payoff function for the punishment game; applies to both subgroups.,1,N/R,Figure 3 and 4 show approximate percentages but not numeric means by treatment and player type.,0,N/R,"Only qualitative: contributions low when punishment non-deterrent, higher when deterrent.",0,N/R,No treatment-level payoff/efficiency figures.,0,N/R,Group earnings not separately reported for this treatment.,0,Germany,Same subject pool as other treatments.,1,N/R,No age summary in the text.,0,N/R,Gender distribution not reported.,0,Undergraduate students,All treatments share the same undergraduate student sample.,1,Online,Same online oTree setup used for all treatments.,1,"6-player public goods game with two subgroups (3 red, 3 blue). Only red players vote on punishment; middle of three red proposals is implemented as punishment rate on red players only. Blue players never punished and do not vote. MGR=1.5; cooperation profitable for group but not for red subgroup (marginal subgroup return 0.75). Theory predicts zero punishment and zero contributions by red and blue. Observed median punishment 0, average 18.53; zero punishment 65% of the time, deterrent and maximum punishment together about 23%; contributions of red players are high when they face deterrent punishment and low otherwise; blue players respond positively but partially to red punishment choices.","Summarizes institutional incompleteness (only red bound), theoretical predictions, and punishment statistics from Table 2 and text.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2025.107071.md,"3-Vote-2.5 (Strictly incomplete punishment institution, MGR=2.5, red subgroup bound)",Treatment label and parameters from Table 1: 3-Vote with MGR 2.5.,1,"Punishment institution type: strictly incomplete (3-Vote, only red players vote and are punished); Marginal group return (MGR)=2.5",Same institutional incompleteness as 3-Vote-1.5 but with higher MGR 2.5.,1,True,Condition is part of the human-subject experiment.,1,True,Controlled between-subject design.,1,False,Run online.,1,False,No simulation-only component.,1,False,Empirical results collected from subjects.,1,6,Three red and three blue players per group.,1,24,Six phases × four rounds as in all punishment treatments.,1,1,Continuous contribution choice from endowment.,1,0,Endowment initially kept privately.,1,0.4167,Same MGR=2.5 as 6-Vote-2.5 ⇒ α = 2.5/6 ≈ 0.4167.,1,1,Private chat with experimenter available across treatments.,1,1,"Outcome screen shows contributions, deductions, and payoffs for both red and blue.",1,0,Randomized within-subgroup ordering prevents individual identification.,1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Structure with 6 phases would typically be visible; inferred.,0.6,1,Red subgroup can implement punishment institution applying only to red players.,1,0,Enforcement is costless by assumption.,1,1,Deduction rate equals chosen punishment rate p on un-contributed tokens.,1,0,No reward feature is part of the design.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Same 100-token endowment per round in the punishment game.,1,N/R,Figures show qualitative differences; no precise averages by treatment/player type given.,0,N/R,"Only that red players contribute close to 100% under deterrent punishment, about 25% under non-deterrent.",0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures reported.,0,N/R,Average payoffs not given by treatment.,0,Germany,Same German universities sample.,1,N/R,No aggregate ages.,0,N/R,No gender summary reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Recruitment from undergraduate student population.,1,Online,Same online platform for all treatments.,1,"Structurally identical to 3-Vote-1.5 except MGR=2.5, so cooperation profitable both for the whole group and for the red subgroup. Only red players decide on punishment; middle red proposal applies to red only. Theoretical prediction: deterrent punishment (≥59%) and full contributions by red, zero by blue. Observed median punishment 20, average 41.33; zero punishment 42.11%, deterrent 36.84%, maximum 28.95%. Many groups select non-deterrent punishment despite profitability of deterrence. Contributions: red contribute close to 100% when exposed to deterrent punishment, around 25% with non-deterrent; blue contributions also increase when red impose deterrent punishment, though not to 100%.",Summarizes design and empirical punishment/contribution patterns from Table 2 and narrative.,1
10.1016_j.jebo.2025.107071.md,"3+3-Vote-1.5 (Potentially incomplete punishment institution, MGR=1.5, separate red/blue institutions)",Uses treatment label in Table 1: 3+3-Vote with MGR 1.5.,1,"Punishment institution type: potentially incomplete (3+3-Vote, red and blue subgroups vote separately for their own institution); Marginal group return (MGR)=1.5",Condition differs by allowing both subgroups to create their own formal punishment but with MGR 1.5.,1,True,Human-subject online experiment condition.,1,True,Controlled design with random assignment to treatments.,1,False,Study is conducted online.,1,False,No simulation-only analysis.,1,False,Empirical data collected from participants.,1,6,Each group has 3 red and 3 blue players.,1,24,Common game structure: six 4-round phases.,1,1,Contribution g_i chosen freely from endowment.,1,0,Tokens begin in private account each round.,1,0.25,MGR=1.5 with n=6 ⇒ α=0.25.,1,1,Private experimenter chat allowed in all games.,1,1,"Feedback screen includes subgroup-level contributions, deductions, and payoffs for both subgroups.",1,0,Individual identities obscured via random ordering within subgroups.,1,0,No rewards are implemented.,1,1,Fixed phase structure likely displayed; inferred.,0.6,1,Both subgroups can implement a punishment rate for their own members.,1,0,Punishment enforcement is free of cost by design.,1,1,Subgroup-specific punishment rate p deducts p fraction of each punished member’s un-contributed tokens.,1,0,Treatment involves only punishment institutions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanisms present.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Same 100-token endowment per round with payoff function including α Σ g_j.,1,N/R,Graphs show patterns but not numeric averages per treatment.,0,N/R,"Text only gives qualitative contributions (near 100% under deterrent punishment, low under non-deterrent).",0.3,N/R,No explicit treatment-level efficiency numbers.,0,N/R,Group payoffs by treatment are not reported.,0,Germany,Same German university subject pool.,1,N/R,Age stats not shown.,0,N/R,No gender distribution reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Subjects are undergraduate students recruited from general student population.,1,Online,All treatments implemented online with oTree.,1,"In this potentially incomplete institution, both red and blue subgroups independently propose and implement their own punishment rates; each subgroup’s middle proposal is applied only within that subgroup. MGR=1.5; cooperation profitable at group level but not at subgroup level (marginal return within subgroup 0.75). Theory (weakly dominant strategies) predicts zero punishment and zero contributions by both subgroups. Observed median punishment 7.5, average 37.16; zero punishment 44.58%, deterrent 32.08%, maximum 30.83%. Many subgroups implement positive and sometimes deterrent punishment even though subgroup cooperation is unprofitable. Contributions are very high within subgroups facing deterrent punishment and low otherwise; coordination between subgroups over time often drifts toward both choosing non-deterrent punishment.","Describes structure of two independent punishment institutions, theoretical predictions, and empirical punishment stats from Table 2 and Section 4.4.",1
10.1016_j.jebo.2025.107071.md,"3+3-Vote-2.5 (Potentially incomplete punishment institution, MGR=2.5, separate red/blue institutions)",Label and parameters from Table 1: 3+3-Vote with MGR 2.5.,1,"Punishment institution type: potentially incomplete (3+3-Vote, separate subgroup institutions); Marginal group return (MGR)=2.5",Condition features potentially complete coverage via two subgroup-specific institutions at high MGR.,1,True,Part of the human-subject online experiment.,1,True,Randomized controlled game.,1,False,Conducted via online platform.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical-only simulation.,1,False,Study reports experimental outcomes.,1,6,Three red and three blue players per group.,1,24,"Six phases of four rounds, as elsewhere.",1,1,"Continuous contribution decision g_i∈[0,100].",1,0,All endowment initially in private account.,1,0.4167,MGR=2.5 with n=6 ⇒ α≈0.4167.,1,1,"Private chat with experimenter available, as described in implementation.",1,1,"Round feedback displays subgroup-level contributions, deductions, and payoffs.",1,0,Individuals cannot be tracked over time; only anonymized subgroup info.,1,0,No rewards or reward identities.,1,1,Design with 6 phases would normally be displayed; inferred.,0.6,1,Each subgroup votes each phase on its own punishment rate.,1,0,Enforcement is costless for subgroups.,1,1,"As in other treatments, p proportion of un-contributed tokens is deducted.",1,0,Only punishment institutions are studied.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Same 100-token endowment in the punishment game.,1,N/R,Figures show high contributions under deterrent punishment but no numeric average per treatment.,0,N/R,No exact treatment-level means reported.,0,N/R,Efficiency not quantified per treatment.,0,N/R,Average payoffs by treatment not provided.,0,Germany,Subjects from German universities in Kassel and Magdeburg.,1,N/R,No age statistics reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not described.,0,Undergraduate students,Recruitment explicitly from undergraduate students.,1,Online,All treatments executed online via oTree.,1,"Both red and blue subgroups independently propose punishment rates for their own members; each subgroup’s median proposal implemented each phase. MGR=2.5 makes cooperation profitable at both group and subgroup levels. Weakly dominant prediction: both subgroups implement deterrent punishment (≥59%) and fully contribute, leading to a de facto complete institution. Empirically, median punishment 50, average 52.69; zero punishment 32.92%, deterrent 48.75%, maximum 42.08%. Many subgroups choose non-deterrent punishment despite profitability. Contributions are close to 100% in subgroups facing deterrent punishment and low otherwise; coordination between subgroups over time often remains stuck in miscoordinated or low-punishment states, with about equal chances of coordinating up to deterrence or down to non-deterrence.",Combines the theoretical predictions for potentially incomplete institutions at high MGR with descriptive punishment statistics from Table 2 and coordination dynamics from Fig. 5.,1
