ff matrices.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism is included in the model.,1.0,N/A,No human subjects; agents do not have an interface showing total or remaining rounds.,1.0,1,"Model explicitly includes a punishment action P by cooperators, with cost ρ and fine β in matrices M2 and M3.",1.0,0.3,"Punishment cost parameter is ρ. While ρ is varied, many core analyses (Figs. 6, 8–10, 11, 12) use ρ=0.3, which is a representative configuration.",0.7,0.8,"They specify that punishers impose punishment quantified as β on defectors and later fix β=0.8 in several figures (e.g., Figs. 6–10, 12).",0.9,0,No separate positive reward mechanism for cooperators is modeled; only punishment.,1.0,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1.0,N/A,No reward technology or multiplier is defined.,1.0,N/A,"Prisoner's dilemma with payoffs R,S,T,P; no explicit endowment or token budget per round.",1.0,N/A,"Outcome variable is fraction of cooperators f_c, not contributions from an endowment.",1.0,N/A,No contribution quantities are defined; only binary strategies.,1.0,N/R,They report cooperation frequency f_c and spatial patterns but do not report or compute efficiency as payoff relative to maximum possible.,0.9,N/R,Total or average group payoff is not explicitly tabulated in the results.,0.9,N/A,No human participants; only artificial agents.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,N/A,No human participants.,1.0,No human,"Study is based on Monte Carlo simulations of agents, not human-subject research.",1.0,"Spatial prisoner's dilemma on networks with Q-learning and periodic switching between Fermi imitation and aspiration-driven update rules; weak PDG payoffs R=1, S=P=0, T=b>1; square lattice with von Neumann neighborhood and periodic boundaries; action set for cooperators includes punishment P or ignoring I, leading to transition between base game M1 and punishment games M2 and M3.",Summarizes core structural aspects of the game and update rules directly described in the Mode