10.1002_mde.4492.md,"Treatment 1 – Human, Low opportunity cost",Table 1 defines Treatment 1 as Human × Low opportunity cost.,1,Decision-maker identity: Human; Opportunity cost of unproductive investment: Low (Account 2 multiplier = 1),Design is 2×2: human vs computer (between-subject) and low vs high opportunity cost (within-subject). This row corresponds to human treatment and low-cost block where Account 2 tokens are not multiplied.,1,True,Uses human subjects in a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with randomized treatments and incentivized choices.,1,True,Conducted at METU-FEAS Behavioral and Experimental Laboratory.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation is described.,1,False,"Paper reports an experiment, not a purely analytical model.",1,2,Subjects are randomly paired; payoffs determined within a pair.,1,2,Two blocks; within the human treatment each subject plays both low- and high-cost blocks once.,0.8,0,"First-stage choice is binary (Option A or B), not a continuous contribution decision.",1,0,All 150 tokens are initially under the subject’s control; nothing is pre-assigned to a public or shared account.,0.7,N/R,"This is not a standard linear public good; surplus from Account 2 is a dictator-game pie, so MPCR is not defined.",1,0,Subjects were instructed not to talk; no communication channel is mentioned.,1,0,Subjects receive no feedback about others’ allocation or distribution decisions during the experiment.,1,0,No punishment stage or targeted sanctions are present.,1,0,No explicit rewarding mechanism beyond dictator allocation.,1,1,"Instructions explain there are two blocks and that one block will be randomly chosen for payment, so horizon is known.",0.9,0,"No costly punishment action is available; lower giving is part of baseline dictator decision, not a separate punishment technology.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate costly rewarding stage; transfers are the main outcome of the dictator game.,1,N/A,No distinct reward technology.,1,N/A,No distinct reward technology.,1,150,Each subject has 150 tokens in the first stage.,1,N/R,"Outcomes reported are recognition choice frequencies and dictator giving, not normalized contribution to a public good.",1,N/R,"Paper does not report per-subject token allocations (A vs B) as raw averages, only frequencies of R choice.",0.6,N/R,No aggregate efficiency measure (realized surplus relative to maximum) is reported.,1,N/R,Group-level average payoffs are not provided.,1,Turkey,"Experiment conducted at Middle East Technical University in Ankara, Turkey, using METU undergraduate students.",1,Mean 21.28; individual ages N/R,Paper reports average age 21.28.,1,48% male; 52% female (assuming binary reporting),Paper states 48% male; remaining assumed female as no other categories mentioned.,0.9,Undergraduate university students,Subjects recruited from BEL database consisting of undergraduate students at METU.,1,On site,Sessions took place in the METU-FEAS Behavioral and Experimental Laboratory.,1,"Two-stage, two-player game. Stage 1: each chooses Option A (50 in Account 1, 100 in Account 2) or B (100 in Account 1, 50 in Account 2). In low opportunity cost, Account 2 tokens are not multiplied; joint surplus equals sum in Account 2. Recognition probability to be dictator is own Account 1 tokens divided by total Account 1 in the pair. Stage 2: strategy-method dictator game; each states how much of the joint surplus to give to the other for each possible (own, other) choice profile. One block is randomly selected for payment; dictator within pair is randomly chosen using recognition probabilities; that dictator’s stated transfer is implemented.","Summarizes design details from Experimental Design and instructions, specific to low-cost human treatment.",1
10.1002_mde.4492.md,"Treatment 2 – Human, High opportunity cost",Table 1 defines Treatment 2 as Human × High opportunity cost.,1,Decision-maker identity: Human; Opportunity cost of unproductive investment: High (Account 2 multiplier = 3),High-cost condition multiplies Account 2 tokens by three; human treatment refers to subjects choosing A/B for themselves.,1,True,Same human lab experiment with different block (high cost).,1,True,Controlled manipulation of opportunity cost within subjects.,1,True,Conducted in the same laboratory environment.,1,False,No non-human simulation.,1,False,Not a purely theoretical study.,1,2,Same pairing structure as low-cost block.,1,2,Two blocks; this is the high-cost block for human treatment subjects.,0.8,0,Binary choice between two fixed allocations.,1,0,Tokens are entirely at the subject’s discretion initially.,0.7,N/R,"Game is not a linear public good; surplus generation is via multiplier on Account 2, but no MPCR is reported or standard.",1,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,No feedback about others’ actions during play.,1,0,No punishment stage.,1,0,No separate reward stage.,1,1,Subjects know there are two blocks and one will be paid.,0.9,0,No costly punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism apart from sharing choice.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward technology.,1,150,Each subject has 150 tokens in the first stage in both blocks.,1,N/R,"Outcome measures are recognition choice and giving shares, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,No average token allocations to Account 2 are reported separately for this block.,0.6,N/R,No efficiency index reported for high-cost treatment.,1,N/R,Average pair payoffs are not provided.,1,Turkey,Same subject pool as other blocks.,1,Mean 21.28; individual ages N/R,Same pool; overall average reported only.,1,48% male; 52% female (assuming binary reporting),Same sample description applies.,0.9,Undergraduate university students,Same BEL undergraduate pool.,1,On site,Same lab sessions.,1,"Identical structure to low-cost human block except the productive Account 2 tokens are multiplied by three before forming the surplus. Example: if one chooses A and the other B, Account 2 totals 150 tokens, which are multiplied to 450; dictator is chosen using recognition probabilities based on Account 1 tokens; strategy-method dictator decisions for each (own, other) choice profile determine final allocation in the realized state.",Summarizes modifications specific to high-cost block as described in Section 3 and instructions.,1
10.1002_mde.4492.md,"Treatment 3 – Computer, Low opportunity cost",Table 1 defines Treatment 3 as Computer × Low opportunity cost.,1,Decision-maker identity: Computer; Opportunity cost of unproductive investment: Low (Account 2 multiplier = 1),"In computer treatments, the computer selects Option A or B with 1/2 probability for each subject; this row focuses on the low-cost block.",1,True,Human subjects’ payoffs depend on computer-chosen A/B and their own dictator decisions.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to human vs computer conditions.,1,True,Conducted in METU-FEAS Behavioral and Experimental Laboratory.,1,False,The computer only randomizes choices; it is not a separate simulation study without humans.,1,False,Not purely theoretical.,1,2,Subjects are still paired; each pair’s allocations are determined by computer choices for both members.,1,2,Two blocks per subject; this row corresponds to the low-cost block.,0.8,0,Computer chooses between two discrete options; subjects do not choose amounts.,1,0,"Tokens originate under each subject’s control conceptually; computer assigns them to accounts, but there is no pre-funding of a public account.",0.6,N/R,Not a standard public good; no MPCR given.,1,0,No communication allowed.,1,0,No feedback during experiment about others’ outcomes.,1,0,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No distinct reward mechanism.,1,1,Instructions inform subjects of two blocks and that one is randomly paid.,0.9,0,No explicit punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,Dictator transfers are not modeled as a separate costly reward instrument.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,N/A,No separate reward technology.,1,150,Each notional subject has 150 tokens allocated by the computer between Accounts 1 and 2.,1,N/R,"Paper reports giving percentages and recognition classification, not contributions.",1,N/R,Only probabilities of A/B chosen by computer (1/2) are specified; realized averages are not reported.,0.6,N/R,No efficiency statistics given.,1,N/R,No group payoff numbers given.,1,Turkey,"Same subject pool, same institution.",1,Mean 21.28; individual ages N/R,Overall sample description applies.,1,48% male; 52% female (assuming binary reporting),Sample-level gender share reported; applies to this treatment as part of full sample.,0.9,Undergraduate university students,All subjects recruited from METU undergraduate pool.,1,On site,Same laboratory sessions.,1,"Two-player, two-stage game. In low-cost computer treatment, the computer randomly selects Option A or B with probability 1/2 for each subject. Option A puts 50 tokens into Account 1 and 100 into Account 2; Option B reverses these. Account 2 tokens from both players are summed to determine the joint surplus (no multiplier). Account 1 tokens determine recognition probabilities for being dictator (own A1 / total A1). Stage 2 uses the strategy method: each subject specifies how much of the joint surplus they would give to their partner for all possible computer-chosen (A, B) combinations for themselves and the partner.",Summarizes aspects specific to computer low-cost implementation from instructions.,1
10.1002_mde.4492.md,"Treatment 4 – Computer, High opportunity cost",Table 1 defines Treatment 4 as Computer × High opportunity cost.,1,Decision-maker identity: Computer; Opportunity cost of unproductive investment: High (Account 2 multiplier = 3),Computer chooses A/B; high-cost block multiplies Account 2 by three.,1,True,Human subjects make dictator decisions based on computer-chosen allocations.,1,True,Between-subject manipulation of computer vs human combined with within-subject high/low cost.,1,True,Run in a physical experimental laboratory.,1,False,Computer randomization occurs within an empirical experiment.,1,False,Not a theoretical simulation-only study.,1,2,Subjects are randomly paired into dyads.,1,2,Two blocks; this row covers the high-cost block in the computer treatment.,0.8,0,"Choice is discrete between two options, made by the computer.",1,0,No initial pre-funding of any common account; 150 tokens are conceptually allocated by the computer between two private accounts per subject.,0.6,N/R,"Contest/dictator hybrid, not linear public-good; MPCR not defined.",1,0,Experiment rules prohibit communication.,1,0,No interim feedback about others’ decisions.,1,0,No punishment technology.,1,0,No separate reward technology.,1,1,"Structure (two blocks, one randomly paid) is explained in instructions.",0.9,0,No explicit costly punishment action.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No separate rewarding mechanism besides the dictator allocation.,1,N/A,No distinct reward technology.,1,N/A,No distinct reward technology.,1,150,Each subject notionally has 150 tokens; computer allocates them between accounts.,1,N/R,Study focuses on giving and classification of fairness ideals; does not report contributions as fractions of endowment.,1,N/R,No average Account 2 contributions are reported for this block.,0.6,N/R,No efficiency metric is reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not numerically summarized.,1,Turkey,Same experiment location and sample.,1,Mean 21.28; individual ages N/R,Overall sample description.,1,48% male; 52% female (assuming binary reporting),Same pool description applies.,0.9,Undergraduate university students,Recruited from METU undergraduate database.,1,On site,Sessions conducted in the METU laboratory.,1,"Same structure as low-cost computer treatment except that the total tokens in Account 2 from both players are multiplied by three before determining the joint surplus. Strategy-method dictator decisions are elicited for each hypothetical combination of computer-chosen A/B for self and partner. A random block is selected for payment; within that block, recognition probabilities based on Account 1 tokens determine which subject’s sharing decision is implemented.",Summarizes modifications specific to high-cost computer block based on design and instructions.,1
10.1017_ehs.2024.41.md,Costly Punishment,One of four between-subjects conditions; labelled 'Costly Punishment condition' in Methods 2.4.,1,"Post-contribution option: costly monetary punishment available vs. none vs. public disapproval vs. private disapproval. This condition: costly punishment available (L$1 spent reduces target's earnings by 10%, up to 100%).",Independent variable is the sanction/disapproval option after each round; this condition provides costly punishment.,1,True,Human participants (undergraduate students) played the game in a lab.,1,True,Random assignment in a between-subjects design manipulating punishment/disapproval options.,1,True,Conducted in a campus computer lab with z-tree.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No purely theoretical/analytical model presented as a condition.,1,4,They used 4-player groups in all conditions.,1,40,Participants 'played 40 rounds of public goods games' in all conditions.,1,1,Players could 'contribute any integer amount' from the endowment; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,Each round they were endowed with 20 L$ and then chose how much to contribute; endowment starts in private account.,1,0.4,Total group contributions multiplied by 1.6 and split among 4; MPCR = 1.6 / 4 = 0.4.,1,0,Sessions conducted in silence during the game; no communication allowed.,1,1,"After each round, players were informed of contributions and earnings of all group members.",1,0,Players learned only the aggregate punishment they received; no identification of which member punished.,1,0,No reward mechanism exists in any condition.,1,0,Participants were not told how many rounds they would play.,1,1,This is explicitly the costly punishment condition.,1,1,Every L$1 spent on punishing a player reduces that player's earnings by 10%; so cost per punishment point is 1 L$.,1,0.1,Each L$1 spent reduces target's earnings by 10% of that round's earnings; effectiveness per unit cost is 0.1 (10% per 1 L$).,0.8,0,"No option to increase others' earnings, only reduce them.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in this condition.,1,20,Players were endowed with 20 lab dollars at the beginning of each round.,1,0.6249,Table 1: 'Mean percentage contribution' across all 40 rounds is 62.49%; normalized rate = 0.6249.,1,12.498,Endowment 20 L$. Average rate 0.6249 → 20 * 0.6249 ≈ 12.498 L$ average contribution per round.,0.9,N/R,No explicit ratio of actual earnings to maximum possible earnings reported.,0,N/R,"Only relative earnings and trends are reported, not mean total group payoff in tokens or money.",0,Canada,"Participants were undergraduates at the University of Guelph, Ontario, Canada.",1,"Mean 18.8 years (SD 1.5), undergraduate students",Reported mean age and SD in Participants section.,1,"78 male, 146 female",Sample composition explicitly stated.,1,Undergraduate university students (psychology participant pool),Participants recruited from pool of psychology students at University of Guelph.,1,On site,Conducted in a campus computer lab with participants physically present.,1,"Four-player linear public goods game; each round, each player endowed with 20 L$ and could contribute any integer amount to a public fund. Sum of contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly divided. Groups and player numbers fixed across 40 rounds. After feedback on all players' contributions and earnings, players could assign up to 10 costly punishment points to each of the other 3 members; each 1 L$ spent reduced the target's earnings for that round by 10%, up to 100%. Punishment expenditure deducted from punisher's earnings and constrained by that round's earnings.",Summary of procedural details given in Methods 2.4.,1
10.1017_ehs.2024.41.md,Public Disapproval,Labelled 'Public Disapproval condition' in Methods 2.4.,1,Post-contribution option: costless disapproval ratings communicated to targets vs. other sanction options. This condition: public disapproval ratings (0–10 points per target) communicated to targets and no monetary effect.,Independent variable is the form of sanction/disapproval available. Here: non-costly disapproval that is revealed to others.,1,True,Human participants played the game.,1,True,Condition is part of a randomized between-subjects experimental design.,1,True,Ran in an on-campus computer lab using z-tree.,1,False,No non-human simulation for this condition.,1,False,Not a theoretical/analytical model condition.,1,4,All conditions used 4-player groups.,1,40,All groups played 40 rounds.,1,1,Players could contribute any integer amount from 0 to 20 each round.,1,0,"20 L$ endowment each round given privately, then contribution chosen.",1,0.4,Same public goods technology in all conditions: multiplier 1.6 divided by 4 players.,1,0,Participants required to remain silent during the game; no chat or discussion.,1,1,After each round players saw contributions and earnings of all group members.,1,0,"Targets learned aggregate disapproval points from others, not which player assigned which points.",1,0,No reward option exists.,1,0,Participants were not told total number of rounds.,1,1,"Though non-monetary, disapproval is the sanctioning mechanism; coded as punishmentExists since it is the only negative sanction.",0.7,0,Disapproval points had no effect on earnings and did not cost the sender any money.,1,0,"Disapproval points did not change payoffs, so monetary impact per point is zero.",1,0,No mechanism to increase others' payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Endowment 20 L$ per round in all conditions.,1,0.3762,Table 1: Mean percentage contribution across all 40 rounds is 37.62%; normalized to 0.3762.,1,7.524,Endowment 20 L$ × 0.3762 average rate ≈ 7.524 L$ per round.,0.9,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported.,0,N/R,Only trends and relative earnings are described; no mean total payoffs by condition over all rounds.,0,Canada,"Same participant pool for all conditions (University of Guelph, Canada).",1,"Mean 18.8 years (SD 1.5), undergraduate students",Sample-level demographics apply equally to this condition.,1,"78 male, 146 female (across all conditions)",Gender breakdown only given for full sample; not broken down by condition.,0.7,Undergraduate university students (psychology participant pool),All participants drawn from same undergraduate pool.,1,On site,Sessions held in a computer lab on campus.,1,"Same 4-player linear public goods game as other conditions: 20 L$ endowment each round, contribute any integer amount, total contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly divided. Fixed groups and player numbers. After observing all contributions and earnings, each player could assign 0–10 'dissatisfaction/disapproval' points to each of the other 3 group members. Aggregate points received were shown to each player but had no effect on any earnings and were costless to assign.",Condensed description of this condition's procedure from Methods 2.4.,1
10.1017_ehs.2024.41.md,Private Disapproval,"Referred to as 'first control condition, which we call Private Disapproval' in Methods 2.4.",1,"Post-contribution option: private, uncommunicated disapproval ratings vs. other sanction options. This condition: private disapproval ratings (0–10 per target) not shown to others and with no monetary effect.",Independent variable is type/visibility of sanction. Here: disapproval is recorded but never communicated nor payoff relevant.,1,True,Human subjects participated.,1,True,"Condition in a controlled, between-subjects experimental design.",1,True,Performed in a campus computer lab.,1,False,No simulation model for this condition.,1,False,Not an analytical model.,1,4,Groups of four in all conditions.,1,40,Participants played 40 rounds.,1,1,Any integer amount of the 20 L$ endowment could be contributed.,1,0,All 20 L$ start in the private account; contributions are chosen.,1,0.4,Same 1.6 multiplier across 4 players.,1,0,No communication allowed during the game.,1,1,Contributions and earnings of all group members revealed after each round.,1,0,"No one, including targets, ever sees the disapproval ratings, so identities are not revealed.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Number of rounds not disclosed to participants.,1,0,Disapproval ratings have no effect on others’ payoffs or information; authors state they 'could not influence anyone's behaviour'. Treated as absence of an operative punishment mechanism.,0.8,N/A,No operative punishment mechanism (disapproval is neither seen nor payoff-relevant).,0.9,N/A,No operative punishment mechanism.,0.9,0,No option to positively adjust others’ earnings.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,20 L$ each round for each player in all conditions.,1,0.5787,Table 1: Mean percentage contribution over all 40 rounds is 57.87%; normalized to 0.5787.,1,11.574,Endowment 20 L$ × 0.5787 ≈ 11.574 L$ average contribution.,0.9,N/R,Efficiency not explicitly reported.,0,N/R,Mean group payoffs not provided in absolute terms by condition across all rounds.,0,Canada,Same Canadian undergraduate pool.,1,"Mean 18.8 years (SD 1.5), undergraduate students",Applies across all conditions.,1,"78 male, 146 female (across all conditions)",No condition-specific gender breakdown.,0.7,Undergraduate university students (psychology participant pool),Same recruitment method.,1,On site,Conducted in a university computer lab.,1,"Identical 4-player repeated public goods structure (20 L$ endowment, linear MPCR 0.4, 40 rounds, fixed groups). After seeing all players’ contributions and earnings, each participant privately rated their disapproval of each of the other 3 players (0–10 points). These ratings were stored but explicitly not shared with any players and had no effect on anyone’s earnings.",Summarizes the condition-specific post-round procedure.,1
10.1017_ehs.2024.41.md,Nothing (Baseline),"Described as 'second control condition, Nothing' and 'baseline public goods game' in Methods 2.4.",1,Post-contribution option: no punishment or disapproval stage (baseline) vs. disapproval vs. costly punishment. This condition: no additional stage; proceed directly to next round.,"Independent variable is availability of sanction/disapproval; here, none.",1,True,Uses human participants.,1,True,One arm of a randomized lab experiment.,1,True,"Run in a lab with computers, like other conditions.",1,False,No simulation here.,1,False,Not an analytical condition.,1,4,All groups are of size 4.,1,40,40 rounds of the public goods game.,1,1,Contributions can be any integer amount from 0 to 20.,1,0,Endowment given privately and then allocated voluntarily.,1,0.4,Multiplier 1.6 divided among 4 players.,1,0,Silence required during the game.,1,1,"After each round, contributions and earnings of all group members shown to participants.",1,0,No punishment or disapproval exists; identity revelation irrelevant.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,0,Participants were not informed how many rounds they would play.,1,0,No opportunity for punishment or disapproval; players simply proceed to next round.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No mechanism to reward others.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,"20 L$ per round per player, same as other conditions.",1,0.3532,Table 1: Mean percentage contribution across 40 rounds is 35.32%; normalized to 0.3532.,1,7.064,Endowment 20 L$ × 0.3532 ≈ 7.064 L$ average contribution.,0.9,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,0,N/R,"While some earnings comparisons are described, no overall mean group payoff is reported for the entire 40 rounds per condition.",0,Canada,"Participants were students at University of Guelph, Canada.",1,"Mean 18.8 years (SD 1.5), undergraduate students",Applies to full sample that includes this condition.,1,"78 male, 146 female (across all conditions)",Reported only at sample level.,0.7,Undergraduate university students (psychology participant pool),Same recruitment method as other conditions.,1,On site,Participants attended computer lab sessions on campus.,1,"Standard 4-player linear public goods game played for 40 rounds with fixed groups and identities anonymized via player numbers. Each round: 20 L$ endowment, any integer contribution to group fund, total contributions multiplied by 1.6 and evenly divided. After each round, all players see every player's contribution and earnings. No subsequent punishment, disapproval, or additional decision stage; game proceeds directly to next contribution round.",Description based on Methods 2.4 for 'Nothing' condition and shared PGG structure.,1
