10.3233_jifs-232322.md,"Monte Carlo simulations – static punishment mechanism (heterogeneous social preferences, scale-free network)",Second of three simulations in Section 3.3 introduces static punishment with costs M1 and M2 for heterogeneous preferences on a scale-free network.,0.95,Heterogeneous social preferences (25% each type); static punishment mechanism with cooperator cost M1=1 and defector fine M2=re; enhancement factor re varied; scale-free network,Static punishment definition from Section 2.3 and parameter choices from Table 1 and Section 3.3.,0.95,False,Purely simulated.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Monte Carlo computational model with static punishment.,1,False,"Static punishment payoff equations are defined, but results are via simulation.",1,100,N=100 default; not changed.,0.7,100000,Assumed same T=10^5 simulation horizon and averaging protocol for punishment comparison.,0.8,1,"Agents either cooperate or defect; punishment is an outcome of C punishing D, not an independent contribution decision.",0.9,N/A,No human endowment setting.,1,N/R,Public good payoff multiplier per capita re/(k+1) depends on degree and re; no fixed MPCR reported.,0.4,N/A,No modeled communication.,1,0,Agents only observe own and neighbor utilities.,0.8,0,Model includes punishment as payoff adjustments but does not include identity revelation or reputational components.,0.8,N/A,No rewards exist.,1,0,Horizon not an information variable.,0.8,1,Static punishment with fixed M1 and M2 is applied.,1,1,Static punishment: cooperators spend fixed cost M1=1 when punishing.,1,1,"Defectors pay fixed fine M2; per unit cooperator cost of 1 induces a defector fine of M2= re. For a 'unit' of punishment costing 1, fine is re; but since schema asks for magnitude per coin, treat ratio as M2/M1 = re; however re is variable, so we approximate tech as N/R. To avoid misrepresentation, set to 1 and explain it is not a standard per-unit tech.",0.2,0,No reward scheme modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Same PGG with c=1.,0.7,N/R,Figure 8 compares cooperation under static vs dynamic vs no punishment as function of re; numerical data not given.,0.5,N/R,No absolute contributions.,0.3,N/R,"No explicit efficiency metrics, though authors discuss resolving second-order dilemma qualitatively.",0.2,N/R,Group payoffs not numerically reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results from computational model.,1,"Static punishment mechanism: if C punishes D, cooperator’s payoff reduced by M1, defector’s by M2; here M1=1, M2=re increasing with enhancement factor. Used alongside dynamic punishment to assess which better addresses second-order free riding; cooperation under static punishment is higher than without punishment but lower than with dynamic punishment.",Combines payoff definition from Section 2.3 with parameter setting in Table 1 and discussion in Section 3.3.,0.9
10.3233_jifs-232322.md,"Monte Carlo simulations – dynamic punishment mechanism (heterogeneous social preferences, scale-free network)",Third simulation in Section 3.3 applies the dynamic punishment mechanism where punishment cost and fine depend on fraction of defectors.,0.95,"Heterogeneous social preferences (25% each type); dynamic punishment mechanism with cooperator cost [φ^D(t) M1 + m1] and defector fine [φ^D(t) M2 + m2], where M1=1, M2=re, m1=M1/2, m2=M2/2; enhancement factor re varied; scale-free network",Dynamic punishment formulation and parameterization are described in Sections 2.3 and 3.3.,0.95,False,Simulation only.,1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,Not a lab or field environment.,1,True,Monte Carlo simulations with dynamic punishment.,1,False,Dynamic punishment equations are defined but evaluated numerically.,1,100,Default N=100 applied here.,0.7,100000,Assumed same T=10^5 as other Section 3 simulations.,0.8,1,Players still only choose C or D; punishment is conditional on these choices.,0.9,N/A,No human endowment process.,1,N/R,"MPCR is re/(k+1), varying with degrees and re.",0.4,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,0,Agents observe only local information.,0.8,0,"Punishment affects payoffs, not identities.",0.8,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,0,Total rounds not an input to agents.,0.8,1,Dynamic punishment mechanism explicitly present.,1,1,Base punishment parameter M1=1; dynamic cost per cooperator is φ^D M1 + m1; we report base unit cost as 1.,0.8,1,"Base fine parameter M2=re; defector pays φ^D M2 + m2. Per unit of 'base cost' the fine scales with re and φ^D, so no fixed tech; default 1 reported with low confidence due to schema constraints.",0.2,0,No reward mechanism introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,1,Same contribution cost c=1.,0.7,N/R,"Fig. 8 shows dynamic punishment yields highest cooperation vs re, but numeric values are not given.",0.5,N/R,Absolute contributions not reported.,0.3,N/R,"No quantitative efficiency measure, though authors claim dynamic punishment more effectively resolves second-order dilemma.",0.2,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not provided.,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulations in Python.,1,"Dynamic punishment: payoff of D is Px = Σ p_{x,i} − [φ^D(t) M2 + m2]; payoff of C is Px = Σ p_{x,i} − [φ^D(t) M1 + m1]; integral constraints ensure same total punishment burden as static mechanism, giving m1=M1/2, m2=M2/2. Simulation results show dynamic punishment increases cooperation more than static punishment and mitigates second-order free riding.",Combines dynamic punishment equation from Section 2.3 with parameter setting and qualitative findings from Section 3.3.,0.95
