proportion.",0.2,5.6,Reported mean investment in own-money-punishment condition: mean = 5.60.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency index reported.,0.1,N/R,Group payoff values by condition not reported.,0.1,Germany,Same participant pool as other blocks.,0.9,"Mean 27.57 years (SD = 9.33), all at least 18 years old.",Single sample across all blocks.,1,79.44% female (85 of 107); remaining approximately 20.56% other genders (not further specified).,Same sample used for all four blocks.,0.9,Primarily university students; recruited through local advertisements and could receive course credit.,Same recruitment description applies.,0.6,On site,Laboratory setting; identical to other blocks.,1,"Same 4-player repeated one-shot public goods game with 50 trials. After seeing contributions and payouts, participants can assign punishment 0–10 credits (integers) to each of the 3 other players using only their own current-trial earnings as source. If attempted punishment exceeds available own credits, they must reduce it. Punishment reduces both the punisher’s credits and the punished players’ credits by the same total amount. No option to punish from the pool or to choose source.",Summarizes features specific to own-money-punishment block from Paradigm and Calculation of the credits sections.,1
10.1038_s41598-024-71106-x.md,Punishment-pool block (pool-money-punishment condition),Described as condition where punishment credits are taken from the common pool.,1,Punishment availability and source: punishment only from common pool (pool-money-punishment option); investments of fictitious others varied across 10 fairness combinations.,Condition differs by enabling punishment funded solely from common pool while keeping investment patterns of others as systematic variation.,1,True,Human participants played this condition.,1,True,Punishment source is an experimental manipulation.,1,True,Implemented in laboratory environment.,1,False,Human data collected; fictitious opponents controlled but not main method.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model as the primary contribution.,1,4,Same 1 participant + 3 fictitious players structure.,1,50,Each block consisted of 50 trials.,1,1,Participants selected any integer investment 0–20.,1,0,20-credit endowment initially private.,1,0.1,Same linear public goods production function across blocks.,1,0,No facility for communication described.,1,1,Participants see others' investments and final credits.,1,0,No player identities beyond anonymous slots.,0.8,0,No rewards implemented.,1,0,Paper does not mention showing number of rounds.,0.4,1,This block allows punishment funded by the pool.,1,0,"For the punisher there is effectively no cost: punishment credits taken from pool, and 'calculation of the participant's credits was done as if no punishment was taken from there'.",0.9,1,Credits for punishment are taken from the pool and reduce punished players’ credits by the amount spent; standard 1:1 deduction implied though not spelled out numerically.,0.7,0,No rewarding behavior described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,Same per-trial endowment as other blocks.,1,N/R,Only mean contribution amount reported.,0.2,7.24,Reported mean investment where punishment was given from the common pool: mean = 7.24.,1,N/R,No reported efficiency measures per block.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate payoffs not given.,0.1,Germany,Same sample and setting.,0.9,"Mean 27.57 years (SD = 9.33), all at least 18 years old.",Single pool across all conditions.,1,79.44% female (85 of 107); remaining approximately 20.56% other genders (not further specified).,Same sample statistics.,0.9,Primarily university students; recruited through local advertisements and could receive course credit.,No condition-specific differences reported.,0.6,On site,Same laboratory environment.,1,"After observing contributions and redistributions, participants can assign 0–10 credits of punishment to each of the 3 other players. Punishment credits are taken from the common pool; participants’ own payoff is computed as if no pool punishment occurred, so punishment is costless to the punisher but reduces the public good for other players. If attempted punishment exceeds available pool credits, participants must reduce it.",Condenses block-specific punishment rules from Paradigm and Calculation of the credits.,1
10.1038_s41598-024-71106-x.md,Punishment-choice block (choice of punishment source condition),Described as condition where participants could choose between punishing from own money or from the pool.,1,Punishment availability and source: punishment available with choice between own-money-punishment and pool-money-punishment each trial; investments of fictitious others varied across 10 fairness combinations.,Key manipulated factor is the availability of both punishment sources and free choice between them.,1,True,Human participants played the game in this condition.,1,True,Punishment-choice vs single-source vs no-punishment blocks are experimental manipulations.,1,True,All blocks conducted in the lab.,1,False,Not a pure simulation; involves human behavioral data.,1,False,No purely analytical model as main method.,1,4,One real participant + three fictitious opponents.,1,50,Each experimental block has 50 trials.,1,1,"Investment decision between 0 and 20 credits, integer.",1,0,Endowment starts fully private.,1,0.1,Same payoff function.,1,0,No communication mentioned.,1,1,Feedback includes investments and final credits for each player.,1,0,Targets represented only as anonymous players without identifiers.,0.8,0,No reward option exists.,1,0,No indication that remaining number of trials was displayed.,0.4,1,Participants could punish and choose source.,1,N/R,"Cost per unit differs by chosen source (own-money vs pool); exact per-unit mapping for each not numerically specified beyond qualitative description, and mixture makes a single numeric value ill-defined.",0.3,N/R,Effect per credit differs between own-money and pool options and is not fully quantified in this block's mixture context.,0.3,0,"Design includes only punishment, not reward.",1,N/A,No reward in the game.,1,N/A,No reward in the game.,1,20,Same per-trial endowment as other conditions.,1,N/R,"Only mean amount, not normalized rate, is given.",0.2,7.381,Reported mean investment in punishment-choice block: 7.381 credits.,1,N/R,No efficiency statistic provided.,0.1,N/R,No reported group payoff per condition.,0.1,Germany,Same German university sample.,0.9,"Mean 27.57 years (SD = 9.33), all at least 18 years old.",Same sample as other blocks.,1,79.44% female (85 of 107); remaining approximately 20.56% other genders (not further specified).,Same participant pool.,0.9,Primarily university students; recruited through local advertisements and could receive course credit.,Same recruitment description; no block-specific differences.,0.6,On site,All tasks performed in the lab.,1,"After investment, contributions and final payouts are shown. Then participants choose punishment source via mouse click between 'self' and 'pool'. After source choice, they can allocate 0–10 punishment credits to each of the 3 other players, subject to constraints of the chosen source (own earnings limit vs pool credits). If they allocate more than available from chosen source, they must reduce punishments. Mouse trajectories of the source choice are recorded and used to compute area under the curve as an uncertainty measure. In this block, 72% of punishment (on average) was from pool.","Summarizes features specific to punishment-choice block, including mouse-tracking and observed pool-choice percentage.",1
10.1088_1674-1056_ad5aee.md,Pool punishment without fine distribution (baseline spatial PGG model),Paper repeatedly contrasts 'the pool punishment model without distribution of fines' with the new model; treat this as one condition in the parameter sweeps.,1,"Punishment cost β, fine amount, synergy factor r (and implicitly comparison to presence/absence of fine redistribution; here: no redistribution, α not used). Example focal settings: r=2.0, fine=0.95, c=1 with β varied; heatmaps vary (β, fine) for r from 2.0 to 3.0.","From Fig. 1 (vary β at r=2, fine=0.95, α=0.5, c=1); Fig. 6 (fraction of C+PC as function of (β, fine) for r=2.0–3.0). This condition is characterized by the absence of fine distribution.",0.88,False,Study uses Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory with human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Authors explicitly state they employ Monte Carlo simulation on a square lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical treatment is developed; results are numerical.,0.95,10000,"Square lattice size L=100, so number of players/sites is L^2 = 10,000.",1,10000,They state 'all the simulation results require at most 10^4 iterations'; treat this as the number of Monte Carlo steps (rounds) per run.,0.85,1,"Each cooperator (including punisher) pays a fixed cost c=1 rather than choosing a continuous contribution; effectively a binary contribute-or-not choice. In this schema, 0 would indicate only all-or-nothing contribution. However, since there is no explicit contribution level choice beyond this binary strategy, but the field is defined as 1 if players can contribute any amount, 0 otherwise. Here they cannot vary amount, so use 0.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects and no notion of an initial 'default' endowment allocation; contributions are modeled as fixed costs per cooperator.,1,0.4,"MPCR = r/N where r is the synergy factor and N is group size. They use r=2.0 and each group is the focal plus its 4 neighbors, so N=5. Thus MPCR = 2/5 = 0.4.",0.95,N/A,No human interaction; communication not modeled.,1,0,Simulation model has no interface displaying summaries; only payoff-based imitation via Fermi rule.,1,N/A,No player identities or feedback interface; not a human experiment.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards apart from payoff updates; no identity revelation.,1,0,Agents do not observe a round counter; time horizon not part of decision rule.,0.9,1,Pool punishment model: defectors pay fines when punishers are present and punishers pay punishment cost β.,1,0.15,For point a1/a2 they highlight β=0.15 as one focal parameter where cooperation is high in this baseline model; treat that setting as a representative condition.,0.8,0.95,"Each defector in groups with punishers pays 'fine', which is set to 0.95 in the main comparative figures; this is the per-defector fine magnitude caused by the existence of punishers.",0.8,0,No separate rewarding mechanism; only punishment and redistributed fines in the other condition.,1,N/A,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism defined.,1,N/R,They describe a 'cooperation cost c=1' but do not specify an initial per-round endowment in tokens; only cost and fines.,0.3,1,"In a public goods interpretation with binary strategies, all non-defectors (C and PC) contribute c each time; for a given cooperator or punisher, contribution equals the full modeled cooperation cost, so normalized contribution of contributors is 1. The focal macro DV they report is the fraction of cooperative strategies (C+PC).",0.6,1,Each cooperator or punisher pays cooperation cost c=1 when they cooperate.,1,N/R,They report total system payoff evolution curves (Fig. 4) but do not normalize by the theoretical maximum group payoff.,0.4,N/R,Total system payoff is plotted qualitatively; exact numeric steady-state values are not provided.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is a Monte Carlo simulation on a square lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a square lattice (L=100) with periodic boundaries; each player belongs to 5 overlapping groups (self + 4 neighbors). Strategies: cooperators (C) contribute cost c=1; defectors (D) contribute nothing and pay a fine when punishers present; punishers (PC) contribute c and pay punishment cost β. Payoffs: π_C = r(N_C+N_PC)c/N − c, π_D = r(N_C+N_PC)c/N − fine·H(N_PC), π_PC = r(N_C+N_PC)c/N − c − β. Strategy update via Fermi rule with noise k=0.5, synchronous Monte Carlo updates, random initial strategies with equal probability.","Compiled from the model description in Section 2; for this baseline condition, the fine redistribution terms (α) are removed so fines and punishment costs simply leave the system as in standard pool punishment.",0.9
10.1088_1674-1056_ad5aee.md,"Pool punishment with fine distribution (α = 0.5, β = 0.15)","Condition explicitly named as 'the pool punishment model with distribution of fines'; one focal setting is β=0.15, α=0.5, r=2.0, fine=0.95, c=1 (point a2).",1,Presence of fine redistribution and distribution coefficient α = 0.5; punishment cost β = 0.15; synergy factor r = 2.0; fine = 0.95; c = 1.,"From Fig. 1 and associated text describing points a1, a2 at β=0.15 with r=2.0, fine=0.95, α=0.5, c=1.",0.95,False,Lattice-based Monte Carlo simulation with no human participants.,1,False,"Computational simulation, not a controlled human experiment.",1,False,No physical or online laboratory with human subjects.,1,True,Authors explicitly use Monte Carlo simulation to update strategies.,1,False,No closed-form derivations are central to the results; focus is numerical.,0.95,10000,"Square lattice size L=100 ⇒ 100×100 = 10,000 players.",1,10000,They state that results 'require at most 10^4 iterations'; treat this as run length.,0.85,0,"Players choose a discrete strategy (C, D, or PC). Contribution is fixed at c=1 for C and PC and 0 for D; they cannot choose partial contributions.",0.95,N/A,No human endowments or starting allocations; contributions are model parameters.,1,0.4,"MPCR = r/N with r=2.0 and group size N=5 (self + 4 neighbors), so 2/5 = 0.4.",0.95,N/A,No communication channel in a non-human simulation.,1,0,"Agents only use local payoff comparisons, no display of systemwide summaries.",1,N/A,No identities or UI; purely algorithmic state updates.,1,N/A,Redistributed fines enter payoffs but there is no identity revelation mechanism.,1,0,Agents do not condition on the remaining number of iterations.,0.9,1,"Defectors incur fines when punishers are present, and punishers bear a punishment cost β.",1,0.15,Point a2 uses β=0.15 as described around Fig. 2(a2) and Fig. 4(a).,1,0.95,"Fine parameter is set to 0.95, representing the size of the fine imposed on each defector when punishers are present.",0.9,1,The model introduces redistribution of fines as payoffs (rewards) to cooperators and punishers; this functions as a reward-like mechanism funded by fines.,0.9,0,"Redistributed fines are not paid for by the recipients; rewards come from collected fines, so there is no additional cost per reward unit.",0.7,0.5,"Distribution coefficient α=0.5 determines that half of total fines go to cooperators and (1−α or all, depending on presence of cooperators) to punishers. Per-recipient payoff depends on counts, but α is the key reward technology parameter; encode this as 0.5.",0.7,N/R,"Only cooperation cost c=1, fines, and punishment cost β are specified; no explicit per-round endowment reported.",0.3,N/R,"They report cooperation level as fraction of cooperative strategies (C+PC), not normalized per-agent contribution fraction relative to a specified endowment.",0.4,1,Cooperators and punishers each pay cooperation cost c=1 when participating.,1,N/R,"Overall system payoff evolution is plotted (Fig. 4), but no explicit normalization against maximal possible payoff is given.",0.4,N/R,Total system payoff is discussed qualitatively; no numeric steady-state value reported.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice.,1,"Spatial public goods game on a periodic L=100 square lattice. Each player is initially C, D, or PC with equal probability and participates in 5 overlapping groups centered on self and four nearest neighbors. With fine distribution, payoffs are: π_C = r(N_C+N_PC)c/N − c + [α·N_D·fine·H(N_PC)]/N_C; π_D = r(N_C+N_PC)c/N − H(N_PC)·fine; π_PC = r(N_C+N_PC)c/N − c − β + [(1 − α·H(N_C))·N_D·fine]/N_PC. Here c=1, r=2.0, fine=0.95, β=0.15, α=0.5, N=5, and H is the Heaviside function. Strategies update synchronously via the Fermi rule W = 1/(1 + exp[(P_x − P_y)/k]) with k=0.5, using payoffs accumulated over the 5 groups. Simulations run up to 10^4 iterations and averages over 10 independent runs.","Direct transcription and parameterization of the model section, using the specific parameters for the a2 setting.",1
10.1088_1674-1056_ad5aee.md,"Pool punishment with fine distribution (α = 0.5, β = 0.40)","Condition corresponding to point b2: 'the pool punishment model with distribution of fines' at punishment cost β=0.40 with r=2.0, fine=0.95, α=0.5, c=1.",1,Presence of fine redistribution with distribution coefficient α = 0.5; punishment cost β = 0.40; synergy factor r = 2.0; fine = 0.95; c = 1.,"Described around Fig. 1, Fig. 2(b2), Fig. 3(b2), and Fig. 4(b) as the high-punishment-cost case where fine distribution sustains cooperation.",0.95,False,Monte Carlo simulation without human participants.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment; numerical simulation.,1,False,No laboratory setting with human subjects.,1,True,Authors explicitly describe using Monte Carlo simulation on a lattice.,1,False,"Results are obtained via simulation, not analytic solution.",0.95,10000,"Lattice size L=100 ⇒ 10,000 players.",1,10000,They use at most 10^4 iterations per simulation.,0.85,0,"Contribution choice is binary via strategy (C/PC contribute c, D contributes 0).",0.95,N/A,No human endowment allocation concept.,1,0.4,Same group structure and r as other main-case simulations: MPCR = 2.0/5 = 0.4.,0.9,N/A,No communication among simulated agents.,1,0,Agents have only local payoff comparisons for updating strategies.,1,N/A,No identities shown in the simulation.,1,N/A,Redistributed fines do not come with identity information.,1,0,Agents do not observe horizon length.,0.9,1,Defectors pay fines when punishers exist; punishers pay cost β.,1,0.4,Point b2 uses β=0.40 as high punishment cost case.,1,0.95,Fine parameter 'fine' remains 0.95.,0.9,1,Fine redistribution mechanism rewards cooperators and punishers.,0.9,0,"Rewards are financed entirely by fines, not additional costs.",0.7,0.5,"Distribution coefficient α=0.5 splits fine between cooperators and punishers as in Eq. (π_C, π_PC).",0.7,N/R,"Only costs c, fine, and β specified; no initial per-round resources.",0.3,N/R,"Reported DV is cooperation level (C+PC) and overall payoff, not normalized contribution fraction.",0.4,1,Cooperators and punishers each contribute c=1.,1,N/R,Efficiency relative to maximum possible payoff not numerically reported.,0.4,N/R,Total system payoff plotted qualitatively (Fig. 4b) without explicit numeric values.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects involved.,1,No human,Numerical simulation only.,1,"Same spatial PGG with fine redistribution as in the α=0.5, β=0.15 case, but with higher punishment cost β=0.40. Parameters: L=100; r=2.0; c=1; fine=0.95; α=0.5; β=0.40; group size N=5; three strategies C, D, PC with equal initial probability; synchronous Monte Carlo updates; Fermi imitation rule with k=0.5; up to 10^4 iterations averaged over 10 runs. At this high β, without redistribution cooperation collapses, whereas with redistribution a mixed C-D-PC equilibrium with substantial cooperation persists.",Directly based on model description and figure discussions for point b2.,1
10.1088_1674-1056_ad5aee.md,"Pool punishment with fine distribution – α variation (r = 2.0, β = 0.15, fine = 0.95)","Fig. 7 reports strategy fraction evolution for different values of α at fixed r=2.0, c=1, fine=0.95, β=0.15; each α value is a condition, here summarized as the α-variation treatment.",0.9,"Distribution coefficient α varied (multiple values; not all exact numbers reported) at fixed r = 2.0, β = 0.15, fine = 0.95, c = 1 in the fine-distribution model.","Text accompanying Fig. 7: 'In order to investigate the effect of α on the evolution of cooperation, we plot Fig. 7. Figure 7 shows that as α increases, the fraction of defectors tends to grow.'",0.85,False,Again a Monte Carlo simulation.,1,False,Not observational or human-subject; purely numerical.,1,False,No lab with human participants.,1,True,Parameter sweep over α in the computational model.,1,False,Results shown as simulated strategy trajectories.,0.95,10000,Same lattice size L=100 applies throughout.,1,10000,Same simulation protocol (up to 10^4 iterations).,0.85,0,"Binary strategy-based contribution (C/PC contribute, D does not).",0.95,N/A,No human endowment decision.,1,0.4,"r=2.0, N=5 as elsewhere ⇒ MPCR=0.4.",0.9,N/A,No communication of agents.,1,0,Only local payoff comparisons guide updates.,1,N/A,No identity mechanism.,1,N/A,Rewards are modeled as anonymous payoff increments.,1,0,Horizon not exposed to agents.,0.9,1,Model still uses pool punishment with cost β and fines.,1,0.15,"Fig. 7 caption: r=2.0, c=1, fine=0.95, β=0.15.",1,0.95,Fine parameter remains 0.95.,0.9,1,Redistribution of fines (parameter α) is the studied mechanism.,1,0,Rewards from redistributed fines have no additional cost to recipients.,0.7,N/R,Exact numeric values of α for each condition are not reported in the text; only that α is varied and higher α increases defectors.,0.4,N/R,"As before, initial endowment is not specified.",0.3,N/R,"They present strategy fractions (C, D, PC) over time for different α, not normalized contribution metrics.",0.4,1,Contribution per cooperator/punisher remains c=1.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure in α-variation figure.,0.3,N/R,Total payoff vs α is not shown; only strategies' fractions.,0.3,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Purely computational simulation.,1,"Same spatial PGG with fine redistribution as in earlier conditions, but α is varied to shift how much of the fine revenue goes to cooperators vs punishers. Parameters fixed: L=100, r=2.0, c=1, fine=0.95, β=0.15, group size N=5, noise k=0.5, synchronous updates, up to 10^4 iterations. Fig. 7 shows that higher α (more fines to cooperators, less to punishers) increases the fraction of defectors and lowers cooperation; thus to increase cooperation, more fines should be distributed to punishers (smaller α).",Summarizes the α-variation setup as described in the caption and discussion of Fig. 7.,0.95
