10.1016_j.worlddev.2024.106686.md,Peer punishment – Equal capacity – Brazil,Peer punishment stage for equal-capacity groups in Brazilian site.,0.95,Country: Brazil; Endowment inequality: Equal (all participants max 6 plots); Punishment: Peer punishment enabled; Monitoring: Perfect individual monitoring.,Peer punishment treatment defined as one of later stages; inequality treatment is between sessions.,1,True,Human participants in framed field experiment.,1,True,Controlled treatment where punishment technology added.,1,False,Conducted on-site in villages.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Not analytical only.,1,6,Six-person groups.,1,6,Each stage has six rounds; peer punishment is a stage.,1,1,Choice of 0..6 plots deforested per round.,0.9,0,No automatic allocation to public fund; cooperation by forgoing extraction.,0.8,0.4,Same δ in all stages.,1,0,Communication prohibited throughout experiment.,1,1,Subjects must see others’ deforestation levels to target punishment; monitoring is perfect and individual.,0.95,0,"Anonymity maintained via letters changed each stage; punishees do not know identity, only letter; identities not revealed in a way tying to real person.",0.8,0,No rewards.,1,1,Likely informed about six rounds per stage; they use round dummies for learning.,0.6,1,Peer punishment stage explicitly defined.,1,2,"Payoff function: π_it = ... − k g_it − r_it 3k. Punishment tech conveys 3k loss per punishment; each punishment yields 30-point loss according to Table 4 and related text (marginal loss from increasing expected punishment * 30 points). Thus 3k=30 ⇒ k=10. Cost to punisher per punishment is k=10 points; each forest plot yields 10 points, so report cost 10. However config requires coins-per-unit; in terms of payoff points this is 10. Since basic defection gain is 6 points (10−4), more natural calibration is that one punishment removes 30 points from punished such that marginal benefit of defection is 6 per unit; this implies 3k=30, k=10. Therefore punishment cost is 10 points per point assigned and punishment technology is 30-point loss per punishment.",0.6,30,Receiving one punishment reduces payoff by 3k; they repeatedly multiply expected punishment increase by 30 points in Table 4; so per punishment effect is 30 points.,0.8,0,"Only punishment, no positive rewards described.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,6,Equal groups: capacity 6 plots per round.,1,N/R,Average conservation rate in peer punishment stage by condition not numerically reported.,0,N/R,Exact mean deforestation or conservation not reported per condition.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency levels per condition.,0,N/R,Total payoffs in peer punishment stage not provided per condition.,0,Brazil,Condition for Brazilian sessions.,1,Mean 44 years overall; country-stage specific age N/R,Only overall average given.,0.6,52% men overall; breakdown by condition N/R,No more detailed stats.,0.6,N/R,Not described.,0,Field experiment,Framed field experiment with local forest users.,1,"Same CPR structure as baseline, with added peer punishment: after observing others’ deforestation under perfect individual monitoring, each participant can assign punishment points to any peer. Payoff: π_it = x_it + δ(S − x_it − Σx_{−it}) − k g_it − r_it·3k. Letters for IDs changed each stage to preserve anonymity.",Direct description of peer punishment stage.,1
