10.1016_j.hydroa.2024.100188.md,"Base case – with institutions (IR=0.5, DP=0.15)","Section 4.1 describes a base case with institutions active, with likelihood to innovate IR_i=0.5 and likelihood to defect DP_i=0.15.",1,"Institution mechanism active; likelihood to innovate IR_i=0.5; likelihood to defect DP_i=0.15; 100 agents; voting-based institution determining allowable salt application ratio, monitoring intensity, and fines.",Independent factors defining this scenario are presence of institutions and parameter settings IR_i and DP_i as explicitly stated for the base case with institutions.,1,False,Study is an agent-based computer simulation with property owners as simulated agents and no human subjects.,1,False,No controlled human or field experiment; only in silico simulations.,1,False,No lab or in-person experimental setting; purely computational model.,1,True,Described as an agent-based modeling framework executed in NetLogo and Mathematica.,1,False,No closed-form mathematical solution; results obtained via numerical simulation.,1,100,"Table 1: N, Number of agents, setting for case study = 100.",1,8500,"Text describes an 8500 day simulation period (e.g., Fig. 5 caption) for the 21-year horizon.",0.9,1,"Salt application willingness S_i(t) can take values from 0.0 to 1.0 in increments of 0.05, implying continuous/any-amount contributions, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No human economic endowment or contribution game; not applicable to this environmental ABM.,1,N/R,No public goods payoff structure with a multiplier and group size is defined; MPCR not reported.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication interface; agents do not ‘chat’ in the experimental-economics sense.,1,0,"Agents access only local information (own energy, salt concentration, network neighbors’ defect decisions and strategies); no global summary feedback described.",0.7,0,Sanctions are modeled as fines and incentives in energy; there is no concept of identity revelation analogous to human lab experiments.,0.9,0,Incentives are distributed but without identity revelation concepts; model does not describe ID visibility.,0.9,0,Agents do not observe the remaining simulation horizon; no such information is mentioned.,0.9,1,Defecting agents are fined (energy loss) and monitoring intensity determines probability of being caught.,1,N/R,"Model defines fines F(t) in energy units and monitoring fee M(t), but does not specify per-unit cost of punishment in a token framework.",0.5,N/R,Magnitude of sanction per unit ‘cost’ is not parameterized in public-goods terminology; only total fine and monitoring are modeled.,0.5,1,Agents complying and with low salt application receive an incentive (positive energy change) from monitoring fee pool.,1,N/R,Incentive is funded from monitoring fees; no per-unit reward cost structure in token terms is given.,0.5,N/R,No mapping of 1 unit of cost to X units of reward is provided.,0.5,N/A,"No per-round monetary or token endowment for agents; energy is an evolving state, not an initial endowment in a contribution game.",1,N/R,"Outcomes reported are salt application willingness, institution application rate, salinity, energy, and number of defectors; no normalized contribution rate defined.",1,N/R,Salt application M_i(t) is defined but no aggregate or average per-round amount is reported numerically.,0.9,N/R,No ratio of realized group payoff to maximum possible payoff is reported.,1,N/R,"Average energy is shown qualitatively (figures), but no numeric group payoff values are tabulated.",0.8,N/A,No human participants; property owners are simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Explicitly an agent-based computational model with no human subjects.,1,"Agent-based common-pool resource model of deicer (salt) application on private properties affecting groundwater salinity; 100 agents each with pavement area 2000 m^2, permeable area 30 m^2; daily time steps over 21 years; climate forcing from Virginia; agents choose salt application willingness, may defect from an institution, are connected in random social networks (2–10 peers), can innovate or copy strategies, and vote periodically on institutions specifying allowable salt application ratio, monitoring intensity, and fines. In this base case, institutions are active and constrain behavior.","Summary of model rules and environment from Sections 3 and 4, restricted to the with-institutions base case.",1
10.1016_j.hydroa.2024.100188.md,"Base case – without institutions (IR=0.5, DP=0.15)","Section 4.1 defines a base case without institutions for comparison, using the same IR_i=0.5 and DP_i=0.15 but with institution mechanisms turned off.",1,"Institution mechanism inactive (no institution selected, no monitoring, no fines, no incentives); likelihood to innovate IR_i=0.5; likelihood to defect DP_i=0.15; 100 agents.",Independent variable is presence vs absence of institution mechanism; here set to absent with the same behavioral propensities as the institutional base case.,1,False,Agent-based simulation without human participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; purely computational.,1,False,No lab environment with human subjects.,1,True,Scenario run with the same ABM but institutional submodel disabled.,1,False,No analytical derivations; results from simulation.,1,100,Same model configuration: N=100 agents (Table 1).,1,8500,Same 8500 day / 21-year simulation horizon (used for base case without institutions in Fig. 5).,0.9,1,"Salt application willingness S_i(t) spans 0.0–1.0; agents can choose any level, not only all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,No endowment allocation for contributions; environmental ABM.,1,N/R,No MPCR-like parameter defined.,1,N/A,No human communication channel.,1,0,"As in institutional case, no global summary displays; only local agent information.",0.7,0,"No punishment institutions exist, so identity revelation is moot.",1,0,"No reward mechanism exists, so no identity revelation.",1,0,Agents do not observe total or remaining days.,0.9,0,"Without institutions, there is no monitoring or fines; agents only gain or lose energy from deicing and salinity.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this scenario.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this scenario.,1,0,No institutional incentives are described without institutions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No initial monetary endowment in a game-theoretic sense.,1,N/R,No contribution rate metric is reported.,1,N/R,Salt application is defined but no numeric summary of contributions is provided.,0.9,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,Only qualitative trajectories of average energy are shown; no numeric final group payoff reported.,0.8,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely computational agent-based simulation.,1,"Same environmental and agent configuration as the institutional base case (100 agents representing office-park property owners, daily climate forcing from Virginia, coupled TTD hydrologic model, random social networks). In this scenario, no institution is ever formed: agents cannot vote on allowable salt application ratios, monitoring, or fines. Agents adjust their salt application willingness by innovating or copying neighbors in response to energy changes. There is no monitoring fee, no fines, and no incentive pool. As a result, vadose zone salinity tends to rise above the taste threshold and remain high.",Summarizes rules for the control scenario where the institutional submodel is disabled (Section 4.3).,1
10.1016_j.hydroa.2024.100188.md,"Sensitivity scenario – Low defection, moderate innovation (DP=0.10, IR=0.50)","Section 4.4 and Fig. 11(a) define a ‘Low Defection Scenario’ with 10% likelihood to defect and 50% likelihood to innovate, with institutions active.",1,Institutions active; likelihood to defect DP_i=0.10; likelihood to innovate IR_i=0.50; 100 agents; other parameters per Table 1.,This condition is characterized by specific values of DP_i and IR_i under the institutional framework.,1,False,Agent-based computational model with no human subjects.,1,False,Not an empirical or field experiment.,1,False,No physical lab or field setting; simulations only.,1,True,Described as an agent-based modeling sensitivity scenario.,1,False,"Outcomes obtained by simulation, not closed-form analysis.",1,100,N=100 agents throughout all scenarios per Table 1.,1,8500,Sensitivity scenarios use the same 21-year daily horizon as base cases (text and figures).,0.9,1,Salt application willingness remains a continuous choice from 0 to 1 in 0.05 increments.,1,N/A,No human endowments or contributions.,1,N/R,No MPCR reported.,1,N/A,No human communication; only modeled network influence.,1,0,No indication that agents see global summaries.,0.7,0,Identity concepts not modeled; fines applied abstractly.,0.9,0,"Incentives are energy changes, not identity-based rewards.",0.9,0,Agents do not know total simulation length.,0.9,1,Institutions with monitoring and fines for defectors are in place.,1,N/R,Per-unit punishment cost not parameterized in public-goods terms.,0.5,N/R,Model sets fine magnitude F(t) but not a per-token technology coefficient.,0.5,1,Agents with low application receive incentives funded by monitoring fees.,1,N/R,No cost-per-unit reward specification.,0.5,N/R,"Reward magnitude is defined via Eqn. 6, not as a simple per-unit technology.",0.5,N/A,No experimental endowment.,1,N/R,Outcomes shown are salinity trajectories; no normalized contribution figures reported.,1,N/R,No explicit aggregate salt application data reported for this scenario.,0.9,N/R,No efficiency measure described.,1,N/R,Final energy distributions discussed qualitatively but no numeric value is given for this specific scenario.,0.8,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based computational sensitivity experiment.,1,"One of the 441 sensitivity scenarios varying defection propensity DP_i and innovation likelihood IR_i. All other structural features identical to institutional base case. Here DP_i=0.10 (low defection) and IR_i=0.50 (moderate innovation). Institutions emerge via voting and specify allowable salt application ratio, monitoring intensity, and fines. Results (Fig. 11a) show that vadose zone salinity generally remains below the taste threshold, with gradual increases, illustrating high effectiveness of institutions under low defection.",Summarizes the specific parameterization and qualitative outcome from Fig. 11(a).,1
10.1016_j.hydroa.2024.100188.md,"Sensitivity scenario – High defection, low innovation (DP=0.90, IR=0.10)",Section 4.4 and Fig. 11(b) describe the ‘High Defection Low Innovation Scenario’ with 90% likelihood to defect and 10% likelihood to innovate.,1,Institutions active; likelihood to defect DP_i=0.90; likelihood to innovate IR_i=0.10; 100 agents.,Independent condition defined exactly by DP_i and IR_i under institutional framework.,1,False,"Simulation only, no human subjects.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,Agent-based simulation sensitivity setting.,1,False,No analytical solutions.,1,100,Same as other scenarios: N=100.,1,8500,Sensitivity simulations share the same time horizon as base cases.,0.9,1,Agents select continuous salt application willingness.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments.,1,N/R,MPCR not defined.,1,N/A,No human-mediated communication.,1,0,No evidence of global summaries shown to agents.,0.7,0,Identities are not modeled; punishment is abstract.,0.9,0,Rewards are abstract energy incentives without identity visibility.,0.9,0,Agents lack knowledge of total days.,0.9,1,Institutions with fines and monitoring are active.,1,N/R,Per-unit cost of fines and monitoring not specified numerically.,0.5,N/R,No per-unit mapping from punishment spending to penalty.,0.5,1,Incentives for low salt application still present.,1,N/R,No explicit cost-per-unit reward value.,0.5,N/R,Reward technology not expressed in per-unit terms.,0.5,N/A,No experimental endowment.,1,N/R,No contribution rate metric is reported.,1,N/R,Aggregate salt application data not numerically reported.,0.9,N/R,No efficiency measure.,1,N/R,Energy states discussed qualitatively; no numeric final group payoff provided for this scenario.,0.8,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Pure agent-based simulation.,1,"Sensitivity scenario with very high defection propensity (DP_i=0.90) and low innovation (IR_i=0.10). Institutions form via voting as in other institutional scenarios, but many agents frequently defect. Results (Fig. 11b) show that vadose zone salinity typically exceeds the taste threshold in almost all simulations, with only a few runs remaining below due to convergence on low-willingness strategies caused by limited innovation and heavy copying among network neighbors.",Condenses description from Fig. 11(b) and related discussion in Section 4.4.,1
10.1016_j.hydroa.2024.100188.md,"Sensitivity scenario – High defection, high innovation (DP=0.90, IR=0.90)",Section 4.4 and Fig. 11(c) define the ‘High Defection High Innovation Scenario’ with 90% defection and 90% innovation likelihood.,1,Institutions active; likelihood to defect DP_i=0.90; likelihood to innovate IR_i=0.90; 100 agents.,Key independent parameters DP_i and IR_i distinguish this condition within the institutional framework.,1,False,No human participants; computational ABM.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab/field; purely simulation.,1,True,Agent-based simulation sensitivity scenario.,1,False,No analytical derivation.,1,100,All scenarios run with N=100 agents.,1,8500,Uses the same 21-year daily simulation window as other scenarios.,0.9,1,Salt application willingness choice remains continuous.,1,N/A,No contribution endowments.,1,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/A,No human interaction.,1,0,No global summary feedback mentioned.,0.7,0,Identity not modeled.,0.9,0,Identity not modeled.,0.9,0,Agents do not know simulation length.,0.9,1,Institutional fines and monitoring are active.,1,N/R,Per-unit costs not specified.,0.5,N/R,No per-unit punishment technology stated.,0.5,1,Incentive pool exists as in other institutional scenarios.,1,N/R,No per-unit reward cost given.,0.5,N/R,Reward magnitude formulation not reducible to a single scalar technology.,0.5,N/A,No endowment concept.,1,N/R,No normalized contribution rate reported.,1,N/R,No explicit numerical salt application aggregates given.,0.9,N/R,Efficiency is not quantified.,1,N/R,Energy distributions discussed qualitatively; no numeric group payoff reported for this parameter set.,0.8,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,N/A,Simulated agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based computational sensitivity run.,1,"Sensitivity scenario with both very high defection (DP_i=0.90) and very high innovation (IR_i=0.90) under the institutional framework. Institutions still set allowable salt application ratios, monitoring intensity, and fines, but agents frequently change strategies randomly due to high innovation. As described in Fig. 11(c), many simulations maintain vadose zone salt concentrations below the taste threshold despite high defection rates, because frequent innovation allows agents to discover low-salt strategies that improve energy and get reinforced.",Summarizes configuration and qualitative results from Fig. 11(c) and Section 4.4.,1
