st) 50.52%.,1,6.0624,Average contribution = 0.5052 * 12 ≈ 6.0624 points.,0.95,N/R,No normalized efficiency index reported; Table 2 gives gross and net earnings but not scaled efficiency.,0,45.57,Table 2: gross earnings per player 15.19; group gross earnings = 15.19*3 = 45.57.,0.95,Italy,Same Bologna lab subject pool.,0.9,N/R,No age distribution reported.,0,N/R,No gender data.,0,University students,Participants are described as students.,0.9,On site,Conducted in BLESS laboratory.,1,"Same heterogeneous 3-player binary public good as baseline; after contribution stage, cooperators choose whether to punish defectors and defectors choose (via strategy method) whether to counter-punish cooperators. Punishment fee options: 0,1,2 points; fines per cooperator punishing depend on target size: 2 (L), 5 (M), 8 (S), and can accumulate over multiple punishers. Counter-punishment uses same fee/fine structure. Punishment decisions elicited in 'cold' state, simultaneous via strategy method.",Summarized from PGGwP treatment description and formalization.,1
10.1016_j.socec.2024.102211.md,CCwP – Conditional Cooperation with Punishment,Treatment labeled 'CCwP' in Table 1 and text.,1,Treatment: CCwP (adds conditional cooperation plus punishment and counter-punishment),Independent variable is joint availability of conditional contribution strategies and punishment options.,1,True,Uses human participants in lab sessions.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment.,1,True,Run in BLESS laboratory.,1,False,No simulation-only study.,1,False,Main outcome data are experimental.,1,3,"Same 3-player groups (S,M,L).",1,30,All treatments involve 30 repeated rounds.,1,0,Contribution choices are binary (0 or 12) but include unconditional and conditional strategies.,1,0,"Endowment starts private; transferred to public if cooperation condition met, otherwise remains private.",1,0.5,Same underlying public good technology as baseline.,1,0,Communication not allowed.,1,1,End of period: participants observed implemented actions (contributions and implemented punishment decisions).,0.9,1,"Punishment targets defectors, and individual implemented actions within the fixed 3-person group are observable.",0.8,0,No rewards.,1,0,No explicit mention of showing remaining rounds.,0.2,1,Punishment and counter-punishment options included as in PGGwP.,1,1,Same punishment cost structure: per targeted player a fee of 1 per unit (φ=1).,1,1,"Same size-dependent fines (2,5,8) per punisher as in PGGwP, with φ=1.",0.4,0,No rewarding option.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,12,Same 12-point endowment per round.,1,0.5156,Table 2: ex-post cooperation rate 51.56%.,1,6.1872,Average contribution = 0.5156 * 12 ≈ 6.1872.,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency index provided.,0,45.39,Table 2: gross earnings per player 15.13; group gross earnings = 15.13*3 = 45.39.,0.95,Italy,Same Bologna lab setting.,0.9,N/R,Age distribution not provided.,0,N/R,Gender not specified.,0,University students,Described as students.,0.9,On site,"Conducted in BLESS laboratory, using LIONESS platform.",1,"Combination of IwYw and PGGwP: in contribution stage, options are defect, unconditional cooperate, conditionally cooperate (cooperate if at least one other cooperates). In punishment stage, cooperators may punish defectors; defectors may counter-punish cooperators. If a conditional cooperator’s endowment remains in private account (because condition not met), her punishment request is disregarded. Implemented actions, but not ex-ante strategies, are observed at end of period. Same heterogeneous size structure and punishment fine schedule (2,5,8) as in PGGwP.",Summarized from CCwP treatment description.,1
