not as normalized contribution rates.",0.3,N/R,No numeric token contributions reported.,1,N/R,"Effects of sanctions assessed via which equilibria are stable (all-coop, all-defect, mixed), not explicit efficiency ratios.",1,N/R,"Example parameterizations (e.g., figure 4–6) give x_k and g_k but do not report aggregate payoffs as main DV.",0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The system is analyzed mathematically and via computer simulations.,1,"Same division-of-labour tree game as baseline, with added sanction: in the defector sanction system, if a defector is identified they receive a punishment f with probability ρ, directly reducing their payoff. Replicator equations (Appendix B) modify payoff differences by +ρf terms favoring cooperation. Numerical analyses explore how varying ρf and cost patterns (increasing or decreasing downstream) affect convergence to equilibria (premierD, allc, and various mixed states) on symmetric and asymmetric tree networks.","Based on description in section 3, Appendix B, and figures 4–7.",1
10.1098_rsos.230830.md,Premier sanction system,Section 3 and Appendix C define a 'premier sanction system' where the premier is always sanctioned if any defection occurs.,1,Sanction system type = premier sanction; parameters: punishment fine f applied to premier whenever defection occurs downstream.,This condition differs from baseline and defector sanction only in the way sanction is applied.,1,False,The system is purely theoretical and simulated.,1,False,No real-world controlled experiment.,1,False,No lab setting or subjects.,1,True,Replicator dynamics with premier sanction is numerically simulated to check stability predictions.,1,True,Derives replicator equations and local stability for equilibria under premier sanction.,1,1,Frequency dynamics per infinite group; effectively one dynamical variable per node.,0.3,1,Continuous-time dynamics; no explicit discrete rounds.,0.2,1,Strategy set remains cooperate vs defect.,1,N/A,No endowment/contribution decision.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,N/A,No communication layer.,1,0,"Model assumes imitation based on payoffs, not on observables like histories or summaries.",1,0,Sanction targets premier deterministically when any defection occurs; no identity revelation modeled.,0.7,0,No rewards exist.,1,0,No explicit round information.,1,1,Premier is punished with fine f when defection is present anywhere downstream.,1,N/R,Fine f is modeled as direct payoff loss to premier but no separate implementation cost is modeled.,0.6,N/R,No token-per-punishment structure; f is a lump-sum sanction.,0.6,0,Model includes only punitive sanctions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Same payoff primitives as baseline with added premier fine f.,1,N/R,"Main outcomes are which equilibria (premierD, allc, mixed) are stable, not contribution fractions from an endowment.",0.3,N/R,No explicit contribution amounts.,1,N/R,"They compare sanction systems and show that under premier sanction, premierD remains only stable in social dilemma; no efficiency metric.",1,N/R,Do not report aggregate payoffs as central DV.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical/simulation model.,1,"Same division-of-labour tree game as baseline, but with premier sanction: whenever any defection occurs, regardless of who defects, the premier group’s representative is sanctioned by amount f. Replicator equation for premier includes +c_op f term in favor of cooperation; other groups’ equations unaffected directly by f (Appendix C). Analysis shows that in social dilemma conditions, premierD remains the only stable equilibrium under this sanction type, unlike the defector sanction system which can stabilize cooperation and mixed equilibria.",Summarized from section 3 and Appendix C.,1
10.1209_0295-5075_ad0998.md,Simulation – Justice strategy with dynamic aspiration on square lattice (varying A and b),Single agent-based model on a square lattice; conditions differ only by parameter values (aspiration A and temptation b).,0.9,"Initial aspiration level A (0 to 4 in steps of 0.1); temptation payoff b (1 < b ≤ 2); initial fractions and spatial arrangement of C, D, J in some scenarios.",Paper systematically varies A and b and also discusses different initial proportions and position patterns while keeping other parameters fixed.,0.95,False,"Study uses agent-based simulations on a lattice, explicitly no human participants.",1,False,"Numerical simulation study in evolutionary game theory, not a controlled human or field experiment.",1,False,No lab or field setting; purely computational.,1,True,Described as agent-based modeling with lattice of agents updated over 100000 steps.,1,False,Contains qualitative reasoning but no formal closed-form analytical model as primary method; main results from simulations.,0.9,10000,"Square lattice with L=100, so number of agents is L*L = 100*100 = 10000.",1,100000,They state simulations are run for 100000 steps.,1,1,"Prisoner’s dilemma with discrete strategies C, D, J; no partial contribution levels mentioned, so no continuous contribution choice.",0.8,N/A,"Model is a standard PD with payoffs, not a public goods contribution game with endowments.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game with a multiplier; it is a 2-player PD payoff matrix.,1,N/A,No human subjects; communication between simulated agents is not modeled as chat.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback to human participants; only payoff-based updates in a simulation.,1,N/A,No identities or displays; punishment is embedded in payoff matrix between J and D.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identity display; only payoffs from PD interactions.,1,N/A,No human interface; agents are not shown number of rounds.,1,1,"Justice strategy J punishes D, giving both a negative payoff l=-0.3, which is a costly punishment mechanism.",1,-0.3,"When J meets D, both receive payoff l=-0.3; cost to punisher per punished defector is 0.3 relative to baseline 0 or 1.",0.9,-0.3,"Per J-D interaction, defector’s payoff is reduced to l=-0.3 instead of at least 0; magnitude of imposed loss is 0.3 relative to 0 baseline, but they set equal losses so we record same absolute value.",0.7,0,No separate reward mechanism beyond standard PD payoffs; no extra-cost positive transfers.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism modeled.,1,N/A,No explicit per-round endowment; agents just receive payoffs from PD interactions.,1,N/A,"Not a contribution game; outcomes are fractions of strategies, not contributions.",1,N/A,"No contribution variable; only strategy type C, D, or J.",1,N/R,"They report fractions of strategies, not aggregate payoffs or efficiency ratios.",0.3,N/R,No explicit reporting of average total payoff per group or system in results.,0.3,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based numerical simulations without human subjects.,1,"Spatial prisoner’s dilemma on a 100x100 square lattice with periodic boundary conditions and three strategies: Cooperation (C), Defection (D), Justice (J). Payoff matrix: C–C:1, C–D:0 to C, b to D; C–J:1 each; D–D:0; D–J:l=-0.3 each; J–J:1 each. Each agent plays with four von Neumann neighbors. Strategy update: aspiration-based rule where if accumulated payoff P_i < aspiration A_i, agent considers adopting a random neighbor’s strategy via Fermi rule with K=0.1. Aspiration A_i is dynamic and updated as A_i(t+1)=A_i(t)+a*(P_i(t)-A_i(t)) with a=0.05; initial aspiration A common to all agents and varied across simulations. Initial strategies mostly random with specified proportions (often 1/3 C, 1/3 D, 1/3 J), with additional scenarios having skewed proportions or clustered spatial patterns. Simulations run for 100000 steps and averaged over at least 30 independent runs per (A,b) pair; primary outcomes are stationary fractions r_C, r_D, r_J and qualitative spatial patterns.","Summarized directly from the model and results sections describing the lattice structure, payoff matrix, update rules, and parameter settings.",1
