ooperators/punishers.",Summarizes changes specific to T=3 condition relative to base reputation model.,1
10.1016_j.amc.2025.129545.md,Simulation – reputation-based graded punishment with T = 5,"Condition where punisher reputation increment is highest, T=5 (e.g., Figs. 3d, 4d, 5d, 6d, 7d, 8–11).",1,"Punishment cost α, fine β, synergy factor r, initial punishment probability f0, punisher reputation increment T=5, reputation threshold Rmax (typically 100).",Core independent manipulation is high T; other parameters scanned as in other conditions.,1,False,All results from Monte Carlo simulation.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab participants.,1,True,Spatial lattice simulations with evolutionary updates.,1,False,No analytical solution presented.,1,10000,100×100 lattice.,1,10000,"Time evolution inspected up to 10,000 MCS.",0.6,1,Binary contribution decision.,0.8,N/A,No human endowments.,1,0.64,Same SPGG payoff rule; typical r=3.2.,0.6,N/A,No agent communication.,1,0,Agents only observe own and one neighbor's payoff.,1,N/A,Identities not modeled.,1,N/A,Rewards not modeled.,1,0,No finite-horizon awareness.,1,1,Punishment is active mechanism.,1,0.5,Many T=5 demonstrations use α around 0.4–0.5; 0.5 is typical in comparative figures.,0.5,1,One punishment act per punished defector per punisher; cost μ·α and fine β.,0.3,0,No reward feature.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,N/A,Rewards absent.,1,1,"As in other conditions, contribution per C/P is 1.",0.8,N/R,"Outcome reported as ρC trajectory, not normalized contribution rate.",0,N/R,No reporting of aggregate contributions.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency outcomes.,0,N/R,Group payoffs not presented as primary DV.,0,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,No human,All analyses are simulation-based.,1,"Same SPGG with dynamic probabilistic punishment and reputation-based μ as T=1 and T=3, but with strongest punisher reputation increment T=5. This yields the fastest accumulation of reputation, the strongest reduction of μ, lowest effective punishment costs, lowest critical synergy factor r for full cooperation, and fastest convergence to ρC=1.",Highlights what distinguishes the T=5 setting within the same model family.,1
