.,1,N/A,No game endowment is specified since no game is run.,1,N/A,There are no contribution decisions and no dependent variables measured by the author.,1,N/A,No contributions are observed or recorded.,1,N/A,No payoff or efficiency outcomes are empirically measured.,1,N/A,No group payoffs are calculated from experimental data.,1,N/A,No human participants are recruited by the author.,1,N/A,No sample of participants is described.,1,N/A,No human participant pool is used.,1,N/A,No educational background of participants is reported because there are no participants.,1,No human,The work is a theoretical/philosophical and evolutionary analysis without original human-subjects research.,1,N/A,"No specific strategic or game-theoretic experiment is implemented; all discussion of games (e.g., free rider problems, large-game hunting) is conceptual or based on prior literature.",1
10.1016_j.amc.2022.127723.md,"Simulation – Construction + Expression stages across central power (α, β)",Single simulation framework where only the central power parameters α and β are varied; encompasses both construction and expression stages as one condition family.,0.9,"Central power effectiveness α ∈ [0,1] and efficiency β ∈ [0,1]; in some analyses combined as ω = (1 − α)·β","Paper states that central power (characterized by α and β, and later ω) is the only parameter varied in the evolutionary simulations; all other parameters held constant.",1,False,No human participants; purely computational evolutionary simulations and analytical work.,1,False,Not a controlled lab or field experiment with human subjects; parameter sweeps in simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting; simulations only.,1,True,Section 2 and 5 explicitly describe an evolutionary simulation model and its computational setup.,1,True,"Includes closed-form evolutionary stable states (e.g., p̂1, p̂2, p̂3) and analytical derivations in Appendix; combined with simulations.",0.9,20000,Methods 5.1: “a population consisting of 20000 individuals is randomly assigned into 100 communities”.,1,310,Construction stage set to 300 rounds; expression stage run for 10 rounds; total 310 sequential rounds experienced by an individual across both stages.,0.7,1,"Contributions c_i^(k) are integers drawn from 0 to 20 with constraint c_i^(k) ≤ R; implies any amount between 0 and R is allowed, not just all-or-nothing.",1,N/A,Simulation does not describe a default allocation between private and public accounts; only an initial resource R from which agents choose contribution.,1,0.4,"MPCR = μ / n; with μ = 1.6 and n = 4 (Methods 5.1 and Section 2.3.1), MPCR = 1.6 / 4 = 0.4.",1,N/A,No human communication; agent strategies update via mutation and learning rules only.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback summaries described; purely algorithmic updates.,1,N/A,Agents are anonymous in the model; no notion of identity revelation.,1,N/A,No identity-based rewards; central power redistributes resources but without identity revelation mechanisms.,1,N/A,Agents do not receive meta-information about total or remaining rounds; they follow evolutionary update rules only.,1,1,Extended PGG explicitly includes peer punishment with expenditures e_ij and fines of 3·e_ij.,1,1,Each unit of e_ij is directly paid by punisher; cost per punishment unit is 1 resource unit.,1,3,Model: “individual i pays a cost of e_ij resources to decrease 3·e_ij resources of member j”; fine multiplier = 3.,1,1,Central power collects penalties from low contributors and redistributes them as rewards to high contributors between PGG and peer punishment.,1,0,"Rewards in the model are funded by collected penalties, not by voluntary costly reward actions of individuals; no cost per reward unit is described for agents.",0.9,N/R,Total central penalty within a community is equally divided among qualifying contributors; no per-unit cost-to-reward ratio comparable to peer punishment is defined.,0.6,20,Section 2.3.1: “member i is endowed with resource amount R”; Methods 5.1: “R = 20”.,1,N/R,"They report average contribution amounts and contribution rates conceptually (b and c·R) but do not provide a single normalized 0–1 rate across all (α, β) conditions.",0.4,N/R,"Figures (e.g., Fig. 9) show trends and distributions of average contributions but without tabulated numeric values that can be extracted for a specific condition.",0.3,N/R,No explicit reporting of efficiency as realized group payoff divided by the cooperation maximum.,0.2,N/R,Model describes payoff calculation but final aggregate payoff numbers are not reported.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; agents are not associated with countries.,1,N/A,No human participants; age not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; gender not applicable.,1,N/A,No human participants; education not applicable.,1,No human,"Study consists of an evolutionary simulation model and analytical derivations, without real-time human interaction.",1,"Extended repeated public goods game with peer punishment and central power; population N = 20000 split into 100 communities (P = n×g with n = 4, g = 50 per community), construction stage of 300 rounds with central punishment and learning/mutation, followed by expression stage of 10 rounds without central power. Punishment strategies M_i map contribution deviations in bins ([-20,-11], [-10,-1], [0], [1,10], [11,20]) to integer expenditures in [1,10]. Mutation rate 0.05 per round per individual; learning rule uses Fermi imitation with intensity s = 0.5. Central punishment targets contributors with c_i < R/2 by deducting fraction β of current resources r_i with probability 1−α; total deducted resources redistributed equally to those with contributions ≥ R/2.",Summarizes detailed configuration from Sections 2.3.1–2.3.3 and Methods 5.1–5.2 relevant to game structure.,1
10.1007_s10551-024-05865-y.md,PGG with punishment – all punishment technology conditions pooled,Single laboratory PGG with punishment where cost (0 or 2) and impact (2 or 8) are randomly varied every punishment decision; paper analyzes these jointly rather than as separate treatments.,0.98,Within-subject variation of punishment technology (cost of punishment: 0 vs 2 points; impact of punishment on target: 2 vs 8 points) and contribution difference between punisher and target (prosocial vs antisocial situations).,"Methods specify four punishment technologies (0/2/2/8), randomly drawn per decision; prosocial vs antisocial defined by contribution difference; no between-subject factor.",0.98,True,132 university students played in the lab.,1,True,Controlled randomized public goods game with punishment implemented in z-Tree.,1,True,Conducted in a German university lab using desktop terminals.,1,False,"All main PGG data are from human subjects; DDM is fitted to human data, not a standalone simulation study.",0.98,False,Paper includes statistical and DDM modeling but no closed-form analytical game-theoretic model as a separate study.,0.9,4,“Participants played the PGG in groups of four anonymous participants.”,1,20,“They played the PGG for 20 periods.”,1,1,"Contribution choices: 0–20 in integer multiples of 2, not restricted to full/all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each participant receives 20 points and chooses how much to contribute; by default all 20 are in private account.,0.95,0.4,Total contributions multiplied by 1.6 and equally divided among 4 players: MPCR = 1.6 / 4 = 0.4.,1,0,No mention of any communication; standard anonymous stranger-matching PGG.,0.95,1,"After contribution stage participants are informed about contributions of all group members and their payoffs, i.e., summary of others’ contributions/payoffs.",0.9,0,Groups are anonymous and no identities are revealed; only contribution and payoff info is shown.,0.95,0,No rewarding mechanism exists in the game.,1,1,"Standard lab PGGs and instructions indicate 20 periods; although not verbatim, participants are told the number of periods in instructions.",0.7,1,Second stage is explicitly a punishment stage where participants can punish each of three group members.,1,1,"Cost per punishment point: punishment technologies defined by cost (0 or 2) and impact (2 or 8). For cost=2 and impact 2 or 8, cost per impact point is 2/2=1 or 2/8=0.25; for cost=0 ratio is 0. Use 1 token cost per punishment action as canonical unit is ambiguous, so we report 1 as approximate cost per punishment *decision* is either 0 or 2; averaging yields 1.",0.3,4,"Four punishment technologies defined by combinations of cost (0/2) and impact (2/8), randomly drawn with equal probability for each target decision.",1,0,"Design only allows punishment, no rewarding option described.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,20,“Each participant received an endowment of 20 points.”,1,0.635,Mean contribution reported as 12.70 out of 20; normalized rate = 12.70/20 = 0.635.,0.95,12.7,"Results: “The mean contribution was 12.70 with the 95% CI=(12.46, 12.94).”",1,N/R,Paper does not report total payoffs relative to maximum possible cooperative payoff.,0,N/R,Only average individual earnings (€13.80) over incentivized tasks are reported; cannot back out per-round group payoff in points.,0,Germany,Participants were students at “a German University”; specific city not named.,0.95,Mean 21.6 years (SD 2.3),Participants: “age 21.6 ± 2.3.”,1,"72 females, 60 males (total N=132)",Participants section: “132 students (72 females…)”.,1,University students,Described explicitly as students at a German university.,0.98,On site,Conducted in a university lab using z-Tree on lab desktops; no indication of online participation.,1,"Stranger-matching 4-player PGG with punishment over 20 periods. Contribution stage: each player chooses contribution from 0 to 20 (in steps of 2) to a public project; total multiplied by 1.6 and evenly redistributed. Punishment stage: each player makes binary decisions (punish vs withhold) for each of the 3 other group members. For each decision, participants see their own and target’s contributions and the punishment technology (cost: 0 or 2 points to punisher; impact: 2 or 8 points deducted from target). Four cost-impact combinations are randomly drawn with equal probability per target. Groups are randomly rematched each period (stranger matching). Failure to respond within 120 seconds incurs automatic 3-point deduction; all decisions occurred within the time limit. Prosocial vs antisocial punishment situations are defined by whether the punisher contributed more than the target (prosocial) or less than or equal to the target (antisocial).","Summarized from Materials and Procedure sections detailing contribution and punishment stages, matching protocol, and prosocial/antisocial definitions.",1
10.1515_bejte-2019-0057.md,"Condition NoA4 (N=4, No Authority)",Condition label and description from Table 1 and Table 2: NoA4 is the linear public goods game with group size 4 and no authority/punishment.,1,Authority mechanism: absent; Group size N=4,Condition varies whether endogenous authority/punishment exists and group size; this row is the no-authority condition with N=4.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human undergraduates at HKUST.,1,True,"Controlled lab experiment with random matching, treatments, and monetary incentives.",1,True,Conducted in the HKUST laboratory using z-Tree.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,"This row describes an experimental treatment, not the theoretical model.",1,4,Group size N=4 for NoA4 per Table 1 and 2.,1,15,Each condition (NoA or A) is played for 15 rounds within a session.,1,1,Subjects can invest any integer 0–50 tokens in BLUE/RED; not constrained to all-or-nothing.,1,0,All 50 tokens are initially under individual control; nothing is pre-assigned to the public (RED) account.,1,1.5,Each token in RED yields 1.5 points to each group member; this is the marginal per capita return.,1,0,Instructions explicitly forbid any communication; no chat implemented.,1,1,End-of-round feedback includes group total RED investment and own income; summaries of group outcomes are shown.,0.9,0,"No punishment mechanism in NoA conditions, so no punished identity shown.",1,0,No reward mechanism in any condition.,1,1,Subjects told they will participate in 30 rounds (first 15 and second 15); thus total rounds known.,0.9,0,No authority and no fines in NoA conditions by design.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No positive reward/bonus linked to sanctioning; only standard payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,50,Each subject is endowed with 50 tokens each round for BLUE/RED investment.,1,N/R,Paper reports contribution rates mainly for authority vs non-authority and A vs NoA aggregated; no separate aggregate rate just for NoA4 is provided.,0.2,12.64,Row (3) of Table 4: Individual Public Goods Provision in NoA4 is 12.64 tokens.,1,0.0,"Empirical Efficiency Gain defined as total public goods contribution/(N*ω). For NoA4: total contribution=50.56, N*ω=4*50=200 → 50.56/200=0.2528. The table lists only A conditions’ efficiency; here computed value 0.2528.",0.6,150.58,"Row (8) of Table 4 reports mean earnings 150.58 per subject in NoA4; group payoff is 4*150.58=602.32, but table gives only individual; here we keep reported individual as proxy.",0.4,Hong Kong (China),Subjects recruited from HKUST in Hong Kong; country best coded as Hong Kong (China).,0.9,N/R,Only described as undergraduate population; no age statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,No gender composition or balance reported.,0.0,Undergraduate students,Subjects recruited from the undergraduate population at HKUST.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory at HKUST using z-Tree; not online.,1,Linear public goods game with BLUE (private) investment paying 2 to self and RED (public) investment paying 1.5 to each of 4 members; random rematching each round; no authority or fines.,Summarizes payoff structure and matching protocol for NoA4 from procedures section.,1
10.1515_bejte-2019-0057.md,"Condition A4 (N=4, Endogenous Authority and Punishment)",Condition label A4 in Table 1 and 2: N=4 with endogenous authority and punishment via Administrator Fund.,1,Authority mechanism: endogenous authority with discretionary punishment; Group size N=4,This condition adds the pre-LPG contest (Administrator Fund) and authority with punishment power in groups of 4.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,"Random matching, treatment variation (authority vs no authority) under controlled conditions.",1,True,Implemented in HKUST lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation-only results are reported for this condition.,1,False,"This row covers experimental, not theoretical, results.",1,4,Group size 4 in Condition A4.,1,15,Each authority condition (A4 or A8) is played for 15 rounds within a session.,1,1,"Subjects choose integer spending 0–50 on AF, then 0–(50-AF) on BLUE/RED; continuous within bounds.",1,0,Initial 50 tokens are fully under private control before any contribution to RED.,1,1.5,Each token invested in RED yields 1.5 points to each of the 4 members.,1,0,Instructions forbid any communication; no chat functionality in interface.,1,1,"End-of-round summary includes own AF, BLUE, group total RED, imposed fine, and income.",1,1,"Administrator chooses a specific group member to fine; punished individual sees “payoff deduction imposed to you” in feedback, so identity is revealed at least to the punished subject. Whether others see identity is not entirely clear; we code as 1 for existence of identity targeting.",0.6,0,No positive reward mechanism.,1,1,Instructions state there will be 30 rounds (first 15 and second 15); thus horizon known.,0.9,1,Administrator can impose fines (payoff deductions) on one group member.,1,0,"The imposed deduction is taken from the punished player and “will disappear and no one, including the administrator, can get it”; administrator does not sacrifice own tokens beyond AF contest spending. So marginal cost of punishment to the administrator is 0 per unit.",0.8,3,"Max fine equals three times the total AF spending: maximum punishment budget = 3 × Σ AF. Contest spending itself is the ‘input’; with 1 token to AF, total budget increases by up to 3 punishment units. Approximated technology as 3 units of potential fine per AF token.",0.7,0,No mechanism to increase others’ payoffs; only fines.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,50,"In each round, subjects start with 50 tokens for AF plus BLUE/RED allocation.",1,N/R,Table 3 reports contribution rates for A4 and A8 pooled across orders but not a single aggregate rate per condition; no direct numeric for A4 only.,0.2,20.43,Row (3) of Table 4: Individual Public Goods Provision in A4 is 20.43 tokens.,1,0.4086,Row (6) of Table 4: Efficiency Gain (%) for A4 is 40.86%; convert to proportion 0.4086.,1,153.54,"Row (8) of Table 4: Earnings for A4 are 153.54 per subject per round on average; group payoff is 4*153.54=614.16, but only individual mean is explicitly reported; we record reported per-person value.",0.5,Hong Kong (China),Experiments conducted at HKUST in Hong Kong.,0.9,N/R,No age information beyond being undergraduates.,0.2,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate population.,1,On site,Lab experiment at HKUST using z-Tree terminals.,1,Two-stage round: Stage 1 Administrator Fund (AF) contest where each token gives one lottery ticket; one winner becomes administrator. Stage 2: remaining tokens split between BLUE (return 2 to self) and RED (return 1.5 to each of 4 members). Administrator observes all RED investments and may fine exactly one member up to 3 times the group’s total AF spending; fine reduces punished player’s payoff and is destroyed.,"Summarizes contest, public goods, and punishment structure for A4 from procedures.",1
10.1515_bejte-2019-0057.md,"Condition NoA8 (N=8, No Authority)",Condition label and description from Table 1 and 2: NoA8 is the linear public goods game with group size 8 and no authority.,1,Authority mechanism: absent; Group size N=8,This condition is the no-authority baseline with N=8.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled lab design with random matching and treatment variation.,1,True,Run at HKUST lab with z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation-only data presented here.,1,False,"Describes experimental data, not analytic model.",1,8,Group size N=8 for NoA8 per Table 1 and 2.,1,15,Each condition is run for 15 rounds.,1,1,Subjects choose integer 0–50 allocation between BLUE and RED; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,1,0,All 50 tokens initially private; contributions to RED are chosen.,1,1.5,Each token in RED yields 1.5 points to each of 8 members.,1,0,Communication is disallowed by instructions; no chat tool provided.,1,1,Feedback includes group total RED and own payoff each round.,0.9,0,No punishment exists in NoA8.,1,0,No reward system.,1,1,"Instructions state there will be 30 rounds (two blocks of 15), so subjects know the horizon.",0.9,0,"By design, no administrator and no fines in NoA8.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,50,Each subject has 50 tokens for BLUE/RED in each of the first 15 rounds described for N=8.,1,N/R,Contribution rates by condition are not reported numerically for NoA8.,0.2,16.64,Row (3) of Table 4: Individual Public Goods Provision for NoA8 is 16.64 tokens.,1,0.328,"Empirical efficiency gain defined as total public contribution/(N*ω). For NoA8: total contribution=131.20, N*ω=8*50=400 → 131.20/400=0.328.",0.7,266.46,"Row (8) of Table 4 reports mean earnings 266.46 per subject; group total would be 8*266.46, but only individual value is given; we record individual mean.",0.5,Hong Kong (China),Same subject pool at HKUST for all treatments.,0.9,N/R,Age not given.,0.2,N/R,No gender data reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Drawn from undergraduate population at HKUST.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment at HKUST.,1,Linear public goods game identical to NoA4 but with 8 players: BLUE investment returns 2 to self; RED investment returns 1.5 to each of 8 group members; random rematching each round; no authority or fines.,Based on instructions for N=8 first 15 rounds.,1
10.1515_bejte-2019-0057.md,"Condition A8 (N=8, Endogenous Authority and Punishment)",Condition label A8 in Table 1 and 2: N=8 with endogenous authority and punishment.,1,Authority mechanism: endogenous authority with discretionary punishment; Group size N=8,This condition has the AF contest and authority in groups of 8.,1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment manipulating authority and group size.,1,True,Run onsite at HKUST using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulation-only study for this condition.,1,False,"This refers to experimental results, not just theory.",1,8,Group size 8 in A8 per design table.,1,15,Authority condition is played for 15 rounds within each session.,1,1,Participants freely choose integer tokens for AF and for BLUE/RED within constraints.,1,0,All 50 tokens initially private before AF and RED allocation decisions.,1,1.5,Each RED token returns 1.5 to each of 8 members.,1,0,Communication prohibited in instructions; no chat feature.,1,1,"End-of-round summary: AF spending, BLUE, group total RED, fine if any, and income.",1,1,Administrator selects one specific group member; punished player is explicitly informed the deduction was imposed on them in feedback.,0.6,0,No rewards.,1,1,Instructions specify 30 rounds split into first and second 15; horizon known.,0.9,1,Administrator may impose payoff deductions (fines) on one member.,1,0,Fines reduce punished player’s income; administrator’s own income is unaffected by each unit of fine other than sunk AF spending. So marginal cost per fine unit is 0.,0.8,3,"Maximum fine=3 × total AF; as in A4, we treat this as 3 punishment units per AF token contributed on average.",0.7,0,No ability to positively adjust others' payoffs.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,50,Each round starts with 50 tokens per subject for AF and investment decisions.,1,N/R,Contribution rates for A8 appear only in regressions relative to baseline; no single numeric rate is reported for A8 alone.,0.2,23.83,Row (3) of Table 4: Individual Public Goods Provision in A8 is 23.83 tokens.,1,0.4765,Row (6) of Table 4: Efficiency Gain (%) for A8 is 47.65%; converted to proportion 0.4765.,1,317.37,"Row (8) of Table 4: Earnings in A8 are 317.37 per subject per round on average; group total would be 8× this, but only individual figure reported; we record that.",0.5,Hong Kong (China),Same subject pool and lab for all conditions.,0.9,N/R,No age details beyond undergraduates.,0.2,N/R,Gender shares not reported.,0,Undergraduate students,Recruited from undergraduate population at HKUST.,1,On site,Laboratory sessions at HKUST.,1,Two-stage round identical to A4 but with 8 players: Stage 1 AF contest (lottery tickets per AF token) with one administrator selected. Stage 2: remaining tokens allocated between BLUE (2 to self) and RED (1.5 to each of 8 members). Administrator can fine exactly one member up to 3 × total group AF; punishment budget proportional to contest spending.,Summarizes A8 treatment structure from procedures and design section.,1
10.1016_j.jet.2019.104972.md,Analytical model – generic repeated matching game with multiple partners,"The paper presents a general theoretical model of a repeated matching game with multiple partners and modal actions, not broken into empirical conditions.",1,"Not applicable – no experimental treatments; analytical characterization of equilibria as functions of discount factor δ, memory p, number of partners s, population size n, and noise parameters (ε_τ, ε_ν, ε_ω).",The work is purely theoretical; parameters vary in comparative statics but are not experimental conditions.,1,False,No human participants or data collection; the paper develops formal game-theoretic models and proofs.,1,False,No controlled experiment is run; only theoretical models and equilibria are derived.,1,False,No lab or field implementation; entirely theoretical.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical computer simulations are reported; only analytical reasoning.,1,True,The paper defines a formal repeated game with random matching and proves propositions and claims analytically.,1,2,The stage game is a two-player game between a type-1 and type-2 player; population size is 2n but interactions are pairwise.,0.9,-1,The game is infinitely repeated; the paper uses an infinite horizon with discount factor δ.,1,1,Players choose actions from finite action sets A1 and A2; there is no restriction to binary full-contribute vs zero; general actions allowed.,0.8,N/A,No public goods or contribution endowments are modeled; generic stage games like PD with cooperate/defect choices only.,1,N/R,"No public good production function or multiplier is defined; only generic payoffs g1, g2 are specified.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; purely theoretical.,1,N/R,"Information structure is specified as knowledge of partners’ past actions and modal actions, not payoff/punishment summaries; no such summaries mentioned.",0.9,N/R,"Identities exist conceptually, but the model does not discuss revealing identities specifically for punishment decisions.",0.5,N/R,"The model focuses on sanctions via minmax actions, not on rewards; identity revelation for rewards not discussed.",0.5,N/R,Infinite horizon with discounting; the model does not involve players being told the number of rounds.,1,1,Sanctions are implemented by playing minmax actions m_ℓ against players who were non-modal; this is a form of costly punishment.,1,N/R,"The paper defines payoffs under sanctions (w₁, w₂, w₁′, w₂′) but does not normalize a per-unit cost of punishment like in standard PGG punishment technologies.",0.7,N/R,Sanctioning is via choosing a particular action (minmax) rather than a parametric technology with ‘units’ of punishment per cost.,0.7,0,The enforcement mechanism uses only sanctions/minmax actions; rewards are not modeled as a separate institutional option.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/A,Rewards are not part of the model.,1,N/R,"In the generic model, players receive payoffs from the stage game; no monetary or token endowment is specified, except conceptually in the separate overlapping-generations discussion.",0.8,N/R,No contribution variables or cooperation rates are empirically measured; the paper derives equilibrium payoffs v in the payoff space V.,1,N/R,No quantitative contributions are measured; only generic actions and payoffs are discussed.,1,N/R,"Theoretical payoffs are characterized, but no explicit efficiency ratios relative to a formal maximum are computed as outcome variables.",0.7,N/R,"The model tracks payoffs to two players (types) and population-average continuation payoffs, but no single numeric group payoff is reported.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical/analytical with no empirical implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated random matching game with 2n players split into two types. Each period, each type-1 player is uniformly randomly matched with s ≥ 3 type-2 players (s divides n) to play a finite two-player stage game G with action sets A1, A2 and payoff functions g1, g2. Players discount with factor δ and receive s stage-game payoffs per period, averaged. Information structure: in each period t, player i observes, for herself and current partners j ∈ μ(i,t), the last p periods of their own actions and the corresponding modal action sets θ_j^τ, allowing identification of ‘non-modal’ players who deviated from modal behavior. Equilibrium construction: players play target action profile a on the equilibrium path to achieve any v in V*, and sanction partners who were non-modal in the last q ≤ p periods by playing their type’s minmax action m_ℓ; deviators themselves play equilibrium-path action during sanctions. Robustness: the equilibrium is strict, sequential, globally stable, and robust to trembles and noisy players; robustness to observation errors alone fails but is restored when observation errors are sufficiently rare relative to other noise sources.",Summarizes the key structural and informational features of the analytical model described in Sections 3–5.,1
10.1016_j.jet.2019.104972.md,Analytical worked example – Prisoner’s Dilemma with modal sanctions,Section 4 provides a specific worked example applying the general construction to a symmetric Prisoner’s Dilemma stage game.,1,"Stage game specified as a generic Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs (C,C)=(1,1), (D,C)=(1+g,−ℓ), (C,D)=(−ℓ,1+g), (D,D)=(0,0); parameters g, ℓ > 0, g < 1+ℓ; sanctions length q=1; memory p=1; cooperation enforced via modal-action-based sanctions using D as minmax action.",The paper explicitly defines the PD payoff matrix and fixes q=1 and p=1 in the example.,1,False,No human participants; the example is a parameterized analytical instantiation of the general model.,1,False,"The worked example is still theoretical, with no experimental implementation.",1,False,No lab or field setting.,1,False,No numerical or simulation results; only algebraic inequalities.,1,True,"Equilibrium conditions in the PD are derived analytically, showing required bounds on δ.",1,2,The PD is a two-player game; matching pairs one type-1 and one type-2 in each stage game.,1,-1,The PD is embedded in an infinite-horizon repeated matching game.,1,1,"Players choose between C and D; these are standard discrete actions, not a constrained all-or-nothing contribution scheme with continuous amounts.",0.8,N/A,There is no contribution endowment or public good in the PD example.,1,N/R,The PD is not structured as a linear public good with a defined MPCR.,1,N/A,No communication or human implementation.,1,N/R,Information consists of observed actions and modal actions; no payoff or sanction summaries are described.,0.9,N/R,"Identity is implicit in labeling partners, but no special identity-revelation feature for punishment is modeled.",0.5,N/R,Rewards are not modeled in this example.,0.5,N/R,Infinite horizon; no information about total rounds is given to theoretical players.,1,1,"Defection D is used as the minmax/sanctioning action against non-modal players, generating lower payoffs for the sanctioned.",1,N/R,"Payoff differences from using D instead of C are characterized (w_i, w_i′), but not as a normalized ‘cost per unit of punishment’.",0.7,N/R,Sanctioning is binary (play D vs C) rather than a graded punishment technology with a defined mapping per cost unit.,0.7,0,Only sanctions via D are used; no explicit reward mechanism is defined.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"The PD payoffs are normalized utilities (1, 1+g, −ℓ, 0); no explicit resource endowment per round is specified.",0.8,N/R,"The example shows that full cooperation (C by all) can be sustained for δ > max{g/(1+ℓ), ℓ/(1+ℓ)}, but no empirical contribution rates are measured.",0.9,N/R,"Actions are C or D, not quantified contributions, and no empirical data are reported.",1,N/R,"The cooperative payoff (1,1) is efficient relative to PD payoffs, but no explicit efficiency ratio is computed as a DV.",0.8,N/R,"Theoretical payoffs of (1,1) under cooperation are shown, but no empirical group payoff data are reported.",0.9,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,"This is an illustrative analytical example of the theoretical model, not an experiment.",1,"Stage game: symmetric Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoffs defined by Fig. 1: mutual cooperation (C,C) yields (1,1); unilateral defection (D,C) yields (1+g, −ℓ) for the defector/cooperator; (C,D) yields (−ℓ, 1+g); mutual defection (D,D) yields (0,0), with g, ℓ > 0 and g < 1+ℓ. This PD is embedded in the repeated matching game with multiple partners and observable modal actions. Equilibrium construction: choose a_i = C and minmax actions m_i = D for both players. Minmax payoff is 7(v_i) = 0, maximum payoff 3(v_i) = 1+g. During sanctions, sanctioned player plays C while the sanctioner plays D, so w_i = −ℓ (sanctioned) and w_i′ = 1+g (sanctioner). With sanctions length q=1 and memory p=1, conditions for sustaining cooperation (C) as equilibrium-path behavior reduce to δ > g/(1+ℓ) and δ > ℓ/(1+ℓ). Thus for sufficiently patient players (δ above these thresholds), cooperation can be sustained with only a one-period memory and one-period sanctions, using the modal-action-based sanctioning rule of the general model.","Summarizes how the general modal-sanction equilibrium is instantiated in the PD worked example, including the key inequalities for δ and the role of q=1, p=1.",1
10.1016_j.jet.2019.104974.md,Theoretical dynamic network game (all conditions),"The paper presents only a formal theoretical model and examples (e.g., binary formation model, within-between group model, target-partner model) without implementing distinct empirical or simulated experiments; all results are analytical within a single general game framework.",1,"Model parameters and structural assumptions (discount factor δ; formation cost bounds [F_min, F_max]; formation technologies Γ including γ_ij, γ_W, γ_B, γ_S, γ_L; monitoring radius ρ ∈ {1,2,…,∞}; tie categories (strong/weak/asymmetric) via c_ij relative to δ; belief refinement (PBE vs BFE vs SE); network structures (cycles, complete networks, clusters, core-periphery); binary formation parameters (F̲, F̄, c̲, c̄).","Across the theory, comparative statics and examples vary δ, formation technologies, monitoring radius ρ, and equilibrium refinements; there is no single experimental treatment but a parametric theoretical analysis.",1,False,No human participants or real-world observational data are used; the paper is purely theoretical.,1,False,There is no controlled experiment with subjects; only analytical characterizations of equilibria in a game-theoretic model.,1,False,No laboratory or online experiment is run; all results are mathematical.,1,False,"Although numerical examples with specific parameter values are given, there is no agent-based or numerical simulation study; results are derived analytically.",1,True,"The paper develops and solves an analytical repeated-game/network-formation model, proving propositions and providing formal examples.",1,3,"The model allows any finite I; the only explicit cardinality used in a fully specified payoff example is the three-player case in Example 2 (players i, j, k). Since there is no empirical experiment, we record this smallest explicit player count.",0.5,N/R,"The interaction is modeled as an infinite-horizon repeated game (t = 0,1,2,…); no finite number of rounds is set as a design parameter.",1,1,"On each tie in the continuation phase, players choose C or D, but this is not a contribution level from an endowment; the public-goods all-or-nothing notion does not apply. We treat contribution granularity as not applicable and set this to 1 by convention as players can always choose either action, not a forced all-or-nothing contribution from a divisible endowment.",0.3,N/A,There is no endowment or public good; payoffs are defined by benefits 1 and costs c_ij on bilateral ties in a Prisoner’s Dilemma structure.,1,N/A,"The game is a networked repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR is defined.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication protocol; the model assumes only monitoring of actions via radius ρ.,1,N/A,"No user interface or feedback design exists; information is formalized as observability of actions along network paths, not numerical summaries.",1,N/A,Punishment is endogenous via severing ties and contagion; there are no experimental displays of identities.,1,N/A,There is no explicit rewarding institution or interface showing reward identities.,1,N/A,The game is infinite-horizon; there is no interface revealing number of rounds to participants.,1,1,"Punishment occurs via discontinuation of ties following deviations (“grim” strategies, contagion of defection); this is a form of endogenous costly punishment within the game.",1,N/R,Costs of punishment are embedded in foregone cooperative surplus after severing ties; no single numeric ‘cost per punishment unit’ is specified.,0.3,N/R,"There is no parametric mapping from punishment tokens to losses; punishment is discontinuation of interaction, whose effect depends on network and parameters.",0.3,0,There is no separate rewarding stage or positive transfer mechanism designated as reward; only cooperation vs defection decisions.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Players do not receive a per-period numerical endowment; they face costs c_ij and benefits normalized to 1 from cooperation.,1,N/R,Outcomes are characterized in terms of equilibrium network structures and incentive constraints; no empirical or simulated contribution rate is measured.,1,N/R,There are only binary actions C/D without a token endowment; no contribution amount is recorded.,1,N/R,The paper discusses efficiency conceptually but does not compute empirical efficiency measures; it characterizes efficient networks theoretically.,1,N/R,"Payoff expressions are derived symbolically (e.g., (1−c_ij)/(1−δ)), but no numerical experimental group payoff data are reported.",1,N/A,No human participants are involved.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is purely theoretical with no empirical or field implementation.,1,"Infinite-horizon repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma on an endogenously formed bilateral network. Two phases: formation (players choose unilateral investments F_ij within [F_min, F_max] determining tie strengths via a formation technology Γ and tie costs c_ij) and continuation (each period t ≥ 0, players choose C or D independently on each existing tie; mutual C continues the tie, any D permanently severs it). Monitoring is restricted by a radius ρ (players observe actions only on paths up to length ρ), with special attention to ρ = 1 (local monitoring) vs ρ ≥ 2 (partial monitoring). Analysis develops pooled incentive constraints (PIC), local incentive constraints (LIC), and robust incentive constraints (RIC), and compares equilibrium refinements: Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium, Belief-Free Equilibrium, and Sequential Equilibrium. Key structural prediction: under local monitoring and belief-free enforcement, equilibrium networks must satisfy a triadic closure property and exhibit clustering/small-world features.",Summarizes the core game structure and theoretical focus described throughout Sections 2–4.,1
10.1075_is.19011.qir.md,Field study of 46 American intentional communities – overall sample,"Single observational study; no randomized or clearly separated experimental conditions are described, so the entire sample is treated as one condition.",0.9,N/A,Study is descriptive/observational; no manipulated independent variable at the game/interaction level.,1,True,Human participants from intentional communities were interviewed or completed questionnaires.,1,False,"No controlled treatment, randomization, or experimental task; purely observational self-report and document analysis.",1,False,"Data collected via phone interviews/questionnaires in naturalistic community settings, not in a lab.",1,False,No simulations or computational models were conducted.,1,False,Paper does not present a formal mathematical or closed-form analytical model; it is empirical and theoretical discussion.,1,44.8,Mean number of adult members per community reported as 44.8; treated as average group size relevant to cooperation.,0.8,0,No repeated game or round-based interaction was implemented; ongoing real-world interactions instead.,1,1,No formal contribution game with discrete choices was used; contributions are continuous real-world efforts. Coded as allowing any amount.,0.6,N/R,No experimental endowment or default allocation between private and public accounts is described.,1,N/R,No public goods game with a defined multiplier or MPCR was conducted.,1,1,"Real communities make group decisions via meetings, consensus, and discussion; communication is integral, so coded as allowed.",0.9,1,"Members observe and discuss others’ participation and free-riding; rules and enforcement are collectively known, implying information about others’ behavior is available.",0.7,1,"Sanctions such as discussions, fines, or asking members to leave occur in small communities where identities are known; punishment is not anonymous.",0.9,1,"Any social rewards or recognition in small, face-to-face communities would be identifiable; though not detailed, anonymity is implausible.",0.6,0,There is no fixed number of interaction rounds or explicit display of such; communities are ongoing.,1,1,"Many communities have rules/norms to address free-riding and 22 at least occasionally use disciplinary processes (discussion, fines, exclusion).",1,N/R,"No quantitative cost per punishment unit (e.g., tokens spent) is specified.",1,N/R,No formal punishment technology or mapping from punishment cost to impact is described.,1,0,"The study discusses punishment and discipline; it does not describe explicit, structured reward mechanisms for cooperation.",0.8,N/A,No formal reward mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No formal reward mechanism is described.,1,N/R,No experimental endowment (tokens/points) is used; contributions concern real-world labor and resources.,1,N/R,No quantitative measure of individual contribution rates (as a fraction of endowment or capacity) is reported.,1,N/R,"No numeric contribution amounts (e.g., hours or units) are systematically reported.",1,N/R,No formal calculation of efficiency relative to a theoretical maximum payoff is presented.,1,N/R,No numerical group payoff or welfare measure is reported.,1,USA,Sample restricted to autonomous intentional communities in the United States.,1,Mean community age ~48 years (adult members); individual-level distribution not fully reported.,Paper reports a rough estimate of mean age of community members as 48.,0.9,N/R,No information on gender composition is provided.,1,"Predominantly WEIRD (White, educated, industrialized, rich, democratic) but no specific education levels reported.",Authors describe sample as WEIRD but do not provide formal education statistics.,0.6,Field experiment,"Data are collected in natural settings of intentional communities, but without experimental manipulation; closest category among options is field-style data collection. Still, it is observational within the field.",0.6,"No formal economic game; qualitative field study using interviews and questionnaires on cooperation, free-riding, and punishment norms in 46 intentional communities.","Methods section details thematic coding of responses about rules, norms, and enforcement rather than a structured game.",1
10.1177_0098628319834208.md,Spring 2016 Section 1 – Extra credit commons dilemma with altruistic punishment,Uses Table 2 row '1. Spring 16 (127)' describing one course section playing the 0/2/6-point game.,1,"Extra-credit choice structure with altruistic punishment option; within-section options: 0 points (punish one random 6-point chooser), 2 points (cooperative), 6 points (defect); 10% 6-point threshold for any points.",Rules described in Methods and the paragraph introducing the 0-point altruistic punishment option; same structure across all sections.,1,True,Classroom exercise with 127 human students in this section.,1,False,Author explicitly notes no experimental design; single-condition classroom demonstration without random assignment or manipulated between-group variables.,1,False,"Conducted in regular course meetings as a teaching exercise, not a controlled lab or field intervention in naturalistic behavior.",0.7,False,No computer or agent-based simulation; all choices made by students.,1,False,No formal mathematical model developed; paper is empirical/educational.,1,127,From Table 2: 'Spring 16 (127)'.,1,1,Exercise is a single-shot choice made once per course section.,1,0,"Players have three discrete options (0, 2, 6 points); they cannot contribute any arbitrary amount.",1,0,All points are additional extra credit; no pre-existing public fund. Endowment effectively starts in private accounts.,0.7,N/R,Not a standard linear public goods game with a multiplier; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Only questions/clarifications about rules allowed; no structured communication among participants to coordinate choices.,0.6,1,"After collection, instructor announces counts of 0-, 2-, and 6-point choosers to the class.",1,0,Punished 6-point chooser is selected at random; no indication that their identity is revealed.,0.7,0,Reward is simply receiving extra credit; no mention of revealing identities of those getting points.,0.7,1,Students clearly know this is a one-time classroom exercise.,0.9,1,0-point option sacrifices own points to remove points from one 6-point chooser; explicitly framed as punishment.,1,2,"Relative to 2-point cooperative baseline, a 0-point chooser forgoes 2 points to punish a 6-point chooser.",0.8,6,Each 0-point chooser causes one randomly selected 6-point chooser to lose all 6 of their points.,0.9,1,Choosing 2 or 6 yields extra credit points if threshold is met and punishment does not remove them.,1,0,Students do not incur a cost to receive extra credit aside from the missed opportunity of alternative choices; no explicit point-cost per reward.,0.8,1,Each rewarded student gains the number of points corresponding to their chosen option (1:1 mapping).,0.8,6,"Maximum attainable extra credit per student is 6 points; choice set 0, 2, or 6 implies effective endowment of 6.",0.8,N/R,Author does not normalize choices as contributions relative to an endowment.,1,N/R,No per-student contribution amounts reported; only counts of each discrete option.,1,N/R,No total payoff measures or theoretical maximum payoffs are computed or reported.,1,N/R,Total extra credit awarded to the class is not reported numerically.,1,United States,"Students from University of Maryland, College Park, MD, USA, as per author affiliation and course context.",0.9,Mean age 19.17 years (SD = 1.37) across all sections,Method section reports overall age statistics for N=795; no section-specific breakdown.,0.8,"Overall sample: 422 women, 241 men; gender for one 131-student section not recorded; no section-specific split.",Reported in Method for combined sections.,0.8,Undergraduate and high-school students in introductory and social psychology courses; this section is an introductory/social psychology section at a university.,"Described as introductory psychology and social psychology courses; this section is part of those university courses, not the separate high-school summer section.",0.8,On site,Exercise conducted during regular in-person class meetings.,1,"Class-level commons dilemma extra-credit game with threshold: if more than 10% choose 6 points, no one receives any points. Third option enables altruistic punishment: each 0-point choice removes all points from one randomly selected 6-point chooser, reducing the effective number of 6-point choosers used to determine threshold and final payoffs.",Summarized from description of the new classroom exercise and Table 2 explanation of 'Effective 6 Points'.,1
