ood per capita rises markedly to 1.8666 and punishers receive very high mean payoff (115.2850) compared with previous scenarios.,From Table 9 (steady state LC and common good per capita 1.8666) and Table 10 (punisher payoff for Experiment 3).,1
10.1177_0951629815586882.md,Punishment treatment,Condition where subjects could punish teammates after seeing their participation choices.,1,Punishment possibility: punishment allowed; Stage structure: participation + punishment stage,Main treatment manipulates whether punishment is possible; this row refers to sessions/rounds with punishment enabled.,1,True,Human undergraduate subjects in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and fixed game structure.,1,True,Conducted in the Princeton Laboratory for Experimental Social Science.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations described.,1,False,Paper includes theoretical discussion but focal results are from lab experiment with human subjects.,1,8,Each session had eight subjects forming two teams of four each round.,1,20,Each treatment consisted of 20 rounds.,1,0,Binary decision X (participate) or Y (abstain); no continuous contribution level.,1,0,"Endowment of 9 tokens is entirely in private account; choice is whether to incur a cost to participate, not to reallocate existing tokens to a public fund.",0.9,N/A,"Voter participation contest, not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.",1,0,No communication between subjects was allowed or described during rounds.,1,1,In period 2 subjects saw the decisions of the three other team members and the election outcome each round.,1,0,Actions of teammates were displayed in random order and design emphasized no link across rounds; identities of punishers/punished not linked to persistent IDs.,0.7,N/A,No reward mechanism in the game.,1,1,"Standard z-Tree implementations and description of 20-round treatments; though not explicitly stated, subjects are typically informed of total periods; coded as shown.",0.6,1,Punishment stage where subjects could pay to reduce teammates' tokens.,1,1,Each punishment cost the punisher one token.,1,2,Each punished subject lost two tokens per punishment.,1,0,No mechanism to pay to increase others' payoffs described.,1,N/A,No rewards in the design.,1,N/A,No rewards in the design.,1,9,"At the start of each round, each subject received an endowment of nine tokens.",1,0.88,"Across all rounds, subjects participated 88 percent of the time in the punishment treatment; participation is the binary analogue of contribution.",1,0,"Decision is binary (participate vs abstain), not an amount of tokens; no per-round token contribution amount defined separate from the 1-token cost. Set to 0 to avoid fabricating an amount.",0.2,N/R,No explicit or computable measure of overall efficiency ratio is reported.,0,N/R,"Only individual average earnings (10.5 tokens per round, 1–13 range) are reported; average group payoff per round not directly given.",0.2,USA,Experiment conducted at Princeton University in the United States; typical subject pool is local undergraduates.,0.9,Undergraduate students; exact ages not reported,Subjects described only as undergraduate students; no specific age statistics provided.,0.7,N/R,"Some analyses mention gender interactions, but no overall gender composition or proportions reported.",0.3,Undergraduate students,Explicitly states that subjects were undergraduate students.,1,On site,"Conducted in a physical laboratory (Princeton Laboratory for Experimental Social Science), not online.",1,"Two-team (Yellow/Brown) voter participation game; teams of 4 selected each round from 8 subjects; each round: endowment 9 tokens; choose X (participate) or Y (abstain); Y yields 1 token; X yields 0 tokens directly; majority of X on team wins prize 3 tokens per member (0 otherwise; 1.5 each in tie); random rematching each round within same 8 subjects (stranger matching, not absolute stranger). Punishment stage only in this treatment: after observing teammates’ X/Y decisions and election outcome, each subject can punish any teammate(s), paying 1 token per punishment to reduce target’s payoff by 2 tokens. Subjects cannot go below zero for the round. 17 sessions, each subject experiences both punishment and control treatments (order counterbalanced).",Summary from Methods and game description sections.,1
10.1177_0951629815586882.md,No-punishment control treatment,Condition where subjects could not punish teammates after seeing their participation choices.,1,Punishment possibility: punishment not allowed; Stage structure: participation stage only with feedback on teammates’ actions,Main manipulation is the possibility of punishment; this row corresponds to the non-punishment treatment.,1,True,Human undergraduate subjects in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with treatments and fixed game structure.,1,True,Conducted in the Princeton Laboratory for Experimental Social Science.,1,False,No simulation-only condition.,1,False,Condition is part of the same human-subject experiment.,1,8,Each session had eight subjects forming two teams of four each round.,1,20,Each treatment consisted of 20 rounds.,1,0,Binary decision X (participate) or Y (abstain); no continuous contribution.,1,0,"Subjects receive a private endowment of 9 tokens; decision is to incur a participation cost, not to move tokens to a public account.",0.9,N/A,Not a public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,"No communication within rounds mentioned; design focuses on anonymous, non-communicating teams.",1,1,"In this treatment, subjects were informed of the actions that their fellow group members took (X or Y).",1,N/A,No punishment stage or opportunity; question of punishment identities does not arise.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same experimental platform and structure as punishment treatment (20-round block); subjects are typically told session length; assumed consistent.,0.6,0,Subjects were not able to punish in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,N/A,No rewards in the design.,1,N/A,No rewards in the design.,1,9,"At the start of each round, each subject received an endowment of nine tokens in all treatments.",1,0.6,"Across all rounds, subjects participated 60 percent of the time in the no-punishment treatment.",1,0,Binary participate/abstain decision; no explicit numeric contribution amount defined beyond the endogenous participation cost.,0.2,N/R,No explicit or computable efficiency index (actual/maximum group payoff) reported.,0,N/R,"Average individual earnings are reported over the whole experiment, not separated by treatment and not aggregated at group level.",0.2,USA,Experiment at Princeton University in the United States.,0.9,Undergraduate students; exact ages not reported,Subjects described as undergraduate students; no age statistics given.,0.7,N/R,Gender is discussed in interaction analyses but no overall composition or counts are provided.,0.3,Undergraduate students,Explicitly states that subjects were undergraduate students.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical lab setting (Princeton Laboratory for Experimental Social Science).,1,"Same voter participation game as in punishment treatment but without punishment stage: 8 subjects per session, two teams (Yellow/Brown) of 4 per round, random rematching each round (stranger matching). Each round: endowment 9 tokens; choose X (participate) or Y (abstain). If choose Y, receive 1 token; if choose X, receive 0 tokens directly. Team with more X choices wins prize 3 tokens per member (0 otherwise; 1.5 each in tie). In this treatment, after first-period decisions, subjects are informed of teammates’ choices but cannot punish.",Summary of control condition from Methods section.,1
10.1628_093245616x14500948554117.md,Kocher and Matzat – Constitutional convention with choice over L and partner/stranger (aggregated),"Comment paper summarizes Kocher and Matzat’s design with a constitutional convention stage where groups choose between punishment and reward characterized by L in {−4, −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2}, and mentions partner vs stranger treatments but does not separate detailed conditions.",0.5,"Constitutional convention choice over sanction mechanism: L ∈ {−4 (punishment), −3, −2, −1, 0 (standard VCM), 1 (reward, 1:1), 2 (reward, 1:2)}; matching protocol: partner vs stranger (both reported as yielding similar contributions).","Text describes that subjects choose between punishments or rewards where L is chosen from {−4,−3,−2,−1,0,1,2} and separately notes stranger and partner treatments.",0.7,True,Design is a laboratory public-good experiment with human subjects voting over mechanisms and then playing the induced game.,0.9,True,"Controlled public-good game with random assignment to treatments (partner vs stranger, mechanism choices).",0.8,True,Described within the context of laboratory public-good experiments; no indication of field implementation.,0.9,False,No mention of agent-based or numerical simulations; only human-subject experiments and theoretical analysis are discussed.,1,False,"Comment references game-theoretic analysis and utility functions from other work, but Kocher and Matzat’s main contribution discussed here is experimental.",0.7,4,"Author explicitly discusses beliefs about “the three other subjects” and “all four subjects,” implying groups of size 4.",0.95,N/R,Comment states the game can be repeated but does not specify the number of rounds used in Kocher and Matzat.,0.2,1,"Standard public-good game description allows C anywhere between 0 and ω, a continuous or discrete range, not all-or-nothing.",0.9,0,Standard VCM: subjects start with private endowment ω and then decide how much to move into public account; initially all is private.,0.9,N/R,"MPCR is denoted α with 0<α<1<nα, but no numerical value is given for Kocher and Matzat’s experiment.",0.3,0,Public-good game template specifies “without any communication” and no exception is described for Kocher and Matzat’s design.,0.8,1,Standard design description notes that at the end of a round participants see either individual or total contributions; implies some summary of others’ behavior is shown.,0.7,0,Information feedback is described as not revealing the identity of group members; implies anonymity for sanctions as well.,0.6,0,Same anonymity argument as for punishment; no indication that rewarded player learns the identity of the rewarder.,0.6,N/R,No information provided on whether total or remaining rounds are displayed.,0.2,1,"Constitutional convention allows choice of punishment mechanisms where subjects spend 1 unit to take L units; L can be negative values such as −4, −3, −2, −1.",0.9,1,Explicit: subject spends 1 unit of his own endowment to take L units from another subject.,1,-4,"Maximum punishment intensity option described is L = −4; this is the largest number of tokens removed per unit spent, so recorded as the punishment technology parameter.",0.7,1,Constitutional convention also includes reward options where a subject can spend 1 unit to add L units to another’s endowment with L>0.,0.9,1,"Explicit: “spend 1 unit of his own endowment, and ... add L units … to the other subject’s endowment.”",1,2,"Reward option includes “double rewards received for each unit allocated to reward,” i.e., up to 2 units to the recipient per 1 unit cost (L=2).",0.9,N/R,"Endowment is denoted ω in the generic description, but no numerical value is given for Kocher and Matzat’s implementation.",0.3,N/R,"Comment mentions relative contributions (e.g., positive contributions possible) but provides no numeric averages or proportions.",0.1,N/R,No specific token amounts or averages are reported.,0.1,N/R,No payoff efficiency measures or ratios are reported.,0.1,N/R,No aggregate or average payoffs are reported.,0.1,N/R,Kocher and Matzat’s sample location is not mentioned in the comment.,0.1,N/R,No age information is provided.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition information is provided.,0.1,N/R,No details on education or student status.,0.1,On site,"Described as a standard laboratory public-good experiment; absent contrary information, inferred to be an on-site lab study.",0.7,"Two-stage design: (1) constitutional convention stage where group members vote over available sanction/reward mechanisms characterized by parameter L in {−4, −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2}; (2) repeated public-good game with chosen mechanism. Standard VCM structure: each round, each of 4 subjects receives endowment ω and chooses contribution C∈[0,ω] to a public account; payoff is (ω−C)+α·ΣCj. Feedback each round includes either individual or total group contributions without identity. Subjects can, in mechanism-enabled stages, target others by spending 1 token to change the target’s payoff by L tokens, where L<0 is punishment and L>0 is reward. There are stranger and partner matching protocols, which surprisingly yield similar contribution patterns.","Summarizes structural elements explicitly mentioned in the comment, including utility-based predictions and noted partner vs stranger comparison.",0.9
10.1016_j.amc.2015.01.040.md,Theoretical model of n-player infinitely repeated prisoners' dilemma with trigger strategies,"The paper only presents a general analytical model and numerical examples; there are no distinct empirical or simulated treatment conditions, so the whole model is treated as a single analytical condition.",0.94,"Common discount factor δ (in [0,1)) and players' mixed strategies (p_i for each player i); group-separations characterized over intervals of δ given fixed p_i.","The key variables varied in the analysis are the common discount factor δ and the individual cooperation probabilities p_i, which determine lower bounds of SPE and resulting group-separations.",0.96,False,"No human participants, surveys, or behavioral data are used; the paper is purely theoretical and mathematical.",1,False,"There is no controlled experimental manipulation with subjects; the work is analytical and uses definitions, lemmas, and theorems.",1,False,"No lab or physical setting is described, as no empirical experiment is run.",1,False,"Although numerical examples are provided, they are direct evaluations of analytical formulas rather than agent-based or stochastic computer simulations.",0.86,True,"The paper develops formal definitions, proves lemmas and theorems, and derives expressions such as δ(p_i,p_j); all results follow from analytical derivations.",1,8,"Example 1 specifies an n-player set N={1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}, which is the only fully parameterized numerical configuration; the general model allows any n>2, but 8 is used in worked examples.",0.78,0,The interactions are modeled as infinitely repeated prisoners' dilemma games; treating as 0 here since the game is infinite-horizon and not a finite number of rounds.,0.9,1,"In the repeated PD, actions are binary (C or D) each period but players choose mixed strategies with any probability p in [0,1], effectively allowing any contribution probability; mapped as allowing any amount.",0.7,N/A,This is a prisoners' dilemma without an explicit endowment-contribution structure as in public goods games.,1,N/R,"Marginal per capita return is not defined; the game is specified by PD payoffs T,R,P,S instead.",1,N/A,No human-subject interaction or communication protocol; purely theoretical model.,1,0,"Information structure is 'perfect monitoring' of actions, but there is no notion of experimental feedback screens or summaries; this variable is not applicable in a non-empirical setting and is set to 0 by convention.",0.5,N/A,"Punishment is implemented as a trigger strategy within the game-theoretic model, not as an experimental punishment stage with identities.",1,N/A,There is no separate reward mechanism with identities; only payoffs from the PD and trigger-based punishment.,1,0,The game is infinite-horizon with discounting; no user interface displaying remaining rounds is described or applicable.,1,1,"The model explicitly uses trigger strategies where deviation leads to a punishment phase with mutual defection forever, serving as a punishment mechanism.",0.95,N/R,"Costs of punishment in terms of explicit tokens per unit are not parameterized; punishment is embedded in the payoff consequences of switching to (D,D).",0.6,N/R,"No parametric mapping like '1 coin reduces 3 coins' is given; punishment is the equilibrium continuation play (D,D).",0.6,0,No separate reward mechanism beyond standard PD payoffs is modeled.,0.95,N/A,"No reward mechanism is present, so its cost is not applicable.",1,N/A,"No reward mechanism is present, so its technology is not applicable.",1,N/R,"The PD is defined directly in terms of payoffs T,R,P,S; no endowment per period is specified.",1,N/R,No empirical data or simulated trajectories of cooperation rates are reported; only equilibrium conditions and group-separation intervals are derived.,1,N/R,Actions are binary C/D with probabilities; no numeric contribution amounts or empirical averages are given.,1,N/R,The paper does not compute group efficiency measures like total payoff relative to maximum; it focuses on equilibrium existence and group-separation.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff numbers for groups or the whole set N are reported; only payoff functions and SPE conditions are specified.,1,N/A,There are no human participants; thus no participant demographics apply.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,N/A,No human subjects are involved.,1,No human,The study is entirely theoretical and analytical with no human-subject or field data.,1,"Infinitely repeated 2-player prisoners' dilemma on a complete graph of n players, with perfect monitoring, common discount factor δ, mixed strategies b_i=(p_i,1−p_i), and trigger strategies enforcing punishment via permanent defection after deviation. Numerical examples use PD payoffs T=3, R=2, P=0, S=−1 and specific cooperation probabilities p1=0.99, p2=0.93, p3=0.86, p4=0.8, p5=0.6, p6=0.5, p7=0.4, p8=0.34 to illustrate group-separations over δ.",Summarizes the detailed game-theoretic setup explicitly described in Sections 2–5 and Example 1.,0.98
