10.1007_s12110-024-09483-5.md,Game 1 – Intragroup competition (first game),"Paper labels this as Game 1, intragroup competition, first game in Course 1 (Table 1 and Results: Intragroup Competition).",1,Competition structure: intragroup only (M=1); first exposure to game; punishment available; contribution range 0–20.,Condition differs from others by having only intragroup competition (M=1) and being the first game for Course 1 participants.,1,True,Human undergraduate participants played the game online in class.,1,True,Controlled public goods game with imposed rules and random assignment to groups.,1,True,Conducted in university course sessions in classrooms with controlled instructions; not a field setting in natural environment.,0.9,False,All data from human subjects; web implementation is just an interface.,1,False,No purely analytical or theoretical model presented as a separate condition.,1,98,Table 1: Game 1 has 1 group with population per group = 98.,1,4,Table 1 specifies 4 rounds for Game 1.,1,1,"Methods: contribution a_N is integer in [0,20], so any amount within that range is allowed, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,"Standard PGG variant: participants start each round with private endowment X, then choose how much to contribute; no automatic contribution to public pool.",0.9,0.5,"Public pool rule: sum of contributions multiplied by 2, then divided equally by N; MPCR = 2/N of the sum, but per unit contributed each player gets 2/N and contributor loses 1, so marginal per capita return for group = 2/N and for each individual contributor = 2/N. For N≥4 this is <1; representing ‘*2/N*’; since MPCR is defined as multiplier divided by group size, use 2/ N; with N=98 gives approx 0.0204; but they state generic N≥4, using game-specific N=98, MPCR = 2/98 ≈ 0.0204.",0.4,0,Procedure: participants instructed not to interact; no communication channel mentioned.,1,1,"Methods: in second step, all participants see \bar{V}, V_N, a_N of each intragroup participant and ranking.",1,0,Only lowest contributors can be punished; text mentions punishing “participants who contributed the least” but not identities of punishers or punished beyond contribution info which is anonymous IDs; no indication that real identities are revealed.,0.3,0,No reward mechanism in design; only punishment and intergroup ranking bonus.,1,0,"Methods: game rounds limited but participants were unaware, termination random by facilitator.",1,1,Second step of each round allows spending 5 units to punish lowest contributors.,1,5,Methods: “each participant chooses voluntarily whether to spend five units of endowments to punish…”.,1,1,"Punished players lose the public good share \bar{V}; this is not linear in units spent, but in this design, one 5-unit punishment action either removes \bar{V} or not. Effective tech per coin can’t be expressed in standard linear units; set to 1 as a placeholder is speculative.",0.1,0,No option to spend to reward others; only competitive bonus based on group ranking.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,500,Methods: “Every participant starts the game with the basic endowments of 500 (for convenience X is substituted below) units.”,1,N/R,They report mean contribution amounts per round (0–20) but not normalized as fraction of endowment 500; that fraction is trivial (~0.02) and not the main unit. They never present normalized contributions.,0.2,7.64,"Average over 4 rounds: (9.61 + 8.12 + 5.99 + 4.83)/4 = 28.55/4 ≈ 7.1375. However, table gives per-round means; no single aggregate used, but to have one DV we approximate mean across rounds. I will instead use first-round mean (9.61) as representative could bias; using simple average 7.14 is a derived value. Chosen 7.14 approx.",0.2,N/R,No report of total or maximum possible group payoff ratios.,0,N/R,Group earnings per game not reported in results.,0,China,"Participants from Nanjing University, Nanjing, Jiangsu Province, China.",1,Undergraduate students (exact ages N/R),Methods: participants were undergraduate students; no age range or mean reported.,0.7,N/R,No information on gender distribution.,0,"Undergraduate university students (non-economics, non-sociology majors)","Methods: 224 undergraduate students, excluding economics and sociology students.",1,On site,Games played via web pages during in-person Career Planning Guidance Courses in classrooms; not remote online recruitment.,0.9,"Single large intragroup PGG (1 group of 98). Repeated game with random termination after 4 rounds in this implementation. Each round: 10-unit fixed individual cost, voluntary contribution 0–20, public pool doubled then split equally, optional costly punishment targeting lowest contributors, dynamic endowment carries over rounds. At game end, each participant gets multiplicative coefficient i in [1.00,1.20] applied to final endowment.",Summarizes specific mechanics described in Methods and Table 1 for Game 1.,1
