10.3390_math12243919.md,Case I – probabilistic punishment without compensation or fine redistribution,Paper defines Case I as the baseline probabilistic punishment model without compensation or fine distribution; all simulations under this mechanism belong to this condition.,1,"Punishment cost G_p and fine level α varied over ranges; key illustrative settings include r=3, group size N=5, G_p from very low (~0) up to values >0.6, α varied (e.g., around 0.5). No β or φ (β=0, φ=0).","Text states that G_p and α are the key parameters in both cases; β and φ are introduced only in Case II. Multiple figures vary G_p (punishment cost) and α (punishment intensity) while keeping r=3, N=5.",0.8,False,All results are from Monte Carlo simulations on lattice networks; no human participants.,1,False,Study is a computational evolutionary game simulation rather than a controlled experiment with human subjects.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely computational model.,1,True,Authors explicitly use Monte Carlo simulations on an L×L lattice to study evolutionary dynamics.,1,False,"While payoff equations are given, results are not derived analytically but via simulation.",1,5,"Groups consist of N=5: 'neighboring individuals form groups of N=5, comprising one focal participant and its four nearest neighbors.'",1,10000000,They state data are collected after at least 10^7 iterations until a stable state; treat this as the effective number of simulated rounds used for statistics.,0.7,1,Cooperators contribute a fixed amount 1 and defectors/punishers contribute 0; this is effectively an all-or-nothing contribution choice.,1,N/A,"No human endowment allocation; agents choose a strategy (C, D, P) rather than moving a pre-endowed budget.",1,0.6,"Group size N=5 and synergy factor r=3, so MPCR = r/N = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No communication between simulated agents is modeled.,1,N/A,Agents imitate neighbors based on payoffs but there is no interface or displayed summaries as in human experiments.,1,N/A,Model does not include identities or visibility of who punished whom; only strategies and payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in Case I.,1,N/A,Simulated agents do not see the number of remaining iterations.,1,1,"Punishers P pay cost G_p to punish defectors D with fine α, with probability w.",1,0.25,"Representative parameter used repeatedly (e.g., Figure 2 uses G_p=0.25). Exact value varies across scans, but 0.25 is a clearly reported example.",0.6,0.5,Fine per defector per punisher is α; illustrative settings repeatedly use α=0.5.,0.6,0,No explicit reward or bonus to cooperators in Case I; only punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Agents do not have a per-round endowment in experimental-currency terms; only normalized contribution cost c=1 is discussed.,0.3,N/R,"Paper reports proportions of strategies (C, D, P) rather than average contribution fraction; C always contributes 1, D and P contribute 0.",0.4,N/R,Only strategy frequencies are plotted; no explicit average contribution amounts over the population are tabulated.,0.4,N/R,"They discuss cooperation levels and payoffs qualitatively, but do not report normalized efficiency (total payoff over maximum) numerically.",0.2,N/R,"Payoff formulas are given per type, but no aggregate group payoff statistics are reported.",0.2,N/A,No human subjects; agents are abstract nodes on a lattice.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Monte Carlo simulation on lattice network; no empirical environment.,1,"Spatial public goods game on L×L lattice with periodic boundary conditions, group size 5 (von Neumann neighborhood), three strategies (C,D,P), synergy factor r=3, contribution cost c=1, punishment probability w = 1 − exp(−5 G_p), strategy update by Fermi imitation with noise K=0.5 and equal initial fractions of C,D,P.",All these details are explicitly specified in the Materials and Methods section and reused in Case I simulations.,1
10.3390_math12243919.md,Case II – probabilistic punishment with compensation and fine redistribution,Paper defines Case II as an extension of Case I that adds a compensation mechanism (β) for failed punishment and fine distribution (φ) for successful punishment.,1,"Punishment cost G_p and fine level α varied; additional independent parameters are compensation coefficient β and fine distribution coefficient φ. Representative settings: r=3, N=5, α=0.5, G_p varied (e.g., 0.12–0.84), with β fixed at values like 0.1 or 0.15 and φ fixed at values like 0, 0.25 in different simulations.","Authors state key parameters are G_p and α; in Case II they further vary β and φ to assess compensation and fine distribution effects (Figures 2–3, 7–11).",0.85,False,All Case II results are obtained via Monte Carlo simulations; no human subjects are used.,1,False,The study is computational rather than an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely numerical simulations.,1,True,Case II uses the same lattice-based Monte Carlo evolutionary simulations as Case I.,1,False,"They provide payoff equations but analyze behavior using simulations, not closed-form analytical solutions.",1,5,Same spatial public goods game structure as Case I with N=5 group size.,1,10000000,They state that for 'this chapter' (covering both cases) data are collected after at least 10^7 iterations until a stable state.,0.7,1,Cooperators contribute a fixed amount 1 while D and P contribute 0; no intermediate contribution levels.,1,N/A,No human initial endowment; agents are defined by strategy only.,1,0.6,"Synergy factor r=3 and group size N=5, so MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",1,N/A,No communication modeled among agents.,1,N/A,Simulation does not include any user interface; agents only observe neighbors' payoffs for imitation.,1,N/A,Identities are not modeled; only local interactions and payoffs matter.,1,N/A,"Rewards are implemented as automatic payoff transfers (compensation and fine distribution), not as visible identity-based rewards.",1,N/A,Simulated agents do not have information about the total number of iterations.,1,1,"Punishers P pay cost G_p to punish defectors D with fine α, with success probability w dependent on G_p.",1,0.25,"Case II is directly compared to Case I at parameter set r=3, G_p=0.25, α=0.5 (Figure 2), making 0.25 a representative cost value, though other G_p values are also explored.",0.6,0.5,"Fine α per punisher-defector pair; α=0.5 is used as a standard value in multi-figure comparisons (e.g., Figure 2, Figure 3).",0.6,1,"Case II includes two reward-like mechanisms: (i) cooperators receive compensation β(1−w) from punishers when punishment fails, and (ii) punishers receive a share φ of fines α N_D when punishment succeeds.",1,0.1,"Compensation parameter β is an effective per-cooperator reward funded by punishers; a commonly used value in Case II is β=0.1 (e.g., Figure 2 caption mentions β=0.1, φ=0.25).",0.6,0.25,"Fine distribution coefficient φ determines how much of paid fines go to punishers; φ=0.25 is frequently used alongside β=0.1 (e.g., Figure 2 and others).",0.6,N/R,Only a contribution cost c=1 is given; no explicit per-round endowment in tokens is reported.,0.3,N/R,"Outcomes are reported as steady-state fractions of C, D, P. Although overall contribution fraction equals share of C (since C contributes 1 and others 0), no explicit numeric contribution rates are tabulated.",0.5,N/R,They do not provide average numeric contribution amounts; only population shares of cooperators.,0.4,N/R,No quantitative efficiency metric (total payoff relative to maximum) is reported; discussion is qualitative.,0.2,N/R,"Group payoffs could be computed from strategy frequencies and payoff formulas, but the paper does not present concrete numeric group payoff results.",0.3,N/A,No human participants are used in Case II simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Case II is also implemented via Monte Carlo simulations on a lattice network with artificial agents.,1,"Same spatial public goods game as Case I on an L×L lattice with periodic boundary conditions, group size 5, three strategies (C,D,P), synergy factor r=3, contribution cost c=1, punishment success probability w = 1 − exp(−5 G_p). Case II modifies payoffs by adding: (i) compensation to cooperators when punishment fails: each cooperator’s payoff includes +(1−w) β N_p / N_c; (ii) net cost to punishers of compensation −β(1−w); and (iii) fine distribution when punishment succeeds: punishers gain +w α N_D φ. Strategy updating uses the same Fermi rule with noise K=0.5 and equal initial fractions of C,D,P.",Directly taken from the Case II model description and payoff equations in the Methods and Results sections.,1
