10.1371_journal.pone.0307832.md,"PFN – Penalties without communication in Phase 1, facilitated communication with penalties in Phase 2, no communication/penalties in Phase 3","Table 1: PFN (Phase 1 Pen, Phase 2 Facil (Pen), Phase 3 N) described as 'Penalties with no communication followed by facilitated communication and no communication'.",1,Initial regulatory environment: regulatory abuse (peer sanctioning with no communication) in Phase 1; then facilitated communication with peer sanctioning (restorative-justice guidance) in Phase 2; then no communication/penalties in Phase 3.,PFN combines early regulatory abuse with later facilitated discussion of enforcement.,1,True,Human lab experiment condition.,1,True,Manipulated treatment with random assignment.,1,True,Conducted in lab setting.,1,False,Human subjects central.,1,False,No dedicated analytical model.,1,4,Groups of four players.,1,9,Nine rounds per group.,1,1,Continuous harvesting allowed.,0.9,0,No public contribution start.,0.9,N/R,No MPCR specified.,1,1,Facilitated communication with penalties in Phase 2.,1,1,Same interface: visible harvest totals and penalties.,0.9,1,"Sanctions targeted by Player Number, visible to group.",0.9,0,No reward option.,1,1,Assumed same feedback on round progress.,0.5,1,Sanctions available in Phases 1 and 2.,1,0.02,Each sanction costs $0.02.,1,0.04,Each sanction imposes $0.04 penalty.,1,0,Rewards absent in design.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No initial endowment stated.,1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,208.83,"Median Net Tokens 1 for PFN (Phase 1, penalties without communication) from Table 2 as baseline cooperation under initial regulatory abuse.",0.8,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio per treatment.,0.4,208.83,Median net tokens in Phase 1 for PFN.,0.8,United States,Same participant pool.,1,"Mean 19.00 years (SD 1.81), undergraduates",From Participants section.,1,61% female; remainder male/other not further specified,Overall sample-level figure.,0.9,Undergraduate university students,Recruitment from undergraduate courses.,1,On site,In-person lab experiment.,1,"Same foraging game; PFN experiences 3 rounds of penalties without any communication, then 3 rounds with facilitated communication and penalties emphasizing restorative justice (hearings, justification, reconciliation, coordinated sanctions), then 3 rounds without either tool.",Combines treatment ordering (Table 1) and facilitation description.,1
10.1007_s00355-010-0493-2.md,Analytical model – personal enforcement (Strategy 1),Paper defines Strategy 1 as the baseline trigger-strategy with personal enforcement in the repeated matching Prisoner's Dilemma.,1,"Enforcement mechanism: personal enforcement only; population size n; matching probability p=1/n; discount factor δ; PD payoffs a,c,b with a>c>0>b and a+b<2c.","Strategy 1 varies only via structural parameters n, p, δ, and PD payoffs governing incentives; enforcement mechanism fixed to personal enforcement.",1,False,No human subjects; purely theoretical repeated-game analysis.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; a formal game-theoretic model.,1,False,No lab or field data; analytic model only.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations are reported.,1,True,Characterizes sequential equilibrium conditions and critical discount factor analytically.,1,0,"Model uses a generic population N={1,…,n+1} with odd n>1; no single fixed numeric value.",0.7,0,"Game is infinitely repeated over periods t=1,2,... ; not a finite number of rounds.",1,0,"Stage game is a 2×2 PD with binary actions C or D; there is no continuous contribution choice, so all-or-nothing contribution concept is inapplicable.",0.7,N/A,No contribution endowment structure; only discrete PD actions C and D and no human subjects.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,N/A,No explicit communication stage between human subjects; purely theoretical model.,1,0,Information is only about own partner’s past behavior via private states; no summaries of others’ earnings or behavior are described.,0.8,0,"Players always know the identity of their current match, but there is no institutional punishment identification mechanism as in lab PGG punishment stages.",0.5,0,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,0,Infinite-horizon model with discounting; players do not observe a finite horizon or remaining rounds.,1,1,"Punishment is implemented via future play of D in rematches (trigger strategy) after a deviation; i.e., continuation payoffs act as punishment.",1,N/R,"Punishment cost is implicit in foregone cooperative payoffs, not parametrized as ‘cost per punishment point’.",0.2,N/R,No explicit one-to-one punishment technology; punishment is via changing continuation play path.,0.2,0,No mechanism where players pay to increase others’ payoffs beyond cooperation.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this model.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this model.,1,N/R,"Payoff matrix specified by parameters a,c,b; no explicit token endowment per round.",0.7,N/R,Outcomes are PD actions and continuation payoffs; no contribution rate as in PGGs.,1,N/R,No continuous or token-based contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,"Paper discusses payoffs and minimal discount factors, not normalized efficiency measures.",0.7,N/R,Provides generic payoffs per match; no aggregated group payoff reported as a dependent variable.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical repeated-game analysis; no empirical implementation.,1,Infinite-horizon random-matching Prisoner’s Dilemma with private monitoring; each period players are matched in pairs with probability p=1/n and play actions C or D with payoffs satisfying a>c>0>b and a+b<2c; personal enforcement via partner-specific states supports cooperation if δ ≥ δ⁰=(a−c)/(a−(1−p)c).,Summarizes the core formal structure of Strategy 1 as described in Section 2 and Proposition 1.,1
10.1007_s00355-010-0493-2.md,Analytical model – community enforcement with exogenous truthful experience-sharing (Strategy 2),Paper defines Strategy 2 as a community enforcement strategy assuming exogenous truthful experience-sharing and analyzes conditions for cooperation.,1,"Enforcement mechanism: community enforcement with exogenous truthful labels; punishment length T; population size n (only via beliefs, not δ*); discount factor δ; PD payoffs a,c,b with a>c>0>b and a+b<2c.","Strategy 2 is characterized by labels S={0,…,T}, exogenous truthful sharing, and generic structural parameters.",1,False,No human subjects; fully theoretical.,1,False,Not an implemented experiment; game-theoretic equilibrium derivation.,1,False,Model only.,1,False,No simulation runs reported.,1,True,"Derives minimal discount factor δ* and optimal punishment length T* analytically (Proposition 2, Appendix).",1,0,"Population N={1,…,n+1} with odd n>1; analysis emphasizes that δ* does not depend on n.",0.7,0,Infinite-horizon repeated-matching game.,1,0,Binary PD actions C or D; no graded contributions.,0.7,N/A,No contribution endowment concept; and no human subjects.,1,N/R,"Prisoner’s Dilemma, not a linear public good.",1,N/A,No communication stage between human players; labels are assumed to be provided by some institution (“dark spots on foreheads”).,1,0,"Players only observe the label of their current partner, not population summaries of payoffs.",0.8,0,"Identity of current partner is known, but there is no separate punishment stage with identification akin to PGG punishment; punishment is embedded in strategy contingent on labels.",0.5,0,No explicit costly reward mechanism.,1,0,Infinite horizon; no terminal round information.,1,1,Players with positive labels are punished through receiving low payoffs (b or 0) for T periods.,1,N/R,"Punishment cost is embedded in the payoff matrix and continuation values, not parametrized as cost per punishment unit.",0.2,N/R,No explicit mapping of punishment tokens to payoff reduction.,0.2,0,No separate rewarding institution; ‘repenting’ structure benefits punishers but is not a paid reward choice.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Model only specifies per-period payoffs a,c,b.",0.7,N/R,No numeric contributions; only cooperation/defection and associated payoffs and discount factors are analyzed.,1,N/R,No contribution levels.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency ratios; focuses on feasibility of cooperative equilibria and minimal δ.,0.7,N/R,Only generic continuation payoffs V(·) are expressed symbolically.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Analytical repeated-game model only.,1,"Infinite-horizon random-matching PD with exogenous truthful public labels S={0,…,T}. Label 0 = innocent, m>0 = in punishment phase with m periods remaining. Strategy 2: cooperate if partner label is 0, defect otherwise; guilty players ‘repent’ by playing C against innocent partners while receiving b<0 and allowing innocents to earn a>c>0. Community enforcement with repenting punishments supports cooperation if δ ≥ δ* = max[(a−c)/a, b/(b−c)], independent of population size n or matching probability p.",Synthesizes Strategy 2 description and Proposition 2 conditions.,1
10.1007_s00355-010-0493-2.md,Analytical model – community enforcement with endogenous unilateral experience-sharing (Strategy 3),Paper defines Strategy 3 as the main equilibrium with endogenous unilateral announcements and personal punishment for untruthful reporting.,1,"Experience-sharing regime: endogenous unilateral reporting with personal punishment for lying; punishment length T; population size n (via p=1/n); discount factor δ; PD payoffs a,c,b with a>c>0>b and a+b<2c.","Strategy 3 differs from Strategy 2 by making experience-sharing endogenous, adding a cheap-talk announcement stage and personal punishments for misreporting; other structural parameters as in earlier sections.",1,False,No data; purely theoretical.,1,False,No implemented experimental design; model only.,1,False,No lab or field work.,1,False,No simulations are reported; results obtained analytically.,1,True,"Derives equilibrium Strategy 3 and critical discount factor δ** analytically (Proposition 3, Appendix).",1,0,"As before, population is N={1,…,n+1} with odd n>1; results hinge on p=1/n and asymptotics as n→∞.",0.7,0,Infinitely repeated game.,1,0,Binary C/D actions.,0.7,N/A,No contribution endowment or human subjects.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,There is a cheap-talk announcement subgame but no human chat in an experiment; this is part of the theoretical mechanism.,1,0,"Players learn only the single announcement about their current partner from the previous match, not population summaries.",0.8,0,Punishment occurs endogenously via prescribed actions in rematches; no separate punishment stage where identities of punished players are newly revealed.,0.5,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,0,Infinite horizon; no known finite number of remaining rounds.,1,1,Both community punishment (via labels and repenting) and personal punishment for lying (unfavorable play in rematches) are integral parts of Strategy 3.,1,N/R,Costs are implicit in continuation payoffs and parameter b; no per-unit punishment price specified.,0.2,N/R,Punishment technology is strategy-contingent and not a linear mapping from punishment units to payoff losses.,0.2,0,"While cooperators can benefit from repenters, there is no separate costly reward choice.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Only payoff parameters a,c,b are given.",0.7,N/R,"Model outcome assessed via feasibility of cooperative equilibrium and critical δ**, not contribution measures.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts modeled.,1,N/R,The paper compares usefulness via discount factors and punishment severity; no explicit efficiency ratios reported.,0.7,N/R,"Continuation payoffs are given symbolically (e.g., [(1−δ^T)b+δ^T c]/(1−δ)+pδ^T(b−c)); no numeric group payoff outcome presented as a DV.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,This is a theoretical mechanism-design style repeated-game analysis.,1,"Infinite-horizon random-matching PD with private monitoring and an added unilateral experience-sharing (cheap-talk) substage. After each match, partner j announces i’s label x_i∈S={0,…,T}. States s_i^j∈S×S×{0,1} track j’s last experience with i, j’s last announcement about i, and whether i previously reported truthfully. Strategy 3 prescribes: actions based on current received announcement x_{μ(j,t−1)} and relation-specific state s_j^i; forgiving, repenting community punishments based on labels; and minimal personal punishments (via unfavorable play in rematches) for untruthful announcements. Cooperation (C,C) can be supported in sequential equilibrium if δ ≥ δ** > δ*, where δ** depends on p=1/n and converges to δ* as n→∞; thus community enforcement with endogenous experience-sharing is slightly less effective than with exogenous sharing, but becomes similar in large populations.",Summarizes the core structure and main comparative-statics result for Strategy 3 from Section 3 and Proposition 3.,1
10.1007_s11238-024-09977-9.md,"UP_30,60 (Uncoordinated punishment)","One of the four between-subjects treatments; uncoordinated punishment with λ1=0.30, λ2=0.60.",1,"Punishment institution: uncoordinated punishment with λ0=0, λ1=0.30, λ2=0.60 (UP_30,60).","Treatment varies punishment technology across four conditions; this row is the UP_30,60 condition.",1,True,Human participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with between-subjects treatments.,1,True,"Sessions conducted at LINEEX laboratory, University of Valencia.",1,False,No mention of computer-only or agent-based simulations.,1,False,Paper includes theory but this row describes the human-subjects experiment.,1,3,Two investors and one allocator per group in all treatments.,1,15,Subjects played the team investment game for 15 periods.,1,0,"Investors choose binary invest/not invest, but allocator chooses any x∈[0,30]; contributions (returns) are continuous, so game is not all-or-nothing in general.",0.7,0,Each player is endowed with 20 ECUs kept privately; investors pay 5 ECUs only if they choose to invest.,1,N/R,"This is an investment/trust-style game, not a linear public good; no MPCR defined.",0,0,No communication or chat described; only feedback screens.,0.9,1,"End-of-period screen informed subjects about decisions of each investor, amount returned, and punishment decisions.",1,1,"Feedback shows punishment decisions of each investor in the group, implying identities within trio are known.",0.9,0,"No reward mechanism, only punishment.",1,1,Standard z-Tree repeated-game implementations and mention of a 15-period game strongly imply subjects knew horizon; not explicitly stated but typical.,0.6,1,Stage 3 punishment possible in this treatment.,1,5,Punishment decision has an individual cost of 5 ECUs in all punishment treatments.,1,0.3,Fine-to-fee ratio when one investor punishes: allocator payoff multiplied by (1-0.30); we record λ1=0.30 as punishment intensity per active punisher in