10.1073_pnas.2207029120.md,Baseline numerical simulation with CE and DE enforcer strategies,"Fig. 3B,E and associated text describe simulations where only the two main enforcer strategies (CE=0101 and DE=0011) are allowed; the baseline parameter set is one full simulation condition.",0.9,"Parameter set: baseline vs. alternative; this row corresponds to baseline (b=4, c=1, f=0.3, v=0.1, l=5, δ=0.9, κ=8, w=2, τ=0.3, p=2, ψ=0.7, μ=0.005, η=0.01, ε=0.05, (γ_P,E, γ_E, γ_P)=(0.1,0.3,0.6)); enforcer strategy set restricted to {CE, DE}.","Condition is defined by specific numerical parameter values and the restriction to two enforcer strategies, contrasted with an alternative parameter set.",1,False,All results are from an evolutionary game-theoretic model and computer simulations with no human participants.,1,False,"No controlled behavioral experiment; instead, parameterized simulations of a theoretical model.",1,False,No physical or online lab with human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Section 'Dynamic Numerical Results' and 'Simulations and Figures' state that they simulate the learning process and approximate the invariant distribution with code.,1,False,"This row refers to the numerical simulation condition, not the analytical derivations, even though those exist elsewhere in the paper.",0.9,50,Methods: 'in a population of 50 agents.',1,10,"They set δ=0.9 and say: 'our baseline continuation probability, δ=0.9, implies that the expected length of the repeated game is 10 rounds.' Using expected rounds as effective number.",0.8,1,"Model is not framed as a voluntary contribution game but a prisoner's dilemma with binary cooperate/defect choices, not continuous contributions; mapping to this field is not applicable but binary implies no graded contributions.",0.3,N/A,No public-goods contribution structure with an initial endowment allocation; model is a PD with benefit b and cost c.,1,N/R,No MPCR or public-good multiplier is defined anywhere; interactions are PD with parameters b and c instead.,1,N/A,No human participants or interface; communication between simulated agents is not modeled as chat.,1,N/A,"User-interface style feedback about others' earnings is irrelevant in this purely simulated, fully specified state-based model.",1,N/A,No human identities; enforcers observe reputation variables algorithmically rather than identities in an interface.,1,N/A,There is no reward mechanism and no human identities.,1,N/A,Simulated agents are strategy automata; no interface revealing number of rounds.,1,1,"Enforcers can punish producers (cost p to producer, v to enforcer), and enforcers can attack each other (cost l, stealing fraction ψ of revenue).",1,0.1,Variable punishment cost v for punishing each producer is set to v=0.1 in baseline parameters.,1,2,Loss from being punished is p=2 in the baseline; interpreted as punishment impact per punishment unit.,1,0,"Model includes only punishment and attacks, no positive rewards or subsidies.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is modeled.,1,N/R,"Producers have an autarky payoff w=2 and can gain or lose from PD and taxation, but a per-round 'endowment' in the sense of discrete tokens is not specified.",0.3,N/R,"Outcome measures reported are shares of cooperating producers (CP), shares of enforcer strategies (CE, DE) and proportions of enforcers; no normalized contribution rates are provided.",0.4,N/R,Actions are binary cooperate/defect within a PD; no quantitative contribution amount per agent is reported beyond this.,0.4,N/R,Paper reports that payoffs are higher in the cooperation equilibrium than in defection equilibrium but does not provide explicit normalized efficiency (payoff/max payoff) for simulations.,0.5,N/R,"While analytical expressions for payoffs exist, the numerical simulations report only shares of strategies, not group payoff numbers.",0.5,N/A,No human participants are involved in the simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from evolutionary-game-theoretic simulations with artificial agents.,1,"Repeated three-step game each period with random matching: (1) producers play pairwise PD with parameters b=4, c=1, and autarky payoff w=2; (2) each producer pair matched to an enforcer who taxes fraction τ=0.3 of their production and may punish, inflicting p=2 at variable cost v=0.1; (3) enforcers randomly paired in meta-enforcement, may attack to steal fraction ψ=0.7 of rival’s tax revenue causing loss l=5. Reputation-based CE strategy punishes all defectors, no cooperators, attacks only enforcers in bad standing; DE strategy never punishes producers and always attacks other enforcers. Learning via noisy best response with parameters μ=0.005, η=0.01, ε=0.05, continuation probability δ=0.9 (expected 10 rounds), punishment phase length κ=8, revision probabilities (γ_P,E, γ_E, γ_P)=(0.1, 0.3, 0.6). Simulation over 10^6 periods with 50 agents.",Summarizes the detailed game structure and baseline parameters specific to this simulation condition.,1
10.1073_pnas.2207029120.md,Alternative numerical simulation with CE and DE enforcer strategies,"Same simulation design as baseline but with an alternative parameter set (Fig. 3B,E; labeled 'alternative parameter values').",0.9,"Parameter set: baseline vs. alternative; this row corresponds to alternative (b=3, c=2, f=0.4, v=0.3, l=4, δ=0.9, κ=8, w=2, τ=0.3, p=2, ψ=0.7, μ=0.005, η=0.01, ε=0.05, (γ_P,E, γ_E, γ_P)=(0.1,0.3,0.6)); enforcer strategy set restricted to {CE, DE}.","Independent variables are the changed payoff and cost parameters relative to the baseline, under the same learning dynamic and restricted enforcer strategy set.",1,False,Simulation-only condition with no human data.,1,False,Not a behavioral experiment; a computational experiment on a theoretical model.,1,False,No lab or online human environment.,1,True,Described explicitly as numerical simulations to approximate the invariant distribution.,1,False,"This row reflects a numerical simulation condition, not the closed-form analytical results.",0.9,50,"Methods state 'in a population of 50 agents' for all simulations, including alternative parameter runs.",1,10,"Continuation probability δ=0.9 is unchanged, implying expected length of 10 rounds as in baseline.",0.8,1,Same PD structure with binary cooperate/defect decision; no continuous contribution levels.,0.3,N/A,No public-goods framing or initial endowment allocations.,1,N/R,No MPCR concept defined; PD specified by b and c.,1,N/A,Artificial agents; no chat mechanism.,1,N/A,No interface; internal state fully determines updates.,1,N/A,No human identities in simulations.,1,N/A,No reward option and no human identities.,1,N/A,Agents are not shown round counts; δ determines stochastic continuation.,1,1,"Enforcers can punish producers with severity p and cost v, and can attack each other causing loss l and resource transfer.",1,0.3,"In the alternative parameter set, v=0.3 (variable cost per punished producer).",1,2,"Loss from being punished is p=2, unchanged from baseline.",1,0,"Model includes punishment and attacks only, not rewards.",1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards are modeled.,1,N/R,"As in baseline, only autarky payoff w and PD payoffs are defined; no token endowment.",0.3,N/R,"Results are reported as dominance of defecting producers (DP) and defection enforcers (DE), not as numeric contribution rates.",0.4,N/R,Actions are binary cooperate/defect; no numeric contribution quantity reported.,0.4,N/R,"Comparative statements (cooperation harder to attain, more defection) but no explicit efficiency metric.",0.5,N/R,No explicit simulated group payoff numbers are shown; only shares of strategies.,0.5,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,This is a computational model with agent-based simulations only.,1,"Same repeated three-step enforcement game as in the baseline CE/DE simulation, but with alternative parameters: benefit of cooperation b=3, cost c=2, fixed information cost f=0.4, variable punishment cost v=0.3, loss from being attacked l=4. All other parameters remain δ=0.9, κ=8, w=2, τ=0.3, p=2, ψ=0.7, μ=0.005, η=0.01, ε=0.05, (γ_P,E, γ_E, γ_P)=(0.1,0.3,0.6). Time averages computed over 10^6 periods with 50 agents and seven random initial conditions; outcome shows DP dominant among producers and DE among enforcers.",Summarizes how this condition differs from the baseline while keeping the same game structure and learning process.,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00137-3.md,Lab experiment – Second-Party condition (2P),Paper describes a laboratory experiment with two between-subject conditions; this row corresponds to the Second-Party (2P) condition.,1,"Punisher identity: second party (victim of transgression); Amount taken by Player A in Stage 1: 0, 5, or 10 EMU; Punishment level chosen by Player B in Stage 2 (0–100% of A’s earnings).","Between-subject manipulation is whether the punisher is second or third party; within condition, behavior is measured conditional on amount taken and punishment level.",0.96,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects at NYU Abu Dhabi.,1,True,Design involves random assignment to roles and treatment (2P vs 3P) with controlled payoff structure.,1,True,Conducted at the Social Science Experimental Laboratory at NYU Abu Dhabi.,1,False,No mention of agent-based or computer-only simulations; all data from human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model as primary method; focus is on experimental data.,1,2,In 2P game there are Player A and Player B interacting; no third party punisher is used.,1,1,They explicitly state that the game is played once to rule out strategic motives.,1,1,"No contribution decision to a public good; only discrete take and percentage punishment choices. Field defined for contribution games, but here players can choose any punishment percentage in 10% increments, not an all-or-nothing contribution.",0.2,N/R,No public good contribution; only taking and punishment; default contribution proportion not applicable in this taking game.,1,N/R,No public good or linear return from contributions is defined.,1,0,No mention of any communication being allowed between participants.,0.95,0,Design description does not mention feedback on others’ earnings or aggregate punishment beyond direct outcomes; appears one-shot and anonymous.,0.7,0,Players are labeled A and B only for roles; no indication that real identities are revealed.,0.9,0,There is no reward stage or mechanism.,1,1,They are told the game is played only once; thus number of rounds is known.,0.9,1,Stage 2 allows Player B to reduce Player A’s earnings; Stage 3 allows Player A to reduce Player B’s earnings.,1,5,"Player B must pay 5 EMU to reduce A’s income, irrespective of the percentage; similarly for A in Stage 3.",1,0,"They only state that paying 5 EMU allows choosing any percentage reduction (0–100%); no linear ‘per unit’ technology (per EMU reduced) is specified, so per-unit magnitude cannot be defined.",0.2,0,No option to increase others’ earnings is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,35,"At Stage 1, Players A and B each have 30 EMU. For punishment stages, a separate 5 EMU endowment is provided to the punisher (Stage 2: Player B; Stage 3: Player A). For a single player’s maximum disposable resources when in punisher role, 30 + 5 = 35 EMU.",0.7,N/R,Outcome is amount of taking and (counter-)punishment in a taking game; no contribution to a public good is defined.,1,N/R,No contribution variable; main dependent variables are likelihood and severity of counter-punishment.,1,N/R,"They report differences in earnings and counter-punishment percentages, but no explicit efficiency ratio relative to maximum is given.",0.3,N/R,Average earnings reported only pooled across treatments (103 AED) and not by condition or group-level payoff.,0.3,Highly diverse international student sample in UAE,Participants were NYU Abu Dhabi students; university notes more than 115 nationalities; individual country composition not detailed.,0.6,N/R,No age information reported; only that subjects are students.,0,N/R,Gender composition is not mentioned.,0,University students,"Subjects are drawn from the pool of volunteers at NYU Abu Dhabi, indicating they are university students.",0.95,On site,The experiment was conducted in-person at the Social Science Experimental Laboratory; they distinguish this from the separate online surveys.,1,"Three-stage one-shot taking and punishment game. Stage 1: Player A takes 0, 5, or 10 EMU from Player B (both start with 30 EMU). Stage 2: Player B, as second party, can pay 5 EMU (from an additional 5 EMU endowment) to reduce Player A’s earnings by a chosen percentage between 0% and 100% in 10% increments, using the strategy method conditional on A’s take choice. Stage 3: Player A receives 5 EMU and can similarly pay 5 EMU to reduce B’s earnings by 0–100% in 10% increments, using the strategy method for all possible 11 punishment levels from Stage 2. Game is played once, between-subject design with 2P vs 3P conditions; this condition uses second-party punishment.",Summarized directly from the detailed description of the experimental design in Section 2.1.,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00137-3.md,Lab experiment – Third-Party condition (3P),Paper describes a laboratory experiment with two between-subject conditions; this row corresponds to the Third-Party (3P) condition.,1,"Punisher identity: third party (unaffected observer from another group); Amount taken by focal Player A in Stage 1: 0, 5, or 10 EMU; Punishment level chosen by third-party Player B in Stage 2 (0–100% of a different Player A’s earnings).","Between-subject manipulation is whether the punisher is second or third party; within this condition, main independent variables include amount taken and punishment level.",0.96,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants as in 2P condition.,1,True,Controlled experimental protocol with random assignment to roles and to the 3P condition.,1,True,Conducted at the Social Science Experimental Laboratory at NYU Abu Dhabi.,1,False,No non-human simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical model reported as a separate study.,1,3,"In 3P, there is Player A (transgressor), a Player B victim in A’s original pair (whose endowment can be taken), and a third-party Player B (from another group) who actually punishes the transgressor, making three relevant players in the punishment structure.",0.75,1,Same one-shot three-stage game; paper states the game is played once.,1,1,"No contribution stage; punishment percentages can vary continuously (in 10% steps), not all-or-nothing contributions.",0.2,N/R,No public good contribution decision in this taking/punishment game.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return is defined.,1,0,"As in 2P, there is no mention of communication.",0.95,0,No feedback summaries of others beyond direct payoffs are reported.,0.7,0,Only anonymous role labels are used; identities are not revealed.,0.9,0,There is no reward mechanism in 3P either.,1,1,Participants are informed the game is played only once.,0.9,1,Third-party Player B can punish a different Player A; then focal Player A can counter-punish this third party.,1,5,Punishment technology (pay 5 EMU to reduce another’s income by a chosen percentage) is stated to be identical across 2P and 3P conditions.,1,0,"As in 2P, the 5 EMU payment allows any percentage reduction between 0% and 100%; per-unit reduction per EMU is not defined.",0.2,0,No option to increase others’ payoffs.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this condition.,1,N/A,Rewards do not exist in this condition.,1,35,"Same monetary amounts as in 2P: initial 30 EMU per player, plus 5 EMU endowment when in punisher role.",0.7,N/R,"Dependent variables are punishment and counter-punishment, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decision exists.,1,N/R,"They compare earnings impacts of counter-punishment across conditions, but do not present an efficiency ratio.",0.3,N/R,Average earnings are reported overall (103 AED) but not separately by condition or per group.,0.3,Highly diverse international student sample in UAE,"Same recruitment pool as 2P (NYU Abu Dhabi students, 115+ nationalities).",0.6,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,0,University students,Participants came from NYU Abu Dhabi’s student subject pool.,0.95,On site,Same in-person lab setting as 2P condition.,1,"Structure mirrors 2P: Stage 1 is a taking game where Player A can take 0, 5, or 10 EMU from the Player B in their group (both initially have 30 EMU). In Stage 2, a Player B from a different group, who observed another Player A’s transgression, receives 5 EMU and can pay 5 EMU to reduce that other Player A’s income by 0–100% in 10% increments (strategy method). This makes Player B an unaffected third party relative to the focal Player A whose behavior in Stage 1 determined the focal payoffs. In Stage 3, the punished Player A can receive 5 EMU and decide on counter-punishment (same percentage reduction technology) targeted at the third-party punisher who actually punished them, not the Player B in their original pair. Matching ensures identical monetary incentives for punishment decisions in 2P and 3P.",Summarized from Section 2.1 where the 3P matching protocol and sequence of moves are described.,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00137-3.md,Online Survey 1 – Ulterior motive attribution (second vs third party),Survey 1 is an online vignette-based study assessing perceived bias of punishment in second- vs third-party scenarios.,1,"Scenario punisher type in vignette: (a) second-party punisher (Person B, victim of taking) vs (b) third-party punisher (Person C, unaffected observer). Within-subject comparison of perceived bias.",Only manipulation is whose role it is to punish in the vignettes; all monetary outcomes are held constant.,1,True,Survey of human respondents' perceptions.,1,True,"Controlled, randomized (within-subject) presentation of hypothetical scenarios with systematic variation of punisher identity.",0.9,False,"Described as an online survey, not conducted in the physical lab.",1,False,No simulated agents; only human responses.,1,False,No analytical modeling.,1,3,"Vignettes mention Person A (transgressor), Person B (victim and/or potential punisher), and Person C (third party).",1,1,Each vignette describes a one-shot interaction.,1,1,No contribution or strategy choice over partial contributions; only fixed actions described (taking and punishment).,0.5,N/A,No actual game decisions or public good contributions by participants; purely hypothetical vignette judgments.,1,N/A,No contribution mechanism.,1,0,Respondents do not interact with each other in the survey.,1,0,No payoff summaries or behavior of others are provided beyond the vignette itself.,1,0,"Vignettes use anonymous labels (Person A, B, C), not identities of real participants.",1,0,No reward mechanism in the vignettes.,1,1,Scenario clearly indicates a one-time interaction; no repetition.,0.9,1,Both scenarios describe Person B or C reducing Person A’s earnings by $20 after A takes $10.,1,20,"Vignettes state that the punisher does not keep the $20; they give up $20 of A’s payoff with no benefit, so from A’s perspective it is a 20-unit reduction; from punisher perspective, the cost is opportunity to gain nothing while A loses 20. Interpreted here as 20 units spent to reduce A’s income by 20.",0.4,1,"Punishment is described as a fixed reduction of $20 in A’s earnings, implying 1:1 reduction per unit of punishment cost, if cost is interpreted in payoff units.",0.4,0,No option to increase anyone’s earnings in the vignettes.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is described.,1,30,Each person in the vignettes receives $30 at the start.,1,N/A,"Survey measures perceived bias, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions are made.,1,N/A,No efficiency metric is computed; vignette outcomes are fixed.,1,N/A,Survey responses do not include actual monetary outcomes for respondents as the key DV; only perceptions.,1,Highly diverse international student sample in UAE,"Survey 1 participants were recruited from the same NYU Abu Dhabi subject pool, which is described as highly diverse (115+ nationalities).",0.6,N/R,Exact ages not reported for survey participants.,0,N/R,Gender distribution for surveys not reported.,0,University students,"Participants drawn from NYU Abu Dhabi volunteer pool, implying university students.",0.95,Online,Both surveys are explicitly described as conducted online.,1,"Within-subject vignette study. Respondents first read a scenario where they, as Person A, receive $30, take $10 from Person B’s $30, and then Person B reduces their earnings by $20 with no personal gain (second-party punishment). They rate how biased Person B’s action is on an ordered scale (biased to unbiased). They then read a second, otherwise identical scenario where Person C, an uninvolved third party who does not know Person B personally, instead reduces Person A’s earnings by $20 (third-party punishment). They report whether they perceive punishment in the second scenario as more, less, or equally biased compared to the first.",Summary drawn from Section 2.2 describing Survey 1 vignettes and questions.,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00137-3.md,Online Survey 2 – Beliefs about ulterior motive attribution,Survey 2 is an incentivized online survey assessing beliefs about how others in Survey 1 judged second- vs third-party punishment.,1,"Scenario punisher type in vignette (second-party Person B vs third-party Person C), plus belief-elicitation about majority responses from Survey 1.","Same vignettes as Survey 1; manipulation is punisher identity, and respondents predict majority evaluation categories.",1,True,Human subjects survey eliciting beliefs.,1,True,Controlled survey with incentive-compatible belief elicitation (bonus for correct guess).,0.9,False,Conducted online rather than in the physical lab.,1,False,Only human respondents.,1,False,No analytical modeling.,1,3,"Vignettes involve Persons A, B, and C as in Survey 1.",1,1,One-shot hypothetical scenario.,1,1,No contribution choice; fixed actions in vignette.,0.5,N/A,No contribution game is played by respondents.,1,N/A,No contributions or public good.,1,0,No participant communication is described.,1,0,"Participants are not shown detailed distributions of Survey 1 responses, only asked to state beliefs.",0.9,0,"Vignettes maintain anonymity via labels A, B, C.",1,0,No reward option present.,1,1,Scenarios clearly single-shot.,0.9,1,Scenarios describe punishment by B or C reducing A’s earnings by $20.,1,20,As in Survey 1: punishment corresponds to a $20 reduction in A's earnings with no gain to the punisher.,0.4,1,"Punishment magnitude is a fixed $20 reduction, implying a 1:1 mapping between punishment act and earnings reduction.",0.4,0,No rewards described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,30,Vignettes specify that A and B each receive $30; same as Survey 1.,1,N/A,"Main dependent variable is beliefs about the majority’s bias ratings, not contributions.",1,N/A,No contribution decisions.,1,N/A,No efficiency calculation in the belief-elicitation survey.,1,N/A,"Survey focuses on beliefs, not realized payoffs for a group from a game.",1,Highly diverse international student sample in UAE,Survey 2 participants were drawn from the same NYU Abu Dhabi volunteer pool but did not participate in Survey 1.,0.6,N/R,No age data reported.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown for survey participants.,0,University students,Recruited from NYU Abu Dhabi student pool.,0.95,Online,Survey 2 is also explicitly described as conducted online.,1,"Respondents see the same two vignettes as in Survey 1 (second-party B vs third-party C punisher after A takes $10 from B). However, instead of giving their own evaluation of bias, they are asked which response category (‘biased’, ‘somewhat biased’, etc.) they think the majority of Survey 1 participants chose for each vignette, and whether punishment is perceived as more, less, or equally biased between the two scenarios. One of their predictions is randomly selected for payment, with a 20 AED bonus if correct.",Summarized from Section 2.2 description of Survey 2 and belief-elicitation mechanism.,1
10.1111_ecin.13171.md,"Lying game – SG-INDI (small groups, individualistic priming)",Condition explicitly described as small groups of 3 with individualistic mindset priming (SG-INDI treatment).,1,Game type: Lying; Group size: 3 (small group); Mindset priming: Individualistic,Treatment varies group size (3) and priming (INDI) within the lying game.,1,True,Human subjects in laboratory experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment with between-subject treatments.,1,True,Conducted in computer laboratory at Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics.,1,False,No computational or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,No standalone analytical model tested without humans.,1,3,Small groups (SG) consisted of three members.,1,20,Game repeated for 20 periods.,1,1,"Participants report outcomes of three die rolls; effectively choose any number 1–6 each roll, i.e., continuous within discrete range not binary all-or-nothing.",0.8,0,No endowment split between public/private accounts; game is not a contribution game; set to 0 as all earnings start as private claims.,0.5,N/R,Not a public goods game; no marginal per capita return defined.,1,0,Communication explicitly not allowed.,1,0,"Feedback only on own total claim, whether sanction occurred, and own payoff; no summaries about others.",1,0,Sanction is collective and blind; no identities revealed.,1,0,No reward mechanism in design.,1,1,Standard z-Tree repeated-game setup; 20 periods were common knowledge; participants answered control questions about 20 periods.,0.9,1,Probabilistic collective sanction cancels all payoffs if group total claims exceed threshold.,1,N/A,Sanction is automatic; no individual spends tokens to punish.,1,N/A,No per-token punishment technology; sanction cancels all period earnings.,1,0,"Design includes only sanctions, no rewards.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No explicit per-period endowment; participants simply earn tokens from reported outcomes.,0.6,N/R,Study reports average claim per die roll (4.03) but not normalized as fraction of an endowment.,0.2,N/R,"Reports average claim per roll and total claims, but not aggregated in a way that matches a standard 'contribution amount' DV.",0.2,N/R,They compare realized earnings to honest-report benchmark but do not provide normalized efficiency ratios.,0.4,N/R,Only average individual net payoff (37.48 tokens per period) is reported; group-level total not directly reported.,0.6,China,Experiments run in laboratories at Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics and Shandong University in China.,1,N/R,Described as university students; no age statistics reported.,0.4,Mixed; exact proportions N/R,They state gender did not differ significantly across treatments; exact shares not given.,0.6,University students from various disciplines,Participants recruited from universities; described as students from various disciplines.,1,On site,Conducted in physical computer laboratories with participants at terminals.,1,"Group size 3; each period each participant rolls a six-sided die three times in an opaque cup and reports each outcome; each reported point pays 5 tokens unless collective sanction occurs; threshold for risk of sanction is group sum ≥ 36 (12 × group size); if threshold reached there is a 60% probability that all group members lose all earnings for that period; 20 periods with fixed groups; feedback each period on own total claim, whether sanction occurred, and own payoff.",Summarizes key structural details of SG-INDI lying game treatment from design section.,1
10.1111_ecin.13171.md,"Lying game – SG-COLL (small groups, collectivist priming)",Condition explicitly described as small groups of 3 with collectivist mindset priming (SG-COLL treatment).,1,Game type: Lying; Group size: 3 (small group); Mindset priming: Collectivist,Treatment varies group size (3) and priming (COLL) within the lying game.,1,True,Human participants in lab experiment.,1,True,Randomized controlled lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted in computer laboratory setting.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,No purely analytical experiment.,1,3,Small groups (SG) consisted of three members.,1,20,Game repeated for 20 periods.,1,1,Participants can effectively choose any number 1–6 for each roll; not restricted to binary contribution.,0.8,0,Not a standard contribution game; no initial amount in public account.,0.5,N/R,No public good multiplier defined.,1,0,Communication not allowed.,1,0,"Only individual feedback on own total claim, sanction, and payoff.",1,0,Sanctions are collective and anonymous.,1,0,No rewards in design.,1,1,20-period horizon common knowledge in the instructions.,0.9,1,Probabilistic collective sanction cancels earnings.,1,N/A,Sanction automatic; no cost borne by punisher.,1,N/A,No per-unit punishment choice; exogenous 100% loss if sanction realized.,1,0,No reward mechanisms.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/R,Payoffs originate from reported outcomes; no fixed token endowment per period stated.,0.6,N/R,They report average claim (3.93) but not as normalized fraction of maximum possible.,0.2,N/R,Outcomes discussed in terms of claims and payoffs rather than contributions.,0.2,N/R,Only relative to honest benchmark; no explicit efficiency index reported.,0.4,N/R,Average individual payoff per period (38.45 tokens) reported; group total not stated.,0.6,China,Sessions for lying treatments run in Zhejiang University of Finance and Economics lab in China.,1,N/R,Participants are university students; no explicit age stats.,0.4,Mixed; exact proportions N/R,"Text notes no significant gender differences across treatments, but not exact shares.",0.6,University students from various disciplines,"Recruited students from Zhejiang University, Hangzhou Normal University, and Shandong University.",1,On site,Conducted in physical computer laboratory.,1,"Same lying game structure as SG-INDI: group size 3; each period each participant rolls a six-sided die three times privately and reports each outcome; each reported point pays 5 tokens; collective sanction risk if group total claims ≥ 36; sanction probability 60%, cancelling all period earnings if realized; 20 periods, fixed groups; here, participants received collectivist mindset priming via writing about groups they belong to and advantages of blending in.",Summarizes SG-COLL treatment specifics.,1
10.1111_ecin.13171.md,"Lying game – LG-INDI (large groups, individualistic priming)",Condition explicitly described as large groups of 6 with individualistic priming (LG-INDI treatment).,1,Game type: Lying; Group size: 6 (large group); Mindset priming: Individualistic,Treatment varies group size (6) and priming (INDI) within lying game.,1,True,Human subjects in lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with randomized assignment.,1,True,Conducted in computer laboratory.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,Not a purely analytical study.,1,6,Large groups (LG) consisted of six members.,1,20,Game repeated for 20 periods.,1,1,Participants choose a report 1–6 each roll; not binary all-or-nothing.,0.8,0,No shared endowment; individual claims only.,0.5,N/R,No MPCR defined; not a linear public good.,1,0,Communication not allowed.,1,0,Feedback restricted to own outcomes and sanction status.,1,0,"Collective, blind sanction; no IDs.",1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,20-period horizon common knowledge.,0.9,1,Collective sanction cancels all payoffs with 60% probability when threshold exceeded.,1,N/A,No individual-controlled punishment cost.,1,N/A,"Sanction is exogenous event, not per-unit.",1,0,Design includes only sanctions.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Earnings determined by reported points; no fixed endowment mentioned.,0.6,N/R,"Outcomes framed as claims, not contributions.",0.2,N/R,No contribution variable defined.,0.2,N/R,No explicit 0–1 efficiency measure reported.,0.4,N/R,Average individual net payoff per period reported (35.06 tokens) but not group total.,0.6,China,Lying treatments run in Chinese university laboratories.,1,N/R,"University students, but no age stats provided.",0.4,Mixed; exact proportions N/R,Gender balanced across treatments according to tests; exact shares absent.,0.6,University students from various disciplines,Recruitment from multiple universities; described as students.,1,On site,Computer laboratory environment described.,1,"Group size 6; in each of 20 periods, each participant rolls a six-sided die three times privately and reports each outcome; each reported point yields 5 tokens absent sanction; threshold for sanction risk is group sum ≥ 72 (12 × 6), corresponding to average claim 4 per die roll; if threshold reached, there is a 60% chance that all members lose all earnings for that period; fixed groups; participants received individualistic mindset priming (write about uniqueness and advantages of standing out).",Summarizes LG-INDI treatment details.,1
10.1111_ecin.13171.md,"Lying game – LG-COLL (large groups, collectivist priming)",Condition explicitly described as large groups of 6 with collectivist priming (LG-COLL treatment).,1,Game type: Lying; Group size: 6 (large group); Mindset priming: Collectivist,Treatment varies group size (6) and priming (COLL) within lying game.,1,True,Human laboratory experiment.,1,True,Randomized treatment in controlled lab setting.,1,True,Performed in computer laboratory.,1,False,No simulations.,1,False,No purely analytical experiment.,1,6,Large group size is six members.,1,20,Game played for 20 periods.,1,1,Subjects choose reported die outcomes 1–6 each roll; not all-or-nothing.,0.8,0,No contributions; only individual earnings from claims.,0.5,N/R,Public good multiplier not defined.,1,0,Communication prohibited.,1,0,Only own outcome and sanction info given.,1,0,Sanction not linked to identifiable individuals.,1,0,No reward.,1,1,Number of periods (20) known to participants.,0.9,1,Endogenous collective sanction when claims exceed threshold.,1,N/A,No discretionary punishment spending.,1,N/A,Sanction fully cancels payoffs instead of applying a per-unit factor.,1,0,No reward option present.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Earnings from reported die outcomes; no explicit endowment per period.,0.6,N/R,"Outcome variable is claim level, not contribution rate.",0.2,N/R,Contributions not defined.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency relative to maximum not quantified as 0–1 metric.,0.4,N/R,"Reported average individual net payoff per period (38.84 tokens), but not summed at group level.",0.6,China,Lying treatments implemented in Chinese university laboratory.,1,N/R,Sample described as students; no age distribution.,0.4,Mixed; exact proportions N/R,Gender composition reported as not significantly different by treatment; exact numbers not provided.,0.6,University students from various disciplines,Recruited from university campuses; described as students from various disciplines.,1,On site,Described as conducted in computer laboratory.,1,"Same lying game as other lying treatments but with group size 6 and collectivist priming: each of 20 periods, subjects roll a die three times in an opaque cup and report each outcome; each reported point earns 5 tokens unless a collective sanction occurs; threshold for sanction risk is sum of claims in group ≥ 72; if reached, a sanction occurs with probability 60%, cancelling all group members’ earnings for that period; fixed groups; collectivist mindset primed by writing about groups they belong to and benefits of blending in.",Summarizes LG-COLL treatment configuration.,1
10.1111_ecin.13171.md,"No-lying game – SG-COLL No-Lying (small groups, collectivist priming)",Condition explicitly named 'SG-COLL No-Lying treatment' with group size 3 and collectivist priming.,1,Game type: No-lying (direct claims); Group size: 3 (small group); Mindset priming: Collectivist,"This treatment modifies game type (no die, direct claims) while keeping SG and collectivist priming.",1,True,Human subjects in experimental lab.,1,True,Controlled treatment condition within experimental design.,1,True,Conducted in experimental laboratory of Shandong University.,1,False,No simulations here.,1,False,No analytical-only study.,1,3,No-lying game implemented in configuration with small groups of three players.,1,20,Participants made three claims per period for 20 periods.,1,1,Participants freely choose claims between 1 and 6 each time; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,0.8,0,No contributions; participants directly claim earnings.,0.5,N/R,Not a public goods contribution setting.,1,0,No communication was introduced in this treatment.,1,0,"Feedback structure kept same as lying game: only own claim, sanction, and payoff.",0.9,0,Sanction applies to whole group without identifying responsible individuals.,1,0,No rewards implemented.,1,1,"20-period game; common knowledge in instructions, analogous to main game.",0.9,1,Same probabilistic collective sanction as in lying game when threshold exceeded.,1,N/A,Sanction automatic; no individual punishment costs.,1,N/A,Sanction fully cancels payoffs; no per-token factor defined.,1,0,No reward option.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Participants earn tokens from chosen claims; no fixed initial endowment specified.,0.6,N/R,"DV is average claim per choice (4.46), not a contribution rate.",0.2,N/R,No contributions defined.,0.2,N/R,They compare payoffs to other treatments but do not compute normalized efficiency.,0.4,N/R,"Average individual net payoff per period given (36.68 tokens), but not group totals.",0.6,China,No-lying treatment conducted in Shandong University laboratory in China.,1,N/R,Participants are students; exact ages not reported.,0.4,Mixed; exact proportions N/R,Comparisons note no significant gender differences between SG-COLL and SG-COLL No-Lying; exact shares absent.,0.6,University students from various disciplines,Recruited at Shandong University; described as students.,1,On site,Run in experimental laboratory of Shandong University.,1,"Group size 3; participants primed with collectivist mindset using same Goncalo and Staw (2006) procedure; in each of 20 periods, instead of rolling dice, each participant directly chooses three numbers between 1 and 6 as claims; each claimed point yields 5 tokens unless a collective sanction occurs; threshold for sanction risk is group sum of claims > 36, identical to lying game; if threshold exceeded, a random draw yields a 60% chance that all group members lose all earnings for that period; feedback each period on own total claim, whether group was sanctioned, and own payoff.",Summarizes structural details unique to SG-COLL No-Lying treatment.,1
10.1007_s00265-023-03380-0.md,Abstract model – Norm internalization condition,Condition where the abstract (non-spatial) agent-based model includes norm internalizers (NIs) in the strategy space; contrasted with a non-NI condition.,1,"Presence of norm internalization strategy in abstract model: NIs included at 2% initial population; benefit-cost ratio b/c varied in [2.75, 4.25] and migration probability p_mig varied, with default b/c=3.5 and p_mig=0.2",Independent variables are presence vs absence of NI strategy and key parameters systematically varied (b/c and migration). This row describes the NI-present condition for the abstract model.,0.9,False,Study uses agent-based simulations with evolving strategies; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment with participants; it is a computational model.,1,False,No lab or in-person environment; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Described explicitly as agent-based simulations with abstract and naturalistic models.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model; results are from simulations.,1,35,Table 2: n = number of individuals per group = 35 in the abstract model.,1,N/R,Paper discusses many-round dynamics and windows of 10 rounds but does not state total number of rounds per simulation run.,0.2,1,"Decision rule compares expected costs but does not restrict to binary all-or-nothing contributions; however, each decision is effectively cooperate vs defect for a fixed cost c, so contribution choice is binary not continuous amount. According to schema, 1 means players can contribute any amount, 0 means only all-or-nothing; here choice is binary, so coded as 0.",0.7,N/A,"No human participants, so default allocation concept does not apply.",1,N/R,"They specify a public goods game with cost c and group benefit b>c and range of b/c, but not the exact mapping to per-capita return (MPCR = multiplier/group size).",0.3,N/A,No human communication; interactions are algorithmic.,1,N/A,Agents do not see explicit summaries; they only use local payoff information and observation probabilities as per programmed rules.,1,N/A,Punishment is implemented mechanically via withholding public benefit; no notion of identities being revealed in a human sense.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism beyond public goods benefit distribution.,1,N/A,Simulated agents are not described as being informed about remaining rounds.,1,1,Indirect reciprocity mechanism where those observed defecting are excluded from benefit distribution serves as a punishment mechanism.,1,0,Punishment is via withholding benefits and is described as not costly for punishers; cost is borne by punished agents only.,0.9,1,"Being observed defecting leads to full loss of public benefit share for that round; modeled as a 1:1 exclusion effect per defection event. Exact numeric tech is not parameterized, but 1 is used as a nominal unit effect.",0.3,0,No separate costly reward or bonus beyond standard public goods benefit.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model specifies fitness baseline f, cooperation cost c, and benefit b, but not an explicit per-round token endowment.",0.2,N/R,They report various cooperation percentages and surplus cooperation qualitatively and in figures but no single numeric summary per condition that can be extracted here.,0.2,N/R,Cooperation is a binary action with cost c; no absolute token contributions are tabulated.,0.2,N/R,No explicit reporting of efficiency as ratio of realized to maximum possible group payoff.,0.1,N/R,They discuss fitness and average cooperation but do not provide numeric average payoffs per group/condition.,0.1,N/A,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from agent-based computer simulations.,1,"Agent-based public goods game in fixed-size groups (n=35, g=60) with cost of cooperation c, group benefit b (b/c between 2.75 and 4.25), imperfect observation probability p_obs for each action, and indirect-reciprocity punishment via withholding public benefits; strategies parameterized by propensity to cooperate π and learning style (norm internalization, selfish, static). Inter-group conflict with probability p_con=1/13 and migration probability p_mig (default 0.2).",Summarizes the abstract model's public goods structure and selection dynamics from sections 'Basics' and 'Abstract model' and Table 2.,1
10.1007_s00265-023-03380-0.md,Abstract model – No norm internalization condition,"Condition where the abstract model excludes norm internalizers (NIs) from the strategy space, replacing their initial 2% with unconditional cooperators.",1,"Presence of norm internalization strategy in abstract model: NIs excluded; initial 2% of population are unconditional cooperators instead; benefit-cost ratio b/c varied in [2.75, 4.25] and migration probability p_mig varied, with default b/c=3.5 and p_mig=0.2","Independent manipulation is removal of the NI strategy, with unconditional cooperators as baseline cooperative type; other parameters as in NI condition.",0.9,False,Agent-based simulations only.,1,False,Not a human experiment.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Described as an agent-based model.,1,False,No analytic solution.,1,35,Same abstract model structure: n=35 per group.,1,N/R,Total simulated rounds per run not specified.,0.2,1,"Same binary cooperate/defect decision as NI condition; as above, this is an all-or-nothing contribution. Using same coding (0) as the other abstract condition for consistency.",0.7,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/R,MPCR not explicitly reported.,0.3,N/A,No communication for simulated agents.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents follow programmed rules only.,1,N/A,No notion of identity revelation as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards beyond payoffs.,1,N/A,Agents are not informed of horizon.,1,1,Same imperfect indirect reciprocity punishment mechanism as in NI condition.,1,0,Punishment by withholding benefits is costless to punishers.,0.9,1,Observably defecting leads to full exclusion from benefit; nominal unit effect as above.,0.3,0,No separate costly reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Endowments per se not defined; only fitness, cost, and benefit parameters.",0.2,N/R,"Figures compare cooperation between NI and non-NI conditions, but no single numeric value for this specific parameter combination is provided.",0.2,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect action; no raw token contributions reported.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not numerically reported.,0.1,N/R,No explicit average fitness numbers tabulated per condition.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations only.,1,"Same abstract public goods game as in NI condition, with n=35 individuals per group and g=60 groups, b/c in [2.75, 4.25], p_con=1/13, migration probability p_mig varied (default 0.2). Only difference is the strategy space: no norm internalizers; initial 2% are unconditional cooperators, rest are selfish and static learners with π sampled uniformly between -1 and 1.",Derived from the description of the non-NI condition and Table 2.,1
10.1007_s00265-023-03380-0.md,Naturalistic model – Norm internalization condition,Condition where the spatial (naturalistic) agent-based model includes norm internalizers (NIs) in the strategy space; contrasted with a non-NI condition.,1,"Presence of norm internalization strategy in naturalistic model: NIs included at 2% initial population; benefit-cost ratio b/c varied in [2.75, 4.25] with default b/c=3.25; migration probability p_mig varied with default giving ~50% lifetime migration (p_mig≈0.5).",Independent variable is presence vs absence of NI strategy in spatial model; b/c and p_mig are systematically varied with stated defaults.,0.9,False,"Model is implemented as an agent-based simulation, not with human participants.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment but a simulation study.,1,False,No lab or physical environment.,1,True,Explicitly described as the naturalistic agent-based model.,1,False,No closed-form analysis reported.,1,20,Table 2: n = number of individuals per group = 20 in the naturalistic model (starting size).,1,N/R,They show windows of 25 rounds for analysis but do not state total simulation length.,0.2,1,"As in abstract model, agents choose to cooperate or defect for a fixed cost; this is an all-or-nothing binary contribution.",0.7,N/A,No human endowment structure.,1,N/R,b/c range is given but MPCR per capita is not explicitly calculated.,0.3,N/A,No communication mechanism among agents is described.,1,N/A,"Agents operate via programmed rules, no information display.",1,N/A,No representation of visible identities as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards beyond payoff sharing.,1,N/A,Agents are not described as knowing horizon length.,1,1,Same imperfect indirect reciprocity punishment implemented in the distribution step: those observed defecting are excluded from public benefits.,1,0,Withholding benefits is described as not costly for punishers; costs fall on punished agents.,0.9,1,Observed defectors do not receive their share of public benefit; treated as unit exclusion effect.,0.3,0,No separate costly reward institution modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Model specifies payoffs via foraging returns, costs of cooperation, cost of distant foraging, survival and reproduction costs, but not a per-round token endowment.",0.2,N/R,"They report cooperation proportions graphically and comparatively (e.g., NI condition doubles surplus cooperation in mid-range b/c) but do not provide a single numeric value extractable here.",0.3,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect – no amount in tokens provided.,0.2,N/R,No explicit calculation of efficiency vs maximum payoff.,0.1,N/R,Group fitness and growth described qualitatively; no numeric averages given.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Spatial agent-based simulations only.,1,"Spatial public goods game with groups located on a s×s grid (s=10). Starting n=20 individuals per group and g=10 groups. Agents choose whether to cooperate or defect, and where to forage (home or adjacent square) considering a distant foraging cost c_dist=5 and crowding (payoffs divided by n_here on a square). Cooperation has cost c and yields benefit b (b/c in [2.75, 4.25]) that is shared among non-observed defectors. Observation probability p_obs is drawn per agent per round. Groups grow via reproduction and can bud new groups when enough members forage together on a new square. Migration with probability p_mig when foraging on another group’s square (default p_mig≈0.5 for ~50% lifetime migration). Strategies parameterized by propensity to cooperate π and learning style (norm internalization, selfish, static).","Summarizes naturalistic model details from 'Naturalistic model', Figure 1, and Table 2.",1
10.1007_s00265-023-03380-0.md,Naturalistic model – No norm internalization condition,"Condition where the naturalistic (spatial) model excludes norm internalizers (NIs) from the strategy space, replacing initial 2% with unconditional cooperators.",1,"Presence of norm internalization strategy in naturalistic model: NIs excluded; initial 2% of population are unconditional cooperators; benefit-cost ratio b/c varied in [2.75, 4.25] with default b/c=3.25; migration probability p_mig varied with default giving ~50% lifetime migration.",Independent variable is removal of NI strategy in spatial model; all other structural and parameter variations are as in the NI-present condition.,0.9,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Not an experiment with human subjects.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Described as an agent-based spatial model.,1,False,No analytical solutions presented.,1,20,Same naturalistic model structure with starting group size n=20.,1,N/R,Total simulation length not specified.,0.2,1,Cooperation is a binary choice to incur cost c; same as other conditions.,0.7,N/A,No human endowment.,1,N/R,Not explicitly reported.,0.3,N/A,No communication between simulated agents.,1,N/A,Agents do not see explicit summaries; they respond to local payoffs and programmed rules.,1,N/A,No identity-based display as in human experiments.,1,N/A,No distinct rewards beyond payoffs.,1,N/A,Horizon not presented to agents.,1,1,Same indirect reciprocity punishment via withholding public benefits from observed defectors.,1,0,Punishment is implemented as costless withholding by others.,0.9,1,Observed defection leads to exclusion from benefit in that round; nominal unit effect.,0.3,0,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment size not specified; model uses fitness and foraging payoffs instead.,0.2,N/R,Paper provides relative differences in cooperation between NI and non-NI conditions but not precise numeric rates for each parameter set.,0.3,N/R,Binary decision only; no numeric amounts given.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not numerically reported.,0.1,N/R,No explicit average group payoff numbers reported.,0.1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,All work is computational simulations.,1,"Same naturalistic spatial public goods environment as in NI condition: groups on a 10×10 grid, starting n=20 individuals per group and g=10 groups; agents decide where to forage and whether to cooperate or defect; payoffs divided by local forager count with distant foraging cost c_dist=5; b/c between 2.75 and 4.25; imperfect observation p_obs and punishment by exclusion from public benefit; migration probability p_mig varied (default ≈0.5 for ~50% lifetime migration). Strategy space excludes norm internalization; initial 2% of population are unconditional cooperators; remaining agents are selfish and static learners with random π ∈ [−1,1].",Derived from naturalistic model description and the definition of the non-NI condition.,1
10.3390_g14050065.md,"Sim-Sym (Simultaneous order, symmetric show-up fees)","Paper defines four treatments; this row corresponds to simultaneous order protocol with symmetric show-up fees, labeled Sim-Sym.",1,"Order protocol: simultaneous; Show-up fee symmetry: symmetric (EUR 5 each for X, Y, Z).",Treatment varies order (sequential vs simultaneous) and wealth via show-up fees (symmetric vs asymmetric).,1,True,Laboratory experiment with student participants making decisions.,1,True,"Random assignment to roles and treatments, controlled lab environment.",1,True,Sessions conducted in experimental laboratory of Max Planck Institute of Economics in Jena.,1,False,No mention of computer/agent-based simulations; only human subjects.,1,False,"Although theory section is analytical, this row refers to the experimental condition with humans.",1,3,"Game has three players X, Y, Z matched in groups of three each round.",1,3,Each session consists of only three rounds.,1,1,"Y and Z choose between two discrete actions (R or B); not a continuous contribution game, so players cannot contribute arbitrary amounts in a public good sense. However, relative to definition, there is no partial contribution; coded as 1 since field is about contribution flexibility and here there is no contribution mechanism.",0.3,N/R,No public good contribution or endowment allocation decision; only action choices R/B and X choosing sanction split.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.,1,0,Any communication between participants strictly forbidden.,1,1,"At end of each round, each participant was informed about all decisions and the earnings in this round.",1,0,"No identities revealed, only roles and choices; anonymity via terminals and random rematching.",0.8,0,No explicit reward stage or identity revelation beyond role.,1,1,"Instructions state experiment consists of three rounds, read aloud, so total rounds known.",1,1,X allocates sanctions (p and d-p) that reduce Y and Z payoffs when both choose B.,1,0,Assumption 7: X is not affected by p-choice; total sanction d is fixed and costless to X.,0.9,1,"With b=15, c=5, d=10, if both choose B then Y payoff is 15-p and Z is 5+p; each additional unit of p reduces Y by 1 and increases Z by 1, so per-unit sanction on Y is 1 point.",0.9,0,"No mechanism for costly positive rewards; only sanctions in (B,B) case.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,15,"Base payoff e=15 used as starting payoff before sanction/payoff changes; when no damage or unilateral B, players earn 15 or 30. Treat 15 as effective per-round endowment for baseline outcomes.",0.6,N/R,"No contributions to a public good; paper reports choices of B vs R and p, not normalized contribution rates.",1,N/R,No contribution variable defined; behavioral DVs are p (number of black balls) and choices R/B.,1,N/R,"Although efficient outcomes are discussed (one B one R), no numerical efficiency measure (payoff ratio) is reported.",1,N/R,Table 2 shows possible payoffs but no average realized group payoff by treatment presented.,1,Germany,"Participants recruited at Friedrich-Schiller University of Jena and experiment conducted in Jena, Germany.",1,N/R,Only described as student participants; no age statistics reported.,1,N/R,No information on gender composition.,1,University students (various fields),Described as student participants from various fields of study at the university.,1,On site,Sessions held in physical laboratory with PCs connected in a network.,1,"Three-player game where X pre-chooses a sanction split p in {0,…,10} (black balls) allocating total sanction d=10 between Y and Z if both choose B. Y and Z then simultaneously choose R or B without knowing p. Payoffs: if both choose R, all 15; if one chooses B and other R, B-player 30, others 15; if both choose B, X 10, Y 15-p, Z 5+p.",Summarized from Section 2 and Table 2 with specific parameter values for the experimental implementation and the Simultaneous order protocol definition.,1
10.3390_g14050065.md,"Seq-Sym (Sequential order, symmetric show-up fees)","Sequential order protocol with symmetric show-up fees, labeled Seq-Sym in the paper.",1,"Order protocol: sequential; Show-up fee symmetry: symmetric (EUR 5 each for X, Y, Z).",Treatment varies order (sequential vs simultaneous) and wealth via show-up fees (symmetric vs asymmetric).,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random role assignment and treatment manipulation.,1,True,Conducted in lab at Max Planck Institute of Economics in Jena.,1,False,No non-human or computational agents.,1,False,"Row refers to empirical condition, not theoretical section.",1,3,"Groups of X, Y, Z per round.",1,3,Each session has three rounds.,1,1,Action space for Y and Z is discrete (R or B) without partial contributions; no standard contribution decision.,0.3,N/R,No public good endowment or contribution setup.,1,N/R,Game is not a linear public goods game.,1,0,Communication strictly forbidden.,1,1,End-of-round feedback includes all decisions and earnings.,1,0,Only anonymous role-based feedback; no identities attached to sanctions.,0.8,0,No explicit rewards with identity revelation.,1,1,Instructions state there are three rounds.,1,1,"Sanctions via p and d-p applied in (B,B) outcome.",1,0,"X's payoff in (B,B) is 10 regardless of p; assumption 7 states X not affected by p-choice.",0.9,1,"When both choose B, Y payoff 15-p, so each unit of sanction reduces payoff by 1.",0.9,0,No mechanism to spend resources to increase others' payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward stage.,1,15,e=15 base payoff in non-damage or unilateral B outcomes; used as effective starting payoff.,0.6,N/R,No contributions measured.,1,N/R,"Outcomes tracked are choices R/B and p, not contribution amounts.",1,N/R,No reported aggregate efficiency metric per treatment.,1,N/R,No mean group payoff by treatment reported.,1,Germany,"Participants from university in Jena and experiment in Jena, Germany.",1,N/R,Age not provided.,1,N/R,No gender information reported.,1,University students (various fields),Described as student participants from various fields of study.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical laboratory with networked PCs.,1,"Same three-player game and parameters (b=15, c=5, d=10, e=15) as Sim-Sym. In sequential protocol, X first chooses p (0–10 black balls) and this is revealed to Y and Z before they simultaneously choose R or B. Payoffs identical to Table 2 description.",Sequential order protocol definition: Y and Z informed of X's p-choice before their decisions.,1
10.3390_g14050065.md,"Sim-Asym (Simultaneous order, asymmetric show-up fees)","Simultaneous order protocol with asymmetric show-up fees, labeled Sim-Asym.",1,Order protocol: simultaneous; Show-up fee symmetry: asymmetric (X: EUR 5; Y: EUR 2.50; Z: EUR 7.50).,Treatment varies order and wealth asymmetry via show-up fee differences.,1,True,Condition is part of a lab experiment with human participants.,1,True,Controlled laboratory design with random assignment.,1,True,Performed in experimental laboratory in Jena.,1,False,No simulations mentioned.,1,False,"This row concerns empirical data, not only theory.",1,3,"Game involves players X, Y, Z matched per round.",1,3,Experiment has three rounds per session.,1,1,"Y and Z have binary action set {R,B}; no gradual contributions.",0.3,N/R,No contribution or endowment-allocation decision.,1,N/R,Not a public good with MPCR concept.,1,0,Communication prohibited.,1,1,Participants see all decisions and earnings at end of each round.,1,0,"Sanctions linked only to roles, not personal identities.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Instructions announce three rounds.,1,1,"X sanctions Y and Z in (B,B) via p and d-p splits.",1,0,"X's payoff in (B,B) is 10 regardless of p; assumption that X not affected by total sanction allocation.",0.9,1,"Y payoff decreases by 1 per unit increase in p (in (B,B) outcome).",0.9,0,No costly reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,15,Parameter e=15 is baseline payoff per player absent sanctions; used as effective starting payoff per round.,0.6,N/R,"DVs are choices B/R and p, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decision in public-goods sense.,1,N/R,Efficiency discussed qualitatively; no numeric efficiency outcome per treatment reported.,1,N/R,No average group payoff by treatment provided.,1,Germany,"Subjects from Jena university; experiment in Jena, Germany.",1,N/R,Age data not reported.,1,N/R,Gender not mentioned.,1,University students (various fields),Participants are students from various fields of study.,1,On site,Lab sessions at Max Planck Institute of Economics.,1,"Same three-player game and parameterization (b=15, c=5, d=10, e=15) as in other treatments. Under simultaneous order protocol, X, Y, and Z all choose their actions independently and without observing others' choices. X chooses p (0–10 black balls), then Y and Z choose R or B without knowing p.",Described in Section 2.2 and experimental procedure; differs from sequential protocol only by information timing.,1
10.3390_g14050065.md,"Seq-Asym (Sequential order, asymmetric show-up fees)","Sequential order protocol with asymmetric show-up fees, labeled Seq-Asym.",1,Order protocol: sequential; Show-up fee symmetry: asymmetric (X: EUR 5; Y: EUR 2.50; Z: EUR 7.50).,This treatment combines sequential order with wealth asymmetry via show-up fees.,1,True,Part of the laboratory experiment using human participants.,1,True,Controlled randomized design in a lab.,1,True,Sessions run in Max Planck Institute of Economics laboratory.,1,False,No simulated agents.,1,False,This row describes empirical treatment rather than theory alone.,1,3,"Groups consist of X, Y, Z each round.",1,3,Experiment has three rounds.,1,1,Players Y and Z have only two discrete options each (R or B).,0.3,N/R,No contribution to a common project.,1,N/R,No linear public goods structure.,1,0,Instructions forbid any communication between participants.,1,1,End-of-round feedback shows all decisions and earnings to each participant.,1,0,"Sanctioning information is tied only to roles and choices, not real identities.",0.8,0,No positive reward stage.,1,1,Participants informed experiment consists of three rounds.,1,1,Sanctions via p and d-p when both Y and Z choose B.,1,0,"X's payoff under (B,B) is fixed at 10 regardless of p; X does not bear a cost per unit of sanction.",0.9,1,Each additional black ball increases sanction on Y by 1 point (reduces Y payoff from 15 by 1).,0.9,0,"Game includes only sanctions, not reward mechanisms.",1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,15,Parameter e=15 is baseline payoff when there is no damage; interpreted as per-round endowment base.,0.6,N/R,No contribution decision; main behaviors are R/B and p choices.,1,N/R,No contribution amount to a public good reported.,1,N/R,Paper discusses efficiency-enhancing outcomes but does not report numeric efficiency measures per treatment.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics per treatment are provided.,1,Germany,"Subjects from Jena university and lab in Jena, Germany.",1,N/R,No age information provided.,1,N/R,Gender composition not reported.,1,University students (various fields),Recruited student participants from various fields of study.,1,On site,Experiment in physical lab with PCs.,1,"Same underlying three-person game and parameters (b=15, c=5, d=10, e=15). Under sequential order protocol, X chooses p (0–10) and this is revealed to Y and Z before they simultaneously choose R or B. Wealth is asymmetric due to different show-up fees: Y poorer and Z richer.",Appendix A (Seq-Asym instructions) detail wealth asymmetry and sequential structure.,1
10.1007_s10539-023-09936-8.md,Model 1 – Strong reciprocity,Paper labels this as 'Model 1: strong reciprocity'; single parameterization reported.,1,"Presence of strong reciprocity (conditional cooperation + punishment); model without mimicry or appearance. Parameters: N=100, generations=100000, mutation rate μ=0.1, termination probability ∂=0.0001.",Independent structure is whether agents conditionally cooperate and punish vs the initial proof-of-concept PD without these; here we record the strong reciprocity condition with the parameter set explicitly given.,0.9,False,Agent-based model with simulated agents only; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with subjects; purely computational modeling.,1,False,No lab or field setting; numerical simulation.,1,True,Described as 'agent-based models' and 'simulated data' implemented in R.,1,False,No closed-form mathematical derivations reported for results; all results from simulations.,1,2,Each interaction is a dyadic Prisoner’s Dilemma: 'random dyadic pairing'.,1,1,Each generation consists of random pairings with a single PD interaction before reproduction; no repeated PD rounds per dyad described.,0.7,1,"Classic PD modeled as binary cooperate/defect choice, not a continuous contribution level; encode as full-action choice.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game with contributions from an endowment; binary PD actions only and no human subjects.,1,N/R,MPCR concept applies to public goods; not reported or applicable for this binary PD payoff structure.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication; purely simulated agents.,1,0,Agents have only 'perfect knowledge of each other's actions' in the current pairing; no summaries or histories are displayed.,0.8,0,Agents know partner’s current action but there is no notion of identity revelation beyond that; no ID exposure parameter discussed.,0.6,0,No reward mechanism implemented; thus no reward identity to reveal.,1,0,Agents are not described as knowing how many generations or interactions remain.,0.7,1,Strong reciprocity includes punishing defectors; punishment payoffs given in Table 4.,1,0.1,Table 4: 'Ego punishes partner for defecting' payoff to ego is -0.1.,1,0.4,Table 4: 'Ego is punished for defecting' payoff to ego is -0.4; interpret as magnitude of harm per punishment act.,1,0,No positive-cost reward or bonus for others; only punishment is modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model.,1,N/R,"Model tracks potential to reproduce (PTR) updates, not explicit token endowments.",1,1,"Outcome: 'cooperation quickly rose to fixation, and held near 100%'; thus long-run fraction of cooperative actions ≈1.",0.9,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect choices with no numeric contribution quantity reported.,1,N/R,No summary of average total payoff relative to maximum possible is reported.,1,N/R,Only per-individual payoff increments (PTR changes) are given; no aggregate group payoff statistics reported.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation only.,1,N/A,No human participants; agent-based simulation only.,1,No human,Explicitly an agent-based computational model; implemented in R with simulated agents.,1,"Iterated across generations with random dyadic pairing each generation. Payoff structure: cc=+0.1, cd=-0.1, dc=+0.2, dd=0 applied to PTR; cooperators defect against and punish defectors such that only 0 and 0.1 outcomes occur in practice because cooperators never cooperate with defectors. Population size N=100. Mutation in default action with probability μ=0.1; PTR fluctuates by random f in [-0.4,0.4] after reproduction.",Summarizes detailed PD and evolutionary dynamics as described in Methods and Results for Model 1.,0.9
10.1007_s10539-023-09936-8.md,Model 2 – Mimicry,Paper labels this as 'Model 2: mimicry'; one main parameter setting reported for analyses.,1,"Introduction of type (honest vs mimic) plus continuous mimicry and sensitivity traits; mimics default to defect and may deceive cooperators when mimicry_focal > 2 × sensitivity_partner. Parameters: N=100, generations=100000, μ=0.01, ∂=0.","Independent structural change is adding mimicry & sensitivity and type mutation to the strong reciprocity baseline, with parameter values explicitly reported.",0.9,False,Agent-based computational model only.,1,False,"Simulation study, not an empirical experiment.",1,False,No physical lab or field environment; simulations in R.,1,True,Described as 'agent-based models' with R scripts and simulated data.,1,False,"Results are numerical, not analytical solutions.",1,2,Interactions are random dyadic PD pairings.,1,1,Each pairing yields a single PD payoff prior to reproduction; no repeated interactions per pairing.,0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect PD choices only.,1,N/A,No explicit contribution from an endowment; not a PGG and no human subjects.,1,N/R,Marginal per capita return not defined for this PD; not reported.,1,N/A,No communication between simulated agents.,1,0,"Agents react based on partner’s mimicry/sensitivity and type, not on aggregate payoff summaries.",0.8,0,"Model assumes knowledge of partner’s current behaviour and sometimes detectability via sensitivity, but no identity revelation variable.",0.6,0,No rewards modeled.,1,0,No description that agents know the remaining generations.,0.7,1,Same strong reciprocity partner-control punishment as in Model 1 is retained.,1,0.1,Punishment cost parameters held constant across models; Table 4.,1,0.4,Punished defectors incur -0.4 to PTR as in Table 4.,1,0,No reward mechanism introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards exist in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards exist in the model.,1,N/R,Model uses PTR rather than explicit endowments.,1,0.82,Results: 'median cooperation over the whole period is 82% (range 44-99%)'; use 0.82 as normalized contribution rate.,0.95,N/R,No token-level contribution amounts reported; only cooperation percentages.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff efficiency measure reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not summarized; only frequencies of behaviours and types.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same PD payoff matrix as Model 1 (cc=+0.1, cd=-0.1, dc=+0.2, dd=0; punishment cost -0.1 to punisher, -0.4 to punished). New traits: 'type' (honest, mimic), 'mimicry' and 'sensitivity' in [0,1]. Mimics default to defect but may switch to cooperate when partner’s sensitivity ≥ 0.5 × mimicry_focal; if mimicry_focal > 2 × sensitivity_partner, mimics deceive partners into cooperating and defect to gain dc payoff while partner gets sucker’s payoff, then revert to default defect. Type and strategy mutate independently with probability μ=0.01. N=100, generations=100000, ∂=0. Median cooperation ≈82%, yet defectors are more frequent than cooperators.",Summarizes main structural additions and dynamic behaviours as described under Model 2.,0.9
10.1007_s10539-023-09936-8.md,Model 3 – Covert mimicry (silent defectors),Paper labels this as 'Model 3: silent defectors' or 'covert mimicry model'; parameter values reported explicitly.,1,"Addition of appearance dimension (overt vs covert/silent) for mimics, with mutation rate μ_s=0.0001; covert mimics can defect without detection and still punish others. Parameters: N=100, generations=100000, μ=0.01, μ_s=0.0001, ∂=0.","Independent structural change compared to Model 2 is the 'appearance' trait and silent defection capability, with μ_s, on top of existing mimicry framework.",0.9,False,Purely computational agent-based model.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No human subjects or lab environment; R simulations.,1,True,Explicitly an agent-based simulation implemented in R.,1,False,No analytical solutions; numerical simulations only.,1,2,Random dyadic PD interactions as in previous models.,1,1,Single PD interaction per dyad before reproduction each generation.,0.7,1,Binary cooperate/defect PD actions.,1,N/A,No endowment-based contributions or human subjects.,1,N/R,Public goods MPCR not applicable; not reported.,1,N/A,No communication among computational agents.,1,0,Agents respond only to partner state/traits and appearance; no summaries disclosed.,0.8,0,"Although 'appearance' affects detectability, no explicit identity revelation toggles are modeled.",0.6,0,No rewards exist in this model.,1,0,No indication agents know the total number of generations.,0.7,1,Strong reciprocity with punishment is retained; silent mimics also punish defectors.,1,0.1,Punishment parameters unchanged from Table 4 across all models.,1,0.4,Punished defectors’ PTR reduced by 0.4 as before.,1,0,No reward functionality introduced.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,N/R,"Model based on PTR changes, not on material endowments.",1,0.6,"Model 3 results: 'median cooperation, 60% [range 27-97%]'; use 0.60 as normalized cooperation rate.",0.95,N/R,"No numeric contribution amounts, only cooperation frequencies.",1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,No aggregate payoff statistics provided.,1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,N/A,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation in R; no human environment.,1,"Extends Model 2 by adding 'appearance' (overt vs silent/covert). All individuals start overt; at each reproduction phase, mimics mutate to silent appearance with probability μ_s=0.0001, and silent individuals can mutate back to overt with same probability. Silent mimics with mimicry_focal > sensitivity_partner defect without detection and do not incur punishment, yet they still punish observed defectors to address the second-order free rider problem. Once the first mutation to covert appears, silent appearance rapidly goes to fixation; defector strategy and mimic type also rise to nearly 100%, while median cooperation remains about 60%. Parameters: N=100, generations=100000, μ=0.01, μ_s=0.0001, ∂=0.",Summarizes Model 3’s additional mechanisms and key dynamic outcomes relative to Model 2.,0.9
10.1017_psrm.2023.52.md,N – No punishment baseline,Treatment N is the baseline public goods game with imperfect monitoring and no punishment stage.,1,Punishment regime: No punishment; Commitment: No; Target: N/A,Condition varies the availability/type of punishment; here there is no punishment option.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects repeatedly playing a public goods game.,1,True,Design randomly assigns subjects to treatments in a controlled lab environment.,1,True,Described as a laboratory experiment; no field setting mentioned.,1,False,"All results are from human-subject lab sessions, not simulations.",1,False,Analytical model exists but this row refers to the empirical treatment.,1,4,“Subjects were randomly matched in groups of four which remained constant for the entire duration of the experiment.”,1,50,“participants interacted in a 50-period repeated public good game.”,1,0,"Each participant could “either contribute all of her endowments to a group account or not contribute at all” so only two discrete options, i.e., all-or-nothing is enforced.",1,0,Endowment starts entirely under individual control; contribution is an active choice from private to group account.,0.9,0.5,"Each contributed token returns 10 to each of 4 members; endowment is 20, so payoff function uses α=0.5 as marginal per capita return.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat between participants.,0.9,1,"After each round, participants saw total contributions and, where applicable, punishment-related earnings; this counts as summary info about others.",0.9,0,No punishment exists in this treatment; identity display of punishers is not applicable but coded 0 as there is no mechanism.,0.9,0,No rewarding mechanism is present in any treatment.,1,1,"Standard in repeated public-goods lab designs; 50 periods are explicitly known and analyses reference early vs. late periods, implying subjects knew horizon.",0.7,0,"Explicitly: “no punishment treatment, N participants simply played the contribution game without a punishment stage.”",1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,Only punishment technologies described; no rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards available.,1,N/A,No rewards available.,1,20,“each participant received an endowment of 20 tokens and could decide to either contribute all…or not contribute at all.”,1,N/R,Treatment-specific average contribution fraction is not reported numerically for N alone.,0,N/R,Only binary contribute 0 or 20; treatment averages not given.,0,N/R,No normalized efficiency statistic reported.,0,N/R,Only individual profits (ECU) are discussed comparatively; no treatment-level group payoff reported for N alone.,0,N/R,Recruitment location or nationality not specified; only authors’ affiliations given.,0,N/R,No demographic information reported.,0,N/R,No demographic information reported.,0,N/R,No demographic or student-status information reported.,0,On site,Described as a laboratory experiment; no indications of online implementation.,0.9,"Binary linear public goods game with 4 players over 50 periods, endowment 20 (plus non-contributable 10), MPCR 0.5, imperfect monitoring of individual contributions via signals with accuracy 0.6, perfect observation of total group contributions.",Summarizes common features of the game in this treatment.,1
10.1017_psrm.2023.52.md,S – Standard peer-to-peer punishment without commitment,Treatment S is labeled as the standard peer-to-peer punishment treatment without commitment.,1,Punishment regime: Peer-to-peer; Commitment: No; Punishment points: 15; Punisher cost per act: 5,Condition varies type of punishment; here it is standard P2P without prior commitment.,1,True,Human subjects in a controlled laboratory experiment.,1,True,Random assignment to punishment regimes with controlled procedures.,1,True,Explicitly referred to as a laboratory experiment.,1,False,Not a computational or agent-based simulation.,1,False,"This row summarizes empirical treatment S, not the theoretical model.",1,4,Groups of four remained constant over 50 periods.,1,50,All treatments used a 50-period repeated public goods game.,1,0,Binary contribute-all or contribute-nothing decision.,1,0,Endowment starts in individual control; contributing is an active choice.,0.9,0.5,Same linear public good technology with α=0.5 across treatments.,1,0,No communication capabilities are mentioned between subjects.,0.9,1,"After contribution and punishment stages, subjects saw total contributions and payoff components.",0.9,0,"Paper does not report identities being revealed; only information about signals, totals, and payoffs is referenced.",0.6,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Finite horizon of 50 periods is common knowledge in such designs; analyses refer to ‘last 10 periods’.,0.7,1,Standard peer-to-peer punishment stage after contributions is implemented.,1,5,"“Punishment costs are set at β = 1/3, implying that punishing another player costs 5 tokens” with P=15.",1,15,Choosing to punish subtracts 15 points from each targeted participant.,1,0,No rewarding institution is described.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,N/A,No rewards present.,1,20,Same endowment of 20 tokens per period as baseline.,1,N/R,Exact fraction of contributors in S alone isn’t given numerically; only regression effects vs other treatments.,0,N/R,Only contribution dummy and comparative effects reported.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratio reported by treatment.,0,N/R,Profits reported comparatively; no standalone average group payoff for S.,0,N/R,No location of subject pool given.,0,N/R,Demographics are not described.,0,N/R,Demographics are not described.,0,N/R,Demographics are not described; likely students but not stated.,0,On site,"Laboratory experiment, no mention of online procedures.",0.9,"After each contribution stage, each player i could choose for each other j whether to subtract 15 points from j at a personal cost of 5. Signals about each other’s contributions had accuracy 0.6 and were independent; total group contributions were perfectly observed.",Summarizes punishment and information structure specific to S.,1
10.1017_psrm.2023.52.md,C – Collective punishment without commitment,Treatment C is described as the collective punishment treatment without commitment.,1,Punishment regime: Collective (group-wide); Commitment: No; Punishment points per member: 15; Cost per member: 5; One decision-maker randomly implemented,Treatment varies target of punishment (group vs individual) without commitment.,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Condition is part of the controlled 2×2 lab design.,1,True,Run in a lab environment.,1,False,Not a computational simulation.,1,False,This entry is for the empirical treatment C.,1,4,Groups of four in all treatments.,1,50,Fifty periods in each treatment.,1,0,Binary contribute-all or nothing as in other treatments.,1,0,Endowment is initially entirely private.,0.9,0.5,Same payoff function with α=0.5.,1,0,No communication stage is described.,0.9,1,Feedback includes group totals and payoff consequences of any collective punishment.,0.9,0,"Although one player’s decision is randomly chosen for implementation, identities are not described as revealed to others.",0.6,0,No rewards in the design.,1,1,Fifty-period horizon is fixed and treated as known in analysis.,0.7,1,Collective punishment option after contribution stage.,1,5,“whether she would like to subtract 15 points from everybody (including herself) at a cost of 5 to everybody.”,1,15,"If chosen, 15 points are subtracted from every group member.",1,0,Only punishment institutions considered.,1,N/A,No rewards exist.,1,N/A,No rewards exist.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment per period.,1,N/R,Average contribution rate for C alone is not numerically reported; only compared in joint regressions.,0,N/R,No unconditional averages reported for this treatment.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency statistic by treatment.,0,N/R,Payoff regressions mainly compare C-Comm vs S and N; C without commitment is not separately tabulated.,0,N/R,Location/nationality of participants not provided.,0,N/R,No age information reported.,0,N/R,No gender information reported.,0,N/R,No education or student-status info provided.,0,On site,Experiment conducted in a lab.,0.9,"After contributions and receiving noisy signals and total G, each participant could choose whether to activate a collective punishment subtracting 15 points from all (including self) at a cost of 5 to all. One participant’s choice was randomly selected and implemented.",Captures specifics of collective punishment without commitment.,1
10.1017_psrm.2023.52.md,S-Comm – Peer-to-peer punishment with commitment,Treatment S-Comm is defined as peer-to-peer punishment with ex ante commitment.,1,Punishment regime: Peer-to-peer; Commitment: Yes; Punishment points: 15; Cost per act: 5; Commitment based on noisy signals about each individual,This condition adds commitment dimension to peer-to-peer punishment.,1,True,Implemented as a lab experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Controlled manipulation of punishment regime.,1,True,Run in the laboratory.,1,False,No computational simulation.,1,False,Analytical model exists separately; this entry is empirical.,1,4,Groups of four across all treatments.,1,50,Fifty-period repeated game in every treatment.,1,0,Binary decision to contribute entire endowment or nothing.,1,0,Initial endowment is fully private.,0.9,0.5,Same public good production function with α=0.5.,1,0,No communication option described.,0.9,1,Subjects see total contributions and payoff components including automatic punishment effects.,0.9,0,No indication that identities of who committed to punish or triggered punishment are revealed to others.,0.6,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite horizon (50 periods) treated as common knowledge in analysis.,0.7,1,Peer-to-peer committed punishment is available.,1,5,Same P=15 and β=1/3 implies cost 5 per punishment.,1,15,Committed plans subtract 15 points from each punished player.,1,0,No rewards included.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment structure.,1,N/R,Paper does not give a single numeric contribution fraction for S-Comm; only relative regression effects.,0,N/R,"Only qualitative levels (e.g., ~50–60% under certain commitment counts) are discussed; no overall mean reported.",0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios.,0,N/R,Profits for S-Comm not reported as a standalone average.,0,N/R,Subject pool location not given.,0,N/R,No ages reported.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown.,0,N/R,Education level not described.,0,On site,Lab experiment; not stated as online.,0.9,"Before each round’s contribution decision, each participant chooses a contingent plan indicating for each other group member whether to punish them if the signal indicates they contributed or did not contribute. Once signals are realized, punishment is applied automatically according to these plans.",Summarizes commitment stage and automatic punishment rule.,1
10.1017_psrm.2023.52.md,C-Comm – Collective punishment with commitment,Treatment C-Comm is described as collective punishment with commitment and is the focal treatment in the paper.,1,Punishment regime: Collective (group-wide); Commitment: Yes; Punishment points per member: 15; Cost per member: 5; Target: group punished if total contributions < 4*20,This condition combines collective punishment and commitment; punishment is automatic if group contributions fall short of the target.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects.,1,True,Random assignment to punishment regimes within controlled lab.,1,True,Conducted in a lab setting.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,"This row summarizes the empirical treatment, not the theoretical model.",1,4,Subjects randomly matched in groups of four that remain fixed.,1,50,All experimental treatments last 50 periods.,1,0,Participants must choose between contributing the full endowment or zero.,1,0,Contributions move tokens from private to group account; initial allocation is fully private.,0.9,0.5,Linear return with α=0.5 used in all treatments.,1,0,No communication or chat functionality described.,0.9,1,Subjects observe total contributions and payoff impact of any automatic collective punishment.,0.9,0,"A randomly selected player’s commitment decision is implemented, but there is no indication that their identity is disclosed.",0.6,0,No reward mechanisms are used.,1,1,"Finite horizon (50 periods) is standard and discussed in terms like ‘last 10 periods’, implying subjects knew total length.",0.7,1,Collective punishment institution with commitment is central to this treatment.,1,5,Same collective punishment magnitudes as C: 15 points removed at a cost of 5 per member.,1,15,"When triggered, 15 points are subtracted from each group member.",1,0,No reward structures described.,1,N/A,No rewards exist.,1,N/A,No rewards exist.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment per period as in all conditions.,1,N/R,"Numeric overall fraction is not explicitly stated, though the paper notes high and stable contributions and 94% in last round for groups where punishment is committed.",0.3,N/R,Only binary choice; no unconditional means are tabulated.,0,N/R,No explicit efficiency statistic is given.,0,N/R,"Profits are compared (e.g., ~3 ECU higher than N in last 10 periods and 6.585 higher than S), but no standalone average group payoff for C-Comm is listed.",0.3,N/R,Paper does not describe subject pool’s country.,0,N/R,No age data reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,N/R,No information on participants’ education status.,0,On site,Described as a laboratory experiment; no suggestion of online execution.,0.9,"Before each period’s public goods game, each participant chooses whether they commit to a collective rule that punishes everyone (including themselves) if someone does not contribute (i.e., if total group contributions are below 4×20). One participant’s decision is randomly selected and announced; if collective punishment is in force, and total contributions fall short of the target, then 15 points are subtracted from every member at a cost of 5 per member automatically.",Summarizes key institutional and timing features specific to collective punishment with commitment.,1
10.3390_bs14010039.md,"T1 – Gains, No punishment (Control DG)",Paper’s Table 1 lists T1 as gains domain without punishment; standard dictator game.,1,Domain: gains; Punishment mechanism: none (control).,Treatment differs by gain vs loss domain and presence/strength of punishment; here gains and no punishment.,1,True,Human participants in dictator game.,1,True,"Random assignment to roles, controlled monetary incentives.",1,True,"Conducted in lab (2018) and online replication; authors pooled, so environment predominantly experimental lab/online, not field.",0.9,False,No computer simulations reported.,1,False,No formal analytical model as main method.,1,2,Standard dictator and receiver; no third party in control DG.,1,1,All games are explicitly one-shot.,1,1,"Dictator chooses any amount from EUR 0–10 in 0.50 increments, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment starts fully with dictator (Player 1) who ‘receives an amount of EUR 10’.,1,N/R,"Dictator game, not public goods; no multiplier.",1,0,Instructions explicitly prohibit communication.,1,0,Receiver only sees own and dictator’s payoff; no summary statistics described.,0.8,0,No punishment stage in this treatment.,1,0,No reward mechanism in any treatment.,1,1,Games described as one-shot; participants informed they play the game once.,0.8,0,Control DG without punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment stage.,1,0,Only punishment is discussed; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,10,Dictator divides EUR 10.,1,N/R,"Paper reports dictator’s ‘demand’ (share kept), not normalized giving rates in 0–1 form.",0.3,N/R,"Only mean percentage kept (≈64%) is reported, not mean EUR given; would require raw data.",0.4,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios reported.,0.2,N/R,No absolute group payoff statistics reported.,0.2,Germany,Subjects recruited from universities in Leipzig and Magdeburg; >93% German nationality reported.,1,Mean 27.2 years (gains treatments pooled),Table 2 shows ag