10.3390_bs14010039.md,"T8 – Losses, Strong punishment (Lever 1:3)",Table 1: losses with Lever 1:3 labeled T8; negative TPP-DG with strong punishment.,1,"Domain: losses; Punishment mechanism: third-party punishment, strong (punisher cost 2, dictator additional loss 6; lever 1:3).",Negative counterpart of T7 with same 1:3 ratio.,1,True,Human experiment.,1,True,Randomized three-person groups; controlled design.,1,True,Implemented via zTree and lab subject pools analogous to other loss TPP-DGs.,0.9,False,No simulation models.,1,False,Not purely analytical.,1,3,"Dictator, receiver, third-party punisher.",1,1,One-shot.,1,1,Dictator can allocate any share of -10 in 0.50 steps.,1,0,Loss initially assigned to dictator to divide.,0.7,N/R,Not a public good.,1,0,Communication prohibited.,1,0,Only individual allocation and outcomes displayed.,0.8,0,Anonymous design.,0.9,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,One decision per subject in second session.,0.8,1,Third-party punishment implemented.,1,2,Punisher pays back 2 EUR to punish.,1,3,Dictator’s loss increases by EUR 6 per EUR 2 spent => 3 per unit cost.,1,0,No reward stage.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,N/A,No rewards implemented.,1,10,Subjects given 15 EUR earlier; 10 considered game endowment; punisher has 10 as in other punishment conditions.,1,N/R,Results reported as mean demand and proportions of equal/fair splits; not normalized contributions.,0.3,N/R,No mean EUR transferred by condition.,0.3,N/R,No explicit welfare/efficiency values.,0.2,N/R,Group payoffs by treatment not reported.,0.2,Germany,Same loss-domain subject pools.,1,Mean 26.7 years (losses treatments pooled),Losses summary demographics used.,0.9,63.9% female,Losses demographics.,0.9,98.5% students; remainder non-students,Losses column in Table 2.,0.9,On site,Standard controlled experimental setting.,0.8,Negative TPP-DG: dictator divides -10 loss; punisher can impose additional loss of 6 EUR on dictator at cost of 2 EUR; evidence suggests strong impact on fairness.,From Section 2.2 and results section on strong punishment.,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00153-3.md,N-experiment (no punishment),Paper explicitly defines two treatments: an N-experiment without punishment and a P-experiment with punishment.,1,Punishment availability: no punishment stage; Between-subject treatment: N-experiment,Main treatment difference is presence vs absence of punishment; this row is the no-punishment condition.,1,True,Human subjects in a public goods experiment at Boğaziçi University.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the Economics Laboratory of Boğaziçi University.,1,False,"This treatment uses human participants, not simulated agents.",1,False,No purely analytical model presented for this treatment; it is an experiment.,1,4,“subjects are randomly matched into groups of 4 and interact within the same group for 10 periods.”,1,10,Both treatments repeat the stage game for 10 periods.,1,1,"Subjects choose ‘how much to invest’ from 20 tokens, implying any integer amount from 0 to 20, not all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment of 20 tokens is given to each subject; they decide how much to move to group project. Endowment starts in private account (standard VCM).,0.9,0.4,"For every token invested in group project, each group member earns 0.4 tokens; marginal per capita return is 0.4.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat; design based on Fehr & Gächter (2000) which does not allow communication.,0.8,1,After each period subjects are informed of total payoffs of all group members and total contribution; this is summary info about others.,1,0,"No punishment stage in N-experiment, so identities of punishers cannot be shown.",1,0,No reward mechanism is described.,1,1,Game length is known (10 periods) following Fehr & Gächter (2000); implies subjects know remaining rounds.,0.9,0,N-experiment is explicitly defined as no-punishment condition.,1,N/A,No punishment in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment in this treatment.,1,0,No reward or positive sanction mechanism mentioned.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,"“Each period, subjects receive an endowment of 20 tokens.”",1,6.1,"Average contribution over all periods is 6.1 tokens out of 20. Normalized rate = 6.1/20 = 0.305; however table is in tokens. To avoid fabricating higher precision, we record the raw average (6.1) here as reported, acknowledging this slightly conflicts with ‘rate’ definition.",0.5,6.1,"Table 1: Istanbul-this study, N, ‘Contribution in all periods’ = 6.1 tokens.",1,N/R,No efficiency measure (payoff / max payoff) reported or derivable from available aggregate statistics.,0.2,23.6,"Table 1: Istanbul-this study, N, ‘Earnings’ = 23.6 tokens (per subject per period average).",0.9,Turkey,Experiment conducted at Boğaziçi University in Istanbul.,1,N/R,Only that subjects are from the university subject pool; no age statistics provided.,0.2,N/R,Gender collected as a control but no distribution reported.,0.2,University students (exact level N/R),"Subjects recruited via Economics Laboratory at Boğaziçi University; typical lab pool is university students, but level not specified.",0.7,On site,Conducted in a physical Economics Laboratory; sessions lasted ~45 minutes and subjects were paid in cash on site.,1,"Standard linear public goods game (VCM) with fixed partners: 4 players, 10 periods, endowment 20 tokens, contribution to group project yields 0.4 tokens to each group member per token contributed; payoffs π_i = 20 − g_i + 0.4G; subjects see their contribution and total group payoff after each period; no punishment or rewards.",Summarized from ‘Experimental design and procedures’ and Appendix sections on the contribution stage.,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00153-3.md,P-experiment (with punishment),"Paper labels second treatment as P-experiment, which adds a punishment stage to the N-experiment.",1,"Punishment availability: costly peer punishment enabled (1 token cost, 3 token fine); Between-subject treatment: P-experiment",Independent variable is presence of punishment stage; in this condition punishment is allowed.,1,True,Human subjects in a public goods experiment at Boğaziçi University.,1,True,Controlled lab experiment with random assignment to P vs N treatments.,1,True,Conducted at the Economics Laboratory of Boğaziçi University.,1,False,"This row refers to the actual P-experiment with human subjects, not the agent-based model.",1,False,No purely analytical model for this treatment; it is empirical.,1,4,Same grouping as N-experiment: groups of 4 fixed for 10 periods.,1,10,Contribution and punishment stages are repeated for 10 periods.,1,1,Subjects decide ‘how much to invest’ from 20 tokens; implies any integer amount from 0–20.,1,0,Same VCM structure as N-experiment: each period subjects get 20 tokens and choose how much to contribute; initial endowment is private.,0.9,0.4,"For every token invested in the group project, each group member earns 0.4 tokens; MPCR is 0.4.",1,0,No communication or chat option is mentioned; design follows Fehr & Gächter which does not allow chat.,0.8,1,"After punishment stage, subjects are informed of punishment sent/received and total payoffs of all members (summary information).",1,0,"“After observing group members’ contributions (but not their identities), subjects can assign costly punishment points.” Identities are never revealed.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists.,1,1,Game length is fixed and known (10 periods) as in Fehr & Gächter (2000).,0.9,1,P-experiment ‘adds a punishment stage’ after contributions.,1,1,"“For each token player i assigns to player j, player i loses 1 token.” Cost per punishment point = 1.",1,3,Each punishment point reduces target’s earnings by 3 tokens.,1,0,Only costly deduction (punishment) described; no reward or positive points.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism implemented.,1,20,"“In each period, subjects are given an initial endowment of 20 tokens.” Applies to both treatments.",1,10.9,"Average contribution over all periods in Table 1 for Istanbul P-experiment is 10.9 tokens out of 20. Normalized rate would be 0.545, but only 10.9 is reported; recorded here as in table, noting mismatch with ‘rate’ definition.",0.5,10.9,"Table 1: Istanbul-this study, P, ‘Contribution in all periods’ = 10.9 tokens.",1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported and cannot be accurately computed from averages alone given punishment losses.,0.2,19.4,"Table 1: Istanbul-this study, P, ‘Earnings’ = 19.4 tokens (per subject per period average).",0.9,Turkey,Experiment run in Istanbul at Boğaziçi University.,1,N/R,Age collected as a control in regressions but no descriptive statistics reported.,0.2,N/R,Gender collected as control but no distribution reported.,0.2,University students (exact level N/R),"Subjects from Boğaziçi University economics lab pool; typical participants are university students, but level not specified.",0.7,On site,Conducted in physical Economics Laboratory with on-site cash payment.,1,"Linear public goods game with fixed groups of 4 for 10 periods; endowment 20 tokens per period; contribution to group project yields 0.4 tokens to each group member per token; contribution-stage payoff π_i = 20 − g_i + 0.4G; then a punishment stage where each subject can assign integer punishment points to each other group member, each point costing the punisher 1 token and reducing the target’s payoff by 3 tokens, with earnings truncated at zero: π_i = max{0, 20 − g_i + 0.4G − p_i − 3p_{−i}}; subjects observe others’ contributions (anonymous), punishment sent/received, and total payoffs of all group members after each period; no communication, no rewards.",Summarized from design description and Appendix sections on punishment stage and procedures.,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00153-3.md,Agent-based model for P-experiment (simulation),Section 4 describes an ‘agent-based model’ and Monte Carlo simulations using estimated decision rules and first-period data.,1,Initial first-period contributions (empirical) and estimated linear decision rules for contribution updating and punishment; no alternative parameter treatments within this model reported.,Simulation uses empirical first-period contributions and fixed estimated rules; no separate experimental conditions within the simulation are mentioned.,0.8,False,Agent-based model simulates agents using empirical parameters but no human decisions during simulation.,1,False,"This is a computational simulation, not a controlled human experiment.",1,False,No lab participants; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Described as ‘agent-based modeling’ with Monte Carlo simulations generating contribution paths.,1,False,Simulation uses estimated linear rules numerically; no closed-form analytical solution.,1,4,Model simulates the same 15 groups of 4 subjects as in the P-experiment.,1,10,Simulation reproduces contribution dynamics over the same 10 periods as experiment (see Fig. 4).,1,1,Simulated contributions are continuous/integers over 0–20 following experimental design; not restricted to all-or-nothing.,0.9,N/A,No real endowment is allocated; this is a numerical simulation referencing experimental structure.,1,0.4,"Model uses same payoff function as P-experiment, with contribution return 0.4 per token.",1,N/A,No human communication in simulations; chat concept does not apply.,1,N/A,Information display to humans is irrelevant; agents follow programmed rules.,1,N/A,No human identities; agents’ decisions are based on numerical states only.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism in the model, mirroring experiment.",1,N/A,Agents do not ‘see’ rounds; horizon is exogenously set in code.,1,1,Simulation includes punishment decisions and sanction points based on estimated probabilities (step 3 in model description).,1,1,Model uses same cost structure: each sanction point costs 1 token.,1,3,"Each simulated punishment point reduces target payoff by 3 tokens, as in experiment.",1,0,No positive sanctioning is simulated; only costly punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,Tokens are only bookkeeping devices in the model; no actual endowment to humans.,1,N/R,Paper shows trajectories and fit qualitatively (Fig. 4) but does not report summary contribution averages from simulations.,0.2,N/R,No table of simulated average contributions or final-period contributions is provided; only plots.,0.2,N/R,No explicit efficiency measures from simulations reported.,0.2,N/R,Simulated payoffs are not tabulated; figures only show contributions.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; only simulated agents based on Istanbul parameters.,1,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,N/A,No human participants in the simulation.,1,No human,Agent-based Monte Carlo simulation without human subjects.,1,"Agent-based model replicates the P-experiment structure: 15 groups of 4 agents, 10 periods, contribution stage with MPCR 0.4, and punishment stage with 1:3 cost/fine. First-period contributions are taken from the empirical P-experiment; in subsequent periods, each agent either keeps or changes their contribution based on state-dependent probabilities estimated from logit regressions (Table 3). If they change, new contributions are drawn from linear updating rules estimated via OLS (Table 4), differing depending on whether last contribution was below or at/above group average. Punishment decisions are simulated using probit-estimated probabilities (Table 6), primarily as binary choices of 0 or 1 punishment point with 85.06% of cases being 0 or 1. Monte Carlo simulations are run for each group, generating contribution trajectories compared to the empirical paths (Fig. 4).",Summarizes ‘Details of the agent-based model’ section and description around Fig. 3 and Fig. 4.,1
10.1111_exsy.13527.md,Series 1 – Full reward × punishment grid (100 × 100 conditions),"The first series of simulations runs 10,000 conditions defined by all integer combinations of reward and punishment from 1 to 100, with the full 9-gene architecture and no memory.",1,"Reward level (1–100) and Punishment level (1–100) determining payoff matrix; all 10,000 reward–punishment combinations","Section 2.1–2.3: reward and punishment are varied independently from 1 to 100, creating 10,000 conditions.",1,False,The study simulates 1000 virtual organisms via evolutionary algorithms; no human participants.,1,False,"This is a numerical simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects.",1,False,No physical lab setting or human subjects; purely computational.,1,True,Described as Monte Carlo evolutionary simulations implemented in Python with virtual organisms.,1,False,"Although some analytical expressions (e.g., EU) are derived, the main results come from numerical simulations.",1,2,Each decision situation is a pairing of two organisms (Individual 1 and Individual 2 in Table 2).,1,500,They simulate evolution over 500 cycles/generations and record outcomes at the 500th generation.,1,1,"Each organism chooses between two discrete actions (cooperate or confront); there is no continuous contribution choice, so treated as ‘all or nothing’ in the contribution sense.",0.9,N/A,There is no divisible endowment or public-good contribution; agents have health and binary actions.,1,N/R,No public goods game or linear multiplier structure is defined; only a confrontation/cooperation payoff matrix.,1,N/A,No human communication; purely simulated organisms with no messaging.,1,0,Organisms do not receive round-by-round summaries; only health is tracked internally in the model description.,0.9,N/A,No identities or visibility of who punished whom are modelled; payoffs are internal calculations.,1,N/A,No identity-based visibility of rewards is modelled.,1,0,Organisms do not have cognition about the number of cycles; there is no display of remaining rounds.,0.9,1,The payoff matrix includes a Punishment parameter affecting health when both confront.,1,N/R,"Punishment is a state variable in the payoff matrix, not a cost per punishment unit chosen by a player.",1,N/R,There is no separate ‘technology’ translating punishment spending into losses; punishment directly parameterizes the outcome matrix.,1,1,Reward parameter determines positive payoffs when cooperation or successful confrontation occurs.,1,N/R,"Reward is an exogenous payoff parameter, not something agents pay to bestow.",1,N/R,No technology that converts spending into rewards is defined.,1,N/R,"Organisms have health starting at 100, but there is no per-round token endowment in a game-theoretic sense.",0.8,N/A,No contribution or public-good decision; only binary confront/cooperate choices.,1,N/A,No continuous contribution variable exists.,1,N/R,"They report expected utilities, number of survivors, and mean health but not normalized efficiency relative to a maximum cooperative benchmark.",0.7,N/R,Outcomes are tracked as individual health and survivors; aggregate group payoff is not explicitly reported.,0.6,N/A,No human participants; only virtual organisms.,1,N/A,"No human participants; age is a simulated property of organisms, not demographic.",1,N/A,No human or gendered agents are described.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The work is explicitly a simulation using evolutionary algorithms and Monte Carlo methods with virtual organisms.,1,"Pairwise 2×2 confrontation/cooperation game with payoffs parameterized by Reward (1–100) and Punishment (1–100). Payoff matrix: (C,C) → Reward/2 each; (C,F) → 0 for cooperator, Reward for confronter; (F,F) → (Reward − Punishment)/2 each. Population of 1000 organisms evolves over 500 cycles with selection of top 10% by health and 1% mutation rate on 9 genes controlling confrontation probability and its modulation by context.","Summarized from Table 2 and Section 2.1–2.3 describing pairing, payoffs, genetic architecture, population size, and evolutionary process.",1
10.1111_exsy.13527.md,Series 2 – Zero-sum environment without memory,Second series of simulations manipulates punishment in a zero-sum variant and includes a memory factor (on/off); this row captures the ‘no memory’ condition across punishment levels.,0.95,Zero-sum payoff structure with punishment level varied (50 levels) and memory disabled,"Section 5–6: simulations in zero-sum games with punishment varying; memory is an experimental factor with two levels, this row is the no-memory level.",0.9,False,Again purely virtual organisms; no human subjects.,1,False,Still a numerical simulation rather than an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab or field setting; computational.,1,True,Described as the second round of simulations using the same model with modifications.,1,False,"Results come from simulated gene evolution and ANOVA, not closed-form derivations alone.",1,2,They continue to use pairwise confront/cooperate interactions as in the first model.,1,500,"They state the ‘second round of simulations used the same model’, which previously used 500 cycles.",0.9,1,Same binary choice between cooperation and confrontation as Series 1.,1,N/A,No endowment or contribution decision.,1,N/R,No public good or linear return structure.,1,N/A,Virtual agents with no communication mechanism.,1,0,No notion of agents viewing others’ histories; only internal memory of own last fight is later added (not in this condition).,0.9,N/A,No identities or observation model for punishment assignments.,1,N/A,No identity-based reward information.,1,0,Agents do not know horizon length; nothing is displayed.,0.9,1,They explicitly manipulate ‘level of punishment’ and examine its effect on risk taking in zero-sum games.,1,N/R,"Punishment remains an exogenous outcome parameter in the payoff structure, not a chosen costly action.",1,N/R,No explicit mapping from punishment expenditure to losses.,1,1,"Even in zero-sum games, rewards (gains in health) exist but are exactly offset by costs to the opponent.",1,N/R,"Rewards are outcomes of confrontation/cooperation, not actively bestowed at a cost.",1,N/R,No reward ‘technology’ parameter is defined.,1,N/R,Baseline health is 100 but not described as a per-round endowment.,0.8,N/A,No contributions.,1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"They report ANOVA on gene CP and others as a function of punishment and memory, not overall efficiency metrics.",0.7,N/R,They focus on gene values and not on aggregate payoffs.,0.6,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Computational simulation only.,1,"Zero-sum modification of the confrontation/cooperation game: the cost of confrontation is removed for the winner and fully imposed on the loser; the environment is controlled so that the expected utility (EU) of both cooperation and confrontation is always zero. Additionally, the cost of cooperating when the opponent confronts is set to half the available reward. Same 1000-organism evolutionary framework and 9-gene architecture as in Series 1, but analysed as a function of punishment levels in ANOVA.","Summarized from Methods section describing three modifications for zero-sum simulations, including payoff redefinition and EU=0 constraint.",1
10.1111_exsy.13527.md,Series 2 – Zero-sum environment with memory,Same zero-sum simulations as previous row but with the memory mechanism turned on; memory is treated as an additional experimental factor.,0.95,Zero-sum payoff structure with punishment level varied (50 levels) and memory enabled (±10% adjustment to confrontation probability based on last fight outcome),Methods: memory is implemented as a modifier of confrontation probability based on the last decision’s health outcome; ANOVA factors: Memory (on/off) × Punishment levels.,0.9,False,Still purely simulated organisms.,1,False,Numerical experiment but not an empirical human or field experiment.,1,False,Not a lab experiment with participants.,1,True,It is the ‘second round of simulations’ with an added memory mechanism.,1,False,Main outcomes come from simulation plus ANOVA.,1,2,Pairwise interactions remain as in the base model.,1,500,They state the second round uses the same model; the first model ran for 500 cycles.,0.9,1,Binary cooperation vs confrontation choice remains unchanged.,1,N/A,No divisible endowment or contribution.,1,N/R,No public goods aspect.,1,N/A,Simulated organisms have no communication channel.,1,0,Agents only use their own last outcome via memory; they do not view others’ earnings or histories.,0.9,N/A,No identity visibility modelled.,1,N/A,No identification of reward sources.,1,0,"Agents do not know the total number of cycles; memory is only of last encounter, not horizon length.",0.9,1,Punishment magnitude is manipulated and drives risk taking in zero-sum games.,1,N/R,Punishment is part of the exogenous payoff structure.,1,N/R,No separate technology mapping is described.,1,1,Rewards still exist as health gains offset by others’ losses in zero-sum environment.,1,N/R,"Rewards are outcomes, not deliberate costly actions.",1,N/R,No such parameter defined.,1,N/R,"Health starts at 100, but not treated as a per-round monetary endowment.",0.8,N/A,No contribution decision.,1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,They report how gene CP and others vary with punishment and memory; no explicit efficiency metric.,0.7,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not presented; focus is on gene-level ANOVA results.,0.6,N/A,No human data.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Entirely simulation-based with artificial organisms plus a memory mechanism.,1,"Zero-sum version of the confrontation/cooperation game where: (1) the confrontation cost is removed for the winner and fully imposed on the loser; (2) the environment is controlled so that the EU of cooperation and confrontation is always zero; (3) the cost of cooperating against a confronting opponent equals half the available reward. Memory rule: if the last decision was to confront and it improved health, the organism becomes 10% more likely to confront next time; if the last confrontation reduced health, it becomes 10% less likely to confront. Same 1000-organism population, genetic architecture, and evolutionary selection process as in other simulations, with ANOVA showing effects of punishment level and memory on genes (particularly CP).",Directly from Methods (memory implementation and zero-sum modifications) and Results (ANOVA table on memory × punishment).,1
10.1007_s40881-023-00157-z.md,Reanalysis of Herrmann et al. (2008) public goods game with punishment (all cities pooled),"The paper conducts a secondary analysis of the existing experimental data from Herrmann et al. (2008), pooling across all 16 cities.",1,"Individual contributions (0, 1–9, 10–19, 20 tokens), target contributions (0, 1–9, 10–19, 20 tokens), and study location (16 cities) as predictors in punishment regressions.",Section 3 divides contribution decisions into four categories and includes both punisher and target contribution categories plus city indicators in regressions.,0.9,True,Data come from human-subject public goods experiments in Herrmann et al. (2008).,1,True,Original data were generated in controlled public goods experiments; this paper reuses that experimental dataset.,1,True,Herrmann et al. (2008) is a standard lab-style public goods experiment run in multiple cities; no indication of field/online setting.,0.9,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation is described; only reanalysis of human experimental data.,1,False,"The paper uses regression/statistical analysis, not formal analytical modeling.",1,4,Herrmann et al. (2008) used standard 4-person public goods games with punishment; this is background knowledge about that study.,0.8,10,Herrmann et al. (2008) used 10 periods in the public goods with punishment condition; this is standard and widely cited.,0.8,1,"Public goods game allows any contribution between 0 and 20 tokens (0, 1–9, 10–19, 20 categories).",1,0,Standard linear public goods setup: all tokens start in private accounts; participants choose how much to contribute.,0.9,N/R,"This reanalysis paper does not report the multiplier or MPCR, and it is not derivable from text provided.",0,0,No mention of communication; original Herrmann et al. (2008) public goods with punishment design did not allow chat.,0.8,1,"In public goods with punishment experiments of this type, subjects observe others’ contributions and resulting payoffs to assign punishment; implied by ‘after seeing the decisions of other group members, participants can pay an additional cost to impose fines’.",0.7,0,Standard Herrmann et al. (2008) design uses anonymous punishment; no mention here of identities being revealed.,0.7,0,There is no reward stage in this design; only costly punishment.,1,N/R,The paper does not state whether participants were informed about the total number of periods or remaining rounds.,0,1,The core focus is on punishment in the public goods game; text describes a standard punishment stage after contributions.,1,N/R,Cost per punishment point (and fine magnitude) from Herrmann et al. (2008) is not reported in this paper text.,0,N/R,The fine imposed per unit spent is not specified in this reanalysis paper.,0,0,Only punishment (fines) is discussed; no mention of any reward or positive sanction mechanism.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism exists, so no cost parameter.",1,N/A,"No reward mechanism exists, so no effectiveness parameter.",1,20,"Section 3 defines full contribution as 20 tokens and other categories relative to 20, implying each player’s endowment is 20 tokens per period.",0.9,N/R,"The paper reports relationships (e.g., high, low, full contribution) but not average normalized contribution rates.",0,N/R,"No specific mean or median contribution amounts are reported, only categorical ranges and qualitative patterns.",0,N/R,The paper does not report group efficiency measures or ratios of realized to maximum payoffs.,0,N/R,No numeric group payoff results are given; only punishment probabilities and amounts are discussed.,0,Multiple (16 cities in industrialized countries worldwide),"Abstract: ‘public goods games conducted in industrialized countries throughout the world (Herrmann et al. 2008)’. Specific cities are not listed here, only that there are 16.",0.7,N/R,Age ranges or means are not reported in this reanalysis paper.,0,N/R,No gender composition information is presented.,0,N/R,The paper does not state whether subjects are students or provide education demographics.,0,On site,Original Herrmann et al. (2008) experiments were run in physical lab settings in different cities; this paper only reuses those data.,0.9,"Standard linear public goods game with punishment: endowment 20 tokens; contributions can be 0–20; after observing others’ contributions, participants can pay to impose fines on others. Data pooled from 16 cities; analyses focus on who is punished (by contribution level) and who punishes (defectors vs contributors) and how this varies across cities.","Summarized from sections describing the design and the contribution categories (defect, low, high, full) and punishment stage.",0.9
10.1007_s40881-023-00159-x.md,Baseline game – No fine (F = 0),Condition with the baseline prisoner's dilemma payoffs (Table 1a) and no formal fine; contrasted with the fine condition in Table 1b.,1,Presence of fine in current match: No fine (F = 0).,Main institutional variable randomly assigned each match is whether a fine F = 10 is imposed upon defection.,1,True,Human participants in a lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled randomized lab experiment manipulating fines across matches.,1,True,Conducted at Ecole Polytechnique experimental laboratory.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported.,1,False,Paper includes theory but this condition refers to the lab experiment with humans.,1,2,"Each match is a 2-player prisoner's dilemma (“partner”, “players”).",1,0,Matches are indefinitely repeated with random continuation (δ=0.75); realized rounds vary; a fixed number is not defined.,0.2,1,"Binary choice between C and D each round, not a continuous contribution game; all-or-nothing contribution interpretation does not apply.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game with divisible endowment; binary C/D prisoner’s dilemma.,1,N/A,"Prisoner’s dilemma payoff matrix, not a linear public goods game.",1,0,Interactions are anonymous with no mention of communication.,0.9,0,Participants only receive full feedback about actions in the current match; no summary of others’ histories or payoffs.,0.9,0,No punishment mechanism; players are anonymously matched; no identities revealed.,1,0,No rewarding mechanism in the design.,1,0,Random continuation rule with δ=0.75; no indication that remaining rounds or total length are displayed.,0.8,0,In the baseline matrix (Table 1a) there is no formal fine or punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in this condition.,1,0,Design only introduces fines for defection in the other condition; no rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Payoffs are reported directly as token outcomes per action profile; no explicit per-round endowment stated.,0.2,0.19,"Average first-round cooperation rate in baseline environment is 19% (“average cooperation rate jumps from 19% in the baseline, to 46% with a fine”). Rate corresponds to cooperation in a binary C/D PD.",1,N/R,Only binary actions C/D are observed; no numerical ‘amount’ contributed is defined.,1,N/R,Paper does not report efficiency as total payoff relative to maximum.,0,N/R,"Average or total group payoffs are not reported, only individual earnings summary (12.1 Euros, average 20 matches).",0,France,Sessions conducted at Ecole Polytechnique (France); participants are students and employees of the university.,0.9,N/R,Age is collected in a questionnaire but no summary statistics are reported.,0,N/R,Gender was asked but distributions are not reported.,0,Mainly university students; some university employees,“46 participants are both students (85% of the experiment pool) and employees of the university (15%).”,1,On site,Lab sessions at Ecole Polytechnique experimental laboratory imply in-person on-site participation.,1,"Indefinitely repeated 2-player prisoner’s dilemma with random continuation δ=0.75; quasi-stranger rematching between matches; payoffs: (C,C)=40,40; (C,D)=12,60; (D,C)=60,12; (D,D)=35,35. First round decisions analyzed as sufficient statistics under strategy restriction (AD, TFT, GT).","Summarized from Sect. 2.1, Table 1a, Appendix 1.",1
