/R,Gender composition not reported.,1,Undergraduate students at Missouri State University,Explicitly stated.,1,On site,Standard physical lab sessions using oTree and ORSEE.,0.95,"Three-player two-period Bertrand game repeated 16 rounds with random role assignment (Role 1 with link; Role 2 isolated) and random rematching each round. Two linked players send simultaneous restricted-form messages before Period 1; isolated player has no message stage. Payments: pay-one-random-round, 8 points = $1 plus $5 show-up.",Summarizes N1-specific communication asymmetry and general procedures.,1
10.1002_soej.12626.md,N2 – Two links with leader (star network),Treatment N2 has two communication links: one player (leader) communicates with both others; the other two cannot communicate with each other.,1,Communication network structure: N2 (two links; star network with one leader communicating bilaterally with two followers).,"N2 defined as one player communicating with the other two, others not connected.",1,True,Laboratory experiment with human undergraduates.,1,True,Controlled lab treatment varying network structure.,1,True,Conducted at Missouri State University using oTree.,1,False,No computer-only runs.,1,False,No standalone analytical experiment.,1,3,Three-person game in all treatments.,1,16,Sessions had 16 rounds.,1,1,Players choose between discrete actions; they can always pick either option.,0.6,N/R,No contribution endowment.,1,N/R,Not a linear public good.,1,1,Leader and the two followers exchange structured messages along the two links before Period 1.,1,1,"After Period 2, players observed actions from both periods each round.",0.8,0,No explicit costly punishment stage.,1,0,No explicit reward stage.,1,1,Finite 16-round horizon is part of instructions.,0.8,0,No dedicated punishment technology beyond equilibrium punishments via Y in Period 2.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No dedicated reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Only payoffs from actions shown; no explicit endowment.,0.5,N/R,"Study reports cooperation frequencies (e.g., B in Period 1, Z in Period 2) not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Efficiency relative to 120-per-player maximum not reported.,0.6,N/R,Only average individual payoff (64.9) reported for N2.,0.7,USA,"Experiment location in Missouri, USA.",1,Undergraduate students; exact age distribution N/R,Subjects are undergraduates; ages not stated.,0.9,N/R,Gender not discussed.,1,Undergraduate students at Missouri State University,Explicitly indicated.,1,On site,Lab-based sessions with in-person participation.,0.95,Three-player two-period Bertrand game with star communication network. One Role 1 (leader) and two Role 2 players per group each round; roles randomly assigned each round. All communicators send the restricted-form message (suggested Period 1 action and contingent Period 2 actions) simultaneously before Period 1. Random rematching each of 16 rounds. Payment via one randomly selected round with 8 points = $1 plus $5 show-up.,Captures N2-specific leadership communication and general design.,1
10.1002_soej.12626.md,N3 – Full communication (complete network),Treatment N3 has three communication links; all three players can communicate with each other.,1,Communication network structure: N3 (three links; complete network with full two-way communication between all players).,N3 defined as full communication where everyone communicates.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human participants.,1,True,Randomized controlled treatment in lab.,1,True,Conducted at Missouri State University lab via oTree.,1,False,No simulation-only results.,1,False,Not a separate analytical exercise.,1,3,Three-person TPBG for all treatments.,1,16,Each session had 16 rounds.,1,1,Binary action set each period; players free to pick either action.,0.6,N/R,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,Not a public goods setting.,1,1,All players send and receive messages via three communication links before Period 1.,1,1,Players observed actions from both periods after Period 2 each round.,0.8,0,No explicit punishment tokens or IDs.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,1,Finite horizon (16 rounds) known to subjects.,0.8,0,No separate costly punishment institution; only strategic Y/Z choices.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No explicit reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,Only payoffs per action given; no starting tokens specified.,0.5,N/R,"Main DVs are cooperation rates, regression coefficients, and average payoffs.",1,N/R,No contribution variable defined.,1,N/R,Paper reports average payoff for N3 (71) and cooperation frequencies but not normalized efficiency.,0.6,N/R,Group payoff not directly reported; only mean payoff per player.,0.7,USA,"Session location at Missouri State University, USA.",1,Undergraduate students; exact age distribution N/R,Participants are undergraduates; no specific age stats given.,0.9,N/R,No report of gender shares.,1,Undergraduate students at Missouri State University,Explicitly noted.,1,On site,In-person lab sessions described; no online implementation mentioned.,0.95,Three-player two-period Bertrand game with complete communication network. All three players send restricted-form messages simultaneously before Period 1 to all others. 16 rounds with random rematching each round. Average total payoff in N3 is 71 points per player; this treatment yields the highest cooperation rates in both periods and higher payoffs than N1 and N2.,Captures N3-specific full communication and main performance outcome.,1
10.1038_s41598-024-57842-0.md,Analytical/simulation model – compulsory panoptical PGG with punishment parameters d and u,"The paper presents a single mathematical model of a compulsory, fully observable public goods game with centralized punishment, characterized by parameters d (fraction of defectors punished) and u (severity of payoff reduction). There are no distinct empirical treatments; d and u are varied continuously in analysis and figures.",0.9,"Fraction of defectors punished d ∈ [0,1] and punishment severity u ≥ 0 (combined as α = d·u), with example parameter sets in Fig. 2: (u=0, varying d); (u=0.4, varying d); (u=1, varying d); (u=1.2, varying d).","Model explicitly defines d as the fraction of defectors sanctioned and u as the fraction by which payoffs are reduced, and explores their effects (g(x,α), equilibria).",1,False,No human participants; the study uses evolutionary game theory and replicator dynamics.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only theoretical analysis and numerical illustration.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting; purely theoretical work.,1,True,"They numerically explore the replicator dynamics (e.g., Fig. 2 described as numerical simulations) without human subjects.",0.9,True,"Derives closed-form payoffs, equilibrium points, Jacobian, and stability conditions analytically.",1,5,"General model allows any n≥2 but specific numerical examples (Fig. 2) use n=5 (“Parameters: r=3, n=5”). Use that as the concrete configuration.",0.7,1,Stage game is a single public goods interaction; repeated interactions are modeled via continuous-time replicator dynamics rather than discrete experimental rounds.,0.7,1,"Each individual chooses whether or not to contribute c=1; no intermediate contribution levels are mentioned, so contribution is binary all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,No human endowments or default allocation; purely theoretical payoffs with decision to contribute c=1.,1,0.6,"MPCR = r/n. For the numerical example with r=3 and n=5, MPCR = 3/5 = 0.6.",0.9,N/A,No human communication; model is evolutionary dynamics without explicit signaling.,1,N/A,"No information treatment for human participants; observation is modeled as perfect monitoring in payoffs, not an interface feature.",1,N/A,No individuals or identities; defectors are punished randomly as a fraction d without identity structure.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the model; only punishment of defectors.,1,N/A,No subjects or user interface; time is continuous in replicator dynamics.,1,1,Core of the model is a centralized punishment system that reduces defectors’ payoffs by a fraction u for a fraction d of defectors.,1,N/R,Model reduces defectors’ payoffs but does not specify an explicit cost borne by punishers or an institution.,0.2,N/R,Punishment is represented as a proportional reduction u of defectors’ payoffs; no cost-per-token or multiplier technology is specified in experimental-economics terms.,0.3,0,Only negative sanctions on defectors are modeled; no positive rewards for cooperators.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism included.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism included.,1,N/R,"Each cooperator contributes c=1, but there is no explicit statement of an overall token endowment per player.",0.3,N/R,"Outcomes are described in terms of asymptotic frequencies of strategies (x for cooperators, y for defectors) but no specific steady-state contribution fractions are numerically reported as DVs; analysis is qualitative regarding full cooperation vs full defection.",0.3,N/R,No specific numerical token contributions per player (beyond generic c=1) are presented as a measured dependent variable.,0.2,N/R,They do not compute group payoff relative to maximum possible as an explicit scalar; they focus on stability of cooperation vs defection.,0.2,N/R,Total group payoffs are not reported as outcome statistics; only individual payoffs and their differences are given analytically.,0.2,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical/simulation work.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Study is an evolutionary game-theoretic model plus numerical illustrations; no online, lab, or field data collection.",1,"Compulsory, fully observable public goods game with well-mixed population and two strategies (cooperate: contribute c=1; defect: contribute 0). Group size n is general but numerical illustrations use n=5 and multiplication factor r=3. A centralized punishment institution randomly sanctions a fraction d of defectors, reducing their payoff by a fraction u (possibly u>1). Dynamics follow two-strategy replicator equations with payoffs p_c = r·(n_c/n) − 1 and p_d adjusted by punishment. Key combined punishment parameter α = d·u determines existence and stability of equilibria.","Summarized from Methods and Analysis sections, including payoff definitions, compulsory participation, perfect monitoring, parameter choices, and replicator dynamics.",1
10.1017_ehs.2024.18.md,Asocial control (no social information),Control group in part two that received no social information; explicitly contrasted with six social information conditions.,1,Social information: none (asocial control); no specified source or content.,"Independent variable is experimentally manipulated social information with factors source (majority vs most successful) and behaviour (spite, neutral, altruism). Control has no information.",1,True,Human MTurk participants played an online game.,1,True,"Between-participants randomized assignment to conditions, controlled online game.",1,False,"Conducted online via MTurk, not in a physical lab.",1,False,"Human subjects, not purely simulated agents.",1,False,No formal analytical model as the primary method; Bayesian stats applied to experimental data.,1,2,"Participants believed they interacted with one other participant/bot partner (""partner"", ""second participant"").",0.9,1,Part two decision is explicitly one-shot: a single score-change decision by the decider.,1,1,"In part two, a slider allowed any amount between maximum spite and maximum altruism (continuous choice, not binary).",1,0,All points/earnings remained with the participant until they chose to spend points to change partner’s score; no default allocation to a public fund.,0.8,N/A,"Not a public goods game; payoff change is a 3:1 ratio of partner score-change to own cost, but not framed as MPCR.",0.4,0,No communication or chat between participants mentioned; interaction only via decisions and displayed info.,1,0,Control condition received no social information about others’ decisions or earnings.,1,0,Partner was anonymous; no identities revealed for any actions.,1,0,"As above, all interactions were anonymous.",1,1,Participants were told part two was a one-shot decision; total number of decisions (one) was made clear.,0.9,1,Participants could pay points to decrease partner’s score (spiteful option).,1,1,"Text: ""It cost the participant one point for every three points ... withdrawn from their partner's score.""",1,3,"Each 1 point spent changes partner’s score by 3 points (""3:1 ratio of partner's score-change to cost"").",1,1,Participants could pay points to increase partner’s score (altruistic option).,1,1,Same 1 point cost per 3 points increase for partner.,1,3,Same 3:1 ratio applied for donations to partner.,1,N/R,"Endowment in part two is the accumulated score from part one, which varies per participant and is not summarized numerically.",0.3,N/R,"They report frequencies and mean of ""social behaviour"" (scale -10 to 10), but not normalized fraction of endowment spent.",0.2,N/R,"Only summary statistics of ""social behaviour"" metric are given; no mean raw points spent or transferred by condition.",0.2,N/R,No report or derivation of efficiency (payoff relative to maximum) by condition.,0.1,N/R,No mean total payoffs for pairs reported by condition.,0.1,N/R,Recruited from MTurk; no explicit restriction or distribution by country given.,0.2,Median 32 years; IQR 9,"Methods: median age 32, IQR = 9; no condition-wise breakdown.",1,"197 male, 75 female, 2 non-binary (overall sample)",Overall counts reported; no allocation by condition.,1,N/R,Education level not reported.,0.1,Online,Conducted online via Dallinger with participants recruited on MTurk.,1,"Two-part online task. Part one: five-round interaction with a bot, 10 points per round, could send any amount; bot returned own send plus random -2 to +5 (capped 0–12). Part two: one-shot decider role with slider to pay up to 10 points to change partner’s score by up to ±30, at 3:1 ratio; decisions framed as increasing (altruism), decreasing (spite), or no change (neutral). Control receives no social information before deciding.",Summary of detailed procedure and payoff structure from methods section.,1
