10.1103_PhysRevE.109.054303.md,Baseline setup,Paper defines four game setups; this condition is labeled (1) Baseline.,1,"Game setup: baseline (migration without rewards or migration cost); enhancement factor r ∈ [1,3]; migration probability p ∈ [0,1]",Baseline differs from other setups by absence of discount factor and migration cost; r and p are systematically varied in Fig. 3 and Fig. 4.,1,False,"Study uses numerical simulations with agents, no human participants.",1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational model.,1,False,No laboratory setting; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Described explicitly as numerical/computational simulations of agent-based PGG on a lattice.,1,False,No closed-form analytical model or proofs; all results from simulations.,1,1440,"Text: 144 groups on 12×12 grid, with 10 agents in each group initially (144×10).",1,800,Main heatmaps and time series (Figs. 2–4) use 800 rounds; this is the principal configuration described for the four setups.,0.9,1,"Agents choose contributions from {0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1}, so contributions are fractional, not binary all-or-nothing.",1,0,Each round endowment E=1 is given privately and agents choose how much to contribute; no part starts in public fund.,1,N/R,Only enhancement factor r and group size n_i are given; MPCR=r/n_i is not provided because n_i varies endogenously through migration and no single value is reported.,0.5,N/A,No human participants; communication not applicable.,1,0,Blind model: agents lack any knowledge about contributions and payoffs of neighbors; implies no summaries of others shown.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment actions by agents or identification of punishers in this simulation setting.,1,N/A,No peer reward actions or identities; rewards are institutional and automatic in other setups only.,1,0,No indication agents know total or remaining rounds; typical assumption in such simulations is myopic/indefinite horizon.,0.5,0,Baseline has only migration option without any explicit cost or penalty beyond standard PGG payoffs.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism defined in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment defined.,1,0,No extra payoff or discount factor in baseline; only standard PGG returns.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in baseline setup.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in baseline setup.,1,1,"Equation section: E represents agent round endowment, set to E=1.",1,N/R,"Outcome contributions are reported as color scales/curves over ranges of r and p, not a single numerical value for this condition.",0.3,N/R,Only mean contribution trajectories and heatmaps are shown graphically; no explicit numeric values tabulated.,0.3,N/R,No explicit efficiency (payoff relative to max) reported or derivable as a single value for this setup.,0.1,N/R,Group payoffs are not explicitly given; only qualitative behavior described.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entire study is based on numerical simulations with artificial agents.,1,"Spatial public goods game on 12×12 grid (144 groups); initial group size 10; agents start with contributions in {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1} sampled uniformly; endowment per round E=1; blind satisfaction rule based on benefit b_i≥c_i; unsatisfied agents migrate to one of four neighboring groups with probability p or decrease contribution by 0.25 with probability 1−p.",Summarized directly from Methods sections describing spatial structure and blind model dynamics for baseline.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.109.054303.md,Rewarding scheme setup,Paper defines four game setups; this condition is labeled (2) Rewarding scheme.,1,"Game setup: rewarding scheme (institutional discount factor δ_i increasing with group loyalty, no migration cost); enhancement factor r ∈ [1,3]; migration probability p ∈ [0,1]","Rewarding scheme differs from baseline by inclusion of discount factor δ_i, varied over same r and p ranges in Fig. 3(b) and Fig. 4(b).",1,False,This is a numerical simulation with artificial agents.,1,False,Not a human or field experiment.,1,False,No lab environment; purely computational.,1,True,Game dynamics under rewarding scheme implemented in simulations.,1,False,No analytical derivation for this condition.,1,1440,Same grid size 12×12 with 10 agents per group at start as in baseline and Fig. 3 description.,1,800,Fig. 3(b) and Fig. 4(b) use 800 rounds as stated; this is the main configuration across setups.,0.9,1,"Same contribution choice set {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1}; not binary.",1,0,As in general model: endowment E=1 in private account; agents choose contribution.,1,N/R,"MPCR depends on r and current group size n_i, which varies; no fixed value reported.",0.5,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,Still under blind model; agents lack info about others' contributions/payoffs.,1,N/A,Institutional discount factor is not peer punishment; no identities involved.,1,N/A,Institutional rewarding scheme; no visibility of rewarding identities.,1,0,No indication of horizon information used by agents.,0.5,1,Authors state rewarding scheme incorporates both punishment and reward via discount factor δ_i<1 when entering group; this reduces payoff relative to neutral baseline.,0.8,0,"Punishment is implemented as an institutional payoff discount, not as a costly action chosen by agents; there is no per-unit cost for applying it.",0.7,N/R,Model gives payoff formula π_i=(E−c_i)+rδ_i c_i with δ_i starting at 0.6; but no simple ‘per coin’ punishment mapping like standard punishment tokens is defined.,0.4,1,Discount rate δ_i increases by 0.1 per round up to 1 for staying in same group; described as a rewarding scheme or loyalty reward.,1,0,Institutional reward is automatic without agent-borne cost; no separate resource spent for rewarding.,0.7,N/R,"Reward magnitude per unit is embedded in δ_i, but there is no simple per-token reward parameter.",0.5,1,Same base public goods game with E=1 per round.,1,N/R,Results shown as heatmaps and time series for various r and p; no single aggregate number for this entire condition reported.,0.3,N/R,Only graphical summaries; no numeric aggregates directly provided.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency not explicitly calculated for the rewarding scheme.,0.1,N/R,No direct payoff numbers or tables.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations only.,1,"Same spatial PGG as baseline (12×12 groups, initial group size 10, E=1, contributions from {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1}, blind model). Additional institutional discount factor δ_i: each incoming agent starts with δ_i=0.6, which increases by 0.1 per round of staying in the same group until δ_i=1; if the agent migrates, δ_i resets to 0.6. Payoff becomes π_i=(E−c_i)+r δ_i c_i.",Taken from Methods section describing rewarding scheme modification.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.109.054303.md,Migration cost setup,Paper defines four game setups; this condition is labeled (3) Migration cost.,1,"Game setup: migration cost (institutional migration cost c_m=0.2 for unsatisfied migrants; no discount-based rewarding); enhancement factor r ∈ [1,3]; migration probability p ∈ [0,1]",Migration cost setup adds c_m=0.2 to baseline dynamics and is explored over same r and p ranges in Fig. 3(c) and Fig. 4(c).,1,False,"Simulation with artificial agents, no human participants.",1,False,Not a human/field experiment.,1,False,No physical lab; computational model.,1,True,Results from numerical simulations under migration cost rule.,1,False,No analytical treatment of this condition.,1,1440,Same 12×12 grid and initial 10 agents per group as other setups in Fig. 3.,1,800,Heatmaps for migration cost setup use 800 rounds; explicitly same setup as Fig. 3.,0.9,1,"Contribution options unchanged: {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1}.",1,0,Endowment kept privately until contributed; no default allocation to public good.,1,N/R,MPCR depends on evolving group size; no single value given.,0.5,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,Blind model: agents have no information about others; thus no summaries shown.,1,N/A,Punishment is institutional via migration cost; agents do not select targets; no identity revelation.,1,N/A,No rewarding scheme in this setup.,1,0,No evidence agents know the total rounds.,0.5,1,Migration cost is explicitly described as a punishment mechanism applied when migrating to neighboring groups.,1,0.2,Migration cost chosen as c_m=0.2.,1,N/R,Text defines condition c_i−c_m≤b_i for migration but does not specify an explicit per-unit reduction scheme comparable to typical punishment tokens.,0.5,0,No loyalty reward or discount factor here; solely migration cost added to baseline.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Same PGG with E=1 per round.,1,N/R,Contributions reported by heatmaps over r and p; no single numeric DV for entire condition.,0.3,N/R,No tabulated final contributions; only figures.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency is not directly computed for migration cost scenario.,0.1,N/R,Payoffs not given numerically.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation only.,1,"Same spatial PGG and blind decision rule as baseline, but when an unsatisfied agent is willing to migrate, the condition is adjusted to c_i−c_m ≤ b_i, with migration cost c_m=0.2. If this condition holds and migration is selected (probability p), the agent moves to a neighboring group while keeping its contribution level; otherwise, it reduces contribution by 0.25 without migrating.",Summarizes migration cost modification described in Methods.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.109.054303.md,Combined setup (rewarding scheme + migration cost),Paper defines four game setups; this condition is labeled (4) Combined.,1,"Game setup: combined (both loyalty-based rewarding scheme via discount factor δ_i and migration cost c_m=0.2); enhancement factor r ∈ [1,3]; migration probability p ∈ [0,1]",Combined setup includes both mechanisms previously described and is explored over r and p in Fig. 3(d) and Fig. 4(d).,1,False,"Numerical simulations, no humans.",1,False,Not a human-subject or field experiment.,1,False,Purely computational.,1,True,Condition implemented via computational simulations like other setups.,1,False,No analytical model presented.,1,1440,Same grid and initial agents per group as for other setups in Fig. 3.,1,800,"Fig. 3(d) and Fig. 4(d) results obtained with 800 rounds, consistent with description.",0.9,1,"Contribution options are unchanged: {0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1}.",1,0,Same PGG endowment handling: contributions chosen from E=1 private endowment.,1,N/R,MPCR=r/n_i depends on dynamic group size; not a single value.,0.5,N/A,No human agents.,1,0,Blind model; no information about others’ contributions/payoffs available.,1,N/A,"Punishment is institutional (migration cost), no identities.",1,N/A,"Rewarding is via institutional discount factor δ_i, not via identified agents.",1,0,No mention of horizon knowledge in the agent rules.,0.5,1,Migration cost c_m=0.2 is present in this combined setup as in migration cost setup.,1,0.2,Migration cost parameter c_m set to 0.2; combined setup includes this value.,1,N/R,"As in migration cost setup, condition c_i−c_m ≤ b_i is used, but no explicit per-unit punishment technology parameter reported.",0.5,1,Combined setup also includes rewarding scheme via δ_i as defined previously.,1,0,Reward is implemented institutionally via higher δ_i for loyal agents; no explicit agent-borne cost.,0.7,N/R,Reward magnitude embedded in δ_i progression; no simple per-unit reward factor reported.,0.5,1,Same PGG base game with E=1.,1,N/R,Contribution outcomes shown graphically over r and p; no single value for this entire condition.,0.3,N/R,Final contributions are not numerically tabulated.,0.3,N/R,Efficiency measures not reported for combined condition.,0.1,N/R,Payoffs only discussed qualitatively.,0.1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational simulations only.,1,"Spatial PGG on 12×12 grid with blind model dynamics, incorporating both mechanisms: (i) loyalty-based discount factor δ_i starting at 0.6 for new group members and increasing by 0.1 per round up to 1 when staying in same group (rewarding scheme), and (ii) migration cost c_m=0.2 applied via the condition c_i−c_m ≤ b_i for unsatisfied agents deciding to migrate. Payoff formula with δ_i is π_i=(E−c_i)+r δ_i c_i.",Combines described rules from rewarding scheme and migration cost sections.,1
