10.3390_g15030016.md,Numerical simulations under Expected Utility Theory (EUT),"Paper describes numerical integration of replicator dynamics for EUT with specified parameters and varying punishment strength s (Figure 1a–c, s = 5.6, 5.1, 4.6) as one analysis block.",0.86,"Decision model: Expected Utility Theory; Punishment strength s ∈ {5.6, 5.1, 4.6}; Punishment probability δ = 0.2; Benefit b = 5.0; Cost of helping c = 1.0; Implementation error ε = 0.05; Punishment institution cost d = 0.05","EUT vs PT is the main theoretical manipulation; within EUT section, s is varied across simulations while other parameters are fixed as stated.",0.89,False,"Authors explicitly state there are no data and the work is an evolutionary game-theoretical model with numerical integration, not human participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only mathematical model and simulations.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,True,They numerically integrate the replicator equation and present trajectories and vector fields for different parameter values.,0.98,True,They derive closed-form expressions for expected payoffs and stability conditions under EUT in addition to simulations.,0.94,2,Donation game is defined as a two-player interaction between randomly chosen players A and B.,1,0,"Model assumes players are randomly matched infinitely many times in an ongoing population process, not a finite repeated game with rounds.",0.8,0,Each player has only two discrete actions (help C or defect D); no continuous contribution levels.,1,N/A,No public goods endowment structure or initial allocation; only binary help decision in a donation game.,1,N/R,Model is not a linear public goods game; no marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human or experimental interface; communication not modeled.,1,0,No feedback about others’ earnings or punishments is described; only payoffs to focal player from pairwise interaction and institutional punishment.,0.85,0,Punishment is imposed by an impersonal institution with a small probability; identities of punished players are not modeled as visible.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism exists in the model.,1,0,No notion of displayed finite horizon or remaining rounds; infinite-time evolutionary process.,0.95,1,They explicitly study a pool punishment institution that fines defectors with probability δ.,1,0.05,Cost d to maintain punishment institution is given as d = 0.05 in numerical investigations; this is the per-player cost.,0.96,1,"Fine magnitude is s; they report thresholds in terms of δ·s compared to c and d, implying 1 unit of punishment intensity is directly s; cost-to-fine conversion per defect is s per punishment event so tech coded as 1 (fine per fine-unit).",0.6,0,Only punishment institution is modeled; no reward or bonus for cooperation is introduced.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Players pay fixed cost c when helping and receive benefit b, but no explicit per-round endowment is defined.",0.6,N/R,They discuss cooperation rate p qualitatively but do not report specific numerical outcome values for each simulation condition.,0.55,N/R,No absolute token/endowment contributions are reported; only binary help/defect decisions.,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency or normalized group payoff metric is reported.,0.95,N/R,They analyze strategy payoffs analytically but do not report aggregate group payoff values for specific simulations.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study is entirely theoretical/numerical with no human subjects.,1,"Donation (giving) game with binary actions C (help at cost c, give benefit b to other) or D (defect); three strategies in the population: AC (unconditional cooperator with implementation error ε), AD (defector/free-rider that does not pay for punishment institution), and S (pay-it-forward strategy that cooperates with probability 1−ε conditional on having been helped in previous game). Pool punishment institution fines defectors with probability δ and fine magnitude s; cooperators never punished. Evolution governed by replicator dynamics in a large well-mixed population.",Summarizes description in Sections 3.1–3.5 and 4.1.,0.98
10.3390_g15030016.md,Numerical simulations under Prospect Theory (PT) with moderate to high punishment (s = 5.6 and s = 5.1),"Paper presents trajectories and vector fields for PT case with s = 5.6 and s = 5.1 (Figure 2a,b) where cooperation or pay-it-forward evolve; grouped as same independent framework (PT) with varied s.",0.86,"Decision model: Prospect Theory with value function parameters α = 0.88, λ = 2.25 and probability weighting parameter γ = 0.65; Punishment strength s ∈ {5.6, 5.1}; Punishment probability δ = 0.2; Benefit b = 5.0; Cost of helping c = 1.0; Implementation error ε = 0.05; Punishment institution cost d = 0.05","PT vs EUT is key manipulation; within PT section, s is varied (5.6, 5.1) with same numerical parameter set as EUT, plus explicit PT parameters from Appendix B.",0.9,False,The work is a theoretical evolutionary game with no subjects; authors explicitly note no data.,1,False,No controlled behavioral experiment; only model plus simulations.,1,False,No physical laboratory or field setting.,1,True,"They numerically integrate replicator dynamics under PT to obtain trajectories and fixed points (Figure 2a,b).",0.98,True,"They define PT-modified payoff and weighting functions and derive expected payoff expressions, even if stability is then studied numerically.",0.9,2,Same donation game between two players A and B.,1,0,Same infinite interaction evolutionary setting with no explicit round count.,0.8,0,Binary C/D actions only.,1,N/A,No endowment allocation; binary donation decision.,1,N/R,Not a public goods MPCR setting.,1,N/A,No human interaction or communication modeled.,1,0,Model does not include information about others’ previous payoffs/punishments being shown; only strategies and payoffs matter.,0.85,0,Institutional punishment is impersonal; no individual identity revelation modeled.,0.9,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,"Infinite horizon replicator dynamics, not finite displayed rounds.",0.95,1,Same pool punishment institution as in EUT case.,1,0.05,d = 0.05 is unchanged in PT simulations.,0.96,1,Fine per punishment event is s; same mapping as EUT coding.,0.6,0,Only punishment studied.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment per round not defined; only costs/benefits b and c are specified.,0.6,N/R,"Cooperation rate p is discussed (e.g., at fixed point T p≈0.6, at S p=0) but no systematic numerical reporting per condition beyond qualitative description.",0.6,N/R,No explicit token amounts; only binary help decisions.,0.95,N/R,No explicit reporting of normalized efficiency/statistical efficiency.,0.95,N/R,No aggregate group payoff results are tabulated for these simulations.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely theoretical and numerical model.,1,"Same donation game and three-strategy evolutionary setup as EUT, but expected payoffs are computed using prospect theory: a nonlinear value function v(x) with α = 0.88, λ = 2.25 and a probability weighting function w(x) with γ = 0.65 are applied to payoffs and probabilities respectively; institution cost d is transformed via value function only. Under PT with s=5.1, cooperation (AC) can be ESS where under EUT AD was ESS; with s=5.6 AC dominates; fixed point structures differ from EUT.",Summarizes PT modifications described in Section 3.5.2 and qualitative PT results in Section 4.2.,0.98
10.3390_g15030016.md,Numerical simulations under Prospect Theory (PT) with weak punishment (s = 4.6 and s = 3.0),"Paper highlights PT behavior with smaller punishment strength (Figure 2c for s=4.6 with bistability including pay-it-forward fixed point T, and Figure 4 for s=3 where AD dominates); grouped as PT with low s.",0.86,"Decision model: Prospect Theory with α = 0.88, λ = 2.25, γ = 0.65; Punishment strength s ∈ {4.6, 3.0}; Punishment probability δ = 0.2; Benefit b = 5.0; Cost of helping c = 1.0; Implementation error ε = 0.05; Punishment institution cost d = 0.05",Same PT setup but exploring lower s values to show emergence of mixed fixed point T and eventual failure of cooperation when s is too small.,0.9,False,No data or subjects; model only.,1,False,Not an experiment; simulations and analysis.,1,False,No laboratory environment.,1,True,They compute replicator dynamics numerically for s=4.6 and s=3.0 under PT and show trajectories (Figures 2c and 4).,0.98,True,"Underlying PT payoff structure and replicator dynamics are analytically specified, even if fixed points are studied numerically.",0.9,2,Two-player donation game.,1,0,Infinite evolutionary interaction; no fixed rounds.,0.8,0,Binary cooperate/defect choice.,1,N/A,No contribution endowment structure.,1,N/R,Not defined in this donation game.,1,N/A,No human experiment.,1,0,No mechanism of displaying others’ payoffs/punishments.,0.85,0,Institutional punishment without identity revelation.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,"Replicator dynamics over continuous time, not a finite displayed horizon.",0.95,1,Same punishment institution as in other PT cases.,1,0.05,Parameter d fixed at 0.05 across simulations.,0.96,1,Fine s per punished defection; same as other conditions.,0.6,0,No rewards modeled.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment per game unspecified; only parameters b and c given.,0.6,N/R,"They discuss cooperation rate p qualitatively (e.g., at fixed point T near x1≈0.1 resulting in p≈0.6, but no systematically tabulated values).",0.6,N/R,No continuous amounts; binary actions only.,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric provided.,0.95,N/R,Group payoff statistics not reported for these parameter regimes.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Only computational simulations of an evolutionary model.,1,"Same donation game and strategies as other conditions. Under PT with s=4.6, system exhibits bistability with a stable mixed fixed point T on AC–S edge dominated by S (pay-it-forward) players and another attractor at AD; cooperation rate p is near 0.6 at T when x1≈0.1. With s reduced further to 3.0, all trajectories converge to AD under PT, eliminating qualitative differences between PT and EUT for these low punishment strengths.",Drawn from qualitative description in Section 4.2 and Figure 3–4 discussion.,0.97
10.1080_13504851.2024.2363290.md,Similar Personality Framing (SPF),"Paper describes two treatments: similar personality framing (SPF, n=570) and dissimilar personality framing (DPF, n=384). This row corresponds to the SPF condition.",1,"Personality framing: similar personality framing (SPF); Game parameters: 9-round hypothetical VCM with punishment-like mechanism; rounds 1–3 two-player, 4–9 four-player.",Independent variable across conditions is framing of others' personality (similar vs dissimilar). Game structure is held constant.,1,True,Online survey with 954 human respondents from UAE; SPF is one of the two human-subject treatments.,1,True,Controlled treatment variation (SPF vs DPF) embedded in an incentivized game within a survey.,0.9,False,"Conducted via Qualtrics online survey, not in a physical laboratory.",1,False,Decisions were made by human participants; random matching and payoffs but no agent-based or purely computational simulation.,1,False,"Although the payoff function is written formally, the core study is an empirical survey experiment.",1,4,Game framed as 2-player in rounds 1–3 and 4-player in rounds 4–9. For the main social dilemma with framing and reciprocity (rounds 4–9) the group size is 4 including the respondent.,1,9,“They completed nine rounds…”,1,1,"Respondents could choose “an amount between 0 and 50 tokens”; continuous/discrete range rather than forced all-or-nothing, which we code as flexible (1).",1,0,Each round: endowment of 150 tokens and a choice of how many (0–50) to donate. Endowment is initially fully private; public account is empty unless contributed.,1,N/R,"Game is a reversed public goods game with penalties, not a standard linear public good with stated multiplier; MPCR not defined or derivable in standard sense.",0.4,0,No mention of communication or chat; standard one-shot decision per round in survey.,0.9,0,"Participants ‘see the resulting payoff’ but no indication they receive summaries of others’ earnings, punishments, or contributions.",0.7,0,Penalty is an automatic function of own non-contribution shared equally; no individual punishment decisions or identities described.,1,0,No reward mechanism is present in the design.,1,1,"Instructions mention ‘nine rounds’ and separate explanations before round 1 and round 4; participants are informed about round structure, implying they know the horizon.",0.8,1,Each player incurs an individual penalty p_i = 100 - 2*d_i; shared penalty S splits this among players. This is a punishment-like mechanism for low contributions.,1,0,"Penalty is automatic, not chosen by other players spending tokens. There is no costly punishment decision; cost per punishment unit is thus not defined, coded as 0.",0.4,N/A,"No strategic punishment action; penalty is an exogenous function of contributions, so no per-token ‘technology’ chosen by players.",1,0,No mention of any reward or bonus for contributing beyond the payoff formula.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,150,“Respondents were endowed with 150 tokens in each round.”,1,N/R,"Paper reports average contributions in tokens (e.g., 32.8, 34.3) but not normalized as a fraction of endowment or max possible across all rounds/conditions.",0.7,34.3,"For SPF, they report average contribution in round 7 as 34.3 tokens; this is the primary post-treatment outcome used in comparison to round 4.",1,N/R,No reporting or derivation of efficiency (ratio of achieved to maximum possible group payoff) in the article.,0.3,N/R,"Only individual average contributions and regression results are reported, not payoffs.",0.3,United Arab Emirates (general population sample with multiple nationalities),"“Diverse sample of 954 respondents from the general population of the United Arab Emirates.” National composition (Emirati, Indian, Filipino, etc.) provided in Table 2.",1,"Mostly 18–44; Age 18–24: 15.4%, 25–34: 42.3%, 35–44: 31.4%",Age distribution by bands for SPF given in Table 2.,1,"Male 45.4%, Female 54.6% (by implication)",Table 2 lists percentage male for SPF (45.4%); remainder is presumably female.,0.95,N/R,No educational attainment variables reported.,0.1,Online,Study repeatedly described as an online survey conducted using Qualtrics.,1,"Reversed public goods (safety-net) game with automatic penalty: each player chooses donation d_i ∈ [0,50] from 150-token endowment. Individual penalty p_i = 100 − 2*d_i; shared penalty S = Σ_i p_i / n is subtracted from each player’s payoff: π_i = 150 − d_i − S. Rounds 1–3 framed as 2-player, rounds 4–9 as 4-player. Random matching with hypothetical donations from others (0–50), no real-time interaction; one round and one player are randomly chosen for actual payment (1 token = 1 AED). Personality traits (Big Five, 2 items per trait) measured before game; in SPF treatment, before round 7 respondents are told that their coplayers have similar personality traits to them.","Summarizes formal payoff structure, framing, and incentive details explicitly described in Section II and footnote on reversed public goods game.",1
10.1080_13504851.2024.2363290.md,Dissimilar Personality Framing (DPF),"Paper describes two treatments: similar personality framing (SPF) and dissimilar personality framing (DPF, n=384). This row corresponds to the DPF condition.",1,"Personality framing: dissimilar personality framing (DPF); Game parameters: 9-round hypothetical VCM with punishment-like mechanism; rounds 1–3 two-player, 4–9 four-player.",Independent variable across conditions is framing of others' personality (similar vs dissimilar). This row specifies the dissimilar framing.,1,True,Online survey with 954 human respondents from UAE; DPF comprises 384 of these.,1,True,Controlled personality framing manipulation (SPF vs DPF) in an incentivized game setting.,0.9,False,"Implemented in Qualtrics as an online survey, not a physical lab.",1,False,"Outcomes derive from human decisions, not purely simulated agents.",1,False,Study is empirical rather than purely theoretical.,1,4,"Same game design as SPF: 2-player rounds 1–3, 4-player rounds 4–9; framing manipulation occurs before round 7 in this 4-player setting.",1,9,Identical nine-round structure across treatments.,1,1,"Contribution choice between 0 and 50 tokens, not forced all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment starts fully private at 150 tokens; joint account only gets what is donated.,1,N/R,Same reversed public goods penalty structure; no standard MPCR stated or easily mapped.,0.4,0,No communication described in the protocol.,0.9,0,Respondents observe their own payoffs but no explicit summary information about others.,0.7,0,No player-chosen punishment with identities; penalty is automatic.,1,0,No reward scheme implemented.,1,1,Round structure (1–9) and the two checks before rounds 1 and 4 imply that total number of rounds is known.,0.8,1,Same penalty structure p_i = 100 − 2*d_i; S = Σ_i p_i / n; π_i = 150 − d_i − S.,1,0,Automatic penalty rather than costly peer punishment; no resource cost spent by players to inflict it.,0.4,N/A,No punishment decision variable.,1,0,No rewarding options described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,150,Same per-round endowment: 150 tokens.,1,N/R,"Reported in tokens, not as normalized fractions.",0.7,32.9,"For DPF, average contribution in round 7 is 32.9 tokens; main post-treatment measure compared to 34.0 in round 4.",1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,0.3,N/R,"Only average contribution levels and regression coefficients reported, not payoff totals.",0.3,United Arab Emirates (general population sample with multiple nationalities),Same UAE-based general population sampling frame; composition by nationality for DPF given in Table 2.,1,"Mostly 18–44; Age 18–24: 16.9%, 25–34: 39.6%, 35–44: 34.6%",Age band shares for DPF from Table 2.,1,"Male 55.4%, Female 44.6% (by implication)",Table 2 reports male share for DPF (55.4%); remainder is presumably female.,0.95,N/R,No education variables for DPF reported.,0.1,Online,Same Qualtrics online survey setup as SPF; Table 2 notes SPF survey was August 2023 and DPF survey February 2024.,1,"Identical reversed public goods structure as SPF: each round respondents choose d_i ∈ [0,50] from 150 tokens. Penalty p_i = 100 − 2*d_i, shared penalty S = Σ_i p_i / n, payoff π_i = 150 − d_i − S. Rounds 1–3 framed as 2-player, 4–9 as 4-player. In DPF treatment, before round 7 respondents are told to assume that the other players have personality traits different from their own. Matching is hypothetical with random donations from others; one player and one of the nine rounds are selected for real payment (1 token = 1 AED).","Replicates the same payoff and structural details, adding the specific dissimilar framing manipulation before round 7.",1
