10.3390_bs14070601.md,Random Leader Reward (RLR) group,Authors define RLR as the group where randomly selected leaders can reward others at personal cost.,1,"Leadership and sanction power condition: random leader with reward power (cost-benefit ratio 1:3), no punishment power",Between-subject factor is leadership power type; this condition gives leaders reward but not punishment power.,1,True,Human participants play repeated public goods game with added reward stage.,1,True,Condition is part of randomized lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted in on-site lab rooms using z-Tree.,1,False,No computational-only model.,1,False,"Empirical behavioral experiment, not analytical derivation.",1,4,Same 4-person teams as in other groups.,1,32,All groups play 32 rounds.,1,1,Contributions can be any integer 0–20.,1,0,"20-token private endowment per round, then voluntary contribution decision.",1,0.4,Total public tokens multiplied by 1.6 and evenly divided among 4 members => MPCR 0.4.,1,0,Same no-communication rule applies.,1,1,"After stage 2 participants see own contribution, total group contribution, and own payoff for that round.",0.9,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in RLR group.,1,0,"Leaders see others’ contributions and choose whom to reward, but identities are numerical and anonymous; paper does not indicate that identities of rewarders are revealed to recipients.",0.6,1,Game described as having 32 rounds; typical interface shows round number.,0.7,0,RLR group is explicitly defined as leaders having right to reward others; no punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,1,Leaders may use their tokens to increase others’ tokens; this is the reward mechanism.,1,1,Cost-benefit ratio for reward is 1:3; leader pays 1 token per reward unit.,1,3,Each token spent by the leader increases a targeted follower’s tokens by three.,1,20,All group members receive 20 tokens at the start of each round.,1,N/R,Only token averages reported; no normalized fraction of e