10.1007_s11538-024-01344-7.md,"Analytical and numerical model – Donation Game (DG) and Public Goods Game (PGG) with institutional reward, punishment, and hybrid incentives","The paper develops a general evolutionary game-theoretic model (Markov chain + replicator-style analysis) covering DG and PGG with reward, punishment, and hybrid incentives for various thresholds t and mutation rates; there are no separate human-subject experimental conditions.",1,"Game type (Donation Game vs Public Goods Game); incentive type (reward vs punishment vs hybrid); incentive threshold t (1 to N−1); selection intensity β; mutation rate μ; per-capita incentive cost θ; population size N; PGG parameters r, n, c; DG parameters B, c",These are the parameters systematically varied in the analytical expressions and numerical simulations; the model is the same structural condition across the paper.,1,False,"The study is purely theoretical and numerical (Markov chains, analytical derivations, and numerical simulations) with no human participants.",1,False,No controlled experiment with human or animal subjects; only theoretical analysis and simulations.,1,False,No laboratory setting is used; all results are analytical or computational.,1,True,They numerically evaluate the Markov-chain-based dynamics and cost functions for different parameter values; these are computer simulations without human subjects.,1,True,"Most of the paper consists of closed-form derivations (e.g., cost function E_r(θ), limits as β→0, β→∞) using linear algebra and recurrence relations.",1,6,"Example numerical plots use N=3,4,6,7, etc.; there is no single fixed N. A representative value N=6 is taken from several figures (e.g., PGG with N=6) but this is heterogeneous across analyses.",0.2,N/R,The evolutionary process is modeled as a Markov chain over generations until absorption or stationary behavior; there is no fixed finite number of game rounds reported.,0.9,1,"In DG each interaction is cooperate or defect (binary action), but the framework is a standard 2-strategy social dilemma without discrete all-or-nothing contribution levels on a continuous endowment; by the given coding, players can choose either strategy, so treat as 1 (not an imposed all-or-nothing endowment contribution constraint).",0.4,N/A,"No human subjects and no explicit endowment allocation into private vs public accounts; the games are payoff matrices, not endowment-allocation tasks.",1,N/R,"For PGG they specify total contribution is multiplied by factor r and shared in group of size n, but MPCR=r/n is not explicitly labeled or tied to a single parameter set; no single value is extractable.",0.7,N/A,No human interaction or communication; purely simulated strategies in evolutionary dynamics.,1,N/A,"No user interface or feedback to human participants, only payoff functions in the model.",1,N/A,No identities or observability of individual actions in a human sense; only aggregate state j of cooperators is used.,1,N/A,"As above, no individual-level identity or visibility in a human experiment.",1,N/A,No human subjects or on-screen information; generations are an internal time index.,1,1,Section 2.3 defines punishment incentives; Section 4 and Appendix include punishment and hybrid (reward+punishment) cost functions and simulations.,1,N/R,"They denote institutional cost to punish as θ/b per defector with efficiency b, but do not instantiate b or a numeric per-unit cost; only symbolic parameters a,b,θ.",0.9,N/R,"Effectiveness is parameterized via b such that paying θ/b changes payoff by θ, but no single numeric b is fixed.",0.9,1,"Reward incentives are the main focus (institutional reward, t=1, t=2 etc.) with explicit cost function E_r(θ).",1,N/R,"They assume rewarding a cooperator costs θ/a to increase payoff by θ, with a>0 as efficiency ratio, and then normalize a=1 for analysis; θ is a free variable, not a fixed quantity.",0.9,N/R,"The mapping from institutional expenditure to payoff change is θ per rewarded individual (after normalizing a=1), but no fixed numeric parameter beyond θ is given.",0.8,N/R,DG and PGG are defined in terms of cost c and benefit B or factor r; no individual monetary endowment per round is specified.,0.9,N/R,"Outcomes are expressed as cooperation frequency (fixation probabilities and stationary frequencies), not normalized contributions from a continuous endowment.",0.9,N/R,"Again, only binary strategies C/D with payoffs B,c,r are modeled; no token contribution amounts per player are tabulated as results.",0.9,N/R,"They study institutional cost E(θ) and cooperation levels, but do not report efficiency as ratio of realized group payoff to maximum possible in a numeric way.",0.9,N/R,"Payoff functions Π_C(j), Π_D(j) are defined symbolically, but no numeric group payoffs are reported as primary outcomes across conditions.",0.9,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The study is entirely theoretical and simulation-based with no physical or online experimental environment.,1,"Well-mixed finite populations of size N using a Fermi update rule on an absorbing Markov chain with (N+1) states S_j (j cooperators); individuals play one-shot Donation Game (DG) or Public Goods Game (PGG). In DG, cooperation costs c and gives partner benefit B>c. In PGG, group size n, contribution cost c, total multiplied by r (1<r<n) and shared equally. Institutional incentives: reward cooperators, punish defectors, or hybrid, applied only when number of cooperators (or defectors) is below/above threshold t (1≤t≤N−1). Under reward, for 1≤j≤t, θ_j = (j/a)θ (a normalized to 1), and 0 otherwise; analogous for punishment and hybrid. Evolutionary dynamics analyzed under small-mutation limit and general mutation μ, with selection intensity β, and cooperation frequency derived from fixation probabilities ρ_{D,C}, ρ_{C,D}. Cost functions E_r(θ), E_p(θ), E_mix(θ) are defined as expected institutional expenditure over generations.",Summarizes the key structural features of the DG/PGG and incentive scheme as described in Sections 2–5.,1
10.1103_PhysRevE.110.034301.md,Analytical-simulation condition: slow resource growth (r = 0.4),Uses specific parameter set in Fig. 1 to illustrate dynamics when resource growth is slow.,1,Resource growth rate r = 0.4; w = 0.5; δ = 0.04; N = 1000; R_m = 1000; b_m = 0.5; α = 0.5,Condition defined by explicit numerical parameters listed for Fig. 1 (slowly growing resource).,1,False,Purely theoretical feedback-evolving game analyzed by differential equations; infinite population and no human participants.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only theoretical/numerical analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting; only mathematical model.,1,True,Uses numerical phase diagrams and temporal evolution for the ODE system with specified parameters.,1,True,Derives equilibria and stability conditions analytically using Jacobian and replicator dynamics.,1,1000,Parameter N = 1000 for this condition.,1,0,"Continuous-time dynamical system, not a discrete repeated game; set to 0.",0.9,1,Players choose between two discrete strategies (C or D) rather than contributing a continuous amount; treated as all-or-nothing strategic choice.,0.9,N/A,"No explicit endowment or contribution to a public good; model is resource extraction, not contribution.",1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,No interface or feedback descriptions about others’ payoffs; theoretical model only.,1,N/A,"Punishment is implemented by a third-party institution in an anonymous, aggregate way; identities not modeled.",1,N/A,Rewards are distributed uniformly to cooperators without identity modeling.,1,0,Continuous-time model; no notion of shown number of rounds.,1,1,Portion (1−w) of tax revenue allocated as fines to defectors; described as punishment.,1,1,"Text: ""cost of punishing each defector is the same as the fine imposed ... with a punishment intensity of 1"", implying 1 unit cost per 1 unit fine.",0.9,1,"Punishment intensity is stated as 1, meaning each cost unit translates to equal payoff reduction.",0.9,1,Fraction w of tax revenue allocated as rewards to cooperators.,1,1,Tax δ is collected and redistributed fully; one unit of budget yields one unit of reward; symmetric to punishment intensity assumption.,0.8,1,Each unit of redistributed tax increases payoff by one; no multiplier specified.,0.8,N/R,"Model specifies benefits from resource pool (b_L, b_V) but not an initial endowment per player.",1,N/R,Outcome variables are cooperation frequency x and resource level y; no direct contribution amount defined.,1,N/R,"Model is about extraction, not contributions.",1,N/R,Paper discusses sustainability and cooperation but not normalized efficiency as defined in the instructions.,1,N/R,"Payoffs P_C and P_D are defined, but no aggregate group payoff reported for this condition.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely mathematical and numerical analysis.,1,"Infinite well-mixed population; two strategies (cooperate, defect); common resource with logistic growth; extraction benefits b_L = (b_m y)/R_m for cooperators and b_V = (1+α)b_L for defectors; tax δ per player; fraction w of total tax to cooperators as rewards, 1−w to defectors as fines; dynamics via coupled ODEs in x and y.",Summarizes the core structure of the feedback-evolving game used in this parameter condition.,1
