10.1007_s10668-024-05335-5.md,Simulation – SRPM (Static Reward and Punishment Mechanism),Paper separates analysis into system Π under SRPM vs system π under DRPM; Sect. 5.1 reports a distinct numerical example and Matlab simulations for SRPM.,1,Reward and punishment mechanism type = SRPM with fixed punishment P=30 and fixed reward A=3,"Within the numerical example, mechanism type (static vs dynamic) and parameterization of punishment/reward are what differ between Π and π.",1,False,No human participants; all results are from mathematical modeling and Matlab simulations.,1,False,No controlled experiment with subjects; only simulations based on analytical model.,1,False,No laboratory setting or human subjects.,1,True,Section 5 explicitly describes Matlab simulation of evolutionary game dynamics for system Π.,1,True,Sections 3 and 5 derive and analyze differential equations and Jacobian matrices for system Π.,1,3,"Tripartite evolutionary game among village enterprises, the government, and villagers.",1,0,"Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics simulated over time intervals (e.g., t in [0,30]); not a discrete round game.",1,1,"Each population chooses probabilities over two discrete strategies; not a continuous contribution game, but closest mapping is that actions are not constrained to all-or-nothing contributions. However, contribution notion is not applicable.",0.2,N/A,No public goods contribution or endowment model; only strategy probabilities.,1,N/A,Not a public goods or linear contribution game; no MPCR defined.,1,N/A,No human participants; communication not modeled.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to human players; purely theoretical system.,1,N/A,No identities or information structure over individuals; only aggregate populations.,1,N/A,No identities modeled.,1,N/A,No interface or discrete rounds to display.,1,1,Government imposes fixed punishment P on illegal emissions in SRPM.,1,N/R,"Model specifies punishment level P (30) affecting payoffs, but not an explicit per-unit cost to punisher in contribution units.",0.3,N/R,Punishment is a lump-sum penalty P; no specification of technological effect per unit spent.,0.3,1,Government gives fixed reward A to villagers who actively supervise under certain conditions.,1,N/R,Reward A=3 is a payoff to villagers; explicit cost per reward unit to the government is not separately parameterized.,0.3,N/R,No mapping from expenditure to effect size beyond the lump-sum A.,0.3,N/A,No per-period monetary endowment or contributions; only profits and payoffs from strategies.,1,N/A,"No contribution decisions; only strategy probabilities x,y,z.",1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,"Paper discusses stability and strategy dynamics, not explicit efficiency ratios vs. maximum payoff.",0.4,N/R,"Payoff functions defined, but no single reported aggregate payoff outcome or numeric group payoff for the simulation.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Study relies on mathematical modeling and Matlab simulations only.,1,"Tripartite evolutionary game with three populations (village enterprises, government, villagers), each with two strategies. Under SRPM, government punishment P=30 and reward A=3 are constant; parameter values: R_L=100, R_I=120, C=2, D=5, T=20, ΔC=10. Replicator dynamics analyzed for system Π; MSNE approximately (x=0.7673, y=0.4555, z=0.2123) but is a center with periodic orbits, not an ESS.",Values and qualitative behavior described in Sect. 5.1 (parameter assignment and MSNE) and earlier analytical sections.,1
10.1007_s10668-024-05335-5.md,Simulation – DRPM (Dynamic Reward and Punishment Mechanism),System π under DRPM is treated as distinct from Π; Sect. 5.2 presents separate parameterization and Matlab simulations.,1,"Reward and punishment mechanism type = DRPM with dynamic punishment P(x)=(1−x)·P_m, P_m=50 and dynamic reward A(z)=(1−z)·A_m, A_m=5","Main manipulation versus SRPM is switching to dynamic, state-dependent punishment and reward and changing their caps.",1,False,No human participants; dynamics are simulated computationally.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; purely simulation based on theory.,1,False,No lab setting or subjects.,1,True,Matlab R2017b simulations of dynamic system π are explicitly described.,1,True,Section 4 provides analytical derivation and Jacobian analysis for DRPM system π.,1,3,"Same tripartite structure as SRPM: enterprises, government, villagers.",1,0,"Continuous-time evolutionary dynamics simulated over time horizons (e.g., t in [0,10]); no discrete rounds.",1,1,"Model involves choice among discrete strategies with continuous population shares, not contributions; contribution framing is inapplicable but coded as non-all-or-nothing.",0.2,N/A,No contribution or endowment structure.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No interface or feedback; only modeled payoffs and dynamics.,1,N/A,Players are populations without individual identities.,1,N/A,No identity revelation modeled.,1,N/A,Continuous dynamics; no fixed round count.,1,1,Government imposes dynamic punishment P(x) on illegal emitters.,1,N/R,"Punishment magnitude P_m=50 defined, but no explicit cost per unit punishment is parameterized.",0.3,N/R,Effect is encoded directly as payoff reduction; no separate technology ratio reported.,0.3,1,Government provides dynamic reward A(z) to active villagers.,1,N/R,Reward cap A_m=5 defined as villagers’ benefit; per-unit cost to government not separately given.,0.3,N/R,Reward directly increases villagers’ payoff; no cost-to-impact ratio given.,0.3,N/A,No explicit endowment or contribution stage.,1,N/A,No contribution decisions.,1,N/A,No contribution variable.,1,N/R,Outcomes are described in terms of strategy stability and ESS; efficiency vs. maximum payoff is not computed.,0.4,N/R,"Although payoffs are parameterized, no specific aggregate payoff at ESS is numerically reported.",0.4,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Environment is purely computational (Matlab) with no physical or online subjects.,1,"Same tripartite evolutionary game as under SRPM, but with dynamic mechanisms: punishment P(x)=(1−x)·P_m with P_m=50, reward A(z)=(1−z)·A_m with A_m=5. Other parameters unchanged: R_L=100, R_I=120, C=2, D=5, T=20, ΔC=10. Replicator system π exhibits a mixed-strategy ESS at approximately (x=0.6239, y=0.6149, z=0.5850), to which trajectories from many initial conditions converge asymptotically. Paper also explores varying P_m and A_m and finds that higher P_m increases legal emissions and reduces need for strict regulation and active supervision, while higher A_m mainly boosts supervision but can reduce legal emissions and burden government.","Parameter values, MSNE and stability properties detailed in Sect. 4 and 5.2, including subsections on reward and punishment caps.",1
