10.1371_journal.pone.0282112.md,Endogenous punishment – High cooperators,Table 6 row 'High cooperator (i=2)' under endogenous punishment institutions.,1,"Endogenous voting over punishment institutions; agent type = high cooperator (i=2), homogeneous group",Condition defined by agent type and endogenous institution mechanism.,1,False,Agent-based simulation.,1,False,Not an experiment with human participants.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,Simulation in EconSim.,1,False,Simulation-based outcomes.,1,5,Five agents per group.,1,100,Endogenous decision phase lasts 100 rounds after 1500 learning rounds (Section 3.6).,1,1,Continuous contributions like exogenous case.,0.8,N/A,Artificial agents only.,1,0.3,Underlying public goods payoff unchanged.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/R,Feedback mechanisms not described.,0,N/A,Institutional fines; no identities.,1,N/A,No reward scheme.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0,1,Punishment is an institutional option; high cooperators choose 'No' punishment 80% and 'Mild' 20% (Table 6). We code punishmentExists=1 because punishment is available and sometimes implemented.,0.8,5,"When punishment is chosen, it is mostly mild (20%; no severe reported); fee for mild=5; however, majority outcome is no punishment. We record 5 as the cost of the implemented punishment type.",0.6,50,Mild punishment fine=50; severe rarely or never chosen for high cooperators.,0.6,0,No rewards included.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Same endowment 100 per round.,1,0.9879,Table 6 contribution 98.79 / 100.,1,98.79,"Table 6: 'High cooperator (i=2), No (80%)' average contribution 98.79.",1,N/R,Normalized efficiency not provided.,0,N/R,Only average payoff per agent (148.40) reported.,0.7,N/A,Simulation; no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation; no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation; no human subjects.,1,N/A,Simulation; no human subjects.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model.,1,High cooperator agents vote mostly for no punishment (80%) and sometimes for mild punishment (20%); contributions remain near full; learning phase of 1500 rounds with random institutions precedes 100-round voting phase; reinforcement learning used both for contributions and institution choices.,From Section 3.6 and Table 6.,1
