10.1371_journal.pone.0282112.md,Endogenous punishment – Competitive low cooperators,Competitive low cooperator (i=4) row in Table 6.,1,"Endogenous voting over punishment institutions; agent type = competitive low cooperator (i=4), homogeneous group",Agent type and voting regime distinguish this simulation.,1,False,Agent-based model.,1,False,No human data.,1,False,Not a laboratory experiment.,1,True,Implemented as EconSim agent-based simulation.,1,False,Numerical results.,1,5,Simulation uses groups of five.,1,100,Endogenous phase is 100 rounds.,1,1,Continuous contributions.,0.8,N/A,No default public contribution; artificial agents.,1,0.3,Same payoff function as exogenous simulations.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/R,Not mentioned.,0,N/A,"Institution, not peer-specific punishment.",1,N/A,No reward scheme.,1,N/R,Not specified.,0,1,"Punishment institution is voted upon; for this type, the most chosen institution is 'No' punishment 100% in last 50 periods (Table 6). We still mark existence as 1 because the mechanism is present, even if rejected.",0.8,0,"In equilibrium, no punishment is implemented (100% 'No'); hence effective institutional fee is 0 in last 50 periods.",0.9,0,"With no punishment implemented, no fines are levied; effective punishment technology is 0 in last 50 periods.",0.9,0,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,100,Endowment per round remains 100.,1,0.0023,Average contribution 0.23 / 100 (Table 6).,1,0.23,"Table 6: 'Competitive low cooperator (i=4), No (100%)' contribution 0.23.",1,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0,N/R,Per-agent payoff (100.11) is given; group total not explicitly.,0.7,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,N/A,No humans involved.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation without human participants.,1,Competitive low cooperators use reinforcement learning over institutions but eventually vote for no punishment 100% of time; free-ride with near-zero contributions and receive about 100 tokens (the Nash equilibrium payoff) in last 50 periods; preceded by 1500 learning periods with random institutions.,From Section 3.6 and Table 6.,1
10.1080_13504851.2022.2156458.md,Part 1 – Voluntary Contributions Mechanism (no monitoring/punishment),Paper describes Part 1 (periods 1–9/10) as a standard VCM without monitoring or punishment; treated as one condition.,0.97,"Institution: Voluntary contributions mechanism only (no monitoring, no punishment).",Only institutional feature in Part 1 is the basic VCM; contrasted with Part 2 where monitoring and punishment are available.,0.99,True,Fifty-two human subjects participated in a laboratory public good experiment.,1,True,Controlled assignment to groups and payoff rules; standard lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted at University of Massachusetts Lowell with subjects in an experiment session; instructions and payments on site.,0.99,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; only human subjects.,1,False,No formal analytical model; focus is on experimental data.,1,4,"“In the experiment, each participant is randomly assigned to a group of 4. You will be part of the same group throughout the entire experiment.”",1,9,Main text: “Part 1 (periods 1–9) introduces a voluntary contributions mechanism (VCM).” Instructions section mentions 10 periods but note clarifies only parts 1 and 2 are discussed; periods 1–9 used for Part 1 analysis.,0.7,1,"“You can allocate any integer between 0 and 20 to the group account.” This allows any amount, not just all-or-nothing.",1,0,Endowment is given to individuals and they choose an allocation to the group account; default is all in private account (0 in group).,0.95,0.5,Payoff function specifies α = 0.5 as the marginal per capita return from the public good.,1,0,Instructions explicitly prohibit communication among participants.,1,1,"After Stage 1, “you will be shown your group account allocation, the total group account allocation, and your stage 1 earnings.” This is a summary of others’ contributions via the total.",0.9,0,"No punishment stage exists in Part 1, so identities for punishment cannot be shown.",1,0,No reward mechanism exists in any part of the experiment.,1,1,"Instructions for Part 1 state the number of periods (10) in advance; subjects know the finite horizon, implying they know total rounds.",0.85,0,Part 1 is a pure VCM with no monitoring or punishment stage.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this part.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in this part.,1,0,No mention of reward or positive sanctioning; only standard public good returns.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is available.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism is available.,1,20,"“In every period, each group member…will be given an endowment of 20 EDs.”",1,N/R,"Paper reports contributions in EDs (e.g., predicted contributions ~11.91 EDs) but not normalized as a 0–1 fraction; conversion possible (divide by 20) but they don’t report averages by part in that form.",0.5,11.63,"Footnote 5: “Disregarding grid-group designations, average contributions across part 1 (11.63** (0.776))…”",0.95,N/R,No reported ratio of realized group payoffs to maximum possible payoffs.,0.1,N/R,Group earnings are not reported as a raw total per group or per period.,0.1,USA,"Subjects recruited at University of Massachusetts Lowell, which is in the USA.",0.95,N/R,No age or age range reported.,0.1,N/R,No information on gender composition of the sample.,0.1,Likely university students (exact level N/R),"Experiment conducted at a university; typical subject pool is students, but not explicitly stated.",0.6,On site,"Instructions detail in-person procedures, cash payment at end, and prohibition on communication, indicating a physical lab setting.",0.99,"Linear public good game (VCM) with fixed groups of 4; endowment 20 EDs; contribution decision is any integer 0–20; payoff: (20 − own contribution) + 0.5 × (sum of group contributions). Groups remain fixed over 33 total periods of the experiment, with this part covering periods 1–9.",Summarizes the game description and payoff formula provided in the method and instructions.,1
10.1080_13504851.2022.2156458.md,Part 2 – Public good with monitoring and costly peer punishment,Part 2 (periods 10–27/18 periods) adds monitoring and punishment to the same public good structure; treated as a separate institutional condition.,0.97,"Institution: Voluntary contributions mechanism with optional monitoring (shared cost 4 ED) and costly peer punishment (1 ED per RP imposed, 4 ED per RP received).",Only change from Part 1 is the introduction of the monitoring stage and the ability to assign reduction points in Stage 2.,0.99,True,Same human-subject experiment; Part 2 is a continuation with additional institutional features.,1,True,Controlled modification of rules between parts; classic lab experiment design.,1,True,Same laboratory session and procedures as Part 1 at University of Massachusetts Lowell.,0.99,False,No non-human simulation reported.,1,False,No closed-form theoretical model as the primary method.,1,4,Group size remains 4 and “You will be part of the same group throughout the entire experiment.”,1,18,"Main text: Part 2 is periods 10–27, i.e., 18 periods. Instructions: “Part 2 consists of 18 periods.”",1,1,Stage 1 decision structure is unchanged: “You can allocate any integer between 0 and 20 to the group account.”,1,0,"As in Part 1, endowment is initially fully private; subjects decide how much to move to the group account.",0.95,0.5,Part 2 payoff without monitoring is “identical to part 1” and uses the same α = 0.5.,1,0,Same communication prohibition applies throughout the experiment.,1,1,"After Stage 1 participants see own allocation, total group allocation, and Stage 1 earnings; with monitoring they may also see individual allocations via anonymous IDs.",0.95,0,"When monitoring occurs, “each group member will be shown the group account allocations of each group member by random ID…randomly shuffled each period” so punishment targets are anonymous; no real identities revealed.",0.95,0,No reward mechanism exists anywhere in the design.,1,1,"Instructions state Part 2 consists of 18 periods, so total number of remaining rounds is known to subjects.",0.85,1,Stage 2 allows assigning Reduction Points that reduce others’ earnings at a cost; characterized as punishment in the text.,1,1,“For each RP you impose on another member you will pay 1 ED.”,1,4,“For each RP you receive from another member you will pay 4 EDs.” Thus each RP imposed reduces target’s payoff by 4 EDs.,1,0,Only negative reduction points are available; no positive transfers or rewards.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,20,Endowment of 20 EDs per period is constant across parts.,1,N/R,"They report average contributions in EDs (e.g., 13.46 EDs in footnote 5, and predicted values in Table 2) but not normalized as 0–1.",0.5,13.46,Footnote 5: “average contributions across part 2 (13.46** (1.23))…”,0.95,N/R,No explicit efficiency measure or ratio to the social optimum is reported.,0.1,N/R,Aggregate group payoffs per period or overall are not reported.,0.1,USA,Subjects are from University of Massachusetts Lowell in the USA.,0.95,N/R,Age information not provided.,0.1,N/R,No gender breakdown is given.,0.1,Likely university students (exact level N/R),"Experiment appears to use a university subject pool, but this is not specified explicitly.",0.6,On site,Same on-site lab environment as Part 1; in-person instructions and cash payment.,0.99,"Two-stage linear public good game with fixed groups of 4. Stage 1 identical to Part 1: each period each participant receives 20 EDs and chooses an integer contribution 0–20 to the group account; payoff from PG is 0.5 × sum of group contributions. Stage 2: each member first chooses whether to pay to observe individual contributions; if at least one chooses to monitor, a total monitoring cost of 4 EDs is split evenly among monitors (individual cost = 4 / number of monitors), and all group members (monitors and non-monitors) then can assign up to 3 Reduction Points to each other member. Each RP costs the punisher 1 ED and reduces the target’s payoff by 4 EDs. Individual contributions are shown via randomly shuffled anonymous IDs.","Summarizes detailed instructions and payoff equations for Part 2, including monitoring and punishment structure.",1
10.1017_s1355770x12000381.md,"Baseline protocol – Setting A (Ripon students, costless movement)","Baseline protocol uses only Setting A for three trials of 8 Ripon students each; all share identical rules (no moving cost, no communication, no sanctioning).",0.93,"Movement cost = 0; No communication; No sanctioning; Two commons (Alpha, Beta) available each round.",Setting A description: costless movement; no mention of communication or sanctioning; two-harbor CPR structure common to all settings.,0.96,True,Human subjects (Ripon College students) participated in lab trials.,1,True,Controlled laboratory economic game with randomized subject groups and imposed rules.,1,True,Conducted in college experimental sessions with action cards and experimenters; no field setting.,0.96,False,No computer-only or agent-based simulations described.,1,False,"Paper contains theoretical analysis but this condition refers to behavioral lab data, not a purely analytical model.",0.9,8,Each trial used 8 subjects; this is the group size in all settings.,1,20,Baseline protocol uses 20 rounds in setting A (single 20-round block).,0.9,1,"Subjects choose any integer hours (0–15) to fish; remainder to construction, so contribution is continuous, not binary all-or-nothing.",0.95,0,"All 15 hours are free to allocate; no automatic fishing allocation, so default to private (construction) if they chose 0 hours fishing.",0.7,N/R,Marginal per capita return for contributions is not stated as a single MPCR; only quadratic production and construction payoff are given.,0.2,0,No communication is mentioned in Setting A; communication is introduced only in Settings C and D.,0.98,1,"At end of each round, total hours and per-hour fishing payoff in each area are publicly announced, giving group-level outcome summaries.",0.95,0,No punishment mechanism exists in Setting A.,1,0,No reward mechanism is implemented in any setting.,1,1,"Subjects are told blocks are for 10 or 20 rounds; for Setting A they are told they will play 10 rounds for a block, but baseline protocol uses 20 rounds; subjects know when block ends.",0.7,0,Baseline/Setting A has no sanctioning mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,N/A,No punishment available.,1,0,No positive reward mechanism is described in any setting.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,15,Each player has 15 hours per round to allocate between fishing and construction.,1,N/R,Paper reports net efficiency and average effort hours but not normalized effort as a 0–1 share; insufficient detail by condition.,0.3,N/R,"Some averages are described informally, but not reported numerically by protocol/setting for baseline.",0.3,N/R,"Baseline protocol gives only min/max efficiencies (0.688–0.988) and examples, not a single average per condition.",0.3,N/R,"Only efficiencies and theoretical payoff benchmarks (1,440–1,920) are given, not observed average payoffs for baseline.",0.2,USA,"Subjects from Ripon College (Wisconsin, USA).",0.9,N/R,Not reported; only that subjects were undergraduate students.,0.1,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Subject pool described as undergraduate students from Ripon College.,0.98,On site,Physical lab sessions with cards and in-person communication recording; not online.,0.98,"Two-stage common-pool resource game with 8 players choosing between two commons (Alpha, Beta) and hours of fishing vs construction. Production: Q_A = 24*H_A - 0.2*H_A^2, Q_B = 24*H_B - 0.12*H_B^2. Construction yields 12 points/hour. Payoff uses average product of fishing; movement between commons is costless.","Summarizes core game structure common to all settings, adding that in baseline movement cost m_j=0.",1
10.1017_s1355770x12000381.md,"Enforcement protocol – Setting B (Ripon students, moving cost)","Enforcement protocol consists solely of Setting B with movement cost, run in four trials of 8 Ripon students, 40 rounds each.",0.95,Movement cost = 24 points for changing commons between consecutive rounds (after round 1 of block); No communication; No sanctioning.,Setting B identical to A except moving cost 24 points starting after first round of each block; no communication or sanctioning mentioned.,0.97,True,Human subjects (Ripon students) in lab CPR experiment.,1,True,Controlled experimental manipulation of movement cost.,1,True,Run at college labs with experimenters and physical materials.,0.96,False,No agent-based or purely computational simulation.,1,False,This condition concerns observed subject behavior.,0.9,8,Each trial uses 8 subjects.,1,40,"Enforcement protocol uses 40 rounds in Setting B, in four 10-round blocks (B, B, B).",0.98,1,Same continuous effort choice between 0 and 15 as in Setting A.,0.95,0,Same allocation structure: no automatic fishing; all hours initially uncommitted.,0.7,N/R,No single MPCR parameter is reported.,0.2,0,No communication allowed in Setting B; communication is only in C and D.,0.98,1,Same feedback as baseline: total hours on each commons and per-hour payoffs announced.,0.95,0,No punishment mechanism in Setting B.,1,0,No rewards provided.,1,1,Subjects are told about blocks of 10 rounds; protocol description makes rounds and blocks explicit.,0.75,0,No sanctioning mechanism in Setting B.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No explicit rewarding beyond normal payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,15,Same 15-hour endowment per round.,1,N/R,"Only aggregate average effort trends (e.g., 11.8 to 14.725 hours) and efficiencies are given, not normalized per-capita effort by setting.",0.4,N/R,Average individual effort levels are mentioned but not systematically tabulated per setting/protocol.,0.4,0.839,Mean efficiency in rounds with three–five split is 0.839; this is the main efficiency benchmark for successful coordination under enforcement.,0.7,N/R,Only efficiencies are reported; no corresponding mean point totals are given.,0.2,USA,"Ripon College is in Wisconsin, USA.",0.9,N/R,No age distribution reported.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition not stated.,0.1,Undergraduate students,Subject type specified as Ripon students.,0.98,On site,Lab sessions with physical presence and no online features.,0.98,"Same two-harbor CPR game as baseline (Alpha, Beta, quadratic production, 12 points/hour construction). Key change: movement between harbors after round 1 of each 10-round block costs 24 points, modeling enforcement of geographic separation.",Summarizes shared structure but highlights enforcement (moving cost) manipulation.,1
10.1017_s1355770x12000381.md,"Treatment protocol – Setting B (Bates students and fishermen, pre-information, enforcement only)","Treatment protocol uses Setting B in blocks 1–2, but block 1 (rounds 1–10) occurs before the information session; this condition covers block 1 behavior under enforcement with no extra information, communication, or sanctioning.",0.9,Movement cost = 24 points; No communication; No sanctioning; No prior information session.,"Block 1 of treatment uses Setting B; information session occurs only 'before round 11', so no extra information in rounds 1–10.",0.95,True,Human participants (Bates students and professional fishermen) in lab experiment.,1,True,"Controlled manipulation of movement cost and later information, communication, and sanctioning.",1,True,Trials conducted in campus labs with controlled environment and in-person communication.,0.96,False,No computer-only simulations reported.,1,False,"This row refers to experimental data, not a model.",0.9,8,Each trial (student or fishermen) consists of 8 subjects.,1,10,Condition specific to Block 1 (rounds 1–10) of treatment protocol.,1,1,Continuous choice of fishing hours between 0 and 15.,0.95,0,Hours not pre-committed to fishing.,0.7,N/R,No explicit MPCR given.,0.2,0,Communication is only allowed in Setting C and D; this condition is B.,0.99,1,Public announcement of total hours and per-hour payoff for each commons continues in all settings.,0.95,0,No punishment exists in Setting B.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Blocks and their round counts are described to subjects.,0.75,0,Sanctioning is introduced only in Setting D.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No separate reward action.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,15,Same 15-hour time endowment.,1,N/R,Tables 5–7 give averages by group but do not report normalized contribution (hours/15) separately for this condition.,0.4,N/R,"Average joint effort levels are reported aggregated over block 1 by group, but not consolidated for this condition across all groups.",0.4,0.78,"Table 6 shows block 1 average efficiencies by group; averaging six student groups and two fishermen groups yields approx. (0.764+0.875+0.742+0.745+0.789+0.692+0.912+0.915)/8 ≈ 0.804. However, the question is at condition level; to avoid over-aggregation, we approximate as 0.78 (midpoint of student-only and overall averages).",0.4,N/R,Only efficiencies given; group payoffs not reported.,0.2,USA,"Bates College is in Maine, USA; lobster and groundfish professionals from Maine industries.",0.9,N/R,Ages of students and fishermen are not detailed.,0.1,N/R,Gender composition is not discussed.,0.1,Mixed: Undergraduate students and non-student professionals,Treatment protocol includes 6 trials of Bates students and 2 trials of professional fishermen (lobstermen and groundfishermen).,0.95,On site,In-person lab sessions with voice-recorded communication in later blocks.,0.96,"Same two-harbor CPR game with enforcement (24-point movement cost after first round of block), but before any information session or communication. Used to study baseline behavior of more diverse subject pool under enforcement only.",Clarifies that only enforcement is active and subject pool includes students plus professionals.,0.95
10.1017_s1355770x12000381.md,"Treatment protocol – Setting B with information (Bates students and fishermen, block 2)","Block 2 (rounds 11–20) of treatment protocol uses Setting B again, but now after an information session about efficient effort levels.",0.95,Movement cost = 24 points; No communication; No sanctioning; Information session providing efficient total effort levels in each area before round 11.,"Information session described as occurring before round 11; block 2 remains Setting B, with no communication or sanctioning.",0.98,True,Same human-subject setting as other treatment blocks.,1,True,Experimental manipulation adds information while holding enforcement constant.,1,True,Conducted in physical lab environment.,0.96,False,No pure simulations.,1,False,Empirical behavior measured.,0.9,8,Unchanged group size of 8.,1,10,Block 2 is 10 rounds (11–20).,1,1,Continuous effort choice between 0 and 15.,0.95,0,No automatic fishing allocation.,0.7,N/R,No MPCR stated.,0.2,0,Communication is only permitted from Setting C onward.,0.99,1,Same public feedback as before.,0.95,0,No sanctions in Setting B.,1,0,No rewards.,1,1,Subjects know block structure and round progression.,0.75,0,Sanctions only in Setting D.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,N/A,No punishment option.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,15,Same 15-hour endowment.,1,N/R,Tables report block-level average efforts and efficiencies but not normalized contributions.,0.4,N/R,Average joint effort levels per group are given (Table 7) but not pooled across all groups for this condition.,0.4,0.83,"Table 6 reports block 2 average efficiencies: students (mean over 6 groups ≈0.811) and fishermen (mean ≈0.873). Pooled approximate average is (0.814+0.86)/2 ≈ 0.837, rounded to 0.83.",0.5,N/R,Only efficiencies are presented.,0.2,USA,Bates College and Maine fishing industries are in the USA.,0.9,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,Mixed: Undergraduate students and non-student professionals,Same treatment protocol subject pool as previous block.,0.95,On site,Same lab setting.,0.96,"Two-harbor CPR game under movement-cost enforcement, plus an information session revealing efficient total effort (30 hours in Alpha, 50 in Beta) before block 2. No communication or sanctioning allowed yet.",Highlights informational treatment while core game remains unchanged.,0.98
10.1017_s1355770x12000381.md,"Treatment protocol – Setting C (Communication, Bates students and fishermen, block 3)","Block 3 of treatment protocol uses Setting C, adding within-commons communication to the enforced two-harbor game.",0.98,Movement cost = 24 points; Within-commons face-to-face communication allowed up to 3 minutes per round; No sanctioning; Information about efficient efforts previously provided.,Setting C described as allowing communication after area choice but before effort decision; moving cost as in B; no sanctions mentioned.,0.99,True,Human participants in lab interacting via communication.,1,True,Controlled introduction of communication as a treatment variable.,1,True,On-site lab with voice recorders.,0.97,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,"Experimental data, not analytical model.",0.9,8,Unchanged group size.,1,10,Block 3 spans 10 rounds (21–30).,1,1,Effort remains continuous choice.,0.95,0,Same allocation framework as prior settings.,0.7,N/R,MPCR not given.,0.2,1,Setting C explicitly allows communication within each commons; recorded with a voice recorder.,1,1,Outcome feedback unchanged (total hours and per-hour payoffs announced).,0.95,0,No sanctioning mechanism yet in Setting C.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Round structure is known as before.,0.75,0,Sanctioning is introduced only in Setting D.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment.,1,0,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,15,Same 15-hour endowment.,1,N/R,Paper presents efficiencies but not normalized efforts per subject.,0.4,N/R,No direct reporting of average hours per subject by setting.,0.3,0.98,"Table 8 shows block 3 average efficiencies by group, ranging 0.952–0.997; approximate mean across 8 groups is about 0.979, rounded to 0.98.",0.8,N/R,"Only efficiencies reported, not raw payoffs.",0.2,USA,Bates students and Maine fishermen operate in USA.,0.9,N/R,Ages not specified.,0.1,N/R,Gender data not provided.,0.1,Mixed: Undergraduate students and non-student professionals,Same mixed subject pool for all treatment blocks.,0.95,On site,In-person group communication and recorded audio indicate on-site environment.,0.98,"Enforced two-harbor CPR game with prior information about efficient efforts. Additional feature: up to 3 minutes of within-commons communication each round to discuss area membership and individual effort, enabling coordination and trigger strategies.",Summarizes communication treatment and its timing.,1
10.1017_s1355770x12000381.md,"Treatment protocol – Setting D (Communication with informal and formal sanctioning, Bates students and fishermen, block 4)","Block 4 uses Setting D, which adds a sanctioning mechanism on top of communication and movement-cost enforcement.",0.99,"Movement cost = 24 points; Within-commons communication allowed; Informal and formal sanctioning enabled: first two sanctions cost 0 to sanctioned, third and subsequent cost 50 points to sanctioned; no cost to punisher.",Setting D description: same communication design as C plus sanctioning where third and later sanctions impose 50-point penalty; punisher has no direct cost but must state reason.,1,True,Human subjects in lab with communication and sanctioning.,1,True,Controlled variation in sanctioning availability.,1,True,On-site lab sessions with sanctioning choices and communication.,0.97,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Behavioral experiment.,0.9,8,Same group size.,1,10,Block 4 covers 10 rounds (31–40).,1,1,Effort allocation unchanged.,0.95,0,Same decision structure for hours.,0.7,N/R,No MPCR parameter.,0.2,1,Same communication protocol as Setting C applies.,1,1,Outcome feedback is unchanged.,0.95,1,"Sanctions are interpersonal and require each person to identify reason for punishment; given small group and within-commons discussion, punished individual is identifiable.",0.7,0,No reward action; only punishment/sanctioning.,1,1,Subjects know they are in the fourth 10-round block.,0.8,1,Setting D explicitly introduces sanctioning mechanism.,1,0,Punisher incurs no direct monetary cost; only potential indirect cost of explaining reason (non-monetary).,0.95,50,Third and subsequent sanctions cost the sanctioned subject 50 points; interpreted as 50-point effectiveness per sanction when threshold reached.,0.9,0,No positive reward mechanism described.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,N/A,No reward option.,1,15,Endowment of hours per round remains 15.,1,N/R,No normalized contribution measure is tabulated.,0.3,N/R,Effort levels are discussed qualitatively; specific averages per setting not provided.,0.3,0.99,Table 9 shows block 4 efficiencies by group (0.927–1.000). Approximate mean over 8 groups is around 0.986; rounded to 0.99.,0.8,N/R,Only efficiencies are reported.,0.2,USA,Same subject pool locations.,0.9,N/R,Not specified.,0.1,N/R,Not specified.,0.1,Mixed: Undergraduate students and non-student professionals,Same 6 student groups and 2 professional groups as prior treatment blocks.,0.95,On site,Sanctioning and communication were face-to-face in lab setting.,0.98,"Enforced two-harbor CPR game with prior information and communication. Additional sanctioning stage: subjects can assign up to two costless informal sanctions and further sanctions that impose a 50-point penalty on the sanctioned individual. Punishers must state reasons, which may create social pressure.",Describes sanctioning mechanism layered on existing game.,1
10.3390_g12020050.md,Public good – Baseline (no punishment),Condition is explicitly labeled as the public good structure without punishment in the 2×3 design and listed as 'PUBLIC GOOD | BASELINE' in Table 1.,1,"Network structure: Public good; Punishment regime: None (baseline, no second-stage decision)",Authors describe a 2×3 design manipulating network structure (public good vs generalized exchange) and punishment (baseline / costly (2;1) / costless (2;0)). This row corresponds to the public good × baseline cell.,1,True,Human subjects in a laboratory experiment at VCEE are described in Section 3.1.,1,True,Design involves random assignment to treatments and controlled manipulation of conditions in the lab.,1,True,Implemented in laboratories of the Vienna Center for Experimental Economics (VCEE).,1,False,No computer/agent-based simulations are reported; all results are from human subjects.,1,False,"Paper reports an experiment, not formal analytical derivations as a separate study.",1,4,Repeated four-player prisoner's dilemma; groups of four formed and fixed throughout the experiment.,1,16,"Main experiment implemented for 16 periods, same across all treatments.",1,0,"Players choose between two discrete options (cooperate vs defect), not a continuous contribution choice.",1,0,Standard linear public good framing where endowment (3 MU private) starts in private account and must be given up to generate 10 MU social benefit; thus default is entirely private.,0.8,10,"In public good, each cooperator generates a 10 MU social benefit divided among 3 others, giving each 10/3 ≈ 3.33; standard definition MPCR is per capita return to 1 MU sacrificed; here each 1 MU of sacrificed private payoff (3 MU forgoing) yields 10 MU total, i.e., 10/1 = 10. Using total social return per unit of private cost.",0.4,0,No mention of any communication; interactions were anonymous and computerized.,1,1,"In public good, participants observed identifiers and decisions of all group members each period, which implies they see outcome-relevant information from others.",0.8,0,No punishment in baseline; identities of punishers cannot be shown.,1,0,No reward mechanism is implemented in any treatment.,1,0,Participants were not informed about the exact number of repetitions to avoid endgame effects; suggests no display of total/remaining rounds.,0.9,0,Baseline treatment explicitly contains no second-stage decision and hence no punishment.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in baseline.,1,N/A,No punishment stage in baseline.,1,0,Only punishment is discussed; no positive reward mechanism is implemented.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,3,"In each round, defection yields a private benefit of 3 MU while cooperation yields 0 MU private benefit, implying an effective per-round endowment of 3 MU that can be kept or sacrificed. The initial 10 MU at the experiment start is a buffer, not a per-round endowment.",0.8,0.52,Table 1 lists COOPERATION for PUBLIC GOOD BASELINE as 0.52.,1,N/R,Only cooperation frequency is reported; no per-round MU contributions beyond the binary choice are given.,0,N/R,No ratio of realized to maximum possible earnings is reported or directly computable without full joint distribution.,0,26.52,"Table 1 reports average earnings per period of 6.63 MU per player; with 4 players per group, average group payoff per period ≈ 4 × 6.63 = 26.52 MU.",0.8,Austria,Experiment conducted at Vienna Center for Experimental Economics; Vienna is in Austria. No other countries mentioned.,0.8,Mostly 20–27 years,Participants described as mostly aged between 20 and 27 years.,0.9,57% female,Text states 57 percent (148/260) of participants were female.,1,Undergraduate students in various disciplines,Participants were undergraduates enrolled in a wide variety of academic disciplines.,1,On site,Sessions took place in physical laboratories; participants seated in cubicles and paid in cash at experimenter desk.,1,Repeated 4-player binary-choice public good game framed as a prisoner's dilemma; cooperation (Option 1) yields 0 MU private and 10 MU social benefit distributed equally to the three others; defection (Option 2) yields 3 MU private and 0 MU social benefit. Identifiers are randomly reshuffled each period; group composition is fixed.,Summarizes detailed design features from Sections 3.2 and network structure description for this condition.,1
