10.3390_g12020050.md,Generalized exchange – Costless punishment (2;0),Condition labeled 'PUNISHMENT (COSTLESS)' under 'GENERALIZED EXCHANGE' in Table 1 and described with punishment regime (2;0).,1,"Network structure: Generalized exchange (circular chain); Punishment regime: Costless peer punishment (penalty 2 MU, cost 0 MU) limited to immediate neighbors",Punishment regime (2;0) is implemented inside the generalized exchange structure with sanctions restricted to neighbors.,1,True,Uses human subjects.,1,True,Part of the designed 2×3 lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted at VCEE laboratory.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,"Experimental condition, not analytical.",1,4,Four participants in each circular exchange group.,1,16,Game length same across all conditions.,1,0,Binary choose to send (cooperate) or keep (defect).,1,0,Same general exchange payoff structure as other GE treatments.,0.8,10,Cooperation entails forgoing 3 MU to provide 10 MU to recipient.,0.4,0,No chat or communication allowed.,1,1,Subjects see sender’s action and payoff consequences and any punishment applied.,0.7,1,Punishment possible only between identified sender-recipient neighbors; punished player can infer identity of punisher.,0.7,0,No rewards used.,1,0,Number of periods not given to participants.,0.9,1,Costless punishment stage with regime (2;0) is present.,1,0,Costless punishment regime (2;0): cost c = 0 MU.,1,2,Punishment regime (2;0): each act reduces target’s payoff by 2 MU.,1,0,No reward mechanism specified.,1,N/A,No positive rewards.,1,N/A,No positive rewards.,1,3,"Per-round choice between keeping 3 MU or providing 10 MU to recipient, as in other treatments.",0.8,0.59,Table 1: COOPERATION for GENERALIZED EXCHANGE PUNISHMENT (COSTLESS) is 0.59.,1,N/R,No raw contribution MU is reported.,0,N/R,Efficiency metrics not given.,0,26.16,Average earnings per period are 6.54 MU per player (Table 1). Group payoff ≈ 4 × 6.54 = 26.16 MU.,0.8,Austria,"Conducted in Vienna, Austria.",0.8,Mostly 20–27 years,Same participant description.,0.9,57% female,Overall sample statistic used for all treatments.,0.9,Undergraduate students in various disciplines,Same subject pool.,1,On site,Lab experiment at VCEE.,1,Same 4-player generalized exchange as baseline with a second-stage costless punishment option restricted to the exchange relation: each recipient can punish their sender (and each sender can be punished by their recipient). Each punishment imposes a 2 MU loss on the neighbor and has zero cost to the punisher. Maximum aggregate punishment on any subject per round is 2 MU.,Captures the interaction of generalized exchange structure with costless punishment.,1
10.1155_2014_148915.md,Simulation – Punishment neighbor-selection comparison,"Figure 3 contrasts two punishment/rewiring rules: (i) neighbors chosen by payoff and (ii) neighbors chosen randomly (unchosen situation). This row describes the condition where the two neighbors to cut links are selected according to their payoff (""choosing situation"").",0.9,Punishment neighbor-selection rule: neighbors with least payoff among those of the most frequent defector are chosen to sever links and reconnect,Independent variable is how two neighbors of the most frequently defecting individual are selected during punishment; this condition selects the two neighbors with least payoff.,0.95,False,The study is a numerical simulation of agents on a small-world network; no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment with subjects; it is a computational model study.,1,False,No physical lab or human-subject setup; entirely simulated.,1,True,"Authors repeatedly refer to simulations on networks and show numerical results for parameters N, m, n, b, k, beta.",1,False,"Paper describes model and simulation results, not closed-form analytical solutions.",1,200,"Figure 3 caption: ""with N=200, m=8, n=5, b=1.3, <k>=4, and β=0.01"".",1,400,"Text: ""Repeat the two aforementioned steps with the time step T=400."" T is the number of evolution time steps (rounds at population level).",0.8,1,"Standard PDG on networks with strategies C/D and payoffs per interaction; no contribution level choice as in PGG, so no continuous contribution decision. For this schema, PDG corresponds best to all-or-nothing cooperation choice, encoded as 1 (any amount) vs 0 (all-or-nothing). Ambiguous but treat as any-amount not applicable, set to 1 by convention.",0.3,N/A,"Not a public goods or contribution game; it is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with binary strategies, so this field does not apply to simulations without endowments.",1,N/R,Marginal per capita return is not defined for this pairwise PDG; no such parameter reported.,1,N/A,No human subjects; agents do not have a communication variable.,1,N/A,Computer simulation without interfaces; display-related features are not applicable.,1,N/A,"No identities shown to human participants, as there are none; punishment is algorithmic.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or human interface.,1,N/A,No user interface; agents are not shown round numbers.,1,1,Core of the model is a punishment and feedback mechanism where neighbors of frequent defectors break links and rewire.,1,N/R,No explicit cost parameter per punishment action is defined in payoff terms; only structural link changes are described.,0.2,N/R,"Punishment is implemented as link deletion/rewiring, not as a numeric fine per unit cost; no per-unit tech factor specified.",0.2,0,Model contains only punishment via breaking links with defectors; no rewarding of cooperators is described.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"PDG is parameterized only by payoff matrix A with entries (1,0;b,0); no initial endowment per round is described.",0.2,N/A,"There is no continuous contribution; outcome variable is fraction of cooperators, not contribution level.",1,N/A,Same as above: only C/D strategies in PDG.,1,N/R,Paper does not compute efficiency as payoff relative to maximum; figures report only fraction of cooperators.,0.2,N/R,Only fraction of cooperators over time is plotted; no aggregate payoff time series or values given.,0.2,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,All results are from computational simulations on networks without human subjects.,1,"Pairwise Prisoner’s Dilemma on a small-world network (initially Watts–Strogatz-type) with payoff matrix A=[[1,0],[b,0]] with 1<b<2; strategy update follows a Fermi rule with intensity β; evolution proceeds in repeated n-round PDG blocks with memory of neighbors’ defection frequency and subsequent punishment via link rewiring; parameters for this condition: N=200, m=8 punished-neighbor selectors per step, n=5 rounds per PDG block, b=1.3, average degree <k>=4, rewiring probability p=0.6 for initial network, β=0.01; 50 independent runs, average fraction of cooperators reported over T=400 time steps.",Synthesized from model description in Section 2 and Figure 3 caption and related text.,1
