10.1057_978-1-137-50247-6_2.md,Appendix 1 – Morale model with altruism norm majority (τ(0) > 1/2),Analytical model in Appendix 1 identifies a long-run morale equilibrium when the fraction of altruism-enforced norms exceeds 1/2 initially.,1,Initial share of agents with altruism-enforced norm τ(0) > 1/2; individual norm parameter μ_i = μ+ > 0 (altruism) versus μ− < 0 (envy). This condition assumes altruism-norm majority so that τ(t) → 1.,"The only varied factor across the two steady states is the initial fraction τ(0) of altruistic types, which drives convergence to the altruism or envy norm and hence to different effort equilibria.",1,False,Model is purely theoretical with no human subjects; utilities and dynamics are defined analytically.,1,False,No controlled experiment or data collection; it is an analytical model.,1,False,No laboratory or lab-like setup with participants; only equations.,1,False,"Although a dynamic equation is presented, no numerical or computational simulations are described.",0.9,True,"Norm evolution and effort levels are derived in closed form (Lemma 1.1, Proposition 2.1).",1,2,"Group has N individuals, but the core payoff function and evolution are written from perspective of representative agent i versus others; no fixed N is specified, so we record minimal interacting unit as 2.",0.3,0,"Time is discrete t=0,1,2,… and analysis concerns asymptotic steady states, not a finite number of rounds in a game-theoretic experiment.",0.3,1,Effort e_i is continuous ≥ 0; not restricted to all-or-nothing contributions.,1,N/A,No public-goods contribution decision by human players; only a continuous effort choice in an analytical model.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return for a public good is defined; output is f(e)=Σ e_j but not framed as a linear public good with MPCR.,1,N/A,No human interaction protocol is implemented.,1,N/A,No information-feedback structure for human subjects.,1,N/A,No punishment stage or identities of punishers in this appendix.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding technology in the model.,1,N/A,No experimental interface; infinite-horizon analytical model.,1,0,Appendix 1 contains no explicit costly punishment operator; only effort and norm evolution.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism is modeled; only utility from others’ payoffs via μ_i.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/A,No explicit reward technology.,1,N/R,Agents choose effort but no fixed endowment of tokens or wealth is defined.,1,N/R,"Outcome of interest is equilibrium effort level e*, not a normalized contribution rate in a public-good game.",1,N/R,"While e* is derived, no numeric value is instantiated; model stays symbolic.",0.9,N/R,No explicit comparison of realized output to a maximum-efficiency benchmark is calculated.,1,N/R,"Group output f(e)=Σ e_j is defined, but no concrete numerical group payoff is reported.",0.9,N/A,No human participants; purely theoretical model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Appendix 1 is an analytical model of norm evolution and morale; no empirical environment.,1,"Analytical model of a finite N-person team production setting: output f(e)=Σ e_j, individual utility U_i = f(e)/N − c(e_i) + μ_i (N−1) f(e)/N with μ_i capturing envy (μ−<0) or altruism (μ+>0). Norms (μ types) evolve via random matching and socialization with probabilities q(τ); if initial share of altruism types τ(0) > 1/2, τ(t) → 1 and the unique long-run morale equilibrium has symmetric effort e = {1 + μ+ (N−1)}/N.",Summarizes the structure and key equilibrium characterization for the altruism-majority case.,1
10.1057_978-1-137-50247-6_2.md,Appendix 1 – Morale model with envy norm majority (τ(0) < 1/2),Second long-run morale equilibrium in Appendix 1 arises when the fraction of envy-enforced norms exceeds 1/2 initially.,1,Initial share of agents with altruism-enforced norm τ(0) < 1/2; individual norm parameter μ_i = μ− < 0 (envy) versus μ+ > 0 (altruism). This condition assumes envy-norm majority so that τ(t) → 0.,Changing only τ(0) flips which norm becomes dominant and yields a distinct low-morale effort equilibrium.,1,False,Same analytical framework as in the altruism-majority case; no data.,1,False,No controlled intervention with subjects.,1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,False,"Norm dynamics are solved analytically, with no explicit simulations.",0.9,True,Equilibrium norm and effort levels are given in Lemma 1.1 and Proposition 2.1 analytically.,1,2,Same underlying N-person model; N not numerically instantiated. Represent minimal interacting unit.,0.3,0,Infinite-horizon dynamics rather than finite rounds.,0.3,1,"Effort e_i is continuous, not binary.",1,N/A,No experimental endowment/allocation stage.,1,N/R,No MPCR is defined.,1,N/A,No communication protocol.,1,N/A,No subject interface.,1,N/A,No punishment in this model.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,Not an experiment with rounds.,1,0,Model includes envy but not a punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,N/A,No punishment technology.,1,0,No dedicated reward stage or parameters.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/A,No reward technology.,1,N/R,No finite token/wealth endowment is given; only effort cost function.,1,N/R,"Outcome is effort, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,Effort e* is symbolic; no numeric amount is reported.,0.9,N/R,No efficiency ratio reported.,1,N/R,Group output is defined but not numerically instantiated for this condition.,0.9,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,N/A,No subjects.,1,No human,Pure theory.,1,"Same team-production and norm-evolution model as in the altruism-majority case. When initial share of altruism-enforced norms satisfies τ(0) < 1/2, the dynamic τ(t+1)−τ(t) = {(N−n(t))/(N−1)} τ(t){q(τ(t))−q(1−τ(t))} implies τ(t) → 0. The unique long-run social norm is envy-enforced (μ_i = μ−<0), and the symmetric morale equilibrium has effort e = {1 + μ− (N−1)}/N for all agents, interpreted as a low-morale (low-effort) organizational steady state.",Summarizes the structure and equilibrium characterization for the envy-majority case.,1
10.1057_978-1-137-50247-6_2.md,Appendix 2 – Norm compliance model with effective punishment (high p via low b),"Appendix 2 develops a theoretical model of norm compliance with punishment and reputation, with comparative statics for high vs low punishment probability p. This condition corresponds to the case where the benefit–cost ratio of punishment b is low, yielding higher p and lower μ*.",1,"Punishers’ benefit–cost ratio b = B/T low enough that p = (D − bC)/(2C) < 1 and relatively large; given D > C and fixed costs C, D, T, this implies a high probability of decentralized punishment p and thus a lower critical norm-following share μ* = 2(D − pC)f.","Within the model, varying b changes p (eq. 2.15), which shifts the critical threshold μ* for norm sustainability (eq. 2.17), giving distinct regimes analyzed in Proposition 2.2.",1,False,No experiments or observational data; it is a stylized analytical model.,1,False,No controlled manipulation with participants; only hypothetical agents.,1,False,No lab environment described.,1,False,Dynamics are discussed via differential equations; no simulation results are reported.,0.9,True,Utilities and dynamic equation dμ/dt are derived symbolically; propositions state qualitative results.,1,2,Society is modeled as two matched agents (one from group i and one from group j) per interaction; population is a continuum but the basic game is dyadic.,0.8,0,Continuous-time dynamic dμ/dt; no finite round structure.,0.4,0,"Group i agent chooses x ∈ {0,1} (comply or not), and group j chooses p ∈ [0,1]; compliance is binary, so contributions to the norm are all-or-nothing.",0.9,N/A,No endowment-contribution decision; action is a binary norm-compliance choice.,1,N/R,Model is not a public-goods game; no multiplier or MPCR is defined.,1,N/A,No communication stage specified.,1,N/A,No experimental feedback design.,1,N/A,Punishment is probabilistic and anonymous; identities are not modeled.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No rounds shown to agents; purely analytical.,1,1,"Agents in group j choose punishment probability p against norm violators, incurring cost T and imposing cost C on violators.",1,N/R,"The punisher’s cost parameter is T, but no numerical value is given; only the ratio b = B/T is defined.",0.9,N/R,"Punishment reduces violator’s utility by fixed cost C, but no numeric magnitude per unit of expenditure is specified beyond C.",0.9,0,There is no explicit pro-social reward or bonus; only punishment and reputation-derived utility.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"Agents do not receive a numeric monetary endowment in a game; costs D, C, B, T enter directly into utility.",1,N/R,No normalized contribution metric; main outcome is steady-state share μ of norm followers.,1,N/R,Compliance is binary; there is no continuous contribution amount.,1,N/R,Model does not compute efficiency relative to a benchmark allocation.,1,N/R,Only individual utilities U and V are defined; no aggregate payoff is calculated.,1,N/A,Analytical; no real participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,N/A,No participants.,1,No human,Appendix 2 is a theoretical model with continuum of agents; not an implemented study.,1,"Continuum of agents split evenly into group i (potential norm compliers) and group j (third-party punishers). Group i agent of type g with distribution F(g) on [0,G] chooses x ∈ {0,1} (comply or violate). Utility: U = x(R(g,μ) − D) − (1−x) p C, with reputation value R(g,μ)=gμ and fixed compliance cost D>0, punishment cost to violator C>0, and μ the current fraction of compliers in group i. Group j agent chooses punishment probability p ∈ [0,1], utility V = Pr(x=1)B − (1−Pr(x=1)) p T, with psychological benefit B when opponent complies and punishment cost T>B. Types g with g > (D − pC)/μ comply, yielding Pr(x=1)=1−(D−pC)f/μ for uniform density f=1/G. Optimal punishment for an interior case is p = (D − bC)/(2C) with b=B/T. Norm-following share evolves as dμ/dt = α[Pr(x=1)−1/2] with adjustment rate α. Critical threshold μ* = min{2(D−pC)f, 1/2}. For high punishment (low b, higher p), μ* decreases so norms can be sustained from smaller initial μ, as in Proposition 2.2(a).",Summarizes all key structural equations and clarifies the high-punishment comparative-static condition.,1
10.1057_978-1-137-50247-6_2.md,Appendix 2 – Norm compliance model with strong reputation effect (low p via high b),"This is the contrasting analytical regime in Appendix 2 where the benefit–cost ratio of punishment b is high, leading to lower p and larger μ*, so norm compliance relies more on reputation than punishment.",1,"Punishers’ benefit–cost ratio b = B/T high enough that p = (D − bC)/(2C) is relatively small (or at its lower bound) and μ* = 2(D − pC)f is relatively large, so sustaining the norm requires a larger initial μ but can occur with weak punishment due to strong reputational motivation.","Appendix 2’s Fig. 2.4 and Proposition 2.2(b) analyze the case where b is higher, p lower, and norm sustainability stems from a large μ and reputation-driven utility R(g,μ).",1,False,Still the same analytical model; no data.,1,False,No empirical experiment is conducted.,1,False,No lab protocol.,1,False,No numerical simulation; only qualitative phase-diagram reasoning.,0.9,True,Equations and propositions fully characterize behavior.,1,2,Same pairwise interaction structure between one group i and one group j agent.,0.8,0,"Continuous-time evolutionary adjustment, not rounds.",0.4,0,Compliance decision x is binary.,0.9,N/A,No contribution task.,1,N/R,Not a public-goods payoffs structure.,1,N/A,No communication modeled.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,"No identity revelation, only abstract probability p.",1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No finite rounds.,1,1,Punishment remains part of the model; only its equilibrium intensity p is low.,1,N/R,Punisher’s cost T and violator’s cost C are symbolic; no calibrated value.,0.9,N/R,"As above, only fixed cost C is specified; no per-unit technology.",0.9,0,No separate reward stage.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism present.,1,N/R,"As before, only costs enter utilities.",1,N/R,Norm-following share μ is the key state variable; not expressed as contribution rate in a game.,1,N/R,Compliance is binary; there is no continuous amount.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric given.,1,N/R,"Aggregate welfare is discussed qualitatively, not numerically.",1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Purely theoretical exploration of punishment–reputation interaction.,1,"Same societal model as the high-p condition: continuum of agents, two groups, binary norm-compliance choice x for group i and punishment probability p for group j, utilities U and V, reputation value R(g,μ)=gμ, and adjustment dynamics dμ/dt = α[Pr(x=1)−1/2]. When the benefit–cost ratio of punishment b=B/T is higher, optimal p from p = (D − bC)/(2C) is smaller. Condition (2.17) shows μ* = min{2(D−pC)f, 1/2}; lower p shifts μ* right. Proposition 2.2(b) states that with sufficiently large initial μ and strong reputation value R(g,μ), the norm can be sustained even when punishment is weak (low p), reflecting a reputation-based compliance regime.","Summarizes how the same structure yields a distinct, reputation-intensive compliance condition when b is high and p is low.",1
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT simulations – Avoidance (AVOID) strategy in one-shot Public Goods Game,"Section 9.2.3 describes an EGT study [17] extending a public goods game with commitment-based strategies, contrasting AVOID vs RESTRICT. This row captures the AVOID condition.",0.76,"Commitment-based continuation strategy toward non-committers: AVOID (do not enact the public good if some co-players refuse commitment); cost of arranging commitment, cost/benefit of public good, and group size varied parametrically","Text explicitly defines two strategies, AVOID and RESTRICT, differing in how proposers react to non-committers. Other parameters are said to vary (group size, benefit and costs).",0.78,False,The work is described as EGT agent-based computer simulations; no human participants are mentioned.,1,True,"Although not a lab experiment with humans, the agent-based simulations systematically manipulate conditions (strategies and parameters), fitting a computational experiment.",0.79,False,No lab or field with humans; simulations are in silico.,1,True,Explicitly referred to as EGT methods in agent-based computer simulations.,1,False,This part is presented as simulation-based; analytical treatment is highlighted later for other work.,0.7,0,Exact group size in the PGG is not specified in the chapter; only that players meet in groups of a fixed size and that results especially concern larger groups.,0.01,1,Described as a one-shot Public Goods Game (PGG).,0.95,1,Standard PGG: players choose whether to cooperate and contribute or to defect without contributing; typical model allows contributing full endowment vs zero; partial contributions are not mentioned. Marked as all-or-nothing.,0.6,N/A,No human subjects; default allocation concept does not apply.,1,N/R,Only states that total contribution is multiplied by a constant factor and distributed equally; no numeric factor or group size is given to compute MPCR.,0.01,N/A,No human participants; communication interface not applicable.,1,0,Standard theoretical PGG/EGT model; no mention of feedback displays such as earnings summaries.,0.6,0,"Punishment occurs only as compensation within agreements and restriction of benefits, with no notion of identities or observability discussed.",0.6,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identity revelation is described.,0.9,0,One-shot PGG; the notion of showing remaining rounds is not relevant or discussed.,0.9,1,"Non-contributors that committed must compensate proposers; also, AVOID removes benefits from non-committers as an institutional punishment-like effect.",0.85,N/R,Compensation for broken commitment is noted but its cost per unit is not quantified.,0.01,N/R,Magnitude of loss imposed per unit of punishment/compensation is not specified.,0.01,0,No explicit costly positive reward/tipping mechanism is described; only benefit from the public good.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowment per player is not reported.,0.01,N/R,"Results are discussed qualitatively (emergence of cooperation, more favorable societal outcomes) without numeric contribution rates.",0.01,N/R,No token-level contribution amounts are provided.,0.01,N/R,Only qualitative statements about societal outcomes; no explicit efficiency ratios.,0.01,N/R,Group payoff levels are not numerically reported.,0.01,N/A,No human participants in simulations.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based EGT simulations only.,1,"One-shot Public Goods Game with prior commitment stage. Commitment proposers pay a personal proposer’s cost to arrange agreements. If all requested co-players accept, the public good is enacted and all are expected to contribute; those who commit but fail to contribute must compensate proposers. Under AVOID, if any co-player refuses commitment, proposers do not enact the public good, removing benefits from both non-committers and would-be contributors. EGT evolutionary dynamics over mixed populations of strategies are simulated, with parameters such as group size, public good benefit, and arrangement/restriction costs varied.",Summarized from the description of the extended PGG and AVOID strategy in section 9.2.3 and reference to [17].,0.86
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT simulations – Restriction (RESTRICT) strategy in one-shot Public Goods Game,Same study [17] in section 9.2.3 contrasts AVOID with RESTRICT; this row captures the RESTRICT condition.,0.76,"Commitment-based continuation strategy toward non-committers: RESTRICT (establish boundaries so that only committers have equal or better access to the public good, reducing non-contributors’ benefits); cost of arranging commitment, cost of restricting, benefit of public good, and group size varied parametrically",Text defines RESTRICT as establishing boundaries on the common good so only those who have truly committed have better access or non-contributors get reduced benefit; costs and benefits are varied in the analysis.,0.82,False,"Described as EGT in the framework of the one-shot PGG, using computational models.",1,True,"Simulated conditions (AVOID vs RESTRICT) are systematically compared, which fits a computational experiment.",0.79,False,No human lab setting.,1,True,Explicitly part of EGT studies using simulation techniques.,1,False,This particular PGG extension is presented as simulation-based; analytical results are emphasized later for another model.,0.7,0,Only notes that PGG involves groups of fixed size and that effects are stronger in larger groups; specific numeric group size not provided.,0.01,1,Explicitly a one-shot PGG.,0.95,1,Same PGG structure as in AVOID: binary choice to contribute or defect is emphasized; partial contributions not discussed.,0.6,N/A,No human players.,1,N/R,"Multiplier factor and group size are not numerically given, so MPCR cannot be computed.",0.01,N/A,Simulation with no human interaction.,1,0,No display or reporting mechanism is described in the simulation.,0.6,0,Restriction of benefits is modeled at payoff level without any mention of identities or visibility.,0.6,0,No explicit reward mechanism with identity.,0.9,0,"One-shot game, no round counter.",0.9,1,Non-committers or defecting committers suffer reduced or zero benefit due to restriction and/or compensation; this functions as punishment.,0.85,N/R,The model includes a cost of restricting and compensation payments but does not specify per-unit punishment cost.,0.01,N/R,Magnitude of negative impact per cost unit is not reported.,0.01,0,No additional positive reward scheme; only participation in the public good.,0.9,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Player endowment is not numerically stated.,0.01,N/R,"Only reports that RESTRICT leads to higher contribution levels, especially with larger group size and benefit, but without numeric values.",0.01,N/R,No raw contributions reported.,0.01,N/R,Mentions 'more favorable societal outcomes' but not efficiency ratios or numbers.,0.01,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes provided.,0.01,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,EGT agent-based simulations only.,1,"Same extended one-shot PGG with a prior commitment stage as in AVOID. Commitment proposers pay a cost to set up agreements; all must accept to proceed. Those who commit but do not contribute later must compensate proposers. Under RESTRICT, commitment proposers who face non-committers implement measures to delimit access to the common good—e.g., only true committers receive full benefits or non-contributors’ benefits are reduced—incurring an additional restriction cost. EGT simulations examine how AVOID vs RESTRICT perform under varying group sizes, commitment and restriction costs, and public good benefit, finding RESTRICT generally yields higher cooperation and better societal outcomes.",Structured from section 9.2.3’s description of the RESTRICT strategy and comparative findings.,0.86
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT simulations – Agreements vs ex post costly punishment in pairwise cooperation dilemmas,Section 9.2.2 summarizes work [22] that compares arranging prior explicit agreements (commitments) with a strategy of costly punishment applied after defection.,0.72,"Mechanism to promote cooperation in pairwise cooperation dilemmas: (a) Prior explicit commitment agreements with compensation for breach (costly to arrange), including shared vs unilateral arrangement costs; contrasted with (b) ex post costly punishment without prior agreements. This row captures the commitment-based agreements condition.","The text states they compare prior agreements (commitment proposers, pledges, compensation) with costly punishment that punishes after the fact, and examines shared vs unilateral arrangement costs.",0.8,False,Described as using EGT and agent-based computer simulations to study mechanisms promoting cooperation.,1,True,"Different mechanisms (commitment vs punishment) and cost structures are systematically manipulated in simulations, constituting computational experiments.",0.8,False,"Simulations only, not a lab with humans.",1,True,"Explicitly: 'This research demonstrates ... Our study, EGT is used to show ...'; previous paragraph notes agent-based computer simulations.",1,False,This particular work is framed as simulation-based; analytical treatment is mentioned later for a different model.,0.7,2,Described as 'pairwise interactions' and examples like buying a house with a partner; standard is two players.,0.95,N/R,"The game is discussed as starting new projects and arranging agreements; it fits a one-shot or few-round cooperation dilemma, but the exact number of rounds per interaction is not given.",0.2,1,"Described in terms of cooperating vs free riding on investments; no evidence of continuous contribution levels, suggesting a binary cooperate/defect structure.",0.6,N/A,No human subjects; endowment allocation concept is not meaningful.,1,N/R,The exact game payoff matrix and multipliers are not provided.,0.01,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,The model does not include any UI-like features; only evolutionary payoffs.,0.7,0,No notion of identity visibility beyond strategy types is discussed.,0.7,0,No explicit identity-based reward mechanism.,0.9,0,Simulations track evolutionary time but not round information presented to agents.,0.8,1,"Within the agreement mechanism, compensation for unhonored deals functions as punishment; the comparison also references a costly punishment strategy.",0.9,N/R,Costs of compensation and punishment are conceptually described but not numerically specified.,0.01,N/R,The impact per cost unit is not given.,0.01,0,There is no separate reward mechanism besides successful cooperation payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/R,"Endowments in the abstract cooperation project (e.g., buying a house) are not quantified.",0.01,N/R,"Outcomes are described qualitatively (high levels of cooperation, prevalence of commitment proposers) but not with normalized contribution metrics.",0.01,N/R,No exact contribution amounts reported.,0.01,N/R,Text refers to 'more effective' and 'higher levels of cooperation' but no explicit efficiency ratio.,0.01,N/R,No group payoff numbers provided.,0.01,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,EGT simulations only.,1,"Pairwise cooperation dilemmas modeled using EGT and agent-based simulations. One condition assumes prior explicit agreements: players may propose commitments, paying an arrangement cost (e.g., cost of hiring a lawyer). Agreements specify compensation to be paid if a deal is not honored, which can be shared equally or not between partners. Commitment proposers become prevalent when arrangement costs are justified by benefits and compensation is sufficiently high. The model reveals that requiring partners to share in the cost of setting up the deal leads to higher cooperation and removes fake cooperators. Outcomes are compared against a separate costly-punishment-only strategy where no prior agreements are made and defectors are punished ex post.","Condensed from the description of [22] in section 9.2.2, focusing on the agreement-based mechanism.",0.84
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT simulations – Ex post costly punishment without prior agreements in pairwise cooperation dilemmas,Complementary condition to the agreements mechanism in [22]; described as a separate strategy that punishes bad behavior after the fact without prior commitments.,0.72,Mechanism to promote cooperation: ex post costly punishment of defectors with no prior explicit agreements; contrasted with prior commitment agreements with compensation (separate condition),"Section 9.2.2 explicitly mentions comparison with 'costly punishment, a strategy that does not make any prior agreements and simply punishes afterwards.'",0.84,False,Same underlying EGT simulation framework as the agreements condition.,1,True,Part of the same computational experiment varying mechanisms; this row is the punishment-only condition.,0.8,False,Not a human lab study.,1,True,Mechanism compared using EGT and simulation.,1,False,No closed-form analytical solution is reported for this part.,0.7,2,Same pairwise cooperation setting as the agreements condition.,0.95,N/R,Number of repeated interactions is not specified.,0.2,1,Binary cooperate/defect frame; no indication of continuous effort.,0.6,N/A,No humans.,1,N/R,Game payoff parameters are not detailed.,0.01,N/A,Simulation.,1,0,No feedback UI modeled.,0.7,0,Identity and observability of punishers not discussed.,0.7,0,No explicit rewards.,0.9,0,Not modeled as information to agents.,0.8,1,Defined as a costly punishment strategy that punishes bad behavior after the fact.,0.98,N/R,"Punishment must be 'quite excessive' sometimes to promote cooperation, but values are not given.",0.01,N/R,Impact per unit of punishment cost is unspecified.,0.01,0,No positive rewards; cooperation is promoted via punishment.,0.95,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Endowments for the underlying game are not specified.,0.01,N/R,"Discussion is qualitative, indicating that punishment must be excessive to obtain significant levels of cooperation, but without numeric rates.",0.01,N/R,No amounts provided.,0.01,N/R,"Only relative effectiveness of mechanisms is discussed, not efficiency ratios.",0.01,N/R,No numeric payoffs.,0.01,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,EGT simulations only.,1,"In the punishment-only mechanism, agents do not arrange prior commitments. Instead, after defections occur, they may punish defectors at a personal cost. Prior literature is cited showing that sufficiently strong (sometimes excessive) punishment can promote cooperation, but it is costly. This mechanism is compared directly with commitment-based agreements that specify consequences in advance; the latter achieves higher cooperation with lower punishment intensity and better cost-to-impact ratios.","Summarized from the comparison discussion in section 9.2.2, focusing on the punishment-only strategy.",0.83
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT simulations – Apology only (no commitments) in iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma,Section 9.2.4 summarizes work [24] on apology with and without commitments in the IPD; this row captures the non-committed interactions with apology.,0.78,Availability and cost of explicit apology in the IPD in commitment-free interactions; apology sincerity level (compensation amount) varied; no prior commitments allowed,"Text states they studied strategies that explicitly apologize in between rounds, and analyze apology behavior in non-committed interactions, especially with varying cost (sincerity).",0.83,False,Described as an EGT model and agent-based simulations in the IPD context.,1,True,They construct models and simulate different strategy types and apology costs; this is a computational experiment.,0.82,False,No human subjects.,1,True,"Explicit: 'Employing EGT, we show...' regarding the model with apology strategies.",1,False,This work is presented as simulation-based.,0.7,2,Standard IPD is a two-player game; no indication of group extensions here.,0.98,N/R,IPD implies repeated interactions but the exact number of rounds is not specified.,0.2,1,IPD is a binary cooperate/defect game.,1,N/A,No human players; no endowment-based contributions.,1,N/A,Not a public goods game; IPD has a payoff matrix rather than MPCR.,1,N/A,Simulation without human communication.,1,0,No display variables relevant to human subjects.,0.8,0,No identities modeled beyond strategies.,0.8,0,No separate reward mechanism.,0.95,0,Infinite or stochastic horizon IPD is typical; information about N is not modeled as shown to agents.,0.8,1,"Apology involves compensating the co-player after an error, which acts as a self-imposed costly transfer akin to negative payoff to the apologizer and positive to the other, i.e., a punishment of self for mistaken defection.",0.7,N/R,Apology cost amounts are varied but not numerically specified in the chapter.,0.01,N/R,Exact mapping between apology cost and payoff adjustments is not provided.,0.01,0,"Apology gives compensation to others but is framed as apology, not a general reward scheme; no variable 'reward' mechanism is modeled.",0.8,N/A,No explicit reward parameter.,1,N/A,No explicit reward parameter.,1,N/R,IPD payoffs are not parametrized via endowments in the description.,0.01,N/R,"Outcomes described as frequency of cooperation and frequency of apologizing, but no numeric cooperation rates reported.",0.01,N/A,IPD is a binary action game without monetary contributions.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metrics reported.,0.01,N/R,No numeric payoffs or averages.,0.01,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Model is implemented as EGT simulations.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with noise (erroneous moves). In this condition, strategies may explicitly apologize after making an error between rounds. An apologizing act consists of compensating the co-player by an amount (costly apology) to encourage cooperation in the next actual round. The population includes apologizers and non-apologizers; some accept apology compensation but do not apologize when they err (fake apologizers). Simulations show that in non-committed interactions, apologizing acts are rare, especially when cooperation is very costly, and apology alone is insufficient to achieve high cooperation. The sincerity of apology (its cost) is varied to see how it affects evolution of cooperation and vulnerability to exploitation.",Summarized from section 9.2.4’s description of the apology model for commitment-free interactions.,0.87
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT simulations – Apology with prior commitments in iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma,"Same study [24] in section 9.2.4, focusing on interactions where commitments are arranged before IPD play; this row captures the committed interactions with apology.",0.78,Availability and cost of explicit apology in the IPD with a prior commitment stage; apology sincerity (cost) and presence of commitments varied,The text compares scenarios where apology occurs in systems where players first ask for commitments with those without; also examines sincerity (cost) of apology.,0.84,False,Same EGT/agent-based simulation framework.,1,True,Different combinations of commitment and apology costs are simulated and compared.,0.82,False,"Simulation, not human lab.",1,True,"Explicitly noted: 'Employing EGT, we show that when the apology occurs in a system where the players first ask for a commitment...' ",1,False,Described as modeling and EGT simulation rather than derivation-only.,0.7,2,Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma between two agents.,0.98,N/R,Number of IPD rounds not provided.,0.2,1,IPD uses binary actions.,1,N/A,No endowment contributions with humans.,1,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,0,No human-facing summaries.,0.8,0,Identities are not a feature of the theoretical model.,0.8,0,No explicit reward identities.,0.95,0,Not included as an information variable for strategies.,0.8,1,"Within commitments, breaking a commitment leads to penalties; apology costs act as compensatory transfers.",0.85,N/R,Arrangement and compensation costs are not quantified.,0.01,N/R,Exact payoffs not given.,0.01,0,No separate reward component beyond mutual cooperation within commitments.,0.9,N/A,No reward parameter.,1,N/A,No reward parameter.,1,N/R,IPD payoffs are not described via endowments.,0.01,N/R,Results reported qualitatively: apology supported by prior commitment leads to significantly higher cooperation; no exact cooperation rates.,0.01,N/A,No token contributions; IPD actions only.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency values.,0.01,N/R,No numeric payoffs.,0.01,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,EGT simulation.,1,"Iterated Prisoner’s Dilemma with noise and a prior commitment stage. Players can propose commitments before interacting; accepted commitments create expectations of cooperation and potential penalties for failure. Strategies may apologize explicitly when mistakes occur, offering compensation whose magnitude indicates sincerity. Populations include sincere apologizers, fake apologizers (who accept apologies but do not apologize themselves), and non-apologizers, with and without commitments. Simulations show: (i) Apology alone is insufficient for high cooperation; (ii) Apology combined with prior commitments yields significantly higher cooperation; (iii) Apology must be sufficiently costly (sincere) to be effective in either setting; (iv) Individuals use a much costlier apology in committed relationships because it better identifies free riders like fake committers.",Based on the detailed qualitative summary of findings in section 9.2.4.,0.88
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT analytical and numerical model – Commitment with revenge (no apology/forgiveness) in repeated IPD,"Section 9.2.5 summarizes work [33] where they first analyze commitment strategies with revenge in IPD, then extend to apology and forgiveness. This row represents the baseline commitment-with-revenge outcome without the apology-forgiveness extension.",0.78,Presence of commitment agreements with compensation and revenge strategies in repeated IPD; mistake (error) rate varied; no apology/forgiveness mechanism enabled,"They study commitment strategies under repeated interactions with mistakes; in the baseline, when agreements fail, participants take revenge by defecting thereafter.",0.82,False,Described as providing analytical and numerical results using EGT; no human participants.,1,True,Numerical simulations exploring parameter spaces complement analytical derivations; we classify this as a computational experiment.,0.76,False,No human lab.,1,True,Includes numerical EGT simulations alongside analytical work.,0.9,True,Explicitly: 'Our analytical and numerical results [33] reveal ...'; they derive conditions for viability of strategies analytically.,0.95,2,"Model is 'in the context of the IPD', which is two-player.",0.98,N/R,"Described as 'repeated interactions', 'on-going agreements', and 'remaining interactions', but the horizon or distribution is not specified.",0.2,1,Standard repeated IPD with binary cooperate/defect actions.,1,N/A,No human endowments.,1,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,0,No user interface.,0.8,0,"Model focuses on strategy dynamics, not identity visibility.",0.8,0,No reward identities.,0.95,0,Not modeled as information to agents.,0.8,1,Revenge is implemented by defection in remaining interactions after commitment breach; compensation exists for broken commitments.,0.9,N/R,Penalties and compensation are conceptual; no numeric costs are specified in the chapter.,0.01,N/R,"Mapping from punishment actions (revenge, compensation) to payoffs is not quantified.",0.01,0,No separate rewarding mechanism beyond mutual cooperation.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward scheme.,1,N/A,No explicit reward scheme.,1,N/R,Payoff matrix parameters are not described via endowments.,0.01,N/R,"Outcomes are described conceptually (most successful individuals are those who propose commitments and then cooperate unless a mistake occurs, revenge vs forgiveness payoffs) but no numeric cooperation rates.",0.01,N/A,"IPD actions, not numeric contributions.",1,N/R,No explicit efficiency ratios; only relative success of strategies.,0.01,N/R,Average payoffs or group payoffs are not numerically specified.,0.01,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical plus numerical EGT model.,1,"Repeated IPD with a commitment stage. Individuals can propose, accept, or reject agreements before repeated interactions. Agreements may end before all interactions are finished. Strategies specify how to behave when the agreement is present or absent, and what to do when mistakes occur (e.g., trembling-hand errors). In the revenge-only baseline, if the commitment is broken, individuals end the agreement, collect compensation, and then take revenge by defecting in all remaining interactions. Analytical and numerical analysis reveals that the most successful individuals are those who propose commitments (willing to pay arrangement costs) and, conditional on an agreement, cooperate unless a mistake occurs; upon breach they switch to revenge. This revenge-based outcome can outperform TFT-like reciprocal strategies, especially when arrangement costs are amortized over many interactions.","Summarized from section 9.2.5’s description of commitment, mistakes, and revenge strategies prior to adding apology and forgiveness.",0.88
10.1007_978-3-319-29354-7_9.md,EGT analytical and numerical model – Commitment with apology and forgiveness in repeated IPD,Section 9.2.5 then extends the commitment-revenge model to include costly apology and forgiveness; this row captures that extended condition.,0.82,Presence and sincerity level (cost) of apology-forgiveness mechanism layered on top of commitment and revenge in repeated IPD; error (mistake) frequency varied; apology cost modeled as external or individual parameter,"The text clarifies they add apology and forgiveness, define apology cost either externally or per individual, and analyze when these evolve with different mistake frequencies.",0.87,False,Same analytical and numerical EGT framework; no humans.,1,True,"Numerical experiments scan over apology costs and error rates to determine viability regions, complementing analytical results.",0.8,False,The work is theoretical/simulation-based.,1,True,The paper provides numerical (simulation) insight in addition to analytical derivations.,0.9,True,"Explicitly: 'Our analytical and numerical results [33] reveal for the first time under which conditions revenge, apology and forgiveness can evolve...'",0.95,2,Still in the context of repeated IPD.,0.98,N/R,Number of IPD rounds per agreement not given.,0.2,1,Standard IPD with binary actions.,1,N/A,No human endowment contributions.,1,N/A,Not a PGG.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,0,No UI.,0.8,0,No identity exposure modeled.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,0.95,0,Not information shown to players.,0.8,1,Revenge (defecting after breach) and compensation mechanisms remain; apology costs also function as compensatory transfers.,0.9,N/R,"Arrangement and compensation costs, as well as apology costs, are discussed qualitatively but not numerically.",0.01,N/R,Relative payoff changes due to punishment are not numerically parameterized in the chapter.,0.01,0,No additional reward mechanism besides cooperative payoffs.,0.9,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/A,No explicit rewards.,1,N/R,Endowments not specified.,0.01,N/R,"They report that cooperation can remain high and that the extension produces even higher cooperation levels than the revenge-only model, but no numeric cooperation proportions are given.",0.01,N/A,IPD with binary actions.,1,N/R,"Efficiency is framed qualitatively (higher levels of cooperation, evolutionarily viable) without explicit values.",0.01,N/R,Group or pair payoffs not numerically reported.,0.01,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical and numerical EGT modeling only.,1,"The commitment IPD model is augmented with an apology-forgiveness mechanism. When errors occur during an ongoing agreement, individuals may apologize by paying a cost (either externally set or individual-specific), and partners may choose to forgive and continue the agreement rather than break it and take revenge. The model explores under what conditions revenge, apology, and forgiveness are evolutionarily viable. It finds: (i) When mistakes are frequent, there exists a sincerity threshold for apology (cost must exceed cost of cooperation but not be too high) under which agreements are preserved and cooperation remains high; (ii) This extension yields even higher cooperation levels than the revenge-only outcome; (iii) If apology is insincere (too cheap), fake committers can dominate by repeatedly defecting and apologizing, thereby destroying cooperation; (iv) Forgivers only outperform pure avengers when the benefit-to-cost ratio is high enough. The model thus delineates parameter regions where apology and forgiveness sustain long-term cooperative agreements despite frequent errors.",Derived from section 9.2.5’s synthesis of analytical and numerical findings regarding apology and forgiveness added to commitments.,0.9
