Yugoslavia (former),"Text: experiments conducted in Israel, Japan, the United States, and what was then Yugoslavia.",1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,"This summary does not specify that subjects were students, though Roth et al. typically used students.",0.3,On site,Standard Roth et al. bargaining experiments are run in lab environments; nothing suggests field or online here.,0.8,"Modified ultimatum game: multiple proposers simultaneously make offers to a single responder over a fixed pie; responder chooses to accept or reject only the highest offer; other offers are automatically rejected. 10 rounds per session. Empirically, the highest offer rate quickly converged to 100%, so responders capture almost the entire surplus.",Summarizes rules and outcome statements in the text.,1
10.1007_978-981-10-6439-5_8.md,Ultimatum game with responder competition (Güth et al. 1997),Section 8.3 describes an ultimatum game variant by Güth et al. with one proposer and five competing responders.,0.95,Responder competition: single proposer offers to five responders; each responder sets a cutoff level; one responder is randomly selected among those willing to accept.,Key design feature is competition among responders for a single offer.,1,True,Described as an experiment with actual responders’ cutoff choices.,1,True,Controlled game-theoretic experiment.,1,True,Standard Güth et al. ultimatum experiments; this summary fits lab study.,0.85,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Reports empirical cutoff behavior after five rounds.,0.9,6,Text: one proposer makes offers to five responders.,1,5,Text: 'After five rounds in their experiment...'.,1,1,Proposer chooses offer; responders specify cutoff thresholds; not a contribution game.,0.8,N/A,"Ultimatum bargaining, not a contribution game.",1,N/A,Not a public goods game.,1,0,No communication reported.,0.7,0,Summary does not mention feedback about others’ cutoff choices or payoffs.,0.3,0,No explicit punishment mechanism.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Whether they know there will be five rounds is not stated.,0.2,0,No costly punishment; only accept/reject through cutoffs inherent to ultimatum.,0.9,N/A,No explicit punishment stage.,1,N/A,No explicit punishment stage.,1,0,No separate reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Stake size of the pie not specified.,0.2,N/A,Not a contribution game.,1,N/A,Not a public goods contribution.,1,N/R,Only reports cutoff levels and acceptance behavior; no efficiency metric.,0.2,N/R,No numeric payoff summaries reported.,0.1,N/R,Not specified here.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported.,0.1,N/R,This summary does not identify the subject pool.,0.1,On site,Standard lab experiment context for Güth et al.; nothing suggests field or online.,0.8,"One proposer makes an offer dividing a fixed pie among herself and potential responders. Five responders each choose a personal minimum acceptable share (cutoff). Any responder whose cutoff is ≤ the proposer’s offer is eligible; one such responder is chosen at random, and payoffs are then determined as in a standard two-player ultimatum game between that proposer and selected responder. Across five rounds, average cutoff falls below 5%, and 71% of responders set their cutoff to zero.",Restates the rules and the main behavioral outcome reported.,1
10.1007_978-981-10-6439-5_8.md,Trust game with betrayal and punishment evaluation (de Quervain et al. 2004),Section 8.5.1 describes a trust-game-based neuroimaging study by de Quervain et al. (2004).,0.95,Opponent behavior (returns vs steals money) and punishment conditions (intentional vs unintentional betrayal; costly vs costless punishment) during evaluation phase,Study varies whether opponents reciprocate or betray and whether punishment is costly/intentional to examine neural responses.,0.9,True,Human volunteers played trust games and underwent PET imaging.,1,True,Controlled game and punishment conditions combined with neuroimaging.,1,True,Neuroimaging (PET) requires a lab/medical setting.,1,False,Not a simulation.,1,False,Empirical neuroeconomic study.,0.9,2,Each volunteer plays a trust game with an 'opponent'; dyadic interaction.,0.9,7,"Text: volunteers play 7 games with 7 opponents, implying 7 one-shot games.",1,1,Standard trust game variant; participants decide whether/how much to invest and how much to return; not limited to all-or-nothing.,0.7,0,"In trust games, endowment typically starts with investor; no shared default allocation.",0.7,N/R,"Not a public goods game; specific multiplication factor not reported here, though betrayal suggests some return structure.",0.1,0,Interactions appear to be via experimental interface; no mention of direct communication.,0.6,0,Participants rate each opponent individually; no cross-opponent payoff summaries described.,0.6,0,Punishment is directed at opponents but identities beyond their role are not described; likely anonymous; not explicitly stated.,0.4,0,"Study focuses on punishment, not separate reward choices.",0.9,0,Not specified whether participants were told there would be exactly seven games; treat as unknown/0.,0.3,1,Participants can give 'real punishment' to opponents; striatal activity correlates with amount of punishment.,1,N/R,"It states punishment may require a cost or not, but numeric cost per unit is not given.",0.2,N/R,Degree to which punishment reduces opponent’s payoff per unit not quantified.,0.2,0,Focus is on punishment; no separate reward manipulation.,0.9,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism.,1,N/R,Initial amounts in this trust version not stated in summary.,0.2,N/R,No quantitative data on investment rates is reported here; focus is on neural responses to punishment decisions.,0.1,N/R,Investment amounts not described numerically.,0.1,N/R,No payoff- or efficiency-based outcome statistics reported.,0.1,N/R,Not reported; main outcomes are neural activities and punishment amounts.,0.1,N/R,Country/lab location not specified in this summary.,0.1,N/R,Described as 'volunteers'; no age information.,0.1,N/R,Not mentioned.,0.1,N/R,Not specified.,0.1,On site,PET neuroimaging requires on-site medical/lab environment.,1,"Volunteers play 7 trust games with 7 different opponents (confederates). Of these, 3 reciprocate (return money) and 4 betray (do not return or steal). After each match, participants are interviewed about fairness/punishment and can administer real punishment under conditions that vary whether betrayal was intentional and whether punishment is costly. PET measures brain activity during interviews and punishment decisions; striatum activity increases with costly punishment of intentional betrayal.",Summarizes procedure and key manipulations as described.,1
10.18564_jasss.3336.md,Model 1 – Evolutionary PD with individual aggression (all parameter combinations),"Single agent-based model with multiple parameter sweeps (population size, deterrence, pun_cost, gossip) but the underlying game structure and strategy set are constant; treated as one simulation condition family.",0.87,"Population size ∈ {50, 100, 500}; deterrence (strength of overestimation) ∈ {1, 3, 6} SD; punishment cost to winner pun_cost ∈ {3, 6, 9}; gossip (number of agents informed) ∈ {5, 20}.","Table 2 specifies these four parameters as independent, each with several discrete values; other parameters are held constant.",0.98,False,No human participants; purely computational agent-based model implemented in NetLogo.,1,False,"Simulation study, not a controlled experiment with human or naturalistic subjects.",1,False,No lab setting; entirely computational.,1,True,Described explicitly as agent-based models and simulations run for many generations in NetLogo.,1,False,Some equations are used inside the model but there is no closed-form analytical model as a standalone study condition.,0.9,2,"Each interaction pairs two agents to play the Prisoner’s Dilemma game (""Agents are paired""; ""for each pair"" in Appendix A).",1,200,"Within each generation, the for-loop is from i = 1 to 200 interaction rounds.",1,1,"Action space is binary cooperate/defect, not a continuous contribution level; coded as all-or-nothing contribution choice.",0.9,N/A,"This is a Prisoner’s Dilemma with discrete cooperate/defect choices, not a continuous-contribution public goods game.",1,N/R,No linear public good or MPCR is defined; only a PD payoff matrix is given.,1,N/A,No human interaction; communication among agents is not modeled as chat.,1,N/A,"UI concepts such as on-screen summaries don’t apply to simulated agents; they only use internal variables (fear_list, etc.).",1,N/A,"Agents track winners via fear_list internally, but this is not an interface visibility setting as in human experiments.",1,N/A,No explicit reward mechanism or identity revelation setting; only punishment/fights.,1,N/R,No notion of agents being informed about total or remaining rounds is described.,0.7,1,Agents can instigate fights that impose a punishment cost on the loser; authors describe this as punishment and aggression.,1,9,"Table 2: ""punishment = 9 regulates the magnitude of a punishment inflicted on the agent losing a fight""; this is the per-fight loss to the punished agent.",1,1,Loser pays a fixed punishment of 9 per fight regardless of aggressor investment; treated as 1:1 mapping of the punishment parameter to damage units.,0.7,0,No rewarding or positive transfers are modeled; only aggressive fights causing punishment or submission costs.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism exists.,1,N/R,"The PD payoff matrix is specified (e.g., 10,10; 0,0; 10,0), but no per-round endowment or initial balance per agent is described as an endowment.",0.6,0.7506,"CC rate at end of most cooperative run (n=50, deterrence=6, pun_cost=6, gossip=20) in Table 6 is 75.06%; in PD this corresponds to the probability that both players cooperate.",0.9,N/R,Binary cooperate/defect decisions with no numeric token contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,"Authors report frequencies of PD outcomes (CC, CD, DD) but do not compute efficiency as realized/maximum payoffs.",0.7,N/R,No aggregate payoffs per group or per generation are tabulated.,0.7,N/A,No human participants; simulated agents only.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,"Agent-based simulations in NetLogo, not run with human participants in any setting.",1,"Two-player Prisoner’s Dilemma with payoff matrix: (C,C) → (6,6), (D,C) → (10,0), (C,D) → (0,10), (D,D) → (0,0); strength-based decision rule where agents defect when they believe they are stronger; four aggression strategies (pacifists, avengers, bullies, harassers) determine post-game fights that impose punishment=9 on losers, pun_cost ∈ {3,6,9} on winners, and submit_cost=6 on non-fighting victims; deterrence biases perceived opponent strength if they are listed as tough in fear_list; gossip parameter controls how many others receive information about winners; population evolves for 2000 generations via replicator dynamics based on fitness accumulated from PD payoffs and fights.","Compiled from Model 1 description, Appendix A algorithm, Table 1, Table 2, and Appendix B.",1
10.18564_jasss.3336.md,Model 2 – Moralized aggression with ranger Always Cooperate rule (all parameter combinations),Model 2 adds third-party intervention strategies; here considering the subset where the ranger decision rule is Always Cooperate (AC in Table 7) across all population sizes and deterrence values as one condition family.,0.86,"Ranger decision rule = Always Cooperate; population size ∈ {50, 100, 500}; deterrence ∈ {1, 3, 6}; pun_cost=6; gossip=20; coal_sensitivity=0.3.","Table 4 defines Rule ∈ {alwaysC, DefectD, DefectD&N}; Appendix C notes pun_cost and gossip fixed at 6 and 20 in Model 2; coal_sensitivity=0.3.",0.97,False,Purely computational agent-based model with simulated agents.,1,False,Simulation study rather than an empirical experiment.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting.,1,True,Explicitly described as agent-based Model 2 using NetLogo.,1,False,No standalone analytical solution beyond simulation code.,0.9,2,"The underlying interaction is still a two-player PD game between a pair of agents, now with third-party observers who may join coalitions.",1,200,Model 2 reuses the interaction loop structure from Model 1 (200 interaction rounds per generation).,0.95,1,Same binary cooperate/defect PD structure as Model 1.,0.95,N/A,Not a continuous public goods game; no contribution endowment distribution.,1,N/R,No public good multiplier structure; pairwise PD with fixed payoffs.,1,N/A,No human communication or chat; only programmatic coalition decisions.,1,N/A,Simulation without participant interface.,1,N/A,"Identity tracking happens via internal fear_lists, not via a display to human subjects.",1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,No statement that agents know the total or remaining number of rounds.,0.7,1,Coalitional and individual fights cause punishment losses for losers and submission costs for those who retreat.,1,9,"Model 2 inherits ""punishment = 9"" value from Model 1 (Table 2) for the magnitude of damage to losers, not redefined in Table 4.",0.9,1,Punishment parameter directly specifies loss per punished agent in both individual and coalitional fights.,0.7,0,Only punishment/fights; no positive reward operations.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"As in Model 1, PD payoffs are specified but no initial per-round endowment is described.",0.6,0.06045,"For AC rule with n=100, deterrence=1 (Table 7), CC rate at end is 0.03% and DD 93.925%; for AC the structure generally yields near-100% DD, so approximate cooperation rate is near zero. Using 6.045% CDs + 0.03% CC implies very low cooperation; taking CC fraction 0.0003 as contribution rate; rounded here as 0.06045 based on 6.045% CDs where one player cooperates, normalized approx per-player cooperation frequency. This is an approximation as per available outcome frequencies.",0.4,N/R,No numeric contribution tokens are tracked.,1,N/R,"Only outcome frequencies (DD, CD, CC) are reported; no explicit efficiency measure.",0.7,N/R,Aggregate payoffs are not tabulated.,0.7,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,N/A,Simulation with no human participants.,1,No human,Agent-based computational model implemented in software.,1,"Extends Model 1 by allowing some observers to become morally sensitive agents who can join fights as third parties. Two new strategies: Rangers (support cooperators) and Thugs (support defectors). Under the Always Cooperate rule, rangers always cooperate in the PD regardless of history. Observed PD profiles (C,D) or (D,D) can trigger coalitional fights where rangers side with cooperators and thugs with defectors per rules in Appendix C. Group fight outcome determined by sigmoid of difference in summed strengths with coal_sensitivity=0.3. Cost to losers = punishment (9); cost to each winner = pun_cost divided by squared coalition size, with pun_cost fixed at 6; submission cost if one side retreats is submit_cost=6 inherited from Model 1. As rangers always cooperate, simulations show thugs invade and DD rates approach 100%, leading to widespread costly private or group fights (Table 7).","Summarizes Model 2 description, Appendix C algorithm, Table 4, and AC rows in Table 7.",1
10.18564_jasss.3336.md,Model 2 – Moralized aggression with ranger Defect Defectors rule (all parameter combinations),"Same structural model as Model 2, but with rangers’ decision rule set to Defect Defectors (DD in Table 7) across population sizes and deterrence values.",0.9,"Ranger decision rule = Defect Defectors; population size ∈ {50, 100, 500}; deterrence ∈ {1, 3, 6}; pun_cost=6; gossip=20; coal_sensitivity=0.3.",Rule value specified in Table 4 (DefectD) and rows labeled DD in Table 7; other Model 2 parameters as in Appendix C.,0.98,False,Purely computational.,1,False,"Agent-based simulations, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No human lab context.,1,True,Described throughout as agent-based simulations (Model 2).,1,False,No closed-form analyses as distinct experimental conditions.,0.9,2,Underlying interaction remains a two-player PD pair per round.,1,200,Same generational structure reused from Model 1.,0.95,1,Binary cooperate/defect PD decisions.,0.95,N/A,No divisible contribution amounts.,1,N/R,No MPCR is defined; PD game.,1,N/A,No human communication.,1,N/A,No participant interface.,1,N/A,"Identity is internal to simulation (fear_lists), not an interface property.",1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,N/R,No mention that agents know the horizon.,0.7,1,Coalitional and private fights impose punishment on losers and submission costs on avoiders.,1,9,Same punishment magnitude from Model 1 (9) used; not redefined for Model 2.,0.9,1,Punishment parameter directly determines loss per punished agent.,0.7,0,No explicit rewarding.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,"Endowments are not specified, only PD payoffs and fight/submission costs.",0.6,0.86245,"From Figure 8 description: for a representative successful run with n=100, Defect Defectors rule and moderate deterrence, CC rate during last ten interactions is 86.25%.",0.95,N/R,No numeric contributions; only binary actions.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not computed explicitly.,0.7,N/R,Group-level payoff aggregates are not reported.,0.7,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,N/A,No human sample.,1,No human,Agent-based simulations in software.,1,"Same structure as Model 2 baseline with third-party interventions. Under the Defect Defectors rule, rangers cooperate by default but defect against agents recorded in defector_list (those observed defecting in prior PD games). Thugs estimate their prevalence (ratio of rangers to thugs) from observed coalitional fights, and defect in the PD as long as they believe they are in the majority (ratio < 1). When PD outcomes involve asymmetry (C,D) or, in some ranger-involved DD cases, morally sensitive observers (rangers and thugs) choose sides: rangers normally support cooperators, thugs support defectors. Coalitions’ willingness to fight is determined probabilistically from coalitional strength differences via sigmoid with coal_sensitivity=0.3. The cost per winner in a victorious coalition is pun_cost / (#members^2) with pun_cost=6; losers pay punishment=9; neutral or retreating agents pay submit_cost=6 as in Model 1. Rangers additionally maintain defector_list and neutral_list to guide future Defect Defectors behavior. Results: for large populations (n=500) thugs reliably invade and DD ≈ 97–98% (Figure 7, Table 7). For small and medium populations (n=50,100) and various deterrence values, either rangers or thugs can invade depending on stochastic early dynamics; when rangers prevail they establish high CC rates (e.g., 86.25% in Figure 8).","Synthesizes Model 2 description, Appendix C (ranger/thug decision rules and coalition logic), Table 4 parameter choices, and DD rows in Table 7 plus Figures 7–9.",1
10.18564_jasss.3336.md,Model 2 – Moralized aggression with ranger Defect Defectors and Neutrals rule (all parameter combinations),Same Model 2 framework but with rangers’ decision rule set to Defect Defectors and Neutrals (DD&N in Table 7) across population sizes and deterrence values.,0.9,"Ranger decision rule = Defect Defectors and Neutrals; population size ∈ {50, 100, 500}; deterrence ∈ {1, 3, 6}; pun_cost=6; gossip=20; coal_sensitivity=0.3.",Rule case DefectD&N defined in Table 4 and implemented as DD&N rows in Table 7; Model 2 parameterization otherwise identical.,0.98,False,No human participants; purely simulated agents.,1,False,Simulation rather than experiment.,1,False,No physical lab or field context.,1,True,Described as agent-based simulations.,1,False,"Analytical expressions are supporting details, not a separate analytical condition.",0.9,2,Core game remains a two-player PD between paired agents.,1,200,Same generational interaction loop as Model 1.,0.95,1,Cooperate vs defect only.,0.95,N/A,No continuous contribution choices.,1,N/R,"PD game, not a linear public good.",1,N/A,No human chat.,1,N/A,No subject-facing display.,1,N/A,Agents keep internal lists; no UI.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,No indication agents know the game horizon.,0.7,1,Group and private fights impose punishment and submission costs.,1,9,Same punishment magnitude (9) from Model 1.,0.9,1,Punishment parameter applied directly per punished agent.,0.7,0,No reward construct.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,"As elsewhere, no endowment per se is specified; only payoffs and costs.",0.6,0.89945,"For a highly cooperative DD&N case (n=100, deterrence=1; Table 7), CC = 89.945%; used here as the representative contribution rate.",0.9,N/R,"Decisions are binary, not numeric contributions.",1,N/R,Efficiency is not reported.,0.7,N/R,No group payoff aggregates reported.,0.7,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,N/A,Simulated agents.,1,No human,Purely computational environment.,1,"Same Model 2 structure with third-party coalitional fights, but rangers now defect not only against known defectors but also against agents recorded as neutrals in previous conflicts (those who remained neutral when a ranger or thug was present). Algorithm: rangers track defector_list and neutral_list; with Defect Defectors and Neutrals rule, they defect in PD if opponent ∈ defector_list OR opponent ∈ neutral_list, otherwise cooperate. Thug behavior, coalition formation, strength-based fight decision, and cost structure (punishment=9; pun_cost=6; submit_cost=6; winner cost sharing as pun_cost / #members^2; coal_sensitivity=0.3) mirror other Model 2 conditions. DD&N rule increases pressure on bystanders to align with the moral alliance lest they be targeted as neutrals. Results (Table 7 and Table 9) show that for large groups (n=500) thugs dominate (DD ≈ 97–98%), while for smaller groups (n=50,100) rangers can sometimes invade and maintain high CC levels (e.g., CC ≈ 89.945%), but invasion success is stochastic (around 63–65% of runs for favorable parameter combinations).","Compiled from Model 2 description, Appendix C (DD&N rule logic), Table 4, DD&N rows in Table 7, and invasion statistics in Table 9.",1
10.1007_s11403-017-0195-2.md,Analytical Q-learning model with punishment and risk-taking preferences (baseline two-action game),"Paper presents an analytical Q-learning model for a two-player, two-action cooperation/defection game with parameters alpha, beta, phi, T, r1, r2; this is the core condition studied in Section 2–3.",0.9,"Penalty for defection (phi), share from free-riding (beta), exploration rate/temperature (T), rewards for cooperation vs defection (r1, r2)","Model explicitly varies or analyses outcomes as functions of phi, beta, T, and reward parameters r1, r2.",0.9,False,No human participants; purely theoretical/mathematical treatment.,1,False,No controlled experiment is conducted; only theoretical analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting; fully theoretical.,1,False,"Despite referring to learning dynamics, the paper does not report numerical or agent-based simulations; only analytical derivations and phase diagrams.",0.8,True,"Derives closed-form dynamics, rest-point equations, bifurcation curves, and propositions analytically.",1,2,The model explicitly considers two agents/players throughout (two-agent scenario).,1,0,"Infinite-horizon or continuous-time learning dynamics; no fixed finite number of rounds is specified, so coded as 0.",0.7,1,"Only two discrete actions (cooperate or defect); contribution is binary rather than a continuous amount, thus not an all-or-nothing contribution level within a continuous range; by definition here, players choose an action, not an amount, so set to 1 (can 'contribute' or not).",0.5,N/A,No explicit endowment or default allocation between private/public accounts is modelled; purely action-based payoffs.,1,N/R,"No marginal per capita return for a standard public goods function is defined; only abstract payoffs using alpha, r1, r2, beta, phi.",1,0,No communication or signaling between agents is modelled.,1,0,Agents observe rewards but there is no explicit mechanism of summarized feedback like earnings tables; abstract reinforcement only.,0.7,N/A,No identities or anonymity notion; just two symmetric agents in a theoretical model.,1,N/A,No concept of identities vis-à-vis rewards.,1,0,Infinite/continuous learning; no user interface indicating remaining rounds.,1,1,"A penalty for not cooperating, phi, directly enters payoff and Q-updating; core of the model.",1,N/R,Penalty phi is exogenous; no explicit cost paid by punishers is separately specified.,0.6,N/R,Mapping from punishment effort to effective penalty is not parameterized as a separate technology; phi directly is the penalty.,0.7,0,Model includes benefits from cooperation and from free-riding but no explicit reward mechanism where agents pay to reward others.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/R,No initial endowment or token budget is defined; only abstract payoffs.,1,N/R,"Outcome variables are probabilities of choosing cooperation (x,y) and stability conditions; no normalized contribution rate relative to an endowment is reported in public-good terms.",0.7,N/R,Binary action model; no contribution amount in tokens.,1,N/R,No explicit calculation of total payoffs relative to a theoretical efficiency frontier is given.,0.8,N/R,Payoff matrix is given symbolically; no numeric group payoff summary is emphasized as a result variable.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical/theoretical study of agent learning dynamics.,1,"Two-player, two-action repeated social dilemma with cooperation (C) and defection (D); payoffs depend on learning rate alpha, cooperative and defective rewards r1 and r2, penalty for defection phi, and free-riding benefit share beta. Action selection follows Boltzmann Q-learning with exploration temperature T controlling risk-taking preferences.",Summarized from Section 2–3 including payoff matrix and Boltzmann action rule.,1
10.1007_s11403-017-0195-2.md,Q-learning model with punishment and high exploration (large T) – cooperative equilibrium analysis (Proposition 3.1),"Proposition 3.1 focuses on the case of very broad propensity for exploration (large T) and examines existence of symmetric cooperative equilibrium for certain phi and beta, effectively a specific parameter regime of the baseline model.",0.86,"High exploration rate (large T), positive penalty (phi) and/or small free-riding share (beta), with r1 ≥ 2r2",Proposition 3.1 explicitly studies the case 'If the propensity for the exploration T is very broad' with conditions on phi and beta.,0.9,False,Still the same analytical Q-learning model; no participants.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment; parameter-regime analysis.,1,False,No lab context.,1,False,"Results obtained via equations and bifurcation analysis, not by computer simulations.",0.9,True,"Derives conditions on T, phi, beta for equilibrium analytically (e.g., rest point equation a x + b = log(x/(1-x))).",1,2,Same two-agent model.,1,0,"Continuous-time learning, not finite rounds.",0.7,1,Binary cooperation/defection choice; see baseline explanation.,0.5,N/A,No explicit endowment structure.,1,N/R,No public goods MPCR.,1,0,No communication channel is introduced in this parameter regime.,1,0,No additional feedback structure beyond payoff-based learning.,0.7,N/A,No identities in the analytical model.,1,N/A,No identities or explicit rewarding agent.,1,0,No round display; continuous dynamics.,1,1,Penalty phi is crucial in Proposition 3.1 (large phi helps achieve cooperative equilibrium).,1,N/R,Phi is not decomposed into cost and effect; treated as given.,0.6,N/R,No separate technology parameter; phi directly modifies payoffs.,0.7,0,No separate rewarding institution.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Not specified.,1,N/R,"They characterize equilibrium in terms of x = y in (1/2,1) but do not frame it as a normalized contribution rate to an endowment.",0.6,N/R,"Binary strategy probabilities, no amounts.",1,N/R,"Stability of cooperative equilibrium is the focus, not efficiency ratios.",0.8,N/R,Group payoff levels at equilibrium are not explicitly quantified.,0.7,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Analytical parameter-regime analysis only.,1,"Parameter regime where exploration temperature T is large and penalty phi is sufficiently high (or beta small) yields a unique symmetric cooperative rest point x = y in (1/2,1) that is globally stable. Bifurcation analysis shows how rest points change as T varies.",Summarizes Proposition 3.1 and subsequent stability discussion.,1
10.1007_s11403-017-0195-2.md,"Q-learning model with punishment, memory of partner’s last action, and intrinsic motivation (Section 4, Proposition 4.1)",Section 4 introduces a modified Q-learning model with an indicator I(t) capturing the partner’s last action and discusses intrinsic motivation and forgiveness; Proposition 4.1 analyzes cooperation under this modified setup.,0.92,"Presence of memory of the other agent’s last action (I(t)), penalty for defection (phi), free-riding share (beta), learning rate alpha, utility from cooperation (intrinsic motivation via r1, r2)","New model equations introduce I(t), and Proposition 4.1 discusses conditions under varying alpha, beta, phi, and relative utilities Q1, Q2 (linked to r1, r2).",0.9,False,Still theoretical; no experimental subjects.,1,False,"No empirical manipulation, only model extension.",1,False,Not a lab study.,1,False,Derivations and qualitative analysis; no simulation results presented.,0.9,True,"Provides closed-form solutions for Q1(t), Q2(t) and conditions for stationary states.",1,2,"Same two-agent framework, now with I(t) indicating the other’s previous action.",1,0,Continuous-time / infinite-horizon dynamics.,0.7,1,Agents still choose between cooperate and defect; no graded contribution.,0.6,N/A,No explicit endowment split.,1,N/R,No classic public good MPCR defined.,1,0,Model includes memory of past actions but no communication.,1,0,Agents observe last action indirectly via I(t) but there is no explicit summary screen or statistics.,0.7,N/A,No identity dimension; only two symmetric agents.,1,N/A,No explicit rewarding interactions with identity.,1,0,"Infinite/continuous learning, no round display.",1,1,Penalty term phi is still present through (-beta + phi + 1) r2 in Q1 and Q2 dynamics.,1,N/R,No separate cost parameter for imposing punishment is specified.,0.6,N/R,Penalty enters directly in payoff structure; no separate technology multiplier.,0.7,0,Still no active rewarding institution for cooperators.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/R,Endowment not defined.,1,N/R,"They discuss probabilities of cooperation (x1^s) qualitatively, but do not frame it as a normalized contribution rate tied to an endowment.",0.6,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,"Focus is on possibility and stability of cooperation under intrinsic motivation, not efficiency metrics.",0.8,N/R,Group payoff not summarized as a dependent outcome in this section.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,The memory-augmented Q-learning model is purely analytical.,1,"Same two-player, two-action cooperation/defection game, but the agent’s Q-updates depend on an indicator I(t) representing whether the other agent cooperated in the previous period. If the partner last cooperated (I(t)=1), dynamics mirror the baseline model; if not (I(t)=0), the cooperative Q-value incorporates payoff from punishment of the free-rider. Proposition 4.1 shows that, with sufficiently high utility from cooperation (intrinsic motivation), cooperation can arise even after past defection, but high free-riding benefits (large beta) undermine this.",Summarizes the extended model equations and the qualitative result of Proposition 4.1.,1
