10.1080_17419166.2020.1756264.md,Conceptual model of small-group peer punishment vs. large-group institutional punishment,"Paper presents a conceptual (theoretical) model contrasting small-group peer punishment with large-group institutional punishment using illustrative figures (Figures 1, 3, 4, 5), not an empirical or simulated experiment.",0.95,"Type of punishment regime (peer punishment in small groups vs. institutional punishment in large groups); punishment status (presence, disappearance, reappearance); reputation, trust, transparency as modifiers","Model explicitly varies punishment type and timing (disappearance/reappearance) and discusses modifiers like trust, transparency, and reputation affecting contribution/defection delays.",0.95,False,No human-subject data or experiments are conducted; the paper uses real-world historical episodes only illustratively and does not collect or analyze participant-level data.,1,False,No controlled manipulation with random assignment or controlled conditions; the work is conceptual and uses illustrative case discussions.,1,False,No laboratory setting is used.,1,False,"No computer or agent-based simulations are reported; figures are qualitative sketches, not outputs of a simulation.",1,True,Core contribution is a conceptual/analytical model of contribution and defection delays as functions of punishment regime characteristics; no numerical solution but clearly theoretical.,0.95,0,No explicit game with defined players is instantiated; small vs. large groups are discussed only conceptually.,1,0,No repeated-game implementation with explicit round counts; time is continuous and illustrative.,1,1,"When the public-goods framework is mentioned, contributions are generic, not restricted to binary choices; defaulting to continuous contribution possibility.",0.6,N/A,No human-subject or explicit game implementation; default endowment allocation is not defined.,1,N/R,No numerical payoff or multiplier structure is specified; only quali