10.1111_ssqu.12910.md,Democracy condition – high-ranking participants,Condition defined by democracy vs. nondemocracy and by role (high vs. low rank). This row captures democratic groups' high-ranking members.,1,Institutional selection rule: Democracy (leaders selected by election every 3 rounds); Role: High rank (high-ranking position holder in a given round).,Primary manipulation is democracy vs. nondemocracy; analyses also distinguish high vs. low rank as an independent variable affecting behavior.,1,True,176 undergraduate volunteers participated in a behavioral experiment.,1,True,Participants were randomly assigned to democracy vs. nondemocracy and interacted under controlled payoff rules.,1,True,Conducted in a laboratory at a large public university with participants at computer terminals.,1,False,All decisions made by human participants; no agent-based or numerical simulation.,1,False,No closed-form or purely theoretical model tested without data.,1,4,"Each group consisted of four participants (one high rank, three low rank).",1,21,Participants interacted for 21 rounds of the public goods game.,1,1,Participants could contribute any amount from 0 up to their full endowment in whole-dollar increments.,1,0,Endowment is initially fully in the private account; nothing is pre-allocated to the group fund.,1,0.4,"Total contributions multiplied by 1.6 and divided among 4 members, so each receives 1.6/4 = 0.4 per 1 contributed.",1,0,Participants interacted only via decision screens; no mention of any communication channel.,1,1,"After contributions, all participants saw each group member’s contributions each round; at elections, they saw each member’s contributions over 3 rounds.",1,1,Participants saw individual contribution amounts and high-ranking player chose whom to punish; implies identities (labels) of punished players were known.,0.8,0,"No reward mechanism existed, only costly punishment.",1,1,Design used fixed 21 rounds with elections every 3 rounds; participants had practice rounds and instructions typical for fixed-horizon PG; likely informed of total rounds.,0.7,1,High-ranking member could spend L$1 to reduce another’s earnings by L$3.,1,1,Punisher paid L$1 per punishment unit.,1,3,Each L$1 spent on punishment reduced the target’s earnings by L$3.,1,0,No mechanism for costly positive transfers; only costly punishment.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,High-ranking participants received L$100 per round.,1,0.368,Base constant in contribution model is 44.439% for low-rank nondemocracy. High rank coefficient −7.614 and democracy +10.503; democracy×rank ~0. Thus predicted mean for high-rank democracy ≈44.439 − 7.614 + 10.503 ≈ 47.3%. However Figure 3 shows lower contributions for high-rank democracy than low-rank democracy and somewhat below ~0.5; reading from Figure 3(b) high-rank democracy is around 36–38% of endowment. Use 0.368 as approximate normalized rate.,0.3,N/R,Paper reports only percentages of endowment and not raw L$ by condition/role.,0,0.78,Group-level efficiency for democracy averaged roughly 0.78 (approximate from Table 3 coefficient 0.663 baseline and +0.117 democracy; baseline at nondemocracy about 0.663 so democracy about 0.780). Assign 0.78 for a typical democratic group; this row represents high-rank members within these groups but efficiency is group-level.,0.6,N/R,"Total earnings in L$ or USD by condition/role not given, only efficiency ratios and failure rates.",0,United States,Participants were undergraduates at a large public university in the Midwestern United States.,1,N/R,Age not reported.,0,"Predominantly female; 109 women, 51 men, 16 unknown across all conditions",Overall gender composition provided but not broken down by condition.,0.8,Undergraduate students,Participants described as undergraduate volunteers.,1,On site,Participants came to the lab and used networked computers in private cubicles.,1,"Public goods game with fluctuating external threat (0–100%) that could cause loss of current and previous round earnings; true threat reduced by 0.5 percentage points per L$1 contributed to public good. Participants could spend up to L$10 per round to increase or decrease announced (but not actual) threat by 5 percentage points per L$1. One high-ranking and three low-ranking players; high rank randomly assigned initially and then updated every 3 rounds based on elections. Elections: at end of every third round, all 4 members vote for high-ranking member, seeing each member’s contributions in prior three rounds; ties broken randomly.",Summary of key structural features specific to democracy condition and general game that are not captured by other fields.,1
10.1111_ssqu.12910.md,Democracy condition – low-ranking participants,Same institutional condition (democracy) but focusing on low-ranking group members’ behavior.,1,Institutional selection rule: Democracy (leaders selected by election every 3 rounds); Role: Low rank (non–high-ranking member in a given round).,Behavior analyzed separately by rank status as an independent variable; this captures low-rank in democratic groups.,1,True,Human subjects experiment with undergraduates.,1,True,Random assignment to democracy vs. nondemocracy with controlled incentives.,1,True,Conducted in a physical lab with networked computers.,1,False,No computer-only agents.,1,False,Empirical data rather than pure theory.,1,4,Four-person groups throughout.,1,21,21 rounds specified.,1,1,Contributions could vary from 0 to full endowment.,1,0,Endowment initially private.,1,0.4,1.6 multiplier / 4 players.,1,0,No communication channel described.,1,1,"All members saw others’ contributions and, in democracy, 3-round contribution histories at election times.",1,1,"Low-ranking participants saw individual contributions and observed being punished or not, implying identity of targets.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism.,1,1,Fixed 21-round design likely communicated; not explicitly stated but typical and elections scheduled every 3 rounds.,0.7,1,"Only high-ranking player could impose costly punishment, but this affects low-ranking participants’ environment.",1,1,High rank pays L$1 to reduce target earnings by L$3.,1,3,Each L$1 in punishment reduced target’s payoff by L$3.,1,0,No rewards available.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,50,Low-ranking individuals received L$50 each round.,1,0.505,"From Table 2, Model 1: baseline (low-rank nondemocracy) about 44.4%. Democracy coefficient +10.503 for both ranks and rank×democracy ~0. So predicted mean for low-rank democracy ≈ 44.4 + 10.5 ≈ 54.9%. Figure 3(b) suggests slightly above 0.5; use 0.505 as approximate proportion.",0.4,N/R,"Only percentages reported, not average L$ contributions.",0,0.78,Group-level efficiency for democratic groups approximated at 0.78 (see first row explanation based on Table 3). Same for all roles within democratic groups.,0.6,N/R,Group earnings in L$ per round or per experiment not broken down by condition.,0,United States,Same sample across all conditions.,1,N/R,Age data not provided.,0,"Predominantly female; 109 women, 51 men, 16 unknown across all conditions",Only aggregate gender counts provided.,0.8,Undergraduate students,Participants were undergraduates.,1,On site,Lab study with participants physically present.,1,Same public goods with fluctuating threat structure as in democratic high-rank condition. Low-ranking participants could not punish but were asked how they would punish (hypothetical judgments). Threat manipulation decisions (increasing or decreasing announced threat by up to 50 percentage points per round) and contribution decisions made each round; elections for high-rank held every three rounds using 3-round contribution histories.,Summarizes structure relevant from low-ranking participants’ perspective in democratic groups.,1
10.1111_ssqu.12910.md,Nondemocracy condition – high-ranking participants,"Condition defined by nondemocratic, resource-based competition for rank and by being in high-ranking role.",1,Institutional selection rule: Nondemocracy (resource-based competition; high-rank retained/assigned based on resources kept over prior 3 rounds); Role: High rank.,"Main treatment is democracy vs. nondemocracy; within that, role (high vs. low rank) is a key independent variable.",1,True,Undergraduate volunteers in a controlled lab game.,1,True,Random assignment to democratic vs. resource-based condition; payoff-relevant treatments.,1,True,Conducted in a university lab using networked PCs.,1,False,"Humans, not simulated agents.",1,False,"Empirical test, not pure derivation.",1,4,Four-person groups are fixed across conditions.,1,21,Identical 21-round interaction.,1,1,Contributions could be any whole-dollar amount from 0 to endowment.,1,0,No automatic contribution; all endowment starts private.,1,0.4,Same public goods technology as democracy condition: 1.6/4.,1,0,No chat or verbal communication.,1,1,High and low rank saw all group members’ contributions each round.,1,1,High-ranking participant chose whom to punish after seeing each member’s contribution; implies identifiable targets.,0.8,0,No rewards or positive transfers.,1,1,"Fixed 21 rounds; likely disclosed in instructions, though not literally quoted.",0.7,1,High-ranking member could use costly punishment; same as democracy.,1,1,L$1 cost per unit of punishment.,1,3,Each L$1 spent reduced target’s payoff by L$3.,1,0,Only costly punishment present.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,100,High-ranking individual receives L$100 per round in all conditions.,1,0.368,"From Table 2, Model 1, high-rank main effect −7.614% relative to baseline 44.439%; predicted percent ≈36.8% in nondemocracy for high rank. That matches constant 44.439 − 7.614 ≈ 36.8%.",0.8,N/R,Raw L$ contribution by condition and rank not directly reported.,0,0.663,"From Table 3, constant 0.663 is baseline efficiency for nondemocracy (group-level). Use this for both ranks in nondemocratic groups.",0.8,N/R,"Only efficiency and failure probability, not total L$ earnings per group/condition.",0,United States,Participants drawn from Midwestern U.S. university.,1,N/R,Age not listed.,0,"Predominantly female; 109 women, 51 men, 16 unknown across all conditions",Only overall sample gender distribution provided.,0.8,Undergraduate students,All were undergraduate volunteers.,1,On site,Lab-based experiment.,1,"Same public goods with fluctuating threat and threat manipulation as democratic condition. Rank determined nondemocratically: after each block of 3 rounds, current high rank kept position if total amount they kept from their endowments over 3 rounds exceeded the combined amount kept by the three low-rank members. Otherwise, high rank was replaced by a low-rank individual selected with probability proportional to their kept resources.",Summarizes specific mechanism of resource-based competition for rank.,1
10.1111_ssqu.12910.md,Nondemocracy condition – low-ranking participants,Resource-based (nondemocratic) condition focusing on low-ranking group members.,1,Institutional selection rule: Nondemocracy (resource-based rank competition); Role: Low rank.,To distinguish behavior of low-rank participants in nondemocracy from other role/treatment combinations.,1,True,Same human-subject laboratory experiment.,1,True,Randomly assigned conditions and experimentally controlled payoffs.,1,True,Study conducted on-site in a lab.,1,False,No artificial agents used.,1,False,Based on observed decisions of participants.,1,4,Groups of four throughout.,1,21,21 repeated rounds as described.,1,1,Contributions are continuous (in integer units) not all-or-nothing.,1,0,No automatic public allocation at start of round.,1,0.4,1.6 multiplier divided by 4 players.,1,0,No interaction outside programmed decisions.,1,1,Low-ranking participants saw each group member’s contribution decisions each round.,1,1,"Low-rank members viewed contributions and saw punishment outcomes, implying identifiable punished individuals.",0.8,0,No reward mechanism existed.,1,1,"Fixed 21-round experiment, likely disclosed.",0.7,1,High-ranking player could punish; this shapes low-rank environment.,1,1,Punisher pays L$1 per unit.,1,3,Punishment subtracts L$3 from target per L$1 spent.,1,0,Rewards not part of this design.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,50,Low-ranking members receive L$50 per round.,1,0.444,Model 1 constant 44.439% corresponds to low-rank in nondemocracy baseline; use 0.444 as normalized contribution rate.,0.9,N/R,"No direct L$ values by condition; only overall averages for electoral success example, pooled over democracy condition.",0,0.663,From Table 3: baseline group earnings efficiency ≈0.663 for nondemocracy.,0.8,N/R,Group earnings not decomposed into L$ figures by condition in the text.,0,United States,Same pool of undergraduates at Midwestern U.S. university.,1,N/R,Authors do not report age.,0,"Predominantly female; 109 women, 51 men, 16 unknown across all conditions",Gender distribution not stratified by condition.,0.8,Undergraduate students,All participants were undergraduates.,1,On site,Laboratory experiment.,1,Same fluctuating threat and threat manipulation system as other conditions. High-ranking position allocated through resource-based competition: current high rank maintains position only if their kept resources over 3 rounds exceed combined kept resources of all low-rank members; otherwise replaced by a low-rank member with selection probability proportional to kept resources. Low-ranking members make hypothetical punishment recommendations but cannot actually punish.,Highlights aspects of the game relevant to low-ranking members’ incentives in nondemocratic groups.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Numerical simulation – Situation 1 (x = -0.4, y = -0.4)","Paper defines three situations; under situation 1 they choose parameters yielding x = -0.4, y = -0.4 and simulate the evolution path (Figures 2a, 3a).",1,"Model parameters set: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, γ_i = 0.1, γ_j = 0.1, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.5, β_j = 0.5, W_i = 0.8, W_j = 0.8, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; implied x = -0.4, y = -0.4 (situation 1).",These are the parameter values explicitly listed for situation 1 before Figure 3; x and y are computed by the formulas given in the model.,1,False,Study uses an evolutionary game-theoretic model with numerical simulations; no human participants are involved.,1,False,No controlled assignment of human subjects; only theoretical and numerical analysis.,1,False,No lab or field setting with human participants; only simulations.,1,True,"Authors explicitly describe using numerical simulation on the evolutionary game model (e.g., Section IV).",1,True,"Paper derives replicator dynamics, equilibrium points, and Jacobian analytically in Section III.",1,2,Model is explicitly between two SDN enterprises i and j.,1,0,"Continuous-time replicator dynamics, not discrete repeated rounds; no fixed number of rounds is defined.",0.7,1,Players choose between two pure strategies: cooperation or selfishness; no continuous contribution level is modeled.,1,N/A,No human subjects or explicit endowment contribution structure; model is not a standard public goods game.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier or marginal per capita return is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,N/A,Simulation of payoff dynamics only; no user interface or feedback to human players.,1,N/A,"Government penalty is a model parameter, not an identity-based punishment between individuals.",1,N/A,Government reward is a model parameter; no identities of punishers/rewarders.,1,N/A,No user interface or discrete round count in this numerical simulation.,1,1,"Government penalty D_i, D_j is explicitly modeled and non-zero in this setting.",1,N/R,Model specifies magnitude of penalty D but not any cost per unit penalty to a punisher; government is not modeled as a strategic player bearing costs.,1,N/R,Paper does not decompose penalty into cost and effect multipliers; D_i and D_j are lump-sum penalties.,1,1,"Government reward W_i, W_j is explicitly included and positive in this situation.",1,N/R,No cost per unit reward to any decision-maker is defined; government budget is not modeled.,1,N/R,"Reward appears directly in payoffs as W_i, W_j without a separate technology parameter.",1,N/R,"There is no fixed token endowment per round; parameters are knowledge resources Λ_i, Λ_j and revenues Π_i, Π_j.",1,N/R,"Outcome measure is evolution of cooperation probability x,y over time, not normalized contributions.",1,N/R,"Model uses binary cooperation/selfishness decisions, not contribution amounts.",1,N/R,No explicit ratio of realized payoffs to maximum possible group payoff is reported.,1,N/R,"Paper presents qualitative evolution outcomes, not specific numerical group payoff totals for this parameter set.",0.7,N/A,No human participants are involved; only simulated enterprises.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Pure numerical simulation based on analytical model; no experimental environment.,1,"Two-player evolutionary game between SDN enterprises with binary strategies (cooperation vs selfishness), continuous-time replicator dynamics, parameters include knowledge investment (Λ_i, Λ_j), profit coefficients (α_i, α_j), risk coefficients (λ_i, λ_j), cost coefficients (γ_i, γ_j), knowledge spillover (β_i, β_j), government reward (W_i, W_j) and penalty (D_i, D_j). Situation 1 parameterization implies that cooperation becomes the unique stable equilibrium (M(1,1)).",Summary of the model structure and the qualitative outcome for this specific parameter regime as described around Figures 2a and 3a and in the stability analysis.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Numerical simulation – Situation 2a (x,y between 0 and 1; x = 0.2727, y = 0.2727)","Within situation 2, authors provide a case with x < 1/2, y < 1/2 using β_i = β_j = 0.2, W_i = W_j = 0.3, leading to x = 0.2727, y = 0.2727 (Figures 2b, 3b).",1,"Model parameters set: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, γ_i = 0.1, γ_j = 0.1, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.2, β_j = 0.2, W_i = 0.3, W_j = 0.3, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; implied x ≈ 0.2727, y ≈ 0.2727 (situation 2, low x,y).","Explicit parameter values for this subcase are listed prior to Figure 3; x,y are reported as 0.2727.",1,False,"Simulation on a theoretical model, no human participants.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,"Trajectory of x,y over time is obtained via numerical simulation for this parameter set.",1,True,Same analytical model underlies all situations.,1,2,Two enterprises i and j.,1,0,"Continuous evolutionary dynamics, no discrete finite rounds.",0.7,1,Only two strategies: cooperate or be selfish.,1,N/A,Not a contribution game with endowments.,1,N/R,No public goods multiplier specified.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No user interface or feedback to humans.,1,N/A,"Government penalty in model, not identity-based peer punishment.",1,N/A,"Rewards modeled as parameters, no identities.",1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,"Penalty parameters D_i, D_j set to 0.1.",1,N/R,No cost structure for imposing penalties is modeled.,1,N/R,Penalty D directly subtracts from the selfish player's payoff.,1,1,Government rewards W_i = W_j = 0.3 are included.,1,N/R,No explicit cost of rewards to a decision-maker.,1,N/R,"Rewards appear additively as W_i, W_j.",1,N/R,Model uses revenues Π and knowledge resources Λ instead of standard endowments.,1,N/R,Main DV is whether strategy converges to cooperation or selfishness; no fractional contributions.,1,N/R,No continuous contribution decision.,1,N/R,They do not compute efficiency ratios for this case.,1,N/R,Only qualitative evolution path is shown; no explicit group payoff number is given for this parameter combination.,0.7,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Numerical simulation only.,1,"Same two-player cooperation vs selfishness evolutionary game. For situation 2 with parameters β_i = β_j = 0.2 and W_i = W_j = 0.3, the resulting x and y lie in (0,1), with x,y ≈ 0.2727. The system has two stable equilibria: S(0,0) (selfishness) and M(1,1) (cooperation). For this initial condition (x<1/2, y<1/2), trajectories converge to S(0,0) (cooperation) according to Figures 2b and 3b.","Description from stability analysis of situation 2 and the specific low-x,y case.",1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Numerical simulation – Situation 2b (x = 0.5, y = 0.5)","Within situation 2, they set β_i = β_j = 0.45, W_i = W_j = 0.3, with other parameters as before, yielding x = 0.5, y = 0.5 (Figures 2c, 3c).",1,"Model parameters set: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, γ_i = 0.1, γ_j = 0.1, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.45, β_j = 0.45, W_i = 0.3, W_j = 0.3, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; implied x = 0.5, y = 0.5 (situation 2, boundary case).","Parameter values and resulting x,y are explicitly stated in the text discussing situation 2.",1,False,Simulation only.,1,False,No empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab setting.,1,True,They simulate the dynamics for this parameter set and plot paths.,1,True,Uses same analytical framework.,1,2,Model is between two enterprises.,1,0,Continuous replicator dynamics.,0.7,1,Binary strategies.,1,N/A,No contribution endowment framework.,1,N/R,Not a public goods game with MPCR.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No experimental interface.,1,N/A,No identity-based punishment mechanism between players.,1,N/A,No identity-based rewarding.,1,N/A,"Continuous time, no rounds.",1,1,"Penalty parameters D_i, D_j are 0.1.",1,N/R,Penalty cost not modeled.,1,N/R,Penalty enters payoff directly.,1,1,"Reward parameters W_i, W_j = 0.3.",1,N/R,No cost specified.,1,N/R,Reward acts additively.,1,N/R,No player endowment in tokens.,1,N/R,"DV is path of cooperation probabilities, not contribution fractions.",1,N/R,No continuous contributions.,1,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,Numerical group payoff not given for this case.,0.7,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,No human,Pure simulation.,1,"Same two-player evolutionary game. In situation 2 with x = y = 0.5, the system has two stable equilibria (cooperation and selfishness) separated by a saddle point P(x,y). The text notes that from this initial condition the final strategy may stabilize at cooperation or selfishness, depending on the evolution path.",Summarizes description linked to Figures 2c and 3c and the stability table for situation 2.,1
