10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Numerical simulation – Situation 3 (x = 1.4, y = 1.4)","Situation 3 is parameterized with higher cooperation cost and low reward, yielding x = 1.4, y = 1.4; they simulate the evolution path in Figures 2e and 3e.",1,"Model parameters set: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, γ_i = 0.2, γ_j = 0.2, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.5, β_j = 0.5, W_i = 0.1, W_j = 0.1, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; implied x = 1.4, y = 1.4 (situation 3).",These specific values are listed in Section IV for situation 3 before Figure 3; they report that x and y equal 1.4.,1,False,"Purely simulated enterprises, no human data.",1,False,No empirical control or randomization.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,They run numerical simulations for this parameter set to illustrate evolution towards selfishness.,1,True,Situation 3’s stability properties are derived analytically in Table 6.,1,2,Two enterprises.,1,0,"Continuous evolutionary dynamic, not discrete rounds.",0.7,1,Binary strategies cooperation/selfishness only.,1,N/A,Not a token contribution setting.,1,N/R,No marginal per capita return specified.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No feedback interface.,1,N/A,No identity-based punishment.,1,N/A,No identity-based rewards.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,"Penalty parameters D_i, D_j present and non-zero.",1,N/R,No cost to punisher modeled.,1,N/R,Penalty modeled as direct payoff reduction.,1,1,"Reward parameters W_i, W_j exist though small.",1,N/R,No reward cost parameter.,1,N/R,Rewards added directly to payoffs.,1,N/R,No fixed endowment specification.,1,N/R,"Outcome is probability of cooperation, not normalized contribution.",1,N/R,No amount decision.,1,N/R,No efficiency ratio reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs for this numeric example are not tabulated.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Entirely simulation-based.,1,"In situation 3, with relatively high cooperation costs (γ_i = γ_j = 0.2) and low rewards (W_i = W_j = 0.1), the stability analysis (Table 6) shows S(0,0) (selfishness) is the only stable equilibrium. Simulations for x = y = 1.4 illustrate that the system’s strategy path moves from M(1,1) (cooperation) to S(0,0) (selfishness), i.e., cooperation collapses under these parameter conditions.",Drawn from text describing situation 3 and associated figures.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Sensitivity analysis – Varying knowledge spillover β (x = 0.35, y = 0.35)","In sensitivity analysis (Section IV.B.1) they hold most parameters fixed, set x = y = 0.35, and vary β_i, β_j from 0.20 to 0.28 in situation 2.",1,"Fixed parameters: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, γ_i = 0.1, γ_j = 0.1, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, W_i = 0.3, W_j = 0.3, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; initial x = 0.35, y = 0.35; treatment: β_i and β_j varied from 0.20 to 0.28.",Explicitly described in the subsection on knowledge spillover coefficient β and Figure 4.,1,False,"This is a parameter sweep in a simulation model, not involving humans.",1,False,No empirical experimental design.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,They simulate the dynamic trajectories for multiple β values.,1,True,Based on the previously derived analytical model.,1,2,Two enterprises.,1,0,"Continuous-time trajectories, no finite round count.",0.7,1,Binary strategies in the underlying game.,1,N/A,No endowment or contributions.,1,N/R,No public goods structure.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No interface layer.,1,N/A,"Government penalties, no identities.",1,N/A,"Government rewards, no identities.",1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,"D_i, D_j = 0.1 > 0.",1,N/R,Cost of applying D not modeled.,1,N/R,Penalty directly changes payoff.,1,1,"Rewards W_i, W_j = 0.3 included.",1,N/R,No cost modeled.,1,N/R,Rewards act additively.,1,N/R,No endowments defined.,1,N/R,"Outcomes described as number of steps to reach cooperation, not contribution rates.",1,N/R,No contribution amounts.,1,N/R,Efficiency measures not reported.,1,N/R,"They mention steps and difficulty of cooperation, not payoff magnitudes for each β.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Numerical sensitivity analysis on a model.,1,"They report that as β_i and β_j increase from 0.20 to 0.28 (keeping other parameters fixed in situation 2), the system still moves to cooperation but the number of evolution steps increases from 14 to 20, indicating that higher knowledge spillover coefficients make cooperation harder to achieve under this reward/cost configuration.",Summary of the narrative around Figure 4.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Sensitivity analysis – Varying government reward W (x = 0.35, y = 0.35)","Section IV.B.2 varies W_i, W_j from 0.30 to 0.38 with other parameters fixed and initial x = y = 0.35 (Figure 5).",1,"Fixed parameters: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, γ_i = 0.1, γ_j = 0.1, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.2, β_j = 0.2, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; initial x = 0.35, y = 0.35; treatment: W_i and W_j varied from 0.30 to 0.38.",Explicit parameter specification in the subsection on government reward.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,False,Not an experimental study.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,They numerically simulate trajectories for several reward levels.,1,True,Within same analytical game framework.,1,2,Two enterprises.,1,0,Continuous dynamic model.,0.7,1,Binary cooperate/selfish strategies.,1,N/A,No budget allocation decision.,1,N/R,No MPCR notion.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,No identity-based punishment.,1,N/A,No identity-based rewarding.,1,N/A,No rounds.,1,1,"D_i, D_j fixed at 0.1.",1,N/R,No cost parameter.,1,N/R,Penalty is modeled directly.,1,1,"Rewards W_i,W_j > 0 in all variants.",1,N/R,No cost to rewarding.,1,N/R,Rewards enter payoffs additively.,1,N/R,No endowment structure.,1,N/R,"Measured outcome is steps to reach cooperation, not contribution rate.",1,N/R,No such variable.,1,N/R,Efficiency is not computed.,1,N/R,"Reported qualitative effect on speed of convergence, not payoff levels.",0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation-only sensitivity analysis.,1,"They find that increasing government rewards from 0.30 to 0.38 reduces the number of steps to reach cooperation from 15 to 10, indicating that higher rewards accelerate convergence to cooperative R&D between SDN enterprises.",Taken from the discussion accompanying Figure 5.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Sensitivity analysis – Varying government penalty D (x = 0.35, y = 0.35)","Section IV.B.3 varies D_i, D_j from 0.10 to 0.18 while keeping other parameters fixed and x = y = 0.35 (Figure 6).",1,"Fixed parameters: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, γ_i = 0.1, γ_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.2, β_j = 0.2, W_i = 0.3, W_j = 0.3, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; initial x = 0.35, y = 0.35; treatment: D_i and D_j varied from 0.10 to 0.18.",Parameter set is described explicitly in the subsection on government penalty.,1,False,Simulation-based.,1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No physical lab.,1,True,They simulate the dynamics for different penalty levels.,1,True,Uses the same analytical game model.,1,2,Two enterprises in the model.,1,0,"Continuous dynamics, no rounds.",0.7,1,Binary strategies.,1,N/A,No contributions/endowments.,1,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,"Penalties are modeled from government, not from identified peers.",1,N/A,Rewards not linked to identities.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,D_i and D_j > 0 by design of this analysis.,1,N/R,No explicit cost of punishing.,1,N/R,Penalty D directly reduces payoff.,1,1,"W_i,W_j = 0.3 remain positive.",1,N/R,Reward cost not modeled.,1,N/R,Reward directly added to payoff.,1,N/R,No endowment structure.,1,N/R,"Outcome is speed of convergence to cooperation, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution decision.,1,N/R,Efficiency not computed.,1,N/R,They do not provide payoff values by D level.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation sensitivity analysis.,1,"They report that increasing government penalties from 0.10 to 0.18 causes strategies to move toward cooperation, and higher penalties shorten the number of steps required to reach cooperation, indicating that stronger penalties for not spilling over knowledge promote faster cooperative R&D.",From narrative associated with Figure 6.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Sensitivity analysis – Varying cooperation cost γ (x = 0.7, y = 0.7)","Section IV.B.4 keeps x = y = 0.7 and varies γ_i, γ_j from 0.10 to 0.18 with other parameters fixed (Figure 7).",1,"Fixed parameters: λ_i = 0.4, λ_j = 0.4, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.5, β_j = 0.5, W_i = 0.3, W_j = 0.3, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; initial x = 0.7, y = 0.7; treatment: γ_i and γ_j varied from 0.10 to 0.18.",Explicitly listed in subsection on cooperation cost coefficient and Figure 7 caption.,1,False,All simulated.,1,False,No empirical design.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Dynamic simulations for different γ values.,1,True,Uses analytical framework to interpret results.,1,2,Two enterprises.,1,0,Continuous process.,0.7,1,Binary choices.,1,N/A,No contribution framework.,1,N/R,No public goods setup.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No interface.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,"D_i,D_j = 0.1.",1,N/R,Not modeled.,1,N/R,Penalty D is direct.,1,1,"Rewards W_i,W_j = 0.3.",1,N/R,Not modeled.,1,N/R,Reward W adds to payoff.,1,N/R,No endowment.,1,N/R,"Outcome is transition from cooperation to selfishness and speed, not contributions.",1,N/R,No contribution amount.,1,N/R,Not computed.,1,N/R,No group payoff numbers by γ level reported.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation-based sensitivity analysis.,1,"They find that with x = y = 0.7, increasing cooperation cost coefficients γ_i and γ_j from 0.10 to 0.18 leads the system’s strategy to shift from cooperation to selfishness, and higher γ shortens the steps needed to reach selfishness, implying that higher cooperation costs discourage R&D cooperation.",Summarized from text and Figure 7.,1
10.1109_access.2021.3128647.md,"Sensitivity analysis – Varying cooperation risk λ (x = 0.7, y = 0.7)","Section IV.B.5 varies λ_i, λ_j from 0.60 to 1.00 with x = y = 0.7 and other parameters fixed (Figure 8).",1,"Fixed parameters: γ_i = 0.1, γ_j = 0.1, α_i = 0.5, α_j = 0.5, β_i = 0.5, β_j = 0.5, W_i = 0.3, W_j = 0.3, D_i = 0.1, D_j = 0.1, Λ_i = 2, Λ_j = 2; initial x = 0.7, y = 0.7; treatment: λ_i and λ_j varied from 0.60 to 1.00.",Explicitly stated in cooperation risk coefficient subsection.,1,False,"No human data, only simulations.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab environment.,1,True,Dynamic simulation across risk levels.,1,True,The same analytical model applies.,1,2,Two enterprises.,1,0,Continuous dynamic.,0.7,1,Binary strategy set.,1,N/A,No endowment structure.,1,N/R,Not defined.,1,N/A,No human players.,1,N/A,No user interface.,1,N/A,No identity-based punishment.,1,N/A,No identity-based reward.,1,N/A,No discrete rounds.,1,1,"Penalties D_i,D_j present.",1,N/R,Cost not modeled.,1,N/R,Penalties modeled directly via D.,1,1,"W_i,W_j > 0.",1,N/R,No reward cost.,1,N/R,Rewards added to payoff.,1,N/R,Endowments not defined.,1,N/R,Outcome is convergence to selfishness and speed; no contributions.,1,N/R,No contribution decisions.,1,N/R,No efficiency outcomes presented.,1,N/R,Paper does not provide payoff values as a function of λ.,0.7,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Simulation sensitivity analysis.,1,"Results indicate that when cooperation risk coefficients λ_i and λ_j increase from 0.60 to 1.00 (with x = y = 0.7), the evolution path moves to selfishness, and higher risk shortens the number of steps to reach selfishness. This suggests that higher perceived cooperation risk discourages R&D cooperation and accelerates collapse into selfish behavior.",Based on description and Figure 8 caption.,1
10.1111_jems.12451.md,U – Baseline ultimatum game,"Paper defines four treatments corresponding to U, UR, UP, and UPR games; U is the baseline without reward or punishment.",1,Responder instruments available: none (standard ultimatum game U),"Across treatments the responder’s post-acceptance options vary: none (U), reward (UR), punishment (UP), both (UPR). This row is the condition with no additional instruments.",1,True,Lab experiment with 144 human undergraduate subjects at FSU; U is one of the four treatments.,1,True,Controlled laboratory experiment with random assignment to roles and treatments.,1,True,Sessions conducted at the XS/FS experimental laboratory at Florida State University.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulations reported; only human-subject lab data.,1,False,"Paper contains theoretical discussion but the treatment data are from lab experiment, not pure analytical model.",1,2,Each game is an ultimatum bargaining interaction between one proposer and one responder.,1,10.8,Table 2 reports ‘Average # of rounds’ for U as 10.8; sessions varied between 5 and 13 periods.,0.9,1,Proposer chooses any integer offer X between 0 and 500; not an all-or-nothing contribution choice.,1,0,Not a public goods or contribution game; there is no default allocation between private/public accounts. By convention set to 0 when all payoff starts as privately held endowment.,0.5,N/A,"Bilateral ultimatum bargaining, no public good or linear production function; MPCR not defined.",1,0,"Silence enforced; only structured decisions allowed, no communication reported.",1,0,Instructions and description do not mention feedback about others’ earnings beyond own pair outcome.,0.8,0,Pairs are anonymous and re-matched; punishments are not personalized public signals of identity.,0.9,0,No reward instrument exists in U; by definition IDs for rewards are not shown.,1,1,Instructions say ‘This part of the experiment will consist of several decision-making rounds’ and subjects see round number; implies they know how many rounds or at least current round within a known sequence.,0.7,0,"Standard ultimatum game: responder can only accept or reject, no costly punishment option.",1,N/A,No punishment instrument in this treatment.,1,N/A,No punishment instrument in this treatment.,1,0,U treatment has no post-acceptance reward option.,1,N/A,No reward instrument in this treatment.,1,N/A,No reward instrument in this treatment.,1,500,"Amount for division each round is $5.00, expressed as S=500 cents.",1,0.413,"Here ‘contribution’ is the offer to responder. Average offer in U is 206.7 cents, 41% of the 500-cent pie as reported (41%). Use 206.7/500≈0.413.",1,206.7,Table 2 ‘Average offer’ for U is 206.7 cents.,1,0.83,Section 3.5: ‘Relative to full efficiency…average aggregate payoff is at 83.0% in U’.,1,414.9,Sum of average proposer and responder payoffs from Table 2: 235.7 + 179.2 = 414.9 cents.,1,USA,"Subjects are FSU undergraduates in Tallahassee, Florida, USA.",0.95,N/R,Only described as undergraduate students; no age statistics reported.,0,53.5% female overall; treatment-specific breakdown N/R,Overall sample gender share reported; not by treatment.,0.7,Undergraduate students,Subjects are volunteers from ‘population of undergraduate students at FSU’.,1,On site,Conducted in a physical experimental laboratory at FSU using z-Tree.,1,"One-shot-style ultimatum bargaining repeated with random rematching (‘zipper’ protocol); roles fixed (proposer vs responder). Pie S=500 cents; proposer chooses integer offer X∈[0,500]; responder accepts or rejects; two randomly chosen rounds are paid.","Summarized from Sections 2.1 and 2.3 and Appendix C instructions for the generic structure, with U lacking any Stage-3 reward/punishment.",1
10.1111_jems.12451.md,UR – Ultimatum with reward only,Paper defines UR as ultimatum with reward and lists it as one of four treatments in Table 1 and Section 2.1.,1,"Responder instruments available: costly reward only (UR); reward effectiveness k=1, no punishment","UR game: responder, if she accepts offer x, can reward proposer by transferring t∈[0,x]; parameters used are k=1, l not applicable.",1,True,UR is one of the four human-subject lab treatments.,1,True,Controlled treatment in lab with randomly assigned roles and repeated interactions.,1,True,Conducted at XS/FS experimental laboratory at FSU.,1,False,No simulations reported.,1,False,Analytical model in appendix is separate from empirical treatments.,1,2,Each interaction involves one proposer and one responder.,1,10,Table 1 and 2: UR has two sessions with 10 periods each; Table 2 ‘Average # of rounds’ for UR is 10.0.,1,1,Proposer chooses integer offer X between 0 and 500; continuous choice.,1,0,No public vs private account; bargaining over a fixed pie. Default taken as 0 by convention.,0.5,N/A,No public-good technology; just division of a fixed pie.,1,0,Silence maintained throughout; no mention of chat or communication.,1,0,Feedback limited to own pair outcome; no summaries of others’ outcomes/punishments.,0.8,0,No punishment instrument in UR; identities are anonymous.,1,0,"Although responder can reward, matching is anonymous and rewards are within dyad; no public identity revelation.",0.9,1,Same multi-round structure as U; subjects see round-related info.,0.7,0,UR provides only a reward option; punishment is not available.,1,N/A,No punishment instrument.,1,N/A,No punishment instrument.,1,1,"Section 2.1 defines UR: responder can reward proposer by transferring t∈[0,x].",1,1,They implement k=1: ‘we implement rewards and punishments with cost equal to one’; each cent transferred costs responder 1 cent.,1,1,UR game with k=1: one token spent increases proposer’s payoff by kt=1·t.,1,500,Same S=500 cents to be divided each round.,1,0.368,"Average offer in UR is 183.8 cents, reported as 37% of 500; 183.8/500≈0.368.",1,183.8,Table 2 ‘Average offer’ for UR is 183.8.,1,0.75,Section 3.5: average aggregate payoff in UR is 75.0% of full efficiency.,1,375,Sum of proposer and responder payoffs from Table 2: 222.7 + 152.3 = 375.0.,1,USA,"All sessions conducted at FSU in Florida, USA.",0.95,N/R,Only identified as undergraduates; no age statistics by treatment.,0,53.5% female overall; treatment-specific breakdown N/R,Overall fraction female provided; no per-treatment numbers.,0.7,Undergraduate students,Recruitment from undergraduate subject pool via ORSEE.,1,On site,"Physical lab sessions at XS/FS lab, implemented in z-Tree.",1,"Ultimatum bargaining with post-acceptance costly reward. Pie S=500 cents. Stage 1: proposer chooses integer offer X∈[0,500]. Stage 2: responder accepts or rejects; rejection gives 0 to both. Stage 3 (if accepted): responder may transfer t∈[0,X] to proposer at 1:1 cost, leading to payoffs (500−X+t, X−t). Repeated with zipper rematching; fixed roles; two rounds randomly paid.","Summarized from Section 2.1 (UR definition), Appendix C style instructions, and general design description.",1
10.1111_jems.12451.md,UP – Ultimatum with punishment only,Paper defines UP as ultimatum with punishment and lists it as one of the four treatments.,1,"Responder instruments available: costly punishment only (UP); punishment effectiveness l=1, no reward","UP game: responder, if she accepts offer x, can punish by reducing both players’ payoffs by r∈[0, min{x, 500−x}]; parameters used are l=1, k not applicable.",1,True,UP is a human-subject laboratory treatment.,1,True,Controlled treatment in a lab experiment.,1,True,Sessions held at FSU experimental laboratory.,1,False,No simulation study is reported for UP.,1,False,Analytical results for generic UP game exist in Appendix A but are not the empirical condition.,1,2,Two players per interaction: proposer and responder.,1,8.8,Table 2 ‘Average # of rounds’ for UP is 8.8 (sessions had 10 and 7 periods).,0.9,1,Proposer chooses integer X from 0 to 500; flexible offers.,1,0,Bargaining over a fixed pie; no default public contribution.,0.5,N/A,No linear public good environment.,1,0,Silence enforced; no chat functionality described.,1,0,"Only see own pair’s outcomes, not others’ summaries.",0.8,0,Punishment is dyadic and anonymous within a rematching design; no public identities.,0.9,0,No reward instrument in UP; hence no reward identity.,1,1,"Subjects informed about repeated nature and see round info, as in instructions.",0.7,1,Section 2.1 defines UP: responder can punish proposer after accepting.,1,1,Implementation with l=1: one cent of punishment reduces both players’ payoffs by 1 cent for responder (cost) and 1 cent for proposer; marginal cost to responder is 1.,1,1,UP game description: responder’s spending r reduces proposer’s payoff by lr with l=1 in this experiment.,1,0,"UP only has a punishment option, no reward.",1,N/A,No reward instrument.,1,N/A,No reward instrument.,1,500,Same S=500 cents per round as in other treatments.,1,0.412,"Average offer in UP is 205.8 cents (Table 2), about 41% of 500; 205.8/500≈0.412.",1,205.8,Table 2 ‘Average offer’ for UP is 205.8.,1,0.815,Section 3.5: ‘average aggregate payoff is at 81.5% in UP’.,1,407.7,Sum of average proposer and responder payoffs: 228.7 + 179.0 = 407.7.,1,USA,"All treatments run at FSU, USA.",0.95,N/R,Age not reported; only that they are undergraduates.,0,53.5% female overall; treatment-specific breakdown N/R,Only pooled gender share reported.,0.7,Undergraduate students,Same subject pool as other treatments.,1,On site,Conducted in physical lab using z-Tree.,1,"Ultimatum bargaining with post-acceptance costly punishment. Pie S=500 cents. Stage 1: proposer chooses X∈[0,500]. Stage 2: responder accepts or rejects; rejection yields 0 to both. Stage 3 (if accepted): responder may choose r∈[0, min{X, 500−X}] to reduce both players’ payoffs by r (payoffs 500−X−r and X−r). Repeated zipper rematching with fixed roles; two randomly selected rounds determine payment.",Summarized from Section 2.1 (UP definition) and general procedures.,1
10.1111_jems.12451.md,UPR – Ultimatum with reward and punishment,UPR is defined as ultimatum with both reward and punishment and is one of the four treatments (Table 1).,1,Responder instruments available: both costly reward (k=1) and punishment (l=1) (UPR),"UPR game: responder can, after accepting x, either reward or punish as in UR and UP combined; parameters implemented with k=l=1.",1,True,UPR is a human-subject laboratory treatment.,1,True,Treatment condition within controlled lab experiment.,1,True,Conducted at FSU’s XS/FS experimental lab.,1,False,No simulations for UPR are reported.,1,False,"Analytical appendix covers generic games, but empirical UPR is lab-based.",1,2,Each bargaining game involves one proposer and one responder.,1,8.5,Table 2 gives ‘Average # of rounds’ for UPR as 8.5 (sessions of 8 and 9 periods).,0.9,1,Proposer’s offer is an integer between 0 and 500; flexible.,1,0,No public good; bargaining over fixed pie. Use 0 by convention.,0.5,N/A,Not a contribution/public goods game.,1,0,"Instructions emphasize silence, no direct communication.",1,0,Only individual round result screens; no mention of others’ summary statistics.,0.8,0,Rematching and anonymity; punishments are not publicly linked to IDs.,0.9,0,Rewards (and punishments) are private within pair; no public identity revelation.,0.9,1,Instructions and on-screen information show round progression.,0.7,1,UPR allows punishment as in UP.,1,1,"Implemented with l=1, so each cent of punishment costs responder 1 cent.",1,1,Responder’s spending r reduces proposer’s payoff by lr with l=1.,1,1,UPR offers both reward and punishment options.,1,1,Rewards implemented with k=1: each cent transferred costs the responder 1 cent.,1,1,Responder’s spending t increases proposer’s payoff by kt with k=1.,1,500,Same division amount S=500 cents.,1,0.439,"Average offer in UPR is 219.7 cents, reported as 44% of 500; 219.7/500≈0.439.",1,219.7,Table 2 ‘Average offer’ for UPR is 219.7.,1,0.852,Section 3.5: ‘average aggregate payoff is at 85.2% in UPR’.,1,426,Average proposer payoff 234.4 plus responder payoff 191.6 equals 426.0.,1,USA,All treatments run in FSU lab (USA).,0.95,N/R,No explicit age stats reported.,0,53.5% female overall; treatment-specific breakdown N/R,Only overall gender fraction provided.,0.7,Undergraduate students,Same subject pool description applies.,1,On site,Conducted in XS/FS experimental laboratory using z-Tree.,1,"Ultimatum bargaining with both post-acceptance reward and punishment. Pie S=500 cents. Stage 1: proposer picks X∈[0,500]. Stage 2: responder accepts or rejects; rejection yields 0,0. Stage 3 (if accepted): responder can either (i) reward by choosing t∈[0,X], giving payoffs (500−X+t, X−t), or (ii) punish by choosing r∈[0, min{X, 500−X}], giving (500−X−r, X−r). Only one of reward or punishment can be chosen per accepted offer. Repeated zipper rematching; fixed roles; two rounds randomly paid.",Based on Section 2.1 (UPR definition) and detailed sample instructions for UPR in Appendix C.,1
