10.1007_s11229-022-03717-8.md,Analytical model – coupling and redundancy (Maria and Pedro bats),Paper presents a formal probabilistic model in Section 3.1 and Appendix A using two vampire bats (Maria and Pedro) to analyze how coupling affects success of a redundant group task.,1,Level of coupling between redundant members’ success probabilities (from independent to positively coupled); individual success probability a held constant.,"Section 3.1 and Appendix A vary the conditional probabilities P(h_M|h_P) and P(h_P|h_M) relative to a, keeping P(h_i)=a constant, to study effects of coupling on group success.",1,False,No human or animal subjects are observed or experimented on; it is a formal probabilistic example.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment but a theoretical illustration.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical.,1,False,No numerical or agent-based simulation is run; only algebraic probability arguments.,1,True,Appendix A explicitly provides a formal probabilistic derivation of how coupling changes P(h_M ∨ h_P).,1,2,"Model explicitly considers two bats, Maria and Pedro.",1,1,Single ‘night’/task completion event is modeled; no repeated interactions are described.,0.8,0,Not a contribution game; individuals either succeed or fail at hunting. All-or-nothing contribution concept does not apply.,0.5,N/A,No human public-goods endowment or contribution process; analytical model of task success.,1,N/R,No public-goods multiplier or MPCR is defined in the model.,1,N/A,No human participants or communication protocol.,1,0,No information feedback or payoffs are shown; not a game with interfaces.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in the analytical model.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in the analytical model.,1,0,No rounds or displays; purely analytical.,1,0,No punishment or coercion mechanism is modeled in the Maria–Pedro example.,1,N/A,Punishment is absent.,1,N/A,Punishment is absent.,1,0,No explicit reward mechanism; only survival if at least one succeeds.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No tokens or endowments; analytical success/failure model.,1,N/A,No contribution decision; individuals are always ‘attempting’ the task.,1,N/A,No quantitative contributions are modeled.,1,N/R,No explicit payoff maximization or efficiency metric is defined; only probability of success is analyzed.,1,N/R,"Model does not assign numerical payoffs, only success probabilities.",1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Purely analytical model without human subjects.,1,"Analytical reliability-style model of a redundant two-member group; each member succeeds with probability a, and group success is P(h_M ∨ h_P)=2a−P(h_M∧h_P). Coupling increases P(h_M∧h_P) and therefore decreases group success and increases joint failure probability.","Summarized from Section 3.1 and Appendix A, where the conditional probabilities defining coupling are introduced and manipulated algebraically.",1
10.1007_s11229-022-03717-8.md,Analytical model – redundancy vs number of group members (1−0.4^n curve),Section 3 introduces an idealized reliability example and Fig. 1: chance of completing a group task is 1−0.4^n with n group members.,1,"Number of redundant group members n (n ≥ 1), with individual failure probability 0.4 and independence across members.","Text states each member has 60% success rate (40% failure) and that group success is 1−0.4^n, varying n.",1,False,No observed data; only a constructed numerical example.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment; it is a theoretical reliability calculation.,1,False,No lab or field implementation.,1,False,Formula 1−0.4^n is used directly; no agent-based or stochastic simulation is described.,1,True,Closed-form expression for success probability is derived from independent failures assumption.,1,1,"Model defines group success probability as a function of n; to avoid choosing a specific n, use minimal case and treat n as analytical parameter.",0.5,1,Single-shot task completion probability is modeled; no repetition.,0.9,0,"Model is about binary success/failure of individuals, not contribution choices; public-goods contribution framing does not apply directly.",0.5,N/A,No endowments or contributions; just probability of success.,1,N/R,No multiplier or group return parameter is defined.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,No interface or information feedback; analytical.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,0,No rounds or displays in an analytical example.,1,0,No punishment or coercion is part of this numeric redundancy example.,1,N/A,Punishment absent.,1,N/A,Punishment absent.,1,0,No explicit reward beyond task success.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No tokens or payoffs per se; focus is on success probability.,1,N/A,No contribution choice modeled.,1,N/A,No contributions quantified.,1,N/R,"No efficiency metric defined, only success probability.",1,N/R,No numerical group payoff specified.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Analytical calculation with no empirical interface.,1,"Idealized reliability-style redundancy model: each of n members has 60% chance of success and 40% chance of failure, independently. Group succeeds if at least one succeeds; success probability is 1−0.4^n, showing diminishing marginal gains from extra redundant members.",Section 3 and Fig. 1 directly specify individual success rate (60%) and formula 1−0.4^n as function of group size.,1
10.1007_s11229-022-03743-6.md,"Simulation – No punishment (c=0, l=0), m=0.3, e=0.9","Condition described in Sect. 3.2 as the baseline with c=l=0, m=0.3, e=0.9, varying T and a.",1,"Punishment parameters c=0, l=0 (no punishment); benefit when needy a∈[0.2,0.4]; expected repetitions T varying; fixed need probability m=0.3 and lie detection e=0.9.",Paper compares this baseline to cases with positive c and l while varying a and T.,0.94,False,Study is an evolutionary game-theoretic simulation with no human subjects.,1,False,Not a controlled behavioral experiment; it is a model-based simulation.,1,False,No lab or field setting; purely theoretical/simulation.,1,True,Sect. 3.2 explicitly describes simulations of replicator dynamics over 3000 time periods and 500 runs.,1,False,"Although some analytical derivations exist (e.g., Sect. 3.1), this condition refers to the numerical simulations.",0.8,2,Sir Philip Sidney game and repeated interactions are defined between a sender and a receiver (two players).,1,-1,Game is repeated with continuation probability w leading to expected repetitions T; exact discrete round count is not fixed and varies via T; coded as -1 since schema requires number.,0.4,1,"Players’ decisions are binary (donate vs not, signal vs not); no graded contribution levels as in standard PGGs, so contribution amount is effectively all-or-nothing in each interaction.",0.7,-1,No public goods endowment structure; not a contribution game in that sense.,1,N/R,"Model is a dyadic signaling/donation game, not a linear public goods game; no MPCR defined.",1,-1,"No human subjects; communication is modeled as signals within the game, not chat between experimental participants.",1,0,No interface or feedback summaries; only payoffs within the game are modeled.,1,-1,No human identities; punishment is abstract within the model.,1,-1,No rewards distinct from donations and payoffs; no identities.,1,0,"Agents do not observe a fixed horizon; repetition is probabilistic via w and T,