en needy a (horizontal axis). Simulation protocol unchanged: replicator dynamics over 3000 periods, 500 runs. Outcomes: better lie detection (higher e) increases honesty and prevalence of punishment, especially when a is high; lower e leads to more defection.",Summarized from Sect. 3.2.1 and Fig. 5.,0.96
10.1007_s11229-022-03743-6.md,"Simulation – Punish defection only (c=0.1, l=0.2), m=0.3, e=0.9","Condition in Sect. 3.3 and Fig. 6a–c where punishment targets defection but not lying, with c=0.1, l=0.2.",1,"Punishment of defection enabled (c=0.1, l=0.2), no punishment of lying; benefit when needy a∈[0.2,0.4]; T varying; m=0.3, e=0.9.","Sect. 3.3: game is augmented to allow punishment of defection and results reported for c=0.1, l=0.2.",0.95,False,"Simulation, no human subjects.",1,False,Not an empirical experiment.,1,False,No lab implementation.,1,True,Replicator dynamics simulations as elsewhere in Sect. 3.,1,False,Effects shown via heatmaps (numerical).,0.9,2,Dyadic repeated game.,1,-1,T varies as expected repetitions; no fixed round count given.,0.4,1,Actions are discrete (donate vs not; punish vs not).,0.8,-1,No public goods endowment.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,-1,No human interface.,1,0,No display of other players’ histories beyond what strategies implicitly use.,1,-1,No explicit identities.,1,-1,No rewards.,1,0,No explicit horizon known to agents.,0.8,1,"Punishment option present but applied to defection (e.g., failure to donate when counterpart cooperates).",1,0.1,Same cost c=0.1 is used for punishing defection.,1,0.2,Same penalty parameter l=0.2 used but directed at defectors.,1,0,No explicit rewards beyond standard payoffs.,1,-1,Not applicable.,1,-1,Not applicable.,1,N/R,No token endowment is specified.,1,N/R,"Outcomes given as proportions of cooperative vs defecting strategies, not contributions per se.",1,N/R,No explicit donation amounts.,1,N/R,No explicit efficiency metric reported.,1,N/R,Group payoffs are not quantified.,1,N/A,No human population.,1,N/A,No human population.,1,N/A,No human population.,1,N/A,No human population.,1,No human,Simulation study.,1,"Repeated Sir Philip Sidney game extended so that receivers can punish defections (failure to donate when cooperation is expected), at cost c=0.1, imposing penalty l=0.2 on defectors. In this treatment, lying is not punished as such. Analysis in Sect. 3.3 (Fig. 6a–c, Fig. 7a) shows punishment of defection substantially increases the basin of attraction for cooperation, similarly to Vanderschraaf’s augmented stag hunt results.",Summarized from Sect. 3.3 text and figures.,0.93
10.1007_s11229-022-03743-6.md,"Simulation – Punish lying and defection (c=0.1, l=0.2), m=0.3, e=0.9","Combined punishment condition in Sect. 3.3 and Fig. 6d–f where both lying and defection can be punished with c=0.1, l=0.2.",1,"Punishment of both lying and defection enabled with c=0.1, l=0.2; benefit when needy a∈[0.2,0.4]; T varying; m=0.3, e=0.9.",Sect. 3.3: “we allow both punishment of lying and punishment of defection” using the same cost and penalty as earlier.,0.95,False,"No human subjects, only simulations.",1,False,Not an experiment in the empirical sense.,1,False,No lab.,1,True,Replicator dynamics simulations over parameter grid.,1,False,"Outcomes presented via numerical heatmaps, not closed-form.",0.9,2,Same two-player game.,1,-1,Repetition handled via T; not a fixed known number of rounds.,0.4,1,Actions are discrete; no partial donations.,0.8,-1,No endowment split.,1,N/R,Not a linear public goods game.,1,-1,No human interaction medium.,1,0,No interface.,1,-1,No identities tracked.,1,-1,No separate rewards.,1,0,Agents do not observe the length of interaction.,0.8,1,Model includes costly punishment targeting both deception and defection.,1,0.1,Cost per punishment c=0.1 for both lie- and defection-punishment.,1,0.2,Penalty magnitude l=0.2 for both types of punished behavior.,1,0,No reward mechanism added.,1,-1,Not applicable.,1,-1,Not applicable.,1,N/R,Endowment not explicitly specified.,1,N/R,"Outcomes described via prevalence of cooperative vs defecting strategies and honesty, not contributions.",1,N/R,No numerical donation amounts reported.,1,N/R,Efficiency not reported quantitatively.,1,N/R,Group payoffs not given.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,All results are from simulations.,1,"Same Sir Philip Sidney framework, but agents can punish both misreporting of need (lying) and defection (failure to donate when cooperation is expected), each at cost c=0.1 imposing penalty l=0.2. Sect. 3.3 (Fig. 6d–f, Fig. 7b) shows that cooperation outcomes are intermediate: combining the two punishments sometimes improves cooperation relative to punishing lying only, but still leaves parameter regions where punishment reduces successful cooperation compared to the no-punishment baseline.",Summarized from Sect. 3.3 combined punishment discussion.,0.94
10.1007_s10683-022-09758-7.md,"MAIN – Type A (heterogeneous, exclusion allowed)",MAIN is the main heterogeneous treatment with exclusion; this row represents Type A members within those teams.,1,"Treatment: MAIN (heterogeneous productivity, exclusion enabled); Player type: Type A",Independent variables varied across conditions are treatment (MAIN vs other treatments) and player productivity type (A vs B).,1,True,Human-subject laboratory experiment with university students.,1,True,Game rules and treatments are experimentally controlled and randomized.,1,True,Conducted at the EconLab of the University of Innsbruck (physical lab).,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulation; only human gameplay.,1,False,"Paper contains theoretical discussion but primary results are from lab experiment, not closed-form analytical model only.",1,4,“Participants play in teams of 4 subjects.”,1,15,“over a game horizon of 15 periods” and “The experiment consisted of 15 periods”.,1,1,"Each period each subject allocates an endowment of 10 tokens between production and theft; any integer split is allowed, not only all-or-nothing.",1,0,All 10 tokens start under the subject’s individual control and must be allocated; no initial public fund. Thus 0 proportion pre-allocated to a group account.,0.9,N/R,"This is not a linear public goods game; returns are from individual production functions and theft, so a single MPCR is not defined.",1,0,No mention of communication or chat among participants; structure only includes allocation and voting stages plus information table.,0.95,1,"Stage 2 shows an information table with each team member’s earnings, theft, production, and losses to theft.",1,1,Exclusion votes are by player number within fixed teams; identities of excluded members and who is back in the team are observable via fixed player numbers.,0.8,0,No reward mechanism exists in the design.,1,0,"Paper does not report that the remaining or total number of periods is displayed on screen, only that there are 15 periods overall.",0.4,1,Peer exclusion by majority vote is an ostracism/punishment mechanism.,1,1,“A vote that results in a successful exclusion costs 1 ECU.”,1,1,"One successful majority decision (1 ECU cost to each voter whose vote is pivotal) causes the target to be excluded for one period and receive a fixed 5 ECU instead of playing. For coding, we approximate 1 ECU spent leads to 1 period of zero decision rights and reduced earning potential, encoded as 1.",0.5,0,No mechanism to spend resources to increase others' payoffs.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,N/A,No reward institution.,1,10,“All team members receive an endowment of 10 tokens in each of the periods.”,1,N/R,"Design is a production–theft allocation game, not a standard public goods contribution measure; no normalized contribution fraction reported.",1,N/R,"Paper reports tokens invested in theft, not directly tokens invested in production; production allocation could be inferred as 10 - theft, but not reported by type and treatment explicitly.",0.4,N/R,They discuss efficiency qualitatively and via payoffs but do not provide normalized efficiency (payoff / maximum) values.,0.3,N/R,"Table 2 reports individual earnings by type and treatment, not group-level payoffs.",0.3,Austria,"Experiment conducted at the University of Innsbruck, Austria; subject pool is university students there.",0.95,N/R,Only described as students from various academic backgrounds; no age statistics given.,1,60.6% female; remainder male/other,“In total 444 students (60.6% female) participated…”; gender not given by treatment or type.,0.9,University students (various academic backgrounds),“The subject pool consists of students from various academic backgrounds.”,1,On site,Conducted in the EconLab with z-Tree and cash payment in person; indicates physical lab sessions.,1,"Heterogeneous productivity: 2 Type A and 2 Type B per team. Each period: Stage 1 – allocate 10 tokens between own production (concave production function; Type A marginal outputs per token: 24,22,20,18,16,14,12,10,8,6; Type B: 18,16,14,12,10,8,6,4,2,0) and theft. Each token invested in theft yields +15 ECU to thief and −15 ECU to chosen target. Stage 2 – information table showing for each team member: earnings from production, from theft, and losses to theft. Then exclusion voting: each active member may vote to exclude others; majority (≥2 votes) leads to exclusion next period. Excluded members receive 5 ECU, cannot allocate tokens or vote, but see info table; automatically re-enter after one period. First interaction period has an additional initial voting stage before Stage 1.",Summary of the detailed framework and MAIN treatment description in sections 3.1 and 3.2 plus Table 1.,1
10.1007_s10683-022-09758-7.md,"MAIN – Type B (heterogeneous, exclusion allowed)",Same MAIN treatment; this row represents Type B members within those teams.,1,"Treatment: MAIN (heterogeneous productivity, exclusion enabled); Player type: Type B","Independent variables varied across conditions are treatment and player productivity type; here, Type B in MAIN.",1,True,Human-subject lab experiment.,1,True,Controlled random assignment to types and treatments.,1,True,Executed in physical EconLab at University of Innsbruck.,1,False,No non-human simulation.,1,False,Primary data from lab experiment.,1,4,Teams of 4 subjects.,1,15,15 periods per session.,1,1,Subjects choose any allocation of 10 tokens between activities.,1,0,All tokens initially under individual control; no default public allocation.,0.9,N/R,Nonlinear individual production and theft; MPCR not defined.,1,0,No communication feature described.,0.95,1,"Information table shows each member’s production, theft, and losses.",1,1,"Players have fixed numbers and see who is excluded/returns, so punished identities are known.",0.8,0,No rewards.,1,0,Not stated that number of remaining rounds is displayed.,0.4,1,Exclusion by majority vote.,1,1,Successful exclusion vote costs 1 ECU for the voter.,1,1,Each successful vote causes one period exclusion for the target; coded as 1 unit effect per 1 ECU for consistency.,0.5,0,No rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,10,10 tokens per period per team member.,1,N/R,No standard contribution rate reported.,1,N/R,Only theft tokens are tabulated; production tokens are implied but not directly reported by type.,0.4,N/R,Efficiency discussed qualitatively; no normalized ratio.,0.3,N/R,"Only individual earnings by type reported, not team totals.",0.3,Austria,"University of Innsbruck, Austria, lab subject pool.",0.95,N/R,No age information reported.,1,60.6% female; remainder male/other,Overall sample gender composition given; not by type.,0.9,University students (various academic backgrounds),Subjects are university students.,1,On site,Conducted in physical EconLab with in-person payment.,1,"Same as MAIN Type A condition: 2 Type A and 2 Type B per team; Type B production function has lower marginal outputs (18,16,14,12,10,8,6,4,2,0) than Type A. Incentive-compatible best response for Type B under selfish preferences is 2 tokens to production and 8 to theft; each theft token yields +15 ECU to thief and −15 ECU to target. Majority-vote ostracism for one period following information about current-period production and theft.","Same treatment as MAIN; only player type differs, so game structure identical but incentives differ via productivity function.",1
