 as elsewhere.,1,On site,Same EconLab environment.,1,"All subjects have Type B production function (lower marginal productivity). Subjects are randomly assigned to one of two color groups (yellow or red) with no monetary consequences, generating minimal in-group vs out-group identities. Game phases are identical to MAIN: in each period players allocate 10 tokens between production and theft, observe the information table, and vote on exclusion using a majority rule that yields one-period ostracism.",MIB is defined as minimal group treatment with Type B production function and same exclusion structure.,1
10.1007_s11403-022-00363-8.md,"Control – Heterogeneous MPCR, No Incentives",Paper describes a control condition with MPCR 0.9/0.4 and no incentivizing stage; used wherever periods have no punishment/reward.,1,Incentive institution: None (control); MPCR structure within group: one high-benefit member with MPCR 0.9 and two low-benefit members with MPCR 0.4,Control differs from treatment conditions only by absence of incentivizing stage; MPCR heterogeneity is fixed across all conditions.,1,True,Laboratory experiment with human subjects recruited via ORSEE.,1,True,Design includes random assignment to groups and controlled treatments in a lab.,1,True,Conducted in a university experimental lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No computer-only simulations described; all data from human participants.,1,False,"Theory appears in appendix but main results are experimental, not purely analytical.",1,3,Game is played in three-person groups.,1,10,Each block (control or treatment) has 10 periods; this row refers to the no-incentive block.,1,1,"Subjects choose 'how much he contributes' out of 20 tokens; implies continuous or discrete 0–20, not binary.",0.9,0,All 20 tokens are initially in private endowment; subjects actively decide how much to move to public account.,1,0.5666667,"One member has MPCR 0.9, two have 0.4. Average per-capita marginal return across three players: (0.9+0.4+0.4)/3=1.7/3≈0.5667.",0.7,0,Communication between participants is explicitly disallowed.,1,1,At end of each period participants see each group member's contributions and earnings.,1,0,"Participants only see total points received from the two others, not each individual’s spend; identities of punishers/rewarders are hidden.",1,0,Same incentivizing-stage information structure; only totals from two others are shown.,1,0,No statement that remaining or total periods are displayed on screen; cannot infer.,0.2,0,Control condition includes only contribution stage without incentivizing opportunities.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in control condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in control condition.,1,0,No reward stage in control condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in control condition.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in control condition.,1,20,Subjects earn '20 experimental tokens at the start of each period'.,1,0.2704,"Table 2: average group total contribution 16.225 out of 60 max per group (3×20), so 16.225/60≈0.2704.",1,16.225,Table 2 reports 'Group total' contributions for Control as 16.225 tokens.,1,N/R,Efficiency ratio (total payoff / maximum) is not reported and cannot be reconstructed cleanly for heterogeneous MPCR without full distribution details.,0.2,23.86,"Section 4.3.2: average per-period earnings per participant in control is 23.860; group of 3 implies 3×23.860≈71.58, but only per-person value is explicitly given. To keep consistent units, report per-person value noted in text.",0.5,Australia,"Experiment approved by UNSW Sydney ethics and run in UNSW BizLab, implying Australian location and subject pool.",0.8,N/R,No age range or mean reported.,0,N/R,No gender composition information reported.,0,University students,"Subjects recruited through a university lab (UNSW BizLab/ORSEE), standard for undergraduate/graduate student pools; no other demographics given.",0.7,On site,Sessions conducted in a physical laboratory using z-Tree; no indication of online implementation.,1,"Three-person linear public goods game with heterogeneous MPCRs (one high-benefit MPCR 0.9, two low-benefit MPCR 0.4). Fixed groups for 10 periods. Each period: subjects earn 20 tokens via calculation tasks, then simultaneously choose contributions from 0–20 to a public account. Payoff: π_i = e_i − c_i + a_i × Σ c_i in control. Feedback each period includes all members’ contributions and earnings. No communication allowed.",Summary from design description and payoff formula excluding incentivizing terms (no m_ij in control).,1
10.1007_s11403-022-00363-8.md,"Punishment – Heterogeneous MPCR, Peer Punishment",Treatment condition with incentivizing stage where subjects can decrease others' earnings at a cost; referred to as 'punish' condition.,1,Incentive institution: Peer punishment (costly decreasing of others' earnings); MPCR structure within group: one high-benefit member with MPCR 0.9 and two low-benefit members with MPCR 0.4,Treatment differs from control by adding a punishment incentivizing stage; MPCR heterogeneity remains identical.,1,True,Uses human participants in lab sessions.,1,True,Random assignment to groups and exogenous treatment conditions.,1,True,Conducted in UNSW experimental lab using z-Tree.,1,False,No simulations described for main results.,1,False,Analytical model only in appendix; this row refers to experimental treatment.,1,3,Three-person groups throughout.,1,10,Each session contains 10 periods with incentivizing opportunities and 10 without; this row refers to the 10 punishment periods within relevant sessions.,1,1,Subjects choose any amount from their 20-token endowment to contribute to the public good.,0.9,0,Endowments are private; contributions are active decisions.,1,0.5666667,"Within-group MPCRs 0.9, 0.4, 0.4; average per capita is (0.9+0.4+0.4)/3≈0.5667.",0.7,0,Communication between participants not allowed in any condition.,1,1,"End-of-period feedback includes each group member's contributions and earnings, plus total punishment points received/assigned.",1,0,Participants only see total points the two others assigned; individual identities are not revealed.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism in punishment condition.,1,0,Not reported that GUI displays periods remaining; cannot infer.,0.2,1,Punishment condition defined by presence of costly decreasing of others’ earnings.,1,1,Each point assigned costs the punisher 1 token.,1,3,Each punishment point decreases receiver's earnings by 3 tokens (−3 term in payoff).,1,0,Only decreasing (punishment) is implemented; reward treatment is separate.,1,N/A,No reward stage in punishment condition.,1,N/A,No reward stage in punishment condition.,1,20,Same 20-token endowment per period earned via calculation tasks as in control.,1,0.2563,Table 2: group total in Punish condition is 15.379 out of 60; 15.379/60≈0.2563.,1,15.379,Table 2 reports 'Group total' contributions for Punish as 15.379 tokens.,1,N/R,"Net efficiency (relative to maximum with full contributions and no punishment) not directly computed; needs full distribution of contributions/punishments by type, which is not provided.",0.2,17.007,Section 4.3.2: average per-period earnings per participant in punishment condition are 17.007 tokens.,1,Australia,Experiment run at UNSW Sydney in Australia.,0.8,N/R,No age information given.,0,N/R,No gender breakdown reported.,0,University students,Typical for UNSW lab (BizLab) subject pool; no contrary information.,0.7,On site,Sessions described as laboratory sessions using z-Tree; no remote component mentioned.,1,"Same three-person linear public goods game and heterogeneous MPCRs as control. Each period: Stage 1 contribution decision from 20-token endowment; Stage 2 punishment stage where each participant can assign 0–10 punishment points across the other two members. Each point costs 1 token to the punisher and reduces the receiver's earnings by 3 tokens. Payoff: π_i = e_i − c_i + a_i × Σ c_i − 3 Σ_{j≠i} m_ji − Σ_{j≠i} m_ij. Fixed groups over 10 punishment periods. Feedback each period includes contributions, earnings, and total punishment points assigned/received; identities of punishers hidden.","Compiled from design description, payoff formula sign (minus for punishment), and footnote on incentivizing points.",1
10.1007_s11403-022-00363-8.md,"Reward – Heterogeneous MPCR, Peer Reward",Treatment condition with incentivizing stage where subjects can increase others' earnings at a cost; referred to as 'reward' condition.,1,Incentive institution: Peer reward (costly increasing of others' earnings); MPCR structure within group: one high-benefit member with MPCR 0.9 and two low-benefit members with MPCR 0.4,Reward treatment adds a reward incentivizing stage but keeps MPCR heterogeneity unchanged.,1,True,Based on behavior of human subjects in the lab.,1,True,Random assignment to sessions with reward vs punishment; fixed groups; controlled rules.,1,True,Implemented in UNSW lab with z-Tree; on-site sessions.,1,False,No agent-based or numerical simulations reported for this treatment.,1,False,Analytical models are separate from the experimental implementation.,1,3,Groups of three in all conditions.,1,10,Reward opportunities provided in a 10-period block within reward sessions.,1,1,Participants choose any amount 0–20 to contribute each period.,0.9,0,Tokens start as private endowment; public account only via active contribution.,1,0.5666667,"Within-group MPCRs 0.9, 0.4, 0.4; average per capita MPCR is 1.7/3≈0.5667.",0.7,0,Instructions explicitly forbid communication between participants.,1,1,"Participants see each member’s contributions, earnings, and total reward points assigned/received per period.",1,N/A,No punishment mechanism exists in reward treatment.,1,0,Subjects only see combined number of reward points from the other two; individual identities not revealed.,1,0,The display of remaining rounds is not mentioned.,0.2,0,Reward treatment uses only positive incentives; no decreasing of payoffs is allowed.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in reward condition.,1,N/A,No punishment mechanism in reward condition.,1,1,Reward condition defined by second stage where subjects can increase others' earnings at a cost.,1,1,Each assigned reward point costs the rewarder 1 token.,1,3,Each reward point increases receiver's earnings by 3 tokens (+3 factor in payoff).,1,20,Same earning tasks and 20-token per-period endowment apply in all conditions.,1,0.4193,Table 2: reward group total is 25.159 of 60; 25.159/60≈0.4193.,1,25.159,Table 2 'Group total' for Reward is 25.159 tokens.,1,N/R,Text discusses efficiency qualitatively and via average earnings but does not provide normalized efficiency index relative to theoretical maximum.,0.2,31.685,Section 4.3.2: average per-period earnings per participant in reward condition are 31.685 tokens.,1,Australia,Experiment run at UNSW Sydney.,0.8,N/R,No explicit age data.,0,N/R,No gender composition reported.,0,University students,Subjects recruited through UNSW BizLab/ORSEE; standard student subject pool is implied.,0.7,On site,Described as experimental sessions in a lab using z-Tree; no online implementation.,1,"Same public goods structure and heterogeneous MPCRs as control and punishment. Each period: Stage 1, each subject contributes 0–20 tokens from a 20-token endowment; Stage 2, reward stage where each participant has up to 10 reward points to assign across the two others. Each point costs the rewarder 1 token and increases the receiver’s payoff by 3 tokens. Payoff: π_i = e_i − c_i + a_i × Σ c_i + 3 Σ_{j≠i} m_ji − Σ_{j≠i} m_ij. Fixed groups over 10 reward periods. Feedback shows contributions, earnings, and total reward points assigned/received; identities of rewarders hidden.",Taken from incentivizing description and general payoff formula with positive '±3' in reward condition.,1
10.3390_math10152823.md,Simulation – Constant punishment intensity approach (PGG with pool punishment),Paper contrasts constant punishment intensity approach with negative feedback punishment approach in the same simulation environment (Appendix A.1); treat each punishment method as a separate condition.,1,"Punishment method = constant punishment intensity; punishment intention k varied in [0,1] with step 0.01; punishment intensity t^(p) = k.",Section 2.1.2 defines constant punishment intensity approach as t^(p)=k and Figure 2a varies k from 0 to 1 with sampling interval 0.01.,1,False,Study uses evolutionary simulations with artificial agents; no human participants are described.,1,False,Not a controlled human or field experiment; it is a computational simulation study.,1,False,No laboratory setting with human subjects; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Authors repeatedly refer to evolutionary simulation and specify simulation setup in Appendix A.1.,1,False,"This condition corresponds to numerical simulations, not the separate analytical derivations in Section 3.3.",1,100,Appendix A.1: 'The group consisted of 100 individuals.',1,300,Appendix A.1: 'The evolution consisted of 300 rounds under each punishment intention k.',1,1,"Agents either invest r_c=4 (C) or r_d=2 (D) from endowment R=5; they cannot choose arbitrary continuous amounts, so contributions are discrete strategy-based rather than a continuous choice set. Interpreted as not all-or-nothing in the standard sense (any amount choice not present); coding as 1 to reflect that they can have intermediate investment level relative to R.",0.4,0,"At the start of each round each individual 'is given R units of resources and then simultaneously invests resources into the public pool based on his/her strategy'; initial resources are in private account until invested, so default proportion in public fund is 0.",0.8,0.03,"MPCR = multiplier / group size = μ / n = 3 / 100 = 0.03, using μ=3 and n=100 from Appendix A.1.",1,N/A,No human subjects; communication among agents is not modeled.,1,0,Simulation description only involves agents observing others' payoffs for learning; there is no interface or explicit summary display akin to an experiment.,0.6,N/A,No identities shown; agents are anonymous in a homogeneous population model.,1,N/A,"No reward mechanism in the model, only punishment.",1,0,Agents do not have explicit knowledge of total rounds in the simulation description.,0.6,1,By design this is PGG with pool punishment; defectors are punished with rate t^(p).,1,N/R,Cost to punisher (institution) per unit of punishment is not modeled; only victims lose resources.,1,N/R,No separate technology parameter; punishment is a proportional loss r_i^(p) * t^(p).,1,0,Model only specifies punishment of defectors; no rewards for cooperators.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward mechanism.,1,5,Appendix A.1: 'the initial resource R=5.',1,N/R,"Figures report cooperation proportion (strategy choice), not normalized contribution rate relative to R.",0.3,N/R,"While r_c=4 and r_d=2 are defined, average actual contribution per round under this condition is not tabulated.",0.4,N/R,Paper does not report total payoffs relative to maximum possible in numerical form.,0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoff outcomes are reported; only qualitative cost proxies (cumulative punishment intensity).,0.2,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,No human,Evolutionary simulation with artificial agents only.,1,"Two-strategy PGG with pool punishment: n=100, R=5, cooperators invest r_c=4, defectors invest r_d=2, public pool multiplied by μ=3 and shared equally; learning via pairwise comparison with logit rule parameter β=0.1 and mutation rate α=0.05; punishment intensity t^(p)=k constant across rounds for given k; sanctioning institution uses binary search over k to target cooperation threshold θ=0.95.",Summarized from Sections 2.1.1–2.1.2 and Appendix A.1–A.2.,1
10.3390_math10152823.md,"Simulation – Negative feedback punishment approach (baseline exponential form g(x,k)=k×5^{-x})","Main proposed method; compared directly with constant punishment intensity with same base PGG parameters (Sections 2.1.2, 3.1, Figure 2b).",1,"Punishment method = negative feedback punishment with exponential form g(x,k)=k×5^{-x}; punishment intention k varied in [0,1] with step 0.01; punishment intensity t^(p)=g(f_c^(p),k) updated each round based on current cooperation proportion f_c^(p).","Section 2.1.2: 'We apply g(x,k)=k×5^{-x} as the punishment intensity function in the negative feedback punishment approach'; Figure 2b uses same k-grid as 2a.",1,False,Simulation with artificial agents only.,1,False,Not an empirical human experiment.,1,False,No physical lab; computational environment only.,1,True,Explicitly described as evolutionary simulation; setup in Appendix A.1.,1,False,"This row refers to numerical simulation, not analytic results.",1,100,Appendix A.1: 'The group consisted of 100 individuals.' Same base game as constant intensity condition.,1,300,Appendix A.1: evolution consisted of 300 rounds under each punishment intention k; same applies here.,1,1,"Same discrete strategy structure (C invests 4, D invests 2 from R=5) as in constant punishment condition.",0.4,0,Same as other condition: endowment R=5 allocated privately then invested based on strategy.,0.8,0.03,"Same n=100 and μ=3, so MPCR=3/100.",1,N/A,No human subjects or communication channel.,1,0,No explicit display of others' earnings/punishments; only payoff-based learning in the algorithm.,0.6,N/A,No identities; homogeneous agents in simulation.,1,N/A,No rewards in the model.,1,0,Agents are not described as having information about total or remaining rounds.,0.6,1,Pool punishment of defectors is central to the model.,1,N/R,No explicit cost to punisher; only defectors' resource loss is modeled.,1,N/R,Punishment implemented as proportional loss r_i^(p)×t^(p); no separate per-unit technology parameter.,1,0,Model contains only punishment; no rewarding mechanism.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,5,Appendix A.1 sets R=5 for the PGG with pool punishment.,1,N/R,Only cooperation proportion (strategy share) and punishment intensity are reported; no normalized contribution rate outcome.,0.3,N/R,"While contributions per strategy are known (4 or 2), aggregate or average contributions per round/outcome are not tabulated.",0.4,N/R,"Outcomes focus on stability, time (rounds), and cumulative punishment intensity; no explicit efficiency ratio reported.",0.2,N/R,No numeric group payoff series reported; cost proxy is cumulative punishment intensity instead.,0.2,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,No human,Condition is a computer simulation without human participants.,1,"Same base PGG with pool punishment as constant-intensity condition: n=100, R=5, cooperators invest r_c=4, defectors r_d=2, public pool multiplied by μ=3 and shared equally; learning via pairwise comparison with parameter β=0.1 and mutation rate α=0.05. Punishment intensity each round p is t^(p)=g(f_c^(p),k)=k×5^{-f_c^(p)}, where f_c^(p) is current cooperation proportion; t^(p) is negatively related to f_c^(p) and increasing in k. Sanctioning institution performs binary search on k over [0,1] to achieve target cooperation θ=0.95, with stopping threshold ε=0.01 and possible reset if performance remains low.","Summarized from Sections 2.1.1–2.1.2, 3.1–3.2, and Appendix A.1–A.2.",1
10.3390_math10152823.md,Simulation – Punishing within a certain percentage range (±2% around k),Section 3.2 and Figure 3 introduce 'punishing within a certain percentage range' as a separate punishment method compared against negative feedback and others.,1,"Punishment method = percentage-range punishment on rate: punishment intensity t^(p) ∈ [0.98k, 1.02k]; k adapted by binary search.",Section 3.2: 'Punishing within a certain percentage range means that the punishment intensity varies within 98–102% of the punishment intention k.',1,False,Described in the context of simulation comparisons in Figure 3; no humans.,1,False,"Numerical simulation, not empirical experiment.",1,False,No lab with subjects.,1,True,Figure 3 description references same simulation environment as Appendix A.1.,1,False,"This row is for numerical results, not theoretical derivations.",1,100,Same base simulation setup as in Appendix A.1 for all punishment methods compared in Figure 3.,1,300,Appendix A.1 indicates 300 rounds per punishment intention; applied uniformly.,0.9,1,Same C/D contribution structure as base model.,0.4,0,Same as base PGG with pool punishment.,0.8,0.03,Same n and μ as base model.,1,N/A,No human participants.,1,0,No explicit summaries; algorithmic learning only.,0.6,N/A,No identities in simulation.,1,N/A,No rewards modeled.,1,0,Agents not given round-count information.,0.6,1,Punishment intensity t^(p) used to penalize defectors.,1,N/R,Cost to sanctioning institution not modeled.,1,N/R,Only proportional loss to defectors specified.,1,0,No reward scheme.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,5,Same base parameters as Appendix A.1.,1,N/R,Figure 3 only reports evolutionary rounds and cumulative punishment intensity; no contribution measures.,0.2,N/R,No aggregate contributions reported for this method.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metric given.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff not numerically reported.,0.2,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Computer simulation.,1,"Same PGG with pool punishment as base model. Punishment intensity each round drawn within [0.98k,1.02k], where k is the institution’s current punishment intention; binary search over k seeks high cooperation with low cost, as in Appendix A.2. Time cost measured as evolutionary rounds to reach stopping; monetary cost proxied by cumulative punishment intensity.",Section 3.2 and Figure 3 plus base setup in Appendix A.1–A.2.,0.9
10.3390_math10152823.md,Simulation – Penalizing within a certain amount range (±2% around k×R),Section 3.2 distinguishes 'penalizing within a certain amount range' as a separate punishment method examined in Figure 3.,1,"Punishment method = amount-range punishment: punishment intensity (interpreted as absolute fine) t^(p) ∈ [0.98k×R, 1.02k×R]; k adapted by binary search.","Section 3.2: 'When the sanctioning institution penalizes within a certain amount range, the punishment intensity t ∈ [0.98k×R, 1.02k×R].'",1,False,Condition is part of the simulation comparison.,1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,Simulation only.,1,True,Figure 3 refers to simulated operations across punishment methods.,1,False,Not a purely analytical model.,1,100,Assumed same n=100 as main simulation; not restated but implied by 'detailed setup ... Appendix A.1.',0.9,300,Uses same simulation environment as Appendix A.1; 300 rounds per k.,0.8,1,Same binary strategies C/D.,0.4,0,Same as base model.,0.8,0.03,Same μ and n.,1,N/A,No human subjects.,1,0,No such display in simulation description.,0.6,N/A,No identities.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,0,Agents lack explicit round count information.,0.6,1,Condition defines fines based on k and R.,1,N/R,Sanctioning institution's cost not modeled.,1,N/R,Implementation details of how t^(p) as amount interacts with r_i^(p) are not fully specified.,0.3,0,Only punishment varies across methods.,1,N/A,No reward scheme.,1,N/A,No reward scheme.,1,5,"R=5 from Appendix A.1; used in definition t ∈ [0.98k×R, 1.02k×R].",1,N/R,Figure 3 reports only rounds and cumulative punishment.,0.2,N/R,No explicit contribution outcomes given.,0.2,N/R,No efficiency metric reported.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff not numerically provided.,0.2,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,N/A,Simulation only.,1,No human,Numerical agent-based simulation.,1,"Same PGG with pool punishment as base. Here, punishment intensity variable t^(p) is interpreted as an absolute fine amount drawn from [0.98k×R,1.02k×R]; the institution uses binary search over k to approach high cooperation with low costs, as in Appendix A.2. Time and monetary losses are summarized in Figure 3.",Based on Section 3.2 description of amount-range punishment and reference to Appendix A.1.,0.7
10.3390_math10152823.md,Simulation – Punishing with a constant amount (t = k×R),Section 3.2: 'punishing with a constant amount' described and compared in Figure 3.,1,"Punishment method = constant-amount punishment: punishment intensity t^(p) = k×R (absolute fine), independent of group performance; k adapted by binary search.","Section 3.2: 'When punishing with a constant amount, t=k×R.'",1,False,Simulation-based analysis.,1,False,No empirical data.,1,False,No lab experiment.,1,True,Compared via numerical simulation in Figure 3.,1,False,Not just analytic derivation.,1,100,Assumed same n=100 per Appendix A.1 for all punishment methods.,0.9,300,Same 300-round evolution per k is implied.,0.8,1,Same C/D binary contribution structure.,0.4,0,Same starting endowment allocation.,0.8,0.03,Same μ and n as base model.,1,N/A,No humans.,1,0,Not an experimental interface; algorithm only.,0.6,N/A,No identities modeled.,1,N/A,No reward.,1,0,Agents not given horizon information.,0.6,1,Defectors are fined t=k×R each round as needed.,1,N/R,Institution cost not modeled.,1,N/R,Details of how fixed amount interacts with resources not fully elaborated.,0.3,0,No reward scheme present.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,N/A,No rewards.,1,5,R=5; used in definition t=k×R.,1,N/R,Only time and cumulative punishment reported.,0.2,N/R,No explicit contributions given per method.,0.2,N/R,Efficiency not reported.,0.2,N/R,Group payoff metrics not provided.,0.2,N/A,Simulation model only.,1,N/A,Simulation model only.,1,N/A,Simulation model only.,1,N/A,Simulation model only.,1,No human,Agent-based simulation.,1,"Same PGG with pool punishment as base model. Punishment intensity t^(p) is a constant absolute fine k×R, independent of cooperation level; k is updated via binary search based on observed cooperation to find low-cost, high-cooperation regime. Performance compared to other methods via mean evolutionary rounds and cumulative punishment intensity in Figure 3.",Section 3.2 description plus Appendix A.1–A.2 setup.,0