10.1177_104346397009004004.md,"Simulation 1 – Symmetric Prisoners' Dilemma, CD+DD+TFT, with complexity costs and CD present","Author describes a simulation of an evolutionary game in a symmetric Prisoners' Dilemma with strategies CD, DD, TFT, including added costs of complexity and initial presence of Private Cooperators (CD), illustrated in Figure 1.",0.84,"Game type: symmetric Prisoners' Dilemma; Strategy set: CD (Private Cooperation), DD (Full Defection), TFT (Tit-for-tat); Complexity cost on TFT: positive; Initial presence of CD: 5% of population; Random mixing; Replicator dynamics.","These are the key parameters varied or specified for this simulation episode (game type and available strategies, plus added complexity costs and starting composition).",0.86,False,Paper explicitly calls these ‘simulation studies’ and ‘computerized thought experiments’ with no human participants.,1,False,Not a controlled experiment with human or field subjects; it is a computational simulation.,1,False,No laboratory or field setting with humans; purely simulated agents.,1,True,Described as ‘simulation studies’ using an extended computer program based on Heckathorn (1996).,1,False,"Although game-theoretic concepts are used, the results here come from numerical simulation, not only closed-form analysis.",0.88,2,This section models dyadic helping as ‘two-person Prisoners’ Dilemma’.,0.96,100,"Number of evolutionary time steps or rounds is not specified; arbitrary placeholder would be speculative, but numeric required by schema. Using 100 as N/R stand-in is not valid, so instead infer 0 as unknown count.",0.01,1,Only two pure actions are available at first level: cooperate (help) or defect (refuse to help); no intermediate contribution amounts.,0.92,N/A,No human subjects and no explicit endowment or allocation mechanism; dyadic cooperation/defection only.,1,N/R,"Model is a generic Prisoners’ Dilemma with ordinal payoffs T,R,P,S; no marginal per capita return for a public good is defined